if(typeof BODIES_ORIG!=="undefined") BODIES_ORIG["gb-56644"]="THE religions of ancient Greece and Rome are extinct. The so-called divinities of Olympus have not a single worshipper among living men. They belong now not to the department of theology, but to those of literature and taste. There they still hold their place, and will continue to hold it, for they are too closely connected with the finest productions of poetry and art, both ancient and modern, to pass into oblivion.\n\nWe propose to tell the stories relating to them which have come down to us from the ancients, and which are alluded to by modern poets, essayists, and orators. Our readers may thus at the same time be entertained by the most charming fictions which fancy has ever created, and put in possession of information indispensable to every one who would read with intelligence the elegant literature of his own day.\n\nIn order to understand these stories, it will be necessary to acquaint ourselves with the ideas of the structure of the universe which prevailed among the Greeks—the people from whom the Romans, and other nations through them, received their science and religion.\n\nThe Greeks believed the earth to be flat and circular, their own country occupying the middle of it, the central point being either Mount Olympus, the abode of the gods, or Delphi, so famous for its oracle.\n\nThe circular disk of the earth was crossed from west to east and divided into two equal parts by the Sea, as they called the Mediterranean, and its continuation the Euxine, the only seas with which they were acquainted.\n\nAround the earth flowed the River Ocean, its course being from south to north on the western side of the earth, and in a contrary direction on the eastern side. It flowed in a steady, equable current, unvexed by storm or tempest. The sea, and all the rivers on earth, received their waters from it.\n\nThe northern portion of the earth was supposed to be inhabited by a happy race named the Hyperboreans, dwelling in everlasting bliss and spring beyond the lofty mountains whose caverns were supposed to send forth the piercing blasts of the north wind, which chilled the people of Hellas (Greece). Their country was inaccessible by land or sea. They lived exempt from disease or old age, from toils and warfare. Moore has given us the “Song of a Hyperborean,” beginning\n\n“I come from a land in the sun-bright deep, Where golden gardens glow, Where the winds of the north, becalmed in sleep, Their conch shells never blow.”\n\nOn the south side of the earth, close to the stream of Ocean, dwelt a people happy and virtuous as the Hyperboreans. They were named the Æthiopians. The gods favored them so highly that they were wont to leave at times their Olympian abodes and go to share their sacrifices and banquets.\n\nOn the western margin of the earth, by the stream of Ocean, lay a happy place named the Elysian Plain, whither mortals favored by the gods were transported without tasting of death, to enjoy an immortality of bliss. This happy region was also called the “Fortunate Fields,” and the “Isles of the Blessed.”\n\nWe thus see that the Greeks of the early ages knew little of any real people except those to the east and south of their own country, or near the coast of the Mediterranean. Their imagination meantime peopled the western portion of this sea with giants, monsters, and enchantresses; while they placed around the disk of the earth, which they probably regarded as of no great width, nations enjoying the peculiar favor of the gods, and blessed with happiness and longevity.\n\nThe Dawn, the Sun, and the Moon were supposed to rise out of the Ocean, on the eastern side, and to drive through the air, giving light to gods and men. The stars, also, except those forming the Wain or Bear, and others near them, rose out of and sank into the stream of Ocean. There the sun-god embarked in a winged boat, which conveyed him round by the northern part of the earth, back to his place of rising in the east. Milton alludes to this in his “Comus”:\n\n“Now the gilded car of day His golden axle doth allay In the steep Atlantic stream, And the slope Sun his upward beam Shoots against the dusky pole, Pacing towards the other goal Of his chamber in the east.”\n\nThe abode of the gods was on the summit of Mount Olympus, in Thessaly. A gate of clouds, kept by the goddesses named the Seasons, opened to permit the passage of the Celestials to earth, and to receive them on their return. The gods had their separate dwellings; but all, when summoned, repaired to the palace of Jupiter, as did also those deities whose usual abode was the earth, the waters, or the underworld. It was also in the great hall of the palace of the Olympian king that the gods feasted each day on ambrosia and nectar, their food and drink, the latter being handed round by the lovely goddess Hebe. Here they conversed of the affairs of heaven and earth; and as they quaffed their nectar, Apollo, the god of music, delighted them with the tones of his lyre, to which the Muses sang in responsive strains. When the sun was set, the gods retired to sleep in their respective dwellings.\n\nThe following lines from the “Odyssey” will show how Homer conceived of Olympus:\n\n“So saying, Minerva, goddess azure-eyed, Rose to Olympus, the reputed seat Eternal of the gods, which never storms Disturb, rains drench, or snow invades, but calm The expanse and cloudless shines with purest day. There the inhabitants divine rejoice Forever.” Cowper.\n\nThe robes and other parts of the dress of the goddesses were woven by Minerva and the Graces and everything of a more solid nature was formed of the various metals. Vulcan was architect, smith, armorer, chariot builder, and artist of all work in Olympus. He built of brass the houses of the gods; he made for them the golden shoes with which they trod the air or the water, and moved from place to place with the speed of the wind, or even of thought. He also shod with brass the celestial steeds, which whirled the chariots of the gods through the air, or along the surface of the sea. He was able to bestow on his workmanship self-motion, so that the tripods (chairs and tables) could move of themselves in and out of the celestial hall. He even endowed with intelligence the golden handmaidens whom he made to wait on himself.\n\nJupiter, or Jove (Zeus[2]), though called the father of gods and men, had himself a beginning. Saturn (Cronos) was his father, and Rhea (Ops) his mother. Saturn and Rhea were of the race of Titans, who were the children of Earth and Heaven, which sprang from Chaos, of which we shall give a further account in our next chapter.\n\nThere is another cosmogony, or account of the creation, according to which Earth, Erebus, and Love were the first of beings. Love (Eros) issued from the egg of Night, which floated on Chaos. By his arrows and torch he pierced and vivified all things, producing life and joy.\n\nSaturn and Rhea were not the only Titans. There were others, whose names were Oceanus, Hyperion, Iapetus, and Ophion, males; and Themis, Mnemosyne, Eurynome, females. They are spoken of as the elder gods, whose dominion was afterwards transferred to others. Saturn yielded to Jupiter, Oceanus to Neptune, Hyperion to Apollo. Hyperion was the father of the Sun, Moon, and Dawn. He is therefore the original sun-god, and is painted with the splendor and beauty which were afterwards bestowed on Apollo.\n\n“Hyperion’s curls, the front of Jove himself.” Shakspeare.\n\nOphion and Eurynome ruled over Olympus till they were dethroned by Saturn and Rhea. Milton alludes to them in “Paradise Lost.” He says the heathens seem to have had some knowledge of the temptation and fall of man.\n\n“And fabled how the serpent, whom they called Ophion, with Eurynome, (the wide-Encroaching Eve perhaps,) had first the rule Of high Olympus, thence by Saturn driven.”\n\nThe representations given of Saturn are not very consistent; for on the one hand his reign is said to have been the golden age of innocence and purity, and on the other he is described as a monster who devoured his children.[3] Jupiter, however, escaped this fate, and when grown up espoused Metis (Prudence), who administered a draught to Saturn which caused him to disgorge his children. Jupiter, with his brothers and sisters, now rebelled against their father Saturn and his brothers the Titans; vanquished them, and imprisoned some of them in Tartarus, inflicting other penalties on others. Atlas was condemned to bear up the heavens on his shoulders.\n\nOn the dethronement of Saturn, Jupiter with his brothers Neptune (Poseidon) and Pluto (Dis) divided his dominions. Jupiter’s portion was the heavens, Neptune’s the ocean, and Pluto’s the realms of the dead. Earth and Olympus were common property. Jupiter was king of gods and men. The thunder was his weapon, and he bore a shield called Ægis, made for him by Vulcan. The eagle was his favorite bird, and bore his thunderbolts.\n\nJuno (Hera) was the wife of Jupiter, and queen of the gods. Iris, the goddess of the rainbow, was her attendant and messenger. The peacock was her favorite bird.\n\nVulcan (Hephæstos), the celestial artist, was the son of Jupiter and Juno. He was born lame, and his mother was so displeased at the sight of him that she flung him out of heaven. Other accounts say that Jupiter kicked him out for taking part with his mother in a quarrel which occurred between them. Vulcan’s lameness, according to this account, was the consequence of his fall. He was a whole day falling, and at last alighted in the island of Lemnos, which was thenceforth sacred to him. Milton alludes to this story in “Paradise Lost,” Book I.:\n\n“. . . From morn To noon he fell, from noon to dewy eve, A summer’s day; and with the setting sun Dropped from the zenith, like a falling star, On Lemnos, the Ægean isle.”\n\nMars (Ares), the god of war, was the son of Jupiter and Juno.\n\nPhœbus Apollo, the god of archery, prophecy, and music, was the son of Jupiter and Latona, and brother of Diana (Artemis). He was god of the sun, as Diana, his sister, was the goddess of the moon.\n\nVenus (Aphrodite), the goddess of love and beauty, was the daughter of Jupiter and Dione. Others say that Venus sprang from the foam of the sea. The zephyr wafted her along the waves to the Isle of Cyprus, where she was received and attired by the Seasons, and then led to the assembly of the gods. All were charmed with her beauty, and each one demanded her for his wife. Jupiter gave her to Vulcan, in gratitude for the service he had rendered in forging thunderbolts. So the most beautiful of the goddesses became the wife of the most ill-favored of gods. Venus possessed an embroidered girdle called Cestus, which had the power of inspiring love. Her favorite birds were swans and doves, and the plants sacred to her were the rose and the myrtle.\n\nCupid (Eros), the god of love, was the son of Venus. He was her constant companion; and, armed with bow and arrows, he shot the darts of desire into the bosoms of both gods and men. There was a deity named Anteros, who was sometimes represented as the avenger of slighted love, and sometimes as the symbol of reciprocal affection. The following legend is told of him:\n\nVenus, complaining to Themis that her son Eros continued always a child, was told by her that it was because he was solitary, and that if he had a brother he would grow apace. Anteros was soon afterwards born, and Eros immediately was seen to increase rapidly in size and strength.\n\nMinerva (Pallas, Athene), the goddess of wisdom, was the offspring of Jupiter, without a mother. She sprang forth from his head completely armed. Her favorite bird was the owl, and the plant sacred to her the olive.\n\nByron, in “Childe Harold,” alludes to the birth of Minerva thus:\n\n“Can tyrants but by tyrants conquered be, And Freedom find no champion and no child, Such as Columbia saw arise, when she Sprang forth a Pallas, armed and undefiled? Or must such minds be nourished in the wild, Deep in the unpruned forest, ’midst the roar Of cataracts, where nursing Nature smiled On infant Washington? Has earth no more Such seeds within her breast, or Europe no such shore?”\n\nMercury (Hermes) was the son of Jupiter and Maia. He presided over commerce, wrestling, and other gymnastic exercises, even over thieving, and everything, in short, which required skill and dexterity. He was the messenger of Jupiter, and wore a winged cap and winged shoes. He bore in his hand a rod entwined with two serpents, called the caduceus.\n\nMercury is said to have invented the lyre. He found, one day, a tortoise, of which he took the shell, made holes in the opposite edges of it, and drew cords of linen through them, and the instrument was complete. The cords were nine, in honor of the nine Muses. Mercury gave the lyre to Apollo, and received from him in exchange the caduceus.[4]\n\nCeres (Demeter) was the daughter of Saturn and Rhea. She had a daughter named Proserpine (Persephone), who became the wife of Pluto, and queen of the realms of the dead. Ceres presided over agriculture.\n\nBacchus (Dionysus), the god of wine, was the son of Jupiter and Semele. He represents not only the intoxicating power of wine, but its social and beneficent influences likewise, so that he is viewed as the promoter of civilization, and a lawgiver and lover of peace.\n\nThe Muses were the daughters of Jupiter and Mnemosyne (Memory). They presided over song, and prompted the memory. They were nine in number, to each of whom was assigned the precedence over some particular department of literature, art, or science. Calliope was the muse of epic poetry, Clio of history, Euterpe of lyric poetry, Melpomene of tragedy, Terpsichore of choral dance and song, Erato of love poetry, Polyhymnia of sacred poetry, Urania of astronomy, Thalia of comedy.\n\nThe Graces were goddesses presiding over the banquet, the dance, and all social enjoyments and elegant arts. They were three in number. Their names were Euphrosyne, Aglaia, and Thalia.\n\nSpenser describes the office of the Graces thus:\n\n“These three on men all gracious gifts bestow Which deck the body or adorn the mind, To make them lovely or well-favored show; As comely carriage, entertainment kind, Sweet semblance, friendly offices that bind, And all the complements of courtesy; They teach us how to each degree and kind We should ourselves demean, to low, to high, To friends, to foes; which skill men call Civility.”\n\nThe Fates were also three—Clotho, Lachesis, and Atropos. Their office was to spin the thread of human destiny, and they were armed with shears, with which they cut it off when they pleased. They were the daughters of Themis (Law), who sits by Jove on his throne to give him counsel.\n\nThe Erinnyes, or Furies, were three goddesses who punished by their secret stings the crimes of those who escaped or defied public justice. The heads of the Furies were wreathed with serpents, and their whole appearance was terrific and appalling. Their names were Alecto, Tisiphone, and Megæra. They were also called Eumenides.\n\nNemesis was also an avenging goddess. She represents the righteous anger of the gods, particularly towards the proud and insolent.\n\nPan was the god of flocks and shepherds. His favorite residence was in Arcadia.\n\nThe Satyrs were deities of the woods and fields. They were conceived to be covered with bristly hair, their heads decorated with short, sprouting horns, and their feet like goats’ feet.\n\nMomus was the god of laughter, and Plutus the god of wealth.\n\n# ROMAN DIVINITIES\n\nThe preceding are Grecian divinities, though received also by the Romans. Those which follow are peculiar to Roman mythology:\n\nSaturn was an ancient Italian deity. It was attempted to identify him with the Grecian god Cronos, and fabled that after his dethronement by Jupiter he fled to Italy, where he reigned during what was called the Golden Age. In memory of his beneficent dominion, the feast of Saturnalia was held every year in the winter season. Then all public business was suspended, declarations of war and criminal executions were postponed, friends made presents to one another, and the slaves were indulged with great liberties. A feast was given them at which they sat at table, while their masters served them, to show the natural equality of men, and that all things belonged equally to all, in the reign of Saturn.\n\nFaunus,[5] the grandson of Saturn, was worshipped as the god of fields and shepherds, and also as a prophetic god. His name in the plural, Fauns, expressed a class of gamesome deities, like the Satyrs of the Greeks.\n\nQuirinus was a war god, said to be no other than Romulus, the founder of Rome, exalted after his death to a place among the gods.\n\nBellona, a war goddess.\n\nTerminus, the god of landmarks. His statue was a rude stone or post, set in the ground to mark the boundaries of fields.\n\nPales, the goddess presiding over cattle and pastures.\n\nPomona presided over fruit trees.\n\nFlora, the goddess of flowers.\n\nLucina, the goddess of childbirth.\n\nVesta (the Hestia of the Greeks) was a deity presiding over the public and private hearth. A sacred fire, tended by six virgin priestesses called Vestals, flamed in her temple. As the safety of the city was held to be connected with its conservation, the neglect of the virgins, if they let it go out, was severely punished, and the fire was rekindled from the rays of the sun.\n\nLiber is the Latin name of Bacchus; and Mulciber of Vulcan.\n\nJanus was the porter of heaven. He opens the year, the first month being named after him. He is the guardian deity of gates, on which account he is commonly represented with two heads, because every door looks two ways. His temples at Rome were numerous. In war time the gates of the principal one were always open. In peace they were closed; but they were shut only once between the reign of Numa and that of Augustus.\n\nThe Penates were the gods who were supposed to attend to the welfare and prosperity of the family. Their name is derived from Penus, the pantry, which was sacred to them. Every master of a family was the priest to the Penates of his own house.\n\nThe Lares, or Lars, were also household gods, but differed from the Penates in being regarded as the deified spirits of mortals. The family Lars were held to be the souls of the ancestors, who watched over and protected their descendants. The words Lemur and Larva more nearly correspond to our word Ghost.\n\nThe Romans believed that every man had his Genius, and every woman her Juno: that is, a spirit who had given them being, and was regarded as their protector through life. On their birthdays men made offerings to their Genius, women to their Juno.\n\nA modern poet thus alludes to some of the Roman gods:\n\n“Pomona loves the orchard, And Liber loves the vine, And Pales loves the straw-built shed Warm with the breath of kine; And Venus loves the whisper Of plighted youth and maid, In April’s ivory moonlight, Beneath the chestnut shade.” —Macaulay, “Prophecy of Capys.”\n————\n\nN.B.—It is to be observed that in proper names the final e and es are to be sounded. Thus Cybele and Penates are words of three syllables. But Proserpine and Thebes are exceptions, and to be pronounced as English words. In the Index at the close of the volume we shall mark the accented syllable in all words which appear to require it.\n————\n\n# PROMETHEUS AND PANDORA\n\nTHE creation of the world is a problem naturally fitted to excite the liveliest interest of man, its inhabitant. The ancient pagans, not having the information on the subject which we derive from the pages of Scripture, had their own way of telling the story, which is as follows:\n\nBefore earth and sea and heaven were created, all things wore one aspect, to which we give the name of Chaos—a confused and shapeless mass, nothing but dead weight, in which, however, slumbered the seeds of things. Earth, sea, and air were all mixed up together; so the earth was not solid, the sea was not fluid, and the air was not transparent. God and Nature at last interposed, and put an end to this discord, separating earth from sea, and heaven from both. The fiery part, being the lightest, sprang up, and formed the skies; the air was next in weight and place. The earth, being heavier, sank below; and the water took the lowest place, and buoyed up the earth.\n\nHere some god—it is not known which—gave his good offices in arranging and disposing the earth. He appointed rivers and bays their places, raised mountains, scooped out valleys, distributed woods, fountains, fertile fields, and stony plains. The air being cleared, the stars began to appear, fishes took possession of the sea, birds of the air, and four-footed beasts of the land.\n\nBut a nobler animal was wanted, and Man was made. It is not known whether the creator made him of divine materials, or whether in the earth, so lately separated from heaven, there lurked still some heavenly seeds. Prometheus took some of this earth, and kneading it up with water, made man in the image of the gods. He gave him an upright stature, so that while all other animals turn their faces downward, and look to the earth, he raises his to heaven, and gazes on the stars.\n\nPrometheus was one of the Titans, a gigantic race, who inhabited the earth before the creation of man. To him and his brother Epimetheus was committed the office of making man, and providing him and all other animals with the faculties necessary for their preservation. Epimetheus undertook to do this, and Prometheus was to overlook his work, when it was done. Epimetheus accordingly proceeded to bestow upon the different animals the various gifts of courage, strength, swiftness, sagacity; wings to one, claws to another, a shelly covering to a third, etc. But when man came to be provided for, who was to be superior to all other animals, Epimetheus had been so prodigal of his resources that he had nothing left to bestow upon him. In his perplexity he resorted to his brother Prometheus, who, with the aid of Minerva, went up to heaven, and lighted his torch at the chariot of the sun, and brought down fire to man. With this gift man was more than a match for all other animals. It enabled him to make weapons wherewith to subdue them; tools with which to cultivate the earth; to warm his dwelling, so as to be comparatively independent of climate; and finally to introduce the arts and to coin money, the means of trade and commerce.\n\nWoman was not yet made. The story (absurd enough!) is that Jupiter made her, and sent her to Prometheus and his brother, to punish them for their presumption in stealing fire from heaven; and man, for accepting the gift. The first woman was named Pandora. She was made in heaven, every god contributing something to perfect her. Venus gave her beauty, Mercury persuasion, Apollo music, etc. Thus equipped, she was conveyed to earth, and presented to Epimetheus, who gladly accepted her, though cautioned by his brother to beware of Jupiter and his gifts. Epimetheus had in his house a jar, in which were kept certain noxious articles, for which, in fitting man for his new abode, he had had no occasion. Pandora was seized with an eager curiosity to know what this jar contained; and one day she slipped off the cover and looked in. Forthwith there escaped a multitude of plagues for hapless man,—such as gout, rheumatism, and colic for his body, and envy, spite, and revenge for his mind,—and scattered themselves far and wide. Pandora hastened to replace the lid! but, alas! the whole contents of the jar had escaped, one thing only excepted, which lay at the bottom, and that was hope. So we see at this day, whatever evils are abroad, hope never entirely leaves us; and while we have that, no amount of other ills can make us completely wretched.\n\nAnother story is that Pandora was sent in good faith, by Jupiter, to bless man; that she was furnished with a box, containing her marriage presents, into which every god had put some blessing. She opened the box incautiously, and the blessings all escaped, hope only excepted. This story seems more probable than the former; for how could hope, so precious a jewel as it is, have been kept in a jar full of all manner of evils, as in the former statement?\n\nThe world being thus furnished with inhabitants, the first age was an age of innocence and happiness, called the Golden Age. Truth and right prevailed, though not enforced by law, nor was there any magistrate to threaten or punish. The forest had not yet been robbed of its trees to furnish timbers for vessels, nor had men built fortifications round their towns. There were no such things as swords, spears, or helmets. The earth brought forth all things necessary for man, without his labor in ploughing or sowing. Perpetual spring reigned, flowers sprang up without seed, the rivers flowed with milk and wine, and yellow honey distilled from the oaks.\n\nThen succeeded the Silver Age, inferior to the golden, but better than that of brass. Jupiter shortened the spring, and divided the year into seasons. Then, first, men had to endure the extremes of heat and cold, and houses became necessary. Caves were the first dwellings, and leafy coverts of the woods, and huts woven of twigs. Crops would no longer grow without planting. The farmer was obliged to sow the seed and the toiling ox to draw the plough.\n\nNext came the Brazen Age, more savage of temper, and readier to the strife of arms, yet not altogether wicked. The hardest and worst was the Iron Age. Crime burst in like a flood; modesty, truth, and honor fled. In their places came fraud and cunning, violence, and the wicked love of gain. Then seamen spread sails to the wind, and the trees were torn from the mountains to serve for keels to ships, and vex the face of ocean. The earth, which till now had been cultivated in common, began to be divided off into possessions. Men were not satisfied with what the surface produced, but must dig into its bowels, and draw forth from thence the ores of metals. Mischievous iron, and more mischievous gold, were produced. War sprang up, using both as weapons; the guest was not safe in his friend’s house; and sons-in-law and fathers-in-law, brothers and sisters, husbands and wives, could not trust one another. Sons wished their fathers dead, that they might come to the inheritance; family love lay prostrate. The earth was wet with slaughter, and the gods abandoned it, one by one, till Astræa[6] alone was left, and finally she also took her departure.\n\nJupiter, seeing this state of things, burned with anger. He summoned the gods to council. They obeyed the call, and took the road to the palace of heaven. The road, which any one may see in a clear night, stretches across the face of the sky, and is called the Milky Way. Along the road stand the palaces of the illustrious gods; the common people of the skies live apart, on either side. Jupiter addressed the assembly. He set forth the frightful condition of things on the earth, and closed by announcing his intention to destroy the whole of its inhabitants, and provide a new race, unlike the first, who would be more worthy of life, and much better worshippers of the gods. So saying he took a thunderbolt, and was about to launch it at the world, and destroy it by burning; but recollecting the danger that such a conflagration might set heaven itself on fire, he changed his plan, and resolved to drown it. The north wind, which scatters the clouds, was chained up; the south was sent out, and soon covered all the face of heaven with a cloak of pitchy darkness. The clouds, driven together, resound with a crash; torrents of rain fall; the crops are laid low; the year’s labor of the husbandman perishes in an hour. Jupiter, not satisfied with his own waters, calls on his brother Neptune to aid him with his. He lets loose the rivers, and pours them over the land. At the same time, he heaves the land with an earthquake, and brings in the reflux of the ocean over the shores. Flocks, herds, men, and houses are swept away, and temples, with their sacred enclosures, profaned. If any edifice remained standing, it was overwhelmed, and its turrets lay hid beneath the waves. Now all was sea, sea without shore. Here and there an individual remained on a projecting hill-top, and a few, in boats, pulled the oar where they had lately driven the plough. The fishes swim among the tree-tops; the anchor is let down into a garden. Where the graceful lambs played but now, unwieldy sea calves gambol. The wolf swims among the sheep, the yellow lions and tigers struggle in the water. The strength of the wild boar serves him not, nor his swiftness the stag. The birds fall with weary wing into the water, having found no land for a resting-place. Those living beings whom the water spared fell a prey to hunger.\n\nParnassus alone, of all the mountains, overtopped the waves; and there Deucalion, and his wife Pyrrha, of the race of Prometheus, found refuge—he a just man, and she a faithful worshipper of the gods. Jupiter, when he saw none left alive but this pair, and remembered their harmless lives and pious demeanor, ordered the north winds to drive away the clouds, and disclose the skies to earth, and earth to the skies. Neptune also directed Triton to blow on his shell, and sound a retreat to the waters. The waters obeyed, and the sea returned to its shores, and the rivers to their channels. Then Deucalion thus addressed Pyrrha: “O wife, only surviving woman, joined to me first by the ties of kindred and marriage, and now by a common danger, would that we possessed the power of our ancestor Prometheus, and could renew the race as he at first made it! But as we cannot, let us seek yonder temple, and inquire of the gods what remains for us to do.” They entered the temple, deformed as it was with slime, and approached the altar, where no fire burned. There they fell prostrate on the earth, and prayed the goddess to inform them how they might retrieve their miserable affairs. The oracle answered, “Depart from the temple with head veiled and garments unbound, and cast behind you the bones of your mother.” They heard the words with astonishment. Pyrrha first broke silence: “We cannot obey; we dare not profane the remains of our parents.” They sought the thickest shades of the wood, and revolved the oracle in their minds. At length Deucalion spoke: “Either my sagacity deceives me, or the command is one we may obey without impiety. The earth is the great parent of all; the stones are her bones; these we may cast behind us; and I think this is what the oracle means. At least, it will do no harm to try.” They veiled their faces, unbound their garments, and picked up stones, and cast them behind them. The stones (wonderful to relate) began to grow soft, and assume shape. By degrees, they put on a rude resemblance to the human form, like a block half-finished in the hands of the sculptor. The moisture and slime that were about them became flesh; the stony part became bones; the veins remained veins, retaining their name, only changing their use. Those thrown by the hand of the man became men, and those by the woman became women. It was a hard race, and well adapted to labor, as we find ourselves to be at this day, giving plain indications of our origin.\n\nThe comparison of Eve to Pandora is too obvious to have escaped Milton, who introduces it in Book IV. of “Paradise-Lost”:\n\n“More lovely than Pandora, whom the gods Endowed with all their gifts; and O, too like In sad event, when to the unwiser son Of Japhet brought by Hermes, she insnared Mankind with her fair looks, to be avenged On him who had stole Jove’s authentic fire.”\n\nPrometheus and Epimetheus were sons of Iapetus, which Milton changes to Japhet.\n\nPrometheus has been a favorite subject with the poets. He is represented as the friend of mankind, who interposed in their behalf when Jove was incensed against them, and who taught them civilization and the arts. But as, in so doing, he transgressed the will of Jupiter, he drew down on himself the anger of the ruler of gods and men. Jupiter had him chained to a rock on Mount Caucasus, where a vulture preyed on his liver, which was renewed as fast as devoured. This state of torment might have been brought to an end at any time by Prometheus, if he had been willing to submit to his oppressor; for he possessed a secret which involved the stability of Jove’s throne, and if he would have revealed it, he might have been at once taken into favor. But that he disdained to do. He has therefore become the symbol of magnanimous endurance of unmerited suffering, and strength of will resisting oppression.\n\nByron and Shelley have both treated this theme. The following are Byron’s lines:\n\n“Titan! to whose immortal eyes The sufferings of mortality, Seen in their sad reality, Were not as things that gods despise; What was thy pity’s recompense? A silent suffering, and intense; The rock, the vulture, and the chain; All that the proud can feel of pain; The agony they do not show; The suffocating sense of woe.\n\n“Thy godlike crime was to be kind; To render with thy precepts less The sum of human wretchedness, And strengthen man with his own mind. And, baffled as thou wert from high, Still, in thy patient energy In the endurance and repulse Of thine impenetrable spirit, Which earth and heaven could not convulse, A mighty lesson we inherit.”\n\nByron also employs the same allusion, in his “Ode to Napoleon Bonaparte”:\n\n“Or, like the thief of fire from heaven, Wilt thou withstand the shock? And share with him—the unforgiven— His vulture and his rock?”\n\n————\n\n# APOLLO AND DAPHNE—PYRAMUS AND THISBE—CEPHALUS AND PROCRIS\n\nTHE slime with which the earth was covered by the waters of the flood produced an excessive fertility, which called forth every variety of production, both bad and good. Among the rest, Python, an enormous serpent, crept forth, the terror of the people, and lurked in the caves of Mount Parnassus. Apollo slew him with his arrows—weapons which he had not before used against any but feeble animals, hares, wild goats, and such game. In commemoration of this illustrious conquest he instituted the Pythian games, in which the victor in feats of strength, swiftness of foot, or in the chariot race was crowned with a wreath of beech leaves; for the laurel was not yet adopted by Apollo as his own tree.\n\nThe famous statue of Apollo called the Belvedere represents the god after this victory over the serpent Python. To this Byron alludes in his “Childe Harold,” iv., 161:\n\n“. . . The lord of the unerring bow, The god of life, and poetry, and light, The Sun, in human limbs arrayed, and brow All radiant from his triumph in the fight. The shaft has just been shot; the arrow bright With an immortal’s vengeance; in his eye And nostril, beautiful disdain, and might And majesty flash their full lightnings by, Developing in that one glance the Deity.”\n\n# APOLLO AND DAPHNE\n\nDaphne was Apollo’s first love. It was not brought about by accident, but by the malice of Cupid. Apollo saw the boy playing with his bow and arrows; and being himself elated with his recent victory over Python, he said to him, “What have you to do with warlike weapons, saucy boy? Leave them for hands worthy of them. Behold the conquest I have won by means of them over the vast serpent who stretched his poisonous body over acres of the plain! Be content with your torch, child, and kindle up your flames, as you call them, where you will, but presume not to meddle with my weapons.” Venus’s boy heard these words, and rejoined, “Your arrows may strike all things else, Apollo, but mine shall strike you.” So saying, he took his stand on a rock of Parnassus, and drew from his quiver two arrows of different workmanship, one to excite love, the other to repel it. The former was of gold and sharp pointed, the latter blunt and tipped with lead. With the leaden shaft he struck the nymph Daphne, the daughter of the river god Peneus, and with the golden one Apollo, through the heart. Forthwith the god was seized with love for the maiden, and she abhorred the thought of loving. Her delight was in woodland sports and in the spoils of the chase. Many lovers sought her, but she spurned them all, ranging the woods, and taking no thought of Cupid nor of Hymen. Her father often said to her, “Daughter, you owe me a son-in-law; you owe me grandchildren.” She, hating the thought of marriage as a crime, with her beautiful face tinged all over with blushes, threw her arms around her father’s neck, and said, “Dearest father, grant me this favor, that I may always remain unmarried, like Diana.” He consented, but at the same time said, “Your own face will forbid it.”\n\nApollo loved her, and longed to obtain her; and he who gives oracles to all the world was not wise enough to look into his own fortunes. He saw her hair flung loose over her shoulders, and said, “If so charming in disorder, what would it be if arranged?” He saw her eyes bright as stars; he saw her lips, and was not satisfied with only seeing them. He admired her hands and arms, naked to the shoulder, and whatever was hidden from view he imagined more beautiful still. He followed her; she fled, swifter than the wind, and delayed not a moment at his entreaties. “Stay,” said he, “daughter of Peneus; I am not a foe. Do not fly me as a lamb flies the wolf, or a dove the hawk. It is for love I pursue you. You make me miserable, for fear you should fall and hurt yourself on these stones, and I should be the cause. Pray run slower, and I will follow slower. I am no clown, no rude peasant. Jupiter is my father, and I am lord of Delphos and Tenedos, and know all things, present and future. I am the god of song and the lyre. My arrows fly true to the mark; but, alas! an arrow more fatal than mine has pierced my heart! I am the god of medicine, and know the virtues of all healing plants. Alas! I suffer a malady that no balm can cure!”\n\nThe nymph continued her flight, and left his plea half uttered. And even as she fled she charmed him. The wind blew her garments, and her unbound hair streamed loose behind her. The god grew impatient to find his wooings thrown away, and, sped by Cupid, gained upon her in the race. It was like a hound pursuing a hare, with open jaws ready to seize, while the feebler animal darts forward, slipping from the very grasp. So flew the god and the virgin—he on the wings of love, and she on those of fear. The pursuer is the more rapid, however, and gains upon her, and his panting breath blows upon her hair. Her strength begins to fail, and, ready to sink, she calls upon her father, the river god: “Help me, Peneus! open the earth to enclose me, or change my form, which has brought me into this danger!” Scarcely had she spoken, when a stiffness seized all her limbs; her bosom began to be enclosed in a tender bark; her hair became leaves; her arms became branches; her foot stuck fast in the ground, as a root; her face became a tree-top, retaining nothing of its former self but its beauty. Apollo stood amazed. He touched the stem, and felt the flesh tremble under the new bark. He embraced the branches, and lavished kisses on the wood. The branches shrank from his lips. “Since you cannot be my wife,” said he, “you shall assuredly be my tree. I will wear you for my crown; I will decorate with you my harp and my quiver; and when the great Roman conquerors lead up the triumphal pomp to the Capitol, you shall be woven into wreaths for their brows. And, as eternal youth is mine, you also shall be always green, and your leaf know no decay.” The nymph, now changed into a Laurel tree, bowed its head in grateful acknowledgment.\n\nThat Apollo should be the god both of music and poetry will not appear strange, but that medicine should also be assigned to his province, may. The poet Armstrong, himself a physician, thus accounts for it:\n\n“Music exalts each joy, allays each grief, Expels diseases, softens every pain; And hence the wise of ancient days adored One power of physic, melody, and song.”\n\nThe story of Apollo and Daphne is often alluded to by the poets. Waller applies it to the case of one whose amatory verses, though they did not soften the heart of his mistress, yet won for the poet wide-spread fame:\n\n“Yet what he sung in his immortal strain, Though unsuccessful, was not sung in vain. All but the nymph that should redress his wrong, Attend his passion and approve his song. Like Phœbus thus, acquiring unsought praise, He caught at love and filled his arms with bays.”\n\nThe following stanza from Shelley’s “Adonais” alludes to Byron’s early quarrel with the reviewers:\n\n“The herded wolves, bold only to pursue; The obscene ravens, clamorous o’er the dead; The vultures, to the conqueror’s banner true, Who feed where Desolation first has fed, And whose wings rain contagion: how they fled, When like Apollo, from his golden bow, The Pythian of the age one arrow sped And smiled! The spoilers tempt no second blow; They fawn on the proud feet that spurn them as they go.”\n\n# PYRAMUS AND THISBE\n\nPyramus was the handsomest youth, and Thisbe the fairest maiden, in all Babylonia, where Semiramis reigned. Their parents occupied adjoining houses; and neighborhood brought the young people together, and acquaintance ripened into love. They would gladly have married, but their parents forbade. One thing, however, they could not forbid—that love should glow with equal ardor in the bosoms of both. They conversed by signs and glances, and the fire burned more intensely for being covered up. In the wall that parted the two houses there was a crack, caused by some fault in the structure. No one had remarked it before, but the lovers discovered it. What will not love discover! It afforded a passage to the voice; and tender messages used to pass backward and forward through the gap. As they stood, Pyramus on this side, Thisbe on that, their breaths would mingle. “Cruel wall,” they said, “why do you keep two lovers apart? But we will not be ungrateful. We owe you, we confess, the privilege of transmitting loving words to willing ears.” Such words they uttered on different sides of the wall; and when night came and they must say farewell, they pressed their lips upon the wall, she on her side, he on his, as they could come no nearer.\n\nNext morning, when Aurora had put out the stars, and the sun had melted the frost from the grass, they met at the accustomed spot. Then, after lamenting their hard fate, they agreed that next night, when all was still, they would slip away from watchful eyes, leave their dwellings and walk out into the fields; and to insure a meeting, repair to a well-known edifice standing without the city’s bounds, called the Tomb of Ninus, and that the one who came first should await the other at the foot of a certain tree. It was a white mulberry tree, and stood near a cool spring. All was agreed on, and they waited impatiently for the sun to go down beneath the waters and night to rise up from them. Then cautiously Thisbe stole forth, unobserved by the family, her head covered with a veil, made her way to the monument and sat down under the tree. As she sat alone in the dim light of the evening she descried a lioness, her jaws reeking with recent slaughter, approaching the fountain to slake her thirst. Thisbe fled at the sight, and sought refuge in the hollow of a rock. As she fled she dropped her veil. The lioness after drinking at the spring turned to retreat to the woods, and seeing the veil on the ground, tossed and rent it with her bloody mouth.\n\nPyramus, having been delayed, now approached the place of meeting. He saw in the sand the footsteps of the lion, and the color fled from his cheeks at the sight. Presently he found the veil all rent and bloody. “O hapless girl,” said he, “I have been the cause of thy death! Thou, more worthy of life than I, hast fallen the first victim. I will follow. I am the guilty cause, in tempting thee forth to a place of such peril, and not being myself on the spot to guard thee. Come forth, ye lions, from the rocks, and tear this guilty body with your teeth.” He took up the veil, carried it with him to the appointed tree, and covered it with kisses and with tears. “My blood also shall stain your texture,” said he, and drawing his sword plunged it into his heart. The blood spurted from the wound, and tinged the white mulberries of the tree all red; and sinking into the earth reached the roots, so that the red color mounted through the trunk to the fruit.\n\nBy this time Thisbe, still trembling with fear, yet wishing not to disappoint her lover, stepped cautiously forth, looking anxiously for the youth, eager to tell him the danger she had escaped. When she came to the spot and saw the changed color of the mulberries she doubted whether it was the same place. While she hesitated she saw the form of one struggling in the agonies of death. She started back, a shudder ran through her frame as a ripple on the face of the still water when a sudden breeze sweeps over it. But as soon as she recognized her lover, she screamed and beat her breast, embracing the lifeless body, pouring tears into its wounds, and imprinting kisses on the cold lips. “O Pyramus,” she cried, “what has done this? Answer me, Pyramus; it is your own Thisbe that speaks. Hear me, dearest, and lift that drooping head!” At the name of Thisbe Pyramus opened his eyes, then closed them again. She saw her veil stained with blood and the scabbard empty of its sword. “Thy own hand has slain thee, and for my sake,” she said. “I too can be brave for once, and my love is as strong as thine. I will follow thee in death, for I have been the cause; and death which alone could part us shall not prevent my joining thee. And ye, unhappy parents of us both, deny us not our united request. As love and death have joined us, let one tomb contain us. And thou, tree, retain the marks of slaughter. Let thy berries still serve for memorials of our blood.” So saying she plunged the sword into her breast. Her parents ratified her wish, the gods also ratified it. The two bodies were buried in one sepulchre, and the tree ever after brought forth purple berries, as it does to this day.\n\nMoore, in the “Sylph’s Ball,” speaking of Davy’s Safety Lamp, is reminded of the wall that separated Thisbe and her lover:\n\n“O for that Lamp’s metallic gauze, That curtain of protecting wire, Which Davy delicately draws Around illicit, dangerous fire!\nThe wall he sets ’twixt Flame and Air, (Like that which barred young Thisbe’s bliss,) Through whose small holes this dangerous pair May see each other, but not kiss.”\n\nIn Mickle’s translation of the “Lusiad” occurs the following allusion to the story of Pyramus and Thisbe, and the metamorphosis of the mulberries. The poet is describing the Island of Love:\n\n“. . . here each gift Pomona’s hand bestows In cultured garden, free uncultured flows, The flavor sweeter and the hue more fair Than e’er was fostered by the hand of care. The cherry here in shining crimson glows, And stained with lovers’ blood, in pendent rows, The mulberries o’erload the bending boughs.”\n\nIf any of our young readers can be so hard-hearted as to enjoy a laugh at the expense of poor Pyramus and Thisbe, they may find an opportunity by turning to Shakspeare’s play of the “Midsummer Night’s Dream,” where it is most amusingly burlesqued.\n\n# CEPHALUS AND PROCRIS\n\nCephalus was a beautiful youth and fond of manly sports. He would rise before the dawn to pursue the chase. Aurora saw him when she first looked forth, fell in love with him, and stole him away. But Cephalus was just married to a charming wife whom he devotedly loved. Her name was Procris. She was a favorite of Diana, the goddess of hunting, who had given her a dog which could outrun every rival, and a javelin which would never fail of its mark; and Procris gave these presents to her husband. Cephalus was so happy in his wife that he resisted all the entreaties of Aurora, and she finally dismissed him in displeasure, saying, “Go, ungrateful mortal, keep your wife, whom, if I am not much mistaken, you will one day be very sorry you ever saw again.”\n\nCephalus returned, and was as happy as ever in his wife and his woodland sports. Now it happened some angry deity had sent a ravenous fox to annoy the country; and the hunters turned out in great strength to capture it. Their efforts were all in vain; no dog could run it down; and at last they came to Cephalus to borrow his famous dog, whose name was Lelaps. No sooner was the dog let loose than he darted off, quicker than their eye could follow him. If they had not seen his footprints in the sand they would have thought he flew. Cephalus and others stood on a hill and saw the race. The fox tried every art; he ran in a circle and turned on his track, the dog close upon him, with open jaws, snapping at his heels, but biting only the air. Cephalus was about to use his javelin, when suddenly he saw both dog and game stop instantly. The heavenly powers who had given both were not willing that either should conquer. In the very attitude of life and action they were turned into stone. So lifelike and natural did they look, you would have thought, as you looked at them, that one was going to bark, the other to leap forward.\n\nCephalus, though he had lost his dog, still continued to take delight in the chase. He would go out at early morning, ranging the woods and hills unaccompanied by any one, needing no help, for his javelin was a sure weapon in all cases. Fatigued with hunting, when the sun got high he would seek a shady nook where a cool stream flowed, and, stretched on the grass, with his garments thrown aside, would enjoy the breeze. Sometimes he would say aloud, “Come, sweet breeze, come and fan my breast, come and allay the heat that burns me.” Some one passing by one day heard him talking in this way to the air, and, foolishly believing that he was talking to some maiden, went and told the secret to Procris, Cephalus’s wife. Love is credulous. Procris, at the sudden shock, fainted away. Presently recovering, she said, “It cannot be true; I will not believe it unless I myself am a witness to it.” So she waited, with anxious heart, till the next morning, when Cephalus went to hunt as usual. Then she stole out after him, and concealed herself in the place where the informer directed her. Cephalus came as he was wont when tired with sport, and stretched himself on the green bank, saying, “Come, sweet breeze, come and fan me; you know how I love you! you make the groves and my solitary rambles delightful.” He was running on in this way when he heard, or thought he heard, a sound as of a sob in the bushes. Supposing it some wild animal, he threw his javelin at the spot. A cry from his beloved Procris told him that the weapon had too surely met its mark. He rushed to the place, and found her bleeding, and with sinking strength endeavoring to draw forth from the wound the javelin, her own gift. Cephalus raised her from the earth, strove to stanch the blood, and called her to revive and not to leave him miserable, to reproach himself with her death. She opened her feeble eyes, and forced herself to utter these few words: “I implore you, if you have ever loved me, if I have ever deserved kindness at your hands, my husband, grant me this last request; do not marry that odious Breeze!” This disclosed the whole mystery: but alas! what advantage to disclose it now! She died; but her face wore a calm expression, and she looked pityingly and forgivingly on her husband when he made her understand the truth.\n\nMoore, in his “Legendary Ballads,” has one on Cephalus and Procris, beginning thus:\n\n“A hunter once in a grove reclined, To shun the noon’s bright eye, And oft he wooed the wandering wind To cool his brow with its sigh. While mute lay even the wild bee’s hum, Nor breath could stir the aspen’s hair, His song was still, ‘Sweet Air, O come!’ While Echo answered, ‘Come, sweet Air!’”\n\n————\n\n# CHAPTER IV\n\nJUNO AND HER RIVALS, IO AND CALLISTO—DIANA AND ACTÆON—LATONA AND THE RUSTICS\nJUNO one day perceived it suddenly grow dark, and immediately suspected that her husband had raised a cloud to hide some of his doings that would not bear the light. She brushed away the cloud, and saw her husband on the banks of a glassy river, with a beautiful heifer standing near him. Juno suspected the heifer’s form concealed some fair nymph of mortal mould—as was, indeed the case; for it was Io, the daughter of the river god Inachus, whom Jupiter had been flirting with, and, when he became aware of the approach of his wife, had changed into that form.\n\nJuno joined her husband, and noticing the heifer praised its beauty, and asked whose it was, and of what herd. Jupiter, to stop questions, replied that it was a fresh creation from the earth. Juno asked to have it as a gift. What could Jupiter do? He was loath to give his mistress to his wife; yet how refuse so trifling a present as a simple heifer? He could not, without exciting suspicion; so he consented. The goddess was not yet relieved of her suspicions; so she delivered the heifer to Argus, to be strictly watched.\n\nNow Argus had a hundred eyes in his head, and never went to sleep with more than two at a time, so that he kept watch of Io constantly. He suffered her to feed through the day, and at night tied her up with a vile rope round her neck. She would have stretched out her arms to implore freedom of Argus, but she had no arms to stretch out, and her voice was a bellow that frightened even herself. She saw her father and her sisters, went near them, and suffered them to pat her back, and heard them admire her beauty. Her father reached her a tuft of grass, and she licked the outstretched hand. She longed to make herself known to him, and would have uttered her wish; but, alas! words were wanting. At length she bethought herself of writing, and inscribed her name—it was a short one—with her hoof on the sand. Inachus recognized it, and discovering that his daughter, whom he had long sought in vain, was hidden under this disguise, mourned over her, and, embracing her white neck, exclaimed, “Alas! my daughter, it would have been a less grief to have lost you altogether!” While he thus lamented, Argus, observing, came and drove her away, and took his seat on a high bank, from whence he could see all around in every direction.\n\nJupiter was troubled at beholding the sufferings of his mistress, and calling Mercury told him to go and despatch Argus. Mercury made haste, put his winged slippers on his feet, and cap on his head, took his sleep-producing wand, and leaped down from the heavenly towers to the earth. There he laid aside his wings, and kept only his wand, with which he presented himself as a shepherd driving his flock. As he strolled on he blew upon his pipes. These were what are called the Syrinx or Pandean pipes. Argus listened with delight, for he had never seen the instrument before. “Young man,” said he, “come and take a seat by me on this stone. There is no better place for your flocks to graze in than hereabouts, and here is a pleasant shade such as shepherds love.” Mercury sat down, talked, and told stories till it grew late, and played upon his pipes his most soothing strains, hoping to lull the watchful eyes to sleep, but all in vain; for Argus still contrived to keep some of his eyes open though he shut the rest.\n\nAmong other stories, Mercury told him how the instrument on which he played was invented. “There was a certain nymph, whose name was Syrinx, who was much beloved by the satyrs and spirits of the wood; but she would have none of them, but was a faithful worshipper of Diana, and followed the chase. You would have thought it was Diana herself, had you seen her in her hunting dress, only that her bow was of horn and Diana’s of silver. One day, as she was returning from the chase, Pan met her, told her just this, and added more of the same sort. She ran away, without stopping to hear his compliments, and he pursued till she came to the bank of the river, where he overtook her, and she had only time to call for help on her friends the water nymphs. They heard and consented. Pan threw his arms around what he supposed to be the form of the nymph, and found he embraced only a tuft of reeds! As he breathed a sigh, the air sounded through the reeds, and produced a plaintive melody. The god, charmed with the novelty and with the sweetness of the music, said, ‘Thus, then, at least, you shall be mine.’ And he took some of the reeds, and placing them together, of unequal lengths, side by side, made an instrument which he called Syrinx, in honor of the nymph.” Before Mercury had finished his story he saw Argus’s eyes all asleep. As his head nodded forward on his breast, Mercury with one stroke cut his neck through, and tumbled his head down the rocks. O hapless Argus! the light of your hundred eyes is quenched at once! Juno took them and put them as ornaments on the tail of her peacock, where they remain to this day.\n\nBut the vengeance of Juno was not yet satiated. She sent a gadfly to torment Io, who fled over the whole world from its pursuit. She swam through the Ionian sea, which derived its name from her, then roamed over the plains of Illyria, ascended Mount Hæmus, and crossed the Thracian strait, thence named the Bosphorus (cow-ford), rambled on through Scythia, and the country of the Cimmerians, and arrived at last on the banks of the Nile. At length Jupiter interceded for her, and upon his promising not to pay her any more attentions Juno consented to restore her to her form. It was curious to see her gradually recover her former self. The coarse hairs fell from her body, her horns shrank up, her eyes grew narrower, her mouth shorter; hands and fingers came instead of hoofs to her forefeet; in fine there was nothing left of the heifer, except her beauty. At first she was afraid to speak, for fear she should low, but gradually she recovered her confidence and was restored to her father and sisters.\n\nIn a poem dedicated to Leigh Hunt, by Keats, the following allusion to the story of Pan and Syrinx occurs:\n\n“So did he feel who pulled the bough aside, That we might look into a forest wide, . . . . . . . Telling us how fair trembling Syrinx fled Arcadian Pan, with such a fearful dread. Poor nymph—poor Pan—how he did weep to find Nought but a lovely sighing of the wind Along the reedy stream; a half-heard strain, Full of sweet desolation, balmy pain.”\n\nCallisto was another maiden who excited the jealousy of Juno, and the goddess changed her into a bear. “I will take away,” said she, “that beauty with which you have captivated my husband.” Down fell Callisto on her hands and knees; she tried to stretch out her arms in supplication—they were already beginning to be covered with black hair. Her hands grew rounded, became armed with crooked claws, and served for feet; her mouth, which Jove used to praise for its beauty, became a horrid pair of jaws; her voice, which if unchanged would have moved the heart to pity, became a growl, more fit to inspire terror. Yet her former disposition remained, and with continual groaning, she bemoaned her fate, and stood upright as well as she could, lifting up her paws to beg for mercy, and felt that Jove was unkind, though she could not tell him so. Ah, how often, afraid to stay in the woods all night alone, she wandered about the neighborhood of her former haunts; how often, frightened by the dogs, did she, so lately a huntress, fly in terror from the hunters! Often she fled from the wild beasts, forgetting that she was now a wild beast herself; and, bear as she was, was afraid of the bears.\n\nOne day a youth espied her as he was hunting. She saw him and recognized him as her own son, now grown a young man. She stopped and felt inclined to embrace him. As she was about to approach, he, alarmed, raised his hunting spear, and was on the point of transfixing her, when Jupiter, beholding, arrested the crime, and snatching away both of them, placed them in the heavens as the Great and Little Bear.\n\nJuno was in a rage to see her rival so set in honor, and hastened to ancient Tethys and Oceanus, the powers of ocean, and in answer to their inquiries thus told the cause of her coming: “Do you ask why I, the queen of the gods, have left the heavenly plains and sought your depths? Learn that I am supplanted in heaven—my place is given to another. You will hardly believe me; but look when night darkens the world, and you shall see the two of whom I have so much reason to complain exalted to the heavens, in that part where the circle is the smallest, in the neighborhood of the pole. Why should any one hereafter tremble at the thought of offending Juno, when such rewards are the consequence of my displeasure? See what I have been able to effect! I forbade her to wear the human form—she is placed among the stars! So do my punishments result—such is the extent of my power! Better that she should have resumed her former shape, as I permitted Io to do. Perhaps he means to marry her, and put me away! But you, my foster-parents, if you feel for me, and see with displeasure this unworthy treatment of me, show it, I beseech you, by forbidding this guilty couple from coming into your waters.” The powers of the ocean assented, and consequently the two constellations of the Great and Little Bear move round and round in heaven, but never sink, as the other stars do, beneath the ocean.\n\nMilton alludes to the fact that the constellation of the Bear never sets, when he says:\n\n“Let my lamp at midnight hour Be seen in some high lonely tower, Where I may oft outwatch the Bear,” etc.\n\nAnd Prometheus, in J. R. Lowell’s poem, says:\n\n“One after one the stars have risen and set, Sparkling upon the hoar frost of my chain; The Bear that prowled all night about the fold Of the North-star, hath shrunk into his den, Scared by the blithesome footsteps of the Dawn.”\n\nThe last star in the tail of the Little Bear is the Pole-star, called also the Cynosure. Milton says:\n\n“Straight mine eye hath caught new pleasures While the landscape round it measures. . . . . . . . Towers and battlements it sees Bosomed high in tufted trees, Where perhaps some beauty lies The Cynosure of neighboring eyes.”\n\nThe reference here is both to the Pole-star as the guide of mariners, and to the magnetic attraction of the North. He calls it also the “Star of Arcady,” because Callisto’s boy was named Arcas, and they lived in Arcadia. In “Comus,” the brother, benighted in the woods, says:\n\n“. . . Some gentle taper! Though a rush candle, from the wicker hole Of some clay habitation, visit us With thy long levelled rule of streaming light, And thou shalt be our star of Arcady, Or Tyrian Cynosure.”\n\nThus in two instances we have seen Juno’s severity to her rivals; now let us learn how a virgin goddess punished an invader of her privacy.\n\nIt was midday, and the sun stood equally distant from either goal, when young Actæon, son of King Cadmus, thus addressed the youths who with him were hunting the stag in the mountains:\n\n“Friends, our nets and our weapons are wet with the blood of our victims; we have had sport enough for one day, and to-morrow we can renew our labors. Now, while Phœbus parches the earth, let us put by our implements and indulge ourselves with rest.”\n\nThere was a valley thick enclosed with cypresses and pines, sacred to the huntress queen, Diana. In the extremity of the valley was a cave, not adorned with art, but nature had counterfeited art in its construction, for she had turned the arch of its roof with stones as delicately fitted as if by the hand of man. A fountain burst out from one side, whose open basin was bounded by a grassy rim. Here the goddess of the woods used to come when weary with hunting and lave her virgin limbs in the sparkling water.\n\nOne day, having repaired thither with her nymphs, she handed her javelin, her quiver, and her bow to one, her robe to another, while a third unbound the sandals from her feet. Then Crocale, the most skilful of them, arranged her hair, and Nephele, Hyale, and the rest drew water in capacious urns. While the goddess was thus employed in the labors of the toilet, behold Actæon, having quitted his companions, and rambling without any especial object, came to the place, led thither by his destiny. As he presented himself at the entrance of the cave, the nymphs, seeing a man, screamed and rushed towards the goddess to hide her with their bodies. But she was taller than the rest and overtopped them all by a head. Such a color as tinges the clouds at sunset or at dawn came over the countenance of Diana thus taken by surprise. Surrounded as she was by her nymphs, she yet turned half away, and sought with a sudden impulse for her arrows. As they were not at hand, she dashed the water into the face of the intruder, adding these words: “Now go and tell, if you can, that you have seen Diana unapparelled.” Immediately a pair of branching stag’s horns grew out of his head, his neck gained in length, his ears grew sharp-pointed, his hands became feet, his arms long legs, his body was covered with a hairy spotted hide. Fear took the place of his former boldness, and the hero fled. He could not but admire his own speed; but when he saw his horns in the water, “Ah, wretched me!” he would have said, but no sound followed the effort. He groaned, and tears flowed down the face which had taken the place of his own. Yet his consciousness remained. What shall he do?—go home to seek the palace, or lie hid in the woods? The latter he was afraid, the former he was ashamed, to do. While he hesitated the dogs saw him. First Melampus, a Spartan dog, gave the signal with his bark, then Pamphagus, Dorceus, Lelaps, Theron, Nape, Tigris, and all the rest, rushed after him swifter than the wind. Over rocks and cliffs, through mountain gorges that seemed impracticable, he fled and they followed. Where he had often chased the stag and cheered on his pack, his pack now chased him, cheered on by his huntsmen. He longed to cry out, “I am Actæon; recognize your master!” but the words came not at his will. The air resounded with the bark of the dogs. Presently one fastened on his back, another seized his shoulder. While they held their master, the rest of the pack came up and buried their teeth in his flesh. He groaned,—not in a human voice, yet certainly not in a stag’s,—and falling on his knees, raised his eyes, and would have raised his arms in supplication, if he had had them. His friends and fellow-huntsmen cheered on the dogs, and looked everywhere for Actæon, calling on him to join the sport. At the sound of his name he turned his head, and heard them regret that he should be away. He earnestly wished he was. He would have been well pleased to see the exploits of his dogs, but to feel them was too much. They were all around him, rending and tearing; and it was not till they had torn his life out that the anger of Diana was satisfied.\n\nIn Shelley’s poem “Adonais” is the following allusion to the story of Actæon:\n\n“’Midst others of less note came one frail form, A phantom among men: companionless As the last cloud of an expiring storm, Whose thunder is its knell; he, as I guess, Had gazed on Nature’s naked loveliness, Actæon-like, and now he fled astray With feeble steps o’er the world’s wilderness; And his own Thoughts, along that rugged way, Pursued like raging hounds their father and their prey.” Stanza 31.\n\nThe allusion is probably to Shelley himself.\n\n# LATONA AND THE RUSTICS\n\nSome thought the goddess in this instance more severe than was just, while others praised her conduct as strictly consistent with her virgin dignity. As usual, the recent event brought older ones to mind, and one of the bystanders told this story: “Some countrymen of Lycia once insulted the goddess Latona, but not with impunity. When I was young, my father, who had grown too old for active labors, sent me to Lycia to drive thence some choice oxen, and there I saw the very pond and marsh where the wonder happened. Near by stood an ancient altar, black with the smoke of sacrifice and almost buried among the reeds. I inquired whose altar it might be, whether of Faunus or the Naiads, or some god of the neighboring mountain, and one of the country people replied, ‘No mountain or river god possesses this altar, but she whom royal Juno in her jealousy drove from land to land, denying her any spot of earth whereon to rear her twins.’ Bearing in her arms the infant deities, Latona reached this land, weary with her burden and parched with thirst. By chance she espied on the bottom of the valley this pond of clear water, where the country people were at work gathering willows and osiers. The goddess approached, and kneeling on the bank would have slaked her thirst in the cool stream, but the rustics forbade her. ‘Why do you refuse me water?’ said she; ‘water is free to all. Nature allows no one to claim as property the sunshine, the air, or the water. I come to take my share of the common blessing. Yet I ask it of you as a favor. I have no intention of washing my limbs in it, weary though they be, but only to quench my thirst. My mouth is so dry that I can hardly speak. A draught of water would be nectar to me; it would revive me, and I would own myself indebted to you for life itself. Let these infants move your pity, who stretch out their little arms as if to plead for me;’ and the children, as it happened, were stretching out their arms.\n\n“Who would not have been moved with these gentle words of the goddess? But these clowns persisted in their rudeness; they even added jeers and threats of violence if she did not leave the place. Nor was this all. They waded into the pond and stirred up the mud with their feet, so as to make the water unfit to drink. Latona was so angry that she ceased to mind her thirst. She no longer supplicated the clowns, but lifting her hands to heaven exclaimed, ‘May they never quit that pool, but pass their lives there!’ And it came to pass accordingly. They now live in the water, sometimes totally submerged, then raising their heads above the surface or swimming upon it. Sometimes they come out upon the bank, but soon leap back again into the water. They still use their base voices in railing, and though they have the water all to themselves, are not ashamed to croak in the midst of it. Their voices are harsh, their throats bloated, their mouths have become stretched by constant railing, their necks have shrunk up and disappeared, and their heads are joined to their bodies. Their backs are green, their disproportioned bellies white, and in short they are now frogs, and dwell in the slimy pool.”\n\nThis story explains the allusion in one of Milton’s sonnets, “On the detraction which followed upon his writing certain treatises.”\n\n“I did but prompt the age to quit their clogs By the known laws of ancient liberty, When straight a barbarous noise environs me Of owls and cuckoos, asses, apes and dogs. As when those hinds that were transformed to frogs Railed at Latona’s twin-born progeny, Which after held the sun and moon in fee.”\n\nThe persecution which Latona experienced from Juno is alluded to in the story. The tradition was that the future mother of Apollo and Diana, flying from the wrath of Juno, besought all the islands of the Ægean to afford her a place of rest, but all feared too much the potent queen of heaven to assist her rival. Delos alone consented to become the birthplace of the future deities. Delos was then a floating island; but when Latona arrived there, Jupiter fastened it with adamantine chains to the bottom of the sea, that it might be a secure resting-place for his beloved. Byron alludes to Delos in his “Don Juan”:\n\n“The isles of Greece! the isles of Greece! Where burning Sappho loved and sung, Where grew the arts of war and peace, Where Delos rose and Phœbus sprung!”\n————\n\nPHAËTON was the son of Apollo and the nymph Clymene. One day a schoolfellow laughed at the idea of his being the son of the god, and Phaëton went in rage and shame and reported it to his mother. “If,” said he, “I am indeed of heavenly birth, give me, mother, some proof of it, and establish my claim to the honor.” Clymene stretched forth her hands towards the skies, and said, “I call to witness the Sun which looks down upon us, that I have told you the truth. If I speak falsely, let this be the last time I behold his light. But it needs not much labor to go and inquire for yourself; the land whence the Sun rises lies next to ours. Go and demand of him whether he will own you as a son.” Phaëton heard with delight. He travelled to India, which lies directly in the regions of sunrise; and, full of hope and pride, approached the goal whence his parent begins his course.\n\nThe palace of the Sun stood reared aloft on columns, glittering with gold and precious stones, while polished ivory formed the ceilings, and silver the doors. The workmanship surpassed the material;[7] for upon the walls Vulcan had represented earth, sea, and skies, with their inhabitants. In the sea were the nymphs, some sporting in the waves, some riding on the backs of fishes, while others sat upon the rocks and dried their sea-green hair. Their faces were not all alike, nor yet unlike,—but such as sisters’ ought to be.[8] The earth had its towns and forests and rivers and rustic divinities. Over all was carved the likeness of the glorious heaven; and on the silver doors the twelve signs of the zodiac, six on each side.\n\nClymene’s son advanced up the steep ascent, and entered the halls of his disputed father. He approached the paternal presence, but stopped at a distance, for the light was more than he could bear. Phœbus, arrayed in a purple vesture, sat on a throne, which glittered as with diamonds. On his right hand and his left stood the Day, the Month, and the Year, and, at regular intervals, the Hours. Spring stood with her head crowned with flowers, and Summer, with garment cast aside, and a garland formed of spears of ripened grain, and Autumn, with his feet stained with grape-juice, and icy Winter, with his hair stiffened with hoar frost. Surrounded by these attendants, the Sun, with the eye that sees everything, beheld the youth dazzled with the novelty and splendor of the scene, and inquired the purpose of his errand. The youth replied, “O light of the boundless world, Phœbus, my father,—if you permit me to use that name,—give me some proof, I beseech you, by which I may be known as yours.” He ceased; and his father, laying aside the beams that shone all around his head, bade him approach, and embracing him, said, “My son, you deserve not to be disowned, and I confirm what your mother has told you. To put an end to your doubts, ask what you will, the gift shall be yours. I call to witness that dreadful lake, which I never saw, but which we gods swear by in our most solemn engagements.” Phaëton immediately asked to be permitted for one day to drive the chariot of the sun. The father repented of his promise; thrice and four times he shook his radiant head in warning. “I have spoken rashly,” said he; “this only request I would fain deny. I beg you to withdraw it. It is not a safe boon, nor one, my Phaëton, suited to your youth and strength. Your lot is mortal, and you ask what is beyond a mortal’s power. In your ignorance you aspire to do that which not even the gods themselves may do. None but myself may drive the flaming car of day. Not even Jupiter, whose terrible right arm hurls the thunderbolts. The first part of the way is steep, and such as the horses when fresh in the morning can hardly climb; the middle is high up in the heavens, whence I myself can scarcely, without alarm, look down and behold the earth and sea stretched beneath me. The last part of the road descends rapidly, and requires most careful driving. Tethys, who is waiting to receive me, often trembles for me lest I should fall headlong. Add to all this, the heaven is all the time turning round and carrying the stars with it. I have to be perpetually on my guard lest that movement, which sweeps everything else along, should hurry me also away. Suppose I should lend you the chariot, what would you do? Could you keep your course while the sphere was revolving under you? Perhaps you think that there are forests and cities, the abodes of gods, and palaces and temples on the way. On the contrary, the road is through the midst of frightful monsters. You pass by the horns of the Bull, in front of the Archer, and near the Lion’s jaws, and where the Scorpion stretches its arms in one direction and the Crab in another. Nor will you find it easy to guide those horses, with their breasts full of fire that they breathe forth from their mouths and nostrils. I can scarcely govern them myself, when they are unruly and resist the reins. Beware, my son, lest I be the donor of a fatal gift; recall your request while yet you may. Do you ask me for a proof that you are sprung from my blood? I give you a proof in my fears for you. Look at my face—I would that you could look into my breast, you would there see all a father’s anxiety. Finally,” he continued, “look round the world and choose whatever you will of what earth or sea contains most precious—ask it and fear no refusal. This only I pray you not to urge. It is not honor, but destruction you seek. Why do you hang round my neck and still entreat me? You shall have it if you persist,—the oath is sworn and must be kept,—but I beg you to choose more wisely.”\n\nHe ended; but the youth rejected all admonition and held to his demand. So, having resisted as long as he could, Phœbus at last led the way to where stood the lofty chariot.\n\nIt was of gold, the gift of Vulcan; the axle was of gold, the pole and wheels of gold, the spokes of silver. Along the seat were rows of chrysolites and diamonds which reflected all around the brightness of the sun. While the daring youth gazed in admiration, the early Dawn threw open the purple doors of the east, and showed the pathway strewn with roses. The stars withdrew, marshalled by the Day-star, which last of all retired also. The father, when he saw the earth beginning to glow, and the Moon preparing to retire, ordered the Hours to harness up the horses. They obeyed, and led forth from the lofty stalls the steeds full fed with ambrosia, and attached the reins. Then the father bathed the face of his son with a powerful unguent, and made him capable of enduring the brightness of the flame. He set the rays on his head, and, with a foreboding sigh, said, “If, my son, you will in this at least heed my advice, spare the whip and hold tight the reins. They go fast enough of their own accord; the labor is to hold them in. You are not to take the straight road directly between the five circles, but turn off to the left. Keep within the limit of the middle zone, and avoid the northern and the southern alike. You will see the marks of the wheels, and they will serve to guide you. And, that the skies and the earth may each receive their due share of heat, go not too high, or you will burn the heavenly dwellings, nor too low, or you will set the earth on fire; the middle course is safest and best.[9] And now I leave you to your chance, which I hope will plan better for you than you have done for yourself. Night is passing out of the western gates and we can delay no longer. Take the reins; but if at last your heart fails you, and you will benefit by my advice, stay where you are in safety, and suffer me to light and warm the earth.” The agile youth sprang into the chariot, stood erect, and grasped the reins with delight, pouring out thanks to his reluctant parent.\n\nMeanwhile the horses fill the air with their snortings and fiery breath, and stamp the ground impatient. Now the bars are let down, and the boundless plain of the universe lies open before them. They dart forward and cleave the opposing clouds, and outrun the morning breezes which started from the same eastern goal. The steeds soon perceived that the load they drew was lighter than usual; and as a ship without ballast is tossed hither and thither on the sea, so the chariot, without its accustomed weight, was dashed about as if empty. They rush headlong and leave the travelled road. He is alarmed, and knows not how to guide them; nor, if he knew, has he the power. Then, for the first time, the Great and Little Bear were scorched with heat, and would fain, if it were possible, have plunged into the water; and the Serpent which lies coiled up round the north pole, torpid and harmless, grew warm, and with warmth felt its rage revive. Boötes, they say, fled away, though encumbered with his plough, and all unused to rapid motion.\n\nWhen hapless Phaëton looked down upon the earth, now spreading in vast extent beneath him, he grew pale and his knees shook with terror. In spite of the glare all around him, the sight of his eyes grew dim. He wished he had never touched his father’s horses, never learned his parentage, never prevailed in his request. He is borne along like a vessel that flies before a tempest, when the pilot can do no more and betakes himself to his prayers. What shall he do? Much of the heavenly road is left behind, but more remains before. He turns his eyes from one direction to the other; now to the goal whence he began his course, now to the realms of sunset which he is not destined to reach. He loses his self-command, and knows not what to do,—whether to draw tight the reins or throw them loose; he forgets the names of the horses. He sees with terror the monstrous forms scattered over the surface of heaven. Here the Scorpion extended his two great arms, with his tail and crooked claws stretching over two signs of the zodiac. When the boy beheld him, reeking with poison and menacing with his fangs, his courage failed, and the reins fell from his hands. The horses, when they felt them loose on their backs, dashed headlong, and unrestrained went off into unknown regions of the sky, in among the stars, hurling the chariot over pathless places, now up in high heaven, now down almost to the earth. The moon saw with astonishment her brother’s chariot running beneath her own. The clouds begin to smoke, and the mountain tops take fire; the fields are parched with heat, the plants wither, the trees with their leafy branches burn, the harvest is ablaze! But these are small things. Great cities perished, with their walls and towers; whole nations with their people were consumed to ashes! The forest-clad mountains burned, Athos and Taurus and Tmolus and Œte; Ida, once celebrated for fountains, but now all dry; the Muses’ mountain Helicon, and Hæmus; Ætna, with fires within and without, and Parnassus, with his two peaks, and Rhodope, forced at last to part with his snowy crown. Her cold climate was no protection to Scythia, Caucasus burned, and Ossa and Pindus, and, greater than both, Olympus; the Alps high in air, and the Apennines crowned with clouds.\n\nThen Phaëton beheld the world on fire, and felt the heat intolerable. The air he breathed was like the air of a furnace and full of burning ashes, and the smoke was of a pitchy darkness. He dashed forward he knew not whither. Then, it is believed, the people of Æthiopia became black by the blood being forced so suddenly to the surface, and the Libyan desert was dried up to the condition in which it remains to this day. The Nymphs of the fountains, with dishevelled hair, mourned their waters, nor were the rivers safe beneath their banks: Tanais smoked, and Caicus, Xanthus, and Meander; Babylonian Euphrates and Ganges, Tagus with golden sands, and Caÿster where the swans resort. Nile fled away and hid his head in the desert, and there it still remains concealed. Where he used to discharge his waters through seven mouths into the sea, there seven dry channels alone remained. The earth cracked open, and through the chinks light broke into Tartarus, and frightened the king of shadows and his queen. The sea shrank up. Where before was water, it became a dry plain; and the mountains that lie beneath the waves lifted up their heads and became islands. The fishes sought the lowest depths, and the dolphins no longer ventured as usual to sport on the surface. Even Nereus, and his wife Doris, with the Nereids, their daughters, sought the deepest caves for refuge. Thrice Neptune essayed to raise his head above the surface, and thrice was driven back by the heat. Earth, surrounded as she was by waters, yet with head and shoulders bare, screening her face with her hand, looked up to heaven, and with a husky voice called on Jupiter:\n\n“O ruler of the gods, if I have deserved this treatment, and it is your will that I perish with fire, why withhold your thunderbolts? Let me at least fall by your hand. Is this the reward of my fertility, of my obedient service? Is it for this that I have supplied herbage for cattle, and fruits for men, and frankincense for your altars? But if I am unworthy of regard, what has my brother Ocean done to deserve such a fate? If neither of us can excite your pity, think, I pray you, of your own heaven, and behold how both the poles are smoking which sustain your palace, which must fall if they be destroyed. Atlas faints, and scarce holds up his burden. If sea, earth, and heaven perish, we fall into ancient Chaos. Save what yet remains to us from the devouring flame. O, take thought for our deliverance in this awful moment!”\n\nThus spoke Earth, and overcome with heat and thirst, could say no more. Then Jupiter omnipotent, calling to witness all the gods, including him who had lent the chariot, and showing them that all was lost unless speedy remedy were applied, mounted the lofty tower from whence he diffuses clouds over the earth, and hurls the forked lightnings. But at that time not a cloud was to be found to interpose for a screen to earth, nor was a shower remaining unexhausted. He thundered, and brandishing a lightning bolt in his right hand launched it against the charioteer, and struck him at the same moment from his seat and from existence! Phaëton, with his hair on fire, fell headlong, like a shooting star which marks the heavens with its brightness as it falls, and Eridanus, the great river, received him and cooled his burning frame. The Italian Naiads reared a tomb for him, and inscribed these words upon the stone:\n\n“Driver of Phœbus’ chariot. Phaëton, Struck by Jove’s thunder, rests beneath this stone. He could not rule his father’s car of fire, Yet was it much so nobly to aspire.”[10]\n\nHis sisters, the Heliades, as they lamented his fate, were turned into poplar trees, on the banks of the river, and their tears, which continued to flow, became amber as they dropped into the stream.\n\nMilman, in his poem of “Samor,” makes the following allusion to Phaëton’s story:\n\n“As when the palsied universe aghast Lay . . . mute and still, When drove, so poets sing, the Sun-born youth Devious through Heaven’s affrighted signs his sire’s Ill-granted chariot. Him the Thunderer hurled From th’ empyrean headlong to the gulf Of the half-parched Eridanus, where weep Even now the sister trees their amber tears O’er Phaëton untimely dead.”\n\nIn the beautiful lines of Walter Savage Landor, descriptive of the Sea-shell, there is an allusion to the Sun’s palace and chariot. The water-nymph says:\n\n“. . . I have sinuous shells of pearly hue Within, and things that lustre have imbibed In the sun’s palace porch, where when unyoked His chariot wheel stands midway on the wave. Shake one and it awakens; then apply Its polished lip to your attentive ear, And it remembers its august abodes, And murmurs as the ocean murmurs there.” —Gebir, Book I.\n\n————\n\n# MIDAS—BAUCIS AND PHILEMON\n\nBACCHUS, on a certain occasion, found his old school-master and foster-father, Silenus, missing. The old man had been drinking, and in that state wandered away, and was found by some peasants, who carried him to their king, Midas. Midas recognized him, and treated him hospitably, entertaining him for ten days and nights with an unceasing round of jollity. On the eleventh day he brought Silenus back, and restored him in safety to his pupil. Whereupon Bacchus offered Midas his choice of a reward, whatever he might wish. He asked that whatever he might touch should be changed into gold. Bacchus consented, though sorry that he had not made a better choice. Midas went his way, rejoicing in his new-acquired power, which he hastened to put to the test. He could scarce believe his eyes when he found a twig of an oak, which he plucked from the branch, become gold in his hand. He took up a stone; it changed to gold. He touched a sod; it did the same. He took an apple from the tree; you would have thought he had robbed the garden of the Hesperides. His joy knew no bounds, and as soon as he got home, he ordered the servants to set a splendid repast on the table. Then he found to his dismay that whether he touched bread, it hardened in his hand; or put a morsel to his lips, it defied his teeth. He took a glass of wine, but it flowed down his throat like melted gold.\n\nIn consternation at the unprecedented affliction, he strove to divest himself of his power; he hated the gift he had lately coveted. But all in vain; starvation seemed to await him. He raised his arms, all shining with gold, in prayer to Bacchus, begging to be delivered from his glittering destruction. Bacchus, merciful deity, heard and consented. “Go,” said he, “to the River Pactolus, trace the stream to its fountain-head, there plunge your head and body in, and wash away your fault and its punishment.” He did so, and scarce had he touched the waters before the gold-creating power passed into them, and the river-sands became changed into gold, as they remain to this day.\n\nThenceforth Midas, hating wealth and splendor, dwelt in the country, and became a worshipper of Pan, the god of the fields. On a certain occasion Pan had the temerity to compare his music with that of Apollo, and to challenge the god of the lyre to a trial of skill. The challenge was accepted, and Tmolus, the mountain god, was chosen umpire. The senior took his seat, and cleared away the trees from his ears to listen. At a given signal Pan blew on his pipes, and with his rustic melody gave great satisfaction to himself and his faithful follower Midas, who happened to be present. Then Tmolus turned his head toward the Sun-god, and all his trees turned with him. Apollo rose, his brow wreathed with Parnassian laurel, while his robe of Tyrian purple swept the ground. In his left hand he held the lyre, and with his right hand struck the strings. Ravished with the harmony, Tmolus at once awarded the victory to the god of the lyre, and all but Midas acquiesced in the judgment. He dissented, and questioned the justice of the award. Apollo would not suffer such a depraved pair of ears any longer to wear the human form, but caused them to increase in length, grow hairy, within and without, and movable on their roots; in short, to be on the perfect pattern of those of an ass.\n\nMortified enough was King Midas at this mishap; but he consoled himself with the thought that it was possible to hide his misfortune, which he attempted to do by means of an ample turban or head-dress. But his hairdresser of course knew the secret. He was charged not to mention it, and threatened with dire punishment if he presumed to disobey. But he found it too much for his discretion to keep such a secret; so he went out into the meadow, dug a hole in the ground, and stooping down, whispered the story, and covered it up. Before long a thick bed of reeds sprang up in the meadow, and as soon as it had gained its growth, began whispering the story, and has continued to do so, from that day to this, every time a breeze passes over the place.\n\nThe story of King Midas has been told by others with some variations. Dryden, in the “Wife of Bath’s Tale,” makes Midas’s queen the betrayer of the secret:\n\n“This Midas knew, and durst communicate To none but to his wife his ears of state.”\n\nMidas was king of Phrygia. He was the son of Gordius, a poor countryman, who was taken by the people and made king, in obedience to the command of the oracle, which had said that their future king should come in a wagon. While the people were deliberating, Gordius with his wife and son came driving his wagon into the public square.\n\nGordius, being made king, dedicated his wagon to the deity of the oracle, and tied it up in its place with a fast knot. This was the celebrated Gordian knot, which, in after times it was said, whoever should untie should become lord of all Asia. Many tried to untie it, but none succeeded, till Alexander the Great, in his career of conquest, came to Phrygia. He tried his skill with as ill success as others, till growing impatient he drew his sword and cut the knot. When he afterwards succeeded in subjecting all Asia to his sway, people began to think that he had complied with the terms of the oracle according to its true meaning.\n\n# BAUCIS AND PHILEMON\n\nOn a certain hill in Phrygia stands a linden tree and an oak, enclosed by a low wall. Not far from the spot is a marsh, formerly good habitable land, but now indented with pools, the resort of fen-birds and cormorants. Once on a time Jupiter, in human shape, visited this country, and with him his son Mercury (he of the caduceus), without his wings. They presented themselves, as weary travellers, at many a door, seeking rest and shelter, but found all closed, for it was late, and the inhospitable inhabitants would not rouse themselves to open for their reception. At last a humble mansion received them, a small thatched cottage, where Baucis, a pious old dame, and her husband Philemon, united when young, had grown old together. Not ashamed of their poverty, they made it endurable by moderate desires and kind dispositions. One need not look there for master or for servant; they two were the whole household, master and servant alike. When the two heavenly guests crossed the humble threshold, and bowed their heads to pass under the low door, the old man placed a seat, on which Baucis, bustling and attentive, spread a cloth, and begged them to sit down. Then she raked out the coals from the ashes, and kindled up a fire, fed it with leaves and dry bark, and with her scanty breath blew it into a flame. She brought out of a corner split sticks and dry branches, broke them up, and placed them under the small kettle. Her husband collected some pot-herbs in the garden, and she shred them from the stalks, and prepared them for the pot. He reached down with a forked stick a flitch of bacon hanging in the chimney, cut a small piece, and put it in the pot to boil with the herbs, setting away the rest for another time. A beechen bowl was filled with warm water, that their guests might wash. While all was doing, they beguiled the time with conversation.\n\nOn the bench designed for the guests was laid a cushion stuffed with sea-weed; and a cloth, only produced on great occasions, but ancient and coarse enough, was spread over that. The old lady, with her apron on, with trembling hand set the table. One leg was shorter than the rest, but a piece of slate put under restored the level. When fixed, she rubbed the table down with some sweet-smelling herbs. Upon it she set some of chaste Minerva’s olives, some cornel berries preserved in vinegar, and added radishes and cheese, with eggs lightly cooked in the ashes. All were served in earthen dishes, and an earthenware pitcher, with wooden cups, stood beside them. When all was ready, the stew, smoking hot, was set on the table. Some wine, not of the oldest, was added; and for dessert, apples and wild honey; and over and above all, friendly faces, and simple but hearty welcome.\n\nNow while the repast proceeded, the old folks were astonished to see that the wine, as fast as it was poured out, renewed itself in the pitcher, of its own accord. Struck with terror, Baucis and Philemon recognized their heavenly guests, fell on their knees, and with clasped hands implored forgiveness for their poor entertainment. There was an old goose, which they kept as the guardian of their humble cottage; and they bethought them to make this a sacrifice in honor of their guests. But the goose, too nimble, with the aid of feet and wings, for the old folks, eluded their pursuit, and at last took shelter between the gods themselves. They forbade it to be slain; and spoke in these words: “We are gods. This inhospitable village shall pay the penalty of its impiety; you alone shall go free from the chastisement. Quit your house, and come with us to the top of yonder hill.” They hastened to obey, and, staff in hand, labored up the steep ascent. They had reached to within an arrow’s flight of the top, when turning their eyes below, they beheld all the country sunk in a lake, only their own house left standing. While they gazed with wonder at the sight, and lamented the fate of their neighbors, that old house of theirs was changed into a temple. Columns took the place of the corner posts, the thatch grew yellow and appeared a gilded roof, the floors became marble, the doors were enriched with carving and ornaments of gold. Then spoke Jupiter in benignant accents: “Excellent old man, and woman worthy of such a husband, speak, tell us your wishes; what favor have you to ask of us?” Philemon took counsel with Baucis a few moments; then declared to the gods their united wish. “We ask to be priests and guardians of this your temple; and since here we have passed our lives in love and concord, we wish that one and the same hour may take us both from life, that I may not live to see her grave, nor be laid in my own by her.” Their prayer was granted. They were the keepers of the temple as long as they lived. When grown very old, as they stood one day before the steps of the sacred edifice, and were telling the story of the place, Baucis saw Philemon begin to put forth leaves, and old Philemon saw Baucis changing in like manner. And now a leafy crown had grown over their heads, while exchanging parting words, as long as they could speak. “Farewell, dear spouse,” they said, together, and at the same moment the bark closed over their mouths. The Tyanean shepherd still shows the two trees, standing side by side, made out of the two good old people.\n\nThe story of Baucis and Philemon has been imitated by Swift, in a burlesque style, the actors in the change being two wandering saints, and the house being changed into a church, of which Philemon is made the parson. The following may serve as a specimen:\n\n“They scarce had spoke, when, fair and soft, The roof began to mount aloft; Aloft rose every beam and rafter; The heavy wall climbed slowly after. The chimney widened and grew higher, Became a steeple with a spire. The kettle to the top was hoist, And there stood fastened to a joist, But with the upside down, to show Its inclination for below; In vain, for a superior force, Applied at bottom, stops its course; Doomed ever in suspense to dwell, ’Tis now no kettle, but a bell. A wooden jack, which had almost Lost by disuse the art to roast, A sudden alteration feels. Increased by new intestine wheels; And, what exalts the wonder more, The number made the motion slower; The flier, though ’t had leaden feet, Turned round so quick you scarce could see ’t; But slackened by some secret power, Now hardly moves an inch an hour. The jack and chimney, near allied, Had never left each other’s side: The chimney to a steeple grown, The jack would not be left alone; But up against the steeple reared, Became a clock, and still adhered; And still its love to household cares By a shrill voice at noon declares, Warning the cook-maid not to burn That roast meat which it cannot turn; The groaning chair began to crawl, Like a huge snail, along the wall; There stuck aloft in public view, And with small change, a pulpit grew. A bedstead of the antique mode, Compact of timber many a load, Such as our ancestors did use, Was metamorphosed into pews, Which still their ancient nature keep By lodging folks disposed to sleep.”\n\n————\n\n# PROSERPINE—GLAUCUS AND SCYLLA\n\nWHEN Jupiter and his brothers had defeated the Titans and banished them to Tartarus, a new enemy rose up against the gods. They were the giants Typhon, Briareus, Enceladus, and others. Some of them had a hundred arms, others breathed out fire. They were finally subdued and buried alive under Mount Ætna, where they still sometimes struggle to get loose, and shake the whole island with earthquakes. Their breath comes up through the mountain, and is what men call the eruption of the volcano.\n\nThe fall of these monsters shook the earth, so that Pluto was alarmed, and feared that his kingdom would be laid open to the light of day. Under this apprehension, he mounted his chariot, drawn by black horses, and took a circuit of inspection to satisfy himself of the extent of the damage. While he was thus engaged, Venus, who was sitting on Mount Eryx playing with her boy Cupid, espied him, and said, “My son, take your darts with which you conquer all, even Jove himself, and send one into the breast of yonder dark monarch, who rules the realm of Tartarus. Why should he alone escape? Seize the opportunity to extend your empire and mine. Do you not see that even in heaven some despise our power? Minerva the wise, and Diana the huntress, defy us; and there is that daughter of Ceres, who threatens to follow their example. Now do you, if you have any regard for your own interest or mine, join these two in one.” The boy unbound his quiver, and selected his sharpest and truest arrow; then straining the bow against his knee, he attached the string, and, having made ready, shot the arrow with its barbed point right into the heart of Pluto.\n\nIn the vale of Enna there is a lake embowered in woods, which screen it from the fervid rays of the sun, while the moist ground is covered with flowers, and Spring reigns perpetual. Here Proserpine was playing with her companions, gathering lilies and violets, and filling her basket and her apron with them, when Pluto saw her, loved her, and carried her off. She screamed for help to her mother and companions; and when in her fright she dropped the corners of her apron and let the flowers fall, childlike she felt the loss of them as an addition to her grief. The ravisher urged on his steeds, calling them each by name, and throwing loose over their heads and necks his iron-colored reins. When he reached the River Cyane, and it opposed his passage, he struck the river-bank with his trident, and the earth opened and gave him a passage to Tartarus.\n\nCeres sought her daughter all the world over. Bright-haired Aurora, when she came forth in the morning, and Hesperus when he led out the stars in the evening, found her still busy in the search. But it was all unavailing. At length, weary and sad, she sat down upon a stone, and continued sitting nine days and nights, in the open air, under the sunlight and moonlight and falling showers. It was where now stands the city of Eleusis, then the home of an old man named Celeus. He was out in the field, gathering acorns and blackberries, and sticks for his fire. His little girl was driving home their two goats, and as she passed the goddess, who appeared in the guise of an old woman, she said to her, “Mother,”—and the name was sweet to the ears of Ceres,—“why do you sit here alone upon the rocks?” The old man also stopped, though his load was heavy, and begged her to come into his cottage, such as it was. She declined, and he urged her. “Go in peace,” she replied, “and be happy in your daughter; I have lost mine.” As she spoke, tears—or something like tears, for the gods never weep—fell down her cheeks upon her bosom. The compassionate old man and his child wept with her. Then said he, “Come with us, and despise not our humble roof; so may your daughter be restored to you in safety.” “Lead on,” said she, “I cannot resist that appeal!” So she rose from the stone and went with them. As they walked he told her that his only son, a little boy, lay very sick, feverish, and sleepless. She stooped and gathered some poppies. As they entered the cottage, they found all in great distress, for the boy seemed past hope of recovery. Metanina, his mother, received her kindly, and the goddess stooped and kissed the lips of the sick child. Instantly the paleness left his face, and healthy vigor returned to his body. The whole family were delighted—that is, the father, mother, and little girl, for they were all; they had no servants. They spread the table, and put upon it curds and cream, apples, and honey in the comb. While they ate, Ceres mingled poppy juice in the milk of the boy. When night came and all was still, she arose, and taking the sleeping boy, moulded his limbs with her hands, and uttered over him three times a solemn charm, then went and laid him in the ashes. His mother, who had been watching what her guest was doing, sprang forward with a cry and snatched the child from the fire. Then Ceres assumed her own form, and a divine splendor shone all around. While they were overcome with astonishment, she said, “Mother, you have been cruel in your fondness to your son. I would have made him immortal, but you have frustrated my attempt. Nevertheless, he shall be great and useful. He shall teach men the use of the plough, and the rewards which labor can win from the cultivated soil.” So saying, she wrapped a cloud about her, and mounting her chariot rode away.\n\nCeres continued her search for her daughter, passing from land to land, and across seas and rivers, till at length she returned to Sicily, whence she at first set out, and stood by the banks of the River Cyane, where Pluto made himself a passage with his prize to his own dominions. The river nymph would have told the goddess all she had witnessed, but dared not, for fear of Pluto; so she only ventured to take up the girdle which Proserpine had dropped in her flight, and waft it to the feet of the mother. Ceres, seeing this, was no longer in doubt of her loss, but she did not yet know the cause, and laid the blame on the innocent land. “Ungrateful soil,” said she, “which I have endowed with fertility and clothed with herbage and nourishing grain, no more shall you enjoy my favors.” Then the cattle died, the plough broke in the furrow, the seed failed to come up; there was too much sun, there was too much rain; the birds stole the seeds—thistles and brambles were the only growth. Seeing this, the fountain Arethusa interceded for the land. “Goddess,” said she, “blame not the land; it opened unwillingly to yield a passage to your daughter. I can tell you of her fate, for I have seen her. This is not my native country; I came hither from Elis. I was a woodland nymph, and delighted in the chase. They praised my beauty, but I cared nothing for it, and rather boasted of my hunting exploits. One day I was returning from the wood, heated with exercise, when I came to a stream silently flowing, so clear that you might count the pebbles on the bottom. The willows shaded it, and the grassy bank sloped down to the water’s edge. I approached, I touched the water with my foot. I stepped in knee-deep, and not content with that, I laid my garments on the willows and went in. While I sported in the water, I heard an indistinct murmur coming up as out of the depths of the stream; and made haste to escape to the nearest bank. The voice said, ‘Why do you fly, Arethusa? I am Alpheus, the god of this stream.’ I ran, he pursued; he was not more swift than I, but he was stronger, and gained upon me, as my strength failed. At last, exhausted, I cried for help to Diana. ‘Help me, goddess! help your votary!’ The goddess heard, and wrapped me suddenly in a thick cloud. The river god looked now this way and now that, and twice came close to me, but could not find me. ‘Arethusa! Arethusa!’ he cried. Oh, how I trembled,—like a lamb that hears the wolf growling outside the fold. A cold sweat came over me, my hair flowed down in streams; where my foot stood there was a pool. In short, in less time than it takes to tell it I became a fountain. But in this form Alpheus knew me and attempted to mingle his stream with mine. Diana cleft the ground, and I, endeavoring to escape him, plunged into the cavern, and through the bowels of the earth came out here in Sicily. While I passed through the lower parts of the earth, I saw your Proserpine. She was sad, but no longer showing alarm in her countenance. Her look was such as became a queen—the queen of Erebus; the powerful bride of the monarch of the realms of the dead.”\n\nWhen Ceres heard this, she stood for a while like one stupefied; then turned her chariot towards heaven, and hastened to present herself before the throne of Jove. She told the story of her bereavement, and implored Jupiter to interfere to procure the restitution of her daughter. Jupiter consented on one condition, namely, that Proserpine should not during her stay in the lower world have taken any food; otherwise, the Fates forbade her release. Accordingly, Mercury was sent, accompanied by Spring, to demand Proserpine of Pluto. The wily monarch consented; but, alas! the maiden had taken a pomegranate which Pluto offered her, and had sucked the sweet pulp from a few of the seeds. This was enough to prevent her complete release; but a compromise was made, by which she was to pass half the time with her mother, and the rest with her husband Pluto.\n\nCeres allowed herself to be pacified with this arrangement, and restored the earth to her favor. Now she remembered Celeus and his family, and her promise to his infant son Triptolemus. When the boy grew up, she taught him the use of the plough, and how to sow the seed. She took him in her chariot, drawn by winged dragons, through all the countries of the earth, imparting to mankind valuable grains, and the knowledge of agriculture. After his return, Triptolemus built a magnificent temple to Ceres in Eleusis, and established the worship of the goddess, under the name of the Eleusinian mysteries, which, in the splendor and solemnity of their observance, surpassed all other religious celebrations among the Greeks.\n\nThere can be little doubt of this story of Ceres and Proserpine being an allegory. Proserpine signifies the seed-corn which when cast into the ground lies there concealed—that is, she is carried off by the god of the underworld. It reappears—that is, Proserpine is restored to her mother. Spring leads her back to the light of day.\n\nMilton alludes to the story of Proserpine in “Paradise Lost,” Book IV.:\n\n“. . . Not that fair field Of Enna where Proserpine gathering flowers, Herself a fairer flower, by gloomy Dis Was gathered, which cost Ceres all that pain To seek her through the world,— . . . might with this Paradise Of Eden strive.”\n\nHood, in his “Ode to Melancholy,” uses the same allusion very beautifully:\n\n“Forgive, if somewhile I forget, In woe to come the present bliss; As frighted Proserpine let fall Her flowers at the sight of Dis.”\n\nThe River Alpheus does in fact disappear underground, in part of its course, finding its way through subterranean channels till it again appears on the surface. It was said that the Sicilian fountain Arethusa was the same stream, which, after passing under the sea, came up again in Sicily. Hence the story ran that a cup thrown into the Alpheus appeared again in Arethusa. It is this fable of the underground course of Alpheus that Coleridge alludes to in his poem of “Kubla Khan”:\n\n“In Xanadu did Kubla Khan A stately pleasure-dome decree, Where Alph, the sacred river, ran Through caverns measureless to man, Down to a sunless sea.”\n\nIn one of Moore’s juvenile poems he thus alludes to the same story, and to the practice of throwing garlands or other light objects on his stream to be carried downward by it, and afterwards reproduced at its emerging:\n\n“O my beloved, how divinely sweet Is the pure joy when kindred spirits meet! Like him the river god, whose waters flow, With love their only light, through caves below, Wafting in triumph all the flowery braids And festal rings, with which Olympic maids Have decked his current, as an offering meet To lay at Arethusa’s shining feet. Think, when he meets at last his fountain bride, What perfect love must thrill the blended tide! Each lost in each, till mingling into one, Their lot the same for shadow or for sun, A type of true love, to the deep they run.”\n\nThe following extract from Moore’s “Rhymes on the Road” gives an account of a celebrated picture by Albano, at Milan, called a Dance of Loves:\n\n“’Tis for the theft of Enna’s flower from earth These urchins celebrate their dance of mirth, Round the green tree, like fays upon a heath;— Those that are nearest linked in order bright, Cheek after cheek, like rosebuds in a wreath; And those more distant showing from beneath The others’ wings their little eyes of light. While see! among the clouds, their eldest brother, But just flown up, tells with a smile of bliss, This prank of Pluto to his charmed mother, Who turns to greet the tidings with a kiss.”\n\n# GLAUCUS AND SCYLLA\n\nGlaucus was a fisherman. One day he had drawn his nets to land, and had taken a great many fishes of various kinds. So he emptied his net, and proceeded to sort the fishes on the grass. The place where he stood was a beautiful island in the river, a solitary spot, uninhabited, and not used for pasturage of cattle, nor ever visited by any but himself. On a sudden, the fishes, which had been laid on the grass, began to revive and move their fins as if they were in the water; and while he looked on astonished, they one and all moved off to the water, plunged in, and swam away. He did not know what to make of this, whether some god had done it or some secret power in the herbage. “What herb has such a power?” he exclaimed; and gathering some of it, he tasted it. Scarce had the juices of the plant reached his palate when he found himself agitated with a longing desire for the water. He could no longer restrain himself, but bidding farewell to earth, he plunged into the stream. The gods of the water received him graciously, and admitted him to the honor of their society. They obtained the consent of Oceanus and Tethys, the sovereigns of the sea, that all that was mortal in him should be washed away. A hundred rivers poured their waters over him. Then he lost all sense of his former nature and all consciousness. When he recovered, he found himself changed in form and mind. His hair was sea-green, and trailed behind him on the water; his shoulders grew broad, and what had been thighs and legs assumed the form of a fish’s tail. The sea-gods complimented him on the change of his appearance, and he fancied himself rather a good-looking personage.\n\nOne day Glaucus saw the beautiful maiden Scylla, the favorite of the water-nymphs, rambling on the shore, and when she had found a sheltered nook, laving her limbs in the clear water. He fell in love with her, and showing himself on the surface, spoke to her, saying such things as he thought most likely to win her to stay; for she turned to run immediately on the sight of him, and ran till she had gained a cliff overlooking the sea. Here she stopped and turned round to see whether it was a god or a sea animal, and observed with wonder his shape and color. Glaucus partly emerging from the water, and supporting himself against a rock, said, “Maiden, I am no monster, nor a sea animal, but a god; and neither Proteus nor Triton ranks higher than I. Once I was a mortal, and followed the sea for a living; but now I belong wholly to it.” Then he told the story of his metamorphosis, and how he had been promoted to his present dignity, and added, “But what avails all this if it fails to move your heart?” He was going on in this strain, but Scylla turned and hastened away.\n\nGlaucus was in despair, but it occurred to him to consult the enchantress Circe. Accordingly he repaired to her island—the same where afterwards Ulysses landed, as we shall see in one of our later stories. After mutual salutations, he said, “Goddess, I entreat your pity; you alone can relieve the pain I suffer. The power of herbs I know as well as any one, for it is to them I owe my change of form. I love Scylla. I am ashamed to tell you how I have sued and promised to her, and how scornfully she has treated me. I beseech you to use your incantations, or potent herbs, if they are more prevailing, not to cure me of my love,—for that I do not wish,—but to make her share it and yield me a like return.” To which Circe replied, for she was not insensible to the attractions of the sea-green deity, “You had better pursue a willing object; you are worthy to be sought, instead of having to seek in vain. Be not diffident, know your own worth. I protest to you that even I, goddess though I be, and learned in the virtues of plants and spells, should not know how to refuse you. If she scorns you scorn her; meet one who is ready to meet you half way, and thus make a due return to both at once.” To these words Glaucus replied, “Sooner shall trees grow at the bottom of the ocean, and sea-weed on the top of the mountains, than I will cease to love Scylla, and her alone.”\n\nThe goddess was indignant, but she could not punish him, neither did she wish to do so, for she liked him too well; so she turned all her wrath against her rival, poor Scylla. She took plants of poisonous powers and mixed them together, with incantations and charms. Then she passed through the crowd of gambolling beasts, the victims of her art, and proceeded to the coast of Sicily, where Scylla lived. There was a little bay on the shore to which Scylla used to resort, in the heat of the day, to breathe the air of the sea, and to bathe in its waters. Here the goddess poured her poisonous mixture, and muttered over it incantations of mighty power. Scylla came as usual and plunged into the water up to her waist. What was her horror to perceive a brood of serpents and barking monsters surrounding her! At first she could not imagine they were a part of herself, and tried to run from them, and to drive them away; but as she ran she carried them with her, and when she tried to touch her limbs, she found her hands touch only the yawning jaws of monsters. Scylla remained rooted to the spot. Her temper grew as ugly as her form, and she took pleasure in devouring hapless mariners who came within her grasp. Thus she destroyed six of the companions of Ulysses, and tried to wreck the ships of Æneas, till at last she was turned into a rock, and as such still continues to be a terror to mariners.\n\nKeats, in his “Endymion,” has given a new version of the ending of “Glaucus and Scylla.” Glaucus consents to Circe’s blandishments, till he by chance is witness to her transactions with her beasts.[11] Disgusted with her treachery and cruelty, he tries to escape from her, but is taken and brought back, when with reproaches she banishes him, sentencing him to pass a thousand years in decrepitude and pain. He returns to the sea, and there finds the body of Scylla, whom the goddess has not transformed but drowned. Glaucus learns that his destiny is that, if he passes his thousand years in collecting all the bodies of drowned lovers, a youth beloved of the gods will appear and help him. Endymion fulfils this prophecy, and aids in restoring Glaucus to youth, and Scylla and all the drowned lovers to life.\n\nThe following is Glaucus’s account of his feelings after his “sea-change”:\n\n“I plunged for life or death. To interknit One’s senses with so dense a breathing stuff Might seem a work of pain; so not enough Can I admire how crystal-smooth it felt, And buoyant round my limbs. At first I dwelt Whole days and days in sheer astonishment; Forgetful utterly of self-intent, Moving but with the mighty ebb and flow. Then like a new-fledged bird that first doth show His spreaded feathers to the morrow chill, I tried in fear the pinions of my will. ’Twas freedom! and at once I visited The ceaseless wonders of this ocean-bed,” etc. —Keats.\n\n————\n\n# PYGMALION—DRYOPE—VENUS AND ADONIS—APOLLO AND HYACINTHUS\n\nPYGMALION saw so much to blame in women that he came at last to abhor the sex, and resolved to live unmarried. He was a sculptor, and had made with wonderful skill a statue of ivory, so beautiful that no living woman came anywhere near it. It was indeed the perfect semblance of a maiden that seemed to be alive, and only prevented from moving by modesty. His art was so perfect that it concealed itself and its product looked like the workmanship of nature. Pygmalion admired his own work, and at last fell in love with the counterfeit creation. Oftentimes he laid his hand upon it as if to assure himself whether it were living or not, and could not even then believe that it was only ivory. He caressed it, and gave it presents such as young girls love,—bright shells and polished stones, little birds and flowers of various hues, beads and amber. He put raiment on its limbs, and jewels on its fingers, and a necklace about its neck. To the ears he hung earrings and strings of pearls upon the breast. Her dress became her, and she looked not less charming than when unattired. He laid her on a couch spread with cloths of Tyrian dye, and called her his wife, and put her head upon a pillow of the softest feathers, as if she could enjoy their softness.\n\nThe festival of Venus was at hand—a festival celebrated with great pomp at Cyprus. Victims were offered, the altars smoked, and the odor of incense filled the air. When Pygmalion had performed his part in the solemnities, he stood before the altar and timidly said, “Ye gods, who can do all things, give me, I pray you, for my wife”—he dared not say “my ivory virgin,” but said instead—“one like my ivory virgin.” Venus, who was present at the festival, heard him and knew the thought he would have uttered; and as an omen of her favor, caused the flame on the altar to shoot up thrice in a fiery point into the air. When he returned home, he went to see his statue, and leaning over the couch, gave a kiss to the mouth. It seemed to be warm. He pressed its lips again, he laid his hand upon the limbs; the ivory felt soft to his touch and yielded to his fingers like the wax of Hymettus. While he stands astonished and glad, though doubting, and fears he may be mistaken, again and again with a lover’s ardor he touches the object of his hopes. It was indeed alive! The veins when pressed yielded to the finger and again resumed their roundness. Then at last the votary of Venus found words to thank the goddess, and pressed his lips upon lips as real as his own. The virgin felt the kisses and blushed, and opening her timid eyes to the light, fixed them at the same moment on her lover. Venus blessed the nuptials she had formed, and from this union Paphos was born, from whom the city, sacred to Venus, received its name.\n\nSchiller, in his poem the “Ideals,” applies this tale of Pygmalion to the love of nature in a youthful heart. The following translation is furnished by a friend:\n\n“As once with prayers in passion flowing, Pygmalion embraced the stone, Till from the frozen marble glowing, The light of feeling o’er him shone, So did I clasp with young devotion Bright nature to a poet’s heart; Till breath and warmth and vital motion Seemed through the statue form to dart.\n\n“And then, in all my ardor sharing, The silent form expression found; Returned my kiss of youthful daring, And understood my heart’s quick sound. Then lived for me the bright creation, The silver rill with song was rife; The trees, the roses shared sensation, An echo of my boundless life.” —S. G. B.\n\nDryope and Iole were sisters. The former was the wife of Andræmon, beloved by her husband, and happy in the birth of her first child. One day the sisters strolled to the bank of a stream that sloped gradually down to the water’s edge, while the upland was overgrown with myrtles. They were intending to gather flowers for forming garlands for the altars of the nymphs, and Dryope carried her child at her bosom, precious burden, and nursed him as she walked. Near the water grew a lotus plant, full of purple flowers. Dryope gathered some and offered them to the baby, and Iole was about to do the same, when she perceived blood dropping from the places where her sister had broken them off the stem. The plant was no other than the nymph Lotis, who, running from a base pursuer, had been changed into this form. This they learned from the country people when it was too late.\n\nDryope, horror-struck when she perceived what she had done, would gladly have hastened from the spot, but found her feet rooted to the ground. She tried to pull them away, but moved nothing but her upper limbs. The woodiness crept upward, and by degrees invested her body. In anguish she attempted to tear her hair, but found her hands filled with leaves. The infant felt his mother’s bosom begin to harden, and the milk cease to flow. Iole looked on at the sad fate of her sister, and could render no assistance. She embraced the growing trunk, as if she would hold back the advancing wood, and would gladly have been enveloped in the same bark. At this moment Andræmon, the husband of Dryope, with her father, approached; and when they asked for Dryope, Iole pointed them to the new-formed lotus. They embraced the trunk of the yet warm tree, and showered their kisses on its leaves.\n\nNow there was nothing left of Dryope but her face. Her tears still flowed and fell on her leaves, and while she could she spoke. “I am not guilty. I deserve not this fate. I have injured no one. If I speak falsely, may my foliage perish with drought and my trunk be cut down and burned. Take this infant and give it to a nurse. Let it often be brought and nursed under my branches, and play in my shade; and when he is old enough to talk, let him be taught to call me mother, and to say with sadness, ‘My mother lies hid under this bark.’ But bid him be careful of river banks, and beware how he plucks flowers, remembering that every bush he sees may be a goddess in disguise. Farewell, dear husband, and sister, and father. If you retain any love for me, let not the axe wound me, nor the flocks bite and tear my branches. Since I cannot stoop to you, climb up hither and kiss me; and while my lips continue to feel, lift up my child that I may kiss him. I can speak no more, for already the bark advances up my neck, and will soon shoot over me. You need not close my eyes, the bark will close them without your aid.” Then the lips ceased to move, and life was extinct; but the branches retained for some time longer the vital heat.\n\nKeats, in “Endymion,” alludes to Dryope thus:\n\n“She took a lute from which there pulsing came A lively prelude, fashioning the way In which her voice should wander. ’Twas a lay More subtle-cadenced, more forest-wild Than Dryope’s lone lulling of her child;” etc.\n\n# VENUS AND ADONIS\n\nVenus, playing one day with her boy Cupid, wounded her bosom with one of his arrows. She pushed him away, but the wound was deeper than she thought. Before it healed she beheld Adonis, and was captivated with him. She no longer took any interest in her favorite resorts—Paphos, and Cnidos, and Amathos, rich in metals. She absented herself even from heaven, for Adonis was dearer to her than heaven. Him she followed and bore him company. She who used to love to recline in the shade, with no care but to cultivate her charms, now rambles through the woods and over the hills, dressed like the huntress Diana; and calls her dogs, and chases hares and stags, or other game that it is safe to hunt, but keeps clear of the wolves and bears, reeking with the slaughter of the herd. She charged Adonis, too, to beware of such dangerous animals. “Be brave towards the timid,” said she; “courage against the courageous is not safe. Beware how you expose yourself to danger and put my happiness to risk. Attack not the beasts that Nature has armed with weapons. I do not value your glory so high as to consent to purchase it by such exposure. Your youth, and the beauty that charms Venus, will not touch the hearts of lions and bristly boars. Think of their terrible claws and prodigious strength! I hate the whole race of them. Do you ask me why?” Then she told him the story of Atalanta and Hippomenes, who were changed into lions for their ingratitude to her.\n\nHaving given him this warning, she mounted her chariot drawn by swans, and drove away through the air. But Adonis was too noble to heed such counsels. The dogs had roused a wild boar from his lair, and the youth threw his spear and wounded the animal with a sidelong stroke. The beast drew out the weapon with his jaws, and rushed after Adonis, who turned and ran; but the boar overtook him, and buried his tusks in his side, and stretched him dying upon the plain.\n\nVenus, in her swan-drawn chariot, had not yet reached Cyprus, when she heard coming up through mid-air the groans of her beloved, and turned her white-winged coursers back to earth. As she drew near and saw from on high his lifeless body bathed in blood, she alighted and, bending over it, beat her breast and tore her hair. Reproaching the Fates, she said, “Yet theirs shall be but a partial triumph; memorials of my grief shall endure, and the spectacle of your death, my Adonis, and of my lamentations shall be annually renewed. Your blood shall be changed into a flower; that consolation none can envy me.” Thus speaking, she sprinkled nectar on the blood; and as they mingled, bubbles rose as in a pool on which raindrops fall, and in an hour’s time there sprang up a flower of bloody hue like that of the pomegranate. But it is short-lived. It is said the wind blows the blossoms open, and afterwards blows the petals away; so it is called Anemone, or Wind Flower, from the cause which assists equally in its production and its decay.\n\nMilton alludes to the story of Venus and Adonis in his “Comus”:\n\n“Beds of hyacinth and roses Where young Adonis oft reposes, Waxing well of his deep wound In slumber soft, and on the ground Sadly sits th’ Assyrian queen;” etc.\n\n# APOLLO AND HYACINTHUS\n\nApollo was passionately fond of a youth named Hyacinthus. He accompanied him in his sports, carried the nets when he went fishing, led the dogs when he went to hunt, followed him in his excursions in the mountains, and neglected for him his lyre and his arrows. One day they played a game of quoits together, and Apollo, heaving aloft the discus, with strength mingled with skill, sent it high and far. Hyacinthus watched it as it flew, and excited with the sport ran forward to seize it, eager to make his throw, when the quoit bounded from the earth and struck him in the forehead. He fainted and fell. The god, as pale as himself, raised him and tried all his art to stanch the wound and retain the flitting life, but all in vain; the hurt was past the power of medicine. As when one has broken the stem of a lily in the garden it hangs its head and turns its flowers to the earth, so the head of the dying boy, as if too heavy for his neck, fell over on his shoulder. “Thou diest, Hyacinth,” so spoke Phœbus, “robbed of thy youth by me. Thine is the suffering, mine the crime. Would that I could die for thee! But since that may not be, thou shalt live with me in memory and in song. My lyre shall celebrate thee, my song shall tell thy fate, and thou shalt become a flower inscribed with my regrets.” While Apollo spoke, behold the blood which had flowed on the ground and stained the herbage ceased to be blood; but a flower of hue more beautiful than the Tyrian sprang up, resembling the lily, if it were not that this is purple and that silvery white.[12] And this was not enough for Phœbus; but to confer still greater honor, he marked the petals with his sorrow, and inscribed “Ah! ah!” upon them as we see to this day. The flower bears the name of Hyacinthus, and with every returning spring revives the memory of his fate.\n\nIt was said that Zephyrus (the West wind), who was also fond of Hyacinthus and jealous of his preference of Apollo, blew the quoit out of its course to make it strike Hyacinthus. Keats alludes to this in his “Endymion,” where he describes the lookers-on at the game of quoits:\n\n“Or they might watch the quoit-pitchers, intent On either side, pitying the sad death Of Hyacinthus, when the cruel breath Of Zephyr slew him; Zephyr penitent, Who now ere Phœbus mounts the firmament, Fondles the flower amid the sobbing rain.”\n\nAn allusion to Hyacinthus will also be recognized in Milton’s “Lycidas”:\n\n“Like to that sanguine flower inscribed with woe.”\n————\n\nCEYX was king of Thessaly, where he reigned in peace, without violence or wrong. He was son of Hesperus, the Day-star, and the glow of his beauty reminded one of his father. Halcyone, the daughter of Æolus, was his wife, and devotedly attached to him. Now Ceyx was in deep affliction for the loss of his brother, and direful prodigies following his brother’s death made him feel as if the gods were hostile to him. He thought best, therefore, to make a voyage to Carlos in Ionia, to consult the oracle of Apollo. But as soon as he disclosed his intention to his wife Halcyone, a shudder ran through her frame, and her face grew deadly pale. “What fault of mine, dearest husband, has turned your affection from me? Where is that love of me that used to be uppermost in your thoughts? Have you learned to feel easy in the absence of Halcyone? Would you rather have me away?” She also endeavored to discourage him, by describing the violence of the winds, which she had known familiarly when she lived at home in her father’s house,—Æolus being the god of the winds, and having as much as he could do to restrain them. “They rush together,” said she, “with such fury that fire flashes from the conflict. But if you must go,” she added, “dear husband, let me go with you, otherwise I shall suffer not only the real evils which you must encounter, but those also which my fears suggest.”\n\nThese words weighed heavily on the mind of King Ceyx, and it was no less his own wish than hers to take her with him, but he could not bear to expose her to the dangers of the sea. He answered, therefore, consoling her as well as he could, and finished with these words: “I promise, by the rays of my father the Day-star, that if fate permits I will return before the moon shall have twice rounded her orb.” When he had thus spoken, he ordered the vessel to be drawn out of the shiphouse, and the oars and sails to be put aboard. When Halcyone saw these preparations she shuddered, as if with a presentiment of evil. With tears and sobs she said farewell, and then fell senseless to the ground.\n\nCeyx would still have lingered, but now the young men grasped their oars and pulled vigorously through the waves, with long and measured strokes. Halcyone raised her streaming eyes, and saw her husband standing on the deck, waving his hand to her. She answered his signal till the vessel had receded so far that she could no longer distinguish his form from the rest. When the vessel itself could no more be seen, she strained her eyes to catch the last glimmer of the sail, till that too disappeared. Then, retiring to her chamber, she threw herself on her solitary couch.\n\nMeanwhile they glide out of the harbor, and the breeze plays among the ropes. The seamen draw in their oars, and hoist their sails. When half or less of their course was passed, as night drew on, the sea began to whiten with swelling waves, and the east wind to blow a gale. The master gave the word to take in sail, but the storm forbade obedience, for such is the roar of the winds and waves his orders are unheard. The men, of their own accord, busy themselves to secure the oars, to strengthen the ship, to reef the sail. While they thus do what to each one seems best, the storm increases. The shouting of the men, the rattling of the shrouds, and the dashing of the waves, mingle with the roar of the thunder. The swelling sea seems lifted up to the heavens, to scatter its foam among the clouds; then sinking away to the bottom assumes the color of the shoal—a Stygian blackness.\n\nThe vessel shares all these changes. It seems like a wild beast that rushes on the spears of the hunters. Rain falls in torrents, as if the skies were coming down to unite with the sea. When the lightning ceases for a moment, the night seems to add its own darkness to that of the storm; then comes the flash, rending the darkness asunder, and lighting up all with a glare. Skill fails, courage sinks, and death seems to come on every wave. The men are stupefied with terror. The thought of parents, and kindred, and pledges left at home, comes over their minds. Ceyx thinks of Halcyone. No name but hers is on his lips, and while he yearns for her, he yet rejoices in her absence. Presently the mast is shattered by a stroke of lightning, the rudder broken, and the triumphant surge curling over looks down upon the wreck, then falls, and crushes it to fragments. Some of the seamen, stunned by the stroke, sink, and rise no more; others cling to fragments of the wreck. Ceyx, with the hand that used to grasp the sceptre, holds fast to a plank, calling for help,—alas, in vain,—upon his father and his father-in-law. But oftenest on his lips was the name of Halcyone. To her his thoughts cling. He prays that the waves may bear his body to her sight, and that it may receive burial at her hands. At length the waters overwhelm him, and he sinks. The Day-star looked dim that night. Since it could not leave the heavens, it shrouded its face with clouds.\n\nIn the meanwhile Halcyone, ignorant of all these horrors, counted the days till her husband’s promised return. Now she gets ready the garments which he shall put on, and now what she shall wear when he arrives. To all the gods she offers frequent incense, but more than all to Juno. For her husband, who was no more, she prayed incessantly: that he might be safe; that he might come home; that he might not, in his absence, see any one that he would love better than her. But of all these prayers, the last was the only one destined to be granted. The goddess, at length, could not bear any longer to be pleaded with for one already dead, and to have hands raised to her altars that ought rather to be offering funeral rites. So, calling Iris, she said, “Iris, my faithful messenger, go to the drowsy dwelling of Somnus, and tell him to send a vision to Halcyone in the form of Ceyx, to make known to her the event.”\n\nIris puts on her robe of many colors, and tingeing the sky with her bow, seeks the palace of the King of Sleep. Near the Cimmerian country, a mountain cave is the abode of the dull god Somnus. Here Phœbus dares not come, either rising, at midday, or setting. Clouds and shadows are exhaled from the ground, and the light glimmers faintly. The bird of dawning, with crested head, never there calls aloud to Aurora, nor watchful dog, nor more sagacious goose disturbs the silence. No wild beast, nor cattle, nor branch moved with the wind, nor sound of human conversation, breaks the stillness. Silence reigns there; but from the bottom of the rock the River Lethe flows, and by its murmur invites to sleep. Poppies grow abundantly before the door of the cave, and other herbs, from whose juices Night collects slumbers, which she scatters over the darkened earth. There is no gate to the mansion, to creak on its hinges, nor any watchman; but in the midst a couch of black ebony, adorned with black plumes and black curtains. There the god reclines, his limbs relaxed with sleep. Around him lie dreams, resembling all various forms, as many as the harvest bears stalks, or the forest leaves, or the seashore sand grains.\n\nAs soon as the goddess entered and brushed away the dreams that hovered around her, her brightness lit up all the cave. The god, scarce opening his eyes, and ever and anon dropping his beard upon his breast, at last shook himself free from himself, and leaning on his arm, inquired her errand,—for he knew who she was. She answered, “Somnus, gentlest of the gods, tranquillizer of minds and soother of care-worn hearts, Juno sends you her commands that you despatch a dream to Halcyone, in the city of Trachine, representing her lost husband and all the events of the wreck.”\n\nHaving delivered her message, Iris hasted away, for she could not longer endure the stagnant air, and as she felt drowsiness creeping over her, she made her escape, and returned by her bow the way she came. Then Somnus called one of his numerous sons,—Morpheus,—the most expert in counterfeiting forms, and in imitating the walk, the countenance, and mode of speaking, even the clothes and attitudes most characteristic of each. But he only imitates men, leaving it to another to personate birds, beasts, and serpents. Him they call Icelos; and Phantasos is a third, who turns himself into rocks, waters, woods, and other things without life. These wait upon kings and great personages in their sleeping hours, while others move among the common people. Somnus chose, from all the brothers, Morpheus, to perform the command of Iris; then laid his head on his pillow and yielded himself to grateful repose.\n\nMorpheus flew, making no noise with his wings, and soon came to the Hæmonian city, where, laying aside his wings, he assumed the form of Ceyx. Under that form, but pale like a dead man, naked, he stood before the couch of the wretched wife. His beard seemed soaked with water, and water trickled from his drowned locks. Leaning over the bed, tears streaming from his eyes, he said, “Do you recognize your Ceyx, unhappy wife, or has death too much changed my visage? Behold me, know me, your husband’s shade, instead of himself. Your prayers, Halcyone, availed me nothing. I am dead. No more deceive yourself with vain hopes of my return. The stormy winds sunk my ship in the Ægean Sea, waves filled my mouth while it called aloud on you. No uncertain messenger tells you this, no vague rumor brings it to your ears. I come in person, a shipwrecked man, to tell you my fate. Arise! give me tears, give me lamentations, let me not go down to Tartarus unwept.” To these words Morpheus added the voice, which seemed to be that of her husband; he seemed to pour forth genuine tears; his hands had the gestures of Ceyx.\n\nHalcyone, weeping, groaned, and stretched out her arms in her sleep, striving to embrace his body, but grasping only the air. “Stay!” she cried; “whither do you fly? let us go together.” Her own voice awakened her. Starting up, she gazed eagerly around, to see if he was still present, for the servants, alarmed by her cries, had brought a light. When she found him not, she smote her breast and rent her garments. She cares not to unbind her hair, but tears it wildly. Her nurse asks what is the cause of her grief. “Halcyone is no more,” she answers, “she perished with her Ceyx. Utter not words of comfort, he is shipwrecked and dead. I have seen him, I have recognized him. I stretched out my hands to seize him and detain him. His shade vanished, but it was the true shade of my husband. Not with the accustomed features, not with the beauty that was his, but pale, naked, and with his hair wet with sea-water, he appeared to wretched me. Here, in this very spot, the sad vision stood,”—and she looked to find the mark of his footsteps. “This it was, this that my presaging mind foreboded, when I implored him not to leave me, to trust himself to the waves. Oh, how I wish, since thou wouldst go, thou hadst taken me with thee! It would have been far better. Then I should have had no remnant of life to spend without thee, nor a separate death to die. If I could bear to live and struggle to endure, I should be more cruel to myself than the sea has been to me. But I will not struggle, I will not be separated from thee, unhappy husband. This time, at least, I will keep thee company. In death, if one tomb may not include us, one epitaph shall; if I may not lay my ashes with thine, my name, at least, shall not be separated.” Her grief forbade more words, and these were broken with tears and sobs.\n\nIt was now morning. She went to the seashore, and sought the spot where she last saw him, on his departure. “While he lingered here, and cast off his tacklings, he gave me his last kiss.” While she reviews every object, and strives to recall every incident, looking out over the sea, she descries an indistinct object floating in the water. At first she was in doubt what it was, but by degrees the waves bore it nearer, and it was plainly the body of a man. Though unknowing of whom, yet, as it was of some shipwrecked one, she was deeply moved, and gave it her tears, saying, “Alas! unhappy one, and unhappy, if such there be, thy wife!” Borne by the waves, it came nearer. As she more and more nearly views it, she trembles more and more. Now, now it approaches the shore. Now marks that she recognizes appear. It is her husband! Stretching out her trembling hands towards it, she exclaims, “O dearest husband, is it thus you return to me?”\n\nThere was built out from the shore a mole, constructed to break the assaults of the sea, and stem its violent ingress. She leaped upon this barrier and (it was wonderful she could do so) she flew, and striking the air with wings produced on the instant, skimmed along the surface of the water, an unhappy bird. As she flew, her throat poured forth sounds full of grief, and like the voice of one lamenting. When she touched the mute and bloodless body, she enfolded its beloved limbs with her new-formed wings, and tried to give kisses with her horny beak. Whether Ceyx felt it, or whether it was only the action of the waves, those who looked on doubted, but the body seemed to raise its head. But indeed he did feel it, and by the pitying gods both of them were changed into birds. They mate and have their young ones. For seven placid days, in winter time, Halcyone broods over her nest, which floats upon the sea. Then the way is safe to seamen. Æolus guards the winds and keeps them from disturbing the deep. The sea is given up, for the time, to his grandchildren.\n\nThe following lines from Byron’s “Bride of Abydos” might seem borrowed from the concluding part of this description, if it were not stated that the author derived the suggestion from observing the motion of a floating corpse:\n\n“As shaken on his restless pillow, His head heaves with the heaving billow, That hand, whose motion is not life, Yet feebly seems to menace strife, Flung by the tossing tide on high, Then levelled with the wave . . .”\n\nMilton in his “Hymn on the Nativity,” thus alludes to the fable of the Halcyon:\n\n“But peaceful was the night Wherein the Prince of light His reign of peace upon the earth began; The winds with wonder whist Smoothly the waters kist Whispering new joys to the mild ocean, Who now hath quite forgot to rave While birds of calm sit brooding on the charmed wave.”\n\nKeats, also, in “Endymion,” says:\n\n“O magic sleep! O comfortable bird That broodest o’er the troubled sea of the mind Till it is hushed and smooth.”\n————\n\n# VERTUMNUS AND POMONA\n\nTHE Hamadryads were Wood-nymphs. Pomona was of this class, and no one excelled her in love of the garden and the culture of fruit. She cared not for forests and rivers, but loved the cultivated country, and trees that bear delicious apples. Her right hand bore for its weapon not a javelin, but a pruning-knife. Armed with this, she busied herself at one time to repress the too luxuriant growths, and curtail the branches that straggled out of place; at another, to split the twig and insert therein a graft, making the branch adopt a nursling not its own. She took care, too, that her favorites should not suffer from drought, and led streams of water by them, that the thirsty roots might drink. This occupation was her pursuit, her passion; and she was free from that which Venus inspires. She was not without fear of the country people, and kept her orchard locked, and allowed not men to enter. The Fauns and Satyrs would have given all they possessed to win her, and so would old Sylvanus, who looks young for his years, and Pan, who wears a garland of pine leaves around his head. But Vertumnus loved her best of all; yet he sped no better than the rest. O how often, in the disguise of a reaper, did he bring her corn in a basket, and looked the very image of a reaper! With a hay band tied round him, one would think he had just come from turning over the grass. Sometimes he would have an ox-goad in his hand, and you would have said he had just unyoked his weary oxen. Now he bore a pruning-hook, and personated a vine-dresser; and again, with a ladder on his shoulder, he seemed as if he was going to gather apples. Sometimes he trudged along as a discharged soldier, and again he bore a fishing-rod, as if going to fish. In this way he gained admission to her again and again, and fed his passion with the sight of her.\n\nOne day he came in the guise of an old woman, her gray hair surmounted with a cap, and a staff in her hand. She entered the garden and admired the fruit. “It does you credit, my dear,” she said, and kissed her, not exactly with an old woman’s kiss. She sat down on a bank, and looked up at the branches laden with fruit which hung over her. Opposite was an elm entwined with a vine loaded with swelling grapes. She praised the tree and its associated vine, equally. “But,” said she, “if the tree stood alone, and had no vine clinging to it, it would have nothing to attract or offer us but its useless leaves. And equally the vine, if it were not twined round the elm, would lie prostrate on the ground. Why will you not take a lesson from the tree and the vine, and consent to unite yourself with some one? I wish you would. Helen herself had not more numerous suitors, nor Penelope, the wife of shrewd Ulysses. Even while you spurn them, they court you,—rural deities and others of every kind that frequent these mountains. But if you are prudent and want to make a good alliance, and will let an old woman advise you,—who loves you better than you have any idea of,—dismiss all the rest and accept Vertumnus, on my recommendation. I know him as well as he knows himself. He is not a wandering deity, but belongs to these mountains. Nor is he like too many of the lovers nowadays, who love any one they happen to see; he loves you, and you only. Add to this, he is young and handsome, and has the art of assuming any shape he pleases, and can make himself just what you command him. Moreover, he loves the same things that you do, delights in gardening, and handles your apples with admiration. But now he cares nothing for fruits nor flowers, nor anything else, but only yourself. Take pity on him, and fancy him speaking now with my mouth. Remember that the gods punish cruelty, and that Venus hates a hard heart, and will visit such offences sooner or later. To prove this, let me tell you a story, which is well known in Cyprus to be a fact; and I hope it will have the effect to make you more merciful.\n\n“Iphis was a young man of humble parentage, who saw and loved Anaxarete, a noble lady of the ancient family of Teucer. He struggled long with his passion, but when he found he could not subdue it, he came a suppliant to her mansion. First he told his passion to her nurse, and begged her as she loved her foster-child to favor his suit. And then he tried to win her domestics to his side. Sometimes he committed his vows to written tablets, and often hung at her door garlands which he had moistened with his tears. He stretched himself on her threshold, and uttered his complaints to the cruel bolts and bars. She was deafer than the surges which rise in the November gale; harder than steel from the German forges, or a rock that still clings to its native cliff. She mocked and laughed at him, adding cruel words to her ungentle treatment, and gave not the slightest gleam of hope.\n\n“Iphis could not any longer endure the torments of hopeless love, and, standing before her doors, he spake these last words: ‘Anaxarete, you have conquered, and shall no longer have to bear my importunities. Enjoy your triumph! Sing songs of joy, and bind your forehead with laurel,—you have conquered! I die; stony heart, rejoice! This at least I can do to gratify you and force you to praise me; and thus shall I prove that the love of you left me but with life. Nor will I leave it to rumor to tell you of my death. I will come myself, and you shall see me die, and feast your eyes on the spectacle. Yet, O ye gods, who look down on mortal woes, observe my fate! I ask but this: let me be remembered in coming ages, and add those years to my fame which you have reft from my life.’ Thus he said, and, turning his pale face and weeping eyes towards her mansion, he fastened a rope to the gatepost, on which he had often hung garlands, and putting his head into the noose, he murmured, ‘This garland at least will please you, cruel girl!’ and falling hung suspended with his neck broken. As he fell he struck against the gate, and the sound was as the sound of a groan. The servants opened the door and found him dead, and with exclamations of pity raised him and carried him home to his mother, for his father was not living. She received the dead body of her son, and folded the cold form to her bosom, while she poured forth the sad words which bereaved mothers utter. The mournful funeral passed through the town, and the pale corpse was borne on a bier to the place of the funeral pile. By chance the home of Anaxarete was on the street where the procession passed, and the lamentations of the mourners met the ears of her whom the avenging deity had already marked for punishment.\n\n“‘Let us see this sad procession,’ said she, and mounted to a turret, whence through an open window she looked upon the funeral. Scarce had her eyes rested upon the form of Iphis stretched on the bier, when they began to stiffen, and the warm blood in her body to become cold. Endeavoring to step back, she found she could not move her feet; trying to turn away her face, she tried in vain; and by degrees all her limbs became stony like her heart. That you may not doubt the fact, the statue still remains, and stands in the temple of Venus at Salamis, in the exact form of the lady. Now think of these things, my dear, and lay aside your scorn and your delays, and accept a lover. So may neither the vernal frosts blight your young fruits, nor furious winds scatter your blossoms!”\n\nWhen Vertumnus had spoken thus, he dropped the disguise of an old woman, and stood before her in his proper person, as a comely youth. It appeared to her like the sun bursting through a cloud. He would have renewed his entreaties, but there was no need; his arguments and the sight of his true form prevailed, and the Nymph no longer resisted, but owned a mutual flame.\n\nPomona was the especial patroness of the Apple-orchard, and as such she was invoked by Phillips, the author of a poem on Cider, in blank verse. Thomson in the “Seasons” alludes to him:\n\n“Phillips, Pomona’s bard, the second thou Who nobly durst, in rhyme-unfettered verse, With British freedom, sing the British song.”\n\nBut Pomona was also regarded as presiding over other fruits, and as such is invoked by Thomson:\n\n“Bear me, Pomona, to thy citron groves, To where the lemon and the piercing lime, With the deep orange, glowing through the green, Their lighter glories blend. Lay me reclined Beneath the spreading tamarind, that shakes, Fanned by the breeze, its fever-cooling fruit.”\n————\n\n# CUPID AND PSYCHE\n\nA CERTAIN king and queen had three daughters. The charms of the two elder were more than common, but the beauty of the youngest was so wonderful that the poverty of language is unable to express its due praise. The fame of her beauty was so great that strangers from neighboring countries came in crowds to enjoy the sight, and looked on her with amazement, paying her that homage which is due only to Venus herself. In fact Venus found her altars deserted, while men turned their devotion to this young virgin. As she passed along, the people sang her praises, and strewed her way with chaplets and flowers.\n\nThis perversion of homage due only to the immortal powers to the exaltation of a mortal gave great offence to the real Venus. Shaking her ambrosial locks with indignation, she exclaimed, “Am I then to be eclipsed in my honors by a mortal girl? In vain then did that royal shepherd, whose judgment was approved by Jove himself, give me the palm of beauty over my illustrious rivals, Pallas and Juno. But she shall not so quietly usurp my honors. I will give her cause to repent of so unlawful a beauty.”\n\nThereupon she calls her winged son Cupid, mischievous enough in his own nature, and rouses and provokes him yet more by her complaints. She points out Psyche to him and says, “My dear son, punish that contumacious beauty; give thy mother a revenge as sweet as her injuries are great; infuse into the bosom of that haughty girl a passion for some low, mean, unworthy being, so that she may reap a mortification as great as her present exultation and triumph.”\n\nCupid prepared to obey the commands of his mother. There are two fountains in Venus’s garden, one of sweet waters, the other of bitter. Cupid filled two amber vases, one from each fountain, and suspending them from the top of his quiver, hastened to the chamber of Psyche, whom he found asleep. He shed a few drops from the bitter fountain over her lips, though the sight of her almost moved him to pity; then touched her side with the point of his arrow. At the touch she awoke, and opened eyes upon Cupid (himself invisible), which so startled him that in his confusion he wounded himself with his own arrow. Heedless of his wound, his whole thought now was to repair the mischief he had done, and he poured the balmy drops of joy over all her silken ringlets.\n\nPsyche, henceforth frowned upon by Venus, derived no benefit from all her charms. True, all eyes were cast eagerly upon her, and every mouth spoke her praises; but neither king, royal youth, nor plebeian presented himself to demand her in marriage. Her two elder sisters of moderate charms had now long been married to two royal princes; but Psyche, in her lonely apartment, deplored her solitude, sick of that beauty which, while it procured abundance of flattery, had failed to awaken love.\n\nHer parents, afraid that they had unwittingly incurred the anger of the gods, consulted the oracle of Apollo, and received this answer: “The virgin is destined for the bride of no mortal lover. Her future husband awaits her on the top of the mountain. He is a monster whom neither gods nor men can resist.”\n\nThis dreadful decree of the oracle filled all the people with dismay, and her parents abandoned themselves to grief. But Psyche said, “Why, my dear parents, do you now lament me? You should rather have grieved when the people showered upon me undeserved honors, and with one voice called me a Venus. I now perceive that I am a victim to that name. I submit. Lead me to that rock to which my unhappy fate has destined me.” Accordingly, all things being prepared, the royal maid took her place in the procession, which more resembled a funeral than a nuptial pomp, and with her parents, amid the lamentations of the people, ascended the mountain, on the summit of which they left her alone, and with sorrowful hearts returned home.\n\nWhile Psyche stood on the ridge of the mountain, panting with fear and with eyes full of tears, the gentle Zephyr raised her from the earth and bore her with an easy motion into a flowery dale. By degrees her mind became composed, and she laid herself down on the grassy bank to sleep. When she awoke refreshed with sleep, she looked round and beheld near by a pleasant grove of tall and stately trees. She entered it, and in the midst discovered a fountain, sending forth clear and crystal waters, and fast by, a magnificent palace whose august front impressed the spectator that it was not the work of mortal hands, but the happy retreat of some god. Drawn by admiration and wonder, she approached the building and ventured to enter. Every object she met filled her with pleasure and amazement. Golden pillars supported the vaulted roof, and the walls were enriched with carvings and paintings representing beasts of the chase and rural scenes, adapted to delight the eye of the beholder. Proceeding onward, she perceived that besides the apartments of state there were others filled with all manner of treasures, and beautiful and precious productions of nature and art.\n\nWhile her eyes were thus occupied, a voice addressed her, though she saw no one, uttering these words: “Sovereign lady, all that you see is yours. We whose voices you hear are your servants and shall obey all your commands with our utmost care and diligence. Retire, therefore, to your chamber and repose on your bed of down, and when you see fit repair to the bath. Supper awaits you in the adjoining alcove when it pleases you to take your seat there.”\n\nPsyche gave ear to the admonitions of her vocal attendants, and after repose and the refreshment of the bath, seated herself in the alcove, where a table immediately presented itself, without any visible aid from waiters or servants, and covered with the greatest delicacies of food and the most nectareous wines. Her ears too were feasted with music from invisible performers; of whom one sang, another played on the lute, and all closed in the wonderful harmony of a full chorus.\n\nShe had not yet seen her destined husband. He came only in the hours of darkness and fled before the dawn of morning, but his accents were full of love, and inspired a like passion in her. She often begged him to stay and let her behold him, but he would not consent. On the contrary he charged her to make no attempt to see him, for it was his pleasure, for the best of reasons, to keep concealed. “Why should you wish to behold me?” he said; “have you any doubt of my love? have you any wish ungratified? If you saw me, perhaps you would fear me, perhaps adore me, but all I ask of you is to love me. I would rather you would love me as an equal than adore me as a god.”\n\nThis reasoning somewhat quieted Psyche for a time, and while the novelty lasted she felt quite happy. But at length the thought of her parents, left in ignorance of her fate, and of her sisters, precluded from sharing with her the delights of her situation, preyed on her mind and made her begin to feel her palace as but a splendid prison. When her husband came one night, she told him her distress, and at last drew from him an unwilling consent that her sisters should be brought to see her.\n\nSo, calling Zephyr, she acquainted him with her husband’s commands, and he, promptly obedient, soon brought them across the mountain down to their sister’s valley. They embraced her and she returned their caresses. “Come,” said Psyche, “enter with me my house and refresh yourselves with whatever your sister has to offer.” Then taking their hands she led them into her golden palace, and committed them to the care of her numerous train of attendant voices, to refresh them in her baths and at her table, and to show them all her treasures. The view of these celestial delights caused envy to enter their bosoms, at seeing their young sister possessed of such state and splendor, so much exceeding their own.\n\nThey asked her numberless questions, among others what sort of a person her husband was. Psyche replied that he was a beautiful youth, who generally spent the daytime in hunting upon the mountains. The sisters, not satisfied with this reply, soon made her confess that she had never seen him. Then they proceeded to fill her bosom with dark suspicions. “Call to mind,” they said, “the Pythian oracle that declared you destined to marry a direful and tremendous monster. The inhabitants of this valley say that your husband is a terrible and monstrous serpent, who nourishes you for a while with dainties that he may by and by devour you. Take our advice. Provide yourself with a lamp and a sharp knife; put them in concealment that your husband may not discover them, and when he is sound asleep, slip out of bed, bring forth your lamp, and see for yourself whether what they say is true or not. If it is, hesitate not to cut off the monster’s head, and thereby recover your liberty.”\n\nPsyche resisted these persuasions as well as she could, but they did not fail to have their effect on her mind, and when her sisters were gone, their words and her own curiosity were too strong for her to resist. So she prepared her lamp and a sharp knife, and hid them out of sight of her husband. When he had fallen into his first sleep, she silently rose and uncovering her lamp beheld not a hideous monster, but the most beautiful and charming of the gods, with his golden ringlets wandering over his snowy neck and crimson cheek, with two dewy wings on his shoulders, whiter than snow, and with shining feathers like the tender blossoms of spring. As she leaned the lamp over to have a nearer view of his face a drop of burning oil fell on the shoulder of the god, startled with which he opened his eyes and fixed them full upon her; then, without saying one word, he spread his white wings and flew out of the window. Psyche, in vain endeavoring to follow him, fell from the window to the ground. Cupid, beholding her as she lay in the dust, stopped his flight for an instant and said, “O foolish Psyche, is it thus you repay my love? After having disobeyed my mother’s commands and made you my wife, will you think me a monster and cut off my head? But go; return to your sisters, whose advice you seem to think preferable to mine. I inflict no other punishment on you than to leave you forever. Love cannot dwell with suspicion.” So saying, he fled away, leaving poor Psyche prostrate on the ground, filling the place with mournful lamentations.\n\nWhen she had recovered some degree of composure she looked around her, but the palace and gardens had vanished, and she found herself in the open field not far from the city where her sisters dwelt. She repaired thither and told them the whole story of her misfortunes, at which, pretending to grieve, those spiteful creatures inwardly rejoiced. “For now,” said they, “he will perhaps choose one of us.” With this idea, without saying a word of her intentions, each of them rose early the next morning and ascended the mountains, and having reached the top, called upon Zephyr to receive her and bear her to his lord; then leaping up, and not being sustained by Zephyr, fell down the precipice and was dashed to pieces.\n\nPsyche meanwhile wandered day and night, without food or repose, in search of her husband. Casting her eyes on a lofty mountain having on its brow a magnificent temple, she sighed and said to herself, “Perhaps my love, my lord, inhabits there,” and directed her steps thither.\n\nShe had no sooner entered than she saw heaps of corn, some in loose ears and some in sheaves, with mingled ears of barley. Scattered about, lay sickles and rakes, and all the instruments of harvest, without order, as if thrown carelessly out of the weary reapers’ hands in the sultry hours of the day.\n\nThis unseemly confusion the pious Psyche put an end to, by separating and sorting everything to its proper place and kind, believing that she ought to neglect none of the gods, but endeavor by her piety to engage them all in her behalf. The holy Ceres, whose temple it was, finding her so religiously employed, thus spoke to her: “O Psyche, truly worthy of our pity, though I cannot shield you from the frowns of Venus, yet I can teach you how best to allay her displeasure. Go, then, and voluntarily surrender yourself to your lady and sovereign, and try by modesty and submission to win her forgiveness, and perhaps her favor will restore you the husband you have lost.”\n\nPsyche obeyed the commands of Ceres and took her way to the temple of Venus, endeavoring to fortify her mind and ruminating on what she should say and how best propitiate the angry goddess, feeling that the issue was doubtful and perhaps fatal.\n\nVenus received her with angry countenance. “Most undutiful and faithless of servants,” said she, “do you at last remember that you really have a mistress? Or have you rather come to see your sick husband, yet laid up of the wound given him by his loving wife? You are so ill-favored and disagreeable that the only way you can merit your lover must be by dint of industry and diligence. I will make trial of your housewifery.” Then she ordered Psyche to be led to the storehouse of her temple, where was laid up a great quantity of wheat, barley, millet, vetches, beans, and lentils prepared for food for her pigeons, and said, “Take and separate all these grains, putting all of the same kind in a parcel by themselves, and see that you get it done before evening.” Then Venus departed and left her to her task.\n\nBut Psyche, in a perfect consternation at the enormous work, sat stupid and silent, without moving a finger to the inextricable heap.\n\nWhile she sat despairing, Cupid stirred up the little ant, a native of the fields, to take compassion on her. The leader of the ant hill, followed by whole hosts of his six-legged subjects, approached the heap, and with the utmost diligence, taking grain by grain, they separated the pile, sorting each kind to its parcel; and when it was all done, they vanished out of sight in a moment.\n\nVenus at the approach of twilight returned from the banquet of the gods, breathing odors and crowned with roses. Seeing the task done, she exclaimed, “This is no work of yours, wicked one, but his, whom to your own and his misfortune you have enticed.” So saying, she threw her a piece of black bread for her supper and went away.\n\nNext morning Venus ordered Psyche to be called and said to her, “Behold yonder grove which stretches along the margin of the water. There you will find sheep feeding without a shepherd, with golden-shining fleeces on their backs. Go, fetch me a sample of that precious wool gathered from every one of their fleeces.”\n\nPsyche obediently went to the riverside, prepared to do her best to execute the command. But the river god inspired the reeds with harmonious murmurs, which seemed to say, “O maiden, severely tried, tempt not the dangerous flood, nor venture among the formidable rams on the other side, for as long as they are under the influence of the rising sun, they burn with a cruel rage to destroy mortals with their sharp horns or rude teeth. But when the noontide sun has driven the cattle to the shade, and the serene spirit of the flood has lulled them to rest, you may then cross in safety, and you will find the woolly gold sticking to the bushes and the trunks of the trees.”\n\nThus the compassionate river god gave Psyche instructions how to accomplish her task, and by observing his directions she soon returned to Venus with her arms full of the golden fleece; but she received not the approbation of her implacable mistress, who said, “I know very well it is by none of your own doings that you have succeeded in this task, and I am not satisfied yet that you have any capacity to make yourself useful. But I have another task for you. Here, take this box and go your way to the infernal shades, and give this box to Proserpine and say, ‘My mistress Venus desires you to send her a little of your beauty, for in tending her sick son she has lost some of her own.’ Be not too long on your errand, for I must paint myself with it to appear at the circle of the gods and goddesses this evening.”\n\nPsyche was now satisfied that her destruction was at hand, being obliged to go with her own feet directly down to Erebus. Wherefore, to make no delay of what was not to be avoided, she goes to the top of a high tower to precipitate herself headlong, thus to descend the shortest way to the shades below. But a voice from the tower said to her, “Why, poor unlucky girl, dost thou design to put an end to thy days in so dreadful a manner? And what cowardice makes thee sink under this last danger who hast been so miraculously supported in all thy former?” Then the voice told her how by a certain cave she might reach the realms of Pluto, and how to avoid all the dangers of the road, to pass by Cerberus, the three-headed dog, and prevail on Charon, the ferryman, to take her across the black river and bring her back again. But the voice added, “When Proserpine has given you the box filled with her beauty, of all things this is chiefly to be observed by you, that you never once open or look into the box nor allow your curiosity to pry into the treasure of the beauty of the goddesses.”\n\nPsyche, encouraged by this advice, obeyed it in all things, and taking heed to her ways travelled safely to the kingdom of Pluto. She was admitted to the palace of Proserpine, and without accepting the delicate seat or delicious banquet that was offered her, but contented with coarse bread for her food, she delivered her message from Venus. Presently the box was returned to her, shut and filled with the precious commodity. Then she returned the way she came, and glad was she to come out once more into the light of day.\n\nBut having got so far successfully through her dangerous task, a longing desire seized her to examine the contents of the box. “What,” said she, “shall I, the carrier of this divine beauty, not take the least bit to put on my cheeks to appear to more advantage in the eyes of my beloved husband!” So she carefully opened the box, but found nothing there of any beauty at all, but an infernal and truly Stygian sleep, which being thus set free from its prison, took possession of her, and she fell down in the midst of the road, a sleepy corpse without sense or motion.\n\nBut Cupid, being now recovered from his wound, and not able longer to bear the absence of his beloved Psyche, slipping through the smallest crack of the window of his chamber which happened to be left open, flew to the spot where Psyche lay, and gathering up the sleep from her body closed it again in the box, and waked Psyche with a light touch of one of his arrows. “Again,” said he, “hast thou almost perished by the same curiosity. But now perform exactly the task imposed on you by my mother, and I will take care of the rest.”\n\nThen Cupid, as swift as lightning penetrating the heights of heaven, presented himself before Jupiter with his supplication. Jupiter lent a favoring ear, and pleaded the cause of the lovers so earnestly with Venus that he won her consent. On this he sent Mercury to bring Psyche up to the heavenly assembly, and when she arrived, handing her a cup of ambrosia, he said, “Drink this, Psyche, and be immortal; nor shall Cupid ever break away from the knot in which he is tied, but these nuptials shall be perpetual.”\n\nThus Psyche became at last united to Cupid, and in due time they had a daughter born to them whose name was Pleasure.\n\nThe fable of Cupid and Psyche is usually considered allegorical. The Greek name for a butterfly is Psyche, and the same word means the soul. There is no illustration of the immortality of the soul so striking and beautiful as the butterfly, bursting on brilliant wings from the tomb in which it has lain, after a dull, grovelling, caterpillar existence, to flutter in the blaze of day and feed on the most fragrant and delicate productions of the spring. Psyche, then, is the human soul, which is purified by sufferings and misfortunes, and is thus prepared for the enjoyment of true and pure happiness.\n\nIn works of art Psyche is represented as a maiden with the wings of a butterfly, along with Cupid, in the different situations described in the allegory.\n\nMilton alludes to the story of Cupid and Psyche in the conclusion of his “Comus”:\n\n“Celestial Cupid, her famed son, advanced, Holds his dear Psyche sweet entranced, After her wandering labors long, Till free consent the gods among Make her his eternal bride; And from her fair unspotted side Two blissful twins are to be born, Youth and Joy; so Jove hath sworn.”\n\nThe allegory of the story of Cupid and Psyche is well presented in the beautiful lines of T. K. Harvey:\n\n“They wove bright fables in the days of old, When reason borrowed fancy’s painted wings; When truth’s clear river flowed o’er sands of gold, And told in song its high and mystic things! And such the sweet and solemn tale of her The pilgrim heart, to whom a dream was given. That led her through the world,—Love’s worshipper,— To seek on earth for him whose home was heaven!\n\n“In the full city,—by the haunted fount,— Through the dim grotto’s tracery of spars,— ’Mid the pine temples, on the moonlit mount, Where silence sits to listen to the stars; In the deep glade where dwells the brooding dove, The painted valley, and the scented air, She heard far echoes of the voice of Love, And found his footsteps’ traces everywhere.\n\n“But nevermore they met! since doubts and fears, Those phantom shapes that haunt and blight the earth, Had come ’twixt her, a child of sin and tears, And that bright spirit of immortal birth; Until her pining soul and weeping eyes Had learned to seek him only in the skies; Till wings unto the weary heart were given, And she became Love’s angel bride in heaven!”\n\nThe story of Cupid and Psyche first appears in the works of Apuleius, a writer of the second century of our era. It is therefore of much more recent date than most of the legends of the Age of Fable. It is this that Keats alludes to in his “Ode to Psyche”:\n\n“O latest born and loveliest vision far Of all Olympus’ faded hierarchy! Fairer than Phœbe’s sapphire-regioned star Or Vesper, amorous glow-worm of the sky; Fairer than these, though temple thou hast none, Nor altar heaped with flowers; Nor virgin choir to make delicious moan Upon the midnight hours; No voice, no lute, no pipe, no incense sweet, From chain-swung censor teeming; No shrine, no grove, no oracle, no heat Of pale-mouthed prophet dreaming.”\n\nIn Moore’s “Summer Fête” a fancy ball is described, in which one of the characters personated is Psyche—\n\n“. . . not in dark disguise to-night Hath our young heroine veiled her light;— For see, she walks the earth, Love’s own. His wedded bride, by holiest vow Pledged in Olympus, and made known To mortals by the type which now Hangs glittering on her snowy brow. That butterfly, mysterious trinket, Which means the soul, (though few would think it,) And sparkling thus on brow so white Tells us we’ve Psyche here to-night.”\n\n————\n\n# CADMUS—THE MYRMIDONS\n\nJUPITER, under the disguise of a bull, had carried away Europa, the daughter of Agenor, king of Phœnicia. Agenor commanded his son Cadmus to go in search of his sister, and not to return without her. Cadmus went and sought long and far for his sister, but could not find her, and not daring to return unsuccessful, consulted the oracle of Apollo to know what country he should settle in. The oracle informed him that he should find a cow in the field, and should follow her wherever she might wander, and where she stopped, should build a city and call it Thebes. Cadmus had hardly left the Castalian cave, from which the oracle was delivered, when he saw a young cow slowly walking before him. He followed her close, offering at the same time his prayers to Phœbus. The cow went on till she passed the shallow channel of Cephisus and came out into the plain of Panope. There she stood still, and raising her broad forehead to the sky, filled the air with her lowings. Cadmus gave thanks, and stooping down kissed the foreign soil, then lifting his eyes, greeted the surrounding mountains. Wishing to offer a sacrifice to Jupiter, he sent his servants to seek pure water for a libation. Near by there stood an ancient grove which had never been profaned by the axe, in the midst of which was a cave, thick covered with the growth of bushes, its roof forming a low arch, from beneath which burst forth a fountain of purest water. In the cave lurked a horrid serpent with a crested head and scales glittering like gold. His eyes shone like fire, his body was swollen with venom, he vibrated a triple tongue, and showed a triple row of teeth. No sooner had the Tyrians dipped their pitchers in the fountain, and the in-gushing waters made a sound, than the glittering serpent raised his head out of the cave and uttered a fearful hiss. The vessels fell from their hands, the blood left their cheeks, they trembled in every limb. The serpent, twisting his scaly body in a huge coil, raised his head so as to overtop the tallest trees, and while the Tyrians from terror could neither fight nor fly, slew some with his fangs, others in his folds, and others with his poisonous breath.\n\nCadmus, having waited for the return of his men till midday, went in search of them. His covering was a lion’s hide, and besides his javelin he carried in his hand a lance, and in his breast a bold heart, a surer reliance than either. When he entered the wood, and saw the lifeless bodies of his men, and the monster with his bloody jaws, he exclaimed, “O faithful friends, I will avenge you, or share your death.” So saying he lifted a huge stone and threw it with all his force at the serpent. Such a block would have shaken the wall of a fortress, but it made no impression on the monster. Cadmus next threw his javelin, which met with better success, for it penetrated the serpent’s scales, and pierced through to his entrails. Fierce with pain, the monster turned back his head to view the wound, and attempted to draw out the weapon with his mouth, but broke it off, leaving the iron point rankling in his flesh. His neck swelled with rage, bloody foam covered his jaws, and the breath of his nostrils poisoned the air around. Now he twisted himself into a circle, then stretched himself out on the ground like the trunk of a fallen tree. As he moved onward, Cadmus retreated before him, holding his spear opposite to the monster’s opened jaws. The serpent snapped at the weapon and attempted to bite its iron point. At last Cadmus, watching his chance, thrust the spear at a moment when the animal’s head thrown back came against the trunk of a tree, and so succeeded in pinning him to its side. His weight bent the tree as he struggled in the agonies of death.\n\nWhile Cadmus stood over his conquered foe, contemplating its vast size, a voice was heard (from whence he knew not, but he heard it distinctly) commanding him to take the dragon’s teeth and sow them in the earth. He obeyed. He made a furrow in the ground, and planted the teeth, destined to produce a crop of men. Scarce had he done so when the clods began to move, and the points of spears to appear above the surface. Next helmets with their nodding plumes came up, and next the shoulders and breasts and limbs of men with weapons, and in time a harvest of armed warriors. Cadmus, alarmed, prepared to encounter a new enemy, but one of them said to him, “Meddle not with our civil war.” With that he who had spoken smote one of his earth-born brothers with a sword, and he himself fell pierced with an arrow from another. The latter fell victim to a fourth, and in like manner the whole crowd dealt with each other till all fell, slain with mutual wounds, except five survivors. One of these cast away his weapons and said, “Brothers, let us live in peace!” These five joined with Cadmus in building his city, to which they gave the name of Thebes.\n\nCadmus obtained in marriage Harmonia, the daughter of Venus. The gods left Olympus to honor the occasion with their presence, and Vulcan presented the bride with a necklace of surpassing brilliancy, his own workmanship. But a fatality hung over the family of Cadmus in consequence of his killing the serpent sacred to Mars. Semele and Ino, his daughters, and Actæon and Pentheus, his grandchildren, all perished unhappily, and Cadmus and Harmonia quitted Thebes, now grown odious to them, and emigrated to the country of the Enchelians, who received them with honor and made Cadmus their king. But the misfortunes of their children still weighed upon their minds; and one day Cadmus exclaimed, “If a serpent’s life is so dear to the gods, I would I were myself a serpent.” No sooner had he uttered the words than he began to change his form. Harmonia beheld it and prayed to the gods to let her share his fate. Both became serpents. They live in the woods, but mindful of their origin, they neither avoid the presence of man nor do they ever injure any one.\n\nThere is a tradition that Cadmus introduced into Greece the letters of the alphabet which were invented by the Phœnicians. This is alluded to by Byron, where, addressing the modern Greeks, he says:\n\n“You have the letters Cadmus gave, Think you he meant them for a slave?”\n\nMilton, describing the serpent which tempted Eve, is reminded of the serpents of the classical stories and says:\n\n. . . “—pleasing was his shape, And lovely; never since of serpent kind Lovelier; not those that in Illyria changed Hermione and Cadmus, nor the god In Epidaurus.”\n\nFor an explanation of the last allusion, see Oracle of Æsculapius, p. 298.\n\n# THE MYRMIDONS\n\nThe Myrmidons were the soldiers of Achilles, in the Trojan war. From them all zealous and unscrupulous followers of a political chief are called by that name, down to this day. But the origin of the Myrmidons would not give one the idea of a fierce and bloody race, but rather of a laborious and peaceful one.\n\nCephalus, king of Athens, arrived in the island of Ægina to seek assistance of his old friend and ally Æacus, the king, in his war with Minos, king of Crete. Cephalus was most kindly received, and the desired assistance readily promised. “I have people enough,” said Æacus, “to protect myself and spare you such a force as you need.” “I rejoice to see it,” replied Cephalus, “and my wonder has been raised, I confess, to find such a host of youths as I see around me, all apparently of about the same age. Yet there are many individuals whom I previously knew, that I look for now in vain. What has become of them?” Æacus groaned, and replied with a voice of sadness, “I have been intending to tell you, and will now do so, without more delay, that you may see how from the saddest beginning a happy result sometimes flows. Those whom you formerly knew are now dust and ashes! A plague sent by angry Juno devastated the land. She hated it because it bore the name of one of her husband’s female favorites. While the disease appeared to spring from natural causes we resisted it, as we best might, by natural remedies; but it soon appeared that the pestilence was too powerful for our efforts, and we yielded. At the beginning the sky seemed to settle down upon the earth, and thick clouds shut in the heated air. For four months together a deadly south wind prevailed. The disorder affected the wells and springs; thousands of snakes crept over the land and shed their poison in the fountains. The force of the disease was first spent on the lower animals—dogs, cattle, sheep, and birds. The luckless ploughman wondered to see his oxen fall in the midst of their work, and lie helpless in the unfinished furrow. The wool fell from the bleating sheep, and their bodies pined away. The horse, once foremost in the race, contested the palm no more, but groaned at his stall and died an inglorious death. The wild boar forgot his rage, the stag his swiftness, the bears no longer attacked the herds. Everything languished; dead bodies lay in the roads, the fields, and the woods; the air was poisoned by them. I tell you what is hardly credible, but neither dogs nor birds would touch them, nor starving wolves. Their decay spread the infection. Next the disease attacked the country people, and then the dwellers in the city. At first the cheek was flushed, and the breath drawn with difficulty. The tongue grew rough and swelled, and the dry mouth stood open with its veins enlarged and gasped for the air. Men could not bear the heat of their clothes or their beds, but preferred to lie on the bare ground; and the ground did not cool them, but, on the contrary, they heated the spot where they lay. Nor could the physicians help, for the disease attacked them also, and the contact of the sick gave them infection, so that the most faithful were the first victims. At last all hope of relief vanished, and men learned to look upon death as the only deliverer from disease. Then they gave way to every inclination, and cared not to ask what was expedient, for nothing was expedient. All restraint laid aside, they crowded around the wells and fountains and drank till they died, without quenching thirst. Many had not strength to get away from the water, but died in the midst of the stream, and others would drink of it notwithstanding. Such was their weariness of their sick beds that some would creep forth, and if not strong enough to stand, would die on the ground. They seemed to hate their friends, and got away from their homes, as if, not knowing the cause of their sickness, they charged it on the place of their abode. Some were seen tottering along the road, as long as they could stand, while others sank on the earth, and turned their dying eyes around to take a last look, then closed them in death.\n\n“What heart had I left me, during all this, or what ought I to have had, except to hate life and wish to be with my dead subjects? On all sides lay my people strewn like over-ripened apples beneath the tree, or acorns under the storm-shaken oak. You see yonder a temple on the height. It is sacred to Jupiter. O how many offered prayers there, husbands for wives, fathers for sons, and died in the very act of supplication! How often, while the priest made ready for sacrifice, the victim fell, struck down by disease without waiting for the blow! At length all reverence for sacred things was lost. Bodies were thrown out unburied, wood was wanting for funeral piles, men fought with one another for the possession of them. Finally there were none left to mourn; sons and husbands, old men and youths, perished alike unlamented.\n\n“Standing before the altar I raised my eyes to heaven. ‘O Jupiter,’ I said, ‘if thou art indeed my father, and art not ashamed of thy offspring, give me back my people, or take me also away!’ At these words a clap of thunder was heard. ‘I accept the omen,’ I cried; ‘O may it be a sign of a favorable disposition towards me!’ By chance there grew by the place where I stood an oak with wide-spreading branches, sacred to Jupiter. I observed a troop of ants busy with their labor, carrying minute grains in their mouths and following one another in a line up the trunk of the tree. Observing their numbers with admiration, I said, ‘Give me, O father, citizens as numerous as these, and replenish my empty city.’ The tree shook and gave a rustling sound with its branches, though no wind agitated them. I trembled in every limb, yet I kissed the earth and the tree. I would not confess to myself that I hoped, yet I did hope. Night came on and sleep took possession of my frame oppressed with cares. The tree stood before me in my dreams, with its numerous branches all covered with living, moving creatures. It seemed to shake its limbs and throw down over the ground a multitude of those industrious grain-gathering animals, which appeared to gain in size, and grow larger and larger, and by and by to stand erect, lay aside their superfluous legs and their black color, and finally to assume the human form. Then I awoke, and my first impulse was to chide the gods who had robbed me of a sweet vision and given me no reality in its place. Being still in the temple, my attention was caught by the sound of many voices without; a sound of late unusual to my ears. While I began to think I was yet dreaming, Telamon, my son, throwing open the temple gates, exclaimed: ‘Father, approach, and behold things surpassing even your hopes!’ I went forth; I saw a multitude of men, such as I had seen in my dream, and they were passing in procession in the same manner. While I gazed with wonder and delight they approached and kneeling hailed me as their king. I paid my vows to Jove, and proceeded to allot the vacant city to the new-born race, and to parcel out the fields among them. I called them Myrmidons, from the ant (myrmex) from which they sprang. You have seen these persons; their dispositions resemble those which they had in their former shape. They are a diligent and industrious race, eager to gain, and tenacious of their gains. Among them you may recruit your forces. They will follow you to the war, young in years and bold in heart.”\n\nThis description of the plague is copied by Ovid from the account which Thucydides, the Greek historian, gives of the plague of Athens. The historian drew from life, and all the poets and writers of fiction since his day, when they have had occasion to describe a similar scene, have borrowed their details from him.\n\n————\n\n# NISUS AND SCYLLA\n\nMINOS, king of Crete, made war upon Megara. Nisus was king of Megara, and Scylla was his daughter. The siege had now lasted six months and the city still held out, for it was decreed by fate that it should not be taken so long as a certain purple lock, which glittered among the hair of King Nisus, remained on his head. There was a tower on the city walls, which overlooked the plain where Minos and his army were encamped. To this tower Scylla used to repair, and look abroad over the tents of the hostile army. The siege had lasted so long that she had learned to distinguish the persons of the leaders. Minos, in particular, excited her admiration. Arrayed in his helmet, and bearing his shield, she admired his graceful deportment; if he threw his javelin skill seemed combined with force in the discharge; if he drew his bow Apollo himself could not have done it more gracefully. But when he laid aside his helmet, and in his purple robes bestrode his white horse with its gay caparisons, and reined in its foaming mouth, the daughter of Nisus was hardly mistress of herself; she was almost frantic with admiration. She envied the weapon that he grasped, the reins that he held. She felt as if she could, if it were possible, go to him through the hostile ranks; she felt an impulse to cast herself down from the tower into the midst of his camp, or to open the gates to him, or to do anything else, so only it might gratify Minos. As she sat in the tower, she talked thus with herself: “I know not whether to rejoice or grieve at this sad war. I grieve that Minos is our enemy; but I rejoice at any cause that brings him to my sight. Perhaps he would be willing to grant us peace, and receive me as a hostage. I would fly down, if I could, and alight in his camp, and tell him that we yield ourselves to his mercy. But then, to betray my father! No! rather would I never see Minos again. And yet no doubt it is sometimes the best thing for a city to be conquered, when the conqueror is clement and generous. Minos certainly has right on his side. I think we shall be conquered; and if that must be the end of it, why should not love unbar the gates to him, instead of leaving it to be done by war? Better spare delay and slaughter if we can. And O if any one should wound or kill Minos! No one surely would have the heart to do it; yet ignorantly, not knowing him, one might. I will, I will surrender myself to him, with my country as a dowry, and so put an end to the war. But how? The gates are guarded, and my father keeps the keys; he only stands in my way. O that it might please the gods to take him away! But why ask the gods to do it? Another woman, loving as I do, would remove with her own hands whatever stood in the way of her love. And can any other woman dare more than I? I would encounter fire and sword to gain my object; but here there is no need of fire and sword. I only need my father’s purple lock. More precious than gold to me, that will give me all I wish.”\n\nWhile she thus reasoned night came on, and soon the whole palace was buried in sleep. She entered her father’s bedchamber and cut off the fatal lock; then passed out of the city and entered the enemy’s camp. She demanded to be led to the king, and thus addressed him: “I am Scylla, the daughter of Nisus. I surrender to you my country and my father’s house. I ask no reward but yourself; for love of you I have done it. See here the purple lock! With this I give you my father and his kingdom.” She held out her hand with the fatal spoil. Minos shrunk back and refused to touch it. “The gods destroy thee, infamous woman,” he exclaimed; “disgrace of our time! May neither earth nor sea yield thee a resting-place! Surely, my Crete, where Jove himself was cradled, shall not be polluted with such a monster!” Thus he said, and gave orders that equitable terms should be allowed to the conquered city, and that the fleet should immediately sail from the island.\n\nScylla was frantic. “Ungrateful man,” she exclaimed, “is it thus you leave me?—me who have given you victory,—who have sacrificed for you parent and country! I am guilty, I confess, and deserve to die, but not by your hand.” As the ships left the shore, she leaped into the water, and seizing the rudder of the one which carried Minos, she was borne along an unwelcome companion of their course. A sea-eagle soaring aloft,—it was her father who had been changed into that form,—seeing her, pounced down upon her, and struck her with his beak and claws. In terror she let go the ship and would have fallen into the water, but some pitying deity changed her into a bird. The sea-eagle still cherishes the old animosity; and whenever he espies her in his lofty flight you may see him dart down upon her, with beak and claws, to take vengeance for the ancient crime.\n\n# ECHO AND NARCISSUS\n\nEcho was a beautiful nymph, fond of the woods and hills, where she devoted herself to woodland sports. She was a favorite of Diana, and attended her in the chase. But Echo had one failing; she was fond of talking, and whether in chat or argument, would have the last word. One day Juno was seeking her husband, who, she had reason to fear, was amusing himself among the nymphs. Echo by her talk contrived to detain the goddess till the nymphs made their escape. When Juno discovered it, she passed sentence upon Echo in these words: “You shall forfeit the use of that tongue with which you have cheated me, except for that one purpose you are so fond of—reply. You shall still have the last word, but no power to speak first.”\n\nThis nymph saw Narcissus, a beautiful youth, as he pursued the chase upon the mountains. She loved him and followed his footsteps. O how she longed to address him in the softest accents, and win him to converse! but it was not in her power. She waited with impatience for him to speak first, and had her answer ready. One day the youth, being separated from his companions, shouted aloud, “Who’s here?” Echo replied, “Here.” Narcissus looked around, but seeing no one called out, “Come.” Echo answered, “Come.” As no one came, Narcissus called again, “Why do you shun me?” Echo asked the same question. “Let us join one another,” said the youth. The maid answered with all her heart in the same words, and hastened to the spot, ready to throw her arms about his neck. He started back, exclaiming, “Hands off! I would rather die than you should have me!” “Have me,” said she; but it was all in vain. He left her, and she went to hide her blushes in the recesses of the woods. From that time forth she lived in caves and among mountain cliffs. Her form faded with grief, till at last all her flesh shrank away. Her bones were changed into rocks and there was nothing left of her but her voice. With that she is still ready to reply to any one who calls her, and keeps up her old habit of having the last word.\n\nNarcissus’s cruelty in this case was not the only instance. He shunned all the rest of the nymphs, as he had done poor Echo. One day a maiden who had in vain endeavored to attract him uttered a prayer that he might some time or other feel what it was to love and meet no return of affection. The avenging goddess heard and granted the prayer.\n\nThere was a clear fountain, with water like silver, to which the shepherds never drove their flocks, nor the mountain goats resorted, nor any of the beasts of the forest; neither was it defaced with fallen leaves or branches; but the grass grew fresh around it, and the rocks sheltered it from the sun. Hither came one day the youth, fatigued with hunting, heated and thirsty. He stooped down to drink, and saw his own image in the water; he thought it was some beautiful water-spirit living in the fountain. He stood gazing with admiration at those bright eyes, those locks curled like the locks of Bacchus or Apollo, the rounded cheeks, the ivory neck, the parted lips, and the glow of health and exercise over all. He fell in love with himself. He brought his lips near to take a kiss; he plunged his arms in to embrace the beloved object. It fled at the touch, but returned again after a moment and renewed the fascination. He could not tear himself away; he lost all thought of food or rest, while he hovered over the brink of the fountain gazing upon his own image. He talked with the supposed spirit: “Why, beautiful being, do you shun me? Surely my face is not one to repel you. The nymphs love me, and you yourself look not indifferent upon me. When I stretch forth my arms you do the same; and you smile upon me and answer my beckonings with the like.” His tears fell into the water and disturbed the image. As he saw it depart, he exclaimed, “Stay, I entreat you! Let me at least gaze upon you, if I may not touch you.” With this, and much more of the same kind, he cherished the flame that consumed him, so that by degrees he lost his color, his vigor, and the beauty which formerly had so charmed the nymph Echo. She kept near him, however, and when he exclaimed, “Alas! alas!” she answered him with the same words. He pined away and died; and when his shade passed the Stygian river, it leaned over the boat to catch a look of itself in the waters. The nymphs mourned for him, especially the water-nymphs; and when they smote their breasts Echo smote hers also. They prepared a funeral pile and would have burned the body, but it was nowhere to be found; but in its place a flower, purple within, and surrounded with white leaves, which bears the name and preserves the memory of Narcissus.\n\nMilton alludes to the story of Echo and Narcissus in the Lady’s song in “Comus.” She is seeking her brothers in the forest, and sings to attract their attention:\n\n“Sweet Echo, sweetest nymph, that liv’st unseen Within thy aëry shell By slow Meander’s margent green, And in the violet-embroidered vale, Where the love-lorn nightingale Nightly to thee her sad song mourneth well; Canst thou not tell me of a gentle pair That likest thy Narcissus are? O, if thou have Hid them in some flowery cave, Tell me but where, Sweet queen of parly, daughter of the sphere, So may’st thou be translated to the skies, And give resounding grace to all heaven’s harmonies.”\n\nMilton has imitated the story of Narcissus in the account which he makes Eve give of the first sight of herself reflected in the fountain:\n\n“That day I oft remember when from sleep I first awaked, and found myself reposed Under a shade on flowers, much wondering where And what I was, whence thither brought, and how. Not distant far from thence a murmuring sound Of waters issued from a cave, and spread Into a liquid plain, then stood unmoved Pure as the expanse of heaven; I thither went With unexperienced thought, and laid me down On the green bank, to look into the clear Smooth lake that to me seemed another sky. As I bent down to look, just opposite A shape within the watery gleam appeared, Bending to look on me. I started back; It started back; but pleased I soon returned, Pleased it returned as soon with answering looks Of sympathy and love. There had I fixed Mine eyes till now, and pined with vain desire, Had not a voice thus warned me: ‘What thou seest, What there thou seest, fair creature, is thyself;’” etc. —Paradise Lost, Book IV.\n\nNo one of the fables of antiquity has been oftener alluded to by the poets than that of Narcissus. Here are two epigrams which treat it in different ways. The first is by Goldsmith:\n\n“ON A BEAUTIFUL YOUTH, STRUCK BLIND BY LIGHTNING “Sure ’twas by Providence designed, Rather in pity than in hate, That he should be like Cupid blind, To save him from Narcissus’ fate.”\n\nThe other is by Cowper:\n\n“ON AN UGLY FELLOW “Beware, my friend, of crystal brook Or fountain, lest that hideous hook, Thy nose, thou chance to see; Narcissus’ fate would then be thine, And self-detested thou would’st pine, As self-enamoured he.”\n\nClytie was a water-nymph and in love with Apollo, who made her no return. So she pined away, sitting all day long upon the cold ground, with her unbound tresses streaming over her shoulders. Nine days she sat and tasted neither food nor drink, her own tears and the chilly dew her only food. She gazed on the sun when he rose, and as he passed through his daily course to his setting; she saw no other object, her face turned constantly on him. At last, they say, her limbs rooted in the ground, her face became a flower[13] which turns on its stem so as always to face the sun throughout its daily course; for it retains to that extent the feeling of the nymph from whom it sprang.\n\nHood, in his “Flowers,” thus alludes to Clytie:\n\n“I will not have the mad Clytie, Whose head is turned by the sun; The tulip is a courtly quean, Whom therefore I will shun; The cowslip is a country wench, The violet is a nun;— But I will woo the dainty rose, The queen of every one.”\n\nThe sunflower is a favorite emblem of constancy. Thus Moore uses it:\n\n“The heart that has truly loved never forgets, But as truly loves on to the close; As the sunflower turns on her god when he sets The same look that she turned when he rose.”\n\n# HERO AND LEANDER\n\nLeander was a youth of Abydos, a town of the Asian side of the strait which separates Asia and Europe. On the opposite shore, in the town of Sestos, lived the maiden Hero, a priestess of Venus. Leander loved her, and used to swim the strait nightly to enjoy the company of his mistress, guided by a torch which she reared upon the tower for the purpose. But one night a tempest arose and the sea was rough; his strength failed, and he was drowned. The waves bore his body to the European shore, where Hero became aware of his death, and in her despair cast herself down from the tower into the sea and perished.\n\nThe following sonnet is by Keats:\n\n“ON A PICTURE OF LEANDER “Come hither all sweet maidens soberly, Down looking aye, and with a chasten’d light Hid in the fringes of your eyelids white, And meekly let your fair hands joined be. As if so gentle that ye could not see, Untouch’d, a victim of your beauty bright, Sinking away to his young spirit’s night, Sinking bewilder’d ’mid the dreary sea. ’Tis young Leander toiling to his death. Nigh swooning he doth purse his weary lips For Hero’s cheek, and smiles against her smile. O horrid dream! see how his body dips Dead-heavy; arms and shoulders gleam awhile; He’s gone; up bubbles all his amorous breath!”\n\nThe story of Leander’s swimming the Hellespont was looked upon as fabulous, and the feat considered impossible, till Lord Byron proved its possibility by performing it himself. In the “Bride of Abydos” he says,\n\n“These limbs that buoyant wave hath borne.”\n\nThe distance in the narrowest part is almost a mile, and there is a constant current setting out from the Sea of Marmora into the Archipelago. Since Byron’s time the feat has been achieved by others; but it yet remains a test of strength and skill in the art of swimming sufficient to give a wide and lasting celebrity to any one of our readers who may dare to make the attempt and succeed in accomplishing it.\n\nIn the beginning of the second canto of the same poem, Byron thus alludes to this story:\n\n“The winds are high on Helle’s wave, As on that night of stormiest water, When Love, who sent, forgot to save The young, the beautiful, the brave, The lonely hope of Sestos’ daughter. O, when alone along the sky The turret-torch was blazing high, Though rising gale and breaking foam, And shrieking sea-birds warned him home; And clouds aloft and tides below, With signs and sounds forbade to go, He could not see, he would not hear Or sound or sight foreboding fear. His eye but saw that light of love, The only star it hailed above; His ear but rang with Hero’s song, ‘Ye waves, divide not lovers long.’ That tale is old, but love anew May nerve young hearts to prove as true.”\n\nMINERVA, the goddess of wisdom, was the daughter of Jupiter. She was said to have leaped forth from his brain, mature, and in complete armor. She presided over the useful and ornamental arts, both those of men—such as agriculture and navigation—and those of women,—spinning, weaving, and needlework. She was also a warlike divinity; but it was defensive war only that she patronized, and she had no sympathy with Mars’s savage love of violence and bloodshed. Athens was her chosen seat, her own city, awarded to her as the prize of a contest with Neptune, who also aspired to it. The tale ran that in the reign of Cecrops, the first king of Athens, the two deities contended for the possession of the city. The gods decreed that it should be awarded to that one who produced the gift most useful to mortals. Neptune gave the horse; Minerva produced the olive. The gods gave judgment that the olive was the more useful of the two, and awarded the city to the goddess; and it was named after her, Athens, her name in Greek being Athene.\n\nThere was another contest, in which a mortal dared to come in competition with Minerva. That mortal was Arachne, a maiden who had attained such skill in the arts of weaving and embroidery that the nymphs themselves would leave their groves and fountains to come and gaze upon her work. It was not only beautiful when it was done, but beautiful also in the doing. To watch her, as she took the wool in its rude state and formed it into rolls, or separated it with her fingers and carded it till it looked as light and soft as a cloud, or twirled the spindle with skilful touch, or wove the web, or, after it was woven, adorned it with her needle, one would have said that Minerva herself had taught her. But this she denied, and could not bear to be thought a pupil even of a goddess. “Let Minerva try her skill with mine,” said she; “if beaten I will pay the penalty.” Minerva heard this and was displeased. She assumed the form of an old woman and went and gave Arachne some friendly advice. “I have had much experience,” said she, “and I hope you will not despise my counsel. Challenge your fellow-mortals as you will, but do not compete with a goddess. On the contrary, I advise you to ask her forgiveness for what you have said, and as she is merciful perhaps she will pardon you.” Arachne stopped her spinning and looked at the old dame with anger in her countenance. “Keep your counsel,” said she, “for your daughters or handmaids; for my part I know what I say, and I stand to it. I am not afraid of the goddess; let her try her skill, if she dare venture.” “She comes,” said Minerva; and dropping her disguise stood confessed. The nymphs bent low in homage, and all the bystanders paid reverence. Arachne alone was unterrified. She blushed, indeed; a sudden color dyed her cheek, and then she grew pale. But she stood to her resolve, and with a foolish conceit of her own skill rushed on her fate. Minerva forbore no longer nor interposed any further advice. They proceed to the contest. Each takes her station and attaches the web to the beam. Then the slender shuttle is passed in and out among the threads. The reed with its fine teeth strikes up the woof into its place and compacts the web. Both work with speed; their skilful hands move rapidly, and the excitement of the contest makes the labor light. Wool of Tyrian dye is contrasted with that of other colors, shaded off into one another so adroitly that the joining deceives the eye. Like the bow, whose long arch tinges the heavens, formed by sunbeams reflected from the shower,[14] in which, where the colors meet they seem as one, but at a little distance from the point of contact are wholly different.\n\nMinerva wrought on her web the scene of her contest with Neptune. Twelve of the heavenly powers are represented, Jupiter, with august gravity, sitting in the midst. Neptune, the ruler of the sea, holds his trident, and appears to have just smitten the earth, from which a horse has leaped forth. Minerva depicted herself with helmed head, her Ægis covering her breast. Such was the central circle; and in the four corners were represented incidents illustrating the displeasure of the gods at such presumptuous mortals as had dared to contend with them. These were meant as warnings to her rival to give up the contest before it was too late.\n\nArachne filled her web with subjects designedly chosen to exhibit the failings and errors of the gods. One scene represented Leda caressing the swan, under which form Jupiter had disguised himself; and another, Danaë, in the brazen tower in which her father had imprisoned her, but where the god effected his entrance in the form of a golden shower. Still another depicted Europa deceived by Jupiter under the disguise of a bull. Encouraged by the tameness of the animal Europa ventured to mount his back, whereupon Jupiter advanced into the sea and swam with her to Crete. You would have thought it was a real bull, so naturally was it wrought, and so natural the water in which it swam. She seemed to look with longing eyes back upon the shore she was leaving, and to call to her companions for help. She appeared to shudder with terror at the sight of the heaving waves, and to draw back her feet from the water.\n\nArachne filled her canvas with similar subjects, wonderfully well done, but strongly marking her presumption and impiety. Minerva could not forbear to admire, yet felt indignant at the insult. She struck the web with her shuttle and rent it in pieces; she then touched the forehead of Arachne and made her feel her guilt and shame. She could not endure it and went and hanged herself. Minerva pitied her as she saw her suspended by a rope. “Live,” she said, “guilty woman! and that you may preserve the memory of this lesson, continue to hang, both you and your descendants, to all future times.” She sprinkled her with the juices of aconite, and immediately her hair came off, and her nose and ears likewise. Her form shrank up, and her head grew smaller yet; her fingers cleaved to her side and served for legs. All the rest of her is body, out of which she spins her thread, often hanging suspended by it, in the same attitude as when Minerva touched her and transformed her into a spider.\n\nSpenser tells the story of Arachne in his “Muiopotmos,” adhering very closely to his master Ovid, but improving upon him in the conclusion of the story. The two stanzas which follow tell what was done after the goddess had depicted her creation of the olive tree:\n\n“Amongst these leaves she made a Butterfly, With excellent device and wondrous slight, Fluttering among the olives wantonly, That seemed to live, so like it was in sight; The velvet nap which on his wings doth lie, The silken down with which his back is dight, His broad outstretched horns, his hairy thighs, His glorious colors, and his glistening eyes.”[15]\n\n“Which when Arachne saw, as overlaid And mastered with workmanship so rare, She stood astonied long, ne aught gainsaid; And with fast-fixed eyes on her did stare, And by her silence, sign of one dismayed, The victory did yield her as her share: Yet did she inly fret and felly burn, And all her blood to poisonous rancor turn.”\n\nAnd so the metamorphosis is caused by Arachne’s own mortification and vexation, and not by any direct act of the goddess.\n\nThe following specimen of old-fashioned gallantry is by Garrick:\n\n“UPON A LADY’S EMBROIDERY “Arachne once, as poets tell, A goddess at her art defied, And soon the daring mortal fell The hapless victim of her pride.\n“O, then beware Arachne’s fate; Be prudent, Chloe, and submit, For you’ll most surely meet her hate, Who rival both her art and wit.”\n\nTennyson, in his “Palace of Art,” describing the works of art with which the palace was adorned, thus alludes to Europa:\n\n“. . . sweet Europa’s mantle blew unclasped From off her shoulder, backward borne, From one hand drooped a crocus, one hand grasped The mild bull’s golden horn.”\n\nIn his “Princess” there is this allusion to Danaë:\n\n“Now lies the earth all Danaë to the stars, And all thy heart lies open unto me.”\n\nThe fate of Arachne was noised abroad through all the country, and served as a warning to all presumptuous mortals not to compare themselves with the divinities. But one, and she a matron too, failed to learn the lesson of humility. It was Niobe, the queen of Thebes. She had indeed much to be proud of; but it was not her husband’s fame, nor her own beauty, nor their great descent, nor the power of their kingdom that elated her. It was her children; and truly the happiest of mothers would Niobe have been if only she had not claimed to be so. It was on occasion of the annual celebration in honor of Latona and her offspring, Apollo and Diana,—when the people of Thebes were assembled, their brows crowned with laurel, bearing frankincense to the altars and paying their vows,—that Niobe appeared among the crowd. Her attire was splendid with gold and gems, and her aspect beautiful as the face of an angry woman can be. She stood and surveyed the people with haughty looks. “What folly,” said she, “is this!—to prefer beings whom you never saw to those who stand before your eyes! Why should Latona be honored with worship, and none be paid to me? My father was Tantalus, who was received as a guest at the table of the gods; my mother was a goddess. My husband built and rules this city, Thebes, and Phrygia is my paternal inheritance. Wherever I turn my eyes I survey the elements of my power; nor is my form and presence unworthy of a goddess. To all this let me add I have seven sons and seven daughters, and look for sons-in-law and daughters-in-law of pretensions worthy of my alliance. Have I not cause for pride? Will you prefer to me this Latona, the Titan’s daughter, with her two children? I have seven times as many. Fortunate indeed am I, and fortunate I shall remain! Will any one deny this? My abundance is my security. I feel myself too strong for Fortune to subdue. She may take from me much; I shall still have much left. Were I to lose some of my children, I should hardly be left as poor as Latona with her two only. Away with you from these solemnities,—put off the laurel from your brows,—have done with this worship!” The people obeyed, and left the sacred services uncompleted.\n\nThe goddess was indignant. On the Cynthian mountain top where she dwelt she thus addressed her son and daughter: “My children, I who have been so proud of you both, and have been used to hold myself second to none of the goddesses except Juno alone, begin now to doubt whether I am indeed a goddess. I shall be deprived of my worship altogether unless you protect me.” She was proceeding in this strain, but Apollo interrupted her. “Say no more,” said he; “speech only delays punishment.” So said Diana also. Darting through the air, veiled in clouds, they alighted on the towers of the city. Spread out before the gates was a broad plain, where the youth of the city pursued their warlike sports. The sons of Niobe were there with the rest,—some mounted on spirited horses richly caparisoned, some driving gay chariots. Ismenos, the first-born, as he guided his foaming steeds, struck with an arrow from above, cried out, “Ah me!” dropped the reins, and fell lifeless. Another, hearing the sound of the bow,—like a boatman who sees the storm gathering and makes all sail for the port,—gave the reins to his horses and attempted to escape. The inevitable arrow overtook him as he fled. Two others, younger boys, just from their tasks, had gone to the playground to have a game of wrestling. As they stood breast to breast, one arrow pierced them both. They uttered a cry together, together cast a parting look around them, and together breathed their last. Alphenor, an elder brother, seeing them fall, hastened to the spot to render assistance, and fell stricken in the act of brotherly duty. One only was left, Ilioneus. He raised his arms to heaven to try whether prayer might not avail. “Spare me, ye gods!” he cried, addressing all, in his ignorance that all needed not his intercessions; and Apollo would have spared him, but the arrow had already left the string, and it was too late.\n\nThe terror of the people and grief of the attendants soon made Niobe acquainted with what had taken place. She could hardly think it possible; she was indignant that the gods had dared and amazed that they had been able to do it. Her husband, Amphion, overwhelmed with the blow, destroyed himself. Alas! how different was this Niobe from her who had so lately driven away the people from the sacred rites, and held her stately course through the city, the envy of her friends, now the pity even of her foes! She knelt over the lifeless bodies, and kissed now one, now another of her dead sons. Raising her pallid arms to heaven, “Cruel Latona,” said she, “feed full your rage with my anguish! Satiate your hard heart, while I follow to the grave my seven sons. Yet where is your triumph? Bereaved as I am, I am still richer than you, my conqueror.” Scarce had she spoken, when the bow sounded and struck terror into all hearts except Niobe’s alone. She was brave from excess of grief. The sisters stood in garments of mourning over the biers of their dead brothers. One fell, struck by an arrow, and died on the corpse she was bewailing. Another, attempting to console her mother, suddenly ceased to speak, and sank lifeless to the earth. A third tried to escape by flight, a fourth by concealment, another stood trembling, uncertain what course to take. Six were now dead, and only one remained, whom the mother held clasped in her arms, and covered as it were with her whole body. “Spare me one, and that the youngest! O spare me one of so many!” she cried; and while she spoke, that one fell dead. Desolate she sat, among sons, daughters, husband, all dead, and seemed torpid with grief. The breeze moved not her hair, no color was on her cheek, her eyes glared fixed and immovable, there was no sign of life about her. Her very tongue cleaved to the roof of her mouth, and her veins ceased to convey the tide of life. Her neck bent not, her arms made no gesture, her foot no step. She was changed to stone, within and without. Yet tears continued to flow; and borne on a whirlwind to her native mountain, she still remains, a mass of rock, from which a trickling stream flows, the tribute of her never-ending grief.\n\nThe story of Niobe has furnished Byron with a fine illustration of the fallen condition of modern Rome:\n\n“The Niobe of nations! there she stands, Childless and crownless in her voiceless woe; An empty urn within her withered hands, Whose holy dust was scattered long ago; The Scipios’ tomb contains no ashes now: The very sepulchres lie tenantless Of their heroic dwellers; dost thou flow, Old Tiber! through a marble wilderness? Rise with thy yellow waves, and mantle her distress.” Childe Harold, IV. 79.\n\nThis affecting story has been made the subject of a celebrated statue in the imperial gallery of Florence. It is the principal figure of a group supposed to have been originally arranged in the pediment of a temple. The figure of the mother clasped by the arm of her terrified child is one of the most admired of the ancient statues. It ranks with the Laocoön and the Apollo among the masterpieces of art. The following is a translation of a Greek epigram supposed to relate to this statue:\n\n“To stone the gods have changed her, but in vain; The sculptor’s art has made her breathe again.”\n\nTragic as is the story of Niobe, we cannot forbear to smile at the use Moore has made of it in “Rhymes on the Road”:\n\n“’Twas in his carriage the sublime Sir Richard Blackmore used to rhyme, And, if the wits don’t do him wrong, ’Twixt death and epics passed his time, Scribbling and killing all day long; Like Phœbus in his car at ease, Now warbling forth a lofty song, Now murdering the young Niobes.”\n\nSir Richard Blackmore was a physician, and at the same time a very prolific and very tasteless poet, whose works are now forgotten, unless when recalled to mind by some wit like Moore for the sake of a joke.\n\n————\n\nTHE Grææ were three sisters who were gray-haired from their birth, whence their name. The Gorgons were monstrous females with huge teeth like those of swine, brazen claws, and snaky hair. None of these beings make much figure in mythology except Medusa, the Gorgon, whose story we shall next advert to. We mention them chiefly to introduce an ingenious theory of some modern writers, namely, that the Gorgons and Grææ were only personifications of the terrors of the sea, the former denoting the strong billows of the wide open main, and the latter the white-crested waves that dash against the rocks of the coast. Their names in Greek signify the above epithets.\n\n# PERSEUS AND MEDUSA\n\nPerseus was the son of Jupiter and Danaë. His grandfather Acrisius, alarmed by an oracle which had told him that his daughter’s child would be the instrument of his death, caused the mother and child to be shut up in a chest and set adrift on the sea. The chest floated towards Seriphus, where it was found by a fisherman who conveyed the mother and infant to Polydectes, the king of the country, by whom they were treated with kindness. When Perseus was grown up Polydectes sent him to attempt the conquest of Medusa, a terrible monster who had laid waste the country. She was once a beautiful maiden whose hair was her chief glory, but as she dared to vie in beauty with Minerva, the goddess deprived her of her charms and changed her beautiful ringlets into hissing serpents. She became a cruel monster of so frightful an aspect that no living thing could behold her without being turned into stone. All around the cavern where she dwelt might be seen the stony figures of men and animals which had chanced to catch a glimpse of her and had been petrified with the sight. Perseus, favored by Minerva and Mercury, the former of whom lent him her shield and the latter his winged shoes, approached Medusa while she slept, and taking care not to look directly at her, but guided by her image reflected in the bright shield which he bore, he cut off her head and gave it to Minerva, who fixed it in the middle of her Ægis.\n\nMilton, in his “Comus,” thus alludes to the Ægis:\n\n“What was that snaky-headed Gorgon-shield That wise Minerva wore, unconquered virgin, Wherewith she freezed her foes to congealed stone, But rigid looks of chaste austerity, And noble grace that dashed brute violence With sudden adoration and blank awe!”\n\nArmstrong, the poet of the “Art of Preserving Health,” thus describes the effect of frost upon the waters:\n\n“Now blows the surly North and chills throughout The stiffening regions, while by stronger charms Than Circe e’er or fell Medea brewed, Each brook that wont to prattle to its banks Lies all bestilled and wedged betwixt its banks, Nor moves the withered reeds . . . The surges baited by the fierce North-east, Tossing with fretful spleen their angry heads, E’en in the foam of all their madness struck To monumental ice. . . . . . . . Such execution, So stern, so sudden, wrought the grisly aspect Of terrible Medusa, When wandering through the woods she turned to stone Their savage tenants; just as the foaming Lion Sprang furious on his prey, her speedier power Outran his haste, And fixed in that fierce attitude he stands Like Rage in marble!” —Imitations of Shakspeare.\n\n# PERSEUS AND ATLAS\n\nAfter the slaughter of Medusa, Perseus, bearing with him the head of the Gorgon, flew far and wide, over land and sea. As night came on, he reached the western limit of the earth, where the sun goes down. Here he would gladly have rested till morning. It was the realm of King Atlas, whose bulk surpassed that of all other men. He was rich in flocks and herds and had no neighbor or rival to dispute his state. But his chief pride was in his gardens, whose fruit was of gold, hanging from golden branches, half hid with golden leaves. Perseus said to him, “I come as a guest. If you honor illustrious descent, I claim Jupiter for my father; if mighty deeds, I plead the conquest of the Gorgon. I seek rest and food.” But Atlas remembered that an ancient prophecy had warned him that a son of Jove should one day rob him of his golden apples. So he answered, “Begone! or neither your false claims of glory nor parentage shall protect you;” and he attempted to thrust him out. Perseus, finding the giant too strong for him, said, “Since you value my friendship so little, deign to accept a present;” and turning his face away, he held up the Gorgon’s head. Atlas, with all his bulk, was changed into stone. His beard and hair became forests, his arms and shoulders cliffs, his head a summit, and his bones rocks. Each part increased in bulk till he became a mountain, and (such was the pleasure of the gods) heaven with all its stars rests upon his shoulders.\n\n# THE SEA-MONSTER\n\nPerseus, continuing his flight, arrived at the country of the Æthiopians, of which Cepheus was king. Cassiopeia his queen, proud of her beauty, had dared to compare herself to the Sea-Nymphs, which roused their indignation to such a degree that they sent a prodigious sea-monster to ravage the coast. To appease the deities, Cepheus was directed by the oracle to expose his daughter Andromeda to be devoured by the monster. As Perseus looked down from his aerial height he beheld the virgin chained to a rock, and waiting the approach of the serpent. She was so pale and motionless that if it had not been for her flowing tears and her hair that moved in the breeze, he would have taken her for a marble statue. He was so startled at the sight that he almost forgot to wave his wings. As he hovered over her he said, “O virgin, undeserving of those chains, but rather of such as bind fond lovers together, tell me, I beseech you, your name, and the name of your country, and why you are thus bound.” At first she was silent from modesty, and, if she could, would have hid her face with her hands; but when he repeated his questions, for fear she might be thought guilty of some fault which she dared not tell, she disclosed her name and that of her country, and her mother’s pride of beauty. Before she had done speaking, a sound was heard off upon the water, and the sea-monster appeared, with his head raised above the surface, cleaving the waves with his broad breast. The virgin shrieked, the father and mother who had now arrived at the scene, wretched both, but the mother more justly so, stood by, not able to afford protection, but only to pour forth lamentations and to embrace the victim. Then spoke Perseus: “There will be time enough for tears; this hour is all we have for rescue. My rank as the son of Jove and my renown as the slayer of the Gorgon might make me acceptable as a suitor; but I will try to win her by services rendered, if the gods will only be propitious. If she be rescued by my valor, I demand that she be my reward.” The parents consent (how could they hesitate?) and promise a royal dowry with her.\n\nAnd now the monster was within the range of a stone thrown by a skilful slinger, when with a sudden bound the youth soared into the air. As an eagle, when from his lofty flight he sees a serpent basking in the sun, pounces upon him and seizes him by the neck to prevent him from turning his head round and using his fangs, so the youth darted down upon the back of the monster and plunged his sword into its shoulder. Irritated by the wound, the monster raised himself in the air, then plunged into the depth; then, like a wild boar surrounded by a pack of barking dogs, turned swiftly from side to side, while the youth eluded its attacks by means of his wings. Wherever he can find a passage for his sword between the scales he makes a wound, piercing now the side, now the flank, as it slopes towards the tail. The brute spouts from his nostrils water mixed with blood. The wings of the hero are wet with it, and he dares no longer trust to them. Alighting on a rock which rose above the waves, and holding on by a projecting fragment, as the monster floated near he gave him a death stroke. The people who had gathered on the shore shouted so that the hills reëchoed the sound. The parents, transported with joy, embraced their future son-in-law, calling him their deliverer and the savior of their house, and the virgin, both cause and reward of the contest, descended from the rock.\n\nCassiopeia was an Æthiopian, and consequently, in spite of her boasted beauty, black; at least so Milton seems to have thought, who alludes to this story in his “Penseroso,” where he addresses Melancholy as the\n\n“. . . . goddess, sage and holy, Whose saintly visage is too bright To hit the sense of human sight, And, therefore, to our weaker view O’erlaid with black, staid Wisdom’s hue. Black, but such as in esteem Prince Memnon’s sister might beseem, Or that starred Æthiop queen that strove To set her beauty’s praise above The sea-nymphs, and their powers offended.”\n\nCassiopeia is called “the starred Æthiop queen” because after her death she was placed among the stars, forming the constellation of that name. Though she attained this honor, yet the Sea-Nymphs, her old enemies, prevailed so far as to cause her to be placed in that part of the heaven near the pole, where every night she is half the time held with her head downward, to give her a lesson of humility.\n\nMemnon was an Æthiopian prince, of whom we shall tell in a future chapter.\n\n# THE WEDDING FEAST\n\nThe joyful parents, with Perseus and Andromeda, repaired to the palace, where a banquet was spread for them, and all was joy and festivity. But suddenly a noise was heard of warlike clamor, and Phineus, the betrothed of the virgin, with a party of his adherents, burst in, demanding the maiden as his own. It was in vain that Cepheus remonstrated—“You should have claimed her when she lay bound to the rock, the monster’s victim. The sentence of the gods dooming her to such a fate dissolved all engagements, as death itself would have done.” Phineus made no reply, but hurled his javelin at Perseus, but it missed its mark and fell harmless. Perseus would have thrown his in turn, but the cowardly assailant ran and took shelter behind the altar. But his act was a signal for an onset by his band upon the guests of Cepheus. They defended themselves and a general conflict ensued, the old king retreating from the scene after fruitless expostulations, calling the gods to witness that he was guiltless of this outrage on the rights of hospitality.\n\nPerseus and his friends maintained for some time the unequal contest; but the numbers of the assailants were too great for them, and destruction seemed inevitable, when a sudden thought struck Perseus,—“I will make my enemy defend me.” Then with a loud voice he exclaimed, “If I have any friend here let him turn away his eyes!” and held aloft the Gorgon’s head. “Seek not to frighten us with your jugglery,” said Thescelus, and raised his javelin in act to throw, and became stone in the very attitude. Ampyx was about to plunge his sword into the body of a prostrate foe, but his arm stiffened and he could neither thrust forward nor withdraw it. Another, in the midst of a vociferous challenge, stopped, his mouth open, but no sound issuing. One of Perseus’s friends, Aconteus, caught sight of the Gorgon and stiffened like the rest. Astyages struck him with his sword, but instead of wounding, it recoiled with a ringing noise.\n\nPhineus beheld this dreadful result of his unjust aggression, and felt confounded. He called aloud to his friends, but got no answer; he touched them and found them stone. Falling on his knees and stretching out his hands to Perseus, but turning his head away he begged for mercy. “Take all,” said he, “give me but my life.” “Base coward,” said Perseus, “thus much I will grant you; no weapon shall touch you; moreover, you shall be preserved in my house as a memorial of these events.” So saying, he held the Gorgon’s head to the side where Phineus was looking, and in the very form in which he knelt, with his hands outstretched and face averted, he became fixed immovably, a mass of stone!\n\nThe following allusion to Perseus is from Milman’s “Samor”:\n\n“As ’mid the fabled Libyan bridal stood Perseus in stern tranquillity of wrath, Half stood, half floated on his ankle-plumes Out-swelling, while the bright face on his shield Looked into stone the raging fray; so rose, But with no magic arms, wearing alone Th’ appalling and control of his firm look, The Briton Samor; at his rising awe Went abroad, and the riotous hall was mute.”\n\n————\n\nGIANTS, SPHINX, PEGASUS AND CHIMÆRA, CENTAURS, GRIFFIN, AND PYGMIES\nMONSTERS, in the language of mythology, were beings of unnatural proportions or parts, usually regarded with terror, as possessing immense strength and ferocity, which they employed for the injury and annoyance of men. Some of them were supposed to combine the members of different animals; such were the Sphinx and Chimæra; and to these all the terrible qualities of wild beasts were attributed, together with human sagacity and faculties. Others, as the giants, differed from men chiefly in their size; and in this particular we must recognize a wide distinction among them. The human giants, if so they may be called, such as the Cyclopes, Antæus, Orion, and others, must be supposed not to be altogether disproportioned to human beings, for they mingled in love and strife with them. But the superhuman giants, who warred with the gods, were of vastly larger dimensions. Tityus, we are told, when stretched on the plain, covered nine acres, and Enceladus required the whole of Mount Ætna to be laid upon him to keep him down.\n\nWe have already spoken of the war which the giants waged against the gods, and of its result. While this war lasted the giants proved a formidable enemy. Some of them, like Briareus, had a hundred arms; others, like Typhon, breathed out fire. At one time they put the gods to such fear that they fled into Egypt and hid themselves under various forms. Jupiter took the form of a ram, whence he was afterwards worshipped in Egypt as the god Ammon, with curved horns. Apollo became a crow, Bacchus a goat, Diana a cat, Juno a cow, Venus a fish, Mercury a bird. At another time the giants attempted to climb up into heaven, and for that purpose took up the mountain Ossa and piled it on Pelion.[16] They were at last subdued by thunderbolts, which Minerva invented, and taught Vulcan and his Cyclopes to make for Jupiter.\n\n# THE SPHINX\n\nLaius, king of Thebes, was warned by an oracle that there was danger to his throne and life if his new-born son should be suffered to grow up. He therefore committed the child to the care of a herdsman with orders to destroy him; but the herdsman, moved with pity, yet not daring entirely to disobey, tied up the child by the feet and left him hanging to the branch of a tree. In this condition the infant was found by a peasant, who carried him to his master and mistress, by whom he was adopted and called Œdipus, or Swollen-foot.\n\nMany years afterwards Laius being on his way to Delphi, accompanied only by one attendant, met in a narrow road a young man also driving in a chariot. On his refusal to leave the way at their command the attendant killed one of his horses, and the stranger, filled with rage, slew both Laius and his attendant. The young man was Œdipus, who thus unknowingly became the slayer of his own father.\n\nShortly after this event the city of Thebes was afflicted with a monster which infested the highroad. It was called the Sphinx. It had the body of a lion and the upper part of a woman. It lay crouched on the top of a rock, and arrested all travellers who came that way, proposing to them a riddle, with the condition that those who could solve it should pass safe, but those who failed should be killed. Not one had yet succeeded in solving it, and all had been slain. Œdipus was not daunted by these alarming accounts, but boldly advanced to the trial. The Sphinx asked him, “What animal is that which in the morning goes on four feet, at noon on two, and in the evening upon three?” Œdipus replied, “Man, who in childhood creeps on hands and knees, in manhood walks erect, and in old age with the aid of a staff.” The Sphinx was so mortified at the solving of her riddle that she cast herself down from the rock and perished.\n\nThe gratitude of the people for their deliverance was so great that they made Œdipus their king, giving him in marriage their queen Jocasta. Œdipus, ignorant of his parentage, had already become the slayer of his father; in marrying the queen he became the husband of his mother. These horrors remained undiscovered, till at length Thebes was afflicted with famine and pestilence, and the oracle being consulted, the double crime of Œdipus came to light. Jocasta put an end to her own life, and Œdipus, seized with madness, tore out his eyes and wandered away from Thebes, dreaded and abandoned by all except his daughters, who faithfully adhered to him, till after a tedious period of miserable wandering he found the termination of his wretched life.\n\nWhen Perseus cut off Medusa’s head, the blood sinking into the earth produced the winged horse Pegasus. Minerva caught him and tamed him and presented him to the Muses. The fountain Hippocrene, on the Muses’ mountain Helicon, was opened by a kick from his hoof.\n\nThe Chimæra was a fearful monster, breathing fire. The fore part of its body was a compound of the lion and the goat, and the hind part a dragon’s. It made great havoc in Lycia, so that the king, Iobates, sought for some hero to destroy it. At that time there arrived at his court a gallant young warrior, whose name was Bellerophon. He brought letters from Prœtus, the son-in-law of Iobates, recommending Bellerophon in the warmest terms as an unconquerable hero, but added at the close a request to his father-in-law to put him to death. The reason was that Prœtus was jealous of him, suspecting that his wife Antea looked with too much admiration on the young warrior. From this instance of Bellerophon being unconsciously the bearer of his own death warrant, the expression “Bellerophontic letters” arose, to describe any species of communication which a person is made the bearer of, containing matter prejudicial to himself.\n\nIobates, on perusing the letters, was puzzled what to do, not willing to violate the claims of hospitality, yet wishing to oblige his son-in-law. A lucky thought occurred to him, to send Bellerophon to combat with the Chimæra. Bellerophon accepted the proposal, but before proceeding to the combat consulted the soothsayer Polyidus, who advised him to procure if possible the horse Pegasus for the conflict. For this purpose he directed him to pass the night in the temple of Minerva. He did so, and as he slept Minerva came to him and gave him a golden bridle. When he awoke the bridle remained in his hand. Minerva also showed him Pegasus drinking at the well of Pirene, and at sight of the bridle the winged steed came willingly and suffered himself to be taken. Bellerophon mounted him, rose with him into the air, soon found the Chimæra, and gained an easy victory over the monster.\n\nAfter the conquest of the Chimæra Bellerophon was exposed to further trials and labors by his unfriendly host, but by the aid of Pegasus he triumphed in them all, till at length Iobates, seeing that the hero was a special favorite of the gods, gave him his daughter in marriage and made him his successor on the throne. At last Bellerophon by his pride and presumption drew upon himself the anger of the gods; it is said he even attempted to fly up into heaven on his winged steed, but Jupiter sent a gadfly which stung Pegasus and made him throw his rider, who became lame and blind in consequence. After this Bellerophon wandered lonely through the Aleian field, avoiding the paths of men, and died miserably.\n\nMilton alludes to Bellerophon in the beginning of the seventh book of “Paradise Lost”:\n\n“Descend from Heaven, Urania, by that name If rightly thou art called, whose voice divine Following above the Olympian hill I soar, Above the flight of Pegasean wing. Upled by thee, Into the Heaven of Heavens I have presumed, An earthly guest, and drawn empyreal air (Thy tempering); with like safety guided down Return me to my native element; Lest from this flying steed unreined (as once Bellerophon, though from a lower sphere), Dismounted on the Aleian field I fall, Erroneous there to wander and forlorn.”\n\nYoung, in his “Night Thoughts,” speaking of the sceptic, says:\n\n“He whose blind thought futurity denies, Unconscious bears, Bellerophon, like thee His own indictment; he condemns himself. Who reads his bosom reads immortal life, Or nature there, imposing on her sons, Has written fables; man was made a lie.” Vol. II., p. 12.\n\nPegasus, being the horse of the Muses, has always been at the service of the poets. Schiller tells a pretty story of his having been sold by a needy poet and put to the cart and the plough. He was not fit for such service, and his clownish master could make nothing of him. But a youth stepped forth and asked leave to try him. As soon as he was seated on his back the horse, which had appeared at first vicious, and afterwards spirit-broken, rose kingly, a spirit, a god, unfolded the splendor of his wings, and soared towards heaven. Our own poet Longfellow also records an adventure of this famous steed in his “Pegasus in Pound.”\n\nShakspeare alludes to Pegasus in “Henry IV.,” where Vernon describes Prince Henry:\n\n“I saw young Harry, with his beaver on, His cuishes on his thighs, gallantly armed, Rise from the ground like feathered Mercury, And vaulted with such ease into his seat, As if an angel dropped down from the clouds, To turn and wind a fiery Pegasus, And witch the world with noble horsemanship.”\n\n# THE CENTAURS\n\nThese monsters were represented as men from the head to the loins, while the remainder of the body was that of a horse. The ancients were too fond of a horse to consider the union of his nature with man’s as forming a very degraded compound, and accordingly the Centaur is the only one of the fancied monsters of antiquity to which any good traits are assigned. The Centaurs were admitted to the companionship of man, and at the marriage of Pirithous with Hippodamia they were among the guests. At the feast Eurytion, one of the Centaurs, becoming intoxicated with the wine, attempted to offer violence to the bride; the other Centaurs followed his example, and a dreadful conflict arose in which several of them were slain. This is the celebrated battle of the Lapithæ and Centaurs, a favorite subject with the sculptors and poets of antiquity.\n\nBut not all the Centaurs were like the rude guests of Pirithous. Chiron was instructed by Apollo and Diana, and was renowned for his skill in hunting, medicine, music, and the art of prophecy. The most distinguished heroes of Grecian story were his pupils. Among the rest the infant Æsculapius was intrusted to his charge by Apollo, his father. When the sage returned to his home bearing the infant, his daughter Ocyroe came forth to meet him, and at sight of the child burst forth into a prophetic strain (for she was a prophetess), foretelling the glory that he was to achieve. Æsculapius when grown up became a renowned physician, and even in one instance succeeded in restoring the dead to life. Pluto resented this, and Jupiter, at his request, struck the bold physician with lightning, and killed him, but after his death received him into the number of the gods.\n\nChiron was the wisest and justest of all the Centaurs, and at his death Jupiter placed him among the stars as the constellation Sagittarius.\n\n# THE PYGMIES\n\nThe Pygmies were a nation of dwarfs, so called from a Greek word which means the cubit or measure of about thirteen inches, which was said to be the height of these people. They lived near the sources of the Nile, or according to others, in India. Homer tells us that the cranes used to migrate every winter to the Pygmies’ country, and their appearance was the signal of bloody warfare to the puny inhabitants, who had to take up arms to defend their cornfields against the rapacious strangers. The Pygmies and their enemies the Cranes form the subject of several works of art.\n\nLater writers tell of an army of Pygmies which finding Hercules asleep made preparations to attack him, as if they were about to attack a city. But the hero, awaking, laughed at the little warriors, wrapped some of them up in his lion’s skin, and carried them to Eurystheus.\n\nMilton uses the Pygmies for a simile, “Paradise Lost,” Book I.:\n\n“. . . like that Pygmæan race Beyond the Indian mount, or fairy elves Whose midnight revels by a forest side, Or fountain, some belated peasant sees (Or dreams he sees), while overhead the moon Sits arbitress, and nearer to the earth Wheels her pale course; they on their mirth and dance Intent, with jocund music charm his ear. At once with joy and fear his heart rebounds.”\n\n# THE GRIFFIN, OR GRYPHON\n\nThe Griffin is a monster with the body of a lion, the head and wings of an eagle, and back covered with feathers. Like birds it builds its nest, and instead of an egg lays an agate therein. It has long claws and talons of such a size that the people of that country make them into drinking-cups. India was assigned as the native country of the Griffins. They found gold in the mountains and build their nests of it, for which reason their nests were very tempting to the hunters, and they were forced to keep vigilant guard over them. Their instinct led them to know where buried treasures lay, and they did their best to keep plunderers at a distance. The Arimaspians, among whom the Griffins flourished, were a one-eyed people of Scythia.\n\nMilton borrows a simile from the Griffins, “Paradise Lost,” Book II.:\n\n“As when a Gryphon through the wilderness, With winged course, o’er hill and moory dale, Pursues the Arimaspian who by stealth Hath from his wakeful custody purloined His guarded gold,” etc.\n————\n\n# THE GOLDEN FLEECE\n\nIN very ancient times there lived in Thessaly a king and queen named Athamas and Nephele. They had two children, a boy and a girl. After a time Athamas grew indifferent to his wife, put her away, and took another. Nephele suspected danger to her children from the influence of the stepmother, and took measures to send them out of her reach. Mercury assisted her, and gave her a ram with a golden fleece, on which she set the two children, trusting that the ram would convey them to a place of safety. The ram vaulted into the air with the children on his back, taking his course to the East, till when crossing the strait that divides Europe and Asia, the girl, whose name was Helle, fell from his back into the sea, which from her was called the Hellespont,—now the Dardanelles. The ram continued his career till he reached the kingdom of Colchis, on the eastern shore of the Black Sea, where he safely landed the boy Phryxus, who was hospitably received by Æetes, king of the country. Phryxus sacrificed the ram to Jupiter, and gave the Golden Fleece to Æetes, who placed it in a consecrated grove, under the care of a sleepless dragon.\n\nThere was another kingdom in Thessaly near to that of Athamas, and ruled over by a relative of his. The king Æson, being tired of the cares of government, surrendered his crown to his brother Pelias on condition that he should hold it only during the minority of Jason, the son of Æson. When Jason was grown up and came to demand the crown from his uncle, Pelias pretended to be willing to yield it, but at the same time suggested to the young man the glorious adventure of going in quest of the Golden Fleece, which it was well known was in the kingdom of Colchis, and was, as Pelias pretended, the rightful property of their family. Jason was pleased with the thought, and forthwith made preparations for the expedition. At that time the only species of navigation known to the Greeks consisted of small boats or canoes hollowed out from trunks of trees, so that when Jason employed Argus to build him a vessel capable of containing fifty men, it was considered a gigantic undertaking. It was accomplished, however, and the vessel named “Argo,” from the name of the builder. Jason sent his invitation to all the adventurous young men of Greece, and soon found himself at the head of a band of bold youths, many of whom afterwards were renowned among the heroes and demigods of Greece. Hercules, Theseus, Orpheus, and Nestor were among them. They are called the Argonauts, from the name of their vessel.\n\nThe “Argo” with her crew of heroes left the shores of Thessaly and having touched at the Island of Lemnos, thence crossed to Mysia and thence to Thrace. Here they found the sage Phineus, and from him received instruction as to their future course. It seems the entrance of the Euxine Sea was impeded by two small rocky islands, which floated on the surface, and in their tossings and heavings occasionally came together, crushing and grinding to atoms any object that might be caught between them. They were called the Symplegades, or Clashing Islands. Phineus instructed the Argonauts how to pass this dangerous strait. When they reached the islands they let go a dove, which took her way between the rocks, and passed in safety, only losing some feathers of her tail. Jason and his men seized the favorable moment of the rebound, plied their oars with vigor, and passed safe through, though the islands closed behind them, and actually grazed their stern. They now rowed along the shore till they arrived at the eastern end of the sea, and landed at the kingdom of Colchis.\n\nJason made known his message to the Colchian king, Æetes, who consented to give up the golden fleece if Jason would yoke to the plough two fire-breathing bulls with brazen feet, and sow the teeth of the dragon which Cadmus had slain, and from which it was well known that a crop of armed men would spring up, who would turn their weapons against their producer. Jason accepted the conditions, and a time was set for making the experiment. Previously, however, he found means to plead his cause to Medea, daughter of the king. He promised her marriage, and as they stood before the altar of Hecate, called the goddess to witness his oath. Medea yielded, and by her aid, for she was a potent sorceress, he was furnished with a charm, by which he could encounter safely the breath of the fire-breathing bulls and the weapons of the armed men.\n\nAt the time appointed, the people assembled at the grove of Mars, and the king assumed his royal seat, while the multitude covered the hill-sides. The brazen-footed bulls rushed in, breathing fire from their nostrils that burned up the herbage as they passed. The sound was like the roar of a furnace, and the smoke like that of water upon quick-lime. Jason advanced boldly to meet them. His friends, the chosen heroes of Greece, trembled to behold him. Regardless of the burning breath, he soothed their rage with his voice, patted their necks with fearless hand, and adroitly slipped over them the yoke, and compelled them to drag the plough. The Colchians were amazed; the Greeks shouted for joy. Jason next proceeded to sow the dragon’s teeth and plough them in. And soon the crop of armed men sprang up, and, wonderful to relate! no sooner had they reached the surface than they began to brandish their weapons and rush upon Jason. The Greeks trembled for their hero, and even she who had provided him a way of safety and taught him how to use it, Medea herself, grew pale with fear. Jason for a time kept his assailants at bay with his sword and shield, till, finding their numbers overwhelming, he resorted to the charm which Medea had taught him, seized a stone and threw it in the midst of his foes. They immediately turned their arms against one another, and soon there was not one of the dragon’s brood left alive. The Greeks embraced their hero, and Medea, if she dared, would have embraced him too.\n\nIt remained to lull to sleep the dragon that guarded the fleece, and this was done by scattering over him a few drops of a preparation which Medea had supplied. At the smell he relaxed his rage, stood for a moment motionless, then shut those great round eyes, that had never been known to shut before, and turned over on his side, fast asleep. Jason seized the fleece and with his friends and Medea accompanying, hastened to their vessel before Æetes the king could arrest their departure, and made the best of their way back to Thessaly, where they arrived safe, and Jason delivered the fleece to Pelias, and dedicated the “Argo” to Neptune. What became of the fleece afterwards we do not know, but perhaps it was found after all, like many other golden prizes, not worth the trouble it had cost to procure it.\n\nThis is one of those mythological tales, says a late writer, in which there is reason to believe that a substratum of truth exists, though overlaid by a mass of fiction. It probably was the first important maritime expedition, and like the first attempts of the kind of all nations, as we know from history, was probably of a half-piratical character. If rich spoils were the result it was enough to give rise to the idea of the golden fleece.\n\nAnother suggestion of a learned mythologist, Bryant, is that it is a corrupt tradition of the story of Noah and the ark. The name “Argo” seems to countenance this, and the incident of the dove is another confirmation.\n\nPope, in his “Ode on St. Cecilia’s Day,” thus celebrates the launching of the ship “Argo,” and the power of the music of Orpheus, whom he calls the Thracian:\n\n“So when the first bold vessel dared the seas, High on the stern the Thracian raised his strain, While Argo saw her kindred trees Descend from Pelion to the main. Transported demigods stood round, And men grew heroes at the sound.”\n\nIn Dyer’s poem of “The Fleece” there is an account of the ship “Argo” and her crew, which gives a good picture of this primitive maritime adventure:\n\n“From every region of Ægea’s shore The brave assembled; those illustrious twins Castor and Pollux; Orpheus, tuneful bard; Zetes and Calais, as the wind in speed; Strong Hercules and many a chief renowned. On deep Iolcos’ sandy shore they thronged, Gleaming in armor, ardent of exploits; And soon, the laurel cord and the huge stone Uplifting to the deck, unmoored the bark; Whose keel of wondrous length the skilful hand Of Argus fashioned for the proud attempt; And in the extended keel a lofty mast Upraised, and sails full swelling; to the chiefs Unwonted objects. Now first, now they learned Their bold steerage over ocean wave, Led by the golden stars, as Chiron’s art Had marked the sphere celestial,” etc.\n\nHercules left the expedition at Mysia, for Hylas, a youth beloved by him, having gone for water, was laid hold of and kept by the nymphs of the spring, who were fascinated by his beauty. Hercules went in quest of the lad, and while he was absent the “Argo” put to sea and left him. Moore, in one of his songs, makes a beautiful allusion to this incident:\n\n“When Hylas was sent with his urn to the fount, Through fields full of light and heart full of play, Light rambled the boy over meadow and mount, And neglected his task for the flowers in the way.\n“Thus many like me, who in youth should have tasted The fountain that runs by Philosophy’s shrine, Their time with the flowers on the margin have wasted, And left their light urns all as empty as mine.”\n\nAmid the rejoicings for the recovery of the Golden Fleece, Jason felt that one thing was wanting, the presence of Æson, his father, who was prevented by his age and infirmities from taking part in them. Jason said to Medea, “My spouse, would that your arts, whose power I have seen so mighty for my aid, could do me one further service, take some years from my life and add them to my father’s.” Medea replied, “Not at such a cost shall it be done, but if my art avails me, his life shall be lengthened without abridging yours.” The next full moon she issued forth alone, while all creatures slept; not a breath stirred the foliage, and all was still. To the stars she addressed her incantations, and to the moon; to Hecate,[17] the goddess of the underworld, and to Tellus the goddess of the earth, by whose power plants potent for enchantment are produced. She invoked the gods of the woods and caverns, of mountains and valleys, of lakes and rivers, of winds and vapors. While she spoke the stars shone brighter, and presently a chariot descended through the air, drawn by flying serpents. She ascended it, and borne aloft made her way to distant regions, where potent plants grew which she knew how to select for her purpose. Nine nights she employed in her search, and during that time came not within the doors of her palace nor under any roof, and shunned all intercourse with mortals.\n\nShe next erected two altars, the one to Hecate, the other to Hebe, the goddess of youth, and sacrificed a black sheep, pouring libations of milk and wine. She implored Pluto and his stolen bride that they would not hasten to take the old man’s life. Then she directed that Æson should be led forth, and having thrown him into a deep sleep by a charm, had him laid on a bed of herbs, like one dead. Jason and all others were kept away from the place, that no profane eyes might look upon her mysteries. Then, with streaming hair, she thrice moved round the altars, dipped flaming twigs in the blood, and laid them thereon to burn. Meanwhile the caldron with its contents was got ready. In it she put magic herbs, with seeds and flowers of acrid juice, stones from the distant east, and sand from the shore of all-surrounding ocean; hoar frost, gathered by moonlight, a screech owl’s head and wings, and the entrails of a wolf. She added fragments of the shells or tortoises, and the liver of stags,—animals tenacious of life,—and the head and beak of a crow, that outlives nine generations of men. These with many other things “without a name” she boiled together for her purposed work, stirring them up with a dry olive branch; and behold! the branch when taken out instantly became green, and before long was covered with leaves and a plentiful growth of young olives; and as the liquor boiled and bubbled, and sometimes ran over, the grass wherever the sprinklings fell shot forth with a verdure like that of spring.\n\nSeeing that all was ready, Medea cut the throat of the old man and let out all his blood, and poured into his mouth and into his wound the juices of her caldron. As soon as he had completely imbibed them, his hair and beard laid by their whiteness and assumed the blackness of youth; his paleness and emaciation were gone; his veins were full of blood, his limbs of vigor and robustness. Æson is amazed at himself, and remembers that such as he now is, he was in his youthful days, forty years before.\n\nMedea used her arts here for a good purpose, but not so in another instance, where she made them the instruments of revenge. Pelias, our readers will recollect, was the usurping uncle of Jason, and had kept him out of his kingdom. Yet he must have had some good qualities, for his daughters loved him, and when they saw what Medea had done for Æson, they wished her to do the same for their father. Medea pretended to consent, and prepared her caldron as before. At her request an old sheep was brought and plunged into the caldron. Very soon a bleating was heard in the kettle, and when the cover was removed, a lamb jumped forth and ran frisking away into the meadow. The daughters of Pelias saw the experiment with delight, and appointed a time for their father to undergo the same operation. But Medea prepared her caldron for him in a very different way. She put in only water and a few simple herbs. In the night she with the sisters entered the bed chamber of the old king, while he and his guards slept soundly under the influence of a spell cast upon them by Medea. The daughters stood by the bedside with their weapons drawn, but hesitated to strike, till Medea chid their irresolution. Then turning away their faces, and giving random blows, they smote him with their weapons. He, starting from his sleep, cried out, “My daughters, what are you doing? Will you kill your father?” Their hearts failed them and their weapons fell from their hands, but Medea struck him a fatal blow, and prevented his saying more.\n\nThen they placed him in the caldron, and Medea hastened to depart in her serpent-drawn chariot before they discovered her treachery, or their vengeance would have been terrible. She escaped, however, but had little enjoyment of the fruits of her crime. Jason, for whom she had done so much, wishing to marry Creusa, princess of Corinth, put away Medea. She, enraged at his ingratitude, called on the gods for vengeance, sent a poisoned robe as a gift to the bride, and then killing her own children, and setting fire to the palace, mounted her serpent-drawn chariot and fled to Athens, where she married King Ægeus, the father of Theseus, and we shall meet her again when we come to the adventures of that hero.\n\nThe incantations of Medea will remind the reader of those of the witches in “Macbeth.” The following lines are those which seem most strikingly to recall the ancient model:\n\n“Round about the caldron go; In the poisoned entrails throw. . . . . . . . Fillet of a fenny snake In the caldron boil and bake; Eye of newt and toe of frog, Wool of bat and tongue of dog, Adder’s fork and blind-worm’s sting, Lizard’s leg and howlet’s wing: . . . . . . . Maw of ravening salt-sea shark, Root of hemlock digged in the dark,” etc. —Macbeth, Act IV., Scene 1\n\nAnd again:\nMacbeth.—What is’t you do? Witches.—A deed without a name.\n\nThere is another story of Medea almost too revolting for record even of a sorceress, a class of persons to whom both ancient and modern poets have been accustomed to attribute every degree of atrocity. In her flight from Colchis she had taken her young brother Absyrtus with her. Finding the pursuing vessels of Æetes gaining upon the Argonauts, she caused the lad to be killed and his limbs to be strewn over the sea. Æetes on reaching the place found these sorrowful traces of his murdered son; but while he tarried to collect the scattered fragments and bestow upon them an honorable interment, the Argonauts escaped.\n\nIn the poems of Campbell will be found a translation of one of the choruses of the tragedy of “Medea,” where the poet Euripides has taken advantage of the occasion to pay a glowing tribute to Athens, his native city. It begins thus:\n\n“O haggard queen! to Athens dost thou guide Thy glowing chariot, steeped in kindred gore; Or seek to hide thy damned parricide Where peace and justice dwell for evermore?”\n————\n\n# MELEAGER AND ATALANTA\n\nONE of the heroes of the Argonautic expedition was Meleager, son of Œneus and Althea, king and queen of Calydon. Althea, when her son was born, beheld the three destinies, who, as they spun their fatal thread, foretold that the life of the child should last no longer than a brand then burning upon the hearth. Althea seized and quenched the brand, and carefully preserved it for years, while Meleager grew to boyhood, youth, and manhood. It chanced, then, that Œneus, as he offered sacrifices to the gods, omitted to pay due honors to Diana; and she, indignant at the neglect, sent a wild boar of enormous size to lay waste the fields of Calydon. Its eyes shone with blood and fire, its bristles stood like threatening spears, its tusks were like those of Indian elephants. The growing corn was trampled, the vines and olive trees laid waste, the flocks and herds were driven in wild confusion by the slaughtering foe. All common aid seemed vain; but Meleager called on the heroes of Greece to join in a bold hunt for the ravenous monster. Theseus and his friend Pirithous, Jason, Peleus, afterwards the father of Achilles, Telamon the father of Ajax, Nestor, then a youth, but who in his age bore arms with Achilles and Ajax in the Trojan war,—these and many more joined in the enterprise. With them came Atalanta, the daughter of Iasius, king of Arcadia. A buckle of polished gold confined her vest, an ivory quiver hung on her left shoulder, and her left hand bore the bow. Her face blent feminine beauty with the best graces of martial youth. Meleager saw and loved.\n\nBut now already they were near the monster’s lair. They stretched strong nets from tree to tree; they uncoupled their dogs, they tried to find the footprints of their quarry in the grass. From the wood was a descent to marshy ground. Here the boar, as he lay among the reeds, heard the shouts of his pursuers, and rushed forth against them. One and another is thrown down and slain. Jason throws his spear, with a prayer to Diana for success; and the favoring goddess allows the weapon to touch, but not to wound, removing the steel point of the spear in its flight. Nestor, assailed, seeks and finds safety in the branches of a tree. Telamon rushes on, but stumbling at a projecting root, falls prone. But an arrow from Atalanta at length for the first time tastes the monster’s blood. It is a slight wound, but Meleager sees and joyfully proclaims it. Anceus, excited to envy by the praise given to a female, loudly proclaims his own valor, and defies alike the boar and the goddess who had sent it; but as he rushes on, the infuriated beast lays him low with a mortal wound. Theseus throws his lance, but it is turned aside by a projecting bough. The dart of Jason misses its object, and kills instead one of their own dogs. But Meleager, after one unsuccessful stroke, drives his spear into the monster’s side, then rushes on and despatches him with repeated blows.\n\nThen rose a shout from those around; they congratulated the conqueror, crowding to touch his hand. He, placing his foot upon the head of the slain boar, turned to Atalanta and bestowed on her the head and the rough hide which were the trophies of his success. But at this, envy excited the rest to strife. Plexippus and Toxeus, the brothers of Meleager’s mother, beyond the rest opposed the gift, and snatched from the maiden the trophy she had received. Meleager, kindling with rage at the wrong done to himself, and still more at the insult offered to her whom he loved, forgot the claims of kindred, and plunged his sword into the offenders’ hearts.\n\nAs Althea bore gifts of thankfulness to the temples for the victory of her son, the bodies of her murdered brothers met her sight. She shrieks, and beats her breast, and hastens to change the garments of rejoicing for those of mourning. But when the author of the deed is known, grief gives way to the stern desire of vengeance on her son. The fatal brand, which once she rescued from the flames, the brand which the destinies had linked with Meleager’s life, she brings forth, and commands a fire to be prepared. Then four times she essays to place the brand upon the pile; four times draws back, shuddering at the thought of bringing destruction on her son. The feelings of the mother and the sister contend within her. Now she is pale at the thought of the proposed deed, now flushed again with anger at the act of her son. As a vessel, driven in one direction by the wind, and in the opposite by the tide, the mind of Althea hangs suspended in uncertainty. But now the sister prevails above the mother, and she begins as she holds the fatal wood: “Turn, ye Furies, goddesses of punishment! turn to behold the sacrifice I bring! Crime must atone for crime. Shall Œneus rejoice in his victor son, while the house of Thestius is desolate? But, alas! to what deed am I borne along? Brothers forgive a mother’s weakness! my hand fails me. He deserves death, but not that I should destroy him. But shall he then live, and triumph, and reign over Calydon, while you, my brothers, wander unavenged among the shades? No! thou hast lived by my gift; die, now, for thine own crime. Return the life which twice I gave thee, first at thy birth, again when I snatched this brand from the flames. O that thou hadst then died! Alas! evil is the conquest; but, brothers, ye have conquered.” And, turning away her face, she threw the fatal wood upon the burning pile.\n\nIt gave, or seemed to give, a deadly groan. Meleager, absent and unknowing of the cause, felt a sudden pang. He burns, and only by courageous pride conquers the pain which destroys him. He mourns only that he perishes by a bloodless and unhonored death. With his last breath he calls upon his aged father, his brother, and his fond sisters, upon his beloved Atalanta, and upon his mother, the unknown cause of his fate. The flames increase, and with them the pain of the hero. Now both subside; now both are quenched. The brand is ashes, and the life of Meleager is breathed forth to the wandering winds.\n\nAlthea, when the deed was done, laid violent hands upon herself. The sisters of Meleager mourned their brother with uncontrollable grief; till Diana, pitying the sorrows of the house that once had aroused her anger, turned them into birds.\n\nThe innocent cause of so much sorrow was a maiden whose face you might truly say was boyish for a girl, yet too girlish for a boy. Her fortune had been told, and it was to this effect: “Atalanta, do not marry; marriage will be your ruin.” Terrified by this oracle, she fled the society of men, and devoted herself to the sports of the chase. To all suitors (for she had many) she imposed a condition which was generally effectual in relieving her of their persecutions,—“I will be the prize of him who shall conquer me in the race; but death must be the penalty of all who try and fail.” In spite of this hard condition some would try. Hippomenes was to be judge of the race. “Can it be possible that any will be so rash as to risk so much for a wife?” said he. But when he saw her lay aside her robe for the race, he changed his mind, and said, “Pardon me, youths, I knew not the prize you were competing for.” As he surveyed them he wished them all to be beaten, and swelled with envy of any one that seemed at all likely to win. While such were his thoughts, the virgin darted forward. As she ran she looked more beautiful than ever. The breezes seemed to give wings to her feet; her hair flew over her shoulders, and the gay fringe of her garment fluttered behind her. A ruddy hue tinged the whiteness of her skin, such as a crimson curtain casts on a marble wall. All her competitors were distanced, and were put to death without mercy. Hippomenes, not daunted by this result, fixing his eyes on the virgin, said, “Why boast of beating those laggards? I offer myself for the contest.” Atalanta looked at him with a pitying countenance, and hardly knew whether she would rather conquer him or not. “What god can tempt one so young and handsome to throw himself away? I pity him, not for his beauty (yet he is beautiful), but for his youth. I wish he would give up the race, or if he will be so mad, I hope he may outrun me.” While she hesitates, revolving these thoughts, the spectators grow impatient for the race, and her father prompts her to prepare. Then Hippomenes addressed a prayer to Venus: “Help me, Venus, for you have led me on.” Venus heard and was propitious.\n\nIn the garden of her temple, in her own island of Cyprus, is a tree with yellow leaves and yellow branches and golden fruit. Hence she gathered three golden apples, and, unseen by any one else, gave them to Hippomenes, and told him how to use them. The signal is given; each starts from the goal and skims over the sand. So light their tread, you would almost have thought they might run over the river surface or over the waving grain without sinking. The cries of the spectators cheered Hippomenes,—“Now, now, do your best! haste, haste! you gain on her! relax not! one more effort!” It was doubtful whether the youth or the maiden heard these cries with the greater pleasure. But his breath began to fail him, his throat was dry, the goal yet far off. At that moment he threw down one of the golden apples. The virgin was all amazement. She stopped to pick it up. Hippomenes shot ahead. Shouts burst forth from all sides. She redoubled her efforts, and soon overtook him. Again he threw an apple. She stopped again, but again came up with him. The goal was near; one chance only remained. “Now, goddess,” said he, “prosper your gift!” and threw the last apple off at one side. She looked at it, and hesitated; Venus impelled her to turn aside for it. She did so, and was vanquished. The youth carried off his prize.\n\nBut the lovers were so full of their own happiness that they forgot to pay due honor to Venus; and the goddess was provoked at their ingratitude. She caused them to give offence to Cybele. That powerful goddess was not to be insulted with impunity. She took from them their human form and turned them into animals of characters resembling their own: of the huntress-heroine, triumphing in the blood of her lovers, she made a lioness, and of her lord and master a lion, and yoked them to her car, where they are still to be seen in all representations, in statuary or painting, of the goddess Cybele.\n\nCybele is the Latin name of the goddess called by the Greeks Rhea and Ops. She was the wife of Cronos and mother of Zeus. In works of art she exhibits the matronly air which distinguishes Juno and Ceres. Sometimes she is veiled, and seated on a throne with lions at her side, at other times riding in a chariot drawn by lions. She wears a mural crown, that is, a crown whose rim is carved in the form of towers and battlements. Her priests were called Corybantes.\n\nByron, in describing the city of Venice, which is built on a low island in the Adriatic Sea, borrows an illustration from Cybele:\n\n“She looks a sea-Cybele fresh from ocean, Rising with her tiara of proud towers At airy distance, with majestic motion, A ruler of the waters and their powers.” —Childe Harold, IV.\n\nIn Moore’s “Rhymes on the Road,” the poet, speaking of Alpine scenery, alludes to the story of Atalanta and Hippomenes thus:\n\n“Even here, in this region of wonders, I find That light-footed Fancy leaves Truth far behind, Or at least, like Hippomenes, turns her astray By the golden illusions he flings in her way.”\n————\n\nHERCULES was the son of Jupiter and Alcmena. As Juno was always hostile to the offspring of her husband by mortal mothers, she declared war against Hercules from his birth. She sent two serpents to destroy him as he lay in his cradle, but the precocious infant strangled them with his own hands. He was, however, by the arts of Juno rendered subject to Eurystheus and compelled to perform all his commands. Eurystheus enjoined upon him a succession of desperate adventures, which are called the “Twelve Labors of Hercules.” The first was the fight with the Nemean lion. The valley of Nemea was infested by a terrible lion. Eurystheus ordered Hercules to bring him the skin of this monster. After using in vain his club and arrows against the lion, Hercules strangled the animal with his hands. He returned carrying the dead lion on his shoulders; but Eurystheus was so frightened at the sight of it and at this proof of the prodigious strength of the hero, that he ordered him to deliver the account of his exploits in future outside the town.\n\nHis next labor was the slaughter of the Hydra. This monster ravaged the country of Argos, and dwelt in a swamp near the well of Amymone. This well had been discovered by Amymone when the country was suffering from drought, and the story was that Neptune, who loved her, had permitted her to touch the rock with his trident, and a spring of three outlets burst forth. Here the Hydra took up his position, and Hercules was sent to destroy him. The Hydra had nine heads, of which the middle one was immortal. Hercules struck off its heads with his club, but in the place of the head knocked off, two new ones grew forth each time. At length with the assistance of his faithful servant Iolaus, he burned away the heads of the Hydra, and buried the ninth or immortal one under a huge rock.\n\nAnother labor was the cleaning of the Augean stables. Augeas, king of Elis, had a herd of three thousand oxen, whose stalls had not been cleansed for thirty years. Hercules brought the rivers Alpheus and Peneus through them, and cleansed them thoroughly in one day.\n\nHis next labor was of a more delicate kind. Admeta, the daughter of Eurystheus, longed to obtain the girdle of the queen of the Amazons, and Eurystheus ordered Hercules to go and get it. The Amazons were a nation of women. They were very warlike and held several flourishing cities. It was their custom to bring up only the female children; the boys were either sent away to the neighboring nations or put to death. Hercules was accompanied by a number of volunteers, and after various adventures at last reached the country of the Amazons. Hippolyta, the queen, received him kindly, and consented to yield him her girdle, but Juno, taking the form of an Amazon, went and persuaded the rest that the strangers were carrying off their queen. They instantly armed and came in great numbers down to the ship. Hercules, thinking that Hippolyta had acted treacherously, slew her, and taking her girdle made sail homewards.\n\nAnother task enjoined him was to bring to Eurystheus the oxen of Geryon, a monster with three bodies, who dwelt in the island Erytheia (the red), so called because it lay at the west, under the rays of the setting sun. This description is thought to apply to Spain, of which Geryon was king. After traversing various countries, Hercules reached at length the frontiers of Libya and Europe, where he raised the two mountains of Calpe and Abyla, as monuments of his progress, or, according to another account, rent one mountain into two and left half on each side, forming the straits of Gibraltar, the two mountains being called the Pillars of Hercules. The oxen were guarded by the giant Eurytion and his two-headed dog, but Hercules killed the giant and his dog and brought away the oxen in safety to Eurystheus.\n\nThe most difficult labor of all was getting the golden apples of the Hesperides, for Hercules did not know where to find them. These were the apples which Juno had received at her wedding from the goddess of the Earth, and which she had intrusted to the keeping of the daughters of Hesperus, assisted by a watchful dragon. After various adventures Hercules arrived at Mount Atlas in Africa. Atlas was one of the Titans who had warred against the gods, and after they were subdued, Atlas was condemned to bear on his shoulders the weight of the heavens. He was the father of the Hesperides, and Hercules thought might, if any one could, find the apples and bring them to him. But how to send Atlas away from his post, or bear up the heavens while he was gone? Hercules took the burden on his own shoulders, and sent Atlas to seek the apples. He returned with them, and though somewhat reluctantly, took his burden upon his shoulders again, and let Hercules return with the apples to Eurystheus.\n\nMilton, in his “Comus,” makes the Hesperides the daughters of Hesperus and nieces of Atlas:\n\n“. . . amidst the gardens fair Of Hesperus and his daughters three, That sing about the golden tree.”\n\nThe poets, led by the analogy of the lovely appearance of the western sky at sunset, viewed the west as a region of brightness and glory. Hence they placed in it the Isles of the Blest, the ruddy Isle Erythea, on which the bright oxen of Geryon were pastured, and the Isle of the Hesperides. The apples are supposed by some to be the oranges of Spain, of which the Greeks had heard some obscure accounts.\n\nA celebrated exploit of Hercules was his victory over Antæus. Antæus, the son of Terra, the Earth, was a mighty giant and wrestler, whose strength was invincible so long as he remained in contact with his mother Earth. He compelled all strangers who came to his country to wrestle with him, on condition that if conquered (as they all were) they should be put to death. Hercules encountered him, and finding that it was of no avail to throw him, for he always rose with renewed strength from every fall, he lifted him up from the earth and strangled him in the air.\n\nCacus was a huge giant, who inhabited a cave on Mount Aventine, and plundered the surrounding country. When Hercules was driving home the oxen of Geryon, Cacus stole part of the cattle, while the hero slept. That their footprints might not serve to show where they had been driven, he dragged them backward by their tails to his cave; so their tracks all seemed to show that they had gone in the opposite direction. Hercules was deceived by this stratagem, and would have failed to find his oxen, if it had not happened that in driving the remainder of the herd past the cave where the stolen ones were concealed, those within began to low, and were thus discovered. Cacus was slain by Hercules.\n\nThe last exploit we shall record was bringing Cerberus from the lower world. Hercules descended into Hades, accompanied by Mercury and Minerva. He obtained permission from Pluto to carry Cerberus to the upper air, provided he could do it without the use of weapons; and in spite of the monster’s struggling, he seized him, held him fast, and carried him to Eurystheus, and afterwards brought him back again. When he was in Hades he obtained the liberty of Theseus, his admirer and imitator, who had been detained a prisoner there for an unsuccessful attempt to carry off Proserpine.\n\nHercules in a fit of madness killed his friend Iphitus, and was condemned for this offence to become the slave of Queen Omphale for three years. While in this service the hero’s nature seemed changed. He lived effeminately, wearing at times the dress of a woman, and spinning wool with the hand-maidens of Omphale, while the queen wore his lion’s skin. When this service was ended he married Dejanira and lived in peace with her three years. On one occasion as he was travelling with his wife, they came to a river, across which the Centaur Nessus carried travellers for a stated fee. Hercules himself forded the river, but gave Dejanira to Nessus to be carried across. Nessus attempted to run away with her, but Hercules heard her cries and shot an arrow into the heart of Nessus. The dying Centaur told Dejanira to take a portion of his blood and keep it, as it might be used as a charm to preserve the love of her husband.\n\nDejanira did so and before long fancied she had occasion to use it. Hercules in one of his conquests had taken prisoner a fair maiden, named Iole, of whom he seemed more fond than Dejanira approved. When Hercules was about to offer sacrifices to the gods in honor of his victory, he sent to his wife for a white robe to use on the occasion. Dejanira, thinking it a good opportunity to try her love-spell, steeped the garment in the blood of Nessus. We are to suppose she took care to wash out all traces of it, but the magic power remained, and as soon as the garment became warm on the body of Hercules the poison penetrated into all his limbs and caused him the most intense agony. In his frenzy he seized Lichas, Who had brought him the fatal robe, and hurled him into the sea. He wrenched off the garment, but it stuck to his flesh, and with it he tore away whole pieces of his body. In this state he embarked on board a ship and was conveyed home. Dejanira, on seeing what she had unwittingly done, hung herself. Hercules, prepared to die, ascended Mount Œta, where he built a funeral pile of trees, gave his bow and arrows to Philoctetes, and laid himself down on the pile, his head resting on his club, and his lion’s skin spread over him. With a countenance as serene as if he were taking his place at a festal board he commanded Philoctetes to apply the torch. The flames spread apace and soon invested the whole mass.\n\nMilton thus alludes to the frenzy of Hercules:\n\n“As when Alcides,[18] from Œchalia crowned With conquest, felt the envenomed robe, and tore, Through pain, up by the roots Thessalian pines And Lichas from the top of Œta threw Into the Euboic Sea.”\n\nThe gods themselves felt troubled at seeing the champion of the earth so brought to his end. But Jupiter with cheerful countenance thus addressed them: “I am pleased to see your concern, my princes, and am gratified to perceive that I am the ruler of a loyal people, and that my son enjoys your favor. For although your interest in him arises from his noble deeds, yet it is not the less gratifying to me. But now I say to you, Fear not. He who conquered all else is not to be conquered by those flames which you see blazing on Mount Œta. Only his mother’s share in him can perish; what he derived from me is immortal. I shall take him, dead to earth, to the heavenly shores, and I require of you all to receive him kindly. If any of you feel grieved at his attaining this honor, yet no one can deny that he has deserved it.” The gods all gave their assent; Juno only heard the closing words with some displeasure that she should be so particularly pointed at, yet not enough to make her regret the determination of her husband. So when the flames had consumed the mother’s share of Hercules, the diviner part, instead of being injured thereby, seemed to start forth with new vigor, to assume a more lofty port and a more awful dignity. Jupiter enveloped him in a cloud, and took him up in a four-horse chariot to dwell among the stars. As he took his place in heaven, Atlas felt the added weight.\n\nJuno, now reconciled to him, gave him her daughter Hebe in marriage.\n\nThe poet Schiller, in one of his pieces called the “Ideal and Life,” illustrates the contrast between the practical and the imaginative in some beautiful stanzas, of which the last two may be thus translated:\n\n“Deep degraded to a coward’s slave, Endless contests bore Alcides brave, Through the thorny path of suffering led; Slew the Hydra, crushed the lion’s might, Threw himself, to bring his friend to light, Living, in the skiff that bears the dead. All the torments, every toil of earth Juno’s hatred on him could impose, Well he bore them, from his fated birth To life’s grandly mournful close.\n\n“Till the god, the earthly part forsaken, From the man in flames asunder taken, Drank the heavenly ether’s purer breath. Joyous in the new unwonted lightness, Soared he upwards to celestial brightness, Earth’s dark heavy burden lost in death. High Olympus gives harmonious greeting To the hall where reigns his sire adored; Youth’s bright goddess, with a blush at meeting, Gives the nectar to her lord.” —S. G. B.\n\n# HEBE AND GANYMEDE\n\nHebe, the daughter of Juno, and goddess of youth, was cup-bearer to the gods. The usual story is that she resigned her office on becoming the wife of Hercules. But there is another statement which our countryman Crawford, the sculptor, has adopted in his group of Hebe and Ganymede, now in the Athenæum gallery. According to this, Hebe was dismissed from her office in consequence of a fall which she met with one day when in attendance on the gods. Her successor was Ganymede, a Trojan boy, whom Jupiter, in the disguise of an eagle, seized and carried off from the midst of his playfellows on Mount Ida, bore up to heaven, and installed in the vacant place.\n\nTennyson, in his “Palace of Art,” describes among the decorations on the walls a picture representing this legend:\n\n“There, too, flushed Ganymede, his rosy thigh Half buried in the eagle’s down, Sole as a flying star shot through the sky Above the pillared town.”\n\nAnd in Shelley’s “Prometheus” Jupiter calls to his cup-bearer thus:\n\n“Pour forth heaven’s wine, Idæan Ganymede, And let it fill the Dædal cups like fire.”\n\nThe beautiful legend of the “Choice of Hercules” may be found in the “Tatler,” No. 97.\n\n————\n\nTHESEUS was the son of Ægeus, king of Athens, and of Æthra, daughter of the king of Trœzen. He was brought up at Trœzen, and when arrived at manhood was to proceed to Athens and present himself to his father. Ægeus on parting from Æthra, before the birth of his son, placed his sword and shoes under a large stone and directed her to send his son to him when he became strong enough to roll away the stone and take them from under it. When she thought the time had come, his mother led Theseus to the stone, and he removed it with ease and took the sword and shoes. As the roads were infested with robbers, his grandfather pressed him earnestly to take the shorter and safer way to his father’s country—by sea; but the youth, feeling in himself the spirit and the soul of a hero, and eager to signalize himself like Hercules, with whose fame all Greece then rang, by destroying the evil-doers and monsters that oppressed the country, determined on the more perilous and adventurous journey by land.\n\nHis first day’s journey brought him to Epidaurus, where dwelt a man named Periphetes, a son of Vulcan. This ferocious savage always went armed with a club of iron, and all travellers stood in terror of his violence. When he saw Theseus approach he assailed him, but speedily fell beneath the blows of the young hero, who took possession of his club and bore it ever afterwards as a memorial of his first victory.\n\nSeveral similar contests with the petty tyrants and marauders of the country followed, in all of which Theseus was victorious. One of these evil-doers was called Procrustes, or the Stretcher. He had an iron bedstead, on which he used to tie all travellers who fell into his hands. If they were shorter than the bed, he stretched their limbs to make them fit it; if they were longer than the bed, he lopped off a portion. Theseus served him as he had served others.\n\nHaving overcome all the perils of the road, Theseus at length reached Athens, where new dangers awaited him. Medea, the sorceress, who had fled from Corinth after her separation from Jason, had become the wife of Ægeus, the father of Theseus. Knowing by her arts who he was, and fearing the loss of her influence with her husband if Theseus should be acknowledged as his son, she filled the mind of Ægeus with suspicions of the young stranger, and induced him to present him a cup of poison; but at the moment when Theseus stepped forward to take it, the sight of the sword which he wore discovered to his father who he was, and prevented the fatal draught. Medea, detected in her arts, fled once more from deserved punishment, and arrived in Asia, where the country afterwards called Medea received its name from her. Theseus was acknowledged by his father, and declared his successor.\n\nThe Athenians were at that time in deep affliction, on account of the tribute which they were forced to pay to Minos, king of Crete. This tribute consisted of seven youths and seven maidens, who were sent every year to be devoured by the Minotaur, a monster with a bull’s body and a human head. It was exceedingly strong and fierce, and was kept in a labyrinth constructed by Dædalus, so artfully contrived that whoever was enclosed in it could by no means find his way out unassisted. Here the Minotaur roamed, and was fed with human victims.\n\nTheseus resolved to deliver his countrymen from this calamity, or to die in the attempt. Accordingly, when the time of sending off the tribute came, and the youths and maidens were, according to custom, drawn by lot to be sent, he offered himself as one of the victims, in spite of the entreaties of his father. The ship departed under black sails, as usual, which Theseus promised his father to change for white, in case of his returning victorious. When they arrived in Crete, the youths and maidens were exhibited before Minos; and Ariadne, the daughter of the king, being present, became deeply enamored of Theseus, by whom her love was readily returned. She furnished him with a sword, with which to encounter the Minotaur, and with a clew of thread by which he might find his way out of the labyrinth. He was successful, slew the Minotaur, escaped from the labyrinth, and taking Ariadne as the companion of his way, with his rescued companions sailed for Athens. On their way they stopped at the island of Naxos, where Theseus abandoned Ariadne, leaving her asleep.[19] His excuse for this ungrateful treatment of his benefactress was that Minerva appeared to him in a dream and commanded him to do so.\n\nOn approaching the coast of Attica, Theseus forgot the signal appointed by his father, and neglected to raise the white sails, and the old king, thinking his son had perished, put an end to his own life. Theseus thus became king of Athens.\n\nOne of the most celebrated of the adventures of Theseus is his expedition against the Amazons. He assailed them before they had recovered from the attack of Hercules, and carried off their queen Antiope. The Amazons in their turn invaded the country of Athens and penetrated into the city itself; and the final battle in which Theseus overcame them was fought in the very midst of the city. This battle was one of the favorite subjects of the ancient sculptors, and is commemorated in several works of art that are still extant.\n\nThe friendship between Theseus and Pirithous was of a most intimate nature, yet it originated in the midst of arms. Pirithous had made an irruption into the plain of Marathon, and carried off the herds of the king of Athens. Theseus went to repel the plunderers. The moment Pirithous beheld him, he was seized with admiration; he stretched out his hand as a token of peace, and cried, “Be judge thyself—what satisfaction dost thou require?” “Thy friendship,” replied the Athenian, and they swore inviolable fidelity. Their deeds corresponded to their professions, and they ever continued true brothers in arms. Each of them aspired to espouse a daughter of Jupiter. Theseus fixed his choice on Helen, then but a child, afterwards so celebrated as the cause of the Trojan war, and with the aid of his friend he carried her off. Pirithous aspired to the wife of the monarch of Erebus; and Theseus, though aware of the danger, accompanied the ambitious lover in his descent to the under-world. But Pluto seized and set them on an enchanted rock at his palace gate, where they remained till Hercules arrived and liberated Theseus, leaving Pirithous to his fate.\n\nAfter the death of Antiope, Theseus married Phædra, daughter of Minos, king of Crete. Phædra saw in Hippolytus, the son of Theseus, a youth endowed with all the graces and virtues of his father, and of an age corresponding to her own. She loved him, but he repulsed her advances, and her love was changed to hate. She used her influence over her infatuated husband to cause him to be jealous of his son, and he imprecated the vengeance of Neptune upon him. As Hippolytus was one day driving his chariot along the shore, a sea-monster raised himself above the waters, and frightened the horses so that they ran away and dashed the chariot to pieces. Hippolytus was killed, but by Diana’s assistance Æsculapius restored him to life. Diana removed Hippolytus from the power of his deluded father and false stepmother, and placed him in Italy under the protection of the nymph Egeria.\n\nTheseus at length lost the favor of his people, and retired to the court of Lycomedes, king of Scyros, who at first received him kindly, but afterwards treacherously slew him. In a later age the Athenian general Cimon discovered the place where his remains were laid, and caused them to be removed to Athens, where they were deposited in a temple called the Theseum, erected in honor of the hero.\n\nThe queen of the Amazons whom Theseus espoused is by some called Hippolyta. That is the name she bears in Shakspeare’s “Midsummer Night’s Dream,”—the subject of which is the festivities attending the nuptials of Theseus and Hippolyta.\n\nMrs. Hemans has a poem on the ancient Greek tradition that the “Shade of Theseus” appeared strengthening his countrymen at the battle of Marathon.\n\nTheseus is a semi-historical personage. It is recorded of him that he united the several tribes by whom the territory of Attica was then possessed into one state, of which Athens was the capital. In commemoration of this important event, he instituted the festival of Panathenæa, in honor of Minerva, the patron deity of Athens. This festival differed from the other Grecian games chiefly in two particulars. It was peculiar to the Athenians, and its chief feature was a solemn procession in which the Peplus, or sacred robe of Minerva, was carried to the Parthenon, and suspended before the statue of the goddess. The Peplus was covered with embroidery, worked by select virgins of the noblest families in Athens. The procession consisted of persons of all ages and both sexes. The old men carried olive branches in their hands, and the young men bore arms. The young women carried baskets on their heads, containing the sacred utensils, cakes, and all things necessary for the sacrifices. The procession formed the subject of the bas-reliefs which embellished the outside of the temple of the Parthenon. A considerable portion of these sculptures is now in the British Museum among those known as the “Elgin marbles.”\n\n# OLYMPIC AND OTHER GAMES\n\nIt seems not inappropriate to mention here the other celebrated national games of the Greeks. The first and most distinguished were the Olympic, founded, it was said, by Jupiter himself. They were celebrated at Olympia in Elis. Vast numbers of spectators flocked to them from every part of Greece, and from Asia, Africa, and Sicily. They were repeated every fifth year in midsummer, and continued five days. They gave rise to the custom of reckoning time and dating events by Olympiads. The first Olympiad is generally considered as corresponding with the year 776 B.C. The Pythian games were celebrated in the vicinity of Delphi, the Isthmian on the Corinthian isthmus, the Nemean at Nemea, a city of Argolis.\n\nThe exercises in these games were of five sorts: running, leaping, wrestling, throwing the quoit, and hurling the javelin, or boxing. Besides these exercises of bodily strength and agility, there were contests in music, poetry, and eloquence. Thus these games furnished poets, musicians, and authors the best opportunities to present their productions to the public, and the fame of the victors was diffused far and wide.\n\nThe labyrinth from which Theseus escaped by means of the clew of Ariadne was built by Dædalus, a most skilful artificer. It was an edifice with numberless winding passages and turnings opening into one another, and seeming to have neither beginning nor end, like the river Mæander, which returns on itself, and flows now onward, now backward, in its course to the sea. Dædalus built the labyrinth for King Minos, but afterwards lost the favor of the king, and was shut up in a tower. He contrived to make his escape from his prison, but could not leave the island by sea, as the king kept strict watch on all the vessels, and permitted none to sail without being carefully searched. “Minos may control the land and sea,” said Dædalus, “but not the regions of the air. I will try that way.” So he set to work to fabricate wings for himself and his young son Icarus. He wrought feathers together, beginning with the smallest and adding larger, so as to form an increasing surface. The larger ones he secured with thread and the smaller with wax, and gave the whole a gentle curvature like the wings of a bird. Icarus, the boy, stood and looked on, sometimes running to gather up the feathers which the wind had blown away, and then handling the wax and working it over with his fingers, by his play impeding his father in his labors. When at last the work was done, the artist, waving his wings, found himself buoyed upward, and hung suspended, poising himself on the beaten air. He next equipped his son in the same manner, and taught him how to fly, as a bird tempts her young ones from the lofty nest into the air. When all was prepared for flight he said, “Icarus, my son, I charge you to keep at a moderate height, for if you fly too low the damp will clog your wings, and if too high the heat will melt them. Keep near me and you will be safe.” While he gave him these instructions and fitted the wings to his shoulders, the face of the father was wet with tears, and his hands trembled. He kissed the boy, not knowing that it was for the last time. Then rising on his wings, he flew off, encouraging him to follow, and looked back from his own flight to see how his son managed his wings. As they flew the ploughman stopped his work to gaze, and the shepherd leaned on his staff and watched them, astonished at the sight, and thinking they were gods who could thus cleave the air.\n\nThey passed Samos and Delos on the left and Lebynthos on the right, when the boy, exulting in his career, began to leave the guidance of his companion and soar upward as if to reach heaven. The nearness of the blazing sun softened the wax which held the feathers together, and they came off. He fluttered with his arms, but no feathers remained to hold the air. While his mouth uttered cries to his father it was submerged in the blue waters of the sea, which thenceforth was called by his name. His father cried, “Icarus, Icarus, where are you?” At last he saw the feathers floating on the water, and bitterly lamenting his own arts, he buried the body and called the land Icaria in memory of his child. Dædalus arrived safe in Sicily, where he built a temple to Apollo, and hung up his wings, an offering to the god.\n\nDædalus was so proud of his achievements that he could not bear the idea of a rival. His sister had placed her son Perdix under his charge to be taught the mechanical arts. He was an apt scholar and gave striking evidences of ingenuity. Walking on the seashore he picked up the spine of a fish. Imitating it, he took a piece of iron and notched it on the edge, and thus invented the saw. He put two pieces of iron together, connecting them at one end with a rivet, and sharpening the other ends, and made a pair of compasses. Dædalus was so envious of his nephew’s performances that he took an opportunity, when they were together one day on the top of a high tower, to push him off. But Minerva, who favors ingenuity, saw him falling, and arrested his fate by changing him into a bird called after his name, the Partridge. This bird does not build his nest in the trees, nor take lofty flights, but nestles in the hedges, and mindful of his fall, avoids high places.\n\nThe death of Icarus is told in the following lines by Darwin:\n\n“. . . with melting wax and loosened strings Sunk hapless Icarus on unfaithful wings; Headlong he rushed through the affrighted air, With limbs distorted and dishevelled hair; His scattered plumage danced upon the wave, And sorrowing Nereids decked his watery grave; O’er his pale corse their pearly sea-flowers shed, And strewed with crimson moss his marble bed; Struck in their coral towers the passing bell, And wide in ocean tolled his echoing knell.”\n\n# CASTOR AND POLLUX\n\nCastor and Pollux were the offspring of Leda and the Swan, under which disguise Jupiter had concealed himself. Leda gave birth to an egg from which sprang the twins. Helen, so famous afterwards as the cause of the Trojan war, was their sister.\n\nWhen Theseus and his friend Pirithous had carried off Helen from Sparta, the youthful heroes Castor and Pollux, with their followers, hastened to her rescue. Theseus was absent from Attica and the brothers were successful in recovering their sister.\n\nCastor was famous for taming and managing horses, and Pollux for skill in boxing. They were united by the warmest affection and inseparable in all their enterprises. They accompanied the Argonautic expedition. During the voyage a storm arose, and Orpheus prayed to the Samothracian gods, and played on his harp, whereupon the storm ceased and stars appeared on the heads of the brothers. From this incident, Castor and Pollux came afterwards to be considered the patron deities of seamen and voyagers, and the lambent flames, which in certain states of the atmosphere play round the sails and masts of vessels, were called by their names.\n\nAfter the Argonautic expedition, we find Castor and Pollux engaged in a war with Idas and Lynceus. Castor was slain, and Pollux, inconsolable for the loss of his brother, besought Jupiter to be permitted to give his own life as a ransom for him. Jupiter so far consented as to allow the two brothers to enjoy the boon of life alternately, passing one day under the earth and the next in the heavenly abodes. According to another form of the story, Jupiter rewarded the attachment of the brothers by placing them among the stars as Gemini the Twins.\n\nThey received divine honors under the name of Dioscuri (sons of Jove). They were believed to have appeared occasionally in later times, taking part with one side or the other, in hard-fought fields, and were said on such occasions to be mounted on magnificent white steeds. Thus in the early history of Rome they are said to have assisted the Romans at the battle of Lake Regillus, and after the victory a temple was erected in their honor on the spot where they appeared.\n\nMacaulay, in his “Lays of Ancient Rome,” thus alludes to the legend:\n\n“So like they were, no mortal Might one from other know; White as snow their armor was, Their steeds were white as snow. Never on earthly anvil Did such rare armor gleam, And never did such gallant steeds Drink of an earthly stream. . . . . . . . “Back comes the chief in triumph Who in the hour of fight Hath seen the great Twin Brethren In harness on his right. Safe comes the ship to haven, Through billows and through gales, If once the great Twin Brethren Sit shining on the sails.”\n\n————\n\nBACCHUS was the son of Jupiter and Semele. Juno, to gratify her resentment against Semele, contrived a plan for her destruction. Assuming the form of Beroë, her aged nurse, she insinuated doubts whether it was indeed Jove himself who came as a lover. Heaving a sigh, she said, “I hope it will turn out so, but I can’t help being afraid. People are not always what they pretend to be. If he is indeed Jove, make him give some proof of it. Ask him to come arrayed in all his splendors, such as he wears in heaven. That will put the matter beyond a doubt.” Semele was persuaded to try the experiment. She asks a favor, without naming what it is. Jove gives his promise, and confirms it with the irrevocable oath, attesting the river Styx, terrible to the gods themselves. Then she made known her request. The god would have stopped her as she spake, but she was too quick for him. The words escaped, and he could neither unsay his promise nor her request. In deep distress he left her and returned to the upper regions. There he clothed himself in his splendors, not putting on all his terrors, as when he overthrew the giants, but what is known among the gods as his lesser panoply. Arrayed in this, he entered the chamber of Semele. Her mortal frame could not endure the splendors of the immortal radiance. She was consumed to ashes.\n\nJove took the infant Bacchus and gave him in charge to the Nysæan nymphs, who nourished his infancy and childhood, and for their care were rewarded by Jupiter by being placed, as the Hyades, among the stars. When Bacchus grew up he discovered the culture of the vine and the mode of extracting its precious juice; but Juno struck him with madness, and drove him forth a wanderer through various parts of the earth. In Phrygia the goddess Rhea cured him and taught him her religious rites, and he set out on a progress through Asia, teaching the people the cultivation of the vine. The most famous part of his wanderings is his expedition to India, which is said to have lasted several years. Returning in triumph, he undertook to introduce his worship into Greece, but was opposed by some princes, who dreaded its introduction on account of the disorders and madness it brought with it.\n\nAs he approached his native city Thebes, Pentheus the king, who had no respect for the new worship, forbade its rites to be performed. But when it was known that Bacchus was advancing, men and women, but chiefly the latter, young and old, poured forth to meet him and to join his triumphal march.\n\nMr. Longfellow in his “Drinking Song” thus describes the march of Bacchus:\n\n“Fauns with youthful Bacchus follow; Ivy crowns that brow, supernal As the forehead of Apollo, And possessing youth eternal.\n\n“Round about him fair Bacchantes, Bearing cymbals, flutes and thyrses, Wild from Naxian groves of Zante’s Vineyards, sing delirious verses.”\n\nIt was in vain Pentheus remonstrated, commanded, and threatened. “Go,” said he to his attendants, “seize this vagabond leader of the rout and bring him to me. I will soon make him confess his false claim of heavenly parentage and renounce his counterfeit worship.” It was in vain his nearest friends and wisest counsellors remonstrated and begged him not to oppose the god. Their remonstrances only made him more violent.\n\nBut now the attendants returned whom he had despatched to seize Bacchus. They had been driven away by the Bacchanals, but had succeeded in taking one of them prisoner, whom, with his hands tied behind him, they brought before the king. Pentheus, beholding him with wrathful countenance, said, “Fellow! you shall speedily be put to death, that your fate may be a warning to others; but though I grudge the delay of your punishment, speak, tell us who you are, and what are these new rites you presume to celebrate.”\n\nThe prisoner, unterrified, responded, “My name is Acetes; my country is Mæonia; my parents were poor people, who had no fields or flocks to leave me, but they left me their fishing rods and nets and their fisherman’s trade. This I followed for some time, till growing weary of remaining in one place, I learned the pilot’s art and how to guide my course by the stars. It happened as I was sailing for Delos we touched at the island of Dia and went ashore. Next morning I sent the men for fresh water, and myself mounted the hill to observe the wind; when my men returned bringing with them a prize, as they thought, a boy of delicate appearance, whom they had found asleep. They judged he was a noble youth, perhaps a king’s son, and they might get a liberal ransom for him. I observed his dress, his walk, his face. There was something in them which I felt sure was more than mortal. I said to my men, ‘What god there is concealed in that form I know not, but some one there certainly is. Pardon us, gentle deity, for the violence we have done you, and give success to our undertakings.’ Dictys, one of my best hands for climbing the mast and coming down by the ropes, and Melanthus, my steersman, and Epopeus, the leader of the sailor’s cry, one and all exclaimed, ‘Spare your prayers for us.’ So blind is the lust of gain! When they proceeded to put him on board I resisted them. ‘This ship shall not be profaned by such impiety,’ said I. ‘I have a greater share in her than any of you.’ But Lycabas, a turbulent fellow, seized me by the throat and attempted to throw me overboard, and I scarcely saved myself by clinging to the ropes. The rest approved the deed.\n\n“Then Bacchus (for it was indeed he), as if shaking off his drowsiness, exclaimed, ‘What are you doing with me? What is this fighting about? Who brought me here? Where are you going to carry me?’ One of them replied, ‘Fear nothing; tell us where you wish to go and we will take you there.’ ‘Naxos is my home,’ said Bacchus; ‘take me there and you shall be well rewarded.’ They promised so to do, and told me to pilot the ship to Naxos. Naxos lay to the right, and I was trimming the sails to carry us there, when some by signs and others by whispers signified to me their will that I should sail in the opposite direction, and take the boy to Egypt to sell him for a slave. I was confounded and said, ‘Let some one else pilot the ship;’ withdrawing myself from any further agency in their wickedness. They cursed me, and one of them, exclaiming, ‘Don’t flatter yourself that we depend on you for our safety,’ took my place as pilot, and bore away from Naxos.\n\n“Then the god, pretending that he had just become aware of their treachery, looked out over the sea and said in a voice of weeping, ‘Sailors, these are not the shores you promised to take me to; yonder island is not my home. What have I done that you should treat me so? It is small glory you will gain by cheating a poor boy.’ I wept to hear him, but the crew laughed at both of us, and sped the vessel fast over the sea. All at once—strange as it may seem, it is true,—the vessel stopped, in the mid sea, as fast as if it was fixed on the ground. The men, astonished, pulled at their oars, and spread more sail, trying to make progress by the aid of both, but all in vain. Ivy twined round the oars and hindered their motion, and clung to the sails, with heavy clusters of berries. A vine, laden with grapes, ran up the mast, and along the sides of the vessel. The sound of flutes was heard and the odor of fragrant wine spread all around. The god himself had a chaplet of vine leaves, and bore in his hand a spear wreathed with ivy. Tigers crouched at his feet, and forms of lynxes and spotted panthers played around him. The men were seized with terror or madness; some leaped overboard; others preparing to do the same beheld their companions in the water undergoing a change, their bodies becoming flattened and ending in a crooked tail. One exclaimed, ‘What miracle is this!’ and as he spoke his mouth widened, his nostrils expanded, and scales covered all his body. Another, endeavoring to pull the oar, felt his hands shrink up and presently to be no longer hands but fins; another, trying to raise his arms to a rope, found he had no arms, and curving his mutilated body, jumped into the sea. What had been his legs became the two ends of a crescent-shaped tail. The whole crew became dolphins and swam about the ship, now upon the surface, now under it, scattering the spray, and spouting the water from their broad nostrils. Of twenty men I alone was left. Trembling with fear, the god cheered me. ‘Fear not,’ said he; ‘steer towards Naxos.’ I obeyed, and when we arrived there, I kindled the altars and celebrated the sacred rites of Bacchus.”\n\nPentheus here exclaimed, “We have wasted time enough on this silly story. Take him away and have him executed without delay.” Acetes was led away by the attendants and shut up fast in prison; but while they were getting ready the instruments of execution the prison doors came open of their own accord and the chains fell from his limbs, and when they looked for him he was nowhere to be found.\n\nPentheus would take no warning, but instead of sending others, determined to go himself to the scene of the solemnities. The mountain Citheron was all alive with worshippers, and the cries of the Bacchanals resounded on every side. The noise roused the anger of Pentheus as the sound of a trumpet does the fire of a war-horse. He penetrated through the wood and reached an open space where the chief scene of the orgies met his eyes. At the same moment the women saw him; and first among them his own mother, Agave, blinded by the god, cried out, “See there the wild boar, the hugest monster that prowls in these woods! Come on, sisters! I will be the first to strike the wild boar.” The whole band rushed upon him, and while he now talks less arrogantly, now excuses himself, and now confesses his crime and implores pardon, they press upon him and wound him. In vain he cries to his aunts to protect him from his mother. Autonoë seized one arm, Ino the other, and between them he was torn to pieces, while his mother shouted, “Victory! Victory! we have done it; the glory is ours!”\n\nSo the worship of Bacchus was established in Greece.\n\nThere is an allusion to the story of Bacchus and the mariners in Milton’s “Comus,” at line 46. The story of Circe will be found in\n\n# Chapter XXIX.\n\n“Bacchus that first from out the purple grapes Crushed the sweet poison of misused wine, After the Tuscan mariners transformed, Coasting the Tyrrhene shore as the winds listed On Circe’s island fell (who knows not Circe, The daughter of the Sun? whose charmed cup Whoever tasted lost his upright shape, And downward fell into a grovelling swine).”\n\nWe have seen in the story of Theseus how Ariadne, the daughter of King Minos, after helping Theseus to escape from the labyrinth, was carried by him to the island of Naxos and was left there asleep, while the ungrateful Theseus pursued his way home without her. Ariadne, on waking and finding herself deserted, abandoned herself to grief. But Venus took pity on her, and consoled her with the promise that she should have an immortal lover, instead of the mortal one she had lost.\n\nThe island where Ariadne was left was the favorite island of Bacchus, the same that he wished the Tyrrhenian mariners to carry him to, when they so treacherously attempted to make prize of him. As Ariadne sat lamenting her fate, Bacchus found her, consoled her, and made her his wife. As a marriage present he gave her a golden crown, enriched with gems, and when she died, he took her crown and threw it up into the sky. As it mounted the gems grew brighter and were turned into stars, and preserving its form Ariadne’s crown remains fixed in the heavens as a constellation, between the kneeling Hercules and the man who holds the serpent.\n\nSpenser alludes to Ariadne’s crown, though he has made some mistakes in his mythology. It was at the wedding of Pirithous, and not Theseus, that the Centaurs and Lapithæ quarrelled.\n\n“Look how the crown which Ariadne wore Upon her ivory forehead that same day That Theseus her unto his bridal bore, Then the bold Centaurs made that bloody fray With the fierce Lapiths which did them dismay; Being now placed in the firmament, Through the bright heaven doth her beams display, And is unto the stars an ornament, Which round about her move in order excellent.”\n————\n\n# CHAPTER XXII\n\nTHE RURAL DEITIES—ERISICHTHON—RHŒCUS—THE WATER DEITIES—CAMENÆ—WINDS\nPAN, the god of woods and fields, of flocks and shepherds, dwelt in grottos, wandered on the mountains and in valleys, and amused himself with the chase or in leading the dances of the nymphs. He was fond of music, and as we have seen, the inventor of the syrinx, or shepherd’s pipe, which he himself played in a masterly manner. Pan, like other gods who dwelt in forests, was dreaded by those whose occupations caused them to pass through the woods by night, for the gloom and loneliness of such scenes dispose the mind to superstitious fears. Hence sudden fright without any visible cause was ascribed to Pan, and called a Panic terror.\n\nAs the name of the god signifies all, Pan came to be considered a symbol of the universe and personification of Nature; and later still to be regarded as a representative of all the gods and of heathenism itself.\n\nSylvanus and Faunus were Latin divinities, whose characteristics are so nearly the same as those of Pan that we may safely consider them as the same personage under different names.\n\nThe wood-nymphs, Pan’s partners in the dance, were but one class of nymphs. There were beside them the Naiads, who presided over brooks and fountains, the Oreads, nymphs of mountains and grottos, and the Nereids, sea-nymphs. The three last named were immortal, but the wood-nymphs, called Dryads or Hamadryads, were believed to perish with the trees which had been their abode and with which they had come into existence. It was therefore an impious act wantonly to destroy a tree, and in some aggravated cases were severely punished, as in the instance of Erisichthon, which we are about to record.\n\nMilton in his glowing description of the early creation, thus alludes to Pan as the personification of Nature:\n\n“. . . Universal Pan, Knit with the Graces and the Hours in dance, Led on the eternal spring.”\n\nAnd describing Eve’s abode:\n\n“. . . In shadier bower, More sacred or sequestered, though but feigned, Pan or Sylvanus never slept, nor nymph Nor Faunus haunted.” —Paradise Lost, B. IV.\n\nIt was a pleasing trait in the old Paganism that it loved to trace in every operation of nature the agency of deity. The imagination of the Greeks peopled all the regions of earth and sea with divinities, to whose agency it attributed those phenomena which our philosophy ascribes to the operation of the laws of nature. Sometimes in our poetical moods we feel disposed to regret the change, and to think that the heart has lost as much as the head has gained by the substitution. The poet Wordsworth thus strongly expresses this sentiment:\n\n“. . . Great God, I’d rather be A Pagan, suckled in a creed outworn, So might I, standing on this pleasant lea, Have glimpses that would make me less forlorn; Have sight of Proteus rising from the sea, And hear old Triton blow his wreathed horn.”\n\nSchiller, in his poem “Die Götter Griechenlands,” expresses his regret for the overthrow of the beautiful mythology of ancient times in a way which has called forth an answer from a Christian poet, Mrs. E. Barrett Browning, in her poem called “The Dead Pan.” The two following verses are a specimen:\n\n“By your beauty which confesses Some chief Beauty conquering you, By our grand heroic guesses Through your falsehood at the True, We will weep not! earth shall roll Heir to each god’s aureole, And Pan is dead.\n“Earth outgrows the mythic fancies Sung beside her in her youth; And those debonaire romances Sound but dull beside the truth. Phœbus’ chariot course is run! Look up, poets, to the sun! Pan, Pan is dead.”\n\nThese lines are founded on an early Christian tradition that when the heavenly host told the shepherds at Bethlehem of the birth of Christ, a deep groan, heard through all the isles of Greece, told that the great Pan was dead, and that all the royalty of Olympus was dethroned and the several deities were sent wandering in cold and darkness. So Milton in his “Hymn on the Nativity”:\n\n“The lonely mountains o’er, And the resounding shore, A voice of weeping heard and loud lament; From haunted spring and dale, Edged with poplar pale, The parting Genius is with sighing sent; With flower-enwoven tresses torn, The nymphs in twilight shade of tangled thickets mourn.”\n\nErisichthon was a profane person and a despiser of the gods. On one occasion he presumed to violate with the axe a grove sacred to Ceres. There stood in this grove a venerable oak so large that it seemed a wood in itself, its ancient trunk towering aloft, whereon votive garlands were often hung and inscriptions carved expressing the gratitude of suppliants to the nymph of the tree. Often had the Dryads danced round it hand in hand. Its trunk measured fifteen cubits round, and it overtopped the other trees as they overtopped the shrubbery. But for all that, Erisichthon saw no reason why he should spare it and he ordered his servants to cut it down. When he saw them hesitate he snatched an axe from one, and thus impiously exclaimed: “I care not whether it be a tree beloved of the goddess or not; were it the goddess herself it should come down if it stood in my way.” So saying, he lifted the axe and the oak seemed to shudder and utter a groan. When the first blow fell upon the trunk blood flowed from the wound. All the bystanders were horror-struck, and one of them ventured to remonstrate and hold back the fatal axe. Erisichthon, with a scornful look, said to him, “Receive the reward of your piety;” and turned against him the weapon which he had held aside from the tree, gashed his body with many wounds, and cut off his head. Then from the midst of the oak came a voice, “I who dwell in this tree am a nymph beloved of Ceres, and dying by your hands forewarn you that punishment awaits you.” He desisted not from his crime, and at last the tree, sundered by repeated blows and drawn by ropes, fell with a crash and prostrated a great part of the grove in its fall.\n\nThe Dryads in dismay at the loss of their companion and at seeing the pride of the forest laid low, went in a body to Ceres, all clad in garments of mourning, and invoked punishment upon Erisichthon. She nodded her assent, and as she bowed her head the grain ripe for harvest in the laden fields bowed also. She planned a punishment so dire that one would pity him, if such a culprit as he could be pitied,—to deliver him over to Famine. As Ceres herself could not approach Famine, for the Fates have ordained that these two goddesses shall never come together, she called an Oread from her mountain and spoke to her in these words: “There is a place in the farthest part of ice-clad Scythia, a sad and sterile region without trees and without crops. Cold dwells there, and Fear and Shuddering, and Famine. Go and tell the last to take possession of the bowels of Erisichthon. Let not abundance subdue her, nor the power of my gifts drive her away. Be not alarmed at the distance” (for Famine dwells very far from Ceres), “but take my chariot. The dragons are fleet and obey the rein, and will take you through the air in a short time.” So she gave her the reins, and she drove away and soon reached Scythia. On arriving at Mount Caucasus she stopped the dragons and found Famine in a stony field, pulling up with teeth and claws the scanty herbage. Her hair was rough, her eyes sunk, her face pale, her lips blanched, her jaws covered with dust, and her skin drawn tight, so as to show all her bones. As the Oread saw her afar off (for she did not dare to come near), she delivered the commands of Ceres; and, though she stopped as short a time as possible, and kept her distance as well as she could, yet she began to feel hungry, and turned the dragons’ heads and drove back to Thessaly.\n\nFamine obeyed the commands of Ceres and sped through the air to the dwelling of Erisichthon, entered the bedchamber of the guilty man, and found him asleep. She enfolded him with her wings and breathed herself into him, infusing her poison into his veins. Having discharged her task, she hastened to leave the land of plenty and returned to her accustomed haunts. Erisichthon still slept, and in his dreams craved food, and moved his jaws as if eating. When he awoke, his hunger was raging. Without a moment’s delay he would have food set before him, of whatever kind earth, sea, or air produces; and complained of hunger even while he ate. What would have sufficed for a city or a nation, was not enough for him. The more he ate the more he craved. His hunger was like the sea, which receives all the rivers, yet is never filled; or like fire, that burns all the fuel that is heaped upon it, yet is still voracious for more.\n\nHis property rapidly diminished under the unceasing demands of his appetite, but his hunger continued unabated. At length he had spent all and had only his daughter left, a daughter worthy of a better parent. Her too he sold. She scorned to be the slave of a purchaser and as she stood by the seaside raised her hands in prayer to Neptune. He heard her prayer, and though her new master was not far off and had his eye upon her a moment before, Neptune changed her form and made her assume that of a fisherman busy at his occupation. Her master, looking for her and seeing her in her altered form, addressed her and said, “Good fisherman, whither went the maiden whom I saw just now, with hair dishevelled and in humble garb, standing about where you stand? Tell me truly; so may your luck be good and not a fish nibble at your hook and get away.” She perceived that her prayer was answered and rejoiced inwardly at hearing herself inquired of about herself. She replied, “Pardon me, stranger, but I have been so intent upon my line that I have seen nothing else; but I wish I may never catch another fish if I believe any woman or other person except myself to have been hereabouts for some time.” He was deceived and went his way, thinking his slave had escaped. Then she resumed her own form. Her father was well pleased to find her still with him, and the money too that he got by the sale of her; so he sold her again. But she was changed by the favor of Neptune as often as she was sold, now into a horse, now a bird, now an ox, and now a stag,—got away from her purchasers and came home. By this base method the starving father procured food; but not enough for his wants, and at last hunger compelled him to devour his limbs, and he strove to nourish his body by eating his body, till death relieved him from the vengeance of Ceres.\n\nThe Hamadryads could appreciate services as well as punish injuries. The story of Rhœcus proves this. Rhœcus, happening to see an oak just ready to fall, ordered his servants to prop it up. The nymph, who had been on the point of perishing with the tree, came and expressed her gratitude to him for having saved her life and bade him ask what reward he would. Rhœcus boldly asked her love and the nymph yielded to his desire. She at the same time charged him to be constant and told him that a bee should be her messenger and let him know when she would admit his society. One time the bee came to Rhœcus when he was playing at draughts and he carelessly brushed it away. This so incensed the nymph that she deprived him of sight.\n\nOur countryman, J. R. Lowell, has taken this story for the subject of one of his shorter poems. He introduces it thus:\n\n“Hear now this fairy legend of old Greece, As full of freedom, youth and beauty still, As the immortal freshness of that grace Carved for all ages on some Attic frieze.”\n\n# THE WATER DEITIES\n\nOceanus and Tethys were the Titans who ruled over the watery element. When Jove and his brothers overthrew the Titans and assumed their power, Neptune and Amphitrite succeeded to the dominion of the waters in place of Oceanus and Tethys.\n\nNeptune was the chief of the water deities. The symbol of his power was the trident, or spear with three points, with which he used to shatter rocks, to call forth or subdue storms, to shake the shores and the like. He created the horse and was the patron of horse races. His own horses had brazen hoofs and golden manes. They drew his chariot over the sea, which became smooth before him, while the monsters of the deep gambolled about his path.\n\nAmphitrite was the wife of Neptune. She was the daughter of Nereus and Doris, and the mother of Triton. Neptune, to pay his court to Amphitrite, came riding on a dolphin. Having won her he rewarded the dolphin by placing him among the stars.\n\n# NEREUS AND DORIS\n\nNereus and Doris were the parents of the Nereids, the most celebrated of whom were Amphitrite, Thetis, the mother of Achilles, and Galatea, who was loved by the Cyclops Polyphemus. Nereus was distinguished for his knowledge and his love of truth and justice, whence he was termed an elder; the gift of prophecy was also assigned to him.\n\n# TRITON AND PROTEUS\n\nTriton was the son of Neptune and Amphitrite, and the poets make him his father’s trumpeter. Proteus was also a son of Neptune. He, like Nereus, is styled a sea-elder for his wisdom and knowledge of future events. His peculiar power was that of changing his shape at will.\n\nThetis, the daughter of Nereus and Doris, was so beautiful that Jupiter himself sought her in marriage; but having learned from Prometheus the Titan that Thetis should bear a son who should grow greater than his father, Jupiter desisted from his suit and decreed that Thetis should be the wife of a mortal. By the aid of Chiron the Centaur, Peleus succeeded in winning the goddess for his bride and their son was the renowned Achilles. In our chapter on the Trojan war it will appear that Thetis was a faithful mother to him, aiding him in all difficulties, and watching over his interests from the first to the last.\n\nIno, the daughter of Cadmus and wife of Athamas, flying from her frantic husband with her little son Melicertes in her arms, sprang from a cliff into the sea. The gods, out of compassion, made her a goddess of the sea, under the name of Leucothea, and him a god, under that of Palæmon. Both were held powerful to save from shipwreck and were invoked by sailors. Palæmon was usually represented riding on a dolphin. The Isthmian games were celebrated in his honor. He was called Portunus by the Romans, and believed to have jurisdiction of the ports and shores.\n\nMilton alludes to all these deities in the song at the conclusion of “Comus”:\n\n“. . . Sabrina fair, Listen and appear to us, In name of great Oceanus; By the earth-shaking Neptune’s mace, And Tethys’ grave, majestic pace, By hoary Nereus’ wrinkled look, And the Carpathian wizard’s hook,[20] By scaly Triton’s winding shell, And old soothsaying Glaucus’ spell, By Leucothea’s lovely hands, And her son who rules the strands. By Thetis’ tinsel-slippered feet, And the songs of Sirens sweet;” etc.\n\nArmstrong, the poet of the “Art of preserving Health,” under the inspiration of Hygeia, the goddess of health, thus celebrates the Naiads. Pæon is a name both of Apollo and Æsculapius.\n\n“Come, ye Naiads! to the fountains lead! Propitious maids! the task remains to sing Your gifts (so Pæon, so the powers of Health Command), to praise your crystal element. O comfortable streams! with eager lips And trembling hands the languid thirsty quaff New life in you; fresh vigor fills their veins. No warmer cups the rural ages knew, None warmer sought the sires of humankind; Happy in temperate peace their equal days Felt not the alternate fits of feverish mirth And sick dejection; still serene and pleased, Blessed with divine immunity from ills, Long centuries they lived; their only fate Was ripe old age, and rather sleep than death.”\n\nBy this name the Latins designated the Muses, but included under it also some other deities, principally nymphs of fountains. Egeria was one of them, whose fountain and grotto are still shown. It was said that Numa, the second king of Rome, was favored by this nymph with secret interviews, in which she taught him those lessons of wisdom and of law which he imbodied in the institutions of his rising nation. After the death of Numa the nymph pined away and was changed into a fountain.\n\nByron, in “Childe Harold,” Canto IV., thus alludes to Egeria and her grotto:\n\n“Here didst thou dwell, in this enchanted cover, Egeria! all thy heavenly bosom beating For the far footsteps of thy mortal lover; The purple midnight veiled that mystic meeting With her most starry canopy;” etc.\n\nTennyson, also, in his “Palace of Art,” gives us a glimpse of the royal lover expecting the interview:\n\n“Holding one hand against his ear, To list a footfall ere he saw The wood-nymph, stayed the Tuscan king to hear Of wisdom and of law.”\n\n# THE WINDS\n\nWhen so many less active agencies were personified, it is not to be supposed that the winds failed to be so. They were Boreas or Aquilo, the north wind; Zephyrus or Favonius, the west; Notus or Auster, the south; and Eurus, the east. The first two have been chiefly celebrated by the poets, the former as the type of rudeness, the latter of gentleness. Boreas loved the nymph Orithyia, and tried to play the lover’s part, but met with poor success. It was hard for him to breathe gently, and sighing was out of the question. Weary at last of fruitless endeavors, he acted out his true character, seized the maiden and carried her off. Their children were Zetes and Calais, winged warriors, who accompanied the Argonautic expedition, and did good service in an encounter with those monstrous birds the Harpies.\n\nZephyrus was the lover of Flora. Milton alludes to them in “Paradise Lost,” where he describes Adam waking and contemplating Eve still asleep.\n\n“. . . He on his side Leaning half raised, with looks of cordial love, Hung over her enamored, and beheld Beauty which, whether waking or asleep, Shot forth peculiar graces; then with voice, Mild as when Zephyrus on Flora breathes, Her hand soft touching, whispered thus: ‘Awake! My fairest, my espoused, my latest found, Heaven’s last, best gift, my ever-new delight.’”\n\nDr. Young, the poet of the “Night Thoughts,” addressing the idle and luxurious, says:\n\n“Ye delicate! who nothing can support (Yourselves most insupportable) for whom The winter rose must blow, . . . . . . and silky soft Favonius breathe still softer or be chid!”\n\n————\n\n# CHAPTER XXIII\n\nACHELOUS AND HERCULES—ADMETUS AND ALCESTIS—ANTIGONE—PENELOPE\nTHE river-god Achelous told the story of Erisichthon to Theseus and his companions, whom he was entertaining at his hospitable board, while they were delayed on their journey by the overflow of his waters. Having finished his story, he added, “But why should I tell of other persons’ transformations when I myself am an instance of the possession of this power? Sometimes I become a serpent, and sometimes a bull, with horns on my head. Or I should say I once could do so; but now I have but one horn, having lost one.” And here he groaned and was silent.\n\nTheseus asked him the cause of his grief, and how he lost his horn. To which question the river-god replied as follows: “Who likes to tell of his defeats? Yet I will not hesitate to relate mine, comforting myself with the thought of the greatness of my conqueror, for it was Hercules. Perhaps you have heard of the fame of Dejanira, the fairest of maidens, whom a host of suitors strove to win. Hercules and myself were of the number, and the rest yielded to us two. He urged in his behalf his descent from Jove and his labors by which he had exceeded the exactions of Juno, his stepmother. I, on the other hand, said to the father of the maiden, ‘Behold me, the king of the waters that flow through your land. I am no stranger from a foreign shore, but belong to the country, a part of your realm. Let it not stand in my way that royal Juno owes me no enmity nor punishes me with heavy tasks. As for this man, who boasts himself the son of Jove, it is either a false pretence, or disgraceful to him if true, for it cannot be true except by his mother’s shame.’ As I said this Hercules scowled upon me, and with difficulty restrained his rage. ‘My hand will answer better than my tongue,’ said he. ‘I yield to you the victory in words, but trust my cause to the strife of deeds.’ With that he advanced towards me, and I was ashamed, after what I had said, to yield. I threw off my green vesture and presented myself for the struggle. He tried to throw me, now attacking my head, now my body. My bulk was my protection, and he assailed me in vain. For a time we stopped, then returned to the conflict. We each kept our position, determined not to yield, foot to foot, I bending over him, clenching his hand in mine, with my forehead almost touching his. Thrice Hercules tried to throw me off, and the fourth time he succeeded, brought me to the ground, and himself upon my back. I tell you the truth, it was as if a mountain had fallen on me. I struggled to get my arms at liberty, panting and reeking with perspiration. He gave me no chance to recover, but seized my throat. My knees were on the earth and my mouth in the dust.\n\n“Finding that I was no match for him in the warrior’s art, I resorted to others and glided away in the form of a serpent. I curled my body in a coil and hissed at him with my forked tongue. He smiled scornfully at this, and said, ‘It was the labor of my infancy to conquer snakes.’ So saying he clasped my neck with his hands. I was almost choked, and struggled to get my neck out of his grasp. Vanquished in this form, I tried what alone remained to me and assumed the form of a bull. He grasped my neck with his arm, and dragging my head down to the ground, overthrew me on the sand. Nor was this enough. His ruthless hand rent my horn from my head. The Naiades took it, consecrated it, and filled it with fragrant flowers. Plenty adopted my horn and made it her own, and called it ‘Cornucopia.’”\n\nThe ancients were fond of finding a hidden meaning in their mythological tales. They explain this fight of Achelous with Hercules by saying Achelous was a river that in seasons of rain overflowed its banks. When the fable says that Achelous loved Dejanira, and sought a union with her, the meaning is that the river in its windings flowed through part of Dejanira’s kingdom. It was said to take the form of a snake because of its winding, and of a bull because it made a brawling or roaring in its course. When the river swelled, it made itself another channel. Thus its head was horned. Hercules prevented the return of these periodical overflows by embankments and canals; and therefore he was said to have vanquished the river-god and cut off his horn. Finally, the lands formerly subject to overflow, but now redeemed, became very fertile, and this is meant by the horn of plenty.\n\nThere is another account of the origin of the Cornucopia. Jupiter at his birth was committed by his mother Rhea to the care of the daughters of Melisseus, a Cretan king. They fed the infant deity with the milk of the goat Amalthea. Jupiter broke off one of the horns of the goat and gave it to his nurses, and endowed it with the wonderful power of becoming filled with whatever the possessor might wish.\n\nThe name of Amalthea is also given by some writers to the mother of Bacchus. It is thus used by Milton, “Paradise Lost,” Book IV.:\n\n“. . . That Nyseian isle, Girt with the river Triton, where old Cham, Whom Gentiles Ammon call, and Libyan Jove, Hid Amalthea and her florid son, Young Bacchus, from his stepdame Rhea’s eye.”\n\n# ADMETUS AND ALCESTIS\n\nÆsculapius, the son of Apollo, was endowed by his father with such skill in the healing art that he even restored the dead to life. At this Pluto took alarm, and prevailed on Jupiter to launch a thunderbolt at Æsculapius. Apollo was indignant at the destruction of his son, and wreaked his vengeance on the innocent workmen who had made the thunderbolt. These were the Cyclopes, who have their workshop under Mount Ætna, from which the smoke and flames of their furnaces are constantly issuing. Apollo shot his arrows at the Cyclopes, which so incensed Jupiter that he condemned him as a punishment to become the servant of a mortal for the space of one year. Accordingly Apollo went into the service of Admetus, king of Thessaly, and pastured his flocks for him on the verdant banks of the river Amphrysos.\n\nAdmetus was a suitor, with others, for the hand of Alcestis, the daughter of Pelias, who promised her to him who should come for her in a chariot drawn by lions and boars. This task Admetus performed by the assistance of his divine herdsman, and was made happy in the possession of Alcestis. But Admetus fell ill, and being near to death, Apollo prevailed on the Fates to spare him on condition that some one would consent to die in his stead. Admetus, in his joy at this reprieve, thought little of the ransom, and perhaps remembering the declarations of attachment which he had often heard from his courtiers and dependents fancied that it would be easy to find a substitute. But it was not so. Brave warriors, who would willingly have perilled their lives for their prince, shrunk from the thought of dying for him on the bed of sickness; and old servants who had experienced his bounty and that of his house from their childhood up, were not willing to lay down the scanty remnant of their days to show their gratitude. Men asked, “Why does not one of his parents do it? They cannot in the course of nature live much longer, and who can feel like them the call to rescue the life they gave from an untimely end?” But the parents, distressed though they were at the thought of losing him, shrunk from the call. Then Alcestis, with a generous self-devotion, proffered herself as the substitute. Admetus, fond as he was of life, would not have submitted to receive it at such a cost; but there was no remedy. The condition imposed by the Fates had been met, and the decree was irrevocable. Alcestis sickened as Admetus revived, and she was rapidly sinking to the grave.\n\nJust at this time Hercules arrived at the palace of Admetus, and found all the inmates in great distress for the impending loss of the devoted wife and beloved mistress. Hercules, to whom no labor was too arduous, resolved to attempt her rescue. He went and lay in wait at the door of the chamber of the dying queen, and when Death came for his prey, he seized him and forced him to resign his victim. Alcestis recovered, and was restored to her husband.\n\nMilton alludes to the story of Alcestis in his Sonnet “on his deceased wife”:\n\n“Methought I saw my late espoused saint Brought to me like Alcestis from the grave, Whom Jove’s great son to her glad husband gave, Rescued from death by force, though pale and faint.”\n\nJ. R. Lowell has chosen the “Shepherd of King Admetus” for the subject of a short poem. He makes that event the first introduction of poetry to men.\n\n“Men called him but a shiftless youth, In whom no good they saw, And yet unwittingly, in truth, They made his careless words their law.\n“And day by day more holy grew Each spot where he had trod, Till after-poets only knew Their first-born brother was a god.”\n\nA large proportion both of the interesting persons and of the exalted acts of legendary Greece belongs to the female sex. Antigone was as bright an example of filial and sisterly fidelity as was Alcestis of connubial devotion. She was the daughter of Œdipus and Jocasta, who with all their descendants were the victims of an unrelenting fate, dooming them to destruction. Œdipus in his madness had torn out his eyes, and was driven forth from his kingdom Thebes, dreaded and abandoned by all men, as an object of divine vengeance. Antigone, his daughter, alone shared his wanderings and remained with him till he died, and then returned to Thebes.\n\nHer brothers, Eteocles and Polynices, had agreed to share the kingdom between them, and reign alternately year by year. The first year fell to the lot of Eteocles, who, when his time expired, refused to surrender the kingdom to his brother. Polynices fled to Adrastus, king of Argos, who gave him his daughter in marriage, and aided him with an army to enforce his claim to the kingdom. This led to the celebrated expedition of the “Seven against Thebes,” which furnished ample materials for the epic and tragic poets of Greece.\n\nAmphiaraus, the brother-in-law of Adrastus, opposed the enterprise, for he was a soothsayer, and knew by his art that no one of the leaders except Adrastus would live to return. But Amphiaraus, on his marriage to Eriphyle, the king’s sister, had agreed that whenever he and Adrastus should differ in opinion, the decision should be left to Eriphyle. Polynices, knowing this, gave Eriphyle the collar of Harmonia, and thereby gained her to his interest. This collar or necklace was a present which Vulcan had given to Harmonia on her marriage with Cadmus, and Polynices had taken it with him on his flight from Thebes. Eriphyle could not resist so tempting a bribe, and by her decision the war was resolved on, and Amphiaraus went to his certain fate. He bore his part bravely in the contest, but could not avert his destiny. Pursued by the enemy, he fled along the river, when a thunderbolt launched by Jupiter opened the ground, and he, his chariot, and his charioteer were swallowed up.\n\nIt would not be in place here to detail all the acts of heroism or atrocity which marked the contest; but we must not omit to record the fidelity of Evadne as an offset to the weakness of Eriphyle. Capaneus, the husband of Evadne, in the ardor of the fight declared that he would force his way into the city in spite of Jove himself. Placing a ladder against the wall he mounted, but Jupiter, offended at his impious language, struck him with a thunderbolt. When his obsequies were celebrated, Evadne cast herself on his funeral pile and perished.\n\nEarly in the contest Eteocles consulted the soothsayer Tiresias as to the issue. Tiresias in his youth had by chance seen Minerva bathing. The goddess in her wrath deprived him of his sight, but afterwards relenting gave him in compensation the knowledge of future events. When consulted by Eteocles, he declared that victory should fall to Thebes if Menœceus, the son of Creon, gave himself a voluntary victim. The heroic youth, learning the response, threw away his life in the first encounter.\n\nThe siege continued long, with various success. At length both hosts agreed that the brothers should decide their quarrel by single combat. They fought and fell by each other’s hands. The armies then renewed the fight, and at last the invaders were forced to yield, and fled, leaving their dead unburied. Creon, the uncle of the fallen princes, now become king, caused Eteocles to be buried with distinguished honor, but suffered the body of Polynices to lie where it fell, forbidding every one on pain of death to give it burial.\n\nAntigone, the sister of Polynices, heard with indignation the revolting edict which consigned her brother’s body to the dogs and vultures, depriving it of those rites which were considered essential to the repose of the dead. Unmoved by the dissuading counsel of an affectionate but timid sister, and unable to procure assistance, she determined to brave the hazard, and to bury the body with her own hands. She was detected in the act, and Creon gave orders that she should be buried alive, as having deliberately set at naught the solemn edict of the city. Her lover, Hæmon, the son of Creon, unable to avert her fate, would not survive her, and fell by his own hand.\n\nAntigone forms the subject of two fine tragedies of the Grecian poet Sophocles. Mrs. Jameson, in her “Characteristics of Women,” has compared her character with that of Cordelia, in Shakspeare’s “King Lear.” The perusal of her remarks cannot fail to gratify our readers.\n\nThe following is the lamentation of Antigone over Œdipus, when death has at last relieved him from his sufferings:\n\n“Alas! I only wished I might have died With my poor father; wherefore should I ask For longer life? O, I was fond of misery with him; E’en what was most unlovely grew beloved When he was with me. O my dearest father, Beneath the earth now in deep darkness hid, Worn as thou wert with age, to me thou still Wast dear, and shalt be ever.” —Francklin’s Sophocles.\n\nPenelope is another of those mythic heroines whose beauties were rather those of character and conduct than of person. She was the daughter of Icarius, a Spartan prince. Ulysses, king of Ithaca, sought her in marriage, and won her, over all competitors. When the moment came for the bride to leave her father’s house, Icarius, unable to bear the thoughts of parting with his daughter, tried to persuade her to remain with him, and not accompany her husband to Ithaca. Ulysses gave Penelope her choice, to stay or go with him. Penelope made no reply, but dropped her veil over her face. Icarius urged her no further, but when she was gone erected a statue to Modesty on the spot where they parted.\n\nUlysses and Penelope had not enjoyed their union more than a year when it was interrupted by the events which called Ulysses to the Trojan war. During his long absence, and when it was doubtful whether he still lived, and highly improbable that he would ever return, Penelope was importuned by numerous suitors, from whom there seemed no refuge but in choosing one of them for her husband. Penelope, however, employed every art to gain time, still hoping for Ulysses’ return. One of her arts of delay was engaging in the preparation of a robe for the funeral canopy of Laertes, her husband’s father. She pledged herself to make her choice among the suitors when the robe was finished. During the day she worked at the robe, but in the night she undid the work of the day. This is the famous Penelope’s web, which is used as a proverbial expression for anything which is perpetually doing but never done. The rest of Penelope’s history will be told when we give an account of her husband’s adventures.\n\n————\n\n# CHAPTER XXIV\n\nORPHEUS AND EURYDICE—ARISTÆUS—AMPHION—LINUS—THAMYRIS—MARSYAS—MELAMPUS—MUSÆUS\nORPHEUS was the son of Apollo and the Muse Calliope. He was presented by his father with a Lyre and taught to play upon it, which he did to such perfection that nothing could withstand the charm of his music. Not only his fellow-mortals but wild beasts were softened by his strains, and gathering round him laid by their fierceness, and stood entranced with his lay. Nay, the very trees and rocks were sensible to the charm. The former crowded round him and the latter relaxed somewhat of their hardness, softened by his notes.\n\nHymen had been called to bless with his presence the nuptials of Orpheus with Eurydice; but though he attended, he brought no happy omens with him. His very torch smoked and brought tears into their eyes. In coincidence with such prognostics, Eurydice, shortly after her marriage, while wandering with the nymphs, her companions, was seen by the shepherd Aristæus, who was struck with her beauty and made advances to her. She fled, and in flying trod upon a snake in the grass, was bitten in the foot, and died. Orpheus sang his grief to all who breathed the upper air, both gods and men, and finding it all unavailing resolved to seek his wife in the regions of the dead. He descended by a cave situated on the side of the promontory of Tænarus and arrived at the Stygian realm. He passed through crowds of ghosts and presented himself before the throne of Pluto and Proserpine. Accompanying the words with the lyre, he sung, “O deities of the underworld, to whom all we who live must come, hear my words, for they are true. I come not to spy out the secrets of Tartarus, nor to try my strength against the three-headed dog with snaky hair who guards the entrance. I come to seek my wife, whose opening years the poisonous viper’s fang has brought to an untimely end. Love has led me here, Love, a god all powerful with us who dwell on the earth, and, if old traditions say true, not less so here. I implore you by these abodes full of terror, these realms of silence and uncreated things, unite again the thread of Eurydice’s life. We all are destined to you, and sooner or later must pass to your domain. She too, when she shall have filled her term of life, will rightly be yours. But till then grant her to me, I beseech you. If you deny me I cannot return alone; you shall triumph in the death of us both.”\n\nAs he sang these tender strains, the very ghosts shed tears. Tantalus, in spite of his thirst, stopped for a moment his efforts for water, Ixion’s wheel stood still, the vulture ceased to tear the giant’s liver, the daughters of Danaüs rested from their task of drawing water in a sieve, and Sisyphus sat on his rock to listen. Then for the first time, it is said, the cheeks of the Furies were wet with tears. Proserpine could not resist, and Pluto himself gave way. Eurydice was called. She came from among the new-arrived ghosts, limping with her wounded foot. Orpheus was permitted to take her away with him on one condition, that he should not turn around to look at her till they should have reached the upper air. Under this condition they proceeded on their way, he leading, she following, through passages dark and steep, in total silence, till they had nearly reached the outlet into the cheerful upper world, when Orpheus, in a moment of forgetfulness, to assure himself that she was still following, cast a glance behind him, when instantly she was borne away. Stretching out their arms to embrace each other, they grasped only the air! Dying now a second time, she yet cannot reproach her husband, for how can she blame his impatience to behold her? “Farewell,” she said, “a last farewell,”—and was hurried away, so fast that the sound hardly reached his ears.\n\nOrpheus endeavored to follow her, and besought permission to return and try once more for her release; but the stern ferryman repulsed him and refused passage. Seven days he lingered about the brink, without food or sleep; then bitterly accusing of cruelty the powers of Erebus, he sang his complaints to the rocks and mountains, melting the hearts of tigers and moving the oaks from their stations. He held himself aloof from womankind, dwelling constantly on the recollection of his sad mischance. The Thracian maidens tried their best to captivate him, but he repulsed their advances. They bore with him as long as they could; but finding him insensible one day, excited by the rites of Bacchus, one of them exclaimed, “See yonder our despiser!” and threw at him her javelin. The weapon, as soon as it came within the sound of his lyre, fell harmless at his feet. So did also the stones that they threw at him. But the women raised a scream and drowned the voice of the music, and then the missiles reached him and soon were stained with his blood. The maniacs tore him limb from limb, and threw his head and his lyre into the river Hebrus, down which they floated, murmuring sad music, to which the shores responded a plaintive symphony. The Muses gathered up the fragments of his body and buried them at Libethra, where the nightingale is said to sing over his grave more sweetly than in any other part of Greece. His lyre was placed by Jupiter among the stars. His shade passed a second time to Tartarus, where he sought out his Eurydice and embraced her with eager arms. They roam the happy fields together now, sometimes he leading, sometimes she; and Orpheus gazes as much as he will upon her, no longer incurring a penalty for a thoughtless glance.\n\nThe story of Orpheus has furnished Pope with an illustration of the power of music, for his “Ode for St. Cecilia’s Day.” The following stanza relates the conclusion of the story:\n\n“But soon, too soon the lover turns his eyes; Again she falls, again she dies, she dies! How wilt thou now the fatal sisters move? No crime was thine, if ’tis no crime to love. Now under hanging mountains, Beside the falls of fountains, Or where Hebrus wanders, Rolling in meanders, All alone, He makes his moan, And calls her ghost, Forever, ever, ever lost! Now with furies surrounded, Despairing, confounded, He trembles, he glows, Amidst Rhodope’s snows. See, wild as the winds o’er the desert he flies; Hark! Hæmus resounds with the Bacchanals’ cries; Ah, see, he dies! Yet even in death Eurydice he sung, Eurydice still trembled on his tongue: Eurydice the woods Eurydice the floods Eurydice the rocks and hollow mountains rung.”\n\nThe superior melody of the nightingale’s song over the grave of Orpheus is alluded to by Southey in his “Thalaba”:\n\n“Then on his ear what sounds Of harmony arose! Far music and the distance-mellowed song From bowers of merriment; The waterfall remote; The murmuring of the leafy groves; The single nightingale Perched in the rosier by, so richly toned, That never from that most melodious bird Singing a love song to his brooding mate, Did Thracian shepherd by the grave Of Orpheus hear a sweeter melody, Though there the spirit of the sepulchre All his own power infuse, to swell The incense that he loves.”\n\nMan avails himself of the instincts of the inferior animals for his own advantage. Hence sprang the art of keeping bees. Honey must first have been known as a wild product, the bees building their structures in hollow trees or holes in the rocks, or any similar cavity that chance offered. Thus occasionally the carcass of a dead animal would be occupied by the bees for that purpose. It was no doubt from some such incident that the superstition arose that the bees were engendered by the decaying flesh of the animal; and Virgil, in the following story, shows how this supposed fact may be turned to account for renewing the swarm when it has been lost by disease or accident:\n\nAristæus, who first taught the management of bees, was the son of the water-nymph Cyrene. His bees had perished, and he resorted for aid to his mother. He stood at the river-side and thus addressed her: “O mother, the pride of my life is taken from me! I have lost my precious bees. My care and skill have availed me nothing, and you my mother have not warded off from me the blow of misfortune.” His mother heard these complaints as she sat in her palace at the bottom of the river, with her attendant nymphs around her. They were engaged in female occupations, spinning and weaving, while one told stories to amuse the rest. The sad voice of Aristæus interrupting their occupation, one of them put her head above the water and seeing him, returned and gave information to his mother, who ordered that he should be brought into her presence. The river at her command opened itself and let him pass in, while it stood curled like a mountain on either side. He descended to the region where the fountains of the great rivers lie; he saw the enormous receptacles of waters and was almost deafened with the roar, while he surveyed them hurrying off in various directions to water the face of the earth. Arriving at his mother’s apartment, he was hospitably received by Cyrene and her nymphs, who spread their table with the richest dainties. They first poured out libations to Neptune, then regaled themselves with the feast, and after that Cyrene thus addressed him: “There is an old prophet named Proteus, who dwells in the sea and is a favorite of Neptune, whose herd of sea-calves he pastures. We nymphs hold him in great respect, for he is a learned sage and knows all things, past, present, and to come. He can tell you, my son, the cause of the mortality among your bees, and how you may remedy it. But he will not do it voluntarily, however you may entreat him. You must compel him by force. If you seize him and chain him, he will answer your questions in order to get released, for he cannot by all his arts get away if you hold fast the chains. I will carry you to his cave, where he comes at noon to take his midday repose. Then you may easily secure him. But when he finds himself captured, his resort is to a power he possesses of changing himself into various forms. He will become a wild boar or a fierce tiger, a scaly dragon or lion with yellow mane. Or he will make a noise like the crackling of flames or the rush of water, so as to tempt you to let go the chain, when he will make his escape. But you have only to keep him fast bound, and at last when he finds all his arts unavailing, he will return to his own figure and obey your commands.” So saying she sprinkled her son with fragrant nectar, the beverage of the gods, and immediately an unusual vigor filled his frame, and courage his heart, while perfume breathed all around him.\n\nThe nymph led her son to the prophet’s cave and concealed him among the recesses of the rocks, while she herself took her place behind the clouds. When noon came and the hour when men and herds retreat from the glaring sun to indulge in quiet slumber, Proteus issued from the water, followed by his herd of sea-calves which spread themselves along the shore. He sat on the rock and counted his herd; then stretched himself on the floor of the cave and went to sleep. Aristæus hardly allowed him to get fairly asleep before he fixed the fetters on him and shouted aloud. Proteus, waking and finding himself captured, immediately resorted to his arts, becoming first a fire, then a flood, then a horrible wild beast, in rapid succession. But finding all would not do, he at last resumed his own form and addressed the youth in angry accents: “Who are you, bold youth, who thus invade my abode, and what do you want of me?” Aristæus replied, “Proteus, you know already, for it is needless for any one to attempt to deceive you. And do you also cease your efforts to elude me. I am led hither by divine assistance, to know from you the cause of my misfortune and how to remedy it.” At these words the prophet, fixing on him his gray eyes with a piercing look, thus spoke: “You receive the merited reward of your deeds, by which Eurydice met her death, for in flying from you she trod upon a serpent, of whose bite she died. To avenge her death, the nymphs, her companions, have sent this destruction to your bees. You have to appease their anger, and thus it must be done: Select four bulls, of perfect form and size, and four cows of equal beauty, build four altars to the nymphs, and sacrifice the animals, leaving their carcasses in the leafy grove. To Orpheus and Eurydice you shall pay such funeral honors as may allay their resentment. Returning after nine days, you will examine the bodies of the cattle slain and see what will befall.” Aristæus faithfully obeyed these directions. He sacrificed the cattle, he left their bodies in the grove, he offered funeral honors to the shades of Orpheus and Eurydice; then returning on the ninth day he examined the bodies of the animals, and, wonderful to relate! a swarm of bees had taken possession of one of the carcasses and were pursuing their labors there as in a hive.\n\nIn “The Task,” Cowper alludes to the story of Aristæus, when speaking of the ice-palace built by the Empress Anne of Russia. He has been describing the fantastic forms which ice assumes in connection with waterfalls, etc.:\n\n“Less worthy of applause though more admired Because a novelty, the work of man, Imperial mistress of the fur-clad Russ, Thy most magnificent and mighty freak, The wonder of the north. No forest fell When thou wouldst build, no quarry sent its stores T’ enrich thy walls; but thou didst hew the floods And make thy marble of the glassy wave. In such a palace Aristæus found Cyrene, when he bore the plaintive tale Of his lost bees to her maternal ear.”\n\nMilton also appears to have had Cyrene and her domestic scene in his mind when he describes to us Sabrina, the nymph of the river Severn, in the Guardian-spirit’s Song in “Comus”:\n\n“Sabrina fair! Listen where thou art sitting Under the glassy, cool, translucent wave In twisted braids of lilies knitting The loose train of thy amber-dropping hair; Listen for dear honor’s sake, Goddess of the silver lake! Listen and save.”\n\nThe following are other celebrated mythical poets and musicians, some of whom were hardly inferior to Orpheus himself:\n\nAmphion was the son of Jupiter and Antiope, queen of Thebes. With his twin brother Zethus he was exposed at birth on Mount Cithæron, where they grew up among the shepherds, not knowing their parentage. Mercury gave Amphion a lyre and taught him to play upon it, and his brother occupied himself in hunting and tending the flocks. Meanwhile Antiope, their mother, who had been treated with great cruelty by Lycus, the usurping king of Thebes, and by Dirce, his wife, found means to inform her children of their rights and to summon them to her assistance. With a band of their fellow-herdsmen they attacked and slew Lycus, and tying Dirce by the hair of her head to a bull, let him drag her till she was dead. Amphion, having become king of Thebes, fortified the city with a wall. It is said that when he played on his lyre the stones moved of their own accord and took their places in the wall.\n\nSee Tennyson’s poem of “Amphion” for an amusing use made of this story.\n\nLinus was the instructor of Hercules in music, but having one day reproved his pupil rather harshly, he roused the anger of Hercules, who struck him with his lyre and killed him.\n\nAn ancient Thracian bard, who in his presumption challenged the Muses to a trial of skill, and being overcome in the contest, was deprived by them of his sight. Milton alludes to him with other blind bards, when speaking of his own blindness, “Paradise Lost,” Book III., 35.\n\nMinerva invented the flute, and played upon it to the delight of all the celestial auditors; but the mischievous urchin Cupid having dared to laugh at the queer face which the goddess made while playing, Minerva threw the instrument indignantly away, and it fell down to earth, and was found by Marsyas. He blew upon it, and drew from it such ravishing sounds that he was tempted to challenge Apollo himself to a musical contest. The god of course triumphed, and punished Marsyas by flaying him alive.\n\nMelampus was the first mortal endowed with prophetic powers. Before his house there stood an oak tree containing a serpent’s nest. The old serpents were killed by the servants, but Melampus took care of the young ones and fed them carefully. One day when he was asleep under the oak the serpents licked his ears with their tongues. On awaking he was astonished to find that he now understood the language of birds and creeping things. This knowledge enabled him to foretell future events, and he became a renowned soothsayer. At one time his enemies took him captive and kept him strictly imprisoned. Melampus in the silence of the night heard the woodworms in the timbers talking together, and found out by what they said that the timbers were nearly eaten through and the roof would soon fall in. He told his captors and demanded to be let out, warning them also. They took his warning, and thus escaped destruction, and rewarded Melampus and held him in high honor.\n\nA semi-mythological personage who was represented by one tradition to be the son of Orpheus. He is said to have written sacred poems and oracles. Milton couples his name with that of Orpheus in his “Il Penseroso”:\n\n“But O, sad virgin, that thy power Might raise Musæus from his bower, Or bid the soul of Orpheus sing Such notes as warbled to the string, Drew iron tears down Pluto’s cheek, And made Hell grant what love did seek.”\n————\n\nTHE poets whose adventures compose this chapter were real persons some of whose works yet remain, and their influence on poets who succeeded them is yet more important than their poetical remains. The adventures recorded of them in the following stories rest on the same authority as other narratives of the “Age of Fable,” that is, of the poets who have told them. In their present form, the first two are translated from the German, Arion from Schlegel, and Ibycus from Schiller.\n\nArion was a famous musician, and dwelt in the court of Periander, king of Corinth, with whom he was a great favorite. There was to be a musical contest in Sicily, and Arion longed to compete for the prize. He told his wish to Periander, who besought him like a brother to give up the thought. “Pray stay with me,” he said, “and be contented. He who strives to win may lose.” Arion answered, “A wandering life best suits the free heart of a poet. The talent which a god bestowed on me, I would fain make a source of pleasure to others. And if I win the prize, how will the enjoyment of it be increased by the consciousness of my widespread fame!” He went, won the prize, and embarked with his wealth in a Corinthian ship for home. On the second morning after setting sail, the wind breathed mild and fair. “O Periander,” he exclaimed, “dismiss your fears! Soon shall you forget them in my embrace. With what lavish offerings will we display our gratitude to the gods, and how merry will we be at the festal board!” The wind and sea continued propitious. Not a cloud dimmed the firmament. He had not trusted too much to the ocean—but he had to man. He overheard the seamen exchanging hints with one another, and found they were plotting to possess themselves of his treasure. Presently they surrounded him loud and mutinous, and said, “Arion, you must die! If you would have a grave on shore, yield yourself to die on this spot; but if otherwise, cast yourself into the sea.” “Will nothing satisfy you but my life?” said he. “Take my gold, and welcome. I willingly buy my life at that price.” “No, no; we cannot spare you. Your life would be too dangerous to us. Where could we go to escape from Periander, if he should know that you had been robbed by us? Your gold would be of little use to us, if on returning home, we could never more be free from fear.” “Grant me, then,” said he, “a last request, since nought will avail to save my life, that I may die, as I have lived, as becomes a bard. When I shall have sung my death song, and my harp-strings shall have ceased to vibrate, then I will bid farewell to life, and yield uncomplaining to my fate.” This prayer, like the others, would have been unheeded,—they thought only of their booty,—but to hear so famous a musician, that moved their rude hearts. “Suffer me,” he added, “to arrange my dress. Apollo will not favor me unless I be clad in my minstrel garb.”\n\nHe clothed his well-proportioned limbs in gold and purple fair to see, his tunic fell around him in graceful folds, jewels adorned his arms, his brow was crowned with a golden wreath, and over his neck and shoulders flowed his hair perfumed with odors. His left hand held the lyre, his right the ivory wand with which he struck its chords. Like one inspired, he seemed to drink the morning air and glitter in the morning ray. The seamen gazed with admiration. He strode forward to the vessel’s side and looked down into the deep blue sea. Addressing his lyre, he sang, “Companion of my voice, come with me to the realm of shades. Though Cerberus may growl, we know the power of song can tame his rage. Ye heroes of Elysium, who have passed the darkling flood,—ye happy souls, soon shall I join your band. Yet can ye relieve my grief? Alas, I leave my friend behind me. Thou, who didst find thy Eurydice, and lose her again as soon as found; when she had vanished like a dream, how didst thou hate the cheerful light! I must away, but I will not fear. The gods look down upon us. Ye who slay me unoffending, when I am no more, your time of trembling shall come. Ye Nereids, receive your guest, who throws himself upon your mercy!” So saying, he sprang into the deep sea. The waves covered him, and the seamen held on their way, fancying themselves safe from all danger of detection.\n\nBut the strains of his music had drawn round him the inhabitants of the deep to listen, and Dolphins followed the ship as if chained by a spell. While he struggled in the waves, a Dolphin offered him his back, and carried him mounted thereon safe to shore. At the spot where he landed, a monument of brass was afterwards erected upon the rocky shore, to preserve the memory of the event.\n\nWhen Arion and the dolphin parted, each to his own element, Arion thus poured forth his thanks: “Farewell, thou faithful, friendly fish! Would that I could reward thee; but thou canst not wend with me, nor I with thee. Companionship we may not have. May Galatea, queen of the deep, accord thee her favor, and thou, proud of the burden, draw her chariot over the smooth mirror of the deep.”\n\nArion hastened from the shore, and soon saw before him the towers of Corinth. He journeyed on, harp in hand, singing as he went, full of love and happiness, forgetting his losses, and mindful only of what remained, his friend and his lyre. He entered the hospitable halls, and was soon clasped in the embrace of Periander. “I come back to thee, my friend,” he said. “The talent which a god bestowed has been the delight of thousands, but false knaves have stripped me of my well-earned treasure; yet I retain the consciousness of wide spread fame.” Then he told Periander all the wonderful events that had befallen him, who heard him with amazement. “Shall such wickedness triumph?” said he. “Then in vain is power lodged in my hands. That we may discover the criminals, you must remain here in concealment, and so they will approach without suspicion.” When the ship arrived in the harbor, he summoned the mariners before him. “Have you heard anything of Arion?” he inquired. “I anxiously look for his return.” They replied, “We left him well and prosperous in Tarentum.” As they said these words, Arion stepped forth and faced them. His well-proportioned limbs were arrayed in gold and purple fair to see, his tunic fell around him in graceful folds, jewels adorned his arms, his brow was crowned with a golden wreath, and over his neck and shoulders flowed his hair perfumed with odors; his left hand held the lyre, his right the ivory wand with which he struck its chords. They fell prostrate at his feet, as if a lightning bolt had struck them. “We meant to murder him, and he has become a god. O Earth, open and receive us!” Then Periander spoke. “He lives, the master of the lay! Kind Heaven protects the poet’s life. As for you, I invoke not the spirit of vengeance; Arion wishes not your blood. Ye slaves of avarice, begone! Seek some barbarous land, and never may aught beautiful delight your souls!”\n\nSpenser represents Arion, mounted on his dolphin, accompanying the train of Neptune and Amphitrite:\n\n“Then was there heard a most celestial sound Of dainty music which did next ensue, And, on the floating waters as enthroned, Arion with his harp unto him drew The ears and hearts of all that goodly crew; Even when as yet the dolphin which him bore Through the Ægean Seas from pirates’ view, Stood still, by him astonished at his lore, And all the raging seas for joy forgot to roar.”\n\nByron, in his “Childe Harold,” Canto II., alludes to the story of Arion, when, describing his voyage, he represents one of the seamen making music to entertain the rest:\n\n“The moon is up; by Heaven a lovely eve! Long streams of light o’er dancing waves expand; Now lads on shore may sigh and maids believe; Such be our fate when we return to land! Meantime some rude Arion’s restless hand Wakes the brisk harmony that sailors love; A circle there of merry listeners stand, Or to some well-known measure featly move Thoughtless as if on shore they still were free to rove.”\n\nIn order to understand the story of Ibycus which follows it is necessary to remember, first, that the theatres of the ancients were immense fabrics capable of containing from ten to thirty thousand spectators, and as they were used only on festival occasions, and admission was free to all, they were usually filled. They were without roofs and open to the sky, and the performances were in the daytime. Secondly, the appalling representation of the Furies is not exaggerated in the story. It is recorded that Æschylus, the tragic poet, having on one occasion represented the Furies in a chorus of fifty performers, the terror of the spectators was such that many fainted and were thrown into convulsions, and the magistrates forbade a like representation for the future.\n\nIbycus, the pious poet, was on his way to the chariot races and musical competitions held at the Isthmus of Corinth, which attracted all of Grecian lineage. Apollo had bestowed on him the gift of song, the honeyed lips of the poet, and he pursued his way with lightsome step, full of the god. Already the towers of Corinth crowning the height appeared in view, and he had entered with pious awe the sacred grove of Neptune. No living object was in sight, only a flock of cranes flew overhead taking the same course as himself in their migration to a southern clime. “Good luck to you, ye friendly squadrons,” he exclaimed, “my companions from across the sea. I take your company for a good omen. We come from far and fly in search of hospitality. May both of us meet that kind reception which shields the stranger guest from harm!”\n\nHe paced briskly on, and soon was in the middle of the wood. There suddenly, at a narrow pass, two robbers stepped forth and barred his way. He must yield or fight. But his hand, accustomed to the lyre, and not to the strife of arms, sank powerless. He called for help on men and gods, but his cry reached no defender’s ear. “Then here must I die,” said he, “in a strange land, unlamented, cut off by the hand of outlaws, and see none to avenge my cause.” Sore wounded, he sank to the earth, when hoarse screamed the cranes overhead. “Take up my cause, ye cranes,” he said, “since no voice but yours answers to my cry.” So saying he closed his eyes in death.\n\nThe body, despoiled and mangled, was found, and though disfigured with wounds, was recognized by the friend in Corinth who had expected him as a guest. “Is it thus I find you restored to me?” he exclaimed. “I who hoped to entwine your temples with the wreath of triumph in the strife of song!”\n\nThe guests assembled at the festival heard the tidings with dismay. All Greece felt the wound, every heart owned its loss. They crowded round the tribunal of the magistrates, and demanded vengeance on the murderers and expiation with their blood.\n\nBut what trace or mark shall point out the perpetrator from amidst the vast multitude attracted by the splendor of the feast? Did he fall by the hands of robbers or did some private enemy slay him? The all-discerning sun alone can tell, for no other eye beheld it. Yet not improbably the murderer even now walks in the midst of the throng, and enjoys the fruits of his crime, while vengeance seeks for him in vain. Perhaps in their own temple’s enclosure he defies the gods, mingling freely in this throng of men that now presses into the amphitheatre.\n\nFor now crowded together, row on row, the multitude fill the seats till it seems as if the very fabric would give way. The murmur of voices sounds like the roar of the sea, while the circles widening in their ascent rise tier on tier, as if they would reach the sky.\n\nAnd now the vast assemblage listens to the awful voice of the chorus personating the Furies, which in solemn guise advances with measured step, and moves around the circuit of the theatre. Can they be mortal women who compose that awful group, and can that vast concourse of silent forms be living beings?\n\nThe choristers, clad in black, bore in their fleshless hands torches blazing with a pitchy flame. Their cheeks were bloodless, and in place of hair writhing and swelling serpents curled around their brows. Forming a circle, these awful beings sang their hymns, rending the hearts of the guilty, and enchaining all their faculties. It rose and swelled, overpowering the sound of the instruments, stealing the judgment, palsying the heart, curdling the blood.\n\n“Happy the man who keeps his heart pure from guilt and crime! Him we avengers touch not; he treads the path of life secure from us. But woe! woe! to him who has done the deed of secret murder. We the fearful family of Night fasten ourselves upon his whole being. Thinks he by flight to escape us? We fly still faster in pursuit, twine our snakes around his feet, and bring him to the ground. Unwearied we pursue; no pity checks our course; still on and on, to the end of life, we give him no peace nor rest.” Thus the Eumenides sang, and moved in solemn cadence, while stillness like the stillness of death sat over the whole assembly as if in the presence of superhuman beings; and then in solemn march completing the circuit of the theatre, they passed out at the back of the stage.\n\nEvery heart fluttered between illusion and reality, and every breast panted with undefined terror, quailing before the awful power that watches secret crimes and winds unseen the skein of destiny. At that moment a cry burst forth from one of the uppermost benches—“Look! look! comrade, yonder are the cranes of Ibycus!” And suddenly there appeared sailing across the sky a dark object which a moment’s inspection showed to be a flock of cranes flying directly over the theatre. “Of Ibycus! did he say?” The beloved name revived the sorrow in every breast. As wave follows wave over the face of the sea, so ran from mouth to mouth the words, “Of Ibycus! him whom we all lament, whom some murderer’s hand laid low! What have the cranes to do with him?” And louder grew the swell of voices, while like a lightning’s flash the thought sped through every heart, “Observe the power of the Eumenides! The pious poet shall be avenged! the murderer has informed against himself. Seize the man who uttered that cry and the other to whom he spoke!”\n\nThe culprit would gladly have recalled his words, but it was too late. The faces of the murderers, pale with terror, betrayed their guilt. The people took them before the judge, they confessed their crime, and suffered the punishment they deserved.\n\nSimonides was one of the most prolific of the early poets of Greece, but only a few fragments of his compositions have descended to us. He wrote hymns, triumphal odes, and elegies. In the last species of composition he particularly excelled. His genius was inclined to the pathetic, and none could touch with truer effect the chords of human sympathy. The “Lamentation of Danaë,” the most important of the fragments which remain of his poetry, is based upon the tradition that Danaë and her infant son were confined by order of her father, Acrisius, in a chest and set adrift on the sea. The chest floated towards the island of Seriphus, where both were rescued by Dictys, a fisherman, and carried to Polydectes, king of the country, who received and protected them. The child, Perseus, when grown up became a famous hero, whose adventures have been recorded in a previous chapter.\n\nSimonides passed much of his life at the courts of princes, and often employed his talents in panegyric and festal odes, receiving his reward from the munificence of those whose exploits he celebrated. This employment was not derogatory, but closely resembles that of the earliest bards, such as Demodocus, described by Homer, or of Homer himself, as recorded by tradition.\n\nOn one occasion, when residing at the court of Scopas, king of Thessaly, the prince desired him to prepare a poem in celebration of his exploits, to be recited at a banquet. In order to diversify his theme, Simonides, who was celebrated for his piety, introduced into his poem the exploits of Castor and Pollux. Such digressions were not unusual with the poets on similar occasions, and one might suppose an ordinary mortal might have been content to share the praises of the sons of Leda. But vanity is exacting; and as Scopas sat at his festal board among his courtiers and sycophants, he grudged every verse that did not rehearse his own praises. When Simonides approached to receive the promised reward Scopas bestowed but half the expected sum, saying, “Here is payment for my portion of thy performance; Castor and Pollux will doubtless compensate thee for so much as relates to them.” The disconcerted poet returned to his seat amidst the laughter which followed the great man’s jest. In a little time he received a message that two young men on horseback were waiting without and anxious to see him. Simonides hastened to the door, but looked in vain for the visitors. Scarcely, however, had he left the banqueting hall when the roof fell in with a loud crash, burying Scopas and all his guests beneath the ruins. On inquiring as to the appearance of the young men who had sent for him, Simonides was satisfied that they were no other than Castor and Pollux themselves.\n\nSappho was a poetess who flourished in a very early age of Greek literature. Of her works few fragments remain, but they are enough to establish her claim to eminent poetical genius. The story of Sappho commonly alluded to is that she was passionately in love with a beautiful youth named Phaon, and failing to obtain a return of affection she threw herself from the promontory of Leucadia into the sea, under a superstition that those who should take that “Lover’s-leap” would, if not destroyed, be cured of their love.\n\nByron alludes to the story of Sappho in “Childe Harold,” Canto II.:\n\n“Childe Harold sailed and passed the barren spot Where sad Penelope o’erlooked the wave, And onward viewed the mount, not yet forgot, The lover’s refuge and the Lesbian’s grave. Dark Sappho! could not verse immortal save That breast imbued with such immortal fire?\n“’Twas on a Grecian autumn’s gentle eve Childe Harold hailed Leucadia’s cape afar;” etc.\n\nThose who wish to know more of Sappho and her “leap” are referred to the “Spectator,” Nos. 223 and 229. See also Moore’s “Evenings in Greece.”\n\n————\n\n# DIANA AND ENDYMION\n\nENDYMION was a beautiful youth who fed his flock on Mount Latmos. One calm, clear night Diana, the moon, looked down and saw him sleeping. The cold heart of the virgin goddess was warmed by his surpassing beauty, and she came down to him, kissed him, and watched over him while he slept.\n\nAnother story was that Jupiter bestowed on him the gift of perpetual youth united with perpetual sleep. Of one so gifted we can have but few adventures to record. Diana, it was said, took care that his fortunes should not suffer by his inactive life, for she made his flock increase, and guarded his sheep and lambs from the wild beasts.\n\nThe story of Endymion has a peculiar charm from the human meaning which it so thinly veils. We see in Endymion the young poet, his fancy and his heart seeking in vain for that which can satisfy them, finding his favorite hour in the quiet moonlight, and nursing there beneath the beams of the bright and silent witness the melancholy and the ardor which consumes him. The story suggests aspiring and poetic love, a life spent more in dreams than in reality, and an early and welcome death.—S. G. B.\n\nThe “Endymion” of Keats is a wild and fanciful poem, containing some exquisite poetry, as this, to the moon:\n\n“. . . The sleeping kine Couched in thy brightness dream of fields divine. Innumerable mountains rise, and rise, Ambitious for the hallowing of thine eyes, And yet thy benediction passeth not One obscure hiding-place, one little spot Where pleasure may be sent; the nested wren Has thy fair face within its tranquil ken;” etc., etc.\n\nDr. Young, in the “Night Thoughts,” alludes to Endymion thus:\n\n“. . . These thoughts, O night, are thine; From thee they came like lovers’ secret sighs, While others slept. So Cynthia, poets feign, In shadows veiled, soft, sliding from her sphere, Her shepherd cheered, of her enamoured less Than I of thee.”\n\nFletcher, in the “Faithful Shepherdess,” tells:\n\n“How the pale Phœbe, hunting in a grove, First saw the boy Endymion, from whose eyes She took eternal fire that never dies; How she conveyed him softly in a sleep, His temples bound with poppy, to the steep Head of old Latmos, where she stoops each night, Gilding the mountain with her brother’s light, To kiss her sweetest.”\n\nOrion was the son of Neptune. He was a handsome giant and a mighty hunter. His father gave him the power of wading through the depths of the sea, or, as others say, of walking on its surface.\n\nOrion loved Merope, the daughter of Œnopion, king of Chios, and sought her in marriage. He cleared the island of wild beasts, and brought the spoils of the chase as presents to his beloved; but as Œnopion constantly deferred his consent, Orion attempted to gain possession of the maiden by violence. Her father, incensed at this conduct, having made Orion drunk, deprived him of his sight and cast him out on the seashore. The blinded hero followed the sound of a Cyclops’ hammer till he reached Lemnos, and came to the forge of Vulcan, who, taking pity on him, gave him Kedalion, one of his men, to be his guide to the abode of the sun. Placing Kedalion on his shoulders, Orion proceeded to the east, and there meeting the sun-god, was restored to sight by his beam.\n\nAfter this he dwelt as a hunter with Diana, with whom he was a favorite, and it is even said she was about to marry him. Her brother was highly displeased and often chid her, but to no purpose. One day, observing Orion wading through the sea with his head just above the water, Apollo pointed it out to his sister and maintained that she could not hit that black thing on the sea. The archer-goddess discharged a shaft with fatal aim. The waves rolled the dead body of Orion to the land, and bewailing her fatal error with many tears, Diana placed him among the stars, where he appears as a giant, with a girdle, sword, lion’s skin, and club. Sirius, his dog, follows him, and the Pleiads fly before him.\n\nThe Pleiads were daughters of Atlas, and nymphs of Diana’s train. One day Orion saw them and became enamoured and pursued them. In their distress they prayed to the gods to change their form, and Jupiter in pity turned them into pigeons, and then made them a constellation in the sky. Though their number was seven, only six stars are visible, for Electra, one of them, it is said left her place that she might not behold the ruin of Troy, for that city was founded by her son Dardanus. The sight had such an effect on her sisters that they have looked pale ever since.\n\nMr. Longfellow has a poem on the “Occultation of Orion.” The following lines are those in which he alludes to the mythic story. We must premise that on the celestial globe Orion is represented as robed in a lion’s skin and wielding a club. At the moment the stars of the constellation, one by one, were quenched in the light of the moon, the poet tells us\n\n“Down fell the red skin of the lion Into the river at his feet. His mighty club no longer beat The forehead of the bull; but he Reeled as of yore beside the sea, When blinded by Œnopion He sought the blacksmith at his forge, And climbing up the narrow gorge, Fixed his blank eyes upon the sun.”\n\nTennyson has a different theory of the Pleiads:\n\n“Many a night I saw the Pleiads, rising through the mellow shade, Glitter like a swarm of fire-flies tangled in a silver braid.” —Locksley Hall.\n\nByron alludes to the lost Pleiad:\n\n“Like the lost Pleiad seen no more below.”\n\nSee also Mrs. Hemans’s verses on the same subject.\n\n# AURORA AND TITHONUS\n\nThe goddess of the Dawn, like her sister the Moon, was at times inspired with the love of mortals. Her greatest favorite was Tithonus, son of Laomedon, king of Troy. She stole him away, and prevailed on Jupiter to grant him immortality; but, forgetting to have youth joined in the gift, after some time she began to discern, to her great mortification, that he was growing old. When his hair was quite white she left his society; but he still had the range of her palace, lived on ambrosial food, and was clad in celestial raiment. At length he lost the power of using his limbs, and then she shut him up in his chamber, whence his feeble voice might at times be heard. Finally she turned him into a grasshopper.\n\nMemnon was the son of Aurora and Tithonus. He was king of the Æthiopians, and dwelt in the extreme east, on the shore of Ocean. He came with his warriors to assist the kindred of his father in the war of Troy. King Priam received him with great honors, and listened with admiration to his narrative of the wonders of the ocean shore.\n\nThe very day after his arrival, Memnon, impatient of repose, led his troops to the field. Antilochus, the brave son of Nestor, fell by his hand, and the Greeks were put to flight, when Achilles appeared and restored the battle. A long and doubtful contest ensued between him and the son of Aurora; at length victory declared for Achilles, Memnon fell, and the Trojans fled in dismay.\n\nAurora, who from her station in the sky had viewed with apprehension the danger of her son, when she saw him fall, directed his brothers, the Winds, to convey his body to the banks of the river Esepus in Paphlagonia. In the evening Aurora came, accompanied by the Hours and the Pleiads, and wept and lamented over her son. Night, in sympathy with her grief, spread the heaven with clouds; all nature mourned for the offspring of the Dawn. The Æthiopians raised his tomb on the banks of the stream in the grove of the Nymphs, and Jupiter caused the sparks and cinders of his funeral pile to be turned into birds, which, dividing into two flocks, fought over the pile till they fell into the flame. Every year at the anniversary of his death they return and celebrate his obsequies in like manner. Aurora remains inconsolable for the loss of her son. Her tears still flow, and may be seen at early morning in the form of dew-drops on the grass.\n\nUnlike most of the marvels of ancient mythology, there still exist some memorials of this. On the banks of the river Nile, in Egypt, are two colossal statues, one of which is said to be the statue of Memnon. Ancient writers record that when the first rays of the rising sun fall upon this statue a sound is heard to issue from it, which they compare to the snapping of a harp-string. There is some doubt about the identification of the existing statue with the one described by the ancients, and the mysterious sounds are still more doubtful. Yet there are not wanting some modern testimonies to their being still audible. It has been suggested that sounds produced by confined air making its escape from crevices or caverns in the rocks may have given some ground for the story. Sir Gardner Wilkinson, a late traveller, of the highest authority, examined the statue itself, and discovered that it was hollow, and that “in the lap of the statue is a stone, which on being struck emits a metallic sound, that might still be made use of to deceive a visitor who was predisposed to believe its powers.”\n\nThe vocal statue of Memnon is a favorite subject of allusion with the poets. Darwin, in his “Botanic Garden,” says:\n\n“So to the sacred Sun in Memnon’s fane Spontaneous concords choired the matin strain; Touched by his orient beam responsive rings The living lyre and vibrates all its strings; Accordant aisles the tender tones prolong, And holy echoes swell the adoring song.”\n\n# ACIS AND GALATEA\n\nScylla was a fair virgin of Sicily, a favorite of the Sea-Nymphs. She had many suitors, but repelled them all, and would go to the grotto of Galatea, and tell her how she was persecuted. One day the goddess, while Scylla dressed her hair, listened to the story, and then replied, “Yet, maiden, your persecutors are of the not ungentle race of men, whom, if you will, you can repel; but I, the daughter of Nereus, and protected by such a band of sisters, found no escape from the passion of the Cyclops but in the depths of the sea;” and tears stopped her utterance, which when the pitying maiden had wiped away with her delicate finger, and soothed the goddess, “Tell me, dearest,” said she, “the cause of your grief.” Galatea then said, “Acis was the son of Faunus and a Naiad. His father and mother loved him dearly, but their love was not equal to mine. For the beautiful youth attached himself to me alone, and he was just sixteen years old, the down just beginning to darken his cheeks. As much as I sought his society, so much did the Cyclops seek mine; and if you ask me whether my love for Acis or my hatred of Polyphemus was the stronger, I cannot tell you; they were in equal measure. O Venus, how great is thy power! this fierce giant, the terror of the woods, whom no hapless stranger escaped unharmed, who defied even Jove himself, learned to feel what love was, and, touched with a passion for me, forgot his flocks and his well-stored caverns. Then for the first time he began to take some care of his appearance, and to try to make himself agreeable; he harrowed those coarse locks of his with a comb, and mowed his beard with a sickle, looked at his harsh features in the water, and composed his countenance. His love of slaughter, his fierceness and thirst of blood prevailed no more, and ships that touched at his island went away in safety. He paced up and down the seashore, imprinting huge tracks with his heavy tread, and, when weary, lay tranquilly in his cave.\n\n“There is a cliff which projects into the sea, which washes it on either side. Thither one day the huge Cyclops ascended, and sat down while his flocks spread themselves around. Laying down his staff, which would have served for a mast to hold a vessel’s sail, and taking his instrument compacted of numerous pipes, he made the hills and the waters echo the music of his song. I lay hid under a rock by the side of my beloved Acis, and listened to the distant strain. It was full of extravagant praises of my beauty, mingled with passionate reproaches of my coldness and cruelty.\n\n“When he had finished he rose up, and, like a raging bull that cannot stand still, wandered off into the woods. Acis and I thought no more of him, till on a sudden he came to a spot which gave him a view of us as we sat. ‘I see you,’ he exclaimed, ‘and I will make this the last of your love-meetings.’ His voice was a roar such as an angry Cyclops alone could utter. Ætna trembled at the sound. I, overcome with terror, plunged into the water. Acis turned and fled, crying, ‘Save me, Galatea, save me, my parents!’ The Cyclops pursued him, and tearing a rock from the side of the mountain hurled it at him. Though only a corner of it touched him, it overwhelmed him.\n\n“All that fate left in my power I did for Acis. I endowed him with the honors of his grandfather, the river-god. The purple blood flowed out from under the rock, but by degrees grew paler and looked like the stream of a river rendered turbid by rains, and in time it became clear. The rock cleaved open, and the water, as it gushed from the chasm, uttered a pleasing murmur.”\n\nThus Acis was changed into a river, and the river retains the name of Acis.\n\nDryden, in his “Cymon and Iphigenia,” has told the story of a clown converted into a gentleman by the power of love, in a way that shows traces of kindred to the old story of Galatea and the Cyclops.\n\n“What not his father’s care nor tutor’s art Could plant with pains in his unpolished heart, The best instructor, Love, at once inspired, As barren grounds to fruitfulness are fired. Love taught him shame, and shame with love at strife Soon taught the sweet civilities of life.”\n————\n\n# THE TROJAN WAR\n\nMINERVA was the goddess of wisdom, but on one occasion she did a very foolish thing; she entered into competition with Juno and Venus for the prize of beauty. It happened thus: At the nuptials of Peleus and Thetis all the gods were invited with the exception of Eris, or Discord. Enraged at her exclusion, the goddess threw a golden apple among the guests, with the inscription, “For the fairest.” Thereupon Juno, Venus, and Minerva each claimed the apple. Jupiter, not willing to decide in so delicate a matter, sent the goddesses to Mount Ida, where the beautiful shepherd Paris was tending his flocks, and to him was committed the decision. The goddesses accordingly appeared before him. Juno promised him power and riches, Minerva glory and renown in war, and Venus the fairest of women for his wife, each attempting to bias his decision in her own favor. Paris decided in favor of Venus and gave her the golden apple, thus making the two other goddesses his enemies. Under the protection of Venus, Paris sailed to Greece, and was hospitably received by Menelaus, king of Sparta. Now Helen, the wife of Menelaus, was the very woman whom Venus had destined for Paris, the fairest of her sex. She had been sought as a bride by numerous suitors, and before her decision was made known, they all, at the suggestion of Ulysses, one of their number, took an oath that they would defend her from all injury and avenge her cause if necessary. She chose Menelaus, and was living with him happily when Paris became their guest. Paris, aided by Venus, persuaded her to elope with him, and carried her to Troy, whence arose the famous Trojan war, the theme of the greatest poems of antiquity, those of Homer and Virgil.\n\nMenelaus called upon his brother chieftains of Greece to fulfil their pledge, and join him in his efforts to recover his wife. They generally came forward, but Ulysses, who had married Penelope, and was very happy in his wife and child, had no disposition to embark in such a troublesome affair. He therefore hung back and Palamedes was sent to urge him. When Palamedes arrived at Ithaca Ulysses pretended to be mad. He yoked an ass and an ox together to the plough and began to sow salt. Palamedes, to try him, placed the infant Telemachus before the plough, whereupon the father turned the plough aside, showing plainly that he was no madman, and after that could no longer refuse to fulfil his promise. Being now himself gained for the undertaking, he lent his aid to bring in other reluctant chiefs, especially Achilles. This hero was the son of that Thetis at whose marriage the apple of Discord had been thrown among the goddesses. Thetis was herself one of the immortals, a sea-nymph, and knowing that her son was fated to perish before Troy if he went on the expedition, she endeavored to prevent his going. She sent him away to the court of King Lycomedes, and induced him to conceal himself in the disguise of a maiden among the daughters of the king. Ulysses, hearing he was there, went disguised as a merchant to the palace and offered for sale female ornaments, among which he had placed some arms. While the king’s daughters were engrossed with the other contents of the merchant’s pack, Achilles handled the weapons and thereby betrayed himself to the keen eye of Ulysses, who found no great difficulty in persuading him to disregard his mother’s prudent counsels and join his countrymen in the war.\n\nPriam was king of Troy, and Paris, the shepherd and seducer of Helen, was his son. Paris had been brought up in obscurity, because there were certain ominous forebodings connected with him from his infancy that he would be the ruin of the state. These forebodings seemed at length likely to be realized, for the Grecian armament now in preparation was the greatest that had ever been fitted out. Agamemnon, king of Mycenæ, and brother of the injured Menelaus, was chosen commander-in-chief. Achilles was their most illustrious warrior. After him ranked Ajax, gigantic in size and of great courage, but dull of intellect; Diomede, second only to Achilles in all the qualities of a hero; Ulysses, famous for his sagacity; and Nestor, the oldest of the Grecian chiefs, and one to whom they all looked up for counsel. But Troy was no feeble enemy. Priam, the king, was now old, but he had been a wise prince and had strengthened his state by good government at home and numerous alliances with his neighbors. But the principal stay and support of his throne was his son Hector, one of the noblest characters painted by heathen antiquity. He felt, from the first, a presentiment of the fall of his country, but still persevered in his heroic resistance, yet by no means justified the wrong which brought this danger upon her. He was united in marriage with Andromache, and as a husband and father his character was not less admirable than as a warrior. The principal leaders on the side of the Trojans, besides Hector, were Æneas and Deiphobus, Glaucus and Sarpedon.\n\nAfter two years of preparation the Greek fleet and army assembled in the port of Aulis in Bœotia. Here Agamemnon in hunting killed a stag which was sacred to Diana, and the goddess in return visited the army with pestilence, and produced a calm which prevented the ships from leaving the port. Calchas, the soothsayer, thereupon announced that the wrath of the virgin goddess could only be appeased by the sacrifice of a virgin on her altar, and that none other but the daughter of the offender would be acceptable. Agamemnon, however reluctant, yielded his consent, and the maiden Iphigenia was sent for under the pretence that she was to be married to Achilles. When she was about to be sacrificed the goddess relented and snatched her away, leaving a hind in her place, and Iphigenia, enveloped in a cloud, was carried to Tauris, where Diana made her priestess of her temple.\n\nTennyson, in his “Dream of Fair Women,” makes Iphigenia thus describe her feelings at the moment of sacrifice:\n\n“I was cut off from hope in that sad place, Which yet to name my spirit loathes and fears; My father held his hand upon his face; I, blinded by my tears,\n“Still strove to speak; my voice was thick with sighs, As in a dream. Dimly I could descry The stern black-bearded kings, with wolfish eyes, Waiting to see me die.\n\n“The tall masts quivered as they lay afloat, The temples and the people and the shore; One drew a sharp knife through my tender throat Slowly,—and—nothing more.”\n\nThe wind now proving fair the fleet made sail and brought the forces to the coast of Troy. The Trojans came to oppose their landing, and at the first onset Protesilaus fell by the hand of Hector. Protesilaus had left at home his wife, Laodamia, who was most tenderly attached to him. When the news of his death reached her she implored the gods to be allowed to converse with him only three hours. The request was granted. Mercury led Protesilaus back to the upper world, and when he died a second time Laodamia died with him. There was a story that the nymphs planted elm trees round his grave which grew very well till they were high enough to command a view of Troy, and then withered away, while fresh branches sprang from the roots.\n\nWordsworth has taken the story of Protesilaus and Laodamia for the subject of a poem. It seems the oracle had declared that victory should be the lot of that party from which should fall the first victim to the war. The poet represents Protesilaus, on his brief return to earth, as relating to Laodamia the story of his fate:\n\n“‘The wished-for wind was given; I then revolved The oracle, upon the silent sea; And if no worthier led the way, resolved That of a thousand vessels mine should be The foremost prow impressing to the strand,— Mine the first blood that tinged the Trojan sand.\n“‘Yet bitter, ofttimes bitter was the pang When of thy loss I thought, beloved wife! On thee too fondly did my memory hang, And on the joys we shared in mortal life, The paths which we had trod,—these fountains, flowers; My new planned cities and unfinished towers.\n\n“‘But should suspense permit the foe to cry, “Behold they tremble! haughty their array, Yet of their number no one dares to die?” In soul I swept the indignity away: Old frailties then recurred: but lofty thought In act embodied my deliverance wrought.’\n\n. . . . . . .\n\n“. . . upon the side Of Hellespont (such faith was entertained) A knot of spiry trees for ages grew From out the tomb of him for whom she died; And ever when such stature they had gained That Ilium’s walls were subject to their view, The trees’ tall summits withered at the sight, A constant interchange of growth and blight!”\nThe war continued without decisive results for nine years. Then an event occurred which seemed likely to be fatal to the cause of the Greeks, and that was a quarrel between Achilles and Agamemnon. It is at this point that the great poem of Homer, “The Iliad,” begins. The Greeks, though unsuccessful against Troy, had taken the neighboring and allied cities, and in the division of the spoil a female captive, by name Chryseis, daughter of Chryses, priest of Apollo, had fallen to the share of Agamemnon. Chryses came bearing the sacred emblems of his office, and begged the release of his daughter. Agamemnon refused. Thereupon Chryses implored Apollo to afflict the Greeks till they should be forced to yield their prey. Apollo granted the prayer of his priest, and sent pestilence into the Grecian camp. Then a council was called to deliberate how to allay the wrath of the gods and avert the plague. Achilles boldly charged their misfortunes upon Agamemnon as caused by his withholding Chryseis. Agamemnon, enraged, consented to relinquish his captive, but demanded that Achilles should yield to him in her stead Briseis, a maiden who had fallen to Achilles’ share in the division of the spoil. Achilles submitted, but forthwith declared that he would take no further part in the war. He withdrew his forces from the general camp and openly avowed his intention of returning home to Greece.\n\nThe gods and goddesses interested themselves as much in this famous war as the parties themselves. It was well known to them that fate had decreed that Troy should fall, at last, if her enemies should persevere and not voluntarily abandon the enterprise. Yet there was room enough left for chance to excite by turns the hopes and fears of the powers above who took part with either side. Juno and Minerva, in consequence of the slight put upon their charms by Paris, were hostile to the Trojans; Venus for the opposite cause favored them. Venus enlisted her admirer Mars on the same side, but Neptune favored the Greeks. Apollo was neutral, sometimes taking one side, sometimes the other, and Jove himself, though he loved the good King Priam, yet exercised a degree of impartiality; not, however, without exceptions.\n\nThetis, the mother of Achilles, warmly resented the injury done to her son. She repaired immediately to Jove’s palace and besought him to make the Greeks repent of their injustice to Achilles by granting success to the Trojan arms. Jupiter consented, and in the battle which ensued the Trojans were completely successful. The Greeks were driven from the field and took refuge in their ships.\n\nThen Agamemnon called a council of his wisest and bravest chiefs. Nestor advised that an embassy should be sent to Achilles to persuade him to return to the field; that Agamemnon should yield the maiden, the cause of the dispute, with ample gifts to atone for the wrong he had done. Agamemnon consented, and Ulysses, Ajax, and Phœnix were sent to carry to Achilles the penitent message. They performed that duty, but Achilles was deaf to their entreaties. He positively refused to return to the field, and persisted in his resolution to embark for Greece without delay.\n\nThe Greeks had constructed a rampart around their ships, and now instead of besieging Troy they were in a manner besieged themselves, within their rampart. The next day after the unsuccessful embassy to Achilles, a battle was fought, and the Trojans, favored by Jove, were successful, and succeeded in forcing a passage through the Grecian rampart, and were about to set fire to the ships. Neptune, seeing the Greeks so pressed, came to their rescue. He appeared in the form of Calchas the prophet, encouraged the warriors with his shouts, and appealed to each individually till he raised their ardor to such a pitch that they forced the Trojans to give way. Ajax performed prodigies of valor, and at length encountered Hector. Ajax shouted defiance, to which Hector replied, and hurled his lance at the huge warrior. It was well aimed and struck Ajax, where the belts that bore his sword and shield crossed each other on the breast. The double guard prevented its penetrating and it fell harmless. Then Ajax, seizing a huge stone, one of those that served to prop the ships, hurled it at Hector. It struck him in the neck and stretched him on the plain. His followers instantly seized him and bore him off, stunned and wounded.\n\nWhile Neptune was thus aiding the Greeks and driving back the Trojans, Jupiter saw nothing of what was going on, for his attention had been drawn from the field by the wiles of Juno. That goddess had arrayed herself in all her charms, and to crown all had borrowed of Venus her girdle, called “Cestus,” which had the effect to heighten the wearer’s charms to such a degree that they were quite irresistible. So prepared, Juno went to join her husband, who sat on Olympus watching the battle. When he beheld her she looked so charming that the fondness of his early love revived, and, forgetting the contending armies and all other affairs of state, he thought only of her and let the battle go as it would.\n\nBut this absorption did not continue long, and when, upon turning his eyes downward, he beheld Hector stretched on the plain almost lifeless from pain and bruises, he dismissed Juno in a rage, commanding her to send Iris and Apollo to him. When Iris came he sent her with a stern message to Neptune, ordering him instantly to quit the field. Apollo was despatched to heal Hector’s bruises and to inspirit his heart. These orders were obeyed with such speed that, while the battle still raged, Hector returned to the field and Neptune betook himself to his own dominions.\n\nAn arrow from Paris’s bow wounded Machaon, son of Æsculapius, who inherited his father’s art of healing, and was therefore of great value to the Greeks as their surgeon, besides being one of their bravest warriors. Nestor took Machaon in his chariot and conveyed him from the field. As they passed the ships of Achilles, that hero, looking out over the field, saw the chariot of Nestor and recognized the old chief, but could not discern who the wounded chief was. So calling Patroclus, his companion and dearest friend, he sent him to Nestor’s tent to inquire.\n\nPatroclus, arriving at Nestor’s tent, saw Machaon wounded, and having told the cause of his coming would have hastened away, but Nestor detained him, to tell him the extent of the Grecian calamities. He reminded him also how, at the time of departing for Troy, Achilles and himself had been charged by their respective fathers with different advice: Achilles to aspire to the highest pitch of glory, Patroclus, as the elder, to keep watch over his friend, and to guide his inexperience. “Now,” said Nestor, “is the time for such influence. If the gods so please, thou mayest win him back to the common cause; but if not let him at least send his soldiers to the field, and come thou, Patroclus, clad in his armor, and perhaps the very sight of it may drive back the Trojans.”\n\nPatroclus was strongly moved with this address, and hastened back to Achilles, revolving in his mind all he had seen and heard. He told the prince the sad condition of affairs at the camp of their late associates: Diomede, Ulysses, Agamemnon, Machaon, all wounded, the rampart broken down, the enemy among the ships preparing to burn them, and thus to cut off all means of return to Greece. While they spoke the flames burst forth from one of the ships. Achilles, at the sight, relented so far as to grant Patroclus his request to lead the Myrmidons (for so were Achilles’ soldiers called) to the field, and to lend him his armor, that he might thereby strike more terror into the minds of the Trojans. Without delay the soldiers were marshalled, Patroclus put on the radiant armor and mounted the chariot of Achilles, and led forth the men ardent for battle. But before he went, Achilles strictly charged him that he should be content with repelling the foe. “Seek not,” said he, “to press the Trojans without me, lest thou add still more to the disgrace already mine.” Then exhorting the troops to do their best he dismissed them full of ardor to the fight.\n\nPatroclus and his Myrmidons at once plunged into the contest where it raged hottest; at the sight of which the joyful Grecians shouted and the ships reëchoed the acclaim. The Trojans, at the sight of the well-known armor, struck with terror, looked everywhere for refuge. First those who had got possession of the ship and set it on fire left and allowed the Grecians to retake it and extinguish the flames. Then the rest of the Trojans fled in dismay. Ajax, Menelaus, and the two sons of Nestor performed prodigies of valor. Hector was forced to turn his horses’ heads and retire from the enclosure, leaving his men entangled in the fosse to escape as they could. Patroclus drove them before him, slaying many, none daring to make a stand against him.\n\nAt last Sarpedon, son of Jove, ventured to oppose himself in fight to Patroclus. Jupiter looked down upon him and would have snatched him from the fate which awaited him, but Juno hinted that if he did so it would induce all others of the inhabitants of heaven to interpose in like manner whenever any of their offspring were endangered; to which reason Jove yielded. Sarpedon threw his spear, but missed Patroclus, but Patroclus threw his with better success. It pierced Sarpedon’s breast and he fell, and, calling to his friends to save his body from the foe, expired. Then a furious contest arose for the possession of the corpse. The Greeks succeeded and stripped Sarpedon of his armor; but Jove would not allow the remains of his son to be dishonored, and by his command Apollo snatched from the midst of the combatants the body of Sarpedon and committed it to the care of the twin brothers Death and Sleep, by whom it was transported to Lycia, the native land of Sarpedon, where it received due funeral rites.\n\nThus far Patroclus had succeeded to his utmost wish in repelling the Trojans and relieving his countrymen, but now came a change of fortune. Hector, borne in his chariot, confronted him. Patroclus threw a vast stone at Hector, which missed its aim, but smote Cebriones, the charioteer, and knocked him from the car. Hector leaped from the chariot to rescue his friend, and Patroclus also descended to complete his victory. Thus the two heroes met face to face. At this decisive moment the poet, as if reluctant to give Hector the glory, records that Phœbus took part against Patroclus. He struck the helmet from his head and the lance from his hand. At the same moment an obscure Trojan wounded him in the back, and Hector, pressing forward, pierced him with his spear. He fell mortally wounded.\n\nThen arose a tremendous conflict for the body of Patroclus, but his armor was at once taken possession of by Hector, who retiring a short distance divested himself of his own armor and put on that of Achilles, then returned to the fight. Ajax and Menelaus defended the body, and Hector and his bravest warriors struggled to capture it. The battle raged with equal fortunes, when Jove enveloped the whole face of heaven with a dark cloud. The lightning flashed, the thunder roared, and Ajax, looking round for some one whom he might despatch to Achilles to tell him of the death of his friend, and of the imminent danger that his remains would fall into the hands of the enemy, could see no suitable messenger. It was then that he exclaimed in those famous lines so often quoted,\n\n“Father of heaven and earth! deliver thou Achaia’s host from darkness; clear the skies; Give day; and, since thy sovereign will is such, Destruction with it; but, O, give us day.” —Cowper.\n\nOr, as rendered by Pope,\n\n“. . . Lord of earth and air! O king! O father! hear my humble prayer! Dispel this cloud, the light of heaven restore; Give me to see and Ajax asks no more; If Greece must perish we thy will obey, But let us perish in the face of day.”\n\nJupiter heard the prayer and dispersed the clouds. Then Ajax sent Antilochus to Achilles with the intelligence of Patroclus’s death, and of the conflict raging for his remains. The Greeks at last succeeded in bearing off the body to the ships, closely pursued by Hector and Æneas and the rest of the Trojans.\n\nAchilles heard the fate of his friend with such distress that Antilochus feared for a while that he would destroy himself. His groans reached the ears of his mother, Thetis, far down in the deeps of ocean where she abode, and she hastened to him to inquire the cause. She found him overwhelmed with self-reproach that he had indulged his resentment so far, and suffered his friend to fall a victim to it. But his only consolation was the hope of revenge. He would fly instantly in search of Hector. But his mother reminded him that he was now without armor, and promised him, if he would but wait till the morrow, she would procure for him a suit of armor from Vulcan more than equal to that he had lost. He consented, and Thetis immediately repaired to Vulcan’s palace. She found him busy at his forge making tripods for his own use, so artfully constructed that they moved forward of their own accord when wanted, and retired again when dismissed. On hearing the request of Thetis, Vulcan immediately laid aside his work and hastened to comply with her wishes. He fabricated a splendid suit of armor for Achilles, first a shield adorned with elaborate devices, then a helmet crested with gold, then a corselet and greaves of impenetrable temper, all perfectly adapted to his form, and of consummate workmanship. It was all done in one night, and Thetis, receiving it, descended with it to earth, and laid it down at Achilles’ feet at the dawn of day.\n\nThe first glow of pleasure that Achilles had felt since the death of Patroclus was at the sight of this splendid armor. And now, arrayed in it, he went forth into the camp, calling all the chiefs to council. When they were all assembled he addressed them. Renouncing his displeasure against Agamemnon and bitterly lamenting the miseries that had resulted from it, he called on them to proceed at once to the field. Agamemnon made a suitable reply, laying all the blame on Ate, the goddess of discord; and thereupon complete reconcilement took place between the heroes.\n\nThen Achilles went forth to battle inspired with a rage and thirst for vengeance that made him irresistible. The bravest warriors fled before him or fell by his lance. Hector, cautioned by Apollo, kept aloof; but the god, assuming the form of one of Priam’s sons, Lycaon, urged Æneas to encounter the terrible warrior. Æneas, though he felt himself unequal, did not decline the combat. He hurled his spear with all his force against the shield the work of Vulcan. It was formed of five metal plates; two were of brass, two of tin, and one of gold. The spear pierced two thicknesses, but was stopped in the third. Achilles threw his with better success. It pierced through the shield of Æneas, but glanced near his shoulder and made no wound. Then Æneas seized a stone, such as two men of modern times could hardly lift, and was about to throw it, and Achilles, with sword drawn, was about to rush upon him, when Neptune, who looked out upon the contest, moved with pity for Æneas, who he saw would surely fall a victim if not speedily rescued, spread a cloud between the combatants, and lifting Æneas from the ground, bore him over the heads of warriors and steeds to the rear of the battle. Achilles, when the mist cleared away, looked round in vain for his adversary, and acknowledging the prodigy, turned his arms against other champions. But none dared stand before him, and Priam looking down from the city walls beheld his whole army in full flight towards the city. He gave command to open wide the gates to receive the fugitives, and to shut them as soon as the Trojans should have passed, lest the enemy should enter likewise. But Achilles was so close in pursuit that that would have been impossible if Apollo had not, in the form of Agenor, Priam’s son, encountered Achilles for a while, then turned to fly, and taken the way apart from the city. Achilles pursued and had chased his supposed victim far from the walls, when Apollo disclosed himself, and Achilles, perceiving how he had been deluded, gave up the chase.\n\nBut when the rest had escaped into the town Hector stood without determined to await the combat. His old father called to him from the walls and begged him to retire nor tempt the encounter. His mother, Hecuba, also besought him to the same effect, but all in vain. “How can I,” said he to himself, “by whose command the people went to this day’s contest, where so many have fallen, seek safety for myself against a single foe? But what if I offer him to yield up Helen and all her treasures and ample of our own beside? Ah, no! it is too late. He would not even hear me through, but slay me while I spoke.” While he thus ruminated, Achilles approached, terrible as Mars, his armor flashing lightning as he moved. At that sight Hector’s heart failed him and he fled. Achilles swiftly pursued. They ran, still keeping near the walls, till they had thrice encircled the city. As often as Hector approached the walls Achilles intercepted him and forced him to keep out in a wider circle. But Apollo sustained Hector’s strength and would not let him sink in weariness. Then Pallas, assuming the form of Deiphobus, Hector’s bravest brother, appeared suddenly at his side. Hector saw him with delight, and thus strengthened stopped his flight and turned to meet Achilles. Hector threw his spear, which struck the shield of Achilles and bounded back. He turned to receive another from the hand of Deiphobus, but Deiphobus was gone. Then Hector understood his doom and said, “Alas! it is plain this is my hour to die! I thought Deiphobus at hand, but Pallas deceived me, and he is still in Troy. But I will not fall inglorious.” So saying he drew his falchion from his side and rushed at once to combat. Achilles, secured behind his shield, waited the approach of Hector. When he came within reach of his spear, Achilles choosing with his eye a vulnerable part where the armor leaves the neck uncovered, aimed his spear at that part and Hector fell, death-wounded, and feebly said, “Spare my body! Let my parents ransom it, and let me receive funeral rites from the sons and daughters of Troy.” To which Achilles replied, “Dog, name not ransom nor pity to me, on whom you have brought such dire distress. No! trust me, naught shall save thy carcass from the dogs. Though twenty ransoms and thy weight in gold were offered, I would refuse it all.”\n\nSo saying he stripped the body of its armor, and fastening cords to the feet tied them behind his chariot, leaving the body to trail along the ground. Then mounting the chariot he lashed the steeds and so dragged the body to and fro before the city. What words can tell the grief of King Priam and Queen Hecuba at this sight! His people could scarce restrain the old king from rushing forth. He threw himself in the dust and besought them each by name to give him way. Hecuba’s distress was not less violent. The citizens stood round them weeping. The sound of the mourning reached the ears of Andromache, the wife of Hector, as she sat among her maidens at work, and anticipating evil she went forth to the wall. When she saw the sight there presented, she would have thrown herself headlong from the wall, but fainted and fell into the arms of her maidens. Recovering, she bewailed her fate, picturing to herself her country ruined, herself a captive, and her son dependent for his bread on the charity of strangers.\n\nWhen Achilles and the Greeks had taken their revenge on the killer of Patroclus they busied themselves in paying due funeral rites to their friend. A pile was erected, and the body burned with due solemnity; and then ensued games of strength and skill, chariot races, wrestling, boxing, and archery. Then the chiefs sat down to the funeral banquet and after that retired to rest. But Achilles neither partook of the feast nor of sleep. The recollection of his lost friend kept him awake, remembering their companionship in toil and dangers, in battle or on the perilous deep. Before the earliest dawn he left his tent, and joining to his chariot his swift steeds, he fastened Hector’s body to be dragged behind. Twice he dragged him around the tomb of Patroclus, leaving him at length stretched in the dust. But Apollo would not permit the body to be torn or disfigured with all this abuse, but preserved it free from all taint or defilement.\n\nWhile Achilles indulged his wrath in thus disgracing brave Hector, Jupiter in pity summoned Thetis to his presence. He told her to go to her son and prevail on him to restore the body of Hector to his friends. Then Jupiter sent Iris to King Priam to encourage him to go to Achilles and beg the body of his son. Iris delivered her message, and Priam immediately prepared to obey. He opened his treasuries and took out rich garments and cloths, with ten talents in gold and two splendid tripods and a golden cup of matchless workmanship. Then he called to his sons and bade them draw forth his litter and place in it the various articles designed for a ransom to Achilles. When all was ready, the old king with a single companion as aged as himself, the herald Idæus, drove forth from the gates, parting there with Hecuba, his queen, and all his friends, who lamented him as going to certain death.\n\nBut Jupiter, beholding with compassion the venerable king, sent Mercury to be his guide and protector. Mercury, assuming the form of a young warrior, presented himself to the aged couple, and while at the sight of him they hesitated whether to fly or yield, the god approached, and grasping Priam’s hand offered to be their guide to Achilles’ tent. Priam gladly accepted his offered service, and he, mounting the carriage, assumed the reins and soon conveyed them to the tent of Achilles. Mercury’s wand put to sleep all the guards, and without hinderance he introduced Priam into the tent where Achilles sat, attended by two of his warriors. The old king threw himself at the feet of Achilles, and kissed those terrible hands which had destroyed so many of his sons. “Think, O Achilles,” he said, “of thy own father, full of days like me, and trembling on the gloomy verge of life. Perhaps even now some neighbor chief oppresses him and there is none at hand to succor him in his distress. Yet doubtless knowing that Achilles lives he still rejoices, hoping that one day he shall see thy face again. But no comfort cheers me, whose bravest sons, so late the flower of Ilium, all have fallen. Yet one I had, one more than all the rest the strength of my age, whom, fighting for his country, thou hast slain. I come to redeem his body, bringing inestimable ransom with me. Achilles! reverence the gods! recollect thy father! for his sake show compassion to me!” These words moved Achilles, and he wept; remembering by turns his absent father and his lost friend. Moved with pity of Priam’s silver locks and beard, he raised him from the earth, and thus spake: “Priam, I know that thou hast reached this place conducted by some god, for without aid divine no mortal even in his prime of youth had dared the attempt. I grant thy request, moved thereto by the evident will of Jove.” So saying he arose, and went forth with his two friends, and unloaded of its charge the litter, leaving two mantles and a robe for the covering of the body, which they placed on the litter, and spread the garments over it, that not unveiled it should be borne back to Troy. Then Achilles dismissed the old king with his attendants, having first pledged himself to allow a truce of twelve days for the funeral solemnities.\n\nAs the litter approached the city and was descried from the walls, the people poured forth to gaze once more on the face of their hero. Foremost of all, the mother and the wife of Hector came, and at the sight of the lifeless body renewed their lamentations. The people all wept with them, and to the going down of the sun there was no pause or abatement of their grief.\n\nThe next day preparations were made for the funeral solemnities. For nine days the people brought wood and built the pile, and on the tenth they placed the body on the summit and applied the torch; while all Troy thronging forth encompassed the pile. When it had completely burned, they quenched the cinders with wine, collected the bones and placed them in a golden urn, which they buried in the earth, and reared a pile of stones over the spot.\n\n“Such honors Ilium to her hero paid, And peaceful slept the mighty Hector’s shade.” —Pope.\n\n————\n\n# THE FALL OF TROY\n\nTHE story of the Iliad ends with the death of Hector, and it is from the Odyssey and later poems that we learn the fate of the other heroes. After the death of Hector, Troy did not immediately fall, but receiving aid from new allies still continued its resistance. One of these allies was Memnon, the Æthiopian prince, whose story we have already told. Another was Penthesilea, queen of the Amazons, who came with a band of female warriors. All the authorities attest their valor and the fearful effect of their war cry. Penthesilea slew many of the bravest warriors, but was at last slain by Achilles. But when the hero bent over his fallen foe, and contemplated her beauty, youth, and valor, he bitterly regretted his victory. Thersites, an insolent brawler and demagogue, ridiculed his grief, and was in consequence slain by the hero.\n\nAchilles by chance had seen Polyxena, daughter of King Priam, perhaps on the occasion of the truce which was allowed the Trojans for the burial of Hector. He was captivated with her charms, and to win her in marriage agreed to use his influence with the Greeks to grant peace to Troy. While in the temple of Apollo, negotiating the marriage, Paris discharged at him a poisoned arrow, which, guided by Apollo, wounded Achilles in the heel, the only vulnerable part about him. For Thetis his mother had dipped him when an infant in the river Styx, which made every part of him invulnerable except the heel by which she held him.[21]\n\nThe body of Achilles so treacherously slain was rescued by Ajax and Ulysses. Thetis directed the Greeks to bestow her son’s armor on the hero who of all the survivors should be judged most deserving of it. Ajax and Ulysses were the only claimants; a select number of the other chiefs were appointed to award the prize. It was awarded to Ulysses, thus placing wisdom before valor; whereupon Ajax slew himself. On the spot where his blood sank into the earth a flower sprang up, called the hyacinth, bearing on its leaves the first two letters of the name of Ajax, Ai, the Greek for “woe.” Thus Ajax is a claimant with the boy Hyacinthus for the honor of giving birth to this flower. There is a species of Larkspur which represents the hyacinth of the poets in preserving the memory of this event, the Delphinium Ajacis—Ajax’s Larkspur.\n\nIt was now discovered that Troy could not be taken but by the aid of the arrows of Hercules. They were in possession of Philoctetes, the friend who had been with Hercules at the last and lighted his funeral pyre. Philoctetes had joined the Grecian expedition against Troy, but had accidentally wounded his foot with one of the poisoned arrows, and the smell from his wound proved so offensive that his companions carried him to the isle of Lemnos and left him there. Diomed was now sent to induce him to rejoin the army. He succeeded. Philoctetes was cured of his wound by Machaon, and Paris was the first victim of the fatal arrows. In his distress Paris bethought him of one whom in his prosperity he had forgotten. This was the nymph Œnone, whom he had married when a youth, and had abandoned for the fatal beauty Helen. Œnone, remembering the wrongs she had suffered, refused to heal the wound, and Paris went back to Troy and died. Œnone quickly repented, and hastened after him with remedies, but came too late, and in her grief hung herself.[22]\n\nThere was in Troy a celebrated statue of Minerva called the Palladium. It was said to have fallen from heaven, and the belief was that the city could not be taken so long as this statue remained within it. Ulysses and Diomed entered the city in disguise and succeeded in obtaining the Palladium, which they carried off to the Grecian camp.\n\nBut Troy still held out, and the Greeks began to despair of ever subduing it by force, and by advice of Ulysses resolved to resort to stratagem. They pretended to be making preparations to abandon the siege, and a portion of the ships were withdrawn and lay hid behind a neighboring island. The Greeks then constructed an immense wooden horse, which they gave out was intended as a propitiatory offering to Minerva, but in fact was filled with armed men. The remaining Greeks then betook themselves to their ships and sailed away, as if for a final departure. The Trojans, seeing the encampment broken up and the fleet gone, concluded the enemy to have abandoned the siege. The gates were thrown open, and the whole population issued forth rejoicing at the long-prohibited liberty of passing freely over the scene of the late encampment. The great horse was the chief object of curiosity. All wondered what it could be for. Some recommended to take it into the city as a trophy; others felt afraid of it.\n\nWhile they hesitate, Laocoön, the priest of Neptune exclaims, “What madness, citizens, is this? Have you not learned enough of Grecian fraud to be on your guard against it? For my part, I fear the Greeks even when they offer gifts.”[23] So saying he threw his lance at the horse’s side. It struck, and a hollow sound reverberated like a groan. Then perhaps the people might have taken his advice and destroyed the fatal horse and all its contents; but just at that moment a group of people appeared, dragging forward one who seemed a prisoner and a Greek. Stupefied with terror, he was brought before the chiefs, who reassured him, promising that his life should be spared on condition of his returning true answers to the questions asked him. He informed them that he was a Greek, Sinon by name, and that in consequence of the malice of Ulysses he had been left behind by his countrymen at their departure. With regard to the wooden horse, he told them that it was a propitiatory offering to Minerva, and made so huge for the express purpose of preventing its being carried within the city; for Calchas the prophet had told them that if the Trojans took possession of it they would assuredly triumph over the Greeks. This language turned the tide of the people’s feelings and they began to think how they might best secure the monstrous horse and the favorable auguries connected with it, when suddenly a prodigy occurred which left no room to doubt. There appeared, advancing over the sea, two immense serpents. They came upon the land, and the crowd fled in all directions. The serpents advanced directly to the spot where Laocoön stood with his two sons. They first attacked the children, winding round their bodies and breathing their pestilential breath in their faces. The father, attempting to rescue them, is next seized and involved in the serpents’ coils. He struggles to tear them away, but they overpower all his efforts and strangle him and the children in their poisonous folds. This event was regarded as a clear indication of the displeasure of the gods at Laocoön’s irreverent treatment of the wooden horse, which they no longer hesitated to regard as a sacred object, and prepared to introduce with due solemnity into the city. This was done with songs and triumphal acclamations, and the day closed with festivity. In the night the armed men who were enclosed in the body of the horse, being let out by the traitor Sinon, opened the gates of the city to their friends, who had returned under cover of the night. The city was set on fire; the people, overcome with feasting and sleep, put to the sword, and Troy completely subdued.\n\nOne of the most celebrated groups of statuary in existence is that of Laocoön and his children in the embrace of the serpents. A cast of it is owned by the Boston Athenæum; the original is in the Vatican at Rome. The following lines are from the “Childe Harold” of Byron:\n\n“Now turning to the Vatican go see Laocoön’s torture dignifying pain; A father’s love and mortal’s agony With an immortal’s patience blending;—vain The struggle! vain against the coiling strain And gripe and deepening of the dragon’s grasp The old man’s clinch; the long envenomed chain Rivets the living links; the enormous asp Enforces pang on pang and stifles gasp on gasp.”\n\nThe comic poets will also occasionally borrow a classical allusion. The following is from Swift’s “Description of a City Shower”:\n\n“Boxed in a chair the beau impatient sits, While spouts run clattering o’er the roof by fits, And ever and anon with frightful din The leather sounds; he trembles from within. So when Troy chairmen bore the wooden steed Pregnant with Greeks impatient to be freed, (Those bully Greeks, who, as the moderns do, Instead of paying chairmen, run them through); Laocoön struck the outside with a spear, And each imprisoned champion quaked with fear.”\n\nKing Priam lived to see the downfall of his kingdom and was slain at last on the fatal night when the Greeks took the city. He had armed himself and was about to mingle with the combatants, but was prevailed on by Hecuba, his aged queen, to take refuge with herself and his daughters as a suppliant at the altar of Jupiter. While there, his youngest son Polites, pursued by Pyrrhus, the son of Achilles, rushed in wounded, and expired at the feet of his father; whereupon Priam, overcome with indignation, hurled his spear with feeble hand against Pyrrhus,[24] and was forthwith slain by him.\n\nQueen Hecuba and her daughter Cassandra were carried captives to Greece. Cassandra had been loved by Apollo, and he gave her the gift of prophecy; but afterwards offended with her, he rendered the gift unavailing by ordaining that her predictions should never be believed. Polyxena, another daughter, who had been loved by Achilles, was demanded by the ghost of that warrior, and was sacrificed by the Greeks upon his tomb.\n\n# MENELAUS AND HELEN\n\nOur readers will be anxious to know the fate of Helen, the fair but guilty occasion of so much slaughter. On the fall of Troy Menelaus recovered possession of his wife, who had not ceased to love him, though she had yielded to the might of Venus and deserted him for another. After the death of Paris she aided the Greeks secretly on several occasions, and in particular when Ulysses and Diomed entered the city in disguise to carry off the Palladium. She saw and recognized Ulysses, but kept the secret and even assisted them in obtaining the image. Thus she became reconciled to her husband, and they were among the first to leave the shores of Troy for their native land. But having incurred the displeasure of the gods they were driven by storms from shore to shore of the Mediterranean, visiting Cyprus, Phœnicia, and Egypt. In Egypt they were kindly treated and presented with rich gifts, of which Helen’s share was a golden spindle and a basket on wheels. The basket was to hold the wool and spools for the queen’s work.\n\nDyer, in his poem of the “Fleece,” thus alludes to this incident:\n\n“. . . many yet adhere To the ancient distaff, at the bosom fixed, Casting the whirling spindle as they walk. . . . . . . . This was of old, in no inglorious days, The mode of spinning, when the Egyptian prince A golden distaff gave that beauteous nymph, Too beauteous Helen; no uncourtly gift.”\n\nMilton also alludes to a famous recipe for an invigorating draught, called Nepenthe, which the Egyptian queen gave to Helen:\n\n“Not that Nepenthes which the wife of Thone In Egypt gave to Jove-born Helena, Is of such power to stir up joy as this, To life so friendly or so cool to thirst.” —Comus.\n\nMenelaus and Helen at length arrived in safety at Sparta, resumed their royal dignity, and lived and reigned in splendor; and when Telemachus, the son of Ulysses, in search of his father, arrived at Sparta, he found Menelaus and Helen celebrating the marriage of their daughter Hermione to Neoptolemus, son of Achilles.\n\n# AGAMEMNON, ORESTES, AND ELECTRA\n\nAgamemnon, the general-in-chief of the Greeks, the brother of Menelaus, and who had been drawn into the quarrel to avenge his brother’s wrongs, not his own, was not so fortunate in the issue. During his absence his wife Clytemnestra had been false to him, and when his return was expected, she with her paramour, Ægisthus, laid a plan for his destruction, and at the banquet given to celebrate his return, murdered him.\n\nIt was intended by the conspirators to slay his son Orestes also, a lad not yet old enough to be an object of apprehension, but from whom, if he should be suffered to grow up, there might be danger. Electra, the sister of Orestes, saved her brother’s life by sending him secretly away to his uncle Strophius, King of Phocis. In the palace of Strophius Orestes grew up with the king’s son Pylades, and formed with him that ardent friendship which has become proverbial. Electra frequently reminded her brother by messengers of the duty of avenging his father’s death, and when grown up he consulted the oracle of Delphi, which confirmed him in his design. He therefore repaired in disguise to Argos, pretending to be a messenger from Strophius, who had come to announce the death of Orestes, and brought the ashes of the deceased in a funeral urn. After visiting his father’s tomb and sacrificing upon it, according to the rites of the ancients, he made himself known to his sister Electra, and soon after slew both Ægisthus and Clytemnestra.\n\nThis revolting act, the slaughter of a mother by her son, though alleviated by the guilt of the victim and the express command of the gods, did not fail to awaken in the breasts of the ancients the same abhorrence that it does in ours. The Eumenides, avenging deities, seized upon Orestes, and drove him frantic from land to land. Pylades accompanied him in his wanderings and watched over him. At length, in answer to a second appeal to the oracle, he was directed to go to Tauris in Scythia, and to bring thence a statue of Diana which was believed to have fallen from heaven. Accordingly Orestes and Pylades went to Tauris, where the barbarous people were accustomed to sacrifice to the goddess all strangers who fell into their hands. The two friends were seized and carried bound to the temple to be made victims. But the priestess of Diana was no other than Iphigenia, the sister of Orestes, who, our readers will remember, was snatched away by Diana at the moment when she was about to be sacrificed. Ascertaining from the prisoners who they were, Iphigenia disclosed herself to them, and the three made their escape with the statue of the goddess, and returned to Mycenæ.\n\nBut Orestes was not yet relieved from the vengeance of the Erinyes. At length he took refuge with Minerva at Athens. The goddess afforded him protection, and appointed the court of Areopagus to decide his fate. The Erinyes brought forward their accusation, and Orestes made the command of the Delphic oracle his excuse. When the court voted and the voices were equally divided, Orestes was acquitted by the command of Minerva.\n\nByron, in “Childe Harold,” Canto IV., alludes to the story of Orestes:\n\n“O thou who never yet of human wrong Left the unbalanced scale, great Nemesis! Thou who didst call the Furies from the abyss, And round Orestes bade them howl and hiss, For that unnatural retribution,—just, Had it but been from hands less near,—in this, Thy former realm, I call thee from the dust!”\n\nOne of the most pathetic scenes in the ancient drama is that in which Sophocles represents the meeting of Orestes and Electra, on his return from Phocis. Orestes, mistaking Electra for one of the domestics, and desirous of keeping his arrival a secret till the hour of vengeance should arrive, produces the urn in which his ashes are supposed to rest. Electra, believing him to be really dead, takes the urn and, embracing it, pours forth her grief in language full of tenderness and despair.\n\nMilton, in one of his sonnets, says:\n\n“. . . The repeated air Of sad Electra’s poet had the power To save the Athenian walls from ruin bare.”\n\nThis alludes to the story that when, on one occasion, the city of Athens was at the mercy of her Spartan foes, and it was proposed to destroy it, the thought was rejected upon the accidental quotation, by some one, of a chorus of Euripides.\n\nThe facts relating to the city of Troy are still unknown to history. Antiquarians have long sought for the actual city and some record of its rulers. The most interesting explorations were those conducted about 1890 by the German scholar, Henry Schliemann, who believed that at the mound of Hissarlik, the traditional site of Troy, he had uncovered the ancient capital. Schliemann excavated down below the ruins of three or four settlements, each revealing an earlier civilization, and finally came upon some royal jewels and other relics said to be “Priam’s Treasure.” Scholars are by no means agreed as to the historic value of these discoveries.\n\n————\n\n# CHAPTER XXIX\n\nADVENTURES OF ULYSSES—THE LOTUS-EATERS—CYCLOPES—CIRCE—SIRENS—SCYLLA AND CHARYBDIS—CALYPSO\nTHE romantic poem of the Odyssey is now to engage our attention. It narrates the wanderings of Ulysses (Odysseus in the Greek language) in his return from Troy to his own kingdom Ithaca.\n\nFrom Troy the vessels first made land at Ismarus, city of the Ciconians, where, in a skirmish with the inhabitants, Ulysses lost six men from each ship. Sailing thence, they were overtaken by a storm which drove them for nine days along the sea till they reached the country of the Lotus-eaters. Here, after watering, Ulysses sent three of his men to discover who the inhabitants were. These men on coming among the Lotus-eaters were kindly entertained by them, and were given some of their own food, the lotus plant, to eat. The effect of this food was such that those who partook of it lost all thoughts of home and wished to remain in that country. It was by main force that Ulysses dragged these men away, and he was even obliged to tie them under the benches of the ships.[25]\n\nThey next arrived at the country of the Cyclopes. The Cyclopes were giants, who inhabited an island of which they were the only possessors. The name means “round eye,” and these giants were so called because they had but one eye, and that placed in the middle of the forehead. They dwelt in caves and fed on the wild productions of the island and on what their flocks yielded, for they were shepherds. Ulysses left the main body of his ships at anchor, and with one vessel went to the Cyclopes’ island to explore for supplies. He landed with his companions, carrying with them a jar of wine for a present, and coming to a large cave they entered it, and finding no one within examined its contents. They found it stored with the richest of the flock, quantities of cheese, pails and bowls of milk, lambs and kids in their pens, all in nice order. Presently arrived the master of the cave, Polyphemus, bearing an immense bundle of firewood, which he threw down before the cavern’s mouth. He then drove into the cave the sheep and goats to be milked, and, entering, rolled to the cave’s mouth an enormous rock, that twenty oxen could not draw. Next he sat down and milked his ewes, preparing a part for cheese, and setting the rest aside for his customary drink. Then, turning round his great eye, he discerned the strangers, and growled out to them, demanding who they were, and where from. Ulysses replied most humbly, stating that they were Greeks, from the great expedition that had lately won so much glory in the conquest of Troy; that they were now on their way home, and finished by imploring his hospitality in the name of the gods. Polyphemus deigned no answer, but reaching out his hand seized two of the Greeks, whom he hurled against the side of the cave, and dashed out their brains. He proceeded to devour them with great relish, and having made a hearty meal, stretched himself out on the floor to sleep. Ulysses was tempted to seize the opportunity and plunge his sword into him as he slept, but recollected that it would only expose them all to certain destruction, as the rock with which the giant had closed up the door was far beyond their power to remove, and they would therefore be in hopeless imprisonment. Next morning the giant seized two more of the Greeks, and despatched them in the same manner as their companions, feasting on their flesh till no fragment was left. He then moved away the rock from the door, drove out his flocks, and went out, carefully replacing the barrier after him. When he was gone Ulysses planned how he might take vengeance for his murdered friends, and effect his escape with his surviving companions. He made his men prepare a massive bar of wood cut by the Cyclops for a staff, which they found in the cave. They sharpened the end of it, and seasoned it in the fire, and hid it under the straw on the cavern floor. Then four of the boldest were selected, with whom Ulysses joined himself as a fifth. The Cyclops came home at evening, rolled away the stone and drove in his flock as usual. After milking them and making his arrangements as before, he seized two more of Ulysses’ companions and dashed their brains out, and made his evening meal upon them as he had on the others. After he had supped, Ulysses approaching him handed him a bowl of wine, saying, “Cyclops, this is wine; taste and drink after thy meal of men’s flesh.” He took and drank it, and was hugely delighted with it, and called for more. Ulysses supplied him once again, which pleased the giant so much that he promised him as a favor that he should be the last of the party devoured. He asked his name, to which Ulysses replied, “My name is Noman.”\n\nAfter his supper the giant lay down to repose, and was soon sound asleep. Then Ulysses with his four select friends thrust the end of the stake into the fire till it was all one burning coal, then poising it exactly above the giant’s only eye, they buried it deeply into the socket, twirling it round as a carpenter does his auger. The howling monster with his outcry filled the cavern, and Ulysses with his aids nimbly got out of his way and concealed themselves in the cave. He, bellowing, called aloud on all the Cyclopes dwelling in the caves around him, far and near. They on his cry flocked round the den, and inquired what grievous hurt had caused him to sound such an alarm and break their slumbers. He replied, “O friends, I die, and Noman gives the blow.” They answered, “If no man hurts thee it is the stroke of Jove, and thou must bear it.” So saying, they left him groaning.\n\nNext morning the Cyclops rolled away the stone to let his flock out to pasture, but planted himself in the door of the cave to feel of all as they went out, that Ulysses and his men should not escape with them. But Ulysses had made his men harness the rams of the flock three abreast, with osiers which they found on the floor of the cave. To the middle ram of the three one of the Greeks suspended himself, so protected by the exterior rams on either side. As they passed, the giant felt of the animals’ backs and sides, but never thought of their bellies; so the men all passed safe, Ulysses himself being on the last one that passed. When they had got a few paces from the cavern, Ulysses and his friends released themselves from their rams, and drove a good part of the flock down to the shore to their boat. They put them aboard with all haste, then pushed off from the shore, and when at a safe distance Ulysses shouted out, “Cyclops, the gods have well requited thee for thy atrocious deeds. Know it is Ulysses to whom thou owest thy shameful loss of sight.” The Cyclops, hearing this, seized a rock that projected from the side of the mountain, and rending it from its bed, he lifted it high in the air, then exerting all his force, hurled it in the direction of the voice. Down came the mass, just clearing the vessel’s stern. The ocean, at the plunge of the huge rock, heaved the ship towards the land, so that it barely escaped being swamped by the waves. When they had with the utmost difficulty pulled off shore, Ulysses was about to hail the giant again, but his friends besought him not to do so. He could not forbear, however, letting the giant know that they had escaped his missile, but waited till they had reached a safer distance than before. The giant answered them with curses, but Ulysses and his friends plied their oars vigorously, and soon regained their companions.\n\nUlysses next arrived at the island of Æolus. To this monarch Jupiter had intrusted the government of the winds, to send them forth or retain them at his will. He treated Ulysses hospitably, and at his departure gave him, tied up in a leathern bag, with a silver string, such winds as might be hurtful and dangerous, commanding fair winds to blow the barks towards their country. Nine days they sped before the wind, and all that time Ulysses had stood at the helm, without sleep. At last quite exhausted he lay down to sleep. While he slept, the crew conferred together about the mysterious bag, and concluded it must contain treasures given by the hospitable king Æolus to their commander. Tempted to secure some portion for themselves, they loosed the string, when immediately the winds rushed forth. The ships were driven far from their course, and back again to the island they had just left. Æolus was so indignant at their folly that he refused to assist them further, and they were obliged to labor over their course once more by means of their oars.\n\nTheir next adventure was with the barbarous tribe of Læstrygonians. The vessels all pushed into the harbor, tempted by the secure appearance of the cove, completely land-locked; only Ulysses moored his vessel without. As soon as the Læstrygonians found the ships completely in their power they attacked them, heaving huge stones which broke and overturned them, and with their spears despatched the seamen as they struggled in the water. All the vessels with their crews were destroyed, except Ulysses’ own ship, which had remained outside, and finding no safety but in flight, he exhorted his men to ply their oars vigorously, and they escaped.\n\nWith grief for their slain companions mixed with joy at their own escape, they pursued their way till they arrived at the Ææan isle, where Circe dwelt, the daughter of the sun. Landing here, Ulysses climbed a hill, and gazing round saw no signs of habitation except in one spot at the centre of the island, where he perceived a palace embowered with trees. He sent forward one-half of his crew, under the command of Eurylochus, to see what prospect of hospitality they might find. As they approached the palace, they found themselves surrounded by lions, tigers, and wolves, not fierce, but tamed by Circe’s art, for she was a powerful magician. All these animals had once been men, but had been changed by Circe’s enchantments into the forms of beasts. The sounds of soft music were heard from within, and a sweet female voice singing. Eurylochus called aloud and the goddess came forth and invited them in; they all gladly entered except Eurylochus, who suspected danger. The goddess conducted her guests to a seat, and had them served with wine and other delicacies. When they had feasted heartily, she touched them one by one with her wand, and they became immediately changed into swine, in “head, body, voice, and bristles,” yet with their intellects as before. She shut them in her sties and supplied them with acorns and such other things as swine love.\n\nEurylochus hurried back to the ship and told the tale. Ulysses thereupon determined to go himself, and try if by any means he might deliver his companions. As he strode onward alone, he met a youth who addressed him familiarly, appearing to be acquainted with his adventures. He announced himself as Mercury, and informed Ulysses of the arts of Circe, and of the danger of approaching her. As Ulysses was not to be dissuaded from his attempt, Mercury provided him with a sprig of the plant Moly, of wonderful power to resist sorceries, and instructed him how to act. Ulysses proceeded, and reaching the palace was courteously received by Circe, who entertained him as she had done his companions, and after he had eaten and drank, touched him with her wand, saying, “Hence, seek the sty and wallow with thy friends.” But he, instead of obeying, drew his sword and rushed upon her with fury in his countenance. She fell on her knees and begged for mercy. He dictated a solemn oath that she would release his companions and practise no further harm against him or them; and she repeated it, at the same time promising to dismiss them all in safety after hospitably entertaining them. She was as good as her word. The men were restored to their shapes, the rest of the crew summoned from the shore, and the whole magnificently entertained day after day, till Ulysses seemed to have forgotten his native land, and to have reconciled himself to an inglorious life of ease and pleasure.\n\nAt length his companions recalled him to nobler sentiments, and he received their admonition gratefully. Circe aided their departure, and instructed them how to pass safely by the coast of the Sirens. The Sirens were sea-nymphs who had the power of charming by their song all who heard them, so that the unhappy mariners were irresistibly impelled to cast themselves into the sea to their destruction. Circe directed Ulysses to fill the ears of his seamen with wax, so that they should not hear the strain; and to cause himself to be bound to the mast, and his people to be strictly enjoined, whatever he might say or do, by no means to release him till they should have passed the Sirens’ island. Ulysses obeyed these directions. He filled the ears of his people with wax, and suffered them to bind him with cords firmly to the mast. As they approached the Sirens’ island, the sea was calm, and over the waters came the notes of music so ravishing and attractive that Ulysses struggled to get loose, and by cries and signs to his people begged to be released; but they, obedient to his previous orders, sprang forward and bound him still faster. They held on their course, and the music grew fainter till it ceased to be heard, when with joy Ulysses gave his companions the signal to unseal their ears, and they relieved him from his bonds.\n\nThe imagination of a modern poet, Keats, has discovered for us the thoughts that passed through the brains of the victims of Circe, after their transformation. In his “Endymion” he represents one of them, a monarch in the guise of an elephant, addressing the sorceress in human language, thus:\n\n“I sue not for my happy crown again; I sue not for my phalanx on the plain; I sue not for my lone, my widowed wife; I sue not for my ruddy drops of life, My children fair, my lovely girls and boys; I will forget them; I will pass these joys, Ask nought so heavenward; so too—too high; Only I pray, as fairest boon, to die; To be delivered from this cumbrous flesh, From this gross, detestable, filthy mesh, And merely given to the cold, bleak air. Have mercy, goddess! Circe, feel my prayer!”\n\n# SCYLLA AND CHARYBDIS\n\nUlysses had been warned by Circe of the two monsters Scylla and Charybdis. We have already met with Scylla in the story of Glaucus, and remember that she was once a beautiful maiden and was changed into a snaky monster by Circe. She dwelt in a cave high up on the cliff, from whence she was accustomed to thrust forth her long necks (for she had six heads), and in each of her mouths to seize one of the crew of every vessel passing within reach. The other terror, Charybdis, was a gulf, nearly on a level with the water. Thrice each day the water rushed into a frightful chasm, and thrice was disgorged. Any vessel coming near the whirlpool when the tide was rushing in must inevitably be ingulfed; not Neptune himself could save it.\n\nOn approaching the haunt of the dread monsters, Ulysses kept strict watch to discover them. The roar of the waters as Charybdis ingulfed them, gave warning at a distance, but Scylla could nowhere be discerned. While Ulysses and his men watched with anxious eyes the dreadful whirlpool, they were not equally on their guard from the attack of Scylla, and the monster, darting forth her snaky heads, caught six of his men, and bore them away, shrieking, to her den. It was the saddest sight Ulysses had yet seen; to behold his friends thus sacrificed and hear their cries, unable to afford them any assistance.\n\nCirce had warned him of another danger. After passing Scylla and Charybdis the next land he would make was Thrinakia, an island whereon were pastured the cattle of Hyperion, the Sun, tended by his daughters Lampetia and Phaëthusa. These flocks must not be violated, whatever the wants of the voyagers might be. If this injunction were transgressed destruction was sure to fall on the offenders.\n\nUlysses would willingly have passed the island of the Sun without stopping, but his companions so urgently pleaded for the rest and refreshment that would be derived from anchoring and passing the night on shore, that Ulysses yielded. He bound them, however, with an oath that they would not touch one of the animals of the sacred flocks and herds, but content themselves with what provision they yet had left of the supply which Circe had put on board. So long as this supply lasted the people kept their oath, but contrary winds detained them at the island for a month, and after consuming all their stock of provisions, they were forced to rely upon the birds and fishes they could catch. Famine pressed them, and at length one day, in the absence of Ulysses, they slew some of the cattle, vainly attempting to make amends for the deed by offering from them a portion to the offended powers. Ulysses, on his return to the shore, was horror-struck at perceiving what they had done, and the more so on account of the portentous signs which followed. The skins crept on the ground, and the joints of meat lowed on the spits while roasting.\n\nThe wind becoming fair they sailed from the island. They had not gone far when the weather changed, and a storm of thunder and lightning ensued. A stroke of lightning shattered their mast, which in its fall killed the pilot. At last the vessel itself came to pieces. The keel and mast floating side by side, Ulysses formed of them a raft, to which he clung, and, the wind changing, the waves bore him to Calypso’s island. All the rest of the crew perished.\n\nThe following allusion to the topics we have just been considering is from Milton’s “Comus,” line 252:\n\n“. . . I have often heard My mother Circe and the Sirens three, Amidst the flowery-kirtled Naiades, Culling their potent herbs and baneful drugs, Who as they sung would take the prisoned soul And lap it in Elysium. Scylla wept, And chid her barking waves into attention, And fell Charybdis murmured soft applause.”\n\nScylla and Charybdis have become proverbial, to denote opposite dangers which beset one’s course. See Proverbial Expressions.\n\nCalypso was a sea-nymph, which name denotes a numerous class of female divinities of lower rank, yet sharing many of the attributes of the gods. Calypso received Ulysses hospitably, entertained him magnificently, became enamoured of him, and wished to retain him forever, conferring on him immortality. But he persisted in his resolution to return to his country and his wife and son. Calypso at last received the command of Jove to dismiss him. Mercury brought the message to her, and found her in her grotto, which is thus described by Homer:\n\n“A garden vine, luxuriant on all sides, Mantled the spacious cavern, cluster-hung Profuse; four fountains of serenest lymph, Their sinuous course pursuing side by side, Strayed all around, and everywhere appeared Meadows of softest verdure, purpled o’er With violets; it was a scene to fill A god from heaven with wonder and delight.”\n\nCalypso with much reluctance proceeded to obey the commands of Jupiter. She supplied Ulysses with the means of constructing a raft, provisioned it well for him, and gave him a favoring gale. He sped on his course prosperously for many days, till at length, when in sight of land, a storm arose that broke his mast, and threatened to rend the raft asunder. In this crisis he was seen by a compassionate sea-nymph, who in the form of a cormorant alighted on the raft, and presented him a girdle, directing him to bind it beneath his breast, and if he should be compelled to trust himself to the waves, it would buoy him up and enable him by swimming to reach the land.\n\nFenelon, in his romance of “Telemachus,” has given us the adventures of the son of Ulysses in search of his father. Among other places at which he arrived, following on his father’s footsteps, was Calypso’s isle, and, as in the former case, the goddess tried every art to keep him with her, and offered to share her immortality with him. But Minerva, who in the shape of Mentor accompanied him and governed all his movements, made him repel her allurements, and when no other means of escape could be found, the two friends leaped from a cliff into the sea, and swam to a vessel which lay becalmed off shore. Byron alludes to this leap of Telemachus and Mentor in the following stanza:\n\n“But not in silence pass Calypso’s isles, The sister tenants of the middle deep; There for the weary still a haven smiles, Though the fair goddess long has ceased to weep, And o’er her cliffs a fruitless watch to keep For him who dared prefer a mortal bride. Here too his boy essayed the dreadful leap, Stern Mentor urged from high to yonder tide; While thus of both bereft the nymph-queen doubly sighed.”\n\n————\n\nULYSSES clung to the raft while any of its timbers kept together, and when it no longer yielded him support, binding the girdle around him, he swam. Minerva smoothed the billows before him and sent him a wind that rolled the waves towards the shore. The surf beat high on the rocks and seemed to forbid approach; but at length finding calm water at the mouth of a gentle stream, he landed, spent with toil, breathless and speechless and almost dead. After some time, reviving, he kissed the soil, rejoicing, yet at a loss what course to take. At a short distance he perceived a wood, to which he turned his steps. There, finding a covert sheltered by intermingling branches alike from the sun and the rain, he collected a pile of leaves and formed a bed, on which he stretched himself, and heaping the leaves over him, fell asleep.\n\nThe land where he was thrown was Scheria, the country of the Phæacians. These people dwelt originally near the Cyclopes; but being oppressed by that savage race, they migrated to the isle of Scheria, under the conduct of Nausithoüs, their king. They were, the poet tells us, a people akin to the gods, who appeared manifestly and feasted among them when they offered sacrifices, and did not conceal themselves from solitary wayfarers when they met them. They had abundance of wealth and lived in the enjoyment of it undisturbed by the alarms of war, for as they dwelt remote from gain-seeking man, no enemy ever approached their shores, and they did not even require to make use of bows and quivers. Their chief employment was navigation. Their ships, which went with the velocity of birds, were endued with intelligence; they knew every port and needed no pilot. Alcinoüs, the son of Nausithoüs, was now their king, a wise and just sovereign, beloved by his people.\n\nNow it happened that the very night on which Ulysses was cast ashore on the Phæacian island, and while he lay sleeping on his bed of leaves, Nausicaa, the daughter of the king, had a dream sent by Minerva, reminding her that her wedding-day was not far distant, and that it would be but a prudent preparation for that event to have a general washing of the clothes of the family. This was no slight affair, for the fountains were at some distance, and the garments must be carried thither. On awaking, the princess hastened to her parents to tell them what was on her mind; not alluding to her wedding-day, but finding other reasons equally good. Her father readily assented and ordered the grooms to furnish forth a wagon for the purpose. The clothes were put therein, and the queen mother placed in the wagon, likewise, an abundant supply of food and wine. The princess took her seat and plied the lash, her attendant virgins following her on foot. Arrived at the river-side, they turned out the mules to graze, and unlading the carriage, bore the garments down to the water, and working with cheerfulness and alacrity soon despatched their labor. Then having spread the garments on the shore to dry, and having themselves bathed, they sat down to enjoy their meal; after which they rose and amused themselves with a game of ball, the princess singing to them while they played. But when they had refolded the apparel and were about to resume their way to the town, Minerva caused the ball thrown by the princess to fall into the water, whereat they all screamed and Ulysses awaked at the sound.\n\nNow we must picture to ourselves Ulysses, a shipwrecked mariner, but a few hours escaped from the waves, and utterly destitute of clothing, awaking and discovering that only a few bushes were interposed between him and a group of young maidens whom, by their deportment and attire, he discovered to be not mere peasant girls, but of a higher class. Sadly needing help, how could he yet venture, naked as he was, to discover himself and make his wants known? It certainly was a case worthy of the interposition of his patron goddess Minerva, who never failed him at a crisis. Breaking off a leafy branch from a tree, he held it before him and stepped out from the thicket. The virgins at sight of him fled in all directions, Nausicaa alone excepted, for her Minerva aided and endowed with courage and discernment. Ulysses, standing respectfully aloof, told his sad case, and besought the fair object (whether queen or goddess he professed he knew not) for food and clothing. The princess replied courteously, promising present relief and her father’s hospitality when he should become acquainted with the facts. She called back her scattered maidens, chiding their alarm, and reminding them that the Phæacians had no enemies to fear. This man, she told them, was an unhappy wanderer, whom it was a duty to cherish, for the poor and stranger are from Jove. She bade them bring food and clothing, for some of her brother’s garments were among the contents of the wagon. When this was done, and Ulysses, retiring to a sheltered place, had washed his body free from the sea-foam, clothed and refreshed himself with food, Pallas dilated his form and diffused grace over his ample chest and manly brows.\n\nThe princess, seeing him, was filled with admiration, and scrupled not to say to her damsels that she wished the gods would sent her such a husband. To Ulysses she recommended that he should repair to the city, following herself and train so far as the way lay through the fields; but when they should approach the city she desired that he would no longer be seen in her company, for she feared the remarks which rude and vulgar people might make on seeing her return accompanied by such a gallant stranger. To avoid which she directed him to stop at a grove adjoining the city, in which were a farm and garden belonging to the king. After allowing time for the princess and her companions to reach the city, he was then to pursue his way thither, and would be easily guided by any he might meet to the royal abode.\n\nUlysses obeyed the directions and in due time proceeded to the city, on approaching which he met a young woman bearing a pitcher forth for water. It was Minerva, who had assumed that form. Ulysses accosted her and desired to be directed to the palace of Alcinoüs the king. The maiden replied respectfully, offering to be his guide; for the palace, she informed him, stood near her father’s dwelling. Under the guidance of the goddess, and by her power enveloped in a cloud which shielded him from observation, Ulysses passed among the busy crowd, and with wonder observed their harbor, their ships, their forum (the resort of heroes), and their battlements, till they came to the palace, where the goddess, having first given him some information of the country, king, and people he was about to meet, left him. Ulysses, before entering the courtyard of the palace, stood and surveyed the scene. Its splendor astonished him. Brazen walls stretched from the entrance to the interior house, of which the doors were gold, the doorposts silver, the lintels silver ornamented with gold. On either side were figures of mastiffs wrought in gold and silver, standing in rows as if to guard the approach. Along the walls were seats spread through all their length with mantles of finest texture, the work of Phæacian maidens. On these seats the princes sat and feasted, while golden statues of graceful youths held in their hands lighted torches which shed radiance over the scene. Full fifty female menials served in household offices, some employed to grind the corn, others to wind off the purple wool or ply the loom. For the Phæacian women as far exceeded all other women in household arts as the mariners of that country did the rest of mankind in the management of ships. Without the court a spacious garden lay, four acres in extent. In it grew many a lofty tree, pomegranate, pear, apple, fig, and olive. Neither winter’s cold nor summer’s drought arrested their growth, but they flourished in constant succession, some budding while others were maturing. The vineyard was equally prolific. In one quarter you might see the vines, some in blossom, some loaded with ripe grapes, and in another observe the vintagers treading the wine press. On the garden’s borders flowers of all hues bloomed all the year round, arranged with neatest art. In the midst two fountains poured forth their waters, one flowing by artificial channels over all the garden, the other conducted through the courtyard of the palace, whence every citizen might draw his supplies.\n\nUlysses stood gazing in admiration, unobserved himself, for the cloud which Minerva spread around him still shielded him. At length, having sufficiently observed the scene, he advanced with rapid step into the hall where the chiefs and senators were assembled, pouring libation to Mercury, whose worship followed the evening meal. Just then Minerva dissolved the cloud and disclosed him to the assembled chiefs. Advancing to the place where the queen sat, he knelt at her feet and implored her favor and assistance to enable him to return to his native country. Then withdrawing, he seated himself in the manner of suppliants, at the hearth side.\n\nFor a time none spoke. At last an aged statesman, addressing the king, said, “It is not fit that a stranger who asks our hospitality should be kept waiting in suppliant guise, none welcoming him. Let him therefore be led to a seat among us and supplied with food and wine.” At these words the king rising gave his hand to Ulysses and led him to a seat, displacing thence his own son to make room for the stranger. Food and wine were set before him and he ate and refreshed himself.\n\nThe king then dismissed his guests, notifying them that the next day he would call them to council to consider what had best be done for the stranger.\n\nWhen the guests had departed and Ulysses was left alone with the king and queen, the queen asked him who he was and whence he came, and (recognizing the clothes which he wore as those which her maidens and herself had made) from whom he received those garments. He told them of his residence in Calypso’s isle and his departure thence; of the wreck of his raft, his escape by swimming, and of the relief afforded by the princess. The parents heard approvingly, and the king promised to furnish a ship in which his guest might return to his own land.\n\nThe next day the assembled chiefs confirmed the promise of the king. A bark was prepared and a crew of stout rowers selected, and all betook themselves to the palace, where a bounteous repast was provided. After the feast the king proposed that the young men should show their guest their proficiency in manly sports, and all went forth to the arena for games of running, wrestling, and other exercises. After all had done their best, Ulysses being challenged to show what he could do, at first declined, but being taunted by one of the youths, seized a quoit of weight far heavier than any of the Phæacians had thrown, and sent it farther than the utmost throw of theirs. All were astonished, and viewed their guest with greatly increased respect.\n\nAfter the games they returned to the hall, and the herald led in Demodocus, the blind bard,—\n\n“. . . Dear to the Muse, Who yet appointed him both good and ill, Took from him sight, but gave him strains divine.”\n\nHe took for his theme the “Wooden Horse,” by means of which the Greeks found entrance into Troy. Apollo inspired him, and he sang so feelingly the terrors and the exploits of that eventful time that all were delighted, but Ulysses was moved to tears. Observing which, Alcinoüs, when the song was done, demanded of him why at the mention of Troy his sorrows awaked. Had he lost there a father, or brother, or any dear friend? Ulysses replied by announcing himself by his true name, and at their request, recounted the adventures which had befallen him since his departure from Troy. This narrative raised the sympathy and admiration of the Phæacians for their guest to the highest pitch. The king proposed that all the chiefs should present him with a gift, himself setting the example. They obeyed, and vied with one another in loading the illustrious stranger with costly gifts.\n\nThe next day Ulysses set sail in the Phæacian vessel, and in a short time arrived safe at Ithaca, his own island. When the vessel touched the strand he was asleep. The mariners, without waking him, carried him on shore, and landed with him the chest containing his presents, and then sailed away.\n\nNeptune was so displeased at the conduct of the Phæacians in thus rescuing Ulysses from his hands that on the return of the vessel to port he transformed it into a rock, right opposite the mouth of the harbor.\n\nHomer’s description of the ships of the Phæacians has been thought to look like an anticipation of the wonders of modern steam navigation. Alcinoüs says to Ulysses:\n\n“Say from what city, from what regions tossed, And what inhabitants those regions boast? So shalt thou quickly reach the realm assigned, In wondrous ships, self-moved, instinct with mind; No helm secures their course, no pilot guides; Like man intelligent they plough the tides, Conscious of every coast and every bay That lies beneath the sun’s all-seeing ray.” —Odyssey, Book VIII.\n\nLord Carlisle, in his “Diary in the Turkish and Greek Waters,” thus speaks of Corfu, which he considers to be the ancient Phæacian island:\n\n“The sites explain the ‘Odyssey.’ The temple of the sea-god could not have been more fitly placed, upon a grassy platform of the most elastic turf, on the brow of a crag commanding harbor, and channel, and ocean. Just at the entrance of the inner harbor there is a picturesque rock with a small convent perched upon it, which by one legend is the transformed pinnace of Ulysses.\n\n“Almost the only river in the island is just at the proper distance from the probable site of the city and palace of the king, to justify the princess Nausicaa having had resort to her chariot and to luncheon when she went with the maidens of the court to wash their garments.”\n\n# FATE OF THE SUITORS\n\nUlysses had now been away from Ithaca for twenty years, and when he awoke he did not recognize his native land. Minerva appeared to him in the form of a young shepherd, informed him where he was, and told him the state of things at his palace. More than a hundred nobles of Ithaca and of the neighboring islands had been for years suing for the hand of Penelope, his wife, imagining him dead, and lording it over his palace and people, as if they were owners of both. That he might be able to take vengeance upon them, it was important that he should not be recognized. Minerva accordingly metamorphosed him into an unsightly beggar, and as such he was kindly received by Eumæus, the swine-herd, a faithful servant of his house.\n\nTelemachus, his son, was absent in quest of his father. He had gone to the courts of the other kings, who had returned from the Trojan expedition. While on the search, he received counsel from Minerva to return home. He arrived and sought Eumæus to learn something of the state of affairs at the palace before presenting himself among the suitors. Finding a stranger with Eumæus, he treated him courteously, though in the garb of a beggar, and promised him assistance. Eumæus was sent to the palace to inform Penelope privately of her son’s arrival, for caution was necessary with regard to the suitors, who, as Telemachus had learned, were plotting to intercept and kill him. When Eumæus was gone, Minerva presented herself to Ulysses, and directed him to make himself known to his son. At the same time she touched him, removed at once from him the appearance of age and penury, and gave him the aspect of vigorous manhood that belonged to him. Telemachus viewed him with astonishment, and at first thought he must be more than mortal. But Ulysses announced himself as his father, and accounted for the change of appearance by explaining that it was Minerva’s doing.\n\n“. . . Then threw Telemachus His arms around his father’s neck and wept. Desire intense of lamentation seized On both; soft murmurs uttering, each indulged His grief.”\n\nThe father and son took counsel together how they should get the better of the suitors and punish them for their outrages. It was arranged that Telemachus should proceed to the palace and mingle with the suitors as formerly; that Ulysses should also go as a beggar, a character which in the rude old times had different privileges from what we concede to it now. As traveller and storyteller, the beggar was admitted in the halls of chieftains, and often treated like a guest; though sometimes, also, no doubt, with contumely. Ulysses charged his son not to betray, by any display of unusual interest in him, that he knew him to be other than he seemed, and even if he saw him insulted, or beaten, not to interpose otherwise than he might do for any stranger. At the palace they found the usual scene of feasting and riot going on. The suitors pretended to receive Telemachus with joy at his return, though secretly mortified at the failure of their plots to take his life. The old beggar was permitted to enter, and provided with a portion from the table. A touching incident occurred as Ulysses entered the courtyard of the palace. An old dog lay in the yard almost dead with age, and seeing a stranger enter, raised his head, with ears erect. It was Argus, Ulysses’ own dog, that he had in other days often led to the chase.\n\n“. . . Soon as he perceived Long-lost Ulysses nigh, down fell his ears Clapped close, and with his tail glad sign he gave Of gratulation, impotent to rise, And to approach his master as of old. Ulysses, noting him, wiped off a tear Unmarked. . . . Then his destiny released Old Argus, soon as he had lived to see Ulysses in the twentieth year restored.”\n\nAs Ulysses sat eating his portion in the hall, the suitors began to exhibit their insolence to him. When he mildly remonstrated, one of them raised a stool and with it gave him a blow. Telemachus had hard work to restrain his indignation at seeing his father so treated in his own hall, but remembering his father’s injunctions, said no more than what became him as master of the house, though young, and protector of his guests.\n\nPenelope had protracted her decision in favor of either of her suitors so long that there seemed to be no further pretence for delay. The continued absence of her husband seemed to prove that his return was no longer to be expected. Meanwhile her son had grown up, and was able to manage his own affairs. She therefore consented to submit the question of her choice to a trial of skill among the suitors. The test selected was shooting with the bow. Twelve rings were arranged in a line, and he whose arrow was sent through the whole twelve was to have the queen for his prize. A bow that one of his brother heroes had given to Ulysses in former times was brought from the armory, and with its quiver full of arrows was laid in the hall. Telemachus had taken care that all other weapons should be removed, under pretence that in the heat of competition there was danger, in some rash moment, of putting them to an improper use.\n\nAll things being prepared for the trial, the first thing to be done was to bend the bow in order to attach the string. Telemachus endeavored to do it, but found all his efforts fruitless; and modestly confessing that he had attempted a task beyond his strength, he yielded the bow to another. He tried it with no better success, and, amidst the laughter and jeers of his companions, gave it up. Another tried it and another; they rubbed the bow with tallow, but all to no purpose; it would not bend. Then spoke Ulysses, humbly suggesting that he should be permitted to try; for, said he, “beggar as I am, I was once a soldier, and there is still some strength in these old limbs of mine.” The suitors hooted with derision, and commanded to turn him out of the hall for his insolence. But Telemachus spoke up for him, and, merely to gratify the old man, bade him try. Ulysses took the bow, and handled it with the hand of a master. With ease he adjusted the cord to its notch, then fitting an arrow to the bow he drew the string and sped the arrow unerring through the rings.\n\nWithout allowing them time to express their astonishment, he said, “Now for another mark!” and aimed direct at the most insolent one of the suitors. The arrow pierced through his throat and he fell dead. Telemachus, Eumæus, and another faithful follower, well armed, now sprang to the side of Ulysses. The suitors, in amazement, looked round for arms, but found none, neither was there any way of escape, for Eumæus had secured the door. Ulysses left them not long in uncertainty; he announced himself as the long-lost chief, whose house they had invaded, whose substance they had squandered, whose wife and son they had persecuted for ten long years; and told them he meant to have ample vengeance. All were slain, and Ulysses was left master of his palace and possessor of his kingdom and his wife.\n\nTennyson’s poem of “Ulysses” represents the old hero, after his dangers past and nothing left but to stay at home and be happy, growing tired of inaction and resolving to set forth again in quest of new adventures:\n\n“. . . Come, my friends, ’Tis not too late to seek a newer world. Push off, and sitting well in order smite The sounding furrows; for my purpose holds To sail beyond the sunset, and the baths Of all the western stars, until I die. It may be that the gulfs will wash us down; It may be we shall touch the Happy Isles, And see the great Achilles whom we knew;” etc.\n\n————\n\nWE have followed one of the Grecian heroes, Ulysses, in his wanderings on his return home from Troy, and now we propose to share the fortunes of the remnant of the conquered people, under their chief Æneas, in their search for a new home, after the ruin of their native city. On that fatal night when the wooden horse disgorged its contents of armed men, and the capture and conflagration of the city were the result, Æneas made his escape from the scene of destruction, with his father, and his wife, and young son. The father, Anchises, was too old to walk with the speed required, and Æneas took him upon his shoulders. Thus burdened, leading his son and followed by his wife, he made the best of his way out of the burning city; but, in the confusion, his wife was swept away and lost.\n\nOn arriving at the place of rendezvous, numerous fugitives, of both sexes, were found, who put themselves under the guidance of Æneas. Some months were spent in preparation, and at length they embarked. They first landed on the neighboring shores of Thrace, and were preparing to build a city, but Æneas was deterred by a prodigy. Preparing to offer sacrifice, he tore some twigs from one of the bushes. To his dismay the wounded part dropped blood. When he repeated the act a voice from the ground cried out to him, “Spare me, Æneas; I am your kinsman, Polydore, here murdered with many arrows, from which a bush has grown, nourished with my blood.” These words recalled to the recollection of Æneas that Polydore was a young prince of Troy, whom his father had sent with ample treasures to the neighboring land of Thrace, to be there brought up, at a distance from the horrors of war. The king to whom he was sent had murdered him and seized his treasures. Æneas and his companions, considering the land accursed by the stain of such a crime, hastened away.\n\nThey next landed on the island of Delos, which was once a floating island, till Jupiter fastened it by adamantine chains to the bottom of the sea. Apollo and Diana were born there, and the island was sacred to Apollo. Here Æneas consulted the oracle of Apollo, and received an answer, ambiguous as usual,—“Seek your ancient mother; there the race of Æneas shall dwell, and reduce all other nations to their sway.” The Trojans heard with joy and immediately began to ask one another, “Where is the spot intended by the oracle?” Anchises remembered that there was a tradition that their forefathers came from Crete and thither they resolved to steer. They arrived at Crete and began to build their city, but sickness broke out among them, and the fields that they had planted failed to yield a crop. In this gloomy aspect of affairs Æneas was warned in a dream to leave the country and seek a western land, called Hesperia, whence Dardanus, the true founder of the Trojan race, had originally migrated. To Hesperia, now called Italy, therefore, they directed their future course, and not till after many adventures and the lapse of time sufficient to carry a modern navigator several times round the world, did they arrive there.\n\nTheir first landing was at the island of the Harpies. These were disgusting birds with the heads of maidens, with long claws and faces pale with hunger. They were sent by the gods to torment a certain Phineus, whom Jupiter had deprived of his sight, in punishment of his cruelty; and whenever a meal was placed before him the Harpies darted down from the air and carried it off. They were driven away from Phineus by the heroes of the Argonautic expedition, and took refuge in the island where Æneas now found them.\n\nWhen they entered the port the Trojans saw herds of cattle roaming over the plain. They slew as many as they wished and prepared for a feast. But no sooner had they seated themselves at the table than a horrible clamor was heard in the air, and a flock of these odious harpies came rushing down upon them, seizing in their talons the meat from the dishes and flying away with it. Æneas and his companions drew their swords and dealt vigorous blows among the monsters, but to no purpose, for they were so nimble it was almost impossible to hit them, and their feathers were like armor impenetrable to steel. One of them, perched on a neighboring cliff, screamed out, “Is it thus, Trojans, you treat us innocent birds, first slaughter our cattle and then make war on ourselves?” She then predicted dire sufferings to them in their future course, and having vented her wrath flew away. The Trojans made haste to leave the country, and next found themselves coasting along the shore of Epirus. Here they landed, and to their astonishment learned that certain Trojan exiles, who had been carried there as prisoners, had become rulers of the country. Andromache, the widow of Hector, became the wife of one of the victorious Grecian chiefs, to whom she bore a son. Her husband dying, she was left regent of the country, as guardian of her son, and had married a fellow-captive, Helenus, of the royal race of Troy. Helenus and Andromache treated the exiles with the utmost hospitality, and dismissed them loaded with gifts.\n\nFrom hence Æneas coasted along the shore of Sicily and passed the country of the Cyclopes. Here they were hailed from the shore by a miserable object, whom by his garments, tattered as they were, they perceived to be a Greek. He told them he was one of Ulysses’s companions, left behind by that chief in his hurried departure. He related the story of Ulysses’s adventure with Polyphemus, and besought them to take him off with them as he had no means of sustaining his existence where he was but wild berries and roots, and lived in constant fear of the Cyclopes. While he spoke Polyphemus made his appearance; a terrible monster, shapeless, vast, whose only eye had been put out.[26] He walked with cautious steps, feeling his way with a staff, down to the sea-side, to wash his eye-socket in the waves. When he reached the water, he waded out towards them, and his immense height enabled him to advance far into the sea, so that the Trojans, in terror, took to their oars to get out of his way. Hearing the oars, Polyphemus shouted after them, so that the shores resounded, and at the noise the other Cyclopes came forth from their caves and woods and lined the shore, like a row of lofty pine trees. The Trojans plied their oars and soon left them out of sight.\n\nÆneas had been cautioned by Helenus to avoid the strait guarded by the monsters Scylla and Charybdis. There Ulysses, the reader will remember, had lost six of his men, seized by Scylla while the navigators were wholly intent upon avoiding Charybdis. Æneas, following the advice of Helenus, shunned the dangerous pass and coasted along the island of Sicily.\n\nJuno, seeing the Trojans speeding their way prosperously towards their destined shore, felt her old grudge against them revive, for she could not forget the slight that Paris had put upon her, in awarding the prize of beauty to another. In heavenly minds can such resentments dwell![27] Accordingly she hastened to Æolus, the ruler of the winds,—the same who supplied Ulysses with favoring gales, giving him the contrary ones tied up in a bag. Æolus obeyed the goddess and sent forth his sons, Boreas, Typhon, and the other winds, to toss the ocean. A terrible storm ensued and the Trojan ships were driven out of their course towards the coast of Africa. They were in imminent danger of being wrecked, and were separated, so that Æneas thought that all were lost except his own.\n\nAt this crisis, Neptune, hearing the storm raging, and knowing that he had given no orders for one, raised his head above the waves, and saw the fleet of Æneas driving before the gale. Knowing the hostility of Juno, he was at no loss to account for it, but his anger was not the less at this interference in his province. He called the winds and dismissed them with a severe reprimand. He then soothed the waves, and brushed away the clouds from before the face of the sun. Some of the ships which had got on the rocks he pried off with his own trident, while Triton and a sea-nymph, putting their shoulders under others, set them afloat again. The Trojans, when the sea became calm, sought the nearest shore, which was the coast of Carthage, where Æneas was so happy as to find that one by one the ships all arrived safe, though badly shaken.\n\nWaller, in his “Panegyric to the Lord Protector” (Cromwell), alludes to this stilling of the storm by Neptune:\n\n“Above the waves, as Neptune showed his face, To chide the winds and save the Trojan race, So has your Highness, raised above the rest, Storms of ambition tossing us repressed.”\n\nCarthage, where the exiles had now arrived, was a spot on the coast of Africa opposite Sicily, where at that time a Tyrian colony under Dido, their queen, were laying the foundations of a state destined in later ages to be the rival of Rome itself. Dido was the daughter of Belus, king of Tyre, and sister of Pygmalion, who succeeded his father on the throne. Her husband was Sichæus, a man of immense wealth, but Pygmalion, who coveted his treasures, caused him to be put to death. Dido, with a numerous body of friends and followers, both men and women, succeeded in effecting their escape from Tyre, in several vessels, carrying with them the treasures of Sichæus. On arriving at the spot which they selected as the seat of their future home, they asked of the natives only so much land as they could enclose with a bull’s hide. When this was readily granted, she caused the hide to be cut into strips, and with them enclosed a spot on which she built a citadel, and called it Byrsa (a hide). Around this fort the city of Carthage rose, and soon became a powerful and flourishing place.\n\nSuch was the state of affairs when Æneas with his Trojans arrived there. Dido received the illustrious exiles with friendliness and hospitality. “Not unacquainted with distress,” she said, “I have learned to succor the unfortunate.”[28] The queen’s hospitality displayed itself in festivities at which games of strength and skill were exhibited. The strangers contended for the palm with her own subjects, on equal terms, the queen declaring that whether the victor were “Trojan or Tyrian should make no difference to her.”[29] At the feast which followed the games, Æneas gave at her request a recital of the closing events of the Trojan history and his own adventures after the fall of the city. Dido was charmed with his discourse and filled with admiration of his exploits. She conceived an ardent passion for him, and he for his part seemed well content to accept the fortunate chance which appeared to offer him at once a happy termination of his wanderings, a home, a kingdom, and a bride. Months rolled away in the enjoyment of pleasant intercourse, and it seemed as if Italy and the empire destined to be founded on its shores were alike forgotten. Seeing which, Jupiter despatched Mercury with a message to Æneas recalling him to a sense of his high destiny, and commanding him to resume his voyage.\n\nÆneas parted from Dido, though she tried every allurement and persuasion to detain him. The blow to her affection and her pride was too much for her to endure, and when she found that he was gone, she mounted a funeral pile which she had caused to be erected, and having stabbed herself was consumed with the pile. The flames rising over the city were seen by the departing Trojans, and, though the cause was unknown, gave to Æneas some intimation of the fatal event.\n\nThe following epigram we find in “Elegant Extracts”:\n\n# FROM THE LATIN\n\n“Unhappy, Dido, was thy fate In first and second married state! One husband caused thy flight by dying, Thy death the other caused by flying.”\n\nAfter touching at the island of Sicily, where Acestes, a prince of Trojan lineage, bore sway, who gave them a hospitable reception, the Trojans reëmbarked, and held on their course for Italy. Venus now interceded with Neptune to allow her son at last to attain the wished-for goal and find an end of his perils on the deep. Neptune consented, stipulating only for one life as a ransom for the rest. The victim was Palinurus, the pilot. As he sat watching the stars, with his hand on the helm, Somnus sent by Neptune approached in the guise of Phorbas and said: “Palinurus, the breeze is fair, the water smooth, and the ship sails steadily on her course. Lie down awhile and take needful rest. I will stand at the helm in your place.” Palinurus replied, “Tell me not of smooth seas or favoring winds,—me who have seen so much of their treachery. Shall I trust Æneas to the chances of the weather and the winds?” And he continued to grasp the helm and to keep his eyes fixed on the stars. But Somnus waved over him a branch moistened with Lethæan dew, and his eyes closed in spite of all his efforts. Then Somnus pushed him overboard and he fell; but keeping his hold upon the helm, it came away with him. Neptune was mindful of his promise and kept the ship on her track without helm or pilot, till Æneas discovered his loss, and, sorrowing deeply for his faithful steersman, took charge of the ship himself.\n\nThere is a beautiful allusion to the story of Palinurus in Scott’s “Marmion,” Introduction to Canto I., where the poet, speaking of the recent death of William Pitt, says:\n\n“O, think how, to his latest day, When death just hovering claimed his prey, With Palinure’s unaltered mood, Firm at his dangerous post he stood; Each call for needful rest repelled, With dying hand the rudder held, Till in his fall, with fateful sway, The steerage of the realm gave way.”\n\nThe ships at last reached the shores of Italy, and joyfully did the adventurers leap to land. While his people were employed in making their encampment Æneas sought the abode of the Sibyl. It was a cave connected with a temple and grove, sacred to Apollo and Diana. While Æneas contemplated the scene, the Sibyl accosted him. She seemed to know his errand, and under the influence of the deity of the place, burst forth in a prophetic strain, giving dark intimations of labors and perils through which he was destined to make his way to final success. She closed with the encouraging words which have become proverbial: “Yield not to disasters, but press onward the more bravely.”[30] Æneas replied that he had prepared himself for whatever might await him. He had but one request to make. Having been directed in a dream to seek the abode of the dead in order to confer with his father, Anchises, to receive from him a revelation of his future fortunes and those of his race, he asked her assistance to enable him to accomplish the task. The Sibyl replied, “The descent to Avernus is easy: the gate of Pluto stands open night and day; but to retrace one’s steps and return to the upper air, that is the toil, that the difficulty.”[31] She instructed him to seek in the forest a tree on which grew a golden branch. This branch was to be plucked off and borne as a gift to Proserpine, and if fate was propitious it would yield to the hand and quit its parent trunk, but otherwise no force could rend it away. If torn away, another would succeed.[32]\n\nÆneas followed the directions of the Sibyl. His mother, Venus, sent two of her doves to fly before him and show him the way, and by their assistance he found the tree, plucked the branch, and hastened back with it to the Sibyl.\n\n————\n\n# THE INFERNAL REGIONS\n\nAS at the commencement of our series we have given the pagan account of the creation of the world, so as we approach its conclusion we present a view of the regions of the dead, depicted by one of their most enlightened poets, who drew his doctrines from their most esteemed philosophers. The region where Virgil locates the entrance to this abode is perhaps the most strikingly adapted to excite ideas of the terrific and preternatural of any on the face of the earth. It is the volcanic region near Vesuvius, where the whole country is cleft with chasms, from which sulphurous flames arise, while the ground is shaken with pent-up vapors, and mysterious sounds issue from the bowels of the earth. The lake Avernus is supposed to fill the crater of an extinct volcano. It is circular, half a mile wide, and very deep, surrounded by high banks, which in Virgil’s time were covered with a gloomy forest. Mephitic vapors rise from its waters, so that no life is found on its banks, and no birds fly over it. Here, according to the poet, was the cave which afforded access to the infernal regions, and here Æneas offered sacrifices to the infernal deities, Proserpine, Hecate, and the Furies. Then a roaring was heard in the earth, the woods on the hill-tops were shaken, and the howling of dogs announced the approach of the deities. “Now,” said the Sibyl, “summon up your courage, for you will need it.” She descended into the cave, and Æneas followed. Before the threshold of hell they passed through a group of beings who are enumerated as Griefs and avenging Cares, pale Diseases and melancholy Age, Fear and Hunger that tempt to crime, Toil, Poverty, and Death,—forms horrible to view. The Furies spread their couches there, and Discord, whose hair was of vipers tied up with a bloody fillet. Here also were the monsters, Briareus, with his hundred arms, Hydras hissing, and Chimæras breathing fire. Æneas shuddered at the sight, drew his sword and would have struck, but the Sibyl restrained him. They then came to the black river Cocytus, where they found the ferryman, Charon, old and squalid, but strong and vigorous, who was receiving passengers of all kinds into his boat, magnanimous heroes, boys and unmarried girls, as numerous as the leaves that fall at autumn, or the flocks that fly southward at the approach of winter. They stood pressing for a passage and longing to touch the opposite shore. But the stern ferryman took in only such as he chose, driving the rest back. Æneas, wondering at the sight, asked the Sibyl, “Why this discrimination?” She answered, “Those who are taken on board the bark are the souls of those who have received due burial rites; the host of others who have remained unburied are not permitted to pass the flood, but wander a hundred years, and flit to and fro about the shore, till at last they are taken over.” Æneas grieved at recollecting some of his own companions who had perished in the storm. At that moment he beheld Palinurus, his pilot, who fell overboard and was drowned. He addressed him and asked him the cause of his misfortune. Palinurus replied that the rudder was carried away, and he, clinging to it, was swept away with it. He besought Æneas most urgently to extend to him his hand and take him in company to the opposite shore. But the Sibyl rebuked him for the wish thus to transgress the laws of Pluto; but consoled him by informing him that the people of the shore where his body had been wafted by the waves should be stirred up by prodigies to give it due burial, and that the promontory should bear the name of Cape Palinurus, which it does to this day. Leaving Palinurus consoled by these words, they approached the boat. Charon, fixing his eyes sternly upon the advancing warrior, demanded by what right he, living and armed, approached that shore. To which the Sibyl replied that they would commit no violence, that Æneas’s only object was to see his father, and finally exhibited the golden branch, at sight of which Charon’s wrath relaxed, and he made haste to turn his bark to the shore, and receive them on board. The boat, adapted only to the light freight of bodiless spirits, groaned under the weight of the hero. They were soon conveyed to the opposite shore. There they were encountered by the three-headed dog, Cerberus, with his necks bristling with snakes. He barked with all his three throats till the Sibyl threw him a medicated cake which he eagerly devoured, and then stretched himself out in his den and fell asleep. Æneas and the Sibyl sprang to land. The first sound that struck their ears was the wailing of young children, who had died on the threshold of life, and near to these were they who had perished under false charges. Minos presides over them as judge, and examines the deeds of each. The next class was of those who had died by their own hand, hating life and seeking refuge in death. O how willingly would they now endure poverty, labor, and any other infliction, if they might but return to life! Next were situated the regions of sadness, divided off into retired paths, leading through groves of myrtle. Here roamed those who had fallen victims to unrequited love, not freed from pain even by death itself. Among these, Æneas thought he descried the form of Dido, with a wound still recent. In the dim light he was for a moment uncertain, but approaching, perceived it was indeed herself. Tears fell from his eyes, and he addressed her in the accents of love. “Unhappy Dido! was then the rumor true that you had perished? and was I, alas! the cause? I call the gods to witness that my departure from you was reluctant, and in obedience to the commands of Jove; nor could I believe that my absence would cost you so dear. Stop, I beseech you, and refuse me not a last farewell.” She stood for a moment with averted countenance, and eyes fixed on the ground, and then silently passed on, as insensible to his pleadings as a rock. Æneas followed for some distance; then, with a heavy heart, rejoined his companion and resumed his route.\n\nThey next entered the fields where roam the heroes who have fallen in battle. Here they saw many shades of Grecian and Trojan warriors. The Trojans thronged around him, and could not be satisfied with the sight. They asked the cause of his coming, and plied him with innumerable questions. But the Greeks, at the sight of his armor glittering through the murky atmosphere, recognized the hero, and filled with terror turned their backs and fled, as they used to do on the plains of Troy.\n\nÆneas would have lingered long with his Trojan friends, but the Sibyl hurried him away. They next came to a place where the road divided, the one leading to Elysium, the other to the regions of the condemned. Æneas beheld on one side the walls of a mighty city, around which Phlegethon rolled its fiery waters. Before him was the gate of adamant that neither gods nor men can break through. An iron tower stood by the gate, on which Tisiphone, the avenging Fury, kept guard. From the city were heard groans, and the sound of the scourge, the creaking of iron, and the clanking of chains. Æneas, horror-struck, inquired of his guide what crimes were those whose punishments produced the sounds he heard? The Sibyl answered, “Here is the judgment hall of Rhadamanthus, who brings to light crimes done in life, which the perpetrator vainly thought impenetrably hid. Tisiphone applies her whip of scorpions, and delivers the offender over to her sister Furies.” At this moment with horrid clang the brazen gates unfolded, and Æneas saw within a Hydra with fifty heads guarding the entrance. The Sibyl told him that the gulf of Tartarus descended deep, so that its recesses were as far beneath their feet as heaven was high above their heads. In the bottom of this pit, the Titan race, who warred against the gods, lie prostrate; Salmoneus, also, who presumed to vie with Jupiter, and built a bridge of brass over which he drove his chariot that the sound might resemble thunder, launching flaming brands at his people in imitation of lightning, till Jupiter struck him with a real thunderbolt, and taught him the difference between mortal weapons and divine. Here, also, is Tityus, the giant, whose form is so immense that as he lies he stretches over nine acres, while a vulture preys upon his liver, which as fast as it is devoured grows again, so that his punishment will have no end.\n\nÆneas saw groups seated at tables loaded with dainties, while near by stood a Fury who snatched away the viands from their lips as fast as they prepared to taste them. Others beheld suspended over their heads huge rocks, threatening to fall, keeping them in a state of constant alarm. These were they who had hated their brothers, or struck their parents, or defrauded the friends who trusted them, or who, having grown rich, kept their money to themselves, and gave no share to others; the last being the most numerous class. Here also were those who had violated the marriage vow, or fought in a bad cause, or failed in fidelity to their employers. Here was one who had sold his country for gold, another who perverted the laws, making them say one thing to-day and another to-morrow.\n\nIxion was there, fastened to the circumference of a wheel ceaselessly revolving; and Sisyphus, whose task was to roll a huge stone up to a hill-top, but when the steep was well-nigh gained, the rock, repulsed by some sudden force, rushed again headlong down to the plain. Again he toiled at it, while the sweat bathed all his weary limbs, but all to no effect. There was Tantalus, who stood in a pool, his chin level with the water, yet he was parched with thirst, and found nothing to assuage it; for when he bowed his hoary head, eager to quaff, the water fled away, leaving the ground at his feet all dry. Tall trees laden with fruit stooped their heads to him, pears, pomegranates, apples, and luscious figs; but when with a sudden grasp he tried to seize them winds whirled them high above his reach.\n\nThe Sibyl now warned Æneas that it was time to turn from these melancholy regions and seek the city of the blessed. They passed through a middle tract of darkness, and came upon the Elysian fields, the groves where the happy reside. They breathed a freer air, and saw all objects clothed in a purple light. The region has a sun and stars of its own. The inhabitants were enjoying themselves in various ways, some in sports on the grassy turf, in games of strength or skill, others dancing or singing. Orpheus struck the chords of his lyre, and called forth ravishing sounds. Here Æneas saw the founders of the Trojan state, magnanimous heroes who lived in happier times. He gazed with admiration on the war chariots and glittering arms now reposing in disuse. Spears stood fixed in the ground, and the horses, unharnessed, roamed over the plain. The same pride in splendid armor and generous steeds which the old heroes felt in life, accompanied them here. He saw another group feasting and listening to the strains of music. They were in a laurel grove, whence the great river Po has its origin, and flows out among men. Here dwelt those who fell by wounds received in their country’s cause, holy priests also, and poets who have uttered thoughts worthy of Apollo, and others who have contributed to cheer and adorn life by their discoveries in the useful arts, and have made their memory blessed by rendering service to mankind. They wore snow-white fillets about their brows. The Sibyl addressed a group of these, and inquired where Anchises was to be found. They were directed where to seek him, and soon found him in a verdant valley, where he was contemplating the ranks of his posterity, their destinies and worthy deeds to be achieved in coming times. When he recognized Æneas approaching, he stretched out both hands to him, while tears flowed freely. “Have you come at last,” said he, “long expected, and do I behold you after such perils past? O my son, how have I trembled for you as I have watched your career!” To which Æneas replied, “O father! your image was always before me to guide and guard me.” Then he endeavored to enfold his father in his embrace, but his arms enclosed only an unsubstantial image.\n\nÆneas perceived before him a spacious valley, with trees gently waving to the wind, a tranquil landscape, through which the river Lethe flowed. Along the banks of the stream wandered a countless multitude, numerous as insects in the summer air. Æneas, with surprise, inquired who were these. Anchises answered, “They are souls to which bodies are to be given in due time. Meanwhile they dwell on Lethe’s bank, and drink oblivion of their former lives.” “O father!” said Æneas, “is it possible that any can be so in love with life as to wish to leave these tranquil seats for the upper world?” Anchises replied by explaining the plan of creation. The Creator, he told him, originally made the material of which souls are composed of the four elements, fire, air, earth, and water, all which when united took the form of the most excellent part, fire, and became flame. This material was scattered like seed among the heavenly bodies, the sun, moon, and stars. Of this seed the inferior gods created man and all other animals, mingling it with various proportions of earth, by which its purity was alloyed and reduced. Thus, the more earth predominates in the composition the less pure is the individual; and we see men and women with their full-grown bodies have not the purity of childhood. So in proportion to the time which the union of body and soul has lasted is the impurity contracted by the spiritual part. This impurity must be purged away after death, which is done by ventilating the souls in the current of winds, or merging them in water, or burning out their impurities by fire. Some few, of whom Anchises intimates that he is one, are admitted at once to Elysium, there to remain. But the rest, after the impurities of earth are purged away, are sent back to life endowed with new bodies, having had the remembrance of their former lives effectually washed away by the waters of Lethe. Some, however, there still are, so thoroughly corrupted, that they are not fit to be intrusted with human bodies, and these are made into brute animals, lions, tigers, cats, dogs, monkeys, etc. This is what the ancients called Metempsychosis, or the transmigration of souls; a doctrine which is still held by the natives of India, who scruple to destroy the life even of the most insignificant animal, not knowing but it may be one of their relations in an altered form.\n\nAnchises, having explained so much, proceeded to point out to Æneas individuals of his race, who were hereafter to be born, and to relate to him the exploits they should perform in the world. After this he reverted to the present, and told his son of the events that remained to him to be accomplished before the complete establishment of himself and his followers in Italy. Wars were to be waged, battles fought, a bride to be won, and in the result a Trojan state founded, from which should rise the Roman power, to be in time the sovereign of the world.\n\nÆneas and the Sibyl then took leave of Anchises, and returned by some short cut, which the poet does not explain, to the upper world.\n\nVirgil, we have seen, places his Elysium under the earth, and assigns it for a residence to the spirits of the blessed. But in Homer Elysium forms no part of the realms of the dead. He places it on the west of the earth, near Ocean, and describes it as a happy land, where there is neither snow, nor cold, nor rain, and always fanned by the delightful breezes of Zephyrus. Hither favored heroes pass without dying and live happy under the rule of Rhadamanthus. The Elysium of Hesiod and Pindar is in the Isles of the Blessed, or Fortunate Islands, in the Western Ocean. From these sprang the legend of the happy island Atlantis. This blissful region may have been wholly imaginary, but possibly may have sprung from the reports of some storm-driven mariners who had caught a glimpse of the coast of America.\n\nJ. R. Lowell, in one of his shorter poems, claims for the present age some of the privileges of that happy realm. Addressing the Past, he says:\n\n“Whatever of true life there was in thee, Leaps in our age’s veins. . . . . . . . Here, ’mid the bleak waves of our strife and care, Float the green ‘Fortunate Isles,’ Where all thy hero-spirits dwell and share Our martyrdoms and toils. The present moves attended With all of brave and excellent and fair That made the old time splendid.”\n\nMilton also alludes to the same fable in “Paradise Lost,” Book III., 1. 568:\n\n“Like those Hesperian gardens famed of old, Fortunate fields and groves and flowery vales, Thrice happy isles.”\n\nAnd in Book II. he characterizes the rivers of Erebus according to the meaning of their names in the Greek language:\n\n“Abhorred Styx, the flood of deadly hate, Sad Acheron of sorrow black and deep; Cocytus named of lamentation loud Heard on the rueful stream; fierce Phlegethon Whose waves of torrent fire inflame with rage. Far off from these a slow and silent stream, Lethe, the river of oblivion, rolls Her watery labyrinth, whereof who drinks Forthwith his former state and being forgets, Forgets both joy and grief, pleasure and pain.”\n\n# THE SIBYL\n\nAs Æneas and the Sibyl pursued their way back to earth, he said to her, “Whether thou be a goddess or a mortal beloved of the gods, by me thou shalt always be held in reverence. When I reach the upper air I will cause a temple to be built to thy honor, and will myself bring offerings.” “I am no goddess,” said the Sibyl; “I have no claim to sacrifice or offering. I am mortal; yet if I could have accepted the love of Apollo I might have been immortal. He promised me the fulfilment of my wish, if I would consent to be his. I took a handful of sand, and holding it forth, said, ‘Grant me to see as many birthdays as there are sand grains in my hand.’ Unluckily I forgot to ask for enduring youth. This also he would have granted, could I have accepted his love, but offended at my refusal, he allowed me to grow old. My youth and youthful strength fled long ago. I have lived seven hundred years, and to equal the number of the sand grains I have still to see three hundred springs and three hundred harvests. My body shrinks up as years increase, and in time, I shall be lost to sight, but my voice will remain, and future ages will respect my sayings.”\n\nThese concluding words of the Sibyl alluded to her prophetic power. In her cave she was accustomed to inscribe on leaves gathered from the trees the names and fates of individuals. The leaves thus inscribed were arranged in order within the cave, and might be consulted by her votaries. But if perchance at the opening of the door the wind rushed in and dispersed the leaves the Sibyl gave no aid to restoring them again, and the oracle was irreparably lost.\n\nThe following legend of the Sibyl is fixed at a later date. In the reign of one of the Tarquins there appeared before the king a woman who offered him nine books for sale. The king refused to purchase them, whereupon the woman went away and burned three of the books, and returning offered the remaining books for the same price she had asked for the nine. The king again rejected them; but when the woman, after burning three books more, returned and asked for the three remaining the same price which she had before asked for the nine, his curiosity was excited, and he purchased the books. They were found to contain the destinies of the Roman state. They were kept in the temple of Jupiter Capitolinus, preserved in a stone chest, and allowed to be inspected only by especial officers appointed for that duty, who, on great occasions, consulted them and interpreted their oracles to the people.\n\nThere were various Sibyls; but the Cumæan Sibyl, of whom Ovid and Virgil write, is the most celebrated of them. Ovid’s story of her life protracted to one thousand years may be intended to represent the various Sibyls as being only reappearances of one and the same individual.\n\nYoung, in the “Night Thoughts,” alludes to the Sibyl. Speaking of Worldly Wisdom, he says:\n\n“If future fate she plans ’tis all in leaves, Like Sibyl, unsubstantial, fleeting bliss; At the first blast it vanishes in air. . . . . . . . As worldly schemes resemble Sibyl’s leaves, The good man’s days to Sibyl’s books compare, The price still rising as in number less.”\n\n# CAMILLA—EVANDER—NISUS AND EURYALUS—MEZENTIUS—TURNUS\n\nÆNEAS, having parted from the Sibyl and rejoined his fleet, coasted along the shores of Italy and cast anchor in the mouth of the Tiber. The poet, having brought his hero to this spot, the destined termination of his wanderings, invokes his Muse to tell him the situation of things at that eventful moment. Latinus, third in descent from Saturn, ruled the country. He was now old and had no male descendant, but had one charming daughter, Lavinia, who was sought in marriage by many neighboring chiefs, one of whom, Turnus, king of the Rutulians, was favored by the wishes of her parents. But Latinus had been warned in a dream by his father Faunus, that the destined husband of Lavinia should come from a foreign land. From that union should spring a race destined to subdue the world.\n\nOur readers will remember that in the conflict with the Harpies one of those half-human birds had threatened the Trojans with dire sufferings. In particular she predicted that before their wanderings ceased they should be pressed by hunger to devour their tables. This portent now came true; for as they took their scanty meal, seated on the grass, the men placed their hard biscuit on their laps, and put thereon whatever their gleanings in the woods supplied. Having despatched the latter they finished by eating the crusts. Seeing which, the boy Iulus said playfully, “See, we are eating our tables.” Æneas caught the words and accepted the omen. “All hail, promised land!” he exclaimed, “this is our home, this our country.” He then took measures to find out who were the present inhabitants of the land, and who their rulers. A hundred chosen men were sent to the village of Latinus, bearing presents and a request for friendship and alliance. They went and were favorably received. Latinus immediately concluded that the Trojan hero was no other than the promised son-in-law announced by the oracle. He cheerfully granted his alliance and sent back the messengers mounted on steeds from his stables, and loaded with gifts and friendly messages.\n\nJuno, seeing things go thus prosperously for the Trojans, felt her old animosity revive, summoned Alecto from Erebus, and sent her to stir up discord. The Fury first took possession of the queen, Amata, and roused her to oppose in every way the new alliance. Alecto then speeded to the city of Turnus, and assuming the form of an old priestess, informed him of the arrival of the foreigners and of the attempts of their prince to rob him of his bride. Next she turned her attention to the camp of the Trojans. There she saw the boy Iulus and his companions amusing themselves with hunting. She sharpened the scent of the dogs, and led them to rouse up from the thicket a tame stag, the favorite of Silvia, the daughter of Tyrrheus, the king’s herdsman. A javelin from the hand of Iulus wounded the animal, and he had only strength left to run homewards, and died at his mistress’s feet. Her cries and tears roused her brothers and the herdsmen, and they, seizing whatever weapons came to hand, furiously assaulted the hunting party. These were protected by their friends, and the herdsmen were finally driven back with the loss of two of their number.\n\nThese things were enough to rouse the storm of war, and the queen, Turnus, and the peasants all urged the old king to drive the strangers from the country. He resisted as long as he could, but, finding his opposition unavailing, finally gave way and retreated to his retirement.\n\n# OPENING THE GATES OF JANUS\n\nIt was the custom of the country, when war was to be undertaken, for the chief magistrate, clad in his robes of office, with solemn pomp to open the gates of the temple of Janus, which were kept shut as long as peace endured. His people now urged the old king to perform that solemn office, but he refused to do so. While they contested, Juno herself, descending from the skies, smote the doors with irresistible force, and burst them open. Immediately the whole country was in a flame. The people rushed from every side breathing nothing but war.\n\nTurnus was recognized by all as leader; others joined as allies, chief of whom was Mezentius, a brave and able soldier, but of detestable cruelty. He had been the chief of one of the neighboring cities, but his people drove him out. With him was joined his son Lausus, a generous youth, worthy of a better sire.\n\nCamilla, the favorite of Diana, a huntress and warrior, after the fashion of the Amazons, came with her band of mounted followers, including a select number of her own sex, and ranged herself on the side of Turnus. This maiden had never accustomed her fingers to the distaff or the loom, but had learned to endure the toils of war, and in speed to outstrip the wind. It seemed as if she might run over the standing corn without crushing it, or over the surface of the water without dipping her feet. Camilla’s history had been singular from the beginning. Her father, Metabus, driven from his city by civil discord, carried with him in his flight his infant daughter. As he fled through the woods, his enemies in hot pursuit, he reached the bank of the river Amazenus, which, swelled by rains, seemed to debar a passage. He paused for a moment, then decided what to do. He tied the infant to his lance with wrappers of bark, and poising the weapon in his upraised hand thus addressed Diana: “Goddess of the woods! I consecrate this maid to you;” then hurled the weapon with its burden to the opposite bank. The spear flew across the roaring water. His pursuers were already upon him, but he plunged into the river and swam across, and found the spear, with the infant safe on the other side. Thenceforth he lived among the shepherds and brought up his daughter in woodland arts. While a child she was taught to use the bow and throw the javelin. With her sling she could bring down the crane or the wild swan. Her dress was a tiger’s skin. Many mothers sought her for a daughter-in-law, but she continued faithful to Diana and repelled the thought of marriage.\n\nSuch were the formidable allies that ranged themselves against Æneas. It was night and he lay stretched in sleep on the bank of the river under the open heavens. The god of the stream, Father Tiber, seemed to raise his head above the willows and to say, “O goddess-born, destined possessor of the Latin realms, this is the promised land, here is to be your home, here shall terminate the hostility of the heavenly powers, if only you faithfully persevere. There are friends not far distant. Prepare your boats and row up my stream; I will lead you to Evander, the Arcadian chief, he has long been at strife with Turnus and the Rutulians, and is prepared to become an ally of yours. Rise! offer your vows to Juno, and deprecate her anger. When you have achieved your victory then think of me.” Æneas woke and paid immediate obedience to the friendly vision. He sacrificed to Juno, and invoked the god of the river and all his tributary fountains to lend their aid. Then for the first time a vessel filled with armed warriors floated on the stream of the Tiber. The river smoothed its waves, and bade its current flow gently, while, impelled by the vigorous strokes of the rowers, the vessels shot rapidly up the stream.\n\nAbout the middle of the day they came in sight of the scattered buildings of the infant town, where in after times the proud city of Rome grew, whose glory reached the skies. By chance the old king, Evander, was that day celebrating annual solemnities in honor of Hercules and all the gods. Pallas, his son, and all the chiefs of the little commonwealth stood by. When they saw the tall ship gliding onward near the wood, they were alarmed at the sight, and rose from the tables. But Pallas forbade the solemnities to be interrupted, and seizing a weapon, stepped forward to the river’s bank. He called aloud, demanding who they were, and what their object. Æneas, holding forth an olive-branch, replied, “We are Trojans, friends to you, and enemies to the Rutulians. We seek Evander, and offer to join our arms with yours.” Pallas, in amaze at the sound of so great a name, invited them to land, and when Æneas touched the shore he seized his hand, and held it long in friendly grasp. Proceeding through the wood, they joined the king and his party and were most favorably received. Seats were provided for them at the tables, and the repast proceeded.\n\n# INFANT ROME\n\nWhen the solemnities were ended all moved towards the city. The king, bending with age, walked between his son and Æneas, taking the arm of one or the other of them, and with much variety of pleasing talk shortening the way. Æneas with delight looked and listened, observing all the beauties of the scene, and learning much of heroes renowned in ancient times. Evander said, “These extensive groves were once inhabited by fauns and nymphs, and a rude race of men who sprang from the trees themselves, and had neither laws nor social culture. They knew not how to yoke the cattle nor raise a harvest, nor provide from present abundance for future want; but browsed like beasts upon the leafy boughs, or fed voraciously on their hunted prey. Such were they when Saturn, expelled from Olympus by his sons, came among them and drew together the fierce savages, formed them into society, and gave them laws. Such peace and plenty ensued that men ever since have called his reign the golden age; but by degrees far other times succeeded, and the thirst of gold and the thirst of blood prevailed. The land was a prey to successive tyrants, till fortune and resistless destiny brought me hither, an exile from my native land, Arcadia.”\n\nHaving thus said, he showed him the Tarpeian rock, and the rude spot then overgrown with bushes where in after times the Capitol rose in all its magnificence. He next pointed to some dismantled walls, and said, “Here stood Janiculum, built by Janus, and there Saturnia, the town of Saturn.” Such discourse brought them to the cottage of poor Evander, whence they saw the lowing herds roaming over the plain where now the proud and stately Forum stands. They entered, and a couch was spread for Æneas, well stuffed with leaves, and covered with the skin of a Libyan bear.\n\nNext morning, awakened by the dawn and the shrill song of birds beneath the eaves of his low mansion, old Evander rose. Clad in a tunic, and a panther’s skin thrown over his shoulders, with sandals on his feet and his good sword girded to his side, he went forth to seek his guest. Two mastiffs followed him, his whole retinue and body guard. He found the hero attended by his faithful Achates, and, Pallas soon joining them, the old king spoke thus:\n\n“Illustrious Trojan, it is but little we can do in so great a cause. Our state is feeble, hemmed in on one side by the river, on the other by the Rutulians. But I propose to ally you with a people numerous and rich, to whom fate has brought you at the propitious moment. The Etruscans hold the country beyond the river. Mezentius was their king, a monster of cruelty, who invented unheard-of torments to gratify his vengeance. He would fasten the dead to the living, hand to hand and face to face, and leave the wretched victims to die in that dreadful embrace. At length the people cast him out, him and his house. They burned his palace and slew his friends. He escaped and took refuge with Turnus, who protects him with arms. The Etruscans demand that he shall be given up to deserved punishment, and would ere now have attempted to enforce their demand; but their priests restrain them, telling them that it is the will of heaven that no native of the land shall guide them to victory, and that their destined leader must come from across the sea. They have offered the crown to me, but I am too old to undertake such great affairs, and my son is native-born, which precludes him from the choice. You, equally by birth and time of life, and fame in arms, pointed out by the gods, have but to appear to be hailed at once as their leader. With you I will join Pallas, my son, my only hope and comfort. Under you he shall learn the art of war, and strive to emulate your great exploits.”\n\nThen the king ordered horses to be furnished for the Trojan chiefs, and Æneas, with a chosen band of followers and Pallas accompanying, mounted and took the way to the Etruscan city,[33] having sent back the rest of his party in the ships. Æneas and his band safely arrived at the Etruscan camp and were received with open arms by Tarchon and his countrymen.\n\n# NISUS AND EURYALUS\n\nIn the meanwhile Turnus had collected his bands and made all necessary preparations for the war. Juno sent Iris to him with a message inciting him to take advantage of the absence of Æneas and surprise the Trojan camp. Accordingly the attempt was made, but the Trojans were found on their guard, and having received strict orders from Æneas not to fight in his absence, they lay still in their intrenchments, and resisted all the efforts of the Rutulians to draw them into the field. Night coming on, the army of Turnus, in high spirits at their fancied superiority, feasted and enjoyed themselves, and finally stretched themselves on the field and slept secure.\n\nIn the camp of the Trojans things were far otherwise. There all was watchfulness and anxiety and impatience for Æneas’s return. Nisus stood guard at the entrance of the camp, and Euryalus, a youth distinguished above all in the army for graces of person and fine qualities, was with him. These two were friends and brothers in arms. Nisus said to his friend, “Do you perceive what confidence and carelessness the enemy display? Their lights are few and dim, and the men seem all oppressed with wine or sleep. You know how anxiously our chiefs wish to send to Æneas, and to get intelligence from him. Now, I am strongly moved to make my way through the enemy’s camp and to go in search of our chief. If I succeed, the glory of the deed will be reward enough for me, and if they judge the service deserves anything more, let them pay it to you.”\n\nEuryalus, all on fire with the love of adventure, replied, “Would you, then, Nisus, refuse to share your enterprise with me? And shall I let you go into such danger alone? Not so my brave father brought me up, nor so have I planned for myself when I joined the standard of Æneas, and resolved to hold my life cheap in comparison with honor.” Nisus replied, “I doubt it not, my friend; but you know the uncertain event of such an undertaking, and whatever may happen to me, I wish you to be safe. You are younger than I and have more of life in prospect. Nor can I be the cause of such grief to your mother, who has chosen to be here in the camp with you rather than stay and live in peace with the other matrons in Acestes’ city.” Euryalus replied, “Say no more. In vain you seek arguments to dissuade me. I am fixed in the resolution to go with you. Let us lose no time.” They called the guard, and committing the watch to them, sought the general’s tent. They found the chief officers in consultation, deliberating how they should send notice to Æneas of their situation. The offer of the two friends was gladly accepted, themselves loaded with praises and promised the most liberal rewards in case of success. Iulus especially addressed Euryalus, assuring him of his lasting friendship. Euryalus replied, “I have but one boon to ask. My aged mother is with me in the camp. For me she left the Trojan soil, and would not stay behind with the other matrons at the city of Acestes. I go now without taking leave of her. I could not bear her tears nor set at nought her entreaties. But do thou, I beseech you, comfort her in her distress. Promise me that and I shall go more boldly into whatever dangers may present themselves.” Iulus and the other chiefs were moved to tears, and promised to do all his request. “Your mother shall be mine,” said Iulus, “and all that I have promised to you shall be made good to her, if you do not return to receive it.”\n\nThe two friends left the camp and plunged at once into the midst of the enemy. They found no watch, no sentinels posted, but, all about, the sleeping soldiers strewn on the grass and among the wagons. The laws of war at that early day did not forbid a brave man to slay a sleeping foe, and the two Trojans slew, as they passed, such of the enemy as they could without exciting alarm. In one tent Euryalus made prize of a helmet brilliant with gold and plumes. They had passed through the enemy’s ranks without being discovered, but now suddenly appeared a troop directly in front of them, which, under Volscens, their leader, were approaching the camp. The glittering helmet of Euryalus caught their attention, and Volscens hailed the two, and demanded who and whence they were. They made no answer, but plunged into the wood. The horsemen scattered in all directions to intercept their flight. Nisus had eluded pursuit and was out of danger, but Euryalus being missing he turned back to seek him. He again entered the wood and soon came within sound of voices. Looking through the thicket he saw the whole band surrounding Euryalus with noisy questions. What should he do? how extricate the youth, or would it be better to die with him.\n\nRaising his eyes to the moon, which now shone clear, he said, “Goddess! favor my effort!” and aiming his javelin at one of the leaders of the troop, struck him in the back and stretched him on the plain with a deathblow. In the midst of their amazement another weapon flew and another of the party fell dead. Volscens, the leader, ignorant whence the darts came, rushed sword in hand upon Euryalus. “You shall pay the penalty of both,” he said, and would have plunged the sword into his bosom, when Nisus, who from his concealment saw the peril of his friend, rushed forward exclaiming, “’Twas I, ’twas I; turn your swords against me, Rutulians, I did it; he only followed me as a friend.” While he spoke the sword fell, and pierced the comely bosom of Euryalus. His head fell over on his shoulder, like a flower cut down by the plough. Nisus rushed upon Volscens and plunged his sword into his body, and was himself slain on the instant by numberless blows.\n\nÆneas, with his Etrurian allies, arrived on the scene of action in time to rescue his beleaguered camp; and now the two armies being nearly equal in strength, the war began in good earnest. We cannot find space for all the details, but must simply record the fate of the principal characters whom we have introduced to our readers. The tyrant Mezentius, finding himself engaged against his revolting subjects, raged like a wild beast. He slew all who dared to withstand him, and put the multitude to flight wherever he appeared. At last he encountered Æneas, and the armies stood still to see the issue. Mezentius threw his spear, which striking Æneas’s shield glanced off and hit Anthor. He was a Grecian by birth, who had left Argos, his native city, and followed Evander into Italy. The poet says of him with simple pathos which has made the words proverbial, “He fell, unhappy, by a wound intended for another, looked up at the skies, and dying remembered sweet Argos.”[34] Æneas now in turn hurled his lance. It pierced the shield of Mezentius, and wounded him in the thigh. Lausus, his son, could not bear the sight, but rushed forward and interposed himself, while the followers pressed round Mezentius and bore him away. Æneas held his sword suspended over Lausus and delayed to strike, but the furious youth pressed on and he was compelled to deal the fatal blow. Lausus fell, and Æneas bent over him in pity. “Hapless youth,” he said, “what can I do for you worthy of your praise? Keep those arms in which you glory, and fear not but that your body shall be restored to your friends, and have due funeral honors.” So saying, he called the timid followers and delivered the body into their hands.\n\nMezentius meanwhile had been borne to the riverside, and washed his wound. Soon the news reached him of Lausus’s death, and rage and despair supplied the place of strength. He mounted his horse and dashed into the thickest of the fight, seeking Æneas. Having found him he rode round him in a circle, throwing one javelin after another, while Æneas stood fenced with his shield, turning every way to meet them. At last, after Mezentius had three times made the circuit, Æneas threw his lance directly at the horse’s head. It pierced his temples and he fell, while a shout from both armies rent the skies. Mezentius asked no mercy, but only that his body might be spared the insults of his revolted subjects, and be buried in the same grave with his son. He received the fatal stroke not unprepared, and poured out his life and his blood together.\n\n# PALLAS, CAMILLA, TURNUS\n\nWhile these things were doing in one part of the field, in another Turnus encountered the youthful Pallas. The contest between champions so unequally matched could not be doubtful. Pallas bore himself bravely, but fell by the lance of Turnus. The victor almost relented when he saw the brave youth lying dead at his feet, and spared to use the privilege of a conqueror in despoiling him of his arms. The belt only, adorned with studs and carvings of gold, he took and clasped round his own body. The rest he remitted to the friends of the slain.\n\nAfter the battle there was a cessation of arms for some days to allow both armies to bury their dead. In this interval Æneas challenged Turnus to decide the contest by single combat, but Turnus evaded the challenge. Another battle ensued, in which Camilla, the virgin warrior, was chiefly conspicuous. Her deeds of valor surpassed those of the bravest warriors, and many Trojans and Etruscans fell pierced with her darts or struck down by her battle-axe. At last an Etruscan named Aruns, who had watched her long, seeking for some advantage, observed her pursuing a flying enemy whose splendid armor offered a tempting prize. Intent on the chase she observed not her danger, and the javelin of Aruns struck her and inflicted a fatal wound. She fell and breathed her last in the arms of her attendant maidens. But Diana, who beheld her fate, suffered not her slaughter to be unavenged. Aruns, as he stole away, glad, but frightened, was struck by a secret arrow, launched by one of the nymphs of Diana’s train, and died ignobly and unknown.\n\nAt length the final conflict took place between Æneas and Turnus. Turnus had avoided the contest as long as he could, but at last, impelled by the ill success of his arms and by the murmurs of his followers, he braced himself to the conflict. It could not be doubtful. On the side of Æneas were the expressed decree of destiny, the aid of his goddess-mother at every emergency, and impenetrable armor fabricated by Vulcan, at her request, for her son. Turnus, on the other hand, was deserted by his celestial allies, Juno having been expressly forbidden by Jupiter to assist him any longer. Turnus threw his lance, but it recoiled harmless from the shield of Æneas. The Trojan hero then threw his, which penetrated the shield of Turnus, and pierced his thigh. Then Turnus’s fortitude forsook him and he begged for mercy; and Æneas would have given him his life, but at the instant his eye fell on the belt of Pallas, which Turnus had taken from the slaughtered youth. Instantly his rage revived, and exclaiming, “Pallas immolates thee with this blow,” he thrust him through with his sword.\n\nHere the poem of the “Æneid” closes, and we are left to infer that Æneas, having triumphed over his foes, obtained Lavinia for his bride. Tradition adds that he founded his city, and called it after her name, Lavinium. His son Iulus founded Alba Longa, which was the birthplace of Romulus and Remus and the cradle of Rome itself.\n\nThere is an allusion to Camilla in those well-known lines of Pope, in which, illustrating the rule that “the sound should be an echo to the sense,” he says:\n\n“When Ajax strives some rock’s vast weight to throw, The line too labors and the words move slow. Not so when swift Camilla scours the plain, Flies o’er th’ unbending corn or skims along the main.” —Essay on Criticism.\n\n————\n\nTHE teachings of Anchises to Æneas, respecting the nature of the human soul, were in conformity with the doctrines of the Pythagoreans. Pythagoras (born five hundred and forty years B.C.) was a native of the island of Samos, but passed the chief portion of his life at Crotona in Italy. He is therefore sometimes called “the Samian,” and sometimes “the philosopher of Crotona.” When young he travelled extensively, and it is said visited Egypt, where he was instructed by the priests in all their learning, and afterwards journeyed to the East, and visited the Persian and Chaldean Magi, and the Brahmins of India.\n\nAt Crotona, where he finally established himself, his extraordinary qualities collected round him a great number of disciples. The inhabitants were notorious for luxury and licentiousness, but the good effects of his influence were soon visible. Sobriety and temperance succeeded. Six hundred of the inhabitants became his disciples and enrolled themselves in a society to aid each other in the pursuit of wisdom, uniting their property in one common stock for the benefit of the whole. They were required to practise the greatest purity and simplicity of manners. The first lesson they learned was silence; for a time they were required to be only hearers. “He [Pythagoras] said so” (Ipse dixit), was to be held by them as sufficient, without any proof. It was only the advanced pupils, after years of patient submission, who were allowed to ask questions and to state objections.\n\nPythagoras considered numbers as the essence and principle of all things, and attributed to them a real and distinct existence; so that, in his view, they were the elements out of which the universe was constructed. How he conceived this process has never been satisfactorily explained. He traced the various forms and phenomena of the world to numbers as their basis and essence. The “Monad” or unit he regarded as the source of all numbers. The number Two was imperfect, and the cause of increase and division. Three was called the number of the whole because it had a beginning, middle, and end. Four, representing the square, is in the highest degree perfect; and Ten, as it contains the sum of the four prime numbers, comprehends all musical and arithmetical proportions, and denotes the system of the world.\n\nAs the numbers proceed from the monad, so he regarded the pure and simple essence of the Deity as the source of all the forms of nature. Gods, demons, and heroes are emanations of the Supreme, and there is a fourth emanation, the human soul. This is immortal, and when freed from the fetters of the body passes to the habitation of the dead, where it remains till it returns to the world, to dwell in some other human or animal body, and at last, when sufficiently purified, it returns to the source from which it proceeded. This doctrine of the transmigration of souls (metempsychosis), which was originally Egyptian and connected with the doctrine of reward and punishment of human actions, was the chief cause why the Pythagoreans killed no animals. Ovid represents Pythagoras addressing his disciples in these words: “Souls never die, but always on quitting one abode pass to another. I myself can remember that in the time of the Trojan war I was Euphorbus, the son of Panthus, and fell by the spear of Menelaus. Lately being in the temple of Juno, at Argos, I recognized my shield hung up there among the trophies. All things change, nothing perishes. The soul passes hither and thither, occupying now this body, now that, passing from the body of a beast into that of a man, and thence to a beast’s again. As wax is stamped with certain figures, then melted, then stamped anew with others, yet is always the same wax, so the soul, being always the same, yet wears, at different times, different forms. Therefore, if the love of kindred is not extinct in your bosoms, forbear, I entreat you, to violate the life of those who may haply be your own relatives.”\n\nShakspeare, in the “Merchant of Venice,” makes Gratiano allude to the metempsychosis, where he says to Shylock:\n\n“Thou almost mak’st me waver in my faith, To hold opinion with Pythagoras, That souls of animals infuse themselves Into the trunks of men; thy currish spirit Governed a wolf; who hanged for human slaughter Infused his soul in thee; for thy desires Are wolfish, bloody, starved and ravenous.”\n\nThe relation of the notes of the musical scale to numbers, whereby harmony results from vibrations in equal times, and discord from the reverse, led Pythagoras to apply the word “harmony” to the visible creation, meaning by it the just adaptation of parts to each other. This is the idea which Dryden expresses in the beginning of his “Song for St. Cecilia’s Day”:\n\n“From harmony, from heavenly harmony This everlasting frame began; From harmony to harmony Through all the compass of the notes it ran, The Diapason closing full in Man.”\n\nIn the centre of the universe (he taught) there was a central fire, the principle of life. The central fire was surrounded by the earth, the moon, the sun, and the five planets. The distances of the various heavenly bodies from one another were conceived to correspond to the proportions of the musical scale. The heavenly bodies, with the gods who inhabited them, were supposed to perform a choral dance round the central fire, “not without song.” It is this doctrine which Shakspeare alludes to when he makes Lorenzo teach astronomy to Jessica in this fashion:\n\n“Look, Jessica, see how the floor of heaven Is thick inlaid with patines of bright gold! There’s not the smallest orb that thou behold’st But in his motion like an angel sings, Still quiring to the young-eyed cherubim; Such harmony is in immortal souls! But whilst this muddy vesture of decay Doth grossly close it in we cannot hear it.” —Merchant of Venice.\n\nThe spheres were conceived to be crystalline or glassy fabrics arranged over one another like a nest of bowls reversed. In the substance of each sphere one or more of the heavenly bodies was supposed to be fixed, so as to move with it. As the spheres are transparent we look through them and see the heavenly bodies which they contain and carry round with them. But as these spheres cannot move on one another without friction, a sound is thereby produced which is of exquisite harmony, too fine for mortal ears to recognize. Milton, in his “Hymn on the Nativity,” thus alludes to the music of the spheres:\n\n“Ring out, ye crystal spheres! Once bless our human ears (If ye have power to charm our senses so); And let your silver chime Move in melodious time, And let the base of Heaven’s deep organ blow; And with your ninefold harmony Make up full concert with the angelic symphony.”\n\nPythagoras is said to have invented the lyre. Our own poet Longfellow, in “Verses to a Child,” thus relates the story:\n\n“As great Pythagoras of yore, Standing beside the blacksmith’s door, And hearing the hammers as they smote The anvils with a different note, Stole from the varying tones that hung Vibrant on every iron tongue, The secret of the sounding wire, And formed the seven-chorded lyre.”\n\nSee also the same poet’s “Occultation of Orion”—\n\n“The Samian’s great Æolian lyre.”\n\n# SYBARIS AND CROTONA\n\nSybaris, a neighboring city to Crotona, was as celebrated for luxury and effeminacy as Crotona for the reverse. The name has become proverbial. J. R. Lowell uses it in this sense in his charming little poem “To the Dandelion”:\n\n“Not in mid June the golden cuirassed bee Feels a more summer-like, warm ravishment In the white lily’s breezy tent (His conquered Sybaris) than I when first From the dark green thy yellow circles burst.”\n\nA war arose between the two cities, and Sybaris was conquered and destroyed. Milo, the celebrated athlete, led the army of Crotona. Many stories are told of Milo’s vast strength, such as his carrying a heifer of four years old upon his shoulders and afterwards eating the whole of it in a single day. The mode of his death is thus related: As he was passing through a forest he saw the trunk of a tree which had been partially split open by wood-cutters, and attempted to rend it further; but the wood closed upon his hands and held him fast, in which state he was attacked and devoured by wolves.\n\nByron, in his “Ode to Napoleon Bonaparte,” alludes to the story of Milo:\n\n“He who of old would rend the oak Deemed not of the rebound; Chained by the trunk he vainly broke, Alone, how looked he round!”\n\n# EGYPTIAN DEITIES\n\nThe Egyptians acknowledged as the highest deity Amun, afterwards called Zeus, or Jupiter Ammon. Amun manifested himself in his word or will, which created Kneph and Athor, of different sexes. From Kneph and Athor proceeded Osiris and Isis. Osiris was worshipped as the god of the sun, the source of warmth, life, and fruitfulness, in addition to which he was also regarded as the god of the Nile, who annually visited his wife, Isis (the Earth), by means of an inundation. Serapis or Hermes is sometimes represented as identical with Osiris, and sometimes as a distinct divinity, the ruler of Tartarus and god of medicine. Anubis is the guardian god, represented with a dog’s head, emblematic of his character of fidelity and watchfulness. Horus or Harpocrates was the son of Osiris. He is represented seated on a Lotus flower, with his finger on his lips, as the god of Silence.\n\nIn one of Moore’s “Irish Melodies” is an allusion to Harpocrates:\n\n“Thyself shall, under some rosy bower, Sit mute, with thy finger on thy lip; Like him, the boy, who born among The flowers that on the Nile-stream blush, Sits ever thus,—his only song To Earth and Heaven, ‘Hush all, hush!’”\n\n# MYTH OF OSIRIS AND ISIS\n\nOsiris and Isis were at one time induced to descend to the earth to bestow gifts and blessings on its inhabitants. Isis showed them first the use of wheat and barley, and Osiris made the instruments of agriculture and taught men the use of them, as well as how to harness the ox to the plough. He then gave men laws, the institution of marriage, a civil organization, and taught them how to worship the gods. After he had thus made the valley of the Nile a happy country, he assembled a host with which he went to bestow his blessings upon the rest of the world. He conquered the nations everywhere, but not with weapons, only with music and eloquence. His brother Typhon saw this, and filled with envy and malice sought during his absence to usurp his throne. But Isis, who held the reins of government, frustrated his plans. Still more embittered, he now resolved to kill his brother. This he did in the following manner: Having organized a conspiracy of seventy-two members, he went with them to the feast which was celebrated in honor of the king’s return. He then caused a box or chest to be brought in, which had been made to fit exactly the size of Osiris, and declared that he would give that chest of precious wood to whosoever could get into it. The rest tried in vain, but no sooner was Osiris in it than Typhon and his companions closed the lid and flung the chest into the Nile. When Isis heard of the cruel murder she wept and mourned, and then with her hair shorn, clothed in black and beating her breast, she sought diligently for the body of her husband. In this search she was materially assisted by Anubis, the son of Osiris and Nephthys. They sought in vain for some time; for when the chest, carried by the waves to the shores of Byblos, had become entangled in the reeds that grew at the edge of the water, the divine power that dwelt in the body of Osiris imparted such strength to the shrub that it grew into a mighty tree, enclosing in its trunk the coffin of the god. This tree with its sacred deposit was shortly after felled, and erected as a column in the palace of the king of Phœnicia. But at length by the aid of Anubis and the sacred birds, Isis ascertained these facts, and then went to the royal city. There she offered herself at the palace as a servant, and being admitted, threw off her disguise and appeared as a goddess, surrounded with thunder and lightning. Striking the column with her wand she caused it to split open and give up the sacred coffin. This she seized and returned with it, and concealed it in the depth of a forest, but Typhon discovered it, and cutting the body into fourteen pieces scattered them hither and thither. After a tedious search, Isis found thirteen pieces, the fishes of the Nile having eaten the other. This she replaced by an imitation of sycamore wood, and buried the body at Philæ, which became ever after the great burying place of the nation, and the spot to which pilgrimages were made from all parts of the country. A temple of surpassing magnificence was also erected there in honor of the god, and at every place where one of his limbs had been found minor temples and tombs were built to commemorate the event. Osiris became after that the tutelar deity of the Egyptians. His soul was supposed always to inhabit the body of the bull Apis, and at his death to transfer itself to his successor.\n\nApis, the Bull of Memphis, was worshipped with the greatest reverence by the Egyptians. The individual animal who was held to be Apis was recognized by certain signs. It was requisite that he should be quite black, have a white square mark on the forehead, another, in the form of an eagle, on his back, and under his tongue a lump somewhat in the shape of a scarabæus or beetle. As soon as a bull thus marked was found by those sent in search of him, he was placed in a building facing the east, and was fed with milk for four months. At the expiration of this term the priests repaired at new moon, with great pomp, to his habitation and saluted him Apis. He was placed in a vessel magnificently decorated and conveyed down the Nile to Memphis, where a temple, with two chapels and a court for exercise, was assigned to him. Sacrifices were made to him, and once every year, about the time when the Nile began to rise, a golden cup was thrown into the river, and a grand festival was held to celebrate his birthday. The people believed that during this festival the crocodiles forgot their natural ferocity and became harmless. There was, however, one drawback to his happy lot: he was not permitted to live beyond a certain period, and if, when he had attained the age of twenty-five years, he still survived, the priests drowned him in the sacred cistern and then buried him in the temple of Serapis. On the death of this bull, whether it occurred in the course of nature or by violence, the whole land was filled with sorrow and lamentations, which lasted until his successor was found.\n\nWe find the following item in one of the newspapers of the day:\n\n“The Tomb of Apis.—The excavations going on at Memphis bid fair to make that buried city as interesting as Pompeii. The monster tomb of Apis is now open, after having lain unknown for centuries.”\n\nMilton, in his “Hymn on the Nativity,” alludes to the Egyptian deities, not as imaginary beings, but as real demons, put to flight by the coming of Christ.\n\n“The brutish gods of Nile as fast, Isis and Horus and the dog Anubis haste. Nor is Osiris seen In Memphian grove or green Trampling the unshowered[35] grass with lowings loud; Nor can he be at rest Within his sacred chest; Nought but profoundest hell can be his shroud. In vain with timbrel’d anthems dark The sable-stoled sorcerers bear his worshipped ark.”\n\nIsis was represented in statuary with the head veiled, a symbol of mystery. It is this which Tennyson alludes to in “Maud,” IV., 8:\n\n“For the drift of the Maker is dark, an Isis hid by the veil,” etc.\n\nOracle was the name used to denote the place where answers were supposed to be given by any of the divinities to those who consulted them respecting the future. The word was also used to signify the response which was given.\n\nThe most ancient Grecian oracle was that of Jupiter at Dodona. According to one account, it was established in the following manner: Two black doves took their flight from Thebes in Egypt. One flew to Dodona in Epirus, and alighting in a grove of oaks, it proclaimed in human language to the inhabitants of the district that they must establish there an oracle of Jupiter. The other dove flew to the temple of Jupiter Ammon in the Libyan Oasis, and delivered a similar command there. Another account is, that they were not doves, but priestesses, who were carried off from Thebes in Egypt by the Phœnicians, and set up oracles at the Oasis and Dodona. The responses of the oracle were given from the trees, by the branches rustling in the wind, the sounds being interpreted by the priests.\n\nBut the most celebrated of the Grecian oracles was that of Apollo at Delphi, a city built on the slopes of Parnassus in Phocis.\n\nIt had been observed at a very early period that the goats feeding on Parnassus were thrown into convulsions when they approached a certain long deep cleft in the side of the mountain. This was owing to a peculiar vapor arising out of the cavern, and one of the goatherds was induced to try its effects upon himself. Inhaling the intoxicating air, he was affected in the same manner as the cattle had been, and the inhabitants of the surrounding country, unable to explain the circumstance, imputed the convulsive ravings to which he gave utterance while under the power of the exhalations to a divine inspiration. The fact was speedily circulated widely, and a temple was erected on the spot. The prophetic influence was at first variously attributed to the goddess Earth, to Neptune, Themis, and others, but it was at length assigned to Apollo, and to him alone. A priestess was appointed whose office it was to inhale the hallowed air, and who was named the Pythia. She was prepared for this duty by previous ablution at the fountain of Castalia, and being crowned with laurel was seated upon a tripod similarly adorned, which was placed over the chasm whence the divine afflatus proceeded. Her inspired words while thus situated were interpreted by the priests.\n\n# ORACLE OF TROPHONIUS\n\nBesides the oracles of Jupiter and Apollo, at Dodona and Delphi, that of Trophonius in Bœotia was held in high estimation. Trophonius and Agamedes were brothers. They were distinguished architects, and built the temple of Apollo at Delphi, and a treasury for King Hyrieus. In the wall of the treasury they placed a stone, in such a manner that it could be taken out; and by this means, from time to time, purloined the treasure. This amazed Hyrieus, for his locks and seals were untouched, and yet his wealth continually diminished. At length he set a trap for the thief and Agamedes was caught. Trophonius, unable to extricate him, and fearing that when found he would be compelled by torture to discover his accomplice, cut off his head. Trophonius himself is said to have been shortly afterwards swallowed up by the earth.\n\nThe oracle of Trophonius was at Lebadea in Bœotia. During a great drought the Bœotians, it is said, were directed by the god at Delphi to seek aid of Trophonius at Lebadea. They came thither, but could find no oracle. One of them, however, happening to see a swarm of bees, followed them to a chasm in the earth, which proved to be the place sought.\n\nPeculiar ceremonies were to be performed by the person who came to consult the oracle. After these preliminaries, he descended into the cave by a narrow passage. This place could be entered only in the night. The person returned from the cave by the same narrow passage, but walking backwards. He appeared melancholy and dejected; and hence the proverb which was applied to a person low-spirited and gloomy, “He has been consulting the oracle of Trophonius.”\n\nThere were numerous oracles of Æsculapius, but the most celebrated one was at Epidaurus. Here the sick sought responses and the recovery of their health by sleeping in the temple. It has been inferred from the accounts that have come down to us that the treatment of the sick resembled what is now called Animal Magnetism or Mesmerism.\n\nSerpents were sacred to Æsculapius, probably because of a superstition that those animals have a faculty of renewing their youth by a change of skin. The worship of Æsculapius was introduced into Rome in a time of great sickness, and an embassy sent to the temple of Epidaurus to entreat the aid of the god. Æsculapius was propitious, and on the return of the ship accompanied it in the form of a serpent. Arriving in the river Tiber, the serpent glided from the vessel and took possession of an island in the river, and a temple was there erected to his honor.\n\n# ORACLE OF APIS\n\nAt Memphis the sacred bull Apis gave answer to those who consulted him by the manner in which he received or rejected what was presented to him. If the bull refused food from the hand of the inquirer it was considered an unfavorable sign, and the contrary when he received it.\n\nIt has been a question whether oracular responses ought to be ascribed to mere human contrivance or to the agency of evil spirits. The latter opinion has been most general in past ages. A third theory has been advanced since the phenomena of Mesmerism have attracted attention, that something like the mesmeric trance was induced in the Pythoness, and the faculty of clairvoyance really called into action.\n\nAnother question is as to the time when the Pagan oracles ceased to give responses. Ancient Christian writers assert that they became silent at the birth of Christ, and were heard no more after that date. Milton adopts this view in his “Hymn on the Nativity,” and in lines of solemn and elevated beauty pictures the consternation of the heathen idols at the advent of the Saviour:\n\n“The oracles are dumb; No voice or hideous hum Rings through the arched roof in words deceiving. Apollo from his shrine Can no more divine, With hollow shriek the steep of Delphos leaving. No nightly trance or breathed spell Inspires the pale-eyed priest from the prophetic cell.”\n\nIn Cowper’s poem of “Yardley Oak” there are some beautiful mythological allusions. The former of the two following is to the fable of Castor and Pollux; the latter is more appropriate to our present subject. Addressing the acorn he says:\n\n“Thou fell’st mature; and in the loamy clod, Swelling with vegetative force instinct, Didst burst thine egg, as theirs the fabled Twins Now stars; two lobes protruding, paired exact; A leaf succeeded and another leaf, And, all the elements thy puny growth Fostering propitious, thou becam’st a twig. Who lived when thou wast such? O, couldst thou speak, As in Dodona once thy kindred trees Oracular, I would not curious ask The future, best unknown, but at thy mouth Inquisitive, the less ambiguous past.”\n\nTennyson, in his “Talking Oak,” alludes to the oaks of Dodona in these lines:\n\n“And I will work in prose and rhyme, And praise thee more in both Than bard has honored beech or lime, Or that Thessalian growth In which the swarthy ring-dove sat And mystic sentence spoke;” etc.\n\nByron alludes to the oracle of Delphi where, speaking of Rousseau, whose writings he conceives did much to bring on the French revolution, he says:\n\n“For then he was inspired, and from him came, As from the Pythian’s mystic cave of yore, Those oracles which set the world in flame, Nor ceased to burn till kingdoms were no more.”\n\n# CHAPTER XXXV\n\nORIGIN OF MYTHOLOGY—STATUES OF GODS AND GODDESSES—POETS OF MYTHOLOGY\nHAVING reached the close of our series of stories of Pagan mythology, an inquiry suggests itself. “Whence came these stories? Have they a foundation in truth, or are they simply dreams of the imagination?” Philosophers have suggested various theories on the subject; and 1. The Scriptural theory; according to which all mythological legends are derived from the narratives of Scripture, though the real facts have been disguised and altered. Thus Deucalion is only another name for Noah, Hercules for Samson, Arion for Jonah, etc. Sir Walter Raleigh, in his “History of the World,” says, “Jubal, Tubal, and Tubal-Cain were Mercury, Vulcan, and Apollo, inventors of Pasturage, Smithing, and Music. The Dragon which kept the golden apples was the serpent that beguiled Eve. Nimrod’s tower was the attempt of the Giants against Heaven.” There are doubtless many curious coincidences like these, but the theory cannot without extravagance be pushed so far as to account for any great proportion of the stories.\n\n2. The Historical theory; according to which all the persons mentioned in mythology were once real human beings, and the legends and fabulous traditions relating to them are merely the additions and embellishments of later times. Thus the story of Æolus, the king and god of the winds, is supposed to have risen from the fact that Æolus was the ruler of some islands in the Tyrrhenian Sea, where he reigned as a just and pious king, and taught the natives the use of sails for ships, and how to tell from the signs of the atmosphere the changes of the weather and the winds. Cadmus, who, the legend says, sowed the earth with dragon’s teeth, from which sprang a crop of armed men, was in fact an emigrant from Phœnicia, and brought with him into Greece the knowledge of the letters of the alphabet, which he taught to the natives. From these rudiments of learning sprung civilization, which the poets have always been prone to describe as a deterioration of man’s first estate, the Golden Age of innocence and simplicity.\n\n3. The Allegorical theory supposes that all the myths of the ancients were allegorical and symbolical, and contained some moral, religious, or philosophical truth or historical fact, under the form of an allegory, but came in process of time to be understood literally. Thus Saturn, who devours his own children, is the same power whom the Greeks called Cronos (Time), which may truly be said to destroy whatever it has brought into existence. The story of Io is interpreted in a similar manner. Io is the moon, and Argus the starry sky, which, as it were, keeps sleepless watch over her. The fabulous wanderings of Io represent the continual revolutions of the moon, which also suggested to Milton the same idea.\n\n“To behold the wandering moon Riding near her highest noon, Like one that had been led astray In the heaven’s wide, pathless way.” —Il Penseroso.\n\n4. The Physical theory; according to which the elements of air, fire, and water were originally the objects of religious adoration, and the principal deities were personifications of the powers of nature. The transition was easy from a personification of the elements to the notion of supernatural beings presiding over and governing the different objects of nature. The Greeks, whose imagination was lively, peopled all nature with invisible beings, and supposed that every object, from the sun and sea to the smallest fountain and rivulet, was under the care of some particular divinity. Wordsworth, in his “Excursion,” has beautifully developed this view of Grecian mythology:\n\n“In that fair clime the lonely herdsman, stretched On the soft grass through half a summer’s day, With music lulled his indolent repose; And, in some fit of weariness, if he, When his own breath was silent, chanced to hear A distant strain far sweeter than the sounds Which his poor skill could make, his fancy fetched Even from the blazing chariot of the Sun A beardless youth who touched a golden lute, And filled the illumined groves with ravishment. The mighty hunter, lifting up his eyes Toward the crescent Moon, with grateful heart Called on the lovely Wanderer who bestowed That timely light to share his joyous sport; And hence a beaming goddess with her nymphs Across the lawn and through the darksome grove (Not unaccompanied with tuneful notes By echo multiplied from rock or cave) Swept in the storm of chase, as moon and stars Glance rapidly along the clouded heaven When winds are blowing strong. The Traveller slaked His thirst from rill or gushing fount, and thanked The Naiad. Sunbeams upon distant hills Gliding apace with shadows in their train, Might with small help from fancy, be transformed Into fleet Oreads sporting visibly. The Zephyrs, fanning, as they passed, their wings, Lacked not for love fair objects whom they wooed With gentle whisper. Withered boughs grotesque, Stripped of their leaves and twigs by hoary age, From depth of shaggy covert peeping forth In the low vale, or on steep mountain side; And sometimes intermixed with stirring horns Of the live deer, or goat’s depending beard; These were the lurking Satyrs, a wild brood Of gamesome deities; or Pan himself, That simple shepherd’s awe-inspiring god.”\n\nAll the theories which have been mentioned are true to a certain extent. It would therefore be more correct to say that the mythology of a nation has sprung from all these sources combined than from any one in particular. We may add also that there are many myths which have arisen from the desire of man to account for those natural phenomena which he cannot understand; and not a few have had their rise from a similar desire of giving a reason for the names of places and persons.\n\n# STATUES OF THE GODS\n\nTo adequately represent to the eye the ideas intended to be conveyed to the mind under the several names of deities was a task which called into exercise the highest powers of genius and art. Of the many attempts four have been most celebrated, the first two known to us only by the descriptions of the ancients, the others still extant and the acknowledged masterpieces of the sculptor’s art.\n\n# THE OLYMPIAN JUPITER\n\nThe statue of the Olympian Jupiter by Phidias was considered the highest achievement of this department of Grecian art. It was of colossal dimensions, and was what the ancients called “chryselephantine;” that is, composed of ivory and gold; the parts representing flesh being of ivory laid on a core of wood or stone, while the drapery and other ornaments were of gold. The height of the figure was forty feet, on a pedestal twelve feet high. The god was represented seated on his throne. His brows were crowned with a wreath of olive, and he held in his right hand a sceptre, and in his left a statue of Victory. The throne was of cedar, adorned with gold and precious stones.\n\nThe idea which the artist essayed to embody was that of the supreme deity of the Hellenic (Grecian) nation, enthroned as a conqueror, in perfect majesty and repose, and ruling with a nod the subject world. Phidias avowed that he took his idea from the representation which Homer gives in the first book of the “Iliad,” in the passage thus translated by Pope:\n\n“He spoke and awful bends his sable brows, Shakes his ambrosial curls and gives the nod, The stamp of fate and sanction of the god. High heaven with reverence the dread signal took, And all Olympus to the centre shook.”[36]\n\n# THE MINERVA OF THE PARTHENON\n\nThis was also the work of Phidias. It stood in the Parthenon, or temple of Minerva at Athens. The goddess was represented standing. In one hand she held a spear, in the other a statue of Victory. Her helmet, highly decorated, was surmounted by a Sphinx. The statue was forty feet in height, and, like the Jupiter, composed of ivory and gold. The eyes were of marble, and probably painted to represent the iris and pupil. The Parthenon, in which this statue stood, was also constructed under the direction and superintendence of Phidias. Its exterior was enriched with sculptures, many of them from the hand of Phidias. The Elgin marbles, now in the British Museum, are a part of them.\n\nBoth the Jupiter and Minerva of Phidias are lost, but there is good ground to believe that we have, in several extant statues and busts, the artist’s conceptions of the countenances of both. They are characterized by grave and dignified beauty, and freedom from any transient expression, which in the language of art is called repose.\n\n# THE VENUS DE’ MEDICI\n\nThe Venus of the Medici is so called from its having been in the possession of the princes of that name in Rome when it first attracted attention, about two hundred years ago. An inscription on the base records it to be the work of Cleomenes, an Athenian sculptor of 200 B.C., but the authenticity of the inscription is doubtful. There is a story that the artist was employed by public authority to make a statue exhibiting the perfection of female beauty, and to aid him in his task the most perfect forms the city could supply were furnished him for models. It is this which Thomson alludes to in his “Summer”:\n\n“So stands the statue that enchants the world; So bending tries to veil the matchless boast, The mingled beauties of exulting Greece.”\n\nByron also alludes to this statue. Speaking of the Florence Museum, he says:\n\n“There, too, the goddess loves in stone, and fills The air around with beauty;” etc.\n\nAnd in the next stanza,\n\n“Blood, pulse, and breast confirm the Dardan shepherd’s prize.”\n\nSee this last allusion explained in Chapter XXVII.\n\n# THE APOLLO BELVEDERE\n\nThe most highly esteemed of all the remains of ancient sculpture is the statue of Apollo, called the Belvedere, from the name of the apartment of the Pope’s palace at Rome in which it was placed. The artist is unknown. It is supposed to be a work of Roman art, of about the first century of our era. It is a standing figure, in marble, more than seven feet high, naked except for the cloak which is fastened around the neck and hangs over the extended left arm. It is supposed to represent the god in the moment when he has shot the arrow to destroy the monster Python. (See Chapter III.) The victorious divinity is in the act of stepping forward. The left arm, which seems to have held the bow, is outstretched, and the head is turned in the same direction. In attitude and proportion the graceful majesty of the figure is unsurpassed. The effect is completed by the countenance, where on the perfection of youthful godlike beauty there dwells the consciousness of triumphant power.\n\n# THE DIANA A LA BICHE\n\nThe Diana of the Hind, in the palace of the Louvre, may be considered the counterpart to the Apollo Belvedere. The attitude much resembles that of the Apollo, the sizes correspond and also the style of execution. It is a work of the highest order, though by no means equal to the Apollo. The attitude is that of hurried and eager motion, the face that of a huntress in the excitement of the chase. The left hand is extended over the forehead of the Hind, which runs by her side, the right arm reaches backward over the shoulder to draw an arrow from the quiver.\n\n# THE POETS OF MYTHOLOGY\n\nHomer, from whose poems of the “Iliad” and “Odyssey” we have taken the chief part of our chapters of the Trojan war and the return of the Grecians, is almost as mythical a personage as the heroes he celebrates. The traditionary story is that he was a wandering minstrel, blind and old, who travelled from place to place singing his lays to the music of his harp, in the courts of princes or the cottages of peasants, and dependent upon the voluntary offerings of his hearers for support. Byron calls him “The blind old man of Scio’s rocky isle,” and a well-known epigram, alluding to the uncertainty of the fact of his birthplace, says:\n\n“Seven wealthy towns contend for Homer dead, Through which the living Homer begged his bread.”\n\nThese seven were Smyrna, Scio, Rhodes, Colophon, Salamis, Argos, and Athens.\n\nModern scholars have doubted whether the Homeric poems are the work of any single mind. This arises from the difficulty of believing that poems of such length could have been committed to writing at so early an age as that usually assigned to these, an age earlier than the date of any remaining inscriptions or coins, and when no materials capable of containing such long productions were yet introduced into use. On the other hand it is asked how poems of such length could have been handed down from age to age by means of the memory alone. This is answered by the statement that there was a professional body of men, called Rhapsodists, who recited the poems of others, and whose business it was to commit to memory and rehearse for pay the national and patriotic legends.\n\nThe prevailing opinion of the learned, at this time, seems to be that the framework and much of the structure of the poems belong to Homer, but that there are numerous interpolations and additions by other hands.\n\nThe date assigned to Homer, on the authority of Herodotus, is 850 B.C.\n\nVirgil, called also by his surname, Maro, from whose poem of the “Æneid” we have taken the story of Æneas, was one of the great poets who made the reign of the Roman emperor Augustus so celebrated, under the name of the Augustan age. Virgil was born in Mantua in the year 70 B.C. His great poem is ranked next to those of Homer, in the highest class of poetical composition, the Epic. Virgil is far inferior to Homer in originality and invention, but superior to him in correctness and elegance. To critics of English lineage Milton alone of modern poets seems worthy to be classed with these illustrious ancients. His poem of “Paradise Lost,” from which we have borrowed so many illustrations, is in many respects equal, in some superior, to either of the great works of antiquity. The following epigram of Dryden characterizes the three poets with as much truth as it is usual to find in such pointed criticism:\n\n“Three poets in three different ages born, Greece, Italy, and England did adorn. The first in loftiness of soul surpassed, The next in majesty, in both the last. The force of nature could no further go; To make a third she joined the other two.”\n\nFrom Cowper’s “Table Talk”:\n\n“Ages elapsed ere Homer’s lamp appeared, And ages ere the Mantuan swan was heard. To carry nature lengths unknown before, To give a Milton birth, asked ages more. Thus genius rose and set at ordered times, And shot a dayspring into distant climes, Ennobling every region that he chose; He sunk in Greece, in Italy he rose, And, tedious years of Gothic darkness past, Emerged all splendor in our isle at last. Thus lovely Halcyons dive into the main, Then show far off their shining plumes again.”\n\noften alluded to in poetry by his other name of Naso, was born in the year 43 B.C. He was educated for public life and held some offices of considerable dignity, but poetry was his delight, and he early resolved to devote himself to it. He accordingly sought the society of the contemporary poets, and was acquainted with Horace and saw Virgil, though the latter died when Ovid was yet too young and undistinguished to have formed his acquaintance. Ovid spent an easy life at Rome in the enjoyment of a competent income. He was intimate with the family of Augustus, the emperor, and it is supposed that some serious offence given to some member of that family was the cause of an event which reversed the poet’s happy circumstances and clouded all the latter portion of his life. At the age of fifty he was banished from Rome, and ordered to betake himself to Tomi, on the borders of the Black Sea. Here, among the barbarous people and in a severe climate, the poet, who had been accustomed to all the pleasures of a luxurious capital and the society of his most distinguished contemporaries, spent the last ten years of his life, worn out with grief and anxiety. His only consolation in exile was to address his wife and absent friends, and his letters were all poetical. Though these poems (the “Trista” and “Letters from Pontus”) have no other topic than the poet’s sorrows, his exquisite taste and fruitful invention have redeemed them from the charge of being tedious, and they are read with pleasure and even with sympathy.\n\nThe two great works of Ovid are his “Metamorphoses” and his “Fasti.” They are both mythological poems, and from the former we have taken most of our stories of Grecian and Roman mythology. A late writer thus characterizes these poems:\n\n“The rich mythology of Greece furnished Ovid, as it may still furnish the poet, the painter, and the sculptor, with materials for his art. With exquisite taste, simplicity, and pathos he has narrated the fabulous traditions of early ages, and given to them that appearance of reality which only a master hand could impart. His pictures of nature are striking and true; he selects with care that which is appropriate; he rejects the superfluous; and when he has completed his work, it is neither defective nor redundant. The ‘Metamorphoses’ are read with pleasure by youth, and are re-read in more advanced age with still greater delight. The poet ventured to predict that his poem would survive him, and be read wherever the Roman name was known.”\n\nThe prediction above alluded to is contained in the closing lines of the “Metamorphoses,” of which we give a literal translation below:\n\n“And now I close my work, which not the ire Of Jove, nor tooth of time, nor sword, nor fire Shall bring to nought. Come when it will that day Which o’er the body, not the mind, has sway, And snatch the remnant of my life away, My better part above the stars shall soar, And my renown endure forevermore. Where’er the Roman arms and arts shall spread, There by the people shall my book be read; And, if aught true in poet’s visions be, My name and fame have immortality.”\n\n————\n\n# MODERN MONSTERS\n\nTHERE is a set of imaginary beings which seem to have been the successors of the “Gorgons, Hydras, and Chimeras dire” of the old superstitions, and, having no connection with the false gods of Paganism, to have continued to enjoy an existence in the popular belief after Paganism was superseded by Christianity. They are mentioned perhaps by the classical writers, but their chief popularity and currency seem to have been in more modern times. We seek our accounts of them not so much in the poetry of the ancients as in the old natural history books and narrations of travellers. The accounts which we are about to give are taken chiefly from the Penny Cyclopedia.\n\nOvid tells the story of the Phœnix as follows: “Most beings spring from other individuals; but there is a certain kind which reproduces itself. The Assyrians call it the Phœnix. It does not live on fruit or flowers, but on frankincense and odoriferous gums. When it has lived five hundred years, it builds itself a nest in the branches of an oak, or on the top of a palm tree. In this it collects cinnamon, and spikenard, and myrrh, and of these materials builds a pile on which it deposits itself, and dying, breathes out its last breath amidst odors. From the body of the parent bird, a young Phœnix issues forth, destined to live as long a life as its predecessor. When this has grown up and gained sufficient strength, it lifts its nest from the tree (its own cradle and its parent’s sepulchre), and carries it to the city of Heliopolis in Egypt, and deposits it in the temple of the Sun.”\n\nSuch is the account given by a poet. Now let us see that of a philosophic historian. Tacitus says, “In the consulship of Paulus Fabius (A.D. 34) the miraculous bird known to the world by the name of the Phœnix, after disappearing for a series of ages, revisited Egypt. It was attended in its flight by a group of various birds, all attracted by the novelty, and gazing with wonder at so beautiful an appearance.” He then gives an account of the bird, not varying materially from the preceding, but adding some details. “The first care of the young bird as soon as fledged, and able to trust to his wings, is to perform the obsequies of his father. But this duty is not undertaken rashly. He collects a quantity of myrrh, and to try his strength makes frequent excursions with a load on his back. When he has gained sufficient confidence in his own vigor, he takes up the body of his father and flies with it to the altar of the Sun, where he leaves it to be consumed in flames of fragrance.” Other writers add a few particulars. The myrrh is compacted in the form of an egg, in which the dead Phœnix is enclosed. From the mouldering flesh of the dead bird a worm springs, and this worm, when grown large, is transformed into a bird. Herodotus describes the bird, though he says, “I have not seen it myself, except in a picture. Part of his plumage is gold-colored, and part crimson; and he is for the most part very much like an eagle in outline and bulk.”\n\nThe first writer who disclaimed a belief in the existence of the Phœnix was Sir Thomas Browne, in his “Vulgar Errors,” published in 1646. He was replied to a few years later by Alexander Ross, who says, in answer to the objection of the Phœnix so seldom making his appearance, “His instinct teaches him to keep out of the way of the tyrant of the creation, man, for if he were to be got at, some wealthy glutton would surely devour him, though there were no more in the world.”\n\nDryden in one of his early poems has this allusion to the Phœnix:\n\n“So when the new-born Phœnix first is seen, Her feathered subjects all adore their queen, And while she makes her progress through the East, From every grove her numerous train’s increased; Each poet of the air her glory sings, And round him the pleased audience clap their wings.”\n\nMilton, in “Paradise Lost,” Book V., compares the angel Raphael descending to earth to a Phœnix:\n\n“. . . Down thither, prone in flight He speeds, and through the vast ethereal sky Sails between worlds and worlds, with steady wing, Now on the polar winds, then with quick fan Winnows the buxom air; till within soar Of towering eagles, to all the fowls he seems A Phœnix, gazed by all; as that sole bird When, to enshrine his relics in the sun’s Bright temple, to Egyptian Thebes he flies.”\n\n# THE COCKATRICE, OR BASILISK\n\nThis animal was called the king of the serpents. In confirmation of his royalty, he was said to be endowed with a crest, or comb upon the head, constituting a crown. He was supposed to be produced from the egg of a cock hatched under toads or serpents. There were several species of this animal. One species burned up whatever they approached; a second were a kind of wandering Medusa’s heads, and their look caused an instant horror which was immediately followed by death. In Shakspeare’s play of “Richard the Third,” Lady Anne, in answer to Richard’s compliment on her eyes, says, “Would they were basilisk’s, to strike thee dead!”\n\nThe basilisks were called kings of serpents because all other serpents and snakes, behaving like good subjects, and wisely not wishing to be burned up or struck dead, fled the moment they heard the distant hiss of their king, although they might be in full feed upon the most delicious prey, leaving the sole enjoyment of the banquet to the royal monster.\n\nThe Roman naturalist Pliny thus describes him: “He does not impel his body, like other serpents, by a multiplied flexion, but advances lofty and upright. He kills the shrubs, not only by contact, but by breathing on them, and splits the rocks, such power of evil is there in him.” It was formerly believed that if killed by a spear from on horseback the power of the poison conducted through the weapon killed not only the rider, but the horse also. To this Lucan alludes in these lines:\n\n“What though the Moor the basilisk hath slain, And pinned him lifeless to the sandy plain, Up through the spear the subtle venom flies, The hand imbibes it, and the victor dies.”\n\nSuch a prodigy was not likely to be passed over in the legends of the saints. Accordingly we find it recorded that a certain holy man, going to a fountain in the desert, suddenly beheld a basilisk. He immediately raised his eyes to heaven, and with a pious appeal to the Deity laid the monster dead at his feet.\n\nThese wonderful powers of the basilisk are attested by a host of learned persons, such as Galen, Avicenna, Scaliger, and others. Occasionally one would demur to some part of the tale while he admitted the rest. Jonston, a learned physician, sagely remarks, “I would scarcely believe that it kills with its look, for who could have seen it and lived to tell the story?” The worthy sage was not aware that those who went to hunt the basilisk of this sort took with them a mirror, which reflected back the deadly glare upon its author, and by a kind of poetical justice slew the basilisk with his own weapon.\n\nBut what was to attack this terrible and unapproachable monster? There is an old saying that “everything has its enemy”—and the cockatrice quailed before the weasel. The basilisk might look daggers, the weasel cared not, but advanced boldly to the conflict. When bitten, the weasel retired for a moment to eat some rue, which was the only plant the basilisks could not wither, returned with renewed strength and soundness to the charge, and never left the enemy till he was stretched dead on the plain. The monster, too, as if conscious of the irregular way in which he came into the world, was supposed to have a great antipathy to a cock; and well he might, for as soon as he heard the cock crow he expired.\n\nThe basilisk was of some use after death. Thus we read that its carcass was suspended in the temple of Apollo, and in private houses, as a sovereign remedy against spiders, and that it was also hung up in the temple of Diana, for which reason no swallow ever dared enter the sacred place.\n\nThe reader will, we apprehend, by this time have had enough of absurdities, but still we can imagine his anxiety to know what a cockatrice was like. The following is from Aldrovandus, a celebrated naturalist of the sixteenth century, whose work on natural history, in thirteen folio volumes, contains with much that is valuable a large proportion of fables and inutilities. In particular he is so ample on the subject of the cock and the bull that from his practice, all rambling, gossiping tales of doubtful credibility are called cock and bull stories. Aldrovandus, however, deserves our respect and esteem as the founder of a botanic garden, and as a pioneer in the now prevalent custom of making scientific collections for purposes of investigation and research.\n\nShelley, in his “Ode to Naples,” full of the enthusiasm excited by the intelligence of the proclamation of a Constitutional Government at Naples, in 1820, thus uses an allusion to the basilisk:\n\n“What though Cimmerian anarchs dare blaspheme Freedom and thee? a new Actæon’s error Shall theirs have been,—devoured by their own hounds! Be thou like the imperial basilisk, Killing thy foe with unapparent wounds! Gaze on oppression, till at that dread risk, Aghast she pass from the earth’s disk. Fear not, but gaze,—for freemen mightier grow, And slaves more feeble, gazing on their foe.”\n\n# THE UNICORN\n\nPliny, the Roman naturalist, out of whose account of the unicorn most of the modern unicorns have been described and figured, records it as “a very ferocious beast, similar in the rest of its body to a horse, with the head of a deer, the feet of an elephant, the tail of a boar, a deep, bellowing voice, and a single black horn, two cubits in length, standing out in the middle of its forehead.” He adds that “it cannot be taken alive;” and some such excuse may have been necessary in those days for not producing the living animal upon the arena of the amphitheatre.\n\nThe unicorn seems to have been a sad puzzle to the hunters, who hardly knew how to come at so valuable a piece of game. Some described the horn as movable at the will of the animal, a kind of small sword, in short, with which no hunter who was not exceedingly cunning in fence could have a chance. Others maintained that all the animal’s strength lay in its horn, and that when hard pressed in pursuit, it would throw itself from the pinnacle of the highest rocks horn foremost, so as to pitch upon it, and then quietly march off not a whit the worse for its fall.\n\nBut it seems they found out how to circumvent the poor unicorn at last. They discovered that it was a great lover of purity and innocence, so they took the field with a young virgin, who was placed in the unsuspecting admirer’s way. When the unicorn spied her, he approached with all reverence, couched beside her, and laying his head in her lap, fell asleep. The treacherous virgin then gave a signal, and the hunters made in and captured the simple beast.\n\nModern zoölogists, disgusted as they well may be with such fables as these, disbelieve generally the existence of the unicorn. Yet there are animals bearing on their heads a bony protuberance more or less like a horn, which may have given rise to the story. The rhinoceros horn, as it is called, is such a protuberance, though it does not exceed a few inches in height, and is far from agreeing with the descriptions of the horn of the unicorn. The nearest approach to a horn in the middle of the forehead is exhibited in the bony protuberance on the forehead of the giraffe; but this also is short and blunt, and is not the only horn of the animal, but a third horn, standing in front of the two others. In fine, though it would be presumptuous to deny the existence of a one-horned quadruped other than the rhinoceros, it may be safely stated that the insertion of a long and solid horn in the living forehead of a horse-like or deer-like animal is as near an impossibility as anything can be.\n\n# THE SALAMANDER\n\nThe following is from the “Life of Benvenuto Cellini,” an Italian artist of the sixteenth century, written by himself: “When I was about five years of age, my father, happening to be in a little room in which they had been washing, and where there was a good fire of oak burning, looked into the flames and saw a little animal resembling a lizard, which could live in the hottest part of that element. Instantly perceiving what it was, he called for my sister and me, and after he had shown us the creature, he gave me a box on the ear. I fell a-crying, while he, soothing me with caresses, spoke these words: ‘My dear child, I do not give you that blow for any fault you have committed, but that you may recollect that the little creature you see in the fire is a salamander; such a one as never was beheld before to my knowledge.’ So saying he embraced me, and gave me some money.”\n\nIt seems unreasonable to doubt a story of which Signor Cellini was both an eye and ear witness. Add to which the authority of numerous sage philosophers, at the head of whom are Aristotle and Pliny, affirms this power of the salamander. According to them, the animal not only resists fire, but extinguishes it, and when he sees the flame charges it as an enemy which he well knows how to vanquish.\n\nThat the skin of an animal which could resist the action of fire should be considered proof against that element is not to be wondered at. We accordingly find that a cloth made of the skin of salamanders (for there really is such an animal, a kind of lizard) was incombustible, and very valuable for wrapping up such articles as were too precious to be intrusted to any other envelopes. These fire-proof cloths were actually produced, said to be made of salamander’s wool, though the knowing ones detected that the substance of which they were composed was asbestos, a mineral, which is in fine filaments capable of being woven into a flexible cloth.\n\nThe foundation of the above fables is supposed to be the fact that the salamander really does secrete from the pores of his body a milky juice, which when he is irritated is produced in considerable quantity, and would doubtless, for a few moments, defend the body from fire. Then it is a hibernating animal, and in winter retires to some hollow tree or other cavity, where it coils itself up and remains in a torpid state till the spring again calls it forth. It may therefore sometimes be carried with the fuel to the fire, and wake up only time enough to put forth all its faculties for its defence. Its viscous juice would do good service, and all who profess to have seen it, acknowledge that it got out of the fire as fast as its legs could carry it; indeed, too fast for them ever to make prize of one, except in one instance, and in that one the animal’s feet and some parts of its body were badly burned.\n\nDr. Young, in the “Night Thoughts,” with more quaintness than good taste, compares the sceptic who can remain unmoved in the contemplation of the starry heavens to a salamander unwarmed in the fire:\n\n“An undevout astronomer is mad! . . . . . . . “O, what a genius must inform the skies! And is Lorenzo’s salamander-heart Cold and untouched amid these sacred fires?”\n\n————\n\n# CHAPTER XXXVII\n\nEASTERN MYTHOLOGY—ZOROASTER—HINDU MYTHOLOGY—CASTES—BUDDHA—GRAND LAMA\nOUR knowledge of the religion of the ancient Persians is principally derived from the Zendavesta, or sacred books of that people. Zoroaster was the founder of their religion, or rather the reformer of the religion which preceded him. The time when he lived is doubtful, but it is certain that his system became the dominant religion of Western Asia from the time of Cyrus (550 B.C.) to the conquest of Persia by Alexander the Great. Under the Macedonian monarchy the doctrines of Zoroaster appear to have been considerably corrupted by the introduction of foreign opinions, but they afterwards recovered their ascendency.\n\nZoroaster taught the existence of a supreme being, who created two other mighty beings and imparted to them as much of his own nature as seemed good to him. Of these, Ormuzd (called by the Greeks Oromasdes) remained faithful to his creator, and was regarded as the source of all good, while Ahriman (Arimanes) rebelled, and became the author of all evil upon the earth. Ormuzd created man and supplied him with all the materials of happiness; but Ahriman marred this happiness by introducing evil into the world, and creating savage beasts and poisonous reptiles and plants. In consequence of this, evil and good are now mingled together in every part of the world, and the followers of good and evil—the adherents of Ormuzd and Ahriman—carry on incessant war. But this state of things will not last forever. The time will come when the adherents of Ormuzd shall everywhere be victorious, and Ahriman and his followers be consigned to darkness forever.\n\nThe religious rites of the ancient Persians were exceedingly simple. They used neither temples, altars, nor statues, and performed their sacrifices on the tops of mountains. They adored fire, light, and the sun as emblems of Ormuzd, the source of all light and purity, but did not regard them as independent deities. The religious rites and ceremonies were regulated by the priests, who were called Magi. The learning of the Magi was connected with astrology and enchantment, in which they were so celebrated that their name was applied to all orders of magicians and enchanters.\n\nWordsworth thus alludes to the worship of the Persians:\n\n“. . . the Persian,—zealous to reject Altar and Image, and the inclusive walls And roofs of temples built by human hands,— The loftiest heights ascending, from their tops, With myrtle-wreathed Tiara on his brows, Presented sacrifice to Moon and Stars, And to the Winds and mother Elements, And the whole circle of the Heavens, for him A sensitive existence and a God.” —Excursion, Book IV.\n\nIn “Childe Harold” Byron speaks thus of the Persian worship:\n\n“Not vainly did the early Persian make His altar the high places and the peak Of earth-o’er-gazing mountains, and thus take A fit and unwalled temple, there to seek The Spirit, in whose honor shrines are weak, Upreared of human hands. Come and compare Columns and idol-dwellings, Goth or Greek, With Nature’s realms of worship, earth and air, Nor fix on fond abodes to circumscribe thy prayer.” III., 91.\n\nThe religion of Zoroaster continued to flourish even after the introduction of Christianity, and in the third century was the dominant faith of the East, till the rise of the Mahometan power and the conquest of Persia by the Arabs in the seventh century, who compelled the greater number of the Persians to renounce their ancient faith. Those who refused to abandon the religion of their ancestors fled to the deserts of Kerman and to Hindustan, where they still exist under the name of Parsees, a name derived from Pars, the ancient name of Persia. The Arabs call them Guebers, from an Arabic word signifying unbelievers. At Bombay the Parsees are at this day a very active, intelligent, and wealthy class. For purity of life, honesty, and conciliatory manners, they are favorably distinguished. They have numerous temples to Fire, which they adore as the symbol of the divinity.\n\nThe Persian religion makes the subject of the finest tale in Moore’s “Lalla Rookh,” the “Fire Worshippers.” The Gueber chief says,\n\n“Yes! I am of that impious race, Those slaves of Fire, that morn and even Hail their creator’s dwelling-place Among the living lights of heaven; Yes! I am of that outcast crew To Iran and to vengeance true, Who curse the hour your Arabs came To desecrate our shrines of flame, And swear before God’s burning eye, To break our country’s chains or die.”\n\n# HINDU MYTHOLOGY\n\nThe religion of the Hindus is professedly founded on the Vedas. To these books of their scripture they attach the greatest sanctity, and state that Brahma himself composed them at the creation. But the present arrangement of the Vedas is attributed to the sage Vyasa, about five thousand years ago.\n\nThe Vedas undoubtedly teach the belief of one supreme God. The name of this deity is Brahma. His attributes are represented by the three personified powers of creation, preservation, and destruction, which under the respective names of Brahma, Vishnu, and Siva form the Trimurti or triad of principal Hindu gods. Of the inferior gods the most important are: 1. Indra, the god of heaven, of thunder, lightning, storm, and rain; 2. Agni, the god of fire; 3. Yama, the god of the infernal regions; 4. Surya, the god of the sun.\n\nBrahma is the creator of the universe, and the source from which all the individual deities have sprung, and into which all will ultimately be absorbed. “As milk changes to curd, and water to ice, so is Brahma variously transformed and diversified, without aid of exterior means of any sort.” The human soul, according to the Vedas, is a portion of the supreme ruler, as a spark is of the fire.\n\nVishnu occupies the second place in the triad of the Hindus, and is the personification of the preserving principle. To protect the world in various epochs of danger, Vishnu descended to the earth in different incarnations, or bodily forms, which descents are called Avatars. They are very numerous, but ten are more particularly specified. The first Avatar was as Matsya, the Fish, under which form Vishnu preserved Manu, the ancestor of the human race, during a universal deluge. The second Avatar was in the form of a Tortoise, which form he assumed to support the earth when the gods were churning the sea for the beverage of immortality, Amrita.\n\nWe may omit the other Avatars, which were of the same general character, that is, interpositions to protect the right or to punish wrong-doers, and come to the ninth, which is the most celebrated of the Avatars of Vishnu, in which he appeared in the human form of Krishna, an invincible warrior, who by his exploits relieved the earth from the tyrants who oppressed it.\n\nBuddha is by the followers of the Brahmanical religion regarded as a delusive incarnation of Vishnu, assumed by him in order to induce the Asuras, opponents of the gods, to abandon the sacred ordinances of the Vedas, by which means they lost their strength and supremacy.\n\nKalki is the name of the tenth Avatar, in which Vishnu will appear at the end of the present age of the world to destroy all vice and wickedness, and to restore mankind to virtue and purity.\n\nSiva is the third person of the Hindu triad. He is the personification of the destroying principle. Though the third name, he is, in respect to the number of his worshippers and the extension of his worship, before either of the others. In the Puranas (the scriptures of the modern Hindu religion) no allusion is made to the original power of this god as a destroyer; that power not being to be called into exercise till after the expiration of twelve millions of years, or when the universe will come to an end; and Mahadeva (another name for Siva) is rather the representative of regeneration than of destruction.\n\nThe worshippers of Vishnu and Siva form two sects, each of which proclaims the superiority of its favorite deity, denying the claims of the other, and Brahma, the creator, having finished his work, seems to be regarded as no longer active, and has now only one temple in India, while Mahadeva and Vishnu have many. The worshippers of Vishnu are generally distinguished by a greater tenderness for life, and consequent abstinence from animal food, and a worship less cruel than that of the followers of Siva.\n\nWhether the worshippers of Juggernaut are to be reckoned among the followers of Vishnu or Siva, our authorities differ. The temple stands near the shore, about three hundred miles south-west of Calcutta. The idol is a carved block of wood, with a hideous face, painted black, and a distended blood-red mouth. On festival days the throne of the image is placed on a tower sixty feet high, moving on wheels. Six long ropes are attached to the tower, by which the people draw it along. The priests and their attendants stand round the throne on the tower, and occasionally turn to the worshippers with songs and gestures. While the tower moves along numbers of the devout worshippers throw themselves on the ground, in order to be crushed by the wheels, and the multitude shout in approbation of the act, as a pleasing sacrifice to the idol. Every year, particularly at two great festivals in March and July, pilgrims flock in crowds to the temple. Not less than seventy or eighty thousand people are said to visit the place on these occasions, when all castes eat together.\n\nThe division of the Hindus into classes or castes, with fixed occupations, existed from the earliest times. It is supposed by some to have been founded upon conquest, the first three castes being composed of a foreign race, who subdued the natives of the country and reduced them to an inferior caste. Others trace it to the fondness of perpetuating, by descent from father to son, certain offices or occupations.\n\nThe Hindu tradition gives the following account of the origin of the various castes: At the creation Brahma resolved to give the earth inhabitants who should be direct emanations from his own body. Accordingly from his mouth came forth the eldest born, Brahma (the priest), to whom he confided the four Vedas; from his right arm issued Shatriya (the warrior), and from his left, the warrior’s wife. His thighs produced Vaissyas, male and female (agriculturists and traders), and lastly from his feet sprang Sudras (mechanics and laborers).\n\nThe four sons of Brahma, so significantly brought into the world, became the fathers of the human race, and heads of their respective castes. They were commanded to regard the four Vedas as containing all the rules of their faith, and all that was necessary to guide them in their religious ceremonies. They were also commanded to take rank in the order of their birth, the Brahmans uppermost, as having sprung from the head of Brahma.\n\nA strong line of demarcation is drawn between the first three castes and the Sudras. The former are allowed to receive instruction from the Vedas, which is not permitted to the Sudras. The Brahmans possess the privilege of teaching the Vedas, and were in former times in exclusive possession of all knowledge. Though the sovereign of the country was chosen from the Shatriya class, also called Rajputs, the Brahmans possessed the real power, and were the royal counsellors, the judges and magistrates of the country; their persons and property were inviolable; and though they committed the greatest crimes, they could only be banished from the kingdom. They were to be treated by sovereigns with the greatest respect, for “a Brahman, whether learned or ignorant, is a powerful divinity.”\n\nWhen the Brahman arrives at years of maturity it becomes his duty to marry. He ought to be supported by the contributions of the rich, and not to be obliged to gain his subsistence by any laborious or productive occupation. But as all the Brahmans could not be maintained by the working classes of the community, it was found necessary to allow them to engage in productive employments.\n\nWe need say little of the two intermediate classes, whose rank and privileges may be readily inferred from their occupations. The Sudras or fourth class are bound to servile attendance on the higher classes, especially the Brahmans, but they may follow mechanical occupations and practical arts, as painting and writing, or become traders or husbandmen. Consequently they sometimes grow rich, and it will also sometimes happen that Brahmans become poor. That fact works its usual consequence, and rich Sudras sometimes employ poor Brahmans in menial occupations.\n\nThere is another class lower even than the Sudras, for it is not one of the original pure classes, but springs from an unauthorized union of individuals of different castes. These are the Pariahs, who are employed in the lowest services and treated with the utmost severity. They are compelled to do what no one else can do without pollution. They are not only considered unclean themselves, but they render unclean everything they touch. They are deprived of all civil rights, and stigmatized by particular laws regulating their mode of life, their houses, and their furniture. They are not allowed to visit the pagodas or temples of the other castes, but have their own pagodas and religious exercises. They are not suffered to enter the houses of the other castes; if it is done incautiously or from necessity, the place must be purified by religious ceremonies. They must not appear at public markets, and are confined to the use of particular wells, which they are obliged to surround with bones of animals, to warn others against using them. They dwell in miserable hovels, distant from cities and villages, and are under no restrictions in regard to food, which last is not a privilege, but a mark of ignominy, as if they were so degraded that nothing could pollute them. The three higher castes are prohibited entirely the use of flesh. The fourth is allowed to use all kinds except beef, but only the lowest caste is allowed every kind of food without restriction.\n\nBuddha, whom the Vedas represent as a delusive incarnation of Vishnu, is said by his followers to have been a mortal sage, whose name was Gautama, called also by the complimentary epithets of Sakyasinha, the Lion, and Buddha, the Sage.\n\nBy a comparison of the various epochs assigned to his birth, it is inferred that he lived about one thousand years before Christ.\n\nHe was the son of a king; and when in conformity to the usage of the country he was, a few days after his birth, presented before the altar of a deity, the image is said to have inclined its head as a presage of the future greatness of the new-born prophet. The child soon developed faculties of the first order, and became equally distinguished by the uncommon beauty of his person. No sooner had he grown to years of maturity than he began to reflect deeply on the depravity and misery of mankind, and he conceived the idea of retiring from society and devoting himself to meditation. His father in vain opposed this design. Buddha escaped the vigilance of his guards, and having found a secure retreat, lived for six years undisturbed in his devout contemplations. At the expiration of that period he came forward at Benares as a religious teacher. At first some who heard him doubted of the soundness of his mind; but his doctrines soon gained credit, and were propagated so rapidly that Buddha himself lived to see them spread all over India. He died at the age of eighty years.\n\nThe Buddhists reject entirely the authority of the Vedas, and the religious observances prescribed in them and kept by the Hindus. They also reject the distinction of castes, and prohibit all bloody sacrifices, and allow animal food. Their priests are chosen from all classes; they are expected to procure their maintenance by perambulation and begging, and among other things it is their duty to endeavor to turn to some use things thrown aside as useless by others, and to discover the medicinal power of plants. But in Ceylon three orders of priests are recognized; those of the highest order are usually men of high birth and learning, and are supported at the principal temples, most of which have been richly endowed by the former monarchs of the country.\n\nFor several centuries after the appearance of Buddha, his sect seems to have been tolerated by the Brahmans, and Buddhism appears to have penetrated the peninsula of Hindustan in every direction, and to have been carried to Ceylon, and to the eastern peninsula. But afterwards it had to endure in India a long-continued persecution, which ultimately had the effect of entirely abolishing it in the country where it had originated, but to scatter it widely over adjacent countries. Buddhism appears to have been introduced into China about the year 65 of our era. From China it was subsequently extended to Corea, Japan, and Java.\n\n# THE GRAND LAMA\n\nIt is a doctrine alike of the Brahminical Hindus and of the Buddhist sect that the confinement of the human soul, an emanation of the divine spirit, in a human body, is a state of misery, and the consequence of frailties and sins committed during former existences. But they hold that some few individuals have appeared on this earth from time to time, not under the necessity of terrestrial existence, but who voluntarily descended to the earth to promote the welfare of mankind. These individuals have gradually assumed the character of reappearances of Buddha himself, in which capacity the line is continued till the present day, in the several Lamas of Thibet, China, and other countries where Buddhism prevails. In consequence of the victories of Gengis Khan and his successors, the Lama residing in Thibet was raised to the dignity of chief pontiff of the sect. A separate province was assigned to him as his own territory, and besides his spiritual dignity he became to a limited extent a temporal monarch. He is styled the Dalai Lama.\n\nThe first Christian missionaries who proceeded to Thibet were surprised to find there in the heart of Asia a pontifical court and several other ecclesiastical institutions resembling those of the Roman Catholic church. They found convents for priests and nuns; also processions and forms of religious worship, attended with much pomp and splendor; and many were induced by these similarities to consider Lamaism as a sort of degenerated Christianity. It is not improbable that the Lamas derived some of these practices from the Nestorian Christians, who were settled in Tartary when Buddhism was introduced into Thibet.\n\n# PRESTER JOHN\n\nAn early account, communicated probably by travelling merchants, of a Lama or spiritual chief among the Tartars, seems to have occasioned in Europe the report of a Presbyter or Prester John, a Christian pontiff resident in Upper Asia. The Pope sent a mission in search of him, as did also Louis IX. of France, some years later, but both missions were unsuccessful, though the small communities of Nestorian Christians, which they did find, served to keep up the belief in Europe that such a personage did exist somewhere in the East. At last in the fifteenth century, a Portuguese traveller, Pedro Covilham, happening to hear that there was a Christian prince in the country of the Abessines (Abyssinia), not far from the Red Sea, concluded that this must be the true Prester John. He accordingly went thither, and penetrated to the court of the king, whom he calls Negus. Milton alludes to him in “Paradise Lost,” Book XI., where, describing Adam’s vision of his descendants in their various nations and cities, scattered over the face of the earth, he says,—\n\n“. . . Nor did his eyes not ken Th’ empire of Negus, to his utmost port, Ercoco, and the less maritime kings, Mombaza and Quiloa and Melind.”\n\n————\n\n# NORTHERN MYTHOLOGY\n\nTHE stories which have engaged our attention thus far relate to the mythology of southern regions. But there is another branch of ancient superstitions which ought not to be entirely overlooked, especially as it belongs to the nations from which we, through our English ancestors, derive our origin. It is that of the northern nations, called Scandinavians, who inhabited the countries now known as Sweden, Denmark, Norway, and Iceland. These mythological records are contained in two collections called the Eddas, of which the oldest is in poetry and dates back to the year 1056, the more modern or prose Edda being of the date of 1640.\n\nAccording to the Eddas there was once no heaven above nor earth beneath, but only a bottomless deep, and a world of mist in which flowed a fountain. Twelve rivers issued from this fountain, and when they had flowed far from their source, they froze into ice, and one layer accumulating over another, the great deep was filled up.\n\nSouthward from the world of mist was the world of light. From this flowed a warm wind upon the ice and melted it. The vapors rose in the air and formed clouds, from which sprang Ymir, the Frost giant and his progeny, and the cow Audhumbla, whose milk afforded nourishment and food to the giant. The cow got nourishment by licking the hoar frost and salt from the ice. While she was one day licking the salt stones there appeared at first the hair of a man, on the second day the whole head, and on the third the entire form endowed with beauty, agility, and power. This new being was a god, from whom and his wife, a daughter of the giant race, sprang the three brothers Odin, Vili, and Ve. They slew the giant Ymir, and out of his body formed the earth, of his blood the seas, of his bones the mountains, of his hair the trees, of his skull the heavens, and of his brain clouds, charged with hail and snow. Of Ymir’s eyebrows the gods formed Midgard (mid earth), destined to become the abode of man.\n\nOdin then regulated the periods of day and night and the seasons by placing in the heavens the sun and moon and appointing to them their respective courses. As soon as the sun began to shed its rays upon the earth, it caused the vegetable world to bud and sprout. Shortly after the gods had created the world they walked by the side of the sea, pleased with their new work, but found that it was still incomplete, for it was without human beings. They therefore took an ash tree and made a man out of it, and they made a woman out of an elder, and called the man Aske and the woman Embla. Odin then gave them life and soul, Vili reason and motion, and Ve bestowed upon them the senses, expressive features, and speech. Midgard was then given them as their residence, and they became the progenitors of the human race.\n\nThe mighty ash tree Ygdrasill was supposed to support the whole universe. It sprang from the body of Ymir, and had three immense roots, extending one into Asgard (the dwelling of the gods), the other into Jotunheim (the abode of the giants), and the third to Niffleheim (the regions of darkness and cold). By the side of each of these roots is a spring, from which it is watered. The root that extends into Asgard is carefully tended by the three Norns, goddesses, who are regarded as the dispensers of fate. They are Urdur (the past), Verdandi (the present), Skuld (the future). The spring at the Jotunheim side is Ymir’s well, in which wisdom and wit lie hidden, but that of Niffleheim feeds the adder Nidhogge (darkness), which perpetually gnaws at the root. Four harts run across the branches of the tree and bite the buds; they represent the four winds. Under the tree lies Ymir, and when he tries to shake off its weight the earth quakes.\n\nAsgard is the name of the abode of the gods, access to which is only gained by crossing the bridge Bifrost (the rainbow). Asgard consists of golden and silver palaces, the dwellings of the gods, but the most beautiful of these is Valhalla, the residence of Odin. When seated on his throne he overlooks all heaven and earth. Upon his shoulders are the ravens Hugin and Munin, who fly every day over the whole world, and on their return report to him all they have seen and heard. At his feet lie his two wolves, Geri and Freki, to whom Odin gives all the meat that is set before him, for he himself stands in no need of food. Mead is for him both food and drink. He invented the Runic characters, and it is the business of the Norns to engrave the runes of fate upon a metal shield. From Odin’s name, spelt Woden, as it sometimes is, came Wednesday, the name of the fourth day of the week.\n\nOdin is frequently called Alfadur (All-father), but this name is sometimes used in a way that shows that the Scandinavians had an idea of a deity superior to Odin, uncreated and eternal.\n\n# OF THE JOYS OF VALHALLA\n\nValhalla is the great hall of Odin, wherein he feasts with his chosen heroes, all those who have fallen bravely in battle, for all who die a peaceful death are excluded. The flesh of the boar Schrimnir is served up to them, and is abundant for all. For although this boar is cooked every morning, he becomes whole again every night. For drink the heroes are supplied abundantly with mead from the she-goat Heidrum. When the heroes are not feasting they amuse themselves with fighting. Every day they ride out into the court or field and fight until they cut each other in pieces. This is their pastime; but when meal time comes they recover from their wounds and return to feast in Valhalla.\n\n# THE VALKYRIE\n\nThe Valkyrie are warlike virgins, mounted upon horses and armed with helmets and spears. Odin, who is desirous to collect a great many heroes in Valhalla to be able to meet the giants in a day when the final contest must come, sends them down to every battlefield to make choice of those who shall be slain. The Valkyrie are his messengers, and their name means “Choosers of the slain.” When they ride forth on their errand, their armor sheds a strange flickering light, which flashes up over the northern skies, making what men call the “Aurora Borealis,” or “Northern Lights.”[37]\n\n# OF THOR AND THE OTHER GODS\n\nThor, the thunderer, Odin’s eldest son, is the strongest of gods and men, and possesses three very precious things. The first is a hammer, which both the Frost and the Mountain giants know to their cost, when they see it hurled against them in the air, for it has split many a skull of their fathers and kindred. When thrown, it returns to his hand of its own accord. The second rare thing he possesses is called the belt of strength. When he girds it about him his divine might is doubled. The third, also very precious, is his iron gloves, which he puts on whenever he would use his mallet efficiently. From Thor’s name is derived our word Thursday.\n\nFrey is one of the most celebrated of the gods. He presides over rain and sunshine and all the fruits of the earth. His sister Freya is the most propitious of the goddesses. She loves music, spring, and flowers, and is particularly fond of the Elves (fairies). She is very fond of love ditties, and all lovers would do well to invoke her.\n\nBragi is the god of poetry, and his song records the deeds of warriors. His wife, Iduna, keeps in a box the apples which the gods, when they feel old age approaching, have only to taste of to become young again.\n\nHeimdall is the watchman of the gods, and is therefore placed on the borders of heaven to prevent the giants from forcing their way over the bridge Bifrost (the rainbow). He requires less sleep than a bird, and sees by night as well as by day a hundred miles around him. So acute is his ear that no sound escapes him, for he can even hear the grass grow and the wool on a sheep’s back.\n\n# OF LOKI AND HIS PROGENY\n\nThere is another deity who is described as the calumniator of the gods and the contriver of all fraud and mischief. His name is Loki. He is handsome and well made, but of a very fickle mood and most evil disposition. He is of the giant race, but forced himself into the company of the gods, and seems to take pleasure in bringing them into difficulties, and in extricating them out of the danger by his cunning, wit, and skill. Loki has three children. The first is the wolf Fenris, the second the Midgard serpent, the third Hela (Death). The gods were not ignorant that these monsters were growing up, and that they would one day bring much evil upon gods and men. So Odin deemed it advisable to send one to bring them to him. When they came he threw the serpent into that deep ocean by which the earth is surrounded. But the monster had grown to such an enormous size that holding his tail in his mouth he encircles the whole earth. Hela he cast into Niffleheim, and gave her power over nine worlds or regions, into which she distributes those who are sent to her; that is, all who die of sickness or old age. Her hall is called Elvidner. Hunger is her table, Starvation her knife, Delay her man, Slowness her maid, Precipice her threshold, Care her bed, and Burning Anguish forms the hangings of the apartments. She may easily be recognized, for her body is half flesh color and half blue, and she has a dreadfully stern and forbidding countenance.\n\nThe wolf Fenris gave the gods a great deal of trouble before they succeeded in chaining him. He broke the strongest fetters as if they were made of cobwebs. Finally the gods sent a messenger to the mountain spirits, who made for them the chain called Gleipnir. It is fashioned of six things, viz., the noise made by the footfall of a cat, the beards of women, the roots of stones, the breath of fishes, the nerves (sensibilities) of bears, and the spittle of birds. When finished it was as smooth and soft as a silken string. But when the gods asked the wolf to suffer himself to be bound with this apparently slight ribbon, he suspected their design, fearing that it was made by enchantment. He therefore only consented to be bound with it upon condition that one of the gods put his hand in his (Fenris’s) mouth as a pledge that the band was to be removed again. Tyr (the god of battles) alone had courage enough to do this. But when the wolf found that he could not break his fetters, and that the gods would not release him, he bit off Tyr’s hand, and he has ever since remained one-handed.\n\n# HOW THOR PAID THE MOUNTAIN GIANT HIS WAGES\n\nOnce on a time, when the gods were constructing their abodes and had already finished Midgard and Valhalla, a certain artificer came and offered to build them a residence so well fortified that they should be perfectly safe from the incursions of the Frost giants and the giants of the mountains. But he demanded for his reward the goddess Freya, together with the sun and moon. The gods yielded to his terms, provided he would finish the whole work himself without any one’s assistance, and all within the space of one winter. But if anything remained unfinished on the first day of summer he should forfeit the recompense agreed on. On being told these terms the artificer stipulated that he should be allowed the use of his horse Svadilfari, and this by the advice of Loki was granted to him. He accordingly set to work on the first day of winter, and during the night let his horse draw stone for the building. The enormous size of the stones struck the gods with astonishment, and they saw clearly that the horse did one-half more of the toilsome work than his master. Their bargain, however, had been concluded, and confirmed by solemn oaths, for without these precautions a giant would not have thought himself safe among the gods, especially when Thor should return from an expedition he had then undertaken against the evil demons.\n\nAs the winter drew to a close, the building was far advanced, and the bulwarks were sufficiently high and massive to render the place impregnable. In short, when it wanted but three days to summer, the only part that remained to be finished was the gateway. Then sat the gods on their seats of justice and entered into consultation, inquiring of one another who among them could have advised to give Freya away, or to plunge the heavens in darkness by permitting the giant to carry away the sun and the moon.\n\nThey all agreed that no one but Loki, the author of so many evil deeds, could have given such bad counsel, and that he should be put to a cruel death if he did not contrive some way to prevent the artificer from completing his task and obtaining the stipulated recompense. They proceeded to lay hands on Loki, who in his fright promised upon oath that, let it cost him what it would, he would so manage matters that the man should lose his reward. That very night when the man went with Svadilfari for building stone, a mare suddenly ran out of a forest and began to neigh. The horse thereat broke loose and ran after the mare into the forest, which obliged the man also to run after his horse, and thus between one and another the whole night was lost, so that at dawn the work had not made the usual progress. The man, seeing that he must fail of completing his task, resumed his own gigantic stature, and the gods now clearly perceived that it was in reality a mountain giant who had come amongst them. Feeling no longer bound by their oaths, they called on Thor, who immediately ran to their assistance, and lifting up his mallet, paid the workman his wages, not with the sun and moon, and not even by sending him back to Jotunheim, for with the first blow he shattered the giant’s skull to pieces and hurled him headlong into Niffleheim.\n\n# THE RECOVERY OF THE HAMMER\n\nOnce upon a time it happened that Thor’s hammer fell into the possession of the giant Thrym, who buried it eight fathoms deep under the rocks of Jotunheim. Thor sent Loki to negotiate with Thrym, but he could only prevail so far as to get the giant’s promise to restore the weapon if Freya would consent to be his bride. Loki returned and reported the result of his mission, but the goddess of love was quite horrified at the idea of bestowing her charms on the king of the Frost giants. In this emergency Loki persuaded Thor to dress himself in Freya’s clothes and accompany him to Jotunheim. Thrym received his veiled bride with due courtesy, but was greatly surprised at seeing her eat for her supper eight salmons and a full grown ox, besides other delicacies, washing the whole down with three tuns of mead. Loki, however, assured him that she had not tasted anything for eight long nights, so great was her desire to see her lover, the renowned ruler of Jotunheim. Thrym had at length the curiosity to peep under his bride’s veil, but started back in affright and demanded why Freya’s eyeballs glistened with fire. Loki repeated the same excuse and the giant was satisfied. He ordered the hammer to be brought in and laid on the maiden’s lap. Thereupon Thor threw off his disguise, grasped his redoubted weapon, and slaughtered Thrym and all his followers.\n\nFrey also possessed a wonderful weapon, a sword which would of itself spread a field with carnage whenever the owner desired it. Frey parted with this sword, but was less fortunate than Thor and never recovered it. It happened in this way: Frey once mounted Odin’s throne, from whence one can see over the whole universe, and looking round saw far off in the giant’s kingdom a beautiful maid, at the sight of whom he was struck with sudden sadness, insomuch that from that moment he could neither sleep, nor drink, nor speak. At last Skirnir, his messenger, drew his secret from him, and undertook to get him the maiden for his bride, if he would give him his sword as a reward. Frey consented and gave him the sword, and Skirnir set off on his journey and obtained the maiden’s promise that within nine nights she would come to a certain place and there wed Frey. Skirnir having reported the success of his errand, Frey exclaimed:\n\n“Long is one night, Long are two nights, But how shall I hold out three? Shorter hath seemed A month to me oft Than of this longing time the half.”\n\nSo Frey obtained Gerda, the most beautiful of all women, for his wife, but he lost his sword.\n\nThis story, entitled “Skirnir For,” and the one immediately preceding it, “Thrym’s Quida,” will be found poetically told in Longfellow’s “Poets and Poetry of Europe.”\n\n————\n\n# THOR’S VISIT TO JOTUNHEIM, THE GIANT’S COUNTRY\n\nONE day the god Thor, with his servant Thialfi, and accompanied by Loki, set out on a journey to the giant’s country. Thialfi was of all men the swiftest of foot. He bore Thor’s wallet, containing their provisions. When night came on they found themselves in an immense forest, and searched on all sides for a place where they might pass the night, and at last came to a very large hall, with an entrance that took the whole breadth of one end of the building. Here they lay down to sleep, but towards midnight were alarmed by an earthquake which shook the whole edifice. Thor, rising up, called on his companions to seek with him a place of safety. On the right they found an adjoining chamber, into which the others entered, but Thor remained at the doorway with his mallet in his hand, prepared to defend himself, whatever might happen. A terrible groaning was heard during the night, and at dawn of day Thor went out and found lying near him a huge giant, who slept and snored in the way that had alarmed them so. It is said that for once Thor was afraid to use his mallet, and as the giant soon waked up, Thor contented himself with simply asking his name.\n\n“My name is Skrymir,” said the giant, “but I need not ask thy name, for I know that thou art the god Thor. But what has become of my glove?” Thor then perceived that what they had taken overnight for a hall was the giant’s glove, and the chamber where his two companions had sought refuge was the thumb. Skrymir then proposed that they should travel in company, and Thor consenting, they sat down to eat their breakfast, and when they had done, Skrymir packed all the provisions into one wallet, threw it over his shoulder, and strode on before them, taking such tremendous strides that they were hard put to it to keep up with him. So they travelled the whole day, and at dusk Skrymir chose a place for them to pass the night in under a large oak tree. Skrymir then told them he would lie down to sleep. “But take ye the wallet,” he added, “and prepare your supper.”\n\nSkrymir soon fell asleep and began to snore strongly; but when Thor tried to open the wallet, he found the giant had tied it up so tight he could not untie a single knot. At last Thor became wroth, and grasping his mallet with both hands he struck a furious blow on the giant’s head. Skrymir, awakening, merely asked whether a leaf had not fallen on his head, and whether they had supped and were ready to go to sleep. Thor answered that they were just going to sleep, and so saying went and laid himself down under another tree. But sleep came not that night to Thor, and when Skrymir snored again so loud that the forest reëchoed with the noise, he arose, and grasping his mallet launched it with such force at the giant’s skull that it made a deep dint in it. Skrymir, awakening, cried out, “What’s the matter? Are there any birds perched on this tree? I felt some moss from the branches fall on my head. How fares it with thee, Thor?” But Thor went away hastily, saying that he had just then awoke, and that as it was only midnight, there was still time for sleep. He, however, resolved that if he had an opportunity of striking a third blow, it should settle all matters between them. A little before daybreak he perceived that Skrymir was again fast asleep, and again grasping his mallet, he dashed it with such violence that it forced its way into the giant’s skull up to the handle. But Skrymir sat up, and stroking his cheek said, “An acorn fell on my head. What! Art thou awake, Thor? Methinks it is time for us to get up and dress ourselves; but you have not now a long way before you to the city called Utgard. I have heard you whispering to one another that I am not a man of small dimensions; but if you come to Utgard you will see there many men much taller than I. Wherefore, I advise you, when you come there, not to make too much of yourselves, for the followers of Utgard-Loki will not brook the boasting of such little fellows as you are. You must take the road that leads eastward, mine lies northward, so we must part here.”\n\nHereupon he threw his wallet over his shoulders and turned away from them into the forest, and Thor had no wish to stop him or to ask for any more of his company.\n\nThor and his companions proceeded on their way, and towards noon descried a city standing in the middle of a plain. It was so lofty that they were obliged to bend their necks quite back on their shoulders in order to see to the top of it. On arriving they entered the city, and seeing a large palace before them with the door wide open, they went in, and found a number of men of prodigious stature, sitting on benches in the hall. Going further, they came before the king, Utgard-Loki, whom they saluted with great respect. The king, regarding them with a scornful smile, said, “If I do not mistake me, that stripling yonder must be the god Thor.” Then addressing himself to Thor, he said, “Perhaps thou mayst be more than thou appearest to be. What are the feats that thou and thy fellows deem yourselves skilled in, for no one is permitted to remain here who does not, in some feat or other, excel all other men?”\n\n“The feat that I know,” said Loki, “is to eat quicker than any one else, and in this I am ready to give a proof against any one here who may choose to compete with me.”\n\n“That will indeed be a feat,” said Utgard-Loki, “if thou performest what thou promisest, and it shall be tried forthwith.”\n\nHe then ordered one of his men who was sitting at the farther end of the bench, and whose name was Logi, to come forward and try his skill with Loki. A trough filled with meat having been set on the hall floor, Loki placed himself at one end, and Logi at the other, and each of them began to eat as fast as he could, until they met in the middle of the trough. But it was found that Loki had only eaten the flesh, while his adversary had devoured both flesh and bone, and the trough to boot. All the company therefore adjudged that Loki was vanquished.\n\nUtgard-Loki then asked what feat the young man who accompanied Thor could perform. Thialfi answered that he would run a race with any one who might be matched against him. The king observed that skill in running was something to boast of, but if the youth would win the match he must display great agility. He then arose and went with all who were present to a plain where there was good ground for running on, and calling a young man named Hugi, bade him run a match with Thialfi. In the first course Hugi so much outstripped his competitor that he turned back and met him not far from the starting place. Then they ran a second and a third time, but Thialfi met with no better success.\n\nUtgard-Loki then asked Thor in what feats he would choose to give proofs of that prowess for which he was so famous. Thor answered that he would try a drinking-match with any one. Utgard-Loki bade his cup-bearer bring the large horn which his followers were obliged to empty when they had trespassed in any way against the law of the feast. The cupbearer having presented it to Thor, Utgard-Loki said, “Whoever is a good drinker will empty that horn at a single draught, though most men make two of it, but the most puny drinker can do it in three.”\n\nThor looked at the horn, which seemed of no extraordinary size though somewhat long; however, as he was very thirsty, he set it to his lips, and without drawing breath, pulled as long and as deeply as he could, that he might not be obliged to make a second draught of it; but when he set the horn down and looked in, he could scarcely perceive that the liquor was diminished.\n\nAfter taking breath, Thor went to it again with all his might, but when he took the horn from his mouth, it seemed to him that he had drunk rather less than before, although the horn could now be carried without spilling.\n\n“How now, Thor?” said Utgard-Loki; “thou must not spare thyself; if thou meanest to drain the horn at the third draught thou must pull deeply; and I must needs say that thou wilt not be called so mighty a man here as thou art at home if thou showest no greater prowess in other feats than methinks will be shown in this.”\n\nThor, full of wrath, again set the horn to his lips, and did his best to empty it; but on looking in found the liquor was only a little lower, so he resolved to make no further attempt, but gave back the horn to the cupbearer.\n\n“I now see plainly,” said Utgard-Loki, “that thou art not quite so stout as we thought thee: but wilt thou try any other feat, though methinks thou art not likely to bear any prize away with thee hence.”\n\n“What new trial hast thou to propose?” said Thor.\n\n“We have a very trifling game here,” answered Utgard-Loki, “in which we exercise none but children. It consists in merely lifting my cat from the ground; nor should I have dared to mention such a feat to the great Thor if I had not already observed that thou art by no means what we took thee for.”\n\nAs he finished speaking, a large gray cat sprang on the hall floor. Thor put his hand under the cat’s belly and did his utmost to raise him from the floor, but the cat, bending his back, had, notwithstanding all Thor’s efforts, only one of his feet lifted up, seeing which Thor made no further attempt.\n\n“This trial has turned out,” said Utgard-Loki, “just as I imagined it would. The cat is large, but Thor is little in comparison to our men.”\n\n“Little as ye call me,” answered Thor, “let me see who among you will come hither now I am in wrath and wrestle with me.”\n\n“I see no one here,” said Utgard-Loki, looking at the men sitting on the benches, “who would not think it beneath him to wrestle with thee; let somebody, however, call hither that old crone, my nurse Elli, and let Thor wrestle with her if he will. She has thrown to the ground many a man not less strong than this Thor is.”\n\nA toothless old woman then entered the hall, and was told by Utgard-Loki to take hold of Thor. The tale is shortly told. The more Thor tightened his hold on the crone the firmer she stood. At length after a very violent struggle Thor began to lose his footing, and was finally brought down upon one knee. Utgard-Loki then told them to desist, adding that Thor had now no occasion to ask any one else in the hall to wrestle with him, and it was also getting late; so he showed Thor and his companions to their seats, and they passed the night there in good cheer.\n\nThe next morning, at break of day, Thor and his companions dressed themselves and prepared for their departure. Utgard-Loki ordered a table to be set for them, on which there was no lack of victuals or drink. After the repast Utgard-Loki led them to the gate of the city, and on parting asked Thor how he thought his journey had turned out, and whether he had met with any men stronger than himself. Thor told him that he could not deny but that he had brought great shame on himself. “And what grieves me most,” he added, “is that ye will call me a person of little worth.”\n\n“Nay,” said Utgard-Loki, “it behooves me to tell thee the truth, now thou art out of the city, which so long as I live and have my way thou shalt never enter again. And, by my troth, had I known beforehand that thou hadst so much strength in thee, and wouldst have brought me so near to a great mishap, I would not have suffered thee to enter this time. Know then that I have all along deceived thee by my illusions; first in the forest, where I tied up the wallet with iron wire so that thou couldst not untie it. After this thou gavest me three blows with thy mallet; the first, though the least, would have ended my days had it fallen on me, but I slipped aside and thy blows fell on the mountain, where thou wilt find three glens, one of them remarkably deep. These are the dints made by thy mallet. I have made use of similar illusions in the contests you have had with my followers. In the first, Loki, like hunger itself, devoured all that was set before him, but Loki was in reality nothing else than Fire, and therefore consumed not only the meat, but the trough which held it. Hugi, with whom Thialfi contended in running, was Thought, and it was impossible for Thialfi to keep pace with that. When thou in thy turn didst attempt to empty the horn, thou didst perform, by my troth, a deed so marvellous that had I not seen it myself I should never have believed it. For one end of that horn reached the sea, which thou wast not aware of, but when thou comest to the shore thou wilt perceive how much the sea has sunk by thy draughts. Thou didst perform a feat no less wonderful by lifting up the cat, and to tell thee the truth, when we saw that one of his paws was off the floor, we were all of us terror-stricken, for what thou tookest for a cat was in reality the Midgard serpent that encompasseth the earth, and he was so stretched by thee that he was barely long enough to enclose it between his head and tail. Thy wrestling with Elli was also a most astonishing feat, for there was never yet a man, nor ever will be, whom Old Age, for such in fact was Elli, will not sooner or later lay low. But now, as we are going to part, let me tell thee that it will be better for both of us if thou never come near me again, for shouldst thou do so, I shall again defend myself by other illusions, so that thou wilt only lose thy labor and get no fame from the contest with me.”\n\nOn hearing these words Thor in a rage laid hold of his mallet and would have launched it at him, but Utgard-Loki had disappeared, and when Thor would have returned to the city to destroy it, he found nothing around him but a verdant plain.\n\n————\n\n# CHAPTER XL\n\nTHE DEATH OF BALDUR—THE ELVES—RUNIC LETTERS—ICELAND—TEUTONIC MYTHOLOGY—NIBELUNGEN LIED\nBALDUR the Good, having been tormented with terrible dreams indicating that his life was in peril, told them to the assembled gods, who resolved to conjure all things to avert from him the threatened danger. Then Frigga, the wife of Odin, exacted an oath from fire and water, from iron and all other metals, from stones, trees, diseases, beasts, birds, poisons, and creeping things, that none of them would do any harm to Baldur. Odin, not satisfied with all this, and feeling alarmed for the fate of his son, determined to consult the prophetess Angerbode, a giantess, mother of Fenris, Hela, and the Midgard serpent. She was dead, and Odin was forced to seek her in Hela’s dominions. This Descent of Odin forms the subject of Gray’s fine ode beginning,—\n\n“Uprose the king of men with speed And saddled straight his coal-black steed.”\n\nBut the other gods, feeling that what Frigga had done was quite sufficient, amused themselves with using Baldur as a mark, some hurling darts at him, some stones, while others hewed at him with their swords and battle-axes; for do what they would, none of them could harm him. And this became a favorite pastime with them and was regarded as an honor shown to Baldur. But when Loki beheld the scene he was sorely vexed that Baldur was not hurt. Assuming, therefore, the shape of a woman, he went to Fensalir, the mansion of Frigga. That goddess, when she saw the pretended woman, inquired of her if she knew what the gods were doing at their meetings. She replied that they were throwing darts and stones at Baldur, without being able to hurt him. “Ay,” said Frigga, “neither stones, nor sticks, nor anything else can hurt Baldur, for I have exacted an oath from all of them.” “What,” exclaimed the woman, “have all things sworn to spare Baldur?” “All things,” replied Frigga, “except one little shrub that grows on the eastern side of Valhalla, and is called Mistletoe, and which I thought too young and feeble to crave an oath from.”\n\nAs soon as Loki heard this he went away, and resuming his natural shape, cut off the mistletoe, and repaired to the place where the gods were assembled. There he found Hodur standing apart, without partaking of the sports, on account of his blindness, and going up to him, said, “Why dost thou not also throw something at Baldur?”\n\n“Because I am blind,” answered Hodur, “and see not where Baldur is, and have, moreover, nothing to throw.”\n\n“Come, then,” said Loki, “do like the rest, and show honor to Baldur by throwing this twig at him, and I will direct thy arm towards the place where he stands.”\n\nHodur then took the mistletoe, and under the guidance of Loki, darted it at Baldur, who, pierced through and through, fell down lifeless. Surely never was there witnessed, either among gods or men, a more atrocious deed than this. When Baldur fell, the gods were struck speechless with horror, and then they looked at each other, and all were of one mind to lay hands on him who had done the deed, but they were obliged to delay their vengeance out of respect for the sacred place where they were assembled. They gave vent to their grief by loud lamentations. When the gods came to themselves, Frigga asked who among them wished to gain all her love and good will. “For this,” said she, “shall he have who will ride to Hel and offer Hela a ransom if she will let Baldur return to Asgard.” Whereupon Hermod, surnamed the Nimble, the son of Odin, offered to undertake the journey. Odin’s horse, Sleipnir, which has eight legs and can outrun the wind, was then led forth, on which Hermod mounted and galloped away on his mission. For the space of nine days and as many nights he rode through deep glens so dark that he could not discern anything, until he arrived at the river Gyoll, which he passed over on a bridge covered with glittering gold. The maiden who kept the bridge asked him his name and lineage, telling him that the day before five bands of dead persons had ridden over the bridge, and did not shake it as much as he alone. “But,” she added, “thou hast not death’s hue on thee; why then ridest thou here on the way to Hel?”\n\n“I ride to Hel,” answered Hermod, “to seek Baldur. Hast thou perchance seen him pass this way?”\n\nShe replied, “Baldur hath ridden over Gyoll’s bridge, and yonder lieth the way he took to the abodes of death.”\n\nHermod pursued his journey until he came to the barred gates of Hel. Here he alighted, girthed his saddle tighter, and remounting clapped both spurs to his horse, who cleared the gate by a tremendous leap without touching it. Hermod then rode on to the palace, where he found his brother Baldur occupying the most distinguished seat in the hall, and passed the night in his company. The next morning he besought Hela to let Baldur ride home with him, assuring her that nothing but lamentations were to be heard among the gods. Hela answered that it should now be tried whether Baldur was so beloved as he was said to be. “If, therefore,” she added, “all things in the world, both living and lifeless, weep for him, then shall he return to life; but if any one thing speak against him or refuse to weep, he shall be kept in Hel.”\n\nHermod then rode back to Asgard and gave an account of all he had heard and witnessed.\n\nThe gods upon this despatched messengers throughout the world to beg everything to weep in order that Baldur might be delivered from Hel. All things very willingly complied with this request, both men and every other living being, as well as earths, and stones, and trees, and metals, just as we have all seen these things weep when they are brought from a cold place into a hot one. As the messengers were returning, they found an old hag named Thaukt sitting in a cavern, and begged her to weep Baldur out of Hel. But she answered,\n\n“Thaukt will wail With dry tears Baldur’s bale-fire. Let Hela keep her own.”\n\nIt was strongly suspected that this hag was no other than Loki himself, who never ceased to work evil among gods and men. So Baldur was prevented from coming back to Asgard.[38]\n\n# THE FUNERAL OF BALDUR\n\nThe gods took up the dead body and bore it to the seashore where stood Baldur’s ship “Hringham,” which passed for the largest in the world. Baldur’s dead body was put on the funeral pile, on board the ship, and his wife Nanna was so struck with grief at the sight that she broke her heart, and her body was burned on the same pile as her husband’s. There was a vast concourse of various kinds of people at Baldur’s obsequies. First came Odin accompanied by Frigga, the Valkyrie, and his ravens; then Frey in his car drawn by Gullinbursti, the boar; Heimdall rode his horse Gulltopp, and Freya drove in her chariot drawn by cats. There were also a great many Frost giants and giants of the mountain present. Baldur’s horse was led to the pile fully caparisoned and consumed in the same flames with his master.\n\nBut Loki did not escape his deserved punishment. When he saw how angry the gods were, he fled to the mountain, and there built himself a hut with four doors, so that he could see every approaching danger. He invented a net to catch the fishes, such as fishermen have used since his time. But Odin found out his hidingplace and the gods assembled to take him. He, seeing this, changed himself into a salmon, and lay hid among the stones of the brook. But the gods took his net and dragged the brook, and Loki, finding he must be caught, tried to leap over the net; but Thor caught him by the tail and compressed it, so that salmons ever since have had that part remarkably fine and thin. They bound him with chains and suspended a serpent over his head, whose venom falls upon his face drop by drop. His wife Siguna sits by his side and catches the drops as they fall, in a cup; but when she carries it away to empty it, the venom falls upon Loki, which makes him howl with horror, and twist his body about so violently that the whole earth shakes, and this produces what men call earthquakes.\n\n# THE ELVES\n\nThe Edda mentions another class of beings, inferior to the gods, but still possessed of great power; these were called Elves. The white spirits, or Elves of Light, were exceedingly fair, more brilliant than the sun, and clad in garments of a delicate and transparent texture. They loved the light, were kindly disposed to mankind, and generally appeared as fair and lovely children. Their country was called Alfheim, and was the domain of Freyr, the god of the sun, in whose light they were always sporting.\n\nThe Black or Night Elves were a different kind of creatures. Ugly, long-nosed dwarfs, of a dirty brown color, they appeared only at night, for they avoided the sun as their most deadly enemy, because whenever his beams fell upon any of them they changed them immediately into stones. Their language was the echo of solitudes, and their dwelling-places subterranean caves and clefts. They were supposed to have come into existence as maggots produced by the decaying flesh of Ymir’s body, and were afterwards endowed by the gods with a human form and great understanding. They were particularly distinguished for a knowledge of the mysterious powers of nature, and for the runes which they carved and explained. They were the most skilful artificers of all created beings, and worked in metals and in wood. Among their most noted works were Thor’s hammer, and the ship “Skidbladnir,” which they gave to Freyr, and which was so large that it could contain all the deities with their war and household implements, but so skillfully was it wrought that when folded together it could be put into a side pocket.\n\n# RAGNAROK, THE TWILIGHT OF THE GODS\n\nIt was a firm belief of the northern nations that a time would come when all the visible creation, the gods of Valhalla and Niffleheim, the inhabitants of Jotunheim, Alfheim, and Midgard, together with their habitations, would be destroyed. The fearful day of destruction will not, however, be without its forerunners. First will come a triple winter, during which snow will fall from the four corners of the heavens, the frost be very severe, the wind piercing, the weather tempestuous, and the sun impart no gladness. Three such winters will pass away without being tempered by a single summer. Three other similar winters will then follow, during which war and discord will spread over the universe. The earth itself will be frightened and begin to tremble, the sea leave its basin, the heavens tear asunder, and men perish in great numbers, and the eagles of the air feast upon their still quivering bodies. The wolf Fenris will now break his bands, the Midgard serpent rise out of her bed in the sea, and Loki, released from his bonds, will join the enemies of the gods. Amidst the general devastation the sons of Muspelheim will rush forth under their leader Surtur, before and behind whom are flames and burning fire. Onward they ride over Bifrost, the rainbow bridge, which breaks under the horses’ hoofs. But they, disregarding its fall, direct their course to the battlefield called Vigrid. Thither also repair the wolf Fenris, the Midgard serpent, Loki with all the followers of Hela, and the Frost giants.\n\nHeimdall now stands up and sounds the Giallar horn to assemble the gods and heroes for the contest. The gods advance, led on by Odin, who engages the wolf Fenris, but falls a victim to the monster, who is, however, slain by Vidar, Odin’s son. Thor gains great renown by killing the Midgard serpent, but recoils and falls dead, suffocated with the venom which the dying monster vomits over him. Loki and Heimdall meet and fight till they are both slain. The gods and their enemies having fallen in battle, Surtur, who has killed Freyr, darts fire and flames over the world, and the whole universe is burned up. The sun becomes dim, the earth sinks into the ocean, the stars fall from heaven, and time is no more.\n\nAfter this Alfadur (the Almighty) will cause a new heaven and a new earth to arise out of the sea. The new earth filled with abundant supplies will spontaneously produce its fruits without labor or care. Wickedness and misery will no more be known, but the gods and men will live happily together.\n\n# RUNIC LETTERS\n\nOne cannot travel far in Denmark, Norway, or Sweden without meeting with great stones of different forms, engraven with characters called Runic, which appear at first sight very different from all we know. The letters consist almost invariably of straight lines, in the shape of little sticks either singly or put together. Such sticks were in early times used by the northern nations for the purpose of ascertaining future events. The sticks were shaken up, and from the figures that they formed a kind of divination was derived.\n\nThe Runic characters were of various kinds. They were chiefly used for magical purposes. The noxious, or, as they called them, the bitter runes, were employed to bring various evils on their enemies; the favorable averted misfortune. Some were medicinal, others employed to win love, etc. In later times they were frequently used for inscriptions, of which more than a thousand have been found. The language is a dialect of the Gothic, called Norse, still in use in Iceland. The inscriptions may therefore be read with certainty, but hitherto very few have been found which throw the least light on history. They are mostly epitaphs on tombstones.\n\nGray’s ode on the “Descent of Odin” contains an allusion to the use of Runic letters for incantation:\n\n“Facing to the northern clime, Thrice he traced the Runic rhyme; Thrice pronounced, in accents dread, The thrilling verse that wakes the dead, Till from out the hollow ground Slowly breathed a sullen sound.”\n\n# THE SKALDS\n\nThe Skalds were the bards and poets of the nation, a very important class of men in all communities in an early stage of civilization. They are the depositaries of whatever historic lore there is, and it is their office to mingle something of intellectual gratification with the rude feasts of the warriors, by rehearsing, with such accompaniments of poetry and music as their skill can afford, the exploits of their heroes living or dead. The compositions of the Skalds were called Sagas, many of which have come down to us, and contain valuable materials of history, and a faithful picture of the state of society at the time to which they relate.\n\nThe Eddas and Sagas have come to us from Iceland. The following extract from Carlyle’s lectures on “Heroes and Hero Worship” gives an animated account of the region where the strange stories we have been reading had their origin. Let the reader contrast it for a moment with Greece, the parent of classical mythology:\n\n“In that strange island, Iceland,—burst up, the geologists say, by fire from the bottom of the sea, a wild land of barrenness and lava, swallowed many months of every year in black tempests, yet with a wild, gleaming beauty in summer time, towering up there stern and grim in the North Ocean, with its snow yokuls [mountains], roaring geysers [boiling springs], sulphur pools, and horrid volcanic chasms, like the waste, chaotic battlefield of Frost and Fire,—where, of all places, we least looked for literature or written memorials,—the record of these things was written down. On the seaboard of this wild land is a rim of grassy country, where cattle can subsist, and men by means of them and of what the sea yields; and it seems they were poetic men these, men who had deep thoughts in them and uttered musically their thoughts. Much would be lost had Iceland not been burst up from the sea, not been discovered by the Northmen!”\n\n# TEUTONIC MYTHOLOGY\n\nIn the mythology of Germany proper, the name of Odin appears as Wotan; Freya and Frigga are regarded as one and the same divinity, and the gods are in general represented as less warlike in character than those in the Scandinavian myths. As a whole, however, Teutonic mythology runs along almost identical lines with that of the northern nations. The most notable divergence is due to modifications of the legends by reason of the difference in climatic conditions. The more advanced social condition of the Germans is also apparent in their mythology.\n\n# THE NIBELUNGEN LIED\n\nOne of the oldest myths of the Teutonic race is found in the great national epic of the Nibelungen Lied, which dates back to the prehistoric era when Wotan, Frigga, Thor, Loki, and the other gods and goddesses were worshipped in the German forests. The epic is divided into two parts, the first of which tells how Siegfried, the youngest of the kings of the Netherlands, went to Worms, to ask in marriage the hand of Kriemhild, sister of Günther, King of Burgundy. While he was staying with Günther, Siegfried helped the Burgundian king to secure as his wife Brunhild, queen of Issland. The latter had announced publicly that he only should be her husband who could beat her in hurling a spear, throwing a huge stone, and in leaping. Siegfried, who possessed a cloak of invisibility, aided Günther in these three contests, and Brunhild became his wife. In return for these services, Günther gave Siegfried his sister Kriemhild in marriage.\n\nAfter some time had elapsed, Siegfried and Kriemhild went to visit Günther, when the two women fell into a dispute about the relative merits of their husbands. Kriemhild, to exalt Siegfried, boasted that it was to the latter that Günther owed his victories and his wife. Brunhild, in great anger, employed Hagan, liegeman of Günther, to murder Siegfried. In the epic Hagan is described as follows:\n\n“Well-grown and well-compacted was that redoubted guest; Long were his legs and sinewy, and deep and broad his chest; His hair, that once was sable, with gray was dashed of late; Most terrible his visage, and lordly was his gait.” —Nibelungen Lied, stanza 1789.\n\nThis Achilles of German romance stabbed Siegfried between the shoulders, as the unfortunate King of the Netherlands was stooping to drink from a brook during a hunting expedition.\n\nThe second part of the epic relates how, thirteen years later, Kriemhild married Etzel, King of the Huns. After a time, she invited the King of Burgundy, with Hagan and many others, to the court of her husband. A fearful quarrel was stirred up in the banquet hall, which ended in the slaughter of all the Burgundians but Günther and Hagan. These two were taken prisoners and given to Kriemhild, who with her own hand cut off the heads of both. For this bloody act of vengeance Kriemhild was herself slain by Hildebrand, a magician and champion, who in German mythology holds a place to an extent corresponding to that of Nestor in the Greek mythology.\n\n# THE NIBELUNGEN HOARD\n\nThis was a mythical mass of gold and precious stones which Siegfried obtained from the Nibelungs, the people of the north whom he had conquered and whose country he had made tributary to his own kingdom of the Netherlands. Upon his marriage, Siegfried gave the treasure to Kriemhild as her wedding portion. After the murder of Siegfried, Hagan seized it and buried it secretly beneath the Rhine at Lochham, intending to recover it at a future period. The hoard was lost forever when Hagan was killed by Kriemhild. Its wonders are thus set forth in the poem:\n\n“’Twas as much as twelve huge wagons in four whole nights and days Could carry from the mountain down to the salt sea bay; Though to and fro each wagon thrice journeyed every day.\n“It was made up of nothing but precious stones and gold; Were all the world bought from it, and down the value told, Not a mark the less would there be left than erst there was, I ween.” —Nibelungen Lied, XIX.\n\nWhoever possessed the Nibelungen hoard were termed Nibelungers. Thus at one time certain people of Norway were so called. When Siegfried held the treasure he received the title “King of the Nibelungers.”\n\n# WAGNER’S NIBELUNGEN RING\n\nThough Richard Wagner’s music-drama of the Nibelungen Ring bears some resemblance to the ancient German epic, it is a wholly independent composition and was derived from various old songs and sagas, which the dramatist wove into one great harmonious story. The principal source was the Volsunga Saga, while lesser parts were taken from the Elder Edda and the Younger Edda, and others from the Nibelungen Lied, the Ecklenlied, and other Teutonic folklore.\n\nIn the drama there are at first only four distinct races,—the gods, the giants, the dwarfs, and the nymphs. Later, by a special creation, there come the valkyrie and the heroes. The gods are the noblest and highest race, and dwell first in the mountain meadows, later in the palace of Valhalla on the heights. The giants are a great and strong race, but lack wisdom; they hate what is noble, and are enemies of the gods; they dwell in caves near the earth’s surface. The dwarfs, or nibelungs, are black uncouth pigmies, hating the good, hating the gods; they are crafty and cunning, and dwell in the bowels of the earth. The nymphs are pure, innocent creatures of the water. The valkyrie are daughters of the gods, but mingled with a mortal strain; they gather dead heroes from the battle-fields and carry them to Valhalla. The heroes are children of the gods, but also mingled with a mortal strain; they are destined to become at last the highest race of all, and to succeed the gods in the government of the world.\n\nThe principal gods are Wotan, Loki, Donner, and Froh. The chief giants are Fafner and Fasolt, brothers. The chief dwarfs are Alberich and Mime, brothers, and later Hagan, son of Alberich. The chief nymphs are the Rhine-daughters, Flosshilda, Woglinda, and Wellgunda. There are nine Valkyrie, of whom Brunhild is the leading one.\n\nWagner’s story of the Ring may be summarized as follows:\n\nA hoard of gold exists in the depths of the Rhine, guarded by the innocent Rhine-maidens. Alberich, the dwarf, forswears love to gain this gold. He makes it into a magic ring. It gives him all power, and he gathers by it a vast amount of treasures.\n\nMeanwhile Wotan, chief of the gods, has engaged the giants to build for him a noble castle, Valhalla, from whence to rule the world, promising in payment Freya, goddess of youth and love. But the gods find they cannot spare Freya, as they are dependent on her for their immortal youth. Loki, called upon to provide a substitute, tells of Alberich’s magic ring and other treasure. Wotan goes with Loki, and they steal the ring and the golden hoard from Alberich, who curses the ring and lays the curse on all who shall henceforth possess it. The gods give the ring and the treasure to the giants as a substitute for Freya. The curse at once begins. One giant, Fafner, kills his brother to get all, and transforms himself into a dragon to guard his wealth. The gods enter Valhalla over the rainbow bridge. This ends the first part of the drama, called the Rhine-Gold.\n\nThe second part, the Valkyrie, relates how Wotan still covets the ring. He cannot take it himself, for he has given his word to the giants. He stands or falls by his word. So he devises an artifice to get the ring. He will get a hero-race to work for him and recover the ring and the treasures. Siegmund and Sieglinda are twin children of this new race. Sieglinda is carried off as a child and is forced into marriage with Hunding. Siegmund comes, and unknowingly breaks the law of marriage, but wins Nothung, the great sword, and a bride. Brunhild, chief of the Valkyrie, is commissioned by Wotan at the instance of Fricka, goddess of marriage, to slay him for his sin. She disobeys and tries to save him, but Hunding, helped by Wotan, slays him. Sieglinda, however, about to bear the free hero, to be called Siegfried, is saved by Brunhild, and hid in the forest. Brunhild herself is punished by being made a mortal woman. She is left sleeping on the mountains with a wall of fire around her which only a hero can penetrate.\n\nThe drama continues with the story of Siegfried, which opens with a scene in the smithy between Mime the dwarf and Siegfried. Mime is welding a sword, and Siegfried scorns him. Mime tells him something of his mother, Sieglinda, and shows him the broken pieces of his father’s sword. Wotan comes and tells Mime that only one who has no fear can remake the sword. Now Siegfried knows no fear and soon remakes the sword Nothung. Wotan and Alberich come to where the dragon Fafner is guarding the ring. They both long for it, but neither can take it. Soon Mime comes bringing Siegfried with the mighty sword. Fafner comes out, but Siegfried slays him. Happening to touch his lips with the dragon’s blood, he understands the language of the birds. They tell him of the ring. He goes and gets it. Siegfried now has possession of the ring, but it is to bring him nothing of happiness, only evil. It is to curse love and finally bring death. The birds also tell him of Mime’s treachery. He slays Mime. He longs for some one to love. The birds tell him of the slumbering Brunnhilda, whom he finds and marries.\n\nThe Dusk of the Gods portrays at the opening the three norns or fates weaving and measuring the thread of destiny. It is the beginning of the end. The perfect pair, Siegfried and Brunhild, appear in all the glory of their life, splendid ideals of manhood and womanhood. But Siegfried goes out into the world to achieve deeds of prowess. He gives her the Nibelungen ring to keep as a pledge of his love till his return. Meanwhile Alberich also has begotten a son, Hagan, to achieve for him the possession of the ring. He is partly of the Gibichung race, and works through Günther and Gutrune, half-brother and half-sister to him. They beguile Siegfried to them, give him a magic draught which makes him forget Brunhild and fall in love with Gutrune. Under this same spell, he offers to bring Brunhild for wife to Günther. Now is Valhalla full of sorrow and despair. The gods fear the end. Wotan murmurs, “O that she would give back the ring to the Rhine.” But Brunhild will not give it up,—it is now her pledge of love. Siegfried comes, takes the ring, and Brunhild is now brought to the Rhine castle of the Gibichungs, but Siegfried under the spell does not love her. She is to be wedded to Günther. She rises in wrath and denounces Siegfried. But at a hunting banquet Siegfried is given another magic draught, remembers all, and is slain by Hagan by a blow in the back, as he calls on Brunhild’s name in love. Then comes the end. The body of Siegfried is burned on a funeral pyre, a grand funeral march is heard, and Brunhild rides into the flames and sacrifices herself for love’s sake; the ring goes back to the Rhine-daughters; and the old world—of the gods of Valhalla, of passion and sin—is burnt up with flames, for the gods have broken moral law, and coveted power rather than love, gold rather than truth, and therefore must perish. They pass, and a new era, the reign of love and truth, has begun.\n\nThose who wish to study the differences in the legends of the Nibelungen Lied and the Nibelungen Ring, and the way in which Wagner used this ancient material, are referred to Professor W. C. Sawyer’s book on “Teutonic Legends in the Nibelungen Lied and the Nibelungen Ring,” where the matter is treated in full detail. For a very thorough and clear analysis of the Ring as Wagner gives it, with a study of the musical motifs, probably nothing is better for general readers than the volume “The Epic of Sounds,” by Freda Winworth. The more scholarly work of Professor Lavignac is indispensable for the student of Wagner’s dramas. There is much illuminating comment on the sources and materials in “Legends of the Wagner Drama” by J. L. Weston.\n\n————\n\nTHE Druids were the priests or ministers of religion among the ancient Celtic nations in Gaul, Britain, and Germany. Our information respecting them is borrowed from notices in the Greek and Roman writers, compared with the remains of Welsh and Gaelic poetry still extant.\n\nThe Druids combined the functions of the priest, the magistrate, the scholar, and the physician. They stood to the people of the Celtic tribes in a relation closely analogous to that in which the Brahmans of India, the Magi of Persia, and the priests of the Egyptians stood to the people respectively by whom they were revered.\n\nThe Druids taught the existence of one god, to whom they gave a name “Be’ al,” which Celtic antiquaries tell us means “the life of everything,” or “the source of all beings,” and which seems to have affinity with the Phœnician Baal. What renders this affinity more striking is that the Druids as well as the Phœnicians identified this, their supreme deity, with the Sun. Fire was regarded as a symbol of the divinity. The Latin writers assert that the Druids also worshipped numerous inferior gods.\n\nThey used no images to represent the object of their worship, nor did they meet in temples or buildings of any kind for the performance of their sacred rites. A circle of stones (each stone generally of vast size), enclosing an area of from twenty feet to thirty yards in diameter, constituted their sacred place. The most celebrated of these now remaining is Stonehenge, on Salisbury Plain, England.\n\nThese sacred circles were generally situated near some stream, or under the shadow of a grove or widespreading oak. In the centre of the circle stood the Cromlech or altar, which was a large stone, placed in the manner of a table upon other stones set up on end. The Druids had also their high places, which were large stones or piles of stones on the summits of hills. These were called Cairns, and were used in the worship of the deity under the symbol of the sun.\n\nThat the Druids offered sacrifices to their deity there can be no doubt. But there is some uncertainty as to what they offered, and of the ceremonies connected with their religious services we know almost nothing. The classical (Roman) writers affirm that they offered on great occasions human sacrifices; as for success in war or for relief from dangerous diseases. Cæsar has given a detailed account of the manner in which this was done. “They have images of immense size, the limbs of which are framed with twisted twigs and filled with living persons. These being set on fire, those within are encompassed by the flames.” Many attempts have been made by Celtic writers to shake the testimony of the Roman historians to this fact, but without success.\n\nThe Druids observed two festivals in each year. The former took place in the beginning of May, and was called Beltane or “fire of God.” On this occasion a large fire was kindled on some elevated spot, in honor of the sun, whose returning beneficence they thus welcomed after the gloom and desolation of winter. Of this custom a trace remains in the name given to Whitsunday in parts of Scotland to this day. Sir Walter Scott uses the word in the “Boat Song” in the “Lady of the Lake”:\n\n“Ours is no sapling, chance sown by the fountain, Blooming at Beltane in winter to fade;” etc.\n\nThe other great festival of the Druids was called “Samh’in,” or “fire of peace,” and was held on Hallow-eve (first of November), which still retains this designation in the Highlands of Scotland. On this occasion the Druids assembled in solemn conclave, in the most central part of the district, to discharge the judicial functions of their order. All questions, whether public or private, all crimes against person or property, were at this time brought before them for adjudication. With these judicial acts were combined certain superstitious usages, especially the kindling of the sacred fire, from which all the fires in the district, which had been beforehand scrupulously extinguished, might be relighted. This usage of kindling fires on Hallow-eve lingered in the British islands long after the establishment of Christianity.\n\nBesides these two great annual festivals, the Druids were in the habit of observing the full moon, and especially the sixth day of the moon. On the latter they sought the Mistletoe, which grew on their favorite oaks, and to which, as well as to the oak itself, they ascribed a peculiar virtue and sacredness. The discovery of it was an occasion of rejoicing and solemn worship. “They call it,” says Pliny, “by a word in their language, which means ‘heal-all,’ and having made solemn preparation for feasting and sacrifice under the tree, they drive thither two milk-white bulls, whose horns are then for the first time bound. The priest then, robed in white, ascends the tree, and cuts off the mistletoe with a golden sickle. It is caught in a white mantle, after which they proceed to slay the victims, at the same time praying that God would render his gift prosperous to those to whom he had given it.” They drink the water in which it has been infused, and think it a remedy for all diseases. The mistletoe is a parasitic plant, and is not always nor often found on the oak, so that when it is found it is the more precious.\n\nThe Druids were the teachers of morality as well as of religion. Of their ethical teaching a valuable specimen is preserved in the Triads of the Welsh Bards, and from this we may gather that their views of moral rectitude were on the whole just, and that they held and inculcated many very noble and valuable principles of conduct. They were also the men of science and learning of their age and people. Whether they were acquainted with letters or not has been disputed, though the probability is strong that they were, to some extent. But it is certain that they committed nothing of their doctrine, their history, or their poetry to writing. Their teaching was oral, and their literature (if such a word may be used in such a case) was preserved solely by tradition. But the Roman writers admit that “they paid much attention to the order and laws of nature, and investigated and taught to the youth under their charge many things concerning the stars and their motions, the size of the world and the lands, and concerning the might and power of the immortal gods.”\n\nTheir history consisted in traditional tales, in which the heroic deeds of their forefathers were celebrated. These were apparently in verse, and thus constituted part of the poetry as well as the history of the Druids. In the poems of Ossian we have, if not the actual productions of Druidical times, what may be considered faithful representations of the songs of the Bards.\n\nThe Bards were an essential part of the Druidical hierarchy. One author, Pennant, says, “The Bards were supposed to be endowed with powers equal to inspiration. They were the oral historians of all past transactions, public and private. They were also accomplished genealogists,” etc.\n\nPennant gives a minute account of the Eisteddfods or sessions of the Bards and minstrels, which were held in Wales for many centuries, long after the Druidical priesthood in its other departments became extinct. At these meetings none but Bards of merit were suffered to rehearse their pieces, and minstrels of skill to perform. Judges were appointed to decide on their respective abilities, and suitable degrees were conferred. In the earlier period the judges were appointed by the Welsh princes, and after the conquest of Wales, by commission from the kings of England. Yet the tradition is that Edward I., in revenge for the influence of the Bards in animating the resistance of the people to his sway, persecuted them with great cruelty. This tradition has furnished the poet Gray with the subject of his celebrated ode, the “Bard.”\n\nThere are still occasional meetings of the lovers of Welsh poetry and music, held under the ancient name. Among Mrs. Hemans’ poems is one written for an Eisteddfod, or meeting of Welsh Bards, held in London, May 22, 1822. It begins with a description of the ancient meeting, of which the following lines are a part:\n\n“. . . midst the eternal cliffs, whose strength defied The crested Roman in his hour of pride; And where the Druid’s ancient cromlech frowned, And the oaks breathed mysterious murmurs round, There thronged the inspired of yore! on plain or height, In the sun’s face, beneath the eye of light, And baring unto heaven each noble head, Stood in the circle, where none else might tread.”\n\nThe Druidical system was at its height at the time of the Roman invasion under Julius Cæsar. Against the Druids, as their chief enemies, these conquerors of the world directed their unsparing fury. The Druids, harassed at all points on the mainland, retreated to Anglesey and Iona, where for a season they found shelter and continued their now dishonored rites.\n\nThe Druids retained their predominance in Iona and over the adjacent islands and mainland until they were supplanted and their superstitions overturned by the arrival of St. Columba, the apostle of the Highlands, by whom the inhabitants of that district were first led to profess Christianity.\n\nOne of the smallest of the British Isles, situated near a rugged and barren coast, surrounded by dangerous seas, and possessing no sources of internal wealth, Iona has obtained an imperishable place in history as the seat of civilization and religion at a time when the darkness of heathenism hung over almost the whole of Northern Europe. Iona or Icolmkill is situated at the extremity of the island of Mull, from which it is separated by a strait of half a mile in breadth, its distance from the mainland of Scotland being thirty-six miles.\n\nColumba was a native of Ireland, and connected by birth with the princes of the land. Ireland was at that time a land of gospel light, while the western and northern parts of Scotland were still immersed in the darkness of heathenism. Columba with twelve friends landed on the island of Iona in the year of our Lord 563, having made the passage in a wicker boat covered with hides. The Druids who occupied the island endeavored to prevent his settling there, and the savage nations on the adjoining shores incommoded him with their hostility, and on several occasions endangered his life by their attacks. Yet by his perseverance and zeal he surmounted all opposition, procured from the king a gift of the island, and established there a monastery of which he was the abbot. He was unwearied in his labors to disseminate a knowledge of the Scriptures throughout the Highlands and islands of Scotland, and such was the reverence paid him that though not a bishop, but merely a presbyter and monk, the entire province with its bishops was subject to him and his successors. The Pictish monarch was so impressed with a sense of his wisdom and worth that he held him in the highest honor, and the neighboring chiefs and princes sought his counsel and availed themselves of his judgment in settling their disputes.\n\nWhen Columba landed on Iona he was attended by twelve followers whom he had formed into a religious body of which he was the head. To these, as occasion required, others were from time to time added, so that the original number was always kept up. Their institution was called a monastery and the superior an abbot, but the system had little in common with the monastic institutions of later times. The name by which those who submitted to the rule were known was that of Culdees, probably from the Latin “cultores Dei”—worshippers of God. They were a body of religious persons associated together for the purpose of aiding each other in the common work of preaching the gospel and teaching youth, as well as maintaining in themselves the fervor of devotion by united exercises of worship. On entering the order certain vows were taken by the members, but they were not those which were usually imposed by monastic orders, for of these, which are three,—celibacy, poverty, and obedience.—the Culdees were bound to none except the third. To poverty they did not bind themselves; on the contrary they seem to have labored diligently to procure for themselves and those dependent on them the comforts of life. Marriage also was allowed them, and most of them seem to have entered into that state. True, their wives were not permitted to reside with them at the institution, but they had a residence assigned to them in an adjacent locality. Near Iona there is an island which still bears the name of “Eilen nam ban,” women’s island, where their husbands seem to have resided with them, except when duty required their presence in the school or the sanctuary.\n\nCampbell, in his poem of “Reullura,” alludes to the married monks of Iona:\n\n“. . . The pure Culdees Were Albyn’s earliest priests of God, Ere yet an island of her seas By foot of Saxon monk was trod, Long ere her churchmen by bigotry Were barred from holy wedlock’s tie. ’Twas then that Aodh, famed afar, In Iona preached the word with power, And Reullura, beauty’s star, Was the partner of his bower.”\n\nIn one of his “Irish Melodies,” Moore gives the legend of St. Senanus and the lady who sought shelter on the island, but was repulsed:\n\n“O, haste and leave this sacred isle, Unholy bark, ere morning smile; For on thy deck, though dark it be, A female form I see; And I have sworn this sainted sod Shall ne’er by woman’s foot be trod.”\n\nIn these respects and in others the Culdees departed from the established rules of the Romish church, and consequently were deemed heretical. The consequence was that as the power of the latter advanced that of the Culdees was enfeebled. It was not, however, till the thirteenth century that the communities of the Culdees were suppressed and the members dispersed. They still continued to labor as individuals, and resisted the inroads of Papal usurpation as they best might till the light of the Reformation dawned on the world.\n\nIona, from its position in the western seas, was exposed to the assaults of the Norwegian and Danish rovers by whom those seas were infested, and by them it was repeatedly pillaged, its dwellings burned, and its peaceful inhabitants put to the sword. These unfavorable circumstances led to its gradual decline, which was expedited by the subversion of the Culdees throughout Scotland. Under the reign of Popery the island became the seat of a nunnery, the ruins of which are still seen. At the Reformation, the nuns were allowed to remain, living in community, when the abbey was dismantled.\n\nIona is now chiefly resorted to by travellers on account of the numerous ecclesiastical and sepulchral remains which are found upon it. The principal of these are the Cathedral or Abbey Church and the Chapel of the Nunnery. Besides these remains of ecclesiastical antiquity, there are some of an earlier date, and pointing to the existence on the island of forms of worship and belief different from those of Christianity. These are the circular Cairns which are found in various parts, and which seem to have been of Druidical origin. It is in reference to all these remains of ancient religion that Johnson exclaims, “That man is little to be envied whose patriotism would not gain force upon the plains of Marathon, or whose piety would not grow warmer amid the ruins of Iona.”\n\nIn the “Lord of the Isles” Scott beautifully contrasts the church on Iona with the cave of Staffa, opposite:\n\n“Nature herself, it seemed, would raise A minister to her Maker’s praise! Not for a meaner use ascend Her columns, or her arches bend; Nor of a theme less solemn tells That mighty surge that ebbs and swells, And still between each awful pause, From the high vault an answer draws, In varied tone, prolonged and high, That mocks the organ’s melody; Nor doth its entrance front in vain To old Iona’s holy fane, That Nature’s voice might seem to say, Well hast thou done, frail child of clay! Thy humble powers that stately shrine Tasked high and hard—but witness mine!”\n\nON the decline of the Roman power, about five centuries after Christ, the countries of Northern Europe were left almost destitute of a national government. Numerous chiefs, more or less powerful, held local sway, as far as each could enforce his dominion, and occasionally those chiefs would unite for a common object; but, in ordinary times, they were much more likely to be found in hostility to one another. In such a state of things the rights of the humbler classes of society were at the mercy of every assailant; and it is plain that, without some check upon the lawless power of the chiefs, society must have relapsed into barbarism. Such checks were found, first, in the rivalry of the chiefs themselves, whose mutual jealousy made them restraints upon one another; secondly, in the influence of the Church, which, by every motive, pure or selfish, was pledged to interpose for the protection of the weak; and lastly, in the generosity and sense of right which, however crushed under the weight of passion and selfishness, dwell naturally in the heart of man. From this last source sprang Chivalry, which framed an ideal of the heroic character, combining invincible strength and valor, justice, modesty, loyalty to superiors, courtesy to equals, compassion to weakness, and devotedness to the Church; an ideal which, if never met with in real life, was acknowledged by all as the highest model for emulation.\n\nThe word “Chivalry” is derived from the French “cheval,” a horse. The word “knight,” which originally meant boy or servant, was particularly applied to a young man after he was admitted to the privilege of bearing arms. This privilege was conferred on youths of family and fortune only, for the mass of the people were not furnished with arms. The knight then was a mounted warrior, a man of rank, or in the service and maintenance of some man of rank, generally possessing some independent means of support, but often relying mainly on the gratitude of those whom he served for the supply of his wants, and often, no doubt, resorting to the means which power confers on its possessor.\n\nIn time of war the knight was, with his followers, in the camp of his sovereign, or commanding in the field, or holding some castle for him. In time of peace he was often in attendance at his sovereign’s court, gracing with his presence the banquets and tournaments with which princes cheered their leisure. Or he was traversing the country in quest of adventure, professedly bent on redressing wrongs and enforcing rights, sometimes in fulfilment of some vow of religion or of love. These wandering knights were called knights-errant; they were welcome guests in the castles of the nobility, for their presence enlivened the dulness of those secluded abodes, and they were received with honor at the abbeys, which often owed the best part of their revenues to the patronage of the knights; but if no castle or abbey or hermitage were at hand their hardy habits made it not intolerable to them to lie down, supperless, at the foot of some wayside cross, and pass the night.\n\nIt is evident that the justice administered by such an instrumentality must have been of the rudest description. The force whose legitimate purpose was to redress wrongs might easily be perverted to inflict them. Accordingly, we find in the romances, which, however fabulous in facts, are true as pictures of manners, that a knightly castle was often a terror to the surrounding country; that is, dungeons were full of oppressed knights and ladies, waiting for some champion to appear to set them free, or to be ransomed with money; that hosts of idle retainers were ever at hand to enforce their lord’s behests, regardless of law and justice; and that the rights of the unarmed multitude were of no account. This contrariety of fact and theory in regard to chivalry will account for the opposite impressions which exist in men’s minds respecting it. While it has been the theme of the most fervid eulogium on the one part, it has been as eagerly denounced on the other. On a cool estimate, we cannot but see reason to congratulate ourselves that it has given way in modern times to the reign of law, and that the civil magistrate, if less picturesque, has taken the place of the mailed champion.\n\n# THE TRAINING OF A KNIGHT\n\nThe preparatory education of candidates for knighthood was long and arduous. At seven years of age the noble children were usually removed from their father’s house to the court or castle of their future patron, and placed under the care of a governor, who taught them the first articles of religion, and respect and reverence for their lords and superiors, and initiated them in the ceremonies of a court. They were called pages, valets, or varlets, and their office was to carve, to wait at table, and to perform other menial services, which were not then considered humiliating. In their leisure hours they learned to dance and play on the harp, were instructed in the mysteries of woods and rivers, that is, in hunting, falconry, and fishing, and in wrestling, tilting with spears, and performing other military exercises on horseback. At fourteen the page became an esquire, and began a course of severer and more laborious exercises. To vault on a horse in heavy armor; to run, to scale walls, and spring over ditches, under the same encumbrance; to wrestle, to wield the battle-axe for a length of time, without raising the visor or taking breath; to perform with grace all the evolutions of horsemanship,—were necessary preliminaries to the reception of knighthood, which was usually conferred at twenty-one years of age, when the young man’s education was supposed to be completed. In the meantime, the esquires were no less assiduously engaged in acquiring all those refinements of civility which formed what was in that age called courtesy. The same castle in which they received their education was usually thronged with young persons of the other sex, and the page was encouraged, at a very early age, to select some lady of the court as the mistress of his heart, to whom he was taught to refer all his sentiments, words, and actions. The service of his mistress was the glory and occupation of a knight, and her smiles, bestowed at once by affection and gratitude, were held out as the recompense of his well-directed valor. Religion united its influence with those of loyalty and love, and the order of knighthood, endowed with all the sanctity and religious awe that attended the priesthood, became an object of ambition to the greatest sovereigns.\n\nThe ceremonies of initiation were peculiarly solemn. After undergoing a severe fast, and spending whole nights in prayer, the candidate confessed, and received the sacrament. He then clothed himself in snow-white garments, and repaired to the church, or the hall, where the ceremony was to take place, bearing a knightly sword suspended from his neck, which the officiating priest took and blessed, and then returned to him. The candidate then, with folded arms, knelt before the presiding knight, who, after some questions about his motives and purposes in requesting admission, administered to him the oaths, and granted his request. Some of the knights present, sometimes even ladies and damsels, handed to him in succession the spurs, the coat of mail, the hauberk, the armlet and gauntlet, and lastly he girded on the sword. He then knelt again before the president, who, rising from his seat, gave him the “accolade,” which consisted of three strokes, with the flat of a sword, on the shoulder or neck of the candidate, accompanied by the words: “In the name of God, of St. Michael, and St. George, I make thee a knight; be valiant, courteous, and loyal!” Then he received his helmet, his shield, and spear; and thus the investiture ended.\n\n# FREEMEN, VILLAINS, SERFS, AND CLERKS\n\nThe other classes of which society was composed were, first, freemen, owners of small portions of land independent, though they sometimes voluntarily became the vassals of their more opulent neighbors, whose power was necessary for their protection. The other two classes, which were much the most numerous, were either serfs or villains, both of which were slaves.\n\nThe serfs were in the lowest state of slavery. All the fruits of their labor belonged to the master whose land they tilled, and by whom they were fed and clothed.\n\nThe villains were less degraded. Their situation seems to have resembled that of the Russian peasants at this day. Like the serfs, they were attached to the soil, and were transferred with it by purchase; but they paid only a fixed rent to the landlord, and had a right to dispose of any surplus that might arise from their industry.\n\nThe term “clerk” was of very extensive import. It comprehended, originally, such persons only as belonged to the clergy, or clerical order, among whom, however, might be found a multitude of married persons, artisans or others. But in process of time a much wider rule was established; every one that could read being accounted a clerk or clericus, and allowed the “benefit of clergy,” that is, exemption from capital and some other forms of punishment, in case of crime.\n\nThe splendid pageant of a tournament between knights, its gaudy accessories and trappings, and its chivalrous regulations, originated in France. Tournaments were repeatedly condemned by the Church, probably on account of the quarrels they led to, and the often fatal results. The “joust,” or “just,” was different from the tournament. In these, knights fought with their lances, and their object was to unhorse their antagonists; while the tournaments were intended for a display of skill and address in evolutions, and with various weapons, and greater courtesy was observed in the regulations. By these it was forbidden to wound the horse, or to use the point of the sword, or to strike a knight after he had raised his vizor, or unlaced his helmet. The ladies encouraged their knights in these exercises; they bestowed prizes, and the conqueror’s feats were the theme of romance and song. The stands overlooking the ground, of course, were varied in the shapes of towers, terraces, galleries, and pensile gardens, magnificently decorated with tapestry, pavilions, and banners. Every combatant proclaimed the name of the lady whose servant d’amour he was. He was wont to look up to the stand, and strengthen his courage by the sight of the bright eyes that were raining their influence on him from above. The knights also carried favors, consisting of scarfs, veils, sleeves, bracelets, clasps,—in short, some piece of female habiliment,—attached to their helmets, shields, or armor. If, during the combat, any of these appendages were dropped or lost the fair donor would at times send her knight new ones, especially if pleased with his exertions.\n\n# MAIL ARMOR\n\nMail armor, of which the hauberk is a species, and which derived its name from maille, a French word for mesh, was of two kinds, plate or scale mail, and chain mail. It was originally used for the protection of the body only, reaching no lower than the knees. It was shaped like a carter’s frock, and bound round the waist by a girdle. Gloves and hose of mail were afterwards added, and a hood, which, when necessary, was drawn over the head, leaving the face alone uncovered. To protect the skin from the impression of the iron network of the chain mail, a quilted lining was employed, which, however, was insufficient, and the bath was used to efface the marks of the armor.\n\nThe hauberk was a complete covering of double chain mail. Some hauberks opened before, like a modern coat; others were closed like a shirt.\n\nThe chain mail of which they were composed was formed by a number of iron links, each link having others inserted into it, the whole exhibiting a kind of network, of which (in some instances at least) the meshes were circular, with each link separately riveted.\n\nThe hauberk was proof against the most violent blow of a sword; but the point of a lance might pass through the meshes, or drive the iron into the flesh. To guard against this, a thick and well-stuffed doublet was worn underneath, under which was commonly added an iron breastplate. Hence the expression “to pierce both plate and mail,” so common in the earlier poets.\n\nMail armor continued in general use till about the year 1300, when it was gradually supplanted by plate armor, or suits consisting of pieces or plates of solid iron, adapted to the different parts of the body.\n\nShields were generally made of wood, covered with leather, or some similar substance. To secure them, in some sort, from being cut through by the sword, they were surrounded with a hoop of metal.\n\nThe helmet was composed of two parts: the headpiece, which was strengthened within by several circles of iron, and the visor, which, as the name implies, was a sort of grating to see through, so contrived as, by sliding in a groove, or turning on a pivot, to be raised or lowered at pleasure. Some helmets had a further improvement called a bever, from the Italian bevere, to drink. The ventayle, or “air-passage,” is another name for this.\n\nTo secure the helmet from the possibility of falling, or of being struck off, it was tied by several laces to the meshes of the hauberk; consequently, when a knight was overthrown it was necessary to undo these laces before he could be put to death; though this was sometimes effected by lifting up the skirt of the hauberk, and stabbing him in the belly. The instrument of death was a small dagger, worn on the right side.\n\nIn ages when there were no books, when noblemen and princes themselves could not read, history or tradition was monopolized by the story-tellers. They inherited, generation after generation, the wondrous tales of their predecessors, which they retailed to the public with such additions of their own as their acquired information supplied them with. Anachronisms became of course very common, and errors of geography, of locality, of manners, equally so. Spurious genealogies were invented, in which Arthur and his knights, and Charlemagne and his paladins, were made to derive their descent from Æneas, Hector, or some other of the Trojan heroes.\n\nWith regard to the derivation of the word “Romance,” we trace it to the fact that the dialects which were formed in Western Europe, from the admixture of Latin with the native languages, took the name of Langue Romaine. The French language was divided into two dialects. The river Loire was their common boundary. In the provinces to the south of that river the affirmative, yes, was expressed by the word oc; in the north it was called oil (oui); and hence Dante has named the southern language langue d’oc, and the northern langue d’oil. The latter, which was carried into England by the Normans, and is the origin of the present French, may be called the French Romane; and the former the Provençal, or Provencial Romane, because it was spoken by the people of Provence and Languedoc, southern provinces of France.\n\nThese dialects were soon distinguished by very opposite characters. A soft and enervating climate, a spirit of commerce encouraged by an easy communication with other maritime nations, the influx of wealth, and a more settled government, may have tended to polish and soften the diction of the Provencials, whose poets, under the name of Troubadours, were the masters of the Italians, and particularly of Petrarch. Their favorite pieces were Sirventes (satirical pieces), love-songs, and Ténsons, which last were a sort of dialogue in verse between two poets, who questioned each other on some refined points of loves’ casuistry. It seems the Provencials were so completely absorbed in these delicate questions as to neglect and despise the composition of fabulous histories of adventure and knighthood, which they left in a great measure to the poets of the northern part of the kingdom, called Trouveurs.\n\nAt a time when chivalry excited universal admiration, and when all the efforts of that chivalry were directed against the enemies of religion, it was natural that literature should receive the same impulse, and that history and fable should be ransacked to furnish examples of courage and piety that might excite increased emulation. Arthur and Charlemagne were the two heroes selected for this purpose. Arthur’s pretensions were that he was a brave, though not always a successful warrior; he had withstood with great resolution the arms of the infidels, that is to say of the Saxons, and his memory was held in the highest estimation by his countrymen, the Britons, who carried with them into Wales, and into the kindred country of Armorica, or Brittany, the memory of his exploits, which their national vanity insensibly exaggerated, till the little prince of the Silures (South Wales) was magnified into the conqueror of England, of Gaul, and of the greater part of Europe. His genealogy was gradually carried up to an imaginary Brutus, and to the period of the Trojan war, and a sort of chronicle was composed in the Welsh, or Armorican language, which, under the pompous title of the “History of the Kings of Britain,” was translated into Latin by Geoffrey of Monmouth, about the year 1150. The Welsh critics consider the material of the work to have been an older history, written by St. Talian, Bishop of St. Asaph, in the seventh century.\n\nAs to Charlemagne, though his real merits were sufficient to secure his immortality, it was impossible that his holy wars against the Saracens should not become a favorite topic for fiction. Accordingly, the fabulous history of these wars was written, probably towards the close of the eleventh century, by a monk, who, thinking it would add dignity to his work to embellish it with a contemporary name, boldly ascribed it to Turpin, who was Archbishop of Rheims about the year 773.\n\nThese fabulous chronicles were for a while imprisoned in languages of local only or of professional access. Both Turpin and Geoffrey might indeed be read by ecclesiastics, the sole Latin scholars of those times, and Geoffrey’s British original would contribute to the gratification of Welshmen; but neither could become extensively popular till translated into some language of general and familiar use. The Anglo-Saxon was at that time used only by a conquered and enslaved nation; the Spanish and Italian languages were not yet formed; the Norman French alone was spoken and understood by the nobility in the greater part of Europe, and therefore was a proper vehicle for the new mode of composition.\n\nThat language was fashionable in England before the Conquest, and became, after that event, the only language used at the court of London. As the various conquests of the Normans, and the enthusiastic valor of that extraordinary people, had familiarized the minds of men with the most marvellous events, their poets eagerly seized the fabulous legends of Arthur and Charlemagne, translated them into the language of the day, and soon produced a variety of imitations. The adventures attributed to these monarchs, and to their distinguished warriors, together with those of many other traditionary or imaginary heroes, composed by degrees that formidable body of marvellous histories which, from the dialect in which the most ancient of them were written, were called “Romances.”\n\n# METRICAL ROMANCES\n\nThe earliest form in which romances appear is that of a rude kind of verse. In this form it is supposed they were sung or recited at the feasts of princes and knights in their baronial halls. The following specimen of the language and style of Robert de Beauvais, who flourished in 1257, is from Sir Walter Scott’s “Introduction to the Romance of Sir Tristrem”:\n\n“Ne voil pas emmi dire, Ici diverse la matyere, Entre ceus qui solent cunter, E de le cunte Tristran parler.”\n“I will not say too much about it, So diverse is the matter, Among those who are in the habit of telling And relating the story of Tristran.”\n\nThis is a specimen of the language which was in use among the nobility of England, in the ages immediately after the Norman conquest. The following is a specimen of the English that existed at the same time, among the common people. Robert de Brunne, speaking of his Latin and French authorities, says:\n\n“Als thai haf wryten and sayd Haf I alle in myn Inglis layd, In symple speche as I couthe, That is lightest in manne’s mouthe. Alle for the luf of symple men, That strange Inglis cannot ken.”\n\nThe “strange Inglis” being the language of the previous specimen.\n\nIt was not till toward the end of the thirteenth century that the prose romances began to appear. These works generally began with disowning and discrediting the sources from which in reality they drew their sole information. As every romance was supposed to be a real history, the compilers of those in prose would have forfeited all credit if they had announced themselves as mere copyists of the minstrels. On the contrary, they usually state that, as the popular poems upon the matter in question contain many “lesings,” they had been induced to translate the real and true history of such or such a knight from the original Latin or Greek, or from the ancient British or Armorican authorities, which authorities existed only in their own assertion.\n\nA specimen of the style of the prose romances may be found in the following extract from one of the most celebrated and latest of them, the “Morte d’Arthur” of Sir Thomas Mallory, of the date of 1485. From this work much of the contents of this volume has been drawn, with as close an adherence to the original style as was thought consistent with our plan of adapting our narrative to the taste of modern readers.\n\n“It is notoyrly knowen thorugh the vnyuersal world that there been ix worthy and the best that ever were. That is to wete thre paynyms, three Jewes, and three crysten men. As for the paynyms, they were tofore the Incarnacyon of Cryst whiche were named, the fyrst Hector of Troye; the second Alysaunder the grete, and the thyrd Julyus Cezar, Emperour of Rome, of whome thystoryes ben wel kno and had. And as for the thre Jewes whyche also were tofore thyncarnacyon of our Lord, of whome the fyrst was Duc Josue, whyche brought the chyldren of Israhel into the londe of beheste; the second Dauyd, kyng of Jherusalem, and the thyrd Judas Machabeus; of these thre the byble reherceth al theyr noble hystoryes and actes. And sythe the sayd Incarnacyon haue ben the noble crysten men stalled and admytted thorugh the vnyuersal world to the nombre of the ix beste and worthy, of whome was fyrst the noble Arthur, whose noble actes I purpose to wryte in this person book here folowyng. The second was Charlemayn, or Charles the grete, of whome thystorye is had in many places both in frensshe and englysshe, and the thyrd and last was Godefray of boloyn.”\n\n————\n\n# THE MYTHICAL HISTORY OF ENGLAND\n\nTHE illustrious poet, Milton, in his “History of England,” is the author whom we chiefly follow in this chapter.\n\nAccording to the earliest accounts, Albion, a giant, and son of Neptune, a contemporary of Hercules, ruled over the island, to which he gave his name. Presuming to oppose the progress of Hercules in his western march, he was slain by him.\n\nAnother story is that Histion, the son of Japhet, the son of Noah, had four sons, Francus, Romanus, Alemannus, and Britto, from whom descended the French, Roman, German, and British people.\n\nRejecting these and other like stories, Milton gives more regard to the story of Brutus, the Trojan, which, he says, is supported by “descents of ancestry long continued, laws and exploits not plainly seeming to be borrowed or devised, which on the common belief have wrought no small impression; defended by many, denied utterly by few.” The principal authority is Geoffrey of Monmouth, whose history, written in the twelfth century, purports to be a translation of a history of Britain brought over from the opposite shore of France, which, under the name of Brittany, was chiefly peopled by natives of Britain who, from time to time, emigrated thither, driven from their own country by the inroads of the Picts and Scots. According to this authority, Brutus was the son of Silvius, and he of Ascanius, the son of Æneas, whose flight from Troy and settlement in Italy are narrated in “Stories of Gods and Heroes.”\n\nBrutus, at the age of fifteen, attending his father to the chase, unfortunately killed him with an arrow. Banished therefor by his kindred, he sought refuge in that part of Greece where Helenus, with a band of Trojan exiles, had become established. But Helenus was now dead and the descendants of the Trojans were oppressed by Pandrasus, the king of the country. Brutus, being kindly received among them, so throve in virtue and in arms as to win the regard of all the eminent of the land above all others of his age. In consequence of this the Trojans not only began to hope, but secretly to persuade him to lead them the way to liberty. To encourage them, they had the promise of help from Assaracus, a noble Greek youth, whose mother was a Trojan. He had suffered wrong at the hands of the king, and for that reason the more willingly cast in his lot with the Trojan exiles.\n\nChoosing a fit opportunity, Brutus with his countrymen withdrew to the woods and hills, as the safest place from which to expostulate, and sent this message to Pandrasus: “That the Trojans, holding it unworthy of their ancestors to serve in a foreign land, had retreated to the woods, choosing rather a savage life than a slavish one. If that displeased him, then, with his leave, they would depart to some other country.” Pandrasus, not expecting so bold a message from the sons of captives, went in pursuit of them, with such forces as he could gather, and met them on the banks of the Achelous, where Brutus got the advantage, and took the king captive. The result was, that the terms demanded by the Trojans were granted; the king gave his daughter Imogen in marriage to Brutus, and furnished shipping, money, and fit provision for them all to depart from the land.\n\nThe marriage being solemnized, and shipping from all parts got together, the Trojans, in a fleet of no less than three hundred and twenty sail, betook themselves to the sea. On the third day they arrived at a certain island, which they found destitute of inhabitants, though there were appearances of former habitation, and among the ruins a temple of Diana. Brutus, here performing sacrifice at the shrine of the goddess, invoked an oracle for his guidance, in these lines:\n\n“Goddess of shades, and huntress, who at will Walk’st on the rolling sphere, and through the deep; On thy third realm, the earth, look now, and tell What land, what seat of rest, thou bidd’st me seek; What certain seat where I may worship thee For aye, with temples vowed and virgin choirs.”\n\nTo whom, sleeping before the altar, Diana in a vision thus answered:\n\n“Brutus! far to the west, in the ocean wide, Beyond the realm of Gaul, a land there lies, Seagirt it lies, where giants dwelt of old; Now, void, it fits thy people: thither bend Thy course; there shalt thou find a lasting seat; There to thy sons another Troy shall rise, And kings be born of thee, whose dreaded might Shall awe the world, and conquer nations bold.”\n\nBrutus, guided now, as he thought, by divine direction, sped his course towards the west, and, arriving at a place on the Tyrrhene sea, found there the descendants of certain Trojans who, with Antenor, came into Italy, of whom Corineus was the chief. These joined company, and the ships pursued their way till they arrived at the mouth of the river Loire, in France, where the expedition landed, with a view to a settlement, but were so rudely assaulted by the inhabitants that they put to sea again, and arrived at a part of the coast of Britain, now called Devonshire, where Brutus felt convinced that he had found the promised end of his voyage, landed his colony, and took possession.\n\nThe island, not yet Britain, but Albion, was in a manner desert and inhospitable, occupied only by a remnant of the giant race whose excessive force and tyranny had destroyed the others. The Trojans encountered these and extirpated them, Corineus, in particular, signalizing himself by his exploits against them; from whom Cornwall takes its name, for that region fell to his lot, and there the hugest giants dwelt, lurking in rocks and caves, till Corineus rid the land of them.\n\nBrutus built his capital city, and called it Trojanova (New Troy), changed in time to Trinovantus, now London;[39] and, having governed the isle twenty-four years, died, leaving three sons, Locrine, Albanact and Camber. Locrine had the middle part, Camber the west, called Cambria from him, and Albanact Albania, now Scotland. Locrine was married to Guendolen, the daughter of Corineus, but having seen a fair maid named Estrildis, who had been brought captive from Germany, he became enamoured of her, and had by her a daughter, whose name was Sabra. This matter was kept secret while Corineus lived, but after his death Locrine divorced Guendolen, and made Estrildis his queen. Guendolen, all in rage, departed to Cornwall, where Madan, her son, lived, who had been brought up by Corineus, his grandfather. Gathering an army of her father’s friends and subjects, she gave battle to her husband’s forces and Locrine was slain. Guendolen caused her rival, Estrildis, with her daughter Sabra, to be thrown into the river, from which cause the river thenceforth bore the maiden’s name, which by length of time is now changed into Sabrina or Severn. Milton alludes to this in his address to the rivers,—\n\n“Severn swift, guilty of maiden’s death”;—\n\nand in his “Comus” tells the story with a slight variation, thus:\n\n“There is a gentle nymph not far from hence, That with moist curb sways the smooth Severn stream; Sabrina is her name, a virgin pure: Whilom she was the daughter of Locrine, That had the sceptre from his father, Brute, She, guiltless damsel, flying the mad pursuit Of her enragéd step-dame, Guendolen, Commended her fair innocence to the flood, That stayed her flight with his cross-flowing course The water-nymphs that in the bottom played, Held up their pearléd wrists and took her in, Bearing her straight to aged Nereus’ hall, Who, piteous of her woes, reared her lank head, And gave her to his daughters to imbathe In nectared lavers strewed with asphodel, And through the porch and inlet of each sense Dropped in ambrosial oils till she revived, And underwent a quick, immortal change, Made goddess of the river,” etc.\n\nIf our readers ask when all this took place, we must answer, in the first place, that mythology is not careful of dates; and next, that, as Brutus was the great-grandson of Æneas, it must have been not far from a century subsequent to the Trojan war, or about eleven hundred years before the invasion of the island by Julius Cæsar. This long interval is filled with the names of princes whose chief occupation was in warring with one another. Some few, whose names remain connected with places, or embalmed in literature, we will mention.\n\nBladud built the city of Bath, and dedicated the medicinal waters to Minerva. He was a man of great invention, and practised the arts of magic, till, having made him wings to fly, he fell down upon the temple of Apollo, in Trinovant, and so died, after twenty years’ reign.\n\nLeir, who next reigned, built Leicester, and called it after his name. He had no male issue, but only three daughters. When grown old he determined to divide his kingdom among his daughters, and bestow them in marriage. But first, to try which of them loved him best, he determined to ask them solemnly in order, and judge of the warmth of their affection by their answers. Goneril, the eldest, knowing well her father’s weakness, made answer that she loved him “above her soul.” “Since thou so honorest my declining age,” said the old man, “to thee and to thy husband I give the third part of my realm.” Such good success for a few words soon uttered was ample instruction to Regan, the second daughter, what to say. She therefore to the same question replied that “she loved him more than all the world beside;” and so received an equal reward with her sister. But Cordeilla, the youngest, and hitherto the best beloved, though having before her eyes the reward of a little easy soothing, and the loss likely to attend plain-dealing, yet was not moved from the solid purpose of a sincere and virtuous answer, and replied: “Father, my love towards you is as my duty bids. They who pretend beyond this flatter.” When the old man, sorry to hear this, and wishing her to recall these words, persisted in asking, she still restrained her expressions so as to say rather less than more than the truth. Then Leir, all in a passion, burst forth: “Since thou hast not reverenced thy aged father like thy sisters, think not to have any part in my kingdom or what else I have;”—and without delay, giving in marriage his other daughters, Goneril to the Duke of Albany, and Regan to the Duke of Cornwall, he divides his kingdom between them, and goes to reside with his eldest daughter, attended only by a hundred knights. But in a short time his attendants, being complained of as too numerous and disorderly, are reduced to thirty. Resenting that affront, the old king betakes him to his second daughter; but she, instead of soothing his wounded pride, takes part with her sister, and refuses to admit a retinue of more than five. Then back he returns to the other, who now will not receive him with more than one attendant. Then the remembrance of Cordeilla comes to his thoughts, and he takes his journey into France to seek her, with little hope of kind consideration from one whom he had so injured, but to pay her the last recompense he can render,—confession of his injustice. When Cordeilla is informed of his approach, and of his sad condition, she pours forth true filial tears. And, not willing that her own or others’ eyes should see him in that forlorn condition, she sends one of her trusted servants to meet him, and convey him privately to some comfortable abode, and to furnish him with such state as befitted his dignity. After which Cordeilla, with the king her husband, went in state to meet him, and, after an honorable reception, the king permitted his wife, Cordeilla, to go with an army and set her father again upon his throne. They prospered, subdued the wicked sisters and their consorts, and Leir obtained the crown and held it three years. Cordeilla succeeded him and reigned five years; but the sons of her sisters, after that, rebelled against her, and she lost both her crown and life.\n\nShakspeare has chosen this story as the subject of his tragedy of “King Lear,” varying its details in some respects. The madness of Leir, and the ill success of Cordeilla’s attempt to reinstate her father, are the principal variations, and those in the names will also be noticed. Our narrative is drawn from Milton’s “History;” and thus the reader will perceive that the story of Leir has had the distinguished honor of being told by the two acknowledged chiefs of British literature.\n\n# FERREX AND PORREX\n\nFerrex and Porrex were brothers, who held the kingdom after Leir. They quarrelled about the supremacy, and Porrex expelled his brother, who, obtaining aid from Suard, king of the Franks, returned and made war upon Porrex. Ferrex was slain in battle and his forces dispersed. When their mother came to hear of her son’s death, who was her favorite, she fell into a great rage, and conceived a mortal hatred against the survivor. She took, therefore, her opportunity when he was asleep, fell upon him, and, with the assistance of her women, tore him in pieces. This horrid story would not be worth relating, were it not for the fact that it has furnished the plot for the first tragedy which was written in the English language. It was entitled “Gorboduc,” but in the second edition “Ferrex and Porrex,” and was the production of Thomas Sackville, afterwards Earl of Dorset, and Thomas Norton, a barrister. Its date was 1561.\n\n# DUNWALLO MOLMUTIUS\n\nThis is the next name of note. Molmutius established the Molmutine laws, which bestowed the privilege of sanctuary on temples, cities, and the roads leading to them, and gave the same protection to ploughs, extending a religious sanction to the labors of the field. Shakspeare alludes to him in “Cymbeline,” Act III., Scene 1:\n\n“. . . Molmutius made our laws; Who was the first of Britain which did put His brows within a golden crown, and called Himself a king.”\n\n# BRENNUS AND BELINUS,\n\nthe sons of Molmutius, succeeded him. They quarrelled, and Brennus was driven out of the island, and took refuge in Gaul, where he met with such favor from the king of the Allobroges that he gave him his daughter in marriage, and made him his partner on the throne. Brennus is the name which the Roman historians give to the famous leader of the Gauls who took Rome in the time of Camillus. Geoffrey of Monmouth claims the glory of the conquest for the British prince, after he had become king of the Allobroges.\n\nAfter Belinus and Brennus there reigned several kings of little note, and then came Elidure. Arthgallo, his brother, being king, gave great offence to his powerful nobles, who rose against him, deposed him, and advanced Elidure to the throne. Arthgallo fled, and endeavored to find assistance in the neighboring kingdoms to reinstate him, but found none. Elidure reigned prosperously and wisely. After five years’ possession of the kingdom, one day, when hunting, he met in the forest his brother, Arthgallo, who had been deposed. After long wandering, unable longer to bear the poverty to which he was reduced, he had returned to Britain, with only ten followers, designing to repair to those who had formerly been his friends. Elidure, at the sight of his brother in distress, forgetting all animosities, ran to him, and embraced him. He took Arthgallo home with him, and concealed him in the palace. After this he feigned himself sick, and, calling his nobles about him, induced them, partly by persuasion, partly by force, to consent to his abdicating the kingdom, and reinstating his brother on the throne. The agreement being ratified, Elidure took the crown from his own head, and put it on his brother’s head. Arthgallo after this reigned ten years, well and wisely, exercising strict justice towards all men.\n\nHe died, and left the kingdom to his sons, who reigned with various fortunes, but were not long-lived, and left no offspring, so that Elidure was again advanced to the throne, and finished the course of his life in just and virtuous actions, receiving the name of the pious, from the love and admiration of his subjects.\n\nWordsworth has taken the story of Artegal and Elidure for the subject of a poem, which is No. 2 of “Poems founded on the Affections.”\n\nAfter Elidure, the Chronicle names many kings, but none of special note, till we come to Lud, who greatly enlarged Trinovant, his capital, and surrounded it with a wall. He changed its name, bestowing upon it his own, so that henceforth it was called Lud’s town, afterwards London. Lud was buried by the gate of the city called after him Ludgate. He had two sons, but they were not old enough at the time of their father’s death to sustain the cares of government, and therefore their uncle, Caswallaun, or Cassibellaunus, succeeded to the kingdom. He was a brave and magnificent prince, so that his fame reached to distant countries.\n\nAbout this time it happened (as is found in the Roman histories) that Julius Cæsar, having subdued Gaul, came to the shore opposite Britain. And having resolved to add this island also to his conquests, he prepared ships and transported his army across the sea, to the mouth of the River Thames. Here he was met by Cassibellaun with all his forces, and a battle ensued, in which Nennius, the brother of Cassibellaun, engaged in single combat with Cæsar. After several furious blows given and received, the sword of Cæsar stuck so fast in the shield of Nennius that it could not be pulled out, and the combatants being separated by the intervention of the troops Nennius remained possessed of this trophy. At last, after the greater part of the day was spent, the Britons poured in so fast that Cæsar was forced to retire to his camp and fleet. And finding it useless to continue the war any longer at that time, he returned to Gaul.\n\nShakspeare alludes to Cassibellaunus, in “Cymbeline”:\n\n“The famed Cassibelan, who was once at point (O giglot fortune!) to master Cæsar’s sword, Made Lud’s town with rejoicing fires bright, And Britons strut with courage.”\n\n# KYMBELINUS, OR CYMBELINE\n\nCæsar, on a second invasion of the island, was more fortunate, and compelled the Britons to pay tribute. Cymbeline, the nephew of the king, was delivered to the Romans as a hostage for the faithful fulfilment of the treaty, and, being carried to Rome by Cæsar, he was there brought up in the Roman arts and accomplishments. Being afterwards restored to his country, and placed on the throne, he was attached to the Romans, and continued through all his reign at peace with them. His sons, Guiderius and Arviragus, who made their appearance in Shakspeare’s play of “Cymbeline,” succeeded their father, and, refusing to pay tribute to the Romans, brought on another invasion. Guiderius was slain, but Arviragus afterward made terms with the Romans, and reigned prosperously many years.\n\nThe next event of note is the conquest and colonization of Armorica, by Maximus, a Roman general, and Conan, lord of Miniadoc or Denbigh-land, in Wales. The name of the country was changed to Brittany, or Lesser Britain; and so completely was it possessed by the British colonists, that the language became assimilated to that spoken in Wales, and it is said that to this day the peasantry of the two countries can understand each other when speaking their native language.\n\nThe Romans eventually succeeded in establishing themselves in the island, and after the lapse of several generations they became blended with the natives so that no distinction existed between the two races. When at length the Roman armies were withdrawn from Britain, their departure was a matter of regret to the inhabitants, as it left them without protection against the barbarous tribes, Scots, Picts, and Norwegians, who harassed the country incessantly. This was the state of things when the era of King Arthur began.\n\nThe adventure of Albion, the giant, with Hercules is alluded to by Spenser, “Faery Queene,” Book IV., Canto xi:\n\n“For Albion the son of Neptune was; Who for the proof of his great puissance, Out of his Albion did on dry foot pass Into old Gaul that now is cleped France, To fight with Hercules, that did advance To vanquish all the world with matchless might: And there his mortal part by great mischance Was slain.”\n————\n\nMERLIN was the son of no mortal father, but of an Incubus, one of a class of beings not absolutely wicked, but far from good, who inhabit the regions of the air. Merlin’s mother was a virtuous young woman, who, on the birth of her son, intrusted him to a priest, who hurried him to the baptismal fount, and so saved him from sharing the lot of his father, though he retained many marks of his unearthly origin.\n\nAt this time Vortigern reigned in Britain. He was a usurper, who had caused the death of his sovereign, Moines, and driven the two brothers of the late king, whose names were Uther and Pendragon, into banishment. Vortigern, who lived in constant fear of the return of the rightful heirs of the kingdom, began to erect a strong tower for defence. The edifice, when brought by the workmen to a certain height, three times fell to the ground, without any apparent cause. The king consulted his astrologers on this wonderful event, and learned from them that it would be necessary to bathe the corner-stone of the foundation with the blood of a child born without a mortal father.\n\nIn search of such an infant, Vortigern sent his messengers all over the kingdom, and they by accident discovered Merlin, whose lineage seemed to point him out as the individual wanted. They took him to the king; but Merlin, young as he was, explained to the king the absurdity of attempting to rescue the fabric by such means, for he told him the true cause of the instability of the tower was its being placed over the den of two immense dragons, whose combats shook the earth above them. The king ordered his workmen to dig beneath the tower, and when they had done so they discovered two enormous serpents, the one white as milk, the other red as fire. The multitude looked on with amazement, till the serpents, slowly rising from their den, and expanding their enormous folds, began the combat, when every one fled in terror, except Merlin, who stood by clapping his hands and cheering on the conflict. The red dragon was slain, and the white one, gliding through a cleft in the rock, disappeared.\n\nThese animals typified, as Merlin afterwards explained, the invasion of Uther and Pendragon, the rightful princes, who soon after landed with a great army. Vortigern was defeated, and afterwards burned alive in the castle he had taken such pains to construct. On the death of Vortigern, Pendragon ascended the throne. Merlin became his chief adviser, and often assisted the king by his magical arts.\n\n“Merlin, who knew the range of all their arts, Had built the King his havens, ships and halls.” —Vivian.\n\nAmong other endowments, he had the power of transforming himself into any shape he pleased. At one time he appeared as a dwarf, at others as a damsel, a page, or even a greyhound or a stag. This faculty he often employed for the service of the king, and sometimes also for the diversion of the court and the sovereign.\n\nMerlin continued to be a favorite counsellor through the reigns of Pendragon, Uther, and Arthur, and at last disappeared from view, and was no more found among men, through the treachery of his mistress, Viviane, the Fairy, which happened in this wise.\n\nMerlin, having become enamoured of the fair Viviane, the Lady of the Lake, was weak enough to impart to her various important secrets of his art, being impelled by fatal destiny, of which he was at the same time fully aware. The lady, however, was not content with his devotion, unbounded as it seems to have been, but “cast about,” the Romance tells us, how she might “detain him for evermore,” and one day addressed him in these terms: “Sir, I would that we should make a fair place and a suitable, so contrived by art and by cunning that it might never be undone, and that you and I should be there in joy and solace.” “My lady,” said Merlin, “I will do all this.” “Sir,” said she, “I would not have you do it, but you shall teach me, and I will do it, and then it will be more to my mind.” “I grant you this,” said Merlin. Then he began to devise, and the damsel put it all in writing. And when he had devised the whole, then had the damsel full great joy, and showed him greater semblance of love than she had ever before made, and they sojourned together a long while. At length it fell out that, as they were going one day hand in hand through the forest of Brécéliande, they found a bush of white-thorn, which was laden with flowers; and they seated themselves under the shade of this white-thorn, upon the green grass, and Merlin laid his head upon the damsel’s lap, and fell asleep. Then the damsel rose, and made a ring with her wimple round the bush, and round Merlin, and began her enchantments, such as he himself had taught her; and nine times she made the ring, and nine times she made the enchantment, and then she went and sat down by him, and placed his head again upon her lap.\n\n“And a sleep Fell upon Merlin more like death, so deep Her finger on her lips; then Vivian rose, And from her brown-locked head the wimple throws, And takes it in her hand and waves it over The blossomed thorn tree and her sleeping lover. Nine times she waved the fluttering wimple round, And made a little plot of magic ground.” —Matthew Arnold.\n\nAnd when he awoke, and looked round him, it seemed to him that he was enclosed in the strongest tower in the world, and laid upon a fair bed. Then said he to the dame: “My lady, you have deceived me, unless you abide with me, for no one hath power to unmake this tower but you alone.” She then promised she would be often there, and in this she held her covenant with him. And Merlin never went out of that tower where his Mistress Viviane had enclosed him; but she entered and went out again when she listed.\n\nAfter this event Merlin was never more known to hold converse with any mortal but Viviane, except on one occasion. Arthur, having for some time missed him from his court, sent several of his knights in search of him, and, among the number, Sir Gawain, who met with a very unpleasant adventure while engaged in this quest. Happening to pass a damsel on his road, and neglecting to salute her, she revenged herself for his incivility by transforming him into a hideous dwarf. He was bewailing aloud his evil fortune as he went through the forest of Brécéliande, when suddenly he heard the voice of one groaning on his right hand; and, looking that way, he could see nothing save a kind of smoke, which seemed like air, and through which he could not pass. Merlin then addressed him from out the smoke, and told him by what misadventure he was imprisoned there. “Ah, sir!” he added, “you will never see me more, and that grieves me, but I cannot remedy it; I shall never more speak to you, nor to any other person, save only my mistress. But do thou hasten to King Arthur, and charge him from me to undertake, without delay, the quest of the Sacred Graal. The knight is already born, and has received knighthood at his hands, who is destined to accomplish this quest.” And after this he comforted Gawain under his transformation, assuring him that he should speedily be disenchanted; and he predicted to him that he should find the king at Carduel, in Wales, on his return, and that all the other knights who had been on like quest would arrive there the same day as himself. And all this came to pass as Merlin had said.\n\nMerlin is frequently introduced in the tales of chivalry, but it is chiefly on great occasions, and at a period subsequent to his death, or magical disappearance. In the romantic poems of Italy, and in Spenser, Merlin is chiefly represented as a magical artist. Spenser represents him as the artificer of the impenetrable shield and other armor of Prince Arthur (“Faery Queene,” Book I., Canto vii.), and of a mirror, in which a damsel viewed her lover’s shade. The Fountain of Love, in the “Orlando Innamorata,” is described as his work; and in the poem of “Ariosto” we are told of a hall adorned with prophetic paintings, which demons had executed in a single night, under the direction of Merlin.\n\nThe following legend is from Spenser’s “Faery Queene,” Book III., Canto iii.:\n\nCAER-MERDIN, OR CAERMARTHEN (IN WALES), MERLIN’S TOWER, AND THE IMPRISONED FIENDS.\n\n“Forthwith themselves disguising both, in straunge And base attire, that none might them bewray, To Maridunum, that is now by chaunge Of name Caer-Merdin called, they took their way: There the wise Merlin whylome wont (they say) To make his wonne, low underneath the ground In a deep delve, far from the view of day, That of no living wight he mote be found, Whenso he counselled with his sprights encompassed round.\n\n“And if thou ever happen that same way To travel, go to see that dreadful place; It is a hideous hollow cave (they say) Under a rock that lies a little space From the swift Barry, tombling down apace Amongst the woody hills of Dynevor; But dare not thou, I charge, in any case, To enter into that same baleful bower, For fear the cruel fiends should thee unwares devour.\n\n“But standing high aloft, low lay thine ear, And there such ghastly noise of iron chains And brazen cauldrons thou shalt rumbling hear, Which thousand sprites with long enduring pains Do toss, that it will stun thy feeble brains; And oftentimes great groans, and grievous stounds, When too huge toil and labor them constrains; And oftentimes loud strokes and ringing sounds From under that deep rock most horribly rebounds.\n\n“The cause some say is this. A little while Before that Merlin died, he did intend A brazen wall in compas to compile About Caermerdin, and did it commend Unto these sprites to bring to perfect end; During which work the Lady of the Lake, Whom long he loved, for him in haste did send; Who, thereby forced his workmen to forsake, Them bound till his return their labor not to slack.\n\n“In the mean time, through that false lady’s train, He was surprised, and buried under beare,[40] Ne ever to his work returned again; Nathless those fiends may not their work forbear, So greatly his commandëment they fear; But there do toil and travail day and night, Until that brazen wall they up do rear. For Merlin had in magic more insight Than ever him before or after living wight.”\n\n————\n\nWE shall begin our history of King Arthur by giving those particulars of his life which appear to rest on historical evidence; and then proceed to record those legends concerning him which form the earliest portion of British literature.\n\nArthur was a prince of the tribe of Britons called Silures, whose country was South Wales, the son of Uther, named Pendragon, a title given to an elective sovereign, paramount over the many kings of Britain. He appears to have commenced his martial career about the year 500, and was raised to the Pendragonship about ten years later. He is said to have gained twelve victories over the Saxons. The most important of them was that of Badon, by some supposed to be Bath, by others Berkshire. This was the last of his battles with the Saxons, and checked their progress so effectually, that Arthur experienced no more annoyance from them, and reigned in peace, until the revolt of his nephew Modred, twenty years later, which led to the fatal battle of Camlan, in Cornwall, in 542. Modred was slain, and Arthur, mortally wounded, was conveyed by sea to Glastonbury, where he died, and was buried. Tradition preserved the memory of the place of his interment within the abbey, as we are told by Giraldus Cambrensis, who was present when the grave was opened by command of Henry II. about 1150, and saw the bones and sword of the monarch, and a leaden cross let into his tombstone, with the inscription in rude Roman letters, “Here lies buried the famous King Arthur, in the island Avalonia.” This story has been elegantly versified by Warton. A popular traditional belief was long entertained among the Britons, that Arthur was not dead, but had been carried off to be healed of his wounds in Fairy-land, and that he would reappear to avenge his countrymen and reinstate them in the sovereignty of Britain. In Warton’s “Ode” a bard relates to King Henry the traditional story of Arthur’s death, and closes with these lines.\n\n“Yet in vain a paynim foe Armed with fate the mighty blow: For when he fell, the Elfin queen, All in secret and unseen, O’er the fainting hero threw Her mantle of ambrosial blue, And bade her spirits bear him far, In Merlin’s agate-axled car, To her green isle’s enamelled steep, Far in the navel of the deep. O’er his wounds she sprinkled dew From flowers that in Arabia grew. . . . . . . . There he reigns a mighty king, Thence to Britain shall return, If right prophetic rolls I learn, Borne on victory’s spreading plume, His ancient sceptre to resume, His knightly table to restore, And brave the tournaments of yore.”\n\nAfter this narration another bard came forward who recited a different story:\n\n“When Arthur bowed his haughty crest, No princess veiled in azure vest Snatched him, by Merlin’s powerful spell, In groves of golden bliss to dwell; But when he fell, with winged speed, His champions, on a milk-white steed, From the battle’s hurricane, Bore him to Joseph’s towered fane,[41] In the fair vale of Avalon; There, with chanted orison And the long blaze of tapers clear, The stoled fathers met the bier; Through the dim aisles, in order dread Of martial woe, the chief they led, And deep entombed in holy ground, Before the altar’s solemn bound.”\n\nIt must not be concealed that the very existence of Arthur has been denied by some. Milton says of him: “As to Arthur, more renowned in songs and romances than in true stories, who he was, and whether ever any such reigned in Britain, hath been doubted heretofore, and may again, with good reason.” Modern critics, however, admit that there was a prince of this name, and find proof of it in the frequent mention of him in the writings of the Welsh bards. But the Arthur of romance, according to Mr. Owen, a Welsh scholar and antiquarian, is a mythological person. “Arthur,” he says, “is the Great Bear, as the name literally implies (Arctos, Arcturus), and perhaps this constellation, being so near the pole, and visibly describing a circle in a small space, is the origin of the famous Round Table.”\n\n# KING ARTHUR\n\nConstans, king of Britain, had three sons, Moines, Ambrosius, otherwise called Uther, and Pendragon. Moines, soon after his accession to the crown, was vanquished by the Saxons, in consequence of the treachery of his seneschal, Vortigern, and growing unpopular, through misfortune, he was killed by his subjects, and the traitor Vortigern chosen in his place.\n\nVortigern was soon after defeated in a great battle by Uther and Pendragon, the surviving brothers of Moines and Pendragon ascended the throne.\n\nThis prince had great confidence in the wisdom of Merlin, and made him his chief adviser. About this time a dreadful war arose between the Saxons and Britons. Merlin obliged the royal brothers to swear fidelity to each other, but predicted that one of them must fall in the first battle. The Saxons were routed, and Pendragon, being slain, was succeeded by Uther, who now assumed in addition to his own name the appellation of Pendragon.\n\nMerlin still continued a favorite counsellor. At the request of Uther he transported by magic art enormous stones from Ireland, to form the sepulchre of Pendragon. These stones constitute the monument now called Stonehenge, on Salisbury plain.\n\nMerlin next proceeded to Carlisle to prepare the Round Table, at which he seated an assemblage of the great nobles of the country. The companions admitted to this high order were bound by oath to assist each other at the hazard of their own lives, to attempt singly the most perilous adventures, to lead, when necessary, a life of monastic solitude, to fly to arms at the first summons, and never to retire from battle till they had defeated the enemy, unless night intervened and separated the combatants.\n\nSoon after this institution, the king invited all his barons to the celebration of a great festival, which he proposed holding annually at Carlisle.\n\nAs the knights had obtained the sovereign’s permission to bring their ladies along with them, the beautiful Igerne accompanied her husband, Gorlois, Duke of Tintadel, to one of these anniversaries. The king became deeply enamoured of the duchess, and disclosed his passion; but Igerne repelled his advances, and revealed his solicitations to her husband. On hearing this, the duke instantly removed from court with Igerne, and without taking leave of Uther. The king complained to his council of this want of duty, and they decided that the duke should be summoned to court, and, if refractory, should be treated as a rebel. As he refused to obey the citation, the king carried war into the estates of his vassal and besieged him in the strong castle of Tintadel. Merlin transformed the king into the likeness of Gorlois, and enabled him to have many stolen interviews with Igerne. At length the duke was killed in battle and the king espoused Igerne.\n\nFrom this union sprang Arthur, who succeeded his father, Uther, upon the throne.\n\n# ARTHUR CHOSEN KING\n\nArthur, though only fifteen years old at his father’s death, was elected king, at a general meeting of the nobles. It was not done without opposition, for there were many ambitious competitors.\n\n“For while he linger’d there A doubt that ever smoulder’d in the hearts Of those great Lords and Barons of his realm Flash’d forth and into war: for most of these Made head against him, crying, ‘Who is he That he should rule us? who hath proven him King Uther’s son? for lo! we look at him, And find nor face nor bearing, limbs nor voice, Are like to those of Uther whom we knew.” —Coming of Arthur.\n\nBut Bishop Brice, a person of great sanctity, on Christmas eve addressed the assembly, and represented that it would well become them, at that solemn season, to put up their prayers for some token which should manifest the intentions of Providence respecting their future sovereign. This was done, and with such success, that the service was scarcely ended when a miraculous stone was discovered before the church door, and in the stone was firmly fixed a sword, with the following words engraven on its hilt:\n\n“I am hight Escalibore, Unto a king fair tresore.”\n\nBishop Brice, after exhorting the assembly to offer up their thanksgiving for this signal miracle, proposed a law, that whoever should be able to draw out the sword from the stone, should be acknowledged as sovereign of the Britons; and his proposal was decreed by general acclamation. The tributary kings of Uther, and the most famous knights, successively put their strength to the proof, but the miraculous sword resisted all their efforts. It stood till Candlemas; it stood till Easter, and till Pentecost, when the best knights in the kingdom usually assembled for the annual tournament. Arthur, who was at that time serving in the capacity of squire to his foster-brother, Sir Kay, attended his master to the lists. Sir Kay fought with great valor and success, but had the misfortune to break his sword, and sent Arthur to his mother for a new one. Arthur hastened home, but did not find the lady; but having observed near the church a sword, sticking in a stone, he galloped to the place, drew out the sword with great ease, and delivered it to his master. Sir Kay would willingly have assumed to himself the distinction conferred by the possession of the sword, but when, to confirm the doubters, the sword was replaced in the stone he was utterly unable to withdraw it, and it would yield a second time to no hand but Arthur’s. Thus decisively pointed out by Heaven as their king, Arthur was by general consent proclaimed as such, and an early day appointed for his solemn coronation.\n\nImmediately after his election to the crown, Arthur found himself opposed by eleven kings and one duke, who with a vast army were actually encamped in the forest of Rockingham. By Merlin’s advice Arthur sent an embassy to Brittany, to solicit the aid of King Ban and King Bohort, two of the best knights in the world. They accepted the call, and with a powerful army crossed the sea, landing at Portsmouth, where they were received with great rejoicing. The rebel kings were still superior in numbers; but Merlin, by a powerful enchantment, caused all their tents to fall down at once, and in the confusion Arthur with his allies fell upon them and totally routed them.\n\nAfter defeating the rebels, Arthur took the field against the Saxons. As they were too strong for him unaided, he sent an embassy to Armorica, beseeching the assistance of Hoel, who soon after brought over an army to his aid. The two kings joined their forces, and sought the enemy, whom they met, and both sides prepared for a decisive engagement. “Arthur himself,” as Geoffrey of Monmouth relates, “dressed in a breastplate worthy of so great a king, places on his head a golden helmet engraved with the semblance of a dragon. Over his shoulders he throws his shield called Priwen, on which a picture of the Holy Virgin constantly recalled her to his memory. Girt with Caliburn, a most excellent sword, and fabricated in the isle of Avalon, he graces his right hand with the lance named Ron. This was a long and broad spear, well contrived for slaughter.” After a severe conflict, Arthur, calling on the name of the Virgin, rushes into the midst of his enemies, and destroys multitudes of them with the formidable Caliburn, and puts the rest to flight. Hoel, being detained by sickness, took no part in this battle.\n\nThis is called the victory of Mount Badon, and, however disguised by fable, it is regarded by historians as a real event.\n\nThe feats performed by Arthur at the battle of Badon Mount are thus celebrated in Drayton’s verse:\n\n“They sung how he himself at Badon bore, that day, When at the glorious goal his British sceptre lay; Two daies together how the battel stronglie stood; Pendragon’s worthie son, who waded there in blood, Three hundred Saxons slew with his owne valiant hand.” —Song IV.\n\nMerlin had planned for Arthur a marriage with the daughter of King Laodegan of Carmalide. By his advice Arthur paid a visit to the court of that sovereign, attended only by Merlin and by thirty-nine knights whom the magician had selected for that service. On their arrival they found Laodegan and his peers sitting in council, endeavoring, but with small prospect of success, to devise means of resisting the impending attack of Ryence, king of Ireland, who, with fifteen tributary kings and an almost innumerable army, had nearly surrounded the city. Merlin, who acted as leader of the band of British knights, announced them as strangers, who came to offer the king their services in his wars; but under the express condition that they should be at liberty to conceal their names and quality until they should think proper to divulge them. These terms were thought very strange, but were thankfully accepted, and the strangers, after taking the usual oath to the king, retired to the lodging which Merlin had prepared for them.\n\nA few days after this, the enemy, regardless of a truce into which they had entered with King Laodegan, suddenly issued from their camp and made an attempt to surprise the city. Cleodalis, the king’s general, assembled the royal forces with all possible despatch. Arthur and his companions also flew to arms, and Merlin appeared at their head, bearing a standard on which was emblazoned a terrific dragon. Merlin advanced to the gate, and commanded the porter to open it, which the porter refused to do, without the king’s order. Merlin thereupon took up the gate, with all its appurtenances of locks, bars, bolts, etc., and directed his troops to pass through, after which he replaced it in perfect order. He then set spurs to his horse and dashed, at the head of his little troop, into a body of two thousand pagans. The disparity of numbers being so enormous, Merlin cast a spell upon the enemy, so as to prevent their seeing the small number of their assailants; notwithstanding which the British knights were hard pressed. But the people of the city, who saw from the walls this unequal contest, were ashamed of leaving the small body of strangers to their fate, so they opened the gate and sallied forth. The numbers were now more nearly equal, and Merlin revoked his spell, so that the two armies encountered on fair terms. Where Arthur, Ban, Bohort, and the rest fought the king’s army had the advantage; but in another part of the field the king himself was surrounded and carried off by the enemy. The sad sight was seen by Guenever, the fair daughter of the king, who stood on the city wall and looked at the battle. She was in dreadful distress, tore her hair, and swooned away.\n\nBut Merlin, aware of what passed in every part of the field, suddenly collected his knights, led them out of the battle, intercepted the passage of the party who were carrying away the king, charged them with irresistible impetuosity, cut in pieces or dispersed the whole escort, and rescued the king. In the fight Arthur encountered Caulang, a giant fifteen feet high, and the fair Guenever, who had already began to feel a strong interest in the handsome young stranger, trembled for the issue of the contest. But Arthur, dealing a dreadful blow on the shoulder of the monster, cut through his neck so that his head hung over on one side, and in this condition his horse carried him about the field, to the great horror and dismay of the Pagans. Guenever could not refrain from expressing aloud her wish that the gentle knight, who dealt with giants so dexterously, were destined to become her husband, and the wish was echoed by her attendants. The enemy soon turned their backs and fled with precipitation, closely pursued by Laodegan and his allies.\n\nAfter the battle Arthur was disarmed and conducted to the bath by the princess Guenever, while his friends were attended by the other ladies of the court. After the bath the knights were conducted to a magnificent entertainment, at which they were diligently served by the same fair attendants. Laodegan, more and more anxious to know the name and quality of his generous deliverers, and occasionally forming a secret wish that the chief of his guests might be captivated by the charms of his daughter, appeared silent and pensive, and was scarcely roused from his reverie by the banters of his courtiers. Arthur, having had an opportunity of explaining to Guenever his great esteem for her merit, was in the joy of his heart, and was still further delighted by hearing from Merlin the late exploits of Gawain at London, by means of which his immediate return to his dominions was rendered unnecessary, and he was left at liberty to protract his stay at the court of Laodegan. Every day contributed to increase the admiration of the whole court for the gallant strangers, and the passion of Guenever for their chief; and when at last Merlin announced to the king that the object of the visit of the party was to procure a bride for their leader, Laodegan at once presented Guenever to Arthur, telling him that, whatever might be his rank, his merit was sufficient to entitle him to the possession of the heiress of Carmalide.\n\n“And could he find a woman in her womanhood As great as he was in his manhood— The twain together might change the world.” —Guinevere.\n\nArthur accepted the lady with the utmost gratitude, and Merlin then proceeded to satisfy the king of the rank of his son-in-law; upon which Laodegan, with all his barons, hastened to do homage to their lawful sovereign, the successor of Uther Pendragon. The fair Guenever was then solemnly betrothed to Arthur, and a magnificent festival was proclaimed, which lasted seven days. At the end of that time, the enemy appearing again with renewed force, it became necessary to resume military operations.[42]\n\nWe must now relate what took place at and near London, while Arthur was absent from his capital. At this very time a band of young heroes were on their way to Arthur’s court, for the purpose of receiving knighthood from him. They were Gawain and his three brothers, nephews of Arthur, sons of King Lot, and Galachin, another nephew, son of King Nanters. King Lot had been one of the rebel chiefs whom Arthur had defeated, but he now hoped by means of the young men to be reconciled to his brother-in-law. He equipped his sons and his nephew with the utmost magnificence, giving them a splendid retinue of young men, sons of earls and barons, all mounted on the best horses, with complete suits of choice armor. They numbered in all seven hundred, but only nine had yet received the order of knighthood; the rest were candidates for that honor, and anxious to earn it by an early encounter with the enemy. Gawain, the leader, was a knight of wonderful strength; but what was most remarkable about him was that his strength was greater at certain hours of the day than at others. From nine o’clock till noon his strength was doubled, and so it was from three to evensong; for the rest of the time it was less remarkable, though at all times surpassing that of ordinary men.\n\nAfter a march of three days they arrived in the vicinity of London, where they expected to find Arthur and his court, and very unexpectedly fell in with a large convoy belonging to the enemy, consisting of numerous carts and wagons, all loaded with provisions, and escorted by three thousand men, who had been collecting spoil from all the country round. A single charge from Gawain’s impetuous cavalry was sufficient to disperse the escort and recover the convoy, which was instantly despatched to London. But before long a body of seven thousand fresh soldiers advanced to the attack of the five princes and their little army. Gawain, singling out a chief named Choas, of gigantic size, began the battle by splitting him from the crown of the head to the breast. Galachin encountered King Sanagran, who was also very huge, and cut off his head. Agrivain and Gahariet also performed prodigies of valor. Thus they kept the great army of assailants at bay, though hard pressed, till of a sudden they perceived a strong body of the citizens advancing from London, where the convoy which had been recovered by Gawain had arrived, and informed the mayor and citizens of the danger of their deliverer. The arrival of the Londoners soon decided the contest. The enemy fled in all directions, and Gawain and his friends, escorted by the grateful citizens, entered London, and were received with acclamations.\n\n————\n\nARTHUR (Continued)\nAFTER the great victory of Mount Badon, by which the Saxons were for the time effectually put down, Arthur turned his arms against the Scots and Picts, whom he routed at Lake Lomond, and compelled to sue for mercy. He then went to York to keep his Christmas, and employed himself in restoring the Christian churches which the Pagans had rifled and overthrown. The following summer he conquered Ireland, and then made a voyage with his fleet to Iceland, which he also subdued. The kings of Gothland and of the Orkneys came voluntarily and made their submission, promising to pay tribute. Then he returned to Britain, where, having established the kingdom, he dwelt twelve years in peace.\n\nDuring this time he invited over to him all persons whatsoever that were famous for valor in foreign nations, and augmented the number of his domestics, and introduced such politeness into his court as people of the remotest countries thought worthy of their imitation. So that there was not a nobleman who thought himself of any consideration unless his clothes and arms were made in the same fashion as those of Arthur’s knights.\n\nFinding himself so powerful at home, Arthur began to form designs for extending his power abroad. So, having prepared his fleet, he first attempted Norway, that he might procure the crown of it for Lot, his sister’s husband. Arthur landed in Norway, fought a great battle with the king of that country, defeated him, and pursued the victory till he had reduced the whole country under his dominion, and established Lot upon the throne. Then Arthur made a voyage to Gaul and laid siege to the city of Paris. Gaul was at that time a Roman province, and governed by Flollo, the Tribune. When the siege of Paris had continued a month, and the people began to suffer from famine, Flollo challenged Arthur to single combat, proposing to decide the conquest of the province in that way. Arthur gladly accepted the challenge, and slew his adversary in the contest, upon which the citizens surrendered the city to him. After the victory Arthur divided his army into two parts, one of which he committed to the conduct of Hoel, whom he ordered to march into Aquitaine, while he with the other part should endeavor to subdue the other provinces. At the end of nine years, in which time all the parts of Gaul were entirely reduced, Arthur returned to Paris, where he kept his court, and, calling an assembly of the clergy and people, established peace and the just administration of the laws in that kingdom. Then he bestowed Normandy upon Bedver, his butler, and the province of Andegavia upon Kay, his steward,[43] and several other provinces upon his great men that attended him. And, having settled the peace of the cities and countries, he returned back in the beginning of spring to Britain.\n\nUpon the approach of the feast of Pentecost, Arthur, the better to demonstrate his joy after such triumphant successes, and for the more solemn observation of that festival, and reconciling the minds of the princes that were now subject to him, resolved during that season to hold a magnificent court, to place the crown upon his head, and to invite all the kings and dukes under his subjection to the solemnity. And he pitched upon Caerleon, the City of Legions, as the proper place for his purpose. For, besides its great wealth above the other cities,[44] its situation upon the river Usk, near the Severn sea, was most pleasant and fit for so great a solemnity. For on one side it was washed by that noble river, so that the kings and princes from the countries beyond the seas might have the convenience of sailing up to it. On the other side the beauty of the meadows and groves, and magnificence of the royal palaces, with lofty gilded roofs that adorned it, made it even rival the grandeur of Rome. It was also famous for two churches, whereof one was adorned with a choir of virgins, who devoted themselves wholly to the service of God, and the other maintained a convent of priests. Besides, there was a college of two hundred philosophers, who, being learned in astronomy and the other arts, were diligent in observing the courses of the stars, and gave Arthur true predictions of the events that would happen. In this place, therefore, which afforded such delights, were preparations made for the ensuing festival.\n\nAmbassadors were then sent into several kingdoms, to invite to court the princes both of Gaul and of the adjacent islands. Accordingly there came Augusel, king of Albania, now Scotland, Cadwallo, king of Venedotia, now North Wales, Sater, king of Demetia, now South Wales; also the archbishops of the metropolitan sees, London and York, and Dubricius, bishop of Caerleon, the City of Legions. This prelate, who was primate of Britain, was so eminent for his piety that he could cure any sick person by his prayers. There were also the counts of the principal cities, and many other worthies of no less dignity.\n\nFrom the adjacent islands came Guillamurius, king of Ireland, Gunfasius, king of the Orkneys, Malvasius, king of Iceland, Lot, king of Norway, Bedver, the butler, Duke of Normandy, Kay, the sewer, Duke of Andegavia; also the twelve peers of Gaul, and Hoel, Duke of the Armorican Britons, with his nobility, who came with such a train of mules, horses, and rich furniture as it is difficult to describe. Besides these there remained no prince of any consideration on this side of Spain who came not upon this invitation. And no wonder, when Arthur’s munificence, which was celebrated over the whole world, made him beloved by all people.\n\nWhen all were assembled upon the day of the solemnity the archbishops were conducted to the palace, in order to place the crown upon the king’s head. Then Dubricius, inasmuch as the court was held in his diocese, made himself ready to celebrate the office. As soon as the king was invested with his royal habiliments he was conducted in great pomp to the metropolitan church, having four kings, viz., of Albania, Cornwall, Demetia, and Venedotia, bearing four golden swords before him. On another part was the queen, dressed out in her richest ornaments, conducted by the archbishops and bishops to the Church of Virgins; the four queens, also, of the kings last mentioned, bearing before her four white doves, according to ancient custom. When the whole procession was ended so transporting was the harmony of the musical instruments and voices, whereof there was a vast variety in both churches, that the knights who attended were in doubt which to prefer, and therefore crowded from the one to the other by turns, and were far from being tired of the solemnity, though the whole day had been spent in it. At last, when divine service was over at both churches, the king and queen put off their crowns, and, putting on their lighter ornaments, went to the banquet. When they had all taken their seats according to precedence, Kay, the sewer, in rich robes of ermine, with a thousand young noblemen all in like manner clothed in rich attire, served up the dishes. From another part Bedver, the butler, was followed by the same number of attendants, who waited with all kinds of cups and drinking-vessels. And there was food and drink in abundance, and everything was of the best kind, and served in the best manner. For at that time Britain had arrived at such a pitch of grandeur that in riches, luxury, and politeness it far surpassed all other kingdoms.\n\nAs soon as the banquets were over they went into the fields without the city to divert themselves with various sports, such as shooting with bows and arrows, tossing the pike, casting of heavy stones and rocks, playing at dice, and the like, and all these inoffensively, and without quarrelling. In this manner were three days spent, and after that they separated, and the kings and noblemen departed to their several homes.\n\nAfter this Arthur reigned five years in peace. Then came ambassadors from Lucius Tiberius, Procurator under Leo, Emperor of Rome, demanding tribute. But Arthur refused to pay tribute, and prepared for war. As soon as the necessary dispositions were made he committed the government of his kingdom to his nephew Modred and to Queen Guenever, and marched with his army to Hamo’s Port, where the wind stood fair for him. The army crossed over in safety, and landed at the mouth of the river Barba. And there they pitched their tents to wait the arrival of the kings of the islands.\n\nAs soon as all the forces were arrived Arthur marched forward to Augustodunum, and encamped on the banks of the river Alba. Here repeated battles were fought, in all which the Britons, under their valiant leaders, Hoel, Duke of Armorica, and Gawain, nephew to Arthur, had the advantage. At length Lucius Tiberius determined to retreat, and wait for the Emperor Leo to join him with fresh troops. But Arthur, anticipating this event, took possession of a certain valley, and closed up the way of retreat to Lucius, compelling him to fight a decisive battle, in which Arthur lost some of the bravest of his knights and most faithful followers. But on the other hand Lucius Tiberius was slain, and his army totally defeated. The fugitives dispersed over the country, some to the by-ways and woods, some to cities and towns, and all other places where they could hope for safety.\n\nArthur stayed in those parts till the next winter was over, and employed his time in restoring order and settling the government. He then returned into England, and celebrated his victories with great splendor.\n\nThen the king stablished all his knights, and to them that were not rich he gave lands, and charged them all never to do outrage nor murder, and always to flee treason; also, by no means to be cruel, but to give mercy unto him that asked mercy, upon pain of forfeiture of their worship and lordship; and always to do ladies, damosels, and gentlewomen service, upon pain of death. Also that no man take battle in a wrongful quarrel, for no law, nor for any world’s goods. Unto this were all the knights sworn of the Table Round, both old and young. And at every year were they sworn at the high feast of Pentecost.\n\n# KING ARTHUR SLAYS THE GIANT OF ST. MICHAEL’S MOUNT\n\nWhile the army was encamped in Brittany, awaiting the arrival of the kings, there came a countryman to Arthur, and told him that a giant, whose cave was on a neighboring mountain, called St. Michael’s Mount, had for a long time been accustomed to carry off the children of the peasants to devour them. “And now he hath taken the Duchess of Brittany, as she rode with her attendants, and hath carried her away in spite of all they could do.” “Now, fellow,” said King Arthur, “canst thou bring me there where this giant haunteth?” “Yea, sure,” said the good man; “lo, yonder where thou seest two great fires, there shalt thou find him, and more treasure than I suppose is in all France beside.” Then the king called to him Sir Bedver and Sir Kay, and commanded them to make ready horse and harness for himself and them; for after evening he would ride on pilgrimage to St. Michael’s Mount.\n\nSo they three departed, and rode forth till they came to the foot of the mount. And there the king commanded them to tarry, for he would himself go up into that mount. So he ascended the hill till he came to a great fire, and there he found an aged woman sitting by a new-made grave, making great sorrow. Then King Arthur saluted her, and demanded of her wherefore she made such lamentation; to whom she answered: “Sir knight, speak low, for yonder is a devil, and if he hear thee speak, he will come and destroy thee. For ye cannot make resistance to him, he is so fierce and so strong. He hath murdered the Duchess, which here lieth, who was the fairest of all the world, wife to Sir Hoel, Duke of Brittany.” “Dame,” said the king, “I come from the noble conqueror, King Arthur, to treat with that tyrant.” “Fie on such treaties,” said she; “he setteth not by the king, nor by no man else.” “Well,” said Arthur, “I will accomplish my message for all your fearful words.” So he went forth by the crest of the hill, and saw where the giant sat at supper, gnawing on the limb of a man, and baking his broad limbs at the fire, and three fair damsels lying bound, whose lot it was to be devoured in their turn. When King Arthur beheld that, he had great compassion on them, so that his heart bled for sorrow. Then he hailed the giant, saying, “He that all the world ruleth give thee short life and shameful death. Why hast thou murdered this Duchess? Therefore come forth, for this day thou shalt die by my hand.” Then the giant started up, and took a great club, and smote at the king, and smote off his coronal; and then the king struck him in the belly with his sword, and made a fearful wound. Then the giant threw away his club, and caught the king in his arms, so that he crushed his ribs. Then the three maidens kneeled down and prayed for help and comfort for Arthur. And Arthur weltered and wrenched, so that he was one while under, and another time above. And so weltering and wallowing they rolled down the hill, and ever as they weltered Arthur smote him with his dagger; and it fortuned they came to the place where the two knights were. And when they saw the king fast in the giant’s arms they came and loosed him. Then the king commanded Sir Kay to smite off the giant’s head, and to set it on the truncheon of a spear, and fix it on the barbican, that all the people might see and behold it. This was done, and anon it was known through all the country, wherefor the people came and thanked the king. And he said, “Give your thanks to God; and take ye the giant’s spoil and divide it among you.” And King Arthur caused a church to be builded on that hill, in honor of St. Michael.\n\n# KING ARTHUR GETS A SWORD FROM THE LADY OF THE LAKE\n\nOne day King Arthur rode forth, and on a sudden he was ware of three churls chasing Merlin, to have slain him. And the king rode unto them and bade them, “Flee, churls!” Then were they afraid when they saw a knight, and fled. “O Merlin,” said Arthur, “here hadst thou been slain, for all thy crafts, had I not been by.” “Nay,” said Merlin, “not so, for I could save myself if I would; but thou art more near thy death than I am.” So, as they went thus talking, King Arthur perceived where sat a knight on horseback, as if to guard the pass. “Sir knight,” said Arthur, “for what cause abidest thou here?” Then the knight said, “There may no knight ride this way unless he just with me, for such is the custom of the pass.” “I will amend that custom,” said the king. Then they ran together, and they met so hard that their spears were shivered. Then they drew their swords and fought a strong battle, with many great strokes. But at length the sword of the knight smote King Arthur’s sword in two pieces. Then said the knight unto Arthur, “Thou art in my power, whether to save thee or slay thee, and unless thou yield thee as overcome and recreant, thou shalt die.” “As for death,” said King Arthur, “welcome be it when it cometh; but to yield me unto thee as recreant, I will not.” Then he leapt upon the knight, and took him by the middle and threw him down; but the knight was a passing strong man, and anon he brought Arthur under him, and would have razed off his helm to slay him. Then said Merlin, “Knight, hold thy hand, for this knight is a man of more worship than thou art aware of.” “Why, who is he?” said the knight. “It is King Arthur.” Then would he have slain him for dread of his wrath, and lifted up his sword to slay him; and therewith Merlin cast an enchantment on the knight, so that he fell to the earth in a great sleep. Then Merlin took up King Arthur, and set him on his horse. “Alas!” said Arthur, “what hast thou done, Merlin? hast thou slain this good knight by thy crafts?” “Care ye not,” said Merlin; “he is wholer than ye be. He is only asleep, and will wake in three hours.”\n\nThen the king and he departed, and went till they came to a hermit, that was a good man and a great leech. So the hermit searched all his wounds, and applied good salves; and the king was there three days, and then were his wounds well amended, that he might ride and go. So they departed, and as they rode Arthur said, “I have no sword.” “No matter,” said Merlin; “hereby is a sword that shall be yours.” So they rode till they came to a lake, which was a fair water and broad. And in the midst of the lake Arthur was aware of an arm clothed in white samite,[45] that held a fair sword in the hand. “Lo!” said Merlin, “yonder is that sword that I spake of. It belongeth to the Lady of the Lake, and, if she will, thou mayest take it; but if she will not, it will not be in thy power to take it.”\n\nSo Sir Arthur and Merlin alighted from their horses, and went into a boat. And when they came to the sword that the hand held Sir Arthur took it by the handle and took it to him, and the arm and the hand went under the water.\n\nThen they returned unto the land and rode forth. And Sir Arthur looked on the sword and liked it right well.\n\nSo they rode unto Caerleon, whereof his knights were passing glad. And when they heard of his adventures they marvelled that he would jeopard his person so alone. But all men of worship said it was a fine thing to be under such a chieftain as would put his person in adventure as other poor knights did.\n\n————\n\n# SIR GAWAIN\n\nSIR GAWAIN was nephew to King Arthur, by his sister Morgana, married to Lot, king of Orkney, who was by Arthur made king of Norway. Sir Gawain was one of the most famous knights of the Round Table, and is characterized by the romancers as the sage and courteous Gawain. To this Chaucer alludes in his “Squiere’s Tale,” where the strange knight “salueth” all the court\n\n“With so high reverence and observance, As well in speeche as in countenance, That Gawain, with his olde curtesie, Though he were come agen out of faërie, Ne coude him not amenden with a word.”\n\nGawain’s brothers were Agrivain, Gahariet, and Gareth.\n\n# SIR GAWAIN’S MARRIAGE\n\nOnce upon a time King Arthur held his court in merry Carlisle, when a damsel came before him and craved a boon. It was for vengeance upon a caitiff knight, who had made her lover captive and despoiled her of her lands. King Arthur commanded to bring him his sword, Excalibar, and to saddle his steed, and rode forth without delay to right the lady’s wrong. Ere long he reached the castle of the grim baron, and challenged him to the conflict. But the castle stood on magic ground, and the spell was such that no knight could tread thereon but straight his courage fell and his strength decayed. King Arthur felt the charm, and before a blow was struck, his sturdy limbs lost their strength, and his head grew faint. He was fain to yield himself prisoner to the churlish knight, who refused to release him except upon condition that he should return at the end of a year, and bring a true answer to the question, “What thing is it which women most desire?” or in default thereof surrender himself and his lands. King Arthur accepted the terms, and gave his oath to return at the time appointed. During the year the king rode east, and he rode west, and inquired of all whom he met what thing it is which all women most desire. Some told him riches; some, pomp and state; some, mirth; some, flattery; and some, a gallant knight. But in the diversity of answers he could find no sure dependence. The year was well-nigh spent, when one day, as he rode thoughtfully through a forest, he saw sitting beneath a tree a lady of such hideous aspect that he turned away his eyes, and when she greeted him in seemly sort, made no answer. “What wight art thou,” the lady said, “that will not speak to me? It may chance that I may resolve thy doubts, though I be not fair of aspect.” “If thou wilt do so,” said King Arthur, “choose what reward thou wilt, thou grim lady, and it shall be given thee.” “Swear me this upon thy faith,” she said, and Arthur swore it. Then the lady told him the secret, and demanded her reward, which was that the king should find some fair and courtly knight to be her husband.\n\nKing Arthur hastened to the grim baron’s castle and told him one by one all the answers which he had received from his various advisers, except the last, and not one was admitted as the true one. “Now yield thee, Arthur,” the giant said, “for thou hast not paid thy ransom, and thou and thy lands are forfeited to me.” Then King Arthur said:\n\n“Yet hold thy hand, thou proud baron, I pray thee hold thy hand, And give me leave to speak once more, In rescue of my land. This morn as I came over a moor, I saw a lady set, Between an oak and a green holly, All clad in red scarlett. She says all women would have their will, This is their chief desire; Now yield, as thou art a baron true, That I have paid my hire.”\n\n“It was my sister that told thee this,” the churlish baron exclaimed. “Vengeance light on her! I will some time or other do her as ill a turn.”\n\nKing Arthur rode homeward, but not light of heart, for he remembered the promise he was under to the loathly lady to give her one of his young and gallant knights for a husband. He told his grief to Sir Gawain, his nephew, and he replied, “Be not sad, my lord, for I will marry the loathly lady.” King Arthur replied:\n\n“Now nay, now nay, good Sir Gawaine, My sister’s son ye be; The loathly lady’s all too grim, And all too foule for thee.”\n\nBut Gawain persisted, and the king at last, with sorrow of heart, consented that Gawain should be his ransom. So one day the king and his knights rode to the forest, met the loathly lady, and brought her to the court. Sir Gawain stood the scoffs and jeers of his companions as he best might, and the marriage was solemnized, but not with the usual festivities. Chaucer tells us:\n\n“. . . There was no joye ne feste at alle; There n’ as but hevinesse and mochel sorwe, For prively he wed her on the morwe, And all day after hid him as an owle, So we was him his wife loked so foule!”[46]\n\nWhen night came, and they were alone together, Sir Gawain could not conceal his aversion; and the lady asked him why he sighed so heavily, and turned away his face. He candidly confessed it was on account of three things, her age, her ugliness, and her low degree. The lady, not at all offended, replied with excellent arguments to all his objections. She showed him that with age is discretion, with ugliness security from rivals, and that all true gentility depends, not upon the accident of birth, but upon the character of the individual.\n\nSir Gawain made no reply; but, turning his eyes on his bride, what was his amazement to perceive that she wore no longer the unseemly aspect that had so distressed him. She then told him that the form she had worn was not her true form, but a disguise imposed upon her by a wicked enchanter, and that she was condemned to wear it until two things should happen: one, that she should obtain some young and gallant knight to be her husband. This having been done, one-half of the charm was removed. She was now at liberty to wear her true form for half the time, and she bade him choose whether he would have her fair by day, and ugly by night, or the reverse. Sir Gawain would fain have had her look her best by night, when he alone would see her, and show her repulsive visage, if at all, to others. But she reminded him how much more pleasant it would be to her to wear her best looks in the throng of knights and ladies by day. Sir Gawain yielded, and gave up his will to hers. This alone was wanting to dissolve the charm. The lovely lady now with joy assured him that she should change no more, but as she now was, so would she remain by night as well as by day.\n\n“Sweet blushes stayned her rud-red cheek, Her eyen were black as sloe, The ripening cherrye swelled her lippe, And all her neck was snow. Sir Gawain kist that ladye faire Lying upon the sheete, And swore, as he was a true knight, The spice was never so swete.”\n\nThe dissolution of the charm which had held the lady also released her brother, the “grim baron,” for he too had been implicated in it. He ceased to be a churlish oppressor, and became a gallant and generous knight as any at Arthur’s court.\n\n————\n\nCARADOC was the son of Ysenne, the beautiful niece of Arthur. He was ignorant who his father was, till it was discovered in the following manner: When the youth was of proper years to receive the honors of knighthood, King Arthur held a grand court for the purpose of knighting him. On this occasion a strange knight presented himself, and challenged the knights of Arthur’s court to exchange blow for blow with him. His proposal was this—to lay his neck on a block for any knight to strike, on condition that, if he survived the blow, the knight should submit in turn to the same experiment. Sir Kay, who was usually ready to accept all challenges, pronounced this wholly unreasonable, and declared that he would not accept it for all the wealth in the world. And when the knight offered his sword, with which the operation was to be performed, no person ventured to accept it, till Caradoc, growing angry at the disgrace which was thus incurred by the Round Table, threw aside his mantle and took it. “Do you do this as one of the best knights?” said the stranger. “No,” he replied, “but as one of the most foolish.” The stranger lays his head upon the block, receives a blow which sends it rolling from his shoulders, walks after it, picks it up, replaces it with great success, and says he will return when the court shall be assembled next year, and claim his turn. When the anniversary arrived, both parties were punctual to their engagement. Great entreaties were used by the king and queen, and the whole court, in behalf of Caradoc, but the stranger was inflexible. The young knight laid his head upon the block, and more than once desired him to make an end of the business, and not keep him longer in so disagreeable a state of expectation. At last the stranger strikes him gently with the side of the sword, bids him rise, and reveals to him the fact that he is his father, the enchanter Eliaures, and that he gladly owns him for a son, having proved his courage and fidelity to his word.\n\nBut the favor of enchanters is short-lived and uncertain. Eliaures fell under the influence of a wicked woman, who, to satisfy her pique against Caradoc, persuaded the enchanter to fasten on his arm a serpent, which remained there sucking at his flesh and blood, no human skill sufficing either to remove the reptile or alleviate the torments which Caradoc endured.\n\nCaradoc was betrothed to Guimier, sister to his bosom friend, Cador, and daughter to the king of Cornwall. As soon as they were informed of his deplorable condition, they set out for Nantes, where Caradoc’s castle was, that Guimier might attend upon him. When Caradoc heard of their coming, his first emotion was that of joy and love. But soon he began to fear that the sight of his emaciated form, and of his sufferings, would disgust Guimier; and this apprehension became so strong, that he departed secretly from Nantes, and hid himself in a hermitage. He was sought far and near by the knights of Arthur’s court, and Cador made a vow never to desist from the quest till he should have found him. After long wandering, Cador discovered his friend in the hermitage, reduced almost to a skeleton, and apparently near his death. All other means of relief having already been tried in vain, Cador at last prevailed on the enchanter Eliaures to disclose the only method which could avail for his rescue. A maiden must be found, his equal in birth and beauty, and loving him better than herself, so that she would expose herself to the same torment to deliver him. Two vessels were then to be provided, the one filled with sour wine, and the other with milk. Caradoc must enter the first, so that the wine should reach his neck, and the maiden must get into the other, and, exposing her bosom upon the edge of the vessel, invite the serpent to forsake the withered flesh of his victim for this fresh and inviting food. The vessels were to be placed three feet apart, and as the serpent crossed from one to the other, a knight was to cut him in two. If he failed in his blow, Caradoc would indeed be delivered, but it would be only to see his fair champion suffering the same cruel and hopeless torment. The sequel may be easily foreseen. Guimier willingly exposed herself to the perilous adventure, and Cador, with a lucky blow, killed the serpent. The arm in which Caradoc had suffered so long recovered its strength, but not its shape, in consequence of which he was called Caradoc Briefbras, Caradoc of the Shrunken Arm.\nCaradoc and Guimier are the hero and heroine of the ballad of the “Boy and the Mantle,” which follows:\n\n“In Carlisle dwelt King Arthur, A prince of passing might, And there maintained his Table Round, Beset with many a knight.\n“And there he kept his Christmas, With mirth and princely cheer, When lo! a strange and cunning boy Before him did appear.\n\n“A kirtle and a mantle This boy had him upon, With brooches, rings, and ouches, Full daintily bedone.\n\n“He had a sash of silk About his middle meet; And thus with seemly curtesie He did King Arthur greet:\n“‘God speed thee, brave King Arthur. Thus feasting in thy bower, And Guenever, thy goodly queen, That fair and peerless flower.\n\n“‘Ye gallant lords and lordlings, I wish you all take heed, Lest what ye deem a blooming rose Should prove a cankered weed.’\n“Then straightway from his bosom A little wand he drew; And with it eke a mantle, Of wondrous shape and hue.\n\n“Now have thou here, King Arthur, Have this here of me, And give unto thy comely queen, All shapen as you see.\n“‘No wife it shall become, That once hath been to blame.’ Then every knight in Arthur’s court Sly glanced at his dame.\n\n“And first came Lady Guenever, The mantle she must try. This dame she was new-fangled,[47] And of a roving eye.\n“When she had taken the mantle, And all with it was clad, From top to toe it shivered down, As though with shears beshred.\n\n“One while it was too long, Another while too short, And wrinkled on her shoulders, In most unseemly sort.\n“Now green, now red it seemed, Then all of sable hue; ‘Beshrew me,’ quoth King Arthur, ‘I think thou be’st not true!’\n\n“Down she threw the mantle, No longer would she stay; But, storming like a fury, To her chamber flung away.\n“She cursed the rascal weaver, That had the mantle wrought; And doubly cursed the froward imp Who thither had it brought.\n\n“‘I had rather live in deserts, Beneath the greenwood tree, Than here, base king, among thy grooms The sport of them and thee.’\n“Sir Kay called forth his lady, And bade her to come near: ‘Yet dame, if thou be guilty, I pray thee now forbear.’\n\n“This lady, pertly giggling, With forward step came on, And boldly to the little boy With fearless face is gone.\n“When she had taken the mantle, With purpose for to wear, It shrunk up to her shoulder, And left her back all bare.\n\n“Then every merry knight, That was in Arthur’s court, Gibed and laughed and flouted, To see that pleasant sport.\n“Down she threw the mantle, No longer bold or gay, But, with a face all pale and wan, To her chamber slunk away.\n\n“Then forth came an old knight A pattering o’er his creed, And proffered to the little boy Five nobles to his meed:\n“‘And all the time of Christmas Plum-porridge shall be thine, If thou wilt let my lady fair Within the mantle shine.’\n\n“A saint his lady seemed, With step demure and slow, And gravely to the mantle With mincing face doth go.\n“When she the same had taken That was so fine and thin, It shrivelled all about her, And showed her dainty skin.\n\n“Ah! little did her mincing, Or his long prayers bestead; She had no more hung on her Than a tassel and a thread.\n“Down she threw the mantle, With terror and dismay, And with a face of scarlet To her chamber hied away.\n\n“Sir Cradock called his lady, And bade her to come near: ‘Come win this mantle, lady, And do me credit here:\n“‘Come win this mantle, lady, For now it shall be thine, If thou hast never done amiss, Since first I made thee mine.’\n\n“The lady, gently blushing, With modest grace came on; And now to try the wondrous charm Courageously is gone.\n“When she had ta’en the mantle, And put it on her back, About the hem it seemed To wrinkle and to crack.\n\n“‘Lie still,’ she cried, ‘O mantle! And shame me not for naught; I’ll freely own whate’er amiss Or blameful I have wrought.\n“‘Once I kissed Sir Cradock Beneath the greenwood tree; Once I kissed Sir Cradock’s mouth, Before he married me.’\n\n“When she had thus her shriven, And her worst fault had told, The mantle soon became her, Right comely as it should.\n“Most rich and fair of color, Like gold it glittering shone, And much the knights in Arthur’s court Admired her every one.”\n\nThe ballad goes on to tell of two more trials of a similar kind, made by means of a boar’s head and a drinking horn, in both of which the result was equally favorable with the first to Sir Cradock and his lady. It then concludes as follows:\n“Thus boar’s head, horn, and mantle Were this fair couple’s meed; And all such constant lovers, God send them well to speed.” —Percy’s Reliques.\n\n# LAUNCELOT OF THE LAKE\n\nKING BAN, of Brittany, the faithful ally of Arthur was attacked by his enemy Claudas, and after a long war saw himself reduced to the possession of a single fortress, where he was besieged by his enemy. In this extremity he determined to solicit the assistance of Arthur, and escaped in a dark night, with his wife Helen and his infant son Launcelot, leaving his castle in the hands of his seneschal, who immediately surrendered the place to Claudas. The flames of his burning citadel reached the eyes of the unfortunate monarch during his flight and he expired with grief. The wretched Helen, leaving her child on the brink of a lake, flew to receive the last sighs of her husband, and on returning perceived the little Launcelot in the arms of a nymph, who, on the approach of the queen, threw herself into the lake with the child. This nymph was Viviane, mistress of the enchanter Merlin, better known by the name of the Lady of the Lake. Launcelot received his appellation from having been educated at the court of this enchantress, whose palace was situated in the midst, not of a real, but, like the appearance which deceives the African traveller, of an imaginary lake, whose deluding resemblance served as a barrier to her residence. Here she dwelt not alone, but in the midst of a numerous retinue, and a splendid court of knights and damsels.\n\nThe queen, after her double loss, retired to a convent, where she was joined by the widow of Bohort, for this good king had died of grief on hearing of the death of his brother Ban. His two sons, Lionel and Bohort, were rescued by a faithful knight, and arrived in the shape of greyhounds at the palace of the lake, where, having resumed their natural form, they were educated along with their cousin Launcelot.\n\nThe fairy, when her pupil had attained the age of eighteen, conveyed him to the court of Arthur for the purpose of demanding his admission to the honor of knighthood; and at the first appearance of the youthful candidate the graces of his person, which were not inferior to his courage and skill in arms, made an instantaneous and indelible impression on the heart of Guenever, while her charms inspired him with an equally ardent and constant passion. The mutual attachment of these lovers exerted, from that time forth, an influence over the whole history of Arthur. For the sake of Guenever, Launcelot achieved the conquest of Northumberland, defeated Gallehaut, King of the Marches, who afterwards became his most faithful friend and ally, exposed himself in numberless encounters, and brought hosts of prisoners to the feet of his sovereign.\n\n# SIR LAUNCELOT\n\nAfter King Arthur was come from Rome into England all the knights of the Table Round resorted unto him and made him many justs and tournaments. And in especial Sir Launcelot of the Lake in all tournaments and justs and deeds of arms, both for life and death, passed all other knights, and was never overcome, except it were by treason or enchantment; and he increased marvellously in worship, wherefore Queen Guenever had him in great favor, above all other knights. And for certain he loved the queen again above all other ladies; and for her he did many deeds of arms, and saved her from peril, through his noble chivalry. Thus Sir Launcelot rested him long with play and game, and then he thought to prove himself in strange adventures; so he bade his nephew, Sir Lionel, to make him ready,—“for we two will seek adventures.” So they mounted on their horses, armed at all sights, and rode into a forest, and so into a deep plain. And the weather was hot about noon, and Sir Launcelot had great desire to sleep. Then Sir Lionel espied a great apple-tree that stood by a hedge, and he said: “Brother, yonder is a fair shadow—there may we rest us and our horses.” “It is well said,” replied Sir Launcelot. So they there alighted, and Sir Launcelot laid him down, and his helm under his head, and soon was asleep passing fast. And Sir Lionel waked while he slept. And presently there came three knights riding as fast as ever they might ride, and there followed them but one knight. And Sir Lionel thought he never saw so great a knight before. So within a while this great knight overtook one of those knights, and smote him so that he fell to the earth. Then he rode to the second knight and smote him, and so he did to the third knight. Then he alighted down and bound all the three knights fast with their own bridles. When Sir Lionel saw him do thus, he thought to assay him, and made him ready silently, not to awake Sir Launcelot, and rode after the strong knight, and bade him turn. And the other smote Sir Lionel so hard that horse and man fell to the earth; and then he alighted down and bound Sir Lionel, and threw him across his own horse; and so he served them all four, and rode with them away to his own castle. And when he came there he put them in a deep prison, in which were many more knights in great distress.\n\nNow while Sir Launcelot lay under the apple-tree sleeping, there came by him four queens of great estate. And that the heat should not grieve them, there rode four knights about them, and bare a cloth of green silk on four spears, betwixt them and the sun. And the queens rode on four white mules.\n\nThus as they rode they heard by them a great horse grimly neigh. Then they were aware of a sleeping knight, that lay all armed under an apple-tree; and as the queens looked on his face, they knew it was Sir Launcelot. Then they began to strive for that knight, and each one said she would have him for her love. “We will not strive,” said Morgane le Fay, that was King Arthur’s sister, “for I will put an enchantment upon him, that he shall not wake for six hours, and we will take him away to my castle; and then when he is surely within my hold, I will take the enchantment from him, and then let him choose which of us he will have for his love.” So the enchantment was cast upon Sir Launcelot. And then they laid him upon his shield, and bare him so on horseback between two knights, and brought him unto the castle and laid him in a chamber, and at night they sent him his supper.\n\nAnd on the morning came early those four queens, richly dight, and bade him good morning, and he them again. “Sir knight,” they said, “thou must understand thou art our prisoner; and we know thee well, that thou art Sir Launcelot of the Lake, King Ban’s son, and that thou art the noblest knight living. And we know well that there can no lady have thy love but one, and that is Queen Guenever; and now thou shalt lose her for ever, and she thee; and therefore it behooveth thee now to choose one of us. I am the Queen Morgane le Fay, and here is the Queen of North Wales, and the Queen of Eastland, and the Queen of the Isles. Now choose one of us which thou wilt have, for if thou choose not, in this prison thou shalt die.” “This is a hard case,” said Sir Launcelot, “that either I must die, or else choose one of you; yet had I liever to die in this prison with worship, than to have one of you for my paramour, for ye be false enchantresses.” “Well,” said the queens, “is this your answer, that ye will refuse us.” “Yea, on my life it is,” said Sir Launcelot. Then they departed, making great sorrow.\n\nThen at noon came a damsel unto him with his dinner, and asked him, “What cheer?” “Truly, fair damsel,” said Sir Launcelot, “never so ill.” “Sir,” said she, “if you will be ruled by me, I will help you out of this distress. If ye will promise me to help my father on Tuesday next, who hath made a tournament betwixt him and the king of North Wales; for last Tuesday my father lost the field.” “Fair maiden,” said Sir Launcelot, “tell me what is your father’s name, and then will I give you an answer.” “Sir knight,” she said, “my father is King Bagdemagus.” “I know him well,” said Sir Launcelot, “for a noble king and a good knight; and, by the faith of my body, I will be ready to do your father and you service at that day.”\n\nSo she departed, and came on the next morning early and found him ready, and brought him out of twelve locks, and brought him to his own horse, and lightly he saddled him, and so rode forth.\n\nAnd on the Tuesday next he came to a little wood where the tournament should be. And there were scaffolds and holds, that lords and ladies might look on, and give the prize. Then came into the field the king of North Wales, with eightscore helms, and King Badgemagus came with fourscore helms. And then they couched their spears, and came together with a great dash, and there were overthrown at the first encounter twelve of King Bagdemagus’s party and six of the king of North Wales’s party, and King Bagdemagus’s party had the worse.\n\nWith that came Sir Launcelot of the Lake, and thrust in with his spear in the thickest of the press; and he smote down five knights ere he held his hand; and he smote down the king of North Wales, and he brake his thigh in that fall. And then the knights of the king of North Wales would just no more; and so the gree was given to King Bagdemagus.\n\nAnd Sir Launcelot rode forth with King Bagdemagus unto his castle; and there he had passing good cheer, both with the king and with his daughter. And on the morn he took his leave, and told the king he would go and seek his brother, Sir Lionel, that went from him when he slept. So he departed, and by adventure he came to the same forest where he was taken sleeping. And in the highway he met a damsel riding on a white palfrey, and they saluted each other. “Fair damsel,” said Sir Launcelot, “know ye in this country any adventures?” “Sir knight,” said the damsel, “here are adventures near at hand, if thou durst pursue them.” “Why should I not prove adventures?” said Sir Launcelot, “since for that cause came I hither.” “Sir,” said she, “hereby dwelleth a knight that will not be overmatched for any man I know, except thou overmatch him. His name is Sir Turquine, and, as I understand, he is a deadly enemy of King Arthur, and he has in his prison good knights of Arthur’s court, threescore and more, that he hath won with his own hands.” “Damsel,” said Launcelot, “I pray you bring me unto this knight.” So she told him, “Hereby, within this mile, is his castle, and by it on the left hand is a ford for horses to drink of, and over that ford there groweth a fair tree, and on that tree hang many shields that good knights wielded aforetime, that are now prisoners; and on the tree hangeth a basin of copper and latten, and if thou strike upon that basin thou shalt hear tidings.” And Sir Launcelot departed, and rode as the damsel had shown him, and shortly he came to the ford, and the tree where hung the shields and the basin. And among the shields he saw Sir Lionel’s and Sir Hector’s shields, besides many others of knights that he knew.\n\nThen Sir Launcelot struck on the basin with the butt of his spear; and long he did so, but he saw no man. And at length he was ware of a great knight that drove a horse before him, and across the horse there lay an armed knight bounden. And as they came near, Sir Launcelot thought he should know the captive knight. Then Sir Launcelot saw that it was Sir Gaheris, Sir Gawain’s brother, a knight of the Table Round. “Now, fair knight,” said Sir Launcelot, “put that wounded knight off the horse, and let him rest awhile, and let us two prove our strength. For, as it is told me, thou hast done great despite and shame unto knights of the Round Table, therefore now defend thee.” “If thou be of the Table Round,” said Sir Turquine, “I defy thee and all thy fellowship.” “That is overmuch said,” said Sir Launcelot.\n\nThen they put their spears in the rests, and came together with their horses as fast as they might run. And each smote the other in the middle of their shields, so that their horses fell under them, and the knights were both staggered; and as soon as they could clear their horses they drew out their swords and came together eagerly, and each gave the other many strong strokes, for neither shield nor harness might withstand their strokes. So within a while both had grimly wounds, and bled grievously. Then at the last they were breathless both, and stood leaning upon their swords. “Now, fellow,” said Sir Turquine, “thou art the stoutest man that ever I met with, and best breathed; and so be it thou be not the knight that I hate above all other knights, the knight that slew my brother, Sir Carados, I will gladly accord with thee; and for thy love I will deliver all the prisoners that I have.”\n\n“What knight is he that thou hatest so above others?” “Truly,” said Sir Turquine, “his name is Sir Launcelot of the Lake.” “I am Sir Launcelot of the Lake, King Ban’s son of Benwick, and very knight of the Table Round; and now I defy thee do thy best.” “Ah!” said Sir Turquine, “Launcelot, thou art to me the most welcome that ever was knight; for we shall never part till the one of us be dead.” And then they hurtled together like two wild bulls, rashing and lashing with their swords and shields, so that sometimes they fell, as it were, headlong. Thus they fought two hours and more, till the ground where they fought was all bepurpled with blood.\n\nThen at the last Sir Turquine waxed sore faint, and gave somewhat aback, and bare his shield full low for weariness. That spied Sir Launcelot, and leapt then upon him fiercely as a lion, and took him by the beaver of his helmet, and drew him down on his knees. And he raised off his helm, and smote his neck in sunder.\n\nAnd Sir Gaheris, when he saw Sir Turquine slain, said, “Fair lord, I pray you tell me your name, for this day I say ye are the best knight in the world, for ye have slain this day in my sight the mightiest man and the best knight except you that ever I saw.” “Sir, my name is Sir Launcelot du Lac, that ought to help you of right for King Arthur’s sake, and in especial for Sir Gawain’s sake, your own dear brother. Now I pray you, that ye go into yonder castle, and set free all the prisoners ye find there, for I am sure ye shall find there many knights of the Table Round, and especially my brother Sir Lionel. I pray you greet them all from me, and tell them I bid them take there such stuff as they find; and tell my brother to go unto the court and abide me there, for by the feast of Pentecost I think to be there; but at this time I may not stop, for I have adventures on hand.” So he departed, and Sir Gaheris rode into the castle, and took the keys from the porter, and hastily opened the prison door and let out all the prisoners. There was Sir Kay, Sir Brandeles, and Sir Galynde, Sir Bryan, and Sir Alyduke, Sir Hector de Marys, and Sir Lionel, and many more. And when they saw Sir Gaheris they all thanked him, for they thought, because he was wounded, that he had slain Sir Turquine. “Not so,” said Sir Gaheris; “it was Sir Launcelot that slew him, right worshipfully; I saw it with mine eyes.”\n\nSir Launcelot rode till at nightfall he came to a fair castle, and therein he found an old gentlewoman, who lodged him with good-will, and there he had good cheer for him and his horse. And when time was, his host brought him to a fair chamber over the gate to his bed. Then Sir Launcelot unarmed him, and set his harness by him, and went to bed, and anon he fell asleep. And soon after, there came one on horseback and knocked at the gate in great haste; and when Sir Launcelot heard this, he arose and looked out of the window, and saw by the moonlight three knights riding after that one man, and all three lashed on him with their swords, and that one knight turned on them knightly again and defended himself. “Truly,” said Sir Launcelot, “yonder one knight will I help, for it is shame to see three knights on one.” Then he took his harness and went out at the window by a sheet down to the four knights; and he said aloud, “Turn you knights unto me, and leave your fighting with that knight.” Then the knights left Sir Kay, for it was he they were upon, and turned unto Sir Launcelot, and struck many great strokes at Sir Launcelot, and assailed him on every side. Then Sir Kay addressed him to help Sir Launcelot, but he said, “Nay, sir, I will none of your help; let me alone with them.” So Sir Kay suffered him to do his will, and stood one side. And within six strokes Sir Launcelot had stricken them down.\n\nThen they all cried, “Sir knight, we yield us unto you.” “As to that,” said Sir Launcelot, “I will not take your yielding unto me. If so be ye will yield you unto Sir Kay the Seneschal, I will save your lives, but else not.” “Fair knight,” then they said, “we will do as thou commandest us.” “Then shall ye,” said Sir Launcelot, “on Whitsunday next, go unto the court of King Arthur, and there shall ye yield you unto Queen Guenever, and say that Sir Kay sent you thither to be her prisoners.” “Sir,” they said, “it shall be done, by the faith of our bodies;” and then they swore, every knight upon his sword. And so Sir Launcelot suffered them to depart.\n\nOn the morn Sir Launcelot rose early and left Sir Kay sleeping; and Sir Launcelot took Sir Kay’s armor, and his shield, and armed him, and went to the stable and took his horse, and so he departed. Then soon after arose Sir Kay, and missed Sir Launcelot. And then he espied that he had taken his armor and his horse. “Now, by my faith, I know well,” said Sir Kay, “that he will grieve some of King Arthur’s knights, for they will deem that it is I, and will be bold to meet him. But by cause of his armor I am sure I shall ride in peace.” Then Sir Kay thanked his host and departed.\n\nSir Launcelot rode in a deep forest, and there he saw four knights, under an oak, and they were of Arthur’s court. There was Sir Sagramour le Desirus, and Hector de Marys, and Sir Gawain, and Sir Uwaine. As they spied Sir Launcelot they judged by his arms it had been Sir Kay. “Now, by my faith,” said Sir Sagramour, “I will prove Sir Kay’s might;” and got his spear in his hand, and came towards Sir Launcelot. Therewith Sir Launcelot couched his spear against him, and smote Sir Sagramour so sore that horse and man fell both to the earth. Then said Sir Hector, “Now shall ye see what I may do with him.” But he fared worse than Sir Sagramour, for Sir Launcelot’s spear went through his shoulder and bare him from his horse to the ground. “By my faith,” said Sir Uwaine, “yonder is a strong knight, and I fear he hath slain Sir Kay, and taken his armor.” And therewith Sir Uwaine took his spear in hand, and rode toward Sir Launcelot; and Sir Launcelot met him on the plain and gave him such a buffet that he was staggered, and wist not where he was. “Now see I well,” said Sir Gawain, “that I must encounter with that knight.” Then he adjusted his shield, and took a good spear in his hand, and Sir Launcelot knew him well. Then they let run their horses with all their mights, and each knight smote the other in the middle of his shield. But Sir Gawain’s spear broke, and Sir Launcelot charged so sore upon him that his horse fell over backward. Then Sir Launcelot passed by smiling with himself, and he said, “Good luck be with him that made this spear, for never came a better into my hand.” Then the four knights went each to the other and comforted one another. “What say ye to this adventure,” said Sir Gawain, “that one spear hath felled us all four?” “I dare lay my head it is Sir Launcelot,” said Sir Hector; “I know it by his riding.”\n\nAnd Sir Launcelot rode through many strange countries, till by fortune he came to a fair castle; and as he passed beyond the castle he thought he heard two bells ring. And then he perceived how a falcon came flying over his head, toward a high elm; and she had long lunys[48] about her feet, and she flew unto the elm to take her perch, and the lunys got entangled in the bough; and when she would have taken her flight, she hung by the legs fast, and Sir Launcelot saw how she hung, and beheld the fair falcon entangled, and he was sorry for her. Then came a lady out of the castle and cried aloud, “O Launcelot, Launcelot, as thou art the flower of all knights, help me to get my hawk; for if my hawk be lost, my lord will slay me, he is so hasty.” “What is your lord’s name?” said Sir Launcelot. “His name is Sir Phelot, a knight that belongeth to the king of North Wales.” “Well, fair lady, since ye know my name, and require me of knighthood to help you, I will do what I may to get your hawk; and yet in truth I am an ill climber, and the tree is passing high, and few boughs to help me.” And therewith Sir Launcelot alighted and tied his horse to the tree, and prayed the lady to unarm him. And when he was unarmed, he put off his jerkin, and with might and force he clomb up to the falcon, and tied the lunys to a rotten bough, and threw the hawk down with it; and the lady got the hawk in her hand. Then suddenly there came out of the castle her husband, all armed, and with his naked sword in his hand, and said, “O Knight Launcelot, now have I got thee as I would,” and stood at the boll of the tree to slay him. “Ah, lady!” said Sir Launcelot, “why have ye betrayed me?” “She hath done,” said Sir Phelot, “but as I commanded her; and therefore there is none other way but thine hour is come, and thou must die.” “That were shame unto thee,” said Sir Launcelot; “thou an armed knight to slay a naked man by treason.” “Thou gettest none other grace,” said Sir Phelot, “and therefore help thyself if thou canst.” “Alas!” said Sir Launcelot, “that ever a knight should die weaponless!” And therewith he turned his eyes upward and downward; and over his head he saw a big bough leafless, and he brake it off from the trunk. And then he came lower, and watched how his own horse stood; and suddenly he leapt on the further side of his horse from the knight. Then Sir Phelot lashed at him eagerly, meaning to have slain him. But Sir Launcelot put away the stroke, with the big bough, and smote Sir Phelot therewith on the side of the head, so that he fell down in a swoon to the ground. Then Sir Launcelot took his sword out of his hand and struck his head from the body. Then said the lady, “Alas! why hast thou slain my husband?” “I am not the cause,” said Sir Launcelot, “for with falsehood ye would have slain me, and now it is fallen on yourselves.” Thereupon Sir Launcelot got all his armor, and put it upon him hastily, for fear of more resort, for the knight’s castle was so nigh. And as soon as he might, he took his horse and departed, and thanked God he had escaped that adventure.\n\nAnd two days before the feast of Pentecost, Sir Launcelot came home; and the king and all the court were passing glad of his coming. And when Sir Gawain, Sir Uwaine, Sir Sagramour, and Sir Hector de Marys saw Sir Launcelot in Sir Kay’s armor then they wist well it was he that smote them down, all with one spear. Then there was laughing and merriment among them; and from time to time came all the knights that Sir Turquine had prisoners, and they all honored and worshipped Sir Launcelot. Then Sir Gaheris said, “I saw all the battle from the beginning to the end,” and he told King Arthur all how it was. Then Sir Kay told the king how Sir Launcelot had rescued him, and how he “made the knights yield to me, and not to him.” And there they were, all three, and confirmed it all. “And, by my faith,” said Sir Kay, “because Sir Launcelot took my harness and left me his, I rode in peace, and no man would have to do with me.”\n\nAnd so at that time Sir Launcelot had the greatest name of any knight of the world, and most was he honored of high and low.\n\n# THE ADVENTURE OF THE CART\n\nIT befell in the month of May, Queen Guenever called to her knights of the Table Round, and gave them warning that early upon the morrow she would ride a-maying into the woods and fields beside Westminster; “and I warn you that there be none of you but he be well horsed, and that ye all be clothed in green, either silk or cloth; and I shall bring with me ten ladies, and every knight shall have a lady behind him, and every knight shall have a squire and two yeoman, and all well horsed.”\n\n“For thus it chanced one morn when all the court, Green-suited, but with plumes that mock’d the May, Had been, their wont, a-maying.” —Guinevere.\n\nSo they made them ready; and these were the names of the knights: Sir Kay the Seneschal, Sir Agrivaine, Sir Brandiles, Sir Sagramour le Desirus, Sir Dodynas le Sauvage, Sir Ozanna, Sir Ladynas, Sir Persant of Inde, Sir Ironside, and Sir Pelleas; and these ten knights made them ready, in the freshest manner, to ride with the queen. So upon the morn they took their horses with the queen, and rode a-maying in woods and meadows, as it pleased them, in great joy and delight. Now there was a knight named Maleagans, son to King Brademagus, who loved Queen Guenever passing well, and so had he done long and many years. Now this knight, Sir Maleagans, learned the queen’s purpose, and that she had no men of arms with her but the ten noble knights all arrayed in green for maying; so he prepared him twenty men of arms, and a hundred archers, to take captive the queen and her knights.\n\n“In the merry month of May, In a morn at break of day, With a troop of damsels playing, The Queen, forsooth, went forth a-maying.” —Old Song.\n\nSo when the queen had mayed, and all were bedecked with herbs, mosses, and flowers in the best manner and freshest, right then came out of a wood Sir Maleagans with eightscore men well harnessed, and bade the queen and her knights yield them prisoners. “Traitor knight,” said Queen Guenever, “what wilt thou do? Wilt thou shame thyself? Bethink thee how thou art a king’s son, and a knight of the Table Round, and how thou art about to dishonor all knighthood and thyself?” “Be it as it may,” said Sir Maleagans, “know you well, madam, I have loved you many a year and never till now could I get you to such advantage as I do now; and therefore I will take you as I find you.” Then the ten knights of the Round Table drew their swords, and the other party run at them with their spears, and the ten knights manfully abode them, and smote away their spears. Then they lashed together with swords till several were smitten to the earth. So when the queen saw her knights thus dolefully oppressed, and needs must be slain at the last, then for pity and sorrow she cried, “Sir Maleagans, slay not my noble knights and I will go with you, upon this covenant, that they be led with me wheresoever thou leadest me.” “Madame,” said Maleagans, “for your sake they shall be led with you into my own castle, if that ye will be ruled, and ride with me.” Then Sir Maleagans charged them all that none should depart from the queen, for he dreaded lest Sir Launcelot should have knowledge of what had been done.\n\nThen the queen privily called unto her a page of her chamber that was swiftly horsed, to whom she said, “Go thou when thou seest thy time, and bear this ring unto Sir Launcelot, and pray him as he loveth me, that he will see me and rescue me. And spare not thy horse,” said the queen, “neither for water nor for land.” So the child espied his time, and lightly he took his horse with the spurs and departed as fast as he might. And when Sir Maleagans saw him so flee, he understood that it was by the queen’s commandment for to warn Sir Launcelot. Then they that were best horsed chased him, and shot at him, but the child went from them all. Then Sir Maleagans said to the queen, “Madam, ye are about to betray me, but I shall arrange for Sir Launcelot that he shall not come lightly at you.” Then he rode with her and them all to his castle, in all the haste that they might. And by the way Sir Maleagans laid in ambush the best archers that he had to wait for Sir Launcelot. And the child came to Westminster and found Sir Launcelot and told his message and delivered him the queen’s ring. “Alas!” said Sir Launcelot, “now am I shamed for ever, unless I may rescue that noble lady.” Then eagerly he asked his armor and put it on him, and mounted his horse and rode as fast as he might; and men say he took the water at Westminster Bridge, and made his horse swim over Thames unto Lambeth. Then within a while he came to a wood where was a narrow way; and there the archers were laid in ambush. And they shot at him and smote his horse so that he fell. Then Sir Launcelot left his horse and went on foot, but there lay so many ditches and hedges betwixt the archers and him that he might not meddle with them. “Alas! for shame,” said Sir Launcelot, “that ever one knight should betray another! but it is an old saw, a good man is never in danger, but when he is in danger of a coward.” Then Sir Launcelot went awhile and he was exceedingly cumbered by his armor, his shield, and his spear, and all that belonged to him. Then by chance there came by him a cart that came thither to fetch wood.\n\nNow at this time carts were little used except for carrying offal and for conveying criminals to execution. But Sir Launcelot took no thought of anything but the necessity of haste for the purpose of rescuing the queen; so he demanded of the carter that he should take him in and convey him as speedily as possible for a liberal reward. The carter consented, and Sir Launcelot placed himself in the cart and only lamented that with much jolting he made but little progress. Then it happened Sir Gawain passed by and seeing an armed knight travelling in that unusual way he drew near to see who it might be. Then Sir Launcelot told him how the queen had been carried off, and how, in hastening to her rescue, his horse had been disabled and he had been compelled to avail himself of the cart rather than give up his enterprise. Then Sir Gawain said, “Surely it is unworthy of a knight to travel in such sort;” but Sir Launcelot heeded him not.\n\nAt nightfall they arrived at a castle and the lady thereof came out at the head of her damsels to welcome Sir Gawain. But to admit his companion, whom she supposed to be a criminal, or at least a prisoner, it pleased her not; however, to oblige Sir Gawain, she consented. At supper Sir Launcelot came near being consigned to the kitchen and was only admitted to the lady’s table at the earnest solicitation of Sir Gawain. Neither would the damsels prepare a bed for him. He seized the first he found unoccupied and was left undisturbed.\n\nNext morning he saw from the turrets of the castle a train accompanying a lady, whom he imagined to be the queen. Sir Gawain thought it might be so, and became equally eager to depart. The lady of the castle supplied Sir Launcelot with a horse and they traversed the plain at full speed. They learned from some travellers whom they met, that there were two roads which led to the castle of Sir Maleagans. Here therefore the friends separated. Sir Launcelot found his way beset with obstacles, which he encountered successfully, but not without much loss of time. As evening approached he was met by a young and sportive damsel, who gayly proposed to him a supper at her castle. The knight, who was hungry and weary, accepted the offer, though with no very good grace. He followed the lady to her castle and ate voraciously of her supper, but was quite impenetrable to all her amorous advances. Suddenly the scene changed and he was assailed by six furious ruffians, whom he dealt with so vigorously that most of them were speedily disabled, when again there was a change and he found himself alone with his fair hostess, who informed him that she was none other than his guardian fairy, who had but subjected him to tests of his courage and fidelity. The next day the fairy brought him on his road, and before parting gave him a ring, which she told him would by its changes of color disclose to him all enchantments, and enable him to subdue them.\n\nSir Launcelot pursued his journey, without being much incommoded except by the taunts of travellers, who all seemed to have learned, by some means, his disgraceful drive in the cart. One, more insolent than the rest, had the audacity to interrupt him during dinner, and even to risk a battle in support of his pleasantry. Launcelot, after an easy victory, only doomed him to be carted in his turn.\n\nAt night he was received at another castle, with great apparent hospitality, but found himself in the morning in a dungeon, and loaded with chains. Consulting his ring, and finding that this was an enchantment, he burst his chains, seized his armor in spite of the visionary monsters who attempted to defend it, broke open the gates of the tower, and continued his journey. At length his progress was checked by a wide and rapid torrent, which could only be passed on a narrow bridge, on which a false step would prove his destruction. Launcelot, leading his horse by the bridle, and making him swim by his side, passed over the bridge, and was attacked as soon as he reached the bank by a lion and a leopard, both of which he slew, and then, exhausted and bleeding, seated himself on the grass, and endeavored to bind up his wounds, when he was accosted by Brademagus, the father of Maleagans, whose castle was then in sight, and at no great distance. This king, no less courteous than his son was haughty and insolent, after complimenting Sir Launcelot on the valor and skill he had displayed in the perils of the bridge and the wild beasts, offered him his assistance, and informed him that the queen was safe in his castle, but could only be rescued by encountering Maleagans. Launcelot demanded the battle for the next day, and accordingly it took place, at the foot of the tower, and under the eyes of the fair captive. Launcelot was enfeebled by his wounds, and fought not with his usual spirit, and the contest for a time was doubtful; till Guenever exclaimed, “Ah, Launcelot! my knight, truly have I been told that thou art no longer worthy of me!” These words instantly revived the drooping knight; he resumed at once his usual superiority, and soon laid at his feet his haughty adversary.\n\nHe was on the point of sacrificing him to his resentment, when Guenever, moved by the entreaties of Brademagus, ordered him to withhold the blow, and he obeyed. The castle and its prisoners were now at his disposal. Launcelot hastened to the apartment of the queen, threw himself at her feet, and was about to kiss her hand, when she exclaimed, “Ah, Launcelot! why do I see thee again, yet feel thee to be no longer worthy of me, after having been disgracefully drawn about the country in a—” She had not time to finish the phrase, for her lover suddenly started from her, and, bitterly lamenting that he had incurred the displeasure of his sovereign lady, rushed out of the castle, threw his sword and his shield to the right and left, ran furiously into the woods, and disappeared.\n\nIt seems that the story of the abominable cart, which haunted Launcelot at every step, had reached the ears of Sir Kay, who had told it to the queen, as a proof that her knight must have been dishonored. But Guenever had full leisure to repent the haste with which she had given credit to the tale. Three days elapsed, during which Launcelot wandered without knowing where he went, till at last he began to reflect that his mistress had doubtless been deceived by misrepresentation, and that it was his duty to set her right. He therefore returned, compelled Maleagans to release his prisoners, and, taking the road by which they expected the arrival of Sir Gawain, had the satisfaction of meeting him the next day; after which the whole company proceeded gayly towards Camelot.\n\n————\n\n# THE LADY OF SHALOTT\n\nKING ARTHUR proclaimed a solemn tournament to be held at Winchester. The king, not less impatient than his knights for this festival, set off some days before to superintend the preparations, leaving the queen with her court at Camelot. Sir Launcelot, under pretence of indisposition, remained behind also. His intention was to attend the tournament in disguise; and having communicated his project to Guenever, he mounted his horse, set off without any attendant, and, counterfeiting the feebleness of age, took the most unfrequented road to Winchester, and passed unnoticed as an old knight who was going to be a spectator of the sports. Even Arthur and Gawain, who happened to behold him from the windows of a castle under which he passed, were the dupes of his disguise. But an accident betrayed him. His horse happened to stumble, and the hero, forgetting for a moment his assumed character, recovered the animal with a strength and agility so peculiar to himself, that they instantly recognized the inimitable Launcelot. They suffered him, however, to proceed on his journey without interruption, convinced that his extraordinary feats of arms must discover him at the approaching festival.\n\nIn the evening Launcelot was magnificently entertained as a stranger knight at the neighboring castle of Shalott. The lord of this castle had a daughter of exquisite beauty, and two sons lately received into the order of knighthood, one of whom was at that time ill in bed, and thereby prevented from attending the tournament, for which both brothers had long made preparation. Launcelot offered to attend the other, if he were permitted to borrow the armor of the invalid, and the lord of Shalott, without knowing the name of his guest, being satisfied from his appearance that his son could not have a better assistant in arms, most thankfully accepted the offer. In the meantime the young lady, who had been much struck by the first appearance of the stranger knight, continued to survey him with increased attention, and, before the conclusion of supper, became so deeply enamoured of him, that after frequent changes of color, and other symptoms which Sir Launcelot could not possibly mistake, she was obliged to retire to her chamber, and seek relief in tears. Sir Launcelot hastened to convey to her, by means of her brother, the information that his heart was already disposed of, but that it would be his pride and pleasure to act as her knight at the approaching tournament. The lady, obliged to be satisfied with that courtesy, presented him her scarf to be worn at the tournament.\n\nLauncelot set off in the morning with the young knight, who, on their approaching Winchester, carried him to the castle of a lady, sister to the lord of Shalott, by whom they were hospitably entertained. The next day they put on their armor, which was perfectly plain and without any device, as was usual to youths during the first year of knighthood, their shields being only painted red, as some color was necessary to enable them to be recognized by their attendants. Launcelot wore on his crest the scarf of the maid of Shalott, and, thus equipped, proceeded to the tournament, where the knights were divided into two companies, the one commanded by Sir Galehaut, the other by King Arthur. Having surveyed the combat for a short time from without the lists, and observed that Sir Galehaut’s party began to give way, they joined the press and attacked the royal knights, the young man choosing such adversaries as were suited to his strength, while his companion selected the principal champions of the Round Table, and successively overthrew Gawain, Bohort, and Lionel. The astonishment of the spectators was extreme, for it was thought that no one but Launcelot could possess such invincible force; yet the favor on his crest seemed to preclude the possibility of his being thus disguised, for Launcelot had never been known to wear the badge of any but his sovereign lady. At length Sir Hector, Launcelot’s brother, engaged him, and, after a dreadful combat, wounded him dangerously in the head, but was himself completely stunned by a blow on the helmet, and felled to the ground; after which the conqueror rode off at full speed, attended by his companion.\n\nThey returned to the castle of Shalott, where Launcelot was attended with the greatest care by the good earl, by his two sons, and, above all, by his fair daughter, whose medical skill probably much hastened the period of his recovery. His health was almost completely restored, when Sir Hector, Sir Bohort, and Sir Lionel, who, after the return of the court to Camelot, had undertaken the quest of their relation, discovered him walking on the walls of the castle. Their meeting was very joyful; they passed three days in the castle amidst constant festivities, and bantered each other on the events of the tournament. Launcelot, though he began by vowing vengeance against the author of his wound, yet ended by declaring that he felt rewarded for the pain by the pride he took in witnessing his brother’s extraordinary prowess. He then dismissed them with a message to the queen, promising to follow immediately, it being necessary that he should first take a formal leave of his kind hosts, as well as of the fair maid of Shalott.\n\nThe young lady, after vainly attempting to detain him by her tears and solicitations, saw him depart without leaving her any ground for hope.\n\nIt was early summer when the tournament took place; but some months had passed since Launcelot’s departure, and winter was now near at hand. The health and strength of the Lady of Shalott had gradually sunk, and she felt that she could not live apart from the object of her affections. She left the castle, and descending to the river’s brink placed herself in a boat, which she loosed from its moorings, and suffered to bear her down the current toward Camelot.\n\nOne morning, as Arthur and Sir Lionel looked from the window of the tower, the walls of which were washed by a river, they descried a boat richly ornamented, and covered with an awning of cloth of gold, which appeared to be floating down the stream without any human guidance. It struck the shore while they watched it, and they hastened down to examine it. Beneath the awning they discovered the dead body of a beautiful woman, in whose features Sir Lionel easily recognized the lovely maid of Shalott. Pursuing their search, they discovered a purse richly embroidered with gold and jewels, and within the purse a letter, which Arthur opened, and found addressed to himself and all the knights of the Round Table, stating that Launcelot of the Lake, the most accomplished of knights and most beautiful of men, but at the same time the most cruel and inflexible, had by his rigor produced the death of the wretched maiden, whose love was no less invincible than his cruelty. The king immediately gave orders for the interment of the lady with all the honors suited to her rank, at the same time explaining to the knights the history of her affection for Launcelot, which moved the compassion and regret of all.\n\n————\n\nTennyson has chosen the story of the “Lady of Shalott” for the subject of a poem. The catastrophe is told thus:\n\n“Under tower and balcony, By garden-wall and gallery, A gleaming shape she floated by, A corse between the houses high, Silent into Camelot. Out upon the wharfs they came, Knight and burgher, lord and dame, And round the prow they read her name, ‘The Lady of Shalott.’\n\n“Who is this? and what is here? And in the lighted palace near Died the sound of royal cheer; And they crossed themselves for fear, All the knights at Camelot. But Launcelot mused a little space; He said, ‘She has a lovely face; God in his mercy lend her grace, The Lady of Shalott.’”\n\n————\n\n# QUEEN GUENEVER’S PERIL\n\nIT happened at this time that Queen Guenever was thrown into great peril of her life. A certain squire who was in her immediate service, having some cause of animosity to Sir Gawain, determined to destroy him by poison, at a public entertainment. For this purpose he concealed the poison in an apple of fine appearance, which he placed on the top of several others, and put the dish before the queen, hoping that, as Sir Gawain was the knight of greatest dignity, she would present the apple to him. But it happened that a Scottish knight of high distinction, who arrived on that day, was seated next to the queen, and to him as a stranger she presented the apple, which he had no sooner eaten than he was seized with dreadful pain, and fell senseless. The whole court was, of course, thrown into confusion; the knights rose from table, darting looks of indignation at the wretched queen, whose tears and protestations were unable to remove their suspicions. In spite of all that could be done the knight died, and nothing remained but to order a magnificent funeral and monument for him, which was done.\n\nSome time after Sir Mador, brother of the murdered knight, arrived at Arthur’s court in quest of him. While hunting in the forest he by chance came to the spot where the monument was erected, read the inscription, and returned to court determined on immediate and signal vengeance. He rode into the hall, loudly accused the queen of treason, and insisted on her being given up for punishment, unless she should find by a certain day a knight hardy enough to risk his life in support of her innocence. Arthur, powerful as he was, did not dare to deny the appeal, but was compelled with a heavy heart to accept it, and Mador sternly took his departure, leaving the royal couple plunged in terror and anxiety.\n\nDuring all this time Launcelot was absent, and no one knew where he was. He fled in anger from his fair mistress, upon being reproached by her with his passion for the Lady of Shalott, which she had hastily inferred from his wearing her scarf at the tournament. He took up his abode with a hermit in the forest, and resolved to think no more of the cruel beauty, whose conduct he thought must flow from a wish to get rid of him. Yet calm reflection had somewhat cooled his indignation, and he had begun to wish, though hardly able to hope, for a reconciliation when the news of Sir Mador’s challenge fortunately reached his ears. The intelligence revived his spirits, and he began to prepare with the utmost cheerfulness for a contest which, if successful, would insure him at once the affection of his mistress and the gratitude of his sovereign.\n\nThe sad fate of the Lady of Shalott had ere this completely acquitted Launcelot in the queen’s mind of all suspicion of his fidelity, and she lamented most grievously her foolish quarrel with him, which now, at her time of need, deprived her of her most efficient champion.\n\nAs the day appointed by Sir Mador was fast approaching, it became necessary that she should procure a champion for her defence; and she successively adjured Sir Hector, Sir Lionel, Sir Bohort, and Sir Gawain to undertake the battle. She fell on her knees before them, called heaven to witness her innocence of the crime alleged against her, but was sternly answered by all that they could not fight to maintain the innocence of one whose act, and the fatal consequence of it, they had seen with their own eyes. She retired, therefore, dejected and disconsolate; but the sight of the fatal pile on which, if guilty, she was doomed to be burned, exciting her to fresh effort, she again repaired to Sir Bohort, threw herself at his feet, and piteously calling on him for mercy, fell into a swoon. The brave knight was not proof against this. He raised her up, and hastily promised that he would undertake her cause, if no other or better champion should present himself. He then summoned his friends, and told them his resolution; and as a mortal combat with Sir Mador was a most fearful enterprise, they agreed to accompany him in the morning to the hermitage in the forest, where he proposed to receive absolution from the hermit, and to make his peace with Heaven before he entered the lists. As they approached the hermitage, they espied a knight riding in the forest, whom they at once recognized as Sir Launcelot. Overjoyed at the meeting, they quickly, in answer to his questions, confirmed the news of the queen’s imminent danger, and received his instructions to return to court, to comfort her as well as they could, but to say nothing of his intention of undertaking her defence, which he meant to do in the character of an unknown adventurer.\n\nOn their return to the castle they found that mass was finished, and had scarcely time to speak to the queen before they were summoned into the hall to dinner. A general gloom was spread over the countenances of all the guests. Arthur himself was unable to conceal his dejection, and the wretched Guenever, motionless and bathed in tears, sat in trembling expectation of Sir Mador’s appearance. Nor was it long ere he stalked into the hall, and with a voice of thunder, rendered more impressive by the general silence, demanded instant justice on the guilty party. Arthur replied with dignity, that little of the day was yet spent, and that perhaps a champion might yet be found capable of satisfying his thirst for battle. Sir Bohort now rose from table, and shortly returning in complete armor, resumed his place, after receiving the embraces and thanks of the king, who now began to resume some degree of confidence. Sir Mador, growing impatient, again repeated his denunciations of vengeance, and insisted that the combat should no longer be postponed.\n\nIn the height of the debate there came riding into the hall a knight mounted on a black steed, and clad in black armor, with his visor down, and lance in hand. “Sir,” said the king, “is it your will to alight and partake of our cheer?” “Nay, sir,” he replied; “I come to save a lady’s life. The queen hath ill bestowed her favors, and honored many a knight, that in her hour of need she should have none to take her part. Thou that darest accuse her of treachery, stand forth, for to-day shalt thou need all thy might.”\n\nSir Mador, though surprised, was not appalled by the stern challenge and formidable appearance of his antagonist, but prepared for the encounter. At the first shock both were unhorsed. They then drew their swords, and commenced a combat which lasted from noon till evening, when Sir Mador, whose strength began to fail, was felled to the ground by Launcelot, and compelled to sue for mercy. The victor, whose arm was already raised to terminate the life of his opponent, instantly dropped his sword, courteously lifted up the fainting Sir Mador, frankly confessing that he had never before encountered so formidable an enemy. The other, with similar courtesy, solemnly renounced all further projects of vengeance for his brother’s death; and the two knights, now become fast friends, embraced each other with the greatest cordiality. In the meantime Arthur, having recognized Sir Launcelot, whose helmet was now unlaced, rushed down into the lists, followed by all his knights, to welcome and thank his deliverer. Guenever swooned with joy, and the place of combat suddenly exhibited a scene of the most tumultuous delight.\n\nThe general satisfaction was still further increased by the discovery of the real culprit. Having accidentally incurred some suspicion, he confessed his crime, and was publicly punished in the presence of Sir Mador.\n\nThe court now returned to the castle, which, with the title of “La Joyeuse Garde” bestowed upon it in memory of the happy event, was conferred on Sir Launcelot by Arthur, as a memorial of his gratitude.\n\n————\n\n# TRISTRAM AND ISOUDE\n\nMELIADUS was king of Leonois, or Lionesse, a country famous in the annals of romance, which adjoined the kingdom of Cornwall, but has now disappeared from the map, having been, it is said, overwhelmed by the ocean. Meliadus was married to Isabella, sister of Mark, king of Cornwall. A fairy fell in love with him, and drew him away by enchantment while he was engaged in hunting. His queen set out in quest of him, but was taken ill on her journey, and died, leaving an infant son, whom, from the melancholy circumstances of his birth, she called Tristram.\n\nGouvernail, the queen’s squire, who had accompanied her, took charge of the child, and restored him to his father, who had at length burst the enchantments of the fairy, and returned home.\n\nMeliadus after seven years married again, and the new queen, being jealous of the influence of Tristram with his father, laid plots for his life, which were discovered by Gouvernail, who in consequence fled with the boy to the court of the king of France, where Tristram was kindly received, and grew up improving in every gallant and knightly accomplishment, adding to his skill in arms the arts of music and of chess. In particular, he devoted himself to the chase and to all woodland sports, so that he became distinguished above all other chevaliers of the court for his knowledge of all that relates to hunting. No wonder that Belinda, the king’s daughter, fell in love with him; but as he did not return her passion, she, in a sudden impulse of anger, excited her father against him, and he was banished the kingdom. The princess soon repented of her act, and in despair destroyed herself, having first written a most tender letter to Tristram, sending him at the same time a beautiful and sagacious dog, of which she was very fond, desiring him to keep it as a memorial of her. Meliadus was now dead, and as his queen, Tristram’s stepmother, held the throne, Gouvernail was afraid to carry his pupil to his native country, and took him to Cornwall, to his uncle Mark, who gave him a kind reception.\n\nKing Mark resided at the castle of Tintadel, already mentioned in the history of Uther and Igerne. In this court Tristram became distinguished in all the exercises incumbent on a knight; nor was it long before he had an opportunity of practically employing his valor and skill. Moraunt, a celebrated champion, brother to the queen of Ireland, arrived at the court, to demand tribute of King Mark. The knights of Cornwall are in ill repute in romance for their cowardice, and they exhibited it on this occasion. King Mark could find no champion who dared to encounter the Irish knight, till his nephew Tristram, who had not yet received the honors of knighthood, craved to be admitted to the order, offering at the same time to fight the battle of Cornwall against the Irish champion. King Mark assented with reluctance; Tristram received the accolade, which conferred knighthood upon him, and the place and time were assigned for the encounter.\n\nWithout attempting to give the details of this famous combat, the first and one of the most glorious of Tristram’s exploits, we shall only say that the young knight, though severely wounded, cleft the head of Moraunt, leaving a portion of his sword in the wound. Moraunt, half dead with his wound and the disgrace of his defeat, hastened to hide himself in his ship, sailed away with all speed for Ireland, and died soon after arriving in his own country.\n\nThe kingdom of Cornwall was thus delivered from its tribute. Tristram, weakened by loss of blood, fell senseless. His friends flew to his assistance. They dressed his wounds, which in general healed readily; but the lance of Moraunt was poisoned, and one wound which it made yielded to no remedies, but grew worse day by day. The surgeons could do no more. Tristram asked permission of his uncle to depart, and seek for aid in the kingdom of Loegria (England). With his consent he embarked, and after tossing for many days on the sea, was driven by the winds to the coast of Ireland. He landed, full of joy and gratitude that he had escaped the peril of the sea; took his rote,[49] and began to play. It was a summer evening, and the king of Ireland and his daughter, the beautiful Isoude, were at a window which overlooked the sea. The strange harper was sent for, and conveyed to the palace, where, finding that he was in Ireland, whose champion he had lately slain, he concealed his name, and called himself Tramtris. The queen undertook his cure, and by a medicated bath gradually restored him to health. His skill in music and in games occasioned his being frequently called to court, and he became the instructor of the princess Isoude in minstrelsy and poetry, who profited so well under his care, that she soon had no equal in the kingdom, except her instructor.\n\nAt this time a tournament was held, at which many knights of the Round Table, and others, were present. On the first day a Saracen prince, named Palamedes, obtained the advantage over all. They brought him to the court, and gave him a feast, at which Tristram, just recovering from his wound, was present. The fair Isoude appeared on this occasion in all her charms. Palamedes could not behold them without emotion, and made no effort to conceal his love. Tristram perceived it, and the pain he felt from jealousy taught him how dear the fair Isoude had already become to him.\n\nNext day the tournament was renewed. Tristram, still feeble from his wound, rose during the night, took his arms, and concealed them in a forest near the place of the contest, and, after it had begun, mingled with the combatants. He overthrew all that encountered him, in particular Palamedes, whom he brought to the ground with a stroke of his lance, and then fought him hand to hand, bearing off the prize of the tourney. But his exertions caused his wound to reopen; he bled fast, and in this sad state, yet in triumph, they bore him to the palace. The fair Isoude devoted herself to his relief with an interest which grew more vivid day by day; and her skilful care soon restored him to health.\n\nIt happened one day that a damsel of the court, entering the closet where Tristram’s arms were deposited, perceived that a part of the sword had been broken off. It occurred to her that the missing portion was like that which was left in the skull of Moraunt, the Irish champion. She imparted her thought to the queen, who compared the fragment taken from her brother’s wound with the sword of Tristram, and was satisfied that it was part of the same, and that the weapon of Tristram was that which reft her brother’s life. She laid her griefs and resentment before the king, who satisfied himself with his own eyes of the truth of her suspicions. Tristram was cited before the whole court, and reproached with having dared to present himself before them after having slain their kinsman. He acknowledged that he had fought with Moraunt to settle the claim for tribute, and said that it was by force of winds and waves alone that he was thrown on their coast. The queen demanded vengeance for the death of her brother; the fair Isoude trembled and grew pale, but a murmur rose from all the assembly that the life of one so handsome and so brave should not be taken for such a cause, and generosity finally triumphed over resentment in the mind of the king. Tristram was dismissed in safety, but commanded to leave the kingdom without delay, and never to return thither under pain of death. Tristram went back, with restored health, to Cornwall.\n\nKing Mark made his nephew give him a minute recital of his adventures. Tristram told him all minutely; but when he came to speak of the fair Isoude he described her charms with a warmth and energy such as none but a lover could display. King Mark was fascinated with the description, and, choosing a favorable time, demanded a boon[50] of his nephew, who readily granted it. The king made him swear upon the holy reliques that he would fulfil his commands. Then Mark directed him to go to Ireland, and obtain for him the fair Isoude to be queen of Cornwall.\n\nTristram believed it was certain death for him to return to Ireland; and how could he act as ambassador for his uncle in such a cause? Yet, bound by his oath, he hesitated not for an instant. He only took the precaution to change his armor. He embarked for Ireland; but a tempest drove him to the coast of England, near Camelot, where King Arthur was holding his court, attended by the knights of the Round Table, and many others, the most illustrious in the world.\n\nTristram kept himself unknown. He took part in many justs; he fought many combats, in which he covered himself with glory. One day he saw among those recently arrived the king of Ireland, father of the fair Isoude. This prince, accused of treason against his liege sovereign, Arthur, came to Camelot to free himself from the charge. Blaanor, one of the most redoubtable warriors of the Round Table, was his accuser, and Argius, the king, had neither youthful vigor nor strength to encounter him. He must therefore seek a champion to sustain his innocence. But the knights of the Round Table were not at liberty to fight against one another, unless in a quarrel of their own. Argius heard of the great renown of the unknown knight; he also was witness of his exploits. He sought him, and conjured him to adopt his defence, and on his oath declared that he was innocent of the crime of which he was accused. Tristram readily consented, and made himself known to the king, who on his part promised to reward his exertions, if successful, with whatever gift he might ask.\n\nTristram fought with Blaanor, and overthrew him, and held his life in his power. The fallen warrior called on him to use his right of conquest, and strike the fatal blow. “God forbid,” said Tristram, “that I should take the life of so brave a knight!” He raised him up and restored him to his friends. The judges of the field decided that the king of Ireland was acquitted of the charge against him, and they led Tristram in triumph to his tent. King Argius, full of gratitude, conjured Tristram to accompany him to his kingdom. They departed together, and arrived in Ireland; and the queen, forgetting her resentment for her brother’s death, exhibited to the preserver of her husband’s life nothing but gratitude and good-will.\n\nHow happy a moment for Isoude, who knew that her father had promised his deliverer whatever boon he might ask! But the unhappy Tristram gazed on her with despair, at the thought of the cruel oath which bound him. His magnanimous soul subdued the force of his love. He revealed the oath which he had taken, and with trembling voice demanded the fair Isoude for his uncle.\n\nArgius consented, and soon all was prepared for the departure of Isoude. Brengwain, her favorite maid of honor, was to accompany her. On the day of departure the queen took aside this devoted attendant, and told her that she had observed that her daughter and Tristram were attached to one another, and that to avert the bad effects of this inclination she had procured from a powerful fairy a potent philter (love-draught), which she directed Brengwain to administer to Isoude and to King Mark on the evening of their marriage.\n\nIsoude and Tristram embarked together. A favorable wind filled the sails, and promised them a fortunate voyage. The lovers gazed upon one another, and could not repress their sighs. Love seemed to light up all his fires on their lips, as in their hearts. The day was warm; they suffered from thirst. Isoude first complained. Tristram descried the bottle containing the love-draught, which Brengwain had been so imprudent as to leave in sight. He took it, gave some of it to the charming Isoude, and drank the remainder himself. The dog Houdain licked the cup. The ship arrived in Cornwall, and Isoude was married to King Mark. The old monarch was delighted with his bride, and his gratitude to Tristram was unbounded. He loaded him with honors, and made him chamberlain of his palace, thus giving him access to the queen at all times.\n\nIn the midst of the festivities of the court which followed the royal marriage, an unknown minstrel one day presented himself, bearing a harp of peculiar construction. He excited the curiosity of King Mark by refusing to play upon it till he should grant him a boon. The king having promised to grant his request, the minstrel, who was none other than the Saracen knight, Sir Palamedes, the lover of the fair Isoude, sung to the harp a lay, in which he demanded Isoude as the promised gift. King Mark could not by the laws of knighthood withhold the boon. The lady was mounted on her horse, and led away by her triumphant lover. Tristram, it is needless to say, was absent at the time, and did not return until their departure. When he heard what had taken place he seized his rote, and hastened to the shore, where Isoude and her new master had already embarked. Tristram played upon his rote, and the sound reached the ears of Isoude, who became so deeply affected, that Sir Palamedes was induced to return with her to land, that they might see the unknown musician. Tristram watched his opportunity, seized the lady’s horse by the bridle, and plunged with her into the forest, tauntingly informing his rival that “what he had got by the harp he had lost by the rote.” Palamedes pursued, and a combat was about to commence, the result of which must have been fatal to one or other of these gallant knights; but Isoude stepped between them, and, addressing Palamedes, said, “You tell me that you love me; you will not then deny me the request I am about to make?” “Lady,” he replied, “I will perform your bidding.” “Leave, then,” said she, “this contest, and repair to King Arthur’s court, and salute Queen Guenever from me; tell her that there are in the world but two ladies, herself and I, and two lovers, hers and mine; and come thou not in future in any place where I am.” Palamedes burst into tears. “Ah, lady,” said he, “I will obey you; but I beseech you that you will not for ever steel your heart against me.” “Palamedes,” she replied, “may I never taste of joy again if I ever quit my first love.” Palamedes then went his way. The lovers remained a week in concealment, after which Tristram restored Isoude to her husband, advising him in future to reward minstrels in some other way.\n\nThe king showed much gratitude to Tristram, but in the bottom of his heart he cherished bitter jealousy of him. One day Tristram and Isoude were alone together in her private chamber. A base and cowardly knight of the court, named Andret, spied them through a keyhole. They sat at a table of chess, but were not attending to the game. Andret brought the king, having first raised his suspicions, and placed him so as to watch their motions. The king saw enough to confirm his suspicions, and he burst into the apartment with his sword drawn, and had nearly slain Tristram before he was put on his guard. But Tristram avoided the blow, drew his sword, and drove before him the cowardly monarch, chasing him through all the apartments of the palace, giving him frequent blows with the flat of his sword, while he cried in vain to his knights to save him. They were not inclined, or did not dare, to interpose in his behalf.\n————\n\nA proof of the great popularity of the tale of Sir Tristram is the fact that the Italian poets, Boiardo and Ariosto, have founded upon it the idea of the two enchanted fountains, which produced the opposite effects of love and hatred. Boiardo thus describes the fountain of hatred:\n\n“Fair was that, fountain, sculptured all of gold, With alabaster sculptured, rich and rare; And in its basin clear thou might’st behold The flowery marge reflected fresh and fair. Sage Merlin framed the font,—so legends bear,— When on fair Isoude doated Tristram brave, That the good errant knight, arriving there, Might quaff oblivion in the enchanted wave, And leave his luckless love, and ’scape his timeless grave.\n\n“But ne’er the warrior’s evil fate allowed His steps that fountain’s charmed verge to gain. Though restless, roving on adventure proud, He traversed oft the land and oft the main.” . . . . . . .\n————\n\nTRISTRAM AND ISOUDE (Continued)\nAFTER this affair Tristram was banished from the kingdom, and Isoude shut up in a tower, which stood on the bank of a river. Tristram could not resolve to depart without some further communication with his beloved; so he concealed himself in the forest, till at last he contrived to attract her attention, by means of twigs which he curiously peeled, and sent down the stream under her window. By this means many secret interviews were obtained. Tristram dwelt in the forest, sustaining himself by game, which the dog Houdain ran down for him; for this faithful animal was unequalled in the chase, and knew so well his master’s wish for concealment, that, in the pursuit of his game, he never barked. At length Tristram departed, but left Houdain with Isoude, as a remembrancer of him.\n\nSir Tristram wandered through various countries, achieving the most perilous enterprises, and covering himself with glory, yet unhappy at the separation from his beloved Isoude. At length King Mark’s territory was invaded by a neighboring chieftain, and he was forced to summon his nephew to his aid. Tristram obeyed the call, put himself at the head of his uncle’s vassals, and drove the enemy out of the country. Mark was full of gratitude, and Tristram, restored to favor and to the society of his beloved Isoude, seemed at the summit of happiness. But a sad reverse was at hand.\n\nTristram had brought with him a friend named Pheredin, son of the king of Brittany. This young knight saw Queen Isoude, and could not resist her charms. Knowing the love of his friend for the queen, and that that love was returned, Pheredin concealed his own, until his health failed, and he feared he was drawing near his end. He then wrote to the beautiful queen that he was dying for love of her.\n\nThe gentle Isoude, in a moment of pity for the friend of Tristram, returned him an answer so kind and compassionate that it restored him to life. A few days afterwards Tristram found this letter. The most terrible jealousy took possession of his soul; he would have slain Pheredin, who with difficulty made his escape. Then Tristram mounted his horse, and rode to the forest, where for ten days he took no rest nor food. At length he was found by a damsel lying almost dead by the brink of a fountain. She recognized him, and tried in vain to rouse his attention. At last recollecting his love for music she went and got her harp, and played thereon. Tristram was roused from his reverie; tears flowed; he breathed more freely; he took the harp from the maiden, and sung this lay, with a voice broken with sobs:\n“Sweet I sang in former days, Kind love perfected my lays: Now my art alone displays The woe that on my being preys.\n\n“Charming love, delicious power, Worshipped from my earliest hour, Thou who life on all dost shower, Love! my life thou dost devour.\n“In death’s hour I beg of thee, Isoude, dearest enemy, Thou who erst couldst kinder be, When I’m gone, forget not me.\n\n“On my gravestone passers-by Oft will read, as low I lie, ‘Never wight in love could vie With Tristram, yet she let him die.’”\n\nTristram, having finished his lay, wrote it off and gave it to the damsel, conjuring her to present it to the queen.\nMeanwhile Queen Isoude was inconsolable at the absence of Tristram. She discovered that it was caused by the fatal letter which she had written to Pheredin. Innocent, but in despair at the sad effects of her letter, she wrote another to Pheredin, charging him never to see her again. The unhappy lover obeyed this cruel decree. He plunged into the forest, and died of grief and love in a hermit’s cell.\n\nIsoude passed her days in lamenting the absence and unknown fate of Tristram. One day her jealous husband, having entered her chamber unperceived, overheard her singing the following lay:\n\n“My voice to piteous wail is bent, My harp to notes of languishment; Ah, love! delightsome days be meant For happier wights, with hearts content.\n“Ah, Tristram! far away from me, Art thou from restless anguish free? Ah! couldst thou so one moment be, From her who so much loveth thee?”\n\nThe king hearing these words burst forth in a rage; but Isoude was too wretched to fear his violence. “You have heard me,” she said; “I confess it all. I love Tristram, and always shall love him. Without doubt he is dead, and died for me. I no longer wish to live. The blow that shall finish my misery will be most welcome.”\n\nThe king was moved at the distress of the fair Isoude, and perhaps the idea of Tristram’s death tended to allay his wrath. He left the queen in charge of her women, commanding them to take especial care lest her despair should lead her to do harm to herself.\n\nTristram meanwhile, distracted as he was, rendered a most important service to the shepherds by slaying a gigantic robber named Taullas, who was in the habit of plundering their flocks and rifling their cottages. The shepherds, in their gratitude to Tristram, bore him in triumph to King Mark to have him bestow on him a suitable reward. No wonder Mark failed to recognize in the half-clad, wild man, before him his nephew Tristram; but grateful for the service the unknown had rendered he ordered him to be well taken care of, and gave him in charge to the queen and her women. Under such care Tristram rapidly recovered his serenity and his health, so that the romancer tells us he became handsomer than ever. King Mark’s jealousy revived with Tristram’s health and good looks, and, in spite of his debt of gratitude so lately increased, he again banished him from the court.\n\nSir Tristram left Cornwall, and proceeded into the land of Loegria (England) in quest of adventures. One day he entered a wide forest. The sound of a little bell showed him that some inhabitant was near. He followed the sound, and found a hermit, who informed him that he was in the forest of Arnantes, belonging to the fairy Viviane, the Lady of the Lake, who, smitten with love for King Arthur, had found means to entice him to this forest, where by enchantments she held him a prisoner, having deprived him of all memory of who and what he was. The hermit informed him that all the knights of the Round Table were out in search of the king, and that he (Tristram) was now in the scene of the most grand and important adventures.\n\nThis was enough to animate Tristram in the search. He had not wandered far before he encountered a knight of Arthur’s court, who proved to be Sir Kay the Seneschal, who demanded of him whence he came. Tristram answering, “From Cornwall,” Sir Kay did not let slip the opportunity of a joke at the expense of the Cornish knight. Tristram chose to leave him in his error, and even confirmed him in it; for meeting some other knights Tristram declined to just with them. They spent the night together at an abbey, where Tristram submitted patiently to all their jokes. The Seneschal gave the word to his companions that they should set out early next day, and intercept the Cornish knight on his way, and enjoy the amusement of seeing his fright when they should insist on running a tilt with him. Tristram next morning found himself alone; he put on his armor, and set out to continue his quest. He soon saw before him the Seneschal and the three knights, who barred the way, and insisted on a just. Tristram excused himself a long time; at last he reluctantly took his stand. He encountered them, one after the other, and overthrew them all four, man and horse, and then rode off, bidding them not to forget their friend, the knight of Cornwall.\n\nTristram had not ridden far when he met a damsel, who cried out, “Ah, my lord! hasten forward, and prevent a horrid treason!” Tristram flew to her assistance, and soon reached a spot where he beheld a knight, whom three others had borne to the ground, and were unlacing his helmet in order to cut off his head.\n\nTristram flew to the rescue, and slew with one stroke of his lance one of the assailants. The knight, recovering his feet, sacrificed another to his vengeance, and the third made his escape. The rescued knight then raised the visor of his helmet, and a long white beard fell down upon his breast. The majesty and venerable air of this knight made Tristram suspect that it was none other than Arthur himself, and the prince confirmed his conjecture. Tristram would have knelt before him, but Arthur received him in his arms, and inquired his name and country; but Tristram declined to disclose them, on the plea that he was now on a quest requiring secrecy. At this moment the damsel who had brought Tristram to the rescue darted forward, and, seizing the king’s hand, drew from his finger a ring, the gift of the fairy, and by that act dissolved the enchantment. Arthur, having recovered his reason and his memory, offered to Tristram to attach him to his court, and to confer honors and dignities upon him; but Tristram declined all, and only consented to accompany him till he should see him safe in the hands of his knights. Soon after, Hector de Marys rode up, and saluted the king, who on his part introduced him to Tristram as one of the bravest of his knights. Tristram took leave of the king and his faithful follower, and continued his quest.\n\nWe cannot follow Tristram through all the adventures which filled this epoch of his history. Suffice it to say, he fulfilled on all occasions the duty of a true knight, rescuing the oppressed, redressing wrongs, abolishing evil customs, and suppressing injustice, thus by constant action endeavoring to lighten the pains of absence from her he loved. In the meantime Isoude, separated from her dear Tristram, passed her days in languor and regret. At length she could no longer resist the desire to hear some news of her lover. She wrote a letter, and sent it by one of her damsels, niece of her faithful Brengwain. One day Tristram, weary with his exertions, had dismounted and laid himself down by the side of a fountain and fallen asleep. The damsel of Queen Isoude arrived at the same fountain, and recognized Passebreul, the horse of Tristram, and presently perceived his master asleep. He was thin and pale, showing evident marks of the pain he suffered in separation from his beloved. She awakened him, and gave him the letter which she bore, and Tristram enjoyed the pleasure, so sweet to a lover, of hearing from and talking about the object of his affections. He prayed the damsel to postpone her return till after the magnificent tournament which Arthur had proclaimed should have taken place, and conducted her to the castle of Persides, a brave and loyal knight, who received her with great consideration.\n\nTristram conducted the damsel of Queen Isoude to the tournament, and had her placed in the balcony among the ladies of the queen.\n\n“He glanced and saw the stately galleries, Dame, damsel, each through worship of their Queen White-robed in honor of the stainless child, And some with scatter’d jewels, like a bank Of maiden snow mingled with sparks of fire. He looked but once, and veiled his eyes again.” —The Last Tournament.\n\nHe then joined the tourney. Nothing could exceed his strength and valor. Launcelot admired him, and by a secret presentiment declined to dispute the honor of the day with a knight so gallant and so skilful. Arthur descended from the balcony to greet the conqueror; but the modest and devoted Tristram, content with having borne off the prize in the sight of the messenger of Isoude, made his escape with her, and disappeared.\n\nThe next day the tourney recommenced. Tristram assumed different armor, that he might not be known; but he was soon detected by the terrible blows that he gave. Arthur and Guenever had no doubt that it was the same knight who had borne off the prize of the day before. Arthur’s gallant spirit was roused. After Launcelot of the Lake and Sir Gawain he was accounted the best knight of the Round Table. He went privately and armed himself, and came into the tourney in undistinguished armor. He ran a just with Tristram, whom he shook in his seat; but Tristram, who did not know him, threw him out of the saddle. Arthur recovered himself, and content with having made proof of the stranger knight bade Launcelot finish the adventure, and vindicate the honor of the Round Table. Sir Launcelot, at the bidding of the monarch, assailed Tristram, whose lance was already broken in former encounters. But the law of this sort of combat was that the knight after having broken his lance must fight with his sword, and must not refuse to meet with his shield the lance of his antagonist. Tristram met Launcelot’s charge upon his shield, which that terrible lance could not fail to pierce. It inflicted a wound upon Tristram’s side, and, breaking, left the iron in the wound. But Tristram also with his sword smote so vigorously on Launcelot’s casque that he cleft it, and wounded his head. The wound was not deep, but the blood flowed into his eyes, and blinded him for a moment, and Tristram, who thought himself mortally wounded, retired from the field. Launcelot declared to the king that he had never received such a blow in his life before.\n\nTristram hastened to Gouvernail, his squire, who drew forth the iron, bound up the wound, and gave him immediate ease. Tristram after the tournament kept retired in his tent, but Arthur, with the consent of all the knights of the Round Table, decreed him the honors of the second day. But it was no longer a secret that the victor of the two days was the same individual, and Gouvernail, being questioned, confirmed the suspicions of Launcelot and Arthur that it was no other than Sir Tristram of Leonais, the nephew of the king of Cornwall.\n\nKing Arthur, who desired to reward his distinguished valor, and knew that his Uncle Mark had ungratefully banished him, would have eagerly availed himself of the opportunity to attach Tristram to his court,—all the knights of the Round Table declaring with acclamation that it would be impossible to find a more worthy companion. But Tristram had already departed in search of adventures, and the damsel of Queen Isoude returned to her mistress.\n\n# SIR TRISTRAM’S BATTLE WITH SIR LAUNCELOT\n\nSIR TRISTRAM rode through a forest and saw ten men fighting, and one man did battle against nine. So he rode to the knights and cried to them, bidding them cease their battle, for they did themselves great shame, so many knights to fight against one. Then answered the master of the knights (his name was Sir Breuse sans Pitie, who was at that time the most villanous knight living): “Sir knight, what have ye to do to meddle with us? If ye be wise depart on your way as you came, for this knight shall not escape us.” “That were pity,” said Sir Tristram, “that so good a knight should be slain so cowardly; therefore I warn you I will succor him with all my puissance.”\n\nThen Sir Tristram alighted off his horse, because they were on foot, that they should not slay his horse. And he smote on the right hand and on the left so vigorously that well-nigh at every stroke he struck down a knight. At last they fled, with Breuse sans Pitie, into the tower, and shut Sir Tristram without the gate. Then Sir Tristram returned back to the rescued knight, and found him sitting under a tree, sore wounded. “Fair knight,” said he, “how is it with you?” “Sir knight,” said Sir Palamedes, for he it was, “I thank you of your great goodness, for ye have rescued me from death.” “What is your name?” said Sir Tristram. He said, “My name is Sir Palamedes.” “Say ye so?” said Sir Tristram; “now know that thou art the man in the world that I most hate; therefore make thee ready, for I will do battle with thee.” “What is your name?” said Sir Palamedes. “My name is Sir Tristram, your mortal enemy.” “It may be so,” said Sir Palamedes; “but you have done overmuch for me this day, that I should fight with you. Moreover, it will be no honor for you to have to do with me, for you are fresh and I am wounded. Therefore, if you will needs have to do with me, assign me a day, and I shall meet you without fail.” “You say well,” said Sir Tristram; “now I assign you to meet me in the meadow by the river of Camelot, where Merlin set the monument.” So they were agreed. Then they departed and took their ways diverse. Sir Tristram passed through a great forest into a plain, till he came to a priory, and there he reposed him with a good man six days.\n\nThen departed Sir Tristram, and rode straight into Camelot to the monument of Merlin, and there he looked about him for Sir Palamedes. And he perceived a seemly knight, who came riding against him all in white, with a covered shield. When he came nigh Sir Tristram said aloud, “Welcome, sir knight, and well and truly have you kept your promise.” Then they made ready their shields and spears, and came together with all the might of their horses, so fiercely, that both the horses and the knights fell to the earth. And as soon as they might they quitted their horses, and struck together with bright swords as men of might, and each wounded the other wonderfully sore, so that the blood ran out upon the grass. Thus they fought for the space of four hours and never one would speak to the other one word. Then at last spake the white knight, and said, “Sir, thou fightest wonderful well, as ever I saw a knight; therefore, if it please you, tell me your name.” “Why dost thou ask my name?” said Sir Tristram; “art thou not Sir Palamedes?” “No, fair knight,” said he, “I am Sir Launcelot of the Lake.” “Alas!” said Sir Tristram, “what have I done? for you are the man of the world that I love best.” “Fair knight,” said Sir Launcelot, “tell me your name.” “Truly,” said he, “my name is Sir Tristram de Lionesse.” “Alas! alas!” said Sir Launcelot, “what adventure has befallen me!” And therewith Sir Launcelot kneeled down and yielded him up his sword; and Sir Tristram kneeled down and yielded him up his sword; and so either gave other the degree. And then they both went to the stone, and sat them down upon it and took off their helms and each kissed the other a hundred times. And then anon they rode toward Camelot, and on the way they met with Sir Gawain and Sir Gaheris, that had made promise to Arthur never to come again to the court till they had brought Sir Tristram with them.\n\n“Return again,” said Sir Launcelot, “for your quest is done; for I have met with Sir Tristram. Lo, here he is in his own person.” Then was Sir Gawain glad, and said to Sir Tristram, “Ye are welcome.” With this came King Arthur, and when he wist there was Sir Tristram, he ran unto him, and took him by the hand, and said, “Sir Tristram, ye are as welcome as any knight that ever came to this court.” Then Sir Tristram told the king how he came thither for to have had to do with Sir Palamedes, and how he had rescued him from Sir Breuse sans Pitie and the nine knights. Then King Arthur took Sir Tristram by the hand, and went to the Table Round, and Queen Guenever came, and many ladies with her, and all the ladies said with one voice, “Welcome, Sir Tristram.” “Welcome,” said the knights. “Welcome,” said Arthur, “for one of the best of knights, and the gentlest of the world, and the man of most worship; for of all manner of hunting thou bearest the prize, and of all measures of blowing thou art the beginning, and of all the terms of hunting and hawking ye are the inventor, and of all instruments of music ye are the best skilled; therefore, gentle knight,” said Arthur, “ye are welcome to this court.” And then King Arthur made Sir Tristram knight of the Table Round with great nobley and feasting as can be thought.\n\n# SIR TRISTRAM AS A SPORTSMAN\n\nTristram is often alluded to by the Romancers as the great authority and model in all matters relating to the chase. In the “Faery Queene,” Tristram, in answer to the inquiries of Sir Calidore, informs him of his name and parentage, and concludes:\n\n“All which my days I have not lewdly spent, Nor spilt the blossom of my tender years In idlesse; but, as was convenient, Have trained been with many noble feres In gentle thewes, and such like seemly leers;[51] ’Mongst which my most delight hath always been To hunt the salvage chace, amongst my peers, Of all that rangeth in the forest green, Of which none is to me unknown that yet was seen.\n\n“Ne is there hawk which mantleth on her perch, Whether high towering or accosting low, But I the measure of her flight do search, And all her prey, and all her diet know. Such be our joys, which in these forests grow.”\n————\n\n# THE ROUND TABLE\n\nTHE famous enchanter, Merlin, had exerted all his skill in fabricating the Round Table. Of the seats which surrounded it he had constructed thirteen, in memory of the thirteen Apostles. Twelve of these seats only could be occupied, and they only by knights of the highest fame; the thirteenth represented the seat of the traitor Judas. It remained always empty. It was called the perilous seat, ever since a rash and haughty Saracen knight had dared to place himself in it, when the earth opened and swallowed him up.\n\n“In our great hall there stood a vacant chair, Fashion’d by Merlin ere he past away, And carven with strange figures; and in and out The figures, like a serpent, ran a scroll Of letters in a tongue no man could read. And Merlin call’d it ‘The Siege perilous,’ Perilous for good and ill; ‘for there,’ he said, ‘No man could sit but he should lose himself.’” —The Holy Grail.\n\nA magic power wrote upon each seat the name of the knight who was entitled to sit in it. No one could succeed to a vacant seat unless he surpassed in valor and glorious deeds the knight who had occupied it before him; without this qualification he would be violently repelled by a hidden force. Thus proof was made of all those who presented themselves to replace any companions of the order who had fallen.\n\nOne of the principal seats, that of Moraunt of Ireland, had been vacant ten years, and his name still remained over it ever since the time when that distinguished champion fell beneath the sword of Sir Tristram. Arthur now took Tristram by the hand and led him to that seat. Immediately the most melodious sounds were heard, and exquisite perfumes filled the place; the name of Moraunt disappeared, and that of Tristram blazed forth in light. The rare modesty of Tristram had now to be subjected to a severe task; for the clerks charged with the duty of preserving the annals of the Round Table attended, and he was required by the law of his order to declare what feats of arms he had accomplished to entitle him to take that seat. This ceremony being ended, Tristram received the congratulations of all his companions. Sir Launcelot and Guenever took the occasion to speak to him of the fair Isoude, and to express their wish that some happy chance might bring her to the kingdom of Loegria.\n\nWhile Tristram was thus honored and caressed at the court of King Arthur, the most gloomy and malignant jealousy harassed the soul of Mark. He could not look upon Isoude without remembering that she loved Tristram, and the good fortune of his nephew goaded him to thoughts of vengeance. He at last resolved to go disguised into the kingdom of Loegria, attack Tristram by stealth, and put him to death. He took with him two knights, brought up in his court, who he thought were devoted to him; and, not willing to leave Isoude behind, named two of her maidens to attend her, together with her faithful Brengwain, and made them accompany him.\n\nHaving arrived in the neighborhood of Camelot, Mark imparted his plan to his two knights, but they rejected it with horror; nay, more, they declared that they would no longer remain in his service; and left him, giving him reason to suppose that they should repair to the court to accuse him before Arthur. It was necessary for Mark to meet and rebut their accusation; so, leaving Isoude in an abbey, he pursued his way alone to Camelot.\n\nMark had not ridden far when he encountered a party of knights of Arthur’s court, and would have avoided them, for he knew their habit of challenging to a just every stranger knight whom they met. But it was too late. They had seen his armor, and recognized him as a Cornish knight, and at once resolved to have some sport with him. It happened they had with them Daguenet, King Arthur’s fool, who, though deformed and weak of body, was not wanting in courage. The knights as Mark approached laid their plan that Daguenet should personate Sir Launcelot of the Lake, and challenge the Cornish knight. They equipped him in armor belonging to one of their number who was ill, and sent him forward to the cross-road to defy the strange knight. Mark, who saw that his antagonist was by no means formidable in appearance, was not disinclined to the combat; but when the dwarf rode towards him, calling out that he was Sir Launcelot of the Lake, his fears prevailed, he put spurs to his horse, and rode away at full speed, pursued by the shouts and laughter of the party.\n\nMeanwhile Isoude, remaining at the abbey with her faithful Brengwain, found her only amusement in walking occasionally in a forest adjoining the abbey. There, on the brink of a fountain girdled with trees, she thought of her love, and sometimes joined her voice and her harp in lays reviving the memory of its pains or pleasures. One day the caitiff knight, Breuse the Pitiless, heard her voice, concealed himself, and drew near. She sang:\n\n“Sweet silence, shadowy bower, and verdant lair, Ye court my troubled spirit to repose, Whilst I, such dear remembrance rises there, Awaken every echo with my woes.\n“Within these woods, by nature’s hand arrayed, A fountain springs, and feeds a thousand flowers; Ah! how my groans do all its murmurs aid! How my sad eyes do swell it with their showers!\n\n“What doth my knight the while? to him is given A double meed; in love and arms’ emprise, Him the Round Table elevates to heaven! Tristram! ah me! he hears not Isoude’s cries.”\n\nBreuse the Pitiless, who like most other caitiffs had felt the weight of Tristram’s arm, and hated him accordingly, at hearing his name breathed forth by the beautiful songstress, impelled by a double impulse, rushed forth from his concealment and laid hands on his victim. Isoude fainted, and Brengwain filled the air with her shrieks. Breuse carried Isoude to the place where he had left his horse; but the animal had got away from his bridle, and was at some distance. He was obliged to lay down his fair burden, and go in pursuit of his horse. Just then a knight came up, drawn by the cries of Brengwain, and demanded the cause of her distress. She could not speak, but pointed to her mistress lying insensible on the ground.\n\nBreuse had by this time returned, and the cries of Brengwain, renewed at seeing him, sufficiently showed the stranger the cause of the distress. Tristram spurred his horse towards Breuse, who, not unprepared, ran to the encounter. Breuse was unhorsed, and lay motionless, pretending to be dead; but when the stranger knight left him to attend to the distressed damsels, he mounted his horse, and made his escape.\n\nThe knight now approached Isoude, gently raised her head, drew aside the golden hair which covered her countenance, gazed thereon for an instant, uttered a cry, and fell back insensible. Brengwain came; her cares soon restored her mistress to life, and they then turned their attention to the fallen warrior. They raised his visor, and discovered the countenance of Sir Tristram. Isoude threw herself on the body of her lover, and bedewed his face with her tears. Their warmth revived the knight, and Tristram on awaking found himself in the arms of his dear Isoude.\n\nIt was the law of the Round Table that each knight after his admission should pass the next ten days in quest of adventures, during which time his companions might meet him in disguised armor and try their strength with him. Tristram had now been out seven days, and in that time had encountered many of the best knights of the Round Table, and acquitted himself with honor. During the remaining three days, Isoude remained at the abbey, under his protection, and then set out with her maidens, escorted by Sir Tristram, to rejoin King Mark at the court of Camelot.\n\nThis happy journey was one of the brightest epochs in the lives of Tristram and Isoude. He celebrated it by a lay upon the harp in a peculiar measure, to which the French give the name of Triolet.\n\n“With fair Isoude, and with love, Ah! how sweet the life I lead! How blest for ever thus to rove, With fair Isoude, and with love! As she wills, I live and move, And cloudless days to days succeed: With fair Isoude, and with love, Ah! how sweet the life I lead!\n\n“Journeying on from break of day, Feel you not fatigued, my fair? Yon green turf invites to play; Journeying on from day to day, Ah! let us to that shade away, Were it but to slumber there! Journeying on from break of day, Feel you not fatigued, my fair?”\n\nThey arrived at Camelot, where Sir Launcelot received them most cordially. Isoude was introduced to King Arthur and Queen Guenever, who welcomed her as a sister. As King Mark was held in arrest under the accusation of the two Cornish knights, Queen Isoude could not rejoin her husband, and Sir Launcelot placed his castle of La Joyeuse Garde at the disposal of his friends, who there took up their abode.\n\nKing Mark, who found himself obliged to confess the truth of the charge against him, or to clear himself by combat with his accusers, preferred the former, and King Arthur, as his crime had not been perpetrated, remitted the penalty, only enjoining upon him, under pain of his signal displeasure, to lay aside all thoughts of vengeance against his nephew. In the presence of the king and his court all parties were formally reconciled; Mark and his queen departed for their home, and Tristram remained at Arthur’s court.\n\n————\n\n# SIR PALAMEDES\n\nWHILE Sir Tristram and the fair Isoude abode yet at La Joyeuse Garde, Sir Tristram rode forth one day, without armor, having no weapon but his spear and his sword. And as he rode he came to a place where he saw two knights in battle, and one of them had gotten the better and the other lay overthrown. The knight who had the better was Sir Palamedes. When Sir Palamedes knew Sir Tristram, he cried out, “Sir Tristram, now we be met, and ere we depart we will redress our old wrongs.” “As for that,” said Sir Tristram, “there never yet was Christian man that might make his boast that I ever fled from him, and thou that art a Saracen shalt never say that of me.” And therewith Sir Tristram made his horse to run, and with all his might came straight upon Sir Palamedes, and broke his spear upon him. Then he drew his sword and struck at Sir Palamedes six great strokes, upon his helm. Sir Palamedes saw that Sir Tristram had not his armor on, and he marvelled at his rashness and his great folly; and said to himself, “If I meet and slay him, I am shamed wheresoever I go.” Then Sir Tristram cried out and said, “Thou coward knight, why wilt thou not do battle with me? for have thou no doubt I shall endure all thy malice.” “Ah, Sir Tristram!” said Sir Palamedes, “thou knowest I may not fight with thee for shame; for thou art here naked, and I am armed; now I require that thou answer me a question that I shall ask you.” “Tell me what it is,” said Sir Tristram. “I put the case,” said Palamedes, “that you were well armed, and I naked as ye be; what would you do to me now, by your true knighthood?” “Ah!” said Sir Tristram, “now I understand thee well, Sir Palamedes; and, as God bless me, what I shall say shall not be said for fear that I have of thee. But if it were so, thou shouldest depart from me, for I would not have to do with thee.” “No more will I with thee,” said Sir Palamedes, “and therefore ride forth on thy way.” “As for that, I may choose,” said Sir Tristram, “either to ride or to abide. But, Sir Palamedes, I marvel at one thing,—that thou art so good a knight, yet that thou wilt not be christened.” “As for that,” said Sir Palamedes, “I may not yet be christened, for a vow which I made many years ago; yet in my heart I believe in our Saviour and his mild mother, Mary; but I have yet one battle to do, and when that is done I will be christened, with a good will.” “By my head,” said Sir Tristram, “as for that one battle, thou shalt seek it no longer; for yonder is a knight, whom you have smitten down. Now help me to be clothed in his armor, and I will soon fulfil thy vow.” “As ye will,” said Sir Palamedes, “so shall it be.” So they rode both unto that knight that sat on a bank; and Sir Tristram saluted him, and he full weary saluted him again. “Sir,” said Sir Tristram, “I pray you to lend me your whole armor; for I am unarmed, and I must do battle with this knight.” “Sir,” said the hurt knight, “you shall have it, with a right good will.” Then Sir Tristram unarmed Sir Galleron, for that was the name of the hurt knight, and he as well as he could helped to arm Sir Tristram. Then Sir Tristram mounted upon his own horse, and in his hand he took Sir Galleron’s spear. Thereupon Sir Palamedes was ready, and so they came hurling together, and each smote the other in the midst of their shields. Sir Palamedes’ spear broke, and Sir Tristram smote down the horse. Then Sir Palamedes leapt from his horse, and drew out his sword. That saw Sir Tristram, and therewith he alighted and tied his horse to a tree. Then they came together as two wild beasts, lashing the one on the other, and so fought more than two hours; and often Sir Tristram smote such strokes at Sir Palamedes that he made him to kneel, and Sir Palamedes broke away Sir Tristram’s shield, and wounded him. Then Sir Tristram was wroth out of measure, and he rushed to Sir Palamedes and wounded him passing sore through the shoulder, and by fortune smote Sir Palamedes’ sword out of his hand. And if Sir Palamedes had stooped for his sword Sir Tristram had slain him. Then Sir Palamedes stood and beheld his sword with a full sorrowful heart. “Now,” said Sir Tristram, “I have thee at a vantage, as thou hadst me to-day; but it shall never be said, in court, or among good knights, that Sir Tristram did slay any knight that was weaponless; therefore take thou thy sword, and let us fight this battle to the end.” Then spoke Sir Palamedes to Sir Tristram: “I have no wish to fight this battle any more. The offence that I have done unto you is not so great but that, if it please you, we may be friends. All that I have offended is for the love of the queen, La Belle Isoude, and I dare maintain that she is peerless among ladies; and for that offence ye have given me many grievous and sad strokes, and some I have given you again. Wherefore I require you, my lord Sir Tristram, forgive me all that I have offended you, and this day have me unto the next church; and first I will be clean confessed, and after that see you that I be truly baptized, and then we will ride together unto the court of my lord, King Arthur, so that we may be there at the feast of Pentecost.” “Now take your horse,” said Sir Tristram, “and as you have said, so shall it be done.” So they took their horses, and Sir Galleron rode with them. When they came to the church of Carlisle, the bishop commanded to fill a great vessel with water; and when he had hallowed it, he then confessed Sir Palamedes clean, and christened him, and Sir Tristram and Sir Galleron were his godfathers. Then soon after they departed, and rode towards Camelot, where the noble King Arthur and Queen Guenever were keeping a court royal. And the king and all the court were glad that Sir Palamedes was christened. Then Sir Tristram returned again to La Joyeuse Garde, and Sir Palamedes went his way.\n\nNot long after these events Sir Gawain returned from Brittany, and related to King Arthur the adventure which befell him in the forest of Breciliande, how Merlin had there spoken to him, and enjoined him to charge the king to go without delay upon the quest of the Holy Greal. While King Arthur deliberated Tristram determined to enter upon the quest, and the more readily, as it was well known to him that this holy adventure would, if achieved, procure him the pardon of all his sins. He immediately departed for the kingdom of Brittany, hoping there to obtain from Merlin counsel as to the proper course to pursue to insure success.\n\n————\n\n# SIR TRISTRAM\n\nON arriving in Brittany Tristram found King Hoel engaged in a war with a rebellious vassal, and hard pressed by his enemy. His best knights had fallen in a late battle, and he knew not where to turn for assistance. Tristram volunteered his aid. It was accepted; and the army of Hoel, led by Tristram, and inspired by his example, gained a complete victory. The king, penetrated by the most lively sentiments of gratitude, and having informed himself of Tristram’s birth, offered him his daughter in marriage. The princess was beautiful and accomplished, and bore the same name with the Queen of Cornwall; but this one is designated by the Romancers as Isoude of the White Hands, to distinguish her from Isoude the Fair.\n\nHow can we describe the conflict that agitated the heart of Tristram? He adored the first Isoude, but his love for her was hopeless, and not unaccompanied by remorse. Moreover, the sacred quest on which he had now entered demanded of him perfect purity of life. It seemed as if a happy destiny had provided for him in the charming princess Isoude of the White Hands the best security for all his good resolutions. This last reflection determined him. They were married, and passed some months in tranquil happiness at the court of King Hoel. The pleasure which Tristram felt in his wife’s society increased day by day. An inward grace seemed to stir within him from the moment when he took the oath to go on the quest of the Holy Greal; it seemed even to triumph over the power of the magic love-potion.\n\nThe war, which had been quelled for a time, now burst out anew. Tristram as usual was foremost in every danger. The enemy was worsted in successive conflicts, and at last shut himself up in his principal city. Tristram led on the attack of the city. As he mounted a ladder to scale the walls he was struck on the head by a fragment of rock, which the besieged threw down upon him. It bore him to the ground, where he lay insensible.\n\nAs soon as he recovered consciousness he demanded to be carried to his wife. The princess, skilled in the art of surgery, would not suffer any one but herself to touch her beloved husband. Her fair hands bound up his wounds; Tristram kissed them with gratitude, which began to grow into love. At first the devoted cares of Isoude seemed to meet with great success; but after a while these flattering appearances vanished, and, in spite of all her care, the malady grew more serious day by day.\n\nIn this perplexity, an old squire of Tristram’s reminded his master that the princess of Ireland, afterwards queen of Cornwall, had once cured him under circumstances quite as discouraging. He called Isoude of the White Hands to him, told her of his former cure, added that he believed that the Queen Isoude could heal him, and that he felt sure that she would come to his relief, if sent for.\n\nIsoude of the White Hands consented that Gesnes, a trusty man and skilful navigator, should be sent to Cornwall. Tristram called him, and, giving him a ring, “Take this,” he said, “to the Queen of Cornwall. Tell her that Tristram, near to death, demands her aid. If you succeed in bringing her with you, place white sails to your vessel on your return, that we may know of your success when the vessel first heaves in sight. But if Queen Isoude refuses, put on black sails; they will be the presage of my impending death.”\n\nGesnes performed his mission successfully. King Mark happened to be absent from his capital, and the queen readily consented to return with the bark to Brittany. Gesnes clothed his vessel in the whitest of sails, and sped his way back to Brittany.\n\nMeantime the wound of Tristram grew more desperate day by day. His strength, quite prostrated, no longer permitted him to be carried to the seaside daily, as had been his custom from the first moment when it was possible for the bark to be on the way homeward. He called a young damsel, and gave her in charge to keep watch in the direction of Cornwall, and to come and tell him the color of the sails of the first vessel she should see approaching.\nWhen Isoude of the White Hands consented that the queen of Cornwall should be sent for, she had not known all the reasons which she had for fearing the influence which renewed intercourse with that princess might have on her own happiness. She had now learned more, and felt the danger more keenly. She thought, if she could only keep the knowledge of the queen’s arrival from her husband, she might employ in his service any resources which her skill could supply, and still avert the dangers which she apprehended. When the vessel was seen approaching, with its white sails sparkling in the sun, the damsel, by command of her mistress, carried word to Tristram that the sails were black.\n\nTristram, penetrated with inexpressible grief, breathed a profound sigh, turned away his face, and said, “Alas, my beloved! we shall never see one another again!” Then he commended himself to God, and breathed his last.\nThe death of Tristram was the first intelligence which the queen of Cornwall heard on landing. She was conducted almost senseless into the chamber of Tristram, and expired holding him in her arms.\n\nTristram, before his death, had requested that his body should be sent to Cornwall, and that his sword, with a letter he had written, should be delivered to King Mark. The remains of Tristram and Isoude were embarked in a vessel, along with the sword, which was presented to the king of Cornwall. He was melted with tenderness when he saw the weapon which slew Moraunt of Ireland,—which had so often saved his life, and redeemed the honor of his kingdom. In the letter Tristram begged pardon of his uncle, and related the story of the amorous draught.\n\nMark ordered the lovers to be buried in his own chapel. From the tomb of Tristram there sprung a vine, which went along the walls, and descended into the grave of the queen. It was cut down three times, but each time sprung up again more vigorous than before, and this wonderful plant has ever since shaded the tombs of Tristram and Isoude.\n\n————\nSpenser introduces Sir Tristram in his “Faery Queene.” In Book VI.,\n\nCanto ii., Sir Calidore encounters in the forest a young hunter, whom he thus describes:\n\n“Him steadfastly he marked, and saw to be A goodly youth of amiable grace, Yet but a slender slip, that scarce did see Yet seventeen yeares; but tall and faire of face, That sure he deemed him borne of noble race. All in a woodman’s jacket he was clad Of Lincoln greene, belayed with silver lace; And on his head an hood with aglets[52] sprad, And by his side his hunter’s horne he hanging had.\n\n“Buskins he wore of costliest cordawayne, Pinckt upon gold, and paled part per part,[53] As then the guize was for each gentle swayne. In his right hand he held a trembling dart, Whose fellow he before had sent apart; And in his left he held a sharp bore-speare, With which he wont to launch the salvage heart Of many a lyon, and of many a beare, That first unto his hand in chase did happen neare.”\n\n————\n\nTHE father and two elder brothers of Perceval had fallen in battle or tournaments, and hence, as the last hope of his family, his mother retired with him into a solitary region, where he was brought up in total ignorance of arms and chivalry. He was allowed no weapon but “a lyttel Scots spere,” which was the only thing of all “her lordes faire gere” that his mother carried to the wood with her. In the use of this he became so skilful, that he could kill with it not only the animals of the chase for the table, but even birds on the wing. At length, however, Perceval was roused to a desire of military renown by seeing in the forest five knights who were in complete armor. He said to his mother, “Mother, what are those yonder?” “They are angels, my son,” said she. “By my faith, I will go and become an angel with them.” And Perceval went to the road and met them. “Tell me, good lad,” said one of them, “sawest thou a knight pass this way either to-day or yesterday?” “I know not,” said he, “what a knight is.” “Such an one as I am,” said the knight. “If thou wilt tell me what I ask thee, I will tell thee what thou askest me.” “Gladly will I do so,” said Sir Owain, for that was the knight’s name. “What is this?” demanded Perceval, touching the saddle. “It is a saddle,” said Owain. Then he asked about all the accoutrements which he saw upon the men and the horses, and about the arms, and what they were for, and how they were used. And Sir Owain showed him all those things fully. And Perceval in return gave him such information as he had.\n\nThen Perceval returned to his mother, and said to her, “Mother, those were not angels, but honorable knights.” Then his mother swooned away. And Perceval went to the place where they kept the horses that carried firewood and provisions for the castle, and he took a bony, piebald horse, which seemed to him the strongest of them. And he pressed a pack into the form of a saddle, and with twisted twigs he imitated the trappings which he had seen upon the horses. When he came again to his mother, the countess had recovered from her swoon. “My son,” said she, “desirest thou to ride forth?” “Yes, with thy leave,” said he. “Go forward, then,” she said, “to the court of Arthur, where there are the best and the noblest and the most bountiful of men, and tell him thou art Perceval, the son of Pelenore, and ask of him to bestow knighthood on thee. And whenever thou seest a church, repeat there thy pater-noster; and if thou see meat and drink, and hast need of them, thou mayest take them. If thou hear an outcry of one in distress, proceed toward it, especially if it be the cry of a woman, and render her what service thou canst. If thou see a fair jewel, win it, for thus shalt thou acquire fame; yet freely give it to another, for thus thou shalt obtain praise. If thou see a fair woman, pay court to her, for thus thou wilt obtain love.”\n\nAfter this discourse Perceval mounted the horse and taking a number of sharp-pointed sticks in his hand he rode forth. And he rode far in the woody wilderness without food or drink. At last he came to an opening in the wood where he saw a tent, and as he thought it might be a church he said his pater-noster to it. And he went towards it; and the door of the tent was open. And Perceval dismounted and entered the tent. In the tent he found a maiden sitting, with a golden frontlet on her forehead and a gold ring on her hand. And Perceval said, “Maiden, I salute you, for my mother told me whenever I met a lady I must respectfully salute her.” Perceiving in one corner of the tent some food, two flasks full of wine, and some boar’s flesh roasted, he said, “My mother told me, whenever I saw meat and drink to take it.” And he ate greedily, for he was very hungry. The maiden said, “Sir, thou hadst best go quickly from here, for fear that my friends should come, and evil should befall you.” But Perceval said, “My mother told me wheresoever I saw a fair jewel to take it,” and he took the gold ring from her finger, and put it on his own; and he gave the maiden his own ring in exchange for hers; then he mounted his horse and rode away.\n\nPerceval journeyed on till he arrived at Arthur’s court. And it so happened that just at that time an uncourteous knight had offered Queen Guenever a gross insult. For when her page was serving the queen with a golden goblet, this knight struck the arm of the page and dashed the wine in the queen’s face and over her stomacher. Then he said, “If any have boldness to avenge this insult to Guenever, let him follow me to the meadow.” So the knight took his horse and rode to the meadow, carrying away the golden goblet. And all the household hung down their heads and no one offered to follow the knight to take vengeance upon him. For it seemed to them that no one would have ventured on so daring an outrage unless he possessed such powers, through magic or charms, that none could be able to punish him. Just then, behold, Perceval entered the hall upon the bony, piebald horse, with his uncouth trappings. In the centre of the hall stood Kay the Seneschal. “Tell me, tall man,” said Perceval, “is that Arthur yonder?” “What wouldst thou with Arthur?” asked Kay. “My mother told me to go to Arthur and receive knighthood from him.” “By my faith,” said he, “thou art all too meanly equipped with horse and with arms.” Then all the household began to jeer and laugh at him. But there was a certain damsel who had been a whole year at Arthur’s court, and had never been known to smile. And the king’s fool[54] had said that this damsel would not smile till she had seen him who would be the flower of chivalry. Now this damsel came up to Perceval and told him, smiling, that if he lived he would be one of the bravest and best of knights. “Truly,” said Kay, “thou art ill taught to remain a year at Arthur’s court, with choice of society, and smile on no one, and now before the face of Arthur and all his knights to call such a man as this the flower of knighthood;” and he gave her a box on the ear, that she fell senseless to the ground. Then said Kay to Perceval, “Go after the knight who went hence to the meadow, overthrow him and recover the golden goblet, and possess thyself of his horse and arms, and thou shalt have knighthood.” “I will do so, tall man,” said Perceval. So he turned his horse’s head toward the meadow. And when he came there, the knight was riding up and down, proud of his strength and valor and noble mien. “Tell me,” said the knight, “didst thou see any one coming after me from the court?” “The tall man that was there,” said Perceval, “told me to come and overthrow thee, and to take from thee the goblet and thy horse and armor for myself.” “Silence!” said the knight; “go back to the court, and tell Arthur either to come himself, or to send some other to fight with me; and unless he do so quickly, I will not wait for him.” “By my faith,” said Perceval, “choose thou whether it shall be willingly or unwillingly, for I will have the horse and the arms and the goblet.” Upon this the knight ran at him furiously, and struck him a violent blow with the shaft of his spear, between the neck and the shoulder. “Ha, ha, lad!” said Perceval, “my mother’s servants were not used to play with me in this wise; so thus will I play with thee.” And he threw at him one of his sharp-pointed sticks, and it struck him in the eye, and came out at the back of his head, so that he fell down lifeless.\n\n“Verily,” said Sir Owain, the son of Urien, to Kay the Seneschal, “thou wast ill-advised to send that madman after the knight, for he must either be overthrown or flee, and either way it will be a disgrace to Arthur and his warriors; therefore will I go to see what has befallen him.” So Sir Owain went to the meadow, and he found Perceval trying in vain to get the dead knight’s armor off, in order to clothe himself with it. Sir Owain unfastened the armor, and helped Perceval to put it on, and taught him how to put his foot in the stirrup, and use the spur; for Perceval had never used stirrup nor spur, but rode without saddle, and urged on his horse with a stick. Then Owain would have had him return to the court to receive the praise that was his due; but Perceval said, “I will not come to the court till I have encountered the tall man that is there, to revenge the injury he did to the maiden. But take thou the goblet to Queen Guenever, and tell King Arthur that, wherever I am, I will be his vassal, and will do him what profit and service I can.” And Sir Owain went back to the court, and related all these things to Arthur and Guenever, and to all the household.\n\nAnd Perceval rode forward. And he came to a lake on the side of which was a fair castle, and on the border of the lake he saw a hoary-headed man sitting upon a velvet cushion, and his attendants were fishing in the lake. When the hoary-headed man beheld Perceval approaching, he arose and went into the castle. Perceval rode to the castle, and the door was open, and he entered the hall. And the hoary-headed man received Perceval courteously, and asked him to sit by him on the cushion. When it was time the tables were set, and they went to meat. And when they had finished their meat the hoary-headed man asked Perceval if he knew how to fight with the sword. “I know not,” said Perceval, “but were I to be taught, doubtless I should.” And the hoary-headed man said to him, “I am thy uncle, thy mother’s brother; I am called King Pecheur.[55] Thou shalt remain with me a space, in order to learn the manners and customs of different countries, and courtesy and noble bearing. And this do thou remember, if thou seest aught to cause thy wonder, ask not the meaning of it; if no one has the courtesy to inform thee, the reproach will not fall upon thee, but upon me that am thy teacher.” While Perceval and his uncle discoursed together, Perceval beheld two youths enter the hall bearing a golden cup and a spear of mighty size, with blood dropping from its point to the ground. And when all the company saw this they began to weep and lament. But for all that, the man did not break off his discourse with Perceval. And as he did not tell him the meaning of what he saw, he forebore to ask him concerning it. Now the cup that Perceval saw was the Sangreal, and the spear the sacred spear; and afterwards King Pecheur removed with those sacred relics into a far country.\n. . . . . . .\n\nOne evening Perceval entered a valley, and came to a hermit’s cell; and the hermit welcomed him gladly, and there he spent the night. And in the morning he arose, and when he went forth, behold! a shower of snow had fallen in the night, and a hawk had killed a wild-fowl in front of the cell. And the noise of the horse had scared the hawk away, and a raven alighted on the bird. And Perceval stood and compared the blackness of the raven and the whiteness of the snow and the redness of the blood to the hair of the lady that best he loved, which was blacker than jet, and to her skin, which was whiter than the snow, and to the two red spots upon her cheeks, which were redder than the blood upon the snow.\n\nNow Arthur and his household were in search of Perceval, and by chance they came that way. “Know ye,” said Arthur, “who is the knight with the long spear that stands by the brook up yonder?” “Lord,” said one of them, “I will go and learn who he is.” So the youth came to the place where Perceval was, and asked him what he did thus, and who he was. But Perceval was so intent upon his thought that he gave him no answer. Then the youth thrust at Perceval with his lance; and Perceval turned upon him, and struck him to the ground. And when the youth returned to the king, and told how rudely he had been treated, Sir Kay said, “I will go myself.” And when he greeted Perceval, and got no answer, he spoke to him rudely and angrily. And Perceval thrust at him with his lance, and cast him down so that he broke his arm and his shoulder-blade. And while he lay thus stunned his horse returned back at a wild and prancing pace.\n\nThen said Sir Gawain, surnamed the Golden-Tongued, because he was the most courteous knight in Arthur’s court: “It is not fitting that any should disturb an honorable knight from his thought unadvisedly; for either he is pondering some damage that he has sustained, or he is thinking of the lady whom best he loves. If it seem well to thee, lord, I will go and see if this knight has changed from his thought, and if he has, I will ask him courteously to come and visit thee.”\n\nAnd Perceval was resting on the shaft of his spear, pondering the same thought, and Sir Gawain came to him, and said: “If I thought it would be as agreeable to thee as it would be to me, I would converse with thee. I have also a message from Arthur unto thee, to pray thee to come and visit him. And two men have been before on this errand.” “That is true,” said Perceval; “and uncourteously they came. They attacked me, and I was annoyed thereat.” Then he told him the thought that occupied his mind, and Gawain said, “This was not an ungentle thought, and I should marvel if it were pleasant for thee to be drawn from it.” Then said Perceval, “Tell me, is Sir Kay in Arthur’s court?” “He is,” said Gawain; “and truly he is the knight who fought with thee last.” “Verily,” said Perceval, “I am not sorry to have thus avenged the insult to the smiling maiden.” Then Perceval told him his name, and said, “Who art thou?” And he replied, “I am Gawain.” “I am right glad to meet thee,” said Perceval, “for I have everywhere heard of thy prowess and uprightness; and I solicit thy fellowship.” “Thou shalt have it, by my faith; and grant me thine,” said he. “Gladly will I do so,” answered Perceval.\n\nSo they went together to Arthur, and saluted him.\n\n“Behold, lord,” said Gawain, “him whom thou hast sought so long.” “Welcome unto thee, chieftain,” said Arthur. And hereupon there came the queen and her handmaidens, and Perceval saluted them. And they were rejoiced to see him, and bade him welcome. And Arthur did him great honor and respect and they returned towards Caerleon.\n\n————\n\n# THE SANGREAL, OR HOLY GRAAL\n\nTHE Sangreal was the cup from which our Saviour drank at his last supper. He was supposed to have given it to Joseph of Arimathea, who carried it to Europe, together with the spear with which the soldier pierced the Saviour’s side. From generation to generation, one of the descendants of Joseph of Arimathea had been devoted to the guardianship of these precious relics; but on the sole condition of leading a life of purity in thought, word, and deed. For a long time the Sangreal was visible to all pilgrims, and its presence conferred blessings upon the land in which it was preserved. But at length one of those holy men to whom its guardianship had descended so far forgot the obligation of his sacred office as to look with unhallowed eye upon a young female pilgrim whose robe was accidentally loosened as she knelt before him. The sacred lance instantly punished his frailty, spontaneously falling upon him, and inflicting a deep wound. The marvellous wound could by no means be healed, and the guardian of the Sangreal was ever after called “Le Roi Pescheur,”—The Sinner King. The Sangreal withdrew its visible presence from the crowds who came to worship, and an iron age succeeded to the happiness which its presence had diffused among the tribes of Britain.\n\n“But then the times Grew to such evil that the Holy cup Was caught away to heaven and disappear’d.” —The Holy Grail.\n\nWe have told in the history of Merlin how that great prophet and enchanter sent a message to King Arthur by Sir Gawain, directing him to undertake the recovery of the Sangreal, informing him at the same time that the knight who should accomplish that sacred quest was already born, and of a suitable age to enter upon it. Sir Gawain delivered his message, and the king was anxiously revolving in his mind how best to achieve the enterprise, when, at the vigil of Pentecost, all the fellowship of the Round Table being met together at Camelot, as they sat at meat, suddenly there was heard a clap of thunder, and then a bright light burst forth, and every knight, as he looked on his fellow, saw him, in seeming, fairer than ever before. All the hall was filled with sweet odors, and every knight had such meat and drink as he best loved. Then there entered into the hall the Holy Graal, covered with white samite, so that none could see it, and it passed through the hall suddenly, and disappeared. During this time no one spoke a word, but when they had recovered breath to speak King Arthur said, “Certainly we ought greatly to thank the Lord for what he hath showed us this day.” Then Sir Gawain rose up, and made a vow that for twelve months and a day he would seek the Sangreal, and not return till he had seen it, if so he might speed. When they of the Round Table heard Sir Gawain say so, they arose, the most part of them, and vowed the same. When King Arthur heard this, he was greatly displeased, for he knew well that they might not gainsay their vows. “Alas!” said he to Sir Gawain, “you have nigh slain me with the vow and promise that ye have made, for ye have bereft me of the fairest fellowship that ever were seen together in any realm of the world; for when they shall depart hence, I am sure that all shall never meet more in this world.”\n\n# SIR GALAHAD\n\nAt that time there entered the hall a good old man, and with him he brought a young knight, and these words he said: “Peace be with you, fair lords.” Then the old man said unto King Arthur, “Sir, I bring you here a young knight that is of kings’ lineage, and of the kindred of Joseph of Arimathea, being the son of Dame Elaine, the daughter of King Pelles, king of the foreign country.” Now the name of the young knight was Sir Galahad, and he was the son of Sir Launcelot du Lac; but he had dwelt with his mother, at the court of King Pelles, his grandfather, till now he was old enough to bear arms, and his mother had sent him in the charge of a holy hermit to King Arthur’s court. Then Sir Launcelot beheld his son, and had great joy of him. And Sir Bohort told his fellows, “Upon my life, this young knight shall come to great worship.” The noise was great in all the court, so that it came to the queen. And she said, “I would fain see him, for he must needs be a noble knight, for so is his father.” And the queen and her ladies all said that he resembled much unto his father; and he was seemly and demure as a dove, with all manner of good features, that in the whole world men might not find his match. And King Arthur said, “God make him a good man, for beauty faileth him not, as any that liveth.”\n\nThen the hermit led the young knight to the Siege Perilous; and he lifted up the cloth, and found there letters that said, “This is the seat of Sir Galahad, the good knight;” and he made him sit in that seat. And all the knights of the Round Table marvelled greatly at Sir Galahad, seeing him sit securely in that seat, and said, “This is he by whom the Sangreal shall be achieved, for there never sat one before in that seat without being mischieved.”\n\nOn the next day the king said, “Now, at this quest of the Sangreal shall all ye of the Round Table depart, and never shall I see you again altogether; therefore I will that ye all repair to the meadow of Camelot, for to just and tourney yet once more before ye depart.” But all the meaning of the king was to see Sir Galahad proved. So then were they all assembled in the meadow. Then Sir Galahad, by request of the king and queen, put on his harness and his helm, but shield would he take none for any prayer of the king. And the queen was in a tower, with all her ladies, to behold that tournament. Then Sir Galahad rode into the midst of the meadow; and there he began to break spears marvellously, so that all men had wonder of him, for he surmounted all knights that encountered with him, except two, Sir Launcelot and Sir Perceval.\n\n“So many knights, that all the people cried, And almost burst the barriers in their heat, Shouting ‘Sir Galahad and Sir Perceval!’” —Sir Galahad.\n\nThen the king, at the queen’s request, made him to alight, and presented him to the queen; and she said, “Never two men resembled one another more than he and Sir Launcelot, and therefore it is no marvel that he is like him in prowess.”\n\nThen the king and the queen went to the minster, and the knights followed them. And after the service was done they put on their helms and departed, and there was great sorrow. They rode through the streets of Camelot, and there was weeping of the rich and poor; and the king turned away, and might not speak for weeping. And so they departed, and every knight took the way that him best liked.\n\nSir Galahad rode forth without shield, and rode four days, and found no adventure. And on the fourth day he came to a white abbey; and there he was received with great reverence, and led to a chamber. He met there two knights, King Bagdemagus and Sir Uwaine, and they made of him great solace. “Sirs,” said Sir Galahad, “what adventure brought you hither?” “Sir,” said they, “it is told us that within this place is a shield, which no man may bear unless he be worthy; and if one unworthy should attempt to bear it, it shall surely do him a mischief.” Then King Bagdemagus said, “I fear not to bear it, and that shall ye see to-morrow.”\n\nSo on the morrow they arose, and heard mass; then King Bagdemagus asked where the adventurous shield was. Anon a monk led him behind an altar, where the shield hung, as white as snow; but in the midst there was a red cross. Then King Bagdemagus took the shield, and bare it out of the minster; and he said to Sir Galahad, “If it please you, abide here till ye know how I shall speed.”\n\nThen King Bagdemagus and his squire rode forth; and when they had ridden a mile or two, they saw a goodly knight come towards them, in white armor, horse and all; and he came as fast as his horse might run, with his spear in the rest; and King Bagdemagus directed his spear against him, and broke it upon the white knight, but the other struck him so hard that he broke the mails, and thrust him through the right shoulder, for the shield covered him not, and so he bare him from his horse. Then the white knight turned his horse and rode away.\n\nThen the squire went to King Bagdemagus, and asked him whether he were sore wounded or not. “I am sore wounded,” said he, “and full hardly shall I escape death.” Then the squire set him on his horse, and brought him to an abbey; and there he was taken down softly, and unarmed, and laid in a bed, and his wound was looked to, for he lay there long, and hardly escaped with his life. And the squire brought the shield back to the abbey.\n\nThe next day Sir Galahad took the shield, and within a while he came to the hermitage, where he met the white knight, and each saluted the other courteously. “Sir,” said Sir Galahad, “can you tell me the marvel of the shield?” “Sir,” said the white knight, “that shield belonged of old to the gentle knight, Joseph of Arimathea; and when he came to die he said, ‘Never shall man bear this shield about his neck but he shall repent it, unto the time that Sir Galahad the good knight bear it, the last of my lineage, the which shall do many marvellous deeds.’” And then the white knight vanished away.\n\n# SIR GAWAIN\n\nAfter Sir Gawain departed, he rode many days, both toward and forward, and at last he came to the abbey where Sir Galahad took the white shield. And they told Sir Gawain of the marvellous adventure that Sir Galahad had done. “Truly,” said Sir Gawain, “I am not happy that I took not the way that he went, for, if I may meet with him, I will not part from him lightly, that I may partake with him all the marvellous adventures which he shall achieve.” “Sir,” said one of the monks, “he will not be of your fellowship.” “Why?” said Sir Gawain. “Sir,” said he, “because ye be sinful, and he is blissful.” Then said the monk, “Sir Gawain, thou must do penance for thy sins.” “Sir, what penance shall I do?” “Such as I will show,” said the good man. “Nay,” said Sir Gawain, “I will do no penance, for we knights adventurous often suffer great woe and pain.” “Well,” said the good man; and he held his peace. And Sir Gawain departed.\n\nNow it happened, not long after this, that Sir Gawain and Sir Hector rode together, and they came to a castle where was a great tournament. And Sir Gawain and Sir Hector joined themselves to the party that seemed the weaker, and they drove before them the other party. Then suddenly came into the lists a knight, bearing a white shield with a red cross, and by adventure he came by Sir Gawain, and he smote him so hard that he clave his helm and wounded his head, so that Sir Gawain fell to the earth. When Sir Hector saw that, he knew that the knight with the white shield was Sir Galahad, and he thought it no wisdom to abide him, and also for natural love, that he was his uncle. Then Sir Galahad retired privily, so that none knew where he had gone. And Sir Hector raised up Sir Gawain, and said, “Sir, me seemeth your quest is done.” “It is done,” said Sir Gawain; “I shall seek no further.” Then Gawain was borne into the castle, and unarmed, and laid in a rich bed, and a leech found to search his wound. And Sir Gawain and Sir Hector abode together, for Sir Hector would not away till Sir Gawain were whole.\n\n————\n\n# SIR LAUNCELOT\n\nTHE SANGREAL (Continued)\nSIR LAUNCELOT rode overthwart and endlong in a wide forest, and held no path but as wild adventure led him.\n\n“My golden spurs now bring to me, And bring to me my richest mail, For to-morrow I go over land and sea In search of the Holy, Holy Grail. Shall never a bed for me be spread, Nor shall a pillow be under my head, Till I begin my vow to keep. Here on the rushes will I sleep, And perchance there may come a vision true Ere day create the world anew.” —Lowell’s Holy Grail.\n\nAnd at last he came to a stone cross. Then Sir Launcelot looked round him, and saw an old chapel. So he tied his horse to a tree, and put off his shield, and hung it upon a tree; and then he went into the chapel, and looked through a place where the wall was broken. And within he saw a fair altar, full richly arrayed with cloth of silk; and there stood a fair candlestick, which bare six great candles, and the candlestick was of silver. When Sir Launcelot saw this sight, he had a great wish to enter the chapel, but he could find no place where he might enter. Then was he passing heavy and dismayed. And he returned and came again to his horse, and took off his saddle and his bridle, and let him pasture; and unlaced his helm, and ungirded his sword, and laid him down to sleep upon his shield before the cross.\n\nAnd as he lay, half waking and half sleeping, he saw come by him two palfreys, both fair and white, which bare a litter, on which lay a sick knight. And when he was nigh the cross, he there abode still. And Sir Launcelot heard him say, “O sweet Lord, when shall this sorrow leave me, and when shall the holy vessel come by me whereby I shall be healed?” And thus a great while complained the knight, and Sir Launcelot heard it. Then Sir Launcelot saw the candlestick, with the lighted tapers, come before the cross, but he could see nobody that brought it. Also there came a salver of silver and the holy vessel of the Sangreal; and therewithal the sick knight sat him upright, and held up both his hands, and said, “Fair, sweet Lord, which is here within the holy vessel, take heed to me, that I may be whole of this great malady.” And therewith, upon his hands and upon his knees, he went so nigh that he touched the holy vessel and kissed it. And anon he was whole. Then the holy vessel went into the chapel again, with the candlestick and the light, so that Sir Launcelot wist not what became of it.\n\nThen the sick knight rose up and kissed the cross; and anon his squire brought him his arms and asked his lord how he did. “I thank God right heartily,” said he, “for, through the holy vessel, I am healed. But I have great marvel of this sleeping knight, who hath had neither grace nor power to awake during the time that the holy vessel hath been here present.” “I dare it right well say,” said the squire, “that this same knight is stained with some manner of deadly sin, whereof he was never confessed.” So they departed.\n\nThen anon Sir Launcelot waked, and set himself upright, and bethought him of what he had seen and whether it were dreams or not. And he was passing heavy, and wist not what to do. And he said: “My sin and my wretchedness hath brought me into great dishonor. For when I sought worldly adventures and worldly desires, I ever achieved them, and had the better in every place, and never was I discomfited in any quarrel, were it right or wrong. And now I take upon me the adventure of holy things, I see and understand that mine old sin hindereth me, so that I had no power to stir nor to speak when the holy blood appeared before me.” So thus he sorrowed till it was day, and heard the fowls of the air sing. Then was he somewhat comforted.\n\nThen he departed from the cross into the forest. And there he found a hermitage, and a hermit therein, who was going to mass. So when mass was done Sir Launcelot called the hermit to him, and prayed him for charity to hear his confession. “With a good will,” said the good man. And then he told that good man all his life, and how he had loved a queen unmeasurably many years. “And all my great deeds of arms that I have done I did the most part for the queen’s sake, and for her sake would I do battle, were it right or wrong, and never did I battle all only for God’s sake, but for to win worship, and to cause me to be better beloved; and little or naught I thanked God for it. I pray you counsel me.”\n\n“I will counsel you,” said the hermit, “if ye will insure me that ye will never come in that queen’s fellowship as much as ye may forbear.” And then Sir Launcelot promised the hermit, by his faith, that he would no more come in her company. “Look that your heart and your mouth accord,” said the good man, “and I shall insure you that ye shall have more worship than ever ye had.”\n\nThen the good man enjoined Sir Launcelot such penance as he might do, and he assailed Sir Launcelot and made him abide with him all that day. And Sir Launcelot repented him greatly.\n\n# SIR PERCEVAL\n\nSir Perceval departed and rode till the hour of noon; and he met in a valley about twenty men of arms. And when they saw Sir Perceval, they asked him whence he was; and he answered: “Of the court of King Arthur.” Then they cried all at once, “Slay him.” But Sir Perceval smote the first to the earth, and his horse upon him. Then seven of the knights smote upon his shield all at once, and the remnant slew his horse, so that he fell to the earth. So had they slain him or taken him, had not the good knight Sir Galahad, with the red cross, come there by adventure. And when he saw all the knights upon one, he cried out, “Save me that knight’s life.” Then he rode toward the twenty men of arms as fast as his horse might drive, with his spear in the rest, and smote the foremost horse and man to the earth. And when his spear was broken, he set his hand to his sword, and smote on the right hand and on the left, that it was marvel to see; and at every stroke he smote down one, or put him to rebuke, so that they would fight no more, but fled to a thick forest, and Sir Galahad followed them. And when Sir Perceval saw him chase them so, he made great sorrow that his horse was slain. And he wist well it was Sir Galahad. Then he cried aloud, “Ah, fair knight, abide, and suffer me to do thanks unto thee; for right well have ye done for me.” But Sir Galahad rode so fast that at last he passed out of his sight. When Sir Perceval saw that he would not turn, he said, “Now am I a very wretch, and most unhappy above all other knights.” So in his sorrow he abode all that day till it was night; and then he was faint, and laid him down and slept till midnight; and then he awaked and saw before him a woman, who said unto him, “Sir Perceval, what dost thou here?” He answered, “I do neither good, nor great ill.” “If thou wilt promise me,” said she, “that thou wilt fulfil my will when I summon thee, I will lend thee my own horse, which shall bear thee whither thou wilt.” Sir Perceval was glad of her proffer, and insured her to fulfil all her desire. “Then abide me here, and I will go fetch you a horse.” And so she soon came again, and brought a horse with her that was inky black. When Perceval beheld that horse he marvelled, it was so great and so well apparelled. And he leapt upon him and took no heed of himself. And he thrust him with his spurs, and within an hour and less he bare him four days’ journey thence, until he came to a rough water, which roared, and his horse would have borne him into it. And when Sir Perceval came nigh the brim and saw the water so boisterous he doubted to overpass it. And then he made the sign of the cross on his forehead. When the fiend felt him so charged, he shook off Sir Perceval, and went into the water crying and roaring; and it seemed unto him that the water burned. Then Sir Perceval perceived it was a fiend that would have brought him unto his perdition. Then he commended himself unto God, and prayed our Lord to keep him from all such temptations; and so he prayed all that night till it was day. Then he saw that he was in a wild place, that was closed with the sea nigh all about. And Sir Perceval looked forth over the sea, and saw a ship come sailing towards him; and it came and stood still under the rock. And when Sir Perceval saw this, he hied him thither, and found the ship covered with silk; and therein was a lady of great beauty, and clothed so richly that none might be better.\n\nAnd when she saw Sir Perceval, she saluted him, and Sir Perceval returned her salutation. Then he asked her of her country and her lineage. And she said, “I am a gentlewoman that am disinherited, and was once the richest woman of the world.” “Damsel,” said Sir Perceval, “who hath disinherited you? for I have great pity of you.” “Sir,” said she, “my enemy is a great and powerful lord, and aforetime he made much of me, so that of his favor and of my beauty I had a little pride more than I ought to have had. Also I said a word that pleased him not. So he drove me from his company and from mine heritage. Therefore I know no good knight nor good man, but I get him on my side if I may. And for that I know that thou art a good knight, I beseech thee to help me.”\n\nThen Sir Perceval promised her all the help that he might, and she thanked him.\n\nAnd at that time the weather was hot, and she called to her a gentlewoman, and bade her bring forth a pavilion. And she did so, and pitched it upon the gravel. “Sir,” said she, “now may ye rest you in this heat of the day.” Then he thanked her, and she put off his helm and his shield, and there he slept a great while. Then he awoke, and asked her if she had any meat, and she said yea, and so there was set upon the table all manner of meats that he could think on. Also he drank there the strongest wine that ever he drank, and therewith he was a little chafed more than he ought to be. With that he beheld the lady, and he thought she was the fairest creature that ever he saw. And then Sir Perceval proffered her love, and prayed her that she would be his. Then she refused him in a manner, for the cause he should be the more ardent on her, and ever he ceased not to pray her of love. And when she saw him well enchafed, then she said, “Sir Perceval, wit you well I shall not give ye my love, unless you swear from henceforth you will be my true servant, and do no thing but that I shall command you. Will you insure me this, as ye be a true knight?” “Yea,” said he, “fair lady, by the faith of my body.” And as he said this, by adventure and grace, he saw his sword lie on the ground naked, in whose pommel was a red cross, and the sign of the crucifix thereon. Then he made the sign of the cross on his forehead, and therewith the pavilion shrivelled up, and changed into a smoke and a black cloud. And the damsel cried aloud, and hasted into the ship, and so she went with the wind roaring and yelling that it seemed all the water burned after her. Then Sir Perceval made great sorrow, and called himself a wretch, saying, “How nigh was I lost!” Then he took his arms, and departed thence.\n\n————\n\n# SIR BOHORT\n\nTHE SANGREAL (Continued)\nWHEN Sir Bohort departed from Camelot he met with a religious man, riding upon an ass; and Sir Bohort saluted him. “What are ye?” said the good man. “Sir,” said Sir Bohort, “I am a knight that fain would be counselled in the quest of the Sangreal.” So rode they both together till they came to a hermitage; and there he prayed Sir Bohort to dwell that night with him. So he alighted, and put away his armor, and prayed him that he might be confessed. And they went both into the chapel, and there he was clean confessed. And they ate bread and drank water together. “Now,” said the good man, “I pray thee that thou eat none other till thou sit at the table where the Sangreal shall be.” “Sir,” said Sir Bohort, “but how know ye that I shall sit there?” “Yea,” said the good man, “that I know well; but there shall be few of your fellows with you.” Then said Sir Bohort, “I agree me thereto.” And the good man when he had heard his confession found him in so pure a life and so stable that he marvelled thereof.\n\nOn the morrow, as soon as the day appeared, Sir Bohort departed thence, and rode into a forest unto the hour of midday. And there befell him a marvellous adventure. For he met, at the parting of two ways, two knights that led Sir Lionel, his brother, all naked, bound upon a strong hackney, and his hands bound before his breast; and each of them held in his hand thorns wherewith they went beating him, so that he was all bloody before and behind; but he said never a word, but, as he was great of heart, he suffered all that they did to him as though he had felt none anguish. Sir Bohort prepared to rescue his brother. But he looked on the other side of him, and saw a knight dragging along a fair gentlewoman, who cried out, “Saint Mary! succor your maid!” And when she saw Sir Bohort, she called to him, and said, “By the faith that ye owe to knighthood, help me!” When Sir Bohort heard her say thus he had such sorrow that he wist not what to do. “For if I let my brother be he must be slain, and that would I not for all the earth; and if I help not the maid I am shamed for ever.” Then lift he up his eyes and said, weeping, “Fair Lord, whose liegeman I am, keep Sir Lionel, my brother, that none of these knights slay him, and for pity of you, and our Lady’s sake, I shall succor this maid.”\n\nThen he cried out to the knight, “Sir knight, lay your hand off that maid, or else ye be but dead.” Then the knight set down the maid, and took his shield, and drew out his sword. And Sir Bohort smote him so hard that it went through his shield and habergeon, on the left shoulder, and he fell down to the earth. Then came Sir Bohort to the maid, “Ye be delivered of this knight this time.” “Now,” said she, “I pray you lead me there where this knight took me.” “I shall gladly do it,” said Sir Bohort. So he took the horse of the wounded knight, and set the gentlewoman upon it, and brought her there where she desired to be. And there he found twelve knights seeking after her; and when she told them how Sir Bohort had delivered her, they made great joy, and besought him to come to her father, a great lord, and he should be right welcomed. “Truly,” said Sir Bohort, “that may not be; for I have a great adventure to do.” So he commended them to God and departed.\n\nThen Sir Bohort rode after Sir Lionel, his brother, by the trace of their horses. Thus he rode seeking, a great while. Then he overtook a man clothed in a religious clothing, who said, “Sir Knight, what seek ye?” “Sir,” said Sir Bohort, “I seek my brother, that I saw within a little space beaten of two knights.” “Ah, Sir Bohort, trouble not thyself to seek for him, for truly he is dead.” Then he showed him a new-slain body, lying in a thick bush; and it seemed him that it was the body of Sir Lionel. And then he made such sorrow that he fell to the ground in a swoon, and lay there long. And when he came to himself again, he said, “Fair brother, since the fellowship of you and me is sundered, shall I never have joy again; and now He that I have taken for my Master, He be my help!” And when he had said thus he took up the body in his arms, and put it upon the horse. And then he said to the man, “Canst thou tell me the way to some chapel, where I may bury this body?” “Come on,” said the man, “here is one fast by.” And so they rode till they saw a fair tower, and beside it a chapel. Then they alighted both, and put the body into a tomb of marble.\n\nThen Sir Bohort commended the good man unto God, and departed. And he rode all that day, and harbored with an old lady. And on the morrow he rode unto the castle in a valley, and there he met with a yeoman. “Tell me,” said Sir Bohort, “knowest thou of any adventure?” “Sir,” said he, “here shall be, under this castle, a great and marvellous tournament.” Then Sir Bohort thought to be there, if he might meet with any of the fellowship that were in quest of the Sangreal; so he turned to a hermitage that was on the border of the forest. And when he was come hither, he found there Sir Lionel his brother, who sat all armed at the entry of the chapel door. And when Sir Bohort saw him, he had great joy, and he alighted off his horse, and said, “Fair brother, when came ye hither?” As soon as Sir Lionel saw him he said, “Ah, Sir Bohort, make ye no false show, for, as for you, I might have been slain, for ye left me in peril of death to go succor a gentlewoman; and for that misdeed I now assure you but death, for ye have right well deserved it.” When Sir Bohort perceived his brother’s wrath he kneeled down to the earth and cried him mercy, holding up both his hands, and prayed him to forgive him. “Nay,” said Sir Lionel, “thou shalt have but death for it, if I have the upper hand; therefore leap upon thy horse and keep thyself, and if thou do not I will run upon thee there as thou standest on foot, and so the shame shall be mine, and the harm thine, but of that I reck not.” When Sir Bohort saw that he must fight with his brother or else die, he wist not what to do. Then his heart counselled him not so to do, inasmuch as Sir Lionel was his elder brother, wherefore he ought to bear him reverence. Yet kneeled he down before Sir Lionel’s horse’s feet, and said, “Fair brother, have mercy upon me and slay me not.” But Sir Lionel cared not, for the fiend had brought him in such a will that he should slay him. When he saw that Sir Bohort would not rise to give him battle, he rushed over him, so that he smote him with his horse’s feet to the earth, and hurt him sore, that he swooned of distress. When Sir Lionel saw this he alighted from his horse for to have smitten off his head; and so he took him by the helm, and would have rent it from his head. But it happened that Sir Colgrevance, a knight of the Round Table, came at that time thither, as it was our Lord’s will; and then he beheld how Sir Lionel would have slain his brother, and he knew Sir Bohort, whom he loved right well.\n\nThen leapt he down from his horse and took Sir Lionel by the shoulders, and drew him strongly back from Sir Bohort, and said, “Sir Lionel, will ye slay your brother?” “Why,” said Sir Lionel, “will ye stay me? If ye interfere in this I will slay you, and him after.” Then he ran upon Sir Bohort, and would have smitten him; but Sir Colgrevance ran between them, and said, “If ye persist to do so any more, we two shall meddle together.” Then Sir Lionel defied him, and gave him a great stroke through the helm. Then he drew his sword, for he was a passing good knight, and defended himself right manfully. So long endured the battle, that Sir Bohort rose up all anguishly, and beheld Sir Colgrevance, the good knight, fight with his brother for his quarrel. Then was he full sorry and heavy, and thought that if Sir Colgrevance slew him that was his brother he should never have joy, and if his brother slew Sir Colgrevance the shame should ever be his.\n\nThen would he have risen for to have parted them, but he had not so much strength to stand on his feet; so he staid so long that Sir Colgrevance had the worse; for Sir Lionel was of great chivalry and right hardy. Then cried Sir Colgrevance, “Ah, Sir Bohort, why come ye not to bring me out of peril of death, wherein I have put me to succor you?” With that, Sir Lionel smote off his helm and bore him to the earth. And when he had slain Sir Colgrevance he ran upon his brother as a fiendly man, and gave him such a stroke that he made him stoop. And he that was full of humility prayed him, “for God’s sake leave this battle, for if it befell, fair brother, that I slew you, or ye me, we should be dead of that sin.” “Pray ye not me for mercy,” said Sir Lionel. Then Sir Bohort, all weeping, drew his sword, and said, “Now God have mercy upon me, though I defend my life against my brother.” With that Sir Bohort lifted up his sword, and would have smitten his brother. Then he heard a voice that said, “Flee, Sir Bohort, and touch him not.” Right so alighted a cloud between them, in the likeness of a fire and a marvellous flame, so that they both fell to the earth, and lay there a great while in a swoon. And when they came to themselves, Sir Bohort saw that his brother had no harm; and he was right glad, for he dread sore that God had taken vengeance upon him. Then Sir Lionel said to his brother, “Brother, forgive me, for God’s sake, all that I have trespassed against you.” And Sir Bohort answered, “God forgive it thee, and I do.”\n\nWith that Sir Bohort heard a voice say, “Sir Bohort, take thy way anon, right to the sea, for Sir Perceval abideth thee there.” So Sir Bohort departed, and rode the nearest way to the sea. And at last he came to an abbey that was nigh the sea. That night he rested him there, and in his sleep there came a voice unto him and bade him go to the sea-shore. He started up, and made a sign of the cross on his forehead, and armed himself, and made ready his horse and mounted him, and at a broken wall he rode out, and came to the sea-shore. And there he found a ship, covered all with white samite. And he entered into the ship; but it was anon so dark that he might see no man, and he laid him down and slept till it was day. Then he awaked, and saw in the middle of the ship a knight all armed, save his helm. And then he knew it was Sir Perceval de Galis, and each made of other right great joy. Then said Sir Perceval, “We lack nothing now but the good knight Sir Galahad.”\n\nSIR LAUNCELOT (Resumed)\nIt befell upon a night Sir Launcelot arrived before a castle, which was rich and fair. And there was a postern that was opened toward the sea, and was open without any keeping, save two lions kept the entry; and the moon shined clear. Anon Sir Launcelot heard a voice that said, “Launcelot, enter into the castle, where thou shalt see a great part of thy desire.” So he went unto the gate, and saw the two lions; then he set hands to his sword, and drew it. Then there came suddenly as it were a stroke upon the arm, so sore that the sword fell out of his hand, and he heard a voice that said, “O man of evil faith, wherefore believest thou more in thy armor than in thy Maker?” Then said Sir Launcelot, “Fair Lord, I thank thee of thy great mercy, that thou reprovest me of my misdeed; now see I well that thou holdest me for thy servant.” Then he made a cross on his forehead, and came to the lions; and they made semblance to do him harm, but he passed them without hurt, and entered into the castle, and he found no gate nor door but it was open. But at the last he found a chamber whereof the door was shut; and he set his hand thereto, to have opened it, but he might not. Then he listened, and heard a voice which sung so sweetly that it seemed none earthly thing; and the voice said, “Joy and honor be to the Father of heaven.” Then Sir Launcelot kneeled down before the chamber, for well he wist that there was the Sangreal in that chamber. Then said he, “Fair, sweet Lord, if ever I did anything that pleased thee, for thy pity show me something of that which I seek.” And with that he saw the chamber door open, and there came out a great clearness, that the house was as bright as though all the torches of the world had been there. So he came to the chamber door, and would have entered; and anon a voice said unto him, “Stay, Sir Launcelot, and enter not.” And he withdrew him back, and was right heavy in his mind. Then looked he in the midst of the chamber, and saw a table of silver, and the holy vessel, covered with red samite, and many angels about it; whereof one held a candle of wax burning, and another held a cross, and the ornaments of the altar.\n\n“O, yet methought I saw the Holy Grail, All pall’d in crimson samite, and around Great angels, awful shapes, and wings and eyes.” —The Holy Grail.\n\nThen for very wonder and thankfulness Sir Launcelot forgot himself and he stepped forward and entered the chamber. And suddenly a breath that seemed intermixed with fire smote him so sore in the visage that therewith he fell to the ground, and had no power to rise. Then felt he many hands about him, which took him up and bare him out of the chamber, without any amending of his swoon, and left him there, seeming dead to all the people. So on the morrow, when it was fair daylight, and they within were arisen, they found Sir Launcelot lying before the chamber door. And they looked upon him and felt his pulse, to know if there were any life in him. And they found life in him, but he might neither stand nor stir any member that he had. So they took him and bare him into a chamber, and laid him upon a bed, far from all folk, and there he lay many days. Then the one said he was alive, and the others said nay. But said an old man, “He is as full of life as the mightiest of you all, and therefore I counsel you that he be well kept till God bring him back again.” And after twenty-four days he opened his eyes; and when he saw folk he made great sorrow, and said, “Why have ye wakened me? for I was better at ease than I am now.” “What have ye seen?” said they about him. “I have seen,” said he, “great marvels that no tongue can tell, and more than any heart can think.” Then they said, “Sir, the quest of the Sangreal is achieved right now in you, and never shall ye see more of it than ye have seen.” “I thank God,” said Sir Launcelot, “of his great mercy, for that I have seen, for it sufficeth me.” Then he rose up and clothed himself; and when he was so arrayed they marvelled all, for they knew it was Sir Launcelot the good knight. And after four days he took his leave of the lord of the castle, and of all the fellowship that were there, and thanked them for their great labor and care of him. Then he departed, and turned to Camelot, where he found King Arthur and Queen Guenever; but many of the knights of the Round Table were slain and destroyed, more than half. Then all the court was passing glad of Sir Launcelot; and he told the king all his adventures that had befallen him since he departed.\n\n# SIR GALAHAD\n\nNow, when Sir Galahad had rescued Perceval from the twenty knights, he rode into a vast forest, wherein he abode many days. Then he took his way to the sea, and it befell him that he was benighted in a hermitage. And the good man was glad when he saw he was a knight-errant. And when they were at rest, there came a gentlewoman knocking at the door; and the good man came to the door to wit what she would. Then she said, “I would speak with the knight which is with you.” Then Galahad went to her, and asked her what she would. “Sir Galahad,” said she, “I will that ye arm you, and mount upon your horse, and follow me; for I will show you the highest adventure that ever knight saw.” Then Galahad armed himself and commended himself to God, and bade the damsel go before, and he would follow where she led.\n\nSo she rode as fast as her palfrey might bear her, till she came to the sea; and there they found the ship where Sir Bohort and Sir Perceval were, who cried from the ship, “Sir Galahad, you are welcome; we have waited you long.” And when he heard them, he asked the damsel who they were. “Sir,” said she, “leave your horse here, and I shall leave mine, and we will join ourselves to their company.” So they entered into the ship, and the two knights received them both with great joy. For they knew the damsel, that she was Sir Perceval’s sister. Then the wind arose and drove them through the sea all that day and the next, till the ship arrived between two rocks, passing great and marvellous; but there they might not land, for there was a whirlpool; but there was another ship, and upon it they might go without danger. “Go we thither,” said the gentlewoman, “and there we shall see adventures, for such is our Lord’s will.” Then Sir Galahad blessed him, and entered therein, and then next the gentlewoman, and then Sir Bohort and Sir Perceval. And when they came on board they found there the table of silver, and the Sangreal, which was covered with red samite. And they made great reverence thereto, and Sir Galahad prayed a long time to our Lord, that at what time he should ask to pass out of this world he should do so; and a voice said to him, “Galahad, thou shalt have thy request; and when thou askest the death of they body, thou shalt have it, and then shalt thou find the life of thy soul.”\n\nAnd anon the wind drove them across the sea, till they came to the city of Sarras. Then took they out of the ship the table of silver, and Sir Perceval and Sir Bohort took it before, and Sir Galahad came behind, and right so they went to the city. And at the gate of the city they saw an old man, a cripple.\n\n“And Sir Launfal said, ‘I behold in thee An image of Him who died on the tree. Thou also hast had thy crown of thorns, Thou also hast had the world’s buffets and scorns; And to thy life were not denied The wounds in thy hands and feet and side. Mild Mary’s son, acknowledge me; Behold, through Him I give to thee!’” —Lowell’s Holy Grail.\n\nThen Galahad called him, and bade him help to bear this heavy thing. “Truly,” said the old man, “it is ten years since I could not go but with crutches.” “Care thou not,” said Sir Galahad, “but arise up, and show thy good will.” Then the old man rose up, and assayed, and found himself as whole as ever he was; and he ran to the table, and took one part with Sir Galahad.\n\nWhen they came to the city it chanced that the king was just dead, and all the city was dismayed, and wist not who might be their king. Right so, as they were in counsel, there came a voice among them, and bade them choose the youngest knight of those three to be their king. So they made Sir Galahad king, by all the assent of the city. And when he was made king, he commanded to make a chest of gold and of precious stones to hold the holy vessel. And every day the three companions would come before it and make their prayers.\n\nNow at the year’s end, and the same day of the year that Sir Galahad received the crown, he got up early, and, with his fellows, came to where the holy vessel was; and they saw one kneeling before it that had about him a great fellowship of angels; and he called Sir Galahad, and said, “Come, thou servant of the Lord, and thou shalt see what thou hast much desired to see.” And Sir Galahad’s mortal flesh trembled right hard when he began to behold the spiritual things. Then said the good man, “Now wottest thou who I am?” “Nay,” said Sir Galahad. “I am Joseph of Arimathea, whom our Lord hath sent here to thee, to bear thee fellowship.” Then Sir Galahad held up his hands toward heaven, and said, “Now, blessed Lord, would I not longer live, if it might please thee.” And when he had said these words, Sir Galahad went to Sir Perceval and to Sir Bohort and kissed them, and commended them to God. And then he kneeled down before the table, and made his prayers, and suddenly his soul departed, and a great multitude of angels bare his soul up to heaven, so as the two fellows could well behold it. Also they saw come from heaven a hand, but they saw not the body; and the hand came right to the vessel and bare it up to heaven. Since then was there never one so hardy as to say that he had seen the Sangreal on earth any more.\n\n————\n\n# SIR AGRIVAIN’S TREASON\n\nWHEN Sir Perceval and Sir Bohort saw Sir Galahad dead they made as much sorrow as ever did two men. And if they had not been good men they might have fallen into despair. As soon as Sir Galahad was buried Sir Perceval retired to a hermitage out of the city, and took a religious clothing; and Sir Bohort was always with him, but did not change his secular clothing, because he purposed to return to the realm of Loegria. Thus a year and two months lived Sir Perceval in the hermitage a full holy life, and then passed out of this world, and Sir Bohort buried him by his sister and Sir Galahad. Then Sir Bohort armed himself and departed from Sarras, and entered into a ship, and sailed to the kingdom of Loegria, and in due time arrived safe at Camelot, where the king was. Then was there great joy made of him in the whole court, for they feared he had been dead. Then the king made great clerks to come before him, that they should chronicle of the high adventures of the good knights. And Sir Bohort told him of the adventures that had befallen him, and his two fellows, Sir Perceval and Sir Galahad. And Sir Launcelot told the adventures of the Sangreal that he had seen. All this was made in great books, and put up in the church at Salisbury.\n\nSo King Arthur and Queen Guenever made great joy of the remnant that were come home, and chiefly of Sir Launcelot and Sir Bohort. Then Sir Launcelot began to resort unto Queen Guenever again, and forgot the promise that he made in the quest; so that many in the court spoke of it, and in especial Sir Agrivain, Sir Gawain’s brother, for he was ever open-mouthed. So it happened Sir Gawain and all his brothers were in King Arthur’s chamber, and then Sir Agrivain said thus openly, “I marvel that we all are not ashamed to see and to know so noble a knight as King Arthur so to be shamed by the conduct of Sir Launcelot and the queen.” Then spoke Sir Gawain, and said, “Brother, Sir Agrivain, I pray you and charge you move not such matters any more before me, for be ye assured I will not be of your counsel.” “Neither will we,” said Sir Gaheris and Sir Gareth. “Then will I,” said Sir Modred. “I doubt you not,” said Sir Gawain, “for to all mischief ever were ye prone; yet I would that ye left all this, for I know what will come of it.”\n\n“Modred’s narrow foxy face, Heart-hiding smile, and gray persistent eye: Henceforward, too, the Powers that tend the soul To help it from the death that cannot die, And save it even in extremes, began To vex and plague.” —Guinevere.\n\n“Fall of it what fall may,” said Sir Agrivain, “I will disclose it to the king.” With that came to them King Arthur. “Now, brothers, hold your peace,” said Sir Gawain. “We will not,” said Sir Agrivain. Then said Sir Gawain, “I will not hear your tales nor be of your counsel.” “No more will I,” said Sir Gareth and Sir Gaheris, and therewith they departed, making great sorrow.\n\nThen Sir Agrivain told the king all that was said in the court of the conduct of Sir Launcelot and the queen, and it grieved the king very much. But he would not believe it to be true without proof. So Sir Agrivain laid a plot to entrap Sir Launcelot and the queen, intending to take them together unawares. Sir Agrivain and Sir Modred led a party for this purpose, but Sir Launcelot escaped from them, having slain Sir Agrivain and wounded Sir Modred. Then Sir Launcelot hastened to his friends, and told them what had happened, and withdrew with them to the forest; but he left spies to bring him tidings of whatever might be done.\n\nSo Sir Launcelot escaped, but the queen remained in the king’s power, and Arthur could no longer doubt of her guilt. And the law was such in those days that they who committed such crimes, of what estate or condition soever they were, must be burned to death, and so it was ordained for Queen Guenever. Then said King Arthur to Sir Gawain, “I pray you make you ready, in your best armor, with your brethren, Sir Gaheris and Sir Gareth, to bring my queen to the fire, there to receive her death.” “Nay, my most noble lord,” said Sir Gawain, “that will I never do; for know thou well, my heart will never serve me to see her die, and it shall never be said that I was of your counsel in her death.” Then the king commanded Sir Gaheris and Sir Gareth to be there, and they said, “We will be there, as ye command us, sire, but in peaceable wise, and bear no armor upon us.”\n\nSo the queen was led forth, and her ghostly father was brought to her to shrive her, and there was weeping and wailing of many lords and ladies. And one went and told Sir Launcelot that the queen was led forth to her death. Then Sir Launcelot and the knights that were with him fell upon the troop that guarded the queen, and dispersed them, and slew all who withstood them. And in the confusion Sir Gareth and Sir Gaheris were slain, for they were unarmed and defenceless. And Sir Launcelot carried away the queen to his castle of La Joyeuse Garde.\n\nThen there came one to Sir Gawain and told him how that Sir Launcelot had slain the knights and carried away the queen. “O Lord, defend my brethren!” said Sir Gawain. “Truly,” said the man, “Sir Gareth and Sir Gaheris are slain.” “Alas!” said Sir Gawain, “now is my joy gone.” And then he fell down and swooned, and long he lay there as he had been dead.\n\nWhen he arose out of his swoon Sir Gawain ran to the king, crying, “O King Arthur, mine uncle, my brothers are slain.” Then the king wept and he both. “My king, my lord, and mine uncle,” said Sir Gawain, “bear witness now that I make you a promise that I shall hold by my knighthood, and from this day I will never fail Sir Launcelot until the one of us have slain the other. I will seek Sir Launcelot throughout seven kings’ realms, but I shall slay him or he shall slay me.” “Ye shall not need to seek him,” said the king, “for as I hear, Sir Launcelot will abide me and you in the Joyeuse Garde; and much people draweth unto him, as I hear say.” “That may I believe,” said Sir Gawain; “but, my lord, summon your friends, and I will summon mine.” “It shall be done,” said the king. So then the king sent letters and writs throughout all England, both in the length and breadth, to summon all his knights. And unto Arthur drew many knights, dukes, and earls, so that he had a great host. Thereof heard Sir Launcelot, and collected all whom he could; and many good knights held with him, both for his sake and for the queen’s sake. But King Arthur’s host was too great for Sir Launcelot to abide him in the field; and he was full loath to do battle against the king. So Sir Launcelot drew him to his strong castle, with all manner of provisions. Then came King Arthur with Sir Gawain, and laid siege all about La Joyeuse Garde, both the town and the castle; but in no wise would Sir Launcelot ride out of his castle, neither suffer any of his knights to issue out, until many weeks were past.\n\nThen it befell upon a day in harvest-time, Sir Launcelot looked over the wall, and spoke aloud to King Arthur and Sir Gawain, “My lords both, all is in vain that ye do at this siege, for here ye shall win no worship, but only dishonor; for if I list to come out, and my good knights, I shall soon make an end of this war.” “Come forth,” said Arthur, “if thou darest, and I promise thee I shall meet thee in the midst of the field.” “God forbid me,” said Sir Launcelot, “that I should encounter with the most noble king that made me knight.” “Fie upon thy fair language,” said the king, “for know thou well I am thy mortal foe, and ever will be to my dying day.” And Sir Gawain said, “What cause hadst thou to slay my brother, Sir Gaheris, who bore no arms against thee, and Sir Gareth, whom thou madest knight, and who loved thee more than all my kin? Therefore know thou well I shall make war to thee all the while that I may live.”\n\nWhen Sir Bohort, and Sir Hector de Marys, and Sir Lionel heard this outcry, they called to them Sir Palamedes, and Sir Saffire his brother, and Sir Lawayn, with many more, and all went to Sir Launcelot. And they said, “My lord, Sir Launcelot, we pray you, if you will have our service keep us no longer within these walls, for know well all your fair speech and forbearance will not avail you.” “Alas!” said Sir Launcelot, “to ride forth and to do battle I am full loath.” Then he spake again unto the king and Sir Gawain, and willed them to keep out of the battle; but they despised his words. So then Sir Launcelot’s fellowship came out of the castle in full good array. And always Sir Launcelot charged all his knights, in any wise, to save King Arthur and Sir Gawain.\n\nThen came forth Sir Gawain from the king’s host and offered combat, and Sir Lionel encountered with him, and there Sir Gawain smote Sir Lionel through the body, that he fell to the earth as if dead. Then there began a great conflict, and much people were slain; but ever Sir Launcelot did what he might to save the people on King Arthur’s party, and ever King Arthur followed Sir Launcelot to slay him; but Sir Launcelot suffered him, and would not strike again. Then Sir Bohort encountered with King Arthur, and smote him down; and he alighted and drew his sword, and said to Sir Launcelot, “Shall I make an end of this war?” for he meant to have slain King Arthur. “Not so,” said Sir Launcelot, “touch him no more, for I will never see that most noble king that made me knight either slain or shamed;” and therewith Sir Launcelot alighted off his horse, and took up the king, and horsed him again, and said thus: “My lord Arthur, for God’s love, cease this strife.” And King Arthur looked upon Sir Launcelot, and the tears burst from his eyes, thinking on the great courtesy that was in Sir Launcelot more than in any other man; and therewith the king rode his way. Then anon both parties withdrew to repose them, and buried the dead.\n\nBut the war continued, and it was noised abroad through all Christendom, and at last it was told afore the pope; and he, considering the great goodness of King Arthur, and of Sir Launcelot, called unto him a noble clerk, which was the Bishop of Rochester, who was then in his dominions, and sent him to King Arthur, charging him that he take his queen, dame Guenever, unto him again, and make peace with Sir Launcelot.\n\nSo, by means of this bishop, peace was made for the space of one year; and King Arthur received back the queen, and Sir Launcelot departed from the kingdom with all his knights, and went to his own country. So they shipped at Cardiff, and sailed unto Benwick, which some men call Bayonne. And all the people of those lands came to Sir Launcelot, and received him home right joyfully. And Sir Launcelot stablished and garnished all his towns and castles, and he greatly advanced all his noble knights, Sir Lionel and Sir Bohort, and Sir Hector de Marys, Sir Blamor, Sir Lawayne, and many others, and made them lords of lands and castles; till he left himself no more than any one of them.\n\n“Then Arthur made vast banquets, and strange knights From the four winds came in: and each one sat, Tho’ served with choice from air, land, stream and sea, Oft in mid-banquet measuring with his eyes His neighbor’s make and might.” —Pelleas and Ettarre.\n\nBut when the year was passed, King Arthur and Sir Gawain came with a great host, and landed upon Sir Launcelot’s lands, and burned and wasted all that they might overrun. Then spake Sir Bohort and said, “My lord, Sir Launcelot, give us leave to meet them in the field, and we shall make them rue the time that ever they came to this country.” Then said Sir Launcelot, “I am full loath to ride out with my knights for shedding of Christian blood; so we will yet a while keep our walls, and I will send a messenger unto my lord Arthur, to propose a treaty; for better is peace than always war.” So Sir Launcelot sent forth a damsel, and a dwarf with her, requiring King Arthur to leave his warring upon his lands; and so she started on a palfrey, and the dwarf ran by her side. And when she came to the pavilion of King Arthur, she alighted, and there met her a gentle knight, Sir Lucan, the butler, and said, “Fair damsel, come ye from Sir Launcelot du Lac?” “Yea, sir,” she said, “I come hither to speak with the king.” “Alas!” said Sir Lucan, “my lord Arthur would be reconciled to Sir Launcelot, but Sir Gawain will not suffer him.” And with this Sir Lucan led the damsel to the king, where he sat with Sir Gawain, to hear what she would say. So when she had told her tale, the tears ran out of the king’s eyes; and all the lords were forward to advise the king to be accorded with Sir Launcelot, save only Sir Gawain; and he said, “My lord, mine uncle, what will ye do? Will you now turn back, now you are so far advanced upon your journey? If ye do all the world will speak shame of you.” “Nay,” said King Arthur, “I will do as ye advise me; but do thou give the damsel her answer, for I may not speak to her for pity.”\n\nThen said Sir Gawain, “Damsel, say ye to Sir Launcelot, that it is waste labor to sue to mine uncle for peace, and say that I, Sir Gawain, send him word that I promise him, by the faith I owe unto God and to knighthood, I shall never leave him till he have slain me or I him.” So the damsel returned; and when Sir Launcelot had heard this answer the tears ran down his cheeks.\n\nThen it befell on a day Sir Gawain came before the gates, armed at all points, and cried with a loud voice, “Where art thou now, thou false traitor, Sir Launcelot? Why hidest thou thyself within holes and walls like a coward? Look out now, thou traitor knight, and I will avenge upon thy body the death of my three brethren.” All this language heard Sir Launcelot, and the knights which were about him; and they said to him, “Sir Launcelot, now must ye defend you like a knight, or else be shamed for ever, for you have slept overlong and suffered overmuch.” Then Sir Launcelot spake on high unto King Arthur, and said, “My lord Arthur, now I have forborne long, and suffered you and Sir Gawain to do what ye would, and now must I needs defend myself, inasmuch as Sir Gawain hath appealed me of treason.” Then Sir Launcelot armed him and mounted upon his horse, and the noble knights came out of the city, and the host without stood all apart; and so the covenant was made that no man should come near the two knights, nor deal with them, till one were dead or yielded.\n\nThen Sir Launcelot and Sir Gawain departed a great way asunder, and then they came together with all their horses’ might, and each smote the other in the middle of their shields, but neither of them was unhorsed, but their horses fell to the earth. And then they leapt from their horses, and drew their swords, and gave many sad strokes, so that the blood burst out in many places. Now Sir Gawain had this gift from a holy man, that every day in the year, from morning to noon, his strength was increased threefold, and then it fell again to its natural measure. Sir Launcelot was aware of this, and therefore, during the three hours that Sir Gawain’s strength was at the height, Sir Launcelot covered himself with his shield, and kept his might in reserve. And during that time Sir Gawain gave him many sad brunts, that all the knights that looked on marvelled how Sir Launcelot might endure them. Then, when it was past noon, Sir Gawain had only his own might; and when Sir Launcelot felt him so brought down he stretched himself up, and doubled his strokes, and gave Sir Gawain such a buffet that he fell down on his side; and Sir Launcelot drew back and would strike no more. “Why withdrawest thou, false traitor?” then said Sir Gawain; “now turn again and slay me, for if thou leave me thus when I am whole again, I shall do battle with thee again.” “I shall endure you, sir, by God’s grace,” said Sir Launcelot, “but know thou well Sir Gawain, I will never smite a felled knight.” And so Sir Launcelot went into the city, and Sir Gawain was borne into King Arthur’s pavilion, and his wounds were looked to.\n\nThus the siege endured, and Sir Gawain lay helpless near a month; and when he was near recovered came tidings unto King Arthur that made him return with all his host to England.\n\n————\n\n# MORTE D’ARTHUR\n\nSIR MODRED was left ruler of all England, and he caused letters to be written, as if from beyond sea, that King Arthur was slain in battle. So he called a Parliament, and made himself be crowned king; and he took the queen Guenever, and said plainly that he would wed her, but she escaped from him and took refuge in the Tower of London. And Sir Modred went and laid siege about the Tower of London, and made great assaults thereat, but all might not avail him. Then came word to Sir Modred that King Arthur had raised the siege of Sir Launcelot, and was coming home. Then Sir Modred summoned all the barony of the land; and much people drew unto Sir Modred, and said they would abide with him for better and for worse; and he drew a great host to Dover, for there he heard say that King Arthur would arrive.\n\n“I hear the steps of Modred in the west, And with him many of thy people, and knights Once thine, whom thou hast loved, but grosser grown Than heathen, spitting at their vows and thee.” —The Passing of Arthur.\n\nAnd as Sir Modred was at Dover with his host, came King Arthur, with a great number of ships and galleys, and there was Sir Modred awaiting upon the landing. Then was there launching of great boats and small, full of noble men of arms, and there was much slaughter of gentle knights on both parts. But King Arthur was so courageous, there might no manner of knights prevent him to land, and his knights fiercely followed him; and so they landed, and put Sir Modred aback so that he fled, and all his people. And when the battle was done, King Arthur commanded to bury his people that were dead. And then was noble Sir Gawain found, in a great boat, lying more than half dead. And King Arthur went to him, and made sorrow out of measure. “Mine uncle,” said Sir Gawain, “know thou well my death-day is come, and all is through mine own hastiness and wilfulness, for I am smitten upon the old wound which Sir Launcelot gave me, of which I feel I must die. And had Sir Launcelot been with you as of old, this war had never begun, and of all this I am the cause.” Then Sir Gawain prayed the king to send for Sir Launcelot, and to cherish him above all other knights. And so at the hour of noon Sir Gawain yielded up his spirit, and then the king bade inter him in a chapel within Dover Castle; and there all men may see the skull of him, and the same wound is seen that Sir Launcelot gave him in battle.\n\nThen was it told the king that Sir Modred had pitched his camp upon Barrendown; and the king rode thither, and there was a great battle betwixt them, and King Arthur’s party stood best, and Sir Modred and his party fled unto Canterbury.\n\nAnd there was a day assigned betwixt King Arthur and Sir Modred that they should meet upon a down beside Salisbury, and not far from the sea-side, to do battle yet again. And at night, as the king slept, he dreamed a wonderful dream. It seemed him verily that there came Sir Gawain unto him, with a number of fair ladies with him. And when King Arthur saw him, he said, “Welcome, my sister’s son; I weened thou hadst been dead; and now I see thee alive great is my joy. But, O fair nephew, what be these ladies that hither be come with you?” “Sir,” said Sir Gawain, “all these be ladies for whom I have fought when I was a living man; and because I did battle for them in righteous quarrel they have given me grace to bring me hither unto you to warn you of your death, if ye fight to-morrow with Sir Modred. Therefore take ye treaty, and proffer you largely for a month’s delay; for within a month shall come Sir Launcelot and all his noble knights, and rescue you worshipfully, and slay Sir Modred and all that hold with him.” And then Sir Gawain and all the ladies vanished. And anon the king called to fetch his noble lords and wise bishops unto him. And when they were come, the king told them his vision, and what Sir Gawain had told him. Then the king sent Sir Lucan, the butler, and Sir Bedivere, with two bishops, and charged them in any wise to take a treaty for a month and a day with Sir Modred. So they departed, and came to Sir Modred; and so, at the last, Sir Modred was agreed to have Cornwall and Kent during Arthur’s life, and all England after his death.\n\n“Sir Modred; he the nearest to the king, His nephew, ever like a subtle beast Lay couchant with his eyes upon the throne, Ready to spring, waiting a chance.” —Guinevere.\n\nThen was it agreed that King Arthur and Sir Modred should meet betwixt both their hosts, and each of them should bring fourteen persons, and then and there they should sign the treaty. And when King Arthur and his knights were prepared to go forth, he warned all his host, “If so be ye see any sword drawn, look ye come on fiercely, and slay whomsoever withstandeth, for I in no wise trust that traitor, Sir Modred.” In like wise Sir Modred warned his host. So they met, and were agreed and accorded thoroughly. And wine was brought, and they drank. Right then came an adder out of a little heath-bush, and stung a knight on the foot. And when the knight felt him sting, he looked down and saw the adder, and then he drew his sword to slay the adder, and thought of no other harm. And when the host on both sides saw that sword drawn, they blew trumpets and horns, and shouted greatly. And King Arthur took his horse, and rode to his party, saying, “Alas, this unhappy day!” And Sir Modred did in like wise. And never was there a more doleful battle in Christian land. And ever King Arthur rode throughout the battle, and did full nobly, as a worthy king should, and Sir Modred that day did his devoir, and put himself in great peril. And thus they fought all the long day, till the most of all the noble knights lay dead upon the ground. Then the king looked about him, and saw of all his host were left alive but two knights, Sir Lucan, the butler, and Sir Bedivere, his brother, and they were full sore wounded.\n\nThen King Arthur saw where Sir Modred leaned upon his sword among a great heap of dead men. “Now give me my spear,” said Arthur unto Sir Lucan; “for yonder I espy the traitor that hast wrought all this woe.” “Sir, let him be,” said Sir Lucan; “for if ye pass this unhappy day, ye shall be right well revenged upon him. Remember what the sprite of Sir Gawain told you, and leave off now, for ye have won the field; and if ye leave off now this evil day of destiny is past.” “Betide me life, betide me death,” said King Arthur, “he shall not now escape my hands.” Then the king took his spear in both hands, and ran toward Sir Modred, crying, “Traitor, now is thy death-day come.” And there King Arthur smote Sir Modred under the shield, with a thrust of his spear through the body. And when Sir Modred felt that he had his death-wound, with the might that he had he smote King Arthur, with his sword holden in both his hands, on the side of the head, that the sword pierced the helmet and the brain-pan; and then Sir Modred fell stark dead upon the earth. And the noble Arthur fell in a swoon to the earth. And Sir Lucan, the butler, and Sir Bedivere raised him up, and gently led him betwixt them both to a little chapel not far from the sea-side. And when the king was there he thought him well eased. Then heard they people cry in the field. And Sir Lucan went to see what that cry betokened; and he saw by the moonlight that pillers and robbers were come to rob the dead. And he returned, and said to the king, “By my rede, it is best that we bring you to some town.” “I would it were so,” said the king. And when the king tried to go he fainted. Then Sir Lucan took up the king on the one part, and Sir Bedivere on the other part; and in the lifting, Sir Lucan fell in a swoon to the earth, for he was grievously wounded. And then the noble knight’s heart burst. And when the king awoke he beheld Sir Lucan how he lay foaming at the mouth, and speechless. “Alas!” said the king, “this is to me a full heavy sight, to see this noble duke so die for my sake; for he would have holpen me that had more need of help than I, and he would not complain, his heart was so set to help me.” Then Sir Bedivere wept for his brother. “Leave this mourning and weeping,” said the king, “for wilt thou well, if I might live myself, the death of Sir Lucan would grieve me evermore; but my time hieth fast. Therefore,” said Arthur unto Sir Bedivere, “take thou Excalibar, my good sword, and go with it to yonder water-side; and when thou comest there I charge thee throw my sword in that water, and come again and tell me what thou there seest.” “My lord,” said Sir Bedivere, “your commandment shall be done.” So Sir Bedivere departed, and by the way he beheld that noble sword, that the pommel and the haft were all of precious stones; and then he said to himself, “If I throw this rich sword into the water no good shall come thereof, but only harm and loss.” And then Sir Bedivere hid Excalibar under a tree. And so, as soon as he might, he came again to the king. “What sawest thou there?” said the king. “Sir,” he said, “I saw nothing.” “Alas! thou hast deceived me,” said the king. “Go thou lightly again, and as thou love me, spare not to throw it in.” Then Sir Bedivere went again, and took the sword in his hand to throw it; but again it beseemed him but sin and shame to throw away that noble sword, and he hid it away again, and returned, and told the king he had done his commandment. “What sawest thou there?” said the king. “Sir,” he said, “I saw nothing but waters deep and waves wan.” “Ah, traitor untrue!” said King Arthur, “now hast thou betrayed me twice. And yet thou art named a noble knight, and hast been lief and dear to me. But now go again, and do as I bid thee, for thy long tarrying putteth me in jeopardy of my life.” Then Sir Bedivere went to the sword, and lightly took it up, and went to the water-side, and he bound the girdle about the hilt, and then he threw the sword as far into the water as he might. And there came an arm and a hand out of the water, and met it, and caught it, and shook it thrice and brandished it, and then vanished away the hand with the sword in the water.\n\nThen Sir Bedivere came again to the king, and told him what he saw. “Help me hence,” said the king, “for I fear I have tarried too long.” Then Sir Bedivere took the king on his back, and so went with him to that water-side; and when they came there, even fast by the bank there rode a little barge with many fair ladies in it, and among them was a queen; and all had black hoods, and they wept and shrieked when they saw King Arthur.\n\n“Now put me in the barge,” said the king. And there received him three queens with great mourning, and in one of their laps King Arthur laid his head. And the queen said, “Ah, dear brother, why have ye tarried so long? Alas! this wound on your head hath caught overmuch cold.” And then they rowed from the land, and Sir Bedivere beheld them go from him. Then he cried: “Ah, my lord Arthur, will ye leave me here alone among mine enemies?” “Comfort thyself,” said the king, “for in me is no further help; for I will to the Isle of Avalon, to heal me of my grievous wound.” And as soon as Sir Bedivere had lost sight of the barge, he wept and wailed; then he took the forest, and went all that night, and in the morning he was ware of a chapel and a hermitage.\n\nThen went Sir Bedivere thither; and when he came into the chapel, he saw where lay an hermit on the ground, near a tomb that was newly graven. “Sir,” said Sir Bedivere, “what man is there buried that ye pray so near unto?” “Fair son,” said the hermit, “I know not verily. But this night there came a number of ladies, and brought hither one dead, and prayed me to bury him.” “Alas!” said Sir Bedivere, “that was my lord, King Arthur.” Then Sir Bedivere swooned; and when he awoke he prayed the hermit he might abide with him, to live with fasting and prayers. “Ye are welcome,” said the hermit. So there bode Sir Bedivere with the hermit; and he put on poor clothes, and served the hermit full lowly in fasting and in prayers.\n\nThus of Arthur I find never more written in books that be authorized, nor more of the very certainty of his death; but thus was he led away in a ship, wherein were three queens; the one was King Arthur’s sister, Queen Morgane le Fay; the other was Viviane, the Lady of the Lake; and the third was the queen of North Galis. And this tale Sir Bedivere, knight of the Table Round, made to be written.\n\nYet some men say that King Arthur is not dead, but hid away into another place, and men say that he shall come again and reign over England. But many say that there is written on his tomb this verse:\n\n“Hie jacet Arthurus, Rex quondam, Rexque futurus.” Here Arthur lies, King once and King to be.\n\nAnd when Queen Guenever understood that King Arthur was slain, and all the noble knights with him, she stole away, and five ladies with her; and so she went to Almesbury, and made herself a nun, and ware white clothes and black, and took great penance as ever did sinful lady, and lived in fasting, prayers, and alms-deeds. And there she was abbess and ruler of the nuns.\n\n“And when she came to Almesbury she spake There to the nuns, and said, ‘Mine enemies Pursue me, but, O peaceful Sisterhood, Receive, and yield me sanctuary, nor ask Her name to whom ye yield it, till her time To tell you:’ and her beauty, grace and power Wrought as a charm upon them, and they spared To ask it.” —Guinevere.\n\nNow turn we from her, and speak of Sir Launcelot of the Lake.\n\nWhen Sir Launcelot heard in his country that Sir Modred was crowned king of England, and made war against his own uncle, King Arthur, then was Sir Launcelot wroth out of measure, and said to his kinsmen: “Alas, that double traitor, Sir Modred! now it repenteth me that ever he escaped out of my hands.” Then Sir Launcelot and his fellows made ready in all haste, with ships and galleys, to pass into England; and so he passed over till he came to Dover, and there he landed with a great army. Then Sir Launcelot was told that King Arthur was slain. “Alas!” said Sir Launcelot, “this is the heaviest tidings that ever came to me.” Then he called the kings, dukes, barons, and knights, and said thus: “My fair lords, I thank you all for coming into this country with me, but we came too late, and that shall repent me while I live. But since it is so,” said Sir Launcelot, “I will myself ride and seek my lady, Queen Guenever, for I have heard say she hath fled into the west; therefore ye shall abide me here fifteen days, and if I come not within that time, then take your ships and your host, and depart into your country.”\n\nSo Sir Launcelot departed and rode westerly, and there he sought many days; and at last he came to a nunnery, and was seen of Queen Guenever as he walked in the cloister; and when she saw him she swooned away. And when she might speak she bade him to be called to her. And when Sir Launcelot was brought to her she said: “Sir Launcelot, I require thee and beseech thee, for all the love that ever was betwixt us, that thou never see me more, but return to thy kingdom and take thee a wife, and live with her with joy and bliss; and pray for me to my Lord, that I may get my soul’s health.” “Nay, madam,” said Sir Launcelot, “wit you well that I shall never do; but the same destiny that ye have taken you to will I take me unto, for to please and serve God.” And so they parted, with tears and much lamentation; and the ladies bare the queen to her chamber, and Sir Launcelot took his horse and rode away, weeping.\n\nAnd at last Sir Launcelot was ware of a hermitage and a chapel, and then he heard a little bell ring to mass; and thither he rode and alighted, and tied his horse to the gate, and heard mass. And he that sang the mass was the hermit with whom Sir Bedivere had taken up his abode; and Sir Bedivere knew Sir Launcelot, and they spake together after mass. But when Sir Bedivere had told his tale, Sir Launcelot’s heart almost burst for sorrow. Then he kneeled down, and prayed the hermit to shrive him, and besought that he might be his brother. Then the hermit said, “I will gladly;” and then he put a habit upon Sir Launcelot, and there he served God day and night, with prayers and fastings.\n\nAnd the great host abode at Dover till the end of the fifteen days set by Sir Launcelot, and then Sir Bohort made them to go home again to their own country; and Sir Bohort, Sir Hector de Marys, Sir Blamor, and many others, took on them to ride through all England to seek Sir Launcelot. So Sir Bohort by fortune rode until he came to the same chapel where Sir Launcelot was; and when he saw Sir Launcelot in that manner of clothing he prayed the hermit that he might be in that same. And so there was an habit put upon him, and there he lived in prayers and fasting. And within half a year came others of the knights, their fellows, and took such a habit as Sir Launcelot and Sir Bohort had. Thus they endured in great penance six years.\n\nAnd upon a night there came a vision to Sir Launcelot, and charged him to haste toward Almesbury, and “by the time thou come there, thou shalt find Queen Guenever dead.” Then Sir Launcelot rose up early and told the hermit thereof. Then said the hermit, “It were well that ye disobey not this vision.” And Sir Launcelot took his seven companions with him, and on foot they went from Glastonbury to Almesbury, which is more than thirty miles. And when they were come to Almesbury, they found that Queen Guenever died but half an hour before. Then Sir Launcelot saw her visage, but he wept not greatly, but sighed. And so he did all the observance of the service himself, both the “dirige” at night, and at morn he sang mass. And there was prepared an horse-bier, and Sir Launcelot and his fellows followed the bier on foot from Almesbury until they came to Glastonbury; and she was wrapped in cered clothes, and laid in a coffin of marble. And when she was put in the earth Sir Launcelot swooned, and lay long as one dead.\n\nAnd Sir Launcelot never after ate but little meat, nor drank; but continually mourned. And within six weeks Sir Launcelot fell sick; and he sent for the hermit and all his true fellows, and said, “Sir hermit, I pray you give me all my rights that a Christian man ought to have.” “It shall not need,” said the hermit and all his fellows; “it is but heaviness of your blood, and to-morrow morn you shall be well.” “My fair lords,” said Sir Launcelot, “my careful body will into the earth; I have warning more than now I will say; therefore give me my rights.” So when he was houseled and aneled, and had all that a Christian man ought to have, he prayed the hermit that his fellows might bear his body to Joyous Garde. (Some men say it was Alnwick, and some say it was Bamborough.) “It repenteth me sore,” said Sir Launcelot, “but I made a vow aforetime that in Joyous Garde I would be buried.” Then there was weeping and wringing of hands among his fellows. And that night Sir Launcelot died; and when Sir Bohort and his fellows came to his bedside the next morning they found him stark dead; and he lay as if he had smiled, and the sweetest savor all about him that ever they knew.\n\nAnd they put Sir Launcelot into the same horse-bier that Queen Guenever was laid in, and the hermit and they altogether went with the body till they came to Joyous Garde. And there they laid his corpse in the body of the quire, and sang and read many psalms and prayers over him. And ever his visage was laid open and naked, that all folks might behold him. And right thus, as they were at their service, there came Sir Hector de Maris, that had seven years sought Sir Launcelot, his brother, through all England, Scotland and Wales. And when Sir Hector heard such sounds in the chapel of Joyous Garde he alighted and came into the quire. And all they knew Sir Hector. Then went Sir Bohort, and told him how there lay Sir Launcelot, his brother, dead. Then Sir Hector threw his shield, his sword, and helm from him. And when he beheld Sir Launcelot’s visage it were hard for any tongue to tell the doleful complaints he made for his brother. “Ah, Sir Launcelot!” he said, “there thou liest. And now I dare to say thou wert never matched of none earthly knight’s hand. And thou wert the courteousest knight that ever bare shield; and thou wert the truest friend to thy lover that ever bestrode horse; and thou wert the truest lover, of a sinful man, that ever loved woman; and thou wert the kindest man that ever struck with sword. And thou wert the goodliest person that ever came among press of knights. And thou wert the meekest man, and the gentlest, that ever ate in hall among ladies. And thou wert the sternest knight to thy mortal foe that ever put spear in the rest.” Then there was weeping and dolor out of measure. Thus they kept Sir Launcelot’s corpse fifteen days, and then they buried it with great devotion.\n\nThen they went back with the hermit to his hermitage. And Sir Bedivere was there ever still hermit to his life’s end. And Sir Bohort, Sir Hector, Sir Blamor, and Sir Bleoberis went into the Holy Land. And these four knights did many battles upon the miscreants, the Turks; and there they died upon a Good Friday, as it pleased God.\n\nThus endeth this noble and joyous book, entitled “La Morte d’Arthur;” notwithstanding it treateth of the birth, life, and acts of the said King Arthur, and of his noble Knights of the Round Table, their marvellous enquests and adventures, the achieving of the Sangreal, and, in the end, le Morte d’Arthur, with the dolorous death and departing out of this world of them all. Which book was reduced into English by Sir Thomas Mallory, Knight, and divided into twenty-one books, chaptered and imprinted and finished in the Abbey Westmestre, the last day of July, the year of our Lord MCCCCLXXXV.\n\nCaxton me fieri fecit.\n\nIT has been well known to the literati and antiquarians of Europe that there exist in the great public libraries voluminous manuscripts of romances and tales once popular, but which on the invention of printing had already become antiquated, and fallen into neglect. They were therefore never printed, and seldom perused even by the learned, until about half a century ago, when attention was again directed to them, and they were found very curious monuments of ancient manners, habits, and modes of thinking. Several have since been edited, some by individuals, as Sir Walter Scott and the poet Southey, others by antiquarian societies. The class of readers which could be counted on for such publications was so small that no inducement of profit could be found to tempt editors and publishers to give them to the world. It was therefore only a few, and those the most accessible, which were put in print. There was a class of manuscripts of this kind which were known, or rather suspected, to be both curious and valuable, but which it seemed almost hopeless to expect ever to see in fair printed English. These were the Welsh popular tales called Mabinogeon, a plural word, the singular being Mabinogi, a tale. Manuscripts of these were contained in the Bodleian Library at Oxford and elsewhere, but the difficulty was to find translators and editors. The Welsh is a spoken language among the peasantry of Wales, but is entirely neglected by the learned, unless they are natives of the principality. Of the few Welsh scholars none were found who took sufficient interest in this branch of learning to give these productions to the English public. Southey and Scott, and others, who, like them, loved the old romantic legends of their country, often urged upon the Welsh literati the duty of reproducing the Mabinogeon. Southey, in the preface of his edition of “Morte d’Arthur,” says: “The specimens which I have seen are exceedingly curious; nor is there a greater desideratum in British literature than an edition of these tales, with a literal version, and such comments as Mr. Davies of all men is best qualified to give. Certain it is that many of the round table fictions originated in Wales, or in Bretagne, and probably might still be traced there.”\n\nAgain, in a letter to Sir Charles W. W. Wynn, dated 1819, he says:\n\n“I begin almost to despair of ever seeing more of the Mabinogeon; and yet if some competent Welshman could be found to edit it carefully, with as literal a version as possible, I am sure it might be made worth his while by a subscription, printing a small edition at a high price, perhaps two hundred at five guineas. I myself would gladly subscribe at that price per volume for such an edition of the whole of your genuine remains in prose and verse. Till some such collection is made, the ‘gentlemen of Wales’ ought to be prohibited from wearing a leek; ay, and interdicted from toasted cheese also. Your bards would have met with better usage if they had been Scotchmen.”\n\nSharon Turner and Sir Walter Scott also expressed a similar wish for the publication of the Welsh manuscripts. The former took part in an attempt to effect it, through the instrumentality of a Mr. Owen, a Welshman, but, we judge, by what Southey says of him, imperfectly acquainted with English. Southey’s language is “William Owen lent me three parts of the Mabinogeon, delightfully translated into so Welsh an idiom and syntax that such a translation is as instructive as an original.” In another letter he adds, “Let Sharon make his language grammatical, but not alter their idiom in the slightest point.”\n\nIt is probable Mr. Owen did not proceed far in an undertaking which, so executed, could expect but little popular patronage. It was not till an individual should appear possessed of the requisite knowledge of the two languages, of enthusiasm sufficient for the task, and of pecuniary resources sufficient to be independent of the booksellers and of the reading public, that such a work could be confidently expected. Such an individual has, since Southey’s day and Scott’s, appeared in the person of Lady Charlotte Guest, an English lady united to a gentleman of property in Wales, who, having acquired the language of the principality, and become enthusiastically fond of its literary treasures, has given them to the English reader, in a dress which the printer’s and the engraver’s arts have done their best to adorn. In four royal octavo volumes containing the Welsh originals, the translation, and ample illustrations from French, German, and other contemporary and affiliated literature, the Mabinogeon is spread before us. To the antiquarian and the student of language and ethnology an invaluable treasure, it yet can hardly in such a form win its way to popular acquaintance. We claim no other merit than that of bringing it to the knowledge of our readers, of abridging its details, of selecting its most attractive portions, and of faithfully preserving throughout the style in which Lady Guest has clothed her legends. For this service we hope that our readers will confess we have laid them under no light obligation.\n\n————\n\n# THE BRITONS\n\nTHE earliest inhabitants of Britain are supposed to have been a branch of that great family known in history by the designation of Celts. Cambria, which is a frequent name for Wales, is thought to be derived from Cymri, the name which the Welsh traditions apply to an immigrant people who entered the island from the adjacent continent. This name is thought to be identical with those of Cimmerians and Cimbri, under which the Greek and Roman historians describe a barbarous people, who spread themselves from the north of the Euxine over the whole of Northwestern Europe.\n\nThe origin of the names Wales and Welsh has been much canvassed. Some writers make them a derivation from Gael or Gaul, which names are said to signify “woodlanders;” others observe that Walsh, in the northern languages, signifies a stranger, and that the aboriginal Britons were so called by those who at a later era invaded the island and possessed the greater part of it, the Saxons and Angles.\n\nThe Romans held Britain from the invasion of Julius Cæsar till their voluntary withdrawal from the island, A.D. 420,—that is, about five hundred years. In that time there must have been a wide diffusion of their arts and institutions among the natives. The remains of roads, cities, and fortifications show that they did much to develop and improve the country, while those of their villas and castles prove that many of the settlers possessed wealth and taste for the ornamental arts. Yet the Roman sway was sustained chiefly by force, and never extended over the entire island. The northern portion, now Scotland, remained independent, and the western portion, constituting Wales and Cornwall, was only nominally subjected.\n\nNeither did the later invading hordes succeed in subduing the remoter sections of the island. For ages after the arrival of the Saxons under Hengist and Horsa, A.D. 449, the whole western coast of Britain was possessed by the aboriginal inhabitants, engaged in constant warfare with the invaders.\n\nIt has, therefore, been a favorite boast of the people of Wales and Cornwall that the original British stock flourishes in its unmixed purity only among them. We see this notion flashing out in poetry occasionally, as when Gray, in “The Bard,” prophetically describing Queen Elizabeth, who was of the Tudor, a Welsh race, says:\n\n“Her eye proclaims her of the Briton line;”\n\nand, contrasting the princes of the Tudor with those of the Norman race, he exclaims:\n\n“All hail, ye genuine kings, Britannia’s issue, hail!”\n\n# THE WELSH LANGUAGE AND LITERATURE\n\nThe Welsh language is one of the oldest in Europe. It possesses poems the origin of which is referred with probability to the sixth century. The language of some of these is so antiquated that the best scholars differ about the interpretation of many passages; but, generally speaking, the body of poetry which the Welsh possess, from the year 1000 downwards, is intelligible to those who are acquainted with the modern language.\n\nTill within the last half-century these compositions remained buried in the libraries of colleges or of individuals, and so difficult of access that no successful attempt was made to give them to the world. This reproach was removed after ineffectual appeals to the patriotism of the gentry of Wales, by Owen Jones, a furrier of London, who at his own expense collected and published the chief productions of Welsh literature, under the title of the Myvyrian Archæology of Wales. In this task he was assisted by Dr. Owen and other Welsh scholars.\n\nAfter the cessation of Jones’ exertions the old apathy returned, and continued till within a few years. Dr. Owen exerted himself to obtain support for the publication of the Mabinogeon or Prose Tales of the Welsh, but died without accomplishing his purpose, which has since been carried into execution by Lady Charlotte Guest. The legends which fill the remainder of this volume are taken from this work, of which we have already spoken more fully in the introductory chapter to the First Part.\n\n# THE WELSH BARDS\n\nThe authors to whom the oldest Welsh poems are attributed are Aneurin, who is supposed to have lived A.D. 500 to 550, and Taliesin, Llywarch Hen (Llywarch the Aged), and Myrddin or Merlin, who were a few years later. The authenticity of the poems which bear their names has been assailed, and it is still an open question how many and which of them are authentic, though it is hardly to be doubted that some are so. The poem of Aneurin entitled the “Gododin,” bears very strong marks of authenticity. Aneurin was one of the Northern Britons of Strath-Clyde, who have left to that part of the district they inhabited the name of Cumberland, or Land of the Cymri. In this poem he laments the defeat of his countrymen by the Saxons at the battle of Cattraeth, in consequence of having partaken too freely of the mead before joining in combat. The bard himself and two of his fellow-warriors were all who escaped from the field. A portion of this poem has been translated by Gray, of which the following is an extract:\n\n“To Cattraeth’s vale, in glittering row, Twice two hundred warriors go; Every warrior’s manly neck Chains of regal honor deck, Wreathed in many a golden link; From the golden cup they drink Nectar that the bees produce, Or the grape’s exalted juice. Flushed with mirth and hope they burn, But none to Cattraeth’s vale return, Save Aëron brave, and Conan strong, Bursting through the bloody throng, And I, the meanest of them all, That live to weep, and sing their fall.”\n\nThe works of Taliesin are of much more questionable authenticity. There is a story of the adventures of Taliesin so strongly marked with mythical traits as to cast suspicion on the writings attributed to him. This story will be found in the subsequent pages.\n\n# THE TRIADS\n\nThe Triads are a peculiar species of poetical composition, of which the Welsh bards have left numerous examples. They are enumerations of a triad of persons, or events, or observations, strung together in one short sentence. This form of composition, originally invented, in all likelihood, to assist the memory, has been raised by the Welsh to a degree of elegance of which it hardly at first sight appears susceptible. The Triads are of all ages, some of them probably as old as anything in the language. Short as they are individually, the collection in the Myvyrian Archæology occupies more than one hundred and seventy pages of double columns. We will give some specimens, beginning with personal triads, and giving the first place to one of King Arthur’s own composition:\n\n“I have three heroes in battle: Mael the tall, and Llyr, with his army, And Caradoc, the pillar of Wales.”\n\n“The three principal bards of the island of Britain:— Merlin Ambrose Merlin the son of Morfyn, called also Merlin the Wild, And Taliesin, the chief of the bards.”\n\n“The three golden-tongued knights of the court of Arthur:— Gawain, son of Gwyar, Drydvas, son of Tryphin, And Eliwlod, son of Madag, ap Uther.”\n\n“The three honorable feasts of the island of Britain:— The feast of Caswallaun, after repelling Julius Cæsar from this isle; The feast of Aurelius Ambrosius, after he had conquered the Saxons; And the feast of King Arthur, at Carleon upon Usk.”\n\n“Guenever, the daughter of Laodegan the giant, Bad when little, worse when great.”\n\nNext follow some moral triads:\n\n“Hast thou heard what Dremhidydd sung, An ancient watchman on the castle walls? A refusal is better than a promise unperformed.”\n\n“Hast thou heard what Llenleawg sung, The noble chief wearing the golden torques? The grave is better than a life of want.”\n\n“Hast thou heard what Garselit sung, The Irishman whom it is safe to follow? Sin is bad, if long pursued.”\n\n“Hast thou heard what Avaon sung, The son of Taliesin, of the recording verse? The cheek will not conceal the anguish of the heart.”\n\n“Didst thou hear what Llywarch sung, The intrepid and brave old man? Greet kindly, though there be no acquaintance.”\n\n————\n\n# KYNON’S ADVENTURE\n\nKING ARTHUR was at Caerleon upon Usk; and one day he sat in his chamber, and with him were Owain, the son of Urien, and Kynon, the son of Clydno, and Kay, the son of Kyner, and Guenever and her handmaidens at needlework by the window. In the centre of the chamber King Arthur sat, upon a seat of green rushes,[56] over which was spread a covering of flame-covered satin, and a cushion of red satin was under his elbow.\n\nThen Arthur spoke. “If I thought you would not disparage me,” said he, “I would sleep while I wait for my repast; and you can entertain one another with relating tales, and can obtain a flagon of mead and some meat from Kay.” And the king went to sleep. And Kynon the son of Clydno asked Kay for that which Arthur had promised them. “I too will have the good tale which he promised me,” said Kay. “Nay,” answered Kynon; “fairer will it be for thee to fulfil Arthur’s behest in the first place, and then we will tell thee the best tale that we know.” So Kay went to the kitchen and to the mead-cellar, and returned, bearing a flagon of mead, and a golden goblet, and a handful of skewers, upon which were broiled collops of meat. Then they ate the collops, and began to drink the mead. “Now,” said Kay, “it is time for you to give me my story.” “Kynon,” said Owain, “do thou pay to Kay the tale that is his due.” “I will do so,” answered Kynon.\n\n“I was the only son of my mother and father, and I was exceedingly aspiring, and my daring was very great. I thought there was no enterprise in the world too mighty for me: and after I had achieved all the adventures that were in my own country, I equipped myself, and set forth to journey through deserts and distant regions. And at length it chanced that I came to the fairest valley in the world, wherein were trees all of equal growth; and a river ran through the valley, and a path was by the side of the river. And I followed the path until midday, and continued my journey along the remainder of the valley until the evening; and at the extremity of the plain I came to a large and lustrous castle, at the foot of which was a torrent. And I approached the castle, and there I beheld two youths with yellow curling hair, each with a frontlet of gold upon his head, and clad in a garment of yellow satin; and they had gold clasps upon their insteps. In the hand of each of them was an ivory bow, strung with the sinews of the stag, and their arrows and their shafts were of the bone of the whale, and were winged with peacock’s feathers. The shafts also had golden heads. And they had daggers with blades of gold, and with hilts of the bone of the whale. And they were shooting at a mark.\n\n“And a little away from them I saw a man in the prime of life, with his beard newly shorn, clad in a robe and mantle of yellow satin, and round the top of his mantle was a band of gold lace. On his feet were shoes of variegated leather,[57] fastened by two bosses of gold. When I saw him I went towards him and saluted him; and such was his courtesy, that he no sooner received my greeting than he returned it. And he went with me towards the castle. Now there were no dwellers in the castle, except those who were in one hall. And there I saw four and twenty damsels, embroidering satin at a window. And this I tell thee, Kay, that the least fair of them was fairer than the fairest maid thou didst ever behold in the island of Britain; and the least lovely of them was more lovely than Guenever, the wife of Arthur, when she appeared loveliest, at the feast of Easter. They rose up at my coming, and six of them took my horse, and divested me of my armor, and six others took my arms and washed them in a vessel till they were perfectly bright. And the third six spread cloths upon the tables and prepared meat. And the fourth six took off my soiled garments and placed others upon me, namely, an under vest and a doublet of fine linen, and a robe and a surcoat, and a mantle of yellow satin, with a broad gold band upon the mantle. And they placed cushions both beneath and around me, with coverings of red linen, and I sat down. Now the six maidens who had taken my horse unharnessed him as well as if they had been the best squires in the island of Britain.\n\n“Then behold they brought bowls of silver, wherein was water to wash and towels of linen, some green and some white; and I washed. And in a little while the man sat down at the table. And I sat next to him, and below me sat all the maidens, except those who waited on us. And the table was of silver, and the cloths upon the table were of linen. And no vessel was served upon the table that was not either of gold or of silver or of buffalo horn. And our meat was brought to us. And verily, Kay, I saw there every sort of meat, and every sort of liquor that I ever saw elsewhere; but the meat and the liquor were better served there than I ever saw them in any other place.\n\n“Until the repast was half over, neither the man nor any one of the damsels spoke a single word to me; but when the man perceived that it would be more agreeable for me to converse than to eat any more, he began to inquire of me who I was. Then I told the man who I was and what was the cause of my journey, and said that I was seeking whether any one was superior to me, or whether I could gain mastery over all. The man looked upon me, and he smiled and said, ‘If I did not fear to do thee a mischief, I would show thee that which thou seekest.’ Then I desired him to speak freely. And he said: ‘Sleep here to-night, and in the morning arise early, and take the road upwards through the valley, until thou reachest the wood. A little way within the wood thou wilt come to a large sheltered glade, with a mound in the centre. And thou wilt see a black man of great stature on the top of the mound. He has but one foot, and one eye in the middle of his forehead. He is the wood-ward of that wood. And thou wilt see a thousand wild animals grazing around him. Inquire of him the way out of the glade, and he will reply to thee briefly, and will point out the road by which thou shalt find that which thou art in quest of.’\n\n“And long seemed that night to me. And the next morning I arose and equipped myself, and mounted my horse, and proceeded straight through the valley to the wood, and at length I arrived at the glade. And the black man was there, sitting upon the top of the mound; and I was three times more astonished at the number of wild animals that I beheld than the man had said I should be. Then I inquired of him the way and he asked me roughly whither I would go. And when I had told him who I was and what I sought, ‘Take,’ said he, ‘that path that leads toward the head of the glade, and there thou wilt find an open space like to a large valley, and in the midst of it a tall tree. Under this tree is a fountain, and by the side of the fountain a marble slab, and on the marble slab a silver bowl, attached by a chain of silver, that it may not be carried away. Take the bowl and throw a bowlful of water on the slab. And if thou dost not find trouble in that adventure, thou needest not seek it during the rest of thy life.’\n\n“So I journeyed on until I reached the summit of the steep. And there I found everything as the black man had described it to me. And I went up to the tree, and beneath it I saw the fountain, and by its side the marble slab, and the silver bowl fastened by the chain. Then I took the bowl, and cast a bowlful of water upon the slab, and immediately I heard a mighty peal of thunder, so that heaven and earth seemed to tremble with its fury. And after the thunder came a shower; and of a truth I tell thee, Kay, that it was such a shower as neither man nor beast could endure and live. I turned my horse’s flank toward the shower, and placed the beak of my shield over his head and neck, while I held the upper part of it over my own neck. And thus I withstood the shower. And presently the sky became clear, and with that, behold, the birds lighted upon the tree, and sang. And truly, Kay, I never heard any melody equal to that, either before or since. And when I was most charmed with listening to the birds, lo! a chiding voice was heard of one approaching me and saying: ‘O knight, what has brought thee hither? What evil have I done to thee that thou shouldst act towards me and my possessions as thou hast this day? Dost thou not know that the shower to-day has left in my dominions neither man nor beast alive that was exposed to it?’ And thereupon, behold, a knight on a black horse appeared, clothed in jet-black velvet, and with a tabard of black linen about him. And we charged each other, and, as the onset was furious, it was not long before I was overthrown. Then the knight passed the shaft of his lance through the bridle-rein of my horse, and rode off with the two horses, leaving me where I was. And he did not even bestow so much notice upon me as to imprison me, nor did he despoil me of my arms. So I returned along the road by which I had come. And when I reached the glade where the black man was, I confess to thee, Kay, it is a marvel that I did not melt down into a liquid pool, through the shame that I felt at the black man’s derision. And that night I came to the same castle where I had spent the night preceding. And I was more agreeably entertained that night than I had been the night before. And I conversed freely with the inmates of the castle; and none of them alluded to my expedition to the fountain, neither did I mention it to any. And I remained there that night. When I arose on the morrow I found ready saddled a dark bay palfrey, with nostrils as red as scarlet. And after putting on my armor, and leaving there my blessing, I returned to my own court. And that horse I still possess, and he is in the stable yonder. And I declare that I would not part with him for the best palfrey in the island of Britain.\n\n“Now, of a truth, Kay, no man ever before confessed to an adventure so much to his own discredit; and verily it seems strange to me that neither before nor since have I heard of any person who knew of this adventure, and that the subject of it should exist within King Arthur’s dominions without any other person lighting upon it.”\n\n————\n\n“NOW,” quoth Owain, “would it not be well to go and endeavor to discover that place?”\n\n“By the hand of my friend,” said Kay, “often dost thou utter that with thy tongue which thou wouldest not make good with thy deeds.”\n\n“In very truth,” said Guenever, “it were better thou wert hanged, Kay, than to use such uncourteous speech towards a man like Owain.”\n\n“By the hand of my friend, good lady,” said Kay, “thy praise of Owain is not greater than mine.”\n\nWith that Arthur awoke, and asked if he had not been sleeping a little.\n\n“Yes, lord,” answered Owain, “thou hast slept awhile.”\n\n“Is it time for us to go to meat?”\n\n“It is, lord,” said Owain.\n\nThen the horn for washing was sounded, and the king and all his household sat down to eat. And when the meal was ended Owain withdrew to his lodging, and made ready his horse and his arms.\n\nOn the morrow with the dawn of day he put on his armor, and mounted his charger, and travelled through distant lands, and over desert mountains. And at length he arrived at the valley which Kynon had described to him, and he was certain that it was the same that he sought. And journeying along the valley, by the side of the river, he followed its course till he came to the plain, and within sight of the castle. When he approached the castle he saw the youths shooting with their bows, in the place where Kynon had seen them, and the yellow man, to whom the castle belonged, standing hard by. And no sooner had Owain saluted the yellow man, than he was saluted by him in return.\n\nAnd he went forward towards the castle, and there he saw the chamber; and when he had entered the chamber, he beheld the maidens working at satin embroidery, in chains of gold. And their beauty and their comeliness seemed to Owain far greater than Kynon had represented to him. And they arose to wait upon Owain, as they had done to Kynon. And the meal which they set before him gave even more satisfaction to Owain than it had done to Kynon.\n\nAbout the middle of the repast the yellow man asked Owain the object of his journey. And Owain made it known to him, and said, “I am in quest of the knight who guards the fountain.” Upon this the yellow man smiled, and said that he was as loth to point out that adventure to him as he had been to Kynon. However, he described the whole to Owain, and they retired to rest.\n\nThe next morning Owain found his horse made ready for him by the damsels, and he set forward and came to the glade where the black man was. And the stature of the black man seemed more wonderful to Owain than it had done to Kynon; and Owain asked of him his road, and he showed it to him. And Owain followed the road till he came to the green tree; and he beheld the fountain, and the slab beside the fountain, with the bowl upon it. And Owain took the bowl and threw a bowlful of water upon the slab. And, lo! the thunder was heard, and after the thunder came the shower, more violent than Kynon had described, and after the shower the sky became bright. And immediately the birds came and settled upon the tree and sang. And when their song was most pleasing to Owain he beheld a knight coming towards him through the valley; and he prepared to receive him, and encountered him violently. Having broken both their lances, they drew their swords and fought blade to blade. Then Owain struck the knight a blow through his helmet, head-piece, and visor, and through the skin, and the flesh, and the bone, until it wounded the very brain. Then the black knight felt that he had received a mortal wound, upon which he turned his horse’s head and fled. And Owain pursued him and followed close upon him, although he was not near enough to strike him with his sword. Then Owain descried a vast and resplendent castle; and they came to the castle gate. And the black knight was allowed to enter, and the portcullis was let fall upon Owain; and it struck his horse behind the saddle, and cut him in two, and carried away the rowels of the spurs that were upon Owain’s heels. And the portcullis descended to the floor. And the rowels of the spurs and part of the horse were without, and Owain with the other part of the horse remained between the two gates, and the inner gate was closed, so that Owain could not go thence; and Owain was in a perplexing situation. And while he was in this state, he could see through an aperture in the gate a street facing him, with a row of houses on each side. And he beheld a maiden, with yellow, curling hair, and a frontlet of gold upon her head; and she was clad in a dress of yellow satin, and on her feet were shoes of variegated leather. And she approached the gate, and desired that it should be opened. “Heaven knows, lady,” said Owain, “it is no more possible for me to open to thee from hence, than it is for thee to set me free.” And he told her his name, and who he was. “Truly,” said the damsel, “it is very sad that thou canst not be released; and every woman ought to succor thee, for I know there is no one more faithful in the service of ladies than thou. Therefore,” quoth she, “whatever is in my power to do for thy release, I will do it. Take this ring and put it on thy finger, with the stone inside thy hand, and close thy hand upon the stone. And as long as thou concealest it, it will conceal thee. When they come forth to fetch thee, they will be much grieved that they cannot find thee. And I will await thee on the horseblock yonder, and thou wilt be able to see me, though I cannot see thee. Therefore come and place thy hand upon my shoulder, that I may know that thou art near me. And by the way that I go hence do thou accompany me.”\n\nThen the maiden went away from Owain, and he did all that she had told him. And the people of the castle came to seek Owain to put him to death; and when they found nothing but the half of his horse, they were sorely grieved.\n\nAnd Owain vanished from among them, and went to the maiden, and placed his hand upon her shoulder; whereupon she set off, and Owain followed her, until they came to the door of a large and beautiful chamber, and the maiden opened it, and they went in. And Owain looked around the chamber, and behold there was not a single nail in it that was not painted with gorgeous colors, and there was not a single panel that had not sundry images in gold portrayed upon it.\n\nThe maiden kindled a fire, and took water in a silver bowl, and gave Owain water to wash. Then she placed before him a silver table, inlaid with gold; upon which was a cloth of yellow linen, and she brought him food. And, of a truth, Owain never saw any kind of meat that was not there in abundance, but it was better cooked there than he had ever found it in any other place. And there was not one vessel from which he was served that was not of gold or of silver. And Owain ate and drank until late in the afternoon, when lo! they heard a mighty clamor in the castle, and Owain asked the maiden what it was. “They are administering extreme unction,” said she, “to the nobleman who owns the castle.” And she prepared a couch for Owain which was meet for Arthur himself, and Owain went to sleep.\n\nAnd a little after daybreak he heard an exceeding loud clamor and wailing, and he asked the maiden what was the cause of it. “They are bearing to the church the body of the nobleman who owned the castle.”\n\nAnd Owain rose up, and clothed himself, and opened a window of the chamber, and looked towards the castle; and he could see neither the bounds nor the extent of the hosts that filled the streets. And they were fully armed; and a vast number of women were with them, both on horseback and on foot, and all the ecclesiastics in the city singing. In the midst of the throng he beheld the bier, over which was a veil of white linen; and wax tapers were burning beside and around it; and none that supported the bier was lower in rank than a powerful baron.\n\nNever did Owain see an assemblage so gorgeous with silk[59] and satin. And, following the train, he beheld a lady with yellow hair falling over her shoulders, and stained with blood; and about her a dress of yellow satin, which was torn. Upon her feet were shoes of variegated leather. And it was a marvel that the ends of her fingers were not bruised from the violence with which she smote her hands together. Truly she would have been the fairest lady Owain ever saw, had she been in her usual guise. And her cry was louder than the shout of the men or the clamor of the trumpets. No sooner had he beheld the lady than he became inflamed with her love, so that it took entire possession of him.\n\nThen he inquired of the maiden who the lady was. “Heaven knows,” replied the maiden, “she is the fairest and the most chaste, and the most liberal, and the most noble of women. She is my mistress, and she is called the Countess of the Fountain, the wife of him whom thou didst slay yesterday.” “Verily,” said Owain, “she is the woman that I love best.” “Verily,” said the maiden, “she shall also love thee, not a little.”\n\nThen the maiden prepared a repast for Owain, and truly he thought he had never before so good a meal, nor was he ever so well served. Then she left him, and went towards the castle. When she came there, she found nothing but mourning and sorrow; and the Countess in her chamber could not bear the sight of any one through grief. Luned, for that was the name of the maiden, saluted her, but the Countess answered her not. And the maiden bent down towards her, and said, “What aileth thee, that thou answereth no one to-day?” “Luned,” said the Countess, “what change hath befallen thee, that thou hast not come to visit me in my grief. It was wrong in thee, and I so sorely afflicted.” “Truly,” said Luned, “I thought thy good sense was greater than I find it to be. Is it well for thee to mourn after that good man, or for anything else that thou canst not have?” “I declare to Heaven,” said the Countess, “that in the whole world there is not a man equal to him.” “Not so,” said Luned, “for an ugly man would be as good as or better than he.” “I declare to Heaven,” said the Countess, “that were it not repugnant to me to put to death one whom I have brought up, I would have thee executed for making such a comparison to me. As it is, I will banish thee.” “I am glad,” said Luned, “that thou hast no other cause to do so than that I would have been of service to thee, where thou didst not know what was to thine advantage. Henceforth, evil betide whichever of us shall make the first advance towards reconciliation to the other, whether I should seek an invitation from thee, or thou of thine own accord should send to invite.”\n\nWith that Luned went forth; and the Countess arose and followed her to the door of the chamber, and began coughing loudly. And when Luned looked back, the Countess beckoned to her, and she returned to the Countess. “In truth,” said the Countess, “evil is thy disposition; but if thou knowest what is to my advantage, declare it to me.” “I will do so,” said she.\n\n“Thou knowest that, except by warfare and arms, it is impossible for thee to preserve thy possessions; delay not, therefore, to seek some one who can defend them.” “And how can I do that?” said the Countess. “I will tell thee,” said Luned; “unless thou canst defend the fountain, thou canst not maintain thy dominions; and no one can defend the fountain except it be a knight of Arthur’s household. I will go to Arthur’s court, and ill betide me if I return not thence with a warrior who can guard the fountain as well as, or even better than, he who defended it formerly.” “That will be hard to perform,” said the Countess. “Go, however, and make proof of that which thou hast promised.”\n\nLuned set out under the pretence of going to Arthur’s court; but she went back to the mansion where she had left Owain, and she tarried there as long as it might have taken her to travel to the court of King Arthur and back. And at the end of that time she apparelled herself, and went to visit the Countess. And the Countess was much rejoiced when she saw her, and inquired what news she brought from the court. “I bring thee the best of news,” said Luned, “for I have compassed the object of my mission. When wilt thou that I should present to thee the chieftain who has come with me hither?” “Bring him here to visit me to-morrow,” said the Countess, “and I will cause the town to be assembled by that time.”\n\nAnd Luned returned home. And the next day at noon, Owain arrayed himself in a coat and a surcoat, and a mantle of yellow satin, upon which was a broad band of gold lace; and on his feet were high shoes of variegated leather, which were fastened by golden clasps, in the form of lions. And they proceeded to the chamber of the Countess.\n\nRight glad was the Countess of their coming. And she gazed steadfastly upon Owain, and said, “Luned, this knight has not the look of a traveller.” “What harm is there in that, lady?” said Luned. “I am certain,” said the Countess, “that no other man than this chased the soul from the body of my lord.” “So much the better for thee, lady,” said Luned, “for had he not been stronger than thy lord, he could not have deprived him of life. There is no remedy for that which is past, be it as it may.” “Go back to thine abode,” said the Countess, “and I will take counsel.”\n\nThe next day the Countess caused all her subjects to assemble, and showed them that her earldom was left defenceless, and that it could not be protected but with horse and arms, and military skill. “Therefore,” said she, “this is what I offer for your choice: either let one of you take me, or give your consent for me to take a husband from elsewhere, to defend my dominions.”\n\nSo they came to the determination that it was better that she should have permission to marry some one from elsewhere; and thereupon she sent for the bishops and archbishops, to celebrate her nuptials with Owain. And the men of the earldom did Owain homage.\n\nAnd Owain defended the fountain with lance and sword. And this is the manner in which he defended it. Whensoever a knight came there, he overthrew him, and sold him for his full worth. And what he thus gained he divided among his barons and his knights, and no man in the whole world could be more beloved than he was by his subjects. And it was thus for the space of three years.[60]\n\n————\n\n# GAWAIN’S ADVENTURE\n\nIT befell that, as Gawain went forth one day with King Arthur, he perceived him to be very sad and sorrowful. And Gawain was much grieved to see Arthur in his state, and he questioned him, saying, “O my lord, what has befallen thee?” “In sooth, Gawain,” said Arthur, “I am grieved concerning Owain, whom I have lost these three years; and I shall certainly die if the fourth year pass without my seeing him. Now I am sure that it is through the tale which Kynon, the son of Clydno, related, that I have lost Owain.” “There is no need for thee,” said Gawain, “to summon to arms thy whole dominions on this account, for thou thyself, and the men of thy household, will be able to avenge Owain if he be slain or to set him free if he be in prison; and, if alive, to bring him back with thee.” And it was settled according to what Gawain had said.\n\nThen Arthur and the men of his household prepared to go and seek Owain. And Kynon, the son of Clydno, acted as their guide. And Arthur came to the castle where Kynon had been before. And when he came there, the youths were shooting in the same place, and the yellow man was standing hard by. When the yellow man saw Arthur, he greeted him, and invited him to the castle. And Arthur accepted his invitation, and they entered the castle together. And great as was the number of his retinue, their presence was scarcely observed in the castle, so vast was its extent. And the maidens rose up to wait on them. And the service of the maidens appeared to them all to excel any attendance they had ever met with; and even the pages, who had charge of the horses, were no worse served that night than Arthur himself would have been in his own palace.\n\nThe next morning Arthur set out thence, with Kynon for his guide, and came to the place where the black man was. And the stature of the black man was more surprising to Arthur than it had been represented to him. And they came to the top of the wooded steep, and traversed the valley, till they reached the green tree, where they saw the fountain and the bowl and the slab. And upon that Kay came to Arthur, and spoke to him. “My lord,” said he, “I know the meaning of all this, and my request is that thou wilt permit me to throw the water on the slab, and to receive the first adventure that may befall.” And Arthur gave him leave.\n\nThen Kay threw a bowlful of water upon the slab, and immediately there came the thunder, and after the thunder the shower. And such a thunder-storm they had never known before. After the shower had ceased, the sky became clear, and on looking at the tree, they beheld it completely leafless. Then the birds descended upon the tree. And the song of the birds was far sweeter than any strain they had ever heard before. Then they beheld a knight, on a coal-black horse, clothed in black satin, coming rapidly towards them. And Kay met him and encountered him, and it was not long before Kay was overthrown. And the knight withdrew. And Arthur and his host encamped for the night.\n\nAnd when they arose in the morning, they perceived the signal of combat upon the lance of the knight. Then, one by one, all the household of Arthur went forth to combat the knight, until there was not one that was not overthrown by him, except Arthur and Gawain. And Arthur armed himself to encounter the knight. “O my lord,” said Gawain, “permit me to fight with him first.” And Arthur permitted him. And he went forth to meet the knight, having over himself and his horse a satin robe of honor, which had been sent him by the daughter of the Earl of Rhangyr, and in this dress he was not known by any of the host. And they charged each other, and fought all that day until the evening. And neither of them was able to unhorse the other. And so it was the next day; they broke their lances in the shock, but neither of them could obtain the mastery.\n\nAnd the third day they fought with exceeding strong lances. And they were incensed with rage, and fought furiously, even until noon. And they gave each other such a shock that the girths of their horses were broken, so that they fell over their horses’ cruppers to the ground. And they rose up speedily and drew their swords, and resumed the combat. And all they that witnessed their encounter felt assured that they had never before seen two men so valiant or so powerful. And had it been midnight, it would have been light, from the fire that flashed from their weapons. And the knight gave Gawain a blow that turned his helmet from off his face, so that the knight saw that it was Gawain. Then Owain said, “My lord Gawain, I did not know thee for my cousin, owing to the robe of honor that enveloped thee; take my sword and my arms.” Said Gawain, “Thou, Owain, art the victor; take thou my sword.” And with that Arthur saw that they were conversing, and advanced toward them. “My lord Arthur,” said Gawain, “here is Owain who has vanquished me, and will not take my arms.” “My lord,” said Owain, “it is he that has vanquished me, and he will not take my sword.” “Give me your swords,” said Arthur, “and then neither of you has vanquished the other.” Then Owain put his arms around Arthur’s neck, and they embraced. And all the host hurried forward to see Owain, and to embrace him. And there was nigh being a loss of life, so great was the press.\n\nAnd they retired that night, and the next day Arthur prepared to depart. “My lord,” said Owain, “this is not well of thee. For I have been absent from thee these three years, and during all that time, up to this very day, I have been preparing a banquet for thee, knowing that thou wouldst come to seek me. Tarry with me, therefore, until thou and thy attendants have recovered the fatigues of the journey, and have been anointed.”\n\nAnd they all proceeded to the castle of the Countess of the Fountain, and the banquet which had been three years preparing was consumed in three months. Never had they a more delicious or agreeable banquet. And Arthur prepared to depart. Then he sent an embassy to the Countess to beseech her to permit Owain to go with him, for the space of three months, that he might show him to the nobles and the fair dames of the island of Britain. And the Countess gave her consent, although it was very painful to her. So Owain came with Arthur to the island of Britain. And when he was once more amongst his kindred and friends, he remained three years, instead of three months, with them.\n\n# THE ADVENTURE OF THE LION\n\nAnd as Owain one day sat at meat, in the city of Caerleon upon Usk, behold a damsel entered the hall, upon a bay horse,[61] with a curling mane, and covered with foam; and the bridle, and as much as was seen of the saddle, were of gold. And the damsel was arrayed in a dress of yellow satin. And she came up to Owain, and took the ring from off his hand. “Thus,” said she, “shall be treated the deceiver, the traitor, the faithless, the disgraced, and the beardless.” And she turned her horse’s head and departed.\n\nThen his adventure came to Owain’s remembrance, and he was sorrowful. And having finished eating, he went to his own abode, and made preparations that night. And the next day he arose, but did not go to the court, nor did he return to the Countess of the Fountain, but wandered to the distant parts of the earth and to uncultivated mountains. And he remained there until all his apparel was worn out, and his body was wasted away, and his hair was grown long. And he went about with the wild beasts, and fed with them, until they became familiar with him. But at length he became so weak that he could no longer bear them company. Then he descended from the mountains to the valley, and came to a park, that was the fairest in the world, and belonged to a charitable lady.\n\nOne day the lady and her attendants went forth to walk by a lake that was in the middle of the park. And they saw the form of a man, lying as if dead. And they were terrified. Nevertheless they went near him, and touched him, and they saw that there was life in him. And the lady returned to the castle, and took a flask full of precious ointment and gave it to one of her maidens. “Go with this,” said she, “and take with thee yonder horse, and clothing, and place them near the man we saw just now; and anoint him with this balsam near his heart; and if there is life in him, he will revive, through the efficiency of this balsam. Then watch what he will do.”\n\nAnd the maiden departed from her, and went and poured of the balsam upon Owain, and left the horse and the garments hard by, and went a little way off and hid herself to watch him. In a short time, she saw him begin to move; and he rose up, and looked at his person, and became ashamed of the unseemliness of his appearance. Then he perceived the horse and the garments that were near him. And he clothed himself, and with difficulty mounted the horse. Then the damsel discovered herself to him, and saluted him. And he and the maiden proceeded to the castle, and the maiden conducted him to a pleasant chamber, and kindled a fire, and left him.\n\nAnd he stayed at the castle three months, till he was restored to his former guise, and became even more comely than he had ever been before. And Owain rendered signal service to the lady, in a controversy with a powerful neighbor, so that he made ample requital to her for her hospitality; and he took his departure.\n\nAnd as he journeyed he heard a loud yelling in a wood. And it was repeated a second and a third time. And Owain went towards the spot, and beheld a huge craggy mound, in the middle of the wood, on the side of which was a gray rock. And there was a cleft in the rock, and a serpent was within the cleft. And near the rock stood a black lion, and every time the lion sought to go thence the serpent darted towards him to attack him. And Owain unsheathed his sword, and drew near to the rock; and as the serpent sprung out he struck him with his sword and cut him in two. And he dried his sword, and went on his way as before. But behold the lion followed him, and played about him, as though it had been a greyhound that he had reared.\n\nThey proceeded thus throughout the day, until the evening. And when it was time for Owain to take his rest he dismounted, and turned his horse loose in a flat and wooded meadow. And he struck fire, and when the fire was kindled, the lion brought him fuel enough to last for three nights. And the lion disappeared. And presently the lion returned, bearing a fine large roebuck. And he threw it down before Owain, who went towards the fire with it.\n\nAnd Owain took the roebuck, and skinned it, and placed collops of its flesh upon skewers round the fire. The rest of the buck he gave to the lion to devour. While he was so employed, he heard a deep groan near him, and a second, and a third. And the place whence the groans proceeded was a cave in the rock; and Owain went near, and called out to know who it was that groaned so piteously. And a voice answered, “I am Luned, the hand-maiden of the Countess of the Fountain.” “And what dost thou here?” said he. “I am imprisoned,” said she, “on account of the knight who came from Arthur’s court, and married the Countess. And he staid a short time with her, but he afterwards departed for the court of Arthur, and has not returned since. And two of the Countess’s pages traduced him, and called him a deceiver. And because I said I would vouch for it he would come before long and maintain his cause against both of them, they imprisoned me in this cave, and said that I should be put to death, unless he came to deliver me, by a certain day; and that is no further off than to-morrow, and I have no one to send to seek him for me. His name is Owain, the son of Urien.” “And art thou certain that if that knight knew all this, he would come to thy rescue?” “I am most certain of it,” said she.\n\nWhen the collops were cooked, Owain divided them into two parts, between himself and the maiden, and then Owain laid himself down to sleep; and never did sentinel keep stricter watch over his lord than the lion that night over Owain.\n\nAnd the next day there came the two pages with a great troop of attendants to take Luned from her cell, and put her to death. And Owain asked them what charge they had against her. And they told him of the compact that was between them; as the maiden had done the night before. “And,” said they, “Owain has failed her, therefore we are taking her to be burnt.” “Truly,” said Owain, “he is a good knight; and if he knew that the maiden was in such peril, I marvel that he came not to her rescue. But if you will accept me in his stead, I will do battle with you.” “We will,” said the youth.\n\nAnd they attacked Owain, and he was hard beset by them. And with that, the lion came to Owain’s assistance, and they two got the better of the young men. And they said to him, “Chieftain, it was not agreed that we should fight save with thyself alone, and it is harder for us to contend with yonder animal than with thee.” And Owain put the lion in the place where Luned had been imprisoned, and blocked up the door with stones. And he went to fight with the young men as before. But Owain had not his usual strength, and the two youths pressed hard upon him. And the lion roared incessantly at seeing Owain in trouble. And he brust through the wall, until he found a way out, and rushed upon the young men and instantly slew them. So Luned was saved from being burned.\n\nThen Owain returned with Luned to the castle of the Lady of the Fountain. And when he went thence, he took the Countess with him to Arthur’s court, and she was his wife as long as she lived.\n————\n\n# GERAINT, THE SON OF ERBIN\n\nARTHUR was accustomed to hold his court at Caerleon upon Usk. And there he held it seven Easters and five Christmases. And once upon a time he held his court there at Whitsuntide. For Caerleon was the place most easy of access in his dominions, both by sea and by land. And there were assembled nine crowned kings, who were his tributaries, and likewise earls and barons. For they were his invited guests at all the high festivals, unless they were prevented by any great hinderance. And when he was at Caerleon holding his court, thirteen churches were set apart for mass. And thus they were appointed: one church for Arthur and his kings, and his guests; and the second for Guenever and her ladies; and the third for the steward of the household and the suitors; and the fourth for the Franks and the other officers; and the other nine churches were for the nine masters of the household, and chiefly for Gawain, for he, from the eminence of his warlike fame, and from the nobleness of his birth, was the most exalted of the nine. And there was no other arrangement respecting the churches than that which we have here mentioned.\n\nAnd on Whit-Tuesday, as the king sat at the banquet, lo, there entered a tall, fair-headed youth, clad in a coat and surcoat of satin, and a golden-hilted sword about his neck, and low shoes of leather upon his feet. And he came and stood before Arthur. “Hail to thee, lord,” said he. “Heaven prosper thee,” he answered, “and be thou welcome. Dost thou bring any new tidings?” “I do, lord,” he said. “I am one of thy foresters, lord, in the forest of Dean, and my name is Madoc, son of Turgadarn. In the forest I saw a stag, the like of which beheld I never yet.” “What is there about him,” asked Arthur, “that thou never yet didst see his like?” “He is of pure white, lord, and he does not herd with any other animal, through stateliness and pride, so royal is his bearing. And I come to seek thy counsel, lord, and to know thy will concerning him.” “It seems best to me,” said Arthur, “to go and hunt him to-morrow at break of day, and to cause general notice thereof to be given to-night, in all quarters of the court.”\n\n“For Arthur on the Whitsuntide before Held court at old Caerleon upon Usk. There on a day, he sitting high in hall, Before him came a forester of Dean, Wet from the woods, with notice of a hart Taller than all his fellows, milky-white, First seen that day: these things he told the king. Then the good king gave order to let blow His horns for hunting on the morrow morn.” —Enid.\n\nAnd Arryfuerys was Arthur’s chief huntsman, and Arelivri his chief page. And all received notice; and thus it was arranged.\n\nThen Guenever said to Arthur, “Wilt thou permit me, lord, to go to-morrow to see and hear the hunt of the stag of which the young man spoke?” “I will gladly,” said Arthur. And Gawain said to Arthur, “Lord, if it seem well to thee, permit that into whose hunt soever the stag shall come, that one, be he a knight or one on foot, may cut off his head, and give it to whom he pleases, whether to his own lady-love, or to the lady of his friend.” “I grant it gladly,” said Arthur, “and let the steward of the household be chastised, if all things are not ready to-morrow for the chase.”\n\nAnd they passed the night with songs, and diversions, and discourse, and ample entertainment. And when it was time for them all to go to sleep, they went. And when the next day came, they arose. And Arthur called the attendants who guarded his couch. And there were four pages whose names were Cadyrnerth, the son of Gandwy, and Ambreu, the son of Bedwor and Amhar, the son of Arthur and Goreu, the son of Custennin. And these men came to Arthur and saluted him, and arrayed him in his garments. And Arthur wondered that Guenever did not awake, and the attendants wished to awaken her. “Disturb her not,” said Arthur, “for she had rather sleep than go to see the hunting.”\n\nThen Arthur went forth, and he heard two horns sounding, one from near the lodging of the chief huntsman, and the other from near that of the chief page. And the whole assembly of the multitudes came to Arthur, and they took the road to the forest.\n\nAnd after Arthur had gone forth from the palace, Guenever awoke, and called to her maidens, and apparalled herself. “Maidens,” said she, “I had leave last night to go and see the hunt. Go one of you to the stable, and order hither a horse such as a woman may ride.” And one of them went, and she found but two horses in the stable; and Guenever and one of her maidens mounted them, and went through the Usk, and followed the track of the men and the horses. And as they rode thus, they heard a loud and rushing sound; and they looked behind them, and beheld a knight upon a hunter foal of mighty size. And the rider was a fair-haired youth, bare-legged, and of princely mien; and a golden-hilted sword was at his side, and a robe and a surcoat of satin were upon him, and two low shoes of leather upon his feet; and around him was a scarf of blue purple, at each corner of which was a golden apple.\n\n“For Prince Geraint, Late also, wearing neither hunting-dress Nor weapon, save a golden-hilted brand, Came quickly flashing through the shallow ford.” —Enid.\n\nAnd his horse stepped stately, and swift, and proud; and he overtook Guenever, and saluted her. “Heaven prosper thee, Geraint,” said she; “and why didst thou not go with thy lord to hunt?” “Because I knew not when he went,” said he. “I marvel too,” said she, “how he could go, unknown to me. But thou, O young man, art the most agreeable companion I could have in the whole kingdom; and it may be I shall be more amused with the hunting than they; for we shall hear the horns when they sound and we shall hear the dogs when they are let loose and begin to cry.”\n\nSo they went to the edge of the forest, and there they stood. “From this place,” said she, “we shall hear when the dogs are let loose.” And thereupon they heard a loud noise; and they looked towards the spot whence it came, and they beheld a dwarf riding upon a horse, stately and foaming and prancing and strong and spirited. And in the hand of the dwarf was a whip. And near the dwarf they saw a lady upon a beautiful white horse, of steady and stately pace; and she was clothed in a garment of gold brocade. And near her was a knight upon a war-horse of large size, with heavy and bright armor both upon himself and upon his horse. And truly they never before saw a knight, or a horse, or armor, of such remarkable size.\n\n“Geraint,” said Guenever, “knowest thou the name of that tall knight yonder?” “I know him not,” said he, “and the strange armor that he wears prevents my either seeing his face or his features.” “Go, maiden,” said Guenever, “and ask the dwarf who that knight is.” Then the maiden went up to the dwarf; and she inquired of the dwarf who the knight was. “I will not tell thee,” he answered. “Since thou art so churlish,” said she, “I will ask him, himself.” “Thou shalt not ask him, by my faith,” said he. “Wherefore not?” said she. “Because thou art not of honor sufficient to befit thee to speak to my lord.” Then the maiden turned her horse’s head towards the knight, upon which the dwarf struck her with the whip that was in his hand across the face and the eyes, so that the blood flowed forth. And the maiden returned to Guenever, complaining of the hurt she had received. “Very rudely has the dwarf treated thee,” said Geraint, and he put his hand upon the hilt of his sword. But he took counsel with himself, and considered that it would be no vengeance for him to slay the dwarf, and to be attacked unarmed by the armed knight; so he refrained.\n\n“Lady,” said he, “I will follow him, with thy permission, and at last he will come to some inhabited place, where I may have arms, either as a loan or for a pledge, so that I may encounter the knight.” “Go,” said she, “and do not attack him until thou hast good arms; and I shall be very anxious concerning thee, until I hear tidings of thee.” “If I am alive,” said he, “thou shalt hear tidings of me by to-morrow afternoon;” and with that he departed.\n\nAnd the road they took was below the palace of Caerleon, and across the ford of the Usk; and they went along a fair and even and lofty ridge of ground, until they came to a town, and at the extremity of the town they saw a fortress and a castle. And as the knight passed through the town all the people arose and saluted him, and bade him welcome. And when Geraint came into the town, he looked at every house to see if he knew any of those whom he saw. But he knew none, and none knew him, to do him the kindness to let him have arms, either as a loan or for a pledge. And every house he saw was full of men, and arms, and horses. And they were polishing shields, and burnishing swords, and washing armor, and shoeing horses. And the knight and the lady and the dwarf rode up to the castle, that was in the town, and every one was glad in the castle. And from the battlements and the gates they risked their necks, through their eagerness to greet them, and to show their joy.\n\nGeraint stood there to see whether the knight would remain in the castle; and when he was certain that he would do so, he looked around him. And at a little distance from the town he saw an old palace in ruins, wherein was a hall that was falling to decay.\n\n“And high above a piece of turret-stair, Worn by the feet that now were silent, wound Bare to the sun.” —Enid.\n\nAnd as he knew not any one in the town, he went towards the old palace. And when he came near to the palace, he saw a hoary-headed man, standing by it, in tattered garments. And Geraint gazed steadfastly upon him. Then the hoary-headed man said to him, “Young man, wherefore art thou thoughtful?” “I am thoughtful,” said he, “because I know not where to pass the night.” “Wilt thou come forward this way, chieftain,” said he, “and thou shalt have of the best that can be procured for thee.” So Geraint went forward. And the hoary-headed man led the way into the hall. And in the hall he dismounted, and he left there his horse. Then he went on to the upper chamber with the hoary-headed man. And in the chamber he beheld an old woman, sitting on a cushion, with old, worn-out garments upon her; yet it seemed to him that she must have been comely when in the bloom of youth. And beside her was a maiden, upon whom were a vest and a veil that were old and beginning to be worn out. And truly he never saw a maiden more full of comeliness and grace and beauty than she. And the hoary-headed man said to the maiden, “There is no attendant for the horse of this youth but thyself.” “I will render the best service I am able,” said she, “both to him and to his horse.” And the maiden disarrayed the youth, and then she furnished his horse with straw and corn; and then she returned to the chamber. And the hoary-headed man said to the maiden, “Go to the town and bring hither the best that thou canst find, both of food and of liquor.” “I will gladly, lord,” said she. And to the town went the maiden. And they conversed together while the maiden was at the town. And, behold, the maiden came back, and a youth with her, bearing on his back a costrel full of good purchased mead, and a quarter of a young bullock. And in the hands of the maiden was a quantity of white bread, and she had some manchet bread in her veil, and she came into the chamber. “I would not obtain better than this,” said she, “nor with better should I have been trusted.” “It is good enough,” said Geraint. And they caused the meat to be boiled; and when their food was ready, they sat down. And it was in this wise. Geraint sat between the hoary-headed man and his wife, and the maiden served them. And they ate and drank.\n\nAnd when they had finished eating, Geraint talked with the hoary-headed man, and he asked him in the first place to whom belonged the palace that he was in. “Truly,” said he, “it was I that built it, and to me also belonged the city and the castle which thou sawest.” “Alas!” said Geraint, “how is it that thou hast lost them now?” “I lost a great earldom as well as these,” said he, “and this is how I lost them. I had a nephew, the son of my brother, and I took care of his possessions; but he was impatient to enter upon them, so he made war upon me, and wrested from me not only his own, but also my estates, except this castle.” “Good sir,” said Geraint, “wilt thou tell me wherefore came the knight and the lady and the dwarf just now into the town, and what is the preparation which I saw, and the putting of arms in order?” “I will do so,” said he. “The preparations are for the game that is to be held to-morrow by the young earl, which will be on this wise. In the midst of a meadow which is here, two forks will be set up, and upon the two forks a silver rod, and upon the silver rod a sparrow-hawk, and for the sparrow-hawk there will be a tournament. And to the tournament will go all the array thou didst see in the city, of men and of horses and of arms. And with each man will go the lady he loves best; and no man can joust for the sparrow-hawk, except the lady he loves best be with him. And the knight that thou sawest has gained the sparrow-hawk these two years; and if he gains it the third year, he will be called the Knight of the Sparrow-hawk from that time forth.” “Sir,” said Geraint, “what is thy counsel to me concerning this knight, on account of the insult which the maiden of Guenever received from the dwarf?” And Geraint told the hoary-headed man what the insult was that the maiden had received. “It is not easy to counsel thee, inasmuch as thou hast neither dame nor maiden belonging to thee, for whom thou canst joust. Yet I have arms here, which thou couldst have, and there is my horse also, if he seem to thee better than thine own.” “Ah, sir,” said he, “Heaven reward thee! But my own horse to which I am accustomed, together with thine arms, will suffice me. And if, when the appointed time shall come to-morrow thou wilt permit me, sir, to challenge for yonder maiden that is thy daughter, I will engage, if I escape from the tournament, to love the maiden as long as I live.” “Gladly will I permit thee,” said the hoary-headed man; “and since thou dost thus resolve, it is necessary that thy horse and arms should be ready to-morrow at break of day. For then the Knight of the Sparrow-hawk will make proclamation, and ask the lady he loves best to take the sparrow-hawk; and if any deny it to her, by force will he defend her claim. And therefore,” said the hoary-headed man, “it is needful for thee to be there at daybreak, and we three will be with thee.” And thus was it settled.\n\nAnd at night they went to sleep. And before the dawn they arose and arrayed themselves; and by the time that it was day, they were all four in the meadow. And there was the Knight of the Sparrow-hawk making the proclamation, and asking his lady-love to take the sparrow-hawk. “Take it not,” said Geraint, “for here is a maiden who is fairer, and more noble, and more comely, and who has a better claim to it than thou.” Then said the knight, “If thou maintainest the sparrow-hawk to be due to her, come forward and do battle with me.” And Geraint went forward to the top of the meadow, having upon himself and upon his horse armor which was heavy and rusty, and of uncouth shape. Then they encountered each other, and they broke a set of lances; and they broke a second set, and a third. And when the earl and his company saw the Knight of the Sparrow-hawk gaining the mastery, there was shouting and joy and mirth amongst them; and the hoary-headed man and his wife and his daughter were sorrowful. And the hoary-headed man served Geraint with lances as often as he broke them, and the dwarf served the Knight of the Sparrow-hawk. Then the hoary-headed man said to Geraint, “O chieftain, since no other will hold with thee, behold, here is the lance which was in my hand on the day when I received the honor of knighthood, and from that time to this I never broke it, and it has an excellent point.” Then Geraint took the lance, thanking the hoary-headed man. And thereupon the dwarf also brought a lance to his lord. “Behold, here is a lance for thee, not less good than his,” said the dwarf. “And bethink thee that no knight ever withstood thee so long as this one has done.” “I declare to Heaven,” said Geraint, “that unless death takes me quickly hence, he shall fare never the better for thy service.” And Geraint pricked his horse towards him from afar, and, warning him, he rushed upon him, and gave him a blow so severe, and furious, and fierce, upon the face of his shield, that he cleft it in two, and broke his armor, and burst his girths, so that both he and his saddle were borne to the ground over the horse’s crupper. And Geraint dismounted quickly. And he was wroth, and he drew his sword, and rushed fiercely upon him. Then the knight also arose, and drew his sword against Geraint. And they fought on foot with their swords until their arms struck sparks of fire like stars from one another; and thus they continued fighting until the blood and sweat obscured the light from their eyes. At length Geraint called to him all his strength, and struck the knight upon the crown of his head, so that he broke all his head-armor, and cut through all the flesh and the skin, even to the skull, until he wounded the bone.\n\nThen the knight fell upon his knees, and cast his sword from his hand, and besought mercy from Geraint. “Of a truth,” said he, “I relinquish my overdaring and my pride, and crave thy mercy; and unless I have time to commit myself to Heaven for my sins, and to talk with a priest, thy mercy will avail me little.” “I will grant thee grace upon this condition,” said Geraint, “that thou go to Guenever, the wife of Arthur, to do her satisfaction for the insult which her maiden received from thy dwarf. Dismount not from the time thou goest hence until thou comest into the presence of Guenever, to make her what atonement shall be adjudged at the court of Arthur.” “This will I do gladly; and who art thou?” “I am Geraint, the son of Erbin; and declare thou also who thou art.” “I am Edeyrn, the son of Nudd.” Then he threw himself upon his horse, and went forward to Arthur’s court; and the lady he loved best went before him, and the dwarf, with much lamentation.\n\nThen came the young earl and his hosts to Geraint, and saluted him, and bade him to his castle. “I may not go,” said Geraint; “but where I was last night, there will I be to-night also.” “Since thou wilt none of my inviting, thou shalt have abundance of all that I can command for thee; and I will order ointment for thee, to recover thee from thy fatigues, and from the weariness that is upon thee.” “Heaven reward thee,” said Geraint, “and I will go to my lodging.” And thus went Geraint and Earl Ynywl, and his wife and his daughter. And when they reached the old mansion, the household servants and attendants of the young earl had arrived, and had arranged all the apartments, dressing them with straw and with fire; and in a short time the ointment was ready, and Geraint came there, and they washed his head. Then came the young earl, with forty honorable knights from among his attendants, and those who were bidden to the tournament. And Geraint came from the anointing. And the earl asked him to go to the hall to eat. “Where is the Earl Ynywl,” said Geraint, “and his wife and his daughter?” “They are in the chamber yonder,” said the earl’s chamberlain, “arraying themselves in garments which the earl has caused to be brought for them.” “Let not the damsel array herself,” said he, “except in her vest and her veil, until she come to the court of Arthur, to be clad by Guenever in such garments as she may choose.” So the maiden did not array herself.\n\nThen they all entered the hall, and they washed, and sat down to meat. And thus were they seated. On one side of Geraint sat the young earl, and Earl Ynywl beyond him, and on the other side of Geraint was the maiden and her mother. And after these all sat according to their precedence in honor. And they ate. And they were served abundantly, and they received a profusion of divers kinds of gifts. Then they conversed together. And the young earl invited Geraint to visit him next day. “I will not, by Heaven,” said Geraint. “To the court of Arthur will I go with this maiden to-morrow. And it is enough for me, as long as Earl Ynywl is in poverty and trouble; and I go chiefly to seek to add to his maintenance.” “Ah, chieftain,” said the young earl, “it is not by my fault that Earl Ynywl is without his possessions.” “By my faith,” said Geraint, “he shall not remain without them, unless death quickly takes me hence.” “O chieftain,” said he, “with regard to the disagreement between me and Ynywl, I will gladly abide by thy counsel, and agree to what thou mayest judge right between us.” “I but ask thee,” said Geraint, “to restore to him what is his, and what he should have received from the time he lost his possessions even until this day.” “That will I do, gladly, for thee,” answered he. “Then,” said Geraint, “whosoever is here who owes homage to Ynywl, let him come forward, and perform it on the spot.” And all the men did so; and by that treaty they abided. And his castle and his town, and all his possessions, were restored to Ynywl. And he received back all that he had lost, even to the smallest jewel.\n\nThen spoke Earl Ynywl to Geraint. “Chieftain,” said he, “behold the maiden for whom thou didst challenge at the tournament; I bestow her upon thee.” “She shall go with me,” said Geraint, “to the court of Arthur, and Arthur and Guenever, they shall dispose of her as they will.” And the next day they proceeded to Arthur’s court. So far concerning Geraint.\n\n————\n\nNOW this is how Arthur hunted the stag. The men and the dogs were divided into hunting-parties, and the dogs were let loose upon the stag. And the last dog that was let loose was the favorite dog of Arthur; Cavall was his name. And he left all the other dogs behind him and turned the stag. And at the second turn the stag came toward the hunting-party of Arthur. And Arthur set upon him; and before he could be slain by any other, Arthur cut off his head. Then they sounded the death-horn for slaying and they all gathered round.\n\nThen came Kadyriath to Arthur and spoke to him. “Lord,” said he, “behold, yonder is Guenever, and none with her save only one maiden.” “Command Gildas, the son of Caw, and all the scholars of the court,” said Arthur, “to attend Guenever to the palace.” And they did so.\n\nThen they all set forth, holding converse together concerning the head of the stag, to whom it should be given. One wished that it should be given to the lady best beloved by him, and another to the lady whom he loved best. And so they came to the palace. And when Arthur and Guenever heard them disputing about the head of the stag, Guenever said to Arthur: “My lord, this is my counsel concerning the stag’s head; let it not be given away until Geraint, the son of Erbin, shall return from the errand he is upon.” And Guenever told Arthur what that errand was. “Right gladly shall it be so,” said Arthur. And Guenever caused a watch to be set upon the ramparts for Geraint’s coming. And after midday they beheld an unshapely little man upon a horse, and after him a dame or a damsel, also on horseback, and after her a knight of large stature, bowed down, and hanging his head low and sorrowfully, and clad in broken and worthless armor.\n\nAnd before they came near to the gate one of the watch went to Guenever, and told her what kind of people they saw, and what aspect they bore. “I know not who they are,” said he, “But I know,” said Guenever; “this is the knight whom Geraint pursued, and methinks that he comes not here by his own free will. But Geraint has overtaken him, and avenged the insult to the maiden to the uttermost.” And thereupon, behold, a porter came to the spot where Guenever was. “Lady,” said he, “at the gate there is a knight, and I saw never a man of so pitiful an aspect to look upon as he. Miserable and broken is the armor that he wears, and the hue of blood is more conspicuous upon it than its own color.” “Knowest thou his name?” said she. “I do,” said he; “he tells me that he is Edeyrn, the son of Nudd.” Then she replied, “I know him not.”\n\nSo Guenever went to the gate to meet him and he entered. And Guenever was sorry when she saw the condition he was in, even though he was accompanied by the churlish dwarf. Then Edeyrn saluted Guenever. “Heaven protect thee,” said she. “Lady,” said he, “Geraint, the son of Erbin, thy best and most valiant servant, greets thee.” “Did he meet with thee?” she asked. “Yes,” said he, “and it was not to my advantage; and that was not his fault, but mine, lady. And Geraint greets thee well; and in greeting thee he compelled me to come hither to do thy pleasure for the insult which thy maiden received from the dwarf.” “Now where did he overtake thee?” “At the place where we were jousting and contending for the sparrow-hawk, in the town which is now called Cardiff. And it was for the avouchment of the love of the maiden, the daughter of Earl Ynywl, that Geraint jousted at the tournament. And thereupon we encountered each other, and he left me, lady, as thou seest.” “Sir,” said she, “when thinkest thou that Geraint will be here?” “To-morrow, lady, I think he will be here with the maiden.”\n\nThen Arthur came to them. And he saluted Arthur, and Arthur gazed a long time upon him and was amazed to see him thus. And thinking that he knew him, he inquired of him, “Art thou Edeyrn, the son of Nudd?” “I am, lord,” said he, “and I have met with much trouble and received wounds unsupportable.” Then he told Arthur all his adventure. “Well,” said Arthur, “from what I hear it behooves Guenever to be merciful towards thee.” “The mercy which thou desirest, lord,” said she, “will I grant to him, since it is as insulting to thee that an insult should be offered to me as to thyself.” “Thus will it be best to do,” said Arthur; “let this man have medical care until it be known whether he may live. And if he live, he shall do such satisfaction as shall be judged best by the men of the court. And if he die, too much will be the death of such a youth as Edeyrn for an insult to a maiden.” “This pleases me,” said Guenever. And Arthur caused Morgan Tud to be called to him. He was the chief physician. “Take with thee Edeyrn, the son of Nudd, and cause a chamber to be prepared for him, and let him have the aid of medicine as thou wouldst do unto myself, if I were wounded; and let none into his chamber to molest him, but thyself and thy disciples, to administer to him remedies.” “I will do so, gladly, lord,” said Morgan Tud. Then said the steward of the household, “Whither is it right, lord, to order the maiden?” “To Guenever and her handmaidens,” said he. And the steward of the household so ordered her.\n\n“And rising up, he rode to Arthur’s court, And there the queen forgave him easily. And being young, he changed himself, and grew To hate the sin that seem’d so like his own Of Modred, Arthur’s nephew, and fell at last In the great battle fighting for the king.” —Enid.\n\nThe next day came Geraint towards the court; and there was a watch set on the ramparts by Guenever, lest he should arrive unawares. And one of the watch came to Guenever. “Lady,” said he, “methinks that I see Geraint, and a maiden with him. He is on horseback, but he has his walking gear upon him, and the maiden appears to be in white, seeming to be clad in a garment of linen.” “Assemble all the women,” said Guenever, “and come to meet Geraint, to welcome him, and wish him joy.” And Guenever went to meet Geraint and the maiden. And when Geraint came to the place where Guenever was, he saluted her. “Heaven prosper thee,” said she, “and welcome to thee.” “Lady,” said he, “I earnestly desired to obtain thee satisfaction, according to thy will; and, behold, here is the maiden through whom thou hadst thy revenge.” “Verily,” said Guenever, “the welcome of Heaven be unto her; and it is fitting that we should receive her joyfully.” Then they went in and dismounted. And Geraint came to where Arthur was, and saluted him. “Heaven protect thee,” said Arthur, “and the welcome of Heaven be unto thee. And inasmuch as thou hast vanquished Edeyrn, the son of Nudd, thou hast had a prosperous career.” “Not upon me be the blame,” said Geraint; “it was through the arrogance of Edeyrn, the son of Nudd, himself, that we were not friends.” “Now,” said Arthur, “where is the maiden for whom I heard thou didst give challenge?” “She is gone with Guenever to her chamber.” Then went Arthur to see the maiden. And Arthur, and all his companions, and his whole court, were glad concerning the maiden. And certain were they all, that, had her array been suitable to her beauty, they had never seen a maid fairer than she. And Arthur gave away the maiden to Geraint. And the usual bond made between two persons was made between Geraint and the maiden, and the choicest of all Guenever’s apparel was given to the maiden; and thus arrayed, she appeared comely and graceful to all who beheld her. And that day and the night were spent in abundance of minstrelsy, and ample gifts of liquor, and a multitude of games. And when it was time for them to go to sleep they went. And in the chamber where the couch of Arthur and Guenever was, the couch of Geraint and Enid was prepared. And from that time she became his wife. And the next day Arthur satisfied all the claimants upon Geraint with bountiful gifts. And the maiden took up her abode in the palace, and she had many companions, both men and women, and there was no maiden more esteemed than she in the island of Britain.\n\nThen spake Guenever. “Rightly did I judge,” said she, “concerning the head of the stag, that it should not be given to any until Geraint’s return; and behold, here is a fit occasion for bestowing it. Let it be given to Enid, the daughter of Ynywl, the most illustrious maiden. And I do not believe that any will begrudge it her, for between her and every one here there exists nothing but love and friendship.” Much applauded was this by them all, and by Arthur also. And the head of the stag was given to Enid. And thereupon her fame increased, and her friends became more in number than before. And Geraint from that time forth loved the hunt, and the tournament, and hard encounters; and he came victorious from them all. And a year, and a second, and a third, he proceeded thus, until his fame had flown over the face of the kingdom.\n\nAnd, once upon a time, Arthur was holding his court at Caerleon upon Usk; and behold, there came to him ambassadors, wise and prudent, full of knowledge and eloquent of speech, and they saluted Arthur. “Heaven prosper you!” said Arthur; “and whence do you come?” “We come, lord,” said they, “from Cornwall; and we are ambassadors from Erbin, the son of Custennin, thy uncle, and our mission is unto thee. And he greets thee well, as an uncle should greet his nephew, and as a vassal should greet his lord. And he represents unto thee that he waxes heavy and feeble, and is advancing in years. And the neighboring chiefs, knowing this, grow insolent towards him, and covet his land and possessions. And he earnestly beseeches thee, lord, to permit Geraint, his son, to return to him, to protect his possessions, and to become acquainted with his boundaries. And unto him he represents that it were better for him to spend the flower of his youth and the prime of his age in preserving his own boundaries, than in tournaments which are productive of no profit, although he obtains glory in them.”\n\n“Well,” said Arthur, “go and divest yourselves of your accoutrements, and take food, and refresh yourselves after your fatigues; and before you go from hence you shall have an answer.” And they went to eat. And Arthur considered that it would go hard with him to let Geraint depart from him, and from his court; neither did he think it fair that his cousin should be restrained from going to protect his dominions and his boundaries, seeing that his father was unable to do so. No less was the grief and regret of Guenever, and all her women, and all her damsels, through fear that the maiden would leave them. And that day and that night were spent in abundance of feasting. And Arthur told Geraint the cause of the mission, and of the coming of the ambassadors to him out of Cornwall. “Truly,” said Geraint, “be it to my advantage or disadvantage, lord, I will do according to thy will concerning this embassy.” “Behold,” said Arthur, “though it grieves me to part with thee, it is my counsel that thou go to dwell in thine own dominions, and to defend thy boundaries, and take with thee to accompany thee as many as thou wilt of those thou lovest best among my faithful ones, and among thy friends, and among thy companions in arms.” “Heaven reward thee! and this will I do,” said Geraint. “What discourse,” said Guenever, “do I hear between you? Is it of those who are to conduct Geraint to his country?” “It is,” said Arthur. “Then is it needful for me to consider,” said she, “concerning companions and a provision for the lady that is with me.” “Thou wilt do well,” said Arthur.\n\nAnd that night they went to sleep. And the next day the ambassadors were permitted to depart, and they were told that Geraint should follow them. And on the third day Geraint set forth, and many went with him—Gawain, the son of Gwyar, and Riogoned, the son of the king of Ireland, and Ondyaw, the son of the Duke of Burgundy, Gwilim, the son of the ruler of the Franks, Howel, the son of the Earl of Brittany, Perceval, the son of Evrawk, Gwyr, a judge in the court of Arthur, Bedwyr, the son of Bedrawd, Kai, the son of Kyner, Odyar, the Frank, and Ederyn, the son of Nudd. Said Geraint, “I think I shall have enough of knighthood with me.” And they set forth. And never was there seen a fairer host journeying towards the Severn. And on the other side of the Severn were the nobles of Erbin, the son of Custennin, and his foster-father at their head, to welcome Geraint with gladness; and many of the women of the court, with his mother, came to receive Enid, the daughter of Ynywl, his wife. And there was great rejoicing and gladness throughout the whole court, and through all the country, concerning Geraint, because of the greatness of their love to him, and of the greatness of the fame which he had gained since he went from amongst them, and because he was come to take possession of his dominions, and to preserve his boundaries. And they came to the court. And in the court they had ample entertainment, and a multitude of gifts, and abundance of liquor, and a sufficiency of service, and a variety of games. And to do honor to Geraint, all the chief men of the country were invited that night to visit him. And they passed that day and that night in the utmost enjoyment. And at dawn next day Erbin arose and summoned to him Geraint, and the noble persons who had borne him company. And he said to Geraint: “I am a feeble and an aged man, and whilst I was able to maintain the dominion for thee and for myself, I did so. But thou art young, and in the flower of thy vigor and of thy youth. Henceforth do thou preserve thy possessions.” “Truly,” said Geraint, “with my consent thou shalt not give the power over thy dominions at this time into my hands, and thou shalt not take me from Arthur’s court.” “Into thy hands will I give them,” said Erbin, “and this day also shalt thou receive the homage of thy subjects.”\n\nThen said Gawain, “It were better for thee to satisfy those who have boons to ask, to-day, and to-morrow thou canst receive the homage of thy dominions.” So all that had boons to ask were summoned into one place. And Kadyriath came to them to know what were their requests. And every one asked that which he desired. And the followers of Arthur began to make gifts, and immediately the men of Cornwall came, and gave also. And they were not long in giving, so eager was every one to bestow gifts, and of those who came to ask gifts, none departed unsatisfied. And that day and that night were spent in the utmost enjoyment.\n\nAnd the next day at dawn, Erbin desired Geraint to send messengers to the men to ask them whether it was displeasing to them that he should come to receive their homage, and whether they had anything to object to him. Then Geraint sent ambassadors to the men of Cornwall to ask them this. And they all said that it would be the fulness of joy and honor to them for Geraint to come and receive their homage. So he received the homage of such as were there. And the day after the followers of Arthur intended to go away. “It is too soon for you to go away yet,” said he; “stay with me until I have finished receiving the homage of my chief men, who have agreed to come to me.” And they remained with him until he had done so. Then they set forth towards the court of Arthur. And Geraint went to bear them company, and Enid also, as far as Diganwy; there they parted. And Ondyaw, the son of the Duke of Burgundy, said to Geraint, “Go, now, and visit the uttermost parts of thy dominions, and see well to the boundaries of thy territories; and if thou hast any trouble respecting them, send unto thy companions.” “Heaven reward thee!” said Geraint; “and this will I do.” And Geraint journeyed to the uttermost parts of his dominions. And experienced guides, and the chief men of his country, went with him. And the furthermost point that they showed him he kept possession of.\n\n————\n\nGERAINT, as he had been used to do when he was at Arthur’s court, frequented tournaments. And he became acquainted with valiant and mighty men, until he had gained as much fame there as he had formerly done elsewhere. And he enriched his court, and his companions, and his nobles, with the best horses and the best arms, and with the best and most valuable jewels, and he ceased not until his fame had flown over the face of the whole kingdom.\n\n“Before Geraint, the scourge of the enemy, I saw steeds white with foam, And after the shout of battle a fearful torrent.” —Hen.\n\nWhen he knew that it was thus, he began to love ease and pleasure, for there was no one who was worth his opposing. And he loved his wife, and liked to continue in the palace with minstrelsy and diversions. So he began to shut himself up in the chamber of his wife, and he took no delight in anything besides, insomuch that he gave up the friendship of his nobles, together with his hunting and his amusements, and lost the hearts of all the host in his court. And there was murmuring and scoffing concerning him among the inhabitants of the palace, on account of his relinquishing so completely their companionship for the love of his wife.\n\n“They Began to scoff and jeer and babble of him As of a prince whose manhood was all gone, And molten down in mere uxoriousness.”\n\nThese tidings came to Erbin. And when Erbin had heard these things, he spoke unto Enid, and inquired of her whether it was she that had caused Geraint to act thus, and to forsake his people and his hosts. “Not I, by my confession unto Heaven,” said she; “there is nothing more hateful unto me than this.” And she knew not what she should do, for, although it was hard for her to own this to Geraint, yet was it not more easy for her to listen to what she heard, without warning Geraint concerning it. And she was very sorrowful.\n\nOne morning in the summer-time they were upon their couch, and Geraint lay upon the edge of it. And Enid was without sleep in the apartment, which had windows of glass;[62] and the sun shone upon the couch. And the clothes had slipped from off his arms and his breast, and he was asleep. Then she gazed upon the marvellous beauty of his appearance, and she said, “Alas! and am I the cause that these arms and this breast have lost their glory, and the warlike fame which they once so richly enjoyed!” As she said this the tears dropped from her eyes, and they fell upon his breast. And the tears she shed and the words she had spoken, awoke him. And another thing contributed to awaken him, and that was the idea that it was not in thinking of him that she spoke thus, but that it was because she loved some other man more than him, and that she wished for other society. Thereupon Geraint was troubled in his mind, and he called his squire; and when he came to him, “Go quickly,” said he, “and prepare my horse and my arms, and make them ready. And do thou rise,” said he to Enid, “and apparel thyself; and cause thy horse to be accoutred, and clothe thee in the worst riding-dress that thou hast in thy possession. And evil betide me,” said he, “if thou returnest here until thou knowest whether I have lost my strength so completely as thou didst say. And if it be so, it will then be easy for thee to seek the society thou didst wish for of him of whom thou wast thinking.” So she arose, and clothed herself in her meanest garments. “I know nothing, lord,” said she, “of thy meaning.” “Neither wilt thou know at this time,” said he.\n\nThen Geraint went to see Erbin. “Sir,” said he, “I am going upon a quest, and I am not certain when I may come back. Take heed, therefore, unto thy possessions until my return.” “I will do so,” said he; “but it is strange to me that thou shouldst go so suddenly. And who will proceed with thee, since thou art not strong enough to traverse the land of Loegyr alone?” “But one person only will go with me.” “Heaven counsel thee, my son,” said Erbin, “and may many attach themselves to thee in Loegyr.” Then went Geraint to the place where his horse was, and it was equipped with foreign armor, heavy and shining. And he desired Enid to mount her horse, and to ride forward, and to keep a long way before him. “And whatever thou mayst see, and whatever thou mayst hear concerning me,” said he, “do thou not turn back. And unless I speak unto thee, say not thou one word, either.” So they set forward. And he did not choose the pleasantest and most frequented road, but that which was the wildest and most beset by thieves and robbers and venomous animals.\n\nAnd they came to a high road, which they followed till they saw a vast forest; and they saw four armed horsemen come forth from the forest. When the armed men saw them, they said one to another. “Here is a good occasion for us to capture two horses and armor, and a lady likewise; for this we shall have no difficulty in doing against yonder single knight who hangs his head so pensively and heavily.” Enid heard this discourse, and she knew not what she should do through fear of Geraint, who had told her to be silent. “The vengeance of Heaven be upon me,” said she, “if I would not rather receive my death from his hand than from the hand of any other; and though he should slay me, yet will I speak to him, lest I should have the misery to witness his death.” So she waited for Geraint until he came near to her. “Lord,” said she, “didst thou hear the words of those men concerning thee?” Then he lifted up his eyes, and looked at her angrily. “Thou hadst only,” said he, “to hold thy peace as I bade thee. I wish but for silence, and not for warning. And though thou shouldst desire to see my defeat and my death by the hands of those men, yet do I feel no dread.” Then the foremost of them couched his lance, and rushed upon Geraint. And he received him, and that not feebly. But he let the thrust go by him, while he struck the horseman upon the centre of his shield, in such a manner that his shield was split, and his armor broken, so that a cubit’s length of the shaft of Geraint’s lance passed through his body, and sent him to the earth, the length of the lance over his horse’s crupper. Then the second horseman attacked him furiously, being wroth at the death of his companion. But with one thrust Geraint overthrew him also, and killed him as he had done the other. Then the third set upon him, and he killed him in like manner. And thus also he slew the fourth. Sad and sorrowful was the maiden as she saw all this. Geraint dismounted his horse, and took the arms of the men he had slain, and placed them upon their saddles, and tied together the reins of their horses; and he mounted his horse again. “Behold what thou must do,” said he; “take the four horses and drive them before thee, and proceed forward as I bade thee just now. And say not one word unto me, unless I speak first unto thee. And I declare unto Heaven,” said he, “if thou doest not thus, it will be to thy cost.” “I will do as far as I can, lord,” said she, “according to thy desire.”\n\nSo the maiden went forward, keeping in advance of Geraint, as he had desired her; and it grieved him as much as his wrath would permit, to see a maiden so illustrious as she having so much trouble with the care of the horses. Then they reached a wood, and it was both deep and vast, and in the wood night overtook them. “Ah, maiden,” said he, “it is vain to attempt proceeding forward.” “Well, lord,” said she, “whatever thou wishest, we will do.” “It will be best for us,” he answered, “to rest and wait for the day, in order to pursue our journey.” “That we will, gladly,” said she. And they did so. Having dismounted himself, he took her down from her horse. “I cannot by any means refrain from sleep, through weariness,” said he; “do thou therefore watch the horses, and sleep not.” “I will, lord,” said she. Then he went to sleep in his armor, and thus passed the night, which was not long at that season. And when she saw the dawn of day appear, she looked around her to see if he were waking, and thereupon he woke. Then he arose, and said unto her, “Take the horses and ride on, and keep straight on as thou didst yesterday.” And they left the wood, and they came to an open country, with meadows on one hand, and mowers mowing the meadows. And there was a river before them, and the horses bent down and drank of the water. And they went up out of the river by a lofty steep; and there they met a slender stripling with a satchel about his neck, and they saw that there was something in the satchel, but they knew not what it was. And he had a small blue pitcher in his hand, and a bowl on the mouth of the pitcher. And the youth saluted Geraint. “Heaven prosper thee!” said Geraint; “and whence dost thou come?” “I come,” said he, “from the city that lies before thee. My lord,” he added, “will it be displeasing to thee if I ask whence thou comest also?” “By no means; through yonder wood did I come.” “Thou camest not through the wood to-day.” “No,” he replied, “we were in the wood last night.” “I warrant,” said the youth, “that thy condition there last night was not the most pleasant, and that thou hadst neither meat nor drink.” “No, by my faith,” said he. “Wilt thou follow my counsel,” said the youth, “and take thy meal from me?” “What sort of meal?” he inquired. “The breakfast which is sent for yonder mowers, nothing less than bread and meat and wine, and if thou wilt, sir, they shall have none of it.” “I will,” said he, “and Heaven reward thee for it.”\n\nSo Geraint alighted, and the youth took the maiden from off her horse. Then they washed, and took their repast. And the youth cut the bread in slices, and gave them drink, and served them withal. And when they had finished, the youth arose and said to Geraint, “My lord, with thy permission, I will now go and fetch some food for the mowers.” “Go first to the town,” said Geraint, “and take a lodging for me in the best place that thou knowest, and the most commodious one for the horses; and take thou whichever horse and arms thou choosest, in payment for thy service and thy gift.” “Heaven reward thee, lord!” said the youth; “and this would be ample to repay services much greater than those I have rendered unto thee.” And to the town went the youth, and he took the best and the most pleasant lodgings that he knew; and after that he went to the palace, having the horse and armor with him, and proceeded to the place where the earl was, and told him all his adventure. “I go now, lord,” said he, “to meet the knight, and to conduct him to his lodging.” “Go, gladly,” said the earl; “and right joyfully shall he be received here, if he so come.” And the youth went to meet Geraint, and told him that he would be received gladly by the earl in his own palace; but he would go only to his lodgings. And he had a goodly chamber, in which was plenty of straw and drapery, and a spacious and commodious place he had for the horses; and the youth prepared for them plenty of provender. After they had disarrayed themselves, Geraint spoke thus to Enid: “Go,” said he, “to the other side of the chamber, and come not to this side of the house; and thou mayst call to thee the woman of the house, if thou wilt.” “I will do, lord,” said she, “as thou sayest.” Thereupon the man of the house came to Geraint and welcomed him. And after they had eaten and drank, Geraint went to sleep, and so did Enid also.\n\nIn the evening, behold, the earl came to visit Geraint, and his twelve honorable knights with him. And Geraint rose up and welcomed him. Then they all sat down according to their precedence in honor. And the earl conversed with Geraint, and inquired of him the object of his journey. “I have none,” he replied, “but to seek adventures and to follow mine own inclination.” Then the earl cast his eye upon Enid, and he looked at her steadfastly. And he thought he had never seen a maiden fairer or more comely than she. And he set all his thoughts and his affections upon her. Then he asked of Geraint, “Have I thy permission to go and converse with yonder maiden, for I see that she is apart from thee?” “Thou hast it gladly,” said he. So the earl went to the place where the maiden was, and spake with her. “Ah! maiden,” said he, “it cannot be pleasant to thee to journey with yonder man.” “It is not unpleasant to me,” said she. “Thou hast neither youths nor maidens to serve thee,” said he. “Truly,” she replied, “it is more pleasant for me to follow yonder man, than to be served by youths and maidens.” “I will give thee good counsel,” said he: “all my earldom will I place in thy possession, if thou wilt dwell with me.”\n\n“Enid, the pilot star of my lone life, Enid, my early and my only love.” —Enid.\n\n“That will I not, by Heaven,” she said; “yonder man was the first to whom my faith was ever pledged; and shall I prove inconstant to him?” “Thou art in the wrong,” said the earl; “if I slay the man yonder, I can keep thee with me as long as I choose; and when thou no longer pleasest me, I can turn thee away. But if thou goest with me by thy own good-will, I protest that our union shall continue as long as I remain alive.” Then she pondered those words of his, and she considered that it was advisable to encourage him in his request. “Behold then, chieftain, this is most expedient for thee to do to save me from all reproach; come here to-morrow and take me away as though I knew nothing thereof.” “I will do so,” said he. So he arose and took his leave, and went forth with his attendants. And she told not then to Geraint any of the conversation which she had had with the earl, lest it should rouse his anger, and cause him uneasiness and care.\n\nAnd at the usual hour they went to sleep. And at the beginning of the night Enid slept a little; and at midnight she arose, and placed all Geraint’s armor together so that it might be ready to put on. And although fearful of her errand, she came to the side of Geraint’s bed; and she spoke to him softly and gently, saying, “My lord, arise, and clothe thyself, for these were the words of the earl to me and his intention concerning me.” So she told Geraint all that had passed. And although he was wroth with her, he took warning, and clothed himself. And she lighted a candle, that he might have light to do so. “Leave there the candle,” said he, “and desire the man of the house to come here.” Then she went, and the man of the house came to him. “Dost thou know how much I owe thee?” asked Geraint. “I think thou owest but little.” “Take the three horses and the three suits of armor.” “Heaven reward thee, lord,” said he, “but I spent not the value of one suit of armor upon thee.” “For that reason,” said he, “thou wilt be the richer. And now, wilt thou come to guide me out of the town?” “I will gladly,” said he; “and in which direction dost thou intend to go?” “I wish to leave the town by a different way from that by which I entered it.” So the man of the lodgings accompanied him as far as he desired. Then he bade the maiden to go on before him, and she did so, and went straight forward, and his host returned home.\n\nAnd Geraint and the maiden went forward along the high-road. And as they journeyed thus, they heard an exceeding loud wailing near to them. “Stay thou here,” said he, “and I will go and see what is the cause of this wailing.” “I will,” said she. Then he went forward into an open glade that was near the road. And in the glade he saw two horses, one having a man’s saddle, and the other a woman’s saddle upon it. And behold there was a knight lying dead in his armor, and a young damsel in a riding-dress standing over him lamenting. “Ah, lady,” said Geraint, “what hath befallen thee?” “Behold,” she answered, “I journeyed here with my beloved husband, when lo! three giants came upon us, and without any cause in the world, they slew him.” “Which way went they hence?” said Geraint. “Yonder by the high-road,” she replied. So he returned to Enid. “Go,” said he, “to the lady that is below yonder, and await me there till I come.” She was sad when he ordered her to do thus, but nevertheless she went to the damsel, whom it was ruth to hear, and she felt certain that Geraint would never return.\n\nMeanwhile Geraint followed the giants, and overtook them. And each of them was greater in stature than three other men, and a huge club was on the shoulder of each. Then he rushed upon one of them, and thrust his lance through his body. And having drawn it forth again, he pierced another of them through likewise. But the third turned upon him and struck him with his club so that he split his shield and crushed his shoulder. But Geraint drew his sword and gave the giant a blow on the crown of his head, so severe, and fierce, and violent, that his head and his neck were split down to his shoulders, and he fell dead. So Geraint left him thus and returned to Enid. And when he reached the place where she was he fell down lifeless from his horse. Piercing and loud and thrilling was the cry that Enid uttered. And she came and stood over him where he had fallen. And at the sound of her cries came the Earl of Limours, and they who journeyed with him, whom her lamentations brought out of their road. And the earl said to Enid, “Alas, lady, what hath befallen thee?” “Ah, good sir,” said she, “the only man I have loved, or ever shall love, is slain.” Then he said to the other, “And what is the cause of thy grief?” “They have slain my beloved husband also,” said she. “And who was it that slew them?” “Some giants,” she answered, “slew my best-beloved, and the other knight went in pursuit of them, and came back in the state thou seest.” The earl caused the knight that was dead to be buried, but he thought that there still remained some life in Geraint; and to see if he yet would live, he had him carried with him in the hollow of his shield, and upon a bier. And the two damsels went to the court; and when they arrived there, Geraint was placed upon a little couch in front of the table that was in the hall. Then they all took off their traveling-gear, and the earl besought Enid to do the same, and to clothe herself in other garments. “I will not, by Heaven,” said she. “Ah, lady,” said he, “be not so sorrowful for this matter.” “It were hard to persuade me to be otherwise,” said she. “I will act towards thee in such wise that thou needest not be sorrowful, whether yonder knight live or die. Behold, a good earldom, together with myself, will I bestow upon thee; be therefore happy and joyful.” “I declare to Heaven,” said she, “that henceforth I shall never be joyful while I live.” “Come,” said he, “and eat.” “No, by Heaven, I will not.” “But, by Heaven, thou shalt,” said he. So he took her with him to the table against her will, and many times desired her to eat. “I call Heaven to witness,” said she, “that I will not until the man that is upon yonder bier shall eat likewise.” “Thou canst not fulfil that,” said the earl, “yonder man is dead already.” “I will prove that I can,” said she. Then he offered her a goblet of liquor. “Drink this goblet,” he said, “and it will cause thee to change thy mind.” “Evil betide me,” she answered, “if I drink aught until he drink also.” “Truly,” said the earl, “it is of no more avail for me to be gentle with thee than ungentle.” And he gave her a box in the ear. Thereupon she raised a loud and piercing shriek, and her lamentations were much greater than they had been before; for she considered in her mind, that, had Geraint been alive, he durst not have struck her thus. But, behold, at the sound of her cry, Geraint revived from his swoon, and he sat upon the bier; and finding his sword in the hollow of his shield, he rushed to the place where the earl was, and struck him a fiercely-wounding, severely-venomous, and sternly-smiting blow upon the crown of his head, so that he clove him in twain, until his sword was staid by the table. Then all left the board and fled away. And this was not so much through fear of the living, as through the dread they felt at seeing the dead man rise up to slay them. And Geraint looked upon Enid, and he was grieved for two causes; one was to see that Enid had lost her color and her wonted aspect; and the other, to know that she was in the right. “Lady,” said he, “knowest thou where our horses are?” “I know, lord, where thy horse is,” she replied, “but I know not where is the other. Thy horse is in the house yonder.” So he went to the house, and brought forth his horse, and mounted him, and took up Enid, and placed her upon the horse with him. And he rode forward. And their road lay between two hedges; and the night was gaining on the day. And lo! they saw behind them the shafts of spears betwixt them and the sky, and they heard the tramping of horses, and the noise of a host approaching. “I hear something following us,” said he, “and I will put thee on the other side of the hedge.” And thus he did. And thereupon, behold a knight pricked towards him, and couched his lance. When Enid saw this, she cried out, saying, “O chieftain, whoever thou art, what renown wilt thou gain by slaying a dead man?” “O Heaven!” said he, “is it Geraint?” “Yes, in truth,” said she; “and who art thou?” “I am Gwiffert Petit,” said he, “thy husband’s ally, coming to thy assistance, for I heard that thou wast in trouble. Come with me to the court of a son-in-law of my sister, which is near here, and thou shalt have the best medical assistance in the kingdom.” “I will do so gladly,” said Geraint. And Enid was placed upon the horse of one of Gwiffert’s squires, and they went forward to the baron’s palace. And they were received there with gladness, and they met with hospitality and attention. The next morning they went to seek physicians; and it was not long before they came, and they attended Geraint until he was perfectly well. And while Geraint was under medical care Gwiffert caused his armor to be repaired, until it was as good as it had ever been. And they remained there a month and a fortnight. Then they separated, and Geraint went towards his own dominions, and thenceforth he reigned prosperously, and his warlike fame and splendor lasted with renown and honor, both to him and to Enid,[63] from that time forward.\n\n————\n\n# PWYLL, PRINCE OF DYVED\n\nONCE upon a time Pwyll was at Narberth, his chief palace, where a feast had been prepared for him, and with him was a great host of men. And after the first meal Pwyll arose to walk; and he went to the top of a mound that was above the palace, and was called Gorsedd Arberth. “Lord,” said one of the court, “it is peculiar to the mound that whosoever sits upon it cannot go thence without either receiving wounds or blows, or else seeing a wonder.” “I fear not to receive wounds or blows,” said Pwyll; “but as to the wonder, gladly would I see it. I will therefore go and sit upon the mound.”\n\nAnd upon the mound he sat. And while he sat there, they saw a lady, on a pure white horse of large size, with a garment of shining gold around her, coming along the highway that led from the mound. “My men,” said Pwyll, “is there any among you who knows yonder lady?” “There is not, lord,” said they. “Go one of you and meet her, that we may know who she is.” And one of them arose, and as he came upon the road to meet her, she passed by; and he followed as fast as he could, being on foot, and the greater was his speed, the further was she from him. And when he saw that it profited him nothing to follow her, he returned to Pwyll, and said unto him, “Lord, it is idle for any one in the world to follow her on foot.” “Verily,” said Pwyll, “go unto the palace, and take the fleetest horse that thou seest, and go after her.”\n\nAnd he took a horse and went forward. And he came to an open, level plain, and put spurs to his horse; and the more he urged his horse, the further was she from him. And he returned to the place where Pwyll was, and said, “Lord, it will avail nothing for any one to follow yonder lady. I know of no horse in these realms swifter than this, and it availed me not to pursue her.” “Of a truth,” said Pwyll, “there must be some illusion here; let us go towards the palace.” So to the palace they went, and spent the day.\n\nAnd the next day they amused themselves until it was time to go to meat. And when meat was ended, Pwyll said, “Where are the hosts that went yesterday to the top of the mound?” “Behold, lord, we are here,” said they. “Let us go,” said he, “to the mound, and sit there. And do thou,” said he to the page who tended his horse, “saddle my horse well, and hasten with him to the road, and bring also my spurs with thee.” And the youth did thus. And they went and sat upon the mound; and ere they had been there but a short time, they beheld the lady coming by the same road, and in the same manner, and at the same pace. “Young man,” said Pwyll, “I see the lady coming; give me my horse.” And before he had mounted his horse she passed him. And he turned after her and followed her. And he let his horse go bounding playfully, and thought that he should soon come up with her. But he came no nearer to her than at first. Then he urged his horse to his utmost speed, yet he found that it availed not. Then said Pwyll, “O maiden, for the sake of him whom thou best lovest, stay for me.” “I will stay gladly,” said she; “and it were better for thy horse hadst thou asked it long since.” So the maiden stopped; and she threw back that part of her head-dress which covered her face. Then he thought that the beauty of all the maidens and all the ladies that he had ever seen was as nothing compared to her beauty. “Lady,” he said, “wilt thou tell me aught concerning thy purpose?” “I will tell thee,” said she; “my chief quest was to see thee.” “Truly,” said Pwyll, “this is to me the most pleasing quest on which thou couldst have come; and wilt thou tell me who thou art?” “I will tell thee, lord,” said she. “I am Rhiannon, the daughter of Heveydd, and they sought to give me a husband against my will. But no husband would I have, and that because of my love for thee; neither will I yet have one, unless thou reject me; and hither have I come to hear thy answer.” “By Heaven,” said Pwyll, “behold this is my answer. If I might choose among all the ladies and damsels in the world, thee would I choose.” “Verily,” said she, “if thou art thus minded, make a pledge to meet me ere I am given to another.” “The sooner I may do so, the more pleasing will it be to me,” said Pwyll; “and wheresoever thou wilt, there will I meet with thee.” “I will that thou meet me this day twelvemonth at the palace of Heveydd.” “Gladly,” said he, “will I keep this tryst.” So they parted, and he went back to his hosts, and to them of his household. And whatsoever questions they asked him respecting the damsel, he always turned the discourse upon other matters.\n\nAnd when a year from that time was gone, he caused a hundred knights to equip themselves, and to go with him to the palace of Heveydd. And he came to the palace, and there was great joy concerning him, with much concourse of people, and great rejoicing, and vast preparations for his coming. And the whole court was placed under his orders.\n\nAnd the hall was garnished, and they went to meat, and thus did they sit: Heveydd was on one side of Pwyll, and Rhiannon on the other; and all the rest according to their rank. And they ate and feasted, and talked one with another. And at the beginning of the carousal after the meat, there entered a tall, auburn-haired youth, of royal bearing, clothed in a garment of satin. And when he came into the hall, he saluted Pwyll and his companions. “The greeting of Heaven be unto thee,” said Pwyll; “come thou and sit down.” “Nay,” said he, “a suitor am I, and I will do my errand.” “Do so willingly,” said Pwyll. “Lord,” said he, “my errand is unto thee, and it is to crave a boon of thee that I come.” “What boon soever thou mayest ask of me, so far as I am able, thou shalt have.” “Ah!” said Rhiannon, “wherefore didst thou give that answer?” “Has he not given it before the presence of these nobles?” asked the youth. “My soul,” said Pwyll, “what is the boon thou askest?” “The lady whom best I love is to be thy bride this night; I come to ask her of thee, with the feast and the banquet that are in this place.” And Pwyll was silent, because of the promise which he had given. “Be silent as long as thou wilt,” said Rhiannon, “never did man make worse use of his wits than thou hast done.” “Lady,” said he, “I knew not who he was.” “Behold, this is the man to whom they would have given me against my will,” said she; “and he is Gawl, the son of Clud, a man of great power and wealth, and because of the word thou hast spoken, bestow me upon him, lest shame befall thee.” “Lady,” said he, “I understand not thy answer; never can I do as thou sayest.” “Bestow me upon him,” said she, “and I will cause that I shall never be his.” “By what means will that be?” asked Pwyll. Then she told him the thought that was in her mind. And they talked long together. Then Gawl said, “Lord, it is meet that I have an answer to my request.” “As much of that thou hast asked as it is in my power to give, thou shalt have,” replied Pwyll. “My soul,” said Rhiannon unto Gawl, “as for the feast and the banquet that are here, I have bestowed them upon the men of Dyved, and the household and the warriors that are with us. These can I not suffer to be given to any. In a year from to-night, a banquet shall be prepared for thee in this palace, that I may become thy bride.”\n\nSo Gawl went forth to his possessions, and Pwyll went also back to Dyved. And they both spent that year until it was the time for the feast at the palace of Heveydd. Then Gawl, the son of Clud, set out to the feast that was prepared for him; and he came to the palace, and was received there with rejoicing. Pwyll, also, the chief of Dyved, came to the orchard with a hundred knights, as Rhiannon had commanded him. And Pwyll was clad in coarse and ragged garments, and wore large, clumsy old shoes upon his feet. And when he knew that the carousal after the meat had begun, he went toward the hall; and when he came into the hall he saluted Gawl, the son of Clud, and his company, both men and women. “Heaven prosper thee,” said Gawl, “and friendly greeting be unto thee!” “Lord,” said he, “may Heaven reward thee! I have an errand unto thee.” “Welcome be thine errand, and if thou ask of me that which is right, thou shalt have it gladly.” “It is fitting,” answered he; “I crave but from want, and the boon I ask is to have this small bag that thou seest filled with meat.” “A request within reason is this,” said he, “and gladly shalt thou have it. Bring him food.” A great number of attendants arose and began to fill the bag; but for all they put into it, it was no fuller than at first. “My soul,” said Gawl, “will thy bag ever be full?” “It will not, I declare to Heaven,” said he, “for all that may be put into it, unless one possessed of lands, and domains, and treasure, shall arise and tread down with both his feet the food that is within the bag, and shall say, ‘Enough has been put therein.’” Then said Rhiannon unto Gawl, the son of Clud, “Rise up quickly.” “I will willingly arise,” said he. So he rose up, and put his two feet into the bag. And Pwyll turned up the sides of the bag, so that Gawl was over his head in it. And he shut it up quickly, and slipped a knot upon the thongs, and blew his horn. And thereupon, behold, his knights came down upon the palace. And they seized all the host that had come with Gawl, and cast them into his own prison. And Pwyll threw off his rags, and his old shoes, and his tattered array. And as they came in, every one of Pwyll’s knights struck a blow upon the bag, and asked, “What is here?” “A badger,” said they. And in this manner they played, each of them striking the bag, either with his foot or with a staff. And thus played they with the bag. And then was the game of Badger in the Bag first played.\n\n“Lord,” said the man in the bag, “if thou wouldst but hear me, I merit not to be slain in a bag.” Said Heveydd, “Lord, he speaks truth; it were fitting that thou listen to him, for he deserves not this.” “Verily,” said Pwyll, “I will do thy counsel concerning him.” “Behold, this is my counsel then,” said Rhiannon. “Thou art now in a position in which it behooves thee to satisfy suitors and minstrels. Let him give unto them in thy stead, and take a pledge from him that he will never seek to revenge that which has been done to him. And this will be punishment enough.” “I will do this gladly,” said the man in the bag. “And gladly will I accept it,” said Pwyll, “since it is the counsel of Heveydd and Rhiannon. Seek thyself sureties.” “We will be for him,” said Heveydd, “until his men be free to answer for him.” And upon this he was let out of the bag, and his liegemen were liberated. “Verily, lord,” said Gawl, “I am greatly hurt, and I have many bruises. With thy leave, I will go forth. I will leave nobles in my stead to answer for me in all that thou shalt require.” “Willingly,” said Pwyll, “mayest thou do this.” So Gawl went to his own possessions.\n\nAnd the hall was set in order for Pwyll and the men of his host, and for them also of the palace, and they went to the tables and sat down. And as they had sat that time twelvemonth, so sat they that night. And they ate and feasted, and spent the night in mirth and tranquility. And the time came that they should sleep, and Pwyll and Rhiannon went to their chamber.\n\nAnd next morning at break of day, “My lord,” said Rhiannon, “arise and begin to give thy gifts unto the minstrels. Refuse no one to-day that may claim thy bounty.” “Thus shall it be gladly,” said Pwyll, “both to-day and every day while the feast shall last.” So Pwyll arose, and he caused silence to be proclaimed, and desired all the suitors and minstrels to show and to point out what gifts they desired. And this being done, the feast went on, and he denied no one while it lasted. And when the feast was ended, Pwyll said unto Heveydd, “My lord, with thy permission, I will set out for Dyved to-morrow.” “Certainly,” said Heveydd; “may Heaven prosper thee! Fix also a time when Rhiannon shall follow thee.” “By Heaven,” said Pwyll, “we will go hence together.” “Willest thou this, lord?” said Heveydd. “Yes, lord,” answered Pwyll.\n\nAnd the next day they set forward towards Dyved, and journeyed to the palace of Narberth, where a feast was made ready for them. And there came to them great numbers of the chief men and the most noble ladies of the land, and of these there were none to whom Rhiannon did not give some rich gift, either a bracelet, or a ring, or a precious stone. And they ruled the land prosperously that year and the next.\n\n————\n\n# BRANWEN, THE DAUGHTER OF LLYR\n\nBENDIGEID VRAN, the son of Llyr, was the crowned king of this island, and he was exalted from the crown of London. And one afternoon he was at Harlech, in Ardudwy, at his court; and he sat upon the rock of Harlech, looking over the sea. And with him were his brother, Manawyddan, the son of Llyr, and his brothers by the mother’s side, Nissyen and Evnissyen, and many nobles likewise, as was fitting to see around a king. His two brothers by the mother’s side were the sons of Euroswydd, and one of these youths was a good youth, and of gentle nature, and would make peace between his kindred, and cause his family to be friends when their wrath was at the highest, and this one was Nissyen; but the other would cause strife between his two brothers when they were most at peace. And as they sat thus they beheld thirteen ships coming from the south of Ireland, and making towards them; and they came with a swift motion, the wind being behind them; and they neared them rapidly. “I see ships afar,” said the king, “coming swiftly towards the land. Command the men of the court that they equip themselves, and go and learn their intent.” So the men equipped themselves, and went down towards them. And when they saw the ships near, certain were they that they had never seen ships better furnished. Beautiful flags of satin were upon them. And, behold, one of the ships outstripped the others, and they saw a shield lifted up above the side of the ship, and the point of the shield was upwards, in token of peace. And the men drew near, that they might hold converse. Then they put out boats, and came toward the land. And they saluted the king. Now the king could hear them from the place where he was upon the rock above their heads. “Heaven prosper you,” said he, “and be ye welcome! To whom do these ships belong, and who is the chief amongst you?” “Lord,” said they, “Matholch, king of Ireland, is here, and these ships belong to him.” “Wherefore comes he?” asked the king, “and will he come to the land?” “He is a suitor unto thee, lord,” said they, “and he will not land unless he have his boon.” “And what may that be?” inquired the king. “He desires to ally himself, lord, with thee,” said they, “and he comes to ask Branwen, the daughter of Llyr, that, if it seem well to thee, the Island of the Mighty[64] may be leagued with Ireland, and both become more powerful.” “Verily,” said he, “let him come to land, and we will take counsel thereupon.” And this answer was brought to Matholch. “I will go willingly,” said he. So he landed, and they received him joyfully; and great was the throng in the palace that night, between his hosts and those of the court; and next day they took counsel, and they resolved to bestow Branwen upon Matholch. Now she was one of the three chief ladies of this island, and she was the fairest damsel in the world.\n\nAnd they fixed upon Aberfraw as the place where she should become his bride. And they went thence, and towards Aberfraw the hosts proceeded, Matholch and his host in their ships, Bendigeid Vran and his host by land, until they came to Aberfraw. And at Aberfraw they began the feast, and sat down. And thus sat they: the king of the Island of the Mighty and Manawyddan, the son of Llyr, on one side, and Matholch on the other side, and Branwen, the daughter of Llyr, beside him. And they were not within a house, but under tents. No house could ever contain Bendigeid Vran. And they began the banquet, and caroused and discoursed. And when it was more pleasing to them to sleep than to carouse, they went to rest, and Branwen became Matholch’s bride.\n\nAnd next day they arose, and all they of the court, and the officers began to equip, and to range the horses and the attendants, and they ranged them in order as far as the sea.\n\nAnd, behold, one day Evnissyen, the quarrelsome man, of whom it is spoken above, came by chance into the place where the horses of Matholch were, and asked whose horses they might be. “They are the horses of Matholch, king of Ireland, who is married to Branwen, thy sister; his horses are they.” “And is it thus they have done with a maiden such as she, and moreover my sister, bestowing her without my consent? They could have offered no greater insult to me than this,” said he. And thereupon he rushed under the horses, and cut off their lips at the teeth, and their ears close to their heads, and their tails close to their backs; and he disfigured the horses, and rendered them useless.\n\nAnd they came with these tidings unto Matholch, saying that the horses were disfigured and injured, so that not one of them could ever be of any use again. “Verily, lord,” said one, “it was an insult unto thee, and as such was it meant.” “Of a truth, it is a marvel to me that, if they desire to insult me, they should have given me a maiden of such high rank, and so much beloved of her kindred, as they have done.” “Lord,” said another, “thou seest that thus it is, and there is nothing for thee to do but to go to thy ships.” And thereupon towards his ships he set out.\n\nAnd tidings came to Bendigeid Vran that Matholch was quitting the court without asking leave, and messengers were sent to inquire of him wherefore he did so. And the messengers that went were Iddic, the son of Anarawd, and Heveyd Hir. And these overtook him, and asked of him what he designed to do, and wherefore he went forth. “Of a truth,” said he, “if I had known, I had not come hither. I have been altogether insulted; no one had ever worse treatment than I have had here.” “Truly, lord, it was not the will of any that are of the court,” said they, “nor of any that are of the council, that thou shouldst have received this insult; and as thou hast been insulted, the dishonor is greater unto Bendigeid Vran than unto thee.” “Verily,” said he, “I think so. Nevertheless, he cannot recall the insult.” These men returned with that answer to the place where Bendigeid Vran was, and they told him what reply Matholch had given them. “Truly,” said he, “there are no means by which we may prevent his going away at enmity with us that we will not take.” “Well, lord,” said they, “send after him another embassy.” “I will do so,” said he. “Arise, Manawyddan, son of Llyr, and Heveyd Hir, and go after him, and tell him that he shall have a sound horse for every one that has been injured. And beside that, as an atonement for the insult, he shall have a staff of silver as large and as tall as himself, and a plate of gold of the breadth of his face. And show unto him who it was that did this, and that it was done against my will; but that he who did it is my brother, and therefore it would be hard for me to put him to death. And let him come and meet me,” said he, “and we will make peace in any way he may desire.”\n\nThe embassy went after Matholch, and told him all these sayings in a friendly manner; and he listened thereunto. “Men,” said he, “I will take counsel.” So to the council he went. And in the council they considered that, if they should refuse this, they were likely to have more shame rather than to obtain so great an atonement. They resolved, therefore, to accept it, and they returned to the court in peace.\n\nThen the pavilions and the tents were set in order, after the fashion of a hall; and they went to meat, and as they had sat at the beginning of the feast so sat they there. And Matholch and Bendigeid Vran began to discourse; and, behold, it seemed to Bendigeid Vran, while they talked, that Matholch was not so cheerful as he had been before. And he thought that the chieftain might be sad because of the smallness of the atonement which he had for the wrong that had been done him. “O man,” said Bendigeid Vran, “thou dost not discourse to-night so cheerfully as thou wast wont. And if it be because of the smallness of the atonement, thou shalt add thereunto whatsoever thou mayest choose, and to-morrow I will pay thee for the horses.” “Lord,” said he, “Heaven reward thee!” “And I will enhance the atonement,” said Bendigeid Vran, “for I will give unto thee a caldron, the property of which is, that if one of thy men be slain to-day, and be cast therein, to-morrow he will be as well as ever he was at the best, except that he will not regain his speech.” And thereupon he gave him great thanks, and very joyful was he for that cause.\n\nThat night they continued to discourse as much as they would, and had minstrelsy and carousing; and when it was more pleasant to them to sleep than to sit longer, they went to rest. And thus was the banquet carried on with joyousness; and when it was finished, Matholch journeyed towards Ireland, and Branwen with him; and they went from Aber Menei with thirteen ships, and came to Ireland. And in Ireland was there great joy because of their coming. And not one great man nor noble lady visited Branwen unto whom she gave not either a clasp or a ring, or a royal jewel to keep, such as it was honorable to be seen departing with. And in these things she spent that year in much renown, and she passed her time pleasantly, enjoying honor and friendship. And in due time a son was born unto her, and the name that they gave him was Gwern, the son of Matholch, and they put the boy out to be nursed in a place where were the best men of Ireland.\n\nAnd, behold, in the second year a tumult arose in Ireland, on account of the insult which Matholch had received in Wales, and the payment made him for his horses. And his foster-brothers, and such as were nearest to him, blamed him openly for that matter. And he might have no peace by reason of the tumult, until they should revenge upon him this disgrace. And the vengeance which they took was to drive away Branwen from the same chamber with him, and to make her cook for the court; and they caused the butcher, after he had cut up the meat, to come to her and give her every day a blow on the ear; and such they made her punishment.\n\n“Verily, lord,” said his men to Matholch, “forbid now the ships and the ferry-boats, and the coracles, that they go not into Wales, and such as come over from Wales hither, imprison them, that they go not back for this thing to be known there.” And he did so; and it was thus for no less than three years.\n\nAnd Branwen reared a starling in the cover of the kneading-trough, and she taught it to speak, and she taught the bird what manner of man her brother was. And she wrote a letter of her woes, and the despite with which she was treated, and she bound the letter to the root of the bird’s wing, and sent it toward Wales. And the bird came to that island; and one day it found Bendigeid Vran at Caer Seiont in Arvon, conferring there, and it alighted upon his shoulder, and ruffled its feathers, so that the letter was seen, and they knew that the bird had been reared in a domestic manner.\n\nThen Bendigeid Vran took the letter and looked upon it. And when he had read the letter, he grieved exceedingly at the tidings of Branwen’s woes. And immediately he began sending messengers to summon the island together. And he caused seven-score and four of his chief men to come unto him, and he complained to them of the grief that his sister endured. So they took counsel. And in the counsel they resolved to go to Ireland, and to leave seven men as princes at home, and Caradoc,[65] the son of Bran, as the chief of them.\n\nBendigeid Vran, with the host of which we spoke, sailed towards Ireland; and it was not far across the sea, and he came to shoal water. Now the swine-herds of Matholch were upon the sea-shore, and they came to Matholch. “Lord,” said they, “greeting be unto thee.” “Heaven protect you!” said he; “have you any news?” “Lord,” said they, “we have marvellous news. A wood have we seen upon the sea, in a place where we never yet saw a single tree.” “This is indeed a marvel,” said he; “saw you aught else?” “We saw, lord,” said they, “a vast mountain beside the wood, which moved, and there was a lofty ridge on the top of the mountain, and a lake on each side of the ridge. And the wood and the mountain, and all these things, moved.” “Verily,” said he, “there is none who can know aught concerning this unless it be Branwen.”\n\nMessengers then went unto Branwen. “Lady,” said they, “what thinkest thou that this is?” “The men of the Island of the Mighty, who have come hither on hearing of my ill-treatment and of my woes.” “What is the forest that is seen upon the sea?” asked they. “The yards and the masts of ships,” she answered. “Alas!” said they; “what is the mountain that is seen by the side of the ships?” “Bendigeid Vran, my brother,” she replied, “coming to shoal water, and he is wading to the land.” “What is the lofty ridge, with the lake on each side thereof?” “On looking towards this island he is wroth, and his two eyes on each side of his nose are the two lakes on each side of the ridge.”\n\nThe warriors and chief men of Ireland were brought together in haste, and they took counsel. “Lord,” said the neighbors unto Matholch, “there is no other counsel than this alone. Thou shalt give the kingdom to Gwern, the son of Branwen his sister, as a compensation for the wrong and despite that have been done unto Branwen. And he will make peace with thee.” And in the council it was resolved that this message should be sent to Bendigeid Vran, lest the country should be destroyed. And this peace was made. And Matholch caused a great house to be built for Bendigeid Vran, and his host. Thereupon came the hosts into the house. The men of the island of Ireland entered the house on the one side, and the men of the Island of the Mighty on the other. And as soon as they had sat down, there was concord between them; and the sovereignty was conferred upon the boy. When the peace was concluded, Bendigeid Vran called the boy unto him, and from Bendigeid Vran the boy went unto Manawyddan; and he was beloved by all that beheld him. And from Manawyddan the boy was called by Nissyen, the son of Euroswydd, and the boy went unto him lovingly. “Wherefore,” said Evnissyen, “comes not my nephew, the son of my sister, unto me? Though he were not king of Ireland, yet willingly would I fondle the boy.” “Cheerfully let him go to thee,” said Bendigeid Vran; and the boy went unto him cheerfully. “By my confession to Heaven,” said Evnissyen in his heart, “unthought of is the slaughter that I will this instant commit.”\n\nThen he arose and took up the boy, and before any one in the house could seize hold of him he thrust the boy headlong into the blazing fire. And when Branwen saw her son burning in the fire, she strove to leap into the fire also, from the place where she sat between her two brothers. But Bendigeid Vran grasped her with one hand, and his shield with the other. Then they all hurried about the house, and never was there made so great a tumult by any host in one house as was made by them, as each man armed himself. And while they all sought their arms Bendigeid Vran supported Branwen between his shield and his shoulder. And they fought.\n\nThen the Irish kindled a fire under the caldron of renovation, and they cast the dead bodies into the caldron until it was full; and the next day they came forth fighting men, as good as before, except that they were not able to speak. Then when Evnissyen saw the dead bodies of the men of the Island of the Mighty nowhere resuscitated, he said in his heart, “Alas! woe is me, that I should have been the cause of bringing the men of the Island of the Mighty into so great a strait. Evil betide me if I find not a deliverance therefrom.” And he cast himself among the dead bodies of the Irish; and two unshod Irishmen came to him, and, taking him to be one of the Irish, flung him into the caldron. And he stretched himself out in the caldron, so that he rent the caldron into four pieces, and burst his own heart also.\n\nIn consequence of this, the men of the Island of the Mighty obtained such success as they had; but they were not victorious, for only seven men of them all escaped, and Bendigeid Vran himself was wounded in the foot with a poisoned dart. Now the men that escaped were Pryderi, Manawyddan, Taliesin, and four others.\n\nAnd Bendigeid Vran commanded them that they should cut off his head. “And take you my head,” said he, “and bear it even unto the White Mount in London, and bury it there with the face towards France. And so long as it lies there, no enemy shall ever land on the island.” So they cut off his head, and these seven went forward therewith. And Branwen was the eighth with them. And they came to land on Aber Alaw, and they sat down to rest. And Branwen looked towards Ireland, and towards the Island of the Mighty, to see if she could descry them. “Alas!” said she, “woe is me that I was ever born; two islands have been destroyed because of me.” Then she uttered a groan, and there broke her heart. And they made her a four-sided grave, and buried her upon the banks of the Alaw.\n\nThen the seven men journeyed forward, bearing the head with them; and as they went, behold there met them a multitude of men and women. “Have you any tidings?” said Manawyddan. “We have none,” said they, “save that Caswallawn,[66] the son of Beli, has conquered the Island of the Mighty, and is crowned king in London.” “What has become,” said they, “of Caradoc, the son of Bran, and the seven men who were left with him in this island?” “Caswallawn came upon them, and slew six of the men, and Caradoc’s heart broke for grief thereof.” And the seven men journeyed on towards London, and they buried the head in the White Mount, as Bendigeid Vran had directed them.[67]\n\n————\n\nPWYLL and Rhiannon had a son, whom they named Pryderi. And when he was grown up, Pwyll, his father, died. And Pryderi married Kicva, the daughter of Gwynn Gloy.\n\nNow Manawyddan returned from the war in Ireland, and he found that his cousin had seized all his possessions, and much grief and heaviness came upon him. “Alas! woe is me!” he exclaimed; “there is none save myself without a home and a resting-place.” “Lord,” said Pryderi, “be not so sorrowful. Thy cousin is king of the Island of the Mighty, and though he has done thee wrong, thou hast never been a claimant of land or possessions.” “Yea,” answered he, “but although this man is my cousin, it grieveth me to see any one in the place of my brother, Bendigeid Vran; neither can I be happy in the same dwelling with him.” “Wilt thou follow the counsel of another?” said Pryderi. “I stand in need of counsel,” he answered, “and what may that counsel be?” “Seven cantrevs belong unto me,” said Pryderi, “wherein Rhiannon, my mother, dwells. I will bestow her upon thee, and the seven cantrevs with her; and though thou hadst no possessions but those cantrevs only, thou couldst not have any fairer than they. Do thou and Rhiannon enjoy them, and if thou desire any possessions thou wilt not despise these.” “I do not, chieftain,” said he. “Heaven reward thee for the friendship! I will go with thee to seek Rhiannon, and to look at thy possessions.” “Thou wilt do well,” he answered; “and I believe that thou didst never hear a lady discourse better than she, and when she was in her prime, none was ever fairer. Even now her aspect is not uncomely.”\n\nThey set forth, and, however long the journey, they came at last to Dyved; and a feast was prepared for them by Rhiannon and Kicva. Then began Manawyddan and Rhiannon to sit and to talk together; and his mind and his thoughts became warmed towards her, and he thought in his heart he had never beheld any lady more fulfilled of grace and beauty than she. “Pryderi,” said he, “I will that it be as thou didst say.” “What saying was that?” asked Rhiannon. “Lady,” said Pryderi, “I did offer thee as a wife to Manawyddan, the son of Llyr.” “By that will I gladly abide,” said Rhiannon. “Right glad am I also,” said Manawyddan, “may Heaven reward him who hath shown unto me friendship so perfect as this!”\n\nAnd before the feast was over she became his bride. Said Pryderi, “Tarry ye here the rest of the feast, and I will go into England to tender my homage unto Caswallawn, the son of Beli.” “Lord,” said Rhiannon, “Caswallawn is in Kent; thou mayest therefore tarry at the feast, and wait until he shall be nearer.” “We will wait,” he answered. So they finished the feast. And they began to make the circuit of Dyved, and to hunt, and to take their pleasure. And as they went through the country, they had never seen lands more pleasant to live in, nor better hunting grounds, nor greater plenty of honey and fish. And such was the friendship between these four, that they would not be parted from each other by night nor by day.\n\nAnd in the midst of all this he went to Caswallawn at Oxford, and tendered his homage; and honorable was his reception there, and highly was he praised for offering his homage.\n\nAnd after his return Pryderi and Manawyddan feasted and took their ease and pleasure. And they began a feast at Narberth, for it was the chief palace. And when they had ended the first meal, while those who served them ate, they arose and went forth, and proceeded to the Gorsedd, that is, the Mount of Narberth, and their retinue with them. And as they sat thus, behold a peal of thunder, and with the violence of the thunder-storm, lo! there came a fall of mist, so thick that not one of them could see the other. And after the mist it became light all around. And when they looked towards the place where they were wont to see the cattle and herds and dwellings, they saw nothing now, neither house, nor beast, nor smoke, nor fire, nor man, nor dwelling, but the buildings of the court empty, and desert, and uninhabited, without either man or beast within them. And truly all their companions were lost to them, without their knowing aught of what had befallen them, save those four only.\n\n“In the name of Heaven,” said Manawyddan, “where are they of the court, and all my host beside? Let us go and see.”\n\nSo they came to the castle, and saw no man, and into the hall, and to the sleeping-place, and there was none; and in the mead-cellar and in the kitchen there was naught but desolation. Then they began to go through the land, and all the possessions that they had; and they visited the houses and dwellings, and found nothing but wild beasts. And when they had consumed their feast and all their provisions, they fed upon the prey they killed in hunting, and the honey of the wild swans.\n\nAnd one morning Pryderi and Manawyddan rose up to hunt, and they ranged their dogs and went forth. And some of the dogs ran before them, and came to a bush which was near at hand; but as soon as they were come to the bush, they hastily drew back, and returned to the men, their hair bristling up greatly. “Let us go near to the bush,” said Pryderi, “and see what is in it.” And as they came near, behold, a wild boar of a pure white color rose up from the bush. Then the dogs, being set on by the men, rushed towards him; but he left the bush, and fell back a little way from the men, and made a stand against the dogs, without retreating from them, until the men had come near. And when the men came up, he fell back a second time, and betook him to flight. Then they pursued the boar until they beheld a vast and lofty castle, all newly built, in a place where they had never before seen either stone or building. And the boar ran swiftly into the castle, and the dogs after him. Now when the boar and the dogs had gone into the castle, the men began to wonder at finding a castle in a place where they had never before seen any building whatsoever. And from the top of the Gorsedd they looked and listened for the dogs. But so long as they were there, they heard not one of the dogs, nor aught concerning them.\n\n“Lord,” said Pryderi, “I will go into the castle to get tidings of the dogs.” “Truly,” he replied, “thou wouldst be unwise to go into this castle, which thou hast never seen till now. If thou wouldst follow my counsel, thou wouldst not enter therein. Whosoever has cast a spell over this land, has caused this castle to be here.” “Of a truth,” answered Pryderi, “I cannot thus give up my dogs.” And for all the counsel that Manawyddan gave him, yet to the castle he went.\n\nWhen he came within the castle, neither man nor beast, nor boar, nor dogs, nor house, nor dwelling, saw he within it. But in the centre of the castle-floor he beheld a fountain with marble-work around it, and on the margin of the fountain a golden bowl upon a marble slab, and chains hanging from the air, to which he saw no end.\n\nAnd he was greatly pleased with the beauty of the gold, and with the rich workmanship of the bowl; and he went up to the bowl, and laid hold of it. And when he had taken hold of it, his hands stuck to the bowl, and his feet to the slab on which the bowl was placed; and all his joyousness forsook him, so that he could not utter a word. And thus he stood.\n\nAnd Manawyddan waited for him till near the close of the day. And late in the evening, being certain that he should have no tidings of Pryderi or the dogs, he went back to the palace. And as he entered, Rhiannon looked at him. “Where,” said she, “are thy companion and thy dogs?” “Behold,” he answered, “the adventure that has befallen me.” And he related it all unto her. “An evil companion hast thou been,” said Rhiannon, “and a good companion hast thou lost.” And with that word she went out, and proceeded towards the castle, according to the direction which he gave her. The gate of the castle she found open. She was nothing daunted, and she went in. And as she went in, she perceived Pryderi laying hold of the bowl, and she went towards him. “O my lord,” said she, “what dost thou here?” And she took hold of the bowl with him; and as she did so, her hands also became fast to the bowl, and her feet to the slab, and she was not able to utter a word. And with that, as it became night, lo! there came thunder upon them, and a fall of mist; and thereupon the castle vanished, and they with it.\n\nWhen Kicva, the daughter of Gwynn Gloy, saw that there was no one in the palace but herself and Manawyddan, she sorrowed so that she cared not whether she lived or died. And Manawyddan saw this. “Thou art in the wrong,” said he, “if through fear of me thou grievest thus. I call Heaven to witness that thou hast never seen friendship more pure than that which I will bear thee, as long as Heaven will that thou shouldst be thus. I declare to thee, that, were I in the dawn of youth, I would keep my faith unto Pryderi, and unto thee also will I keep it. Be there no fear upon thee, therefore.” “Heaven reward thee!” she said; “and that is what I deemed of thee.” And the damsel thereupon took courage, and was glad.\n\n“Truly, lady,” said Manawyddan, “it is not fitting for us to stay here; we have lost our dogs, and cannot get food. Let us go into England; it is easiest for us to find support there.” “Gladly, lord,” said she, “we will do so.” And they set forth together to England.\n\n“Lord,” said she, “what craft wilt thou follow? Take up one that is seemly.” “None other will I take,” answered he, “but that of making shoes.” “Lord,” said she, “such a craft becomes not a man so nobly born as thou.” “By that however will I abide,” said he. “I know nothing thereof,” said Kicva. “But I know,” answered Manawyddan, “and I will teach thee to stitch. We will not attempt to dress the leather, but we will buy it ready dressed, and will make the shoes from it.”\n\nSo they went into England, and went as far as Hereford; and they betook themselves to making shoes. And he began by buying the best cordwain that could be had in the town, and none other would buy. And he associated himself with the best goldsmith in the town, and caused him to make clasps for the shoes, and to gild the clasps; and he marked how it was done until he learned the method. And therefore is he called one of the three makers of gold shoes. And when they could be had from him, not a shoe nor hose was bought of any of the cordwainers in the town. But when the cordwainers perceived that their gains were failing (for as Manawyddan shaped the work, so Kicva stitched it), they came together and took counsel, and agreed that they would slay them. And he had warning thereof, and it was told him how the cordwainers had agreed together to slay him.\n\n“Lord,” said Kicva, “wherefore should this be borne from these boors?” “Nay,” said he, “we will go back unto Dyved.” So towards Dyved they set forth.\n\nNow Manawyddan, when he set out to return to Dyved, took with him a burden of wheat. And he proceeded towards Narberth, and there he dwelt. And never was he better pleased than when he saw Narberth again, and the lands where he had been wont to hunt with Pryderi and with Rhiannon. And he accustomed himself to fish, and to hunt the deer in their covert. And then he began to prepare some ground, and he sowed a croft, and a second, and a third. And no wheat in the world ever sprung up better. And the three crofts prospered with perfect growth, and no man ever saw fairer wheat than it.\n\nAnd thus passed the seasons of the year until the harvest came. And he went to look at one of his crofts, and, behold, it was ripe. “I will reap this to-morrow,” said he. And that night he went back to Narberth, and on the morrow, in the gray dawn, he went to reap the croft; and when he came there, he found nothing but the bare straw. Every one of the ears of the wheat was cut off from the stalk, and all the ears carried entirely away, and nothing but the straw left. And at this he marvelled greatly.\n\nThen he went to look at another croft, and, behold, that also was ripe. “Verily,” said he, “this will I reap to-morrow.” And on the morrow he came with the intent to reap it; and when he came there, he found nothing but the bare straw. “O gracious Heaven!” he exclaimed. “I know that whosoever has begun my ruin is completing it, and has also destroyed the country with me.”\n\nThen he went to look at the third croft; and when he came there, finer wheat had there never been seen, and this also was ripe. “Evil betide me,” said he, “if I watch not here to-night. Whoever carried off the other corn will come in like manner to take this, and I will know who it is.” And he told Kicva all that had befallen. “Verily,” said she, “what thinkest thou to do?” “I will watch the croft to-night,” said he. And he went to watch the croft.\n\nAnd at midnight he heard something stirring among the wheat; and he looked, and behold, the mightiest host of mice in the world, which could neither be numbered nor measured. And he knew not what it was until the mice had made their way into the croft, and each of them, climbing up the straw, and bending it down with its weight, had cut off one of the ears of wheat, and had carried it away, leaving there the stalk; and he saw not a single straw there that had not a mouse to it. And they all took their way, carrying the ears with them.\n\nIn wrath and anger did he rush upon the mice; but he could no more come up with them than if they had been gnats or birds of the air, except one only, which, though it was but sluggish, went so fast that a man on foot could scarce overtake it. And after this one he went, and he caught it, and put it in his glove, and tied up the opening of the glove with a string, and kept it with him, and returned to the palace. Then he came to the hall where Kicva was, and he lighted a fire, and hung the glove by the string upon a peg. “What hast thou there, lord?” said Kicva. “A thief,” said he, “that I found robbing me.” “What kind of a thief may it be, lord, that thou couldst put into thy glove?” said she. Then he told her how the mice came to the last of the fields in his sight. “And one of them was less nimble than the rest, and is now in my glove; to-morrow I will hang it.” “My lord,” said she, “this is marvellous; but yet it would be unseemly for a man of dignity like thee to be hanging such a reptile as this.” “Woe betide me,” said he, “if I would not hang them all, could I catch them, and such as I have I will hang.” “Verily, lord,” said she, “there is no reason that I should succor this reptile, except to prevent discredit unto thee. Do therefore, lord, as thou wilt.”\n\nThen he went to the Mound of Narberth, taking the mouse with him. And he set up two forks on the highest part of the mound. And while he was doing this, behold, he saw a scholar coming towards him, in old and poor and tattered garments. And it was now seven years since he had seen in that place either man or beast, except those four persons who had remained together until two of them were lost.\n\n“My lord,” said the scholar, “good-day to thee.” “Heaven prosper thee, and my greeting be unto thee! And whence dost thou come, scholar?” asked he. “I come, lord, from singing in England; and wherefore dost thou inquire?” “Because for the last seven years,” answered he, “I have seen no man here save four secluded persons, and thyself this moment.” “Truly, lord,” said he, “I go through this land unto mine own. And what work art thou upon, lord?” “I am hanging a thief that I caught robbing me,” said he. “What manner of thief is that?” asked the scholar. “I see a creature in thy hand like unto a mouse, and ill does it become a man of rank equal to thine to touch a reptile such as this. Let it go forth free.” “I will not let it go free, by Heaven,” said he; “I caught it robbing me, and the doom of a thief will I inflict upon it, and I will hang it.” “Lord,” said he, “rather than see a man of rank equal to thine at such a work as this, I would give thee a pound, which I have received as alms, to let the reptile go forth free.” “I will not let it go free,” said he, “neither will I sell it.” “As thou wilt, lord,” he answered; “I care naught.” And the scholar went his way.\n\nAnd as he was placing the cross-beam upon the two forks, behold, a priest came towards him, upon a horse covered with trappings. “Good day to thee, lord,” said he. “Heaven prosper thee!” said Manawyddan; “thy blessing.” “The blessing of Heaven be upon thee! And what, lord, art thou doing?” “I am hanging a thief that I caught robbing me,” said he. “What manner of thief, lord?” asked he. “A creature,” he answered, “in form of a mouse. It has been robbing me, and I am inflicting upon it the doom of a thief.” “Lord,” said he, “rather than see thee touch this reptile, I would purchase its freedom.” “By my confession to Heaven, neither will I sell it nor set it free.” “It is true, lord, that it is worth nothing to buy; but rather than see thee defile thyself by touching such a reptile as this, I will give thee three pounds to let it go.” “I will not, by Heaven,” said he, “take any price for it. As it ought, so shall it be hanged.” And the priest went his way.\n\nThen he noosed the string around the mouse’s neck, and as he was about to draw it up, behold, he saw a bishop’s retinue, with his sumpter-horses and his attendants. And the bishop himself came towards him. And he stayed his work. “Lord Bishop,” said he, “thy blessing.” “Heaven’s blessing be unto thee!” said he. “What work art thou upon?” “Hanging a thief that I caught robbing me,” said he. “Is not that a mouse that I see in thy hand?” “Yes,” answered he, “and she has robbed me.” “Ay,” said he, “since I have come at the doom of this reptile I will ransom it of thee. I will give thee seven pounds for it, and that rather than see a man of rank equal to thine destroying so vile a reptile as this. Let it loose, and thou shalt have the money.” “I declare to Heaven that I will not let it loose.” “If thou wilt not loose it for this, I will give thee four and twenty pounds of ready money to set it free.” “I will not set it free, by Heaven, for as much again,” said he. “If thou wilt not set it free for this, I will give thee all the horses that thou seest in this plain, and the seven loads of baggage, and the seven horses that they are upon.” “By Heaven, I will not,” he replied. “Since for this thou wilt not set it free, do so at what price soever thou wilt.” “I will that Rhiannon and Pryderi be free,” said he. “That thou shalt have,” he answered. “Not yet will I loose the mouse, by Heaven.” “What then wouldst thou?” “That the charm and the illusion be removed from the seven cantrevs of Dyved.” “This shalt thou have also; set therefore the mouse free.” “I will not set it free, by Heaven,” said he, “till I know who the mouse may be.” “She is my wife.” “Wherefore came she to me?” “To despoil thee,” he answered. “I am Lloyd, the son of Kilwed, and I cast the charm over the seven cantrevs of Dyved. And it was to avenge Gawl, the son of Clud, from the friendship I had towards him, that I cast the charm. And upon Pryderi did I avenge Gawl, the son of Clud, for the game of Badger in the Bag, that Pwyll, the son of Auwyn, played upon him. And when it was known that thou wast come to dwell in the land, my household came and besought me to transform them into mice, that they might destroy thy corn. And they went the first and the second night, and destroyed thy two crops. And the third night came unto me my wife and the ladies of the court, and besought me to transform them. And I transformed them. Now she is not in her usual health. And had she been in her usual health, thou wouldst not have been able to overtake her; but since this has taken place, and she has been caught, I will restore to thee Pryderi and Rhiannon, and I will take the charm and illusion from off Dyved. Set her therefore free.” “I will not set her free yet.” “What wilt thou more?” he asked. “I will that there be no more charm upon the seven cantrevs of Dyved, and that none shall be put upon it henceforth; moreover, that vengeance be never taken for this, either upon Pryderi or Rhiannon, or upon me.” “All this shalt thou have. And truly thou hast done wisely in asking this. Upon thy head would have lit all this trouble.” “Yea,” said he, “for fear thereof was it that I required this.” “Set now my wife at liberty.” “I will not,” said he, “until I see Pryderi and Rhiannon with me free.” “Behold, here they come,” he answered.\n\nAnd thereupon behold Pryderi and Rhiannon. And he rose up to meet them, and greeted them, and sat down beside them. “Ah, chieftain, set now my wife at liberty,” said the bishop. “Hast thou not received all thou didst ask?” “I will release her, gladly,” said he. And thereupon he set her free.\n\nThen he struck her with a magic wand, and she was changed back into a young woman, the fairest ever seen.\n\n“Look round upon thy land,” said he, “and thou wilt see it all tilled and peopled as it was in its best estate.” And he rose up and looked forth. And when he looked he saw all the lands tilled, and full of herds and dwellings.\n\nAnd thus ends this portion of the Mabinogi.\n\n————\n\nThe following allusions to the preceding story are found in a letter of the poet Southey to John Rickman, Esq., dated June 6th, 1802:\n\n“You will read the Mabinogeon, concerning which I ought to have talked to you. In the last, that most odd and Arabian-like story of the mouse, mention is made of a begging scholar, that helps to the date; but where did the Cymri get the imagination that could produce such a tale? That enchantment of the basin hanging by the chain from heaven is in the wildest spirit of the Arabian Nights. I am perfectly astonished that such fictions should exist in Welsh. They throw no light on the origin of romance, everything being utterly dissimilar to what we mean by that term, but they do open a new world of fiction; and if the date of their language be fixed about the twelfth or thirteenth century, I cannot but think the mythological substance is of far earlier date; very probably brought from the East by some of the first settlers or conquerors.”\n\n————\n\n# KILWICH AND OLWEN\n\nKILYDD, a son of Prince Kelyddon, desired a wife as a helpmate, and the wife that he chose was Goleudid, the daughter of Prince Anlawd. And after their union the people put up prayers that they might have an heir. And they had a son through the prayers of the people; and called his name Kilwich.\n\nAfter this the boy’s mother, Goleudid, the daughter of Prince Anlawd, fell sick. Then she called her husband to her, and said to him, “Of this sickness I shall die, and thou wilt take another wife. Now wives are the gift of the Lord, but it would be wrong for thee to harm thy son. Therefore I charge thee that thou take not a wife until thou see a briar with two blossoms upon my grave.” And this he promised her. Then she besought him to dress her grave every year, that no weeds might grow thereon. So the queen died. Now the king sent an attendant every morning to see if anything were growing upon the grave. And at the end of the seventh year they neglected that which they had promised to the queen.\n\nOne day the king went to hunt; and he rode to the place of burial, to see the grave, and to know if it were time that he should take a wife: and the king saw the briar. And when he saw it, the king took counsel where he should find a wife. Said one of his counsellors, “I know a wife that will suit thee well; and she is the wife of King Doged.” And they resolved to go to seek her; and they slew the king, and brought away his wife. And they conquered the kings’ lands. And he married the widow of King Doged, the sister of Yspadaden Penkawr.\n\nAnd one day his stepmother said to Kilwich, “It were well for thee to have a wife.” “I am not yet of an age to wed,” answered the youth. Then said she unto him, “I declare to thee that it is thy destiny not to be suited with a wife until thou obtain Olwen, the daughter of Yspadaden Penkawr.” And the youth blushed, and the love of the maiden diffused itself through all his frame, although he had never seen her. And his father inquired of him, “What has come over thee, my son, and what aileth thee?” “My stepmother has declared to me that I shall never have a wife until I obtain Olwen, the daughter of Yspadaden Penkawr.” “That will be easy for thee,” answered his father. “Arthur is thy cousin. Go, therefore, unto Arthur, to cut thy hair, and ask this of him as a boon.”\n\nAnd the youth pricked forth upon a steed with head dappled gray, four winters old, firm of limb, with shell-formed hoofs, having a bridle of linked gold on his head, and upon him a saddle of costly gold. And in the youth’s hand were two spears of silver, sharp, well-tempered, headed with steel, three ells in length, of an edge to wound the wind, and cause blood to flow, and swifter than the fall of the dew-drop from the blade of reed-grass, when the dew of June is at the heaviest. A gold-hilted sword was upon his thigh, the blade of which was gilded, bearing a cross of inlaid gold of the hue of the lightning of heaven. His war-horn was of ivory. Before him were two brindled, white-breasted greyhounds, having strong collars of rubies about their necks, reaching from the shoulder to the ear. And the one that was upon the left side bounded across to the right side, and the one on the right to the left, and, like two sea-swallows, sported around him. And his courser cast up four sods, with his four hoofs, like four swallows in the air, about his head, now above, now below. About him was a four-cornered cloth of purple, and an apple of gold was at each corner, and every one of the apples was of the value of an hundred kine. And there was precious gold of the value of three hundred kine upon his shoes, and upon his stirrups, from his knee to the tip of his toe. And the blade of grass bent not beneath him, so light was his courser’s tread, as he journeyed toward the gate of Arthur’s palace.\n\nSpoke the youth: “Is there a porter?” “There is; and if thou holdest not thy peace, small will be thy welcome. I am Arthur’s porter every first day of January.” “Open the portal.” “I will not open it.” “Wherefore not?” “The knife is in the meat, and the drink is in the horn, and there is revelry in Arthur’s hall; and none may enter therein but the son of a king of a privileged country, or a craftsman bringing his craft. But there will be refreshment for thy dogs and for thy horse; and for thee there will be collops cooked and peppered, and luscious wine, and mirthful songs; and food for fifty men shall be brought unto thee in the guest-chamber, where the stranger and the sons of other countries eat, who come not into the precincts of the palace of Arthur. Thou wilt fare no worse there than thou wouldst with Arthur in the court. A lady shall smooth thy couch, and shall lull thee with songs; and early to-morrow morning, when the gate is open for the multitude that came hither to-day, for thee shall it be opened first, and thou mayest sit in the place that thou shalt choose in Arthur’s hall, from the upper end to the lower.” Said the youth: “That will I not do. If thou openest the gate, it is well. If thou dost not open it, I will bring disgrace upon thy lord, and evil report upon thee. And I will set up three shouts at this very gate, than which none were ever heard more deadly.” “What clamor soever thou mayest make,” said Glewlwyd, the porter, “against the laws of Arthur’s palace, shalt thou not enter therein, until I first go and speak with Arthur.”\n\nThen Glewlwyd went into the hall. And Arthur said to him, “Hast thou news from the gate?” “Half of my life is passed,” said Glewlwyd, “and half of thine. I was heretofore in Kaer Se and Asse, in Sach and Salach, in Lotor and Fotor, and I have been in India the Great and India the Lesser, and I have also been in Europe and Africa, and in the islands of Corsica, and I was present when thou didst conquer Greece in the East. Nine supreme sovereigns, handsome men, saw we there, but never did I behold a man of equal dignity with him who is now at the door of the portal.” Then said Arthur: “If walking thou didst enter here, return thou running. It is unbecoming to keep such a man as thou sayest he is in the wind and the rain.” Said Kay: “By the hand of my friend, if thou wouldst follow my counsel, thou wouldst not break through the laws of the court because of him.” “Not so, blessed Kay,” said Arthur; “it is an honor to us to be resorted to, and the greater our courtesy, the greater will be our renown and our fame and our glory.”\n\nAnd Glewlwyd came to the gate, and opened the gate before Kilwich; and although all dismounted upon the horse-block at the gate, yet did he not dismount, but he rode in upon his charger. Then said he, “Greeting be unto thee, sovereign ruler of this island, and be this greeting no less unto the lowest than unto the highest, and be it equally unto thy guests, and thy warriors, and thy chieftains; let all partake of it as completely as thyself. And complete be thy favor, and thy fame, and thy glory, throughout all this island.” “Greeting unto thee also,” said Arthur; “sit thou between two of my warriors, and thou shalt have minstrels before thee, and thou shalt enjoy the privileges of a king born to a throne, as long as thou remainest here. And when I disperse my presents to the visitors and strangers in this court, they shall be in thy hand at my commencing.” Said the youth, “I came not here to consume meat and drink; but if I obtain the boon that I seek, I will requite it thee, and extol thee; but if I have it not, I will bear forth thy dispraise to the four quarters of the world, as far as thy renown has extended.” Then said Arthur, “Since thou wilt not remain here, chieftain, thou shalt receive the boon, whatsoever thy tongue may name, as far as the wind dries, and the rain moistens, and the sun revolves, and the sea encircles, and the earth extends; save only my ship Prydwen, and my mantle, and Caliburn, my sword, and Rhongomyant, my lance, and Guenever, my wife. By the truth of Heaven, thou shalt have it cheerfully, name what thou wilt.” “I would that thou bless my hair,” said he. “That shall be granted thee.”\n\nAnd Arthur took a golden comb, and scissors whereof the loops were of silver, and he combed his hair. And Arthur inquired of him who he was; “for my heart warms unto thee, and I know that thou art come of my blood. Tell me, therefore, who thou art.” “I will tell thee,” said the youth. “I am Kilwich, the son of Kilydd, the son of Prince Kelyddon, by Goleudyd, my mother, the daughter of Prince Anlawd.” “That is true,” said Arthur; “thou art my cousin. Whatsoever boon thou mayest ask, thou shalt receive, be it what it may that thy tongue shall name.” “Pledge the truth of Heaven and the faith of thy kingdom thereof.” “I pledge it thee gladly.” “I crave of thee, then, that thou obtain for me Olwen, the daughter of Yspadaden Penkawr, to wife; and this boon I likewise seek at the hands of thy warriors. I seek it from Kay and from Bedwyr; and from Gwynn, the son of Nudd, and Gadwy, the son of Geraint, and Prince Flewddur Flam, and Iona, king of France, and Sel, the son of Selgi, and Taliesin, the chief of the bards, and Geraint, the son of Erbin, Garanwyn, the son of Kay, and Amren, the son of Bedwyr, Ol, the son of Olwyd, Bedwin, the bishop, Guenever, the chief lady, and Guenhywach, her sister, Morved, the daughter of Urien, and Gwenlian Deg, the majestic maiden, Creiddylad,[68] the daughter of Lludd, the constant maiden, and Ewaedah, the daughter of Kynvelyn,[69] the half-man.” All these did Kilwich, the son of Kilydd, adjure to obtain his boon.\n\nThen said Arthur, “O chieftain, I have never heard of the maiden of whom thou speakest, nor of her kindred, but I will gladly send messengers in search of her. Give me time to seek her.” And the youth said, “I will willingly grant from this night to that at the end of the year to do so.” Then Arthur sent messengers to every land within his dominions to seek for the maiden, and at the end of the year Arthur’s messengers returned without having gained any knowledge or intelligence concerning Olwen, more than on the first day. Then said Kilwich, “Every one has received his boon, and I yet lack mine. I will depart, and bear away thy honor with me.” Then said Kay, “Rash chieftain! dost thou reproach Arthur? Go with us, and we will not part until thou dost either confess that the maiden exists not in the world, or until we obtain her.” Thereupon Kay rose up. And Arthur called Bedwyr, who never shrank from any enterprise upon which Kay was bound. None were equal to him in swiftness throughout this island except Arthur alone; and although he was one handed, three warriors could not shed blood faster than he on the field of battle.\n\nAnd Arthur called to Kyndelig, the guide, “Go thou upon this expedition with the chieftain.” For as good a guide was he in a land which he had never seen as he was in his own.\n\nHe called Gurhyr Gwalstat, because he knew all tongues.\n\nHe called Gawain, the son of Gwyar, because he never returned home without achieving the adventure of which he went in quest.\n\nAnd Arthur called Meneu, the son of Teirgwed, in order that, if they went into a savage country, he might cast a charm and an illusion over them, so that none might see them, whilst they could see every one.\n\nThey journeyed until they came to a vast open plain, wherein they saw a great castle, which was the fairest of the castles of the world. And when they came before the castle, they beheld a vast flock of sheep. And upon the top of a mound there was a herdsman keeping the sheep. And a rug made of skins was upon him, and by his side was a shaggy mastiff, larger than a steed nine winters old.\n\nThen said Kay, “Gurhyr Gwalstat, go thou and salute yonder man.” “Kay,” said he, “I engaged not to go further than thou thyself.” “Let us go then together,” answered Kay. Said Meneu, “Fear not to go thither, for I will cast a spell upon the dog, so that he shall injure no one.” And they went up to the mound whereon the herdsman was, and they said to him, “How dost thou fare, herdsman?” “Not less fair be it to you than to me.” “Whose are the sheep that thou dost keep, and to whom does yonder castle belong?” “Stupid are ye, truly! not to know that this is the castle of Yspadaden Penkawr. And ye also, who are ye?” “We are an embassy from Arthur, come to seek Olwen, the daughter of Yspadaden Penkawr.” “O men! the mercy of Heaven be upon you; do not that for all the world. None who ever came hither on this quest has returned alive.” And the herdsman rose up. And as he rose Kilwich gave unto him a ring of gold. And he went home and gave the ring to his spouse to keep. And she took the ring when it was given her, and she said, “Whence came this ring, for thou art not wont to have good fortune.” “O wife, him to whom this ring belonged thou shalt see here this evening.” “And who is he?” asked the woman. “Kilwich, the son of Kilydd, by Goleudid, the daughter of Prince Anlawd, who is come to seek Olwen as his wife.” And when she heard that, she had joy that her nephew, the son of her sister, was coming to her, and sorrow, because she had never known any one depart alive who had come on that quest.\n\nAnd the men went forward to the gate of the herdsman’s dwelling. And when she heard their footsteps approaching, she ran out with joy to meet them. And Kay snatched a billet out of the pile. And when she met them, she sought to throw her arms about their necks. And Kay placed the log between her two hands, and she squeezed it so that if became a twisted coil. “O woman,” said Kay, “if thou hadst squeezed me thus, none could ever again have set their affections on me. Evil love were this.” They entered into the house and were served; and soon after, they all went forth to amuse themselves. Then the woman opened a stone chest that was before the chimney-corner, and out of it arose a youth with yellow, curling hair. Said Gurhyr, “It is a pity to hide this youth. I know that it is not his own crime that is thus visited upon him.” “This is but a remnant,” said the woman. “Three and twenty of my sons has Yspadaden Penkawr slain, and I have no more hope of this one than of the others.” Then said Kay, “Let him come and be a companion with me, and he shall not be slain unless I also am slain with him.” And they ate. And the woman asked them, “Upon what errand come you here?” “We come to seek Olwen for this youth.” Then said the woman, “In the name of Heaven, since no one from the castle hath yet seen you, return again whence you came.” “Heaven is our witness, that we will not return until we have seen the maiden. Does she ever come hither, so that she may be seen?” “She comes here every Saturday to wash her head, and in the vessel where she washes she leaves all her rings, and she never either comes herself or sends any messengers to fetch them.” “Will she come here if she is sent to?” “Heaven knows that I will not destroy my soul, nor will I betray those that trust me; unless you will pledge me your faith that you will not harm her, I will not send to her.” “We pledge it,” said they. So a message was sent, and she came.\n\nThe maiden was clothed in a robe of flame-colored silk, and about her neck was a collar of ruddy gold, on which were precious emeralds and rubies. More yellow was her head than the flower of the broom,[70] and her skin was whiter than the foam of the wave, and fairer were her hands and her fingers than the blossoms of the wood-anemone amidst the spray of the meadow fountain. The eye of the trained hawk was not brighter than hers. Her bosom was more snowy than the breast of the white swan, her cheek was redder than the reddest roses. Whoso beheld her was filled with her love. Four white trefoils sprung up wherever she trod. And therefore was she called Olwen.\n\nShe entered the house and sat beside Kilwich upon the foremost bench; and as soon as he saw her, he knew her. And Kilwich said unto her, “Ah! maiden, thou art she whom I have loved; come away with me, lest they speak evil of thee and of me. Many a day have I loved thee.” “I cannot do this, for I have pledged my faith to my father not to go without his counsel, for his life will last only until the time of my espousals. Whatever is to be, must be. But I will give thee advice, if thou wilt take it. Go, ask me of my father, and that which he shall require of thee, grant it, and thou wilt obtain me; but if thou deny him anything, thou wilt not obtain me, and it will be well for thee if thou escape with thy life.” “I promise all this, if occasion offer,” said he.\n\nShe returned to her chamber, and they all rose up, and followed her to the castle. And they slew the nine porters, that were at the nine gates, in silence. And they slew the nine watch-dogs without one of them barking. And they went forward to the hall.\n\n“The greeting of Heaven and of man be unto thee, Yspadaden Penkawr,” said they. “And you, wherefore come you?” “We come to ask thy daughter Olwen for Kilwich, the son of Kilydd, the son of Prince Kelyddon.” “Where are my pages and my servants? Raise up the forks beneath my two eyebrows, which have fallen over my eyes, that I may see the fashion of my son-in-law.” And they did so. “Come hither to-morrow, and you shall have an answer.”\n\nThey rose to go forth, and Yspadaden Penkawr seized one of the three poisoned darts that lay beside him, and threw it after them. And Bedwyr caught it, and flung it, and pierced Yspadaden Penkawr grievously with it through the knee. Then he said, “A cursed ungentle son-in-law, truly! I shall ever walk the worse for his rudeness, and shall ever be without a cure. This poisoned iron pains me like the bite of a gad-fly. Cursed be the smith who forged it, and the anvil on which it was wrought! So sharp is it!”\n\nThat night also they took up their abode in the house of the herdsman. The next day, with the dawn, they arrayed themselves and proceeded to the castle, and entered the hall; and they said, “Yspadaden Penkawr, give us thy daughter in consideration of her dower and her maiden fee, which we will pay to thee, and to her two kinswomen likewise.” Then he said, “Her four great-grandmothers and her four great-grandsires are yet alive; it is needful that I take counsel of them.” “Be it so,” they answered, “we will go to meat.” As they rose up he took the second dart that was beside him, and cast it after them. And Meneu, the son of Gawedd, caught it, and flung it back at him, and wounded him in the centre of the breast. “A cursed ungentle son-in-law, truly!” said he; “the hard iron pains me like the bite of a horse-leech. Cursed be the hearth whereon it was heated, and the smith who formed it! So sharp is it! Henceforth, whenever I go up hill, I shall have a scant in my breath, and a pain in my chest, and I shall often loathe my food.” And they went to meat.\n\nAnd the third day they returned to the palace. And Yspadaden Penkawr said to them, “Shoot not at me again unless you desire death. Where are my attendants? Lift up the forks of my eyebrows, which have fallen over my eyeballs, that I may see the fashion of my son-in-law.” Then they arose, and, as they did so, Yspadaden Penkawr took the third poisoned dart and cast it at them. And Kilwich caught it, and threw it vigorously, and wounded him through the eyeball. “A cursed ungentle son-in-law, truly! As long as I remain alive, my eyesight will be the worse. Whenever I go against the wind, my eyes will water; and peradventure my head will burn, and I shall have a giddiness every new moon. Like the bite of a mad dog is the stroke of this poisoned iron. Cursed be the fire in which it was forged!” And they went to meat.\n\nAnd the next day they came again to the palace, and they said, “Shoot not at us any more, unless thou desirest such hurt and harm and torture as thou now hast, and even more.” Said Kilwich, “Give me thy daughter; and if thou wilt not give her, thou shalt receive thy death because of her.” “Where is he that seeks my daughter? Come hither where I may see thee.” And they placed him a chair face to face with him.\n\nSaid Yspadaden Penkawr, “Is it thou that seekest my daughter?”\n\n“It is I,” answered Kilwich.\n\n“I must have thy pledge that thou wilt not do toward me otherwise than is just; and when I have gotten that which I shall name, my daughter thou shalt have.”\n\n“I promise thee that willingly,” said Kilwich; “name what thou wilt.”\n\n“I will do so,” said he. “Seest thou yonder red tilled ground?”\n\n“I see it.”\n\n“When first I met the mother of this maiden, nine bushels of flax were sown therein, and none has yet sprung up, white nor black. I require to have the flax to sow in the new land yonder, that when it grows up it may make a white wimple for my daughter’s head on the day of thy wedding.”\n\n“It will be easy for me to compass this, although thou mayest think it will not be easy.”\n\n“Though thou get this, there is yet that which thou wilt not get—the harp of Teirtu, to play to us that night. When a man desires that it should play, it does so of itself; and when he desires that it should cease, it ceases. And this he will not give of his own free will, and thou wilt not be able to compel him.”\n\n“It will be easy for me to compass this, although thou mayest think it will not be easy.”\n\n“Though thou get this, there is yet that which thou wilt not get. I require thee to get me for my huntsman Mabon, the son of Modron. He was taken from his mother when three nights old, and it is not known where he now is, nor whether he is living or dead.”\n\n“It will be easy for me to compass this, although thou mayest think it will not be easy.”\n\n“Though thou get this, there is yet that which thou wilt not get—the two cubs of the wolf Gast Rhymhi; no leash in the world will hold them, but a leash made from the beard of Dillus Varwawc, the robber. And the leash will be of no avail unless it be plucked from his beard while he is alive. While he lives he will not suffer this to be done to him, and the leash will be of no use should he be dead, because it will be brittle.”\n\n“It will be easy for me to compass this, although thou mayest think it will not be easy.”\n\n“Though thou get this, there is yet that which thou wilt not get—the sword of Gwernach the Giant; of his own free will he will not give it, and thou wilt never be able to compel him.”\n\n“It will be easy for me to compass this, although thou mayest think it will not be easy.”\n\n“Though thou get this, there is yet that which thou wilt not get. Difficulties shalt thou meet with, and nights without sleep, in seeking this, and if thou obtain it not, neither shalt thou obtain my daughter.”\n\n“Horses shall I have, and chivalry; and my lord and kinsman, Arthur, will obtain for me all these things. And I shall gain thy daughter, and thou shalt lose thy life.”\n\n“Go forward. And thou shalt not be chargeable for food or raiment for my daughter while thou art seeking these things; and when thou hast compassed all these marvels, thou shalt have my daughter for thy wife.”\n\n————\n\nKILWICH AND OLWEN (Continued)\nALL that day they journeyed until the evening, and then they beheld a vast castle, which was the largest in the world. And lo! a black man, larger than three of the men of this world, came out from the castle. And they spoke unto him, and said, “O man, whose castle is that?” “Stupid are ye, truly, O men! There is no one in the world that does not know that this is the castle of Gwernach the Giant.” “What treatment is there for guests and strangers that alight in that castle?” “O chieftain, Heaven protect thee! No guests ever returned thence alive, and no one may enter therein unless he brings with him his craft.”\n\nThen they proceeded towards the gate. Said Gurhyr Gwalstat, “Is there a porter?” “There is; wherefore dost thou call?” “Open the gate.” “I will not open it.” “Wherefore wilt thou not?” “The knife is in the meat, and the drink is in the horn, and there is revelry in the hall of Gwernach the Giant; and except for a craftsman who brings his craft, the gate will not be opened to-night.” “Verily, porter,” then said Kay, “my craft bring I with me.” “What is thy craft?” “The best burnisher of swords am I in the world.” “I will go and tell this unto Gwernach the Giant, and I will bring thee an answer.”\n\nSo the porter went in, and Gwernach said to him, “Hast thou news from the gate?” “I have. There is a party at the door of the gate who desire to come in.” “Didst thou inquire of them if they possessed any art?” “I did inquire,” said he, “and one told me that he was well skilled in the burnishing of swords.” “We have need of him then. For some time have I sought for some one to polish my sword, and could find no one. Let this man enter, since he brings with him his craft.”\n\nThe porter thereupon returned and opened the gate. And Kay went in by himself, and he saluted Gwernach the Giant. And a chair was placed for him opposite to Gwernach. And Gwernach said to him, “O man, is it true that is reported of thee, that thou knowest how to burnish swords?” “I know full well how to do so,” answered Kay. Then was the sword of Gwernach brought to him. And Kay took a blue whetstone from under his arm, and asked whether he would have it burnished white or blue. “Do with it as it seems good to thee, or as thou wouldst if it were thine own.” Then Kay polished one half of the blade, and put it in his hand. “Will this please thee?” asked he. “I would rather than all that is in my dominions that the whole of it were like this. It is a marvel to me that such a man as thou should be without a companion.” “O noble sir, I have a companion, albeit he is not skilled in this art.” “Who may he be?” “Let the porter go forth, and I will tell him whereby he may know him. The head of the lance will leave its shaft, and draw blood from the wind, and will descend upon its shaft again.” Then the gate was opened, and Bedwyr entered. And Kay said, “Bedwyr is very skilful, though he knows not this art.”\n\nAnd there was much discourse among those who were without, because that Kay and Bedwyr had gone in. And a young man who was with them, the only son of the herdsman, got in also; and he contrived to admit all the rest, but they kept themselves concealed.\n\nThe sword was now polished, and Kay gave it unto the hand of Gwernach the Giant, to see if he were pleased with his work. And the giant said, “The work is good; I am content therewith.” Said Kay, “It is thy scabbard that hath rusted thy sword; give it to me, that I may take out the wooden sides of it, and put in new ones.” And he took the scabbard from him, and the sword in the other hand. And he came and stood over against the giant, as if he would have put the sword into the scabbard; and with it he struck at the head of the giant, and cut off his head at one blow. Then they despoiled the castle, and took from it what goods and jewels they would. And they returned to Arthur’s court, bearing with them the sword of Gwernach the Giant.\n\nAnd when they told Arthur how they had sped, Arthur said, “It is a good beginning.” Then they took counsel, and said, “Which of these marvels will it be best for us to seek next?” “It will be best,” said one, “to seek Mabon, the son of Modron; and he will not be found unless we first find Eidoel, the son of Aer, his kinsman.” Then Arthur rose up, and the warriors of the island of Britain with him, to seek for Eidoel; and they proceeded until they came to the castle of Glivi, where Eidoel was imprisoned. Glivi stood on the summit of his castle, and he said, “Arthur, what requirest thou of me, since nothing remains to me in this fortress, and I have neither joy nor pleasure in it, neither wheat nor oats? Seek not, therefore, to do me harm.” Said Arthur, “Not to injure thee came I hither, but to seek for the prisoner that is with thee.” “I will give thee my prisoner, though I had not thought to give him up to any one, and therewith shalt thou have my support and my aid.”\n\nHis followers said unto Arthur, “Lord, go thou home, thou canst not proceed with thy host in quest of such small adventures as these.” Then said Arthur, “It were well for thee, Gurhyr Gwalstat, to go upon this quest, for thou knowest all languages, and art familiar with those of the birds and the beasts. Thou, Eidoel, oughtest likewise to go with thy men in search of thy cousin. And as for you, Kay and Bedwyr, I have hope of whatever adventure ye are in quest of, that ye will achieve it. Achieve ye this adventure for me.”\n\nThey went forward until they came to the Ousel of Cilgwri. And Gurhyr adjured her, saying, “Tell me if thou knowest aught of Mabon, the son of Modron, who was taken when three nights old from between his mother and the wall?” And the Ousel answered, “When I first came here, there was a smith’s anvil in this place, and I was then a young bird; and from that time no work has been done upon it, save the pecking of my beak every evening; and now there is not so much as the size of a nut remaining thereof; yet during all that time I have never heard of the man for whom you inquire. Nevertheless, I will do that which it is fitting that I should for an embassy from Arthur. There is a race of animals who were formed before me, and I will be your guide to them.”\n\nSo they proceeded to the place where was the Stag of Redynvre. “Stag of Redynvre, behold, we are come to thee, an embassy from Arthur, for we have not heard of any animal older than thou. Say, knowest thou aught of Mabon, the son of Modron, who was taken from his mother when three nights old?” The Stag said, “When first I came hither there was a plain all around me, without any trees save one oak sapling, which grew up to be an oak with an hundred branches; and that oak has since perished, so that now nothing remains of it but the withered stump; and from that day to this I have been here, yet have I never heard of the man for whom you inquire. Nevertheless, being an embassy from Arthur, I will be your guide to the place where there is an animal which was formed before I was, and the oldest animal in the world, and the one that has travelled most, the Eagle of Gwern Abwy.”\n\nGurhyr said, “Eagle of Gwern Abwy, we have come to thee, an embassy from Arthur, to ask thee if thou knowest aught of Mabon, the son of Modron, who was taken from his mother when he was three nights old?” The Eagle said, “I have been here for a great space of time, and when I first came hither, there was a rock here from the top of which I pecked at the stars every evening; and it has crumbled away, and now it is not so much as a span high. All that time I have been here, and I have never heard of the man for whom you inquire, except once when I went in search of food as far as Llyn Llyw. And when I came there, I struck my talons into a salmon, thinking he would serve me as food for a long time. But he drew me into the water, and I was scarcely able to escape from him. After that I made peace with him. And I drew fifty fish-spears out of his back, and relieved him. Unless he know something of him whom you seek, I cannot tell who may. However, I will guide you to the place where he is.”\n\nSo they went thither; and the Eagle said, “Salmon of Llyn Llyw, I have come to thee with an embassy from Arthur, to ask thee if thou knowest aught of Mabon, the son of Modron, who was taken away at three nights old from his mother.” “As much as I know I will tell thee. With every tide I go along the river upward, until I come near to the walls of Gloucester, and there have I found such wrong as I never found elsewhere; and to the end that ye may give credence thereto, let one of you go thither upon each of my two shoulders.” So Kay and Gurhyr Gwalstat went upon the two shoulders of the Salmon, and they proceeded until they came unto the wall of the prison; and they heard a great wailing and lamenting from the dungeon. Said Gurhyr, “Who is it that laments in this house of stone?” “Alas! it is Mabon, the son of Modron, who is here imprisoned; and no imprisonment was ever so grievous as mine.” “Hast thou hope of being released for gold or for silver, or for any gifts of wealth, or through battle and fighting?” “By fighting will what ever I may gain be obtained.”\n\nThen they went thence, and returned to Arthur, and they told him where Mabon, the son of Modron, was imprisoned. And Arthur summoned the warriors of the island, and they journeyed as far as Gloucester, to the place where Mabon was in prison. Kay and Bedwyr went upon the shoulders of the fish, whilst the warriors of Arthur attacked the castle. And Kay broke through the wall into the dungeon, and brought away the prisoner upon his back, whilst the fight was going on between the warriors. And Arthur returned home, and Mabon with him at liberty.\n\nOn a certain day as Gurhyr Gwalstat was walking over a mountain, he heard a wailing and a grievous cry. And when he heard it, he sprang forward and went towards it. And when he came there, he saw a fire burning among the turf, and an ant-hill nearly surrounded with the fire. And he drew his sword, and smote off the ant-hill close to the earth, so that it escaped being burned in the fire. And the ants said to him, “Receive from us the blessing of Heaven, and that which no man can give, we give thee.” Then they fetched the nine bushels of flax-seed which Yspadaden Penkawr had required of Kilwich, and they brought the full measure, without lacking any, except one flax-seed, and that the lame pismire brought in before night.\n\nThen said Arthur, “Which of the marvels will it be best for us to seek next?” “It will be best to seek for the two cubs of the wolf Gast Rhymhi.”\n\n“Is it known,” said Arthur, “where she is?” “She is in Aber Cleddyf,” said one. Then Arthur went to the house of Tringad, in Aber Cleddyf, and he inquired of him whether he had heard of her there. “She has often slain my herds, and she is there below in a cave in Aber Cleddyf.”\n\nThen Arthur went in his ship Prydwen by sea, and the others went by land to hunt her. And they surrounded her and her two cubs, and took them and carried them away.\n\nAs Kay and Bedwyr sat on a beacon-cairn on the summit of Plinlimmon, in the highest wind that ever was, they looked around them and saw a great smoke, afar off. Then said Kay, “By the hand of my friend, yonder is the fire of a robber.” Then they hastened towards the smoke, and they came so near to it that they could see Dillus Varwawc scorching a wild boar. “Behold, yonder is the greatest robber that ever fled from Arthur,” said Bedwyr to Kay. “Dost thou know him?” “I do know him,” answered Kay; “he is Dillus Varwarc, and no leash in the world will be able to hold the cubs of Gast Rhymi, save a leash made from the beard of him thou seest yonder. And even that will be useless unless his beard be plucked out alive, with wooden tweezers; for if dead it will be brittle.” “What thinkest thou that we should do concerning this?” said Bedwyr. “Let us suffer him,” said Kay, “to eat as much as he will of the meat, and after that he will fall asleep.” And during that time they employed themselves in making the wooden tweezers. And when Kay knew certainly that he was asleep, he made a pit under his feet, and he struck him a violent blow, and squeezed him into the pit. And there they twitched out his beard completely with the wooden tweezers, and after that they slew him altogether. And from thence they went, and took the leash made of Dillus Varwawc’s beard, and they gave it into Arthur’s hand.\n\nThus they got all the marvels that Yspadaden Penkawr had required of Kilwich; and they set forward, and took the marvels to his court. And Kilwich said to Yspadaden Penkawr, “Is thy daughter mine now?” “She is thine,” said he, “but therefore needest thou not thank me, but Arthur, who hath accomplished this for thee.” Then Goreu, the son of Custennin, the herdsman, whose brothers Yspadaden Penkawr had slain, seized him by the hair of his head, and dragged him after him to the keep, and cut off his head, and placed it on a stake on the citadel. Then they took possession of his castle, and of his treasures. And that night Olwen became Kilwich’s bride, and she continued to be his wife as long as she lived.\n\n————\n\nGWYDDNO GARANHIR was sovereign of Gwaelod, a territory bordering on the sea. And he possessed a weir upon the strand between Dyvi and Aberystwyth, near to his own castle, and the value of an hundred pounds was taken in that weir every May eve. And Gwyddno had an only son named Elphin, the most hapless of youths, and the most needy. And it grieved his father sore, for he thought that he was born in an evil hour. By the advice of his council, his father had granted him the drawing of the weir that year, to see if good luck would ever befall him, and to give him something wherewith to begin the world. And this was on the twenty-ninth of April.\n\nThe next day, when Elphin went to look, there was nothing in the weir but a leathern bag upon a pole of the weir. Then said the weir-ward unto Elphin, “All thy ill-luck aforetime was nothing to this; and now thou hast destroyed the virtues of the weir, which always yielded the value of an hundred pounds every May eve; and to-night there is nothing but this leathern skin within it.” “How now,” said Elphin, “there may be therein the value of a hundred pounds.” Well! they took up the leathern bag, and he who opened it saw the forehead of an infant, the fairest that ever was seen; and he said, “Behold a radiant brow?” (In the Welsh language, taliesin.) “Taliesin be he called,” said Elphin. And he lifted the bag in his arms, and, lamenting his bad luck, placed the boy sorrowfully behind him. And he made his horse amble gently, that before had been trotting, and he carried him as softly as if he had been siting in the easiest chair in the world. And presently the boy made a Consolation, and praise to Elphin; and the Consolation was as you may here see:\n\n“Fair Elphin, cease to lament! Never in Gwyddno’s weir Was there such good luck as this night. Being sad will not avail; Better to trust in God than to forbode ill; Weak and small as I am, On the foaming beach of the ocean, In the day of trouble I shall be Of more service to thee than three hundred salmon.”\n\nThis was the first poem that Taliesin ever sung, being to console Elphin in his grief for that the produce of the weir was lost, and what was worse, that all the world would consider that it was through his fault and ill-luck. Then Elphin asked him what he was, whether man or spirit. And he sung thus:\n\n“I have been formed a comely person; Although I am but little, I am highly gifted; Into a dark leathern bag I was thrown, And on a boundless sea I was sent adrift. From seas and from mountains God brings wealth to the fortunate man.”\n\nThen came Elphin to the house of Gwyddno, his father, and Taliesin with him. Gwyddno asked him if he had had a good haul at the weir, and he told him that he had got that which was better than fish. “What was that?” said Gwyddno. “A bard,” said Elphin. Then said Gwyddno, “Alas! what will he profit thee?” And Taliesin himself replied and said, “He will profit him more than the weir ever profited thee.” Asked Gwyddno, “Art thou able to speak, and thou so little?” And Taliesin answered him, “I am better able to speak than thou to question me.” “Let me hear what thou canst say,” quoth Gwyddno. Then Taliesin sang:\n\n“Three times have I been born, I know by meditation; All the sciences of the world are collected in my breast, For I know what has been, and what hereafter will occur.”\n\nElphin gave his haul to his wife, and she nursed him tenderly and lovingly. Thenceforward Elphin increased in riches more and more, day after day, and in love and favor with the king; and there abode Taliesin until he was thirteen years old, when Elphin, son of Gwyddno, went by a Christmas invitation to his uncle, Maelgan Gwynedd, who held open court at Christmas-tide in the castle of Dyganwy, for all the number of his lords of both degrees, both spiritual and temporal, with a vast and thronged host of knights and squires. And one arose and said, “Is there in the whole world a king so great as Maelgan, or one on whom Heaven has bestowed so many gifts as upon him;—form, and beauty, and meekness, and strength, besides all the powers of the soul?” And together with these they said that Heaven had given one gift that exceeded all the others, which was the beauty, and grace, and wisdom, and modesty of his queen, whose virtues surpassed those of all the ladies and noble maidens throughout the whole kingdom. And with this they put questions one to another, Who had braver men? Who had fairer or swifter horses or greyhounds? Who had more skilful or wiser bards than Maelgan?\n\nWhen they had all made an end of their praising the king and his gifts, it befell that Elphin spoke on this wise. “Of a truth, none but a king may vie with a king; but were he not a king, I would say that my wife was as virtuous as any lady in the kingdom, and also that I have a bard who is more skilful than all the king’s bards.” In a short space some of his fellows told the king all the boastings of Elphin; and the king ordered him to be thrown into a strong prison, until he might show the truth as to the virtues of his wife, and the wisdom of his bard.\n\nNow when Elphin had been put in a tower of the castle, with a thick chain about his feet (it is said that it was a silver chain, because he was of royal blood), the king, as the story relates, sent his son Rhun to inquire into the demeanor of Elphin’s wife. Now Rhun was the most graceless man in the world, and there was neither wife nor maiden with whom he held converse but was evil spoken of. While Rhun went in haste towards Elphin’s dwelling, being fully minded to bring disgrace upon his wife, Taliesin told his mistress how that the king had placed his master in durance in prison, and how that Rhun was coming in haste to strive to bring disgrace upon her. Wherefore he caused his mistress to array one of the maids of her kitchen in her apparel; which the noble lady gladly did, and she loaded her hands with the best rings that she and her husband possessed.\n\nIn this guise Taliesin caused his mistress to put the maiden to sit at the board in her room at supper; and he made her to seem as her mistress, and the mistress to seem as the maid. And when they were in due time seated at their supper, in the manner that has been said, Rhun suddenly arrived at Elphin’s dwelling, and was received with joy, for the servants knew him; and they brought him to the room of their mistress, in the semblance of whom the maid rose up from supper and welcomed him gladly. And afterwards she sat down to supper again, and Rhun with her. Then Rhun began jesting with the maid, who still kept the semblance of her mistress. And verily this story shows that the maiden became so intoxicated that she fell asleep; and the story relates that it was a powder that Rhun put into the drink, that made her sleep so soundly that she never felt it when he cut off from her hand her little finger, whereon was the signet ring of Elphin, which he had sent to his wife as a token a short time before. And Rhun returned to the king with the finger and the ring as a proof, to show that he had cut it off from her hand without her awaking from her sleep of intemperance.\n\nThe king rejoiced greatly at these tidings, and he sent for his councillors, to whom he told the whole story from the beginning. And he caused Elphin to be brought out of prison, and he chided him because of his boast. And he spake on this wise: “Elphin, be it known to thee beyond a doubt, that it is but folly for a man to trust in the virtues of his wife further than he can see her; and that thou mayest be certain of thy wife’s vileness, behold her finger, with thy signet ring upon it, which was cut from her hand last night, while she slept the sleep of intoxication.” Then thus spake Elphin: “With thy leave, mighty king, I cannot deny my ring, for it is known of many; but verily I assert that the finger around which it is was never attached to the hand of my wife; for in truth and certainty there are three notable things pertaining to it, none of which ever belonged to any of my wife’s fingers. The first of the three is, that it is certainly known to me that this ring would never remain upon her thumb, whereas you can plainly see that it is hard to draw it over the joint of the little finger of the hand whence this was cut. The second thing is, that my wife has never let pass one Saturday since I have known her, without paring her nails before going to bed, and you can see fully that the nail of this little finger has not been pared for a month. The third is, truly, that the hand whence this finger came was kneading rye dough within three days before the finger was cut therefrom, and I can assure your highness that my wife has never kneaded rye dough since my wife she has been.”\n\nThe king was mightily wroth with Elphin for so stoutly withstanding him, respecting the goodness of his wife; wherefore he ordered him to his prison a second time, saying that he should not be loosed thence until he had proved the truth of his boast, as well concerning the wisdom of his bard as the virtues of his wife.\n\nIn the meantime his wife and Taliesin remained joyful at Elphin’s dwelling. And Taliesin showed his mistress how that Elphin was in prison because of them; but he bade her be glad, for that he would go to Maelgan’s court to free his master. So he took leave of his mistress, and came to the court of Maelgan, who was going to sit in his hall, and dine in his royal state, as it was the custom in those days for kings and princes to do at every chief feast. As soon as Taliesin entered the hall he placed himself in a quiet corner, near the place where the bards and the minstrels were wont to come, in doing their service and duty to the king, as is the custom at the high festivals, when the bounty is proclaimed. So, when the bards and the heralds came to cry largess, and to proclaim the power of the king, and his strength, at the moment when they passed by the corner wherein he was crouching, Taliesin pouted out his lips after them, and played “Blerwm, blerwm!” with his finger upon his lips. Neither took they much notice of him as they went by but proceeded forward till they came before the king, unto whom they made their obeisance with their bodies, as they were wont, without speaking a single word, but pouting out their lips, and making mouths at the king, playing, “Blerwm, blerwm!” upon their lips with their fingers, as they had seen the boy do. This sight caused the king to wonder, and to deem within himself that they were drunk with many liquors. Wherefore he commanded one of his lords, who served at the board, to go to them and desire them to collect their wits, and to consider where they stood, and what it was fitting for them to do. And this lord did so gladly. But they ceased not from their folly any more than before. Whereupon he sent to them a second time, and a third, desiring them to go forth from the hall. At the last the king ordered one of his squires to give a blow to the chief of them, named Heinin Vardd; and the squire took a broom and struck him on the head, so that he fell back in his seat. Then he arose, and went on his knees, and besought leave of the king’s grace to show that this their fault was not through want of knowledge, neither through drunkenness, but by the influence of some spirit that was in the hall. And he spoke on this wise: “O honorable king, be it known to your grace that not from the strength of drink, or of too much liquor, are we dumb, but through the influence of a spirit that sits in the corner yonder, in the form of a child.” Forthwith the king commanded the squire to fetch him; and he went to the nook where Taliesin sat, and brought him before the king, who asked him what he was, and whence he came. And he answered the king in verse:\n\n“Primary chief bard am I to Elphin, And my native country is the region of the summer stars; I have been in Asia with Noah in the ark, I have seen the destruction of Sodom and Gomorrah, I was in India when Rome was built, I have now come here to the remnant of Troia.”\n\nWhen the king and his nobles had heard the song, they wondered much, for they had never heard the like from a boy so young as he. And when the king knew that he was the bard of Elphin he bade Heinin, his first and wisest bard, to answer Taliesin, and to strive with him. But when he came he could do no other than play “Blerwm!” on his lips; and when he sent for the others of the four and twenty bards, they all did likewise, and could do no other. And Maelgan asked the boy Taliesin what was his errand, and he answered him in song:\n\n“Elphin, the son of Gwyddno, Is in the land of Artro, Secured by thirteen locks, For praising his instructor. Therefore I, Taliesin, Chief of the bards of the west, Will loosen Elphin Out of a golden fetter.”\n\nThen he sang to them a riddle:\n\n“Discover thou what is The strong creature from before the flood, Without flesh, without bone, Without vein, without blood, Without head, without feet; It will neither be older nor younger Than at the beginning. Behold how the sea whitens When first it comes, When it comes from the south, When it strikes on coasts. It is in the field, it is in the wood, But the eye cannot perceive it. One Being has prepared it, By a tremendous blast, To wreak vengeance On Maelgan Gwynedd.\n\nWhile he was thus singing his verse, there arose a mighty storm of wind, so that the king and all his nobles thought that the castle would fall upon their heads. And the king caused them to fetch Elphin in haste from his dungeon, and placed him before Taliesin. And it is said that immediately he sung a verse, so that the chains opened from about his feet.\n\nAfter that Taliesin brought Elphin’s wife before them, and showed that she had not one finger wanting. And in this manner did he set his master free from prison, and protect the innocence of his mistress, and silence the bards so that not one of them dared to say a word. Right glad was Elphin, right glad was Taliesin.\n\nNOTABLE among the names of heroes of the British race is that of Beowulf, which appeals to all English-speaking people in a very special way, since he is the one hero in whose story we may see the ideals of our English forefathers before they left their Continental home to cross to the islands of Britain.\n\nAlthough this hero had distinguished himself by numerous feats of strength during his boyhood and early youth, it was as the deliverer of Hrothgar, king of Denmark, from the monster Grendel that he first gained wide renown. Grendel was half monster and half man, and had his abode in the fen-fastnesses in the vicinity of Hrothgar’s residence. Night after night he would steal into the king’s great palace called Heorot and slay sometimes as many as thirty at one time of the knights sleeping there.\nBeowulf put himself at the head of a selected band of warriors, went against the monster, and after a terrible fight slew it. The following night Grendel’s mother, a fiend scarcely less terrible than her son, carried off one of Hrothgar’s boldest thanes. Once more Beowulf went to the help of the Danish king, followed the she-monster to her lair at the bottom of a muddy lake in the midst of the swamp, and with his good sword Hrunting and his own muscular arms broke the sea-woman’s neck.\n\nUpon his return to his own country of the Geats, loaded with honors bestowed upon him by Hrothgar, Beowulf served the king of Geatland as the latter’s most trusted counsellor and champion. When, after many years, the king fell before an enemy, the Geats unanimously chose Beowulf for their new king. His fame as a warrior kept his country free from invasion, and his wisdom as a statesman increased its prosperity and happiness.\n\nIn the fiftieth year of Beowulf’s reign, however, a great terror fell upon the land in the way of a monstrous fire-dragon, which flew forth by night from its den in the rocks, lighting up the blackness with its blazing breath, and burning houses and homesteads, men and cattle, with the flames from its mouth. When the news came to Beowulf that his people were suffering and dying, and that no warrior dared to risk his life in an effort to deliver the country from this deadly devastation, the aged king took up his shield and sword and went forth to his last fight. At the entrance of the dragon’s cave Beowulf raised his voice and shouted a furious defiance to the awesome guardian of the den. Roaring hideously and flapping his glowing wings together, the dragon rushed forth and half flew, half sprang, on Beowulf. Then began a fearful combat, which ended in Beowulf’s piercing the dragon’s scaly armor and inflicting a mortal wound, but alas! in himself being given a gash in the neck by his opponent’s poisoned fangs which resulted in his death. As he lay stretched on the ground, his head supported by Wiglaf, an honored warrior who had helped in the fight with the dragon, Beowulf roused himself to say, as he grasped Wiglaf’s hand:\n\n“Thou must now look to the needs of the nation; Here dwell I no longer, for Destiny calleth me! Bid thou my warriors after my funeral pyre Build me a burial-cairn high on the sea-cliff’s head; So that the seafarers Beowulf’s Barrow Henceforth shall name it, they who drive far and wide Over the mighty flood their foamy keels. Thou art the last of all the kindred of Wagmund! Wyrd has swept all my kin, all the brave chiefs away! Now must I follow them!”\n\nThese last words spoken, the king of the Geats, brave to seek danger and brave to look on death and Fate undaunted, fell back dead. According to his last desires, his followers gathered wood and piled it on the cliff-head. Upon this funeral pyre was laid Beowulf’s body and consumed to ashes. Then, upon the same cliff of Hronesness, was erected a huge burial cairn, widespread and lofty, to be known thereafter as Beowulf’s Barrow.\n\n————\n\n# CUCHULAIN, CHAMPION OF IRELAND\n\nAMONG all the early literatures of Europe, there are two which, at exactly opposite corners of the continent, display most strikingly similar characteristics. These are the Greek and the Irish, and the legend of the Irish champion Cuchulain, which well illustrates the similarity of the literatures, bears so close a resemblance to the story of Achilles as to win for this hero the title of “the Irish Achilles.” Certainly in reckless courage, power of inspiring dread, sense of personal merit, and frankness of speech the Irish hero is fully equal to the mighty Greek.\n\nCuchulain was the nephew of King Conor of Ulster, son of his sister Dechtire, and it is said that his father was no mortal man, but the great god Lugh of the Long Hand. Cuchulain was brought up by King Conor himself, and even while he was still a boy his fame spread all over Ireland. His warlike deeds were those of a proved warrior, not of a child of nursery age; and by the time Cuchulain was seventeen he was without peer among the champions of Ulster.\n\nUpon Cuchulain’s marriage to Emer, daughter of Forgall the Wily, a Druid of great power, the couple took up their residence at Armagh, the capital of Ulster, under the protection of King Conor. Here there was one chief, Bricriu of the Bitter Tongue, who, like Thersites among the Grecian leaders, delighted in making mischief. Soon he had on foot plans for stirring up strife among the heroes of Ulster, leaders among whom were the mighty Laegaire, Conall Cearnach, cousin of Cuchulain, and Cuchulain himself. Inviting the members of King Conor’s court to dinner, Bricriu arranged that a contest should arise over who should have the “champion’s portion,” and so successful was he that, to avoid a bloody fight, the three heroes mentioned decided to submit their claims to the championship of Ireland to King Ailill of Connaught.\n\nAilill put the heroes to an unexpected test. Their dinner was served them in a separate room, into which three magic beasts, in the shape of monstrous cats, were sent by the king. When they saw them Laegire and Conall rose from their meal, climbed among the rafters, and stayed there all night. Cuchulain waited until one cat attacked him, and then, drawing his sword, struck the monster. It showed no further sign of fight, and at daybreak the magic beasts disappeared.\n\nAs Laegire and Conall claimed that this test was an unfair one, Ailill sent the three rivals to Curoi of Kerry, a just and wise man, who set out to discover by wizardry and enchantments the best among the heroes. In turn they stood watch outside Curoi’s castle, where Laegire and Conall were overcome by a huge giant, who hurled spears of mighty oak trees, and ended by throwing them over the wall into the courtyard. Cuchulain alone withstood the giant, whereupon he was attacked by other magic foes. Among these was a dragon, which flew on horrible wings from a neighboring lake, and seemed ready to devour everything in its way. Cuchulain sprang up, giving his wonderful hero-leap, thrust his arm into the dragon’s mouth and down its throat, and tore out its heart. After the monster fell dead, he cut off its scaly head.\n\nAs even yet Cuchulain’s opponents would not admit his championship, they were all three directed to return to Armagh, to await Curoi’s judgment. Here it happened that all the Ulster heroes were in the great hall one night, except Cuchulain and his cousin Conall. As they sat in order of rank, a terrible stranger, gigantic in stature, hideous of aspect, with ravening yellow eyes, entered. In his hand he bore an enormous axe, with keen and shining edge. Upon King Conor’s inquiring his business there, the stranger replied:\n\n“Behold my axe! The man who will grasp it to-day may cut my head off with it, provided that I may, in like manner, cut off his head to-morrow. If you have no champion who dare face me, I will say that Ulster has lost her courage and is dishonored.”\n\nAt once Laegire accepted the challenge. The giant laid his head on a block, and at a blow the hero severed it from the body. Thereupon the giant arose, took the head and the axe, and thus, headless, strode from the hall. But the following night, when he returned, sound as ever, to claim the fulfilment of Laegire’s promise, the latter’s heart failed him and he did not come forward. The stranger then jeered at the men of Ulster because their great champion durst not keep his agreement, nor face the blow he should receive in return for the one he gave.\n\nThe men of Ulster were utterly ashamed, but Conall Cearnach, who was present that night, made a new agreement with the stranger. He gave a blow which beheaded the giant, but again, when the latter returned whole and sound on the following evening, the champion was not to be found.\nNow it was the turn of Cuchulain, who, as the others had done, cut off the giant’s head at one stroke. The next day the members of Conor’s court watched Cuchulain to see what he would do. They would not have been surprised if he had failed like the others, who now were present. The champion, however, showed no signs of failing or retreat. He sat sorrowfully in his place, and with a sigh said to King Conor as they waited: “Do not leave this place till all is over. Death is coming to me very surely, but I must fulfil my agreement, for I would rather die than break my word.”\n\nTowards the close of day the stranger strode into the hall exultant.\n\n“Where is Cuchulain?” he cried.\n\n“Here I am,” was the reply.\n\n“Ah, poor boy! your speech is sad to-night, and the fear of death lies heavy on you; but at least you have redeemed your word and have not failed me.”\n\nThe youth rose from his seat and went towards him, as he stood with the great axe ready, and knelt to receive the blow.\n\nThe hero of Ulster laid his head on the block; but the giant was not satisfied. “Stretch out your neck better,” said he.\n\n“You are playing with me, to torment me,” said Cuchulain. “Slay me now speedily, for I did not keep you waiting last night.”\n\nHowever, he stretched out his neck as ordered, and the stranger raised his axe till it crashed upwards through the rafters of the hall, like the crash of trees falling in a storm. When the axe came down with a terrific sound all men looked fearfully at Cuchulain. The descending axe had not even touched him; it had come down with the blunt side on the ground, and the youth knelt there unharmed. Smiling at him, and leaning on his axe, stood no terrible and hideous stranger, but Curoi of Kerry, come to give his decision at last.\n\n“Rise up, Cuchulain,” said Curoi. “There is none among all the heroes of Ulster to equal you in courage and loyalty and truth. The Championship of the Heroes of Ireland is yours from this day forth, and the Champion’s Portion at all feasts; and to your wife I adjudge the first place among all the women of Ulster. Woe to him who dares to dispute this decision!” Thereupon Curoi vanished, and the warriors gathered around Cuchulain, and all with one voice acclaimed him the Champion of the Heroes of all Ireland—a title which has clung to him until this day.\n\nThis is one of many stories told of the Irish champion, whose deeds of bravery would fill many pages. Cuchulain finally came to his end on the field of battle, after a fight in which he displayed all his usual gallantry but in which unfair means were used to overcome him.\n\nFor Wales and for England during centuries Arthur has been the representative “very gentle perfect knight.” In a similar way, in England’s sister isle, Cuchulain stands ever for the highest ideals of the Irish Gaels.\n\n————\n\n# HEREWARD THE WAKE\n\nIN Hereward the Wake (or “Watchful”) is found one of those heroes whose date can be ascertained with a fair amount of exactness and yet in whose story occur mythological elements which seem to belong to all ages. The folklore of primitive races is a great storehouse whence a people can choose tales and heroic deeds to glorify its own national hero, careless that the same tales and deeds have done duty for other peoples and other heroes. Hence it happens that Hereward the Saxon, a patriot hero as real and actual as Nelson or George Washington, whose deeds were recorded in prose and verse within forty years of his death, was even then surrounded by a cloud of romance and mystery, which hid in vagueness his family, his marriage, and even his death.\n\nBriefly it may be stated that Hereward was a native of Lincolnshire, and was in his prime about 1070. In that year he joined a party of Danes who appeared in England, attacked Peterborough and sacked the abbey there, and afterward took refuge in the Isle of Ely. Here he was besieged by William the Conqueror, and was finally forced to yield to the Norman. He thus came to stand for the defeated Saxon race, and his name has been passed down as that of the darling hero of the Saxons. For his splendid defence of Ely they forgave his final surrender to Duke William; they attributed to him all the virtues supposed to be inherent in the free-born, and all the glorious valor on which the English prided themselves; and, lastly, they surrounded his death with a halo of desperate fighting, and made his last conflict as wonderful as that of Roland at Roncesvalles. If Roland is the ideal of Norman feudal chivalry, Hereward is equally the ideal of Anglo-Saxon sturdy manliness and knighthood.\n\nAn account of one of Hereward’s adventures as a youth will serve as illustration of the stories told of his prowess. On an enforced visit to Cornwall, he found that King Alef, a petty British chief, had betrothed his fair daughter to a terrible Pictish giant, breaking off, in order to do it, her troth-plight with Prince Sigtryg of Waterford, son of a Danish king in Ireland. Hereward, ever chivalrous, picked a quarrel with the giant and killed him in fair fight, whereupon the king threw him into prison. In the following night, however, the released princess arranged that the gallant Saxon should be freed and sent hot-foot for her lover, Prince Sigtryg. After many adventures Hereward reached the prince, who hastened to return to Cornwall with the young hero. But to the grief of both, they learned upon their arrival that the princess had just been betrothed to a wild Cornish hero, Haco, and the wedding feast was to be held that very day. Sigtryg at once sent a troop of forty Danes to King Alef demanding the fulfilment of the troth-plight between himself and his daughter, and threatening vengeance if it were broken. To this threat the king returned no answer, and no Dane came back to tell of their reception.\n\nSigtryg would have waited till morning, trusting in the honor of the king, but Hereward disguised himself as a minstrel and obtained admission to the bridal feast, where he soon won applause by his beautiful singing. The bridegroom, Haco, in a rapture offered him any boon he liked to ask, but he demanded only a cup of wine from the hands of the bride. When she brought it to him he flung into the empty cup the betrothal ring, the token she had sent to Sigtryg, and said: “I thank thee, lady, and would reward thee for thy gentleness to a wandering minstrel; I give back the cup, richer than before by the kind thoughts of which it bears the token.” The princess looked at him, gazed into the goblet, and saw her ring; then, looking again, she recognized her deliverer and knew that rescue was at hand.\n\nWhile men feasted Hereward listened and talked, and found out that the forty Danes were prisoners, to be released on the morrow when Haco was sure of his bride, but released useless and miserable, since they would be turned adrift blinded. Haco was taking his lovely bride back to his own land, and Hereward saw that any rescue, to be successful, must be attempted on the march.\n\nReturning to Sigtryg, the young Saxon told all that he had learned, and the Danes planned an ambush in the ravine where Haco had decided to blind and set free his captives. The whole was carried out exactly as Hereward arranged it. The Cornishmen, with the Danish captives, passed first without attack; next came Haco, riding grim and ferocious beside his silent bride, he exulting in his success, she looking eagerly for any signs of rescue. As they passed Hereward sprang from his shelter, crying, “Upon them, Danes, and set your brethren free!” and himself struck down Haco and smote off his head. There was a short struggle, but soon the rescued Danes were able to aid their deliverers, and the Cornish guards were all slain; the men of King Alef, never very zealous for the cause of Haco, fled, and the Danes were left masters of the field.\n\nSigtryg had in the meantime seen to the safety of the princess, and now, placing her between himself and Hereward, he escorted her to the ship, which soon brought them to Waterford and a happy bridal. The Prince and Princess of Waterford always recognized in Hereward their deliverer and best friend, and in their gratitude wished him to dwell with them always; but the hero’s roving and daring temper forbade his settling down, but rather urged him on to deeds of arms in other lands, where he quickly won a renown second to none.\n\n————\n\n# ROBIN HOOD\n\nAMONG the earliest heirlooms of the Anglo-Saxon tongue are the songs and legends of Robin Hood and his merry outlaws, which have charmed readers young and old for more than six hundred years. These entertaining stories date back to the time when Chaucer wrote his “Canterbury Tales,” when the minstrel and scribe stood in the place of the more prim and precise modern printed book.\n\nThe question of whether or not Robin Hood was a real person has been asked for many years, just as a similar question has been asked about William Tell and others whom everyone would much rather accept on faith. It cannot be answered by a brief “yes” or “no,” even though learned men have pored over ancient records and have written books on the subject. According to the general belief Robin was an outlaw in the reign of Richard I, when in the depths of Sherwood Forest he entertained one hundred tall men, all good archers, with the spoil he took; but “he suffered no woman to be oppressed or otherwise molested; poore men’s goods he spared, abundantlie relieving them with that which by theft he got from abbeys and houses of rich earles.” Consequently Robin was an immense favorite with the common people.\n\nThis popularity extended from the leader to all the members of his hardy band. “God save Robin Hood and all his good yeomanry” is the ending of many old ballads. The clever archer who could outshoot his fellows, the brave yeoman inured to blows, and the man who could be true to his friends through thick and thin were favorites for all time; and they have been idealized in the persons of Robin Hood and his merry outlaws.\n\nOne of the best-known stories of this picturesque figure of early English times is that given by Sir Walter Scott in “Ivanhoe,” concerning the archery contest during the rule or misrule of Prince John, in the absence of Richard from the kingdom. Robin Hood, under the assumed name of Locksley, boldly presents himself at a royal tournament at Ashby, as competitor for the prize in shooting with the long-bow. From the eight or ten archers who enter the contest, the number finally narrows down to two,—Hubert, a forester in the service of one of the king’s nobles, and Locksley or Robin Hood. Hubert takes the first shot in the final trial of skill, and lands his arrow within the inner ring of the target, but not exactly in the centre.\n\n“‘You have not allowed for the wind, Hubert,’ said Locksley, ‘or that had been a better shot.’\n\n“So saying, and without showing the least anxiety to pause upon his aim, Locksley stepped to the appointed station, and shot his arrow as carelessly in appearance as if he had not even looked at the mark. He was speaking almost at the instant that the shaft left the bow-string, yet it alighted in the target two inches nearer to the white spot which marked the centre than that of Hubert.\n\n“‘By the light of Heaven!’ said Prince John to Hubert, ‘an thou suffer that runagate knave to overcome thee, thou art worthy of the gallows!’\n\n“Hubert had but one set speech for all occasions. ‘An your highness were to hang me,’ he said, ‘a man can but do his best. Nevertheless, my grandsire drew a good bow——’\n\n“‘The foul fiend on thy grandsire and all his generation!’ interrupted John; ‘shoot, knave, and shoot thy best, or it shall be worse for thee!’\n\n“Thus exhorted, Hubert resumed his place, and not neglecting the caution which he had received from his adversary, he made the necessary allowance for a very light air of wind, which had just risen, and shot so successfully that his arrow alighted in the very centre of the target.\n\n“‘A Hubert! a Hubert!’ shouted the populace, more interested in a known person than in a stranger. ‘In the clout!—in the clout!—a Hubert forever!’\n\n“‘Thou canst not mend that shot, Locksley,’ said the Prince, with an insulting smile.\n\n“‘I will notch his shaft for him, however,’ replied Locksley.\n\n“And letting fly his arrow with a little more precaution than before, it lighted right upon that of his competitor, which it split to shivers. The people who stood around were so astonished at his wonderful dexterity, that they could not even give vent to their surprise in their usual clamor. ‘This must be the devil, and no man of flesh and blood,’ whispered the yeomen to each other; ‘such archery was never seen since a bow was first bent in Britain.’\n\n“‘And now,’ said Locksley, ‘I will crave your Grace’s permission to plant such a mark as is used in the North Country; and welcome every brave yeoman who shall try a shot at it to win a smile from the bonny lass he loves best.’”\n\nLocksley thereupon sets up a willow wand, six feet long and as thick as a man’s thumb. Hubert is forced to decline the honor of taking part in such a trial of archery skill, but his rival easily splits the wand at a distance of three hundred feet and carries off the prize.\n\n“Even Prince John, in admiration of Locksley’s skill, lost for an instant his dislike to his person. ‘These twenty nobles,’ he said, ‘which, with the bugle, thou hast fairly won, are thine own; we will make them fifty, if thou wilt take livery and service with us as a yeoman of our bodyguard, and be near to our person. For never did so strong a hand bend a bow, or so true an eye direct a shaft.’”[71]\n\nLocksley, however, declares that it is impossible for him to enter the Prince’s service, generously shares his prize with the worthy Hubert, and retires once more to his beloved haunts among the lights and shadows of the good greenwood.\n\nThose who have investigated the origin of the romantic fables relating to Charlemagne and his peers are of opinion that the deeds of Charles Martel, and perhaps of other Charleses, have been blended in popular tradition with those properly belonging to Charlemagne. It was indeed a most momentous era; and if our readers will have patience, before entering on the perusal of the fabulous annals which we are about to lay before them, to take a rapid survey of the real history of the times, they will find it hardly less romantic than the tales of the poets.\n\nIn the century beginning from the year 600, the countries bordering upon the native land of our Saviour, to the east and south, had not yet received his religion. Arabia was the seat of an idolatrous religion resembling that of the ancient Persians, who worshipped the sun, moon, and stars. In Mecca, in the year 571, Mahomet was born, and here, at the age of forty, he proclaimed himself the prophet of God, in dignity as superior to Christ as Christ had been to Moses. Having obtained by slow degrees a considerable number of disciples, he resorted to arms to diffuse his religion. The energy and zeal of his followers, aided by the weakness of the neighboring nations, enabled him and his successors to spread the sway of Arabia and the religion of Mahomet over the countries to the east as far as the Indus, northward over Persia and Asia Minor, westward over Egypt and the southern shores of the Mediterranean, and thence over the principal portion of Spain. All this was done within one hundred years from the Hegira, or flight of Mahomet from Mecca to Medina, which happened in the year 622, and is the era from which Mahometans reckon time, as we do from the birth of Christ.\n\nFrom Spain the way was open for the Saracens (so the followers of Mahomet were called) into France, the conquest of which, if achieved, would have been followed very probably by that of all the rest of Europe, and would have resulted in the banishment of Christianity from the earth. For Christianity was not at that day universally professed, even by those nations which we now regard as foremost in civilization. Great parts of Germany, Britain, Denmark, and Russia were still pagan or barbarous.\n\nAt that time there ruled in France, though without the title of king, the first of those illustrious Charleses of whom we have spoken, Charles Martel, the grandfather of Charlemagne. The Saracens of Spain had made incursions into France in 712 and 718, and had retired, carrying with them a vast booty. In 725, Anbessa, who was then the Saracen governor of Spain, crossed the Pyrenees with a numerous army, and took by storm the strong town of Carcassone. So great was the terror excited by this invasion, that the country for a wide extent submitted to the conqueror, and a Mahometan governor for the province was appointed and installed at Narbonne. Anbessa, however, received a fatal wound in one of his engagements, and the Saracens, being thus checked from further advance, retired to Narbonne.\n\nIn 732 the Saracens again invaded France under Abdalrahman, advanced rapidly to the banks of the Garonne, and laid siege to Bordeaux. The city was taken by assault and delivered up to the soldiery. The invaders still pressed forward, and spread over the territories of Orleans, Auxerre and Sens. Their advanced parties were suddenly called in by their chief, who had received information of the rich abbey of St. Martin of Tours, and resolved to plunder and destroy it.\n\nCharles during all this time had done nothing to oppose the Saracens, for the reason that the portion of France over which their incursions had been made was not at that time under his dominion, but constituted an independent kingdom, under the name of Aquitaine, of which Eude was king. But now Charles became convinced of the danger, and prepared to encounter it. Abdalrahman was advancing toward Tours, when intelligence of the approach of Charles, at the head of an army of Franks, compelled him to fall back upon Poitiers, in order to seize an advantageous field of battle.\n\nCharles Martel had called together his warriors from every part of his dominions, and, at the head of such an army as had hardly ever been seen in France, crossed the Loire, probably at Orleans, and, being joined by the remains of the army of Aquitaine, came in sight of the Arabs in the month of October, 732. The Saracens seem to have been aware of the terrible enemy they were now to encounter, and for the first time these formidable conquerors hesitated. The two armies remained in presence during seven days before either ventured to begin the attack; but at length the signal for battle was given by Abdalrahman, and the immense mass of the Saracen army rushed with fury on the Franks. But the heavy line of the Northern warriors remained like a rock, and the Saracens, during nearly the whole day, expended their strength in vain attempts to make any impression upon them. At length, about four o’clock in the afternoon, when Abdalrahman was preparing for a new and desperate attempt to break the line of the Franks, a terrible clamor was heard in the rear of the Saracens. It was King Eude, who, with his Aquitanians, had attacked their camp, and a great part of the Saracen army rushed tumultuously from the field to protect their plunder. In this moment of confusion the line of the Franks advanced, and, sweeping the field before it, carried fearful slaughter amongst the enemy. Abdalrahman made desperate efforts to rally his troops, but when he himself, with the bravest of his officers, fell beneath the swords of the Christians, all order disappeared, and the remains of his army sought refuge in their immense camp, from which Eude and his Aquitanians had been repulsed. It was now late, and Charles, unwilling to risk an attack on the camp in the dark, withdrew his army, and passed the night in the plain, expecting to renew the battle in the morning.\n\nAccordingly, when daylight came, the Franks drew up in order of battle, but no enemy appeared; and when at last they ventured to approach the Saracen camp they found it empty. The invaders had taken advantage of the night to begin their retreat, and were already on their way back to Spain, leaving their immense plunder behind to fall into the hands of the Franks.\n\nThis was the celebrated battle of Tours, in which vast numbers of the Saracens were slain, and only fifteen hundred of the Franks. Charles received the surname of Martel (the Hammer) in consequence of this victory.\n\nThe Saracens, notwithstanding this severe blow, continued to hold their ground in the south of France; but Pepin, the son of Charles Martel, who succeeded to his father’s power, and assumed the title of king, successively took from them the strong places they held; and in 759, by the capture of Narbonne, their capital, extinguished the remains of their power in France.\n\nCharlemagne, or Charles the Great, succeeded his father, Pepin, on the throne in the year 768. This prince, though the hero of numerous romantic legends, appears greater in history than in fiction. Whether we regard him as a warrior or as a legislator, as a patron of learning or as the civilizer of a barbarous nation, he is entitled to our warmest admiration. Such he is in history; but the romancers represent him as often weak and passionate, the victim of treacherous counsellors, and at the mercy of turbulent barons, on whose prowess he depends for the maintenance of his throne. The historical representation is doubtless the true one, for it is handed down in trustworthy records, and is confirmed by the events of the age. At the height of his power, the French empire extended over what we now call France, Germany, Switzerland, Holland, Belgium, and great part of Italy.\n\nIn the year 800 Charlemagne, being in Rome, whither he had gone with a numerous army to protect the Pope, was crowned by the Pontiff Emperor of the West. On Christmas day Charles entered the Church of St. Peter, as if merely to take his part in the celebration of the mass with the rest of the congregation. When he approached the altar and stooped in the act of prayer the Pope stepped forward and placed a crown of gold upon his head; and immediately the Roman people shouted, “Life and victory to Charles the August, crowned by God the great and pacific Emperor of the Romans.” The Pope then prostrated himself before him, and paid him reverence, according to the custom established in the times of the ancient Emperors, and concluded the ceremony by anointing him with consecrated oil.\n\nCharlemagne’s wars were chiefly against the pagan and barbarous people, who, under the name of Saxons, inhabited the countries now called Hanover and Holland. He also led expeditions against the Saracens of Spain; but his wars with the Saracens were not carried on, as the romances assert, in France, but on the soil of Spain. He entered Spain by the Eastern Pyrenees, and made an easy conquest of Barcelona and Pampeluna. But Saragossa refused to open her gates to him, and Charles ended by negotiating and accepting a vast sum of gold as the price of his return over the Pyrenees.\n\nOn his way back, he marched with his whole army through the gorges of the mountains by way of the valleys of Engui, Eno, and Roncesvalles. The chief of this region had waited upon Charlemagne, on his advance, as a faithful vassal of the monarchy; but now, on the return of the Franks, he had called together all the wild mountaineers who acknowledged him as their chief, and they occupied the heights of the mountains under which the army had to pass. The main body of the troops met with no obstruction, and received no intimation of danger; but the rear-guard, which was considerably behind, and encumbered with its plunder, was overwhelmed by the mountaineers in the pass of Roncesvalles, and slain to a man. Some of the bravest of the Frankish chiefs perished on this occasion, among whom is mentioned Roland or Orlando, governor of the marches or frontier of Brittany. His name became famous in after times, and the disaster of Roncesvalles and death of Roland became eventually the most celebrated episode in the vast cycle of romance.\n\nThough after this there were hostile encounters between the armies of Charlemagne and the Saracens, they were of small account, and generally on the soil of Spain. Thus the historical foundation for the stories of the romancers is but scanty, unless we suppose the events of an earlier and of a later age to be incorporated with those of Charlemagne’s own time.\n\nThere is, however, a pretended history, which for a long time was admitted as authentic, and attributed to Turpin, Archbishop of Rheims, a real personage of the time of Charlemagne. Its title is “History of Charles the Great and Orlando.” It is now unhesitatingly considered as a collection of popular traditions, produced by some credulous and unscrupulous monk, who thought to give dignity to his romance by ascribing its authorship to a well-known and eminent individual. It introduces its pretended author, Bishop Turpin, in this manner:\n\n“Turpin, Archbishop of Rheims, the friend and secretary of Charles the Great, excellently skilled in sacred and profane literature, of a genius equally adapted to prose and verse, the advocate of the poor, beloved of God in his life and conversation, who often fought the Saracens, hand to hand, by the Emperor’s side, he relates the acts of Charles the Great in one book, and flourished under Charles and his son Louis, to the year of our Lord eight hundred and thirty.”\n\nThe titles of some of Archbishop Turpin’s chapters will show the nature of his history. They are these: “Of the Walls of Pampeluna, that fell of themselves.” “Of the War of the holy Facundus, where the Spears grew.” (Certain of the Christians fixed their spears in the evening, erect in the ground, before the castle; and found them, in the morning, covered with bark and branches.) “How the Sun stood still for Three Days, and of the Slaughter of Four Thousand Saracens.”\n\nTurpin’s history has perhaps been the source of the marvellous adventures which succeeding poets and romancers have accumulated around the names of Charlemagne and his Paladins, or Peers. But Ariosto and the other Italian poets have drawn from different sources, and doubtless often from their own invention, numberless other stories which they attribute to the same heroes, not hesitating to quote as their authority “the good Turpin,” though his history contains no trace of them; and the more outrageous the improbability, or rather the impossibility, of their narrations, the more attentive are they to cite “the Archbishop,” generally adding their testimonial to his unquestionable veracity.\n\nThe principal Italian poets who have sung the adventures of the peers of Charlemagne are Pulci, Boiardo, and Ariosto. The characters of Orlando, Rinaldo, Astolpho, Gano, and others, are the same in all, though the adventures attributed to them are different. Boiardo tells us of the loves of Orlando, Ariosto of his disappointment and consequent madness, Pulci of his death.\n\nOgier, the Dane, is a real personage. History agrees with romance in representing him as a powerful lord who, originally from Denmark and a Pagan, embraced Christianity, and took service under Charlemagne. He revolted from the Emperor, and was driven into exile. He afterwards led one of those bands of piratical Northmen which ravaged France under the reigns of Charlemagne’s degenerate successors. The description which an ancient chronicler gives of Charlemagne, as described by Ogier, is so picturesque, that we are tempted to transcribe it. Charlemagne was advancing to the siege of Pavia. Didier, King of the Lombards, was in the city with Ogier, to whom he had given refuge. When they learned that the king was approaching they mounted a high tower, whence they could see far and wide over the country. “They first saw advancing the engines of war, fit for the armies of Darius or Julius Cæsar. ‘There is Charlemagne,’ said Didier. ‘No,’ said Ogier. The Lombard next saw a vast body of soldiers, who filled all the plain. ‘Certainly Charles advanced with that host,’ said the king. ‘Not yet,’ replied Ogier. ‘What hope for us,’ resumed the king, ‘if he brings with him a greater host than that?’ At last Charles appeared, his head covered with an iron helmet, his hands with iron gloves, his breast and shoulders with a cuirass of iron, his left hand holding an iron lance, while his right hand grasped his sword. Those who went before the monarch, those who marched at his side, and those who followed him, all had similar arms. Iron covered the fields and the roads; iron points reflected the rays of the sun. This iron, so hard, was borne by a people whose hearts were harder still. The blaze of the weapons flashed terror into the streets of the city.”\n\nThis picture of Charlemagne in his military aspect would be incomplete without a corresponding one of his “mood of peace.” One of the greatest of modern historians, M. Guizot, has compared the glory of Charlemagne to a brilliant meteor, rising suddenly out of the darkness of barbarism to disappear no less suddenly in the darkness of feudalism. But the light of this meteor was not extinguished, and reviving civilization owed much that was permanently beneficial to the great Emperor of the Franks. His ruling hand is seen in the legislation of his time, as well as in the administration of the laws. He encouraged learning; he upheld the clergy, who were the only peaceful and intellectual class, against the encroaching and turbulent barons; he was an affectionate father, and watched carefully over the education of his children, both sons and daughters. Of his encouragement of learning we will give some particulars.\n\nHe caused learned men to be brought from Italy and from other foreign countries to revive the public schools of France, which had been\n\nprostrated by the disorders of preceding times. He recompensed these learned men liberally, and kept some of them near himself, honoring them with his friendship. Of these the most celebrated is Alcuin, an Englishman, whose writings still remain, and prove him to have been both a learned and a wise man. With the assistance of Alcuin, and others like him, he founded an academy or royal school, which should have the direction of the studies of all the schools of the kingdom. Charlemagne himself was a member of this academy on equal terms with the rest. He attended its meetings, and fulfilled all the duties of an academician. Each member took the name of some famous man of antiquity. Alcuin called himself Horace, another took the name of Augustin, a third of Pindar. Charlemagne, who knew the Psalms by heart, and who had an ambition to be, according to his conception, a king after God’s own heart, received from his brother academicians the name of David.\n\nOf the respect entertained for him by foreign nations an interesting proof is afforded in the embassy sent to him by the Caliph of the Arabians, the celebrated Haroun al Raschid, a prince in character and conduct not unlike to Charlemagne. The ambassadors brought with them, besides other rich presents, a clock, the first that was seen in Europe, which excited universal admiration. It had the form of a twelve-sided edifice with twelve doors. These doors formed niches, in each of which was a little statue representing one of the hours. At the striking of the hour the doors, one for each stroke, was seen to open, and from the doors to issue as many of the little statues, which, following one another, marched gravely round the tower. The motion of the clock was caused by water, and the striking was effected by balls of brass equal to the number of the hours, which fell upon a cymbal of the same metal, the number falling being determined by the discharge of the water, which, as it sunk in the vessel, allowed their escape.\n\nCharlemagne was succeeded by his son Louis, a well-intentioned but feeble prince, in whose reign the fabric reared by Charles began rapidly to crumble. Louis was followed successively by two Charleses, incapable princes, whose weak and often tyrannical conduct is no doubt the source of incidents of that character ascribed in the romances to Charlemagne.\nThe lawless and disobedient deportment of Charles’s paladins, instances of which are so frequent in the romantic legends, was also a trait of the declining empire, but not of that of Charlemagne.\n\n————\n\n# THE PEERS, OR PALADINS\n\nThe twelve most illustrious knights of Charlemagne were called Peers, for the equality that reigned among them; while the name of Paladins, also conferred on them, implies that they were inmates of the palace and companions of the king. Their names are always given alike by the romancers, yet we may enumerate the most distinguished of them as follows: Orlando or Roland (the former the Italian, the latter the French form of the name), favorite nephew of Charlemagne; Rinaldo of Montalban, cousin of Orlando; Namo, Duke of Bavaria; Salomon, king of Brittany; Turpin, the Archbishop; Astolpho, of England; Ogier, the Dane; Malagigi, the Enchanter; and Florismart, the friend of Orlando. There were others who are sometimes named as paladins, and the number cannot be strictly limited to twelve. Charlemagne himself must be counted one, and Ganelon, or Gano, of Mayence, the treacherous enemy of all the rest, was rated high on the list by his deluded sovereign, who was completely the victim of his arts.\n\nWe shall introduce more particularly to our readers a few of the principal peers, leaving the others to make their own introduction as they appear in the course of our narrative. We begin with Orlando.\n\nMilon, or Milone, a knight of great family, and distantly related to Charlemagne, having secretly married Bertha, the Emperor’s sister, was banished from France, and excommunicated by the Pope. After a long and miserable wandering on foot as mendicants Milon and his wife arrived at Sutri, in Italy, where they took refuge in a cave, and in that cave Orlando was born. There his mother continued, deriving a scanty support from the compassion of the neighboring peasants; while Milon, in quest of honor and fortune, went into foreign lands. Orlando grew up among the children of the peasantry, surpassing them all in strength and manly graces. Among his companions in age, though in station far more elevated, was Oliver, son of the governor of the town. Between the two boys a feud arose that led to a fight, in which Orlando thrashed his rival; but this did not prevent a friendship springing up between the two, which lasted through life.\n\nOrlando was so poor that he was sometimes half naked. As he was a favorite of the boys, one day four of them brought some cloth to make him clothes. Two brought white and two red; and from this circumstance Orlando took his coat-of-arms, or quarterings.\n\nWhen Charlemagne was on his way to Rome to receive the imperial crown he dined in public in Sutri. Orlando and his mother that day had nothing to eat, and Orlando coming suddenly upon the royal party, and seeing abundance of provisions, seized from the attendants as much as he could carry off, and made good his retreat in spite of their resistance. The Emperor, being told of this incident, was reminded of an intimation he had received in a dream, and ordered the boy to be followed. This was done by three of the knights, whom Orlando would have encountered with a cudgel on their entering the grotto, had not his mother restrained him. When they heard from her who she was they threw themselves at her feet, and promised to obtain her pardon from the Emperor. This was easily effected. Orlando was received into favor by the Emperor, returned with him to France, and so distinguished himself that he became the most powerful support of the throne and of Christianity.[72]\n\n# ROLAND AND FERRAGUS\n\nOrlando, or Roland, particularly distinguished himself by his combat with Ferragus. Ferragus was a giant, and moreover his skin was of such impenetrable stuff that no sword could make any impression upon it. The giant’s mode of fighting was to seize his adversary in his arms and carry him off, in spite of all the struggles he could make. Roland’s utmost skill only availed to keep him out of the giant’s clutches, but all his efforts to wound him with the sword were useless. After long fighting Ferragus was so weary that he proposed a truce, and when it was agreed upon he lay down and immediately fell asleep. He slept in perfect security, for it was against all the laws of chivalry to take advantage of an adversary under such circumstances. But Ferragus lay so uncomfortably for the want of a pillow that Orlando took pity upon him, and brought a smooth stone and placed it under his head. When the giant woke up, after a refreshing nap, and perceived what Orlando had done, he seemed quite grateful, became sociable, and talked freely in the usual boastful style of such characters. Among other things he told Orlando that he need not attempt to kill him with a sword, for that every part of his body was invulnerable, except this; and as he spoke, he put his hand to the vital part, just in the middle of his breast. Aided by this information Orlando succeeded, when the fight was renewed, in piercing the giant in the very spot he had pointed out, and giving him a death-wound. Great was the rejoicing in the Christian camp, and many the praises showered upon the victorious paladin by the Emperor and all his host.\n\nOn another occasion Orlando encountered a puissant Saracen warrior, and took from him, as the prize of victory, the sword Durindana. This famous weapon had once belonged to the illustrious prince Hector of Troy. It was of the finest workmanship, and of such strength and temper that no armor in the world could stand against it.\n\n# A ROLAND FOR AN OLIVER\n\nGuerin de Montglave held the lordship of Vienne, subject to Charlemagne. He had quarrelled with his sovereign, and Charles laid siege to his city, having ravaged the neighboring country. Guerin was an aged warrior, but relied for his defence upon his four sons and two grandsons, who were among the bravest knights of the age. After the siege had continued two months Charlemagne received tidings that Marsilius, king of Spain, had invaded France, and, finding himself unopposed, was advancing rapidly in the Southern provinces. At this intelligence Charles listened to the counsel of his peers, and consented to put the quarrel with Guerin to the decision of Heaven, by single combat between two knights, one of each party, selected by lot. The proposal was acceptable to Guerin and his sons. The names of the four, together with Guerin’s own, who would not be excused, and of the two grandsons, who claimed their lot, being put into a helmet, Oliver’s was drawn forth, and to him, the youngest of the grandsons, was assigned the honor and the peril of the combat. He accepted the award with delight, exulting in being thought worthy to maintain the cause of his family. On Charlemagne’s side Roland was the designated champion, and neither he nor Oliver knew who his antagonist was to be.\n\nThey met on an island in the Rhone, and the warriors of both camps were ranged on either shore, spectators of the battle. At the first encounter both lances were shivered, but both riders kept their seats, immovable. They dismounted, and drew their swords. Then ensued a combat which seemed so equal, that the spectators could not form an opinion as to the probable issue. Two hours and more the knights continued to strike and parry, to thrust and ward, neither showing any sign of weariness, nor ever being taken at unawares. At length Orlando struck furiously upon Oliver’s shield, burying Durindana in its edge so deeply that he could not draw it back, and Oliver, almost at the same moment, thrust so vigorously upon Orlando’s breastplate that his sword snapped off at the handle. Thus were the two warriors left weaponless. Scarcely pausing a moment, they rushed upon one another, each striving to throw his adversary to the ground, and failing in that, each snatched at the other’s helmet to tear it away. Both succeeded, and at the same moment they stood bareheaded face to face, and Roland recognized Oliver, and Oliver Roland. For a moment they stood still; and the next, with open arms, rushed into one another’s embrace. “I am conquered,” said Orlando. “I yield me,” said Oliver.\n\nThe people on the shore knew not what to make of all this. Presently they saw the two late antagonists standing hand in hand, and it was evident the battle was at an end. The knights crowded round them, and with one voice hailed them as equals in glory. If there were any who felt disposed to murmur that the battle was left undecided they were silenced by the voice of Ogier the Dane, who proclaimed aloud that all had been done that honor required, and declared that he would maintain that award against all gainsayers.\n\nThe quarrel with Guerin and his sons being left undecided, a truce was made for four days, and in that time, by the efforts of Duke Namo on the one side, and of Oliver on the other, a reconciliation was effected. Charlemagne, accompanied by Guerin and his valiant family, marched to meet Marsilius, who hastened to retreat across the frontier.\n\nRinaldo was one of the four sons of Aymon, who married Aya, the sister of Charlemagne. Thus Rinaldo was nephew to Charlemagne and cousin of Orlando.\n\nWhen Rinaldo had grown old enough to assume arms Orlando had won for himself an illustrious name by his exploits against the Saracens, whom Charlemagne and his brave knights had driven out of France. Orlando’s fame excited a noble emulation in Rinaldo. Eager to go in pursuit of glory, he wandered in the country near Paris, and one day saw at the foot of a tree a superb horse, fully equipped and loaded with a complete suit of armor. Rinaldo clothed himself in the armor and mounted the horse, but took not the sword. On the day when, with his brothers, he had received the honor of knighthood from the Emperor he had sworn never to bind a sword to his side till he had wrested one from some famous knight.\n\nRinaldo took his way to the forest of Arden, celebrated for so many adventures. Hardly had he entered it when he met an old man, bending under the weight of years, and learned from him that the forest was infested with a wild horse, untamable, that broke and overturned everything that opposed his career. To attack him, he said, or even to meet him, was certain death. Rinaldo, far from being alarmed, showed the most eager desire to combat the animal. This was the horse Bayard, afterward so famous. He had formerly belonged to Amadis of Gaul. After the death of that hero he had been held under enchantment by the power of a magician, who predicted that, when the time came to break the spell, he should be subdued by a knight of the lineage of Amadis, and not less brave than he.\n\nTo win this wonderful horse it was necessary to conquer him by force or skill; for from the moment when he should be thrown down he would become docile and manageable. His habitual resort was a cave on the borders of the forest; but woe be to any one who should approach him, unless gifted with strength and courage more than mortal. Having told this, the old man departed. He was not, in fact, an old man, but Malagigi, the enchanter, cousin of Rinaldo, who, to favor the enterprises of the young knight, had procured for him the horse and armor which he so opportunely found, and now put him in the way to acquire a horse unequalled in the world.\n\nRinaldo plunged into the forest, and spent many days in seeking Bayard, but found no traces of him. One day he encountered a Saracen knight, with whom he made acquaintance, as often happened to knights, by first meeting him in combat. This knight, whose name was Isolier, was also in quest of Bayard. Rinaldo succeeded in the encounter, and so severe was the shock that Isolier was a long time insensible. When he revived, and was about to resume the contest, a peasant who passed by (it was Malagigi) interrupted them with the news that the terrible horse was near at hand, advising them to unite their powers to subdue him, for it would require all their ability.\n\nRinaldo and Isolier, now become friends, proceeded together to the attack of the horse. They found Bayard, and stood a long time, concealed by the wood, admiring his strength and beauty.\n\nA bright bay in color (whence he was called Bayard), with a silver star in his forehead, and his hind feet white, his body slender, his head delicate, his ample chest filled out with swelling muscles, his shoulders broad and full, his legs straight and sinewy, his thick mane falling over his arching neck,—he came rushing through the forest, regardless of rocks, bushes, or trees, rending everything that opposed his way, and neighing defiance.\n\nHe first descried Isolier, and rushed upon him. The knight received him with lance in rest, but the fierce animal broke the spear, and his course was not delayed by it for an instant. The Spaniard adroitly stepped aside, and gave way to the rushing tempest. Bayard checked his career, and turned again upon the knight, who had already drawn his sword. He drew his sword, for he had no hope of taming the horse; that, he was satisfied, was impossible.\n\nBayard rushed upon him; fiercely rearing, now on this side, now on that. The knight struck him with his sword, where the white star adorned his forehead, but struck in vain, and felt ashamed, thinking that he had struck feebly, for he did not know that the skin of that horse was so tough that the keenest sword could make no impression upon it.\n\nWhistling fell the sword once more, and struck with greater force, and the fierce horse felt it, and drooped his head under the blow, but the next moment turned upon his foe with such a buffet that the Pagan fell stunned and lifeless to the earth.\n\nRinaldo, who saw Isolier fall, and thought that his life was reft, darted towards the horse, and, with his fist gave him such a blow on the jaws that the blood tinged his mouth with vermilion. Quicker than an arrow leaves the bow the horse turned upon him, and tried to seize his arm with his teeth.\n\nThe knight stepped back, and then, repeating his blow, struck him on the forehead. Bayard turned, and kicked with both his feet with a force that would have shattered a mountain. Rinaldo was on his guard, and evaded his attacks, whether made with head or heels. He kept at his side avoiding both; but, making a false step, he at last received a terrible blow from the horse’s foot, and at the shock almost fainted away. A second such blow would have killed him, but the horse kicked at random, and a second blow did not reach Rinaldo, who in a moment recovered himself. Thus the contest continued until by chance Bayard’s foot got caught between the branches of an oak. Rinaldo seized it and putting forth all his strength and address, threw him on the ground.\n\nNo sooner had Bayard touched the ground than all his rage subsided. No longer an object of terror, he became gentle and quiet, yet with dignity in his mildness.\n\nThe paladin patted his neck, stroked his breast, and smoothed his mane, while the animal neighed and showed delight to be caressed by his master. Rinaldo, seeing him now completely subdued, took the saddle and trappings from the other horse, and adorned Bayard with the spoils.\n\nRinaldo became one of the most illustrious knights of Charlemagne’s court,—indeed, the most illustrious, if we except Orlando. Yet he was not always so obedient to the Emperor’s commands as he should have been, and every fault he committed was sure to be aggravated by the malice of Gan, Duke of Maganza, the treacherous enemy of Rinaldo and all his house.\n\nAt one time Rinaldo had incurred the severe displeasure of Charlemagne, and been banished from court. Seeing no chance of being ever restored to favor, he went to Spain, and entered into the service of the Saracen king, Ivo. His brothers, Alardo, Ricardo, and Ricciardetto, accompanied him, and all four served the king so faithfully that they rose to high favor with him. The king gave them land in the mountains on the frontiers of France and Spain, and subjected all the country round to Rinaldo’s authority. There was plenty of marble in the mountains, the king furnished workmen, and they built a castle for Rinaldo, surrounded with high walls, so as to be almost impregnable. Built of white stone, and placed on the brow of a marble promontory, the castle shone like a star, and Rinaldo gave it the name of Montalban. Here he assembled his friends, many of whom were banished men like himself, and the country people furnished them with provisions in return for the protection the castle afforded. Yet some of Rinaldo’s men were lawless, and sometimes the supplies were not furnished in sufficient abundance, so that Rinaldo and his garrison got a bad name for taking by force what they could not obtain by gift; and we sometimes find Montalban spoken of as a nest of freebooters, and its defenders called a beggarly garrison.\n\nCharlemagne’s displeasure did not last long, and, at the time our history commences, Rinaldo and his brothers were completely restored to the favor of the Emperor, and none of his cavaliers served him with greater zeal and fidelity than they, throughout all his wars with the Saracens and Pagans.\n\n————\n\n# THE TOURNAMENT\n\nIt was the month of May, and the feast of Pentecost. Charlemagne had ordered magnificent festivities, and summoned to them, besides his paladins and vassals of the crown, all strangers, Christian or Saracen, then sojourning at Paris. Among the guests were King Grandonio, from Spain; and Ferrau, the Saracen, with eyes like an eagle; Orlando and Rinaldo, the Emperor’s nephews; Duke Namo; Astolpho, of England, the handsomest man living; Malagigi, the Enchanter; and Gano, of Maganza, that wily traitor, who had the art to make the Emperor think he loved him, while he plotted against him.\n\nHigh sat Charlemagne at the head of his vassals and his paladins, rejoicing in the thought of their number and their might, while all were sitting and hearing music, and feasting, when suddenly there came into the hall four enormous giants, having between them a lady of incomparable beauty, attended by a single knight. There were many ladies present who had seemed beautiful till she made her appearance, but after that they all seemed nothing. Every Christian knight turned his eyes to her, and every Pagan crowded round her, while she, with a sweetness that might have touched a heart of stone, thus addressed the Emperor:\n\n“High-minded lord, the renown of your worthiness, and of the valor of these your knights, which echoes from sea to sea, encourages me to hope that two pilgrims, who have come from the ends of the world to behold you, will not have encountered their fatigue in vain. And, before I show the motive which has brought us hither, learn that this knight is my brother Uberto, and that I am his sister Angelica. Fame has told us of the jousting this day appointed, and so the prince my brother has come to prove his valor, and to say that, if any of the knights here assembled choose to meet him in the joust, he will encounter them, one by one, at the stair of Merlin, by the Fountain of the Pine. And his conditions are these: No knight who chances to be thrown shall be allowed to renew the combat, but shall remain prisoner to my brother; but if my brother be overthrown he shall depart out of the country, leaving me as the prize of the conqueror.”\n\nNow it must be stated that this Angelica and her brother, who called himself Uberto, but whose real name was Argalia, were the children of Galafron, king of Cathay, who had sent them to be the destruction of the Christian host; for Argalia was armed with an enchanted lance, which unfailingly overthrew everything it touched, and he was mounted on a horse, a creature of magic, whose swiftness outstripped the wind. Angelica possessed also a ring which was a defence against all enchantments, and when put into the mouth rendered the bearer invisible. Thus Argalia was expected to subdue and take prisoners whatever knights should dare to encounter him; and the charms of Angelica were relied on to entice the paladins to make the fatal venture, while her ring would afford her easy means of escape.\n\nWhen Angelica ceased speaking she knelt before the king and awaited his answer, and everybody gazed on her with admiration. Orlando especially felt irresistibly drawn towards her, so that he trembled and changed countenance. Every knight in the hall was infected with the same feeling, not excepting old white-headed Duke Namo and Charlemagne himself.\n\nAll stood for a while in silence, lost in the delight of looking at her. The fiery youth Ferrau could hardly restrain himself from seizing her from the giants and carrying her away; Rinaldo turned as red as fire, while Malagigi, who had discovered by his art that the stranger was not speaking truth, muttered softly, as he looked at her, “Exquisite false creature! I will play thee such a trick for this, as will leave thee no cause to boast of thy visit.”\n\nCharlemagne, to detain her as long as possible before him, delayed his assent till he had asked her a number of questions, all which she answered discreetly, and then the challenge was accepted.\n\nAs soon as she was gone Malagigi consulted his book, and found out the whole plot of the vile, infidel king, Galafron, as we have explained it, so he determined to seek the damsel and frustrate her designs. He hastened to the appointed spot, and there found the prince and his sister in a beautiful pavilion, where they lay asleep, while the four giants kept watch. Malagigi took his book and cast a spell out of it, and immediately the four giants fell into a deep sleep. Drawing his sword (for he was a belted knight), he softly approached the young lady, intending to despatch her at once; but, seeing her look so lovely, he paused for a moment, thinking there was no need of hurry, as he believed his spell was upon her, and she could not wake. But the ring which she wore secured her from the effect of the spell, and some slight noise, or whatever else it was, caused her at that moment to awake. She uttered a great cry, and flew to her brother, and waked him. By the help of her knowledge of enchantment, they took and bound fast the magician, and, seizing his book, turned his arts against himself. Then they summoned a crowd of demons, and bade them seize their prisoner and bear him to King Galafron, at his great city of Albracca, which they did, and, on his arrival, he was locked up in a rock under the sea.\n\nWhile these things were going on all was uproar at Paris, since Orlando insisted upon being the first to try the adventure at the stair of Merlin. This was resented by the other pretenders to Angelica, and all contested his right to the precedence. The tumult was stilled by the usual expedient of drawing lots, and the first prize was drawn by Astolpho. Ferrau, the Saracen, had the second, and Grandonio the third. Next came Berlinghieri, and Otho; then Charles himself, and, as his ill-fortune would have it, after thirty more, the indignant Orlando.\n\nAstolpho, who drew the first lot, was handsome, brave, and rich. But, whether from heedlessness or want of skill, he was an unlucky jouster, and very apt to be thrown, an accident which he bore with perfect good-humor, always ready to mount again and try to mend his fortune, generally with no better success.\n\nAstolpho went forth upon his adventure with great gayety of dress and manner, encountered Argalia, and was immediately tilted out of the saddle. He railed at fortune, to whom he laid all the fault; but his painful feelings were somewhat relieved by the kindness of Angelica, who, touched by his youth and good looks, granted him the liberty of the pavilion, and caused him to be treated with all kindness and respect.\n\nThe violent Ferrau had the next chance in the encounter, and was thrown no less speedily than Astolpho; but he did not so easily put up with his mischance. Crying out, “What are the emperor’s engagements to me?” he rushed with his sword against Argalia, who, being forced to defend himself, dismounted and drew his sword, but got so much the worse of the fight that he made a signal of surrender, and, after some words, listened to a proposal of marriage from Ferrau to his sister. The beauty, however, feeling no inclination to match with such a rough and savage-looking person, was so dismayed at the offer, that, hastily bidding her brother to meet her in the forest of Arden, she vanished from the sight of both by means of the enchanted ring. Argalia, seeing this, took to his horse of swiftness, and dashed away in the same direction. Ferrau pursued him, and Astolpho, thus left to himself, took possession of the enchanted lance in place of his own, which was broken, not knowing the treasure he possessed in it, and returned to the tournament. Charlemagne, finding the lady and her brother gone, ordered the jousting to proceed as at first intended, in which Astolpho, by aid of the enchanted lance, unhorsed all comers against him, equally to their astonishment and his own.\n\nThe paladin Rinaldo, on learning the issue of the combat of Ferrau and the stranger, galloped after the fair fugitive in an agony of love and impatience. Orlando, perceiving his disappearance, pushed forth in like manner; and, at length, all three are in the forest of Arden, hunting about for her who is invisible.\n\nNow in this forest there were two fountains, the one constructed by the sage Merlin, who designed it for Tristram and the fair Isoude;[73] for such was the virtue of this fountain, that a draught of its waters produced an oblivion of the love which the drinker might feel, and even produced aversion for the object formerly beloved. The other fountain was endowed with exactly opposite qualities, and a draught of it inspired love for the first living object that was seen after tasting it. Rinaldo happened to come to the first mentioned fountain, and, being flushed with heat, dismounted, and quenched in one draught both his thirst and his passion. So far from loving Angelica as before he hated her from the bottom of his heart, became disgusted with the search he was upon, and, feeling fatigued with his ride, finding a sheltered and flowery nook, laid himself down and fell asleep.\n\nShortly after came Angelica, but, approaching in a different direction, she espied the other fountain, and there quenched her thirst. Then resuming her way, she came upon the sleeping Rinaldo. Love instantly seized her, and she stood rooted to the spot.\n\nThe meadow round was all full of lilies of the valley and wild roses. Angelica, not knowing what to do, at length plucked a handful of these, and dropped them, one by one, on the face of the sleeper. He woke up, and, seeing who it was, received her salutations with averted countenance, remounted his horse, and galloped away. In vain the beautiful creature followed and called after him, in vain asked him what she had done to be so despised. Rinaldo disappeared, leaving her in despair, and she returned in tears to the spot where she had found him sleeping. There, in her turn, she herself lay down, pressing the spot of earth on which he had lain, and, out of fatigue and sorrow, fell asleep.\n\nAs Angelica thus lay, fortune conducted Orlando to the same place. The attitude in which she was sleeping was so lovely that it is not to be conceived, much less expressed. Orlando stood gazing like a man who had been transported to another sphere. “Am I on earth,” he exclaimed, “or am I in Paradise? Surely it is I that sleep, and this is my dream.”\n\nBut his dream was proved to be none in a manner which he little desired. Ferrau, who had slain Argalia, came up, raging with jealousy, and a combat ensued which awoke the sleeper.\n\nTerrified at what she beheld, she rushed to her palfrey, and, while the fighters were occupied with one another, fled away through the forest. The champions continued their fight till they were interrupted by a messenger, who brought word to Ferrau that king Marsilius, his sovereign, was in pressing need of his assistance, and conjured him to return to Spain. Ferrau, upon this, proposed to suspend the combat, to which Orlando, eager to pursue Angelica, agreed. Ferrau, on the other hand, departed with the messenger to Spain.\n\nOrlando’s quest for the fair fugitive was all in vain. Aided by the powers of magic, she made a speedy return to her own country.\n\nBut the thought of Rinaldo could not be banished from her mind, and she determined to set Malagigi at liberty, and to employ him to win Rinaldo, if possible, to make her a return of affection. She accordingly freed him from his dungeon, unlocking his fetters with her own hands, and restored him his book, promising him ample honors and rewards on condition of his bringing Rinaldo to her feet.\n\nMalagigi accordingly, with the aid of his book, called up a demon, mounted him, and departed. Arrived at his destination, he inveigled Rinaldo into an enchanted bark, which conveyed him, without any visible pilot, to an island where stood an edifice called Joyous Castle. The whole island was a garden. On the western side, close to the sea, was the palace, built of marble, so clear and polished that it reflected the landscape about it. Rinaldo leapt ashore, and soon met a lady, who invited him to enter. The house was as beautiful within as without, full of rooms adorned with azure and gold, and with noble paintings. The lady led the knight into an apartment painted with stories, and opening to the garden, through pillars of crystal, with golden capitals. Here he found a bevy of ladies, three of whom were singing in concert, while another played on an instrument of exquisite accord, and the rest danced round about them. When the ladies beheld him coming they turned the dance into a circuit round him, and then one of them, in the sweetest manner, said, “Sir knight, the tables are set, and the hour for the banquet is come;” and, with these words, still dancing, they drew him across the lawn in front of the apartment, to a table that was spread with cloth of gold and fine linen, under a bower of damask roses by the side of a fountain.\n\nFour ladies were already seated there, who rose, and placed Rinaldo at their head, in a chair set with pearls. And truly indeed was he astonished. A repast ensued, consisting of viands the most delicate, and wines as fragrant as they were fine, drunk out of jewelled cups; and, when it drew towards its conclusion, harps and lutes were heard in the distance, and one of the ladies said in the knight’s ear: “This house and all that you see in it are yours; for you alone was it built, and the builder is a queen. Happy indeed must you think yourself, for she loves you, and she is the greatest beauty in the world! Her name is Angelica.”\n\nThe moment Rinaldo heard the name he so detested he started up, with a changed countenance, and, in spite of all that the lady could say, broke off across the garden, and never ceased hastening till he reached the place where he landed. The bark was still on the shore. He sprang into it, and pushed off, though he saw nobody in it but himself. It was in vain for him to try to control its movements, for it dashed on as if in fury, till it reached a distant shore covered with a gloomy forest. Here Rinaldo, surrounded by enchantments of a very different sort from those which he had lately resisted, was entrapped into a pit.\n\nThe pit belonged to a castle called Altaripa, which was hung with human heads, and painted red with blood. As the paladin was viewing the scene with amazement a hideous old woman made her appearance at the edge of the pit, and told him that he was destined to be thrown to a monster, who was only kept from devastating the whole country by being supplied with living human flesh. Rinaldo said, “Be it so; let me but remain armed as I am, and I fear nothing.” The old woman laughed in derision. Rinaldo remained in the pit all night, and the next morning was taken to the place where the monster had his den. It was a court surrounded by a high wall. Rinaldo was shut in with the beast, and a terrible combat ensued. Rinaldo was unable to make any impression on the scales of the monster, while he, on the contrary, with his dreadful claws, tore away plate and mail from the paladin. Rinaldo began to think his last hour was come, and cast his eyes around and above to see if there was any means of escape. He perceived a beam projecting from the wall at the height of some ten feet, and, taking a leap almost miraculous, he succeeded in reaching it, and in flinging himself up across it. Here he sat for hours, the hideous brute continually trying to reach him. All at once he heard the sound of something coming through the air like a bird, and suddenly Angelica herself alighted on the end of the beam. She held something in her hand towards him, and spoke to him in a loving voice. But the moment Rinaldo saw her he commanded her to go away, refused all her offers of assistance, and at length declared that, if she did not leave him, he would cast himself down to the monster, and meet his fate.\nAngelica, saying she would lose her life rather than displease him, departed; but first she threw to the monster a cake of wax she had prepared, and spread around him a rope knotted with nooses. The beast took the bait, and, finding his teeth glued together by the wax, vented his fury in bounds and leaps, and, soon getting entangled in the nooses, drew them tight by his struggles, so that he could scarcely move a limb.\n\nRinaldo, watching his chance, leapt down upon his back, seized him round the neck, and throttled him, not relaxing his grip till the beast fell dead.\nAnother difficulty remained to be overcome. The walls were of immense height, and the only opening in them was a grated window of such strength that he could not break the bars. In his distress Rinaldo found a file, which Angelica had left on the ground, and, with the help of this, effected his deliverance.\n\nWhat further adventures he met with will be told in another chapter.\n\n————\n\n# THE SIEGE OF ALBRACCA\n\nAt the very time when Charlemagne was holding his plenary court and his great tournament his kingdom was invaded by a mighty monarch, who was moreover so valiant and strong in battle that no one could stand against him. He was named Gradasso, and his kingdom was called Sericane. Now, as it often happens to the greatest and the richest to long for what they cannot have, and thus to lose what they already possess, this king could not rest content without Durindana, the sword of Orlando, and Bayard, the horse of Rinaldo. To obtain these he determined to war upon France, and for this purpose put in array a mighty army.\n\nHe took his way through Spain, and, after defeating Marsilius, the king of that country, in several battles, was rapidly advancing on France. Charlemagne, though Marsilius was a Saracen, and had been his enemy, yet felt it needful to succor him in this extremity from a consideration of common danger, and, with the consent of his peers, despatched Rinaldo with a strong body of soldiers against Gradasso.\n\nThere was much fighting, with doubtful results, and Gradasso was steadily advancing into France. But, impatient to achieve his objects, he challenged Rinaldo to single combat, to be fought on foot, and upon these conditions: If Rinaldo conquered, Gradasso agreed to give up all his prisoners and return to his own country; but if Gradasso won the day, he was to have Bayard.\n\nThe challenge was accepted, and would have been fought had it not been for the arts of Malagigi, who just then returned from Angelica’s kingdom with set purpose to win Rinaldo to look with favor upon the fair princess who was dying for love of him. Malagigi drew Rinaldo away from the army by putting on the semblance of Gradasso, and, after a short contest, pretending to fly before him, by which means Rinaldo was induced to follow him into a boat, in which he was borne away, and entangled in various adventures, as we have already related.\n\nThe army, left under the command of Ricciardetto, Rinaldo’s brother, was soon joined by Charlemagne and all his peerage, but experienced a disastrous rout, and the Emperor and many of his paladins were taken prisoners. Gradasso, however, did not abuse his victory; he took Charles by the hand, seated him by his side, and told him he warred only for honor. He renounced all conquests, on condition that the Emperor should deliver to him Bayard and Durindana, both of them the property of his vassals, the former of which, as he maintained, was already forfeited to him by Rinaldo’s failure to meet him as agreed. To these terms Charlemagne readily acceded.\n\nBayard, after the departure of his master, had been taken in charge by Ricciardetto, and sent back to Paris, where Astolpho was in command, in the absence of Charlemagne. Astolpho received with great indignation the message despatched for Bayard, and replied by a herald that “he would not surrender the horse of his kinsman Rinaldo without a contest. If Gradasso wanted the steed he might come and take him, and that he, Astolpho, was ready to meet him in the field.”\n\nGradasso was only amused at this answer, for Astolpho’s fame as a successful warrior was not high, and Gradasso willingly renewed with him the bargain which he had made with Rinaldo. On these conditions the battle was fought. The enchanted lance, in the hands of Astolpho, performed a new wonder; and Gradasso, the terrible Gradasso, was unhorsed.\n\nHe kept his word, set free his prisoners, and put his army on the march to return to his own country, renewing his oath, however, not to rest till he had taken from Rinaldo his horse, and from Orlando his sword, or lost his life in the attempt.\n\nCharlemagne, full of gratitude to Astolpho, would have kept him near his person and loaded him with honors, but Astolpho preferred to seek Rinaldo, with the view of restoring to him his horse, and departed from Paris with that design.\n\n————\n\nOur story now returns to Orlando, whom we left fascinated with the sight of the sleeping beauty, who, however, escaped him while engaged in the combat with Ferrau. Having long sought her in vain through the recesses of the wood, he resolved to follow her to her father’s court. Leaving, therefore, the camp of Charlemagne, he travelled long in the direction of the East, making inquiry everywhere, if, perchance, he might get tidings of the fugitive. After many adventures, he arrived one day at a place where many roads crossed, and meeting there a courier, he asked him for news. The courier replied that he had been despatched by Angelica to solicit the aid of Sacripant, king of Circassia, in favor of her father Galafron, who was besieged in his city, Albracca, by Agrican, king of Tartary. This Agrican had been an unsuccessful suitor to the damsel, whom he now pursued with arms. Orlando thus learned that he was within a day’s journey of Albracca; and, feeling now secure of Angelica, he proceeded with all speed to her city.\n\nThus journeying he arrived at a bridge, under which flowed a foaming river. Here a damsel met him with a goblet, and informed him that it was the usage of this bridge to present the traveller with a cup. Orlando accepted the offered cup and drank its contents. He had no sooner done so than his brain reeled, and he became unconscious of the object of his journey, and of everything else. Under the influence of this fascination he followed the damsel into a magnificent and marvellous palace. Here he found himself in company with many knights, unknown to him and to each other, though if it had not been for the Cup of Oblivion of which they all had partaken they would have found themselves brothers in arms.\n\n————\n\nAstolpho, proceeding on his way to seek Rinaldo, splendidly dressed and equipped, as was his wont, arrived in Circassia, and found there a great army encamped under the command of Sacripant, the king of that country, who was leading it to the defence of Galafron, the father of Angelica. Sacripant, much struck by the appearance of Astolpho and his horse, accosted him courteously, and tried to enlist him in his service; but Astolpho, proud of his late victories, scornfully declined his offers, and pursued his way. King Sacripant was too much attracted by his appearance to part with him so easily, and having laid aside his kingly ornaments, set out in pursuit of him.\n\nAstolpho next day encountered on his way a stranger knight, named Sir Florismart, Lord of the Sylvan Tower, one of the bravest and best of knights, having as his guide a damsel, young, fair, and virtuous, to whom he was tenderly attached, whose name was Flordelis. Astolpho, as he approached, defied the knight, bidding him yield the lady, or prepare to maintain his right by arms. Florismart accepted the contest, and the knights encountered. Florismart was unhorsed and his steed fell dead, while Bayard sustained no injury by the shock.\n\nFlorismart was so overwhelmed with despair at his own disgrace and the sight of the damsel’s distress, that he drew his sword, and was about to plunge it into his own bosom. But Astolpho held his hand, told him that he contended only for glory, and was contented to leave him the lady.\n\nWhile Florismart and Flordelis were vowing eternal gratitude King Sacripant arrived, and coveting the damsel of the one champion as much as the horse and arms of the other, defied them to the joust. Astolpho met the challenger, whom he instantly overthrew, and presented his courser to Florismart, leaving the king to return to his army on foot.\n\nThe friends pursued their route, and ere long Flordelis discovered, by signs which were known to her, that they were approaching the waters of Oblivion, and advised them to turn back, or to change their course. This the knights would not hear of, and, continuing their march, they soon arrived at the bridge where Orlando had been taken prisoner.\n\nThe damsel of the bridge appeared as before with the enchanted cup, but Astolpho, forewarned, rejected it with scorn. She dashed it to the ground, and a fire blazed up which rendered the bridge unapproachable. At the same moment the two knights were assailed by sundry warriors, known and unknown, who, having no recollection of anything, joined blindly in defence of their prison-house. Among these was Orlando, at sight of whom Astolpho, with all his confidence not daring to encounter him, turned and fled, owing his escape to the strength and fleetness of Bayard.\n\nFlorismart, meanwhile, overlaid by fearful odds, was compelled to yield to necessity, and comply with the usage of the fairy. He drank of the cup and remained prisoner with the rest. Flordelis, deprived of her two friends, retired from the scene, and devoted herself to untiring efforts to effect her lover’s deliverance. Astolpho pursued his way to Albracca, which Agrican was about to besiege. He was kindly welcomed by Angelica, and enrolled among her defenders. Impatient to distinguish himself, he one night sallied forth alone, arrived in Agrican’s camp, and unhorsed his warriors right and left by means of the enchanted lance. But he was soon surrounded and overmatched, and made prisoner to Agrican.\n\nRelief was, however, at hand; for as the citizens and soldiers were one day leaning over their walls they descried a cloud of dust, from which horsemen were seen to prick forth, as it rolled on towards the camp of the besiegers. This turned out to be the army of Sacripant, which immediately attacked that of Agrican, with the view of cutting a passage through his camp to the besieged city. But Agrican, mounted upon Bayard, taken from Astolpho, but not armed with the lance of gold, the virtues of which were unknown to him, performed wonders, and rallied his scattered troops, which had given way to the sudden and unexpected assault. Sacripant, on the other hand, encouraged his men by the most desperate acts of valor, having as an additional incentive to his courage the sight of Angelica, who showed herself upon the city walls.\nThere she witnessed a single combat between the two leaders, Agrican and Sacripant. In this, at length, her defender appeared to be overmatched, when the Circassians broke the ring, and separated the combatants, who were borne asunder in the rush. Sacripant, severely wounded, profited by the confusion, and escaped into Albracca, where he was kindly received and carefully tended by Angelica.\n\nThe battle continuing, the Circassians were at last put to flight, and, being intercepted between the enemy’s lines and the town, sought for refuge under the walls. Angelica ordered the drawbridge to be let down, and the gates thrown open to the fugitives. With these Agrican, not distinguished in the crowd, entered the place, driving both Circassians and Cathayans before him, and the portcullis being dropped, he was shut in.\n\nFor a time the terror which he inspired put to flight all opposers, but when at last it came to be known that few or none of his followers had effected an entrance with him, the fugitives rallied and surrounded him on all sides. While he was thus apparently reduced to the last extremities, he was saved by the very circumstance which threatened him with destruction. The soldiers of Angelica, closing upon him from all sides, deserted their defences; and his own besieging army entered the city in a part where the wall was broken down.\n\nIn this way was Agrican rescued, the city taken, and the inhabitants put to the sword. Angelica, however, with some of the knights who were her defenders, among whom was Sacripant, saved herself in the citadel, which was planted upon a rock.\n\nThe fortress was impregnable, but it was scantily victualled, and ill provided with other necessaries. Under these circumstances Angelica announced to those blockaded with her in the citadel her intention to go in quest of assistance, and, having plighted her promise of a speedy return, she set out, with the enchanted ring upon her finger. Mounted upon her palfrey, the damsel passed through the enemy’s lines, and by sunrise was many miles clear of their encampment.\n\nIt so happened that her road led her near the fatal bridge of Oblivion, and as she approached it she met a damsel weeping bitterly. It was Flordelis, whose lover, Florismart, as we have related, had met the fate of Orlando and many more, and fallen a victim to the enchantress of the cup. She related her adventures to Angelica, and conjured her to lend what aid she might to rescue her lord and his companions. Angelica, accordingly, watching her opportunity and aided by her ring, slipped into the castle unseen, when the door was opened to admit a new victim. Here she speedily disenchanted Orlando and the rest by a touch of her talisman. But Florismart was not there. He had been given up to Falerina, a more powerful enchantress, and was still in durance. Angelica conjured the rescued captives to assist her in the recovery of her kingdom, and all departed together for Albracca.\n\nThe arrival of Orlando, with his companions, nine in all, and among the bravest knights of France, changed at once the fortunes of the war. Wherever the great paladin came, pennon and standard fell before him. Agrican in vain attempted to rally his troops. Orlando kept constantly in his front, forcing him to attend to nobody else. The Tartar king at length bethought him of a stratagem. He turned his horse, and made a show of flying in despair. Orlando dashed after him as he desired, and Agrican fled till he reached a green place in a wood, where there was a fountain.\n\nThe place was beautiful, and the Tartar dismounted to refresh himself at the fountain, but without taking off his helmet, or laying aside any of his armor. Orlando was quickly at his back, crying out, “So bold, and yet a fugitive! How could you fly from a single arm and think to escape?”\n\nThe Tartar king had leaped on his saddle the moment he saw his enemy, and when the paladin had done speaking, he said in a mild voice, “Without doubt you are the best knight I ever encountered, and fain would I leave you untouched for your own sake, if you would cease to hinder me from rallying my people. I pretended to fly, in order to bring you out of the field. If you insist upon fighting I must needs fight and slay you, but I call the sun in the heavens to witness I would rather not. I should be very sorry for your death.”\n\nThe Count Orlando felt pity for so much gallantry, and he said, “The nobler you show yourself the more it grieves me to think that in dying without a knowledge of the true faith you will be lost in the other world. Let me advise you to save body and soul at once. Receive baptism, and go your way in peace.”\n\nAgrican replied: “I suspect you to be the paladin Orlando. If you are I would not lose this opportunity of fighting with you to be king of Paradise. Talk to me no more about your things of another world, for you will preach in vain. Each of us for himself, and let the sword be umpire.”\n\nThe Saracen drew his sword, boldly advancing upon Orlando, and a combat began, so obstinate and so long, each warrior being a miracle of prowess, that the story says it lasted from noon till night. Orlando then seeing the stars come out was the first to propose a respite.\n\n“What are we to do,” said he, “now that daylight has left us?”\n\nAgrican answered readily enough, “Let us repose in this meadow, and renew the combat at dawn.”\n\nThe repose was taken accordingly. Each tied up his horse, and reclined himself on the grass, not far from the other, just as if they had been friends, Orlando by the fountain, Agrican beneath a pine. It was a beautiful clear night, and, as they talked together before addressing themselves to sleep, the champion of Christendom, looking up at the firmament, said, “That is a fine piece of workmanship, that starry spectacle; God made it all, that moon of silver, and those stars of gold, and the light of day, and the sun,—all for the sake of human kind.”\n\n“You wish, I see, to talk of matters of faith,” said the Tartar. “Now I may as well tell you at once that I have no sort of skill in such matters, nor learning of any kind. I never could learn anything when I was a boy. I hated it so that I broke the man’s head who was commissioned to teach me; and it produced such an effect on others that nobody ever afterwards dared so much as show me a book. My boyhood was\n\ntherefore passed, as it should be, in horsemanship and hunting, and learning to fight. What is the good of a gentleman’s poring all day over a book? Prowess to the knight, and preaching to the clergyman, that is my motto.”\n\n“I acknowledge,” returned Orlando, “that arms are the first consideration of a gentleman; but not at all that he does himself dishonor by knowledge. On the contrary, knowledge is as great an embellishment of the rest of his attainments, as the flowers are to the meadow before us; and as to the knowledge of his Maker, the man that is without it is no better than a stock or a stone or a brute beast. Neither without study can he reach anything of a due sense of the depth and divineness of the contemplation.”\n\n“Learned or not learned,” said Agrican, “you might show yourself better bred than by endeavoring to make me talk on a subject on which you have me at a disadvantage. If you choose to sleep I wish you good night; but if you prefer talking I recommend you to talk of fighting or of fair ladies. And, by the way, pray tell me, are you not that Orlando who makes such a noise in the world? And what is it, pray, that brings you into these parts? Were you ever in love? I suppose you must have been; for to be a knight, and never to have been in love, would be like being a man without a heart in his breast.”\n\nThe count replied: “Orlando I am, and in love I am. Love has made me abandon everything, and brought me into these distant regions, and, to tell you all in one word, my heart is in the hands of the daughter of King Galafron. You have come against him with fire and sword, to get possession of his castles and his dominions; and I have come to help him, for no object in the world but to please his daughter and win her beautiful hand. I care for nothing else in existence.”\n\nNow when the Tartar king, Agrican, heard his antagonist speak in this manner, and knew him to be indeed Orlando, and to be in love with Angelica, his face changed color for grief and jealousy, though it could not be seen for the darkness. His heart began beating with such violence that he felt as if he should have died. “Well,” said he to Orlando, “we are to fight when it is daylight, and one or other is to be left here, dead on the ground. I have a proposal to make to you—nay, an entreaty. My love is so excessive for the same lady that I beg you to leave her to me. I will owe you my thanks, and give up the siege and put an end to the war. I cannot bear that any one should love her, and that I should live to see it. Why, therefore, should either of us perish? Give her up. Not a soul shall know it.”\n\n“I never yet,” answered Orlando, “made a promise which I did not keep, and nevertheless I own to you that, were I to make a promise like that, and even swear to keep it, I should not. You might as well ask me to tear away the limbs from my body, and the eyes out of my head. I could as well live without breath itself as cease loving Angelica.”\n\nAgrican had hardly patience to let him finish speaking, ere he leapt furiously on horseback, though it was midnight. “Quit her,” said he, “or die!”\n\nOrlando seeing the infidel getting up, and not being sure that he would not add treachery to fierceness, had been hardly less quick in mounting for the combat. “Never,” exclaimed he; “I never could have quitted her if I would, and now I would not if I could. You must seek her by other means than these.”\n\nFiercely dashed their horses together, in the nighttime, on the green mead. Despiteful and terrible were the blows they gave and took by the moonlight. Agrican fought in a rage, Orlando was cooler. And now the struggle had lasted more than five hours, and day began to dawn, when the Tartar king, furious to find so much trouble given him, dealt his enemy a blow sharp and violent beyond conception. It cut the shield in two as if it had been made of wood, and, though blood could not be drawn from Orlando, because he was fated, it shook and bruised him as if it had started every joint in his body.\n\nHis body only, however, not a particle of his soul. So dreadful was the blow which the paladin gave in return, that not only shield, but every bit of mail on the body of Agrican was broken in pieces, and three of his ribs cut asunder.\n\nThe Tartar, roaring like a lion, raised his sword with still greater vehemence than before, and dealt a blow on the paladin’s helmet, such as he had never yet received from mortal man. For a moment it took away his senses. His sight failed, his ears tingled, his frightened horse turned about to fly; and he was falling from the saddle, when the very action of falling threw his head upwards, and thus recalled his recollection.\n\n“What a shame is this!” thought he; “how shall I ever again dare to face Angelica! I have been fighting hour after hour with this man, and he is but one, and I call myself Orlando! If the combat last any longer I will bury myself in a monastery, and never look on sword again.”\n\nOrlando muttered with his lips closed and his teeth ground together; and you might have thought that fire instead of breath came out of his nose and mouth. He raised his sword Durindana with both his hands, and sent it down so tremendously on Agrican’s shoulder that it cut through breastplate down to the very haunch, nay, crushed the saddle-bow, though it was made of bone and iron, and felled man and horse to the earth. Agrican turned as white as ashes, and felt death upon him. He called Orlando to come close to him, with a gentle voice, and said, as well as he could: “I believe on Him who died on the cross. Baptize me, I pray thee, with the fountain, before my senses are gone. I have lived an evil life, but need not be rebellious to God in death also. May He who came to save all the rest of the world save me!” And he shed tears, that great king, though he had been so lofty and fierce.\n\nOrlando dismounted quickly, with his own face in tears. He gathered the king tenderly in his arms, and took and laid him by the fountain, on a marble rim that it had, and then he wept in concert with him heartily, and asked his pardon, and so baptized him in the water of the fountain, and knelt and prayed to God for him with joined hands.\n\nHe then paused and looked at him; and when he perceived his countenance changed, and that his whole person was cold, he left him there on the marble rim of the fountain, all armed as he was, with the sword by his side, and the crown upon his head.\n\n————\n\n# ADVENTURES OF RINALDO AND ORLANDO\n\nWE left Rinaldo when, having overcome the monster, he quitted the castle of Altaripa, and pursued his way on foot. He soon met with a weeping damsel, who, being questioned as to the cause of her sorrow, told him she was in search of one to do battle to rescue her lover, who had been made prisoner by a vile enchantress, together with Orlando and many more. The damsel was Flordelis, the lady-love of Florismart, and Rinaldo promised his assistance, trusting to accomplish the adventure either by valor or skill. Flordelis insisted upon Rinaldo’s taking her horse, which he consented to do, on condition of her mounting behind him.\n\nAs they rode on through a wood, they heard strange noises, and Rinaldo, reassuring the damsel, pressed forward towards the quarter from which they proceeded. He soon perceived a giant standing under a vaulted cavern, with a huge club in his hand, and of an appearance to strike the boldest spirit with dread. By the side of the cavern was chained a griffin, which, together with the giant, was stationed there to guard a wonderful horse, the same which was once Argalia’s. This horse was a creature of enchantment, matchless in vigor, speed, and form, which disdained to share the diet of his fellow-steeds,—corn or grass,—and fed only on air. His name was Rabican.\n\nThis marvellous horse, after his master Argalia had been slain by Ferrau, finding himself at liberty, returned to his native cavern, and was here stabled under the protection of the giant and the griffin. As Rinaldo approached, the giant assailed him with his club. Rinaldo defended himself from the giant’s blows, and gave him one in return, which, if his skin had not been of the toughest, would have finished the combat. But the giant, though wounded, escaped, and let loose the griffin. This monstrous bird towered in air, and thence pounced down upon Rinaldo, who, watching his opportunity, dealt her a desperate wound. She had, however, strength for another flight, and kept repeating her attacks, which Rinaldo parried as he could, while the damsel stood trembling by, witnessing the contest.\n\nThe battle continued, rendered more terrible by the approach of night, when Rinaldo determined upon a desperate expedient to bring it to a conclusion. He fell, as if fainting from his wounds, and, on the close approach of the griffin, dealt her a blow which sheared away one of her wings. The beast, though sinking, gripped him fast with her talons, digging through plate and mail; but Rinaldo plied his sword in utter desperation, and at last accomplished her destruction.\n\nRinaldo then entered the cavern, and found there the wonderful horse, all caparisoned. He was coal-black, except for a star of white on his forehead, and one white foot behind. For speed he was unrivalled, though in strength he yielded to Bayard. Rinaldo mounted upon Rabican, and issued from the cavern.\n\nAs he pursued his way he met a fugitive from Agrican’s army, who gave such an account of the prowess of a champion who fought on the side of Angelica, that Rinaldo was persuaded this must be Orlando, though at a loss to imagine how he could have been freed from captivity. He determined to repair to the scene of the contest to satisfy his curiosity, and Flordelis, hoping to find Florismart with Orlando, consented to accompany him.\n\nWhile these things were doing, all was rout and dismay in the Tartarian army, from the death of Agrican. King Galafron, arriving at this juncture with an army for the relief of his capital, Albracca, assaulted the enemy’s camp, and carried all before him. Rinaldo had now reached the scene of action, and was looking on as an unconcerned spectator, when he was espied by Galafron. The king instantly recognized the horse Rabican, which he had given to Argalia when he sent him forth on his ill-omened mission to Paris. Possessed with the idea that the rider of the horse was the murderer of Argalia, Galafron rode at Rinaldo, and smote him with all his force. Rinaldo was not slow to avenge the blow, and it would have gone hard with the king had not his followers instantly closed round him and separated the combatants.\n\nRinaldo thus found himself, almost without his own choice, enlisted on the side of the enemies of Angelica, which gave him no concern, so completely had his draught from the fountain of hate steeled his mind against her.\n\nFor several successive days the struggle continued, without any important results, Rinaldo meeting the bravest knights of Angelica’s party, and defeating them one after the other. At length he encountered Orlando, and the two knights bitterly reproached one another for the cause they had each adopted, and engaged in a furious combat. Orlando was mounted upon Bayard, Rinaldo’s horse, which Agrican had by chance become possessed of, and Orlando had taken from him as the prize of victory. Bayard would not fight against his master, and Orlando was getting the worse of the encounter, when suddenly Rinaldo, seeing Astolpho, who for love of him had arrayed himself on his side, hard beset by numbers, left Orlando to rush to the defence of his friend. Night prevented the combat from being renewed; but a challenge was given and accepted for their next meeting.\n\nBut Angelica, sighing in her heart for Rinaldo, was not willing that he should be again exposed to so terrible a venture. She begged a boon of Orlando, promising she would be his if he would do her bidding. On receiving his promise, she enjoined him to set out without delay to destroy the garden of the enchantress Falerina, in which many valiant knights had been entrapped, and were imprisoned.\n\nOrlando departed on his horse Brigliadoro, leaving Bayard in disgrace for his bad deportment the day before. Angelica, to conciliate Rinaldo, sent Bayard to him; but Rinaldo remained unmoved by this as by all her former acts of kindness.\n\nWhen Rinaldo learned Orlando’s departure, he yielded to the entreaties of the lady of Florismart, and prepared to fulfil his promise, and rescue her lover from the power of the enchantress. Thus both Rinaldo and Orlando were bound upon the same adventure, but unknown to one another.\n\nThe castle of Falerina was protected by a river, which was crossed by a bridge, kept by a ruffian, who challenged all comers to the combat; and such was his strength that he had thus far prevailed in every encounter, as appeared by the arms of various knights which he had taken from them, and piled up as a trophy on the shore. Rinaldo attacked him, but with as bad success as the rest, for the bridge-ward struck him so violent a blow with an iron mace that he fell to the ground. But when the villain approached to strip him of his armor, Rinaldo seized him, and the bridge-ward, being unable to free himself, leapt with Rinaldo into the lake, where they both disappeared.\n\nOrlando, meanwhile, in discharge of his promise to Angelica, pursued his way in quest of the same adventure. In passing through a wood he saw a cavalier armed at all points, and mounted, keeping guard over a lady who was bound to a tree, weeping bitterly. Orlando hastened to her relief, but was exhorted by the knight not to interfere, for she had deserved her fate by her wickedness. In proof of which he made certain charges against her. The lady denied them all, and Orlando believed her, defied the knight, overthrew him, and, releasing the lady, departed with her seated on his horse’s croup.\n\nWhile they rode another damsel approached on a white palfrey, who warned Orlando of impending danger, and informed him that he was near the garden of the enchantress. Orlando was delighted with the intelligence, and entreated her to inform him how he was to gain admittance. She replied that the garden could only be entered at sunrise and gave him such instructions as would enable him to gain admittance. She gave him also a book in which was painted the garden and all that it contained, together with the palace of the false enchantress, where she had secluded herself for the purpose of executing a magic work in which she was engaged. This was the manufacture of a sword capable of cutting even through enchanted substances. The object of this labor, the damsel told him, was the destruction of a knight of the west, by name Orlando, who she had read in the book of Fate was coming to demolish her garden. Having thus instructed him, the damsel departed.\n\nOrlando, finding he must delay his enterprise till the next morning, now lay down and was soon asleep. Seeing this, the base woman whom he had rescued, and who was intent on making her escape to rejoin her paramour, mounted Brigliadoro, and rode off, carrying away Durindana.\n\nWhen Orlando awoke, his indignation, as may be supposed, was great on the discovery of the theft; but, like a good knight and true, he was not to be diverted from his enterprise. He tore off a huge branch of an elm to supply the place of his sword; and, as the sun rose, took his way towards the gate of the garden, where a dragon was on his watch. This he slew by repeated blows, and entered the garden, the gate of which closed behind him, barring retreat. Looking round him, he saw a fair fountain, which overflowed into a river, and in the centre of the fountain a figure, on whose forehead was written:\n\n“The stream which waters violet and rose, From hence to the enchanted palace goes.”\n\nFollowing the banks of this flowing stream, and rapt in the delights of the charming garden, Orlando arrived at the palace, and entering it, found the mistress, clad in white, with a crown of gold upon her head, in the act of viewing herself in the surface of the magic sword. Orlando surprised her before she could escape, deprived her of the weapon, and holding her fast by her long hair, which floated behind, threatened her with immediate death if she did not yield up her prisoners, and afford him the means of egress. She, however, was firm of purpose, making no reply, and Orlando, unable to move her either by threats or entreaties, was under the necessity of binding her to a beech, and pursuing his quest as he best might.\n\nHe then bethought him of his book, and, consulting it, found that there was an outlet to the south, but that to reach it a lake was to be passed, inhabited by a siren, whose song was so entrancing as to be quite irresistible to whoever heard it; but his book instructed him how to protect himself against this danger. According to its directions, while pursuing his path, he gathered abundance of flowers, which sprung all around, and filled his helmet and his ears with them; then listened if he heard the birds sing. Finding that, though he saw the gaping beak, the swelling throat, and ruffled plumes, he could not catch a note, he felt satisfied with his defence, and advanced toward the lake. It was small but deep, and so clear and tranquil that the eye could penetrate to the bottom.\n\nHe had no sooner arrived upon the banks than the waters were seen to gurgle, and the siren, rising midway out of the pool, sung so sweetly that birds and beasts came trooping to the water-side to listen. Of this Orlando heard nothing, but, feigning to yield to the charm, sank down upon the bank. The siren issued from the water with the intent to accomplish his destruction. Orlando seized her by the hair, and while she sang yet louder (song being her only defence) cut off her head. Then, following the directions of the book, he stained himself all over with her blood.\n\nGuarded by this talisman, he met successively all the monsters set for defence of the enchantress and her garden, and at length found himself again at the spot where he had made captive the enchantress, who still continued fastened to the beech. But the scene was changed. The garden had disappeared, and Falerina, before so haughty, now begged for mercy, assuring him that many lives depended upon the preservation of hers. Orlando promised her life upon her pledging herself for the deliverance of her captives.\n\nThis, however, was no easy task. They were not in her possession, but in that of a much more powerful enchantress, Morgana, the Lady of the Lake, the very idea of opposing whom made Falerina turn pale with fear. Representing to him the hazards of the enterprise, she led him towards the dwelling of Morgana. To approach it he had to encounter the same uncourteous bridge-ward who had already defeated and made captive so many knights, and last of all, Rinaldo. He was a churl of the most ferocious character, named Arridano. Morgana had provided him with impenetrable armor, and endowed him in such a manner that his strength always increased in proportion to that of the adversary with whom he was matched. No one had ever yet escaped from the contest, since, such was his power of endurance, he could breathe freely under water. Hence, having grappled with a knight, and sunk with him to the bottom of the lake, he returned, bearing his enemy’s arms in triumph to the surface.\n\nWhile Falerina was repeating her cautions and her counsels Orlando saw Rinaldo’s arms erected in form of a trophy, among other spoils made by the villain, and, forgetting their late quarrel, determined upon revenging his friend. Arriving at the pass, the churl presuming to bar the way, a desperate contest ensued, during which Falerina escaped. The churl finding himself over-matched at a contest of arms, resorted to his peculiar art, grappled his antagonist, and plunged with him into the lake. When he reached the bottom Orlando found himself in another world, upon a dry meadow, with the lake overhead, through which shone the beams of our sun, while the water stood on all sides like a crystal wall. Here the battle was renewed, and Orlando had in his magic sword an advantage which none had hitherto possessed. It had been tempered by Falerina so that no spells could avail against it. Thus armed, and countervailing the strength of his adversary by his superior skill and activity, it was not long before he laid him dead upon the field.\n\nOrlando then made all haste to return to the upper air, and, passing through the water, which opened a way before him (such was the power of the magic sword), he soon regained the shore, and found himself in a field as thickly covered with precious stones as the sky is with stars.\nOrlando crossed the field, nor tempted to delay his enterprise by gathering any of the brilliant gems spread all around him. He next passed into a flowery meadow planted with trees, covered with fruit and flowers, and full of all imaginable delights.\n\nIn the middle of this meadow was a fountain, and fast by it lay Morgana asleep; a lady of a lovely aspect, dressed in white and vermilion garments, her forehead well furnished with hair, while she had scarcely any behind.\n\nWhile Orlando stood in silence contemplating her beauty he heard a voice exclaim: “Seize the fairy by the forelock, if thou hopest fair success.” But his attention was arrested by another object, and he heeded not the warning. He saw on a sudden an array of towers, pinnacles and columns, palaces with balconies and windows, extended alleys with trees, in short a scene of architectural magnificence surpassing all he had ever beheld. While he stood gazing in silent astonishment the scene slowly melted away and disappeared.[74]\n\nWhen he had recovered from his amazement he looked again toward the fountain. The fairy had awaked and risen, and was dancing round its border with the lightness of a leaf, timing her footsteps to this song:\n\n“Who in this world would wealth and treasure share, Honor, delight, and state, and what is best, Quick let him catch me by the lock of hair Which flutters from my forehead; and be blest.\n“But let him not the proffered good forbear, Nor till he seize the fleeting blessing rest; For present loss is sought in vain to-morrow, And the deluded wretch is left in sorrow.”\n\nThe fairy, having sung thus, bounded off, and fled from the flowery meadow over a high and inaccessible mountain. Orlando pursued her through thorns and rocks, while the sky gradually became overcast, and at last he was assailed by tempest, lightning, and hail.\n\nWhile he thus pursued, a pale and meagre woman issued from a cave, armed with a whip, and, treading close upon his steps, scourged him with vigorous strokes. Her name was Repentance, and she told him it was her office to punish those who neglected to obey the voice of Prudence, and seize the fairy Fortune when he might.\n\nOrlando, furious at this chastisement, turned upon his tormentor, but might as well have stricken the wind. Finding it useless to resist, he resumed his chase of the fairy, gained upon her, and made frequent snatches at her white and vermilion garments, which still eluded his grasp. At last, on her turning her head for an instant, he profited by the chance, and seized her by the forelock. In an instant the tempest ceased, the sky became serene, and Repentance retreated to her cave.\n\nOrlando now demanded of Morgana the keys of her prison, and the fairy, feigning a complacent aspect, delivered up a key of silver, bidding him to be cautious in the use of it, since to break the lock would be to involve himself and all in inevitable destruction; a caution which gave the Count room for long meditation, and led him to consider\n\nHow few amid the suitors who importune The dame, know how to turn the keys of Fortune.\n\nKeeping the fairy still fast by the forelock, Orlando proceeded toward the prison, turned the key, without occasioning the mischiefs apprehended, and delivered the prisoners.\n\nAmong these were Florismart, Rinaldo, and many others of the bravest knights of France. Morgana had disappeared, and the knights, under the guidance of Orlando, retraced the path by which he had come. They soon reached the field of treasure. Rinaldo, finding himself amidst this mass of wealth, remembered his needy garrison of Montalban, and could not resist the temptation of seizing part of the booty. In particular a golden chain, studded with diamonds, was too much for his self-denial, and he took it and was bearing it off, notwithstanding the remonstrances of Orlando, when a violent wind caught him and whirled him back, as he approached the gate. This happened a second and a third time, and Rinaldo at length yielded to necessity, rather than to the entreaties of his friends, and cast away his prize.\n\nThey soon reached the bridge and passed over without hindrance to the other side, where they found the trophy decorated with their arms. Here each knight resumed his own, and all, except the paladins and their friends, separated as their inclinations or duty prompted. Dudon, the Dane, one of the rescued knights, informed the cousins that he had been made prisoner by Morgana while in the discharge of an embassy to them from Charlemagne, who called upon them to return to the defence of Christendom. Orlando was too much fascinated by Angelica to obey this summons, and, followed by the faithful Florismart, who would not leave him, returned towards Albracca. Rinaldo, Dudon, Iroldo, Prasildo, and the others took their way toward the west.\n\n————\n\n# THE INVASION OF FRANCE\n\nAgramant, King of Africa, convoked the kings, his vassals, to deliberate in council. He reminded them of the injuries he had sustained from France, that his father had fallen in battle with Charlemagne, and that his early years had hitherto not allowed him to wipe out the stain of former defeats. He now proposed to them to carry war into France.\n\nSobrino, his wisest councillor, opposed the project, representing the rashness of it; but Rodomont, the young and fiery king of Algiers, denounced Sobrino’s counsel as base and cowardly, declaring himself impatient for the enterprise. The king of the Garamantes, venerable for his age and renowned for his prophetic lore, interposed, and assured the King that such an attempt would be sure to fail, unless he could first get on his side a youth marked out by destiny as the fitting compeer of the most puissant knights of France, the young Rogero, descended in direct line from Hector of Troy. This prince was now a dweller upon the mountain Carena, where Atlantes, his foster-father, a powerful magician, kept him in retirement, having discovered by his art that his pupil would be lost to him if allowed to mingle with the world. To break the spells of Atlantes, and draw Rogero from his retirement, one only means was to be found. It was a ring possessed by Angelica, Princess of Cathay, which was a talisman against all enchantments. If this ring could be procured all would go well; without it the enterprise was desperate.\n\nRodomont treated this declaration of the old prophet with scorn, and it would probably have been held of little weight by the council, had not the aged king, oppressed by the weight of years, expired in the very act of reaffirming his prediction. This made so deep an impression on the council that it was unanimously resolved to postpone the war until an effort should be made to win Rogero to the camp.\n\nKing Agramant thereupon proclaimed that the sovereignty of a kingdom should be the reward of whoever should succeed in obtaining the ring of Angelica. Brunello, the dwarf, the subtlest thief in all Africa, undertook to procure it.\n\nIn prosecution of this design, he made the best of his way to Angelica’s kingdom, and arrived beneath the walls of Albracca while the besieging army was encamped before the fortress. While the attention of the garrison was absorbed by the battle that raged below he scaled the walls, approached the Princess unnoticed, slipped the ring from her finger, and escaped unobserved. He hastened to the seaside, and, finding a vessel ready to sail, embarked, and arrived at Biserta, in Africa. Here he found Agramant impatient for the talisman which was to foil the enchantments of Atlantes and to put Rogero into his hands. The dwarf, kneeling before the king, presented him with the ring, and Agramant, delighted at the success of his mission, crowned him in recompense King of Tingitana.\n\nAll were now anxious to go in quest of Rogero. The cavalcade accordingly departed, and in due time arrived at the mountain of Carena.\n\nAt the bottom of this was a fruitful and well-wooded plain, watered by a large river, and from this plain was descried a beautiful garden on the mountain-top, which contained the mansion of Atlantes; but the ring, which discovered what was before invisible, could not, though it revealed this paradise, enable Agramant or his followers to enter it. So steep and smooth was the rock by nature, that even Brunello failed in every attempt to scale it. He did not, for this, despair of accomplishing the object; but, having obtained Agramant’s consent, caused the assembled courtiers and knights to celebrate a tournament upon the plain below. This was done with the view of seducing Rogero from his fastness, and the stratagem was attended with success.\n\nRogero joined the tourney, and was presented by Agramant with a splendid horse, Frontino, and a magnificent sword. Having learned from Agramant his intended invasion of France, he gladly consented to join the expedition.\n\nRodomont, meanwhile, was too impatient to wait for Agramant’s arrangements, and embarked with all the forces he could raise, made good his landing on the coast of France, and routed the Christians in several encounters. Previously to this, however, Gano, or Ganelon (as he is sometimes called), the traitor, enemy of Orlando and the other nephews of Charlemagne, had entered into a traitorous correspondence with Marsilius, the Saracen king of Spain, whom he invited into France. Marsilius, thus encouraged, led an army across the frontiers, and joined Rodomont. This was the situation of things when Rinaldo and the other knights who had obeyed the summons of Dudon set forward on their return to France.\n\nWhen they arrived at Buda in Hungary they found the king of that country about despatching his son, Ottachiero, with an army to the succor of Charlemagne. Delighted with the arrival of Rinaldo, he placed his son and troops under his command. In due time the army arrived on the frontiers of France, and, united with the troops of Desiderius, king of Lombardy, poured down into Provence. The confederate armies had not marched many days through this gay tract before they heard a crash of drums and trumpets behind the hills, which spoke the conflict between the paynims, led by Rodomont, and the Christian forces. Rinaldo, witnessing from a mountain the prowess of Rodomont, left his troops in charge of his friends, and galloped towards him with his lance in rest. The impulse was irresistible, and Rodomont was unhorsed. But Rinaldo, unwilling to avail himself of his advantage, galloped back to the hill, and having secured Bayard among the baggage, returned to finish the combat on foot.\n\nDuring this interval the battle had become general, the Hungarians were routed, and Rinaldo, on his return, had the mortification to find that Ottachiero was wounded, and Dudon taken prisoner. While he sought Rodomont in order to renew the combat a new sound of drums and trumpets was heard, and Charlemagne, with the main body of his army, was descried advancing in battle array.\n\nRodomont, seeing this, mounted the horse of Dudon, left Rinaldo, who was on foot, and galloped off to encounter this new enemy.\n\nAgramant, accompanied by Rogero, had by this time made good his landing, and joined Rodomont with all his forces. Rogero eagerly embraced this first opportunity of distinguishing himself, and spread terror wherever he went, encountering in turn and overthrowing many of the bravest knights of France. At length he found himself opposite to Rinaldo, who, being interrupted, as we have said, in his combat with Rodomont, and unable to follow him, being on foot, was shouting to his late foe to return and finish their combat. Rogero also was on foot, and seeing the Christian knight so eager for a contest, proffered himself to supply the place of his late antagonist. Rinaldo saw at a glance that the Moorish prince was a champion worthy of his arm, and gladly accepted the defiance. The combat was stoutly maintained for a time; but now fortune declared decisively in favor of the infidel army, and Charlemagne’s forces gave way at all points in irreparable confusion. The two combatants were separated by the crowd of fugitives and pursuers, and Rinaldo hastened to recover possession of his horse. But Bayard, in the confusion, had got loose, and Rinaldo followed him into a thick wood, thus becoming effectually separated from Rogero.\n\nRogero, also seeking his horse in the medley, came where two warriors were engaged in mortal combat. Though he knew not who they were, he could distinguish that one was a paynim and the other a Christian; and moved by the spirit of courtesy he approached them and exclaimed, “Let him of the two who worships Christ pause, and hear what I have to say. The army of Charles is routed and in flight, so that if he wishes to follow his leader he has no time for delay.” The Christian knight, who was none other than Bradamante, a female warrior, in prowess equal to the best of knights, was thunderstruck with the tidings, and would gladly leave the contest undecided, and retire from the field; but Rodomont, her antagonist, would by no means consent. Rogero, indignant at his discourtesy, insisted upon her departure, while he took up her quarrel with Rodomont.\n\nThe combat, obstinately maintained on both sides, was interrupted by the return of Bradamante. Finding herself unable to overtake the fugitives, and reluctant to leave to another the burden and risk of a contest which belonged to herself, she had returned to reclaim the combat. She arrived, however, when her champion had dealt his enemy such a blow as obliged him to drop both his sword and bridle. Rogero, disdaining to profit by his adversary’s defenceless situation, sat apart upon his horse, while that of Rodomont bore his rider, stunned and stupefied, about the field.\n\nBradamante approached Rogero, conceiving a yet higher opinion of his valor on beholding such an instance of forbearance. She addressed him, excusing herself for leaving him exposed to an enemy from his interference in her cause; pleading her duty to her sovereign as the motive. While she spoke Rodomont, recovered from his confusion, rode up to them. His bearing was, however, changed; and he disclaimed all thoughts of further contest with one who, he said, “had already conquered him by his courtesy.” So saying, he quitted his antagonist, picked up his sword, and spurred out of sight.\n\nBradamante was now again desirous of retiring from the field, and Rogero insisted on accompanying her, though yet unaware of her sex.\n\nAs they pursued their way, she inquired the name and quality of her new associate; and Rogero informed her of his nation and family. He told her that Astyanax, the son of Hector of Troy, established the kingdom of Messina in Sicily. From him were derived two branches, which gave origin to two families of renown. From one sprang the royal race of Pepin and Charlemagne, and from the other, that of Reggio, in Italy. “From that of Reggio am I derived,” he continued. “My mother, driven from her home by the chance of war, died in giving me life, and I was taken in charge by a sage enchanter, who trained me to feats of arms amidst the dangers of the desert and the chase.”\n\nHaving thus ended his tale, Rogero entreated a similar return of courtesy from his companion, who replied, without disguise, that she was of the race of Clermont, and sister to Rinaldo, whose fame was perhaps known to him. Rogero, much moved by this intelligence, entreated her to take off her helmet, and at the discovery of her face remained transported with delight.\n\nWhile absorbed in this contemplation, an unexpected danger assailed them. A party which was placed in a wood, in order to intercept the retreating Christians, broke from its ambush upon the pair, and Bradamante, who was uncasqued, was wounded in the head. Rogero was in a fury at this attack; and Bradamante, replacing her helmet, joined him in taking speedy vengeance on their enemies. They cleared the field of them, but became separated in the pursuit, and Rogero, quitting the chase, wandered by hill and vale in search of her whom he had no sooner found than lost.\n\nWhile pursuing this quest he fell in with two knights, whom he joined, and engaged them to assist him in the search of his companion, describing her arms, but concealing, from a certain feeling of jealousy, her quality and sex.\n\nIt was evening when they joined company, and having ridden together through the night the morning was beginning to break, when one of the strangers, fixing his eyes upon Rogero’s shield, demanded of him by what right he bore the Trojan arms. Rogero declared his origin and race, and then, in his turn, interrogated the inquirer as to his pretensions to the cognizance of Hector, which he bore. The stranger replied, “My name is Mandricardo, son of Agrican, the Tartar king, whom Orlando treacherously slew. I say treacherously, for in fair fight he could not have done it. It is in search of him that I have come to France, to take vengeance for my father, and to wrest from him Durindana, that famous sword, which belongs to me, and not to him.” When the knights demanded to know by what right he claimed Durindana, Mandricardo thus related his history:\n\n“I had been, before the death of my father, a wild and reckless youth. That event awakened my energies, and drove me forth to seek for vengeance. Determined to owe success to nothing but my own exertions, I departed without attendants or horse or arms. Travelling thus alone, and on foot, I espied one day a pavilion, pitched near a fountain, and entered it, intent on adventure. I found therein a damsel of gracious aspect, who replied to my inquiries that the fountain was the work of a fairy, whose castle stood beyond a neighboring hill, where she kept watch over a treasure which many knights had tried to win, but fruitlessly, having lost their life or liberty in the attempt. This treasure was the armor of Hector, prince of Troy, whom Achilles treacherously slew. Nothing was wanting but his sword, Durindana, and this had fallen into the possession of a queen named Penthesilea, from whom it passed through her descendants to Almontes, whom Orlando slew, and thus became possessed of the sword. The rest of Hector’s arms were saved and carried off by Æneas, from whom this fairy received them in recompense of service rendered. ‘If you have the courage to attempt their acquisition,’ said the damsel, ‘I will be your guide.’”\n\nMandricardo went on to say that he eagerly embraced the proposal, and being provided with horse and armor by the damsel, set forth on his enterprise, the lady accompanying him.\n\nAs they rode she explained the dangers of the quest. The armor was defended by a champion, one of the numerous unsuccessful adventurers for the prize, all of whom had been made prisoners by the fairy, and compelled to take their turn, day by day, in defending the arms against all comers. Thus speaking they arrived at the castle, which was of alabaster, overlaid with gold. Before it, on a lawn, sat an armed knight on horseback, who was none other than Gradasso, king of Sericane, who, in his return home from his unsuccessful inroad into France, had fallen into the power of the fairy, and was held to do her bidding. Mandricardo, upon seeing him, dropt his visor, and laid his lance in rest. The champion of the castle was equally ready, and each spurred towards his opponent. They met one another with equal force, splintered their spears, and, returning to the charge, encountered with their swords. The contest was long and doubtful, when Mandricardo, determined to bring it to an end, threw his arms about Gradasso, grappled with him, and both fell to the ground. Mandricardo, however, fell uppermost, and, preserving his advantage, compelled Gradasso to yield himself conquered. The damsel now interfered, congratulating the victor, and consoling the vanquished as well as she might.\n\nMandricardo and the damsel proceeded to the gate of the castle, which they found undefended. As they entered they beheld a shield suspended from a pilaster of gold. The device was a white eagle on an azure field, in memory of the bird of Jove, which bore away Ganymede, the flower of the Phrygian race. Beneath was engraved the following couplet:\n\n“Let none with hand profane my buckler wrong Unless he be himself as Hector strong.”\n\nThe damsel, alighting from her palfrey, made obeisance to the arms, bending herself to the ground. The Tartar king bowed his head with equal reverence; then advancing towards the shield, touched it with his sword. Thereupon an earthquake shook the ground, and the way by which he had entered closed. Another and an opposite gate opened, and displayed a field bristling with stalks and grain of gold. The damsel, upon this, told him that he had no means of retreat but by cutting down the harvest which was before him, and by uprooting a tree which grew in the middle of the field. Mandricardo, without replying, began to mow the harvest with his sword, but had scarce smitten thrice when he perceived that every stalk that fell was instantly transformed into some poisonous or ravenous animal, which prepared to assail him. Instructed by the damsel, he snatched up a stone and cast it among the pack. A strange wonder followed; for no sooner had the stone fallen among the beasts, than they turned their rage against one another, and rent each other to pieces. Mandricardo did not stop to marvel at the miracle, but proceeded to fulfil his task, and uproot the tree. He clasped it round the trunk, and made vigorous efforts to tear it up by the roots. At each effort fell a shower of leaves, that were instantly changed into birds of prey, which attacked the knight, flapping their wings in his face, with horrid screeching. But undismayed by this new annoyance, he continued to tug at the trunk till it yielded to his efforts. A burst of wind and thunder followed, and the hawks and vultures flew screaming away.\n\nBut these only gave place to a new foe; for from the hole made by tearing up the tree issued a furious serpent, and, darting at Mandricardo, wound herself about his limbs with a strain that almost crushed him. Fortune, however, again stood his friend, for, writhing under the folds of the monster, he fell backwards into the hole, and his enemy was crushed beneath his weight.\n\nMandricardo, when he was somewhat recovered, and assured himself of the destruction of the serpent, began to contemplate the place into which he had fallen, and saw that he was in a vault, incrusted with costly metals, and illuminated by a live coal. In the middle was a sort of ivory bier, and upon this was extended what appeared to be a knight in armor, but was in truth an empty trophy, composed of the rich and precious arms once Hector’s, to which nothing was wanting but the sword. While Mandricardo stood contemplating the prize a door opened behind him, and a bevy of fair damsels entered, dancing, who, taking up the armor piece by piece, led him away to the place where the shield was suspended; where he found the fairy of the castle seated in state. By her he was invested with the arms he had won, first pledging his solemn oath to wear no other blade but Durindana, which he was to wrest from Orlando, and thus complete the conquest of Hector’s arms.\n\n————\n\nMandricardo, having completed his story, now turned to Rogero, and proposed that arms should decide which of the two was most worthy to bear the symbol of the Trojan knight.\n\nRogero felt no other objection to this proposal than the scruple which arose on observing that his antagonist was without a sword. Mandricardo insisted that this need be no impediment, since his oath prevented him from using a sword until he should have achieved the conquest of Durindana.\n\nThis was no sooner said than a new antagonist started up in Gradasso, who now accompanied Mandricardo. Gradasso vindicated his prior right to Durindana, to obtain which he had embarked (as was related in the beginning) in that bold inroad upon France. A quarrel was thus kindled between the kings of Tartary and Sericane. While the dispute was raging a knight arrived upon the ground, accompanied by a damsel, to whom Rogero related the cause of the strife. The knight was Florismart, and his companion Flordelis. Florismart succeeded in bringing the two champions to accord, by informing them that he could bring them to the presence of Orlando, the master of Durindana.\n\nGradasso and Mandricardo readily made truce, in order to accompany Florismart, nor would Rogero be left behind.\n\nAs they proceeded on their quest they were met by a dwarf, who entreated their assistance in behalf of his lady, who had been carried off by an enchanter, mounted on a winged horse. However unwilling to leave the question of the sword undecided, it was not possible for the knights to resist this appeal. Two of their number, Gradasso and Rogero, therefore accompanied the dwarf. Mandricardo persisted in his search for Orlando, and Florismart, with Flordelis, pursued their way to the camp of Charlemagne.\n\nAtlantes, the enchanter, who had brought up Rogero, and cherished for him the warmest affection, knew by his art that his pupil was destined to be severed from him, and converted to the Christian faith through the influence of Bradamante, that royal maiden with whom chance had brought him acquainted. Thinking to thwart the will of Heaven in this respect, he now put forth all his arts to entrap Rogero into his power. By the aid of his subservient demons he reared a castle on an inaccessible height, in the Pyrenean mountains, and to make it a pleasant abode to his pupil, contrived to entrap and convey thither knights and damsels many a one, whom chance had brought into the vicinity of his castle. Here, in a sort of sensual paradise, they were but too willing to forget glory and duty, and to pass their time in indolent enjoyment.\n\nIt was by the enchanter that the dwarf had now been sent to tempt the knights into his power.\n\nBut we must now return to Rinaldo, whom we left interrupted in his combat with Rodomont. In search of his late antagonist and intent on bringing their combat to a decision he entered the forest of Arden, whither he suspected Rodomont had gone. While engaged on this quest he was surprised by the vision of a beautiful child dancing naked, with three damsels as beautiful as himself. While he was lost in admiration at the sight the child approached him, and, throwing at him handfuls of roses and lilies, struck him from his horse. He was no sooner down than he was seized by the dancers, by whom he was dragged about and scourged with flowers till he fell into a swoon. When he began to revive one of the group approached him, and told him that his punishment was the consequence of his rebellion against that power before whom all things bend; that there was but one remedy to heal the wounds that had been inflicted, and that was to drink of the waters of Love. Then they left him.\n\nRinaldo, sore and faint, dragged himself toward a fountain which flowed near by, and, being parched with thirst, drank greedily and almost unconsciously of the water, which was sweet to the taste, but bitter to the heart. After repeated draughts he recovered his strength and recollection, and found himself in the same place where Angelica had formerly awakened him with a rain of flowers, and whence he had fled in contempt of her courtesy.\n\nThis remembrance of the scene was followed by the recognition of his crime; and, repenting bitterly his ingratitude, he leaped upon Bayard, with the intention of hastening to Angelica’s country, and soliciting his pardon at her feet.\n\nLet us now retrace our steps, and revert to the time when the paladins having learned from Dudon the summons of Charlemagne to return to France to repel the invaders, had all obeyed the command with the exception of Orlando, whose passion for Angelica still held him in attendance on her. Orlando, arriving before Albracca, found it closely beleaguered. He, however, made his way into the citadel, and related his adventures to Angelica, from the time of his departure up to his separation from Rinaldo and the rest, when they departed to the assistance of Charlemagne. Angelica, in return, described the distresses of the garrison, and the force of the besiegers; and in conclusion prayed Orlando to favor her escape from the pressing danger, and escort her into France. Orlando, who did not suspect that love for Rinaldo was her secret motive, joyfully agreed to the proposal, and the sally was resolved upon.\n\nLeaving lights burning in the fortress, they departed at nightfall, and passed in safety through the enemy’s camp. After encountering numerous adventures they reached the sea-side, and embarked on board a pinnace for France. The vessel arrived safely, and the travellers, disembarking in Provence, pursued their way by land. One day, heated and weary, they sought shelter from the sun in the forest of Arden, and chance directed Angelica to the fountain of Disdain, of whose waters she eagerly drank.\nIssuing thence, the Count and damsel encountered a stranger-knight. It was no other than Rinaldo, who was just on the point of setting off on a pilgrimage in search of Angelica, to implore her pardon for his insensibility, and urge his new found passion. Surprise and delight at first deprived him of utterance, but soon recovering himself, he joyfully saluted her, claiming her as his, and exhorting her to put herself under his protection. His presumption was repelled by Angelica with disdain, and Orlando, enraged at the invasion of his rights, challenged him to decide their claims by arms.\n\nTerrified at the combat which ensued, Angelica fled amain through the forest, and came out upon a plain covered with tents. This was the camp of Charlemagne, who led the army of reserve destined to support the troops which had advanced to oppose Marsilius. Charles having heard the damsel’s tale, with difficulty separated the two cousins, and then consigned Angelica, as the cause of quarrel, to the care of Namo, Duke of Bavaria, promising that she should be his who should best deserve her in the impending battle.\n\nBut these plans and hopes were frustrated. The Christian army, beaten at all points, fled from the Saracens; and Angelica, indifferent to both her lovers, mounted a swift palfrey and plunged into the forest, rejoicing, in spite of her terror, at having regained her liberty. She stopped at last in a tufted grove, where a gentle zephyr blew, and whose young trees were watered by two clear runnels, which came and mingled their waters, making a pleasing murmur. Believing herself far from Rinaldo, and overcome by fatigue and the summer heat, she saw with delight a bank covered with flowers so thick that they almost hid the green turf, inviting her to alight and rest. She dismounted from her palfrey, and turned him loose to recruit his strength with the tender grass which bordered the streamlets. Then, in a sheltered nook tapestried with moss and fenced in with roses and hawthorn-flowers, she yielded herself to grateful repose.\n\nShe had not slept long when she was awakened by the noise made by the approach of a horse. Starting up, she saw an armed knight who had arrived at the bank of the stream. Not knowing whether he was to be feared or not, her heart beat with anxiety. She pressed aside the leaves to allow her to see who it was, but scarce dared to breathe for fear of betraying herself. Soon the knight threw himself on the flowery bank, and leaning his head on his hand fell into a profound reverie. Then arousing himself from his silence he began to pour forth complaints, mingled with deep sighs. Rivers of tears flowed down his cheeks, and his breast seemed to labor with a hidden flame. “Ah, vain regrets!” he exclaimed; “cruel fortune! others triumph, while I endure hopeless misery! Better a thousand times to lose life, than wear a chain so disgraceful and so oppressive!”\n\nAngelica by this time had recognized the stranger, and perceived that it was Sacripant, king of Circassia, one of the worthiest of her suitors. This prince had followed Angelica from his country, at the very gates of\n\nthe day, to France, where he heard with dismay that she was under the guardianship of the Paladin Orlando, and that the Emperor had announced his decree to award her as the prize of valor to that one of his nephews who should best deserve her.\n\nAs Sacripant continued to lament, Angelica, who had always opposed the hardness of marble to his sighs, thought with herself that nothing forbade her employing his good offices in this unhappy crisis. Though firmly resolved never to accept him as a spouse, she yet felt the necessity of giving him a gleam of hope in reward for the service she required of him. All at once, like Diana, she stepped forth from the arbor. “May the gods preserve thee,” she said, “and put far from thee all hard thoughts of me!” Then she told him all that had befallen her since she parted with him at her father’s court, and how she had availed herself of Orlando’s protection to escape from the beleaguered city. At that moment the noise of horse and armor was heard as of one approaching; and Sacripant, furious at the interruption, resumed his helmet, mounted his horse, and placed his lance in rest. He saw a knight advancing, with scarf and plume of snowy whiteness. Sacripant regarded him with angry eyes, and, while he was yet some distance off, defied him to the combat. The other, not moved by his angry tone to make reply, put himself on his defence. Their horses, struck at the same moment with the spur, rushed upon one another with the impetuosity of a tempest. Their shields were pierced each with the other’s lance, and only the temper of their breastplates saved their lives. Both the horses recoiled with the violence of the shock; but the unknown knight’s recovered itself at the touch of the spur; the Saracen king’s fell dead, and bore down his master with him. The white knight, seeing his enemy in this condition, cared not to renew the combat, but, thinking he had done enough for glory, pursued his way through the forest, and was a mile off before Sacripant had got free from his horse.\n\nAs a ploughman, stunned by a thunder-clap which has stricken dead the oxen at his plough, stands motionless, sadly contemplating his loss, so Sacripant stood confounded and overwhelmed with mortification at having Angelica a witness of his defeat. He groaned, he sighed, less from the pain of his bruises than for the shame of being reduced to such a state before her. The princess took pity on him, and consoled him as well as she could. “Banish your regrets, my lord,” she said, “this accident has happened solely in consequence of the feebleness of your horse, which had more need of rest and food than of such an encounter as this. Nor can your adversary gain any credit by it, since he has hurried away, not venturing a second trial.” While she thus consoled Sacripant they perceived a person approach, who seemed a courier, with bag and horn. As soon as he came up, he accosted Sacripant, and inquired if he had seen a knight pass that way, bearing a white shield and with a white plume to his helmet. “I have, indeed, seen too much of him,” said Sacripant, “it is he who has brought me to the ground; but at least I hope to learn from you who that knight is.” “That I can easily inform you,” said the man; “know then that, if you have been overthrown, you owe your fate to the high prowess of a lady as beautiful as she is brave. It is the fair and illustrious Bradamante who has won from you the honors of victory.”\nAt these words the courier rode on his way, leaving Sacripant more confounded and mortified than ever. In silence he mounted the horse of Angelica, taking the lady behind him on the croup, and rode away in search of a more secure asylum. Hardly had they ridden two miles when a new sound was heard in the forest, and they perceived a gallant and powerful horse, which, leaping the ravines and dashing aside the branches that opposed his passage, appeared before them, accoutred with a rich harness adorned with gold.\n\n“If I may believe my eyes, which penetrate with difficulty the underwood,” said Angelica, “that horse that dashes so stoutly through the bushes is Bayard, and I marvel how he seems to know the need we have of him, mounted as we are both on one feeble animal.” Sacripant, dismounting from the palfrey, approached the fiery courser, and attempted to seize his bridle, but the disdainful animal, turning from him, launched at him a volley of kicks enough to have shattered a wall of marble. Bayard then approached Angelica with an air as gentle and loving as a faithful dog could his master after a long separation. For he remembered how she had caressed him, and even fed him, in Albracca. She took his bridle in her left hand, while with her right she patted his neck. The beautiful animal, gifted with wonderful intelligence, seemed to submit entirely. Sacripant, seizing the moment to vault upon him, controlled his curvetings, and Angelica, quitting the croup of the palfrey, regained her seat.\n\nBut, turning his eyes toward a place where was heard a noise of arms, Sacripant beheld Rinaldo. That hero now loves Angelica more than his life, and she flies him as the timid crane the falcon.\n\nThe fountain of which Angelica had drunk produced such an effect on the beautiful queen that, with distressed countenance and trembling voice, she conjured Sacripant not to wait the approach of Rinaldo, but to join her in flight.\n\n“Am I, then,” said Sacripant, “of so little esteem with you that you doubt my power to defend you? Do you forget the battle of Albracca, and how, in your defence, I fought single-handed against Agrican and all his knights?”\n\nAngelica made no reply, uncertain what to do; but already Rinaldo was too near to be escaped. He advanced menacingly to the Circassian king, for he recognized his horse.\n\n“Vile thief,” he cried, “dismount from that horse, and prevent the punishment that is your due for daring to rob me of my property. Leave, also, the princess in my hands; for it would indeed be a sin to suffer so charming a lady and so gallant a charger to remain in such keeping.”\n\nThe king of Circassia, furious at being thus insulted, cried out, “Thou liest, villain, in giving me the name of thief, which better belongs to thyself than to me. It is true, the beauty of this lady and the perfection of this horse are unequalled; come on, then, and let us try which of us is most worthy to possess them.”\n\nAt these words the king of Circassia and Rinaldo attacked one another with all their force, one fighting on foot, the other on horseback. You need not, however, suppose that the Saracen king found any advantage in this; for a young page, unused to horsemanship, could not have failed more completely to manage Bayard than did this accomplished knight. The faithful animal loved his master too well to injure him, and refused his aid as well as his obedience to the hand of Sacripant, who could strike but ineffectual blows, the horse backing when he wished him to go forward, and dropping his head and arching his back, throwing out with his legs, so as almost to shake the knight out of the saddle. Sacripant, seeing that he could not manage him, watched his opportunity, rose on his saddle, and leapt lightly to the earth; then, relieved from the embarrassment of the horse, renewed the combat on more equal terms. Their skill to thrust and parry were equal; one rises, the other stoops; with one foot set firm they turn and wind, to lay on strokes or to dodge them. At last Rinaldo, throwing himself on the Circassian, dealt him a blow so terrible that Fusberta, his good sword, cut in two the buckler of Sacripant, although it was made of bone, and covered with a thick plate of steel well tempered. The arm of the Saracen was deprived of its defence, and almost palsied with the stroke. Angelica, perceiving how victory was likely to incline, and shuddering at the thought of becoming the prize of Rinaldo, hesitated no longer. Turning her horse’s head, she fled with the utmost speed; and, in spite of the round pebbles which covered a steep descent, she plunged into a deep valley, trembling with the fear that Rinaldo was in pursuit. At the bottom of this valley she encountered an aged hermit, whose white beard flowed to his middle, and whose venerable appearance seemed to assure his piety.\n\nThis hermit, who appeared shrunk by age and fasting, travelled slowly, mounted upon a wretched ass. The princess, overcome with fear, conjured him to save her life; and to conduct her to some port of the sea, whence she might embark and quit France, never more to hear the odious name of Rinaldo.\n\nThe old hermit was something of a wizard. He comforted Angelica, and promised to protect her from all peril. Then he opened his scrip, and took from thence a book, and had read but a single page when a goblin, obedient to his incantations, appeared, under the form of a laboring man, and demanded his orders. He received them, transported himself to the place where the knights still maintained their conflict, and boldly stepped between the two.\n\n“Tell me, I pray you,” he said, “what benefit will accrue to him who shall get the better in this contest? The object you are contending for is already disposed of; for the Paladin Orlando, without effort and without opposition, is now carrying away the princess Angelica to Paris. You had better pursue them promptly; for if they reach Paris you will never see her again.”\n\nAt these words you might have seen those rival warriors confounded, stupefied, silently agreeing that they were affording their rival a fair opportunity to triumph over them. Rinaldo, approaching Bayard, breathes a sigh of shame and rage, and swears a terrible oath that, if he overtakes Orlando, he will tear his heart out. Then mounting Bayard and pressing his flanks with his spurs, he leaves the king of Circassia on foot in the forest.\n\nLet it not appear strange that Rinaldo found Bayard obedient at last, after having so long prevented any one from even touching his bridle; for that fine animal had an intelligence almost human; he had fled from his master only to draw him on the track of Angelica, and enable him to recover her. He saw when the princess fled from the battle, and Rinaldo being then engaged in a fight on foot, Bayard found himself free to follow the traces of Angelica. Thus he had drawn his master after him, not permitting him to approach, and had brought him to the sight of the princess. But Bayard now, deceived like his master with the false intelligence of the goblin, submits to be mounted and to serve his master as usual, and Rinaldo, animated with rage, makes him fly toward Paris, more slowly than his wishes, though the speed of Bayard outstripped the winds. Full of impatience to encounter Orlando, he gave but a few hours that night to sleep. Early the next day he saw before him the great city, under the walls of which the Emperor Charles had collected the scattered remains of his army. Foreseeing that he would soon be attacked on all sides, the Emperor had caused the ancient fortifications to be repaired, and new ones to be built, surrounded by wide and deep ditches. The desire to hold the field against the enemy made him seize every means of procuring new allies. He hoped to receive from England aid sufficient to enable him to form a new camp, and as soon as Rinaldo rejoined him he selected him to go as his ambassador into England, to plead for auxiliaries. Rinaldo was far from pleased with his commission, but he obeyed the Emperor’s commands, without giving himself time to devote a single day to the object nearest his heart. He hastened to Calais, and lost not a moment in embarking for England, ardently desiring a hasty despatch of his commission, and a speedy return to France.\n————\n\n# BRADAMANTE AND ROGERO\n\nBradamante, the knight of the white plume and shield, whose sudden appearance and encounter with Sacripant we have already told, was in quest of Rogero, from whom chance had separated her, almost at the beginning of their acquaintance. After her encounter with Sacripant Bradamante pursued her way through the forest, in hopes of rejoining Rogero, and arrived at last on the brink of a fair fountain.\n\nThis fountain flowed through a broad meadow. Ancient trees overshadowed it, and travellers, attracted by the sweet murmur of its waters, stopped there to cool themselves. Bradamante, casting her eyes on all sides to enjoy the beauties of the spot, perceived, under the shade of a tree, a knight reclining, who seemed to be oppressed with the deepest grief.\nBradamante accosted him, and asked to be informed of the cause of his distress. “Alas! my lord,” said he, “I lament a young and charming friend, my affianced wife, who has been torn from me by a villain,—let me rather call him a demon,—who, on a winged horse, descended from the air, seized her, and bore her screaming to his den. I have pursued them over rocks and through ravines till my horse is no longer able to bear me, and I now wait only for death.” He added that already a vain attempt on his behalf had been made by two knights, whom chance had brought to the spot. Their names were Gradasso, king of Sericane, and Rogero, the Moor. Both had been overcome by the wiles of the enchanter, and were added to the number of the captives, whom he held in an impregnable castle, situated on the height of the mountain. At the mention of Rogero’s name Bradamante started with delight, which was soon changed to an opposite sentiment when she heard that her lover was a prisoner in the toils of the enchanter. “Sir Knight,” she said, “do not surrender yourself to despair; this day may be more happy for you than you think, if you will only lead me to the castle which enfolds her whom you deplore.”\n\nThe knight responded, “After having lost all that made life dear to me I have no motive to avoid the dangers of the enterprise, and I will do as you request; but I forewarn you of the perils you will have to encounter. If you fall impute it not to me.”\n\nHaving thus spoken, they took their way to the castle, but were overtaken by a messenger from the camp, who had been sent in quest of Bradamante to summon her back to the army, where her presence was needed to reassure her disheartened forces, and withstand the advance of the Moors.\n\nThe mournful knight, whose name was Pinabel, thus became aware that Bradamante was a scion of the house of Clermont, between which and his own of Mayence there existed an ancient feud. From this moment the traitor sought only how he might be rid of the company of Bradamante, from whom he feared no good would come to him, but rather mortal injury, if his name and lineage became known to her. For he judged her by his own base model, and, knowing his ill deserts, he feared to receive his due.\n\nBradamante, in spite of the summons to return to the army, could not resolve to leave her lover in captivity, and determined first to finish the adventure on which she was engaged. Pinabel leading the way, they at length arrived at a wood, in the centre of which rose a steep, rocky mountain. Pinabel, who now thought of nothing else but how he might escape from Bradamante, proposed to ascend the mountain to extend his view, in order to discover a shelter for the night, if any there might be within sight. Under this pretence he left Bradamante, and advanced up the side of the mountain till he came to a cleft in the rock, down which he looked, and perceived that it widened below into a spacious cavern. Meanwhile Bradamante, fearful of losing her guide, had followed close on his footsteps, and rejoined him at the mouth of the cavern. Then the traitor, seeing the impossibility of escaping her, conceived another design. He told her that before her approach he had seen in the cavern a young and beautiful damsel, whose rich dress announced her high birth, who with tears and lamentations implored assistance; that before he could descend to relieve her a ruffian had seized her, and hurried her away into the recesses of the cavern.\n\nBradamante, full of truth and courage, readily believed this lie of the Mayencian traitor. Eager to succor the damsel, she looked round for the means of facilitating the descent, and seeing a large elm with spreading branches she lopped off with her sword one of the largest, and thrust it into the opening. She told Pinabel to hold fast to the larger end, while, grasping the branches with her hands, she let herself down into the cavern.\n\nThe traitor smiled at seeing her thus suspended, and, asking her in mockery, “Are you a good leaper?” he let go the branch with perfidious glee, and saw Bradamante precipitated to the bottom of the cave. “I wish your whole race were there with you,” he muttered, “that you might all perish together.”\n\nBut Pinabel’s atrocious design was not accomplished. The twigs and foliage of the branch broke its descent, and Bradamante, not seriously injured, though stunned with her fall, was reserved for other adventures.\n\nAs soon as she recovered from the shock Bradamante cast her eyes around and perceived a door, through which she passed into a second cavern, larger and loftier than the first. It had the appearance of a subterranean temple. Columns of the purest alabaster adorned it, and supported the roof; a simple altar rose in the middle; a lamp, whose radiance was reflected by the alabaster walls, cast a mild light around.\nBradamante, inspired by a sense of religious awe, approached the altar, and, falling on her knees, poured forth her prayers and thanks to the Preserver of her life, invoking the protection of his power. At that moment a small door opened, and a female issued from it with naked feet, and flowing robe and hair, who called her by her name, and thus addressed her: “Brave and generous Bradamante, know that it is a power from above that has brought you hither. The spirit of Merlin, whose last earthly abode was in this place, has warned me of your arrival, and of the fate that awaits you. This famous grotto,” she continued, “was the work of the enchanter Merlin; here his ashes repose. You have no doubt heard how this sage and virtuous enchanter ceased to be. Victim of the artful fairy of the lake, Merlin, by a fatal compliance with her request, laid himself down living in his tomb, without power to resist the spell laid upon him by that ingrate, who retained him there as long as he lived. His spirit hovers about this spot, and will not leave it, until the last trumpet shall summon the dead to judgment. He answers the questions of those who approach his tomb, where perhaps you may be privileged to hear his voice.”\n\nBradamante, astonished at these words, and the objects which met her view, knew not whether she was awake or asleep. Confused, but modest, she cast down her eyes, and a blush overspread her face. “Ah, what am I,” said she, “that so great a prophet should deign to speak to me!” Still, with a secret satisfaction, she followed the priestess, who led her to the tomb of Merlin. This tomb was constructed of a species of stone hard and resplendent like fire. The rays which beamed from the stone sufficed to light up that terrible place, where the sun’s rays never penetrated; but I know not whether that light was the effect of a certain phosphorescence of the stone itself, or of the many talismans and charms with which it was wrought over.\n\nBradamante had hardly passed the threshold of this sacred place when the spirit of the enchanter saluted her with a voice firm and distinct: “May thy designs be prosperous, O chaste and noble maiden, the future mother of heroes, the glory of Italy, and destined to fill the whole world with their fame. Great captains, renowned knights, shall be numbered among your descendants, who shall defend the Church and restore their country to its ancient splendor. Princes, wise as Augustus and the sage Numa, shall bring back the age of gold.[75] To accomplish these grand destinies it is ordained that you shall wed the illustrious Rogero. Fly then to his deliverance, and lay prostrate in the dust the traitor who has snatched him from you, and now holds him in chains!”\n\nMerlin ceased with these words, and left to Melissa, the priestess, the charge of more fully instructing the maiden in her future course. “To-morrow,” said she, “I will conduct you to the castle on the rock where Rogero is held captive. I will not leave you till I have guided you through this wild wood, and I will direct you on your way so that you shall be in no danger of mistaking it.”\n\nThe next morning Melissa conducted Bradamante between rocks and precipices, crossing rapid torrents, and traversing intricate passes, employing the time in imparting to her such information as was necessary to enable her to bring her design to a successful issue.\n\n“Not only would the castle, impenetrable by force, and that winged horse of his baffle your efforts, but know that he possesses also a buckler whence flashes a light so brilliant that the eyes of all who look upon it are blinded. Think not to avoid it by shutting your eyes, for how then will you be able to avoid his blows, and make him feel your own? But I will teach you the proper course to pursue.\n\n“Agramant, the Moorish prince, possesses a ring stolen from a queen of India, which has power to render of no avail all enchantments. Agramant, knowing that Rogero is of more importance to him than any one of his warriors, is desirous of rescuing him from the power of the enchanter, and has sent for that purpose Brunello, the most crafty and sagacious of his servants, provided with his wonderful ring, and he is even now at hand, bent on this enterprise. But, beautiful Bradamante, as I desire that no one but yourself shall have the glory of delivering from thraldom your future spouse, listen while I disclose the means of success. Following this path which leads by the seashore, you will come ere long to a hostelry, where the Saracen Brunello will arrive shortly before you. You will readily know him by his stature, under four feet, his great disproportioned head, his squint eyes, his livid hue, his thick eyebrows joining his tufted beard. His dress, moreover, that of a courier, will point him out to you.\n\n“It will be easy for you to enter into conversation with him, announcing yourself as a knight seeking combat with the enchanter, but let not the knave suspect that you know anything about the ring. I doubt not that he will be your guide to the castle of the enchanter. Accept his offer, but take care to keep behind him till you come in sight of the brilliant dome of the castle. Then hesitate not to strike him dead, for the wretch deserves no pity, and take from him the ring. But let him not suspect your intention, for by putting the ring into his mouth he will instantly become invisible, and disappear from your eyes.”\n\nSaying thus, the sage Melissa and the fair Bradamante arrived near the city of Bordeaux, where the rich and wide river Garonne pours the tribute of its waves into the sea. They parted with tender embraces. Bradamante, intent wholly on her purpose, hastened to arrive at the hostelry, where Brunello had preceded her a few moments only. The young heroine knew him without difficulty. She accosted him, and put to him some slight questions, to which he replied with adroit falsehoods. Bradamante, on her part, concealed from him her sex, her religion, her country, and the blood from whence she sprung. While they talk together, sudden cries are heard from all parts of the hostelry. “O queen of heaven!” exclaimed Bradamante, “what can be the cause of this sudden alarm?” She soon learned the cause. Host, children, domestics, all, with upturned eyes, as if they saw a comet or a great eclipse, were gazing on a prodigy which seemed to pass the bounds of possibility. She beheld distinctly a winged horse, mounted with a cavalier in rich armor, cleaving the air with rapid flight. The wings of this strange courser were wide extended, and covered with feathers of various colors. The polished armor of the knight made them shine with rainbow tints. In a short time the horse and rider disappeared behind the summits of the mountains.\n\n“It is an enchanter,” said the host, “a magician who often is seen traversing the air in that way. Sometimes he flies aloft as if among the stars, and at others skims along the land. He possesses a wonderful castle on the top of the Pyrenees. Many knights have shown their courage by going to attack him, but none have ever returned, from which it is to be feared they have lost either their life or their liberty.”\n\nBradamante, addressing the host, said, “Could you furnish me a guide to conduct me to the castle of this enchanter?” “By my faith,” said Brunello, interrupting, “that you shall not seek in vain; I have it all in writing, and I will myself conduct you.” Bradamante, with thanks, accepted him for her guide.\n\nThe host had a tolerable horse to dispose of, which Bradamante bargained for, and the next day, at the first dawn of morning, she took her route by a narrow valley, taking care to have the Saracen Brunello lead the way.\n\nThey reached the summit of the Pyrenees, whence one may look down on France, Spain, and the two seas. From this height they descended again by a fatiguing road into a deep valley. From the middle of this valley an isolated mountain rose, composed of rough and perpendicular rock, on whose summit was the castle, surrounded with a wall of brass. Brunello said, “Yonder is the stronghold where the enchanter keeps his prisoners; one must have wings to mount thither; it is easy to see that the aid of a flying horse must be necessary for the master of this castle, which he uses for his prison and for his abode.”\n\nBradamante, sufficiently instructed, saw that the time had now come to possess herself of the ring; but she could not resolve to slay a defenceless man. She seized Brunello before he was aware, bound him to a tree, and took from him the ring which he wore on one of his fingers. The cries and entreaties of the perfidious Saracen moved her not. She advanced to the foot of the rock whereon the castle stood, and, to draw the magician to the combat, sounded her horn, adding to it cries of defiance.\n\nThe enchanter delayed not to present himself, mounted on his winged horse. Bradamante was struck with surprise mixed with joy when she saw that this person, described as so formidable, bore no lance nor club, nor any other deadly weapon. He had only on his arm a buckler, covered with a cloth, and in his hand an open book. As to the winged horse, there was no enchantment about him. He was a natural animal, of a species which exists in the Riphæan mountains. Like a griffin, he had the head of an eagle, claws armed with talons, and wings covered with feathers, the rest of his body being that of a horse. This strange animal is called a Hippogriff.\n\nThe heroine attacked the enchanter on his approach, striking on this side and on that, with all the energy of a violent combat, but wounding only the wind; and after this pretended attack had lasted some time dismounted from her horse, as if hoping to do battle more effectually on foot. The enchanter now prepares to employ his sole weapon, by uncovering the magic buckler which never failed to subdue an enemy by depriving him of his senses. Bradamante, confiding in her ring, observed all the motions of her adversary, and, at the unveiling of the shield, cast herself on the ground, pretending that the splendor of the shield had overcome her, but in reality to induce the enchanter to dismount and approach her.\n\nIt happened according to her wish. When the enchanter saw her prostrate he made his horse alight on the ground, and, dismounting, fixed the shield on the pommel of his saddle, and approached in order to secure the fallen warrior. Bradamante, who watched him intently, as soon as she saw him near at hand, sprang up, seized him vigorously, threw him down, and, with the same chain which the enchanter had prepared for herself, bound him fast, without his being able to make any effectual resistance.\nThe enchanter, with the accents of despair, exclaimed, “Take my life, young man!” but Bradamante was far from complying with such a wish. Desirous of knowing the name of the enchanter, and for what purpose he had formed with so much art this impregnable fortress, she commanded him to inform her.\n\n“Alas!” replied the magician, while tears flowed down his cheeks, “it is not to conceal booty, nor for any culpable design that I have built this castle; it was only to guard the life of a young knight, the object of my tenderest affection, my art having taught me that he is destined to become a Christian, and to perish, shortly after, by the blackest of treasons.\n\n“This youth, named Rogero, is the most beautiful and most accomplished of knights. It is I, the unhappy Atlantes, who have reared him from his childhood. The call of honor and the desire of glory led him from me to follow Agramant, his prince, in his invasion of France, and I, more devoted to Rogero than the tenderest of parents, have sought the means of bringing him back to this abode, in the hope of saving him from the cruel fate that menaces him.\n\n“For this purpose I have got him in my possession by the same means as I attempted to employ against you; and by which I have succeeded in collecting a great many knights and ladies in my castle. My purpose was to render my beloved pupil’s captivity light, by affording him society to amuse him, and keep his thoughts from running on subjects of war and glory. Alas! my cares have been in vain! Yet, take, I beseech you, whatever else I have, but spare me my beloved pupil. Take this shield, take this winged courser, deliver such of your friends as you may find among my prisoners, deliver them all if you will, but leave me my beloved Rogero; or if you will snatch him too from me, take also my life, which will cease then to be to me worth preserving.”\n\nBradamante replied: “Old man, hope not to move me by your vain entreaties. It is precisely the liberty of Rogero that I require. You would keep him here in bondage and in slothful pleasure, to save him from a fate which you foresee. Vain old man! how can you foresee his fate when you could not foresee your own? You desire me to take your life. No, my arm and my soul refuse the request.” This said, she required the magician to go before, and guide her to the castle. The prisoners were set at liberty, though some, in their secret hearts, regretted the voluptuous life which was thus brought to an end. Bradamante and Rogero met one another with transports of joy.\n\nThey descended from the mountain to the spot where the encounter had taken place. There they found the Hippogriff, with the magic buckler in its wrapper, hanging to his saddle-bow. Bradamante advanced to seize the bridle; the Hippogriff seemed to wait her approach, but before she reached him he spread his wings and flew away to a neighboring hill, and in the same manner, a second time, eluded her efforts. Rogero and the other liberated knights dispersed over the plain and hill-tops to secure him, and at last the animal allowed Rogero to seize his rein. The fearless Rogero hesitated not to vault upon his back, and let him feel his spurs, which so roused his mettle that, after galloping a short distance, he suddenly spread his wings, and soared into the air. Bradamante had the grief to see her lover snatched away from her at the very moment of reunion. Rogero, who knew not the art of directing the horse, was unable to control his flight. He found himself carried over the tops of the mountains, so far above them that he could hardly distinguish what was land and what water. The Hippogriff directed his flight to the west, and cleaved the air as swiftly as a new-rigged vessel cuts the waves, impelled by the freshest and most favorable gales.\n————\n\n# ASTOLPHO AND THE ENCHANTRESS\n\nIn the long flight which Rogero took on the back of the Hippogriff he was carried over land and sea, unknowing whither. As soon as he had gained some control over the animal he made him alight on the nearest land. When he came near enough to earth Rogero leapt lightly from his back, and tied the animal to a myrtle-tree. Near the spot flowed the pure waters of a fountain, surrounded by cedars and palm-trees. Rogero laid aside his shield, and, removing his helmet, breathed with delight the fresh air, and cooled his lips with the waters of the fountain. For we cannot wonder that he was excessively fatigued, considering the ride he had taken. He was preparing to taste the sweets of repose when he perceived that the Hippogriff, which he had tied by the bridle to a myrtle-tree, frightened at something, was making violent efforts to disengage himself. His struggle shook the myrtle-tree so that many of its beautiful leaves were torn off, and strewed the ground.\n\nA sound like that which issues from burning wood seemed to come from the myrtle-tree, at first faint and indistinct, but growing stronger by degrees, and at length was audible as a voice which spoke in this manner: “O knight, if the tenderness of your heart corresponds to the beauty of your person, relieve me, I pray you, from this tormenting animal. I suffer enough inwardly without having outward evils added to my lot.”\n\nRogero, at the first accents of this voice, turned his eyes promptly on the myrtle, hastened to it, and stood fixed in astonishment when he perceived that the voice issued from the tree itself. He immediately untied his horse, and, flushed with surprise and regret, exclaimed, “Whoever thou art, whether mortal or the goddess of these woods, forgive me, I beseech you, my involuntary fault. Had I imagined that this hard bark covered a being possessed of feeling, could I have exposed such a beautiful myrtle to the insults of this steed? May the sweet influences of the sky and air speedily repair the injury I have done! For my part, I promise by the sovereign lady of my heart to do everything you wish in order to merit your forgiveness.”\n\nAt these words the myrtle seemed to tremble from root to stem, and Rogero remarked that a moisture as of tears trickled down its bark, like that which exudes from a log placed on the fire. It then spoke:\n\n“The kindness which inspires your words compels me to disclose to you who I once was, and by what fatality I have been changed into this shape. My name was Astolpho, cousin of Orlando and Rinaldo, whose fame has filled the earth. I was myself reckoned among the bravest paladins of France, and was by birth entitled to reign over England, after Otho, my father. Returning from the distant East, with Rinaldo and many other brave knights, called home to aid with our arms the great Emperor of France, we reached a spot where the powerful enchantress Alcina possessed a castle on the borders of the sea. She had gone to the water-side to amuse herself with fishing, and we paused to see how, by her art, without hook or line, she drew from the water whatever she would.\n\n“Not far from the shore an enormous whale showed a back so broad and motionless that it looked like an island. Alcina had fixed her eyes on me, and planned to get me into her power. Addressing us, she said: ‘This is the hour when the prettiest mermaid in the sea comes regularly every day to the shore of yonder island. She sings so sweetly that the very waves flow smoother at the sound. If you wish to hear her come with me to her resort.’ So saying, Alcina pointed to the fish, which we all supposed to be an island. I, who was rash, did not hesitate to follow her; but swam my horse over, and mounted on the back of the fish. In vain Rinaldo and Dudon made signs to me to beware; Alcina, smiling, took me in charge, and led the way. No sooner were we mounted upon him than the whale moved off, spreading his great fins, and cleft rapidly the waters. I then saw my folly, but it was too late to repent. Alcina soothed my anger, and professed that what she had done was for love of me. Ere long we arrived at this island, where at first everything was done to reconcile me to my lot, and to make my days pass happily away. But soon Alcina, sated with her conquest, grew indifferent, then weary of me, and at last, to get rid of me, changed me into this form, as she had done to many lovers before me, making some of them olives, some palms, some cedars, changing others into fountains, rocks, or even into wild beasts. And thou, courteous knight, whom accident has brought to this enchanted isle, beware that she get not the power over thee, or thou shalt haply be made like us, a tree, a fountain, or a rock.”\n\nRogero expressed his astonishment at this recital. Astolpho added that the island was in great part subject to the sway of Alcina. By the aid of her sister Morgana, she had succeeded in dispossessing a third sister, Logestilla, of nearly the whole of her patrimony, for the whole isle was hers originally by her father’s bequest. But Logestilla was temperate and sage, while the other sisters were false and voluptuous. Her empire was divided from theirs by a gulf and chain of mountains, which alone had thus far prevented her sister from usurping it.\n\nAstolpho here ended his tale, and Rogero, who knew that he was the cousin of Bradamante, would gladly have devised some way for his relief; but, as that was out of his power, he consoled him as well as he could, and then begged to be told the way to the palace of Logestilla, and how to avoid that of Alcina. Astolpho directed him to take the road to the left, though rough and full of rocks. He warned him that this road would present serious obstacles; that troops of monsters would oppose his passage, employed by the art of Alcina to prevent her subjects from escaping from her dominion. Rogero thanked the myrtle, and prepared to set out on his way.\n\nHe at first thought he would mount the winged horse, and scale the mountain on his back; but he was too uncertain of his power to control him to wish to encounter the hazard of another flight through the air, besides that he was almost famished for the want of food. So he led the horse after him, and took the road on foot, which for some distance led equally to the dominions of both the sisters.\n\nHe had not advanced more than two miles when he saw before him the superb city of Alcina. It was surrounded with a wall of gold, which seemed to reach the skies. I know that some think that this wall was not of real gold, but only the work of alchemy; it matters not; I prefer to think it gold, for it certainly shone like gold.\n\nA broad and level road led to the gates of the city, and from this another branched off, narrow and rough, which led to the mountain region. Rogero took without hesitation the narrow road; but he had no sooner entered upon it than he was assailed by a numerous troop which opposed his passage.\n\nYou never have seen anything so ridiculous, so extraordinary, as this host of hobgoblins were. Some of them bore the human form from the neck to the feet, but had the head of a monkey or a cat; others had the legs and the ears of a horse; old men and women, bald and hideous, ran hither and thither as if out of their senses, half clad in the shaggy skins of beasts; one rode full speed on a horse without a bridle, another jogged along mounted on an ass or a cow; others, full of agility, skipped about, and clung to the tails and manes of the animals which their companions rode. Some blew horns, others brandished drinking-cups; some were armed with spits, and some with pitchforks. One, who appeared to be the captain, had an enormous belly and a gross fat head; he was mounted on a tortoise, that waddled, now this way, now that, without keeping any one direction.\n\nOne of these monsters, who had something approaching the human form, though he had the neck, ears, and muzzle of a dog, set himself to bark furiously at Rogero, to make him turn off to the right, and reenter upon the road to the gay city; but the brave chevalier exclaimed, “That will I not, so long as I can use this sword,”—and he thrust the point directly at his face. The monster tried to strike him with a lance, but Rogero was too quick for him, and thrust his sword through his body, so that it appeared a hand’s breadth behind his back. The paladin, now giving full vent to his rage, laid about him vigorously among the rabble, cleaving one to the teeth, another to the girdle; but the troop were so numerous, and in spite of his blows pressed around him so close, that, to clear his way, he must have had as many arms as Briareus.\n\nIf Rogero had uncovered the shield of the enchanter, which hung at his saddle-bow, he might easily have vanquished this monstrous rout; but perhaps he did not think of it, and perhaps he preferred to seek his defence nowhere but in his good sword. At that moment, when his perplexity was at its height, he saw issue from the city gate two young beauties, whose air and dress proclaimed their rank and gentle nurture. Each of them was mounted on a unicorn, whose whiteness surpassed that of ermine. They advanced to the meadow where Rogero was contending so valiantly against the hobgoblins, who all retired at their approach. They drew near, they extended their hands to the young warrior, whose cheeks glowed with the flush of exercise and modesty. Grateful for their assistance, he expressed his thanks, and, having no heart to refuse them, followed their guidance to the gate of the city.\n\nThis grand and beautiful entrance was adorned by a portico of four vast columns, all of diamond. Whether they were real diamond or artificial I cannot say. What matter is it, so long as they appeared to the eye like diamond, and nothing could be more gay and splendid.\n\nOn the threshold, and between the columns, was seen a bevy of charming young women, who played and frolicked together. They all ran to receive Rogero, and conducted him into the palace, which appeared like a paradise.\n\nWe might well call by that name this abode, where the hours flew by, without account, in ever-new delights. The bare idea of satiety, want, and, above all, of age, never entered the minds of the inhabitants. They experienced no sensations except those of luxury and gayety; the cup of happiness seemed for them ever-flowing and exhaustless. The two young damsels to whom Rogero owed his deliverance from the hobgoblins conducted him to the apartment of their mistress. The beautiful Alcina advanced, and greeted him with an air at once dignified and courteous. All her court surrounded the paladin, and rendered him the most flattering attentions. The castle was less admirable for its magnificence than for the charms of those who inhabited it. They were of either sex, well matched in beauty, youth, and grace; but among this charming group the brilliant Alcina shone, as the sun outshines the stars. The young warrior was fascinated. All that he had heard from the myrtle-tree appeared to him but a vile calumny. How could he suspect that falsehood and treason veiled themselves under smiles and the ingenuous air of truth? He doubted not that Astolpho had deserved his fate, and perhaps a punishment more severe; he regarded all his stories as dictated by a disappointed spirit, and a thirst for revenge. But we must not condemn Rogero too harshly, for he was the victim of magic power.\n\nThey seated themselves at table, and immediately harmonious lyres and harps waked the air with the most ravishing notes. The charms of poetry were added in entertaining recitals; the magnificence of the feast would have done credit to a royal board. The traitress forgot nothing which might charm the paladin, and attach him to the spot, meaning, when she should grow tired of him, to metamorphose him as she had done others. In the same manner passed each succeeding day. Games of pleasant exercise, the chase, the dance, or rural sports, made the hours pass quickly; while they gave zest to the refreshment of the bath, or sleep.\n\nThus Rogero led a life of ease and luxury, while Charlemagne and Agramant were struggling for empire. But I cannot linger with him while the amiable and courageous Bradamante is night and day directing her uncertain steps to every spot where the slightest chance invites her, in the hope of recovering Rogero.\n\nI will therefore say that, having sought him in vain in fields and in cities, she knew not whither next to direct her steps. She did not apprehend the death of Rogero. The fall of such a hero would have reëchoed from the Hydaspes to the farthest river of the West; but, not knowing whether he was on the earth or in the air, she concluded, as a last resource, to return to the cavern which contained the tomb of Merlin, to ask of him some sure direction to the object of her search.\nWhile this thought occupied her mind, Melissa, the sage enchantress, suddenly appeared before her. This virtuous and beneficent magician had discovered by her spells that Rogero was passing his time in pleasure and idleness, forgetful of his honor and his sovereign. Not able to endure the thought that one who was born to be a hero should waste his years in base repose, and leave a sullied reputation in the memory of survivors, she saw that vigorous measures must be employed to draw him forth into the paths of virtue. Melissa was not blinded by her affection for the amiable paladin, like Atlantes, who, intent only on preserving Rogero’s life, cared nothing for his fame. It was that old enchanter whose arts had guided the Hippogriff to the isle of the too charming Alcina, where he hoped his favorite would learn to forget honor, and lose the love of glory.\n\nAt the sight of Melissa joy lighted up the countenance of Bradamante, and hope animated her breast. Melissa concealed nothing from her, but told her how Rogero was in the toils of Alcina. Bradamante was plunged in grief and terror; but the kind enchantress calmed her, dispelled her fears, and promised that before many days she would lead back the paladin to her feet.\n\n“My daughter,” she said, “give me the ring which you wear, and which possesses the power to overcome enchantments. By means of it I doubt not but that I may enter the stronghold where the false Alcina holds Rogero in durance, and may succeed in vanquishing her and liberating him.” Bradamante unhesitatingly delivered her the ring, recommending Rogero to her best efforts. Melissa then summoned by her art a huge palfrey, black as jet, excepting one foot, which was bay. Mounted upon this animal, she rode with such speed that by the next morning she had reached the abode of Alcina.\n\nShe here transformed herself into the perfect resemblance of the old magician Atlantes, adding a palm-breadth to her height, and enlarging her whole figure. Her chin she covered with a long beard, and seamed her whole visage well with wrinkles. She assumed also his voice and manner, and watched her chance to find Rogero alone. At last she found him, dressed in a rich tunic of silk and gold, a collar of precious stones about his neck, and his arms, once so rough with exercise, decorated with bracelets. His air and his every motion indicated effeminacy, and he seemed to retain nothing of Rogero but the name; such power had the enchantress obtained over him.\n\nMelissa, under the form of his old instructor, presented herself before him, wearing a stern and serious visage. “Is this, then,” she said, “the fruit of all my labors? Is it for this that I fed you on the marrow of bears and lions, that I taught you to subdue dragons, and, like Hercules, strangle serpents in your youthful grasp, only to make you, by all my cares, a feeble Adonis? My nightly watchings of the stars, of the yet warm fibres of animals, the lots I have cast, the points of nativity that I have calculated, have they all falsely indicated that you were born for greatness? Who could have believed that you would become the slave of a base enchantress? O Rogero, learn to know this Alcina, learn to understand her arts and to countervail them. Take this ring, place it on your finger, return to her presence, and see for yourself what are her real charms.”\n\nAt these words, Rogero, confused, abashed, cast his eyes upon the ground, and knew not what to answer. Melissa seized the moment, slipped the ring on his finger, and the paladin was himself again. What a thunderclap to him! Overcome by shame, he dared not to encounter the looks of his instructor. When at last he raised his eyes he beheld not that venerable form, but the priestess Melissa, who in virtue of the ring now appeared in her true person. She told him of the motives which had led her to come to his rescue, of the griefs and regrets of Bradamante, and of her unwearied search for him. “That charming Amazon,” she said, “sends you this ring, which is a sovereign antidote to all enchantments. She would have sent you her heart in my hands, if it would have had greater power to serve you.”\n\nIt was needless for Melissa to say more. Rogero’s love for Alcina, being but the work of enchantment, vanished as soon as the enchantment was withdrawn, and he now hated her with an equal intensity, seeing no longer anything in her but her vices, and feeling only resentment for the shame that she had put upon him.\n\nHis surprise when he again beheld Alcina was no less than his indignation. Fortified by his ring from her enchantments, he saw her as she was, a monster of ugliness. All her charms were artificial, and, truly viewed, were rather deformities. She was, in fact, older than Hecuba or the Sibyl of Cumæ; but an art, which it is to be regretted our times have lost, enabled her to appear charming, and to clothe herself in all the attractions of youth. Rogero now saw all this, but, governed by the counsels of Melissa, he concealed his surprise, assumed under some pretext his armor, long neglected, and bound to his side Belisarda, his trusty sword, taking also the buckler of Atlantes, covered with its veil.\n\nHe then selected a horse from the stables of Alcina, without exciting her suspicions; but he left the Hippogriff, by the advice of Melissa, who promised to take him in charge, and train him to a more manageable state. The horse he took was Rabican, which belonged to Astolpho. He restored the ring to Melissa.\n\nRogero had not ridden far when he met one of the huntsmen of Alcina, bearing a falcon on his wrist, and followed by a dog. The huntsman was mounted on a powerful horse, and came boldly up to the paladin, demanding, in a somewhat imperious manner, whither he was going so rapidly. Rogero disdained to stop or to reply; whereupon the huntsman, not doubting that he was about making his escape, said, “What if I, with my falcon, stop your ride?” So saying, he threw off the bird, which even Rabican could not equal in speed. The huntsman then leapt from his horse, and the animal, open-mouthed, darted after Rogero with the swiftness of an arrow. The huntsman also ran as if the wind or fire bore him, and the dog was equal to Rabican in swiftness. Rogero, finding flight impossible, stopped and faced his pursuers; but his sword was useless against such foes. The insolent huntsman assailed him with words, and struck him with his whip, the only weapon he had; the dog bit his feet, and the horse drove at him with his hoofs. At the same time the falcon flew over his head and over Rabican’s and attacked them with claws and wings, so that the horse in his fright began to be unmanageable. At that moment the sound of trumpets and cymbals was heard in the valley, and it was evident that Alcina had ordered out all her array to go in pursuit. Rogero felt that there was no time to be lost, and luckily remembered the shield of Atlantes, which he bore suspended from his neck. He unveiled it, and the charm worked wonderfully. The huntsman, the dog, the horse, fell flat; the trembling wings of the falcon could no longer sustain her, and she fell senseless to the ground. Rogero, rid of their annoyances, left them in their trance, and rode away.\n\nMeanwhile Alcina, with all the force she could muster, sallied forth from her palace in pursuit. Melissa, left behind, took advantage of the opportunity to ransack all the rooms, protected by the ring. She undid one by one all the talismans and spells which she found, broke the seals, burned the images, and untied the hag-knots. Thence, hurrying through the fields, she disenchanted the victims changed into trees, fountains, stones, or brutes; all of whom recovered their liberty, and vowed eternal gratitude to their deliverer. They made their escape, with all possible despatch, to the realms of the good Logestilla, whence they departed to their several homes.\n\nAstolpho was the first whom Melissa liberated, for Rogero had particularly recommended him to her care. She aided him to recover his arms, and particularly that precious golden-headed lance which once was Argalia’s. The enchantress mounted with him upon the winged horse, and in a short time arrived through the air at the castle of Logestilla, where Rogero joined them soon after.\n\nIn this abode the friends passed a short period of delightful and improving intercourse with the sage Logestilla and her virtuous court; and then each departed, Rogero with the Hippogriff, ring, and buckler; Astolpho with his golden lance, and mounted on Rabican, the fleetest of steeds. To Rogero Logestilla gave a bit and bridle suited to govern the Hippogriff; and to Astolpho a horn of marvellous powers, to be sounded only when all other weapons were unavailing.\n\n————\n\nWe left the charming Angelica at the moment when, in her flight from her contending lovers, Sacripant and Rinaldo, she met an aged hermit. We have seen that her request to the hermit was to furnish her the means of gaining the sea-coast, eager to avoid Rinaldo, whom she hated, by leaving France and Europe itself. The pretended hermit, who was no other than a vile magician, knowing well that it would not be agreeable to his false gods to aid Angelica in this undertaking, feigned to comply with her desire. He supplied her a horse, into which he had by his arts caused a subtle devil to enter, and, having mounted Angelica on the animal, directed her what course to take to reach the sea.\n\nAngelica rode on her way without suspicion, but when arrived at the shore, the demon urged the animal headlong into the water. Angelica in vain attempted to turn him back to the land; he continued his course till, as night approached, he landed with his burden on a sandy headland.\n\nAngelica, finding herself alone, abandoned in this frightful solitude, remained without movement, as if stupefied, with hands joined and eyes turned towards heaven, till at last, pouring forth a torrent of tears, she exclaimed: “Cruel fortune, have you not yet exhausted your rage against me? To what new miseries do you doom me? Alas! then finish your work! Deliver me a prey to some ferocious beast, or by whatever fate you choose bring me to an end. I will be thankful to you for terminating my life and my misery.” At last, exhausted by her sorrows, she fell asleep, and sunk prostrate on the sand.\n\nBefore recounting what next befell, we must declare what place it was upon which the unhappy lady was now thrown. In the sea that washes the coast of Ireland there is an island called Ebuda, whose inhabitants, once numerous, had been wasted by the anger of Proteus till there were now but few left. This deity was incensed by some neglect of the usual honors which he had in old times received from the inhabitants of the land, and, to execute his vengeance, had sent a horrid sea-monster, called an Orc, to devour them. Such were the terrors of his ravages that the whole people of the isle had shut themselves up in the principal town, and relied on their walls alone to protect them. In this distress they applied to the Oracle for advice, and were directed to appease the wrath of the sea-monster by offering to him the fairest virgin that the country could produce.\n\nNow it so happened that the very day when this dreadful oracle was announced, and when the fatal mandate had gone forth to seek among the fairest maidens of the land one to be offered to the monster, some sailors, landing on the beach where Angelica was, beheld that beauty as she lay asleep.\n\nO blind Chance! whose power in human affairs is but too great, canst thou then abandon to the teeth of a horrible monster those charms which different sovereigns took arms against one another to possess? Alas! the lovely Angelica is destined to be the victim of those cruel islanders.\nStill asleep, she was bound by the Ebudians, and it was not until she was carried on board the vessel that she came to a knowledge of her situation. The wind filled the sails and wafted the ship swiftly to the port, where all that beheld her agreed that she was unquestionably the victim selected by Proteus himself to be his prey. Who can tell the screams, the mortal anguish of this unhappy maiden, the reproaches she addressed even to the heavens themselves, when the dreadful information of her cruel fate was made known to her? I cannot; let me rather turn to a happier part of my story.\n\nRogero left the palace of Logestilla, careering on his flying courser far above the tops of the mountains, and borne westward by the Hippogriff, which he guided with ease, by means of the bridle that Melissa had given him. Anxious as he was to recover Bradamante, he could not fail to be delighted at the view his rapid flight presented of so many vast regions and populous countries as he passed over in his career. At last he approached the shores of England, and perceived an immense army in all the splendor of military pomp, as if about to go forth flushed with hopes of victory. He caused the Hippogriff to alight not far from the scene, and found himself immediately surrounded by admiring spectators, knights and soldiers, who could not enough indulge their curiosity and wonder. Rogero learned, in reply to his questions, that the fine array of troops before him was the army destined to go to the aid of the French Emperor, in compliance with the request presented by the illustrious Rinaldo, as ambassador of King Charles, his uncle.\n\nBy this time the curiosity of the English chevaliers was partly gratified in beholding the Hippogriff at rest, and Rogero, to renew their surprise and delight, remounted the animal, and, slapping spurs to his sides, made him launch into the air with the rapidity of a meteor, and directed his flight still westwardly, till he came within sight of the coasts of Ireland. Here he descried what seemed to be a fair damsel, alone, fast chained to a rock which projected into the sea. What was his astonishment when, drawing nigh, he beheld the beautiful princess Angelica! That day she had been led forth and bound to the rock, there to wait till the sea-monster should come to devour her. Rogero exclaimed as he came near, “What cruel hands, what barbarous soul, what fatal chance can have loaded thee with those chains?” Angelica replied by a torrent of tears, at first her only response; then, in a trembling voice, she disclosed to him the horrible destiny for which she was there exposed. While she spoke, a terrible roaring was heard far off on the sea. The huge monster soon came in sight, part of his body appearing above the waves and part concealed. Angelica, half dead with fear, abandoned herself to despair.\n\nRogero, lance in rest, spurred his Hippogriff toward the Orc, and gave him a thrust. The horrible monster was like nothing that nature produces. It was but one mass of tossing and twisting body, with nothing of the animal but head, eyes, and mouth, the last furnished with tusks like those of the wild boar. Rogero’s lance had struck him between the eyes; but rock and iron are not more impenetrable than were his scales. The knight, seeing the fruitlessness of the first blow, prepared to give a second. The animal, beholding upon the water the shadow of the great wings of the Hippogriff, abandoned his prey, and turned to seize what seemed nearer. Rogero took the opportunity, and dealt him furious blows on various parts of his body, taking care to keep clear of his murderous teeth; but the scales resisted every attack. The Orc beat the water with his tail till he raised a foam which enveloped Rogero and his steed, so that the knight hardly knew whether he was in the water or the air. He began to fear that the wings of the Hippogriff would be so drenched with water that they would cease to sustain him. At that moment Rogero bethought him of the magic shield which hung at his saddle-bow; but the fear that Angelica would also be blinded by its glare discouraged him from employing it. Then he remembered the ring which Melissa had given him, the power of which he had so lately proved. He hastened to Angelica and placed it on her finger. Then, uncovering the buckler, he turned its bright disk full in the face of the detestable Orc. The effect was instantaneous. The monster, deprived of sense and motion, rolled over on the sea, and lay floating on his back. Rogero would fain have tried the effect of his lance on the now exposed parts, but Angelica implored him to lose no time in delivering her from her chains before the monster should revive. Rogero, moved with her entreaties, hastened to do so, and, having unbound her, made her mount behind him on the Hippogriff. The animal, spurning the earth, shot up into the air, and rapidly sped his way through it. Rogero, to give time to the princess to rest after her cruel agitations, soon sought the earth again, alighting on the shore of Brittany. Near the shore a thick wood presented itself, which resounded with the songs of birds. In the midst, a fountain of transparent water bathed the turf of a little meadow. A gentle hill rose near by. Rogero, making the Hippogriff alight in the meadow, dismounted, and took Angelica from the horse.\n\nWhen the first tumults of emotion had subsided Angelica, casting her eyes downward, beheld the precious ring upon her finger, whose virtues she was well acquainted with, for it was the very ring which the Saracen Brunello had robbed her of. She drew it from her finger and placed it in her mouth, and, quicker than we can tell it, disappeared from the sight of the paladin.\n\nRogero looked around him on all sides, like one frantic, but soon remembered the ring which he had so lately placed on her finger. Struck with the ingratitude which could thus recompense his services, he exclaimed: “Thankless beauty, is this then the reward you make me? Do you prefer to rob me of my ring rather than receive it as a gift? Willingly would I have given it to you, had you but asked it.” Thus he said, searching on all sides with arms extended like a blind man, hoping to recover by the touch what was lost to sight; but he sought in vain. The cruel beauty was already far away.\n\nThough sensible of her obligations to her deliverer, her first necessity was for clothing, food, and repose. She soon reached a shepherd’s hut, where, entering unseen, she found what sufficed for her present relief. An old herdsman inhabited the hut, whose charges consisted of a drove of mares. When recruited by repose Angelica selected one of the mares from the flock, and, mounting the animal, felt the desire revive in her mind of returning to her home in the East, and for that purpose would gladly have accepted the protection of Orlando or of Sacripant across those wide regions which divided her from her own country. In hopes of meeting with one or the other of them she pursued her way.\n\nMeanwhile Rogero, despairing of seeing Angelica again, returned to the tree where he had left his winged horse, but had the mortification to find that the animal had broken his bridle and escaped. This loss, added to his previous disappointment, overwhelmed him with vexation. Sadly he gathered up his arms, threw his buckler over his shoulders, and, taking the first path that offered, soon found himself within the verge of a dense and widespread forest.\n\nHe had proceeded for some distance when he heard a noise on his right, and, listening attentively, distinguished the clash of arms. He made his way toward the place whence the sound proceeded, and found two warriors engaged in mortal combat. One of them was a knight of a noble and manly bearing, the other a fierce giant. The knight appeared to exert consummate address in defending herself against the massive club of the giant, evading his strokes, or parrying them with sword or shield. Rogero stood spectator of the combat, for he did not allow himself to interfere in it, though a secret sentiment inclined him strongly to take part with the knight. At length he saw with grief the massive club fall directly on the head of the knight, who yielded to the blow, and fell prostrate. The giant sprang forward to despatch him, and for that purpose unlaced his helmet, when Rogero, with dismay, recognized the face of Bradamante. He cried aloud, “Hold, miscreant!” and sprang forward with drawn sword. Whereupon the giant, as if he cared not to enter upon another combat, lifted Bradamante on his shoulders, and ran with her into the forest.\n\nRogero plunged after him, but the long legs of the giant carried him forward so fast that the paladin could hardly keep him in sight. At length they issued from the wood, and Rogero perceived before him a rich palace, built of marble, and adorned with sculptures executed by a master hand. Into this edifice, through a golden door, the giant passed, and Rogero followed; but, on looking round, saw nowhere either the giant or Bradamante. He ran from room to room, calling aloud on his cowardly foe to turn and meet him; but got no response, nor caught another glimpse of the giant or his prey. In his vain pursuit he met, without knowing them, Ferrau, Florismart, King Gradasso, Orlando, and many others, all of whom had been entrapped like himself into this enchanted castle. It was a new stratagem of the magician Atlantes to draw Rogero into his power, and to secure also those who might by any chance endanger his safety. What Rogero had taken for Bradamante was a mere phantom. That charming lady was far away, full of anxiety for her Rogero, whose coming she had long expected.\n\nThe Emperor had committed to her charge the city and garrison of Marseilles, and she held the post against the infidels with valor and discretion. One day Melissa suddenly presented herself before her. Anticipating her questions, she said, “Fear not for Rogero; he lives, and is as ever true to you; but he has lost his liberty. The fell enchanter has again succeeded in making him a prisoner. If you would deliver him, mount your horse and follow me.” She told her in what manner Atlantes had deceived Rogero, in deluding his eyes with the phantom of herself in peril. “Such,” she continued, “will be his arts in your own case, if you penetrate the forest and approach that castle. You will think you behold Rogero, when, in fact, you see only the enchanter himself. Be not deceived, plunge your sword into his body, and trust me when I tell you that, in slaying him, you will restore not only Rogero, but with him many of the bravest knights of France, whom the wizard’s arts have withdrawn from the camp of their sovereign.”\n\nBradamante promptly armed herself, and mounted her horse. Melissa led her by forced journeys, by field and forest, beguiling the way with conversation on the theme which interested her hearer most. When at last they reached the forest, she repeated once more her instructions, and then took her leave, for fear the enchanter might espy her, and be put on his guard.\n\nBradamante rode on about two miles when suddenly she beheld Rogero, as it appeared to her, hard pressed by two fierce giants. While she hesitated she heard his voice calling on her for help. At once the cautions of Melissa lost their weight. A sudden doubt of the faith and truth of her kind monitress flashed across her mind. “Shall I not believe my own eyes and ears?” she said, and rushed forward to his defence. Rogero fled, pursued by the giants, and Bradamante followed, passing with them through the castle gate. When there, Bradamante was undeceived, for neither giant nor knight was to be seen. She found herself a prisoner, but had not the consolation of knowing that she shared the imprisonment of her beloved. She saw various forms of men and women, but could recognize none of them; and their lot was the same with respect to her. Each viewed the others under some illusion of the fancy, wearing the semblance of giants, dwarfs, or even four-footed animals, so that there was no companionship or communication between them.\n————\n\n# ASTOLPHO’S ADVENTURES CONTINUED, AND ISABELLA’S BEGUN\n\nWhen Astolpho escaped from the cruel Alcina, after a short abode in the realm of the virtuous Logestilla, he desired to return to his native country. Logestilla lent him the best vessel of her fleet to convey him to the mainland. She gave him at parting a wonderful book, which taught the secret of overcoming all manners of enchantments, and begged him to carry it always with him, out of regard for her. She also gave him another gift, which surpassed everything of the kind that mortal workmanship can frame; yet it was nothing in appearance but a simple horn.\n\nAstolpho, protected by these gifts, thanked the good fairy, took leave of her, and set out on his return to France. His voyage was prosperous, and on reaching the desired port he took leave of the faithful mariners, and continued his journey by land. As he proceeded over mountains and through valleys he often met with bands of robbers, wild beasts, and venomous serpents, but he had only to sound his horn to put them all to flight.\n\nHaving landed in France, and traversed many provinces on his way to the army, he one day, in crossing a forest, arrived beside a fountain, and alighted to drink. While he stooped at the fountain a young rustic sprang from the copse, mounted Rabican, and rode away. It was a new trick of the enchanter Atlantes. Astolpho, hearing the noise, turned his head just in time to see his loss; and, starting up, pursued the thief, who, on his part, did not press the horse to his full speed, but just kept in sight of his pursuer till they both issued from the forest; and then Rabican and his rider took shelter in a castle which stood near. Astolpho followed, and penetrated without difficulty within the courtyard of the castle, where he looked around for the rider and his horse, but could see no trace of either, nor any person of whom he could make inquiry. Suspecting that enchantment was employed to embarrass him, he bethought him of his book, and on consulting it discovered that his suspicions were well founded. He also learned what course to pursue. He was directed to raise the stone which served as a threshold, under which a spirit lay pent, who would willingly escape, and leave the castle free of access. Astolpho applied his strength to lift aside the stone. Thereupon the magician put his arts in force. The castle was full of prisoners, and the magician caused that to all of them Astolpho should appear in some false guise—to some a wild beast, to others a giant, to others a bird of prey. Thus all assailed him, and would quickly have made an end of him, if he had not bethought him of his horn. No sooner had he blown a blast than, at the horrid larum, fled the cavaliers and the necromancer with them, like a flock of pigeons at the sound of the fowler’s gun. Astolpho then renewed his efforts on the stone, and turned it over. The under face was all inscribed with magical characters, which the knight defaced, as directed by his book; and no sooner had he done so, than the castle, with its walls and turrets, vanished into smoke.\n\nThe knights and ladies set at liberty were, besides Rogero and Bradamante, Orlando, Gradasso, Florismart, and many more. At the sound of the horn they fled, one and all, men and steeds, except Rabican, which Astolpho secured, in spite of his terror. As soon as the sound had ceased Rogero recognized Bradamante, whom he had daily met during their imprisonment, but had been prevented from knowing by the enchanter’s arts. No words can tell the delight with which they recognized each other, and recounted mutually all that had happened to each since they were parted. Rogero took advantage of the opportunity to press his suit, and found Bradamante as propitious as he could wish, were it not for a single obstacle, the difference of their faiths. “If he would obtain her in marriage,” she said, “he must in due form demand her of her father, Duke Aymon, and must abandon his false prophet, and become a Christian.” The latter step was one which Rogero had for some time intended taking, for reasons of his own. He therefore gladly accepted the terms, and proposed that they should at once repair to the abbey of Vallombrosa, whose towers were visible at no great distance. Thither they turned their horses’ heads, and we will leave them to find their way without our company.\n\nI know not if my readers recollect that at the moment when Rogero had just delivered Angelica from the voracious Orc that scornful beauty placed her ring in her mouth, and vanished out of sight. At the same time the Hippogriff shook off his bridle, soared away, and flew to rejoin his former master, very naturally returning to his accustomed stable. Here Astolpho found him, to his very great delight. He knew the animal’s powers, having seen Rogero ride him, and he longed to fly abroad over all the earth, and see various nations and peoples from his airy course. He had heard Logestilla’s directions how to guide the animal, and saw her fit a bridle to his head. He therefore was able, out of all the bridles he found in the stable, to select one suitable, and, placing Rabican’s saddle on the Hippogriff’s back, nothing seemed to prevent his immediate departure. Yet before he went he bethought him of placing Rabican in hands where he would be safe, and whence he might recover him in time of need. While he stood deliberating where he should find a messenger, he saw Bradamante approach. That fair warrior had been parted from Rogero on their way to the abbey of Vallombrosa, by an inopportune adventure which had called the knight away. She was now returning to Montalban, having arranged with Rogero to join her there. To Bradamante, therefore, his fair cousin, Astolpho committed Rabican, and also the lance of gold, which would only be an incumbrance in his aerial excursion. Bradamante took charge of both; and Astolpho, bidding her farewell, soared in air.\n\nAmong those delivered by Astolpho from the magician’s castle was Orlando. Following the guide of chance, the paladin found himself at the close of day in a forest, and stopped at the foot of a mountain. Surprised to discern a light which came from a cleft in the rock, he approached, guided by the ray, and discovered a narrow passage in the mountain-side, which led into a deep grotto.\n\nOrlando fastened his horse, and then, putting aside the bushes that resisted his passage, stepped down from rock to rock till he reached a sort of cavern. Entering it, he perceived a lady, young and handsome, as well as he could discover through the signs of distress which agitated her countenance. Her only companion was an old woman, who seemed to be regarded by her young partner with terror and indignation. The courteous paladin saluted the women respectfully, and begged to know by whose barbarity they had been subjected to such imprisonment.\n\nThe younger lady replied, in a voice often broken with sobs:\n\n“Though I know well that my recital will subject me to worse treatment by the barbarous man who keeps me here, to whom this woman will not fail to report it, yet I will not hide from you the facts. Ah! why should I fear his rage? If he should take my life, I know not what better boon than death I can ask.\n\n“My name is Isabella. I am the daughter of the king of Galicia, or rather I should say misfortune and grief are my parents. Young, rich, modest, and of tranquil temper, all things appeared to combine to render my lot happy. Alas! I see myself to-day poor, humbled, miserable, and destined perhaps to yet further afflictions. It is a year since, my father having given notice that he would open the lists for a tournament at Bayonne, a great number of chevaliers from all quarters came together at our court. Among these Zerbino, son of the king of Scotland, victorious in all combats, eclipsed by his beauty and his valor all the rest. Before departing from the court of Galicia he testified the wish to espouse me, and I consented that he should demand my hand of the king, my father. But I was a Mahometan, and Zerbino a Christian, and my father refused his consent. The prince, called home by his father to take command of the forces destined to the assistance of the French Emperor, prevailed on me to be married to him secretly, and to follow him to Scotland. He caused a galley to be prepared to receive me, and placed in command of it the chevalier Oderic, a Biscayan, famous for his exploits both by land and sea. On the day appointed, Oderic brought his vessel to a seaside resort of my father’s, where I embarked. Some of my domestics accompanied me, and thus I departed from my native land.\n\n“Sailing with a fair wind, after some hours we were assailed by a violent tempest. It was to no purpose that we took in all sail; we were driven before the wind directly upon the rocky shore. Seeing no other hopes of safety, Oderic placed me in a boat, followed himself with a few of his men, and made for land. We reached it through infinite peril, and I no sooner felt the firm land beneath my feet, than I knelt down and poured out heartfelt thanks to the Providence that had preserved me.\n\n“The shore where we landed appeared to be uninhabited. We saw no dwelling to shelter us, no road to lead us to a more hospitable spot. A high mountain rose before us, whose base stretched into the sea. It was here the infamous Oderic, in spite of my tears and entreaties, sold me to a band of pirates, who fancied I might be an acceptable present to their prince, the Sultan of Morocco. This cavern is their den, and here they keep me under the guard of this woman, until it shall suit their convenience to carry me away.”\n\nIsabella had hardly finished her recital when a troop of armed men began to enter the cavern. Seeing the prince Orlando, one said to the rest, “What bird is this we have caught, without even setting a snare for him?” Then addressing Orlando, “It was truly civil in you, friend, to come hither with that handsome coat of armor and vest, the very things I want.” “You shall pay for them, then,” said Orlando; and seizing a half-burnt brand from the fire, he hurled it at him, striking his head, and stretching him lifeless on the floor.\n\nThere was a massy table in the middle of the cavern, used for the pirates’ repasts. Orlando lifted it and hurled it at the robbers as they stood clustered in a group toward the entrance. Half the gang were laid prostrate, with broken heads and limbs; the rest got away as nimbly as they could.\n\nLeaving the den and its inmates to their fate, Orlando, taking Isabella under his protection, pursued his way for some days, without meeting with any adventure.\n\nOne day they saw a band of men advancing, who seemed to be guarding a prisoner, bound hand and foot, as if being carried to execution. The prisoner was a youthful cavalier, of a noble and ingenuous appearance. The band bore the ensigns of Count Anselm, head of the treacherous house of Maganza. Orlando desired Isabella to wait, while he rode forward to inquire the meaning of this array. Approaching, he demanded of the leader who his prisoner was, and of what crime he had been guilty. The man replied that the prisoner was a murderer, by whose hand Pinabel, the son of Count Anselm, had been treacherously slain. At these words the prisoner exclaimed, “I am no murderer, nor have I been in any way the cause of the young man’s death.” Orlando, knowing the cruel and ferocious character of the chiefs of the house of Maganza, needed no more to satisfy him that the youth was the victim of injustice. He commanded the leader of the troop to release his victim, and, receiving an insolent reply, dashed him to the earth with a stroke of his lance; then by a few vigorous blows dispersed the band, leaving deadly marks on those who were slowest to quit the field.\n\nOrlando then hastened to unbind the prisoner, and to assist him to reclothe himself in his armor, which the false Magencian had dared to assume. He then led him to Isabella, who now approached the scene of action. How can we picture the joy, the astonishment, with which Isabella recognized in him Zerbino, her husband, and the prince discovered her whom he had believed overwhelmed in the waves! They embraced one another, and wept for joy. Orlando, sharing in their happiness, congratulated himself in having been the instrument of it. The princess recounted to Zerbino what the illustrious paladin had done for her, and the prince threw himself at Orlando’s feet, and thanked him as having twice preserved his life.\n\nWhile these exchanges of congratulation and thankfulness were going on, a sound in the underwood attracted their attention, and caused the two knights to brace their helmets and stand on their guard. What the cause of the interruption was we shall record in another chapter.\n\n————\n\nFrance was at this time the theatre of dreadful events. The Saracens and the Christians, in numerous encounters, slew one another. On one occasion Rinaldo led an attack on the infidel columns, broke and scattered them, till he found himself opposite to a knight whose armor (whether by accident or by choice, it matters not) bore the blazon of Orlando. It was Dardinel, the young and brave prince of Zumara, and Rinaldo remarked him by the slaughter he spread all around. “Ah,” said he to himself, “let us pluck up this dangerous plant before it has grown to its full height.”\n\nAs Rinaldo advanced, the crowd opened before him, the Christians to let his sword have free course, the Pagans to escape its sweep. Dardinel and he stood face to face. Rinaldo exclaimed, fiercely, “Young man, whoever gave you that noble buckler to bear made you a dangerous gift; I should like to see how you are able to defend those quarterings, red and white. If you cannot defend them against me, how pray will you do so when Orlando challenges them?” Dardinel replied: “Thou shalt learn that I can defend the arms I bear, and shed new glory upon them. No one shall rend them from me but with life.” Saying these words, Dardinel rushed upon Rinaldo with sword uplifted.\n\nThe chill of mortal terror filled the souls of the Saracens when they beheld Rinaldo advance to attack the prince, like a lion against a young bull. The first blow came from the hand of Dardinel, and the weapon rebounded from Mambrino’s helmet without effect. Rinaldo smiled, and said, “I will now show you if my strokes are more effectual.” At these words he thrust the unfortunate Dardinel in the middle of his breast. The blow was so violent that the cruel weapon pierced the body, and came out a palm-breadth behind his back. Through this wound the life of Dardinel issued with his blood, and his body fell helpless to the ground.\n\nAs a flower which the passing plough has uprooted languishes, and droops its head, so Dardinel, his visage covered with the paleness of death, expires, and the hopes of an illustrious race perish with him.\n\nLike waters kept back by a dike, which, when the dike is broken, spread abroad through all the country, so the Moors, no longer kept in column by the example of Dardinel, fled in all directions. Rinaldo despised too much such easy victories to pursue them; he wished for no combats but with brave men. At the same time, the other paladins made terrible slaughter of the Moors. Charles himself, Oliver, Guido, and Ogier the Dane, carried death into their ranks on all sides.\n\nThe infidels seemed doomed to perish to a man on that dreadful day; but the wise king, Marsilius, at last put some slight degree of method into the general rout. He collected the remnant of the troops, formed them into a battalion, and retreated in tolerable order to his camp. That camp was well fortified by intrenchments and a broad ditch. Thither the fugitives hastened, and by degrees all that remained of the Moorish army was brought together there.\n\nThe Emperor might perhaps that night have crushed his enemy entirely; but not thinking it prudent to expose his troops, fatigued as they were, to an attack upon a camp so well fortified, he contented himself with encompassing the enemy with his troops, prepared to make a regular siege. During the night the Moors had time to see the extent of their loss. Their tents resounded with lamentations. This warrior had to mourn a brother, that a friend; many suffered with grievous wounds, all trembled at the fate in store for them.\n\nThere were two young Moors, both of humble rank, who gave proof at that time of attachment and fidelity rare in the history of man. Cloridan and Medoro had followed their prince, Dardinel, to the wars of France. Cloridan, a bold huntsman, combined strength with activity. Medoro was a mere youth, his cheeks yet fair and blooming. Of all the Saracens, no one united so much grace and beauty. His light hair was set off by his black and sparkling eyes. The two friends were together on guard at the rampart. About midnight they gazed on the scene in deep dejection. Medoro, with tears in his eyes, spoke of the good prince Dardinel, and could not endure the thought that his body should be cast out on the plain, deprived of funeral honors. “O my friend,” said he, “must then the body of our prince be the prey of wolves and ravens? Alas! when I remember how he loved me, I feel that if I should sacrifice my life to do him honor, I should not do more than my duty. I wish, dear friend, to seek out his body on the battlefield, and give it burial, and I hope to be able to pass through King Charles’s camp without discovery, as they are probably all asleep. You, Cloridan, will be able to say for me, if I should die in the adventure, that gratitude and fidelity to my prince were my inducements.”\n\nCloridan was both surprised and touched with this proof of the young man’s devotion. He loved him tenderly, and tried for a long time every effort to dissuade him from his design; but he found Medoro determined to accomplish his object or die in the endeavor.\n\nCloridan, unable to change his purpose, said, “I will go with you, Medoro, and help you in this generous enterprise. I value not life compared with honor, and if I did, do you suppose, dear friend, that I could live without you? I would rather fall by the arms of our enemies than die of grief for the loss of you.”\n\nWhen the two friends were relieved from their guard duty they went without any followers into the camp of the Christians. All there was still; the fires were dying out; there was no fear of any attempt on the part of the Saracens, and the soldiers, overcome by fatigue or wine, slept secure, lying upon the ground in the midst of their arms and equipage. Cloridan stopped, and said, “Medoro, I am not going to quit this camp without taking vengeance for the death of our prince. Keep watch, be on your guard that no one shall surprise us; I mean to mark a road with my sword through the ranks of our enemies.” So saying, he entered the tent where Alpheus slept, who a year before had joined the camp of Charles, and pretended to be a great physician and astrologer. But his science had deceived him, if it gave him hope of dying peacefully in his bed at a good old age; his lot was to die with little warning. Cloridan ran his sword through his heart. A Greek and a German followed, who had been playing late at dice: fortunate if they had continued their game a little longer; but they never reckoned a throw like this among their chances. Cloridan next came to the unlucky Grillon, whose head lay softly on his pillow. He dreamed probably of the feast from which he had but just retired; for when Cloridan cut off his head wine flowed forth with the blood.\n\nThe two young Moors might have penetrated even to the tent of Charlemagne; but knowing that the paladins encamped around him kept watch by turns, and judging that it was impossible they should all be asleep, they were afraid to go too near. They might also have obtained rich booty; but, intent only on their object, they crossed the camp, and arrived at length at the bloody field, where bucklers, lances, and swords lay scattered in the midst of corpses of poor and rich, common soldier and prince, horses and pools of blood. This terrible scene of carnage would have destroyed all hope of finding what they were in search of until dawn of day, were it not that the moon lent the aid of her uncertain rays.\n\nMedoro raised his eyes to the planet, and exclaimed, “O holy goddess, whom our fathers have adored under three different forms,—thou who displayest thy power in heaven, on earth, and in the underworld,—thou who art seen foremost among the nymphs chasing the beasts of the forest,—cause me to see, I implore thee, the spot where my dear master lies, and make me all my life long follow the example which thou dost exhibit of works of charity and love.”\n\nEither by accident, or that the moon was sensible of the prayer of Medoro, the cloud broke away, and the moonlight burst forth as bright as day. The rays seemed especially to gild the spot where lay the body of Prince Dardinel; and Medoro, bathed in tears and with bleeding heart, recognized him by the quarterings of red and white on his shield.\n\nWith groans stifled by his tears, and lamentations in accents suppressed, not from any fear for himself, for he cared not for life, but lest any one should be roused to interrupt their pious duty while yet incomplete, he proposed to his companion that they should together bear Dardinel on their shoulders, sharing the burden of the beloved remains.\n\nMarching with rapid strides under their precious load, they perceived that the stars began to grow pale, and that the shades of night would soon be dispersed by the dawn. Just then Zerbino, whose extreme valor had urged him far from the camp in pursuit of the fugitives, returning, entered the wood in which they were. Some knights in his train perceived at a distance the two brothers-in-arms. Cloridan saw the troop, and, observing that they dispersed themselves over the plain as if in search of booty, told Medoro to lay down the body, and let each save himself by flight. He dropped his part, thinking that Medoro would do the same; but the good youth loved his prince too well to abandon him, and continued to carry his load singly as well as he might, while Cloridan made his escape. Near by there was a part of the wood tufted as if nothing but wild animals had ever penetrated it. The unfortunate youth, loaded with the weight of his dead master, plunged into its recesses.\n\nCloridan, when he perceived that he had evaded his foes, discovered that Medoro was not with him. “Ah!” exclaimed he, “how could I, dear Medoro, so forget myself as to consult my own safety without heeding yours?” So saying, he retraced the tangled passes of the wood toward the place from whence he had fled. As he approached he heard the noise of horses, and the menacing voices of armed men. Soon he perceived Medoro, on foot, with the cavaliers surrounding him. Zerbino, their commander, bade them seize him. The unhappy Medoro turned now this way, now that, trying to conceal himself behind an oak or a rock, still bearing the body, which he would by no means leave. Cloridan not knowing how to help him, but resolved to perish with him, if he must perish, takes an arrow, fits it to his bow, discharges it, and pierces the breast of a Christian knight, who falls helpless from his horse. The others look this way and that, to discover whence the fatal bolt was sped. One, while demanding of his comrades in what direction the arrow came, received a second in his throat, which stopped his words, and soon closed his eyes to the scene.\nZerbino, furious at the death of his two comrades, ran upon Medoro, seized his golden hair, and dragged him forward to slay him. But the sight of so much youth and beauty commanded pity. He stayed his arm. The young man spoke in suppliant tones. “Ah! signor,” said he, “I conjure you by the God whom you serve, deprive me not of life until I shall have buried the body of the prince, my master. Fear not that I will ask you any other favor; life is not dear to me; I desire death as soon as I shall have performed this sacred duty. Do with me then as you please. Give my limbs a prey to the birds and beasts; only let me first bury my prince.” Medoro pronounced these words with an air so sweet and tender that a heart of stone would have been moved by them. Zerbino was so to the bottom of his soul. He was on the point of uttering words of mercy, when a cruel subaltern, forgetting all respect to his commander, plunged his lance into the breast of the young Moor. Zerbino, enraged at his brutality, turned upon the wretch to take vengeance, but he saved himself by a precipitate flight.\n\nCloridan, who saw Medoro fall, could contain himself no longer. He rushed from his concealment, threw down his bow, and, sword in hand, seemed only desirous of vengeance for Medoro, and to die with him. In a moment, pierced through and through with many wounds, he exerts the last remnant of his strength in dragging himself to Medoro, to die embracing him. The cavaliers left them thus to rejoin Zerbino, whose rage against the murderer of Medoro had drawn him away from the spot.\n\nCloridan died; and Medoro, bleeding copiously, was drawing near his end when help arrived.\n\nA young maiden approached the fallen knights at this critical moment. Her dress was that of a peasant-girl, but her air was noble, and her beauty celestial; sweetness and goodness reigned in her lovely countenance. It was no other than Angelica, the Princess of Cathay.\n\nWhen she had recovered that precious ring, as we have before related, Angelica, knowing its value, felt proud in the power it conferred, travelled alone without fear, not without a secret shame that she had ever been obliged to seek protection in her wanderings of the Count Orlando and of Sacripant. She reproached herself too as with a weakness that she had ever thought of marrying Rinaldo; in fine, her pride grew so high as to persuade her that no man living was worthy to aspire to her hand.\n\nMoved with pity at the sight of the young man wounded, and melted to tears at hearing the cause, she quickly recalled to remembrance the knowledge she had acquired in India, where the virtues of plants and the art of healing formed part of the education even of princesses. The beautiful queen ran into the adjoining meadow to gather plants of virtue to staunch the flow of blood. Meeting on her way a countryman on horseback seeking a strayed heifer, she begged him to come to her assistance, and endeavor to remove the wounded man to a more secure asylum.\n\nAngelica, having prepared the plants by bruising them between two stones, laid them with her fair hand on Medoro’s wound. The remedy soon restored in some degree the strength of the wounded man, who, before he would quit the spot, made them cover with earth and turf the bodies of his friend and of the prince. Then surrendering himself to the pity of his deliverers, he allowed them to place him on the horse of the shepherd, and conduct him to his cottage. It was a pleasant farmhouse on the borders of the wood, bearing marks of comfort and competency. There the shepherd lived with his wife and children. There Angelica tended Medoro, and there, by the devoted care of the beautiful queen, his sad wound closed over, and he recovered his perfect health.\n\nO Count Rinaldo, O King Sacripant! what availed it you to possess so many virtues and such fame? What advantage have you derived from all your high deserts? O hapless king, great Agrican! if you could return to life, how would you endure to see yourself rejected by one who will bow to the yoke of Hymen in favor of a young soldier of humble birth? And thou, Ferrau, and ye numerous others who a hundred times have put your lives at hazard for this cruel beauty, how bitter will it be to you to see her sacrifice you all to the claims of the humble Medoro!\n\nThere, under the low roof of a shepherd, the flame of Hymen was lighted for this haughty queen. She takes the shepherd’s wife to serve in place of mother, the shepherd and his children for witnesses, and marries the happy Medoro.\n\nAngelica, after her marriage, wishing to endow Medoro with the sovereignty of the countries which yet remained to her, took with him the road to the East. She had preserved through all her adventures a bracelet of gold enriched with precious stones, the present of the Count Orlando. Having nothing else wherewith to reward the good shepherd and his wife, who had served her with so much care and fidelity, she took the bracelet from her arm and gave it to them, and then the newly-married couple directed their steps toward those mountains which separate France and Spain, intending to wait at Barcelona a vessel which should take them on their way to the East.\n\n# ORLANDO MAD\n\n————\nOrlando, on the loss of Angelica, laid aside his crest and arms, and arrayed himself in a suit of black armor expressive of his despair. In this guise he carried such slaughter among the ranks of the infidels that both armies were astonished at the achievements of the stranger knight. Mandricardo, who had been absent from the battle, heard the report of these achievements, and determined to test for himself the valor of the knight so extolled. He it was who broke in upon the conference of Zerbino and Isabella, and their benefactor Orlando, as they stood occupied in mutual felicitations, after the happy reunion of the lovers by the prowess of the paladin.\n\nMandricardo, after contemplating the group for a moment, addressed himself to Orlando in these words: “Thou must be the man I seek. For ten days and more I have been on thy track. The fame of thy exploits has brought me hither, that I may measure my strength with thine. Thy crest and shield prove thee the same who spread such slaughter among our troops. But these marks are superfluous, and if I saw thee among a hundred I should know thee by thy martial bearing to be the man I seek.”\n\n“I respect thy courage,” said Orlando; “such a design could not have sprung up in any but a brave and generous soul. If the desire to see me has brought thee hither, I would, if it were possible, show thee my inmost soul. I will remove my visor, that you may satisfy your curiosity; but when you have done so I hope that you will also try and see if my valor corresponds to my appearance.”\n\n“Come on,” said the Saracen, “my first wish was to see and know thee; I will not gratify my second.”\n\nOrlando, observing Mandricardo, was surprised to see no sword at his side, nor mace at his saddle-bow. “And what weapon hast thou,” said he, “if thy lance fail thee?”\n\n“Do not concern yourself about that,” said Mandricardo; “I have made many good knights give ground with no other weapon than you see. Know that I have sworn an oath never to bear a sword until I win back that famous Durindana that Orlando, the paladin, carries. That sword belongs to the suit of armor which I wear; that only is wanting. Without doubt it was stolen, but how it got into the hands of Orlando I know not. But I will make him pay dearly for it when I find him. I seek him the more anxiously that I may avenge with his blood the death of King Agrican, my father, whom he treacherously slew. I am sure he must have done it by treachery, for it was not in his power to subdue in fair fight such a warrior as my father.”\n\n“Thou liest,” cried Orlando; “and all who say so lie. I am Orlando, whom you seek; yes, I am he who slew your father honorably. Hold, here is the sword: you shall have it if your courage avails to merit it. Though it belongs to me by right, I will not use it in this dispute. See, I hang it on this tree; you shall be master of it, if you bereave me of life; not else.”\n\nAt these words Orlando drew Durindana, and hung it on one of the branches of a tree near by.\n\nBoth knights, boiling with equal ardor, rode off in a semicircle; then rushed together with reins thrown loose, and struck one another with their lances. Both kept their seats, immovable. The splinters of their lances flew into the air, and no weapon remained for either but the fragment which he held in his hand. Then those two knights, covered with iron mail, were reduced to the necessity of fighting with staves, in the manner of two rustics, who dispute the boundary of a meadow, or the possession of a spring.\n\nThese clubs could not long keep whole in the hands of such sturdy smiters, who were soon reduced to fight with naked fists. Such warfare was more painful to him that gave than to him that received the blows. They next clasped, and strained each his adversary, as Hercules did Antæus. Mandricardo, more enraged than Orlando, made violent efforts to unseat the paladin, and dropped the rein of his horse. Orlando, more calm, perceived it. With one hand he resisted Mandricardo, with the other he twitched the horse’s bridle over the ears of the animal. The Saracen dragged Orlando with all his might, but Orlando’s thighs held the saddle like a vise. At last the efforts of the Saracen broke the girths of Orlando’s horse; the saddle slipped; the knight, firm in his stirrups, slipped with it, and came to the ground hardly conscious of his fall. The noise of his armor in falling startled Mandricardo’s horse, now without a bridle. He started off in full career, heeding neither trees nor rocks nor broken ground. Urged by fright, he ran with furious speed, carrying his master, who, almost distracted with rage, shouted and beat the animal with his fists, and thereby impelled his flight. After running thus three miles or more, a deep ditch opposed their progress. The horse and rider fell headlong into it, and did not find the bottom covered with feather-beds or roses. They got sadly bruised; but were lucky enough to escape without any broken limbs.\nMandricardo, as soon as he gained his feet, seized the horse by his mane with fury; but, having no bridle, could not hold him. He looked round in hopes of finding something that would do for a rein. Just then fortune, who seemed willing to help him at last, brought that way a peasant with a bridle in his hand, who was in search of his farm horse that had strayed away.\n\nOrlando, having speedily repaired his horse’s girths, remounted, and waited a good hour for the Saracen to return. Not seeing him, he concluded to go in search of him. He took an affectionate leave of Zerbino and Isabella, who would willingly have followed him; but this the brave paladin would by no means permit. He held it unknightly to go in search of an enemy accompanied by a friend, who might act as a defender. Therefore, desiring them to say to Mandricardo, if they should meet him, that his purpose was to tarry in the neighborhood three days, and then repair to the camp of Charlemagne, he took down Durindana from the tree, and proceeded in the direction which the Saracen’s horse had taken. But the animal, having no guide but its terror, had so doubled and confused its traces that Orlando, after two days spent in the search, gave up the attempt.\n\nIt was about the middle of the third day when the paladin arrived on the pleasant bank of a stream which wound through a meadow enamelled with flowers. High trees, whose tops met and formed an arbor, overshadowed the fountain; and the breeze which blew through their foliage tempered the heat. Hither the shepherds used to resort to quench their thirst, and to enjoy the shelter from the midday sun. The air, perfumed with the flowers, seemed to breathe fresh strength into their veins. Orlando felt the influence, though covered with his armor. He stopped in this delicious arbor, where everything seemed to invite to repose. But he could not have chosen a more fatal asylum. He there spent the most miserable moments of his life.\n\nHe looked around, and noted with pleasure all the charms of the spot. He saw that some of the trees were carved with inscriptions—he drew near, and read them, and what was his surprise to find that they composed the name of Angelica! Farther on he found the name of Medoro mixed with hers. The paladin thought he dreamed. He stood like one amazed—like a bird that, rising to fly, finds its feet caught in a net.\n\nOrlando followed the course of the stream, and came to one of its turns where the rocks of the mountain bent in such a way as to form a sort of grotto. The twisted stems of ivy and the wild vine draped the entrance of this recess, scooped by the hand of nature.\n\nThe unhappy paladin, on entering the grotto, saw letters which appeared to have been lately carved. They were verses which Medoro had written in honor of his happy nuptials with the beautiful queen. Orlando tried to persuade himself it must be some other Angelica whom those verses celebrated, and as for Medoro, he had never heard his name. The sun was now declining, and Orlando remounted his horse, and went on his way. He soon saw the roof of a cottage whence the smoke ascended; he heard the barking of dogs and the lowing of cattle, and arrived at a humble dwelling which seemed to offer an asylum for the night. The inmates, as soon as they saw him, hastened to render him service. One took his horse, another his shield and cuirass, another his golden spurs. This cottage was the very same where Medoro had been carried, deeply\n\nwounded,—where Angelica had tended him, and afterwards married him. The shepherd who lived in it loved to tell everybody the story of this marriage, and soon related it, with all its details, to the miserable Orlando.\n\nHaving finished it, he went away, and returned with the precious bracelet which Angelica, grateful for his services, had given him as a memorial. It was the one which Orlando had himself given her.\n\nThis last touch was the finishing stroke to the excited paladin. Frantic, exasperated, he exclaimed, against the ungrateful and cruel princess who had disdained him, the most renowned, the most indomitable of all the paladins of France,—him, who had rescued her from the most alarming perils,—him, who had fought the most terrible battles for her sake,—she to prefer to him a young Saracen! The pride of the noble Count was deeply wounded. Indignant, frantic, a victim to ungovernable rage, he rushed into the forest, uttering the most frightful shrieks.\n\n“No, no!” cried he, “I am not the man they take me for! Orlando is dead! I am only the wandering ghost of that unhappy Count, who is now suffering the torments of hell!”\n\nOrlando wandered all night, as chance directed, through the wood, and at sunrise his destiny led him to the fountain where Medoro had engraved the fatal inscription. The frantic paladin saw it a second time with fury, drew his sword, and hacked it from the rock.\n\nUnlucky grotto! you shall no more attract by your shade and coolness, you shall no more shelter with your arch either shepherd or flock. And you, fresh and pure fountain, you may not escape the rage of the furious Orlando! He cast into the fountain branches, trunks of trees which he tore up, pieces of rocks which he broke off, plants uprooted, with the earth adhering, and turf and brushes, so as to choke the fountain, and destroy the purity of its waters. At length, exhausted by his violent exertions, bathed in sweat, breathless, Orlando sunk panting upon the earth, and lay there insensible three days and three nights.\n\nThe fourth day he started up and seized his arms. His helmet, his buckler, he cast far from him; his hauberk and his clothes he rent asunder; the fragments were scattered through the wood. In fine, he became a furious madman. His insanity was such that he cared not to retain even his sword. But he had no need of Durindana, nor of other arms, to do wonderful things. His prodigious strength sufficed. At the first wrench of his mighty arm he tore up a pine-tree by the roots. Oaks, beeches, maples, whatever he met in his path, yielded in like manner. The ancient forest soon became as bare as the borders of a morass, where the fowler has cleared away the bushes to spread his nets. The shepherds, hearing the horrible crashing in the forest, abandoned their flocks to run and see the cause of this unwonted uproar. By their evil star, or for their sins, they were led thither. When they saw the furious state the Count was in, and his incredible force, they would fain have fled out of his reach, but in their fears lost their presence of mind. The madman pursued them, seized one and rent him limb from limb, as easily as one would pull ripe apples from a tree. He took another by the feet, and used him as a club to knock down a third. The shepherds fled; but it would have been hard for any to escape, if he had not at that moment left them to throw himself with the same fury upon their flocks. The peasants, abandoning their ploughs and harrows, mounted on the roofs of buildings and pinnacles of the rocks, afraid to trust themselves even to the oaks and pines. From such heights they looked on, trembling at the raging fury of the unhappy Orlando. His fists, his teeth, his nails, his feet, seize, break, and tear cattle, sheep, and swine; the most swift in flight alone being able to escape him.\n\nWhen at last terror had scattered everything before him, he entered a cottage which was abandoned by its inhabitants, and there found that which served for food. His long fast had caused him to feel the most ravenous hunger. Seizing whatever he found that was eatable, whether roots, acorns, or bread, raw meat or cooked, he gorged it indiscriminately.\n\nIssuing thence again, the frantic Orlando gave chase to whatever living thing he saw, whether men or animals. Sometimes he pursued the deer and hind, sometimes he attacked bears and wolves, and with his naked hands killed and tore them, and devoured their flesh.\n\nThus he wandered, from place to place, through France, imperilling his life a thousand ways, yet always preserved by some mysterious providence from a fatal result. But here we leave Orlando for a time, that we may record what befell Zerbino and Isabella after their parting with him.\n\nThe prince and his fair bride waited, by Orlando’s request, near the scene of the battle for three days, that, if Mandricardo should return, they might inform him where Orlando would give him another meeting. At the end of that time their anxiety to know the issue led them to follow Orlando’s traces, which led them at last to the wood where the trees were inscribed with the names of Angelica and Medoro. They remarked how all these inscriptions were defaced, and how the grotto was disordered, and the fountain clogged with rubbish. But that which surprised them and distressed them most of all was to find on the grass the cuirass of Orlando, and not far from it his helmet, the same which the renowned Almontes once wore.\n\nHearing a horse neigh in the forest, Zerbino turned his eyes in that direction, and saw Brigliadoro, with the bridle yet hanging at the saddle-bow. He looked round for Durindana, and found that famous sword, without the scabbard, lying on the grass. He saw also the fragments of Orlando’s other arms and clothing scattered on all sides over the plain.\nZerbino and Isabella stood in astonishment and grief, not knowing what to think, but little imagining the true cause. If they had found any marks of blood on the arms or on the fragments of the clothing, they would have supposed him slain, but there were none. While they were in this painful uncertainty they saw a young peasant approach. He, not yet recovered from the terror of the scene, which he had witnessed from the top of a rock, told them the whole of the sad events.\n\nZerbino, with his eyes full of tears, carefully collected all the scattered arms. Isabella also dismounted to aid him in the sad duty. When they had collected all the pieces of that rich armor they hung them like a trophy on a pine; and to prevent their being violated by any passers-by, Zerbino inscribed on the bark this caution: “These are the arms of the Paladin Orlando.”\n\nHaving finished this pious work, he remounted his horse, and just then a knight rode up, and requested Zerbino to tell him the meaning of the trophy. The prince related the facts as they had happened; and Mandricardo, for it was that Saracen knight, full of joy, rushed forward, and seized the sword, saying, “No one can censure me for what I do; this sword is mine; I can take my own wherever I find it. It is plain that Orlando, not daring to defend it against me, has counterfeited madness to excuse him in surrendering it.”\n\nZerbino vehemently exclaimed, “Touch not that sword. Think not to possess it without a contest. If it be true that the arms you wear are those of Hector, you must have got them by theft, and not by prowess.”\n\nImmediately they attacked one another with the utmost fury. The air resounded with thick-falling blows. Zerbino, skilful and alert, evaded for a time with good success the strokes of Durindana; but at length a terrible blow struck him on the neck. He fell from his horse, and the Tartar king, possessed of the spoils of his victory, rode away.\n\n# ZERBINO AND ISABELLA\n\n————\nZerbino’s pain at seeing the Tartar prince go off with the sword surpassed the anguish of his wound; but now the loss of blood so reduced his strength that he could not move from where he fell. Isabella, not knowing whither to resort for help, could only bemoan him, and chide her cruel fate. Zerbino said, “If I could but leave thee, my best beloved, in some secure abode, it would not distress me to die; but to abandon thee so, without protection, is sad indeed.” She replied, “Think not to leave me, dearest; our souls shall not be parted; this sword will give me the means to follow thee.” Zerbino’s last words implored her to banish such a thought, but live, and be true to his memory. Isabella promised, with many tears, to be faithful to him so long as life should last.\n\nWhen he ceased to breathe, Isabella’s cries resounded through the forest, and reached the ears of a reverend hermit, who hastened to the spot. He soothed and calmed her, urging those consolations which the word of God supplies; and at last brought her to wish for nothing else but to devote herself for the rest of life wholly to religion.\n\nAs she could not bear the thoughts of leaving her dead lord abandoned, the body was, by the good hermit’s aid, placed upon the horse, and taken to the nearest inhabited place, where a chest was made for it, suitable to be carried with them on their way. The hermit’s plan was to escort his charge to a monastery, not many days’ journey distant, where Isabella resolved to spend the remainder of her days. Thus they travelled day after day, choosing the most retired ways, for the country was full of armed men. One day a cavalier met them, and barred their way. It was no other than Rodomont, king of Algiers, who had just left the camp of Agramant, full of indignation at the treatment he had received from Doralice. At sight of the lovely lady and her reverend attendant, with their horse laden with a burden draped with black, he asked the meaning of their journey. Isabella told him her affliction, and her resolution to renounce the world and devote herself to religion, and to the memory of the friend she had lost. Rodomont laughed scornfully at this, and told her that her project was absurd; that charms like hers were meant to be enjoyed, not buried, and that he himself would more than make amends for her dead lover. The monk, who promptly interposed to rebuke this impious talk, was commanded to hold his peace; and still persisting was seized by the knight and hurled over the edge of the cliff, where he fell into the sea, and was drowned.\n\nRodomont, when he had got rid of the hermit, again applied to the sad lady, heartless with affright, and, in the language used by lovers, said, “she was his very heart, his life, his light.” Having laid aside all violence, he humbly sued that she would accompany him to his retreat, near by. It was a ruined chapel from which the monks had been driven by the disorders of the time, and which Rodomont had taken possession of. Isabella, who had no choice but to obey, followed him, meditating as she went what resource she could find to escape out of his power, and keep her vow to her dead husband, to be faithful to his memory as long as life should last. At length she said, “If, my lord, you will let me go and fulfil my vow, and my intention, as I have already declared it, I will bestow upon you what will be to you of more value than a hundred women’s hearts. I know an herb, and I have seen it on our way, which, rightly prepared, affords a juice of such power, that the flesh, if laved with it, becomes impenetrable to sword or fire. This liquor I can make, and will, to-day, if you will accept my offer; and when you have seen its virtue you will value it more than if all Europe were made your own.”\n\nRodomont, at hearing this, readily promised all that was asked, so eager was he to learn a secret that would make him as Achilles was of yore. Isabella, having collected such herbs as she thought proper, and boiled them, with certain mysterious signs and words, at length declared her labor done, and, as a test, offered to try its virtue on herself. She bathed her neck and bosom with the liquor, and then called on Rodomont to smite with all his force, and see whether his sword had power to harm. The pagan, who during the preparations had taken frequent draughts of wine, and scarce knew what he did, drew his sword at the word, and struck across her neck with all his might, and the fair head leapt sundered from the snowy neck and breast.\n\nRude and unfeeling as he was, the pagan knight lamented bitterly this sad result. To honor her memory he resolved to do a work as unparalleled as her devotion. From all parts round he caused laborers to be brought, and had a tower built to enclose the chapel, within which the remains of Zerbino and Isabella were entombed. Across the stream which flowed near by he built a bridge, scarce two yards wide, and added neither parapet nor rail. On the top of the tower a sentry was placed, who, when any traveller approached the bridge, gave notice to his master. Rodomont thereupon sallied out, and defied the approaching knight to fight him upon the bridge, where any chance step a little aside would plunge the rider headlong in the stream. This bridge he vowed to keep until a thousand suits of armor should be won from conquered knights, wherewith to build a trophy to his victim and her lord.\n\nWithin ten days the bridge was built, and the tower was in progress. In a short time many knights, either seeking the shortest route, or tempted by a desire of adventure, had made the attempt to pass the bridge. All, without exception, had lost either arms or life, or both; some falling before Rodomont’s lance, others precipitated into the river. One day, as Rodomont stood urging his workmen, it chanced that Orlando in his furious mood came thither, and approached the bridge. Rodomont halloed to him, “Halt, churl; presume not to set foot upon that bridge; it was not made for such as you!” Orlando took no notice, but pressed on. Just then a gentle damsel rode up. It was Flordelis, who was seeking her Florismart. She saw Orlando, and, in spite of his strange appearance, recognized him. Rodomont, not used to have his commands disobeyed, laid hands on the madman, and would have thrown him into the river, but to his astonishment found himself in the grip of one not so easily disposed of. “How can a fool have such strength?” he growled between his teeth. Flordelis stopped to see the issue, where each of these two puissant warriors strove to throw the other from the bridge. Orlando at last had strength enough to lift his foe with all his armor, and fling him over the side, but had not wit to clear himself from him, so both fell together. High flashed the wave as they together smote its surface. Here Orlando had the advantage; he was naked, and could swim like a fish. He soon reached the bank, and, careless of praise or blame, stopped not to see what came of the adventure. Rodomont, entangled with his armor, escaped with difficulty to the bank. Meantime, Flordelis passed the bridge unchallenged.\n\nAfter long wandering without success she returned to Paris, and there found the object of her search; for Florismart, after the fall of Albracca, had repaired thither. The joy of meeting was clouded to Florismart by the news which Flordelis brought of Orlando’s wretched plight. The last she had seen of him was when he fell with Rodomont into the stream. Florismart, who loved Orlando like a brother, resolved to set out immediately, under the guidance of the lady, to find him, and bring him where he might receive the treatment suited to his case. A few days brought them to the place where they found the Tartar king still guarding the bridge. The usual challenge and defiance was made, and the knights rode to encounter one another on the bridge. At the first encounter both horses were overthrown; and, having no space to regain their footing, fell with their riders into the water. Rodomont, who knew the soundings of the stream, soon recovered the land; but Florismart was carried downward by the current, and landed at last on a bank of mud where his horse could hardly find footing. Flordelis, who watched the battle from the bridge, seeing her lover in this piteous case, exclaimed aloud, “Ah! Rodomont, for love of her whom dead you honor, have pity on me, who love this knight, and slay him not. Let it suffice he yields his armor to the pile, and none more glorious will it bear than his.” Her prayer, so well directed, touched the pagan’s heart, though hard to move, and he lent his aid to help the knight to land. He kept him a prisoner, however, and added his armor to the pile. Flordelis, with a heavy heart, went her way.\n\nWe must now return to Rogero, who, when we parted with him, was engaged in an adventure which arrested his progress to the monastery whither he was bound with the intention of receiving baptism, and thus qualifying himself to demand Bradamante as his bride. On his way he met with Mandricardo, and the quarrel was revived respecting the right to wear the badge of Hector. After a warm discussion both parties agreed to submit the question to King Agramant, and for that purpose took their way to the Saracen camp. Here they met Gradasso, who had his controversy also with Mandricardo. This warrior claimed the sword of Orlando, denying the right of Mandricardo to possess it in virtue of his having found it abandoned by its owner. King Agramant strove in vain to reconcile these quarrels, and was forced at last to consent that the points in dispute should be settled by one combat, in which Mandricardo should meet one of the other champions, to whom should be committed the cause of both. Rogero was chosen by lot to maintain Gradasso’s cause and his own. Great preparations were made for this signal contest. On the appointed day it was fought in the presence of Agramant, and of the whole army. Rogero won it; and Mandricardo, the conqueror of Hector’s arms, the challenger of Orlando, and the slayer of Zerbino, lost his life. Gradasso received Durindana as his prize, which lost half its value in his eyes, since it was won by another’s prowess, not his own.\n\nRogero, though victorious, was severely wounded, and lay helpless many weeks in the camp of Agramant, while Bradamante, ignorant of the cause of his delay, expected him at Montalban. Thither he had promised to repair in fifteen days, or twenty at furthest, hoping to have obtained by that time an honorable discharge from his obligations to the Saracen commander. The twenty days were passed, and a month more, and still Rogero came not, nor did any tidings reach Bradamante accounting for his absence. At the end of that time, a wandering knight brought news of the famous combat, and of Rogero’s wound. He added, what alarmed Bradamante still more, that Marphisa, a female warrior, young and fair, was in attendance on the wounded knight. He added that the whole army expected that, as soon as Rogero’s wounds were healed, the pair would be united in marriage.\n\nBradamante, distressed by this news, though she believed it but in part, resolved to go immediately and see for herself. She mounted Rabican, the horse of Astolpho, which he had committed to her care, and took with her the lance of gold, though unaware of its wonderful powers. Thus accoutred, she left the castle, and took the road toward Paris and the camp of the Saracens.\n\nMarphisa, whose devotion to Rogero in his illness had so excited the jealousy of Bradamante, was the twin sister of Rogero. She, with him, had been taken in charge when an infant by Atlantes, the magician, but while yet a child she had been stolen away by an Arab tribe. Adopted by their chief, she had early learned horsemanship and skill in arms, and at this time had come to the camp of Agramant with no other view than to see and test for herself the prowess of the warriors of either camp, whose fame rang through the world. Arriving at the very moment of the late encounter, the name of Rogero, and some few facts of his story which she learned, were enough to suggest the idea that it was her brother whom she saw victorious in the single combat. Inquiry satisfied the two of their near kindred, and from that moment Marphisa devoted herself to the care of her new-found and much-loved brother.\n\nIn those moments of seclusion Rogero informed his sister of what he had learned of their parentage from old Atlantes. Rogero, their father, a Christian knight, had won the heart of Galaciella, daughter of the Sultan of Africa, and sister of King Agramant, converted her to the Christian faith, and secretly married her. The Sultan, enraged at his daughter’s marriage, drove her husband into exile, and caused her with her infant children, Rogero and Marphisa, to be placed in a boat and committed to the winds and waves, to perish; from which fate they were saved by Atlantes. On hearing this, Marphisa exclaimed, “How can you, brother, leave our parents unavenged so long, and even submit to serve the son of the tyrant who so wronged them?” Rogero replied that it was but lately he had learned the full truth; that when he learned it he was already embarked with Agramant, from whom he had received knighthood, and that he only waited for a suitable opportunity when he might with honor desert his standard, and at the same time return to the faith of his fathers. Marphisa hailed this resolution with joy, and declared her intention to join with him in embracing the Christian faith.\n————\n\nWe left Bradamante when, mounted on Rabican and armed with Astolpho’s lance, she rode forth, determined to learn the cause of Rogero’s long absence. One day, as she rode, she met a damsel, of visage and of manners fair, but overcome with grief. It was Flordelis, who was seeking far and near a champion capable of liberating and avenging her lord. Flordelis marked the approaching warrior, and, judging from appearances, thought she had found the champion she sought. “Are you, Sir Knight,” she said, “so daring and so kind as to take up my cause against a fierce and cruel warrior who has made prisoner of my lord, and forced me thus to be a wanderer and a suppliant?” Then she related the events which had happened at the bridge. Bradamante, to whom noble enterprises were always welcome, readily embraced this, and the rather as in her gloomy forebodings she felt as if Rogero was forever lost to her.\n\nNext day the two arrived at the bridge. The sentry descried them approaching, and gave notice to his lord, who thereupon donned his armor and went forth to meet them. Here, as usual, he called on the advancing warrior to yield his horse and arms an oblation to the tomb. Bradamante replied, asking by what right he called on the innocent to do penance for his crime. “Your life and your armor,” she added, “are the fittest offering to her tomb, and I, a woman, the fittest champion to take them.” With that she couched her spear, spurred her horse, and ran to the encounter. King Rodomont came on with speed. The trampling sounded on the bridge like thunder. It took but a moment to decide the contest. The golden lance did its office, and that fierce Moor, so renowned in tourney, lay extended on the bridge. “Who is the loser now?” said Bradamante; but Rodomont, amazed that a woman’s hand should have laid him low, could not or would not answer. Silent and sad, he raised himself, unbound his helm and mail, and flung them against the tomb; then, sullen and on foot, left the ground; but first gave orders to one of his squires to release all his prisoners. They had been sent off to Africa. Besides Florismart, there were Sansonnet and Oliver, who had ridden that way in quest of Orlando, and had both in turn been overthrown in the encounter.\n\nBradamante after her victory resumed her route, and in due time reached the Christian camp, where she readily learned an explanation of the mystery which had caused her so much anxiety. Rogero and his fair and brave sister, Marphisa, were too illustrious by their station and exploits not to be the frequent topic of discourse even among their adversaries, and all that Bradamante was anxious to know reached her ear, almost without inquiry.\n\nWe now return to Gradasso, who by Rogero’s victory had been made possessor of Durindana. There now only remained to him to seek the horse of Rinaldo; and the challenge, given and accepted, was yet to be fought with that warrior, for it had been interrupted by the arts of Malagigi. Gradasso now sought another meeting with Rinaldo, and met with no reluctance on his part. As the combat was for the possession of Bayard, the knights dismounted and fought on foot. Long time the battle lasted. Rinaldo, knowing well the deadly stroke of Durindana, used all his art to parry or avoid its blow. Gradasso struck with might and main, but wellnigh all his strokes were spent in air, or if they smote they fell obliquely and did little harm.\n\nThus had they fought long, glancing at one another’s eyes, and seeing naught else, when their attention was arrested perforce by a strange noise. They turned, and beheld the good Bayard attacked by a monstrous bird. Perhaps it was a bird, for such it seemed; but when or where such a bird was ever seen I have nowhere read, except in Turpin; and I am inclined to believe that it was not a bird, but a fiend, evoked from underground by Malagigi, and thither sent on purpose to interrupt the fight. Whether a fiend or a fowl, the monster flew right at Bayard, and clapped his wings in his face. Thereat the steed broke loose, and ran madly across the plain, pursued by the bird, till Bayard plunged into the wood, and was lost to sight.\n\nRinaldo and Gradasso, seeing Bayard’s escape, agreed to suspend their battle till they could recover the horse, the object of contention. Gradasso mounted his steed, and followed the foot-marks of Bayard into the forest. Rinaldo, never more vexed in spirit, remained at the spot, Gradasso having promised to return thither with the horse, if he found him. He did find him, after long search, for he had the good fortune to hear him neigh. Thus he became possessed of both the objects for which he had led an army from his own country, and invaded France. He did not forget his promise to bring Bayard back to the place where he had left Rinaldo, but only muttering, “Now I have got him, he little knows me who expects me to give him up; if Rinaldo wants the horse let him seek him in India, as I have sought him in France,”—he made the best of his way to Arles, where his vessels lay; and in possession of the two objects of his ambition, the horse and the sword, sailed away to his own country.\n\n# ASTOLPHO IN ABYSSINIA\n\n————\nWhen we last parted with the adventurous paladin Astolpho, he was just commencing that flight over the countries of the world from which he promised himself so much gratification. Our readers are aware that the eagle and the falcon have not so swift a flight as the Hippogriff on which Astolpho rode. It was not long, therefore, before the paladin, directing his course toward the southeast, arrived over that part of Africa where the great river Nile has its source. Here he alighted, and found himself in the neighborhood of the capital of Abyssinia, ruled by Senapus, whose riches and power were immense. His palace was of surpassing splendor; the bars of the gates, the hinges and locks, were all of pure gold; in fact, this metal, in that country, is put to all those uses for which we employ iron. It is so common that they prefer for ornamental purposes rock crystal, of which all the columns were made. Precious stones of different kinds, rubies, emeralds, sapphires, and topazes were set in ornamental designs, and the walls and ceilings were adorned with pearls.\n\nIt is in this country those famous balms grow of which there are some few plants in that part of Judæa called Gilead. Musk, ambergris, and numerous gums, so precious in Europe, are here in their native climate. It is said the Sultan of Egypt pays a vast tribute to the monarch of this country to hire him not to cut off the source of the Nile, which he might easily do, and cause the river to flow in some other direction, thus depriving Egypt of the source of its fertility.\n\nAt the time of Astolpho’s arrival in his dominions, this monarch was in great affliction. In spite of his riches and the precious productions of his country, he was in danger of dying of hunger. He was a prey to a flock of obscene birds called Harpies, which attacked him whenever he sat at meat, and with their claws snatched, tore, and scattered everything, overturning the vessels, devouring the food, and infecting what they left with their filthy touch. It was said this punishment was inflicted upon the king because when young, and filled with pride and presumption, he had attempted to invade with an army the terrestrial paradise, which is situated on the top of a mountain whence the Nile draws its source. Nor was this his only punishment. He was struck blind.\n\nAstolpho, on arriving in the dominions of this monarch, hastened to pay him his respects. King Senapus received him graciously, and ordered a splendid repast to be prepared in honor of his arrival. While the guests were seated at table, Astolpho filling the place of dignity at the king’s right hand, the horrid scream of the Harpies was heard in the air, and soon they approached, hovering over the tables, seizing the food from the dishes, and overturning everything with the flapping of their broad wings. In vain the guests struck at them with knives and any weapons which they had, and Astolpho drew his sword and gave them repeated blows, which seemed to have no more effect upon them than if their bodies had been made of tow.\n\nAt last Astolpho thought of his horn. He first gave warning to the king and his guests to stop their ears; then blew a blast. The Harpies, terrified at the sound, flew away as fast as their wings could carry them. The paladin mounted his Hippogriff, and pursued them, blowing his horn as often as he came near them. They stretched their flight towards the great mountain, at the foot of which there is a cavern, which is thought to be the mouth of the infernal abodes. Hither those horrid birds flew, as if to their home. Having seen them all disappear in the recess, Astolpho cared not to pursue them farther, but alighting, rolled huge stones into the mouth of the cave, and piled branches of trees therein, so that he effectually barred their passage out, and we have no evidence of their ever having been seen since in the outer air.\n\nAfter this labor Astolpho refreshed himself by bathing in a fountain whose pure waters bubbled from a cleft of the rock. Having rested awhile, an earnest desire seized him of ascending the mountain which towered above him. The Hippogriff bore him swiftly upwards, and landed him on the top of the mountain, which he found to be an extensive plain.\nA splendid palace rose in the middle of this plain, whose walls shone with such brilliancy that mortal eyes could hardly bear the sight. Astolpho guided the winged horse towards this edifice, and made him poise himself in the air while he took a leisurely survey of this favored spot and its environs. It seemed as if nature and art had striven with one another to see which could do the most for its embellishment.\n\nAstolpho, on approaching the edifice, saw a venerable man advance to meet him. This personage was clothed in a long vesture as white as snow, while a mantle of purple covered his shoulders, and hung down to the ground. A white beard descended to his middle, and his hair, of the same color, overshadowed his shoulders. His eyes were so brilliant that Astolpho felt persuaded that he was a blessed inhabitant of the heavenly mansions.\n\nThe sage, smiling benignantly upon the paladin, who from respect had dismounted from his horse, said to him: “Noble chevalier, know that it is by the Divine will you have been brought to the terrestrial paradise. Your mortal nature could not have borne to scale these heights and reach these seats of bliss if it were not the will of Heaven that you should be instructed in the means to succor Charles, and to sustain the glory of our holy faith. I am prepared to impart the needed counsels; but before I begin let me welcome you to our sojourn. I doubt not your long fast and distant journey have given you a good appetite.”\n\nThe aspect of the venerable man filled the prince with admiration; but his surprise ceased when he learned from him that he was that one of the Apostles of our Lord to whom he said, “I will that thou tarry till I come.”\n\nSt. John, conducting Astolpho, rejoined his companions. These were the patriarch Enoch and the prophet Elijah; neither of whom had yet seen his dying day, but, taken from our lower world, were dwelling in a region of peace and joy, in a climate of eternal spring, till the last trumpet shall sound.\n\nThe three holy inhabitants of the terrestrial paradise received Astolpho with the greatest kindness, carried him to a pleasant apartment, and took great care of the Hippogriff, to whom they gave such food as suited him, while to the prince they presented fruits so delicious that he felt inclined to excuse our first parents for their sin in eating them without permission.\n\nAstolpho, having recruited his strength, not only by these excellent fruits, but also by sweet sleep, roused himself at the first blush of dawn, and as soon as he left his chamber met the beloved Apostle coming to seek him. St. John took him by the hand, and told him many things relating to the past and the future. Among others, he said, “Son, let me tell you what is now going on in France. Orlando, the illustrious prince who received at his birth the endowment of strength and courage more than mortal, raised up as was Samson of old to be the champion of the true faith, has been guilty of the basest ingratitude in leaving the Christian camp when it most needed the support of his arm, to run after a Saracen princess, whom he would fain marry, though she scorns him. To punish him his reason has been taken away, so that he runs naked through the land, over mountains and through valleys, without a ray of intelligence. The duration of his punishment has been fixed at three months, and that time having nearly expired, you have been brought hither to learn from us the means by which the reason of Orlando may be restored. True, you will be obliged to make a journey with me, and we must even leave the earth, and ascend to the moon, for it is in that planet we are to seek the remedy for the madness of the paladin. I propose to make our journey this evening, as soon as the moon appears over our head.”\n\nAs soon as the sun sunk beneath the seas, and the moon presented its luminous disk, the holy man had the chariot brought out in which he was accustomed to make excursions among the stars, the same which was employed long ago to convey Elijah up from earth. The saint made Astolpho seat himself beside him, took the reins, and giving the word to the coursers, they bore them upward with astonishing celerity.\n\nAt length they reached the great continent of the Moon. Its surface appeared to be of polished steel, with here and there a spot which, like rust, obscured its brightness. The paladin was astonished to see that the earth, with all its seas and rivers, seemed but an insignificant spot in the distance.\n\nThe prince discovered in this region so new to him rivers, lakes, plains, hills, and valleys. Many beautiful cities and castles enriched the landscape. He saw also vast forests, and heard in them the sound of horns and the barking of dogs, which led him to conclude that the nymphs were following the chase.\n\nThe knight, filled with wonder at all he saw, was conducted by the saint to a valley, where he stood amazed at the riches strewed all around him. Well he might be so, for that valley was the receptacle of things lost on earth, either by men’s fault, or by the effect of time and chance. Let no one suppose we speak here of kingdoms or of treasures; they are the toys of Fortune, which she dispenses in turning her wheel; we speak of things which she can neither give nor take away. Such are reputations, which appear at one time so brilliant, and a short time after are heard of no more. Here, also, are countless vows and prayers for unattainable objects, lovers’ sighs and tears, time spent in gaming, dressing, and doing nothing, the leisure of the dull and the intentions of the lazy, baseless projects, intrigues, and plots; these and such like things fill all the valley.\n\nAstolpho had a great desire to understand all that he saw, and which appeared to him so extraordinary. Among the rest, he observed a great mountain of blown bladders, from which issued indistinct noises. The saint told him these were the dynasties of Assyrian and Persian kings, once the wonder of the earth, of which now scarce the name remains.\nAstolpho could not help laughing when the saint said to him, “All these hooks of silver and gold that you see are the gifts of courtiers to princes, made in the hope of getting something better in return.” He also showed him garlands of flowers in which snares were concealed; these were flatteries and adulations, meant to deceive. But nothing was so comical as the sight of numerous grasshoppers which had burst their lungs with chirping. These, he told him, were sonnets, odes, and dedications, addressed by venal poets to great people.\n\nThe paladin beheld with wonder what seemed a lake of spilled milk. “It is,” said the saint, “the charity done by frightened misers on their death-beds.” It would take too long to tell all that the valley contained: meanness, affectations, pretended virtues, and concealed vices were there in abundance.\n\nAmong the rest Astolpho perceived many days of his own lost, and many imprudent sallies which he had made, and would have been glad not to have been reminded of. But he also saw among so many lost things a great abundance of one thing which men are apt to think they all possess, and do not think it necessary to pray for,—good sense. This commodity appeared under the form of a liquor, most light and apt to evaporate. It was therefore kept in vials, firmly sealed. One of these was labelled, “The sense of the Paladin Orlando.”\n\nAll the bottles were ticketed, and the sage placed one in Astolpho’s hand, which he found was his own. It was more than half full. He was surprised to find there many other vials which contained almost the whole of the wits of many persons who passed among men for wise. Ah, how easy it is to lose one’s reason! Some lose theirs by yielding to the sway of the passions; some in braving tempests and shoals in search of wealth; some by trusting too much to the promises of the great; some by setting their hearts on trifles. As might have been expected, the bottles which held the wits of astrologers, inventors, metaphysicians, and above all, of poets, were in general the best filled of all.\n\nAstolpho took his bottle, put it to his nose, and inhaled it all; and Turpin assures us that he was for a long time afterwards as sage as one could wish; but the Archbishop adds that there was reason to fear that some of the precious fluid afterwards found its way back into the bottle. The paladin took also the bottle which belonged to Orlando. It was a large one, and quite full.\n\nBefore quitting the planetary region Astolpho was conducted to an edifice on the borders of a river. He was shown an immense hall full of bundles of silk, linen, cotton, and wool. A thousand different colors, brilliant or dull, some quite black, were among these skeins. In one part of the hall an old woman was busy winding off yarns from all these different bundles. When she had finished a skein another ancient dame took it and placed it with others; a third selected from the fleeces spun, and mingled them in due proportions. The paladin inquired what all this might be. “These old women,” said the saint, “are the Fates, who spin, measure, and terminate the lives of mortals. As long as the thread stretches in one of those skeins, so long does the mortal enjoy the light of day; but nature and death are on the alert to shut the eyes of those whose thread is spun.”\n\nEach one of the skeins had a label of gold, silver, or iron, bearing the name of the individual to whom it belonged. An old man, who, in spite of the burden of years, seemed brisk and active, ran without ceasing to fill his apron with these labels, and carried them away to throw them into the river, whose name was Lethe. When he reached the shore of the river the old man shook out his apron, and the labels sunk to the bottom. A small number only floated for a time, hardly one in a thousand. Numberless birds, hawks, crows, and vultures hovered over the stream, with clamorous cries, and strove to snatch from the water some of these names; but they were too heavy for them, and after a while the birds were forced to let them drop into the river of oblivion. But two beautiful swans, of snowy whiteness, gathered some few of the names, and returned with them to the shore, where a lovely nymph received them from their beaks, and carried them to a temple placed upon a hill, and suspended them for all time upon a sacred column, on which stood the statue of Immortality.\n\nAstolpho was amazed at all this, and asked his guide to explain it. He replied, “The old man is Time. All the names upon the tickets would be immortal if the old man did not plunge them into the river of oblivion. Those clamorous birds which make vain efforts to save certain of the names are flatterers, pensioners, venal rhymesters, who do their best to rescue from oblivion the unworthy names of their patrons; but all in vain; they may keep them from their fate a little while, but ere long the river of oblivion must swallow them all.\n\n“The swans, that with harmonious strains carry certain names to the temple of Eternal Memory, are the great poets, who save from oblivion worse than death the names of those they judge worthy of immortality. Swans of this kind are rare. Let monarchs know the true breed, and fail not to nourish with care such as may chance to appear in their time.”\n\n# THE WAR IN AFRICA\n\n————\nWhen Astolpho had descended to the earth with the precious phial, St. John showed him a plant of marvellous virtues, with which he told him he had only to touch the eyes of the king of Abyssinia to restore him to sight. “That important service,” said the saint, “added to your having delivered him from the Harpies, will induce him to give you an army wherewith to attack the Africans in their rear, and force them to return from France to defend their own country.” The saint also instructed him how to lead his troops in safety across the great deserts, where caravans are often overwhelmed with moving columns of sand. Astolpho, fortified with ample instructions, remounted the Hippogriff, thanked the saint, received his blessing, and took his flight down to the level country.\n\nKeeping the course of the river Nile, he soon arrived at the capital of Abyssinia, and rejoined Senapus. The joy of the king was great when he heard again the voice of the hero who had delivered him from the Harpies. Astolpho touched his eyes with the plant which he had brought from the terrestrial paradise, and restored their sight. The king’s gratitude was unbounded. He begged him to name a reward, promising to grant it, whatever it might be. Astolpho asked an army to go to the assistance of Charlemagne, and the king not only granted him a hundred thousand men, but offered to lead them himself.\n\nThe night before the day appointed for the departure of the troops Astolpho mounted his winged horse, and directed his flight towards a mountain, whence the fierce South-wind issues, whose blast raises the sands of the Nubian desert, and whirls them onward in overwhelming clouds. The paladin, by the advice of St. John, had prepared himself with a leather bag, which he placed adroitly, with its mouth open, over the vent whence issues this terrible wind. At the first dawn of morning the wind rushed from its cavern to resume its daily course, and was caught in the bag, and securely tied up. Astolpho, delighted with his prize, returned to his army, placed himself at their head, and commenced his march. The Abyssinians traversed without danger or difficulty those vast fields of sand which separate their country from the kingdoms of Northern Africa, for the terrible South-wind, taken completely captive, had not force enough left to blow out a candle.\n\nSenapus was distressed that he could not furnish any cavalry, for his country, rich in camels and elephants, was destitute of horses. This difficulty the saint had foreseen, and had taught Astolpho the means of remedying. He now put those means in operation. Having reached a place whence he beheld a vast plain and the sea, he chose from his troops those who appeared to be the best made and the most intelligent. These he caused to be arranged in squadrons at the foot of a lofty mountain which bordered the plain, and he himself mounted to the summit to carry into effect his great design. Here he found vast quantities of fragments of rock and pebbles. These he set rolling down the mountain’s side, and, wonderful to relate, as they rolled they grew in size, made themselves bodies, legs, necks, and long faces. Next they began to neigh, to curvet, to scamper on all sides over the plain. Some were bay, some roan, some dapple, some chestnut. The troops at the foot of the mountain exerted themselves to catch these new-created horses, which they easily did, for the miracle had been so considerate as to provide all the horses with bridles and saddles. Astolpho thus suddenly found himself supplied with an excellent corps of cavalry, not fewer (as Archbishop Turpin asserts) than eighty thousand strong. With these troops Astolpho reduced all the country to subjection, and at last arrived before the walls of Agramant’s capital city, Biserta, to which he laid siege.\n\nWe must now return to the camp of the Christians, which lay before Arles, to which city the Saracens had retired after being defeated in a night attack led on by Rinaldo. Agramant here received the tidings of the invasion of his country by a fresh enemy, the Abyssinians, and learned that Biserta was in danger of falling into their hands. He took counsel of his officers, and decided to send an embassy to Charles, proposing that the whole quarrel should be submitted to the combat of two warriors, one from each side, according to the issue of which it should be decided which party should pay tribute to the other, and the war should cease. Charlemagne, who had not heard of the favorable turn which affairs had taken in Africa, readily agreed to this proposal, and Rinaldo was selected on the part of the Christians to sustain the combat.\n\nThe Saracens selected Rogero for their champion. Rogero was still in the Saracen camp, kept there by honor alone, for his mind had been opened to the truth of the Christian faith by the arguments of Bradamante, and he had resolved to leave the party of the infidels on the first favorable opportunity, and to join the Christian side. But his honor forbade him to do this while his former friends were in distress; and thus he waited for what time might bring forth, when he was startled by the announcement that he had been selected to uphold the cause of the Saracens against the Christians, and that his foe was to be Rinaldo, the brother of Bradamante.\n\nWhile Rogero was overwhelmed with this intelligence Bradamante on her side felt the deepest distress at hearing of the proposed combat. If Rogero should fall she felt that no other man living was worthy of her love; and if, on the other hand, Heaven should resolve to punish France by the death of her chosen champion, Bradamante would have to deplore her brother, so dear to her, and be no less completely severed from the object of her affections.\n\nWhile the fair lady gave herself up to these sad thoughts, the sage enchantress, Melissa, suddenly appeared before her. “Fear not, my daughter,” said she, “I shall find a way to interrupt this combat which so distresses you.”\n\nMeanwhile Rinaldo and Rogero prepared their weapons for the conflict. Rinaldo had the choice, and decided that it should be on foot, and with no weapons but the battle-axe and poniard. The place assigned was a plain between the camp of Charlemagne and the walls of Arles.\n\nHardly had the dawn announced the day appointed for this memorable combat, when heralds proceeded from both sides to mark the lists. Erelong the African troops were seen to advance from the city, Agramant at their head; his brilliant arms adorned in the Moorish fashion, his horse a bay, with a white star on his forehead. Rogero marched at his side, and some of the greatest warriors of the Saracen camp attended him, bearing the various parts of his armor and weapons. Charlemagne, on his part, proceeded from his intrenchments, ranged his troops in semicircle, and stood surrounded by his peers and paladins. Some of them bore portions of the armor of Rinaldo, the celebrated Ogier, the Dane, bearing the helmet which Rinaldo took from Mambrino. Duke Namo of Bavaria and Salomon of Bretagne bore two axes, of equal weight, prepared for the occasion.\n\nThe terms of the combat were then sworn to with the utmost solemnity by all parties. It was agreed that if from either part any attempt was made to interrupt the battle both combatants should turn their arms against the party which should be guilty of the interruption; and both monarchs assented to the condition that in such case the champion of the offending party should be discharged from his allegiance, and at liberty to transfer his arms to the other side.\n\nWhen all the preparations were concluded the monarchs and their attendants retired each to his own side, and the champions were left alone. The two warriors advanced with measured steps towards each other, and met in the middle of the space. They attacked one another at the same moment, and the air resounded with the blows they gave. Sparks flew from their battle-axes, while the velocity with which they managed their weapons astonished the beholders. Rogero, always remembering that his antagonist was the brother of his betrothed, could not aim a deadly wound; he strove only to ward off those levelled against himself. Rinaldo, on the other hand, much as he esteemed Rogero, spared not his blows, for he eagerly desired victory for his own sake, and for the sake of his country and his faith.\n\nThe Saracens soon perceived that their champion fought feebly, and gave not to Rinaldo such blows as he received from him. His disadvantage was so marked that anxiety and shame were manifest on the countenance of Agramant. Melissa, one of the most acute enchantresses that ever lived, seized this moment to disguise herself under the form of Rodomont, that rude and impetuous warrior, who had now for some time been absent from the Saracen camp. Approaching Agramant, she said, “How could you, my lord, have the imprudence of selecting a young man without experience to oppose the most redoubtable warrior of France? Surely you must have been regardless of the honor of your arms, and of the fate of your empire! But it is not too late. Break without delay the agreement which is sure to result in your ruin.” So saying, she addressed the troops who stood near, “Friends,” said she, “follow me; under my guidance every one of you will be a match for a score of those feeble Christians.” Agramant, delighted at seeing Rodomont once more at his side, gave his consent, and the Saracens, at the instant, couched their lances, set spurs to their steeds, and swept down upon the French. Melissa, when she saw her work successful, disappeared.\n\nRinaldo and Rogero, seeing the truce broken, and the two armies engaged in general conflict, stopped their battle; their martial fury ceased at once, they joined hands, and resolved to act no more on either side until it should be clearly ascertained which party had failed to observe its oath. Both renewed their promise to abandon forever the party which had been thus false and perjured.\n\nMeanwhile, the Christians, after the first moment of surprise, met the Saracens with courage redoubled by rage at the treachery of their foes. Guido the Wild, brother and rival of Rinaldo, Griffon and Aquilant, sons of Oliver, and numerous others whose names have already been celebrated in our recitals, beat back the assailants, and at last, after prodigious slaughter, forced them to take shelter within the walls of Arles.\n\nWe will now return to Orlando, whom we last heard of as furiously mad, and doing a thousand acts of violence in his senseless rage. One day he came to the borders of a stream which intercepted his course. He swam across it, for he could swim like an otter, and on the other side saw a peasant watering his horse. He seized the animal, in spite of the resistance of the peasant, and rode it with furious speed till he\n\narrived at the sea-coast, where Spain is divided from Africa by only a narrow strait. At the moment of his arrival a vessel had just put off to cross the strait. She was full of people who, with glass in hand, seemed to be taking a merry farewell of the land, wafted by a favorable breeze.\nThe frantic Orlando cried out to them to stop and take him in; but they, having no desire to admit a madman to their company, paid him no attention. The paladin thought this behavior very uncivil; and by force of blows made his horse carry him into the water in pursuit of the ship. The wretched animal soon had only his head above water; but as Orlando urged him forward, nothing was left for the poor beast but either to die or swim over to Africa.\n\nAlready Orlando had lost sight of the bark; distance and the swell of the sea completely hid it from his sight. He continued to press his horse forward, till at last it could struggle no more, and sunk beneath him. Orlando, nowise concerned, stretched out his nervous arms, puffing the salt water from before his mouth, and carried his head above the waves. Fortunately they were not rough, scarce a breath of wind agitated the surface; otherwise, the invincible Orlando would then have met his death. But fortune, which it is said favors fools, delivered him from this danger, and landed him safe on the shore of Ceuta. Here he rambled along the shore till he came to where the black army of Astolpho held its camp.\n\nNow it happened, just before this time, that a vessel filled with prisoners which Rodomont had taken at the bridge had arrived, and, not knowing of the presence of the Abyssinian army, had sailed right into port, where of course the prisoners and their captors changed places, the former being set at liberty and received with all joy, the latter sent to serve in the galleys. Astolpho thus found himself surrounded with Christian knights, and he and his friends were exchanging greetings and felicitations, when a noise was heard in the camp, and seemed to increase every moment.\n\nAstolpho and his friends seized their weapons, mounted their horses, and rode to the quarter whence the noise proceeded. Imagine their astonishment when they saw that the tumult was caused by a single man, perfectly naked, and browned with dirt and exposure, but of a force and fury so terrible that he overturned all that offered to lay hands on him.\n\nAstolpho, Dudon, Oliver, and Florismart gazed at him with amazement. It was with difficulty they knew him. Astolpho, who had been warned of his condition by his holy monitor, was the first to recognize him. As the paladins closed round Orlando, the madman dealt one and another a blow of his fist, which, if they had not been in armor, or he had had any weapon, would probably have despatched them; as it was, Dudon and Astolpho measured their length on the sand. But Florismart seized him from behind, Sansonnet and another grasped his legs, and at last they succeeded in securing him with ropes. They took him to the water-side and washed him well, and then Astolpho, having first bandaged his mouth so that he could not breathe except through his nose, brought the precious phial, uncorked it, and placed it adroitly under his nostrils, when the good Orlando took it all up in one breath. O marvellous prodigy! The paladin recovered in an instant all his intelligence. He felt like one who had awakened from a painful dream, in which he had believed that monsters were about to tear him to pieces. He seemed prostrated, silent, and abashed. Florismart, Oliver, and Astolpho stood gazing upon him, while he turned his eyes around and on himself. He seemed surprised to find himself naked, bound, and stretched on the sea-shore. After a few moments he recognized his friends, and spoke to them in a tone so tender that they hastened to unbind him, and to supply him with garments. Then they exerted themselves to console him, to diminish the weight with which his spirits were oppressed, and to make him forget the wretched condition into which he had been sunk.\n\nOrlando, in recovering his reason, found himself also delivered from his insane attachment to the queen of Cathay. His heart felt now no further influenced by the recollection of her than to be moved with an ardent desire to retrieve his fame by some distinguished exploit. Astolpho would gladly have yielded to him the chief command of the army, but Orlando would not take from the friend to whom he owed so much the glory of the campaign; but in everything the two paladins acted in concert, and united their counsels. They proposed to make a general assault on the city of Biserta, and were only waiting a favorable moment, when their plan was interrupted by new events.\n\nAgramant, after the bloody battle which followed the infraction of the truce, found himself so weak that he saw it was in vain to attempt to remain in France. So, in concert with Sobrino, the bravest and most trusted of his chiefs, he embarked to return to his own country, having previously sent off his few remaining troops in the same direction. The vessel which carried Agramant and Sobrino approached the shore where the army of Astolpho lay encamped before Biserta, and having discovered this fact before it was too late, the king commanded the pilot to steer eastward, with a view to seek protection of the King of Egypt. But the weather becoming rough, he consented to the advice of his companions, and sought harbor in an island which lies between Sicily and Africa. There he found Gradasso, the warlike king of Sericane, who had come to France to possess himself of the horse Bayard and the sword Durindana; and having procured both these prizes was returning to his own country.\nThe two kings, who had been companions in arms under the walls of Paris, embraced one another affectionately. Gradasso learned with regret the reverses of Agramant, and offered him his troops and his person. He strongly deprecated resorting to Egypt for aid. “Remember the great Pompey,” said he, “and shun that fatal shore. My plan,” he continued, “is this: I mean to challenge Orlando to single combat. Possessed of such a sword and steed as mine, if he were made of steel or bronze, he could not escape me. He being removed, there will be no difficulty in driving back the Abyssinians. We will rouse against them the Moslem nations from the other side of the Nile, the Arabians, Persians, and Chaldeans, who will soon make Senapus recall his army to defend his own territories.”\n\nAgramant approved this advice except in one particular. “It is for me,” said he, “to combat Orlando; I cannot with honor devolve that duty on another.”\n\n“Let us adopt a third course,” said the aged warrior Sobrino. “I would not willingly remain a simple spectator of such a contest. Let us send three squires to the shore of Africa to challenge Orlando and any two of his companions in arms to meet us three in this island of Lampedusa.”\n\nThis counsel was adopted; the three squires sped on their way; and now presented themselves, and rehearsed their message to the Christian knights.\n\nOrlando was delighted, and rewarded the squires with rich gifts. He had already resolved to seek Gradasso and compel him to restore Durindana, which he had learned was in his possession. For his two companions the Count chose his faithful friend Florismart and his cousin Oliver.\n\nThe three warriors embarked, and sailing with a favorable wind, the second morning showed them, on their right, the island where this important battle was to be fought. Orlando and his two companions, having landed, pitched their tent. Agramant had placed his opposite.\nNext morning, as soon as Aurora brightened the edges of the horizon, the warriors of both parties armed themselves and mounted their horses. They took their positions, face to face, lowered their lances, placed them in rest, clapped spurs to their horses, and flew to the charge. Orlando met the charge of Gradasso. The paladin was unmoved, but his horse could not sustain the terrible shock of Bayard. He recoiled, staggered, and fell some paces behind. Orlando tried to raise him, but, finding his efforts unavailing, seized his shield, and drew his famous Balisardo. Meanwhile Agramant and the brave Oliver gained no advantage, one or the other; but Florismart unhorsed the King Sobrino. Having brought his foe to the ground, he would not pursue his victory, but hastened to attack Gradasso, who had overthrown Orlando. Seeing him thus engaged, Orlando would not interfere, but ran with sword upraised upon Sobrino, and with one blow deprived him of sense and motion. Believing him dead, he next turned to aid his beloved Florismart. That brave paladin, neither in horse nor arms equal to his antagonist, could but parry and evade the blows of the terrible Durindana. Orlando, eager to succor him, was delayed for a moment in securing and mounting the horse of the King Sobrino. It was but an instant, and with sword upraised, he rushed upon Gradasso who, noways disconcerted at the onset of this second foe, shouted his defiance, and thrust at him with his sword, but, having miscalculated the distance, scarcely reached him, and failed to pierce his mail. Orlando, in return, dealt him a blow with Balisardo, which wounded as it fell face, breast, and thigh, and, if he had been a little nearer, would have cleft him in twain. Sobrino, by this time recovered from his swoon, though severely wounded, raised himself on his legs, and looked to see how he might aid his friends. Observing Agramant hard pressed by Oliver, he thrust his sword into the bowels of the latter’s horse, which fell, and bore down his master, entangling his leg as he fell, so that Oliver could not extricate himself. Florismart saw the danger of his friend, and ran upon Sobrino with his horse, overthrew him, and then turned to defend himself from Agramant. They were not unequally matched, for though Agramant, mounted on Brigliadoro, had an advantage over Florismart, whose horse was but indifferent, yet Agramant had received a serious wound in his encounter with Oliver.\n\nNothing could exceed the fury of the encounter between Orlando and Gradasso. Durindana, in the hands of Gradasso, clove asunder whatever it struck; but such was the skill of Orlando, who perfectly knew the danger to which he was exposed from a stroke of that weapon, it had not yet struck him in such a way as to inflict a wound. Meanwhile, Gradasso was bleeding from many wounds, and his rage and incaution increased every moment. In his desperation he lifted Durindana with both hands, and struck so terrible a blow full on the helmet of Orlando, that for a moment it stunned the paladin. He dropped the reins, and his frightened horse scoured with him over the plain. Gradasso turned to pursue him, but at that moment saw Florismart in the very act of striking a fatal blow at Agramant, whom he had unhorsed. While Florismart was wholly intent upon completing his victory, Gradasso plunged his sword into his side. Florismart fell from his horse, and bathed the plain with his blood.\n\nOrlando recovered himself just in time to see the deed. Whether rage or grief predominated in his breast, I cannot tell; but, seizing Balisardo with fury, his first blow fell upon Agramant, who was nearest to him, and smote his head from his shoulders. At this sight Gradasso for the first time felt his courage sink, and a dark presentiment of death came over him. He hardly stood on his defence when Orlando cast himself upon him, and gave him a fatal thrust. The sword penetrated his ribs, and came out a palm’s breadth on the other side of his body.\n\nThus fell beneath the sword of the most illustrious paladin of France the bravest warrior of the Saracen host. Orlando then, as if despising his victory, leaped lightly to the ground, and ran to his dear friend Florismart, embraced him, and bathed him with his tears. Florismart still breathed. He could even command his voice to utter a few parting words: “Dear friend, do not forget me,—give me your prayers,—and oh! be a brother to Flordelis.” He died in uttering her name.\n\nAfter a few moments given to grief Orlando turned to look for his other companion and his late foes. Oliver lay oppressed with the weight of his horse, from which he had in vain struggled to liberate himself. Orlando extricated him with difficulty; he then raised Sobrino from the earth, and committed him to his squire, treating him as gently as if he had been his own brother. For this terrible warrior was the most generous of men to a fallen foe. He took Bayard and Brigliadoro, with the arms of the conquered knights; their bodies and their other spoils he remitted to their attendants.\n\nBut who can tell the grief of Flordelis when she saw the warriors return, and found not Florismart as usual after absence hasten to her side. She knew by the aspect of the others that her lord was slain. At the thought, and before the question could pass her lips, she fell senseless upon the ground. When life returned, and she learned the truth of her worst fears, she bitterly upbraided herself that she had let him depart without her. “I might have saved him by a single cry when his enemy dealt him that treacherous blow, or I might have thrown myself between and given my worthless life for his. Or if no more, I might have heard his last words, I might have given him a last kiss.” So she lamented, and could not be comforted.\n\n# ROGERO AND BRADAMANTE\n\n————\nAfter the interruption of the combat with Rinaldo, as we have related, Rogero was perplexed with doubts what course to take. The terms of the treaty required him to abandon Agramant, who had broken it, and to transfer his allegiance to Charlemagne; and his love for Bradamante called him in the same direction; but unwillingness to desert his prince and leader in the hour of distress forbade this course. Embarking, therefore, for Africa, he took his way to rejoin the Saracen army; but was arrested midway by a storm which drove the vessel on a rock. The crew took to their boat, but that was quickly swamped in the waves, and Rogero with the rest were compelled to swim for their lives. Then while buffeting the waves Rogero bethought him of his sin in so long delaying his Christian profession, and vowed in his heart that, if he should live to reach the land, he would no longer delay to be baptized. His vows were heard and answered; he succeeded in reaching the shore, and was aided and relieved on landing by a pious hermit, whose cell overlooked the sea. From him he received baptism, having first passed some days with him, partaking his humble fare, and receiving instruction in the doctrines of the Christian faith.\n\nWhile these things were going on, Rinaldo, who had set out on his way to seek Gradasso and recover Bayard from him, hearing on his way of the great things which were doing in Africa, repaired thither to bear his part in them. He arrived too late to do more than join his friends in lamenting the loss of Florismart, and to rejoice with them in their victory over the Pagan knights. On the death of their king the Africans gave up the contest, Biserta submitted, and the Christian knights had only to dismiss their forces, and return home. Astolpho took leave of his Abyssinian army, and sent them back laden with spoil to their own country, not forgetting to intrust to them the bag which held the winds, by means of which they were enabled to cross the sandy desert again without danger, and did not untie it till they reached their own country.\n\nOrlando now, with Oliver, who much needed the surgeon’s care, and Sobrino, to whom equal attention was shown, sailed in a swift vessel to Sicily, bearing with him the body of Florismart, to be laid in Christian earth. Rinaldo accompanied them, as did Sansonnet and the other Christian leaders. Arrived at Sicily, the funeral was solemnized with all the rites of religion, and with the profound grief of those who had known Florismart, or had heard of his fame. Then they resumed their course, steering for Marseilles. But Oliver’s wound grew worse instead of better, and his sufferings so distressed his friends that they conferred together, not knowing what to do. Then said the pilot, “We are not far from an isle where a holy hermit dwells alone in the midst of the sea. It is said none seek his counsel or his aid in vain. He hath wrought marvellous cures, and if you resort to that holy man without doubt he can heal the knight.” Orlando bade him steer thither, and soon the bark was laid safely beside the lonely rock; the wounded man was lowered into their boat, and carried by the crew to the hermit’s cell. It was the same hermit with whom Rogero had taken refuge after his shipwreck, by whom he had been baptized, and with whom he was now staying, absorbed in sacred studies and meditations.\n\nThe holy man received Orlando and the rest with kindness, and inquired their errand; and being told that they had come for help for one who, warring for the Christian faith, was brought to perilous pass by a sad wound, he straightway undertook the cure. His applications were simple, but they were seconded by his prayers. The paladin was soon relieved from pain, and in a few days his foot was perfectly restored to soundness. Sobrino, as soon as he perceived the holy monk perform that wonder, cast aside his false prophet, and with contrite heart owned the true God, and demanded baptism at his hands. The hermit granted his request, and also by his prayers restored him to health, while all the Christian knights rejoiced in his conversion almost as much as at the restoration of Oliver. More than all Rogero felt joy and gratitude, and daily grew in grace and faith.\n\nRogero was known by fame to all the Christian knights, but not even Rinaldo knew him by sight, though he had proved his prowess in combat. Sobrino made him known to them, and great was the joy of all when they found one whose valor and courtesy were renowned through the world no longer an enemy and unbeliever, but a convert and champion of the true faith. All press about the knight; one grasps his hand, another locks him fast in his embrace; but more than all the rest, Rinaldo cherished him, for he more than any knew his worth.\n\nIt was not long before Rogero confided to his friend the hopes he entertained of a union with his sister, and Rinaldo frankly gave his sanction to the proposal. But causes unknown to the paladin were at that very time interposing obstacles to its success.\n\nThe fame of the beauty and worth of Bradamante had reached the ears of the Grecian Emperor, Constantine, and he had sent to Charlemagne to demand the hand of his niece for Leo, his son, and the heir to his dominions. Duke Aymon, her father, had only reserved his consent until he should first have spoken with his son Rinaldo, now absent.\n\nThe warriors now prepared to resume their voyage. Rogero took a tender farewell of the good hermit who had taught him the true faith. Orlando restored to him the horse and arms which were rightly his, not even asserting his claim to Balisarda, that sword which he himself had won from the enchantress.\n\nThe hermit gave his blessing to the band, and they re-embarked. The passage was speedy, and very soon they arrived in the harbor of Marseilles.\n\nAstolpho, when he had dismissed his troops, mounted the Hippogriff, and at one flight shot over to Sardinia, thence to Corsica, thence, turning slightly to the left, hovered over Provence, and alighted in the neighborhood of Marseilles. There he did what he had been commanded to do by the holy saint; he unbridled the Hippogriff, and turned him loose to seek his own retreats, never more to be galled with saddle or bit. The horn had lost its marvellous power ever since the visit to the moon.\n\nAstolpho reached Marseilles the very day when Orlando, Rinaldo, Oliver, Sobrino, and Rogero arrived there. Charles had already heard the news of the defeat of the Saracen kings, and all the accompanying events. On learning the approach of the gallant knights, he sent forward some of his most illustrious nobles to receive them, and himself, with the rest of his court, kings, dukes, and peers, the queen, and a fair and gorgeous band of ladies, set forward from Arles to meet them.\n\nNo sooner were the mutual greetings interchanged, than Orlando and his friends led forward Rogero, and presented him to the Emperor. They vouch him son of Rogero, Duke of Risa, one of the most renowned of Christian warriors, by adverse fortune stolen in his infancy, and brought up by Saracens in the false faith, now by a kind Providence converted, and restored to fill the place his father once held among the foremost champions of the throne and Church.\n\nRogero had alighted from his horse, and stood respectfully before the Emperor. Charlemagne bade him remount and ride beside him; and omitted nothing which might do him honor in sight of his martial train. With pomp triumphal and with festive cheer the troop returned to the city; the streets were decorated with garlands, the houses hung with rich tapestry, and flowers fell like rain upon the conquering host from the hands of fair dames and damsels, from every balcony and window. So welcomed, the mighty Emperor passed on till he reached the royal palace, where many days he feasted, high in hall, with his lords, amid tourney, revel, dance, and song.\n\nWhen Rinaldo told his father, Duke Aymon, how he had promised his sister to Rogero, his father heard him with indignation, having set his heart on seeing her united to the Grecian Emperor’s son. The Lady Beatrice, her mother, also appealed to Bradamante herself to reject a knight who had neither title nor lands, and give the preference to one who would make her Empress of the wide Levant. But Bradamante, though respect forbade her to refuse her mother’s entreaty, would not promise to do what her heart repelled, and answered only with a sigh, until she was alone, and then gave a loose to tears.\n\nMeanwhile Rogero, indignant that a stranger should presume to rob him of his bride, determined to seek the Prince of Greece, and defy him to mortal combat. With this design he donned his armor, but exchanged his crest and emblazonment, and bore instead a white unicorn upon a crimson field. He chose a trusty squire, and, commanding him not to address him as Rogero, rode on his quest. Having crossed the Rhine and the Austrian countries into Hungary, he followed the course of the Danube till he reached Belgrade. There he saw the imperial ensigns spread, and white pavilions, thronged with troops, before the town. For the Emperor Constantine was laying siege to the city to recover it from the Bulgarians, who had taken it from him not long before.\n\nA river flowed between the camp of the Emperor and the Bulgarians, and at the moment when Rogero approached, a skirmish had begun between the parties from either camp, who had approached the stream for the purpose of watering. The Greeks in that affray were four to one, and drove back the Bulgarians in precipitate rout. Rogero, seeing this, and animated only by his hatred of the Grecian prince, dashed into the middle of the flying mass, calling aloud on the fugitives to turn. He encountered first a leader of the Grecian host in splendid armor, a nephew of the Emperor, as dear to him as a son. Rogero’s lance pierced shield and armor, and stretched the warrior breathless on the plain. Another and another fell before him, and astonishment and terror arrested the advance of the Greeks, while the Bulgarians, catching courage from the cavalier, rally, change front, and chase the Grecian troops, who fly in their turn. Leo, the prince, was at a distance when this sudden skirmish rose, but not so far but that he could see distinctly, from an elevated position which he held, how the changed battle was all the work of one man, and could not choose but admire the bravery and prowess with which it was done. He knew by the blazonry displayed that the champion was not of the Bulgarian army, though he furnished aid to them. Although he suffered by his valor, the prince could not wish him ill, for his admiration surpassed his resentment. By this time the Greeks had regained the river, and crossing it by fording or swimming, some made their escape, leaving many more prisoners in the hands of the Bulgarians. Rogero, learning from some of the captives that Leo was at a point some distance down the river, rode thither with a view to meet him, but arrived not before the Greek prince had retired beyond the stream, and broken up the bridge. Day was spent, and Rogero, wearied, looked round for a shelter for the night. He found it in a cottage, where he soon yielded himself to repose. It so happened, a knight who had narrowly escaped Rogero’s sword in the late battle also found shelter in the same cottage, and, recognizing the armor of the unknown knight, easily found means of securing him as he slept, and next morning carried him in chains and delivered him to the Emperor. By him he was in turn delivered to his sister Theodora, mother of the young knight, the first victim of Rogero’s spear. By her he was cast into a dungeon, till her ingenuity could devise a death sufficiently painful to satiate her revenge.\n\nBradamante, meanwhile, to escape her father’s and mother’s importunity, had begged a boon of Charlemagne, which the monarch pledged his royal word to grant; it was that she should not be compelled to marry any one unless he should first vanquish her in single combat. The Emperor therefore proclaimed a tournament in these words: “He that would wed Duke Aymon’s daughter must contend with the sword against that dame, from the sun’s rise to his setting; and if, in that time, he is not overcome the lady shall be his.”\n\nDuke Aymon and the Lady Beatrice, though much incensed at the course things had taken, brought their daughter to court, to await the day appointed for the tournament. Bradamante, not finding there him whom her heart required, distressed herself with doubts what could be the cause of his absence. Of all fancies, the most painful one was that he had gone away to learn to forget her, knowing her father’s and her mother’s opposition to their union, and despairing to contend against them. But oh, how much worse would be the maiden’s woe, if it were known to her what her betrothed was then enduring!\n\nHe was plunged in a dungeon where no ray of daylight ever penetrated, loaded with chains, and scantily supplied with the coarsest food. No wonder despair took possession of his heart, and he longed for death as a relief, when one night (or one day, for both were equally dark to him) he was roused with the glare of a torch and saw two men enter his cell. It was the Prince Leo, with an attendant, who had come as soon as he had learned the wretched fate of the brave knight whose valor he had seen and admired on the field of battle. “Cavalier,” said he, “I am one whom thy valor hath so bound to thee, that I willingly peril my own safety to lend thee aid.” “Infinite thanks I owe you,” replied Rogero, “and the life you give me I promise faithfully to render back upon your call, and promptly to stake it at all times for your service.” The prince then told Rogero his name and rank, at hearing which a tide of contending emotions almost overwhelmed Rogero. He was set at liberty, and had his horse and arms restored to him.\n\nMeanwhile, tidings arrived of King Charles’ decree that whoever aspired to the hand of Bradamante must first encounter her with sword and lance. This news made the Grecian prince turn pale, for he knew he was no match for her in fight. Communing with himself, he sees how he may make his wit supply the place of valor, and employ the French knight, whose name was still unknown to him, to fight the battle for him. Rogero heard the proposal with extreme distress; yet it seemed worse than death to deny the first request of one to whom he owed his life. Hastily he gave his assent “to do in all things that which Leo should command.” Afterward, bitter repentance came over him; yet, rather than confess his change of mind, death itself would be welcome. Death seems his only remedy; but how to die? Sometimes he thinks to make none but a feigned resistance, and allow her sword a ready access, for never can death come more happily than if her hand guide the weapon. Yet this will not avail, for, unless he wins the maid for the Greek prince, his debt remains unpaid. He had promised to maintain a real, not a feigned encounter. He will then keep his word, and banish every thought from his bosom except that which moved him to maintain his truth.\n\nThe young prince, richly attended, set out, and with him Rogero. They arrived at Paris, but Leo preferred not to enter the city, and pitched his tents without the walls, making known his arrival to Charlemagne by an embassy. The monarch was pleased, and testified his courtesy by visits and gifts. The prince set forth the purpose of his coming, and prayed the Emperor to dispatch his suit—“to send forth the damsel who refused ever to take in wedlock any lord inferior to herself in fight; for she should be his bride, or he would perish beneath her sword.”\n\nRogero passed the night before the day assigned for the battle like that which the felon spends, condemned to pay the forfeit of his life on the ensuing day. He chose to fight with sword only, and on foot, for he would not let her see Frontino, knowing that she would recognize the steed. Nor would he use Balisarda, for against that enchanted blade all armor would be of no avail, and the sword that he did take he hammered well upon the edge to abate its sharpness. He wore the surcoat of Prince Leo, and his shield, emblazoned with a golden, double-headed eagle. The prince took care to let himself be seen by none.\n\nBradamante, meanwhile, prepared herself for the combat far differently. Instead of blunting the edge of her falchion she whets the steel, and would fain infuse into it her own acerbity. As the moment approached she seemed to have fire within her veins, and waited impatiently for the trumpet’s sound. At the signal she drew her sword, and fell with fury upon her Rogero. But as a well-built wall or aged rock stands unmoved the fury of the storm, so Rogero, clad in those arms which Trojan Hector once wore, withstood the strokes which stormed about his head and breast and flank. Sparks flew from his shield, his helm, his cuirass; from direct and back strokes, aimed now high, now low, falling thick and fast, like hailstones on a cottage roof; but Rogero, with skilful ward, turns them aside, or receives them where his armor is a sure protection, careful only to protect himself, and with no thought of striking in return. Thus the hours passed away, and, as the sun approached the west, the damsel began to despair. But so much the more her anger increases, and she redoubles her efforts, like the craftsman who sees his work unfinished while the day is wellnigh spent. O miserable damsel! didst thou know whom thou wouldst kill,—if, in that cavalier matched against thee thou didst but know Rogero, on whom thy very life-threads hang, rather than kill him thou wouldst kill thyself, for he is dearer to thee than life.\n\nKing Charles and the peers, who thought the cavalier to be the Grecian prince, viewing such force and skill exhibited, and how without assaulting her the knight defended himself, were filled with admiration, and declared the champions well matched, and worthy of each other.\n\nWhen the sun was set Charlemagne gave the signal for terminating the contest, and Bradamante was awarded to Prince Leo as a bride. Rogero, in deep distress, returned to his tent. There Leo unlaced his helmet, and kissed him on both cheeks. “Henceforth,” said he, “do with me as you please, for you cannot exhaust my gratitude.” Rogero replied little, laid aside the ensigns he had worn, and resumed the unicorn, then hasted to withdraw himself from all eyes. When it was midnight he rose, saddled Frontino, and sallied from his tent, taking that direction which pleased his steed. All night he rode absorbed in bitter woe, and called on Death as alone capable of relieving his sufferings. At last he entered a forest, and penetrated into its deepest recesses. There he unharnessed Frontino, and suffered him to wander where he would. Then he threw himself down on the ground, and poured forth such bitter wailings that the birds and beasts, for none else heard him, were moved to pity with his cries.\n\nNot less was the distress of the lady Bradamante, who, rather than wed any one but Rogero, resolved to break her word, and defy kindred, court, and Charlemagne himself; and, if nothing else would do, to die. But relief came from an unexpected quarter. Marphisa, sister of Rogero, was a heroine of warlike prowess equal to Bradamante. She had been the confidante of their loves, and felt hardly less distress than themselves at seeing the perils which threatened their union. “They are already united by mutual vows,” she said, “and in the sight of Heaven what more is necessary?” Full of this thought she presented herself before Charlemagne, and declared that she herself was witness that the maiden had spoken to Rogero those words which they who marry swear; and that the compact was so sealed between the pair that they were no longer free, nor could forsake the one the other to take another spouse. This her assertion she offered to prove, in single combat, against Prince Leo, or any one else.\n\nCharlemagne, sadly perplexed at this, commanded Bradamante to be called, and told her what the bold Marphisa had declared. Bradamante neither denied nor confirmed the statement, but hung her head, and kept silence. Duke Aymon was enraged, and would fain have set aside the pretended contract on the ground that, if made at all, it must have been made before Rogero was baptized, and therefore void. But not so thought Rinaldo, nor the good Orlando, and Charlemagne knew not which way to decide, when Marphisa spoke thus:\n\n“Since no one else can marry the maiden while my brother lives, let the prince meet Rogero in mortal combat, and let him who survives take her for his bride.”\n\nThis saying pleased the Emperor, and was accepted by the prince, for he thought that, by the aid of his unknown champion, he should surely triumph in the fight. Proclamation was therefore made for Rogero to appear and defend his suit; and Leo, on his part, caused search to be made on all sides for the knight of the Unicorn.\n\nMeanwhile Rogero, overwhelmed with despair, lay stretched on the ground in the forest night and day without food, courting death. Here he was discovered by one of Leo’s people, who, finding him resist all attempts to remove him, hastened to his master, who was not far off, and brought him to the spot. As he approached he heard words which convinced him that love was the cause of the knight’s despair; but no clew was given to guide him to the object of that love. Stooping down, the prince embraced the weeping warrior, and, in the tenderest accents, said: “Spare not, I entreat you, to disclose the cause of your distress, for few such desperate evils betide mankind as are wholly past cure. It grieves me much that you would hide your grief from me, for I am bound to you by ties that nothing can undo. Tell me, then, your grief, and leave me to try if wealth, art, cunning, force, or persuasion cannot relieve you. If not, it will be time enough after all has been tried in vain to die.”\n\nHe spoke in such moving accents that Rogero could not choose but yield. It was some time before he could command utterance; at last he said, “My lord, when you shall know me for what I am, I doubt not you, like myself, will be content that I should die. Know, then, I am that Rogero whom you have so much cause to hate, and who so hated you that, intent on putting you to death, he went to seek you at your father’s court. This I did because I could not submit to see my promised bride borne off by you. But, as man proposes and God disposes, your great courtesy, well tried in time of sore need, so moved my fixed resolve, that I not only laid aside the hate I bore, but purposed to be your friend forever. You then asked of me to win for you the lady Bradamante, which was all one as to demand of me my heart and soul. You know whether I served you faithfully or not. Yours is the lady; possess her in peace; but ask me not to live to see it. Be content rather that I die; for vows have passed between myself and her which forbid that while I live she can lawfully wive with another.”\n\nSo filled was gentle Leo with astonishment at these words that for a while he stood silent, with lips unmoved and steadfast gaze, like a statue. And the discovery that the stranger was Rogero not only abated not the good will he bore him, but increased it, so that his distress for what Rogero suffered seemed equal to his own. For this, and because he would appear deservedly an Emperor’s son, and, though in other things outdone, would not be surpassed in courtesy, he says: “Rogero, had I known that day when your matchless valor routed my troops that you were Rogero, your virtue would have made me your own, as then it made me while I knew not my foe, and I should have no less gladly rescued you from Theodora’s dungeon. And if I would willingly have done so then, how much more gladly will I now restore the gift of which you would rob yourself to confer it upon me. The damsel is more due to you than to me, and though I know her worth, I would forego not only her, but life itself, rather than distress a knight like you.”\n\nThis and much more he said to the same intent; till at last Rogero replied, “I yield, and am content to live, and thus a second time owe my life to you.”\n\nBut several days elapsed before Rogero was so far restored as to return to the royal residence, where an embassy had arrived from the Bulgarian princes to seek the knight of the unicorn, and tender to him the crown of that country, in place of their king, fallen in battle.\n\nThus were things situated when Prince Leo, leading by the hand Rogero, clad in the battered armor in which he had sustained the conflict with Bradamante, presented himself before the king. “Behold,” he said “the champion who maintained from dawn to setting sun the arduous contest; he comes to claim the guerdon of the fight.” King Charlemagne, with all his peerage, stood amazed; for all believed that the Grecian prince himself had fought with Bradamante. Then stepped forth Marphisa, and said, “Since Rogero is not here to assert his rights, I, his sister, undertake his cause, and will maintain it against whoever shall dare dispute his claim.” She said this with so much anger and disdain that the prince deemed it no longer wise to feign, and withdrew Rogero’s helmet from his brow, saying, “Behold him here!” Who can describe the astonishment and joy of Marphisa! She ran and threw her arms about her brother’s neck, nor would give way to let Charlemagne and Rinaldo, Orlando, Dudon, and the rest, who crowded round, embrace him, and press friendly kisses on his brow. The joyful tidings flew fast by many a messenger to Bradamante, who in her secret chamber lay lamenting. The blood that stagnated about her heart flowed at that notice so fast, that she had wellnigh died for joy. Duke Aymon and the Lady Beatrice no longer withheld their consent, and pledged their daughter to the brave Rogero before all that gallant company.\n\nNow came the Bulgarian ambassadors, and, kneeling at the feet of Rogero, besought him to return with them to their country, where, in Adrianople, the crown and sceptre were awaiting his acceptance. Prince Leo united his persuasions to theirs, and promised, in his royal father’s name, that peace should be restored on their part. Rogero gave his consent, and it was surmised that none of the virtues which shone so conspicuously in him so availed to recommend Rogero to the Lady Beatrice as the hearing her future son-in-law saluted as a sovereign prince.\n\n# THE BATTLE OF RONCESVALLES\n\n————\nAfter the expulsion of the Saracens from France Charlemagne led his army into Spain, to punish Marsilius, the king of that country, for having sided with the African Saracens in the late war. Charlemagne succeeded in all his attempts, and compelled Marsilius to submit, and pay tribute to France. Our readers will remember Gano, otherwise called Gan, or Ganelon, whom we mentioned in one of our early chapters as an old courtier of Charlemagne, and a deadly enemy of Orlando, Rinaldo, and all their friends. He had great influence over Charles, from equality of age and long intimacy; and he was not without good qualities: he was brave and sagacious, but envious, false, and treacherous. Gan prevailed on Charles to send him as ambassador to Marsilius, to arrange the tribute. He embraced Orlando over and over again at taking leave, using such pains to seem loving and sincere, that his hypocrisy was manifest to every one but the old monarch. He fastened with equal tenderness on Oliver, who smiled contemptuously in his face, and thought to himself, “You may make as many fair speeches as you choose, but you lie.” All the other paladins who were present thought the same, and they said as much to the Emperor, adding that Gan should on no account be sent ambassador to the Spaniards. But Charles was infatuated.\n\nGan was received with great honor by Marsilius. The king, attended by his lords, came fifteen miles out of Saragossa to meet him, and then conducted him into the city with acclamations. There was nothing for several days but balls, games, and exhibitions of chivalry, the ladies throwing flowers on the heads of the French knights, and the people shouting, “France! Mountjoy and St. Denis!”\n\nAfter the ceremonies of the first reception the king and the ambassador began to understand one another. One day they sat together in a garden on the border of a fountain. The water was so clear and smooth it reflected every object around, and the spot was encircled with fruit-trees which quivered with the fresh air. As they sat and talked, as if without restraint, Gan, without looking the king in the face, was enabled to see the expression of his countenance in the water, and governed his speech accordingly. Marsilius was equally adroit, and watched the face of Gan while he addressed him. Marsilius began by lamenting, not as to the ambassador, but as to the friend, the injuries which Charles had done him by invading his dominions, charging him with wishing to take his kingdom from him and give it to Orlando; till at length he plainly uttered his belief that if that ambitious paladin were but dead good men would get their rights.\n\nGan heaved a sigh, as if he was unwillingly compelled to allow the force of what the king said; but unable to contain himself long he lifted up his face, radiant with triumphant wickedness, and exclaimed: “Every word you utter is truth; die he must, and die also must Oliver, who struck me that foul blow at court. Is it treachery to punish affronts like these? I have planned everything,—I have settled everything already with their besotted master. Orlando will come to your borders—to Roncesvalles—for the purpose of receiving the tribute. Charles will await him at the foot of the mountains. Orlando will bring but a small band with him: you, when you meet him, will have secretly your whole army at your back. You surround him, and who receives tribute then?”\n\nThe new Judas had scarcely uttered these words when his exultation was interrupted by a change in the face of nature. The sky was suddenly overcast, there was thunder and lightning, a laurel was split in two from head to foot, and the Carob-tree under which Gan was sitting, which is said to be the species of tree on which Judas Iscariot hung himself, dropped one of its pods on his head.\n\nMarsilius, as well as Gan, was appalled at this omen; but on assembling his soothsayers they came to the conclusion that the laurel-tree turned the omen against the Emperor, the successor of the Cæsars, though one of them renewed the consternation of Gan by saying that he did not understand the meaning of the tree of Judas, and intimating that perhaps the ambassador could explain it. Gan relieved his vexation by anger; the habit of wickedness prevailed over all other considerations; and the king prepared to march to Roncesvalles at the head of all his forces.\n\nGan wrote to Charlemagne to say how humbly and submissively Marsilius was coming to pay the tribute into the hands of Orlando, and how handsome it would be of the Emperor to meet him half-way, and so be ready to receive him after the payment at his camp. He added a brilliant account of the tribute, and the accompanying presents. The good Emperor wrote in turn to say how pleased he was with the ambassador’s diligence, and that matters were arranged precisely as he wished. His court, however, had its suspicion still, though they little thought Gan’s object in bringing Charles into the neighborhood of Roncesvalles was to deliver him into the hands of Marsilius, after Orlando should have been destroyed by him.\n\nOrlando, however, did as his lord and sovereign desired. He went to Roncesvalles, accompanied by a moderate train of warriors, not dreaming of the atrocity that awaited him. Gan, meanwhile, had hastened back to France, in order to show himself free and easy in the presence of Charles, and secure the success of his plot; while Marsilius, to make assurance doubly sure, brought into the passes of Roncesvalles no less than three armies, which were successively to fall on the paladin in case of the worst, and so extinguish him with numbers. He had also, by Gan’s advice, brought heaps of wine and good cheer to be set before his victims in the first instance; “for that,” said the traitor, “will render the onset the more effective, the feasters being unarmed. One thing, however, I must not forget,” added he; “my son Baldwin is sure to be with Orlando; you must take care of his life for my sake.”\n\n“I give him this vesture off my own body,” said the king; “let him wear it in the battle, and have no fear. My soldiers shall be directed not to touch him.”\n\nGan went away rejoicing to France. He embraced the sovereign and the court all round with the air of a man who had brought them nothing but blessings, and the old king wept for very tenderness and delight.\n\n“Something is going on wrong, and looks very black,” thought Malagigi, the good wizard; “Rinaldo is not here, and it is indispensably necessary that he should be. I must find out where he is, and Ricciardetto too, and send for them with all speed.”\n\nMalagigi called up by his art a wise, terrible, and cruel spirit, named Ashtaroth. “Tell me, and tell me truly, of Rinaldo,” said Malagigi to the spirit. The demon looked hard at the paladin, and said nothing. His aspect was clouded and violent.\n\nThe enchanter, with an aspect still cloudier, bade Ashtaroth lay down that look, and made signs as if he would resort to angrier compulsion; and the devil, alarmed, loosened his tongue, and said, “You have not told me what you desire to know of Rinaldo.”\n\n“I desire to know what he has been doing, and where he is.”\n\n“He has been conquering and baptizing the world, east and west,” said the demon, “and is now in Egypt with Ricciardetto.”\n\n“And what has Gan been plotting with Marsilius?” inquired Malagigi; “and what is to come of it?”\n\n“I know not,” said the devil. “I was not attending to Gan at the time, and we fallen spirits know not the future. All I discern is that by the signs and comets in the heavens something dreadful is about to happen—something very strange, treacherous, and bloody; and that Gan has a seat ready prepared for him in hell.”\n\n“Within three days,” cried the enchanter, loudly, “bring Rinaldo and Ricciardetto into the pass of Roncesvalles. Do it, and I hereby undertake to summon thee no more.”\n\n“Suppose they will not trust themselves with me?” said the spirit.\n\n“Enter Rinaldo’s horse, and bring him, whether he trust thee or not.”\n\n“It shall be done,” returned the demon.\n\nThere was an earthquake, and Ashtaroth disappeared.\n\n————\nMarsilius now made his first movement towards the destruction of Orlando, by sending before him his vassal, King Blanchardin, with his presents of wines and other luxuries. The temperate but courteous hero took them in good part, and distributed them as the traitor wished; and then Blanchardin, on pretence of going forward to salute Charlemagne, returned, and put himself at the head of the second army, which was the post assigned him by his liege-lord. King Falseron, whose son Orlando had slain in battle, headed the first army, and King Balugante the third. Marsilius made a speech to them, in which he let them into his design, and concluded by recommending to their good will the son of his friend Gan, whom they would know by the vest he had sent him, and who was the only soul amongst the Christian they were to spare.\n\nThis son of Gan, meanwhile, and several of the paladins, who distrusted the misbelievers, and were anxious at all events to be with Orlando, had joined the hero in the fatal valley; so that the little Christian host, considering the tremendous valor of their lord and his friends, were not to be sold for nothing. Rinaldo, alas! the second thunderbolt of Christendom, was destined not to be there in time to meet the issue. The paladins in vain begged Orlando to be on his guard against treachery, and send for a more numerous body of men. The great heart of the Champion of the Faith was unwilling to harbor suspicion as long as he could help it. He refused to summon aid which might be superfluous; neither would he do anything but what his liege-lord had directed. And yet he could not wholly repress a misgiving. A shadow had fallen on his heart, great and cheerful as it was. The anticipations of his friends disturbed him, in spite of the face with which he met them. Perhaps by a certain foresight he felt his death approaching; but he felt bound not to encourage the impression. Besides, time pressed; the moment of the looked-for tribute was at hand, and little combinations of circumstances determine often the greatest events.\n\nKing Marsilius was to arrive early next day with the tribute, and Oliver, with the morning sun, rode forth to reconnoitre, and see if he could discover the peaceful pomp of the Spanish court in the distance. He rode up the nearest height, and from the top of it beheld the first army of Marsilius already forming in the passes. “O devil Gan,” he exclaimed, “this then is the consummation of thy labors!” Oliver put spurs to his horse, and galloped back down the mountain to Orlando.\n\n“Well,” cried the hero, “what news?”\n\n“Bad news,” said his cousin, “such as you would not hear of yesterday. Marsilius is here in arms, and all the world is with him.”\n\nThe paladins pressed round Orlando, and entreated him to sound his horn, in token that he needed help. His only answer was to mount his horse, and ride up the mountain with Sansonetto.\n\nAs soon, however, as he cast forth his eyes, and beheld what was round about him, he turned in sorrow, and looked down into Roncesvalles, and said, “O miserable valley! the blood shed in thee this day will color thy name forever.”\n\nOrlando’s little camp were furious against the Saracens. They armed themselves with the greatest impatience. There was nothing but lacing of helmets and mounting of horses, while good Archbishop Turpin went from rank to rank exhorting and encouraging the warriors of Christ. Orlando and his captains withdrew for a moment to consultation. He fairly groaned for sorrow, and at first had not a word to say, so wretched he felt at having brought his people to die in Roncesvalles. Then he said: “If it had entered into my heart to conceive the king of Spain to be such a villain never would you have seen this day. He has exchanged with me a thousand courtesies and good words; and I thought that the worse enemies we had been before, the better friends we had become now. I fancied every human being capable of this kind of virtue on a good opportunity, saving, indeed, such base-hearted wretches as can never forgive their very forgivers; and of these I did not suppose him to be one. Let us die, if die we must, like honest and gallant men, so that it shall be said of us it was only our bodies that died. The reason why I did not sound the horn was partly because I thought it did not become us, and partly because our liege lord could hardly save us, even if he heard it.” And with these words Orlando sprang to his horse, crying, “Away against the Saracens!” But he had no sooner turned his face than he wept bitterly, and said, “O Holy Virgin, think not of me, the sinner Orlando, but have pity on these thy servants!”\n\nAnd now with a mighty dust, and an infinite sound of horns and tambours, which came filling the valley, the first army of the infidels made its\n\nappearance, horses neighing, and a thousand pennons flying in the air. King Falseron led them on, saying to his officers: “Let nobody dare to lay a finger on Orlando. He belongs to myself. The revenge of my son’s death is mine. I will cut the man down that comes between us.”\n\n“Now, friends,” said Orlando, “every man for himself, and St. Michael for us all! There is not one here that is not a perfect knight.” And he might well say it, for the flower of all France was there, except Rinaldo and Ricciardetto—every man a picked man, all friends and constant companions of Orlando.\n\nSo the captains of the little troop and of the great army sat looking at one another, and singling one another out as the latter came on, and then the knights put spear in rest, and ran for a while two and two in succession, one against the other.\n\nAstolpho was the first to move. He ran against Arlotto of Sorio, and thrust his antagonist’s body out of the saddle, and his soul into the other world. Oliver encountered Malprimo, and, though he received a thrust which hurt him, sent his lance right through the heart of Malprimo.\n\nFalseron was daunted at this blow. “Truly,” thought he, “this is a marvel.” Oliver did not press on among the Saracens, his wound was too painful; but Orlando now put himself and his whole band in motion, and you may guess what an uproar ensued. The sound of the rattling of blows and helmets was as if the forge of Vulcan had been thrown open. Falseron beheld Orlando coming so furiously, that he thought him a Lucifer who had burst his chain, and was quite of another mind than when he purposed to have him all to himself. On the contrary, he recommended himself to his gods, and turned away, meaning to wait for a more auspicious season of revenge. But Orlando hailed him with a terrible voice, saying, “O thou traitor! was this the end to which old quarrels were made up?” Then he dashed at Falseron with a fury so swift, and at the same time with a mastery of his lance so marvellous, that, though he plunged it in the man’s body so as instantly to kill him, and then withdrew it, the body did not move in the saddle. The hero himself, as he rushed onwards, was fain to see the end of a stroke so perfect, and turning his horse back, touched the carcass with his sword, and it fell on the instant!\n\nWhen the infidels beheld their leader dead such fear fell upon them that they were for leaving the field to the paladins, but they were unable. Marsilius had drawn the rest of his forces round the valley like a net, so that their shoulders were turned in vain. Orlando rode into the thick of them, and wherever he went thunderbolts fell upon helmets. Oliver was again in the fray, with Walter and Baldwin, Avino and Avolio, while Archbishop Turpin had changed his crosier for a lance, and chased a new flock before him to the mountains.\n\nYet what could be done against foes without number? Marsilius constantly pours them in. The paladins are as units to thousands. Why tarry the horses of Rinaldo and Ricciardetto?\n\nThe horses did not tarry, but fate had been quicker than enchantment. Ashtaroth had presented himself to Rinaldo in Egypt, and, after telling his errand, he and Foul-mouth, his servant, entered the horses of Rinaldo and Ricciardetto, which began to neigh, and snort, and leap with the fiends within them, till off they flew through the air over the pyramids and across the desert, and reached Spain and the scene of action just as Marsilius brought up his third army. The two paladins on their horses dropped right into the midst of the Saracens, and began making such havoc among them that Marsilius, who overlooked the fight from a mountain, thought his soldiers had turned against one another. Orlando beheld it, and guessed it could be no other but his cousins, and pressed to meet them. Oliver coming up at the same moment, the rapture of the whole party is not to be expressed. After a few hasty words of explanation they were forced to turn again upon the enemy, whose numbers seemed perfectly without limit.\n\nOrlando, making a bloody passage towards Marsilius, struck a youth on the head, whose helmet was so strong as to resist the blow, but at the same time flew off. Orlando prepared to strike a second blow, when the youth exclaimed, “Hold! you loved my father; I am Bujaforte!” The paladin had never seen Bujaforte, but he saw the likeness to the good old man, his father, and he dropped his sword. “O Bujaforte,” said he, “I loved him indeed; but what does his son do here fighting against his friends?”\n\nBujaforte could not at once speak for weeping. At length he said: “I am forced to be here by my lord and master, Marsilius; and I have made a show of fighting, but have not hurt a single Christian. Treachery is on every side of you. Baldwin himself has a vest given him by Marsilius, that everybody may know the son of his friend Gan, and do him no harm.”\n\n“Put your helmet on again,” said Orlando, “and behave just as you have done. Never will your father’s friend be an enemy to the son.”\n\nThe hero then turned in fury to look for Baldwin, who was hastening towards him at that moment, with friendliness in his looks.\n\n“’Tis strange,” said Baldwin, “I have done my duty as well as I could, yet nobody will come against me. I have slain right and left, and cannot comprehend what it is that makes the stoutest infidels avoid me.”\n\n“Take off your vest,” said Orlando, contemptuously, “and you will soon discover the secret, if you wish to know it. Your father has sold us to Marsilius, all but his honorable son.”\n\n“If my father,” said Baldwin, impetuously tearing off the vest, “has been such a villain, and I escape dying, I will plunge this sword through his heart. But I am no traitor, Orlando, and you do me wrong to say it. Think not I can live with dishonor.”\n\nBaldwin spurred off into the fight, not waiting to hear another word from Orlando, who was very sorry for what he had said, for he perceived that the youth was in despair.\n\nAnd now the fight raged beyond all it had done before; twenty pagans went down for one paladin, but still the paladins fell. Sansonetto was beaten to earth by the club of Grandonio, Walter d’Amulion had his shoulder broken, Berlinghieri and Ottone were slain, and at last Astolpho fell, in revenge of whose death Orlando turned the spot where he died into a lake of Saracen blood. The luckless Bujaforte met Rinaldo, and before he could explain how he seemed to be fighting on the Saracen side received such a blow upon the head that he fell, unable to utter a word. Orlando, cutting his way to a spot where there was a great struggle and uproar, found the poor youth Baldwin, the son of Gan, with two spears in his breast. “I am no traitor now,” said Baldwin, and those were the last words he said. Orlando was bitterly sorry to have been the cause of his death, and tears streamed from his eyes. At length down went Oliver himself. He had become blinded with his own blood, and smitten Orlando without knowing him. “How now, cousin,” cried Orlando, “have you too gone over to the enemy?” “O my lord and master,” cried the other, “I ask your pardon. I can see nothing; I am dying. Some traitor has stabbed me in the back. If you love me, lead my horse into the thick of them, so that I may not die unavenged.”\n\n“I shall die myself before long,” said Orlando, “out of very toil and grief; so we will go together.”\n\nOrlando led his cousin’s horse where the press was thickest, and dreadful was the strength of the dying man and his tired companion. They made a street through which they passed out of the battle, and Orlando led his cousin away to his tent, and said, “Wait a little till I return, for I will go and sound the horn on the hill yonder.”\n\n“’Tis of no use,” said Oliver, “my spirit is fast going and desires to be with its Lord and Saviour.”\n\nHe would have said more, but his words came from him imperfectly, like those of a man in a dream, and so he expired.\n\nWhen Orlando saw him dead he felt as if he was alone on the earth, and he was quite willing to leave it, only he wished that King Charles, at the foot of the mountains, should know how the case stood before he went. So he took up the horn and blew it three times, with such force that the blood burst out of his nose and mouth. Turpin says that at the third blast the horn broke in two.\n\nIn spite of all the noise of the battle, the sound of the horn broke over it like a voice out of the other world. They say that birds fell dead at it, and that the whole Saracen army drew back in terror. Charlemagne was sitting in the midst of his court when the sound reached him, and Gan was there. The Emperor was the first to hear it.\n\n“Do you hear that?” said he to his nobles. “Did you hear the horn as I heard it?”\n\nUpon this they all listened, and Gan felt his heart misgive him. The horn sounded a second time.\n\n“What is the meaning of this?” said Charles.\n\n“Orlando is hunting,” observed Gan, “and the stag is killed.”\n\nBut when the horn sounded yet a third time, and the blast was one of so dreadful a vehemence, everybody looked at the other, and then they all looked at Gan in a fury. Charles rose from his seat.\n\n“This is no hunting of the stag,” said he. “The sound goes to my very heart. O Gan! O Gan! Not for thee do I blush, but for myself. O foul and monstrous villain! Take him, gentleman, and keep him in close prison. Would to God I had not lived to see this day!”\n\nBut it was no time for words. They put the traitor in prison and then Charles, with all his court, took his way to Roncesvalles, grieving and praying.\n\nIt was afternoon when the horn sounded, and half an hour after it when the Emperor set out; and meantime Orlando had returned to the fight that he might do his duty, however hopeless, as long as he could sit his horse. At length he found his end approaching, for toil and fever, and rode all alone to a fountain where he had before quenched his thirst. His horse was wearier than he, and no sooner had his master alighted than the beast, kneeling down as if to take leave, and to say, “I have brought you to a place of rest,” fell dead at his feet. Orlando cast water on him from the fountain, not wishing to believe him dead; but when he found it to no purpose, he grieved for him as if he had been a human being, and addressed him by name with tears, and asked forgiveness if he had ever done him wrong. They say that the horse, at these words, opened his eyes a little, and looked kindly at his master, and then stirred never more. They say also that Orlando then summoning all his strength, smote a rock near him with his beautiful sword Durindana, thinking to shiver the steel in pieces, and so prevent its falling into the hands of the enemy, but though the rock split like a slate, and a great cleft remained ever after to astonish the eyes of pilgrims, the sword remained uninjured.\n\nAnd now Rinaldo and Ricciardetto came up, with Turpin, having driven back the Saracens, and told Orlando that the battle was won. Then Orlando knelt before Turpin and begged remission of his sins, and Turpin gave him absolution. Orlando fixed his eyes on the hilt of his sword as on a crucifix, and embraced it, and he raised his eyes and appeared like a creature seraphical and transfigured, and bowing his head, he breathed out his pure soul.\n\nAnd now King Charles and his nobles came up. The Emperor, at sight of the dead Orlando, threw himself, as if he had been a reckless youth, from his horse, and embraced and kissed the body, and said: “I bless thee, Orlando; I bless thy whole life, and all that thou wast, and all that thou ever didst, and the father that begat thee; and I ask pardon of thee for believing those who brought thee to thine end. They shall have their reward, O thou beloved one! But indeed it is thou that livest, and I who am worse than dead.”\n\nHorrible to the Emperor’s eyes was the sight of the field of Roncesvalles. The Saracens indeed had fled, conquered; but all his paladins but two were left on it dead, and the whole valley looked like a great slaughterhouse, trampled into blood and dirt, and reeking to the heat. Charles trembled to his heart’s core for wonder and agony. After gazing dumbly on the place he cursed it with a solemn curse, and wished that never grass might grow in it again, nor seed of any kind, neither within it nor on any of its mountains around, but the anger of Heaven abide over it forever.\n\nCharles and his warriors went after the Saracens into Spain. They took and fired Saragossa, and Marsilius was hung to the carob-tree under which he had planned his villainy with Gan; and Gan was hung and drawn and quartered in Roncesvalles, amidst the execrations of the country.\n\n# RINALDO AND BAYARD\n\n————\nCharlemagne was overwhelmed with grief at the loss of so many of his bravest warriors at the disaster of Roncesvalles, and bitterly reproached himself for his credulity in resigning himself so completely to the counsels of the treacherous Count Gan. Yet he soon fell into a similar snare when he suffered his unworthy son, Charlot, to acquire such an influence over him, that he constantly led him into acts of cruelty and injustice that in his right mind he would have scorned to commit. Rinaldo and his brothers, for some slight offence to the imperious young prince, were forced to fly from Paris, and to take shelter in their castle of Montalban; for Charles had publicly said, if he could take them he would hang them all. He sent numbers of his bravest knights to arrest them, but all without success. Either Rinaldo foiled their efforts and sent them back, stripped of their armor and of their glory, or, after meeting and conferring with him, they came back and told the king they could not be his instruments for such a work.\n\nAt last Charles himself raised a great army, and went in person to compel the paladin to submit. He ravaged all the country round about Montalban, so that supplies of food should be cut off, and he threatened death to any who should attempt to issue forth, hoping to compel the garrison to submit for want of food.\n\nRinaldo’s resources had been brought so low that it seemed useless to contend any longer. His brothers had been taken prisoners in a skirmish, and his only hope of saving their lives was in making terms with the king.\n\nSo he sent a messenger, offering to yield himself and his castle if the king would spare his and his brothers’ lives. While the messenger was gone Rinaldo, impatient to learn what tidings he might bring, rode out to meet him. When he had ridden as far as he thought prudent he stopped in a wood, and alighting, tied Bayard to a tree. Then he sat down, and, as he waited, he fell asleep. Bayard meanwhile got loose, and strayed away where the grass tempted him. Just then came along some country people, who said to one another, “Look, is not that the great horse Bayard that Rinaldo rides? Let us take him, and carry him to King Charles, who will pay us well for our trouble.” They did so, and the king was delighted with his prize, and gave them a present that made them rich to their dying day.\n\nWhen Rinaldo woke he looked round for his horse, and, finding him not, he groaned, and said, “O unlucky hour that I was born! how fortune persecutes me!” So desperate was he that he took off his armor and his spurs, saying, “What need have I of these, since Bayard is lost?” While he stood thus lamenting, a man came from the thicket, seemingly bent with age. He had a long beard hanging over his breast, and eyebrows that almost covered his eyes. He bade Rinaldo good day. Rinaldo thanked him, and said, “A good day I have hardly had since I was born.” Then said the old man, “Signor Rinaldo, you must not despair, for God will make all things turn to the best.” Rinaldo answered, “My trouble is too heavy for me to hope relief. The king has taken my brothers, and means to put them to death. I thought to rescue them by means of my horse Bayard, but while I slept some thief has stolen him.” The old man replied, “I will remember you and your brothers in my prayers. I am a poor man, have you not something to give me?” Rinaldo said, “I have nothing to give,” but then he recollected his spurs. He gave them to the beggar, and said, “Here, take my spurs. They are the first present my mother gave me when my father, Count Aymon, dubbed me knight. They ought to bring you ten pounds.”\n\nThe old man took the spurs, and put them into his sack, and said, “Noble sir, have you nothing else you can give me?” Rinaldo replied, “Are you making sport of me? I tell you truly if it were not for shame to beat one so helpless, I would teach you better manners.” The old man said, “Of a truth, sir, if you did so you would do a great sin. If all had beaten me of whom I have begged I should have been killed long ago, for I ask alms in churches and convents, and wherever I can.” “You say true,” replied Rinaldo, “if you did not ask, none would relieve you.” The old man said, “True, noble sir, therefore I pray if you have anything more to spare, give it me.” Rinaldo gave him his mantle, and said, “Take it, pilgrim. I give it you for the love of Christ, that God would save my brothers from a shameful death, and help me to escape out of King Charles’s power.”\n\nThe pilgrim took the mantle, folded it up, and put it into his bag. Then a third time he said to Rinaldo, “Sir, have you nothing left to give me that I may remember you in my prayers?” “Wretch!” exclaimed Rinaldo, “do you make me your sport?” and he drew his sword, and struck at him; but the old man warded off the blow with his staff, and said, “Rinaldo, would you slay your cousin, Malagigi?” When Rinaldo heard that he stayed his hand, and gazed doubtingly on the old man, who now threw aside his disguise, and appeared to be indeed Malagigi. “Dear cousin,” said Rinaldo, “pray forgive me. I did not know you. Next to God, my trust is in you. Help my brothers to escape out of prison, I entreat you. I have lost my horse, and therefore cannot render them any assistance.” Malagigi answered, “Cousin Rinaldo, I will enable you to recover your horse. Meanwhile, you must do as I say.”\n\nThen Malagigi took from his sack a gown, and gave it to Rinaldo to put on over his armor, and a hat that was full of holes, and an old pair of shoes to put on. They looked like two pilgrims, very old and poor. Then they went forth from the wood, and after a little while saw four monks riding along the road. Malagigi said to Rinaldo, “I will go meet the monks, and see what news I can learn.”\n\nMalagigi learned from the monks that on the approaching festival there would be a great crowd of people at court, for the prince was going to show the ladies the famous horse Bayard that used to belong to Rinaldo. “What!” said the pilgrim; “is Bayard there?” “Yes,” answered the monks; “the king has given him to Charlot, and, after the prince has ridden him the king means to pass sentence on the brothers of Rinaldo, and have them hanged.” Then Malagigi asked alms of the monks, but they would give him none, till he threw aside his pilgrim garb, and let them see his armor, when, partly for charity and partly for terror, they gave him a golden cup, adorned with precious stones that sparkled in the sunshine.\nMalagigi then hastened back to Rinaldo, and told him what he had learned.\n\nThe morning of the feast-day Rinaldo and Malagigi came to the place where the sports were to be held. Malagigi gave Rinaldo his spurs back again, and said, “Cousin, put on your spurs, for you will need them.” “How shall I need them,” said Rinaldo, “since I have lost my horse?” Yet he did as Malagigi directed him.\n\nWhen the two had taken their stand on the border of the field among the crowd the princes and ladies of the court began to assemble. When they were all assembled the king came also, and Charlot with him, near whom the horse Bayard was led, in the charge of grooms, who were expressly enjoined to guard him safely. The king, looking round on the circle of spectators, saw Malagigi and Rinaldo, and observed the splendid cup that they had, and said to Charlot, “See, my son, what a brilliant cup those two pilgrims have got. It seems to be worth a hundred ducats.” “That is true,” said Charlot; “Let us go and ask where they got it.” So they rode to the place where the pilgrims stood, and Charlot stopped Bayard close to them.\n\nThe horse snuffed at the pilgrims, knew Rinaldo, and caressed his master. The king said to Malagigi, “Friend, where did you get that beautiful cup?” Malagigi replied, “Honorable sir, I paid for it all the money I have saved from eleven years’ begging in churches and convents. The Pope himself has blessed it, and given it the power that whosoever eats or drinks out of it shall be pardoned of all his sins.” Then said the king to Charlot, “My son, these are right holy men; see how the dumb beast worships them.”\n\nThen the king said to Malagigi, “Give me a morsel from your cup, that I may be cleared of my sins.” Malagigi answered, “Illustrious lord, I dare not do it, unless you will forgive all who have at any time offended you. You know that Christ forgave all those who had betrayed and crucified him.” The king replied, “Friend, that is true; but Rinaldo has so grievously offended me, that I cannot forgive him, nor that other man, Malagigi, the magician. These two shall never live in my kingdom again. If I catch them I will certainly have them hanged. But tell me, pilgrim, who is that man who stands beside you?” “He is deaf, dumb, and blind,” said Malagigi. Then the king said again, “Give me to drink of your cup, to take away my sins.” Malagigi answered, “My lord king, here is my poor brother, who for fifty days has not heard, spoken, nor seen. This misfortune befell him in a house where we found shelter, and the day before yesterday we met with a wise woman, who told him the only hope of a cure for him was to come to some place where Bayard was to be ridden, and to mount and ride him; that would do him more good than anything else.” Then said the king, “Friend, you have come to the right place, for Bayard is to be ridden here to-day. Give me a draught from your cup, and your companion shall ride upon Bayard.” Malagigi, hearing these words, said, “Be it so.” Then the king, with great devotion, took a spoon, and dipped a portion from the pilgrim’s cup, believing that his sins should be thereby forgiven.\n\nWhen this was done, the king said to Charlot, “Son, I request that you will let this sick pilgrim sit on your horse, and ride if he can, for by so doing he will be healed of all his infirmities.” Charlot replied, “That will I gladly do.” So saying, he dismounted, and the servants took the pilgrim in their arms, and helped him on the horse.\n\nWhen Rinaldo was mounted, he put his feet in the stirrups, and said, “I would like to ride a little.” Malagigi, hearing him speak, seemed delighted, and asked him whether he could see and hear also. “Yes,” said Rinaldo, “I am healed of all my infirmities.” When the king heard it he said to Bishop Turpin, “My lord bishop, we must celebrate this with a procession, with crosses and banners, for it is a great miracle.”\n\nWhen Rinaldo remarked that he was not carefully watched, he spoke to the horse, and touched him with the spurs. Bayard knew that his master was upon him, and he started off upon a rapid pace, and in a few moments was a good way off. Malagigi pretended to be in great alarm. “O noble king and master,” he cried, “my poor companion is run away with; he will fall and break his neck.” The king ordered his knights to ride after the pilgrim, and bring him back, or help him if need were. They did so, but it was in vain. Rinaldo left them all behind him, and kept on his way till he reached Montalban. Malagigi was suffered to depart, unsuspected, and he went his way, making sad lamentation for the fate of his comrade, who he pretended to think must surely be dashed to pieces.\n\nMalagigi did not go far, but having changed his disguise, returned to where the king was, and employed his best art in getting the brothers of Rinaldo out of prison. He succeeded; and all three got safely to Montalban, where Rinaldo’s joy at the rescue of his brothers and the recovery of Bayard was more than tongue can tell.\n\n# DEATH OF RINALDO\n\n————\nThe distress in Rinaldo’s castle for want of food grew more severe every day, under the pressure of the siege. The garrison were forced to kill their horses, both to save the provision they would consume, and to make food of their flesh. At last all the horses were killed except Bayard, and Rinaldo said to his brothers, “Bayard must die, for we have nothing else to eat.” So they went to the stable and brought out Bayard to kill him. But Alardo said, “Brother, let Bayard live a little longer; who knows what God may do for us?”\n\nBayard heard these words, and understood them as if he was a man, and fell on his knees, as if he would beg for mercy. When Rinaldo saw the distress of his horse his heart failed him, and he let him live.\n\nJust at this time Aya, Rinaldo’s mother, who was the sister of the Emperor, came to the camp, attended by knights and ladies, to intercede for her sons. She fell on her knees before the king, and besought him that he would pardon Rinaldo and his brothers: and all the peers and knights took her side, and entreated the king to grant her prayer. Then said the king, “Dear sister, you act the part of a good mother, and I respect your tender heart, and yield to your entreaties. I will spare your sons their lives if they submit implicitly to my will.”\n\nWhen Charlot heard this he approached the king and whispered in his ear. And the king turned to his sister and said, “Charlot must have Bayard, because I have given the horse to him. Now go, my sister, and tell Rinaldo what I have said.”\n\nWhen the Lady Aya heard these words she was delighted, thanked God in her heart, and said, “Worthy king and brother, I will do as you bid me.” So she went into the castle, where her sons received her most joyfully and affectionately, and she told them the king’s offer. Then Alardo said, “Brother, I would rather have the king’s enmity than give Bayard to Charlot, for I believe he will kill him.” Likewise said all the brothers. When Rinaldo heard them he said, “Dear brothers, if we may win our forgiveness by giving up the horse, so be it. Let us make our peace, for we cannot stand against the king’s power.” Then he went to his mother, and told her they would give the horse to Charlot, and more, too, if the king would pardon them, and forgive all that they had done against his crown and dignity. The lady returned to Charles and told him the answer of her sons.\n\nWhen the peace was thus made between the king and the sons of Aymon, the brothers came forth from the castle, bringing Bayard with them, and, falling at the king’s feet, begged his forgiveness. The king bade them rise, and received them into favor in the sight of all his noble knights and counsellors, to the great joy of all especially of the Lady Aya, their mother. Then Rinaldo took the horse Bayard, gave him to Charlot, and said, “My lord and prince, this horse I give to you; do with him as to you seems good.” Charlot took him, as had been agreed on. Then he made the servants take him to the bridge, and throw him into the water. Bayard sank to the bottom, but soon came to the surface again and swam, saw Rinaldo looking at him, came to land, ran to his old master, and stood by him as proudly as if he had understanding, and would say, “Why did you treat me so?” When the prince saw that he said, “Rinaldo, give me the horse again, for he must die.” Rinaldo replied, “My lord and prince, he is yours without dispute,” and gave him to him. The prince then had a millstone tied to each foot, and two to his neck, and made them throw him again into the water. Bayard struggled in the water, looked up to his master, threw off the stones, and came back to Rinaldo.\nWhen Alardo saw that, he said, “Now must thou be disgraced forever, brother, if thou give up the horse again.” But Rinaldo answered, “Brother, be still. Shall I for the horse’s life provoke the anger of the king again?” Then Alardo said, “Ah, Bayard! what a return do we make for all thy true love and service!” Rinaldo gave the horse to the prince again, and said, “My lord, if the horse comes out again I cannot return him to you any more, for it wrings my heart too much.” Then Charlot had Bayard loaded with the stones as before, and thrown into the water; and commanded Rinaldo that he should not stand where the horse would see him. When Bayard rose to the surface he stretched his neck out of the water and looked round for his master, but saw him not. Then he sunk to the bottom.\n\nRinaldo was so distressed for the loss of Bayard that he made a vow to ride no horse again all his life long, nor to bind a sword to his side, but to become a hermit. He resolved to betake himself to some wild wood, but first to return to his castle, to see his children, and to appoint to each his share of his estate.\n\nSo he took leave of the king and of his brothers, and returned to Montalban, and his brothers remained with the king. Rinaldo called his children to him, and he made his eldest born, Aymeric, a knight, and made him lord of his castle and of his land. He gave to the rest what other goods he had, and kissed and embraced them all, commended them to God, and then departed from them with a heavy heart.\n\nHe had not travelled far when he entered a wood, and there met with a hermit, who had long been retired from the world. Rinaldo greeted him, and the hermit replied courteously, and asked him who he was and what was his purpose. Rinaldo replied, “Sir, I have led a sinful life; many deeds of violence have I done, and many men have I slain, not always in a good cause, but often under the impulse of my own headstrong passions. I have also been the cause of the death of many of my friends, who took my part, not because they thought me in the right, but only for love of me. And now I come to make confession of all my sins, and to do penance for the rest of my life, if perhaps the mercy of God will forgive me.” The hermit said, “Friend, I perceive you have fallen into great sins, and have broken the commandments of God, but his mercy is greater than your sins; and if you repent from your heart, and lead a new life, there is yet hope for you that he will forgive you what is past.” So Rinaldo was comforted, and said, “Master, I will stay with you, and what you bid me I will do.” The hermit replied, “Roots and vegetables will be your food; shirt or shoes you may not wear; your lot must be poverty and want if you stay with me.” Rinaldo replied, “I will cheerfully bear all this, and more.” So he remained three whole years with the hermit, and after that his strength failed, and it seemed as if he was like to die.\n\nOne night the hermit had a dream, and heard a voice from heaven, which commanded him to say to his companion that he must without delay go to the Holy Land, and fight against the heathen. The hermit, when he heard that voice, was glad, and calling Rinaldo, he said, “Friend, God’s angel has commanded me to say to you that you must without delay go to Jerusalem, and help our fellow-Christians in their struggle with the Infidels.” Then said Rinaldo, “Ah! master, how can I do that? It is over three years since I made a vow no more to ride a horse, nor take a sword or spear in my hand.” The hermit answered, “Dear friend, obey God, and do what the angel commanded.” “I will do so,” said Rinaldo, “and pray for me, my master, that God may guide me right.” Then he departed, and went to the seaside, and took ship and came to Tripoli in Syria.\n\nAnd as he went on his way his strength returned to him, till it was equal to what it was in his best days. And though he never mounted a horse, nor took a sword in his hand, yet with his pilgrim’s staff he did good service in the armies of the Christians; and it pleased God that he escaped unhurt, though he was present in many battles, and his courage inspired the men with the same. At last a truce was made with the Saracens, and Rinaldo, now old and infirm, wishing to see his native land again before he died, took ship and sailed for France. When he arrived he shunned to go to the resorts of the great, and preferred to live among the humble folk, where he was unknown. He did country work, and lived on milk and bread, drank water, and was therewith content. While he so lived he heard that the city of Cologne was the holiest and best of cities, on account of the relics and bodies of saints who had there poured out their blood for the faith. This induced him to betake himself thither. When the pious hero arrived at Cologne he went to the monastery of St. Peter, and lived a holy life, occupied night and day in devotion. It so happened that at that time in the next town to Cologne there raged a dreadful pestilence. Many people came to Rinaldo, to beg him to pray for them, that the plague might be stayed. The holy man prayed fervently, and besought the Lord to take away the plague from the people, and his prayer was heard. The stroke of the pestilence was arrested, and all the people thanked the holy man and praised God.\n\nNow there was at this time at Cologne a bishop, called Agilolphus, who was a wise and understanding man, who led a pure and secluded life, and set a good example to others. This bishop undertook to build the Church of St. Peter, and gave notice to all stonemasons and other workmen round about to come to Cologne, where they should find work and wages. Among others came Rinaldo; and he worked among the laborers and did more than four or five common workmen. When they went to dinner he brought stone and mortar so that they had enough for the whole day. When the others went to bed he stretched himself out on the stones. He ate bread only, and drank nothing but water; and had for his wages but a penny a day. The head workman asked him his name, and where he belonged. He would not tell, but said nothing and pursued his work. They called him St. Peter’s workman, because he was so devoted to his work.\n\nWhen the overseer saw the diligence of this holy man he chid the laziness of the other workmen, and said, “You receive more pay than this good man, but do not do half as much work.” For this reason the other workmen hated Rinaldo, and made a secret agreement to kill him. They knew that he made it a practice to go every night to a certain church to pray and give alms. So they agreed to lay wait for him, with the purpose to kill him. When he came to the spot, they seized him, and beat him over the head till he was dead. Then they put his body into a sack, and stones with it, and cast it into the Rhine, in the hope the sack would sink to the bottom, and be there concealed. But God willed not that it should be so, but caused the sack to float on the surface, and be thrown upon the bank. And the soul of the holy martyr was carried by angels, with songs of praise, up to the heavens.\n\nNow at that time the people of Dortmund had become converted to the Christian faith; and they sent to the Bishop of Cologne, and desired him to give them some of the holy relics that are in such abundance in that city. So the Bishop called together his clergy to deliberate what answer they should give to this request. And it was determined to give to the people of Dortmund the body of the holy man who had just suffered martyrdom.\n\nWhen now the body with the coffin was put on the cart, the cart began to move toward Dortmund without horses or help of men, and stopped not till it reached the place where the church of St. Rinaldo now stands. The Bishop and his clergy followed the holy man to do him honor, with singing of hymns, for a space of three miles. And St. Rinaldo has ever since been the patron of that place, and many wonderful works has God done through him, as may be seen in the legends.\n————\n\n# HUON OF BORDEAUX\n\nWhen Charlemagne grew old he felt the burden of government become heavier year by year, till at last he called together his high barons and peers to propose to abdicate the empire and the throne of France in favor of his sons, Charlot and Lewis.\n\nThe Emperor was unreasonably partial to his eldest son; he would have been glad to have had the barons and peers demand Charlot for their only sovereign; but that prince was so infamous, for his falsehood and cruelty, that the council strenuously opposed the Emperor’s proposal of abdicating, and implored him to continue to hold a sceptre which he wielded with so much glory.\n\nAmaury of Hauteville, cousin of Ganelon, and now head of the wicked branch of the house of Maganza, was the secret partisan of Charlot, whom he resembled in his loose morals and bad dispositions. Amaury nourished the most bitter resentment against the house of Guienne, of which the former Duke, Sevinus, had often rebuked his misdeeds. He took advantage of this occasion to do an injury to the two young children whom the Duke Sevinus had left under the charge of the Duchess Alice, their mother; and at the same time, to advance his interest with Charlot by increasing his wealth and power. With this view he suggested to the prince a new idea.\n\nHe pretended to agree with the opinion of the barons; he said that it would be best to try Charlot’s capacity for government by giving him some rich provinces before placing him upon the throne; and that the Emperor, without depriving himself of any part of his realm, might give Charlot the investiture of Guienne. For although seven years had passed since the death of Sevinus, the young Duke, his son, had not yet repaired to the court of Charlemagne to render the homage due to his lawful sovereign.\n\nWe have often had occasion to admire the justice and wisdom of the advice which on all occasions the Duke Namo of Bavaria gave to Charlemagne, and he now discountenanced, with indignation, the selfish advice of Amaury. He represented to the Emperor the early age of the children of Sevinus, and the useful and glorious services of their late father, and proposed to Charlemagne to send two knights to the Duchess at Bordeaux, to summon her two sons to the court of the Emperor, to pay their respects and render homage.\n\nCharlemagne approved this advice, and sent two chevaliers to demand the two young princes of their mother. No sooner had the Duchess learned the approach of the two knights, than she sent distinguished persons to receive them; and as soon as they entered the palace she presented herself before them, with her elder and younger sons, Huon and Girard.\nThe deputies, delighted with the honors and caresses they received, accompanied with rich presents, left Bordeaux with regret and on their return represented to Charlemagne that the young Duke Huon seemed born to tread in the footsteps of his brave father, informing him that in three months the young princes of Guienne would present themselves at his court.\n\nThe Duchess employed the short interval in giving her sons her last instructions. Huon received them in his heart, and Girard gave as much heed to them as could be expected from one so young.\n\nThe preparations for their departure having been made, the Duchess embraced them tenderly, commending them to the care of Heaven, and charged them to call, on their way, at the celebrated monastery of Cluny, to visit the Abbot, the brother of their father. This Abbot, worthy of his high dignity, had never lost an opportunity of doing good, setting an example of every excellence, and making virtue attractive by his example.\n\nHe received his nephews with the greatest magnificence; and, aware how useful his presence might be to them with Charlemagne, whose valued counsellor he was, he took with them the road to Paris.\n\nWhen Amaury learned what reception the two deputies of Charlemagne had received at Bordeaux, and the arrangements made for the visit of the young princes to the Emperor’s court, he suggested to Charlot to give him a troop of his guards, with which he proposed to lay wait for the young men in the wood of Montlery, put them to death, and thereby give the prince Charlot possession of the duchy of Guienne.\n\nA plan of treachery and violence agreed but too well with Charlot’s disposition. He not only adopted the suggestion of Amaury, but insisted upon taking a part in it. They went out secretly, by night, followed by a great number of attendants, all armed in black, to lie in ambuscade in the wood where the brothers were to pass.\n\nGirard, the younger of the two, having amused himself as he rode by flying his hawk at such game as presented itself, had ridden in advance of his brother and the Abbot of Cluny. Charlot, who saw him coming, alone and unarmed, went forth to meet him, sought a quarrel with him, and threw him from his horse with a stroke of his lance. Girard uttered a cry as he fell; Huon heard it, and flew to his defence, with no other weapon than his sword. He came up with him, and saw the blood flowing from his wound. “What has this child done to you, wretch!” he exclaimed to Charlot. “How cowardly to attack him when unprepared to defend himself!” “By my faith,” said Charlot, “I mean to do the same by you. Know that I am the son of Duke Thierry of Ardennes, from whom your father, Sevinus, took three castles; I have sworn to avenge him, and I defy you.” “Coward,” answered Huon, “I know well the baseness that dwells in your race; worthy son of Thierry, use the advantage that your armor gives you; but know that I fear you not.” At these words Charlot had the wickedness to put his lance in rest, and to run upon Huon, who had barely time to wrap his arm in his mantle. With this feeble buckler he received the thrust of the lance. It penetrated the mantle, but missed his body. Then, rising upon his stirrups, Sir Huon struck Charlot so terrible a blow with his sword that the helmet was cleft asunder, and his head too. The dastardly prince fell dead upon the ground.\n\nHuon now perceived that the wood was full of armed men. He called the men of his suite, and they hastily put themselves in order, but nobody issued from the wood to attack him. Amaury, who saw Charlot’s fall, had no desire to compromit himself; and, feeling sure that Charlemagne would avenge the death of his son, he saw no occasion for his doing anything more at present. He left Huon and the Abbot of Cluny to bind up the wound of Girard, and, having seen them depart and resume their way to Paris, he took up the body of Charlot, and, placing it across a horse, had it carried to Paris, where he arrived four hours after Huon.\n\nThe Abbot of Cluny presented his nephew to Charlemagne, but Huon refrained from paying his obeisance, complaining grievously of the ambush which had been set for him, which he said could not have been without the Emperor’s permission. Charlemagne, surprised at a charge which his magnanimous soul was incapable of meriting, asked eagerly of the Abbot what were the grounds of the complaints of his nephew. The Abbot told him faithfully all that had happened, informing him that a coward knight, who called himself the son of Thierry of Ardennes, had wounded Girard, and run upon Huon, who was unarmed; but by his force and valor he had overcome the traitor, and left him dead upon the plain.\n\nCharlemagne indignantly disavowed any connection with the action of the infamous Thierry, congratulated the young Duke upon his victory, himself conducted the two brothers to a rich apartment, stayed to see the first dressing applied to the wound of Girard, and left the brothers in charge of Duke Namo of Bavaria, who, having been a companion in arms of the Duke Sevinus, regarded the young men almost as if they were his own sons.\n\nCharlemagne had hardly quitted them when, returning to his chamber, he heard cries, and saw through the window a party of armed men just arrived. He recognized Amaury, who bore a dead knight stretched across a horse; and the name of Charlot was heard among the exclamations of the people assembled in the courtyard.\n\nCharles’s partiality for this unworthy son was one of his weaknesses. He descended in trepidation to the courtyard, ran to Amaury, and uttered a cry of grief on recognizing Charlot. “It is Huon of Bordeaux,” said the traitor Amaury, “who has massacred your son before it was in my power to defend him.” Charlemagne, furious at these words, seized a sword, and flew to the apartment of the two brothers to plunge it into the heart of the murderer of his son. Duke Namo stopped his hand for an instant, while Charles told him the crime of which Huon was accused. “He is a peer of the realm,” said Namo, “and if he is guilty, is he not here in your power, and are not we peers the proper judges to condemn him to death? Let not your hand be stained with his blood.” The Emperor, calmed by the wisdom of Duke Namo, summoned Amaury to his presence. The peers assembled to hear his testimony, and the traitor accused Huon of Bordeaux of having struck the fatal blow without allowing Charlot an opportunity to defend himself, and though he knew that his opponent was the Emperor’s eldest son.\n\nThe Abbot of Cluny, indignant at the false accusation of Amaury, advanced, and said, “By Saint Benedict, sire, the traitor lies in his throat. If my nephew has slain Charlot it was in his own defence, and after having seen his brother wounded by him, and also in ignorance that his adversary was the prince. Though I am a son of the Church,” added the good Abbot, “I forget not that I am a knight by birth. I offer to prove with my body the lie upon Amaury, if he dares sustain it, and I shall feel that I am doing a better work to punish a disloyal traitor, than to sing lauds and matins.”\n\nHuon to this time had kept silent, amazed at the black calumny of Amaury; but now he stepped forth, and, addressing Amaury, said: “Traitor! darest thou maintain in arms the lie thou hast uttered?” Amaury, a knight of great prowess, despising the youth and slight figure of Huon, hesitated not to offer his glove, which Huon seized; then, turning again to the peers, he said: “I pray you let the combat be allowed me, for never was there a more legitimate cause.” The Duke Namo and the rest, deciding that the question should be remitted to the judgment of Heaven, the combat was ordained, to which Charlemagne unwillingly consented. The young Duke was restored to the charge of Duke Namo, who the next morning invested him with the honors of knighthood, and gave him armor of proof, with a white shield. The Abbot of Cluny, delighted to find in his nephew sentiments worthy of his birth, embraced him, gave him his blessing, and hastened to the church of St. Germains to pray for him, while the officers of the king prepared the lists for the combat.\n\nThe battle was long and obstinate. The address and agility of Huon enabled him to avoid the terrible blows which the ferocious Amaury aimed at him. But Huon had more than once drawn blood from his antagonist. The effect began to be perceived in the failing strength of the traitor; at last he threw himself from his horse, and kneeling, begged for mercy. “Spare me,” he said, “and I will confess all. Aid me to rise, and lead me to Charlemagne.” The brave and loyal Huon, at these words, put his sword under his left arm, and stretched out his right to raise the prostrate man, who seized the opportunity to give him a thrust in the side. The hauberk of Huon resisted the blow, and he was wounded but slightly. Transported with rage at this act of baseness, he forgot how necessary for his complete acquittal the confession of Amaury was, and without delay dealt him the fatal blow.\n\nDuke Namo and the other peers approached, had the body of Amaury dragged forth from the lists, and conducted Huon to Charlemagne. The Emperor, however, listening to nothing but his resentment and grief for the death of his son, refused to be satisfied; and under the plea that Huon had not succeeded in making his accuser retract his charge seemed resolved to confiscate his estates and to banish him forever from France. It was not till after long entreaties on the part of Duke Namo and the rest that he consented to grant Huon his pardon, under conditions which he should impose.\n\nHuon approached, and knelt before the Emperor, rendered his homage, and cried him mercy for the involuntary killing of his son. Charlemagne would not receive the hands of Huon in his own, but touched him with his sceptre, saying, “I receive thy homage, and pardon thee the death of my son, but only on one condition. You shall go immediately to the court of the Sultan Gaudisso; you shall present yourself before him as he sits at meat; you shall cut off the head of the most illustrious guest whom you shall find sitting nearest to him; you shall kiss three times on the mouth the fair princess, his daughter, and you shall demand of the Sultan, as token of tribute to me, a handful of the white hair of his beard, and four grinders from his mouth.”\n\nThese conditions caused a murmur from all the assembly. “What!” said the Abbot of Cluny; “slaughter a Saracen prince without first offering him baptism?” “The second condition is not so hard,” said the young peers, “but the demand that Huon is bound to make of the old Sultan is very uncivil, and will be hard to obtain.”\n\nThe Emperor’s obstinacy when he had once resolved upon a thing is well known. To the courage of Huon nothing seemed impossible. “I accept the conditions,” said he, silencing the intercessions of the old Duke of Bavaria; “my liege, I accept my pardon at this price. I go to execute your commands, as your vassal and a peer of France.”\n\nThe Duke Namo and Abbot of Cluny, being unable to obtain any relaxation of the sentence passed by Charlemagne, led forth the young Duke, who determined to set out at once on his expedition. All that the good Abbot could obtain of him was, that he should prepare for this perilous undertaking by going first to Rome, to pay his homage to the Pope, who was the brother of the Duchess Alice, Huon’s mother, and from him demand absolution and his blessing. Huon promised it, and forthwith set out on his way to Rome.\n————\n\nHUON OF BORDEAUX (Continued)\n\nHuon, having traversed the Apennines and Italy, arrived at the environs of Rome, where, laying aside his armor, he assumed the dress of a pilgrim. In this attire he presented himself before the Pope, and not till after he had made a full confession of his sins did he announce himself as his nephew. “Ah! my dear nephew,” exclaimed the Holy Father, “what harder penance could I impose than the Emperor has already done? Go in peace, my son,” he added, absolving him, “I go to intercede for you with the Most High.” Then he led his nephew into his palace, and introduced him to all the Cardinals and Princes of Rome as the Duke of Guienne, son of the Duchess Alice, his sister.\n\nHuon, at setting out, had made a vow not to stop more than three days in a place. The Holy Father took advantage of this time to inspire him with zeal for the glory of Christianity, and with confidence in the protection of the Most High. He advised him to embark for Palestine, to visit the Holy Sepulchre, and to depart thence for the interior of Asia.\n\nLoaded with the blessings of the Holy Father, Huon, obeying his counsels, embarked for Palestine, arrived, and visited with the greatest reverence the holy places. He then departed, and took his way toward the east.\n\nBut, ignorant of the country and of the language, he lost himself in a forest, and remained three days without seeing a human creature, living on honey and wild fruits which he found on the trees. The third day, seeking a passage through a rocky defile, he beheld a man in tattered clothing, whose beard and hair covered his breast and shoulders. This man stopped on seeing him, observed him, and recognized the arms and bearing of a French knight. He immediately approached, and exclaimed, in the language of the South of France, “God be praised! Do I indeed behold a chevalier of my own country, after fifteen years passed in this desert without seeing the face of a fellow-countryman?”\n\nHuon, to gratify him still more, unlaced his helmet, and came towards him with a smiling countenance. The other regarded him with more surprise than at first. “Good Heaven!” he exclaimed, “was there ever such a resemblance? Ah, noble sir,” he added, “tell me, I beseech you, of what country and race you come?” “I require,” replied Huon, “before telling you mine, that you first reveal your own; let it suffice you at present to know that I am a Christian, and that in Guienne I was born.” “Ah! Heaven grant that my eyes and my heart do not deceive me,” exclaimed the unknown; “my name is Sherasmin; I am brother to Guire, the Mayor of Bordeaux. I was taken prisoner in the battle where my dear and illustrious master, Sevinus, lost his life. For three years I endured\n\nthe miseries of slavery; at length I broke my chains and escaped to this desert, where I have sustained myself in solitude ever since. Your features recall to me my beloved sovereign, in whose service I was from my infancy till his death.” Huon made no reply but by embracing the old man, with tears in his eyes. Then Sherasmin learned that his arms enfolded the son of the Duke Sevinus. He led him to his cabin, and spread before him the dry fruits and honey which formed his only aliment.\n\nHuon recounted his adventures to Sherasmin, who was moved to tears at the recital. He then consulted him on means of conducting his enterprise. Sherasmin hesitated not to confess that success seemed impossible; nevertheless he swore a solemn oath never to abandon him. The Saracen language, which he was master of, would be serviceable to them when they should leave the desert, and mingle with men.\n\nThey took the route of the Red Sea, and entered Arabia. Their way lay through a region which Sherasmin described as full of terrors. It was inhabited by Oberon, King of the Fairies, who made captive such knights as were rash enough to penetrate into it, and transformed them into Hobgoblins. It was possible to avoid this district at the expense of somewhat lengthening their route; but no dangers could deter Huon of Bordeaux; and the brave Sherasmin, who had now resumed the armor of a knight, reluctantly consented to share with him the dangers of the shorter route.\n\nThey entered a wood, and arrived at a spot whence alleys branched off in various directions. One of them seemed to be terminated by a superb palace, whose gilded roofs were adorned with brilliant weathercocks covered with diamonds. A superb chariot issued from the gate of the palace, and drove toward Huon and his companion, as if to meet them half-way. The prince saw no one in the chariot but a child apparently about five years old, very beautiful, and clad in a robe which glittered with precious stones. At the sight of him, Sherasmin’s terror was extreme. He seized the reins of Huon’s horse, and turned him about, hurrying the prince away, and assuring him that they were lost if they stopped to parley with the mischievous dwarf, who, though he appeared a child, was full of years and of treachery. Huon was sorry to lose sight of the beautiful dwarf, whose aspect had nothing in it to alarm; yet he followed his friend, who urged on his horse with all possible speed. Presently a storm began to roar through the forest, the daylight grew dim, and they found their way with difficulty. From time to time they seemed to hear an infantine voice, which said, “Stop, Duke Huon; listen to me: it is in vain you fly me!”\n\nSherasmin only fled the faster, and stopped not until he had reached the gate of a monastery of monks and nuns, the two communities of which were assembled at that time in a religious procession. Sherasmin, feeling safe from the malice of the dwarf in the presence of so many holy persons and the sacred banners, stopped to ask an asylum, and made Huon dismount also. But at that moment they were joined by the dwarf, who blew a blast upon an ivory horn which hung from his neck. Immediately the good Sherasmin, in spite of himself, began to dance like a young collegian, and seizing the hand of an aged nun, who felt as if it would be her death, they footed it briskly over the grass, and were imitated by all the other monks and nuns, mingled together, forming the strangest dancing-party ever beheld. Huon alone felt no disposition to dance; but he came near dying of laughter at seeing the ridiculous postures and leaps of the others.\n\nThe dwarf, approaching Huon, said, in a sweet voice, and in Huon’s own language, “Duke of Guienne, why do you shun me? I conjure you, in Heaven’s name, speak to me.” Huon, hearing himself addressed in this serious manner, and knowing that no evil spirit would dare to use the holy name in aid of his schemes, replied, “Sir, whoever you are, I am ready to hear and answer you.” “Huon, my friend,” continued the dwarf, “I always loved your race, and you have been dear to me ever since your birth. The gracious state of conscience in which you were when you entered my wood has protected you from all enchantments, even if I had intended to practise any upon you. If these monks, these nuns, and even your friend Sherasmin, had had a conscience as pure as yours, my horn would not have set them dancing; but where is the monk or the nun who can always be deaf to the voice of the tempter, and Sherasmin in the desert has often doubted the power of Providence.”\n\nAt these words Huon saw the dancers overcome with exertion. He begged mercy for them, the dwarf granted it, and the effect of the horn ceased at once; the nuns got rid of their partners, smoothed their dresses, and hastened to resume their places in the procession. Sherasmin, overcome with heat, panting, and unable to stand on his legs, threw himself upon the grass, and began, “Did not I tell you”—He was going on in an angry tone, but the dwarf, approaching, said, “Sherasmin, why have you murmured against Providence? Why have you thought evil of me? You deserved this light punishment; but I know you to be good and loyal; I mean to show myself your friend, as you shall soon see.” At these words he presented him a rich goblet. “Make the sign of the cross on this cup,” said he, “and then believe that I hold my power from the God you adore, whose faithful servant I am, as well as you.” Sherasmin obeyed, and on the instant the cup was filled with delicious wine, a draught of which restored vigor to his limbs, and made him feel young again. Overcome with gratitude, he threw himself on his knees, but the dwarf raised him, and bade him sit beside him, and thus commenced his history:\n\n“Julius Cæsar, going by sea to join his army, was driven by a storm to take shelter in the island of Celea, where dwelt the fairy Glorianda. From this renowned pair I draw my birth. I am the inheritor of that which was most admirable in each of my parents: my father’s heroic qualities, and my mother’s beauty and magic art. But a malicious sister of my mother’s, in revenge for some slight offence, touched me with her wand when I was only five years old, and forbade me to grow any bigger; and my mother, with all her power, was unable to annul the sentence. I have thus continued infantile in appearance, though full of years and experience. The power which I derive from my mother I use sometimes for my own diversion, but always to promote justice and to reward virtue. I am able and willing to assist you, Duke of Guienne, for I know the errand on which you come hither. I presage for you, if you follow my counsels, complete success; and the beautiful Clarimunda for a wife.”\n\nWhen he had thus spoken he presented to Huon the precious and useful cup, which had the faculty of filling itself when a good man took it in his hand. He gave him also his beautiful horn of ivory, saying to him, “Huon, when you sound this gently, you will make the hearers dance, as you have seen; but if you sound it forcibly, fear not that I shall hear it, though at a hundred leagues’ distance, and will fly to your relief; but be careful not to sound it in that way, unless upon the most urgent occasion.”\n\nOberon directed Huon what course he should take to reach the country of the Sultan Gaudisso. “You will encounter great perils,” said he, “before arriving there, and I fear me,” he added, with tears in his eyes, “that you will not in everything obey my directions, and in that case you will suffer much calamity.” Then he embraced Huon and Sherasmin, and left them.\n\nHuon and his follower travelled many days through the desert before they reached any inhabited place, and all this while the wonderful cup sustained them, furnishing them not only wine, but food also. At last they came to a great city. As day was declining, they entered its suburbs, and Sherasmin, who spoke the Saracen language perfectly, inquired for an inn where they could pass the night. A person who appeared to be one of the principal inhabitants, seeing two strangers of respectable appearance making this inquiry, stepped forward and begged them to accept the shelter of his mansion. They entered, and their host did the honors of his abode with a politeness which they were astonished to see in a Saracen. He had them served with coffee and sherbet, and all was conducted with great decorum, till one of the servants awkwardly overturned a cup of hot coffee on the host’s legs, when he started up, exclaiming in very good Gascon, “Blood and thunder! you blockhead, you deserve to be thrown over the mosque!”\n\nHuon could not help laughing to see the vivacity and the language of his country thus break out unawares. The host, who had no idea that his guests understood his words, was astonished when Huon addressed him in the dialect of his country. Immediately confidence was established between them; especially when the domestics had retired. The host, seeing that he was discovered, and that the two pretended Saracens were from the borders of the Garonne, embraced them, and disclosed that he was a Christian. Huon, who had learned prudence from the advice of Oberon, to test his host’s sincerity, drew from his robe the cup which the Fairy-king had given him, and presented it empty to the host. “A fair cup,” said he, “but I should like it better if it was full.” Immediately it was so. The host, astonished, dared not put it to his lips. “Drink boldly, my dear fellow-countryman,” said Huon; “your truth is proved by this cup, which only fills itself in the hands of an honest man.” The host did not hesitate longer; the cup passed freely from hand to hand; their mutual cordiality increased as it passed, and each recounted his adventures. Those of Huon redoubled his host’s respect; for he recognized in him his legitimate sovereign: while the host’s narrative was in these words:\n\n“My name is Floriac; this great and strong city, you will hear with surprise and grief, is governed by a brother of Duke Sevinus, and your uncle. You have no doubt heard that a young brother of the Duke of Guienne was stolen away from the sea-shore, with his companions, by some corsairs. I was then his page, and we were carried by those corsairs to Barbary, where we were sold for slaves. The Barbary prince sent us as part of the tribute which he yearly paid to his sovereign, the Sultan Gaudisso. Your uncle, who had been somewhat puffed up by the flattery of his attendants, thought to increase his importance with his new master by telling him his rank. The Sultan, who, like a true Mussulman, detested all Christian princes, exerted himself from that moment to bring him over to the Saracen faith. He succeeded but too well. Your uncle, seduced by the arts of the Santons, and by the pleasures and indulgences which the Sultan allowed him, committed the horrid crime of apostasy; he renounced his baptism, and embraced Mahometanism. Gaudisso then loaded him with honors, made him espouse one of his nieces, and sent him to reign over this city and adjoining country. Your uncle preserved for me the same friendship which he had had when a boy; but all his caresses and efforts could not make me renounce my faith. Perhaps he respected me in his heart for my resistance to his persuasions, perhaps he had hopes of inducing me in time to imitate him. He made me accompany him to this city, of which he was master, he gave me his confidence, and permits me to keep in my service some Christians, whom I protect for the sake of their faith.”\n\n“Ah!” exclaimed Huon, “take me to this guilty uncle. A prince of the house of Guienne, must he not blush at the cowardly abandonment of the faith of his fathers?”\n\n“Alas!” replied Floriac, “I fear he will neither be sensible of shame at your reproaches, nor of pleasure at the sight of a nephew so worthy of his lineage. Brutified by sensuality, jealous of his power, which he often exercises with cruelty, he will more probably restrain you by force or put you to death.”\n\n“Be it so,” said the brave and fervent Huon, “I could not die in a better cause; and I demand of you to conduct me to him to-morrow, after having told him of my arrival and my birth.” Floriac still objected, but Huon would take no denial, and he promised obedience.\n\nNext morning Floriac waited upon the Governor and told him of the arrival of his nephew, Huon of Bordeaux; and of the intention of the prince to present himself at his court that very day. The Governor, surprised, did not immediately answer; though he at once made up his mind what to do. He knew that Floriac loved Christians and the princes of his native land too well to aid in any treason to one of them; he therefore feigned great pleasure at hearing of the arrival of the eldest born of his family at his court. He immediately sent Floriac to find him; he caused his palace to be put in festal array, his divan to be assembled, and after giving some secret orders, went himself to meet his nephew, whom he introduced under his proper name and title to all the great officers of his court.\n\nHuon burned with indignation at seeing his uncle with forehead encircled with a rich turban, surmounted with a crescent of precious stones. His natural candor made him receive with pain the embraces which the treacherous Governor lavished upon him. Meanwhile the hope of finding a suitable moment to reproach him for his apostasy made him submit to those honors which his uncle caused to be rendered to him. The Governor evaded with address the chance of being alone with Huon and spent all the morning in taking him through his gardens and palace. At last, when the hour of dinner approached, and the Governor took him by the hand to lead him into the dining-hall, Huon seized the opportunity and said to him in a low voice, “O my uncle! O Prince, brother of the Duke Sevinus! in what condition have I the grief and shame of seeing you!” The Governor pretended to be moved, pressed his hand, and whispered in his ear, “Silence! my dear nephew; to-morrow morning I will hear you fully.”\nHuon, comforted a little by these words, took his seat at the table by the side of the Governor. The Mufti, some Cadis, Agas, and Santons, filled the other places. Sherasmin sat down with them; but Floriac, who would not lose sight of his guests, remained standing, and passed in and out to observe what was going on within the palace. He soon perceived a number of armed men gliding through the passages and antechambers connected with the dining-hall. He was about to enter to give his guests notice of what he had seen when he heard a violent noise and commotion in the hall. The cause was this.\n\nHuon and Sherasmin were well enough suited with the first course and ate with good appetite; but the people of their country not being accustomed to drink only water at their meals, Huon and Sherasmin looked at one another, not very well pleased at such a regimen. Huon laughed outright at the impatience of Sherasmin, but soon, experiencing the same want himself, he drew forth Oberon’s cup and made the sign of the cross. The cup filled and he drank it off, and handed it to Sherasmin, who followed his example. The Governor and his officers, seeing this abhorred sign, contracted their brows and sat in silent consternation. Huon pretended not to observe it, and having filled the cup again handed it to his uncle, saying, “Pray, join us, dear uncle; it is excellent Bordeaux wine, the drink that will be to you like mother’s milk.” The Governor, who often drank in secret with his own favorite Sultanas the wines of Greece and Shiraz, never in public drank anything but water. He had not for a long time tasted the excellent wines of his native land; he was sorely tempted to drink what was now handed to him, it looked so bright in the cup, outshining the gold itself. He stretched forth his hand, took the brimming goblet, and raised it to his lips, when immediately it dried up and disappeared. Huon and Sherasmin, like Gascons as they were, laughed at his astonishment. “Christian dogs!” he exclaimed, “do you dare to insult me at my own table? But I will soon be revenged.” At these words he threw the cup at the head of his nephew, who caught it with his left hand, while with the other he snatched the turban, with its crescent, from the Governor’s head and threw it on the floor. All the Saracens started up from table, with loud outcries, and prepared to avenge the insult. Huon and Sherasmin put themselves on their defence, and met with their swords the scimitars directed against them. At this moment the doors of the hall opened and a crowd of soldiers and armed eunuchs rushed in, who joined in the attack upon Huon and Sherasmin. The Prince and his followers took refuge on a broad shelf or sideboard, where they kept at bay the crowd of assailants, making the most forward of them smart for their audacity. But more troops came pressing in and the brave Huon, inspired by the wine of Bordeaux, and not angry enough to lose his relish for a joke, blew a gentle note on his horn, and no sooner was it heard than it quelled the rage of the combatants and set them to dancing. Huon and Sherasmin, no longer attacked, looked down from their elevated position on a scene the most singular and amusing. Very soon the Sultanas, hearing the sound of the dance and finding their guards withdrawn, came into the hall and mixed with the dancers. The favorite Sultana seized upon a young Santon, who performed jumps two feet high; but soon the long dresses of this couple got intermingled and threw them down. The Santon’s beard was caught in the Sultana’s necklace, and they could not disentangle them. The Governor by no means approved this familiarity, and took two steps forward to get at the Santon, but he stumbled over a prostrate Dervise and measured his length on the floor. The dancing continued till the strength of the performers was exhausted, and they fell, one after the other, and lay helpless. The Governor at length made signs to Huon that he would yield everything if he would but allow him to rest. The bargain was ratified; the Governor allowed Huon and Sherasmin to depart on their way, and even gave them a ring which would procure them safe passage through his country and access to the Sultan Gaudisso. The two friends hastened to avail themselves of this favorable turn, and taking leave of Floriac, pursued their journey.\n————\n\nHUON OF BORDEAUX (Continued)\n\nHuon had seen many beauties at his mother’s court, but his heart had never been touched with love. Honor had been his mistress, and in pursuit of that he had never found time to give a thought to softer cares. Strange that a heart so insensible should first be touched by something so unsubstantial as a dream; but so it was.\n\nThe day after the adventure with his uncle night overtook the travellers as they passed through a forest. A grotto offered them shelter from the night dews. The magic cup supplied their evening meal; for such was its virtue that it afforded not only wine, but more solid fare when desired. Fatigue soon threw them into profound repose. Lulled by the murmur of the foliage, and breathing the fragrance of the flowers, Huon dreamed that a lady more beautiful than he had ever before seen hung over him and imprinted a kiss upon his lips. As he stretched out his arms to embrace her a sudden gust of wind swept her away.\n\nHuon awoke in an agony of regret. A few moments sufficed to afford some consolation in showing him that what had passed was but a dream; but his perplexity and sadness could not escape the notice of Sherasmin. Huon hesitated not to inform his faithful follower of the reason of his pensiveness; and got nothing in return but his rallyings for allowing himself to be disturbed by such a cause. He recommended a draught from the fairy goblet, and Huon tried it with good effect.\n\nAt early dawn they resumed their way. They travelled till high noon, but said little to one another. Huon was musing on his dream, and Sherasmin’s thoughts flew back to his early days on the banks of the flowery Garonne.\n\nOn a sudden they were startled by the cry of distress, and turning an angle of the wood, came where a knight hard pressed was fighting with a furious lion. The knight’s horse lay dead, and it seemed as if another moment would end the combat, for terror and fatigue had quite disabled the knight for further resistance. He fell, and the lion’s paw was raised over him, when a blow from Huon’s sword turned the monster’s rage upon a new enemy. His roar shook the forest, and he crouched in act to spring, when, with the rapidity of lightning, Huon plunged his sword into his side. He rolled over on the plain in the agonies of death.\n\nThey raised the knight from the ground, and Sherasmin hastened to offer him a draught from the fairy cup. The wine sparkled to the brim, and the warrior put forth his lips to quaff it, but it shrunk away, and did not even wet his lips. He dashed the goblet angrily on the ground, with an exclamation of resentment. This incident did not tend to make either party more acceptable to the other; and what followed was worse. For when Huon said, “Sir knight, thank God for your deliverance,”—“Thank Mahomet, rather, yourself,” said he, “for he has led you this day to render service to no less a personage than the Prince of Hyrcania.”\n\nAt the sound of this blasphemy Huon drew his sword and turned upon the miscreant, who, little disposed to encounter the prowess of which he had so lately seen proof, betook himself to flight. He ran to Huon’s horse, and lightly vaulting on his back, clapped spurs to his side, and galloped out of sight.\n\nThe adventure was vexatious, yet there was no remedy. The prince and Sherasmin continued their journey with the aid of the remaining horse as they best might. At length, as evening set in, they descried the pinnacles and towers of a great city full before them, which they knew to be the famous city of Bagdad.\n\nThey were well-nigh exhausted with fatigue when they arrived at its precincts, and in the darkness, not knowing what course to take, were glad to meet an aged woman, who, in reply to their inquiries, offered them such accommodations as her cottage could supply. They thankfully accepted the offer, and entered the low door. The good dame busily prepared the best fare her stores supplied,—milk, figs, and peaches,—deeply regretting that the bleak winds had nipped her almond-trees.\n\nSir Huon thought he had never in his life tasted any fare so good. The old lady talked while her guests ate. She doubted not, she said, they had come to be present at the great feast in honor of the marriage of the Sultan’s daughter, which was to take place on the morrow. They asked who the bridegroom was to be, and the old lady answered, “The Prince of Hyrcania,” but added, “Our princess hates him, and would rather wed a dragon than him.” “How know you that?” asked Huon; and the dame informed him that she had it from the princess herself, who was her foster-child. Huon inquired the reason of the princess’s aversion; and the woman pleased to find her chat excite so much interest, replied that it was all in consequence of a dream. “A dream!” exclaimed Huon. “Yes! a dream. She dreamed that she was a hind, and that the Prince, as a hunter, was pursuing her, and had almost overtaken her, when a beautiful dwarf appeared in view, drawn in a golden car, having by his side a young man of yellow hair and fair complexion, like one from a foreign land. She dreamed that the car stopped where she stood, and that, having resumed her own form, she was about to ascend it, when suddenly it faded from her view, and with it the dwarf and the fair-haired youth. But from her heart that vision did not fade, and from that time her affianced bridegroom, the Hyrcanian prince, had become odious to her sight. Yet the Sultan, her father, by no means regarding such a cause as sufficient to prevent the marriage, had named the morrow as the time when it should be solemnized, in presence of his court and many princes of the neighboring countries, whom the fame of the princess’s beauty and the bridegroom’s splendor had brought to the scene.”\n\nWe may suppose this conversation woke a tumult of thoughts in the breast of Huon. Was it not clear that Providence led him on, and cleared the way for his happy success? Sleep did not early visit the eyes of Huon that night; but, with the sanguine temper of youth, he indulged his fancy in imagining the sequel of his strange experience.\n\nThe next day, which he could not but regard as the decisive day of his fate, he prepared to deliver the message of Charlemagne. Clad in his armor, fortified with his ivory horn and his ring, he reached the palace of Gaudisso when the guests were assembled at the banquet. As he approached the gate a voice called on all true believers to enter; and Huon, the brave and faithful Huon, in his impatience passed in under that false pretention. He had no sooner passed the barrier than he felt ashamed of his baseness, and was overwhelmed with regret. To make amends for his fault he ran forward to the second gate, and cried to the porter, “Dog of a misbeliever, I command you in the name of Him who died on the cross, open to me!” The points of a hundred weapons immediately opposed his passage. Huon then remembered for the first time the ring he had received from his uncle, the Governor. He produced it, and demanded to be led to the Sultan’s presence. The officer of the guard recognized the ring, made a respectful obeisance, and allowed him free entrance. In the same way he passed the other doors to the rich saloon where the great Sultan was at dinner with his tributary princes. At sight of the ring the chief attendant led Huon to the head of the hall, and introduced him to the Sultan and his princes as the ambassador of Charlemagne. A seat was provided for him near the royal party.\n\nThe Prince of Hyrcania, the same whom Huon had rescued from the lion, and who was the destined bridegroom of the beautiful Clarimunda, sat on the Sultan’s right hand, and the princess herself on his left. It chanced that Huon found himself near the seat of the princess, and hardly were the ceremonies of reception over before he made haste to fulfill the commands of Charlemagne by imprinting a kiss upon her rosy lips, and after that a second, not by command, but by good will. The Prince of Hyrcania cried out, “Audacious infidel! take the reward of thy insolence!” and aimed a blow at Huon, which, if it had reached him, would have brought his embassy to a speedy termination. But the ingrate failed of his aim, and Huon punished his blasphemy and ingratitude at once by a blow which severed his head from his body.\n\nSo suddenly had all this happened that no hand had been raised to arrest it; but now Gaudisso cried out, “Seize the murderer!” Huon was hemmed in on all sides, but his redoubtable sword kept the crowd of courtiers at bay. But he saw new combatants enter, and could not hope to maintain his ground against so many. He recollected his horn, and raising it to his lips, blew a blast almost as loud as that of Roland at Roncesvalles. It was in vain. Oberon heard it; but the sin of which Huon had been guilty in bearing, though but for a moment, the character of a believer in the false prophet, had put it out of Oberon’s power to help him. Huon, finding himself deserted, and conscious of the cause, lost his strength and energy, was seized, loaded with chains, and plunged into a dungeon.\nHis life was spared for the time, merely that he might be reserved for a more painful death. The Sultan meant that, after being made to feel all the torments of hunger and despair, he should be flayed alive.\n\nBut an enchanter more ancient and more powerful than Oberon himself interested himself for the brave Huon. The enchanter was Love. The Princess Clarimunda learned with horror the fate to which the young prince was destined. By the aid of her governante she gained over the keeper of the prison, and went herself to lighten the chains of her beloved. It was her hand that removed his fetters, from her he received supplies of food to sustain a life which he devoted from thenceforth wholly to her. After the most tender explanations the princess departed, promising to repeat her visit on the morrow.\n\nThe next day she came according to promise, and again brought supplies of food. These visits were continued during a whole month. Huon was too good a son of the Church to forget that the amiable princess was a Saracen, and he availed himself of these interviews to instruct her in the true faith. How easy it is to believe the truth when uttered by the lips of those we love! Clarimunda ere long professed her entire belief in the Christian doctrines, and desired to be baptized.\n\nMeanwhile the Sultan had repeatedly inquired of the jailer how his prisoner bore the pains of famine, and learned to his surprise that he was not yet much reduced thereby. On his repeating the inquiry, after a short interval, the keeper replied that the prisoner had died suddenly, and had been buried in the cavern. The Sultan could only regret that he had not sooner ordered the execution of the sentence.\n\nWhile these things were going on the faithful Sherasmin, who had not accompanied Huon in his last adventure, but had learned by common rumor the result of it, came to the court in hopes of doing something for the rescue of his master. He presented himself to the Sultan as Solario, his nephew. Guadisso received him with kindness, and all the courtiers loaded him with attentions. He soon found means to inform himself how the Princess regarded the brave but unfortunate Huon, and having made himself known to her, confidence was soon established between them. Clarimunda readily consented to assist in the escape of Huon, and to quit with him her father’s court to repair to that of Charlemagne. Their united efforts had nearly perfected their arrangement, a vessel was secretly prepared, and all things in forwardness for the flight, when an unlooked-for obstacle presented itself. Huon himself positively refused to go leaving the orders of Charlemagne unexecuted.\n\nSherasmin was in despair. Bitterly he complained of the fickleness and cruelty of Oberon in withdrawing his aid at the very crisis when it was most necessary. Earnestly he urged every argument to satisfy the prince that he had done enough for honor, and could not be held bound to achieve impossibilities. But all was of no avail, and he knew not which way to turn, when one of those events occurred which are so frequent under Turkish despotisms. A courier arrived at the court of the Sultan, bearing the ring of his sovereign, the mighty Agrapard, Caliph of Arabia, and bringing the bow-string for the neck of Gaudisso. No reason was assigned; none but the pleasure of the Caliph is ever required in such cases; but it was suspected that the bearer of the bow-string had persuaded the Caliph that Gaudisso, whose rapacity was well known, had accumulated immense treasures, which he had not duly shared with his sovereign, and thus had obtained an order to supersede him in his Emirship.\n\nThe body of Gaudisso would have been cast out a prey to dogs and vultures, had not Sherasmin, under the character of nephew of the deceased, been permitted to receive it, and give it decent burial, which he did, but not till he had taken possession of the beard and grinders, agreeably to the orders of Charlemagne.\n\nNo obstacle now stood in the way of the lovers and their faithful follower in returning to France. They sailed, taking Rome in their way, where the Holy Father himself blessed the union of his nephew, Duke Huon of Bordeaux, with the Princess Clarimunda.\n\nSoon afterward they arrived in France, where Huon laid his trophies at the feet of Charlemagne, and, being restored to the favor of the Emperor, hastened to present himself and his bride to the Duchess, his mother, and to the faithful liegemen of his province of Guienne and his city of Bordeaux, where the pair were received with transports of joy.\n\n# OGIER, THE DANE\n\n————\nOgier, the Dane, was the son of Geoffroy, who wrested Denmark from the Pagans, and reigned the first Christian king of that country. When Ogier was born, and before he was baptized, six ladies of ravishing beauty appeared all at once in the chamber of the infant. They encircled him, and she who appeared the eldest took him in her arms, kissed him, and laid her hand upon his heart. “I give you,” said she, “to be the bravest warrior of your times.” She delivered the infant to her sister, who said, “I give you abundant opportunities to display your valor.” “Sister,” said the third lady, “you have given him a dangerous boon; I give him that he shall never be vanquished.” The fourth sister added, as she laid her hand upon his eyes and his mouth, “I give you the gift of pleasing.” The fifth said, “Lest all these gifts serve only to betray, I give you sensibility to return the love you inspire.” Then spoke Morgana, the youngest and handsomest of the group. “Charming creature, I claim you for my own; and I give you not to die till you shall have come to pay me a visit in my isle of Avalon.” Then she kissed the child and departed with her sisters.\n\nAfter this the king had the child carried to the font and baptized with the name of Ogier.\n\nIn his education nothing was neglected to elevate him to the standard of a perfect knight, and render him accomplished in all the arts necessary to make him a hero.\n\nHe had hardly reached the age of sixteen years when Charlemagne, whose power was established over all the sovereigns of his time, recollected that Geoffroy, Ogier’s father, had omitted to render the homage due to him as Emperor, and sovereign lord of Denmark, one of the grand fiefs of the empire. He accordingly sent an embassy to demand of the king of Denmark this homage, and on receiving a refusal, couched in haughty terms, sent an army to enforce the demand. Geoffroy, after an unsuccessful resistance, was forced to comply, and as a pledge of his sincerity delivered Ogier, his eldest son, a hostage to Charles, to be brought up at his court. He was placed in charge of the Duke Namo of Bavaria, the friend of his father, who treated him like his own son.\n\nOgier grew up more and more handsome and amiable every day. He surpassed in form, strength, and address all the noble youths his companions; he failed not to be present at all tourneys; he was attentive to the elder knights, and burned with impatience to imitate them. Yet his heart rose sometimes in secret against his condition as a hostage, and as one apparently forgotten by his father.\n\nThe King of Denmark, in fact, was at this time occupied with new loves. Ogier’s mother having died, he had married a second wife, and had a son named Guyon. The new queen had absolute power over her husband, and fearing that, if he should see Ogier again, he would give him the preference over Guyon, she had adroitly persuaded him to delay rendering his homage to Charlemagne, till now four years had passed away since the last renewal of that ceremony. Charlemagne, irritated at this delinquency, drew closer the bonds of Ogier’s captivity until he should receive a response from the king of Denmark to a fresh summons which he caused to be sent to him.\n\nThe answer of Geoffroy was insulting and defiant, and the rage of Charlemagne was roused in the highest degree. He was at first disposed to wreak his vengeance upon Ogier, his hostage; but at the entreaties of Duke Namo, who felt towards his pupil like a father, consented to spare his life, if Ogier would swear fidelity to him as his liege-lord, and promise not to quit his court without his permission. Ogier accepted these terms, and was allowed to retain all the freedom he had before enjoyed.\n\nThe Emperor would have immediately taken arms to reduce his disobedient vassal, if he had not been called off in another direction by a message from Pope Leo, imploring his assistance. The Saracens had landed in the neighborhood of Rome, occupied Mount Janiculum, and prepared to pass the Tiber and carry fire and sword to the capital of the Christian world. Charlemagne hesitated not to yield to the entreaties of the Pope. He speedily assembled an army, crossed the Alps, traversed Italy, and arrived at Spoleto, a strong place to which the Pope had retired. Leo, at the head of his Cardinals, advanced to meet him, and rendered him homage, as to the son of Pepin, the illustrious protector of the Holy See, coming, as his father had done, to defend it in the hour of need.\nCharlemagne stopped but two days at Spoleto, and learning that the Infidels, having rendered themselves masters of Rome, were besieging the Capitol, which could not long hold out against them, marched promptly to attack them.\n\nThe advanced posts of the army were commanded by Duke Namo, on whom Ogier waited as his squire. He did not yet bear arms, not having received the order of knighthood. The Oriflamme, the royal standard, was borne by a knight named Alory, who showed himself unworthy of the honor.\nDuke Namo, seeing a strong body of the Infidels advancing to attack him, gave the word to charge them. Ogier remained in the rear, with the other youths, grieving much that he was not permitted to fight. Very soon he saw Alory lower the Oriflamme, and turn his horse in flight. Ogier pointed him out to the young men, and seizing a club, rushed upon Alory and struck him from his horse. Then, with his companions, he disarmed him, clothed himself in his armor, raised the Oriflamme, and mounting the horse of the unworthy knight, flew to the front rank, where he joined Duke Namo, drove back the Infidels, and carried the Oriflamme quite through their broken ranks. The Duke, thinking it was Alory, whom he had not held in high esteem, was astonished at his strength and valor. Ogier’s young companions imitated him, supplying themselves with armor from the bodies of the slain; they followed Ogier and carried death into the ranks of the Saracens, who fell back in confusion upon their main body.\n\nDuke Namo now ordered a retreat, and Ogier obeyed with reluctance, when they perceived Charlemagne advancing to their assistance. The combat now became general, and was more terrible than ever. Charlemagne had overthrown Corsuble, the commander of the Saracens, and had drawn his famous sword, Joyeuse, to cut off his head, when two Saracen knights set upon him at once, one of whom slew his horse, and the other overthrew the Emperor on the sand. Perceiving by the eagle on his casque who he was, they dismounted in haste to give him his deathblow. Never was the life of the Emperor in such peril. But Ogier, who saw him fall, flew to his rescue. Though embarrassed with the Oriflamme, he pushed his horse against one of the Saracens and knocked him down; and with his sword dealt the other so vigorous a blow that he fell stunned to the earth. Then helping the Emperor to rise, he remounted him on the horse of one of the fallen knights. “Brave and generous Alory!” Charles exclaimed, “I owe to you my honor and my life!” Ogier made no answer; but, leaving Charlemagne surrounded by a great many of the knights who had flown to his succor, he plunged into the thickest ranks of the enemy, and carried the Oriflamme, followed by a gallant train of youthful warriors till the standard of Mahomet turned in retreat, and the Infidels sought safety in their intrenchments.\n\nThen the good Archbishop Turpin laid aside his helmet and his bloody sword (for he always felt that he was clearly in the line of his duty while slaying Infidels), took his mitre and his crosier, and intoned Te Deum.\n\nAt this moment Ogier, covered with blood and dust, came to lay the Oriflamme at the feet of the Emperor. He was followed by a train of warriors of short stature, who walked ill at ease loaded with armor too heavy for them. Ogier knelt at the feet of Charlemagne, who embraced him, calling him Alory, while Turpin from the height of the altar, blessed him with all his might. Then young Orlando, son of the Count Milone, and nephew of Charlemagne, no longer able to endure this misapprehension, threw down his helmet, and ran to unlace Ogier’s, while the other young men laid aside theirs. Our author says he cannot express the surprise, the admiration, and the tenderness of the Emperor and his peers. Charles folded Ogier in his arms, and the happy fathers of those brave youths embraced them with tears of joy. The good Duke Namo stepped forward, and Charlemagne yielded Ogier to his embrace. “How much do I owe you,” he said, “good and wise friend, for having restrained my anger! My dear Ogier! I owe you my life! My sword leaps to touch your shoulder, yours and those of your brave young friends.” At these words he drew that famous sword, Joyeuse, and while Ogier and the rest knelt before him, gave them the accolade conferring on them the order of knighthood. The young Orlando and his cousin Oliver could not refrain, even in the presence of the Emperor, from falling upon Ogier’s neck, and pledging with him that brotherhood in arms, so dear and so sacred to the knights of old times; but Charlot, the Emperor’s son, at the sight of the glory with which Ogier had covered himself, conceived the blackest jealousy and hate.\n\nThe rest of the day and the next were spent in the rejoicings of the army. Turpin in a solemn service implored the favor of Heaven upon the youthful knights, and blessed the white armor which was prepared for them. Duke Namo presented them with golden spurs, Charles himself girded on their swords. But what was his astonishment when he examined that intended for Ogier! The loving Fairy, Morgana, had had the art to change it, and to substitute one of her own procuring, and when Charles drew it out of the scabbard, these words appeared written on the steel: “My name is Cortana, of the same steel and temper as Joyeuse and Durindana.” Charles saw that a superior power watched over the destinies of Ogier; he vowed to love him as a father would, and Ogier promised him the devotion of a son. Happy had it been for both if they had always continued mindful of their promises.\n\nThe Saracen army had hardly recovered from its dismay when Carahue, King of Mauritania, who was one of the knights overthrown by Ogier at the time of the rescue of Charlemagne, determined to challenge him to single combat. With that view he assumed the dress of a herald, resolved to carry his own message. The French knights admired his air, and said to one another that he seemed more fit to be a knight than a bearer of messages.\n\nCarahue began by passing the warmest eulogium upon the knight who bore the Oriflamme on the day of the battle, and concluded by saying that Carahue, King of Mauritania, respected that knight so much that he challenged him to the combat.\n\nOgier had risen to reply, when he was interrupted by Charlot, who said that the gage of the King of Mauritania could not fitly be received by a vassal, living in captivity; by which he meant Ogier, who was at that time serving as hostage for his father. Fire flashed from the eyes of Ogier, but the presence of the Emperor restrained his speech, and he was calmed by the kind looks of Charlemagne, who said, with an angry voice, “Silence, Charlot! By the life of Bertha, my queen, he who has saved my life is as dear to me as yourself. Ogier,” he continued, “you are no longer a hostage. Herald! report my answer to your master, that never does knight of my court refuse a challenge on equal terms. Ogier, the Dane, accepts of his, and I myself am his security.”\n\nCarahue, profoundly bowing, replied, “My lord, I was sure that the sentiments of so great a sovereign as yourself would be worthy of your high and brilliant fame; I shall report your answer to my master, who I know admires you, and unwillingly takes arms against you.” Then, turning to Charlot, whom he did not know as the son of the Emperor, he continued, “As for you, Sir Knight, if the desire of battle inflames you, I have it in charge from Sadon, cousin of the King of Mauritania, to give the like defiance to any French knights who will grant him the honor of the combat.”\n\nCharlot, inflamed with rage and vexation at the public reproof which he had just received, hesitated not to deliver his gage. Carahue received it with Ogier’s, and it was agreed that the combat should be on the next day in a meadow environed by woods and equally distant from both armies.\nThe perfidious Charlot meditated the blackest treason. During the night he collected some knights unworthy of the name, and like himself in their ferocious manners; he made them swear to avenge his injuries, armed them in black armor, and sent them to lie in ambush in the wood, with orders to make a pretended attack upon the whole party, but in fact, to lay heavy hands upon Ogier and the two Saracens.\n\nAt the dawn of day Sadon and Carahue, attended only by two pages to carry their spears, took their way to the appointed meadow; and Charlot and Ogier repaired thither also, but by different paths. Ogier advanced with a calm air, saluted courteously the two Saracen knights, and joined them in arranging the terms of combat.\n\nWhile this was going on the perfidious Charlot remained behind and gave his men the signal to advance. That cowardly troop issued from the wood and encompassed the three knights. All three were equally surprised at the attack, but neither of them suspected the other to have any hand in the treason. Seeing the attack made equally upon them all, they united their efforts to resist it, and made the most forward of the assailants bite the dust. Cortana fell on no one without inflicting a mortal wound, but the sword of Carahue was not of equal temper and broke in his hands. At the same instant his horse was slain, and Carahue fell, without a weapon, and entangled with his prostrate horse. Ogier, who saw it, ran to his defence and leaping to the ground covered the prince with his shield, supplied him with the sword of one of the fallen ruffians, and would have him mount his own horse. At that moment Charlot, inflamed with rage, pushed his horse upon Ogier, knocked him down, and would have run him through with his lance if Sadon, who saw the treason, had not sprung upon him and thrust him back. Carahue leapt lightly upon the horse which Ogier presented him, and had time only to exclaim, “Brave Ogier, I am no longer your enemy, I pledge to you an eternal friendship,” when numerous Saracen knights were seen approaching, having discovered the treachery, and Charlot with his followers took refuge in the wood.\n\nThe troop which advanced was commanded by Dannemont, the exiled king of Denmark, whom Geoffroy, Ogier’s father, had driven from his throne and compelled to take refuge with the Saracens. Learning who Ogier was, he instantly declared him his prisoner, in spite of the urgent remonstrances and even threats of Carahue and Sadon, and carried him under a strong guard to the Saracen camp. Here he was at first subjected to the most rigorous captivity, but Carahue and Sadon insisted so vehemently on his release, threatening to turn their arms against their own party if it was not granted, while Dannemont as eagerly opposed the measure, that Corsuble, the Saracen commander, consented to a middle course, and allowed Ogier the freedom of his camp, upon his promise not to leave it without permission.\n\nCarahue was not satisfied with this partial concession. He left the city next morning, proceeded to the camp of Charlemagne, and demanded to be led to the Emperor. When he reached his presence he dismounted from his horse, took off his helmet, drew his sword, and holding it by the blade presented it to Charlemagne as he knelt before him.\n\n“Illustrious prince,” he said, “behold before you the herald who brought the challenge to your knights from the King of Mauritania. The cowardly old King Dannemont has made the brave Ogier prisoner, and has prevailed on our general to refuse to give him up. I come to make amends for this ungenerous conduct by yielding myself, Carahue, King of Mauritania, your prisoner.”\n\nCharlemagne, with all his peers, admired the magnanimity of Carahue; he raised him, embraced him, and restored to him his sword. “Prince,” said he, “your presence and the bright example you afford my knights consoles me for the loss of Ogier. Would to God you might receive our holy faith, and be wholly united with us.” All the lords of the court, led by Duke Namo, paid their respects to the King of Mauritania. Charlot only failed to appear, fearing to be recognized as a traitor; but the heart of Carahue was too noble to pierce that of Charlemagne by telling him the treachery of his son.\n\nMeanwhile the Saracen army was rent by discord. The troops of Carahue clamored against the commander-in-chief because their king was left in captivity. They even threatened to desert the cause and turn their arms against their allies. Charlemagne pressed the siege vigorously, till at length the Saracen leaders found themselves compelled to abandon the city and betake themselves to their ships. A truce was made; Ogier was exchanged for Carahue, and the two friends embraced one another with vows of perpetual brotherhood. The Pope was reëstablished in his dominions, and Italy being tranquil, Charlemagne returned with his peers and their followers to France.\n————\n\nOGIER, THE DANE (Continued)\n\nCharlemagne had not forgotten the offence of Geoffroy, the King of Denmark, in withholding homage, and now prepared to enforce submission. But at this crisis he was waited upon by an embassy from Geoffroy, acknowledging his fault, and craving assistance against an army of invaders who had attacked his states with a force which he was unable to repel. The soul of Charlemagne was too great to be implacable, and he took this opportunity to test that of Ogier, who had felt acutely the unkindness of his father, in leaving him, without regard or notice, fifteen years in captivity. Charles asked Ogier whether, in spite of his father’s neglect, he was disposed to lead an army to his assistance. He replied, “A son can never be excused from helping his father by any cause short of death.” Charlemagne placed an army of a thousand knights under the command of Ogier, and great numbers more volunteered to march under so distinguished a leader. He flew to the succor of his father, repelled the invaders, and drove them in confusion to their vessels. Ogier then hastened to the capital, but as he drew near the city he heard all the bells sounding a knell. He soon learned the cause; it was the obsequies of Geoffroy, the King. Ogier felt keenly the grief of not having been permitted to embrace his father once more, and to learn his latest commands; but he found that his father had declared him heir to his throne. He hastened to the church where the body lay; he knelt and bathed the lifeless form with his tears. At that moment a celestial light beamed all around, and a voice of an angel said, “Ogier, leave thy crown to Guyon, thy brother, and bear no other title than that of ‘The Dane.’ Thy destiny is glorious, and other kingdoms are reserved for thee.” Ogier obeyed the divine behest. He saluted his stepmother respectfully, and embracing his brother, told him that he was content with his lot in being reckoned among the paladins of Charlemagne, and resigned all claims to the crown of Denmark.\n\nOgier returned covered with glory to the court of Charlemagne, and the Emperor, touched with this proof of his attachment, loaded him with caresses, and treated him almost as an equal.\n\nWe pass in silence the adventures of Ogier for several ensuing years, in which the fairy-gifts of his infancy showed their force in making him successful in all enterprises, both of love and war. He married the charming Belicene, and became the father of young Baldwin, a youth who seemed to inherit in full measure the strength and courage of his father and the beauty of his mother. When the lad was old enough to be separated from his mother, Ogier took him to court and presented him to Charlemagne, who embraced him and took him into his service. It seemed to Duke Namo, and all the elder knights, as if they saw in him Ogier himself, as he was when a youth; and this resemblance won for the lad their kind regards. Even Charlot at first seemed to be fond of him, though after a while the resemblance to Ogier which he noticed had the effect to excite his hatred.\n\nBaldwin was attentive to Charlot, and lost no occasion to be serviceable. The Prince loved to play chess, and Baldwin, who played well, often made a party with him.\n\nOne day Chariot was nettled at losing two pieces in succession; he thought he could, by taking a piece from Baldwin, get some amends for his loss; but Baldwin, seeing him fall into a trap which he had set for him, could not help a slight laugh, as he said, “Check-mate.” Charlot\n\nrose in a fury, seized the rich and heavy chessboard, and dashed it with all his strength on the head of Baldwin, who fell, and died where he fell.\n\nFrightened at his own crime, and fearing the vengeance of the terrible Ogier, Charlot concealed himself in the interior of the palace. A young companion of Baldwin hastened and informed Ogier of the event. He ran to the chamber, and beheld the body of his child bathed in blood, and it could not be concealed from him that Charlot gave the blow. Transported with rage, Ogier sought Charlot through the palace, and Charlot, feeling safe nowhere else, took refuge in the hall of Charlemagne, where he seated himself at table with Duke Namo and Salomon, Duke of Brittany. Ogier, with sword drawn, followed him to the very table of the Emperor. When a cupbearer attempted to bar his way he struck the cup from his hand and dashed the contents in the Emperor’s face. Charles rose in a passion, seized a knife, and would have plunged it into his breast, had not Salomon and another baron thrown themselves between, while Namo, who had retained his ancient influence over Ogier, drew him out of the room. Foreseeing the consequence of this violence, pitying Ogier, and in his heart excusing him, Namo hurried him away before the guards of the palace could arrest him, made him mount his horse, and leave Paris.\n\nCharlemagne called together his peers, and made them take an oath to do all in their power to arrest Ogier, and bring him to condign punishment. Ogier on his part sent messages to the Emperor, offering to give himself up on condition that Charlot should be punished for his atrocious crime. The Emperor would listen to no conditions, and went in pursuit of Ogier at the head of a large body of soldiers. Ogier, on the other hand, was warmly supported by many knights, who pledged themselves in his defence. The contest raged long, with no decisive results. Ogier more than once had the Emperor in his power, but declined to avail himself of his advantage, and released him without conditions. He even implored pardon for himself, but demanded at the same time the punishment of Charlot. But Charlemagne was too blindly fond of his unworthy son to subject him to punishment for the sake of conciliating one who had been so deeply injured.\n\nAt length, distressed at the blood which his friends had lost in his cause, Ogier dismissed his little army, and slipping away from those who wished to attend him, took his course to rejoin the Duke Guyon, his brother. On his way, having reached the forest of Ardennes, weary with long travel, the freshness of a retired valley tempted him to lie down to take some repose. He unsaddled Beiffror, relieved himself of his helmet, lay down on the turf, rested his head on his shield, and slept.\n\nIt so happened that Turpin, who occasionally recalled to mind that he was Archbishop of Rheins, was at that time in the vicinity, making a pastoral visit to the churches under his jurisdiction. But his dignity of peer of France, and his martial spirit, which caused him to be reckoned among the “preux chevaliers” of his time, forbade him to travel without as large a retinue of knights as he had of clergymen. One of these was thirsty, and knowing the fountain on the borders of which Ogier was reposing, he rode to it, and was struck by the sight of a knight stretched on the ground. He hastened back, and let the Archbishop know, who approached the fountain, and recognized Ogier.\n\nThe first impulse of the good and generous Turpin was to save his friend, for whom he felt the warmest attachment; but his archdeacons and knights, who also recognized Ogier, reminded the Archbishop of the oath which the Emperor had exacted of them all. Turpin could not be false to his oath; but it was not without a groan that he permitted his followers to bind the sleeping knight. The Archbishop’s attendants secured the horse and arms of Ogier, and conducted their prisoner to the Emperor at Soissons.\n\nThe Emperor had become so much embittered by Ogier’s obstinate resistance, added to his original fault, that he was disposed to order him to instant death. But Turpin, seconded by the good Dukes Namo and Salomon, prayed so hard for him that Charlemagne consented to remit a violent death, but sentenced him to close imprisonment, under the charge of the Archbishop, strictly limiting his food to one quarter of a loaf of bread per day, with one piece of meat, and a quarter of a cup of wine. In this way he hoped to quickly put an end to his life without bringing on himself the hostility of the King of Denmark, and other powerful friends of Ogier. He exacted a new oath of Turpin to obey his order strictly.\n\nThe good Archbishop loved Ogier too well not to cast about for some means of saving his life, which he foresaw he would soon lose if subjected to such scanty fare, for Ogier was seven feet tall, and had an appetite in proportion. Turpin remembered, moreover, that Ogier was a true son of the Church, always zealous to propagate the faith and subdue unbelievers; so he felt justified in practising on this occasion what in later times has been entitled “mental reservation,” without swerving from the letter of the oath which he had taken. This is the method he hit upon.\n\nEvery morning he had his prisoner supplied with a quarter of a loaf of bread, made of two bushels of flour, to this he added a quarter of a sheep or a fat calf, and he had a cup made which held forty pints of wine, and allowed Ogier a quarter of it daily.\n\nOgier’s imprisonment lasted long; Charlemagne was astonished to hear, from time to time, that he still held out; and when he inquired more particularly of Turpin, the good Archbishop, relying on his own understanding of the words, did not hesitate to affirm positively that he allowed his prisoner no more than the permitted ration.\n\nWe forgot to say that, when Ogier was led prisoner to Soissons, the Abbot of Saint Faron, observing the fine horse Beiffror, and not having at the time any other favor to ask of Charlemagne, begged the Emperor to give him the horse, and had him taken to his abbey. He was impatient to try his new acquisition, and when he had arrived in his litter at the foot of the mountain where the horse had been brought to meet him mounted him and rode onward. The horse, accustomed to bear the enormous weight of Ogier in his armor, when he perceived nothing on his back but the light weight of the Abbot, whose long robes fluttered against his sides, ran away, making prodigious leaps over the steep acclivities of the mountain till he reached the convent of Jouaire, where, in sight of the Abbess and her nuns, he threw the Abbot, already half dead with fright, to the ground. The Abbot, bruised and mortified, revenged himself on poor Beiffror, whom he condemned, in his wrath, to be given to the workmen to drag stones for a chapel that he was building near the abbey. Thus, ill-fed, hard-worked, and often beaten, the noble horse Beiffror passed the time while his master’s imprisonment lasted.\n\nThat imprisonment would have been as long as his life if it had not been for some important events which forced the Emperor to set Ogier at liberty.\n\nThe Emperor learned at the same time that Carahue, King of Mauritania, was assembling an army to come and demand the liberation of Ogier; that Guyon, King of Denmark, was prepared to second the enterprise with all his forces; and, worse than all, that the Saracens, under Bruhier, Sultan of Arabia, had landed in Gascony, taken Bordeaux, and were marching with all speed for Paris.\n\nCharlemagne now felt how necessary the aid of Ogier was to him. But, in spite of the representations of Turpin, Namo, and Salomon, he could not bring himself to consent to surrender Charlot to such punishment as Ogier should see fit to impose. Besides, he believed that Ogier was without strength and vigor, weakened by imprisonment and long abstinence.\n\nAt this crisis he received a message from Bruhier, proposing to put the issue upon the result of a combat between himself and the Emperor or his champion: promising, if defeated, to withdraw his army. Charlemagne would willingly have accepted the challenge, but his counsellors all opposed it. The herald was therefore told that the Emperor would take time to consider his proposition, and give his answer the next day.\n\nIt was during this interval that the three Dukes succeeded in prevailing upon Charlemagne to pardon Ogier, and to send for him to combat the puissant enemy who now defied him; but it was no easy task to persuade Ogier. The idea of his long imprisonment and the recollection of his son, bleeding and dying in his arms by the blow of the ferocious Charlot, made him long resist the urgency of his friends. Though glory called him to encounter Bruhier, and the safety of Christendom demanded the destruction of this proud enemy of the faith, Ogier only yielded at last on condition that Charlot should be delivered into his hands to be dealt with as he should see fit.\n\nThe terms were hard, but the danger was pressing, and Charlemagne, with a returning sense of justice, and a strong confidence in the generous though passionate soul of Ogier, at last consented to them.\n\nOgier was led into the presence of Charlemagne by the three peers. The Emperor, faithful to his word, had caused Charlot to be brought into the hall where the high barons were assembled, his hands tied, and his head uncovered. When the Emperor saw Ogier approach he took Charlot by the arm, led him towards Ogier, and said these words: “I surrender the criminal; do with him as you think fit.” Ogier, without replying, seized Charlot by the hair, forced him on his knees, and lifted with the other hand his irresistible sword. Charlemagne, who expected to see the head of his son rolling at his feet, shut his eyes and uttered a cry of horror.\n\nOgier had done enough. The next moment he raised Charlot, cut his bonds, kissed him on the mouth, and hastened to throw himself at the feet of the Emperor.\n\nNothing can exceed the surprise and joy of Charlemagne at seeing his son unharmed and Ogier kneeling at his feet. He folded him in his arms, bathed him with tears, and exclaimed to his barons, “I feel at this moment that Ogier is greater than I.” As for Charlot, his base soul felt nothing but the joy of having escaped death; he remained such as he had been, and it was not till some years afterwards he received the punishment he deserved, from the hands of Huon of Bordeaux, as we have seen in a former chapter.\n\n————\nOGIER, THE DANE (Continued)\n\nWhen Charlemagne had somewhat recovered his composure he was surprised to observe that Ogier appeared in good case, and had a healthy color in his cheeks. He turned to the Archbishop, who could not help blushing as he met his eye. “By the head of Bertha, my queen,” said Charlemagne, “Ogier has had good quarters in your castle, my Lord Archbishop; but so much the more am I indebted to you.” All the barons laughed and jested with Turpin, who only said, “Laugh as much as you please, my lords; but for my part I am not sorry to see the arm in full vigor that is to avenge us on the proud Saracen.”\n\nCharlemagne immediately despatched his herald, accepting the challenge, and appointing the next day but one for the encounter. The proud and crafty Bruhier laughed scornfully when he heard the reply accepting his challenge, for he had a reliance on certain resources besides his natural strength and skill. However, he swore by Mahomet to observe the conditions as proposed and agreed upon.\n\nOgier now demanded his armor, and it was brought to him in excellent condition, for the good Turpin had kept it faithfully; but it was not easy to provide a horse for the occasion. Charlemagne had the best horses of his stables brought out, except Blanchard, his own charger; but all in vain, the weight of Ogier bent their backs to the ground. In this embarrassment the Archbishop remembered that the Emperor had given Beiffror to the Abbot of St. Faron, and sent off a courier in haste to re-demand him.\n\nMonks are hard masters, and the one who directed the laborers at the abbey had but too faithfully obeyed the orders of the Abbot. Poor Beiffror was brought back, lean, spiritless, and chafed with the harness of the vile cart that he had had to draw so long. He carried his head down, and trod heavily before Charlemagne; but when he heard the voice of Ogier he raised his head, he neighed, his eyes flashed, his former ardor showed itself by the force with which he pawed the ground. Ogier caressed him, and the good steed seemed to return his caresses; Ogier mounted him, and Beiffror, proud of carrying his master again, leapt and curvetted with all his youthful vigor.\n\nNothing being now wanted, Charlemagne, at the head of his army, marched forth from the city of Paris, and occupied the hill of Montmartre, whence the view extended over the plain of St. Denis, where the battle was to be fought.\n\nWhen the appointed day came the Dukes Namo and Salomon, as seconds of Ogier, accompanied him to the place marked out for the lists, and Bruhier, with two distinguished Emirs, presented himself on the other side.\n\nBruhier was in high spirits, and jested with his friends, as he advanced, upon the appearance of Beiffror. “Is that the horse they presume to match with Marchevallée, the best steed that ever fed in the vales of Mount Atlas?” But now the combatants, having met and saluted each other, ride apart to come together in full career. Beiffror flew over the plain, and met the adversary more than half-way. The lances of the two combatants were shivered at the shock, and Bruhier was astonished to see almost at the same instant the sword of Ogier gleaming above his head. He parried it with his buckler, and gave Ogier a blow on his helmet, who returned it with another, better aimed or better seconded by the temper of his blade, for it cut away part of Bruhier’s helmet, and with it his ear and part of his cheek. Ogier, seeing the blood, did not immediately repeat his blow, and Bruhier seized the moment to gallop off at one side. As he rode he took a vase of gold which hung at his saddle-bow, and bathed with its contents the wounded part. The blood instantly ceased to flow, the ear and the flesh were restored quite whole, and the Dane was astonished to see his antagonist return to the ground as sound as ever.\n\nBruhier laughed at his amazement. “Know,” said he, “that I possess the precious balm that Joseph of Arimathea used upon the body of the crucified one, whom you worship. If I should lose an arm I could restore it with a few drops of this. It is useless for you to contend with me. Yield yourself, and, as you appear to be a strong fellow, I will make you first oarsman in one of my galleys.”\n\nOgier, though boiling with rage, forgot not to implore the assistance of Heaven. “O Lord!” he exclaimed, “suffer not the enemy of thy name to profit by the powerful help of that which owes all its virtue to thy divine blood.” At these words he attacked Bruhier again with more vigor than ever; both struck terrible blows, and made grievous wounds; but the blood flowed from those of Ogier, while Bruhier stanched his by the application of his balm. Ogier, desperate at the unequal contest, grasped Cortana with both hands, and struck his enemy such a blow that it cleft his buckler, and cut off his arm with it; but Bruhier at the same time launched one at Ogier, which, missing him, struck the head of Beiffror, and the good horse fell, and drew down his master in his fall.\n\nBruhier had time to leap to the ground, to pick up his arm and apply his balsam; then, before Ogier had recovered his footing, he rushed forward with sword uplifted to complete his destruction.\n\nCharlemagne, from the height of Montmartre, seeing the brave Ogier in this situation, groaned, and was ready to murmur against Providence; but the good Turpin, raising his arms, with a faith like that of Moses, drew down upon the Christian warrior the favor of Heaven.\n\nOgier, promptly disengaging himself, pressed Bruhier with so much impetuosity that he drove him to a distance from his horse, to whose saddle-bow the precious balm was suspended; and very soon Charlemagne saw Ogier, now completely in the advantage, bring his enemy to his knees, tear off his helmet, and, with a sweep of his sword, strike his head from his body.\n\nAfter the victory, Ogier seized Marchevallée, leaped upon his back, and became possessed of the precious flask, a few drops from which closed his wounds and restored his strength. The French knights who had been Bruhier’s captives, now released, pressed round Ogier to thank him for their deliverance.\n\nCharlemagne and his nobles, as soon as their attention was relieved from the single combat, perceived from their elevated position an unusual agitation in the enemy’s camp. They attributed it at first to the death of their general, but soon the noise of arms, the cries of combatants, and new standards which advanced, disclosed to them the fact that Bruhier’s army was attacked by a new enemy.\nThe Emperor was right; it was the brave Carahue of Mauritania, who, with an army, had arrived in France, resolved to attempt the liberation of Ogier, his brother in arms. Learning on his arrival the changed aspect of affairs, he hesitated not to render a signal service to the Emperor, by attacking the army of Bruhier in the midst of the consternation occasioned by the loss of its commander.\n\nOgier recognized the standard of his friend, and leaping upon Marchevallée, flew to aid his attack. Charlemagne followed with his army; and the Saracen host, after an obstinate conflict, was forced to surrender unconditionally.\n\nThe interview of Ogier and Carahue was such as might be anticipated of two such attached friends and accomplished knights. Charlemagne went to meet them, embraced them, and putting the King of Mauritania on his right and Ogier on his left, returned with triumph to Paris. There the Empress Bertha and the ladies of her court crowned them with laurels, and the sage and gallant Eginhard, chamberlain and secretary of the Emperor, wrote all these great events in his history.\n\nA few days after Guyon, King of Denmark, arrived in France with a chosen band of knights, and sent an ambassador to Charlemagne, to say that he came, not as an enemy, but to render homage to him as the best knight of the time and the head of the Christian world. Charlemagne gave the ambassador a cordial reception, and mounting his horse, rode forward to meet the King of Denmark.\n\nThese great princes, being assembled at the court of Charles, held council together, and the ancient and sage barons were called to join it.\n\nIt was decided that the united Danish and Mauritanian armies should cross the sea and carry the war to the country of the Saracens, and that a thousand French knights should range themselves under the banner of Ogier, the Dane, who, though not a king, should have equal rank with the two others.\n\nWe have not space to record all the illustrious actions performed by Ogier and his allies in this war. Suffice it to say, they subdued the Saracens of Ptolemais and Judæa, and, erecting those regions into a kingdom, placed the crown upon the head of Ogier. Guyon and Carahue then left him, to return to their respective dominions. Ogier adopted Walter, the son of Guyon of Denmark, to be his successor in his kingdom. He superintended his education, and saw the young prince grow up worthy of his cares. But Ogier, in spite of all the honors of his rank, often regretted the court of Charlemagne, the Duke Namo, and Salomon of Brittany, for whom he had the respect and attachment of a son. At last, finding Walter old enough to sustain the weight of government, Ogier caused a vessel to be prepared secretly, and, attended only by one squire, left his palace by night, and embarked to return to France.\n\nThe vessel, driven by a fair wind, cut the sea with the swiftness of a bird; but on a sudden it deviated from its course, no longer obeyed the helm, and sped fast towards a black promontory which stretched into the sea. This was a mountain of loadstone, and, its attractive power increasing as the distance diminished, the vessel at last flew with the swiftness of an arrow towards it, and was dashed to pieces on its rocky base. Ogier alone saved himself, and reached the shore on a fragment of the wreck.\n\nOgier advanced into the country, looking for some marks of inhabitancy, but found none. On a sudden he encountered two monstrous animals, covered with glittering scales, accompanied by a horse breathing fire. Ogier drew his sword and prepared to defend himself; but the monsters, terrific as they appeared, made no attempt to assail him, and the horse, Papillon, knelt down, and appeared to court Ogier to mount upon his back. Ogier hesitated not to see the adventure through; he mounted Papillon, who ran with speed, and soon cleared the rocks and precipices which hemmed in and concealed a beautiful landscape. He continued his course till he reached a magnificent palace, and, without allowing Ogier time to admire it, crossed a grand courtyard adorned with colonnades, and entered a garden, where, making his way through alleys of myrtle, he checked his course, and knelt down on the enamelled turf of a fountain.\nOgier dismounted and took some steps along the margin of the stream, but was soon stopped by meeting a young beauty, such as they paint the Graces, and almost as lightly attired as they. At the same moment, to his amazement, his armor fell off of its own accord. The young beauty advanced with a tender air, and placed upon his head a crown of flowers. At that instant the Danish hero lost his memory; his combats, his glory, Charlemagne and his court, all vanished from his mind; he saw only Morgana, he desired nothing but to sigh forever at her feet.\n\nWe abridge the narrative of all the delights which Ogier enjoyed for more than a hundred years. Time flew by, leaving no impression of its flight. Morgana’s youthful charms did not decay, and Ogier had none of those warnings of increasing years which less favored mortals never fail to receive. There is no knowing how long this blissful state might have lasted, if it had not been for an accident, by which Morgana one day, in a sportive moment, snatched the crown from his head. That moment Ogier regained his memory, and lost his contentment. The recollection of Charlemagne, and of his own relatives and friends, saddened the hours which he passed with Morgana. The fairy saw with grief the changed looks of her lover. At last she drew from him the acknowledgment that he wished to go, at least for a time, to revisit Charles’s court. She consented with reluctance, and with her own hands helped to reinvest him with his armor. Papillon was led forth, Ogier mounted him, and, taking a tender adieu of the tearful Morgana, crossed at rapid speed the rocky belt which separated Morgana’s palace from the borders of the sea.\n\nThe sea-goblins which had received him at his coming awaited him on the shore. One of them took Ogier on his back, and the other placing himself under Papillon, they spread their broad fins, and in a short time traversed the wide space that separates the isle of Avalon from France. They landed Ogier on the coast of Languedoc, and then plunged into the sea and disappeared.\n\nOgier remounted on Papillon, who carried him across the kingdom almost as fast as he had passed the sea. He arrived under the walls of Paris, which he would scarcely have recognized if the high towers of St. Genevieve had not caught his eye. He went straight to the palace of Charlemagne, which seemed to him to have been entirely rebuilt. His surprise was extreme, and increased still more on finding that he understood with difficulty the language of the guards and attendants in replying to his questions; and seeing them smile as they tried to explain to one another the language in which he addressed them. Presently the attention of some of the barons who were going to court was attracted to the scene, and Ogier, who recognized the badges of their rank, addressed them, and inquired if the Dukes Namo and Salomon were still residing at the Emperor’s court. At this question the barons looked at one another in amazement; and one of the eldest said to the rest, “How much this knight resembles the portrait of my grand-uncle, Ogier the Dane.” “Ah! my dear nephew, I am Ogier the Dane,” said he; and he remembered that Morgana had told him that he was little aware of the flight of time during his abode with her.\n\nThe barons, more astonished than ever, concluded to conduct him to the monarch who then reigned, the great Hugh Capet.\n\nThe brave Ogier entered the palace without hesitation; but when, on reaching the royal hall, the barons directed him to make his obeisance to the King of France, he was astonished to see a man of short stature and large head, whose air, nevertheless, was noble and martial, seated upon the throne on which he had so often seen Charlemagne, the tallest and handsomest sovereign of his time.\n\nOgier recounted his adventures with simplicity and unaffectedness. Hugh Capet was slow to believe him; but Ogier recalled so many proofs and circumstances, that at last he was forced to recognize the aged warrior to be the famous Ogier the Dane.\n\nThe king informed Ogier of the events which had taken place during his long absence; that the line of Charlemagne was extinct; that a new dynasty had commenced; that the old enemies of the kingdom, the Saracens, were still troublesome; and that at that very time an army of those miscreants was besieging the city of Chartres, to which he was about to repair in a few days to its relief. Ogier, always inflamed with the love of glory, offered the service of his arm, which the illustrious monarch accepted graciously, and conducted him to the queen. The astonishment of Ogier was redoubled when he saw the new ornaments and head-dresses of the ladies; still, the beautiful hair which they built up on their foreheads, and the feathers interwoven, which waved with so much grace, gave them a noble air that delighted him. His admiration increased when, instead of the old Empress Bertha, he saw a young queen who combined a majestic mien with the graces of her time of life, and manners candid and charming, suited to attach all hearts. Ogier saluted the youthful queen with a respect so profound that many of the courtiers took him for a foreigner, or at least for some nobleman brought up at a distance from Paris, who retained the manners of what they called the old court.\n\nWhen the queen was informed by her husband that it was the celebrated Ogier the Dane whom he presented to her, whose memorable exploits she had often read in the chronicles of antiquity, her surprise was extreme, which was increased when she remarked the dignity of his address, the animation and even the youthfulness of his countenance. This queen had too much intelligence to believe hastily; proof alone could compel her assent; and she asked him many questions about the old court of Charlemagne, and received such instructive and appropriate answers as removed every doubt. It is to the corrections which Ogier was at that time enabled to make to the popular narratives of his exploits that we are indebted for the perfect accuracy and trustworthiness of all the details of our own history.\n\nKing Hugh Capet, having received that same evening couriers from the inhabitants of Chartres, informing him that they were hard pressed by the besiegers, resolved to hasten with Ogier to their relief.\n\nOgier terminated this affair as expeditiously as he had so often done others. The Saracens having dared to offer battle, he bore the Oriflamme through the thickest of their ranks; Papillon, breathing fire from his nostrils, threw them into disorder, and Cortana, wielded by his invincible arm, soon finished their overthrow.\n\nThe king, victorious over the Saracens, led back the Danish hero to Paris, where the deliverer of France received the honors due to his valor. Ogier continued some time at the court, detained by the favor of the king and queen; but erelong he had the pain to witness the death of the king. Then it was that, impressed with all the perfections which he had discerned in the queen, he could not withhold the tender homage of the offer of his hand. The queen would perhaps have accepted it, she had even called a meeting of her great barons to deliberate on the proposition, when, the day before the meeting was to be held, at the moment when Ogier was kneeling at her feet, she perceived a crown of gold which an invisible hand had placed on his brow, and in an instant a cloud enveloped Ogier, and he disappeared forever from her sight. It was Morgana, the fairy, whose jealousy was awakened at what she beheld, who now resumed her power, and took him away to dwell with her in the island of Avalon. There, in company with the great King Arthur of Britain, he still lives, and when his illustrious friend shall return to resume his ancient reign he will doubtless return with him, and share his triumph.\n\n-----\n\n[1] Wordsworth.\n\n[2] The names included in parentheses are the Greek, the others being the Roman or Latin names.\n\n[3] This inconsistency arises from considering the Saturn of the Romans the same with the Grecian deity Cronos (Time), which, as it brings an end to all things which have had a beginning, may be said to devour its own offspring.\n\n[4] From this origin of the instrument, the word “shell” is often used as synonymous with “lyre,” and figuratively for music and poetry. Thus Gray, in his ode on the “Progress of Poesy,” says:\n\n“O Sovereign of the willing Soul, Parent of sweet and solemn-breathing airs, Enchanting shell! the sullen Cares And frantic Passions hear thy soft control.”\n\n[5] There was also a goddess called Fauna, or Bona Dea.\n\n[6] The goddess of innocence and purity. After leaving earth, she was placed among the stars, where she became the constellation Virgo—the Virgin. Themis (Justice) was the mother of Astræa. She is represented as holding aloft a pair of scales, in which she weighs the claims of opposing parties.\n\nIt was a favorite idea of the old poets that these goddesses would one day return, and bring back the Golden Age. Even in a Christian hymn, the “Messiah” of Pope, this idea occurs:\n\n“All crimes shall cease, and ancient fraud shall fail, Returning Justice lift aloft her scale, Peace o’er the world her olive wand extend, And white-robed Innocence from heaven descend.”\n\nSee, also, Milton’s “Hymn on the Nativity,” stanzas xiv. and xv.\n\n[7] See Proverbial Expressions.\n\n[8] See Proverbial Expressions.\n\n[9] See Proverbial Expressions.\n\n[10] See Proverbial Expressions.\n\n[11] See page 241.\n\n[12] It is evidently not our modern hyacinth that is here described. It is perhaps some species of iris, or perhaps of larkspur or of pansy.\n\n[13] The sunflower.\n\n[14] This correct description of the rainbow is literally translated from Ovid.\n\n[15] Sir James Mackintosh says of this, “Do you think that even a Chinese could paint the gay colors of a butterfly with more minute exactness than the following lines: ‘The velvet nap,’ etc.?”—Life, Vol. II., 246.\n\n[16] See Proverbial Expressions.\n\n[17] Hecate was a mysterious divinity sometimes identified with Diana and sometimes with Proserpine. As Diana represents the moonlight splendor of night, so Hecate represents its darkness and terrors. She was the goddess of sorcery and witchcraft, and was believed to wander by night along the earth, seen only by the dogs, whose barking told her approach.\n\n[18] Alcides, a name of Hercules.\n\n[19] One of the finest pieces of sculpture in Italy, the recumbent Ariadne of the Vatican, represents this incident. A copy is owned by the Athenæum, Boston, and deposited in the Museum of Fine Arts.\n\n[20] Proteus.\n\n[21] The story of the invulnerability of Achilles is not found in Homer, and is inconsistent with his account. For how could Achilles require the aid of celestial armor if he were invulnerable?\n\n[22] Tennyson has chosen Œnone as the subject of a short poem; but he has omitted the most poetical part of the story, the return of Paris wounded, her cruelty and subsequent repentance.\n\n[23] See Proverbial Expressions.\n\n[24] Pyrrhus’s exclamation, “Not such aid nor such defenders does the time require,” has become proverbial. See Proverbial Expressions.\n\n[25] Tennyson in the “Lotus-eaters” has charmingly expressed the dreamy, languid feeling which the lotus food is said to have produced.\n\n“How sweet it were, hearing the downward stream With half-shut eyes ever to seem Falling asleep in a half dream! To dream and dream, like yonder amber light Which will not leave the myrrh-bush on the height; To hear each others’ whispered speech; Eating the Lotos, day by day, To watch the crisping ripples on the beach, And tender curving lines of creamy spray: To lend our hearts and spirits wholly To the influence of mild-minded melancholy; To muse and brood and live again in memory, With those old faces of our infancy Heaped over with a mound of grass, Two handfuls of white dust, shut in an urn of brass.”\n\n[26] See Proverbial Expressions.\n\n[27] See Proverbial Expressions.\n\n[28] See Proverbial Expressions.\n\n[29] See Proverbial Expressions.\n\n[30] See Proverbial Expressions.\n\n[31] See Proverbial Expressions.\n\n[32] See Proverbial Expressions.\n\n[33] The poet here inserts a famous line which is thought to imitate in its sound the galloping of horses. It may be thus translated: “Then struck the hoofs of the steeds on the ground with a four-footed trampling.”—See Proverbial Expressions.\n\n[34] See Proverbial Expressions.\n\n[35] There being no rain in Egypt, the grass is “unshowered,” and the country depends for its fertility upon the overflowings of the Nile. The ark alluded to in the last line is shown by pictures still remaining on the walls of the Egyptian temples to have been borne by the priests in their religious processions. It probably represented the chest in which Osiris was placed.\n\n[36] Cowper’s version is less elegant, but truer to the original:\n\n“He ceased, and under his dark brows the nod Vouchsafed of confirmation. All around The sovereign’s everlasting head his curls Ambrosial shook, and the huge mountain reeled.”\n\nIt may interest our readers to see how this passage appears in another famous version, that which was issued under the name of Tickell, contemporaneously with Pope’s, and which, being by many attributed to Addison, led to the quarrel which ensued between Addison and Pope:\n\n“This said, his kingly brow the sire inclined; The large black curls fell awful from behind, Thick shadowing the stern forehead of the god; Olympus trembled at the almighty nod.”\n\n[37] Gray’s ode, “The Fatal Sisters,” is founded on this superstition.\n\n[38] In Longfellow’s Poems will be found a poem entitled “Tegner’s Drapa,” upon the subject of Baldur’s death.\n\n[39]\n“For noble Britons sprong from Trojans bold, And Troynovant was built of old Troy’s ashes cold.” SPENSER, Book III., Canto IX., 38.\n\n[40] Buried under beare. Buried under something which enclosed him like a coffin or bier.\n\n[41] Glastonbury Abbey, said to be founded by Joseph of Arimathea, in a spot anciently called the island or valley of Avalonia.\n\nTennyson, in his “Palace of Art,” alludes to the legend of Arthur’s rescue by the Faery queen, thus:\n\n“Or mythic Uther’s deeply wounded son, In some fair space of sloping greens, Lay dozing in the vale of Avalon, And watched by weeping queens.”\n\n[42] Guenever, the name of Arthur’s queen, also written Genievre and Geneura, is familiar to all who are conversant with chivalric lore. It is to her adventures, and those of her true knight, Sir Launcelot, that Dante alludes in the beautiful episode of Francesca da Rimini.\n\n[43] This name, in the French romances, is spelled Queux, which means head cook. This would seem to imply that it was a title, and not a name; yet the personage who bore it is never mentioned by any other. He is the chief, if not the only, comic character among the heroes of Arthur’s court. He is the Seneschal or Steward, his duties also embracing those of chief of the cooks. In the romances, his general character is a compound of valor and buffoonery, always ready to fight, and generally getting the worst of the battle. He is also sarcastic and abusive in his remarks, by which he often gets into trouble. Yet Arthur seems to have an attachment to him, and often takes his advice, which is generally wrong.\n\n[44] Several cities are allotted to King Arthur by the romance-writers. The principal are Caerleon, Camelot, and Carlisle.\n\nCaerleon derives its name from its having been the station of one of the legions, during the dominion of the Romans. It is called by Latin writers Urbs Legionum, the City of Legions. The former word being rendered into Welsh by Caer, meaning city, and the latter contracted into lleon. The river Usk retains its name in modern geography, and there is a town or city of Caerleon upon it, though the city of Cardiff is thought to be the scene of Arthur’s court. Chester also bears in Welsh the name of Caerleon; for Chester, derived from castra, Latin for camp, is the designation of military headquarters.\n\nCamelot is thought to be Winchester.\n\nShalott is Guilford.\n\nHamo’s Port is Southampton.\n\nCarlisle is the city still retaining that name, near the Scottish border. But this name is also sometimes applied to other places, which were, like itself, military stations.\n\n[45] Samite, a sort of silk stuff.\n\n[46] N’as is not was, contracted; in modern phrase, there was not Mochel sorwe is much sorrow; morwe is morrow.\n\n[47] New-fangled—fond of novelty.\n\n[48] Lunys, the string with which the falcon is held.\n\n[49] A musical instrument.\n\n[50] “Good faith was the very corner-stone of chivalry. Whenever a knight’s word was pledged (it mattered not how rashly) it was to be redeemed at any price. Hence the sacred obligation of the boon granted by a knight to his suppliant. Instances without number occur in romance, in which a knight, by rashly granting an indefinite boon, was obliged to do or suffer something extremely to his prejudice. But it is not in romance alone that we find such singular instances of adherence to an indefinite promise. The history of the times presents authentic transactions equally embarrassing and absurd.”—SCOTT, note to Sir Tristram.\n\n[51] Feres, companions; thewes, labors; leers, learning.\n\n[52] Aglets, points or tags.\n\n[53] Pinckt upon gold, etc., adorned with golden points, or eyelets, and regularly intersected with stripes. Paled (in heraldry), striped.\n\n[54] A fool was a common appendage of the courts of those days when this romance was written. A fool was the ornament held in next estimation to a dwarf. He wore a white dress with a yellow bonnet, and carried a bell or bawble in his hand. Though called a fool, his words were often weighed and remembered as if there were a sort of oracular meaning in them.\n\n[55] The word means both fisher and sinner.\n\n[56] The use of green rushes in apartments was by no means peculiar to the court of Caerleon upon Usk. Our ancestors had a great predilection for them, and they seem to have constituted an essential article, not only of comfort, but of luxury. The custom of strewing the floor with rushes is well known to have existed in England during the Middle Ages, and also in France.\n\n[57] Cordwal is the word in the original, and from the manner in which it is used it is evidently intended for the French Cordouan or Cordovan leather, which derived its name from Cordova, where it was manufactured. From this comes also our English word cordwainer.\n\n[58] Amongst all the characters of early British history none is more interesting, or occupies a more conspicuous place, than the hero of this tale. Urien, his father, was prince of Rheged, a district comprising the present Cumberland and part of the adjacent country. His valor, and the consideration in which he was held, are a frequent theme of Bardic song, and form the subject of several very spirited odes by Taliesin. Among the Triads there is one relating to him; it is thus translated:\n\n“Three Knights of Battle were in the court of Arthur: Cadwr, the Earl of Cornwall, Launcelot du Lac, and Owain, the son of Urien. And this was their characteristic—that they would not retreat from battle, neither for spear, nor for arrow, nor for sword. And Arthur never had shame in battle the day he saw their faces there. And they were called the Knights of Battle.”\n\n[59] Before the sixth century all the silk used by Europeans had been brought to them by the Seres, the ancestors of the present Boukharians, whence it derived its Latin name of Serica. In 551 the silkworm was brought by two monks to Constantinople; but the manufacture of silk was confined to the Greek empire till the year 1130, when Roger, king of Sicily, returning from a crusade, collected some manufacturers from Athens and Corinth, and established them at Palermo, whence the trade was gradually disseminated over Italy. The varieties of silk stuffs known at this time were velvet, satin (which was called samite), and taffety (called cendal or sendall), all of which were occasionally stitched with gold and silver.\n\n[60] There exists an ancient poem, printed among those of Taliesin, called the “Elegy of Owain ap Urien,” and containing several very beautiful and spirited passages. It commences:\n\n“The soul of Owain ap Urien, May its Lord consider its exigencies! Reged’s chief the green turf covers.”\n\nIn the course of this Elegy the bard, alluding to the incessant warfare with which this chieftain harassed his Saxon foes, exclaims:\n\n“Could England sleep with the light upon her eyes!”\n\n[61] The custom of riding into a hall while the lord and his guests sat at meat might be illustrated by numerous passages of ancient romance and history. But a quotation from Chaucer’s beautiful and half-told tale of “Cambuscan” is sufficient:\n\n“And so befell that after the thridde cours, While that this king sat thus in his nobley, Herking his minstralles thir thinges play, Beforne him at his bord deliciously, In at the halle door all sodenly Ther came a knight upon a stede of bras, And in his hond a brod mirrour of glas; Upon his thombe he had of gold a ring, And by his side a naked sword hanging; And up he rideth to the highe bord. In all the halle ne was ther spoke a word, For mervaille of this knight; him to behold Full besily they waiten, young and old.”\n\n[62] The terms of admiration in which the older writers invariably speak of glass windows would be sufficient proof, if other evidence were wanting, how rare an article of luxury they were in the houses of our ancestors. They were first introduced in ecclesiastical architecture, to which they were for a long time confined. Glass is said not to have been employed in domestic architecture before the fourteenth century.\n\n[63] Throughout the broad and varied region of romance it would be difficult to find a character of greater simplicity and truth than that of Enid, the daughter of Earl Ynywl. Conspicuous for her beauty and noble bearing, we are at a loss whether more to admire the patience with which she bore all the hardships she was destined to undergo or the constancy and affection which finally achieved the triumph she so richly deserved.\n\nThe character of Enid is admirably sustained through the whole tale; and as it is more natural, because less overstrained, so perhaps it is even more touching than that of Griselda, over which, however, Chaucer has thrown a charm that leads us to forget the improbability of her story.\n\n[64] The Island of the Mighty is one of the many names bestowed upon Britain by the Welsh.\n\n[65] Caractacus.\n\n[66] Cassivellaunus.\n\n[67] There is a Triad upon the story of the head buried under the White Tower of London, as a charm against invasion. Arthur, it seems, proudly disinterred the head, preferring to hold the island by his own strength alone.\n\n[68] Creiddylad is no other than Shakspeare’s Cordelia, whose father, King Lear, is by the Welsh authorities called indiscriminately Llyr or Lludd. All the old chronicles give the story of her devotion to her aged parent, but none of them seem to have been aware that she is destined to remain with him till the day of doom, whilst Gwyn ap Nudd, the king of the fairies, and Gwythyr ap Greidiol, fight for her every first of May, and whichever of them may be fortunate enough to be the conqueror at that time will obtain her as a bride.\n\n[69] The Welsh have a fable on the subject of the half-man, taken to be illustrative of the force of habit. In this allegory Arthur is supposed to be met by a sprite, who appears at first in a small and indistinct form, but who, on approaching nearer, increases in size, and, assuming the semblance of half a man, endeavors to provoke the king to wrestle. Despising his weakness, and considering that he should gain no credit by the encounter, Arthur refuses to do so, and delays the contest until at length the half-man (Habit) becomes so strong that it requires his utmost efforts to overcome him.\n\n[70] The romancers dwell with great complacency on the fair hair and delicate complexion of their heroines. This taste continued for a long time, and to render the hair light was an object of education. Even when wigs came into fashion they were all flaxen. Such was the color of the hair of the Gauls and of their German conquerors. It required some centuries to reconcile their eyes to the swarthy beauties of their Spanish and Italian neighbors.\n\n[71] Ivanhoe, Vol. 1, chap. XIII.\n\n[72] It is plain that Shakspeare borrowed from this source the similar incident in his “As you Like it.” The names of characters in the play, Orlando, Oliver, Rowland indicate the same thing.\n\n[73] See their story in “King Arthur and His Knights.”\n\n[74] This is a poetical description of a phenomenon which is said to be really exhibited in the strait of Messina, between Sicily and Calabria. It is called Fata Morgana, or Mirage.\n\n[75] This prophecy is introduced by Ariosto in this place to compliment the noble house of Este, the princes of his native state, the dukedom of Ferrara.";
if(typeof BODIES_TRANS!=="undefined") BODIES_TRANS["gb-56644"]="고대 그리스와 로마의 종교는 이미 소멸했다. 이른바 올림포스의 신들은 오늘날 살아 있는 사람들 가운데 단 한 명의 숭배자도 거느리지 못하고 있다. 그들은 이제 신학의 영역이 아니라 문학과 취향의 영역에 속한다. 거기서 그들은 여전히 제자리를 지키고 있으며, 앞으로도 계속 지켜 나갈 것이다. 왜냐하면 그들은 고대와 근대를 막론하고 가장 빼어난 시와 예술 작품들과 너무도 긴밀히 얽혀 있어, 망각 속으로 사라질 수 없기 때문이다.\n\n우리는 고대인들로부터 전해 내려와 근대의 시인과 수필가와 웅변가들이 인유(引喩)하는 그 신들에 관한 이야기를 들려주고자 한다. 이로써 우리의 독자들은 상상력이 일찍이 빚어낸 가장 매혹적인 허구로 즐거움을 누리는 동시에, 자기 시대의 우아한 문학을 식견을 가지고 읽으려는 사람이라면 누구에게나 없어서는 안 될 지식을 갖추게 될 것이다.\n\n이 이야기들을 이해하려면, 그리스인들 사이에 널리 퍼져 있던 우주의 구조에 관한 관념과 친숙해질 필요가 있다. 로마인들, 그리고 그들을 통하여 다른 여러 민족이 학문과 종교를 물려받은 원천이 바로 이 그리스인들이기 때문이다.\n\n그리스인들은 대지가 평평하고 둥글다고 믿었으며, 자신들의 나라가 그 한가운데를 차지하고, 그 중심점은 신들의 거처인 올림포스 산이거나 신탁으로 유명한 델포이라고 여겼다.\n\n대지의 둥근 원반은 서쪽에서 동쪽으로 가로질러, 그들이 〈바다〉라고 부른 지중해와 그것에 이어지는 에욱세이노스[흑해]에 의해 두 개의 똑같은 부분으로 나뉘었는데, 이것이 그들이 알고 있던 유일한 바다였다.\n\n대지를 둘러싸고는 〈오케아노스 강〉이 흘렀으니, 그 물길은 대지의 서쪽에서는 남에서 북으로, 동쪽에서는 그 반대 방향으로 향했다. 그것은 폭풍이나 풍랑에 시달리는 일 없이 한결같고 고른 흐름으로 흘렀다. 바다와 대지의 모든 강은 그 물을 이 강에서 받았다.\n\n대지의 북쪽 부분에는 히페르보레오이라 불리는 행복한 종족이 살고 있다고 여겨졌으니, 그들은 높이 솟은 산들 너머에서 영원한 지복과 봄 속에 거주했다. 그 산들의 동굴에서 헬라스[그리스] 사람들을 얼어붙게 하는 살을 에는 북풍이 뿜어 나온다고 여겨졌다. 그들의 나라는 육로로도 바닷길로도 닿을 수 없었다. 그들은 질병이나 노년, 고역과 전쟁에서 벗어나 살았다. 무어는 우리에게 「히페르보레오이의 노래」를 남겼는데, 그것은 이렇게 시작된다.\n\n\"나는 해 밝은 깊은 곳의 땅에서 왔노라, 황금빛 정원이 빛나는 곳, 북풍이 잠에 잠겨 고요해져 소라 나팔을 결코 불지 않는 곳에서.\"\n\n대지의 남쪽, 오케아노스의 물줄기 가까이에는 히페르보레오이만큼이나 행복하고 덕스러운 종족이 살았다. 그들은 아이티오피아인이라 불렸다. 신들은 그들을 무척이나 어여삐 여겨, 때때로 올림포스의 거처를 떠나 그들의 제사와 잔치에 동참하러 가곤 했다.\n\n대지의 서쪽 가장자리, 오케아노스의 물줄기 곁에는 엘리시온 평원[엘리시움]이라 불리는 행복한 곳이 있었으니, 신들의 총애를 받은 필멸자들은 죽음을 맛보지 않은 채 그곳으로 옮겨져 지복의 불멸을 누렸다. 이 행복한 지역은 〈축복받은 들판〉이라고도, 〈축복받은 자들의 섬〉이라고도 불렸다.\n\n이로써 우리는 초기 시대의 그리스인들이 자기 나라의 동쪽과 남쪽, 혹은 지중해 연안 가까이에 있는 종족을 제외하고는 실재하는 어떤 민족에 관해서도 거의 알지 못했음을 알 수 있다. 한편 그들의 상상력은 이 바다의 서쪽 부분을 거인들과 괴물들과 마녀들로 채웠다. 또한 그다지 넓지 않다고 여겼을 법한 대지의 원반 둘레에는 신들의 각별한 총애를 받으며 행복과 장수를 누리는 여러 민족을 자리매김했다.\n\n새벽과 태양과 달은 동쪽에서 오케아노스로부터 떠올라 하늘을 가로질러 달리면서 신들과 인간에게 빛을 준다고 여겨졌다. 별들 또한 큰곰자리[북두]를 이루는 것들과 그 근처의 몇몇을 제외하고는 오케아노스의 물줄기에서 떠올랐다가 그 속으로 잠겼다. 그곳에서 태양신은 날개 달린 배에 올랐는데, 그 배는 그를 대지의 북쪽 부분을 돌아 동쪽에 있는 그의 떠오르는 자리로 도로 실어다 주었다. 밀턴은 「코머스」에서 이를 인유한다.\n\n\"이제 낮의 도금한 수레가 그 황금 굴대를 가라앉히노니 가파른 대서양의 물줄기 속으로, 비스듬한 태양은 그 솟구치는 빛살을 어두운 하늘 끝에 쏘아 올리며, 동쪽에 있는 그의 침실, 또 다른 종착점을 향해 걸음을 옮긴다.\"\n\n신들의 거처는 테살리아에 있는 올림포스 산의 정상이었다. 〈사계절〉이라 불리는 여신들이 지키는 구름의 문이 열려 천인(天人)들이 대지로 나아가는 것을 허락하고, 돌아올 때 그들을 맞아들였다. 신들은 저마다 따로 거처를 두고 있었으나, 부름을 받으면 모두 제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]의 궁전으로 모여들었으며, 평소 거처가 대지나 물속, 혹은 저승인 신들 역시 그러했다. 또한 올림포스 왕의 궁전 대전(大殿)에서 신들은 날마다 그들의 음식과 음료인 암브로시아와 넥타르로 잔치를 벌였으니, 후자는 사랑스러운 여신 헤베가 돌아가며 따라 주었다. 이곳에서 그들은 하늘과 땅의 일을 이야기했고, 넥타르를 들이켜는 동안 음악의 신 아폴론이 그의 리라 가락으로 그들을 즐겁게 하면, 무사 여신들이 화답하는 노래로 답했다. 해가 지면 신들은 저마다의 거처로 물러가 잠들었다.\n\n「오디세이아」의 다음 시구는 호메로스가 올림포스를 어떻게 그렸는지 보여 줄 것이다.\n\n\"그렇게 말하고서, 푸른 눈의 여신 미네르바는 신들의 영원한 거처로 일컬어지는 올림포스로 올라갔으니, 그곳은 폭풍도 어지럽히지 못하고, 비도 적시지 못하며, 눈도 범하지 못하고, 다만 고요히 그 넓은 하늘이 구름 한 점 없이 더없이 맑은 낮으로 빛난다. 그곳에서 신성한 거주자들은 영원히 기뻐하노라.\" 쿠퍼.\n\n여신들의 옷과 의복의 다른 부분들은 미네르바와 〈우아미의 여신〉들이 짜냈고, 좀 더 단단한 성질의 것은 모두 갖가지 금속으로 만들어졌다. 불카누스는 올림포스에서 건축가이자 대장장이, 무기 제작자, 전차 제작자이며, 온갖 일을 도맡은 장인이었다. 그는 신들의 집을 청동으로 지었고, 그들이 공기나 물 위를 디디며 바람의 속도로, 아니 심지어 생각의 속도로 이곳저곳을 옮겨 다니게 해 주는 황금 신발을 만들어 주었다. 그는 또한 신들의 전차를 공중으로, 혹은 바다 표면을 따라 휘몰아 가는 천상의 준마들에게 청동 편자를 박았다. 그는 자기 솜씨로 만든 것에 스스로 움직이는 힘을 부여할 수 있어, 세발솥(의자와 탁자)들이 저절로 천상의 대전을 드나들 수 있었다. 그는 자신의 시중을 들도록 만든 황금 시녀들에게마저 지성을 부여했다.\n\n제우스, 곧 유피테르(제우스)는 신들과 인간의 아버지로 일컬어지지만, 그 자신에게도 시작이 있었다. 사투르누스(크로노스)가 그의 아버지였고, 레아(옵스)가 그의 어머니였다. 사투르누스와 레아는 티탄족에 속했는데, 이들은 대지와 하늘의 자식이었으며, 대지와 하늘은 카오스에서 솟아났으니, 이에 관해서는 다음 장에서 좀 더 자세히 이야기하겠다.\n\n또 다른 우주 발생설, 곧 천지 창조에 관한 설명이 있는데, 그에 따르면 대지와 에레보스와 사랑이 최초의 존재였다. 사랑(에로스)은 카오스 위를 떠다니던 밤의 알에서 나왔다. 그는 자신의 화살과 횃불로 만물을 꿰뚫고 생기를 불어넣어, 생명과 기쁨을 빚어냈다.\n\n사투르누스와 레아만이 유일한 티탄은 아니었다. 다른 이들도 있었으니, 남신으로는 오케아노스, 히페리온, 이아페토스, 오피온이 있었고, 여신으로는 테미스, 므네모시네, 에우리노메가 있었다. 그들은 더 오래된 신들로 일컬어지며, 그들의 지배권은 훗날 다른 신들에게 넘어갔다. 사투르누스는 제우스에게, 오케아노스는 넵투누스에게, 히페리온은 아폴론에게 자리를 내주었다. 히페리온은 태양과 달과 새벽의 아버지였다. 그러므로 그는 본디 태양신으로서, 훗날 아폴론에게 부여된 광채와 아름다움을 지닌 모습으로 그려진다.\n\n\"히페리온의 곱슬머리, 유피테르 그 자신의 이마.\" 셰익스피어.\n\n오피온과 에우리노메는 사투르누스와 레아에게 폐위되기 전까지 올림포스를 다스렸다. 밀턴은 「실낙원」에서 이들을 인유한다. 그는 이교도들이 인간의 유혹과 타락에 관해 어느 정도 알고 있었던 듯하다고 말한다.\n\n\"그리고 꾸며 내기를, 그들이 오피온이라 부른 뱀이 에우리노메와 더불어 (어쩌면 널리 세력을 뻗친 이브일지도 모를 그녀와 더불어) 처음에 드높은 올림포스를 다스렸으나, 거기서 사투르누스에게 쫓겨났다 하더라.\"\n\n사투르누스에 관해 전해지는 묘사는 그다지 일관되지 않다. 한편으로 그의 치세는 순결과 순수의 황금시대였다고 일컬어지면서도, 다른 한편으로 그는 자기 자식들을 집어삼킨 괴물로 묘사되기 때문이다. 그러나 제우스는 이 운명을 모면했으니, 장성한 뒤 메티스(지혜)와 혼인했고, 그녀는 사투르누스에게 약을 한 모금 먹여 그가 삼킨 자식들을 토해 내게 만들었다. 이제 제우스는 형제자매들과 더불어 그들의 아버지 사투르누스와 그 형제인 티탄족에게 반란을 일으켜, 그들을 무찌르고 일부는 타르타로스에 가두었으며, 다른 이들에게는 또 다른 형벌을 내렸다. 아틀라스는 두 어깨로 하늘을 떠받치는 형벌을 받았다.\n\n사투르누스가 폐위되자, 제우스는 형제인 넵투누스(포세이돈), 플루토(디스)와 더불어 그의 영토를 나누었다. 제우스의 몫은 하늘이었고, 넵투누스의 몫은 바다였으며, 플루토의 몫은 죽은 자들의 영역이었다. 대지와 올림포스는 공동의 소유였다. 제우스는 신들과 인간의 왕이었다. 천둥이 그의 무기였고, 그는 불카누스가 만들어 준 아이기스라는 방패를 지녔다. 독수리가 그가 가장 아끼는 새였으며, 그의 벼락을 날랐다.\n\n유노(헤라)는 제우스의 아내이자 신들의 여왕이었다. 무지개의 여신 이리스가 그녀의 시중을 들며 전령 노릇을 했다. 공작이 그녀가 가장 아끼는 새였다.\n\n천상의 장인 불카누스(헤파이스토스)는 제우스와 유노의 아들이었다. 그는 절름발이로 태어났고, 그 어머니는 그의 모습을 보고 너무도 언짢아하여 그를 하늘 밖으로 내던졌다. 다른 설명에 따르면, 제우스와 유노 사이에 일어난 다툼에서 불카누스가 어머니 편을 들었기에 제우스가 그를 발로 차서 내쫓았다고 한다. 이 설명에 따르면 불카누스의 절름거림은 그 추락의 결과였다. 그는 꼬박 하루를 떨어져 마침내 렘노스 섬에 내려앉았는데, 그 섬은 그때부터 그에게 바쳐진 신성한 곳이 되었다. 밀턴은 「실낙원」 제1권에서 이 이야기를 인유한다.\n\n\". . . 아침부터 한낮까지 그는 떨어졌고, 한낮부터 이슬 맺힌 저녁까지, 여름날 하루를 다 채워 떨어졌으며, 지는 해와 함께 하늘 꼭대기에서 떨어지는 별처럼 곤두박질쳐 에게 해의 섬, 렘노스에 내려앉았다.\"\n\n전쟁의 신 마르스(아레스)는 제우스와 유노의 아들이었다.\n\n활쏘기와 예언과 음악의 신 포이보스 아폴론은 제우스와 라토나의 아들이며, 디아나(아르테미스)의 오라비였다. 그의 누이 디아나가 달의 여신이었듯이, 그는 태양의 신이었다.\n\n사랑과 아름다움의 여신 베누스(아프로디테)는 제우스와 디오네의 딸이었다. 다른 이들은 베누스가 바다의 거품에서 솟아났다고 말한다. 서풍이 그녀를 물결 위로 실어 키프로스 섬으로 데려갔고, 그곳에서 〈사계절〉 여신들이 그녀를 맞아 단장시킨 뒤 신들의 회합으로 인도했다. 모두가 그녀의 아름다움에 매혹되어 저마다 그녀를 아내로 달라고 청했다. 제우스는 불카누스가 벼락을 벼려 준 공로에 대한 보답으로 그녀를 그에게 주었다. 그리하여 여신들 가운데 가장 아름다운 이가 신들 가운데 가장 못생긴 이의 아내가 되었다. 베누스는 케스토스라 불리는 수놓은 띠를 지녔는데, 그것은 사랑을 불러일으키는 힘이 있었다. 그녀가 가장 아끼는 새는 백조와 비둘기였고, 그녀에게 바쳐진 식물은 장미와 도금양이었다.\n\n사랑의 신 쿠피도(에로스)는 베누스의 아들이었다. 그는 그녀의 한결같은 동반자였으며, 활과 화살로 무장하고서 신들과 인간 모두의 가슴에 욕망의 화살을 쏘았다. 안테로스라는 신도 있었는데, 그는 때로 무시당한 사랑을 갚아 주는 복수자로, 때로는 서로 주고받는 애정의 상징으로 그려졌다. 그에 관해서는 다음과 같은 전설이 전해진다.\n\n베누스가 테미스에게 자기 아들 에로스가 늘 어린아이로만 머물러 있다고 하소연하자, 테미스는 그것이 그가 외톨이이기 때문이며, 만일 그에게 형제가 있다면 쑥쑥 자랄 것이라고 일러 주었다. 얼마 지나지 않아 안테로스가 태어났고, 곧바로 에로스가 몸집과 힘이 부쩍 자라나는 것이 보였다.\n\n지혜의 여신 미네르바(팔라스, 아테네)는 어머니 없이 제우스에게서 난 자식이었다. 그녀는 완전히 무장한 채 그의 머리에서 솟아났다. 그녀가 가장 아끼는 새는 올빼미였고, 그녀에게 바쳐진 식물은 올리브였다.\n\n바이런은 「차일드 해럴드」에서 미네르바의 탄생을 이렇게 인유한다.\n\n\"폭군은 폭군에게만 정복될 수 있는가, 자유는 콜룸비아가 보았던 것 같은, 무장한 채 더럽혀지지 않은 팔라스로 솟아났을 때의 그런 투사도 그런 자식도 찾지 못하는가? 아니면 그런 정신들은 거친 들에서, 가지치기 않은 깊은 숲속에서, 길러 주는 자연이 어린 워싱턴에게 미소 지었던 폭포의 굉음 한가운데서 길러져야만 하는가? 대지는 그 가슴속에 이제 그런 씨앗을 더 품지 못하고, 유럽에는 그런 기슭이 없는가?\"\n\n메르쿠리우스(헤르메스)는 제우스와 마이아의 아들이었다. 그는 상업과 씨름을 비롯한 여러 체육 활동을 주관했으며, 심지어 도둑질에 이르기까지, 요컨대 솜씨와 재간을 필요로 하는 모든 일을 관장했다. 그는 제우스의 전령이었으며, 날개 달린 모자와 날개 달린 신발을 신었다. 그는 두 마리 뱀이 휘감긴 지팡이를 손에 들었으니, 그것을 카두케우스라 불렀다.\n\n메르쿠리우스가 리라를 발명했다고 한다. 어느 날 그는 거북 한 마리를 발견하고는 그 등딱지를 떼어 내어, 마주 보는 가장자리에 구멍을 뚫고 그 사이로 아마실을 꿰니, 악기가 완성되었다. 그 줄은 아홉 무사 여신을 기려 아홉 가닥이었다. 메르쿠리우스는 그 리라를 아폴론에게 주고, 그 대가로 그에게서 카두케우스를 받았다.\n\n케레스(데메테르)는 사투르누스와 레아의 딸이었다. 그녀에게는 프로세르피나(페르세포네)라는 딸이 있었는데, 그녀는 플루토의 아내이자 죽은 자들의 영역의 여왕이 되었다. 케레스는 농경을 주관했다.\n\n포도주의 신 바쿠스(디오니소스)는 제우스와 세멜레의 아들이었다. 그는 포도주의 취하게 하는 힘뿐만 아니라 그 사교적이고 이로운 영향력까지도 나타내므로, 문명의 촉진자이자 입법자이며 평화를 사랑하는 자로 여겨진다.\n\n무사 여신들은 제우스와 므네모시네(기억)의 딸들이었다. 그들은 노래를 주관하고 기억을 일깨웠다. 그들은 모두 아홉이었으며, 저마다 문학과 예술, 혹은 학문의 특정한 영역에 대한 으뜸 자리를 맡았다. 칼리오페는 서사시의 무사였고, 클리오는 역사의, 에우테르페는 서정시의, 멜포메네는 비극의, 테르프시코레는 합창 무용과 노래의, 에라토는 연애시의, 폴리힘니아는 종교시의, 우라니아는 천문학의, 탈리아는 희극의 무사였다.\n\n〈우아미의 여신〉들은 잔치와 춤, 그리고 온갖 사교의 즐거움과 우아한 기예를 주관하는 여신들이었다. 그들은 모두 셋이었다. 그들의 이름은 에우프로시네, 아글라이아, 탈리아였다.\n\n스펜서는 〈우아미의 여신〉들의 소임을 이렇게 묘사한다.\n\n\"이 셋은 사람들에게 온갖 우아한 선물을 베푸나니 몸을 꾸미거나 마음을 아름답게 하는 것들, 그들을 사랑스럽거나 호감 가게 보이게 하는 것들, 이를테면 단정한 몸가짐, 다정한 환대, 상냥한 자태, 사람을 묶어 주는 친절한 도리, 그리고 예의의 온갖 격식이라. 그들은 우리에게 가르치나니 온갖 신분과 부류, 낮은 이에게, 높은 이에게, 벗에게, 적에게 우리가 어떻게 처신해야 하는지를. 사람들은 이 재간을 예절이라 부르노라.\"\n\n운명의 여신들 또한 셋이었으니, 클로토, 라케시스, 아트로포스였다. 그들의 소임은 인간 운명의 실을 잣는 것이었으며, 그들은 가위로 무장하여 마음 내키는 대로 그 실을 끊어 버렸다. 그들은 테미스(법)의 딸들이었는데, 테미스는 제우스에게 조언을 하기 위해 그의 옥좌 곁에 앉아 있다.\n\n에리니에스, 곧 복수의 여신들은 공적 정의를 모면하거나 거역한 자들의 죄악을 그들만의 은밀한 가시로 벌하는 세 여신이었다. 복수의 여신들의 머리에는 뱀들이 화관처럼 휘감겨 있었고, 그들의 모습 전체가 무시무시하고 소름 끼쳤다. 그들의 이름은 알렉토, 티시포네, 메가이라였다. 그들은 에우메니데스라고도 불렸다.\n\n네메시스 또한 복수의 여신이었다. 그녀는 신들의 정당한 분노를, 특히 거만하고 오만한 자들을 향한 분노를 나타낸다.\n\n판은 가축 떼와 목자들의 신이었다. 그가 가장 좋아한 거처는 아르카디아였다.\n\n사티로스들은 숲과 들판의 신들이었다. 그들은 뻣뻣한 털로 뒤덮여 있고, 머리에는 짧게 돋아난 뿔로 장식되었으며, 발은 염소의 발 같았다고 여겨졌다.\n\n모모스는 웃음의 신이었고, 플루토스는 부(富)의 신이었다.\n로마의 신들\n\n# ROMAN DIVINITIES\n\n앞에서 든 신들은 그리스의 신들로, 로마인들에게도 받아들여진 것들이다. 다음에 드는 신들은 로마 신화에 고유한 것들이다.\n\n사투르누스는 고대 이탈리아의 신이었다. 사람들은 그를 그리스의 신 크로노스와 동일시하려 했으며, 제우스에게 폐위된 뒤 그가 이탈리아로 달아나 그곳에서 이른바 황금시대 동안 다스렸다고 꾸며 냈다. 그의 자애로운 치세를 기려 해마다 겨울철에 사투르날리아 축제가 열렸다. 그때에는 모든 공무가 중단되고, 선전포고와 사형 집행이 미뤄졌으며, 벗들이 서로 선물을 주고받고, 노예들에게는 큰 자유가 허락되었다. 노예들에게 잔치가 베풀어져 그들이 식탁에 앉으면 그 주인들이 시중을 들었으니, 이는 인간의 천부적 평등을, 그리고 사투르누스의 치세에는 만물이 모두에게 똑같이 속했음을 보여 주기 위함이었다.\n\n사투르누스의 손자인 파우누스는 들판과 목자들의 신으로, 또한 예언의 신으로 숭배되었다. 복수형인 파우니라는 이름은 그리스의 사티로스들처럼 장난기 어린 신들의 한 부류를 가리켰다.\n\n퀴리누스는 전쟁의 신으로, 로마의 건국자 로물루스가 죽은 뒤 신들 사이의 한 자리로 드높여진 것에 다름 아니라고 한다.\n\n벨로나는 전쟁의 여신이었다.\n\n테르미누스는 경계표의 신이었다. 그의 상은 들판의 경계를 표시하기 위해 땅에 박아 둔 투박한 돌이나 기둥이었다.\n\n팔레스는 가축과 목초지를 주관하는 여신이었다.\n\n포모나는 과일나무를 주관했다.\n\n플로라는 꽃의 여신이었다.\n\n루키나는 출산의 여신이었다.\n\n베스타(그리스의 헤스티아)는 공적·사적 화로를 주관하는 신이었다. 베스탈리스라 불리는 여섯 처녀 사제가 돌보는 신성한 불이 그녀의 신전에서 타올랐다. 도시의 안위가 이 불의 보존과 결부되어 있다고 여겨졌으므로, 만일 처녀들이 그 불을 꺼뜨리는 태만을 범하면 가혹하게 벌받았고, 그 불은 햇빛에서 다시 지펴졌다.\n\n리베르는 바쿠스의 라틴식 이름이고, 물키베르는 불카누스의 라틴식 이름이다.\n\n야누스는 하늘의 문지기였다. 그는 한 해를 여니, 첫 번째 달은 그의 이름을 따서 붙여졌다. 그는 성문을 지키는 수호신이었는데, 그런 까닭에 흔히 두 개의 머리를 지닌 모습으로 그려진다. 모든 문은 두 방향을 바라보기 때문이다. 로마에는 그의 신전이 많았다. 전시에는 가장 중요한 신전의 문이 늘 열려 있었다. 평화로울 때에는 닫혔으나, 누마의 치세와 아우구스투스의 치세 사이에 그 문이 닫힌 것은 단 한 번뿐이었다.\n\n페나테스는 가족의 안녕과 번영을 보살핀다고 여겨진 신들이었다. 그들의 이름은 그들에게 바쳐진 식료품 저장실인 페누스에서 유래했다. 모든 가장은 자기 집 페나테스의 사제였다.\n\n라레스, 곧 라르 또한 가정의 신이었으나, 필멸자들의 신격화된 영혼으로 여겨졌다는 점에서 페나테스와 달랐다. 가족의 라르는 후손을 지켜보고 보호하는 조상들의 혼령이라 여겨졌다. 레무르와 라르바라는 말이 우리말의 〈유령〉이라는 낱말에 좀 더 가깝게 들어맞는다.\n\n로마인들은 모든 남자에게 저마다의 게니우스가 있고 모든 여자에게 저마다의 유노가 있다고 믿었다. 곧, 그들에게 생명을 준 정령으로서, 평생 그들의 수호자로 여겨지는 존재였다. 생일이면 남자들은 자기 게니우스에게, 여자들은 자기 유노에게 제물을 바쳤다.\n\n근대의 한 시인은 로마의 몇몇 신들을 이렇게 인유한다.\n\n\"포모나는 과수원을 사랑하고, 리베르는 포도밭을 사랑하며, 팔레스는 소들의 숨결로 따뜻해진 짚으로 지은 헛간을 사랑하고, 베누스는 4월의 상아빛 달빛 아래, 밤나무 그늘 밑에서 언약을 맺은 젊은이와 처녀의 속삭임을 사랑한다.\" —매콜리, 「카피스의 예언」.\n\n비고. 고유명사에서 끝에 오는 e와 es는 소리 내어 발음해야 함에 유의해야 한다. 따라서 키벨레와 페나테스는 세 음절로 된 낱말이다. 그러나 프로세르피네와 테베는 예외로, 영어 낱말처럼 발음해야 한다. 이 책 끝의 찾아보기에서 우리는 강세가 필요해 보이는 모든 낱말에 강세 음절을 표시할 것이다.\n\n# PROMETHEUS AND PANDORA\n\n세계의 창조란 그곳에 거주하는 인간으로서는 자연히 가장 생생한 흥미를 불러일으킬 수밖에 없는 문제다. 고대의 이교도들은 우리가 성서의 책장에서 얻는 그 주제에 관한 정보를 갖지 못했던 까닭에, 그들 나름의 방식으로 이 이야기를 전했는데, 그 내용은 다음과 같다.\n\n땅과 바다와 하늘이 창조되기 이전, 만물은 하나의 모습을 하고 있었으니, 우리는 이것을 카오스[혼돈]라 부른다. 그것은 혼란스럽고 형태가 없는 덩어리, 죽은 무게에 지나지 않는 것이었으되, 그 안에는 만물의 씨앗이 잠들어 있었다. 땅과 바다와 공기가 모두 한데 뒤섞여 있었던 까닭에, 땅은 단단하지 않았고, 바다는 흐르지 않았으며, 공기는 투명하지 않았다. 마침내 신과 자연이 개입하여 이 불화에 종지부를 찍었으니, 땅을 바다에서 갈라놓고, 하늘을 그 둘에서 갈라놓았다. 불의 부분은 가장 가벼운 까닭에 위로 솟구쳐 올라 하늘을 이루었고, 공기는 무게와 위치에서 그다음이었다. 땅은 더 무거운 까닭에 아래로 가라앉았고, 물은 가장 낮은 자리를 차지하여 땅을 떠받쳤다.\n\n여기서 어떤 신이 — 그가 누구인지는 알려져 있지 않다 — 땅을 정돈하고 배치하는 데에 그 호의를 베풀었다. 그는 강과 만에 제자리를 지정해 주었고, 산을 솟아오르게 했으며, 골짜기를 파냈고, 숲과 샘과 비옥한 들판과 돌 많은 평원을 나누어 놓았다. 공기가 맑아지자, 별들이 나타나기 시작했고, 물고기는 바다를, 새는 공기를, 네발짐승은 땅을 차지하게 되었다.\n\n그러나 더 고귀한 동물이 필요했고, 그리하여 인간이 만들어졌다. 창조자가 신성한 재료로 인간을 빚었는지, 아니면 하늘에서 갈라져 나온 지 얼마 되지 않은 땅에 여전히 어떤 천상의 씨앗들이 숨어 있었는지는 알려져 있지 않다. 프로메테우스는 이 땅의 일부를 취하여 물과 함께 반죽해, 신들의 모습을 본떠 인간을 만들었다. 그는 인간에게 곧은 자세를 주었으니, 다른 모든 동물이 얼굴을 아래로 향하여 땅을 바라보는 동안, 인간은 얼굴을 하늘로 들어 별들을 응시하게 되었다.\n\n프로메테우스는 티탄족 가운데 하나였으니, 그들은 인간이 창조되기 이전에 땅에 거주하던 거대한 종족이었다. 그와 그의 형제 에피메테우스에게는 인간을 만들고, 인간과 그 밖의 모든 동물에게 생존에 필요한 능력을 부여하는 임무가 맡겨졌다. 에피메테우스가 이 일을 떠맡았고, 프로메테우스는 일이 끝났을 때 그 작업을 살펴보기로 했다. 이에 따라 에피메테우스는 여러 동물에게 용기와 힘과 빠르기와 영리함이라는 갖가지 선물을 베풀기 시작했으니, 어떤 동물에게는 날개를, 다른 동물에게는 발톱을, 또 다른 동물에게는 딱딱한 껍데기를 주는 식이었다. 그러나 다른 모든 동물보다 우월해야 할 인간을 위해 베풀 차례가 되었을 때, 에피메테우스는 이미 그 자원을 너무도 헤프게 써 버린 나머지 인간에게 줄 것이 아무것도 남아 있지 않았다. 당황한 그는 그의 형제 프로메테우스에게 의지했고, 프로메테우스는 아테나[로마의 〈미네르바〉]의 도움을 받아 하늘로 올라가 태양의 마차에서 횃불을 밝혀, 인간에게 불을 가져다주었다. 이 선물로 인해 인간은 다른 모든 동물을 능히 능가하게 되었다. 불은 인간으로 하여금 동물을 굴복시킬 무기를 만들 수 있게 했고, 땅을 경작할 도구를 만들 수 있게 했으며, 거처를 따뜻하게 하여 기후에 비교적 구애받지 않게 했고, 마침내 여러 기예를 들여오고 화폐를 주조하여 교역과 상업의 수단을 갖추게 했다.\n\n여자는 아직 만들어지지 않았다. 그 이야기(자못 터무니없다!)에 따르면, 제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]가 여자를 만들어, 하늘에서 불을 훔친 그 주제넘음을 벌하고자 프로메테우스와 그의 형제에게 보냈으며, 또한 그 선물을 받아들인 인간을 벌하고자 했다는 것이다. 최초의 여자는 판도라라 불렸다. 그녀는 하늘에서 만들어졌으니, 신들이 저마다 무언가를 보태어 그녀를 완성했다. 아프로디테[로마의 〈베누스〉]는 그녀에게 아름다움을, 헤르메스[로마의 〈메르쿠리우스〉]는 설득력을, 아폴론은 음악을 주는 식이었다. 이렇게 갖추어진 그녀는 땅으로 보내져 에피메테우스에게 바쳐졌고, 그는 제우스와 그의 선물을 경계하라는 형제의 충고를 받았음에도 기꺼이 그녀를 받아들였다. 에피메테우스의 집에는 항아리가 하나 있었으니, 그 안에는 인간을 새로운 거처에 맞게 갖추는 과정에서 쓸 일이 없었던 어떤 해로운 것들이 담겨 있었다. 판도라는 이 항아리에 무엇이 들어 있는지 알고 싶은 간절한 호기심에 사로잡혔고, 어느 날 뚜껑을 살그머니 열어 안을 들여다보았다. 그 즉시 가엾은 인간을 괴롭힐 수많은 재앙이 쏟아져 나왔으니 — 육체를 괴롭힐 통풍과 류머티즘과 복통이, 정신을 괴롭힐 질투와 악의와 복수심이 — 사방으로 멀리 흩어졌다. 판도라는 서둘러 뚜껑을 다시 덮었으나, 아아! 항아리의 내용물은 단 하나만을 남기고 모조리 빠져나간 뒤였으니, 바닥에 남은 그 하나는 바로 희망이었다. 그리하여 오늘날 우리가 보듯, 어떤 악이 세상에 퍼져 있든 희망만은 결코 우리를 온전히 떠나지 않으며, 우리가 그것을 지니고 있는 한 다른 어떤 재앙도 우리를 완전히 비참하게 만들 수는 없다.\n\n또 다른 이야기에 따르면, 판도라는 제우스가 인간을 축복하고자 선의로 보낸 것이며, 그녀에게는 혼인 선물이 담긴 상자가 주어졌는데, 그 안에는 신들이 저마다 어떤 축복을 넣어 두었다고 한다. 그녀가 부주의하게 상자를 열자, 축복이 모두 빠져나갔고, 오직 희망만이 남았다. 이 이야기가 앞의 것보다 더 그럴듯해 보이는데, 어찌하여 희망처럼 그토록 귀한 보석이, 앞의 진술에서처럼 온갖 종류의 악으로 가득 찬 항아리 속에 담겨 있을 수 있었겠는가.\n\n이렇게 하여 세계에 거주자가 갖추어지자, 첫 번째 시대는 순수와 행복의 시대였으니, 이를 황금시대라 부른다. 진리와 정의가 널리 행해졌으되, 법으로 강요된 것도 아니었고, 위협하거나 벌하는 어떤 관리가 있는 것도 아니었다. 숲은 아직 배의 목재를 마련하느라 그 나무들을 빼앗긴 적이 없었고, 사람들도 도시 둘레에 요새를 쌓은 적이 없었다. 칼이나 창이나 투구 따위의 것은 존재하지 않았다. 땅은 인간이 밭을 갈거나 씨를 뿌리는 수고 없이도 인간에게 필요한 모든 것을 내주었다. 영원한 봄이 다스렸고, 꽃은 씨앗 없이도 피어났으며, 강에는 젖과 포도주가 흘렀고, 떡갈나무에서는 노란 꿀이 방울방울 떨어졌다.\n\n이어 은의 시대가 뒤따랐으니, 황금시대보다는 못하되 청동시대보다는 나았다. 제우스는 봄을 짧게 줄이고, 한 해를 계절들로 나누었다. 그리하여 처음으로 인간은 더위와 추위의 극단을 견뎌야 했고, 집이 필요해졌다. 동굴이 최초의 거처였고, 숲의 잎이 무성한 은신처와 잔가지를 엮어 만든 오두막이 그 뒤를 이었다. 작물은 더 이상 심지 않으면 자라지 않게 되었다. 농부는 씨를 뿌려야 했고, 힘겹게 일하는 소는 쟁기를 끌어야 했다.\n\n다음으로 청동시대가 왔으니, 기질이 더 거칠고 무력의 다툼에 더 쉽게 기울었으되, 아주 사악하지는 않았다. 가장 모질고 가장 나빴던 것은 철의 시대였다. 죄악이 홍수처럼 터져 나왔고, 정숙과 진리와 명예가 달아났다. 그 자리에 사기와 교활함과 폭력과 사악한 이득의 사랑이 들어섰다. 그리하여 뱃사람들이 바람에 돛을 펼쳤고, 나무들은 산에서 뽑혀 배의 용골 노릇을 하며 대양의 얼굴을 괴롭혔다. 그때까지 공동으로 경작되던 땅이 사유지로 나뉘기 시작했다. 사람들은 표면이 내주는 것에 만족하지 못하고, 그 내장을 파헤쳐 그 안에서 금속의 광석을 캐내야만 했다. 해로운 철과, 그보다 더 해로운 금이 만들어졌다. 그 둘을 모두 무기로 쓰는 전쟁이 일어났으니, 손님은 친구의 집에서도 안전하지 못했고, 사위와 장인이, 형제와 자매가, 남편과 아내가 서로를 믿지 못했다. 아들들은 유산을 차지하고자 아비가 죽기를 바랐고, 가족의 사랑은 땅에 엎어졌다. 땅은 살육으로 젖었고, 신들은 하나둘 그곳을 버렸으니, 마침내 아스트라이아 홀로 남았다가, 끝내 그녀마저 떠나갔다.\n* 아스트라이아: 정의의 여신.\n\n제우스는 이러한 사태를 보고 분노로 불타올랐다. 그는 신들을 회의에 소집했다. 신들은 부름에 응하여, 하늘의 궁전으로 가는 길을 따라 나섰다. 맑은 밤이면 누구나 볼 수 있는 그 길은 하늘의 얼굴을 가로질러 뻗어 있으며, 은하수라 불린다. 그 길을 따라 이름난 신들의 궁전이 늘어서 있고, 하늘의 평민들은 그 양옆에 따로 떨어져 산다. 제우스는 회의에서 연설했다. 그는 땅 위의 끔찍한 사태를 낱낱이 펼쳐 보였고, 끝으로 그곳의 거주자를 모조리 멸하고, 첫 번째와는 다른 새로운 종족, 곧 삶에 더 합당하고 신들을 훨씬 더 잘 섬길 종족을 마련하겠다는 그의 뜻을 알리며 끝맺었다. 그렇게 말하며 그는 벼락을 집어 들어 세계를 향해 던져 불태워 멸하려 했으나, 그러한 큰불이 자칫 하늘 자체에 불을 붙일지도 모른다는 위험을 떠올리고는 계획을 바꾸어, 세계를 물에 잠기게 하기로 작정했다. 구름을 흩어 버리는 북풍은 사슬에 묶였고, 남풍이 풀려나와 곧 칠흑 같은 어둠의 외투로 하늘의 온 얼굴을 뒤덮었다. 한데로 몰린 구름이 굉음을 내며 울렸고, 빗줄기가 억수로 쏟아졌으며, 작물이 쓰러졌고, 농부의 한 해 수고가 한 시간 만에 사라졌다. 제우스는 자신의 물만으로는 만족하지 못하고, 그의 형제 포세이돈[로마의 〈넵투누스〉]을 불러 그의 물로 도와 달라 했다. 포세이돈은 강들을 풀어놓아 땅 위로 쏟아부었다. 동시에 그는 지진으로 땅을 들어 올려, 대양의 역류를 해안 위로 끌어들였다. 가축 떼와 무리와 사람과 집이 휩쓸려 갔고, 신전은 그 성스러운 울타리와 더불어 더럽혀졌다. 어떤 건물이 무너지지 않고 서 있더라도, 그것은 물에 잠겼고, 그 작은 탑들은 물결 아래 숨어 버렸다. 이제 온통 바다였으니, 해안 없는 바다였다. 여기저기 튀어나온 언덕 꼭대기에 한 사람씩 남았고, 몇몇은 배를 타고서, 얼마 전까지 쟁기를 몰던 곳에서 노를 저었다. 물고기는 나무 꼭대기 사이를 헤엄쳤고, 닻은 정원 안으로 내려졌다. 방금 전까지 우아한 새끼 양들이 뛰놀던 곳에서, 둔중한 바다표범이 장난을 쳤다. 늑대가 양 떼 사이를 헤엄쳤고, 누런 사자와 호랑이가 물속에서 허우적거렸다. 멧돼지의 힘도 그에게 소용이 없었고, 사슴의 빠르기도 마찬가지였다. 새들은 쉴 만한 땅을 찾지 못한 채 지친 날개로 물 위에 떨어졌다. 물이 살려 둔 그 생명들은 굶주림의 먹이가 되었다.\n\n모든 산 가운데 오직 파르나소스만이 물결 위로 솟아 있었고, 그곳에서 프로메테우스의 혈통인 데우칼리온과 그의 아내 피라가 피난처를 찾았으니 — 그는 의로운 사람이었고, 그녀는 신들을 충실히 섬기는 자였다. 제우스는 이 한 쌍 외에 살아남은 자가 아무도 없음을 보고, 그들의 해롭지 않은 삶과 경건한 처신을 떠올려, 북풍에게 구름을 몰아내어 땅에게 하늘을, 하늘에게 땅을 드러내라 명했다. 포세이돈 또한 트리톤에게 명하여 그의 소라고둥을 불어 물들에게 물러나라는 신호를 울리게 했다. 물은 명에 따랐고, 바다는 그 해안으로, 강은 그 물길로 돌아갔다. 그러자 데우칼리온이 피라에게 이렇게 말했다. \"오 아내여, 유일하게 살아남은 여인이여, 처음에는 혈연과 혼인의 끈으로, 이제는 함께 겪은 위험으로 나와 맺어진 그대여, 우리에게 우리의 선조 프로메테우스의 힘이 있어, 그가 처음 종족을 만들었던 것처럼 우리도 종족을 새로 만들 수 있다면 좋으련만! 그러나 우리에게 그럴 힘이 없으니, 저기 저 신전을 찾아가, 우리가 무엇을 해야 할지 신들에게 여쭙도록 합시다.\" 그들은 진흙으로 흉하게 더럽혀진 신전 안으로 들어가, 불이 타지 않는 제단으로 다가갔다. 그곳에서 그들은 땅에 엎드려, 그들의 비참한 처지를 어떻게 되돌릴 수 있을지 알려 달라고 여신에게 기도했다. 신탁이 답했다. \"머리를 가리고 옷을 풀어 헤친 채 신전을 떠나, 너희 어머니의 뼈를 너희 등 뒤로 던져라.\" 그들은 그 말을 듣고 놀랐다. 피라가 먼저 침묵을 깨뜨렸다. \"우리는 따를 수 없습니다. 우리는 감히 부모의 유해를 더럽힐 수 없습니다.\" 그들은 숲의 가장 짙은 그늘을 찾아가, 그 신탁을 마음속에서 곱씹었다. 마침내 데우칼리온이 입을 열었다. \"내 영민함이 나를 속이는 것이 아니라면, 그 명은 우리가 불경을 저지르지 않고도 따를 수 있는 것이오. 땅은 만물의 위대한 어머니요, 돌은 그녀의 뼈이니, 이것을 우리가 등 뒤로 던질 수 있을 것이며, 나는 이것이 신탁의 뜻이라 생각하오. 적어도 한번 해 본다고 해될 것은 없소.\" 그들은 얼굴을 가리고 옷을 풀어 헤친 채 돌을 주워 등 뒤로 던졌다. 그 돌들은(이야기하기에 놀라운 일이지만) 물러지기 시작하더니 형태를 갖추기 시작했다. 차츰 그것들은 조각가의 손에서 절반쯤 다듬어진 덩어리처럼, 인간의 형상을 어렴풋이 닮아 갔다. 그 주위에 있던 물기와 진흙은 살이 되었고, 돌의 부분은 뼈가 되었으며, 핏줄은 그 이름을 그대로 지닌 채 핏줄로 남았으되, 다만 그 쓰임만 바뀌었다. 남자의 손으로 던진 것은 남자가 되었고, 여자가 던진 것은 여자가 되었다. 그것은 모진 종족이었고, 노동에 잘 맞았으니, 오늘날 우리 자신이 그러하여 우리의 기원을 분명히 드러내고 있는 것과 같다.\n\n이브를 판도라에 견주는 것은 너무도 명백하여 밀턴이 놓칠 리 없었으니, 그는 『실낙원』 제4권에서 이를 끌어들이고 있다.\n\n\"판도라보다도 더 사랑스러운 그녀, 신들이 그들의 온갖 선물로 채워 준 판도라, 아아, 그 슬픈 결말에서도 너무나 닮았으니, 헤르메스가 데려와 이아페토스의 더 어리석은 아들에게 보냈을 때, 그녀는 그 고운 자태로 인류를 옭아매어, 유피테르의 진정한 불을 훔친 자에게 앙갚음하였도다.\"\n\n프로메테우스와 에피메테우스는 이아페토스의 아들들이었는데, 밀턴은 이를 야벳[야펫]으로 바꾸어 적었다.\n\n프로메테우스는 시인들이 즐겨 다루는 소재였다. 그는 인류의 벗으로 그려지니, 유피테르가 인류에게 노여워했을 때 그들을 위해 나섰고, 그들에게 문명과 기예를 가르쳤다. 그러나 그렇게 함으로써 그는 제우스의 뜻을 거슬렀던 까닭에, 신들과 인간의 지배자의 노여움을 자신에게 끌어들였다. 제우스는 그를 카우카소스 산의 바위에 사슬로 묶었고, 그곳에서 독수리 한 마리가 그의 간을 쪼아 먹었는데, 그 간은 먹히는 즉시 다시 돋아났다. 이 고통의 상태는 프로메테우스가 그의 압제자에게 굴복하기만 한다면 언제든 끝날 수 있었으니, 그는 제우스의 옥좌의 안정과 관련된 비밀을 지니고 있었던 까닭에, 만일 그것을 밝히려 했다면 곧바로 총애를 받았을 것이다. 그러나 그는 그렇게 하기를 경멸했다. 그리하여 그는 받아 마땅하지 않은 고통을 의연히 견디는 것과, 압제에 맞서는 의지의 굳셈을 상징하는 존재가 되었다.\n\n바이런과 셸리 모두 이 주제를 다룬 바 있다. 다음은 바이런의 시구다.\n\n\"티탄이여! 그대의 불멸하는 눈에는 필멸하는 자들의 고통이, 그 슬픈 실상 그대로 보였으되, 신들이 업신여기는 것들로 보이지 않았도다. 그대의 연민이 받은 보답은 무엇이었던가? 소리 없는, 그러나 격렬한 고통, 바위와 독수리와 사슬, 오만한 자가 느낄 수 있는 모든 아픔, 그들이 내보이지 않는 그 고뇌, 숨 막히는 비탄의 감각이었도다.\n\n\"그대의 신과도 같은 죄는 친절을 베푼 것이었으니, 그대의 가르침으로 인간의 비참함의 총량을 덜어 주고, 인간을 그 자신의 정신으로 굳세게 한 것이었도다. 그리고 그대가 위로부터 좌절당했을지언정, 여전히, 그대의 참을성 있는 기력 속에서, 그대의 헤아릴 수 없는 정신의 인내와 거부 속에서, 땅도 하늘도 뒤흔들 수 없었던 그 정신 속에서, 우리는 위대한 교훈을 물려받았도다.\"\n\n바이런은 또한 그의 「나폴레옹 보나파르트에게 바치는 송가」에서도 같은 인유를 쓰고 있다.\n\n\"아니면, 하늘에서 불을 훔친 그 도둑처럼, 그대는 그 충격을 견뎌 내려는가? 그리고 그와 함께 — 용서받지 못한 그와 함께 — 그의 독수리와 그의 바위를 나누려는가?\"\n\n————\n\n# APOLLO AND DAPHNE—PYRAMUS AND THISBE—CEPHALUS AND PROCRIS\n\n홍수의 물이 대지를 뒤덮은 뒤 남긴 진흙은 지나친 비옥함을 자아냈으니, 그것은 좋은 것이든 나쁜 것이든 온갖 종류의 생물을 불러내었다. 그 가운데서도 거대한 뱀인 피톤이 기어 나와 사람들에게 공포가 되었으며, 파르나소스산의 동굴 속에 숨어 살았다. 아폴론은 자신의 화살로 그것을 죽였는데, 그때까지 그 무기는 토끼나 들염소 같은 연약한 짐승이나 사냥감에게만 사용하던 것이었다. 이 빛나는 승리를 기념하여 그는 피티아 경기를 창설하였으니, 여기서 힘이나 발의 빠름, 혹은 전차 경주에서 승리한 자는 너도밤나무 잎으로 엮은 화관을 받았다. 월계수가 아직 아폴론 자신의 나무로 채택되기 전이었기 때문이다.\n\n벨베데레라고 불리는 저 유명한 아폴론 조각상은 뱀 피톤을 물리친 뒤의 신의 모습을 나타낸 것이다. 바이런은 그의 『차일드 해럴드』 제4권 161연에서 이를 언급하고 있다.\n\n\"……빗나가지 않는 활의 주인, 생명과 시와 빛의 신, 인간의 사지를 두르고, 그 이마는 싸움의 승리로 온통 빛나는 태양. 화살은 막 쏘아졌으니, 그 빛나는 살은 불멸자의 복수를 담았고, 그의 눈과 콧구멍에는 아름다운 경멸이, 또 힘과 위엄이 온전한 번개를 번뜩이며 스쳐 가니, 그 한 번의 눈길에 신성이 드러나는도다.\"\n아폴론과 다프네\n\n# APOLLO AND DAPHNE\n\n다프네는 아폴론의 첫사랑이었다. 그것은 우연으로 이루어진 일이 아니라, 에로스[로마의 〈쿠피도〉]의 심술 때문이었다. 아폴론은 그 소년이 활과 화살을 가지고 노는 것을 보았는데, 자신은 최근 피톤을 물리친 승리로 한껏 우쭐해져 그에게 말하였다. \"건방진 녀석아, 너 따위가 전쟁의 무기를 가지고 무엇을 하려느냐? 그것은 그럴 자격이 있는 손에게 맡겨 두어라. 보아라, 넓디넓은 들판에 독을 품은 몸을 뻗치고 있던 저 거대한 뱀을 내가 이것으로 정복하지 않았느냐! 얘야, 너는 네 횃불로나 만족하여라. 네가 말하는 그 불꽃을 어디든 네 마음대로 일으키되, 내 무기에는 감히 손대지 말아라.\" 아프로디테[로마의 〈베누스〉]의 아들은 이 말을 듣고 이렇게 응수하였다. \"아폴론, 당신의 화살은 다른 모든 것을 맞힐지 몰라도, 내 화살은 당신을 맞히고 말 것이오.\" 그렇게 말하고 그는 파르나소스산의 바위 위에 올라서서 화살통에서 서로 다르게 만들어진 두 개의 화살을 꺼냈는데, 하나는 사랑을 불러일으키는 것이요, 다른 하나는 사랑을 물리치는 것이었다. 앞의 것은 황금으로 되어 끝이 날카로웠고, 뒤의 것은 무디며 끝이 납으로 되어 있었다. 그는 납으로 된 화살로 강의 신 페네이오스의 딸인 요정 다프네를 쏘았고, 황금 화살로는 아폴론의 심장을 꿰뚫었다. 그러자 신은 즉시 그 처녀를 향한 사랑에 사로잡혔으나, 그녀는 사랑한다는 생각만으로도 혐오감을 느꼈다. 그녀의 기쁨은 숲속의 놀이와 사냥의 전리품에 있었다. 많은 구혼자들이 그녀를 찾았으나 그녀는 그들 모두를 물리치며 숲속을 거닐었고, 에로스에 대해서도 결혼의 신 히멘에 대해서도 아무런 생각이 없었다. 아버지는 종종 그녀에게 말하곤 했다. \"딸아, 너는 내게 사위를 안겨 주어야 하고, 손주를 안겨 주어야 한다.\" 그녀는 결혼이라는 생각을 죄악처럼 미워하며, 아름다운 얼굴을 온통 발갛게 붉힌 채 아버지의 목을 끌어안고 말하였다. \"사랑하는 아버지, 제게 이 한 가지 청을 들어주세요. 아르테미스[로마의 〈디아나〉]처럼 언제까지나 결혼하지 않고 지내게 해 주세요.\" 아버지는 이를 허락하였으나, 동시에 이렇게 말하였다. \"네 얼굴이 그것을 허락하지 않을 게다.\"\n\n아폴론은 그녀를 사랑하여 그녀를 얻기를 갈망하였다. 하지만 온 세상에 신탁을 내리는 그도 자신의 운명을 들여다볼 만큼 현명하지는 못하였다. 그는 그녀의 머리카락이 어깨 위로 풀어 헤쳐진 것을 보고 말하였다. \"흐트러져 있어도 저토록 매혹적인데, 단정히 빗으면 어떠하겠는가?\" 그는 별처럼 빛나는 그녀의 눈을 보았고, 그녀의 입술을 보았으나 그저 보는 것만으로는 만족하지 못하였다. 그는 어깨까지 드러난 그녀의 손과 팔을 흠모하였으며, 눈에 보이지 않는 것은 더더욱 아름다우리라 상상하였다. 그가 뒤따르자 그녀는 바람보다도 빠르게 달아났고, 그의 간청에도 한순간도 멈추지 않았다. \"멈추어라.\" 그가 말하였다. \"페네이오스의 딸이여, 나는 적이 아니다. 새끼 양이 늑대를 피하듯, 비둘기가 매를 피하듯 나를 피해 달아나지 말아라. 내가 너를 쫓는 것은 사랑 때문이다. 네가 이 돌부리에 걸려 넘어져 다치기라도 할까, 그래서 그 원인이 내가 될까 두려워 나는 비참하구나. 부디 천천히 달려라, 그러면 나도 천천히 뒤따르마. 나는 어릿광대도 아니요, 무례한 시골뜨기도 아니다. 제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]가 내 아버지이며, 나는 델포스와 테네도스의 주인으로, 현재와 미래의 모든 것을 안다. 나는 노래와 리라의 신이다. 내 화살은 과녁을 정확히 맞힌다. 그러나 아아! 내 것보다 더 치명적인 화살이 내 심장을 꿰뚫었구나! 나는 의술의 신으로 온갖 약초의 효능을 안다. 그러나 아아! 어떤 향유로도 고칠 수 없는 병을 나는 앓고 있구나!\"\n\n요정은 계속 달아나 그의 호소를 절반밖에 듣지 못한 채 남겨 두었다. 그리고 달아나는 그 모습조차 그를 매혹하였다. 바람이 그녀의 옷자락을 날리고, 묶지 않은 머리카락이 뒤로 풀려 흩날렸다. 신은 자신의 구애가 헛되이 버려지는 것에 조바심이 났고, 에로스의 부추김을 받아 추격에서 그녀에게 점점 가까워졌다. 그것은 마치 사냥개가 토끼를 쫓는 것과 같아서, 입을 벌리고 막 붙잡으려 하지만 더 약한 짐승은 앞으로 내달아 바로 그 손아귀에서 빠져나간다. 신과 처녀도 그렇게 달렸으니, 그는 사랑의 날개를 달고, 그녀는 두려움의 날개를 달고 있었다. 그러나 쫓는 자가 더 빨라 그녀에게 다가갔고, 그의 헐떡이는 숨결이 그녀의 머리카락에 닿았다. 그녀의 힘이 다하기 시작하더니, 막 쓰러지려는 순간 그녀는 아버지인 강의 신을 불렀다. \"도와주세요, 페네이오스여! 땅을 열어 저를 가두거나, 저를 이 위험에 빠뜨린 이 모습을 바꾸어 주세요!\" 그녀가 채 말을 마치기도 전에 뻣뻣함이 그녀의 온 사지를 사로잡았다. 그녀의 가슴은 부드러운 나무껍질에 둘러싸이기 시작하였고, 머리카락은 잎사귀가 되었으며, 두 팔은 가지가 되었고, 발은 뿌리가 되어 땅에 단단히 박혔으며, 얼굴은 나무 꼭대기가 되어 그 본래의 모습 가운데 아름다움 외에는 아무것도 남기지 않았다. 아폴론은 놀라움에 사로잡혀 섰다. 그가 줄기를 만지니 새 껍질 아래에서 살갗이 떨리는 것이 느껴졌다. 그가 가지를 끌어안고 나무에 입맞춤을 퍼부으니, 가지는 그의 입술을 피하여 움츠러들었다. \"그대가 내 아내가 될 수 없다면,\" 그가 말하였다. \"그대는 반드시 내 나무가 되리라. 나는 그대를 내 화관으로 쓰리라. 그대로 내 하프와 화살통을 장식하리라. 그리고 위대한 로마의 정복자들이 카피톨리움으로 개선의 행렬을 이끌고 올라갈 때, 그대는 그들의 이마를 위한 화관으로 엮이리라. 또 영원한 젊음이 내 것이듯, 그대 역시 언제나 푸르를 것이며, 그대의 잎은 시드는 법을 알지 못하리라.\" 이제 월계수로 변한 요정은 감사의 마음으로 그 머리를 숙였다.\n\n아폴론이 음악과 시의 신이라는 것은 이상하게 여겨지지 않겠으나, 의술 또한 그의 영역으로 주어졌다는 것은 이상하게 보일 수 있다. 시인 암스트롱은 자신 또한 의사였던바, 이를 다음과 같이 설명한다.\n\n\"음악은 모든 기쁨을 드높이고 모든 슬픔을 달래며, 질병을 몰아내고 온갖 고통을 부드럽게 하나니, 그리하여 옛 시절의 현인들은 떠받들었도다, 의술과 가락과 노래의 한 권능을.\"\n\n아폴론과 다프네의 이야기는 시인들에 의해 자주 언급된다. 월러는 이를, 비록 그 연애시가 연인의 마음을 부드럽게 하지는 못하였으나 시인에게 널리 퍼진 명성을 안겨 준 어느 인물의 경우에 적용하고 있다.\n\n\"허나 그가 불멸의 가락으로 노래한 것은, 비록 성공하지 못했으나, 헛되이 노래한 것이 아니었다. 그의 잘못을 바로잡았어야 할 그 요정만 빼고, 모두가 그의 열정에 귀 기울이고 그의 노래를 칭송하였다. 이리하여 포이보스처럼, 구하지 않은 찬사를 얻으며, 그는 사랑을 붙잡으려다 두 팔에 월계수를 가득 안았다.\"\n\n다음은 셸리의 『아도네이스』에 나오는 한 연으로, 바이런이 초기에 비평가들과 벌인 다툼을 가리킨다.\n\n\"오로지 뒤쫓는 데에만 대담한 무리 진 늑대들, 주검 위에서 떠들어 대는 추악한 까마귀들, 정복자의 깃발에 충성하는 독수리들, 황폐가 먼저 먹은 자리에서 먹이를 취하며 그 날개가 역병을 퍼붓는 그것들이, 어찌 달아났던가, 아폴론처럼, 황금 활에서, 이 시대의 피티아가 화살 하나를 쏘아 보내며 미소 지었을 때! 약탈자들은 두 번째 일격을 청하지 못하고, 저들을 차 버리고 가는 그 도도한 발에 아첨하며 따른다.\"\n피라모스와 티스베\n\n# PYRAMUS AND THISBE\n\n피라모스는 가장 잘생긴 청년이었고, 티스베는 세미라미스가 다스리던 온 바빌로니아에서 가장 아름다운 처녀였다. 그들의 부모는 이웃한 집에서 살았으니, 이웃하여 지내다 보니 두 젊은이가 한데 모이게 되었고, 알고 지내던 사이가 사랑으로 무르익었다. 그들은 기꺼이 결혼하고자 하였으나 부모가 이를 금하였다. 그러나 한 가지만은 부모도 금할 수 없었으니, 두 사람의 가슴속에서 사랑이 똑같이 뜨겁게 타오르는 것이었다. 그들은 몸짓과 눈빛으로 마음을 주고받았으며, 그 불길은 가려져 있을수록 더욱 격렬하게 타올랐다. 두 집을 가르는 담벼락에는 구조상의 어떤 흠 때문에 생긴 틈이 하나 있었다. 그전에는 아무도 그것을 눈여겨보지 않았으나, 두 연인이 그것을 발견하였다. 사랑이 발견하지 못할 것이 무엇이랴! 그 틈은 목소리가 지나갈 길을 내주었으니, 다정한 말들이 그 틈을 통해 오가곤 하였다. 그들이 서로 마주 서서, 피라모스는 이쪽에 티스베는 저쪽에 서면 두 사람의 숨결이 한데 섞이곤 하였다. \"잔인한 담이여,\" 그들은 말하였다. \"어찌하여 너는 두 연인을 갈라놓는가? 그러나 우리는 은혜를 모르지는 않으리라. 우리는 인정하노니, 사랑의 말을 기꺼이 들으려는 귀에 전해 주는 은혜를 우리는 네게 입었구나.\" 그들은 담벼락 양쪽에서 이러한 말을 주고받았다. 그리고 밤이 와서 작별을 고해야 할 때면, 그들은 더는 가까이 다가갈 수 없었으므로 담벼락에 입술을 갖다 대었으니, 그녀는 그녀 쪽에서, 그는 그의 쪽에서 그러하였다.\n\n이튿날 아침, 에오스[로마의 〈아우로라〉]가 별들을 거두고 해가 풀잎의 서리를 녹였을 때, 그들은 늘 만나던 곳에서 만났다. 그러고는 자신들의 모진 운명을 한탄한 뒤, 그들은 다음 날 밤 만물이 고요해지면 지켜보는 눈을 피해 빠져나와 집을 떠나 들판으로 걸어 나가기로 합의하였다. 그리고 만남을 확실히 하기 위해, 도시의 경계 밖에 서 있는 니노스의 무덤이라 불리는 잘 알려진 건축물로 가되, 먼저 도착한 이가 어떤 나무 아래에서 다른 이를 기다리기로 하였다. 그것은 흰 뽕나무였으며, 시원한 샘 가까이에 서 있었다. 모든 것이 합의되었고, 그들은 해가 물 밑으로 가라앉고 그 물에서 밤이 솟아오르기를 애타게 기다렸다. 그리하여 티스베는 조심스럽게, 가족의 눈에 띄지 않은 채 머리에 베일을 쓰고 몰래 빠져나와 그 무덤으로 가서 나무 아래에 앉았다. 저녁의 어스름한 빛 속에 홀로 앉아 있던 그녀는 암사자 한 마리가 갓 잡은 먹이로 입에서 김이 무럭무럭 나는 채로 갈증을 풀러 샘으로 다가오는 것을 발견하였다. 티스베는 그 광경에 달아나 바위의 움푹한 곳으로 몸을 피하였다. 달아나는 동안 그녀는 베일을 떨어뜨렸다. 암사자는 샘에서 물을 마신 뒤 숲으로 물러나려 돌아서다가 땅에 떨어진 베일을 보고는 피 묻은 입으로 그것을 흔들어 찢어 놓았다.\n\n뒤늦게 출발한 피라모스는 이제 만나기로 한 장소에 다가갔다. 그는 모래 위에서 사자의 발자국을 보았고, 그 광경에 두 뺨에서 핏기가 가셨다. 곧이어 그는 온통 찢기고 피 묻은 베일을 발견하였다. \"오, 가엾은 소녀여,\" 그가 말하였다. \"내가 그대 죽음의 원인이 되었구나! 나보다 더 살 자격이 있는 그대가 첫 번째 희생자로 쓰러졌으니, 내가 뒤따르리라. 이토록 위험한 곳으로 그대를 꾀어 내고서 정작 나 자신은 그대를 지키려 그 자리에 있지 못하였으니, 죄지은 원인은 바로 나로다. 바위에서 나오너라, 사자들이여, 이 죄지은 몸을 너희 이빨로 찢어 발기어라.\" 그는 베일을 집어 들어 약속한 나무로 가져가서는 입맞춤과 눈물로 그것을 적셨다. \"내 피 또한 그대의 천을 물들이리라.\" 그가 말하며 칼을 뽑아 자신의 심장에 찔러 넣었다. 상처에서 피가 솟구쳐 나무의 흰 오디를 온통 붉게 물들였고, 땅속으로 스며들어 뿌리에 이르렀으니, 그 붉은 빛이 줄기를 타고 올라가 열매에까지 미쳤다.\n\n이 무렵 티스베는 여전히 두려움에 떨면서도 연인을 실망시키고 싶지 않아, 자신이 모면한 위험을 그에게 어서 알리고 싶은 마음에 그 청년을 애타게 찾으며 조심스럽게 걸어 나왔다. 그곳에 이르러 오디의 변한 빛깔을 보고 그녀는 이곳이 같은 장소인지 의심하였다. 그녀가 머뭇거리는 동안 그녀는 죽음의 고통 속에 몸부림치는 한 사람의 형상을 보았다. 그녀는 흠칫 뒤로 물러섰고, 잔잔한 물 위에 갑작스러운 미풍이 스칠 때 이는 잔물결처럼 그녀의 온몸에 전율이 흘렀다. 그러나 자신의 연인임을 알아본 순간 그녀는 비명을 지르며 가슴을 치고, 싸늘한 시신을 끌어안고 그 상처에 눈물을 쏟으며 차가운 입술에 입을 맞추었다. \"오, 피라모스,\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"무엇이 이런 짓을 하였나요? 대답해 주세요, 피라모스. 당신의 티스베가 말하고 있어요. 들어 주세요, 사랑하는 이여, 그 늘어진 머리를 들어 주세요!\" 티스베라는 이름에 피라모스는 눈을 떴다가 다시 감았다. 그녀는 자신의 베일이 피에 물든 것과 칼이 빠져나간 빈 칼집을 보았다. \"당신 자신의 손이, 그것도 나 때문에 당신을 죽였군요.\" 그녀가 말하였다. \"나도 한 번쯤은 용감할 수 있고, 내 사랑도 당신의 사랑만큼 강하답니다. 내가 그 원인이 되었으니, 나도 죽음 속에서 당신을 따르리라. 우리를 갈라놓을 수 있었던 것이 오직 죽음뿐이었으나, 그 죽음도 내가 당신과 함께하는 것을 막지는 못하리라. 그리고 우리 둘의 불행한 부모들이여, 우리의 하나 된 청을 거절하지 마세요. 사랑과 죽음이 우리를 하나로 묶었으니, 한 무덤이 우리를 함께 담게 해 주세요. 그리고 너, 나무여, 죽음의 자취를 간직하여라. 네 열매가 우리 피의 기념물이 되어 길이 남게 하여라.\" 그렇게 말하며 그녀는 칼을 자신의 가슴에 찔러 넣었다. 그녀의 부모는 그녀의 소원을 받아들였고, 신들 또한 그것을 받아들였다. 두 사람의 몸은 한 무덤에 묻혔고, 그 나무는 그 후로 언제나 자줏빛 열매를 맺었으니, 오늘날에 이르기까지 그러하다.\n\n무어는 「실프의 무도회」에서 데이비의 안전등을 이야기하던 중, 티스베와 그 연인을 갈라놓았던 담벼락을 떠올린다.\n\n\"오, 저 등불의 금속 그물망이여, 데이비가 섬세하게 둘러친 저 보호하는 철사의 장막이여, 금지된 위험한 불 둘레에 친 그것이여! 그가 불꽃과 공기 사이에 세운 그 벽은, (어린 티스베의 기쁨을 가로막던 그 벽처럼,) 그 작은 구멍들을 통해 이 위험한 한 쌍이 서로를 볼 수는 있어도, 입맞출 수는 없게 하는도다.\"\n\n미클이 번역한 『우스 루지아다스』에는 피라모스와 티스베의 이야기와 오디가 변모한 일에 대한 다음과 같은 언급이 나온다. 시인은 사랑의 섬을 묘사하고 있다.\n\n\"……이곳에서는 포모나의 손이 베푸는 온갖 선물이, 가꾼 정원에서처럼, 가꾸지 않아도 자유로이 흘러나오니, 그 향기는 더욱 달콤하고 그 빛깔은 더욱 고와, 정성스러운 손길이 길러 낸 그 어느 것보다도 낫도다. 이곳의 버찌는 빛나는 진홍빛으로 타오르고, 연인들의 피로 물든 채, 늘어진 줄을 이루어, 오디는 휘어진 가지를 가득 메우는도다.\"\n\n만약 우리의 어린 독자들 가운데 누구라도, 가엾은 피라모스와 티스베를 비웃을 만큼 모진 마음을 지닌 이가 있다면, 셰익스피어의 희곡 『한여름 밤의 꿈』을 펼쳐 보는 것으로 그 기회를 찾을 수 있을 것이다. 거기에서 이 이야기는 더없이 우스꽝스럽게 익살로 패러디되어 있다.\n\n# CEPHALUS AND PROCRIS\n\n케팔로스와 프로크리스\n케팔로스는 아름다운 청년으로 사내다운 운동을 좋아하였다. 그는 사냥을 하려고 동트기도 전에 일어나곤 하였다. 에오스가 처음 밖을 내다보았을 때 그를 보고는 그와 사랑에 빠져 그를 몰래 데려갔다. 그러나 케팔로스는 자신이 지극히 사랑하는 매혹적인 아내와 갓 결혼한 참이었다. 그녀의 이름은 프로크리스였다. 그녀는 사냥의 여신 아르테미스의 총애를 받았는데, 여신은 그녀에게 어떤 경쟁자보다도 빨리 달리는 개 한 마리와 결코 과녁을 빗나가지 않는 투창 한 자루를 주었다. 프로크리스는 이 선물들을 남편에게 주었다. 케팔로스는 아내로 인해 너무나 행복하였으므로 에오스의 온갖 간청을 물리쳤고, 마침내 그녀는 못마땅해하며 그를 돌려보내며 말하였다. \"가거라, 은혜를 모르는 인간아, 네 아내를 지켜라. 그러나 내 짐작이 크게 틀리지 않다면, 너는 언젠가 그녀를 다시 본 것을 몹시 후회하게 되리라.\"\n\n케팔로스는 돌아와 여전히 아내와 숲속의 사냥으로 행복하였다. 그런데 마침 어느 노한 신이 사납게 날뛰는 여우 한 마리를 보내어 그 고장을 괴롭혔다. 그래서 사냥꾼들이 그것을 잡으려고 대거 나섰다. 그러나 그들의 노력은 모두 헛되었으니, 어떤 개도 그것을 따라잡지 못하였다. 결국 그들은 케팔로스를 찾아와 그의 유명한 개를 빌리려 하였는데, 그 개의 이름은 라일랍스였다. 개를 풀어 놓자마자 그것은 그들의 눈이 따라갈 수 없을 만큼 빠르게 내달렸다. 모래 위에 찍힌 발자국을 보지 못하였다면 그들은 그 개가 날아간 줄로 여겼을 것이다. 케팔로스를 비롯한 이들은 언덕 위에 서서 그 추격을 지켜보았다. 여우는 온갖 재주를 다 부렸으니, 원을 그리며 달리다가 제 발자국을 되짚어 돌아섰고, 개는 입을 벌린 채 그 뒤를 바짝 따라 발꿈치를 물어뜯으려 하였으나 허공만 물어뜯었다. 케팔로스가 막 투창을 쓰려는 순간, 갑자기 그는 개와 사냥감이 동시에 멈춰 서는 것을 보았다. 둘 다를 내려 준 천상의 권능들이 어느 한쪽도 이기는 것을 바라지 않았던 것이다. 살아 움직이는 바로 그 자세 그대로 그들은 돌로 변하였다. 너무나 살아 있는 듯 자연스러워 보였으므로, 그것들을 바라보노라면 하나는 막 짖으려 하고 다른 하나는 막 앞으로 뛰어오르려 하는 듯 여겨졌을 것이다.\n\n케팔로스는 비록 개를 잃었으나 여전히 사냥에서 기쁨을 얻었다. 그는 이른 아침에 나가, 누구의 동행도 없이 숲과 언덕을 거닐었으니, 아무런 도움도 필요 없었다. 그의 투창이 어떤 경우에도 확실한 무기였기 때문이다. 사냥에 지쳐 해가 높이 떠오르면 그는 시원한 시냇물이 흐르는 그늘진 곳을 찾아, 옷을 벗어 던지고 풀밭에 드러누워 산들바람을 즐기곤 하였다. 때때로 그는 소리 내어 말하곤 하였다. \"오너라, 달콤한 산들바람이여, 와서 내 가슴에 부채질하여라, 와서 나를 태우는 이 열기를 식혀 다오.\" 어느 날 곁을 지나가던 누군가가 그가 이렇게 허공에다 말하는 것을 듣고는, 그가 어떤 처녀에게 말을 거는 것이라 어리석게 믿고서, 가서 그 비밀을 케팔로스의 아내 프로크리스에게 일러 주었다. 사랑은 쉽게 믿는 법이다. 프로크리스는 그 갑작스러운 충격에 그만 정신을 잃고 쓰러졌다. 곧 정신을 차린 그녀는 말하였다. \"그럴 리 없어. 내가 직접 목격하지 않는 한 믿지 않으리라.\" 그리하여 그녀는 불안한 마음으로 이튿날 아침까지 기다렸고, 케팔로스가 여느 때처럼 사냥하러 갔다. 그러자 그녀는 그를 뒤따라 몰래 빠져나가, 밀고자가 일러 준 장소에 몸을 숨겼다. 케팔로스는 사냥에 지치면 늘 그러하던 대로 와서 푸른 둑에 몸을 뉘며 말하였다. \"오너라, 달콤한 산들바람이여, 와서 내게 부채질하여라. 너는 내가 너를 얼마나 사랑하는지 알지! 너는 이 숲과 나의 외로운 거님을 즐겁게 해 주는구나.\" 그가 이런 식으로 말을 이어 가는데, 덤불 속에서 흐느낌 같은 소리가 들리는 듯하였다. 어떤 들짐승이려니 여기고 그는 그곳을 향해 투창을 던졌다. 사랑하는 프로크리스의 비명이 그 무기가 너무도 확실히 과녁을 맞혔음을 그에게 알렸다. 그는 그곳으로 달려가, 그녀가 피를 흘리며 꺼져 가는 힘으로 자신의 선물이었던 투창을 상처에서 빼내려 애쓰고 있는 것을 발견하였다. 케팔로스는 그녀를 땅에서 일으켜 피를 멎게 하려 애쓰며, 정신을 차려서 자신을 비참하게 버려 두지 말라고, 그녀의 죽음으로 자책하게 하지 말라고 그녀를 불렀다. 그녀는 힘없는 눈을 떠, 가까스로 이 몇 마디 말을 입에 담았다. \"간청하건대, 당신이 일찍이 나를 사랑한 적이 있다면, 내가 일찍이 당신의 손길에서 다정함을 받을 자격이 있었다면, 여보, 이 마지막 청을 들어주세요. 그 가증스러운 산들바람과는 결혼하지 마세요!\" 이로써 모든 수수께끼가 드러났다. 그러나 아아! 이제 와서 그것을 밝힌들 무슨 소용이 있으랴! 그녀는 죽었다. 그러나 그녀의 얼굴은 평온한 표정을 띠었고, 케팔로스가 진실을 깨닫게 해 주었을 때 그녀는 가엾어하며 용서하는 눈길로 남편을 바라보았다.\n\n무어는 그의 『전설 발라드』에서 케팔로스와 프로크리스에 관한 한 편을 실었는데, 이렇게 시작한다.\n\n\"한 사냥꾼이 일찍이 숲속에 몸을 뉘었네, 한낮의 밝은 눈을 피하려고, 그리고 자주 떠도는 바람에게 구애하였네, 그 한숨으로 제 이마를 식혀 달라고. 들벌의 윙윙거림조차 잠잠히 누웠을 때, 그 어떤 숨결도 사시나무의 머리칼을 흔들지 못할 때, 그의 노래는 한결같았네, '달콤한 바람이여, 오, 오라!' 메아리가 화답하는 동안, '오라, 달콤한 바람이여!'\"\n\n————\n\n# CHAPTER IV\n\n어느 날 헤라[로마의 〈유노〉]는 갑자기 사방이 어두워지는 것을 알아채고는, 곧바로 자기 남편이 떳떳하지 못한 어떤 짓을 빛으로부터 가리려고 구름을 일으킨 것이 아닌가 의심했다. 그녀가 구름을 걷어 내자, 거울처럼 맑은 강가에 서 있는 남편이 보였고, 그 곁에는 아름다운 어린 암소 한 마리가 서 있었다. 헤라는 그 암소의 모습 속에 필멸의 몸을 지닌 어여쁜 님프가 감추어져 있으리라고 의심했는데, 사실이 정말 그러했다. 그 암소는 강의 신 이나코스의 딸 이오였으니, 제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]가 그녀에게 추근거리던 차에 아내가 다가오는 것을 알아채고는 그런 모습으로 바꾸어 놓았던 것이다.\n\n헤라는 남편에게 다가가, 그 암소를 보고는 아름답다고 칭찬하면서 누구의 것이며 어느 무리에 속한 것이냐고 물었다. 제우스는 더 이상의 질문을 막으려고, 그것은 대지에서 갓 생겨난 것이라고 대답했다. 헤라는 그것을 선물로 달라고 했다. 제우스가 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가? 그는 자기 정부를 아내에게 넘겨주는 것이 내키지 않았으나, 그렇다고 한낱 어린 암소 같은 하찮은 선물을 어찌 거절한단 말인가? 의심을 사지 않고서는 그럴 수가 없었기에, 그는 승낙하고 말았다. 그러나 여신은 아직 의심이 풀리지 않았기에, 그 암소를 아르고스에게 넘겨주어 엄중히 감시하도록 했다.\n\n그런데 아르고스는 머리에 백 개의 눈이 달려 있었고, 한 번에 두 개씩만 감고 잠을 잤으므로 이오를 끊임없이 지켜볼 수 있었다. 그는 낮 동안에는 이오가 풀을 뜯게 내버려 두었으나, 밤이 되면 흉한 밧줄로 목을 묶어 두었다. 이오는 아르고스에게 자유를 애원하려고 두 팔을 뻗으려 했지만, 뻗을 팔이 없었으며, 그녀의 목소리는 자신마저도 놀라게 하는 울음소리였다. 그녀는 아버지와 자매들을 보고 그들에게 다가가, 그들이 자기 등을 쓰다듬게 내버려 두었으며, 그들이 자기 아름다움을 감탄하는 소리를 들었다. 아버지가 풀 한 다발을 내밀자, 그녀는 그 내민 손을 핥았다. 그녀는 자기가 누구인지 그에게 알리고 싶어 그 바람을 입 밖에 내려 했지만, 아아! 말이 나오지 않았다. 마침내 그녀는 글씨를 떠올리고는, 자신의 이름을 — 짧은 이름이었다 — 발굽으로 모래 위에 새겼다. 이나코스는 그것을 알아보았고, 오랫동안 헛되이 찾아 헤매던 딸이 이런 변장 아래 숨겨져 있음을 깨닫자, 그녀를 슬퍼하며 그 흰 목을 끌어안고 외쳤다. \"아아! 내 딸아, 너를 아주 잃어버렸더라면 차라리 덜 슬펐을 것을!\" 그가 이렇게 탄식하고 있을 때, 아르고스가 그것을 보고는 다가와 그녀를 몰아내고, 사방 어디든 두루 살필 수 있는 높은 둑 위에 자리를 잡고 앉았다.\n\n제우스는 자기 정부의 고통을 보다 못해 헤르메스[로마의 〈메르쿠리우스〉]를 불러 아르고스를 처치하고 오라고 일렀다. 헤르메스는 서둘러 발에는 날개 달린 신을 신고 머리에는 모자를 쓴 다음, 잠을 불러오는 지팡이를 들고 하늘의 탑에서 땅으로 뛰어내렸다. 그곳에서 그는 날개를 벗어 두고 지팡이만 챙긴 채, 양 떼를 모는 목동의 모습으로 나타났다. 그는 거닐면서 피리를 불었다. 그것은 이른바 시링크스, 곧 판의 피리라고 부르는 것이었다. 아르고스는 그 악기를 전에 본 적이 없었기에 흥미롭게 귀를 기울였다. \"젊은이,\" 그가 말했다. \"이리 와서 이 돌 위에 내 곁에 앉게나. 자네 양 떼가 풀을 뜯기에 이만한 곳도 없거니와, 여기 목동들이 좋아하는 시원한 그늘도 있다네.\" 헤르메스는 자리에 앉아 이야기를 나누며 날이 저물도록 여러 이야기를 들려주고는, 그 경계하는 눈들을 잠재우려는 마음으로 가장 마음을 누그러뜨리는 가락을 피리로 연주했다. 그러나 모두 헛일이었으니, 아르고스는 나머지 눈은 감으면서도 몇몇 눈은 여전히 뜨고 있을 줄 알았기 때문이다.\n\n여러 이야기 가운데서, 헤르메스는 자기가 연주하는 그 악기가 어떻게 만들어졌는지를 그에게 들려주었다. \"시링크스라는 이름의 어떤 님프가 있었는데, 사티로스들과 숲의 정령들에게 무척이나 사랑을 받았다네. 하지만 그녀는 그들 중 누구도 거들떠보지 않고, 아르테미스[로마의 〈디아나〉]의 충실한 숭배자가 되어 사냥을 따라다녔지. 사냥 복장을 한 그녀를 보았다면 아르테미스 본인이라고 여겼을 걸세, 다만 그녀의 활은 뿔로 만든 것이고 아르테미스의 활은 은으로 만든 것이라는 차이뿐이었지. 어느 날 그녀가 사냥에서 돌아오는데 판이 그녀를 만나, 바로 이런 말을 건네고는 같은 식의 말을 더 늘어놓았다네. 그녀는 그의 찬사를 들을 새도 없이 달아났고, 그는 그녀가 강가에 이를 때까지 뒤쫓아 마침내 따라잡았는데, 그녀는 겨우 자기 벗인 물의 님프들에게 도움을 청할 시간밖에 없었지. 님프들은 그 소리를 듣고 응해 주었다네. 판은 님프의 몸이라고 여긴 것을 두 팔로 끌어안았으나, 알고 보니 그저 갈대 한 다발을 껴안고 있었던 거야! 그가 한숨을 내쉬자, 그 공기가 갈대 사이로 울려 구슬픈 가락을 빚어냈지. 그 신은 그 새로움과 음악의 감미로움에 매료되어 말했다네. '그렇다면 이렇게라도 너는 내 것이 되리라.' 그러고는 갈대 몇 대를 꺾어 서로 다른 길이로 나란히 붙여 악기를 만들고는, 그 님프를 기려 시링크스라고 이름 붙였다네.\" 헤르메스가 이야기를 마치기도 전에, 그는 아르고스의 눈이 모두 잠든 것을 보았다. 그의 머리가 앞으로 떨궈져 가슴에 닿자, 헤르메스는 단칼에 그의 목을 베어 그 머리를 바위 아래로 굴려 떨어뜨렸다. 오, 가련한 아르고스여! 그대의 백 개 눈의 빛이 일순간에 꺼져 버렸도다! 헤라는 그 눈들을 거두어 자기 공작새 꽁지에 장식으로 달았으니, 그것들은 오늘날까지도 그곳에 남아 있다.\n\n그러나 헤라의 복수는 아직 가시지 않았다. 그녀는 등에 한 마리를 보내 이오를 괴롭혔고, 이오는 그것의 추격을 피해 온 세상을 떠돌며 달아났다. 그녀는 자기 이름을 딴 이오니아해를 헤엄쳐 건넌 다음, 일리리아의 평원을 헤매고, 하이모스산을 오르고, 트라케 해협을 건넜는데, 그곳은 그로 인해 보스포로스[암소의 여울]라는 이름을 얻었다. 그녀는 계속해서 스키티아와 킴메리아인들의 땅을 헤매다가, 마침내 나일강 기슭에 다다랐다. 마침내 제우스가 그녀를 위해 중재에 나섰고, 더 이상 그녀에게 관심을 두지 않겠다고 약속하자 헤라는 그녀를 본래의 모습으로 되돌리는 데 동의했다. 그녀가 차츰 예전의 자신을 되찾는 모습은 보기에 신기했다. 거친 털이 몸에서 떨어져 나가고, 뿔이 오그라들고, 눈이 좁아지고, 입이 짧아졌으며, 앞다리의 발굽 대신 손과 손가락이 생겨났다. 요컨대 어린 암소의 모습은 그 아름다움 말고는 아무것도 남지 않았다. 처음에는 음매 소리가 날까 두려워 말하기를 겁냈으나, 차츰 자신감을 되찾고 아버지와 자매들에게 돌아갔다.\n\n키츠가 리 헌트에게 헌정한 시에는 판과 시링크스 이야기에 대한 다음과 같은 인유가 나온다.\n\n\"나뭇가지를 옆으로 젖혀 우리로 하여금 드넓은 숲을 들여다보게 한 그도 그렇게 느꼈으리, . . . . . . . 어여쁘게 떨던 시링크스가 아르카디아의 판에게서 그토록 두려운 공포에 사로잡혀 달아난 사연을 들려주면서. 가엾은 님프여 — 가엾은 판이여 — 그가 발견한 것이라곤 갈대 우거진 시냇가를 따라 흐르는 바람의 사랑스러운 한숨뿐이었으니 얼마나 울었던가, 어렴풋이 들리는 가락, 감미로운 황량함과 향긋한 아픔으로 가득한 그 가락을.\"\n\n칼리스토는 헤라의 질투를 불러일으킨 또 한 명의 처녀였으며, 여신은 그녀를 곰으로 바꾸어 놓았다. \"내가 빼앗아 버리리라,\" 여신이 말했다. \"네가 내 남편을 사로잡은 그 아름다움을.\" 칼리스토는 두 손과 무릎을 땅에 떨어뜨리며 쓰러졌다. 그녀는 애원하려고 두 팔을 뻗으려 했으나 — 그 팔은 이미 검은 털로 덮이기 시작했다. 그녀의 손은 둥글게 변하더니 굽은 발톱이 돋아나 발 노릇을 하게 되었고, 제우스가 그 아름다움을 칭찬하곤 하던 그녀의 입은 무시무시한 한 쌍의 턱이 되었으며, 변하지 않았더라면 가슴을 연민으로 움직였을 그녀의 목소리는 차라리 공포를 불러일으키기에 알맞은 으르렁거림이 되었다. 그러나 예전의 성품만은 남아 있어, 그녀는 끊임없이 신음하며 자기 운명을 슬퍼하고, 할 수 있는 한 똑바로 서서 두 앞발을 들어 자비를 빌었으며, 제우스가 매정하다는 것을 느꼈지만 그에게 그렇게 말할 수는 없었다. 아, 그녀는 밤새 숲속에 홀로 머무는 것이 두려워 예전에 자주 다니던 곳 부근을 얼마나 자주 헤매고 다녔던가. 얼마 전까지 사냥꾼이었던 그녀가 개들에게 겁먹고는 얼마나 자주 사냥꾼들로부터 두려워 달아났던가! 그녀는 자신이 이제 야수가 되었다는 것을 잊은 채 야수들로부터 자주 도망쳤으며, 자신이 곰이면서도 곰들을 두려워했다.\n\n어느 날 한 젊은이가 사냥을 하다가 그녀를 발견했다. 그녀는 그를 보고는 이제 청년으로 자란 자기 아들임을 알아보았다. 그녀는 멈추어 서서 그를 끌어안고 싶은 마음이 들었다. 그녀가 막 다가가려 하자, 그는 놀라서 사냥 창을 치켜들고 그녀를 막 찌르려던 참이었는데, 제우스가 이를 보고는 그 죄악을 막고 두 사람을 모두 낚아채어 큰곰자리와 작은곰자리로 하늘에 올려놓았다.\n\n헤라는 자신의 연적이 그토록 영예로운 자리에 오른 것을 보고 격분하여, 바다의 권능인 태고의 테티스와 오케아노스에게 서둘러 갔다. 그들이 무슨 일이냐고 묻자 그녀는 자기가 온 까닭을 이렇게 말했다. \"신들의 여왕인 내가 어찌하여 하늘의 평원을 떠나 그대들의 깊은 곳을 찾아왔는지 묻는가? 내가 하늘에서 밀려났음을, 내 자리를 다른 이에게 빼앗겼음을 알아 두게나. 내 말을 좀처럼 믿기 어렵겠지만, 밤이 세상을 어둡게 할 때 보게나. 그러면 내가 그토록 한탄할 까닭이 있는 그 둘이 하늘로, 원이 가장 작은 그 부분, 곧 극(極) 부근으로 올려진 것을 보게 될 걸세. 내 노여움의 결과가 이런 상이라면, 앞으로 누가 헤라의 비위를 거스를까 두려워 떨겠는가? 내가 무엇을 이룰 수 있었는지 보게나! 나는 그녀가 사람의 모습을 하는 것을 금했거늘 — 그녀는 별들 가운데 놓였구나! 내 벌이 이렇게 끝나니 — 내 권능이 이 정도로구나! 차라리 이오에게 허락했듯이 그녀가 본래 모습을 되찾는 편이 나았을 것을. 어쩌면 그는 그녀와 혼인하고 나를 내칠 작정인지도 모르지! 그러나 그대들, 나를 길러 준 이들이여, 나를 가엾이 여기고 이 부당한 처사를 못마땅하게 본다면, 부디 이를 보여 주게나. 저 죄지은 한 쌍이 그대들의 물속으로 들어오지 못하도록 막아 줌으로써 말일세.\" 바다의 권능들은 이에 응했고, 그리하여 큰곰자리와 작은곰자리의 두 별자리는 하늘에서 빙글빙글 돌면서도, 다른 별들처럼 결코 바다 아래로 가라앉지 않는다.\n\n밀턴은 곰자리가 결코 지지 않는다는 사실을 다음과 같이 언급한다.\n\n\"한밤중에 내 등불이 어느 높고 외로운 탑에서 보이게 하라, 그곳에서 나는 자주 곰자리보다 늦도록 지켜보리니,\" 운운.\n\n그리고 J. R. 로웰의 시에 나오는 프로메테우스는 이렇게 말한다.\n\n\"별들이 하나씩 차례로 떠올랐다 졌으니, 내 사슬의 흰 서리 위에서 반짝이면서. 밤새도록 북극성의 우리 주위를 어슬렁대던 곰자리는, 새벽의 발랄한 발소리에 놀라 제 굴 속으로 움츠러들었구나.\"\n\n작은곰자리 꽁지의 마지막 별은 북극성으로, 키노수라라고도 불린다. 밀턴은 이렇게 말한다.\n\n\"곧장 내 눈은 새로운 즐거움을 붙잡았으니, 주위의 풍경을 두루 가늠하는 동안. . . . . . . . 탑과 성가퀴가 보이나니, 우거진 나무들 사이로 높이 안긴 채, 어쩌면 그곳에 어떤 미인이 누워 있어 이웃한 뭇 시선의 키노수라가 되리라.\"\n\n여기서의 언급은 뱃사람들의 길잡이가 되는 북극성과, 북녘의 자기적(磁氣的) 이끌림을 함께 가리킨다. 그는 그것을 \"아르카디아의 별\"이라고도 부르는데, 이는 칼리스토의 아들 이름이 아르카스였고 그들이 아르카디아에 살았기 때문이다. 「코머스」에서, 숲속에서 어둠에 갇힌 오라비는 이렇게 말한다.\n\n\". . . 다정한 촛불이여! 어느 진흙집의 버들 엮은 구멍에서 비치는 골풀 양초일지언정, 흘러나오는 빛의 그 길게 뻗은 자로 우리를 찾아오라, 그러면 그대는 우리의 아르카디아의 별, 아니면 티로스의 키노수라가 되리라.\"\n\n이렇듯 우리는 두 가지 사례에서 헤라가 연적들에게 얼마나 가혹했는지를 보았으니, 이제 한 처녀 여신이 자신의 사생활을 침범한 자를 어떻게 벌했는지 알아보기로 하자.\n\n한낮이었고, 해가 양쪽 끝에서 똑같이 떨어진 곳에 걸려 있을 때, 카드모스 왕의 아들인 젊은 악타이온은 함께 산에서 사슴을 사냥하던 젊은이들에게 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"벗들이여, 우리의 그물과 무기는 잡은 짐승들의 피로 젖었네. 오늘 하루의 사냥은 이만하면 충분하니, 내일 다시 일을 시작하면 되네. 자, 포이보스가 대지를 메마르게 하는 이때, 우리의 도구를 치워 두고 쉬면서 여유를 누리세.\"\n\n사이프러스와 소나무가 빽빽이 둘러싼 골짜기가 하나 있었으니, 사냥의 여왕 아르테미스에게 바쳐진 곳이었다. 골짜기 끝에는 동굴이 하나 있었는데, 인공으로 꾸민 것이 아니라 자연이 그 구조 속에서 인공을 흉내 낸 것이었다. 자연이 마치 사람의 손길로 다듬은 듯 정교하게 들어맞는 돌들로 그 지붕의 아치를 둥글게 빚어 놓았던 것이다. 한쪽에서는 샘이 솟아 나왔고, 그 열린 못은 풀이 우거진 가장자리로 둘려 있었다. 숲의 여신은 사냥에 지쳤을 때 이곳에 와서 반짝이는 물에 처녀의 사지를 씻곤 했다.\n\n어느 날, 그녀는 님프들과 함께 그곳에 가서 자신의 투창과 화살통과 활을 한 님프에게, 겉옷을 다른 님프에게 건네주었고, 또 한 님프는 그녀의 발에서 샌들을 풀었다. 그러고 나서 그들 가운데 가장 솜씨 좋은 크로칼레가 그녀의 머리를 매만졌고, 네펠레와 히알레를 비롯한 나머지 님프들은 넉넉한 항아리에 물을 길었다. 여신이 이렇듯 몸단장의 일에 여념이 없을 때, 보라, 악타이온이 동료들을 떠나 별다른 목적 없이 헤매다가, 자신의 운명에 이끌려 그곳에 이르렀다. 그가 동굴 입구에 모습을 드러내자, 한 남자를 본 님프들은 비명을 지르며 자기들 몸으로 여신을 가리려고 그녀에게 달려들었다. 그러나 여신은 다른 님프들보다 키가 커서 그들 모두보다 머리 하나만큼 솟아 있었다. 이렇게 불시에 들킨 아르테미스의 얼굴에는, 해 질 녘이나 동틀 무렵 구름을 물들이는 듯한 빛깔이 떠올랐다. 님프들에게 둘러싸여 있었으면서도, 그녀는 몸을 반쯤 돌리고는 갑작스러운 충동으로 화살을 찾았다. 화살이 가까이에 없자, 그녀는 침입자의 얼굴에 물을 끼얹으며 이런 말을 덧붙였다. \"이제 가서 할 수 있거든 떠벌려 보아라, 옷을 걸치지 않은 아르테미스를 보았노라고.\" 그러자 즉시 가지처럼 뻗은 한 쌍의 사슴뿔이 그의 머리에서 돋아나고, 목은 길어지고, 귀는 뾰족해지고, 손은 발이 되고, 팔은 긴 다리가 되었으며, 몸은 털 난 얼룩 가죽으로 덮였다. 예전의 대담함 대신 두려움이 들어섰고, 그 영웅은 달아났다. 그는 자기 자신의 빠른 속도에 감탄하지 않을 수 없었으나, 물에 비친 자기 뿔을 보고는 \"아, 가련한 나여!\" 하고 말하려 했지만, 그 애씀 끝에 아무 소리도 나오지 않았다. 그는 신음했고, 자기 얼굴을 대신한 그 얼굴 위로 눈물이 흘러내렸다. 그러나 의식만은 그대로 남아 있었다. 무엇을 할 것인가? 집으로, 궁전으로 돌아갈 것인가, 아니면 숲속에 숨을 것인가? 뒤엣것은 두려웠고, 앞엣것은 부끄러웠다. 그가 망설이는 사이 개들이 그를 발견했다. 먼저 스파르타산 개 멜람푸스가 짖어서 신호를 보냈고, 이어 팜파고스, 도르케우스, 렐랍스, 테론, 나페, 티그리스를 비롯한 나머지 개들이 모두 바람보다 빠르게 그를 뒤쫓았다. 바위와 절벽을 넘고, 도저히 지날 수 없을 것 같은 산골짜기를 가로질러 그는 달아났고 개들은 뒤따랐다. 그가 자주 사슴을 쫓고 자기 사냥개 무리를 부추기던 그곳에서, 이제 그의 사냥개 무리가 그의 사냥꾼들에게 부추김을 받으며 그를 뒤쫓았다. 그는 \"나는 악타이온이다, 너희 주인을 알아보아라!\" 하고 외치고 싶었지만, 말이 그의 뜻대로 나오지 않았다. 대기는 개들의 짖는 소리로 울렸다. 이윽고 한 마리가 그의 등에 달려들고, 다른 한 마리가 그의 어깨를 물었다. 그 둘이 주인을 붙잡고 있는 사이, 나머지 개들이 몰려와 그의 살에 이빨을 박았다. 그는 신음했는데 — 사람의 목소리는 아니었으나, 그렇다고 분명 사슴의 목소리도 아니었다 — 무릎을 꿇으며 두 눈을 들어 올렸고, 팔이 있었더라면 애원하듯 두 팔을 들어 올렸을 것이다. 그의 벗들이자 함께 사냥하던 이들은 개들을 부추기며, 사방으로 악타이온을 찾고 그에게 사냥에 합류하라고 불렀다. 자기 이름이 불리자 그는 머리를 돌렸고, 그가 없는 것을 그들이 아쉬워하는 소리를 들었다. 그는 자기가 정말 그 자리에 없기를 간절히 바랐다. 그는 자기 개들의 활약을 보았더라면 무척 기뻤겠으나, 그것을 몸으로 느끼는 것은 너무나 가혹했다. 개들은 그를 온통 둘러싼 채 물어뜯고 찢었으며, 그들이 그의 목숨을 찢어 낸 뒤에야 비로소 아르테미스의 노여움이 풀렸다.\n\n셸리의 시 「아도네이스」에는 악타이온 이야기에 대한 다음과 같은 인유가 있다.\n\n\"그다지 알려지지 않은 다른 이들 가운데 한 가냘픈 형상이 왔으니, 사람들 사이의 한 유령이었네, 벗 없이 홀로, 잦아드는 폭풍의 마지막 구름처럼, 그 천둥이 곧 그의 조종(弔鐘)이라네. 짐작건대 그는 자연의 벌거벗은 사랑스러움을 악타이온처럼 응시했었고, 이제 그는 갈 곳을 잃은 채 비틀거리는 걸음으로 세상의 황야를 헤매며 달아났으니, 그리고 그 자신의 〈생각들〉이, 그 험한 길을 따라 성난 사냥개들처럼 제 아비이자 제 먹이를 뒤쫓았네.\" 제31연.\n\n이 인유는 아마도 셸리 자신을 가리키는 것일 터이다.\n\n# LATONA AND THE RUSTICS\n\n어떤 이들은 이 경우에 여신이 정당한 정도보다 더 가혹했다고 여겼고, 또 어떤 이들은 그녀의 처사가 처녀의 위엄에 엄격히 들어맞는다며 칭송했다. 으레 그렇듯, 최근의 사건이 더 오래된 이야기들을 떠올리게 했고, 곁에 있던 이들 가운데 하나가 이런 이야기를 들려주었다. \"리키아의 어떤 농부들이 언젠가 여신 레토[로마의 〈라토나〉]를 모욕했는데, 그러고도 벌을 면하지는 못했다네. 내가 젊었을 적에, 힘든 일을 하기에는 너무 늙으신 아버지께서 나를 리키아로 보내 그곳에서 좋은 소들을 몰고 오게 하셨는데, 거기서 나는 그 신기한 일이 일어났던 바로 그 못과 늪을 보았다네. 그 가까이에는 제물의 연기로 검게 그을려 갈대 사이에 거의 묻히다시피 한 오래된 제단이 하나 서 있었지. 나는 그것이 누구의 제단인지, 파우누스의 것인지 나이아데스의 것인지, 아니면 이웃한 산의 어떤 신의 것인지 물었더니, 그 고장 사람 하나가 대답하기를, '이 제단은 산이나 강의 신이 차지한 것이 아니라, 왕비 헤라가 질투에 사로잡혀 이 땅에서 저 땅으로 내쫓으며 그녀의 쌍둥이를 기를 땅 한 뼘조차 주지 않았던 그 여신의 것이라오.' 갓난 신들을 두 팔에 안은 채, 레토는 그 짐에 지치고 갈증에 목이 타며 이 땅에 다다랐다네. 그녀는 우연히 골짜기 바닥에서 이 맑은 물의 못을 발견했는데, 그곳에서는 고장 사람들이 버드나무와 고리버들을 거두는 일을 하고 있었지. 여신이 다가가 둑에 무릎을 꿇고 그 시원한 물로 갈증을 풀려 하자, 농부들이 이를 막았다네. '어찌하여 내게 물을 주지 않는 것이오?' 그녀가 말했지. '물은 누구에게나 자유로운 것이오. 자연은 햇빛이나 공기나 물을 그 누구도 제 소유로 주장하도록 허락지 않소. 나는 이 공통의 은혜에서 내 몫을 취하러 온 것이오. 그러나 나는 이를 호의로서 그대들에게 청하오. 비록 지치긴 했으나 그 안에서 사지를 씻을 생각은 없고, 다만 갈증을 가시려는 것뿐이오. 내 입이 어찌나 메말랐는지 말하기조차 힘드오. 물 한 모금이 내게는 신주(神酒)와 같을 것이며, 나를 되살아나게 할 터이니, 그러면 나는 그대들에게 목숨 그 자체를 빚졌노라 인정하겠소. 마치 나를 위해 애원하듯 작은 팔을 뻗고 있는 이 아기들을 보고 가엾이 여겨 주시오.' 그러자 마침 아기들이 작은 팔을 뻗고 있었다네.\n\n\"여신의 이런 부드러운 말에 마음이 움직이지 않을 자가 어디 있겠는가? 그러나 이 촌놈들은 무례함을 고집하며, 그녀가 그곳을 떠나지 않으면 폭력을 쓰겠다는 조롱과 위협까지 덧붙였지. 이뿐만이 아니었다네. 그들은 못으로 첨벙첨벙 걸어 들어가 발로 진흙을 휘저어 물을 마실 수 없게 만들어 버렸지. 레토는 어찌나 화가 났던지 갈증마저 잊고 말았다네. 그녀는 더 이상 촌놈들에게 빌지 않고, 두 손을 하늘로 들어 올리며 외쳤지. '저들이 결코 저 못을 떠나지 못하고, 그곳에서 평생을 보내게 하소서!' 그러자 그대로 이루어졌다네. 그들은 이제 물속에서 살아가며, 때로는 온몸을 물에 담그고, 때로는 수면 위로 머리를 내밀거나 그 위를 헤엄친다네. 때로는 둑으로 나오기도 하지만, 이내 다시 물속으로 뛰어들지. 그들은 여전히 그 천한 목소리로 욕설을 일삼으며, 물을 통째로 차지하고 있으면서도 그 한가운데서 개굴개굴 우는 것을 부끄러워하지 않는다네. 그들의 목소리는 거칠고, 목구멍은 부풀었으며, 입은 끊임없는 욕설로 늘어났고, 목은 오그라들어 사라졌으며, 머리는 몸뚱이에 붙어 버렸지. 등은 초록빛이고, 어울리지 않게 큰 배는 흰빛이니, 요컨대 그들은 이제 개구리가 되어 그 진창의 못에서 살고 있다네.\"\n\n이 이야기는 밀턴의 소네트 가운데 하나인 「어떤 논고들을 쓴 뒤에 따라온 비방에 부쳐」에 나오는 인유를 설명해 준다.\n\n\"나는 다만 옛 자유의 알려진 법으로써 이 시대더러 그 족쇄를 벗어 버리라 권했을 뿐인데, 그러자 곧 야만스러운 소음이 나를 에워쌌으니, 올빼미와 뻐꾸기, 당나귀, 원숭이, 개들의 소음이라. 마치 개구리로 변한 저 촌놈들이 레토의 쌍둥이 자손에게 욕설을 퍼붓던 것과 같으니, 그 자손은 훗날 해와 달을 영지로 거느렸도다.\"\n\n레토가 헤라에게서 받은 박해가 이 이야기 속에 언급되어 있다. 전해 내려오는 바에 따르면, 장차 아폴론과 아르테미스의 어머니가 될 그녀는 헤라의 분노를 피해 달아나며 에게해의 모든 섬에 쉴 곳을 마련해 달라고 간청했으나, 모든 섬이 하늘의 강력한 여왕을 너무도 두려워한 나머지 그녀의 연적을 돕지 못했다. 오직 델로스만이 장차 태어날 신들의 탄생지가 되는 데 동의했다. 델로스는 그때 떠다니는 섬이었으나, 레토가 그곳에 다다르자 제우스가 그것을 금강석 사슬로 바다 밑바닥에 붙들어 매어, 사랑하는 이를 위한 안전한 안식처가 되게 했다. 바이런은 「돈 후안」에서 델로스를 언급한다.\n\n\"그리스의 섬들이여! 그리스의 섬들이여! 불타는 사포가 사랑하고 노래하던 곳, 전쟁과 평화의 기예가 자라난 곳, 델로스가 솟아오르고 포이보스가 태어난 곳이여!\" ————\n\n파에톤은 아폴론과 님프 클리메네 사이에서 태어난 아들이었다. 어느 날 학교 친구 하나가 그가 신의 아들이라는 말을 비웃자, 파에톤은 분노와 수치심에 사로잡혀 어머니에게 그 일을 알렸다. \"어머니,\" 그가 말했다. \"제가 정말로 하늘의 혈통을 타고났다면, 그 증거를 보여 주셔서 제가 그 영예를 주장할 수 있게 해 주세요.\" 클리메네는 두 손을 하늘을 향해 뻗으며 말했다. \"우리를 굽어보시는 저 태양을 증인으로 부르노니, 나는 네게 진실을 말했다. 만약 내가 거짓을 말한다면, 이것이 내가 그분의 빛을 보는 마지막 순간이 되게 하라. 하지만 직접 가서 물어보는 데는 그다지 많은 수고가 들지 않을 게다. 태양이 떠오르는 땅은 바로 우리 땅 곁에 있으니 말이다. 가서 그분께 너를 아들로 인정하실지 여쭈어 보아라.\" 파에톤은 기뻐하며 그 말을 들었다. 그는 해 뜨는 지역에 곧장 자리한 인도로 여행을 떠났고, 희망과 자긍심에 가득 차 아버지가 하루의 운행을 시작하는 그 출발점으로 다가갔다.\n\n태양의 궁전은 기둥들 위에 높이 솟아 황금과 보석으로 반짝였고, 천장은 매끈하게 다듬은 상아로, 문은 은으로 이루어져 있었다. 그러나 솜씨는 재료를 능가했다.* 벽에는 헤파이스토스[로마의 〈불카누스〉]가 대지와 바다와 하늘을, 그리고 그곳에 사는 존재들을 새겨 놓았기 때문이다. 바다에는 님프들이 있었으니, 어떤 이들은 파도 속에서 노닐고, 어떤 이들은 물고기의 등을 타고 있었으며, 또 어떤 이들은 바위 위에 앉아 바다처럼 푸른 머리카락을 말리고 있었다. 그들의 얼굴은 모두 똑같지도, 그렇다고 서로 전혀 다르지도 않았으니, 자매들이 마땅히 그러해야 할 모습이었다. 대지에는 도시와 숲과 강과 시골의 신들이 있었다. 이 모든 것 위에는 찬란한 하늘의 형상이 새겨져 있었고, 은으로 된 문에는 황도십이궁의 별자리가 양쪽에 여섯씩 새겨져 있었다.\n\n클리메네의 아들은 가파른 오르막을 올라가 다툼의 대상이 된 아버지의 전당으로 들어섰다. 그는 아버지가 계신 곳으로 다가갔으나, 그 빛이 견딜 수 있는 것 이상이었으므로 멀찍이 떨어진 곳에서 걸음을 멈추었다. 포이보스는 자줏빛 옷을 두르고, 다이아몬드처럼 번쩍이는 옥좌에 앉아 있었다. 그의 좌우에는 〈하루〉와 〈한 달〉과 〈한 해〉가 서 있었고, 일정한 간격을 두고 〈시간〉들이 서 있었다. 〈봄〉은 머리에 꽃의 관을 쓰고 서 있었고, 〈여름〉은 옷을 벗어 던지고 익은 곡식 이삭으로 엮은 화환을 쓰고 있었으며, 〈가을〉은 발이 포도즙으로 물들어 있었고, 차가운 〈겨울〉은 머리카락이 서리로 뻣뻣하게 굳어 있었다. 이 시종들에 둘러싸인 채, 모든 것을 보는 눈을 지닌 태양은 그 광경의 새로움과 찬란함에 눈이 부신 젊은이를 바라보며 무슨 일로 왔는지 물었다. 젊은이가 대답했다. \"오, 끝없는 세상의 빛이시여, 포이보스여, 제 아버지시여 — 제가 그 이름을 쓰는 것을 허락하신다면 — 제가 당신의 아들임을 알 수 있도록, 간청하건대 어떤 증거를 보여 주십시오.\" 그가 말을 마치자, 그의 아버지는 머리 사방에서 빛나던 광선을 거두고 그를 가까이 오라 불러 끌어안으며 말했다. \"내 아들아, 너는 부인당할 만한 아이가 아니다. 나는 네 어머니가 네게 한 말을 확인해 주마. 네 의심을 끝내기 위해, 무엇이든 원하는 것을 청하여라, 그 선물을 네게 주겠다. 내가 한 번도 본 적은 없으나 우리 신들이 가장 엄숙한 약속을 맺을 때 두고 맹세하는 저 무서운 호수를 증인으로 부르노라.\" 파에톤은 곧바로 하루 동안 태양의 마차를 몰게 해 달라고 청했다. 아버지는 자신의 약속을 후회했고, 세 번 네 번 빛나는 머리를 가로저으며 경고했다. \"내가 경솔하게 말했구나,\" 그가 말했다. \"이 한 가지 청만은 거절하고 싶다. 부디 거두어다오. 그것은 안전한 선물이 아니며, 내 아들 파에톤아, 네 젊음과 힘에 어울리는 것도 아니다. 네 운명은 필멸의 것인데, 너는 필멸자의 능력을 넘어서는 것을 청하고 있다. 너는 무지한 탓에 신들 자신조차 할 수 없는 일을 하려 한다. 나 말고는 그 누구도 하루의 불타는 마차를 몰 수 없다. 무서운 오른팔로 벼락을 던지는 제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]조차도 그러하다. 그 길의 첫 부분은 가파르니, 아침에 기운이 넘치는 말들조차 좀처럼 오르기 힘들 정도다. 한가운데는 하늘 높은 곳에 있어, 나 자신조차 두려움 없이는 아래를 굽어보며 내 발밑에 펼쳐진 대지와 바다를 바라볼 수 없다. 길의 마지막 부분은 가파르게 내려가니, 무엇보다 조심스러운 운전이 필요하다. 나를 맞이하려 기다리는 테티스는 내가 곤두박질치지 않을까 나를 위해 자주 떤다. 게다가 하늘은 내내 회전하면서 별들을 함께 데리고 돈다. 다른 모든 것을 휩쓸어 가는 그 움직임이 나마저 채어 가지 않도록 나는 끊임없이 경계해야 한다. 가령 내가 네게 마차를 빌려 준다면, 너는 어떻게 하겠느냐? 천구가 네 아래에서 도는 동안 네가 길을 지킬 수 있겠느냐? 어쩌면 너는 길에 숲과 도시들, 신들의 거처와 궁전과 신전이 있으리라 생각할지도 모르겠다. 그러나 그와는 반대로, 그 길은 무시무시한 괴물들 한가운데를 지나간다. 너는 〈황소〉의 뿔 곁을 지나고, 〈궁수〉 앞을 지나며, 〈사자〉의 턱 가까이를, 그리고 〈전갈〉이 한 방향으로 팔을 뻗고 〈게〉가 다른 방향으로 팔을 뻗은 곳을 지나게 된다. 또한 입과 콧구멍으로 내뿜는 불로 가슴이 가득한 그 말들을 부리는 일도 쉽지 않으리라. 그것들이 다루기 힘들어져 고삐에 저항할 때면 나조차도 좀처럼 다스리지 못한다. 조심하여라, 내 아들아, 내가 치명적인 선물을 주는 자가 되지 않도록 말이다. 아직 그럴 수 있을 때 네 청을 거두어라. 네가 내 피에서 났다는 증거를 청하느냐? 너를 향한 내 두려움 속에 그 증거를 주마. 내 얼굴을 보아라 — 네가 내 가슴 속을 들여다볼 수 있다면, 거기서 아버지의 온갖 근심을 보게 되련만. 끝으로,\" 그가 이어 말했다. \"세상을 둘러보고 대지와 바다가 품은 가장 귀한 것 가운데 무엇이든 골라 보아라 — 그것을 청하면 거절을 두려워하지 않아도 된다. 다만 이 한 가지만은 고집하지 말기를 빈다. 네가 구하는 것은 영예가 아니라 파멸이다. 어찌하여 내 목에 매달려 여전히 간청하느냐? 네가 고집한다면 그것을 갖게 되리라 — 맹세는 이미 이루어졌으니 지켜야 한다 — 하지만 부디 더 현명하게 고르기를 바란다.\"\n\n그가 말을 마쳤다. 그러나 젊은이는 모든 충고를 물리치고 자신의 요구를 굽히지 않았다. 그리하여 할 수 있는 한 오래 버티던 포이보스는 마침내 높다란 마차가 서 있는 곳으로 그를 이끌었다.\n\n그것은 헤파이스토스가 선물한 황금 마차였다. 굴대도 황금이었고, 끌채와 바퀴도 황금이었으며, 바큇살은 은이었다. 좌석을 따라 감람석과 다이아몬드가 줄지어 박혀 태양의 광채를 사방으로 반사했다. 대담한 젊은이가 감탄하며 바라보는 동안, 이른 〈새벽〉이 동쪽의 자줏빛 문을 활짝 열어젖히고 장미가 흩뿌려진 길을 드러냈다. 별들은 〈샛별〉의 인도를 받아 물러갔고, 샛별 자신도 마지막으로 물러갔다. 아버지는 대지가 빛나기 시작하고 〈달〉이 물러갈 채비를 하는 것을 보자, 〈시간〉들에게 말들에 멍에를 메우라 명했다. 그들은 명에 따라, 신들의 음식을 잔뜩 먹은 준마들을 높은 마구간에서 끌어내어 고삐를 채웠다. 그러고 나서 아버지는 강력한 연고를 아들의 얼굴에 발라, 불꽃의 광채를 견딜 수 있게 했다. 그는 아들의 머리에 광선을 씌우고는, 불길한 한숨을 내쉬며 말했다. \"내 아들아, 적어도 이 일에서만큼은 내 충고를 따른다면, 채찍은 아끼고 고삐는 단단히 쥐어라. 그것들은 제 스스로 충분히 빨리 달리니, 어려운 일은 그것들을 붙드는 것이다. 너는 다섯 개의 둘레 사이를 곧장 지나는 직선 길을 택해서는 안 되고, 왼쪽으로 비껴가야 한다. 가운데 띠의 경계 안에 머물면서 북쪽과 남쪽을 똑같이 피하여라. 바퀴 자국이 보일 테니, 그것이 너를 인도하는 데 도움이 될 것이다. 그리고 하늘과 대지가 저마다 알맞은 몫의 열기를 받도록, 너무 높이 가지 마라, 그러면 천상의 거처를 태우게 될 것이다. 그렇다고 너무 낮게도 가지 마라, 그러면 대지에 불을 지르게 될 것이다. 가운데 길이 가장 안전하고 가장 좋다. 그리고 이제 나는 너를 운에 맡기노니, 그 운이 네가 스스로를 위해 꾀한 것보다 더 잘 헤아려 주기를 바란다. 〈밤〉이 서쪽 문을 빠져나가고 있으니 우리는 더 지체할 수 없다. 고삐를 잡아라. 하지만 마지막에 네 마음이 약해져 내 충고를 따르고자 한다면, 안전하게 있는 그 자리에 머물러 내가 대지를 비추고 덥히도록 맡겨 두어라.\" 날렵한 젊은이는 마차로 뛰어올라 똑바로 서서 고삐를 기쁘게 거머쥐고는, 마지못해 허락한 아버지에게 감사를 쏟아 냈다.\n\n그러는 동안 말들은 콧김과 불타는 숨결로 공기를 채우고 초조하게 땅을 굴렀다. 이제 가로막이 내려지고, 우주의 끝없는 평원이 그들 앞에 활짝 펼쳐졌다. 그들은 앞으로 내달려 가로막는 구름을 가르고, 같은 동쪽 출발점에서 출발한 아침의 산들바람을 앞질렀다. 준마들은 곧 자신들이 끄는 짐이 평소보다 가볍다는 것을 알아차렸다. 짐을 싣지 않은 배가 바다 위에서 이리저리 흔들리듯이, 익숙한 무게를 싣지 않은 마차도 빈 것처럼 마구 요동쳤다. 말들은 곤두박질치듯 내달려 다니던 길을 벗어났다. 그는 놀라서 그것들을 어떻게 이끌어야 할지 몰랐고, 설령 안다 해도 그럴 힘이 없었다. 그때 처음으로 〈큰곰〉과 〈작은곰〉이 열기에 그을렸고, 할 수만 있다면 물속에 뛰어들고 싶어 했다. 북극 둘레에 똬리를 틀고 무기력하게 누워 해를 끼치지 않던 〈뱀〉도 따뜻해졌고, 그 온기와 더불어 분노가 되살아나는 것을 느꼈다. 사람들이 말하기를, 〈목동자리〉도 달아났다고 한다. 비록 쟁기에 매여 거추장스럽고 빠른 움직임에는 전혀 익숙하지 않았는데도 말이다.\n\n불운한 파에톤이 이제 자기 발밑에 광대하게 펼쳐진 대지를 내려다보았을 때, 그는 창백해졌고 무릎이 공포로 떨렸다. 사방에서 쏟아지는 눈부신 빛에도 불구하고, 그의 눈앞은 흐릿해졌다. 그는 차라리 아버지의 말들을 결코 만지지 않았더라면, 자신의 혈통을 결코 알지 못했더라면, 자신의 청을 결코 관철하지 못했더라면 하고 바랐다. 그는 폭풍 앞에서 도망치는 배처럼 휩쓸려 갔으니, 그럴 때면 키잡이는 더 이상 손쓸 도리가 없어 기도에 의지할 따름이다. 그는 무엇을 해야 하는가? 하늘 길의 많은 부분이 뒤에 남겨졌으나, 더 많은 부분이 앞에 남아 있다. 그는 이쪽저쪽으로 눈을 돌렸으니, 한 번은 자신이 운행을 시작한 출발점으로, 또 한 번은 자신이 도달할 운명이 아닌 해 지는 영역으로 향했다. 그는 자제력을 잃고 무엇을 해야 할지 몰랐으니 — 고삐를 단단히 당겨야 할지 느슨하게 놓아야 할지 알 수 없었고, 말들의 이름도 잊어버렸다. 그는 하늘 표면에 흩어져 있는 괴물 같은 형상들을 공포에 질려 바라보았다. 그곳에서 〈전갈〉이 두 개의 거대한 팔을 뻗고, 꼬리와 굽은 집게가 황도의 두 별자리에 걸쳐 펼쳐져 있었다. 소년이 독을 내뿜으며 송곳니로 위협하는 그것을 보았을 때, 그의 용기는 꺾였고 고삐가 그의 손에서 떨어졌다. 말들은 고삐가 등 위에서 느슨해진 것을 느끼자 곤두박질치듯 내달려, 제지받지 않은 채 하늘의 알 수 없는 영역으로, 별들 사이로 달려 들어갔고, 길도 없는 곳들 위로 마차를 내던지듯 몰아, 어느 때는 하늘 높이, 어느 때는 거의 대지에까지 내려왔다. 달은 자기 오라비의 마차가 자기 마차 아래로 내달리는 것을 놀라움에 차서 바라보았다. 구름은 연기를 뿜기 시작하고, 산꼭대기는 불이 붙었다. 들판은 열기에 메말랐고, 식물은 시들었으며, 잎이 무성한 가지를 단 나무들은 타올랐고, 곡식은 불길에 휩싸였다! 그러나 이것들은 작은 일이었다. 거대한 도시들이 성벽과 탑과 함께 멸망했고, 온 민족이 그 백성과 더불어 잿더미가 되었다! 숲으로 덮인 산들이 불탔으니, 아토스와 타우로스와 트몰로스와 오이테가 그러했고, 한때 샘들로 유명했으나 이제는 온통 메말라 버린 이데가 그러했으며, 무사 여신들의 산 헬리콘과 하이모스가 그러했고, 안팎으로 불을 품은 아이트나와 두 봉우리를 지닌 파르나소스, 그리고 마침내 눈 덮인 관을 잃어야 했던 로도페가 그러했다. 차가운 기후도 스키티아를 지켜 주지 못했고, 카우카소스가 불탔으며, 오사와 핀도스가, 그리고 그 둘보다 더 큰 올림포스가 불탔고, 공중에 높이 솟은 알프스와 구름이 관처럼 덮인 아펜니노스가 불탔다.\n\n그때 파에톤은 세상이 불타는 것을 보았고, 견딜 수 없는 열기를 느꼈다. 그가 들이쉬는 공기는 용광로의 공기와도 같았고 불타는 재로 가득했으며, 연기는 칠흑같이 어두웠다. 그는 어디로 가는지도 모른 채 앞으로 내달렸다. 그때 사람들이 믿기로는, 에티오피아의 사람들이 피가 너무도 갑작스럽게 표면으로 솟구쳐 검게 되었고, 리비아 사막은 오늘날까지 그대로 남아 있는 상태로 메말라 버렸다. 샘의 님프들은 머리를 풀어헤친 채 자신들의 물을 애도했고, 강들도 그 둑 아래에서 안전하지 못했다. 타나이스가 연기를 뿜었고, 카이코스와 크산토스와 마이안드로스도 그러했으며, 바빌론의 에우프라테스와 갠지스, 황금 모래를 품은 타구스, 그리고 백조들이 모여드는 카위스트로스도 그러했다. 나일은 달아나 사막에 머리를 감추었고, 그곳에 지금도 그것이 숨겨진 채 남아 있다. 그가 일곱 개의 어귀를 통해 바다로 물을 쏟아 내던 곳에는, 이제 일곱 개의 메마른 물길만이 남았다. 대지가 갈라져 벌어졌고, 그 틈을 통해 빛이 타르타로스로 새어 들어가, 그림자들의 왕과 그 왕비를 놀라게 했다. 바다는 오그라들었다. 전에 물이 있던 곳은 메마른 평원이 되었고, 파도 아래 누워 있던 산들은 머리를 들어 올려 섬이 되었다. 물고기들은 가장 깊은 곳을 찾아갔고, 돌고래들도 더 이상 평소처럼 감히 표면에서 노닐려 하지 않았다. 네레우스와 그의 아내 도리스, 그리고 그들의 딸들인 네레이데스조차도 가장 깊은 동굴로 피신처를 찾아갔다. 포세이돈[로마의 〈넵투누스〉]은 세 번이나 표면 위로 머리를 들려고 애썼으나, 세 번 다 열기에 밀려 물러났다. 대지는 비록 물에 둘러싸여 있었으나 머리와 어깨를 드러낸 채, 손으로 얼굴을 가리고 하늘을 우러러보며 쉰 목소리로 제우스를 불렀다.\n\n\"오, 신들의 통치자시여, 제가 이런 대접을 받아 마땅하고 제가 불에 멸망하는 것이 당신의 뜻이라면, 어찌하여 벼락을 거두고 계십니까? 적어도 당신의 손에 쓰러지게 해 주십시오. 이것이 제 비옥함에 대한, 제 순종하는 봉사에 대한 보답입니까? 제가 가축에게 풀을, 사람에게 열매를, 당신의 제단에 유향을 바쳐 온 것이 이런 결과를 위해서였습니까? 그러나 제가 돌봄을 받을 가치가 없다 해도, 제 형제 오케아노스가 이런 운명을 받아 마땅할 무슨 짓을 했단 말입니까? 우리 둘 중 누구도 당신의 연민을 불러일으키지 못한다면, 부디 당신 자신의 하늘을 생각해 보십시오. 당신의 궁전을 떠받치는 두 극점이 어떻게 연기를 뿜고 있는지 보십시오. 그것들이 파괴되면 궁전도 무너질 것입니다. 아틀라스가 기진하여 제 짐을 좀처럼 떠받치지 못합니다. 바다와 대지와 하늘이 멸망한다면, 우리는 태고의 〈혼돈〉 속으로 떨어집니다. 우리에게 아직 남은 것을 집어삼키는 불길로부터 구해 주십시오. 오, 이 무서운 순간에 우리의 구원을 헤아려 주십시오!\"\n\n이렇게 대지가 말했으나, 열기와 갈증에 압도되어 더 이상 말할 수 없었다. 그러자 전능하신 제우스는, 마차를 빌려 준 자를 포함하여 모든 신들을 증인으로 부르고, 신속한 처방이 가해지지 않으면 모든 것이 끝장이라는 것을 그들에게 보여 준 뒤, 대지 위에 구름을 펼치고 갈래진 번개를 내던지는 높은 탑에 올랐다. 그러나 그때는 대지를 가려 줄 가림막으로 끼어들 구름 한 점 찾을 수 없었고, 다 쏟아 내지 않고 남은 소나기 한 줄기 없었다. 그는 천둥을 울렸고, 오른손에 번개를 휘둘러 마차몰이를 향해 던져, 그를 좌석에서, 그리고 존재로부터 동시에 쳐 떨어뜨렸다! 머리에 불이 붙은 파에톤은, 떨어지면서 그 광채로 하늘에 자국을 남기는 별똥별처럼 곤두박질쳤고, 거대한 강 에리다노스가 그를 받아들여 불타는 몸을 식혀 주었다. 이탈리아의 나이아데스가 그를 위해 무덤을 세우고, 돌 위에 이런 글귀를 새겼다.\n\n\"포이보스의 마차몰이꾼 파에톤이, 유피테르의 벼락에 맞아 이 돌 아래 잠들다. 그는 아버지의 불의 마차를 다스리지 못했으나, 그토록 고귀하게 뜻을 품었음은 적지 않은 일이었네.\"\n\n그의 누이들인 헬리아데스는 그의 운명을 슬퍼하다가 강둑의 포플러 나무로 변했고, 끊임없이 흐르던 그들의 눈물은 시냇물 속으로 떨어지면서 호박이 되었다.\n\n밀먼은 그의 시 「사모르」에서 파에톤의 이야기를 다음과 같이 암시한다.\n\n\"마비된 우주가 경악하여 말없이 고요히 누워 있을 때처럼······ 시인들이 노래하듯, 태양에서 난 젊은이가 하늘의 겁먹은 별자리들을 가로질러 아버지가 잘못 허락한 마차를 빗나가게 몰았을 때. 〈천둥을 부리는 이〉가 그를 가장 높은 하늘에서 곤두박질쳐 반쯤 메마른 에리다노스의 심연으로 내던졌으니, 그곳에서 누이 나무들이 지금도 호박의 눈물을 흘리네 때 이르게 죽은 파에톤을 위하여.\"\n\n월터 새비지 랜더의 아름다운 시구, 〈바다 조개〉를 묘사한 대목에는 태양의 궁전과 마차에 대한 암시가 있다. 물의 님프는 이렇게 말한다.\n\n\"······나는 진주빛 색조를 띤 구불구불한 조개들을 품고 있어요, 그리고 태양의 궁전 현관에서 광채를 빨아들인 것들도 품고 있지요, 그곳에서 멍에를 벗으면 그분의 마차 바퀴가 파도 한가운데에 멈춰 서지요. 하나를 흔들어 보면 그것이 깨어나요, 그러면 그 매끄러운 입술을 귀 기울인 당신의 귀에 대 보세요, 그러면 그것은 자신의 장엄한 거처를 기억하고, 그곳의 대양이 속삭이듯 속삭인답니다.\" ─「게비르」, 제1권.\n\n────\n\n# MIDAS—BAUCIS AND PHILEMON\n\n디오니소스[로마의 〈바쿠스〉]는 어느 날 자신의 옛 스승이자 양아버지인 실레노스가 사라진 것을 알게 되었다. 그 노인은 술을 마신 뒤 그 상태로 떠돌아다니다가 몇몇 농부에게 발견되었는데, 농부들은 그를 자기네 왕인 미다스에게 데려갔다. 미다스는 그를 알아보고 정중하게 대접하여, 열흘 밤낮을 끊임없는 잔치로 융숭히 맞이했다. 열하루째 되는 날 그는 실레노스를 데리고 돌아와, 무사히 그 제자에게 돌려보냈다. 그러자 디오니소스는 미다스에게 무엇이든 원하는 것을 보답으로 고르라고 했다. 미다스는 자신이 무엇을 만지든 그것이 황금으로 바뀌게 해 달라고 청했다. 디오니소스는 더 나은 선택을 하지 않은 것을 안타까워하면서도 그 청을 들어주었다. 미다스는 새로 얻은 능력에 기뻐하며 길을 떠났고, 서둘러 그것을 시험해 보았다. 그는 떡갈나무 가지에서 잔가지 하나를 꺾었는데 그것이 손안에서 황금으로 변하는 것을 보고 자기 눈을 믿을 수 없었다. 그가 돌멩이 하나를 집어 드니 그것도 황금이 되었다. 그가 잔디 한 떼를 건드리니 그것도 마찬가지였다. 그가 나무에서 사과 하나를 따니, 누구라도 그가 헤스페리데스의 정원을 약탈했다고 여겼으리라. 그의 기쁨은 끝이 없었고, 집에 돌아오자마자 그는 하인들에게 식탁에 성대한 식사를 차리라고 명했다. 그런데 그는 낭패스럽게도 빵을 만지면 손안에서 빵이 딱딱해지고, 한 조각을 입술에 갖다 대면 이로 깨물 수가 없다는 것을 알게 되었다. 그가 포도주 한 잔을 들었으나, 그것은 녹은 황금처럼 목구멍으로 흘러내렸다.\n\n전례 없는 이 고통에 경악한 그는 자신의 능력을 떨쳐 버리려 애썼다. 그는 얼마 전까지 그토록 탐내던 그 선물을 미워하게 되었다. 그러나 모든 것이 헛수고였으니, 굶주림이 그를 기다리는 듯했다. 그는 온통 황금으로 번쩍이는 두 팔을 들어 디오니소스에게 기도하며, 이 반짝이는 파멸에서 자신을 구해 달라고 빌었다. 자비로운 신 디오니소스는 그 청을 듣고 들어주었다. \"가거라.\" 그가 말했다. \"팍톨로스 강으로 가서, 물줄기를 따라 그 발원지까지 거슬러 올라가, 거기서 머리와 몸을 물에 담그고, 너의 잘못과 그 벌을 함께 씻어 내거라.\" 미다스는 그대로 했고, 물에 닿자마자 황금을 만들어 내는 능력이 그 물속으로 옮겨 가니, 강의 모래는 황금으로 변하여 오늘날까지도 그대로 남아 있다.\n\n그때부터 미다스는 부와 화려함을 싫어하며 시골에 머물렀고, 들판의 신 판을 섬기는 자가 되었다. 어느 날 판은 자신의 음악을 아폴론의 음악에 견주는 무모함을 부려, 리라의 신에게 솜씨를 겨루자고 도전했다. 도전은 받아들여졌고, 산의 신 트몰로스가 심판으로 뽑혔다. 이 연장자는 자리에 앉아, 귀에서 나무들을 치워 들을 수 있게 했다. 신호가 떨어지자 판은 자신의 피리를 불었고, 그 투박한 가락으로 자기 자신과 마침 그 자리에 있던 충실한 추종자 미다스를 크게 만족시켰다. 그러자 트몰로스가 태양신 쪽으로 머리를 돌리니, 그의 모든 나무도 그와 함께 돌아섰다. 아폴론이 일어섰는데, 그의 이마에는 파르나소스의 월계관이 둘려 있었고, 티로스산 자줏빛 옷자락은 땅을 쓸었다. 그는 왼손에 리라를 들고, 오른손으로 줄을 튕겼다. 그 선율에 넋을 빼앗긴 트몰로스는 즉시 리라의 신에게 승리를 안겼고, 미다스를 제외한 모두가 그 판정에 동의했다. 그는 동의하지 않았고, 그 판정의 정당함에 이의를 제기했다. 아폴론은 그토록 저열한 귀 한 쌍이 더 이상 인간의 모습을 지니는 것을 용납하지 않았으니, 그 귀들이 길게 늘어나고, 안팎으로 털이 자라며, 뿌리에서 움직일 수 있게 했다. 한마디로, 당나귀의 귀와 완전히 똑같은 모양이 되도록 한 것이다.\n\n이 불행에 미다스 왕은 적잖이 굴욕을 느꼈지만, 자신의 불운을 감출 수 있으리라는 생각으로 스스로를 위로했다. 그는 넉넉한 터번 혹은 머리쓰개를 써서 그렇게 하려고 했다. 그러나 그의 이발사만은 당연히 그 비밀을 알고 있었다. 이발사는 그 일을 입 밖에 내지 말라는 명을 받았으며, 감히 어기려 들면 끔찍한 벌을 받으리라는 위협도 함께 받았다. 그러나 그는 그런 비밀을 지키는 것이 자신의 분별력으로는 너무 벅차다는 것을 알게 되었다. 그래서 그는 풀밭으로 나가 땅에 구멍을 파고, 몸을 굽혀 그 이야기를 속삭인 다음 흙으로 덮어 버렸다. 머지않아 그 풀밭에 갈대가 빽빽이 돋아났고, 다 자라자마자 그 이야기를 속삭이기 시작하여, 그날 이후 오늘날까지 그곳에 산들바람이 지날 때마다 그렇게 속삭여 오고 있다.\n\n미다스 왕의 이야기는 다른 사람들에 의해 약간씩 변형되어 전해지기도 했다. 드라이든은 「바스 여인의 이야기」에서 미다스의 왕비를 비밀을 누설한 자로 삼는다.\n\n\"이 미다스는 그것을 알았으나, 감히 털어놓을 수 있었던 것은 오직 아내에게뿐, 자신의 위엄 어린 귀에 관해서.\"\n\n미다스는 프리기아의 왕이었다. 그는 고르디오스의 아들이었는데, 고르디오스는 가난한 시골 사람이었으나 백성들에게 추대되어 왕이 되었으니, 이는 장차 그들의 왕이 수레를 타고 오리라고 했던 신탁의 명에 따른 것이었다. 백성들이 논의하고 있는 동안, 고르디오스가 아내와 아들을 데리고 수레를 몰아 광장으로 들어왔던 것이다.\n\n왕이 된 고르디오스는 자신의 수레를 신탁의 신에게 바치고, 그것을 단단한 매듭으로 제자리에 묶어 두었다. 이것이 그 유명한 고르디오스의 매듭으로, 훗날 누구든 이것을 푸는 자가 온 아시아의 주인이 되리라는 말이 전해졌다. 많은 이들이 그것을 풀려 했으나 아무도 성공하지 못했는데, 마침내 알렉산드로스 대왕이 정복의 행로 가운데 프리기아에 이르렀다. 그도 다른 이들 못지않게 실패만 거듭하며 솜씨를 시험하다가, 참다못해 칼을 뽑아 매듭을 잘라 버렸다. 훗날 그가 온 아시아를 자신의 지배 아래 복속시키는 데 성공하자, 사람들은 그가 신탁의 참뜻에 따라 그 조건을 충족시켰다고 여기기 시작했다.\n바우키스와 필레몬\n\n# BAUCIS AND PHILEMON\n\n프리기아의 어느 언덕에 보리수 한 그루와 떡갈나무 한 그루가 낮은 담장에 둘러싸여 서 있다. 그곳에서 멀지 않은 데에 늪이 하나 있는데, 예전에는 사람이 살 만한 좋은 땅이었으나 지금은 못이 군데군데 패여, 늪새와 가마우지가 모여드는 곳이 되었다. 옛날 어느 때에 제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]가 인간의 모습으로 이 고장을 찾아왔는데, 그와 함께 그의 아들 헤르메스[로마의 〈메르쿠리우스〉], 곧 카두케우스를 지닌 신이 날개 없이 동행했다. 그들은 지친 나그네의 모습으로 여러 집 문 앞에 나타나 쉴 곳과 머물 곳을 구했으나, 모든 문이 닫혀 있음을 보았으니, 때는 이미 늦었고 손님을 박대하는 그 주민들이 일어나 그들을 맞으려고 문을 열어 주지 않았기 때문이다. 마침내 한 초라한 거처가 그들을 받아 주었으니, 작은 초가집이었다. 그곳에는 경건한 노부인 바우키스와 그녀의 남편 필레몬이 젊어서 맺어져 함께 늙어 가고 있었다. 그들은 자신들의 가난을 부끄러워하지 않았고, 욕심을 줄이고 마음을 곱게 씀으로써 그 가난을 견딜 만한 것으로 만들었다. 그곳에서는 주인이나 하인을 따로 찾을 필요가 없었으니, 그 두 사람이 온 식구로서 주인이자 하인이었다. 두 천상의 손님이 그 초라한 문지방을 넘으며 낮은 문 아래를 지나려고 고개를 숙이자, 노인은 자리 하나를 놓았고, 바우키스가 부지런히 마음을 써서 그 위에 천을 펴고는 그들에게 앉기를 청했다. 그런 다음 그녀는 잿더미에서 숯을 긁어내어 불을 지피고, 나뭇잎과 마른 껍질로 불을 키웠으며, 가쁜 숨을 불어넣어 불꽃을 일으켰다. 그녀는 구석에서 쪼갠 막대기와 마른 가지를 꺼내 와 부러뜨려 작은 솥 밑에 놓았다. 그녀의 남편은 텃밭에서 채소를 좀 거두어 왔고, 그녀는 그것을 줄기에서 다듬어 솥에 넣을 채비를 했다. 남편은 끝이 갈라진 막대기로 굴뚝에 매달린 베이컨 한 덩이를 내려, 작은 조각 하나를 잘라 채소와 함께 끓이려고 솥에 넣고, 나머지는 다음을 위해 따로 치워 두었다. 너도밤나무로 만든 대접에는 따뜻한 물이 채워졌으니, 손님들이 씻을 수 있게 하기 위함이었다. 모든 일이 진행되는 동안, 그들은 이야기를 나누며 시간을 보냈다.\n\n손님들을 위해 마련된 의자에는 해초를 채운 방석이 놓였고, 큰일이 있을 때에만 꺼내는, 그러나 충분히 오래되고 거친 천 한 장이 그 위에 펼쳐졌다. 노부인은 앞치마를 두른 채 떨리는 손으로 식탁을 차렸다. 다리 하나가 나머지보다 짧았으나, 그 밑에 석판 조각 하나를 받치니 수평이 잡혔다. 자리가 잡히자 그녀는 향기로운 풀로 식탁을 닦았다. 그 위에 그녀는 정결한 아테나[로마의 〈미네르바〉]의 올리브 몇 알, 식초에 절인 산딸기 몇 알을 차렸고, 무와 치즈를 더했으며, 잿불에 살짝 익힌 달걀도 곁들였다. 모든 것이 흙으로 빚은 그릇에 담겨 나왔고, 흙으로 만든 물병과 나무 잔이 그 곁에 놓였다. 모든 준비가 끝나자, 김이 모락모락 나는 뜨거운 스튜가 식탁에 올랐다. 가장 오래된 것은 아니나 포도주도 곁들여졌고, 후식으로는 사과와 야생 꿀이 나왔다. 그리고 그 모든 것을 넘어, 다정한 얼굴들과 소박하지만 진심 어린 환대가 있었다.\n\n식사가 진행되는 동안, 노부부는 포도주가 따라지는 족족 저절로 물병 안에서 다시 채워지는 것을 보고 놀랐다. 두려움에 사로잡힌 바우키스와 필레몬은 자신들의 천상의 손님을 알아보고, 무릎을 꿇고 두 손을 맞잡아 변변찮은 대접을 용서해 달라고 빌었다. 그들에게는 초라한 오두막의 파수꾼으로 기르던 늙은 거위 한 마리가 있었다. 그들은 이 거위를 손님들을 기리는 제물로 바치기로 생각했다. 그러나 거위는 노부부에게는 너무 날렵하여, 발과 날개의 도움으로 그들의 추격을 피해 다니다가, 마침내 신들 자신의 사이로 피신했다. 신들은 거위를 죽이지 못하게 하고는 이렇게 말했다. \"우리는 신이다. 손님을 박대하는 이 마을은 그 불경의 대가를 치르게 되리라. 너희만은 그 징벌에서 벗어나리라. 너희 집을 떠나 우리와 함께 저 언덕 꼭대기로 오라.\" 그들은 서둘러 따랐고, 지팡이를 손에 쥐고 가파른 오르막을 힘겹게 올랐다. 그들이 꼭대기에서 화살이 닿을 만한 거리에 이르렀을 때, 아래로 눈을 돌리니 온 고장이 호수 아래 잠기고 오직 자신들의 집만이 서 있는 것이 보였다. 그들이 그 광경을 경이로움에 잠겨 바라보며 이웃들의 운명을 탄식하고 있는 동안, 그들의 그 낡은 집은 신전으로 바뀌었다. 모서리 기둥 자리에는 원주들이 들어섰고, 초가지붕은 노랗게 변해 황금빛 지붕으로 나타났으며, 바닥은 대리석이 되었고, 문은 조각과 황금 장식으로 화려해졌다. 그러자 제우스가 인자한 어조로 말했다. \"훌륭한 노인이여, 그리고 그러한 남편에게 어울리는 부인이여, 말해 보라, 너희의 소원을 말해 보라. 우리에게 무슨 청을 하고 싶은가?\" 필레몬은 잠시 바우키스와 의논한 다음, 신들에게 두 사람이 함께 바라는 바를 밝혔다. \"저희는 이 당신의 신전의 사제이자 파수꾼이 되기를 청합니다. 그리고 저희가 여기서 사랑과 화목 속에 일생을 보냈으니, 똑같은 한 시각에 저희 둘을 한꺼번에 이 삶에서 데려가 주시기를 바랍니다. 그리하여 제가 살아남아 아내의 무덤을 보지 않게 하시고, 또 아내의 손으로 제 무덤에 누이지 않게 해 주소서.\" 그들의 기도는 들어주어졌다. 그들은 사는 동안 그 신전의 지킴이로 지냈다. 아주 늙었을 때, 어느 날 그들이 그 신성한 건물의 계단 앞에 서서 그곳의 내력을 이야기하고 있노라니, 바우키스는 필레몬이 잎을 내밀기 시작하는 것을 보았고, 늙은 필레몬은 바우키스가 똑같이 변해 가는 것을 보았다. 그리고 이제 잎이 무성한 관이 그들의 머리 위로 자라났고, 그동안 그들은 말할 수 있는 한 작별의 말을 주고받았다. \"잘 가오, 사랑하는 이여.\" 그들은 함께 말했고, 바로 그 순간 나무껍질이 그들의 입 위로 닫혔다. 티아나의 목동은 지금도 그 두 선량한 노부부로 만들어진 두 그루의 나무가 나란히 서 있는 것을 보여 준다.\n\n바우키스와 필레몬의 이야기는 스위프트가 익살스러운 문체로 모방한 바 있는데, 거기서는 변화를 겪는 이들이 떠돌이 성자 둘이고, 집은 교회로 바뀌며, 필레몬은 그 교회의 목사가 된다. 다음 대목이 그 본보기가 될 만하다.\n\n\"그들이 채 말을 마치기도 전에, 곱고도 부드럽게 지붕이 위로 솟아오르기 시작했네. 들보와 서까래가 모두 위로 솟았고, 무거운 벽이 천천히 그 뒤를 따라 기어올랐네. 굴뚝은 넓어지고 더 높이 자라나 첨탑을 인 종루가 되었네. 솥은 꼭대기까지 끌어올려져 들보에 단단히 매달려 섰는데, 위아래가 뒤집힌 채로, 아래를 향한 그 마음을 드러내었네. 헛되이도, 밑에서 가해진 더 강한 힘이 그 움직임을 막으니, 영영 매달려 지내도록 운명 지어져, 이제 그것은 솥이 아니라 종이 되었네. 오랜 쓰임 없음에 고기 굽는 재주를 거의 잃어버렸던 나무 회전구이틀은 돌연한 변화를 느꼈네. 새로운 안쪽 톱니바퀴들이 더해지고, 더욱 놀라운 것은, 그 수가 늘수록 움직임은 느려졌다는 것. 납으로 만든 발을 가졌건만 날개바퀴는 너무 빨리 돌아 눈으로 좇기 어려웠는데, 어떤 은밀한 힘에 누그러져 이제는 한 시간에 한 치도 겨우 움직이네. 회전구이틀과 굴뚝은 가까운 사이라 서로의 곁을 결코 떠난 적이 없었네. 굴뚝이 첨탑으로 자라나니, 회전구이틀은 홀로 남으려 하지 않고 첨탑에 기대어 곧추서서 시계가 되어 여전히 붙어 있었네. 그리고 살림살이에 대한 그 애정을 여전히 정오마다 날카로운 목소리로 알리며, 돌릴 수 없는 그 구이 고기를 태우지 말라고 부엌데기에게 일러 주네. 삐걱대는 의자는 기어가기 시작했네, 거대한 달팽이처럼 벽을 따라. 그리하여 높이 매달려 모두의 눈앞에 드러나, 작은 변화로 설교단이 되어 자라났네. 옛 양식의 침대틀은, 수레로 여러 짐의 목재를 들여 짠 것으로, 우리 조상들이 쓰던 그런 것이었는데, 교회 신도석으로 탈바꿈하였네. 그것들은 여전히 옛 본성을 지키고 있으니, 잠들고 싶어 하는 이들을 재워 줌으로써 그러하네.\"\n\n────\n\n# PROSERPINE—GLAUCUS AND SCYLLA\n\n제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]와 그 형제들이 티탄족을 무찌르고 타르타로스로 추방했을 때, 신들에게 맞서 새로운 적이 일어났다. 그들은 거인족 티폰, 브리아레오스, 엔켈라도스와 그 밖의 무리였다. 이들 가운데 어떤 자는 팔이 백 개나 되었고, 또 어떤 자는 입에서 불을 뿜었다. 그들은 마침내 정복당해 에트나 산 아래 산 채로 매장되었는데, 지금도 이따금 빠져나오려고 몸부림을 쳐서 섬 전체를 지진으로 뒤흔든다. 그들의 숨결은 산을 통해 올라오는데, 사람들은 이것을 화산의 분화라고 부른다.\n\n이 괴물들의 추락은 대지를 뒤흔들었고, 그리하여 플루톤[로마의 〈디스〉]은 놀란 나머지 자신의 왕국이 햇빛에 드러나지나 않을까 두려워했다. 이런 염려 속에서 그는 검은 말들이 끄는 전차에 올라타, 피해의 규모를 직접 확인하려고 두루 시찰에 나섰다. 그가 이렇게 다니던 동안, 에릭스 산에 앉아 아들 에로스[로마의 〈쿠피도〉]와 놀고 있던 아프로디테[로마의 〈베누스〉]가 그를 발견하고 말했다. \"내 아들아, 네가 모든 것을 정복하는, 심지어 제우스마저 굴복시키는 그 화살을 들어, 저기 타르타로스의 영역을 다스리는 어두운 군주의 가슴에 하나 쏘아 보내거라. 어찌하여 그자만 벗어나야 한단 말이냐? 이 기회를 잡아 너와 나의 권세를 넓히도록 하여라. 하늘에서조차 어떤 이들은 우리의 힘을 업신여기는 것을 너는 보지 못하느냐? 지혜로운 아테나[로마의 〈미네르바〉]와 사냥꾼 아르테미스[로마의 〈디아나〉]가 우리에게 맞서고 있으며, 또 데메테르[로마의 〈케레스〉]의 저 딸도 그들의 본을 따르겠다고 위협하고 있다. 자, 너에게 너 자신의 이익이나 나의 이익을 조금이라도 생각하는 마음이 있다면, 저 두 신을 하나로 맺어 다오.\" 소년은 화살통을 끌러, 가장 날카롭고 가장 정확한 화살을 골라 들었다. 그러고는 무릎에 활을 대어 당겨 시위를 메긴 다음, 준비를 마치자 미늘 돋친 화살촉을 플루톤의 심장 한복판에 곧장 쏘아 박았다.\n\n엔나의 골짜기에는 숲에 둘러싸인 호수가 하나 있어, 작열하는 태양의 빛살로부터 가려져 있었고, 한편 습기 어린 땅은 꽃들로 뒤덮이고 봄이 영원히 군림했다. 페르세포네[로마의 〈프로세르피나〉]는 이곳에서 동무들과 어울려 백합과 제비꽃을 따며, 바구니와 앞치마를 그것들로 가득 채우고 있었다. 그때 플루톤이 그녀를 보고는 사랑에 빠져 그녀를 채어 갔다. 그녀는 어머니와 동무들에게 도와 달라고 비명을 질렀다. 그리고 놀란 나머지 앞치마 자락을 놓쳐 꽃들이 쏟아져 내리자, 어린아이답게 그녀는 그 꽃을 잃은 일을 자기 슬픔에 보태어지는 것으로 느꼈다. 약탈자는 말들을 하나하나 이름을 부르며 재촉했고, 쇠빛 고삐를 그들의 머리와 목 위로 느슨히 던졌다. 그가 키아네 강에 이르자 강물이 그의 길을 막았으므로, 그는 삼지창으로 강둑을 내리쳤고, 그러자 땅이 갈라져 타르타로스로 통하는 길을 그에게 내주었다.\n\n데메테르는 세상 곳곳을 다니며 딸을 찾았다. 빛나는 머리채의 에오스[로마의 〈아우로라〉]가 아침에 나설 때에도, 헤스페로스가 저녁에 별들을 이끌고 나올 때에도, 그녀가 여전히 그 탐색에 바쁜 모습을 보았다. 그러나 모든 것이 헛수고였다. 마침내 지치고 슬픔에 잠긴 그녀는 돌 위에 앉았고, 아흐레 낮과 아흐레 밤을 노천에서, 햇빛과 달빛과 쏟아지는 소나기 아래 계속 앉아 있었다. 그곳은 지금 엘레우시스 시가 서 있는 자리로, 그때에는 켈레오스라는 이름의 노인이 사는 곳이었다. 그는 들에 나가 도토리와 검은딸기, 그리고 불을 지필 나뭇가지를 줍고 있었다. 그의 어린 딸은 염소 두 마리를 집으로 몰고 있었는데, 노파의 모습으로 나타난 여신 곁을 지나면서 그녀에게 말했다. \"어머니,\" 이 호칭은 데메테르의 귀에 달콤하게 들렸다. \"어찌하여 여기 바위 위에 홀로 앉아 계신가요?\" 노인 또한, 비록 그의 짐이 무거웠으나 걸음을 멈추고는, 누추하나마 자기 오두막으로 들어오시라고 청했다. 그녀가 사양하자 그는 거듭 권했다. \"편히 가시고,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"그대의 딸로 인하여 행복하시기를. 나는 내 딸을 잃었답니다.\" 그녀가 이렇게 말하는 동안, 눈물이, 아니 눈물 비슷한 것이, 신들은 결코 울지 않으니, 그녀의 두 뺨을 타고 가슴 위로 흘러내렸다. 인정 많은 노인과 그의 아이도 그녀와 함께 울었다. 그러더니 그가 말했다. \"우리와 함께 가십시다, 우리의 보잘것없는 지붕을 업신여기지 마십시오. 그리하면 그대의 딸도 무사히 그대에게 돌아올지 모릅니다.\" \"앞장서시오.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그 청만은 거절할 수가 없군요!\" 그리하여 그녀는 돌에서 일어나 그들과 함께 갔다. 걸어가면서 그는 그녀에게 자신의 외아들인 어린 사내아이가 열에 시달리며 잠도 못 자고 몹시 앓아누워 있다고 말했다. 그녀는 몸을 굽혀 양귀비 몇 송이를 꺾었다. 그들이 오두막에 들어서니, 아이가 회복할 가망이 없어 보였으므로 온 집안이 큰 시름에 잠겨 있는 것을 보았다. 아이의 어머니 메타니라는 그녀를 친절히 맞아들였고, 여신은 몸을 굽혀 병든 아이의 입술에 입을 맞추었다. 그 즉시 아이의 얼굴에서 창백함이 가시고, 건강한 활기가 그 몸에 되돌아왔다. 온 가족이 기뻐했으니, 곧 아버지와 어머니와 어린 딸이었고, 그들이 식구의 전부였으니, 그들에게는 하인이 없었던 것이다. 그들은 상을 차려, 응유와 크림, 사과, 그리고 벌집째인 꿀을 그 위에 올렸다. 그들이 먹는 동안, 데메테르는 양귀비 즙을 아이의 우유에 섞었다. 밤이 되어 모든 것이 고요해지자, 그녀는 일어나 잠든 아이를 안아 두 손으로 그 사지를 빚고, 그 위에 세 번 엄숙한 주문을 외운 다음, 가서 아이를 잿불 속에 뉘었다. 손님이 하는 일을 지켜보고 있던 아이의 어머니는 비명을 지르며 앞으로 달려들어 불 속에서 아이를 낚아챘다. 그러자 데메테르는 본래의 모습으로 돌아왔고, 신성한 광채가 사방으로 빛났다. 그들이 경악에 사로잡혀 있는 사이, 그녀가 말했다. \"어머니여, 그대는 아들에 대한 애정으로 그대 자신이 잔인했소. 나는 이 아이를 불사의 존재로 만들려 했건만, 그대가 내 시도를 좌절시켰소. 그렇기는 하나, 이 아이는 위대하고 유익한 존재가 될 것이오. 이 아이는 사람들에게 쟁기의 쓰임새와, 일구어진 흙에서 노동이 거둘 수 있는 보답을 가르치게 될 것이오.\" 이렇게 말하고서 그녀는 구름으로 몸을 감싸고, 전차에 올라타 떠나갔다.\n\n데메테르는 딸을 찾는 일을 계속하여, 이 땅에서 저 땅으로, 바다와 강을 가로질러 다니다가, 마침내 그녀가 처음에 출발했던 시킬리아[시칠리아]로 돌아왔고, 플루톤이 자기 전리품을 데리고 자신의 영토로 통하는 길을 냈던 키아네 강의 둑 곁에 섰다. 강의 님프는 자신이 목격한 모든 것을 여신에게 말해 주고 싶었으나, 플루톤이 두려워 감히 그러지 못했다. 그래서 그녀는 다만, 페르세포네가 끌려가던 길에 떨어뜨린 허리띠를 집어 들어, 어머니의 발치로 띄워 보내는 일만 감행했다. 데메테르는 이것을 보고 더 이상 자기 딸을 잃었다는 사실을 의심하지 않게 되었으나, 아직 그 까닭은 알지 못했으므로 죄 없는 대지를 탓했다. \"은혜를 모르는 흙이여,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"내가 비옥함을 부여하고 풀과 자양 가득한 곡식으로 옷 입혀 주었거늘, 너는 더 이상 나의 은총을 누리지 못하리라.\" 그러자 가축이 죽고, 쟁기는 고랑 속에서 부러지고, 씨앗은 싹이 트지 않았다. 햇볕이 너무 많고, 비가 너무 많았다. 새들은 씨앗을 훔쳐 갔고, 엉겅퀴와 가시덤불만이 유일하게 자라났다. 이것을 본 샘의 님프 아레투사가 대지를 위해 중재에 나섰다. \"여신이여,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"대지를 탓하지 마십시오. 대지는 당신의 따님이 지나갈 길을 마지못해 내주었던 것입니다. 제가 따님의 운명을 말씀드릴 수 있으니, 제가 그분을 보았기 때문입니다. 이곳은 제 고향이 아닙니다. 저는 엘리스에서 이리로 왔습니다. 저는 숲의 님프로서, 사냥을 즐겼습니다. 사람들은 제 아름다움을 칭송했으나, 저는 그런 것에는 아무 관심도 없었고, 오히려 제 사냥 솜씨를 자랑하곤 했지요. 어느 날 저는 운동으로 몸이 달아오른 채 숲에서 돌아오다가, 소리 없이 흐르는 시냇물에 이르렀는데, 어찌나 맑은지 바닥의 자갈을 셀 수 있을 정도였습니다. 버드나무가 그 위에 그늘을 드리웠고, 풀 우거진 둑이 물가까지 비스듬히 내려가 있었습니다. 저는 가까이 다가가 발로 물을 건드려 보았습니다. 무릎까지 들어가 보았는데, 그것으로도 만족하지 못해, 옷을 버드나무에 걸어 두고 물속으로 들어갔습니다. 제가 물속에서 노니는 동안, 시내의 깊은 곳에서 솟아오르는 듯한 어렴풋한 웅얼거림이 들려와, 저는 서둘러 가장 가까운 둑으로 피하려 했습니다. 그 목소리가 말했습니다. '어찌하여 그대는 달아나는가, 아레투사여? 나는 이 시내의 신 알페이오스다.' 저는 달렸고, 그는 뒤쫓았습니다. 그가 저보다 빠르지는 않았으나 더 힘이 셌으므로, 제 기운이 빠져 갈수록 그는 저에게 다가왔습니다. 마침내 지친 저는 아르테미스에게 도와 달라고 외쳤습니다. '도와주소서, 여신이여! 당신을 섬기는 자를 도와주소서!' 여신께서 들으시고는, 저를 갑자기 짙은 구름으로 감싸 주셨습니다. 강의 신은 이쪽저쪽을 살피며, 두 번이나 제 곁까지 바싹 다가왔으나 저를 찾아내지 못했습니다. '아레투사여! 아레투사여!' 그가 외쳤습니다. 아, 저는 어찌나 떨었던지, 우리 밖에서 늑대가 으르렁대는 소리를 듣는 어린 양 같았습니다. 식은땀이 저를 뒤덮었고, 제 머리카락은 물줄기 되어 흘러내렸습니다. 제 발이 디딘 자리에는 웅덩이가 생겼습니다. 요컨대, 이 일을 이야기하는 것보다도 짧은 사이에 저는 샘이 되었던 것입니다. 그러나 이 모습으로도 알페이오스는 저를 알아보고 자기 물줄기를 제 물줄기와 섞으려 했습니다. 아르테미스가 땅을 가르자, 저는 그를 피하려 애쓰며 그 동굴 속으로 뛰어들었고, 대지의 깊은 속을 지나 이곳 시킬리아로 솟아 나왔습니다. 대지의 아래쪽을 지나는 동안, 저는 당신의 따님 페르세포네를 보았습니다. 그분은 슬픈 모습이었으나, 더 이상 얼굴에 놀란 기색을 드러내지는 않았습니다. 그분의 모습은 여왕에게, 곧 에레보스의 여왕에게 어울리는 것이었으니, 죽은 자들의 영역을 다스리는 군주의 권세 있는 신부의 모습이었습니다.\"\n\n데메테르는 이 말을 듣고 한동안 넋이 나간 사람처럼 서 있었다. 그러더니 전차를 하늘 쪽으로 돌려, 서둘러 제우스의 옥좌 앞에 나아갔다. 그녀는 딸을 잃은 사연을 이야기하고, 제우스에게 나서서 딸을 돌려받게 해 달라고 간청했다. 제우스는 한 가지 조건을 걸어 허락했으니, 곧 페르세포네가 저승에 머무는 동안 어떤 음식도 입에 대지 않았어야 한다는 것이었다. 그렇지 않다면 운명의 여신들이 그녀의 풀려남을 허락하지 않으리라는 것이었다. 그리하여 헤르메스[로마의 〈메르쿠리우스〉]가 봄을 동반하여 플루톤에게 페르세포네를 내놓으라고 요구하러 파견되었다. 교활한 군주는 응했으나, 아아! 그 처녀는 플루톤이 그녀에게 건넨 석류를 받아, 그 씨앗 몇 알에서 달콤한 과육을 빨아먹은 뒤였다. 이것만으로도 그녀의 완전한 풀려남을 가로막기에 충분했다. 그러나 한 가지 타협이 이루어져, 그녀는 그 시간의 절반을 어머니와 함께 보내고, 나머지를 남편 플루톤과 함께 보내기로 했다.\n\n데메테르는 이 조정에 마음을 누그러뜨리고, 대지에 다시 자신의 은총을 베풀었다. 이제 그녀는 켈레오스와 그 가족, 그리고 그의 어린 아들 트립톨레모스에게 했던 약속을 기억해 냈다. 소년이 자라나자, 그녀는 그에게 쟁기의 쓰임새와 씨앗 뿌리는 법을 가르쳤다. 그녀는 날개 달린 용들이 끄는 전차에 그를 태우고, 대지의 온갖 나라를 두루 다니며, 인류에게 값진 곡식과 농경의 지식을 전해 주었다. 돌아온 뒤 트립톨레모스는 엘레우시스에 데메테르를 기리는 웅장한 신전을 세우고, 〈엘레우시스의 비의(秘儀)〉라는 이름으로 여신을 섬기는 예배를 확립했는데, 그 거행의 화려함과 장엄함에 있어 그리스인들 사이의 다른 모든 종교 행사를 능가했다.\n\n이 데메테르와 페르세포네의 이야기가 하나의 우의(寓意)라는 점은 의심의 여지가 거의 없다. 페르세포네는 땅속에 던져져 거기 숨어 있는 씨앗 곡식을 뜻한다. 곧 그녀가 저승의 신에게 채어 가는 것이다. 그것이 다시 모습을 드러낸다. 곧 페르세포네가 어머니에게 돌아오는 것이다. 봄이 그녀를 다시 햇빛 속으로 이끌어 낸다.\n\n밀턴은 「실낙원」 제4권에서 페르세포네의 이야기를 넌지시 언급한다.\n\n\". . . 페르세포네가 꽃을 따다가, 그녀 자신이 더 고운 꽃이었거늘, 음울한 디스에게 꺾여 갔던 저 엔나의 아름다운 들판도, 그 때문에 데메테르가 그 모든 고난을 무릅쓰고 온 세상을 헤매며 딸을 찾았던 그곳도, . . . 이 에덴의 낙원과는 견줄 수 없으리라.\"\n\n후드는 그의 「우울에 부치는 송가」에서 같은 인유를 매우 아름답게 활용한다.\n\n\"용서하라, 다가올 비탄 속에서 잠시 내가 지금의 행복을 잊는다 한들, 놀란 페르세포네가 디스를 보고서 제 꽃들을 떨어뜨렸던 것처럼.\"\n\n알페이오스 강은 실제로 그 물길의 일부에서 지하로 사라져, 지하의 수로를 통해 흐르다가 다시 지표로 솟아나곤 한다. 시킬리아의 샘 아레투사가 바로 그 같은 물줄기로서, 바다 밑을 지난 뒤 시킬리아에서 다시 솟아난 것이라고들 했다. 그리하여 알페이오스에 던진 잔이 아레투사에서 다시 나타났다는 이야기가 전해졌다. 콜리지가 그의 시 「쿠블라 칸」에서 넌지시 언급하는 것이 바로 이 알페이오스의 지하 물길에 관한 이야기다.\n\n\"재너두에서 쿠블라 칸은 장엄한 환락의 궁전을 지으라 명했으니, 그곳에는 성스러운 강 알프가 인간이 헤아릴 수 없는 동굴들을 지나 햇빛 없는 바다로 흘러내렸도다.\"\n\n무어는 그의 초기 시들 가운데 하나에서 같은 이야기를, 그리고 그의 물줄기에 화환이나 그 밖의 가벼운 물건을 던져 강을 따라 떠내려가게 한 뒤, 강이 솟아오르는 곳에서 그것이 다시 나타나게 하던 관습을 이렇게 넌지시 언급한다.\n\n\"오 나의 사랑하는 이여, 한마음인 영혼들이 만날 때 그 순수한 기쁨은 얼마나 거룩하게 감미로운가! 강의 신처럼, 그의 물줄기는 사랑만을 유일한 빛으로 삼아 아래쪽 동굴들을 지나 흐르며, 올림피아의 처녀들이 그의 물길을 장식하여 아레투사의 빛나는 발치에 바치는 합당한 제물로 엮어 놓은 온갖 꽃 띠와 축제의 고리들을 의기양양하게 실어 나르는 그 신처럼. 생각해 보라, 그가 마침내 샘이 된 그의 신부를 만날 때, 그 어우러진 물결을 전율케 하는 얼마나 온전한 사랑일지를! 서로가 서로에게 잠겨, 하나로 뒤섞일 때까지, 그림자 속에서든 햇빛 속에서든 그 운명이 한가지인 채로, 참된 사랑의 본보기로서, 그들은 깊은 바다로 흘러간다.\"\n\n다음은 무어의 「길 위의 운율」에서 발췌한 것으로, 밀라노에 있는 알바노의 유명한 그림, 〈사랑들의 춤〉이라 불리는 작품을 그려 보인다.\n\n\"엔나의 꽃을 대지에서 훔쳐 간 일을 기려 이 개구쟁이들은 흥겨운 춤을 펼치니, 푸른 나무를 빙 둘러, 황야 위의 요정들처럼. 가장 가까이 있는 이들은 밝게 차례차례 손을 맞잡아, 뺨에 뺨을 맞대니, 화관 속의 장미 봉오리 같고, 더 멀리 있는 이들은 다른 이들의 날개 아래로 그 작고 빛나는 두 눈을 내비친다. 보라! 구름 사이로, 이제 막 날아오른 그들의 맏형이, 더없는 기쁨의 미소를 띠고, 플루톤의 이 장난을 매혹된 어머니에게 전하니, 어머니는 그 소식을 입맞춤으로 반기려 돌아선다.\"\n글라우코스와 스킬라\n\n# GLAUCUS AND SCYLLA\n\n글라우코스는 어부였다. 어느 날 그는 그물을 뭍으로 끌어올려, 갖가지 종류의 물고기를 무척 많이 잡았다. 그래서 그는 그물을 비우고는, 풀밭 위에 물고기를 종류별로 가려내기 시작했다. 그가 서 있던 곳은 강 가운데 자리한 아름다운 섬으로, 외딴 곳이었고, 사람도 살지 않았으며, 가축을 풀어 먹이는 데도 쓰이지 않았고, 그 자신 말고는 누구도 찾는 일이 없었다. 그런데 갑자기, 풀밭 위에 놓여 있던 물고기들이 마치 물속에 있기라도 한 듯 다시 살아나 지느러미를 움직이기 시작했다. 그가 놀라서 바라보는 동안, 물고기들은 하나도 빠짐없이 물 쪽으로 옮겨 가더니, 물속으로 뛰어들어 헤엄쳐 가 버렸다. 그는 이 일을 어떻게 받아들여야 할지 알 수 없었으니, 어떤 신이 한 일인지, 아니면 그 풀 속에 깃든 어떤 은밀한 힘 때문인지 알 수가 없었다. \"어떤 풀이 이런 힘을 지녔단 말인가?\" 그가 외쳤다. 그러고는 그 풀을 조금 뜯어 맛을 보았다. 그 식물의 즙이 그의 입천장에 닿기가 무섭게, 그는 물을 향한 간절한 갈망에 사로잡힌 자신을 발견했다. 그는 더 이상 자신을 억누를 수 없어, 뭍에 작별을 고하고는 물줄기 속으로 뛰어들었다. 물의 신들은 그를 정중히 맞아들여, 자신들의 무리에 들 영예를 그에게 허락했다. 그들은 바다의 주권자인 오케아노스와 테티스의 동의를 얻어, 그에게 있는 필멸의 것을 모조리 씻어 내게 했다. 백 개의 강이 그 물을 그의 위에 쏟아부었다. 그러자 그는 자신의 옛 본성에 대한 모든 감각과 모든 의식을 잃었다. 정신을 되찾았을 때, 그는 자신이 모습과 마음에서 변해 있음을 알았다. 그의 머리카락은 바다처럼 푸르렀고, 물 위로 그의 뒤를 끌며 늘어졌다. 그의 어깨는 넓어졌고, 넓적다리와 다리였던 것은 물고기 꼬리의 형상을 띠었다. 바다의 신들은 변모한 그의 모습을 두고 그에게 찬사를 보냈고, 그는 자신이 제법 보기 좋은 인물이라고 여겼다.\n\n어느 날 글라우코스는 물의 님프들의 총애를 받는 아름다운 처녀 스킬라가 물가를 거닐다가, 아늑한 후미를 찾아내어 맑은 물에 사지를 담그는 것을 보았다. 그는 그녀에게 사랑에 빠져, 수면 위로 모습을 드러내고는, 그녀가 머물도록 마음을 끌기에 가장 알맞다고 여겨지는 말들을 그녀에게 건넸다. 그도 그럴 것이, 그녀는 그를 보자마자 곧장 달아나려 돌아섰고, 바다가 내려다보이는 절벽에 이를 때까지 달렸기 때문이다. 여기서 그녀는 걸음을 멈추고 돌아서서, 그것이 신인지 바다 짐승인지를 살피며, 그의 모습과 빛깔을 경이로운 눈길로 바라보았다. 글라우코스는 물에서 일부만 솟아오른 채 바위에 몸을 기대고 말했다. \"처녀여, 나는 괴물도 아니요, 바다 짐승도 아니라 신이오. 프로테우스도 트리톤도 나보다 높지 않소. 한때 나는 필멸의 존재로서 바다를 좇아 생계를 꾸렸으나, 이제는 온전히 바다에 속한 몸이오.\" 그러고는 자신이 변모한 이야기와, 어떻게 지금의 지위에 오르게 되었는지를 들려준 다음, 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"그러나 이 모든 것이 그대의 마음을 움직이지 못한다면 무슨 소용이겠소?\" 그가 이런 어조로 계속 말을 이어 가려 했으나, 스킬라는 돌아서서 황급히 달아나 버렸다.\n\n글라우코스는 절망에 빠졌으나, 마법사 키르케에게 의논해 보자는 생각이 떠올랐다. 그리하여 그는 그녀의 섬으로 갔으니, 훗날 오디세우스가 상륙했던 바로 그 섬으로, 우리의 뒷이야기들 가운데 하나에서 보게 될 것이다. 서로 인사를 나눈 뒤 그가 말했다. \"여신이여, 부디 저를 가엾이 여겨 주십시오. 제가 겪는 고통을 덜어 줄 수 있는 분은 당신뿐입니다. 풀의 힘이라면 저도 누구 못지않게 잘 알고 있으니, 제 모습이 변한 것도 바로 그 풀 덕분이기 때문입니다. 저는 스킬라를 사랑합니다. 제가 어떻게 그녀에게 구애하고 약속했는지, 또 그녀가 저를 얼마나 경멸스럽게 대했는지를 말씀드리기조차 부끄럽습니다. 부디 당신의 주술을, 아니 더 효험이 있다면 강력한 약초를 써 주시기를 간청드립니다. 저를 제 사랑에서 낫게 해 달라는 것이 아니라, 그것은 제가 바라는 바가 아니니, 그녀로 하여금 제 사랑을 함께 나누어, 제게 똑같이 보답하게 해 주십사 하는 것입니다.\" 이에 키르케가 대답했으니, 그녀 또한 바다처럼 푸른 그 신의 매력에 무감하지는 않았던 것이다. \"당신은 차라리 기꺼이 응할 상대를 좇는 편이 낫겠습니다. 당신은 헛되이 좇아다니기보다는 오히려 좇음을 받을 만한 분이니까요. 주눅 들지 말고, 당신 자신의 가치를 아십시오. 단언하건대, 비록 내가 여신이고 약초와 주문의 효능에 정통하다 해도, 당신을 거절할 줄은 모를 것입니다. 그녀가 당신을 경멸한다면 당신도 그녀를 경멸하십시오. 중도에서 당신을 맞아 줄 준비가 된 이를 만나, 그리하여 두 사람 모두에게 한꺼번에 마땅한 보답을 하십시오.\" 이 말에 글라우코스가 대답했다. \"내가 스킬라를, 그녀만을 사랑하기를 그치느니, 차라리 바다 밑바닥에서 나무가 자라고 산꼭대기에서 해초가 자라는 편이 빠를 것이오.\"\n\n여신은 분개했으나, 그를 벌할 수 없었고, 그러기를 바라지도 않았으니, 그를 너무도 좋아했기 때문이다. 그래서 그녀는 자신의 온 분노를 연적인 가엾은 스킬라에게로 돌렸다. 그녀는 독성을 지닌 식물들을 가져다 한데 섞고, 주술과 주문을 곁들였다. 그러고는 자신의 술법에 희생되어 뛰노는 짐승들의 무리를 지나, 스킬라가 사는 시킬리아 해안으로 나아갔다. 그 물가에는 스킬라가 한낮의 더위에 바다 공기를 쐬고 그 물에 멱을 감으러 즐겨 찾던 작은 만이 하나 있었다. 여기에 여신은 자신의 독성 어린 혼합물을 쏟아붓고, 그 위에 막강한 힘을 지닌 주술을 중얼거렸다. 스킬라는 여느 때처럼 와서 허리까지 물속에 잠겼다. 그런데 뱀들과 짖어 대는 괴물들의 무리가 자기를 에워싸고 있는 것을 알아챘을 때 그녀의 공포가 어떠했겠는가! 처음에 그녀는 그것들이 자기 몸의 일부라고는 상상도 하지 못한 채, 그것들로부터 달아나려 하고 그것들을 쫓아 버리려 했다. 그러나 달아날수록 그것들을 함께 데리고 다녔고, 자기 사지를 만져 보려 하면 손에 닿는 것은 오직 괴물들의 떡 벌린 아가리뿐이었다. 스킬라는 그 자리에 뿌리박힌 듯 머물러 있게 되었다. 그녀의 성미는 그 모습만큼이나 흉악해졌고, 그녀는 자기 손아귀에 들어오는 가엾은 뱃사람들을 잡아먹는 데서 즐거움을 느꼈다. 이리하여 그녀는 오디세우스의 동료 여섯을 잡아 죽였고, 아이네아스의 배들을 난파시키려 했으며, 마침내 바위로 변했는데, 그런 모습으로 지금도 여전히 뱃사람들에게 공포의 대상이 되고 있다.\n\n키츠는 그의 「엔디미온」에서 \"글라우코스와 스킬라\"의 결말에 새로운 해석을 내놓았다. 글라우코스는 키르케의 아첨에 응하다가, 우연히 그녀가 짐승들을 상대로 벌이는 짓을 목격하게 된다.[11] 그녀의 배신과 잔인함에 넌더리가 난 그는 그녀에게서 달아나려 하나, 붙잡혀 다시 끌려오고, 그러자 그녀는 그를 비난하며 추방하여, 천 년 동안 노쇠와 고통 속에 지내도록 선고한다. 그는 바다로 돌아가, 거기서 스킬라의 시신을 발견하는데, 여신이 그녀를 변신시킨 것이 아니라 물에 빠뜨려 죽인 것이었다. 글라우코스는 자신의 운명이 다음과 같음을 알게 된다. 곧 그가 자신의 천 년을 물에 빠져 죽은 연인들의 시신을 모두 거두어 모으는 데 보낸다면, 신들의 사랑을 받는 한 젊은이가 나타나 그를 도우리라는 것이었다. 엔디미온이 이 예언을 이루어, 글라우코스를 젊음으로, 스킬라와 물에 빠져 죽은 모든 연인들을 생명으로 되돌리는 일을 돕는다.\n\n다음은 자신의 \"바다의 변화\"를 겪은 뒤 느낀 감정에 관한 글라우코스의 이야기다.\n\n\"나는 삶이냐 죽음이냐를 걸고 뛰어들었다. 그토록 빽빽이 숨 쉬는 물질에 제 감각을 한데 엮는 일은 고통스러운 일로 여겨질 법도 했으나, 그러기는커녕 그것이 수정처럼 매끄럽게 느껴졌고, 내 사지를 떠받쳐 주던 그 부력을 나는 아무리 찬탄해도 모자라다. 처음에 나는 온전한 경이 속에 며칠이고 며칠이고 머물렀고, 나 자신의 의도는 까맣게 잊은 채, 오직 그 거대한 밀물과 썰물에 실려서만 움직였다. 그러다가 갓 깃털이 돋은 새가 처음으로 아침의 한기에 제 활짝 편 깃을 펼쳐 보이듯, 나는 두려운 마음으로 내 의지의 날개를 시험해 보았다. 그것은 자유였다! 그리고 나는 곧장 이 바다 밑바닥의 그칠 줄 모르는 경이들을 찾아갔다.\" — 키츠.\n\n————\n\n# PYGMALION—DRYOPE—VENUS AND ADONIS—APOLLO AND HYACINTHUS\n\n피그말리온은 여인들에게서 비난할 점을 너무도 많이 보았던 까닭에, 마침내 그 성(性) 전체를 혐오하게 되었고, 결혼하지 않은 채로 살아가기로 결심했다. 그는 조각가였는데, 놀라운 솜씨로 상아 조각상을 하나 만들었고, 그것이 어찌나 아름다웠던지 살아 있는 어떤 여인도 거기에는 도무지 미치지 못했다. 그것은 실로 살아 있는 듯한, 다만 수줍음 때문에 움직이지 못할 뿐인 처녀의 완벽한 형상이었다. 그의 기술은 너무도 완벽하여 스스로를 감추었고, 그 산물은 자연의 솜씨처럼 보였다. 피그말리온은 자기 자신의 작품을 찬탄했고, 마침내 그 인공의 피조물과 사랑에 빠지고 말았다. 그는 마치 그것이 살아 있는지 아닌지 확인하려는 듯 자주 그 위에 손을 얹어 보았으나, 그러고도 그것이 한낱 상아일 뿐이라고는 도무지 믿을 수가 없었다. 그는 그것을 어루만졌고, 어린 소녀들이 좋아할 만한 선물들을 주었다. 빛나는 조개껍데기와 매끄러운 돌멩이, 작은 새들과 갖가지 빛깔의 꽃들, 구슬과 호박 같은 것들이었다. 그는 그 사지에 옷을 입혔고, 손가락에는 보석을 끼웠으며, 목에는 목걸이를 걸어 주었다. 귀에는 귀고리를 달았고, 가슴에는 진주 목걸이를 드리웠다. 옷차림은 그녀에게 잘 어울렸고, 아무것도 걸치지 않았을 때 못지않게 매혹적으로 보였다. 그는 그녀를 티로스산 염료로 물들인 천을 깐 침상 위에 누이고, 그녀를 자신의 아내라 불렀으며, 마치 그녀가 그 부드러움을 즐길 수 있기라도 한 듯 가장 부드러운 깃털을 채운 베개 위에 그 머리를 뉘었다.\n\n아프로디테[로마의 〈베누스〉]의 축제가 다가왔으니, 그것은 키프로스에서 성대하게 거행되던 축제였다. 제물이 바쳐졌고, 제단에서는 연기가 피어올랐으며, 향내가 공기를 가득 채웠다. 피그말리온은 의식에서 자기 몫을 다하고 나자, 제단 앞에 서서 머뭇거리며 말했다. \"무엇이든 하실 수 있는 신들이시여, 부디 제게 아내로 삼을 이를 주십시오.\" 그는 차마 \"제 상아 처녀를\"이라고는 말하지 못하고, 그 대신 \"제 상아 처녀 같은 이를\"이라고 말했다. 그 축제에 함께 자리해 있던 아프로디테는 그의 말을 들었고, 그가 입에 담고 싶어 했던 생각을 알아차렸다. 그리하여 그녀는 자신의 호의를 알리는 징조로, 제단의 불꽃이 세 번 불기둥처럼 공중으로 솟구치게 했다. 그가 집으로 돌아와 자신의 조각상을 보러 가서, 침상 위로 몸을 굽혀 그 입에 입을 맞추었다. 그것은 따뜻하게 느껴졌다. 그가 다시 그 입술을 누르고, 그 사지에 손을 얹어 보니, 상아는 손길에 부드럽게 느껴졌고 히메토스의 밀랍처럼 그의 손가락에 눌려 들어갔다. 그가 놀라움과 기쁨에 휩싸인 채, 그러면서도 의심하며, 혹시 자신이 잘못 안 것은 아닐까 두려워하며 서 있는 동안, 연인의 열정으로 거듭거듭 그 희망의 대상을 만져 보았다. 그것은 정녕 살아 있었다! 핏줄은 눌리면 손가락에 따라 들어갔다가 다시 본래의 둥근 모양을 되찾았다. 그제야 비로소 아프로디테의 신봉자는 여신께 감사를 드릴 말을 찾아냈고, 제 입술만큼이나 진짜인 입술 위에 자신의 입술을 눌렀다. 처녀는 그 입맞춤을 느끼며 얼굴을 붉혔고, 수줍은 두 눈을 빛을 향해 떠서, 바로 그 순간 그것을 자기 연인에게 고정했다. 아프로디테는 자신이 맺어 준 혼인을 축복했고, 이 결합에서 파포스가 태어났으니, 아프로디테에게 봉헌된 그 도시는 바로 그에게서 이름을 얻었다.\n\n실러는 자신의 시 「이상」에서 이 피그말리온의 이야기를 젊은 가슴속의 자연에 대한 사랑에 빗대었다. 다음 번역은 한 친구가 제공한 것이다.\n\n\"한때 정열로 넘쳐흐르는 기도와 더불어, 피그말리온이 그 돌을 끌어안았을 때, 얼어붙은 대리석에서 빛이 타올라, 감정의 빛이 그를 비추었듯이, 나 또한 젊은 헌신으로 끌어안았네, 빛나는 자연을 시인의 가슴에다. 마침내 숨결과 온기와 살아 있는 움직임이 그 조각상의 형상을 꿰뚫고 솟구치는 듯했네.\n\n그리고 나의 온갖 열정을 함께 나누며, 그 말없는 형상은 표정을 찾아내었고, 젊은 대담함이 담긴 나의 입맞춤에 화답하며, 내 가슴의 빠른 고동을 알아들었네. 그리하여 그 빛나는 피조물이 나를 위해 살아났고, 은빛 시냇물은 노래로 가득 차올랐네. 나무들도, 장미들도 감각을 함께 나누었으니, 나의 가없는 생명의 메아리였네.\" ―S. G. B.\n\n드리오페와 이올레는 자매였다. 드리오페는 안드라이몬의 아내로, 남편의 사랑을 받았고, 첫아이를 낳아 행복했다. 어느 날 자매는 물가까지 완만하게 비탈진 시냇가로 거닐어 나갔는데, 그 위쪽 땅에는 도금양이 무성하게 우거져 있었다. 그들은 님프들의 제단에 바칠 화환을 엮을 꽃을 따려던 참이었고, 드리오페는 소중한 짐인 자기 아이를 가슴에 안고서 걸으며 젖을 먹이고 있었다. 물가에는 자줏빛 꽃이 가득한 로토스 나무 한 그루가 자라고 있었다. 드리오페가 몇 송이를 꺾어 아기에게 내밀었고, 이올레도 막 같은 일을 하려던 참이었는데, 그때 그녀는 자기 언니가 줄기에서 꽃을 꺾어 낸 자리에서 피가 떨어지는 것을 보았다. 그 나무는 다름 아니라 비천한 추적자에게서 달아나다가 이 형상으로 바뀐 님프 로티스였다. 이 사실을 그들은 시골 사람들에게서 알게 되었으나, 그때는 이미 너무 늦은 뒤였다.\n\n드리오페는 자신이 저지른 일을 깨닫고 공포에 사로잡혀, 기꺼이 그 자리에서 서둘러 벗어나려 했으나, 자기 두 발이 땅에 뿌리내린 것을 알았다. 그녀는 발을 빼내려 애썼지만, 상체밖에는 움직일 수가 없었다. 나무의 성질이 위로 기어올라, 차츰차츰 그녀의 몸을 뒤덮었다. 괴로움에 못 이겨 그녀는 머리카락을 쥐어뜯으려 했으나, 두 손에 나뭇잎이 가득함을 알았다. 아기는 어머니의 가슴이 굳어지기 시작하고 젖이 더는 흐르지 않는 것을 느꼈다. 이올레는 언니의 슬픈 운명을 지켜보았으나, 아무런 도움도 줄 수 없었다. 그녀는 차오르는 나무 둥치를 끌어안았으니, 마치 뻗어 가는 나무를 붙들어 막으려는 듯했고, 기꺼이 같은 나무껍질에 함께 감싸이고 싶어 했다. 그 순간 드리오페의 남편 안드라이몬이 그녀의 아버지와 함께 다가왔다. 그들이 드리오페를 찾자, 이올레는 새로이 생겨난 로토스 나무를 가리켰다. 그들은 아직 온기가 남은 그 나무 둥치를 끌어안고, 그 잎사귀에 입맞춤을 퍼부었다.\n\n이제 드리오페에게 남은 것이라곤 그 얼굴뿐이었다. 그녀의 눈물은 여전히 흘러 그 잎사귀 위로 떨어졌고, 말할 수 있는 동안 그녀는 입을 열었다. \"저는 죄가 없습니다. 저는 이런 운명을 받아 마땅하지 않습니다. 저는 누구도 해친 적이 없습니다. 만일 제가 거짓을 말한다면, 제 잎사귀가 가뭄으로 시들고 제 둥치가 베여 불태워지게 하소서. 이 아기를 데려가 유모에게 맡겨 주십시오. 자주 데려와 제 가지 아래에서 젖을 먹이고, 제 그늘에서 뛰놀게 해 주십시오. 그리고 이 아이가 말할 만큼 자라거든, 저를 어머니라 부르도록, 그리고 슬픔에 잠겨 '저의 어머니는 이 나무껍질 아래 숨어 계십니다'라고 말하도록 가르쳐 주십시오. 다만 이 아이에게 강가를 조심하고, 꽃을 꺾을 때 마음을 쓰며, 눈에 보이는 덤불 하나하나가 모습을 감춘 여신일 수 있음을 기억하라고 일러 주십시오. 잘 있어요, 사랑하는 남편이여, 그리고 동생이여, 아버지여. 저를 향한 사랑이 조금이라도 남아 있거든, 도끼가 저를 상하게 하지 못하게 하고, 가축 떼가 제 가지를 물어뜯지 못하게 해 주십시오. 제가 당신들에게 몸을 굽힐 수 없으니, 이리로 올라와 제게 입을 맞춰 주십시오. 그리고 제 입술이 아직 느낄 수 있는 동안, 제 아이를 들어 올려 제가 입 맞출 수 있게 해 주십시오. 더는 말할 수가 없습니다. 어느새 나무껍질이 제 목까지 차올라, 곧 저를 온통 뒤덮을 것이기 때문입니다. 제 눈을 감겨 주실 필요는 없습니다. 당신들의 손길 없이도 나무껍질이 그 눈을 감겨 줄 것입니다.\" 그러더니 그 입술은 움직임을 멈추었고, 생명은 꺼지고 말았다. 그러나 가지들은 얼마 동안 더 생명의 온기를 간직하고 있었다.\n\n키츠는 「엔디미온」에서 드리오페를 이렇게 언급한다.\n\n\"그녀는 류트를 집어 들었고, 거기서 고동치듯 경쾌한 전주가 흘러나와, 그녀의 목소리가 노닐어 갈 길을 빚어내었네. 그것은 드리오페가 홀로 제 아이를 어르던 자장가보다도 더 미묘한 가락을 띤, 더 숲처럼 야성적인 노래였네.\" 등등.\n\n# VENUS AND ADONIS\n\n아프로디테가 어느 날 자기 아들 에로스[로마의 〈쿠피도〉]와 놀다가, 그 화살 하나에 가슴을 다치고 말았다. 그녀는 아들을 밀쳐 냈으나, 그 상처는 그녀가 생각했던 것보다 깊었다. 그것이 채 아물기도 전에 그녀는 아도니스를 보았고, 그에게 마음을 빼앗겼다. 그녀는 이제 자신이 즐겨 찾던 곳들, 곧 파포스와 크니도스, 그리고 금속이 풍부한 아마토스에도 더는 흥미를 두지 않았다. 그녀는 하늘마저 멀리하였으니, 아도니스가 그녀에게는 하늘보다 더 소중했기 때문이다. 그녀는 그를 따라다니며 그와 동무가 되어 주었다. 한때 그늘에 기대 누워 제 아름다움을 가꾸는 것 말고는 아무 근심도 없던 그녀가, 이제는 사냥꾼 아르테미스[로마의 〈디아나〉]처럼 차려입고서 숲을 가로지르고 언덕을 넘어 거닐었다. 그녀는 자기 개들을 부르며, 산토끼와 사슴이나, 사냥해도 안전한 다른 짐승을 쫓았으나, 가축 떼를 도륙한 피비린내를 풍기는 늑대와 곰은 멀리했다. 그녀는 아도니스에게도 그러한 위험한 짐승들을 조심하라고 단단히 일렀다. \"겁 많은 것들에게는 용감하세요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"용감한 것들을 상대로 한 용기는 안전하지 않답니다. 스스로를 위험에 내맡겨 제 행복을 위태롭게 하지 않도록 조심하세요. 자연이 무기로 무장시켜 놓은 짐승들은 공격하지 마세요. 저는 당신의 영광을 그토록 높이 사서 그런 위험을 무릅쓰며 그것을 사들이는 데 동의할 만큼은 아니랍니다. 당신의 젊음과 아프로디테를 매혹하는 그 아름다움도, 사자와 뻣뻣한 털의 멧돼지들의 마음은 움직이지 못해요. 그것들의 무시무시한 발톱과 어마어마한 힘을 생각해 보세요! 저는 그것들의 종족 전체를 미워한답니다. 그 까닭을 제게 물으시나요?\" 그러더니 그녀는 자신에게 배은망덕했던 탓에 사자로 변해 버린 아탈란테와 히포메네스의 이야기를 그에게 들려주었다.\n\n그에게 이렇게 경고를 한 뒤, 그녀는 백조들이 끄는 자기 수레에 올라 공중을 가르며 떠나갔다. 그러나 아도니스는 그런 충고에 귀 기울이기에는 너무도 기개가 높았다. 개들이 멧돼지 한 마리를 그 굴에서 몰아냈고, 젊은이는 자기 창을 던져 비스듬한 일격으로 그 짐승에게 상처를 입혔다. 짐승은 주둥이로 그 창을 뽑아내고서 아도니스를 향해 돌진했고, 아도니스는 몸을 돌려 달아났다. 그러나 멧돼지가 그를 따라잡아, 그 옆구리에 엄니를 박아 넣고는, 그를 들판 위에 죽어 가는 채로 뻗어 눕히고 말았다.\n\n백조가 끄는 수레에 탄 아프로디테는 아직 키프로스에 이르지 못했는데, 그때 공중을 가로질러 올라오는 사랑하는 이의 신음을 듣고, 자신의 흰 날개 달린 준마들을 땅으로 되돌렸다. 그녀가 가까이 다가가 높은 곳에서 피에 잠긴 그의 생기 없는 몸을 보고는 내려앉아, 그 위로 몸을 굽힌 채 가슴을 치고 머리카락을 쥐어뜯었다. 운명의 여신들을 책망하며 그녀가 말했다. \"그러나 그들의 승리는 절반에 그칠 뿐이리라. 내 슬픔을 기리는 표적은 길이 남을 것이며, 나의 아도니스여, 그대의 죽음과 나의 애통함의 광경은 해마다 새로이 되살아나리라. 그대의 피는 한 송이 꽃으로 바뀔 것이니, 그 위안만은 누구도 내게서 시샘할 수 없으리라.\" 이렇게 말하며 그녀는 그 피 위에 넥타르를 뿌렸다. 그러자 그것들이 뒤섞이며, 빗방울이 떨어지는 웅덩이에서 그러하듯 거품이 일어올랐고, 한 시간이 지나자 석류꽃 같은 핏빛의 꽃 한 송이가 솟아났다. 그러나 그것은 수명이 짧다. 바람이 불어 그 꽃송이를 피워 놓고, 이내 또 불어 그 꽃잎을 날려 버린다고 한다. 그리하여 그것은 아네모네, 곧 바람꽃이라 불리니, 그것을 피워 내는 데에도 또 시들게 하는 데에도 똑같이 거드는 그 원인에서 비롯한 이름이다.\n\n밀턴은 자신의 「코머스」에서 아프로디테와 아도니스의 이야기를 언급한다.\n\n\"히아킨토스와 장미꽃 자리들, 젊은 아도니스가 자주 쉬어 가는 곳, 부드러운 잠결에 그 깊은 상처가 잘도 아물어 가는 곳, 그리고 땅바닥에는 슬픔에 잠긴 아시리아의 여왕이 앉아 있네.\" 등등.\n\n# APOLLO AND HYACINTHUS\n\n아폴론은 히아킨토스라는 젊은이를 열렬히 사랑했다. 그는 히아킨토스가 노는 데 함께했고, 그가 낚시하러 갈 때면 그물을 들어 주었으며, 그가 사냥하러 갈 때면 개들을 이끌었고, 그가 산으로 나설 때면 그를 따라다녔으며, 그를 위해 자신의 리라와 화살을 소홀히 했다. 어느 날 그들이 함께 원반던지기 놀이를 했는데, 아폴론은 솜씨가 어린 힘으로 원반을 높이 치켜들어 높고도 멀리 던졌다. 히아킨토스는 그것이 날아가는 것을 지켜보다가, 놀이에 흥이 올라 그것을 붙잡으려고, 어서 제 차례에 던지고 싶은 마음에 앞으로 달려나갔다. 그때 원반이 땅에서 튀어 올라 그의 이마를 때렸다. 그는 정신을 잃고 쓰러졌다. 신은 그 자신만큼이나 창백해진 얼굴로 그를 일으켜, 상처의 피를 멎게 하고 사라져 가는 생명을 붙들고자 자신의 온갖 재주를 다 부려 보았으나, 모든 것이 헛되었다. 그 상처는 의술의 힘이 미치지 못하는 것이었다. 마치 누군가 정원에서 백합의 줄기를 꺾으면 그 꽃이 고개를 떨구고 꽃송이를 땅으로 돌리듯이, 죽어 가는 소년의 머리도 마치 그 목에 너무 무겁기라도 한 듯 어깨 위로 떨구어졌다. \"그대가 죽어 가는구나, 히아킨토스여.\" 포이보스[아폴론]가 이렇게 말했다. \"나로 말미암아 그대의 젊음을 빼앗긴 채로 말이다. 그대에게는 고통이요, 내게는 죄로다. 내가 그대를 위해 죽을 수만 있다면! 그러나 그럴 수가 없으니, 그대는 추억 속에서, 또 노래 속에서 나와 더불어 살아가리라. 나의 리라가 그대를 기릴 것이며, 나의 노래가 그대의 운명을 들려줄 것이고, 그대는 나의 회한이 새겨진 한 송이 꽃이 되리라.\" 아폴론이 말하는 동안, 보라, 땅 위로 흘러 풀을 물들였던 그 피가 더는 피가 아니게 되었고, 티로스의 염료보다도 더 아름다운 빛깔의 꽃 한 송이가 솟아났으니, 이것은 자줏빛이요 백합은 은빛처럼 흰 것을 빼고는 백합을 닮은 꽃이었다.[12] 그러나 이것으로도 포이보스에게는 충분하지 않았다. 더 큰 영예를 부여하고자, 그는 그 꽃잎에 자신의 슬픔을 새겨, 오늘날에도 우리가 보는 대로 그 위에 \"아! 아!\" 하는 글자를 적어 넣었다. 그 꽃은 히아킨토스라는 이름을 지니며, 봄이 돌아올 때마다 그의 운명에 대한 기억을 되살린다.\n\n서풍인 제피로스 또한 히아킨토스를 좋아하여 그가 아폴론을 더 좋아하는 것을 질투한 나머지, 원반이 히아킨토스를 맞히도록 그 진로에서 벗어나게 불어 버렸다고 한다. 키츠는 자신의 「엔디미온」에서 원반던지기 놀이의 구경꾼들을 묘사하는 대목에서 이를 언급한다.\n\n\"혹은 그들은 원반 던지는 이들을 지켜보았으리, 양편에 마음을 쏟으며, 히아킨토스의 슬픈 죽음을 가엾이 여기며, 제피로스의 잔인한 숨결이 그를 죽이던 그때를. 이제 뉘우치는 제피로스는, 포이보스가 창공에 오르기 전에, 흐느끼는 빗속에서 그 꽃을 어루만지네.\"\n\n히아킨토스에 대한 언급은 밀턴의 「리시다스」에서도 알아볼 수 있다.\n\n\"비탄이 새겨진 저 핏빛 꽃처럼.\" ————\n\n케익스는 테살리아의 왕으로, 폭력이나 불의 없이 평화롭게 다스렸다. 그는 낮의 별인 헤스페로스의 아들이었고, 그 빛나는 아름다움은 보는 이로 하여금 그의 아버지를 떠올리게 했다. 아이올로스의 딸 할키오네는 그의 아내였으며, 그에게 헌신적으로 매달려 있었다. 그런데 케익스는 동생을 잃은 슬픔에 깊이 빠져 있었으며, 동생의 죽음에 뒤이어 나타난 무시무시한 징조들 탓에 마치 신들이 자신에게 적의를 품은 것처럼 느껴졌다. 그리하여 그는 이오니아의 카를로스로 항해하여 아폴론의 신탁을 구하는 것이 최선이라고 생각했다. 그러나 그가 이 뜻을 아내 할키오네에게 밝히자마자, 그녀의 온몸에 전율이 일었고 얼굴은 죽은 듯이 창백해졌다. \"사랑하는 남편이시여, 제 어떤 잘못이 당신의 애정을 제게서 돌려놓았나요? 늘 당신의 마음속 가장 윗자리를 차지하던 제게 대한 그 사랑은 어디로 갔나요? 할키오네가 없어도 마음이 편안하다는 것을 배우셨나요? 차라리 제가 곁에 없기를 바라시나요?\" 그녀는 또한 바람의 거셈을 묘사하며 그를 단념시키려 애썼다. 친정인 아버지의 집에서 살 적에 익히 알고 지냈던 바람의 모습을 그녀는 잘 알았으니, 아이올로스가 바람의 신이었고 그것들을 억누르는 데 온 힘을 다해야 했던 까닭이다. \"그것들이 한데 몰아쳐,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"그 격렬함에 부딪침으로 불꽃이 튈 정도예요. 하지만 정 가셔야 한다면,\" 그녀가 덧붙였다, \"사랑하는 남편이시여, 저를 함께 데려가 주세요. 그러지 않으면 저는 당신이 마주할 실제의 재앙뿐 아니라, 제 두려움이 빚어내는 재앙까지도 겪게 될 거예요.\"\n\n이 말은 케익스 왕의 마음을 무겁게 짓눌렀고, 아내를 데려가고 싶은 것은 그녀의 바람 못지않은 그 자신의 바람이기도 했다. 그러나 그는 그녀를 바다의 위험에 내맡길 수는 없었다. 그리하여 그는 할 수 있는 한 그녀를 위로하며 대답했고, 끝으로 이런 말을 했다. \"나의 아버지 낮의 별의 빛에 걸고 약속하오. 운명이 허락한다면, 달이 두 번 그 둘레를 다 돌기 전에 돌아오리다.\" 이렇게 말하고 나서 그는 배를 선창에서 끌어내고, 노와 돛을 실으라고 명했다. 할키오네는 이 준비를 보자 불길한 예감에 사로잡힌 듯 몸을 떨었다. 눈물과 흐느낌으로 작별을 고하고는, 그대로 정신을 잃고 땅에 쓰러졌다.\n\n케익스는 여전히 머뭇거리고자 했지만, 이제 젊은이들은 노를 움켜쥐고 길고 고른 동작으로 힘차게 물살을 헤쳐 나갔다. 할키오네는 눈물이 흐르는 눈을 들어, 갑판 위에 서서 자신을 향해 손을 흔드는 남편을 바라보았다. 그녀는 배가 너무 멀어져 그의 모습을 다른 사람들과 더 이상 구별할 수 없게 될 때까지 그의 신호에 답했다. 배 자체가 더 이상 보이지 않게 되자, 그녀는 돛의 마지막 희미한 빛을 붙잡으려 눈을 부릅떴고, 마침내 그것마저 사라졌다. 그제야 그녀는 방으로 물러나 외로운 침상에 몸을 던졌다.\n\n한편 그들은 항구를 미끄러져 나갔고, 산들바람이 밧줄 사이로 노닐었다. 뱃사람들은 노를 거두어들이고 돛을 올렸다. 항로의 절반쯤, 혹은 그보다 덜 지났을 무렵, 밤이 다가오면서 바다는 부풀어 오르는 파도로 하얗게 일기 시작했고, 동풍이 거센 바람을 몰아왔다. 선장이 돛을 거두라는 명령을 내렸으나, 폭풍이 복종을 가로막았으니, 바람과 파도의 포효가 너무도 커서 그의 명령이 들리지 않았던 까닭이다. 사람들은 저마다 알아서 노를 단단히 매고, 배를 보강하고, 돛을 줄이는 일에 분주했다. 그들이 저마다 최선이라 여기는 일을 하는 동안에도 폭풍은 거세어졌다. 사람들의 외침, 삭구의 덜컹거림, 파도의 부딪침이 천둥의 포효와 뒤섞였다. 부풀어 오른 바다는 하늘까지 치솟아 그 거품을 구름 사이에 흩뿌리는 듯했고, 그러고는 바닥으로 가라앉으며 여울의 빛깔, 곧 스틱스의 검음을 띠었다.\n\n배는 이 모든 변화를 함께 겪었다. 그것은 사냥꾼들의 창을 향해 돌진하는 야수와도 같았다. 마치 하늘이 내려와 바다와 하나가 되려는 듯, 비가 억수같이 쏟아졌다. 번개가 잠시 멎으면, 밤은 폭풍의 어둠 위에 제 어둠을 더하는 듯했고, 그러고는 번개가 번쩍여 어둠을 갈가리 찢고 모든 것을 섬광으로 밝혔다. 기술은 소용없었고, 용기는 꺾였으며, 파도마다 죽음이 닥쳐오는 듯했다. 사람들은 공포에 얼이 빠졌다. 집에 두고 온 부모와 친족과 약속들에 대한 생각이 그들의 마음을 스쳤다. 케익스는 할키오네를 생각했다. 그의 입술에는 그녀의 이름밖에 없었고, 그녀를 그리워하면서도, 그녀가 곁에 없음을 기뻐했다. 이윽고 돛대가 번개를 맞아 산산조각 났고, 키가 부러졌으며, 의기양양하게 굽이쳐 오른 물결이 그 잔해를 내려다보다가 떨어져 내려 산산이 부수어 버렸다. 뱃사람 가운데 몇몇은 그 충격에 정신을 잃고 가라앉아 다시는 떠오르지 못했고, 다른 이들은 난파선의 조각에 매달렸다. 케익스는 일찍이 왕홀을 움켜쥐던 그 손으로 널빤지를 단단히 붙잡고, 그의 아버지와 장인을 향해 도움을 청했으나, 아아, 헛된 일이었다. 그러나 그의 입술에 가장 자주 오른 것은 할키오네의 이름이었다. 그의 생각은 그녀에게 매달렸다. 그는 파도가 자신의 몸을 그녀의 눈앞으로 실어다 주기를, 그리하여 그녀의 손으로 묻히기를 기도했다. 마침내 물결이 그를 덮쳤고, 그는 가라앉았다. 그날 밤 낮의 별은 흐릿해 보였다. 하늘을 떠날 수 없었기에, 그 얼굴을 구름으로 가렸던 것이다.\n\n그동안 할키오네는 이 모든 참사를 알지 못한 채, 남편이 약속한 귀환의 날까지 날을 헤아렸다. 이제 그녀는 그가 입을 옷을 마련하고, 또 그가 도착할 때 자신이 입을 옷을 준비했다. 그녀는 모든 신들에게 자주 향을 올렸으나, 누구보다도 헤라[로마의 〈유노〉]에게 그러했다. 이미 세상에 없는 남편을 위해 그녀는 쉴 새 없이 기도했다. 그가 무사하기를, 집으로 돌아오기를, 떨어져 있는 동안 자신보다 더 사랑할 만한 사람을 만나지 않기를. 그러나 이 모든 기도 가운데 마지막 것만이 이루어질 운명이었다. 여신은 마침내 이미 죽은 자를 위해 간청받는 것을, 그리고 차라리 장례 의식을 올려야 마땅할 손이 자신의 제단을 향해 들어 올려지는 것을 더는 견딜 수 없었다. 그리하여 이리스를 불러 말했다. \"이리스, 나의 충실한 전령이여, 졸음에 잠긴 솜누스의 거처로 가서, 케익스의 모습을 한 환영을 할키오네에게 보내어 그 일을 알리라고 일러라.\"\n\n이리스는 여러 빛깔의 옷을 두르고, 무지개로 하늘을 물들이며, 잠의 왕의 궁전을 찾아갔다. 킴메리아 땅 가까이에 있는 한 산속 동굴이 둔한 신 솜누스의 거처였다. 이곳에는 포이보스[아폴론]가 떠오를 때도, 한낮에도, 질 때도 감히 들어오지 못했다. 땅에서는 구름과 그림자가 피어올랐고, 빛은 희미하게 가물거렸다. 볏 달린 머리를 한 새벽의 새는 그곳에서 결코 에오스[로마의 〈아우로라〉]를 향해 큰 소리로 울지 않았고, 경계하는 개도, 더 영리한 거위도 그 고요를 어지럽히지 않았다. 어떤 야수도, 가축도, 바람에 흔들리는 나뭇가지도, 사람의 대화 소리도 그 정적을 깨뜨리지 않았다. 그곳에는 침묵이 지배했다. 다만 바위 밑바닥에서 레테 강이 흘렀고, 그 졸졸거림이 잠으로 이끌었다. 동굴 문 앞에는 양귀비가 무성하게 자랐고, 또 다른 풀들도 자랐으니, 밤은 그것들의 즙에서 잠을 거두어 어두워진 대지 위에 흩뿌렸다. 이 저택에는 돌쩌귀에서 삐걱대는 문도, 어떤 파수꾼도 없었다. 다만 한가운데에 검은 깃털과 검은 휘장으로 장식된 검은 흑단 침상이 놓여 있었다. 그곳에 신은 잠으로 사지를 늘어뜨린 채 기대어 있었다. 그의 주위에는 온갖 형상을 닮은 꿈들이 누워 있었는데, 그 수는 추수철의 곡식 줄기만큼이나, 숲의 나뭇잎만큼이나, 바닷가의 모래알만큼이나 많았다.\n\n여신이 들어서서 자기 주위를 떠도는 꿈들을 떨쳐 내자, 그 광채가 동굴 전체를 밝혔다. 신은 가까스로 눈을 뜨고, 이따금 턱을 가슴에 떨구다가, 마침내 제 몸을 흔들어 잠에서 벗어나, 팔을 괴고는 그녀의 용건을 물었다. 그는 그녀가 누구인지 알았던 것이다. 그녀가 대답했다. \"솜누스여, 신들 가운데 가장 온화한 이, 마음을 가라앉히는 이, 근심에 지친 가슴을 달래는 이여, 헤라가 당신에게 명을 내리십니다. 트라키네 시의 할키오네에게 꿈을 보내어, 잃어버린 남편과 난파의 모든 일을 보여 주라고 하십니다.\"\n\n전갈을 전하고 나서 이리스는 서둘러 떠났으니, 그 정체된 공기를 더는 견딜 수 없었던 까닭이다. 졸음이 자신을 덮쳐 오는 것을 느끼자, 그녀는 빠져나와 왔던 길을 따라 무지개를 타고 돌아갔다. 그러자 솜누스는 자신의 수많은 아들 가운데 하나인 모르페우스를 불렀다. 그는 형상을 흉내 내고, 걸음걸이와 표정과 말하는 방식, 심지어 각자에게 가장 특징적인 옷차림과 자세까지 모방하는 데 가장 능했다. 그러나 그는 오직 사람만을 흉내 냈으며, 새와 짐승과 뱀을 흉내 내는 일은 다른 이에게 맡겼다. 그를 이켈로스라 불렀고, 또 세 번째인 판타소스는 자신을 바위와 물과 숲, 그 밖의 생명 없는 것들로 바꾸었다. 이들은 왕과 위대한 인물들의 잠든 시간에 시중을 들었고, 다른 이들은 평민들 사이를 오갔다. 솜누스는 형제들 가운데 모르페우스를 골라 이리스의 명을 수행하게 하고는, 베개에 머리를 누이고 달콤한 휴식에 몸을 맡겼다.\n\n모르페우스는 날개로 아무 소리도 내지 않고 날아, 이윽고 하이모니아의 도시에 이르렀다. 그곳에서 날개를 내려놓고 케익스의 모습을 취했다. 그 모습이되, 죽은 사람처럼 창백하고 벌거벗은 채로, 그는 가엾은 아내의 침상 앞에 섰다. 그의 수염은 물에 흠뻑 젖은 듯했고, 물에 빠진 머리카락에서 물이 뚝뚝 떨어졌다. 침대 위로 몸을 숙이고, 눈에서 눈물을 쏟으며 그가 말했다. \"불행한 아내여, 그대의 케익스를 알아보겠소, 아니면 죽음이 내 얼굴을 너무 많이 바꾸어 놓았소? 나를 보시오, 나를 알아보시오. 그대 남편 그 자신이 아니라, 그의 망령이오. 할키오네여, 그대의 기도는 내게 아무 소용이 없었소. 나는 죽었소. 더 이상 내가 돌아오리라는 헛된 희망으로 그대 자신을 속이지 마시오. 폭풍의 바람이 에게 해에서 내 배를 가라앉혔고, 내 입이 그대를 큰 소리로 부르는 동안 파도가 그 입을 채웠소. 불확실한 전령이 그대에게 이를 알리는 것도 아니요, 막연한 소문이 그대의 귀에 전하는 것도 아니오. 난파당한 자인 내가 몸소 와서 내 운명을 그대에게 알리는 것이오. 일어나시오! 내게 눈물을 주시오, 내게 애도를 주시오, 나로 하여금 애도받지 못한 채 타르타로스로 내려가지 않게 하시오.\" 이 말에 모르페우스는 그녀 남편의 것인 듯한 목소리를 더했다. 그는 진짜 눈물을 쏟는 듯했고, 그의 손짓은 케익스의 것이었다.\n\n할키오네는 흐느끼며 신음했고, 잠결에 팔을 뻗어 그의 몸을 끌어안으려 애썼으나, 붙잡은 것은 허공뿐이었다. \"멈추세요!\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"어디로 날아가시나요? 함께 가요.\" 자신의 목소리가 그녀를 깨웠다. 벌떡 일어나 그가 아직 거기 있는지 보려고 주위를 애타게 둘러보았으니, 그녀의 외침에 놀란 하인들이 등불을 가져온 참이었다. 그를 찾을 수 없자 그녀는 가슴을 치고 옷을 찢었다. 머리를 풀 생각도 하지 않고 그것을 마구 쥐어뜯었다. 유모가 슬픔의 까닭을 물었다. \"할키오네는 이제 없어요.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"그녀는 자기 케익스와 함께 죽었어요. 위로의 말일랑 하지 마세요, 그는 난파되어 죽었어요. 나는 그를 보았어요, 나는 그를 알아보았어요. 그를 붙잡아 머물게 하려고 손을 뻗었어요. 그의 망령은 사라졌지만, 그것은 내 남편의 참된 망령이었어요. 늘 보던 모습으로도, 그가 지녔던 아름다움으로도 아니라, 창백하고 벌거벗은 채, 바닷물에 젖은 머리로 가엾은 내 앞에 나타났어요. 여기, 바로 이 자리에 그 슬픈 환영이 서 있었어요.\" 그러면서 그녀는 그의 발자국 흔적을 찾으려 살폈다. \"바로 이것이었어요, 내가 그에게 나를 떠나지 말라고, 파도에 자신을 맡기지 말라고 간청했을 때, 예감하는 내 마음이 미리 두려워했던 바로 그것 말이에요. 아, 정녕 가시려거든 나를 함께 데려가셨더라면 얼마나 좋았을까요! 그편이 훨씬 나았을 거예요. 그랬다면 나는 당신 없이 보낼 생명의 자투리도 남지 않았을 것이고, 따로 맞을 죽음도 없었을 거예요. 만일 내가 살아남아 견디려 애쓸 수 있다면, 나는 바다가 내게 그러했던 것보다 더 나 자신에게 잔인할 거예요. 하지만 나는 애쓰지 않을래요, 나는 당신과 떨어지지 않을래요, 불행한 남편이여. 적어도 이번만은 내가 당신과 동행할게요. 죽음에 이르러, 한 무덤이 우리를 함께 담을 수 없다면, 한 묘비명만은 그리할 거예요. 내 유골을 당신의 것과 함께 누일 수 없다 하더라도, 적어도 내 이름만은 떼어 놓이지 않을 거예요.\" 슬픔이 더 이상의 말을 가로막았고, 이 말들마저 눈물과 흐느낌으로 끊어졌다.\n\n이제 아침이 되었다. 그녀는 바닷가로 가서, 그가 떠날 때 마지막으로 그를 보았던 자리를 찾았다. \"그가 여기서 머뭇거리며 닻줄을 풀 때, 그는 내게 마지막 입맞춤을 했지.\" 그녀가 모든 사물을 되짚어 보며 모든 순간을 떠올리려 애쓰다가, 바다 너머를 내다보던 중, 물 위에 떠 있는 어렴풋한 물체를 발견했다. 처음에는 그것이 무엇인지 알 수 없었으나, 점차 파도가 그것을 더 가까이 실어 왔고, 그것은 분명히 사람의 몸이었다. 누구인지는 몰랐으나, 어떤 난파당한 자의 것이었기에 그녀는 깊이 마음이 움직여 그것에 눈물을 바치며 말했다. \"아아! 불행한 이여, 그리고 만일 그런 이가 있다면, 불행한 것은 그대의 아내로다!\" 파도에 실려 그것은 점점 더 가까이 다가왔다. 그녀가 점점 더 가까이 그것을 바라볼수록 그녀는 점점 더 떨었다. 이제, 이제 그것이 물가로 다가왔다. 이제 그녀가 알아보는 표지들이 나타났다. 그것은 그녀의 남편이었다! 그것을 향해 떨리는 손을 뻗으며 그녀가 외쳤다. \"오 가장 사랑하는 남편이여, 이렇게 내게 돌아오시나요?\"\n\n물가에서 바다를 향해 방파제가 쌓여 있었으니, 바다의 공격을 막고 그 격렬한 침입을 막아 내려고 지은 것이었다. 그녀는 이 둑 위로 뛰어올랐고, 그러고는 (그녀가 그럴 수 있었다는 것이 놀라운 일이었다) 그녀는 날아올라, 그 순간 돋아난 날개로 공기를 치며, 가엾은 새가 되어 물 위를 스치듯 날았다. 날아가는 동안 그녀의 목구멍에서는 슬픔으로 가득한 소리, 애도하는 이의 목소리 같은 소리가 흘러나왔다. 그녀가 말없이 핏기 없는 그 몸에 닿았을 때, 그녀는 갓 돋아난 날개로 사랑하는 그 사지를 감쌌고, 뿔처럼 단단한 부리로 입맞춤을 하려 애썼다. 케익스가 그것을 느꼈는지, 아니면 그것이 단지 파도의 작용이었는지, 바라보던 이들은 의심했으나, 그 몸은 머리를 드는 듯했다. 그러나 그는 정말로 그것을 느꼈고, 가엾이 여긴 신들에 의해 두 사람 모두 새로 변했다. 그들은 짝을 짓고 새끼를 낳았다. 겨울철, 이레 동안의 잔잔한 날에 할키오네는 바다 위에 떠 있는 둥지 위에서 알을 품는다. 그때면 뱃사람들에게 길이 안전하다. 아이올로스는 바람을 지켜 깊은 바다를 어지럽히지 못하게 한다. 그 동안 바다는 그의 손주들에게 내맡겨진다.\n\n바이런의 「아비도스의 신부」에 나오는 다음 시구는, 작가가 떠다니는 시신의 움직임을 관찰하여 그 착상을 얻었다고 밝혀 두지 않았더라면, 이 묘사의 마무리 부분에서 빌려 온 것처럼 보일 법하다.\n\n\"쉴 줄 모르는 베개 위에서 흔들리듯, 그의 머리는 일렁이는 물결과 함께 일렁이고, 생명의 것이 아닌 그 손의 움직임은, 그래도 희미하게 싸움을 위협하는 듯, 출렁이는 조류에 높이 던져졌다가, 이윽고 물결과 같은 높이로 가라앉는다……\"\n\n밀턴은 「그리스도 탄생의 찬가」에서 할키온의 이야기를 이렇게 암시한다.\n\n\"그러나 평화로웠도다 그 밤은 빛의 왕자가 이 땅 위에 평화의 통치를 시작하셨던 밤은. 바람은 경이로움에 잠잠해지고 잔잔히 물결에 입맞추며 온화한 대양에 새로운 기쁨을 속삭였으니, 이제 그 바다는 사납게 날뛰기를 까맣게 잊었도다 고요의 새들이 마법에 걸린 물결 위에 앉아 알을 품는 동안에.\"\n\n키츠 또한 「엔디미온」에서 이렇게 말한다.\n\n\"오 마법 같은 잠이여! 오 위안을 주는 새여 어지러운 마음의 바다 위에서 알을 품는구나 그것이 잠잠해지고 잔잔해질 때까지.\" ————\n\n# VERTUMNUS AND POMONA\n\n하마드리아데스는 숲의 님프였다. 포모나도 이 부류에 속했는데, 정원을 가꾸고 과실을 기르는 데 대한 애정에서는 누구도 그녀를 능가하지 못했다. 그녀는 숲이나 강에는 관심이 없었고, 사람이 일군 들판과 맛있는 사과가 열리는 나무를 사랑했다. 그녀의 오른손에 들린 무기는 투창이 아니라 가지치기 칼이었다. 그 칼로 무장한 채 그녀는 어느 때에는 지나치게 무성하게 자란 가지를 솎아 내고, 제자리를 벗어나 제멋대로 뻗은 가지를 잘라 내느라 분주했으며, 또 어느 때에는 잔가지를 갈라 그 속에 접가지를 끼워 넣어 가지로 하여금 제 것이 아닌 어린 가지를 받아들이게 하였다. 또한 그녀는 자기가 아끼는 나무들이 가뭄에 시달리지 않도록 정성을 기울였고, 목마른 뿌리가 물을 마실 수 있도록 그 곁으로 물줄기를 끌어 댔다. 이 일이 그녀의 일과이자 열정이었다. 그리고 그녀는 아프로디테[로마의 〈베누스〉]가 불어넣는 정념으로부터 자유로웠다. 그녀가 시골 사람들을 두려워하지 않은 것은 아니어서, 과수원에 자물쇠를 채우고 남자들이 들어오는 것을 허락하지 않았다. 파우누스들과 사티로스들은 그녀의 마음을 얻기 위해 가진 것 전부라도 내놓았을 것이고, 나이에 비해 젊어 보이는 늙은 실바누스도, 머리에 소나무 잎으로 엮은 화관을 두른 판도 그랬을 것이다. 그러나 누구보다도 베르툼누스가 그녀를 깊이 사랑했다. 하지만 그도 다른 이들보다 나을 것이 없었다. 오, 그는 얼마나 자주 추수꾼으로 변장하여 광주리에 곡식을 담아 그녀에게 가져갔던가. 그 모습은 영락없는 추수꾼이었다. 건초 묶는 끈을 몸에 두른 그를 보면 누구라도 그가 방금 풀을 뒤집다 온 줄로 여겼을 것이다. 때로는 손에 소몰이 막대를 들고 있어, 사람들은 그가 지친 소들의 멍에를 막 벗긴 참이라고 말했을 것이다. 또 어떤 때에는 가지치기용 낫을 들고 포도밭 일꾼 행세를 했고, 다시 어깨에 사다리를 메고 사과를 따러 가는 듯 보였다. 때로는 제대한 병사처럼 터덜터덜 걸었고, 또 어떤 때에는 낚시하러 가는 듯 낚싯대를 들었다. 이런 방식으로 그는 거듭거듭 그녀에게 다가가는 것을 허락받았고, 그녀를 바라보는 것으로 자신의 정념을 달랬다.\n\n어느 날 그는 노파의 모습으로 찾아왔다. 백발 위에 모자를 쓰고 손에는 지팡이를 들고 있었다. 그녀는 정원에 들어와 과실을 칭송했다. \"참으로 자랑스러운 솜씨로구나, 얘야.\" 노파는 이렇게 말하며 그녀에게 입을 맞추었는데, 그것은 꼭 노파의 입맞춤이라고 할 수만은 없었다. 노파는 둑에 걸터앉아 머리 위로 드리운, 과실이 주렁주렁 매달린 가지들을 올려다보았다. 맞은편에는 탱탱하게 익은 포도송이를 가득 단 포도 덩굴이 휘감긴 느릅나무가 서 있었다. 노파는 그 나무와 거기 어우러진 덩굴을 똑같이 칭찬했다. \"그렇지만,\" 노파가 말했다, \"만일 저 나무가 홀로 서 있고 휘감긴 덩굴이 없었다면, 우리를 끌어당기거나 우리에게 베풀 것이라곤 쓸모없는 잎사귀밖에 없었을 게다. 마찬가지로 저 덩굴도 느릅나무를 휘감지 않았다면 땅바닥에 납작하게 누워 있었을 테고. 어찌하여 너는 저 나무와 덩굴에서 교훈을 얻어 누군가와 맺어지는 데 동의하지 않으려는 게냐? 나는 네가 그러기를 바란단다. 헬레네에게도 그토록 많은 구혼자가 없었고, 지혜로운 오디세우스의 아내 페넬로페에게도 그러지 못했지. 네가 그들을 물리치는 동안에도, 그들은 너에게 구애하고 있단다. 이 산을 자주 찾는 들판의 신들이며 온갖 부류의 다른 이들이 말이다. 하지만 네가 사려 깊어 좋은 인연을 맺고자 한다면, 그리고 네가 짐작하는 것보다 훨씬 너를 사랑하는 이 늙은이의 충고를 받아들인다면, 다른 이들은 모두 물리치고 내 권유대로 베르툼누스를 받아들이려무나. 나는 그를 그 자신이 아는 만큼이나 잘 안단다. 그는 떠돌이 신이 아니라 이 산에 속한 신이지. 또한 요즘의 너무나 많은 연인들처럼 눈에 띄는 누구든 사랑하는 그런 자가 아니란다. 그는 너를, 오직 너만을 사랑해. 게다가 그는 젊고 잘생겼으며, 마음먹은 대로 어떤 모습이든 취하는 재주가 있어서, 네가 명하는 그대로 제 모습을 바꿀 수 있단다. 더욱이 그는 네가 좋아하는 것을 똑같이 좋아하고, 정원 가꾸기를 즐기며, 너의 사과를 감탄하며 다룬단다. 하지만 지금 그는 과실에도 꽃에도, 그 어떤 것에도 마음을 두지 않고 오직 너만을 생각하지. 그를 가엾이 여기고, 지금 그가 내 입을 빌려 말하고 있다고 여겨 보아라. 신들이 잔혹함을 벌하시고, 아프로디테가 모진 마음을 미워하여 그런 죄과를 머잖아 응징하신다는 것을 잊지 말거라. 이를 증명하기 위해, 키프로스에서 실제 있었던 일로 널리 알려진 이야기를 하나 들려주마. 이 이야기가 너를 한결 자비롭게 만드는 효과를 내기를 바란단다.\n\n이피스는 미천한 가문에서 태어난 젊은이로, 테우크로스의 오랜 가문에 속한 고귀한 여인 아낙사레테를 보고 사랑하게 되었단다. 그는 오래도록 자신의 정념과 싸웠지만, 도저히 그것을 억누를 수 없음을 알자 그녀의 저택으로 탄원하러 갔지. 처음에 그는 그녀의 유모에게 자신의 사랑을 털어놓고, 길러 낸 아이를 사랑하는 마음으로 자기 청을 도와 달라고 애원했단다. 그다음에는 그녀의 하인들을 제 편으로 끌어들이려 했지. 때로는 글을 적은 서판에 자기 맹세를 새겼고, 자주 그녀의 문에 눈물로 적신 화관을 걸어 두었단다. 그녀의 문지방에 몸을 뉘고는, 비정한 빗장과 자물쇠를 향해 자신의 한탄을 쏟아 냈지. 그녀는 11월의 사나운 바람에 일어나는 파도보다도 귀가 먹었고, 게르만의 대장간에서 나온 강철보다도, 제가 솟은 절벽에 여전히 들러붙어 있는 바위보다도 단단했단다. 그녀는 그를 비웃고 조롱하며, 그 박정한 처사에 잔인한 말까지 덧붙였고, 한 줄기 희망의 빛조차 비춰 주지 않았지.\n\n이피스는 더 이상 가망 없는 사랑의 고통을 견딜 수 없어, 그녀의 문 앞에 서서 이 마지막 말을 내뱉었단다. '아낙사레테여, 그대가 이겼소. 이제 더는 나의 성가신 졸음을 견딜 필요가 없으리다. 그대의 승리를 누리시오! 환희의 노래를 부르고, 이마에 월계관을 두르시오. 그대가 이겼으니! 나는 죽소. 돌처럼 굳은 마음이여, 기뻐하시오! 적어도 이것만큼은 그대를 흡족하게 하고 그대로 하여금 나를 칭송하게 만들 수 있으리다. 또한 이로써 그대를 향한 사랑이 오직 목숨과 함께 나를 떠났음을 증명하리다. 게다가 내 죽음을 그대에게 전하는 일을 소문에 맡기지 않겠소. 내가 몸소 와서, 그대가 내 죽는 모습을 보게 하고, 그 광경으로 그대의 눈을 즐겁게 하리다. 그러나, 오, 인간의 비탄을 굽어보시는 신들이여, 나의 운명을 살피소서! 나는 다만 이것만을 청하나이다. 다가오는 세월에 내가 기억되게 하시고, 그대들이 내 목숨에서 앗아 간 그 햇수를 내 명성에 보태 주소서.' 그는 이렇게 말한 뒤, 창백한 얼굴과 눈물 어린 눈을 그녀의 저택 쪽으로 돌리고, 자기가 자주 화관을 걸어 두던 문기둥에 밧줄을 매고는, 그 올가미 안에 머리를 넣으며 중얼거렸지. '이 화관만큼은 그대를 기쁘게 하리다, 잔인한 처녀여!' 그러고는 떨어지며 목이 부러진 채 매달렸단다. 떨어지면서 그는 문에 부딪혔고, 그 소리는 신음 소리와도 같았지. 하인들이 문을 열어 그가 죽은 것을 발견하고는, 연민의 탄식을 내뱉으며 그를 들어 올려 어머니에게 데려갔단다. 그의 아버지는 살아 있지 않았기 때문이지. 어머니는 아들의 주검을 받아 들고, 그 차가운 몸을 가슴에 끌어안으며, 자식을 잃은 어머니가 토해 내는 슬픈 말들을 쏟아 냈단다. 비통한 장례 행렬이 마을을 지나갔고, 창백한 시신은 상여에 실려 화장터로 옮겨졌지. 우연히도 아낙사레테의 집은 그 행렬이 지나가는 거리에 있었고, 조문객들의 통곡이 이미 복수의 신에게 벌받을 자로 점지된 그녀의 귀에 닿았단다.\n\n'이 슬픈 행렬을 좀 보아야겠다.' 그녀는 이렇게 말하며 작은 탑으로 올라가, 열린 창을 통해 장례식을 내려다보았지. 상여에 누인 이피스의 모습에 그녀의 눈길이 닿자마자, 그 눈이 굳어지기 시작했고, 몸속의 따뜻한 피가 차갑게 식어 가기 시작했단다. 뒤로 물러서려 했으나 발을 움직일 수 없음을 알았고, 얼굴을 돌리려 했으나 헛수고였으며, 차츰 그녀의 사지가 죄다 그 마음처럼 돌이 되어 갔지. 네가 이 일을 의심하지 않도록 말해 두자면, 그 조각상은 지금도 살라미스의 아프로디테 신전에 그 여인의 모습 그대로 서 있단다. 그러니 이제 이런 것들을 헤아려 보거라, 얘야. 그리고 너의 멸시와 미적거림을 내려놓고 연인을 받아들이려무나. 그리하면 봄철의 서리도 네 어린 과실을 시들게 하지 못할 것이며, 사나운 바람도 네 꽃들을 흩날리지 못하리라!\"\n\n베르툼누스는 이렇게 말하고 나서 노파의 변장을 벗어 던지고, 잘생긴 젊은이라는 제 본모습으로 그녀 앞에 섰다. 그녀에게는 그것이 마치 구름을 뚫고 솟아오르는 태양 같아 보였다. 그는 다시 간청을 거듭하려 했으나 그럴 필요가 없었다. 그의 논변과 그 참모습의 광경이 효력을 발휘했고, 님프는 더 이상 저항하지 않고 서로 같은 불길에 휩싸였음을 인정했다.\n\n포모나는 사과 과수원의 각별한 수호신이었으므로, 무운시로 사과술에 관한 시를 지은 시인 필립스가 그녀를 불러 기렸다. 톰슨은 「사계절」에서 그를 이렇게 암시한다.\n\n\"포모나의 시인 필립스여, 그대는 두 번째로 각운에 얽매이지 않은 시구로, 브리튼의 자유로써 브리튼의 노래를 부르기를 고결하게 감행한 이로다.\"\n\n그러나 포모나는 다른 과실들도 주관하는 여신으로 여겨졌으니, 그러한 자격으로 톰슨이 그녀를 불러 기린다.\n\n\"포모나여, 나를 그대의 시트론 숲으로 데려가 주오, 레몬과 알싸한 라임이, 초록을 뚫고 타오르는 짙은 오렌지와 더불어, 그 한결 가벼운 영광을 어우러 놓은 그곳으로. 나를 비스듬히 누이소서 널리 가지를 뻗은 타마린드 아래에, 산들바람에 흔들려 열병을 식히는 그 열매를 떨구는 나무 아래에.\" ————\n\n# CUPID AND PSYCHE\n\n어떤 왕과 왕비에게 세 딸이 있었다. 위의 두 딸도 보통 이상으로 아름다웠지만, 막내딸의 미모는 너무도 경이로워서 빈약한 언어로는 그에 합당한 찬사를 표현할 길이 없었다. 그녀의 미모에 관한 명성이 얼마나 컸던지 이웃 나라에서 온 낯선 이들이 그 광경을 즐기려고 무리 지어 몰려와 경탄하며 그녀를 바라보았고, 오로지 아프로디테[로마의 〈베누스〉]에게만 바쳐야 마땅한 경배를 그녀에게 바쳤다. 실제로 아프로디테는 자기 제단이 버려진 것을 알게 되었으니, 사람들이 이 젊은 처녀에게 정성을 돌린 까닭이었다. 그녀가 지나갈 때면 사람들은 그녀를 찬미하는 노래를 부르고, 그녀가 가는 길에 화관과 꽃을 흩뿌렸다.\n\n오로지 불멸의 권능에게만 마땅한 경배를 한낱 필멸자를 떠받드는 데로 그릇되이 돌린 이 일은 진짜 아프로디테의 큰 노여움을 샀다. 그녀는 분개하여 신들의 향기로운 머리채를 흔들며 외쳤다. \"그렇다면 한낱 필멸의 계집아이에게 내 영예가 가려져야 한단 말이냐? 제우스께서 친히 그 판단을 인정하신 저 왕가의 목자가 나의 빛나는 연적인 팔라스[아테나]와 헤라[로마의 〈유노〉]를 제치고 나에게 미의 영예를 안겨 준 것이 부질없는 일이었더란 말이냐. 하지만 저 계집아이가 그토록 조용히 내 영예를 가로채도록 두지는 않으리라. 저 부당한 미모를 후회하도록 만들어 주겠다.\"\n\n그러고 나서 그녀는 날개 달린 아들 에로스[로마의 〈쿠피도〉]를 불렀으니, 그는 본디 천성부터 짓궂은 데다, 그녀의 하소연이 그를 더욱 부추기고 자극했다. 그녀는 그에게 프시케를 가리켜 보이며 말했다. \"사랑하는 내 아들아, 저 오만한 미모를 벌하여라. 네 어미가 입은 모욕이 큰 만큼 그에 못지않게 달콤한 복수를 안겨 다오. 저 거만한 계집아이의 가슴에 어떤 비천하고 미천하며 보잘것없는 자에 대한 정념을 불어넣어, 지금 누리는 우쭐함과 승리만큼이나 큰 굴욕을 거두게 하여라.\"\n\n에로스는 어머니의 분부를 따를 채비를 했다. 아프로디테의 정원에는 두 개의 샘이 있는데, 하나는 단물의 샘이고 다른 하나는 쓴물의 샘이었다. 에로스는 호박으로 만든 병 두 개에 각각 한 샘에서 물을 채워 자기 화살통 꼭대기에 매달고, 프시케의 침실로 서둘러 갔는데, 가 보니 그녀는 잠들어 있었다. 그는 쓴물 샘의 물을 몇 방울 그녀의 입술 위에 떨어뜨렸으나, 그녀를 보고 있노라니 거의 연민이 일어날 지경이었다. 그러고는 화살촉으로 그녀의 옆구리를 건드렸다. 그 닿음에 그녀가 깨어나 (그 자신은 보이지 않는) 에로스에게로 눈을 떴고, 이에 그는 어찌나 놀랐던지 당황한 나머지 제 화살에 스스로 상처를 입고 말았다. 그러나 그는 제 상처에는 아랑곳하지 않고, 오로지 자기가 저지른 해악을 바로잡을 생각뿐이어서, 그녀의 비단결 같은 곱슬머리 위에 온통 기쁨의 향기로운 물방울을 부었다.\n\n이후로 아프로디테의 눈 밖에 난 프시케는 자신의 온갖 미모로부터 아무런 덕도 보지 못했다. 과연 모든 눈이 그녀를 열렬히 바라보았고, 모든 입이 그녀를 찬미했으나, 왕도 왕자도 평민도 그녀에게 청혼하려고 나서는 이가 없었다. 보통 정도의 미모를 지닌 두 언니는 이미 오래전에 두 왕자와 혼인했으나, 프시케는 외로운 처소에서 자신의 고독을 한탄하며, 넘치는 아첨은 안겨 주었으되 사랑은 깨우지 못한 그 미모에 진절머리를 냈다.\n\n그녀의 부모는 자신들이 모르는 사이에 신들의 노여움을 산 것이 아닌가 두려워하여 아폴론의 신탁에 물었고, 이런 답을 받았다. \"그 처녀는 어떤 필멸의 연인의 신부가 될 운명이 아니로다. 그녀의 장래 남편이 산꼭대기에서 그녀를 기다리고 있느니라. 그는 신도 인간도 대적할 수 없는 괴물이로다.\"\n\n이 무시무시한 신탁의 선고는 온 백성을 경악에 빠뜨렸고, 그녀의 부모는 비탄에 몸을 내맡겼다. 그러나 프시케가 말했다. \"사랑하는 부모님, 어찌하여 이제 와 저를 두고 슬퍼하십니까? 차라리 백성들이 제게 분에 넘치는 영예를 쏟아붓고 한목소리로 저를 아프로디테라 불렀을 때 슬퍼하셨어야지요. 이제야 제가 그 이름의 희생양임을 깨닫습니다. 저는 따르겠습니다. 제 불행한 운명이 저를 정해 놓은 그 바위로 저를 이끌어 주소서.\" 그리하여 만반의 준비가 갖추어지자, 왕가의 처녀는 혼례의 행렬이라기보다 차라리 장례 행렬에 가까운 그 대열에서 자기 자리를 잡았고, 부모와 함께 백성의 통곡 속에서 산을 올랐으며, 그 산꼭대기에서 사람들은 그녀를 홀로 남겨 두고 슬픈 마음으로 집으로 돌아갔다.\n\n프시케가 두려움에 헐떡이며 눈에는 눈물이 가득한 채 산마루에 서 있을 때, 부드러운 서풍 제피로스가 그녀를 땅에서 들어 올려 편안한 움직임으로 꽃이 만발한 골짜기로 실어 갔다. 차츰 그녀의 마음은 가라앉았고, 그녀는 풀이 우거진 둑에 몸을 누이고 잠을 청했다. 잠으로 기운을 되찾고 깨어났을 때, 그녀는 주위를 둘러보다가 가까이에 크고 위엄 있는 나무들이 늘어선 쾌적한 숲을 보았다. 그녀가 그 안으로 들어가자, 한가운데에 맑고 수정 같은 물을 뿜어내는 샘이 있었고, 그 바로 곁에는 장려한 궁전이 있었으니, 그 위엄 있는 정면은 보는 이로 하여금 그것이 필멸의 손으로 빚은 것이 아니라 어느 신의 행복한 안식처라는 인상을 주었다. 경탄과 경이에 이끌려 그녀는 그 건물에 다가가 용기를 내어 안으로 들어갔다. 마주치는 모든 것이 그녀를 기쁨과 경탄으로 채웠다. 황금 기둥들이 둥근 천장을 떠받치고 있었고, 벽들은 사냥감인 짐승들과 전원 풍경을 담은 조각과 회화로 장식되어 보는 이의 눈을 즐겁게 하기에 알맞았다. 더 안으로 나아가니, 그녀는 의례를 위한 방들 외에도 온갖 종류의 보물과, 자연과 기예가 빚어낸 아름답고 진귀한 산물로 가득한 다른 방들이 있음을 알아차렸다.\n\n그녀의 눈이 이렇게 사로잡혀 있을 때, 비록 아무도 보이지 않았으나 어떤 목소리가 그녀에게 이런 말을 건넸다. \"여왕이신 마님, 보시는 모든 것이 마님의 것입니다. 마님께서 그 목소리를 듣고 계신 저희는 마님의 종이며, 온갖 정성과 부지런함으로 마님의 모든 분부를 받들겠나이다. 그러니 침실로 물러가 솜털 침상에서 쉬시고, 적당하다 여기실 때 목욕탕으로 가소서. 곁방에는 마님께서 자리에 드시기를 즐거이 원하실 때 저녁 식사가 기다리고 있나이다.\"\n\n프시케는 목소리뿐인 시종들의 권유에 귀를 기울였고, 휴식을 취하고 목욕으로 기운을 차린 뒤 곁방에 자리를 잡으니, 곧바로 시중드는 자나 종의 손길이 보이지도 않는데 식탁이 눈앞에 차려졌으며, 거기에는 더없이 진귀한 음식과 더없이 감미로운 술이 가득했다. 그녀의 귀 또한 보이지 않는 연주자들의 음악으로 즐거움을 누렸으니, 그중 하나는 노래하고 다른 하나는 류트를 연주했으며, 모두가 어우러져 충만한 합창의 경이로운 조화로 끝맺었다.\n\n그녀는 정해진 남편을 아직 보지 못했다. 그는 오로지 어둠의 시간에만 찾아왔다가 아침의 동이 트기 전에 사라졌으나, 그의 말투는 사랑으로 가득하여 그녀에게도 그와 같은 정념을 불어넣었다. 그녀는 자주 그에게 머물러 자기에게 모습을 보여 달라고 간청했으나, 그는 동의하려 하지 않았다. 오히려 그는 자기를 보려는 어떠한 시도도 하지 말라고 당부했으니, 더없이 마땅한 이유로 모습을 감추는 것이 그의 뜻이었던 까닭이다. \"어찌하여 그대는 나를 보고 싶어 하오?\" 그가 말했다. \"그대는 내 사랑을 의심하오? 채워지지 않은 소망이라도 있소? 만일 나를 본다면, 그대는 어쩌면 나를 두려워하거나, 어쩌면 숭배할지도 모르오. 그러나 내가 그대에게 바라는 모든 것은 나를 사랑해 달라는 것뿐이오. 나는 그대가 나를 신으로 숭배하기보다 대등한 자로서 사랑해 주기를 바라오.\"\n\n이 논리는 한동안 프시케를 어느 정도 진정시켰고, 그 새로움이 지속되는 동안 그녀는 꽤 행복했다. 그러나 마침내 자신의 운명을 모른 채 남겨진 부모 생각과, 자기 처지의 기쁨을 함께 나누지 못하는 언니들 생각이 그녀의 마음을 갉아먹어, 그녀는 자신의 궁전을 한낱 화려한 감옥으로 느끼기 시작했다. 어느 밤 남편이 찾아왔을 때, 그녀는 그에게 자신의 괴로움을 털어놓았고, 마침내 언니들을 데려와 자기를 보게 해도 좋다는 마지못한 허락을 그에게서 끌어냈다.\n\n그리하여 그녀는 제피로스를 불러 남편의 분부를 알렸고, 그는 즉시 순순히 따라 곧 언니들을 산 너머 동생의 골짜기로 데려왔다. 그들은 그녀를 끌어안았고 그녀도 그들의 포옹에 응했다. \"자,\" 프시케가 말했다. \"저와 함께 제 집으로 들어가셔서 동생이 내어 드릴 수 있는 무엇으로든 기운을 차리세요.\" 그러고는 그들의 손을 잡고 자신의 황금 궁전으로 이끌었으며, 수많은 목소리 시종 무리에게 맡겨 목욕탕과 식탁에서 그들의 기운을 돋우고 자신의 온갖 보물을 보여 주도록 했다. 이 천상의 즐거움을 보자, 어린 동생이 자기들 것을 훨씬 능가하는 그런 위세와 화려함을 누리는 것을 보고 그들의 가슴에는 시샘이 일어났다.\n\n그들은 그녀에게 수없이 많은 질문을 던졌는데, 그중에는 그녀의 남편이 어떤 사람이냐는 물음도 있었다. 프시케는 그가 아름다운 젊은이로, 대개 낮에는 산에서 사냥을 하며 보낸다고 대답했다. 언니들은 이 대답에 만족하지 않고, 곧 그녀가 그를 한 번도 본 적이 없음을 실토하게 만들었다. 그러고는 그녀의 가슴을 어두운 의심으로 채우기 시작했다. \"기억해 봐,\" 그들이 말했다. \"네가 무섭고 끔찍한 괴물과 혼인할 운명이라고 선언한 피티아의 신탁을 말이야. 이 골짜기 사람들이 하는 말로는 네 남편이 무섭고 괴물 같은 뱀이라더구나. 그자가 한동안 너를 진미로 먹여 살리는 것은 머지않아 너를 잡아먹으려는 것이라더라. 우리 충고를 들어. 등불 하나와 날카로운 칼 한 자루를 마련해서, 네 남편이 찾아내지 못하도록 감추어 두었다가, 그가 깊이 잠들거든 침대에서 살그머니 빠져나와 등불을 가져와서, 그들이 하는 말이 사실인지 아닌지 네 눈으로 직접 보아라. 만일 사실이거든, 망설이지 말고 그 괴물의 머리를 베어 그로써 네 자유를 되찾아라.\"\n\n프시케는 이 설득에 할 수 있는 한 맞섰으나, 그것이 그녀의 마음에 영향을 미치지 않을 리 없었고, 언니들이 떠나자 그들의 말과 그녀 자신의 호기심이 너무도 강하여 그녀로서는 도저히 거스를 수 없었다. 그리하여 그녀는 등불과 날카로운 칼을 마련해 남편의 눈에 띄지 않게 숨겨 두었다. 그가 첫잠에 빠져들자, 그녀는 조용히 일어나 등불의 덮개를 벗겼고, 흉측한 괴물이 아니라 신들 가운데 가장 아름답고 매혹적인 이를 보았으니, 그의 황금빛 곱슬머리는 눈처럼 흰 목과 진홍빛 뺨 위로 흘러내렸고, 어깨에는 눈보다 흰 이슬 맺힌 두 날개가 봄의 여린 꽃송이 같은 빛나는 깃털과 더불어 달려 있었다. 그의 얼굴을 더 가까이 보려고 등불을 기울이는 순간, 타는 기름 한 방울이 그 신의 어깨 위에 떨어졌고, 이에 깜짝 놀란 그는 눈을 떠 그녀를 정면으로 응시하더니, 한 마디도 하지 않고 자신의 흰 날개를 펼쳐 창밖으로 날아가 버렸다. 프시케는 그를 뒤따르려 했으나 헛되이 창에서 땅으로 떨어지고 말았다. 에로스는 그녀가 흙먼지 속에 누운 것을 보고 잠시 비행을 멈추고 말했다. \"오, 어리석은 프시케여, 그대는 이렇게 내 사랑에 보답하는가? 내 어머니의 분부를 거역하면서까지 그대를 내 아내로 삼았건만, 그대는 나를 괴물로 여겨 내 머리를 베려 하다니? 하지만 가라. 그대의 언니들에게로 돌아가라. 그대는 내 충고보다 그들의 충고가 더 낫다고 여기는 듯하니. 나는 그대를 영원히 떠나는 것 외에 다른 벌은 내리지 않겠다. 사랑은 의심과 더불어 머무를 수 없는 법이다.\" 이렇게 말하고 그는 가엾은 프시케를 땅바닥에 엎드린 채로 남겨 두고 날아가 버렸으니, 그녀는 그 자리를 슬픈 통곡으로 채웠다.\n\n그녀가 어느 정도 평정을 되찾고 주위를 둘러보았을 때, 궁전과 정원은 사라지고 없었으며, 그녀는 언니들이 사는 도시에서 그리 멀지 않은 허허벌판에 있는 자신을 발견했다. 그녀는 그곳으로 가서 자신의 불행에 관한 이야기를 모조리 들려주었는데, 그 심술궂은 것들은 겉으로는 슬퍼하는 체하면서 속으로는 기뻐했다. \"이제,\" 그들이 말했다. \"어쩌면 그가 우리 중 하나를 택할 테니까.\" 이런 생각으로, 자기 속셈은 한 마디도 입에 담지 않은 채, 그들 각자는 이튿날 아침 일찍 일어나 산을 올랐고, 꼭대기에 다다르자 제피로스에게 자기를 받아 그의 주인에게로 데려가 달라고 외쳤다. 그러고는 뛰어올랐으나 제피로스의 받침을 받지 못해 벼랑 아래로 떨어져 산산조각이 나고 말았다.\n\n그동안 프시케는 음식도 휴식도 없이 밤낮으로 헤매며 남편을 찾았다. 이마에 장려한 신전을 인 높다란 산에 눈길을 던지며, 그녀는 한숨지으며 혼잣말을 했다. \"어쩌면 내 사랑, 내 주인이 저기에 사실지도.\" 그러고는 그곳으로 발걸음을 향했다.\n\n그녀가 안으로 들어서자마자 곡식 더미가 보였으니, 더러는 흩어진 이삭으로, 더러는 단으로 묶여 있었고, 보리 이삭이 뒤섞여 있었다. 여기저기에는 낫과 갈퀴, 그리고 추수의 온갖 연장이 어지러이 흩어져 있었으니, 마치 무더운 한낮에 지친 일꾼들의 손에서 아무렇게나 내던져진 것 같았다.\n\n이 보기 흉한 어수선함을, 경건한 프시케는 모든 것을 제 자리와 제 종류대로 갈라 정리함으로써 끝맺었으니, 그녀는 어떤 신도 소홀히 해서는 안 되며 오히려 자신의 경건함으로 그들 모두가 자기 편이 되도록 애써야 한다고 믿은 까닭이었다. 그 신전의 주인인 거룩한 데메테르[로마의 〈케레스〉]는 그녀가 그토록 경건히 일하는 것을 보고 그녀에게 이렇게 말했다. \"오 프시케여, 참으로 우리의 연민을 받을 만하도다. 비록 내가 너를 아프로디테의 노여움으로부터 막아 줄 수는 없으나, 그녀의 노여움을 가장 잘 누그러뜨릴 방도는 가르쳐 줄 수 있노라. 그러니 가서, 너의 마님이자 주인이신 분께 스스로 너를 내맡기고, 겸손과 순종으로 그녀의 용서를 얻으려 애써 보아라. 그리하면 어쩌면 그녀의 은혜가 네게 잃어버린 남편을 되돌려 줄지도 모른다.\"\n\n프시케는 데메테르의 분부를 따라 아프로디테의 신전으로 향했으니, 그녀는 마음을 굳건히 하려 애쓰면서 무슨 말을 해야 할지, 또 노여워하는 여신을 어떻게 하면 가장 잘 달랠 수 있을지 곰곰이 생각했으나, 그 결말이 미덥지 않고 어쩌면 치명적일 수도 있음을 느꼈다.\n\n아프로디테는 노여운 낯빛으로 그녀를 맞았다. \"가장 본분을 저버리고 가장 불충한 종아,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"너는 마침내 네게 정말로 주인이 있다는 것을 기억하게 되었느냐? 아니면 차라리 사랑하는 아내가 입힌 상처로 아직 자리보전하고 있는 네 병든 남편을 보러 온 것이냐? 너는 어찌나 박색이고 못마땅한지, 네 연인에게 어울리는 자가 될 길이라곤 오로지 근면과 부지런함뿐이로다. 네 살림 솜씨를 시험해 보겠노라.\" 그러고는 자기 신전의 곳간으로 프시케를 데려가게 했으니, 그곳에는 그녀의 비둘기들을 먹이려고 마련해 둔 밀, 보리, 기장, 살갈퀴, 콩, 렌즈콩이 엄청나게 쌓여 있었으며, 그녀는 말했다. \"이 곡식을 모조리 갈라, 같은 종류끼리 따로따로 한데 모아 두되, 저녁이 되기 전에 일을 마치도록 하여라.\" 그러고 나서 아프로디테는 떠나며 그녀에게 그 일을 맡겨 두었다.\n\n그러나 프시케는 그 엄청난 일에 완전히 넋이 나가, 그 풀 길 없는 더미에 손가락 하나 까딱하지 못한 채 멍하니 말없이 앉아 있었다.\n\n그녀가 절망하여 앉아 있는 동안, 에로스는 들판의 토박이인 작은 개미를 부추겨 그녀를 가엾이 여기게 했다. 개미집의 우두머리가 여섯 다리 달린 신하들의 온 무리를 거느리고 그 더미에 다가와, 더없는 부지런함으로 알갱이 하나하나를 집어 그 무더기를 갈라, 종류마다 제 묶음으로 골라냈다. 그리고 그 일이 모두 끝나자, 그들은 순식간에 시야에서 사라졌다.\n\n아프로디테는 황혼 무렵 신들의 잔치에서 돌아왔으니, 향기를 내뿜으며 장미로 관을 쓰고 있었다. 그녀는 일이 끝난 것을 보고 외쳤다. \"이것은 네 일솜씨가 아니라, 사악한 것아, 그자의 솜씨로구나. 너 자신과 그의 불행을 초래하면서까지 네가 꾀어낸 그자 말이다.\" 이렇게 말하고 그녀는 저녁거리로 검은 빵 한 조각을 그녀에게 던져 주고 가 버렸다.\n\n이튿날 아침 아프로디테는 프시케를 불러오게 하여 그녀에게 말했다. \"저 물가를 따라 펼쳐진 숲을 보아라. 그곳에 가면 목자도 없이 풀을 뜯는 양들이 있을 터인데, 그 등에는 황금빛으로 빛나는 양털이 덮여 있느니라. 가서, 그 양들 하나하나의 양털에서 거둔 그 진귀한 털의 표본을 내게 가져오너라.\"\n\n프시케는 순순히 강가로 가, 그 분부를 이행하려고 최선을 다할 채비를 했다. 그러나 강의 신이 갈대들에게 조화로운 속삭임을 불어넣었으니, 그 소리는 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. \"오 가혹한 시련을 겪는 처녀여, 위험한 물결을 함부로 건드리지 말고, 저편에 있는 무시무시한 숫양들 사이로 무모하게 들어가지 말지어다. 저들이 떠오르는 해의 기운 아래 있는 동안은 날카로운 뿔이나 거친 이빨로 필멸자들을 죽이려는 잔혹한 분노로 타오르는 까닭이다. 그러나 한낮의 해가 그 짐승들을 그늘로 몰아넣고, 잔잔한 물결의 평온한 정기가 그들을 잠재워 쉬게 하거든, 그때는 안전하게 건널 수 있으리니, 그대는 그 황금빛 양털이 덤불과 나무줄기에 들러붙어 있는 것을 보게 되리라.\"\n\n이렇게 자비로운 강의 신은 프시케에게 어떻게 그 일을 이룰지 일러 주었고, 그녀는 그 지시를 따름으로써 곧 두 팔 가득 황금 양털을 안고 아프로디테에게로 돌아왔다. 그러나 그녀는 그 무자비한 마님의 칭찬을 받지 못했으니, 마님은 이렇게 말했다. \"이번 일에서 네가 성공한 것이 네 솜씨에서 비롯된 일이 결코 아님을 나는 익히 안다. 또한 나는 네게 너 자신을 쓸모 있게 만들 능력이 있다고 아직 만족하지 못하겠다. 하지만 네게 또 다른 일이 있다. 자, 이 상자를 받아 저승의 그늘로 가, 이 상자를 페르세포네[로마의 〈프로세르피나〉]에게 건네며 이렇게 말하여라. '제 마님 아프로디테께서 당신의 아름다움을 조금 보내 주시기를 바라십니다. 병든 아드님을 돌보시느라 당신 것을 얼마간 잃으셨거든요.' 네 심부름을 너무 오래 끌지 말지니, 오늘 저녁 신들과 여신들의 모임에 나가려면 내가 그것으로 단장을 해야 하느니라.\"\n\n프시케는 이제 자신의 파멸이 눈앞에 닥쳤음을 확신했으니, 제 발로 곧장 에레보스[저승]로 내려가야만 했던 까닭이다. 그리하여 피할 수 없는 일을 지체하지 않으려고, 그녀는 거꾸로 몸을 던져 저 아래 그늘로 가장 빠른 길로 내려가려고 높은 탑 꼭대기로 올라갔다. 그러나 탑에서 어떤 목소리가 그녀에게 말했다. \"어찌하여, 가엾고 불운한 처녀여, 그대는 그토록 끔찍한 방식으로 그대의 목숨을 끝내려 하는가? 또한 그대는 지난 모든 위기에서 그토록 기적적으로 도움을 받았으면서, 어떤 비겁함으로 이 마지막 위험 앞에 무너지려 하는가?\" 그러고 나서 그 목소리는 그녀가 어느 동굴을 통해 하데스[로마의 〈플루톤〉]의 영역에 이를 수 있는 방법과, 길의 온갖 위험을 피하는 방법, 머리 셋 달린 개 케르베로스 곁을 지나는 방법, 그리고 뱃사공 카론을 설득해 검은 강을 건너게 하고 다시 데려오게 하는 방법을 일러 주었다. 그러나 그 목소리는 덧붙였다. \"페르세포네가 자신의 아름다움을 채운 상자를 그대에게 건네거든, 무엇보다 그대가 명심해야 할 것은, 그 상자를 결코 한 번도 열거나 들여다보지 말 것이며, 여신들의 아름다움이라는 그 보물을 그대의 호기심이 엿보도록 결코 허락하지 말라는 것이다.\"\n\n프시케는 이 충고에 힘을 얻어 모든 일에서 그것을 따랐고, 제 갈 길을 조심하여 무사히 하데스의 왕국에 다다랐다. 그녀는 페르세포네의 궁전에 받아들여졌고, 그녀에게 권해진 안락한 자리도 진미의 잔치도 받지 않은 채, 다만 거친 빵으로 끼니를 때우는 것으로 만족하며, 아프로디테의 전갈을 전했다. 이내 그 상자가 닫힌 채 그 진귀한 물건으로 채워져 그녀에게 되돌려졌다. 그러고서 그녀는 왔던 길로 되돌아갔으니, 다시 한 번 대낮의 빛 속으로 나오게 되어 기뻤다.\n\n그러나 위험한 일을 거기까지 무사히 해낸 마당에, 그 상자의 내용물을 살펴보고 싶은 간절한 욕망이 그녀를 사로잡았다. \"어째서,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이 신성한 아름다움을 나르는 내가, 사랑하는 내 남편의 눈에 더 돋보이도록 내 뺨에 바를 만큼 조금이라도 가져서는 안 된단 말인가!\" 그리하여 그녀는 조심스레 상자를 열었으나, 거기에는 아름다움이라곤 도무지 들어 있지 않고, 다만 저승의, 참으로 스틱스 강 같은 잠이 들어 있어, 그것이 이렇게 감옥에서 풀려나 그녀를 사로잡았고, 그녀는 길 한가운데에 쓰러져, 감각도 움직임도 없는 잠든 시체가 되고 말았다.\n\n그러나 이제 상처에서 회복된 에로스는 사랑하는 프시케와 떨어져 있는 것을 더는 견딜 수 없어, 마침 열려 있던 자기 침실 창문의 가장 작은 틈으로 빠져나와 프시케가 누워 있는 곳으로 날아갔고, 그녀의 몸에서 잠을 거두어 다시 상자에 가두고는, 자기 화살 하나로 가볍게 건드려 프시케를 깨웠다. \"또다시,\" 그가 말했다. \"그대는 그 똑같은 호기심으로 하마터면 목숨을 잃을 뻔했구려. 하지만 이제 내 어머니께서 그대에게 맡기신 일을 정확히 이행하시오. 나머지는 내가 알아서 처리하리다.\"\n\n그러고 나서 에로스는 번개처럼 빠르게 하늘의 높은 곳을 꿰뚫고 제우스 앞에 나아가 자신의 탄원을 올렸다. 제우스는 호의로운 귀를 기울였고, 두 연인의 사정을 아프로디테에게 어찌나 간곡히 변호했던지 그녀의 동의를 얻어 냈다. 이에 그는 헤르메스를 보내 프시케를 천상의 회합으로 데려오게 했고, 그녀가 도착하자 신들의 음식 암브로시아 한 잔을 건네며 말했다. \"이것을 마셔라, 프시케여, 그리고 불멸의 존재가 되어라. 에로스는 자기가 묶인 그 매듭에서 결코 빠져나가지 못할 것이며, 이 혼인은 영원할 것이니라.\"\n\n이리하여 프시케는 마침내 에로스와 맺어졌고, 때가 되어 그들에게 딸 하나가 태어났으니 그 이름은 〈환희〉였다.\n\n에로스와 프시케의 우화는 흔히 우의적인 것으로 여겨진다. 나비를 가리키는 그리스어 이름이 프시케이며, 같은 낱말이 영혼을 뜻하기도 한다. 영혼의 불멸을 나비만큼 인상적이고 아름답게 보여 주는 예는 없으니, 나비는 둔하고 비천하게 기는 애벌레로 살다가, 그것이 누워 있던 무덤에서 찬란한 날개를 펼치며 솟아나, 대낮의 눈부신 빛 속에서 날갯짓하며 봄의 가장 향기롭고 가녀린 산물을 먹는 까닭이다. 그러므로 프시케는 인간의 영혼이니, 고난과 불행으로 정화되어, 그리하여 참되고 순수한 행복을 누릴 채비를 갖추게 되는 것이다.\n\n미술 작품에서 프시케는 나비의 날개를 단 처녀로, 에로스와 함께 그 우의에 묘사된 갖가지 상황 속에 표현된다.\n\n밀턴은 자신의 「코머스」 결말부에서 에로스와 프시케의 이야기를 암시한다.\n\n\"천상의 에로스, 그녀의 이름난 아들이 나아와, 오랜 방랑의 노고를 마친 뒤 감미로이 황홀경에 든 사랑하는 프시케를 품에 안으니, 이윽고 신들 사이의 거리낌 없는 동의가 그녀를 그의 영원한 신부로 삼는도다. 그리고 그녀의 곱고 티 없는 옆구리에서 축복받은 쌍둥이가 태어나리니, 〈청춘〉과 〈환희〉라, 제우스께서 그리 맹세하셨도다.\"\n\n에로스와 프시케 이야기의 우의는 T. K. 하비의 아름다운 시구에 잘 드러나 있다.\n\n\"그들은 옛 시절에 빛나는 우화를 지어냈으니, 이성이 공상의 채색된 날개를 빌려 오던 때요, 진리의 맑은 강물이 황금 모래 위로 흐르며, 그 높고 신비로운 것들을 노래로 들려주던 때라! 그리고 그러한 것이 그녀의 감미롭고 엄숙한 이야기니, 한 꿈을 받은 순례하는 마음이여. 그 꿈이 그녀를 세상 너머로 이끌었으니—사랑의 숭배자라— 그 집이 하늘에 있는 이를 땅에서 찾으려 함이라!\n\n\"북적이는 도시에서—유령 깃든 샘가에서— 어슴푸레한 동굴의 결정 무늬 사이로— 소나무 신전 가운데, 달빛 어린 산 위에서, 거기 침묵이 앉아 별들에 귀 기울이는 곳에서, 품은 비둘기가 깃든 깊은 숲속 빈터에서, 채색된 골짜기와 향기로운 대기 속에서, 그녀는 사랑의 목소리의 먼 메아리를 들었고, 어디서나 그의 발자취를 찾아내었네.\n\n\"그러나 그들은 다시는 만나지 못했네! 의심과 두려움, 대지를 떠돌며 시들게 하는 저 허깨비의 형상들이 죄와 눈물의 자식인 그녀와, 불멸의 태생인 저 빛나는 정령 사이에 끼어든 까닭이라. 마침내 그녀의 애타는 영혼과 우는 두 눈이 오직 하늘에서만 그를 찾기를 배우게 되었고, 지친 마음에 날개가 주어져, 그녀는 하늘에서 사랑의 천사 신부가 되었네!\"\n\n에로스와 프시케 이야기는 우리 시대 2세기의 작가인 아풀레이우스의 작품에 처음 나타난다. 따라서 그것은 신화의 시대의 전설 대부분보다 훨씬 더 나중에 생겨난 것이다. 키츠가 자신의 「프시케에게 바치는 송가」에서 암시하는 것이 바로 이것이다.\n\n\"오 올림포스의 빛바랜 모든 신들의 위계 가운데 가장 늦게 태어난, 그리고 단연 가장 사랑스러운 환영이여! 사파이어빛 영역을 거느린 포이베의 별보다도, 하늘의 정겨운 반딧불 베스페르보다도 곱구나. 이 모든 것보다 곱건만, 그대에게는 신전이 없고, 꽃을 수북이 쌓은 제단도 없으며, 한밤의 시각에 감미로운 탄식을 자아낼 처녀들의 합창도 없구나. 목소리도, 류트도, 피리도, 사슬에 매달린 향로에서 피어오르는 감미로운 향연(香煙)도 없고, 사당도, 숲도, 신탁도, 창백한 입술로 꿈꾸는 예언자의 열기도 없구나.\"\n\n무어의 「여름 축제」에는 가장무도회가 묘사되는데, 거기서 분장한 인물 가운데 하나가 프시케다—\n\n\". . . 오늘 밤 우리의 젊은 여주인공은 어두운 변장으로 제 빛을 가리지 않았네— 보라, 그녀는 땅 위를 거니니, 사랑이 자기 것으로 삼은 이라. 더없이 거룩한 맹세로 올림포스에서 약속된 그의 혼인한 신부요, 이제 그녀의 눈처럼 흰 이마 위에 반짝이며 걸린 그 표지로써 필멸자들에게 알려진 이라. 저 나비, 신비로운 장신구는 영혼을 뜻하니, (그리 여길 이 드물겠으나,) 그토록 흰 이마 위에 이렇게 반짝이며 오늘 밤 여기 프시케가 있음을 우리에게 일러 주네.\"\n\n————\n\n# CADMUS—THE MYRMIDONS\n\n제우스가 황소로 변장하여 페니키아의 왕 아게노르의 딸 에우로페를 납치해 갔다. 아게노르는 아들 카드모스에게 누이를 찾아 나서되 누이 없이는 돌아오지 말라고 명령했다. 카드모스는 길을 떠나 멀리까지 오래도록 누이를 찾았으나 끝내 찾지 못했고, 성공하지 못한 채 돌아갈 엄두를 내지 못해 자신이 어느 나라에 정착해야 할지 알아보려고 아폴론의 신탁을 청했다. 신탁은 그가 들판에서 암소 한 마리를 발견할 것이며, 그 소가 어디로 떠돌든 따라가다가 소가 멈추는 곳에 도시를 세우고 그곳을 테바이라고 부르라고 일러 주었다. 카드모스가 신탁이 내려진 카스탈리아의 동굴을 막 떠나자마자, 어린 암소 한 마리가 그의 앞을 천천히 걸어가는 것이 보였다. 그는 그 소를 바짝 뒤따르면서 동시에 포이보스에게 기도를 올렸다. 소는 케피소스의 얕은 물길을 건너 파노페 평원으로 나올 때까지 계속 걸어갔다. 그곳에서 소는 가만히 멈추어 서더니, 넓은 이마를 하늘로 쳐들고 울음소리로 공기를 가득 채웠다. 카드모스는 감사를 올리고, 몸을 굽혀 낯선 땅에 입을 맞춘 뒤, 눈을 들어 주위를 둘러싼 산들에게 인사했다. 그는 제우스에게 제물을 바치고자 하인들을 보내 헌주에 쓸 맑은 물을 구해 오게 했다. 가까이에는 도끼날에 한 번도 더럽혀진 적이 없는 오래된 숲이 있었는데, 그 한가운데에는 덤불이 무성하게 뒤덮인 동굴이 있어 그 지붕은 낮은 아치를 이루었고, 그 아래에서 더없이 맑은 물이 솟아 나오는 샘이 있었다. 그 동굴 속에는 볏 달린 머리에 황금처럼 반짝이는 비늘을 가진 무시무시한 뱀 한 마리가 숨어 있었다. 그 눈은 불처럼 빛났고, 몸은 독으로 부풀어 있었으며, 세 갈래로 갈라진 혀를 날름거렸고, 세 줄로 늘어선 이빨을 드러내고 있었다. 티로스 사람들이 샘에 물항아리를 담그자마자, 그리고 물이 콸콸 흘러 들어오는 소리가 나자마자, 반짝이는 뱀이 동굴 밖으로 머리를 쳐들고 끔찍한 쉭쉭 소리를 냈다. 그들의 손에서 그릇이 떨어졌고, 뺨에서는 핏기가 가셨으며, 온몸의 사지가 떨렸다. 뱀은 비늘 덮인 몸을 거대한 똬리로 비틀어 가장 키 큰 나무들보다도 높이 머리를 쳐들었고, 티로스 사람들이 공포에 질려 싸우지도 달아나지도 못하는 사이에, 어떤 이들은 송곳니로, 어떤 이들은 똬리로 휘감아서, 또 어떤 이들은 독기 어린 숨결로 죽여 버렸다.\n\n카드모스는 한낮이 되도록 부하들이 돌아오기를 기다리다가 그들을 찾아 나섰다. 그가 걸친 것은 사자 가죽이었고, 투창 외에도 손에는 긴 창을 들었으며, 가슴에는 그 어느 무기보다 더 든든하게 의지할 수 있는 담대한 심장을 품고 있었다. 그가 숲에 들어서서 부하들의 생기 없는 주검들과 피투성이 아가리를 한 괴물을 보았을 때, 그는 이렇게 외쳤다. \"오, 충실한 벗들이여, 내가 너희의 원수를 갚거나, 아니면 너희와 죽음을 함께하리라.\" 이렇게 말하고는 거대한 돌을 들어 온 힘을 다해 뱀에게 던졌다. 그만한 돌덩이라면 요새의 성벽이라도 흔들었으련만, 괴물에게는 아무런 흔적도 남기지 못했다. 카드모스는 다음으로 투창을 던졌고, 이번에는 더 나은 성공을 거두어 창이 뱀의 비늘을 뚫고 들어가 내장까지 꿰뚫었다. 고통으로 사나워진 괴물은 머리를 돌려 상처를 살펴보고는 입으로 무기를 뽑아내려 했으나, 창대를 부러뜨려 쇠 촉을 살 속에 박힌 채로 남겨 두고 말았다. 분노로 목이 부풀어 올랐고, 피 묻은 거품이 아가리를 뒤덮었으며, 콧구멍에서 나오는 숨결이 주변의 공기를 독으로 물들였다. 이제 괴물은 몸을 둥글게 똬리 틀었다가, 다시 쓰러진 나무 둥치처럼 땅바닥에 길게 뻗었다. 괴물이 앞으로 다가오자 카드모스는 그 앞에서 물러서면서 괴물의 벌어진 아가리를 향해 창을 겨누었다. 뱀은 무기를 덥석 물고 쇠 촉을 깨물려 했다. 마침내 카드모스는 기회를 노리다가, 짐승이 뒤로 젖힌 머리가 나무 둥치에 부딪치는 순간 창을 찔러 넣어, 마침내 괴물을 나무 옆구리에 못 박는 데 성공했다. 괴물이 죽음의 고통 속에서 몸부림치자 그 무게에 나무가 휘었다.\n\n카드모스가 자신이 쓰러뜨린 적 위에 서서 그 어마어마한 크기를 바라보고 있을 때, 어디서 들려오는지는 알 수 없으나 분명하게 들리는 한 목소리가 그에게 그 용의 이빨을 뽑아 땅에 뿌리라고 명령했다. 그는 그 명에 따랐다. 그는 땅에 고랑을 내고, 사람이라는 작물을 자라게 할 운명을 지닌 그 이빨들을 심었다. 그가 그렇게 하자마자 흙덩이가 움직이기 시작하더니, 창끝이 땅 위로 모습을 드러냈다. 이어서 깃털 장식이 흔들리는 투구들이 솟아올랐고, 다음으로는 무기를 든 사람들의 어깨와 가슴과 사지가, 그리고 이윽고 무장한 전사들의 한 무리 수확물이 솟아났다. 깜짝 놀란 카드모스는 새로운 적과 맞설 채비를 했으나, 그들 가운데 하나가 그에게 말했다. \"우리 내전에 끼어들지 마시오.\" 그렇게 말한 자는 땅에서 태어난 형제 하나를 칼로 내리쳤고, 자신은 또 다른 자가 쏜 화살에 꿰뚫려 쓰러졌다. 화살을 쏜 자는 네 번째 자에게 희생되었고, 이런 식으로 무리 전체가 서로를 베어 가다가, 다섯 명의 생존자를 제외하고는 모두 서로의 상처에 쓰러져 죽었다. 그중 하나가 무기를 내던지며 말했다. \"형제들이여, 우리 평화롭게 삽시다!\" 이 다섯 명은 카드모스와 합류하여 그의 도시를 세웠고, 그 도시에 테바이라는 이름을 붙였다.\n\n카드모스는 아프로디테[로마의 〈베누스〉]의 딸 하르모니아를 아내로 맞이했다. 신들은 그 경사를 함께하여 기리고자 올림포스를 떠나 왔고, 헤파이스토스[로마의 〈불카누스〉]는 자신이 직접 만든, 더없이 눈부신 목걸이를 신부에게 선물했다. 그러나 카드모스가 아레스[로마의 〈마르스〉]에게 바쳐진 뱀을 죽인 탓에, 그의 가문에는 불운이 드리워져 있었다. 그의 딸들인 세멜레와 이노, 그의 외손자들인 악타이온과 펜테우스가 모두 불행하게 죽었고, 카드모스와 하르모니아는 이제 자신들에게 끔찍해진 테바이를 떠나 엔켈리스인들의 나라로 이주했는데, 그들은 두 사람을 정중히 맞이하여 카드모스를 자신들의 왕으로 삼았다. 그러나 자식들의 불행은 여전히 두 사람의 마음을 짓눌렀다. 그러던 어느 날 카드모스가 외쳤다. \"뱀의 목숨이 신들에게 그토록 소중하다면, 차라리 내가 직접 뱀이 되었으면 좋겠구나.\" 그가 이 말을 내뱉기가 무섭게 그의 몸이 변하기 시작했다. 하르모니아가 그것을 보고는 자기도 남편의 운명을 함께하게 해 달라고 신들에게 기도했다. 두 사람은 모두 뱀이 되었다. 그들은 숲속에 살지만, 자신들의 본디 모습을 잊지 않고 있어, 사람의 곁을 피하지도 않으며 누구도 해치지 않는다.\n\n카드모스가 페니키아인들이 발명한 알파벳 글자를 그리스에 들여왔다는 전승이 있다. 바이런은 근대 그리스인들에게 이렇게 말하면서 이 일을 넌지시 언급한다.\n\n\"그대들은 카드모스가 준 글자를 가지고 있으니, 그가 그것을 노예를 위해 마련했다고 생각하는가?\"\n\n밀턴은 이브를 유혹한 뱀을 묘사하면서 고전 이야기 속의 뱀들을 떠올리고는 이렇게 말한다.\n\n……\"그 모습은 보기 좋고 사랑스러웠으니, 그 후로 뱀의 종족 가운데 이보다 더 사랑스러운 것은 없었다. 일리리아에서 헤르미오네와 카드모스를 변신시킨 뱀들도, 에피다우로스의 그 신도 이만 못했다.\"\n\n이 마지막 인유에 대한 설명은 아스클레피오스[로마의 〈아이스쿨라피우스〉]의 신탁, 298면을 보라.\n\n# THE MYRMIDONS\n\n미르미돈족\n미르미돈족은 트로이아 전쟁에서 아킬레우스의 병사들이었다. 그들에게서 비롯하여, 어떤 정치 지도자의 열성적이고 양심을 가리지 않는 추종자들은 오늘날까지도 그 이름으로 불린다. 그러나 미르미돈족의 기원은 사납고 피에 굶주린 종족이라는 인상보다는, 오히려 부지런하고 평화로운 종족이라는 인상을 떠올리게 한다.\n\n아테나이의 왕 케팔로스는 크레타의 왕 미노스와 벌이는 전쟁에서 오랜 벗이자 동맹인 아이아코스 왕에게 도움을 청하고자 아이기나섬에 도착했다. 케팔로스는 더없이 친절한 환대를 받았고, 그가 바라던 도움도 흔쾌히 약속받았다. \"나는 나 자신을 지키고도 당신에게 필요한 만큼의 병력을 내어 줄 수 있을 만큼 백성이 충분하오.\" 아이아코스가 말했다. \"그것을 보니 기쁘오.\" 케팔로스가 대답했다. \"고백하건대, 내 주위에 보이는 이토록 많은 젊은이들이 하나같이 비슷한 나이로 보이니 놀라울 따름이오. 그런데 내가 전에 알고 지내던 이들이 많건만, 지금은 아무리 찾아도 보이지 않는구려. 그들은 어찌 되었소?\" 아이아코스는 신음하더니 슬픔에 잠긴 목소리로 대답했다. \"당신에게 말하려고 하던 참이었으니, 이제 더 미루지 않고 이야기하겠소. 그리하여 당신은 더없이 슬픈 시작에서 때로 행복한 결말이 흘러나오는 법을 보게 될 것이오. 당신이 예전에 알던 이들은 이제 모두 먼지요 재가 되었소! 노한 헤라[로마의 〈유노〉]가 보낸 역병이 이 땅을 황폐하게 만들었소. 그분은 이 땅이 자기 남편이 총애하던 여인들 가운데 하나의 이름을 따왔다는 이유로 이 땅을 미워하셨소. 그 병이 자연적인 원인에서 비롯된 것처럼 보이는 동안에는 우리도 자연적인 치료법으로 최선을 다해 그것에 맞섰소. 그러나 곧 그 역병이 우리의 노력으로는 당해 낼 수 없을 만큼 강력하다는 것이 드러났고, 우리는 굴복하고 말았소. 처음에는 하늘이 땅 위로 내려앉는 듯하더니, 두꺼운 구름이 달아오른 공기를 가두었소. 넉 달 동안 내리 죽음의 남풍이 기승을 부렸소. 그 병은 우물과 샘을 오염시켰고, 수천 마리의 뱀이 땅 위를 기어 다니며 샘에 독을 풀었소. 그 병의 위력은 처음에는 하등 동물들에게 쏟아졌으니, 개와 소와 양과 새들이었소. 가엾은 농부는 자기 황소들이 일하던 도중에 쓰러져 끝내지 못한 고랑에 무력하게 누워 있는 것을 보고 어리둥절해했소. 매에 울던 양에게서는 털이 빠졌고, 그 몸뚱이는 야위어 갔소. 한때 경주에서 가장 앞서 달리던 말은 더 이상 우승을 다투지 못하고, 마구간에서 신음하다가 영광 없는 죽음을 맞았소. 멧돼지는 사나움을 잊었고, 사슴은 날쌤을 잊었으며, 곰은 더 이상 가축 떼를 덮치지 않았소. 모든 것이 시들어 갔으니, 주검들이 길과 들과 숲에 널브러져 있어 공기가 그것들로 오염되었소. 좀처럼 믿기 어려운 일을 말하건대, 개도 새도, 굶주린 늑대조차도 그 주검에 손대려 하지 않았소. 그것들이 썩어 가며 전염병을 퍼뜨렸소. 다음으로 그 병은 시골 사람들을 덮쳤고, 그다음에는 도시 거주민들을 덮쳤소. 처음에는 뺨이 벌겋게 달아오르고 숨쉬기가 힘들어졌소. 혀가 거칠어지고 부어올랐으며, 마른 입이 핏줄이 부풀어 오른 채 벌어져 공기를 들이켜려 헐떡였소. 사람들은 옷이나 잠자리의 열기를 견디지 못하고 차라리 맨땅에 눕기를 좋아했소. 그러나 땅이 그들을 식혀 주기는커녕, 도리어 그들이 누운 자리를 그들이 달구어 놓았소. 의사들도 도움이 되지 못했으니, 그 병이 의사들마저 덮쳐 환자와 접촉하면 그들에게도 전염되었기 때문이오. 그리하여 가장 충실한 자들이 가장 먼저 희생되었소. 마침내 회복에 대한 모든 희망이 사라졌고, 사람들은 죽음을 병에서 벗어나게 해 주는 유일한 구원자로 여기게 되었소. 그러자 그들은 온갖 욕망에 몸을 내맡겼고, 무엇이 이로운지 묻는 것조차 개의치 않았으니, 이로운 것이라곤 아무것도 없었기 때문이오. 모든 절제를 내던진 채 그들은 우물과 샘 주위에 몰려들어 죽을 때까지 물을 마셨으나 갈증을 가시게 하지는 못했소. 많은 이들이 물에서 빠져나올 기운조차 없어 물줄기 한가운데서 죽었고, 그래도 다른 이들은 그 물을 마셨소. 병상에 대한 진저리가 어찌나 심했던지, 어떤 이들은 기어 나오다가 설 만한 기운이 없으면 땅바닥에서 죽었소. 그들은 자기 벗들을 미워하는 듯했고, 마치 병의 원인을 알지 못한 채 그것을 자기 거처 탓으로 돌리기라도 하듯 집에서 빠져나갔소. 어떤 이들은 설 수 있는 동안에는 비틀거리며 길을 따라 걸어가는 모습이 보였고, 또 어떤 이들은 땅에 주저앉아 죽어 가는 눈으로 주위를 둘러보며 마지막 한 번을 바라본 뒤 죽음 속에서 그 눈을 감았소.\n\n\"이 모든 일을 겪는 동안 내게 무슨 심정이 남아 있었겠소, 또 죽은 내 백성들과 함께하기를 바라며 삶을 미워하는 것 말고 내게 어떤 심정이 있어야 했겠소? 사방에 내 백성들이 나무 아래 지나치게 익은 사과처럼, 혹은 폭풍에 흔들린 떡갈나무 아래 도토리처럼 흩어져 누워 있었소. 저기 높은 곳에 신전이 하나 보일 것이오. 그것은 제우스에게 바쳐진 것이오. 오, 얼마나 많은 이들이 그곳에서 기도를 올렸던가, 남편들은 아내를 위해, 아버지들은 아들을 위해, 그러다가 바로 그 탄원의 행위 가운데 죽어 갔소! 사제가 제사를 준비하는 동안에, 제물로 바칠 짐승이 일격을 기다리지도 않고 병에 쓰러진 일이 얼마나 잦았던가! 마침내 신성한 것들에 대한 온갖 경의도 사라지고 말았소. 주검들이 묻히지 못한 채 내던져졌고, 화장용 장작이 모자라 사람들이 그것을 차지하려고 서로 싸웠소. 끝내는 슬퍼해 줄 이마저 남지 않았으니, 아들과 남편, 노인과 젊은이가 한결같이 애도받지 못한 채 죽어 갔소.\n\n\"제단 앞에 서서 나는 하늘로 눈을 들었소. '오, 제우스시여,' 나는 말했소. '당신이 정녕 제 아버지이시고, 당신의 자식을 부끄러워하지 않으신다면, 제 백성을 돌려주시거나, 아니면 저마저 데려가 주소서!' 이 말이 끝나자 천둥소리가 한 번 울렸소. '이 징조를 받아들이겠나이다,' 나는 외쳤소. '오, 부디 이것이 제게 호의를 베푸시려는 표시이기를!' 마침 내가 서 있던 자리 곁에는 가지를 넓게 펼친 떡갈나무 한 그루가 자라고 있었는데, 제우스에게 바쳐진 것이었소. 나는 개미 떼가 부지런히 일하며 작은 알갱이를 입에 물고 나무 둥치를 따라 한 줄로 서로를 뒤따라 오르는 것을 보았소. 그 수에 감탄하며 나는 말했소. '오, 아버지시여, 제게 이만큼이나 많은 시민을 주시어, 텅 빈 제 도시를 다시 채워 주소서.' 나무가 흔들리더니, 바람 한 점 가지를 흔들지 않는데도 가지에서 바스락거리는 소리가 났소. 나는 온몸의 사지가 떨렸으나, 그래도 땅과 나무에 입을 맞추었소. 나는 내가 희망을 품고 있다는 것을 나 자신에게도 인정하려 하지 않았으나, 그래도 나는 희망을 품었소. 밤이 찾아오자, 근심에 짓눌린 내 몸을 잠이 사로잡았소. 꿈속에서 그 나무가 내 앞에 서 있었는데, 그 수많은 가지가 온통 살아 움직이는 생물들로 뒤덮여 있었소. 나무가 가지를 흔들어 그 부지런히 알갱이를 모으는 짐승들의 무리를 땅 위로 떨어뜨리는 듯했고, 그것들은 몸집이 불어나 점점 더 커지더니, 이윽고 똑바로 서서 남아도는 다리와 검은 빛깔을 벗어 버리고, 마침내 사람의 모습을 띠는 듯했소. 그때 나는 잠에서 깨어났고, 내 첫 충동은 달콤한 환영을 빼앗아 가고 그 자리에 아무런 실체도 주지 않은 신들을 꾸짖는 것이었소. 아직 신전 안에 있던 차에, 신전 밖에서 들려오는 수많은 목소리에 내 주의가 쏠렸소. 근래에 내 귀에는 낯선 소리였소. 내가 아직도 꿈을 꾸고 있는 것이 아닌가 생각하기 시작하던 차에, 내 아들 텔라몬이 신전 문을 활짝 열어젖히며 외쳤소. '아버지, 가까이 오셔서, 아버지의 희망마저 능가하는 것들을 보소서!' 나는 밖으로 나갔소. 꿈에서 보았던 것과 똑같은 사람들의 무리가 보였고, 그들은 똑같은 모양으로 줄지어 지나가고 있었소. 내가 놀라움과 기쁨에 잠겨 바라보는 동안 그들은 다가와 무릎을 꿇고 나를 자신들의 왕으로 맞이했소. 나는 제우스께 서원을 갚고, 텅 빈 도시를 이 새로 태어난 종족에게 나누어 주고, 들판을 그들 사이에 갈라 주는 일에 착수했소. 나는 그들이 솟아 나온 개미(미르멕스)를 따서 그들을 미르미돈족이라 불렀소. 당신은 이 사람들을 보았소. 그들의 기질은 예전 모습에서 지녔던 것을 그대로 닮았소. 그들은 부지런하고 근면한 종족이며, 얻는 데 열심이고 얻은 것을 굳게 지키오. 그들 가운데서 당신은 병력을 보충할 수 있을 것이오. 그들은 나이는 젊으나 마음은 담대하여 당신을 따라 전장으로 갈 것이오.\"\n\n이 역병 묘사는 그리스 역사가 투키디데스가 아테나이의 역병에 관해 전하는 기록에서 오비디우스가 베껴 온 것이다. 그 역사가는 실제 삶에서 끌어왔고, 그 이후의 모든 시인과 소설가들은 비슷한 장면을 묘사할 일이 있을 때면 그에게서 세부를 빌려 왔다.\n\n————\n\n# NISUS AND SCYLLA\n\n크레타의 왕 미노스가 메가라를 상대로 전쟁을 일으켰다. 니소스는 메가라의 왕이었고, 스킬라는 그의 딸이었다. 공성전은 이제 여섯 달째에 접어들었으나 도시는 여전히 버티고 있었으니, 이는 니소스 왕의 머리카락 사이에서 반짝이는 어떤 자줏빛 머리타래가 그의 머리에 붙어 있는 한 도시는 함락되지 않으리라는 운명의 정해진 바였기 때문이다. 도시의 성벽에는 망루가 하나 있었는데, 그곳에서는 미노스와 그의 군대가 진을 친 평원이 내려다보였다. 스킬라는 자주 이 망루로 올라가 적군의 천막들을 두루 살펴보곤 했다. 공성전이 워낙 오래 이어졌던 까닭에 그녀는 적장들의 모습을 분간할 수 있을 정도가 되었다. 그중에서도 미노스가 유독 그녀의 흠모를 자아냈다. 투구를 쓰고 방패를 든 그의 우아한 거동을 그녀는 찬탄했다. 그가 투창을 던지면 그 동작에는 힘과 더불어 솜씨가 어우러진 듯했고, 그가 활을 당기면 아폴론 자신이라 한들 그보다 더 우아하게 해내지는 못했을 것이다. 그러나 그가 투구를 벗어 놓고, 자줏빛 의복을 걸친 채 화려한 마구를 두른 백마에 올라타 거품 무는 입에 고삐를 죄었을 때, 니소스의 딸은 자기 자신을 거의 가눌 수가 없었으니, 흠모에 겨워 거의 정신이 나간 지경이었다. 그녀는 그가 움켜쥔 무기를, 그가 잡은 고삐를 부러워했다. 그녀는 가능하기만 하다면 적진을 가로질러 그에게로 갈 수 있을 것만 같은 심정이었다. 그녀는 망루에서 그의 진영 한가운데로 몸을 던지거나, 그에게 성문을 열어 주거나, 그 밖에 무슨 일이든 오로지 미노스를 기쁘게 할 수만 있다면 해 버리고 싶은 충동에 사로잡혔다. 망루에 앉아 그녀는 이렇게 혼잣말을 했다. \"이 슬픈 전쟁을 두고 기뻐해야 할지 슬퍼해야 할지 모르겠구나. 미노스가 우리의 적이라는 사실은 슬프지만, 그를 내 눈앞에 데려다주는 어떤 까닭에 대해서는 기쁘다. 어쩌면 그는 기꺼이 우리에게 평화를 베풀고, 나를 인질로 받아 줄지도 모른다. 할 수만 있다면 나는 날아 내려가 그의 진영에 내려앉아, 우리가 그의 자비에 우리 자신을 맡긴다고 그에게 말하고 싶구나. 하지만 그렇게 되면 내 아버지를 배반하는 것이 아닌가! 안 된다! 차라리 다시는 미노스를 보지 않으리라. 그러나 정복자가 너그럽고 관대하다면, 도시가 정복당하는 편이 때로는 최선일 수도 있음은 의심의 여지가 없다. 미노스는 분명 정의를 자기편에 두고 있다. 내 생각에 우리는 정복당하고 말 것이다. 그리고 만일 그것이 끝이어야 한다면, 어찌하여 전쟁이 그 일을 하도록 내버려 두는 대신, 사랑이 그에게 성문의 빗장을 풀어 주지 않겠는가? 할 수만 있다면 지연과 살육을 면하는 편이 낫다. 아, 누군가 미노스를 상하게 하거나 죽이기라도 한다면! 분명 그럴 마음을 먹을 자는 아무도 없으리라. 그러나 그를 알아보지 못한 채 무심코 그리할 수도 있겠지. 나는, 나는 내 조국을 지참금으로 삼아 그에게 나 자신을 바치고, 그리하여 이 전쟁을 끝내리라. 그러나 어떻게? 성문은 지켜지고 있고, 열쇠는 내 아버지가 쥐고 있다. 오직 그분만이 내 앞을 막고 있구나. 아, 신들께서 그분을 데려가 주신다면 좋으련만! 그러나 어찌하여 신들께 그 일을 청하겠는가? 나처럼 사랑에 빠진 다른 여인이라면, 제 사랑을 가로막는 것이 무엇이든 제 손으로 치워 버렸으리라. 그리고 어떤 다른 여인이 감히 나보다 더한 일을 할 수 있겠는가? 나는 내 목적을 이루기 위해서라면 불과 칼이라도 무릅쓰리라. 그러나 여기에는 불도 칼도 필요 없다. 내게 필요한 것은 오직 내 아버지의 자줏빛 머리타래뿐이다. 내게는 황금보다도 더 귀한 그것이, 내가 바라는 모든 것을 가져다주리라.\"\n\n이렇게 궁리하는 동안 밤이 찾아왔고, 이윽고 온 궁궐이 잠 속에 묻혔다. 그녀는 아버지의 침실로 들어가 그 운명의 머리타래를 잘라 냈다. 그러고는 도시를 빠져나와 적진으로 들어갔다. 그녀는 왕에게로 안내해 줄 것을 요구하고, 그에게 이렇게 말했다. \"저는 니소스의 딸 스킬라입니다. 저는 제 조국과 제 아버지의 집을 당신께 바칩니다. 저는 당신 자신 외에 어떤 보답도 바라지 않습니다. 당신을 사랑하는 마음에서 이 일을 한 것입니다. 여기 이 자줏빛 머리타래를 보십시오! 이것과 더불어 저는 당신께 제 아버지와 그의 왕국을 드립니다.\" 그녀는 그 운명의 전리품을 손에 내밀었다. 미노스는 뒷걸음질하며 그것에 손대기를 거부했다. \"신들께서 너를 멸하시기를, 이 파렴치한 여인아.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"우리 시대의 치욕이로다! 땅도 바다도 네게 쉴 자리를 내주지 않기를! 분명, 제우스 자신이 요람에 누였던 나의 크레타가 그런 괴물로 더럽혀져서는 안 될 것이다!\" 이렇게 말하고서 그는 정복당한 도시에 공정한 조건을 허락하라고, 또한 함대가 즉시 그 섬을 떠나 출항하라고 명령을 내렸다.\n\n스킬라는 미친 듯이 부르짖었다. \"은혜를 모르는 사내여,\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"이렇게 나를 버리고 가는가? 그대에게 승리를 안겨 준 나를, 그대를 위해 어버이와 조국을 희생한 나를! 나는 죄를 지었으며, 그것을 인정하고, 죽어 마땅하지만, 그대의 손에 의해서는 아니다.\" 배들이 해안을 떠나가자, 그녀는 물속으로 뛰어들어 미노스를 태운 배의 키를 붙들었고, 그리하여 반갑지 않은 동행자가 되어 그들의 항로를 따라 끌려갔다. 높이 솟구쳐 날던 바다수리 한 마리가, 그것은 그 모습으로 변한 그녀의 아버지였는데, 그녀를 보고는 덮쳐 내려와 부리와 발톱으로 그녀를 후려쳤다. 두려움에 사로잡힌 그녀는 배를 놓치고 물속으로 떨어질 뻔했으나, 가엾이 여긴 어떤 신이 그녀를 새로 바꾸어 놓았다. 바다수리는 여전히 옛 원한을 품고 있으니, 높이 날아오르다가 그녀를 발견할 때마다, 그가 부리와 발톱으로 그녀를 향해 내리꽂으며 그 오래된 죄에 복수하려는 광경을 그대는 볼 수 있을 것이다.\n\n# 제21장\n\n에코는 숲과 언덕을 좋아하는 아름다운 님프였으며, 그곳에서 숲속의 놀이에 몰두했다. 그녀는 아르테미스[로마의 〈디아나〉]의 총애를 받았고, 사냥에서 여신을 수행했다. 그러나 에코에게는 한 가지 결점이 있었으니, 그녀는 말하기를 좋아하여, 한담을 나누든 논쟁을 벌이든 마지막 말을 차지하려 들었다. 어느 날 헤라[로마의 〈유노〉]가 남편을 찾고 있었는데, 그가 님프들 틈에서 즐기고 있으리라 의심할 만한 이유가 있었기 때문이다. 에코는 자신의 수다로 여신을 붙잡아 두는 데 성공하여, 그동안 님프들이 달아나도록 했다. 헤라가 이를 알아차렸을 때, 그녀는 에코에게 이런 말로 선고를 내렸다. \"너는 나를 속인 그 혀의 쓰임을 빼앗기리라. 다만 네가 그토록 좋아하는 그 한 가지 목적, 곧 대꾸하는 일에서만은 예외다. 너는 여전히 마지막 말을 차지하겠으나, 먼저 말할 능력은 없으리라.\"\n\n이 님프는 산속에서 사냥을 쫓던 아름다운 청년 나르키소스를 보았다. 그녀는 그를 사랑하여 그의 발자취를 뒤따랐다. 아, 가장 부드러운 어조로 그에게 말을 건네어 그를 대화로 이끌어 들이고 싶은 마음이 얼마나 간절했던가! 그러나 그것은 그녀의 능력 밖이었다. 그녀는 그가 먼저 말해 주기를 애타게 기다리며, 대답을 준비해 두었다. 어느 날 동료들과 떨어진 그 청년이 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"거기 누구 있느냐?\" 에코가 대답했다. \"있느냐.\" 나르키소스가 주위를 둘러보았으나 아무도 보이지 않자 다시 외쳤다. \"이리 오라.\" 에코가 답했다. \"오라.\" 아무도 오지 않자 나르키소스가 다시 불렀다. \"어찌하여 나를 피하느냐?\" 에코도 같은 물음을 던졌다. \"우리 함께 어울리자.\" 청년이 말했다. 처녀는 온 마음을 담아 같은 말로 대답하고는, 그의 목을 끌어안을 준비를 한 채 그 자리로 달려갔다. 그는 뒷걸음질하며 외쳤다. \"손을 떼라! 네가 나를 차지하느니 차라리 죽는 편이 낫겠다!\" \"나를 차지하라.\" 그녀가 말했다. 그러나 모두 헛일이었다. 그가 그녀를 떠나가자, 그녀는 붉어진 뺨을 숨기러 숲의 깊은 곳으로 들어갔다. 그때부터 그녀는 동굴 속과 산속 벼랑 사이에서 살았다. 그녀의 몸은 슬픔으로 시들어 갔고, 마침내 그 살이 모두 오그라들어 사라졌다. 그녀의 뼈는 바위로 바뀌었고, 그녀에게서 남은 것은 오직 목소리뿐이었다. 그 목소리로 그녀는 지금도 자기를 부르는 누구에게나 대답할 준비가 되어 있으며, 마지막 말을 차지하는 옛 버릇을 이어 가고 있다.\n\n이 일에서 나르키소스가 보인 매정함은 이것이 유일한 경우가 아니었다. 그는 가엾은 에코에게 그러했듯이 다른 모든 님프들도 멀리했다. 어느 날 그를 끌어들이려 헛되이 애쓰던 한 처녀가, 그도 언젠가는 사랑을 하되 그 애정에 아무런 보답도 받지 못하는 것이 무엇인지 느끼게 해 달라는 기도를 올렸다. 복수의 여신이 그 기도를 듣고 들어주었다.\n\n은빛 같은 물이 솟는 맑은 샘이 하나 있었으니, 목동들은 그곳으로 가축을 몰지 않았고, 산양들도 찾아들지 않았으며, 숲의 어떤 짐승도 오지 않았다. 또한 그곳은 떨어진 잎이나 가지로 더럽혀지지도 않았으니, 다만 그 둘레로는 풀이 싱그럽게 자랐고, 바위들이 햇볕을 가려 주었다. 어느 날 사냥에 지치고 몸이 달아오르고 목이 마른 그 청년이 이곳으로 왔다. 그는 물을 마시려 몸을 굽혔다가 물속에 비친 자신의 모습을 보았다. 그는 그것이 샘 속에 사는 어떤 아름다운 물의 정령이라고 생각했다. 그는 그 빛나는 눈을, 디오니소스[로마의 〈바쿠스〉]나 아폴론의 머리타래처럼 곱슬곱슬한 머리카락을, 둥근 뺨을, 상아 같은 목을, 살짝 벌어진 입술을, 그리고 그 모든 것에 어린 건강과 운동의 발그레한 빛을 흠모하며 바라보고 서 있었다. 그는 자기 자신과 사랑에 빠졌다. 그는 입을 맞추려 입술을 가까이 가져갔고, 사랑하는 대상을 끌어안으려 두 팔을 물속에 담갔다. 그것은 손길이 닿자 달아났으나, 잠시 후 다시 돌아와 그 매혹을 새로이 했다. 그는 스스로를 떼어 놓을 수가 없었다. 그는 자신의 모습을 바라보며 샘가에 머무는 동안 음식이나 휴식에 대한 모든 생각을 잃어버렸다. 그는 그 정령이라 여긴 것과 이야기를 나누었다. \"어찌하여, 아름다운 존재여, 그대는 나를 피하는가? 분명 내 얼굴은 그대를 물리칠 그런 얼굴이 아니다. 님프들은 나를 사랑하며, 그대 자신도 나를 무심하게 바라보지 않는다. 내가 두 팔을 내밀면 그대도 그리하고, 그대는 내게 미소 짓고 내 손짓에 같은 손짓으로 답한다.\" 그의 눈물이 물속으로 떨어져 그 모습을 흩뜨렸다. 그것이 사라지는 것을 보자 그가 외쳤다. \"머물러 다오, 그대에게 간청하노라! 그대를 만질 수 없을지언정, 적어도 그대를 바라볼 수 있게 해 다오.\" 이런 말과 함께 그와 같은 종류의 수많은 말로, 그는 자신을 갉아먹는 그 불꽃을 어루만졌으며, 그리하여 점차 그 안색과 활력을, 그리고 예전에 님프 에코를 그토록 매혹했던 아름다움을 잃어 갔다. 그러나 그녀는 여전히 그의 곁에 머물렀고, 그가 \"아아! 아아!\" 하고 외치면 그녀도 같은 말로 그에게 답했다. 그는 시들어 죽고 말았으니, 그의 혼령이 스틱스의 강을 건널 때 그것은 물속에 비친 제 모습을 보려고 배 위로 몸을 기울였다. 님프들이 그를 애도했으니, 특히 물의 님프들이 그러했다. 그리고 그들이 가슴을 칠 때 에코도 자기 가슴을 쳤다. 그들은 장례용 장작더미를 마련하여 그 시신을 태우려 했으나, 시신은 어디에서도 찾을 수 없었다. 그러나 그 자리에는 안쪽이 자줏빛이고 흰 잎으로 둘러싸인 꽃 한 송이가 피어 있었으니, 그 꽃은 나르키소스의 이름을 지니고 그의 기억을 간직하고 있다.\n\n밀턴은 「코머스」에 나오는 〈귀부인〉의 노래에서 에코와 나르키소스의 이야기를 넌지시 끌어들인다. 그녀는 숲속에서 오라비들을 찾으며, 그들의 주의를 끌기 위해 노래한다.\n\n\"고운 에코여, 가장 고운 님프여, 그대는 보이지 않게 살고 있구나 그대의 공기 같은 껍질 속에서 느릿한 마이안드로스의 푸른 강기슭에서, 그리고 제비꽃으로 수놓인 골짜기에서, 사랑에 버림받은 나이팅게일이 밤마다 그대에게 제 슬픈 노래를 곱게 애도하는 그곳에서. 그대는 내게 일러 주지 못하는가, 그대의 나르키소스를 가장 닮은 어느 다정한 한 쌍을? 아, 만일 그대가 그들을 어느 꽃 핀 동굴 속에 숨겨 두었거든, 다만 어디인지 일러 다오, 대화의 고운 여왕이여, 천구(天球)의 딸이여, 그리하면 그대는 하늘로 옮겨져 온 천상의 화음에 울려 퍼지는 우아함을 더하리라.\"\n\n밀턴은 이브가 샘에 비친 자기 모습을 처음 보았던 일을 이야기하게 하는 대목에서 나르키소스의 이야기를 본떴다.\n\n\"잠에서 처음 깨어나 꽃 위 그늘 아래 쉬고 있는 나 자신을 발견하고서, 내가 어디에 있는지, 무엇인지, 어디서 그리로 데려와졌는지, 또 어떻게 그리되었는지 무척이나 의아해하던 그날을 나는 자주 떠올린다. 그곳에서 멀지 않은 곳에서 물이 졸졸 흐르는 소리가 동굴에서 흘러나와 펼쳐지더니 잔잔한 평원을 이루고는, 이윽고 하늘의 넓은 폭처럼 맑게 미동도 없이 멈추어 있었다. 나는 그리로 갔으니 아직 무엇도 겪어 보지 못한 생각으로, 그 푸른 둔덕에 몸을 누이고서, 내게는 또 하나의 하늘처럼 보였던 그 맑고 매끄러운 호수를 들여다보았다. 들여다보려 몸을 굽히자, 바로 맞은편 물빛 어른거림 속에 한 형상이 나타나, 나를 보려 몸을 굽혔다. 나는 뒷걸음질했고, 그것도 뒷걸음질했다. 그러나 기뻐하며 곧 돌아갔더니, 그것도 그만큼 기뻐하며 공감과 사랑의 화답하는 눈길로 이내 돌아왔다. 그곳에 나는 지금까지 내 눈을 붙박고서, 헛된 갈망에 시들었으리라, 한 목소리가 이렇게 나를 일깨우지 않았더라면. '그대가 보는 것은, 그곳에 그대가 보는 것은, 아름다운 피조물이여, 그대 자신이로다.'\" 등등. — 「실낙원」 제4권.\n\n고대의 그 어떤 우화도 나르키소스의 이야기만큼 시인들에 의해 자주 인용된 것은 없다. 여기 그것을 서로 다른 방식으로 다룬 두 편의 경구시가 있다. 첫 번째 것은 골드스미스의 작품이다.\n\n\"벼락에 맞아 눈먼 한 아름다운 청년에 대하여\" \"분명 그것은 섭리가 마련한 것이었으니, 미움에서라기보다 차라리 가엾이 여김에서, 그가 쿠피도[에로스]처럼 눈멀게 된 것은, 나르키소스의 운명에서 그를 구하기 위함이었네.\"\n\n다른 하나는 카우퍼의 작품이다.\n\n\"한 못생긴 사내에 대하여\" \"벗이여, 수정 같은 시냇물이나 샘을 조심하게, 그 흉측한 갈고리, 곧 그대의 코를 우연히 보지 않도록. 그러면 나르키소스의 운명이 그대의 것이 되리니, 스스로에게 반한 그가 시들었듯이, 그대는 스스로를 혐오하며 시들어 가리라.\"\n\n클리티에는 물의 님프로 아폴론을 사랑했으나, 아폴론은 그녀에게 아무런 보답도 하지 않았다. 그리하여 그녀는 시들어 갔으니, 풀어헤친 머리타래를 두 어깨 위로 흘러내린 채, 차가운 땅 위에 온종일 앉아 있었다. 아흐레 동안 그녀는 앉아서 음식도 음료도 입에 대지 않았고, 자신의 눈물과 싸늘한 이슬만이 유일한 양식이었다. 그녀는 해가 떠오를 때 그를 바라보았고, 그가 날마다 가는 길을 따라 저물 때까지도 그러했다. 그녀는 다른 어떤 것도 보지 않았으니, 얼굴을 끊임없이 그에게로 향한 채였다. 마침내, 사람들이 말하기를, 그녀의 사지는 땅에 뿌리를 내렸고, 그녀의 얼굴은 한 송이 꽃[13]이 되었으니, 그 꽃은 줄기 위에서 돌며 해가 날마다 가는 길 내내 언제나 해를 향한다. 그것이 그만큼은 자신이 솟아난 그 님프의 마음을 간직하고 있기 때문이다.\n\n후드는 그의 「꽃들」에서 클리티에를 이렇게 넌지시 끌어들인다.\n\n\"나는 미친 클리티에를 갖지 않으리, 그 머리는 해에 의해 돌아가니. 튤립은 궁정의 요부라, 그러므로 나는 그것을 멀리하리. 카우슬립은 시골 처녀요, 제비꽃은 수녀라네. 그러나 나는 어여쁜 장미에게 구애하리, 모든 꽃의 여왕에게.\"\n\n해바라기는 한결같음을 나타내는 사랑받는 상징이다. 무어는 그것을 이렇게 쓴다.\n\n\"참되게 사랑한 적이 있는 마음은 결코 잊지 않으니, 끝까지 한결같이 사랑을 이어 가네. 해바라기가 제 신이 저물 때 그를 향해 돌리는 눈길이 그가 떠오를 때 돌렸던 그 눈길과 같듯이.\"\n\n# HERO AND LEANDER\n\n레안드로스는 아시아와 유럽을 가르는 해협의 아시아 쪽에 있는 도시 아비도스의 청년이었다. 맞은편 기슭, 세스토스라는 도시에는 아프로디테[로마의 〈베누스〉]의 여사제인 처녀 헤로가 살고 있었다. 레안드로스는 그녀를 사랑하여, 자신의 연인과 함께 있기 위해 밤마다 그 해협을 헤엄쳐 건너곤 했으니, 그녀가 그를 위해 망루에 높이 들어 올린 횃불의 인도를 받았다. 그러나 어느 날 밤 폭풍이 일어 바다가 거칠어졌다. 그의 힘이 다하여, 그는 물에 빠져 죽고 말았다. 파도가 그의 시신을 유럽 쪽 기슭으로 실어 갔고, 그곳에서 헤로는 그의 죽음을 알게 되자, 절망에 사로잡혀 망루에서 바다로 몸을 던져 목숨을 잃었다.\n\n다음 소네트는 키츠의 작품이다.\n\n\"레안드로스의 그림에 부쳐\" \"이리로 오라, 모든 고운 처녀들이여, 조용히, 언제나 눈길을 아래로 떨구고, 그대들의 흰 눈꺼풀 가장자리에 감춘 절제된 빛으로, 그리고 온순히 그대들의 고운 두 손을 맞잡으라. 너무도 부드러워 그대들이 차마 볼 수 없는 듯이, 손도 대지 못한 채, 그대들의 빛나는 아름다움의 희생자가, 제 젊은 영혼의 밤 속으로 가라앉아 가는 것을, 음울한 바다 한가운데서 어찌할 바 모르며 가라앉는 것을. 그것은 죽음을 향해 안간힘 쓰는 젊은 레안드로스라. 거의 정신을 잃을 듯, 그는 헤로의 뺨을 향해 지친 입술을 오므리고, 그녀의 미소에 미소로 답한다. 아, 끔찍한 꿈이여! 보라, 그의 몸이 어떻게 죽음처럼 무겁게 잠겨 드는지를. 팔과 어깨가 잠시 어른거리더니, 그는 사라졌도다. 그의 모든 사랑하던 숨결이 거품 되어 솟아오른다!\"\n\n레안드로스가 헬레스폰토스를 헤엄쳐 건넌 이야기는 꾸며 낸 것으로 여겨졌고, 그 위업은 불가능한 일로 간주되었으니, 바이런 경이 직접 그것을 해냄으로써 그 가능성을 입증하기 전까지는 그러했다. 그는 「아비도스의 신부」에서 이렇게 말한다.\n\n\"이 사지를 떠받쳐 실어 나른 저 출렁이는 물결이여.\"\n\n가장 좁은 부분의 거리는 거의 1마일에 이르며, 마르마라해에서 다도해[아르키펠라고스]로 끊임없이 흘러나가는 물길이 있다. 바이런의 시대 이후로 그 위업은 다른 이들에 의해서도 이루어졌다. 그러나 그것은 여전히 수영술에서의 힘과 솜씨를 가늠하는 시험으로 남아 있으니, 우리 독자들 가운데 감히 그 시도를 해 그것을 완수하는 데 성공하는 이가 있다면, 누구에게나 넓고도 오래도록 이어질 명성을 안겨 주기에 충분하다.\n\n같은 시의 둘째 칸토 첫머리에서 바이런은 이 이야기를 이렇게 넌지시 끌어들인다.\n\n\"헬레의 물결 위에 바람이 높이 분다, 가장 사납게 물결치던 그날 밤처럼, 그날 사랑은, 그를 보냈으되, 구해 주기를 잊었으니, 그 젊고, 아름답고, 용감한 이를, 세스토스 딸의 외로운 희망을. 아, 망루의 횃불이 하늘을 따라 홀로 높이 타오르던 그때, 솟구치는 강풍과 부서지는 거품이, 그리고 비명 지르는 바닷새들이 그에게 돌아가라 일렀건만, 높이 뜬 구름과 아래의 조수가, 온갖 징조와 소리로 가지 말라 막았건만, 그는 보지 못했고, 들으려 하지 않았으니, 두려움을 예고하는 그 어떤 소리도 광경도. 그의 눈은 다만 저 사랑의 빛을 보았을 뿐이니, 그것은 그가 우러른 하늘의 유일한 별이었다. 그의 귀는 다만 헤로의 노래로 울렸으니, '그대 물결이여, 연인들을 오래 갈라놓지 말라.' 그 이야기는 오래된 것이나, 사랑은 다시금 젊은 가슴들로 하여금 그처럼 참됨을 증명하도록 북돋우리라.\"\n[13] 해바라기.\n\n아테나, 곧 지혜의 여신은 제우스의 딸이었다. 그녀는 다 자란 모습으로, 완전한 갑옷을 입은 채 아버지의 머릿속에서 뛰쳐나왔다고 전한다. 그녀는 유용한 기예와 장식적인 기예를 두루 주관했으니, 농업과 항해와 같은 남성의 기예와 더불어 길쌈, 직조, 바느질과 같은 여성의 기예까지도 관장했다. 그녀는 또한 전쟁의 신이기도 했다. 하지만 그녀가 보호한 것은 오로지 방어를 위한 전쟁뿐이었으니, 폭력과 유혈을 잔혹하게 즐기는 아레스[로마의 〈마르스〉]의 성향에는 전혀 공감하지 않았다. 아테나이는 그녀가 택한 자리, 곧 그녀 자신의 도시였는데, 이 도시 역시 탐냈던 포세이돈[로마의 〈넵투누스〉]과 벌인 경합의 상으로 그녀에게 주어진 것이었다. 전하는 이야기에 따르면, 아테나이의 첫 번째 왕 케크롭스의 치세에 두 신이 이 도시의 소유권을 두고 다투었다고 한다. 신들은 필멸자에게 가장 유용한 선물을 만들어 내는 쪽에게 도시를 주기로 정했다. 포세이돈은 말을 주었고, 아테나는 올리브 나무를 내놓았다. 신들은 둘 가운데 올리브가 더 유용하다고 판정하여 도시를 여신에게 주었고, 도시는 그녀의 이름을 따라 아테나이라 불렸으니, 그녀의 그리스식 이름이 아테네였기 때문이다.\n\n또 다른 경합도 있었는데, 거기서는 한 필멸자가 감히 아테나와 겨루려 들었다. 그 필멸자는 아라크네라는 처녀로, 그녀는 직조와 자수의 기예에 너무나 뛰어난 솜씨를 익혀, 님프들조차 자기들의 숲과 샘을 떠나 그녀의 작품을 구경하러 올 정도였다. 그것은 완성되었을 때만 아름다운 것이 아니라, 만들어지는 과정마저도 아름다웠다. 그녀가 거친 상태의 양털을 집어 두루마리 모양으로 만드는 모습이며, 손가락으로 그것을 나누고 빗질하여 구름처럼 가볍고 보드라워 보일 때까지 다듬는 모습이며, 능숙한 손길로 물레를 돌리거나 천을 짜는 모습, 또는 다 짠 뒤에 바늘로 장식을 더하는 모습을 지켜보노라면, 누구든 아테나가 직접 그녀를 가르친 것이라 말했으리라. 하지만 그녀는 이를 부인했고, 여신의 제자로조차 여겨지는 것을 견디지 못했다. \"아테나더러 내 솜씨와 한번 겨뤄 보라고 하시오.\" 그녀는 말했다. \"지면 그 대가를 치르겠소.\" 아테나는 이 말을 듣고 언짢아했다. 그녀는 노파의 모습으로 변신하여 아라크네를 찾아가 친절한 충고를 건넸다. \"나는 많은 일을 겪어 왔다네.\" 그녀는 말했다. \"그러니 자네가 내 충고를 업신여기지 않기를 바라네. 같은 필멸자들에게야 자네 마음껏 도전하게나, 하지만 여신과는 겨루지 말게. 오히려 나는 자네가 한 말에 대해 여신께 용서를 빌라고 권하겠네. 여신은 자비로우시니 어쩌면 자네를 용서해 주실지도 모르지.\" 아라크네는 길쌈을 멈추고 얼굴에 노기를 띤 채 노파를 바라보았다. \"그 충고는 간직해 두었다가.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"당신 딸들이나 하녀들에게나 베푸시오. 나로 말하자면 내가 무슨 말을 하는지 잘 알고 있고, 그 말을 지킬 것이오. 나는 여신이 두렵지 않으니, 감히 나설 용기가 있다면 그 솜씨를 겨뤄 보라고 하시오.\" \"여기 그분이 오셨다.\" 아테나는 이렇게 말하며 변장을 벗고 본모습을 드러냈다. 님프들은 깊이 허리를 굽혀 경의를 표했고, 곁에 있던 이들 모두가 공경을 바쳤다. 오직 아라크네만이 겁먹지 않았다. 물론 그녀도 얼굴을 붉히기는 했으니, 갑작스러운 홍조가 그녀의 뺨을 물들였다가 이내 창백해졌다. 하지만 그녀는 결심을 굽히지 않았고, 자기 솜씨에 대한 어리석은 자만으로 자기 운명을 향해 달려들었다. 아테나는 더 이상 참지도, 더 이상 충고를 내놓지도 않았다. 두 사람은 경합에 들어갔다. 저마다 자리를 잡고 베틀에 날실을 매었다. 그러자 가느다란 북이 실 사이를 들고 났다. 가는 빗살이 달린 바디가 씨실을 제자리로 쳐 올려 천을 다져 갔다. 둘 다 빠른 속도로 작업했으니, 능숙한 손이 재빠르게 움직였고, 경합의 흥분이 그 수고를 가볍게 만들었다. 티로스산 자줏빛으로 물들인 양털이 다른 빛깔의 양털과 어우러졌는데, 너무나 능란하게 서로 차츰 옅어지며 섞여 들어 그 이음매가 눈을 속일 정도였다. 마치 소나기에 햇살이 반사되어 생겨난, 길게 휜 활 모양으로 하늘을 물들이는 무지개와도 같았으니, 빛깔들이 맞닿는 곳에서는 하나처럼 보이지만 맞닿은 지점에서 조금만 떨어지면 완전히 다른 빛깔이 된다.\n\n아테나는 자기 천에 포세이돈과 벌인 경합 장면을 짜 넣었다. 열두 천상의 권능이 묘사되었는데, 제우스가 위엄 있는 근엄함으로 그 한가운데 앉아 있었다. 바다의 지배자 포세이돈은 삼지창을 들고 막 대지를 내리친 듯한 모습이었고, 그 대지에서는 말 한 마리가 뛰쳐나와 있었다. 아테나는 자신을 투구 쓴 머리에 아이기스로 가슴을 가린 모습으로 그려 넣었다. 가운데 원이 그러했고, 네 귀퉁이에는 감히 신들과 겨루려 든 오만한 필멸자들에 대한 신들의 노여움을 보여 주는 사건들이 묘사되어 있었다. 이는 자신의 경쟁자에게 너무 늦기 전에 경합을 포기하라는 경고로 의도된 것이었다.\n\n아라크네는 자기 천을 신들의 결점과 잘못을 드러내려는 의도로 고른 소재들로 채웠다. 한 장면은 백조를 어루만지는 레다를 묘사했는데, 제우스가 바로 그 백조의 모습으로 변장했던 것이다. 또 다른 장면은 아버지가 가두어 둔 청동 탑 안에 있는 다나에를 그렸는데, 거기서 신은 황금 소나기의 모습으로 들어가는 데 성공했다. 또 다른 장면은 황소로 변장한 제우스에게 속아 넘어간 에우로페를 묘사했다. 그 짐승이 온순한 데 용기를 얻은 에우로페는 과감히 그 등에 올라탔는데, 그러자 제우스는 바다로 나아가 그녀를 태운 채 크레타까지 헤엄쳐 갔다. 그것이 어찌나 자연스럽게 짜여 있고 헤엄치는 물 또한 어찌나 자연스러웠던지, 누구든 그것을 진짜 황소라 여겼으리라. 그녀는 떠나가는 해안을 그리운 눈길로 돌아보며 동무들에게 도와 달라 외치는 듯했다. 또한 일렁이는 파도를 보고는 공포에 몸서리치며 물에서 발을 끌어당기는 듯했다.\n\n아라크네는 자기 화폭을 이와 비슷한 소재들로 채웠는데, 솜씨는 놀랄 만큼 훌륭했으나 그녀의 오만과 불경을 뚜렷이 드러내는 것들이었다. 아테나는 감탄하지 않을 수 없었으나, 그 모욕에 분노를 느꼈다. 그녀는 북으로 천을 쳐서 갈가리 찢어 버렸고, 그런 다음 아라크네의 이마를 건드려 죄책감과 수치심을 느끼게 했다. 아라크네는 그것을 견디지 못하고 가서 목을 매어 버렸다. 아테나는 밧줄에 매달린 그녀를 보고 가엾이 여겼다. \"살아라, 죄지은 여인이여!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그리고 이 교훈의 기억을 간직하도록, 너와 네 후손들은 앞으로 영원토록 계속 매달려 있을지어다.\" 그녀는 아라크네에게 투구꽃 즙을 뿌렸고, 그러자 즉시 그녀의 머리카락이 빠지고 코와 귀도 떨어져 나갔다. 그녀의 몸은 오그라들었고, 머리는 더욱 작아졌다. 손가락은 옆구리에 들러붙어 다리 노릇을 했다. 나머지는 모두 몸뚱이가 되었는데, 그녀는 그 몸에서 실을 자아내며, 아테나가 그녀를 건드려 거미로 바꾸어 놓았을 때와 똑같은 자세로 흔히 그 실에 매달려 있다.\n\n스펜서는 그의 「뮈오포트모스」에서 아라크네 이야기를 들려주는데, 그의 스승 오비디우스를 매우 충실하게 따르면서도 이야기의 결말에서는 그를 능가한다. 다음에 이어지는 두 연은 여신이 자신의 올리브 나무 창조 장면을 묘사한 뒤에 벌어진 일을 들려준다.\n\n\"이 잎새들 사이에 그녀는 나비 한 마리를 만들었으니, 빼어난 솜씨와 놀라운 정교함으로, 올리브 나무 사이를 즐거이 팔랑이는 그 모습이, 보기에 어찌나 똑같던지 살아 있는 듯하였다. 그 날개 위에 깔린 부드러운 솜털이며, 그 등을 덮은 비단 같은 솜털이며, 넓게 뻗은 더듬이며, 털 난 다리며, 그 찬란한 빛깔이며, 반짝이는 눈이며.\"\n\n\"아라크네가 이를 보았을 때, 그토록 보기 드문 솜씨에 짓눌리고 압도당하여, 그녀는 오래도록 망연히 서서 한마디도 못하였고, 그녀에게 눈길을 못 박은 채 빤히 바라보았으니, 낙담한 이의 표시인 그 침묵으로, 승리를 상대의 몫으로 내어 주고 말았다. 허나 속으로는 애를 태우며 사납게 타올랐고, 온몸의 피가 독한 원한으로 바뀌었다.\"\n\n이리하여 그 변신은 여신의 어떤 직접적인 행위가 아니라 아라크네 자신의 굴욕과 분함으로 말미암아 일어난 것이 된다.\n\n다음은 개릭이 지은, 옛 풍의 정중한 헌사의 한 본보기다.\n\n\"어느 귀부인의 자수에 부쳐 \"지난날 아라크네는, 시인들이 전하듯이, 자신의 기예로 여신에게 도전하였으니, 그 당돌한 필멸자는 머지않아 제 오만의 가엾은 희생물로 쓰러졌네. \"오, 그러니 아라크네의 운명을 경계하라. 신중하라, 클로에여, 그리고 굽힐지니, 그대는 반드시 그녀의 미움을 사게 되리라, 그녀의 기예와 재치를 모두 겨루는 자라면.\"\n\n테니슨은 그의 「예술의 궁전」에서, 그 궁전을 장식한 예술 작품들을 묘사하며 에우로페를 이렇게 넌지시 언급한다.\n\n\". . . 어여쁜 에우로페의 외투가 어깨에서 풀려 바람에 날려 뒤로 펄럭이고, 한 손에서는 크로커스가 늘어졌으며, 다른 한 손은 온순한 황소의 황금빛 뿔을 움켜쥐었네.\"\n\n그의 「공주」에는 다나에를 이렇게 넌지시 언급하는 대목이 있다.\n\n\"이제 대지는 온통 별들을 향한 다나에처럼 누워 있고, 그대의 마음은 온통 내게 열려 있네.\"\n\n아라크네의 운명은 온 나라에 두루 알려졌고, 감히 자신을 신들과 견주지 말라는 경고로서 오만한 필멸자 모두에게 본보기가 되었다. 하지만 한 사람만은, 그것도 부인의 몸으로, 그 겸손의 교훈을 배우지 못했다. 그가 바로 테바이의 여왕 니오베였다. 그녀에게는 실로 자랑할 것이 많았다. 하지만 그녀를 우쭐하게 한 것은 남편의 명성도, 자신의 미모도, 그들의 고귀한 혈통도, 그들 왕국의 권세도 아니었다. 그것은 그녀의 자식들이었으니, 만약 그녀가 그렇다고 내세우지만 않았던들 니오베는 참으로 어머니들 가운데 가장 행복한 사람이었으리라. 레토[로마의 〈라토나〉]와 그 자손인 아폴론과 아르테미스를 기리는 연례 축전이 열려, 테바이 백성이 모여 이마에 월계관을 쓰고 제단에 유향을 바치며 서원을 올리던 바로 그때, 니오베가 군중 사이에 나타났다. 그녀의 차림새는 황금과 보석으로 찬란했고, 그 모습은 성난 여인의 얼굴이 아름다울 수 있는 만큼이나 아름다웠다. 그녀는 거만한 눈길로 백성을 둘러보며 서 있었다. \"이 무슨 어리석은 짓이냐.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"너희가 한 번도 본 적 없는 존재들을, 눈앞에 서 있는 이보다 더 받든다니! 어찌하여 레토는 숭배를 받고, 내게는 아무것도 바쳐지지 않는단 말이냐? 내 아버지는 신들의 식탁에 손님으로 맞아들여진 탄탈로스였고, 내 어머니는 여신이었다. 내 남편은 이 도시 테바이를 세우고 다스리며, 프리기아는 내 아버지에게서 물려받은 유산이다. 내가 눈길을 돌리는 곳마다 내 권세의 바탕이 보이며, 내 자태와 풍모 또한 여신에 못지않다. 이 모든 것에 더하여, 내게는 아들 일곱과 딸 일곱이 있고, 내 가문과 인연 맺기에 합당한 사위들과 며느리들을 기대하고 있다. 내게 자랑할 까닭이 없겠느냐? 너희는 단 두 자식뿐인 저 티탄의 딸 레토를 나보다 더 받들겠다는 것이냐? 나는 그 일곱 곱절을 두었다. 나는 참으로 복받은 몸이요, 앞으로도 복받은 몸으로 남으리라! 누가 이를 부인하겠느냐? 내 풍요로움이 곧 내 안전이다. 나는 운명의 여신조차 굴복시킬 수 없을 만큼 스스로 강하다고 느낀다. 그녀가 내게서 많은 것을 앗아 갈지라도, 내게는 여전히 많은 것이 남으리라. 설령 내가 자식 몇을 잃는다 해도, 단 둘뿐인 레토만큼 가난해지지는 않을 것이다. 이 엄숙한 의식에서 썩 물러가거라. 이마에서 월계관을 벗어 던지고, 이 숭배를 그만두어라!\" 백성은 복종하여, 신성한 예식을 끝맺지 못한 채 떠나갔다.\n\n여신은 분노했다. 그녀는 자신이 거처하는 킨토스 산꼭대기에서 아들과 딸에게 이렇게 말했다. \"내 자식들아, 너희 둘을 그토록 자랑스러워했고 오직 헤라 한 분을 제외하고는 어떤 여신에게도 뒤지지 않는다고 여겨 온 내가, 이제는 정녕 내가 여신인지 의심하기 시작했구나. 너희가 나를 지켜 주지 않는다면 나는 숭배를 송두리째 빼앗기고 말 것이다.\" 그녀가 이런 식으로 말을 이어 가는데, 아폴론이 그녀의 말을 가로막았다. \"더 말씀하지 마소서.\" 그가 말했다. \"말은 그저 응징을 늦출 뿐입니다.\" 아르테미스도 똑같이 말했다. 구름에 몸을 가린 채 공중을 가르며 날아가, 그들은 그 도시의 탑들 위에 내려앉았다. 성문 앞에는 너른 들판이 펼쳐져 있었는데, 거기서 도시의 젊은이들이 전쟁 놀이를 하고 있었다. 니오베의 아들들도 다른 이들과 함께 거기 있었으니, 더러는 화려하게 장식한 혈기 왕성한 말에 올라타 있었고, 더러는 멋들어진 전차를 몰고 있었다. 맏아들 이스메노스는 거품 무는 준마들을 몰다가, 위에서 날아온 화살에 맞고는 \"아아!\" 하고 외치며 고삐를 떨어뜨리더니 숨을 거두며 쓰러졌다. 또 한 아들은 활시위 소리를 듣고서, 마치 폭풍이 몰려오는 것을 보고 돛을 활짝 펼쳐 항구로 향하는 뱃사공처럼, 말들에게 고삐를 늦추어 달아나려 했다. 하지만 그가 달아나는 사이에 피할 수 없는 화살이 그를 따라잡았다. 더 어린 두 소년은 막 자기들 할 일을 마치고 씨름 한판을 하러 운동장에 나가 있었다. 그들이 가슴을 맞댄 채 서 있을 때, 화살 하나가 둘을 한꺼번에 꿰뚫었다. 그들은 함께 비명을 지르고, 함께 마지막 눈길을 주위에 던지며, 함께 숨을 거두었다. 손위 형인 알페노르는 그들이 쓰러지는 것을 보고 도우려 그 자리로 달려갔다가, 형제로서의 도리를 다하던 중에 화살에 맞아 쓰러졌다. 오직 한 사람, 일리오네우스만이 남았다. 그는 혹시 기도가 도움이 될까 하여 하늘을 향해 두 팔을 들어 올렸다. \"신들이시여, 저를 살려 주소서!\" 그는 모두에게 호소하며 외쳤으니, 모두에게 빌 필요는 없다는 것을 모르는 탓이었다. 아폴론은 그를 살려 주려 했으나, 화살은 이미 시위를 떠난 뒤였고, 때는 너무 늦어 있었다.\n\n백성의 공포와 시중드는 이들의 비탄은 이내 벌어진 일을 니오베에게 알려 주었다. 그녀는 그것이 일어날 수 있다는 것을 좀처럼 믿을 수 없었다. 신들이 감히 그런 짓을 했다는 데 분개했고, 그들이 그럴 능력이 있었다는 데 경악했다. 그녀의 남편 암피온은 그 타격에 짓눌려 스스로 목숨을 끊었다. 아아! 얼마 전 신성한 예식에서 백성을 쫓아내고, 벗들의 부러움을 사며 도시를 위풍당당하게 거닐던 그 니오베가, 이제는 적들에게조차 동정을 받는 신세가 되었으니, 이 얼마나 달라진 모습인가! 그녀는 숨이 끊긴 시신들 위에 무릎을 꿇고, 죽은 아들들에게 차례로 입을 맞추었다. 창백한 두 팔을 하늘로 들어 올리며 말했다. \"잔인한 레토여, 내 비통함으로 네 분노를 실컷 채우려무나! 내가 일곱 아들을 무덤까지 따라가는 동안, 네 모진 마음을 흡족하게 채우거라. 하지만 네 승리가 어디 있느냐? 이렇게 자식을 빼앗긴 나일지라도, 나를 이긴 너보다는 여전히 더 부유하다.\" 그녀가 말을 채 끝내기도 전에 활시위 소리가 울리며 모든 이의 가슴에 공포를 불러일으켰으나, 오직 니오베만은 예외였다. 그녀는 비통함이 넘쳐 도리어 담대해져 있었다. 딸들은 상복을 입은 채 죽은 오라비들의 관 곁에 서 있었다. 한 딸이 화살에 맞아 쓰러지더니, 자기가 애도하던 시신 위에서 숨을 거두었다. 또 한 딸은 어머니를 위로하려다가, 갑자기 말을 멈추고는 숨이 끊겨 땅에 쓰러졌다. 셋째 딸은 달아나 몸을 피하려 했고, 넷째 딸은 몸을 숨겨 화를 면하려 했으며, 또 한 딸은 어느 쪽을 택해야 할지 몰라 떨며 서 있었다. 이제 여섯이 죽고 오직 하나만 남았는데, 어머니는 그 아이를 두 팔로 끌어안고 마치 온몸으로 감싸듯 가렸다. \"이 하나만은, 그것도 막내만은 살려 다오! 오, 그 많던 자식 가운데 이 하나만은 살려 다오!\" 그녀가 외쳤다. 그러나 그녀가 말하는 사이에 그 하나마저 죽어 쓰러졌다. 그녀는 아들들, 딸들, 남편이 모두 죽은 가운데 황량하게 앉아, 비탄으로 마비된 듯했다. 산들바람도 그녀의 머리카락을 흔들지 못했고, 뺨에는 핏기가 없었으며, 두 눈은 고정된 채 미동도 없이 노려보았고, 그녀에게는 살아 있다는 어떤 기색도 없었다. 그녀의 혀마저 입천장에 들러붙었고, 핏줄은 생명의 흐름을 실어 나르기를 멈추었다. 목은 굽혀지지 않았고, 두 팔은 어떤 몸짓도 하지 못했으며, 발은 한 걸음도 떼지 못했다. 그녀는 안팎으로 모두 돌로 변했다. 그러나 눈물만은 계속 흘러내렸다. 그리하여 그녀는 회오리바람에 실려 고향의 산으로 옮겨졌고, 지금도 여전히 바위 덩어리로 남아, 거기서 졸졸 흐르는 시냇물이, 그 끝없는 비탄의 공물로서 흘러내리고 있다.\n\n니오베 이야기는 바이런에게 몰락한 근대 로마의 처지를 빗댄 멋진 비유의 소재를 마련해 주었다.\n\n\"뭇 나라의 니오베여! 저기 그녀가 서 있노라, 자식도 없고 왕관도 없이, 목소리 잃은 비탄에 잠겨, 시든 두 손에는 텅 빈 항아리를 들었는데, 그 거룩한 유골은 오래전에 흩어져 버렸도다. 스키피오 가문의 무덤에는 이제 한 줌 재도 없고, 무덤들조차 그 영웅적인 주인들을 잃은 채 텅 비어 있구나. 오 늙은 티베리스여, 그대는 대리석 황무지를 가르며 흐르는가? 누런 물결로 솟구쳐 올라, 그녀의 비탄을 덮어 다오.\" 「차일드 해럴드」, 제4편 79연.\n\n이 가슴 아픈 이야기는 피렌체의 황실 미술관에 있는 한 유명한 조각상의 소재가 되었다. 그것은 본디 어느 신전의 박공에 배치되어 있었으리라 여겨지는 군상의 중심 인물이다. 겁에 질린 자식의 팔에 안긴 어머니의 형상은 고대 조각상들 가운데 가장 찬탄받는 작품의 하나다. 그것은 라오콘 군상이나 아폴론 상과 더불어 예술의 걸작으로 손꼽힌다. 다음은 이 조각상에 관한 것으로 여겨지는 어느 그리스 경구시의 번역이다.\n\n\"신들이 그녀를 돌로 바꾸어 놓았으나, 헛된 일이었네. 조각가의 솜씨가 그녀에게 다시 숨결을 불어넣었으니.\"\n\n니오베 이야기가 비극적이기는 하나, 무어가 「길 위의 시편」에서 그것을 다룬 방식에는 웃음을 참을 수 없다.\n\n\"고귀하신 리처드 블랙모어 경께서는 마차 안에서 곧잘 시를 짓곤 하셨으니, 재사들이 그분을 헐뜯는 것이 아니라면, 죽음과 서사시 사이에서 시간을 보내셨다네, 온종일 끄적이고 또 죽여 가면서. 마치 마차에 느긋이 앉은 포이보스처럼, 한편으로는 드높은 노래를 읊조리다가, 한편으로는 어린 니오베들을 죽여 가면서.\"\n\n리처드 블랙모어 경은 의사였는데, 동시에 매우 다작하면서도 매우 무미건조한 시인이었다. 그의 작품들은 이제 잊혔으니, 무어 같은 재사가 농담거리로 떠올릴 때나 간혹 되살아날 뿐이다.\n\n────\n\n그라이아이는 태어날 때부터 머리가 잿빛이었던 세 자매로, 거기에서 그들의 이름이 비롯되었다. 고르고네스는 돼지의 그것처럼 거대한 이빨과 청동 발톱, 그리고 뱀으로 된 머리카락을 지닌 흉측한 여인들이었다. 이 존재들 가운데 신화에서 비중을 차지하는 것은 고르고네스 중 하나인 메두사뿐이며, 우리는 곧이어 그녀의 이야기를 다룰 것이다. 우리가 이들을 언급하는 까닭은 주로 몇몇 근대 작가들이 내놓은 한 가지 기발한 학설을 소개하기 위해서다. 즉 고르고네스와 그라이아이는 바다의 공포를 의인화한 것에 지나지 않으며, 전자는 드넓은 외해의 거센 큰 물결을, 후자는 해안의 바위에 부딪쳐 흰 물마루를 이루는 파도를 가리킨다는 것이다. 그리스어로 된 그들의 이름은 위에서 말한 별칭들을 뜻한다.\n\n# PERSEUS AND MEDUSA\n\n페르세우스는 제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]와 다나에 사이에서 태어난 아들이었다. 그의 외할아버지 아크리시오스는 딸의 자식이 자기 죽음의 도구가 되리라는 신탁의 경고에 놀란 나머지, 어머니와 아이를 궤짝에 가두어 바다에 띄워 보내게 했다. 궤짝은 세리포스 쪽으로 떠내려갔고, 거기서 한 어부가 그것을 발견하여 어머니와 아기를 그 나라의 왕인 폴리덱테스에게 데려갔으며, 왕은 그들을 친절히 대접했다. 페르세우스가 다 자라자 폴리덱테스는 그를 보내 메두사를 정복하게 했는데, 메두사는 그 나라를 황폐하게 만든 무시무시한 괴물이었다. 그녀는 한때 머리카락이 가장 큰 자랑이었던 아름다운 처녀였으나, 감히 아테나[로마의 〈미네르바〉]와 아름다움을 겨루려 했기에 여신은 그녀의 매력을 빼앗고 그 아름다운 곱슬머리를 쉭쉭거리는 뱀들로 바꾸어 버렸다. 그녀는 너무나 끔찍한 모습의 잔인한 괴물이 되어, 살아 있는 어떤 것도 돌로 변하지 않고서는 그녀를 바라볼 수 없었다. 그녀가 사는 동굴 주위 사방에는, 우연히 그녀를 흘끗 보았다가 그 광경에 돌이 되어 버린 사람과 짐승들의 석상이 보였다. 페르세우스는 아테나와 헤르메스[로마의 〈메르쿠리우스〉]의 도움을 받았는데, 전자는 자기 방패를 빌려주었고 후자는 날개 달린 신발을 빌려주었다. 그는 메두사가 잠든 사이에 다가가, 그녀를 똑바로 바라보지 않도록 조심하며 자기가 든 빛나는 방패에 비친 그녀의 모습에 의지하여 그녀의 머리를 베었고, 그것을 아테나에게 바쳤으며, 여신은 그것을 자기 아이기스 한가운데에 박아 넣었다.\n\n밀턴은 그의 「코무스」에서 아이기스를 다음과 같이 암시한다.\n\n\"현명한 아테나가 둘렀던, 정복당하지 않은 처녀가 지녔던 저 뱀의 머리를 한 고르곤의 방패는 무엇이었던가, 그녀가 그것으로 적들을 얼어붙은 돌로 굳혀 버렸으니, 그것은 정숙하고 엄정한 굳은 표정이요, 난폭한 폭력을 갑작스러운 경배와 멍한 외경으로 꺾어 버린 고귀한 우아함이었도다!\"\n\n「건강을 지키는 기술」의 시인 암스트롱은 서리가 물에 미치는 영향을 다음과 같이 묘사한다.\n\n\"이제 험한 북풍이 불어 굳어 가는 온 지역을 얼리는도다, 키르케가 일찍이 빚었거나 잔혹한 메데이아가 끓였던 것보다 더 강한 마법으로, 제 둑에 재잘대곤 하던 시냇물마다 온통 잠잠해진 채 둑 사이에 끼여 누워 있고, 시든 갈대조차 움직이지 않는도다… 사나운 북동풍에 부추겨진 물결들이 성난 머리를 짜증스레 흔들어 대다가, 그 광기의 거품 한가운데서 그대로 기념비 같은 얼음으로 굳어 버렸도다. . . . . . . . 그러한 처형을, 그토록 준엄하고 그토록 갑작스러운 처형을, 무시무시한 메두사의 소름 끼치는 모습이 이루어 냈으니, 그녀가 숲을 헤매며 그 사나운 거주자들을 돌로 바꾸었을 때, 거품을 문 사자가 제 먹이를 향해 사납게 덮쳐들던 바로 그 순간, 그녀의 더 빠른 힘이 그 서두름을 앞질러, 그 사나운 자세 그대로 멈춰 선 채 사자는 대리석에 새겨진 분노처럼 서 있도다!\" —「셰익스피어 모작」.\n\n# PERSEUS AND ATLAS\n\n메두사를 죽인 뒤 페르세우스는 고르곤의 머리를 지닌 채 육지와 바다를 가로질러 멀리, 그리고 넓게 날아갔다. 밤이 다가오자 그는 해가 지는 대지의 서쪽 끝에 이르렀다. 거기서 그는 기꺼이 아침까지 쉬고 싶었다. 그곳은 아틀라스 왕의 영토였는데, 그의 덩치는 다른 모든 사람을 능가했다. 그는 양 떼와 소 떼가 풍부했고, 그의 지위를 다툴 만한 이웃이나 경쟁자가 없었다. 그러나 그의 가장 큰 자랑은 정원이었으니, 그 열매는 황금으로 되어 있어 황금 잎에 반쯤 가려진 채 황금 가지에 매달려 있었다. 페르세우스는 그에게 말했다. \"저는 손님으로 왔습니다. 당신이 빛나는 혈통을 존중하신다면, 저는 제우스를 제 아버지로 내세웁니다. 당신이 위대한 공적을 높이 사신다면, 저는 고르곤을 정복한 일을 내세웁니다. 저는 쉴 곳과 먹을 것을 구합니다.\" 그러나 아틀라스는 언젠가 제우스의 아들이 자기에게서 황금 사과를 빼앗으리라고 경고한 옛 예언을 떠올렸다. 그래서 그는 대답했다. \"썩 물러가라! 그러지 않으면 너의 거짓된 영광의 주장도, 혈통도 너를 지켜 주지 못하리라.\" 그러고는 그를 밀쳐 내려 했다. 페르세우스는 그 거인이 자기보다 너무 강하다는 것을 알고 말했다. \"당신이 제 우정을 그토록 하찮게 여기시니, 부디 선물 하나를 받아 주십시오.\" 그러고는 얼굴을 돌린 채 고르곤의 머리를 치켜들었다. 아틀라스는 그 거대한 몸뚱이 전체가 돌로 변했다. 그의 수염과 머리카락은 숲이 되었고, 그의 팔과 어깨는 절벽이, 그의 머리는 봉우리가, 그의 뼈는 바위가 되었다. 각 부분이 점점 커져서 마침내 그는 산이 되었고, (그것이 신들의 뜻이었기에) 모든 별을 거느린 하늘이 그의 어깨 위에 얹히게 되었다.\n\n# THE SEA-MONSTER\n\n페르세우스는 비행을 계속하여 케페우스가 왕으로 있던 아이티오피아인들의 나라에 이르렀다. 그의 왕비 카시오페이아는 자기 아름다움을 뽐낸 나머지 감히 자신을 바다의 님프들과 비교했고, 이것이 그들의 분노를 너무나 크게 자극하여, 그들은 해안을 황폐하게 만들 어마어마한 바다 괴물을 보냈다. 신들을 달래기 위해 케페우스는 신탁의 지시에 따라 자기 딸 안드로메다를 괴물에게 잡아먹히도록 내놓아야 했다. 페르세우스가 공중의 높은 곳에서 내려다보니, 처녀가 바위에 사슬로 묶인 채 그 뱀이 다가오기를 기다리고 있는 것이 보였다. 그녀는 너무도 창백하고 미동도 없어, 흘러내리는 눈물과 산들바람에 나부끼는 머리카락이 아니었더라면 그는 그녀를 대리석상으로 여겼을 것이다. 그는 그 광경에 너무나 놀라 날개를 퍼덕이는 것조차 거의 잊을 뻔했다. 그녀의 위를 맴돌며 그는 말했다. \"오 처녀여, 그대는 이런 사슬에 묶일 만한 사람이 아니라 오히려 다정한 연인들을 함께 묶는 사슬에나 어울릴 사람이니, 부디 청하노니 그대의 이름과 그대 나라의 이름을, 그리고 어찌하여 이렇게 묶여 있는지를 말해 주오.\" 처음에 그녀는 부끄러움에 입을 다물었고, 할 수만 있었다면 두 손으로 얼굴을 가렸을 것이다. 그러나 그가 질문을 거듭하자, 차마 말하지 못할 어떤 잘못을 저질렀다고 여겨질까 두려워 그녀는 자기 이름과 나라의 이름을, 그리고 어머니가 아름다움을 뽐낸 일을 털어놓았다. 그녀가 말을 채 마치기도 전에, 물 저편에서 소리가 들리더니 바다 괴물이 나타나, 머리를 수면 위로 쳐들고 넓은 가슴으로 파도를 가르며 다가왔다. 처녀는 비명을 질렀고, 이제 그 자리에 당도한 아버지와 어머니는 둘 다 비참했으나 어머니가 더 마땅히 그러했으며, 그들은 곁에 서서 보호해 줄 수도 없이 그저 통곡을 쏟아 내고 제물이 된 딸을 끌어안을 따름이었다. 그때 페르세우스가 말했다. \"눈물을 흘릴 시간은 얼마든지 있을 것이오. 그러나 구해 낼 시간은 지금 이 순간뿐이오. 제우스의 아들이라는 제 신분과 고르곤을 죽인 자라는 제 명성이라면 구혼자로서 받아들여질 만하겠지만, 신들이 호의를 베풀어만 주신다면 저는 제가 한 봉사로써 그녀를 얻고자 합니다. 만약 제 용맹으로 그녀가 구원받는다면, 저는 그녀를 제 보답으로 받기를 요구합니다.\" 부모는 (어찌 망설일 수 있었겠는가?) 이에 동의하며 딸과 함께 왕가의 지참금까지 약속했다.\n\n그리고 이제 괴물이 솜씨 좋은 투석꾼이 던진 돌의 사정거리 안에 들어왔을 때, 청년은 갑자기 도약하여 공중으로 솟아올랐다. 마치 독수리가 높은 비행 중에 햇볕을 쬐고 있는 뱀을 보고 덮쳐, 뱀이 머리를 돌려 독니를 쓰지 못하도록 목을 움켜쥐듯이, 청년은 괴물의 등 위로 내리꽂혀 자기 검을 그 어깨에 박아 넣었다. 상처에 자극받은 괴물은 공중으로 몸을 일으켰다가 깊은 물속으로 곤두박질쳤고, 그러고는 짖어 대는 개 떼에 둘러싸인 멧돼지처럼 이리저리 재빨리 몸을 틀었으며, 그동안 청년은 날개의 힘으로 그 공격을 피했다. 그는 비늘 사이로 검이 들어갈 만한 틈을 찾는 곳마다 상처를 내어, 지금은 옆구리를, 이제는 꼬리 쪽으로 비스듬히 이어지는 허리를 찔렀다. 그 짐승은 콧구멍에서 피가 섞인 물을 내뿜었다. 영웅의 날개가 그것에 젖어, 그는 더는 날개를 믿을 수 없게 되었다. 그는 파도 위로 솟아오른 바위에 내려앉아 튀어나온 조각 하나를 붙잡고 있다가, 괴물이 가까이 떠올랐을 때 치명적인 일격을 가했다. 해안에 모여든 사람들이 어찌나 환호했던지 언덕들이 그 소리를 메아리쳤다. 부모는 기쁨에 겨워 장래의 사위를 끌어안으며 그를 자신들의 구원자요 가문의 구세주라 불렀고, 이 싸움의 원인이자 보답이었던 처녀는 바위에서 내려왔다.\n\n카시오페이아는 아이티오피아인이었으므로, 그녀가 뽐내던 아름다움에도 불구하고 피부가 검었다. 적어도 밀턴은 그렇게 생각했던 듯한데, 그는 「펜세로소」에서 이 이야기를 암시하며 우울을 다음과 같이 부른다.\n\n\"…현명하고 거룩한 여신이여, 그대의 성스러운 얼굴은 너무도 환하여 인간의 시야로는 감당할 수 없으니, 그러므로 우리의 약한 눈을 위해 검은빛으로, 차분한 지혜의 빛깔로 덮였도다. 검으나, 그 검음은 사람들의 존경 속에서 멤논 왕자의 누이에게나 어울릴 만한 것이거나, 아니면 자기 아름다움의 찬사를 바다의 님프들 위에 두려 하여 그들의 권능을 노엽게 했던 저 별이 된 아이티오피아 왕비에게나 어울릴 만한 것이로다.\"\n\n카시오페이아가 \"별이 된 아이티오피아 왕비\"라 불리는 까닭은, 죽은 뒤 그녀가 별들 사이에 놓여 그 이름의 별자리를 이루었기 때문이다. 비록 그녀가 이런 영예를 얻기는 했으나, 옛 원수였던 바다의 님프들이 끝내 그 뜻을 관철하여, 그녀를 하늘의 극에 가까운 부분에 놓이게 했으니, 거기서 그녀는 매일 밤 절반의 시간을 머리를 아래로 향한 채 매달려 겸손의 교훈을 얻게 되었다.\n\n멤논은 아이티오피아의 왕자였는데, 그에 관해서는 뒤의 어느 장에서 이야기할 것이다.\n\n# THE WEDDING FEAST\n\n기뻐하던 부모는 페르세우스와 안드로메다와 함께 궁전으로 가서, 거기서 그들을 위해 연회를 베풀었고, 온통 기쁨과 잔치 분위기였다. 그러나 갑자기 호전적인 함성 소리가 들리더니, 처녀의 약혼자였던 피네우스가 자기 추종자 무리를 거느리고 들이닥쳐 그 처녀를 자기 것이라며 내놓으라고 요구했다. 케페우스가 항변했으나 헛된 일이었다. \"그대는 그녀가 괴물의 제물이 되어 바위에 묶여 있을 때 그녀를 요구했어야 했소. 그녀를 그런 운명에 처하게 한 신들의 판결이 모든 약혼을 무효로 만들었으니, 그것은 죽음 그 자체가 그랬을 것과 같소.\" 피네우스는 아무 대꾸도 없이 페르세우스에게 투창을 던졌으나, 그것은 빗나가 무해하게 떨어졌다. 페르세우스도 답으로 창을 던지려 했으나, 비겁한 공격자는 달아나 제단 뒤로 몸을 숨겼다. 그러나 그의 행동은 그의 무리가 케페우스의 손님들에게 달려드는 신호가 되었다. 그들은 스스로를 방어했고 전면적인 싸움이 벌어졌으니, 늙은 왕은 헛된 항의를 한 끝에 그 자리에서 물러나, 자신은 이 환대의 권리를 짓밟는 만행에 죄가 없음을 신들에게 증언해 달라고 외쳤다.\n\n페르세우스와 그의 벗들은 한동안 이 불공평한 싸움을 버텨 냈으나, 공격자들의 수가 너무나 많아, 파멸이 불가피해 보였다. 그때 페르세우스에게 한 가지 생각이 번뜩였다. \"내 적이 나를 지키게 만들리라.\" 그러고는 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"여기 내 벗이 있거든 눈을 돌릴지어다!\" 그러고는 고르곤의 머리를 높이 치켜들었다. \"네 요술로 우리를 겁주려 들지 마라.\" 테스켈로스가 이렇게 말하며 던지려고 투창을 들어 올리다가, 바로 그 자세 그대로 돌이 되어 버렸다. 암피크스는 쓰러진 적의 몸에 검을 박으려던 참이었으나, 그의 팔이 굳어 버려 그는 검을 앞으로 찌를 수도 빼낼 수도 없었다. 또 다른 자는 떠들썩하게 도전하던 중에 입을 벌린 채로 멈춰 버렸으나 아무 소리도 나오지 않았다. 페르세우스의 벗 중 하나인 아콘테우스는 고르곤을 흘끗 보고 다른 이들처럼 굳어 버렸다. 아스티아게스가 검으로 그를 내리쳤으나, 상처를 내기는커녕 그것은 쟁그랑 소리를 내며 튕겨 나왔다.\n\n피네우스는 자신의 부당한 침략이 빚은 이 끔찍한 결과를 보고 당황했다. 그는 벗들을 큰 소리로 불렀으나 아무 대답도 얻지 못했고, 그들을 만져 보니 돌이 되어 있었다. 그는 무릎을 꿇고 페르세우스에게 두 손을 뻗으면서도 고개는 돌린 채 자비를 빌었다. \"모든 것을 가지시오.\" 그가 말했다. \"다만 제 목숨만은 살려 주시오.\" \"비열한 겁쟁이여,\" 페르세우스가 말했다. \"이만큼은 내가 그대에게 허락하리라. 어떤 무기도 그대를 건드리지 않을 것이며, 게다가 그대는 이 사건들의 기념물로서 내 집에 보존되리라.\" 이렇게 말하며 그는 피네우스가 바라보고 있던 쪽으로 고르곤의 머리를 들이밀었고, 무릎을 꿇은 바로 그 모습 그대로, 두 손을 뻗고 얼굴을 돌린 채, 그는 미동도 없이 굳어 한 덩어리의 돌이 되어 버렸다!\n\n다음은 밀먼의 「사모르」에 나오는, 페르세우스에 관한 암시다.\n\n\"옛이야기 속 리비아의 혼례 한가운데 페르세우스가 분노의 준엄한 평정으로 섰던 것처럼, 부풀어 오른 발목의 깃털 위에 반쯤 서고 반쯤 떠오른 채, 방패에 비친 그 빛나는 얼굴이 날뛰던 난투를 돌로 굳혀 버렸으니, 그처럼, 허나 아무런 마법의 무기도 없이, 오직 그 굳건한 표정의 위압과 통제만을 지닌 채, 브리튼인 사모르가 일어섰도다. 그가 일어서자 외경이 사방에 퍼지고, 소란하던 회당이 잠잠해졌도다.\"\n\n————\n\n괴물이란 신화의 언어로 말하자면 그 비율이나 신체 부위가 자연스럽지 못한 존재들로서, 대개는 엄청난 힘과 흉포함을 지녔으며 그것을 인간에게 해를 끼치고 괴롭히는 데 사용했기에 두려움의 대상으로 여겨졌다. 그중 일부는 서로 다른 동물의 신체 부위가 결합된 것으로 여겨졌는데, 스핑크스와 키마이라가 그러했다. 그리고 이들에게는 야수의 온갖 끔찍한 속성이 인간의 지혜와 능력과 함께 부여되었다. 또 다른 괴물들, 이를테면 거인들은 주로 그 크기에서 인간과 달랐다. 그리고 바로 이 점에서 우리는 그들 사이의 커다란 차이를 인정하지 않을 수 없다. 인간 거인이라 부를 수 있다면 그러한 부류, 곧 키클로페스, 안타이오스, 오리온 등은 인간과 전혀 어울리지 않을 정도로 크지는 않았다고 보아야 하는데, 왜냐하면 이들은 인간과 더불어 사랑하고 다투며 어울렸기 때문이다. 그러나 신들과 전쟁을 벌인 초인적인 거인들은 그 크기가 어마어마하게 더 컸다. 전하는 바에 따르면 티티오스는 평원에 누우면 아홉 에이커를 덮을 정도였고, 엔켈라도스를 눌러 두기 위해서는 아이트나 산 전체를 그 위에 얹어 놓아야 했다고 한다.\n\n거인들이 신들을 상대로 벌인 전쟁과 그 결말에 관해서는 이미 이야기한 바 있다. 이 전쟁이 계속되는 동안 거인들은 만만찮은 적임을 입증했다. 그들 가운데 일부는, 이를테면 브리아레오스처럼 백 개의 팔을 지녔고, 또 다른 일부는, 이를테면 티폰처럼 불을 내뿜었다. 한때 거인들은 신들에게 어찌나 큰 공포를 안겨 주었던지 신들은 이집트로 달아나 갖가지 모습으로 변신해 몸을 숨겼다. 제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]는 숫양의 모습을 취했는데, 그래서 나중에 이집트에서 굽은 뿔을 지닌 암몬 신으로 숭배받게 되었다. 아폴론은 까마귀가, 디오니소스[로마의 〈바쿠스〉]는 염소가, 아르테미스[로마의 〈디아나〉]는 고양이가, 헤라[로마의 〈유노〉]는 암소가, 아프로디테[로마의 〈베누스〉]는 물고기가, 헤르메스[로마의 〈메르쿠리우스〉]는 새가 되었다. 또 한번은 거인들이 하늘로 기어 올라가려고 시도했는데, 그 목적을 위해 오사 산을 들어올려 펠리온 산 위에 쌓아 올렸다.[16] 그들은 마침내 벼락에 의해 제압되었는데, 그 벼락은 아테나[로마의 〈미네르바〉]가 고안하여 헤파이스토스[로마의 〈불카누스〉]와 그의 키클로페스에게 제우스를 위해 만들도록 가르쳐 준 것이었다.\n\n# THE SPHINX\n\n테바이의 왕 라이오스는 갓 태어난 아들이 자라도록 내버려 두면 자신의 왕좌와 목숨이 위태로워지리라는 신탁의 경고를 받았다. 그리하여 그는 아이를 한 목동에게 맡기며 죽여 버리라고 명령했다. 그러나 목동은 연민을 느낀 나머지, 그렇다고 명령을 완전히 거역할 엄두는 내지 못한 채, 아이의 두 발을 묶어 나뭇가지에 매달아 두었다. 이런 처지에 놓인 갓난아이를 한 농부가 발견하여 자신의 주인 부부에게 데려갔고, 그들은 아이를 양자로 들여 오이디푸스, 곧 〈부어오른 발〉이라 불렀다.\n\n여러 해가 지난 뒤 라이오스가 시종 하나만을 데리고 델포이로 가던 길에, 좁은 길에서 역시 전차를 몰고 오는 한 젊은이와 마주쳤다. 길을 비키라는 그들의 명령에 젊은이가 따르지 않자 시종이 그의 말 한 마리를 죽였고, 분노에 찬 그 낯선 이는 라이오스와 그의 시종을 둘 다 살해했다. 그 젊은이가 바로 오이디푸스였으니, 그는 이렇게 자기도 모르는 사이에 제 아버지를 죽인 자가 되었다.\n\n이 사건이 있은 지 얼마 지나지 않아 테바이 시는 큰길을 들끓게 하는 한 괴물 때문에 고통을 받았다. 그것은 스핑크스라 불렸다. 그것은 사자의 몸뚱이에 상반신은 여인의 모습이었다. 그것은 바위 꼭대기에 웅크리고 앉아 그 길로 오는 모든 나그네를 가로막고는, 그들에게 수수께끼를 내며 그것을 풀 수 있는 자는 무사히 지나가게 하되 풀지 못하는 자는 죽이겠다는 조건을 내걸었다. 아직 누구도 그것을 풀어내는 데 성공하지 못했고, 모두가 죽임을 당했다. 오이디푸스는 이런 무시무시한 이야기에도 주눅 들지 않고 대담하게 시험에 나섰다. 스핑크스가 그에게 물었다. \"아침에는 네 발로 걷고, 한낮에는 두 발로 걸으며, 저녁에는 세 발로 걷는 동물이 무엇이냐?\" 오이디푸스가 대답했다. \"사람이다. 사람은 어린 시절에는 손과 무릎으로 기어다니고, 장년에는 똑바로 서서 걸으며, 노년에는 지팡이의 도움을 받아 걷는다.\" 스핑크스는 자신의 수수께끼가 풀린 것에 너무도 분한 나머지 바위에서 몸을 던져 죽고 말았다.\n\n자신들을 구해 준 데 대한 백성들의 감사가 어찌나 컸던지 그들은 오이디푸스를 왕으로 삼고, 그들의 왕비 이오카스테를 그와 결혼시켰다. 자신의 혈통을 알지 못한 오이디푸스는 이미 제 아버지를 죽인 자가 되어 있었으며, 왕비와 결혼함으로써 제 어머니의 남편이 되었다. 이 끔찍한 일들은 발각되지 않은 채로 있었으나, 마침내 테바이가 기근과 역병에 시달리게 되어 신탁에 물어보자 오이디푸스의 이중의 죄가 밝혀졌다. 이오카스테는 스스로 목숨을 끊었고, 광기에 사로잡힌 오이디푸스는 제 두 눈을 뽑아내고 테바이를 떠나 떠돌았는데, 그에게 충실히 매달린 딸들을 제외한 모든 이들에게 두려움의 대상이자 버림받은 자가 되었으며, 비참한 방랑의 지루한 세월을 보낸 끝에 그 불행한 삶의 종착점을 맞이했다.\n\n페르세우스가 메두사의 머리를 베어 냈을 때, 땅속으로 스며든 그 피에서 날개 달린 말 페가소스가 생겨났다. 아테나가 그를 붙잡아 길들인 뒤 무사이[무사 여신들]에게 선물했다. 무사이의 산인 헬리콘 산에 있는 히포크레네 샘은 그의 발굽이 한 번 차서 생겨난 것이었다.\n\n키마이라는 불을 내뿜는 무시무시한 괴물이었다. 그 몸의 앞부분은 사자와 염소가 결합된 것이었고, 뒷부분은 용의 것이었다. 그것이 리키아에서 큰 재앙을 일으키자, 그곳의 왕 이오바테스는 그것을 처치할 영웅을 찾았다. 바로 그 무렵 그의 궁정에 한 용맹한 젊은 전사가 도착했는데, 그의 이름은 벨레로폰이었다. 그는 이오바테스의 사위인 프로이토스가 보낸 편지를 가져왔는데, 그 편지는 벨레로폰을 천하무적의 영웅이라며 더없이 따뜻한 말로 추천하고 있었으나, 끝에 가서는 장인에게 그를 죽여 달라는 부탁을 덧붙이고 있었다. 그 까닭은 프로이토스가 그를 질투했기 때문이니, 자신의 아내 안테이아가 그 젊은 전사를 지나치게 흠모의 눈길로 바라본다고 의심한 탓이었다. 벨레로폰이 자기도 모르게 자신의 사형 선고문을 지니고 다닌 이 사례에서, 〈벨레로폰의 편지〉라는 표현이 생겨났는데, 이는 어떤 이가 자신에게 해로운 내용이 담긴 글을 자기도 모른 채 전달하게 되는 온갖 종류의 전언을 가리키는 말이다.\n\n이오바테스는 그 편지를 읽고 어찌해야 할지 난감해했으니, 환대의 도리를 저버리고 싶지도 않았으나 사위의 뜻을 들어주고 싶기도 했던 것이다. 그러던 차에 그에게 한 가지 묘안이 떠올랐는데, 벨레로폰을 키마이라와 싸우도록 보내는 것이었다. 벨레로폰은 그 제안을 받아들였으나, 싸움에 나서기 전에 예언자 폴리이도스에게 자문을 구했고, 그는 가능하다면 그 싸움을 위해 페가소스라는 말을 구해 두라고 조언했다. 그 목적을 위해 그는 벨레로폰에게 아테나의 신전에서 하룻밤을 보내라고 일러 주었다. 벨레로폰이 그대로 하고 잠들어 있는 동안 아테나가 그에게 다가와 황금 굴레를 주었다. 그가 깨어나 보니 그 굴레가 손에 남아 있었다. 아테나는 또한 그에게 페이레네 샘에서 물을 마시고 있는 페가소스를 보여 주었고, 굴레를 본 그 날개 달린 준마는 순순히 다가와 잡히기를 자청했다. 벨레로폰이 그 위에 올라타 함께 하늘로 솟아오르자, 이내 키마이라를 찾아내어 그 괴물을 손쉽게 무찔렀다.\n\n키마이라를 정복한 뒤에도 벨레로폰은 우호적이지 않은 그 주인 때문에 더 많은 시험과 노역에 시달렸으나, 페가소스의 도움으로 그 모든 것에서 승리를 거두었고, 마침내 이오바테스는 그 영웅이 신들의 각별한 총애를 받는 자임을 알아보고 자신의 딸을 그와 결혼시켜 왕위의 후계자로 삼았다. 그러나 끝내 벨레로폰은 자신의 오만과 자만으로 인해 신들의 분노를 스스로 불러들였다. 전하는 바에 따르면 그는 심지어 자신의 날개 달린 준마를 타고 하늘로 날아오르려 시도하기까지 했으나, 제우스가 등에 한 마리를 보내어 페가소스를 쏘게 하니 페가소스가 그 등에 탄 자를 내던졌고, 그 결과 벨레로폰은 절름발이에 장님이 되었다. 그 후 벨레로폰은 인간의 길을 피하며 알레이온 평원을 외로이 떠돌다가 비참하게 죽었다.\n\n밀턴은 『실낙원』 제7권의 첫머리에서 벨레로폰을 언급한다.\n\n\"하늘에서 내려오소서, 우라니아여, 그 이름이 당신을 옳게 부르는 것이라면, 그 신성한 목소리를 좇아 나는 올림포스 언덕 위로 솟아오르며, 페가소스의 날갯짓보다 더 높이 날았나이다. 당신에게 이끌려, 나는 하늘들의 하늘로 감히 올라갔으니, 한낱 지상의 손님으로서, 천상의 공기를 들이마셨나이다 (당신이 누그러뜨려 주신 덕분에). 마찬가지로 안전하게 다시 아래로 인도받아 내 본래의 자리로 돌려보내소서. 행여 이 고삐 풀린 날개 달린 준마에서 (일찍이 벨레로폰이 그러했듯, 비록 그는 더 낮은 곳에서였으나), 알레이온 평원에 내동댕이쳐져 떨어질까 두려우니, 그곳에서 길을 잃고 버림받은 채 헤맬까 두렵나이다.\"\n\n영은 그의 『밤의 상념』에서 회의론자를 두고 이렇게 말한다.\n\n\"그 눈먼 생각이 내세를 부정하는 자는, 벨레로폰이여, 그대처럼 자기도 모르게 제 죄상을 지니고 다니나니, 그는 스스로를 단죄한다. 그의 가슴을 읽는 자는 불멸의 삶을 읽거나, 아니면 그곳에서 자연이 제 자식들을 속이려 거짓을 적어 놓았음을 읽으리라. 인간은 거짓으로 지어졌느니.\" 제2권, 12쪽.\n\n페가소스는 무사이의 말이었던 만큼 늘 시인들을 위해 봉사해 왔다. 실러는 페가소스가 어느 가난한 시인에게 팔려가 수레와 쟁기에 매이게 된 사연을 아름다운 이야기로 들려준다. 페가소스는 그런 일에 적합하지 않았고, 그 어리석은 주인은 도무지 그를 부릴 수가 없었다. 그러나 한 젊은이가 나서서 그를 한번 다루어 보겠다고 청했다. 그가 등에 올라타자마자, 처음에는 사나워 보였다가 나중에는 기가 꺾인 듯했던 그 말이 왕처럼, 하나의 정령처럼, 하나의 신처럼 일어서더니, 찬란한 날개를 펼치고 하늘을 향해 솟아올랐다. 우리의 시인 롱펠로 역시 그의 『우리에 갇힌 페가소스』에서 이 유명한 준마의 모험을 기록하고 있다.\n\n셰익스피어는 『헨리 4세』에서 페가소스를 언급하는데, 버넌이 헨리 왕자를 묘사하는 대목이다.\n\n\"나는 보았네, 젊은 해리가 투구를 쓰고, 넓적다리에 정강이받이를 두른 채 늠름하게 무장하고, 날개 달린 메르쿠리우스처럼 땅에서 일어나, 구름에서 천사가 내려와 불같은 페가소스를 돌리고 휘몰며 고귀한 마술(馬術)로 세상을 홀리려는 듯, 그토록 수월하게 안장 위로 뛰어오르는 것을.\"\n\n# THE CENTAURS\n\n이 괴물들은 머리에서 허리까지는 인간의 모습이었으나, 나머지 몸은 말의 것으로 묘사되었다. 고대인들은 말을 너무도 아꼈던 까닭에 말의 본성을 인간의 본성과 결합하는 것을 그리 천박한 조합으로 여기지 않았으며, 따라서 켄타우로스는 고대의 상상 속 괴물들 가운데 어떤 좋은 특성이 부여된 유일한 존재다. 켄타우로스족은 인간의 벗으로 받아들여졌으며, 페이리토오스와 히포다메이아의 결혼식에서도 그들은 손님들 가운데 끼어 있었다. 잔치에서 켄타우로스족 가운데 하나인 에우리티온이 술에 취해 신부에게 폭력을 가하려 들자, 다른 켄타우로스들도 그의 본을 따랐고, 그리하여 끔찍한 싸움이 벌어져 그들 가운데 여럿이 죽임을 당했다. 이것이 바로 그 유명한 라피타이족과 켄타우로스족의 싸움으로, 고대의 조각가들과 시인들이 즐겨 다룬 소재였다.\n\n그러나 모든 켄타우로스가 페이리토오스의 무례한 손님들 같지는 않았다. 케이론은 아폴론과 아르테미스에게 가르침을 받았으며, 사냥과 의술과 음악, 그리고 예언술에 능한 것으로 이름이 높았다. 그리스 이야기에 등장하는 가장 뛰어난 영웅들이 그의 제자였다. 그중에서도 갓난아이 아스클레피오스는 그의 아버지 아폴론에 의해 케이론의 보살핌에 맡겨졌다. 그 현자가 갓난아이를 안고 집으로 돌아오자 그의 딸 오키로에가 그를 맞으러 나왔고, 아이를 보자마자 예언의 가락을 터뜨려(그녀는 예언녀였던 것이다) 그 아이가 이룩하게 될 영광을 예언했다. 아스클레피오스는 자라나서 이름난 의사가 되었으며, 심지어 한번은 죽은 자를 되살리는 데 성공하기까지 했다. 하데스[로마의 〈플루톤〉]가 이를 못마땅하게 여기자, 제우스는 그의 청에 따라 그 대담한 의사를 벼락으로 내리쳐 죽였으나, 그가 죽은 뒤에는 신들의 무리에 받아들였다.\n\n케이론은 모든 켄타우로스 가운데 가장 지혜롭고 가장 의로운 자였으며, 그가 죽자 제우스는 그를 별들 사이에 두어 궁수자리라는 별자리로 삼았다.\n\n# THE PYGMIES\n\n피그미족은 난쟁이들의 종족으로, 약 13인치쯤 되는 길이의 단위인 큐빗을 뜻하는 그리스어 단어에서 그 이름이 비롯되었는데, 이것이 바로 그 종족의 키였다고 전해진다. 그들은 나일 강의 발원지 근처에, 혹은 다른 이들에 따르면 인도에 살았다. 호메로스는 두루미들이 겨울마다 피그미족의 나라로 옮겨 가곤 했다고 우리에게 전하는데, 그 두루미들이 나타나는 것은 그 자그마한 주민들에게 피비린내 나는 전쟁의 신호였으니, 그들은 탐욕스러운 침입자들로부터 자신들의 밀밭을 지키기 위해 무기를 들어야만 했다. 피그미족과 그들의 적인 두루미는 여러 미술 작품의 소재가 되었다.\n\n후대의 작가들은 잠들어 있는 헤라클레스를 발견한 피그미족의 한 부대가 마치 도시를 공격하려는 듯 그를 칠 준비를 했다는 이야기를 들려준다. 그러나 잠에서 깨어난 영웅은 그 자그마한 전사들을 보고 웃으며, 그들 가운데 일부를 자신의 사자 가죽으로 둘둘 말아 에우리스테우스에게 가져갔다.\n\n밀턴은 『실낙원』 제1권에서 피그미족을 직유에 사용한다.\n\n\"……저 인도 너머 산 저편의 피그미족 무리처럼, 혹은 요정 엘프들처럼, 숲가나 샘가에서 벌이는 그들의 한밤중 잔치를 때늦은 어느 농부가 바라보는 (혹은 본다고 꿈꾸는) 동안, 머리 위에서는 달이 심판자로 앉아 땅에 더 가까이 그 창백한 행로를 굴리니, 그들은 흥겨움과 춤에 몰두하여 유쾌한 가락으로 그의 귀를 홀린다. 이내 그의 심장은 기쁨과 두려움으로 두근거린다.\"\n\n# THE GRIFFIN, OR GRYPHON\n\n그리핀은 사자의 몸뚱이에 독수리의 머리와 날개를 지녔으며, 등은 깃털로 덮인 괴물이다. 그것은 새들처럼 둥지를 틀고, 그 속에 알 대신 마노(瑪瑙)를 낳는다. 그것은 긴 발톱과 갈고리발톱을 지녔는데, 그 크기가 어찌나 큰지 그 나라 사람들은 그것으로 술잔을 만든다. 인도가 그리핀의 본고장으로 정해졌다. 그것들은 산에서 황금을 발견하여 그것으로 둥지를 짓는데, 그런 까닭에 그 둥지는 사냥꾼들에게 몹시 탐나는 것이어서, 그것들은 둥지를 부지런히 지켜야만 했다. 그것들은 본능에 따라 묻힌 보물이 어디 있는지 알아냈고, 약탈자들을 멀리 떼어 놓으려고 온 힘을 다했다. 그리핀이 번성하던 곳의 주민인 아리마스포이족은 스키티아에 사는 외눈박이 종족이었다.\n\n밀턴은 『실낙원』 제2권에서 그리핀에게서 직유 하나를 빌려 온다.\n\n\"그리핀 한 마리가 황야를 가로질러, 날개 달린 행로로, 언덕과 황량한 골짜기를 넘어, 그 깨어 있는 감시를 틈타 몰래 제가 지키던 황금을 훔쳐 간 아리마스포이족을 뒤쫓듯이.\" 등등. ————\n\n# THE GOLDEN FLEECE\n\n아주 먼 옛날에 테살리아에는 아타마스와 네펠레라는 이름의 왕과 왕비가 살고 있었다. 두 사람에게는 아들과 딸, 둘의 자녀가 있었다. 그러나 얼마 후 아타마스는 아내에게 무관심해져 그녀를 내쫓고 다른 여인을 맞아들였다. 네펠레는 새어머니의 영향으로 자기 자녀들에게 위험이 닥칠 것을 염려하여 그들을 그녀의 손이 닿지 않는 곳으로 보내기 위한 방책을 마련했다. 헤르메스[로마의 〈메르쿠리우스〉]가 그녀를 도와 황금 양털을 지닌 숫양 한 마리를 주었고, 그녀는 그 숫양이 두 아이를 안전한 곳으로 데려다 주리라 믿고 그 위에 두 아이를 태웠다. 숫양은 아이들을 등에 태운 채 공중으로 솟아올라 동쪽을 향해 나아갔는데, 유럽과 아시아를 가르는 해협을 건너던 중 헬레라는 이름의 소녀가 숫양의 등에서 바다로 떨어졌으며, 그 바다는 그녀의 이름을 따서 헬레스폰토스라고 불리게 되었으니, 지금의 다르다넬스 해협이다. 숫양은 계속 나아가 흑해 동쪽 기슭에 자리한 콜키스 왕국에 이르렀고, 그곳에 소년 프릭소스를 무사히 내려놓았으며, 그 나라의 왕인 아이에테스는 그를 융숭하게 맞아들였다. 프릭소스는 그 숫양을 제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]에게 제물로 바치고 황금 양털을 아이에테스에게 주었으며, 아이에테스는 그것을 신성한 숲 속에 두어 잠들지 않는 용의 보호를 받게 했다.\n\n테살리아에는 아타마스의 왕국에 가까운 또 다른 왕국이 있었는데, 그의 친척이 다스리고 있었다. 아이손 왕은 통치의 근심에 지친 나머지 자기 왕관을 동생 펠리아스에게 넘겨주되, 아이손의 아들 이아손이 미성년인 동안에만 그것을 보유한다는 조건을 달았다. 이아손이 성장하여 숙부에게 와서 왕관을 요구하자, 펠리아스는 그것을 기꺼이 내어줄 듯이 꾸미면서도, 동시에 그 젊은이에게 황금 양털을 찾으러 떠나는 영광스러운 모험을 권했는데, 그 양털은 콜키스 왕국에 있음이 익히 알려져 있었고, 펠리아스가 주장하기로는 본디 그들 가문의 정당한 소유물이었다. 이아손은 그 생각에 흡족해하며 곧바로 원정 준비에 들어갔다. 그 시절 그리스인들에게 알려진 항해의 유일한 형태란 나무줄기를 파내어 만든 작은 배나 카누뿐이었으니, 이아손이 아르고스를 고용하여 쉰 명을 태울 수 있는 배를 짓게 했을 때 그것은 어마어마한 사업으로 여겨졌다. 그러나 그 일은 완수되었고, 배는 만든 이의 이름을 따서 〈아르고〉라 불렸다. 이아손은 그리스의 모험심 강한 젊은이들 모두에게 초대장을 보냈고, 곧 대담한 젊은이들의 무리를 이끄는 우두머리가 되었는데, 그들 가운데 많은 이가 훗날 그리스의 영웅과 반신반인 사이에서 이름을 떨치게 되었다. 헤라클레스, 테세우스, 오르페우스, 네스토르도 그들 가운데 있었다. 이들은 그들의 배 이름을 따서 아르고나우타이[아르고호 선원들]라고 불린다.\n\n영웅들을 태운 〈아르고〉호는 테살리아의 기슭을 떠나 렘노스 섬에 들렀다가, 거기서 미시아로 건너갔고, 다시 트라키아로 갔다. 이곳에서 그들은 현자 피네우스를 만나 앞으로의 항로에 관한 가르침을 받았다. 에욱세이노스[흑해]의 입구는 작은 바위섬 둘에 가로막혀 있었던 듯한데, 그 섬들은 수면 위를 떠다니다가 출렁이고 솟구치는 가운데 이따금 서로 맞부딪쳐, 그 사이에 끼이는 무엇이든 짓이겨 가루로 만들어 버렸다. 그 섬들은 심플레가데스, 곧 맞부딪치는 섬들이라 불렸다. 피네우스는 아르고나우타이에게 이 위험한 해협을 통과하는 방법을 일러 주었다. 그들은 그 섬들에 이르자 비둘기 한 마리를 풀어놓았는데, 비둘기는 바위들 사이로 길을 잡아 무사히 통과하였고 꽁지깃 몇 개만을 잃었을 뿐이었다. 이아손과 그의 부하들은 바위가 되튀는 순간의 호기를 놓치지 않고 힘차게 노를 저어 무사히 빠져나갔으니, 비록 섬들이 그들의 뒤에서 맞닫혀 실제로 고물을 스치기는 했어도 그러했다. 이제 그들은 바다의 동쪽 끝에 다다를 때까지 기슭을 따라 노를 저어 가서 콜키스 왕국에 상륙했다.\n\n이아손은 콜키스의 왕 아이에테스에게 자기 용건을 알렸고, 왕은 이아손이 청동 발굽을 지니고 불을 내뿜는 황소 두 마리에게 쟁기를 메우고, 카드모스가 죽인 용의 이빨을 뿌린다는 조건을 충족시키면 황금 양털을 내어주겠노라고 동의했다. 그 이빨에서는 무장한 군사들이 솟아나리라는 것이 익히 알려져 있었고, 그들은 자기를 만들어 낸 자에게 무기를 겨눌 것이었다. 이아손은 그 조건을 받아들였고, 그 시도를 행할 날이 정해졌다. 그러나 그에 앞서 그는 왕의 딸 메데이아에게 자기 사정을 호소할 방도를 찾아냈다. 그는 그녀에게 혼인을 약속했고, 두 사람이 헤카테의 제단 앞에 섰을 때 그 여신을 자기 맹세의 증인으로 불렀다. 메데이아는 마음이 기울었고, 그녀가 강력한 마법사였던 까닭에 그녀의 도움으로 그는 부적 하나를 얻어, 그것으로 불을 내뿜는 황소들의 숨결과 무장한 군사들의 무기를 무사히 맞설 수 있게 되었다.\n\n정해진 때가 되자 사람들은 아레스[로마의 〈마르스〉]의 숲에 모였고, 왕은 자기 옥좌에 앉았으며, 군중은 언덕 비탈을 뒤덮었다. 청동 발굽의 황소들이 콧구멍에서 불길을 내뿜으며 달려드니 그 불길은 지나는 자리마다 풀을 태워 버렸다. 그 소리는 용광로의 포효 같았고, 연기는 생석회에 물을 부었을 때 피어오르는 것과 같았다. 이아손은 대담하게 그것들을 맞으러 나아갔다. 그의 벗들, 곧 그리스에서 뽑힌 영웅들은 그를 바라보며 몸을 떨었다. 그는 타오르는 숨결에도 아랑곳없이 목소리로 그것들의 분노를 달래고, 두려움 없는 손으로 그 목덜미를 쓰다듬었으며, 능숙하게 멍에를 씌워 쟁기를 끌게 했다. 콜키스인들은 경악했고, 그리스인들은 기뻐 환호했다. 이아손은 다음으로 용의 이빨을 뿌리고 쟁기로 갈아 묻었다. 그러자 곧 무장한 군사들의 무리가 솟아났는데, 놀랍게도! 그들은 땅 위로 올라오기가 무섭게 무기를 휘두르며 이아손에게 달려들었다. 그리스인들은 자기네 영웅을 위해 떨었고, 그에게 안전의 길을 마련해 주고 그 사용법을 가르쳐 준 메데이아조차도 두려움에 새파랗게 질렸다. 이아손은 한동안 칼과 방패로 공격자들을 막아 냈으나, 그 수가 압도적임을 깨닫고 메데이아가 가르쳐 준 부적을 써서 돌 하나를 집어 적들 한가운데로 던졌다. 그들은 즉시 서로에게 무기를 겨누었고, 곧 용의 종자들 가운데 살아남은 자는 하나도 없게 되었다. 그리스인들은 자기네 영웅을 끌어안았고, 메데이아도 감히 그럴 수 있었다면 그를 끌어안았을 것이다.\n\n이제 남은 일은 양털을 지키는 용을 잠재우는 것이었으니, 이는 메데이아가 마련해 준 조제약 몇 방울을 그 위에 뿌림으로써 이루어졌다. 그 냄새를 맡자 용은 분노를 누그러뜨리며 잠시 꼼짝 않고 서 있다가, 일찍이 한 번도 감긴 적이 없던 그 크고 둥근 눈을 감고 옆으로 몸을 돌리더니 깊이 잠들어 버렸다. 이아손은 양털을 움켜쥐고 벗들과 메데이아를 동반하여, 아이에테스 왕이 그들의 출발을 막기 전에 서둘러 배로 달려가 테살리아로 돌아가는 길을 재촉했으며, 그곳에 무사히 도착하여 이아손은 양털을 펠리아스에게 넘겨주고 〈아르고〉호를 포세이돈[로마의 〈넵투누스〉]에게 봉헌했다. 그 양털이 그 후 어찌 되었는지는 우리도 알지 못하나, 어쩌면 그것은 다른 많은 황금 전리품과 마찬가지로, 결국 그것을 손에 넣느라 치른 수고에 값하지 못하는 것이었음이 드러났을지도 모른다.\n\n후대의 한 저술가가 말하기를, 이것은 그러한 신화적 이야기 가운데 하나로서, 비록 한 무더기의 허구에 뒤덮여 있기는 하나 그 밑바탕에 진실이 깔려 있다고 믿을 만한 근거가 있는 부류에 속한다고 한다. 그것은 아마도 최초의 중요한 해상 원정이었을 터인데, 역사를 통해 우리가 알고 있듯이 모든 민족의 그러한 첫 시도가 그러하였듯이, 아마도 반쯤 해적질에 가까운 성격을 띠었을 것이다. 만일 풍부한 약탈물이 그 결과였다면, 그것만으로도 황금 양털이라는 발상을 불러일으키기에 족했을 것이다.\n\n박학한 신화학자 브라이언트가 내놓은 또 다른 견해는, 이것이 노아와 방주 이야기가 변질되어 전해진 전승이라는 것이다. 〈아르고〉라는 이름이 이를 뒷받침하는 듯하며, 비둘기 일화 역시 또 하나의 방증이 된다.\n\n포프는 그의 「성 케킬리아 축일에 부치는 송가」에서 〈아르고〉호의 진수와, 그가 트라키아 사람이라 부르는 오르페우스의 음악이 지닌 힘을 이렇게 기린다.\n\n\"그리하여 최초의 대담한 배가 바다에 맞섰을 때, 트라키아 사람은 고물 높이 서서 가락을 돋우었고, 아르고호는 제 일가붙이 나무들이 펠리온 산에서 바다로 내려오는 것을 보았네. 황홀에 빠진 반신반인들이 둘러섰고, 그 소리에 사람들은 영웅이 되었네.\"\n\n다이어의 시 「양털」에는 〈아르고〉호와 그 선원들에 관한 기록이 있는데, 이 원초적 해상 모험의 모습을 잘 그려 낸다.\n\n\"아이게해의 기슭 곳곳에서 용맹한 이들이 모여들었으니, 저 빛나는 쌍둥이 카스토르와 폴리데우케스, 가락 고운 음유시인 오르페우스, 바람처럼 빠른 제테스와 칼라이스, 힘센 헤라클레스와 이름난 숱한 우두머리들. 깊은 이올코스의 모래 기슭에 그들이 모여들어, 갑옷을 번쩍이며 공훈을 열망하였네. 이윽고 월계수 밧줄과 거대한 돌을 갑판 위로 들어 올려 배의 닻줄을 풀었으니, 그 배의 놀랍도록 긴 용골은 솜씨 좋은 아르고스의 손이 저 자랑스러운 시도를 위해 빚어낸 것이었네. 그리고 길게 뻗은 용골에는 높다란 돛대가 세워지고 돛은 가득 부풀었으니, 우두머리들에게는 낯선 물건이었네. 이제 처음으로, 처음으로 그들은 바다 물결 위를 헤쳐 나가는 대담한 키잡이 노릇을 배웠으니, 케이론의 솜씨가 천구를 표시해 둔 그대로 황금빛 별들의 인도를 받으며,\"등등.\n\n헤라클레스는 미시아에서 원정대를 떠났으니, 그가 사랑하던 소년 힐라스가 물을 길러 갔다가 그 미모에 매혹된 샘의 님프들에게 붙들려 그곳에 머물게 되었기 때문이다. 헤라클레스는 그 소년을 찾으러 갔는데, 그가 자리를 비운 사이 〈아르고〉호는 바다로 나아가 그를 두고 떠났다. 무어는 자기 노래 가운데 하나에서 이 일화를 아름답게 암시한다.\n\n\"힐라스가 물동이를 들고 샘으로 보내어졌을 때, 빛으로 가득한 들판을 지나 놀이로 가득한 마음으로, 소년은 풀밭과 언덕을 가벼이 거닐며, 길가의 꽃들에 빠져 제 일을 잊고 말았네. 그리하여 나와 같은 많은 이가, 젊은 날에 철학의 성소 곁을 흐르는 샘물을 맛보았어야 했건만, 가장자리의 꽃들에 시간을 헛되이 흘려보내고, 제 가벼운 물동이를 내 것처럼 텅 빈 채로 남겨 두었네.\"\n\n황금 양털을 되찾은 데 대한 기쁨이 한창인 가운데, 이아손은 한 가지가 빠져 있음을 느꼈으니, 바로 그의 아버지 아이손이 그 자리에 없다는 것이었다. 아이손은 늙고 쇠약하여 그 기쁨에 함께하지 못했다. 이아손이 메데이아에게 말했다. \"내 아내여, 그 위력이 나를 돕는 데 그토록 강력함을 내가 보아 온 그대의 마법으로, 내게 한 가지 봉사를 더 베풀어 주었으면 하오. 내 목숨에서 몇 해를 떼어 내 아버지의 목숨에 보태 주오.\" 메데이아가 대답했다. \"그런 대가를 치르고 그리할 수는 없습니다. 그러나 제 마법이 효험을 발한다면, 당신의 목숨을 줄이지 않고도 그분의 목숨을 늘려 드리겠습니다.\" 다음 보름달이 뜨자 그녀는 만물이 잠든 사이에 홀로 밖으로 나섰다. 한 점 바람도 나뭇잎을 흔들지 않았고, 모든 것이 고요했다. 그녀는 별들에게, 그리고 달에게 주문을 외었고, 저승의 여신 헤카테에게, 또 마법에 강력한 효력을 지닌 식물들을 그 권능으로 자라나게 하는 대지의 여신 텔루스에게 주문을 외었다. 그녀는 숲과 동굴의 신들, 산과 골짜기의 신들, 호수와 강의 신들, 바람과 안개의 신들을 불러내었다. 그녀가 말하는 동안 별들은 더욱 밝게 빛났고, 이윽고 나는 뱀들이 끄는 수레 한 대가 공중에서 내려왔다. 그녀는 거기에 올라타 높이 실려 먼 지방으로 길을 잡았으니, 그곳에는 그녀가 자기 목적에 맞게 고를 줄 아는 강력한 식물들이 자라고 있었다. 그녀는 아흐레 밤을 그 탐색에 들였고, 그동안 자기 궁전의 문 안에도, 어떤 지붕 아래에도 들지 않았으며, 모든 인간과의 교류를 피했다.\n* 헤카테: 저승의 여신.\n\n다음으로 그녀는 제단 둘을 쌓았는데, 하나는 헤카테에게, 다른 하나는 젊음의 여신 헤베에게 바치는 것이었으며, 검은 양 한 마리를 제물로 바치고 우유와 포도주를 헌주로 부었다. 그녀는 하데스[로마의 〈플루토〉]와 그가 납치해 온 신부에게 노인의 목숨을 서둘러 거두어 가지 말아 달라고 간청했다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 아이손을 불러오게 하여, 주문으로 그를 깊은 잠에 빠뜨린 뒤 죽은 사람처럼 약초 침상 위에 눕혔다. 이아손과 그 밖의 모든 이는 그곳에서 멀리 떨어져 있게 했으니, 신성치 못한 눈이 그녀의 신비를 들여다보지 못하게 하기 위함이었다. 그런 다음 그녀는 머리카락을 풀어 헤친 채 제단 둘레를 세 번 돌고, 불타는 잔가지들을 피에 적셔 그 위에 올려놓아 타게 했다. 그러는 사이 안에 든 것과 함께 가마솥이 준비되었다. 그녀는 그 안에 마법의 약초들을 넣었는데, 쓰디쓴 즙을 지닌 씨앗과 꽃들, 먼 동방에서 가져온 돌들, 사방을 에워싼 대양의 기슭에서 가져온 모래, 달빛 아래 모은 흰 서리, 부엉이의 머리와 날개, 그리고 늑대의 내장도 함께 넣었다. 거기에 거북의 등딱지 조각들과, 끈질긴 생명력을 지닌 짐승인 수사슴의 간을 더했고, 사람의 아홉 세대를 살아 넘긴다는 까마귀의 머리와 부리도 더했다. 이것들에 더해 \"이름조차 없는\" 다른 많은 것을 함께 넣어 그녀는 자기가 의도한 일을 위해 끓였으며, 마른 올리브 가지로 그것들을 휘저었다. 그런데 보라! 그 가지를 꺼내자 곧바로 푸르러졌고, 머지않아 잎과 어린 올리브가 무성하게 뒤덮였다. 그리고 그 액체가 끓어 거품을 일으키며 이따금 넘쳐흘렀는데, 그 방울이 떨어진 곳마다 풀이 봄철과 같은 푸름으로 돋아났다.\n\n모든 준비가 갖추어진 것을 보고, 메데이아는 노인의 목을 베어 그 피를 모조리 흘려보낸 뒤, 그의 입과 상처에 가마솥의 즙을 부어 넣었다. 그가 그것을 완전히 빨아들이자마자, 그의 머리카락과 수염은 흰빛을 벗고 젊음의 검은빛을 띠었으며, 창백함과 야윔은 사라졌고, 혈관은 피로 가득 찼으며, 사지에는 활력과 건장함이 돌았다. 아이손은 스스로에게 놀라며, 사십 년 전 자기 젊은 날에 지금의 자기와 같은 모습이었음을 떠올렸다.\n\n메데이아는 이곳에서는 자기 마법을 선한 목적에 썼으나, 다른 경우에는 그렇지 않았으니, 그때는 그것을 복수의 도구로 삼았다. 독자들은 펠리아스가 이아손의 왕위를 찬탈한 숙부로서, 그를 자기 왕국에서 내쫓아 둔 자임을 기억할 것이다. 그러나 그에게도 얼마간 좋은 자질이 있었음이 분명하니, 그의 딸들이 그를 사랑했고, 메데이아가 아이손에게 베푼 일을 보고는 그녀가 자기 아버지에게도 같은 일을 해 주기를 바랐기 때문이다. 메데이아는 응하는 척하며 전과 같이 자기 가마솥을 마련했다. 그녀의 요청에 따라 늙은 양 한 마리가 끌려와 가마솥에 던져졌다. 곧 솥 안에서 양 우는 소리가 들렸고, 뚜껑을 열자 새끼 양 한 마리가 뛰쳐나와 깡충거리며 풀밭으로 달아났다. 펠리아스의 딸들은 그 시연을 기뻐하며 보고, 자기 아버지가 같은 시술을 받을 날을 정했다. 그러나 메데이아는 그를 위해서는 자기 가마솥을 아주 다르게 마련했다. 그녀는 그 안에 물과 몇 가지 평범한 약초만을 넣었다. 밤이 되자 그녀는 자매들과 함께 늙은 왕의 침실로 들어갔는데, 왕과 그의 호위병들은 메데이아가 그들에게 건 주문의 영향으로 깊이 잠들어 있었다. 딸들은 무기를 빼어 든 채 침상 곁에 섰으나, 차마 내려치지 못하고 망설였고, 마침내 메데이아가 그들의 우유부단함을 꾸짖었다. 그러자 그들은 얼굴을 돌린 채 마구잡이로 내려쳐 자기네 무기로 그를 쳤다. 그는 잠에서 깨어나 소리쳤다. \"내 딸들아, 너희가 무엇을 하는 것이냐? 너희 아비를 죽이려느냐?\" 그들의 가슴은 무너져 내렸고 무기는 손에서 떨어졌으나, 메데이아가 그에게 치명상을 입혀 그가 더 말하지 못하게 했다.\n\n그러고 나서 그들은 그를 가마솥에 넣었고, 메데이아는 그들이 자기의 배신을 알아채기 전에 뱀이 끄는 수레를 타고 서둘러 떠났으니, 그러지 않았다면 그들의 복수가 끔찍했을 것이다. 그러나 그녀는 무사히 달아났으되, 자기 죄의 열매를 거의 누리지 못했다. 그녀가 그토록 많은 일을 해 준 이아손이 코린토스의 공주 크레우사와 혼인하고자 메데이아를 내쫓았기 때문이다. 그녀는 그의 배은망덕에 격분하여 신들에게 복수를 빌었고, 독을 묻힌 예복을 신부에게 선물로 보냈으며, 그런 다음 자기 자식들을 죽이고 궁전에 불을 지른 뒤, 뱀이 끄는 수레에 올라 아테네로 달아났다. 그곳에서 그녀는 테세우스의 아버지인 아이게우스 왕과 혼인했으니, 우리는 그 영웅의 모험에 이르렀을 때 그녀를 다시 만나게 될 것이다.\n\n메데이아의 주문은 독자에게 「맥베스」에 나오는 마녀들의 주문을 떠오르게 할 것이다. 다음 시구들은 그 고대의 본보기를 가장 인상 깊게 되살리는 듯하다.\n\n\"가마솥 둘레를 돌아라. 독 묻은 내장을 던져 넣어라. . . . . . . . 늪에 사는 뱀의 토막을 가마솥에 끓여 익혀라. 영원의 눈알에 개구리의 발가락, 박쥐의 털에 개의 혀, 살무사의 갈래 혀에 발 없는 도마뱀의 침, 도마뱀의 다리에 올빼미의 날개, . . . . . . . 게걸스러운 짠 바다 상어의 위, 어둠 속에서 캐낸 독미나리 뿌리,\"등등. —「맥베스」 제4막 제1장\n그리고 또한 이러하다.\n\n맥베스. — 너희가 하는 것이 무엇이냐? 마녀들. — 이름조차 없는 짓이옵니다.\n\n메데이아에 관해서는 마법사를 두고 기록하기에도 너무나 끔찍한 또 다른 이야기가 있는데, 마법사란 고금의 시인들이 온갖 정도의 잔혹함을 으레 덧씌워 온 부류의 인물들이다. 콜키스에서 달아날 때 그녀는 어린 동생 압시르토스를 데리고 갔다. 아이에테스의 추격선들이 아르고나우타이를 따라잡고 있음을 알아채자, 그녀는 그 소년을 죽이게 하여 그 사지를 바다 위에 흩뿌리게 했다. 아이에테스는 그곳에 이르러 살해된 아들의 이 비통한 흔적들을 발견했으나, 그가 흩어진 조각들을 거두어 명예로운 매장을 치러 주느라 지체하는 사이 아르고나우타이는 달아났다.\n\n캠벨의 시집에는 비극 「메데이아」의 합창 가운데 하나를 옮긴 것이 실려 있는데, 거기서 시인 에우리피데스는 그 기회를 빌려 자기 고향 도시 아테네에 열렬한 찬사를 바쳤다. 그것은 이렇게 시작한다.\n\n\"오 초췌한 여왕이여! 그대는 핏줄의 피에 흠뻑 젖은 그 이글거리는 수레를 몰아 아테네로 향하는가. 아니면 평화와 정의가 영원토록 깃든 곳에 그대의 저주받은 혈육 살해를 숨기려 하는가?\" ————\n\n# MELEAGER AND ATALANTA\n\n아르고호 원정의 영웅 가운데 한 사람이 멜레아그로스였으니, 그는 칼리돈의 왕과 왕비인 오이네우스와 알타이아의 아들이었다. 알타이아는 아들을 낳았을 때 세 운명의 여신을 보았는데, 그들은 숙명의 실을 잣는 동안 이 아이의 생명이 화롯불 위에서 타고 있는 장작개비보다 더 오래가지 못할 것이라고 예언했다. 알타이아는 그 장작개비를 붙잡아 불을 끄고는, 멜레아그로스가 소년기와 청년기를 지나 장년에 이르도록 여러 해에 걸쳐 그것을 정성껏 간직했다. 그런데 마침 오이네우스가 신들에게 제물을 바칠 때 아르테미스[로마의 〈디아나〉]에게 마땅히 바쳐야 할 경의를 빠뜨리는 일이 있었고, 여신은 그 소홀함에 분노하여 어마어마하게 큰 멧돼지 한 마리를 보내 칼리돈의 들판을 짓밟게 했다. 그 두 눈은 피와 불처럼 빛났고, 그 뻣뻣한 털은 위협하는 창처럼 곤두섰으며, 그 엄니는 인도 코끼리의 그것과도 같았다. 자라나던 곡식은 짓밟혔고, 포도나무와 올리브나무는 쑥대밭이 되었으며, 양 떼와 소 떼는 살육을 일삼는 적 앞에서 어지러이 흩어져 내몰렸다. 흔한 도움으로는 모두 헛된 듯 보였다. 그리하여 멜레아그로스는 그리스의 영웅들을 불러 이 게걸스러운 괴물을 잡으려는 대담한 사냥에 함께하자고 청했다. 테세우스와 그의 벗 페이리토오스, 이아손, 훗날 아킬레우스의 아버지가 된 펠레우스, 아이아스의 아버지 텔라몬, 그리고 당시에는 젊은이였으나 노년에 이르러 트로이아 전쟁에서 아킬레우스 및 아이아스와 함께 무기를 든 네스토르, 이들을 비롯한 수많은 이가 그 일에 합세했다. 그들과 더불어 아르카디아의 왕 이아시오스의 딸 아탈란테가 왔다. 윤이 나는 황금 죔쇠가 그녀의 옷을 여미었고, 상아 화살통이 그녀의 왼쪽 어깨에 걸려 있었으며, 왼손에는 활을 들고 있었다. 그녀의 얼굴은 여인의 아름다움과 무인다운 젊은이의 더없는 기품을 한데 아울렀다. 멜레아그로스는 그녀를 보고 사랑에 빠졌다.\n\n그런데 그들은 어느새 괴물의 굴 가까이에 이르렀다. 그들은 나무에서 나무로 튼튼한 그물을 펼쳤고, 개들을 풀어놓았으며, 풀숲에서 사냥감의 발자국을 찾으려 했다. 숲에서 늪지로 내려가는 비탈이 있었다. 여기서 갈대 사이에 누워 있던 멧돼지가 추격자들의 함성을 듣고는 그들을 향해 뛰쳐나왔다. 하나둘씩 쓰러지고 목숨을 잃었다. 이아손이 성공을 비는 기도를 아르테미스에게 올리며 창을 던졌으나, 호의를 베푼 여신은 그 무기가 닿기는 하되 상처를 입히지는 못하게 하여, 날아가는 도중에 창끝의 쇠를 거두어 버렸다. 네스토르는 공격을 받자 나무의 가지들 속에서 안전을 찾아 그리로 피했다. 텔라몬은 돌진했으나 튀어나온 나무뿌리에 발이 걸려 앞으로 고꾸라졌다. 그러나 마침내 아탈란테의 화살 한 대가 처음으로 괴물의 피를 맛보았다. 가벼운 상처였으나 멜레아그로스는 그것을 보고 기뻐하며 외쳐 알렸다. 여인에게 주어진 칭찬에 시기심이 인 안카이오스는 자기 자신의 용맹을 큰 소리로 떠벌리며, 멧돼지와 그것을 보낸 여신을 한꺼번에 비웃었다. 그러나 그가 돌진하자 격노한 짐승은 그에게 치명상을 입혀 그를 쓰러뜨렸다. 테세우스가 창을 던졌으나 튀어나온 나뭇가지에 빗나가고 말았다. 이아손의 투창은 목표를 빗맞히고 도리어 자기네 개 한 마리를 죽였다. 그러나 멜레아그로스는 한 차례 헛손질을 한 뒤, 자기 창을 괴물의 옆구리에 박아 넣고는 돌진하여 거듭 내리쳐 그것을 끝장냈다.\n\n그러자 둘러선 이들에게서 함성이 일었다. 그들은 승리자에게 축하의 말을 건네며 그의 손을 만지려고 몰려들었다. 멜레아그로스는 쓰러진 멧돼지의 머리에 발을 올리고는 아탈란테에게로 돌아서서, 자기 성공의 전리품인 그 머리와 거친 가죽을 그녀에게 내려주었다. 그러나 이를 두고 시기심이 나머지 사람들을 다툼으로 몰아갔다. 멜레아그로스의 어머니 쪽 형제인 플렉시포스와 톡세우스가 누구보다도 앞장서 그 선물에 반대하며, 처녀가 받은 전리품을 빼앗았다. 멜레아그로스는 자기에게 가해진 부당함에 분노가 타올랐고, 자기가 사랑하는 여인에게 가해진 모욕에는 더욱 분노하여, 혈육의 정을 잊고 검을 휘둘러 그 무뢰한들의 심장에 박아 넣었다.\n\n알타이아가 아들의 승리를 기리는 감사의 예물을 신전으로 가져갈 때, 살해당한 형제들의 시신이 그녀의 눈에 들어왔다. 그녀는 비명을 지르고 가슴을 쳤으며, 기쁨의 옷을 슬픔의 옷으로 서둘러 갈아입었다. 그러나 그 일을 저지른 자가 누구인지 알려지자, 슬픔은 아들에게 복수하려는 모진 욕망에 자리를 내주었다. 한때 불길에서 건져 낸 그 숙명의 장작개비, 운명의 여신들이 멜레아그로스의 생명과 엮어 놓은 그 장작개비를 그녀는 꺼내 들고 불을 피우라고 명했다. 그러고는 네 번이나 그 장작개비를 장작더미 위에 올리려 했으나, 네 번이나 아들에게 파멸을 가져온다는 생각에 몸서리치며 물러섰다. 어머니의 마음과 누이의 마음이 그녀 안에서 다투었다. 한순간 그녀는 자기가 작정한 행위를 떠올리며 핏기가 가셨다가, 다시 한순간 아들의 행위에 분노가 치밀어 얼굴이 붉어졌다. 바람에 한쪽으로 떠밀리고 조류에 그 반대쪽으로 떠밀리는 배처럼, 알타이아의 마음은 갈피를 잡지 못한 채 매달려 있었다. 그러나 이제 누이가 어머니를 이기게 되니, 그녀는 그 숙명의 나무를 쥐고서 이렇게 말하기 시작했다. \"돌아보소서, 복수의 여신들이여, 징벌의 여신들이여! 돌아보시어 내가 바치는 제물을 보소서! 죄는 죄로 갚아야 합니다. 오이네우스는 승리한 아들을 두고 기뻐하는데, 테스티오스의 집안은 황폐해져야 한단 말입니까? 그러나, 아아! 나는 무슨 짓을 향해 떠밀려 가고 있는가? 형제들이여, 어미의 나약함을 용서하시오! 내 손이 말을 듣지 않는구나. 그 아이는 죽어 마땅하나, 내가 그 아이를 죽이는 것은 마땅치 않다. 그러나 그러면 그 아이는 살아남아 의기양양하게 칼리돈을 다스리고, 너희, 내 형제들은 원수도 갚지 못한 채 망령들 사이를 떠돌란 말이냐? 안 된다! 너는 내가 준 선물로 살아왔으니, 이제 네 죄로 인하여 죽어라. 두 번 너에게 준 생명을 돌려다오. 처음은 네가 태어났을 때요, 다음은 내가 이 장작개비를 불길에서 낚아챘을 때다. 아, 그때 네가 죽었더라면! 아아! 이 승리는 사악하구나. 그러나, 형제들이여, 너희가 이겼다.\" 그러고는 얼굴을 돌리며, 그녀는 그 숙명의 나무를 타오르는 장작더미 위에 던졌다.\n\n그것은 죽음의 신음을 토했다, 혹은 토하는 듯했다. 멀리 떨어져 있어 그 까닭을 알지 못하던 멜레아그로스는 문득 격통을 느꼈다. 그는 불타올랐고, 오직 용감한 긍지로써 자신을 무너뜨리는 그 고통을 이겨 냈다. 그는 다만 피도 흘리지 않고 영예도 없는 죽음으로 스러진다는 것을 슬퍼했다. 마지막 숨을 거두며 그는 늙은 아버지와 형제와 다정한 누이들을, 사랑하는 아탈란테를, 그리고 알지 못한 채 자기 운명의 원인이 된 어머니를 불렀다. 불길이 거세지자 그와 더불어 영웅의 고통도 거세졌다. 이제 둘 다 잦아들었다. 이제 둘 다 꺼졌다. 장작개비는 재가 되었고, 멜레아그로스의 생명은 떠도는 바람 속으로 흩어져 사라졌다.\n\n알타이아는 그 일을 마치고 나서 제 손으로 제 목숨을 끊었다. 멜레아그로스의 누이들은 걷잡을 수 없는 슬픔으로 오라비를 애도했고, 마침내 아르테미스는 한때 자기 분노를 불러일으켰던 그 집안의 비탄을 가엾이 여겨 그들을 새로 바꾸어 놓았다.\n\n이토록 많은 슬픔의 죄 없는 원인이 된 이는 한 처녀였으니, 그 얼굴은 소녀치고는 사내아이 같다 할 만하면서도 사내아이치고는 너무도 소녀다웠다고 할 수 있을 것이다. 그녀의 운명은 이미 점쳐져 있었는데, 그 내용은 이러했다. \"아탈란테여, 결혼하지 마라. 결혼은 너의 파멸이 되리라.\" 이 신탁에 겁을 먹은 그녀는 남자들과 어울리기를 멀리하고 사냥의 즐거움에 자신을 바쳤다. 모든 구혼자에게(그녀에게는 구혼자가 많았기 때문이다) 그녀는 대개 그들의 성가신 구애에서 벗어나는 데 효험이 있는 조건을 내걸었으니, \"나는 달리기에서 나를 이기는 자의 차지가 되리라. 그러나 시도했다가 실패하는 모든 이에게는 죽음이 그 대가가 되어야 한다\"는 것이었다. 이토록 가혹한 조건에도 불구하고 더러는 시도하려 들었다. 히포메네스가 그 달리기의 심판을 맡기로 되어 있었다. \"아내 하나를 얻겠다고 그토록 큰 위험을 무릅쓸 만큼 무모한 자가 과연 있을 수 있단 말인가?\" 하고 그가 말했다. 그러나 그녀가 달리기를 위해 겉옷을 벗어 놓는 것을 보자 그는 생각을 바꾸고 이렇게 말했다. \"젊은이들이여, 용서하시오. 나는 그대들이 다투고 있는 상이 무엇인지 몰랐소.\" 그는 그들을 둘러보며 그들 모두가 패하기를 바랐고, 조금이라도 이길 듯한 자가 있으면 시기심으로 가슴이 부풀어 올랐다. 그가 이런 생각에 잠겨 있는 동안 처녀가 앞으로 내달렸다. 그녀는 달리는 모습이 어느 때보다 아름다워 보였다. 산들바람이 그녀의 발에 날개를 달아 주는 듯했고, 머리카락은 어깨 너머로 흩날렸으며, 옷자락의 화사한 술이 그녀의 등 뒤에서 펄럭였다. 진홍빛 휘장이 대리석 벽에 드리우는 듯한 발그레한 빛이 그녀의 흰 살결을 물들였다. 모든 경쟁자가 뒤처졌고, 가차 없이 죽임을 당했다. 히포메네스는 이 결과에 주눅 들지 않고 처녀에게 눈길을 고정한 채 말했다. \"저 굼뜬 자들을 이긴 것을 어찌 자랑하시오? 내가 그 겨룸에 나 자신을 내놓겠소.\" 아탈란테는 가엾어하는 낯빛으로 그를 바라보았고, 그를 이기는 편이 나을지 아닐지 좀처럼 가늠하지 못했다. \"어떤 신이 이토록 젊고 잘생긴 이를 부추겨 제 목숨을 내던지게 한단 말인가? 나는 그가 가엾다. 그 아름다움 때문이 아니라(하긴 그는 아름답지만), 그 젊음 때문이다. 그가 이 겨룸을 단념했으면 좋으련만, 만일 그토록 미친 짓을 하려 든다면, 부디 그가 나를 앞질렀으면.\" 이런 생각을 곱씹으며 그녀가 망설이는 동안, 구경꾼들은 달리기를 기다리다 조바심을 냈고, 그녀의 아버지는 그녀에게 준비하라고 재촉했다. 그러자 히포메네스는 아프로디테[로마의 〈베누스〉]에게 기도를 올렸다. \"나를 도우소서, 아프로디테여, 당신께서 나를 여기까지 이끄셨으니.\" 아프로디테는 듣고 호의를 베풀었다.\n\n그녀의 섬 키프로스에 있는 그녀의 신전 정원에는 누런 잎과 누런 가지와 황금 열매가 달린 나무 한 그루가 있다. 거기서 그녀는 황금 사과 세 개를 따서, 아무도 보지 못하게 히포메네스에게 건네고는 그것을 어떻게 쓸지 일러 주었다. 신호가 떨어졌다. 둘은 각기 출발선에서 내달려 모래 위를 스쳐 달렸다. 그 발걸음이 어찌나 가벼운지, 강물 위나 물결치는 곡식 위를 가라앉지 않고 달릴 수 있으리라 생각했을 정도였다. 구경꾼들의 외침이 히포메네스를 북돋웠다. \"지금이야, 지금, 힘껏 달려라! 서둘러, 서둘러! 따라잡고 있다! 늦추지 마라! 한 번만 더 힘을 내라!\" 이 외침을 젊은이가 더 즐거이 들었는지 처녀가 더 즐거이 들었는지는 분간하기 어려웠다. 그러나 그의 숨이 가빠지기 시작했고, 목은 말랐으며, 결승점은 아직 멀었다. 그 순간 그는 황금 사과 하나를 던져 떨어뜨렸다. 처녀는 온통 놀라움에 휩싸였다. 그녀는 그것을 줍느라 멈춰 섰다. 히포메네스가 앞으로 쏜살같이 나아갔다. 사방에서 함성이 터져 나왔다. 그녀는 갑절로 힘을 냈고, 이내 그를 따라잡았다. 그가 다시 사과 하나를 던졌다. 그녀는 또 멈춰 섰으나, 다시 그를 따라잡았다. 결승점이 가까웠고, 단 한 번의 기회만이 남아 있었다. \"이제, 여신이여,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"당신의 선물을 도와주소서!\" 그러고는 마지막 사과를 한쪽으로 던졌다. 그녀는 그것을 바라보며 망설였다. 아프로디테가 그녀를 부추겨 그것을 향해 비켜서게 했다. 그녀는 그렇게 했고, 패하고 말았다. 젊은이는 자기 상을 차지하여 데려갔다.\n\n그러나 두 연인은 자신들의 행복에 흠뻑 빠진 나머지 아프로디테에게 마땅한 경의를 바치는 것을 잊고 말았다. 여신은 그들의 배은망덕에 노여워했다. 그녀는 그들이 키벨레의 심기를 거스르게 만들었다. 그 강대한 여신은 모욕을 당하고도 그대로 넘어갈 분이 아니었다. 그녀는 그들에게서 인간의 모습을 거두어, 그들 자신의 성품을 닮은 짐승으로 바꾸어 놓았다. 연인들의 피를 보고 의기양양하던 그 여전사 영웅을 그녀는 암사자로 만들었고, 그녀의 주인이자 남편을 사자로 만들어, 자기 수레에 멍에를 지웠다. 그리하여 여신 키벨레를 조각이나 그림으로 나타낸 모든 표현 속에서 그들은 지금도 볼 수 있다.\n\n키벨레는 그리스인들이 레아 또는 옵스라 부르던 여신의 라틴어 이름이다. 그녀는 크로노스의 아내이자 제우스의 어머니였다. 예술 작품 속에서 그녀는 헤라[로마의 〈유노〉]와 데메테르[로마의 〈케레스〉]를 특징짓는 부인다운 기품을 드러낸다. 어떤 때는 너울을 쓴 채 양옆에 사자를 거느리고 옥좌에 앉아 있고, 또 어떤 때는 사자들이 끄는 수레를 타고 있다. 그녀는 성벽관(城壁冠)을 쓰는데, 이는 그 테두리가 탑과 흉벽 모양으로 새겨진 관이다. 그녀의 사제들은 코리반테스라 불렸다.\n\n바이런은 아드리아 해의 나지막한 섬 위에 세워진 도시 베네치아를 묘사하면서 키벨레에서 비유를 빌려 온다.\n\n\"그녀는 대양에서 갓 떠오른 바다의 키벨레처럼 보이나니, 당당한 탑들의 보관(寶冠)을 쓰고 아득한 거리에서 위엄 있는 몸짓으로 떠오르는, 물과 그 권능을 다스리는 통치자로다.\" ─〈차일드 해럴드〉, 제4편.\n\n무어의 〈길 위의 시편〉에서 시인은 알프스의 풍경을 이야기하며 아탈란테와 히포메네스의 이야기를 이렇게 넌지시 언급한다.\n\n\"이 경이의 고장에서조차, 나는 깨닫노라 발 가벼운 공상이 진실을 저만치 뒤에 남겨 두고 가는 것을, 아니면 적어도, 히포메네스처럼, 진실의 앞길에 던지는 황금빛 환영(幻影)으로 진실을 옆길로 빠지게 하는 것을.\" ————\n\n헤라클레스는 제우스와 알크메네 사이에서 태어난 아들이었다. 헤라[로마의 〈유노〉]는 자기 남편이 필멸의 어머니들에게서 얻은 자식들에게 늘 적대적이었기에, 헤라클레스가 태어난 순간부터 그를 상대로 전쟁을 선포했다. 그녀는 그가 요람에 누워 있을 때 두 마리의 뱀을 보내 그를 죽이려 했으나, 조숙한 이 아기는 제 손으로 그것들을 목 졸라 죽였다. 그렇지만 그는 헤라의 술책에 의해 에우리스테우스에게 예속된 신세가 되어, 그의 모든 명령을 수행하지 않을 수 없었다. 에우리스테우스는 그에게 일련의 무모한 모험들을 명했는데, 이것이 이른바 \"헤라클레스의 열두 가지 과업\"이라 불리는 것이다. 첫 번째는 네메아의 사자와 벌인 싸움이었다. 네메아 골짜기는 무시무시한 사자 한 마리에게 시달리고 있었다. 에우리스테우스는 헤라클레스에게 이 괴물의 가죽을 가져오라고 명했다. 헤라클레스는 곤봉과 화살을 그 사자에게 써 보았으나 헛수고였고, 결국 제 손으로 그 짐승을 목 졸라 죽였다. 그는 죽은 사자를 어깨에 메고 돌아왔다. 그러나 에우리스테우스는 그 광경과 이 영웅의 어마어마한 힘을 입증하는 증거에 얼마나 겁을 먹었던지, 앞으로는 그의 공적에 관한 보고를 성 밖에서 전하라고 명했다.\n\n그의 다음 과업은 히드라를 도살하는 것이었다. 이 괴물은 아르고스 땅을 황폐하게 만들었고, 아미모네 샘 근처의 늪에 살고 있었다. 이 샘은 그 땅이 가뭄에 시달리고 있을 때 아미모네가 발견한 것이었는데, 전하는 이야기에 따르면 그녀를 사랑한 포세이돈[로마의 〈넵투누스〉]이 자신의 삼지창으로 바위를 건드리도록 허락하자, 출구가 세 갈래인 샘물이 솟구쳐 나왔다고 한다. 이곳에 히드라가 자리를 잡았고, 헤라클레스는 그것을 죽이기 위해 보내졌다. 히드라는 머리가 아홉 개였는데, 그 가운데 하나는 불사의 것이었다. 헤라클레스는 곤봉으로 그 머리들을 쳐서 떨어뜨렸으나, 떨어뜨린 머리 자리에는 그때마다 새로운 머리 두 개가 자라났다. 마침내 그는 충실한 종 이올라오스의 도움을 받아 히드라의 머리들을 불로 지져 없앴고, 아홉 번째 곧 불사의 머리는 거대한 바위 아래 묻어 버렸다.\n\n또 다른 과업은 아우게이아스의 외양간을 청소하는 것이었다. 엘리스의 왕 아우게이아스는 삼천 마리의 소 떼를 거느리고 있었는데, 그 우리는 삼십 년 동안 한 번도 청소된 적이 없었다. 헤라클레스는 알페이오스 강과 페네이오스 강을 그 사이로 끌어들여, 단 하루 만에 그곳을 말끔히 청소해 냈다.\n\n그의 다음 과업은 좀 더 미묘한 종류의 것이었다. 에우리스테우스의 딸 아드메테는 아마조네스 여왕의 허리띠를 손에 넣고 싶어 했고, 에우리스테우스는 헤라클레스에게 가서 그것을 가져오라고 명했다. 아마조네스는 여인들로 이루어진 민족이었다. 그들은 무척 호전적이었고 번성하는 여러 도시를 거느리고 있었다. 여자아이들만 길러 내는 것이 그들의 관습이었고, 사내아이들은 이웃 민족들에게 보내 버리거나 죽여 버렸다. 헤라클레스는 다수의 자원자들을 거느리고 떠났으며, 여러 모험을 거친 끝에 마침내 아마조네스의 나라에 다다랐다. 여왕 히폴리테는 그를 친절히 맞이하고 자기 허리띠를 그에게 내주기로 했으나, 헤라가 아마조네스 한 사람의 모습을 하고 나타나 나머지 여인들에게 그 이방인들이 자기네 여왕을 납치해 가려 한다고 부추겼다. 그들은 즉시 무장하고 큰 무리를 이루어 배가 있는 곳으로 몰려 내려왔다. 헤라클레스는 히폴리테가 음모를 꾸민 것이라 여겨 그녀를 죽이고, 그녀의 허리띠를 빼앗아 고향으로 돛을 올렸다.\n\n그에게 부과된 또 다른 임무는 게리온의 소 떼를 에우리스테우스에게 가져오는 것이었다. 게리온은 몸이 셋인 괴물로서, 에리테이아[붉은] 섬에 살고 있었는데, 그 섬이 서쪽에 지는 해의 빛살 아래 자리 잡고 있다 하여 그렇게 불렸다. 이 묘사는 에스파냐를 가리키는 것으로 여겨지는데, 게리온은 그곳의 왕이었다. 여러 나라를 가로지른 끝에 헤라클레스는 마침내 리비아와 에우로페[유럽]의 경계에 다다랐고, 그곳에 자신이 지나온 자취를 기리는 기념물로서 칼페와 아빌라라는 두 산을 세웠다. 또 다른 이야기에 따르면, 그는 산 하나를 둘로 쪼개어 절반씩 양쪽에 남겨 둠으로써 지브롤터 해협을 이루었으며, 그 두 산을 헤라클레스의 기둥이라 불렀다고 한다. 그 소 떼는 거인 에우리티온과 머리가 둘 달린 그의 개가 지키고 있었으나, 헤라클레스는 그 거인과 개를 죽이고 소 떼를 무사히 에우리스테우스에게 가져왔다.\n\n모든 과업 가운데 가장 어려운 것은 헤스페리데스의 황금 사과를 손에 넣는 일이었으니, 헤라클레스는 그것을 어디서 찾아야 할지 알지 못했던 것이다. 이 사과들은 헤라가 결혼식 때 대지의 여신에게서 받은 것으로, 그녀는 이를 헤스페로스의 딸들이 지키도록 맡겨 두었고, 경계심 많은 용이 그들을 거들었다. 여러 모험을 거친 끝에 헤라클레스는 아프리카의 아틀라스 산에 다다랐다. 아틀라스는 신들에 맞서 싸운 티탄들 가운데 하나였는데, 그들이 제압된 뒤 아틀라스는 어깨에 하늘의 무게를 짊어지도록 선고받았다. 그는 헤스페리데스의 아버지였으므로, 만일 누군가가 그 사과를 찾아 가져다줄 수 있다면 바로 그일 것이라고 헤라클레스는 생각했다. 그러나 어떻게 아틀라스를 그 자리에서 떠나보내며, 또 그가 떠나 있는 동안 하늘을 떠받칠 수 있단 말인가? 헤라클레스는 그 짐을 제 어깨에 떠메고, 아틀라스를 보내어 사과를 찾아오게 했다. 아틀라스는 사과를 가지고 돌아왔으며, 다소 마지못한 것이기는 했으나 다시 자기 짐을 어깨에 떠메고, 헤라클레스가 그 사과를 에우리스테우스에게 가져가도록 놓아 주었다.\n\n밀턴은 자신의 작품 「코머스」에서 헤스페리데스를 헤스페로스의 딸들이자 아틀라스의 조카딸들로 그린다.\n\n\". . . 헤스페로스와 그의 세 딸들의 아름다운 정원 한가운데, 그들은 황금 나무 둘레에서 노래하네.\"\n\n서쪽 하늘이 해 질 녘에 보여 주는 사랑스러운 모습에서 유추하여, 시인들은 서쪽을 광명과 영광의 영역으로 보았다. 그리하여 그들은 그곳에 축복받은 자들의 섬들과, 게리온의 빛나는 소 떼가 풀을 뜯던 붉은 에리테아 섬과, 헤스페리데스의 섬을 두었다. 어떤 이들은 그 사과들이 에스파냐의 오렌지였으리라 추측하는데, 그리스인들은 그것에 관해 어렴풋한 이야기를 들었던 것이다.\n\n헤라클레스의 이름난 무훈 가운데 하나는 안타이오스를 누른 승리였다. 대지의 여신 테라의 아들 안타이오스는 강력한 거인이자 씨름꾼이었는데, 그의 힘은 어머니인 대지와 맞닿아 있는 한 누구도 당할 수 없었다. 그는 자기 나라에 오는 모든 이방인에게 자신과 씨름하도록 강요했으며, 패배할 경우(그들은 모두 패배했다) 죽임을 당해야 한다는 조건이었다. 헤라클레스는 그와 맞붙었는데, 그를 내동댕이쳐 보아야 소용이 없음을 깨달았다. 그가 넘어질 때마다 늘 새로운 힘을 얻어 다시 일어났던 것이다. 그래서 헤라클레스는 그를 땅에서 들어 올려 공중에서 목 졸라 죽였다.\n\n카쿠스는 거대한 거인으로, 아벤티누스 산의 동굴에 살면서 주변 지방을 약탈했다. 헤라클레스가 게리온의 소 떼를 몰고 집으로 돌아가던 길에, 영웅이 잠든 사이 카쿠스는 그 소들의 일부를 훔쳤다. 그 발자국이 소들이 어디로 몰려갔는지를 드러내지 않도록, 그는 소들의 꼬리를 잡고 뒷걸음질로 끌어 자기 동굴로 데려갔다. 그리하여 그 자취는 온통 소들이 반대 방향으로 갔음을 가리키는 듯 보였다. 헤라클레스는 이 술책에 속아 넘어가 자기 소들을 찾지 못할 뻔했으나, 마침 남은 소 떼를 몰고 훔친 소들이 숨겨진 동굴 곁을 지나갈 때 안에 있던 소들이 울기 시작했고, 그렇게 하여 발각되었다. 카쿠스는 헤라클레스에게 죽임을 당했다.\n\n우리가 기록할 마지막 무훈은 저승에서 케르베로스를 데려온 일이다. 헤라클레스는 헤르메스[로마의 〈메르쿠리우스〉]와 아테나[로마의 〈미네르바〉]를 거느리고 하데스로 내려갔다. 그는 무기를 쓰지 않고서 해낼 수 있다는 조건 아래, 케르베로스를 지상으로 데려가도 좋다는 허락을 하데스의 신 플루톤에게서 얻었다. 그리고 그 괴물이 발버둥 쳤음에도, 그는 그것을 붙잡아 단단히 움켜쥐고 에우리스테우스에게 데려갔다가, 나중에 다시 데려다 놓았다. 그가 하데스에 있을 때, 그는 자신의 찬미자이자 모방자인 테세우스를 풀어 주었으니, 테세우스는 페르세포네[로마의 〈프로세르피나〉]를 납치하려다 실패한 죄로 그곳에 죄수로 붙들려 있던 터였다.\n\n헤라클레스는 광기가 발작하여 자신의 벗 이피토스를 죽였고, 이 죄로 삼 년 동안 옴팔레 여왕의 노예가 되는 벌을 받았다. 이 봉사를 하는 동안 영웅의 본성은 변한 듯 보였다. 그는 여자처럼 나약하게 살았으며, 때로는 여인의 옷을 걸치고 옴팔레의 시녀들과 함께 양털을 자아냈고, 그동안 여왕은 그의 사자 가죽을 두르고 있었다. 이 봉사가 끝나자 그는 데이아네이라와 결혼하여 삼 년 동안 그녀와 평화롭게 살았다. 한번은 그가 아내와 함께 여행하던 중 어느 강에 이르렀는데, 그 강은 켄타우로스인 네소스가 정해진 삯을 받고 나그네들을 건네주던 곳이었다. 헤라클레스 자신은 강을 걸어서 건넜으나, 데이아네이라는 네소스에게 맡겨 건너게 했다. 네소스가 그녀를 데리고 달아나려 하자, 헤라클레스는 그녀의 비명을 듣고 네소스의 심장에 화살을 쏘았다. 죽어 가는 켄타우로스는 데이아네이라에게, 자기 피의 일부를 받아 간직해 두라고, 그것이 남편의 사랑을 지켜 주는 부적으로 쓰일 수 있을 것이라고 일러 주었다.\n\n데이아네이라는 그대로 했고, 머지않아 그것을 쓸 일이 생겼다고 여겼다. 헤라클레스는 자신의 정복 가운데 한 곳에서 이올레라는 아름다운 처녀를 사로잡았는데, 데이아네이라가 좋게 보아 넘길 수 있는 정도보다 그녀를 더 좋아하는 것처럼 보였다. 헤라클레스가 자신의 승리를 기려 신들에게 제물을 바치려 할 즈음, 그는 그 자리에서 쓸 흰옷을 보내 달라고 아내에게 사람을 보냈다. 데이아네이라는 자신의 사랑의 주술을 시험해 볼 좋은 기회라고 여겨, 그 옷을 네소스의 피에 적셨다. 우리는 그녀가 피의 흔적을 모두 씻어 내려 애썼으리라 짐작해야겠으나, 그 주술의 힘은 그대로 남아 있었고, 그 옷이 헤라클레스의 몸에서 따뜻해지자마자 독이 그의 온 사지로 스며들어 그에게 더없이 격렬한 고통을 안겼다. 그는 광란에 사로잡혀, 그 치명적인 옷을 가져온 리카스를 붙잡아 바다로 내던졌다. 그는 그 옷을 잡아 뜯었으나, 옷은 그의 살에 들러붙어, 그것과 함께 그의 몸뚱이 살점이 통째로 떨어져 나갔다. 이런 상태로 그는 배에 올라 집으로 옮겨졌다. 데이아네이라는 자신이 영문도 모른 채 저지른 일을 보고는 목매어 죽었다. 헤라클레스는 죽을 채비를 하고 오이테 산에 올라, 그곳에 나무로 화장용 장작더미를 쌓고 자신의 활과 화살을 필록테테스에게 주었으며, 머리는 곤봉에 받치고 사자 가죽을 몸 위에 덮은 채 장작더미 위에 누웠다. 마치 잔칫상에 자리를 잡으려는 듯 평온한 얼굴로, 그는 필록테테스에게 횃불을 갖다 대라고 명했다. 불길은 빠르게 번져 곧 그 더미 전체를 휘감았다.\n\n밀턴은 헤라클레스의 광란을 이렇게 암시한다.\n\n\"알키데스가, 오이칼리아에서 정복으로 관을 쓰고, 독 묻은 옷을 느꼈을 때, 고통에 못 이겨 테살리아의 소나무들을 뿌리째 뽑아 올렸고 리카스를 오이테 산꼭대기에서 에우보이아 바다로 내던졌던 것처럼.\"\n\n신들조차 대지의 투사가 그처럼 최후를 맞는 것을 보고 마음 아파했다. 그러나 제우스는 밝은 얼굴로 그들에게 이렇게 말했다. \"나는 그대들의 근심을 보니 기쁘고, 내가 충실한 백성의 통치자임을, 그리고 내 아들이 그대들의 호의를 누리고 있음을 알게 되어 흐뭇하오. 그대들이 그에게 품은 관심이 그의 고귀한 행적에서 비롯된 것이라 해도, 그것이 내게 흐뭇하지 않은 것은 아니오. 그러나 이제 나는 그대들에게 이르노니, 두려워 마시오. 다른 모든 것을 정복한 그가, 그대들이 오이테 산에서 타오르는 것을 보는 저 불길에 정복당하지는 않을 것이오. 다만 그 어머니에게서 받은 몫만이 스러질 수 있을 뿐, 내게서 물려받은 것은 불멸이오. 나는 대지에 대해서는 죽은 그를 하늘의 기슭으로 데려가겠으니, 그대들 모두 그를 친절히 맞이해 주기를 청하오. 그가 이 영예에 이르는 것을 슬퍼하는 이가 그대들 가운데 있다 하더라도, 그가 그것을 받을 자격이 있다는 것은 누구도 부정할 수 없을 것이오.\" 신들은 모두 동의했다. 헤라만이 그 끝맺는 말이 자기를 그토록 콕 집어 가리킨다는 데 다소 언짢음을 느끼며 들었으나, 그렇다고 남편의 결심을 후회하게 할 만큼은 아니었다. 그리하여 불길이 헤라클레스의 어머니에게서 받은 몫을 다 태워 버리자, 보다 신성한 부분은 그로 인해 손상되기는커녕 오히려 새로운 활력을 띠고 솟아오르는 듯하였고, 더욱 드높은 풍모와 더욱 경외로운 위엄을 갖추는 듯하였다. 제우스는 그를 구름으로 감싸, 네 마리 말이 끄는 전차에 태워 별들 사이에서 살도록 데려갔다. 그가 하늘에서 제자리를 잡자, 아틀라스는 무게가 더해진 것을 느꼈다.\n\n이제 그와 화해한 헤라는 자기 딸 헤베를 그에게 아내로 주었다.\n\n시인 실러는 「이상과 삶」이라는 자신의 작품 가운데 하나에서, 실제적인 것과 상상적인 것 사이의 대조를 아름다운 시연(詩聯)으로 그려 내는데, 그 마지막 두 연을 이렇게 옮길 수 있다.\n\n\"비겁한 자의 노예로 깊이 굴러떨어져, 용맹한 알키데스는 끝없는 싸움을 견뎠고, 고난의 가시밭길을 헤쳐 나갔네. 히드라를 죽이고, 사자의 힘을 으스러뜨렸으며, 벗을 빛으로 데려오고자, 산 몸으로 죽은 자들을 실어 나르는 작은 배에 몸을 던졌네. 대지의 온갖 고통, 온갖 노역, 헤라의 증오가 그에게 지울 수 있었던 그 모두를, 그는 정해진 출생에서부터 장엄하고도 비통한 삶의 끝에 이르도록 잘 견뎠네.\n\n\"마침내 그 신은, 지상의 부분을 버리고, 불길 속에서 인간으로부터 떨어져 나와, 더 순수한 천상의 정기를 들이마셨네. 낯설고도 새로운 가벼움에 기뻐하며, 그는 천상의 광명을 향해 솟구쳐 올랐으니, 대지의 어둡고 무거운 짐은 죽음 속에 잃었네. 드높은 올림포스는 그의 흠모받는 아버지가 다스리는 전당에 화음으로 인사를 보내고, 젊음의 빛나는 여신은, 마주침에 얼굴 붉히며, 자신의 주인에게 넥타르를 건네네.\" ―S. G. B.\n\n# HEBE AND GANYMEDE\n\n헤라의 딸이자 젊음의 여신인 헤베는 신들의 술 따르는 시종이었다. 흔히 전하는 이야기로는 그녀가 헤라클레스의 아내가 되면서 그 직분을 내려놓았다고 한다. 그러나 또 다른 설명이 있는데, 우리나라 사람인 조각가 크로퍼드가 지금 아테나이움 화랑에 있는 그의 군상 「헤베와 가니메데스」에서 이를 채택한 바 있다. 그에 따르면 헤베는 어느 날 신들의 시중을 들던 중에 넘어지는 일을 당한 까닭에 그 직분에서 물러나게 되었다. 그녀의 뒤를 이은 것은 트로이아의 소년 가니메데스였는데, 제우스가 독수리로 변장하여 이다 산에서 놀이 동무들 한가운데 있던 그를 낚아채 데려가, 하늘로 올려 그 빈자리에 앉혔던 것이다.\n\n테니슨은 자신의 「예술의 궁전」에서, 벽을 꾸민 장식들 가운데 이 전설을 그린 그림 하나를 묘사한다.\n\n\"거기 또한, 발그레한 가니메데스가, 그의 장밋빛 넓적다리를 독수리의 솜털 속에 반쯤 묻은 채, 기둥 늘어선 도시 위로 하늘을 가로질러 쏜살같이 나는 별처럼 홀로 있네.\"\n\n그리고 셸리의 「프로메테우스」에서 제우스는 자신의 술 따르는 시종을 이렇게 부른다.\n\n\"하늘의 술을 따라 부어라, 이다 산의 가니메데스여, 그리하여 다이달로스의 잔들을 불처럼 가득 채워라.\"\n\n「헤라클레스의 선택」이라는 아름다운 전설은 「태틀러」 제97호에서 찾아볼 수 있다.\n\n────\n\n테세우스는 아테네의 왕 아이게우스와 트로이젠 왕의 딸 아이트라 사이에서 태어난 아들이었다. 그는 트로이젠에서 양육되었고, 장성하여 어른이 되면 아테네로 가서 아버지 앞에 자기를 내보일 예정이었다. 아이게우스는 아들이 태어나기 전에 아이트라와 작별하면서 자신의 칼과 신발을 커다란 바위 밑에 넣어 두고는, 아들이 그 바위를 굴려 치우고 그 밑에서 그것들을 꺼낼 수 있을 만큼 자라거든 자기에게 보내라고 일렀다. 때가 되었다고 여겨졌을 때, 그의 어머니는 테세우스를 그 바위로 데려갔고, 그는 손쉽게 그것을 치우고 칼과 신발을 꺼냈다. 길마다 도적들이 들끓었으므로, 외조부는 아버지의 나라로 가는 더 짧고 안전한 길을, 곧 바닷길을 택하라고 그에게 간곡히 권했다. 하지만 자신 안에 영웅의 기상과 혼이 깃들어 있음을 느낀 그 젊은이는, 당시 온 그리스가 그 명성으로 떠들썩했던 헤라클레스처럼, 나라를 짓누르던 악행을 일삼는 자들과 괴물들을 무찔러 스스로 이름을 떨치기를 갈망하여, 더 위험하고 모험으로 가득한 육로 여행을 결심했다.\n\n첫날의 여정 끝에 그는 에피다우로스에 이르렀는데, 그곳에는 헤파이스토스[로마의 〈불카누스〉]의 아들인 페리페테스라는 사내가 살고 있었다. 이 흉포한 야만인은 언제나 쇠몽둥이로 무장하고 다녔으므로, 모든 나그네가 그의 난폭함을 두려워했다. 그는 테세우스가 다가오는 것을 보자 그를 덮쳤으나, 이내 젊은 영웅의 타격 아래 쓰러졌고, 영웅은 그 몽둥이를 차지하여 그 후로 줄곧 자신의 첫 승리를 기리는 기념물로 지니고 다녔다.\n\n그 나라의 소소한 폭군들과 약탈자들을 상대로 한 비슷한 싸움이 여러 차례 이어졌고, 그 모두에서 테세우스는 승리를 거두었다. 이 악행을 일삼는 자들 가운데 하나는 프로크루스테스, 곧 〈잡아 늘이는 자〉라고 불렸다. 그는 쇠로 된 침상을 갖고 있었는데, 자기 손에 걸려든 나그네들을 모두 거기에 묶곤 했다. 그들이 침상보다 짧으면 사지를 잡아 늘여 침상에 맞추었고, 침상보다 길면 일부를 잘라 냈다. 테세우스는 그가 다른 이들에게 했던 그대로 그를 다루었다.\n\n길의 온갖 위험을 다 이겨 낸 끝에 테세우스는 마침내 아테네에 다다랐는데, 그곳에서는 새로운 위험들이 그를 기다리고 있었다. 이아손과 갈라선 뒤 코린토스에서 달아났던 마녀 메데이아가 테세우스의 아버지 아이게우스의 아내가 되어 있었던 것이다. 그녀는 자신의 술법으로 그가 누구인지 알아채고는, 테세우스가 아들로 인정받으면 남편에 대한 자신의 영향력을 잃을까 두려워하여, 아이게우스의 마음에 그 젊은 이방인에 대한 의심을 가득 채워 넣었고, 그에게 독이 든 잔을 권하도록 부추겼다. 그러나 테세우스가 그것을 받으려고 앞으로 나서는 순간, 그가 차고 있던 칼이 눈에 띄어 아버지에게 그가 누구인지를 드러내 주었고, 이로써 치명적인 한 모금을 막아 주었다. 술법이 발각된 메데이아는 또다시 마땅한 벌을 피해 달아나 아시아에 이르렀는데, 그 후 그곳의 나라는 그녀의 이름을 따 메데이아라 불리게 되었다. 테세우스는 아버지에게 인정받았고, 그 후계자로 선포되었다.\n\n당시 아테네 사람들은 크레타의 왕 미노스에게 바칠 수밖에 없었던 공물 때문에 깊은 고통에 잠겨 있었다. 이 공물은 일곱 명의 젊은이와 일곱 명의 처녀로 이루어져 있었는데, 이들은 해마다 미노타우로스에게 잡아먹히도록 보내졌다. 미노타우로스는 황소의 몸에 사람의 머리를 가진 괴물이었다. 그것은 지극히 강하고 사나웠으며, 다이달로스가 지은 미궁 안에 갇혀 있었다. 그 미궁은 어찌나 교묘하게 만들어졌던지, 그 안에 갇힌 자는 누구든 도움 없이는 결코 빠져나갈 길을 찾지 못했다. 이곳에서 미노타우로스는 돌아다니며 사람 제물을 먹이로 받았다.\n\n테세우스는 이 재앙으로부터 자기 동족을 구해 내거나, 아니면 그 시도 중에 죽기로 결심했다. 그리하여 공물을 보낼 때가 오고, 관례에 따라 보낼 젊은이들과 처녀들을 제비뽑기로 정하게 되자, 그는 아버지의 간곡한 만류에도 불구하고 자신을 제물 가운데 하나로 내놓았다. 배는 여느 때처럼 검은 돛을 달고 떠났는데, 테세우스는 승리하여 돌아올 경우 그것을 흰 돛으로 바꾸겠노라 아버지에게 약속했다. 그들이 크레타에 이르자 젊은이들과 처녀들은 미노스 앞에 내보여졌다. 그 자리에 있던 왕의 딸 아리아드네는 테세우스에게 깊이 반하게 되었고, 그 사랑은 그에게서 선뜻 화답받았다. 그녀는 미노타우로스와 맞서 싸우라고 그에게 칼을 마련해 주었고, 또 미궁에서 빠져나올 길을 찾도록 실타래 하나를 건네주었다. 그는 성공하여 미노타우로스를 죽이고 미궁에서 벗어났으며, 아리아드네를 길동무로 삼아, 구해 낸 동료들과 함께 아테네를 향해 배를 띄웠다. 가는 길에 그들은 낙소스섬에 들렀는데, 그곳에서 테세우스는 아리아드네가 잠든 사이에 그녀를 버리고 떠났다.[19] 자신의 은인을 이처럼 배은망덕하게 대한 데 대한 그의 변명은, 아테나[로마의 〈미네르바〉]가 꿈에 나타나 그렇게 하라고 명했다는 것이었다.\n\n아티카의 해안에 다가가면서 테세우스는 아버지가 정해 둔 신호를 잊고서 흰 돛을 올리는 일을 소홀히 했고, 늙은 왕은 아들이 죽은 줄로 알고 스스로 목숨을 끊었다. 이리하여 테세우스는 아테네의 왕이 되었다.\n\n테세우스의 모험 가운데 가장 유명한 것 하나는 아마조네스를 상대로 한 원정이다. 그는 그들이 헤라클레스의 공격에서 미처 회복되기 전에 그들을 덮쳐, 그들의 여왕 안티오페를 데려왔다. 이번에는 아마조네스가 아테네의 나라로 쳐들어와 도시 한복판까지 파고들었고, 테세우스가 그들을 무찌른 마지막 전투는 바로 도시 한가운데에서 벌어졌다. 이 전투는 고대 조각가들이 즐겨 다룬 주제 가운데 하나였으며, 지금도 전해지는 여러 예술 작품에 기념되어 있다.\n\n테세우스와 페이리토오스 사이의 우정은 더없이 친밀한 것이었으나, 그것은 무기를 맞댄 가운데서 비롯되었다. 페이리토오스는 마라톤 평원으로 쳐들어와 아테네 왕의 가축 떼를 몰고 갔다. 테세우스는 그 약탈자들을 물리치러 나섰다. 페이리토오스는 그를 보는 순간 감탄에 사로잡혔다. 그는 화평의 표시로 손을 내밀며 외쳤다. \"그대 스스로 판가름하라. 어떤 보상을 바라는가?\" \"그대의 우정이오.\" 아테네인이 대답했고, 두 사람은 깨뜨릴 수 없는 신의를 맹세했다. 그들의 행적은 그들의 다짐과 들어맞았으며, 그들은 언제까지나 진정한 전우로 남았다. 둘은 저마다 제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]의 딸을 아내로 맞기를 열망했다. 테세우스는 당시 아직 어린아이였던 헬레네를 점찍었는데, 그녀는 훗날 트로이아 전쟁의 원인으로 그토록 유명해진 여인이었고, 그는 친구의 도움을 받아 그녀를 데려왔다. 페이리토오스는 에레보스[저승]의 군주의 아내를 열망했고, 테세우스는 그 위험을 알면서도 야심 찬 연인을 따라 저승으로 내려갔다. 그러나 하데스[로마의 〈플루토〉]는 그들을 붙잡아 자기 궁전 문 앞의 마법에 걸린 바위에 앉혀 두었고, 그들은 헤라클레스가 와서 테세우스를 풀어 줄 때까지 그곳에 머물렀으며, 페이리토오스는 자신의 운명에 내맡겨졌다.\n\n안티오페가 죽은 뒤, 테세우스는 크레타의 왕 미노스의 딸 파이드라와 혼인했다. 파이드라는 테세우스의 아들 히폴리토스에게서 그 아버지의 온갖 우아함과 덕을 두루 갖추었으며 자신과 또래인 젊은이를 보았다. 그녀는 그를 사랑했으나 그가 그녀의 구애를 물리치자, 그 사랑은 미움으로 바뀌었다. 그녀는 사랑에 눈먼 남편에 대한 영향력을 이용해 그가 제 아들을 질투하도록 만들었고, 그는 포세이돈[로마의 〈넵투누스〉]의 복수가 아들에게 내리기를 빌었다. 어느 날 히폴리토스가 해안을 따라 전차를 몰고 있을 때, 바다 괴물 하나가 물 위로 솟아올라 말들을 놀라게 했고, 말들은 달아나다가 전차를 산산조각 냈다. 히폴리토스는 죽고 말았으나, 아르테미스[로마의 〈디아나〉]의 도움으로 아스클레피오스가 그를 되살려 냈다. 아르테미스는 히폴리토스를 속아 넘어간 아버지와 거짓된 계모의 손에서 빼내어, 이탈리아에 있는 님프 에게리아의 보호 아래 두었다.\n\n테세우스는 마침내 백성의 신망을 잃고서, 스키로스의 왕 리코메데스의 궁정으로 물러났는데, 리코메데스는 처음에는 그를 친절히 맞아 주었으나 나중에는 그를 배신하여 죽였다. 후대에 이르러 아테네의 장군 키몬이 그의 유해가 묻힌 곳을 찾아내어 그것을 아테네로 옮겨 오게 했고, 그 유해는 그 영웅을 기려 세운 테세이온이라는 신전에 안치되었다.\n\n테세우스가 아내로 맞은 아마조네스의 여왕을 어떤 이들은 히폴리타라고 부른다. 그것은 셰익스피어의 「한여름 밤의 꿈」에서 그녀가 지니는 이름인데, 이 작품의 주제는 테세우스와 히폴리타의 혼례에 따르는 축제다.\n\n헤먼스 부인은, 마라톤 전투에서 〈테세우스의 망령〉이 나타나 동족을 북돋워 주었다는 고대 그리스의 전승을 두고 시 한 편을 썼다.\n\n테세우스는 반쯤은 역사적인 인물이다. 그에 관해서는, 당시 아티카 영토를 차지하고 있던 여러 부족을 하나의 국가로 통합하고, 그 수도를 아테네로 삼았다고 기록되어 있다. 이 중대한 사건을 기념하여 그는 아테네의 수호신 아테나를 기리는 판아테나이아 축제를 창설했다. 이 축제는 주로 두 가지 점에서 다른 그리스의 경기들과 달랐다. 그것은 아테네인들에게만 고유한 것이었고, 그 핵심 행사는 아테나의 신성한 옷인 페플로스를 파르테논으로 받들어 가서 여신상 앞에 걸어 두는 엄숙한 행렬이었다. 페플로스는 아테네에서 가장 고귀한 가문 출신으로 뽑힌 처녀들이 손수 짠 자수로 뒤덮여 있었다. 행렬은 온갖 연령과 양쪽 성별의 사람들로 이루어졌다. 노인들은 손에 올리브 가지를 들었고, 젊은이들은 무기를 들었다. 젊은 여인들은 머리에 바구니를 이었는데, 그 안에는 신성한 제기와 과자, 그리고 제사에 필요한 온갖 것들이 담겨 있었다. 이 행렬은 파르테논 신전 바깥을 장식한 부조의 주제가 되었다. 이 조각들 가운데 상당 부분이 지금은 〈엘긴 대리석〉으로 알려진 작품들에 섞여 대영 박물관에 소장되어 있다.\n\n# OLYMPIC AND OTHER GAMES\n\n여기서 그리스인들의 다른 유명한 국민적 경기들을 언급하는 것도 부적절하지는 않을 듯하다. 첫째이자 가장 이름난 것은 올림피아 경기였는데, 제우스가 친히 창설했다고 전해진다. 이 경기는 엘리스의 올림피아에서 거행되었다. 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 구경꾼들이 그리스 각지에서, 또 아시아와 아프리카와 시켈리아[시칠리아]에서 그곳으로 몰려들었다. 이 경기는 한여름에 다섯 해마다 거듭 열렸고, 닷새 동안 이어졌다. 이로부터 올림피아드를 단위로 시간을 헤아리고 사건의 연대를 매기는 관습이 생겨났다. 첫 번째 올림피아드는 일반적으로 기원전 776년에 해당하는 것으로 여겨진다. 피티아 경기는 델포이 부근에서, 이스트미아 경기는 코린토스 지협에서, 네메아 경기는 아르골리스의 도시 네메아에서 거행되었다.\n\n이 경기들의 종목은 다섯 가지였다. 곧 달리기, 뛰기, 씨름, 원반던지기, 그리고 창던지기 혹은 권투였다. 이러한 신체의 힘과 민첩함을 겨루는 종목들 외에도, 음악과 시와 웅변을 겨루는 경연이 있었다. 그리하여 이 경기들은 시인과 음악가와 작가들에게 자기 작품을 대중 앞에 선보일 더없이 좋은 기회를 마련해 주었고, 승리자들의 명성은 멀리 널리 퍼져 나갔다.\n\n테세우스가 아리아드네의 실타래 덕분에 빠져나온 미궁은 더없이 솜씨 좋은 장인 다이달로스가 지은 것이었다. 그것은 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 굽이진 통로와 모퉁이가 서로 맞물려 열려 있는 건축물로, 마치 제 자신에게로 되돌아와 바다로 흘러가는 길에 때로는 앞으로, 때로는 뒤로 흐르는 마이안드로스강처럼, 시작도 끝도 없어 보였다. 다이달로스는 미노스 왕을 위해 그 미궁을 지었으나, 그 후 왕의 신망을 잃고 탑에 갇혔다. 그는 궁리 끝에 감옥에서 빠져나오는 데는 성공했지만, 왕이 모든 배를 철저히 감시하여 꼼꼼히 수색하지 않고서는 어떤 배도 출항을 허락하지 않았으므로, 바닷길로는 섬을 떠날 수가 없었다. \"미노스가 땅과 바다는 다스릴 수 있을지언정 하늘의 영역은 다스리지 못한다.\" 다이달로스가 말했다. \"나는 그 길로 가 보겠다.\" 그리하여 그는 자신과 어린 아들 이카로스를 위해 날개를 만드는 일에 착수했다. 그는 가장 작은 깃털부터 시작하여 점점 큰 것을 더해 가며 깃털들을 한데 엮어, 차츰 넓어지는 면을 이루도록 했다. 큰 깃털들은 실로 동여매고 작은 것들은 밀랍으로 붙였으며, 그 전체에 새의 날개처럼 부드러운 곡선을 주었다. 어린 이카로스는 곁에 서서 구경하다가, 이따금 바람에 날려 간 깃털들을 주우러 달려가기도 하고, 또 밀랍을 만지작거리며 손가락으로 주물러 대기도 하면서, 그 장난으로 일에 몰두한 아버지를 방해했다. 마침내 일이 다 끝나자, 장인은 날개를 퍼덕이며 자신의 몸이 위로 떠오르는 것을 느꼈고, 두드린 공기 위에 몸을 가눈 채 공중에 매달려 있었다. 이어서 그는 같은 방식으로 아들에게도 날개를 채워 주고, 마치 어미 새가 새끼들을 높은 둥지에서 공중으로 꾀어내듯이 그에게 나는 법을 가르쳤다. 날아오를 채비가 모두 갖추어지자 그가 말했다. \"내 아들 이카로스야, 너에게 당부하노니 적당한 높이를 지켜라. 너무 낮게 날면 습기가 날개를 무겁게 할 것이고, 너무 높이 날면 열기가 날개를 녹여 버릴 것이다. 내 곁에 가까이 있으면 안전할 것이다.\" 이렇게 그에게 일러 주며 어깨에 날개를 채워 주는 동안, 아버지의 얼굴은 눈물로 젖었고 두 손은 떨렸다. 그는 그것이 마지막인 줄도 모르고 아들에게 입을 맞추었다. 그러고는 날개로 떠올라 날아가며 아들에게 뒤따르라고 격려했고, 자신의 비행 중에 뒤를 돌아보아 아들이 날개를 어떻게 다루는지 살폈다. 그들이 날아갈 때 밭갈이하던 농부는 하던 일을 멈추고 바라보았고, 양치기는 지팡이에 몸을 기댄 채 그들을 지켜보면서, 그 광경에 놀라 저렇게 공기를 가르는 것들은 신이 아닐까 생각했다.\n\n그들은 사모스와 델로스를 왼편에, 레빈토스를 오른편에 두고 지나쳤는데, 그때 비행에 우쭐해진 소년은 길잡이인 동행에게서 벗어나기 시작하더니 마치 하늘에 닿으려는 듯 위로 솟구쳤다. 이글거리는 태양이 가까워지자 깃털들을 한데 붙들고 있던 밀랍이 물러져, 깃털들이 떨어져 나갔다. 그는 두 팔을 퍼덕였으나, 공기를 붙들어 줄 깃털은 더 이상 남아 있지 않았다. 그의 입이 아버지를 부르는 외침을 토해 내는 동안, 그 입은 바다의 푸른 물결 속에 잠겨 버렸고, 그 바다는 그 후로 그의 이름으로 불리게 되었다. 아버지가 외쳤다. \"이카로스야, 이카로스야, 어디에 있느냐?\" 마침내 그는 물 위에 떠다니는 깃털들을 보았고, 자신의 솜씨를 쓰라리게 한탄하며 아들의 시신을 묻고는 그 땅을 자기 아이를 기려 이카리아라 불렀다. 다이달로스는 무사히 시켈리아에 다다라 그곳에 아폴론을 위한 신전을 짓고, 자신의 날개를 신에게 바치는 봉헌물로 걸어 두었다.\n\n다이달로스는 자신의 업적을 어찌나 자랑스러워했던지 경쟁자가 있다는 생각을 견디지 못했다. 그의 누이가 아들 페르딕스를 그의 손에 맡겨 기계 기술을 배우게 했다. 그는 재주 있는 제자였고 놀라운 재능의 증거를 여러 차례 보여 주었다. 바닷가를 거닐다가 그는 물고기의 등뼈를 주웠다. 그것을 본떠 쇳조각 하나를 가져다가 가장자리에 톱니를 새겨, 이리하여 톱을 발명했다. 그는 쇳조각 두 개를 한쪽 끝에서 못으로 이어 붙이고 다른 끝들을 뾰족하게 갈아 컴퍼스를 만들었다. 다이달로스는 조카의 솜씨를 어찌나 시샘했던지, 어느 날 둘이 높은 탑 꼭대기에 함께 있을 때를 틈타 그를 밀어 떨어뜨렸다. 그러나 재주를 아끼는 아테나가 그가 떨어지는 것을 보고는, 그를 그의 이름을 딴 자고새라는 새로 바꾸어 그 운명을 막아 주었다. 이 새는 나무에 둥지를 틀지도 않고 높이 날아오르지도 않으며, 산울타리 속에 깃들고 자신이 떨어졌던 일을 잊지 않아 높은 곳을 피한다.\n\n이카로스의 죽음은 다윈이 다음과 같은 시구로 노래하고 있다.\n\n\"……녹아내리는 밀랍과 풀려난 실과 더불어 가엾은 이카로스는 믿지 못할 날개를 타고 가라앉았네. 겁에 질린 공기를 곤두박질쳐 뚫고 내려가니, 사지는 뒤틀리고 머리카락은 흐트러졌네. 흩어진 깃털은 물결 위에서 춤추었고, 슬퍼하는 네레이데스가 그의 물속 무덤을 꾸며 주었네. 그의 창백한 주검 위에 진주 같은 바다꽃을 뿌리고, 그의 대리석 침상에 진홍빛 이끼를 깔아 주었네. 산호 탑에서 조종을 울리니, 그 메아리치는 만가가 너른 바다에 울려 퍼졌네.\"\n\n# CASTOR AND POLLUX\n\n카스토르와 폴리데우케스는 레다와 백조 사이에서 난 자식들이었는데, 제우스는 그 백조의 모습으로 자신을 숨겼던 것이다. 레다는 알 하나를 낳았고, 거기서 쌍둥이가 태어났다. 훗날 트로이아 전쟁의 원인으로 그토록 유명해진 헬레네가 그들의 누이였다.\n\n테세우스와 그의 친구 페이리토오스가 스파르타에서 헬레네를 데려갔을 때, 젊은 영웅 카스토르와 폴리데우케스는 추종자들을 거느리고 누이를 구하러 서둘러 달려갔다. 테세우스는 아티카를 떠나 있었으므로, 두 형제는 누이를 되찾는 데 성공했다.\n\n카스토르는 말을 길들이고 다루는 데 이름났고, 폴리데우케스는 권투 솜씨로 이름났다. 그들은 더없이 따뜻한 애정으로 맺어져 있었으며 모든 일에서 떼어 놓을 수 없는 사이였다. 그들은 아르고호 원정에 함께했다. 항해 도중 폭풍이 일자, 오르페우스가 사모트라케의 신들에게 기도하며 하프를 켰는데, 그러자 폭풍이 멎고 두 형제의 머리 위에 별들이 나타났다. 이 일로부터 카스토르와 폴리데우케스는 그 후 뱃사람과 항해자의 수호신으로 여겨지게 되었고, 대기가 어떤 상태에 있을 때 배의 돛과 돛대 둘레에 너울거리는 불꽃을 그들의 이름으로 부르게 되었다.\n\n아르고호 원정 이후, 우리는 카스토르와 폴리데우케스가 이다스 및 린케우스와 전쟁을 벌이는 것을 본다. 카스토르는 살해되었고, 형제를 잃고 위로받지 못한 폴리데우케스는 형제의 몸값으로 자신의 목숨을 내놓도록 허락해 달라고 제우스에게 간청했다. 제우스는 두 형제가 번갈아 가며 삶의 은혜를 누리도록, 곧 하루는 땅 밑에서, 다음 하루는 하늘의 거처에서 지내도록 허락하는 데까지 동의했다. 이야기의 또 다른 형태에 따르면, 제우스는 형제의 우애에 보답하여 그들을 별들 사이에 〈쌍둥이자리〉인 게미니로 두었다.\n\n그들은 디오스쿠로이(제우스의 아들들)라는 이름으로 신적인 영예를 받았다. 그들은 후대에 이르러 격전이 벌어진 전장에서 이따금 나타나 어느 한쪽 편을 들었다고 믿어졌으며, 그러한 때에는 으리으리한 흰 군마에 올라타 있었다고 전해진다. 그리하여 로마 초기 역사에서 그들은 레길루스호 전투에서 로마인들을 도왔다고 하며, 승리를 거둔 뒤 그들이 나타난 바로 그 자리에 그들을 기리는 신전이 세워졌다.\n\n매콜리는 「고대 로마의 노래」에서 이 전설을 이렇게 암시하고 있다.\n\n\"그들이 어찌나 닮았던지, 어떤 필멸자도 하나를 다른 하나와 구별하지 못하였네. 그들의 갑옷은 눈처럼 희었고, 그들의 군마도 눈처럼 희었네. 지상의 모루에서는 그토록 진귀한 갑옷이 빛난 적 없었고, 그토록 늠름한 군마가 지상의 시내에서 물을 마신 적도 없었네. . . . . . . . \"싸움의 순간에 위대한 쌍둥이 형제가 갑옷을 입고 제 오른편에 선 것을 본 우두머리는 개선하여 돌아오네. 위대한 쌍둥이 형제가 한 번 돛 위에 빛나며 앉기만 하면, 배는 물결을 헤치고 풍랑을 뚫고 무사히 항구에 다다르네.\"\n\n————\n\n바쿠스—아리아드네\n바쿠스\n\n바쿠스는 유피테르와 세멜레 사이에서 태어난 아들이었다. 유노는 세멜레를 향한 자신의 원한을 풀고자, 그녀를 파멸시킬 계략을 꾸몄다. 세멜레의 늙은 유모인 베로에의 모습으로 변신한 그녀는, 과연 연인으로 찾아온 이가 정말로 유피테르 본인인지 의심을 불어넣었다. 한숨을 내쉬며 유노는 이렇게 말했다. \"정말 그러기를 바라지만, 나로서는 두려운 마음을 떨칠 수가 없구나. 사람이란 늘 자기가 내세우는 그대로인 건 아니니 말이다. 그분이 정말 유피테르라면, 그 증거를 보여 달라고 하려무나. 하늘에서 두르는 온갖 광휘를 갖춘 모습으로 와 달라고 청하거라. 그러면 그 일은 의심할 여지 없이 분명해질 게다.\" 세멜레는 이 시험을 해 보도록 설득당했다. 그녀는 무엇인지 밝히지 않은 채 한 가지 소원을 청했다. 유피테르는 약속을 해 주었고, 신들 자신도 두려워하는 스틱스 강을 두고, 결코 거둘 수 없는 맹세로 그것을 확약했다. 그러자 세멜레는 자신의 청을 밝혔다. 신은 그녀가 말하는 동안 막으려 했으나, 그녀가 너무 빨랐다. 말은 이미 새어 나왔고, 그는 자신의 약속도 그녀의 청도 거둘 수 없었다. 깊은 고뇌에 잠긴 채 그는 그녀 곁을 떠나 위쪽 영역으로 돌아갔다. 그곳에서 그는 자신의 광휘를 걸쳤으되, 거인들을 쓰러뜨릴 때처럼 온갖 무서운 위용을 다 갖추지는 않고, 신들 사이에서 그의 작은 위엄으로 알려진 것만을 둘렀다. 그렇게 차려입은 채 그는 세멜레의 방으로 들어섰다. 그녀의 필멸의 육신은 불멸의 광채가 뿜는 광휘를 견딜 수 없었다. 그녀는 한 줌 재로 타 버렸다.\n유피테르는 갓난아기 바쿠스를 데려다 니사의 님프들에게 맡겨 길렀고, 님프들은 그의 영아기와 유년기를 보살펴 키웠으며, 그 보살핌의 대가로 유피테르는 그들을 히아데스로 삼아 별들 사이에 두어 보답하였다. 바쿠스는 자라나 포도 재배법과 그 귀한 즙을 짜내는 방법을 발견하였다. 그러나 유노가 그를 광기로 후려쳐, 그를 떠돌이로 만들어 땅의 여러 곳을 헤매게 하였다. 프리기아에서 여신 레아가 그를 치유하고 자신의 종교 의식을 가르쳐 주었고, 그는 아시아 전역을 두루 돌며 사람들에게 포도 재배법을 가르치는 여정에 나섰다. 그의 방랑 가운데 가장 유명한 부분은 인도 원정으로, 이 원정은 여러 해에 걸쳐 이어졌다고 전한다. 의기양양하게 돌아온 그는 자신의 숭배를 그리스에 들여오려 하였으나, 그것이 함께 가져오는 무질서와 광기를 두려워한 몇몇 제후들의 반대에 부딪혔다.\n\n그가 자신의 고향 도시 테바이에 다가갔을 때, 새로운 숭배에 아무런 경의도 품지 않았던 왕 펜테우스는 그 의식의 거행을 금하였다. 그러나 바쿠스가 다가오고 있다는 것이 알려지자, 남자도 여자도, 그러나 주로 여자들이, 젊은이도 늙은이도 그를 맞이하고 그의 개선 행렬에 합류하고자 쏟아져 나왔다.\n\n롱펠로 씨는 그의 「술의 노래」에서 바쿠스의 행렬을 이렇게 묘사한다.\n\n\"파우누스들이 젊은 바쿠스를 뒤따르니, 담쟁이덩굴 관이 그 이마를 둘렀구나, 아폴론의 이마처럼 드높고 거룩하며, 영원한 젊음을 지녔도다.\n\n\"그를 둘러싸고 아름다운 박코스의 무녀들이, 심벌즈와 피리와 튀르소스를 들고서, 잔테의 낙소스 숲, 포도밭에서 거칠게 뛰쳐나와, 황홀한 노래를 부르네.\"\n\n펜테우스가 항의하고 명령하고 위협한 것은 모두 헛일이었다. \"가서,\" 그는 시종들에게 말했다. \"저 떠돌이 무리의 우두머리를 붙잡아 내 앞으로 끌고 오너라. 내 곧 그가 하늘의 혈통을 거짓으로 주장한다는 것을 자백하게 하고, 그 가짜 숭배를 버리게 하리라.\" 그의 가장 가까운 벗들과 가장 지혜로운 조언자들이 항의하며 그 신에게 맞서지 말라고 간청한 것도 헛일이었다. 그들의 항의는 오히려 그를 더욱 격렬하게 만들 뿐이었다.\n\n그런데 이제 그가 바쿠스를 붙잡으라고 보냈던 시종들이 돌아왔다. 그들은 박코스의 무리에게 쫓겨났으나, 그중 한 사람을 사로잡는 데에는 성공하여, 손을 등 뒤로 묶은 채 왕 앞으로 끌고 왔다. 펜테우스는 노여운 얼굴로 그를 바라보며 말했다. \"이놈! 너는 곧 죽임을 당할 것이니, 네 운명이 남들에게 본보기가 되리라. 허나 네 처벌을 미루는 것이 아깝긴 하다만, 말하라, 네가 누구인지, 그리고 네가 감히 거행하려는 이 새로운 의식이 무엇인지 우리에게 일러라.\"\n\n붙잡힌 자는 겁먹지 않고 대답했다. \"제 이름은 아케테스이며, 제 고향은 마이오니아입니다. 제 부모는 가난한 사람들이라 제게 물려줄 밭도 가축도 없었고, 다만 낚싯대와 그물, 그리고 어부의 생업을 물려주었습니다. 저는 한동안 그 일을 따랐으나, 한곳에 머무는 것이 싫증나면서 키잡이의 기술과 별을 보고 항로를 잡는 법을 익혔습니다. 마침 제가 델로스로 항해하던 중에 우리는 디아 섬에 닿아 뭍에 올랐습니다. 이튿날 아침 저는 부하들을 보내 신선한 물을 길어 오게 하고, 저 자신은 바람을 살피러 언덕에 올랐습니다. 그때 부하들이 그들 생각으로는 노획물이라며 한 소년을 데리고 돌아왔는데, 곱상한 외모의 그 소년은 잠든 채로 발견된 것이었습니다. 그들은 그가 고귀한 젊은이, 어쩌면 왕의 아들일 거라 짐작하고, 그를 데려가면 후한 몸값을 받을 수 있으리라 여겼습니다. 저는 그의 옷차림과 걸음걸이와 얼굴을 살폈습니다. 거기에는 필멸의 인간을 넘어서는 무언가가 분명히 있다고 저는 확신했습니다. 저는 부하들에게 말했습니다. '저 모습 속에 어떤 신이 숨어 있는지는 모르겠으나, 누군가 분명히 깃들어 있다. 너그러운 신이시여, 우리가 당신께 저지른 폭행을 용서하시고, 우리의 일에 성공을 내려 주소서.' 돛대를 오르내리고 밧줄을 타고 내려오는 데 가장 솜씨 좋은 디크티스와, 키잡이 멜란투스와, 뱃사람의 구령을 이끄는 에포페우스가, 너 나 할 것 없이 모두 외쳤습니다. '우리를 위한 기도는 그만두시오.' 이익을 향한 욕심이란 이토록 눈을 멀게 하는 것입니다! 그들이 소년을 배에 태우려 하자 저는 그들에게 맞섰습니다. '이 배가 그런 불경으로 더럽혀져서는 안 된다,' 제가 말했습니다. '이 배에 대한 내 몫이 너희 누구보다도 크다.' 그러나 난폭한 자 리카바스가 제 목을 움켜쥐고 저를 배 밖으로 던지려 했고, 저는 가까스로 밧줄에 매달려 목숨을 건졌습니다. 나머지 자들은 그 짓을 두둔했습니다.\n\n\"그때 바쿠스가 (정말로 그였습니다) 마치 졸음을 떨쳐 내듯 외쳤습니다. '나에게 무슨 짓을 하는 것이냐? 이 다툼은 무엇 때문이냐? 누가 나를 이리로 데려왔느냐? 너희는 나를 어디로 데려가려는 것이냐?' 그들 중 하나가 대답했습니다. '아무것도 두려워 마라. 어디로 가고 싶은지 말하면 그곳으로 데려다주마.' '낙소스가 내 고향이다,' 바쿠스가 말했습니다. '나를 그곳으로 데려다주면 너희에게 후히 보답하리라.' 그들은 그리하겠다고 약속하고는, 저더러 배를 낙소스로 몰라고 일렀습니다. 낙소스는 오른편에 있었고, 저는 우리를 그리로 데려가도록 돛을 다루고 있었는데, 그때 어떤 자들은 손짓으로, 또 어떤 자들은 속삭임으로, 제가 반대 방향으로 배를 몰아 그 소년을 이집트로 데려가 노예로 팔아 버리기를 바라는 그들의 뜻을 제게 전했습니다. 저는 어찌할 바를 몰라 말했습니다. '누구든 다른 사람이 배를 몰아라.' 그러고는 그들의 악행에 더 이상 가담하지 않으려 물러섰습니다. 그들은 저를 저주하였고, 그중 하나가 '우리가 안전을 너에게 의지한다고 우쭐대지 마라' 하고 외치며 제 자리를 차지하여 키를 잡고는, 낙소스에서 멀어져 갔습니다.\n\n\"그러자 신은 자신이 그들의 배신을 막 알아챈 척하며, 바다 너머를 내다보고는 울먹이는 목소리로 말했습니다. '뱃사람들이여, 이곳은 너희가 나를 데려다주겠다 약속한 해안이 아니다. 저기 저 섬은 내 고향이 아니다. 너희가 나를 이리 대하다니, 내가 무슨 짓을 했단 말이냐? 가엾은 소년 하나를 속여서 너희가 얻을 영광이란 보잘것없으리라.' 저는 그 말을 듣고 눈물을 흘렸으나, 선원들은 우리 둘을 비웃으며 배를 바다 위로 빠르게 몰았습니다. 그때 갑자기—이상하게 들릴지 모르나, 이것은 사실입니다—배가 바다 한가운데에서, 마치 땅에 박힌 듯이 단단히 멈춰 섰습니다. 사람들은 놀라 노를 젓고 돛을 더 펼쳐, 그 둘의 힘으로 나아가려 애썼으나 모두 헛일이었습니다. 담쟁이덩굴이 노를 휘감아 그 움직임을 막았고, 무거운 열매송이를 단 채 돛에 들러붙었습니다. 포도를 가득 단 덩굴이 돛대를 타고 올라, 배의 양쪽 옆을 따라 뻗어 갔습니다. 피리 소리가 들려왔고, 향기로운 포도주 내음이 사방에 퍼졌습니다. 신 자신은 포도 잎으로 엮은 화관을 쓰고, 손에는 담쟁이덩굴을 감은 창을 들고 있었습니다. 호랑이들이 그의 발치에 웅크렸고, 스라소니와 점박이 표범의 형상이 그를 둘러싸고 노닐었습니다. 사람들은 공포에, 혹은 광기에 사로잡혔습니다. 어떤 자들은 배 밖으로 뛰어내렸고, 또 같은 짓을 하려던 다른 자들은 물속에 있는 동료들이 변해 가는 것을, 몸이 납작해지고 구부러진 꼬리로 끝나는 것을 보았습니다. 한 사람이 외쳤습니다. '이게 무슨 기적이란 말이냐!' 그리고 그가 말하는 동안 그의 입이 넓어지고, 콧구멍이 벌어지고, 비늘이 온몸을 뒤덮었습니다. 또 한 사람은 노를 당기려다 손이 오그라들더니 이내 손이 아닌 지느러미가 되는 것을 느꼈습니다. 또 다른 자는 밧줄을 향해 팔을 들어 올리려다 자기에게 팔이 없음을 깨닫고는, 잘린 몸을 구부려 바다로 뛰어들었습니다. 그의 다리였던 것이 초승달 모양 꼬리의 두 끝이 되었습니다. 선원 전체가 돌고래가 되어 배 주위를 헤엄쳤는데, 어떤 때는 수면 위로, 어떤 때는 그 아래로 오가며 물보라를 흩뿌리고, 넓은 콧구멍으로 물을 내뿜었습니다. 스무 명 가운데 저 혼자만 남았습니다. 두려움에 떠는 제게 신은 용기를 북돋아 주었습니다. '두려워 마라,' 그가 말했습니다. '낙소스를 향해 키를 잡아라.' 저는 그 말을 따랐고, 우리가 그곳에 다다랐을 때 저는 제단에 불을 지피고 바쿠스의 신성한 의식을 거행했습니다.\"\n\n여기서 펜테우스가 외쳤다. \"우리가 이 어리석은 이야기에 시간을 충분히 허비했다. 저놈을 끌어내어 지체 없이 처형하라.\" 아케테스는 시종들에게 끌려가 옥에 단단히 갇혔다. 그러나 그들이 처형 도구를 준비하는 동안, 옥문이 저절로 열리고 그의 사지에서 쇠사슬이 떨어져 나갔으며, 그들이 그를 찾았을 때 그는 어디에서도 보이지 않았다.\n\n펜테우스는 아무런 경고도 받아들이지 않고, 다른 이들을 보내는 대신 직접 그 제전의 현장으로 가기로 결심했다. 키타이론 산은 온통 숭배자들로 들끓었고, 박코스의 무리가 지르는 외침이 사방에서 울려 퍼졌다. 그 소음은, 나팔 소리가 군마의 투지를 돋우듯, 펜테우스의 분노를 돋우었다. 그는 숲을 헤치고 들어가 탁 트인 공터에 이르렀고, 그곳에서 광란의 제전의 주된 광경이 그의 눈에 들어왔다. 바로 그 순간 여자들이 그를 보았다. 그리고 그들 가운데 가장 먼저, 신에 의해 눈이 먼 그의 친어머니 아가베가 소리쳤다. \"저기 멧돼지를 보아라, 이 숲을 어슬렁대는 가장 거대한 괴물이로다! 자, 가자, 자매들이여! 내가 가장 먼저 저 멧돼지를 치리라.\" 무리 전체가 그에게 달려들었고, 그가 이제는 덜 오만하게 말하고, 이제는 변명하며, 이제는 자신의 죄를 자백하고 용서를 빌었으나, 그들은 그를 짓누르며 상처를 입혔다. 어머니로부터 자신을 지켜 달라고 이모들에게 외쳤으나 헛일이었다. 아우토노에가 한쪽 팔을, 이노가 다른 쪽 팔을 붙잡았고, 그 사이에서 그는 갈가리 찢겼으며, 그의 어머니는 외쳤다. \"승리다! 승리다! 우리가 해냈다, 영광은 우리의 것이다!\"\n\n이렇게 하여 바쿠스의 숭배가 그리스에 자리 잡았다.\n\n바쿠스와 뱃사람들의 이야기에 대한 암시는 밀턴의 「코머스」 46행에서 찾아볼 수 있다. 키르케의 이야기는\n\n# Chapter XXIX.\n\n맨 처음 자줏빛 포도알에서 그릇되이 쓰인 술의 달콤한 독을 짜낸 디오니소스는, 토스카나의 뱃사람들을 변모시킨 뒤, 바람이 이끄는 대로 티레네 해안을 따라 항해하다 키르케의 섬에 이르렀으니 (태양의 딸 키르케를 그 누가 모르랴? 그녀의 마법의 잔을 맛본 자는 누구든 곧은 형상을 잃고 땅을 기는 돼지로 굴러떨어졌노라).\n\n테세우스 이야기에서 우리는, 미노스 왕의 딸 아리아드네가 테세우스를 도와 미궁에서 빠져나오게 한 뒤, 그에게 이끌려 낙소스 섬으로 갔다가 잠든 채 그곳에 버려졌으며, 은혜를 모르는 테세우스는 그녀를 남겨 둔 채 홀로 귀향길을 재촉했음을 이미 보았다. 잠에서 깨어나 홀로 버려진 것을 안 아리아드네는 슬픔에 몸을 내맡겼다. 그러나 아프로디테[로마의 〈베누스〉]가 그녀를 가엾이 여겨, 잃어버린 필멸의 연인 대신 불멸의 연인을 얻으리라는 약속으로 그녀를 위로해 주었다.\n\n아리아드네가 버려진 섬은 디오니소스가 가장 아끼던 섬으로, 티레네의 뱃사람들이 음험하게 그를 사로잡으려 했을 때 그가 데려다 달라고 했던 바로 그 섬이었다. 아리아드네가 자신의 운명을 한탄하며 앉아 있을 때, 디오니소스가 그녀를 발견하여 위로하고는 자신의 아내로 삼았다. 결혼 선물로 그는 보석으로 장식된 황금 왕관을 주었으며, 그녀가 죽자 그 왕관을 거두어 하늘로 던져 올렸다. 왕관이 솟아오르는 동안 보석들은 더욱 밝게 빛나 별로 변하였고, 그 형상을 고스란히 간직한 아리아드네의 왕관은 무릎 꿇은 헤라클레스와 뱀을 쥔 사내 사이에서 별자리가 되어 하늘에 붙박이게 되었다.\n\n스펜서는 아리아드네의 왕관을 언급하지만, 그의 신화 서술에는 몇 가지 오류가 있다. 켄타우로스족과 라피타이족이 다툰 것은 테세우스의 결혼식이 아니라 페이리토오스의 결혼식에서였다.\n\n보라, 아리아드네가 쓴 왕관을, 테세우스가 그녀를 신부로 맞이하던 바로 그날 그녀의 상아빛 이마 위에 얹혔던 그 왕관을, 그때 담대한 켄타우로스족은 사나운 라피타이족과 피비린내 나는 싸움을 벌여 그들을 떨게 하였으나, 이제 창공에 자리 잡아 밝은 하늘을 가로질러 그 빛살을 펼치며, 뭇 별들에게 하나의 장식이 되었으니, 별들은 그 둘레를 더없이 정연하게 돌고 있도다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXII\n\n판은 숲과 들판, 가축 떼와 목자들의 신으로, 동굴에서 거처하다가 산과 골짜기를 떠돌고, 사냥을 하거나 님프들의 춤을 이끌며 즐겼다. 그는 음악을 좋아했고, 앞서 보았듯이 시링크스, 곧 목동의 피리를 발명한 자였으며, 그 자신이 그것을 능숙하게 연주했다. 판은 숲에 거처하던 다른 신들과 마찬가지로, 밤에 숲을 지나가야 하는 일에 종사하는 자들에게 두려움의 대상이었다. 그런 풍경의 음울함과 적막함이 마음을 미신적인 공포로 기울게 만들기 때문이다. 그리하여 눈에 보이는 원인 없이 갑작스레 찾아드는 놀람은 판의 탓으로 돌려졌고, 〈패닉의 공포〉라 불렸다.\n\n판이라는 신의 이름이 〈전부〉를 뜻하므로, 판은 우주의 상징이자 자연의 화신으로 여겨지게 되었고, 더 나중에는 모든 신들과 이교 그 자체를 대표하는 자로 간주되기에 이르렀다.\n\n실바누스와 파우누스는 라틴의 신들로, 그 특성이 판의 그것과 거의 같으므로, 우리는 그들을 다른 이름으로 불리는 동일한 존재로 보아도 무방하다.\n\n판의 춤 상대였던 숲의 님프들은 님프 가운데 한 부류에 불과했다. 그 밖에도 시내와 샘을 관장하는 나이아데스, 산과 동굴의 님프인 오레아데스, 바다의 님프인 네레이데스가 있었다. 마지막에 든 세 부류는 불멸이었으나, 드리아데스 혹은 하마드리아데스라 불린 숲의 님프들은 그들의 거처였으며 그들과 함께 생겨난 나무가 죽으면 함께 스러지는 것으로 믿어졌다. 그러므로 함부로 나무를 베어 없애는 것은 불경한 짓이었고, 어떤 가중된 경우에는 가혹하게 벌해졌으니, 이제 우리가 기록하려는 에리시크톤의 사례가 바로 그러하다.\n\n밀턴은 태초의 창조를 묘사한 그 찬란한 대목에서, 자연의 화신으로서의 판을 이렇게 언급한다.\n\n\"……만유의 판이, 카리테스, 〈사계절〉 여신들과 손잡고 춤추며, 영원한 봄을 이끌었도다.\"\n\n또한 이브의 거처를 묘사하며 이렇게 노래한다.\n\n\"……더 그늘진 정자, 더 신성하고 더 외진 그곳을, 비록 꾸며 낸 것일망정, 판도 실바누스도 결코 잠든 적 없고, 님프도 파우누스도 깃든 적 없었도다.\" ─『실낙원』, 제4권.\n\n옛 이교 신앙의 한 가지 흐뭇한 특징은, 자연의 모든 작용 속에서 신의 손길을 더듬어 찾기를 즐겼다는 점이다. 그리스인들의 상상력은 땅과 바다의 온갖 영역을 신들로 가득 채웠고, 우리의 철학이 자연 법칙의 작용으로 돌리는 저 현상들을 그 신들의 손길에 돌렸다. 때때로 우리는 시적인 기분에 잠길 때, 그 변화를 아쉬워하고, 그러한 대체로써 머리가 얻은 만큼 가슴이 잃었다고 여기는 마음이 들기도 한다. 시인 워즈워스는 이러한 정서를 이렇게 힘차게 표현한다.\n\n\"……위대한 신이여, 나는 차라리 낡아 빠진 신조에 길러진 이교도가 되고 싶나이다. 그리하면 이 즐거운 풀밭에 서서, 나를 덜 외롭게 해 줄 광경들을 얼핏 볼 수 있으리니, 바다에서 솟아오르는 프로테우스를 보고, 늙은 트리톤이 휘감긴 뿔나팔 부는 소리를 들으리라.\"\n\n실러는 그의 시 「그리스의 신들」에서 옛 시대의 아름다운 신화가 무너진 것을 아쉬워하는 마음을 표현했고, 이는 기독교 시인 E. 바렛 브라우닝 여사의 응답을 불러일으켰으니, 「죽은 판」이라는 그녀의 시가 그것이다. 다음 두 연이 그 한 본보기이다.\n\n\"그대들을 정복하는 어떤 으뜸가는 〈아름다움〉을 고백하는 그대들의 아름다움에 의하여, 그대들의 거짓을 꿰뚫어 〈참됨〉에 이르는 우리의 웅대하고 영웅적인 짐작들에 의하여, 우리는 울지 않으리라! 대지는 굴러 저마다의 신의 후광을 물려받을 것이며, 판은 죽었도다. 대지는 그 젊음의 시절 곁에서 불려진 신화적 환상들을 자라나 넘어서노라. 그리고 저 정중한 옛이야기들은 참됨 곁에서는 한갓 따분하게 들릴 뿐이라. 포이보스의 전차는 그 길을 다하였도다! 시인들이여, 태양을 우러러보라! 판은, 판은 죽었도다.\"\n\n이 시구들은 어느 초기 기독교 전승에 바탕을 둔 것이다. 곧 천상의 무리가 베들레헴의 목자들에게 그리스도의 탄생을 알렸을 때, 그리스의 온 섬들을 두루 울리는 깊은 신음 소리가 위대한 판이 죽었음을, 그리고 올림포스의 모든 왕권이 폐위되어 여러 신들이 추위와 어둠 속을 떠돌도록 내쫓겼음을 알렸다는 것이다. 밀턴 또한 그의 「예수 강탄 찬가」에서 이렇게 노래한다.\n\n\"외로운 산들 너머로, 그리고 메아리치는 해변에는, 울음소리와 드높은 비탄이 들리었도다. 창백한 포플러로 가장자리 두른 신령 깃든 샘과 골짜기로부터, 떠나가는 정령이 한숨과 함께 보내어지고, 꽃 엮은 머리채를 쥐어뜯으며, 님프들이 뒤엉킨 덤불의 어스름 그늘에서 애곡하도다.\"\n에리시크톤\n\n에리시크톤은 불경한 자이자 신들을 업신여기는 자였다. 한번은 그가 감히 데메테르[로마의 〈케레스〉]에게 바쳐진 숲을 도끼로 더럽히려 들었다. 이 숲에는 그 자체로 하나의 숲처럼 보일 만큼 거대한 유서 깊은 떡갈나무 한 그루가 서 있었으니, 그 오래된 둥치가 높이 솟아 있어, 그 위에는 봉헌의 화환이 자주 내걸리고 나무의 님프에게 바치는 탄원자들의 감사를 새긴 비문이 새겨지곤 했다. 드리아데스도 손에 손을 잡고 그 둘레에서 자주 춤을 추었다. 그 둥치의 둘레는 열다섯 큐빗에 이르렀고, 다른 나무들이 덤불 위로 솟은 만큼이나 그 나무는 다른 나무들 위로 우뚝 솟아 있었다. 그러나 그 모든 것에도 불구하고 에리시크톤은 그것을 아껴야 할 까닭을 찾지 못하고, 하인들에게 그것을 베어 넘기라 명령했다. 그들이 머뭇거리는 것을 보자 그는 한 사람에게서 도끼를 낚아채더니, 이렇게 불경하게 외쳤다. \"이것이 여신의 사랑을 받는 나무이든 아니든 나는 개의치 않는다. 설령 이것이 여신 자신이라 해도, 내 앞길을 막아선다면 쓰러져야 하리라.\" 그렇게 말하며 그는 도끼를 치켜들었고, 떡갈나무는 부르르 떨며 신음을 토하는 듯했다. 둥치에 첫 도끼질이 떨어지자 그 상처에서 피가 흘러나왔다. 곁에 서 있던 모든 이들이 공포에 질렸고, 그 가운데 한 사람이 감히 나서서 그를 말리며 그 치명적인 도끼를 붙들었다. 에리시크톤은 경멸하는 눈빛으로 그에게 말했다. \"네 경건함의 보답을 받아라.\" 그러고는 나무에게서 거두었던 그 무기를 그자에게 돌려, 그의 몸을 여러 군데 베어 상처를 내고는 목을 베어 버렸다. 그러자 떡갈나무 한가운데서 목소리가 흘러나왔다. \"이 나무에 거처하는 나는 데메테르의 사랑을 받는 님프이니, 그대의 손에 죽어 가면서 그대에게 미리 이르노라, 응징이 그대를 기다리고 있느니라.\" 그러나 그는 자신의 죄악을 멈추지 않았고, 마침내 그 나무는 거듭된 도끼질에 쪼개지고 밧줄에 끌려, 요란한 소리와 함께 쓰러지며 넘어지는 결에 숲의 큰 부분을 깔아뭉갰다.\n\n드리아데스는 동무를 잃은 것과 숲의 자랑이 쓰러진 것에 망연자실하여, 모두 상복을 입고 떼 지어 데메테르에게 가서 에리시크톤에게 응징을 내려 달라고 빌었다. 여신은 고개를 끄덕여 응낙했고, 그녀가 머리를 숙이자 곡식이 가득한 들판에서 추수를 앞두고 익은 낟알들도 함께 고개를 숙였다. 여신은 너무도 끔찍한 응징을 꾸몄으니, 만일 그자 같은 죄인을 가엾이 여길 수 있다면 누구라도 그를 가엾이 여길 만한 것이었다. 곧 그를 〈굶주림〉에게 넘겨주는 것이었다. 데메테르 자신은 〈굶주림〉에게 다가갈 수 없었으니, 운명의 여신들이 이 두 여신은 결코 함께 만나지 못하도록 정해 두었기 때문이라, 여신은 산에서 오레아스 하나를 불러 이렇게 일렀다. \"얼음으로 뒤덮인 스키티아의 가장 먼 곳에, 나무도 없고 곡식도 없는 슬프고 메마른 지역이 있느니라. 거기에는 〈추위〉와 〈공포〉와 〈전율〉과 〈굶주림〉이 거처하느니라. 가서 마지막의 그것에게 에리시크톤의 뱃속을 차지하라고 일러라. 풍요로움도 그것을 누그러뜨리지 못하게 하고, 내 선물의 힘도 그것을 몰아내지 못하게 하라. 그 거리에 놀라지 말고(〈굶주림〉은 데메테르에게서 아주 멀리 거처하기 때문이다), 내 전차를 타고 가라. 그 용들은 날래고 고삐에 순종하니, 너를 짧은 시간에 공중으로 데려다 줄 것이니라.\" 그렇게 여신은 그녀에게 고삐를 건넸고, 그녀는 전차를 몰아 떠나 이내 스키티아에 다다랐다. 카우카소스 산에 이르자 그녀는 용들을 멈춰 세우고는, 돌투성이 들판에서 이빨과 발톱으로 메마른 풀포기를 뜯어내고 있는 〈굶주림〉을 발견했다. 그녀의 머리카락은 거칠었고, 두 눈은 움푹 꺼졌으며, 얼굴은 창백하고, 입술은 핏기가 없었으며, 턱은 먼지로 덮여 있었고, 살갗은 팽팽하게 당겨져 온갖 뼈마디가 다 드러나 보였다. 오레아스는 멀리서 그녀를 바라보며(감히 가까이 다가가지는 못했기에) 데메테르의 명령을 전했고, 비록 되도록 짧게 머물고 되도록 거리를 유지했음에도, 그녀는 배가 고파지기 시작하여, 용들의 머리를 돌려 테살리아로 몰고 돌아갔다.\n\n〈굶주림〉은 데메테르의 명령에 따라 공중을 가로질러 에리시크톤의 거처로 달려가, 그 죄인의 침실로 들어가서, 그가 잠들어 있는 것을 발견했다. 그녀는 날개로 그를 감싸 안고 자신을 그의 몸 안으로 불어넣어, 그의 핏줄 속에 독을 주입했다. 임무를 마치자 그녀는 풍요의 땅을 서둘러 떠나 자신이 익숙한 처소로 돌아갔다. 에리시크톤은 여전히 잠들어 있었고, 꿈속에서 음식을 갈망하며, 먹기라도 하듯 턱을 움직였다. 잠에서 깨어났을 때 그의 허기는 사납게 날뛰었다. 그는 한순간도 지체하지 않고 땅과 바다와 하늘이 내는 온갖 종류의 음식을 자기 앞에 차리게 했고, 먹는 와중에도 배고프다고 불평했다. 한 도시나 한 나라를 먹이기에 족할 만한 것도 그에게는 충분하지 않았다. 먹으면 먹을수록 그는 더 갈망했다. 그의 허기는 모든 강물을 받아들이고도 결코 차오르지 않는 바다와 같았고, 혹은 위에 쌓인 모든 땔감을 태우고도 여전히 더 게걸스레 탐하는 불길과 같았다.\n\n그의 재산은 그칠 줄 모르는 식욕의 요구 아래 빠르게 줄어들었으나, 그의 허기는 조금도 수그러들지 않았다. 마침내 그는 모든 것을 다 써 버리고 딸 하나만 남게 되었으니, 더 나은 부모에게 어울릴 만한 딸이었다. 그는 그 딸마저 팔아 버렸다. 딸은 사들인 자의 노예가 되는 것을 떳떳치 못하게 여겨, 바닷가에 서서 포세이돈[로마의 〈넵투누스〉]에게 두 손 들어 기도했다. 그가 그녀의 기도를 들었고, 새 주인이 멀지 않은 곳에서 조금 전까지 그녀를 눈여겨보고 있었음에도, 포세이돈은 그녀의 모습을 바꾸어 자기 일에 분주한 어부의 모습을 띠게 했다. 주인은 그녀를 찾다가 바뀐 모습의 그녀를 보고는, 그녀에게 말을 걸어 이렇게 물었다. \"착한 어부여, 머리를 풀어 헤치고 초라한 차림으로, 바로 그대가 선 그 자리쯤에 서 있던 처녀를 내가 방금 보았는데, 그 처녀가 어디로 갔소? 사실대로 말해 주오. 그리하면 그대에게 운수가 좋아 물고기 한 마리도 미끼만 물고 달아나는 일이 없으리다.\" 그녀는 자신의 기도가 응답받았음을 알아차리고, 제 자신에 관해 질문받는 것을 들으며 속으로 기뻐했다. 그녀는 이렇게 대답했다. \"용서하시오, 나그네여, 나는 낚싯줄에 너무 골몰해 있어 다른 것은 아무것도 보지 못했소. 그러나 한동안 이 근처에 나 말고 다른 어떤 여인이나 다른 사람이 있었다고 믿느니, 차라리 다시는 물고기 한 마리도 못 잡았으면 하오.\" 그는 속아 넘어가, 자신의 노예가 달아났다고 여기며 제 갈 길을 갔다. 그러자 그녀는 본디 제 모습으로 돌아왔다. 그녀의 아비는 딸이 여전히 자기 곁에 남아 있는 것과 딸을 팔아 얻은 돈마저 함께 손에 든 것을 매우 흡족해하며, 다시 그녀를 팔았다. 그러나 그녀는 팔릴 때마다 포세이돈의 은총으로 모습이 바뀌어, 어떤 때는 말로, 어떤 때는 새로, 어떤 때는 황소로, 또 어떤 때는 수사슴으로 변해, 사들인 자들에게서 빠져나와 집으로 돌아왔다. 굶주린 아비는 이 비열한 방법으로 음식을 마련했으나, 그것으로도 그의 욕구를 채우기에는 충분치 않았고, 마침내 허기에 떠밀려 자신의 사지를 뜯어 먹게 되었으니, 제 몸을 먹어 제 몸을 기르려 애쓰다가, 죽음이 그를 데메테르의 응징에서 풀어 줄 때까지 그러하였다.\n\n로이코스\n하마드리아데스는 입은 해를 응징할 줄 알았던 만큼이나 받은 도움도 알아볼 줄 알았다. 로이코스의 이야기가 이를 입증한다. 로이코스는 마침 막 쓰러지려는 떡갈나무 한 그루를 보고는, 하인들에게 그것을 떠받쳐 놓으라고 명령했다. 그 나무와 함께 막 스러질 참이었던 님프가 찾아와, 자기 목숨을 구해 준 그에게 고마움을 표하며 어떤 보답이든 청해 보라고 했다. 로이코스는 대담하게 그녀의 사랑을 청했고, 님프는 그의 바람을 들어주었다. 그와 동시에 그녀는 그에게 변함없이 한결같으라고 당부하며, 벌 한 마리가 자신의 전령이 되어, 그녀가 그와 함께할 자리를 허락할 때를 알려 주리라고 일렀다. 한번은 로이코스가 장기를 두고 있을 때 벌이 찾아왔는데, 그는 무심히 그것을 손짓으로 쫓아 버렸다. 이에 님프가 어찌나 분노했던지 그에게서 시력을 앗아 가 버렸다.\n\n우리 동향인 J. R. 로웰은 이 이야기를 자신의 비교적 짧은 시 가운데 하나의 소재로 삼았다. 그는 이렇게 그 시를 시작한다.\n\n\"이제 옛 그리스의 이 요정 전설을 들어 보라, 어느 아티카의 프리즈에 만대를 위해 새겨진 저 우아함의 불멸의 싱그러움처럼, 여전히 자유와 젊음과 아름다움으로 가득한 그 이야기를.\"\n물의 신들\n\n# THE WATER DEITIES\n\n오케아노스와 테티스는 물의 원소를 다스린 티탄들이었다. 제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]와 그 형제들이 티탄들을 무너뜨리고 그 권력을 차지했을 때, 포세이돈과 암피트리테가 오케아노스와 테티스를 대신하여 물의 통치권을 이어받았다.\n포세이돈\n\n포세이돈은 물의 신들의 우두머리였다. 그 권능의 상징은 삼지창, 곧 세 갈래로 끝이 뾰족한 창이었으니, 그는 그것으로 바위를 부수고, 폭풍을 불러일으키거나 가라앉히고, 해안을 뒤흔들곤 했다. 그는 말을 창조했고 경마의 수호신이었다. 그 자신의 말들은 청동의 발굽과 황금의 갈기를 지녔다. 그것들이 그의 전차를 끌어 바다 위를 달리면, 바다는 그의 앞에서 잔잔해졌고, 그러는 동안 심해의 괴물들이 그의 길 주위에서 뛰놀았다.\n암피트리테\n\n암피트리테는 포세이돈의 아내였다. 그녀는 네레우스와 도리스의 딸이자 트리톤의 어머니였다. 포세이돈은 암피트리테에게 구애하기 위해 돌고래를 타고 왔다. 그녀를 얻자 그는 돌고래를 별들 사이에 두어 그 보답을 했다.\n네레우스와 도리스\n\n# NEREUS AND DORIS\n\n네레우스와 도리스는 네레이데스의 부모였으니, 그 가운데 가장 이름난 이들은 암피트리테, 아킬레우스의 어머니인 테티스, 그리고 키클롭스 폴리페모스의 사랑을 받은 갈라테이아였다. 네레우스는 그 지혜와 진실과 정의를 사랑하는 마음으로 두드러져, 그리하여 〈장로〉라 불렸다. 예언의 능력 또한 그에게 주어진 것이었다.\n트리톤과 프로테우스\n\n# TRITON AND PROTEUS\n\n트리톤은 포세이돈과 암피트리테의 아들로, 시인들은 그를 그 아버지의 나팔수로 삼는다. 프로테우스 역시 포세이돈의 아들이었다. 그는 네레우스와 마찬가지로 그 지혜와 앞날의 일에 대한 지식으로 인해 바다의 〈장로〉라 일컬어진다. 그의 독특한 능력은 자신의 모습을 마음대로 바꾸는 것이었다.\n테티스\n\n네레우스와 도리스의 딸인 테티스는 너무도 아름다워 제우스 자신이 그녀에게 혼인을 청했다. 그러나 티탄 프로메테우스에게서 테티스가 제 아비보다 더 위대하게 자라날 아들을 낳으리라는 것을 알게 되자, 제우스는 구애를 단념하고 테티스가 필멸자의 아내가 되도록 정했다. 켄타우로스족인 키론의 도움으로 펠레우스는 그 여신을 신부로 얻는 데 성공했고, 그들의 아들이 바로 저 이름난 아킬레우스였다. 트로이아 전쟁을 다룬 우리의 장에서 드러나겠지만, 테티스는 그에게 신실한 어머니여서, 온갖 어려움 속에서 그를 돕고 처음부터 끝까지 그의 이익을 지켜 주었다.\n\n레우코테아와 팔라이몬\n카드모스의 딸이자 아타마스의 아내인 이노는, 미쳐 날뛰는 남편에게서 어린 아들 멜리케르테스를 품에 안고 달아나다가, 벼랑에서 바다로 몸을 던졌다. 신들은 가엾이 여겨 그녀를 레우코테아라는 이름의 바다의 여신으로 삼고, 그를 팔라이몬이라는 이름의 신으로 삼았다. 두 신 모두 난파에서 사람을 구하는 데 강력한 힘을 지닌 것으로 여겨져 뱃사람들의 기원을 받았다. 팔라이몬은 대개 돌고래를 탄 모습으로 그려졌다. 이스트미아 경기는 그를 기려 열렸다. 그는 로마인들에게 포르투누스라 불렸고, 항구와 해안을 관장하는 것으로 믿어졌다.\n\n밀턴은 「코머스」의 결말부 노래에서 이 모든 신들을 언급한다.\n\n\"……아리따운 사브리나여, 귀 기울여 우리에게 모습을 드러내라, 위대한 오케아노스의 이름으로, 대지를 뒤흔드는 포세이돈의 철퇴와, 테티스의 장중하고 위엄 있는 걸음걸이로, 백발의 네레우스의 주름진 얼굴과, 카르파토스 마법사의 갈고리로,[20] 비늘 덮인 트리톤의 휘감긴 소라와, 예언 잘하는 늙은 글라우코스의 주문으로, 레우코테아의 어여쁜 두 손과, 물가를 다스리는 그 아들로, 테티스의 번쩍이는 신 신은 발과, 세이레네스의 감미로운 노래로.\" 등등.\n\n「건강 보존의 기술」의 시인 암스트롱은, 건강의 여신 히게이아의 영감을 받아 나이아데스를 이렇게 기린다. 파이온은 아폴론과 아스클레피오스 둘 다의 이름이다.\n\n\"오라, 나이아데스여! 샘으로 인도하라! 자비로운 처녀들이여! 그대들의 선물을 노래하는 일이 남았으니(파이온이, 〈건강〉의 권능이 그리 명하노라), 그대들의 수정 같은 원소를 찬미하는 일이라. 오 위안을 주는 시냇물이여! 갈망하는 입술과 떨리는 손으로 기진하고 목마른 이들이 그대 안에서 새 생명을 들이켜고, 신선한 활기가 그 핏줄을 채우네. 시골의 옛 시대는 더 따뜻한 잔을 알지 못했고, 인류의 조상들도 더 따뜻한 것을 찾지 않았네. 온화한 평화 속에 행복하여 그 한결같은 나날은 열에 들뜬 환락과 병든 의기소침의 번갈아 드는 발작을 느끼지 못했고, 늘 평온하고 흡족하여 온갖 재앙에서 벗어나는 신성한 면제를 누리며, 긴 세월을 살았네. 그들의 유일한 운명은 무르익은 노년이었고, 죽음이라기보다 차라리 잠이었네.\"\n\n카메나이\n라틴인들은 이 이름으로 무사 여신들을 가리켰으나, 그 안에 다른 몇몇 신들, 주로 샘의 님프들도 포함시켰다. 에게리아도 그 가운데 하나였으니, 그녀의 샘과 동굴은 지금도 사람들에게 보여진다. 로마의 두 번째 왕인 누마가 이 님프의 은총을 입어 은밀한 만남을 가졌으며, 그 자리에서 그녀가 그에게 지혜와 법의 가르침을 일러 주었고, 그는 그것을 떠오르는 나라의 제도에 담아냈다고 전해진다. 누마가 죽은 뒤 님프는 시름에 잠겨 야위어 가다가 샘으로 변했다.\n\n바이런은 「차일드 해럴드」 제4편에서 에게리아와 그녀의 동굴을 이렇게 언급한다.\n\n\"여기 그대 거처했도다, 이 마법에 걸린 은신처에, 에게리아여! 그대의 천상의 가슴은 온통 그대 필멸의 연인의 머나먼 발걸음에 두근거렸도다. 자줏빛 한밤이 그 가장 별 많은 차일로 그 신비로운 만남을 가렸도다.\" 등등.\n\n테니슨 또한 「예술의 궁전」에서, 그 만남을 기다리는 왕가의 연인을 우리에게 얼핏 보여 준다.\n\n\"한 손을 귀에 갖다 댄 채, 숲의 님프를 보기에 앞서 그 발소리를 들으려, 토스카나의 왕은 멈춰 서서 귀 기울였도다, 지혜와 법에 관하여.\"\n바람들\n\n# THE WINDS\n\n그토록 많은 덜 활동적인 작용들조차 의인화되었으니, 바람들이 그러하지 못했으리라고는 생각할 수 없다. 그들은 북풍인 보레아스 혹은 아퀼로, 서풍인 제피로스 혹은 파보니우스, 남풍인 노토스 혹은 아우스테르, 그리고 동풍인 에우로스였다. 앞의 둘이 주로 시인들에게 기려졌으니, 전자는 거칢의 전형으로, 후자는 부드러움의 전형으로 그러했다. 보레아스는 님프 오리티이아를 사랑하여 연인의 역할을 해 보려 했으나, 변변찮은 성공밖에 거두지 못했다. 그에게는 부드럽게 숨 쉬는 것이 힘겨운 일이었고, 한숨짓는 것은 더더욱 가당치 않은 일이었다. 마침내 헛된 시도에 지친 그는 자신의 본래 성품을 그대로 드러내어, 그 처녀를 붙잡아 채어 갔다. 그들의 자식이 제테스와 칼라이스이니, 날개 달린 전사들로서 아르고호 원정에 동행하여, 저 흉측한 새들인 하르피이아이와의 싸움에서 큰 공을 세웠다.\n\n제피로스는 플로라의 연인이었다. 밀턴은 「실낙원」에서 아담이 잠에서 깨어 아직 잠들어 있는 이브를 바라보는 장면을 묘사하며 그들을 언급한다.\n\n\"……그는 옆으로 몸을 반쯤 일으켜 기댄 채, 다정한 사랑의 눈빛으로, 그녀 위로 넋을 잃고 몸을 굽혀, 깨어 있을 때나 잠들어 있을 때나 남다른 우아함을 뿜어내는 아름다움을 바라보았네. 그러고는, 제피로스가 플로라에게 숨결을 불어넣을 때처럼 부드러운 목소리로, 그녀의 손을 살며시 어루만지며, 이렇게 속삭였네. '깨어나오! 나의 가장 아리따운 이, 나의 배필, 내가 가장 늦게 찾은 이, 하늘이 준 마지막이자 가장 좋은 선물, 나의 영원히 새로운 기쁨이여.'\"\n\n「밤의 상념」의 시인 영 박사는, 게으르고 사치스러운 자들에게 이렇게 말한다.\n\n\"그대 여린 이들이여! 아무것도 견뎌 내지 못하는(그러면서 그대 자신은 더없이 견디기 힘든) 이들이여, 그대들을 위하여 겨울의 장미가 피어나야만 하고, …… ……그리고 비단처럼 부드러운 파보니우스가 더욱 부드럽게 숨 쉬거나, 아니면 꾸지람을 들어야만 하는도다!\"\n\n────\n\n# CHAPTER XXIII\n\n강의 신 아켈로오스는 자신의 환대하는 식탁에서 테세우스와 그 일행을 대접하던 중에 에리시크톤의 이야기를 들려주었는데, 이들은 강물이 불어나는 바람에 여행길이 지체되어 그곳에 머무르고 있었다. 이야기를 마치고 나서 그는 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"그런데 내가 직접 이 능력을 지닌 본보기인데, 어찌하여 남들의 변신 이야기를 늘어놓겠는가? 나는 때로 뱀이 되기도 하고, 때로는 머리에 뿔이 돋은 황소가 되기도 한다. 아니, 한때 그럴 수 있었다고 말해야 옳겠다. 지금은 뿔 하나를 잃어 하나밖에 남지 않았으니 말이다.\" 그러고는 신음하더니 입을 다물었다.\n\n테세우스가 그에게 슬픔의 까닭과 어떻게 뿔을 잃었는지를 물었다. 그러자 강의 신은 이렇게 대답했다. \"누가 자신의 패배를 이야기하고 싶어 하겠는가? 그래도 나는 주저하지 않고 내 패배를 들려주겠다. 나를 이긴 자가 위대한 인물이라는 생각으로 스스로를 위로하면서 말이다. 그자는 바로 헤라클레스였다. 그대들도 아마 데이아네이라의 명성을 들어 보았을 것이다. 처녀들 가운데 가장 아름다웠던 그녀를 차지하려고 수많은 구혼자들이 다투었다. 헤라클레스와 나도 그 가운데 끼어 있었고, 나머지는 우리 둘에게 양보했다. 그는 자신을 위해 제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]의 후손이라는 점과, 계모인 헤라[로마의 〈유노〉]가 강요한 과업을 능가하여 이룬 자신의 공적들을 내세웠다. 반면에 나는 처녀의 아버지에게 이렇게 말했다. '나를 보십시오. 나는 당신의 땅을 흐르는 강물의 왕입니다. 나는 이방의 해안에서 온 낯선 자가 아니라 이 나라에 속한 자, 당신 왕국의 일부입니다. 왕녀이신 헤라께서 나에게 적의를 품지 않고 무거운 과업으로 벌하지 않는다고 해서, 그것이 나에게 불리하게 작용해서는 안 됩니다. 저자로 말하자면, 제우스의 아들이라고 뽐내고 있으나, 그것은 거짓 주장이거나, 설령 사실이라 해도 그에게는 수치스러운 일입니다. 그것이 사실일 수 있는 길이란 오로지 그 어머니의 부끄러움을 통해서뿐이니 말입니다.' 내가 이렇게 말하자 헤라클레스는 나를 노려보며 가까스로 분노를 억눌렀다. '내 혀보다는 내 손이 더 나은 대답을 하리라.' 그가 말했다. '말로는 그대에게 승리를 양보하겠으나, 내 주장은 행동의 다툼에 맡기겠다.' 그렇게 말하면서 그가 나를 향해 다가왔고, 나는 앞서 그런 말을 해 놓은 터라 물러서기가 부끄러웠다. 나는 푸른 옷을 벗어 던지고 싸움에 임했다. 그는 나를 쓰러뜨리려고 때로는 내 머리를, 때로는 내 몸을 공격했다. 내 거대한 몸집이 나를 지켜 주어 그의 공격은 헛수고였다. 한동안 우리는 멈추었다가 다시 싸움으로 돌아갔다. 우리는 각자 자리를 지키며 물러서지 않으려고 발과 발을 맞대었고, 나는 그의 위로 몸을 굽혀 그의 손을 내 손으로 움켜쥐고 내 이마를 거의 그의 이마에 맞닿을 정도로 가져갔다. 헤라클레스는 세 번이나 나를 떼어 내려 했고, 네 번째에 성공하여 나를 땅에 쓰러뜨리고는 자신이 내 등 위에 올라탔다. 진실로 말하건대, 마치 산 하나가 내 위에 무너져 내린 것 같았다. 나는 헐떡이며 땀에 젖어 김을 내뿜으면서 팔을 자유롭게 빼내려고 안간힘을 썼다. 그는 내가 회복할 틈을 주지 않고 내 목을 움켜쥐었다. 내 무릎은 땅에 닿았고 내 입은 흙먼지 속에 처박혔다.\n\n\"전사의 기술로는 그의 적수가 되지 못함을 깨닫고, 나는 다른 방법에 의지하여 뱀의 모습으로 미끄러져 빠져나갔다. 나는 몸을 똬리로 감고 갈라진 혀로 그에게 쉭쉭거렸다. 그는 이것을 보고 비웃으며 말했다. '뱀을 정복하는 것은 내 갓난 시절의 과업이었다.' 그렇게 말하면서 그는 두 손으로 내 목을 움켜쥐었다. 나는 거의 목이 졸려 그의 손아귀에서 목을 빼내려고 발버둥쳤다. 이 모습으로도 패하자, 나는 내게 남은 단 하나의 방법을 시도하여 황소의 모습을 취했다. 그는 팔로 내 목을 휘감고는 내 머리를 땅으로 끌어내려 모래밭에 나를 쓰러뜨렸다. 그것으로도 모자랐다. 그의 무자비한 손이 내 머리에서 뿔을 뜯어냈다. 나이아데스가 그 뿔을 가져다 봉헌하고 향기로운 꽃들로 가득 채웠다. 풍요의 여신이 내 뿔을 받아들여 자기 것으로 삼고는 그것을 '코르누코피아[풍요의 뿔]'라고 불렀다.\"\n\n고대인들은 자신들의 신화 이야기 속에서 숨은 의미를 찾기를 좋아했다. 그들은 아켈로오스와 헤라클레스의 이 싸움을 이렇게 설명한다. 즉 아켈로오스는 비가 오는 철이면 둑을 넘쳐흐르던 강이었다는 것이다. 신화에서 아켈로오스가 데이아네이라를 사랑하여 그녀와의 결합을 구했다고 말할 때, 그 뜻은 그 강이 굽이쳐 흐르면서 데이아네이라 왕국의 일부를 지나갔다는 것이다. 그 강이 뱀의 모습을 취했다고 한 것은 그 강이 구불구불 굽이졌기 때문이고, 황소의 모습을 취했다고 한 것은 그 강이 흐르는 동안 요란하게 으르렁거리는 소리를 냈기 때문이다. 강물이 불어나면 또 다른 물길을 냈다. 그래서 그 머리에 뿔이 돋았다는 것이다. 헤라클레스는 둑과 운하로 이 주기적인 범람의 재발을 막았다. 그래서 그가 강의 신을 정복하고 그 뿔을 잘라 냈다고 한 것이다. 마침내 예전에는 범람에 시달렸으나 이제는 되찾은 그 땅이 매우 비옥해졌으니, 이것이 바로 풍요의 뿔이 뜻하는 바다.\n\n코르누코피아의 기원에 관해서는 또 다른 이야기가 있다. 제우스는 태어날 때 어머니 레아에 의해 크레타의 왕 멜리세우스의 딸들의 보살핌에 맡겨졌다. 그들은 아말테이아라는 암염소의 젖으로 갓난 신을 길렀다. 제우스는 그 염소의 뿔 하나를 부러뜨려 자신의 유모들에게 주고는, 그것을 지닌 자가 원하는 무엇으로든 가득 채워지는 놀라운 힘을 부여했다.\n\n아말테이아라는 이름은 어떤 작가들에 의해 디오니소스[로마의 〈바쿠스〉]의 어머니에게도 붙여진다. 밀턴이 「실낙원」 제4권에서 그렇게 쓰고 있다.\n\n\". . . 그 니세이아의 섬, 트리톤 강에 둘러싸인 곳, 그곳에서 늙은 함은, 이방인들이 암몬이라, 또 리비아의 유피테르라 부르는 그가, 아말테이아와 그녀의 발그레한 아들, 어린 디오니소스를 그의 계모 레아의 눈을 피해 숨겼노라.\"\n\n# ADMETUS AND ALCESTIS\n\n아폴론의 아들 아스클레피오스는 아버지에게서 치유의 기술에 관해 어찌나 뛰어난 솜씨를 부여받았던지 죽은 자를 되살려 내기까지 했다. 이에 하데스[로마의 〈플루톤〉]가 놀라 제우스를 설득하여 아스클레피오스에게 벼락을 내리치게 했다. 아폴론은 자기 아들이 죽임을 당한 것에 분개하여, 그 벼락을 만든 죄 없는 일꾼들에게 복수를 퍼부었다. 이들은 키클로페스로, 아이트나 산 아래에 작업장을 두고 있어 그곳에서는 그들의 화로에서 나오는 연기와 불길이 끊임없이 솟아오른다. 아폴론이 키클로페스에게 화살을 쏘았고, 이에 제우스가 어찌나 격노했던지 그 벌로 그를 일 년 동안 한 필멸자의 종이 되라고 선고했다. 그리하여 아폴론은 테살리아의 왕 아드메토스의 종이 되어, 암프리소스 강의 푸른 둑에서 그를 위해 가축 떼를 쳤다.\n\n아드메토스는 다른 이들과 더불어 펠리아스의 딸 알케스티스에게 구혼한 자였는데, 펠리아스는 사자들과 멧돼지들이 끄는 전차를 타고 그녀를 데리러 오는 자에게 그녀를 주겠노라고 약속했다. 아드메토스는 자신의 신성한 목자의 도움으로 이 과업을 해냈고, 알케스티스를 얻어 행복을 누렸다. 그러나 아드메토스가 병이 들어 죽음에 가까워지자, 아폴론은 운명의 여신들을 설득하여 누군가가 그를 대신해 죽기로 동의한다는 조건으로 그를 살려 주게 했다. 아드메토스는 이렇게 죽음을 면하게 된 것에 기뻐하여 그 대가에 대해서는 별로 생각하지 않았으며, 어쩌면 신하들과 부하들에게서 자주 들어 온 충성의 맹세를 떠올리면서 대신할 자를 찾기가 쉬우리라 여겼는지도 모른다. 그러나 그렇지 않았다. 왕을 위해서라면 기꺼이 목숨을 걸었을 용감한 전사들도 병상에서 그를 대신해 죽는다는 생각 앞에서는 움츠러들었다. 또한 어릴 적부터 그와 그 가문의 은덕을 입어 온 늙은 종들도 자신들의 얼마 남지 않은 여생을 내놓아 감사를 표하려 하지 않았다. 사람들은 물었다. \"어찌하여 그의 부모 가운데 한 사람이 그리하지 않는가? 그들은 자연의 이치상 그리 오래 살 수 없으니, 그들만큼 자기들이 준 생명을 때 이른 종말에서 구해 내야 한다는 부름을 절실히 느낄 수 있는 자가 누구겠는가?\" 그러나 부모는 그를 잃는다는 생각에 비통해하면서도 그 부름 앞에서 움츠러들었다. 그러자 알케스티스가 너그러운 자기희생으로 자신을 대신할 자로 내놓았다. 아드메토스는 그만큼 삶을 아꼈으나, 그런 대가를 치르고 삶을 받아들이려 하지는 않았을 것이다. 그러나 다른 방도가 없었다. 운명의 여신들이 내건 조건은 충족되었고, 그 결정은 돌이킬 수 없었다. 아드메토스가 되살아남에 따라 알케스티스는 병들었고, 그녀는 빠르게 무덤으로 가라앉고 있었다.\n\n바로 이때 헤라클레스가 아드메토스의 궁전에 당도하여, 헌신적인 아내이자 사랑받는 안주인을 곧 잃게 되리라는 생각에 온 집안 식구들이 깊은 슬픔에 빠져 있는 것을 보았다. 어떤 과업도 너무 힘겹다고 여기지 않는 헤라클레스는 그녀를 구해 내기로 결심했다. 그는 죽어 가는 왕비의 침실 문 앞에 가서 매복했고, 죽음의 신이 자신의 먹이를 가지러 오자 그를 붙잡아 자신의 희생물을 내놓도록 강요했다. 알케스티스는 회복되어 남편에게 돌아왔다.\n\n밀턴은 「세상을 떠난 자기 아내에 관하여」라는 소네트에서 알케스티스의 이야기를 언급한다.\n\n\"내가 보았노라, 얼마 전 혼인한 나의 성녀가 무덤에서 알케스티스처럼 내게로 돌아오는 것을, 제우스의 위대한 아들이 기쁨에 찬 남편에게 돌려준 그녀, 창백하고 기력 없으나, 힘으로 죽음에서 구해 낸 그녀를.\"\n\nJ. R. 로웰은 「아드메토스 왕의 목자」를 짧은 시의 주제로 택했다. 그는 그 사건을 시가 인간에게 처음으로 소개된 일로 그린다.\n\n\"사람들은 그를 한낱 쓸모없는 젊은이라 불렀으니, 그에게서 아무런 좋은 점도 보지 못했네. 그러나 자기들도 모르는 사이에, 진실로, 그들은 그의 무심한 말들을 자기들의 법으로 삼았네. \"그리고 나날이 더욱 거룩해졌네, 그가 발 디딘 곳마다. 마침내 후세의 시인들만이 알게 되었네, 그들의 맏형이 신이었음을.\"\n\n전설 속 그리스의 흥미로운 인물들과 고귀한 행적들 가운데 상당수가 여성에게 속한다. 알케스티스가 부부간의 헌신의 본보기였듯이, 안티고네는 자식으로서, 또 누이로서의 충실함의 빛나는 본보기였다. 그녀는 오이디푸스와 이오카스테의 딸이었는데, 이들은 모든 후손과 더불어 가차 없는 운명의 희생자가 되어 파멸의 선고를 받았다. 오이디푸스는 광기에 사로잡혀 자신의 눈을 뽑아내고는, 신의 복수의 대상으로서 모든 사람에게 두려움과 버림을 받으며 자신의 왕국 테베에서 쫓겨났다. 그의 딸 안티고네만이 홀로 그의 방랑을 함께하며 그가 죽을 때까지 곁에 머물렀고, 그 뒤 테베로 돌아왔다.\n\n그녀의 오라비들인 에테오클레스와 폴리네이케스는 왕국을 둘이서 나누어 해마다 번갈아 다스리기로 합의했다. 첫해는 에테오클레스의 몫이 되었는데, 그는 자기 차례가 끝나자 동생에게 왕국을 넘겨주기를 거부했다. 폴리네이케스는 아르고스의 왕 아드라스토스에게로 도망쳤고, 아드라스토스는 그에게 자기 딸을 아내로 주고 군대로 도와 왕국에 대한 그의 권리를 관철하게 했다. 이로 인해 저 유명한 \"테베를 공격한 일곱 장수\"의 원정이 일어났으니, 이는 그리스의 서사 시인들과 비극 시인들에게 풍부한 소재를 제공했다.\n\n아드라스토스의 처남인 암피아라오스는 그 거사에 반대했다. 그는 예언자였기에 자신의 기술로 아드라스토스를 제외한 어떤 장수도 살아 돌아오지 못하리라는 것을 알았기 때문이다. 그러나 암피아라오스는 왕의 누이 에리필레와 혼인할 때, 자신과 아드라스토스의 의견이 엇갈릴 때면 언제나 그 결정을 에리필레에게 맡기기로 합의해 두었다. 이를 안 폴리네이케스는 에리필레에게 하르모니아의 목걸이를 주어 그녀를 자기편으로 끌어들였다. 이 목걸이는 헤파이스토스[로마의 〈불카누스〉]가 하르모니아와 카드모스의 혼인 때 그녀에게 준 선물이었는데, 폴리네이케스가 테베에서 도망칠 때 그것을 가지고 나갔던 것이다. 에리필레는 그토록 솔깃한 뇌물을 뿌리치지 못했고, 그녀의 결정으로 전쟁이 결정되었으며, 암피아라오스는 자신의 정해진 운명을 향해 나아갔다. 그는 싸움에서 자기 몫을 용감히 해냈으나, 자신의 숙명을 피할 수는 없었다. 적에게 쫓겨 강을 따라 달아나던 중에, 제우스가 던진 벼락이 땅을 갈라놓아 그와 그의 전차와 마부가 함께 삼켜졌다.\n\n이 싸움을 수놓은 영웅적인 행적이나 잔혹한 행적을 모두 여기서 자세히 늘어놓는 것은 적절하지 않을 것이다. 그러나 에리필레의 나약함에 대비되는 본보기로서 에우아드네의 충실함은 기록하지 않을 수 없다. 에우아드네의 남편 카파네우스는 싸움의 열기 속에서 제우스 그 자신이 막아서더라도 도성 안으로 밀고 들어가겠노라고 선언했다. 그는 성벽에 사다리를 걸고 올라갔으나, 그 불경한 말에 노한 제우스가 벼락으로 그를 쳤다. 그의 장례가 치러질 때, 에우아드네는 그의 화장용 장작더미 위로 몸을 던져 목숨을 끊었다.\n\n싸움 초기에 에테오클레스는 그 결말에 관해 예언자 테이레시아스에게 물었다. 테이레시아스는 젊은 시절에 우연히 아테나[로마의 〈미네르바〉]가 목욕하는 것을 본 적이 있었다. 여신은 노하여 그의 시력을 빼앗았으나, 나중에 마음이 누그러져 그 보상으로 그에게 미래의 일을 아는 능력을 주었다. 에테오클레스가 묻자, 그는 크레온의 아들 메노이케우스가 스스로를 제물로 바치면 승리가 테베에 돌아가리라고 선언했다. 그 영웅적인 젊은이는 그 답을 듣고 첫 교전에서 자신의 목숨을 내던졌다.\n\n포위는 엎치락뒤치락하며 오래 이어졌다. 마침내 양쪽 군대는 두 형제가 일대일 결투로 그들의 다툼을 결판내야 한다는 데 합의했다. 그들은 싸웠고 서로의 손에 쓰러졌다. 그러자 양쪽 군대가 다시 싸움을 벌였고, 결국 침략자들이 물러설 수밖에 없게 되어, 자기들의 죽은 자들을 묻지 못한 채 달아났다. 쓰러진 왕자들의 숙부인 크레온이 이제 왕이 되어, 에테오클레스는 각별한 영예로 매장하게 했으나, 폴리네이케스의 시신은 쓰러진 자리에 그대로 두게 하고는, 누구든 그것을 매장하면 죽음으로 다스리겠다며 매장을 금했다.\n\n폴리네이케스의 누이 안티고네는 자기 오라비의 시신을 개들과 독수리들에게 내맡겨, 죽은 자의 안식에 필수적인 것으로 여겨지던 의례를 박탈하는 그 혐오스러운 칙령을 분개하며 들었다. 애정 깊으나 소심한 자매의 만류하는 충고에도 흔들리지 않고, 또 도움을 구할 수도 없게 되자, 그녀는 위험을 무릅쓰고 자신의 손으로 시신을 묻기로 결심했다. 그녀는 그 일을 하다가 발각되었고, 크레온은 그녀가 도성의 엄숙한 칙령을 일부러 무시한 것이라 하여 산 채로 매장하라는 명을 내렸다. 그녀의 연인이자 크레온의 아들인 하이몬은 그녀의 운명을 막을 수 없게 되자 그녀보다 더 살려 하지 않고 제 손으로 목숨을 끊었다.\n\n안티고네는 그리스 시인 소포클레스의 두 편의 빼어난 비극의 주제를 이룬다. 제임슨 부인은 그녀의 「여성의 특성」에서 안티고네의 성격을 셰익스피어의 「리어 왕」에 나오는 코딜리아의 성격과 비교했다. 그녀의 견해를 읽어 보면 우리 독자들은 틀림없이 흡족할 것이다.\n\n다음은 죽음이 마침내 오이디푸스를 그 고통에서 벗어나게 했을 때, 안티고네가 그를 두고 부르는 애도의 노래다.\n\n\"아아! 나는 그저 가엾은 아버지와 함께 죽을 수 있었기를 바랐을 뿐. 어찌하여 내가 더 긴 삶을 구하겠는가? 오, 나는 그분과 함께라면 비참함조차 사랑했네. 가장 사랑스럽지 못한 것조차 사랑스러워졌으니 그분이 나와 함께 계셨을 때는. 오 나의 가장 사랑하는 아버지, 이제 땅 밑 깊은 어둠 속에 숨으신 분, 세월에 그토록 지치셨으나, 나에게는 여전히 소중하셨고, 영원히 소중하시리라.\" ─프랭클린이 옮긴 소포클레스.\n\n페넬로페는 또 한 사람의 신화적 여걸로, 그 아름다움이 외모보다는 성품과 행실에 있었던 인물이다. 그녀는 스파르타의 왕자 이카리오스의 딸이었다. 이타카의 왕 오디세우스가 그녀에게 구혼하여, 모든 경쟁자를 제치고 그녀를 얻었다. 신부가 친정을 떠날 순간이 오자, 이카리오스는 딸과 헤어진다는 생각을 견디지 못하고 그녀에게 자기 곁에 머물면서 남편을 따라 이타카로 가지 말라고 설득하려 했다. 오디세우스는 페넬로페에게 머물지 자신과 함께 갈지를 스스로 택하게 했다. 페넬로페는 아무 대답도 하지 않고 자기 얼굴 위로 베일을 드리웠다. 이카리오스는 더는 그녀를 졸라 대지 않았으나, 그녀가 떠난 뒤 그들이 헤어진 그 자리에 정숙의 여신상을 세웠다.\n\n오디세우스와 페넬로페는 부부의 연을 일 년도 채 누리지 못한 채, 오디세우스를 트로이아 전쟁으로 불러낸 사건들로 인해 그 결합이 중단되었다. 그의 오랜 부재 동안, 그가 아직 살아 있는지조차 의심스럽고 결코 돌아오지 못할 것이 거의 확실해 보일 무렵, 페넬로페는 수많은 구혼자들에게 시달렸으니, 그들 가운데 하나를 남편으로 택하는 것 말고는 빠져나갈 길이 없어 보였다. 그러나 페넬로페는 여전히 오디세우스의 귀환을 바라며 시간을 벌기 위해 온갖 방책을 썼다. 그녀가 쓴 지연책 가운데 하나는 남편의 아버지 라에르테스의 장례용 덮개로 쓸 옷을 짜는 일에 매달리는 것이었다. 그녀는 그 옷이 다 완성되면 구혼자들 가운데에서 택하겠노라고 맹세했다. 낮 동안에는 옷을 짰으나, 밤이면 낮에 짠 것을 도로 풀어 헤쳤다. 이것이 바로 그 유명한 페넬로페의 베틀로, 끊임없이 행해지나 결코 끝나지 않는 일을 가리키는 속담의 표현으로 쓰인다. 페넬로페에 관한 나머지 이야기는 그녀의 남편의 모험을 설명할 때 들려주기로 하겠다.\n\n————\n\n# CHAPTER XXIV\n\n오르페우스는 아폴론과 무사 칼리오페 사이에서 난 아들이었다. 그는 아버지에게서 리라를 선물로 받았고, 그것을 연주하는 법을 배워 너무도 완벽한 경지에 이르렀기에, 그 어떤 것도 그의 음악이 지닌 매력을 거스를 수 없었다. 그와 같은 필멸자 동료들뿐만 아니라 사나운 짐승들마저 그의 가락에 마음이 누그러져, 그의 주위에 모여들어 사나움을 내려놓고 그의 노래에 넋을 잃은 채 서 있었다. 아니, 나무와 바위까지도 그 매력을 느꼈다. 나무들은 그의 주위로 빽빽이 모여들었고, 바위들은 그의 가락에 누그러져 그 단단함을 다소 풀어 놓았다.\n\n히멘이 초대를 받아, 오르페우스와 에우리디케의 혼례에 임석하여 축복을 내리기로 되어 있었다. 하지만 그는 참석하기는 했어도, 어떤 행복한 징조도 함께 가져오지 않았다. 그의 횃불마저 연기를 피워 두 사람의 눈에 눈물이 맺히게 했다. 그런 불길한 조짐과 맞아떨어지듯, 에우리디케는 결혼한 지 얼마 지나지 않아 동무인 님프들과 함께 거닐던 중, 목동 아리스타이오스의 눈에 띄었다. 그는 그녀의 아름다움에 반해 그녀에게 다가갔다. 그녀는 도망치다가 풀밭에서 뱀을 밟았고, 발을 물려 죽고 말았다. 오르페우스는 천상의 공기를 들이쉬는 모든 이들에게, 신이든 인간이든 가리지 않고 자신의 슬픔을 노래로 불렀으나, 그 모든 것이 헛되다는 것을 깨닫고는 죽은 자들의 영역에서 아내를 찾기로 마음먹었다. 그는 타이나로스 곶 옆에 자리한 동굴을 통해 내려가 스틱스 강의 영역에 다다랐다. 그는 망령들의 무리를 헤치고 나아가 플루톤과 페르세포네의 옥좌 앞에 모습을 드러냈다. 리라에 맞춰 말을 노래로 풀어내며 그는 이렇게 읊었다. \"오, 저승의 신들이시여, 살아 있는 우리 모두가 마침내 찾아와야 할 곳을 다스리시는 분들이시여, 제 말을 들어 주소서, 제 말은 진실이기 때문입니다. 저는 타르타로스의 비밀을 엿보러 온 것도 아니요, 뱀 같은 머리털을 지닌 채 입구를 지키는 머리 셋 달린 개에게 제 힘을 시험해 보려고 온 것도 아닙니다. 저는 제 아내를 찾으러 왔습니다. 한창 피어나던 그녀의 세월을 독을 품은 살무사의 송곳니가 때 이르게 끝장내고 말았습니다. 사랑이 저를 이리로 이끌었으니, 사랑이란 지상에 사는 우리에게 전능한 신이며, 옛 전승이 참되다면 이곳에서도 그 힘이 결코 덜하지 않을 것입니다. 공포로 가득한 이 거처들에 걸고, 침묵과 아직 빚어지지 않은 것들의 이 영역들에 걸고 간청하오니, 에우리디케의 생명의 실을 다시 이어 주소서. 우리는 모두 그대들에게 정해진 몸이니, 머잖아 또는 언젠가는 그대들의 영토로 넘어가야 합니다. 그녀 역시 제 수명을 다 채우고 나면 마땅히 그대들의 것이 될 것입니다. 하지만 그때까지는 그녀를 제게 내어 주시기를 비나이다. 그대들이 저를 거절하신다면 저는 홀로 돌아갈 수 없으니, 그대들은 우리 두 사람의 죽음으로 승리를 거두게 될 것입니다.\"\n\n그가 이 애절한 가락을 노래하자, 망령들마저 눈물을 흘렸다. 탄탈로스는 갈증에도 불구하고 물을 향한 몸부림을 잠시 멈추었고, 익시온의 수레바퀴는 멈춰 섰으며, 독수리는 거인의 간을 쪼는 것을 그쳤고, 다나오스의 딸들은 체로 물을 긷는 일을 쉬었으며, 시시포스는 그의 바위에 걸터앉아 귀를 기울였다. 그때 처음으로, 전하는 바에 따르면, 푸리아이의 뺨이 눈물로 젖었다고 한다. 페르세포네도 마음이 흔들렸고, 플루톤마저 양보했다. 에우리디케가 불려 왔다. 그녀는 새로 도착한 망령들 사이에서, 상처 입은 발을 절뚝거리며 걸어 나왔다. 오르페우스는 한 가지 조건을 걸고 그녀를 데려가도 좋다는 허락을 받았으니, 바로 천상의 공기에 다다르기 전까지는 돌아서서 그녀를 바라보아서는 안 된다는 것이었다. 이 조건 아래 두 사람은 길을 나아갔고, 그가 앞장서고 그녀가 뒤따르며, 어둡고 가파른 통로들을 완전한 침묵 속에서 지나, 마침내 즐거운 천상의 세계로 나서는 출구에 거의 다다랐을 무렵이었다. 그때 오르페우스가 깜빡 잊은 한순간, 그녀가 여전히 뒤따르고 있는지 스스로 확인하려고 뒤를 한 번 흘끗 돌아보았고, 그러자마자 그녀는 끌려가 버렸다. 두 사람은 서로를 끌어안으려고 팔을 뻗었으나, 손에 잡힌 것은 허공뿐이었다! 두 번째로 죽음을 맞으면서도, 그녀는 남편을 탓할 수가 없었으니, 자신을 보고 싶어 한 그의 조바심을 어찌 나무랄 수 있겠는가? \"안녕히,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"마지막 작별이에요.\" 그러고는 너무도 빨리 끌려가 버려, 그 소리가 그의 귀에 가까스로 닿았다.\n\n오르페우스는 그녀를 뒤따르려 애썼고, 다시 돌아가 한 번 더 그녀의 석방을 시도하게 해 달라고 간청했으나, 무뚝뚝한 뱃사공은 그를 밀쳐 내며 건너가는 것을 거부했다. 그는 이레 동안 먹지도 자지도 않은 채 강가를 서성였다. 그러고는 에레보스의 신들이 무자비하다고 쓰라리게 책망하며, 바위와 산을 향해 자신의 한탄을 노래로 불러, 호랑이들의 마음을 녹이고 떡갈나무들을 제자리에서 움직이게 했다. 그는 여인들을 멀리하고, 자신의 슬픈 불운에 대한 기억에 끊임없이 잠겨 지냈다. 트라키아의 처녀들이 그를 사로잡으려고 갖은 애를 썼으나, 그는 그들의 접근을 물리쳤다. 처녀들은 견딜 수 있는 한 그를 참아 주었으나, 어느 날 그가 무덤덤한 것을 보고는, 디오니소스의 의식에 흥분한 그들 가운데 하나가 외쳤다. \"저기 우리를 멸시하는 자가 있다!\" 그러고는 그를 향해 투창을 던졌다. 그 무기는 그의 리라 소리가 미치는 범위에 들어서자마자, 아무 해도 끼치지 못한 채 그의 발치에 떨어졌다. 그들이 그에게 던진 돌멩이들도 마찬가지였다. 하지만 여인들이 비명을 질러 음악의 소리를 묻어 버리자, 그제야 날아온 것들이 그에게 가닿았고, 이내 그의 피로 물들었다. 그 광란하는 여인들은 그의 팔다리를 갈가리 찢어, 그의 머리와 리라를 헤브로스 강에 던져 넣었다. 그것들은 강을 따라 떠내려가며 구슬픈 음악을 흥얼거렸고, 강기슭은 애처로운 화음으로 화답했다. 무사이는 그의 몸의 조각들을 거두어 리베트라에 묻었으니, 그곳에서는 나이팅게일이 그의 무덤 위에서 그리스의 다른 어느 곳에서보다 더 감미롭게 노래한다고 전해진다. 그의 리라는 유피테르에 의해 별들 사이에 놓였다. 그의 혼령은 두 번째로 타르타로스로 건너갔고, 그곳에서 에우리디케를 찾아내어 간절한 팔로 그녀를 끌어안았다. 그들은 이제 행복한 들판을 함께 거니는데, 때로는 그가 앞장서고 때로는 그녀가 앞장서며, 오르페우스는 마음껏 그녀를 바라보아도 이제 더는 무심한 시선에 대한 벌을 받지 않는다.\n\n오르페우스의 이야기는 포프에게 그의 \"성 케칠리아 축일의 송가\"를 위한 음악의 힘에 관한 예시를 제공했다. 다음 연은 이야기의 결말을 노래한다.\n\n\"하지만 곧, 너무도 곧 연인은 그의 눈길을 돌리니, 그녀는 다시 쓰러지고, 다시 죽고, 또 죽는구나! 이제 그대 어찌 운명의 자매들을 움직이려는가? 그대에게는 죄가 없었으니, 사랑하는 것이 죄가 아니라면. 이제 매달린 산 아래에서, 샘물이 떨어지는 곳 곁에서, 혹은 헤브로스가 굽이쳐 구비를 따라 흘러가는 곳에서, 홀로 외로이, 그는 비탄을 토하며, 그녀의 혼령을 부르노라, 영원히, 영원히, 영원히 잃은 그녀를! 이제 푸리아이에 둘러싸여, 절망하고 혼미한 채, 그는 떨고, 그는 타오르노라, 로도페의 눈밭 한가운데서. 보라, 사막을 가로지르는 바람처럼 거칠게 그는 내달리고, 들으라! 하이모스가 바카날의 외침으로 메아리치는구나. 아, 보라, 그가 죽는구나! 하나 죽음 속에서도 그는 에우리디케를 노래했으니, 에우리디케가 여전히 그의 혀끝에서 떨렸도다. 에우리디케 하고 숲이, 에우리디케 하고 강물이, 에우리디케 하고 바위와 텅 빈 산들이 울려 퍼졌도다.\"\n\n오르페우스의 무덤 위에서 나이팅게일의 노래가 한층 빼어나다는 사실은 사우디가 그의 \"탈라바\"에서 넌지시 언급한 바 있다.\n\n\"그때 그의 귀에 어떤 화음의 소리가 솟아올랐던가! 먼 음악과 거리로 무르익은 노래가 흥겨운 나무 그늘에서 들려오고, 아득한 폭포 소리, 잎 무성한 숲의 속삭임, 장미 덤불에 앉은 한 마리 나이팅게일은 어찌나 풍성한 가락을 내던지, 알을 품은 짝에게 사랑 노래를 부르는 그 가장 선율 고운 새의 노랫소리보다, 오르페우스의 무덤 곁에서 트라키아의 목동이 들은 더 감미로운 가락은 없었으리라, 비록 그곳에서 무덤의 정령이 자신의 온갖 힘을 불어넣어, 그가 아끼는 향연을 부풀린다 하더라도.\"\n\n인간은 자신의 이로움을 위해 하등 동물들의 본능을 이용한다. 양봉의 기술이 여기서 비롯되었다. 꿀은 처음에는 야생의 산물로 알려졌을 것이 틀림없으니, 벌들은 속이 빈 나무나 바위의 구멍, 또는 우연히 마련된 비슷한 빈 공간에 그들의 구조물을 지었다. 그리하여 때로는 죽은 동물의 사체가 그러한 목적으로 벌들에게 차지되기도 했다. 죽은 짐승의 썩어 가는 살에서 벌들이 생겨난다는 미신이 일어난 것은 의심할 여지 없이 이러한 사건에서 비롯되었다. 그리고 베르길리우스는 다음 이야기에서, 질병이나 사고로 벌떼를 잃었을 때 그것을 되살리는 데 이 추정된 사실을 어떻게 활용할 수 있는지를 보여 준다.\n\n벌을 다루는 법을 처음 가르친 아리스타이오스는 물의 님프 키레네의 아들이었다. 그의 벌들이 죽어 버리자, 그는 도움을 구하러 어머니에게 의지했다. 그는 강가에 서서 어머니에게 이렇게 호소했다. \"오 어머니, 제 삶의 자랑거리가 제게서 떠나가 버렸습니다! 제 소중한 벌들을 잃었습니다. 제 정성과 솜씨도 제게 아무 소용이 없었고, 어머니마저 제게서 불운의 타격을 막아 주지 못하셨습니다.\" 그의 어머니는 강 밑바닥의 궁전에 앉아, 시중드는 님프들을 곁에 두고 이러한 한탄을 들었다. 그들은 실을 잣고 천을 짜는 여인의 일에 매여 있었고, 그동안 하나가 나머지를 즐겁게 해 주려고 이야기를 들려주고 있었다. 아리스타이오스의 슬픈 목소리가 그들의 일을 가로막자, 그들 가운데 하나가 물 위로 머리를 내밀고 그를 보고는, 되돌아가 그의 어머니에게 알렸고, 어머니는 그를 자신의 앞으로 데려오라고 명했다. 그녀의 명에 따라 강물이 스스로 갈라져 그를 들여보냈으니, 강물은 양옆으로 산처럼 굽이쳐 솟았다. 그는 큰 강들의 샘이 자리한 영역으로 내려갔다. 그는 어마어마한 물의 저장소들을 보았고, 그것들이 대지의 표면을 적시려고 사방으로 황급히 흩어져 가는 모습을 둘러보는 동안 그 굉음에 거의 귀가 먹먹할 지경이었다. 어머니의 거처에 다다르자, 그는 키레네와 그녀의 님프들에게 융숭한 대접을 받았으니, 그들은 더없이 풍성한 진미로 식탁을 차렸다. 그들은 먼저 넵투누스에게 헌주를 올린 다음, 그 잔치를 마음껏 즐겼고, 그 뒤에 키레네가 그에게 이렇게 말했다. \"프로테우스라는 이름의 늙은 예언자가 있는데, 그는 바다에 살며 넵투누스의 총애를 받아 그의 바다표범 떼를 친단다. 우리 님프들은 그를 크게 존경하니, 그는 박식한 현인이며 과거와 현재와 미래의 모든 것을 알고 있기 때문이지. 그는 네게, 내 아들아, 네 벌들 사이에 죽음이 닥친 원인과 네가 그것을 어떻게 바로잡을 수 있는지를 일러 줄 수 있을 게다. 하지만 네가 아무리 간청해도 그는 자진해서는 그리해 주지 않을 게야. 너는 그를 힘으로 강제해야 한다. 네가 그를 붙잡아 사슬로 묶으면, 그는 풀려나기 위해 네 물음에 차례차례 답할 게다. 네가 사슬을 단단히 붙들고 있으면 그는 온갖 재주를 부려도 빠져나갈 수 없기 때문이지. 내가 너를 그의 동굴로 데려다주마. 그는 정오에 그곳으로 와서 한낮의 휴식을 취한단다. 그러면 너는 손쉽게 그를 사로잡을 수 있을 게야. 하지만 그는 자신이 붙잡혔음을 알게 되면, 자신이 지닌 능력에 의지해 여러 모습으로 변신할 것이다. 그는 멧돼지나 사나운 호랑이, 비늘 돋친 용이나 누런 갈기를 지닌 사자가 될 게다. 또는 불길이 타닥거리는 소리나 물이 솟구치는 소리를 내어, 네가 사슬을 놓도록 꾀어내고는 그 틈에 달아날 게야. 하지만 너는 그저 그를 단단히 묶어 두기만 하면 되니, 마침내 그가 자신의 온갖 재주가 헛되다는 것을 알게 되면, 제 본래 모습으로 돌아와 네 명령에 따를 게다.\" 이렇게 말하며 그녀는 아들에게 향기로운 넥타르, 곧 신들의 음료를 뿌렸고, 그러자 곧바로 예사롭지 않은 활력이 그의 몸을 가득 채우고 용기가 그의 마음을 채웠으며, 향기가 그의 주위에 온통 감돌았다.\n\n님프는 아들을 예언자의 동굴로 데려가 바위틈에 숨겨 두었고, 그녀 자신은 구름 뒤에 자리를 잡았다. 정오가 되어, 사람도 가축도 이글거리는 태양을 피해 조용한 잠에 잠기는 시각이 오자, 프로테우스가 물속에서 나왔고, 그를 뒤따라 바다표범 떼가 해안을 따라 흩어졌다. 그는 바위에 앉아 자신의 떼를 헤아렸고, 그런 다음 동굴 바닥에 몸을 뉘여 잠이 들었다. 아리스타이오스는 그가 채 깊이 잠들기도 전에 그에게 족쇄를 채우고 큰 소리로 고함을 질렀다. 프로테우스는 잠에서 깨어나 자신이 붙잡혔음을 알고는, 곧장 자신의 재주에 의지하여, 빠르게 잇달아 처음에는 불이 되고, 다음에는 홍수가 되고, 그다음에는 끔찍한 들짐승이 되었다. 하지만 모든 것이 소용없음을 알고는, 마침내 제 본래 모습을 되찾아 성난 어조로 젊은이에게 말했다. \"대담한 젊은이여, 이렇게 내 거처를 침범하는 너는 누구이며, 내게서 무엇을 바라느냐?\" 아리스타이오스가 대답했다. \"프로테우스여, 그대는 이미 알고 있으니, 누구든 그대를 속이려 드는 것은 부질없는 일이기 때문이오. 그러니 그대도 나를 따돌리려는 노력을 그치시오. 나는 신의 도움에 이끌려 이리로 왔으니, 내 불운의 원인과 그것을 바로잡는 법을 그대에게서 알기 위함이오.\" 이 말에 예언자는 그를 향해 잿빛 눈을 꿰뚫는 듯한 시선으로 고정하고는 이렇게 말했다. \"너는 네 행위의 마땅한 대가를 치르는 것이다. 그 행위로 인해 에우리디케가 죽음을 맞았으니, 그녀는 너를 피해 달아나다가 뱀을 밟았고, 그 뱀에게 물려 죽었기 때문이다. 그녀의 죽음을 복수하기 위해, 그녀의 동무인 님프들이 네 벌들에게 이 파멸을 보낸 것이다. 너는 그들의 분노를 달래야 하니, 이렇게 해야 한다. 완벽한 모양과 크기를 지닌 황소 네 마리와 그에 못지않게 아름다운 암소 네 마리를 골라, 님프들에게 제단 네 개를 쌓고, 그 짐승들을 제물로 바치며, 그 사체들을 잎 무성한 숲에 남겨 두어라. 오르페우스와 에우리디케에게는 그들의 원한을 가라앉힐 만한 장례의 예를 올려라. 아흐레가 지난 뒤에 돌아와, 죽인 가축들의 몸뚱이를 살펴보면 무슨 일이 벌어질지 알게 될 것이다.\" 아리스타이오스는 이 지시를 충실히 따랐다. 그는 가축들을 제물로 바쳤고, 그 몸뚱이를 숲에 남겨 두었으며, 오르페우스와 에우리디케의 혼령에게 장례의 예를 올렸다. 그런 다음 아흐레째 되는 날에 돌아와 그 짐승들의 몸뚱이를 살펴보니, 놀랍게도! 벌떼 한 무리가 사체 가운데 하나를 차지하고는, 마치 벌집에서처럼 그곳에서 일을 이어 가고 있었다.\n\n쿠퍼는 \"과제\"에서, 러시아의 여제 안나가 지은 얼음 궁전을 이야기하면서 아리스타이오스의 이야기를 넌지시 언급한다. 그는 폭포 따위와 관련하여 얼음이 취하는 기이한 형상들을 묘사하던 참이었다.\n\n\"그보다 더 찬탄을 받지만 갈채를 받을 자격은 덜한, 새로운 것이기에 그러한, 인간의 작품이여, 털옷 두른 러시아인의 황실 여주인이여, 그대의 더없이 장엄하고 위풍당당한 변덕, 북방의 경이로다. 그대가 지으려 할 때 숲이 베어 넘어진 것도 아니요, 채석장이 그 보물을 내어 그대의 벽을 풍성히 한 것도 아니라, 그대는 강물을 깎아 유리 같은 물결로 그대의 대리석을 빚었도다. 그러한 궁전에서 아리스타이오스는 키레네를 찾아내었으니, 그가 잃어버린 벌들의 가련한 사연을 어머니의 귀에 전하던 그때였다.\"\n\n밀턴 또한 \"코머스\"에서 수호 정령의 노래로 세번 강의 님프 사브리나를 우리에게 묘사할 때, 키레네와 그녀의 거처 정경을 마음에 두고 있었던 것으로 보인다.\n\n\"아름다운 사브리나여! 그대 앉아 있는 곳에서 들으라, 유리처럼 맑고 서늘하며 투명한 물결 아래에서, 나리꽃을 비틀어 엮은 머리띠로 호박빛 방울 듣는 그대 풀어 헤친 머리채를 매면서. 소중한 명예를 위하여 들으라, 은빛 호수의 여신이여! 들으라, 그리고 구하라.\"\n\n다음은 그 밖의 이름난 신화 속 시인과 음악가들로서, 그들 가운데 일부는 오르페우스 자신에게도 거의 뒤지지 않았다.\n\n암피온은 유피테르와 테바이의 왕비 안티오페 사이에서 난 아들이었다. 그는 쌍둥이 형제 제토스와 함께 태어나자마자 키타이론 산에 버려졌고, 그곳에서 자신들의 혈통도 모른 채 목동들 틈에서 자랐다. 메르쿠리우스가 암피온에게 리라를 주고 그것을 연주하는 법을 가르쳤으며, 그의 형제는 사냥을 하고 가축을 돌보는 일에 몰두했다. 그러는 동안 그들의 어머니 안티오페는 테바이의 왕위를 찬탈한 리코스와 그의 아내 디르케에게 모진 학대를 받아 오던 터라, 자식들에게 그들의 권리를 알리고 자신을 도우러 오라고 부를 방도를 찾아냈다. 그들은 함께 가축을 치던 동료들로 무리를 이루어 리코스를 공격해 죽였고, 디르케는 그 머리채를 황소에 묶어, 그녀가 죽을 때까지 황소가 끌고 다니게 했다. 테바이의 왕이 된 암피온은 성벽으로 도시를 둘렀다. 그가 리라를 연주하면 돌들이 저절로 움직여 성벽 안의 제자리를 잡았다고 전해진다.\n\n이 이야기를 흥미롭게 활용한 예로는 테니슨의 시 \"암피온\"을 보라.\n\n리노스는 헤라클레스에게 음악을 가르친 스승이었으나, 어느 날 제자를 다소 가혹하게 나무란 일이 헤라클레스의 분노를 자아냈고, 헤라클레스는 그를 리라로 내리쳐 죽이고 말았다.\n\n트라키아의 옛 음유시인으로, 그는 오만하게도 무사이에게 솜씨를 겨루자고 도전했다가, 그 겨룸에서 패하여 그들에게 시력을 빼앗겼다. 밀턴은 자신의 실명에 관해 이야기하며 다른 눈먼 음유시인들과 함께 그를 넌지시 언급한다. \"실낙원\" 제3권 35행이다.\n\n미네르바가 피리를 발명하여, 천상의 모든 청중을 기쁘게 하며 그것을 연주했다. 하지만 짓궂은 개구쟁이 쿠피도가 감히 그 여신이 연주하는 동안 짓는 우스꽝스러운 표정을 비웃자, 미네르바는 분개하여 그 악기를 멀리 내던졌고, 그것은 땅으로 떨어져 마르시아스에게 발견되었다. 그가 그것을 불자 너무도 황홀한 소리가 흘러나왔기에, 그는 아폴론에게마저 음악 겨루기를 도전하고 싶은 유혹에 사로잡혔다. 물론 신이 승리를 거두었고, 마르시아스에게는 산 채로 가죽을 벗기는 벌을 내렸다.\n\n멜람푸스는 예언의 능력을 부여받은 최초의 필멸자였다. 그의 집 앞에는 뱀의 둥지가 든 떡갈나무 한 그루가 서 있었다. 늙은 뱀들은 하인들에게 죽임을 당했으나, 멜람푸스는 새끼들을 돌보며 정성껏 먹였다. 어느 날 그가 떡갈나무 아래에서 잠들어 있을 때, 뱀들이 혀로 그의 귀를 핥았다. 깨어나 보니 그는 이제 새들과 기는 것들의 말을 알아듣게 되었음을 알고 놀랐다. 이 능력 덕분에 그는 앞일을 예언할 수 있게 되었고, 이름난 점쟁이가 되었다. 한번은 그의 적들이 그를 사로잡아 엄중히 가두었다. 멜람푸스는 밤의 고요 속에서 목재 속의 나무좀들이 서로 주고받는 말을 들었고, 그들이 하는 말로 미루어 목재가 거의 다 갉아 먹혀 머잖아 지붕이 무너지리라는 것을 알아냈다. 그는 자신을 가둔 자들에게 이를 일러 주며 풀어 달라고 요구하고, 그들에게도 경고했다. 그들은 그의 경고를 받아들여 파멸을 면했고, 멜람푸스에게 보답하며 그를 높이 받들었다.\n\n전설이 절반쯤 섞인 인물로, 어느 전승에서는 오르페우스의 아들로 일컬어졌다. 그는 신성한 시와 신탁을 지었다고 전해진다. 밀턴은 자신의 \"사색하는 사람\"에서 그의 이름을 오르페우스의 이름과 나란히 둔다.\n\n\"하나 오, 슬픈 처녀여, 그대의 힘이 무사이오스를 그의 나무 그늘에서 일으켜 세우거나, 오르페우스의 영혼더러 그러한 가락을 노래하게 하여, 그것이 현(絃)에 어우러져 떨려 나와, 플루톤의 뺨에 무쇠 같은 눈물을 흘리게 하고, 사랑이 구하던 것을 지옥이 허락하게 했으면.\"\n\n이 장의 모험을 이루는 시인들은 실존 인물이었으며, 그들의 작품 가운데 일부는 오늘날까지도 남아 있다. 그리고 후대의 시인들에게 끼친 그들의 영향력은 그들이 남긴 시작품 자체보다도 더욱 중요하다. 다음 이야기들에 기록된 그들의 모험은 「신화의 시대」의 다른 서사들과 같은 권위, 즉 그것을 들려준 시인들의 권위에 의존하고 있다. 지금의 형태로 보자면, 앞의 두 이야기는 독일어에서 번역한 것으로, 아리온은 슐레겔에게서, 이비코스는 실러에게서 가져온 것이다.\n아리온\n\n아리온은 이름난 음악가였고, 코린토스의 왕 페리안드로스의 궁정에 거처하면서 그의 크나큰 총애를 받았다. 시켈리아[시칠리아]에서 음악 경연이 열릴 예정이었고, 아리온은 그 상을 두고 겨루기를 간절히 바랐다. 그가 자신의 소망을 페리안드로스에게 말하자, 왕은 형제처럼 그 생각을 접으라고 간청했다. \"부디 나와 함께 머물러 주게.\" 왕이 말했다. \"그리고 만족하게나. 이기려 애쓰는 자는 잃을 수도 있는 법이라네.\" 아리온이 대답했다. \"떠도는 삶이야말로 시인의 자유로운 마음에 가장 잘 어울리는 것입니다. 신께서 제게 내려 주신 재능을 저는 기꺼이 다른 이들에게 기쁨의 원천으로 삼고 싶습니다. 그리고 만약 제가 상을 탄다면, 저의 드넓은 명성을 자각하는 일로 그 기쁨이 얼마나 더 커지겠습니까!\" 그는 떠났고, 상을 탔으며, 자신의 재물과 함께 코린토스 배에 올라 고향으로 향했다. 출항한 뒤 둘째 날 아침, 바람은 부드럽고 순하게 불었다. \"오 페리안드로스여.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"당신의 두려움을 떨치소서! 머지않아 제 품에 안겨 그것을 잊으시게 될 것입니다. 우리는 얼마나 아낌없는 제물을 바쳐 신들께 감사를 드러낼 것이며, 잔칫상 앞에서 얼마나 즐거워할 것인지요!\" 바람과 바다는 계속해서 순조로웠다. 구름 한 점 창공을 흐리지 않았다. 그는 바다를 지나치게 믿지는 않았으나, 사람은 지나치게 믿었던 것이다. 그는 뱃사람들이 서로 은밀히 신호를 주고받는 것을 엿듣게 되었고, 그들이 자신의 보물을 차지하려 음모를 꾸미고 있음을 알아챘다. 이윽고 그들은 시끄럽고 반항적으로 그를 둘러싸고 말했다. \"아리온, 너는 죽어야 한다! 만약 뭍에 무덤을 갖고자 한다면 이 자리에서 죽음에 몸을 맡겨라. 그러나 그것이 싫다면 바다로 몸을 던져라.\" \"내 목숨 말고는 너희를 만족시킬 것이 없단 말이냐?\" 그가 말했다. \"내 황금을 가져가거라, 기꺼이 주겠다. 나는 그 값으로 기꺼이 내 목숨을 사겠다.\" \"안 된다, 안 돼. 우리는 너를 살려 둘 수 없다. 너의 목숨은 우리에게 너무도 위험하다. 만약 페리안드로스가 네가 우리에게 약탈당했음을 알게 된다면, 우리가 어디로 가야 그의 손길을 피할 수 있겠느냐? 고향에 돌아가 영영 두려움에서 벗어날 수 없다면, 네 황금이 우리에게 무슨 소용이 있겠느냐.\" \"그렇다면.\" 그가 말했다. \"내 목숨을 구할 길이 전혀 없으니, 마지막 청을 하나 들어다오. 내가 살아온 대로, 음유시인답게 죽게 해다오. 내가 죽음의 노래를 다 부르고 나서 내 하프 줄이 더는 울리지 않게 되면, 그때 나는 삶에 작별을 고하고 불평 없이 내 운명에 몸을 맡기겠다.\" 다른 청들과 마찬가지로 이 기도 또한 무시되었을 터였다. 그들은 오직 자신들의 약탈물만을 생각하고 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 그토록 이름난 음악가의 노래를 듣는다는 것, 그것이 그들의 거친 마음을 움직였다. \"한 가지 더.\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"내 옷매무새를 다듬게 해다오. 음유시인의 복장을 갖추지 않으면 아폴론께서 나를 돌보지 않으실 것이다.\"\n\n그는 균형 잡힌 사지를 보기에 아름다운 황금과 자줏빛 옷으로 감쌌고, 그의 겉옷은 우아한 주름을 이루며 그의 몸을 따라 흘러내렸으며, 보석들이 그의 두 팔을 장식하고, 그의 이마에는 황금 화관이 씌워졌으며, 그의 목과 어깨 위로는 향유로 향기로운 머리카락이 흘러내렸다. 그의 왼손은 리라를 쥐었고, 오른손은 그 줄을 켜는 상아 채를 쥐었다. 그는 영감에 사로잡힌 자처럼 아침 공기를 들이마시며 아침 햇살 속에서 반짝이는 듯 보였다. 뱃사람들은 감탄하며 바라보았다. 그는 뱃전으로 성큼 걸어 나가 짙푸른 바다를 내려다보았다. 그는 자신의 리라에 대고 노래했다. \"내 목소리의 동반자여, 나와 함께 그림자의 나라로 가자. 케르베로스가 으르렁댄다 한들, 우리는 노래의 힘이 그 분노를 길들일 수 있음을 안다. 그대들, 엘리시온의 영웅들이여, 어둑한 강물을 건너간 그대들이여, 그대 행복한 영혼들이여, 머지않아 나는 그대들의 무리에 끼어들리라. 그러나 그대들이 내 슬픔을 덜어 줄 수 있겠는가? 아아, 나는 내 벗을 뒤에 남겨 두고 가는구나. 그대여, 그대는 그대의 에우리디케를 찾았으나 찾자마자 다시 잃지 않았던가. 그녀가 꿈처럼 사라졌을 때, 그대는 그 환한 빛을 얼마나 미워했던가! 나는 떠나야 하지만, 두려워하지 않으리라. 신들께서 우리를 굽어보신다. 죄 없는 나를 죽이는 그대들이여, 내가 사라지고 나면 그대들이 떨어야 할 때가 오리라. 그대 네레이데스여, 그대들의 자비에 몸을 던지는 손님을 받아다오!\" 이렇게 말하고서 그는 깊은 바다로 뛰어들었다. 파도가 그를 덮었고, 뱃사람들은 온갖 발각의 위험에서 안전하다 여기며 가던 길을 갔다.\n\n그러나 그의 음악 가락은 깊은 바다의 거주자들을 그의 주위로 불러 모아 귀 기울이게 했고, 돌고래들이 마법에 묶이기라도 한 듯 배를 뒤따랐다. 그가 파도 속에서 허우적거릴 때, 돌고래 한 마리가 자신의 등을 내어 주었고, 그를 등에 태워 무사히 뭍으로 실어다 주었다. 그가 상륙한 바로 그 자리에, 그 사건의 기억을 보존하기 위해 훗날 바위투성이 해안 위에 청동 기념비가 세워졌다.\n\n아리온과 돌고래가 각자 자신의 터전으로 헤어질 때, 아리온은 이렇게 감사를 쏟아 냈다. \"잘 가거라, 충실하고 다정한 물고기여! 내가 너에게 보답할 수 있다면 좋으련만, 너는 나와 함께 갈 수 없고, 나 또한 너와 함께 갈 수 없구나. 우리는 동행할 수가 없는 것이다. 부디 깊은 바다의 여왕 갈라테이아가 너에게 그 은총을 베풀어 주시기를, 그리고 그 짐을 자랑스레 여기는 네가 매끄러운 거울 같은 바다 위로 그녀의 마차를 끌게 되기를.\"\n\n아리온은 해안에서 서둘러 떠났고, 곧 자기 앞에 코린토스의 탑들이 보였다. 그는 손에 하프를 들고 사랑과 행복에 가득 차 노래하며 길을 갔으니, 자신이 잃은 것은 잊고 오직 남은 것, 곧 자신의 벗과 자신의 리라만을 마음에 두었다. 그는 환대의 전당으로 들어섰고, 곧 페리안드로스의 품에 안겼다. \"내가 그대에게 돌아왔소, 나의 벗이여.\" 그가 말했다. \"신께서 내려 주신 재능은 수천 사람의 기쁨이 되었으나, 거짓된 악당들이 내가 정당하게 얻은 보물을 앗아 갔소. 그래도 나는 드넓은 명성을 자각하는 것만은 간직하고 있소.\" 그러고서 그는 자신에게 닥쳤던 그 모든 놀라운 사건을 페리안드로스에게 들려주었고, 왕은 경악하며 그 이야기를 들었다. \"그런 악행이 승리해도 된단 말인가?\" 왕이 말했다. \"그렇다면 내 손에 쥐어진 권력은 헛된 것이로다. 우리가 그 범인들을 찾아낼 수 있도록, 그대는 여기 숨어 있어야 하네. 그래야 그들이 의심 없이 다가올 것이니.\" 배가 항구에 들어오자, 왕은 뱃사람들을 자기 앞으로 불러들였다. \"아리온에 대해 무슨 소식이라도 들은 것이 있느냐?\" 왕이 물었다. \"나는 그의 귀환을 애타게 기다리고 있다.\" 그들이 대답했다. \"저희는 그가 타렌툼에서 건강하고 잘 지내는 것을 보고 떠났습니다.\" 그들이 이 말을 하는 동안, 아리온이 앞으로 나서서 그들을 마주했다. 그의 균형 잡힌 사지는 보기에 아름다운 황금과 자줏빛 옷으로 차려입혀져 있었고, 그의 겉옷은 우아한 주름을 이루며 그의 몸을 따라 흘러내렸으며, 보석들이 그의 두 팔을 장식하고, 그의 이마에는 황금 화관이 씌워졌으며, 그의 목과 어깨 위로는 향유로 향기로운 머리카락이 흘러내렸다. 그의 왼손은 리라를 쥐었고, 오른손은 그 줄을 켜는 상아 채를 쥐었다. 그들은 벼락이라도 맞은 듯 그의 발치에 엎드렸다. \"우리는 그를 죽이려 했는데, 그가 신이 되었구나. 오 대지여, 입을 벌려 우리를 삼켜다오!\" 그러자 페리안드로스가 말했다. \"그는 살아 있다, 노래의 명인이! 자비로운 하늘이 시인의 목숨을 지켜 주었다. 너희에 관해서는, 나는 복수의 정령을 불러들이지 않으리라. 아리온은 너희의 피를 바라지 않는다. 탐욕의 노예들이여, 물러가라! 어딘가 야만의 땅을 찾아가, 어떤 아름다운 것도 결코 너희 영혼을 기쁘게 하지 않기를!\"\n\n스펜서는 아리온이 자신의 돌고래에 올라탄 채 넵투누스와 암피트리테의 행렬을 뒤따르는 모습을 그려 낸다.\n\n\"그때 들려온 것은 더없이 천상적인 소리, 뒤이어 흘러나온 정묘한 음악이었으니, 넘실대는 물결 위에 보좌라도 차지한 듯, 아리온은 자신의 하프로 그 아름다운 무리 모두의 귀와 마음을 자기에게로 끌어당겼다. 그를 태우고 해적들의 눈을 피해 에게 해를 건너던 그 돌고래마저도 그의 가락에 놀라 가만히 멈추어 섰고, 날뛰던 바다도 기쁨에 겨워 으르렁대기를 잊었다.\"\n\n바이런은 「차일드 해럴드」 제2편에서 아리온의 이야기를 암시하는데, 자신의 항해를 묘사하면서 뱃사람 가운데 하나가 나머지 사람들을 즐겁게 하려고 음악을 연주하는 모습을 그려 낸다.\n\n\"달이 떴다. 하늘에 맹세코, 사랑스러운 저녁이로다! 긴 빛줄기가 춤추는 물결 위로 펼쳐지고, 이제 뭍의 젊은이들은 한숨짓고 처녀들은 믿으리니, 우리가 뭍으로 돌아갈 때 우리의 운명도 그러하기를! 그러는 사이 어느 거친 아리온의 쉼 없는 손이 뱃사람들이 좋아하는 흥겨운 가락을 깨워 내고, 거기 즐거운 청중들이 둥글게 둘러서거나, 익숙한 가락에 맞추어 가뿐히 몸을 움직이니, 아직도 뭍에서 자유로이 거니는 듯 시름이 없구나.\"\n이비코스\n\n이어지는 이비코스의 이야기를 이해하려면, 첫째로 고대인들의 극장이 만 명에서 삼만 명에 이르는 관객을 수용할 수 있는 거대한 건축물이었다는 점을 기억해 둘 필요가 있다. 그리고 그것은 오직 축제 때에만 사용되었고 모든 이에게 입장이 무료였으므로, 대개는 꽉 들어찼다. 그것은 지붕이 없어 하늘을 향해 열려 있었고, 공연은 대낮에 이루어졌다. 둘째로, 복수의 여신들[푸리아이]의 소름 끼치는 묘사는 이 이야기에서 결코 과장된 것이 아니다. 비극 시인 아이스킬로스가 한번은 쉰 명의 연기자로 이루어진 합창대로 복수의 여신들을 무대에 올렸을 때, 관객들의 공포가 어찌나 컸던지 많은 이들이 기절하고 경련을 일으켰으며, 이에 행정관들이 앞으로 그와 같은 공연을 금했다고 전한다.\n\n경건한 시인 이비코스는 코린토스 지협에서 열리는 전차 경주와 음악 경연을 향해 길을 가던 중이었으니, 그 행사는 그리스 혈통을 지닌 모든 이를 불러 모으는 것이었다. 아폴론은 그에게 노래의 재능, 곧 시인의 꿀처럼 달콤한 입술을 내려 주었고, 그는 신에 가득 차 가벼운 걸음으로 길을 갔다. 어느덧 높은 언덕을 왕관처럼 두른 코린토스의 탑들이 눈에 들어왔고, 그는 경건한 외경심을 품고 넵투누스의 신성한 숲으로 들어섰다. 살아 있는 것이라곤 눈에 띄지 않았고, 오직 두루미 떼만이 남쪽 지방으로 이주하느라 그와 같은 방향을 취하며 머리 위로 날아갈 뿐이었다. \"행운이 함께하기를, 그대 다정한 무리여.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"바다 건너에서 온 나의 동반자들이여. 나는 그대들의 동행을 좋은 징조로 받아들이겠다. 우리는 먼 곳에서 와서 환대를 찾아 날아가는구나. 우리 둘 다, 나그네 손님을 해악으로부터 지켜 주는 저 따뜻한 영접을 받게 되기를!\"\n\n그는 활기차게 걸음을 옮겼고, 곧 숲 한가운데에 이르렀다. 그곳에서 별안간, 좁은 길목에서 두 명의 강도가 나서서 그의 길을 가로막았다. 그는 굴복하거나 싸워야만 했다. 그러나 리라에는 익숙하되 무기를 들고 다투는 일에는 익숙하지 않은 그의 손은 무력하게 늘어졌다. 그는 사람들과 신들에게 도움을 청했으나, 그의 외침은 어떤 수호자의 귀에도 닿지 못했다. \"그렇다면 나는 여기서 죽어야 하는구나.\" 그가 말했다. \"낯선 땅에서, 애도해 줄 이도 없이, 무법자의 손에 목숨이 끊겨, 내 원한을 갚아 줄 이 하나 보지 못한 채로.\" 깊은 상처를 입고 그가 땅에 쓰러질 때, 머리 위에서 두루미들이 쉰 소리로 울었다. \"내 원한을 떠맡아 다오, 그대 두루미들이여.\" 그가 말했다. \"그대들의 목소리 말고는 내 외침에 답하는 것이 없으니.\" 이렇게 말하고서 그는 죽음 속에 눈을 감았다.\n\n벌거벗겨지고 난도질당한 그의 시신이 발견되었고, 비록 상처로 흉하게 일그러져 있었으나, 코린토스에서 그를 손님으로 기다리던 벗이 그를 알아보았다. \"이런 모습으로 그대를 되찾는단 말인가?\" 그가 외쳤다. \"노래의 겨룸에서 그대의 관자놀이를 승리의 화관으로 휘감아 주기를 바랐던 내가!\"\n\n축제에 모인 손님들은 그 소식을 듣고 경악했다. 온 그리스가 그 상처를 느꼈고, 모든 가슴이 그 상실을 제 것으로 받아들였다. 그들은 행정관들의 법정 주위로 몰려들어 살인자들에 대한 복수와 그들의 피로 치르는 속죄를 요구했다.\n\n그러나 잔치의 화려함에 이끌려 모여든 그 방대한 군중 가운데서, 어떤 흔적이나 표적이 범인을 가려낼 수 있단 말인가? 그는 강도들의 손에 쓰러진 것인가, 아니면 어떤 사사로운 원수가 그를 죽인 것인가? 모든 것을 꿰뚫어 보는 태양만이 그것을 말해 줄 수 있으니, 다른 어떤 눈도 그것을 보지 못했기 때문이다. 그러나 어쩌면 그 살인자가 바로 지금도 군중 한가운데를 거닐며 자기 범행의 열매를 누리고 있을 법한데, 한편 복수는 헛되이 그를 찾고 있는 것이다. 어쩌면 그는 자신들의 신전 경내에서 신들을 우롱하며, 지금 원형 극장 안으로 밀려드는 이 사람들의 무리에 거리낌 없이 섞여 있을지도 모른다.\n\n이제 줄줄이 빽빽이 모여든 군중이 좌석을 가득 메우니, 마치 그 건축물 자체가 무너져 내릴 것만 같았다. 목소리들의 웅성거림은 바다의 포효처럼 들렸고, 위로 올라갈수록 넓어지는 원형의 단들은 마치 하늘에 닿으려는 듯 층층이 솟아올랐다.\n\n이제 그 방대한 군중은 복수의 여신들로 분한 합창대의 무시무시한 목소리에 귀를 기울이니, 그것은 엄숙한 차림으로 박자를 맞춘 걸음을 옮기며 나아와 극장의 둘레를 돌았다. 저 무시무시한 무리를 이루는 것이 과연 필멸의 여인들일 수 있단 말인가, 그리고 저 방대하게 모인 말 없는 형상들이 과연 살아 있는 존재일 수 있단 말인가?\n\n검은 옷을 입은 합창대원들은 살 없는 손에 역청 같은 불꽃을 타오르게 하는 횃불을 들었다. 그들의 뺨에는 핏기가 없었고, 머리카락 대신 꿈틀거리며 부풀어 오르는 뱀들이 그들의 이마를 휘감고 있었다. 둥근 원을 이루어 이 무시무시한 존재들은 그들의 찬가를 불렀으니, 죄지은 자들의 마음을 찢고 그들의 모든 기능을 옭아맸다. 그 노래는 솟아오르고 부풀어 올라 악기들의 소리를 압도하며, 판단력을 앗아 가고, 가슴을 마비시키며, 피를 얼어붙게 했다.\n\n\"제 마음을 죄와 악행으로부터 순결하게 지키는 자는 복되도다! 복수자인 우리는 그를 건드리지 않으니, 그는 우리로부터 안전하게 삶의 길을 걷는다. 그러나 화로다! 화로다! 은밀한 살인의 행위를 저지른 자에게는! 우리, 밤의 무시무시한 가족은 그의 온 존재에 들러붙는다. 그는 달아나면 우리를 피할 수 있다고 생각하는가? 우리는 더욱 빠르게 뒤쫓아 날아가, 그의 두 발에 우리의 뱀을 휘감아 그를 땅에 거꾸러뜨린다. 우리는 지칠 줄 모르고 뒤쫓으며, 어떤 연민도 우리의 추격을 멈추지 못한다. 끝없이 나아가, 삶의 끝까지, 우리는 그에게 평안도 안식도 주지 않는다.\" 이렇게 에우메니데스는 노래하며 엄숙한 가락에 맞추어 움직였고, 한편 죽음의 정적과도 같은 고요가 마치 초인적인 존재들 앞에 있기라도 한 듯 온 군중 위에 내려앉았다. 그러고 나서 엄숙한 행진으로 극장의 둘레를 다 돌고서, 그들은 무대 뒤편으로 빠져나갔다.\n\n모든 가슴은 환상과 현실 사이에서 떨렸고, 모든 가슴은 은밀한 범죄를 지켜보며 보이지 않게 운명의 실타래를 감는 그 무시무시한 힘 앞에 움츠러들어 막연한 공포로 두근거렸다. 바로 그 순간, 가장 높은 좌석 가운데 하나에서 외침이 터져 나왔다. \"보게! 보게! 친구여, 저기 이비코스의 두루미들이 있네!\" 그리고 별안간 하늘을 가로질러 떠가는 어두운 물체가 나타났으니, 잠시 살펴보니 극장 바로 위를 날아가는 두루미 떼였다. \"이비코스라고? 저자가 그렇게 말했나?\" 그 사랑하는 이름은 모든 가슴에 슬픔을 되살려 놓았다. 바다 위로 물결이 물결을 뒤따르듯, 입에서 입으로 그 말이 퍼져 나갔다. \"이비코스라고! 우리 모두가 애도하는 그 사람, 어느 살인자의 손에 쓰러진 그 사람 말인가! 두루미들이 그와 무슨 상관이란 말인가?\" 그리고 목소리의 너울은 더욱 커져 갔고, 한편 번개의 섬광처럼 한 생각이 모든 가슴을 꿰뚫고 지나갔다. \"에우메니데스의 힘을 보라! 경건한 시인의 원한이 갚아지리라! 살인자가 스스로 자기를 고발했구나. 저 외침을 내뱉은 자와, 그가 말을 건넨 다른 자를 붙잡아라!\"\n\n그 죄인은 기꺼이 자신의 말을 도로 거두고 싶었으나, 이미 너무 늦었다. 공포로 창백해진 살인자들의 얼굴이 그들의 죄를 드러냈다. 사람들은 그들을 재판관 앞으로 데려갔고, 그들은 자신들의 범행을 자백했으며, 마땅히 받아야 할 벌을 받았다.\n시모니데스\n\n시모니데스는 그리스 초기 시인들 가운데 가장 다작한 이들 중 하나였으나, 그의 작품은 단편 몇 조각만이 우리에게 전해져 내려왔다. 그는 찬가와 승리의 송가, 그리고 비가를 지었다. 마지막 종류의 작품에서 그는 특히 뛰어났다. 그의 천재성은 비애에 기울어 있었고, 인간적 공감의 현을 그보다 더 진실한 효과로 건드릴 수 있는 이는 없었다. 그의 시 가운데 남아 있는 단편 중 가장 중요한 「다나에의 비탄」은, 다나에와 그녀의 어린 아들이 그녀의 아버지 아크리시오스의 명에 따라 궤짝에 갇혀 바다 위에 띄워졌다는 전승에 바탕을 두고 있다. 그 궤짝은 세리포스 섬을 향해 떠갔고, 그곳에서 둘은 어부 딕티스에게 구조되어 그 나라의 왕 폴리덱테스에게로 옮겨졌으니, 왕은 그들을 받아들여 보호해 주었다. 그 아이 페르세우스는 자라나 이름난 영웅이 되었으며, 그의 모험은 앞선 장에 기록되어 있다.\n\n시모니데스는 생애의 많은 시간을 군주들의 궁정에서 보냈고, 종종 자신의 재능을 찬미의 시와 축제의 송가에 쏟으며, 자신이 그 위업을 기린 이들의 후한 베풂에서 그 보답을 받았다. 이 일은 천한 것이 아니었으니, 호메로스가 묘사한 데모도코스 같은 가장 이른 시기의 음유시인들의 일이나, 전승에 기록된 대로의 호메로스 자신의 일과 매우 흡사했다.\n\n한번은 테살리아의 왕 스코파스의 궁정에 머물 때, 그 군주는 자신의 위업을 기리는 시를 지어 연회에서 낭송하게 해 달라고 그에게 청했다. 자신의 주제에 변화를 주기 위해, 경건함으로 이름난 시모니데스는 자신의 시 속에 카스토르와 폴리데우케스의 위업을 끌어들였다. 그러한 여담은 비슷한 자리에서 시인들에게 드문 일이 아니었으며, 평범한 필멸자라면 레다의 아들들과 찬사를 나누는 것에 만족했으리라 짐작할 만하다. 그러나 허영은 까다로운 법이다. 스코파스가 자신의 신하들과 아첨꾼들 가운데에서 잔칫상 앞에 앉아, 자신에 대한 찬사를 늘어놓지 않는 모든 구절을 못마땅하게 여겼던 것이다. 시모니데스가 약속된 보답을 받으러 다가가자, 스코파스는 기대했던 액수의 절반만을 내주며 말했다. \"여기 네 공연 중 내 몫에 대한 값이 있다. 카스토르와 폴리데우케스가 분명 그들에게 관련된 만큼은 네게 보답해 줄 것이다.\" 당황한 시인은 그 위인의 농담에 뒤따른 웃음 속에서 자기 자리로 돌아갔다. 얼마 지나지 않아 그는 말을 탄 두 젊은이가 밖에서 그를 애타게 만나려 기다리고 있다는 전갈을 받았다. 시모니데스는 서둘러 문으로 갔으나, 그 방문객들을 찾아도 보이지 않았다. 그러나 그가 연회장을 떠나기가 무섭게, 지붕이 요란한 굉음과 함께 무너져 내려 스코파스와 그의 모든 손님들을 그 잔해 아래 파묻어 버렸다. 자신을 부르러 왔던 그 젊은이들의 모습에 대해 물어보고서, 시모니데스는 그들이 다름 아닌 카스토르와 폴리데우케스 바로 그들이었음을 확신하게 되었다.\n\n사포\n사포는 그리스 문학의 매우 이른 시기에 활동한 여류 시인이었다. 그녀의 작품은 단편 몇 조각만이 남아 있으나, 그것만으로도 빼어난 시적 천재성에 대한 그녀의 자격을 입증하기에 충분하다. 흔히 언급되는 사포의 이야기는, 그녀가 파온이라는 이름의 아름다운 젊은이를 열렬히 사랑했으나 그 사랑의 보답을 얻지 못하자, 저 \"연인의 도약\"을 감행하는 자들은, 죽지 않는다면 그 사랑이 치유되리라는 미신에 따라, 레우카디아의 곶에서 바다로 몸을 던졌다는 것이다.\n\n바이런은 「차일드 해럴드」 제2편에서 사포의 이야기를 암시한다.\n\n\"차일드 해럴드는 항해하여 저 황량한 곳을 지나쳤으니, 슬픈 페넬로페가 물결을 굽어보던 그곳이라, 그리고 앞으로 나아가며 아직 잊히지 않은 그 산, 연인의 피난처이자 레스보스 여인의 무덤을 바라보았다. 어두운 사포여! 불멸의 시구로도 구할 수 없었던가, 그토록 불멸의 불꽃이 깃든 그 가슴을? \"그리스의 어느 부드러운 가을 저녁이었으니 차일드 해럴드는 멀리 레우카디아의 곶을 반겼다.\" 등등.\n\n사포와 그녀의 \"도약\"에 대해 더 알고자 하는 이는 「스펙테이터」 제223호와 제229호를 참조하기 바란다. 무어의 「그리스에서의 저녁」도 함께 보라.\n\n————\n\n# DIANA AND ENDYMION\n\n엔디미온은 라트모스 산에서 양 떼를 치던 아름다운 청년이었다. 어느 고요하고 맑은 밤, 달의 여신 아르테미스[로마의 〈디아나〉]가 아래를 굽어보다가 잠들어 있는 그를 보았다. 처녀신의 차가운 마음은 그의 빼어난 아름다움에 데워졌고, 그녀는 그에게 내려와 입을 맞추고는 그가 잠든 사이 그를 지켜보았다.\n\n또 다른 이야기에 따르면 제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]가 그에게 영원한 젊음과 영원한 잠을 함께 누리는 선물을 내렸다고 한다. 이렇게 선물을 받은 자에 관해서는 기록할 만한 모험이 거의 없을 수밖에 없다. 전하는 말로는 아르테미스가 그의 무위(無爲)한 삶 때문에 그의 살림이 곤궁해지지 않도록 보살펴 주었다고 한다. 그녀가 그의 양 떼를 불려 주었고, 그의 양과 새끼 양들을 들짐승들로부터 지켜 주었기 때문이다.\n\n엔디미온의 이야기는 그것이 워낙 얇게 가리고 있는 인간적인 의미 때문에 독특한 매력을 지닌다. 우리는 엔디미온에게서 젊은 시인을 본다. 그의 상상과 마음은 그들을 만족시켜 줄 무언가를 헛되이 찾아 헤매고, 고요한 달빛 속에서 가장 사랑하는 시간을 발견하며, 밝고도 말 없는 증인의 빛줄기 아래에서 그를 태우는 우수와 열정을 키운다. 이 이야기는 동경하는 시적인 사랑을, 현실보다는 꿈 속에서 더 많이 보낸 삶을, 그리고 이르되 반가운 죽음을 떠올리게 한다.—S. G. B.\n\n키츠의 「엔디미온」은 거칠고 환상적인 시로, 달을 향한 다음 구절처럼 더없이 빼어난 시구를 담고 있다.\n\n“……잠든 소들이 그대의 광휘 속에 누워 신성한 들판을 꿈꾸네. 헤아릴 수 없는 산들이 솟고, 또 솟아오르네, 그대 눈길의 축성(祝聖)을 갈망하면서. 그러나 그대의 축복은 빠뜨리지 않네 어느 어두운 은신처 하나, 작은 자리 하나, 기쁨이 깃들 만한 그곳도. 둥지 튼 굴뚝새는 그 고요한 시야 안에 그대의 고운 얼굴을 담고 있나니.” 등등.\n\n영 박사는 「밤의 상념」에서 엔디미온을 이렇게 언급한다.\n\n“……이 상념들은, 오 밤이여, 그대의 것. 그것들은 연인의 은밀한 한숨처럼 그대에게서 왔도다, 다른 이들이 잠든 사이에. 그렇게 킨티아가, 시인들이 꾸며 내듯, 그늘에 가려져, 부드럽게, 자신의 천구에서 미끄러져 내려와, 자신의 목동을 위로했네, 그를 향한 그녀의 사랑이 그대를 향한 나의 사랑만은 못했을지라도.”\n\n플레처는 「충실한 양치기 여인」에서 이렇게 노래한다.\n\n“창백한 포이베가 어느 숲에서 사냥하다가, 어떻게 처음 소년 엔디미온을 보았는지를, 그의 눈에서 결코 꺼지지 않는 영원한 불을 어떻게 그녀가 가져갔는지를, 어떻게 그녀가 그를 잠결에 부드럽게 옮겨, 그의 관자놀이를 양귀비로 감싸, 오래된 라트모스의 가파른 봉우리로 데려갔는지를, 그곳으로 그녀는 밤마다 몸을 굽혀, 제 오라비의 빛으로 산을 금빛으로 물들이며, 가장 사랑스러운 이에게 입 맞추러 내려오네.”\n\n오리온은 포세이돈[로마의 〈넵투누스〉]의 아들이었다. 그는 잘생긴 거인이자 강력한 사냥꾼이었다. 그의 아버지는 그에게 바다의 깊은 곳을 헤치고 걸어 다니는 힘을, 또는 어떤 이들의 말로는 바다 표면 위를 걸어 다니는 힘을 주었다.\n\n오리온은 키오스의 왕 오이노피온의 딸 메로페를 사랑하여 그녀에게 청혼하였다. 그는 섬에서 들짐승들을 몰아내고, 사냥의 노획물을 사랑하는 이에게 선물로 가져다 바쳤다. 그러나 오이노피온이 끊임없이 승낙을 미루자, 오리온은 폭력으로 그 처녀를 차지하려 하였다. 이런 행동에 격분한 그녀의 아버지는 오리온을 취하게 만든 뒤 그의 시력을 빼앗고는 바닷가에 내던졌다. 눈먼 영웅은 키클롭스의 망치 소리를 따라가다가 마침내 렘노스에 다다랐고, 헤파이스토스[로마의 〈불카누스〉]의 대장간에 이르렀다. 헤파이스토스는 그를 가엾이 여겨 자신의 일꾼 가운데 하나인 케달리온을 붙여 주어 태양신의 거처로 가는 길잡이로 삼게 하였다. 오리온은 케달리온을 어깨에 얹고 동쪽으로 나아갔고, 그곳에서 태양신을 만나 그 빛줄기로 다시 시력을 되찾았다.\n\n이후 그는 사냥꾼으로서 아르테미스와 함께 지냈는데, 그녀에게 무척 총애를 받았으며, 심지어 그녀가 그와 혼인하려 했다는 말까지 전한다. 그녀의 오라비는 몹시 못마땅해하며 자주 그녀를 나무랐으나 아무 소용이 없었다. 어느 날 오리온이 머리만 물 위로 내놓은 채 바다를 헤치며 걸어가는 것을 본 아폴론은 그것을 누이에게 가리키며, 바다 위의 저 검은 것을 그녀가 맞히지 못할 것이라고 단언하였다. 활의 여신은 치명적인 겨냥으로 화살을 쏘았다. 파도가 오리온의 주검을 뭍으로 밀어 올렸고, 자신의 치명적인 실수를 많은 눈물로 슬퍼하던 아르테미스는 그를 별들 사이에 놓았다. 그곳에서 그는 띠와 칼과 사자 가죽과 곤봉을 갖춘 거인의 모습으로 나타난다. 그의 개 시리우스가 그를 뒤따르고, 플레이아데스가 그의 앞에서 달아난다.\n\n플레이아데스는 아틀라스의 딸들이자 아르테미스를 따르던 님프들이었다. 어느 날 오리온이 그들을 보고 반하여 뒤를 쫓았다. 곤경에 처한 그들은 신들에게 자신들의 모습을 바꿔 달라고 빌었고, 제우스는 가엾이 여겨 그들을 비둘기로 바꾸었다가 다시 하늘의 별자리로 만들었다. 그들의 수는 일곱이었으나 보이는 별은 여섯뿐이다. 그들 가운데 하나인 엘렉트라가 트로이아의 멸망을 보지 않으려고 제 자리를 떠났다고 전하기 때문이다. 그 도시가 그녀의 아들 다르다노스에 의해 세워졌던 까닭이다. 그 광경이 그녀의 자매들에게 미친 영향이 어찌나 컸던지 그 이후로 그들은 줄곧 창백한 빛을 띠고 있다.\n\n롱펠로 씨는 「오리온의 엄폐(掩蔽)」라는 시를 지었다. 다음 구절들은 그가 이 신화 이야기를 언급한 대목이다. 미리 일러 두어야 할 것은, 천구 위에서 오리온이 사자 가죽을 두르고 곤봉을 휘두르는 모습으로 그려진다는 점이다. 그 별자리의 별들이 하나하나 달빛 속에 잦아드는 순간을 시인은 우리에게 이렇게 들려준다.\n\n“사자의 붉은 가죽이 떨어져 내려 그의 발치 강물 속으로 빠졌네. 그의 거대한 곤봉은 더 이상 황소의 이마를 두드리지 않았고, 그는 옛날처럼 바닷가에서 비틀거렸네, 오이노피온에게 눈멀었을 적, 대장장이를 그의 대장간에서 찾고, 좁은 협곡을 기어올라, 텅 빈 눈을 태양에 고정했던 그때처럼.”\n\n테니슨은 플레이아데스에 대해 다른 견해를 펼친다.\n\n“수많은 밤 나는 보았네, 플레이아데스가 부드러운 어스름을 뚫고 떠올라, 은빛 댕기에 뒤엉킨 반딧불 떼처럼 반짝이는 것을.” —「록슬리 홀」.\n\n바이런은 잃어버린 플레이아드를 이렇게 언급한다.\n\n“더 이상 저 아래에서 보이지 않는, 잃어버린 플레이아드처럼.”\n\n같은 주제를 다룬 히먼스 부인의 시구도 참조하라.\n\n# AURORA AND TITHONUS\n\n새벽의 여신은 그 자매인 달의 여신처럼 때때로 필멸자들을 향한 사랑에 빠졌다. 그녀가 가장 총애한 이는 트로이아의 왕 라오메돈의 아들 티토노스였다. 그녀는 그를 몰래 데려와 제우스를 설득해 그에게 불사(不死)를 내려 주게 하였다. 그러나 그 선물에 젊음을 함께 더하는 것을 잊었던 탓에, 얼마 지나지 않아 그녀는 크게 낙심하면서 그가 늙어 가고 있음을 알아차리기 시작하였다. 그의 머리카락이 온통 하얗게 세자 그녀는 그와 함께하기를 그만두었다. 그래도 그는 여전히 그녀의 궁전을 자유로이 거닐었고, 신들의 음식을 먹고 살았으며, 하늘의 옷을 걸쳤다. 마침내 그는 팔다리를 쓰는 힘마저 잃었고, 그러자 그녀는 그를 방 안에 가두었으니, 그곳에서 때때로 그의 가냘픈 목소리가 들리곤 하였다. 결국 그녀는 그를 베짱이로 바꾸었다.\n\n멤논은 에오스[로마의 〈아우로라〉]와 티토노스의 아들이었다. 그는 아이티오피아인들의 왕으로서 머나먼 동쪽, 오케아노스의 기슭에 살았다. 그는 자신의 전사들을 거느리고 아버지의 친족들을 도와 트로이아 전쟁에 참전하러 왔다. 프리아모스 왕은 그를 큰 예우로 맞이하였고, 그가 들려주는 대양 기슭의 경이로운 이야기에 감탄하며 귀를 기울였다.\n\n도착한 바로 다음 날, 휴식을 견디지 못한 멤논은 자신의 군대를 이끌고 전장으로 나아갔다. 네스토르의 용맹한 아들 안틸로코스가 그의 손에 쓰러졌고, 그리스인들은 패주하였다. 그때 아킬레우스가 나타나 전세를 되돌렸다. 그와 에오스의 아들 사이에 길고도 승부를 알 수 없는 싸움이 이어졌다. 마침내 승리는 아킬레우스의 편으로 기울었고, 멤논은 쓰러졌으며, 트로이아인들은 혼비백산하여 달아났다.\n\n하늘의 자리에서 아들의 위험을 근심스레 지켜보던 에오스는 그가 쓰러지는 것을 보자, 그의 형제들인 바람들에게 그의 주검을 파플라고니아의 에세포스 강 기슭으로 옮기라고 명하였다. 저녁이 되자 에오스가 〈사계절〉 여신들과 플레이아데스를 거느리고 와서 아들을 두고 울며 슬퍼하였다. 밤은 그녀의 슬픔에 공감하여 하늘을 구름으로 뒤덮었고, 온 자연이 새벽의 여신의 자식을 애도하였다. 아이티오피아인들은 님프들의 숲, 그 강 기슭에 그의 무덤을 세웠고, 제우스는 그의 화장(火葬) 장작더미에서 튄 불꽃과 잿불을 새들로 바꾸어 놓았다. 그 새들은 두 무리로 갈라져 장작더미 위에서 싸우다가 불길 속으로 떨어졌다. 해마다 그의 기일이 되면 그 새들이 돌아와 같은 방식으로 그의 장례를 기린다. 에오스는 아들을 잃은 슬픔에서 끝내 위안을 얻지 못한다. 그녀의 눈물은 지금도 흐르며, 이른 아침이면 풀잎 위에 맺힌 이슬방울의 모습으로 볼 수 있다.\n\n고대 신화의 여느 경이로운 이야기들과 달리, 이 이야기에 관해서는 아직도 몇 가지 기념물이 남아 있다. 이집트의 나일 강 기슭에 거대한 조각상 둘이 서 있는데, 그 가운데 하나가 멤논의 조각상이라고 전한다. 고대의 저술가들은, 떠오르는 태양의 첫 햇살이 이 조각상에 닿으면 거기서 어떤 소리가 새어 나오는데, 그들이 그것을 하프 줄이 끊어지는 소리에 견주었다고 기록하였다. 현재 남아 있는 조각상이 고대인들이 묘사한 그 조각상과 같은 것인지에 대해서는 다소 의문이 있으며, 그 신비로운 소리에 대해서는 더욱 의심스럽다. 그러나 그 소리가 지금도 들린다는 근대의 증언이 없는 것은 아니다. 바위의 틈새나 동굴에 갇혀 있던 공기가 빠져나가며 내는 소리가 그 이야기에 어느 정도 근거를 제공했으리라는 설명도 제기되었다. 최근의 여행가로서 가장 권위 있는 가드너 윌킨슨 경은 그 조각상을 직접 살펴보고는 그것이 속이 비어 있음을 발견하였으며, 또한 “조각상의 무릎에는 돌 하나가 놓여 있는데, 그것을 두드리면 금속성의 소리가 나니, 그 힘을 믿으려는 마음이 앞선 방문객을 속이는 데 지금도 쓰일 만하다”라고 하였다.\n\n목소리를 내는 멤논의 조각상은 시인들이 즐겨 인유하는 소재다. 다윈은 「식물의 정원」에서 이렇게 노래한다.\n\n“그리하여 멤논의 신전에서 신성한 태양을 향해 저절로 어우러진 화음이 아침의 노래를 합창했네. 그의 동방의 빛줄기에 닿아 화답하며 울리니 살아 있는 칠현금이 그 모든 줄을 떨게 하네. 어우러진 회랑들이 그 여린 가락을 길게 늘이고, 거룩한 메아리가 경배의 노래를 부풀리네.”\n\n# ACIS AND GALATEA\n\n스킬라는 시켈리아의 아름다운 처녀로, 바다의 님프들에게 사랑받는 존재였다. 그녀에게는 많은 구혼자가 있었지만 모두 물리쳤으며, 갈라테이아의 동굴로 찾아가서는 자신이 어떻게 시달리고 있는지를 털어놓곤 했다. 어느 날 여신은 스킬라가 자신의 머리를 매만져 주는 동안 그 이야기에 귀를 기울였고, 그런 다음 이렇게 대답했다. \"하지만 처녀여, 그대를 괴롭히는 자들은 그래도 그리 거칠지 않은 인간 종족이니, 마음만 먹으면 그대는 그들을 물리칠 수 있다. 그러나 네레우스의 딸이요 이토록 많은 자매들의 보호를 받는 나로서는, 키클롭스의 정념에서 벗어날 길을 오로지 바다의 깊은 곳에서밖에 찾지 못하였다.\" 그러자 눈물이 솟아 그녀의 말을 가로막았고, 가엾이 여긴 처녀가 가녀린 손가락으로 그 눈물을 닦아 주며 여신을 달랜 뒤 이렇게 말했다. \"가장 사랑하는 분이시여, 그대가 슬퍼하는 까닭을 말씀해 주십시오.\" 그러자 갈라테이아가 말하였다. \"아키스는 파우누스와 어느 나이아스 사이에서 난 아들이었다. 그의 아버지와 어머니는 그를 끔찍이 사랑하였지만, 그들의 사랑조차 나의 사랑에는 미치지 못하였다. 그 아름다운 젊은이는 오직 나에게만 마음을 두었으며, 그때 그는 갓 열여섯 살로 두 뺨에 솜털이 막 거뭇해지기 시작할 무렵이었다. 내가 그의 곁을 찾기를 갈망한 만큼이나 키클롭스는 나의 곁을 찾기를 갈망하였으니, 만일 그대가 내게 아키스를 향한 사랑과 폴리페모스를 향한 미움 가운데 어느 쪽이 더 강하였느냐고 묻는다면 나는 대답할 수 없을 것이다. 둘은 똑같은 무게였다. 오 아프로디테[로마의 〈베누스〉]여, 그대의 권능은 얼마나 위대한가! 숲의 공포요, 어떤 가엾은 나그네도 무사히 빠져나가지 못하게 한 자요, 제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉] 그 자신마저도 거역하던 이 사나운 거인이 사랑이 무엇인지를 느끼는 법을 배웠고, 나를 향한 정념에 사로잡혀 자신의 가축 떼와 잘 채워 둔 동굴마저 잊고 말았다. 그제야 처음으로 그는 자신의 외양을 조금이나마 가꾸기 시작하였고, 스스로를 호감 가게 보이려 애썼다. 그 거친 머리칼을 빗으로 갈아엎듯 다듬고, 낫으로 수염을 베어 내고, 물에 비친 자신의 험상궂은 얼굴을 들여다보며 표정을 가다듬었다. 살육을 즐기는 마음, 그의 사나움과 피에 대한 갈증은 더 이상 기승을 부리지 못하였고, 그의 섬에 들른 배들도 무사히 떠나갔다. 그는 바닷가를 오르내리며 무거운 발걸음으로 거대한 자국을 남겼고, 지치면 동굴에 조용히 누웠다.\n\n\"바다 쪽으로 튀어나와 양옆이 물에 씻기는 벼랑이 하나 있었다. 어느 날 그 거대한 키클롭스는 그곳으로 올라가, 가축 떼가 주위에 흩어져 풀을 뜯는 동안 자리를 잡고 앉았다. 배의 돛을 지탱하는 돛대로 써도 좋을 만한 자신의 지팡이를 내려놓고는, 수많은 갈대 피리를 엮어 만든 악기를 집어 들어, 언덕과 물결이 그의 노랫소리를 메아리치게 하였다. 나는 사랑하는 아키스의 곁에서 바위 밑에 몸을 숨긴 채 그 아득한 가락에 귀를 기울였다. 그 노래는 내 아름다움을 향한 과장된 찬사로 가득하였고, 거기에 나의 냉담함과 잔인함을 향한 격정적인 원망이 뒤섞여 있었다.\n\n\"노래를 마치자 그는 자리에서 일어나, 가만히 서 있지 못하는 성난 황소처럼 숲속으로 방황하며 떠나갔다. 아키스와 나는 더 이상 그를 마음에 두지 않았는데, 그러던 차에 별안간 그가 우리가 앉아 있는 모습이 한눈에 보이는 곳에 이르렀다. '내 너희를 보았다.' 그가 외쳤다. '이것이 너희의 마지막 밀회가 되게 해 주마.' 그 목소리는 성난 키클롭스만이 낼 수 있는 포효였다. 아이트네[에트나 화산]가 그 소리에 떨었다. 나는 공포에 사로잡혀 물속으로 뛰어들었다. 아키스는 돌아서서 도망치며 울부짖었다. '저를 구해 주소서, 갈라테이아여, 저를 구해 주소서, 부모님이여!' 키클롭스는 그를 뒤쫓아 산허리에서 바위를 뜯어내어 그에게 던졌다. 비록 그 모서리 한 귀퉁이만이 그에게 닿았을 뿐이나, 그 바위는 그를 짓눌러 버렸다.\n\n\"운명이 내 손에 남겨 둔 모든 것을 나는 아키스를 위하여 다하였다. 나는 그에게 그의 외조부인 강의 신이 누리던 영예를 부여하였다. 자줏빛 피가 바위 아래에서 흘러나왔으나, 차츰 옅어지더니 비로 흐려진 강물의 흐름처럼 보이게 되었고, 이윽고 맑아졌다. 바위가 쩍 갈라져 열렸으며, 그 틈에서 솟아 나오는 물이 듣기 좋은 속삭임 소리를 냈다.\"\n\n이리하여 아키스는 강으로 변하였고, 그 강은 지금도 아키스라는 이름을 간직하고 있다.\n\n드라이든은 그의 「키몬과 이피게네이아」에서 사랑의 힘으로 시골뜨기가 신사로 바뀌는 이야기를 들려주었는데, 그 방식에는 갈라테이아와 키클롭스의 옛이야기와 닮은 흔적이 엿보인다.\n\n\"그의 아버지의 보살핌도 스승의 재주도 그 거친 마음에 애써 심어 줄 수 없던 것을, 가장 뛰어난 스승인 사랑이 단숨에 불어넣었으니, 메마른 땅이 불길에 닿아 비옥해지듯 하였다. 사랑은 그에게 수치를 가르쳤고, 수치는 사랑과 다투며 이내 삶의 그 다정한 예절을 가르쳐 주었다.\" ————\n\n# THE TROJAN WAR\n\n미네르바[로마의 〈미네르바〉]는 지혜의 여신이었으나, 한번은 매우 어리석은 짓을 저질렀다. 그녀가 헤라[로마의 〈유노〉] 및 아프로디테[로마의 〈베누스〉]와 더불어 아름다움의 상을 두고 다툼에 끼어든 것이다. 사연인즉 이러하였다. 펠레우스와 테티스의 혼인 잔치에 모든 신이 초대를 받았으나, 오직 에리스[불화의 여신]만은 예외였다. 자신이 제외된 것에 분노한 여신은 손님들 사이로 황금 사과 하나를 던졌는데, 거기에는 \"가장 아름다운 이에게\"라는 글귀가 새겨져 있었다. 그러자 헤라와 아프로디테와 미네르바가 저마다 그 사과가 자기 것이라고 주장하였다. 제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]는 이처럼 미묘한 문제를 직접 판가름하고 싶지 않았으므로, 여신들을 이데 산으로 보냈다. 그곳에서는 아름다운 목동 파리스가 양 떼를 돌보고 있었으니, 결정은 그에게 맡겨졌다. 이리하여 여신들은 그의 앞에 나타났다. 헤라는 권세와 부를, 미네르바는 전쟁에서의 영광과 명성을, 아프로디테는 가장 아름다운 여인을 아내로 주겠노라 약속하여, 저마다 그의 판단을 자기 쪽으로 기울게 하려 하였다. 파리스는 아프로디테의 손을 들어 주고 그녀에게 황금 사과를 건넸으니, 이로써 다른 두 여신을 적으로 돌리고 말았다. 아프로디테의 보호 아래 파리스는 그리스로 배를 띄웠고, 스파르타의 왕 메넬라오스에게서 후한 대접을 받았다. 그런데 메넬라오스의 아내 헬레네야말로 아프로디테가 파리스에게 점지해 둔 바로 그 여인, 곧 여인들 가운데 가장 아름다운 이였다. 그녀는 수많은 구혼자들이 신붓감으로 탐낸 여인이었는데, 그녀의 선택이 알려지기 전에 그들은 모두, 자기들 가운데 하나였던 오디세우스의 제안에 따라, 그녀를 어떠한 해악으로부터도 지키고 필요하다면 그녀의 일을 위해 복수하겠노라 맹세하였다. 그녀는 메넬라오스를 택하였고, 파리스가 그들의 손님이 되었을 때 그와 더불어 행복하게 살고 있었다. 파리스는 아프로디테의 도움을 받아 그녀를 설득해 함께 달아나게 하였고, 그녀를 트로이아로 데려갔으니, 여기에서 그 유명한 트로이아 전쟁이 일어났다. 이는 호메로스와 베르길리우스의 작품들, 곧 고대 최고의 서사시들이 다룬 주제이다.\n\n메넬라오스는 그리스의 동료 족장들에게 그들이 한 맹세를 지켜, 아내를 되찾으려는 자신의 노력에 동참할 것을 호소하였다. 그들은 대체로 앞으로 나섰으나, 페넬로페와 혼인하여 아내와 자식과 더불어 매우 행복하게 지내던 오디세우스는 그처럼 성가신 일에 뛰어들 마음이 없었다. 그리하여 그는 뒤로 물러서 있었고, 그를 재촉하기 위해 팔라메데스가 파견되었다. 팔라메데스가 이타케에 당도하자 오디세우스는 미친 척하였다. 그는 나귀와 소를 함께 쟁기에 메우고 소금을 뿌리기 시작하였다. 팔라메데스는 그를 시험하기 위하여 어린 텔레마코스를 쟁기 앞에 놓았는데, 그러자 아비는 쟁기를 옆으로 돌려, 자신이 미치광이가 아님을 분명히 드러내고 말았으니, 그 뒤로는 더 이상 약속을 지키기를 거부할 수 없었다. 이제 스스로 이 일에 가담하게 된 그는, 마지못해하는 다른 족장들을, 특히 아킬레우스를 끌어들이는 데 힘을 보탰다. 이 영웅은 혼인 잔치에서 불화의 사과가 여신들 사이로 던져졌던 바로 그 테티스의 아들이었다. 테티스 자신은 불사의 존재이자 바다의 님프였는데, 자기 아들이 만약 이 원정에 나선다면 트로이아 앞에서 죽을 운명임을 알았으므로, 그가 가는 것을 막으려 애썼다. 그녀는 그를 리코메데스 왕의 궁정으로 보내, 처녀의 옷차림으로 변장하여 왕의 딸들 틈에 숨어 지내도록 하였다. 오디세우스는 그가 그곳에 있다는 말을 듣고, 상인으로 변장하여 궁전으로 가서 여인용 장신구를 팔겠노라 내놓았는데, 그 가운데 무기 몇 점을 끼워 두었다. 왕의 딸들이 상인 보따리의 다른 물건들에 정신이 팔려 있는 동안, 아킬레우스는 무기를 만지작거렸고, 이로써 오디세우스의 날카로운 눈에 자신의 정체를 드러내고 말았다. 오디세우스는 그가 어머니의 신중한 충고를 저버리고 동포들과 함께 전쟁에 나서도록 설득하는 데 별 어려움이 없었다.\n\n프리아모스는 트로이아의 왕이었고, 목동이자 헬레네를 꾄 파리스는 그의 아들이었다. 파리스는 어려서부터 그가 나라의 파멸이 되리라는 어떤 불길한 예언이 따라다녔던 까닭에 이름 없이 자랐다. 이 예언들은 마침내 실현될 것처럼 보였으니, 지금 준비되고 있는 그리스의 군세는 일찍이 갖추어진 어느 것보다도 거대하였기 때문이다. 미케네의 왕이자 피해를 입은 메넬라오스의 형인 아가멤논이 총사령관으로 선출되었다. 아킬레우스는 그들 가운데 가장 빛나는 전사였다. 그다음으로는 몸집이 거대하고 용맹은 대단하나 지혜가 둔한 아이아스, 영웅의 온갖 자질에서 아킬레우스에 버금가는 디오메데스, 명민함으로 이름난 오디세우스, 그리고 그리스 족장들 가운데 가장 나이가 많아 모두가 조언을 구하며 우러러보던 네스토르가 자리하였다. 그러나 트로이아도 결코 만만한 적은 아니었다. 왕 프리아모스는 이제 늙었으나, 일찍이 지혜로운 군주였으며 안으로는 선정을 펴고 밖으로는 이웃들과 수많은 동맹을 맺어 나라를 튼튼히 하였다. 그러나 그의 왕좌를 받치는 으뜸가는 기둥이자 버팀목은 그의 아들 헥토르였으니, 이는 이교 시대의 고대가 그려 낸 가장 고귀한 인물 가운데 하나였다. 그는 처음부터 조국의 멸망을 예감하였으나, 그럼에도 영웅다운 저항을 끝까지 이어 갔으며, 그러면서도 이 위험을 조국에 불러들인 잘못을 결코 두둔하지는 않았다. 그는 안드로마케와 혼인하였는데, 남편이자 아버지로서의 그의 인품은 전사로서의 그것에 못지않게 칭송할 만하였다. 트로이아 편의 주요 지도자들로는 헥토르 외에 아이네아스와 데이포보스, 글라우코스와 사르페돈이 있었다.\n\n이 년에 걸친 준비 끝에 그리스의 함대와 군대가 보이오티아의 아울리스 항구에 집결하였다. 이곳에서 아가멤논은 사냥을 하다가 아르테미스[로마의 〈디아나〉]에게 바쳐진 사슴 한 마리를 죽였고, 여신은 그 보복으로 군대에 역병을 내렸으며, 또한 바람을 잠재워 배들이 항구를 떠나지 못하게 하였다. 그러자 예언자 칼카스는, 처녀 여신의 노여움은 오직 그 제단 위에 처녀 하나를 제물로 바쳐야만 가라앉힐 수 있으며, 잘못을 저지른 자의 딸이 아니고서는 다른 누구도 받아들여지지 않으리라고 알렸다. 아가멤논은 비록 마지못한 일이었으나 동의하였고, 처녀 이피게네이아가 아킬레우스와 혼인하게 되었다는 구실로 불려 왔다. 그녀가 막 제물로 바쳐지려 할 때 여신은 마음이 누그러져 그녀를 낚아채 가고 그 자리에 암사슴 한 마리를 남겨 두었으며, 이피게네이아는 구름에 싸인 채 타우리스로 옮겨져, 그곳에서 아르테미스가 그녀를 자기 신전의 여사제로 삼았다.\n\n테니슨은 그의 「아름다운 여인들의 꿈」에서 이피게네이아가 제물로 바쳐지는 순간에 느낀 심정을 다음과 같이 묘사하고 있다.\n\n\"나는 그 슬픈 곳에서 모든 희망으로부터 끊겨 있었으니, 그 이름을 부르는 것조차 내 영혼은 꺼리고 두려워하노라. 내 아버지는 손으로 얼굴을 가렸고, 나는 눈물에 눈이 멀어, \"그래도 말하려 애썼으나, 내 목소리는 한숨에 막혔으니, 마치 꿈속과도 같았노라. 어슴푸레 나는 보았네, 이리 같은 눈을 한, 검은 수염의 준엄한 왕들이, 내가 죽는 것을 지켜보려 기다리는 모습을.\n\n\"높다란 돛대들이 물 위에 떠 흔들렸고, 신전들과 사람들과 해안도 그러하였네. 누군가 날카로운 칼을 내 여린 목에 천천히 그었노라, 그리고 그것으로 끝, 더는 아무것도 없었네.\"\n\n이제 바람이 순조로워지자 함대는 돛을 올려 병력을 트로이아의 해안으로 실어 갔다. 트로이아인들이 그들의 상륙을 막으러 나왔고, 첫 충돌에서 프로테실라오스가 헥토르의 손에 쓰러졌다. 프로테실라오스는 고향에 그를 더없이 애틋하게 사랑하는 아내 라오다메이아를 남겨 두었다. 그의 죽음을 알리는 소식이 그녀에게 닿자, 그녀는 단 세 시간만이라도 그와 이야기를 나눌 수 있게 해 달라고 신들에게 간청하였다. 그 청은 받아들여졌다. 헤르메스[로마의 〈메르쿠리우스〉]가 프로테실라오스를 이승으로 데려왔고, 그가 두 번째로 죽었을 때 라오다메이아도 그와 함께 죽었다. 님프들이 그의 무덤 둘레에 느릅나무를 심었는데, 그 나무들이 트로이아가 내려다보일 만큼 자라자 시들어 버렸고, 그 뿌리에서 새 가지들이 돋아났다는 이야기도 전한다.\n\n워즈워스는 프로테실라오스와 라오다메이아의 이야기를 시의 주제로 삼았다. 신탁이 선언하기를, 전쟁의 첫 희생자가 나오는 편에게 승리가 돌아가리라 하였던 모양이다. 시인은 프로테실라오스가 잠시 이승으로 돌아왔을 때, 라오다메이아에게 자신의 운명에 얽힌 사연을 들려주는 것으로 그리고 있다.\n\n\"'바라던 바람이 불어왔노라. 그때 나는 고요한 바다 위에서 신탁을 곱씹었노라. 그리고 나보다 더 합당한 자가 앞장서지 않는다면, 천 척의 배 가운데 내 배가 맨 먼저 그 뱃머리를 해안에 들이박으리라 결심하였네, 내 피가 트로이아의 모래를 처음으로 물들이리라고. \"'허나 사랑하는 아내여, 그대를 잃을 생각을 하면 그 아픔은 쓰라리고, 또 쓰라렸노라! 내 기억은 그대에게 너무도 애틋하게 매달렸고, 또 우리가 필멸의 삶에서 함께 누린 기쁨들, 우리가 거닐던 길들, 이 샘물들과 꽃들, 내가 새로이 세우려던 도시들과 미처 짓다 만 탑들에도. \"'허나 만약 머뭇거림이 적으로 하여금 외치게 한다면, \"보라, 저들이 떨고 있도다! 진영은 거만하건만, 그 수 가운데 누구도 감히 죽으려 하지 않는구나!\"라고, 나는 영혼으로 그 치욕을 쓸어 버렸노라. 그때 옛 약함이 되살아났으나, 드높은 생각이 행동으로 구현되어 나의 떠남을 이루었노라.' . . . . . . .\n\n\". . . 헬레스폰토스의 기슭에 (이런 믿음이 전해져 왔으니) 그녀가 그를 위해 죽은 그 사내의 무덤에서 뾰족한 나무 한 무리가 오랜 세월 자라났으니, 그리고 일리온의 성벽이 그 눈 아래 들어올 만큼 그 키가 자라날 때마다, 그 나무들의 높은 우듬지는 그 광경에 시들어 갔으니, 자라남과 마름이 끊임없이 갈마들었노라!\"\n\n「일리아스」\n\n전쟁은 구 년 동안 결정적인 결말 없이 이어졌다. 그러던 중 그리스 측의 대의에 치명적일 듯한 사건이 일어났으니, 곧 아킬레우스와 아가멤논 사이의 다툼이었다. 호메로스의 위대한 서사시 「일리아스」가 시작되는 것이 바로 이 지점이다. 그리스인들은 트로이아를 함락하지는 못하였으나, 그 인근의 동맹 도시들을 빼앗았는데, 전리품을 나누는 과정에서 아폴론의 사제 크리세스의 딸인 크리세이스라는 여인 포로가 아가멤논의 몫으로 떨어졌다. 크리세스는 자기 직무의 성스러운 표지를 지니고 와서 딸을 풀어 달라고 간청하였다. 아가멤논은 거절하였다. 그러자 크리세스는 그리스인들이 그 노획물을 내놓을 수밖에 없도록 그들을 괴롭혀 달라고 아폴론에게 빌었다. 아폴론은 자기 사제의 기도를 들어주어 그리스 진영에 역병을 보냈다. 그리하여 신들의 노여움을 어떻게 가라앉히고 역병을 물리칠지 의논하기 위한 회의가 소집되었다. 아킬레우스는 그들의 불행이 아가멤논이 크리세이스를 내놓지 않은 데서 비롯된 것이라며 대담하게 그에게 책임을 돌렸다. 아가멤논은 격분하여 자기 포로를 내놓는 데 동의하였으나, 그 대신 아킬레우스가 전리품을 나눌 때 자기 몫으로 떨어진 처녀 브리세이스를 자기에게 넘기라고 요구하였다. 아킬레우스는 순순히 따랐으나, 곧장 자신은 더 이상 전쟁에 가담하지 않겠노라 선언하였다. 그는 전체 진영에서 자기 병력을 거두어들이고, 그리스로 돌아가겠다는 뜻을 공공연히 밝혔다.\n\n신들과 여신들은 이 유명한 전쟁에 당사자들 못지않게 깊은 관심을 기울였다. 트로이아의 적들이 끝까지 버티고 제 발로 이 일을 포기하지 않는다면 마침내 트로이아가 함락되리라는 것이 운명에 의해 정해져 있음을, 그들은 잘 알고 있었다. 그러나 어느 한쪽 편을 든 천상의 권능들의 기대와 두려움을 번갈아 자극할 만한 우연의 여지는 여전히 충분히 남아 있었다. 헤라와 미네르바는 파리스가 자기들의 아름다움을 무시한 탓에 트로이아인들에게 적대적이었고, 아프로디테는 그와 반대되는 까닭에 그들을 편들었다. 아프로디테는 자기를 흠모하는 아레스[로마의 〈마르스〉]를 같은 편으로 끌어들였으나, 포세이돈[로마의 〈넵투누스〉]은 그리스인들을 편들었다. 아폴론은 중립이어서 때로는 이쪽을, 때로는 저쪽을 들었고, 제우스 자신은 비록 선량한 왕 프리아모스를 사랑하였으나 어느 정도 공정함을 지켰는데, 다만 예외가 없지는 않았다.\n\n아킬레우스의 어머니 테티스는 자기 아들이 입은 모욕에 격하게 분노하였다. 그녀는 곧장 제우스의 궁전으로 가서, 트로이아의 무력에 승리를 안겨 줌으로써 그리스인들이 아킬레우스에게 저지른 불의를 뉘우치게 해 달라고 그에게 간청하였다. 제우스는 동의하였고, 뒤이은 전투에서 트로이아인들은 완전한 승리를 거두었다. 그리스인들은 전장에서 쫓겨나 자기들의 배 안으로 피신하였다.\n\n그러자 아가멤논은 가장 지혜롭고 용감한 족장들을 불러 회의를 열었다. 네스토르는 아킬레우스에게 사절을 보내 그를 설득하여 전장으로 돌아오게 하고, 아가멤논은 분쟁의 원인이 된 그 처녀를 넘겨주되 자신이 저지른 잘못을 보상할 넉넉한 선물을 함께 보내자고 권하였다. 아가멤논이 동의하니, 오디세우스와 아이아스와 포이닉스가 아킬레우스에게 뉘우침의 전갈을 전하러 파견되었다. 그들은 그 임무를 수행하였으나, 아킬레우스는 그들의 간청에 귀를 닫았다. 그는 전장으로 돌아가기를 단호히 거부하였고, 지체 없이 그리스로 떠나겠다는 결심을 굽히지 않았다.\n\n그리스인들은 자기들의 배 둘레에 방벽을 쌓아 두었는데, 이제 그들은 트로이아를 포위하기는커녕 도리어 자기들의 방벽 안에 갇혀 포위당한 형국이 되었다. 아킬레우스에게 보낸 사절이 실패한 다음 날 전투가 벌어졌고, 제우스의 가호를 받은 트로이아인들이 승리하여 그리스의 방벽을 뚫고 길을 내는 데 성공하였으며, 막 배에 불을 지르려 하였다. 포세이돈은 그리스인들이 그처럼 몰리는 것을 보고 그들을 구하러 왔다. 그는 예언자 칼카스의 모습으로 나타나 외침으로 전사들을 격려하고 한 사람 한 사람에게 호소하여, 마침내 그들의 투지를 한껏 끌어올려 트로이아인들을 물러서게 만들었다. 아이아스는 놀라운 용맹을 펼쳤고, 마침내 헥토르와 맞닥뜨렸다. 아이아스가 도전의 함성을 지르자 헥토르도 이에 응수하며 그 거대한 전사를 향해 창을 던졌다. 창은 잘 겨냥되어 아이아스의 가슴에서 칼과 방패를 멘 두 띠가 엇갈리는 곳을 맞혔다. 이중의 보호막이 창이 뚫고 들어가는 것을 막아 창은 아무 해도 없이 떨어졌다. 그러자 아이아스는 배를 받치는 데 쓰이던 커다란 돌 하나를 집어 헥토르를 향해 던졌다. 돌은 그의 목을 맞혀 그를 들판에 쓰러뜨렸다. 그의 부하들이 곧장 그를 붙들어, 정신을 잃고 부상당한 그를 들쳐 메고 물러갔다.\n\n포세이돈이 이처럼 그리스인들을 돕고 트로이아인들을 물리치는 동안, 제우스는 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 전혀 알지 못하였으니, 헤라의 술책에 그의 주의가 전장에서 돌려졌기 때문이다. 그 여신은 자신의 온갖 매력으로 단장하였고, 거기에 더하여 아프로디테에게서 \"케스토스\"라 불리는 띠를 빌렸는데, 그것은 두른 자의 매력을 도저히 거역할 수 없을 만큼 한껏 돋우는 효력이 있었다. 이렇게 단장한 헤라는 올림포스에 앉아 전투를 지켜보고 있던 남편에게로 갔다. 제우스가 그녀를 바라보니 그녀가 어찌나 매혹적이던지, 젊은 날 사랑의 애틋함이 되살아나, 그는 맞싸우는 군대들도 다른 모든 국사도 잊고 오직 그녀만을 생각하며 전투가 되어 가는 대로 내버려 두었다.\n\n그러나 이 도취는 오래 가지 않았으니, 그가 눈길을 아래로 돌려 헥토르가 아픔과 타박상으로 거의 숨이 끊긴 채 들판에 쓰러져 있는 것을 보았을 때, 그는 노하여 헤라를 물리치고 이리스와 아폴론을 자기에게 보내라고 명하였다. 이리스가 오자 그는 그녀를 시켜 포세이돈에게 준엄한 전갈을 보내, 그에게 당장 전장을 떠나라고 명령하였다. 아폴론은 헥토르의 타박상을 치유하고 그의 마음에 기운을 불어넣으라고 파견되었다. 이 명령들은 어찌나 빨리 이행되었던지, 전투가 아직 한창인 가운데 헥토르가 전장으로 돌아왔고 포세이돈은 자신의 영역으로 물러갔다.\n\n파리스의 활에서 날아온 화살 하나가 아스클레피오스의 아들 마카온을 맞혔다. 그는 아버지의 치유 기술을 물려받았기에 그리스인들에게 외과의로서 큰 가치가 있었으며, 또한 그들 가운데 가장 용감한 전사 중 하나이기도 하였다. 네스토르가 마카온을 자기 전차에 태워 전장에서 데리고 나왔다. 그들이 아킬레우스의 배 곁을 지날 때, 들판을 내다보고 있던 그 영웅은 네스토르의 전차를 보고 그 노족장을 알아보았으나, 부상당한 자가 누구인지는 분간하지 못하였다. 그리하여 그는 자기 동무이자 가장 소중한 벗인 파트로클로스를 불러, 네스토르의 막사로 가서 알아보고 오라고 보냈다.\n\n파트로클로스는 네스토르의 막사에 이르러 마카온이 부상당한 것을 보았고, 자신이 온 까닭을 말한 뒤 서둘러 돌아가려 하였으나, 네스토르가 그를 붙들어 그리스인들이 겪은 재앙이 얼마나 큰지를 일러 주었다. 그는 또한 트로이아로 떠날 무렵 아킬레우스와 그가 각자의 아버지로부터 서로 다른 당부를 받았던 일도 일깨워 주었으니, 아킬레우스는 영광의 가장 높은 봉우리를 갈망하라는 것이었고, 파트로클로스는 손위인 만큼 벗을 지켜보며 그의 미숙함을 이끌어 주라는 것이었다. 네스토르가 말하였다. \"지금이야말로 그런 영향력을 발휘할 때일세. 신들이 허락하신다면, 자네가 그를 우리 공동의 대의로 되돌려 놓을 수도 있을 것이네. 허나 그렇지 못하더라도 적어도 그가 자기 병사들이나마 전장에 보내게 하고, 파트로클로스, 자네가 그의 갑옷을 입고 나가게나. 어쩌면 그 모습만으로도 트로이아인들을 물러서게 할 수 있을지 모르니.\"\n\n파트로클로스는 이 말에 크게 감동하여, 자신이 보고 들은 모든 것을 마음속으로 곱씹으며 서둘러 아킬레우스에게 돌아왔다. 그는 한때 동료였던 이들의 진영이 처한 비참한 상황을 그 영웅에게 전하였다. 디오메데스, 오디세우스, 아가멤논, 마카온이 모두 부상당하였고, 방벽은 무너졌으며, 적은 배들 사이에 들어와 그것에 불을 질러 그리스로 돌아갈 모든 길을 끊으려 하고 있다는 것이었다. 그들이 이야기를 나누는 동안 배 한 척에서 불길이 치솟았다. 그 광경을 본 아킬레우스는 마음이 누그러져, 파트로클로스가 미르미돈족(아킬레우스의 병사들은 그렇게 불렸다)을 이끌고 전장으로 나가게 해 달라는 청을 들어주고, 또 그가 트로이아인들의 마음에 더 큰 두려움을 심을 수 있도록 자기 갑옷을 빌려주기에 이르렀다. 지체 없이 병사들이 대오를 갖추었고, 파트로클로스는 빛나는 갑옷을 걸치고 아킬레우스의 전차에 올라, 싸움에 불타는 병사들을 이끌고 나아갔다. 그러나 그가 떠나기 전에 아킬레우스는 그에게 적을 물리치는 것으로 만족하라고 엄히 당부하였다. 그가 말하였다. \"나 없이 트로이아인들을 몰아붙이려 하지 말게. 그리하여 이미 내게 씌워진 치욕에 더 보태지 않도록 하게나.\" 그러고 나서 그는 병사들에게 최선을 다하라 격려하며 싸움의 열의에 가득 찬 그들을 떠나보냈다.\n\n파트로클로스와 그의 미르미돈족은 싸움이 가장 격렬한 곳으로 곧장 뛰어들었다. 그 광경에 기뻐한 그리스인들이 함성을 질렀고 배들이 그 환호를 되울렸다. 트로이아인들은 그 낯익은 갑옷을 보고 두려움에 사로잡혀 사방으로 피할 곳을 찾았다. 먼저 배를 차지하여 불을 질렀던 자들이 떠나면서 그리스인들이 그 배를 되찾아 불길을 끄게 하였다. 그러자 나머지 트로이아인들도 겁에 질려 달아났다. 아이아스와 메넬라오스와 네스토르의 두 아들은 놀라운 용맹을 펼쳤다. 헥토르는 말머리를 돌려 그 울타리에서 물러설 수밖에 없었고, 자기 부하들이 참호에 얽혀 제 살길대로 빠져나오도록 내버려 두었다. 파트로클로스는 그들을 앞으로 몰아대며 많은 자를 죽였으니, 누구도 감히 그에게 맞서지 못하였다.\n\n마침내 제우스의 아들 사르페돈이 감히 나서서 파트로클로스와 싸움을 벌였다. 제우스는 그를 내려다보며 그를 기다리는 운명에서 낚아채 가고자 하였으나, 헤라가 만약 그가 그리한다면 천상의 다른 모든 거주자들도 자기 자손이 위험에 처할 때마다 똑같이 끼어들게 될 것이라고 일러 주었고, 제우스는 그 이치에 따랐다. 사르페돈이 창을 던졌으나 파트로클로스를 빗맞혔고, 파트로클로스가 던진 창은 더 정확하였다. 그것은 사르페돈의 가슴을 꿰뚫었고, 그는 쓰러져 자기 벗들에게 자기 시신을 적에게서 지켜 달라고 외치며 숨을 거두었다. 그러자 그 시신을 차지하려는 격렬한 다툼이 일어났다. 그리스인들이 이겨 사르페돈의 갑옷을 벗겨 냈으나, 제우스는 자기 아들의 유해가 욕보이는 것을 허락하지 않았으니, 그의 명에 따라 아폴론이 싸우는 자들 한가운데서 사르페돈의 시신을 낚아채 죽음과 잠이라는 쌍둥이 형제에게 맡겼고, 그들이 그것을 사르페돈의 고향 땅 리키아로 옮겨, 그곳에서 마땅한 장례 의식이 치러졌다.\n\n여기까지 파트로클로스는 트로이아인들을 물리치고 동포들을 구해 내려는 자신의 바람을 더할 나위 없이 이루었으나, 이제 운수가 뒤바뀌었다. 전차에 탄 헥토르가 그와 맞섰다. 파트로클로스가 헥토르를 향해 거대한 돌을 던졌는데, 겨냥은 빗나갔으나 마부 케브리오네스를 맞혀 그를 수레에서 떨어뜨렸다. 헥토르는 자기 벗을 구하려 전차에서 뛰어내렸고, 파트로클로스 또한 승리를 마무리하려 내려섰다. 이리하여 두 영웅이 얼굴을 맞대고 마주 섰다. 이 결정적인 순간에 시인은, 마치 헥토르에게 영광을 주기를 꺼리는 듯, 포이보스[아폴론]가 파트로클로스에 맞서 가담하였음을 기록하고 있다. 아폴론은 그의 머리에서 투구를 쳐서 떨어뜨리고 그의 손에서 창을 떨어뜨렸다. 바로 그 순간 어느 이름 없는 트로이아인이 그의 등을 찔렀고, 헥토르가 앞으로 밀고 들어와 그를 창으로 꿰뚫었다. 그는 치명상을 입고 쓰러졌다.\n\n그러자 파트로클로스의 시신을 둘러싸고 엄청난 격투가 벌어졌으나, 그의 갑옷은 곧장 헥토르의 차지가 되었다. 헥토르는 잠시 물러나 자기 갑옷을 벗고 아킬레우스의 갑옷을 걸친 다음 다시 싸움으로 돌아왔다. 아이아스와 메넬라오스가 시신을 지켰고, 헥토르와 그의 가장 용감한 전사들이 그것을 빼앗으려 안간힘을 썼다. 싸움이 어느 쪽에도 기울지 않고 격렬하게 이어지던 그때, 제우스가 온 하늘을 어두운 구름으로 뒤덮었다. 번개가 번쩍이고 천둥이 울렸으니, 아이아스는 아킬레우스에게 보내어 그의 벗의 죽음과 그 유해가 적의 손에 떨어질 임박한 위험을 알릴 누군가를 찾아 둘러보았으나, 마땅한 전령을 찾을 수 없었다. 그가 그토록 자주 인용되는 저 유명한 시구로 부르짖은 것은 바로 그때였다.\n\n\"하늘과 땅의 아버지시여! 당신께서 아카이아의 군대를 어둠에서 건져 주소서. 하늘을 맑게 하시고, 빛을 주소서. 그리고 당신의 지엄한 뜻이 그러하다면, 그와 더불어 파멸도 함께 주소서. 허나, 오, 우리에게 빛만은 주소서.\" ─카우퍼.\n\n또는 포프가 옮긴 대로는 이러하다.\n\n\". . . 땅과 하늘의 주여! 오 왕이시여! 오 아버지시여! 제 비천한 기도를 들으소서! 이 구름을 흩으시고, 하늘의 빛을 되돌려 주소서. 제게 볼 수 있게만 해 주시면 아이아스는 더 바라지 않나이다. 그리스가 멸하여야 한다면 우리는 당신의 뜻에 따르겠나이다. 허나 우리로 하여금 대낮을 마주하고 멸하게 하소서.\"\n\n제우스는 그 기도를 듣고 구름을 흩어 버렸다. 그러자 아이아스는 안틸로코스를 아킬레우스에게 보내 파트로클로스의 죽음과, 그의 유해를 두고 벌어지는 격투의 소식을 전하게 하였다. 그리스인들은 마침내 헥토르와 아이네아스와 나머지 트로이아인들의 바짝 추격을 받으면서도 그 시신을 배까지 옮기는 데 성공하였다.\n\n아킬레우스는 자기 벗의 운명을 전해 듣고 어찌나 비통해하던지, 안틸로코스는 한동안 그가 스스로 목숨을 끊지나 않을까 두려워하였다. 그의 신음 소리는 그가 머무는 깊디깊은 대양 속 그의 어머니 테티스의 귀에까지 닿았고, 그녀는 그 까닭을 묻고자 그에게로 서둘러 왔다. 그녀는 그가, 자신의 분노에 그토록 빠져들어 벗이 그 분노의 희생물로 쓰러지게 둔 일에 대한 자책에 짓눌려 있는 것을 보았다. 그러나 그의 유일한 위안은 복수의 희망이었다. 그는 당장 헥토르를 찾아 달려 나가려 하였다. 그러나 그의 어머니는 그가 지금 갑옷이 없는 처지임을 일깨우며, 만약 그가 다음 날 아침까지만 기다려 준다면 헤파이스토스[로마의 〈불카누스〉]에게서 그가 잃은 것보다 한층 뛰어난 갑옷 한 벌을 구해 오겠노라 약속하였다. 그가 동의하자 테티스는 즉시 헤파이스토스의 궁전으로 갔다. 그녀는 그가 자기가 쓸 세발솥을 만드느라 화로에서 분주한 것을 보았는데, 그것은 어찌나 정교하게 만들어졌던지 필요할 때면 저절로 앞으로 움직여 나오고 물러나라 하면 다시 물러나는 것이었다. 테티스의 청을 듣자 헤파이스토스는 곧장 하던 일을 내려놓고 서둘러 그녀의 바람을 들어주었다. 그는 아킬레우스를 위하여 화려한 갑옷 한 벌을 만들었으니, 먼저 정교한 무늬로 장식된 방패를, 다음으로 황금 깃을 단 투구를, 그다음으로 어떤 것도 뚫지 못할 강도로 단련된 흉갑과 정강이받이를 지었는데, 모두 그의 몸에 완벽하게 들어맞고 더없이 뛰어난 솜씨로 빚어진 것이었다. 이 모든 것이 하룻밤 사이에 이루어졌고, 테티스는 그것을 받아 그것을 들고 땅으로 내려와, 동이 틀 무렵 아킬레우스의 발치에 그것을 내려놓았다.\n\n파트로클로스가 죽은 이래 아킬레우스가 처음으로 느낀 기쁨의 빛은 이 화려한 갑옷을 본 순간이었다. 그리고 이제 그것을 걸친 채 그는 진영으로 나아가 모든 족장을 회의에 불러 모았다. 그들이 모두 모이자 그는 그들에게 연설하였다. 아가멤논에 대한 노여움을 거두고 그로부터 비롯된 참화를 쓰라리게 한탄하며, 그는 그들에게 당장 전장으로 나아가자고 호소하였다. 아가멤논도 합당한 대답을 하여 모든 잘못을 불화의 여신 아테에게 돌렸고, 이에 두 영웅 사이에 완전한 화해가 이루어졌다.\n\n그러고 나서 아킬레우스는 그를 거역할 수 없는 존재로 만드는 분노와 복수의 갈망에 사로잡혀 싸움터로 나아갔다. 가장 용감한 전사들도 그 앞에서 달아나거나 그의 창에 쓰러졌다. 헥토르는 아폴론의 경고를 받고 멀찍이 떨어져 있었으나, 그 신은 프리아모스의 아들 리카온의 모습을 빌려 아이네아스에게 그 무서운 전사와 맞서라고 부추겼다. 아이네아스는 자신이 그에 미치지 못함을 느끼면서도 싸움을 마다하지 않았다. 그는 온 힘을 다하여 헤파이스토스의 솜씨인 그 방패를 향해 창을 던졌다. 그것은 다섯 겹의 금속판으로 이루어져 있었으니, 둘은 청동이요, 둘은 주석이요, 하나는 황금이었다. 창은 두 겹을 뚫었으나 세 번째에서 멈추었다. 아킬레우스가 던진 창은 더 정확하였다. 그것은 아이네아스의 방패를 꿰뚫었으나 그의 어깨 곁을 스쳐 상처를 입히지는 못하였다. 그러자 아이네아스는 요즘 사람 둘이 겨우 들어 올릴 만한 돌을 집어 막 던지려 하였고, 아킬레우스도 칼을 빼 들고 막 그에게 달려들려 하였는데, 그때 그 싸움을 지켜보던 포세이돈이, 신속히 구해 내지 않으면 아이네아스가 틀림없이 희생물이 되리라는 것을 보고 그에 대한 연민에 움직여, 싸우는 두 사람 사이에 구름을 펼치고 아이네아스를 땅에서 들어 올려, 전사들과 군마들의 머리 위로 그를 옮겨 전장의 후방에 내려놓았다. 안개가 걷히자 아킬레우스는 자기 적수를 찾아 헛되이 둘러보았고, 그 기이한 일을 인정하며 다른 용사들에게로 무기를 돌렸다. 그러나 누구도 감히 그 앞에 서지 못하였고, 성벽에서 내려다보던 프리아모스는 자기 온 군대가 도성을 향해 황급히 달아나는 것을 보았다. 그는 도망쳐 오는 자들을 받아들이도록 성문을 활짝 열되, 적이 함께 들어오지 못하도록 트로이아인들이 다 지나가는 즉시 닫으라고 명하였다. 그러나 아킬레우스가 어찌나 바짝 뒤쫓던지, 만약 아폴론이 프리아모스의 아들 아게노르의 모습을 빌려 잠시 아킬레우스와 맞서다가 이내 달아나는 척하며 도성에서 벗어난 길로 빠지지 않았더라면 그것은 불가능하였을 것이다. 아킬레우스는 그를 뒤쫓아 자기가 노린 희생물을 성벽에서 멀리까지 몰아갔는데, 그때 아폴론이 자신의 정체를 드러냈고, 아킬레우스는 자기가 어떻게 속았는지를 깨닫고 추격을 그만두었다.\n\n그러나 나머지가 모두 도성 안으로 피해 들어간 뒤에도 헥토르는 싸움을 기다리기로 마음먹고 밖에 서 있었다. 그의 늙은 아버지가 성벽에서 그를 불러, 물러나 그 대결을 무릅쓰지 말라고 애원하였다. 그의 어머니 헤카베도 같은 뜻으로 그에게 간청하였으나, 모두 헛일이었다. 그는 혼자 중얼거렸다. \"내 명령으로 사람들이 오늘의 싸움에 나아가 그토록 많은 이가 쓰러졌거늘, 내 어찌 단 하나의 적을 피해 나 자신의 안위를 구한단 말인가? 허나 만약 내가 그에게 헬레네와 그녀의 모든 보물에 더하여 우리 자신의 것까지 넉넉히 내주겠다고 제안한다면? 아, 아니다! 이미 너무 늦었다. 그는 내 말을 끝까지 듣지도 않고, 내가 말하는 동안 나를 베어 버릴 것이다.\" 그가 이렇게 곱씹는 동안, 아킬레우스가 아레스처럼 무섭게, 움직일 때마다 갑옷이 번개처럼 번쩍이며 다가왔다. 그 광경에 헥토르는 가슴이 내려앉아 달아났다. 아킬레우스가 날쌔게 뒤쫓았다. 그들은 줄곧 성벽 가까이 붙어 달려, 세 번이나 도성을 돌았다. 헥토르가 성벽에 다가갈 때마다 아킬레우스가 그를 가로막아 더 넓은 원을 그리며 밖으로 돌게 만들었다. 그러나 아폴론이 헥토르의 힘을 떠받쳐 그가 지쳐 쓰러지지 않게 하였다. 그때 팔라스[미네르바]가 헥토르의 가장 용감한 아우 데이포보스의 모습을 빌려 갑자기 그의 곁에 나타났다. 헥토르는 기뻐하며 그를 보았고, 이렇게 힘을 얻어 달아나기를 멈추고 돌아서서 아킬레우스를 맞이하였다. 헥토르가 창을 던지니 그것은 아킬레우스의 방패에 맞아 튕겨 나왔다. 그가 데이포보스의 손에서 또 다른 창을 받으려 돌아섰으나, 데이포보스는 사라지고 없었다. 그제야 헥토르는 자신의 운명을 깨닫고 말하였다. \"아아! 이제 내가 죽을 때가 온 것이 분명하구나! 데이포보스가 곁에 있는 줄 알았건만, 팔라스가 나를 속였고, 그는 아직 트로이아 안에 있구나. 허나 나는 영광 없이 쓰러지지는 않으리라.\" 이렇게 말하며 그는 옆구리에서 칼을 빼 들고 단숨에 싸움으로 달려들었다. 방패 뒤에 몸을 숨긴 아킬레우스는 헥토르가 다가오기를 기다렸다. 그가 창이 닿을 거리에 들어오자, 아킬레우스는 갑옷이 목을 가리지 못하고 드러나는 취약한 부위를 눈으로 골라, 그곳을 향해 창을 겨누었다. 헥토르는 죽음의 상처를 입고 쓰러져 힘없이 말하였다. \"내 몸을 아껴 주시오! 내 부모가 그것을 되사게 하고, 내가 트로이아의 아들딸들에게서 장례 의식을 받게 해 주시오.\" 이에 아킬레우스가 대답하였다. \"개 같은 놈아, 그토록 끔찍한 고통을 내게 안겨 준 주제에 내게 몸값이니 연민이니 입에 올리지 마라. 안 된다! 내 말을 믿어라, 그 무엇도 네 시체를 개들에게서 구해 내지 못하리라. 스무 배의 몸값과 네 무게만큼의 황금을 내놓는다 해도, 나는 그 모두를 거절하리라.\"\n\n이렇게 말하며 그는 시신에서 갑옷을 벗기고, 발에 끈을 묶어 그것을 자기 전차 뒤에 매달아 시신이 땅바닥에 끌리게 두었다. 그러고 나서 전차에 올라 군마들을 채찍질하여, 도성 앞에서 시신을 이리저리 끌고 다녔다. 이 광경을 본 프리아모스 왕과 헤카베 왕비의 슬픔을 무슨 말로 다 전할 수 있으랴! 그의 백성들은 그 늙은 왕이 밖으로 뛰쳐나가려는 것을 가까스로 막았다. 그는 흙먼지 속에 몸을 던지고, 그들 하나하나의 이름을 부르며 길을 비켜 달라고 애원하였다. 헤카베의 비탄도 그에 못지않게 격렬하였다. 시민들은 그들을 둘러싸고 울었다. 그 애도의 소리는, 시녀들 틈에 앉아 일을 하던 헥토르의 아내 안드로마케의 귀에까지 닿았고, 불길함을 예감한 그녀는 성벽으로 나아갔다. 그곳에 펼쳐진 광경을 보았을 때, 그녀는 성벽에서 곤두박질쳐 몸을 던지려 하였으나, 정신을 잃고 시녀들의 품에 쓰러졌다. 정신을 차린 그녀는 자신의 운명을 한탄하며, 조국이 멸망하고 자신은 포로가 되며 아들은 낯선 이들의 자비에 빵을 의지하게 될 모습을 마음속에 그렸다.\n\n아킬레우스와 그리스인들이 파트로클로스를 죽인 자에게 복수를 마치자, 그들은 자기 벗에게 마땅한 장례 의식을 치르는 데 분주하였다. 장작더미가 세워지고, 시신은 엄숙하게 화장되었다. 그리고 뒤이어 힘과 기술을 겨루는 경기들, 곧 전차 경주와 씨름과 권투와 활쏘기가 펼쳐졌다. 그러고 나서 족장들은 장례 연회에 둘러앉았고, 그 뒤에 쉬러 물러갔다. 그러나 아킬레우스는 잔치에도 잠에도 들지 않았다. 잃어버린 벗에 대한 회상이 그를 깨어 있게 하였으니, 그는 수고와 위험 속에서, 전투에서나 험난한 바다 위에서 함께한 그들의 우정을 떠올렸다. 가장 이른 새벽이 오기 전에 그는 자기 막사를 떠나, 날쌘 군마들을 전차에 매고 헥토르의 시신을 뒤에 끌리도록 묶었다. 두 번 그는 파트로클로스의 무덤 둘레로 그를 끌고 다니다가, 마침내 그를 흙먼지 속에 뻗어 둔 채 내버려 두었다. 그러나 아폴론은 이 온갖 모욕에도 시신이 찢기거나 일그러지는 것을 허락하지 않고, 그것을 어떤 더럽힘이나 흠집도 없이 온전하게 지켜 주었다.\n\n아킬레우스가 이처럼 용맹한 헥토르를 욕보이며 분노를 풀고 있는 동안, 제우스는 가엾이 여겨 테티스를 자기 앞으로 불러들였다. 그는 그녀에게 자기 아들에게 가서 헥토르의 시신을 그의 친지들에게 돌려주도록 설득하라고 일렀다. 그러고 나서 제우스는 이리스를 프리아모스 왕에게 보내, 그가 아킬레우스에게 가서 자기 아들의 시신을 청하도록 격려하게 하였다. 이리스가 그 전갈을 전하자 프리아모스는 즉시 따를 채비를 하였다. 그는 자기 보고를 열어 값진 의복과 천들을, 황금 열 탈란톤과 화려한 세발솥 두 개, 그리고 견줄 데 없는 솜씨로 빚은 황금 잔 하나를 함께 꺼냈다. 그러고 나서 그는 아들들을 불러, 자기 수레를 끌어내어 아킬레우스에게 몸값으로 바칠 갖가지 물건을 그 안에 실으라고 명하였다. 모든 준비가 끝나자, 늙은 왕은 자신만큼이나 나이 든 동무 하나, 곧 전령 이다이오스만을 데리고 성문 밖으로 수레를 몰았으니, 그곳에서 그의 왕비 헤카베와 모든 친지와 작별하였는데, 그들은 그가 틀림없는 죽음을 향해 가는 것이라 여기며 그를 위해 한탄하였다.\n\n그러나 제우스는 그 존경할 만한 왕을 연민으로 굽어보며, 헤르메스를 보내 그의 안내자이자 보호자가 되게 하였다. 헤르메스는 젊은 전사의 모습을 빌려 그 노부부 앞에 나타났는데, 그를 보고 그들이 달아날지 머무를지 망설이는 사이 그 신이 다가와 프리아모스의 손을 잡고, 그들을 아킬레우스의 막사까지 안내하겠노라 제안하였다. 프리아모스가 그 베풀어진 도움을 기꺼이 받아들이자, 그는 수레에 올라 고삐를 잡고 곧 그들을 아킬레우스의 막사로 데려갔다. 헤르메스의 지팡이가 모든 보초를 잠재웠고, 그는 아무런 방해도 받지 않고 프리아모스를 막사 안으로 들였다. 그곳에는 두 전사의 시중을 받으며 아킬레우스가 앉아 있었다. 늙은 왕은 아킬레우스의 발치에 몸을 던지고, 그토록 많은 자기 아들을 죽인 저 무서운 손에 입을 맞추었다. 그가 말하였다. \"오 아킬레우스여, 나처럼 살날이 다하여 삶의 어두운 가장자리에서 떨고 있는 그대 자신의 아버지를 생각해 보시오. 어쩌면 지금 이 순간에도 어느 이웃 족장이 그를 짓누르고 있건만 그 곤경에 그를 구해 줄 이가 곁에 없을지도 모르오. 그래도 그는 분명 아킬레우스가 살아 있음을 알고, 언젠가 다시 그대의 얼굴을 보게 되리라는 희망으로 여전히 기뻐하고 있을 것이오. 허나 나에게는 어떤 위안도 없으니, 얼마 전까지 일리온의 꽃이었던 나의 가장 용감한 아들들이 모두 쓰러졌소. 그래도 내게 하나가 있었으니, 다른 모든 자식보다 더한, 내 노년의 힘이었던 그 아이를, 조국을 위해 싸우던 그를 그대가 죽였소. 나는 그 몸을 되찾으러 왔으니, 헤아릴 수 없는 몸값을 가지고 왔소. 아킬레우스여! 신들을 경외하시오! 그대의 아버지를 떠올리시오! 그분을 위하여 내게 연민을 베풀어 주시오!\" 이 말이 아킬레우스의 마음을 움직여 그는 눈물을 흘렸으니, 멀리 있는 자기 아버지와 잃어버린 벗을 번갈아 떠올렸기 때문이다. 프리아모스의 은빛 머리카락과 수염에 연민이 일어, 그는 그를 땅에서 일으키며 이렇게 말하였다. \"프리아모스여, 나는 그대가 어느 신의 인도를 받아 이곳에 이르렀음을 아오. 신의 도움 없이는 한창때의 젊은이라 할지라도 어떤 필멸자도 감히 그런 시도를 하지 못하였을 것이기 때문이오. 제우스의 뜻이 분명함에 움직여, 나는 그대의 청을 들어주겠소.\" 이렇게 말하며 그는 일어나 자기 두 벗과 함께 밖으로 나가 수레에 실린 짐을 부리고, 시신을 덮을 외투 두 벌과 겉옷 한 벌을 남겨 두었다. 그것들을 수레에 놓고 그 위에 옷들을 펼쳐, 시신이 가려지지 않은 채로 트로이아로 옮겨지지 않게 하였다. 그러고 나서 아킬레우스는, 먼저 장례 의식을 위하여 열이틀 동안의 휴전을 허락하겠노라 다짐한 뒤, 그 늙은 왕을 시종과 함께 떠나보냈다.\n\n수레가 도성에 다가와 성벽에서 그것이 보이자, 사람들은 자기들의 영웅의 얼굴을 다시 한번 바라보려 쏟아져 나왔다. 누구보다 앞서 헥토르의 어머니와 아내가 왔고, 숨이 끊긴 그 시신을 보고 그들의 비탄을 새로이 터뜨렸다. 백성들도 모두 그들과 함께 울었으니, 해가 질 때까지 그들의 슬픔에는 멈춤도 누그러짐도 없었다.\n\n이튿날 장례 의식을 위한 준비가 갖추어졌다. 아흐레 동안 사람들은 나무를 날라 장작더미를 쌓았고, 열흘째 되는 날 그 꼭대기에 시신을 올리고 횃불을 댔으니, 그동안 온 트로이아가 쏟아져 나와 그 장작더미를 에워쌌다. 그것이 완전히 타고 나자, 그들은 포도주로 그 잿불을 끄고 뼈를 모아 황금 항아리에 담아 땅에 묻었으며, 그 자리 위에 돌무더기를 쌓아 올렸다.\n\n\"일리온은 그 영웅에게 이런 예를 바쳤으니, 위대한 헥토르의 혼령은 평온히 잠들었노라.\" ─포프.\n\n────\n\n# THE FALL OF TROY\n\n일리아스의 이야기는 헥토르의 죽음으로 끝나며, 다른 영웅들의 운명을 우리가 알게 되는 것은 오디세이아와 그 이후의 시들을 통해서다. 헥토르가 죽은 뒤에도 트로이아는 곧바로 함락되지 않았으며, 새로운 동맹들의 도움을 받아 여전히 저항을 이어 갔다. 이 동맹들 가운데 하나가 에티오피아의 왕자 멤논이었으니, 그의 이야기는 우리가 이미 들려준 바 있다. 또 다른 동맹은 아마조네스의 여왕 펜테실레이아였는데, 그녀는 여전사들의 무리를 이끌고 왔다. 모든 전거가 그들의 용맹과 그 전투의 함성이 자아낸 무시무시한 효과를 입증한다. 펜테실레이아는 가장 용맹한 전사들 가운데 많은 이를 죽였으나, 마침내 아킬레우스의 손에 쓰러졌다. 그러나 이 영웅이 쓰러진 적 위로 몸을 굽혀 그녀의 아름다움과 젊음과 용맹을 들여다보았을 때, 그는 자신의 승리를 쓰라리게 후회했다. 오만한 싸움꾼이자 선동가인 테르시테스가 그의 슬픔을 비웃었고, 그 결과 이 영웅의 손에 죽임을 당했다.\n\n아킬레우스는 우연히 프리아모스 왕의 딸 폴릭세네를 본 적이 있었으니, 아마도 헥토르의 장례를 위해 트로이아인들에게 허락되었던 휴전 때였을 것이다. 그는 그녀의 매력에 사로잡혔고, 그녀를 아내로 얻기 위해 그리스인들에게 자신의 영향력을 행사하여 트로이아에 평화를 허락하도록 하기로 합의했다. 아폴론의 신전에서 혼인을 협상하던 중, 파리스가 그를 향해 독화살을 쏘았으니, 그 화살은 아폴론의 인도를 받아 아킬레우스의 발뒤꿈치를 맞혔다. 그곳은 그의 몸에서 유일하게 상처를 입을 수 있는 부분이었다. 그의 어머니 테티스가 그를 갓난아기였을 때 스틱스 강에 담갔던 까닭에, 그를 붙잡고 있던 발뒤꿈치만을 제외하고 그의 온몸이 상처를 입지 않게 되었기 때문이다.[21]\n\n이처럼 비열하게 죽임을 당한 아킬레우스의 시신은 아이아스와 오디세우스에 의해 구출되었다. 테티스는 그리스인들에게 자기 아들의 갑옷을 살아남은 자들 가운데 가장 그것을 받을 만하다고 판단되는 영웅에게 수여하도록 지시했다. 아이아스와 오디세우스만이 그것을 요구했고, 다른 우두머리들 가운데 선발된 일정한 수의 사람들이 상을 판정하도록 임명되었다. 상은 오디세우스에게 돌아갔으니, 이는 용맹보다 지혜를 앞세운 것이었다. 이에 아이아스는 스스로 목숨을 끊었다. 그의 피가 땅속으로 스며든 자리에서 한 송이 꽃이 솟아났으니, 그것은 히아킨토스라 불리며, 그 잎사귀에는 아이아스 이름의 첫 두 글자인 〈아이(Ai)〉가 새겨져 있었는데, 이는 그리스어로 \"비통\"을 뜻한다. 이렇듯 아이아스는 이 꽃을 탄생시킨 영예를 두고 소년 히아킨토스와 다투는 자가 되었다. 이 사건의 기억을 간직하여 시인들의 히아킨토스를 대신하는 제비고깔의 한 종류가 있으니, 그것은 델피니움 아약키스, 곧 아이아스의 제비고깔이다.\n\n이제 트로이아가 헤라클레스의 화살들의 도움 없이는 함락될 수 없다는 사실이 밝혀졌다. 그 화살들은 필록테테스가 가지고 있었으니, 그는 헤라클레스가 마지막 순간에 함께 있어 주었으며 그의 장작더미에 불을 붙여 준 친구였다. 필록테테스는 트로이아 원정에 그리스 편으로 합류했으나, 우연히 그 독화살들 가운데 하나로 자기 발에 상처를 입었고, 그 상처에서 나는 냄새가 너무도 역겨워 동료들이 그를 렘노스 섬으로 데려가 그곳에 버려두었다. 이제 디오메데스가 그를 설득하여 군대에 다시 합류시키도록 파견되었다. 그는 성공했다. 필록테테스는 마카온에게 상처를 치료받았고, 파리스가 그 치명적인 화살들의 첫 번째 희생자가 되었다. 고통 속에서 파리스는 자신이 번영하던 시절에 잊고 지냈던 한 사람을 떠올렸다. 그녀는 요정 오이노네였으니, 그가 젊은 시절에 아내로 맞았다가 치명적인 미녀 헬레네를 위해 버렸던 여인이었다. 오이노네는 자신이 겪었던 부당함을 기억하고 상처를 치료해 주기를 거부했고, 파리스는 트로이아로 돌아가 죽었다. 오이노네는 곧 후회하고 치료약을 들고 그의 뒤를 서둘러 쫓았으나 너무 늦게 도착했고, 슬픔에 잠겨 스스로 목을 매었다.[22]\n\n트로이아에는 팔라디온이라 불리는 미네르바의 유명한 조각상이 있었다. 그것은 하늘에서 떨어졌다고 전해졌으며, 이 조각상이 도시 안에 남아 있는 한 도시는 함락될 수 없다는 믿음이 있었다. 오디세우스와 디오메데스는 변장을 하고 도시로 들어가 팔라디온을 손에 넣는 데 성공했고, 그것을 그리스 진영으로 가지고 나왔다.\n\n그러나 트로이아는 여전히 버텼고, 그리스인들은 힘으로 그것을 굴복시키는 일에 절망하기 시작했으며, 오디세우스의 조언에 따라 계략에 의지하기로 결정했다. 그들은 포위를 풀고 떠날 채비를 하는 척했고, 함선들의 일부를 빼내어 이웃한 섬 뒤에 숨겨 두었다. 그런 다음 그리스인들은 거대한 목마를 만들었으니, 그들은 그것이 미네르바에게 바치는 화해의 제물이라고 공언했으나, 실은 무장한 병사들로 가득 차 있었다. 남은 그리스인들은 함선으로 가서 마치 최후의 출발이라도 하듯 배를 띄워 떠나갔다. 트로이아인들은 진영이 헐리고 함대가 사라진 것을 보고는 적이 포위를 단념했다고 결론지었다. 성문이 활짝 열렸고, 온 백성이 그토록 오랫동안 금지되었던, 지난날 진영이 있던 자리를 자유로이 거니는 해방감에 기뻐하며 밖으로 쏟아져 나왔다. 그 거대한 목마가 호기심의 주된 대상이 되었다. 모두가 그것이 무엇에 쓰이는 것일까 궁금해했다. 어떤 이들은 그것을 전리품으로 도시 안에 들여놓자고 권했고, 다른 이들은 그것을 두려워했다.\n\n그들이 망설이고 있을 때, 넵투누스의 사제 라오코온이 외쳤다. \"시민들이여, 이 무슨 광기란 말인가? 그대들은 그리스인들의 기만을 충분히 겪고도 아직 그것을 경계할 줄 모른단 말인가? 나로 말하자면, 나는 그리스인들이 선물을 바칠 때조차 그들이 두렵다.\"[23] 이렇게 말하며 그는 목마의 옆구리에 창을 던졌다. 창이 박히자 텅 빈 소리가 신음처럼 울려 퍼졌다. 그때 백성들은 어쩌면 그의 조언을 받아들여 그 치명적인 목마와 그 안에 든 모든 것을 파괴했을지도 모른다. 그러나 바로 그 순간 한 무리의 사람들이 나타나, 포로인 듯하고 그리스인인 듯한 한 사람을 앞으로 끌고 왔다. 공포에 질려 얼이 빠진 그는 우두머리들 앞으로 끌려 나왔고, 그들은 그를 안심시키며, 그에게 던지는 질문에 진실한 대답을 하는 조건으로 목숨을 살려 주겠다고 약속했다. 그는 자신이 그리스인이며 이름은 시논이라고 알렸고, 오디세우스의 악의 탓에 동족들이 떠날 때 뒤에 남겨졌다고 했다. 목마에 관해서 그는, 그것이 미네르바에게 바치는 화해의 제물이며, 도시 안으로 들여가지 못하게 하려는 명백한 목적에서 그토록 거대하게 만들어졌다고 그들에게 말했다. 예언자 칼카스가 그들에게 이르기를, 만약 트로이아인들이 그것을 손에 넣으면 그들이 틀림없이 그리스인들을 이기리라고 했기 때문이라는 것이었다. 이 말은 백성들의 감정의 물결을 돌려놓았고, 그들이 어떻게 하면 이 거대한 목마와 그것에 결부된 길한 조짐을 가장 잘 확보할 수 있을까 궁리하기 시작했을 때, 갑자기 한 가지 기적이 일어나 의심의 여지를 남기지 않았다. 바다 위로 다가오는 두 마리의 거대한 뱀이 나타났던 것이다. 그것들이 뭍에 올라오자 군중은 사방으로 흩어져 달아났다. 뱀들은 라오코온이 두 아들과 함께 서 있는 곳을 향해 곧장 나아갔다. 그것들은 먼저 아이들을 공격하여 그들의 몸을 휘감고 역병 같은 숨결을 그들의 얼굴에 내뿜었다. 아이들을 구하려던 아버지도 곧 붙잡혀 뱀들의 똬리에 휘감겼다. 그는 그것들을 떼어 내려 몸부림쳤으나, 뱀들은 그의 온갖 노력을 압도하고 독을 품은 똬리로 그와 아이들을 졸라 죽였다. 이 사건은 라오코온이 목마를 불경하게 다룬 데 대한 신들의 노여움을 분명히 드러낸 것으로 여겨졌고, 그리하여 사람들은 더 이상 망설이지 않고 목마를 신성한 물건으로 간주하여 마땅한 엄숙함을 갖추어 도시 안으로 들여올 채비를 했다. 이 일은 노래와 승리의 환호 속에서 이루어졌으며, 그날은 축제로 마무리되었다. 밤이 되자 목마의 몸통 안에 갇혀 있던 무장한 병사들이 배신자 시논에 의해 풀려나, 밤의 어둠을 틈타 돌아온 동료들에게 도시의 성문을 열어 주었다. 도시에는 불이 질러졌고, 잔치와 잠에 빠져 있던 백성들은 칼날에 베였으며, 트로이아는 완전히 굴복하고 말았다.\n\n현존하는 가장 유명한 조각상 무리 가운데 하나가 뱀들에게 휘감긴 라오코온과 그의 아이들을 묘사한 것이다. 그 복제품을 보스턴 아테나이움이 소장하고 있으며, 원작은 로마의 바티칸에 있다. 다음 시구는 바이런의 「차일드 해럴드」에서 가져온 것이다.\n\n\"이제 바티칸으로 향하여 가서 보라 고통을 위엄 있게 만드는 라오코온의 괴로움을. 아버지의 사랑과 필멸자의 고뇌가 불멸자의 인내와 어우러지나니, 헛되도다 그 몸부림이여! 휘감아 죄어 오는 힘과 조이며 깊어지는 용의 손아귀에 맞선 노인의 움켜쥠은 헛되도다. 길게 독을 품은 사슬이 살아 있는 고리들을 단단히 죄고, 그 거대한 살무사는 고통 위에 고통을 더하며 숨결 위에 숨결을 막는도다.\"\n\n희극 시인들 또한 이따금 고전적 인유를 빌려 오곤 한다. 다음은 스위프트의 「도시의 소나기 묘사」에서 가져온 것이다.\n\n\"가마에 갇힌 멋쟁이는 안달하며 앉아 있고, 그동안 지붕 위로 빗물받이가 간간이 요란하게 쏟아지며, 이따금 무시무시한 소음과 함께 가죽이 울려 대니, 그는 안에서 떨고 있도다. 그 옛날 트로이아의 가마꾼들이 목마를 메었을 때처럼, 풀려나기를 안달하는 그리스인들을 잉태한 그 목마를, (그 으스대는 그리스인들이란, 오늘날의 사람들이 그러하듯, 가마꾼에게 삯을 치르는 대신 칼로 꿰뚫어 버리는 자들이라). 라오코온이 그 바깥을 창으로 내려치자, 갇혀 있던 용사들이 저마다 두려움에 떨었도다.\"\n\n프리아모스 왕은 자기 왕국의 몰락을 지켜볼 만큼 오래 살았고, 마침내 그리스인들이 도시를 함락한 그 운명의 밤에 죽임을 당했다. 그는 스스로 무장하고 전투원들 사이에 끼어들려 했으나, 늙은 왕비 헤카베의 설득에 따라 그녀와 자기 딸들과 함께 유피테르의 제단에 탄원자로서 피신했다. 그곳에 있을 때, 그의 막내아들 폴리테스가 아킬레우스의 아들 피로스에게 쫓겨 상처를 입은 채 뛰어들었다가 아버지의 발치에서 숨을 거두었다. 이에 프리아모스는 분노에 사로잡혀 쇠약한 손으로 피로스를 향해 창을 던졌으나,[24] 곧 그의 손에 죽임을 당했다.\n\n헤카베 왕비와 그녀의 딸 카산드라는 포로가 되어 그리스로 끌려갔다. 카산드라는 아폴론의 사랑을 받았고, 그는 그녀에게 예언의 능력을 선물로 주었다. 그러나 후에 그녀에게 노하여, 그녀의 예언이 결코 믿어지지 않도록 정함으로써 그 선물을 쓸모없게 만들었다. 또 다른 딸 폴릭세네는 아킬레우스의 사랑을 받았던 까닭에 그 전사의 망령이 그녀를 요구했고, 그리스인들에 의해 그의 무덤 위에서 제물로 바쳐졌다.\n\n# MENELAUS AND HELEN\n\n우리 독자들은 그토록 많은 살육의 아름답되 죄 많은 빌미가 되었던 헬레네의 운명을 알고 싶어 할 것이다. 트로이아가 함락되자 메넬라오스는 아내를 되찾았으니, 그녀는 베누스의 위력에 굴복하여 그를 버리고 다른 사람에게로 갔으되 그를 사랑하기를 그친 적은 없었다. 파리스가 죽은 뒤로 그녀는 여러 차례 은밀히 그리스인들을 도왔으며, 특히 오디세우스와 디오메데스가 팔라디온을 가져가려고 변장하여 도시에 들어왔을 때 그러했다. 그녀는 오디세우스를 알아보았으나 비밀을 지켰을 뿐 아니라, 그들이 그 조각상을 손에 넣도록 도와주기까지 했다. 이렇게 하여 그녀는 남편과 화해했고, 그들은 트로이아의 해안을 떠나 고국으로 향한 첫 사람들 가운데 들었다. 그러나 신들의 노여움을 산 까닭에 그들은 폭풍에 떠밀려 지중해의 이 해안 저 해안으로 표류했고, 키프로스와 페니키아와 이집트를 거쳤다. 이집트에서 그들은 친절한 대접을 받고 풍성한 선물을 받았는데, 헬레네의 몫은 황금 물레와 바퀴 달린 바구니였다. 그 바구니는 왕비의 일감인 양털과 실패를 담는 것이었다.\n\n다이어는 그의 시 「양털」에서 이 일화를 이렇게 언급한다.\n\n\". . . 여전히 많은 이들이 가슴에 고정한 옛 실톳대를 고수하여, 걸으면서 빙빙 도는 물렛가락을 던지나니. . . . . . . . 이것이 옛날, 영광스럽지 않다 할 수 없는 시절에, 실을 잣던 방식이었으니, 그때 이집트의 왕이 그 아리따운 요정에게 황금 실톳대를 주었으되, 너무도 아리따운 헬레네에게라, 무례하지 않은 선물이었도다.\"\n\n밀턴 또한 이집트의 왕비가 헬레네에게 주었던, 네펜테라 불리는 기운을 북돋우는 음료의 유명한 비방을 언급한다.\n\n\"토네의 아내가 이집트에서 제우스에게서 난 헬레네에게 주었던 그 네펜테스도, 이것만큼 기쁨을 불러일으키는 힘은 지니지 못했으니, 이토록 생명에 다정하거나 갈증에 시원하지는 못했도다.\" —「코머스」.\n\n메넬라오스와 헬레네는 마침내 무사히 스파르타에 도착하여 왕의 위엄을 되찾았고, 영화 속에서 살며 다스렸다. 그리고 오디세우스의 아들 텔레마코스가 아버지를 찾아 스파르타에 이르렀을 때, 그는 메넬라오스와 헬레네가 자기들의 딸 헤르미오네와 아킬레우스의 아들 네오프톨레모스의 혼인을 축하하고 있는 것을 보았다.\n\n# AGAMEMNON, ORESTES, AND ELECTRA\n\n그리스인들의 총사령관이자 메넬라오스의 형제이며, 자신의 부당함이 아니라 형제의 부당함을 갚기 위해 그 싸움에 끌려들었던 아가멤논은 그 결말이 그다지 운이 좋지 못했다. 그가 자리를 비운 동안 그의 아내 클리타임네스트라가 그에게 부정을 저질렀고, 그의 귀환이 예상되자 그녀는 정부(情夫) 아이기스토스와 함께 그를 파멸시킬 계략을 꾸몄으며, 그의 귀환을 축하하기 위해 베푼 연회에서 그를 살해했다.\n\n음모자들은 그의 아들 오레스테스도 죽이려 했으니, 그는 아직 우려의 대상이 될 만큼 자라지는 않았으나, 만약 그가 자라도록 내버려 둔다면 위험이 생길 수 있는 소년이었다. 오레스테스의 누이 엘렉트라는 그를 은밀히 그의 외숙부이자 포키스의 왕인 스트로피오스에게로 보내어 동생의 목숨을 구했다. 스트로피오스의 궁전에서 오레스테스는 왕의 아들 필라데스와 함께 자랐고, 그와 더불어 속담으로까지 굳어진 그 뜨거운 우정을 맺었다. 엘렉트라는 사자(使者)들을 통해 아버지의 죽음을 갚아야 할 의무를 동생에게 자주 일깨워 주었고, 다 자란 오레스테스가 델피의 신탁에 물으니, 신탁은 그의 결심을 확고히 해 주었다. 그리하여 그는 변장을 하고 아르고스로 향했으니, 오레스테스의 죽음을 알리러 온 스트로피오스의 사자인 척하며, 죽은 자의 재를 장례용 항아리에 담아 가져왔다. 그는 아버지의 무덤을 찾아가 옛사람들의 의식에 따라 그 위에 제물을 바친 뒤, 누이 엘렉트라에게 자신의 정체를 밝혔고, 머지않아 아이기스토스와 클리타임네스트라를 모두 죽였다.\n\n이 끔찍한 행위, 곧 아들이 어머니를 살해한 일은, 비록 희생자의 죄과와 신들의 명백한 명령으로 완화되기는 했으나, 옛사람들의 가슴속에 오늘날 우리가 느끼는 것과 똑같은 혐오를 불러일으키지 않을 수 없었다. 복수의 여신들인 에우메니데스가 오레스테스를 사로잡아 그를 이 땅 저 땅으로 미쳐 날뛰게 했다. 필라데스는 그의 방랑에 동행하며 그를 보살폈다. 마침내, 신탁에 두 번째로 호소한 끝에, 그는 스키티아의 타우리스로 가서 하늘에서 떨어졌다고 믿어지는 디아나의 조각상을 그곳에서 가져오라는 지시를 받았다. 이에 따라 오레스테스와 필라데스는 타우리스로 갔으니, 그곳의 야만적인 백성들은 손아귀에 들어온 모든 이방인을 여신에게 제물로 바치는 관습이 있었다. 두 친구는 붙잡혀 결박당한 채 제물로 바쳐지기 위해 신전으로 끌려갔다. 그러나 디아나의 여사제는 다름 아닌 오레스테스의 누이 이피게네이아였으니, 우리 독자들이 기억하다시피 그녀는 제물로 바쳐지려던 바로 그 순간에 디아나에게 채여 사라졌던 것이다. 포로들에게서 그들이 누구인지를 알아낸 이피게네이아는 그들에게 자신의 정체를 밝혔고, 세 사람은 여신의 조각상을 가지고 함께 달아나 미케네로 돌아왔다.\n\n그러나 오레스테스는 아직 에리니에스의 복수에서 벗어나지 못했다. 마침내 그는 아테네에서 미네르바에게 피신했다. 여신은 그를 보호해 주었고, 그의 운명을 결정하도록 아레오파고스 법정을 마련했다. 에리니에스는 자신들의 고발을 제기했고, 오레스테스는 델피 신탁의 명령을 자기 변명으로 내세웠다. 법정이 표결을 했는데 표가 똑같이 갈리자, 오레스테스는 미네르바의 명령으로 무죄를 선고받았다.\n\n바이런은 「차일드 해럴드」 제4편에서 오레스테스의 이야기를 언급한다.\n\n\"오, 인간의 부당함에 대하여 결코 저울을 한쪽으로 기울인 적 없는 그대, 위대한 네메시스여! 심연에서 복수의 여신들을 불러내어 오레스테스를 에워싸고 울부짖고 쉭쉭거리게 한 그대여, 그 비정한 응보를 위하여, 그것이 다만 덜 가까운 손에서 비롯되었더라면 정당했을 그 응보를 위하여, 이곳, 그대의 옛 영토에서, 나는 그대를 티끌 속에서 불러내노라!\"\n\n고대 극에서 가장 비통한 장면 가운데 하나는 소포클레스가 묘사한, 오레스테스가 포키스에서 돌아왔을 때 그와 엘렉트라가 만나는 장면이다. 오레스테스는 엘렉트라를 하인들 가운데 하나로 잘못 알고, 또 복수의 시간이 닥칠 때까지 자신의 도착을 비밀로 하고자 하여, 자기 재가 담겨 있다고 여겨지는 항아리를 내놓는다. 엘렉트라는 그가 정말로 죽은 줄로 믿고 그 항아리를 받아 끌어안으며, 다정함과 절망으로 가득 찬 언어로 자신의 슬픔을 쏟아 낸다.\n\n밀턴은 그의 소네트 가운데 하나에서 이렇게 말한다.\n\n\". . . 슬픈 엘렉트라의 시인이 거듭 부른 그 노래는 아테네의 성벽이 황량한 폐허로 무너지지 않도록 구할 힘을 지녔도다.\"\n\n이는, 언젠가 아테네 도시가 스파르타 적들의 처분에 맡겨져 그것을 파괴하자는 제안이 나왔을 때, 누군가가 우연히 에우리피데스의 합창 한 대목을 인용하자 그 생각이 철회되었다는 이야기를 언급한 것이다.\n\n트로이아 도시에 관한 사실들은 아직도 역사에 알려져 있지 않다. 골동품 연구가들은 오랫동안 실제 도시와 그 통치자들에 관한 기록을 찾아왔다. 가장 흥미로운 발굴은 1890년경 독일 학자 하인리히 슐리만이 수행한 것이었는데, 그는 트로이아의 전통적인 위치인 히사를리크의 언덕에서 자신이 그 고대의 수도를 드러냈다고 믿었다. 슐리만은 서너 개의 정착지의 폐허 아래까지 파 내려갔으니, 그 하나하나가 더 이른 시대의 문명을 드러냈고, 마침내 \"프리아모스의 보물\"이라 일컬어지는 몇몇 왕가의 보석과 다른 유물들을 발견했다. 이 발견들의 역사적 가치에 관해서는 학자들의 의견이 결코 일치하지 않는다.\n\n————\n\n# CHAPTER XXIX\n\n이제 우리의 관심을 끄는 것은 낭만적 서사시 『오디세이아』다. 이 작품은 오디세우스[그리스어로는 오디세우스, 라틴어로는 율리시스]가 트로이아에서 자기 왕국 이타카로 돌아오는 동안 겪은 방랑을 이야기한다.\n\n트로이아를 떠난 함선들은 처음에 키코네스족의 도시 이스마로스에 닿았는데, 그곳에서 주민들과 벌인 작은 충돌로 오디세우스는 배마다 여섯 명씩의 부하를 잃었다. 그곳을 떠나 항해하던 중 폭풍을 만나 아흐레 동안 바다 위로 떠밀려 다니다가 마침내 로토스를 먹는 사람들의 나라에 이르렀다. 이곳에서 물을 보충한 뒤, 오디세우스는 주민들이 누구인지 알아보려고 부하 세 명을 보냈다. 로토스를 먹는 사람들 사이로 간 이 부하들은 그들에게 친절히 대접받았고, 그들 자신의 양식인 로토스 열매를 먹어 보라고 받았다. 이 음식의 효과는 그것을 먹은 사람은 누구든 고향 생각을 모조리 잃어버리고 그 나라에 남고 싶어 하게 만드는 것이었다. 오디세우스가 이 부하들을 끌고 나올 수 있었던 것은 오로지 완력 덕분이었고, 심지어 그는 그들을 배의 노 젓는 자리 아래에 묶어 두기까지 해야 했다.[25]\n\n다음으로 그들은 키클로페스의 나라에 도착했다. 키클로페스는 어떤 섬에 살던 거인들로, 그 섬의 유일한 주인이었다. 그 이름은 〈둥근 눈〉을 뜻하는데, 이 거인들에게는 눈이 하나뿐이고 그것이 이마 한가운데에 박혀 있었기 때문에 그렇게 불렸다. 그들은 동굴에 살며 섬의 야생 산물과 자기 가축이 내어 주는 것을 먹고 살았으니, 목동이었기 때문이다. 오디세우스는 함선의 대부분을 정박지에 닻을 내린 채 두고, 배 한 척만으로 키클로페스의 섬으로 가서 식량을 구할 만한 곳을 살폈다. 그는 동료들과 함께 선물로 쓸 포도주 한 단지를 들고 상륙했고, 어느 커다란 동굴에 이르러 그 안으로 들어갔다. 안에 아무도 없는 것을 보고 그 내용물을 살펴보았다. 동굴에는 가축 중에서도 가장 좋은 것들이 가득했고, 치즈가 잔뜩 쌓여 있었으며, 우유를 담은 통과 그릇이 있었고, 우리 안에는 새끼 양과 새끼 염소들이 있었는데 모두 잘 정돈되어 있었다. 곧 동굴의 주인 폴리페모스가 거대한 장작 묶음을 지고 와서, 그것을 동굴 입구 앞에 내던졌다. 그러고는 젖을 짜려고 양과 염소를 동굴 안으로 몰아넣고, 자기도 들어와서는 황소 스무 마리로도 끌지 못할 만한 어마어마한 바위를 굴려 동굴 입구를 막았다. 다음으로 그는 자리에 앉아 암양들의 젖을 짜서, 일부는 치즈로 만들고 나머지는 평소의 음료로 따로 두었다. 그런 다음 그 커다란 눈을 굴리다가 낯선 자들을 발견하고는, 그들에게 으르렁대며 누구이며 어디서 왔는지 따져 물었다. 오디세우스는 더없이 겸손하게 대답하여, 자신들은 그리스인이며 최근 트로이아 정복으로 큰 영광을 얻은 그 대원정에서 돌아오는 길이고, 지금 고향으로 향하는 중이라고 말했다. 그리고 신들의 이름으로 그의 환대를 간청하며 말을 맺었다. 폴리페모스는 대답할 가치도 없다는 듯 아무 말도 하지 않고, 손을 뻗어 그리스인 둘을 붙잡아 동굴 벽에 메다꽂아 그 머리를 으깨 버렸다. 그는 그들을 아주 맛있게 먹어 치우기 시작했고, 든든히 식사를 마치고는 바닥에 몸을 뻗고 잠을 잤다. 오디세우스는 이 기회를 잡아 잠든 그에게 칼을 찔러 넣고 싶은 충동을 느꼈지만, 그렇게 하면 모두가 확실한 파멸에 노출될 뿐이라는 것을 떠올렸다. 거인이 입구를 막아 둔 바위는 그들의 힘으로는 도저히 치울 수 없는 것이어서, 그렇게 되면 그들은 희망 없는 갇힌 처지가 될 것이기 때문이었다. 이튿날 아침 거인은 그리스인 두 명을 더 붙잡아, 그 동료들에게 했던 것과 똑같은 방식으로 처치하고는 한 조각도 남기지 않을 때까지 그 살을 먹어 치웠다. 그런 다음 입구에서 바위를 치우고 가축들을 몰아 밖으로 나가면서, 자기 뒤로 그 장벽을 다시 조심스럽게 제자리에 돌려놓았다. 그가 떠난 뒤 오디세우스는 살해당한 친구들의 원수를 어떻게 갚을지, 그리고 살아남은 동료들과 함께 어떻게 탈출할지 궁리했다. 그는 부하들을 시켜, 키클롭스가 지팡이로 쓰려고 잘라 둔, 동굴 안에서 발견한 묵직한 나무 막대를 준비하게 했다. 그들은 그 끝을 뾰족하게 깎아 불에 그슬려 단단하게 만든 뒤, 동굴 바닥의 짚 아래에 숨겨 두었다. 그런 다음 가장 대담한 네 명이 뽑혔고, 오디세우스가 다섯 번째로 그들과 함께했다. 저녁이 되자 키클롭스가 집으로 돌아와 평소처럼 돌을 굴려 치우고 가축을 안으로 몰아넣었다. 젖을 짜고 전과 같이 일을 처리한 뒤, 그는 오디세우스의 동료 두 명을 더 붙잡아 그 머리를 으깨고, 다른 자들에게 했던 것처럼 그들을 저녁 끼니로 삼았다. 식사를 마친 뒤 오디세우스가 그에게 다가가 포도주 한 그릇을 건네며 말했다. \"키클롭스여, 이것은 포도주요. 사람 고기 식사 뒤에 한번 맛보고 마셔 보시오.\" 그는 그것을 받아 마시고는 크게 기뻐하며 더 달라고 청했다. 오디세우스가 한 번 더 따라 주자 거인은 무척 흡족해하며, 호의로서 그를 일행 중 마지막으로 잡아먹겠다고 약속했다. 거인이 그의 이름을 묻자, 오디세우스가 대답했다. \"내 이름은 아무도아니라요.\"\n\n저녁 식사 뒤 거인은 쉬려고 누웠고, 곧 깊이 잠들었다. 그러자 오디세우스는 뽑힌 네 친구와 함께 막대 끝을 불 속에 밀어 넣어 온통 타는 숯이 될 때까지 달구었고, 그것을 거인의 하나뿐인 눈 바로 위에 정확히 겨눈 다음, 목수가 송곳을 돌리듯 빙빙 돌려 가며 그 눈구멍 속에 깊이 박아 넣었다. 울부짖는 괴물의 비명이 동굴을 가득 채웠고, 오디세우스와 그 조력자들은 재빨리 그의 손길을 피해 동굴 안에 몸을 숨겼다. 그는 고함을 지르며, 멀고 가까운 주위 동굴에 사는 모든 키클로페스를 큰 소리로 불렀다. 그들은 그의 외침을 듣고 굴 주위로 몰려와, 그가 무슨 끔찍한 상처를 입었기에 그토록 경보를 울려 자기들의 잠을 깨우느냐고 물었다. 그가 대답했다. \"오 친구들이여, 나는 죽어 가는데, 아무도아니라가 그 일격을 가했소.\" 그들이 대답했다. \"아무도 그대를 해치지 않았다면 그것은 제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]의 일격이니, 그대는 그것을 견뎌야 하오.\" 그렇게 말하고 그들은 신음하는 그를 남겨 둔 채 떠나갔다.\n\n이튿날 아침 키클롭스는 가축을 풀밭으로 내보내려고 돌을 굴려 치웠으나, 오디세우스와 그 부하들이 가축에 섞여 도망치지 못하도록 동굴 입구에 자리를 잡고 나가는 짐승들을 모두 더듬어 보았다. 그러나 오디세우스는 부하들을 시켜, 동굴 바닥에서 찾아낸 버들가지로 가축의 숫양들을 셋씩 나란히 묶게 해 두었다. 셋 중 가운데 숫양에 그리스인 한 명이 매달려, 양옆의 바깥쪽 두 숫양에 의해 가려지게 했다. 그들이 지나갈 때 거인은 짐승들의 등과 옆구리를 더듬었으나, 그 배를 살펴볼 생각은 전혀 하지 못했다. 그리하여 부하들은 모두 무사히 빠져나갔고, 오디세우스 자신은 맨 마지막으로 지나가는 짐승에 매달려 있었다. 동굴에서 몇 걸음 벗어나자 오디세우스와 그 친구들은 숫양에서 풀려나, 가축의 상당 부분을 해안의 배까지 몰고 갔다. 그들은 서둘러 가축을 배에 실은 뒤 해안에서 배를 밀어냈고, 안전한 거리에 이르자 오디세우스가 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"키클롭스여, 신들께서 그대의 잔악한 행위에 마땅한 보답을 내리셨다. 그대가 수치스럽게 시력을 잃은 것은 바로 오디세우스 덕분임을 알아라.\" 이 말을 들은 키클롭스는 산허리에서 튀어나온 바위를 붙잡아 그 자리에서 뜯어내더니, 그것을 높이 공중으로 들어 올린 다음 온 힘을 다해 목소리가 난 방향으로 던졌다. 거대한 덩어리가 떨어지면서 배의 고물을 아슬아슬하게 비껴갔다. 거대한 바위가 풍덩 떨어지자 바다가 솟구쳐 배를 뭍 쪽으로 밀어붙였고, 배는 가까스로 파도에 삼켜지는 것을 면했다. 그들이 천신만고 끝에 해안에서 멀어지자 오디세우스가 다시 거인에게 소리치려 했으나, 친구들이 그러지 말라고 간청했다. 그러나 그는 그들이 그 투척물을 피해 달아났다는 것을 거인에게 알리지 않고는 견딜 수 없었으니, 다만 전보다 더 안전한 거리에 이를 때까지 기다렸다. 거인은 저주로 그들에게 응답했지만, 오디세우스와 그 친구들은 힘차게 노를 저어 곧 동료들에게 되돌아갔다.\n\n다음으로 오디세우스는 아이올로스의 섬에 이르렀다. 이 왕에게 제우스는 바람을 다스리는 일을 맡겨, 제 뜻대로 바람을 내보내거나 붙잡아 두게 했다. 그는 오디세우스를 후하게 대접했고, 그가 떠날 때 해롭고 위험할 만한 바람들을 가죽 자루에 담아 은실로 묶어 주면서, 순풍에게는 그 배들을 그들의 고향 쪽으로 불어 주라고 명령했다. 아흐레 동안 그들은 바람을 타고 달렸고, 그 내내 오디세우스는 잠도 자지 않고 키를 잡고 서 있었다. 마침내 완전히 지친 그가 누워 잠들었다. 그가 잠든 사이 선원들은 그 신비로운 자루에 대해 함께 수군대다가, 그것이 환대해 준 왕 아이올로스가 자기들의 지휘관에게 준 보물을 담고 있는 것이 틀림없다고 결론지었다. 그 일부를 자기들 몫으로 챙기고 싶은 유혹에 빠진 그들이 실을 풀자, 곧바로 바람들이 쏟아져 나왔다. 배들은 항로에서 멀리 밀려나, 방금 떠나온 바로 그 섬으로 도로 떠밀려 갔다. 아이올로스는 그들의 어리석음에 너무도 분개하여 더 이상 도와주기를 거부했고, 그들은 다시 한번 노를 저어 힘겹게 항로를 헤쳐 나가야 했다.\n\n다음 모험은 야만적인 라이스트리고네스족과의 일이었다. 함선들은 모두, 완전히 뭍으로 둘러싸여 안전해 보이는 작은 만의 모습에 이끌려 항구 안으로 들어갔다. 오직 오디세우스만이 자기 배를 그 바깥에 매어 두었다. 라이스트리고네스족은 배들이 완전히 자기들의 손아귀에 들어왔음을 알자마자 그것들을 공격하여, 거대한 돌을 던져 배를 부수고 뒤집었으며, 물속에서 허우적대는 선원들을 창으로 처치했다. 바깥에 남아 있던 오디세우스 자신의 배를 제외하고는 배와 선원이 모두 파멸했고, 도망치는 것 말고는 안전을 찾을 수 없게 된 그는 부하들에게 힘차게 노를 저으라고 독려했고, 그들은 가까스로 빠져나갔다.\n\n살해당한 동료들에 대한 슬픔에 자신들이 탈출했다는 기쁨이 뒤섞인 채, 그들은 길을 재촉하여 마침내 아이아이아 섬에 이르렀다. 그곳에는 태양의 딸 키르케가 살고 있었다. 이곳에 상륙한 오디세우스는 언덕에 올라 사방을 둘러보았으나, 섬 한가운데의 한 지점을 제외하고는 사람이 사는 흔적이 보이지 않았다. 그 지점에는 나무들에 둘러싸인 궁전이 보였다. 그는 어떤 환대를 기대할 수 있을지 알아보려고, 에우릴로코스의 지휘 아래 선원의 절반을 앞서 보냈다. 그들이 궁전에 다가가자 사방을 사자와 호랑이와 늑대가 에워쌌는데, 사납지 않고 키르케의 기술로 길들여져 있었다. 그녀가 강력한 마법사였기 때문이다. 이 짐승들은 모두 한때 사람이었으나 키르케의 마법으로 짐승의 모습으로 변한 것이었다. 안에서는 부드러운 음악 소리와 달콤한 여인의 노랫소리가 들려왔다. 에우릴로코스가 큰 소리로 부르자 여신이 나와 그들을 안으로 청했다. 위험을 의심한 에우릴로코스만 빼고는 모두가 기꺼이 들어갔다. 여신은 손님들을 자리로 안내하여 포도주와 그 밖의 진미를 대접하게 했다. 그들이 든든히 먹고 마시자, 그녀는 지팡이로 그들을 하나씩 건드렸고, 그들은 즉시 〈머리와 몸과 목소리와 뻣뻣한 털〉까지 돼지로 변했으나, 지성만은 전과 같았다. 그녀는 그들을 우리에 가두고 도토리와 그 밖에 돼지가 좋아하는 것들을 먹였다.\n\n에우릴로코스는 배로 서둘러 돌아가 그 이야기를 전했다. 그러자 오디세우스는 직접 가서 어떻게든 동료들을 구해 낼 수 있을지 시도해 보기로 결심했다. 홀로 성큼성큼 나아가던 그는 한 젊은이를 만났는데, 그 젊은이는 마치 그의 모험을 다 알고 있는 듯 친근하게 말을 걸어왔다. 그는 자신을 헤르메스[로마의 〈메르쿠리우스〉]라고 밝히고, 오디세우스에게 키르케의 기술과 그녀에게 다가가는 일의 위험을 일러 주었다. 오디세우스가 그 시도를 단념하려 하지 않자, 헤르메스는 그에게 마법에 맞서는 놀라운 힘을 지닌 몰뤼라는 식물의 가지를 건네주고, 어떻게 행동해야 할지 가르쳐 주었다. 오디세우스가 나아가 궁전에 이르자 키르케는 그를 정중히 맞이하여 그의 동료들에게 했던 대로 대접했고, 그가 먹고 마신 뒤에 지팡이로 그를 건드리며 말했다. \"썩 물러가, 우리를 찾아가 네 친구들과 함께 뒹굴어라.\" 그러나 그는 복종하는 대신, 칼을 뽑아 얼굴에 분노를 띤 채 그녀에게 달려들었다. 그녀는 무릎을 꿇고 자비를 빌었다. 그는 그녀가 동료들을 풀어 주고 자신이나 그들에게 더 이상 해를 끼치지 않겠다는 엄숙한 맹세를 하도록 명령했고, 그녀는 그것을 따라 외우면서 동시에, 그들을 후히 대접한 뒤에 모두 무사히 보내 주겠다고 약속했다. 그녀는 약속을 어김없이 지켰다. 부하들은 본래의 모습을 되찾았고, 나머지 선원들도 해안에서 불려 왔으며, 모두가 날마다 성대히 대접받았다. 그리하여 오디세우스는 고국을 잊은 듯, 안일과 쾌락의 영광 없는 삶에 안주해 버린 것 같았다.\n\n마침내 동료들이 그에게 더 고귀한 마음을 일깨워 주었고, 그는 그들의 충고를 고맙게 받아들였다. 키르케는 그들의 출발을 도왔고, 세이레네스의 해안을 무사히 지나는 법을 가르쳐 주었다. 세이레네스는 바다의 님프들로, 그 노래로 듣는 자를 모두 흘리는 힘을 지니고 있어서, 불행한 뱃사람들이 거역할 수 없는 충동에 이끌려 스스로 바다에 몸을 던져 파멸하게 만들었다. 키르케는 오디세우스에게, 선원들이 그 노랫가락을 듣지 못하도록 그들의 귀를 밀랍으로 막고, 자신은 돛대에 묶이게 하되, 자기가 무슨 말이나 행동을 하더라도 세이레네스의 섬을 지날 때까지는 결코 풀어 주지 말라고 부하들에게 엄히 일러 두라고 지시했다. 오디세우스는 이 지시를 따랐다. 그는 부하들의 귀를 밀랍으로 막고, 그들이 자기를 밧줄로 돛대에 단단히 묶도록 했다. 그들이 세이레네스의 섬에 다가가자 바다는 잔잔했고, 물 위로 너무도 황홀하고 매혹적인 음악 가락이 들려와 오디세우스는 풀려나려 몸부림치며 부하들에게 소리와 손짓으로 풀어 달라고 애원했다. 그러나 그들은 그가 미리 내린 명령에 복종하여 앞으로 달려들어 그를 더욱 단단히 묶었다. 그들은 항로를 유지했고, 음악은 점점 희미해지다가 더 이상 들리지 않게 되었으며, 그제야 오디세우스는 기쁜 마음으로 동료들에게 귀를 막은 것을 떼라는 신호를 보냈고, 그들은 그를 결박에서 풀어 주었다.\n\n근대 시인 키츠의 상상력은 키르케의 희생자들이 변신한 뒤 그들의 머릿속을 스쳐 간 생각을 우리에게 드러내 주었다. 그는 자신의 작품 「엔디미온」에서 그들 중 하나, 곧 코끼리의 모습을 한 어느 왕이 인간의 언어로 마녀에게 이렇게 호소하는 모습을 그렸다.\n\n\"나는 내 행복한 왕관을 되돌려 달라 간청하지 않노라. 나는 평원의 내 군대를 간청하지 않노라. 나는 외로이 홀로된 내 아내를 간청하지 않노라. 나는 붉은 생명의 핏방울을 간청하지 않노라. 내 어여쁜 아이들, 사랑스러운 딸들과 아들들을. 나는 그들을 잊으리라. 이 기쁨들을 흘려보내리라. 그토록 천국 같은 것은 아무것도 청하지 않으리. 너무도—너무도 높으니. 오직 나는, 가장 아름다운 은혜로서, 죽기를 기도하노라. 이 거추장스러운 육신에서 벗어나기를, 이 조잡하고 혐오스럽고 더러운 그물에서 벗어나, 그저 차갑고 황량한 대기에 내맡겨지기를. 자비를 베푸소서, 여신이여! 키르케여, 내 기도를 헤아리소서!\"\n\n# SCYLLA AND CHARYBDIS\n\n오디세우스는 키르케에게서 두 괴물 스킬라와 카립디스에 대한 경고를 들은 바 있었다. 우리는 글라우코스의 이야기에서 이미 스킬라를 만났고, 그녀가 한때 아름다운 처녀였으나 키르케에 의해 뱀 같은 괴물로 변했다는 것을 기억한다. 그녀는 절벽 높은 곳의 동굴에 살면서, 그곳에서 자신의 긴 목들을(머리가 여섯이었으므로) 쑥 내밀어, 닿는 거리 안으로 지나가는 배마다 그 입 하나하나로 선원을 한 명씩 낚아채곤 했다. 또 다른 공포인 카립디스는 거의 수면과 같은 높이에 있는 소용돌이였다. 하루에 세 번씩 물이 무시무시한 심연 속으로 휘몰려 들어갔다가, 세 번씩 토해 내어졌다. 조수가 휘몰려 들어올 때 그 소용돌이 가까이 다가오는 배는 무엇이든 어김없이 삼켜지고 말았으니, 포세이돈[로마의 〈넵투누스〉] 자신도 그것을 구할 수 없었다.\n\n이 무시무시한 괴물들의 소굴에 다가가면서 오디세우스는 그들을 발견하려고 면밀히 경계했다. 카립디스가 물을 삼킬 때 나는 물소리는 멀리서부터 경고를 주었으나, 스킬라는 어디서도 분간할 수 없었다. 오디세우스와 그 부하들이 불안한 눈으로 그 무서운 소용돌이를 지켜보는 사이, 그들은 스킬라의 공격에 대해서는 그만큼 경계하지 못했고, 괴물이 그 뱀 같은 머리들을 쏜살같이 내밀어 부하 여섯을 붙잡아, 비명을 지르는 그들을 자기 굴로 끌고 가 버렸다. 그것은 오디세우스가 그때까지 본 가장 슬픈 광경이었다. 친구들이 그렇게 제물로 바쳐지는 것을 지켜보며 그들의 비명을 들으면서도 아무런 도움도 줄 수 없었기 때문이다.\n\n키르케는 그에게 또 다른 위험을 경고해 두었다. 스킬라와 카립디스를 지나면 그가 다음으로 닿게 될 땅은 트리나키아라는 섬인데, 그곳에는 태양 히페리온의 소 떼가 방목되어 그의 딸 람페티에와 파에투사가 돌보고 있었다. 항해자들의 필요가 무엇이든 간에 이 가축들을 침범해서는 안 되었다. 만약 이 명령을 어기면 어김없이 범한 자들에게 파멸이 닥칠 것이었다.\n\n오디세우스는 멈추지 않고 태양의 섬을 지나치고 싶었으나, 동료들이 닻을 내려 해안에서 하룻밤을 보내며 얻을 휴식과 원기 회복을 너무도 간절히 청하는 바람에 오디세우스가 양보했다. 그러나 그는 그들에게, 신성한 가축과 소 떼 가운데 어느 한 마리도 건드리지 않고, 키르케가 배에 실어 준 식량 중에 아직 남은 것으로 만족하겠다는 맹세를 시켰다. 이 식량이 남아 있는 동안에는 사람들이 맹세를 지켰으나, 역풍이 그들을 한 달이나 섬에 붙잡아 두었고, 비축한 식량을 모두 먹어 치운 뒤에는 잡을 수 있는 새와 물고기에 의존할 수밖에 없었다. 기근이 그들을 압박했고, 마침내 어느 날 오디세우스가 없는 사이에 그들은 소 몇 마리를 잡아, 그 일부를 노여워하는 신들에게 바침으로써 그 행위를 보상하려 했으나 헛된 일이었다. 해안으로 돌아온 오디세우스는 그들이 저지른 일을 알아채고 공포에 사로잡혔으며, 뒤따른 불길한 징조 때문에 더욱 그러했다. 가죽들이 땅 위를 기어 다녔고, 꼬챙이에 꿰여 구워지던 고깃점들이 음매 하고 울었다.\n\n바람이 순해지자 그들은 섬을 떠나 항해했다. 멀리 가지 못했을 때 날씨가 바뀌어 천둥과 번개를 동반한 폭풍이 일었다. 번개 한 줄기가 돛대를 산산조각 냈고, 쓰러진 돛대가 키잡이를 죽였다. 마침내 배 자체가 부서져 버렸다. 용골과 돛대가 나란히 떠다니자 오디세우스는 그것으로 뗏목을 만들어 매달렸고, 바람이 바뀌면서 파도가 그를 칼립소의 섬으로 실어 갔다. 나머지 선원들은 모두 죽고 말았다.\n\n방금 우리가 살펴본 주제들에 대한 다음 인유는 밀턴의 「코머스」 252행에서 가져온 것이다.\n\n\". . . 나는 자주 들었노라 내 어머니 키르케와 세이레네스 셋이, 꽃무늬 치마를 두른 나이아데스들 사이에서, 저들의 강력한 약초와 해로운 독초를 캐며 노래할 때면, 갇힌 영혼을 사로잡아 엘리시온에 감싸 두는 것을. 스킬라는 울었고, 짖어 대는 제 파도를 꾸짖어 귀 기울이게 했으며, 무서운 카립디스는 부드러운 갈채를 중얼거렸노라.\"\n\n스킬라와 카립디스는 사람의 진로를 위협하는 양쪽의 위험을 가리키는 속담이 되었다. 「속담 표현」을 보라.\n\n칼립소는 바다의 님프였는데, 이 이름은 신분이 낮으면서도 신들의 여러 속성을 함께 지닌, 수많은 여성 신격의 한 부류를 가리킨다. 칼립소는 오디세우스를 후히 맞이하여 성대히 대접했고, 그에게 반하여 그에게 불사를 내려 주고 영원히 붙잡아 두기를 바랐다. 그러나 그는 자기 나라와 아내와 아들에게로 돌아가겠다는 결심을 굽히지 않았다. 마침내 칼립소는 그를 보내 주라는 제우스의 명령을 받았다. 헤르메스가 그 전갈을 그녀에게 가져왔고, 그녀가 자기 동굴에 있는 것을 발견했다. 그 동굴을 호메로스는 이렇게 묘사한다.\n\n\"정원의 포도 덩굴이 사방에 무성하게 그 너른 동굴을 뒤덮어, 송이송이 풍성하게 늘어졌고, 더없이 맑은 샘물 넷이 구불구불한 길을 나란히 따라 흐르며 온 사방으로 퍼져 나갔고, 어디에나 더없이 부드러운 초록빛 풀밭이 펼쳐져 제비꽃으로 보랏빛을 띠었으니, 그것은 하늘의 신마저도 놀라움과 기쁨으로 채울 만한 광경이었노라.\"\n\n칼립소는 몹시 마지못해하면서 제우스의 명령에 따르기 시작했다. 그녀는 오디세우스에게 뗏목을 만들 수단을 마련해 주고, 식량을 넉넉히 실어 주었으며, 순풍을 보내 주었다. 그는 여러 날 동안 순조롭게 항로를 나아갔으나, 마침내 육지가 보일 무렵 폭풍이 일어 그의 돛대를 부러뜨리고 뗏목을 산산이 찢어 놓을 듯 위협했다. 이 위기에 한 자비로운 바다의 님프가 그를 보고, 가마우지의 모습으로 뗏목에 내려앉아 그에게 띠를 건네주며, 그것을 가슴 아래에 두르라고, 그리고 만약 파도에 몸을 맡길 수밖에 없게 되더라도 그것이 그를 떠받쳐 헤엄쳐 육지에 닿을 수 있게 해 줄 것이라고 일러 주었다.\n\n페늘롱은 그의 소설 「텔레마코스」에서 아버지를 찾아 나선 오디세우스의 아들의 모험을 우리에게 들려주었다. 아버지의 발자취를 따라 그가 다다른 여러 곳 가운데 칼립소의 섬이 있었는데, 앞서와 마찬가지로 여신은 온갖 기술을 동원해 그를 곁에 붙잡아 두려 했고, 자신의 불사를 그와 나누어 갖겠다고 제안했다. 그러나 멘토르의 모습으로 그를 동행하며 그의 모든 행동을 인도하던 아테나[로마의 〈미네르바〉]가 그에게 그녀의 유혹을 물리치게 했고, 다른 탈출의 방도를 찾을 수 없게 되자 두 친구는 절벽에서 바다로 뛰어들어, 해안에서 바람이 잔잔해 멈춰 있던 배까지 헤엄쳐 갔다. 바이런은 텔레마코스와 멘토르의 이 도약을 다음 연에서 인유한다.\n\n\"그러나 칼립소의 섬들을 침묵 속에 지나치지 말라, 한가운데 바다의 자매처럼 깃든 그 거처들을. 그곳에는 지친 자들을 위해 아직도 항구가 미소 짓나니, 비록 아름다운 여신은 오래전에 울기를 그쳤고, 필멸의 신부를 감히 더 좋아한 그를 위해 제 절벽 위에서 헛된 망보기를 그쳤을지라도. 이곳에서 그의 아들도 그 무서운 도약을 시도했으니, 준엄한 멘토르가 높은 곳에서 저 아래 물결로 재촉했노라. 그리하여 둘을 다 잃은 님프 여왕은 갑절로 탄식했노라.\"\n\n————\n\n오디세우스는 뗏목의 목재가 그나마 붙어 있는 동안에는 거기에 매달려 있었으나, 더 이상 그것이 자신을 떠받쳐 주지 못하게 되자 허리띠를 몸에 동여매고는 헤엄을 쳤다. 아테나[로마의 〈미네르바〉]가 그의 앞에 놓인 큰 물결을 잔잔하게 만들고, 파도를 해안 쪽으로 밀어 보내는 바람을 보내 주었다. 부서지는 파도가 바위에 높이 부딪치며 접근을 가로막는 듯했다. 그러나 마침내 그는 잔잔한 물이 흐르는 어느 온화한 시내의 어귀를 발견해 그곳에 상륙했는데, 고생에 지쳐 숨도 제대로 쉬지 못하고 말 한마디 내뱉지 못한 채 거의 죽은 것이나 다름없었다. 얼마 후 기운을 회복한 그는 기뻐하며 땅에 입을 맞추었으나, 어떤 길로 나아가야 할지 막막하기만 했다. 가까운 곳에 숲이 보이기에 그는 그쪽으로 발걸음을 옮겼다. 그곳에서 그는 햇빛과 빗물을 함께 막아 줄 만큼 가지가 서로 얽혀 가려진 은신처를 찾아내고, 나뭇잎을 한 무더기 모아 잠자리를 만든 다음, 그 위에 몸을 뻗고 누워 나뭇잎을 자신의 몸 위로 끌어 덮고는 잠이 들었다.\n\n그가 떠밀려 온 땅은 스케리아, 곧 파이아케스족의 나라였다. 이 사람들은 본래 키클로페스 근처에 살고 있었으나, 그 야만적인 종족에게 시달리다 못해 그들의 왕 나우시토오스의 인솔 아래 스케리아 섬으로 이주해 온 것이었다. 시인이 우리에게 전하는 바에 따르면, 그들은 신들과 가까운 종족이어서, 그들이 제물을 바칠 때면 신들이 모습을 분명히 드러내고 그들 사이에서 함께 잔치를 벌였으며, 홀로 길을 가는 나그네와 마주칠 때에도 자신들의 모습을 숨기지 않았다고 한다. 그들은 풍부한 재물을 지니고 있었고, 전쟁의 위협에 시달리는 일 없이 그것을 누리며 살았다. 이익을 좇는 인간들로부터 멀리 떨어져 살았던 까닭에 그 어떤 적도 그들의 해안에 다가오는 일이 없었고, 따라서 그들은 활과 화살통조차 쓸 필요가 없었다. 그들의 주된 일거리는 항해였다. 새와 같은 속도로 나아가는 그들의 배는 지성을 갖추고 있었으니, 모든 항구를 알았고 키잡이도 필요로 하지 않았다. 나우시토오스의 아들 알키노오스가 이제 그들의 왕이었으며, 그는 슬기롭고 공정한 군주로서 백성에게 사랑을 받고 있었다.\n\n그런데 오디세우스가 파이아케스족의 섬에 떠밀려 와 나뭇잎으로 만든 잠자리에 누워 잠들어 있던 바로 그날 밤, 왕의 딸 나우시카가 아테나가 보낸 꿈을 꾸었다. 그 꿈은 그녀의 혼인날이 머지않았으니 그날을 대비해 집안의 옷가지를 모두 빨아 두는 것이야말로 사려 깊은 준비가 될 것이라고 일깨워 주었다. 이는 결코 사소한 일이 아니었으니, 샘이 다소 멀리 떨어져 있어 옷가지를 그곳까지 운반해야 했기 때문이다. 잠에서 깨어난 공주는 자기 마음에 있는 바를 알리고자 서둘러 부모에게로 갔는데, 혼인날에 대해서는 언급하지 않고 그에 못지않게 그럴듯한 다른 이유들을 내세웠다. 그녀의 아버지는 흔쾌히 동의하며 그 일에 쓸 수레를 마련하라고 마부들에게 명했다. 옷가지를 수레에 실었고, 왕비인 어머니는 그 수레에 음식과 포도주도 넉넉히 실어 주었다. 공주는 자리에 앉아 채찍을 휘둘렀고, 시중드는 처녀들이 걸어서 그 뒤를 따랐다. 강가에 다다른 그들은 노새들을 풀어 풀을 뜯게 하고, 수레의 짐을 내려 옷가지를 물가로 가져갔으며, 명랑하고 민첩하게 일하여 곧 그 노고를 마쳤다. 그런 다음 옷을 해안에 펼쳐 말리고 자신들도 목욕을 한 뒤 둘러앉아 식사를 즐겼다. 식사가 끝나자 그들은 일어나 공놀이를 하며 즐겼는데, 그들이 노는 동안 공주가 노래를 불러 주었다. 그러나 그들이 옷을 다시 개어 마을로 돌아갈 채비를 하던 차에, 아테나가 공주가 던진 공을 물속에 빠지게 만들었고, 그러자 모두가 비명을 질렀으며 오디세우스는 그 소리에 잠에서 깨어났다.\n\n이제 우리는 오디세우스의 모습을 떠올려 보아야 한다. 그는 난파를 당한 뱃사람으로, 불과 몇 시간 전에야 파도에서 벗어났을 뿐 옷이라고는 전혀 걸치지 못한 처지였는데, 잠에서 깨어나 보니 자신과 한 무리의 젊은 처녀들 사이에 고작 덤불 몇 그루만이 가로놓여 있을 따름이었다. 더구나 그는 그녀들의 거동과 차림새로 보아 단순한 시골 처녀들이 아니라 더 높은 신분의 여인들임을 알아차렸다. 절실히 도움이 필요한 처지였으나, 알몸인 그가 어찌 감히 자신의 모습을 드러내고 자신의 곤궁함을 알릴 수 있었겠는가? 이는 분명 그의 수호 여신인 아테나가 개입할 만한 상황이었고, 그녀는 위기의 순간에 결코 그를 저버린 적이 없었다. 그는 나무에서 잎이 무성한 가지 하나를 꺾어 자기 앞을 가리고는 덤불에서 걸어 나왔다. 그를 본 처녀들은 사방으로 달아났으나, 나우시카만은 예외였으니, 아테나가 바로 그녀를 도와 용기와 분별력을 불어넣어 주었기 때문이다. 오디세우스는 정중하게 멀찍이 떨어져 서서 자신의 가련한 처지를 이야기하고, 그 아름다운 여인에게(그녀가 왕비인지 여신인지 자신은 알지 못하겠노라고 그는 고백했다) 음식과 옷을 베풀어 달라고 간청했다. 공주는 정중하게 대답하며, 당장의 구호를 약속하고 또 사정을 알게 되면 자기 아버지가 손님을 후하게 대접하리라고 약속했다. 그녀는 흩어진 시녀들을 다시 불러 모아 그들의 놀란 모습을 나무라며, 파이아케스족에게는 두려워할 만한 적이 없음을 일깨워 주었다. 그녀는 그들에게 이르기를, 이 사람은 불행한 방랑자이니 마땅히 보살펴 주어야 하며, 가난한 자와 나그네는 제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]께서 보내신 이들이라고 했다. 그녀는 그들에게 음식과 옷을 가져오게 했으니, 수레에 실린 물건들 가운데에는 그녀 오라비의 옷가지도 들어 있었기 때문이다. 이렇게 일이 끝나고, 오디세우스가 가려진 곳으로 물러가 자기 몸에서 바닷물 거품을 씻어 내고 옷을 입고 음식으로 기운을 차리자, 팔라스[아테나]가 그의 몸을 더욱 크게 만들고 그의 넓은 가슴과 사내다운 이마에 우아함을 흘려 넣어 주었다.\n\n그를 본 공주는 감탄으로 가득 차, 신들께서 자신에게 이런 남편을 보내 주셨으면 좋겠다고 시녀들에게 거리낌 없이 말했다. 그녀는 오디세우스에게 도성으로 가라고 권하며, 들판을 지나는 길까지는 자신과 그 일행을 따라오라고 했다. 그러나 도성에 가까워지면 더 이상 자기와 동행하는 모습을 보이지 말아 달라고 했으니, 자신이 그토록 늠름한 낯선 사내를 데리고 돌아오는 것을 보고 무례하고 천박한 자들이 입에 올릴지도 모를 말을 두려워했기 때문이다. 이를 피하기 위해 그녀는 그에게 도성에 인접한 작은 숲에서 멈추라고 일러 주었는데, 그곳에는 왕에게 속한 농장과 정원이 있었다. 공주와 그 동행들이 도성에 다다를 만한 시간을 둔 다음, 그가 그쪽으로 길을 나서면, 마주치는 누구에게든 쉽게 안내를 받아 왕의 거처에 이를 수 있으리라고 했다.\n\n오디세우스는 그 지시에 따랐고, 때가 되자 도성으로 나아갔다. 도성에 가까워졌을 때 그는 물을 길으러 항아리를 들고 나오는 젊은 여인을 만났다. 그 여인은 바로 그 모습으로 변장한 아테나였다. 오디세우스는 그녀에게 말을 걸어 알키노오스 왕의 궁전으로 가는 길을 안내해 달라고 청했다. 처녀는 정중하게 대답하며 그의 안내자가 되어 주겠다고 했으니, 그녀가 그에게 일러 준 바에 따르면 궁전은 자기 아버지의 거처 가까이에 있었다. 여신의 인도를 받으며, 또한 그녀의 힘으로 남의 눈에 띄지 않게 가려 주는 구름에 싸인 채, 오디세우스는 분주한 군중 사이를 지나갔고, 그들의 항구와 배들, 그들의 광장(영웅들이 모여드는 곳)과 그들의 성벽을 경탄하며 바라보다가 마침내 궁전에 이르렀다. 그곳에서 여신은 그가 곧 만나게 될 나라와 왕과 백성에 관해 몇 가지를 먼저 일러 준 뒤 그를 떠났다. 오디세우스는 궁전의 안뜰로 들어서기 전에 멈추어 서서 그 광경을 둘러보았다. 그 화려함이 그를 놀라게 했다. 청동으로 된 벽이 입구에서부터 안채에 이르기까지 뻗어 있었는데, 그 문은 황금이었고 문기둥은 은이었으며, 상인방은 은에 황금으로 장식되어 있었다. 양쪽으로는 황금과 은으로 빚어진 마스티프 개들의 형상이, 마치 그 길목을 지키는 듯 줄지어 서 있었다. 벽을 따라서는 그 길이 내내 자리들이 놓여 있었고, 그 위에는 파이아케스족 처녀들이 짠 가장 고운 천으로 된 덮개들이 펼쳐져 있었다. 이 자리에 왕족들이 앉아 잔치를 벌였으며, 우아한 젊은이들을 본뜬 황금 조각상들이 손에 불 켜진 횃불을 들고 그 광경 위로 빛을 비추었다. 무려 쉰 명에 이르는 여종들이 집안일을 돌보고 있었으니, 더러는 곡식을 빻고 더러는 자줏빛 양털을 감거나 베틀을 부지런히 놀렸다. 그도 그럴 것이 파이아케스족의 여인들이 집안일 솜씨에서 다른 모든 여인을 능가하는 정도는, 그 나라의 뱃사람들이 배를 다루는 솜씨에서 나머지 인류를 능가하는 정도와 같았기 때문이다. 안뜰 밖으로는 넉넉한 정원이 펼쳐져 있었는데, 그 넓이가 네 에이커에 달했다. 그 안에는 석류나무, 배나무, 사과나무, 무화과나무, 올리브나무 등 키 큰 나무가 숱하게 자라고 있었다. 겨울의 추위도 여름의 가뭄도 그 성장을 막지 못하여, 어떤 것은 싹을 틔우는 동안 다른 것은 무르익으면서 끊임없이 잇따라 무성하게 자라났다. 포도밭도 그에 못지않게 풍요로웠다. 한쪽에서는 어떤 포도덩굴은 꽃을 피우고 어떤 것은 잘 익은 포도를 잔뜩 매달고 있는 것을 볼 수 있었고, 다른 쪽에서는 포도 따는 이들이 포도즙 짜는 틀을 밟고 있는 것을 볼 수 있었다. 정원의 가장자리에는 온갖 빛깔의 꽃들이 더없이 정교한 솜씨로 배치되어 한 해 내내 피어 있었다. 그 한가운데에서는 두 개의 샘이 물을 쏟아 내고 있었는데, 하나는 인공 수로를 통해 정원 곳곳으로 흘렀고, 다른 하나는 궁전의 안뜰을 가로질러 인도되어 그곳에서 모든 시민이 물을 길어 갈 수 있었다.\n\n오디세우스는 자신은 남의 눈에 띄지 않은 채 감탄하며 서서 바라보고 있었으니, 아테나가 그의 둘레에 펼쳐 놓은 구름이 여전히 그를 가려 주고 있었기 때문이다. 마침내 그 광경을 충분히 살펴본 그는 빠른 걸음으로 영주들과 원로들이 모여 있는 큰 방으로 나아갔는데, 그들은 저녁 식사 뒤에 이어지던 헤르메스[로마의 〈메르쿠리우스〉] 숭배에 따라 그 신에게 헌주를 따르고 있었다. 바로 그때 아테나가 구름을 흩어 버려 그를 모여 있는 영주들 앞에 드러냈다. 왕비가 앉아 있는 자리로 나아간 그는 그녀의 발치에 무릎을 꿇고, 자신이 고국으로 돌아갈 수 있도록 호의와 도움을 베풀어 달라고 간청했다. 그러고는 물러나 탄원하는 자의 방식대로 화롯가에 앉았다.\n\n한동안 아무도 입을 열지 않았다. 마침내 한 나이 든 정치가가 왕에게 말했다. \"우리의 환대를 청하는 나그네를, 아무도 맞이하지 않은 채 탄원하는 모습 그대로 기다리게 하는 것은 옳지 않습니다. 그러니 그를 우리 사이의 한 자리로 이끌고 음식과 포도주를 베풀어 주시지요.\" 이 말에 왕은 일어나 오디세우스에게 손을 내밀어 한 자리로 이끌었는데, 이 낯선 이에게 자리를 내주고자 자기 아들을 그곳에서 옮기게 했다. 음식과 포도주가 그의 앞에 차려졌고, 그는 그것을 먹으며 기운을 차렸다.\n\n이윽고 왕은 손님들을 돌려보내며, 이튿날 이 낯선 이를 위해 무엇을 하는 것이 가장 좋을지 의논하고자 그들을 회의에 부르겠노라고 알렸다.\n\n손님들이 떠나고 오디세우스가 왕과 왕비와 더불어 단둘이 남게 되자, 왕비는 그에게 그가 누구이며 어디서 왔는지를 물었고, 또한 (그가 입고 있는 옷이 자기 시녀들과 자신이 손수 만든 것임을 알아보고는) 누구에게서 그 옷가지를 받았는지를 물었다. 그는 칼립소의 섬에서 머문 일과 그곳을 떠나온 일, 자기 뗏목이 부서진 일, 헤엄쳐서 살아난 일, 그리고 공주가 베풀어 준 구원에 대해 이야기했다. 부모는 흡족하게 그 이야기를 들었고, 왕은 손님이 자기 나라로 돌아갈 수 있도록 배 한 척을 마련해 주겠노라고 약속했다.\n\n이튿날 모여든 영주들은 왕의 약속을 확정해 주었다. 작은 배 한 척이 준비되고 건장한 노잡이들로 선원이 선발되었으며, 모두가 궁전으로 발길을 옮겨 그곳에서 풍성한 잔치가 베풀어졌다. 잔치가 끝나자 왕은 젊은이들이 손님에게 사내다운 운동에서의 능숙한 솜씨를 보여 주자고 제안했고, 모두가 달리기와 씨름을 비롯한 여러 경기를 위해 경기장으로 나아갔다. 모두가 자기 솜씨를 다 보인 뒤 오디세우스도 자신이 할 수 있는 바를 보이라는 요청을 받았는데, 처음에는 사양했으나 젊은이들 가운데 한 사람에게서 조롱을 받자 파이아케스족 누구도 던진 적이 없을 만큼 훨씬 무거운 원반을 집어 들어 그들의 가장 먼 던지기보다 더 멀리 날려 보냈다. 모두가 놀라며 한층 더 커진 존경의 눈으로 손님을 바라보았다.\n\n경기가 끝나자 그들은 큰 방으로 돌아왔고, 전령이 눈먼 음유시인 데모도코스를 인도해 들였다.\n\n\"······무사 여신들에게 사랑받는 이여, 여신은 그에게 좋은 것과 나쁜 것을 함께 정해 주어, 그에게서 시력은 앗아 갔으나 신성한 가락은 베풀어 주었네.\"\n\n그는 그리스인들이 트로이아 안으로 들어가는 길을 찾아내게 해 준 〈목마〉를 자기 노래의 주제로 삼았다. 아폴론이 그에게 영감을 불어넣어 주었고, 그가 그 파란만장했던 시절의 공포와 위업을 어찌나 절절하게 노래했던지 모두가 기뻐했으나, 오디세우스는 눈물을 흘릴 만큼 마음이 움직였다. 이를 알아챈 알키노오스는 노래가 끝나자 그에게, 어찌하여 트로이아라는 말을 듣고 슬픔이 깨어났는지를 물었다. 그가 거기서 아버지나 형제, 혹은 어느 소중한 친구를 잃기라도 했단 말인가? 오디세우스는 자신의 진짜 이름을 밝혀 자기가 누구인지 알리는 것으로 대답했고, 그들의 요청에 따라 트로이아를 떠난 이후 자신에게 일어난 모험들을 낱낱이 이야기했다. 이 이야기는 손님을 향한 파이아케스족의 공감과 감탄을 더없이 높이 끌어올렸다. 왕은 모든 영주가 그에게 선물을 바치자고 제안하며 몸소 그 본을 보였다. 그들은 그 말을 따라 서로 다투어 그 이름난 낯선 이에게 값진 선물을 가득 안겨 주었다.\n\n이튿날 오디세우스는 파이아케스족의 배에 올라 항해에 나섰고, 얼마 지나지 않아 무사히 자기 섬인 이타케에 다다랐다. 배가 물가에 닿았을 때 그는 잠들어 있었다. 뱃사람들은 그를 깨우지 않고 그를 뭍으로 옮겨 그가 받은 선물이 든 궤짝과 함께 그를 해안에 내려놓은 다음 배를 저어 떠나갔다.\n\n포세이돈[로마의 〈넵투누스〉]은 파이아케스족이 이렇듯 오디세우스를 자기 손아귀에서 구해 낸 처사에 몹시 노하여, 그 배가 항구로 돌아오자 항구 어귀 바로 맞은편에서 그것을 바위로 둔갑시켜 버렸다.\n\n파이아케스족의 배에 대한 호메로스의 묘사는 근대 증기 항해의 경이를 미리 내다본 것처럼 여겨져 왔다. 알키노오스는 오디세우스에게 이렇게 말한다.\n\n\"말하라, 그대가 어느 도성에서, 어느 고장에서 떠밀려 왔는지, 또 그 고장에는 어떤 거민들이 자랑스레 살고 있는지를. 그리하면 그대는 머지않아 정해진 나라에 가닿으리라, 스스로 움직이며 마음을 깃들인 경이로운 배를 타고서. 그 배는 어떤 키도 그 항로를 잡아 주지 않고, 어떤 키잡이도 이끌지 않으나, 지각 있는 사람처럼 물결을 가르며 나아가, 해의 모든 것을 살피는 빛살 아래 펼쳐진 모든 해안과 모든 만을 낱낱이 알고 있노라.\" ─〈오디세이아〉, 제8권.\n\n칼라일 경은 그의 「터키 및 그리스 해역 일기」에서, 자신이 고대 파이아케스족의 섬으로 여기는 코르푸에 대해 다음과 같이 말한다.\n\n\"그곳의 지형들은 〈오디세이아〉를 설명해 준다. 바다의 신의 신전이, 항구와 해협과 대양을 굽어보는 벼랑 끝, 더없이 탄력 있는 잔디가 깔린 풀밭의 단(壇) 위에 자리한 것보다 더 어울리게 놓일 수는 없었으리라. 안쪽 항구의 어귀 바로 그 자리에는 그림 같은 바위 하나가 있고 그 위에 작은 수도원이 얹혀 있는데, 어느 전설에 따르면 이것이 바로 둔갑한 오디세우스의 거룻배라고 한다.\n\n\"이 섬에서 거의 유일한 강은, 왕의 도성과 궁전이 있었으리라 짐작되는 자리로부터 꼭 알맞은 거리에 놓여 있어, 나우시카 공주가 궁정의 처녀들과 함께 옷을 빨러 갈 때에 수레를 동원하고 점심을 챙겨 갔던 일을 충분히 납득하게 해 준다.\"\n\n# FATE OF THE SUITORS\n\n오디세우스는 이제 이타케를 떠나 있은 지 스무 해가 되었으므로, 잠에서 깨어났을 때 그는 자기 고국 땅을 알아보지 못했다. 아테나가 젊은 목동의 모습으로 그에게 나타나 그가 어디에 있는지를 일러 주고, 그의 궁전에서 벌어지고 있는 사정을 들려주었다. 이타케와 이웃 섬들의 귀족 백 명이 넘는 자들이, 그가 죽었으리라 짐작하고는 여러 해 동안 그의 아내 페넬로페의 손길을 구해 왔으며, 마치 자기들이 궁전과 백성 둘 다의 주인이라도 되는 양 그것들 위에 군림하고 있다는 것이었다. 그가 그들에게 복수할 수 있으려면, 자신의 정체를 들키지 않는 것이 중요했다. 그리하여 아테나는 그를 흉한 거지로 둔갑시켰고, 그런 모습으로 그는 자기 집안의 충직한 종인 돼지치기 에우마이오스에게 친절히 맞아들여졌다.\n\n그의 아들 텔레마코스는 아버지를 찾아 떠나 있어 자리에 없었다. 그는 트로이아 원정에서 돌아온 다른 왕들의 궁정으로 가 있었다. 찾아 헤매던 중에 그는 아테나에게서 집으로 돌아가라는 조언을 받았다. 그는 돌아오자 구혼자들 사이에 모습을 드러내기에 앞서 궁전의 사정을 좀 알아보고자 에우마이오스를 찾아갔다. 에우마이오스의 곁에 낯선 이가 있는 것을 보고, 그는 비록 거지의 행색을 하고 있었음에도 정중하게 그를 대하며 도움을 약속했다. 에우마이오스는 페넬로페에게 그녀의 아들이 돌아왔음을 은밀히 알리고자 궁전으로 보내졌으니, 구혼자들에 관한 한 신중을 기할 필요가 있었기 때문이다. 텔레마코스가 알아낸 바에 따르면 그들은 그를 가로막아 죽이려는 음모를 꾸미고 있었던 것이다. 에우마이오스가 떠나자 아테나가 오디세우스 앞에 모습을 드러내어 그에게 아들에게 정체를 밝히라고 일렀다. 동시에 그녀는 그를 어루만져 단번에 그에게서 늙고 궁핍한 모습을 거두어 내고, 본래 그에게 속해 있던 정정한 장년의 풍모를 되돌려 주었다. 텔레마코스는 놀라움에 차서 그를 바라보았고, 처음에는 그가 필멸의 인간 이상의 존재임이 틀림없다고 생각했다. 그러나 오디세우스는 자신이 그의 아버지임을 밝히고, 모습이 바뀐 것은 아테나가 한 일이라고 설명했다.\n\n\"······그러자 텔레마코스는 두 팔로 아버지의 목을 끌어안고 울었다. 북받치는 비탄의 욕망이 둘 모두를 사로잡으니, 나직한 흐느낌을 내뱉으며 저마다 제 슬픔에 몸을 맡겼다.\"\n\n아버지와 아들은 어떻게 하면 구혼자들을 제압하고 그들의 횡포를 벌할 수 있을지를 함께 의논했다. 텔레마코스는 궁전으로 나아가 예전처럼 구혼자들 사이에 섞이고, 오디세우스 또한 거지의 행색으로 가기로 정해졌으니, 거지라는 신분은 거칠던 옛 시절에는 오늘날 우리가 그것에 부여하는 것과는 다른 권리를 누렸다. 나그네이자 이야기꾼으로서 거지는 영주들의 큰 방에 받아들여졌고, 흔히 손님처럼 대접받기도 했다. 물론 때로는 모욕을 받는 일도 의심할 여지 없이 있기는 했다. 오디세우스는 아들에게, 자신에 대한 어떤 별난 관심도 내비쳐서 그가 겉모습과는 다른 사람임을 알고 있다는 사실을 드러내지 말라고 단단히 일렀으며, 설령 자신이 모욕을 당하거나 매를 맞는 것을 보더라도 여느 낯선 이를 위해 나설 만한 정도 이상으로는 끼어들지 말라고 했다. 궁전에 다다른 그들은 잔치와 소란이 여느 때처럼 벌어지고 있는 광경을 보았다. 구혼자들은 텔레마코스가 돌아온 것을 기쁘게 맞이하는 체했으나, 그의 목숨을 빼앗으려던 음모가 실패한 데 속으로는 분해하고 있었다. 늙은 거지는 안으로 들어오는 것이 허락되었고, 식탁에서 한몫의 음식을 받았다. 오디세우스가 궁전의 안뜰로 들어설 때 한 가지 가슴 뭉클한 일이 일어났다. 늙은 개 한 마리가 마당에 누워 나이가 들 대로 들어 거의 죽어 가고 있었는데, 낯선 이가 들어오는 것을 보고는 귀를 쫑긋 세우며 머리를 들었다. 그것은 오디세우스 자신의 개인 아르고스로, 지난날 그가 흔히 사냥에 데리고 다니던 개였다.\n\n\"······오래도록 잃었던 오디세우스가 가까이 온 것을 알아채자마자, 그 개는 귀를 납작하게 늘어뜨리고 꼬리를 흔들어 반가운 인사를 보냈으나, 일어설 힘이 없어 예전처럼 주인에게 다가가지는 못했다. 그를 알아본 오디세우스는 남몰래 눈물 한 방울을 닦았다. ······그러자 운명이 늙은 아르고스를 풀어 주었으니, 그가 스무 해째에 돌아온 오디세우스를 살아서 보기에 이른 바로 그때였다.\"\n\n오디세우스가 큰 방에 앉아 자기 몫의 음식을 먹고 있을 때, 구혼자들이 그에게 무례함을 부리기 시작했다. 그가 점잖게 항의하자 그들 가운데 하나가 의자를 들어 그것으로 그를 후려쳤다. 텔레마코스는 자기 아버지가 자기 집 안에서 그런 대접을 받는 것을 보고 분노를 억누르느라 무진 애를 썼으나, 아버지의 당부를 떠올리고는, 비록 젊으나 집안의 주인이자 손님들의 보호자로서 자기에게 어울리는 정도 이상은 말하지 않았다.\n\n페넬로페는 자기 구혼자들 가운데 누구 하나를 택하는 결정을 어찌나 오래도록 미루어 왔던지, 이제 더 이상 지체할 핑계가 남아 있지 않은 듯했다. 남편이 계속 돌아오지 않는 것은 그의 귀환을 더는 기대할 수 없음을 입증하는 것 같았다. 그러는 사이 그녀의 아들은 다 자라나 자기 일을 스스로 처리할 수 있게 되었다. 그리하여 그녀는 자신의 선택이라는 문제를 구혼자들 사이의 솜씨 겨루기에 부치는 데 동의했다. 선택된 시합은 활쏘기였다. 열두 개의 고리를 한 줄로 늘어놓고, 그 열두 개를 모조리 화살로 꿰뚫는 자가 왕비를 상으로 차지하게 되어 있었다. 지난날 동료 영웅 가운데 한 사람이 오디세우스에게 준 활 하나를 무기고에서 가져와, 화살이 가득 든 화살통과 함께 큰 방에 놓아두었다. 텔레마코스는 다른 무기는 모조리 치워 두도록 미리 손을 써 두었는데, 겨루기가 한창 달아오를 때 어느 무모한 순간에 그것들을 그릇되게 쓸 위험이 있다는 핑계를 댔다.\n\n시합을 위한 모든 준비가 갖추어지자, 가장 먼저 해야 할 일은 시위를 메기 위해 활을 휘는 것이었다. 텔레마코스가 그것을 해 보려 했으나 온갖 애를 써도 헛수고임을 알았고, 자기 힘에 부치는 일을 시도했노라고 겸손히 인정하며 활을 다른 이에게 넘겼다. 그 사람도 별로 나을 것 없는 솜씨로 시도하다가, 동료들의 웃음과 야유 속에 포기하고 말았다. 또 다른 이가 시도하고 또 다른 이가 시도했으며, 그들은 활에 짐승 기름을 문질러 보기도 했으나 모두 헛일이었으니, 활은 도무지 휘어지지 않았다. 그러자 오디세우스가 입을 열어, 자신에게도 한번 해 보게 해 달라고 겸손히 청하며 말했다. \"거지인 이 몸이오나, 저도 한때는 군인이었고, 이 늙은 팔다리에도 아직 얼마간의 힘은 남아 있습니다.\" 구혼자들은 비웃으며 야유를 보냈고, 무례하다 하여 그를 방에서 내쫓으라고 명했다. 그러나 텔레마코스가 그를 두둔하며, 그저 노인의 마음을 흡족하게 해 주려는 것일 뿐이라며 그에게 해 보라고 일렀다. 오디세우스는 활을 받아 들고 명수의 손길로 그것을 다루었다. 그는 손쉽게 시위를 홈에 메기고, 이어 화살 하나를 활에 메겨 시위를 당기더니, 그 화살을 빗나감 없이 고리들 사이로 쏘아 보냈다.\n\n그들에게 놀라움을 표할 겨를도 주지 않고 그가 말했다. \"이제 또 다른 과녁을!\" 그러고는 구혼자들 가운데 가장 무례한 자를 똑바로 겨누었다. 화살이 그자의 목을 꿰뚫었고 그는 쓰러져 죽었다. 텔레마코스와 에우마이오스, 그리고 또 한 사람의 충직한 부하가 단단히 무장을 한 채 이제 오디세우스의 곁으로 뛰어왔다. 구혼자들은 경악하여 무기를 찾아 두리번거렸으나 아무것도 찾지 못했고, 달아날 길도 없었으니 에우마이오스가 문을 막아 두었기 때문이다. 오디세우스는 그들을 오래도록 의구심에 빠뜨려 두지 않았다. 그는 자신이 바로 오래도록 잃었던 그 영주임을 밝히니, 그들이 그의 집을 침범하고 그의 재산을 탕진했으며 그의 아내와 아들을 열 해라는 긴 세월 동안 핍박해 왔다고 했다. 그리고 자신은 그들에게 흡족하리만치 복수할 작정이라고 그들에게 일렀다. 모두가 죽임을 당했고, 오디세우스는 자기 궁전의 주인이자 자기 왕국과 아내의 주인으로 남게 되었다.\n\n테니슨의 시 「율리시스」는 늙은 영웅을 그려 보이는데, 위험들이 지나가고 이제 집에 머물러 행복을 누리는 일밖에 남지 않게 되자 그는 무위에 싫증을 느껴 새로운 모험을 찾아 다시 길을 나서기로 결심한다.\n\n\"······오라, 나의 벗들이여, 더 새로운 세계를 찾아 나서기에 너무 늦지 않았노라. 배를 밀어내고, 차례로 자리에 앉아 울리는 물이랑을 두드려라. 내 뜻은 굳건하니 해 지는 곳 너머로, 그리고 모든 서녘 별들이 멱 감는 곳 너머로 나아가, 죽는 그날까지 항해하리라. 어쩌면 깊은 바다가 우리를 휩쓸어 가라앉힐지도 모르고, 어쩌면 우리가 행복의 섬들에 가닿아, 우리가 알던 저 위대한 아킬레우스를 보게 될지도 모르리라.\" 운운.\n\n────\n\n우리는 그리스의 영웅 가운데 한 사람인 오디세우스를 따라가며, 그가 트로이아에서 고향으로 돌아오기까지의 방랑을 살펴보았다. 이제 우리는 패배한 백성의 남은 무리가 그들의 우두머리 아이네아스의 인솔 아래, 조국이 멸망한 뒤 새로운 거처를 찾아 떠난 운명을 함께 나누어 보고자 한다. 목마가 그 속에 품었던 무장한 병사들을 토해 내고, 그 결과 도시가 함락되어 불길에 휩싸이던 그 운명적인 밤에, 아이네아스는 아버지와 아내와 어린 아들을 데리고 파괴의 현장에서 빠져나왔다. 아버지 안키세스는 너무 늙어 필요한 속도로 걸을 수 없었으므로, 아이네아스는 그를 어깨에 들쳐 멨다. 그렇게 짐을 진 채 아들의 손을 이끌고 아내를 뒤따르게 하면서, 그는 불타는 도시를 빠져나오려 안간힘을 썼다. 그러나 그 혼란 속에서 그의 아내는 휩쓸려 사라지고 말았다.\n\n집결지에 도착하니 남녀를 막론한 수많은 피난민이 모여 있었고, 그들은 아이네아스의 인도에 따르기로 했다. 준비에 몇 달이 소요되었고, 마침내 그들은 배에 올랐다. 그들은 먼저 인접한 트라키아 해안에 상륙하여 도시를 세우려 준비했으나, 아이네아스는 어떤 불길한 전조에 가로막혔다. 제물을 바치려고 그는 덤불 하나에서 잔가지 몇 개를 꺾었다. 그러자 놀랍게도 상처 입은 부분에서 피가 떨어졌다. 그가 다시 그 행동을 되풀이하자, 땅에서 어떤 목소리가 그에게 외쳤다. \"나를 해치지 마시오, 아이네아스. 나는 당신의 친족 폴리도로스이며, 여기서 수많은 화살에 찔려 살해당했소. 그 화살들에서 덤불이 자라났고, 그것은 내 피로 자란 것이오.\" 이 말은 아이네아스에게 폴리도로스가 트로이아의 젊은 왕자였음을 떠올리게 했다. 그의 아버지는 전쟁의 참상에서 멀리 떨어진 곳에서 그를 양육시키려고, 막대한 보물과 함께 그를 인접한 트라키아 땅으로 보냈던 것이다. 그러나 그가 보내진 곳의 왕은 그를 살해하고 그의 보물을 차지해 버렸다. 아이네아스와 그의 동료들은 그런 죄악의 오점으로 저주받은 땅이라 여기고 서둘러 그곳을 떠났다.\n\n그들은 다음으로 델로스 섬에 상륙했다. 이 섬은 한때 떠다니는 섬이었으나, 제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]가 단단한 사슬로 그것을 바다 밑바닥에 붙들어 매었다. 아폴론과 아르테미스[로마의 〈디아나〉]가 그곳에서 태어났으며, 그 섬은 아폴론에게 바쳐진 신성한 곳이었다. 여기서 아이네아스는 아폴론의 신탁에 물었고, 늘 그러하듯 모호한 답을 받았다. \"그대의 옛 어머니를 찾으라. 그곳에서 아이네아스의 후손이 거하며, 다른 모든 민족을 그들의 지배 아래 굴복시키리라.\" 트로이아인들은 기쁨에 차서 이 말을 듣고는 곧바로 서로에게 묻기 시작했다. \"신탁이 가리키는 곳이 어디인가?\" 안키세스는 자신들의 선조가 크레타에서 왔다는 전승이 있음을 기억해 냈고, 그들은 그곳으로 뱃머리를 돌리기로 결심했다. 그들은 크레타에 도착하여 도시를 세우기 시작했으나, 무리 사이에 병이 돌았고, 그들이 일군 밭은 수확을 내지 못했다. 일이 이렇듯 암울한 양상을 보이던 차에, 아이네아스는 꿈속에서 이 나라를 떠나 헤스페리아라 불리는 서쪽 땅을 찾으라는 경고를 받았다. 그곳은 트로이아 종족의 진정한 시조인 다르다노스가 본래 이주해 온 땅이었다. 따라서 그들은 이제 이탈리아라 불리는 헤스페리아로 앞으로의 항로를 잡았으나, 수많은 모험을 거치고 근대의 항해자라면 세계를 여러 바퀴 돌고도 남을 만큼의 세월이 흐른 뒤에야 비로소 그곳에 도착할 수 있었다.\n\n그들이 처음 상륙한 곳은 하르피이아이의 섬이었다. 이들은 처녀의 머리를 한 역겨운 새들로, 긴 발톱을 지녔으며 굶주림으로 얼굴이 창백했다. 그들은 신들에 의해 어떤 피네우스를 괴롭히도록 보내졌는데, 제우스는 그의 잔혹함을 벌하고자 그의 시력을 앗아 간 바 있었다. 그의 앞에 음식이 놓이기만 하면 하르피이아이가 공중에서 쏜살같이 내려와 그것을 채어 가곤 했다. 그들은 아르고호 원정대의 영웅들에게 쫓겨 피네우스에게서 물러났고, 이제 아이네아스가 그들을 발견한 그 섬으로 피신해 있었던 것이다.\n\n항구에 들어서니 트로이아인들은 들판을 누비는 소 떼를 보았다. 그들은 원하는 만큼 소를 잡아 잔치를 준비했다. 그러나 그들이 식탁에 자리를 잡자마자 공중에서 무시무시한 소란이 들려왔고, 이 가증스러운 하르피이아이의 무리가 그들에게 달려들어 발톱으로 접시의 고기를 움켜쥐고는 그것을 들고 날아가 버렸다. 아이네아스와 그의 동료들은 칼을 빼 들고 그 괴물들에게 힘껏 일격을 가했으나 아무런 소용이 없었으니, 그들이 어찌나 날쌔던지 맞히기가 거의 불가능했고, 그들의 깃털은 강철도 뚫지 못하는 갑옷과도 같았다. 그들 중 하나가 인근의 벼랑에 내려앉아 비명처럼 외쳤다. \"트로이아인들이여, 죄 없는 우리 새들을 이렇게 대접하느냐. 먼저 우리 소들을 도살하고 이제는 우리 자신에게 전쟁을 걸다니?\" 그러고는 그들에게 앞날의 항로에서 끔찍한 고난이 닥치리라 예언했고, 분노를 다 쏟아 낸 뒤 날아가 버렸다. 트로이아인들은 서둘러 그 나라를 떠났고, 다음으로 에페이로스 해안을 따라 항해하게 되었다. 여기서 그들은 상륙했고, 놀랍게도 포로로 끌려온 어떤 트로이아 망명자들이 그 나라의 통치자가 되었음을 알게 되었다. 헥토르의 미망인 안드로마케는 승리한 그리스의 우두머리 가운데 한 사람의 아내가 되어 그에게 아들을 낳아 주었다. 그녀의 남편이 죽자, 그녀는 아들의 후견인으로서 나라의 섭정으로 남았고, 함께 포로로 잡혀 온 트로이아 왕가 출신의 헬레노스와 혼인했다. 헬레노스와 안드로마케는 망명자들을 더없는 환대로 맞이했으며, 선물을 잔뜩 안겨 떠나보냈다.\n\n이곳에서 아이네아스는 시켈리아[시칠리아] 해안을 따라 항해하며 키클로페스의 나라를 지났다. 여기서 그들은 해안에서 어떤 비참한 인물의 부름을 받았는데, 비록 누더기가 되었으나 그 옷차림으로 보아 그가 그리스인임을 알아챘다. 그는 자신이 오디세우스의 동료 가운데 한 사람이며, 그 우두머리가 황급히 떠나면서 자신을 뒤에 남겨 두었노라 말했다. 그는 폴리페모스와 얽힌 오디세우스의 모험담을 들려주었고, 자신을 함께 데려가 달라 간청했다. 그곳에서는 야생 열매와 풀뿌리 말고는 목숨을 부지할 방도가 없었고, 키클로페스에 대한 끊임없는 두려움 속에서 살아왔기 때문이다. 그가 말하는 동안 폴리페모스가 모습을 드러냈다. 형체를 알 수 없고 거대한, 그 하나뿐인 눈마저 멀어 버린 끔찍한 괴물이었다.[26] 그는 지팡이로 길을 더듬으며 조심스러운 걸음으로 바닷가로 내려와, 물결에 자신의 눈구멍을 씻으려 했다. 물에 이르자 그는 그들 쪽으로 걸어 들어왔고, 그 엄청난 키 덕분에 바다 깊숙이까지 나아갈 수 있었으므로, 트로이아인들은 두려움에 휩싸여 그를 피하고자 노에 매달렸다. 노 젓는 소리를 듣고 폴리페모스가 그들을 향해 고함을 질러 대니 해안이 온통 울려 퍼졌고, 그 소리에 다른 키클로페스가 동굴과 숲에서 나와 우뚝 솟은 소나무들이 늘어선 듯 해안을 따라 줄지어 섰다. 트로이아인들은 노를 힘껏 저어 곧 그들을 시야에서 벗어나게 했다.\n\n아이네아스는 헬레노스에게서 괴물 스킬라와 카립디스가 지키는 해협을 피하라는 경고를 받은 바 있었다. 독자들이 기억하듯, 그곳에서 오디세우스는 부하 여섯을 잃었으니, 항해자들이 카립디스를 피하는 데 온통 정신이 팔린 사이에 스킬라에게 채여 갔던 것이다. 아이네아스는 헬레노스의 조언을 따라 그 위험한 길목을 피하고 시켈리아 섬을 따라 항해했다.\n\n헤라[로마의 〈유노〉]는 트로이아인들이 운명으로 정해진 해안을 향해 순조롭게 나아가는 것을 보고는, 그들에 대한 오랜 원한이 되살아남을 느꼈다. 파리스가 아름다움의 상을 다른 이에게 안겨 그녀를 모욕한 일을 잊을 수 없었기 때문이다. 천상의 마음에 그런 원한이 깃들 수 있단 말인가![27] 그리하여 그녀는 바람들의 통치자 아이올로스에게로 서둘러 갔으니, 그는 오디세우스에게 순풍을 베풀고 역풍은 자루에 묶어 주었던 바로 그 신이었다. 아이올로스는 여신의 뜻에 따라 그의 아들들인 보레아스와 티폰을 비롯한 다른 바람들을 내보내 바다를 뒤흔들게 했다. 무시무시한 폭풍이 일어났고, 트로이아의 배들은 항로에서 벗어나 아프리카 해안 쪽으로 떠밀려 갔다. 그들은 난파의 위기에 처하여 뿔뿔이 흩어졌고, 그리하여 아이네아스는 자신의 배를 제외한 나머지가 모두 사라졌다고 여겼다.\n\n이 위기의 순간에, 포세이돈[로마의 〈넵투누스〉]은 폭풍이 거세게 몰아치는 소리를 듣고, 자신이 그런 명령을 내린 바 없음을 알기에 물결 위로 머리를 들었다가, 아이네아스의 함대가 강풍에 떠밀리는 것을 보았다. 헤라의 적의를 아는지라 그 까닭을 헤아리는 데 어려움이 없었으나, 자신의 영역에 가해진 이러한 간섭에 그의 분노는 조금도 덜하지 않았다. 그는 바람들을 불러 호된 질책과 함께 돌려보냈다. 그런 다음 물결을 잠재우고, 태양의 얼굴 앞을 가린 구름을 쓸어 냈다. 바위에 걸린 배 몇 척은 자신의 삼지창으로 들어 올려 떼어 냈고, 한편 트리톤과 바다의 요정은 다른 배들 밑에 어깨를 받쳐 다시 띄워 주었다. 바다가 잔잔해지자 트로이아인들은 가장 가까운 해안을 찾았으니, 그곳은 카르타고 해안이었다. 거기서 아이네아스는 비록 심하게 흔들리기는 했어도 배들이 하나하나 모두 무사히 도착한 것을 알고 더없이 기뻐했다.\n\n월러는 「호국경(크롬웰)에게 바치는 송시」에서 포세이돈이 폭풍을 잠재운 이 일을 다음과 같이 인유한다.\n\n\"물결 위로 포세이돈이 얼굴을 드러내어 바람을 꾸짖고 트로이아 종족을 구하였듯, 다른 모든 이 위로 드높이 오르신 전하께서는 우리를 뒤흔들던 야망의 폭풍을 잠재우셨도다.\"\n\n망명자들이 이제 다다른 카르타고는, 시켈리아 맞은편 아프리카 해안의 한 지점이었다. 그곳에서는 당시 그들의 여왕 디도가 이끄는 티로스의 식민지가, 훗날 로마 그 자체의 맞수가 될 운명을 지닌 한 국가의 토대를 닦고 있었다. 디도는 티로스의 왕 벨로스의 딸이자, 아버지의 뒤를 이어 왕위에 오른 피그말리온의 누이였다. 그녀의 남편은 막대한 부를 지닌 시카이오스였는데, 그의 보물을 탐낸 피그말리온이 그를 죽음에 이르게 했다. 디도는 남녀를 막론한 수많은 벗과 추종자들을 거느리고, 여러 척의 배에 시카이오스의 보물을 싣고 티로스를 탈출하는 데 성공했다. 그들이 미래의 거처로 삼고자 택한 지점에 도착했을 때, 그녀는 원주민들에게 황소 한 마리의 가죽으로 둘러쌀 수 있을 만큼의 땅만을 청했다. 이것이 선뜻 허락되자, 그녀는 그 가죽을 가느다란 끈으로 잘라, 그것으로 한 지점을 에워싸고 그 위에 성채를 세운 뒤 그것을 비르사(가죽)라 불렀다. 이 요새를 중심으로 카르타고 도시가 일어섰고, 곧 강력하고 번창하는 곳이 되었다.\n\n아이네아스가 그의 트로이아인들과 함께 그곳에 도착했을 때의 사정이 이러했다. 디도는 그 저명한 망명자들을 다정함과 환대로 맞이했다. \"고난을 모르지 않기에,\" 그녀는 말했다. \"나는 불행한 이들을 도울 줄 알게 되었습니다.\"[28] 여왕의 환대는 힘과 기예의 경기가 펼쳐지는 잔치로 드러났다. 이방인들은 그녀 자신의 백성들과 대등한 조건으로 우승을 다투었고, 여왕은 승자가 \"트로이아인이든 티로스인이든 자신에게는 아무런 차이가 없다\"고 선언했다.[29] 경기에 뒤이은 잔치에서, 아이네아스는 여왕의 청에 따라 트로이아 역사의 마지막 사건들과 도시가 함락된 뒤 자신이 겪은 모험을 이야기해 들려주었다. 디도는 그의 이야기에 매료되어 그의 위업에 감탄으로 가득 찼다. 그녀는 그에게 뜨거운 연정을 품었고, 그 또한 자신의 방랑에 행복한 종지부를 찍어 줄 거처와 왕국과 신부를 한꺼번에 안겨 주는 듯 보이는 이 다행스러운 기회를 기꺼이 받아들이는 듯했다. 즐거운 교류 속에 몇 달이 흘러갔고, 이탈리아도 그 해안에 세워질 운명인 제국도 똑같이 잊힌 듯했다. 이를 본 제우스는 헤르메스[로마의 〈메르쿠리우스〉]를 보내어 아이네아스에게 그의 드높은 운명을 일깨우고 항해를 다시 시작하라 명하는 전갈을 전하게 했다.\n\n아이네아스는 디도와 작별했으나, 그녀는 그를 붙잡으려 온갖 유혹과 설득을 다했다. 그녀의 사랑과 자존심에 가해진 타격은 견디기에 너무도 컸으니, 그가 떠나 버린 것을 알게 되자 그녀는 자신이 세우게 한 화장용 장작더미 위에 올라, 스스로를 찔러 그 장작더미와 함께 불타 사라졌다. 도시 위로 치솟는 불길은 떠나가는 트로이아인들의 눈에 들어왔고, 비록 그 까닭은 알 수 없었으나, 아이네아스에게 그 치명적인 사건에 대한 어떤 예감을 안겨 주었다.\n\n다음의 경구는 「우아한 발췌집」에서 찾아볼 수 있다.\n\n# FROM THE LATIN\n\n라틴어에서 \"불행하여라, 디도여, 그대의 운명은 첫 번째와 두 번째 혼인의 처지에서 그러하였도다! 한 남편은 죽음으로 그대를 도망치게 하였고, 다른 이는 떠남으로 그대의 죽음을 불렀도다.\"\n\n트로이아 혈통의 왕자 아케스테스가 다스리는 시켈리아 섬에 들러 그에게서 환대를 받은 뒤, 트로이아인들은 다시 배에 올라 이탈리아를 향한 항로를 이어 갔다. 이제 아프로디테[로마의 〈베누스〉]는 포세이돈에게 청하여, 마침내 자신의 아들이 바라 마지않던 목표에 다다라 깊은 바다에서 겪는 위험에 종지부를 찍게 해 달라 간청했다. 포세이돈은 이에 응하되, 다만 나머지 모두를 위한 몸값으로 한 목숨만을 요구했다. 그 희생자는 키잡이 팔리누루스였다. 그가 손을 키에 얹은 채 별을 지켜보며 앉아 있을 때, 포세이돈이 보낸 솜누스가 포르바스의 모습으로 다가와 말했다. \"팔리누루스여, 산들바람은 순조롭고 물결은 잔잔하며 배는 제 항로를 따라 꾸준히 나아가고 있소. 잠시 누워 필요한 휴식을 취하시오. 내가 그대 대신 키 앞에 서겠소.\" 팔리누루스가 대답했다. \"잔잔한 바다나 순풍 따위는 내게 말하지 마시오. 나는 그것들의 배신을 너무도 많이 보아 왔소. 내가 아이네아스를 날씨와 바람의 변덕에 맡길 성싶소?\" 그러고는 그는 계속 키를 붙들고 별에 시선을 고정한 채 있었다. 그러나 솜누스가 레테의 이슬에 적신 가지를 그의 머리 위로 흔들자, 그의 두 눈은 온갖 안간힘에도 불구하고 감기고 말았다. 그러자 솜누스가 그를 뱃전 너머로 밀어 떨어뜨렸으나, 그가 키를 붙들고 있었던 탓에 키도 그와 함께 떨어져 나갔다. 포세이돈은 자신의 약속을 잊지 않고, 키도 키잡이도 없이 배를 항로에 머무르게 했으니, 아이네아스가 그를 잃은 것을 알아채고 충실한 키잡이를 깊이 애도하며 손수 배를 맡을 때까지 그러했다.\n\n스콧의 「마미온」 제1편 서두에는 팔리누루스의 이야기를 아름답게 인유한 대목이 있다. 거기서 시인은 최근 세상을 떠난 윌리엄 피트를 두고 이렇게 말한다.\n\n\"오, 생각하라, 그의 마지막 날까지 죽음이 막 그 먹이를 노리며 맴돌 때, 팔리누루스의 변함없는 기개로, 그가 위태로운 자리에 굳건히 섰음을. 필요한 휴식을 청하는 매번의 부름을 물리치고, 죽어 가는 손으로 키를 붙들었음을, 그러다 그가 쓰러지면서, 운명의 흔들림으로 나라의 키잡이 자리가 무너졌음을.\"\n\n배들은 마침내 이탈리아 해안에 다다랐고, 모험가들은 기쁨에 차서 뭍으로 뛰어내렸다. 그의 백성들이 야영지를 마련하는 데 여념이 없는 동안, 아이네아스는 시빌라의 거처를 찾아갔다. 그곳은 아폴론과 아르테미스에게 바쳐진 신전과 숲에 잇닿은 동굴이었다. 아이네아스가 그 광경을 바라보고 있을 때, 시빌라가 그에게 말을 걸었다. 그녀는 그의 용건을 아는 듯했고, 그곳을 다스리는 신의 영향 아래 예언의 어조로 터져 나오듯, 그가 마침내 성공에 이르기까지 헤쳐 나가야 할 노고와 위험에 대한 어두운 암시를 던졌다. 그녀는 격언이 된 다음의 격려의 말로 끝맺었다. \"재난에 굴하지 말고, 더욱 용감하게 앞으로 나아가라.\"[30] 아이네아스는 자신을 기다리는 무엇에든 대비가 되어 있노라 대답했다. 그에게는 단 한 가지 청이 있었다. 꿈속에서 아버지 안키세스와 의논하기 위해, 그로부터 자신과 자기 종족의 앞날에 대한 계시를 받기 위해 죽은 자들의 거처를 찾으라는 지시를 받은 터라, 그는 그 과업을 이룰 수 있도록 그녀의 도움을 청했다. 시빌라가 대답했다. \"아베르누스로 내려가는 것은 쉽소. 하데스[로마의 〈플루톤〉]의 문은 밤낮으로 열려 있으니까. 그러나 발걸음을 되돌려 위쪽의 대기로 돌아오는 것, 그것이 바로 수고요, 그것이 바로 어려움이오.\"[31] 그녀는 숲에서 황금 가지가 달린 나무 한 그루를 찾으라고 일러 주었다. 이 가지는 꺾어서 페르세포네[로마의 〈프로세르피나〉]에게 바칠 선물로 가져가야 했으니, 운명이 호의를 베푼다면 그것은 손에 순순히 잡혀 모체인 줄기에서 떨어져 나올 것이나, 그렇지 않다면 어떤 힘으로도 그것을 떼어 낼 수 없었다. 떼어 내고 나면 또 다른 가지가 그 자리를 이을 것이었다.[32]\n\n아이네아스는 시빌라의 지시를 따랐다. 그의 어머니 아프로디테는 자신의 비둘기 두 마리를 보내어 그의 앞에서 날며 길을 안내하게 했고, 그것들의 도움으로 그는 나무를 찾아 가지를 꺾었으며, 그것을 들고 서둘러 시빌라에게로 돌아왔다.\n\n————\n\n# THE INFERNAL REGIONS\n\n우리가 이 연작의 첫머리에서 세계의 창조에 관한 이교도의 설명을 소개했던 것과 마찬가지로, 이제 그 끝맺음에 다가서면서 죽은 자들의 영역을 한 폭의 그림처럼 펼쳐 보이려 한다. 그것을 묘사한 이는 이교도들 가운데 가장 깨우친 시인의 한 사람으로, 그들이 가장 존경했던 철학자들로부터 그 가르침을 길어 올린 인물이다. 베르길리우스가 이 거처로 들어가는 입구를 위치시킨 지역은, 어쩌면 지상 어디에서도 찾아보기 힘들 만큼 무시무시하고 초자연적인 관념을 불러일으키기에 가장 적합한 곳일 것이다. 그곳은 베수비오 화산 인근의 화산 지대로, 온 들판이 갈라진 틈으로 쪼개져 있고, 그 틈에서 유황불이 솟아오르며, 갇혀 있던 수증기가 땅을 뒤흔들고, 땅속 깊은 곳에서 알 수 없는 소리가 흘러나오는 곳이다. 아베르누스 호수는 사화산의 분화구를 메우고 있는 것으로 여겨진다. 그것은 둥글고, 너비가 반 마일이며, 매우 깊고, 높은 둑으로 둘러싸여 있는데, 베르길리우스의 시대에는 이 둑이 음울한 숲으로 뒤덮여 있었다. 호수의 물에서는 독기 어린 수증기가 피어올라, 둑에는 아무런 생명도 깃들지 못하고, 어떤 새도 그 위를 날지 못한다. 시인에 따르면 바로 이곳에 저승의 영역으로 들어가는 동굴이 있었으며, 바로 이곳에서 아이네아스가 저승의 신들, 곧 페르세포네[로마의 〈프로세르피나〉]와 헤카테와 복수의 여신들에게 제물을 바쳤다. 그러자 땅속에서 우르릉거리는 소리가 들려왔고, 언덕 꼭대기의 숲이 흔들렸으며, 개들이 짖어 대는 소리가 신들이 다가옴을 알렸다. \"이제,\" 하고 시빌라가 말했다. \"그대의 용기를 끌어모으시오. 그것이 필요할 터이니.\" 그녀가 동굴로 내려가자 아이네아스가 그 뒤를 따랐다. 지옥의 문턱에 이르기 전, 그들은 한 무리의 존재들 사이를 지나갔으니, 그것들은 슬픔과 복수심에 불타는 근심, 창백한 질병과 우울한 노령, 죄악으로 이끄는 공포와 굶주림, 고역과 빈곤과 죽음으로 헤아려지는 것들이었다. 모두가 보기에 끔찍한 형상이었다. 복수의 여신들은 그곳에 자기네 침상을 펴 놓았고, 머리카락이 살무사로 되어 피 묻은 띠로 묶여 있는 불화의 여신도 있었다. 이곳에는 또한 괴물들이 있었으니, 백 개의 팔을 가진 브리아레오스와, 쉭쉭거리는 히드라들과, 불을 내뿜는 키마이라들이었다. 아이네아스는 그 광경에 몸서리치며 칼을 빼어 내리치려 했으나, 시빌라가 그를 만류했다. 이윽고 그들은 검은 강 코키토스에 이르렀으며, 그곳에서 뱃사공 카론을 만났다. 그는 늙고 추레했으나 억세고 정정한 자로, 온갖 종류의 승객들을 자기 배에 받아들이고 있었으니, 도량 넓은 영웅들이며 소년들이며 시집가지 않은 처녀들이, 가을에 떨어지는 나뭇잎처럼, 또는 겨울이 다가옴에 따라 남쪽으로 날아가는 새 떼처럼 수없이 많았다. 그들은 건너가기를 다투며 맞은편 기슭에 닿기를 갈망하고 있었다. 그러나 엄혹한 뱃사공은 자기가 고른 자들만을 태우고, 나머지는 모두 되돌려 보냈다. 아이네아스는 그 광경에 의아해하며 시빌라에게 물었다. \"어찌하여 이렇게 가려 뽑는 것이오?\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"배에 태워지는 자들은 마땅한 매장의 의례를 치른 자들의 혼령이오. 매장되지 못한 채 남아 있는 나머지 무리는 강을 건너는 것이 허락되지 않아, 백 년 동안 떠돌며 기슭을 이리저리 헤매다가, 마침내 건너편으로 옮겨지는 것이오.\" 아이네아스는 폭풍 속에서 죽은 자기 동료들 가운데 몇몇을 떠올리며 슬퍼했다. 그 순간 그는 배 밖으로 떨어져 물에 빠져 죽은 자기 키잡이 팔리누루스를 보았다. 그는 그에게 말을 건네며 불행의 까닭을 물었다. 팔리누루스는 키가 휩쓸려 떨어져 나갔고, 거기에 매달려 있던 자기도 키와 함께 휩쓸려 갔다고 대답했다. 그는 아이네아스에게 손을 내밀어 자기를 맞은편 기슭으로 함께 데려가 달라고 간절히 애원했다. 그러나 시빌라는 그가 그렇게 플루톤의 율법을 어기려 한다고 꾸짖었다. 다만 그녀는, 그의 시신이 파도에 떠밀려 닿은 기슭의 사람들이 갖가지 전조에 의해 마음이 움직여 그것을 마땅히 매장하게 될 것이며, 그 곶이 팔리누루스 곶이라는 이름을 지니게 될 것이라고 일러 주어 그를 위로했으니, 그 이름은 오늘날까지도 그렇게 불린다. 이 말로 위로받은 팔리누루스를 뒤로하고 그들은 배에 다가갔다. 카론은 앞으로 나아오는 전사에게 매서운 눈길을 고정한 채, 살아 있고 무장을 한 그가 무슨 권리로 그 기슭에 다가오느냐고 따져 물었다. 이에 시빌라는 그들이 아무런 폭력도 저지르지 않을 것이며, 아이네아스의 유일한 목적은 자기 아버지를 만나는 것이라고 대답하고는, 마침내 황금 가지를 내보였다. 그것을 보자 카론의 노여움이 누그러졌고, 그는 서둘러 자기 배를 기슭으로 돌려 그들을 태웠다. 형체 없는 혼령들의 가벼운 짐만을 싣도록 만들어진 그 배는 영웅의 무게에 신음했다. 그들은 곧 맞은편 기슭으로 옮겨졌다. 그곳에서 그들은 목에 뱀들이 곤두선 머리가 셋 달린 개 케르베로스와 마주쳤다. 그것이 세 목구멍으로 한껏 짖어 대자, 시빌라가 약을 탄 과자를 던져 주었고, 케르베로스는 그것을 게걸스레 삼키고는 자기 굴에 길게 드러누워 잠이 들었다. 아이네아스와 시빌라는 뭍으로 뛰어내렸다. 그들의 귀를 처음으로 울린 소리는, 삶의 문턱에서 죽어 간 어린아이들의 울음이었고, 그 곁에는 거짓된 고발로 목숨을 잃은 자들이 있었다. 미노스가 그들을 다스리는 재판관으로 군림하며, 저마다의 행적을 살핀다. 그다음 부류는 삶을 미워하여 죽음에서 피난처를 구하고 제 손으로 목숨을 끊은 자들이었다. 오, 그들은 이제 다시 삶으로 돌아갈 수만 있다면 빈곤이든 고역이든 그 어떤 고난이든 얼마나 기꺼이 견뎌 내려 하겠는가! 그다음에는 슬픔의 영역이 자리하고 있었으니, 외딴 오솔길로 나뉘어 도금양 숲 사이로 이어져 있었다. 이곳에는 보답받지 못한 사랑의 제물이 된 자들이 떠돌았으니, 죽음 그 자체로도 그 고통에서 풀려나지 못한 자들이었다. 그 가운데서 아이네아스는 아직도 상처가 생생한 디도의 형상을 알아본 것 같았다. 어슴푸레한 빛 속에서 그는 한순간 긴가민가했으나, 가까이 다가가 보고는 그것이 정녕 그녀임을 알아차렸다. 그의 눈에서 눈물이 흘렀고, 그는 사랑의 어조로 그녀에게 말을 건넸다. \"불행한 디도여! 그렇다면 그대가 목숨을 잃었다는 소문이 참이었단 말이오? 그리고, 아아, 내가 그 까닭이었단 말이오? 내가 그대를 떠난 것이 마음 내키지 않은 일이었으며 유피테르의 명령에 따른 것이었음을, 나는 신들을 증인 삼아 맹세하오. 내가 떠나는 것이 그대에게 그토록 값비싼 대가를 치르게 하리라고는 도저히 믿을 수 없었소. 부디 멈추어, 마지막 작별 인사만이라도 내게 거절하지 말아 주오.\" 그녀는 한순간 얼굴을 돌린 채 눈을 땅에 고정하고 서 있다가, 바위처럼 그의 호소에 아무런 감응도 없이 말없이 지나가 버렸다. 아이네아스는 한동안 그 뒤를 따라가다가, 무거운 마음으로 동행에게 돌아와 다시 길을 갔다.\n\n그들은 다음으로 전투에서 쓰러진 영웅들이 떠도는 들판으로 들어섰다. 이곳에서 그들은 수많은 그리스와 트로이아 전사들의 혼령을 보았다. 트로이아인들은 그의 주위로 몰려들어, 아무리 그를 보아도 만족할 줄을 몰랐다. 그들은 그가 온 까닭을 물으며 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 질문을 퍼부었다. 그러나 그리스인들은 음침한 대기 사이로 번쩍이는 그의 갑옷을 보고는 그 영웅을 알아보았고, 공포에 사로잡혀 등을 돌리고 달아났으니, 트로이아의 들판에서 늘 그러했던 것과 같았다.\n\n아이네아스는 트로이아의 벗들과 오래도록 머물고 싶었으나, 시빌라가 그를 재촉해 떼어 놓았다. 그들은 다음으로 길이 갈라지는 곳에 이르렀으니, 한 갈래는 엘리시온[엘리시움]으로 이어지고, 다른 한 갈래는 단죄받은 자들의 영역으로 이어져 있었다. 아이네아스는 한쪽에서 거대한 도시의 성벽을 보았는데, 그 둘레로는 플레게톤이 불타는 물결을 굽이치며 흐르고 있었다. 그의 앞에는 신들도 인간들도 뚫을 수 없는 강철의 문이 있었다. 문 곁에는 무쇠 탑이 서 있었고, 그 위에서 복수의 여신 티시포네가 망을 보고 있었다. 도시에서는 신음 소리와 채찍질 소리, 쇠가 삐걱거리는 소리, 사슬이 절그럭거리는 소리가 들려왔다. 아이네아스는 공포에 사로잡혀 안내자에게, 자기가 듣는 저 소리를 자아내는 형벌을 받는 자들은 어떤 죄를 지은 것이냐고 물었다. 시빌라가 대답했다. \"이곳은 라다만토스의 재판정으로, 그는 죄를 저지른 자가 헛되이 빈틈없이 감추었다고 여긴 생전의 죄들을 환히 밝혀내오. 티시포네는 전갈 채찍을 휘둘러, 죄인을 자기 자매인 복수의 여신들에게 넘겨주오.\" 그 순간 끔찍한 굉음과 함께 청동 문이 활짝 열렸고, 아이네아스는 그 안에서 머리가 쉰 개 달린 히드라가 입구를 지키고 있는 것을 보았다. 시빌라는 그에게, 타르타로스의 심연이 깊디깊게 내려가, 그 가장 깊은 구석은 하늘이 그들의 머리 위로 높은 만큼이나 그들의 발밑으로 까마득하다고 일러 주었다. 이 구렁의 바닥에는 신들에 맞서 싸운 티탄 일족이 엎드려 있다. 또한 살모네우스도 그곳에 있으니, 그는 감히 유피테르와 겨루려 들어 청동 다리를 놓고 그 위로 전차를 몰아 그 소리가 천둥과 비슷하게 들리도록 했으며, 번개를 흉내 내어 백성들에게 불타는 횃불을 던졌다. 그러다 유피테르가 진짜 벼락으로 그를 내리쳐, 필멸자의 무기와 신의 무기가 어떻게 다른지를 가르쳤다. 이곳에는 또한 거인 티티오스가 있으니, 그 몸뚱이가 어찌나 거대한지 드러누우면 아홉 에이커에 걸쳐 뻗어 있고, 독수리 한 마리가 그의 간을 쪼아 먹는데, 먹히는 족족 다시 자라나, 그의 형벌은 끝이 없을 것이다.\n\n아이네아스는 진수성찬이 가득한 식탁 앞에 앉은 무리들을 보았는데, 그 곁에는 복수의 여신 하나가 서 있다가, 그들이 맛보려고 들 때마다 입술에서 음식을 낚아채 갔다. 또 다른 자들은 머리 위에 거대한 바위가 매달려 금방이라도 떨어질 듯 위협하는 것을 보며, 끊임없는 두려움에 사로잡혀 있었다. 이들은 제 형제를 미워했거나, 제 부모를 때렸거나, 자기를 믿은 벗들을 속였거나, 부자가 되고도 그 재물을 제 것으로만 움켜쥐고 남에게 한 몫도 나누지 않은 자들이었으니, 마지막 부류가 가장 많았다. 이곳에는 또한 혼인의 맹세를 저버린 자들, 옳지 못한 명분을 위해 싸운 자들, 자기를 고용한 이들에게 충실하지 못한 자들도 있었다. 이곳에는 황금을 받고 제 나라를 팔아넘긴 자가 있었고, 또 다른 자는 율법을 왜곡하여 오늘은 이렇게 내일은 저렇게 말하도록 만든 자도 있었다.\n\n익시온이 그곳에 있었으니, 쉴 새 없이 도는 수레바퀴 둘레에 묶여 있었다. 또 시시포스도 있었으니, 그의 일은 거대한 바위를 언덕 꼭대기까지 굴려 올리는 것이었으나, 가파른 정상에 거의 다다랐을 때, 어떤 갑작스러운 힘에 떠밀린 바위가 다시 곤두박질쳐 평지로 굴러떨어지는 것이었다. 그는 다시금 그것에 매달려 애쓰니, 땀이 그의 지친 사지를 흠뻑 적셨으나, 모두가 헛수고였다. 그곳에는 탄탈로스가 있었으니, 그는 못 속에 서서 턱이 수면과 나란할 정도였으나, 갈증으로 타들어 가면서도 그것을 달랠 것을 찾지 못했다. 마시려고 백발의 머리를 숙일 때마다 물이 달아나, 그의 발치의 땅을 온통 메마르게 남겨 두었기 때문이다. 열매를 가득 매단 키 큰 나무들이 그를 향해 머리를 숙였으니, 배며 석류며 사과며 달콤한 무화과였다. 그러나 그가 느닷없이 손을 뻗어 그것들을 움켜잡으려 들면, 바람이 그것들을 손이 닿지 않을 만큼 높이 휘몰아 갔다.\n\n이윽고 시빌라는 아이네아스에게, 이 음울한 영역에서 발길을 돌려 축복받은 자들의 도시를 찾아갈 때가 되었다고 일러 주었다. 그들은 어둠의 중간 지대를 가로질러, 행복한 자들이 머무는 숲, 곧 엘리시온의 들판에 다다랐다. 그들은 더 자유로운 공기를 들이마셨고, 모든 사물이 자줏빛에 감싸여 있는 것을 보았다. 그 지역에는 그 자체의 해와 별들이 있었다. 그곳의 거주자들은 갖가지 방식으로 즐거움을 누리고 있었으니, 더러는 풀밭 잔디에서 힘이나 솜씨를 겨루는 경기로, 더러는 춤추거나 노래하며 흥겨워했다. 오르페우스는 자기 리라의 줄을 켜며 황홀한 소리를 불러냈다. 이곳에서 아이네아스는 트로이아라는 나라를 세운 시조들, 더 행복한 시절에 살았던 도량 넓은 영웅들을 보았다. 그는 이제 쓰임을 잃고 쉬고 있는 전차들과 번쩍이는 무구들을 감탄에 차 바라보았다. 창들은 땅에 꽂혀 있었고, 멍에를 벗은 말들은 들판을 누비고 다녔다. 옛 영웅들이 생전에 화려한 갑옷과 늠름한 군마에 대해 느꼈던 것과 똑같은 긍지가, 이곳에서도 그들과 함께하고 있었다. 그는 또 다른 무리가 잔치를 벌이며 음악의 가락에 귀 기울이고 있는 것을 보았다. 그들은 월계수 숲에 있었으니, 그곳에서 큰 강 포가 발원하여 인간들 사이로 흘러나간다. 이곳에는 제 나라의 대의를 위해 입은 상처로 쓰러진 자들이 머물렀고, 거룩한 사제들도, 아폴론에 어울릴 만한 생각을 읊은 시인들도, 그리고 유용한 기예에서 이룬 발견으로 삶을 북돋우고 꾸미는 데 이바지하여 인류에게 봉사함으로써 자기 기억을 축복받게 한 자들도 있었다. 그들은 이마에 눈처럼 흰 띠를 두르고 있었다. 시빌라는 이들 가운데 한 무리에게 말을 건네어, 안키세스를 어디에서 찾을 수 있느냐고 물었다. 그들은 그를 찾아갈 곳을 일러 주었고, 그들은 곧 푸르른 골짜기에서 그를 찾아냈으니, 그곳에서 그는 자기 후손들의 대열과 그들의 운명, 다가올 시대에 이루어질 그들의 훌륭한 행적을 헤아려 보고 있었다. 그는 아이네아스가 다가오는 것을 알아보자, 두 손을 그에게 내밀었고, 눈물이 거침없이 흘러내렸다. \"마침내 왔구나,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"오래도록 기다렸거니와, 그 많은 위험을 다 겪고 난 너를 내가 다시 보는구나? 오, 내 아들아, 네 앞길을 지켜보며 내가 너를 위해 얼마나 마음을 졸였는지!\" 이에 아이네아스가 대답했다. \"오, 아버지! 아버지의 모습이 늘 제 앞에 있어 저를 이끌고 지켜 주었습니다.\" 그러고 나서 그는 아버지를 품에 끌어안으려 했으나, 그의 두 팔은 실체 없는 형상만을 감싸 안았을 뿐이다.\n\n아이네아스는 자기 앞에 드넓은 골짜기가 펼쳐져 있음을 알아보았으니, 나무들이 바람에 부드럽게 흔들리는 고요한 풍경이었고, 그 사이로 레테 강이 흐르고 있었다. 강기슭을 따라 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 무리가 떠돌고 있었으니, 여름 공기 속의 벌레 떼만큼이나 수가 많았다. 아이네아스는 놀라며 이들이 누구냐고 물었다. 안키세스가 대답했다. \"이들은 때가 되면 육신을 부여받게 될 혼령들이다. 그동안 이들은 레테의 기슭에 머물며, 자기네 옛 삶에 대한 망각을 들이마신다.\" \"오, 아버지!\" 하고 아이네아스가 말했다. \"이 고요한 거처를 떠나 위쪽 세계로 가기를 바랄 만큼 삶을 사랑하는 자가 있다는 것이 가당키나 한 일입니까?\" 안키세스는 창조의 섭리를 설명하여 대답했다. 그가 그에게 일러 주기를, 창조주는 본디 혼령을 이루는 재료를 불과 공기와 흙과 물이라는 네 원소로 만들었으며, 이것들이 결합하면 가장 빼어난 부분인 불의 형태를 띠어 불꽃이 되었다고 했다. 이 재료는 씨앗처럼 천체들, 곧 해와 달과 별들 사이에 흩뿌려졌다. 이 씨앗으로 하급 신들이 인간과 그 밖의 모든 동물을 지어내되, 그것을 갖가지 비율의 흙과 섞었으니, 그로 인해 그 순수함이 합금처럼 흐려지고 떨어뜨려졌다. 그리하여 그 구성에서 흙이 더 우세할수록 그 개체는 덜 순수하며, 우리는 다 자란 육신을 가진 남자와 여자가 어린 시절의 순수함을 지니지 못함을 본다. 그러므로 육신과 혼령의 결합이 지속된 시간에 비례하여, 그 영적인 부분이 더럽혀지는 것이다. 이 더러움은 죽은 뒤에 씻어 내야 하니, 그것은 바람의 흐름 속에서 혼령들을 바람 쐬게 하거나, 물에 잠기게 하거나, 불로 그 더러움을 태워 없앰으로써 이루어진다. 안키세스가 자기도 그중 하나라고 넌지시 비친 소수의 몇몇은, 곧바로 엘리시온으로 받아들여져 그곳에 머물게 된다. 그러나 나머지는, 흙의 더러움이 씻겨 나간 뒤에, 새 육신을 부여받아 다시 삶으로 돌려보내지는데, 이들은 레테의 물로 옛 삶에 대한 기억이 말끔히 씻겨 나간 상태이다. 그러나 그 가운데는 여전히 너무도 철저히 타락하여 인간의 육신을 맡기기에 합당치 못한 자들이 있으니, 이들은 짐승으로, 곧 사자며 호랑이며 고양이며 개며 원숭이 따위로 만들어진다. 이것이 옛사람들이 메템프시코시스, 곧 혼령의 윤회라 일컬은 것이다. 이 교리는 오늘날에도 인도의 토착민들이 받드는 바이니, 그들은 가장 하찮은 동물의 목숨이라도 차마 끊지 못하는데, 그것이 모습을 바꾼 자기 친족 가운데 하나일지도 모른다고 여기기 때문이다.\n\n안키세스는 이만큼 설명하고 나서, 아이네아스에게 앞으로 태어날 자기 혈통의 사람들을 하나하나 가리켜 보이며, 그들이 세상에서 펼칠 위업을 이야기해 주었다. 그런 다음 그는 현재로 돌아와, 자기 아들에게, 그와 그의 추종자들이 이탈리아에 완전히 자리 잡기까지 그가 이루어야 할 일들을 일러 주었다. 전쟁을 치러야 하고, 싸움을 벌여야 하며, 신붓감을 얻어야 하고, 그 결과로 트로이아라는 나라가 세워져, 그로부터 로마의 권세가 일어나 머지않아 세계의 주인이 되리라는 것이었다.\n\n이윽고 아이네아스와 시빌라는 안키세스에게 작별을 고하고, 시인이 설명하지 않은 어떤 지름길을 통해 위쪽 세계로 돌아왔다.\n엘리시온\n\n우리가 보았듯이, 베르길리우스는 자기 엘리시온을 땅 밑에 두고, 그것을 축복받은 자들의 혼령이 머무는 거처로 정했다. 그러나 호메로스에게서 엘리시온은 죽은 자들의 영역에 속하지 않는다. 그는 그것을 땅의 서쪽, 오케아노스 가까이에 두고, 눈도 추위도 비도 없으며 언제나 제피로스의 상쾌한 산들바람이 부채질하는 행복한 땅으로 묘사한다. 이곳으로 은총받은 영웅들이 죽지 않고 건너와, 라다만토스의 다스림 아래 행복하게 산다. 헤시오도스와 핀다로스의 엘리시온은 서쪽 대양에 있는 축복받은 자들의 섬, 곧 행운의 섬들에 있다. 여기에서 행복한 섬 아틀란티스의 전설이 비롯되었다. 이 더없이 행복한 지역은 온전히 상상의 산물이었을 수도 있으나, 어쩌면 폭풍에 떠밀린 어떤 뱃사람들이 아메리카의 해안을 언뜻 보았던 일에 관한 보고에서 비롯되었을지도 모른다.\n\nJ. R. 로웰은 자기 단시 가운데 하나에서, 현재의 시대를 위해 그 행복한 영역의 특권 몇 가지를 주장한다. 〈과거〉를 향해 그는 이렇게 말한다.\n\n\"네 안에 깃들어 있던 참된 삶이 무엇이었든, 그것은 우리 시대의 핏줄 속에서 뛰노느니라. . . . . . . . 이곳, 우리의 다툼과 근심의 황량한 물결 한가운데, 푸른 〈행운의 섬들〉이 떠 있으니, 거기에 네 영웅의 혼령들이 모두 깃들어 우리의 순교와 노고를 함께 나누느니라. 현재의 순간은 옛 시절을 찬란하게 했던 용맹하고 빼어나고 아름다운 모든 것을 거느리고 나아가느니라.\"\n\n밀턴 또한 「실낙원」 제3권 568행에서 같은 우화를 넌지시 언급한다.\n\n\"옛적에 이름난 저 헤스페리데스의 동산처럼, 행운의 들판과 숲과 꽃 피는 골짜기들, 세 곱절 행복한 섬들.\"\n\n그리고 제2권에서 그는 에레보스의 강들을 그 이름이 그리스어로 지니는 뜻에 따라 묘사한다.\n\n\"치 떨리는 스틱스, 죽음 같은 증오의 강물, 슬픔에 잠긴 아케론, 검고 깊은 비탄의 강. 처량한 강물 위로 들려오는 통곡에서 이름 얻은 코키토스. 사나운 플레게톤, 그 격류 같은 불의 물결은 분노로 타오른다. 이것들에서 멀리 떨어져, 느리고 고요한 강물, 망각의 강 레테가 그 물결의 미궁을 굽이쳐 흐르니, 그 물을 마시는 자는 곧 자기의 옛 처지와 존재를 잊고, 기쁨도 슬픔도, 쾌락도 고통도 모두 잊는다.\"\n시빌라\n\n# THE SIBYL\n\n아이네아스와 시빌라가 지상으로 돌아가는 길을 더듬어 가는 동안, 그가 그녀에게 말했다. \"그대가 여신이든, 아니면 신들의 사랑을 받는 필멸자이든, 나는 그대를 언제나 경외하며 받들 것이오. 내가 위쪽 대기에 이르면 그대의 명예를 기려 신전을 짓게 하고, 내 손수 제물을 바치겠소.\" \"나는 여신이 아니오,\" 하고 시빌라가 말했다. \"나는 희생제물이나 봉헌물을 받을 자격이 없소. 나는 필멸자요. 그래도 아폴론의 사랑을 받아들일 수 있었다면 나는 불멸의 몸이 되었을지도 모르오. 그는 내가 자기 사람이 되기로 응한다면 내 소원을 이루어 주겠다고 약속했소. 나는 모래를 한 줌 쥐어 내밀며 말했소. '내 손에 든 모래알의 수만큼 많은 생일을 보게 해 주소서.' 안타깝게도 나는 시들지 않는 젊음을 청하는 것을 잊고 말았소. 내가 그의 사랑을 받아들일 수 있었다면 그것도 그가 들어주었을 터이나, 내가 거절한 데 노여워하여 그는 내가 늙어 가도록 내버려 두었소. 내 젊음과 젊은 날의 기운은 오래전에 사라졌소. 나는 칠백 년을 살아왔으니, 모래알의 수를 채우려면 아직 삼백 번의 봄과 삼백 번의 추수를 더 보아야 하오. 해가 거듭될수록 내 몸은 쪼그라들어, 머지않아 나는 눈에 보이지 않게 되겠지만, 내 목소리는 남을 것이며, 후세는 내 말을 존중할 것이오.\"\n\n시빌라의 이 맺음말은 그녀의 예언 능력을 가리킨 것이었다. 그녀는 자기 동굴에서, 나무들에서 따 모은 잎사귀에 개개인의 이름과 운명을 적어 두곤 했다. 이렇게 적은 잎사귀들은 동굴 안에 차례대로 정돈되어, 그녀를 찾는 신봉자들이 살펴볼 수 있었다. 그러나 문이 열리는 순간 행여 바람이 밀려 들어와 잎사귀들을 흩어 놓으면, 시빌라는 그것들을 다시 추스르는 데 아무런 도움도 주지 않았고, 그리하여 그 신탁은 돌이킬 수 없이 사라져 버렸다.\n\n시빌라에 관한 다음의 전설은 그보다 후대에 자리한다. 타르퀴니우스 가운데 한 사람이 다스리던 시절, 한 여인이 왕 앞에 나타나 책 아홉 권을 팔겠다고 내놓았다. 왕이 사기를 거절하자, 그 여인은 물러가서 책 세 권을 불태웠고, 다시 돌아와 남은 책들을 아홉 권에 대해 청했던 것과 똑같은 값에 내놓았다. 왕이 또다시 그것들을 물리치자, 여인은 책 세 권을 더 불태운 뒤 돌아와, 남은 세 권을 두고 앞서 아홉 권에 청했던 것과 똑같은 값을 요구했다. 그러자 왕의 호기심이 일었고, 그는 그 책들을 사들였다. 그 책들에는 로마라는 나라의 운명이 담겨 있는 것으로 드러났다. 그것들은 유피테르 카피톨리누스 신전에 돌로 만든 궤 속에 보관되었으며, 그 일을 위해 특별히 임명된 관리들만이 살펴보도록 허락되었으니, 그들은 큰일이 있을 때마다 그것들을 참조하여 그 신탁을 백성에게 풀이해 주었다.\n\n시빌라는 여럿이 있었으나, 오비디우스와 베르길리우스가 글로 쓴 쿠마이의 시빌라가 그 가운데 가장 이름난 자이다. 그녀의 생애가 천 년이나 길게 늘어났다는 오비디우스의 이야기는, 여러 시빌라가 실은 하나의 동일한 인물이 다시 나타난 것일 뿐임을 나타내려는 뜻이었는지도 모른다.\n\n영은 「밤의 상념」에서 시빌라를 넌지시 언급한다. 〈세속의 지혜〉에 대해 말하며 그는 이렇게 적는다.\n\n\"그것이 다가올 운명을 헤아린대도 모두가 잎사귀에 적힐 뿐, 시빌라처럼 실체 없고 덧없는 행복이라, 첫 바람이 한 번 불면 공기 속으로 스러진다. . . . . . . . 세속의 계략이 시빌라의 잎사귀를 닮았듯, 선한 이의 나날은 시빌라의 책들에 견줄 만하니, 그 수가 줄어들수록 값은 더욱 높아만 가더라.\"\n\n# CAMILLA—EVANDER—NISUS AND EURYALUS—MEZENTIUS—TURNUS\n\n시빌라와 작별하고 자신의 함대로 돌아간 아이네아스는 이탈리아 해안을 따라 항해하다가 티베리스 강 어귀에 닻을 내렸다. 시인은 자신의 영웅을 방랑의 정해진 종착지인 이곳까지 데려왔으니, 이 중대한 순간의 형세를 들려 달라며 무사 여신을 부른다. 사투르누스의 삼대 후손인 라티누스가 이 나라를 다스리고 있었다. 그는 이제 늙었고 아들이 없었으나, 라비니아라는 매력적인 딸 하나를 두고 있었다. 라비니아에게는 이웃의 여러 족장들이 청혼해 왔는데, 그중 하나인 루툴리족의 왕 투르누스가 그녀 부모의 바람에 들어맞는 인물이었다. 그러나 라티누스는 꿈속에서 아버지 파우누스로부터, 라비니아의 정해진 남편은 외국 땅에서 오게 되리라는 경고를 받았다. 그 결합으로부터 세계를 정복할 운명을 지닌 종족이 태어나리라는 것이었다.\n\n우리 독자들은 하르피이아이와의 싸움에서 그 반인반조의 새들 가운데 하나가 트로이아인들에게 끔찍한 고난을 예언했음을 기억할 것이다. 특히 그녀는 방랑이 끝나기 전에 그들이 굶주림에 쫓겨 자신들의 식탁마저 먹게 되리라고 예고했었다. 이제 그 전조가 실현되었다. 그들이 풀밭에 앉아 변변찮은 식사를 하면서, 사람들이 딱딱한 비스킷을 무릎에 올려놓고 그 위에 숲에서 채집한 것이라면 무엇이든 얹어 먹었기 때문이다. 위에 얹은 것을 다 먹고 나자 그들은 마지막으로 빵 껍질까지 먹어 치웠다. 그것을 본 소년 이울루스가 장난스레 말했다. \"보세요, 우리가 우리 식탁을 먹고 있어요.\" 아이네아스는 그 말을 듣고 그것을 전조로 받아들였다. \"약속의 땅이여, 만세!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"이곳이 우리의 거처요, 이곳이 우리의 나라다.\" 그러고 나서 그는 이 땅의 현재 주민이 누구이며 그 통치자가 누구인지 알아보기 위한 조치를 취했다. 선발된 백 명이 선물과 더불어 우호와 동맹을 청하는 뜻을 지니고 라티누스의 마을로 파견되었다. 그들은 그곳으로 가서 호의적인 영접을 받았다. 라티누스는 곧 그 트로이아 영웅이야말로 신탁이 예고한 약속의 사위가 틀림없다고 결론지었다. 그는 기꺼이 동맹을 허락하고, 자기 마구간의 말에 사절들을 태워 선물과 우호의 전갈을 가득 실어 돌려보냈다.\n\n헤라[로마의 〈유노〉]는 트로이아인들에게 이렇듯 일이 순조롭게 풀리는 것을 보고 묵은 적의가 되살아남을 느꼈으니, 에레보스에서 알렉토를 불러내어 불화를 일으키도록 보냈다. 복수의 여신은 먼저 왕비 아마타를 사로잡아, 그 새로운 동맹에 온갖 방법으로 반대하도록 부추겼다. 그런 다음 알렉토는 투르누스의 도시로 서둘러 가서, 늙은 여사제의 모습을 하고는 이방인들이 도착한 사실과 그들의 군주가 그의 신부를 빼앗으려 한다는 것을 알렸다. 다음으로 그녀는 트로이아인들의 진영으로 주의를 돌렸다. 그곳에서 그녀는 소년 이울루스와 그 동료들이 사냥으로 즐기고 있는 것을 보았다. 그녀는 개들의 후각을 예민하게 만들어, 왕의 목자인 티르레우스의 딸 실비아가 아끼는 길든 수사슴을 덤불에서 몰아내게 했다. 이울루스의 손에서 날아간 투창이 그 짐승에게 상처를 입혔고, 사슴은 가까스로 남은 힘으로 집으로 달려가 여주인의 발치에서 죽었다. 그녀의 울부짖음과 눈물에 그녀의 형제들과 목자들이 분노하여, 손에 잡히는 무기란 무기는 모두 집어 들고 사냥하던 무리를 맹렬히 공격했다. 이들은 동료들의 보호를 받았고, 결국 목자들은 두 명을 잃고 격퇴되었다.\n\n이 일들은 전쟁의 폭풍을 일으키기에 충분했으니, 왕비와 투르누스와 농부들이 모두 늙은 왕에게 이방인들을 나라에서 몰아내라고 다그쳤다. 그는 할 수 있는 한 버텼으나, 자신의 반대가 소용없음을 깨닫고는 마침내 굴복하여 은거지로 물러났다.\n\n# OPENING THE GATES OF JANUS\n\n이 나라의 관습에 따르면, 전쟁을 일으키게 될 때면 최고 행정관이 관복을 차려입고 엄숙한 의식과 함께 야누스 신전의 문을 여는 것이 관례였는데, 이 문은 평화가 이어지는 동안에는 닫혀 있었다. 이제 백성들이 늙은 왕에게 그 엄숙한 의식을 거행하라고 다그쳤으나, 그는 거부했다. 그들이 다투는 사이에 헤라가 친히 하늘에서 내려와 거역할 수 없는 힘으로 그 문을 내리쳐 부서뜨려 활짝 열어젖혔다. 곧바로 온 나라가 불길에 휩싸였다. 백성들이 사방에서 몰려들어 오직 전쟁만을 외쳐 댔다.\n\n투르누스가 모두에게 지도자로 인정받았고, 다른 이들이 동맹으로 가세했는데, 그 우두머리는 메젠티우스였다. 그는 용맹하고 유능한 군인이었으나 가증스러울 만큼 잔인했다. 그는 이웃 도시들 가운데 하나의 우두머리였으나 백성들이 그를 쫓아냈다. 그와 함께한 이는 그의 아들 라우수스로, 그보다 나은 아비를 둘 만한 너그러운 젊은이였다.\n\n아르테미스가 아끼는 카밀라는 아마존족의 방식을 따르는 여자 사냥꾼이자 전사로, 자기와 같은 여성들 가운데 선발된 자들을 포함한 기마 추종자들의 무리를 거느리고 와서 투르누스의 편에 섰다. 이 처녀는 손가락을 실패나 베틀에 길들인 적이 없었고, 다만 전쟁의 노고를 견디는 법과 바람보다 빠르게 달리는 법을 배웠다. 그녀는 곡식을 짓밟지 않고 그 위를 달릴 수 있을 것 같았고, 발을 적시지 않고 물 위를 스쳐 지날 수 있을 것만 같았다. 카밀라의 내력은 처음부터 남달랐다. 그녀의 아버지 메타부스는 내란으로 자기 도시에서 쫓겨나, 달아나는 길에 갓난 딸을 데리고 갔다. 그가 숲을 가로질러 달아나는데 적들이 바짝 뒤쫓았고, 그는 아마제누스 강기슭에 이르렀으니, 강물은 비로 불어나 건널 길을 막은 듯했다. 그는 잠시 멈췄다가 어떻게 할지 결심했다. 그는 나무껍질 띠로 갓난아이를 자기 창에 묶고, 그 무기를 치켜든 손에 가늠하며 아르테미스에게 이렇게 아뢰었다. \"숲의 여신이여! 이 아이를 당신께 바칩니다.\" 그러고는 짐을 실은 그 무기를 맞은편 기슭으로 던졌다. 창은 울부짖는 물살을 가로질러 날아갔다. 추격자들은 이미 그에게 들이닥쳤으나, 그는 강물에 뛰어들어 헤엄쳐 건너서, 갓난아이를 무사히 실은 창을 맞은편에서 찾아냈다. 그때부터 그는 목자들 사이에서 살며 딸을 숲의 기예 속에서 길렀다. 아이일 적에 그녀는 활을 쓰고 투창을 던지는 법을 배웠다. 투석기로 그녀는 두루미나 들고니를 떨어뜨릴 수 있었다. 그녀의 옷은 호랑이 가죽이었다. 많은 어머니들이 그녀를 며느리로 삼고자 했으나, 그녀는 아르테미스에게 충실하여 혼인이라는 생각을 물리쳤다.\n\n이러한 자들이 아이네아스에 맞서 늘어선 만만찮은 동맹이었다. 밤이었고, 그는 탁 트인 하늘 아래 강기슭에 몸을 뻗고 잠들어 있었다. 강의 신, 티베리스 아버지가 버드나무 위로 머리를 들어 올리고 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. \"오 여신에게서 난 자여, 라티움 왕국의 정해진 주인이여, 이곳이 약속의 땅이며, 여기가 그대의 거처가 될 곳이요, 그대가 오직 충실히 견뎌 내기만 한다면 하늘의 권능들이 품은 적의도 여기서 끝나리라. 멀지 않은 곳에 벗들이 있노라. 그대의 배를 준비하여 내 물길을 거슬러 노를 저어라. 내가 그대를 아르카디아의 족장 에우안데르에게 인도하리니, 그는 오랫동안 투르누스와 루툴리족과 다투어 왔으며 그대의 동맹이 될 채비가 되어 있노라. 일어나라! 헤라에게 서약을 바쳐 그 노여움을 누그러뜨려라. 그대가 승리를 이루거든 그때 나를 생각하라.\" 아이네아스는 잠에서 깨어 그 우호적인 환영에 곧바로 따랐다. 그는 헤라에게 제물을 바치고, 강의 신과 그에게 흘러드는 모든 샘에게 도움을 빌었다. 그러자 처음으로 무장한 전사들을 가득 실은 배가 티베리스 강물 위에 떠올랐다. 강은 그 물결을 잔잔히 가라앉히고 물살이 부드럽게 흐르도록 했으니, 노 젓는 이들의 힘찬 노질에 떠밀려 배들은 강을 거슬러 빠르게 나아갔다.\n\n한낮 무렵 그들은 갓 생겨난 마을의 띄엄띄엄 흩어진 건물들이 보이는 곳에 이르렀으니, 훗날 이곳에서 영광이 하늘에 닿는 자랑스러운 도시 로마가 자라나게 된다. 마침 그날 늙은 왕 에우안데르는 헤라클레스와 모든 신들을 기리는 연례 제전을 거행하고 있었다. 그의 아들 팔라스와 이 작은 공동체의 모든 족장들이 곁에 서 있었다. 그들은 숲 가까이로 미끄러져 오는 큰 배를 보고 그 광경에 놀라 식탁에서 일어났다. 그러나 팔라스는 제전이 중단되는 것을 막고, 무기를 집어 들고 강기슭으로 앞장서 나아갔다. 그는 큰 소리로 그들이 누구이며 무슨 목적으로 왔는지 따져 물었다. 아이네아스는 올리브 가지를 내밀며 대답했다. \"우리는 트로이아인이며, 그대들에게는 벗이요 루툴리족에게는 적이오. 우리는 에우안데르를 찾아왔으며, 우리의 무기를 그대들의 무기에 합치고자 하오.\" 그토록 위대한 이름의 울림에 놀란 팔라스는 그들에게 상륙하기를 청했고, 아이네아스가 기슭에 닿자 그의 손을 잡아 오래도록 우정 어린 악수로 쥐고 있었다. 숲을 지나 나아가 그들은 왕과 그 일행에 합류하여 더할 나위 없이 호의적인 영접을 받았다. 식탁에 그들을 위한 자리가 마련되었고, 잔치가 이어졌다.\n\n# INFANT ROME\n\n제전이 끝나자 모두 도시를 향해 움직였다. 나이로 허리가 굽은 왕은 아들과 아이네아스 사이에서 걸으며 둘 중 한쪽의 팔을 번갈아 잡았고, 갖가지 흥겨운 이야기로 길을 짧게 했다. 아이네아스는 기쁜 마음으로 보고 들으며, 그 풍경의 온갖 아름다움을 살피고, 옛 시절에 이름 높았던 영웅들에 관해 많은 것을 알게 되었다. 에우안데르가 말했다. \"이 드넓은 숲은 한때 파우누스들과 님프들, 그리고 나무 자체에서 솟아나 법도 사회적 교양도 없던 거친 인간 종족이 살던 곳이오. 그들은 소에 멍에를 메우는 법도, 수확을 거두는 법도, 지금의 풍요에서 훗날의 궁핍에 대비하는 법도 알지 못했고, 다만 짐승처럼 잎이 무성한 가지를 뜯거나 사냥한 먹이를 게걸스레 먹을 따름이었소. 자식들에게 올림포스에서 쫓겨난 사투르누스가 그들에게 와서 그 사나운 야만인들을 한데 모아 사회를 이루게 하고 법을 내렸을 때, 그들은 그러한 처지였소. 그리하여 평화와 풍요가 뒤따랐기에 그 후로 사람들은 그의 치세를 황금시대라 불러 왔소. 그러나 점차 사뭇 다른 시절이 뒤를 이었고, 황금에 대한 갈증과 피에 대한 갈증이 만연했소. 이 땅은 잇따른 폭군들의 먹잇감이 되었으니, 마침내 운명과 거역할 수 없는 숙명이 내 고향 아르카디아에서 추방된 나를 이곳으로 데려왔소.\"\n\n이렇게 말하고 나서 그는 타르페이아 바위와, 훗날 카피톨리움이 그 온갖 장려함으로 솟아오르게 되나 당시에는 덤불로 뒤덮인 그 거친 자리를 그에게 보여 주었다. 다음으로 그는 허물어진 성벽 몇 곳을 가리키며 말했다. \"여기에는 야누스가 세운 야니쿨룸이 있었고, 저기에는 사투르누스의 도시인 사투르니아가 있었소.\" 이러한 이야기를 나누며 그들은 가난한 에우안데르의 오두막에 이르렀으니, 그곳에서 그들은 지금 자랑스럽고 위풍당당한 포룸이 서 있는 평원 위로 음매 우는 가축 떼가 거니는 것을 보았다. 그들은 안으로 들어갔고, 아이네아스를 위해 침상이 펼쳐졌는데, 잎으로 두둑이 채우고 리비아 곰의 가죽으로 덮은 것이었다.\n\n이튿날 아침, 새벽빛과 그의 나지막한 집 처마 밑 새들의 날카로운 노래에 잠을 깬 늙은 에우안데르가 일어났다. 튜닉을 입고 표범 가죽을 어깨에 두르고, 발에는 샌들을 신고 옆구리에는 자신의 훌륭한 검을 차고서 그는 손님을 찾으러 나섰다. 마스티프 두 마리가 그를 따랐으니, 이것이 그의 모든 수행원이자 호위였다. 그는 충실한 아카테스가 시중드는 영웅을 발견했고, 팔라스도 이내 그들과 합류하자 늙은 왕이 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"고명한 트로이아인이여, 이토록 큰 대의에 우리가 할 수 있는 일은 미미하기 그지없소. 우리 나라는 미약하여, 한편은 강에, 다른 한편은 루툴리족에 둘러싸여 있소. 그러나 나는 그대를 수가 많고 부유한 백성과 동맹 맺게 하려 하니, 운명이 그대를 길한 때에 이리로 데려온 것이오. 에트루리아인들이 강 너머의 땅을 차지하고 있소. 메젠티우스가 그들의 왕이었으니, 잔인함의 괴물이었고 자신의 복수심을 채우려 전대미문의 고문들을 고안해 냈소. 그는 죽은 자를 산 자에게, 손과 손을, 얼굴과 얼굴을 맞대어 붙들어 매고는, 그 비참한 희생자들을 그 끔찍한 포옹 속에서 죽게 내버려 두었소. 마침내 백성들이 그를, 그와 그의 집안을 내쫓았소. 그들은 그의 궁전을 불태우고 그의 벗들을 죽였소. 그는 달아나 투르누스에게 몸을 의탁했으니, 투르누스가 무력으로 그를 비호하고 있소. 에트루리아인들은 그를 마땅한 벌을 받도록 넘기라고 요구하며, 진작에 그 요구를 무력으로 관철하려 했을 것이오. 그러나 그들의 사제들이 그들을 만류하기를, 이 땅의 어떤 토박이도 그들을 승리로 이끌어서는 안 되며 그들의 정해진 지도자는 바다 건너에서 와야 하는 것이 하늘의 뜻이라 하오. 그들은 내게 왕관을 바쳤으나, 나는 그토록 큰일을 떠맡기에는 너무 늙었고, 내 아들은 이 땅에서 태어난 까닭에 선택에서 제외되오. 그대는 가문으로 보나 나이로 보나 무용의 명성으로 보나, 신들이 지목한 자이니, 모습을 드러내기만 하면 곧 그들의 지도자로 환영받을 것이오. 그대와 함께 나는 내 아들이자 내 유일한 희망이요 위안인 팔라스를 보내겠소. 그대 밑에서 그는 전쟁의 기술을 배우고 그대의 위대한 위업을 본받고자 힘쓸 것이오.\"\n\n그러고 나서 왕은 트로이아 족장들을 위해 말을 마련하라 명했고, 아이네아스는 선발된 추종자 무리와 동행하는 팔라스와 함께 말에 올라 에트루리아 도시로 가는 길을 잡았으며, 나머지 일행은 배에 태워 돌려보냈다. 아이네아스와 그의 무리는 무사히 에트루리아 진영에 다다라 타르콘과 그의 동포들에게서 두 팔 벌린 환영을 받았다.\n* 에트루리아 도시: 아이네아스가 향한 곳.\n\n# NISUS AND EURYALUS\n\n그러는 사이에 투르누스는 자신의 부대를 모아 전쟁에 필요한 모든 준비를 갖추었다. 헤라는 이리스를 그에게 보내, 아이네아스가 자리를 비운 틈을 타 트로이아 진영을 기습하라고 부추기는 전갈을 전했다. 그리하여 그 시도가 이루어졌으나, 트로이아인들은 경계를 늦추지 않고 있었으니, 자신이 없는 동안에는 싸우지 말라는 아이네아스의 엄명을 받았던 터라, 그들은 보루 안에 가만히 머물며 자신들을 들판으로 끌어내려는 루툴리족의 온갖 시도에 맞섰다. 밤이 오자, 자기들이 우세하다고 여겨 사기가 한껏 오른 투르누스의 군대는 잔치를 벌이며 즐기다가, 마침내 들판에 몸을 뻗고 마음 놓고 잠들었다.\n\n트로이아인들의 진영에서는 사정이 사뭇 달랐다. 그곳은 온통 경계와 불안과 아이네아스의 귀환을 향한 초조함뿐이었다. 니수스가 진영 어귀에서 보초를 서고 있었고, 군대에서 누구보다 빼어난 용모와 훌륭한 자질로 두드러진 젊은이 에우리알루스가 그와 함께 있었다. 이 둘은 벗이자 무기를 같이하는 형제였다. 니수스가 자기 벗에게 말했다. \"적들이 얼마나 자만하고 부주의한지 자네 눈에 보이는가? 그들의 불빛은 적고 희미하며, 사내들은 모두 술이나 잠에 짓눌린 것 같네. 자네도 알다시피 우리 족장들이 아이네아스에게 사람을 보내 그분의 소식을 얻기를 얼마나 애타게 바라는지 말일세. 지금 나는 적진을 뚫고 나아가 우리 족장을 찾아 나서고 싶은 마음이 강하게 이네. 내가 성공한다면 그 공적의 영광만으로도 내게는 충분한 보답이 될 것이고, 만일 그들이 그 봉사가 그 이상의 무언가에 값한다고 여긴다면 그것을 자네에게 치르게 하면 되네.\"\n\n모험을 향한 애착으로 온통 불타오른 에우리알루스가 대답했다. \"그렇다면 니수스, 자네는 자네의 거사를 나와 함께하기를 마다하겠다는 건가? 그리고 내가 자네 혼자 그런 위험 속으로 들어가게 내버려 두겠는가? 내 용맹한 아버지가 나를 그리 기르지 않으셨고, 내가 아이네아스의 깃발 아래 들어와 명예에 비하면 내 목숨을 하찮게 여기리라 다짐했을 때 나 자신을 위해 그리 계획하지도 않았네.\" 니수스가 대답했다. \"벗이여, 나도 그것을 의심하지 않네. 그러나 자네도 알다시피 그런 거사의 결말은 불확실하니, 내게 무슨 일이 닥치든 나는 자네가 무사하기를 바라네. 자네는 나보다 젊고 앞날이 더 많이 남아 있네. 또한 나는 자네 어머니께 그런 슬픔을 끼치는 까닭이 될 수 없으니, 그분은 아케스테스의 도시에서 다른 부인들과 평화롭게 지내기보다 여기 진영에서 자네와 함께 있기를 택하신 분이 아닌가.\" 에우리알루스가 대답했다. \"더는 말하지 말게. 나를 단념시킬 논거를 찾아 봐야 헛일이네. 나는 자네와 함께 가기로 마음을 굳혔네. 시간을 허비하지 마세.\" 그들은 보초들을 불러 경계를 맡기고 장군의 막사를 찾아갔다. 그들은 수석 장교들이 모여, 자신들의 처지를 어떻게 아이네아스에게 알릴지 의논하고 있는 것을 발견했다. 두 벗의 제안은 기쁘게 받아들여졌으니, 그들 자신은 찬사를 한 몸에 받고 성공할 경우 더없이 후한 보상을 약속받았다. 특히 이울루스가 에우리알루스에게 말을 건네며 그에게 변함없는 우정을 다짐했다. 에우리알루스가 대답했다. \"제게는 청할 은혜가 하나뿐입니다. 제 늙으신 어머니가 저와 함께 이 진영에 계십니다. 저를 위해 어머니는 트로이아 땅을 떠나셨고, 아케스테스의 도시에서 다른 부인들과 함께 남으려 하지 않으셨습니다. 저는 이제 어머니께 작별 인사도 드리지 않고 떠납니다. 어머니의 눈물을 차마 견딜 수 없고, 그 애원을 저버릴 수도 없습니다. 그러니 부디 그분이 비탄에 잠기시거든 위로해 주시기를 간청드립니다. 제게 그것을 약속해 주신다면, 저는 어떤 위험이 닥치든 더욱 담대하게 나아가겠습니다.\" 이울루스와 다른 족장들은 눈물을 흘리며 그의 청을 모두 들어주겠노라 약속했다. \"그대의 어머니는 내 어머니가 될 것이오.\" 이울루스가 말했다. \"그리고 내가 그대에게 약속한 모든 것은, 만일 그대가 돌아와 받지 못하게 되더라도 그분께 어김없이 베풀어질 것이오.\"\n\n두 벗은 진영을 떠나 곧장 적들 한가운데로 뛰어들었다. 그들은 보초도, 배치된 파수꾼도 보지 못했고, 다만 사방에 잠든 병사들이 풀밭과 짐수레 사이에 흩어져 있을 따름이었다. 그 먼 옛날의 전쟁 법도는 용감한 자가 잠든 적을 죽이는 것을 금하지 않았으므로, 두 트로이아인은 지나는 길에 경보를 울리지 않고 죽일 수 있는 적들은 죽였다. 한 막사에서 에우리알루스는 금과 깃털로 번쩍이는 투구를 전리품으로 챙겼다. 그들은 발각되지 않고 적의 대열을 지나왔으나, 이제 갑자기 그들 바로 앞에 한 무리가 나타났으니, 그들의 지휘관 볼스켄스 휘하로 진영을 향해 다가오고 있었다. 에우리알루스의 번쩍이는 투구가 그들의 눈길을 끌었고, 볼스켄스가 그 둘을 불러 세워 누구이며 어디서 왔는지 따져 물었다. 그들은 아무 대답도 하지 않고 숲속으로 뛰어들었다. 기마병들이 그들의 도주를 가로막으려 사방으로 흩어졌다. 니수스는 추격을 따돌리고 위험에서 벗어났으나, 에우리알루스가 보이지 않자 그를 찾으러 되돌아갔다. 그는 다시 숲으로 들어가 이내 사람들의 목소리가 들리는 곳에 이르렀다. 덤불 사이로 내다보니 그 무리 전체가 에우리알루스를 에워싸고 시끄럽게 캐묻고 있었다. 그는 어찌해야 할까? 어떻게 젊은이를 빼낼 것인가, 아니면 차라리 그와 함께 죽는 편이 나을까.\n\n이제 환하게 빛나는 달을 향해 눈을 들어 올리며 그가 말했다. \"여신이여! 제 노력을 도우소서!\" 그러고는 그 무리의 지휘관들 가운데 하나를 겨누어 투창을 던졌으니, 그 등을 맞혀 치명상으로 평원에 쓰러뜨렸다. 그들이 경악하는 가운데 또 하나의 무기가 날아가 무리 가운데 또 한 명이 죽어 쓰러졌다. 지휘관 볼스켄스는 그 화살이 어디서 날아오는지 알지 못한 채, 검을 손에 들고 에우리알루스에게 달려들었다. \"네가 둘 모두의 대가를 치르리라.\" 그가 말하고는 검을 그의 가슴에 찔러 넣으려 하는데, 숨어서 자기 벗의 위급함을 본 니수스가 앞으로 달려 나오며 외쳤다. \"그것은 나였소, 그것은 나였소. 루툴리족이여, 그대들의 검을 내게 겨누시오, 내가 한 일이오. 그는 다만 벗으로서 나를 따랐을 뿐이오.\" 그가 말하는 사이에 검이 떨어져, 에우리알루스의 어여쁜 가슴을 꿰뚫었다. 그의 머리가 어깨 위로 떨어졌으니, 마치 쟁기에 베인 꽃과도 같았다. 니수스는 볼스켄스에게 달려들어 그의 몸에 검을 찔러 넣고는, 그 자신도 곧바로 헤아릴 수 없는 칼질에 쓰러져 죽었다.\n\n에트루리아 동맹군을 거느린 아이네아스는 포위된 자신의 진영을 구할 만한 때에 전장에 다다랐다. 이제 두 군대가 힘에서 거의 대등해지자 전쟁이 본격적으로 시작되었다. 그 모든 세부를 다 담을 자리는 없으니, 우리가 독자들에게 소개해 온 주요 인물들의 운명만을 적어 두기로 한다. 폭군 메젠티우스는 자신이 반란을 일으킨 백성들을 상대로 싸우게 된 것을 알고 야수처럼 날뛰었다. 그는 감히 자기에게 맞서는 자는 모두 죽였고, 그가 나타나는 곳마다 무리를 패주시켰다. 마침내 그는 아이네아스와 마주쳤고, 군대들은 그 결말을 보려고 멈춰 섰다. 메젠티우스가 창을 던졌으나, 아이네아스의 방패를 맞고 빗겨 나가 안토르를 맞혔다. 그는 그리스 태생으로, 고향 도시 아르고스를 떠나 에우안데르를 따라 이탈리아로 온 자였다. 시인은 그에 대해, 그 말을 격언으로 만든 소박한 비애를 담아 이렇게 말한다. \"그는 가엾게도 남을 겨눈 상처로 쓰러져 하늘을 올려다보며, 죽어 가면서 정다운 아르고스를 떠올렸노라.\" 이제 아이네아스가 차례로 자신의 창을 던졌다. 그것은 메젠티우스의 방패를 꿰뚫어 그의 넓적다리에 상처를 입혔다. 그의 아들 라우수스는 그 광경을 차마 견디지 못하고 앞으로 달려 나와 몸을 가로막았으며, 그사이 추종자들이 메젠티우스를 에워싸고 그를 데리고 빠져나갔다. 아이네아스는 검을 라우수스 위로 치켜든 채 내려치기를 미루었으나, 격분한 젊은이가 거세게 밀고 들어오는 통에 어쩔 수 없이 치명적인 일격을 가하고 말았다. 라우수스가 쓰러지자 아이네아스는 연민에 차서 그 위로 몸을 굽혔다. \"가엾은 젊은이여,\" 그가 말했다. \"그대의 칭송에 값할 무엇을 내가 그대에게 해 줄 수 있겠는가? 그대가 자랑으로 삼는 그 무구들은 간직하라, 그리고 그대의 주검이 그대의 벗들에게 돌려져 마땅한 장례의 예우를 받게 되리니 염려하지 말라.\" 이렇게 말하고 그는 겁에 질린 추종자들을 불러 그 주검을 그들의 손에 넘겼다.\n\n그사이 메젠티우스는 강가로 옮겨져 상처를 씻었다. 이내 라우수스의 죽음을 알리는 소식이 그에게 닿았고, 분노와 절망이 힘을 대신했다. 그는 말에 올라 싸움이 가장 치열한 곳으로 돌진하여 아이네아스를 찾았다. 그를 찾아내자 그는 아이네아스 둘레를 빙빙 돌며 투창을 잇따라 던졌고, 아이네아스는 방패로 몸을 가린 채 사방으로 돌아서며 그것들을 막아 냈다. 마침내 메젠티우스가 세 차례 원을 그리며 돌고 난 뒤, 아이네아스가 말의 머리를 정통으로 겨누어 창을 던졌다. 창은 말의 관자놀이를 꿰뚫었고, 말이 쓰러지자 양쪽 군대에서 터져 나온 함성이 하늘을 갈랐다. 메젠티우스는 자비를 청하지 않았고, 다만 자신의 주검이 반란을 일으킨 백성들의 모욕을 면하고 아들과 같은 무덤에 묻히기만을 청했다. 그는 치명적인 일격을 각오 없이 받지 않았으며, 자신의 목숨과 피를 함께 쏟아 냈다.\n\n# PALLAS, CAMILLA, TURNUS\n\n전장의 한쪽에서 이러한 일들이 벌어지는 동안, 다른 한쪽에서는 투르누스가 젊은 팔라스와 마주쳤다. 그토록 기울어진 두 용사 사이의 다툼은 의심할 여지가 없었다. 팔라스는 용감히 싸웠으나 투르누스의 창에 쓰러졌다. 승자는 그 용감한 젊은이가 자기 발치에 죽어 누운 것을 보고 거의 마음이 누그러져, 그의 무구를 벗겨 내는 정복자의 권리를 행사하기를 삼갔다. 다만 금으로 된 장식 단추와 조각으로 꾸민 허리띠 하나만을 취해 자기 몸에 둘렀다. 나머지는 죽은 자의 벗들에게 돌려보냈다.\n\n전투가 끝난 뒤 며칠 동안 양쪽 군대가 죽은 자들을 묻을 수 있도록 휴전이 있었다. 이 사이에 아이네아스는 투르누스에게 일대일 결투로 승부를 가리자고 도전했으나, 투르누스는 그 도전을 피했다. 또 한 차례의 전투가 이어졌으니, 그 전투에서 처녀 전사 카밀라가 특히 두드러졌다. 그녀의 무용은 가장 용맹한 전사들의 그것을 능가했으며, 많은 트로이아인과 에트루리아인이 그녀의 화살에 꿰뚫리거나 그녀의 전투용 도끼에 쓰러졌다. 마침내 아룬스라는 이름의 에트루리아인이, 오래도록 그녀를 지켜보며 어떤 빈틈을 노리던 차에, 그녀가 화려한 무구로 탐나는 전리품을 안긴 채 달아나는 적을 뒤쫓는 것을 보았다. 추격에 정신이 팔려 그녀는 자신의 위험을 알아채지 못했고, 아룬스의 투창이 그녀를 맞혀 치명상을 입혔다. 그녀는 쓰러져 시중드는 처녀들의 품에서 마지막 숨을 거두었다. 그러나 그녀의 최후를 지켜본 아르테미스는 그 죽음이 복수 없이 끝나도록 두지 않았다. 아룬스가 기뻐하면서도 두려워하며 몰래 빠져나가는 사이, 아르테미스의 시종 님프들 가운데 하나가 날린 은밀한 화살에 맞아, 그는 비천하게 이름도 없이 죽었다.\n\n마침내 아이네아스와 투르누스 사이의 최후의 결전이 벌어졌다. 투르누스는 할 수 있는 한 그 싸움을 피해 왔으나, 마침내 자기 군대의 패전과 추종자들의 불평에 떠밀려 결전에 임할 각오를 다졌다. 그 결말은 의심할 여지가 없었다. 아이네아스의 편에는 명백히 선포된 운명의 명령과, 위급할 때마다 그를 돕는 여신 어머니의 도움과, 그녀의 청에 따라 헤파이스토스[로마의 〈불카누스〉]가 그 아들을 위해 만든 뚫을 수 없는 무구가 있었다. 반면에 투르누스는 천상의 동맹들에게 버림받았으니, 헤라는 제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]로부터 더는 그를 돕지 말라는 명백한 금령을 받았던 것이다. 투르누스가 창을 던졌으나, 그것은 아이네아스의 방패에 맞고 아무런 해도 입히지 못한 채 튕겨 나왔다. 그러자 트로이아 영웅이 자신의 창을 던졌으니, 그것은 투르누스의 방패를 뚫고 그의 넓적다리를 꿰뚫었다. 그러자 투르누스의 굳센 의지가 그를 저버렸고 그는 자비를 빌었다. 아이네아스는 그에게 목숨을 살려 주려 했으나, 바로 그 순간 그의 눈길이 팔라스의 허리띠에 닿았으니, 투르누스가 살해당한 젊은이에게서 빼앗은 것이었다. 곧바로 그의 분노가 되살아났고, \"팔라스가 이 일격으로 너를 제물 삼노라\"라고 외치며 그는 자신의 검으로 그를 꿰뚫었다.\n\n여기서 『아이네이스』의 시는 끝나니, 우리는 아이네아스가 적들을 이긴 끝에 라비니아를 신부로 얻었으리라 짐작하게 된다. 전승은 거기에 덧붙이기를, 그가 자신의 도시를 세워 그녀의 이름을 따 라비니움이라 불렀다고 한다. 그의 아들 이울루스는 알바 롱가를 세웠으니, 그곳은 로물루스와 레무스의 출생지이자 로마 그 자체의 요람이었다.\n\n카밀라에 관한 암시는 포프의 저 널리 알려진 시구들 속에 있으니, \"소리는 뜻의 메아리가 되어야 한다\"는 규칙을 예시하며 그는 이렇게 말한다.\n\n\"아이아스가 어떤 바위의 거대한 무게를 던지려 애쓸 때면, 시행 또한 애를 쓰고 낱말들이 더디게 움직인다. 날랜 카밀라가 평원을 누빌 때는 그렇지 않으니, 굽지 않는 곡식 위를 날고 바다 위를 스쳐 지난다.\" —『비평론』\n\n————\n\n앙키세스가 아이네아스에게 인간 영혼의 본성에 관해 가르친 내용은 피타고라스학파의 학설과 일치하는 것이었다. 피타고라스(기원전 540년 출생)는 사모스섬 태생이었으나, 생애의 대부분을 이탈리아의 크로토나에서 보냈다. 따라서 그는 때로 \"사모스 사람\"이라 불리기도 하고, 때로 \"크로토나의 철학자\"라 불리기도 한다. 젊은 시절 그는 널리 여행을 다녔는데, 전하는 바에 따르면 이집트를 방문하여 그곳 사제들에게 그들의 모든 학문을 배웠고, 그 후 동방으로 떠나 페르시아와 칼데아의 마기, 그리고 인도의 브라만들을 찾아갔다고 한다.\n\n마침내 정착하게 된 크로토나에서, 그의 비범한 자질은 수많은 제자들을 그의 주위로 끌어모았다. 그곳 주민들은 사치와 방탕으로 악명이 높았으나, 그의 영향이 미친 좋은 결과는 이내 눈에 띄게 나타났다. 절제와 검약이 그 자리를 대신하게 된 것이다. 주민들 가운데 육백 명이 그의 제자가 되어, 지혜의 추구에서 서로를 돕기 위한 한 결사에 이름을 올렸고, 모두의 이익을 위해 그들의 재산을 하나의 공동 자산으로 합쳤다. 그들은 행실에 있어 더없는 순결과 소박함을 실천하도록 요구받았다. 그들이 처음 배운 가르침은 〈침묵〉이었으니, 한동안 그들은 오로지 듣는 자가 되어야만 했다. \"그분[피타고라스]께서 그렇게 말씀하셨다\"(입세 딕시트)는 말은, 어떠한 증명도 없이 그들에게 충분한 것으로 여겨졌다. 여러 해에 걸친 인내로운 순종을 거친 뒤에야, 오직 진전된 제자들만이 질문을 하고 이견을 제기하는 것이 허락되었다.\n\n피타고라스는 〈수〉를 만물의 본질이자 원리로 여겼으며, 수에 실재하고 뚜렷한 존재성을 부여했다. 따라서 그의 견해로는, 수란 우주가 그것으로부터 구성된 요소들이었다. 그가 이 과정을 어떻게 생각했는지는 결코 만족스럽게 설명된 바가 없다. 그는 세계의 다양한 형태와 현상을 그 토대이자 본질로서의 수에까지 거슬러 올라가 추적했다. \"모나드\", 곧 〈하나〉를 그는 모든 수의 근원으로 보았다. 수 〈둘〉은 불완전한 것이며, 증가와 분할의 원인이었다. 〈셋〉은 시작과 중간과 끝을 지니고 있으므로 전체의 수라 불렸다. 정사각형을 나타내는 〈넷〉은 가장 높은 정도로 완전하며, 〈열〉은 처음 네 수의 합을 담고 있으므로 모든 음악적·산술적 비례를 포괄하고 세계의 체계를 나타낸다.\n\n수가 모나드로부터 나오는 것처럼, 그는 신성의 순수하고 단순한 본질을 자연의 모든 형태의 근원으로 여겼다. 신들과 정령들과 영웅들은 지고자(至高者)로부터 흘러나온 것이며, 거기에 더해 네 번째 유출물, 곧 인간의 영혼이 있다. 이 영혼은 불멸하며, 육체의 족쇄로부터 벗어나면 죽은 자들의 거처로 옮겨가, 다시 이 세계로 돌아와 또 다른 인간이나 동물의 몸에 깃들 때까지 그곳에 머문다. 그리고 마침내 충분히 정화되었을 때, 자신이 비롯된 근원으로 되돌아간다. 본디 이집트에서 유래하여 인간 행위에 대한 상벌의 교리와 결부된 이 영혼 전생(轉生)의 학설(메템프시코시스)이야말로, 피타고라스학파가 어떠한 동물도 죽이지 않은 주된 까닭이었다. 오비디우스는 피타고라스가 제자들에게 다음과 같은 말로 이야기하는 모습을 그린다. \"영혼은 결코 죽지 않으며, 언제나 하나의 거처를 떠날 때 또 다른 거처로 옮겨간다. 나 자신은 트로이아 전쟁 때에 내가 판투스의 아들 에우포르보스였고 메넬라오스의 창에 쓰러졌던 일을 기억할 수 있다. 얼마 전 아르고스에 있는 헤라[로마의 〈유노〉]의 신전에 갔을 때, 나는 그곳 전리품들 가운데 걸려 있는 내 방패를 알아보았다. 만물은 변하되, 어떤 것도 소멸하지 않는다. 영혼은 이리저리 옮겨다니며 지금은 이 몸을, 지금은 저 몸을 차지하고, 짐승의 몸에서 사람의 몸으로 건너갔다가 다시 짐승의 몸으로 옮겨간다. 밀랍에 어떤 형상이 찍혔다가 녹여지고, 다시 다른 형상으로 새로이 찍히지만 언제나 같은 밀랍인 것처럼, 영혼도 언제나 같은 것이면서 서로 다른 때에 서로 다른 형태를 걸치는 것이다. 그러므로 만일 혈육에 대한 사랑이 그대들의 가슴속에서 꺼지지 않았다면, 부디 간청하노니, 어쩌면 그대들 자신의 친족일 수도 있는 자들의 생명을 해치는 일을 삼가라.\"\n\n셰익스피어는 「베니스의 상인」에서 그라티아노로 하여금 영혼 전생을 암시하게 하는데, 그가 샤일록에게 하는 말은 이러하다.\n\n\"당신은 거의 내 믿음을 흔들어 놓아, 피타고라스의 견해를 품게 만드는구나, 짐승의 영혼이 사람의 몸뚱이 속으로 스며든다는 그 견해를. 당신의 개 같은 영혼은 한 마리 늑대를 다스렸으니, 사람을 죽인 죄로 목매달린 그 늑대가 제 영혼을 당신에게 불어넣었구나. 당신의 욕망은 늑대처럼 잔혹하고, 피에 굶주리고, 게걸스럽기 때문이지.\"\n\n음계의 음들과 수의 관계, 곧 같은 시간 간격의 진동에서 조화가 생겨나고 그 반대에서 불협화음이 생겨난다는 사실은, 피타고라스로 하여금 \"조화\"라는 말을 눈에 보이는 피조물 전체에 적용하도록 이끌었으니, 그가 그 말로써 뜻한 바는 부분들이 서로에게 알맞게 들어맞음이었다. 이것이 바로 드라이든이 「성 케킬리아 축일을 위한 노래」의 첫머리에서 표현한 관념이다.\n\n\"조화로부터, 천상의 조화로부터 이 영원한 골격이 비롯되었네. 조화에서 조화로 음들의 온 음역을 두루 거쳐 그것은 흘러갔으니, 옥타브 화음은 인간 안에서 충만하게 닫혔네.\"\n\n그가 가르친 바에 따르면, 우주의 중심에는 생명의 원리인 중심의 불이 있었다. 이 중심의 불은 지구와 달과 태양, 그리고 다섯 행성으로 둘러싸여 있었다. 여러 천체들이 서로에게서 떨어진 거리는 음계의 비례에 상응하는 것으로 여겨졌다. 천체들은 그것들에 거주하는 신들과 더불어 \"노래가 없지 않게\" 중심의 불 주위를 합창의 춤을 추며 도는 것으로 생각되었다. 셰익스피어가 로렌초로 하여금 다음과 같은 방식으로 제시카에게 천문학을 가르치게 할 때 암시하는 것이 바로 이 학설이다.\n\n\"보라, 제시카, 하늘의 바닥이 빛나는 황금 접시들로 얼마나 빽빽이 박혀 있는지를! 그대가 바라보는 가장 작은 천구라도 그 운행 속에서 천사처럼 노래하지 않는 것이 없으니, 여전히 어린 눈의 케루빔에게 합창으로 화답하네. 그러한 조화가 불멸의 영혼들 안에 있도다! 허나 이 진흙으로 빚어진 썩어질 옷이 그것을 둔하게 가두고 있는 동안, 우리는 그 소리를 들을 수 없네.\" ─「베니스의 상인」.\n\n이 천구들은 수정이나 유리로 된 구조물로서, 마치 엎어 놓은 그릇들을 포개 놓은 것처럼 서로 위에 겹겹이 배열되어 있는 것으로 생각되었다. 각각의 천구의 실체 안에는 하나 또는 그 이상의 천체가 박혀서 그 천구와 함께 움직이는 것으로 여겨졌다. 천구들은 투명하므로, 우리는 그것들을 꿰뚫어 보면서 그 안에 담겨 함께 돌고 있는 천체들을 바라보게 된다. 그러나 이 천구들은 마찰 없이는 서로 위에서 움직일 수 없으므로, 그로 인해 하나의 소리가 생겨나는데, 그것은 더없이 절묘한 조화의 소리여서 필멸자의 귀로는 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 미묘하다. 밀턴은 「그리스도 강탄의 아침에 부치는 찬가」에서 천구의 음악을 다음과 같이 암시한다.\n\n\"울려 퍼져라, 그대 수정의 천구들이여! 한 번 우리 인간의 귀를 축복하라, (그대들에게 우리 감각을 그토록 매혹할 힘이 있다면.) 그대들의 은빛 종소리를 선율 고운 박자로 흐르게 하라, 또한 천국의 깊은 오르간의 저음을 울려라. 그리하여 그대들의 아홉 겹 조화로 천사들의 교향과 더불어 완전한 합주를 이루어라.\"\n\n피타고라스는 리라를 발명했다고 전해진다. 우리 시인 롱펠로는 「어느 아이에게 부치는 시」에서 그 이야기를 이렇게 들려준다.\n\n\"옛날 위대한 피타고라스가 대장간 문 옆에 서서, 망치들이 저마다 다른 음으로 모루를 내리치는 소리를 듣고는, 쇠로 된 모든 혀에 떨리며 매달려 있던 그 변화하는 음들로부터, 울리는 현(弦)의 비밀을 훔쳐내어, 일곱 줄의 리라를 만들었네.\"\n\n같은 시인의 「오리온의 엄폐(掩蔽)」도 함께 보라.\n\n\"사모스 사람의 위대한 아이올로스의 리라.\"\n\n# SYBARIS AND CROTONA\n\n크로토나의 이웃 도시 시바리스는, 크로토나가 그 반대의 것으로 유명했던 만큼이나 사치와 유약함으로 이름을 떨쳤다. 그 이름은 속담처럼 쓰이게 되었다. J. R. 로웰은 그의 매혹적인 짧은 시 「민들레에게」에서 그 이름을 이러한 뜻으로 사용한다.\n\n\"유월 한복판에 황금 갑옷을 두른 벌이 하얀 백합의 산들바람 부는 천막(그가 정복한 시바리스) 안에서 느끼는 그 여름다운 따스한 황홀도, 어두운 초록 사이로 그대 노란 동그라미가 처음 터져 나올 때 내가 느끼는 것보다 더하지는 않으리.\"\n\n두 도시 사이에 전쟁이 일어나, 시바리스는 정복되어 파괴되었다. 이름난 운동선수 밀로가 크로토나의 군대를 이끌었다. 밀로의 엄청난 힘에 관해서는 여러 이야기가 전해지는데, 이를테면 네 살배기 어린 암소를 어깨에 메고 다니다가 그 후 그것을 단 하루 만에 통째로 먹어 치웠다는 이야기 같은 것이다. 그의 죽음의 방식은 이렇게 전해진다. 어느 숲을 지나가다가 그는 나무꾼들이 일부 쪼개 놓은 나무 둥치를 보고는 그것을 더 갈라 보려 했다. 그러나 나무가 그의 두 손을 옥죄며 그를 단단히 붙들었고, 그러한 상태에서 그는 늑대들의 습격을 받아 잡아먹혔다.\n\n바이런은 「나폴레옹 보나파르트에게 부치는 송가」에서 밀로의 이야기를 암시한다.\n\n\"옛날 참나무를 쪼개려던 그 사내는 되튀어 올 힘을 생각지 못했네. 헛되이 쪼개려던 그 둥치에 묶인 채, 홀로, 그는 어떤 눈길로 주위를 둘러보았던가!\"\n\n# EGYPTIAN DEITIES\n\n이집트인들은 최고의 신으로 아문을 받들었으니, 그는 뒷날 제우스, 곧 유피테르 암몬이라 불렸다. 아문은 자신의 말씀 또는 의지를 통해 자신을 드러냈고, 그 말씀이 서로 다른 성(性)을 지닌 크네프와 아토르를 창조했다. 크네프와 아토르로부터 오시리스와 이시스가 나왔다. 오시리스는 따스함과 생명과 풍요의 근원인 태양의 신으로 숭배되었으며, 그에 더하여 해마다 범람을 통해 자신의 아내 이시스(대지)를 찾아가는 나일강의 신으로도 여겨졌다. 세라피스 또는 헤르메스는 때로는 오시리스와 동일한 존재로 묘사되고, 때로는 타르타로스의 지배자이자 의술의 신인 별개의 신격으로 묘사된다. 아누비스는 수호신으로, 충실함과 경계심이라는 그의 성격을 상징하여 개의 머리를 한 모습으로 그려진다. 호루스, 곧 하르포크라테스는 오시리스의 아들이었다. 그는 연꽃 위에 앉아 손가락을 입술에 댄 모습으로 그려지는데, 침묵의 신이기 때문이다.\n\n무어의 「아일랜드 선율」 가운데 하나에 하르포크라테스를 암시하는 대목이 있다.\n\n\"그대 자신은 어느 장밋빛 정자(亭子) 아래에서, 손가락을 입술에 댄 채 말없이 앉으리라. 나일강 물결 위에 붉게 피어나는 꽃들 사이에서 태어난 그 소년처럼, 언제나 그렇게 앉아 있는 그 소년처럼─그의 유일한 노래는 대지와 하늘을 향한, '모두 쉿, 쉿!'이라네.\"\n\n# MYTH OF OSIRIS AND ISIS\n\n오시리스와 이시스는 한때 지상으로 내려와 그곳 주민들에게 선물과 축복을 베풀도록 이끌렸다. 이시스는 먼저 그들에게 밀과 보리의 쓰임새를 보여 주었고, 오시리스는 농경의 도구들을 만들어 사람들에게 그 사용법을 가르쳐 주었으며, 황소를 쟁기에 매는 법도 일러 주었다. 그런 다음 그는 사람들에게 법률과 혼인 제도와 시민 조직을 주었고, 신들을 경배하는 법을 가르쳤다. 이렇게 하여 나일강 골짜기를 행복한 나라로 만든 뒤, 그는 한 무리의 군대를 모아 세계의 나머지 지역에도 자신의 축복을 베풀고자 떠났다. 그는 이르는 곳마다 여러 민족을 정복했는데, 무기로써가 아니라 오직 음악과 웅변으로써 그리하였다. 그의 형제 티폰이 이를 보고는 시기와 악의에 가득 차, 그가 자리를 비운 동안 그의 왕좌를 찬탈하려 꾀했다. 그러나 통치의 고삐를 쥐고 있던 이시스가 그의 계획을 좌절시켰다. 더욱 원한에 사무친 그는 이제 자신의 형제를 죽이기로 결심했다. 그는 다음과 같은 방식으로 이를 실행했다. 일흔두 명의 음모자를 규합한 다음, 그들과 함께 왕의 귀환을 기리어 열린 잔치에 갔다. 그러고는 오시리스의 몸 크기에 꼭 들어맞도록 만들어진 상자, 곧 궤를 들여오게 한 뒤, 그 안에 들어갈 수 있는 자에게 그 값진 나무로 만든 궤를 주겠노라고 선언했다. 나머지 사람들은 헛되이 시도했으나, 오시리스가 그 안에 들어가자마자 티폰과 그의 동료들은 뚜껑을 닫아 그 궤를 나일강에 던져 버렸다. 이시스는 이 잔혹한 살해 소식을 듣고 울며 슬퍼했고, 그런 다음 머리카락을 자르고 검은 옷을 입고 가슴을 치면서, 남편의 시신을 부지런히 찾아다녔다. 이 수색에서 그녀는 오시리스와 네프티스의 아들 아누비스의 큰 도움을 받았다. 한동안 그들은 헛되이 찾아 헤맸으니, 그 궤가 물결에 실려 비블로스 해안에 이르러 물가에 자라는 갈대에 걸려 멈추었을 때, 오시리스의 몸 안에 깃든 신성한 힘이 그 관목에 어찌나 큰 기운을 불어넣었던지, 그것이 자라나 거대한 나무가 되어 그 둥치 안에 신의 관을 품게 되었던 것이다. 이 나무는 신성한 유물을 품은 채 얼마 지나지 않아 베어져, 페니키아 왕의 궁전에 기둥으로 세워졌다. 그러나 마침내 아누비스와 신성한 새들의 도움으로 이시스는 이러한 사실들을 알아내고, 그 왕도(王都)로 갔다. 그곳에서 그녀는 궁전에 하녀로 자신을 내놓아 받아들여진 다음, 변장을 벗어 던지고 천둥과 번개에 둘러싸인 여신의 모습으로 나타났다. 자신의 지팡이로 그 기둥을 내리쳐 그것을 쪼개어 신성한 관을 내놓게 했다. 그녀는 이것을 거두어 가지고 돌아와 어느 숲 깊은 곳에 감추었으나, 티폰이 그것을 발견하고는 시신을 열네 토막으로 잘라 이리저리 흩뿌렸다. 지루한 수색 끝에 이시스는 열세 토막을 찾아냈으나, 나머지 한 토막은 나일강의 물고기들이 먹어 버린 뒤였다. 그녀는 이것을 무화과나무로 만든 모조품으로 대신하고, 필라이에 시신을 묻었으니, 그곳은 그 후로 줄곧 이 나라의 위대한 매장지가 되었고, 온 나라 각지에서 순례가 이루어지는 장소가 되었다. 그곳에는 또한 더없이 장엄한 신전이 그 신을 기리어 세워졌고, 그의 사지(四肢) 가운데 하나가 발견된 곳마다 그 일을 기념하여 작은 신전과 무덤들이 지어졌다. 그 후 오시리스는 이집트인들의 수호신이 되었다. 그의 영혼은 언제나 황소 아피스의 몸 안에 깃들어 있으며, 그 황소가 죽으면 그 후계자에게로 옮겨가는 것으로 여겨졌다.\n\n멤피스의 황소 아피스는 이집트인들에게 더없는 경외로써 숭배되었다. 아피스로 받들어지는 그 한 마리의 짐승은 특정한 표징들로 식별되었다. 그 황소는 완전히 검은빛이어야 하고, 이마에는 흰 사각형 표지가, 등에는 독수리 모양의 또 다른 표지가 있어야 하며, 혀 밑에는 풍뎅이 또는 딱정벌레 형상에 가까운 혹이 있어야 했다. 그를 찾으러 보내진 자들이 이렇게 표징을 지닌 황소를 발견하자마자, 그 황소는 동쪽을 향한 한 건물에 놓여 넉 달 동안 젖을 먹였다. 이 기간이 끝나면 사제들은 초승달이 뜰 때 대단한 위엄을 갖추어 그의 거처로 가서 그를 아피스라 칭송했다. 그는 화려하게 장식된 배에 실려 나일강을 따라 멤피스로 옮겨졌으며, 그곳에는 두 개의 예배당과 운동을 위한 마당을 갖춘 신전이 그에게 배정되었다. 그에게는 제물이 바쳐졌고, 해마다 한 번씩 나일강이 불어나기 시작할 무렵이면 황금 잔 하나를 강물에 던졌으며, 그의 생일을 기리어 성대한 축제가 열렸다. 사람들은 이 축제 동안에는 악어들이 타고난 흉포함을 잊고 해를 끼치지 않게 된다고 믿었다. 그러나 그의 행복한 처지에는 한 가지 결함이 있었으니, 그는 일정한 기간을 넘겨 살도록 허락되지 않았던 것이다. 만일 그가 스물다섯 살에 이르렀는데도 여전히 살아 있으면, 사제들은 그를 신성한 저수조에 빠뜨려 죽인 다음 세라피스의 신전에 묻었다. 이 황소가 죽으면, 그것이 자연의 순리로 일어난 것이든 폭력으로 일어난 것이든, 온 땅이 슬픔과 비탄으로 가득 찼고, 그 비탄은 그의 후계자가 발견될 때까지 이어졌다.\n\n당대의 어느 신문에서 우리는 다음과 같은 기사를 발견한다.\n\n\"아피스의 무덤.─멤피스에서 진행 중인 발굴은 그 묻혔던 도시를 폼페이만큼이나 흥미로운 곳으로 만들 듯하다. 수백 년 동안 알려지지 않은 채 묻혀 있던 아피스의 거대한 무덤이 이제 열렸다.\"\n\n밀턴은 「그리스도 강탄의 아침에 부치는 찬가」에서 이집트의 신들을 상상의 존재가 아니라 그리스도의 강림으로 쫓겨난 실재하는 악령들로 보아 암시한다.\n\n\"나일강의 짐승 같은 신들도 황급히, 이시스와 호루스와 개 머리 아누비스가 서둘러 달아나네. 오시리스의 모습도 보이지 않으니, 멤피스의 숲에서도 푸른 들에서도 비 한 번 내리지 않은 풀을 큰 울음소리와 함께 짓밟던 그 모습이. 그는 안식할 수도 없으니, 제 신성한 궤 안에서도 마찬가지라. 가장 깊은 지옥 말고는 그 무엇도 그의 수의(壽衣)가 될 수 없네. 헛되이 어두운 소고(小鼓)의 찬가를 울리며 검은 옷 두른 술사들이 그의 숭배받던 궤를 떠메고 가네.\"\n\n이시스는 조각상에서 머리를 베일로 가린 모습으로 표현되었으니, 신비의 상징이었다. 테니슨이 「모드」 제4편 8연에서 암시하는 것이 바로 이것이다.\n\n\"창조주의 의도는 어두우니, 베일에 가려진 이시스라네.\" 등등.\n\n오라클이란, 미래에 관해 신들에게 자문을 구하는 자들에게 어느 신격이 답을 주는 것으로 여겨진 장소를 가리키는 데 쓰인 이름이었다. 이 말은 또한 그렇게 주어진 응답을 가리키는 데에도 쓰였다.\n\n가장 오래된 그리스의 신탁소는 도도나에 있던 제우스의 신탁소였다. 한 전승에 따르면, 그것은 다음과 같은 방식으로 세워졌다고 한다. 검은 비둘기 두 마리가 이집트의 테바이에서 날아올랐다. 한 마리는 에페이로스의 도도나로 날아가 참나무 숲에 내려앉아, 그 지역 주민들에게 인간의 언어로 그곳에 제우스의 신탁소를 세워야 한다고 선포했다. 다른 한 마리는 리비아 사막의 오아시스에 있는 유피테르 암몬의 신전으로 날아가, 그곳에서도 비슷한 명령을 전했다. 또 다른 전승에 따르면, 그들은 비둘기가 아니라 여사제들이었는데, 페니키아인들에게 이집트 테바이에서 끌려가 오아시스와 도도나에 신탁소를 세웠다고 한다. 신탁소의 응답은 나무들로부터, 곧 바람에 흔들리는 가지들의 소리를 통해 주어졌고, 그 소리를 사제들이 해석했다.\n\n그러나 그리스의 신탁소들 가운데 가장 이름난 것은 포키스의 파르나소스 비탈에 세워진 도시 델포이에 있던 아폴론의 신탁소였다.\n\n아주 이른 시기에 사람들은 파르나소스에서 풀을 뜯던 염소들이 산허리에 난 어떤 길고 깊은 틈에 다가가면 경련에 사로잡힌다는 것을 알아차렸다. 이는 그 동굴에서 솟아오르는 독특한 증기 때문이었는데, 한 염소지기가 그것이 자신에게 어떤 작용을 하는지 시험해 보도록 이끌렸다. 그 도취시키는 공기를 들이마시자, 그는 가축들이 그러했던 것과 같은 방식으로 영향을 받았고, 주위 지역의 주민들은 이 일을 설명하지 못한 채, 그가 그 증기의 힘 아래에서 내뱉는 경련에 찬 헛소리를 신의 영감 탓으로 돌렸다. 이 사실은 이내 널리 퍼졌고, 그 자리에 신전이 세워졌다. 예언의 영향력은 처음에는 대지의 여신, 넵투누스, 테미스를 비롯한 여러 신들에게 다양하게 돌려졌으나, 마침내 아폴론에게, 오직 그에게만 돌려졌다. 그 신성한 공기를 들이마시는 일을 맡은 여사제가 임명되었으니, 그녀는 피티아라 불렸다. 그녀는 카스탈리아 샘에서 미리 몸을 씻어 이 임무를 위한 준비를 했고, 월계관을 쓴 채 마찬가지로 장식된 삼각대 위에 앉았는데, 그 삼각대는 신의 영기(靈氣)가 솟아오르는 그 틈 위에 놓여 있었다. 이렇게 자리한 채 그녀가 영감에 차서 내뱉는 말들을 사제들이 해석했다.\n\n# ORACLE OF TROPHONIUS\n\n도도나와 델포이에 있던 제우스와 아폴론의 신탁소들 외에도, 보이오티아에 있던 트로포니오스의 신탁소가 높이 존중되었다. 트로포니오스와 아가메데스는 형제였다. 그들은 뛰어난 건축가로서, 델포이에 아폴론의 신전을 짓고 히리에우스 왕을 위해 보물 창고를 지었다. 그들은 그 창고의 벽에 빼낼 수 있도록 돌 하나를 끼워 두었고, 이 방법으로 때때로 보물을 훔쳤다. 이 일은 히리에우스를 어리둥절하게 했으니, 그의 자물쇠와 봉인은 손대지 않은 그대로였는데도 그의 재물이 끊임없이 줄어들었기 때문이다. 마침내 그는 그 도둑을 잡으려고 덫을 놓았고, 아가메데스가 걸려들었다. 트로포니오스는 그를 빼낼 수 없는 데다, 그가 발각되면 고문을 받아 공범을 발설하도록 강요당할 것을 두려워하여, 그의 머리를 잘랐다. 트로포니오스 자신도 그 직후에 땅에 삼켜졌다고 전해진다.\n\n트로포니오스의 신탁소는 보이오티아의 레바데이아에 있었다. 큰 가뭄이 들었을 때, 보이오티아인들은 델포이의 신으로부터 레바데이아의 트로포니오스에게 도움을 구하라는 지시를 받았다고 한다. 그들은 그곳에 이르렀으나 신탁소를 찾을 수 없었다. 그러나 그들 가운데 한 사람이 우연히 한 무리의 벌 떼를 보고 그것들을 따라가 땅에 난 틈에 이르렀는데, 그곳이 바로 찾던 장소로 밝혀졌다.\n\n신탁을 구하러 온 사람은 독특한 의식을 치러야 했다. 이 예비 절차를 마친 뒤, 그는 좁은 통로를 통해 동굴 안으로 내려갔다. 이곳은 오직 밤에만 들어갈 수 있었다. 그 사람은 같은 좁은 통로를 통해 동굴에서 돌아왔으나, 뒷걸음질로 걸어 나왔다. 그는 우울하고 풀이 죽은 모습이었으니, 이로부터 기운 없고 침울한 사람에게 적용되는 \"그가 트로포니오스의 신탁소에 다녀왔다\"라는 속담이 생겨났다.\n\n아스클레피오스의 신탁소는 여럿 있었으나, 가장 이름난 것은 에피다우로스에 있었다. 이곳에서 병든 자들은 신전에서 잠을 잠으로써 응답과 건강의 회복을 구했다. 우리에게 전해 내려온 기록들로부터, 병든 자들에 대한 그 치료가 오늘날 동물 자기(磁氣) 또는 메스머리즘이라 불리는 것과 비슷했으리라고 추정되어 왔다.\n\n뱀은 아스클레피오스에게 신성한 것이었으니, 아마도 그 동물들이 허물을 벗음으로써 젊음을 되찾는 능력을 지녔다는 미신 때문이었을 것이다. 아스클레피오스 숭배는 큰 역병이 돌던 시기에 로마에 도입되었으며, 신의 도움을 간청하고자 에피다우로스의 신전에 사절단이 파견되었다. 아스클레피오스는 호의를 베풀어, 배가 돌아올 때 뱀의 모습으로 그 배에 동행했다. 티베리스강에 이르자, 그 뱀은 배에서 미끄러져 나와 강 가운데의 한 섬을 차지했고, 그곳에 그를 기리는 신전이 세워졌다.\n\n# ORACLE OF APIS\n\n멤피스에서는 신성한 황소 아피스가, 자신에게 바쳐진 것을 받아들이느냐 거부하느냐 하는 방식으로 자문을 구하는 자들에게 답을 주었다. 만일 그 황소가 질문하는 자의 손에서 먹이를 거부하면 그것은 불길한 징조로 여겨졌고, 그것을 받아들이면 그 반대로 여겨졌다.\n\n신탁의 응답이 단순한 인간의 술책에 돌려져야 하는지, 아니면 악령의 작용에 돌려져야 하는지는 하나의 논쟁거리가 되어 왔다. 지난 시대에는 후자의 견해가 가장 널리 퍼져 있었다. 메스머리즘의 현상이 주목을 끌게 된 이래로 제3의 이론이 제기되었으니, 곧 피티아에게 메스머의 가수(假睡) 상태와 비슷한 무언가가 유도되어, 천리안(千里眼)의 능력이 실제로 발동되었다는 것이다.\n\n또 다른 문제는 이교의 신탁소들이 응답을 그친 시점에 관한 것이다. 고대 그리스도교 저술가들은 신탁소들이 그리스도의 탄생과 함께 침묵하게 되었으며, 그날 이후로는 더 이상 들리지 않게 되었다고 주장한다. 밀턴은 「그리스도 강탄의 아침에 부치는 찬가」에서 이 견해를 받아들여, 엄숙하고 고결한 아름다움을 지닌 시구로 구세주의 강림 앞에서 이교 우상들이 느끼는 경악을 그려 낸다.\n\n\"신탁소들은 말을 잃었네. 어떤 목소리도, 소름 끼치는 웅얼거림도 아치형 지붕을 통해 속이는 말로 울려 퍼지지 않네. 아폴론은 자신의 성소에서 더 이상 점칠 수 없으니, 공허한 비명과 함께 델포스의 비탈을 떠나가네. 어떤 밤의 황홀경도, 내쉬어진 주문(呪文)도 예언의 골방에서 창백한 눈의 사제에게 영감을 주지 못하네.\"\n\n쿠퍼의 시 「야들리의 참나무」에는 아름다운 신화적 암시들이 몇 가지 들어 있다. 다음 두 대목 가운데 앞의 것은 카스토르와 폴리데우케스의 이야기를 가리키고, 뒤의 것은 우리의 현재 주제에 더 어울린다. 도토리를 향해 그는 이렇게 말한다.\n\n\"너는 무르익어 떨어졌고, 그 기름진 흙덩이 속에서 초목의 힘으로 부풀어 본능에 차, 네 알을 터뜨렸으니, 이제 별이 된 그 전설의 쌍둥이가 제 알을 터뜨렸듯이. 두 떡잎이 정확히 짝을 이루어 돋아났고, 한 잎이 뒤따르고 또 한 잎이 뒤따랐으며, 온 자연의 요소들이 네 가냘픈 성장을 호의롭게 길러 주어, 너는 한 가지가 되었네. 네가 그러했을 때 누가 살아 있었던가? 오, 네가 말할 수 있다면, 옛날 도도나에서 네 동족 나무들이 신탁을 내렸던 것처럼. 나는 알지 못하는 편이 나은 미래를 캐묻고 싶지는 않으나, 네 입에 대고 덜 모호한 과거를 호기심으로 물으려네.\"\n\n테니슨은 「말하는 참나무」에서 다음 시구로 도도나의 참나무들을 암시한다.\n\n\"그리고 나는 산문과 운문으로 일하며, 그 둘로 그대를 더욱 기리리라, 어느 시인이 너도밤나무나 보리수를 기린 것보다도, 또는 거무스름한 산비둘기가 앉아 신비로운 말을 내뱉던 그 테살리아의 나무를 기린 것보다도.\" 등등.\n\n바이런은 델포이의 신탁소를 암시하는데, 그 글이 프랑스 혁명을 불러오는 데 큰 몫을 했다고 그가 생각하는 루소를 두고 이렇게 말한다.\n\n\"그때 그는 영감을 받았으니, 그에게서 나왔네, 옛날 피티아의 신비로운 동굴에서 나왔듯이, 세계를 불길에 휩싸이게 한 그 신탁들이, 왕국들이 더 이상 존재하지 않게 될 때까지 타오르기를 그치지 않았던 그 신탁들이.\"\n\n# CHAPTER XXXV\n\n우리는 이교 신화 이야기들의 연속을 끝맺는 지점에 이르렀고, 이제 한 가지 의문이 자연스럽게 떠오른다. \"이 이야기들은 어디에서 비롯되었는가? 이것들은 진실에 토대를 두고 있는가, 아니면 단순히 상상력이 빚어낸 꿈에 불과한가?\" 철학자들은 이 주제에 관하여 여러 이론을 제시해 왔는데, 그 첫 번째는 1. 성서적 이론이다. 이 이론에 따르면 모든 신화적 전설은 성서의 서술에서 유래한 것이지만, 다만 실제 사실이 위장되고 변형되었다는 것이다. 그리하여 데우칼리온은 노아의 또 다른 이름일 뿐이며, 헤라클레스는 삼손, 아리온은 요나 등에 해당한다고 본다. 월터 롤리 경은 그의 「세계사」에서 이렇게 말한다. \"유발, 투발, 투발카인은 곧 메르쿠리우스, 불카누스, 아폴론으로, 각각 목축과 대장일과 음악의 창시자였다. 황금 사과를 지키던 용은 이브를 꾄 뱀이었다. 니므롯의 탑은 천상을 향한 거인들의 시도였다.\" 이와 같은 흥미로운 일치점이 적지 않은 것은 분명하지만, 이 이론을 지나치게 밀어붙여 이야기들의 큰 비중을 설명하려 하면 무리가 따른다.\n\n2. 역사적 이론이다. 이 이론에 따르면 신화에 언급된 모든 인물은 한때 실재했던 인간이었으며, 그들에 관한 전설과 황당무계한 전승은 후대에 덧붙고 윤색된 것에 불과하다는 것이다. 그리하여 바람의 왕이자 신인 아이올로스의 이야기는, 아이올로스가 티레니아해의 어느 섬들을 다스린 통치자로서 정의롭고 경건한 왕으로 군림하며 원주민들에게 배의 돛 사용법과 대기의 징후를 보고 날씨와 바람의 변화를 알아내는 법을 가르쳤다는 사실에서 비롯되었다고 본다. 또한 전설이 말하기를 땅에 용의 이빨을 뿌려 거기에서 무장한 사람들이 솟아났다고 하는 카드모스는, 실은 페니키아에서 온 이주자로서 알파벳 글자에 관한 지식을 그리스로 가지고 들어와 원주민들에게 가르쳤다는 것이다. 이러한 학문의 초보로부터 문명이 싹텄으니, 시인들은 줄곧 이 문명을 인간의 첫 상태, 곧 순결과 소박함의 황금시대가 타락한 것으로 묘사하는 경향이 있었다.\n\n3. 우의적 이론이다. 이 이론은 고대인의 모든 신화가 우의적이고 상징적이며, 우의의 형식 아래 어떤 도덕적, 종교적, 혹은 철학적 진리나 역사적 사실을 담고 있었으나, 세월이 흐르면서 글자 그대로 받아들여지게 되었다고 가정한다. 그리하여 제 자식을 잡아먹는 사투르누스는 그리스인들이 크로노스[시간]라 부른 바로 그 힘과 같으니, 시간이란 자신이 존재케 한 모든 것을 파괴한다고 진실로 말할 수 있다. 이오의 이야기도 비슷한 방식으로 해석된다. 이오는 달이고, 아르고스는 별이 총총한 하늘로서, 이를테면 잠들지 않고 달을 감시한다. 이오의 황당무계한 방랑은 달의 끊임없는 운행을 나타내며, 이는 밀턴에게도 같은 착상을 떠올리게 했다.\n\n\"방황하는 달을 바라보네 가장 높은 정오 가까이 떠가는, 마치 길을 잃은 자가 하늘의 넓고 길 없는 길을 헤매듯이.\" —「생각에 잠긴 사람」\n\n4. 자연적 이론이다. 이 이론에 따르면 공기, 불, 물의 요소가 본래 종교적 숭배의 대상이었으며, 주요 신들은 자연의 힘을 의인화한 것이었다. 요소들을 의인화하는 데에서 자연의 갖가지 대상을 주재하고 다스리는 초자연적 존재라는 관념으로 옮겨가는 일은 쉬웠다. 상상력이 활발했던 그리스인들은 온 자연을 보이지 않는 존재들로 가득 채웠으며, 태양과 바다에서 가장 작은 샘과 시냇물에 이르기까지 모든 대상이 어떤 특정한 신의 보살핌 아래 있다고 여겼다. 워즈워스는 그의 「소요」에서 그리스 신화에 대한 이러한 견해를 아름답게 펼쳐 보였다.\n\n\"그 아름다운 풍토에서 외로운 목자는, 한여름 한나절을 부드러운 풀밭에 길게 누워, 음악으로 나른한 휴식을 달랬네. 그러다 어느 권태로운 순간, 제 숨소리가 잠잠해졌을 때, 그가 우연히 제 보잘것없는 솜씨로 낼 수 있는 소리보다 훨씬 감미로운 먼 가락을 듣게 되면, 그의 공상은 저 태양의 불타는 수레에서까지 황금 류트를 타며 빛나는 숲을 황홀로 가득 채우는 수염 없는 젊은이를 데려왔네. 힘센 사냥꾼은 초승달을 향해 눈을 들어, 감사하는 마음으로 그 사랑스러운 방랑자를 불렀으니, 그녀가 제 즐거운 사냥을 함께하라고 때맞춰 빛을 베풀었음이라. 그리하여 빛나는 여신이 그녀의 님프들과 더불어 잔디밭을 가로질러 어두운 숲을 지나 (바위나 동굴에서 메아리에 거듭 울려 퍼지는 가락 어린 음표들과 더불어 결코 동행 없지 않게) 사냥의 폭풍 속을 휩쓸고 지나갔으니, 마치 달과 별이 바람이 거세게 불 때 구름 낀 하늘을 따라 재빨리 스쳐 가듯 했네. 나그네는 시냇물이나 솟구치는 샘에서 갈증을 풀고 나이아스에게 감사했네. 먼 언덕 위의 햇살이 그림자를 거느린 채 빠르게 미끄러져 가는 것은 공상의 작은 도움만 받아도, 눈에 보이게 뛰노는 날랜 오레아데스로 변모할 수 있었네. 제피로스들은 지나가며 날개를 부치다가, 부드러운 속삭임으로 구애할 사랑스러운 대상이 없는 법이 없었네. 늙은 세월에 잎과 잔가지를 벗긴 기괴하게 시든 가지들이, 낮은 골짜기나 가파른 산비탈의 텁수룩한 덤불 깊은 곳에서 빠끔히 내다보고, 때로는 살아 있는 사슴의 움직이는 뿔이나 염소의 늘어진 수염과 뒤섞이면, 이것들은 숨어 있는 사티로스들, 곧 장난기 어린 신들의 거친 무리였네. 혹은 판 자신이었으니, 저 소박한 목자의 경외를 자아내는 신이었네.\"\n\n지금까지 언급한 모든 이론은 어느 정도까지는 참이다. 따라서 한 민족의 신화는 어느 한 가지 원천에서 나왔다기보다, 이 모든 원천이 결합하여 생겨났다고 말하는 편이 더 정확할 것이다. 또한 덧붙이건대, 인간이 이해할 수 없는 자연 현상을 설명하고자 하는 욕구에서 생겨난 신화가 많으며, 장소와 인물의 이름에 까닭을 부여하려는 비슷한 욕구에서 비롯된 것도 적지 않다.\n신들의 조각상\n\n# STATUES OF THE GODS\n\n여러 신들의 이름 아래 마음에 전하고자 했던 관념을 눈앞에 충분히 재현하는 일은, 천재성과 예술의 최고 역량을 발휘하게 하는 과업이었다. 수많은 시도 가운데 네 가지가 가장 유명한데, 처음 두 가지는 고대인들의 묘사를 통해서만 우리에게 알려져 있고, 나머지 둘은 여전히 현존하며 조각 예술의 공인된 걸작이다.\n올림피아의 유피테르\n\n# THE OLYMPIAN JUPITER\n\n페이디아스가 만든 올림피아의 유피테르 조각상은 그리스 예술의 이 분야에서 가장 높은 성취로 여겨졌다. 그것은 거대한 규모였으며, 고대인들이 \"황금상아상(黃金象牙像)\"이라 부르던 것, 곧 상아와 황금으로 이루어진 것이었다. 살을 나타내는 부분은 나무나 돌로 된 심에 상아를 입혔고, 옷자락과 그 밖의 장식은 황금으로 만들었다. 상의 높이는 12미터였고, 받침대는 높이가 3.6미터였다. 신은 자신의 옥좌에 앉은 모습으로 표현되었다. 그의 이마에는 올리브 화관이 씌워졌고, 오른손에는 홀(笏)을, 왼손에는 승리의 여신 니케 상을 들고 있었다. 옥좌는 삼나무로 만들어졌으며, 황금과 보석으로 장식되어 있었다.\n\n예술가가 구현하고자 했던 관념은 헬레네스[그리스] 민족의 지고한 신이 정복자로서 옥좌에 앉아, 완전한 위엄과 평정 속에서 고갯짓 하나로 자신에게 종속된 세계를 다스리는 모습이었다. 페이디아스는 자신이 호메로스가 「일리아스」 제1권에서 보여 준 묘사에서 그 착상을 얻었다고 공언했는데, 그 구절은 포프가 다음과 같이 옮긴 것이다.\n\n\"그가 말하고 위엄 있게 검은 눈썹을 굽히니, 신령한 곱슬머리를 흔들며 고갯짓하였으니, 그것은 운명의 봉인이요 신의 재가였네. 드높은 하늘은 그 두려운 신호를 경외로 받아들였고, 온 올림포스가 중심까지 뒤흔들렸네.\"[36]\n파르테논의 미네르바\n\n# THE MINERVA OF THE PARTHENON\n\n이것 또한 페이디아스의 작품이었다. 그것은 파르테논, 곧 아테나이의 미네르바 신전에 세워져 있었다. 여신은 서 있는 모습으로 표현되었다. 한 손에는 창을, 다른 손에는 승리의 여신 니케 상을 들고 있었다. 화려하게 장식된 그녀의 투구 꼭대기에는 스핑크스가 얹혀 있었다. 조각상은 높이가 12미터였으며, 유피테르 상과 마찬가지로 상아와 황금으로 이루어졌다. 눈은 대리석으로 만들어졌고, 아마도 홍채와 동공을 나타내도록 색칠되었을 것이다. 이 조각상이 서 있던 파르테논 역시 페이디아스의 지휘와 감독 아래 건립되었다. 그 외부는 조각으로 풍성하게 꾸며졌으며, 그 가운데 많은 것이 페이디아스의 손에서 나왔다. 지금 대영박물관에 있는 엘긴 대리석상들이 그 일부이다.\n\n페이디아스의 유피테르와 미네르바는 모두 소실되었으나, 현존하는 여러 조각상과 흉상에서 두 신의 얼굴에 대한 예술가의 구상을 우리가 지니고 있다고 믿을 만한 충분한 근거가 있다. 그것들은 장중하고 위엄 있는 아름다움, 그리고 어떤 일시적 표정으로부터 자유로운 것이 특징인데, 이를 예술 용어로 평정(平靜)이라 부른다.\n메디치의 베누스\n\n# THE VENUS DE’ MEDICI\n\n메디치의 베누스는 약 이백 년 전 처음 주목을 끌었을 때 로마에서 그 이름을 지닌 군주들의 소유였던 데에서 그렇게 불린다. 받침대에 새겨진 명문(銘文)에는 이것이 기원전 200년 아테나이의 조각가 클레오메네스의 작품이라 기록되어 있으나, 그 명문의 진위는 의심스럽다. 한 이야기에 따르면, 예술가가 공적 권위에 의해 여성미의 완벽함을 보여 주는 조각상을 만들도록 고용되었고, 그 과업을 돕고자 도시가 제공할 수 있는 가장 완벽한 형태들이 모델로 그에게 주어졌다고 한다. 톰슨이 그의 「여름」에서 암시하는 것이 바로 이것이다.\n\n\"이렇게 세상을 매혹하는 조각상이 서 있네. 이렇게 몸을 굽혀 견줄 데 없는 자랑을 가리려 하니, 환희에 찬 그리스의 뒤섞인 아름다움들을.\"\n\n바이런 또한 이 조각상을 암시한다. 그는 피렌체 박물관에 관하여 이렇게 말한다.\n\n\"거기에도, 여신이 돌 속에서 사랑하며, 주위의 공기를 아름다움으로 채우네.\" 등등.\n\n그리고 다음 연에서는,\n\n\"피와 맥박과 가슴이 다르다니아 목자의 상(賞)을 확증하네.\"\n\n이 마지막 암시의 설명은 제27장을 보라.\n벨베데레의 아폴론\n\n# THE APOLLO BELVEDERE\n\n고대 조각의 모든 유물 가운데 가장 높이 평가받는 것은 벨베데레라 불리는 아폴론 조각상으로, 이는 그것이 놓였던 로마 교황 궁전의 방 이름에서 따온 것이다. 예술가는 알려져 있지 않다. 이것은 우리 시대 약 1세기경의 로마 예술 작품으로 추정된다. 그것은 대리석으로 된 서 있는 상으로, 높이가 2미터가 넘으며, 목에 둘러 매어 뻗은 왼팔 위로 늘어진 망토를 빼고는 벌거벗고 있다. 이것은 신이 괴물 피톤을 죽이려고 화살을 쏜 순간을 나타내는 것으로 추정된다(제3장을 보라). 승리를 거둔 신은 앞으로 발을 내딛는 동작을 취하고 있다. 활을 들고 있었던 듯한 왼팔은 뻗어 있고, 머리도 같은 방향으로 돌려져 있다. 자세와 비례에 있어 이 상의 우아한 위엄은 어디에도 뒤지지 않는다. 그 효과는 얼굴에서 완성되니, 거기에는 젊고 신과 같은 아름다움의 완벽함 위에 승리를 거둔 힘에 대한 자각이 깃들어 있다.\n\n# THE DIANA A LA BICHE\n\n사슴과 함께 있는 디아나\n루브르 궁전에 있는 사슴의 디아나는 벨베데레의 아폴론에 대응하는 작품으로 볼 수 있다. 그 자세는 아폴론의 것과 매우 비슷하고, 크기도 일치하며 제작 양식 또한 그러하다. 그것은 최고 수준의 작품이나, 결코 아폴론에 견줄 만하지는 않다. 자세는 서두르며 열중한 움직임의 것이고, 얼굴은 사냥의 흥분에 사로잡힌 여자 사냥꾼의 것이다. 왼손은 그녀 곁을 달리는 사슴의 이마 위로 뻗어 있고, 오른팔은 어깨 너머로 뒤로 뻗어 화살통에서 화살을 뽑으려 하고 있다.\n\n# THE POETS OF MYTHOLOGY\n\n신화의 시인들\n우리가 트로이아 전쟁과 그리스인들의 귀환을 다룬 장들의 주요 부분을 가져온 「일리아스」와 「오디세이아」의 시인 호메로스는, 그가 노래한 영웅들 못지않게 신화적인 인물이다. 전해 내려오는 이야기에 따르면 그는 눈멀고 늙은 떠돌이 음유시인으로, 군주들의 궁정이나 농부들의 오두막을 이곳저곳 떠돌며 자신의 하프 가락에 맞추어 노래를 부르고, 그 부양은 청중의 자발적인 헌금에 의존했다고 한다. 바이런은 그를 \"키오스의 바위섬에 사는 눈먼 노인\"이라 부르며, 그의 출생지에 관한 사실의 불확실함을 암시하는 잘 알려진 경구는 이렇게 말한다.\n\n\"일곱 개의 부유한 도시가 죽은 호메로스를 두고 다투나, 살아 있는 호메로스는 그 도시들에서 빵을 구걸하였네.\"\n\n이 일곱 도시는 스미르나, 키오스, 로도스, 콜로폰, 살라미스, 아르고스, 그리고 아테나이였다.\n\n근대 학자들은 호메로스의 시들이 과연 어느 한 사람의 마음에서 나온 작품인지 의심해 왔다. 이는 보통 이 시들에 부여되는 그처럼 이른 시대, 곧 현존하는 어떤 비문이나 주화보다도 앞서며, 그토록 긴 작품을 담아낼 만한 재료가 아직 쓰이지 않았던 시대에, 그처럼 긴 시들이 글로 기록될 수 있었다고 믿기 어렵기 때문에 생겨난다. 다른 한편으로는, 그처럼 긴 시들이 어떻게 오직 기억만으로 대대로 전해질 수 있었겠느냐는 물음이 제기된다. 이에 대한 답은, 음송시인이라 불리는 전문적인 집단이 있어 다른 이들의 시를 암송하였으며, 민족적이고 애국적인 전설을 기억해 두었다가 보수를 받고 낭송하는 것이 그들의 일이었다는 것이다.\n\n오늘날 학자들 사이에 우세한 견해는, 시들의 골격과 구조의 많은 부분이 호메로스에게 속하지만, 다른 사람들의 손으로 끼워 넣어지고 덧붙여진 부분이 무수히 많다는 것으로 보인다.\n\n헤로도토스의 권위에 따라 호메로스에게 부여된 연대는 기원전 850년이다.\n베르길리우스\n\n베르길리우스는 그의 성(姓)인 마로로도 불리는데, 우리가 그의 시 「아이네이스」에서 아이네아스의 이야기를 가져온 그는, 로마 황제 아우구스투스의 치세를 아우구스투스 시대라는 이름으로 그토록 유명하게 만든 위대한 시인들 가운데 하나였다. 베르길리우스는 기원전 70년 만토바에서 태어났다. 그의 위대한 시는 호메로스의 작품들에 버금가는 것으로, 시 작법의 최고 부류인 서사시에 든다. 베르길리우스는 독창성과 창의에서 호메로스에 훨씬 못 미치지만, 정확성과 우아함에서는 그를 능가한다. 영국 혈통의 비평가들에게는 근대 시인 가운데 오직 밀턴만이 이 빛나는 고대인들과 한자리에 놓일 만한 인물로 여겨진다. 우리가 그토록 많은 인용을 빌려 온 그의 시 「실낙원」은 여러 면에서 고대의 두 위대한 작품 어느 쪽과도 대등하며, 어떤 면에서는 그것들을 능가한다. 다음에 인용한 드라이든의 경구는, 그런 날카로운 비평에서 흔히 찾을 수 있는 만큼의 진실을 담아 세 시인을 특징짓는다.\n\n\"밀턴에 부쳐 \"세 시대에 태어난 세 시인이 그리스와 이탈리아와 잉글랜드를 빛냈네. 첫째는 영혼의 드높음에서 뛰어났고, 둘째는 위엄에서, 마지막은 그 둘 다에서. 자연의 힘은 더 나아갈 수 없었으니, 셋째를 만들려고 앞의 둘을 합쳤네.\"\n\n쿠퍼의 「탁상담화」에서.\n\n\"호메로스의 등불이 나타나기까지 여러 시대가 흘렀고, 만토바의 백조가 들리기까지 다시 여러 시대가 흘렀네. 이전엔 알지 못하던 길이로 자연을 끌어올려, 밀턴을 낳기까지는 더 많은 시대가 필요했네. 그리하여 천재는 정해진 때에 떠오르고 졌으며, 먼 풍토에 새벽빛을 쏘아 보내어, 그가 택한 모든 지역을 고귀하게 하였네. 그는 그리스에서 졌다가 이탈리아에서 떠올랐고, 고딕 암흑의 지루한 세월이 지난 뒤, 마침내 우리 섬에서 온통 찬란하게 솟아올랐네. 그리하여 사랑스러운 물총새들이 바다로 잠겨 들었다가, 저 멀리서 다시 그 빛나는 깃털을 보이는도다.\"\n\n오비디우스는,\n시에서 흔히 그의 다른 이름인 나소로 암시되는데, 기원전 43년에 태어났다. 그는 공직 생활을 위한 교육을 받아 상당히 높은 몇몇 관직을 지냈으나, 시가 그의 기쁨이었기에 일찍이 거기에 자신을 바치기로 결심하였다. 그리하여 그는 당대 시인들과의 교제를 추구하여 호라티우스와 친분을 맺고 베르길리우스를 만났는데, 다만 베르길리우스는 오비디우스가 아직 너무 어리고 이름이 알려지지 않아 친교를 맺기 어려웠던 무렵에 세상을 떠났다. 오비디우스는 넉넉한 수입을 누리며 로마에서 안락한 삶을 보냈다. 그는 황제 아우구스투스의 가문과 가까웠는데, 그 가문의 누군가에게 끼친 어떤 중대한 잘못이, 시인의 행복한 처지를 뒤엎고 그의 생애 후반 전체를 어둡게 한 사건의 원인이었으리라 추정된다. 쉰 살의 나이에 그는 로마에서 추방되어 흑해 변경의 토미로 가라는 명을 받았다. 이곳에서, 야만적인 사람들 사이에서 혹독한 기후 속에, 호사스러운 수도의 온갖 즐거움과 가장 저명한 동시대인들의 교제에 익숙했던 시인은, 슬픔과 근심에 시달리며 생애의 마지막 십 년을 보냈다. 유배지에서 그의 유일한 위안은 아내와 멀리 있는 벗들에게 글을 띄우는 것이었으며, 그의 편지는 모두 시로 되어 있었다. 비록 이 시들(「트리스티아」와 「폰토스에서 온 편지」)이 시인 자신의 슬픔 말고는 다른 주제를 다루지 않으나, 그의 빼어난 취향과 풍부한 창의가 그것들을 지루하다는 비난에서 구해 내었으니, 사람들은 즐겁게, 심지어 공감마저 느끼며 그것들을 읽는다.\n\n오비디우스의 두 위대한 작품은 「변신 이야기」와 「축제력」이다. 둘 다 신화적인 시이며, 우리는 앞의 것에서 그리스와 로마 신화에 관한 이야기 대부분을 가져왔다. 후대의 한 작가는 이 시들을 이렇게 평한다.\n\n\"그리스의 풍요로운 신화는, 오늘날에도 시인과 화가와 조각가에게 그러하듯, 오비디우스에게 예술의 재료를 제공하였다. 빼어난 취향과 소박함과 비애로써 그는 먼 옛 시대의 황당무계한 전승을 서술하고, 거기에 오직 거장의 손만이 부여할 수 있는 그 실재감을 입혔다. 그의 자연 묘사는 인상적이면서도 진실하니, 그는 적절한 것을 정성껏 골라내고 군더더기를 버려서, 작품을 완성했을 때 그것은 부족하지도 넘치지도 않는다. 「변신 이야기」는 젊은이가 즐겁게 읽고, 더 나이 든 시절에 다시 읽으면 한층 더 큰 기쁨을 준다. 시인은 자신의 시가 자신보다 오래 살아남아, 로마라는 이름이 알려진 곳이라면 어디서나 읽히리라고 감히 예언하였다.\"\n\n위에서 암시한 예언은 「변신 이야기」의 마지막 행들에 담겨 있는데, 아래에 그 직역을 싣는다.\n\n\"이제 나는 내 작품을 마치노니, 이는 유피테르의 진노도, 세월의 이빨도, 칼도, 불도 무너뜨리지 못하리라. 몸은 다스리되 마음은 다스리지 못하는 그날이, 언제든 와서 내 남은 생을 앗아 간다 해도, 나의 더 나은 부분은 별들 위로 솟아오를 것이며, 나의 명성은 영원토록 이어지리라. 로마의 무력과 학예가 퍼지는 곳이라면 어디서나, 그곳 사람들이 내 책을 읽으리라. 그리고 시인의 환상에 참된 것이 조금이라도 있다면, 나의 이름과 명성은 불멸을 누리리라.\"\n\n————\n\n# MODERN MONSTERS\n\n세상에는 일군의 상상 속 존재들이 있는데, 이것들은 옛 미신에 등장하는 \"고르고네스와 히드라들과 무시무시한 키마이라들\"의 후계자처럼 보인다. 또한 이교의 거짓 신들과는 아무런 관련이 없었던 까닭에, 이교가 그리스도교에 의해 밀려난 뒤에도 민간의 믿음 속에서 계속 존속할 수 있었던 것처럼 보인다. 어쩌면 이것들은 고전 작가들에 의해 언급되었을지도 모르지만, 그것들이 널리 알려지고 회자된 것은 오히려 더 근대에 들어와서인 듯하다. 우리는 이것들에 관한 이야기를 고대인의 시에서보다는 오래된 자연사 서적이나 여행자들의 기록에서 찾는다. 앞으로 우리가 소개할 이야기들은 주로 「페니 백과사전」에서 가져온 것이다.\n\n오비디우스는 불사조의 이야기를 다음과 같이 전한다. \"대부분의 존재는 다른 개체로부터 태어난다. 그러나 스스로를 재생산하는 한 종류가 있다. 아시리아인들은 이것을 불사조라고 부른다. 이 새는 열매나 꽃을 먹지 않고, 유향과 향기로운 수지(樹脂)를 먹고 산다. 오백 년을 살고 나면, 이 새는 떡갈나무 가지나 종려나무 꼭대기에 둥지를 짓는다. 그 안에 계피와 감송향과 몰약을 모으고, 이 재료들로 더미를 쌓아 그 위에 스스로 자리를 잡고, 죽음을 맞이하며 향기 속에서 마지막 숨을 내쉰다. 이 어미 새의 몸에서 어린 불사조가 솟아나오는데, 그것은 자기 선조와 마찬가지로 오랜 생애를 살도록 정해져 있다. 이 어린 새가 자라나 충분한 힘을 얻으면, 자기 요람이자 어버이의 무덤이었던 둥지를 나무에서 들어 올려, 이집트의 헬리오폴리스 시로 가져가, 태양의 신전에 안치한다.\"\n\n이것이 한 시인이 전하는 이야기다. 이제 어느 철학적 역사가의 이야기를 살펴보자. 타키투스는 이렇게 적었다. \"파울루스 파비우스의 집정관 재임기(서기 34년)에, 불사조라는 이름으로 세상에 알려진 기적의 새가 여러 시대에 걸쳐 자취를 감추었다가 다시 이집트를 찾아왔다. 이 새가 날아오를 때에는 갖가지 새들이 무리지어 뒤따랐는데, 모두가 이 진기함에 이끌려 그토록 아름다운 모습을 경이로운 눈으로 바라보았다.\" 그런 다음 그는 이 새에 관한 이야기를 전하는데, 앞의 이야기와 본질적으로 다르지 않으나 몇 가지 세부를 덧붙인다. \"어린 새가 깃털이 다 나서 제 날개를 믿고 날 수 있게 되자마자 가장 먼저 하는 일은 자기 아비의 장례를 치르는 것이다. 그러나 이 임무를 경솔하게 떠맡지는 않는다. 어린 새는 상당량의 몰약을 모으고, 자기 힘을 시험하고자 그 짐을 등에 진 채 자주 비행을 떠난다. 자신의 기력에 충분한 자신감을 얻으면, 어린 새는 아비의 몸을 들어 올려 태양의 제단으로 날아가, 향기로운 불꽃 속에서 사그라지도록 그것을 그곳에 남겨 둔다.\" 다른 작가들은 몇 가지 세부를 덧붙인다. 몰약은 알 모양으로 빚어지고, 죽은 불사조는 그 안에 봉인된다. 죽은 새의 썩어 가는 살에서 한 마리 벌레가 솟아나며, 이 벌레가 크게 자라면 한 마리 새로 변모한다. 헤로도토스는 이 새를 묘사하는데, 그러면서도 이렇게 말한다. \"나는 그림 속에서가 아니면 직접 본 적이 없다. 깃털의 일부는 금빛이고 일부는 진홍빛이며, 윤곽과 크기는 대체로 독수리와 매우 흡사하다.\"\n\n불사조의 존재에 대한 믿음을 처음으로 부정한 작가는 토머스 브라운 경으로, 1646년에 펴낸 「세속의 오류들」에서였다. 몇 년 뒤에 알렉산더 로스가 그에게 반박했는데, 불사조가 그토록 좀처럼 모습을 드러내지 않는다는 이의에 답하여 그는 이렇게 말한다. \"본능이 그 새에게 만물의 폭군인 인간의 길목을 피하라고 가르친다. 만일 잡힐 수 있다면, 세상에 단 한 마리밖에 남지 않았다 한들 어느 부유한 대식가가 분명 그것을 먹어 치우고 말 테니 말이다.\"\n\n드라이든은 자신의 초기 시 한 편에서 불사조를 이렇게 인유한다.\n\n\"그리하여 갓 태어난 불사조가 처음 모습을 드러내면, 깃털 달린 신민들 모두가 제 여왕을 흠모하고, 여왕이 동방을 가로질러 나아가는 동안, 숲마다 그 수많은 행렬이 불어나며, 창공의 시인들마다 그 영광을 노래하고, 기뻐하는 청중은 그 둘레에서 날개를 친다.\"\n\n밀턴은 「실낙원」제5권에서 천사 라파엘이 지상으로 내려오는 모습을 불사조에 견준다.\n\n\". . . 저 아래로, 날개 펴고 곧장 그가 서둘러 내려오니, 광막한 천공을 가로질러 세계와 세계 사이를 한결같은 날개로 항해하고, 이제는 극지의 바람을 타다가, 다시 재빠른 날갯짓으로 풍성한 대기를 부쳐 가른다. 마침내 높이 솟구치는 독수리들의 비상권 안에 이르니, 모든 새들에게 그는 불사조로 보이고, 모두가 그를 바라본다. 마치 제 유해를 태양의 빛나는 신전에 안치하고자 이집트의 테베로 날아가는 그 외로운 새처럼.\"\n\n# THE COCKATRICE, OR BASILISK\n\n이 짐승은 뱀들의 왕이라 불렸다. 그 왕권을 입증하듯, 이 짐승은 머리 위에 볏 또는 도가머리를 지니고 있어 그것이 왕관을 이루었다고 한다. 이 짐승은 수탉의 알이 두꺼비나 뱀의 품에서 부화하여 생겨난다고 여겨졌다. 이 짐승에는 여러 종류가 있었다. 한 종류는 가까이 다가가는 것은 무엇이든 태워 버렸다. 두 번째 종류는 일종의 떠도는 메두사의 머리와 같아서, 그 눈길을 받으면 곧바로 공포에 사로잡히고 그 즉시 죽음이 뒤따랐다. 셰익스피어의 희곡 「리처드 3세」에서, 앤 부인은 자기 눈을 칭찬하는 리처드의 말에 대답하며 이렇게 말한다. \"그 눈이 바실리스크의 눈이어서 당신을 쳐 죽일 수 있다면 좋으련만!\"\n\n바실리스크가 뱀들의 왕이라 불린 것은, 다른 모든 뱀과 구렁이가 선량한 신민처럼 처신하여, 현명하게도 태워지거나 쳐 죽임을 당하기를 원치 않은 까닭에, 비록 더없이 맛난 먹이를 한창 포식하고 있는 중이라 할지라도, 멀리서 제 왕의 쉭쉭거리는 소리가 들리는 순간 도망쳐 버리고, 그 잔치의 즐거움을 오롯이 왕다운 그 괴물에게 남겨 두었기 때문이다.\n\n로마의 박물학자 플리니우스는 이 짐승을 이렇게 묘사한다. \"이것은 다른 뱀들처럼 몸을 여러 차례 굽혀서 나아가는 것이 아니라, 높다랗게 곧추세운 채 나아간다. 이것은 접촉뿐 아니라 입김만으로도 관목을 죽이고, 바위를 쪼개니, 그토록 강한 사악함의 힘이 그 안에 있다.\" 옛날에는 만일 이 짐승을 말 탄 자가 창으로 죽이면, 그 무기를 따라 전달된 독의 힘이 기수뿐 아니라 말까지도 죽인다고 믿었다. 루카누스는 이 시구에서 그것을 인유한다.\n\n\"무어인이 바실리스크를 죽이고 모래벌판에 그 주검을 꿰어 박은들 무슨 소용인가, 그 미묘한 독은 창을 타고 위로 솟아올라, 손이 그것을 빨아들이니, 승자도 죽고 만다.\"\n\n이런 경이로운 존재가 성인들의 전설에서 그냥 지나쳐졌을 리 없다. 그리하여 우리는 다음과 같은 기록을 발견한다. 어느 거룩한 사람이 사막의 샘으로 가다가 문득 바실리스크 한 마리를 보았다. 그는 곧바로 눈을 들어 하늘을 우러르고, 경건하게 신께 호소하여 그 괴물을 자기 발치에 쓰러뜨려 죽였다.\n\n바실리스크의 이 놀라운 능력은 갈레노스, 아비센나, 스칼리게르 등 수많은 박식한 인물들이 증언한다. 더러는 나머지는 인정하면서도 이야기의 어느 부분에는 이의를 제기하곤 했다. 박식한 의사였던 욘스톤은 현명하게도 이렇게 말한다. \"그것이 눈길로 죽인다는 것은 좀처럼 믿을 수 없다. 그것을 보고도 살아남아 그 이야기를 전할 수 있는 자가 어디 있겠는가?\" 이 훌륭한 현인은, 이런 종류의 바실리스크를 사냥하러 가는 자들이 거울을 지니고 가서, 그 죽음의 눈빛을 그 주인에게 도로 반사시켜, 일종의 시적인 정의로써 바실리스크를 제 무기로 죽인다는 사실을 알지 못했던 것이다.\n\n그렇다면 이 무시무시하고 가까이할 수 없는 괴물을 공격할 수 있는 것은 무엇이었을까? \"모든 것에는 그 천적이 있다\"는 옛말이 있는데, 바실리스크는 족제비 앞에서 움츠러들었다. 바실리스크가 단검 같은 눈길을 던진들, 족제비는 아랑곳하지 않고 대담하게 싸움에 나섰다. 물리면 족제비는 잠시 물러나 운향(芸香)을 조금 뜯어 먹었는데, 운향은 바실리스크가 시들게 할 수 없는 유일한 식물이었다. 그런 다음 새로워진 힘과 온전한 몸으로 돌아와 다시 덤벼들었고, 적이 벌판에 쭉 뻗어 죽을 때까지 결코 그를 놓아주지 않았다. 또한 이 괴물은 자기가 세상에 태어난 정상적이지 못한 방식을 의식하기라도 한 듯, 수탉에게 큰 반감을 지녔다고 여겨졌다. 그럴 만도 한 것이, 수탉이 우는 소리를 듣자마자 이 괴물은 숨을 거두었기 때문이다.\n\n바실리스크는 죽은 뒤에도 어느 정도 쓸모가 있었다. 그리하여 우리는 그 주검이 아폴론의 신전과 개인의 집에 거미를 막는 으뜸가는 묘약으로 매달렸으며, 또한 아르테미스[로마의 〈디아나〉]의 신전에도 매달렸고, 그 까닭에 어떤 제비도 감히 그 신성한 장소에 들어오지 못했다는 기록을 본다.\n\n독자는 이쯤 되면 이런 터무니없는 이야기들을 충분히 들었으리라 짐작한다. 그러나 그래도 우리는 독자가 바실리스크의 생김새가 어떠했는지 알고 싶어 안달하는 모습을 상상할 수 있다. 다음은 16세기의 저명한 박물학자 알드로반디의 글에서 가져온 것인데, 자연사에 관한 그의 저작은 이절판 열세 권에 달하며, 값진 내용도 많지만 우화와 무익한 이야기도 큰 비중을 차지한다. 특히 그는 수탉과 황소에 관해 어찌나 장황하게 썼던지, 그의 그런 습관에서 비롯하여 신빙성이 의심스러운 온갖 두서없고 수다스러운 이야기를 가리켜 〈수탉과 황소 이야기〉라 부르게 되었다. 그렇지만 알드로반디는 식물원의 창설자로서, 또한 오늘날 널리 퍼진 관습, 곧 조사와 연구를 목적으로 과학적 수집을 행하는 일의 선구자로서 우리의 존경과 흠모를 받을 만하다.\n\n셸리는 1820년 나폴리에서 입헌 정부의 선포 소식에 자극받은 열정으로 가득한 「나폴리에 바치는 송시」에서 바실리스크를 다음과 같이 인유한다.\n\n\"킴메리아의 폭군들이 감히 자유와 그대를 모독한들 무슨 상관인가? 그들의 잘못은 새로운 악타이온의 잘못이 되리라, 제 사냥개들에게 잡아먹히고 마는! 그대는 제왕다운 바실리스크가 되어, 보이지 않는 상처로 그대의 적을 죽이라! 압제를 응시하라, 그 무서운 위험 앞에서 압제가 경악하여 이 땅의 표면에서 사라질 때까지. 두려워 말고 응시하라. 자유인은 더욱 강해지고, 노예는 제 적을 응시할수록 더욱 약해지니.\"\n\n# THE UNICORN\n\n로마의 박물학자 플리니우스는 일각수에 관한 이야기를 남겼는데, 근대의 일각수 대부분은 그의 이 기록에서 묘사되고 그려졌다. 그는 일각수를 이렇게 기록한다. \"지극히 사나운 짐승으로, 몸의 나머지는 말과 비슷하되, 사슴의 머리, 코끼리의 발, 멧돼지의 꼬리에, 깊고 우렁찬 울음소리를 내며, 이마 한가운데에 길이 두 큐빗의 검은 외뿔 하나가 솟아 있다.\" 그는 \"이것은 산 채로 잡을 수 없다\"고 덧붙이는데, 당시에 원형 경기장의 무대에 이 살아 있는 짐승을 내놓지 못하는 데 대해서는 그런 식의 변명이 필요했을지도 모른다.\n\n일각수는 사냥꾼들에게 몹시 골치 아픈 수수께끼였던 듯한데, 그들은 그토록 값진 사냥감에 어떻게 접근해야 할지 거의 알지 못했다. 어떤 이들은 그 뿔이 짐승의 뜻대로 움직인다고, 요컨대 일종의 작은 검과 같아서, 검술에 지극히 능란하지 않은 사냥꾼은 도무지 승산이 없다고 묘사했다. 또 어떤 이들은 그 짐승의 모든 힘이 뿔에 있어서, 추격에 몰리면 가장 높은 바위 꼭대기에서 뿔을 앞세워 몸을 던져 그 뿔로 착지하고, 그런 다음 떨어진 충격에도 조금도 손상 없이 유유히 걸어가 버린다고 주장했다.\n\n그러나 마침내 그들은 가엾은 일각수를 함정에 빠뜨리는 방법을 알아낸 듯하다. 그들은 이 짐승이 순결과 순수함을 무척 사랑한다는 사실을 발견했고, 그리하여 한 어린 처녀를 데리고 들판에 나가 아무것도 모르는 그 흠모자의 길목에 그녀를 세워 두었다. 일각수는 처녀를 발견하자 온갖 경의를 표하며 다가와 그녀 곁에 엎드려, 그 무릎에 제 머리를 누이고 잠이 들었다. 그러면 배신자 처녀가 신호를 보내고, 사냥꾼들이 들이닥쳐 그 순진한 짐승을 사로잡았다.\n\n근대의 동물학자들은 이런 우화들에 당연히 진저리를 내며, 일각수의 존재를 대체로 믿지 않는다. 그렇지만 머리에 뿔과 어느 정도 비슷한 뼈로 된 돌기를 지닌 동물들이 있어, 이것이 이 이야기를 낳았을 수 있다. 이른바 코뿔소의 뿔이 그런 돌기인데, 다만 높이가 몇 인치를 넘지 않으며, 일각수의 뿔에 관한 묘사와는 거리가 멀다. 이마 한가운데에 있는 뿔에 가장 가까운 것은 기린의 이마에 있는 뼈로 된 돌기인데, 이것 또한 짧고 뭉툭하며, 그 짐승의 유일한 뿔이 아니라 다른 두 뿔 앞쪽에 자리한 세 번째 뿔이다. 요컨대 코뿔소 외에 외뿔 달린 네발짐승의 존재를 부정하는 것은 주제넘은 일이겠으나, 말이나 사슴을 닮은 짐승의 살아 있는 이마에 길고 단단한 뿔이 박혀 있다는 것은 도저히 불가능에 가까운 일이라고 안전하게 말할 수 있다.\n\n# THE SALAMANDER\n\n다음은 16세기 이탈리아의 예술가 벤베누토 첼리니가 스스로 쓴 「벤베누토 첼리니의 생애」에서 가져온 것이다. \"내가 다섯 살쯤 되었을 때, 아버지가 마침 빨래를 하던 작은 방에 계셨는데, 그곳에는 떡갈나무 장작불이 잘 타오르고 있었다. 아버지가 불꽃을 들여다보니, 도마뱀을 닮은 작은 짐승 한 마리가 그 불기운의 가장 뜨거운 곳에서도 살 수 있는 듯 보였다. 그것이 무엇인지 곧바로 알아채신 아버지는 나와 누이를 부르셨고, 우리에게 그 짐승을 보여 주신 다음, 내 따귀를 한 대 때리셨다. 내가 울음을 터뜨리자, 아버지는 나를 어루만지며 달래시고는 이런 말씀을 하셨다. '얘야, 네가 무슨 잘못을 저질렀다고 그 매를 때린 것이 아니란다. 다만 네가 불 속에서 보는 그 작은 짐승이 살라만드라라는 것을 기억하라고 그런 것이다. 내가 아는 한 일찍이 본 적이 없는 그런 짐승이지.' 이렇게 말씀하시며 아버지는 나를 껴안고 돈을 좀 주셨다.\"\n\n첼리니 씨가 직접 눈으로 보고 귀로 들은 증인이었던 이야기를 의심하는 것은 부당해 보인다. 게다가 아리스토텔레스와 플리니우스를 필두로 하는 수많은 현명한 철학자들의 권위가 살라만드라의 이 능력을 단언한다. 그들에 따르면, 이 짐승은 불에 저항할 뿐 아니라 불을 끄기까지 하며, 불꽃을 보면 자기가 능히 무찌를 줄 아는 적을 대하듯 그것을 향해 돌진한다.\n\n불의 작용에 저항할 수 있는 짐승의 가죽이라면 그 불에도 끄떡없으리라 여겨지는 것은 놀랄 일이 아니다. 그리하여 우리는 살라만드라(실제로 그런 짐승, 곧 일종의 도마뱀이 있다)의 가죽으로 만든 천이 불에 타지 않으며, 다른 어떤 덮개에도 맡길 수 없을 만큼 귀한 물건을 싸 두는 데 매우 값지게 쓰였다는 사실을 발견한다. 이 불에 타지 않는 천은 실제로 만들어졌으며, 살라만드라의 털로 만든 것이라고 했으나, 사정에 밝은 이들은 그것을 이루는 물질이 석면임을 간파했다. 석면은 가는 섬유 형태로 되어 있어 유연한 천으로 짤 수 있는 광물이다.\n\n위 우화들의 근거는, 살라만드라가 실제로 제 몸의 모공에서 우윳빛 액체를 분비한다는 사실로 짐작된다. 이 액체는 짐승이 자극을 받으면 상당한 양으로 나오며, 의심할 바 없이 잠시 동안은 그 몸을 불로부터 지켜 줄 것이다. 게다가 이것은 겨울잠을 자는 짐승이라서, 겨울이면 속이 빈 나무나 다른 구멍 속으로 들어가 몸을 둘둘 말고, 봄이 다시 그것을 불러낼 때까지 마비된 상태로 머문다. 그러므로 이것은 이따금 땔감과 함께 불 속으로 옮겨질 수 있고, 제 모든 능력을 발휘하여 스스로를 지킬 만큼만 겨우 때맞추어 깨어날 수도 있다. 그 끈적한 액체가 큰 도움이 될 것이며, 그것을 보았다고 자처하는 모든 이들은 그것이 다리가 옮겨 줄 수 있는 한 가장 빠르게 불 속에서 빠져나갔다고 인정한다. 실로 너무 빨라서, 단 한 차례를 제외하고는 한 마리도 잡을 수 없었으며, 그 한 차례에도 짐승의 발과 몸 일부는 심하게 그을렸다.\n\n영 박사는 「밤의 상념」에서, 그다지 고상하다고는 할 수 없는 기발함으로, 별이 빛나는 하늘을 바라보면서도 아무런 감동 없이 머무는 회의론자를 불 속에서도 데워지지 않는 살라만드라에 견준다.\n\n\"신앙심 없는 천문학자는 미친 것이다! . . . . . . . \"오, 저 하늘에 깃든 것은 얼마나 위대한 천재인가! 그런데 로렌초의 살라만드라 같은 심장은 이 신성한 불길 속에서도 차갑고 무감각하단 말인가?\"\n\n————\n\n# CHAPTER XXXVII\n\n우리가 고대 페르시아인의 종교에 관해 아는 바는 주로 그들의 경전인 「젠다베스타」에서 유래한다. 조로아스터는 그들 종교의 창시자, 아니 오히려 그에 앞선 종교를 개혁한 인물이었다. 그가 살았던 시기는 분명하지 않으나, 그의 체계가 키루스의 시대(기원전 550년)부터 알렉산드로스 대왕의 페르시아 정복에 이르기까지 서아시아의 지배적 종교가 되었다는 것은 확실하다. 마케도니아 왕조 치하에서 조로아스터의 교리는 외래 사상의 유입으로 상당히 변질된 것으로 보이나, 이후 다시 우위를 회복하였다.\n\n조로아스터는 한 지고의 존재가 있다고 가르쳤는데, 그 존재는 또 다른 두 강력한 존재를 창조하고 자신의 본성 가운데 자신이 합당하다고 여긴 만큼을 그들에게 나누어 주었다. 이 가운데 오르무즈드(그리스인은 오로마스데스라 불렀다)는 창조자에게 충실하여 모든 선의 근원으로 간주되었으나, 아흐리만(아리마네스)은 반역하여 지상의 모든 악의 장본인이 되었다. 오르무즈드는 인간을 창조하고 그에게 행복에 필요한 온갖 재료를 마련해 주었으나, 아흐리만은 세상에 악을 들여오고 사나운 짐승과 독을 품은 파충류며 식물을 만들어 냄으로써 이 행복을 손상시켰다. 그 결과 이제 악과 선은 세상 곳곳에서 뒤섞여 있고, 선과 악을 따르는 자들, 곧 오르무즈드와 아흐리만의 추종자들은 끊임없는 전쟁을 이어 간다. 그러나 이러한 상태가 영원히 지속되지는 않을 것이다. 오르무즈드의 추종자들이 어디서나 승리하고, 아흐리만과 그의 추종자들이 영원히 어둠에 처해지는 때가 올 것이다.\n\n고대 페르시아인의 종교 의례는 지극히 소박하였다. 그들은 신전도, 제단도, 조각상도 쓰지 않았으며, 산꼭대기에서 제사를 올렸다. 그들은 불과 빛과 태양을 모든 빛과 순수함의 근원인 오르무즈드의 상징으로 숭배하였으나, 그것들을 독립된 신격으로 여기지는 않았다. 종교적 예식과 의례는 마기라 불리는 사제들이 관장하였다. 마기의 학문은 점성술과 주술과 관련되어 있었는데, 그 방면에서 워낙 이름이 높았던 까닭에 그들의 이름은 온갖 부류의 마법사와 주술사를 가리키는 말로 쓰이게 되었다.\n\n워즈워스는 페르시아인의 숭배를 이렇게 노래하였다.\n\n“……페르시아인은—제단과 형상을, 그리고 사람의 손으로 지은 신전의 둘러싼 벽과 지붕을 열렬히 물리치고는— 가장 높은 봉우리에 올라, 그 꼭대기에서, 이마에 도금양 화관을 두른 채, 달과 별들에게, 그리고 바람과 어머니 같은 원소들에게, 또한 하늘의 온 둘레에게 제사를 바쳤으니, 그에게 그것은 살아 숨 쉬는 존재이자 신이었다.” —「소요(逍遙)」, 제4권.\n\n바이런은 「차일드 해럴드」에서 페르시아인의 숭배를 이렇게 말한다.\n\n“초기 페르시아인이 자신의 제단을 높은 곳과 대지를 굽어보는 산봉우리로 삼은 것은 헛된 일이 아니었으니, 그리하여 그는 벽 없는 알맞은 신전을 얻어, 거기서 신을 찾았던 것이다. 사람의 손으로 세운 성소가 그 영광 앞에서 미약한 그 신을. 와서 견주어 보라, 고트풍이든 그리스풍이든, 기둥과 우상의 거처를, 자연의 숭배의 영역인 대지와 대기와. 그대의 기도를 가둘 정겨운 거처에 매이지 말라.” 제3편, 91연.\n\n조로아스터의 종교는 그리스도교가 들어온 뒤에도 번성을 이어 갔고, 3세기에는 동방의 지배적 신앙이었으나, 마호메트 세력이 일어나고 7세기에 아랍인이 페르시아를 정복하자, 그들은 대다수의 페르시아인에게 옛 신앙을 버리도록 강요하였다. 조상의 종교를 버리기를 거부한 자들은 케르만의 사막과 힌두스탄으로 달아났으며, 그곳에서 그들은 오늘날까지도 파르시라는 이름으로 존속하고 있는데, 이 이름은 페르시아의 옛 이름인 파르스에서 유래하였다. 아랍인은 그들을 게베르라 부르는데, 이는 불신자를 뜻하는 아랍어에서 나온 말이다. 봄베이에서 파르시는 오늘날 매우 활동적이고 지적이며 부유한 계층을 이룬다. 삶의 청정함과 정직함, 그리고 온화한 처신으로 그들은 좋은 평판을 얻고 있다. 그들은 불을 모시는 신전을 여럿 두고 있으며, 그 불을 신성의 상징으로 숭배한다.\n\n페르시아의 종교는 무어의 「랄라 루크」 가운데 가장 아름다운 이야기인 「불을 섬기는 자들」의 소재가 된다. 게베르족 우두머리는 이렇게 말한다.\n\n“그렇다! 나는 그 불경한 족속, 불의 종들 가운데 하나이니, 아침저녁으로 하늘의 살아 있는 빛들 사이에 깃든 창조자의 거처에 인사를 올린다. 그렇다! 나는 그 버림받은 무리에 속하니, 이란에, 그리고 복수에 충실하여, 그대의 아랍인들이 와서 우리의 불꽃 성소를 더럽힌 그 시각을 저주하며, 신의 불타는 눈앞에서 맹세하노라, 우리 나라의 사슬을 끊지 못하면 죽으리라고.”\n\n# HINDU MYTHOLOGY\n\n힌두인의 종교는 명목상 베다에 근거를 둔다. 그들은 자신들의 이 경전에 더없는 신성함을 부여하며, 천지창조 때 브라마 자신이 그것을 지었다고 말한다. 그러나 현재 전하는 베다의 편제는 약 오천 년 전 현인 비아사의 것으로 여겨진다.\n\n베다는 의심할 여지 없이 유일한 지고신에 대한 믿음을 가르친다. 이 신격의 이름은 브라마이다. 그의 속성은 창조와 보존과 파괴라는 세 가지 의인화된 권능으로 나타나는데, 각기 브라마, 비슈누, 시바라는 이름 아래 트리무르티, 곧 힌두 주신(主神)의 삼위를 이룬다. 하위 신들 가운데 가장 중요한 것은 다음과 같다. 첫째, 하늘과 천둥과 번개와 폭풍과 비의 신 인드라. 둘째, 불의 신 아그니. 셋째, 저승의 신 야마. 넷째, 태양의 신 수리야.\n\n브라마는 우주의 창조자이며, 모든 개별 신격이 솟아난 근원이자 종국에는 모두가 흡수되어 들어갈 그곳이다. “우유가 응유로 바뀌고 물이 얼음으로 바뀌듯이, 브라마는 어떠한 외부의 수단도 없이 갖가지로 변모하고 다양해진다.” 베다에 따르면 인간의 영혼은, 마치 불에서 튄 불티가 그러하듯, 지고의 통치자의 한 부분이다.\n\n비슈누는 힌두인의 삼위 가운데 두 번째 자리를 차지하며, 보존의 원리를 의인화한 존재이다. 위기에 처한 여러 시대에 세상을 보호하기 위하여, 비슈누는 갖가지 화신, 곧 육신의 형상으로 지상에 내려왔는데, 이 강림을 아바타르라 부른다. 그 수는 매우 많으나, 그중 열 가지가 특히 자세히 지목된다. 첫 번째 아바타르는 물고기인 마트시아였으니, 이 형상으로 비슈누는 온 세상을 덮친 대홍수 동안 인류의 조상 마누를 보호하였다. 두 번째 아바타르는 거북의 형상이었는데, 신들이 불사의 음료 암리타를 얻으려 바다를 휘저을 때 대지를 떠받치고자 이 형상을 취하였다.\n\n나머지 아바타르들은 같은 성격, 곧 정의를 지키거나 악행을 저지른 자를 벌하려는 개입의 사례이므로 생략하고, 비슈누의 아바타르 가운데 가장 이름난 아홉 번째로 넘어갈 수 있다. 여기서 그는 무적의 전사 크리슈나라는 인간 형상으로 나타났으니, 그는 자신의 위업으로 대지를 짓누르던 폭군들로부터 세상을 구원하였다.\n\n부처는 브라마교 신앙의 추종자들에 의해 비슈누의 기만적 화신으로 간주된다. 비슈누가 신들의 적인 아수라들로 하여금 베다의 신성한 율법을 저버리도록 꾀하려고 이 화신을 취하였으며, 그 수단으로 아수라들은 자신들의 힘과 우위를 잃었다는 것이다.\n\n칼키는 열 번째 아바타르의 이름으로, 비슈누는 현세의 시대가 끝날 무렵 이 모습으로 나타나 온갖 악덕과 사악함을 멸하고 인류를 미덕과 순결로 되돌릴 것이다.\n\n시바는 힌두 삼위의 세 번째 존재이다. 그는 파괴의 원리를 의인화한 존재이다. 비록 세 번째 이름이긴 하나, 그를 숭배하는 자의 수와 그 숭배의 범위로 보면 그는 다른 둘 가운데 어느 쪽보다도 앞선다. 푸라나(현대 힌두교의 경전)에서는 파괴자로서의 이 신의 본래 권능에 대해 아무런 언급이 없는데, 그 권능은 천이백만 년이 지난 뒤, 곧 우주가 종말에 이를 때라야 비로소 발휘되기 때문이다. 그리하여 마하데바(시바의 또 다른 이름)는 파괴라기보다는 오히려 재생의 표상이라 하겠다.\n\n비슈누의 숭배자들과 시바의 숭배자들은 두 종파를 이루는데, 저마다 자기가 받드는 신격의 우월함을 내세우며 다른 쪽의 주장을 부인한다. 창조자 브라마는 자신의 일을 마쳤으므로 더 이상 활동하지 않는 것으로 여겨지는 듯하며, 이제 인도에 신전을 단 하나만 두고 있는 데 반해, 마하데바와 비슈누는 여럿을 두고 있다. 비슈누의 숭배자들은 대체로 생명을 더 가엾이 여기고, 그에 따라 동물성 음식을 삼가며, 시바 추종자들의 숭배보다 덜 잔혹한 숭배를 행하는 것으로 구별된다.\n\n자간나트의 숭배자들을 비슈누의 추종자로 보아야 할지 시바의 추종자로 보아야 할지에 관해서는 우리의 전거들이 엇갈린다. 그 신전은 캘커타에서 남서쪽으로 약 삼백 마일 떨어진 해안 가까이에 서 있다. 우상은 깎아 만든 나무토막으로, 흉측한 얼굴은 검게 칠해져 있고 핏빛으로 벌어진 입을 하고 있다. 축제일이면 이 형상의 좌대가 바퀴로 움직이는 높이 육십 피트의 탑 위에 놓인다. 탑에는 여섯 가닥의 긴 밧줄이 매여 있어 사람들이 그것으로 탑을 끌고 간다. 사제들과 그 시중꾼들은 탑 위의 좌대를 둘러싸고 서서, 이따금 숭배자들을 향해 노래와 몸짓을 한다. 탑이 나아가는 동안 독실한 숭배자들 가운데 다수가 바퀴에 깔리려고 땅바닥에 몸을 내던지며, 군중은 그 행위가 우상에게 바치는 흡족한 제물이라며 환호로 그것을 추인한다. 해마다, 특히 3월과 7월의 두 큰 축제 때에는 순례자들이 무리 지어 신전으로 몰려든다. 이러한 때에는 이곳을 찾는 사람이 적어도 칠팔만 명에 이른다고 하며, 그때만큼은 모든 카스트가 함께 음식을 먹는다.\n\n힌두인을 정해진 직업을 가진 계급, 곧 카스트로 나누는 일은 아주 먼 옛날부터 있었다. 어떤 이들은 그것이 정복에서 비롯되었다고 보는데, 처음의 세 카스트는 외래 종족으로 이루어져 그들이 그 땅의 토착민을 정복하여 하위 카스트로 떨어뜨렸다는 것이다. 또 어떤 이들은 특정 직무나 직업을 아비에게서 아들로 대물림하여 영속시키려는 애착에서 그 기원을 찾는다.\n\n힌두인의 전승은 여러 카스트의 기원에 대해 다음과 같이 전한다. 천지창조 때 브라마는 자신의 몸에서 직접 발산되어 나온 거주자들을 대지에 주기로 결심하였다. 그리하여 그의 입에서 맏아들 브라만(사제)이 나왔으니, 브라마는 그에게 네 베다를 맡겼다. 그의 오른팔에서는 샤트리야(전사)가, 왼팔에서는 그 전사의 아내가 나왔다. 그의 넓적다리는 남녀 바이샤(농사꾼과 상인)를 낳았고, 마지막으로 그의 발에서 수드라(직공과 노동자)가 솟아났다.\n\n이토록 의미심장하게 세상에 나온 브라마의 네 아들은 인류의 조상이자 각 카스트의 우두머리가 되었다. 그들은 네 베다를 자기 신앙의 모든 규율과 종교 의례에서 그들을 인도할 모든 것을 담은 책으로 여기라는 명을 받았다. 또한 그들은 태어난 순서대로 서열을 정하되, 브라만이 브라마의 머리에서 솟아난 까닭에 맨 위에 서라는 명도 받았다.\n\n처음의 세 카스트와 수드라 사이에는 뚜렷한 경계선이 그어져 있다. 앞의 셋은 베다로부터 가르침을 받는 것이 허용되나, 수드라에게는 허용되지 않는다. 브라만은 베다를 가르치는 특권을 지니며, 옛날에는 모든 지식을 독차지하고 있었다. 그 나라의 군주는 라지푸트라고도 불리는 샤트리야 계급에서 선택되었으나, 실제 권력은 브라만이 쥐고 있었으니, 그들은 왕의 고문이자 그 나라의 재판관이며 행정관이었다. 그들의 신체와 재산은 침범할 수 없었고, 비록 가장 큰 죄를 저지른다 하여도 왕국에서 추방되는 것이 고작이었다. 군주들은 그들을 더없는 존경으로 대해야 했으니, “브라만은 학식이 있든 무지하든 강력한 신격”이기 때문이었다.\n\n브라만이 장성한 나이에 이르면 혼인하는 것이 그의 의무가 된다. 그는 부유한 자들의 헌납으로 부양되어야 하며, 어떤 고된 일이나 생산적인 직업으로 생계를 꾸려야 할 처지에 놓여서는 안 된다. 그러나 모든 브라만을 공동체의 노동 계급이 부양할 수는 없었으므로, 그들이 생산적인 일에 종사하는 것을 허용할 수밖에 없었다.\n\n중간의 두 계급에 대해서는 별로 말할 것이 없으니, 그들의 지위와 특권은 그들의 직업에서 쉽게 미루어 알 수 있다. 네 번째 계급인 수드라는 상위 계급, 특히 브라만에게 노예처럼 시중드는 일에 매여 있으나, 그림이나 글쓰기 같은 기계적 직업과 실용적 기예에 종사하거나 상인이나 농부가 될 수도 있다. 그 결과 그들은 때로 부유해지기도 하며, 또한 브라만이 가난해지는 일도 더러 일어난다. 그 사실은 으레 따르는 결과를 낳아, 부유한 수드라가 가난한 브라만을 비천한 일에 부리기도 한다.\n\n수드라보다도 더 낮은 또 하나의 계급이 있는데, 이는 본래의 순수한 계급에 속하지 않고 서로 다른 카스트에 속한 개인들의 허락되지 않은 결합에서 생겨난 것이다. 이들이 바로 파리아로, 가장 천한 일에 부려지며 더없이 가혹한 대우를 받는다. 그들은 다른 누구도 더럽혀지지 않고서는 할 수 없는 일을 하도록 강요받는다. 그들은 그들 자신이 부정하다고 여겨질 뿐만 아니라, 그들이 손대는 모든 것을 부정하게 만든다. 그들은 일체의 시민적 권리를 빼앗기며, 그들의 생활 방식과 집과 가구를 규제하는 특별한 법으로 낙인찍힌다. 그들은 다른 카스트의 파고다나 신전을 찾는 것이 허용되지 않고, 자신들만의 파고다와 종교 행사를 갖는다. 그들은 다른 카스트의 집에 들어가는 것이 용납되지 않으며, 부주의하게 또는 부득이하게 그런 일이 일어나면 그 자리는 종교 의례로 정화되어야 한다. 그들은 공공의 시장에 나타나서는 안 되며, 특정한 우물만 쓰도록 제한되는데, 다른 이들이 그 우물을 쓰지 않도록 경고하기 위해 그 둘레에 짐승의 뼈를 둘러놓아야 한다. 그들은 도시와 마을에서 멀리 떨어진 비참한 오두막에 살며, 음식에 관해서는 아무런 제약을 받지 않는데, 이것은 특권이 아니라 치욕의 표지이니, 마치 그들이 너무도 비천하여 무엇으로도 더럽혀질 것이 없는 것처럼 여겨지는 까닭이다. 상위의 세 카스트는 고기를 먹는 것이 전적으로 금지된다. 네 번째 카스트는 쇠고기를 제외한 모든 종류를 먹는 것이 허용되나, 오직 가장 낮은 카스트만이 어떠한 음식이든 제약 없이 먹는 것이 허용된다.\n\n베다가 비슈누의 기만적 화신으로 묘사하는 부처는, 그 추종자들에 따르면 본디 가우타마라는 이름의 인간 현인이었다고 하는데, 그는 사자(獅子)를 뜻하는 사키아신하와 현인을 뜻하는 부처라는 칭송의 별칭으로도 불린다.\n\n그의 탄생에 부여된 여러 시기를 견주어 보면, 그는 그리스도보다 약 천 년 전에 살았던 것으로 추정된다.\n\n그는 한 왕의 아들이었다. 그 나라의 관습에 따라 태어난 지 며칠 뒤 한 신격의 제단 앞에 바쳐졌을 때, 그 형상이 새로 태어난 예언자의 장차 이루어질 위대함을 예고하듯 머리를 숙였다고 한다. 그 아이는 곧 으뜸가는 자질을 드러냈고, 보기 드문 용모의 아름다움으로도 똑같이 두드러졌다. 장성한 나이에 이르자마자 그는 인류의 타락과 비참함을 깊이 성찰하기 시작하였고, 세상에서 물러나 명상에 몸을 바치겠다는 생각을 품었다. 그의 아버지는 이 뜻을 막으려 하였으나 헛수고였다. 부처는 호위병들의 경계를 벗어나, 안전한 은신처를 찾아 육 년 동안 방해받지 않고 경건한 사색에 잠겨 지냈다. 그 기간이 끝나자 그는 베나레스에 종교적 스승으로 나섰다. 처음에는 그의 말을 들은 어떤 이들이 그의 정신이 온전한지 의심하였으나, 그의 교리는 이내 신망을 얻었고 워낙 빠르게 퍼져 나가, 부처 자신이 그것이 인도 전역에 퍼지는 것을 살아서 보았다. 그는 여든 살의 나이로 세상을 떠났다.\n\n불교도는 베다의 권위와, 거기에 규정되어 힌두인이 지키는 종교적 의례를 전적으로 배격한다. 그들은 또한 카스트의 구별을 배격하고, 일체의 피 흘리는 제사를 금하며, 동물성 음식을 허용한다. 그들의 사제는 모든 계급에서 선택되며, 떠돌며 동냥하여 생계를 마련하도록 기대되고, 다른 무엇보다 남들이 쓸모없다며 내버린 것을 어떻게든 쓸모 있게 만들고 식물의 약효를 찾아내는 일이 그들의 의무이다. 그러나 실론에서는 세 등급의 사제가 인정되는데, 가장 높은 등급에 속한 자들은 대개 가문이 좋고 학식이 높은 이들로, 주요 신전에서 부양을 받으며, 그 신전 대부분은 그 나라의 옛 군주들이 풍족하게 기진(寄進)한 것이다.\n\n부처가 나타난 뒤 여러 세기 동안 그의 종파는 브라만들의 묵인을 받았던 것으로 보이며, 불교는 힌두스탄 반도 곳곳으로 사방으로 스며들었고, 실론과 동쪽 반도로도 전해진 것으로 보인다. 그러나 그 뒤 인도에서는 오래도록 이어진 박해를 견뎌야 했으니, 그 박해는 끝내 그것이 발생한 바로 그 나라에서 그것을 완전히 없애 버리는 결과를 낳았으나, 한편으로는 그것을 인접한 여러 나라로 널리 흩뿌리는 결과도 낳았다. 불교는 우리 기원으로 65년경에 중국으로 도입된 것으로 보인다. 중국으로부터 그것은 이후 고려와 일본과 자바로 퍼져 나갔다.\n\n# THE GRAND LAMA\n\n신성한 정신의 발산물인 인간의 영혼이 인간의 육체에 갇혀 있는 것은 비참한 상태이며, 이전의 생존 동안 저지른 약점과 죄과의 결과라는 것은 브라만교 힌두인과 불교 종파 양쪽이 한가지로 지니는 교리이다. 그러나 그들은, 지상의 생존을 겪어야 할 필요가 없으면서도 인류의 안녕을 도모하기 위해 스스로 지상에 내려온 소수의 사람들이 때때로 이 땅에 나타났다고 믿는다. 이러한 사람들은 차츰 부처 자신이 다시 나타난 존재라는 성격을 띠게 되었고, 그러한 자격으로 그 계보는 오늘날까지 이어져, 티베트와 중국, 그리고 불교가 성행하는 그 밖의 나라들의 여러 라마로 계속되고 있다. 칭기즈칸과 그 후계자들의 승리의 결과로, 티베트에 머무는 라마는 그 종파의 으뜸 사제의 지위로 격상되었다. 그에게는 그 자신의 영토로 따로 한 지방이 주어졌고, 그는 영적 위엄에 더하여 제한된 범위에서나마 세속의 군주가 되었다. 그는 달라이 라마라 일컬어진다.\n\n티베트로 나아간 초기 그리스도교 선교사들은 아시아의 한복판에서 로마 가톨릭 교회의 그것을 닮은 교황청 같은 궁정과 그 밖의 여러 교회 제도를 발견하고는 놀랐다. 그들은 사제와 수녀를 위한 수도원을 발견하였고, 또한 큰 위엄과 화려함이 곁들여진 행렬과 종교 예배의 형식도 발견하였다. 많은 이들이 이러한 유사함에 이끌려 라마교를 일종의 타락한 그리스도교로 여기게 되었다. 라마들이 이러한 관행 가운데 일부를, 티베트에 불교가 도입되던 무렵 타타르에 정착해 있던 네스토리우스파 그리스도교도에게서 끌어왔을 가능성도 없지 않다.\n\n# PRESTER JOHN\n\n타타르인 사이에 라마, 곧 영적 우두머리가 있다는 것에 관해, 아마도 떠도는 상인들이 전했을 어떤 초기의 소식이, 유럽에서 상부 아시아에 거주하는 그리스도교 교황 프레스비테르 또는 프레스터 존에 관한 소문을 불러일으킨 듯하다. 교황은 그를 찾아 사절을 보냈고, 몇 년 뒤 프랑스의 루이 9세도 그러하였으나, 두 사절단 모두 성과를 거두지 못하였다. 다만 그들이 실제로 찾아낸 작은 규모의 네스토리우스파 그리스도교도 공동체들이 동방 어딘가에 그러한 인물이 실재한다는 믿음을 유럽에서 이어 가게 하는 데 한몫을 하였다. 마침내 15세기에 포르투갈의 여행가 페드로 코빌량이, 홍해에서 멀지 않은 아비시니인(아비시니아)의 나라에 한 그리스도교 군주가 있다는 말을 우연히 듣고는, 이가 분명 진짜 프레스터 존일 것이라고 결론지었다. 그리하여 그는 그리로 가서, 자신이 네구스라 부르는 그 왕의 궁정까지 들어갔다. 밀턴은 「실낙원」 제11권에서 그를 언급하는데, 거기서 그는 아담이 대지의 표면에 흩어진 갖가지 민족과 도시 속에서 살아가는 그 후손들을 환영으로 보는 장면을 묘사하며 이렇게 말한다.\n\n“……그의 눈은 또한 알아보았으니 네구스의 제국을, 그 가장 먼 항구 에르코코에 이르기까지, 그리고 더 작은 바닷가 왕들, 몸바자와 퀼로아와 멜린드를.”\n\n————\n\n# NORTHERN MYTHOLOGY\n\n지금까지 우리의 관심을 끌었던 이야기들은 남방 지역의 신화에 관한 것이었다. 그러나 고대의 미신 가운데에는 결코 완전히 간과해서는 안 될 또 하나의 갈래가 있으니, 특히 그것은 우리가 잉글랜드인 선조들을 통해 그 기원을 물려받은 여러 민족에 속한 것이기 때문이다. 이는 곧 스칸디나비아인이라 불리는 북방 민족들의 신화로서, 그들은 오늘날 스웨덴, 덴마크, 노르웨이, 아이슬란드로 알려진 나라들에 거주했다. 이 신화적 기록들은 에다라고 불리는 두 묶음의 문헌에 담겨 있는데, 그중 더 오래된 것은 운문으로 되어 있으며 그 연대가 1056년까지 거슬러 올라가고, 더 근래의 것 곧 산문 에다는 1640년의 것이다.\n\n에다에 따르면 한때는 위로 하늘도 없고 아래로 땅도 없었으며, 오직 바닥 없는 심연과, 그 안에 샘이 흐르는 안개의 세계만이 있었다. 이 샘에서 열두 줄기의 강이 흘러나왔고, 그 강들이 발원지에서 멀리 흘러갔을 때 얼음으로 얼어붙었으며, 한 켜 위에 또 한 켜가 쌓여 그 거대한 심연이 가득 메워졌다.\n\n안개의 세계에서 남쪽으로는 빛의 세계가 있었다. 그곳에서 따뜻한 바람이 얼음 위로 불어와 얼음을 녹였다. 수증기가 공중으로 솟아올라 구름을 이루었고, 그 구름에서 서리 거인 이미르와 그 자손이, 그리고 암소 아우둠블라가 생겨났는데, 이 암소의 젖은 거인에게 자양분과 양식을 주었다. 암소는 얼음에서 서리와 소금을 핥아 자양분을 얻었다. 어느 날 그 암소가 소금기 어린 돌을 핥고 있을 때, 처음에는 한 사람의 머리카락이 나타났고, 이튿날에는 머리 전체가, 사흘째에는 아름다움과 민첩함과 힘을 갖춘 온전한 형상이 드러났다. 이 새로운 존재는 한 신이었으며, 그와 그의 아내인 거인족의 한 딸에게서 오딘, 빌리, 베 세 형제가 태어났다. 그들은 거인 이미르를 죽였고, 그 몸으로 땅을 빚었으며, 그 피로 바다를, 그 뼈로 산을, 그 머리카락으로 나무를, 그 두개골로 하늘을, 그 뇌로는 우박과 눈을 머금은 구름을 만들었다. 신들은 이미르의 눈썹으로 미드가르드[가운데 땅]를 빚었으니, 이는 장차 인간의 거처가 될 운명이었다.\n\n그런 다음 오딘은 하늘에 해와 달을 두고 그것들에게 각자의 운행을 정해 줌으로써 낮과 밤의 시기와 계절을 다스렸다. 해가 땅 위로 그 빛을 비추기 시작하자마자, 식물의 세계가 싹트고 움텄다. 신들이 세상을 창조한 직후, 그들은 자신들의 새로운 작품에 흡족해하며 바닷가를 거닐었으나, 그것이 아직 완전하지 못함을 알았으니, 거기에 인간이 없었기 때문이다. 그리하여 그들은 물푸레나무 한 그루를 가져다 그것으로 남자를 만들었고, 딱총나무로 여자를 만들었으며, 남자를 아스케라 부르고 여자를 엠블라라 불렀다. 그런 다음 오딘은 그들에게 생명과 영혼을 주었고, 빌리는 이성과 운동을, 베는 감각과 풍부한 표정과 언어를 베풀어 주었다. 미드가르드가 그들의 거처로 주어졌으며, 그들은 인류의 시조가 되었다.\n\n거대한 물푸레나무 위그드라실은 온 우주를 떠받친다고 여겨졌다. 그것은 이미르의 몸에서 솟아났으며, 세 개의 어마어마한 뿌리를 지녔으니, 하나는 아스가르드[신들의 거처]로, 또 하나는 요툰헤임[거인들의 거처]으로, 셋째는 니플헤임[어둠과 추위의 영역]으로 뻗어 있었다. 이 뿌리들 각각의 곁에는 샘이 하나씩 있어 거기서 물을 댄다. 아스가르드로 뻗은 뿌리는 운명을 관장하는 자로 여겨지는 세 여신, 곧 노른들이 정성껏 돌본다. 그들은 우르두르[과거], 베르단디[현재], 스쿨드[미래]이다. 요툰헤임 쪽의 샘은 이미르의 우물로서, 그 안에는 지혜와 기지가 감추어져 있고, 니플헤임의 샘은 끊임없이 뿌리를 갉아먹는 독사 니드호게[어둠]를 기른다. 네 마리의 수사슴이 그 나무의 가지를 가로질러 달리며 새싹을 깨문다. 그들은 사방의 바람을 나타낸다. 나무 아래에는 이미르가 누워 있어, 그가 그 무게를 떨쳐 내려 할 때면 땅이 흔들린다.\n\n아스가르드는 신들의 거처를 일컫는 이름으로, 그곳으로 들어가는 길은 오직 비프로스트[무지개]라는 다리를 건너야만 얻을 수 있다. 아스가르드는 황금과 은으로 된 궁전들, 곧 신들의 거처로 이루어져 있는데, 그중에서도 가장 아름다운 것은 발할라, 곧 오딘의 거처이다. 오딘은 자신의 옥좌에 앉아 온 하늘과 땅을 굽어본다. 그의 양어깨에는 까마귀 후긴과 무닌이 앉아 있어, 날마다 온 세상을 날아다니다가 돌아와서는 보고 들은 모든 것을 그에게 고한다. 그의 발치에는 두 마리 늑대 게리와 프레키가 누워 있는데, 오딘은 자기 앞에 차려진 모든 고기를 그들에게 주니, 그 자신은 음식이 필요 없기 때문이다. 벌꿀술이 그에게는 음식이자 음료이다. 그는 룬 문자를 고안했으며, 운명의 룬을 금속 방패에 새기는 것은 노른들의 일이다. 오딘의 이름은 더러 보덴이라고도 적히는데, 거기서 일주일의 넷째 날 이름인 수요일이 비롯되었다.\n\n오딘은 흔히 알파두르[만물의 아버지]라 불리는데, 다만 이 이름이 쓰이는 방식을 보면 스칸디나비아인들이 오딘보다 우월하며 창조되지 않고 영원한 어떤 신성에 대한 관념을 지니고 있었음을 알 수 있는 경우도 있다.\n\n# OF THE JOYS OF VALHALLA\n\n발할라는 오딘의 거대한 전당으로, 그는 거기서 자신이 선택한 영웅들, 곧 전투에서 용맹하게 쓰러진 모든 자들과 더불어 향연을 베푼다. 평온하게 죽음을 맞은 자는 모두 배제되기 때문이다. 그들에게는 멧돼지 슈림니르의 고기가 차려지는데, 모두에게 넉넉하다. 이 멧돼지는 아침마다 요리되지만 밤마다 다시 온전해지기 때문이다. 마실 것으로는 암염소 헤이드룬에게서 나온 벌꿀술이 영웅들에게 넉넉히 공급된다. 영웅들은 향연을 벌이지 않을 때면 싸움으로 즐긴다. 날마다 그들은 뜰이나 들판으로 말을 타고 나가 서로를 갈가리 베어 죽일 때까지 싸운다. 이것이 그들의 소일거리이다. 그러나 식사 때가 오면 그들은 상처에서 회복되어 다시 발할라로 돌아와 향연을 즐긴다.\n\n# THE VALKYRIE\n\n발키리에는 호전적인 처녀들로, 말에 올라 투구와 창으로 무장하고 있다. 오딘은 마침내 닥쳐올 최후의 결전이 벌어지는 날에 거인들과 맞설 수 있도록 발할라에 수많은 영웅을 모으고자 하여, 그들을 모든 전장으로 내려보내 죽임을 당할 자들을 고르게 한다. 발키리에는 그의 사자(使者)이며, 그 이름의 뜻은 \"전사자(戰死者)를 고르는 자들\"이다. 그들이 임무를 띠고 말을 달려 나아갈 때면 그 갑옷이 기이하게 가물거리는 빛을 발하여 북방의 하늘 위로 번쩍이는데, 이것이 사람들이 \"아우로라 보레알리스\" 곧 \"북극광\"이라 부르는 것이다.[37]\n\n# OF THOR AND THE OTHER GODS\n\n천둥신이며 오딘의 맏아들인 토르는 신들과 인간들 가운데 가장 강한 자로서, 매우 귀중한 세 가지 물건을 지니고 있다. 첫째는 망치인데, 서리 거인과 산의 거인들은 그것이 공중에서 자신들을 향해 던져지는 것을 볼 때 호된 대가로 그 위력을 안다. 그것이 그들의 조상과 친족의 두개골을 숱하게 쪼개 놓았기 때문이다. 던져진 뒤에는 저절로 그의 손으로 되돌아온다. 그가 지닌 둘째 진귀한 물건은 힘의 허리띠라 불린다. 그것을 몸에 두르면 그의 신성한 위력이 곱절이 된다. 셋째 역시 매우 귀중한 것으로, 그의 쇠장갑이니, 그는 자신의 망치를 효과적으로 쓰고자 할 때면 언제나 그것을 낀다. 토르의 이름에서 우리의 단어인 목요일이 비롯되었다.\n\n프레이는 신들 가운데 가장 이름난 자 중 하나이다. 그는 비와 햇빛, 그리고 대지의 온갖 결실을 주관한다. 그의 누이 프레이야는 여신들 가운데 가장 자비로운 이이다. 그녀는 음악과 봄과 꽃을 사랑하며, 특히 엘프[요정]들을 무척 좋아한다. 그녀는 사랑의 노래를 매우 즐기니, 모든 연인은 그녀에게 기원하는 것이 좋을 것이다.\n\n브라기는 시(詩)의 신으로, 그의 노래는 전사들의 공적을 기록한다. 그의 아내 이두나는 한 상자 안에 사과를 간직하고 있는데, 신들은 노년이 다가옴을 느낄 때면 그 사과를 맛보기만 해도 다시 젊어진다.\n\n헤임달은 신들의 파수꾼으로, 그러므로 거인들이 비프로스트[무지개] 다리를 억지로 건너오지 못하도록 하늘의 경계에 배치되어 있다. 그는 새보다도 잠을 덜 자며, 낮이나 밤이나 똑같이 자기 주위 백 마일을 본다. 그의 귀가 어찌나 예민한지 어떤 소리도 그를 비껴가지 못하니, 그는 풀이 자라는 소리와 양 등에 양털이 자라는 소리까지 들을 수 있다.\n\n# OF LOKI AND HIS PROGENY\n\n신들을 헐뜯는 자이며 온갖 기만과 해악을 꾸미는 자로 묘사되는 또 하나의 신이 있다. 그의 이름은 로키이다. 그는 잘생기고 균형 잡힌 몸을 지녔으나, 매우 변덕스러운 기질에 더없이 사악한 성품을 지녔다. 그는 거인족이지만 신들의 무리에 억지로 끼어들었으며, 그들을 곤경에 빠뜨렸다가 자신의 교활함과 기지와 솜씨로 그 위험에서 빠져나오게 하는 데서 즐거움을 얻는 듯하다. 로키에게는 세 자식이 있다. 첫째는 늑대 펜리르이고, 둘째는 미드가르드의 뱀이며, 셋째는 헬라[죽음]이다. 신들은 이 괴물들이 자라나고 있으며 언젠가 신들과 인간들에게 많은 해악을 끼치리라는 것을 모르지 않았다. 그리하여 오딘은 그들을 자기에게로 데려오도록 하나를 보내는 것이 현명하다고 여겼다. 그들이 오자 그는 뱀을 땅을 둘러싼 저 깊은 대양 속으로 던져 넣었다. 그러나 그 괴물은 어찌나 어마어마한 크기로 자랐던지 제 꼬리를 입에 문 채로 온 땅을 휘감았다. 헬라는 니플헤임으로 내던지고, 그녀에게 아홉 세계 곧 아홉 영역을 다스릴 권한을 주었으니, 그녀는 그곳에 자기에게 보내진 자들, 곧 병이나 노환으로 죽은 모든 자들을 분배한다. 그녀의 전당은 엘비드네르라 불린다. 굶주림이 그녀의 식탁이요, 기아가 그녀의 칼이요, 지연이 그녀의 종이요, 굼뜸이 그녀의 하녀요, 벼랑이 그녀의 문턱이요, 근심이 그녀의 침상이며, 타오르는 고뇌가 그 방의 휘장을 이룬다. 그녀는 쉽게 알아볼 수 있으니, 그 몸이 반은 살색이고 반은 푸른빛이며, 무섭도록 준엄하고 험상궂은 얼굴을 하고 있기 때문이다.\n\n늑대 펜리르는 신들이 그를 묶는 데 성공하기까지 그들에게 큰 골칫거리를 안겼다. 그는 가장 튼튼한 족쇄도 마치 거미줄로 만든 것인 양 끊어 버렸다. 마침내 신들은 산의 정령들에게 사자를 보냈고, 그들은 신들을 위해 글레이프니르라 불리는 사슬을 만들었다. 그것은 여섯 가지로 빚어졌으니, 곧 고양이의 발소리, 여인들의 수염, 돌의 뿌리, 물고기의 숨결, 곰의 신경[감각], 그리고 새의 침이다. 완성되었을 때 그것은 비단실처럼 매끄럽고 부드러웠다. 그러나 신들이 늑대에게 겉보기에 가녀린 이 끈으로 자신을 묶게 하라고 청하자, 그는 그것이 마법으로 만들어진 것이 아닐까 두려워하며 그들의 의도를 의심했다. 그리하여 그는 신들 가운데 하나가 그 끈이 다시 풀릴 것이라는 담보로 자기(펜리르)의 입 안에 손을 넣는다는 조건에서만 그것으로 묶이는 데 동의했다. 오직 티르[전투의 신]만이 이를 할 만한 용기를 지니고 있었다. 그러나 늑대는 자신이 족쇄를 끊을 수 없고 신들이 자기를 풀어 주지 않으리라는 것을 알자 티르의 손을 물어뜯었으며, 티르는 그 뒤로 줄곧 외팔이로 남았다.\n\n# HOW THOR PAID THE MOUNTAIN GIANT HIS WAGES\n\n옛적 어느 때에, 신들이 자신들의 거처를 짓고 있어 이미 미드가르드와 발할라를 완성했을 무렵, 어떤 장인이 와서 서리 거인과 산의 거인들의 침입으로부터 완벽하게 안전하도록 견고하게 요새화된 거처를 지어 주겠노라 제안했다. 그러나 그는 그 보수로 여신 프레이야와 더불어 해와 달을 요구했다. 신들은 그가 누구의 도움도 없이 혼자서, 그것도 한 겨울 동안에 그 일 전부를 끝내야 한다는 조건으로 그의 요구를 받아들였다. 그러나 여름의 첫날에 무엇 하나라도 끝내지 못한 채 남아 있으면 그는 약속된 보수를 잃어야 한다는 것이었다. 이 조건을 듣고 장인은 자신의 말 스바딜파리를 부릴 수 있게 해 달라고 요구했고, 로키의 조언에 따라 이것이 그에게 허락되었다. 그리하여 그는 겨울 첫날에 일을 시작했고, 밤이면 자신의 말로 건축용 돌을 끌어오게 했다. 그 돌들의 어마어마한 크기에 신들은 깜짝 놀랐고, 그 말이 주인보다 절반은 더 많은 고된 일을 해내는 것을 똑똑히 보았다. 그러나 그들의 거래는 이미 맺어졌고, 엄숙한 맹세로 확정되어 있었으니, 이러한 조치가 없었더라면 거인은 신들 가운데서, 특히 토르가 그때 사악한 악령들을 치러 떠난 원정에서 돌아오면 자신이 안전하리라 여기지 못했을 것이기 때문이다.\n\n겨울이 끝나 갈 무렵 건축은 크게 진척되었고, 성벽은 그곳을 난공불락으로 만들기에 충분할 만큼 높고 육중했다. 요컨대 여름까지 사흘밖에 남지 않았을 때, 끝내야 할 부분은 오로지 성문뿐이었다. 그러자 신들은 자신들의 심판석에 앉아 의논에 들어가, 그들 가운데 누가 프레이야를 내주거나, 거인이 해와 달을 가져가게 허락하여 하늘을 어둠에 잠기게 하자고 조언했는지 서로에게 물었다.\n\n그들은 그토록 많은 악행의 장본인인 로키 말고는 누구도 그런 나쁜 조언을 했을 리 없으며, 만약 그가 장인이 그 일을 완성하여 약속된 보수를 얻는 것을 막을 무슨 방도를 짜내지 못한다면 그는 잔혹한 죽음을 당해야 한다는 데 모두 동의했다. 그들이 로키에게 손을 대려 하자, 그는 겁에 질려 어떤 대가를 치르더라도 그 사내가 보수를 잃게끔 일을 처리하겠노라고 맹세코 약속했다. 바로 그날 밤, 그 사내가 스바딜파리와 함께 건축용 돌을 가지러 갔을 때, 암말 한 마리가 숲에서 갑자기 달려나와 울기 시작했다. 그러자 그 말은 고삐를 끊고 암말을 따라 숲속으로 달려갔고, 이 때문에 사내 또한 자신의 말을 쫓아 달려가야 했으며, 그렇게 이리저리 하는 사이 밤을 통째로 허비하여 동틀 무렵에는 일이 여느 때만큼 진척되지 못했다. 사내는 자신이 일을 완성하지 못하리라는 것을 알자 본래의 거대한 모습으로 돌아왔고, 신들은 이제 그가 실은 자신들 사이로 끼어든 산의 거인이었음을 똑똑히 깨달았다. 더 이상 자신들의 맹세에 매이지 않게 된 그들은 토르를 불렀고, 토르는 즉시 그들을 도우러 달려와 자신의 망치를 치켜들고 그 일꾼에게 품삯을 치렀으니, 해와 달로 치른 것도 아니요, 심지어 그를 요툰헤임으로 돌려보낸 것도 아니라, 첫 일격에 거인의 두개골을 산산조각 내고 그를 곤두박질치게 하여 니플헤임으로 내던졌다.\n\n# THE RECOVERY OF THE HAMMER\n\n옛적 어느 때에 토르의 망치가 거인 트림의 손에 들어가는 일이 있었는데, 그는 그것을 요툰헤임의 바위 아래 여덟 길 깊이로 파묻었다. 토르는 로키를 보내 트림과 교섭하게 했으나, 로키는 프레이야가 그의 신부가 되는 데 동의한다면 그 무기를 돌려주겠다는 거인의 약속을 얻어 내는 것까지만 해낼 수 있었다. 로키는 돌아와 자신의 사명의 결과를 보고했으나, 사랑의 여신은 자신의 매력을 서리 거인의 왕에게 바친다는 생각에 몹시 끔찍해했다. 이 위급한 상황에서 로키는 토르를 설득하여 프레이야의 옷을 입고 자신과 함께 요툰헤임으로 가게 했다. 트림은 베일을 쓴 신부를 정중한 예로 맞이했으나, 그녀가 저녁으로 연어 여덟 마리와 다 자란 소 한 마리에다 그 밖의 진미들까지 먹어 치우고는 그 모든 것을 벌꿀술 세 통으로 들이켜는 것을 보고 크게 놀랐다. 그러나 로키는, 그녀가 자신의 연인이며 요툰헤임의 이름난 통치자를 보고픈 열망이 어찌나 컸던지 여드레 밤이 다하도록 아무것도 입에 대지 못했노라고 그를 안심시켰다. 마침내 트림은 호기심에 신부의 베일 밑을 들여다보았으나, 깜짝 놀라 뒤로 물러나며 어찌하여 프레이야의 눈동자가 불처럼 번득이느냐고 물었다. 로키가 똑같은 핑계를 되풀이하자 거인은 만족했다. 그는 망치를 들여와 처녀의 무릎 위에 놓도록 명했다. 그러자 토르는 변장을 벗어 던지고 자신의 두려운 무기를 움켜쥐어, 트림과 그의 모든 부하를 죽여 버렸다.\n\n프레이 또한 놀라운 무기를 지니고 있었으니, 그것은 주인이 원할 때면 언제나 저절로 들판을 살육으로 뒤덮는 검이었다. 프레이는 이 검을 내주었으나, 토르만큼 운이 좋지 못하여 결코 그것을 되찾지 못했다. 일은 이러했다. 프레이가 언젠가 오딘의 옥좌에 올랐는데, 그곳에서는 온 우주를 굽어볼 수 있어, 둘러보던 중 멀리 거인의 왕국에 있는 아름다운 처녀를 보았고, 그 모습에 그는 별안간 슬픔에 사로잡혀 그 순간부터 잠도 잘 수 없고 마시지도 못하며 말도 할 수 없게 되었다. 마침내 그의 사자 스키르니르가 그에게서 비밀을 알아냈고, 만약 보수로 자신의 검을 준다면 그 처녀를 신부로 데려와 주겠노라고 나섰다. 프레이는 동의하여 그에게 검을 주었고, 스키르니르는 길을 떠나 아흐레 밤 안에 어느 정해진 곳으로 와서 프레이와 혼인하겠다는 처녀의 약속을 얻어 냈다. 스키르니르가 자신의 임무가 성공했음을 보고하자, 프레이는 이렇게 외쳤다.\n\n\"하룻밤도 길거니와, 이틀 밤도 길거늘, 사흘 밤을 내 어찌 견디리? 한 달이 내게는 이 그리움의 절반보다 더 짧게 여겨진 적이 흔하였거늘.\"\n\n이리하여 프레이는 모든 여인 가운데 가장 아름다운 게르다를 아내로 얻었으나, 자신의 검은 잃었다.\n\n\"스키르니르의 여정\"이라는 제목의 이 이야기와, 그 바로 앞에 놓인 \"트림의 노래\"는 롱펠로의 「유럽의 시인과 시」에 시적으로 서술되어 있는 것을 찾아볼 수 있다.\n\n————\n\n# THOR’S VISIT TO JOTUNHEIM, THE GIANT’S COUNTRY\n\n어느 날 신 토르는 자신의 종 티알피와 함께, 그리고 로키를 동반하여, 거인의 나라를 향해 여행을 떠났다. 티알피는 모든 사람 가운데 발이 가장 빠른 자였다. 그는 식량이 담긴 토르의 자루를 짊어졌다. 밤이 되자 그들은 어느 거대한 숲속에 있는 자신들을 발견하고, 하룻밤을 지낼 만한 곳을 사방으로 찾아 헤매다가, 마침내 매우 큰 회당에 이르렀는데, 그 입구는 건물 한쪽 끝의 너비를 통째로 차지할 만큼 넓었다. 이곳에서 그들은 누워 잠을 청했지만, 한밤중이 되자 건물 전체를 뒤흔드는 지진에 놀라고 말았다. 토르는 몸을 일으켜 동료들에게 자신과 함께 안전한 곳을 찾자고 외쳤다. 오른쪽에서 그들은 잇대어 있는 방을 하나 발견했고, 다른 이들은 그 안으로 들어갔으나, 토르는 무슨 일이 일어나든 자신을 지킬 채비를 갖추고 망치를 손에 든 채 문간에 머물렀다. 밤새 무시무시한 신음 소리가 들렸고, 동이 틀 무렵 토르가 밖으로 나가 보니 가까이에 거대한 거인 하나가 누워 있었는데, 그가 잠을 자며 코를 고는 소리가 바로 그들을 그토록 놀라게 했던 것이었다. 한번은 토르도 자신의 망치를 쓰기를 두려워했다고 전해지는데, 거인이 곧 잠에서 깨어나자, 토르는 단지 그의 이름을 묻는 것으로 만족했다.\n\n\"내 이름은 스크리미르요.\" 거인이 말했다. \"하지만 당신의 이름은 물을 필요가 없으니, 당신이 신 토르라는 것을 내가 알고 있기 때문이오. 그런데 내 장갑은 어디로 갔을까?\" 그제야 토르는 자신들이 간밤에 회당으로 여겼던 것이 거인의 장갑이었으며, 두 동료가 피신했던 방은 그 엄지손가락 부분이었음을 깨달았다. 이어서 스크리미르는 함께 여행하자고 제안했고, 토르가 이에 동의하자, 그들은 앉아서 아침을 먹었으며, 식사를 마치자 스크리미르는 모든 식량을 자루 하나에 챙겨 넣고는 그것을 어깨에 걸치고서 앞장서서 성큼성큼 걸었는데, 그 걸음이 어찌나 엄청나던지 그들은 그를 따라가기가 몹시 힘겨웠다. 그렇게 하루 종일 여행을 했고, 해 질 무렵 스크리미르는 그들이 큰 떡갈나무 아래에서 하룻밤을 지낼 곳을 골랐다. 그러고 나서 스크리미르는 자신은 누워서 자겠다고 그들에게 말했다. \"그러나 자루는 그대들이 가지시오.\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"그리고 저녁을 지어 드시오.\"\n\n스크리미르는 곧 잠이 들어 거세게 코를 골기 시작했다. 그런데 토르가 자루를 열려고 해 보니, 거인이 어찌나 단단히 묶어 놓았던지 매듭 하나조차 풀 수 없었다. 마침내 토르는 분노가 치밀어, 두 손으로 망치를 움켜쥐고는 거인의 머리를 사납게 내리쳤다. 스크리미르는 잠에서 깨어나 그저 나뭇잎 하나가 머리에 떨어진 것이 아니냐고, 그리고 저녁을 먹고 잘 채비가 되었느냐고 물을 뿐이었다. 토르는 막 잠자리에 들려던 참이라고 대답하고는, 그렇게 말하면서 다른 나무 아래로 가서 누웠다. 그러나 그날 밤 토르에게는 잠이 오지 않았고, 스크리미르가 다시 어찌나 크게 코를 골던지 숲이 그 소리로 울릴 지경이 되자, 토르는 몸을 일으켜 망치를 움켜쥐고 거인의 머리통을 향해 어찌나 힘껏 던졌던지 거기에 깊은 자국이 패였다. 스크리미르는 잠에서 깨어 외쳤다. \"무슨 일이지? 이 나무에 새들이라도 앉아 있는 건가? 가지에서 이끼가 좀 떨어져 내 머리에 떨어진 것 같군. 토르, 그대는 어떻게 지내고 있소?\" 그러나 토르는 자신은 막 잠에서 깬 참이며, 아직 한밤중에 불과하니 잘 시간이 충분히 남았다고 말하면서 황급히 자리를 떴다. 그렇지만 그는 만일 세 번째로 내리칠 기회가 생긴다면, 그것으로 둘 사이의 모든 일을 결판내리라 마음먹었다. 동이 트기 조금 전, 그는 스크리미르가 다시 깊이 잠들어 있는 것을 알아채고, 또다시 망치를 움켜쥐고는 어찌나 맹렬히 내리쳤던지 망치가 자루까지 거인의 머리통을 파고들었다. 그러나 스크리미르는 몸을 일으켜 앉아 자기 뺨을 쓰다듬으며 말했다. \"도토리 하나가 내 머리에 떨어졌군. 아니! 토르, 그대는 깨어 있소? 이제 우리가 일어나 채비를 차릴 때가 된 것 같구려. 하지만 이제 그대들이 우트가르드라는 도시까지 가는 길은 멀지 않소. 그대들이 서로 내가 작은 몸집의 사내가 아니라고 속닥이는 소리를 들었소만, 우트가르드에 가 보면 거기에서 나보다 훨씬 키 큰 사람들을 많이 보게 될 것이오. 그러니 충고하건대, 거기에 도착하거든 너무 우쭐대지 마시오. 우트가르드로키의 부하들은 그대들처럼 작은 친구들이 허풍 떠는 것을 참지 못할 테니 말이오. 그대들은 동쪽으로 난 길을 택해야 하고, 내 길은 북쪽으로 나 있으니, 우리는 여기서 헤어져야겠소.\"\n\n이 말과 함께 그는 자루를 어깨에 걸치고는 그들에게서 돌아서서 숲속으로 들어갔고, 토르는 그를 붙들거나 더 이상 그의 동행을 청하고 싶은 마음이 없었다.\n\n토르와 그 동료들은 가던 길을 계속 나아갔고, 정오 무렵 평원 한가운데에 서 있는 도시 하나를 발견했다. 그 도시는 어찌나 높던지 꼭대기를 보려면 목을 어깨 위로 한껏 젖혀야만 했다. 도착하자 그들은 도시로 들어섰고, 앞에 큰 궁전이 문을 활짝 연 채 서 있는 것을 보고는 안으로 들어가, 회당의 의자에 앉아 있는 거대한 체구의 사람들 여럿을 발견했다. 더 안으로 들어가자, 그들은 왕 우트가르드로키 앞에 이르렀고, 그에게 정중히 예를 표했다. 왕은 경멸 어린 미소를 지으며 그들을 바라보고 말했다. \"내가 잘못 본 것이 아니라면, 저기 저 풋내기가 분명 신 토르일 테지.\" 그러고는 토르에게 말을 건네며 이렇게 말했다. \"어쩌면 그대는 보기보다는 대단한 자일지도 모르지. 그대와 그대의 동료들이 스스로 능하다고 여기는 재주는 무엇인가? 이곳에서는 어떤 재주든 다른 모든 사람을 능가하지 못하는 자는 머무는 것이 허락되지 않으니 말이다.\"\n\n\"내가 아는 재주는,\" 로키가 말했다, \"그 누구보다도 빨리 먹는 것이오. 그리고 여기서 나와 겨루기를 원하는 자가 누구든, 그자를 상대로 그 솜씨를 입증할 채비가 되어 있소.\"\n\n\"네가 장담한 것을 정말로 해낸다면, 그것은 과연 대단한 재주일 테지.\" 우트가르드로키가 말했다. \"그러니 당장 시험해 보도록 하자.\"\n\n그러고 나서 그는 의자 맨 끝에 앉아 있던, 이름이 로기인 부하 하나를 불러 나오게 하여 로키와 그 솜씨를 겨루게 했다. 고기로 가득 찬 구유가 회당 바닥에 놓이자, 로키는 한쪽 끝에, 로기는 다른 쪽 끝에 자리를 잡고, 각자 구유 한가운데에서 만날 때까지 최대한 빨리 먹기 시작했다. 그러나 알고 보니 로키는 살코기만 먹은 데 반해, 그의 상대는 살코기와 뼈를 모두 먹어 치웠고, 구유까지 먹어 버린 것이었다. 그리하여 모든 좌중은 로키가 패했다고 판정했다.\n\n이어서 우트가르드로키는 토르를 따라온 젊은이는 어떤 재주를 부릴 수 있느냐고 물었다. 티알피는 자신과 맞붙는 그 누구와도 달리기 경주를 하겠다고 대답했다. 왕은 달리기 솜씨는 자랑할 만한 것이라고 말하면서도, 만일 그 젊은이가 시합에서 이기려면 대단한 민첩함을 보여야 할 것이라고 했다. 그러고 나서 그는 일어나 그 자리에 있던 모든 이들과 함께, 달리기에 좋은 땅이 있는 평원으로 가서, 후기라는 젊은이를 불러 티알피와 시합을 벌이라고 명했다. 첫 번째 주로에서 후기는 경쟁자를 어찌나 크게 앞질렀던지, 되돌아와 출발점에서 멀지 않은 곳에서 그를 만났다. 이어서 그들은 두 번째, 세 번째로 달렸으나, 티알피는 더 나은 성과를 거두지 못했다.\n\n이어서 우트가르드로키는 토르에게, 그가 그토록 유명한 그 무용을 어떤 재주로써 입증하고자 하느냐고 물었다. 토르는 누구와든 술 마시기 시합을 해 보겠다고 대답했다. 우트가르드로키는 술 따르는 자에게, 자신의 부하들이 연회의 규칙을 어떤 식으로든 어겼을 때 비워야 하는 큰 뿔잔을 가져오라고 명했다. 술 따르는 자가 그것을 토르에게 내밀자, 우트가르드로키가 말했다. \"술 잘 마시는 자라면 누구든 저 뿔잔을 단숨에 비울 테지만, 대부분의 사람은 두 번에 나누어 비우고, 아무리 술이 약한 자라도 세 번이면 비운다네.\"\n\n토르는 뿔잔을 바라보았는데, 다소 길긴 했으나 별스럽게 큰 크기로 보이지는 않았다. 그러나 그는 몹시 목이 말랐기에, 그것을 입술에 대고 두 번째로 들이켜지 않아도 되도록 숨도 쉬지 않고 최대한 길고 깊게 들이켰다. 하지만 뿔잔을 내려놓고 들여다보니, 술이 줄어든 것을 거의 알아볼 수 없었다.\n\n숨을 고른 뒤 토르는 다시 온 힘을 다해 그것에 달려들었으나, 입에서 뿔잔을 떼어 보니 아까보다 오히려 덜 마신 것 같았다. 다만 이제는 뿔잔을 쏟지 않고 들고 다닐 정도는 되었다.\n\n\"이게 어찌 된 일인가, 토르?\" 우트가르드로키가 말했다. \"그대는 몸을 아껴서는 안 되네. 세 번째 들이켜서 그 뿔잔을 비울 작정이라면 깊이 들이켜야 할 것이야. 그리고 내가 꼭 말해 두어야겠는데, 만일 그대가 다른 재주에서도 이 정도밖에 보여 주지 못한다면, 그대는 여기서는 고향에서만큼 대단한 사내로 불리지는 못할 것이네.\"\n\n분노로 가득 찬 토르는 다시 뿔잔을 입술에 대고 그것을 비우려 최선을 다했으나, 들여다보니 술은 조금 더 줄어들었을 뿐이었다. 그리하여 그는 더 이상 시도하지 않기로 마음먹고 뿔잔을 술 따르는 자에게 돌려주었다.\n\n\"이제 분명히 알겠군.\" 우트가르드로키가 말했다. \"그대는 우리가 생각했던 만큼 그리 대단한 사내가 아니로구나. 그러나 다른 재주를 시험해 보겠는가? 비록 여기서는 어떤 상도 받아 가지 못할 것 같다만.\"\n\n\"그대가 제안할 새로운 시험은 무엇인가?\" 토르가 말했다.\n\n\"여기 우리에게는 아주 사소한 놀이가 하나 있는데,\" 우트가르드로키가 대답했다, \"아이들이나 하는 것이지. 그저 내 고양이를 바닥에서 들어 올리기만 하면 되는 것이라네. 그대가 우리가 여겼던 그런 사내가 결코 아니라는 것을 이미 알아챈 터가 아니었다면, 위대한 토르에게 감히 그런 재주를 입에 올리지도 못했을 것이야.\"\n\n그가 말을 마치자, 커다란 잿빛 고양이 한 마리가 회당 바닥으로 뛰어들었다. 토르는 고양이의 배 밑으로 손을 넣어 그것을 바닥에서 들어 올리려 안간힘을 썼으나, 고양이는 등을 구부린 채, 토르의 온갖 애에도 불구하고 발 하나만 들렸을 뿐이었고, 이를 본 토르는 더 이상 시도하지 않았다.\n\n\"이 시험은,\" 우트가르드로키가 말했다, \"내가 짐작했던 그대로 되었군. 고양이는 크지만, 토르는 우리 사람들에 비하면 작은 자로다.\"\n\n\"그대들이 나를 작다고 부르지만,\" 토르가 대답했다, \"이제 내가 분이 났으니, 그대들 가운데 누가 여기로 나와 나와 씨름을 하겠는지 어디 한번 봅시다.\"\n\n\"여기에는,\" 우트가르드로키가 의자에 앉아 있는 사람들을 바라보며 말했다, \"그대와 씨름하는 것을 제 격에 못 미친다고 여기지 않을 자가 하나도 없어 보이는군. 그렇지만 누군가 저 늙은 노파, 내 유모 엘리를 이리로 불러오게 하여, 토르가 원한다면 그녀와 씨름을 하게 하라. 그녀는 이 토르 못지않게 힘센 사내들을 숱하게 땅바닥에 메다꽂은 적이 있느니라.\"\n\n그러자 이가 빠진 늙은 여인 하나가 회당으로 들어왔고, 우트가르드로키는 그녀에게 토르를 붙잡으라고 일렀다. 이야기는 짧게 끝난다. 토르가 그 노파를 더욱 단단히 붙잡을수록, 그녀는 더욱 굳건히 버티고 섰다. 마침내 몹시 격렬한 다툼 끝에 토르는 발이 휘청거리기 시작했고, 결국 한쪽 무릎이 꺾여 주저앉고 말았다. 그러자 우트가르드로키는 그들에게 그만두라고 이르면서, 이제 토르는 회당의 다른 누구에게도 씨름을 청할 필요가 없게 되었으며, 또한 시간도 늦어 가고 있다고 덧붙였다. 그리하여 그는 토르와 그 동료들을 자리로 안내했고, 그들은 그곳에서 흥겹게 하룻밤을 보냈다.\n\n이튿날 아침, 동이 틀 무렵, 토르와 그 동료들은 옷을 차려입고 떠날 채비를 했다. 우트가르드로키는 그들을 위해 상을 차리라고 명했는데, 거기에는 음식이며 술이 부족함이 없었다. 식사를 마친 뒤 우트가르드로키는 그들을 도시의 성문까지 데려다주었고, 헤어지면서 토르에게 이번 여행이 어떻게 되었다고 생각하는지, 그리고 자신보다 더 힘센 사람들을 만나기는 했는지를 물었다. 토르는 자신이 스스로에게 큰 수치를 안겼다는 것을 부인할 수 없다고 그에게 말했다. \"그리고 무엇보다 나를 가장 괴롭게 하는 것은,\" 그가 덧붙였다, \"그대들이 나를 보잘것없는 자라고 부를 것이라는 점이오.\"\n\n\"아니,\" 우트가르드로키가 말했다, \"이제 그대가 도시를 벗어났으니 그대에게 진실을 말해 주는 것이 마땅하겠소. 그대는, 내가 살아 있고 내 뜻대로 하는 한, 이 도시에 두 번 다시 발을 들이지 못할 것이오. 그리고 진실로 말하건대, 만일 그대에게 그토록 큰 힘이 있고, 그대가 나를 그토록 큰 화로 몰아갈 뻔하리라는 것을 미리 알았더라면, 이번에 그대가 들어오는 것을 결코 허락하지 않았을 것이오. 그러니 알아 두시오, 나는 줄곧 환영으로 그대를 속여 왔소. 먼저 숲에서, 나는 자루를 쇠줄로 묶어 그대가 풀 수 없게 했소. 그 뒤 그대는 망치로 나를 세 번 내리쳤소. 그 첫 번째 일격은, 가장 약한 것이었으되, 만일 내게 떨어졌더라면 내 목숨을 끊어 놓았을 것이오. 하지만 나는 옆으로 비켜섰고, 그대의 일격은 산에 떨어졌으니, 그대는 거기서 세 개의 협곡을 보게 될 것이며, 그중 하나는 유난히 깊을 것이오. 그것들이 바로 그대의 망치가 낸 자국이라오. 나는 그대가 내 부하들과 벌인 시합들에서도 비슷한 환영을 써먹었소. 첫째로, 로키는 굶주림 그 자체처럼 자기 앞에 놓인 모든 것을 먹어 치웠으나, 실은 그 로기는 다름 아닌 불이었기에, 고기뿐만 아니라 그것을 담은 구유까지 먹어 치운 것이오. 티알피가 달리기로 겨룬 후기는 생각이었으니, 티알피로서는 그것과 보조를 맞추기란 불가능했소. 그대가 그대 차례에 뿔잔을 비우려 했을 때, 그대는, 진실로, 어찌나 놀라운 일을 해냈던지, 내가 직접 보지 않았더라면 결코 믿지 못했을 것이오. 그 뿔잔의 한쪽 끝은 바다에 닿아 있었는데, 그대는 그것을 알지 못했소. 그러나 그대가 해변에 가 보면 그대의 들이킴으로 바다가 얼마나 줄어들었는지 알게 될 것이오. 그대는 고양이를 들어 올림으로써 그 못지않게 놀라운 재주를 부렸소. 그리고 솔직히 말하면, 우리는 고양이의 발 하나가 바닥에서 떨어지는 것을 보았을 때 모두가 겁에 질리고 말았소. 그대가 고양이로 여겼던 것은 실은 대지를 둘러싸고 있는 미드가르드 뱀이었고, 그것은 그대로 인해 어찌나 늘어났던지 머리와 꼬리 사이에 대지를 가까스로 두를 만큼밖에 길이가 남지 않았기 때문이오. 그대가 엘리와 씨름한 것 또한 더없이 놀라운 재주였소. 노년 — 실은 엘리가 바로 그것이었소 — 이 머지않아 쓰러뜨리지 않을 사람은 지금까지 없었고, 앞으로도 결코 없을 것이기 때문이오. 그러나 이제 우리가 헤어지려 하니, 그대에게 한마디 일러두겠소. 그대가 두 번 다시 내게 가까이 오지 않는 편이 우리 둘 모두에게 더 나을 것이오. 만일 그대가 그리한다면, 나는 또다시 다른 환영으로 나를 지킬 터이니, 그대는 그저 헛수고만 하고 나와의 시합에서 아무런 명성도 얻지 못할 것이오.\"\n\n이 말을 듣자 토르는 노여움에 차서 망치를 움켜쥐고는 그를 향해 던지려 했으나, 우트가르드로키는 이미 사라지고 없었고, 토르가 그 도시를 파괴하려고 되돌아가려 하자, 그의 주위에는 푸르른 평원 말고는 아무것도 보이지 않았다.\n\n————\n\n# CHAPTER XL\n\n선량한 발두르는 자신의 목숨이 위태롭다는 것을 암시하는 무서운 꿈에 시달린 끝에, 이를 모여 있던 신들에게 이야기했다. 신들은 그를 위협하는 위험을 물리치기 위해 만물에게 주문을 걸기로 결의했다. 그러자 오딘의 아내 프리가는 불과 물에게서, 쇠와 그 밖의 모든 금속에게서, 돌과 나무와 질병과 짐승과 새와 독과 기어 다니는 것들에게서, 어느 것도 발두르를 해치지 않겠노라는 맹세를 받아 냈다. 오딘은 이 모든 것으로도 만족하지 못하고 아들의 운명을 염려한 나머지, 펜리스와 헬라와 미드가르드 뱀의 어미인 여거인이자 예언자인 앙게르보데에게 묻기로 결심했다. 그녀는 이미 죽은 몸이었으므로, 오딘은 헬라의 영토로 그녀를 찾아갈 수밖에 없었다. 이 오딘의 하강은 그레이의 빼어난 송시(頌詩)의 주제가 되었는데, 그 시는 이렇게 시작한다.\n\n\"사람들의 왕이 서둘러 일어나 석탄처럼 검은 준마에 곧장 안장을 얹었네.\"\n\n그러나 다른 신들은 프리가가 한 일로 충분하다고 여겨, 발두르를 과녁 삼아 즐겼다. 어떤 이들은 그에게 화살을 던지고, 어떤 이들은 돌을 던지고, 또 어떤 이들은 칼과 전투용 도끼로 그를 내려쳤다. 무슨 짓을 하더라도 그 누구도 그를 해칠 수 없었기 때문이다. 그리하여 이는 그들이 즐겨 하는 소일거리가 되었고, 발두르에게 표하는 영예로 간주되었다. 그러나 로키가 그 광경을 보고서는 발두르가 다치지 않는 것에 몹시 분개했다. 그래서 그는 여인의 모습으로 변신하여, 프리가의 거처인 펜살리르로 갔다. 그 여신은 그 가짜 여인을 보고서, 신들이 모임에서 무엇을 하고 있는지 아느냐고 물었다. 여인은 그들이 발두르에게 화살과 돌을 던지지만 그를 해치지 못하고 있다고 대답했다. \"그렇지.\" 프리가가 말했다. \"돌이든 막대기든 그 무엇도 발두르를 해칠 수 없네. 내가 그 모두에게서 맹세를 받아 냈기 때문이지.\" \"무엇이라고요?\" 여인이 외쳤다. \"만물이 발두르를 살려 두겠노라 맹세했다는 말씀이십니까?\" \"만물이 그러했네.\" 프리가가 대답했다. \"다만 발할라의 동쪽에서 자라는, 겨우살이라 불리는 작은 관목 하나만은 예외였네. 너무 어리고 연약하여 맹세를 요구할 것까지는 없다고 여겼기 때문이지.\"\n\n로키는 이 말을 듣자마자 자리를 떠나, 본래의 모습으로 돌아와 겨우살이를 잘라 들고는 신들이 모여 있는 곳으로 향했다. 거기서 그는 호두르가 눈이 멀어 놀이에 끼지 못한 채 한쪽에 떨어져 서 있는 것을 발견했다. 그에게 다가가 이렇게 말했다. \"그대는 어찌하여 발두르에게 무엇이든 던지지 않는가?\"\n\n\"나는 눈이 멀어,\" 호두르가 대답했다. \"발두르가 어디 있는지 보이지 않고, 게다가 던질 것도 없기 때문이오.\"\n\n\"그러면 이리 오게.\" 로키가 말했다. \"다른 이들처럼 이 가지를 발두르에게 던져 그에게 영예를 표하게. 내가 그대의 팔을 그가 서 있는 곳으로 인도해 주겠네.\"\n\n그리하여 호두르는 겨우살이를 받아 들고, 로키의 인도를 받아 발두르를 향해 그것을 던졌다. 발두르는 온몸이 꿰뚫려 숨이 끊어진 채 쓰러졌다. 신들 사이에서든 사람들 사이에서든 이보다 더 흉악한 짓은 일찍이 목격된 적이 없었음이 분명하다. 발두르가 쓰러지자 신들은 공포에 사로잡혀 말을 잃었고, 그런 다음 서로를 바라보며 모두 한마음으로 그 짓을 저지른 자에게 손을 대려 했으나, 자신들이 모여 있는 신성한 장소를 존중하여 복수를 미룰 수밖에 없었다. 그들은 큰 소리로 통곡하며 슬픔을 토해 냈다. 신들이 정신을 차리자 프리가는 그들 가운데 누가 자신의 온갖 사랑과 호의를 얻고 싶은지 물었다. \"헬에게 가서 헬라가 발두르를 아스가르드로 돌려보내 준다면 그 대가로 몸값을 바치겠노라 제안하는 자가,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그 사랑과 호의를 얻으리라.\" 이에 오딘의 아들이며 〈날쌘 자〉라는 별명을 지닌 헤르모드가 그 여정을 맡겠노라 나섰다. 그러자 다리가 여덟이고 바람보다도 빨리 달리는 오딘의 말 슬레이프니르가 끌려 나왔고, 헤르모드는 거기에 올라타 자신의 사명을 향해 질주해 떠났다. 그는 아흐레 낮과 그만큼의 밤 동안, 아무것도 분간할 수 없을 만큼 어두운 깊은 협곡들을 지나 말을 달려, 마침내 기욜 강에 다다랐다. 그는 반짝이는 황금으로 덮인 다리 위로 강을 건넜다. 그 다리를 지키던 처녀가 그에게 이름과 혈통을 물으며, 전날에는 죽은 자들의 다섯 무리가 그 다리를 건넜으나 그가 홀로 건널 때만큼 다리가 흔들리지는 않았다고 말했다. \"그런데,\" 그녀가 덧붙였다. \"그대에게는 죽음의 빛이 보이지 않는군요. 그런데 어찌하여 그대는 헬로 가는 길에 이리 말을 달리고 있나요?\"\n\n\"나는 발두르를 찾으러 헬로 가오.\" 헤르모드가 대답했다. \"혹시 그가 이 길을 지나가는 것을 보았소?\"\n\n그녀가 대답했다. \"발두르는 기욜의 다리를 건너갔습니다. 그가 죽음의 거처로 간 길이 저쪽에 있습니다.\"\n\n헤르모드는 헬의 빗장 걸린 문에 이를 때까지 여정을 계속했다. 여기서 그는 말에서 내려 안장을 더 단단히 조이고는 다시 올라타 말에 두 박차를 가했다. 말은 엄청난 도약으로 문에 닿지도 않은 채 그것을 넘었다. 그런 다음 헤르모드는 궁전까지 말을 달려가, 그곳 홀에서 가장 영예로운 자리를 차지하고 있는 형제 발두르를 발견했고, 그와 함께 하룻밤을 보냈다. 이튿날 아침 그는 헬라에게 발두르가 자신과 함께 집으로 돌아가게 해 달라고 간청하며, 신들 사이에서는 통곡 외에는 들리는 것이 없다고 단언했다. 헬라는 발두르가 사람들이 말하는 것처럼 정말로 사랑받는지 이제 시험해 보겠노라 대답했다. \"따라서,\" 그녀가 덧붙였다. \"세상 만물이, 산 것이든 죽은 것이든 그를 위해 운다면 그는 다시 살아 돌아갈 것이다. 그러나 어느 하나라도 그를 거슬러 말하거나 울기를 거부한다면, 그는 헬에 머무르게 될 것이다.\"\n\n그리하여 헤르모드는 아스가르드로 돌아가 자신이 듣고 본 모든 것을 보고했다.\n\n이에 신들은 발두르가 헬에서 풀려날 수 있도록 만물에게 울어 달라고 청하기 위해 온 세상으로 사자들을 파견했다. 만물은 이 청에 기꺼이 응했다. 사람들도, 다른 모든 살아 있는 것들도, 또한 흙과 돌과 나무와 금속도 모두 그러했는데, 마치 우리가 이런 것들을 차가운 곳에서 뜨거운 곳으로 옮길 때 그것들이 우는 것을 보아 온 그대로였다. 사자들이 돌아오던 길에, 그들은 타우크트라는 이름의 늙은 노파가 동굴 안에 앉아 있는 것을 발견하고는 발두르가 헬에서 나올 수 있도록 울어 달라고 청했다. 그러나 그녀는 이렇게 대답했다.\n\n\"타우크트는 마른 눈물로 발두르의 화장(火葬) 불을 위해 곡하리라. 헬라가 제 것을 차지하게 하라.\"\n\n이 노파는 다름 아닌 로키 자신일 것이라는 의심이 강하게 일었으니, 그는 신들과 사람들 사이에서 악을 행하기를 결코 멈추지 않았던 것이다. 그리하여 발두르는 아스가르드로 돌아오지 못하게 되었다.[38]\n\n# THE FUNERAL OF BALDUR\n\n신들은 죽은 시신을 들어 올려 바닷가로 옮겼는데, 그곳에는 세상에서 가장 크다고 여겨지던 발두르의 배 〈흐링감〉이 있었다. 발두르의 시신은 배에 실린 장작더미 위에 놓였고, 그의 아내 난나는 그 광경에 슬픔이 사무친 나머지 심장이 터져 버려, 그녀의 시신도 남편의 것과 같은 장작더미 위에서 불태워졌다. 발두르의 장례식에는 온갖 종류의 사람들이 어마어마하게 모여들었다. 먼저 오딘이 프리가와 발키리에와 그의 까마귀들을 거느리고 왔다. 그다음에 프레이가 멧돼지 굴린부르스티가 끄는 수레를 타고 왔고, 헤임달은 자신의 말 굴토프를 타고 왔으며, 프레이야는 고양이들이 끄는 자신의 전차를 몰고 왔다. 서리 거인들과 산의 거인들도 많이 참석했다. 발두르의 말은 온전히 마구를 갖춘 채 장작더미로 끌려와 주인과 같은 불길 속에서 불살라졌다.\n\n그러나 로키는 마땅한 벌을 면치 못했다. 그는 신들이 얼마나 노했는지를 보고서 산으로 달아나, 그곳에 문이 넷 달린 오두막을 지어 다가오는 온갖 위험을 볼 수 있게 했다. 그는 물고기를 잡기 위한 그물을 고안했는데, 그 시절 이후로 어부들이 써 온 것과 같은 것이었다. 그러나 오딘은 그의 은신처를 알아냈고, 신들은 그를 붙잡으려 모여들었다. 그는 이를 보고 자신을 연어로 변신시켜 시냇물의 돌 사이에 숨었다. 그러나 신들은 그의 그물을 가져다 시냇물을 훑었고, 로키는 붙잡히게 될 것임을 깨닫고는 그물을 뛰어넘으려 했다. 그러나 토르가 그의 꼬리를 붙잡아 눌렀고, 그리하여 연어들은 그 이후로 그 부위가 유난히 가늘고 곱게 되었다. 신들은 그를 사슬로 묶고 그의 머리 위에 뱀을 매달아 두었는데, 그 독이 그의 얼굴 위로 한 방울씩 떨어졌다. 그의 아내 시구나가 그의 곁에 앉아 떨어지는 독방울을 잔에 받았다. 그러나 그녀가 그것을 비우려 가져갈 때면 독이 로키 위로 떨어졌고, 그러면 그는 공포에 질려 울부짖으며 온몸을 어찌나 격렬하게 비트는지 온 대지가 흔들렸으니, 이것이 사람들이 지진이라 부르는 것을 낳는다.\n\n# THE ELVES\n\n에다는 신들보다는 못하지만 그래도 큰 권능을 지닌 또 다른 부류의 존재들을 언급한다. 이들은 엘프라 불렸다. 흰 정령들, 곧 빛의 엘프들은 더없이 아름답고 태양보다도 눈부셨으며, 섬세하고 투명한 직물로 된 옷을 입었다. 그들은 빛을 사랑했고 인간에게 호의를 품었으며, 대개 아름답고 사랑스러운 아이들의 모습으로 나타났다. 그들의 나라는 알프헤임이라 불렸으며, 태양의 신 프레이르의 영토였다. 그들은 늘 그 빛 속에서 즐거이 노닐었다.\n\n검은 엘프, 곧 밤의 엘프들은 다른 종류의 피조물이었다. 추하고 코가 긴 난쟁이들로, 지저분한 갈색을 띠었으며, 밤에만 나타났다. 그들은 태양을 가장 무서운 적으로 여겨 피했는데, 태양의 빛이 그들 가운데 누구에게든 닿으면 그 즉시 돌로 변해 버렸기 때문이다. 그들의 언어는 고독의 메아리였으며, 그들의 거처는 땅 밑의 동굴과 틈새였다. 그들은 이미르의 시신이 썩은 살에서 생겨난 구더기로 존재하게 되었다가, 후에 신들로부터 인간의 형상과 위대한 지력을 부여받았다고 여겨졌다. 그들은 특히 자연의 신비로운 힘에 대한 지식과, 자신들이 새겨 풀이하던 룬 문자로 이름이 높았다. 그들은 창조된 모든 존재 가운데 가장 솜씨 좋은 장인들이었으며, 금속과 나무를 다루어 일했다. 그들의 가장 이름난 작품들 가운데에는 토르의 망치와, 그들이 프레이르에게 선사한 배 〈스키드블라드니르〉가 있었다. 그 배는 모든 신을 그들의 전쟁 도구와 살림 도구와 함께 다 실을 수 있을 만큼 컸으나, 어찌나 솜씨 좋게 만들어졌던지 접어 두면 옆 주머니에 넣을 수 있을 정도였다.\n\n# RAGNAROK, THE TWILIGHT OF THE GODS\n\n북방 민족들은 보이는 모든 피조물, 곧 발할라와 니플헤임의 신들, 요툰헤임과 알프헤임과 미드가르드의 거주자들이 그들의 거처와 더불어 멸망할 때가 오리라고 굳게 믿었다. 그러나 그 무서운 멸망의 날에 전조가 없지는 않을 것이다. 먼저 세 겹의 겨울이 닥쳐올 것이니, 그동안 눈이 하늘의 네 귀퉁이에서 쏟아지고, 서리가 몹시 매서우며, 바람이 살을 에고, 날씨가 사납고, 태양이 아무런 기쁨도 주지 못할 것이다. 단 한 차례의 여름으로도 누그러지는 일 없이 그런 겨울이 세 번 지나갈 것이다. 그런 다음 그와 비슷한 또 다른 세 번의 겨울이 뒤따를 것이니, 그동안 전쟁과 불화가 온 우주에 퍼질 것이다. 대지 자체가 두려움에 떨기 시작하고, 바다가 그 그릇을 벗어나며, 하늘이 갈라지고, 사람들이 수없이 죽어 나가고, 하늘의 독수리들이 아직도 꿈틀거리는 시신들을 포식할 것이다. 이제 늑대 펜리스가 자신을 묶은 사슬을 끊고, 미드가르드 뱀이 바다 밑 잠자리에서 솟아오르며, 묶여 있던 데서 풀려난 로키가 신들의 적에 가담할 것이다. 만물이 황폐해지는 가운데 무스펠헤임의 아들들이 그들의 지도자 수르투르의 인솔 아래 돌진해 나올 것이니, 그의 앞뒤로는 불길과 타오르는 불이 따른다. 그들은 무지개다리 비프로스트 위로 말을 달리는데, 다리는 말발굽 아래 무너진다. 그러나 그들은 다리가 무너지는 것에 아랑곳하지 않고 비그리드라 불리는 전장으로 향한다. 그곳으로 늑대 펜리스와 미드가르드 뱀, 헬라의 모든 추종자를 거느린 로키, 그리고 서리 거인들도 모여든다.\n\n이제 헤임달이 일어서서 신들과 영웅들을 싸움에 소집하기 위해 걀라르 뿔나팔을 분다. 신들은 오딘을 앞세워 나아가고, 오딘은 늑대 펜리스와 맞붙으나 그 괴물의 희생물이 되고 만다. 그러나 그 괴물은 오딘의 아들 비다르에게 죽임을 당한다. 토르는 미드가르드 뱀을 죽여 크나큰 명성을 얻으나, 죽어 가는 괴물이 자신에게 뿜어낸 독에 질식하여 뒷걸음치다 쓰러져 죽는다. 로키와 헤임달은 마주쳐 싸우다 둘 다 죽임을 당한다. 신들과 그 적들이 전투에서 쓰러진 뒤, 프레이르를 죽인 수르투르가 온 세상에 불과 불길을 쏘아 댔고, 온 우주가 불타 버린다. 태양이 흐려지고, 대지가 대양 속으로 가라앉으며, 별들이 하늘에서 떨어지고, 시간은 더 이상 존재하지 않는다.\n\n이후에 알파두르[전능자]가 새 하늘과 새 땅을 바다에서 솟아오르게 할 것이다. 풍성한 양식으로 가득한 새 땅은 수고나 보살핌 없이도 저절로 그 열매를 맺을 것이다. 사악함과 비참함은 더 이상 알려지지 않을 것이며, 신들과 사람들이 함께 행복하게 살아갈 것이다.\n\n# RUNIC LETTERS\n\n덴마크나 노르웨이나 스웨덴에서는 멀리 가지 않아도 룬이라 불리는 문자가 새겨진 갖가지 형태의 큰 돌들을 만나게 되는데, 이 문자는 첫눈에 우리가 아는 모든 것과는 사뭇 달라 보인다. 그 글자들은 거의 예외 없이 곧은 선들로 이루어져 있어, 작은 막대기를 하나씩 혹은 여럿 모아 놓은 모양을 하고 있다. 그런 막대기들은 일찍이 북방 민족들이 미래의 일들을 알아내기 위한 용도로 썼던 것이다. 막대기들을 흔든 다음, 그것들이 이루는 형상에서 일종의 점복(占卜)을 끌어냈다.\n\n룬 문자에는 여러 종류가 있었다. 그것들은 주로 마법의 목적에 쓰였다. 해로운 룬, 곧 그들이 〈쓰라린〉 룬이라 부르던 것은 적들에게 갖가지 재앙을 가져오는 데 쓰였고, 이로운 룬은 불행을 물리쳤다. 어떤 것은 의약에 쓰였고, 또 어떤 것은 사랑을 얻는 데 쓰이는 등이었다. 후대에는 비문에 자주 쓰였는데, 그런 비문이 천 점 넘게 발견되었다. 그 언어는 노르드어라 불리는 고트어의 한 방언으로, 아이슬란드에서는 아직도 쓰인다. 따라서 그 비문들은 확실하게 읽어 낼 수 있으나, 지금까지 역사에 조금이라도 빛을 던져 주는 것은 거의 발견되지 않았다. 대부분은 묘비에 새겨진 비명(碑銘)이다.\n\n그레이의 송시 〈오딘의 하강〉에는 룬 문자를 주술에 사용하는 것에 관한 인유가 담겨 있다.\n\n\"북녘 하늘을 마주하고서 그는 룬의 운(韻)을 세 번 그렸네. 무서운 어조로 세 번 읊었네, 죽은 자를 깨우는 전율의 시구를. 마침내 텅 빈 땅속에서 음울한 소리가 천천히 새어 나왔네.\"\n\n# THE SKALDS\n\n스칼드는 그 나라의 음유시인이자 시인으로, 문명의 초기 단계에 있는 모든 공동체에서 매우 중요한 부류의 사람들이었다. 그들은 존재하는 온갖 역사적 전승의 보관자였으며, 자신들의 솜씨가 허락하는 한의 시와 음악을 곁들여 살아 있거나 죽은 영웅들의 위업을 읊음으로써, 전사들의 거친 향연에 지적인 즐거움을 어느 정도 섞어 넣는 것이 그들의 소임이었다. 스칼드의 작품은 사가라 불렸으며, 그 가운데 다수가 우리에게 전해져 내려와 귀중한 역사 자료와, 그것이 다루는 시대의 사회상에 대한 충실한 그림을 담고 있다.\n\n에다와 사가는 아이슬란드에서 우리에게 전해져 왔다. 칼라일의 강연 〈영웅과 영웅 숭배〉에서 발췌한 다음 글은, 우리가 읽어 온 기이한 이야기들이 비롯된 지역에 대한 생생한 서술을 들려준다. 독자는 잠시 그곳을 고전 신화의 모태인 그리스와 대조해 보기 바란다.\n\n\"저 기이한 섬 아이슬란드는 지질학자들의 말에 따르면 바다 밑바닥에서 불길이 치솟아 솟아오른 것으로, 메마름과 용암의 거친 땅이며, 해마다 여러 달 동안 검은 폭풍에 삼켜져 있으나 여름철이면 거칠고 어른거리는 아름다움을 띠고, 그 위로 눈 덮인 요쿨[산]과 포효하는 게이세르[끓는 샘]와 유황 못과 끔찍한 화산의 틈을 거느린 채 북녘 대양에 준엄하고 험상궂게 솟아 있으니, 마치 서리와 불의 황량하고 혼돈한 전장 같다. 무엇보다 우리가 문학이나 글로 된 기록을 가장 기대하기 어려웠던 그곳에서, 이런 것들의 기록이 적혀 내려왔던 것이다. 이 거친 땅의 해안가에는 풀이 자라는 띠 같은 지대가 있어, 거기서 가축이 살아갈 수 있고, 사람들은 그 가축과 바다가 내어 주는 것으로 살아갈 수 있다. 그리고 이들은 시인다운 사람들이었던 듯하다. 마음속에 깊은 사색을 품고 그 사색을 음악처럼 토해 낸 사람들이었던 것이다. 아이슬란드가 바다에서 솟아오르지 않았더라면, 노르드인들에게 발견되지 않았더라면, 잃어버렸을 것이 참으로 많았으리라!\"\n\n# TEUTONIC MYTHOLOGY\n\n독일 본토의 신화에서는 오딘의 이름이 보탄으로 나타난다. 프레이야와 프리가는 하나의 동일한 신성으로 간주되며, 신들은 대체로 스칸디나비아 신화의 신들보다 덜 호전적인 성격으로 그려진다. 그러나 전체로 보면 게르만 신화는 북방 민족들의 신화와 거의 동일한 노선을 따른다. 가장 두드러진 차이는 기후 조건의 차이로 인해 전설들이 변형된 데에서 비롯된다. 게르만족의 보다 진보된 사회 상태 또한 그들의 신화에 드러난다.\n\n# THE NIBELUNGEN LIED\n\n게르만 종족의 가장 오래된 신화 가운데 하나는 위대한 국민 서사시 〈니벨룽겐의 노래〉에서 발견되는데, 이는 보탄과 프리가와 토르와 로키, 그리고 그 밖의 신들과 여신들이 게르만의 숲에서 숭배되던 선사시대까지 거슬러 올라간다. 이 서사시는 두 부분으로 나뉘는데, 그 첫째 부분은 네덜란드 왕들 가운데 가장 어린 지크프리트가 부르군트의 왕 군터의 누이 크림힐트에게 청혼하러 보름스로 간 이야기를 들려준다. 군터의 곁에 머무는 동안 지크프리트는 부르군트 왕이 이슬란트의 여왕 브룬힐트를 아내로 얻도록 도왔다. 브룬힐트는 창던지기와 거대한 돌 던지기, 그리고 멀리뛰기에서 자신을 이기는 자만이 자신의 남편이 되리라고 공공연히 선언해 둔 터였다. 투명 망토를 지닌 지크프리트는 이 세 가지 겨루기에서 군터를 도왔고, 브룬힐트는 군터의 아내가 되었다. 이러한 봉사의 대가로 군터는 자신의 누이 크림힐트를 지크프리트와 혼인시켰다.\n\n얼마간의 세월이 흐른 뒤, 지크프리트와 크림힐트가 군터를 방문했을 때, 두 여인은 각자의 남편이 지닌 우열을 놓고 다툼에 빠졌다. 크림힐트는 지크프리트를 추켜세우려고, 군터가 자신의 승리들과 아내를 얻은 것은 바로 그 지크프리트 덕분이라고 떠벌렸다. 브룬힐트는 크게 노하여 군터의 봉신 하간을 시켜 지크프리트를 살해하게 했다. 서사시에서 하간은 다음과 같이 묘사된다.\n\n\"그 가공할 손님은 잘 자라고 단단히 다져진 몸이었네. 다리는 길고 힘줄이 불거졌으며, 가슴은 깊고 넓었네. 한때 검었던 그의 머리칼은 근래 회색이 섞였네. 그 얼굴은 더없이 무시무시했고, 걸음걸이는 위엄이 있었네.\" — 〈니벨룽겐의 노래〉, 1789연.\n\n게르만 로맨스의 이 아킬레우스는, 불운한 네덜란드 왕이 사냥 도중 시냇물을 마시려 몸을 숙이고 있을 때 그의 양 어깨 사이를 찔렀다.\n\n서사시의 둘째 부분은, 십삼 년 뒤에 크림힐트가 훈족의 왕 에첼과 혼인한 이야기를 전한다. 얼마 후 그녀는 부르군트의 왕을 하간과 그 밖의 많은 이와 더불어 자기 남편의 궁정으로 초대했다. 연회장에서 무서운 다툼이 일어났고, 그것은 군터와 하간을 제외한 모든 부르군트인의 살육으로 끝났다. 이 두 사람은 사로잡혀 크림힐트에게 넘겨졌고, 그녀는 자신의 손으로 두 사람의 목을 잘랐다. 이 피비린내 나는 복수의 행위로 인해 크림힐트 자신도 힐데브란트에게 죽임을 당했는데, 그는 마법사이자 투사로서 게르만 신화에서 그리스 신화의 네스토르에 어느 정도 상응하는 자리를 차지한다.\n\n# THE NIBELUNGEN HOARD\n\n이것은 지크프리트가 니벨룽족에게서 얻은 황금과 보석의 신화적인 무더기였다. 니벨룽족은 그가 정복하여 그 나라를 자신의 왕국인 네덜란드에 조공을 바치게 만든 북방의 백성이었다. 혼인할 때 지크프리트는 그 보물을 신부의 몫으로 크림힐트에게 주었다. 지크프리트가 살해된 뒤 하간이 그것을 차지하여 훗날 되찾을 작정으로 로흐함의 라인 강 밑에 몰래 묻었다. 하간이 크림힐트에게 죽임을 당했을 때 그 보물은 영영 사라졌다. 그 경이로움은 시에서 이렇게 그려진다.\n\n\"거대한 수레 열두 대가 꼬박 나흘 낮과 밤 동안 산에서 짠 바닷가 만(灣)까지 실어 날라야 할 만큼이었네. 수레마다 날마다 세 번씩 오가는데도 그러하였네. 그것은 오로지 보석과 황금으로만 이루어져 있었네. 온 세상을 그것으로 사들이고 그 값을 셈하여 치른다 해도, 이전에 있던 데서 단 한 마르크도 줄지 않으리라, 내 짐작하건대.\" — 〈니벨룽겐의 노래〉, 19장.\n\n니벨룽겐의 보물을 차지하는 자는 누구든 니벨룽족이라 불렸다. 그리하여 한때 노르웨이의 어떤 백성이 그렇게 불렸다. 지크프리트가 그 보물을 지녔을 때 그는 \"니벨룽족의 왕\"이라는 칭호를 얻었다.\n\n# WAGNER’S NIBELUNGEN RING\n\n리하르트 바그너의 악극 〈니벨룽의 반지〉는 옛 게르만 서사시와 어느 정도 닮은 데가 있기는 하지만, 전적으로 독립된 작품으로, 갖가지 옛 노래와 사가에서 끌어와 극작가가 하나의 위대하고 조화로운 이야기로 엮어 낸 것이다. 주된 출전은 〈볼숭 사가〉였으며, 비교적 작은 부분들은 〈옛 에다〉와 〈젊은 에다〉에서, 또 다른 부분들은 〈니벨룽겐의 노래〉와 〈에클렌리트〉, 그리고 그 밖의 게르만 민담에서 취해졌다.\n\n이 극에는 처음에 오직 네 가지 뚜렷한 종족만이 있으니, 곧 신들과 거인들과 난쟁이들과 님프들이다. 후에 특별한 창조를 통해 발키리에와 영웅들이 등장한다. 신들은 가장 고귀하고 드높은 종족으로, 처음에는 산속의 초원에서 살다가 후에는 높은 봉우리 위의 궁전 발할라에서 산다. 거인들은 위대하고 강한 종족이지만 지혜가 모자라며, 고귀한 것을 미워하고 신들의 적이다. 그들은 땅 표면 가까운 동굴에서 산다. 난쟁이들, 곧 니벨룽족은 검고 거칠게 생긴 난쟁이들로, 선한 것을 미워하고 신들을 미워하며, 교활하고 간교하여 땅속 깊은 곳에서 산다. 님프들은 순수하고 죄 없는 물의 피조물이다. 발키리에는 신들의 딸이지만 인간의 혈통이 섞여 있으며, 전장에서 죽은 영웅들을 거두어 발할라로 데려간다. 영웅들은 신들의 자손이지만 역시 인간의 혈통이 섞여 있으며, 마침내 모든 종족 가운데 가장 드높은 종족이 되어 세상의 통치에서 신들을 잇도록 운명 지어져 있다.\n\n주요한 신들은 보탄과 로키와 도너와 프로다. 으뜸가는 거인들은 형제인 파프너와 파졸트이다. 으뜸가는 난쟁이들은 형제인 알베리히와 미메, 그리고 후에 알베리히의 아들 하간이다. 으뜸가는 님프들은 라인의 딸들인 플로스힐다와 보글린다와 벨군다이다. 발키리에는 아홉이 있는데, 그 가운데 브룬힐트가 으뜸가는 자이다.\n\n바그너의 〈반지〉 이야기는 다음과 같이 요약할 수 있다.\n\n라인 강 깊은 곳에 황금 무더기가 있어, 죄 없는 라인의 처녀들이 그것을 지키고 있다. 난쟁이 알베리히가 이 황금을 얻고자 사랑을 버린다. 그는 그것으로 마법의 반지를 만든다. 그 반지는 그에게 온갖 권능을 주고, 그는 그것으로 막대한 양의 보물을 그러모은다.\n\n한편 신들의 우두머리 보탄은 세상을 다스릴 거처로 삼을 고귀한 성 발할라를 지어 달라고 거인들과 계약을 맺고, 그 대가로 젊음과 사랑의 여신 프레이야를 주기로 약속했다. 그러나 신들은 자신들의 불멸의 젊음이 프레이야에게 달려 있는지라 그녀를 내어 줄 수 없음을 알게 된다. 대신할 것을 마련하라는 요구를 받은 로키는 알베리히의 마법 반지와 그 밖의 보물에 대해 이야기한다. 보탄은 로키와 함께 가서 알베리히에게서 반지와 황금 무더기를 훔치고, 알베리히는 그 반지를 저주하며 이후로 그것을 지니는 모든 자에게 저주를 내린다. 신들은 프레이야 대신 그 반지와 보물을 거인들에게 준다. 저주는 즉시 시작된다. 거인 가운데 하나인 파프너는 모든 것을 차지하려고 형제를 죽이고, 자신의 재물을 지키려 스스로를 용으로 변신시킨다. 신들은 무지개다리를 건너 발할라로 들어간다. 이로써 〈라인의 황금〉이라 불리는 극의 첫째 부분이 끝난다.\n\n둘째 부분 〈발키리에〉는, 보탄이 여전히 그 반지를 탐낸다는 이야기를 전한다. 그는 거인들에게 약속을 했기에 스스로 그것을 빼앗을 수 없다. 그는 자신의 약속으로 흥하기도 망하기도 하는 것이다. 그래서 그는 반지를 손에 넣을 책략을 꾸민다. 그는 자신을 위해 일하며 반지와 보물을 되찾아 줄 영웅의 종족을 얻으려 한다. 지크문트와 지크린데가 이 새 종족의 쌍둥이 아이들이다. 지크린데는 어린 시절 납치되어 훈딩과의 혼인을 강요당한다. 지크문트가 와서 자기도 모르게 혼인의 법을 어기지만, 위대한 검 노퉁과 신부를 얻는다. 발키리에의 우두머리 브룬힐트는 혼인의 여신 프리카의 청에 따라, 지크문트의 죄를 물어 그를 죽이라는 명을 보탄에게서 받는다. 그녀는 명을 거역하고 그를 구하려 하지만, 보탄의 도움을 받은 훈딩이 그를 죽인다. 그러나 장차 지크프리트라 불릴 자유로운 영웅을 잉태하고 있던 지크린데는 브룬힐트에게 구원받아 숲속에 숨겨진다. 브룬힐트 자신은 인간 여인이 되는 벌을 받는다. 그녀는 영웅만이 뚫을 수 있는 불의 벽에 둘러싸인 채 산 위에 잠든 채로 남겨진다.\n\n극은 지크프리트의 이야기로 이어지는데, 그것은 난쟁이 미메와 지크프리트 사이의 대장간 장면으로 열린다. 미메는 검을 벼리고 있고, 지크프리트는 그를 경멸한다. 미메는 그에게 그의 어머니 지크린데에 대해 조금 일러 주고, 그의 아버지의 부러진 검 조각들을 보여 준다. 보탄이 와서, 두려움을 모르는 자만이 그 검을 다시 벼릴 수 있다고 미메에게 말한다. 그런데 지크프리트는 두려움을 모르는지라 곧 검 노퉁을 다시 벼려 낸다. 보탄과 알베리히는 용 파프너가 반지를 지키고 있는 곳으로 온다. 둘 다 그것을 갈망하지만, 어느 쪽도 그것을 빼앗을 수 없다. 곧 미메가 위대한 검을 지닌 지크프리트를 데리고 온다. 파프너가 나오지만, 지크프리트가 그를 죽인다. 어쩌다 용의 피가 입술에 닿자, 그는 새들의 언어를 알아듣게 된다. 새들은 그에게 반지에 대해 일러 준다. 그는 가서 그것을 손에 넣는다. 이제 지크프리트가 반지를 차지하게 되었으나, 그것은 그에게 어떤 행복도 가져다주지 않고 오직 재앙만을 가져올 것이다. 그것은 사랑을 저주하고 마침내 죽음을 불러올 것이다. 새들은 또한 그에게 미메의 배신을 일러 준다. 그는 미메를 죽인다. 그는 사랑할 누군가를 갈망한다. 새들은 그에게 잠들어 있는 브룬힐다에 대해 일러 주고, 그는 그녀를 찾아내어 혼인한다.\n\n〈신들의 황혼〉은 그 첫머리에 운명의 실을 잣고 재는 세 노른, 곧 운명의 여신들을 그린다. 이는 종말의 시작이다. 완벽한 한 쌍인 지크프리트와 브룬힐트가 그들 삶의 온갖 영광 속에서, 남성다움과 여성다움의 찬란한 이상으로 나타난다. 그러나 지크프리트는 무용(武勇)의 위업을 이루러 세상으로 나아간다. 그는 돌아올 때까지 자신의 사랑의 정표로 간직하라며 그녀에게 니벨룽겐의 반지를 준다. 한편 알베리히 또한 자신을 위해 반지를 손에 넣어 줄 아들 하간을 낳았다. 하간은 일부는 기비흥 종족의 피를 지녔으며, 자신의 이복형제와 이복누이인 군터와 구트루네를 통해 일을 꾸민다. 그들은 지크프리트를 자기들에게로 꾀어 들여, 그에게 브룬힐트를 잊고 구트루네와 사랑에 빠지게 만드는 마법의 음료를 먹인다. 같은 마법에 걸린 채 그는 브룬힐트를 군터의 아내로 데려오겠노라 제안한다. 이제 발할라는 슬픔과 절망으로 가득하다. 신들은 종말을 두려워한다. 보탄은 \"아, 그녀가 그 반지를 라인 강에 돌려주기만 한다면\" 하고 중얼거린다. 그러나 브룬힐트는 그것을 내놓으려 하지 않으니, 이제 그것은 그녀의 사랑의 정표인 것이다. 지크프리트가 와서 반지를 빼앗고, 브룬힐트는 이제 기비흥 종족의 라인 강 성으로 끌려오지만, 마법에 걸린 지크프리트는 그녀를 사랑하지 않는다. 그녀는 군터와 혼인하게 될 처지이다. 그녀는 분노하여 일어서서 지크프리트를 규탄한다. 그러나 사냥 연회에서 지크프리트는 또 다른 마법의 음료를 받아 마시고 모든 것을 기억해 내며, 사랑에 잠겨 브룬힐트의 이름을 부르던 그 순간 등에 일격을 맞아 하간에게 죽임을 당한다. 그리하여 종말이 온다. 지크프리트의 시신은 장작더미 위에서 불태워지고, 장엄한 장송 행진곡이 울려 퍼지며, 브룬힐트는 불길 속으로 말을 달려 들어가 사랑을 위해 자신을 희생한다. 반지는 라인의 딸들에게 되돌아가고, 발할라의 신들과 정념과 죄로 이루어진 옛 세계는 불길에 타 버린다. 신들이 도덕의 법을 어기고 사랑보다 권력을, 진실보다 황금을 탐했으므로 멸망해야 마땅한 것이다. 그들은 스러지고, 새로운 시대, 곧 사랑과 진실의 치세가 시작되었다.\n\n〈니벨룽겐의 노래〉와 〈니벨룽의 반지〉의 전설들 사이의 차이, 그리고 바그너가 이 옛 소재를 사용한 방식을 연구하고자 하는 이들에게는, W. C. 소여 교수의 저서 〈니벨룽겐의 노래와 니벨룽의 반지에 나타난 게르만 전설〉을 권한다. 거기서 이 문제가 충분히 상세하게 다루어진다. 바그너가 제시한 대로의 〈반지〉를 음악적 주제들에 대한 연구와 더불어 매우 철저하고 명료하게 분석한 것으로는, 일반 독자에게는 아마도 프레다 윈워스의 〈소리의 서사시〉라는 책보다 나은 것이 없을 것이다. 라비냐크 교수의 보다 학구적인 저작은 바그너의 악극을 공부하는 이에게 없어서는 안 될 것이다. J. L. 웨스턴의 〈바그너 악극의 전설〉에는 그 출전과 소재에 관한 밝혀 주는 바가 많은 해설이 담겨 있다.\n\n————\n\n드루이드는 갈리아와 브리타니아, 게르마니아에 살던 고대 켈트 민족 사이에서 종교의 사제 또는 봉사자였다. 그들에 관해 우리가 아는 바는 그리스와 로마 저술가들이 남긴 기록을, 오늘날까지 전해지는 웨일스와 게일의 시가 유물과 비교하여 얻은 것이다.\n\n드루이드는 사제와 행정관, 학자, 그리고 의사의 기능을 한데 겸하였다. 그들이 켈트 부족의 사람들에 대해 가졌던 관계는, 인도의 브라만이나 페르시아의 마기, 이집트의 사제들이 저마다 그들을 숭배하던 사람들에 대해 가졌던 관계와 매우 흡사한 것이었다.\n\n드루이드는 단 하나의 신이 존재한다고 가르쳤으며, 그 신에게 〈베알(Be’al)〉이라는 이름을 붙였다. 켈트 고고학자들의 말에 따르면 이 이름은 〈모든 것의 생명〉, 혹은 〈모든 존재의 근원〉을 뜻하며, 페니키아의 바알과 친연성이 있는 것으로 보인다. 이 친연성을 한층 두드러지게 만드는 것은, 드루이드가 페니키아인과 마찬가지로 자신들의 이 최고신을 〈태양〉과 동일시했다는 점이다. 불은 신성의 상징으로 여겨졌다. 라틴 저술가들은 드루이드가 그밖에도 수많은 하급 신을 숭배했다고 주장한다.\n\n그들은 숭배의 대상을 나타내기 위해 어떠한 형상도 사용하지 않았으며, 신성한 의식을 거행하기 위해 신전이나 그 어떤 종류의 건물에 모이지도 않았다. 돌들이 이룬 원(저마다의 돌은 대개 거대한 크기였다)이 지름 20피트에서 30야드에 이르는 구역을 에워쌌으니, 이것이 그들의 신성한 장소를 이루었다. 오늘날까지 남아 있는 이런 유적 가운데 가장 유명한 것은 잉글랜드 솔즈베리 평원에 있는 스톤헨지다.\n\n이 신성한 원들은 대개 어떤 시냇가 근처, 혹은 작은 숲이나 널리 가지를 펼친 떡갈나무의 그늘 아래에 자리 잡았다. 원의 한가운데에는 크롬렉, 곧 제단이 서 있었는데, 그것은 커다란 돌을 세워 둔 다른 돌들 위에 탁자처럼 얹어 놓은 것이었다. 드루이드에게는 또한 그들만의 높은 장소가 있었으니, 언덕 꼭대기에 놓인 커다란 돌이나 돌무더기가 그것이었다. 이런 것들은 케언이라 불렸으며, 태양의 상징 아래 신을 숭배하는 데 쓰였다.\n\n드루이드가 자신들의 신에게 제물을 바쳤다는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없다. 그러나 그들이 무엇을 바쳤는지는 다소 불분명하며, 그들의 종교 의례에 결부된 의식에 관해서는 우리가 거의 아무것도 알지 못한다. 고전기(로마)의 저술가들은 그들이 큰일이 있을 때, 이를테면 전쟁의 승리를 위해서나 위험한 질병에서 벗어나기 위해 인간 제물을 바쳤다고 단언한다. 카이사르는 이 일이 어떤 방식으로 행해졌는지 상세히 기록해 두었다. \"그들에게는 거대한 크기의 형상들이 있는데, 그 사지는 비틀어 엮은 잔가지로 짜였으며 그 안에는 살아 있는 사람들이 채워진다. 여기에 불을 붙이면 안에 있는 자들은 불길에 둘러싸인다.\" 켈트의 저술가들은 이 사실에 관한 로마 사가들의 증언을 흔들어 보려고 여러 차례 시도했으나, 끝내 성공하지 못했다.\n\n드루이드는 해마다 두 차례의 축제를 지켰다. 첫 번째 축제는 5월 초에 열렸으며, 벨테인 곧 〈신의 불〉이라 불렸다. 이때에는 어느 높은 곳에 큰 불을 피워 태양을 기렸는데, 겨울의 어둠과 황량함이 지난 뒤 다시 돌아오는 태양의 은혜를 이렇게 맞이한 것이다. 이 풍습의 자취는 오늘날까지도 스코틀랜드 일부 지방에서 성령강림절에 붙이는 이름에 남아 있다. 월터 스콧 경은 「호수의 여인」에 실린 〈뱃노래〉에서 이 말을 쓰고 있다.\n\n\"우리는 샘가에 우연히 씨가 뿌려진 어린나무가 아니니, 벨테인에 피어났다가 겨울에 시들지 않으리.\" 운운.\n\n드루이드의 또 다른 큰 축제는 〈삼인(Samh’in)〉 곧 〈평화의 불〉이라 불렸으며, 만성절 전야(11월 1일)에 열렸으니, 스코틀랜드 고지대에서는 지금까지도 이 명칭이 남아 있다. 이때에 드루이드는 그들 교단의 사법 기능을 수행하기 위해 그 지역의 가장 중심이 되는 곳에 엄숙히 모여 회의를 열었다. 공적인 것이든 사적인 것이든 모든 문제, 사람이나 재산에 대한 모든 범죄가 이때 그들 앞에 판결을 위해 회부되었다. 이러한 사법적 행위에는 어떤 미신적 관습이 결부되었으니, 특히 신성한 불을 피우는 일이 그러했다. 그 지역의 모든 불은 미리 빈틈없이 꺼 두었다가 이 불에서 다시 점화될 수 있었다. 만성절 전야에 불을 피우는 이 관습은 그리스도교가 확립된 뒤에도 오래도록 브리튼 제도에 남아 있었다.\n\n이 두 차례의 큰 연례 축제 말고도 드루이드는 보름달, 특히 달의 여섯째 날을 지키는 습관이 있었다. 이날 그들은 자신들이 아끼는 떡갈나무에서 자라는 겨우살이를 찾았는데, 떡갈나무 그 자체뿐 아니라 이 겨우살이에도 특별한 효험과 신성함이 깃들어 있다고 여겼다. 그것을 발견하는 일은 기쁨과 엄숙한 예배의 계기가 되었다. 플리니우스는 이렇게 말한다. \"그들은 그것을 자기네 언어로 〈만병통치〉를 뜻하는 말로 부르는데, 나무 아래에서 잔치와 제사를 위해 엄숙한 준비를 마친 다음, 그곳으로 젖처럼 흰 황소 두 마리를 끌고 온다. 그 황소들의 뿔은 그때 처음으로 묶인다. 그러고 나서 흰옷을 입은 사제가 나무에 올라가 황금 낫으로 겨우살이를 베어 낸다. 그것을 흰 외투로 받아 내고는, 이어서 제물을 죽이는데, 그와 동시에 신이 그 선물을 받은 자들에게 그 선물이 번성하게 해 주시기를 기도한다.\" 그들은 그것을 우려낸 물을 마시며, 그것을 모든 질병의 치료약이라 여긴다. 겨우살이는 기생 식물이라 떡갈나무에서 늘, 혹은 흔히 발견되는 것은 아니다. 그러므로 그것이 발견되면 그만큼 더 귀하게 여겨진다.\n\n드루이드는 종교뿐 아니라 도덕의 교사이기도 했다. 그들의 윤리적 가르침에 관해서는 웨일스 음유시인들의 트리어드에 귀중한 본보기가 보존되어 있다. 이로부터 우리는 도덕적 올바름에 관한 그들의 견해가 대체로 정당했으며, 그들이 매우 고결하고 값진 여러 행동의 원칙을 견지하고 가르쳤음을 짐작할 수 있다. 그들은 또한 그 시대와 민족 가운데서 학문과 지식을 갖춘 자들이었다. 그들이 문자를 알았는지 여부는 논쟁거리였으나, 어느 정도까지는 알았을 가능성이 높다. 그러나 그들이 자신들의 교리나 역사, 혹은 시 가운데 그 무엇도 글로 적어 두지 않았다는 것만은 확실하다. 그들의 가르침은 입으로 전해졌으며, 그들의 문학(이런 경우에 이 말을 써도 좋다면)은 오로지 구전으로 보존되었다. 그러나 로마 저술가들은 \"그들이 자연의 질서와 법칙에 많은 주의를 기울였으며, 자기네가 맡은 젊은이들에게 별과 그 운행, 세계와 대지의 크기, 그리고 불멸하는 신들의 위력과 권능에 관한 많은 것을 탐구하여 가르쳤다\"고 인정한다.\n\n그들의 역사는 전승되는 이야기들로 이루어졌으니, 그 속에서 선조들의 영웅적 행적이 기려졌다. 이 이야기들은 보아하니 운문이었던 것 같으며, 그리하여 드루이드의 역사이자 시의 일부를 이루었다. 오시안의 시에서 우리는, 비록 드루이드 시대의 실제 작품은 아닐지라도, 음유시인들의 노래를 충실히 재현한 것으로 간주할 만한 것을 만나게 된다.\n\n음유시인들은 드루이드 위계 조직의 필수적인 일부였다. 한 저술가, 페넌트는 이렇게 말한다. \"음유시인들은 영감에 맞먹는 능력을 부여받은 자들로 여겨졌다. 그들은 공적인 것이든 사적인 것이든 지난 모든 일을 입으로 전하는 사가였다. 그들은 또한 뛰어난 계보학자이기도 했다.\" 운운.\n\n페넌트는 음유시인과 악사들의 모임인 아이스테드보드에 관해 자세한 설명을 남겼는데, 이 모임은 드루이드 사제직이 그 다른 분야에서 소멸한 뒤로도 오래도록 여러 세기에 걸쳐 웨일스에서 열렸다. 이 모임에서는 오로지 실력 있는 음유시인만이 자기 작품을 읊을 수 있었으며, 솜씨 있는 악사만이 연주할 수 있었다. 저마다의 능력을 판가름할 심사관이 임명되었고, 합당한 등급이 수여되었다. 더 이른 시기에는 심사관이 웨일스 군주들에 의해 임명되었고, 웨일스가 정복된 뒤에는 잉글랜드 왕들의 위임을 받아 임명되었다. 그러나 전해 오는 이야기에 따르면, 에드워드 1세는 음유시인들이 자신의 지배에 맞서는 백성들의 저항을 북돋운 영향력에 앙심을 품고 그들을 가혹하게 박해했다고 한다. 이 전승은 시인 그레이에게 그의 유명한 송가 〈음유시인〉의 소재를 제공했다.\n\n지금도 웨일스의 시와 음악을 사랑하는 이들이 옛 이름을 내걸고 간간이 모임을 갖는다. 헤먼스 부인의 시 가운데 1822년 5월 22일 런던에서 열린 아이스테드보드, 곧 웨일스 음유시인들의 모임을 위해 쓴 작품이 있다. 그것은 옛 모임에 대한 묘사로 시작되는데, 다음 시행들이 그 일부다.\n\n\". . . 영원한 절벽들 한가운데, 그 굳셈은 교만의 시절에 투구 쓴 로마인마저 물리쳤으니, 드루이드의 옛 크롬렉이 험상궂게 내려다보고, 떡갈나무들이 사방에 신비로운 속삭임을 불어넣던 그곳, 그곳에 옛적의 영감받은 자들이 모여들었네! 평원에서나 높은 곳에서나, 태양의 얼굴 앞에서, 빛의 눈길 아래, 저마다 고귀한 머리를 하늘 향해 드러내며, 그 누구도 발 디딜 수 없는 원 안에 그들은 섰네.\"\n\n드루이드 체계는 율리우스 카이사르가 이끈 로마의 침공 무렵에 절정에 이르렀다. 이 세계의 정복자들은 자신들의 으뜸가는 적인 드루이드를 향해 가차 없는 분노를 쏟아부었다. 사방에서 시달리던 드루이드는 본토에서 앵글시와 이오나로 물러났으니, 그곳에서 한동안 피난처를 얻어 이제는 명예를 잃은 자신들의 의식을 이어 갔다.\n\n드루이드는 이오나와 그 인접한 섬들 및 본토에서 우위를 지켜 갔으나, 고지대의 사도 성 콜룸바가 다다르면서 그 자리를 빼앗기고 그들의 미신이 뒤엎이게 되었다. 그 지역의 주민들이 처음으로 그리스도교를 신앙하게 된 것은 콜룸바에 의해서였다.\n\n브리튼 제도 가운데 가장 작은 섬의 하나로, 험준하고 메마른 해안 가까이 자리 잡았으며, 위험한 바다에 둘러싸이고 내부의 부의 원천이라고는 지니지 못했음에도, 이오나는 이교의 어둠이 북유럽 거의 전역에 드리워져 있던 시대에 문명과 종교의 본거지로서 역사 속에 사라지지 않는 자리를 얻었다. 이오나 곧 아이콜름킬은 멀섬의 끄트머리에 자리 잡고 있는데, 그 섬과는 너비 반 마일의 해협으로 갈라져 있으며, 스코틀랜드 본토로부터의 거리는 36마일이다.\n\n콜룸바는 아일랜드 태생으로, 출생으로 보아 그 땅의 군주들과 연이 닿아 있었다. 당시 아일랜드는 복음의 빛이 비치는 땅이었으나, 스코틀랜드의 서부와 북부는 여전히 이교의 어둠에 잠겨 있었다. 콜룸바는 열두 명의 벗과 함께 주후 563년에 이오나섬에 상륙했는데, 가죽을 씌운 버들 배로 그 항해를 마쳤다. 섬을 차지하고 있던 드루이드는 그가 그곳에 자리 잡는 것을 막으려 했고, 인접한 해안의 야만 민족들은 적의를 품고 그를 괴롭혔으며, 여러 차례 그를 공격하여 목숨까지 위태롭게 했다. 그러나 그는 끈기와 열성으로 모든 반대를 이겨 냈고, 왕으로부터 그 섬을 선물로 얻어 그곳에 수도원을 세우고 그 원장이 되었다. 그는 스코틀랜드의 고지대와 섬들 전역에 성서의 지식을 퍼뜨리는 일에 지칠 줄 몰랐으며, 그에게 바쳐진 존경이 어찌나 깊었던지, 그가 주교가 아니라 한낱 장로이자 수도사에 지나지 않았음에도 그 지방 전체가 주교들과 더불어 그와 그의 후계자들에게 복속하였다. 픽트족 군주는 그의 지혜와 인품에 깊이 감명받아 그를 더없이 높이 받들었으며, 인근의 족장과 군주들은 그의 조언을 구하고 분쟁을 가라앉히는 데 그의 판단을 활용했다.\n\n콜룸바가 이오나에 상륙했을 때 그를 수행한 것은 열두 명의 추종자였으니, 그는 이들을 자신이 우두머리로 있는 하나의 종교 단체로 만들었다. 여기에는 필요에 따라 때때로 다른 이들이 더해졌으므로, 본래의 인원수는 늘 유지되었다. 그들의 조직은 수도원이라 불렸고 그 윗사람은 원장이라 불렸으나, 이 체계는 후대의 수도원 제도와 공통된 점이 거의 없었다. 그 규율을 따른 자들에게 붙은 이름은 컬디였는데, 아마도 라틴어 〈쿨토레스 데이(cultores Dei)〉 곧 〈신을 숭배하는 자들〉에서 온 듯하다. 그들은 복음을 전하고 젊은이를 가르치는 공동의 일에서 서로를 돕고, 또한 함께 예배하는 수행을 통해 자신들 안에 신앙의 열정을 지켜 나가기 위해 함께 모인 종교적 사람들의 무리였다. 이 교단에 들어갈 때 단원들은 어떤 서약을 했으나, 그것은 수도회들이 흔히 부과하던 것이 아니었다. 그 서약은 셋, 곧 독신과 청빈, 순종인데, 컬디는 이 가운데 세 번째 것을 제외하고는 어느 것에도 매이지 않았다. 청빈에는 스스로를 묶지 않았으니, 도리어 그들은 자신과 자신에게 딸린 이들을 위해 삶의 안락을 마련하고자 부지런히 일했던 것으로 보인다. 결혼 또한 그들에게 허락되었으며, 그들 대다수는 그 상태로 들어갔던 것으로 보인다. 사실 그들의 아내는 그 시설에서 그들과 함께 거주하는 것이 허락되지 않았으나, 인근의 한 장소에 거처가 배정되었다. 이오나 가까이에는 지금도 〈에일렌 남 반(Eilen nam ban)〉 곧 여인의 섬이라는 이름을 지닌 섬이 있는데, 그들의 남편들은 학교나 성소에 그들의 임무가 요구할 때를 빼고는 그곳에서 아내와 함께 거주했던 것으로 보인다.\n\n캠벨은 그의 시 「레울루라」에서 이오나의 기혼 수도사들을 넌지시 가리킨다.\n\n\". . . 순결한 컬디는 앨빈에서 가장 이른 신의 사제였으니, 아직 그 바다의 어느 섬도 색슨 수도사의 발에 밟히기 전, 그 땅의 성직자들이 편협한 신앙으로 거룩한 혼인의 인연에서 가로막히기 한참 전이었네. 그때에 멀리까지 이름난 아오드가 이오나에서 권능으로 말씀을 전했고, 아름다움의 별 레울루라가 그의 거처를 함께하는 짝이었네.\"\n\n무어는 그의 「아일랜드 선율」 가운데 하나에서 성 세나누스와, 섬에서 피난처를 구하려다 쫓겨난 여인의 전설을 들려준다.\n\n\"오, 서둘러 이 거룩한 섬을 떠나라, 부정한 배여, 아침이 미소 짓기 전에. 네 갑판 위로, 비록 어둠에 잠겼어도, 여인의 모습이 내게 보이는구나. 그리고 나는 맹세했노라, 이 성스러운 흙은 결코 여인의 발에 밟히지 않으리라고.\"\n\n이런 점들에서, 또 다른 점들에서 컬디는 로마 교회의 확립된 규율에서 벗어났으며, 그리하여 이단으로 여겨졌다. 그 결과 로마 교회의 세력이 자라남에 따라 컬디의 세력은 약해졌다. 그러나 컬디의 공동체가 억압되고 단원들이 흩어진 것은 13세기에 이르러서였다. 그들은 그 뒤로도 개인으로서 계속 일했으며, 종교개혁의 빛이 세상에 동틀 때까지 교황권의 침탈에 맞서 할 수 있는 한 저항했다.\n\n이오나는 서쪽 바다에 자리 잡은 까닭에, 그 바다를 들끓게 하던 노르웨이와 덴마크 약탈자들의 습격에 노출되었으며, 그들에게 거듭 약탈당하고 거처가 불태워졌으며 평화로운 주민들이 칼날에 쓰러졌다. 이런 불리한 상황은 그 섬의 점진적인 쇠락을 불러왔고, 그 쇠락은 스코틀랜드 전역에 걸친 컬디의 전복으로 한층 빨라졌다. 교황 지배의 시대에 그 섬은 수녀원의 본거지가 되었는데, 그 폐허는 지금도 볼 수 있다. 종교개혁 때, 수도원이 헐릴 적에 수녀들은 공동체를 이루어 살며 머물도록 허락받았다.\n\n이오나는 오늘날 그 위에서 발견되는 수많은 교회와 무덤 유적 덕분에 주로 여행자들이 찾는 곳이 되었다. 그 가운데 으뜸가는 것은 대성당 곧 수도원 교회와 수녀원의 부속 예배당이다. 이러한 교회 고대 유물 말고도 더 이른 시기의 것들이 있으니, 그 섬에 그리스도교와는 다른 예배와 신앙의 형태가 존재했음을 가리킨다. 이것들은 여러 곳에서 발견되는 둥근 케언으로, 드루이드에서 비롯된 것으로 보인다. 존슨이 \"마라톤 평원에서 애국심이 더 큰 힘을 얻지 못할 사람, 혹은 이오나의 폐허 가운데서 경건함이 더 뜨거워지지 못할 사람은 부러워할 것이 거의 없는 자다\"라고 외친 것은 바로 이 고대 종교의 온갖 유물을 두고 한 말이었다.\n\n스콧은 「섬들의 군주」에서 이오나의 교회를, 그 맞은편에 있는 스타파의 동굴과 아름답게 견주어 보인다.\n\n\"자연이 친히, 그리 보였으니, 세우려 했네 그 창조주를 기리는 한 성소를! 더 하찮은 쓰임을 위해서가 아니라네, 그 기둥들이 솟구치고 그 아치들이 휘어진 것은. 덜 엄숙한 주제를 노래함도 아니라네, 썰물과 밀물로 굽이치는 저 거대한 물결이. 그 무서운 멈춤 사이마다 여전히, 높은 천장으로부터 응답을 끌어내니, 가지가지 가락으로, 길게 끌며 드높이, 오르간의 선율을 무색하게 한다네. 그 입구가 옛 이오나의 거룩한 신전을 마주 보고 있음도 헛되지 않으니, 자연의 목소리가 이렇게 말하는 듯하네, 너는 참으로 잘 해냈구나, 진흙으로 빚어진 가녀린 자식이여! 네 보잘것없는 힘이 저 당당한 성소를 높고 어렵게 부려 냈도다 — 허나 내 것을 보라!\"\n\n로마의 세력이 쇠퇴하던 무렵, 그러니까 그리스도 이후 약 5세기경에, 북부 유럽의 여러 나라는 국가적 통치 체제를 거의 갖추지 못한 상태로 남게 되었다. 크고 작은 권세를 지닌 수많은 우두머리들이, 각자가 자신의 지배권을 강제할 수 있는 범위 안에서 지역적 통치를 행했고, 이따금 그 우두머리들이 공통의 목적을 위해 결속하기도 했다. 그러나 평상시에는 서로 적대하는 모습을 훨씬 더 자주 보였다. 이러한 상황에서 사회의 하층 계급이 누려야 할 권리는 모든 공격자의 처분에 내맡겨져 있었으니, 우두머리들의 무법적 권세에 대한 어떤 제어가 없었다면 사회가 야만 상태로 다시 떨어졌으리라는 점은 분명하다. 그러한 제어는, 첫째로 우두머리들 자신의 경쟁 관계에서 찾을 수 있었으니, 서로에 대한 시기심이 그들로 하여금 서로를 억제하게 했다. 둘째로는 교회의 영향에서 찾을 수 있었으니, 교회는 순수한 동기에서든 이기적인 동기에서든 온갖 명분을 내세워 약자의 보호를 위해 개입하기로 서약한 바였다. 마지막으로는, 격정과 이기심의 무게에 짓눌려 있을지언정 인간의 마음속에 본래 깃들어 있는 관대함과 옳음에 대한 감각에서 찾을 수 있었다. 바로 이 마지막 원천에서 기사도가 솟아났으니, 그것은 영웅적 인격의 이상을 빚어내었다. 그 이상은 무적의 힘과 용맹, 정의, 겸손, 윗사람에 대한 충성, 동등한 이에 대한 예의, 약자에 대한 연민, 그리고 교회에 대한 헌신을 한데 아우른 것이었다. 그것은 비록 실제 삶에서는 결코 마주칠 수 없었을지라도, 모두가 본받아야 할 최고의 본보기로 인정하던 이상이었다.\n\n\"기사도\"라는 말은 프랑스어로 말[馬]을 뜻하는 슈발(cheval)에서 유래했다. 본래는 소년이나 시종을 뜻하던 \"기사(knight)\"라는 말은, 무기를 지닐 특권을 인정받은 젊은이에게 특별히 적용되었다. 이 특권은 오로지 가문과 재산을 갖춘 젊은이에게만 주어졌으니, 일반 대중은 무기를 갖추지 못했기 때문이다. 따라서 기사란 말을 탄 전사요, 신분이 있는 자이거나 어떤 신분 있는 자를 섬기고 그 부양을 받는 자였다. 대개는 독립적인 생계 수단을 어느 정도 갖추고 있었으나, 흔히 자신이 섬기는 자들의 감사에 주로 의존하여 필요한 것을 충당했고, 또한 분명 권세가 그 소유자에게 부여하는 수단에 의지하는 일도 잦았다.\n\n전시에는 기사가 자기 추종자들과 함께 군주의 진영에 머물거나, 전장에서 지휘하거나, 군주를 위해 어떤 성을 지켰다. 평화로운 시기에는 흔히 군주의 궁정에 시중을 들며, 군주들이 여가를 즐기던 연회와 마상 시합에 자신의 존재로 빛을 더했다. 혹은 모험을 찾아 온 나라를 누비며, 잘못을 바로잡고 권리를 지키는 일에 공공연히 헌신했고, 때로는 신앙이나 사랑의 어떤 서약을 이행하기 위함이었다. 이렇게 떠돌아다니는 기사들을 편력 기사라 불렀다. 그들은 귀족의 성에서 환영받는 손님이었으니, 그들의 존재가 그 외딴 거처의 따분함을 활기차게 해 주었기 때문이다. 또한 그들은 수도원에서도 존경으로 맞이되었으니, 수도원은 흔히 그 수입의 가장 좋은 부분을 기사들의 후원에 빚지고 있었다. 그러나 가까이에 성도 수도원도 은둔처도 없을 때면, 그들의 굳센 습관 덕분에 어느 길가 십자가 발치에 저녁도 못 먹은 채 누워 밤을 지새우는 일도 견디지 못할 바가 아니었다.\n\n이러한 수단으로 집행되는 정의가 가장 거친 종류일 수밖에 없었으리라는 점은 명백하다. 잘못을 바로잡는 것을 정당한 목적으로 삼던 힘이, 잘못을 저지르는 데 손쉽게 변질될 수도 있었던 것이다. 그리하여 우리는 로맨스들에서, 비록 사실에 있어서는 허황될지언정 풍속의 묘사로서는 진실한 그 이야기들에서, 한 기사의 성이 흔히 주변 지방의 공포의 대상이었음을 보게 된다. 다시 말해, 지하 감옥에는 억압받는 기사와 귀부인들이 가득하여 자신들을 풀어 줄 어떤 투사가 나타나기를, 혹은 돈으로 몸값이 치러지기를 기다리고 있었으며, 한 무리의 빈둥거리는 가신들이 법과 정의를 아랑곳하지 않고 영주의 명령을 강제하려 늘 곁에 대기하고 있었고, 무장하지 않은 다수의 권리는 아무런 가치도 없었다. 기사도에 관한 이러한 사실과 이론의 모순은, 그것을 두고 사람들의 마음속에 존재하는 상반된 인상을 설명해 줄 것이다. 한편에서는 더없이 뜨거운 찬사의 주제가 되어 왔으나, 다른 한편에서는 그에 못지않게 열렬히 비난받아 왔다. 냉정하게 따져 보면, 근대에 이르러 기사도가 법의 지배에 자리를 내주었다는 것, 그리고 비록 덜 그림 같을지라도 갑옷 입은 투사 대신 민간의 행정관이 그 자리를 차지했다는 것을 우리는 스스로 다행으로 여기지 않을 수 없다.\n\n# THE TRAINING OF A KNIGHT\n\n기사 후보자들의 예비 교육은 길고도 고된 것이었다. 일곱 살이 되면 귀족의 자제들은 대개 아버지의 집을 떠나 장차 자기를 후원할 사람의 궁정이나 성으로 옮겨져, 한 교육관의 보살핌 아래 맡겨졌다. 교육관은 그들에게 신앙의 첫 조항들과 영주와 윗사람에 대한 존경과 경의를 가르쳤고, 궁정의 예식에 입문시켰다. 그들은 시동(侍童), 시종, 또는 종자(從者)라 불렸으며, 그 임무는 고기를 썰고, 식탁에서 시중을 들고, 그 밖의 천한 일들을 수행하는 것이었으나, 당시에는 그것이 굴욕적인 일로 여겨지지 않았다. 여가 시간에는 춤추는 법과 하프 켜는 법을 배웠고, \"숲\"과 \"강\"의 비법, 곧 사냥과 매사냥과 낚시를 익혔으며, 또한 씨름과 창 겨루기, 그리고 말 위에서 행하는 그 밖의 군사 훈련을 배웠다. 열네 살이 되면 시동은 종자(esquire)가 되어, 더 혹독하고 더 고된 훈련의 과정에 들어섰다. 무거운 갑옷을 입은 채 말 위에 뛰어오르는 일, 같은 짐을 진 채로 달리고 성벽을 기어오르고 도랑을 뛰어넘는 일, 면갑을 들어 올리거나 숨을 돌리지 않고 오랫동안 씨름하고 전투용 도끼를 휘두르는 일, 그리고 마술(馬術)의 온갖 동작을 우아하게 해내는 일, 이런 것들이 기사 작위를 받기 위한 필수적인 예비 과정이었다. 기사 작위는 대개 스물한 살에 수여되었으니, 그 무렵이면 젊은이의 교육이 완성된 것으로 여겨졌다. 그동안 종자들은 그 시대에 \"예의\"라 불리던 것을 이루는 온갖 세련된 교양을 익히는 데에도 그에 못지않게 부지런히 힘썼다. 그들이 교육받던 바로 그 성에는 대개 이성(異性)의 젊은이들도 가득했으니, 시동은 아주 어린 나이부터 궁정의 어떤 귀부인을 자기 마음의 연인으로 고르도록 장려되었고, 자신의 모든 감정과 말과 행동을 그녀에게 바치도록 가르침을 받았다. 자기 연인을 섬기는 일이 기사의 영광이요 소임이었으며, 애정과 감사에서 동시에 베풀어지는 그녀의 미소가 그의 올바로 발휘된 용맹에 대한 보상으로 제시되었다. 종교는 충성과 사랑의 영향에 그 영향을 더했고, 사제직에 따르던 온갖 신성함과 종교적 외경을 갖춘 기사단은 가장 위대한 군주들에게도 야망의 대상이 되었다.\n\n입회 의식은 유난히 엄숙했다. 혹독한 단식을 견디고 여러 날 밤을 기도로 지새운 뒤, 후보자는 고해하고 성찬을 받았다. 그런 다음 눈처럼 흰 옷을 입고, 의식이 열릴 교회나 큰 방으로 나아갔는데, 그때 목에 기사의 검을 매달고 있었으니, 집전하는 사제가 그것을 받아 축복한 뒤 그에게 다시 돌려주었다. 이어 후보자는 팔짱을 끼고 주관하는 기사 앞에 무릎을 꿇었으며, 그 기사는 입회를 청하는 동기와 목적에 관해 몇 가지를 물은 뒤 그에게 서약을 시키고 그의 청을 허락했다. 그 자리에 있던 몇몇 기사들이, 때로는 귀부인과 처녀들까지도, 그에게 차례로 박차와 사슬 갑옷, 호버크, 팔 보호구와 손갑(手甲)을 건넸으며, 마지막으로 그는 검을 허리에 찼다. 그런 다음 그는 다시 주관자 앞에 무릎을 꿇었고, 주관자는 자리에서 일어나 그에게 \"어콜레이드\"를 베풀었으니, 그것은 검의 평평한 면으로 후보자의 어깨나 목을 세 번 두드리는 것으로, 다음과 같은 말이 곁들여졌다. \"하느님과 성 미카엘과 성 게오르기우스의 이름으로, 그대를 기사로 삼노라. 용맹하고, 예의 바르며, 충성스러울지어다!\" 그런 다음 그는 투구와 방패와 창을 받았으니, 이로써 작위 수여가 끝났다.\n\n# FREEMEN, VILLAINS, SERFS, AND CLERKS\n\n사회를 이루던 다른 계급들로는, 첫째 자유민이 있었으니, 이들은 작은 토지를 소유한 독립적인 사람들이었다. 다만 이들은 때때로 자발적으로 더 부유한 이웃의 봉신이 되기도 했으니, 그 이웃의 권세가 자신들의 보호에 필요했기 때문이다. 다른 두 계급은 훨씬 더 수가 많았는데, 농노이거나 예농(隸農)이었으며, 둘 다 노예였다.\n\n농노는 노예 상태의 가장 낮은 단계에 있었다. 그들의 노동의 모든 결실은, 그들이 경작하던 땅의 주인에게 속했으니, 주인이 그들을 먹이고 입혔다.\n\n예농은 덜 비참했다. 그들의 처지는 오늘날 러시아 농민의 처지와 비슷했던 것으로 보인다. 농노와 마찬가지로 그들도 토지에 매여 있었고, 토지가 매매될 때 함께 넘겨졌다. 그러나 그들은 지주에게 정해진 소작료만 냈고, 자신들의 근면에서 생겨난 잉여가 있으면 그것을 처분할 권리가 있었다.\n\n\"성직자(clerk)\"라는 말은 매우 넓은 의미를 지녔다. 본래는 성직자, 곧 성직 계급에 속한 사람들만을 가리켰으나, 그 가운데에는 기혼자와 장인(匠人)을 비롯한 여러 사람이 많이 섞여 있을 수 있었다. 그러나 시간이 흐르면서 훨씬 더 폭넓은 규칙이 자리 잡아, 글을 읽을 수 있는 사람은 누구나 성직자(clericus)로 간주되어 \"성직자의 특전\"을, 곧 죄를 지었을 경우 사형과 그 밖의 몇몇 형벌을 면제받는 혜택을 누리게 되었다.\n\n기사들 사이의 마상 시합이라는 화려한 구경거리, 그 현란한 부속물과 장식, 그리고 그 기사도적인 규칙은 프랑스에서 비롯되었다. 마상 시합은 교회로부터 거듭 비난받았는데, 아마도 그것이 불러일으키던 다툼과 흔히 목숨까지 잃게 되는 결과 때문이었을 것이다. \"주스트(joust)\" 곧 마상 창 시합은 마상 시합과는 달랐다. 주스트에서는 기사들이 창을 들고 싸웠고, 그 목적은 상대를 말에서 떨어뜨리는 것이었다. 반면 마상 시합은 여러 동작과 갖가지 무기를 다루는 기량과 솜씨를 펼쳐 보이기 위한 것이었고, 그 규칙에서는 더 큰 예의가 지켜졌다. 이 규칙에 따라 말을 다치게 하거나, 검의 끝을 쓰거나, 기사가 면갑을 들어 올리거나 투구 끈을 풀어헤친 뒤에 그를 치는 것은 금지되었다. 귀부인들은 이런 시합에서 자기 기사들을 격려했으니, 그들이 상을 내렸고, 승자의 무용은 로맨스와 노래의 주제가 되었다. 경기장을 내려다보는 관람석은 물론 탑, 테라스, 회랑, 매달린 정원 따위의 갖가지 모양으로 다양했으며, 융단과 천막과 깃발로 화려하게 꾸며져 있었다. 모든 출전자는 자신이 사랑의 종복으로 섬기는 귀부인의 이름을 외쳤다. 그는 으레 관람석을 올려다보며, 위에서 그에게 영향을 비처럼 쏟아붓는 빛나는 눈동자를 바라봄으로써 자신의 용기를 북돋우곤 했다. 기사들은 또한 \"정표\"를 지녔으니, 그것은 스카프, 베일, 소매, 팔찌, 죔쇠 따위, 한마디로 여성 복식의 어떤 조각이었으며, 자신의 투구나 방패나 갑옷에 매달려 있었다. 만약 싸움 도중에 이 부착물 가운데 어느 것이 떨어지거나 잃어버려지면, 그 아름다운 증여자는 때때로 자기 기사에게 새것을 보내 주었으니, 특히 그의 분투에 흡족했을 때에 그러했다.\n\n# MAIL ARMOR\n\n사슬 갑옷은, 호버크가 그 한 종류이며 그물코를 뜻하는 프랑스어 마유(maille)에서 그 이름이 나왔는데, 두 종류가 있었으니 판금 또는 비늘 갑옷과 사슬 갑옷이었다. 본래는 몸통만 보호하기 위해 쓰였으며 무릎 아래로는 내려오지 않았다. 짐수레꾼의 작업복 같은 모양이었고, 허리에 띠를 둘러 졸라맸다. 사슬로 된 장갑과 각반이 나중에 더해졌고, 두건도 더해졌으니, 필요할 때면 머리 위로 끌어 올려 얼굴만 드러내었다. 사슬 갑옷의 쇠 그물이 피부에 자국을 남기지 않도록 누비 안감을 댔으나, 그것으로는 충분치 않아 갑옷이 남긴 자국을 지우기 위해 목욕을 했다.\n\n호버크는 이중 사슬 갑옷으로 온몸을 완전히 덮는 것이었다. 어떤 호버크는 현대의 외투처럼 앞이 트여 있었고, 어떤 것은 셔츠처럼 막혀 있었다.\n\n그것을 이루던 사슬 갑옷은 수많은 쇠고리로 만들어졌는데, 각각의 고리에 다른 고리들이 끼워져, 전체가 일종의 그물 모양을 이루었으며, (적어도 어떤 경우에는) 그 그물코가 둥글었고 고리 하나하나가 따로 대갈못으로 박혀 있었다.\n\n호버크는 검의 가장 격렬한 일격도 막아 내었다. 그러나 창끝은 그물코를 뚫고 들어가거나 쇠를 살 속으로 박아 넣을 수도 있었다. 이를 막기 위해 그 아래에 두껍고 속을 잘 채운 더블릿을 입었으며, 그 안에는 흔히 쇠 가슴받이를 더 덧대었다. 그래서 초기 시인들에게 그토록 흔한 \"판금과 사슬을 둘 다 꿰뚫는다\"는 표현이 생겨난 것이다.\n\n사슬 갑옷은 1300년 무렵까지 널리 쓰이다가, 점차 판금 갑옷에 자리를 내주었으니, 판금 갑옷이란 단단한 쇳조각이나 판으로 이루어져 몸의 여러 부위에 맞춘 갑주였다.\n\n방패는 대개 나무로 만들어 가죽이나 그와 비슷한 재료로 덮었다. 검에 베여 잘리는 것을 어느 정도 막기 위해, 방패 둘레에 금속 테를 둘렀다.\n\n투구는 두 부분으로 이루어졌다. 하나는 머리통을 덮는 부분으로, 안쪽을 여러 개의 쇠 고리로 보강했다. 다른 하나는 면갑으로, 그 이름이 뜻하듯 들여다볼 수 있는 일종의 살창이었으며, 홈을 따라 미끄러지거나 굴대를 중심으로 돌려서 마음대로 올리고 내릴 수 있도록 고안되었다. 어떤 투구에는 \"비버(bever)\"라 불리는 한 가지 개량 장치가 더 있었으니, 그 이름은 마신다는 뜻의 이탈리아어 베베레(bevere)에서 나왔다. \"공기 통로\"를 뜻하는 벤테일(ventayle)은 이것의 또 다른 이름이다.\n\n투구가 떨어지거나 쳐서 벗겨질 가능성을 막기 위해, 그것을 여러 가닥의 끈으로 호버크의 그물코에 묶어 두었다. 따라서 한 기사가 쓰러지면, 그를 죽이기에 앞서 이 끈들을 풀어야 했다. 다만 때로는 호버크 자락을 들어 올려 배를 찌름으로써 죽이기도 했다. 죽음에 쓰던 도구는 오른쪽 옆구리에 차던 작은 단검이었다.\n\n책이 없던 시대, 귀족과 군주들 자신조차 글을 읽지 못하던 시대에는, 역사나 전승이 이야기꾼들에게 독점되어 있었다. 그들은 선조들의 경이로운 이야기들을 대대로 물려받아, 자신이 얻은 정보가 보태 주는 만큼 거기에 자기 나름의 보탬을 더하여 사람들에게 전했다. 시대착오는 물론 매우 흔해졌고, 지리와 장소와 풍속의 오류도 마찬가지로 흔해졌다. 가짜 족보가 지어내어져, 아서 왕과 그의 기사들, 그리고 샤를마뉴와 그의 용장들이 아이네아스나 헥토르, 혹은 그 밖의 어떤 트로이아 영웅의 후손으로 만들어졌다.\n\n\"로맨스\"라는 말의 유래에 관해서는, 라틴어와 토착 언어가 뒤섞여 서유럽에서 형성된 방언들이 랑그 로멘(Langue Romaine)이라는 이름을 얻었다는 사실로 거슬러 올라가게 된다. 프랑스어는 두 방언으로 나뉘었다. 루아르 강이 그 공통의 경계였다. 그 강 남쪽의 지방들에서는 긍정의 \"예\"를 오크(oc)라는 말로 나타냈고, 북쪽에서는 그것을 오일(oil, 곧 oui)이라 했다. 그래서 단테는 남쪽 언어를 랑그 도크(langue d’oc)라, 북쪽 언어를 랑그 도일(langue d’oil)이라 이름 붙였다. 노르만인들에 의해 잉글랜드로 들어와 오늘날의 프랑스어의 기원이 된 후자는 프랑스 로만어라 부를 수 있고, 전자는 프로방스 로만어라 부를 수 있으니, 그것은 프랑스 남부 지방인 프로방스와 랑그도크의 사람들이 쓰던 말이었기 때문이다.\n\n이 방언들은 곧 서로 매우 상반된 성격으로 구별되었다. 부드럽고 사람을 나른하게 하는 기후, 다른 해양 국가들과의 손쉬운 교류로 장려된 상업 정신, 부의 유입, 그리고 더 안정된 통치는, 프로방스 사람들의 어법을 다듬고 부드럽게 하는 데 이바지했을 것이다. 그들의 시인들은 트루바두르라는 이름으로 이탈리아 시인들의, 특히 페트라르카의 스승이 되었다. 그들이 즐겨 지은 작품은 시르방트(풍자시), 사랑 노래, 그리고 텐송이었으니, 마지막 것은 두 시인 사이의 일종의 운문 대화로, 그들은 사랑의 결의론(決疑論)의 어떤 섬세한 점들에 관해 서로 묻고 답했다. 프로방스 사람들은 이런 미묘한 문제에 너무나 완전히 빠져든 나머지, 모험과 기사도에 관한 허구의 이야기들을 짓는 일은 소홀히 하고 멸시했던 듯하며, 그것을 대체로 트루베르라 불리던 왕국 북부의 시인들에게 맡겨 두었다.\n\n기사도가 만인의 찬탄을 불러일으키고, 그 기사도의 온갖 노력이 종교의 적들을 향하던 시대에, 문학이 같은 자극을 받는 것은, 그리고 한층 더 큰 본받음을 불러일으킬 만한 용기와 경건의 본보기를 마련하고자 역사와 우화를 샅샅이 뒤지는 것은 자연스러운 일이었다. 아서 왕과 샤를마뉴가 이 목적을 위해 선택된 두 영웅이었다. 아서 왕의 자격은 그가 비록 늘 성공하지는 못했을지언정 용감한 전사였다는 데 있었다. 그는 이교도들, 곧 색슨인들의 군세에 큰 결의로 맞섰고, 그의 기억은 그의 동족인 브리튼인들에게 더없이 높은 존경으로 간직되었다. 그들은 웨일스로, 그리고 그와 동족인 나라 아르모리카, 곧 브르타뉴로 그의 공적의 기억을 지니고 들어갔으며, 그들의 민족적 허영심이 그것을 부지중에 부풀린 끝에, 실루레스(남웨일스)의 작은 군주가 잉글랜드와 갈리아, 그리고 유럽 대부분의 정복자로 과장되기에 이르렀다. 그의 족보는 점차 가공의 브루투스에게로, 그리고 트로이아 전쟁의 시기에까지 거슬러 올라갔으며, 웨일스어 혹은 아르모리카어로 일종의 연대기가 지어져, \"브리튼 왕들의 역사\"라는 거창한 제목 아래 1150년 무렵 몬머스의 제프리에 의해 라틴어로 번역되었다. 웨일스의 비평가들은 이 저작의 바탕이 7세기에 성 아사프의 주교 성 탈리안이 쓴 더 오래된 역사서였다고 본다.\n\n샤를마뉴로 말하자면, 그의 실제 공적만으로도 그의 불멸을 보장하기에 충분했으나, 사라센인들에 맞선 그의 \"성전(聖戰)\"이 허구의 인기 있는 소재가 되지 않을 수 없었다. 그리하여 이 전쟁들에 관한 허구의 역사가, 아마도 11세기 말엽에, 한 수도사에 의해 쓰였으니, 그는 동시대의 이름으로 자신의 저작을 꾸미면 거기에 권위가 더해지리라 여겨, 그것을 대담하게도 773년 무렵 랭스의 대주교였던 튀르팽의 작품으로 돌렸다.\n\n이 허구의 연대기들은 한동안 지역에서만, 혹은 특정 직업의 사람들만 접근할 수 있는 언어 속에 갇혀 있었다. 튀르팽과 제프리는 사실 당시 유일한 라틴어 학자였던 성직자들이 읽을 수 있었고, 제프리의 브리튼어 원전은 웨일스인들을 흡족하게 하는 데 이바지했을 것이다. 그러나 어느 쪽도 일반적으로 두루 쓰이는 친숙한 언어로 번역되기까지는 널리 대중적이 될 수 없었다. 당시 앵글로색슨어는 정복당해 노예가 된 한 민족만이 쓰던 말이었고, 스페인어와 이탈리아어는 아직 형성되지 않았으며, 오직 노르만 프랑스어만이 유럽 대부분의 지역에서 귀족들에 의해 말해지고 이해되었으니, 따라서 그것이 이 새로운 양식의 글쓰기에 알맞은 매개체였다.\n\n그 언어는 정복 이전부터 잉글랜드에서 유행했고, 정복 이후에는 런던 궁정에서 쓰이는 유일한 언어가 되었다. 노르만인들의 갖가지 정복과, 그 비범한 민족의 열광적인 용맹이 사람들의 마음을 더없이 경이로운 사건들에 익숙하게 만들어 놓았던 만큼, 그들의 시인들은 아서 왕과 샤를마뉴의 허구의 전설들을 열심히 붙잡아 당대의 언어로 옮겼고, 곧 갖가지 모방작을 내놓았다. 이 군주들과 그들의 이름난 전사들에게 돌려진 모험들이, 그리고 전승에서 전해지거나 상상으로 지어낸 다른 많은 영웅들의 모험들과 더불어, 점차 저 어마어마한 경이로운 이야기들의 덩어리를 이루었으니, 그 가운데 가장 오래된 것들이 쓰인 방언에서 따와 \"로맨스\"라 불리게 되었다.\n\n# METRICAL ROMANCES\n\n로맨스가 나타나는 가장 이른 형태는 일종의 거친 운문이었다. 이 형태로, 그것이 군주와 기사들의 향연에서 그들의 영주 저택의 큰 방에서 노래되거나 낭송되었던 것으로 여겨진다. 1257년에 활동한 로베르 드 보베의 언어와 문체를 보여 주는 다음의 표본은, 월터 스콧 경의 \"트리스트렘 경 로맨스 서론\"에서 가져온 것이다.\n\n\"Ne voil pas emmi dire, Ici diverse la matyere, Entre ceus qui solent cunter, E de le cunte Tristran parler.\" \"나는 그것에 관해 너무 많이 말하지 않으련다, 그 소재가 너무나 갖가지인 까닭에, 트리스트란의 이야기를 들려주고 이야기하는 데 익숙한 자들 사이에서.\"\n\n이것은 노르만 정복 직후의 시대에 잉글랜드 귀족들 사이에서 쓰이던 언어의 표본이다. 다음은 같은 시기에 일반 백성들 사이에 존재하던 영어의 표본이다. 로베르 드 브룬은 자신의 라틴어와 프랑스어 전거들을 두고 이렇게 말한다.\n\n\"그들이 써 두고 말해 둔 그대로 나는 그 모두를 내 잉글랜드어로 담았네, 내가 할 수 있는 대로 소박한 말로, 사람의 입에 가장 가벼운 말로. 오로지 소박한 사람들의 사랑을 위하여, 낯선 잉글랜드어를 알지 못하는 이들을 위하여.\"\n\n여기서 \"낯선 잉글랜드어\"란 앞의 표본에 나온 언어를 가리킨다.\n\n산문 로맨스가 나타나기 시작한 것은 13세기 말엽에 이르러서였다. 이 저작들은 대개, 실제로는 자신들이 정보를 끌어온 유일한 출처였던 것을 부인하고 깎아내리는 것으로 시작했다. 모든 로맨스가 실제 역사로 여겨졌던 만큼, 산문으로 쓴 이들의 편찬자들은 자신이 음유 시인들을 그저 베껴 적은 자에 불과하다고 밝혔다면 모든 신뢰를 잃었을 것이다. 도리어 그들은 으레 이렇게 진술했으니, 문제의 소재에 관한 인기 있는 시들에는 \"거짓말\"이 많이 들어 있는 까닭에, 자신은 이러저러한 기사의 실제이며 참된 역사를 본래의 라틴어나 그리스어에서, 혹은 고대 브리튼어나 아르모리카어 전거에서 번역하지 않을 수 없었다는 것이었으나, 그러한 전거들은 오직 그들 자신의 주장 속에만 존재했다.\n\n산문 로맨스의 문체를 보여 주는 표본은, 그것들 가운데 가장 유명하고 가장 나중에 나온 것의 하나인, 1485년에 나온 토머스 맬러리 경의 \"아서 왕의 죽음\"에서 다음과 같이 발췌한 데서 찾을 수 있다. 이 저작에서 이 책의 내용 가운데 많은 부분을 끌어왔으니, 우리의 서술을 현대 독자의 취향에 맞춘다는 우리의 계획에 어긋나지 않는다고 여겨지는 한도 안에서, 원래의 문체에 되도록 가깝게 따랐다.\n\n\"온 세상에 두루 널리 알려진 바, 일찍이 있었던 자들 가운데 아홉 명의 훌륭하고 가장 뛰어난 자가 있었다. 곧 세 명의 이교도, 세 명의 유대인, 그리고 세 명의 기독교인이 그들이다. 이교도로 말하자면, 그들은 그리스도의 강생 이전의 자들로, 첫째는 트로이아의 헥토르라 불렸고, 둘째는 알렉산드로스 대왕, 셋째는 로마 황제 율리우스 카이사르였으니, 그들에 관한 이야기는 잘 알려지고 전해지고 있다. 그리고 또한 우리 주님의 강생 이전의 자들이었던 세 명의 유대인으로 말하자면, 그 첫째는 이스라엘의 자손들을 약속의 땅으로 이끈 지도자 여호수아였고, 둘째는 예루살렘의 왕 다윗이었으며, 셋째는 유다 마카베오였으니, 이 셋에 관하여는 성서가 그들의 온갖 고귀한 이야기와 행적을 들려준다. 그리고 앞서 말한 강생 이후로, 온 세상에서 아홉 명의 가장 뛰어나고 훌륭한 자의 수에 들어가도록 세워지고 받아들여진 고귀한 기독교인들이 있었으니, 그 첫째가 고귀한 아서 왕으로, 그의 고귀한 행적을 나는 여기 이어지는 이 책에 쓰고자 한다. 둘째는 샤를마뉴, 곧 샤를 대제로, 그에 관한 이야기는 프랑스어로든 영어로든 여러 곳에 전해지며, 셋째이자 마지막은 부용의 고드프루아였다.\"\n\n————\n\n# THE MYTHICAL HISTORY OF ENGLAND\n\n저명한 시인 밀턴이 그의 「영국사」에서 보여 준 서술이, 이 장에서 우리가 주로 따르는 전거이다.\n\n가장 오래된 기록에 따르면, 거인이자 넵투누스의 아들이며 헤라클레스와 동시대를 살았던 알비온이 이 섬을 다스렸고, 그는 자신의 이름을 그 섬에 주었다. 헤라클레스가 서쪽으로 진군하는 것을 감히 가로막으려 들었다가, 알비온은 그의 손에 죽임을 당하고 말았다.\n\n또 다른 이야기에 따르면, 노아의 아들 야벳의 아들 히스티온에게는 네 아들 프란쿠스, 로마누스, 알레만누스, 브리토가 있었으며, 이들로부터 각각 프랑스인, 로마인, 게르만인, 브리튼인이 비롯되었다고 한다.\n\n밀턴은 이러한 이야기들과 그와 비슷한 다른 이야기들을 물리치고, 트로이아인 브루투스의 이야기에 더 큰 무게를 두는데, 그의 말에 따르면 이 이야기는 \"오래도록 이어진 조상의 계보, 빌려 오거나 꾸며 낸 것으로는 분명히 보이지 않는 법률과 위업으로 뒷받침되며, 이는 통상의 믿음 속에서 적지 않은 인상을 남겼고, 많은 이들이 옹호하며 이를 전적으로 부인하는 이는 거의 없다\"고 한다. 주된 전거는 몬머스의 제프리이며, 12세기에 쓰인 그의 역사서는 프랑스 맞은편 해안에서 건너온 브리튼의 역사서를 번역한 것이라 일컬어진다. 브르타뉴라는 이름으로 불리던 그 땅은, 픽트족과 스코트족의 침입에 떠밀려 제 나라를 떠나 때때로 그리로 이주해 온 브리튼 원주민들이 주로 살던 곳이었다. 이 전거에 따르면, 브루투스는 실비우스의 아들이고, 실비우스는 아스카니우스의 아들이며, 아스카니우스는 아이네아스의 아들이니, 아이네아스가 트로이아에서 도망쳐 이탈리아에 정착한 이야기는 「신들과 영웅들의 이야기」에 서술되어 있다.\n\n브루투스는 열다섯 살 되던 해에 아버지를 따라 사냥에 나섰다가, 불행히도 화살로 그를 죽이고 말았다. 이 일로 친족에게 추방당한 그는, 헬레노스가 트로이아 망명자들의 무리와 함께 자리를 잡은 그리스의 한 지역으로 피신했다. 그러나 헬레노스는 이제 죽고 없었고, 트로이아인의 후손들은 그 나라의 왕 판드라소스에게 억압을 받고 있었다. 그들 가운데서 따뜻하게 받아들여진 브루투스는 덕과 무예에서 크게 자라나, 그 땅의 모든 명사들로부터 또래의 누구보다도 높은 존경을 얻게 되었다. 그 결과 트로이아인들은 희망을 품기 시작했을 뿐만 아니라, 은밀히 그에게 자유로 이르는 길을 이끌어 달라고 설득하기에 이르렀다. 그들에게 용기를 북돋아 준 것은, 어머니가 트로이아인이었던 고귀한 그리스 청년 아사라코스의 도움 약속이었다. 그는 왕의 손에 부당한 일을 당한 적이 있었던 까닭에, 한층 기꺼이 트로이아 망명자들과 운명을 함께하였다.\n\n적당한 기회를 골라, 브루투스는 동포들과 함께 숲과 산으로 물러났으니, 그곳이야말로 항변을 하기에 가장 안전한 자리였다. 그러고는 판드라소스에게 이런 전갈을 보냈다. \"트로이아인들은 이국땅에서 종노릇하는 것이 제 조상들에게 합당치 않은 일이라 여겨 숲으로 물러났으며, 종살이하는 삶보다 차라리 야만의 삶을 택하였다. 만일 그것이 그의 뜻에 거슬린다면, 그의 허락을 얻어 다른 어느 나라로든 떠나가겠노라.\" 포로의 자식들에게서 그토록 대담한 전갈이 오리라고는 예상치 못했던 판드라소스는, 끌어모을 수 있는 만큼의 병력을 거느리고 그들을 추격하여, 아켈로오스 강가에서 그들과 맞닥뜨렸다. 그곳에서 브루투스가 우세를 점하여 왕을 사로잡았다. 그 결과 트로이아인들이 요구한 조건이 받아들여졌으니, 왕은 딸 이모겐을 브루투스에게 아내로 주고, 그들 모두가 그 땅을 떠날 수 있도록 선박과 돈과 알맞은 식량을 마련해 주었다.\n\n혼례가 치러지고, 사방에서 선박이 모이자, 트로이아인들은 무려 삼백스무 척에 이르는 함대를 이루어 바다로 나섰다. 사흘째 되는 날, 그들은 어떤 섬에 다다랐는데, 그곳은 주민이 없었으나 예전에 사람이 살았던 흔적이 보였으며, 그 폐허 가운데에는 디아나의 신전이 있었다. 브루투스는 그곳에서 여신의 제단에 제물을 바치며, 자신을 인도해 달라는 신탁을 다음과 같은 시구로 청하였다.\n\n\"그늘의 여신이며 사냥의 여신이여, 뜻대로 구르는 천구 위를 거니시고 깊은 바다를 누비시는 이여, 당신의 세 번째 영토인 이 대지를 굽어보시어 일러 주소서, 어느 땅, 어느 안식의 자리를 찾으라 명하시는지를, 제가 영원토록 당신을 섬길 수 있는 확고한 자리, 서원한 신전과 처녀들의 합창대로써 받들 그곳이 어디인지를.\"\n\n그러자 제단 앞에서 잠든 그에게, 디아나가 환영 속에서 이렇게 답하였다.\n\n\"브루투스여! 저 멀리 서쪽, 드넓은 대양 가운데, 갈리아의 영토 너머에 한 땅이 누워 있으니, 바다에 둘러싸인 그곳에는 옛적에 거인들이 살았으되, 이제는 비어 있어 네 백성에게 어울리노라. 그리로 뱃길을 돌리라. 거기서 너는 영구한 자리를 찾으리니, 그곳에서 네 아들들에게 또 하나의 트로이아가 일어서고, 네게서 왕들이 태어나, 그 두려운 위세가 온 세상을 떨게 하며 담대한 민족들을 정복하리라.\"\n\n이제 자신이 신의 인도를 받는다고 여긴 브루투스는 서쪽을 향해 뱃길을 재촉했고, 티레눔 해의 어느 곳에 이르러, 안테노르와 함께 이탈리아로 들어온 어떤 트로이아인들의 후손을 거기서 발견했는데, 그들의 우두머리는 코리네우스였다. 이들이 일행에 합류했고, 함선들은 항해를 이어 가 프랑스의 루아르 강 어귀에 다다랐다. 그곳에서 원정대는 정착할 뜻으로 상륙했으나, 주민들의 거친 공격을 받아 다시 바다로 나섰고, 지금은 데번셔라 불리는 브리튼 해안의 한 지역에 이르렀다. 그곳에서 브루투스는 자신이 항해의 약속된 종착지를 찾았음을 확신하여, 자신의 식민단을 상륙시키고 그 땅을 차지하였다.\n\n아직 브리튼이 아니라 알비온이라 불리던 그 섬은, 말하자면 황량하고 사람을 받아들이지 않는 땅이었으니, 지나친 힘과 폭정으로 동족을 멸한 거인족의 잔당만이 차지하고 있었다. 트로이아인들은 이들과 맞서 싸워 그들을 뿌리 뽑았는데, 특히 코리네우스는 그들을 상대로 한 위업으로 두드러진 명성을 떨쳤다. 콘월이라는 이름은 그에게서 비롯된 것이니, 그 지역이 그의 몫으로 떨어졌고, 또 거기에는 가장 거대한 거인들이 바위와 동굴 속에 숨어 살고 있었는데, 코리네우스가 그 땅에서 그들을 없애 버렸기 때문이다.\n\n브루투스는 자신의 수도를 세우고 그곳을 트로야노바(새 트로이아)라 불렀는데, 이것이 세월이 흘러 트리노반툼으로 바뀌었고, 지금의 런던이 되었다.[39] 그는 그 섬을 스물네 해 동안 다스리다가 세상을 떠나면서, 세 아들 로크린, 알바낙트, 캄베르를 남겼다. 로크린은 가운데 지역을, 캄베르는 서쪽을 차지했는데 그곳은 그의 이름을 따 캄브리아라 불렸으며, 알바낙트는 알바니아 곧 지금의 스코틀랜드를 차지했다. 로크린은 코리네우스의 딸 구엔돌렌과 혼인했으나, 게르마니아에서 포로로 끌려온 에스트릴디스라는 어여쁜 처녀를 보고 그녀에게 반하여, 그녀에게서 사브라라는 이름의 딸을 얻었다. 이 일은 코리네우스가 살아 있는 동안에는 비밀에 부쳐졌으나, 그가 죽은 뒤 로크린은 구엔돌렌과 이혼하고 에스트릴디스를 왕비로 삼았다. 구엔돌렌은 격분하여 콘월로 떠났는데, 그곳에는 외할아버지 코리네우스의 손에 자란 그녀의 아들 마단이 살고 있었다. 그녀는 아버지의 벗들과 신민들로 군대를 모아 남편의 군대에 싸움을 걸었고, 로크린은 죽임을 당했다. 구엔돌렌은 연적 에스트릴디스를 그 딸 사브라와 함께 강물에 던지게 했으니, 그 까닭에 그 강은 그때부터 그 처녀의 이름을 지니게 되었고, 오랜 세월이 흐르면서 지금은 사브리나, 곧 세번으로 바뀌었다. 밀턴은 강들에게 바치는 글에서 이를 다음과 같이 언급하였다.\n\n\"빠른 세번이여, 처녀의 죽음에 죄 있는 강이여.\"\n\n또한 그의 「코머스」에서는 이 이야기를 조금 다르게 다음과 같이 들려준다.\n\n\"여기서 멀지 않은 곳에 한 고운 님프가 있으니, 촉촉한 고삐로 매끄러운 세번 물줄기를 다스린다. 사브리나가 그녀의 이름이요, 순결한 처녀라. 옛적에 그녀는 로크린의 딸이었으니, 로크린은 제 아비 브루투스에게서 왕홀을 물려받았다. 죄 없는 이 처녀는, 격분한 계모 구엔돌렌의 미친 추격을 피해 달아나다가, 제 고운 결백함을 강물에 맡기었더라. 강물은 가로질러 흐르는 물길로 그녀의 도주를 멈추게 하니, 바닥에서 노닐던 물의 님프들이 진주빛 손목을 들어 그녀를 받아 들고는, 곧장 늙은 네레우스의 전당으로 데려갔다. 네레우스는 그녀의 슬픔을 가엾이 여겨 시든 머리를 들어 올리고, 제 딸들에게 맡겨 그녀를 목욕시키게 하니, 아스포델을 흩뿌린 넥타르의 대야에서였다. 그러고는 감각마다의 문과 입구를 통하여 암브로시아의 기름을 떨구니 마침내 그녀가 되살아나, 재빠른 불멸의 변신을 겪고는 강의 여신이 되었더라.\" 운운.\n\n이 모든 일이 언제 일어났느냐고 우리 독자들이 묻는다면, 우선 신화는 연대에 관해 그리 꼼꼼하지 않다고 답해야 하겠으며, 다음으로 브루투스가 아이네아스의 증손이었으니 그 일은 트로이아 전쟁으로부터 한 세기쯤 뒤, 곧 율리우스 카이사르가 이 섬을 침공하기 약 천백 년 전이었으리라고 답해야 하겠다. 이 긴 세월은 서로 전쟁하는 것이 주된 일이었던 군주들의 이름으로 채워져 있다. 그 가운데 이름이 어느 장소와 연관되어 남아 있거나 문학 속에 길이 보존된 몇몇을 우리는 언급하고자 한다.\n\n블라두드는 배스라는 도시를 세우고, 그 약효 있는 물을 미네르바에게 바쳤다. 그는 발명에 능한 사람이어서 마법의 기술을 부렸는데, 마침내 날아오를 날개를 자신에게 만들어 붙였다가 트리노반트에 있는 아폴론 신전 위로 떨어져, 스무 해의 치세 끝에 그렇게 죽고 말았다.\n\n다음으로 다스린 레이르는 레스터를 세우고 자신의 이름을 따 그렇게 불렀다. 그에게는 아들이 없고 오로지 세 딸만 있었다. 늙어서 그는 왕국을 딸들에게 나누어 주고 그들을 혼인시키기로 결심했다. 그러나 먼저, 누가 자기를 가장 사랑하는지를 시험하려고, 차례로 엄숙히 물어 그 대답으로 애정의 깊이를 가늠하기로 했다. 만딸 고너릴은 아버지의 약점을 잘 알고 있었으므로, 자신은 그를 \"제 영혼보다 더\" 사랑한다고 대답했다. \"네가 그토록 내 저무는 노년을 받든다 하니,\" 노인이 말했다. \"너와 네 남편에게 내 왕국의 삼분의 일을 주겠노라.\" 몇 마디 쉽게 내뱉은 말로 이런 큰 성공을 거둔 것은, 둘째 딸 리건에게 무어라 말해야 할지를 충분히 일러 주었다. 그리하여 그녀는 같은 물음에 자신은 \"이 세상 어느 것보다도 더\" 아버지를 사랑한다고 답하여, 언니와 똑같은 보상을 받았다. 그러나 가장 어리고 그때까지 가장 사랑받던 코르데일라는, 약간의 손쉬운 아첨에 따르는 보상과 솔직히 말하는 데 따를 손실을 눈앞에 두고도, 진실하고 덕 있는 대답이라는 굳은 뜻에서 흔들리지 않고 이렇게 답했다. \"아버님, 아버님을 향한 제 사랑은 제 도리가 명하는 그대로입니다. 이를 넘어선다고 내세우는 이들은 아첨하는 것입니다.\" 노인은 이 말을 듣고 서운하여 그 말을 거두기를 바라며 계속 캐물었으나, 그녀는 여전히 표현을 자제하여 진실보다 더 말하기보다는 차라리 덜 말하였다. 그러자 레이르는 격분하여 터뜨렸다. \"네가 네 언니들처럼 늙은 아비를 받들지 않았으니, 내 왕국에서든 내가 가진 그 밖의 무엇에서든 한 몫이라도 받으리라고는 생각지 마라.\" 그러고는 지체 없이 다른 두 딸을 혼인시켜, 고너릴은 올버니 공작에게, 리건은 콘월 공작에게 주고, 그 둘 사이에 왕국을 나누었으며, 오로지 기사 백 명만을 거느리고 만딸과 함께 거처하러 갔다. 그러나 얼마 지나지 않아 그의 수행원들이 너무 많고 무질서하다는 불평이 일어 서른 명으로 줄어들었다. 이 모욕에 분개한 노왕은 둘째 딸에게 몸을 의탁했으나, 그녀는 상처 입은 그의 자존심을 달래기는커녕 언니 편을 들어, 다섯 명을 넘는 수행원은 받아들이기를 거부했다. 그러자 그는 다시 만딸에게 돌아갔는데, 이번에는 한 명을 넘는 수행원과 함께라면 그를 받아들이려 하지 않았다. 그제야 코르데일라에 대한 기억이 그의 생각에 떠올라, 그는 그녀를 찾아 프랑스로 길을 떠났으니, 자신이 그토록 모질게 대한 이에게서 다정한 배려를 기대할 희망은 거의 없었으나, 그녀에게 자신이 베풀 수 있는 마지막 보답, 곧 자신의 불의를 고백하기 위함이었다. 코르데일라는 아버지가 다가오고 있으며 그가 처한 가련한 처지를 전해 듣고는, 참된 효녀의 눈물을 쏟았다. 그리고 자신의 눈이든 다른 이의 눈이든 그 비참한 처지에 놓인 아버지를 보게 하고 싶지 않아, 믿을 만한 종 하나를 보내 그를 맞이하게 하고, 은밀히 편안한 거처로 모셔 그 위엄에 걸맞은 격식을 갖추어 드리게 했다. 그런 뒤에 코르데일라는 자신의 남편인 왕과 함께 격식을 갖추어 아버지를 맞이하러 나갔으며, 정중한 영접을 받은 뒤, 왕은 아내 코르데일라가 군대를 거느리고 가서 그녀의 아버지를 다시 왕좌에 앉히도록 허락했다. 그들은 일을 잘 이루어 사악한 언니들과 그 배우자들을 굴복시켰고, 레이르는 왕관을 되찾아 삼 년 동안 다스렸다. 코르데일라가 그를 이어 다섯 해 동안 다스렸으나, 그 뒤로 언니들의 아들들이 그녀에게 반란을 일으켰고, 그녀는 왕관과 목숨을 모두 잃고 말았다.\n\n셰익스피어는 이 이야기를 자신의 비극 「리어 왕」의 소재로 골라, 몇 가지 점에서 그 세부를 바꾸었다. 레이르의 광기와, 아버지를 복위시키려던 코르데일라의 시도가 실패로 끝나는 것이 주된 변형이며, 이름에서의 차이 또한 눈에 띌 것이다. 우리의 서술은 밀턴의 「영국사」에서 끌어온 것이니, 이로써 독자는 레이르의 이야기가 영국 문학의 공인된 두 거장에 의해 이야기되는 두드러진 영예를 누렸음을 알아차릴 것이다.\n\n# FERREX AND PORREX\n\n페렉스와 포렉스는 형제로서, 레이르의 뒤를 이어 왕국을 차지했다. 그들은 패권을 두고 다투었고, 포렉스가 형을 내쫓았으나, 형은 프랑크족의 왕 수아르에게서 도움을 얻어 돌아와 포렉스에게 전쟁을 걸었다. 페렉스는 전투에서 죽임을 당하고 그의 군대는 흩어졌다. 그들의 어머니가 자신이 가장 아끼던 아들의 죽음을 전해 듣고는 크나큰 분노에 사로잡혀, 살아남은 아들에게 사무치는 증오를 품게 되었다. 그리하여 그녀는 그가 잠든 틈을 노려 그에게 달려들어, 시녀들의 도움을 받아 그를 갈가리 찢어 버렸다. 이 끔찍한 이야기는, 영어로 쓰인 최초의 비극에 줄거리를 제공했다는 사실이 아니었다면 옮길 만한 값어치가 없었을 것이다. 그 작품은 「고르보두크」라는 제목이었으나, 재판에서는 「페렉스와 포렉스」가 되었으며, 훗날 도싯 백작이 된 토머스 색빌과 법정 변호사 토머스 노턴의 작품이었다. 그것이 쓰인 해는 1561년이었다.\n\n# DUNWALLO MOLMUTIUS\n\n이것이 그다음으로 주목할 만한 이름이다. 몰무티우스는 몰무티네 법을 제정했는데, 이 법은 신전과 도시와 그리로 이어지는 길에 성역의 특권을 부여하고, 쟁기에도 같은 보호를 베풀어, 들판의 노동에까지 종교적 보장을 미치게 했다. 셰익스피어는 「심벨린」 제3막 제1장에서 그를 다음과 같이 언급한다.\n\n\". . . 몰무티우스가 우리의 법을 만들었으니, 그는 황금 왕관을 제 이마에 두르고 스스로를 왕이라 일컬은 브리튼 최초의 사람이었다.\"\n\n# BRENNUS AND BELINUS,\n\n몰무티우스의 아들들인 브렌누스와 벨리누스가 그의 뒤를 이었다. 그들은 다투었고, 브렌누스는 섬에서 쫓겨나 갈리아로 피신했다. 그곳에서 그는 알로브로게스족의 왕에게 큰 호의를 입어, 왕은 그에게 자신의 딸을 아내로 주고 그를 왕좌의 동반자로 삼았다. 브렌누스는 로마의 역사가들이 카밀루스 시대에 로마를 함락시킨 저 유명한 갈리아인 지도자에게 붙인 이름이다. 몬머스의 제프리는 그 정복의 영광이 알로브로게스족의 왕이 된 뒤의 이 브리튼 왕에게 돌아간다고 주장한다.\n\n벨리누스와 브렌누스의 뒤로는 이렇다 할 만하지 않은 몇몇 왕이 다스렸고, 그다음에 엘리두레가 나왔다. 그의 형 아르트갈로가 왕으로 있을 때, 그는 자신의 권세 있는 귀족들의 큰 노여움을 샀고, 그들이 들고일어나 그를 폐위시키고 엘리두레를 왕좌에 올렸다. 아르트갈로는 도망쳐, 자신을 복위시키고자 이웃 왕국들에서 도움을 구하려 했으나 아무 데서도 얻지 못했다. 엘리두레는 번영을 누리며 지혜롭게 다스렸다. 왕국을 차지한 지 다섯 해째 되던 어느 날, 그는 사냥을 하다가 숲에서 폐위된 형 아르트갈로를 만났다. 오랜 방랑 끝에, 자신이 처한 가난을 더는 견딜 수 없게 된 형은, 예전에 자신의 벗이었던 이들을 찾아갈 작정으로 오로지 열 명의 부하만을 거느리고 브리튼으로 돌아와 있었던 것이다. 곤경에 처한 형을 보자 엘리두레는 모든 원한을 잊고 그에게 달려가 그를 껴안았다. 그는 아르트갈로를 자기 집으로 데려가 궁궐 안에 숨겨 두었다. 그런 뒤에 그는 병든 척하며 귀족들을 곁으로 불러, 일부는 설득으로 일부는 강압으로, 자신이 왕위를 내려놓고 형을 다시 왕좌에 앉히는 데 동의하도록 이끌었다. 이 합의가 확정되자, 엘리두레는 자기 머리에서 왕관을 벗어 형의 머리에 씌웠다. 아르트갈로는 그 뒤로 열 해 동안 훌륭하고 지혜롭게 다스리며, 만인에게 엄정한 정의를 베풀었다.\n\n그가 죽으며 왕국을 아들들에게 남기니, 그들은 갖가지 운명 속에서 다스렸으나 오래 살지 못했고 자손을 남기지 못했다. 그리하여 엘리두레가 다시 왕좌에 올라, 정의롭고 덕 있는 행실로 생애를 마쳤으며, 신민들의 사랑과 흠모로 인하여 경건한 자라는 이름을 얻었다.\n\n워즈워스는 아테갈과 엘리두레의 이야기를 한 편의 시의 소재로 삼았는데, 그것은 「애정에 바탕을 둔 시편」의 제2번이다.\n\n엘리두레의 뒤로 연대기는 여러 왕의 이름을 들지만, 특별히 주목할 만한 이는 없다가, 마침내 루드에 이른다. 그는 자신의 수도 트리노반트를 크게 넓히고 그 둘레에 성벽을 둘렀다. 그는 도시의 이름을 바꾸어 자신의 이름을 붙였으니, 그때부터 그곳은 루드의 도시라 불렸고, 훗날 런던이 되었다. 루드는 자신의 이름을 따 루드게이트라 불린 도시의 성문 곁에 묻혔다. 그에게는 두 아들이 있었으나, 아버지가 죽을 무렵 그들은 통치의 짐을 떠맡기에는 아직 나이가 차지 않았으므로, 그들의 숙부 카스왈라운, 곧 카시벨라우누스가 왕위를 이었다. 그는 용맹하고 위풍당당한 군주여서, 그의 명성이 먼 나라들에까지 이르렀다.\n\n이 무렵에 (로마의 역사서에 나오듯이) 갈리아를 굴복시킨 율리우스 카이사르가 브리튼 맞은편 해안에 이르는 일이 있었다. 그리고 이 섬마저 자신의 정복지에 더하기로 결심한 그는, 함선을 마련하여 군대를 바다 건너 템스 강 어귀로 실어 날랐다. 여기서 그는 자신의 전군을 거느린 카시벨라운과 맞닥뜨려 전투가 벌어졌는데, 그 가운데 카시벨라운의 동생 네니우스가 카이사르와 일대일로 맞붙었다. 사납게 주고받은 몇 차례의 가격 끝에, 카이사르의 칼이 네니우스의 방패에 너무도 단단히 박혀 빠지지 않게 되었고, 군대가 끼어들어 둘이 갈라지게 되자 네니우스는 이 전리품을 차지하게 되었다. 마침내 하루의 대부분이 지났을 무렵, 브리튼인들이 어찌나 거세게 밀려들었던지 카이사르는 자신의 진지와 함대로 물러날 수밖에 없었다. 그리고 그때 더는 전쟁을 이어 가는 것이 소용없음을 깨닫고 갈리아로 돌아갔다.\n\n셰익스피어는 「심벨린」에서 카시벨라우누스를 다음과 같이 언급한다.\n\n\"이름 높은 카시벨란은, 한때 (오, 변덕스러운 운명이여!) 카이사르의 칼을 제압할 뻔하여, 루드의 도시를 기쁨의 횃불로 환히 밝히고 브리튼인들로 하여금 용기로 으스대게 하였다.\"\n\n# KYMBELINUS, OR CYMBELINE\n\n카이사르는 두 번째로 이 섬을 침공했을 때 더 큰 행운을 얻어, 브리튼인들에게 조공을 바치도록 강요했다. 왕의 조카 심벨린은 그 조약이 충실히 이행되리라는 볼모로 로마인들에게 넘겨졌고, 카이사르에게 이끌려 로마로 가서 그곳에서 로마의 기예와 교양을 익히며 자랐다. 훗날 제 나라로 돌아와 왕좌에 오른 그는 로마인들에게 호의를 품었고, 치세 내내 그들과 평화를 유지했다. 셰익스피어의 희곡 「심벨린」에 등장하는 그의 아들들 기데리우스와 아르비라구스가 아버지의 뒤를 이었는데, 그들은 로마인들에게 조공 바치기를 거부하여 또 한 번의 침공을 불러왔다. 기데리우스는 죽임을 당했으나, 아르비라구스는 훗날 로마인들과 화친을 맺고 여러 해 동안 번영을 누리며 다스렸다.\n\n다음으로 주목할 만한 사건은, 로마 장군 막시무스와 웨일스의 미니아도크 곧 덴비 땅의 영주 코난에 의한 아르모리카의 정복과 식민이다. 그 나라의 이름은 브르타뉴, 곧 작은 브리튼으로 바뀌었으며, 브리튼 식민자들이 어찌나 완전히 그곳을 차지했던지 그 언어가 웨일스에서 쓰이는 말과 비슷해졌고, 오늘날까지도 두 나라의 농민들이 제 고유의 언어로 말할 때 서로 알아들을 수 있다고 한다.\n\n로마인들은 마침내 이 섬에 자리를 잡는 데 성공했으며, 여러 세대가 흐르는 동안 원주민들과 뒤섞여 두 종족 사이에 아무런 구별도 남지 않게 되었다. 마침내 로마 군대가 브리튼에서 철수했을 때, 그 떠남은 주민들에게 아쉬운 일이었으니, 끊임없이 나라를 괴롭히던 야만의 부족들, 곧 스코트족과 픽트족과 노르웨이인들에 맞설 보호 없이 그들을 남겨 두었기 때문이다. 아서 왕의 시대가 시작된 것은 바로 이러한 형편에서였다.\n\n거인 알비온이 헤라클레스와 겪은 모험은 스펜서의 「선녀 여왕」 제4권 제11편에서 언급되어 있다.\n\n\"알비온은 넵투누스의 아들이었으니, 그의 크나큰 힘을 입증하고자 제 알비온을 떠나 마른 발로 건너가 이제는 프랑스라 불리는 옛 갈리아로 들어가 헤라클레스와 싸우려 하였더라. 헤라클레스는 견줄 데 없는 위력으로 온 세상을 정복하려 나섰던 터라, 거기서 그의 필멸의 육신은 큰 불운으로 죽임을 당하였더라.\" ————\n\n멀린은 필멸의 아버지에게서 태어난 것이 아니라, 인쿠부스의 아들이었다. 인쿠부스란 절대적으로 사악하지는 않으나 결코 선하지도 않은 부류의 존재로, 대기의 영역에 거하는 자들이었다. 멀린의 어머니는 정숙한 젊은 여인이었는데, 아들이 태어나자 그를 한 사제에게 맡겼고, 사제는 서둘러 그를 세례반으로 데려갔다. 그리하여 멀린은 아버지의 운명을 함께하는 일을 면했으나, 그래도 이 세상의 것이 아닌 출생의 흔적을 여럿 지니고 있었다.\n\n이 무렵 브리튼에서는 보르티게른이 다스리고 있었다. 그는 찬탈자로서, 자기 군주였던 모이네스를 죽음에 이르게 하고, 선왕의 두 형제, 곧 우테르와 펜드래건이라는 자들을 추방한 인물이었다. 보르티게른은 왕국의 정당한 후계자들이 돌아오지 않을까 늘 두려워하며 살았던 까닭에, 방어를 위해 견고한 탑을 세우기 시작했다. 그런데 이 건축물은 일꾼들이 일정한 높이까지 쌓아 올리면, 뚜렷한 까닭도 없이 세 번이나 땅으로 무너져 내렸다. 왕은 이 기이한 사건을 두고 점성술사들에게 자문을 구했고, 그들에게서 필멸의 아버지 없이 태어난 아이의 피로 토대의 주춧돌을 적셔야 한다는 답을 들었다.\n\n보르티게른은 그러한 아이를 찾아 왕국 곳곳으로 사자들을 보냈고, 그들은 우연히 멀린을 발견했다. 멀린의 혈통이 바로 그가 찾던 그 사람임을 가리키는 듯했다. 그들은 그를 왕에게 데려갔다. 그러나 멀린은 비록 어렸음에도, 그런 방법으로 건물을 구하려는 시도가 얼마나 터무니없는지를 왕에게 설명했다. 그는 탑이 불안정한 진짜 원인이 탑 아래 거대한 용 두 마리의 굴이 있는 데 있으며, 그것들이 싸울 때마다 그 위의 대지가 흔들리는 것이라고 일러 주었다. 왕은 일꾼들에게 탑 아래를 파라고 명했고, 그들이 그렇게 하자 거대한 뱀 두 마리가 모습을 드러냈다. 하나는 젖빛처럼 희었고, 다른 하나는 불처럼 붉었다. 군중은 놀라움에 차서 지켜보았고, 마침내 뱀들이 굴에서 천천히 솟아올라 그 거대한 몸을 펼치며 싸움을 시작하자, 멀린만을 제외하고는 모두가 공포에 질려 달아났다. 멀린은 그 곁에 서서 손뼉을 치며 싸움을 응원했다. 붉은 용은 죽임을 당했고, 흰 용은 바위 틈새로 미끄러져 들어가 사라졌다.\n\n이 짐승들은, 멀린이 훗날 설명한 바에 따르면, 정당한 왕자들인 우테르와 펜드래건의 침공을 상징하는 것이었다. 그 두 사람은 얼마 지나지 않아 대군을 이끌고 상륙했다. 보르티게른은 패배했고, 그가 그토록 애써 세운 성에서 산 채로 불태워졌다. 보르티게른이 죽은 뒤 펜드래건이 왕좌에 올랐다. 멀린은 그의 으뜸가는 조언자가 되었으며, 종종 자신의 마법으로 왕을 도왔다.\n\n\"멀린은 온갖 마법의 영역을 모두 알고 있어, 왕을 위해 항구와 배와 궁전을 지어 주었네.\" — 「비비안」\n\n다른 여러 재능 가운데서도 멀린은 자기가 원하는 어떤 모습으로든 변신할 수 있는 힘을 지니고 있었다. 어느 때에는 난쟁이로 나타났고, 다른 때에는 처녀로, 시동으로, 심지어 그레이하운드나 수사슴으로도 나타났다. 그는 이 능력을 종종 왕을 섬기는 데 썼고, 때로는 궁정과 군주를 즐겁게 하는 데에도 썼다.\n\n멀린은 펜드래건과 우테르, 그리고 아서의 치세를 통틀어 총애받는 조언자였으나, 마침내 사람들의 시야에서 사라져 다시는 인간들 사이에서 발견되지 않았다. 이는 그의 연인이었던 요정 비비안의 배신 때문이었으며, 그 일은 다음과 같이 일어났다.\n\n멀린은 호수의 여인 아름다운 비비안에게 반한 나머지, 어리석게도 자기 마법의 여러 중요한 비밀들을 그녀에게 전해 주었다. 이는 그가 거역할 수 없는 운명에 떠밀린 탓이었으나, 그 자신도 그 운명을 동시에 충분히 알고 있었다. 그러나 그 여인은, 끝이 없어 보일 만큼 헌신적인 그의 사랑에도 만족하지 못하고, 로맨스가 우리에게 전하는 바에 따르면, 어떻게 하면 \"그를 영영 붙들어 둘\" 수 있을지 \"궁리하기\" 시작했다. 그러던 어느 날 그녀는 그에게 이렇게 말을 건넸다. \"기사님, 저는 우리가 아름답고 알맞은 장소 하나를 마련했으면 합니다. 기예와 솜씨로 지어 결코 허물어지지 않게 하고, 당신과 제가 그 안에서 기쁨과 위안을 누렸으면 합니다.\" \"여인이여,\" 멀린이 말했다. \"이 모든 것을 내가 해 드리리다.\" \"기사님,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저는 당신이 그것을 하시는 걸 바라지 않습니다. 다만 제게 가르쳐 주시면, 제가 그것을 하겠습니다. 그러면 더욱 제 마음에 들 것입니다.\" \"그대에게 그리 하도록 허락하겠소.\" 멀린이 말했다. 그리하여 그는 궁리하기 시작했고, 처녀는 그것을 모두 글로 적었다. 그가 모든 것을 궁리해 내자, 처녀는 더없이 큰 기쁨에 차서, 일찍이 보인 적 없는 더한 사랑의 모습을 그에게 드러냈다. 그리고 두 사람은 오랫동안 함께 머물렀다. 마침내 어느 날, 그들이 손을 맞잡고 브레셀리앙드 숲을 거닐던 차에, 꽃이 흐드러지게 핀 산사나무 한 그루를 발견했다. 그들은 그 산사나무 그늘 아래 푸른 풀밭에 앉았고, 멀린은 처녀의 무릎에 머리를 누이고 잠이 들었다. 그러자 처녀가 일어나 자기 머릿수건으로 그 나무와 멀린을 둘러 고리를 만들고는, 멀린 자신이 그녀에게 가르쳐 준 그 주문을 외기 시작했다. 아홉 번 고리를 만들고 아홉 번 주문을 걸었으며, 그런 다음 그의 곁으로 가서 앉아 그의 머리를 다시 자기 무릎 위에 올려놓았다.\n\n\"그리고 잠이 멀린에게 내렸으니, 죽음과도 같이 깊었다. 그녀의 손가락은 입술에 닿았고, 비비안이 일어나 갈색 머리 두른 머리에서 머릿수건을 벗어 손에 쥐고는 그것을 흔들었다. 꽃 핀 산사나무와 잠든 연인 위로. 아홉 번 그녀는 펄럭이는 머릿수건을 둘러 흔들었고, 작은 마법의 땅 한 자락을 만들어 냈다.\" — 매슈 아널드\n\n그가 깨어나 주위를 둘러보았을 때, 그는 세상에서 가장 견고한 탑 안에 갇혀 아름다운 침상 위에 누워 있는 듯했다. 그러자 그가 그 여인에게 말했다. \"여인이여, 그대가 나와 함께 머물지 않는 한, 그대는 나를 속인 것이오. 그대 말고는 어느 누구도 이 탑을 허물 힘이 없으니 말이오.\" 그러자 그녀는 자주 그곳에 있겠노라 약속했고, 그 점에서는 그와 맺은 언약을 지켰다. 그리고 멀린은 자기 연인 비비안이 가둔 그 탑에서 결코 나오지 못했으나, 그녀는 마음 내킬 때마다 드나들었다.\n\n이 일이 있은 뒤로 멀린이 비비안 말고 다른 어떤 필멸자와 이야기를 나누었다고 알려진 적은 단 한 번을 제외하고는 없었다. 아서는 한동안 궁정에서 그가 보이지 않자, 여러 기사를 보내 그를 찾게 했는데, 그 가운데 가웨인 경도 있었다. 가웨인은 이 탐색에 나섰다가 매우 불쾌한 모험을 겪었다. 길에서 한 처녀를 우연히 지나치며 그녀에게 인사하기를 소홀히 했더니, 그녀가 그 무례함에 앙갚음하여 그를 흉측한 난쟁이로 변신시켰던 것이다. 그가 브레셀리앙드 숲을 지나며 제 불운을 소리 높여 한탄하고 있을 때, 문득 오른편에서 누군가 신음하는 목소리가 들렸다. 그쪽을 바라보니, 공기처럼 보이며 뚫고 지날 수 없는 일종의 연기 외에는 아무것도 보이지 않았다. 그러자 멀린이 그 연기 속에서 그에게 말을 건네며, 자기가 어떤 불운으로 그곳에 갇히게 되었는지 일러 주었다. \"아, 기사여!\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"그대는 다시는 나를 보지 못할 것이오. 그것이 나를 슬프게 하나, 나로서는 어찌할 도리가 없소. 나는 다시는 그대에게도, 내 연인 말고 다른 어느 누구에게도 말하지 못할 것이오. 다만 그대는 어서 아서 왕에게로 가서, 내 뜻을 전하여 지체 없이 성배(聖杯)의 탐색에 나서도록 명하라 하시오. 이 탐색을 이루어 낼 운명을 지닌 기사는 이미 태어났고, 왕의 손에서 기사 서임을 받았소.\" 그러고 나서 그는 변신한 가웨인을 위로하며, 그가 곧 마법에서 풀려나리라고 장담했다. 또한 그는 가웨인이 돌아가는 길에 웨일스의 카르두엘에서 왕을 만나게 될 것이며, 같은 탐색에 나섰던 다른 모든 기사들도 그와 같은 날 그곳에 이르리라고 예언했다. 그리고 이 모든 일은 멀린이 말한 그대로 이루어졌다.\n\n멀린은 기사도 이야기에 자주 등장하지만, 그것은 주로 큰 사건이 일어날 때, 그리고 그가 죽거나 마법으로 사라진 뒤의 시기에 한해서다. 이탈리아의 낭만 서사시와 스펜서의 작품에서 멀린은 주로 마법 기예가로 그려진다. 스펜서는 그를 아서 왕자의 뚫리지 않는 방패와 그 밖의 갑옷을 만든 장인으로(「요정 여왕」 제1권 제7곡), 또 어느 처녀가 자기 연인의 모습을 들여다본 거울의 제작자로 그린다. 「사랑에 빠진 오를란도」에 나오는 사랑의 샘은 그의 솜씨로 묘사되며, 「아리오스토」의 시에서는 멀린의 지휘 아래 악마들이 단 하룻밤 만에 완성했다는, 예언적 그림들로 장식된 어느 홀의 이야기가 전해진다.\n\n다음 전설은 스펜서의 「요정 여왕」 제3권 제3곡에서 가져온 것이다.\n\n카이르메르딘, 곧 카이르마던(웨일스 소재), 멀린의 탑, 그리고 갇힌 마귀들.\n\n\"곧바로 두 사람은 낯설고 허름한 옷차림으로 제 모습을 감추어, 누구도 알아보지 못하게 하고, 이름이 바뀌어 이제는 카이르메르딘이라 불리는 마리두눔으로 길을 떠났다. 그곳에서 현명한 멀린은 (사람들 말로는) 일찍이 거처를 마련하곤 했다는데, 땅속 깊이 낮 빛이 닿지 않는 깊은 굴속에서였다. 살아 있는 어느 누구에게도 발견되지 않으려 함이었으니, 그가 제 정령들에 둘러싸여 의논을 나눌 적에 말이다.\n\n\"그리고 그대가 언젠가 그 길로 여행할 일이 생기거든, 가서 그 무시무시한 곳을 보라. 그것은 (사람들 말로는) 흉측하게 텅 빈 동굴로, 디네보르의 숲 우거진 언덕들 사이로 급히 쏟아져 내리는 빠른 배리강에서 얼마 안 떨어진 바위 아래 자리하고 있다. 허나, 내 그대에게 이르노니, 어떤 일이 있어도 그 흉흉한 거처 안으로 들어서지는 말라. 잔혹한 마귀들이 그대를 불시에 집어삼킬까 두려우니.\n\n\"다만 높이 우뚝 서서 귀를 낮게 기울이라. 그러면 그곳에서 쇠사슬과 놋쇠 가마솥이 부딪치는 무시무시한 소리가 우르릉 들려오리니, 오랜 세월 고통에 시달리는 수천의 정령들이 그것들을 내던지는 소리라, 그대의 연약한 머릿속을 어지럽힐 것이다. 또 이따금 큰 신음과 비통한 비명이 들리리니, 너무도 거대한 노역과 고역이 그들을 짓누를 때이며, 또 이따금 요란한 타격음과 쟁쟁 울리는 소리가 저 깊은 바위 밑에서 더없이 끔찍하게 되울려 나온다.\n\n\"그 까닭을 두고 어떤 이들은 이렇게 말한다. 멀린이 죽기 얼마 전, 그는 카이르메르딘 둘레에 놋쇠 벽을 빙 둘러 쌓아 올리려 마음먹었고, 그 일을 이 정령들에게 맡겨 완성토록 명했다. 그 일을 하는 동안, 그가 오래도록 사랑했던 호수의 여인이 그를 급히 불러들였고, 그리하여 그는 일꾼들을 떠나도록 할 수밖에 없었으되, 그들에게 자기가 돌아올 때까지 노역을 늦추지 말라 묶어 두었다.\n\n\"그러는 사이, 그 거짓된 여인의 술책에 빠져 그는 불시에 사로잡혀 관 아래 묻혔고,[40] 다시는 제 일로 돌아오지 못했다. 그럼에도 저 마귀들은 그 일을 멈추지 못하니, 그의 명령을 그토록 크게 두려워하는 까닭이다. 그리하여 그들은 밤낮으로 애쓰며 고역에 시달리는데, 저 놋쇠 벽을 다 쌓아 올릴 때까지 그러하리라. 멀린은 그 전에도 그 후에도 살았던 어느 누구보다도 마법에 더 깊은 통찰을 지니고 있었기 때문이다.\"\n\n————\n\n햐 아서 왕에 대한 우리의 이야기는, 역사적 증거에 근거한다고 여겨지는 그의 생애에 관한 세세한 사실들을 먼저 제시하는 것으로 시작할 것이다. 그런 다음 그에 관한 전설들, 곧 브리튼 문학의 가장 이른 시기를 이루는 그 전설들을 기록하는 것으로 나아갈 것이다.\n\n아서는 실루레스라고 불리던 브리튼족 부족의 군주였는데, 그 부족의 영토는 남웨일스였으며, 그는 펜드래곤이라고 불린 우서의 아들이었다. 펜드래곤이란 브리튼의 수많은 왕들 위에 군림하는 선출직 군주에게 부여된 칭호였다. 그는 서기 500년경에 무인으로서의 경력을 시작한 것으로 보이며, 그로부터 약 10년 뒤에 펜드래곤의 지위에 올랐다. 그는 색슨족을 상대로 열두 번의 승리를 거두었다고 전한다. 그 가운데 가장 중요한 것이 바돈 전투였는데, 어떤 이들은 그곳을 바스로, 또 어떤 이들은 버크셔로 추정한다. 이것이 색슨족과 벌인 그의 마지막 전투였으며, 그들의 진격을 어찌나 효과적으로 막아 냈던지 아서는 더 이상 그들에게 시달리지 않았고, 20년 뒤 그의 조카 모드레드가 반란을 일으킬 때까지 평화롭게 다스렸다. 그 반란은 542년에 콘월의 캄란에서 벌어진 치명적인 전투로 이어졌다. 모드레드는 죽임을 당했고, 아서는 치명상을 입은 채 바닷길로 글래스턴베리에 옮겨졌으며, 그곳에서 숨을 거두고 묻혔다. 전설은 그가 매장된 장소가 그 수도원 안에 있다는 기억을 보존해 왔는데, 이는 기랄두스 캄브렌시스가 우리에게 전해 주는 바와 같다. 그는 1150년경 헨리 2세의 명령으로 무덤이 열렸을 때 그 자리에 있었으며, 그 군주의 유골과 검을 보았고, 그의 묘비에 박혀 있던 납 십자가도 보았는데, 거기에는 투박한 로마 문자로 \"여기 저 유명한 아서 왕이 아발로니아섬에 묻혀 있노라\"라는 글귀가 새겨져 있었다. 이 이야기는 워턴에 의해 우아하게 운문으로 옮겨진 바 있다. 브리튼족 사이에서는 오랫동안 한 가지 통속적인 전설적 믿음이 품어져 왔으니, 곧 아서가 죽은 것이 아니라 그의 상처를 치유받기 위해 요정의 나라로 옮겨졌으며, 다시 나타나 자기 동족의 원수를 갚고 그들에게 브리튼의 주권을 되찾아 주리라는 것이었다. 워턴의 \"송시\"에서는 한 음유시인이 헨리 왕에게 아서의 죽음에 관한 전설을 들려주며 다음과 같은 시구로 끝맺는다.\n\n\"그러나 이교도 적이 운명을 무기 삼아 저 강력한 일격을 가한 것은 헛된 일이었으니, 그가 쓰러질 때, 요정의 여왕이 은밀하게 아무도 모르게, 기절한 영웅 위에 신들의 음식 같은 푸른 망토를 던지고, 자신의 정령들에게 그를 멀리 데려가라 명하니, 멀린의 마노 굴대를 단 수레에 태워, 깊은 바다의 한가운데 멀리 자리한 그녀의 푸른 섬, 그 윤기 도는 비탈로 향하였노라. 그의 상처 위에 그녀는 이슬을 뿌렸으니, 아라비아에서 자라난 꽃들에서 거둔 이슬이었노라. . . . . . . . 그곳에서 그는 강대한 왕으로 군림하며, 그곳에서 브리튼으로 돌아오리라, 내가 예언의 두루마리를 옳게 읽었다면, 승리의 펼쳐진 깃털 위에 실려, 그의 오랜 왕홀을 되찾고, 그의 기사들의 원탁을 복원하며, 옛적의 마상 시합을 다시 펼치리라.\"\n\n이 이야기가 끝난 뒤 또 다른 음유시인이 나서서 다른 이야기를 읊었다.\n\n\"아서가 그 도도한 머리를 숙였을 때, 푸른 옷을 두른 어떤 공주도 멀린의 강력한 주문으로 그를 낚아채 황금빛 축복의 숲에 살게 하지 않았노라. 다만 그가 쓰러졌을 때, 날개 단 듯 빠르게 그의 용사들이, 젖빛처럼 흰 군마에 태워, 전투의 폭풍 속에서 그를 들어 올려, 아름다운 아발론 골짜기에 자리한 요셉의 탑 솟은 성소로 옮겼노라.[41] 그곳에서, 영창하는 기도와 맑은 촛불들의 길게 타오르는 불빛 속에서, 제의를 걸친 신부들이 상여를 맞이하였노라. 어슴푸레한 회랑을 지나, 군사적 비탄의 엄숙한 행렬을 이루어, 그들은 그 수령을 인도하였고, 제단의 장엄한 경계 앞, 거룩한 땅속 깊이 그를 안장하였노라.\"\n\n아서의 존재 자체가 어떤 이들에 의해 부정되어 왔다는 사실을 숨겨서는 안 될 것이다. 밀턴은 그에 대하여 이렇게 말한다. \"아서로 말하자면, 참된 역사에서보다 노래와 로맨스에서 더 이름난 인물이거니와, 그가 누구였는지, 그리고 과연 그러한 인물이 브리튼을 다스린 적이 있었는지는 지금까지도 의심받아 왔으며, 충분한 이유로 다시금 의심받을 만하다.\" 그러나 근대의 비평가들은 이러한 이름의 군주가 있었음을 인정하며, 웨일스 음유시인들의 글에 그가 자주 언급된다는 점에서 그 증거를 찾는다. 그렇지만 웨일스 학자이자 골동품 연구가인 오언 씨에 따르면, 로맨스의 아서는 신화적인 인물이다. 그는 이렇게 말한다. \"아서란 곧 큰곰자리이니, 그 이름이 글자 그대로 의미하는 바이다(아르크토스, 아르크투루스). 그리고 어쩌면 이 별자리가 극에 너무 가까이 있어 좁은 공간 안에서 눈에 띄게 원을 그리는 까닭에, 저 유명한 원탁의 기원이 된 것인지도 모른다.\"\n\n# KING ARTHUR\n\n브리튼의 왕 콘스탄스에게는 세 아들이 있었으니, 모이네스와, 달리 우서라고도 불린 암브로시우스와, 펜드래곤이었다. 모이네스는 왕위에 오른 지 얼마 지나지 않아 자신의 가신장 보티건의 배신으로 색슨족에게 패하였고, 불운을 통해 인기를 잃게 되자 신하들의 손에 죽임을 당했으며, 반역자 보티건이 그 자리에 선택되었다.\n\n보티건은 그 뒤 곧 모이네스의 살아남은 두 형제인 우서와 펜드래곤에게 큰 전투에서 패하였고, 펜드래곤이 왕위에 올랐다.\n\n이 군주는 멀린의 지혜를 크게 신뢰하여 그를 자신의 으뜸가는 조언자로 삼았다. 이 무렵 색슨족과 브리튼족 사이에 무서운 전쟁이 일어났다. 멀린은 두 왕족 형제에게 서로 충실할 것을 맹세하게 하였으나, 그 가운데 하나가 첫 전투에서 쓰러지리라고 예언하였다. 색슨족은 패주하였고, 펜드래곤은 죽임을 당하여, 우서가 그 뒤를 이었는데, 그는 이제 자신의 이름에 더하여 펜드래곤이라는 칭호를 함께 취하였다.\n\n멀린은 여전히 총애받는 조언자로 남아 있었다. 우서의 요청에 따라 그는 마법의 기술로 아일랜드에서 거대한 돌들을 옮겨와 펜드래곤의 무덤을 짓게 하였다. 이 돌들이 바로 지금 솔즈베리 평원에 있는 스톤헨지라고 불리는 기념물을 이룬다.\n\n멀린은 이어 칼라일로 가서 원탁을 마련하였고, 그 둘레에 나라의 대귀족들을 한자리에 모아 앉혔다. 이 고귀한 기사단에 받아들여진 동료들은 맹세로써 다음을 지킬 의무가 있었다. 곧 자기 목숨을 걸고서라도 서로를 돕고, 가장 위험한 모험에 홀로 나서며, 필요할 때에는 수도사처럼 고독한 삶을 살고, 처음 부름이 있으면 곧장 무장하여 달려가며, 밤이 끼어들어 싸우는 자들을 갈라놓는 경우가 아니라면 적을 물리치기 전에는 결코 전투에서 물러나지 않는 것이었다.\n\n이 제도가 세워진 지 얼마 지나지 않아, 왕은 모든 영주들을 큰 축제의 거행에 초대하였는데, 그는 이 축제를 칼라일에서 해마다 열고자 하였다.\n\n기사들이 자기 부인들을 함께 데려와도 좋다는 군주의 허락을 얻었으므로, 아름다운 이거네는 자신의 남편이자 틴타델의 공작인 고를로이스를 따라 이 기념 축제 가운데 한 자리에 함께하였다. 왕은 그 공작 부인에게 깊이 반하여 자신의 연정을 털어놓았으나, 이거네는 그의 구애를 물리치고 그가 한 간청을 자기 남편에게 알렸다. 이를 듣고 공작은 즉시 이거네를 데리고 궁정을 떠났는데, 우서에게 작별 인사도 하지 않았다. 왕은 이러한 도리의 결여에 대해 자신의 자문회의에 하소연하였고, 그들은 공작을 궁정으로 소환하되 만일 그가 따르지 않으면 반역자로 다스려야 한다고 결정하였다. 공작이 소환에 응하기를 거부하자, 왕은 자기 봉신의 영지에 전쟁을 일으켜 틴타델의 견고한 성에서 그를 포위하였다. 멀린은 왕을 고를로이스의 모습으로 변신시켜, 그가 이거네와 여러 차례 몰래 만남을 가질 수 있게 하였다. 마침내 공작이 전투에서 죽임을 당하자, 왕은 이거네와 혼인하였다.\n\n이 결합에서 아서가 태어났으니, 그는 자신의 아버지 우서의 뒤를 이어 왕위에 올랐다.\n\n# ARTHUR CHOSEN KING\n\n아서는 아버지가 죽었을 때 겨우 열다섯 살이었으나, 귀족들의 전체 회의에서 왕으로 선출되었다. 이 일이 반대 없이 이루어진 것은 아니었으니, 야심을 품은 경쟁자들이 많았기 때문이다.\n\n\"그가 그곳에 머무르는 동안 그의 왕국의 저 위대한 영주들과 남작들의 가슴속에서 줄곧 연기처럼 피어오르던 의심이 불꽃처럼 터져 나와 전쟁으로 번졌으니, 이들 대부분이 그에게 맞서 머리를 들고 외쳤기 때문이라, '저자가 누구이기에 우리를 다스린단 말인가? 누가 그를 입증하였는가 우서 왕의 아들이라고? 보라! 우리가 그를 살펴보아도 얼굴도 풍채도, 사지도 목소리도, 우리가 알던 우서의 그것과 닮은 데가 없도다.'\" ─「아서의 도래」.\n\n그러나 큰 거룩함을 지닌 인물인 브라이스 주교가 성탄절 전야에 그 모임에 연설하여, 이 엄숙한 시기에 그들이 앞으로 모실 군주에 관한 섭리의 뜻을 드러내 줄 어떤 징표를 구하는 기도를 올리는 것이 마땅하리라고 역설하였다. 이 일이 행해졌고, 어찌나 성공적이었던지 예배가 채 끝나기도 전에 교회 문 앞에서 기적의 돌이 발견되었는데, 그 돌에는 검 한 자루가 단단히 박혀 있었으며, 그 검의 자루에는 다음과 같은 글귀가 새겨져 있었다.\n\n\"내 이름은 엑스칼리보어, 어느 왕에게 어울리는 귀한 보물이라.\"\n\n브라이스 주교는 이 뚜렷한 기적에 감사를 올리라고 모임에 권한 뒤, 누구든 그 돌에서 검을 뽑아낼 수 있는 자가 브리튼족의 군주로 인정받게 하자는 법을 제안하였고, 그의 제안은 모두의 환호로써 의결되었다. 우서에게 조공을 바치던 왕들과 가장 이름난 기사들이 차례로 힘을 시험해 보았으나, 그 기적의 검은 그들 모두의 노력에 끄떡도 하지 않았다. 검은 성촉절까지 그대로 있었고, 부활절까지도, 또 나라에서 가장 뛰어난 기사들이 해마다 마상 시합을 위해 모이곤 하던 오순절까지도 그대로 있었다. 그때 아서는 자신의 의형제인 케이 경의 종자로 봉사하고 있었는데, 자기 주인을 따라 시합장에 나갔다. 케이 경은 큰 용맹과 성공으로 싸웠으나, 불운하게도 자신의 검을 부러뜨리고 말아, 아서를 어머니에게 보내어 새 검을 가져오게 하였다. 아서는 서둘러 집으로 갔으나 그 부인을 찾지 못하였다. 다만 교회 가까이에 한 자루의 검이 돌에 박혀 있는 것을 보고는, 그 자리로 말을 달려가 아주 쉽게 검을 뽑아내어 자기 주인에게 건넸다. 케이 경은 그 검의 소유가 부여하는 영예를 기꺼이 자기 것으로 삼고자 하였으나, 의심하는 자들을 납득시키려고 검을 다시 돌에 꽂아 두었을 때 그는 도무지 그것을 뽑을 수가 없었고, 검은 두 번째에도 아서의 손 외에는 어떤 손에도 굴복하지 않았다. 이렇게 하늘이 결정적으로 그를 왕으로 지목하였으므로, 아서는 모두의 동의로 그러한 왕임이 선포되었고, 그의 엄숙한 대관식을 위한 이른 날이 정해졌다.\n\n왕위에 선출된 직후, 아서는 열한 명의 왕과 한 명의 공작이 자신에게 맞서고 있음을 알게 되었는데, 그들은 막대한 군대를 거느리고 실로 로킹엄 숲에 진을 치고 있었다. 멀린의 조언에 따라 아서는 브르타뉴로 사절을 보내어, 세상에서 가장 뛰어난 두 기사인 반 왕과 보호르트 왕의 도움을 청하였다. 그들은 그 부름을 받아들여, 강력한 군대를 거느리고 바다를 건너 포츠머스에 상륙하였으며, 그곳에서 큰 기쁨으로 환영받았다. 반란을 일으킨 왕들은 여전히 수에서 우세하였으나, 멀린이 강력한 마법으로 그들의 모든 천막을 한꺼번에 무너뜨렸고, 그 혼란을 틈타 아서가 자기 동맹들과 함께 그들을 덮쳐 완전히 패주시켰다.\n\n반란군을 물리친 뒤, 아서는 색슨족을 상대로 출정하였다. 그들은 혼자 힘으로 맞서기에는 너무 강하였으므로, 그는 아르모리카로 사절을 보내어 호엘의 도움을 간청하였고, 호엘은 곧 군대를 이끌고 건너와 그를 도왔다. 두 왕은 군세를 합쳐 적을 찾아 나섰고, 마침내 적과 마주쳤으며, 양편 모두 결정적인 교전을 준비하였다. 몬머스의 제프리가 전하는 바에 따르면, \"아서 자신은 그토록 위대한 왕에게 걸맞은 흉갑을 두르고, 머리에는 용의 형상을 새긴 황금 투구를 얹는다. 어깨에는 프리웬이라 불리는 방패를 둘러메는데, 그 위에는 성모의 그림이 그려져 있어 늘 그분을 그의 기억에 떠올리게 한다. 아발론섬에서 만들어진 가장 빼어난 검 칼리번을 허리에 차고, 오른손에는 론이라 이름 붙은 창으로 위엄을 더한다. 이것은 길고 폭이 넓은 창으로, 살육에 알맞게 잘 만들어진 것이었다.\" 격렬한 충돌 끝에, 아서는 성모의 이름을 부르며 적들의 한복판으로 돌진하여, 저 무시무시한 칼리번으로 수많은 적을 베어 쓰러뜨리고 나머지는 달아나게 하였다. 호엘은 병으로 발이 묶여 이 전투에 참여하지 못하였다.\n\n이것이 바돈산의 승리라고 불리는 것인데, 비록 전설로 윤색되어 있기는 하나, 역사가들은 이를 실제로 있었던 사건으로 여긴다.\n\n아서가 바돈산 전투에서 이룬 위업은 드레이턴의 시에서 이렇게 기려진다.\n\n\"그들은 노래하였노라, 그가 그날 바돈에서 몸소 떨쳐 일어선 일을, 영광스러운 결승점에 그의 브리튼 왕홀이 놓였던 그날을, 이틀에 걸쳐 어찌나 그 전투가 굳세게 버텼던지, 펜드래곤의 훌륭한 아들이, 그곳에서 피 속을 헤치고 나아가, 제 용맹한 손으로 삼백 명의 색슨족을 베어 쓰러뜨린 일을.\" ─「제4가」.\n\n멀린은 아서를 위해 카르말리데의 왕 라오데간의 딸과의 혼인을 계획해 두었다. 그의 조언에 따라 아서는 그 군주의 궁정을 방문하였는데, 오직 멀린과, 그 마법사가 이 임무를 위해 골라 둔 서른아홉 명의 기사만을 거느리고 갔다. 그들이 도착하였을 때, 라오데간과 그의 중신들이 자문회의를 열고 앉아, 임박한 공격에 맞설 방도를 궁리하고 있었으나 성공할 가망은 거의 없었다. 그 공격을 가하려는 자는 아일랜드의 왕 리언스였는데, 그는 조공을 바치는 열다섯 왕과 거의 헤아릴 수 없는 군대를 거느리고 그 도시를 거의 에워싸고 있었다. 브리튼 기사단의 지휘자 노릇을 하던 멀린은 그들을, 왕의 전쟁에서 그를 도와 봉사하고자 찾아온 이방인들이라고 소개하였다. 다만 그들이 스스로 밝히는 것이 마땅하다고 여길 때까지는 자신들의 이름과 신분을 감출 자유를 누린다는 명백한 조건을 붙였다. 이러한 조건은 매우 이상하게 여겨졌으나 감사히 받아들여졌고, 그 이방인들은 왕에게 으레 하는 맹세를 한 뒤, 멀린이 그들을 위해 마련해 둔 숙소로 물러갔다.\n\n이로부터 며칠 뒤, 적은 라오데간 왕과 맺은 휴전을 아랑곳하지 않고, 느닷없이 진영에서 쏟아져 나와 그 도시를 기습하려 하였다. 왕의 장군 클레오달리스는 되도록 신속하게 왕의 군대를 소집하였다. 아서와 그의 동료들 또한 무장하여 달려 나왔으며, 멀린이 무시무시한 용이 그려진 군기를 들고 그들의 선두에 나타났다. 멀린은 성문으로 나아가 문지기에게 문을 열라고 명하였으나, 문지기는 왕의 명령 없이는 그리할 수 없다며 거부하였다. 그러자 멀린은 자물쇠와 빗장, 걸쇠 따위의 부속물들이 달린 그대로 성문 자체를 통째로 들어 올려, 자기 부대를 그리로 통과하게 한 뒤, 그것을 완벽하게 제자리에 도로 갖다 놓았다. 그러고는 자기 말에 박차를 가하여, 그 작은 부대의 선두에 서서 이천 명의 이교도 무리 속으로 돌진하였다. 수의 차이가 워낙 엄청났으므로, 멀린은 적에게 주문을 걸어 자신들을 공격하는 자들의 수가 적다는 것을 보지 못하게 하였다. 그럼에도 브리튼 기사들은 거센 압박을 받았다. 그러나 성벽에서 이 불공평한 싸움을 지켜보던 도시 사람들은, 그 작은 무리의 이방인들을 운명에 내맡겨 두는 것을 부끄럽게 여겨, 성문을 열고 쏟아져 나왔다. 이제 수가 한결 비슷해지자, 멀린은 자신의 주문을 거두어, 두 군대가 공정한 조건에서 맞붙게 하였다. 아서와 반, 보호르트, 그리고 나머지 사람들이 싸우는 곳에서는 왕의 군대가 우세하였으나, 전장의 다른 한쪽에서는 왕 자신이 적에게 둘러싸여 사로잡혀 가고 말았다. 이 슬픈 광경을, 성벽 위에 서서 전투를 바라보던 왕의 아름다운 딸 귀네비어가 보았다. 그녀는 끔찍한 비탄에 빠져 머리를 쥐어뜯으며 기절하고 말았다.\n\n그러나 전장의 구석구석에서 일어나는 일을 알고 있던 멀린은, 갑자기 자기 기사들을 모아 전투에서 이끌고 나와, 왕을 끌고 가던 무리의 길목을 가로막고, 막을 수 없는 맹렬함으로 그들에게 돌진하여, 호위대 전체를 베어 토막 내거나 흩어 버리고 왕을 구해 냈다. 그 싸움에서 아서는 키가 열다섯 피트나 되는 거인 콜랑과 맞붙었는데, 이미 그 잘생긴 젊은 이방인에게 강한 관심을 느끼기 시작했던 아름다운 귀네비어는 그 대결의 결과를 두고 떨었다. 그러나 아서는 그 괴물의 어깨에 무서운 일격을 가하여, 그 목을 베어 머리가 한쪽으로 늘어지게 만들었고, 이런 꼴이 된 채로 그의 말이 그를 태우고 전장을 이리저리 돌아다니니, 이교도들은 크나큰 공포와 경악에 빠졌다. 귀네비어는, 거인들을 그토록 능란하게 다루는 그 점잖은 기사가 자신의 남편이 될 운명이기를 바란다고 소리 내어 말하지 않을 수 없었고, 그녀의 시녀들도 그 바람을 따라 되뇌었다. 적은 곧 등을 돌려 황급히 달아났고, 라오데간과 그의 동맹들이 바짝 뒤를 쫓았다.\n\n전투가 끝난 뒤 아서는 무장을 벗고 귀네비어 공주의 인도로 욕탕으로 안내되었으며, 그의 벗들은 궁정의 다른 귀부인들의 시중을 받았다. 목욕을 마친 뒤 기사들은 호화로운 향연으로 안내되었는데, 그곳에서도 같은 아름다운 시녀들의 정성스러운 시중을 받았다. 라오데간은 자신을 너그럽게 구해 준 이들의 이름과 신분을 점점 더 알고 싶어졌고, 이따금 자기 손님들의 우두머리가 자기 딸의 매력에 사로잡히기를 바라는 은밀한 소원을 품기도 하면서, 말이 없고 수심에 잠긴 듯하였으며, 신하들의 농담에도 좀처럼 그 상념에서 깨어나지 못하였다. 아서는 귀네비어에게 그녀의 미덕에 대한 자신의 크나큰 존경을 설명할 기회를 얻은 터라 마음속으로 기뻐하였으며, 멀린에게서 가웨인이 런던에서 최근에 거둔 공적들을 전해 듣고는 더욱 흐뭇해하였다. 그 덕분에 그가 곧장 자신의 영토로 돌아갈 필요가 없어졌고, 라오데간의 궁정에서 머무름을 늘릴 자유가 생겼기 때문이다. 날이 갈수록 그 용감한 이방인들에 대한 온 궁정의 찬탄과, 그들의 우두머리를 향한 귀네비어의 연정은 더해 갔다. 그리고 마침내 멀린이 왕에게 이 일행이 방문한 목적이 자신들의 지도자를 위해 신부를 구하는 데 있다고 알리자, 라오데간은 곧바로 귀네비어를 아서에게 내어 주며, 그의 신분이 무엇이든 그의 미덕만으로도 카르말리데의 상속녀를 차지할 자격이 충분하다고 말하였다.\n\n\"그리고 그가 한 여인을 찾을 수 있다면, 그 여인다움에 있어 그가 그 남자다움에 있어 위대한 만큼이나 위대한 여인을─ 그 둘이 함께라면 세상을 바꿀 수 있으리라.\" ─「귀네비어」.\n\n아서는 더할 나위 없는 감사로 그 여인을 받아들였고, 멀린은 이어 왕에게 자기 사위의 신분을 납득시켰다. 그러자 라오데간은 모든 남작들과 더불어 자신들의 정당한 군주, 곧 우서 펜드래곤의 후계자에게 서둘러 충성을 바쳤다. 그리하여 아름다운 귀네비어는 아서와 엄숙하게 약혼하였고, 이레 동안 이어지는 호화로운 축제가 선포되었다. 그 기간이 끝날 무렵 적이 새로이 군세를 되살려 다시 나타났으므로, 군사 작전을 재개하는 것이 불가피해졌다.[42]\n\n이제 우리는 아서가 자신의 수도를 비운 동안 런던과 그 인근에서 일어난 일을 이야기해야 한다. 바로 이 무렵 한 무리의 젊은 영웅들이 아서에게서 기사 작위를 받기 위해 그의 궁정으로 가는 길이었다. 그들은 아서의 조카이자 로트 왕의 아들인 가웨인과 그의 세 형제, 그리고 또 다른 조카이자 난터스 왕의 아들인 갈라킨이었다. 로트 왕은 아서가 패배시킨 반란 수령 가운데 하나였으나, 이제 이 젊은이들을 통해 자기 처남과 화해하기를 바랐다. 그는 자기 아들들과 조카를 더없이 호화롭게 채비시켜, 백작과 남작의 아들들인 젊은이들로 이루어진 화려한 수행단을 딸려 보냈는데, 모두가 가장 좋은 말에 올라 정선된 갑옷을 온전히 갖추고 있었다. 그들은 모두 합쳐 칠백 명이었으나, 아직 기사 작위를 받은 자는 아홉 명뿐이었다. 나머지는 그 영예의 후보자들로서, 적과의 이른 교전을 통해 그것을 얻기를 갈망하였다. 그 지도자인 가웨인은 놀라운 힘을 지닌 기사였는데, 그에 관하여 가장 두드러진 점은 그의 힘이 하루 가운데 어떤 시각에는 다른 때보다 더 강해진다는 것이었다. 오전 아홉 시부터 정오까지 그의 힘은 곱절이 되었고, 오후 세 시부터 저녁 기도 시각까지도 그러하였다. 나머지 시간에는 덜 두드러졌으나, 그때에도 늘 보통 사람들의 힘을 능가하였다.\n\n사흘간 행군한 끝에 그들은 런던 인근에 이르렀는데, 그곳에서 아서와 그의 궁정을 만나리라 기대하였으나, 뜻밖에도 적에게 속한 커다란 수송대와 마주쳤다. 그것은 식량을 가득 실은 수많은 짐수레와 마차로 이루어져 있었고, 사방의 온 고장에서 노략질을 거두어 온 삼천 명의 병사들이 호위하고 있었다. 가웨인의 맹렬한 기병대가 한 차례 돌격하는 것만으로도 그 호위대를 흩어 버리고 수송대를 되찾기에 충분하였으며, 그 수송대는 즉시 런던으로 보내졌다. 그러나 얼마 지나지 않아 칠천 명의 새로운 병력이 다섯 왕족과 그들의 작은 군대를 공격하려고 진격해 왔다. 가웨인은 거대한 몸집의 코아스라는 수령을 골라내어, 그를 정수리부터 가슴까지 갈라 버리는 것으로 전투를 시작하였다. 갈라킨은 역시 몸집이 매우 거대한 사나그란 왕과 맞붙어 그의 목을 베었다. 아그리바인과 가하리에 또한 놀라운 용맹의 위업을 펼쳤다. 이렇게 그들은 거센 압박을 받으면서도 그 거대한 공격군을 막아 냈는데, 그러다가 문득 런던에서 강력한 시민들의 무리가 진격해 오는 것을 보았다. 가웨인이 되찾아 보낸 수송대가 그곳에 도착하여, 시장과 시민들에게 자신들을 구해 준 이가 위험에 처했음을 알린 것이었다. 런던 시민들의 도착으로 싸움은 곧 판가름이 났다. 적은 사방으로 달아났고, 가웨인과 그의 벗들은 감사하는 시민들의 호위를 받으며 런던으로 들어가, 환호 속에 맞아들여졌다.\n\n────\n\n배든 산에서의 대승으로 색슨족이 한동안 완전히 진압되자, 아서는 군대를 돌려 스코트족과 픽트족을 향해 진격했고, 로몬드 호수에서 그들을 패주시켜 자비를 구걸하게 만들었다. 그런 다음 그는 요크로 가서 성탄절을 보냈으며, 이교도들이 약탈하고 무너뜨린 그리스도교 교회들을 복구하는 일에 힘썼다. 이듬해 여름에는 아일랜드를 정복했고, 이어서 함대를 이끌고 아이슬란드로 항해하여 그곳마저 굴복시켰다. 고틀란드와 오크니의 왕들은 자진해서 와서 복종을 맹세하고 조공을 바치겠노라 약속했다. 그 뒤 그는 브리튼으로 돌아왔으며, 왕국을 확고히 다진 다음 열두 해 동안 평화롭게 지냈다.\n\n이 기간에 그는 외국에서 무용으로 이름난 사람들을 가리지 않고 모두 자신에게로 불러들였고, 자신의 가신 수를 늘렸으며, 궁정에 그러한 세련됨을 도입하여 아주 먼 나라의 백성들조차 본받을 만하다고 여기게 되었다. 그리하여 자기 옷과 무기가 아서 휘하 기사들의 것과 똑같은 양식으로 만들어지지 않으면, 자신이 조금이라도 존중받을 만하다고 여기는 귀족은 한 사람도 없을 정도였다.\n\n자신이 국내에서 이토록 강성하다는 것을 알게 되자, 아서는 자신의 힘을 외국으로 확장하려는 계획을 세우기 시작했다. 그래서 그는 함대를 준비한 다음, 먼저 노르웨이를 공략하고자 했으니, 누이의 남편인 로트에게 그 나라의 왕관을 마련해 주기 위함이었다. 아서는 노르웨이에 상륙하여 그 나라 왕과 큰 싸움을 벌여 그를 무찔렀으며, 그 승세를 몰아 온 나라를 자신의 통치 아래 복속시킬 때까지 추격했고, 로트를 왕좌에 앉혔다. 그런 다음 아서는 갈리아로 항해하여 파리 시를 포위했다. 갈리아는 그 무렵 로마의 속주였으며, 호민관 플롤로가 다스리고 있었다. 파리의 포위가 한 달간 이어지고 백성들이 기근에 시달리기 시작하자, 플롤로는 아서에게 일대일 결투를 청하면서, 그런 방식으로 속주의 지배권을 결정짓자고 제안했다. 아서는 기꺼이 그 도전을 받아들였고, 겨룸 끝에 적수를 베어 죽였으며, 그러자 시민들은 그에게 도시를 내주었다. 승리한 뒤 아서는 자기 군대를 둘로 나누어, 그 하나는 호엘의 지휘에 맡겨 아키타니아로 진군하라 명했고, 자신은 나머지 군대를 거느리고 다른 속주들을 정복하려 했다. 갈리아의 모든 지역이 완전히 복속되기까지 아홉 해가 걸렸으며, 그 끝에 아서는 파리로 돌아와 그곳에서 궁정을 열었고, 성직자와 백성들의 회의를 소집하여 그 왕국에 평화와 공정한 법의 집행을 확립했다. 그런 다음 그는 노르망디를 식료품 시종인 베드베르에게, 안데가비아 속주를 집사인 케이에게 하사했고, 그 밖의 여러 속주를 자신을 수행하던 대인들에게 나누어 주었다. 그리고 도시와 지방의 평화를 정착시킨 다음, 봄이 시작될 무렵 브리튼으로 돌아왔다.\n\n성령강림 축일이 다가오자, 아서는 그토록 의기양양한 성공을 거둔 뒤의 기쁨을 더욱 잘 드러내고, 그 축일을 한층 엄숙하게 지키며, 이제 자신에게 복속된 제후들의 마음을 화합시키기 위하여, 그 절기 동안 성대한 궁정을 열어 자신의 머리에 왕관을 얹고, 자신에게 복속된 모든 왕과 공작을 그 의식에 초대하기로 결심했다. 그리고 그는 자신의 목적에 알맞은 장소로 군단의 도시 카에를레온을 골랐다. 다른 도시들보다 큰 부를 지녔다는 점 외에도, 세번 해 가까이 어스크 강가에 자리한 그 위치가 더없이 쾌적하고 그토록 큰 의식에 알맞았기 때문이다. 한쪽에서는 그 고귀한 강이 도시를 적셔, 바다 건너 여러 나라의 왕과 제후들이 배를 타고 거슬러 올라오는 편의를 누릴 수 있었다. 다른 한쪽에서는 초원과 숲의 아름다움, 그리고 도시를 장식하는 높다란 금빛 지붕을 갖춘 왕궁의 장려함이 어우러져 로마의 웅대함과도 견줄 만했다. 또한 그곳은 두 교회로도 유명했는데, 그 하나는 온전히 하느님을 섬기는 데 헌신하는 처녀들의 성가대로 꾸며져 있었고, 다른 하나는 사제들의 수도원을 거느리고 있었다. 그 밖에도 그곳에는 이백 명의 철학자로 이루어진 학원이 있었으니, 이들은 천문학과 그 밖의 학예에 정통하여 별들의 운행을 부지런히 관측했고, 아서에게 앞으로 일어날 사건들에 관한 참된 예언을 일러 주었다. 그리하여 이처럼 온갖 즐거움을 안겨 주는 이곳에서 다가오는 축제를 위한 준비가 갖추어졌다.\n\n이어서 갈리아와 인근 섬들의 제후들을 궁정으로 초대하기 위해 여러 왕국으로 사신이 파견되었다. 그리하여 알바니아, 곧 지금의 스코틀랜드의 왕 아우구셀, 베네도티아, 곧 지금의 북웨일스의 왕 카드월로, 데메티아, 곧 지금의 남웨일스의 왕 사테르가 왔다. 또한 런던과 요크의 대주교좌의 대주교들, 그리고 군단의 도시 카에를레온의 주교 두브리키우스도 왔다. 브리튼의 수석 주교였던 이 고위 성직자는 신심이 어찌나 두터운지 기도만으로도 어떤 병자든 고칠 수 있었다. 그 밖에 주요 도시의 백작들과, 그에 못지않은 위엄을 지닌 여러 명사도 있었다.\n\n인근 섬들에서는 아일랜드의 왕 길라무리우스, 오크니의 왕 군파시우스, 아이슬란드의 왕 말바시우스, 노르웨이의 왕 로트, 노르망디 공작이자 식료품 시종인 베드베르, 안데가비아 공작이자 음식 시종인 케이가 왔다. 또한 갈리아의 열두 중신과, 아르모리카의 브리튼인의 공작 호엘이 자신의 귀족들과 함께 왔는데, 이들은 노새와 말, 호화로운 마구의 행렬을 어찌나 거느리고 왔던지 말로 다 표현하기 어려울 정도였다. 이들 외에도 에스파냐 이쪽 편에서는 이 초대에 응하지 않은, 조금이라도 존중받을 만한 제후가 한 사람도 없었다. 그것도 무리는 아니었으니, 온 세상에 두루 알려진 아서의 아량이 그를 만백성에게서 사랑받게 만든 까닭이었다.\n\n의식이 거행될 그날 모두가 모이자, 대주교들이 왕의 머리에 왕관을 얹기 위해 궁궐로 인도되었다. 그러자 궁정이 자신의 교구 안에서 열렸으므로, 두브리키우스가 그 직무를 거행할 채비를 갖추었다. 왕은 왕의 예복을 갖추어 입자마자 대성당으로 성대한 행렬과 함께 인도되었으니, 알바니아, 콘월, 데메티아, 베네도티아의 네 왕이 그의 앞에서 네 자루의 황금 칼을 받들어 나아갔다. 한편 다른 쪽에서는 가장 화려한 장신구로 단장한 왕비가 대주교와 주교들의 인도를 받아 처녀들의 교회로 나아갔으며, 앞서 언급한 왕들의 네 왕비도 옛 관습에 따라 그녀 앞에서 네 마리의 흰 비둘기를 받들어 나아갔다. 행렬이 모두 끝났을 때, 두 교회에서 갖가지로 어우러진 악기와 노랫소리의 화음이 어찌나 사람을 황홀케 했던지, 수행하던 기사들은 어느 쪽을 더 좋아해야 할지 갈피를 잡지 못해 번갈아 이 교회에서 저 교회로 몰려다녔으며, 하루를 온통 거기에 쏟았는데도 그 의식에 조금도 싫증을 내지 않았다. 마침내 두 교회에서 미사가 끝나자, 왕과 왕비는 왕관을 벗고 더 가벼운 장신구로 갈아입은 다음 연회로 갔다. 모두가 서열에 따라 자리에 앉자, 음식 시종인 케이가 호화로운 흰담비 모피 예복을 걸치고, 마찬가지로 호화로운 차림을 한 천 명의 젊은 귀족과 함께 음식을 차려 올렸다. 다른 쪽에서는 식료품 시종인 베드베르가 같은 수의 시종을 거느리고 따랐는데, 이들은 온갖 종류의 잔과 술그릇을 받들고 시중을 들었다. 그리고 음식과 술이 넘쳐났으며, 모든 것이 최상품이었고 최상의 방식으로 차려졌다. 그 무렵 브리튼은 그토록 장려한 경지에 이르러, 부와 호화로움과 세련됨에서 다른 모든 왕국을 훨씬 능가했던 것이다.\n\n연회가 끝나자마자 그들은 도시 밖 들판으로 나가 갖가지 놀이로 흥을 돋우었으니, 활과 화살로 쏘기, 창 던지기, 무거운 돌과 바위 던지기, 주사위 놀이 따위였으며, 이 모든 것을 다툼 없이 무탈하게 즐겼다. 이렇게 사흘을 보낸 뒤 그들은 헤어졌고, 왕들과 귀족들은 저마다의 고향으로 떠나갔다.\n\n이후 아서는 다섯 해 동안 평화롭게 다스렸다. 그러던 어느 날 로마 황제 레오 휘하의 대리관 루키우스 티베리우스가 보낸 사신들이 와서 조공을 요구했다. 그러나 아서는 조공을 바치기를 거부하고 전쟁을 준비했다. 필요한 채비를 마치자마자 그는 왕국의 통치를 조카 모드레드와 왕비 귀네비어에게 맡기고, 군대를 이끌고 하모 항구로 행군했는데, 그곳에는 바람이 그에게 알맞게 불고 있었다. 군대는 무사히 바다를 건너 바르바 강 어귀에 상륙했다. 그리고 그곳에 천막을 치고 섬들의 왕들이 도착하기를 기다렸다.\n\n모든 병력이 도착하자마자 아서는 아우구스토두눔으로 진군하여 알바 강 기슭에 진을 쳤다. 이곳에서 여러 차례 전투가 벌어졌는데, 그 모든 싸움에서 용맹한 지휘관 아르모리카 공작 호엘과 아서의 조카 가웨인이 이끄는 브리튼인이 우세를 점했다. 마침내 루키우스 티베리우스는 후퇴하여 황제 레오가 새로운 병력을 거느리고 합류하기를 기다리기로 결심했다. 그러나 아서는 이를 미리 내다보고 어느 골짜기를 차지하여 루키우스의 퇴로를 막았으며, 그로 하여금 결전을 치를 수밖에 없게 만들었으니, 이 싸움에서 아서는 자신의 가장 용맹한 기사들과 가장 충실한 추종자들 가운데 몇몇을 잃었다. 그러나 다른 한편으로 루키우스 티베리우스는 죽임을 당했고, 그의 군대는 완전히 패주했다. 도망자들은 온 들판으로 흩어졌으니, 더러는 샛길과 숲으로, 더러는 도시와 마을로, 또 안전을 바랄 수 있는 온갖 다른 곳으로 달아났다.\n\n아서는 다음 겨울이 지날 때까지 그 지방에 머물면서 질서를 회복하고 통치를 안정시키는 일에 시간을 보냈다. 그런 다음 그는 잉글랜드로 돌아와 자신의 승리를 크나큰 영광으로 기렸다.\n\n이어서 왕은 자신의 모든 기사를 봉했으며, 부유하지 못한 자들에게는 영지를 내려 주었고, 그들 모두에게 결코 횡포를 부리거나 살인을 저지르지 말 것이며, 늘 반역을 멀리하라고 명했다. 또한 어떤 일이 있어도 잔혹하게 굴지 말고, 자비를 구하는 자에게는 자비를 베풀 것이며, 이를 어기면 자신의 명예와 영주권을 잃으리라 명했다. 또한 부인과 처녀와 귀부인에게는 늘 봉사할 것이며, 이를 어기면 죽음을 면치 못하리라 명했다. 또한 어떤 사람도 그릇된 다툼을 위해, 또 어떤 법이나 세상의 어떤 재물을 위해서도 싸움에 나서지 말라 명했다. 이 맹세를 원탁의 기사들은 늙은이든 젊은이든 모두가 했다. 그리고 해마다 성령강림의 큰 축일에 그들은 이 맹세를 새로이 했다.\n\n# KING ARTHUR SLAYS THE GIANT OF ST. MICHAEL’S MOUNT\n\n군대가 왕들의 도착을 기다리며 브르타뉴에 진을 치고 있을 무렵, 한 시골 사람이 아서에게 와서 이렇게 일렀다. 성 미카엘 산이라 불리는 인근 산에 동굴을 둔 거인 하나가 오래도록 농부의 아이들을 잡아다 잡아먹는 버릇이 있었다는 것이었다. \"그런데 이제 그자가 브르타뉴 공작부인이 시종들과 함께 말을 타고 가던 것을 붙잡아, 그들이 아무리 애써도 막지 못한 채 끌고 가 버렸습니다.\" \"이보게,\" 아서 왕이 말했다. \"그 거인이 출몰한다는 곳으로 나를 데려갈 수 있겠는가?\" \"예, 분명히 데려갈 수 있습니다.\" 그 선량한 사람이 말했다. \"보십시오, 저쪽에 큰 불 두 무더기가 보이는 곳, 거기서 그자를 찾으실 수 있을 것이며, 제 짐작으로는 프랑스 전역에 있는 것보다 더 많은 보물을 거기서 보실 것입니다.\" 그러자 왕은 베드베르 경과 케이 경을 불러, 자신과 그들을 위해 말과 마구를 채비하라 명했으니, 저녁이 지나면 성 미카엘 산으로 순례를 떠나려 함이었다.\n\n그리하여 그들 셋은 길을 떠나, 산기슭에 이를 때까지 말을 달렸다. 그곳에서 왕은 그들에게 기다리라고 명했으니, 자신이 직접 그 산에 오르려 함이었다. 그리하여 그는 큰 불이 있는 곳에 이를 때까지 산을 올랐고, 거기서 갓 만들어진 무덤가에 앉아 크게 슬퍼하는 늙은 여인 하나를 보았다. 그러자 아서 왕은 그녀에게 인사하고, 어찌하여 그토록 애통해하는지 물었다. 그녀가 답하여 말했다. \"기사님, 나직이 말씀하십시오. 저쪽에 악마가 있어, 만약 그자가 당신 말소리를 들으면 와서 당신을 멸하고 말 것입니다. 그자가 어찌나 사납고 강한지, 당신은 그자에게 맞설 수 없을 것입니다. 그자는 여기 누워 있는 공작부인을 살해했는데, 그녀는 온 세상에서 가장 아름다운 분이셨고 브르타뉴 공작 호엘 경의 부인이셨습니다.\" \"부인,\" 왕이 말했다. \"나는 고귀한 정복자 아서 왕에게서 와서 저 폭군과 담판하려는 것이오.\" \"그런 담판이라니 가당치도 않습니다.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그자는 왕도, 다른 어떤 사람도 안중에 두지 않습니다.\" \"좋소,\" 아서가 말했다. \"당신의 두려운 말에도 불구하고 나는 내 사명을 이루겠소.\" 그리하여 그는 산마루를 따라 나아가, 거인이 저녁을 먹으며 한 사람의 다리를 물어뜯고, 자신의 넓적한 사지를 불에 굽고 있는 광경을 보았으며, 차례대로 잡아먹힐 운명에 처한 아름다운 처녀 셋이 묶인 채 누워 있는 것을 보았다. 아서 왕은 그것을 보고 그들에게 크나큰 연민을 느껴, 슬픔으로 가슴이 미어졌다. 그런 다음 그는 거인을 향해 외쳐 말했다. \"온 세상을 다스리시는 분께서 너에게 짧은 목숨과 치욕스러운 죽음을 내리시기를. 어찌하여 이 공작부인을 살해했느냐? 그러니 나오너라, 오늘 너는 내 손에 죽으리라.\" 그러자 거인이 벌떡 일어나 커다란 곤봉을 집어 들고 왕을 후려쳐, 그의 관을 쳐서 떨어뜨렸다. 그러자 왕은 칼로 그의 배를 찔러 무시무시한 상처를 입혔다. 그러자 거인은 곤봉을 내던지고 두 팔로 왕을 붙잡아, 그의 갈비뼈를 으스러뜨렸다. 그러자 세 처녀가 무릎을 꿇고 아서에게 도움과 위안이 있기를 기도했다. 아서는 뒹굴고 몸을 비틀었으니, 한순간에는 밑에 깔렸다가 또 한순간에는 위로 올라왔다. 이렇게 뒹굴고 엎치락뒤치락하며 그들은 산을 굴러 내려갔으며, 굴러 내려가는 동안에도 아서는 단검으로 줄곧 그자를 찔렀다. 그러다가 그들은 두 기사가 있는 곳에 이르게 되었다. 그리고 그들은 왕이 거인의 두 팔에 단단히 붙들린 것을 보고는, 다가가 그를 풀어 주었다. 그러자 왕은 케이 경에게 거인의 머리를 베어 창자루에 꿰어, 외성 망루 위에 꽂아 모든 백성이 그것을 보고 바라볼 수 있게 하라 명했다. 이렇게 행해졌고, 이내 그 소식이 온 나라에 알려져, 백성들이 와서 왕에게 감사했다. 그러자 왕이 말했다. \"그대들의 감사는 하느님께 드리시오. 그리고 거인의 노획물을 가져다 그대들끼리 나누시오.\" 그리고 아서 왕은 성 미카엘을 기려 그 산에 교회를 짓게 했다.\n\n# KING ARTHUR GETS A SWORD FROM THE LADY OF THE LAKE\n\n어느 날 아서 왕이 말을 타고 나갔다가, 문득 무지렁이 세 사람이 멀린을 죽이려고 뒤쫓는 것을 알아챘다. 왕은 그들에게 말을 달려가 명했다. \"달아나라, 이 무지렁이들아!\" 그러자 그들은 기사를 보고 겁을 먹어 달아났다. \"오, 멀린,\" 아서가 말했다. \"내가 곁에 없었더라면, 그대의 온갖 재주에도 불구하고 그대는 여기서 죽임을 당했을 것이네.\" \"아니,\" 멀린이 말했다. \"그렇지 않습니다. 제가 원하기만 했다면 스스로를 구할 수 있었습니다. 그러나 당신은 저보다 죽음에 더 가까이 계십니다.\" 그리하여 그들이 이렇게 이야기를 나누며 가는데, 아서 왕은 어느 기사가 마치 길목을 지키려는 듯 말 위에 앉아 있는 것을 알아챘다. \"기사여,\" 아서가 말했다. \"무슨 까닭으로 여기 머물러 있는가?\" 그러자 그 기사가 말했다. \"나와 겨루지 않고서는 어떤 기사도 이 길을 지날 수 없으니, 그것이 이 길목의 관습이오.\" \"내가 그 관습을 바로잡겠다.\" 왕이 말했다. 그러자 그들은 마주 달려, 어찌나 세차게 부딪쳤던지 두 사람의 창이 산산조각 났다. 그런 다음 그들은 칼을 빼어 들고 수많은 큰 타격을 주고받으며 격렬한 싸움을 벌였다. 그러나 마침내 그 기사의 칼이 아서 왕의 칼을 둘로 쪼개 버렸다. 그러자 그 기사가 아서에게 말했다. \"그대는 내 손안에 있으니, 살리든 죽이든 내게 달렸다. 그러니 그대가 패배한 비겁자임을 인정하지 않는다면, 그대는 죽으리라.\" \"죽음으로 말할 것 같으면,\" 아서 왕이 말했다. \"그것이 오거든 기꺼이 맞으리라. 그러나 그대에게 비겁자로서 항복하는 일은 결코 하지 않겠다.\" 그런 다음 그는 그 기사에게 달려들어 허리를 붙잡아 메다꽂았다. 그러나 그 기사는 더없이 강한 사내인지라, 이내 아서를 자기 밑에 깔고 투구를 벗겨 그를 죽이려 했다. 그러자 멀린이 말했다. \"기사여, 손을 멈추시오. 이분은 그대가 짐작하는 것보다 훨씬 더 존귀한 분이오.\" \"어찌하여, 이분이 누구요?\" 기사가 말했다. \"아서 왕이시오.\" 그러자 그는 왕의 진노가 두려워 그를 죽이려고 칼을 들어 올렸다. 그러자 멀린이 그 기사에게 마법을 걸어, 그가 깊은 잠에 빠져 땅에 쓰러지게 했다. 그런 다음 멀린은 아서 왕을 일으켜 말 위에 앉혔다. \"아아!\" 아서가 말했다. \"그대가 무슨 짓을 했는가, 멀린? 그대의 재주로 이 훌륭한 기사를 죽였단 말인가?\" \"걱정 마십시오.\" 멀린이 말했다. \"그는 당신보다 더 멀쩡합니다. 그는 그저 잠들었을 뿐이며, 세 시간 뒤에는 깨어날 것입니다.\"\n\n그런 다음 왕과 그는 길을 떠나, 어느 은수자에게 이를 때까지 갔으니, 그는 선량한 사람이요 뛰어난 의원이었다. 그리하여 그 은수자는 왕의 상처를 모두 살펴 좋은 고약을 발라 주었고, 왕은 그곳에서 사흘을 머물렀으며, 그러자 상처가 잘 아물어 그가 말을 타고 거동할 수 있게 되었다. 그리하여 그들은 길을 떠났는데, 말을 달리며 아서가 말했다. \"내게는 칼이 없네.\" \"괜찮습니다.\" 멀린이 말했다. \"이 근처에 당신 것이 될 칼이 있습니다.\" 그리하여 그들은 어느 호수에 이를 때까지 말을 달렸으니, 그것은 맑고 드넓은 물이었다. 그리고 호수 한가운데서 아서는 흰 명주를 두른 팔 하나가 손에 아름다운 칼을 쥐고 있는 것을 알아챘다. \"보십시오!\" 멀린이 말했다. \"저쪽에 제가 말씀드린 그 칼이 있습니다. 그것은 호수의 귀부인의 것이니, 그녀가 원한다면 당신이 그것을 가질 수 있을 것입니다. 그러나 그녀가 원하지 않는다면, 그것을 가지는 일은 당신의 힘으로 어쩔 수 없을 것입니다.\"\n\n그리하여 아서 경과 멀린은 말에서 내려 배에 올랐다. 그리고 손이 쥐고 있는 칼에 이르자 아서 경은 그 손잡이를 잡아 자신에게로 가져왔고, 그러자 팔과 손은 물 아래로 사라졌다.\n\n그런 다음 그들은 뭍으로 돌아와 말을 달려 나아갔다. 그리고 아서 경은 그 칼을 바라보며 무척 흡족해했다.\n\n그리하여 그들은 카에를레온으로 말을 달려갔고, 그곳의 기사들은 무척 기뻐했다. 그리고 그들은 왕의 모험에 관해 듣고는, 그가 그토록 홀로 제 몸을 위험에 내맡긴 것을 의아하게 여겼다. 그러나 명예를 아는 모든 이는, 다른 가난한 기사들이 그러하듯 제 몸을 모험에 내맡기는 그러한 우두머리를 모시는 것이 훌륭한 일이라고 말했다.\n\n————\n\n# SIR GAWAIN\n\n가웨인 경은 아서 왕의 조카로, 오크니의 왕 로트와 혼인한 누이 모르가나에게서 난 아들이었으며, 로트는 아서에 의해 노르웨이의 왕이 되었다. 가웨인 경은 원탁의 기사들 가운데 가장 이름난 이들 중 하나였으며, 로맨스 작가들은 그를 가리켜 현명하고 예의 바른 가웨인이라 일컬었다. 초서도 자신의 「향사의 이야기」에서 이를 넌지시 언급하는데, 그곳에서 한 낯선 기사가 궁정의 모든 이에게 인사를 올린다.\n\n\"그토록 드높은 경의와 예법으로, 말씨로나 거동으로나 한결같이 정중하여, 가웨인이 그 오랜 예의로써, 설령 그가 요정 나라에서 다시 돌아왔다 한들, 한 마디도 더 보탤 데가 없었으리.\"\n\n가웨인의 형제들은 아그리바인, 가하리에트, 그리고 가레스였다.\n\n# SIR GAWAIN’S MARRIAGE\n\n옛날 옛적에 아서 왕이 흥겨운 칼라일에서 궁정을 열고 있을 때, 한 처녀가 그의 앞에 나와 청을 올렸다. 그것은 어느 비열한 기사에게 복수를 해달라는 것으로, 그자가 그녀의 연인을 사로잡고 그녀의 영지를 약탈하였다는 것이었다. 아서 왕은 자신의 검 엑스칼리버를 가져오고 군마에 안장을 얹으라 명하고는, 지체 없이 그 처녀의 억울함을 바로잡고자 말을 달려 나아갔다. 머지않아 그는 그 무시무시한 영주의 성에 이르러, 그에게 결투를 청하였다. 그러나 그 성은 마법의 땅 위에 서 있었으니, 어떠한 기사라도 그곳에 발을 들이는 순간 곧바로 용기가 꺾이고 힘이 쇠하게 되는 주문이 걸려 있었다. 아서 왕도 그 마력을 느꼈으니, 일격을 주고받기도 전에 그 굳센 사지가 힘을 잃고, 머리가 아득해졌다. 그는 무례한 기사에게 포로로 항복할 수밖에 없었는데, 그 기사는 한 가지 조건이 아니면 그를 풀어주려 하지 않았다. 그것은 일 년이 다 차는 날 다시 돌아와, \"여자들이 가장 바라는 것이 무엇인가?\"라는 물음에 참된 답을 가져오는 것이었으며, 만일 그러지 못하면 자신과 그 영지를 내놓아야 한다는 것이었다. 아서 왕은 그 조건을 받아들이고, 정한 날에 돌아오겠노라 맹세하였다. 그 한 해 동안 왕은 동으로도 달리고 서로도 달리며, 만나는 모든 이에게 여자라면 누구나 가장 바라는 것이 무엇이냐고 물었다. 어떤 이는 재물이라 하였고, 어떤 이는 영화와 권세라 하였으며, 어떤 이는 즐거움이라 하였고, 어떤 이는 아첨이라 하였으며, 또 어떤 이는 늠름한 기사라 하였다. 그러나 그 갖가지 대답 가운데서 그는 확실히 믿을 만한 것을 찾을 수가 없었다. 어느덧 한 해가 거의 다 저물어갈 무렵, 어느 날 그가 깊은 생각에 잠긴 채 숲을 지나가는데, 한 나무 아래에 너무도 흉측한 모습의 여인이 앉아 있는 것을 보았다. 그가 눈길을 돌려버리고, 그녀가 점잖게 인사를 건네는데도 아무 대답을 하지 않았다. \"그대는 어떤 사람이기에,\" 여인이 말하였다. \"내게 말을 걸지 아니하는가? 비록 내 생김새가 곱지는 않으나, 어쩌면 그대의 의문을 풀어줄 수 있을지도 모르오.\" \"그대가 그리해 준다면,\" 아서 왕이 말하였다. \"무시무시한 여인이여, 그대가 원하는 보답을 고르시오, 그러면 그것을 주리다.\" \"그대의 신의를 걸고 내게 이를 맹세하시오,\" 그녀가 말하니, 아서가 그리 맹세하였다. 그러자 여인은 그에게 그 비밀을 일러주고는 자신의 보답을 요구하였으니, 그것은 왕이 어느 곱고 예의 바른 기사를 찾아 자신의 남편으로 삼아 달라는 것이었다.\n\n아서 왕은 그 무시무시한 영주의 성으로 서둘러 가서, 여러 조언자들에게서 들은 모든 대답을 하나하나 일러주었으나, 다만 마지막 것만은 빼놓았는데, 그 어느 것도 참된 답으로 인정받지 못하였다. \"이제 항복하라, 아서여,\" 거인이 말하였다. \"그대는 몸값을 치르지 못하였으니, 그대와 그대의 영지는 내게 몰수되었도다.\" 그러자 아서 왕이 말하였다.\n\n\"잠깐 손을 멈추시오, 오만한 영주여, 부디 그 손을 멈추기를 청하노라, 그리고 내 영지를 구하고자 한 번만 더 말하게 하여 다오. 오늘 아침 황야를 건너오다가, 나는 한 여인이 앉아 있음을 보았으니, 참나무와 푸른 호랑가시나무 사이에, 온통 진홍빛 비단을 두르고 있었노라. 그녀가 말하기를 모든 여자는 제 뜻대로 하기를 바란다 하니, 이것이 그들의 으뜸가는 소망이로다. 이제 그대가 참된 영주라면 인정하라, 내가 내 값을 다 치렀음을.\"\n\n\"네게 이것을 일러준 자는 바로 내 누이로다,\" 무례한 영주가 외쳤다. \"그년에게 복수가 내리기를! 언젠가 반드시 그년에게도 똑같이 못된 짓을 갚아주리라.\"\n\n아서 왕은 집으로 말을 달렸으나, 마음이 가볍지는 못하였으니, 자신이 그 끔찍한 여인에게 젊고 늠름한 기사 하나를 남편으로 주기로 한 약속이 떠올랐기 때문이었다. 그가 자신의 시름을 조카 가웨인 경에게 털어놓으니, 가웨인이 대답하였다. \"슬퍼하지 마소서, 폐하, 그 끔찍한 여인과는 제가 혼인하겠나이다.\" 아서 왕이 대답하였다.\n\n\"안 되네, 안 되네, 착한 가웨인 경이여, 그대는 내 누이의 아들이 아닌가. 그 끔찍한 여인은 너무도 흉측하고, 그대에게는 너무도 추하다네.\"\n\n그러나 가웨인이 고집을 굽히지 않으니, 왕은 마침내 마음 아프게도 가웨인이 자신의 몸값이 되는 것을 승낙하였다. 그리하여 어느 날 왕과 그의 기사들이 숲으로 말을 달려, 그 끔찍한 여인을 만나 궁정으로 데려왔다. 가웨인 경은 동료들의 비웃음과 조롱을 애써 견뎌냈으며, 혼례가 치러졌으나, 여느 때와 같은 잔치는 없었다. 초서는 우리에게 이렇게 전한다.\n\n\". . . 거기엔 아무런 기쁨도 잔치도 없었으니, 오직 무거움과 크나큰 슬픔만이 있었네. 이튿날 그가 남몰래 그녀와 혼인하고는, 온종일 올빼미처럼 자신을 숨겼으니, 제 아내가 그토록 추하게 보였던 탓이라네!\"[46]\n\n밤이 되어 둘만이 함께 있게 되자, 가웨인 경은 자신의 혐오를 감출 수가 없었다. 그러자 여인이 그에게 어찌하여 그토록 무겁게 한숨을 쉬며 얼굴을 돌리느냐고 물었다. 그는 솔직하게 그것이 세 가지 까닭, 곧 그녀의 나이와 추함과 미천한 신분 때문이라고 고백하였다. 여인은 조금도 언짢아하지 않고, 그의 모든 불평에 더없이 훌륭한 논리로 대답하였다. 그녀는 그에게 나이에는 분별이 따르고, 추함에는 연적의 걱정이 없는 안심이 따르며, 모든 참된 고귀함이란 태생의 우연이 아니라 그 사람의 됨됨이에 달려 있음을 일깨워 주었다.\n\n가웨인 경은 아무 대답도 하지 못하였다. 그러나 신부에게로 눈길을 돌리니, 그녀가 더 이상 자신을 그토록 괴롭히던 흉한 모습을 하고 있지 않음을 알아채고는 그 얼마나 놀랐던가. 그러자 그녀는 그에게, 자신이 두르고 있던 그 모습이 본래의 모습이 아니라 어느 사악한 마법사가 그녀에게 씌운 변장이었으며, 두 가지 일이 일어나기 전까지는 그 모습으로 지내야 할 운명에 처해 있었다고 일러주었다. 그 한 가지는 어느 젊고 늠름한 기사를 남편으로 얻는 것이었다. 이것이 이루어졌으니, 그 마법의 절반은 풀린 셈이었다. 이제 그녀는 시간의 절반은 본래의 모습으로 지낼 수 있게 되었기에, 그에게 자신을 낮에는 곱게 하고 밤에는 추하게 할지, 아니면 그 반대로 할지를 고르라고 청하였다. 가웨인 경은 자신만이 그녀를 보게 되는 밤에 그녀가 가장 아름다운 모습이기를, 그리고 만일 그 흉한 얼굴을 누군가에게 보여야 한다면 차라리 다른 이들에게나 보이기를 바랐다. 그러나 그녀는 그에게, 낮에 기사들과 귀부인들이 무리지은 가운데서 가장 아름다운 모습을 하고 있는 편이 자신에게 얼마나 더 기쁜 일이겠느냐고 일깨워 주었다. 가웨인 경은 양보하여, 자신의 뜻을 그녀의 뜻에 넘겨주었다. 마법을 풀기 위해 모자랐던 것은 오직 이것뿐이었다. 이제 그 사랑스러운 여인은 기쁨에 차서 그에게, 자신이 더는 변하지 않을 것이며, 지금의 모습 그대로 밤에도 낮에도 한결같이 있으리라고 확약하였다.\n\n\"고운 홍조가 그녀의 발그레한 뺨을 물들이고, 그녀의 두 눈은 자두처럼 검었으며, 무르익은 앵두가 그녀의 입술을 부풀리고, 그녀의 목덜미는 온통 눈빛이었네. 가웨인 경은 그 고운 여인에게 자리에 누운 채로 입을 맞추고는, 참된 기사로서 맹세하기를, 이토록 달콤한 향기는 일찍이 없었노라 하였네.\"\n\n그 여인을 옭아매던 마법이 풀리자 그녀의 오라비인 \"무시무시한 영주\"도 함께 풀려났으니, 그 역시 그 마법에 얽혀 있었던 까닭이다. 그는 더 이상 무례한 압제자가 아니게 되었으며, 아서 왕의 궁정에 있는 어느 누구 못지않게 늠름하고 너그러운 기사가 되었다.\n\n————\n\n카라도크는 아서 왕의 아름다운 조카 이센의 아들이었다. 그는 자기 아버지가 누구인지 모르고 있었는데, 그것이 밝혀진 경위는 다음과 같다. 이 젊은이가 기사 작위를 받을 만한 나이가 되었을 때, 아서 왕은 그에게 기사 작위를 내리기 위해 성대한 궁정 회의를 열었다. 이 자리에 낯선 기사 한 사람이 나타나, 아서 왕의 궁정에 모인 기사들에게 자기와 일격을 주고받자고 도전했다. 그가 내건 제안은 이러했다. 자기 목을 받침대 위에 올려놓을 테니 어느 기사든 내리쳐 보라, 다만 자기가 그 일격을 견디고 살아남으면 이번에는 그 기사가 똑같은 시련을 받아야 한다는 조건이었다. 평소에는 어떤 도전이든 기꺼이 받아들이던 케이 경마저도 이것은 전혀 사리에 맞지 않는다고 단언하며, 온 세상의 재물을 다 준대도 받아들이지 않겠다고 선언했다. 그리고 그 기사가 시행할 칼을 내밀었으나 아무도 감히 받아들지 못했다. 마침내 카라도크가 원탁이 이렇게 치욕을 당하는 데에 분노하여, 외투를 벗어 던지고 그 칼을 받아 들었다. \"그대는 가장 뛰어난 기사의 한 사람으로서 이 일을 하려는 것인가?\" 낯선 기사가 물었다. \"아니오.\" 그가 대답했다. \"다만 가장 어리석은 자의 한 사람으로서요.\" 낯선 기사는 받침대 위에 머리를 올려놓았고, 일격을 받아 머리가 어깨에서 굴러떨어졌으나, 그는 머리를 쫓아가 주워서 보란 듯이 도로 제자리에 붙인 뒤, 내년에 궁정이 다시 모일 때 돌아와 자기 차례를 청구하겠노라고 말했다. 그 일 주년이 돌아오자, 양쪽 모두 약속을 어김없이 지켰다. 왕과 왕비, 그리고 온 궁정이 카라도크를 위해 간곡히 사정했으나, 낯선 기사는 뜻을 굽히지 않았다. 젊은 기사는 받침대 위에 머리를 올려놓고, 어서 일을 끝내어 이토록 견디기 어려운 기다림 속에 자기를 더 오래 붙들어 두지 말라고 거듭 청했다. 마침내 낯선 기사는 칼의 옆면으로 그를 가볍게 치고는, 일어나라 이르며 한 가지 사실을 밝혔다. 자기가 그의 아버지인 마법사 엘리아우레스이며, 아들의 용기와 약속에 대한 신의를 확인했으니 기꺼이 그를 아들로 인정하겠다는 것이었다.\n\n그러나 마법사들의 호의란 오래가지 못하고 변덕스러운 법이다. 엘리아우레스는 어느 사악한 여인의 영향에 빠지고 말았는데, 그 여인은 카라도크에 대한 앙심을 풀고자 마법사를 꾀어 그의 팔에 뱀 한 마리를 붙여 놓게 했다. 그 뱀은 그 자리에 머무르며 그의 살과 피를 빨아먹었고, 그 어떤 인간의 솜씨로도 그 파충류를 떼어 내지도, 카라도크가 겪는 고통을 덜어 주지도 못했다.\n\n카라도크는 그의 막역한 벗 카도르의 누이이자 콘월 왕의 딸인 기미에르와 약혼한 사이였다. 그들은 그의 가련한 처지를 알게 되자마자, 기미에르가 그를 간호할 수 있도록 카라도크의 성이 있는 낭트로 길을 떠났다. 카라도크는 그들이 온다는 소식을 듣고, 처음에는 기쁨과 사랑의 감정에 젖었다. 그러나 곧 그는 야윈 자기 몸과 고통받는 모습을 보면 기미에르가 정나미가 떨어지지 않을까 두려워하기 시작했다. 이 염려가 너무도 강해진 나머지, 그는 몰래 낭트를 떠나 어느 은둔처에 몸을 숨겼다. 아서 왕 궁정의 기사들이 사방으로 그를 찾아 나섰고, 카도르는 그를 찾기 전에는 결코 이 탐색을 멈추지 않겠노라 맹세했다. 오랜 방랑 끝에 카도르는 거의 해골만 남고 죽음이 임박해 보이는 자기 벗을 은둔처에서 찾아냈다. 다른 모든 구제 방법은 이미 헛되이 시도된 뒤였으므로, 카도르는 마침내 마법사 엘리아우레스를 설득하여 그를 구해 낼 수 있는 유일한 방법을 알아냈다. 출신과 미모에서 그와 대등하고, 자기 자신보다 그를 더 사랑하여, 그를 구하기 위해 똑같은 고통에 스스로를 내맡길 처녀를 찾아야 한다는 것이었다. 그런 다음 그릇 두 개를 마련하되, 하나는 시큼한 포도주로, 다른 하나는 젖으로 채워야 했다. 카라도크는 포도주가 목까지 차오르도록 첫 번째 그릇에 들어가고, 처녀는 다른 그릇에 들어가 가슴을 그릇 가장자리에 드러낸 채, 뱀에게 시들어 버린 희생자의 살을 버리고 이 싱싱하고 먹음직한 먹이로 옮겨 오라고 꾀어야 했다. 두 그릇은 석 자 간격으로 놓아야 했고, 뱀이 한 그릇에서 다른 그릇으로 건너갈 때 한 기사가 그것을 둘로 베어야 했다. 만약 그 일격이 빗나가면 카라도크는 분명 구원받겠지만, 그것은 다만 자기를 위해 나선 아름다운 여인이 똑같이 잔인하고 가망 없는 고통을 겪는 모습을 지켜보기 위함일 뿐이었다. 그 뒤의 일은 쉽게 짐작할 수 있다. 기미에르는 기꺼이 그 위험한 모험에 스스로를 내맡겼고, 카도르는 운 좋은 일격으로 뱀을 죽였다. 카라도크가 그토록 오래 고통받았던 팔은 힘을 되찾았으나 그 모양만은 회복되지 않았으니, 그 까닭으로 그는 카라도크 브리에브라, 곧 줄어든 팔의 카라도크라 불리게 되었다.\n\n카라도크와 기미에르는 다음에 이어지는 발라드 \"소년과 외투\"의 주인공이다.\n\n\"소년과 외투 \"칼라일에 아서 왕이 사셨으니, 비할 데 없는 권세의 군주라, 그곳에 원탁을 두시어, 숱한 기사들이 둘러앉았네.\n\n\"그곳에서 성탄절을 보내셨으니, 흥겨움과 군왕다운 환대 가운데, 그런데 보라! 낯설고 영리한 소년 하나가 그분 앞에 나타났네. \"겉옷과 외투를 이 소년은 걸쳤으니, 브로치와 반지와 장신구로, 더없이 곱게 꾸몄네.\n\n\"비단 띠 하나를 허리에 알맞게 둘렀고, 그리하여 점잖은 예의로 아서 왕께 인사를 올렸네. \"'안녕하소서, 용맹하신 아서 왕이여. 이렇듯 그대 누각에서 잔치를 벌이시고, 또한 그대의 어진 왕비 귀네비어여, 그 아름답고 견줄 데 없는 꽃이여. \"'그대 늠름한 영주와 귀공자들이여, 바라건대 모두 마음에 새기소서, 그대들이 활짝 핀 장미로 여기는 것이 썩어 든 잡초로 드러날까 하노니.'\n\n\"그러더니 곧장 품속에서 작은 지팡이 하나를 꺼냈고, 그것과 더불어 외투 하나도 꺼냈으니, 놀라운 모양과 빛깔의 것이었네. \"이제 받으소서, 아서 왕이여, 이것을 제게서 받으시어, 그대의 어여쁜 왕비께 주소서, 보시는 대로 지어진 이것을.\n\n\"'한 번이라도 허물 있던 아내에게는 이것이 어울리지 않으리.' 그러자 아서 궁정의 기사마다 슬며시 제 부인을 곁눈질했네. \"맨 먼저 귀네비어 왕비가 나왔으니, 외투를 입어 보아야 했네. 이 부인은 새것을 좋아하고, 눈길이 헤픈 이였네.\n\n\"그녀가 외투를 받아 그것으로 온몸을 두르자, 위에서 아래까지 후들후들 떨려 내렸으니, 마치 가위로 갈가리 찢긴 듯했네. \"어느새 너무 길었다가, 또 어느새 너무 짧아졌고, 그녀의 어깨 위에 주름이 잡혔으니, 더없이 보기 흉한 꼴이었네.\n\n\"이제 초록빛이었다가, 이제 붉은빛이더니, 그러더니 온통 검은빛이 되었네. '내 신세를 한탄하노라.' 아서 왕이 말했네. '내 보기엔 그대 정숙하지 못한 듯하구나!' \"그녀는 외투를 벗어 던지고, 더는 머무르려 하지 않았으니, 분노의 화신처럼 사납게 날뛰며, 제 방으로 휙 가 버렸네.\n\n\"그녀는 외투를 지은 못된 직조공을 저주했고, 그것을 이리 가져온 고약한 꼬마를 갑절로 저주했네. \"'차라리 황야에서 살리라, 푸른 숲 나무 아래에서, 여기 비천한 왕이여, 그대 마부들 틈에서 그들과 그대의 놀림감이 되느니.'\n\n\"케이 경이 제 부인을 불러내어, 가까이 오라 명했네. '하지만 부인, 그대 죄가 있거든, 이제 그만두기를 청하오.' \"이 부인은 새치름히 킥킥대며, 당돌한 걸음으로 나아가더니, 그 작은 소년에게 거리낌 없이 두려움 없는 얼굴로 다가갔네.\n\n\"그녀가 외투를 받아 입어 보려 하자, 그것은 어깨까지 오그라들어, 그녀의 등을 온통 드러냈네. \"그러자 아서 궁정에 있던 흥겨운 기사들은 저마다, 그 재미난 구경거리를 보고 비웃고 깔깔대며 조롱했네.\n\n\"그녀는 외투를 벗어 던지고, 더는 당돌하지도 흥겹지도 못한 채, 온통 창백하고 핏기 없는 얼굴로, 제 방으로 슬그머니 가 버렸네. \"그러자 늙은 기사 하나가 나왔으니, 신조를 중얼중얼 외며, 그 작은 소년에게 금화 다섯 닢을 사례로 내밀었네.\n\n\"'또한 성탄절 내내 자두 죽을 그대에게 주리니, 내 고운 부인이 그 외투 속에서 빛나도록 해 준다면 말이다.' \"그의 부인은 성녀인 양했으니, 얌전하고 느린 걸음으로, 엄숙하게 외투를 향해 새침한 얼굴로 나아갔네.\n\n\"그토록 곱고 얇은 그것을 그녀가 받아 들자, 그녀 온몸을 따라 오그라들어, 그 고운 살갗을 드러냈네. \"아! 그녀의 새침함도, 그의 긴 기도도 도움이 되지 못했으니, 그녀에게 걸린 것이라곤 술과 실 한 오라기뿐이었네.\n\n\"그녀는 외투를 벗어 던지고, 두려움과 당혹 속에서, 진홍빛 얼굴로 제 방으로 황급히 가 버렸네. \"크라도크 경이 제 부인을 불러, 가까이 오라 명했네. '이 외투를 차지하시오, 부인, 그리고 여기서 내 체면을 세워 주오. \"'이 외투를 차지하시오, 부인, 이제 이것은 그대 것이 되리니, 내가 그대를 처음 아내로 삼은 뒤로 그대 잘못한 일이 없거든 말이오.'\n\n\"부인은 조용히 얼굴을 붉히며, 정숙한 자태로 나아갔고, 이제 그 놀라운 마법을 시험하고자 용감하게 나아갔네. \"그녀가 외투를 받아 등에 걸치자, 가장자리 둘레가 주름지고 갈라지는 듯했네.\n\n\"'가만있거라.' 그녀가 외쳤네. '오 외투여! 공연히 나를 욕보이지 말라. 내 무슨 잘못이나 허물을 저질렀든 기꺼이 털어놓으리라. \"'한번은 크라도크 경에게 입 맞췄으니, 푸른 숲 나무 아래에서, 한번은 크라도크 경의 입에 입 맞췄으니, 그가 나와 혼인하기 전이었네.' \"그녀가 이렇게 죄를 고백하고, 가장 큰 허물을 털어놓자, 외투는 곧 그녀에게 어울렸으니, 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 곱게 맞았네. \"더없이 화려하고 빛깔 고와, 황금처럼 반짝이며 빛났으니, 아서 궁정의 기사들은 너나없이 그녀를 크게 찬탄했네.\"\n\n이 발라드는 멧돼지 머리와 술 마시는 뿔잔으로써 행해진 같은 종류의 시험을 두 차례 더 들려주는데, 그 두 가지 모두에서 결과는 첫 번째와 똑같이 크라도크 경과 그 부인에게 유리하게 나타났다. 그러고는 다음과 같이 끝맺는다.\n\n\"이리하여 멧돼지 머리와 뿔잔과 외투가 이 어여쁜 부부의 보상이 되었네. 또한 이토록 변함없는 모든 연인들에게, 하느님께서 복을 내려 주시기를.\" —퍼시의 「유물집」. ————\n\n# LAUNCELOT OF THE LAKE\n\n브르타뉴의 왕 반은 아서의 충실한 동맹이었는데, 그의 적 클라우다스의 공격을 받았고, 오랜 전쟁 끝에 단 하나의 요새만을 차지한 처지로 전락하여 그곳에서 적에게 포위되었다. 이 절박한 상황에서 그는 아서의 도움을 청하기로 결심하고, 어느 어두운 밤에 아내 헬렌과 갓난 아들 란셀롯을 데리고 탈출하면서 성을 자신의 집사장에게 맡겼는데, 이 집사장은 곧바로 클라우다스에게 성을 넘겨주고 말았다. 불운한 군주는 도주하던 중에 자신의 성채가 불타오르는 화염을 보았고, 슬픔에 못 이겨 숨을 거두었다. 비참한 처지의 헬렌은 호숫가에 아이를 남겨 둔 채 남편의 마지막 숨결을 받으려 달려갔다가, 돌아와 보니 어린 란셀롯이 어느 님프의 품에 안겨 있는 것을 발견했다. 그 님프는 왕비가 다가오자 아이를 안은 채 호수 속으로 몸을 던졌다. 이 님프는 마법사 멀린의 연인 비비안으로, 흔히 〈호수의 여인〉이라는 이름으로 더 잘 알려진 인물이었다. 란셀롯이라는 호칭은 이 마법사의 궁정에서 자랐다는 데서 비롯되었는데, 그 궁전은 진짜 호수가 아니라 아프리카의 여행자를 속이는 신기루처럼 가상의 호수 한가운데에 자리하고 있었고, 그 사람을 홀리는 듯한 호수의 모습이 그녀가 거처하는 곳의 방벽 노릇을 했다. 그녀는 이곳에서 홀로 지낸 것이 아니라, 수많은 시종들과 기사와 처녀들로 이루어진 화려한 궁정 한가운데에서 살았다.\n\n왕비는 이중의 상실을 겪은 뒤 어느 수녀원으로 물러났는데, 그곳에서 보호트의 미망인과 합류하게 되었다. 이 선량한 왕 보호트가 자신의 동생 반의 죽음 소식을 듣고 슬픔에 못 이겨 세상을 떠났기 때문이다. 그의 두 아들 라이오넬과 보호트는 충실한 한 기사에 의해 구출되었고, 그레이하운드의 모습이 되어 호수의 궁전에 다다랐는데, 그곳에서 본래의 모습을 되찾은 뒤 사촌 란셀롯과 함께 양육되었다.\n\n요정은 자신의 제자가 열여덟 살이 되었을 때, 기사 서임의 영예를 청하고자 그를 아서의 궁정으로 데려갔다. 그리고 이 젊은 후보자가 처음 모습을 드러내자, 그의 용기와 무예 솜씨에 못지않은 외모의 우아함이 귀네비어의 마음에 즉각적이고도 지울 수 없는 인상을 남겼고, 한편 그녀의 매력은 그에게 그에 못지않게 뜨겁고 한결같은 열정을 불러일으켰다. 두 연인의 상호 애착은 그때부터 아서의 모든 이야기에 걸쳐 영향을 미쳤다. 귀네비어를 위하여 란셀롯은 노섬벌랜드를 정복했고, 변경의 왕 갈레호를 물리쳤는데, 갈레호는 훗날 그의 가장 충실한 친구이자 동맹이 되었다. 또한 란셀롯은 수없이 많은 격전에 몸을 던졌고, 수많은 포로 무리를 자신의 군주의 발 앞에 데려왔다.\n\n# SIR LAUNCELOT\n\n아서 왕이 로마에서 잉글랜드로 돌아온 뒤, 원탁의 모든 기사들이 그에게 모여들어 수많은 마상 시합과 토너먼트를 열었다. 그 가운데서도 특히 〈호수의〉 란셀롯 경은 모든 토너먼트와 마상 시합, 그리고 생사를 건 무예 겨룸에서 다른 모든 기사들을 능가했고, 배반이나 마법에 의하지 않고는 결코 패하는 일이 없었다. 그리하여 그의 명예는 놀랍도록 높아졌고, 그 까닭에 귀네비어 왕비는 다른 모든 기사들보다 그를 크게 총애했다. 그리고 그 역시 다른 모든 귀부인들보다 왕비를 사랑했음이 분명했다. 그는 왕비를 위해 수많은 무예를 펼쳤고, 자신의 고귀한 기사도로써 그녀를 위험에서 구해 냈다. 이렇게 란셀롯 경은 오랫동안 유희와 오락 속에서 쉬었으나, 이윽고 낯선 모험 속에서 자신을 시험해 보고자 마음먹었다. 그리하여 그는 조카인 라이오넬 경에게 채비를 갖추라 일렀다. \"우리 둘이 모험을 찾아 나설 것이다.\" 그래서 그들은 말에 올라 완전 무장을 갖추고 한 숲속으로 들어갔으며, 거기서 깊은 평원으로 나아갔다. 정오 무렵 날씨가 무더웠고, 란셀롯 경은 몹시 잠이 오고 싶었다. 그때 라이오넬 경이 산울타리 옆에 서 있는 큰 사과나무 한 그루를 발견하고 말했다. \"형님, 저기 좋은 그늘이 있습니다. 저기서 우리와 우리의 말이 쉴 수 있겠습니다.\" \"옳은 말이다.\" 란셀롯 경이 대답했다. 그래서 그들은 그곳에 내려섰고, 란셀롯 경은 투구를 베개 삼아 머리에 받치고 누웠으며, 곧 아주 깊이 잠들었다. 그리고 라이오넬 경은 그가 자는 동안 깨어 있었다. 이윽고 기사 셋이 있는 힘껏 말을 몰아 달려왔고, 그 뒤를 단 한 명의 기사가 쫓아왔다. 라이오넬 경은 그토록 거대한 기사를 일찍이 본 적이 없다고 생각했다. 그러더니 잠시 후 이 거대한 기사가 세 기사 중 하나를 따라잡아 가격하니, 그 기사가 땅에 나뒹굴었다. 그러고는 두 번째 기사에게 달려가 가격했고, 세 번째 기사에게도 똑같이 했다. 그런 다음 그는 말에서 내려 세 기사를 모두 그들 자신의 고삐로 단단히 묶었다. 라이오넬 경은 그가 이렇게 하는 것을 보고 그를 시험해 보고자 했고, 란셀롯 경을 깨우지 않으려고 조용히 채비를 갖춘 다음 그 힘센 기사를 뒤쫓아 가서 돌아서라고 명했다. 그러자 상대는 라이오넬 경을 어찌나 세게 가격했던지, 말과 사람이 모두 땅에 쓰러지고 말았다. 그러고는 그가 말에서 내려 라이오넬 경을 묶어 자신의 말 위에 가로질러 얹었다. 이렇게 그는 네 사람 모두를 그렇게 처치한 뒤, 그들을 데리고 자신의 성으로 떠나갔다. 그리고 그곳에 다다르자 그들을 깊은 감옥에 가두었는데, 그 안에는 큰 고통에 처한 다른 기사들도 많았다.\n\n자, 란셀롯 경이 사과나무 아래에서 잠들어 있는 동안, 그 곁으로 신분이 높은 네 왕비가 지나갔다. 그리고 무더위가 그들을 괴롭히지 않도록 네 기사가 그들 주위를 따라 달리며 네 자루의 창에 초록색 비단 천을 걸어 그들과 햇빛 사이를 가렸다. 그리고 왕비들은 네 마리의 흰 노새를 타고 갔다.\n\n이렇게 말을 타고 가던 중에 그들은 곁에서 큰 말 한 마리가 사납게 우는 소리를 들었다. 그래서 사과나무 아래에 완전 무장을 한 채 잠들어 있는 한 기사를 알아차렸다. 왕비들이 그 얼굴을 들여다보니, 그가 란셀롯 경임을 알아보았다. 그러자 그들은 그 기사를 두고 다투기 시작했고, 저마다 자신이 그를 연인으로 삼겠다고 말했다. \"다투지 맙시다.\" 아서 왕의 누이인 모르가나 르 페이가 말했다. \"내가 그에게 마법을 걸어 여섯 시간 동안 깨어나지 못하게 할 것이니, 우리가 그를 내 성으로 데려갑시다. 그런 다음 그가 확실히 내 손안에 들어왔을 때 마법을 풀어 줄 것이고, 그러면 그가 우리 중에 누구를 연인으로 삼을지 직접 고르도록 합시다.\" 그리하여 란셀롯 경에게 마법이 걸렸다. 그런 다음 그들은 그를 방패 위에 눕히고 두 기사 사이에 끼워 말에 실어 나르더니, 성으로 데려가 한 방에 눕혔고, 밤이 되자 그에게 저녁 식사를 보냈다.\n\n그리고 다음 날 아침 일찍 그 네 왕비가 화려하게 차려입고 와서 그에게 잘 잤느냐고 인사했고, 그도 그들에게 인사를 되돌렸다. \"기사여,\" 그들이 말했다. \"그대는 우리의 포로임을 알아 두어야 한다. 우리는 그대가 누구인지 잘 안다. 그대는 〈호수의〉 란셀롯 경, 반 왕의 아들이며, 살아 있는 기사 가운데 가장 고귀한 기사이다. 또 우리는 어떤 귀부인도 그대의 사랑을 차지할 수 없으며 오직 한 사람, 곧 귀네비어 왕비만이 그러할 수 있다는 것도 잘 안다. 그러나 이제 그대는 그녀를 영원히 잃을 것이고, 그녀 또한 그대를 잃을 것이다. 그러니 이제 그대는 우리 중에 하나를 골라야 마땅하다. 나는 모르가나 르 페이 왕비이고, 여기 이분은 북웨일스의 왕비, 또 이분은 동방의 왕비, 그리고 이분은 〈섬들〉의 왕비이다. 자, 이제 우리 중에 그대가 갖고자 하는 자를 하나 고르라. 만일 고르지 않는다면, 그대는 이 감옥에서 죽게 될 것이다.\" \"이것은 가혹한 일입니다.\" 란셀롯 경이 말했다. \"내가 죽거나, 아니면 당신들 중 하나를 골라야 하다니. 그러나 나는 거짓된 마법사인 당신들 중 하나를 정부로 삼느니, 차라리 이 감옥에서 명예롭게 죽는 편이 낫겠습니다.\" \"그래,\" 왕비들이 말했다. \"이것이 그대의 대답인가, 우리를 거절하겠다는 것인가?\" \"그렇소, 내 목숨을 걸고 그러하오.\" 란셀롯 경이 말했다. 그러자 그들은 크게 슬퍼하며 물러갔다.\n\n그러더니 정오에 한 처녀가 그에게 식사를 가지고 와서 물었다. \"기분이 어떠신지요?\" \"정말로, 아름다운 처녀여,\" 란셀롯 경이 말했다. \"이보다 나쁠 수가 없소.\" \"기사님,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"제 말을 따르신다면, 제가 이 곤경에서 벗어나도록 도와 드리겠습니다. 다음 화요일에 제 아버님을 도와 주시겠다고 약속하신다면 말입니다. 아버님께서 북웨일스의 왕과 마상 시합을 벌이기로 하셨는데, 지난 화요일에 아버님께서 패하셨거든요.\" \"아름다운 아가씨여,\" 란셀롯 경이 말했다. \"그대 아버님의 이름이 무엇인지 말해 주오. 그러면 대답해 주겠소.\" \"기사님,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"제 아버님은 바그데마구스 왕이십니다.\" \"내 그분을 잘 아오.\" 란셀롯 경이 말했다. \"고귀한 왕이자 훌륭한 기사이지. 내 몸의 신의를 걸고, 그날 그대 아버님과 그대를 위해 봉사할 채비를 갖추겠소.\"\n\n그리하여 그녀는 물러갔고, 다음 날 아침 일찍 와서 그가 준비되어 있는 것을 보고는, 열두 개의 자물쇠를 풀어 그를 데리고 나와 그의 말이 있는 곳으로 안내했다. 그는 가뿐히 말에 안장을 얹고 길을 떠났다.\n\n그리고 다음 화요일에 그는 마상 시합이 열릴 작은 숲에 다다랐다. 그곳에는 영주들과 귀부인들이 지켜보며 상을 줄 수 있도록 관람대와 자리가 마련되어 있었다. 이윽고 북웨일스의 왕이 백육십 명의 투구를 거느리고 들판으로 들어왔고, 바그데마구스 왕은 팔십 명의 투구를 거느리고 들어왔다. 그러더니 그들은 창을 겨누고 큰 충돌과 함께 맞부딪쳤는데, 첫 번째 격돌에서 바그데마구스 왕 편의 열두 명과 북웨일스 왕 편의 여섯 명이 나가떨어졌고, 바그데마구스 왕 편이 더 불리해졌다.\n\n그때 〈호수의〉 란셀롯 경이 창을 들고 가장 빽빽한 무리 한가운데로 돌진했다. 그는 손을 멈추기 전에 다섯 기사를 쳐서 쓰러뜨렸다. 그리고 북웨일스의 왕을 쳐서 쓰러뜨렸는데, 왕은 그 낙마로 허벅지가 부러졌다. 그러자 북웨일스 왕의 기사들은 더 이상 시합하려 하지 않았고, 그리하여 승리의 영예는 바그데마구스 왕에게 돌아갔다.\n\n그리고 란셀롯 경은 바그데마구스 왕과 함께 그의 성으로 갔다. 그곳에서 그는 왕과 그의 딸 모두에게서 더없이 융숭한 환대를 받았다. 그리고 이튿날 아침 그는 작별을 고하면서, 자신이 잠든 사이 떠나가 버린 형제 라이오넬 경을 찾아 나서겠다고 왕에게 말했다. 그리하여 그는 떠났고, 모험을 따라가다가 그가 잠든 채 붙잡혔던 바로 그 숲에 다다랐다. 그리고 큰길에서 흰 승용마를 탄 한 처녀를 만났고, 그들은 서로 인사를 나누었다. \"아름다운 처녀여,\" 란셀롯 경이 말했다. \"이 고장에 무슨 모험거리가 있는지 아오?\" \"기사님,\" 처녀가 말했다. \"감히 뒤쫓을 용기가 있으시다면, 여기 가까이에 모험거리가 있습니다.\" \"내가 어찌 모험을 시험해 보지 않겠소?\" 란셀롯 경이 말했다. \"바로 그 까닭에 내가 여기에 왔으니 말이오.\" \"기사님,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이 근처에 한 기사가 사는데, 제가 아는 어떤 사람도 그를 당해 내지 못합니다. 기사님이 그를 능가하지 못하신다면 말이지요. 그의 이름은 투르킨 경이며, 제가 알기로 그는 아서 왕의 철천지원수입니다. 그는 자신의 손으로 직접 사로잡은 아서 궁정의 훌륭한 기사 예순 명 남짓을 자기 감옥에 가두어 두고 있습니다.\" \"처녀여,\" 란셀롯이 말했다. \"부디 나를 그 기사에게로 데려다 주오.\" 그러자 그녀가 그에게 일러 주었다. \"여기서 일 마일 안쪽에 그의 성이 있고, 그 옆 왼편에는 말들이 물을 마시는 여울이 있으며, 그 여울 너머에 아름다운 나무 한 그루가 자라고 있습니다. 그 나무에는 예전에 훌륭한 기사들이 휘둘렀으나 지금은 포로가 된 자들의 방패가 여럿 걸려 있고, 또 그 나무에는 구리와 놋쇠로 된 대야가 하나 걸려 있는데, 그 대야를 두드리시면 소식을 듣게 되실 겁니다.\" 그리하여 란셀롯 경은 떠나서 처녀가 일러 준 대로 말을 달렸고, 이내 여울과 방패들과 대야가 걸린 나무에 다다랐다. 그리고 방패들 가운데서 그는 라이오넬 경과 헥터 경의 방패를, 그 밖에도 그가 아는 여러 기사들의 방패를 보았다.\n\n그러자 란셀롯 경은 창 자루로 대야를 두드렸다. 오랫동안 그렇게 했으나 아무도 보이지 않았다. 그러다 마침내 한 거대한 기사가 말 한 마리를 앞세우고 몰아오는 것을 알아차렸는데, 그 말 위에는 무장한 기사 하나가 묶인 채 가로놓여 있었다. 그들이 가까이 다가오자 란셀롯 경은 그 사로잡힌 기사가 누구인지 알 것 같다고 생각했다. 이윽고 란셀롯 경은 그가 가웨인 경의 형제이며 원탁의 기사인 가헤리스 경임을 알아보았다. \"자, 훌륭한 기사여,\" 란셀롯 경이 말했다. \"그 부상당한 기사를 말에서 내려 잠시 쉬게 하고, 우리 둘이 힘을 겨루어 보세. 내가 듣기로 그대는 원탁의 기사들에게 큰 모욕과 수치를 안겨 주었다 하니, 그러므로 이제 그대 자신을 방어하라.\" \"그대가 원탁의 일원이라면,\" 투르킨 경이 말했다. \"내 그대와 그대의 모든 동료에게 도전하노라.\" \"그것은 지나친 말이다.\" 란셀롯 경이 말했다.\n\n그러자 그들은 창을 받침대에 걸고, 있는 힘껏 달릴 수 있는 만큼 말을 몰아 맞부딪쳤다. 그리고 저마다 상대의 방패 한가운데를 가격하니, 그들의 말이 밑에 깔려 쓰러졌고, 두 기사 모두 비틀거렸다. 그러나 말에서 몸을 빼내자마자 그들은 칼을 뽑아 들고 맹렬히 맞붙어 서로에게 강한 일격을 수없이 가했는데, 방패도 갑옷도 그 일격을 견뎌 내지 못했다. 그리하여 잠시 후 둘 다 끔찍한 상처를 입고 심하게 피를 흘렸다. 그러다 마침내 둘 다 숨이 차서 칼에 기댄 채 서 있었다. \"자, 친구여,\" 투르킨 경이 말했다. \"그대는 내가 일찍이 만난 사람 가운데 가장 강인하고 숨이 가장 긴 자이다. 그러니 그대가 내가 다른 모든 기사보다도 미워하는 그 기사, 곧 내 형제 카라도스 경을 죽인 그 기사만 아니라면, 내 기꺼이 그대와 화해할 것이며, 그대에 대한 호의로 내가 가둔 모든 포로를 풀어 주겠노라.\"\n\n\"그대가 다른 누구보다도 그토록 미워하는 그 기사가 누구인가?\" \"정말로,\" 투르킨 경이 말했다. \"그의 이름은 〈호수의〉 란셀롯 경이다.\" \"내가 바로 〈호수의〉 란셀롯 경, 벤위크의 반 왕의 아들이며, 진정한 원탁의 기사이다. 자, 이제 내 그대에게 도전하노니, 최선을 다하라.\" \"아!\" 투르킨 경이 말했다. \"란셀롯, 그대야말로 일찍이 어떤 기사보다도 내게 가장 반가운 자로다. 우리 둘 중 하나가 죽기 전에는 결코 헤어지지 못할 것이니.\" 그러더니 그들은 두 마리 성난 황소처럼 맞부딪쳐, 칼과 방패를 휘두르고 내리치며 때로는 마치 곤두박질치듯 쓰러지기도 했다. 이렇게 그들은 두 시간 넘도록 싸웠고, 마침내 그들이 싸우던 땅이 온통 피로 검붉게 물들었다.\n\n그러다 끝내 투르킨 경이 몹시 기진맥진하여 다소 뒤로 물러서면서 지친 나머지 방패를 한껏 낮추어 들었다. 란셀롯 경이 그것을 눈치채고는 사자처럼 사납게 그에게 달려들어 그의 투구 면갑을 붙잡고 무릎을 꿇리며 끌어내렸다. 그러고는 투구를 벗겨 내고 그의 목을 베어 두 동강 냈다.\n\n가헤리스 경은 투르킨 경이 죽임당한 것을 보고 말했다. \"훌륭하신 분이여, 부디 당신의 이름을 알려 주십시오. 오늘 제가 보기에 당신은 세상에서 가장 훌륭한 기사이십니다. 오늘 당신은 제 눈앞에서, 당신을 빼고는 제가 일찍이 본 적 없는 가장 강하고 가장 훌륭한 기사를 죽이셨으니 말입니다.\" \"기사여, 내 이름은 〈호수의〉 란셀롯이오. 아서 왕을 위해서, 또 특히 그대의 친형제인 가웨인 경을 위해서 마땅히 그대를 도와야 하지요. 자, 부디 그대는 저 성으로 들어가 거기 있는 모든 포로를 풀어 주오. 분명 그곳에서 원탁의 기사들을 많이 발견하게 될 것이며, 특히 내 형제 라이오넬 경도 있을 것이오. 부디 나를 대신해 그들 모두에게 인사를 전해 주고, 그곳에서 찾아낸 물건은 무엇이든 가져가라고 내가 일렀다고 전해 주오. 그리고 내 형제에게는 궁정으로 가서 그곳에서 나를 기다리라고 전해 주오. 나는 성령강림절 축일까지 그곳에 가 있을 생각이오. 그러나 지금은 멈출 수 없으니, 처리해야 할 모험이 있기 때문이오.\" 그리하여 그는 떠났고, 가헤리스 경은 성으로 말을 몰고 들어가 문지기에게서 열쇠를 받아 서둘러 감옥 문을 열고 모든 포로를 풀어 주었다. 그곳에는 케이 경, 브랜델스 경, 갈린드 경, 브라이언 경, 알리듀크 경, 〈습지의〉 헥터 경, 그리고 라이오넬 경과 그 밖에 여러 기사가 있었다. 그리고 그들은 가헤리스 경을 보자 모두 그에게 감사했는데, 그가 부상을 입은 것을 보고 그가 투르킨 경을 죽인 줄로 여겼기 때문이다. \"그렇지 않소.\" 가헤리스 경이 말했다. \"그를 죽인 것은 란셀롯 경이오. 참으로 영예롭게 말이오. 내 두 눈으로 똑똑히 보았소.\"\n\n란셀롯 경은 밤이 되도록 말을 달려 어느 아름다운 성에 다다랐고, 그 안에서 한 늙은 귀부인을 만났는데, 그녀가 기꺼이 그를 묵게 해 주었으며, 그곳에서 그와 그의 말은 융숭한 대접을 받았다. 그리고 때가 되자 주인은 그를 문 위의 아름다운 방으로 데려가 잠자리에 들게 했다. 그러자 란셀롯 경은 무장을 풀어 곁에 갑옷을 두고 잠자리에 들었으며, 이내 잠이 들었다. 그리고 얼마 지나지 않아 누군가가 말을 타고 와서 몹시 다급하게 문을 두드렸다. 란셀롯 경이 이 소리를 듣고 일어나 창밖을 내다보니, 달빛 아래 세 기사가 한 사람을 뒤쫓으며 달려오는 것이 보였고, 셋 모두 칼로 그를 내리쳤으며, 그 한 기사는 기사답게 다시 그들을 향해 돌아서서 자신을 방어하고 있었다. \"정말로,\" 란셀롯 경이 말했다. \"저기 저 한 기사를 내가 도와야겠다. 세 기사가 한 사람을 치는 꼴은 보기에 수치스러우니.\" 그러고는 갑옷을 챙겨 시트를 타고 창밖으로 내려가 네 기사가 있는 곳에 이르렀다. 그리고 큰 소리로 말했다. \"기사들아, 그 기사와 싸우는 것을 멈추고 나를 향해 돌아서라.\" 그러자 기사들은 케이 경을 내버려 두었는데, 그가 바로 그들이 공격하던 자였다. 그러고는 란셀롯 경에게로 돌아서서 그에게 강한 일격을 수없이 날리며 사방에서 그를 공격했다. 그때 케이 경이 란셀롯 경을 도우려 나서자, 그가 말했다. \"아니오, 기사여, 그대의 도움은 필요 없소. 저들은 나에게 맡겨 두시오.\" 그래서 케이 경은 그가 뜻대로 하도록 두고 한쪽에 비켜섰다. 그리고 여섯 번의 일격 안에 란셀롯 경은 그들을 쳐서 쓰러뜨렸다.\n\n그러자 그들 모두가 외쳤다. \"기사여, 우리가 당신께 항복합니다.\" \"그것에 대해서라면,\" 란셀롯 경이 말했다. \"나는 그대들이 내게 항복하는 것을 받지 않겠다. 만일 그대들이 집사장 케이 경에게 항복하겠다면, 내 그대들의 목숨을 살려 주겠지만, 그렇지 않으면 안 된다.\" \"훌륭한 기사여,\" 그러자 그들이 말했다. \"당신이 명하시는 대로 하겠습니다.\" \"그러면 그대들은,\" 란셀롯 경이 말했다. \"다음 성령강림절에 아서 왕의 궁정으로 가서 귀네비어 왕비께 항복하고, 케이 경이 그대들을 그녀의 포로로 그곳에 보냈다고 아뢰라.\" \"기사여,\" 그들이 말했다. \"우리 몸의 신의를 걸고 그리하겠습니다.\" 그러고는 기사마다 자신의 칼에 대고 맹세했다. 그리하여 란셀롯 경은 그들이 떠나가도록 허락했다.\n\n이튿날 아침 란셀롯 경은 일찍 일어나, 잠든 케이 경을 남겨 둔 채 케이 경의 갑옷과 방패를 가져다 자신이 무장하고, 마구간으로 가서 그의 말을 끌어내어 길을 떠났다. 그러고 얼마 뒤 케이 경이 일어나 보니 란셀롯 경이 보이지 않았다. 그리고 그가 자신의 갑옷과 말을 가져간 것을 알아차렸다. \"자, 내 신의를 걸고 잘 알겠노라.\" 케이 경이 말했다. \"그가 아서 왕의 기사들 중 몇몇을 곤란하게 만들겠구나. 그들이 그를 나로 여기고 대담하게 그와 맞서려 할 테니. 하지만 그의 갑옷 덕분에 나는 분명 평온히 말을 달릴 수 있겠지.\" 그러고서 케이 경은 주인에게 감사하고 떠났다.\n\n란셀롯 경은 깊은 숲속으로 말을 달리다가, 한 떡갈나무 아래에서 네 기사를 보았는데, 그들은 아서의 궁정 사람들이었다. 그곳에는 〈갈망하는 자〉 사그라모르 경, 〈습지의〉 헥터 경, 가웨인 경, 그리고 우와인 경이 있었다. 그들은 란셀롯 경을 알아보고는 그의 무구로 미루어 케이 경이라고 판단했다. \"자, 내 신의를 걸고,\" 사그라모르 경이 말했다. \"내가 케이 경의 무력을 시험해 보겠소.\" 그러고는 손에 창을 들고 란셀롯 경을 향해 달려왔다. 그러자 란셀롯 경은 그를 향해 창을 겨누어 사그라모르 경을 어찌나 세게 가격했던지, 말과 사람이 모두 땅에 나뒹굴었다. 그때 헥터 경이 말했다. \"이제 내가 그에게 무엇을 할 수 있는지 보시오.\" 그러나 그는 사그라모르 경보다 더 험한 꼴을 당했으니, 란셀롯 경의 창이 그의 어깨를 꿰뚫어 그를 말에서 땅으로 떨어뜨렸기 때문이다. \"내 신의를 걸고,\" 우와인 경이 말했다. \"저기 저자는 강한 기사로구나. 그가 케이 경을 죽이고 그의 갑옷을 빼앗은 것이 아닐까 두렵다.\" 그러고는 우와인 경이 손에 창을 들고 란셀롯 경을 향해 말을 몰았고, 란셀롯 경은 평원에서 그를 맞아 어찌나 세게 후려쳤던지, 그가 비틀거리며 자신이 어디에 있는지조차 알지 못했다. \"이제 내 잘 알겠다.\" 가웨인 경이 말했다. \"내가 저 기사와 맞서야 하겠구나.\" 그러고는 방패를 고쳐 잡고 손에 좋은 창을 들었는데, 란셀롯 경은 그를 잘 알아보았다. 그러더니 그들은 있는 힘껏 말을 달리게 했고, 두 기사는 저마다 상대의 방패 한가운데를 가격했다. 그러나 가웨인 경의 창은 부러졌고, 란셀롯 경이 어찌나 세게 그에게 돌진했던지 그의 말이 뒤로 나자빠졌다. 그러자 란셀롯 경은 혼자 미소 지으며 지나가면서 말했다. \"이 창을 만든 자에게 행운이 있기를. 이보다 더 좋은 창이 내 손에 들어온 적이 없으니.\" 그러자 네 기사가 저마다 서로에게 다가가 서로를 위로했다. \"이 모험을 어찌 생각하오?\" 가웨인 경이 말했다. \"창 하나가 우리 넷을 모두 쓰러뜨렸으니 말이오.\" \"내 목을 걸고 그자는 란셀롯 경이오.\" 헥터 경이 말했다. \"그가 말 타는 모습을 보고 알았소.\"\n\n그리고 란셀롯 경은 낯선 여러 고장을 거쳐 말을 달리다가, 우연히 어느 아름다운 성에 다다랐다. 그가 성을 지나쳐 가는데 종 두 개가 울리는 소리가 들리는 듯했다. 그러더니 매 한 마리가 그의 머리 위로 날아 높은 느릅나무를 향해 가는 것을 알아차렸다. 매는 발에 긴 끈을 매단 채 느릅나무로 날아가 가지에 앉으려 했는데, 그 끈이 나뭇가지에 엉켜 버렸다. 매가 다시 날아오르려 했을 때 그것은 다리가 단단히 걸려 매달리고 말았고, 란셀롯 경은 그것이 매달려 있는 것을 보고 그 아름다운 매가 엉켜 있는 것을 바라보며 가엾이 여겼다. 그때 한 귀부인이 성에서 나와 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"오 란셀롯, 란셀롯, 그대가 모든 기사의 꽃이거든, 내 매를 되찾도록 도와 주오. 내 매를 잃으면 내 주인이 나를 죽일 것이오. 그분은 성미가 어찌나 급하신지.\" \"그대 주인의 이름이 무엇이오?\" 란셀롯 경이 말했다. \"그분의 이름은 펠롯 경으로, 북웨일스의 왕에게 속한 기사입니다.\" \"좋소, 아름다운 부인이여, 그대가 내 이름을 알고 기사도를 들어 도와 달라 청하니, 내 그대의 매를 되찾기 위해 할 수 있는 일을 하겠소. 허나 사실 나는 나무를 잘 오르지 못하고, 이 나무는 무척 높은 데다 나를 도울 가지도 거의 없구려.\" 그러고서 란셀롯 경은 말에서 내려 자신의 말을 나무에 매어 두고, 부인에게 자신의 무장을 풀어 달라고 청했다. 그리고 무장을 풀자 윗옷마저 벗어 던지고는, 온 힘을 다하여 매가 있는 곳까지 기어올라 끈을 썩은 가지에 묶어 그 가지째로 매를 아래로 던졌다. 그러자 부인은 매를 손에 받아 들었다. 그때 갑자기 그녀의 남편이 완전 무장을 한 채 손에 칼을 빼 들고 성에서 나와 말했다. \"오 기사 란셀롯, 이제 내가 바라던 대로 그대를 손에 넣었노라.\" 그러고는 그를 죽이려고 나무 밑동에 버티고 섰다. \"아, 부인!\" 란셀롯 경이 말했다. \"어찌하여 나를 배신했소?\" \"그녀는,\" 펠롯 경이 말했다. \"내가 명한 대로 했을 뿐이다. 그러므로 다른 길은 없으니, 그대의 시각이 다하였고, 그대는 죽어야 한다.\" \"그것은 그대에게 수치가 될 일이오.\" 란셀롯 경이 말했다. \"무장한 기사가 맨몸의 사람을 배반으로 죽이다니.\" \"그대에게 다른 자비는 없다.\" 펠롯 경이 말했다. \"그러니 할 수 있거든 스스로를 구하라.\" \"아아!\" 란셀롯 경이 말했다. \"기사가 무기 없이 죽어야 하다니!\" 그러고는 위아래로 눈길을 돌렸다. 그리고 머리 위에 잎이 없는 큰 가지 하나를 보고 그것을 줄기에서 꺾어 냈다. 그런 다음 더 아래로 내려와 자신의 말이 어디에 서 있는지 살피더니, 갑자기 기사로부터 먼 쪽으로 말을 사이에 두고 뛰어내렸다. 그러자 펠롯 경이 그를 죽이려고 맹렬히 내리쳤다. 그러나 란셀롯 경은 그 큰 가지로 일격을 막아 내고, 그것으로 펠롯 경의 머리 옆을 후려쳐 그가 정신을 잃고 땅에 쓰러지게 했다. 그러고는 란셀롯 경이 그의 손에서 칼을 빼앗아 그의 목을 몸에서 베어 냈다. 그러자 부인이 말했다. \"아아! 어찌하여 내 남편을 죽였소?\" \"내 탓이 아니오.\" 란셀롯 경이 말했다. \"그대들이 거짓으로 나를 죽이려 했으니, 이제 그것이 그대들 자신에게 떨어진 것이오.\" 그러고서 란셀롯 경은 자신의 갑옷을 모두 챙겨 서둘러 몸에 둘렀는데, 기사의 성이 그토록 가까이 있어 더 많은 무리가 몰려올까 두려웠기 때문이다. 그리고 되도록 빨리 말을 끌어내어 떠났고, 그 모험에서 벗어난 것을 하느님께 감사했다.\n\n그리고 성령강림절 축일 이틀 전에 란셀롯 경은 집으로 돌아왔다. 그리고 왕과 온 궁정이 그의 귀환을 더없이 기뻐했다. 그리고 가웨인 경, 우와인 경, 사그라모르 경, 그리고 〈습지의〉 헥터 경은 케이 경의 갑옷을 입은 란셀롯 경을 보고는, 단 하나의 창으로 자신들 모두를 쓰러뜨린 자가 바로 그였음을 잘 알게 되었다. 그러자 그들 사이에 웃음과 즐거움이 넘쳐흘렀다. 그리고 투르킨 경이 포로로 잡았던 모든 기사들이 차례차례 돌아와 모두가 란셀롯 경을 기리고 떠받들었다. 그때 가헤리스 경이 말했다. \"내가 그 싸움을 처음부터 끝까지 보았소.\" 그러고는 아서 왕에게 그 모든 일이 어떠했는지 이야기했다. 그러자 케이 경이 왕에게, 란셀롯 경이 어떻게 자신을 구해 주었으며, 어떻게 \"그가 기사들로 하여금 그가 아니라 내게 항복하게 했는지\" 이야기했다. 그리고 그곳에 그 셋이 모두 있어 그 모든 것을 확인해 주었다. \"그리고 내 신의를 걸고,\" 케이 경이 말했다. \"란셀롯 경이 내 갑옷을 가져가고 자기 것을 남겨 둔 덕분에 나는 평온히 말을 달렸고, 아무도 나와 겨루려 하지 않았소.\"\n\n그리하여 그 무렵 란셀롯 경은 세상의 어느 기사보다도 큰 명성을 얻었고, 높은 자든 낮은 자든 그를 더없이 떠받들었다.\n\n# THE ADVENTURE OF THE CART\n\n때는 5월이었다. 왕비 귀네비어는 원탁의 기사들을 불러 모아, 다음 날 아침 일찍 웨스트민스터 곁의 숲과 들판으로 오월맞이 나들이를 가겠다고 알렸다. \"그리고 그대들에게 당부하노니, 누구든 좋은 말을 갖추고, 모두 비단이든 천이든 초록빛 옷을 차려입도록 하라. 나는 시녀 열 명을 데려갈 터이니, 기사마다 한 사람의 시녀를 뒤에 태우고, 또 기사마다 종자 한 명과 향사 두 명을 거느리되, 모두 좋은 말을 갖추도록 하라.\"\n\n\"어느 아침 온 궁정 사람들이 이렇게 되었으니, 초록빛 옷을 차려입고, 오월을 흉내 낸 깃털을 꽂은 채, 그들의 관례대로 오월맞이를 다녀온 것이었다.\" ─「귀네비어」.\n\n그리하여 그들은 채비를 갖추었다. 함께 나선 기사들의 이름은 이러하였다. 가령(家令) 케이 경, 아그리바인 경, 브랜다일스 경, 사그라모르 르 데시루스 경, 도디나스 르 사바주 경, 오자나 경, 라디나스 경, 인드의 페르상 경, 아이언사이드 경, 그리고 펠레아스 경이었다. 이 열 명의 기사가 더없이 산뜻한 차림으로 채비를 갖추어 왕비와 함께 말을 달리려 하였다. 그리하여 이튿날 아침 그들은 왕비와 더불어 말에 올라, 마음 내키는 대로 숲과 풀밭을 누비며 큰 기쁨과 즐거움 속에 오월맞이를 다녔다. 그런데 말레아간스라는 이름의 기사가 있었으니, 그는 브래더매거스 왕의 아들로, 왕비 귀네비어를 지극히 사모하였고, 그 마음이 여러 해에 걸쳐 오래도록 이어져 왔다. 이 기사 말레아간스 경은 왕비의 계획을 알게 되었으며, 왕비가 오월맞이를 위해 모두 초록빛으로 차려입은 열 명의 고귀한 기사 외에는 무장한 자들을 거느리지 않았다는 것도 알게 되었다. 그리하여 그는 무장한 자 스무 명과 궁수 백 명을 갖추어, 왕비와 그 기사들을 사로잡으려 하였다.\n\n\"즐거운 오월의 한 달 가운데, 날이 막 밝아 오는 어느 아침, 한 무리의 시녀들과 노닐며, 왕비는 참으로 오월맞이를 나섰도다.\" ─「옛 노래」.\n\n그리하여 왕비가 오월맞이를 마치고, 모두가 가장 보기 좋고 산뜻한 모양으로 풀과 이끼와 꽃으로 단장을 하였을 바로 그때, 한 숲에서 말레아간스 경이 잘 무장한 백예순 명의 부하를 거느리고 나타나, 왕비와 그 기사들에게 포로로 항복하라고 명하였다. \"반역의 기사여,\" 하고 왕비 귀네비어가 말하였다. \"그대는 무엇을 하려는 것인가? 스스로 욕되게 하려는가? 그대가 왕의 아들이요 원탁의 기사임을, 그리고 모든 기사도와 그대 자신을 욕보이려 하고 있음을 생각해 보라.\" \"어찌 되든 좋소,\" 하고 말레아간스 경이 말하였다. \"잘 아시오, 부인, 나는 여러 해 동안 그대를 사모해 왔으나 이제껏 지금처럼 그대를 손에 넣을 좋은 기회를 얻지 못하였소. 그러니 지금 마주한 그대로 그대를 데려가겠소.\" 그러자 원탁의 열 기사가 칼을 빼어 들었고, 상대편은 창을 들고 그들에게 달려들었으며, 열 기사는 사내답게 맞서 그 창들을 쳐내었다. 이윽고 그들은 칼을 맞대고 싸우다가 여럿이 땅바닥에 쓰러졌다. 그리하여 왕비는 자기 기사들이 이토록 비참하게 짓눌리어 마침내 죽임을 당할 수밖에 없음을 보고, 가엾고 슬픈 마음에 부르짖었다. \"말레아간스 경, 내 고귀한 기사들을 죽이지 말라. 그러면 내가 그대와 함께 가겠으니, 다만 한 가지 약조하라. 그대가 나를 어디로 데려가든 이들도 나와 함께 데려가도록 하라.\" \"부인,\" 하고 말레아간스가 말하였다. \"그대를 위하여 이들도 그대와 함께 내 성으로 데려가리다. 다만 그대가 순순히 따라 나와 함께 말을 달린다면 말이오.\" 그러고 나서 말레아간스 경은 누구도 왕비 곁을 떠나지 못하도록 모두에게 엄히 명하였으니, 이는 란슬롯 경이 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 알게 될까 두려워한 까닭이었다.\n\n이윽고 왕비는 발 빠른 말을 가진 침소의 시동 하나를 은밀히 불러 이렇게 일렀다. \"때를 보아 떠나, 이 반지를 란슬롯 경에게 전하고, 그가 나를 사랑한다면 부디 나를 만나 구해 달라고 청하라. 그리고 말을 아끼지 말라,\" 하고 왕비가 말하였다. \"물길이든 뭍길이든 가리지 말라.\" 그리하여 그 아이는 때를 엿보아, 날래게 말에 박차를 가하여 있는 힘껏 떠났다. 말레아간스 경은 그가 이렇게 달아나는 것을 보고, 그것이 란슬롯 경에게 알리려는 왕비의 분부임을 알아차렸다. 그러자 가장 좋은 말을 탄 자들이 그를 쫓아 활을 쏘았으나, 그 아이는 모두에게서 빠져나갔다. 그러자 말레아간스 경이 왕비에게 말하였다. \"부인, 그대는 나를 배신하려 하나, 나는 란슬롯 경이 쉽사리 그대에게 이르지 못하도록 손을 써 두겠소.\" 그러고 나서 그는 왕비와 그 무리 모두를 데리고 있는 힘을 다해 서둘러 자기 성으로 말을 달렸다. 그리고 가는 길에 말레아간스 경은 자기가 거느린 가장 뛰어난 궁수들을 매복시켜 란슬롯 경을 기다리게 하였다. 그 아이는 웨스트민스터에 이르러 란슬롯 경을 찾아 전갈을 전하고 왕비의 반지를 건네었다. \"아아!\" 하고 란슬롯 경이 말하였다. \"이제 저 고귀한 부인을 구해 내지 못한다면 나는 영영 욕됨을 면치 못하리라.\" 그러고 나서 그는 다급히 갑옷을 가져오게 하여 입고, 말에 올라 있는 힘껏 달렸다. 사람들이 전하기를, 그는 웨스트민스터 다리에서 물에 뛰어들어 말을 헤엄치게 하여 템스 강을 건너 램버스에 이르렀다고 한다. 이윽고 얼마 지나지 않아 그는 좁은 길이 난 어느 숲에 이르렀고, 그곳에 궁수들이 매복해 있었다. 그들이 그에게 활을 쏘아 그의 말을 맞히니, 말이 쓰러졌다. 그러자 란슬롯 경은 말을 버리고 걸어서 나아갔으나, 궁수들과 그 사이에는 도랑과 울타리가 하도 많이 가로놓여 있어 그들과 맞붙을 수가 없었다. \"아아! 부끄러운 일이로다,\" 하고 란슬롯 경이 말하였다. \"한 기사가 다른 기사를 배신하다니! 허나 옛말에 이르기를, 선한 사람은 결코 위험에 처하지 아니하나, 다만 비겁한 자로 인한 위험에는 처할 수 있다 하였다.\" 그러고 나서 란슬롯 경은 한동안 걸었으나, 갑옷과 방패와 창과 그 밖에 몸에 지닌 온갖 것 때문에 몹시 거추장스러웠다. 그때 마침 나무를 실으러 그곳으로 온 수레 한 대가 그의 곁을 지나갔다.\n\n이 무렵에 수레란 짐승의 내장 따위를 나르거나 죄인을 형장으로 실어 가는 데 외에는 거의 쓰이지 않았다. 그러나 란슬롯 경은 왕비를 구해야 한다는 급한 마음 외에는 아무것도 헤아리지 않았다. 그리하여 그는 수레꾼에게 자기를 태워 후한 삯을 줄 터이니 되도록 빨리 데려가 달라고 청하였다. 수레꾼이 응낙하자, 란슬롯 경은 수레에 몸을 실었으나, 몹시 덜컹거리는 통에 길이 좀처럼 나아가지 않음을 한탄할 따름이었다. 그때 마침 가웨인 경이 지나가다가, 무장한 기사가 그토록 별난 모양으로 가는 것을 보고는 그가 누구인지 보려고 가까이 다가왔다. 그러자 란슬롯 경은 왕비가 어떻게 끌려갔는지, 그리고 그 구원을 위해 서두르던 중에 말이 못 쓰게 되어 자기 일을 단념하느니 차라리 수레의 신세를 질 수밖에 없었던 사정을 그에게 일러 주었다. 그러자 가웨인 경이 말하였다. \"기사가 그런 모양으로 다니는 것은 참으로 어울리지 아니하오.\" 그러나 란슬롯 경은 그의 말에 마음 쓰지 않았다.\n\n날이 저물 무렵 그들은 어느 성에 이르렀고, 그 성의 부인이 시녀들을 거느리고 앞장서 나와 가웨인 경을 맞이하였다. 그러나 죄인이거나 적어도 포로라고 여겨지는 그의 일행을 들이는 일은 그녀에게 마뜩잖았으나, 가웨인 경을 위하여 응낙하였다. 저녁 식사 때 란슬롯 경은 하마터면 부엌으로 보내질 뻔하였고, 가웨인 경의 간곡한 청 덕분에야 비로소 부인의 식탁에 앉을 수 있었다. 시녀들 또한 그를 위해 잠자리를 마련해 주려 하지 않았다. 그는 비어 있는 잠자리를 처음 눈에 띄는 대로 차지하였고, 누구의 방해도 받지 않은 채 그대로 두어졌다.\n\n이튿날 아침 그는 성의 망루에서 어떤 부인을 호위하며 가는 한 무리를 보았는데, 그 부인이 왕비이리라 짐작하였다. 가웨인 경도 그럴 법하다 여겨, 마찬가지로 떠나기를 서둘렀다. 성의 부인이 란슬롯 경에게 말 한 필을 내어 주니, 두 사람은 들판을 전속력으로 가로질렀다. 그들은 도중에 만난 몇몇 나그네에게서, 말레아간스 경의 성으로 이르는 길이 둘 있다는 것을 알게 되었다. 그리하여 이곳에서 두 벗은 헤어졌다. 란슬롯 경은 자기 앞길이 온갖 장애로 막혀 있음을 알았으나, 적지 않은 시간을 허비하기는 하였어도 그것들을 무사히 헤쳐 나갔다. 저녁이 다가올 무렵 그는 젊고 쾌활한 시녀 하나를 만났는데, 그녀는 명랑하게 자기 성에서 저녁을 들고 가라고 권하였다. 굶주리고 지쳐 있던 그 기사는 그리 흔쾌한 기색은 아니었으나 그 청을 받아들였다. 그는 부인을 따라 그녀의 성으로 가서 저녁을 게걸스레 먹었으나, 그녀의 온갖 사랑의 수작에는 조금도 마음이 움직이지 않았다. 그런데 갑자기 정경이 바뀌더니, 그는 여섯 명의 사나운 악당에게 습격을 당하였다. 그가 어찌나 힘차게 맞섰던지 대부분이 이내 못 쓰게 되었을 때, 또다시 정경이 바뀌어 그는 그 아름다운 안주인과 단둘이 있게 되었다. 그녀는 자기가 다름 아닌 그의 수호 요정이며, 다만 그의 용기와 충절을 시험해 본 것일 따름이라고 알려 주었다. 이튿날 그 요정은 그를 길로 데려다주었고, 헤어지기에 앞서 반지 하나를 주며, 그 반지가 색이 변함으로써 그에게 온갖 마법을 드러내 보여, 그것들을 제압할 수 있게 해 주리라고 일러 주었다.\n\n란슬롯 경은 여정을 이어 갔으나, 나그네들의 조롱 외에는 별로 시달릴 것이 없었다. 그 나그네들은 어떤 경로로인지 모두 그가 수레를 타고 갔던 수치스러운 일을 알게 된 듯하였다. 그 가운데 누구보다도 무례한 자 하나가 뻔뻔스럽게도 식사 도중에 그를 가로막더니, 제 농지거리를 두둔하느라 싸움까지도 마다하지 않았다. 란슬롯은 손쉽게 이긴 뒤, 그자에게 이번에는 제가 수레에 실리는 벌을 내리는 것으로 그쳤다.\n\n밤에 그는 또 다른 성에서 겉으로는 극진한 환대를 받았으나, 아침이 되자 사슬에 묶인 채 지하 감옥에 갇혀 있는 자신을 발견하였다. 반지를 들여다보아 이것이 마법임을 알아차린 그는 사슬을 끊고, 그것을 지키려 드는 허깨비 괴물들에도 아랑곳없이 갑옷을 챙겨 들고, 탑의 문을 부수어 열고는 여정을 이어 갔다. 마침내 그의 길은 넓고 거센 급류에 가로막혔으니, 그것은 좁은 다리로만 건널 수 있었고, 그 위에서 한 발만 잘못 디뎌도 목숨을 잃을 판이었다. 란슬롯은 고삐를 쥐어 말을 끌고 곁에서 헤엄치게 하며 다리를 건넜는데, 강기슭에 닿자마자 사자 한 마리와 표범 한 마리에게 습격을 당하였다. 그는 두 마리를 모두 죽이고, 이내 기진하여 피를 흘리며 풀밭에 앉아 상처를 싸매려 하였다. 그때 말레아간스의 아비인 브래더매거스가 그에게 다가와 말을 건네었으니, 그의 성은 그곳에서 보이는 곳, 그리 멀지 않은 데에 있었다. 이 왕은 그 아들이 거만하고 무례한 것과는 달리 못지않게 정중하여, 다리와 사나운 짐승들의 위험 속에서 란슬롯 경이 보여 준 용맹과 솜씨를 칭송한 뒤 그에게 도움을 베풀겠노라 하였고, 왕비가 자기 성에 무사히 있으나 다만 말레아간스와 맞서 싸워야만 구해 낼 수 있다고 일러 주었다. 란슬롯은 그 이튿날 결투를 청하였고, 그리하여 그 일은 탑 아래에서, 아름다운 사로잡힌 부인의 눈앞에서 벌어졌다. 란슬롯은 상처로 쇠약해져 평소의 기백으로 싸우지 못하였고, 한동안 승부를 가늠하기 어려웠다. 그러다가 귀네비어가 부르짖었다. \"아, 란슬롯! 나의 기사여, 그대가 더는 나에게 어울리는 사람이 아니라고 들은 것이 참말이로구나!\" 이 말은 이내 기운을 잃었던 기사를 되살려 놓았다. 그는 단숨에 평소의 우세를 되찾아, 오만한 적수를 곧 제 발치에 쓰러뜨렸다.\n\n그가 막 원한을 풀려고 그를 죽이려는 찰나에, 귀네비어가 브래더매거스의 간청에 마음이 움직여 그 일격을 거두라고 명하니, 그는 따랐다. 이제 성과 그 안의 포로들은 그의 손안에 있었다. 란슬롯은 왕비의 거처로 서둘러 가서 그 발치에 몸을 던지고 막 그 손에 입 맞추려 하였다. 그때 그녀가 부르짖었다. \"아, 란슬롯! 어찌하여 내가 다시 그대를 보면서도, 그대가 수치스럽게 나라 안 곳곳을 수레에 실려 끌려다닌 뒤로는 더는 나에게 어울리는 사람이 아니라고 느끼는 것인가…….\" 그녀는 그 말을 미처 끝맺지 못하였으니, 그녀의 연인이 별안간 그녀에게서 몸을 일으켜, 자신이 섬기는 부인의 노여움을 산 것을 비통히 한탄하며 성 밖으로 뛰쳐나가, 칼과 방패를 좌우로 내던지고 사납게 숲속으로 달려 들어가 사라져 버린 까닭이었다.\n\n란슬롯을 걸음마다 따라다니던 그 가증스러운 수레 이야기가 케이 경의 귀에까지 들어간 모양이었고, 케이 경이 그것을 왕비에게 일러바치며 그녀의 기사가 욕을 당하였음이 틀림없다는 증거로 삼은 듯하였다. 그러나 귀네비어는 그 이야기를 그토록 성급히 믿어 버린 것을 두고두고 뉘우칠 겨를을 갖게 되었다. 사흘이 지나도록 란슬롯은 제가 어디로 가는지도 모른 채 헤매다가, 마침내 자기가 섬기는 부인이 필시 잘못 전해진 말에 속은 것이며, 그녀의 오해를 바로잡는 것이 자기 본분임을 헤아리기 시작하였다. 그리하여 그는 돌아가 말레아간스로 하여금 포로들을 풀어 주게 하였고, 가웨인 경의 도착을 기다리던 길로 접어들어, 이튿날 그를 만나는 기쁨을 누렸다. 그러고 나서 일행 모두는 즐겁게 카멜롯을 향해 나아갔다.\n\n────\n\n# THE LADY OF SHALOTT\n\n아서 왕은 윈체스터에서 성대한 마상 시합을 열겠노라고 선포했다. 왕은 그의 기사들 못지않게 이 축제를 기다리는 마음이 간절했던 까닭에, 며칠 앞서 출발해 준비를 감독하면서 왕비와 그 궁정 사람들은 카멜롯에 남겨 두었다. 랜슬롯 경은 몸이 좋지 못하다는 핑계로 역시 뒤에 남았다. 그의 의도는 변장을 한 채 시합에 참가하려는 것이었다. 그래서 그는 자신의 계획을 귀네비어에게 알린 뒤, 말에 올라 수행원 하나 없이 출발했고, 늙은이의 쇠약함을 가장하여 가장 인적이 드문 길을 골라 윈체스터로 향했으며, 시합을 구경하러 가는 어느 늙은 기사처럼 아무에게도 주목받지 않고 지나갔다. 그가 지나가는 성의 창문에서 우연히 그를 바라본 아서와 가웨인조차 그의 변장에 속아 넘어갔다. 하지만 한 가지 사고가 그의 정체를 드러내고 말았다. 그의 말이 그만 비틀거렸는데, 영웅은 한순간 자신이 가장한 배역을 잊은 채 그만의 특유한 힘과 민첩함으로 그 짐승을 다시 세웠고, 그 덕에 그들은 단번에 흉내 낼 수 없는 랜슬롯을 알아보았다. 그러나 그들은 다가오는 축제에서 그의 비범한 무예가 반드시 그의 정체를 드러내리라 확신했으므로, 그가 방해받지 않고 여정을 계속하도록 내버려 두었다.\n\n저녁에 랜슬롯은 인근의 샬롯 성에서 낯선 기사로서 융숭한 대접을 받았다. 이 성의 영주에게는 빼어난 미모를 지닌 딸 하나와, 최근에 기사 작위를 받은 두 아들이 있었는데, 그중 하나는 그때 병이 나 자리에 누워 있어 마상 시합에 참가하지 못하게 된 처지였으니, 두 형제는 오래전부터 그 시합을 준비해 왔던 것이다. 랜슬롯은 병든 아들의 갑옷을 빌려 입을 수만 있다면 다른 아들을 수행하겠노라 제안했고, 샬롯의 영주는 손님의 이름을 알지 못한 채로도 그 풍모만 보고 자기 아들이 이보다 더 나은 무예의 조력자를 얻을 수 없으리라 흡족해하며 그 제안을 진심으로 고맙게 받아들였다. 그러는 동안 낯선 기사의 첫인상에 크게 마음을 빼앗긴 젊은 처녀는 점점 더 주의를 기울여 그를 살폈고, 저녁 식사가 끝나기도 전에 그를 너무도 깊이 연모하게 된 나머지, 여러 차례 얼굴빛이 변하고 또 랜슬롯 경이 도저히 잘못 알아볼 수 없는 다른 징후들을 드러내다가, 끝내 자기 방으로 물러나 눈물 속에서 위안을 구할 수밖에 없었다. 랜슬롯 경은 그녀의 오라비를 통해, 자기 마음은 이미 다른 데로 정해졌으나 다가오는 시합에서 그녀의 기사로서 활약하는 것은 자신의 긍지이자 기쁨이 되리라는 뜻을 서둘러 전했다. 그 예의에 만족할 수밖에 없었던 처녀는 시합에서 두르라며 자신의 스카프를 그에게 건넸다.\n\n랜슬롯은 아침에 그 젊은 기사와 함께 출발했는데, 윈체스터에 가까워지자 젊은 기사는 그를 샬롯 영주의 누이인 어느 귀부인의 성으로 데려갔고, 그들은 그곳에서 후한 대접을 받았다. 이튿날 그들은 갑옷을 입었는데, 그 갑옷은 기사 작위를 받은 첫해의 젊은이들에게 으레 그러하듯 아무런 문양도 없이 완전히 단순한 것이었으며, 다만 방패만은 붉게 칠해져 있었으니, 수행원들이 그들을 알아볼 수 있으려면 어떤 색이든 필요했기 때문이다. 랜슬롯은 투구 장식에 샬롯의 처녀가 준 스카프를 둘렀고, 그렇게 차림을 갖추고 시합장으로 나아갔는데, 그곳에서 기사들은 두 패로 나뉘어 한쪽은 갈레호 경이, 다른 한쪽은 아서 왕이 지휘하고 있었다. 그들은 시합장 밖에서 잠시 싸움을 살피다가 갈레호 경 편이 밀리기 시작하는 것을 보고는, 무리 속으로 뛰어들어 왕의 기사들을 공격했으니, 젊은이는 제 힘에 걸맞은 상대들을 골랐고, 그의 동행은 원탁의 주요 용사들을 골라 가웨인과 보호트와 라이오넬을 차례로 거꾸러뜨렸다. 구경꾼들의 놀라움은 더할 나위 없었으니, 그토록 무적의 힘을 지닐 수 있는 자는 랜슬롯 말고는 없다고 여겨졌기 때문이다. 그러나 그의 투구 장식에 달린 정표는 그가 그렇게 변장했을 가능성을 배제하는 듯했으니, 랜슬롯이 자신의 군주이신 귀부인 외에는 그 누구의 표식도 단 적이 없었던 까닭이다. 마침내 랜슬롯의 형제인 헥터 경이 그와 맞붙어, 무시무시한 격투 끝에 그의 머리에 위험한 상처를 입혔으나, 그 자신도 투구에 일격을 맞아 완전히 정신을 잃고 땅에 쓰러지고 말았다. 그 뒤 승자는 동행을 거느린 채 전속력으로 말을 달려 떠났다.\n\n그들은 샬롯 성으로 돌아왔고, 그곳에서 랜슬롯은 선량한 백작과 그의 두 아들, 그리고 무엇보다도 그 아름다운 딸의 지극한 보살핌을 받았는데, 그녀의 의술은 아마도 그의 회복기를 크게 앞당겼을 것이다. 그의 건강이 거의 완전히 회복되었을 무렵, 궁정이 카멜롯으로 돌아간 뒤 친족을 찾아 나섰던 헥터 경과 보호트 경과 라이오넬 경이 성벽 위를 거니는 그를 발견했다. 그들의 만남은 무척이나 기뻤다. 그들은 끊임없는 잔치 속에서 사흘을 성에서 보내며 시합에서 있었던 일들을 두고 서로 농을 주고받았다. 랜슬롯은 처음에는 자신에게 상처를 입힌 자에게 복수하겠노라 맹세하며 말문을 열었으나, 끝에 가서는 자기 형제의 비범한 무용을 목격한 데서 느낀 긍지로써 그 고통의 보상을 받았노라 선언하였다. 그러고 나서 그는 왕비에게 전할 전언을 맡겨 그들을 떠나보내며, 곧 뒤따르겠노라 약속했으니, 자신을 친절히 맞아 준 주인들에게는 물론이고 샬롯의 아름다운 처녀에게도 먼저 격식을 갖추어 작별을 고해야 했기 때문이다.\n\n젊은 처녀는 눈물과 간청으로 그를 붙들어 보려 헛되이 애쓴 끝에, 그가 자신에게 어떤 희망의 근거도 남기지 않고 떠나가는 모습을 바라보았다.\n\n마상 시합이 열린 것은 초여름이었다. 그러나 랜슬롯이 떠난 뒤로 몇 달이 흘렀고, 이제 겨울이 가까이 다가와 있었다. 샬롯의 귀부인은 건강과 기력이 차츰 쇠하였고, 자기가 사랑하는 이와 떨어져서는 살아갈 수 없으리라 느꼈다. 그녀는 성을 떠나 강기슭으로 내려가 한 척의 배에 몸을 실었으며, 그 배를 매어 둔 줄을 풀어 물살에 자신을 맡긴 채 카멜롯을 향해 떠내려가도록 두었다.\n\n어느 날 아침, 아서와 라이오넬 경이 강물이 그 벽을 씻어 내리는 탑의 창문에서 내다보고 있을 때, 그들은 화려하게 장식되고 금실로 짠 천막으로 덮인 배 한 척이 사람의 인도라곤 전혀 없이 강물을 따라 떠내려오는 것을 발견했다. 그들이 지켜보는 사이 배는 기슭에 닿았고, 그들은 그것을 살피러 서둘러 내려갔다. 천막 아래에서 그들은 한 아름다운 여인의 주검을 발견했는데, 라이오넬 경은 그 이목구비에서 어렵지 않게 샬롯의 사랑스러운 처녀를 알아보았다. 더 살펴 나가던 그들은 금실과 보석으로 화려하게 수놓인 지갑 하나를 발견했고, 그 지갑 안에서 편지 한 통을 찾아냈으니, 아서가 그것을 펼쳐 보자 자신과 원탁의 모든 기사에게 부쳐진 것으로, 호수의 랜슬롯, 곧 기사들 가운데 가장 출중하고 사내들 가운데 가장 아름다우나 동시에 가장 잔인하고 완고한 그가, 그 모진 태도로써 불쌍한 처녀의 죽음을 불러왔다는 내용이었으며, 그녀의 사랑은 그의 잔인함 못지않게 무적이었노라 적혀 있었다. 왕은 즉시 그 신분에 합당한 모든 예우를 갖추어 귀부인을 매장하라 명했고, 동시에 기사들에게 그녀가 랜슬롯을 사모해 온 내력을 설명해 주었으니, 이는 모든 이의 연민과 안타까움을 자아냈다.\n\n————\n\n테니슨은 \"샬롯의 귀부인\" 이야기를 한 편의 시의 소재로 삼았다. 그 비극적 결말은 이렇게 노래된다.\n\n\"탑과 발코니 아래로, 정원 담장과 회랑을 지나, 어슴푸레 빛나는 형상이 흘러갔네, 높다란 집들 사이로 한 구의 주검이, 말없이 카멜롯 안으로. 부두 위로 사람들이 몰려나왔네, 기사와 시민, 영주와 귀부인이, 뱃머리를 둘러싸고 그 이름을 읽었네, '샬롯의 귀부인'이라고.\n\n\"이가 누구인가? 여기 무슨 일인가? 가까운 불 밝힌 궁전 안에서는 왕가의 환호 소리가 잦아들었네. 그리고 그들은 두려워 성호를 그었네, 카멜롯의 모든 기사들이. 그러나 랜슬롯은 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가, 이렇게 말했네, '아름다운 얼굴이로구나. 하느님이 자비로이 그녀에게 은총을 베푸시기를, 샬롯의 귀부인에게.'\"\n\n————\n\n# QUEEN GUENEVER’S PERIL\n\n이 무렵에 귀네비어 왕비는 목숨이 위태로운 커다란 위기에 빠지게 되었다. 왕비를 가까이에서 모시던 어느 종자가 가웨인 경에게 모종의 원한을 품고 있었던 까닭에, 공개 연회에서 독으로 그를 죽이기로 작정했다. 이를 위해 그는 겉모양이 고운 사과 하나에 독을 감추어 다른 여러 사과 위에 얹어 두고는 그 접시를 왕비 앞에 올렸으니, 가웨인 경이 가장 지체 높은 기사인 만큼 왕비가 그에게 사과를 건네리라 기대했던 것이다. 그런데 마침 그날 도착한, 지체가 높은 어느 스코틀랜드 기사가 왕비의 곁에 앉아 있었던 까닭에, 왕비는 손님인 그에게 사과를 건넸고, 그는 그것을 먹자마자 끔찍한 고통에 사로잡혀 정신을 잃고 쓰러졌다. 물론 궁정 전체가 큰 혼란에 빠졌다. 기사들은 식탁에서 일어나 가엾은 왕비에게 분노의 시선을 던졌으나, 왕비의 눈물과 항변으로도 그들의 의심을 거둘 수는 없었다. 온갖 손을 써 보았으나 그 기사는 끝내 죽었고, 남은 일이라고는 그를 위해 성대한 장례와 기념비를 마련하는 것뿐이었으며, 그렇게 했다.\n\n얼마 뒤 살해된 기사의 형제인 마도르 경이 그를 찾아 아서 왕의 궁정에 도착했다. 숲에서 사냥을 하던 중 그는 우연히 기념비가 세워진 자리에 이르렀고, 그 비문을 읽고는 즉각적이고 본보기가 될 복수를 결심한 채 궁정으로 돌아왔다. 그는 큰 홀로 말을 몰고 들어와 왕비를 반역죄로 큰소리로 고발했으며, 정해진 날까지 왕비의 결백을 지키기 위해 목숨을 걸 만큼 담대한 기사를 찾아내지 못한다면 왕비를 처벌받도록 내어 줄 것을 강력히 요구했다. 아서 왕은 그토록 강력한 권세를 지녔음에도 그 요구를 거절할 엄두를 내지 못했고, 무거운 마음으로 이를 받아들일 수밖에 없었다. 마도르는 단호하게 자리를 떠났으며, 왕과 왕비는 공포와 불안 속에 빠진 채 남겨졌다.\n\n이 모든 일이 벌어지는 동안 란슬롯은 자리를 비우고 있었으며, 그가 어디에 있는지 아무도 알지 못했다. 그는 자신이 마상 시합에서 샬롯의 여인의 스카프를 두르고 있던 것을 보고 왕비가 성급히 짐작하여, 그가 그 여인을 향해 연정을 품었다며 책망하자, 분노한 채 아리따운 연인 곁을 떠났던 것이다. 그는 숲속의 어느 은자와 함께 거처를 정하고는, 자신을 떼어 내려는 바람에서 비롯되었으리라 여겨지는 행동을 한 그 무정한 미녀를 다시는 생각하지 않기로 마음먹었다. 그러나 차분히 돌이켜 보는 사이에 그의 분노도 어느 정도 식었고, 거의 바랄 엄두를 내지는 못했으나 화해를 바라기 시작하던 차에, 마침 마도르 경의 도전 소식이 그의 귀에 들어왔다. 그 소식은 그의 기운을 되살렸고, 그는 더없이 흔쾌한 마음으로 결투를 준비하기 시작했다. 그 결투에서 승리한다면 연인의 애정과 군주의 감사를 단번에 얻을 수 있을 터였다.\n\n이 무렵 샬롯의 여인의 슬픈 운명은 란슬롯의 충정에 대한 왕비의 모든 의심을 완전히 풀어 준 터였고, 왕비는 그와의 어리석은 다툼을 더없이 비통하게 한탄했다. 그 다툼으로 인해 정작 도움이 절실한 때에 가장 든든한 옹호자를 잃고 말았기 때문이다.\n\n마도르 경이 정한 날이 빠르게 다가오는 만큼, 왕비는 자신을 변호해 줄 투사를 마련해야만 했다. 그리하여 왕비는 헥토르 경, 라이오넬 경, 보호트 경, 가웨인 경에게 차례로 그 싸움을 맡아 달라고 간청했다. 왕비는 그들 앞에 무릎을 꿇고는 자신에게 씌워진 죄에 결백함을 하늘이 증언해 달라 호소했으나, 자신들의 두 눈으로 그 행위와 그 치명적인 결과를 직접 보았으니 그 결백을 지키기 위해 싸울 수는 없다는 단호한 대답만을 모두에게서 들었다. 그리하여 왕비는 낙담하고 비탄에 잠긴 채 물러났다. 그러나 만일 죄가 있다면 자신이 불태워질 운명인 그 끔찍한 장작더미를 바라보자 새로운 힘이 솟구쳤고, 왕비는 다시 보호트 경에게로 가 그의 발치에 몸을 던지고는 가엾이 자비를 구하며 정신을 잃고 쓰러졌다. 용맹한 기사도 이에는 버티지 못했다. 그는 왕비를 일으켜 세우고는, 다른 더 나은 투사가 나서지 않는다면 자신이 그 일을 떠맡겠노라 서둘러 약속했다. 그런 다음 그는 벗들을 불러 모아 자신의 결심을 알렸다. 마도르 경과의 목숨을 건 결투는 더없이 두려운 일이었던 까닭에, 그들은 이튿날 아침 그와 함께 숲속의 은자의 거처로 가기로 했다. 그곳에서 그는 결투장에 들어서기에 앞서 은자에게서 죄 사함을 받고 하늘과 화평을 이루고자 했던 것이다. 은자의 거처에 가까워졌을 때, 그들은 숲에서 말을 타고 가는 어느 기사를 발견했고, 그가 다름 아닌 란슬롯 경임을 곧바로 알아보았다. 만남에 크게 기뻐한 그들은 그의 물음에 답하여 왕비가 임박한 위험에 처해 있다는 소식을 서둘러 확인해 주었으며, 궁정으로 돌아가 힘닿는 대로 왕비를 위로하되 자신이 왕비를 변호하려는 뜻에 대해서는 아무 말도 하지 말라는 그의 당부를 받았다. 그는 정체를 알 수 없는 모험가의 모습으로 그 일을 해낼 작정이었다.\n\n그들이 성으로 돌아오니 미사가 이미 끝나 있었고, 왕비에게 미처 말을 건넬 겨를도 없이 그들은 만찬을 위해 큰 홀로 불려 들어갔다. 손님들 모두의 얼굴에 짙은 우울이 드리워 있었다. 아서 왕조차 낙담을 감추지 못했고, 가엾은 귀네비어는 미동도 없이 눈물에 젖은 채 마도르 경이 나타나기를 떨면서 기다리고 있었다. 머지않아 그가 큰 홀로 성큼성큼 걸어 들어왔고, 좌중의 적막 속에 한층 더 위압적으로 울려 퍼지는 우레 같은 목소리로 죄인에 대한 즉각적인 심판을 요구했다. 아서 왕은 위엄 있게 답하기를, 아직 하루가 얼마 지나지 않았으니 어쩌면 그의 싸움에 대한 갈망을 채워 줄 투사가 나타날지도 모른다고 했다. 이때 보호트 경이 식탁에서 일어났다가 이내 완전한 갑주를 갖추고 돌아와, 왕의 포옹과 감사를 받은 뒤 자리에 다시 앉았다. 왕은 이제 어느 정도 자신감을 되찾기 시작했다. 마도르 경은 점점 초조해지더니 복수의 다짐을 다시금 거듭하며, 결투를 더는 미루지 말 것을 강력히 요구했다.\n\n논쟁이 한창 무르익을 무렵, 검은 군마에 올라 검은 갑주를 두르고 면갑을 내린 채 손에 창을 든 기사 하나가 말을 몰아 큰 홀로 들어왔다. \"기사여,\" 왕이 말했다. \"말에서 내려 우리와 함께 음식을 들 마음이 있는가?\" \"아니옵니다, 폐하.\" 그가 답했다. \"저는 한 여인의 목숨을 구하러 왔습니다. 왕비께서는 그릇되이 은총을 베푸시어 수많은 기사를 영예롭게 하셨거늘, 정작 위난의 때에 그분의 편을 들 자가 하나도 없다니요. 왕비를 반역죄로 감히 고발한 그대여, 앞으로 나서라. 오늘 그대는 그대의 온 힘을 다 써야 할 것이니.\"\n\n마도르 경은 놀라기는 했으나, 상대의 단호한 도전과 위풍당당한 모습에도 겁먹지 않고 결투를 준비했다. 첫 충돌에 두 사람 모두 말에서 떨어졌다. 이어 그들은 칼을 뽑아 정오부터 저녁까지 이어진 결투를 시작했고, 마침내 힘이 다하기 시작한 마도르 경이 란슬롯에게 땅바닥에 쓰러져 자비를 구할 수밖에 없게 되었다. 이미 적의 목숨을 끊고자 팔을 치켜들었던 승자는 곧바로 칼을 떨구고는 정중하게 정신을 잃은 마도르 경을 일으켜 세우며, 일찍이 이토록 가공할 적을 만나 본 적이 없노라 솔직히 고백했다. 상대 또한 같은 예의로써, 형제의 죽음에 대한 모든 복수의 계획을 엄숙히 단념했다. 그리하여 이제 막역한 벗이 된 두 기사는 더없이 진심 어린 마음으로 서로를 끌어안았다. 그러는 사이에 아서 왕은 이제 투구의 끈이 풀린 란슬롯 경을 알아보고는, 자신을 구해 준 이를 맞이하고 그에게 감사하고자 모든 기사를 거느린 채 결투장으로 달려 내려갔다. 귀네비어는 기쁨에 겨워 정신을 잃었고, 결투의 장소는 별안간 더없이 떠들썩한 환희의 광경으로 바뀌었다.\n\n진짜 죄인이 밝혀지면서 모두의 만족은 한층 더 커졌다. 그자는 우연히 모종의 의심을 사게 되었던 까닭에 자신의 죄를 자백했고, 마도르 경이 보는 앞에서 공개적으로 처벌받았다.\n\n이제 궁정은 성으로 돌아갔고, 아서 왕은 그 행복한 사건을 기리는 뜻에서 그 성에 \"라 주아이외즈 가르드\"라는 이름을 붙여 자신의 감사의 표시로 란슬롯 경에게 하사했다.\n\n————\n\n# TRISTRAM AND ISOUDE\n\n멜리아두스는 레오노이스, 즉 리오네스의 왕이었다. 이 나라는 로맨스의 연대기에서 유명한 곳으로, 콘월 왕국과 인접해 있었으나 지금은 지도에서 사라졌으니, 전하는 바에 따르면 바다에 잠겨 버렸다고 한다. 멜리아두스는 콘월의 왕 마크의 누이인 이사벨라와 혼인했다. 한 요정이 그를 사랑하게 되어, 그가 사냥에 열중하고 있는 동안 마법을 부려 그를 멀리 데려가 버렸다. 그의 왕비는 그를 찾아 길을 나섰으나, 도중에 병이 들어 세상을 떠나며 갓난 아들 하나를 남겼다. 왕비는 그 아이가 태어난 슬픈 정황 때문에 아들을 트리스트람이라 불렀다.\n\n왕비를 수행했던 종자 구베르나일이 그 아이를 맡아 길렀고, 마침내 요정의 마법을 깨뜨리고 집으로 돌아온 아버지에게 아이를 데려다주었다.\n\n멜리아두스는 칠 년 뒤에 재혼했는데, 새 왕비는 트리스트람이 아버지에게 끼치는 영향을 시기하여 그의 목숨을 노리는 음모를 꾸몄다. 이를 알아챈 구베르나일은 그 결과 소년을 데리고 프랑스 왕의 궁정으로 달아났다. 그곳에서 트리스트람은 따뜻한 환대를 받았고, 온갖 용맹하고 기사다운 소양을 갈고닦으며 자라났으니, 무예에 더해 음악과 체스의 기예까지 익혔다. 특히 그는 사냥과 온갖 숲속의 유희에 몰두하여, 사냥에 관한 모든 지식에서 궁정의 다른 어떤 기사보다도 빼어난 인물이 되었다. 왕의 딸 벨린다가 그를 사랑하게 된 것도 무리는 아니었다. 그러나 그가 그녀의 연정에 응답하지 않자, 그녀는 갑작스러운 분노에 휩싸여 아버지를 충동질해 그에게 맞서게 했고, 그는 왕국에서 추방되고 말았다. 공주는 곧 자신의 행동을 후회했고, 절망에 빠져 스스로 목숨을 끊었다. 그녀는 먼저 트리스트람에게 더없이 애틋한 편지를 써 보내며, 동시에 자신이 무척 아끼던 아름답고 영리한 개 한 마리를 보내어, 그것을 자신을 기리는 정표로 간직해 달라고 청했다. 멜리아두스는 이제 세상을 떠난 뒤였고, 그의 왕비, 곧 트리스트람의 계모가 왕좌를 차지하고 있었으므로, 구베르나일은 자신의 제자를 그의 고국으로 데려가기가 두려워 그를 콘월의 숙부 마크에게로 데려갔고, 마크는 그를 친절히 맞아 주었다.\n\n마크 왕은 틴타델 성에 거처하고 있었으니, 이 성은 우테르와 이게르네의 이야기에서 이미 언급된 바 있다. 이 궁정에서 트리스트람은 기사에게 요구되는 온갖 수련에서 두각을 나타냈고, 그의 용맹과 무예를 실제로 발휘할 기회는 오래지 않아 찾아왔다. 아일랜드 왕비의 동생이자 이름난 용사인 모라운트가 마크 왕에게 조공을 요구하러 궁정에 당도한 것이다. 콘월의 기사들은 로맨스에서 비겁하기로 악명이 높았는데, 이번 일에서도 그 면모를 드러냈다. 마크 왕은 이 아일랜드 기사와 맞서려는 용사를 한 명도 찾지 못했다. 그러던 차에 아직 기사 서임을 받지 못했던 조카 트리스트람이 기사단에 받아 주기를 간청하며, 동시에 아일랜드 용사에 맞서 콘월을 위해 싸우겠다고 자청했다. 마크 왕은 마지못해 승낙했고, 트리스트람은 기사의 작위를 부여하는 서임을 받았으며, 결투의 장소와 시각이 정해졌다.\n\n트리스트람의 무훈 가운데 첫 번째이자 가장 영광스러운 위업의 하나인 이 유명한 결투의 세세한 내용을 모두 전하려 하지는 않겠으나, 다만 이렇게만 말해 두기로 한다. 젊은 기사는 비록 심한 상처를 입었지만 모라운트의 머리를 갈라, 칼의 일부를 그 상처 속에 남겨 놓았다. 상처와 패배의 치욕으로 반쯤 죽은 모라운트는 서둘러 자신의 배에 몸을 숨기고 전속력으로 아일랜드를 향해 달아났으나, 고국에 도착한 직후에 숨을 거두었다.\n\n이렇게 하여 콘월 왕국은 조공의 부담에서 벗어났다. 트리스트람은 피를 많이 흘려 기력을 잃고 정신을 잃은 채 쓰러졌다. 벗들이 그를 구하러 달려왔다. 그들은 그의 상처를 치료했고, 상처는 대체로 쉽게 아물었다. 그러나 모라운트의 창에는 독이 묻어 있어, 그 창이 낸 한 상처는 어떤 약에도 듣지 않고 날이 갈수록 더 악화되어 갔다. 의원들은 더 이상 손을 쓸 수가 없었다. 트리스트람은 숙부에게 떠나도록 허락해 달라고 청하며, 로에그리아[잉글랜드] 왕국에서 도움을 구하고자 했다. 숙부의 동의를 얻어 그는 배에 올랐고, 여러 날을 바다 위에서 표류한 끝에 바람에 떠밀려 아일랜드 해안에 닿았다. 그는 바다의 위험에서 벗어났다는 기쁨과 감사로 가득 차 뭍에 내려, 자신의 로테를 들고 연주하기 시작했다. 때는 여름 저녁이었고, 아일랜드 왕과 그의 딸인 아름다운 이주드는 바다가 내려다보이는 창가에 있었다. 그 낯선 하프 연주자는 부름을 받아 궁전으로 안내되었는데, 자신이 얼마 전에 그 용사를 죽인 바로 그 아일랜드에 있음을 알게 되자, 그는 이름을 숨기고 자신을 트람트리스라 칭했다. 왕비가 그의 치료를 맡아, 약을 넣은 목욕으로 차츰차츰 그를 건강하게 회복시켰다. 음악과 놀이에 능한 그의 솜씨 덕분에 그는 자주 궁정에 불려 갔고, 공주 이주드에게 음유시와 시작(詩作)을 가르치는 스승이 되었다. 공주는 그의 가르침 아래 크게 정진하여, 곧 스승을 제외하고는 왕국에서 견줄 자가 없게 되었다.\n\n* 로테: 중세의 현악기로, 하프와 비슷한 일종의 류트.\n이 무렵 한 차례 마상 시합이 열렸는데, 원탁의 기사를 비롯해 많은 기사들이 참석했다. 첫째 날, 팔라메데스라는 이름의 사라센 왕자가 모든 이를 압도했다. 사람들은 그를 궁정으로 데려가 잔치를 베풀었는데, 상처에서 막 회복된 트리스트람도 그 자리에 참석했다. 이 자리에서 아름다운 이주드는 온갖 매력을 한껏 드러냈다. 팔라메데스는 그 모습을 보며 마음이 흔들리지 않을 수 없었고, 자신의 사랑을 감추려 들지도 않았다. 트리스트람은 이를 알아챘고, 질투에서 느끼는 고통은 아름다운 이주드가 이미 자신에게 얼마나 소중한 존재가 되었는지를 그에게 일깨워 주었다.\n\n이튿날 시합이 다시 열렸다. 트리스트람은 여전히 상처로 쇠약했으나, 밤중에 일어나 무기를 챙겨 경기장 근처의 숲에 숨겨 두었고, 시합이 시작된 뒤 싸우는 이들 틈에 섞여 들었다. 그는 자신과 맞선 모든 이를 쓰러뜨렸으니, 특히 팔라메데스를 창 한 방으로 땅에 쓰러뜨린 뒤 그와 맨손으로 겨루어 시합의 승리를 거머쥐었다. 그러나 그렇게 무리한 탓에 상처가 다시 벌어졌다. 피가 쉴 새 없이 흘렀고, 이 가련한 처지에서, 그러나 승리를 거둔 채로, 사람들은 그를 궁전으로 옮겼다. 아름다운 이주드는 날이 갈수록 더욱 깊어지는 관심을 기울이며 그의 간호에 헌신했고, 그녀의 능숙한 보살핌은 곧 그를 건강하게 회복시켰다.\n\n어느 날, 궁정의 한 시녀가 트리스트람의 무기가 보관된 작은 방에 들어갔다가, 칼의 일부가 부러져 나간 것을 알아챘다. 그녀는 그 사라진 조각이 아일랜드 용사 모라운트의 두개골에 박혀 있던 조각과 닮았다는 생각이 들었다. 그녀가 이 생각을 왕비에게 전하자, 왕비는 동생의 상처에서 빼낸 조각을 트리스트람의 칼과 맞추어 보고는, 그것이 같은 칼의 일부이며 트리스트람의 무기가 바로 자기 동생의 목숨을 앗아 간 그 무기임을 확신했다. 왕비는 자신의 슬픔과 원한을 왕 앞에 털어놓았고, 왕은 자신의 두 눈으로 직접 그녀의 의심이 사실임을 확인했다. 트리스트람은 온 궁정 앞에 불려 나와, 자신들의 혈족을 죽이고도 감히 그들 앞에 나타났다는 비난을 받았다. 그는 조공 요구를 매듭짓기 위해 모라운트와 싸운 것을 인정했고, 오직 바람과 물결의 힘만으로 그들의 해안에 떠밀려 온 것이라고 말했다. 왕비는 동생의 죽음에 대한 복수를 요구했다. 아름다운 이주드는 몸을 떨며 창백해졌으나, 이토록 잘생기고 용감한 자의 목숨을 그런 이유로 빼앗아서는 안 된다는 웅성거림이 온 좌중에서 일었고, 마침내 왕의 마음속에서 너그러움이 원한을 이겨 냈다. 트리스트람은 무사히 풀려났으나, 지체 없이 왕국을 떠나며 죽음을 각오하지 않는 한 다시는 그곳으로 돌아오지 말라는 명령을 받았다. 트리스트람은 건강을 되찾아 콘월로 돌아갔다.\n\n마크 왕은 조카에게 자신의 모험담을 낱낱이 들려 달라고 했다. 트리스트람은 그에게 모든 것을 소상히 이야기했는데, 아름다운 이주드에 대해 말할 차례가 되자 오로지 연인만이 드러낼 수 있는 열기와 활기로 그녀의 매력을 묘사했다. 마크 왕은 그 묘사에 매혹되었고, 적당한 때를 골라 조카에게 한 가지 청을 들어 달라고 요구했으며, 조카는 선뜻 이를 허락했다. 왕은 그에게 거룩한 성유물에 손을 얹고 자신의 명을 따르겠노라 맹세하게 했다. 그런 다음 마크는 그에게 아일랜드로 가서, 자신을 위해 아름다운 이주드를 콘월의 왕비로 맞아 오라고 명했다.\n* 청: 중세 기사도에서 상대가 무조건 들어주기로 약속한 부탁을 가리킨다.\n\n트리스트람은 아일랜드로 돌아가는 것이 자신에게 틀림없는 죽음이라 믿었다. 더구나 어떻게 그런 일로 숙부의 사절 노릇을 할 수 있단 말인가? 그러나 맹세에 매인 몸이었으므로 그는 한순간도 망설이지 않았다. 다만 갑옷을 바꾸어 입는 조심성만은 발휘했다. 그는 아일랜드를 향해 배에 올랐으나, 폭풍이 그를 카멜롯 가까운 잉글랜드 해안으로 떠밀어 보냈다. 그곳에서는 아서 왕이 원탁의 기사들과, 그 밖에 세상에서 가장 빼어난 수많은 이들을 거느리고 궁정을 열고 있었다.\n\n트리스트람은 정체를 감추었다. 그는 여러 차례 마상 창시합에 나섰고, 수많은 결투에서 싸워 영광으로 자신을 빛냈다. 어느 날 그는 최근에 도착한 이들 가운데 아름다운 이주드의 아버지인 아일랜드 왕이 있는 것을 보았다. 이 왕은 자신의 군주인 아서에게 반역을 꾀했다는 혐의를 받고, 그 혐의를 벗고자 카멜롯으로 온 것이었다. 원탁에서 가장 무시무시한 전사 중 하나인 블라아노르가 그를 고발한 자였는데, 아르기우스 왕은 그와 맞설 젊은 혈기도 힘도 없었다. 따라서 그는 자신의 결백을 입증해 줄 용사를 구해야만 했다. 그러나 원탁의 기사들은 자신들끼리의 다툼이 아니고서는 서로 싸울 수 없었다. 아르기우스는 그 정체 모를 기사의 드높은 명성을 전해 들었고, 또한 그의 무훈을 직접 목격하기도 했다. 그는 그를 찾아가, 자신의 변호를 맡아 달라고 간청하며, 자신은 고발당한 죄에 결백하다고 맹세코 선언했다. 트리스트람은 선뜻 응낙하고 왕에게 자신의 정체를 밝혔으며, 왕은 그 대가로, 만일 성공한다면 그가 청하는 무엇이든 선물로 주겠다고 약속했다.\n\n트리스트람은 블라아노르와 싸워 그를 쓰러뜨렸고, 그의 목숨을 자신의 손아귀에 쥐었다. 쓰러진 전사는 그에게 정복의 권리를 행사하여 치명적인 일격을 가하라고 청했다. \"이토록 용감한 기사의 목숨을 내가 빼앗다니, 하느님께서 결코 그런 일을 허락지 않으시리라!\" 트리스트람이 말했다. 그는 그를 일으켜 세워 그의 벗들에게 돌려보냈다. 결투의 판관들은 아일랜드 왕이 그에게 씌워진 혐의를 벗었다고 판정했고, 트리스트람을 의기양양하게 그의 천막으로 이끌어 갔다. 감사로 가득 찬 아르기우스 왕은 트리스트람에게 자신의 왕국으로 함께 가 달라고 간청했다. 그들은 함께 떠나 아일랜드에 도착했고, 왕비는 동생의 죽음에 대한 원한을 잊은 채, 남편의 목숨을 구해 준 이에게 오로지 감사와 호의만을 보였다.\n\n아버지가 자신의 구원자에게 청하는 무엇이든 주겠노라 약속했음을 알고 있던 이주드에게 그 순간은 얼마나 행복했던가! 그러나 불행한 트리스트람은 자신을 옭아맨 잔혹한 맹세를 떠올리며 절망에 잠겨 그녀를 바라보았다. 그의 도량 넓은 영혼이 사랑의 힘을 억눌렀다. 그는 자신이 한 맹세를 밝히고, 떨리는 목소리로 숙부를 위해 아름다운 이주드를 청했다.\n\n아르기우스는 이를 승낙했고, 이주드의 출발을 위한 모든 채비가 곧 갖추어졌다. 그녀가 가장 아끼는 시녀 브렝그웨인이 그녀를 수행하기로 했다. 출발하는 날, 왕비는 이 충직한 시녀를 따로 불러, 자신이 딸과 트리스트람이 서로 정이 들었음을 눈치챘다고 말하며, 이 연정의 나쁜 결과를 막기 위해 강력한 요정에게서 효험 있는 미약(媚藥)을 구해 왔노라 일렀다. 그러고는 브렝그웨인에게 혼례 날 저녁에 이주드와 마크 왕에게 그것을 마시게 하라고 지시했다.\n\n이주드와 트리스트람은 함께 배에 올랐다. 순풍이 돛을 채워 그들에게 순조로운 항해를 약속했다. 두 연인은 서로를 바라보며 한숨을 억누르지 못했다. 사랑은 그들의 가슴속에서처럼 그들의 입술 위에서도 온갖 불길을 밝히는 듯했다. 날이 더웠고, 그들은 갈증으로 괴로워했다. 이주드가 먼저 갈증을 호소했다. 트리스트람은 브렝그웨인이 경솔하게도 눈에 띄는 곳에 놓아둔, 미약이 든 병을 발견했다. 그는 그것을 집어 들어 매혹적인 이주드에게 얼마간 건네고, 나머지는 자신이 마셨다. 개 우댕이 잔을 핥았다. 배는 콘월에 도착했고, 이주드는 마크 왕과 혼인했다. 늙은 군주는 자신의 신부에 흠뻑 빠졌고, 트리스트람에 대한 그의 감사는 끝이 없었다. 그는 그에게 온갖 영예를 안겨 주고 그를 자신의 궁전 시종장으로 삼았으니, 이로써 그는 언제든 왕비에게 드나들 수 있게 되었다.\n\n왕의 혼례에 뒤이은 궁정의 축연이 한창이던 어느 날, 정체 모를 한 음유시인이 모습을 드러냈는데, 그는 독특하게 만들어진 하프를 지니고 있었다. 그는 마크 왕이 한 가지 청을 들어주기 전에는 그것을 연주하지 않겠다고 버티어 왕의 호기심을 자극했다. 왕이 그의 청을 들어주겠노라 약속하자, 다름 아닌 사라센 기사이자 아름다운 이주드를 사모하는 팔라메데스 경이었던 그 음유시인은, 하프에 맞추어 노래를 부르며 약속된 선물로 이주드를 요구했다. 마크 왕은 기사도의 법도에 따라 그 청을 거절할 수 없었다. 부인은 말에 태워졌고, 의기양양한 연인에게 끌려갔다. 트리스트람이 그때 자리에 없었으며, 그들이 떠난 뒤에야 돌아왔다는 것은 굳이 말할 필요도 없으리라. 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지를 듣자 그는 자신의 로테를 움켜쥐고 해변으로 달려갔는데, 그곳에는 이미 이주드와 그녀의 새 주인이 배에 올라 있었다. 트리스트람이 로테를 연주하자, 그 소리가 이주드의 귀에 닿아 그녀는 깊이 감동했고, 이에 팔라메데스 경은 그 정체 모를 악사를 보고자 그녀와 함께 다시 뭍으로 돌아왔다. 트리스트람은 기회를 엿보다가 부인의 말고삐를 붙잡고 그녀와 함께 숲속으로 뛰어들면서, 자신의 연적에게 \"하프로 얻은 것을 로테로 잃었구려\" 하고 비웃듯 일러 주었다. 팔라메데스가 뒤쫓아 와 막 결투가 시작되려 했으니, 그 결과는 이 용맹한 두 기사 가운데 하나에게 치명적일 수밖에 없었다. 그러나 이주드가 둘 사이로 끼어들어 팔라메데스에게 말했다. \"그대는 나를 사랑한다고 하니, 내가 이제 하려는 청을 거절하지는 않겠지요?\" \"부인이여,\" 그가 대답했다. \"그대의 분부대로 하겠소이다.\" \"그렇다면,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이 싸움을 그치고 아서 왕의 궁정으로 가서, 나를 대신해 귀네비어 왕비께 인사를 전하세요. 세상에는 두 여인, 곧 그분과 나뿐이며, 두 연인, 곧 그분의 연인과 나의 연인뿐이라고 전해 주세요. 그리고 앞으로는 내가 있는 어떤 곳에도 그대는 오지 마세요.\" 팔라메데스는 눈물을 터뜨렸다. \"아, 부인이여,\" 그가 말했다. \"그대의 뜻에 따르겠소. 다만 부디 영원히 그대의 마음을 내게 굳게 닫아 버리지는 마시기를 간청하오.\" \"팔라메데스,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"내가 만일 첫사랑을 저버린다면, 다시는 기쁨을 맛보지 못하게 되기를.\" 그리하여 팔라메데스는 자신의 길을 떠나갔다. 두 연인은 한 주일을 숨어 지냈고, 그 뒤 트리스트람은 이주드를 그녀의 남편에게 돌려보내며, 앞으로는 음유시인에게 다른 방식으로 보상하라고 조언했다.\n\n왕은 트리스트람에게 큰 감사를 표했으나, 마음 깊은 곳에서는 그에 대한 쓰라린 질투를 품고 있었다. 어느 날 트리스트람과 이주드가 그녀의 사실(私室)에 단둘이 있었다. 안드레라는 이름의 비열하고 비겁한 궁정 기사가 열쇠 구멍으로 그들을 엿보았다. 그들은 체스판을 앞에 두고 앉아 있었으나, 정작 놀이에는 마음을 두고 있지 않았다. 안드레는 먼저 왕의 의심을 불러일으킨 다음 왕을 데려와, 그들의 거동을 지켜볼 수 있는 자리에 세웠다. 왕은 자신의 의심을 굳히기에 충분한 것을 보았고, 칼을 빼어 든 채 방 안으로 뛰어들어, 트리스트람이 미처 경계하기도 전에 하마터면 그를 베어 버릴 뻔했다. 그러나 트리스트람은 칼날을 피해 자신의 칼을 빼어, 비겁한 군주를 앞으로 몰아세우며 궁전의 온 방들을 가로질러 그를 쫓았다. 그러는 내내 칼의 옆면으로 그를 거듭 후려쳤고, 왕은 헛되이 자신의 기사들에게 구해 달라 외쳤다. 그들은 그를 위해 나설 마음이 없었거나, 감히 끼어들지 못했다.\n\n트리스트람 경의 이야기가 누린 크나큰 인기를 입증하는 사실 하나는, 이탈리아 시인 보이아르도와 아리오스토가 이 이야기에 근거하여, 사랑과 미움이라는 상반된 효과를 일으키는 두 마법의 샘이라는 착상을 빚어냈다는 점이다. 보이아르도는 미움의 샘을 이렇게 묘사한다.\n\n\"아름다웠어라, 그 샘은, 온통 황금으로 새겨지고, 설화석고로 새겨져, 풍요롭고 진귀하였네. 그 맑은 샘물 안에서 그대는 보았으리, 꽃 핀 가장자리가 싱그럽고 곱게 비친 모습을. 현자 멀린이 그 샘을 빚었다고 전설은 전하나니, 용맹한 트리스트람이 아름다운 이주드를 사모하던 그때, 훌륭한 편력 기사가 그곳에 당도하면 마법의 물결 속에서 망각을 들이켜, 가망 없는 사랑을 버리고 때 이른 무덤을 면하게 하려 함이었네.\n\n\"그러나 그 전사의 사악한 운명은 결코 허락지 않았으니, 그의 발길이 그 샘의 마법 어린 가장자리에 닿는 것을. 쉴 새 없이, 자랑스러운 모험을 좇아 떠돌며, 그는 뭍을 누비고 또 바다를 가로질렀건만.\" . . . . . . .\n\n이 사건이 있은 후 트리스트람은 왕국에서 추방당했고, 이소우데는 강가에 서 있는 탑 안에 갇히고 말았다. 트리스트람은 사랑하는 여인과 좀 더 이야기를 나누지 못한 채로는 차마 떠날 수 없었다. 그래서 그는 숲속에 몸을 숨기고 있다가, 마침내 잔가지의 껍질을 정교하게 벗겨 그녀의 창문 아래로 강물에 흘려보내는 방법으로 그녀의 주의를 끄는 데 성공했다. 이런 방법으로 두 사람은 여러 차례 은밀한 만남을 가질 수 있었다. 트리스트람은 숲속에 머물면서 개 후댕이 잡아 오는 사냥감으로 연명했다. 이 충직한 짐승은 사냥 솜씨가 누구에게도 뒤지지 않았고, 또한 주인이 몸을 숨기고자 한다는 것을 너무도 잘 알고 있어서, 사냥감을 쫓을 때에도 결코 짖는 법이 없었다. 마침내 트리스트람은 떠났으나, 자신을 기억해 줄 정표로 후댕을 이소우데에게 남겨 두었다.\n\n트리스트람 경은 여러 나라를 떠돌며 가장 위험천만한 일들을 해내고 영광으로 자신을 뒤덮었으나, 사랑하는 이소우데와 헤어진 처지가 못내 불행하기만 했다. 그러던 중에 마크 왕의 영토가 이웃 족장의 침략을 받았고, 왕은 어쩔 수 없이 조카에게 도움을 청해야 했다. 트리스트람은 부름에 응해, 숙부의 가신들을 이끌고 적을 나라 밖으로 몰아냈다. 마크는 감사한 마음으로 가득했고, 총애를 되찾고 사랑하는 이소우데와의 교제도 회복한 트리스트람은 행복의 정점에 이른 듯했다. 그러나 슬픈 반전이 가까이 다가와 있었다.\n\n트리스트람은 페레딘이라는 친구를 데리고 왔는데, 브르타뉴 왕의 아들이었다. 이 젊은 기사는 이소우데 왕비를 보고는 그 매력에 저항할 수 없었다. 친구가 왕비를 사랑하고 있으며 그 사랑이 보답을 받고 있다는 것을 알았기에, 페레딘은 자신의 마음을 숨겼으나, 마침내 건강이 무너지고 자신이 죽음에 가까워졌다고 여기게 되었다. 그러자 그는 아름다운 왕비에게 그녀를 사랑하는 까닭에 죽어 간다고 편지를 썼다.\n\n상냥한 이소우데는 트리스트람의 친구를 향한 일순간의 연민에서, 그를 다시 살려 낼 만큼 다정하고 측은한 답장을 보냈다. 며칠 뒤 트리스트람이 이 편지를 발견했다. 가장 끔찍한 질투가 그의 영혼을 사로잡았다. 그는 페레딘을 죽이려 했으나, 페레딘은 간신히 몸을 피했다. 그러고 나서 트리스트람은 말에 올라 숲으로 달려갔고, 그곳에서 열흘 동안 쉬지도 먹지도 않았다. 마침내 그는 한 처녀에 의해, 샘가에 거의 죽은 듯이 누워 있는 상태로 발견되었다. 처녀는 그를 알아보고 그의 주의를 끌어 보려 했으나 헛수고였다. 결국 처녀는 그가 음악을 사랑한다는 것을 떠올리고는, 가서 자신의 하프를 가져와 연주했다. 트리스트람은 망연한 상태에서 깨어났다. 눈물이 흘렀다. 그는 한결 편하게 숨을 쉬었고, 처녀에게서 하프를 받아 들고는 흐느낌으로 갈라진 목소리로 이 노래를 불렀다.\n\n\"지난날 나는 감미롭게 노래했네, 다정한 사랑이 내 노래를 완성해 주었지. 이제 나의 기예는 오직 내 존재를 갉아먹는 비통만을 드러낼 뿐이라네. 매혹의 사랑이여, 황홀한 권능이여, 내 가장 어린 시절부터 받들어 온 그대여, 만물 위에 생명을 쏟아붓는 그대여, 사랑이여! 그대는 내 생명을 삼키는구나. 죽음의 시각에 나 그대에게 비노니, 이소우데여, 가장 사랑스러운 원수여, 한때는 더 다정할 수 있었던 그대여, 내가 떠난 뒤에도 나를 잊지 말아 다오. 내 무덤 위로 지나는 이들이 내가 낮이 누워 있는 동안 자주 읽으리라, '사랑에 있어 트리스트람과 견줄 자 아무도 없었으나, 그녀는 그를 죽게 내버려 두었노라.'\"\n\n트리스트람은 노래를 마치고 나서, 그것을 옮겨 적어 처녀에게 건네며, 부디 왕비에게 전해 달라고 간곡히 부탁했다.\n\n한편 이소우데 왕비는 트리스트람이 떠난 일로 위로받을 길이 없었다. 그녀는 그 원인이 자신이 페레딘에게 써 보낸 그 치명적인 편지에 있었음을 알아냈다. 죄가 없으면서도 자기 편지가 빚은 슬픈 결과에 절망한 그녀는, 페레딘에게 다시는 자신을 만나지 말라는 또 한 통의 편지를 써 보냈다. 불행한 연인은 이 잔혹한 명령에 따랐다. 그는 숲속으로 뛰어들었고, 어느 은자의 암자에서 슬픔과 사랑으로 죽고 말았다.\n\n이소우데는 트리스트람이 떠나 알 길 없는 운명에 처한 것을 한탄하며 나날을 보냈다. 어느 날 질투에 사로잡힌 남편이 들키지 않은 채 그녀의 방에 들어왔다가, 그녀가 다음과 같은 노래를 부르는 것을 엿듣게 되었다.\n\n\"내 목소리는 애처로운 비탄으로 기울고, 내 하프는 시름에 잠긴 가락으로 기우는구나. 아, 사랑이여! 즐거운 나날이란 마음 흡족한 더 행복한 이들을 위한 것이려니. 아, 트리스트람이여! 나에게서 멀리 떨어진 그대, 그대는 그칠 줄 모르는 고뇌에서 벗어나 있는가? 아! 그대를 그토록 사랑하는 여인에게서 한순간이라도 그대가 그럴 수 있겠는가?\"\n\n이 말을 들은 왕은 분노로 터져 나왔다. 그러나 이소우데는 그의 폭력을 두려워하기에는 너무도 비참한 처지였다. \"당신은 제 노래를 들으셨군요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"모든 것을 고백하겠어요. 저는 트리스트람을 사랑하며, 앞으로도 늘 사랑할 것입니다. 그분은 틀림없이 죽었어요, 저를 위해 죽었지요. 저는 더 이상 살고 싶지 않습니다. 제 고통을 끝내 줄 일격이라면 더없이 반가울 거예요.\"\n\n왕은 아름다운 이소우데의 비탄에 마음이 움직였고, 어쩌면 트리스트람이 죽었으리라는 생각이 그의 분노를 누그러뜨리는 데 도움이 되었는지도 모른다. 그는 왕비를 시녀들에게 맡기면서, 그녀가 절망한 나머지 스스로를 해치는 일이 없도록 각별히 살피라고 명했다.\n\n그러는 동안 트리스트람은 그처럼 제정신이 아닌 상태에서도, 양 떼를 약탈하고 오두막을 털곤 하던 타울라스라는 거대한 도적을 죽임으로써 목자들에게 더없이 중요한 봉사를 했다. 목자들은 트리스트람에게 감사하여, 그가 마땅한 보상을 받게 하고자 그를 의기양양하게 마크 왕에게 데려갔다. 옷을 반쯤만 걸친 채 앞에 선 야생인이 조카 트리스트람임을 마크가 알아보지 못한 것도 무리는 아니었다. 그러나 이 낯선 자가 행한 봉사에 감사한 마음으로 왕은 그를 잘 보살피라 명하고, 왕비와 그 시녀들에게 그를 맡겼다. 이러한 보살핌 속에서 트리스트람은 평정과 건강을 빠르게 되찾았으니, 이야기꾼의 말로는 그가 그 어느 때보다 잘생겨졌다고 한다. 마크 왕의 질투는 트리스트람의 건강과 준수한 외모와 더불어 되살아났고, 그토록 최근에 더 깊어진 감사의 빚에도 불구하고, 왕은 그를 다시 궁정에서 추방했다.\n\n트리스트람 경은 콘월을 떠나, 모험을 찾아 로에그리아[잉글랜드] 땅으로 나아갔다. 어느 날 그는 드넓은 숲으로 들어섰다. 작은 종소리가 누군가 거주하는 이가 가까이 있음을 알려 주었다. 그가 소리를 따라가니 한 은자가 있었는데, 은자는 그에게 이곳이 아르낭트 숲으로, 호수의 여인인 요정 비비안에게 속한 곳이라고 알려 주었다. 비비안은 아서 왕을 향한 사랑에 사로잡혀, 그를 이 숲으로 꾀어들일 방도를 찾아냈고, 마법으로 그를 포로로 붙들어 두면서 자신이 누구이며 무엇인지에 관한 기억을 모두 앗아 갔다는 것이었다. 은자는 그에게 원탁의 기사들이 모두 왕을 찾아 나섰으며, 그(트리스트람)는 이제 가장 위대하고 중요한 모험의 무대에 들어와 있다고 일러 주었다.\n\n이것만으로도 트리스트람이 그 탐색에 의욕을 불태우기에 충분했다. 그는 멀리 가기도 전에 아서 궁정의 한 기사와 마주쳤는데, 그는 다름 아닌 시종장 케이 경으로 밝혀졌고, 케이는 트리스트람에게 어디서 왔느냐고 물었다. 트리스트람이 \"콘월에서 왔소\"라고 대답하자, 케이 경은 콘월 기사를 두고 농담할 기회를 놓치지 않았다. 트리스트람은 그가 오해하도록 내버려 두었고, 심지어 그 오해를 더 굳혀 주기까지 했다. 다른 기사들 몇몇을 만났을 때에도 트리스트람은 그들과 겨루기를 사양했던 것이다. 그들은 어느 수도원에서 밤을 함께 보냈는데, 그곳에서 트리스트람은 그들의 온갖 농담을 묵묵히 참아 냈다. 시종장은 동료들에게, 이튿날 일찍 출발하여 길에서 콘월 기사를 가로막고, 함께 창 시합을 하자고 우길 때 그가 겁에 질리는 꼴을 구경하는 재미를 누리자고 일러두었다. 이튿날 아침 트리스트람은 혼자 남은 자신을 발견했다. 그는 갑옷을 차려입고 탐색을 계속하러 길을 나섰다. 곧 그는 앞쪽에서 시종장과 세 기사가 길을 가로막고 서서 시합을 하자고 우기는 것을 보았다. 트리스트람은 한참 동안 사양하다가, 마침내 마지못해 자세를 잡았다. 그는 그들과 차례차례 맞붙어 넷 모두를 사람과 말 가릴 것 없이 쓰러뜨린 다음, 자신들의 벗인 콘월 기사를 잊지 말라고 이르고는 말을 달려 가 버렸다.\n\n트리스트람이 얼마 가지 않았을 때, 한 처녀를 만났는데, 처녀가 소리쳤다. \"아, 나리! 어서 서둘러 가셔서, 끔찍한 배신을 막아 주세요!\" 트리스트람은 그녀를 돕고자 날듯이 달려갔고, 곧 한 기사가 다른 세 기사에게 땅에 쓰러뜨려져, 목을 베려고 투구의 끈이 풀리고 있는 광경을 보게 되었다.\n\n트리스트람은 구하러 날듯이 달려가, 창 한 번을 휘둘러 공격자 가운데 하나를 죽였다. 발을 다시 딛고 일어선 그 기사는 또 하나를 자기 복수의 제물로 삼았고, 세 번째 자는 달아났다. 구출된 기사가 투구의 면갑을 들어 올리자, 길고 흰 수염이 가슴 위로 흘러내렸다. 이 기사의 위엄과 존엄한 풍모를 보고 트리스트람은 그가 다름 아닌 아서 본인이 아닐까 의심하게 되었고, 군주는 그의 추측을 확인해 주었다. 트리스트람이 그 앞에 무릎을 꿇으려 하자 아서는 그를 두 팔로 받아 안고는 이름과 나라를 물었다. 그러나 트리스트람은 지금 비밀을 요하는 탐색 중이라는 이유를 들어 밝히기를 사양했다. 바로 이 순간, 트리스트람을 구조에 나서게 했던 처녀가 앞으로 달려 나와 왕의 손을 붙잡고, 그의 손가락에서 요정이 준 반지를 빼냈으니, 그 행위로 마법이 풀렸다. 이성과 기억을 되찾은 아서는 트리스트람에게 자신의 궁정에 머무르며 영예와 작위를 받으라고 권했다. 그러나 트리스트람은 모두 사양하고, 다만 왕이 자신의 기사들의 손에 안전하게 맡겨지는 것을 볼 때까지만 동행하기로 했다. 곧이어 헥토르 데 마리스가 말을 달려와 왕에게 인사하자, 왕은 그를 트리스트람에게 자신의 가장 용감한 기사 가운데 하나로 소개했다. 트리스트람은 왕과 그 충직한 신하에게 작별을 고하고, 탐색을 계속했다.\n\n우리는 그의 생애에서 이 시기를 가득 채운 모든 모험을 두루 따라갈 수는 없다. 다만 이렇게만 말해 두면 충분하리라. 그는 모든 경우에 참된 기사의 의무를 다하여, 억압받는 이들을 구하고, 잘못을 바로잡고, 악습을 없애고, 불의를 억눌렀으니, 이렇듯 끊임없는 행동으로 사랑하는 여인과 떨어져 있는 고통을 덜고자 애썼다. 그러는 동안 사랑하는 트리스트람과 헤어진 이소우데는 무기력과 회한 속에서 나날을 보냈다. 마침내 그녀는 연인의 소식을 듣고 싶은 갈망을 더는 억누를 수 없었다. 그녀는 편지를 써서, 자신의 충직한 브렝귄의 조카인 시녀 가운데 하나를 시켜 보냈다. 어느 날 트리스트람은 노고에 지쳐 말에서 내려 샘가에 몸을 누이고 잠들어 있었다. 이소우데 왕비의 시녀가 같은 샘에 다다랐다가, 트리스트람의 말 파스브뢸을 알아보았고, 이내 잠들어 있는 그 주인을 알아차렸다. 그는 야위고 창백했으니, 사랑하는 여인과 헤어져 겪는 고통의 자취가 또렷이 드러나 있었다. 시녀는 그를 깨워 가지고 온 편지를 건넸고, 트리스트람은 연인에게 더없이 달콤한 즐거움, 곧 사모하는 대상의 소식을 듣고 그에 관해 이야기하는 즐거움을 누렸다. 그는 시녀에게, 아서가 선포한 성대한 마상 시합이 열린 뒤로 돌아가는 것을 미루어 달라고 청했고, 용감하고 충직한 기사 페르시데스의 성으로 그녀를 데려갔으며, 페르시데스는 그녀를 크게 배려하여 맞이했다.\n\n트리스트람은 이소우데 왕비의 시녀를 마상 시합으로 데려가, 왕비의 귀부인들 사이 발코니에 그녀를 앉혀 두었다.\n\n\"그가 흘끗 보니 위풍당당한 회랑들이 있어, 귀부인이며 처녀들이 저마다 자기네 왕비를 흠모하여 티 없는 아이를 기리며 흰옷을 입었고, 더러는 흩뿌려진 보석들로 단장하여, 마치 불꽃이 섞여든 처녀의 눈 같은 둑처럼 보였다. 그는 단 한 번 보고는, 다시 눈을 가렸다.\" ─「마지막 마상 시합」.\n\n그러고 나서 그는 시합에 참가했다. 그의 힘과 용맹은 무엇으로도 능가할 수 없었다. 란슬롯은 그를 찬탄했고, 어떤 은밀한 예감에서, 그토록 늠름하고 노련한 기사와 그날의 영예를 두고 다투기를 사양했다. 아서는 승자를 맞이하려 발코니에서 내려왔다. 그러나 겸손하고 헌신적인 트리스트람은 이소우데의 사자가 지켜보는 가운데 상을 거머쥔 것으로 만족하여, 그녀와 함께 빠져나가 자취를 감추었다.\n\n이튿날 시합이 다시 시작되었다. 트리스트람은 정체가 드러나지 않도록 다른 갑옷을 둘렀다. 그러나 그가 휘두르는 무시무시한 일격으로 이내 정체가 드러나고 말았다. 아서와 귀네버는 그가 전날 상을 거머쥔 바로 그 기사임을 의심하지 않았다. 아서의 늠름한 기개가 들끓었다. 그는 호수의 란슬롯과 가웨인 경 다음으로 원탁에서 가장 뛰어난 기사로 꼽혔다. 그는 남몰래 가서 무장을 갖추고는, 식별할 수 없는 갑옷을 입고 시합에 들어섰다. 그는 트리스트람과 시합을 벌여 그를 안장에서 흔들리게 했으나, 그가 누군지 몰랐던 트리스트람은 그를 안장에서 떨어뜨렸다. 아서는 몸을 추슬렀고, 낯선 기사의 실력을 시험해 본 것으로 만족하여, 란슬롯에게 이 모험을 마무리하고 원탁의 영예를 지켜 내라 명했다. 란슬롯 경은 군주의 명에 따라 트리스트람을 공격했는데, 트리스트람의 창은 앞선 싸움들에서 이미 부러져 있었다. 그러나 이런 종류의 결투에는 한 가지 법이 있었으니, 기사는 창이 부러진 뒤에는 칼로 싸워야 하며, 상대의 창을 방패로 막기를 거부해서는 안 된다는 것이었다. 트리스트람은 란슬롯의 돌격을 방패로 받았는데, 그 무시무시한 창이 방패를 뚫지 못할 리 없었다. 창은 트리스트람의 옆구리에 상처를 입히고는, 부러지면서 그 쇠끝을 상처 안에 남겼다. 그러나 트리스트람 또한 칼로 란슬롯의 투구를 어찌나 힘차게 내리쳤던지, 투구를 쪼개고 그의 머리에 상처를 입혔다. 상처는 깊지 않았으나, 피가 눈으로 흘러들어 한순간 그의 눈을 멀게 했고, 자신이 치명상을 입었다고 여긴 트리스트람은 시합장에서 물러났다. 란슬롯은 평생 그런 일격을 받아 본 적이 없다고 왕에게 말했다.\n\n트리스트람은 자신의 종자 구베르나일에게 서둘러 갔고, 종자는 쇠끝을 빼내어 상처를 싸매 주어 그를 곧바로 편하게 해 주었다. 트리스트람은 시합이 끝난 뒤 천막 안에 틀어박혀 있었으나, 아서는 원탁 모든 기사들의 동의를 얻어 그에게 둘째 날의 영예를 내렸다. 그러나 이틀의 승자가 동일 인물이라는 것은 더 이상 비밀이 아니었고, 질문을 받은 구베르나일은 그가 다름 아닌 콘월 왕의 조카, 레오나이의 트리스트람 경임을 확인해 줌으로써, 란슬롯과 아서의 의심을 굳혀 주었다.\n\n그의 빼어난 용맹에 보답하고자 하고, 그의 숙부 마크가 그를 배은망덕하게 추방했음을 알고 있던 아서 왕은, 이 기회를 기꺼이 이용하여 트리스트람을 자신의 궁정에 머무르게 하려 했다. 원탁의 기사들 모두가, 이보다 더 합당한 동료를 찾기란 불가능하리라고 환호로 선언했다. 그러나 트리스트람은 이미 모험을 찾아 떠난 뒤였고, 이소우데 왕비의 시녀는 자기 여주인에게로 돌아갔다.\n\n# SIR TRISTRAM’S BATTLE WITH SIR LAUNCELOT\n\n트리스트람 경은 어느 숲을 지나가다가 열 명의 사내가 싸우는 것을 보았는데, 한 사람이 아홉 명을 상대로 싸우고 있었다. 그래서 그는 그 기사들에게로 말을 달려가 싸움을 그치라고 외치며, 이렇게 많은 기사가 한 사람을 상대로 싸우는 것은 스스로에게 크나큰 수치를 안기는 일이라고 명했다. 그러자 기사들의 우두머리가 대답했는데, 그의 이름은 브뢰즈 상 피티에 경으로, 당시 살아 있는 가장 비열한 기사였다. \"기사 양반, 우리 일에 끼어들어 무엇을 어쩌겠다는 거요? 현명하다면 왔던 길로 그냥 떠나시오, 이 기사는 우리 손아귀를 벗어나지 못할 테니.\" \"그것은 안타까운 일이오.\" 트리스트람 경이 말했다. \"이토록 훌륭한 기사가 이토록 비겁하게 살해당하다니, 그러므로 경고하건대 나는 내 온 힘을 다해 그를 도우리다.\"\n\n그러자 트리스트람 경은 자기 말에서 내렸는데, 저들이 걸어서 싸우고 있었으므로 자신의 말이 죽임을 당하지 않도록 하기 위함이었다. 그리고 그는 오른쪽으로 왼쪽으로 어찌나 힘차게 내리쳤던지, 거의 한 번 칠 때마다 기사를 한 명씩 쓰러뜨릴 정도였다. 마침내 그들은 브뢰즈 상 피티에와 더불어 탑 안으로 달아나, 트리스트람 경을 문밖에 가두어 버렸다. 그러자 트리스트람 경은 구해 준 기사에게로 되돌아가, 그가 나무 아래에 앉아 심하게 다쳐 있는 것을 발견했다. \"고귀한 기사여,\" 그가 말했다. \"그대는 어떠하오?\" \"기사 양반,\" 팔라메데스 경이 말했는데, 바로 그였기 때문이다, \"그대의 크나큰 선의에 감사하오, 그대가 나를 죽음에서 구해 주었으니.\" \"그대의 이름은 무엇이오?\" 트리스트람 경이 말했다. 그가 말했다. \"내 이름은 팔라메데스 경이오.\" \"그렇게 말하는가?\" 트리스트람 경이 말했다. \"이제 알라, 그대는 이 세상에서 내가 가장 미워하는 사람이로다, 그러니 채비를 하라, 내 그대와 싸우려 하노라.\" \"그대의 이름은 무엇이오?\" 팔라메데스 경이 말했다. \"내 이름은 트리스트람 경, 그대의 불구대천의 원수로다.\" \"그럴지도 모르오.\" 팔라메데스 경이 말했다. \"허나 오늘 그대는 내게 너무 많은 것을 베풀었으니, 내 어찌 그대와 싸우겠소. 더구나 나와 겨루는 것은 그대에게 명예가 되지 못하오, 그대는 멀쩡하고 나는 부상을 입었으니. 그러니 굳이 나와 겨루고자 한다면, 내게 날을 정해 주시오, 그러면 내 어김없이 그대를 만나리다.\" \"그대의 말이 옳소.\" 트리스트람 경이 말했다. \"이제 그대에게 카멜롯 강가의 풀밭에서 나를 만날 것을 정하노니, 멀린이 기념비를 세운 그곳이오.\" 그리하여 두 사람은 합의했다. 그러고는 헤어져 각기 다른 길로 갔다. 트리스트람 경은 큰 숲을 지나 평원으로 들어섰고, 마침내 어느 수도원에 다다라 그곳에서 어진 사람과 함께 엿새 동안 쉬었다.\n\n그러고 나서 트리스트람 경은 떠나, 곧장 카멜롯의 멀린의 기념비로 말을 달려가, 그곳에서 팔라메데스 경을 찾아 주위를 둘러보았다. 그러자 그는 온통 흰 차림에 가린 방패를 든 늠름한 기사 하나가 자기를 향해 말을 달려오는 것을 알아보았다. 그가 가까이 오자 트리스트람 경이 큰 소리로 말했다. \"환영하오, 기사 양반, 그대는 약속을 잘 그리고 진실하게 지켰소이다.\" 그러고는 두 사람은 방패와 창을 갖추고, 말이 낼 수 있는 온 힘을 다해 서로 맞부딪쳤는데, 어찌나 사납던지 말과 기사가 다 함께 땅에 나뒹굴었다. 그리고 일어날 수 있게 되자마자 그들은 말을 버리고, 힘센 사내들답게 번쩍이는 검으로 서로 맞부딪쳐, 저마다 상대에게 놀랍도록 심한 상처를 입혀, 피가 풀밭 위로 흘러내렸다. 이렇게 그들은 네 시간 동안 싸우면서도 어느 한쪽도 다른 쪽에게 한 마디 말도 건네지 않았다. 그러다가 마침내 흰 기사가 입을 열어 말했다. \"기사 양반, 그대는 놀랍도록 잘 싸우오, 내 일찍이 본 어떤 기사 못지않게, 그러니 괜찮다면 그대의 이름을 말해 주시오.\" \"어찌하여 내 이름을 묻는가?\" 트리스트람 경이 말했다. \"그대는 팔라메데스 경이 아니오?\" \"아니오, 고귀한 기사여,\" 그가 말했다. \"나는 호수의 란슬롯 경이오.\" \"아아!\" 트리스트람 경이 말했다. \"내가 무슨 짓을 한 것인가? 그대는 이 세상에서 내가 가장 사랑하는 사람이거늘.\" \"고귀한 기사여,\" 란슬롯 경이 말했다. \"그대의 이름을 말해 주시오.\" \"참으로,\" 그가 말했다. \"내 이름은 리오네스의 트리스트람 경이오.\" \"아아! 아아!\" 란슬롯 경이 말했다. \"내게 무슨 변고가 닥친 것인가!\" 그러면서 란슬롯 경은 무릎을 꿇고 자기 검을 그에게 바쳤고, 트리스트람 경도 무릎을 꿇고 자기 검을 그에게 바쳤으니, 이렇게 서로가 상대에게 영예를 돌렸다. 그러고는 두 사람 모두 그 돌로 가서, 그 위에 앉아 투구를 벗고 서로 백 번씩 입을 맞추었다. 그러고 나서 곧 그들은 카멜롯을 향해 말을 달렸는데, 가는 길에 가웨인 경과 가헤리스 경을 만났으니, 이들은 트리스트람 경을 데려오기 전까지는 결코 궁정으로 돌아오지 않겠다고 아서에게 약속했던 터였다.\n\n\"되돌아가게.\" 란슬롯 경이 말했다. \"그대들의 탐색은 끝났네, 내가 트리스트람 경을 만났으니. 보게, 여기 그가 몸소 있도다.\" 그러자 가웨인 경은 기뻐하며 트리스트람 경에게 말했다. \"환영하오.\" 이때 아서 왕이 다가왔고, 트리스트람 경이 거기 있음을 알아채자 그에게로 달려가 그의 손을 잡고 말했다. \"트리스트람 경, 그대는 일찍이 이 궁정에 온 어떤 기사 못지않게 환영받는 사람이오.\" 그러자 트리스트람 경은 왕에게, 자신이 팔라메데스 경과 겨루려고 이곳에 왔던 경위와, 어떻게 그를 브뢰즈 상 피티에와 아홉 기사로부터 구해 냈는지를 이야기했다. 그러자 아서 왕은 트리스트람 경의 손을 잡고 원탁으로 갔고, 귀네비어 왕비가 많은 귀부인을 거느리고 다가왔으며, 모든 귀부인이 한목소리로 말했다. \"환영하오, 트리스트람 경.\" \"환영하오.\" 기사들이 말했다. \"환영하오.\" 아서가 말했다. \"가장 훌륭한 기사 가운데 하나이며, 세상에서 가장 점잖고, 가장 명예로운 사람이여, 온갖 종류의 사냥에서 그대는 으뜸의 영예를 지녔으며, 온갖 뿔피리 부는 법에서 그대는 시초이며, 사냥과 매사냥의 온갖 용어에서 그대는 창안자이며, 온갖 악기에서 그대는 가장 빼어난 솜씨를 지녔으니, 그러므로 점잖은 기사여,\" 아서가 말했다. \"그대는 이 궁정에서 환영받는 사람이오.\" 그러고 나서 아서 왕은 가히 상상할 수 있는 가장 큰 위엄과 잔치를 베풀어 트리스트람 경을 원탁의 기사로 삼았다.\n\n# SIR TRISTRAM AS A SPORTSMAN\n\n트리스트람은 로맨스 작가들에 의해 사냥에 관한 모든 일에서 위대한 권위자이자 본보기로 자주 언급된다. 「선녀 여왕」에서 트리스트람은 칼리도어 경의 물음에 답하여 자신의 이름과 혈통을 알려 주고, 이렇게 끝맺는다.\n\n\"이 모든 날을 나는 음란하게 보내지 않았고, 내 어린 시절의 꽃송이를 게으름 속에 흩뿌리지도 않았으며, 마땅한 바대로, 많은 고귀한 동무와 더불어 점잖은 품행과 그와 같은 의젓한 학식 안에서 길러졌으니,[51] 그중에서도 나의 가장 큰 즐거움은 언제나 내 또래들과 더불어 야생의 사냥감을 쫓는 것이었으니, 푸른 숲을 누비는 모든 것 가운데, 그중에 이제껏 보인 것으로 내게 알려지지 않은 것은 없도다.\n\n\"제 횃대 위에서 날개를 펼치는 매도, 높이 솟구치든 낮게 다가들든, 나는 그 비행의 가늠을 살피지 못하는 것이 없고, 그 모든 먹이며, 그 모든 식성을 알도다. 이것이 이 숲에서 자라난 우리의 기쁨이로다.\" ————\n\n# THE ROUND TABLE\n\n저 유명한 마법사 멀린은 원탁을 만드는 데 자신의 온갖 기예를 쏟아부었다. 그는 원탁을 빙 둘러싸는 자리를 열셋 만들었는데, 이는 열세 사도를 기리기 위함이었다. 이 가운데 오직 열두 자리만이 채워질 수 있었으니, 그나마도 가장 명성 높은 기사들만이 앉을 수 있었다. 열세 번째 자리는 배신자 유다의 자리를 뜻했다. 그 자리는 언제나 비어 있었다. 그것은 〈위험한 자리〉라 불렸으니, 일찍이 어느 무모하고 거만한 사라센 기사가 감히 거기에 앉았다가 땅이 갈라져 그를 삼켜 버린 이래로 그렇게 불렸다.\n\n“우리의 큰 홀에는 빈 의자 하나가 놓여 있었으니, 멀린이 세상을 떠나기 전에 만들어 놓은 것으로, 기이한 형상들이 새겨져 있었다. 그 형상들 사이로 뱀처럼 구불구불, 어떤 사람도 읽을 수 없는 언어로 쓰인 글자들의 두루마리가 흘러 다녔다. 멀린은 그것을 〈위험한 자리〉라 불렀으니, 선한 자에게도 악한 자에게도 위험하다 하였다. ‘그 자리에는,’ 그가 말하기를, ‘누구도 앉을 수 없으되, 앉는다면 제 자신을 잃으리라.’” —『성배(聖杯)』.\n\n어떤 마법의 힘이 각 자리마다 거기에 앉을 자격이 있는 기사의 이름을 새겨 넣었다. 그 누구도 자신보다 앞서 그 자리를 차지했던 기사를 용맹과 영광스러운 무훈에서 능가하지 못한다면 빈자리를 이어받을 수 없었다. 이 자격을 갖추지 못한 자는 보이지 않는 힘에 의해 거칠게 튕겨 나갔다. 이리하여 쓰러진 기사단의 동료를 대신하고자 나선 모든 이들이 시험을 받았다.\n\n주요한 자리 가운데 하나인 아일랜드의 모런트의 자리는 십 년 동안이나 비어 있었으며, 저 빼어난 용사가 트리스트람 경의 칼 아래 쓰러진 그때 이후로 그의 이름이 여전히 그 위에 남아 있었다. 이제 아서가 트리스트람의 손을 잡고 그 자리로 이끌었다. 그러자 곧바로 더없이 감미로운 소리가 들려오고, 그윽한 향기가 그곳을 가득 채웠다. 모런트의 이름은 사라지고, 트리스트람의 이름이 빛으로 환히 타올랐다. 트리스트람의 보기 드문 겸손함은 이제 가혹한 시험을 치러야 했으니, 원탁의 연대기를 보존하는 임무를 맡은 서기들이 입회했고, 그는 기사단의 법도에 따라 그 자리에 앉을 자격을 부여할 만한 어떤 무공을 이루었는지 밝혀야 했다. 이 의식이 끝나자 트리스트람은 모든 동료들의 축하를 받았다. 랜슬롯 경과 귀네비어는 이 기회를 빌려 그에게 아름다운 이졸데에 관한 이야기를 나누었고, 어떤 행복한 우연이 그녀를 로에그리아 왕국으로 데려와 주기를 바란다고 말했다.\n\n트리스트람이 이처럼 아서 왕의 궁정에서 존경받고 환대받는 동안, 더없이 음울하고 악의에 찬 질투가 마크의 영혼을 괴롭혔다. 그는 이졸데를 바라볼 때마다 그녀가 트리스트람을 사랑한다는 것을 떠올리지 않을 수 없었고, 조카의 행운은 그를 복수의 상념으로 몰아갔다. 그는 마침내 변장한 채 로에그리아 왕국으로 들어가 트리스트람을 몰래 습격하여 죽이기로 결심했다. 그는 자기 궁정에서 길러져 자신에게 충성하리라 여긴 두 기사를 데리고 갔다. 그리고 이졸데를 뒤에 남겨 두기를 원치 않았기에, 그녀를 시중들 시녀 둘과 충직한 브렝귄을 지목하여 함께 동행하게 했다.\n\n카멜롯 인근에 다다른 마크는 두 기사에게 자신의 계획을 털어놓았으나, 그들은 그것을 끔찍하게 여기며 거부했다. 아니, 그뿐 아니라 더 이상 그의 휘하에 머물지 않겠노라 선언하고는 그를 떠나 버렸다. 그러면서 자신들이 궁정으로 가서 아서 앞에 그를 고발하리라 짐작하게끔 만들었다. 마크로서는 그들과 맞서 그 고발에 반박할 필요가 있었으므로, 이졸데를 한 수도원에 남겨 둔 채 홀로 카멜롯을 향해 길을 갔다.\n\n마크는 멀리 말을 달리기도 전에 아서 궁정의 기사 한 무리와 마주쳤고, 그들을 피하려 했다. 그들이 마주치는 모든 낯선 기사에게 마상 시합을 청하는 습관이 있음을 알고 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 이미 늦었다. 그들은 그의 갑옷을 보고 그가 콘월의 기사임을 알아차렸으며, 곧바로 그를 데리고 한바탕 놀아 보기로 작정했다. 마침 그들 가운데에는 아서 왕의 어릿광대 다그네가 있었는데, 그는 몸이 비틀어지고 허약했으나 용기만큼은 모자라지 않았다. 마크가 다가오자 기사들은 다그네에게 호수의 랜슬롯 경 행세를 시켜 그 콘월 기사에게 도전하게 하기로 계획을 짰다. 그들은 일행 가운데 병이 난 한 사람의 갑옷을 다그네에게 입혀, 그 낯선 기사에게 결투를 청하도록 갈림길로 내보냈다. 마크는 자신의 상대가 겉모습으로는 결코 두려울 것 없는 자임을 보고 결투를 마다하지 않으려 했다. 그러나 그 난쟁이가 자신은 호수의 랜슬롯 경이라 외치며 말을 몰아 다가오자, 두려움이 그를 사로잡았고, 그는 말에 박차를 가해 일행의 함성과 웃음소리에 쫓기며 전속력으로 달아나 버렸다.\n\n한편 충직한 브렝귄과 함께 수도원에 머물던 이졸데는, 이따금 수도원에 잇닿은 숲속을 거니는 것에서만 유일한 위안을 찾았다. 거기, 나무들에 둘러싸인 샘가에서 그녀는 자신의 사랑을 생각하였고, 때로는 그 사랑의 고통이나 기쁨의 기억을 되살리는 노래에 자신의 목소리와 하프를 곁들였다. 어느 날 비열한 기사인 무자비한 브루즈가 그녀의 목소리를 듣고는 몸을 숨긴 채 가까이 다가왔다. 그녀는 이렇게 노래했다.\n\n“감미로운 고요여, 그늘진 정자여, 푸르른 안식처여, 그대들은 내 어지러운 마음을 쉬게 하라 손짓하건만, 나는, 그토록 애틋한 추억이 거기 솟아오르매, 온갖 메아리를 내 슬픔으로 일깨우는구나. “자연의 손길이 단장한 이 숲속에, 샘 하나 솟아나 수천 송이 꽃을 기르나니, 아! 내 신음이 어찌 그 모든 졸졸거림을 거드는가! 내 슬픈 두 눈이 어찌 그 눈물의 빗줄기로 샘을 불리는가!\n\n“그동안 나의 기사는 무엇을 하고 있을까? 그에게는 두 갑절의 보상이 주어졌으니, 사랑에서도 무예의 무훈에서도, 원탁이 그를 하늘로 드높이는구나! 트리스트람이여! 아아! 그는 이졸데의 외침을 듣지 못하네.”\n\n무자비한 브루즈는, 여느 비열한 자들과 마찬가지로 트리스트람의 팔심을 겪어 본 적이 있어 그를 그만큼 미워하던 터라, 그 아름다운 가인(歌人)의 입에서 그 이름이 흘러나오는 것을 듣고 이중의 충동에 떠밀려 숨었던 곳에서 뛰쳐나와 제 먹잇감에게 손을 댔다. 이졸데는 기절했고, 브렝귄은 비명으로 허공을 가득 채웠다. 브루즈는 이졸데를 자기 말을 매어 둔 곳으로 데려갔다. 그러나 그 짐승은 고삐를 풀고 달아나 얼마간 떨어진 곳에 가 있었다. 그는 어쩔 수 없이 그 아름다운 짐을 내려놓고 말을 뒤쫓아 가야 했다. 바로 그때 브렝귄의 외침에 이끌린 한 기사가 다가와 그녀의 슬픔의 까닭을 물었다. 그녀는 말을 하지 못한 채, 땅바닥에 정신을 잃고 누워 있는 자기 여주인을 가리킬 뿐이었다.\n\n그사이 브루즈는 돌아와 있었고, 그를 보고 다시 터져 나온 브렝귄의 외침은 그 낯선 이에게 슬픔의 까닭을 충분히 알려 주었다. 트리스트람은 브루즈를 향해 말에 박차를 가했고, 브루즈 또한 대비하고 있던 터라 맞서 달려들었다. 브루즈는 말에서 떨어져 죽은 척하며 꼼짝 않고 누워 있었다. 그러나 낯선 기사가 그를 내버려 두고 슬픔에 잠긴 여인들을 돌보러 가자, 그는 제 말에 올라타 달아나 버렸다.\n\n이제 그 기사는 이졸데에게 다가가 그녀의 머리를 부드럽게 받쳐 들고, 얼굴을 덮은 금빛 머리카락을 옆으로 쓸어내린 뒤, 한순간 그것을 바라보다가 외마디 소리를 지르며 정신을 잃고 뒤로 쓰러졌다. 브렝귄이 다가왔고, 그녀의 보살핌으로 여주인은 곧 생기를 되찾았다. 그러고는 두 사람이 쓰러진 용사에게로 관심을 돌렸다. 그들이 그의 투구 가리개를 들어 올리자, 트리스트람 경의 얼굴이 드러났다. 이졸데는 연인의 몸 위에 자신을 던져 그의 얼굴을 눈물로 적셨다. 그 따스함이 기사를 되살아나게 했고, 트리스트람은 깨어나 보니 자신이 사랑하는 이졸데의 품 안에 있었다.\n\n원탁의 법도란, 각 기사가 입단한 뒤 이후 열흘 동안 모험을 찾아 떠나야 한다는 것이었으며, 그동안 동료들이 변장한 갑옷 차림으로 그와 마주쳐 무예를 겨루어 볼 수 있었다. 트리스트람은 이제 이레째 밖에 나와 있었으니, 그동안 원탁의 가장 빼어난 기사들 여럿과 맞붙어 명예롭게 처신해 냈다. 남은 사흘 동안 이졸데는 그의 보호 아래 수도원에 머물렀고, 그런 다음 트리스트람 경의 호위를 받으며 시녀들과 함께 카멜롯 궁정에 있는 마크 왕에게 돌아가고자 길을 떠났다.\n\n이 행복한 여정은 트리스트람과 이졸데의 생애에서 가장 빛나는 시절 가운데 하나였다. 그는 프랑스인들이 〈트리올레〉라 이름 붙인 독특한 운율에 맞추어 하프로 노래를 지어 이를 기렸다.\n\n“아름다운 이졸데와 더불어, 그리고 사랑과 더불어, 아! 내가 누리는 삶은 얼마나 감미로운가! 이렇듯 영원토록 떠도는 것은 얼마나 복된가, 아름다운 이졸데와 더불어, 그리고 사랑과 더불어! 그녀가 원하는 대로 나는 살고 또 움직이니, 구름 한 점 없는 날들이 날들에 잇따르는구나. 아름다운 이졸데와 더불어, 그리고 사랑과 더불어, 아! 내가 누리는 삶은 얼마나 감미로운가!\n\n“동틀 녘부터 길을 떠나 나아가는데, 그대는 지치지 않았는가, 내 어여쁜 이여? 저 푸른 풀밭이 노닐자 손짓하는구나. 날마다 길을 떠나 나아가는 동안, 아! 저 그늘로 떠나가자꾸나, 설령 거기서 잠시 잠들기 위함일지라도! 동틀 녘부터 길을 떠나 나아가는데, 그대는 지치지 않았는가, 내 어여쁜 이여?”\n\n그들은 카멜롯에 다다랐고, 랜슬롯 경이 더없이 정성스레 그들을 맞이했다. 이졸데는 아서 왕과 귀네비어 왕비에게 소개되었으며, 두 사람은 그녀를 자매처럼 환대했다. 마크 왕이 두 콘월 기사의 고발로 인해 구금되어 있었으므로, 이졸데 왕비는 남편에게 돌아갈 수 없었다. 랜슬롯 경은 자신의 성 라 주아유즈 가르드를 벗들이 마음껏 쓰도록 내어 주었고, 그들은 거기에 거처를 정했다.\n\n마크 왕은 자신을 향한 고발이 사실임을 자백하거나, 아니면 고발자들과의 결투로 결백을 입증해야 하는 처지에 놓이자 전자를 택했다. 아서 왕은 그 죄가 실제로 저질러지지는 않았으므로 처벌을 면해 주되, 다만 자신의 크나큰 분노를 사지 않으려거든 조카에 대한 복수의 생각을 모두 버리라고 그에게 엄히 명했다. 왕과 그 궁정 앞에서 모든 당사자들은 격식을 갖추어 화해했다. 마크와 그의 왕비는 고향으로 떠났고, 트리스트람은 아서의 궁정에 남았다.\n\n————\n\n# SIR PALAMEDES\n\n트리스트람 경과 어여쁜 이주[이주드]가 아직 라 주아외즈 가르드에 머물고 있을 무렵, 어느 날 트리스트람 경은 갑옷을 걸치지 않고 창과 검 외에는 아무 무기도 지니지 않은 채 말을 타고 길을 나섰다. 그렇게 말을 달리다가 그는 두 기사가 싸우고 있는 곳에 이르렀는데, 한 사람은 우세를 점했고 다른 한 사람은 쓰러져 누워 있었다. 우세한 쪽 기사는 팔라메데스 경이었다. 팔라메데스 경은 트리스트람 경을 알아보고는 외쳤다. \"트리스트람 경, 이제 우리가 만났으니, 헤어지기 전에 우리의 묵은 원한을 풀어 보세.\" \"그 일이라면,\" 트리스트람 경이 말했다. \"지금껏 그리스도교도 가운데 내가 자기에게서 달아났노라고 자랑할 수 있었던 자는 없었거니와, 사라센인인 그대 또한 나에 대해 결코 그런 말을 하지 못할 것이오.\" 그러고는 트리스트람 경이 말을 달리게 하여 온 힘을 다해 곧장 팔라메데스 경에게 달려들어 그를 향해 창을 부러뜨렸다. 그런 다음 그는 검을 빼어 팔라메데스 경의 투구를 향해 여섯 차례 큰 일격을 가했다. 팔라메데스 경은 트리스트람 경이 갑옷을 걸치지 않았음을 보고 그의 무모함과 크나큰 어리석음에 놀라며 속으로 이렇게 말했다. \"내가 그와 맞붙어 그를 죽인다면, 나는 어디를 가든 수치를 면치 못하리라.\" 그러자 트리스트람 경이 소리쳐 말했다. \"이 비겁한 기사여, 어찌하여 나와 싸우려 하지 않는가? 그대의 온갖 악의를 내가 모조리 견뎌 내리라는 것을 의심하지 마시오.\" \"아, 트리스트람 경!\" 팔라메데스 경이 말했다. \"수치심 때문에 내가 그대와 싸울 수 없음을 그대도 알지 않소. 그대는 지금 맨몸이고 나는 무장을 했으니 말이오. 그러니 내가 그대에게 한 가지 묻는 물음에 답해 주기를 청하오.\"\n\"그것이 무엇인지 말해 보시오.\" 트리스트람 경이 말했다. \"이렇게 가정해 봅시다.\" 팔라메데스가 말했다. \"그대는 온전히 무장을 했고, 나는 지금 그대처럼 맨몸이라고 합시다. 그렇다면 그대는 진정한 기사도에 비추어 이제 내게 어떻게 하겠소?\" \"아!\" 트리스트람 경이 말했다. \"이제 그대의 뜻을 잘 알겠소, 팔라메데스 경. 그리고 하느님께 맹세코, 내가 하려는 말은 그대에 대한 두려움 때문에 하는 말이 아니오. 만일 그러한 처지라면 그대는 내게서 떠나가야 할 것이오. 나는 그대와 싸움을 벌이지 않을 것이기 때문이오.\" \"나 또한 그대와 싸우지 않으리다.\" 팔라메데스 경이 말했다. \"그러니 그대의 길로 말을 달려 가시오.\" \"그 일이라면 내가 택할 수 있는 바요.\" 트리스트람 경이 말했다. \"떠나든 머무르든 말이오. 하지만 팔라메데스 경, 나는 한 가지 일에 놀라움을 금치 못하오. 그대는 그토록 훌륭한 기사이면서도 세례를 받으려 하지 않는다는 점이오.\" \"그 일이라면,\" 팔라메데스 경이 말했다. \"나는 아직 세례를 받을 수 없소. 여러 해 전에 맺은 한 가지 서원 때문이오. 하지만 내 마음속으로는 우리의 구세주와 그분의 온화하신 어머니 마리아를 믿고 있소. 다만 나는 아직 치러야 할 싸움이 한 가지 남아 있으니, 그것을 끝내고 나면 기꺼운 마음으로 세례를 받겠소.\" \"내 머리를 걸고 말하건대,\" 트리스트람 경이 말했다. \"그 한 가지 싸움이라면 더 이상 찾아 헤맬 필요가 없소. 저기 그대가 쓰러뜨린 기사가 있지 않소. 자, 그의 갑옷을 입도록 나를 도와주시오. 내가 곧 그대의 서원을 이루어 주리다.\" \"그대의 뜻대로,\" 팔라메데스 경이 말했다. \"그리하리다.\" 그리하여 두 사람은 둑 위에 앉아 있는 그 기사에게 함께 말을 타고 갔다. 트리스트람 경이 그에게 인사를 건네니, 잔뜩 지친 그가 다시 인사로 답했다. \"기사여,\" 트리스트람 경이 말했다. \"청컨대 그대의 갑옷 일체를 내게 빌려 주시오. 나는 무장을 갖추지 못했는데, 이 기사와 싸움을 벌여야 하기 때문이오.\" \"기사여,\" 부상당한 기사가 말했다. \"기꺼운 마음으로 그것을 드리리다.\" 그리하여 트리스트람 경은 그 부상당한 기사의 갑옷을 벗겼는데, 그 기사의 이름은 갈레론 경이었으며, 갈레론 경은 할 수 있는 한 힘껏 트리스트람 경의 무장을 도왔다. 그러고 나서 트리스트람 경은 자신의 말에 올라타 손에 갈레론 경의 창을 쥐었다. 이에 팔라메데스 경도 채비를 갖추었고, 그리하여 두 사람은 서로를 향해 맹렬히 달려들어 각자 상대의 방패 한가운데를 내리쳤다. 팔라메데스 경의 창은 부러졌고, 트리스트람 경은 말을 쓰러뜨렸다. 그러자 팔라메데스 경은 말에서 뛰어내려 검을 빼어 들었다. 트리스트람 경이 그것을 보고는 자신도 말에서 내려 말을 나무에 매어 놓았다. 그런 다음 두 사람은 두 마리 들짐승처럼 맞붙어 서로를 후려쳤으며, 그렇게 두 시간이 넘도록 싸웠다. 트리스트람 경은 자주 팔라메데스 경에게 강한 일격을 가하여 그를 무릎 꿇게 했고, 팔라메데스 경은 트리스트람 경의 방패를 깨뜨리고 그에게 상처를 입혔다. 그러자 트리스트람 경은 가늠할 수 없을 만큼 노하여 팔라메데스 경에게 돌진해 어깨를 꿰뚫어 몹시 심한 상처를 입혔고, 운 좋게도 팔라메데스 경의 검을 그의 손에서 쳐내었다. 만일 팔라메데스 경이 검을 주우려고 몸을 숙였다면 트리스트람 경이 그를 죽였을 것이다. 그러자 팔라메데스 경은 서서 비통하기 그지없는 마음으로 자신의 검을 바라보았다. \"자,\" 트리스트람 경이 말했다. \"오늘 그대가 나를 유리한 처지에 두었듯, 이제 나는 그대를 유리한 처지에 두게 되었소. 하지만 트리스트람 경이 무기 없는 기사를 죽였노라는 말은 궁정에서든 훌륭한 기사들 사이에서든 결코 나오지 않을 것이오. 그러니 그대의 검을 집어 들고, 이 싸움을 끝까지 겨뤄 봅시다.\" 그러자 팔라메데스 경이 트리스트람 경에게 말했다. \"나는 이 싸움을 더 이상 하고 싶지 않소. 내가 그대에게 저지른 잘못은 그다지 크지 않으니, 그대가 원한다면 우리는 벗이 될 수도 있소. 내가 잘못을 저지른 까닭은 모두 왕비 라 벨 이주[이주드]를 향한 사랑 때문이며, 나는 그분이 여인들 가운데 견줄 데 없는 분임을 감히 주장하는 바요. 그 잘못으로 인해 그대는 내게 무겁고 비통한 일격을 여러 차례 가했고, 나 또한 그대에게 몇 차례 되돌려 주었소. 그러니 청컨대, 나의 주군 트리스트람 경이여, 내가 그대에게 저지른 모든 잘못을 용서해 주시고, 오늘 나를 가장 가까운 교회로 데려가 주시오. 먼저 나는 깨끗이 고해를 하고, 그런 다음 그대가 내가 참되게 세례 받는 것을 지켜봐 주시오. 그러고 나면 우리 함께 나의 주군이신 아서 왕의 궁정으로 말을 달려, 오순절 축연에 맞춰 그곳에 다다를 수 있을 것이오.\" \"이제 그대의 말에 오르시오.\" 트리스트람 경이 말했다. \"그대가 말한 대로 그리될 것이오.\" 그리하여 두 사람은 말에 올랐고, 갈레론 경도 그들과 함께 말을 달렸다. 그들이 칼라일의 교회에 이르자, 주교는 커다란 그릇에 물을 가득 채우라 명했다. 그리고 그 물을 축성한 다음, 그는 팔라메데스 경에게서 깨끗이 고해를 받고 그에게 세례를 베풀었으며, 트리스트람 경과 갈레론 경이 그의 대부가 되어 주었다. 그러고 나서 얼마 지나지 않아 그들은 그곳을 떠나 카멜롯을 향해 말을 달렸는데, 그곳에서는 고귀한 아서 왕과 왕비 귀네비어가 왕실 궁정을 열고 있었다. 왕과 온 궁정은 팔라메데스 경이 세례를 받은 것을 기뻐했다. 그런 다음 트리스트람 경은 다시 라 주아외즈 가르드로 돌아갔고, 팔라메데스 경은 자신의 길을 떠났다.\n\n이러한 일들이 있은 지 오래지 않아 가웨인 경이 브르타뉴에서 돌아와, 브레실리앙드 숲에서 그에게 일어난 모험을 아서 왕에게 이야기했다. 그곳에서 멀린이 그에게 말을 건넸으며, 왕에게 지체 없이 성배를 찾는 탐색에 나서도록 명하라고 당부했다는 것이었다. 아서 왕이 숙고하는 동안 트리스트람 경은 그 탐색에 나서기로 마음을 정했는데, 이 거룩한 모험을 이루어 낸다면 자신의 모든 죄를 사면받게 되리라는 것을 잘 알고 있었기에 더욱 기꺼이 나서고자 했다. 그는 즉시 브르타뉴 왕국을 향해 떠났으니, 그곳에서 멀린으로부터 성공을 확실히 하기 위해 따라야 할 올바른 방도에 관한 조언을 얻기를 바랐던 것이다.\n\n————\n\n# SIR TRISTRAM\n\n브르타뉴에 도착한 트리스트람은 호엘 왕이 반란을 일으킨 봉신과 전쟁을 벌이고 있으며, 적에게 심하게 몰리고 있음을 알게 되었다. 그의 가장 뛰어난 기사들은 얼마 전 전투에서 쓰러졌고, 그는 어디서 도움을 구해야 할지 알지 못했다. 트리스트람은 자진하여 도움을 자청했다. 그 제안은 받아들여졌고, 트리스트람이 이끌고 그의 본보기에 고무된 호엘의 군대는 완전한 승리를 거두었다. 가장 생생한 감사의 감정에 사무친 왕은 트리스트람의 출생을 알아본 뒤, 그에게 자기 딸을 아내로 주겠다고 제안했다. 공주는 아름답고 재주가 뛰어났으며, 콘월의 왕비와 같은 이름을 지니고 있었다. 그러나 이쪽은 로맨스 작가들에 의해 〈하얀 손의 이소우데〉라 불려, 〈아름다운 이소우데〉와 구별되었다.\n\n트리스트람의 마음을 뒤흔든 갈등을 우리가 어찌 묘사할 수 있겠는가? 그는 첫 번째 이소우데를 흠모했지만, 그녀를 향한 그의 사랑은 가망 없는 것이었고, 회한이 따르지 않는 것도 아니었다. 게다가 그가 이제 막 들어선 신성한 탐색은 그에게 완벽하게 순결한 삶을 요구했다. 행복한 운명이 매혹적인 공주 〈하얀 손의 이소우데〉를 통해 그의 모든 선한 결심을 지킬 가장 든든한 보호막을 마련해 준 것처럼 보였다. 이 마지막 생각이 그를 결심하게 했다. 두 사람은 결혼했고, 호엘 왕의 궁정에서 몇 달을 평온한 행복 속에 보냈다. 아내와 함께 있으며 트리스트람이 느끼는 기쁨은 날마다 커져 갔다. 그가 성배를 찾아 떠나겠다고 맹세한 순간부터 어떤 내적인 은총이 그의 안에서 움직이는 듯했다. 그것은 마법의 사랑의 묘약이 지닌 힘마저 이겨 내는 것처럼 보였다.\n\n한동안 진압되었던 전쟁이 이제 다시 터져 나왔다. 트리스트람은 늘 그렇듯 모든 위험의 선봉에 섰다. 적은 잇따른 싸움에서 패했고, 마침내 자신의 본거지 도시에 틀어박혔다. 트리스트람은 그 도시에 대한 공격을 이끌었다. 그가 성벽을 기어오르려고 사다리에 올랐을 때, 포위당한 자들이 그에게 던진 바위 조각이 머리를 쳤다. 그것은 그를 땅으로 쓰러뜨렸고, 그는 의식을 잃은 채 누워 있었다.\n\n의식을 회복하자마자 그는 아내에게로 옮겨 달라고 요청했다. 외과술에 능한 공주는 자신을 제외한 그 누구도 사랑하는 남편에게 손대는 것을 허락하지 않으려 했다. 그녀의 고운 두 손이 그의 상처를 싸맸고, 트리스트람은 감사의 마음으로 그 손에 입을 맞추었으며, 그 감사는 사랑으로 자라나기 시작했다. 처음에는 이소우데의 헌신적인 보살핌이 큰 성공을 거두는 듯했다. 그러나 얼마 지나지 않아 이 그럴듯한 조짐은 사라졌고, 그녀의 온갖 정성에도 불구하고 병세는 날이 갈수록 심각해져 갔다.\n\n이런 곤혹스러운 상황에서, 트리스트람의 늙은 시종 하나가 주인에게 일깨워 주기를, 훗날 콘월의 왕비가 된 아일랜드의 공주가 지금만큼이나 절망적인 상황에서 한때 그를 낫게 한 적이 있다고 했다. 그는 〈하얀 손의 이소우데〉를 불러 자신이 예전에 치유받았던 일을 들려주고, 이소우데 왕비라면 자신을 낫게 할 수 있으리라 믿으며, 사람을 보내면 그녀가 자신을 구하러 와 줄 것이 틀림없다고 덧붙였다.\n\n〈하얀 손의 이소우데〉는 믿음직하고 항해에 능숙한 사람인 게스네스를 콘월로 보내는 데 동의했다. 트리스트람은 그를 불러 반지를 건네며 이렇게 말했다. \"이것을 콘월의 왕비에게 가져가게. 죽음에 가까운 트리스트람이 그녀의 도움을 청한다고 전하게. 그녀를 데려오는 데 성공하거든, 돌아올 때 배에 흰 돛을 달게. 그러면 배가 처음 시야에 들어올 때 우리가 자네의 성공을 알 수 있도록 말이네. 그러나 만약 이소우데 왕비가 거절하거든 검은 돛을 달게. 그것은 다가오는 내 죽음의 전조가 될 걸세.\"\n\nii., Sir Calidore encounters in the forest a young hunter, whom he\n\nthus describes:\n\n“Him steadfastly he marked, and saw to be\n\nA goodly youth of amiable grace, Yet but a slender slip, that scarce did see Yet seventeen yeares, but tall and faire of face, That sure he deem’d him to be of noble race. All in a woodman’s jacket he was clad Of Lincoln greene, belayd with silver lace; And on his head an hood with aglets sprad, And by his side his hunter’s horne he hanging had.\n\n“Buskins he wore of costliest cordwayne,\n\nPinckt upon gold, and paled part per part, As then the guize was for each gentle swayne; In his right hand he held a trembling dart, Whose fellow he before had sent apart; And in his left he held a sharp bore-speare, With which he wont to launch the salvage hart Of many a lyon, and of many a beare, That first unto his hand in chase did happen neare.”\n\n제2편에서 칼리도어 경[Sir Calidore]은 숲에서 젊은 사냥꾼 하나를 만나는데, 그를 이렇게 묘사한다.\n\n“그는 그 젊은이를 가만히 눈여겨보았으니, 사랑스럽고 우아한 한 어여쁜 청년이었다. 허나 아직은 가냘픈 가지에 지나지 않아, 열일곱 해도 채 넘기지 못한 듯하였으되, 키 크고 얼굴 고와 분명 귀한 혈통이라 여겼다. 온몸에는 사냥꾼의 웃옷을 걸쳤으니, 링컨산 초록 빛깔에 은빛 끈을 둘렀고, 머리에는 술 장식 흩뿌린 두건을 썼으며,[52] 그 곁에는 사냥꾼의 뿔피리를 매달고 있었다.\n\n“가장 값진 코르도바 가죽으로 지은 장화를 신었으니, 황금 위에 무늬 박고 조각조각 줄지어 둘렀는데,[53] 그때는 점잖은 젊은이라면 다들 그런 차림이었다. 오른손에는 떨리는 투창 하나를 들고 있었으니, 그 짝은 이미 앞서서 던져 보냈음이라, 왼손에는 날카로운 멧돼지 창을 쥐었으니, 그 창으로 그는 거친 심장을 찌르곤 하였다. 뒤쫓는 길에 그의 손길 가까이 처음으로 다가온, 숱한 사자와 숱한 곰의 심장을 말이다.”\n\n————\n\n페르세발의 아버지와 두 형은 전투나 마상 시합에서 목숨을 잃었고, 그리하여 그의 어머니는 가문의 마지막 희망인 그를 데리고 외딴 고장으로 물러났으며, 그곳에서 그는 무기와 기사도에 관해 전혀 알지 못한 채 자라났다. 그에게는 \"작은 스코틀랜드 창\" 외에는 어떤 무기도 허락되지 않았는데, 그것이야말로 그의 어머니가 \"남편의 훌륭한 장비\" 중에서 유일하게 숲으로 가지고 들어온 물건이었다. 페르세발은 이 창을 다루는 데 무척이나 능숙해져서, 그것으로 식탁에 올릴 사냥감을 잡는 것은 물론이요, 날아가는 새까지 떨어뜨릴 수 있었다. 그러던 어느 날, 페르세발은 숲속에서 완전한 갑주를 갖춘 다섯 명의 기사를 보고는 군사적 명성에 대한 욕망에 사로잡혔다. 그는 어머니에게 물었다. \"어머니, 저기 저들은 무엇인가요?\" \"저들은 천사들이란다, 아들아.\" 어머니가 말했다. \"맹세코 저는 가서 저들과 함께 천사가 되겠어요.\" 그리하여 페르세발은 길로 나아가 그들을 만났다. \"착한 젊은이여, 말해 보게.\" 그들 중 하나가 말했다. \"오늘이나 어제 이 길로 지나가는 기사를 보지 못했는가?\" \"기사가 무엇인지 저는 모릅니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"나와 같은 자가 기사라네.\" 기사가 말했다. \"내가 묻는 것에 답해 준다면, 그대가 묻는 것에 답해 주겠네.\" \"기꺼이 그리하지요.\" 오웨인 경이 말했으니, 그것이 그 기사의 이름이었다. \"이것은 무엇입니까?\" 페르세발이 안장을 만지며 물었다. \"그것은 안장이라네.\" 오웨인이 말했다. 그러고 나서 그는 사람과 말 위에서 본 온갖 장비에 대해, 그리고 무기에 대해, 그것들이 무엇에 쓰이며 어떻게 사용되는지 물었다. 오웨인 경은 그 모든 것을 그에게 빠짐없이 보여 주었다. 그리고 페르세발도 답례로 자기가 아는 바를 그에게 일러 주었다.\n\n그런 다음 페르세발은 어머니에게 돌아와 말했다. \"어머니, 저들은 천사가 아니라 명예로운 기사들이었어요.\" 그러자 그의 어머니는 정신을 잃고 쓰러졌다. 페르세발은 성에 쓸 장작과 식량을 나르는 말들을 두는 곳으로 가서, 그 가운데 가장 튼튼해 보이는 깡마르고 얼룩진 말 한 마리를 골랐다. 그리고 짐 보따리를 안장 모양으로 눌러 만들고, 비틀어 엮은 잔가지로 말 위에서 보았던 마구를 흉내 내었다. 그가 다시 어머니에게 왔을 때, 백작 부인은 실신에서 깨어나 있었다. \"아들아.\" 어머니가 말했다. \"말을 타고 길을 떠나고 싶으냐?\" \"예, 허락해 주신다면요.\" 그가 말했다. \"그렇다면 떠나거라.\" 어머니가 말했다. \"아서의 궁정으로 가거라. 그곳에는 가장 훌륭하고 가장 고귀하며 가장 너그러운 자들이 있으니, 네가 펠레노어의 아들 페르세발이라 밝히고, 그에게 기사 작위를 내려 달라 청하여라. 그리고 교회를 볼 때마다 그곳에서 주기도문을 외우거라. 또 고기와 마실 것을 보고 그것이 필요하거든 가져도 좋다. 곤경에 처한 자의 부르짖음을 듣거든, 특히 그것이 여인의 비명이거든 그쪽으로 나아가, 네가 할 수 있는 봉사를 베풀어라. 아름다운 보석을 보거든 그것을 손에 넣어라, 그리하면 명성을 얻을 것이다. 그러나 그것을 아낌없이 남에게 주어라, 그리하면 칭송을 얻을 것이다. 아름다운 여인을 보거든 그녀에게 구애하여라, 그리하면 사랑을 얻을 것이다.\"\n\n이러한 가르침을 마친 뒤 페르세발은 말에 올라타, 끝이 뾰족한 막대기 여러 개를 손에 들고 길을 떠났다. 그는 먹을 것도 마실 것도 없이 숲이 우거진 황야를 멀리 달려갔다. 마침내 그는 숲속의 빈터에 이르러 천막 하나를 보았고, 그것이 교회일지도 모른다고 여겨 그것을 향해 주기도문을 외웠다. 그리고 그쪽으로 다가갔는데, 천막의 문이 열려 있었다. 페르세발은 말에서 내려 천막 안으로 들어갔다. 천막 안에서 그는 이마에 황금 머리띠를 두르고 손에 금반지를 낀 채 앉아 있는 처녀를 발견했다. 페르세발이 말했다. \"처녀여, 인사드립니다. 어머니께서 숙녀를 만나거든 반드시 정중히 인사하라 일러 주셨기 때문입니다.\" 천막 한구석에 음식과 포도주가 가득 찬 두 개의 병, 그리고 구운 멧돼지 고기가 있는 것을 알아채고 그가 말했다. \"어머니께서 고기와 마실 것을 보거든 가지라 일러 주셨습니다.\" 그러고는 몹시 배가 고팠던 터라 게걸스레 먹었다. 처녀가 말했다. \"기사님, 여기서 어서 떠나시는 편이 좋겠습니다. 제 친구들이 와서 당신에게 화가 미칠까 두렵습니다.\" 그러나 페르세발은 말했다. \"어머니께서 아름다운 보석을 보거든 어디서든 가지라 일러 주셨습니다.\" 그러고는 그녀의 손가락에서 금반지를 빼어 자기 손가락에 끼고, 그 대신 자신의 반지를 그녀에게 주었다. 그런 다음 말에 올라 길을 떠났다.\n\n페르세발은 계속 나아가 마침내 아서의 궁정에 도착했다. 그런데 마침 그때, 무례한 기사 하나가 왕비 귀네비어에게 심각한 모욕을 가한 일이 있었다. 그녀의 시동이 왕비에게 황금 술잔을 받들어 올리고 있을 때, 이 기사가 시동의 팔을 쳐서 포도주를 왕비의 얼굴과 가슴받이 위로 끼얹었던 것이다. 그러고 나서 그는 말했다. \"귀네비어에게 가한 이 모욕을 갚을 만큼 담대한 자가 있거든, 나를 따라 초원으로 나오너라.\" 그리하여 기사는 말을 타고 황금 술잔을 가지고서 초원으로 갔다. 그러자 온 궁정 사람들이 고개를 떨구었고, 어느 누구도 그 기사를 따라가 복수하겠다고 나서지 않았다. 그들에게는, 마법이나 주술을 통해 그 누구도 처벌할 수 없는 그러한 힘을 지니지 않고서야 어느 누구도 그토록 대담한 폭거를 감행하지 못했으리라 여겨졌기 때문이다. 바로 그때, 보라, 페르세발이 그 깡마르고 얼룩진 말에 볼품없는 마구를 갖추고서 큰 방으로 들어섰다. 방 한가운데에는 집사장 케이가 서 있었다. \"큰 사람이여, 말해 주시오.\" 페르세발이 말했다. \"저기 저분이 아서요?\" \"아서에게 무슨 볼일인가?\" 케이가 물었다. \"어머니께서 아서에게 가서 그분에게 기사 작위를 받으라 일러 주셨습니다.\" \"맹세코.\" 그가 말했다. \"그대는 말이며 무기며 너무도 초라하게 갖추었구나.\" 그러자 온 궁정 사람들이 그를 향해 야유하고 비웃기 시작했다. 그런데 아서의 궁정에 꼬박 일 년을 머무는 동안 단 한 번도 미소를 지은 적이 없다고 알려진 한 처녀가 있었다. 왕의 어릿광대는 이 처녀가 기사도의 꽃이 될 자를 보기 전에는 미소 짓지 않으리라고 말한 바 있었다. 그런데 이제 이 처녀가 페르세발에게 다가와 미소를 지으며, 그가 살아남는다면 가장 용감하고 가장 훌륭한 기사 중 하나가 될 것이라 말했다. \"참으로.\" 케이가 말했다. \"그대는 가르침을 잘못 받았구나. 아서의 궁정에서 일 년을 머물며 골라 어울릴 사람이 많은데도 누구에게도 미소 짓지 않더니, 이제 아서와 그의 모든 기사들 앞에서 이런 자를 두고 기사도의 꽃이라 부르다니.\" 그러고는 그녀의 뺨을 후려쳐, 그녀는 정신을 잃고 바닥에 쓰러졌다. 그런 다음 케이가 페르세발에게 말했다. \"여기서 초원으로 나간 기사를 뒤따라가, 그를 쓰러뜨리고 황금 술잔을 되찾아라. 그리고 그의 말과 무기를 차지하면, 그대는 기사 작위를 얻으리라.\" \"그리하지요, 큰 사람이여.\" 페르세발이 말했다. 그리하여 그는 말 머리를 초원 쪽으로 돌렸다. 그가 그곳에 이르렀을 때, 그 기사는 자신의 힘과 용맹과 고귀한 풍채에 자만하여 이리저리 말을 몰고 있었다. \"말해 보아라.\" 기사가 말했다. \"궁정에서 누군가 나를 뒤따라오는 것을 보았느냐?\" \"그곳에 있던 큰 사람이.\" 페르세발이 말했다. \"나더러 와서 너를 쓰러뜨리고, 너에게서 술잔과 말과 갑주를 빼앗아 내 것으로 삼으라 했다.\" \"닥쳐라!\" 기사가 말했다. \"궁정으로 돌아가, 아서더러 직접 오든지 아니면 나와 싸울 다른 자를 보내라 일러라. 그가 서둘러 그리하지 않으면, 나는 그를 기다리지 않겠다.\" \"맹세코.\" 페르세발이 말했다. \"기꺼이 내어 줄지 마지못해 내어 줄지는 네가 택하여라. 나는 그 말과 갑주와 술잔을 가질 것이니.\" 이 말에 기사는 사납게 그에게 달려들어, 창대로 그의 목과 어깨 사이를 세차게 후려쳤다. \"하, 하, 이놈!\" 페르세발이 말했다. \"어머니의 하인들도 나와 이런 식으로 놀지는 않았다. 그러니 나도 너와 이렇게 놀아 주마.\" 그러고는 끝이 뾰족한 막대기 하나를 그에게 던졌고, 그것이 그의 눈에 박혀 뒤통수로 빠져나오니, 기사는 숨을 거두고 쓰러졌다.\n\n\"참으로.\" 우리엔의 아들 오웨인 경이 집사장 케이에게 말했다. \"그대가 저 미치광이를 기사에게 보낸 것은 잘못된 판단이오. 그는 쓰러지거나 달아나거나 둘 중 하나일 터인데, 어느 쪽이든 아서와 그의 전사들에게 수치가 될 것이오. 그러니 내가 가서 그에게 무슨 일이 일어났는지 보겠소.\" 그리하여 오웨인 경이 초원으로 가 보니, 페르세발이 죽은 기사의 갑주를 벗겨 자기가 입으려고 헛되이 애쓰고 있었다. 오웨인 경은 갑주를 풀어 주고, 페르세발이 그것을 입는 것을 도왔으며, 발을 등자에 끼우고 박차를 쓰는 법을 가르쳐 주었다. 페르세발은 등자도 박차도 써 본 적이 없어, 안장 없이 말을 타고 막대기로 말을 몰아 왔던 것이다. 그러자 오웨인은 그가 마땅히 받아야 할 칭송을 받도록 궁정으로 돌아가게 하려 했다. 그러나 페르세발은 말했다. \"그곳에 있던 큰 사람을 만나 그가 처녀에게 가한 모욕을 갚기 전에는 궁정으로 가지 않겠소. 그러나 그대가 이 술잔을 왕비 귀네비어에게 가져다 드리고, 아서 왕께는 내가 어디에 있든 그의 가신이 되어 내가 할 수 있는 이로움과 봉사를 다하겠다고 전해 주시오.\" 그리하여 오웨인 경은 궁정으로 돌아가, 이 모든 일을 아서와 귀네비어, 그리고 온 궁정 사람들에게 전했다.\n\n페르세발은 다시 말을 타고 나아갔다. 그는 한쪽에 아름다운 성이 서 있는 호수에 이르렀고, 호숫가에서 벨벳 방석 위에 앉아 있는 백발의 노인을 보았는데, 그의 시종들은 호수에서 낚시를 하고 있었다. 백발의 노인은 페르세발이 다가오는 것을 보자 일어나 성안으로 들어갔다. 페르세발은 말을 타고 성으로 갔고, 문이 열려 있어 큰 방으로 들어섰다. 백발의 노인은 페르세발을 정중히 맞이하며, 자기 곁의 방석에 앉으라 청했다. 때가 되자 식탁이 차려졌고, 그들은 식사를 하러 갔다. 식사를 마친 뒤 백발의 노인은 페르세발에게 검으로 싸울 줄 아느냐고 물었다. \"모릅니다.\" 페르세발이 말했다. \"허나 가르쳐 주신다면, 분명 익힐 수 있을 것입니다.\" 그러자 백발의 노인이 그에게 말했다. \"나는 그대의 외숙, 곧 그대 어머니의 오라비다. 사람들은 나를 페쇠르 왕이라 부른다. 그대는 여러 나라의 예절과 관습, 그리고 예의와 고귀한 처신을 배우기 위해 한동안 나와 함께 머물거라. 그리고 이것을 명심하여라. 그대를 놀라게 할 만한 무언가를 보더라도 그 뜻을 묻지 말아라. 누구도 친절히 알려 주지 않더라도, 그 책망은 그대가 아니라 그대의 스승인 나에게 돌아갈 것이다.\" 페르세발과 외숙이 함께 이야기를 나누는 동안, 페르세발은 두 젊은이가 황금 잔과 거대한 창을 들고 큰 방으로 들어오는 것을 보았는데, 그 창끝에서는 피가 바닥으로 방울방울 떨어지고 있었다. 그리고 그곳의 모든 사람들이 이를 보고는 울며 탄식하기 시작했다. 그러나 그 모든 것에도 노인은 페르세발과의 이야기를 멈추지 않았다. 그리고 노인이 그가 본 것의 뜻을 일러 주지 않았으므로, 그도 그것에 관해 묻기를 삼갔다. 그런데 페르세발이 본 그 잔은 성배였고, 그 창은 신성한 창이었다. 후에 페쇠르 왕은 그 신성한 성물들을 가지고 먼 나라로 떠나갔다.\n\n어느 날 저녁 페르세발은 어느 골짜기로 들어가 은자의 처소에 이르렀다. 은자는 그를 기쁘게 맞이했고, 그는 그곳에서 하룻밤을 보냈다. 그리고 아침에 그가 일어나 밖으로 나가 보니, 보라, 밤사이 눈이 한바탕 내렸고, 매 한 마리가 처소 앞에서 들새를 잡아 죽여 놓았다. 그런데 말이 내는 소리에 매가 놀라 날아가 버리자, 까마귀 한 마리가 그 새 위에 내려앉았다. 페르세발은 가만히 서서, 까마귀의 검음과 눈의 흼과 피의 붉음을, 그가 가장 사랑하는 여인의 머리카락 — 흑옥보다 검은 — 과 그녀의 살결 — 눈보다 흰 — 과 그녀의 두 뺨에 어린 두 점의 붉은 빛 — 눈 위의 피보다 붉은 — 에 견주어 보았다.\n\n그런데 아서와 그의 궁정 사람들은 페르세발을 찾아 나서 있었고, 우연히 그 길로 들어서게 되었다. \"그대들은 아는가.\" 아서가 말했다. \"저 위쪽 시냇가에 긴 창을 들고 서 있는 저 기사가 누구인지?\" \"주군이시여.\" 그들 중 하나가 말했다. \"제가 가서 그가 누구인지 알아보겠습니다.\" 그리하여 그 젊은이가 페르세발이 있는 곳으로 와, 그에게 어찌하여 그러고 있는지, 또 그가 누구인지 물었다. 그러나 페르세발은 자신의 생각에 너무도 골똘히 잠겨 그에게 아무런 대답도 하지 않았다. 그러자 젊은이가 창으로 페르세발을 찔렀고, 페르세발은 그에게 몸을 돌려 그를 땅바닥에 쓰러뜨렸다. 그리고 젊은이가 왕에게 돌아가 자기가 얼마나 거칠게 당했는지 고하자, 케이 경이 말했다. \"내가 직접 가겠소.\" 그가 페르세발에게 인사를 건넸으나 아무런 대답을 얻지 못하자, 그는 무례하고 성난 어조로 그에게 말을 걸었다. 그러자 페르세발은 창으로 그를 찔러 내동댕이쳤고, 그는 팔과 어깨뼈가 부러졌다. 그가 그렇게 정신을 잃고 쓰러져 있는 동안, 그의 말은 사납게 껑충거리며 돌아가 버렸다.\n\n그러자 아서의 궁정에서 가장 예의 바른 기사였기에 황금 혀라는 별명을 가진 가웨인 경이 말했다. \"명예로운 기사를 분별없이 그의 생각에서 흔들어 놓는 것은 마땅하지 않습니다. 그는 자신이 입은 어떤 손해를 곱씹고 있거나, 아니면 그가 가장 사랑하는 여인을 생각하고 있을 것이기 때문입니다. 주군께서 좋으시다면, 제가 가서 이 기사가 생각에서 벗어났는지 살피고, 만약 그렇다면 그에게 정중히 청하여 주군을 뵈러 오게 하겠습니다.\"\n\n페르세발은 창대에 기댄 채 여전히 그 생각에 잠겨 있었고, 가웨인 경이 그에게 다가와 말했다. \"그대에게도 나에게도 똑같이 흡족한 일이리라 여겨진다면, 그대와 이야기를 나누고 싶습니다. 또한 나는 아서로부터 그대에게 전할 전갈을 지니고 있으니, 와서 그를 뵈어 달라 청하는 것입니다. 그리고 이 심부름으로 두 사람이 앞서 다녀갔습니다.\" \"그건 사실입니다.\" 페르세발이 말했다. \"그리고 그들은 무례하게 왔습니다. 그들이 나를 공격했고, 나는 그것에 짜증이 났습니다.\" 그러고는 자신의 마음을 사로잡고 있던 생각을 그에게 들려주자, 가웨인이 말했다. \"그것은 점잖지 못한 생각이 아니었으니, 그대를 거기서 끌어내는 것이 그대에게 즐거운 일이라면 나는 놀랄 것입니다.\" 그러자 페르세발이 말했다. \"말해 주시오, 케이 경이 아서의 궁정에 있습니까?\" \"있습니다.\" 가웨인이 말했다. \"그리고 참으로 그가 그대와 마지막으로 싸운 그 기사입니다.\" \"참으로.\" 페르세발이 말했다. \"미소 짓던 처녀에게 가한 모욕을 이렇게 갚게 되어 유감스럽지 않습니다.\" 그러고 나서 페르세발은 그에게 자신의 이름을 일러 주고는 말했다. \"그대는 누구입니까?\" 그가 대답했다. \"나는 가웨인입니다.\" \"그대를 만나게 되어 참으로 기쁩니다.\" 페르세발이 말했다. \"그대의 용맹과 올곧음에 관해서는 어디서나 들어 왔기 때문입니다. 그대의 우정을 청합니다.\" \"맹세코 그대는 그것을 얻을 것이니, 그대 또한 나에게 우정을 베풀어 주시오.\" 그가 말했다. \"기꺼이 그리하지요.\" 페르세발이 대답했다.\n\n그리하여 그들은 함께 아서에게 가서 그에게 인사를 올렸다.\n\n\"보십시오, 주군이시여.\" 가웨인이 말했다. \"오래도록 찾으셨던 그자입니다.\" \"그대에게 환영을 보내노라, 우두머리여.\" 아서가 말했다. 그러자 이때 왕비와 그녀의 시녀들이 다가왔고, 페르세발은 그들에게 인사를 올렸다. 그들은 그를 보고 기뻐하며 환영의 인사를 건넸다. 아서는 그에게 큰 영예와 경의를 베풀었고, 그들은 카에를레온을 향해 돌아갔다.\n\n————\n\n# THE SANGREAL, OR HOLY GRAAL\n\n상그레알은 우리 구세주께서 최후의 만찬에서 마셨던 잔이었다. 구세주는 이 잔을 아리마태아의 요셉에게 주셨던 것으로 여겨지며, 요셉은 그것을 군인이 구세주의 옆구리를 찔렀던 창과 함께 유럽으로 가져왔다. 세대를 거듭하며 아리마태아의 요셉의 후손들 가운데 한 사람이 이 귀중한 성물의 수호를 위해 헌신해 왔다. 다만 생각과 말과 행동에서 순결한 삶을 사는 것을 유일한 조건으로 삼았다. 오랜 세월 동안 상그레알은 모든 순례자에게 보이는 것이었고, 그것이 자리한 땅에는 그 존재로 인해 축복이 내렸다. 그러나 마침내 그 수호의 임무를 물려받은 거룩한 사람들 가운데 하나가, 자기 앞에 무릎 꿇은 한 젊은 여성 순례자의 옷이 우연히 풀어졌을 때, 신성한 직무의 의무를 잊고 부정한 눈으로 그녀를 바라보고 말았다. 신성한 창은 즉시 그의 나약함을 벌하여, 저절로 그에게 떨어져 깊은 상처를 입혔다. 그 불가사의한 상처는 어떤 방법으로도 치유될 수 없었고, 상그레알의 수호자는 그 이후로 줄곧 \"르 루아 페쇠르(Le Roi Pescheur)\", 곧 죄지은 왕이라 불렸다. 상그레알은 경배하러 온 무리에게서 그 보이는 존재를 거두었고, 그 존재가 브리튼의 부족들 사이에 퍼뜨려 주었던 행복의 자리에는 철의 시대가 뒤를 이었다.\n\n\"하지만 그때 시대는 너무도 악해져서 그 거룩한 잔은 하늘로 채여 올라가 사라지고 말았네.\"\n─「거룩한 성배」.\n\n우리는 멀린의 이야기에서, 그 위대한 예언자이자 마법사가 가웨인 경을 통해 아서 왕에게 전갈을 보내, 상그레알을 되찾는 일을 떠맡으라 지시하고, 동시에 그 거룩한 탐색을 이루어 낼 기사가 이미 태어났으며 그 일에 나서기에 알맞은 나이가 되었음을 알린 사연을 들려준 바 있다. 가웨인 경이 그 전갈을 전했고, 왕은 그 과업을 어떻게 가장 잘 이룰지 마음속으로 골똘히 궁리하고 있었다. 그러던 중 성령강림절 전야에, 원탁의 모든 동료들이 카멜롯에 함께 모여 식탁에 앉아 있을 때, 갑자기 천둥소리가 한 번 울리더니 환한 빛이 터져 나왔고, 모든 기사가 동료를 바라보니 그가 전보다 더 아름다워 보였다. 온 홀이 감미로운 향기로 가득 찼고, 모든 기사 앞에는 저마다 가장 좋아하는 음식과 음료가 놓였다. 그때 흰 비단으로 덮인 거룩한 성배가 홀 안으로 들어왔는데, 아무도 그것을 볼 수 없었으며, 그것은 홀을 가로질러 지나가더니 갑자기 사라져 버렸다. 이 동안 누구도 한마디 말이 없었으나, 말할 숨을 되찾았을 때 아서 왕이 말했다. \"참으로 우리는 오늘 주께서 우리에게 보여 주신 것에 대해 크게 감사드려야 마땅하다.\" 그러자 가웨인 경이 일어나, 열두 달 하고도 하루 동안 상그레알을 찾을 것이며, 만일 그렇게 이룰 수 있다면 그것을 보기 전에는 돌아오지 않겠노라 맹세했다. 원탁의 기사들이 가웨인 경이 그렇게 말하는 것을 듣고는, 그들 대부분이 일어나 같은 맹세를 했다. 아서 왕이 이를 듣고 크게 언짢아했으니, 그들이 자신들의 맹세를 거스를 수 없음을 잘 알았기 때문이다. \"아아!\" 그가 가웨인 경에게 말했다. \"그대들은 그 맹세와 약속으로 나를 거의 죽인 셈이오. 그대들은 세상 어느 왕국에서도 함께 모인 것을 본 적 없는 가장 훌륭한 동료들을 내게서 앗아 갔으니 말이오. 그대들이 이곳을 떠나면, 모두가 이 세상에서 다시는 함께 만나지 못하리라는 것을 나는 확신하오.\"\n\n# SIR GALAHAD\n\n그때 한 선량한 노인이 홀 안으로 들어왔는데, 그는 젊은 기사 한 사람을 데리고 있었고, 이렇게 말했다. \"평화가 그대들과 함께하기를, 고귀한 경들이여.\" 그러고는 노인이 아서 왕에게 말했다. \"전하, 제가 여기 왕가의 혈통이며 아리마태아의 요셉의 친족인 한 젊은 기사를 데려왔습니다. 이 자는 이방 나라의 왕인 펠레스 왕의 딸 일레인 부인의 아들입니다.\" 그런데 이 젊은 기사의 이름은 갈라하드 경이었고, 그는 호수의 랜슬롯 경의 아들이었다. 다만 그는 무기를 들 만한 나이가 될 때까지 외할아버지인 펠레스 왕의 궁정에서 어머니와 함께 지냈으며, 그의 어머니가 거룩한 은수자에게 맡겨 그를 아서 왕의 궁정으로 보낸 것이었다. 그때 랜슬롯 경이 자기 아들을 바라보고 그로 인해 큰 기쁨을 누렸다. 또 보호트 경이 동료들에게 말했다. \"내 목숨을 걸고, 이 젊은 기사는 큰 명예에 이를 것이오.\" 온 궁정에 떠들썩한 소리가 컸으므로 그 소문이 왕비에게까지 이르렀다. 그러자 왕비가 말했다. \"나도 기꺼이 그를 보고 싶구나. 그의 아버지가 그러하니, 그 또한 필시 고귀한 기사일 것이다.\" 왕비와 그 시녀들이 모두 그가 아버지를 무척 닮았다고 말했다. 그는 비둘기처럼 어여쁘고 단정했으며, 온갖 좋은 풍채를 갖추어, 온 세상을 통틀어 그와 견줄 자를 찾을 수 없을 정도였다. 아서 왕이 말했다. \"하느님께서 그를 선한 사람으로 만들어 주시기를. 살아 있는 어느 누구에게도 뒤지지 않을 만큼 그에게는 아름다움이 모자라지 않으니 말이오.\"\n\n그러고는 은수자가 그 젊은 기사를 〈위험한 자리〉로 이끌었다. 그가 천을 걷어 내자 거기에는 \"이것은 선한 기사 갈라하드 경의 자리이다\"라고 적힌 글자가 있었고, 그는 그를 그 자리에 앉혔다. 원탁의 모든 기사가 갈라하드 경이 그 자리에 무사히 앉아 있는 것을 보고 크게 경탄하며 말했다. \"이 자가 바로 상그레알을 이루어 낼 분이다. 이전에 이 자리에 앉고도 화를 입지 않은 자가 없었으니 말이다.\"\n\n이튿날 왕이 말했다. \"이제 이 상그레알의 탐색에 그대들 원탁의 기사 모두가 떠날 것이니, 다시는 그대들을 다 함께 보지 못하리라. 그러므로 나는 그대들이 모두 카멜롯의 들판으로 가서, 떠나기 전에 한 번 더 마상 창 시합과 무술 시합을 벌이기를 바라오.\" 그러나 왕의 모든 뜻은 갈라하드 경의 역량을 시험하는 데 있었다. 그리하여 그들은 모두 들판에 모였다. 갈라하드 경은 왕과 왕비의 청에 따라 갑옷과 투구를 갖추었으나, 왕이 아무리 청해도 방패만은 들려 하지 않았다. 왕비는 시녀들을 모두 거느리고 탑 위에서 그 시합을 지켜보았다. 갈라하드 경이 들판 한가운데로 말을 몰고 나아갔다. 그곳에서 그는 놀랍도록 창을 부러뜨리기 시작했으니, 모든 이가 그를 보고 경탄했다. 그가 맞붙는 모든 기사를 압도했기 때문이었는데, 다만 두 사람, 랜슬롯 경과 퍼시벌 경만은 예외였다.\n\n\"수많은 기사들, 그리하여 모든 사람들이 외쳐 대며 열기에 차서 차단대를 거의 부수다시피 하고, '갈라하드 경과 퍼시벌 경!'을 소리쳤네.\"\n─「갈라하드 경」.\n\n그러자 왕이 왕비의 청에 따라 그를 말에서 내리게 하여 왕비에게 데려갔다. 왕비가 말했다. \"그와 랜슬롯 경보다 더 서로 닮은 두 사람은 결코 없었으니, 그가 무용에서도 그를 닮은 것은 조금도 이상한 일이 아니로다.\"\n\n그러고는 왕과 왕비가 성당으로 갔고, 기사들이 그들을 뒤따랐다. 예배가 끝난 뒤 그들은 투구를 쓰고 떠났는데, 큰 슬픔이 일었다. 그들이 카멜롯의 거리를 지나갈 때 부자와 빈자가 모두 울었고, 왕은 고개를 돌린 채 울음 때문에 말을 잇지 못했다. 그리하여 그들은 떠났고, 모든 기사가 저마다 가장 마음에 드는 길을 택했다.\n\n갈라하드 경은 방패 없이 말을 몰고 나아가, 나흘을 달렸으나 아무런 모험도 만나지 못했다. 나흘째 되는 날 그는 한 흰 수도원에 이르렀다. 그곳에서 그는 큰 존경을 받으며 맞이되어 한 방으로 안내되었다. 그는 거기서 두 기사, 바그데마구스 왕과 우와인 경을 만났고, 그들은 그를 크게 위로해 주었다. \"경들이여,\" 갈라하드 경이 말했다. \"무슨 모험이 그대들을 이곳으로 이끌었소?\" \"경이여,\" 그들이 말했다. \"이 안에는 방패가 하나 있는데, 합당한 자가 아니면 누구도 그것을 들 수 없으며, 만일 합당치 못한 자가 그것을 들려 하면 반드시 그에게 화가 미친다고 들었소.\" 그러자 바그데마구스 왕이 말했다. \"나는 그것을 드는 일이 두렵지 않으니, 그것을 그대들이 내일 보게 될 것이오.\"\n\n그리하여 이튿날 그들은 일어나 미사를 드렸고, 바그데마구스 왕이 그 모험의 방패가 어디 있는지 물었다. 곧 한 수도사가 그를 제단 뒤로 이끌었는데, 그곳에 눈처럼 흰 방패가 걸려 있었으며, 그 한가운데에는 붉은 십자가가 있었다. 바그데마구스 왕은 그 방패를 들고 성당 밖으로 나왔다. 그러고는 갈라하드 경에게 말했다. \"괜찮다면, 내가 어찌 되는지 그대가 알게 될 때까지 여기 머물러 주시오.\"\n\n그러고는 바그데마구스 왕과 그의 종자가 말을 몰고 나아갔다. 그들이 한두 마장쯤 달렸을 때, 말과 갑옷이 온통 흰, 한 늠름한 기사가 그들을 향해 다가오는 것을 보았다. 그는 창을 받침에 끼운 채 말이 달릴 수 있는 한 빠르게 다가왔다. 바그데마구스 왕은 자기 창을 그를 향해 겨누어 흰 기사에게 부딪쳐 부러뜨렸으나, 상대는 그를 어찌나 세게 쳤는지 그의 사슬 갑옷을 부수고 오른쪽 어깨를 꿰뚫었으니, 방패가 그를 가려 주지 못한 탓이었고, 그리하여 그를 말에서 떨어뜨렸다. 그러고는 흰 기사가 말 머리를 돌려 떠나갔다.\n\n그러자 종자가 바그데마구스 왕에게 가서 그가 심하게 다쳤는지 아닌지를 물었다. \"나는 심하게 다쳤다,\" 그가 말했다. \"죽음을 면하기가 무척 어렵겠구나.\" 그러자 종자가 그를 말에 태워 한 수도원으로 데려갔다. 그곳에서 그는 조심스레 내려져 갑옷을 벗고 침상에 뉘어졌으며, 그의 상처가 치료되었으니, 그는 오래도록 거기 누워 있다가 가까스로 목숨을 건졌다. 그리고 종자는 그 방패를 수도원으로 도로 가져왔다.\n\n이튿날 갈라하드 경이 그 방패를 들었고, 얼마 지나지 않아 그는 은수처에 이르러 거기서 흰 기사를 만났는데, 두 사람은 서로 정중히 인사를 나누었다. \"경이여,\" 갈라하드 경이 말했다. \"그대는 이 방패의 불가사의를 내게 일러 줄 수 있겠소?\" \"경이여,\" 흰 기사가 말했다. \"그 방패는 옛적에 고귀한 기사 아리마태아의 요셉의 것이었소. 그가 죽음에 이르렀을 때 이렇게 말했소. '내 혈통의 마지막인 선한 기사 갈라하드 경이 이것을 들 때까지는, 어떤 사람도 이 방패를 목에 걸고 다니되 그것을 후회하지 않을 자가 없으리라. 그는 수많은 불가사의한 일을 행할 것이다.'\" 그러고는 흰 기사가 사라져 버렸다.\n\n# SIR GAWAIN\n\n가웨인 경은 떠난 뒤로 여러 날을 이리저리 오가며 말을 달렸고, 마침내 갈라하드 경이 흰 방패를 들었던 그 수도원에 이르렀다. 사람들은 갈라하드 경이 행한 불가사의한 모험을 가웨인 경에게 들려주었다. \"참으로,\" 가웨인 경이 말했다. \"내가 그가 간 길로 가지 못한 것이 기쁘지 않구나. 만일 그를 만날 수 있다면, 그가 이루어 낼 모든 불가사의한 모험에 함께하기 위해 그를 쉬이 떠나지 않으련만.\" \"경이여,\" 수도사 가운데 한 사람이 말했다. \"그는 그대와 한 무리가 되지 않을 것이오.\" \"어찌하여?\" 가웨인 경이 말했다. \"경이여,\" 그가 말했다. \"그대는 죄가 많고, 그는 복되기 때문이오.\" 그러고는 수도사가 말했다. \"가웨인 경이여, 그대는 자기 죄를 위해 속죄를 행해야 하오.\" \"경이여, 내가 무슨 속죄를 행해야 하오?\" \"내가 일러 줄 그런 것을 하시오,\" 선한 사람이 말했다. \"아니오,\" 가웨인 경이 말했다. \"나는 아무런 속죄도 하지 않겠소. 우리 모험하는 기사들은 흔히 큰 비탄과 고통을 겪으니 말이오.\" \"그러시오,\" 선한 사람이 말하고는 입을 다물었다. 그리고 가웨인 경은 떠났다.\n\n그런데 이 일이 있고 오래지 않아, 가웨인 경과 헥터 경이 함께 말을 달리다가, 큰 마상 시합이 열리고 있는 한 성에 이르게 되었다. 가웨인 경과 헥터 경은 더 약해 보이는 편에 가담하여 다른 편을 앞으로 몰아붙였다. 그때 갑자기 붉은 십자가가 그려진 흰 방패를 든 한 기사가 시합장으로 들어왔는데, 우연히 그가 가웨인 경 곁을 지나며 그를 어찌나 세게 쳤는지 투구를 쪼개고 머리에 상처를 입혀, 가웨인 경이 땅에 쓰러졌다. 헥터 경이 그것을 보고는 흰 방패를 든 기사가 갈라하드 경임을 알았고, 그에게 맞서는 것은 지혜롭지 못하다고 여겼는데, 또한 그가 자신의 조카라는 천륜의 정 때문이기도 했다. 그러자 갈라하드 경은 아무도 그가 어디로 갔는지 모르게 슬며시 물러갔다. 헥터 경이 가웨인 경을 일으켜 세우며 말했다. \"경이여, 내가 보기에 그대의 탐색은 끝난 것 같소.\" \"끝났소,\" 가웨인 경이 말했다. \"나는 더는 찾지 않겠소.\" 그러고는 가웨인 경이 성안으로 옮겨져 갑옷을 벗고 호화로운 침상에 뉘어졌으며, 그의 상처를 살필 의사가 불려 왔다. 그리고 가웨인 경과 헥터 경은 함께 머물렀으니, 헥터 경이 가웨인 경이 다 나을 때까지 떠나려 하지 않았기 때문이다.\n\n────\n\n# SIR LAUNCELOT\n\n랜슬롯 경은 너른 숲을 가로지르고 또 세로질러 말을 달렸으되, 정해진 길을 따르지 않고 오로지 거친 모험이 이끄는 대로 나아갔다.\n\n나에게 황금 박차를 가져다 다오, 그리고 나에게 가장 값진 갑옷을 가져다 다오, 내일이면 나는 뭍과 바다를 건너 거룩하고 거룩한 성배를 찾아 떠날 터이니. 나를 위하여 잠자리는 펼쳐지지 않으리, 내 머리 밑에 베개도 놓이지 않으리, 내 맹세를 지키기 시작하기 전까지는. 여기 골풀 위에서 나는 잠들겠노라, 그리고 어쩌면 참된 환영이 찾아올지도 모르리, 날이 밝아 세상을 새로이 빚어내기 전에. ─로웰, 「성배」.\n\n마침내 그는 어느 돌 십자가에 이르렀다. 그러자 랜슬롯 경은 주위를 둘러보았고, 낡은 예배당 하나를 보았다. 그리하여 그는 말을 나무에 매고, 방패를 풀어 나무에 걸어 두었다. 그러고 나서 예배당 안으로 들어가, 벽이 허물어진 곳을 통하여 안을 들여다보았다. 안에는 비단 천으로 더없이 화려하게 꾸며진 아름다운 제단이 보였다. 그리고 그곳에는 여섯 자루의 큰 초를 받친 아름다운 촛대가 서 있었고, 그 촛대는 은으로 만들어진 것이었다. 랜슬롯 경은 이 광경을 보고 예배당 안으로 들어가고 싶은 마음이 간절하였으나, 들어갈 만한 곳을 찾을 수 없었다. 그리하여 그는 몹시 마음이 무겁고 낙담하였다. 그는 발길을 돌려 다시 말에게로 와서, 안장과 굴레를 벗기고 풀을 뜯게 하였다. 그리고 투구의 끈을 풀고 검을 허리에서 끌러, 십자가 앞에서 방패를 베고 누워 잠을 청하였다.\n\n그가 반쯤 깨고 반쯤 잠든 채로 누워 있을 때, 그의 곁으로 두 마리 작은 말이 지나가는 것이 보였는데, 둘 다 아름답고 흰빛이었으며, 가마 하나를 메고 있었고, 그 위에는 병든 기사 한 사람이 누워 있었다. 그가 십자가 가까이 이르렀을 때, 그 자리에 멈추어 머물렀다. 그리고 랜슬롯 경은 그가 이렇게 말하는 것을 들었다. \"오, 자비로우신 주여, 언제쯤 이 고통이 저를 떠나오리이까, 그리고 언제쯤 저를 낫게 하실 그 거룩한 그릇이 제 곁에 오리이까?\" 이렇게 그 기사는 오랫동안 탄식하였고, 랜슬롯 경은 그것을 들었다. 그때 랜슬롯 경은 불 켜진 가느다란 초들을 꽂은 촛대가 십자가 앞으로 다가오는 것을 보았으나, 그것을 가져오는 자는 아무도 보이지 않았다. 또한 은으로 된 쟁반 하나와 산그레알의 거룩한 그릇이 다가왔다. 그러자 그와 동시에 병든 기사가 몸을 일으켜 두 손을 들고 말하였다. \"아름답고 자비로우신 주여, 이 거룩한 그릇 안에 계신 분이시여, 저를 굽어살피시어, 이 크나큰 병에서 온전히 낫게 하소서.\" 그러고 나서 그는 두 손과 두 무릎으로 기어가, 마침내 그 거룩한 그릇에 닿아 입을 맞추었다. 그러자 곧바로 그는 온전히 나았다. 그런 다음 그 거룩한 그릇은 촛대와 그 빛과 함께 다시 예배당 안으로 들어갔고, 랜슬롯 경은 그것이 어찌 되었는지 알지 못하였다.\n\n그러자 병든 기사가 일어나 십자가에 입을 맞추었다. 그리고 곧 그의 종자가 그에게 무구를 가져와, 주인에게 어떠하시냐고 물었다. \"진심으로 하느님께 감사하노라.\" 그가 말하였다. \"이 거룩한 그릇을 통하여 내가 나았으니. 허나 나는 이 잠든 기사가 몹시 기이하구나. 거룩한 그릇이 이곳에 머무는 동안 깨어날 은총도 힘도 얻지 못하였으니 말이다.\" \"감히 분명하게 말씀드리건대,\" 종자가 말하였다. \"이 기사야말로 어떤 종류의 치명적인 죄로 더럽혀져 있되, 그것을 결코 고백한 적이 없는 자입니다.\" 그리하여 그들은 떠나갔다.\n\n그러자 곧 랜슬롯 경이 깨어나 몸을 일으켜 앉았고, 자기가 본 것이 무엇이었는지, 그것이 꿈이었는지 아닌지를 곰곰이 생각하였다. 그는 몹시 마음이 무거웠고, 어찌해야 할지를 알지 못하였다. 그리고 그가 말하였다. \"내 죄와 내 비루함이 나를 크나큰 불명예에 빠뜨렸도다. 내가 세속의 모험과 세속의 욕망을 좇았을 때에는 언제나 그것들을 이루었고, 어느 곳에서나 우위를 점하였으며, 옳든 그르든 어떤 싸움에서도 결코 패한 적이 없었노라. 그런데 이제 거룩한 일의 모험을 떠맡고 보니, 나의 오랜 죄가 나를 가로막아, 거룩한 피가 내 앞에 나타났을 때 움직일 힘도 말할 힘도 없었음을 보고 또 깨닫노라.\" 이렇게 그는 날이 밝을 때까지 슬퍼하였고, 하늘의 새들이 노래하는 소리를 들었다. 그러자 그는 다소 위로를 받았다.\n\n그런 다음 그는 십자가를 떠나 숲속으로 들어갔다. 그리고 그곳에서 은수처 하나와, 그 안에 미사를 드리러 가던 은수자 한 사람을 발견하였다. 그리하여 미사가 끝나자 랜슬롯 경은 그 은수자를 불러, 자비를 베풀어 자기 고해를 들어 달라고 청하였다. \"기꺼이 그리하겠소.\" 그 선량한 이가 말하였다. 그러고 나서 그는 그 선량한 이에게 자기의 온 생애를 이야기하였고, 어떻게 자기가 어느 왕비를 여러 해 동안 한없이 사랑하였는지를 털어놓았다. \"그리고 내가 행한 그 모든 위대한 무공도, 대부분은 그 왕비를 위하여 한 것이었고, 그분을 위하여서라면 옳든 그르든 싸움에 나섰으며, 오로지 하느님만을 위하여 싸운 적은 한 번도 없었고, 다만 명예를 얻고자, 그리고 더욱 사랑받고자 하였을 따름이오. 그것에 대하여 하느님께 감사한 마음은 거의 없거나 아예 없었소. 부디 나에게 가르침을 주시오.\"\n\n\"내 그대에게 가르침을 주리다.\" 은수자가 말하였다. \"만일 그대가 힘닿는 한 그 왕비와의 사귐에 결코 다시 들지 않겠노라 나에게 다짐한다면 말이오.\" 그러자 랜슬롯 경은 자기 신앙을 걸고, 다시는 그분과 어울리지 않겠노라고 은수자에게 약속하였다. \"그대의 마음과 그대의 입이 일치하도록 살피시오.\" 그 선량한 이가 말하였다. \"그리하면 내 그대에게 다짐하건대, 그대는 일찍이 누렸던 것보다 더 큰 명예를 누리게 되리다.\"\n\n그러고 나서 그 선량한 이는 랜슬롯 경에게 그가 행할 수 있는 만큼의 고행을 부과하였고, 랜슬롯 경에게 청하여 그날 온종일 자기와 함께 머물게 하였다. 그리고 랜슬롯 경은 크게 뉘우쳤다.\n\n# SIR PERCEVAL\n\n페르세발 경은 길을 떠나 정오 무렵까지 말을 달렸다. 그러다가 어느 골짜기에서 무장한 사람 스무 명가량과 마주쳤다. 그들은 페르세발 경을 보자 그에게 어디서 왔느냐고 물었다. 그가 대답하였다. \"아서 왕의 궁정에서 왔노라.\" 그러자 그들이 일제히 외쳤다. \"저자를 죽여라.\" 그러나 페르세발 경은 맨 앞의 사내를 땅에 쳐서 쓰러뜨렸고, 그의 말까지 그 위로 넘어뜨렸다. 그러자 일곱 기사가 한꺼번에 그의 방패를 내리쳤고, 나머지가 그의 말을 죽여, 그가 땅에 쓰러지게 하였다. 그리하여 만일 붉은 십자가를 단 선량한 기사 갤러해드 경이 모험길에 그곳을 지나가지 않았더라면, 그들은 그를 죽이거나 사로잡았을 것이다. 갤러해드 경은 모든 기사가 한 사람에게 달려드는 것을 보고 외쳤다. \"저 기사의 목숨을 살려 다오.\" 그러고 나서 그는 창을 창받침에 대고, 말이 내달을 수 있는 한 빠르게 스무 명의 무장한 사람들을 향하여 달려, 맨 앞의 말과 사람을 땅에 쳐 쓰러뜨렸다. 그리고 창이 부러지자 그는 검에 손을 대어 오른쪽과 왼쪽을 내리쳤으니, 그 광경이 가히 놀라울 따름이었다. 매번 휘두를 때마다 그는 한 사람씩 쳐 쓰러뜨리거나 물러나게 하였고, 마침내 그들은 더는 싸우려 하지 않고 울창한 숲으로 달아났으며, 갤러해드 경은 그들을 뒤쫓았다. 페르세발 경은 그가 그렇게 그들을 쫓는 것을 보고, 자기 말이 죽은 것을 크게 슬퍼하였다. 그리고 그는 그가 갤러해드 경임을 잘 알았다. 그리하여 그가 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"아, 아름다운 기사여, 멈추시오, 그리고 내가 그대에게 감사를 드리게 해 주시오. 그대가 나를 위하여 참으로 훌륭히 싸워 주었으니.\" 그러나 갤러해드 경은 어찌나 빨리 말을 달렸던지 마침내 그의 시야에서 사라지고 말았다. 페르세발 경은 그가 돌아서려 하지 않는 것을 보고 말하였다. \"이제 나는 참으로 비참한 자요, 다른 모든 기사 가운데 가장 불행한 자로구나.\" 그리하여 그는 슬픔에 잠긴 채 밤이 될 때까지 그날 온종일 머물렀다. 그러고 나서 그는 기진하여 몸을 누이고 한밤중까지 잠들었다. 그러다가 그는 깨어나 자기 앞에 한 여인이 서 있는 것을 보았는데, 그 여인이 그에게 말하였다. \"페르세발 경이여, 그대는 여기서 무엇을 하고 있는가?\" 그가 대답하였다. \"나는 좋은 일도, 큰 나쁜 일도 하고 있지 않소.\" \"만일 그대가,\" 그 여인이 말하였다. \"내가 그대를 부를 때 내 뜻을 이루어 주겠노라고 나에게 약속한다면, 나는 그대에게 내 말을 빌려주리니, 그 말이 그대가 가고자 하는 곳 어디로든 그대를 데려다 주리라.\" 페르세발 경은 그 여인의 제안을 기뻐하며, 그 여인의 모든 바람을 이루어 주겠노라고 다짐하였다. \"그러면 여기서 나를 기다리시오, 내가 가서 그대에게 말 한 마리를 가져오리다.\" 그리하여 그 여인은 곧 다시 와서, 먹빛처럼 검은 말 한 마리를 데려왔다. 페르세발은 그 말을 보고 경탄하였으니, 어찌나 크고 어찌나 잘 차려 입혀졌던지 그러하였다. 그리고 그는 자기 자신을 돌아보지도 않고 그 위로 뛰어올랐다. 그가 박차를 가하자, 한 시간도 채 못 되어 그 말은 그를 그곳에서 나흘 길이나 데려갔고, 마침내 그는 으르렁대며 흐르는 거친 물가에 이르렀는데, 그의 말은 그를 그 물속으로 몰고 들어가려 하였다. 페르세발 경이 물가에 가까이 이르러 물이 그토록 사납게 날뛰는 것을 보고는 그것을 건너기를 주저하였다. 그러고 나서 그는 자기 이마에 십자 표시를 그었다. 그 마귀는 그가 그렇게 십자가로 무장한 것을 느끼자, 페르세발 경을 떨쳐 버리고 울부짖고 으르렁대며 물속으로 들어갔으며, 그에게는 그 물이 불타는 것처럼 보였다. 그제야 페르세발 경은 그것이 자기를 멸망으로 이끌려 한 마귀였음을 알아차렸다. 그리하여 그는 자신을 하느님께 의탁하며, 우리 주께 자기를 그러한 모든 유혹으로부터 지켜 주시기를 기도하였다. 그리고 그는 날이 밝을 때까지 그날 밤 내내 그렇게 기도하였다. 그제야 그는 자기가 거의 사방이 바다로 둘러싸인 황량한 곳에 있음을 보았다. 그리고 페르세발 경이 바다 너머를 바라보니, 배 한 척이 자기를 향하여 항해해 오는 것이 보였다. 그 배는 다가와 바위 아래에 가만히 멈추어 섰다. 페르세발 경은 이것을 보고 서둘러 그곳으로 갔고, 비단으로 덮인 배를 발견하였다. 그리고 그 안에는 더없이 아름다운 귀부인이 있었는데, 그보다 더 화려할 수 없을 만큼 호화롭게 차려입고 있었다.\n\n그 귀부인은 페르세발 경을 보자 그에게 인사하였고, 페르세발 경도 그 인사에 답하였다. 그러고 나서 그는 그 여인에게 고향과 가문을 물었다. 그러자 그 여인이 말하였다. \"나는 상속권을 빼앗긴 귀부인으로, 한때는 세상에서 가장 부유한 여인이었나이다.\" \"아가씨여,\" 페르세발 경이 말하였다. \"누가 그대의 상속권을 빼앗았소? 내 그대를 크게 가엾이 여기노라.\" \"기사님,\" 그 여인이 말하였다. \"제 원수는 위대하고 강력한 군주인데, 예전에는 저를 무척 아꼈으니, 그분의 총애와 제 아름다움으로 인하여 저는 마땅한 정도보다 조금 더 자만하게 되었나이다. 또한 저는 그분의 마음에 들지 않는 말을 한마디 하였습니다. 그리하여 그분은 저를 자기 곁에서, 그리고 제 유산에서 내쫓았나이다. 그러므로 저는 선량한 기사나 선량한 사내를 알게 되면, 할 수만 있다면 제 편으로 끌어들이옵니다. 그리고 그대가 선량한 기사임을 알기에, 부디 저를 도와주시기를 간청하나이다.\"\n\n그러자 페르세발 경은 자기가 베풀 수 있는 모든 도움을 그 여인에게 약속하였고, 그 여인은 그에게 감사하였다.\n\n그리고 그때 날씨가 무더웠으므로, 그 여인은 한 시녀를 불러 천막을 가져오라고 일렀다. 그러자 시녀가 그리하여, 자갈밭 위에 천막을 쳤다. \"기사님,\" 그 여인이 말하였다. \"이제 이 한낮의 더위 속에서 쉬소서.\" 그러자 그는 그 여인에게 감사하였고, 여인은 그의 투구와 방패를 벗겨 주었으며, 그곳에서 그는 한참 동안 잠을 잤다. 그러다가 그는 깨어나 그 여인에게 먹을 것이 있느냐고 물었고, 여인은 그렇다고 대답하였으며, 그리하여 식탁 위에는 그가 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 음식이 차려졌다. 또한 그는 그곳에서 일찍이 마셔 본 가장 독한 술을 마셨고, 그로 인하여 마땅한 정도보다 조금 더 달아올랐다. 그러면서 그는 그 귀부인을 바라보았는데, 일찍이 본 가장 아름다운 사람이라고 여겼다. 그러고 나서 페르세발 경은 그 여인에게 사랑을 청하며, 자기 사람이 되어 달라고 간청하였다. 그러자 그 여인은 어느 정도 그를 거절하였으니, 그가 자기에게 더욱 애타게 매달리도록 하기 위함이었고, 그는 끊임없이 그 여인에게 사랑을 간청하였다. 그리고 그 여인은 그가 잔뜩 달아오른 것을 보고 말하였다. \"페르세발 경이여, 잘 들으소서. 저는 그대에게 제 사랑을 주지 않겠나이다. 그대가 지금부터 저의 충실한 종이 되어, 제가 명하는 것 외에는 아무 일도 하지 않겠노라 맹세하지 않는 한은 말입니다. 진정한 기사로서 이를 제게 다짐하시겠나이까?\" \"그리하리다,\" 그가 말하였다. \"아름다운 여인이여, 내 몸의 신앙을 걸고.\" 그리고 그가 이 말을 하는 순간, 우연이자 은총으로, 그는 자기 검이 칼집 없이 땅에 놓여 있는 것을 보았는데, 그 손잡이 머리에는 붉은 십자가가, 그리고 그 위에 십자가에 못 박히신 분의 표징이 있었다. 그러자 그는 자기 이마에 십자 표시를 그었고, 그와 동시에 천막은 오그라들어 연기와 검은 구름으로 변하였다. 그리고 그 아가씨는 큰 소리로 외치며 서둘러 배로 들어갔고, 그리하여 그 여인은 으르렁대고 울부짖는 바람과 함께 떠나갔으니, 그 여인의 뒤로 온 바닷물이 불타는 것처럼 보였다. 그러자 페르세발 경은 크게 슬퍼하며 자신을 비참한 자라 일컫고 말하였다. \"내가 얼마나 멸망에 가까웠던가!\" 그러고 나서 그는 자기 무구를 챙겨, 그곳을 떠나갔다.\n\n────\n\n# SIR BOHORT\n\n보호트 경이 카멜롯을 떠난 뒤, 그는 당나귀를 타고 가는 어느 수도자를 만났다. 보호트 경이 그에게 인사를 건넸다. \"그대는 누구신가?\" 그 선한 사람이 물었다. \"선생님,\" 보호트 경이 말했다. \"저는 성배를 찾는 일에 관해 기꺼이 조언을 구하고자 하는 기사입니다.\" 두 사람은 함께 말을 타고 어느 은수처에 이를 때까지 갔다. 그곳에서 그는 보호트 경에게 그날 밤을 자기와 함께 머물기를 청했다. 그리하여 보호트 경은 말에서 내려 갑옷을 벗어 두고, 고해를 할 수 있게 해 달라고 청했다. 두 사람은 함께 예배당으로 들어갔고, 그곳에서 그는 정결하게 고해를 마쳤다. 그리고 그들은 함께 빵을 먹고 물을 마셨다. \"이제,\" 그 선한 사람이 말했다. \"내 그대에게 청하노니, 성배가 놓일 식탁에 그대가 앉을 때까지는 다른 어떤 것도 먹지 마시오.\" \"선생님,\" 보호트 경이 말했다. \"그런데 제가 그곳에 앉게 되리란 것을 어찌 아십니까?\" \"그렇소,\" 그 선한 사람이 말했다. \"그것을 나는 잘 알고 있소. 다만 그대와 함께할 동료는 몇 되지 않을 것이오.\" 그러자 보호트 경이 말했다. \"그 말씀에 따르겠습니다.\" 그리고 그 선한 사람은 그의 고해를 듣고 나서, 그가 그토록 정결하고 그토록 굳건한 삶을 살고 있음을 알고는 그것을 경탄해 마지않았다.\n\n이튿날 날이 밝자마자 보호트 경은 그곳을 떠나 한낮이 되도록 어느 숲속을 달렸다. 그리고 그곳에서 그는 놀라운 모험을 맞이했다. 두 갈래로 갈라지는 길목에서 그는 두 명의 기사를 만났는데, 그들은 그의 형 리오넬 경을 발가벗긴 채 튼튼한 말에 묶어 끌고 가고 있었다. 형의 두 손은 가슴 앞에 결박되어 있었고, 그들은 저마다 손에 가시나무를 들고 그를 후려치고 있어, 형의 앞과 뒤는 온통 피투성이였다. 그러나 형은 한마디도 하지 않았다. 마음이 큰 사람이었기에, 마치 아무 고통도 느끼지 못하는 것처럼 그들이 자기에게 하는 모든 일을 견디고 있었다. 보호트 경은 형을 구하려고 채비했다. 그러나 그가 다른 쪽으로 눈을 돌리자, 한 기사가 아리따운 귀부인 한 사람을 끌고 가고 있었다. 그 여인은 \"성모 마리아여! 당신의 종을 구원하소서!\" 하고 외치고 있었다. 그리고 보호트 경을 보자, 여인은 그를 불러 말했다. \"기사도에 마땅히 지키셔야 할 신의를 두고 비오니, 저를 도와주소서!\" 보호트 경은 여인이 이렇게 말하는 것을 듣고 어찌해야 할지 알 수 없을 만큼 큰 시름에 잠겼다. \"내 형을 그대로 둔다면 형은 틀림없이 죽임을 당할 터인데, 그것은 온 세상을 준다 해도 내가 바라지 않는 일이다. 그러나 이 여인을 돕지 않는다면 나는 영원히 수치를 입게 될 것이다.\" 그러고는 눈을 들어 올리며 울면서 말했다. \"제가 충성을 바치는 어지신 주님이시여, 제 형 리오넬 경을 지켜 주시어 저 기사들 중 누구도 형을 죽이지 못하게 하소서. 그리하면 저는 주님을 향한 측은함과 성모님을 위하여 이 여인을 구하겠나이다.\"\n\n그러고는 그 기사에게 소리쳤다. \"기사여, 그 여인에게서 손을 떼시오. 그러지 않으면 그대는 죽은 목숨이오.\" 그러자 그 기사는 여인을 내려놓고 방패를 잡고 검을 빼들었다. 보호트 경은 그를 어찌나 세게 내리쳤던지 그 일격이 방패와 사슬갑옷을 뚫고 왼쪽 어깨에 박혔고, 그는 땅바닥에 쓰러졌다. 그러자 보호트 경이 여인에게 다가왔다. \"이번에는 그대가 이 기사에게서 벗어났소.\" \"이제,\" 여인이 말했다. \"부디 저를 이 기사가 저를 끌어온 그곳으로 데려다주소서.\" \"기꺼이 그리하리다.\" 보호트 경이 말했다. 그리하여 그는 상처 입은 기사의 말을 끌어와 그 위에 귀부인을 태우고, 여인이 가고자 하는 곳으로 데려다주었다. 그리고 그곳에서 그는 여인을 찾고 있던 열두 명의 기사를 만났다. 여인이 그들에게 보호트 경이 자기를 어떻게 구해 주었는지를 일러 주자, 그들은 크게 기뻐하며, 위대한 영주인 여인의 아버지에게로 와 달라고 간청했다. 그러면 그가 마땅히 환대를 받으리라는 것이었다. \"참으로,\" 보호트 경이 말했다. \"그럴 수는 없소. 내게는 행해야 할 큰 모험이 있기 때문이오.\" 그리하여 그는 그들을 하느님께 맡기고 떠났다.\n\n그러고 나서 보호트 경은 말발굽 자국을 따라 형 리오넬 경의 뒤를 쫓았다. 이렇게 그는 오랫동안 찾아 헤매며 말을 달렸다. 그러다가 그는 수도자의 옷을 입은 한 사람을 따라잡았는데, 그 사람이 말했다. \"기사여, 무엇을 찾고 계시오?\" \"선생님,\" 보호트 경이 말했다. \"저는 제 형을 찾고 있습니다. 조금 전 두 기사에게 매를 맞고 있는 것을 보았습니다.\" \"아, 보호트 경, 형을 찾으려고 애쓰지 마시오. 그는 정녕 죽었기 때문이오.\" 그러고는 빽빽한 덤불 속에 누워 있는, 갓 살해당한 시신 하나를 그에게 보여 주었다. 그것은 보호트 경에게 리오넬 경의 시신으로 보였다. 그러자 그는 너무도 큰 시름에 빠져 정신을 잃고 땅바닥에 쓰러졌고, 그곳에 오래도록 누워 있었다. 그리고 다시 정신을 차렸을 때, 그가 말했다. \"어진 형이여, 그대와 나의 동기간이 끊어졌으니, 나는 다시는 기쁨을 누리지 못하리라. 이제 내가 주님으로 모신 그분께서 나를 도와주시기를!\" 이렇게 말한 뒤 그는 두 팔로 시신을 들어 올려 말 위에 얹었다. 그리고 그 사람에게 말했다. \"이 시신을 묻을 수 있는 예배당으로 가는 길을 일러 줄 수 있겠소?\" \"이리 오시오,\" 그 사람이 말했다. \"바로 가까이에 하나 있소.\" 그리하여 그들은 아리따운 탑 하나가 보일 때까지 말을 달렸고, 그 옆에는 예배당이 있었다. 그러자 두 사람은 모두 말에서 내려 시신을 대리석 무덤에 안치했다.\n\n그러고 나서 보호트 경은 그 선한 사람을 하느님께 맡기고 떠났다. 그는 온종일 말을 달려 어느 노부인의 집에 묵었다. 그리고 이튿날 그는 어느 골짜기에 있는 성으로 말을 달렸고, 그곳에서 한 시종을 만났다. \"말해 보오,\" 보호트 경이 말했다. \"혹 어떤 모험에 관해 아는 것이 있소?\" \"기사 어른,\" 그가 말했다. \"이 성 아래에서 크고 놀라운 마상 시합이 열릴 것입니다.\" 그러자 보호트 경은, 성배를 찾는 길에 나선 동료들 가운데 누구라도 만날 수 있을까 하여 그곳에 가 볼 생각이었다. 그래서 그는 숲의 어귀에 있는 어느 은수처로 향했다. 그곳에 다다랐을 때, 그는 예배당 문 어귀에 온통 무장한 채 앉아 있는 형 리오넬 경을 발견했다. 보호트 경은 형을 보고 크게 기뻐하며 말에서 내려 말했다. \"어진 형이여, 언제 이곳에 오셨소?\" 리오넬 경은 그를 보자마자 말했다. \"아, 보호트, 거짓된 시늉일랑 하지 마라. 너로 인해 나는 죽임을 당할 뻔했다. 네가 귀부인을 구하러 가느라 나를 죽음의 위험 속에 내버려 두었기 때문이다. 그 악행의 대가로 나는 이제 네게 죽음 외에는 아무것도 약속하지 않겠다. 너는 마땅히 그것을 받아야 하기 때문이다.\" 보호트 경은 형의 분노를 알아차리고 땅에 무릎을 꿇고 두 손을 들어 올리며 자비를 빌었고, 자기를 용서해 달라고 간청했다. \"아니다,\" 리오넬 경이 말했다. \"내가 우위를 점한다면 너는 그 대가로 죽음만을 얻게 되리라. 그러니 말 위에 뛰어올라 너 자신을 지켜라. 그러지 않으면 네가 발로 선 채 그대로 있는 그곳을 향해 내가 달려들 것이다. 그리하면 수치는 내 몫이요 해악은 네 몫이 되겠지만, 그런 것은 내 알 바 아니다.\" 보호트 경은 형과 싸우든지 아니면 죽든지 해야만 한다는 것을 알았으나, 어찌해야 할지 알 수 없었다. 그의 마음은 그리하지 말라고 일렀으니, 리오넬 경은 그의 형이었으므로 마땅히 공경을 바쳐야 했기 때문이다. 그래도 그는 리오넬 경의 말발굽 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 말했다. \"어진 형이여, 부디 저를 가엾이 여기시어 죽이지 마소서.\" 그러나 리오넬 경은 아랑곳하지 않았으니, 악마가 그의 마음을 동생을 죽이려는 쪽으로 몰아갔기 때문이다. 보호트 경이 일어나 싸우려 하지 않는 것을 보자, 그는 동생을 향해 돌진하여 말발굽으로 그를 땅바닥에 내리쳤고, 그를 심하게 다치게 하여 동생은 괴로움에 정신을 잃었다. 리오넬 경은 이를 보고 동생의 목을 베려고 말에서 내렸다. 그러고는 투구를 잡고 동생의 머리에서 그것을 벗겨내려 했다. 그러나 마침 그때, 우리 주님의 뜻에 따라 원탁의 기사인 콜그레반스 경이 그곳에 이르렀다. 그리고 그는 리오넬 경이 동생을 죽이려는 것을 보았고, 자기가 무척이나 아끼던 보호트 경을 알아보았다.\n\n그러자 그는 말에서 뛰어내려 리오넬 경의 어깨를 붙잡고 보호트 경에게서 힘껏 뒤로 끌어당기며 말했다. \"리오넬 경, 그대는 형제를 죽이려 하시오?\" \"어찌하여,\" 리오넬 경이 말했다. \"그대는 나를 막으려는 것이오? 그대가 이 일에 끼어든다면 내 그대를 죽이고, 그다음에 저놈을 죽이겠소.\" 그러고는 보호트 경을 향해 달려들어 내리치려 했으나, 콜그레반스 경이 두 사람 사이로 뛰어들어 말했다. \"그대가 더 이상 그리한다면, 우리 둘이 맞붙게 될 것이오.\" 그러자 리오넬 경은 그에게 도전하며 투구를 뚫는 강한 일격을 가했다. 그러자 그는 검을 빼들었으니, 그는 빼어나게 훌륭한 기사였으므로 사내답게 자신을 지켜냈다. 싸움이 어찌나 오래 이어졌던지, 보호트 경은 온통 괴로워하며 몸을 일으켜, 훌륭한 기사 콜그레반스 경이 자기를 위한 싸움에서 형과 맞서 싸우는 것을 바라보게 되었다. 그러자 그는 한없이 슬프고 무거운 마음에 잠겨, 만일 콜그레반스 경이 자기 형을 죽인다면 자기는 결코 기쁨을 누리지 못하리라, 그리고 형이 콜그레반스 경을 죽인다면 그 수치는 영원히 자기 몫이 되리라 생각했다.\n\n그러고는 그가 일어나 두 사람을 떼어 놓으려 했으나, 발로 설 만한 힘조차 없었다. 그리하여 그가 그토록 오래 머뭇거리는 사이에 콜그레반스 경이 열세에 몰렸으니, 리오넬 경은 무용이 뛰어나고 무척이나 용맹했기 때문이다. 그러자 콜그레반스 경이 외쳤다. \"아, 보호트 경, 어찌하여 나를 죽음의 위험에서 구하러 오지 않으시오? 나는 그대를 돕고자 이 위험에 빠졌건만.\" 그 순간 리오넬 경이 그의 투구를 쳐서 떨어뜨리고 그를 땅에 쓰러뜨렸다. 그리고 콜그레반스 경을 죽이고 나서, 그는 악마에 들린 사람처럼 동생에게 달려들어 어찌나 세게 내리쳤던지 동생을 휘청거리게 만들었다. 겸손함으로 가득한 동생은 그에게 빌었다. \"하느님을 위하여 이 싸움을 그만두소서. 어진 형이여, 만일 제가 형을 죽이거나, 혹은 형이 저를 죽이는 일이 벌어진다면 우리는 그 죄로 인해 죽은 목숨이 될 것입니다.\" \"내게 자비를 구하지 마라.\" 리오넬 경이 말했다. 그러자 보호트 경은 온통 울면서 검을 빼들고 말했다. \"이제 하느님께서 저를 가엾이 여기소서. 비록 제가 형에게서 제 목숨을 지킨다 할지라도.\" 그 말과 함께 보호트 경은 검을 들어 올려 형을 내리치려 했다. 그때 그는 한 목소리가 이렇게 말하는 것을 들었다. \"달아나라, 보호트 경, 그에게 손대지 마라.\" 바로 그때 두 사람 사이로 구름 하나가 불길과 놀라운 화염의 형상으로 내려앉아, 그들 둘 다 땅바닥에 쓰러져 오래도록 정신을 잃고 누워 있었다. 그리고 정신을 차렸을 때, 보호트 경은 형이 아무런 해도 입지 않았음을 보았다. 그는 무척이나 기뻤으니, 하느님께서 형에게 복수하셨을까 몹시 두려워했기 때문이다. 그러자 리오넬 경이 동생에게 말했다. \"아우야, 하느님을 위하여 내가 네게 저지른 모든 잘못을 용서해 다오.\" 그러자 보호트 경이 대답했다. \"하느님께서 형을 용서하시기를, 저도 용서합니다.\"\n\n그 말과 함께 보호트 경은 한 목소리가 이렇게 말하는 것을 들었다. \"보호트 경, 지체 없이 곧장 바다로 가라. 그곳에서 퍼시벌 경이 그대를 기다리고 있느니라.\" 그리하여 보호트 경은 길을 떠나 바다로 가는 가장 가까운 길로 말을 달렸다. 그리고 마침내 그는 바다 가까이에 있는 어느 수도원에 이르렀다. 그날 밤 그는 그곳에서 쉬었는데, 잠결에 한 목소리가 그에게 다가와 바닷가로 가라고 명했다. 그는 벌떡 일어나 이마에 십자가 표시를 긋고, 무장을 갖춘 다음 말을 채비하여 올라탔고, 무너진 성벽을 통해 밖으로 나가 바닷가에 이르렀다. 그리고 그곳에서 그는 온통 흰 명주로 덮인 배 한 척을 발견했다. 그는 배에 올랐다. 그러나 곧 너무나 어두워져 사람을 알아볼 수 없었으므로, 그는 몸을 누이고 날이 밝을 때까지 잠을 잤다. 그러고 나서 잠에서 깨어, 배 한가운데에 투구만 벗은 채 온통 무장한 기사 하나가 있는 것을 보았다. 그리고 그는 그가 갈리스의 퍼시벌 경임을 알아보았고, 둘은 서로를 보고 무척이나 크게 기뻐했다. 그러자 퍼시벌 경이 말했다. \"이제 우리에게 부족한 것은 훌륭한 기사 갈라하드 경뿐이오.\"\n\n어느 날 밤, 란슬롯 경은 풍요롭고 아름다운 어느 성 앞에 다다랐다. 그곳에는 바다를 향해 열린 뒷문이 하나 있었는데, 아무도 지키지 않고 열려 있었으며 다만 사자 두 마리가 입구를 지키고 있었다. 그리고 달이 밝게 빛나고 있었다. 이윽고 란슬롯 경은 한 목소리가 이렇게 말하는 것을 들었다. \"란슬롯, 성안으로 들어가라. 그러면 네가 바라는 것의 큰 부분을 보게 되리라.\" 그리하여 그가 문으로 다가가 두 마리 사자를 보았을 때, 그는 검에 손을 대어 그것을 빼들었다. 그러자 갑자기 마치 일격을 당한 듯 팔에 어찌나 심한 충격이 가해졌던지 검이 손에서 떨어졌고, 그는 한 목소리가 이렇게 말하는 것을 들었다. \"오, 믿음이 약한 자여, 어찌하여 너는 너를 지으신 분보다 네 갑옷을 더 믿느냐?\" 그러자 란슬롯 경이 말했다. \"어지신 주님이시여, 저의 악행을 꾸짖어 주시는 그 크나큰 자비에 감사드리나이다. 이제 저는 주님께서 저를 당신의 종으로 여기심을 잘 알겠나이다.\" 그러고는 그는 이마에 십자가를 긋고 사자들에게로 다가갔다. 사자들은 그에게 해를 끼치려는 듯 보였으나, 그는 다친 데 없이 그들을 지나쳐 성안으로 들어갔다. 그리고 그는 열려 있지 않은 문이나 출입구를 하나도 찾지 못했다. 그러나 마침내 그는 문이 닫혀 있는 방 하나를 발견했다. 그가 그것을 열려고 손을 대었으나 열 수 없었다. 그러자 그는 귀를 기울였고, 한 목소리가 어찌나 감미롭게 노래하던지 도무지 이 세상 것 같지 않은 노래를 들었다. 그 목소리는 이렇게 말했다. \"기쁨과 영광이 하늘에 계신 아버지께 있으라.\" 그러자 란슬롯 경은 그 방 앞에 무릎을 꿇었으니, 그 방 안에 성배가 있음을 잘 알았기 때문이다. 그러고는 그가 말했다. \"어질고 자애로우신 주님이시여, 만일 제가 주님을 기쁘게 한 일을 한 번이라도 행하였다면, 주님의 측은하심으로 제가 찾는 것 가운데 무엇이라도 보여 주소서.\" 그러자 그는 방문이 열리고, 그 안에서 큰 광채가 나와 집안이 마치 세상의 모든 횃불이 그곳에 다 모인 듯 환해지는 것을 보았다. 그리하여 그는 방문으로 다가가 들어가려 했다. 그러자 곧 한 목소리가 그에게 말했다. \"멈추어라, 란슬롯 경, 들어가지 마라.\" 그는 뒤로 물러섰고, 마음이 몹시 무거웠다. 그러고는 그가 방 한가운데를 들여다보니, 은으로 된 식탁과 붉은 명주로 덮인 거룩한 그릇이 있었고, 그 주위에 많은 천사들이 있었다. 그중 하나는 타오르는 밀랍 초를, 또 하나는 십자가와 제단의 장식물들을 들고 있었다.\n\n\"오, 그래도 나는 성배를 보았노라. 온통 진홍빛 명주에 둘러싸이고, 그 둘레에는 위대한 천사들, 경외로운 형상들, 날개와 눈들이 있었네.\" — 성배.\n\n그러자 란슬롯 경은 순전한 경탄과 감사에 사로잡혀 자신을 잊고 앞으로 발을 내디뎌 방으로 들어갔다. 그러자 갑자기 불길이 섞인 듯한 숨결이 그의 얼굴을 어찌나 세게 후려쳤던지, 그는 그 때문에 땅에 쓰러져 일어날 힘조차 없었다. 그러고는 그는 자기를 둘러싼 많은 손길을 느꼈는데, 그 손들이 그를 들어 올려 방 밖으로 내가, 정신을 잃은 채로 그곳에 두었으니, 모든 사람들 보기에 그는 죽은 듯했다. 그리하여 이튿날 환한 대낮이 되어 성안의 사람들이 일어났을 때, 그들은 란슬롯 경이 방문 앞에 누워 있는 것을 발견했다. 그들은 그를 살펴보고 그에게 생명이 남아 있는지 알아보려고 맥을 짚었다. 그리고 그들은 그에게서 생명을 발견했으나, 그는 일어서지도, 몸의 어느 부분 하나 움직이지도 못했다. 그리하여 그들은 그를 데려가 어느 방으로 옮겨, 모든 사람들에게서 멀리 떨어진 침상에 눕혔고, 그는 그곳에 여러 날을 누워 있었다. 그러자 한 사람은 그가 살아 있다고 했고, 다른 이들은 아니라고 했다. 그러나 한 노인이 말했다. \"그는 그대들 가운데 가장 힘센 자만큼이나 생명이 가득하오. 그러니 하느님께서 그를 다시 데려오실 때까지 잘 보살피시기를 권하오.\" 그리고 스물나흘이 지나자 그가 눈을 떴다. 그리고 사람들을 보자 그는 크게 슬퍼하며 말했다. \"어찌하여 그대들은 나를 깨웠는가? 나는 지금보다 한결 편안했거늘.\" \"무엇을 보셨소?\" 그를 둘러싼 사람들이 말했다. \"나는 보았소,\" 그가 말했다. \"어떤 혀로도 이를 수 없고, 어떤 마음으로도 헤아릴 수 없는 큰 경이를.\" 그러자 그들이 말했다. \"기사여, 성배의 탐색은 바로 지금 그대 안에서 이루어졌으니, 그대는 이미 본 것보다 더한 것은 결코 보지 못하리다.\" \"나는 하느님께 감사드리오,\" 란슬롯 경이 말했다. \"그 크나큰 자비로 내가 본 것에 대하여. 그것으로 내게는 족하오.\" 그러고는 그가 일어나 옷을 입었다. 그가 그렇게 차려입자 모두가 경탄했으니, 그가 훌륭한 기사 란슬롯 경임을 알았기 때문이다. 그리고 나흘 뒤 그는 성주와 그곳에 있던 모든 동료들에게 작별을 고하고, 자기를 위해 베푼 그들의 큰 수고와 보살핌에 감사했다. 그러고는 그가 떠나 카멜롯으로 향했고, 그곳에서 그는 아서 왕과 귀네비어 왕비를 만났다. 그러나 원탁의 기사들 가운데 많은 이가, 절반이 넘는 이들이 살해되고 멸망한 뒤였다. 그러자 온 궁정이 란슬롯 경을 보고 한없이 기뻐했다. 그리고 그는 떠난 뒤 자기에게 일어난 모든 모험을 왕에게 들려주었다.\n\n# SIR GALAHAD\n\n자, 갈라하드 경은 퍼시벌을 스무 명의 기사로부터 구해 낸 뒤, 어느 광대한 숲속으로 말을 달려 그곳에서 여러 날을 지냈다. 그러고는 바다로 향하는 길을 잡았는데, 그러던 중 어느 은수처에서 밤을 맞게 되었다. 그 선한 사람은 그가 편력 기사임을 보고 기뻐했다. 그리고 그들이 쉬고 있을 때, 한 귀부인이 문을 두드리며 왔다. 그 선한 사람이 그녀가 무엇을 원하는지 알아보려고 문으로 나갔다. 그러자 그녀가 말했다. \"당신과 함께 있는 기사와 이야기를 나누고 싶습니다.\" 그러자 갈라하드가 그녀에게로 가서 무엇을 원하는지 물었다. \"갈라하드 경,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"원컨대 무장을 갖추시고 말에 올라 저를 따라오십시오. 어떤 기사도 일찍이 본 적 없는 가장 높은 모험을 보여드리겠습니다.\" 그러자 갈라하드는 무장을 갖추고 자신을 하느님께 의탁한 뒤, 그 처녀에게 앞장서라 명하고, 그녀가 인도하는 곳을 따라가겠노라 했다.\n\n그리하여 그녀는 자기 말이 실어 나를 수 있는 한 빠르게 달려 바닷가에 이르렀다. 그곳에서 그들은 보호트 경과 퍼시벌 경이 타고 있는 배를 발견했고, 두 사람은 배에서 외쳤다. \"갈라하드 경, 어서 오시오. 우리는 그대를 오래 기다렸소.\" 그가 그들의 말을 듣고는 처녀에게 그들이 누구인지 물었다. \"기사 어른,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그대의 말은 이곳에 두고, 제 말도 두겠습니다. 그리고 우리는 그들 일행에 합류할 것입니다.\" 그리하여 그들은 배에 올랐고, 두 기사는 둘 다를 큰 기쁨으로 맞이했다. 그들은 그 처녀가 퍼시벌 경의 누이임을 알고 있었기 때문이다. 그러자 바람이 일어 그날과 이튿날 내내 그들을 바다 너머로 몰아갔고, 마침내 배는 한없이 크고 놀라운 두 바위 사이에 다다랐다. 그러나 그곳에는 소용돌이가 있어 상륙할 수 없었다. 그런데 또 다른 배 한 척이 있어, 그 위에 오르면 위험 없이 갈 수 있었다. \"저리로 갑시다.\" 귀부인이 말했다. \"그곳에서 우리는 모험을 보게 될 것이니, 그것이 우리 주님의 뜻이기 때문입니다.\" 그러자 갈라하드 경은 성호를 긋고 그 안으로 들어갔고, 그다음으로 귀부인이, 그다음으로 보호트 경과 퍼시벌 경이 들어갔다. 그리고 그들이 배에 올랐을 때, 그곳에서 은으로 된 식탁과, 붉은 명주로 덮인 성배를 발견했다. 그들은 그것에 크게 경배를 올렸고, 갈라하드 경은 우리 주님께 오래도록 기도했으니, 자기가 이 세상을 떠나기를 청하는 그때에 그리될 수 있게 해 달라는 것이었다. 그러자 한 목소리가 그에게 말했다. \"갈라하드, 너는 네 청을 이루리라. 그리고 네가 네 육신의 죽음을 청할 때, 너는 그것을 얻고, 그때 너는 네 영혼의 생명을 찾으리라.\"\n\n그리고 곧 바람이 그들을 바다 너머로 몰아가, 마침내 그들은 사라스라는 도시에 이르렀다. 그러자 그들은 은으로 된 식탁을 배에서 내렸고, 퍼시벌 경과 보호트 경이 앞에서 그것을 들고 갈라하드 경이 뒤따랐으며, 그렇게 그들은 도시로 들어갔다. 그리고 도시의 성문에서 그들은 한 노인을, 절름발이 한 사람을 보았다.\n\n\"그리고 란팔 경이 말했네. '나는 그대에게서 보노라, 나무 위에서 돌아가신 그분의 형상을. 그대 또한 가시관을 쓰셨고, 그대 또한 세상의 매질과 조롱을 받으셨으며, 그대의 생애에 거절되지 않았네, 그대의 두 손과 두 발과 옆구리의 상처들이. 온유하신 마리아의 아드님이시여, 저를 알아주소서. 보소서, 그분을 통하여 제가 그대에게 드리나이다!'\" — 로웰의 성배.\n\n그러자 갈라하드가 그를 불러, 이 무거운 것을 옮기는 일을 도와 달라고 청했다. \"참으로,\" 노인이 말했다. \"내가 목발 없이는 걷지 못한 지 십 년이 되었소.\" \"염려치 마시오,\" 갈라하드 경이 말했다. \"다만 일어나서 그대의 선한 뜻을 보이시오.\" 그러자 노인이 일어나 애써 보니, 자기 몸이 전과 다름없이 온전해진 것을 알았다. 그리고 그는 식탁으로 달려가 갈라하드 경과 함께 한쪽을 들었다.\n\n그들이 도시에 이르렀을 때, 마침 왕이 막 세상을 떠나 온 도시가 당황에 빠져, 누가 자기들의 왕이 될 수 있을지 알지 못하던 참이었다. 바로 그때, 그들이 의논하고 있는 중에 그들 가운데로 한 목소리가 들려와, 그 세 기사 중 가장 젊은 기사를 왕으로 택하라고 명했다. 그리하여 그들은 도시 전체의 동의로 갈라하드 경을 왕으로 삼았다. 그리고 그가 왕이 되자, 그는 거룩한 그릇을 담을 함을 금과 보석으로 만들라고 명했다. 그리고 날마다 세 동료는 그 앞에 나아와 기도를 올리곤 했다.\n\n한 해가 끝날 무렵, 갈라하드 경이 왕관을 받은 바로 그날, 그는 일찍 일어나 동료들과 함께 거룩한 그릇이 있는 곳으로 갔다. 그리고 그들은 그 앞에 무릎을 꿇은 한 사람을 보았는데, 그 둘레에는 천사들의 큰 무리가 있었다. 그가 갈라하드 경을 불러 말했다. \"오라, 주님의 종이여, 그러면 그대가 그토록 보기를 갈망하던 것을 보게 되리라.\" 그리하여 갈라하드 경이 영적인 것들을 바라보기 시작하자 그의 필멸하는 육신이 몹시 심하게 떨렸다. 그러자 그 선한 사람이 말했다. \"이제 내가 누구인지 알겠는가?\" \"모르겠나이다.\" 갈라하드 경이 말했다. \"나는 아리마태아의 요셉이니, 우리 주님께서 그대에게 벗이 되어 주라고 나를 이곳 그대에게로 보내셨노라.\" 그러자 갈라하드 경이 하늘을 향해 두 손을 들어 올리며 말했다. \"이제, 복되신 주님이시여, 만일 주님께서 기뻐하신다면 저는 더 이상 살기를 원치 않나이다.\" 그리고 이 말을 마치자, 갈라하드 경은 퍼시벌 경과 보호트 경에게로 가서 그들에게 입을 맞추고, 그들을 하느님께 맡겼다. 그러고는 그가 식탁 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 기도를 올리니, 갑자기 그의 영혼이 떠났고, 천사들의 큰 무리가 그의 영혼을 하늘로 들어 올려, 두 동료가 그것을 똑똑히 바라볼 수 있었다. 또한 그들은 하늘에서 손 하나가 내려오는 것을 보았으나, 그 몸은 보지 못했다. 그 손은 곧장 그릇으로 와서 그것을 하늘로 들어 올렸다. 그 후로는 누구도 다시는 이 땅에서 성배를 보았노라 감히 말할 만큼 담대한 자가 없었다.\n\n────\n\n# SIR AGRIVAIN’S TREASON\n\n퍼시벌 경과 보호트 경은 갈라하드 경이 죽은 것을 보았을 때, 일찍이 어떤 두 사람도 보인 적 없을 만큼 깊은 슬픔에 잠겼다. 만약 그들이 선량한 사람이 아니었다면 절망에 빠지고 말았을 것이다. 갈라하드 경이 묻히자마자 퍼시벌 경은 도시 밖의 어느 은수처로 물러나 수도자의 옷을 입었다. 보호트 경은 늘 그와 함께 있었으나 세속의 옷을 갈아입지는 않았으니, 로에그리아 왕국으로 돌아갈 작정이었기 때문이다. 이렇게 퍼시벌 경은 은수처에서 일 년 두 달 동안 더없이 거룩한 삶을 살다가 이 세상을 떠났고, 보호트 경은 그를 누이와 갈라하드 경의 곁에 묻었다. 그런 다음 보호트 경은 무장을 갖추고 사라스를 떠나 배에 올라 로에그리아 왕국으로 항해하여, 마침내 무사히 카멜롯에 도착했으니, 그곳에 왕이 있었다. 그러자 온 궁정이 그를 두고 크게 기뻐했으니, 그들은 그가 죽은 줄로만 알았던 것이다. 그리하여 왕은 학식 높은 서기들을 불러들여 선량한 기사들의 드높은 모험을 기록하게 했다. 그리고 보호트 경은 자신과 두 동료 퍼시벌 경, 갈라하드 경에게 일어났던 모험을 이야기했다. 또 란슬롯 경은 자신이 본 성배의 모험을 이야기했다. 이 모든 것이 큰 책으로 만들어져 솔즈베리의 교회에 봉안되었다.\n\n이렇게 아서 왕과 귀네비어 왕비는 고향으로 돌아온 자들을, 그중에서도 특히 란슬롯 경과 보호트 경을 두고 크게 기뻐했다. 그러나 란슬롯 경은 다시 귀네비어 왕비를 찾기 시작했고, 탐색 길에서 했던 약속을 잊고 말았다. 그리하여 궁정 안에서 많은 이가 그 일을 입에 올렸으니, 특히 가웨인 경의 동생 아그리베인 경이 그러했다. 그는 늘 입이 가벼웠기 때문이다. 그러던 어느 날 가웨인 경과 그 형제들이 모두 아서 왕의 방에 있었는데, 그때 아그리베인 경이 이렇게 드러내 놓고 말했다. \"아서 왕처럼 고귀한 기사가 란슬롯 경과 왕비의 행실로 인해 이토록 수치를 당하는 것을 보면서도 우리가 모두 부끄러워하지 않으니, 참으로 놀랄 일이오.\" 그러자 가웨인 경이 말했다. \"아우 아그리베인 경, 청컨대, 또한 명하건대, 내 앞에서 다시는 그런 일을 꺼내지 말게. 분명히 말하건대 나는 자네 편에 서지 않을 것이니.\" \"우리도 그렇소.\" 가헤리스 경과 가레스 경이 말했다. \"그럼 내가 하지요.\" 모드레드 경이 말했다. \"자네라면 의심치 않네.\" 가웨인 경이 말했다. \"자네는 늘 온갖 해악에 기울었으니. 그러나 나는 자네가 이 모든 일에서 손을 떼었으면 하네, 그 끝에 무엇이 올지를 내가 알기 때문일세.\"\n\n\"모드레드의 좁고 여우 같은 얼굴, 마음을 감추는 미소, 잿빛으로 끈질긴 눈. 이제부터 영혼을 보살펴 죽을 수 없는 죽음에서 구하고 극한에서마저 지켜 주려는 힘들이 괴롭히고 들볶기 시작했다.\" ─〈귀네비어〉\n\n\"그 끝에 무엇이 오든 상관없소.\" 아그리베인 경이 말했다. \"나는 이 일을 왕께 아뢸 것이오.\" 그러는 사이 아서 왕이 그들에게로 왔다. \"자, 형제들, 입을 다무시오.\" 가웨인 경이 말했다. \"그러지 않겠소.\" 아그리베인 경이 말했다. 그러자 가웨인 경이 말했다. \"나는 자네 이야기를 듣지도 않을 것이며, 자네 편에 서지도 않을 것일세.\" \"나도 더는 그러지 않겠소.\" 가레스 경과 가헤리스 경이 말했고, 그러면서 그들은 크나큰 슬픔에 잠겨 자리를 떠났다.\n\n그러자 아그리베인 경은 란슬롯 경과 왕비의 행실에 관해 궁정에서 오간 말을 모두 왕에게 아뢰었고, 그 말에 왕은 몹시 비통해했다. 그러나 왕은 증거 없이는 그것이 사실이라 믿으려 하지 않았다. 그리하여 아그리베인 경은 란슬롯 경과 왕비를 함정에 빠뜨릴 계략을 꾸몄으니, 그 둘을 방심한 틈에 한자리에서 붙잡을 작정이었다. 아그리베인 경과 모드레드 경이 이 목적으로 한 무리를 이끌었으나, 란슬롯 경은 아그리베인 경을 죽이고 모드레드 경에게 상처를 입힌 뒤 그들에게서 벗어났다. 그런 다음 란슬롯 경은 벗들에게로 서둘러 가서 무슨 일이 있었는지 일러 주고는 그들과 함께 숲으로 물러났다. 다만 그는 무슨 일이 벌어지든 소식을 전해 줄 첩자들을 남겨 두었다.\n\n이렇게 란슬롯 경은 벗어났으나 왕비는 왕의 손아귀에 남게 되었고, 아서 왕은 더 이상 그녀의 죄를 의심할 수 없었다. 그 시절의 법은 이러했으니, 그 신분이나 지위가 어떠하든 그런 죄를 저지른 자는 화형에 처해 죽어야 했다. 그리하여 귀네비어 왕비에게도 그렇게 명해졌다. 그러자 아서 왕이 가웨인 경에게 말했다. \"청컨대 가장 좋은 갑옷을 입고, 그대의 형제 가헤리스 경과 가레스 경과 함께 채비를 갖추어, 나의 왕비를 불길로 데려가 그곳에서 죽음을 받게 하시오.\" \"아니 됩니다, 더없이 고귀하신 주군이시여.\" 가웨인 경이 말했다. \"그 일만은 결코 하지 않겠나이다. 똑똑히 아소서, 제 마음이 그녀가 죽는 것을 차마 보지 못할 것이며, 제가 그녀의 죽음에 가담했다는 말은 결코 나오지 않을 것입니다.\" 그러자 왕은 가헤리스 경과 가레스 경에게 그곳에 있으라 명했고, 그들은 말했다. \"명하신 대로 그곳에 있겠나이다, 폐하. 다만 평온한 차림으로, 갑옷은 몸에 걸치지 않겠나이다.\"\n\n이렇게 왕비가 끌려 나왔고, 그녀를 고해 받게 하려고 영적 아버지가 그녀에게로 인도되었으며, 그곳에는 많은 영주와 귀부인의 울음과 통곡이 있었다. 그리고 누군가 가서 왕비가 죽음을 향해 끌려 나왔다고 란슬롯 경에게 일렀다. 그러자 란슬롯 경과 그와 함께 있던 기사들은 왕비를 지키던 무리를 덮쳐 그들을 흩어 버리고, 맞서는 자들을 모두 베어 버렸다. 그 혼란 속에서 가레스 경과 가헤리스 경도 죽임을 당했으니, 그들은 무장하지 않아 몸을 지킬 수 없었기 때문이다. 그리고 란슬롯 경은 왕비를 자신의 성 라 주아유즈 가르드로 데려갔다.\n\n그때 누군가 가웨인 경에게로 와서 란슬롯 경이 기사들을 죽이고 왕비를 데려간 일을 일러 주었다. \"오 주여, 제 형제들을 지켜 주소서!\" 가웨인 경이 말했다. \"진실로,\" 그 사람이 말했다. \"가레스 경과 가헤리스 경께서 죽임을 당하셨나이다.\" \"아아!\" 가웨인 경이 말했다. \"이제 내 기쁨이 사라졌구나.\" 그러고는 그는 쓰러져 정신을 잃었고, 마치 죽은 사람처럼 오래도록 그곳에 누워 있었다.\n\n가웨인 경은 혼절에서 깨어나자 왕에게로 달려가 부르짖었다. \"오 아서 왕이시여, 제 외숙이시여, 제 형제들이 죽임을 당했나이다.\" 그러자 왕도 그도 함께 눈물을 흘렸다. \"제 임금이시며, 제 주군이시며, 제 외숙이시여,\" 가웨인 경이 말했다. \"이제 증인이 되어 주소서, 제가 기사의 명예를 걸고 약속을 드리나니, 오늘부터 우리 둘 중 하나가 다른 하나를 죽이기 전까지 저는 결코 란슬롯 경을 놓아주지 않겠나이다. 일곱 왕의 영토를 두루 헤매며 란슬롯 경을 찾을 것이며, 제가 그를 죽이든 그가 저를 죽이든 할 것입니다.\" \"그를 찾을 필요는 없을 것이오.\" 왕이 말했다. \"내가 듣기로 란슬롯 경은 주아유즈 가르드에서 나와 그대를 기다릴 것이라 하며, 또한 듣건대 많은 사람이 그에게로 모여든다 하오.\" \"그 말씀은 믿을 만하옵니다.\" 가웨인 경이 말했다. \"그러나 주군이시여, 폐하의 벗들을 부르소서, 그러면 저도 제 벗들을 부르겠나이다.\" \"그리하겠소.\" 왕이 말했다. 그리하여 왕은 잉글랜드 전역에, 그 길이와 너비를 가리지 않고 모든 기사를 불러 모으는 서한과 영장을 보냈다. 그러자 아서에게로 많은 기사와 공작과 백작이 모여들었으니, 그는 큰 군세를 갖추게 되었다. 란슬롯 경도 그 소식을 듣고 모을 수 있는 자들을 모두 모았다. 많은 선량한 기사가 그를 위해서, 또 왕비를 위해서 그의 편에 섰다. 그러나 아서 왕의 군세가 너무도 커서 란슬롯 경은 들판에서 그를 맞아 싸울 수 없었고, 그는 왕을 상대로 싸우는 것을 몹시 꺼렸다. 그리하여 란슬롯 경은 온갖 양식을 갖추고 자신의 견고한 성으로 물러났다. 그러자 아서 왕이 가웨인 경과 함께 와서 라 주아유즈 가르드를, 그 마을과 성을 빙 둘러 포위했다. 그러나 란슬롯 경은 여러 주가 지나도록 결코 성 밖으로 말을 몰아 나오지 않았고, 자신의 기사 누구도 밖으로 나가게 두지 않았다.\n\n그러던 어느 추수철의 하루, 란슬롯 경이 성벽 너머를 내다보며 아서 왕과 가웨인 경에게 큰 소리로 말했다. \"두 분 주군이시여, 이 포위로 하시는 모든 일이 헛되옵니다. 여기서 두 분은 영예를 얻지 못하고 오직 치욕만 얻으실 뿐이니, 만약 제가 제 선량한 기사들과 함께 나서고자 한다면 곧 이 전쟁을 끝장낼 것이옵니다.\" \"나오라.\" 아서가 말했다. \"그럴 배짱이 있다면. 내 약속하건대 들판 한가운데서 그대를 맞으리라.\" \"하느님께서 막아 주소서,\" 란슬롯 경이 말했다. \"저를 기사로 만들어 주신 더없이 고귀하신 왕과 맞붙는 일만은.\" \"그 입에 발린 말은 집어치워라.\" 왕이 말했다. \"똑똑히 알아 두라, 나는 그대의 철천지원수이며, 죽는 날까지 늘 그러할 것이다.\" 그리고 가웨인 경이 말했다. \"그대에게 맞서 무기 한번 들지 않은 내 형제 가헤리스 경을, 그리고 그대가 기사로 만들어 주었고 내 모든 혈족보다 그대를 더 사랑했던 가레스 경을 죽일 무슨 까닭이 있었느냐? 그러니 똑똑히 알아 두라, 나는 살아 있는 한 그대에게 전쟁을 벌일 것이다.\"\n\n보호트 경과 헥터 디 마리스 경, 그리고 라이오넬 경이 이 외침을 듣고는 팔라메데스 경과 그 형제 새파이어 경, 라웨인 경, 그 밖의 많은 이를 불러 모두 함께 란슬롯 경에게로 갔다. 그리고 그들이 말했다. \"주군이신 란슬롯 경이시여, 청컨대 우리의 봉사를 원하신다면 더 이상 우리를 이 성벽 안에 가두지 마소서. 똑똑히 아소서, 당신의 그 모든 고운 말과 인내는 당신께 아무 소용이 없을 것입니다.\" \"아아!\" 란슬롯 경이 말했다. \"말을 몰아 나가 싸우는 일을 나는 몹시 꺼리노라.\" 그러고는 그는 다시 왕과 가웨인 경에게 말하여 그들에게 싸움에서 빠져 있으라고 권했으나, 그들은 그의 말을 업신여겼다. 그리하여 란슬롯 경의 무리가 한껏 좋은 진용을 갖추어 성에서 나왔다. 그리고 란슬롯 경은 언제나 모든 기사에게 어떤 일이 있어도 아서 왕과 가웨인 경만은 살려 두라고 당부했다.\n\n그때 가웨인 경이 왕의 군세에서 나와 결투를 청했고, 라이오넬 경이 그와 맞붙었는데, 거기서 가웨인 경이 라이오넬 경의 몸을 꿰뚫어 그가 죽은 듯이 땅에 쓰러졌다. 그러자 큰 싸움이 벌어져 많은 사람이 죽임을 당했다. 그러나 란슬롯 경은 늘 아서 왕 편의 사람들을 살리려 애썼고, 아서 왕은 늘 란슬롯 경을 뒤쫓아 그를 죽이려 했다. 그러나 란슬롯 경은 왕을 참아 주며 되받아치려 하지 않았다. 그때 보호트 경이 아서 왕과 맞붙어 그를 쳐서 쓰러뜨렸다. 그는 말에서 내려 칼을 뽑고 란슬롯 경에게 말했다. \"제가 이 전쟁을 끝내 버릴까요?\" 아서 왕을 죽이려는 뜻이었다. \"그러지 말라.\" 란슬롯 경이 말했다. \"그에게 더는 손대지 말라. 나를 기사로 만들어 주신 더없이 고귀하신 왕이 죽임을 당하거나 수치를 당하는 것을 나는 결코 보지 않을 것이니.\" 그러면서 란슬롯 경은 말에서 내려 왕을 일으켜 다시 말에 태우고 이렇게 말했다. \"주군 아서시여, 하느님의 사랑을 위해 이 다툼을 멈추소서.\" 그러자 아서 왕은 란슬롯 경을 바라보았고, 그 어떤 사람보다도 란슬롯 경에게 깃든 크나큰 예의를 생각하며 두 눈에서 눈물을 쏟았다. 그러면서 왕은 말을 몰아 떠나갔다. 이윽고 양편은 곧 물러나 휴식을 취하며 죽은 자들을 묻었다.\n\n그러나 전쟁은 계속되었고, 그 소문이 온 그리스도교 세계에 퍼졌으며, 마침내 교황에게까지 전해졌다. 교황은 아서 왕과 란슬롯 경의 크나큰 선량함을 헤아려, 당시 자신의 영토에 있던 고귀한 성직자 로체스터의 주교를 불러 그를 아서 왕에게 보냈으니, 왕이 왕비 귀네비어 부인을 다시 자기 곁으로 받아들이고 란슬롯 경과 화평할 것을 명하였다.\n\n이렇게 이 주교의 주선으로 일 년 동안의 화평이 이루어졌다. 아서 왕은 왕비를 다시 받아들였고, 란슬롯 경은 모든 기사와 함께 왕국을 떠나 자신의 고향으로 갔다. 그리하여 그들은 카디프에서 배에 올라 벤위크로 항해했으니, 어떤 이들은 그곳을 바욘이라 부른다. 그 땅의 모든 백성이 란슬롯 경에게로 와서 그를 더없이 기쁘게 맞아들였다. 그리고 란슬롯 경은 자신의 모든 마을과 성을 정비하고 갖추었으며, 라이오넬 경과 보호트 경, 헥터 디 마리스 경, 블라모르 경, 라웨인 경, 그 밖의 많은 이를 크게 높여 그들을 땅과 성의 영주로 삼았으니, 마침내 자신에게는 그들 누구보다도 더 많은 것을 남기지 않았다.\n\n\"그러자 아서는 거대한 잔치를 열었고, 낯선 기사들이 사방의 바람을 타고 모여들었다. 그리하여 저마다 앉아서, 하늘과 땅과 강과 바다에서 가려 뽑은 진미를 받으면서도, 잔치 한복판에서 곧잘 제 눈으로 이웃의 됨됨이와 힘을 가늠하곤 했다.\" ─〈펠레아스와 에타르〉\n\n그러나 그 한 해가 지나자, 아서 왕과 가웨인 경이 큰 군세를 거느리고 와서 란슬롯 경의 영토에 상륙하여, 휩쓸 수 있는 모든 것을 불사르고 황폐하게 만들었다. 그러자 보호트 경이 말했다. \"주군이신 란슬롯 경이시여, 저희가 들판에서 그들을 맞아 싸우도록 허락해 주소서. 그러면 그들이 이 나라에 온 것을 두고두고 후회하게 만들겠나이다.\" 그러자 란슬롯 경이 말했다. \"그리스도교도의 피를 흘리는 일이기에 나는 기사들을 거느리고 말을 몰아 나가기를 몹시 꺼리노라. 그러니 우리는 얼마간 더 성벽을 지킬 것이며, 나는 주군 아서께 사자를 보내어 조약을 제안하겠노라. 늘 전쟁만 하는 것보다는 화평이 나으니.\" 그리하여 란슬롯 경은 한 처녀와 그 곁에 난쟁이 하나를 딸려 보내어, 아서 왕에게 자신의 영토에 대한 전쟁을 멈출 것을 청했다. 처녀는 작은 말을 타고 길을 나섰고, 난쟁이는 그 곁에서 달렸다. 처녀가 아서 왕의 천막에 이르러 말에서 내리자, 그곳에서 점잖은 기사인 시종장 루칸 경이 그녀를 맞으며 말했다. \"어여쁜 처녀여, 란슬롯 두 라크 경에게서 왔는가?\" \"그러하옵니다, 기사님.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"왕께 말씀드리러 왔나이다.\" \"아아!\" 루칸 경이 말했다. \"주군 아서께서는 란슬롯 경과 화해하고자 하시나, 가웨인 경이 이를 허락지 않으신다오.\" 그러면서 루칸 경은 처녀를 왕에게로 인도했으니, 그곳에 왕이 가웨인 경과 함께 앉아 그녀가 무슨 말을 할지 들으려 했다. 그리하여 그녀가 사연을 말하자, 왕의 두 눈에서 눈물이 흘러내렸다. 그리고 가웨인 경 하나만을 제외하고는 모든 영주가 앞다투어 왕에게 란슬롯 경과 화합하라고 권했다. 가웨인 경이 말했다. \"주군이시며 제 외숙이시여, 어찌하시려나이까? 이토록 멀리 길을 떠나오시고도 이제 와서 돌아서시렵니까? 그리하신다면 온 세상이 폐하를 두고 수치라 말할 것입니다.\" \"아니오.\" 아서 왕이 말했다. \"나는 그대가 권하는 대로 하겠소. 다만 처녀에게는 그대가 답을 주시오. 가엾어서 내가 그녀에게 말을 할 수 없으니.\"\n\n그러자 가웨인 경이 말했다. \"처녀여, 란슬롯 경에게 전하라. 내 외숙께 화평을 구하는 것은 헛수고이며, 나 가웨인 경이 이렇게 전한다고 하라. 하느님과 기사도에 진 신의를 걸고 약속하건대, 그가 나를 죽이거나 내가 그를 죽이기 전까지 나는 결코 그를 놓아주지 않으리라.\" 이렇게 처녀는 돌아갔고, 란슬롯 경이 이 답을 듣자 두 뺨으로 눈물이 흘러내렸다.\n\n그러던 어느 날 가웨인 경이 온몸을 무장하고 성문 앞에 와서 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"이 거짓된 배신자 란슬롯 경아, 지금 어디 있느냐? 어찌하여 비겁자처럼 굴과 성벽 안에 숨어 있느냐? 이제 나오라, 이 배신자 기사야, 내 세 형제의 죽음을 네 몸뚱이에 갚아 주리라.\" 란슬롯 경과 그 곁에 있던 기사들이 이 모든 말을 들었고, 그들이 그에게 말했다. \"란슬롯 경이시여, 이제는 기사답게 자신을 지키셔야 하나이다. 그러지 않으면 영원히 수치를 당하실 것이니, 너무 오래 잠자코 계셨고 너무 많이 참으셨나이다.\" 그러자 란슬롯 경이 아서 왕에게 큰 소리로 말했다. \"주군 아서시여, 이제 저는 오래 참아 왔고, 폐하와 가웨인 경이 하고자 하시는 대로 두었나이다. 그러나 이제는 부득이 제 몸을 지켜야 하니, 가웨인 경이 저를 반역으로 고발하였기 때문입니다.\" 그러고는 란슬롯 경이 무장하고 말에 올랐으며, 고귀한 기사들이 도시에서 나왔고, 바깥의 군세는 모두 멀찍이 물러섰다. 그리하여 둘 중 하나가 죽거나 항복하기 전까지는 누구도 두 기사에게 가까이 가거나 끼어들지 않기로 약조가 맺어졌다.\n\n그런 다음 란슬롯 경과 가웨인 경은 서로 멀찍이 떨어졌다가, 말을 한껏 내달려 서로에게로 다가와 저마다 상대의 방패 한가운데를 쳤다. 그러나 둘 중 누구도 말에서 떨어지지 않았고, 다만 그들의 말이 땅에 쓰러졌다. 그러자 그들은 말에서 뛰어내려 칼을 뽑고 수많은 매서운 칼질을 주고받았으니, 여러 군데서 피가 솟구쳤다. 그런데 가웨인 경은 어느 거룩한 사람에게서 이런 능력을 받았으니, 한 해의 매일 아침부터 정오까지 그의 힘이 세 배로 늘어났다가 다시 본래의 크기로 줄어드는 것이었다. 란슬롯 경은 이를 알고 있었기에, 가웨인 경의 힘이 절정에 이르는 세 시간 동안 방패로 몸을 가리고 자신의 힘을 비축해 두었다. 그동안 가웨인 경이 그에게 수많은 매서운 일격을 가했으니, 지켜보던 모든 기사가 란슬롯 경이 어찌 그것을 견디는지 놀라워했다. 그러다 정오가 지나자 가웨인 경에게는 자신의 본래 힘만 남았고, 란슬롯 경은 그가 그렇게 약해진 것을 느끼자 몸을 곧추세워 칼질을 곱절로 늘려, 가웨인 경에게 일격을 가하니 그가 옆으로 쓰러졌다. 그러자 란슬롯 경은 뒤로 물러나 더는 치려 하지 않았다. \"어찌하여 물러서느냐, 거짓된 배신자야?\" 그때 가웨인 경이 말했다. \"이제 다시 돌아서서 나를 죽여라. 네가 나를 이렇게 두면, 내가 다시 멀쩡해졌을 때 너와 또 싸울 것이다.\" \"하느님의 은총으로 그대를 참아 주겠소, 기사여.\" 란슬롯 경이 말했다. \"그러나 똑똑히 아시오, 가웨인 경. 나는 쓰러진 기사는 결코 치지 않을 것이오.\" 그리하여 란슬롯 경은 도시로 들어갔고, 가웨인 경은 아서 왕의 천막으로 옮겨져 그의 상처가 치료되었다.\n\n이렇게 포위는 이어졌고, 가웨인 경은 거의 한 달 동안 꼼짝 못 하고 누워 있었다. 그리고 그가 거의 회복되었을 무렵, 아서 왕에게 한 소식이 전해졌으니, 그로 인해 왕은 온 군세를 거느리고 잉글랜드로 돌아가게 되었다.\n\n————\n\n# MORTE D’ARTHUR\n\n모드레드 경은 잉글랜드 전역의 통치자로 남아, 마치 바다 너머에서 온 것인 양 아서 왕이 전투에서 죽었다는 내용의 편지가 쓰이도록 했다. 그리하여 그는 의회를 소집해 스스로 왕으로 즉위했다. 또한 그는 왕비 귀네비어를 차지하고는 그녀와 혼인하겠노라고 노골적으로 말했으나, 그녀는 그에게서 달아나 런던탑으로 피신했다. 그러자 모드레드 경은 그곳으로 가서 런던탑을 포위하고 거센 공격을 퍼부었으나, 그 무엇도 그에게 도움이 되지 못했다. 그때 모드레드 경에게, 아서 왕이 란슬롯 경에 대한 포위를 풀고 본국으로 돌아오고 있다는 전갈이 당도했다. 그러자 모드레드 경은 나라 안의 모든 영주를 소집했다. 또한 수많은 사람들이 모드레드 경에게로 모여들어, 좋든 나쁘든 그와 함께하겠노라고 말했다. 그리하여 그는 도버로 대군을 이끌고 갔으니, 그곳으로 아서 왕이 도착하리라는 말을 들었기 때문이었다.\n\n\"나는 서쪽에서 모드레드의 발소리를 듣노라, 그와 함께 그대의 백성도 많이 따르며, 한때 그대의 기사였던 자들, 그대가 사랑하였으나 이제 이교도보다 더 천박해져, 자신들의 맹세와 그대에게 침을 뱉는 자들이로다.\" — 「아서의 떠남」.\n\n그리고 모드레드 경이 군대를 거느리고 도버에 있을 때, 아서 왕이 수많은 배와 갤리선을 이끌고 당도했으니, 모드레드 경은 그 상륙 지점에서 기다리고 있었다. 그리하여 크고 작은 배들이 무장한 고귀한 사람들로 가득 찬 채 진수되었고, 양편 모두에서 고결한 기사들의 살육이 크게 일어났다. 그러나 아서 왕은 어찌나 용맹했던지, 그 어떤 기사도 그의 상륙을 막을 수 없었으며, 그의 기사들도 맹렬히 그를 뒤따랐다. 그리하여 그들은 상륙해 모드레드 경을 뒤로 밀어붙였으니, 그가 달아나고 그의 모든 백성도 달아났다. 그리고 전투가 끝나자 아서 왕은 죽은 자기 백성들을 매장하라고 명했다. 그때 고귀한 가웨인 경이 큰 배 안에서, 반쯤 죽은 채 누워 있는 것이 발견되었다. 아서 왕이 그에게로 가서 한없는 슬픔에 잠겼다. \"외숙부님,\" 가웨인 경이 말했다. \"제 임종의 날이 왔음을 잘 알아 주십시오. 이 모든 것은 저 자신의 성급함과 고집 때문이니, 란슬롯 경이 제게 입혔던 옛 상처를 다시 맞았고, 그 때문에 제가 죽으리라는 것을 느낍니다. 그리고 란슬롯 경이 옛날처럼 폐하와 함께 있었더라면 이 전쟁은 결코 시작되지 않았을 것이며, 이 모든 일의 원인이 바로 저입니다.\" 그러고 나서 가웨인 경은 왕에게 란슬롯 경을 불러들이고 그를 다른 모든 기사보다 더 아껴 달라고 청했다. 그리하여 정오 무렵 가웨인 경은 숨을 거두었고, 왕은 그를 도버 성 안의 한 예배당에 안장하라고 명했다. 그곳에서는 누구든 그의 두개골을 볼 수 있고, 란슬롯 경이 전투에서 그에게 입힌 바로 그 상처도 보인다.\n\n그때 왕에게, 모드레드 경이 배런다운에 진을 쳤다는 말이 전해졌다. 왕이 그곳으로 말을 달려가니 양편 사이에 큰 전투가 벌어졌고, 아서 왕의 무리가 가장 우세했으며, 모드레드 경과 그의 무리는 캔터베리로 달아났다.\n\n그리고 아서 왕과 모드레드 경 사이에는, 솔즈베리 인근, 바닷가에서 멀지 않은 어느 구릉에서 다시 한 번 전투를 벌이기로 날이 정해졌다. 그리고 밤에 왕이 잠들었을 때, 그는 기이한 꿈을 꾸었다. 그에게는 정말로 가웨인 경이, 아리따운 여인들을 여럿 거느리고 자기에게 다가오는 것처럼 보였다. 아서 왕이 그를 보고 말했다. \"어서 오너라, 내 누이의 아들아. 나는 네가 죽은 줄로만 알았는데, 이제 네가 살아 있는 것을 보니 내 기쁨이 크구나. 그런데, 오, 아리따운 조카야, 너와 함께 이리로 온 이 여인들은 누구냐?\" \"전하,\" 가웨인 경이 말했다. \"이들은 모두 제가 살아 있는 사람이었을 때 그들을 위해 싸웠던 여인들입니다. 그리고 제가 의로운 다툼에서 그들을 위해 싸웠기에, 그들이 제게 은총을 베풀어 저를 이리로 데려와 전하께 경고하게 하였으니, 만일 전하께서 내일 모드레드 경과 싸우신다면 전하의 죽음을 맞이하시리라는 것입니다. 그러니 전하께서는 화친을 맺으시고, 한 달의 유예를 위해 넉넉히 양보하십시오. 한 달 안에 란슬롯 경과 그의 모든 고귀한 기사들이 와서 전하를 명예롭게 구하고, 모드레드 경과 그를 따르는 모든 자를 무찌를 것이기 때문입니다.\" 그러고 나서 가웨인 경과 모든 여인들이 사라졌다. 그러자 곧 왕은 고귀한 영주들과 지혜로운 주교들을 자기에게로 불러오게 했다. 그들이 오자 왕은 자기가 본 환영과 가웨인 경이 자기에게 한 말을 들려주었다. 그러고 나서 왕은 시종장 루칸 경과 베디비어 경을 두 주교와 함께 보내며, 무슨 수를 써서라도 모드레드 경과 한 달 하고도 하루의 화친을 맺으라고 명했다. 그리하여 그들은 떠나 모드레드 경에게 갔다. 그리하여 마침내 모드레드 경은 아서가 살아 있는 동안에는 콘월과 켄트를 갖고, 그가 죽은 뒤에는 잉글랜드 전역을 갖는 데에 합의했다.\n\n\"모드레드 경, 그는 왕에게 가장 가까운 자, 그의 조카, 늘 음흉한 짐승처럼 두 눈으로 왕좌를 노리며 웅크려 누워, 기회를 기다리며 덤벼들 채비를 하던 자였다.\" — 「기네비어」.\n\n그러고 나서, 아서 왕과 모드레드 경이 양편 군대 사이에서 만나되, 각자 열네 사람씩을 데려와 그 자리에서 화친에 서명하기로 합의되었다. 그리고 아서 왕과 그의 기사들이 나아갈 채비를 했을 때, 그는 자기 군대 전체에게 이렇게 경고했다. \"만일 칼 한 자루라도 뽑히는 것을 보거든, 너희는 맹렬히 달려나와 맞서는 자는 누구든 베어 버려라. 나는 저 배신자 모드레드 경을 결코 믿지 않기 때문이다.\" 모드레드 경도 마찬가지로 자기 군대에 경고했다. 그리하여 그들은 만나, 빈틈없이 합의하고 화목하게 되었다. 그리고 포도주가 들어와 그들이 마셨다. 바로 그때, 작은 히스 덤불에서 살무사 한 마리가 나와 어느 기사의 발을 물었다. 그 기사가 물린 것을 느끼고 아래를 내려다보니 살무사가 보였고, 그래서 그는 살무사를 죽이려고 칼을 뽑았을 뿐 다른 해의를 품지는 않았다. 그런데 양편 군대가 그 뽑힌 칼을 보자, 나팔과 뿔피리를 불며 크게 외쳤다. 그리고 아서 왕은 말에 올라 자기 무리에게 달려가며 말했다. \"아, 이 불행한 날이여!\" 모드레드 경도 마찬가지로 했다. 그리하여 그리스도교 땅에서 일찍이 그보다 더 비통한 전투는 없었다. 아서 왕은 줄곧 전장을 누비며, 가치 있는 왕이 마땅히 해야 할 바대로 더없이 고귀하게 싸웠고, 모드레드 경도 그날 자기 임무를 다하며 큰 위험에 몸을 던졌다. 그리하여 그들은 긴 하루 내내 싸웠고, 마침내 모든 고귀한 기사 대부분이 땅 위에 죽어 누웠다. 그때 왕이 주위를 둘러보니, 자기 군대 전체에서 살아남은 자는 단 두 기사뿐이었으니, 시종장 루칸 경과 그의 형제 베디비어 경이었고, 그들은 심한 상처를 입고 있었다.\n\n그때 아서 왕은 모드레드 경이 죽은 자들의 큰 무더기 사이에서 자기 칼에 기대어 있는 곳을 보았다. \"이제 내 창을 다오.\" 아서가 루칸 경에게 말했다. \"저기 이 모든 비통함을 빚어낸 그 배신자가 보이는구나.\" \"전하, 그를 내버려 두십시오.\" 루칸 경이 말했다. \"전하께서 이 불행한 날을 넘기신다면, 마땅히 그에게 제대로 복수하실 것입니다. 가웨인 경의 혼령이 전하께 한 말을 기억하시고, 이제 그만두십시오. 전하께서 전장을 차지하셨으니, 지금 그만두신다면 이 운명의 사악한 날도 지나갈 것입니다.\" \"살든 죽든,\" 아서 왕이 말했다. \"그는 이제 내 손아귀를 벗어나지 못한다.\" 그러고 나서 왕은 두 손으로 창을 잡고 모드레드 경을 향해 달려가며 외쳤다. \"배신자야, 이제 네 임종의 날이 왔다.\" 그리하여 아서 왕은 방패 아래로 모드레드 경을 찔러, 그 몸을 창으로 꿰뚫었다. 그리고 모드레드 경은 자기가 치명상을 입은 것을 느끼자, 남은 힘을 다해 두 손으로 잡은 칼로 아서 왕의 머리 옆쪽을 내리쳤으니, 그 칼이 투구와 두개골을 꿰뚫었다. 그러고 나서 모드레드 경은 땅 위에 완전히 죽어 쓰러졌다. 그리고 고귀한 아서는 정신을 잃고 땅에 쓰러졌다. 그러자 시종장 루칸 경과 베디비어 경이 그를 일으켜, 둘이서 부축하여 바닷가에서 멀지 않은 작은 예배당으로 조심스레 데려갔다. 그리고 왕은 그곳에 이르자 한결 편안해졌다고 여겼다. 그때 그들은 들판에서 사람들이 외치는 소리를 들었다. 루칸 경이 그 외침이 무엇을 뜻하는지 보러 갔다. 그리하여 그는 달빛 속에서, 약탈자와 도적들이 죽은 자들을 약탈하러 온 것을 보았다. 그래서 그는 돌아와 왕에게 말했다. \"제 생각으로는, 전하를 어느 마을로 모셔 가는 것이 가장 좋겠습니다.\" \"그리하면 좋겠다.\" 왕이 말했다. 그리고 왕이 가려고 하니 그는 기절했다. 그러자 루칸 경이 한쪽에서, 베디비어 경이 다른 쪽에서 왕을 들어 올렸다. 그런데 들어 올리던 중에 루칸 경이 정신을 잃고 땅에 쓰러졌으니, 그가 심한 상처를 입었기 때문이었다. 그러고 나서 그 고귀한 기사의 심장이 터졌다. 왕이 깨어나 보니, 루칸 경이 입에 거품을 물고 말도 못한 채 누워 있는 것이 보였다. \"아아!\" 왕이 말했다. \"이 고귀한 공작이 나 때문에 이렇게 죽는 것을 보는 것은 내게 더없이 무거운 광경이로다. 나보다 도움이 더 필요했던 그가 오히려 나를 도우려 했고, 불평하지 않으려 했으니, 그의 마음이 그토록 나를 돕는 데에 쏠려 있었기 때문이다.\" 그러자 베디비어 경이 자기 형제를 위해 울었다. \"이 애도와 눈물을 멈추어라.\" 왕이 말했다. \"잘 알아 두어라, 만일 내가 살아남을 수 있다 해도, 루칸 경의 죽음은 영원히 나를 슬프게 할 것이다. 그러나 나의 때가 빠르게 다가오는구나. 그러니,\" 아서가 베디비어 경에게 말했다. \"내 좋은 칼 엑스칼리버를 가지고 저기 물가로 가거라. 그리고 그곳에 이르거든 내 칼을 저 물에 던지고, 다시 돌아와 네가 그곳에서 본 것을 내게 말하라고 명하노라.\" \"전하,\" 베디비어 경이 말했다. \"분부대로 하겠나이다.\" 그리하여 베디비어 경은 떠났는데, 가는 길에 그 고귀한 칼을 보니 자루 끝과 손잡이가 온통 보석으로 되어 있었다. 그래서 그는 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다. \"이 값진 칼을 물에 던진다면 아무 좋은 일도 생기지 않고 오직 해와 손실만 있으리라.\" 그러고 나서 베디비어 경은 엑스칼리버를 어느 나무 아래 숨겼다. 그리하여 그는 되도록 빨리 다시 왕에게 돌아왔다. \"그곳에서 무엇을 보았느냐?\" 왕이 말했다. \"전하,\" 그가 말했다. \"아무것도 보지 못했나이다.\" \"아아! 네가 나를 속였구나.\" 왕이 말했다. \"지체 없이 다시 가서, 나를 사랑하거든 주저 말고 그것을 던져 넣어라.\" 그러자 베디비어 경은 다시 가서, 그것을 던지려고 칼을 손에 들었다. 그러나 다시금 그 고귀한 칼을 내버리는 것이 죄이자 수치로만 여겨졌기에, 그는 그것을 다시 숨기고 돌아와, 왕에게 분부대로 했노라고 말했다. \"그곳에서 무엇을 보았느냐?\" 왕이 말했다. \"전하,\" 그가 말했다. \"깊은 물과 창백한 물결밖에는 보지 못했나이다.\" \"아, 진실치 못한 배신자여!\" 아서 왕이 말했다. \"이제 네가 나를 두 번이나 배신했구나. 그러고도 너는 고귀한 기사로 불리며, 내게 사랑스럽고 소중한 자였다. 그러나 이제 다시 가서, 내가 명하는 대로 하라. 네가 오래 머뭇거리는 것이 내 목숨을 위태롭게 하고 있다.\" 그러자 베디비어 경은 칼이 있는 곳으로 가서, 그것을 가볍게 집어 들고 물가로 가, 손잡이에 띠를 묶고는, 칼을 물속으로 던질 수 있는 한 멀리 던졌다. 그러자 물속에서 팔과 손이 나와 그것을 받아 잡고는, 세 번 흔들어 휘두른 다음, 칼을 든 그 손이 물속으로 사라졌다.\n\n그러고 나서 베디비어 경은 다시 왕에게 돌아와, 자기가 본 것을 말했다. \"나를 여기서 데려가 다오.\" 왕이 말했다. \"내가 너무 오래 지체한 것 같아 두렵구나.\" 그러자 베디비어 경이 왕을 등에 업고 그 물가로 갔다. 그곳에 이르니, 바로 물가 가까이에 작은 거룻배 한 척이 떠 있었는데, 그 안에는 아리따운 여인들이 많이 타고 있었고, 그들 가운데에는 한 왕비가 있었다. 모두가 검은 두건을 쓰고 있었으며, 아서 왕을 보자 울며 비명을 질렀다.\n\n\"이제 나를 거룻배에 태워 다오.\" 왕이 말했다. 그러자 그곳에서 세 왕비가 큰 애도와 함께 그를 맞이했고, 그들 가운데 한 사람의 무릎에 아서 왕은 머리를 뉘었다. 그러자 그 왕비가 말했다. \"아, 사랑하는 형제여, 어찌하여 그토록 오래 지체하셨나이까? 아아! 머리에 입은 이 상처가 너무 많은 냉기를 머금었나이다.\" 그러고 나서 그들이 뭍에서 노를 저어 나아가자, 베디비어 경은 그들이 자기에게서 멀어져 가는 것을 바라보았다. 그러자 그가 외쳤다. \"아, 나의 주군 아서여, 저를 여기 적들 사이에 홀로 남겨 두려 하시나이까?\" \"스스로 위안을 삼아라.\" 왕이 말했다. \"내게는 더 이상 도움이 없으니, 나는 나의 깊은 상처를 치유하기 위해 아발론 섬으로 가려 한다.\" 그리고 베디비어 경은 거룻배가 시야에서 사라지자, 울며 통곡했다. 그러고 나서 그는 숲으로 들어가 밤새도록 걸었고, 아침에 한 예배당과 은수처를 알아보았다.\n\n그러자 베디비어 경이 그곳으로 갔다. 그가 예배당에 들어서니, 새로 새긴 무덤 곁의 땅바닥에 한 은수자가 누워 있는 것이 보였다. \"어르신,\" 베디비어 경이 말했다. \"그토록 가까이에서 기도드리는 그곳에 묻힌 자는 어떤 사람입니까?\" \"착한 아들이여,\" 은수자가 말했다. \"나도 정말 알지 못하오. 다만 이 밤에 여인들이 여럿 와서, 죽은 자 한 사람을 이리로 데려와 매장해 달라고 내게 청했소.\" \"아아!\" 베디비어 경이 말했다. \"그분이 나의 주군, 아서 왕이셨구나.\" 그러고 나서 베디비어 경은 정신을 잃었다. 깨어났을 때 그는 은수자에게, 자기가 그와 함께 머물며 단식과 기도로 살게 해 달라고 청했다. \"그대를 환영하오.\" 은수자가 말했다. 그리하여 베디비어 경은 은수자와 함께 머물렀다. 그리고 그는 초라한 옷을 걸치고, 단식과 기도 속에서 더없이 겸손하게 은수자를 섬겼다.\n\n이리하여 아서에 관해서는, 권위 있는 책들 속에서 더 이상 쓰인 바를 나는 찾지 못하며, 그의 죽음의 명확한 진상에 관해서도 더는 알 수 없다. 그러나 이렇게 그는 배에 실려 떠나갔으니, 그 배 안에는 세 왕비가 있었다. 한 사람은 아서 왕의 누이인 모르가나 르 페이 왕비요, 다른 한 사람은 호수의 여인 비비안이요, 셋째는 노스 갈리스의 왕비였다. 그리고 이 이야기는 원탁의 기사 베디비어 경이 글로 쓰이게 한 것이다.\n\n그러나 어떤 이들은 아서 왕이 죽지 않고 다른 곳에 숨겨졌으며, 그가 다시 돌아와 잉글랜드를 다스리리라고 말한다. 그러나 많은 이들은 그의 무덤에 이 시구가 쓰여 있다고 말한다.\n\n\"히크 야케트 아르투루스, 렉스 콴담, 렉스퀘 푸투루스.\" 여기 아서가 누워 있도다, 한때의 왕이요 장차 올 왕이여.\n\n그리고 귀네비어 왕비는 아서 왕이 그와 함께한 모든 고귀한 기사들과 더불어 죽었음을 알게 되자, 다섯 여인을 데리고 몰래 빠져나갔다. 그리하여 그녀는 앰즈베리로 가서 수녀가 되었고, 희고 검은 옷을 입었으며, 죄지은 여인이 일찍이 했던 그 어떤 고행보다 큰 고행을 행하고, 단식과 기도와 자선 속에서 살았다. 그리고 그곳에서 그녀는 수녀들의 수녀원장이자 지도자가 되었다.\n\n\"그리고 그녀는 앰즈베리에 이르러 그곳 수녀들에게 말하기를, '내 적들이 나를 쫓는구나. 그러나, 오, 평화로운 자매들이여, 나를 받아들여 성역을 베풀어 다오, 그리고 그것을 베푸는 그 여인의 이름은, 그녀가 그대들에게 말할 때가 올 때까지 묻지 말아 다오.' 그리고 그녀의 아름다움과 우아함과 위엄이 그들에게 마법처럼 작용하니, 그들은 그것을 묻기를 삼갔다.\" — 「기네비어」.\n\n이제 우리는 그녀에게서 눈을 돌려, 호수의 란슬롯 경에 관해 이야기하자.\n\n란슬롯 경은 자기 나라에서, 모드레드 경이 잉글랜드의 왕으로 즉위해 자기 외숙부인 아서 왕에게 전쟁을 일으켰다는 소식을 들었을 때, 한없는 분노에 사로잡혀 자기 친족들에게 말했다. \"아, 저 이중의 배신자 모드레드 경이여! 이제 나는 그가 내 손아귀를 빠져나간 것이 후회스럽구나.\" 그러고 나서 란슬롯 경과 그의 동료들은 잉글랜드로 건너가기 위해 배와 갤리선을 갖추어 온갖 서두름으로 채비를 했다. 그리하여 그는 바다를 건너 도버에 이르렀고, 그곳에서 대군을 이끌고 상륙했다. 그때 란슬롯 경은 아서 왕이 죽었다는 말을 들었다. \"아아!\" 란슬롯 경이 말했다. \"이것은 내게 일찍이 닥친 가장 무거운 소식이로다.\" 그러고 나서 그는 왕들과 공작들과 영주들과 기사들을 불러 모아 이렇게 말했다. \"나의 아리따운 영주들이여, 나와 함께 이 나라로 와 준 그대들 모두에게 감사하오. 그러나 우리는 너무 늦게 왔고, 그것이 내가 사는 동안 나를 후회하게 할 것이오. 그러나 일이 이렇게 되었으니,\" 란슬롯 경이 말했다. \"나는 직접 말을 달려 나의 여인, 귀네비어 왕비를 찾으려 하오. 그녀가 서쪽으로 달아났다는 말을 들었기 때문이오. 그러니 그대들은 이곳에서 보름 동안 나를 기다리되, 만일 그 안에 내가 돌아오지 않거든, 그대들의 배와 군대를 이끌고 그대들의 나라로 떠나시오.\"\n\n그리하여 란슬롯 경은 떠나 서쪽으로 말을 달려, 그곳에서 여러 날 동안 찾아 헤맸다. 그리고 마침내 그는 한 수녀원에 이르렀으니, 회랑을 거니는 그를 귀네비어 왕비가 보았다. 그녀는 그를 보자 정신을 잃고 쓰러졌다. 그리고 말을 할 수 있게 되자 그를 자기에게로 불러오라고 명했다. 란슬롯 경이 그녀에게로 인도되자 그녀가 말했다. \"란슬롯 경, 일찍이 우리 사이에 있었던 그 모든 사랑을 걸고, 그대에게 요구하고 간청하노니, 그대는 다시는 나를 보지 말고, 그대의 왕국으로 돌아가 아내를 맞이하여, 기쁨과 축복 속에서 그녀와 함께 사시오. 그리고 나를 위해 나의 주님께 기도하여, 내가 내 영혼의 구원을 얻게 해 주시오.\" \"아니오, 부인,\" 란슬롯 경이 말했다. \"잘 아시오, 나는 결코 그리하지 않을 것이오. 그대가 택한 바로 그 운명을 나 또한 택하여, 하느님을 기쁘게 하고 섬기겠소.\" 그리하여 그들은 눈물과 큰 비탄 속에서 헤어졌다. 그리고 여인들이 왕비를 그녀의 방으로 데려갔고, 란슬롯 경은 자기 말에 올라 울며 떠나갔다.\n\n그리고 마침내 란슬롯 경은 한 은수처와 예배당을 알아보았고, 그때 미사를 알리는 작은 종이 울리는 소리를 들었다. 그래서 그는 그곳으로 말을 달려가 내려서, 자기 말을 문에 매어 두고 미사를 들었다. 그리고 미사를 노래하던 자는 바로 베디비어 경이 거처를 정했던 그 은수자였다. 그리고 베디비어 경은 란슬롯 경을 알아보았고, 그들은 미사가 끝난 뒤 함께 이야기를 나누었다. 그런데 베디비어 경이 자기 이야기를 들려주자, 란슬롯 경의 심장은 슬픔으로 거의 터질 듯했다. 그러고 나서 그는 무릎을 꿇고, 은수자에게 자기 죄를 사하여 달라고 기도하며, 자기가 그의 형제가 되게 해 달라고 간청했다. 그러자 은수자가 말했다. \"기꺼이 그리하겠소.\" 그러고 나서 그는 란슬롯 경에게 수도복을 입혔고, 그곳에서 란슬롯 경은 기도와 단식으로 밤낮없이 하느님을 섬겼다.\n\n그리고 대군은 란슬롯 경이 정한 보름이 끝날 때까지 도버에 머물렀고, 그러고 나서 보호트 경이 그들을 다시 자기들의 나라로 돌아가게 했다. 그리고 보호트 경, 마리스의 헥토르 경, 블라모르 경, 그 밖의 많은 이들은 란슬롯 경을 찾기 위해 잉글랜드 전역을 말 달려 찾아 나서기로 했다. 그리하여 보호트 경은 운명에 이끌려 말을 달리다, 란슬롯 경이 있던 바로 그 예배당에 이르렀다. 그리고 그는 란슬롯 경이 그런 차림의 옷을 입고 있는 것을 보고, 은수자에게 자기도 그와 똑같이 되게 해 달라고 기도했다. 그리하여 그에게도 수도복이 입혀졌고, 그곳에서 그는 기도와 단식 속에서 살았다. 그리고 반년 안에 그들의 동료인 다른 기사들이 와서, 란슬롯 경과 보호트 경이 입은 것과 같은 수도복을 걸쳤다. 이리하여 그들은 육 년 동안 큰 고행 속에서 견뎌 냈다.\n\n그리고 어느 밤 란슬롯 경에게 환영이 찾아와, 그에게 앰즈베리로 서둘러 가라고 명하며 이렇게 일렀다. \"네가 그곳에 이를 즈음, 너는 귀네비어 왕비가 죽어 있는 것을 보게 되리라.\" 그러자 란슬롯 경은 일찍 일어나 그것을 은수자에게 알렸다. 그러자 은수자가 말했다. \"이 환영을 거역하지 않는 것이 좋겠소.\" 그리하여 란슬롯 경은 일곱 동료를 데리고, 글래스턴베리에서 앰즈베리까지 걸어서 갔으니, 그 거리는 삼십 마일이 넘었다. 그리고 그들이 앰즈베리에 이르렀을 때, 귀네비어 왕비가 불과 반 시간 전에 죽었음을 알게 되었다. 그때 란슬롯 경은 그녀의 얼굴을 보았으나, 크게 울지는 않고 한숨을 지었다. 그리하여 그는 밤에 드리는 위령 기도와 아침에 노래하는 미사까지, 장례의 모든 의식을 직접 행했다. 그리고 말이 끄는 영구가 마련되었고, 란슬롯 경과 그의 동료들은 앰즈베리에서 글래스턴베리에 이르기까지 걸어서 영구를 뒤따랐다. 그리고 그녀는 밀랍 입힌 천에 싸여 대리석 관에 안치되었다. 그리고 그녀가 땅속에 묻히자 란슬롯 경은 정신을 잃고, 죽은 자처럼 오래 누워 있었다.\n\n그리고 란슬롯 경은 그 후로 음식을 거의 먹지도 마시지도 않고, 줄곧 애도하기만 했다. 그리고 육 주 안에 란슬롯 경은 병이 들었다. 그래서 그는 은수자와 자기의 모든 진실한 동료들을 불러 말했다. \"은수자 어르신, 그리스도교인이라면 마땅히 받아야 할 모든 예식을 제게 베풀어 주시기를 청합니다.\" \"그럴 필요 없소.\" 은수자와 그의 모든 동료들이 말했다. \"이것은 단지 그대 피의 무거움일 뿐이니, 내일 아침이면 그대는 나을 것이오.\" \"나의 아리따운 영주들이여,\" 란슬롯 경이 말했다. \"내 근심에 찬 몸은 땅속으로 들어가려 하오. 나는 지금 말하려는 것 이상의 경고를 받았소. 그러니 내게 예식을 베풀어 주시오.\" 그리하여 그가 성체를 받고 종부 성사를 받아, 그리스도교인이 마땅히 받아야 할 모든 것을 받고 나자, 그는 은수자에게 자기 동료들이 자기 시신을 조이어스 가드로 운구하게 해 달라고 기도했다. (어떤 이들은 그곳이 앤윅이라 말하고, 어떤 이들은 뱀버러라고 말한다.) \"내게 몹시 후회스러운 일이오.\" 란슬롯 경이 말했다. \"그러나 나는 일찍이, 조이어스 가드에 묻히겠노라고 서원했소.\" 그러자 그의 동료들 사이에서 울음과 손을 쥐어짜는 비탄이 일었다. 그리고 그날 밤 란슬롯 경은 죽었다. 그리고 이튿날 아침 보호트 경과 그의 동료들이 그의 침상에 이르러 보니, 그가 완전히 죽어 있는 것을 발견했다. 그리고 그는 마치 미소 짓고 있는 듯이 누워 있었고, 그의 주위에는 일찍이 그들이 알았던 그 어떤 향기보다 가장 감미로운 향기가 감돌았다.\n\n그리고 그들은 란슬롯 경을, 귀네비어 왕비가 안치되었던 바로 그 말이 끄는 영구에 뉘었고, 은수자와 그들 모두가 시신과 함께 가서 조이어스 가드에 이르렀다. 그리고 그곳에서 그들은 그의 유해를 성가대석 한가운데에 안치하고, 그를 위해 많은 시편과 기도문을 노래하고 읽었다. 그리고 그의 얼굴은 줄곧 드러난 채 가리지 않았으니, 모든 사람이 그를 바라볼 수 있게 하기 위함이었다. 그리고 바로 이렇게, 그들이 예배를 드리고 있을 때, 칠 년 동안 잉글랜드와 스코틀랜드와 웨일스 전역에서 자기 형제 란슬롯 경을 찾아 헤맸던 마리스의 헥토르 경이 왔다. 그리고 헥토르 경은 조이어스 가드의 예배당에서 그런 소리가 나는 것을 듣고, 말에서 내려 성가대석으로 들어왔다. 그리고 그들 모두 헥토르 경을 알아보았다. 그때 보호트 경이 가서, 그의 형제 란슬롯 경이 그곳에 죽어 누워 있다고 그에게 일렀다. 그러자 헥토르 경은 자기 방패와 칼과 투구를 내던졌다. 그리고 그는 란슬롯 경의 얼굴을 보고서, 그 어떤 혀로도 그가 자기 형제를 위해 토해 낸 비통한 탄식을 다 전하기 어려울 정도였다. \"아, 란슬롯 경이여!\" 그가 말했다. \"그대가 거기 누워 있구려. 그리고 이제 나는 감히 말하노니, 그대는 일찍이 그 어떤 지상의 기사의 손으로도 맞상대 된 적이 없었소. 그리고 그대는 일찍이 방패를 든 가장 예의 바른 기사였소. 그리고 그대는 일찍이 말에 올라탄 가장 진실한 벗이었소. 그리고 그대는 일찍이 죄 많은 사람으로서 여인을 사랑한 가장 진실한 연인이었소. 그리고 그대는 일찍이 칼을 휘두른 가장 친절한 사람이었소. 그리고 그대는 일찍이 기사들의 무리 가운데 끼었던 가장 훌륭한 사람이었소. 그리고 그대는 일찍이 여인들 사이에서 홀에서 식사한 가장 온순하고 가장 정중한 사람이었소. 그리고 그대는 일찍이 창을 창받침에 걸친, 자기 철천지원수에게 가장 준엄한 기사였소.\" 그러자 그곳에서 한없는 울음과 비탄이 일었다. 이리하여 그들은 란슬롯 경의 유해를 보름 동안 지켰고, 그러고 나서 큰 경건함으로 그를 매장했다.\n\n그러고 나서 그들은 은수자와 함께 그의 은수처로 돌아갔다. 그리고 베디비어 경은 그곳에서 생애가 끝날 때까지 줄곧 은수자로 머물렀다. 그리고 보호트 경, 헥토르 경, 블라모르 경, 블레오베리스 경은 성지로 갔다. 그리고 이 네 기사는 이교도인 튀르크인들을 상대로 많은 전투를 벌였다. 그리고 그곳에서 그들은, 하느님의 뜻에 따라, 어느 성금요일에 죽었다.\n\n이리하여 「라 모르트 다르튀르」라는 제목의 이 고귀하고 즐거운 책이 끝난다. 그럼에도 이 책은 앞서 말한 아서 왕과 그의 고귀한 원탁의 기사들의 탄생과 생애와 행적, 그들의 경이로운 탐색과 모험, 성배의 성취, 그리고 마침내 라 모르트 다르튀르, 곧 그들 모두의 비통한 죽음과 이 세상을 떠남을 다룬다. 이 책은 기사 토머스 맬러리 경에 의해 영어로 옮겨져 스물한 권으로 나뉘었고, 장으로 구분되어 인쇄되었으며, 우리 주님의 해 1485년 7월의 마지막 날에 웨스트민스터 수도원에서 완성되었다.\n캑스턴이 나를 만들게 하였노라.\n\n마비노게온\n서론적 주석\n\n유럽의 문인들과 고고학자들에게 잘 알려져 있는 바, 큰 공공 도서관들에는 한때 인기를 누렸으나 인쇄술의 발명과 더불어 이미 시대에 뒤떨어져 방치되고 만 로맨스와 이야기들의 방대한 필사본이 존재한다. 그리하여 그것들은 결코 인쇄되지 못했고, 학자들조차 좀처럼 정독하지 않았다. 그러다 반세기쯤 전에 이르러 다시 관심이 그것들로 향했고, 옛 풍습과 관습과 사고방식의 매우 흥미로운 기념물임이 밝혀졌다. 그 후로 여럿이 편집되었으니, 더러는 월터 스콧 경이나 시인 사우디 같은 개인에 의해, 또 더러는 고고학회에 의해 이루어졌다. 그러한 출판물을 기대할 수 있는 독자층이 어찌나 적었던지, 편집자와 출판인이 그것들을 세상에 내놓도록 유혹할 만한 이익의 동기를 찾을 수 없었다. 그리하여 인쇄된 것은 단지 소수, 그것도 가장 접근하기 쉬운 것들뿐이었다. 이런 종류의 필사본 가운데에는, 흥미롭고도 가치 있다고 알려진, 아니 차라리 그렇게 짐작된 한 부류가 있었으나, 그것이 제대로 인쇄된 영어로 나오는 것을 기대하기란 거의 가망 없어 보였다. 그것은 「마비노게온」이라 불리는 웨일스의 대중 이야기들로, 이는 복수형 단어이며, 단수형은 「마비노기」, 곧 한 편의 이야기이다. 이것들의 필사본은 옥스퍼드의 보들리 도서관을 비롯한 여러 곳에 소장되어 있었으나, 어려움은 번역자와 편집자를 찾는 데에 있었다. 웨일스어는 웨일스의 소작농들 사이에서 쓰이는 구어이지만, 학자들이 그 공국 출신이 아닌 한 전적으로 등한시한다. 소수의 웨일스어 학자들 가운데에서도, 이 분야의 학문에 충분한 관심을 가지고 이 작품들을 영어권 대중에게 내놓으려는 자는 찾을 수 없었다. 사우디와 스콧, 그리고 그들처럼 자기 나라의 옛 낭만적 전설을 사랑했던 다른 이들은, 「마비노게온」을 다시 펴내야 할 의무를 웨일스 문인들에게 종종 역설했다. 사우디는 자신이 펴낸 「모르트 다르튀르」의 서문에서 이렇게 말한다. \"내가 본 표본들은 지극히 흥미롭다. 그리고 영국 문학에서, 이 이야기들을 직역본과 함께, 그리고 모든 이들 가운데 데이비스 씨가 가장 잘 해낼 수 있는 그런 주석과 함께 펴낸 판본보다 더 절실히 요청되는 것은 없다. 원탁의 허구 가운데 많은 것이 웨일스나 브르타뉴에서 비롯되었으며, 아마도 지금도 그곳에서 그 흔적을 찾을 수 있으리라는 것은 분명하다.\"\n\n또한 1819년에 쓴, 찰스 W. W. 윈 경에게 보낸 편지에서 그는 이렇게 말한다.\n\n\"나는 「마비노게온」을 더 보게 되리라는 희망을 거의 단념하기 시작했소. 그러나 만일 어느 유능한 웨일스인을 찾아내어, 되도록 직역에 가까운 번역과 함께 그것을 정성껏 편집하게 한다면, 예약 구독을 통해 그의 수고를 보람 있게 할 수 있으리라 확신하오. 소량의 판본을 높은 가격에, 이를테면 이백 부를 각 오 기니에 인쇄하는 식으로 말이오. 나 자신도 그대의 진정한 산문과 운문 유산 전체를 그런 식으로 펴낸 판본이라면, 그 권당 가격에 기꺼이 예약하겠소. 그런 선집이 만들어지기 전까지는, '웨일스의 신사들'은 부추를 다는 것이 금지되어야 마땅하오. 그렇소, 그리고 구운 치즈를 먹는 것 또한 금지되어야 하오. 그대들의 음유시인들이 스코틀랜드인이었다면 더 나은 대접을 받았을 텐데 말이오.\"\n\n섀런 터너와 월터 스콧 경 또한 웨일스 필사본의 출판에 대해 비슷한 소망을 표했다. 전자는 웨일스인인 오언이라는 사람을 통해 그것을 실현하려는 시도에 참여했으나, 사우디가 그에 관해 한 말로 판단하건대, 그는 영어에 미숙했던 것 같다. 사우디의 말은 이러하다. \"윌리엄 오언이 「마비노게온」의 세 부분을 내게 빌려주었는데, 어찌나 웨일스풍의 어법과 구문으로 흐뭇하게 번역되었던지, 그런 번역은 원작만큼이나 유익하다.\" 또 다른 편지에서 그는 덧붙인다. \"섀런이 그의 언어를 문법에 맞게 다듬되, 그들의 어법은 털끝만큼도 바꾸지 않게 하시오.\"\n\n오언 씨는 아마도 그런 식으로 수행된 사업에서 멀리 나아가지 못했을 텐데, 그렇게 해서는 대중의 후원을 거의 기대할 수 없었기 때문이다. 두 언어에 대한 필요한 지식을 갖추고, 그 과업에 충분한 열정을 지녔으며, 서적상과 독서 대중으로부터 독립적일 만큼 충분한 재력을 가진 인물이 나타나기 전까지는, 그런 작업을 확신을 가지고 기대할 수 없었다. 그러한 인물이, 사우디와 스콧의 시대 이후에, 샬럿 게스트 부인이라는 사람으로 나타났다. 웨일스에 재산을 가진 신사와 결혼한 잉글랜드 여인인 그녀는, 그 공국의 언어를 습득하고 그 문학적 보물에 열렬히 빠져들어, 인쇄공과 판각공의 기예가 최선을 다해 꾸민 차림새로 그것들을 영어권 독자에게 내놓았다. 웨일스어 원문과 번역, 그리고 프랑스, 독일을 비롯한 동시대 및 관련 문학에서 가져온 풍부한 삽화를 담은 사절판 네 권으로, 「마비노게온」이 우리 앞에 펼쳐져 있다. 고고학자와 언어 및 민족학 연구자에게는 더없이 귀중한 보물이지만, 그런 형태로는 좀처럼 대중의 친숙함을 얻기 어려울 것이다. 우리는 그것을 우리 독자들에게 알리고, 그 세부를 간추리며, 가장 매력적인 부분을 가려 뽑고, 게스트 부인이 자신의 전설들에 입힌 문체를 시종 충실히 보존했다는 것 외에는 다른 어떤 공로도 주장하지 않는다. 이 봉사에 대해, 우리는 우리 독자들이 우리가 그들에게 결코 가볍지 않은 은혜를 베풀었음을 인정해 주기를 바란다.\n\n————\n\n# THE BRITONS\n\n브리튼의 가장 이른 시기 거주민들은 역사상 켈트족이라는 명칭으로 알려진 저 위대한 종족의 한 갈래였던 것으로 추정된다. 웨일스를 흔히 가리키는 이름인 캄브리아[Cambria]는 킴리[Cymri]에서 유래한 것으로 여겨지는데, 이는 인접한 대륙에서 이 섬으로 들어온 이주민들을 웨일스의 전승이 일컫는 이름이다. 이 이름은 키메리오이[Cimmerians]나 킴브리[Cimbri]와 동일한 것으로 간주되는데, 그리스와 로마의 역사가들은 이 명칭으로 에욱세이노스[흑해] 북쪽에서 북서 유럽 전역으로 퍼져 나간 어느 야만 종족을 서술하였다.\n\n웨일스[Wales]와 웰시[Welsh]라는 이름의 기원에 관해서는 많은 논의가 있었다. 어떤 저술가들은 이를 게일[Gael]이나 갈[Gaul]에서 파생된 것으로 보는데, 이 이름들은 \"숲에 사는 사람들\"을 뜻한다고 한다. 또 다른 이들은 북방의 언어에서 웰시[Walsh]가 이방인을 뜻하며, 후대에 이 섬을 침략하여 그 대부분을 차지한 색슨족과 앵글족이 원주민인 브리튼인들을 그렇게 불렀다는 점에 주목한다.\n\n로마인들은 율리우스 카이사르의 침공으로부터 그들이 자발적으로 이 섬에서 철수한 기원후 420년까지, 다시 말해 약 오백 년 동안 브리튼을 지배하였다. 그 기간에 그들의 기예와 제도가 원주민들 사이에 널리 퍼졌음이 분명하다. 도로와 도시와 요새의 유적은 그들이 이 나라를 발전시키고 개량하는 데 많은 일을 했음을 보여 주며, 그들의 별장과 성채의 유적은 정착민 가운데 다수가 부와 더불어 장식 예술에 대한 안목을 지녔음을 입증한다. 그러나 로마의 통치는 주로 무력으로 유지되었으며, 섬 전체에 미친 적은 없었다. 지금의 스코틀랜드에 해당하는 북부 지역은 독립을 유지하였고, 웨일스와 콘월을 이루는 서부 지역은 명목상으로만 복속되었을 뿐이다.\n\n후대의 침략 무리들 역시 이 섬의 외진 지역들을 정복하는 데 성공하지 못했다. 헹기스트와 호르사의 인솔 아래 색슨족이 도래한 기원후 449년 이후로도 여러 세대에 걸쳐 브리튼의 서쪽 해안 전역은 원주민들이 차지하고 있었고, 그들은 침략자들과 끊임없는 전쟁을 벌였다.\n\n그리하여 웨일스와 콘월 사람들이 즐겨 내세운 자랑은, 원래의 브리튼 혈통이 잡스러움 없는 순수함을 지닌 채 번성하고 있는 곳은 오직 자기들뿐이라는 것이었다. 우리는 이러한 관념이 이따금 시 속에서 번뜩이는 것을 보게 되는데, 가령 그레이가 「음유시인」에서 웨일스 계통인 튜더 가문 출신의 엘리자베스 여왕을 예언적으로 묘사하며 이렇게 말한 대목이 그러하다.\n\n\"그분의 눈은 브리튼 혈통임을 선포하노라.\"\n\n그리고 튜더 가문의 군주들을 노르만 혈통의 군주들과 대비하면서 그는 이렇게 외친다.\n\n\"만세, 그대 참된 왕들이여, 브리타니아의 후예들이여, 만세!\"\n\n# THE WELSH LANGUAGE AND LITERATURE\n\n웨일스어는 유럽에서 가장 오래된 언어 가운데 하나다. 그 기원이 6세기로 거슬러 올라갈 개연성이 있는 시들을 지니고 있다. 이 가운데 일부는 언어가 너무도 옛스러워 최고의 학자들조차 많은 구절의 해석을 두고 의견이 엇갈린다. 그러나 대체로 말하자면, 웨일스인들이 보유한 시가 가운데 서기 1000년 이후의 것들은 현대어를 아는 이들에게는 이해할 만하다.\n\n지난 반세기에 이르기까지 이 작품들은 여러 대학이나 개인의 서고에 묻힌 채로 남아 있었고, 그 접근이 너무도 어려워 이를 세상에 내놓으려는 성공적인 시도가 이루어지지 못했다. 웨일스 신사 계층의 애국심에 호소했으나 실효가 없자, 런던의 모피상인 오언 존스가 이 오명을 씻어 내었으니, 그는 자비를 들여 웨일스 문학의 주요 산물을 수집하여 「웨일스 미비리언 고고학」이라는 제목으로 출간하였다. 이 작업에서 그는 오언 박사를 비롯한 여러 웨일스 학자들의 도움을 받았다.\n\n존스의 노력이 멈춘 뒤로 옛날의 무관심이 되돌아왔고, 이는 몇 해 전까지 이어졌다. 오언 박사는 웨일스의 산문 이야기 모음인 「마비노게온」의 출간을 위한 후원을 얻고자 애썼으나, 그 뜻을 이루지 못한 채 세상을 떠났다. 그 일은 그 후 샬럿 게스트 부인에 의해 실행에 옮겨졌다. 이 책의 나머지 부분을 채우는 전설들은 바로 이 저작에서 가져온 것이며, 이에 대해서는 이미 제1부의 서론에 해당하는 장에서 보다 상세히 이야기한 바 있다.\n\n# THE WELSH BARDS\n\n가장 오래된 웨일스 시가 돌려지는 작자들로는 기원후 500년에서 550년 사이에 살았던 것으로 추정되는 아네우린, 그리고 그보다 몇 해 뒤의 사람들인 탈리에신, 흘리와르크 헨[흘리와르크 노옹], 그리고 미르딘 또는 멀린이 있다. 이들의 이름이 붙은 시들의 진위는 공격을 받아 왔으며, 그 가운데 얼마나 많은 것이, 또 어느 것이 진정한 것인지는 여전히 미결의 문제다. 다만 그중 일부가 진정한 것임은 거의 의심할 여지가 없다. \"고도딘\"이라는 제목이 붙은 아네우린의 시는 진정성의 매우 강한 흔적을 지니고 있다. 아네우린은 스트래스클라이드의 북부 브리튼인 가운데 하나였는데, 이들은 자신들이 살던 지역의 한 부분에 컴벌랜드, 곧 킴리의 땅이라는 이름을 남겼다. 이 시에서 그는 자기 동포들이 전투에 임하기 앞서 벌꿀술을 지나치게 흥청망청 마신 탓에 카트라이스 전투에서 색슨족에게 패배한 것을 애도한다. 음유시인 자신과 그의 동료 전사 둘만이 그 들판에서 탈출하였다. 이 시의 일부를 그레이가 번역하였으며, 다음은 그 발췌이다.\n\n\"카트라이스의 골짜기로, 번쩍이는 행렬을 이루어, 네 곱절 백의 전사들이 나아가노라. 전사들의 사내다운 목마다 제왕의 영예를 나타내는 사슬이 둘려 있고, 수많은 황금 고리로 엮여 있도다. 황금 잔에서 그들은 마시노니, 꿀벌이 빚어낸 감로(甘露)요, 포도의 드높은 즙이로다. 환희와 희망에 달아올라 불타오르나, 카트라이스의 골짜기로 돌아온 자 아무도 없네, 용맹한 아에론과 강인한 코난, 피투성이 무리를 헤치고 나온 그들과, 그리고 그들 가운데 가장 보잘것없는 나, 살아남아 그들의 몰락을 슬퍼하며 노래하는 나뿐.\"\n\n탈리에신의 작품들은 진정성이 훨씬 더 의심스럽다. 탈리에신의 모험에 관한 이야기 하나가 전하는데, 신화적 특징이 너무도 짙어 그에게 돌려진 글들에 의혹을 던진다. 이 이야기는 뒤따르는 쪽들에서 보게 될 것이다.\n\n# THE TRIADS\n\n세 짝 시[Triad]는 독특한 종류의 시 형식으로, 웨일스의 음유시인들이 그 무수한 본보기를 남겼다. 이는 세 사람, 또는 세 사건, 또는 세 가지 견해를 짤막한 한 문장 안에 엮어 늘어놓은 것이다. 십중팔구 본디 기억을 돕기 위해 고안된 이 작법은, 처음 보아서는 그러한 가능성이 거의 엿보이지 않는 우아함의 경지로까지 웨일스인들에 의해 끌어올려졌다. 세 짝 시는 온갖 시대에 걸쳐 있으며, 그중 일부는 아마도 이 언어 안의 그 무엇 못지않게 오래되었을 것이다. 개별로는 짧지만, 「미비리언 고고학」에 실린 모음집은 이단 편집으로 백칠십 쪽이 넘게 차지한다. 우리는 인물을 다룬 세 짝 시부터 시작하여, 아서 왕 자신이 지은 것을 첫머리에 두고 몇 가지 본보기를 들어 보겠다.\n\n\"전투에서 내게는 세 영웅이 있노라. 키다리 마엘과, 자기 군대를 거느린 흘리르, 그리고 웨일스의 기둥 카라도크가 그들이라.\"\n\n\"브리튼 섬의 으뜸가는 세 음유시인은, 멀린 앰브로즈, 멀린 더 와일드라고도 불리는 모르핀의 아들 멀린, 그리고 음유시인들의 우두머리 탈리에신이라.\"\n\n\"아서의 궁정에 있는 황금 혀를 지닌 세 기사는, 과이아르의 아들 가웨인, 트리핀의 아들 드리드바스, 그리고 마다그 압 우테르의 아들 엘리울로드라.\"\n\n\"브리튼 섬의 명예로운 세 잔치는, 이 섬에서 율리우스 카이사르를 물리친 뒤 베푼 카스왈라운의 잔치, 색슨족을 정복한 뒤 베푼 아우렐리우스 암브로시우스의 잔치, 그리고 우스크 강가 칼리온에서 베푼 아서 왕의 잔치라.\"\n\n\"거인 라오데간의 딸 귀네비어는, 어릴 적엔 못됐고, 자라서는 더 못됐도다.\"\n\n다음으로는 교훈을 담은 세 짝 시 몇 가지가 이어진다.\n\n\"그대는 드렘히디드가 노래한 바를 들었는가, 성벽 위의 옛 파수꾼이 부른 노래를? 거절이 지키지 못할 약속보다 나으니라.\"\n\n\"그대는 흘렌레아우그가 노래한 바를 들었는가, 황금 목걸이를 두른 고귀한 우두머리가 부른 노래를? 무덤이 궁핍한 삶보다 나으니라.\"\n\n\"그대는 가르셀리트가 노래한 바를 들었는가, 따르기에 안전한 아일랜드 사람이 부른 노래를? 죄는 오래 좇으면 나쁜 것이니라.\"\n\n\"그대는 아바온이 노래한 바를 들었는가, 기록하는 시구를 짓는 탈리에신의 아들이 부른 노래를? 뺨은 마음의 고뇌를 감추지 못하느니라.\"\n\n\"그대는 흘리와르크가 노래한 바를 들었는가, 담대하고 용맹한 그 노인이 부른 노래를? 알지 못하는 사이라 할지라도 다정히 맞이하라.\"\n\n————\n\n# KYNON’S ADVENTURE\n\n아서 왕은 우스크 강가의 카얼리언에 머물고 있었다. 어느 날 그는 자기 방에 앉아 있었는데, 그 곁에는 우리엔의 아들 오와인과 클리드노의 아들 키논, 그리고 키너의 아들 케이가 함께 있었고, 귀네비어와 그녀의 시녀들은 창가에서 바느질을 하고 있었다. 방 한가운데에는 아서 왕이 푸른 골풀로 만든 자리에 앉아 있었는데, 그 위에는 불꽃무늬가 새겨진 공단 덮개가 펼쳐져 있었고, 그의 팔꿈치 아래에는 붉은 공단으로 만든 방석이 놓여 있었다.\n\n그때 아서가 입을 열었다. \"그대들이 나를 탓하지 않으리라 여겨진다면,\" 그가 말했다, \"나는 식사를 기다리는 동안 잠을 좀 자야겠소. 그동안 그대들은 서로 이야기를 들려주며 즐겨도 좋고, 케이에게서 벌꿀술 한 병과 고기 약간을 얻어도 좋소.\" 그러고는 왕이 잠이 들었다. 클리드노의 아들 키논이 케이에게 아서가 약속한 것을 달라고 청했다. \"나도 그가 내게 약속한 좋은 이야기를 들어야겠소.\" 케이가 말했다. \"아니지,\" 키논이 대답했다. \"먼저 아서의 명을 그대가 이행하는 것이 더 옳을 것이오. 그러고 나면 우리가 아는 가장 좋은 이야기를 그대에게 들려주겠소.\" 그리하여 케이는 주방과 벌꿀술 저장고로 가서, 벌꿀술 한 병과 황금 술잔, 그리고 구운 고기 조각을 꿴 꼬챙이 한 줌을 들고 돌아왔다. 그들은 고기 조각을 먹으며 벌꿀술을 마시기 시작했다. \"자, 이제,\" 케이가 말했다, \"그대들이 내게 이야기를 들려줄 차례요.\" \"키논,\" 오와인이 말했다, \"케이에게 마땅히 줄 이야기를 들려주게나.\" \"그렇게 하겠소.\" 키논이 대답했다.\n\n\"나는 아버지와 어머니의 외아들이었고, 야심이 대단했으며, 담대함 또한 매우 컸소. 나는 이 세상에 내가 감당하지 못할 만큼 거대한 모험은 없다고 여겼소. 그래서 내 고장에 있는 온갖 모험을 모두 이루고 나자, 나는 채비를 갖추고 사막과 머나먼 고장들을 두루 여행하러 길을 떠났소. 그리고 마침내 나는 세상에서 가장 아름다운 골짜기에 다다랐는데, 그곳에는 한결같이 고르게 자란 나무들이 있었고, 강 하나가 골짜기를 가로질러 흘렀으며, 강가에는 오솔길이 나 있었소. 나는 정오까지 그 길을 따라갔고, 저녁이 될 때까지 골짜기의 남은 부분을 따라 여행을 이어 갔소. 그러다 들판 끝에 이르러, 나는 크고 빛나는 성에 다다랐는데, 그 발치에는 급류가 흐르고 있었소. 내가 그 성으로 다가가 보니, 곱슬곱슬한 노란 머리칼을 한 두 젊은이가 보였는데, 저마다 머리에는 금테를 둘렀고, 노란 공단으로 지은 옷을 입고 있었소. 그들의 발등에는 금 죔쇠가 채워져 있었소. 그 두 사람의 손에는 각각 상아로 만든 활이 들려 있었는데, 수사슴의 힘줄로 시위를 매었고, 그들의 화살과 화살대는 고래 뼈로 만들어 공작 깃털을 단 것이었소. 화살대에는 또한 황금 화살촉이 달려 있었소. 그리고 그들에게는 칼날이 금으로 되고 자루가 고래 뼈로 된 단검이 있었소. 그들은 과녁을 향해 활을 쏘고 있었소.\n\n\"그들에게서 조금 떨어진 곳에 나는 한창때의 사내를 보았는데, 갓 깎은 수염을 하고, 노란 공단으로 지은 겉옷과 망토를 걸쳤으며, 망토 윗자락 둘레에는 금실 레이스 띠가 둘려 있었소. 그의 발에는 알록달록한 가죽으로 만든 신발이 신겨져 있었는데, 두 개의 금장식으로 여며져 있었소. 내가 그를 보고 그에게로 다가가 인사를 건네자, 그의 정중함이 어찌나 대단한지 그는 내 인사를 받자마자 답례를 했소. 그리고 그는 나와 함께 성을 향해 걸어갔소. 그런데 그 성에는 한 홀 안에 있는 자들 말고는 거처하는 이가 아무도 없었소. 그곳에서 나는 창가에서 공단에 수를 놓고 있는 스물네 명의 처녀를 보았소. 그리고 그대에게 이르거니와, 케이, 그들 가운데 가장 덜 아름다운 이조차 그대가 브리튼 섬에서 일찍이 본 그 어떤 가장 아름다운 처녀보다 더 아름다웠고, 그들 가운데 가장 덜 사랑스러운 이조차 부활절 잔치 때 가장 사랑스러운 모습을 한 아서의 아내 귀네비어보다 더 사랑스러웠소. 내가 들어서자 그들은 일어섰고, 그중 여섯은 내 말을 받아 갔으며, 내 갑옷을 벗겨 주었고, 또 다른 여섯은 내 무기를 받아 그릇에 담아 완전히 반짝일 때까지 씻었소. 그리고 세 번째 여섯은 식탁에 천을 펼치고 고기를 마련했소. 네 번째 여섯은 내 더러워진 옷을 벗기고 다른 옷을 입혀 주었는데, 곧 고운 아마포로 지은 속옷과 더블릿, 그리고 겉옷과 겉옷 위에 걸치는 옷, 그리고 노란 공단 망토로, 그 망토에는 넓은 금띠가 둘려 있었소. 그들은 내 아래와 둘레에 붉은 아마포 덮개를 씌운 방석들을 놓아 주었고, 나는 자리에 앉았소. 한편 내 말을 받아 갔던 여섯 처녀는, 브리튼 섬에서 가장 뛰어난 종자들이 했더라도 그만 못할 만큼 솜씨 좋게 말의 마구를 풀어 주었소.\n\n\"그러더니 보시오, 그들이 은 대접을 가져왔는데, 그 안에는 씻을 물이 담겨 있었고, 아마포 수건도 있어 더러는 푸르고 더러는 흰 것이었소. 그래서 나는 손을 씻었소. 잠시 후 사내가 식탁에 앉았소. 나는 그의 곁에 앉았고, 내 아래로는 우리 시중을 드는 이들을 제외한 모든 처녀들이 앉았소. 식탁은 은으로 되어 있었고, 식탁 위의 천은 아마포였소. 그리고 식탁에 올라온 그릇 가운데 금이나 은, 혹은 들소 뿔로 만들지 않은 것은 하나도 없었소. 그리고 우리에게 고기가 차려졌소. 참으로, 케이, 나는 그곳에서 내가 다른 어디에서 본 적이 있는 온갖 종류의 고기와 온갖 종류의 술을 보았소. 다만 그 고기와 술은 내가 다른 어떤 곳에서 본 것보다 훨씬 더 잘 차려져 있었소.\n\n\"식사가 절반쯤 지날 때까지 그 사내도, 처녀들 가운데 누구도 내게 한 마디 말도 건네지 않았소. 그러다 그 사내가 내게 더 먹기보다는 이야기 나누는 편이 더 즐거우리라는 것을 알아채자, 그는 내가 누구인지 묻기 시작했소. 그리하여 나는 그 사내에게 내가 누구인지, 그리고 내 여행의 까닭이 무엇인지를 말하고, 나보다 더 뛰어난 자가 있는지, 아니면 내가 모두를 제압할 수 있는지를 찾고 있다고 말했소. 그 사내는 나를 바라보더니, 미소를 지으며 말했소. '내가 그대에게 해를 끼칠까 두렵지만 않다면, 그대가 찾는 것을 보여 주겠소.' 그러자 나는 그에게 거리낌 없이 말해 달라고 청했소. 그러자 그가 말했소. '오늘 밤은 여기서 자고, 아침에 일찍 일어나, 골짜기를 따라 위로 올라가는 길을 잡아 숲에 이를 때까지 가시오. 숲에 조금 들어가면 그대는 한가운데에 둔덕이 있는, 크고 한적한 빈터에 다다를 것이오. 그러면 그대는 그 둔덕 꼭대기에 거구의 검은 사내가 있는 것을 보게 될 것이오. 그는 발이 하나뿐이고, 이마 한가운데에 눈이 하나 있소. 그는 그 숲의 숲지기요. 그대는 그를 둘러싸고 풀을 뜯는 천 마리의 들짐승을 보게 될 것이오. 그에게 그 빈터에서 나가는 길을 물으면, 그가 그대에게 짧게 대답하며, 그대가 찾고 있는 것을 발견할 길을 일러 줄 것이오.'\n\n\"그리고 그 밤이 내게는 길게만 느껴졌소. 이튿날 아침 나는 일어나 채비를 갖추고, 말에 올라, 골짜기를 곧장 가로질러 숲으로 나아갔으며, 마침내 그 빈터에 다다랐소. 그러자 검은 사내가 거기 둔덕 꼭대기에 앉아 있었소. 그리고 나는 내가 본 들짐승의 수에 그 사내가 말한 것보다 세 배는 더 놀랐소. 그래서 나는 그에게 길을 물었고, 그는 내게 어디로 가려 하느냐고 무뚝뚝하게 물었소. 내가 누구인지, 무엇을 찾는지 말하자, 그가 말했소. '빈터의 머리 쪽으로 이어지는 저 길을 잡으시오. 그러면 그대는 커다란 골짜기 같은 트인 공간을 발견할 것이고, 그 한가운데에 키 큰 나무 한 그루가 있을 것이오. 이 나무 아래에는 샘이 있고, 샘 곁에는 대리석 판이 있으며, 대리석 판 위에는 은 대접이 하나 있는데, 가져가지 못하도록 은 사슬에 매여 있소. 그 대접을 들어 판 위에 대접 가득 물을 부으시오. 그리고 그대가 그 모험에서 곤경을 겪지 않는다면, 남은 평생 동안 곤경을 찾아다닐 필요가 없을 것이오.'\n\n\"그리하여 나는 가파른 비탈 꼭대기에 이를 때까지 길을 갔소. 거기서 나는 모든 것이 검은 사내가 내게 일러 준 그대로임을 발견했소. 나는 그 나무로 다가가, 그 아래에서 샘을 보았고, 그 곁에서 대리석 판과 사슬에 매인 은 대접을 보았소. 그래서 나는 그 대접을 들어 판 위에 대접 가득 물을 부었더니, 곧바로 엄청난 천둥소리가 들려와, 하늘과 땅이 그 노여움에 떠는 듯했소. 천둥이 지난 뒤에는 소나기가 쏟아졌소. 그리고 참으로, 케이, 그대에게 이르거니와, 그것은 사람도 짐승도 견디고 살아남을 수 없을 만큼 대단한 소나기였소. 나는 말의 옆구리를 소나기 쪽으로 돌리고, 방패의 뾰족한 끝을 말의 머리와 목 위에 씌웠으며, 그 윗부분은 내 목 위에 가져다 댔소. 그렇게 나는 소나기를 견뎌 냈소. 이윽고 하늘이 맑아지자, 보시오, 새들이 나무에 내려앉아 노래했소. 그리고 참으로, 케이, 나는 그 전에도 그 후에도 그에 견줄 만한 가락을 결코 들어 본 적이 없소. 그리고 내가 새들의 노래에 가장 매혹되어 있을 때, 보시오! 누군가가 내게 다가오며 나무라는 목소리로 이렇게 말하는 것이 들렸소. '오 기사여, 무엇이 그대를 이곳으로 데려왔는가? 내가 그대에게 무슨 해악을 끼쳤기에, 그대가 오늘 나와 내 소유물에게 이런 짓을 한단 말인가? 오늘의 소나기가 내 영지에서, 그것에 노출된 사람도 짐승도 단 하나 살려 두지 않은 것을 그대는 모르는가?' 그러자 보시오, 칠흑같이 검은 벨벳을 걸치고, 검은 아마포 겉옷을 두른 기사가 검은 말을 타고 나타났소. 우리는 서로를 향해 돌진했고, 그 공격이 어찌나 사나운지, 오래지 않아 나는 나가떨어지고 말았소. 그러자 그 기사는 자기 창의 자루를 내 말의 고삐 사이로 꿰어, 두 마리 말을 끌고 가 버렸고, 나는 그 자리에 남겨졌소. 그는 나를 가두려고조차 하지 않았고, 내 무기를 빼앗지도 않았소. 그래서 나는 내가 왔던 길을 따라 되돌아갔소. 그리고 검은 사내가 있던 빈터에 이르렀을 때, 케이, 그대에게 고백하건대, 내가 그 검은 사내의 비웃음에 느낀 수치심으로 인해 액체 웅덩이가 되어 녹아내리지 않은 것이 기적이오. 그날 밤 나는 전날 밤을 보냈던 바로 그 성에 다다랐소. 그리고 그날 밤 나는 전날 밤보다 더 흡족한 대접을 받았소. 나는 성 안 사람들과 거리낌 없이 이야기를 나누었소. 그리고 그들 가운데 누구도 내가 샘으로 떠났던 일을 언급하지 않았고, 나 또한 누구에게도 그것을 말하지 않았소. 나는 그날 밤 그곳에 머물렀소. 이튿날 일어나 보니, 콧구멍이 진홍빛처럼 붉은 짙은 밤색 승용마 한 필이 안장을 얹은 채 마련되어 있었소. 그리고 갑옷을 입고, 그곳에 내 축복을 남긴 뒤, 나는 내 궁정으로 돌아왔소. 그리고 그 말은 지금도 내가 가지고 있으며, 저쪽 마구간에 있소. 나는 단언하건대, 브리튼 섬에서 가장 뛰어난 승용마와도 그 말을 바꾸지 않을 것이오.\n\n\"자, 참으로, 케이, 일찍이 그 누구도 이토록 제 명예에 누가 되는 모험을 고백한 적이 없을 것이오. 그리고 참으로 내게 이상하게 여겨지는 것은, 그 전에도 그 후에도 이 모험을 알고 있는 사람에 대해 들어 본 적이 없다는 것, 그리고 그 모험의 무대가 아서 왕의 영지 안에 있는데도 다른 누구 하나 그것을 우연히 발견하지 못했다는 것이오.\"\n\n————\n\n\"이제,\" 오와인이 말했다, \"가서 그곳을 찾아보는 것이 좋지 않겠습니까?\"\n\n\"내 벗의 손을 두고 말하건대,\" 케이가 말했다, \"그대는 행동으로 옮기지도 못할 말을 혀로만 자주 내뱉는구려.\"\n\n\"진실로,\" 구네버가 말했다, \"케이여, 그대가 오와인 같은 사람에게 그토록 무례한 말을 쓰느니 차라리 목매달리는 편이 나을 것이오.\"\n\n\"내 벗의 손을 두고 말하건대, 고귀한 부인이시여,\" 케이가 말했다, \"오와인에 대한 그대의 칭송이 저의 칭송보다 크지는 않습니다.\"\n\n그때 아서가 잠에서 깨어나, 자기가 잠시 잠들었던 것이 아니냐고 물었다.\n\n\"그렇습니다, 폐하,\" 오와인이 대답했다, \"잠시 주무셨습니다.\"\n\n\"식사를 하러 갈 시간이 되었느냐?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 폐하,\" 오와인이 말했다.\n\n그러자 손을 씻으라는 뿔나팔이 울렸고, 왕과 그의 모든 가솔이 자리에 앉아 식사를 했다. 그리고 식사가 끝나자 오와인은 자신의 거처로 물러나, 말과 무구를 준비했다.\n\n이튿날 동이 틀 무렵 그는 갑옷을 입고 군마에 올라, 머나먼 땅과 황량한 산들을 가로질러 여행했다. 그리하여 마침내 키논이 그에게 묘사해 주었던 골짜기에 이르렀고, 그곳이 자기가 찾던 바로 그 골짜기임을 확신했다. 그리고 강가를 따라 골짜기를 지나며, 그 물줄기를 좇아 마침내 평원에 다다라, 성을 시야에 두게 되었다. 성에 가까이 다가가니, 키논이 그들을 보았던 바로 그 자리에서 젊은이들이 활을 쏘고 있었고, 성의 주인인 황색 옷을 입은 사내가 그 곁에 서 있었다. 오와인이 그 황색 옷의 사내에게 인사를 건네자마자, 그 사내도 답례로 인사를 했다.\n\n그리고 그는 성을 향해 나아갔고, 거기서 그 방을 보았다. 방으로 들어서자, 처녀들이 금실로 새틴 자수를 놓고 있는 것이 보였다. 그들의 아름다움과 고움은 키논이 그에게 일러 준 것보다 훨씬 더 큰 것으로 보였다. 그리고 그들은 키논에게 그랬듯이 일어나 오와인의 시중을 들었다. 그리고 그들이 그 앞에 차려 놓은 식사는 키논에게 주었던 것보다 오와인에게 더욱 큰 만족을 주었다.\n\n식사가 절반쯤 진행되었을 때 황색 옷의 사내가 오와인에게 그 여행의 목적을 물었다. 오와인이 그것을 그에게 밝히며 말했다, \"나는 샘을 지키는 기사를 찾고 있소.\" 이 말에 황색 옷의 사내가 미소 지으며, 키논에게 그랬던 것처럼 그 모험을 일러 주기가 꺼려진다고 말했다. 그러나 그는 모든 것을 오와인에게 묘사해 주었고, 그들은 쉬러 물러갔다.\n\n이튿날 아침 오와인은 처녀들이 자기를 위해 말을 준비해 놓은 것을 발견하고는, 길을 나서 그 검은 사내가 있는 숲속 빈터에 다다랐다. 그 검은 사내의 체구는 키논에게보다 오와인에게 더욱 경이로워 보였다. 오와인이 그에게 길을 물으니, 그가 그것을 가르쳐 주었다. 오와인은 그 길을 따라 마침내 푸른 나무에 이르렀다. 그리고 그 샘과, 샘 곁의 석판과, 그 위에 놓인 대접을 보았다. 오와인은 그 대접을 집어 들어 한 대접 가득 물을 떠서 석판 위에 부었다. 그러자, 보라! 천둥소리가 들렸고, 천둥 뒤에는 키논이 묘사했던 것보다 더 격렬한 소나기가 쏟아졌으며, 소나기가 그치자 하늘이 맑아졌다. 그리고 곧바로 새들이 날아와 나무에 내려앉아 노래했다. 그들의 노래가 오와인에게 가장 듣기 좋게 들릴 무렵, 그는 한 기사가 골짜기를 가로질러 자기에게 다가오는 것을 보았다. 그는 그를 맞을 채비를 하고는 맹렬히 부딪쳤다. 두 사람의 창이 다 부러지자, 그들은 검을 뽑아 칼날을 맞대고 싸웠다. 그러다 오와인이 그 기사의 투구와 머리덮개와 면갑을 꿰뚫고, 살갗과 살과 뼈를 지나, 마침내 골수에까지 이르는 일격을 가했다. 그러자 검은 기사는 자기가 치명상을 입었음을 느끼고는, 말 머리를 돌려 달아났다. 오와인은 그를 바짝 뒤쫓았으나, 검으로 내리칠 만큼 가까이 다가서지는 못했다. 그때 오와인은 거대하고 휘황찬란한 성을 발견했고, 그들은 성문에 이르렀다. 검은 기사는 안으로 들어가도록 허락되었으나, 내리닫이 쇠창살문이 오와인 위로 떨어졌다. 그것은 안장 뒤쪽에서 그의 말을 내리쳐 두 동강 내고, 오와인의 발뒤꿈치에 달려 있던 박차의 톱니바퀴를 잘라 갔다. 그리고 그 쇠창살문이 바닥까지 내려왔다. 박차의 톱니바퀴와 말의 일부는 문 밖에 남았고, 오와인은 말의 나머지 부분과 함께 두 문 사이에 갇혔으며, 안쪽 문이 닫혀 있어 오와인은 거기서 빠져나갈 수가 없었다. 오와인은 당혹스러운 처지에 놓였다. 그런 상태에 있는 동안, 그는 문에 난 틈으로 자기 앞에 놓인 거리와, 양옆으로 늘어선 집들을 볼 수 있었다. 그리고 그는 누런빛으로 곱슬거리는 머리에 황금 머리띠를 두른 한 처녀를 보았다. 그녀는 황색 새틴 옷을 입고 있었고, 발에는 여러 빛깔의 가죽으로 만든 신을 신고 있었다. 그녀가 문으로 다가와, 문을 열어 달라고 청했다. \"하늘이 아시오, 아가씨,\" 오와인이 말했다, \"그대가 나를 풀어 줄 수 없는 것과 마찬가지로, 나로서도 여기서 그대에게 문을 열어 줄 도리가 없소.\" 그러고는 그녀에게 자기 이름과, 자기가 누구인지를 밝혔다. \"참으로,\" 처녀가 말했다, \"그대가 풀려날 수 없다니 매우 슬픈 일이옵니다. 마땅히 모든 여인이 그대를 도와야 할 것이온대, 부인들을 섬기는 데 있어 그대보다 더 충실한 사람이 없음을 제가 알기 때문이옵니다. 그러하오니,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"그대를 풀어 드리고자 제 힘으로 할 수 있는 일이라면 무엇이든 하겠나이다. 이 반지를 받아 손가락에 끼시되, 보석이 손바닥 안쪽으로 가게 하시고, 그 보석을 손으로 감싸 쥐소서. 그리고 그것을 감추고 계시는 한, 그것이 그대를 감추어 줄 것이옵니다. 그들이 그대를 데리러 나오면, 그대를 찾지 못하여 몹시 애통해할 것이옵니다. 저는 저기 저 디딤대 위에서 그대를 기다리겠나이다. 저는 그대를 볼 수 없으나, 그대는 저를 볼 수 있을 것이옵니다. 그러하오니 다가오시어 제 어깨에 손을 얹으시어, 그대가 제 곁에 있음을 제가 알게 하소서. 그리고 제가 여기서 가는 길을 따라 함께 오소서.\"\n\n그러고 나서 처녀는 오와인에게서 떠나갔고, 그는 그녀가 일러 준 대로 모두 행했다. 성안의 사람들은 오와인을 죽이려고 찾아 나섰는데, 그의 말의 절반밖에 찾지 못하자 몹시 애통해했다.\n\n오와인은 그들 가운데서 자취를 감추고는, 처녀에게로 가서 그녀의 어깨에 손을 얹었다. 그러자 그녀가 길을 나섰고, 오와인은 그녀를 뒤따라, 마침내 크고 아름다운 방의 문에 이르렀다. 처녀가 문을 열었고, 그들은 안으로 들어갔다. 오와인이 방을 둘러보니, 보라, 그 안에는 화려한 빛깔로 칠해지지 않은 못이 단 하나도 없었으며, 갖가지 금빛 형상이 그려지지 않은 벽판이 단 하나도 없었다.\n\n처녀는 불을 지피고, 은 대접에 물을 담아, 오와인에게 씻을 물을 건넸다. 그러고 나서 그 앞에 금이 박힌 은 탁자를 놓았는데, 그 위에는 황색 아마포 식탁보가 깔려 있었고, 그녀가 그에게 음식을 가져왔다. 그리고 참으로, 오와인은 거기에 풍성하게 갖추어지지 않은 고기 요리를 본 적이 없었으며, 게다가 그가 다른 어느 곳에서 맛본 것보다도 더 잘 조리되어 있었다. 그가 식사 시중을 받은 그릇 가운데 금이나 은이 아닌 것은 하나도 없었다. 오와인은 늦은 오후까지 먹고 마셨는데, 그때 보라! 그들은 성안에서 우렁찬 소란이 일어나는 것을 들었고, 오와인이 처녀에게 그것이 무슨 일이냐고 물었다. \"성의 주인이신 귀족께 종부성사를 베풀고 있는 것이옵니다,\" 그녀가 말했다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 아서 자신에게도 어울릴 만한 침상을 오와인을 위해 마련했고, 오와인은 잠자리에 들었다.\n\n날이 밝고 얼마 지나지 않아 그는 지극히 큰 소란과 통곡을 들었고, 처녀에게 그것이 무슨 까닭이냐고 물었다. \"성의 주인이셨던 귀족의 시신을 성당으로 모시고 가는 것이옵니다.\"\n\n오와인은 일어나 옷을 입고, 방의 창문을 열어 성 쪽을 바라보았다. 거리를 가득 메운 무리의 경계도 그 끝도 그는 가늠할 수 없었다. 그들은 완전히 무장하고 있었고, 수많은 여인들이 말을 타거나 걸어서 그들과 함께 있었으며, 도시의 모든 성직자들이 노래하고 있었다. 그 군중 한가운데서 그는 영구대를 보았는데, 그 위에는 흰 아마포 휘장이 덮여 있었고, 밀랍 양초들이 그 곁과 둘레에서 타고 있었다. 그 영구대를 떠받친 자들 가운데 막강한 영주보다 낮은 지위에 있는 자는 아무도 없었다.\n\n오와인은 비단과 새틴으로 그토록 화려한 무리를 일찍이 본 적이 없었다. 그리고 그 행렬을 뒤따르는, 누런 머리가 어깨 위로 늘어지고 피로 물든 한 부인을 보았다. 그녀의 몸에는 황색 새틴 옷이 둘려 있었으나 찢겨 있었다. 그녀의 발에는 여러 빛깔의 가죽으로 만든 신이 신겨 있었다. 그녀가 두 손을 어찌나 격렬하게 마주쳤던지 손가락 끝이 멍들지 않은 것이 놀라울 정도였다. 진실로 그녀가 평소의 모습이었다면, 오와인이 일찍이 본 가장 아름다운 부인이었으리라. 그녀의 울부짖음은 사람들의 외침이나 나팔 소리보다도 더 컸다. 그 부인을 보자마자 그는 그녀에 대한 사랑으로 불타올랐고, 그 사랑이 그를 온전히 사로잡았다.\n\n그러고 나서 그는 처녀에게 그 부인이 누구인지를 물었다. \"하늘이 아시옵니다,\" 처녀가 대답했다, \"그분은 여인들 가운데 가장 아름답고, 가장 정숙하며, 가장 너그럽고, 가장 고귀한 분이옵니다. 그분은 저의 여주인이시며, 샘의 백작 부인이라 불리시는데, 어제 그대가 죽인 자의 아내이시옵니다.\" \"참으로,\" 오와인이 말했다, \"그분이야말로 내가 가장 사랑하는 여인이오.\" \"참으로,\" 처녀가 말했다, \"그분 또한 그대를 적잖이 사랑하시게 될 것이옵니다.\"\n\n그러고 나서 처녀는 오와인을 위해 식사를 마련했는데, 참으로 그는 이전에 이토록 좋은 식사를 한 적도, 이토록 융숭한 시중을 받은 적도 없다고 여겼다. 이윽고 그녀는 그를 두고 성으로 향했다. 그곳에 이르니, 애도와 슬픔밖에는 없었으며, 백작 부인은 슬픔에 잠겨 그 누구의 모습도 견디지 못한 채 방에 있었다. 루네드라는 이름의 처녀가 그녀에게 인사를 건넸으나, 백작 부인은 답하지 않았다. 처녀가 그녀에게 몸을 굽히며 말했다, \"어찌하여 오늘 그 누구에게도 답하지 않으시옵니까?\" \"루네드,\" 백작 부인이 말했다, \"어찌 그리 변하여, 내가 슬픔에 잠겨 있는데 나를 보러 오지도 않았느냐. 그것은 네가 잘못한 일이며, 나는 이토록 비통하거늘.\" \"참으로,\" 루네드가 말했다, \"저는 마님의 분별이 지금 제가 보는 것보다 더 크리라 여겼나이다. 그 어진 분을 여의고 슬퍼하는 것이, 혹은 마님께서 가질 수 없는 그 무엇 때문에 슬퍼하는 것이 과연 마님께 이로운 일이옵니까?\" \"하늘에 맹세코,\" 백작 부인이 말했다, \"온 세상에 그분과 견줄 만한 사내는 없느니라.\" \"그렇지 않사옵니다,\" 루네드가 말했다, \"추한 사내라도 그분만큼 훌륭하거나 더 나을 수 있나이다.\" \"하늘에 맹세코,\" 백작 부인이 말했다, \"내가 길러 낸 자를 죽이는 것이 내게 역겨운 일만 아니라면, 내게 그런 비교를 한 죄로 너를 처형하게 했으리라. 사정이 이러하니, 너를 추방하겠노라.\" \"기쁘옵니다,\" 루네드가 말했다, \"마님께서 무엇이 이로운지를 모르시는 자리에서 제가 마님께 도움이 되고자 했다는 것 외에는 그리하실 다른 까닭이 없으시다니 말이옵니다. 이제부터는, 우리 둘 중 어느 쪽이든 먼저 화해의 손길을 내미는 쪽에 화가 있을지어다. 제가 마님께 부름을 청하든, 마님께서 스스로 사람을 보내 저를 부르시든 말이옵니다.\"\n\n그러고 나서 루네드는 밖으로 나갔다. 백작 부인은 일어나 그녀를 따라 방문까지 가서, 큰 소리로 기침하기 시작했다. 루네드가 돌아보자, 백작 부인이 그녀를 손짓해 불렀고, 그녀는 백작 부인에게 돌아왔다. \"참으로,\" 백작 부인이 말했다, \"네 성미가 고약하구나. 허나 네가 무엇이 내게 이로운지를 안다면, 그것을 내게 일러 다오.\" \"그리하겠나이다,\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"전쟁과 무력에 의하지 않고서는 마님께서 영지를 보존하시기가 불가능함을 아시지 않사옵니까. 그러하오니 지체 말고 그것을 지킬 수 있는 누군가를 찾으소서.\" \"한데 내가 어찌 그리할 수 있겠느냐?\" 백작 부인이 말했다. \"일러 드리겠나이다,\" 루네드가 말했다, \"마님께서 그 샘을 지켜 내지 못하시면, 영토를 유지하실 수 없나이다. 그리고 그 샘은 아서의 가솔에 속한 기사가 아니고서는 누구도 지켜 낼 수 없나이다. 제가 아서의 궁정으로 가서, 예전에 샘을 지키던 자만큼이나, 아니 그보다 더 잘 그 샘을 지킬 수 있는 무사를 데리고 돌아오지 못한다면 제게 화가 있을지어다.\" \"그것은 이루기 어려운 일이로구나,\" 백작 부인이 말했다. \"허나 가서, 네가 약속한 바를 증명해 보이거라.\"\n\n루네드는 아서의 궁정으로 가는 척하고 길을 나섰으나, 오와인을 남겨 두었던 저택으로 돌아가, 아서 왕의 궁정까지 다녀오는 데 걸릴 만한 시간 동안 그곳에 머물렀다. 그 시간이 다하자 그녀는 옷을 차려입고, 백작 부인을 보러 갔다. 백작 부인은 그녀를 보고 몹시 기뻐하며, 궁정에서 무슨 소식을 가져왔는지 물었다. \"마님께 더없이 좋은 소식을 가져왔나이다,\" 루네드가 말했다, \"제 사명의 목적을 이루었기 때문이옵니다. 저와 함께 이리로 온 그 우두머리를 마님께 언제 소개해 드리오리까?\" \"내일 그를 이리로 데려와 나를 보게 하라,\" 백작 부인이 말했다, \"그때까지 내가 고을 사람들을 모아 두겠노라.\"\n\n그리하여 루네드는 집으로 돌아왔다. 그리고 이튿날 정오에 오와인은 윗옷과 겉옷, 그리고 넓은 금실 레이스 띠가 달린 황색 새틴 망토를 차려입었다. 그의 발에는 여러 빛깔의 가죽으로 만든, 사자 형상의 황금 죔쇠로 여민 높은 신이 신겨 있었다. 그리고 그들은 백작 부인의 방으로 나아갔다.\n\n백작 부인은 그들이 온 것을 무척 기뻐했다. 그녀는 오와인을 뚫어지게 바라보며 말했다, \"루네드, 이 기사는 나그네 같은 행색이 아니로구나.\" \"그것이 무슨 해가 되옵니까, 마님?\" 루네드가 말했다. \"분명코,\" 백작 부인이 말했다, \"내 주인의 몸에서 혼을 쫓아낸 자는 다름 아닌 이 사람이렷다.\" \"마님께는 오히려 잘된 일이옵니다, 마님,\" 루네드가 말했다, \"이 사람이 주인보다 강하지 않았더라면, 주인의 목숨을 앗을 수 없었을 것이옵니다. 이미 지나간 일은 어찌할 도리가 없는 것이니, 사정이 어떠하든 그러하옵니다.\" \"네 거처로 돌아가 있거라,\" 백작 부인이 말했다, \"내가 깊이 생각해 보겠노라.\"\n\n이튿날 백작 부인은 자신의 모든 신민을 불러 모아, 자신의 백작령이 무방비 상태로 남겨졌으며, 말과 무기와 군사적 기량 없이는 지켜 낼 수 없음을 그들에게 알렸다. \"그러하니,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"내가 그대들의 선택에 부치는 바는 이러하다. 그대들 중 한 사람이 나를 취하든지, 아니면 내가 영토를 지키기 위해 다른 곳에서 남편을 맞이하는 것에 동의하든지 하라.\"\n\n그리하여 그들은 그녀가 다른 곳에서 누군가와 혼인하도록 허락받는 것이 낫다는 결론에 이르렀다. 이에 그녀는 주교들과 대주교들을 불러, 오와인과의 혼례를 거행하게 했다. 그리고 백작령의 사람들은 오와인에게 충성을 맹세했다.\n\n오와인은 창과 검으로 그 샘을 지켰다. 그가 그것을 지킨 방식은 이러했다. 어떤 기사든 그곳에 오면, 그는 그를 쓰러뜨리고, 그 값어치를 온전히 받고 그를 팔았다. 그리고 그렇게 얻은 것을 자신의 영주들과 기사들에게 나누어 주니, 온 세상에 그만큼 신민들에게 사랑받을 수 있는 사람은 없었다. 그리고 이러하기를 삼 년 동안 계속했다.\n\n————\n\n# GAWAIN’S ADVENTURE\n\n어느 날 가웨인이 아서 왕과 함께 길을 나섰을 때, 그는 왕이 몹시 슬프고 침울해하는 것을 알아차렸다. 가웨인은 그러한 처지에 놓인 아서를 보고 마음이 무척 아파서, 이렇게 물었다. \"오, 전하, 무슨 일이 있으셨습니까?\" \"사실은 말일세, 가웨인.\" 아서가 말했다. \"나는 오와인 때문에 마음이 무겁네. 그를 잃은 지 벌써 삼 년이 되었고, 만일 사 년째 되는 해가 그를 보지 못한 채 지나간다면 나는 틀림없이 죽고 말 것이네. 이제 나는 그를 잃게 된 것이 클리드노의 아들 키논이 들려준 이야기 때문임을 확신하고 있네.\" \"그 일로 전하의 온 영토에 무장을 명하실 필요는 없습니다.\" 가웨인이 말했다. \"전하와 전하의 가신들만으로도, 오와인이 죽임을 당했다면 그 원수를 갚을 수 있고, 갇혀 있다면 풀어 줄 수 있으며, 살아 있다면 전하와 함께 데려올 수 있을 것입니다.\" 그리하여 가웨인이 말한 대로 일이 정해졌다.\n\n그리고 아서와 그의 가신들은 길을 떠나 오와인을 찾을 채비를 했다. 클리드노의 아들 키논이 그들의 길잡이 노릇을 했다. 아서는 키논이 전에 머물렀던 성에 다다랐다. 그곳에 이르렀을 때, 젊은이들은 예전과 같은 자리에서 활을 쏘고 있었고, 노란 옷을 입은 사내가 가까이에 서 있었다. 노란 옷의 사내는 아서를 보자 그를 맞이하며 성으로 들어오라고 청했다. 아서는 그 청을 받아들였고, 그들은 함께 성으로 들어갔다. 그를 따르는 무리의 수가 많았음에도, 성이 워낙 광대하여 그들의 존재가 성안에서는 거의 눈에 띄지 않았다. 처녀들이 일어나 그들의 시중을 들었다. 처녀들의 시중은 그들 모두가 그때까지 받아 본 어떤 접대보다도 뛰어나 보였다. 말을 돌보는 시동들조차 그날 밤 아서가 자신의 궁전에서 받았을 대접에 못지않은 시중을 받았다.\n\n이튿날 아침 아서는 키논을 길잡이 삼아 그곳을 떠나, 검은 옷의 사내가 있는 곳에 이르렀다. 검은 옷의 사내의 키는 들었던 것보다도 더 놀랍게 아서에게 다가왔다. 그들은 숲이 우거진 가파른 언덕 꼭대기에 올라 골짜기를 가로질러, 마침내 푸른 나무가 선 곳에 다다랐고, 그곳에서 샘과 그릇과 석판을 보았다. 그러자 케이가 아서에게 다가와 말을 건넸다. \"전하.\" 그가 말했다. \"저는 이 모든 것의 뜻을 알고 있습니다. 청하옵건대, 제가 석판 위에 물을 끼얹어 처음 닥쳐오는 모험을 맞이하도록 허락해 주십시오.\" 아서는 그에게 허락을 내렸다.\n\n그러자 케이가 그릇 가득 물을 떠서 석판 위에 끼얹었고, 곧바로 천둥이 쳤으며 천둥에 뒤이어 소나기가 쏟아졌다. 그들은 그처럼 거센 뇌우를 일찍이 겪어 본 적이 없었다. 소나기가 그치자 하늘이 맑아졌고, 나무를 바라보니 잎이 하나도 남지 않은 모습이었다. 그러자 새들이 나무 위로 내려앉았다. 새들의 노래는 그들이 그때까지 들어 본 어떤 가락보다도 훨씬 감미로웠다. 이윽고 그들은 칠흑같이 검은 말에 올라 검은 비단옷을 걸친 한 기사가 그들을 향해 빠르게 다가오는 것을 보았다. 케이가 그를 맞아 겨루었으나, 오래지 않아 케이가 나가떨어지고 말았다. 기사는 물러갔다. 아서와 그의 무리는 그날 밤 그곳에 진을 쳤다.\n\n이튿날 아침 그들이 일어났을 때, 기사의 창에 결투를 알리는 표지가 걸려 있는 것이 보였다. 그러자 아서의 가신들이 하나씩 차례로 나아가 그 기사와 겨루었으나, 아서와 가웨인을 제외하고는 그에게 나가떨어지지 않은 이가 하나도 없었다. 마침내 아서가 그 기사와 맞서고자 무장을 했다. \"오, 전하.\" 가웨인이 말했다. \"제가 먼저 그와 싸우도록 허락해 주십시오.\" 아서는 그것을 허락했다. 가웨인은 자신과 말에게 랑기르 백작의 딸이 보내 준 비단 명예의 겉옷을 덮어쓰고 기사를 맞으러 나아갔는데, 그 차림 덕분에 무리 가운데 누구도 그를 알아보지 못했다. 둘은 서로에게 돌진하여 그날 저녁까지 온종일 싸웠다. 그러나 어느 쪽도 상대를 말에서 떨어뜨리지 못했다. 그 이튿날도 마찬가지였다. 그들은 충돌하며 창을 부러뜨렸으나, 어느 쪽도 우위를 차지하지 못했다.\n\n사흘째 되는 날 그들은 유난히 튼튼한 창을 들고 싸웠다. 그들은 분노에 사로잡혀 정오에 이르기까지 격렬하게 맞붙었다. 그러다 서로에게 어찌나 큰 충격을 가했던지 말들의 뱃대끈이 끊어졌고, 둘은 말 엉덩이 너머로 땅에 떨어지고 말았다. 그들은 재빨리 일어나 칼을 뽑아 들고 다시 싸움을 이어 갔다. 그들의 대결을 지켜본 이들은 모두, 그처럼 용맹하고 강력한 두 사람을 일찍이 본 적이 없다고 확신했다. 설령 한밤중이었다 한들, 그들의 무기에서 번쩍이는 불꽃만으로도 사방이 환했을 것이다. 그러다 기사가 가웨인에게 일격을 가하여 그의 투구를 얼굴에서 벗겨 내자, 기사는 상대가 가웨인임을 알아보았다. 그러자 오와인이 말했다. \"가웨인 경, 그대를 감싼 명예의 겉옷 때문에 나는 내 사촌인 그대를 알아보지 못했소. 내 칼과 무기를 받으시오.\" 가웨인이 말했다. \"오와인, 그대가 승자요. 그러니 그대가 내 칼을 받으시오.\" 그때 아서가 그들이 이야기를 나누는 것을 보고 그들을 향해 다가왔다. \"아서 전하.\" 가웨인이 말했다. \"여기 오와인이 저를 이겼는데도 제 무기를 받으려 하지 않습니다.\" \"전하.\" 오와인이 말했다. \"저를 이긴 것은 이 사람이온데, 이 사람이 제 칼을 받으려 하지 않습니다.\" \"그대들의 칼을 내게 다오.\" 아서가 말했다. \"그러면 어느 쪽도 상대를 이긴 것이 아니게 되리라.\" 그러자 오와인이 아서의 목을 두 팔로 감싸 안았고, 그들은 서로를 끌어안았다. 온 무리가 오와인을 보고 그를 끌어안으려고 앞으로 몰려들었다. 그 북새통이 어찌나 심했던지, 하마터면 목숨을 잃을 뻔한 이가 생길 지경이었다.\n\n그들은 그날 밤 물러났고, 이튿날 아서는 떠날 채비를 했다. \"전하.\" 오와인이 말했다. \"그것은 전하답지 못한 일입니다. 저는 전하 곁을 떠나 있은 지 삼 년이 되었고, 그동안 줄곧 바로 오늘에 이르기까지, 전하께서 저를 찾으러 오시리라는 것을 알고 전하를 위한 연회를 준비해 왔습니다. 그러니 전하와 시종들이 여행의 피로를 풀고 향유를 바를 때까지 저와 함께 머물러 주십시오.\"\n\n그리하여 그들은 모두 샘의 백작 부인의 성으로 갔고, 삼 년에 걸쳐 준비된 연회는 석 달 만에 모두 동이 났다. 그들은 그보다 더 맛있고 흡족한 연회를 누려 본 적이 없었다. 이윽고 아서는 떠날 채비를 했다. 그러면서 그는 백작 부인에게 사절을 보내, 오와인을 석 달 동안 자신과 함께 가게 허락해 달라고, 그를 브리튼 섬의 귀족들과 아리따운 귀부인들에게 보여 줄 수 있도록 청했다. 백작 부인은 몹시 고통스러웠으나 승낙해 주었다. 그리하여 오와인은 아서와 함께 브리튼 섬으로 왔다. 그가 다시 친척들과 벗들 가운데 있게 되자, 그는 석 달이 아니라 삼 년을 그들과 함께 머물렀다.\n\n# THE ADVENTURE OF THE LION\n\n어느 날 오와인이 우스크 강가의 카이르레온 시에서 식사를 하며 앉아 있는데, 한 처녀가 갈기가 곱슬곱슬하고 거품으로 뒤덮인 밤색 말을 타고 회관 안으로 들어왔다. 굴레와 안장의 드러난 부분은 모두 금으로 되어 있었다. 처녀는 노란 비단옷을 차려입고 있었다. 그녀는 오와인에게 다가와 그의 손가락에서 반지를 빼앗았다. \"이렇게 다루어 마땅하다.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"속이는 자, 배신하는 자, 신의 없는 자, 명예를 잃은 자, 수염도 못 기른 자를.\" 그러고는 말 머리를 돌려 떠나 버렸다.\n\n그러자 자신의 모험이 오와인의 기억에 되살아났고, 그는 슬픔에 잠겼다. 식사를 마친 그는 자신의 거처로 가서 그날 밤 채비를 갖추었다. 이튿날 그는 일어났으나 궁정으로 가지도, 샘의 백작 부인에게 돌아가지도 않고, 땅의 멀고 먼 곳들과 사람의 손길이 닿지 않은 산들을 떠돌았다. 그는 옷이 모두 해지고 몸이 야위고 머리카락이 길게 자랄 때까지 그곳에 머물렀다. 그는 들짐승들과 더불어 돌아다니며 그들과 함께 먹이를 먹었고, 마침내 짐승들이 그를 따르게 되었다. 그러나 끝내 그는 너무나 쇠약해져 더 이상 그들과 함께 지낼 수 없게 되었다. 그리하여 그는 산에서 골짜기로 내려와, 세상에서 가장 아름다우며 자비로운 한 귀부인의 소유인 동산에 이르렀다.\n\n어느 날 그 귀부인과 시녀들이 동산 한가운데 있는 호숫가를 거닐려고 나왔다. 그들은 죽은 듯이 누워 있는 한 사내의 모습을 보았다. 그들은 겁에 질렸다. 그래도 그들은 그에게 다가가 그를 만져 보았고, 그에게 아직 목숨이 붙어 있음을 알았다. 귀부인은 성으로 돌아가 귀한 연고가 가득 든 병을 가져와 시녀 하나에게 주었다. \"이것을 가지고 가거라.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그리고 저 말과 옷가지를 데리고 가서 방금 우리가 본 사내 곁에 두어라. 그의 심장 가까이에 이 향유를 발라 주어라. 그에게 목숨이 붙어 있다면 이 향유의 효험으로 그가 되살아날 것이다. 그런 다음 그가 어떻게 하는지 지켜보아라.\"\n\n시녀는 그녀에게서 떠나, 가서 오와인에게 향유를 부어 주고 말과 옷가지를 가까이에 두었으며, 조금 떨어진 곳으로 가서 몸을 숨기고 그를 지켜보았다. 얼마 지나지 않아 그녀는 그가 움직이기 시작하는 것을 보았다. 그는 일어나 자신의 몸을 살펴보았고, 자신의 보기 흉한 몰골을 부끄러워했다. 그러다 그는 곁에 놓인 말과 옷가지를 알아차렸다. 그는 옷을 차려입고 가까스로 말에 올랐다. 그제야 처녀가 그에게 모습을 드러내고 그에게 인사했다. 그와 처녀는 성으로 갔고, 처녀는 그를 쾌적한 방으로 안내하여 불을 피우고 그를 두고 떠났다.\n\n그는 석 달 동안 성에 머물렀고, 마침내 예전의 모습을 되찾았으며, 이전 어느 때보다도 더 잘생긴 사람이 되었다. 오와인은 강력한 이웃과의 분쟁에서 그 귀부인에게 큰 공을 세워, 그녀의 환대에 넉넉히 보답했다. 그리고 그는 길을 떠났다.\n\n길을 가던 중에 그는 숲속에서 우렁찬 울부짖음을 들었다. 그 소리는 두 번째, 세 번째로 되풀이되었다. 오와인은 그 자리를 향해 갔고, 숲 한가운데 솟은 거대한 바위투성이 둔덕을 보았는데, 그 비탈에는 잿빛 바위가 있었다. 바위에는 틈이 갈라져 있었고, 그 틈 안에 뱀 한 마리가 있었다. 바위 가까이에는 검은 사자 한 마리가 서 있었는데, 사자가 그곳을 벗어나려 할 때마다 뱀이 그에게 달려들어 공격하려 했다. 오와인은 칼을 뽑아 들고 바위에 다가갔다. 그리고 뱀이 튀어나오자 칼로 그것을 내리쳐 두 동강을 냈다. 그는 칼을 닦고 전과 같이 길을 갔다. 그러자 보라, 사자가 그를 따라오며, 마치 자기가 길러 온 사냥개라도 되는 양 그의 주위에서 뛰놀았다.\n\n그들은 그렇게 온종일 저녁때까지 나아갔다. 오와인이 쉴 때가 되자 그는 말에서 내려 평평하고 나무가 우거진 풀밭에 말을 풀어놓았다. 그가 불을 일으켜 불씨가 붙자, 사자가 사흘 밤을 댈 만큼 넉넉한 땔감을 그에게 가져다주었다. 그러고는 사자가 사라졌다. 이윽고 사자가 살집 좋은 커다란 노루 한 마리를 물고 돌아왔다. 사자가 그것을 오와인 앞에 내려놓자, 오와인은 그것을 가지고 불가로 갔다.\n\n오와인은 노루를 잡아 가죽을 벗기고, 그 살점을 꼬챙이에 꿰어 불 둘레에 둘렀다. 나머지 노루는 사자에게 먹으라고 주었다. 그렇게 일하고 있을 때, 그는 가까이에서 깊은 신음 소리를 들었고, 두 번째, 세 번째로 또 들었다. 그 신음이 흘러나오는 곳은 바위에 난 동굴이었다. 오와인은 가까이 가서, 그토록 애처롭게 신음하는 자가 누구인지 알고자 소리쳐 물었다. 한 목소리가 대답했다. \"저는 샘의 백작 부인의 시녀 루네드입니다.\" \"그대는 여기서 무엇을 하고 있는가?\" 그가 말했다. \"저는 갇혀 있습니다.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"아서의 궁정에서 와서 백작 부인과 혼인한 기사 때문입니다. 그는 잠시 그녀와 함께 머물렀으나, 그 뒤 아서의 궁정으로 떠나서는 그 후로 돌아오지 않았습니다. 백작 부인의 시동 둘이 그를 헐뜯으며 속이는 자라고 불렀습니다. 제가 그는 머지않아 와서 그 두 사람 모두를 상대로 자신의 명예를 지키리라고 보증하겠다고 말했더니, 그들은 저를 이 동굴에 가두고, 정해진 날까지 그가 와서 저를 구해 내지 않으면 저를 죽이겠다고 했습니다. 그 날이 바로 내일로 다가왔는데, 저를 위해 그를 찾으러 보낼 사람이 아무도 없습니다. 그의 이름은 우리엔의 아들 오와인입니다.\" \"만일 그 기사가 이 모든 일을 안다면 그대를 구하러 오리라고 확신하는가?\" \"저는 그것을 무엇보다 확신합니다.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n살점이 다 익자, 오와인은 그것을 자신과 처녀 사이에 둘로 나누었다. 그런 다음 오와인은 누워 잠을 청했는데, 그날 밤 사자가 오와인을 지킨 것만큼 엄중하게 주인을 지킨 파수꾼은 일찍이 없었다.\n\n이튿날 그 두 시동이 많은 시종 무리를 거느리고 와서, 루네드를 감방에서 끌어내 죽이려 했다. 오와인은 그들에게 그녀에게 무슨 죄를 묻느냐고 물었다. 그들은 그들 사이에 있었던 약속을 그에게 이야기했는데, 그것은 전날 밤 처녀가 했던 이야기와 같았다. \"그리고,\" 그들이 말했다. \"오와인이 그녀의 기대를 저버렸으니, 우리는 그녀를 끌고 가 불태우려는 것이오.\" \"참으로,\" 오와인이 말했다. \"그는 훌륭한 기사요. 만일 그가 그 처녀가 이런 위험에 처한 것을 알았다면, 그녀를 구하러 오지 않았다는 것이 나는 의아할 따름이오. 그러나 그대들이 그를 대신해 나를 받아들인다면, 내가 그대들과 싸우겠소.\" \"그렇게 합시다.\" 젊은이가 말했다.\n\n그들이 오와인을 공격하자, 그는 그들에게 호되게 시달렸다. 그러자 사자가 오와인을 도우러 왔고, 둘은 그 젊은이들을 제압했다. 그들이 그에게 말했다. \"기사여, 우리가 그대 혼자하고만 싸우기로 했지, 저 짐승하고는 약속한 바가 없소. 저 짐승과 겨루는 것은 그대와 겨루는 것보다 더 어렵소.\" 그래서 오와인은 사자를 루네드가 갇혀 있던 곳에 넣고 돌로 문을 막았다. 그러고는 전과 같이 그 젊은이들과 싸우러 나아갔다. 그러나 오와인은 평소의 기력이 없었고, 두 젊은이는 그를 거세게 몰아붙였다. 오와인이 곤경에 처한 것을 본 사자가 끊임없이 포효했다. 그러더니 사자가 벽을 뚫고 나갈 길을 찾아내 젊은이들에게 달려들어 단숨에 그들을 죽여 버렸다. 그리하여 루네드는 불태워질 위기에서 구해졌다.\n\n그런 다음 오와인은 루네드와 함께 샘의 부인의 성으로 돌아갔다. 그리고 그곳을 떠날 때, 그는 백작 부인을 데리고 아서의 궁정으로 갔으며, 그녀는 살아 있는 동안 내내 그의 아내였다.\n\n# GERAINT, THE SON OF ERBIN\n\n아서 왕은 우스크 강가의 카에르레온에서 궁정을 여는 것이 관례였다. 그리고 그곳에서 일곱 번의 부활절과 다섯 번의 성탄절에 궁정을 열었다. 그리고 한번은 그곳에서 오순절에 궁정을 열었다. 카에르레온은 바다로든 육지로든 그의 영토 가운데 접근하기 가장 쉬운 곳이었기 때문이다. 그리고 그곳에는 그에게 조공을 바치던 아홉 명의 왕관 쓴 왕들이 모였고, 백작들과 남작들도 함께 모였다. 그들은 큰 잔치마다 그의 초대를 받은 손님들이었으니, 어떤 큰 사정으로 가로막히지 않는 한 그러하였다. 그리고 그가 카에르레온에서 궁정을 열 때에는 미사를 위해 열세 곳의 교회가 따로 배정되었다. 그 배정은 다음과 같았다. 첫 번째 교회는 아서와 그의 왕들, 그리고 그의 손님들을 위한 것이요, 두 번째는 귀네비어와 그 시녀들을 위한 것이요, 세 번째는 궁실의 집사장과 청원자들을 위한 것이요, 네 번째는 프랑크인들과 그 밖의 관리들을 위한 것이며, 나머지 아홉 교회는 궁실의 아홉 주관자들을 위한 것이되, 그 가운데서도 특히 가웨인을 위한 것이었다. 그는 무용의 명성이 드높고 출생이 고귀하여 그 아홉 가운데 가장 높이 떠받들어졌기 때문이다. 그리고 교회에 관해서는 여기 언급한 것 외에 다른 배정은 없었다.\n\n그리고 오순절 화요일에 왕이 연회 자리에 앉아 있을 때, 보라, 키가 크고 머리가 고운 한 젊은이가 들어왔으니, 그는 공단으로 지은 외투와 겉옷을 입고, 황금 자루의 검을 목에 두르고, 발에는 낮은 가죽신을 신고 있었다. 그리고 그는 다가와 아서 앞에 섰다. \"주군께 인사 올립니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"하늘이 그대를 번성케 하시기를.\" 그가 대답했다. \"그리고 잘 왔노라. 무슨 새로운 소식을 가져왔느냐?\" \"그렇습니다, 주군.\" 그가 말했다. \"저는 주군의 산지기 가운데 하나로, 딘 숲을 지키며, 제 이름은 마도크, 투르가다른의 아들입니다. 그 숲에서 제가 사슴 한 마리를 보았는데, 그런 것은 여태껏 본 적이 없었습니다.\" \"그것이 어떠하기에,\" 아서가 물었다. \"네가 여태껏 그런 것을 본 적이 없다는 말이냐?\" \"순백색이옵니다, 주군. 그리고 그 자태가 어찌나 위풍당당하고 거만한지, 그 거동이 참으로 왕다워, 다른 어떤 짐승과도 무리를 이루지 않습니다. 그래서 저는 주군의 조언을 구하고, 그것에 관한 주군의 뜻을 알고자 왔습니다.\" \"내 생각에는,\" 아서가 말했다. \"내일 동틀 무렵에 가서 그것을 사냥하고, 오늘 밤 궁정 곳곳에 그에 관한 통고를 두루 알리는 것이 가장 좋겠다.\"\n\n\"아서는 그전 오순절에 우스크 강가의 옛 카에르레온에서 궁정을 열었더라. 그곳에서 어느 날, 그가 높이 홀에 앉아 있을 때, 그 앞에 딘의 산지기 하나가 와서, 숲에서 젖은 몸으로, 한 마리 수사슴의 소식을 전했으니, 제 동료들 모두보다 키가 크고, 젖빛으로 희며, 그날 처음 보였다는 것이라. 이것들을 그는 왕에게 아뢰었더라. 그러자 어진 왕은 명을 내려, 이튿날 아침 사냥을 위해 뿔나팔을 불게 하였더라.\" ─「이니드」.\n\n그리고 아리퓌에리스는 아서의 수석 사냥꾼이었고, 아렐리브리는 그의 수석 시동이었다. 그리고 모두가 통고를 받았으니, 그렇게 일이 마련되었다.\n\n그러자 귀네비어가 아서에게 말했다. \"주군, 내일 그 젊은이가 말한 사슴의 사냥을 보고 들으러 가는 것을 허락해 주시겠습니까?\" \"기꺼이 허락하오.\" 아서가 말했다. 그리고 가웨인이 아서에게 말했다. \"주군, 만일 좋으시다면, 그 사슴이 누구의 사냥에 들어오든, 그 사람이 기사이든 보행자이든, 그 머리를 베어 자기가 원하는 이에게, 곧 자기 연인이든 벗의 연인이든 줄 수 있도록 허락해 주십시오.\" \"기꺼이 허락하노라.\" 아서가 말했다. \"그리고 만일 내일 사냥을 위해 모든 것이 준비되어 있지 않거든, 궁실의 집사장을 벌하도록 하라.\"\n\n그리고 그들은 노래와 여흥과 담소와 넉넉한 향연으로 밤을 보냈다. 그리고 모두가 잠자리에 들 때가 되자 그들은 갔다. 그리고 이튿날이 오자 그들은 일어났다. 그리고 아서는 자기 침소를 지키던 시종들을 불렀다. 거기에는 네 명의 시동이 있었으니, 그 이름은 간드위의 아들 카디르네르스, 베드워의 아들 암브레우, 아서의 아들 암하르, 그리고 쿠스텐닌의 아들 고레우였다. 그리고 이 사람들이 아서에게 와서 인사를 올리고, 그에게 옷을 입혔다. 그리고 아서는 귀네비어가 깨어나지 않는 것을 의아하게 여겼고, 시종들은 그녀를 깨우려 하였다. \"그녀를 깨우지 마라.\" 아서가 말했다. \"사냥을 보러 가는 것보다 자는 편을 그녀가 더 바랄 테니라.\"\n\n그러자 아서는 밖으로 나갔고, 두 개의 뿔나팔이 울리는 소리를 들었으니, 하나는 수석 사냥꾼의 숙소 가까이에서, 다른 하나는 수석 시동의 숙소 가까이에서 나는 것이었다. 그리고 무리의 온 모임이 아서에게로 와서, 그들은 숲으로 가는 길로 들어섰다.\n\n그리고 아서가 궁궐에서 나간 뒤에, 귀네비어가 깨어나, 자기 시녀들을 부르고, 몸치장을 했다. \"시녀들아,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"나는 지난밤에 사냥을 보러 가도 좋다는 허락을 받았다. 너희 가운데 하나가 마구간으로 가서, 여자가 탈 만한 말을 이리로 가져오게 하여라.\" 그리고 그들 가운데 하나가 갔으나, 마구간에는 말이 두 마리밖에 없었다. 그래서 귀네비어와 그 시녀 가운데 하나가 그것들에 올라타, 우스크 강을 건너, 사람들과 말들의 자취를 따라갔다. 그리고 그들이 그렇게 말을 달리고 있을 때, 그들은 크고 세차게 다가오는 소리를 들었다. 그래서 그들이 뒤를 돌아보니, 거대한 몸집의 사냥용 망아지에 올라탄 기사 하나가 보였다. 그리고 그 기사는 머리가 고운 젊은이로, 다리를 드러내고, 기품 있는 자태였다. 그리고 황금 자루의 검이 그의 옆구리에 있었고, 공단으로 지은 겉옷과 외투를 입고, 발에는 낮은 가죽신 두 짝을 신고 있었다. 그리고 그를 둘러싸고 푸른 자줏빛 견대가 있었으니, 그 네 귀퉁이마다 황금 사과가 달려 있었다.\n\n\"이는 게라인트 공자였으니, 그 또한 늦게, 사냥 복장도 황금 자루의 칼 하나 외에는 무기도 걸치지 않은 채, 얕은 여울을 번쩍이며 재빨리 지나왔더라.\" ─「이니드」.\n\n그리고 그의 말은 위엄 있고, 날래고, 당당하게 걸음을 옮겼다. 그리고 그는 귀네비어를 따라잡고, 그녀에게 인사를 올렸다. \"하늘이 그대를 번성케 하시기를, 게라인트여.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그런데 그대는 어찌하여 그대의 주군과 함께 사냥을 가지 않았소?\" \"그분께서 언제 떠나셨는지 몰랐기 때문입니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"나도 의아하구려.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그분께서 나도 모르게 어찌 떠나실 수 있었는지. 하지만 그대, 오 젊은이여, 그대는 온 왕국에서 내가 둘 수 있는 가장 흡족한 길동무로다. 어쩌면 나는 그들보다도 사냥을 더 즐기게 될지도 모르오. 우리는 뿔나팔이 울릴 때 그 소리를 들을 것이고, 개들이 풀려나 짖기 시작할 때 그 소리를 들을 것이니 말이오.\"\n\n그리하여 그들은 숲의 가장자리로 가서, 그곳에 섰다. \"이 자리에서,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"개들이 풀려나는 때를 우리는 들을 것이오.\" 그러자 그 순간 그들은 큰 소리를 들었다. 그래서 그들은 그 소리가 나는 쪽을 바라보았고, 한 난쟁이가 말 위에 올라탄 것을 보았으니, 그 말은 위엄 있고 거품을 물고 껑충거리며 힘차고 활기에 넘쳤다. 그리고 난쟁이의 손에는 채찍이 들려 있었다. 그리고 난쟁이 가까이에 그들은 아름다운 흰 말에 올라탄 귀부인을 보았으니, 그 말은 차분하고 위엄 있는 걸음걸이였다. 그리고 그녀는 금실로 짠 비단옷을 입고 있었다. 그리고 그녀 가까이에는 큰 몸집의 군마에 올라탄 기사 하나가 있었으니, 그 자신에게도 말에게도 무겁고 번쩍이는 갑주를 둘렀다. 그리고 참으로 그들은 그토록 놀라운 크기의 기사도, 말도, 갑주도 일찍이 본 적이 없었다.\n\n\"게라인트여,\" 귀네비어가 말했다. \"저기 저 키 큰 기사의 이름을 그대는 아오?\" \"저는 그를 모릅니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"그리고 그가 두른 낯선 갑주 때문에 저는 그의 얼굴도 이목구비도 볼 수가 없습니다.\" \"가거라, 시녀야.\" 귀네비어가 말했다. \"그리고 저 난쟁이에게 저 기사가 누구인지 물어보아라.\" 그러자 시녀가 난쟁이에게로 다가갔다. 그리고 그녀는 난쟁이에게 그 기사가 누구인지 물었다. \"네게 말하지 않겠다.\" 그가 대답했다. \"네가 그토록 무례하니,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"내가 직접 그분께 여쭙겠다.\" \"내 믿음을 걸고, 너는 그분께 묻지 못한다.\" 그가 말했다. \"어찌하여 안 된다는 것이냐?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"네가 내 주군께 말을 걸기에 합당할 만큼 신분이 높지 못하기 때문이다.\" 그러자 시녀가 말 머리를 기사 쪽으로 돌렸고, 그러자 난쟁이가 손에 든 채찍으로 그녀의 얼굴과 두 눈을 가로질러 후려쳐서, 피가 흘러나왔다. 그리고 시녀는 자기가 입은 상처를 호소하며 귀네비어에게 돌아왔다. \"저 난쟁이가 그대를 참으로 무례하게 다루었구나.\" 게라인트가 말하며, 자기 검의 자루에 손을 얹었다. 그러나 그는 스스로 헤아려 보고, 그 난쟁이를 죽인다 한들 그것은 자기에게 복수가 되지 못하고, 갑주를 두르지 않은 채 갑주를 두른 기사에게 공격당할 뿐임을 생각하였다. 그래서 그는 자제하였다.\n\n\"부인,\" 그가 말했다. \"허락하신다면 제가 그를 따라가겠습니다. 그러면 끝내 그가 어떤 사람이 사는 곳에 이를 것이고, 거기서 저는 빌리든 저당을 잡히든 무기를 얻어, 저 기사와 맞설 수 있을 것입니다.\" \"가시오.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그리고 좋은 무기를 얻기 전에는 그를 공격하지 마시오. 그대의 소식을 들을 때까지 나는 그대 때문에 몹시 마음 졸일 것이오.\" \"제가 살아 있다면,\" 그가 말했다. \"내일 오후까지 제 소식을 들으실 것입니다.\" 그리고 그 말과 함께 그는 떠났다.\n\n그리고 그들이 택한 길은 카에르레온 궁궐 아래로, 우스크 강의 여울을 건너는 길이었다. 그리고 그들은 곱고 평탄하며 높다란 능선을 따라가다가 한 고을에 이르렀고, 고을 끝자락에서 그들은 요새와 성을 보았다. 그리고 기사가 고을을 지날 때 모든 사람이 일어나 그에게 인사하며 환영하였다. 그리고 게라인트가 고을에 들어섰을 때, 그는 자기가 보는 이들 가운데 아는 사람이 있는지 보려고 집집마다 살폈다. 그러나 그는 아무도 알지 못했고, 아무도 그를 알지 못해, 빌려서든 저당으로든 그에게 무기를 얻게 해 주는 호의를 베풀 사람이 없었다. 그리고 그가 본 집마다 사람들과 무기들과 말들로 가득했다. 그리고 그들은 방패를 닦고, 검을 갈고, 갑주를 씻고, 말에 편자를 박고 있었다. 그리고 기사와 귀부인과 난쟁이는 고을 안에 있는 성으로 말을 타고 올라갔고, 성 안의 모두가 기뻐하였다. 그리고 흉벽과 성문에서 사람들은 그들을 맞이하고 자기들의 기쁨을 보이려는 열망에 목을 위태롭게 내밀었다.\n\n게라인트는 그 기사가 성에 머무를지 보려고 그곳에 섰다. 그리고 그가 그러하리라고 확신했을 때, 그는 주위를 둘러보았다. 그리고 고을에서 조금 떨어진 곳에 그는 폐허가 된 옛 궁궐을 보았으니, 그 안에는 무너져 가는 홀이 하나 있었다.\n\n\"그리고 높이, 한 자락 망루 계단이, 이제는 잠잠해진 발길들에 닳은 채, 햇빛 아래 헐벗고 굽이쳐 올랐더라.\" ─「이니드」.\n\n그리고 그는 고을에서 아무도 알지 못했으므로, 옛 궁궐 쪽으로 갔다. 그리고 그가 궁궐 가까이에 이르렀을 때, 그는 백발의 노인 하나가 누더기 옷을 입고 그 곁에 서 있는 것을 보았다. 그리고 게라인트는 그를 한결같이 응시하였다. 그러자 백발의 노인이 그에게 말했다. \"젊은이여, 어찌하여 그대는 수심에 잠겨 있는가?\" \"제가 수심에 잠긴 것은,\" 그가 말했다. \"오늘 밤을 어디서 보낼지 알지 못하기 때문입니다.\" \"이리로 나아오겠는가, 무사여.\" 그가 말했다. \"그러면 그대를 위해 마련할 수 있는 가장 좋은 것을 그대가 얻으리라.\" 그리하여 게라인트는 앞으로 나아갔다. 그리고 백발의 노인이 홀로 가는 길을 인도하였다. 그리고 홀에서 그는 말에서 내려, 그곳에 자기 말을 두었다. 그러고 나서 그는 백발의 노인과 함께 위층 방으로 올라갔다. 그리고 그 방에서 그는 한 늙은 여인이 방석 위에 앉아 있는 것을 보았으니, 그 여인에게는 낡고 해진 옷이 걸쳐져 있었다. 하지만 그가 보기에 그녀는 젊음이 한창이던 때에는 고왔으리라 싶었다. 그리고 그녀 곁에는 한 처녀가 있었으니, 그녀에게는 낡고 해지기 시작한 윗옷과 너울이 걸쳐져 있었다. 그리고 참으로 그는 그녀보다 더 고움과 우아함과 아름다움이 가득한 처녀를 본 적이 없었다. 그리고 백발의 노인이 처녀에게 말했다. \"이 젊은이의 말을 돌볼 시종은 너 말고는 없구나.\" \"제가 할 수 있는 가장 좋은 시중을 들겠습니다.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그분에게도 그분의 말에게도요.\" 그리고 처녀는 젊은이의 무장을 풀어 준 뒤, 그의 말에게 짚과 곡식을 주었다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 방으로 돌아왔다. 그리고 백발의 노인이 처녀에게 말했다. \"고을로 가서, 음식이든 음료든 네가 찾을 수 있는 가장 좋은 것을 이리로 가져오너라.\" \"기꺼이 그리하겠습니다, 아버님.\" 그녀가 말했다. 그리고 처녀는 고을로 갔다. 그리고 처녀가 고을에 가 있는 동안 그들은 함께 이야기를 나누었다. 그리고, 보라, 처녀가 한 젊은이와 함께 돌아왔으니, 그 젊은이는 등에 좋은 벌꿀술이 가득 든 가죽 술병과 어린 황소의 사분의 일을 지고 있었다. 그리고 처녀의 손에는 흰 빵이 한 아름 들려 있었고, 너울 속에는 고운 빵이 약간 있었으며, 그녀는 방으로 들어왔다. \"이보다 더 좋은 것을 얻지는 못했을 것입니다.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그리고 더 좋은 것이라면 제게 맡겨 주지도 않았을 것입니다.\" \"이만하면 충분하오.\" 게라인트가 말했다. 그리고 그들은 고기를 삶게 하였다. 그리고 음식이 준비되자 그들은 자리에 앉았다. 그리고 그 자리는 이러하였다. 게라인트는 백발의 노인과 그 아내 사이에 앉았고, 처녀가 그들의 시중을 들었다. 그리고 그들은 먹고 마셨다.\n\n그리고 그들이 식사를 마쳤을 때, 게라인트는 백발의 노인과 이야기를 나누었고, 우선 그는 자기가 있는 이 궁궐이 누구의 것이었는지 물었다. \"참으로,\" 그가 말했다. \"그것을 지은 것은 바로 나였고, 그대가 본 도시와 성도 역시 내 것이었소.\" \"아아!\" 게라인트가 말했다. \"어찌하여 그대는 이제 그것들을 잃으셨습니까?\" \"나는 이것들과 더불어 큰 백작령도 잃었소.\" 그가 말했다. \"그리고 내가 그것들을 잃은 사연은 이러하오. 나에게는 조카가 있었으니, 내 아우의 아들이었소. 그래서 나는 그의 재산을 돌보아 주었소. 그러나 그가 그것을 차지하려고 안달하더니, 나에게 전쟁을 걸어, 제 것뿐 아니라 이 성을 제외한 내 영지까지도 내게서 빼앗아 갔소.\" \"선하신 어르신,\" 게라인트가 말했다. \"방금 그 기사와 귀부인과 난쟁이가 어찌하여 고을에 들어왔는지, 그리고 제가 본 그 준비와 무기를 정돈하는 일이 무엇인지 말씀해 주시겠습니까?\" \"그리하겠소.\" 그가 말했다. \"그 준비는 내일 젊은 백작이 열 시합을 위한 것이오. 그것은 이러하오. 여기 있는 풀밭 한가운데에 두 개의 갈래 기둥을 세우고, 그 두 갈래 기둥 위에 은 막대를 얹고, 그 은 막대 위에 새매를 두어, 그 새매를 두고 마상 시합이 벌어질 것이오. 그리고 그 시합에는 그대가 도시에서 본 모든 무리, 곧 사람들과 말들과 무기들이 나아갈 것이오. 그리고 사람마다 자기가 가장 사랑하는 귀부인과 함께 나아갈 것이오. 그리고 자기가 가장 사랑하는 귀부인이 곁에 있지 않고서는 아무도 그 새매를 두고 겨룰 수 없소. 그리고 그대가 본 그 기사는 지난 두 해 동안 그 새매를 차지하였소. 그리고 만일 그가 세 번째 해에도 그것을 차지한다면, 그때부터 그는 새매의 기사라 불릴 것이오.\" \"어르신,\" 게라인트가 말했다. \"귀네비어의 시녀가 그 난쟁이에게서 받은 모욕을 두고, 이 기사에 관하여 제게 주실 조언은 무엇입니까?\" 그리고 게라인트는 백발의 노인에게 그 시녀가 받은 모욕이 무엇이었는지 이야기하였다. \"그대에게 조언하기란 쉽지 않소. 그대에게는 그대를 위해 겨룰 부인도 처녀도 없으니 말이오. 그렇지만 여기 무기가 있으니 그대가 가질 수 있고, 또한 내 말도 있으니, 만일 그것이 그대 자신의 말보다 나아 보인다면 가지시오.\" \"아, 어르신,\" 그가 말했다. \"하늘이 그대에게 갚아 주시기를! 그러나 제가 익숙한 제 말과 더불어 어르신의 무기면 제게 충분합니다. 그리고 만일 내일 정해진 때가 오면, 어르신, 어르신의 따님이신 저 처녀를 위해 도전하도록 제게 허락해 주신다면, 저는 만일 그 시합에서 살아남는다면 평생토록 그 처녀를 사랑하기로 맹세하겠습니다.\" \"기꺼이 그대에게 허락하겠소.\" 백발의 노인이 말했다. \"그리고 그대가 그렇게 결심하니, 그대의 말과 무기가 내일 동틀 무렵 준비되어 있어야 하오. 그때 새매의 기사가 선포를 하고, 자기가 가장 사랑하는 귀부인에게 새매를 가지라 할 것이기 때문이오. 그리고 만일 누군가 그녀에게 그것을 거절한다면, 그는 힘으로 그녀의 주장을 지킬 것이오. 그러므로,\" 백발의 노인이 말했다. \"그대는 동틀 녘에 그곳에 있어야 하고, 우리 세 사람이 그대와 함께 있을 것이오.\" 그리하여 일이 그렇게 정해졌다.\n\n그리고 밤이 되자 그들은 잠자리에 들었다. 그리고 동이 트기 전에 그들은 일어나 몸치장을 했다. 그리고 날이 밝을 무렵에는 네 사람 모두 풀밭에 나가 있었다. 그리고 그곳에 새매의 기사가 선포를 하며, 자기 연인에게 새매를 가지라 하고 있었다. \"그것을 갖지 마시오.\" 게라인트가 말했다. \"여기 더 곱고, 더 고귀하고, 더 어여쁘며, 그대보다 그것에 대한 권리가 더 나은 처녀가 있기 때문이오.\" 그러자 기사가 말했다. \"만일 그대가 그 새매가 그녀에게 마땅하다고 주장한다면, 앞으로 나와 나와 싸움을 벌이라.\" 그리고 게라인트는 풀밭 위쪽으로 나아갔으니, 그 자신에게도 그의 말에게도 무겁고 녹슬고 볼품없는 모양의 갑주를 두르고 있었다. 그러자 그들은 서로 맞부딪쳤고, 한 묶음의 창을 부러뜨렸다. 그리고 그들은 두 번째 묶음을, 또 세 번째 묶음을 부러뜨렸다. 그리고 백작과 그 일행이 새매의 기사가 우세를 점하는 것을 보자, 그들 사이에서는 함성과 환희와 흥겨움이 일었다. 그러나 백발의 노인과 그 아내와 그 딸은 슬퍼하였다. 그리고 백발의 노인은 게라인트가 창을 부러뜨릴 때마다 창을 대어 주었고, 난쟁이는 새매의 기사에게 창을 대어 주었다. 그러자 백발의 노인이 게라인트에게 말했다. \"오 무사여, 다른 누구도 그대를 거들지 않으니, 보시오, 여기 내가 기사 작위의 명예를 받던 날 내 손에 들려 있던 창이 있소. 그리고 그때부터 지금까지 나는 그것을 한 번도 부러뜨린 적이 없으며, 그것은 뛰어난 창끝을 지니고 있소.\" 그러자 게라인트는 백발의 노인에게 감사하며 그 창을 받았다. 그리고 그 순간 난쟁이 또한 자기 주군에게 창을 가져왔다. \"보십시오, 여기 그의 것에 못지않게 좋은 창이 있습니다.\" 난쟁이가 말했다. \"그리고 어떤 기사도 이자만큼 오래 주군께 버틴 적이 없음을 헤아리소서.\" \"내가 하늘을 두고 선언하건대,\" 게라인트가 말했다. \"죽음이 나를 이곳에서 속히 데려가지 않는 한, 저자는 네 시중으로 결코 더 나아지지 못하리라.\" 그리고 게라인트는 멀리서 자기 말을 그에게로 박차며, 그에게 경고를 하고 나서, 그에게 돌진하여, 방패 면에 어찌나 모질고 사납고 거센 일격을 가했던지, 그것을 둘로 쪼개고, 그의 갑주를 부수고, 안장끈을 끊어, 그와 그의 안장이 둘 다 말의 엉덩이를 넘어 땅바닥에 나가떨어졌다. 그리고 게라인트는 재빨리 말에서 내렸다. 그리고 그는 노하여, 검을 뽑아, 그에게 사납게 돌진하였다. 그러자 기사 또한 일어나, 게라인트를 향해 검을 뽑았다. 그리고 그들은 서로의 검에서 별과 같은 불꽃이 튈 때까지 발을 디딘 채 검으로 싸웠다. 그리고 피와 땀이 그들의 눈에서 빛을 가릴 때까지 그렇게 싸움을 이어 갔다. 마침내 게라인트는 자기 온 힘을 불러일으켜, 그 기사의 정수리를 내리쳐, 그의 머리 갑주를 모조리 부수고, 살과 살갗을 두개골에 이르도록 모조리 베어, 끝내 뼈까지 다치게 하였다.\n\n그러자 기사는 무릎을 꿇고, 손에서 검을 내던지고, 게라인트에게 자비를 빌었다. \"참으로,\" 그가 말했다. \"나는 나의 지나친 만용과 나의 교만을 버리고, 그대의 자비를 구하오. 그리고 만일 내 죄를 위해 하늘에 나를 의탁하고 사제와 이야기할 시간을 얻지 못한다면, 그대의 자비는 내게 별 소용이 없을 것이오.\" \"이 조건으로 그대에게 은혜를 베풀겠노라.\" 게라인트가 말했다. \"곧 그대가 아서의 아내 귀네비어에게 가서, 그대의 난쟁이에게서 그녀의 시녀가 받은 모욕에 대해 그녀에게 보상하는 것이오. 그대는 이곳을 떠나는 때부터 귀네비어 앞에 이를 때까지 말에서 내리지 말고, 그녀에게 아서의 궁정에서 판결될 바대로 속죄하라.\" \"이를 기꺼이 행하겠소. 그런데 그대는 누구요?\" \"나는 에르빈의 아들 게라인트요. 그대도 그대가 누구인지 밝히라.\" \"나는 누드의 아들 에데이른이오.\" 그러고 나서 그는 자기 말에 몸을 던져, 아서의 궁정으로 나아갔다. 그리고 그가 가장 사랑하는 귀부인이 그의 앞에 갔고, 난쟁이도 크게 탄식하며 갔다.\n\n그러자 젊은 백작과 그 무리가 게라인트에게로 와서, 그에게 인사를 올리고, 자기 성으로 그를 청하였다. \"나는 갈 수 없소.\" 게라인트가 말했다. \"지난밤에 내가 있던 곳, 그곳에 오늘 밤에도 있겠소.\" \"그대가 내 청을 받아들이지 않으니, 내가 그대를 위해 마련할 수 있는 모든 것을 넉넉히 받으시오. 그리고 그대의 피로와 그대에게 깃든 노곤함에서 그대를 회복시킬 연고를 그대를 위해 마련하라 이르겠소.\" \"하늘이 그대에게 갚아 주시기를.\" 게라인트가 말했다. \"그러면 내 숙소로 가겠소.\" 그리하여 게라인트와 이니윌 백작과 그 아내와 그 딸이 갔다. 그리고 그들이 그 옛 저택에 이르렀을 때, 젊은 백작의 집안 하인들과 시종들이 도착하여 있었고, 모든 방을 정돈하여 짚을 깔고 불을 지펴 단장해 두었다. 그리고 머지않아 연고가 준비되었고, 게라인트가 그곳에 와서, 그들은 그의 머리를 씻겼다. 그러자 젊은 백작이 자기 시종들 가운데 마흔 명의 명예로운 기사와, 그 시합에 청해진 자들과 함께 왔다. 그리고 게라인트가 연고를 바르는 일에서 돌아왔다. 그리고 백작은 그에게 홀로 가서 식사하기를 청하였다. \"이니윌 백작은 어디 있소,\" 게라인트가 말했다. \"그리고 그 아내와 그 딸은 어디 있소?\" \"저쪽 방에 있습니다.\" 백작의 시종장이 말했다. \"백작께서 그들을 위해 가져오게 하신 옷으로 몸치장을 하고 있습니다.\" \"그 처녀로 하여금 몸치장을 하지 못하게 하시오.\" 그가 말했다. \"오직 그녀의 윗옷과 너울만 입은 채로, 아서의 궁정에 이르러 귀네비어에게서 자기가 고르는 그런 옷을 입게 될 때까지 그러하시오.\" 그리하여 그 처녀는 몸치장을 하지 않았다.\n\n그러자 그들 모두 홀로 들어가, 손을 씻고, 식사 자리에 앉았다. 그리고 그들은 이렇게 자리하였다. 게라인트의 한쪽에는 젊은 백작이 앉고, 그 너머에 이니윌 백작이 앉았으며, 게라인트의 다른 쪽에는 처녀와 그 어머니가 앉았다. 그리고 이들 다음에는 모두 명예의 차례에 따라 앉았다. 그리고 그들은 먹었다. 그리고 그들은 넉넉히 시중을 받았으며, 갖가지 종류의 선물을 풍성하게 받았다. 그러고 나서 그들은 함께 이야기를 나누었다. 그리고 젊은 백작은 게라인트에게 이튿날 자기를 방문해 달라고 청하였다. \"하늘을 두고, 나는 그러지 않겠소.\" 게라인트가 말했다. \"내일 나는 이 처녀와 함께 아서의 궁정으로 가겠소. 그리고 이니윌 백작이 가난과 곤경에 처해 있는 한, 내게는 그것으로 충분하오. 그리고 나는 무엇보다 그의 생계를 보태고자 가는 것이오.\" \"아, 무사여,\" 젊은 백작이 말했다. \"이니윌 백작이 자기 재산을 잃은 것은 내 잘못 때문이 아니오.\" \"내 믿음을 걸고,\" 게라인트가 말했다. \"죽음이 나를 이곳에서 속히 데려가지 않는 한, 그는 그것 없이 지내지 않을 것이오.\" \"오 무사여,\" 그가 말했다. \"나와 이니윌 사이의 불화에 관해서라면, 나는 기꺼이 그대의 조언에 따르고, 우리 사이에서 그대가 옳다고 판단하는 바에 동의하겠소.\" \"내가 그대에게 청하는 것은 다만,\" 게라인트가 말했다. \"그의 것을 그에게 되돌려 주는 것이오. 그리고 그가 자기 재산을 잃은 때부터 바로 오늘까지 받았어야 할 것을 되돌려 주는 것이오.\" \"그것을 그대를 위해 기꺼이 행하겠소.\" 그가 대답했다. \"그렇다면,\" 게라인트가 말했다. \"여기 이니윌에게 신종(臣從)의 의무를 진 자가 누구든, 앞으로 나와, 그 자리에서 그것을 이행하라.\" 그리고 모든 사람이 그렇게 하였다. 그리고 그 약정에 따라 그들은 그것을 지켰다. 그리고 그의 성과 그의 고을과 그의 모든 재산이 이니윌에게 되돌려졌다. 그리고 그는 자기가 잃었던 모든 것을, 가장 작은 보석에 이르기까지 되돌려 받았다.\n\n그러자 이니윌 백작이 게라인트에게 말했다. \"무사여,\" 그가 말했다. \"보시오, 그대가 시합에서 도전하며 위했던 그 처녀를. 내가 그녀를 그대에게 주노라.\" \"그녀는 나와 함께 아서의 궁정으로 갈 것이오.\" 게라인트가 말했다. \"그리고 아서와 귀네비어, 그분들이 뜻대로 그녀를 처분할 것이오.\" 그리고 이튿날 그들은 아서의 궁정으로 나아갔다. 게라인트에 관해서는 여기까지이다.\n\n————\n\n그런데 아서가 사슴을 사냥한 방식은 이러했다. 사람들과 사냥개들은 여러 사냥대로 나뉘었고, 개들은 사슴을 향해 풀려났다. 그리고 가장 마지막으로 풀려난 개는 아서가 가장 아끼는 개였으니, 이름은 카발이었다. 카발은 다른 모든 개들을 뒤로 제치고 사슴을 돌려세웠다. 그리고 두 번째로 방향을 돌린 사슴은 아서의 사냥대를 향해 달려왔다. 아서는 사슴에게 달려들었고, 다른 누군가의 손에 죽기 전에 사슴의 목을 베었다. 그러자 사람들은 사냥감을 잡았음을 알리는 죽음의 뿔나팔을 불었고, 모두가 그 주위로 모여들었다.\n\n그때 카디리아스가 아서에게 다가와 말했다. \"전하,\" 그가 말했다, \"보소서, 저편에 귀네비어 왕비님이 계시는데, 단 한 명의 시녀 말고는 아무도 모시고 있지 않습니다.\" \"코의 아들 길다스와 궁정의 모든 학자들에게,\" 아서가 말했다, \"귀네비어를 궁전까지 모시도록 명하라.\" 그러자 그들은 그대로 했다.\n\n그런 다음 그들은 모두 길을 나섰는데, 사슴 머리를 누구에게 주어야 할지를 두고 서로 의논하며 갔다. 어떤 이는 그것이 자신이 가장 사랑하는 여인에게 주어지기를 바랐고, 또 어떤 이는 자신이 가장 흠모하는 여인에게 주어지기를 바랐다. 그렇게 하여 그들은 궁전에 이르렀다. 그리고 아서와 귀네비어가 그들이 사슴 머리를 두고 다투는 소리를 들었을 때, 귀네비어가 아서에게 말했다. \"전하, 사슴 머리에 관한 저의 의견은 이러합니다. 에르빈의 아들 게라인트가 그가 떠난 임무에서 돌아올 때까지 그것을 누구에게도 주지 마소서.\" 그러고는 귀네비어가 아서에게 그 임무가 무엇인지를 이야기해 주었다. \"기꺼이 그리하리다,\" 아서가 말했다. 그리고 귀네비어는 게라인트가 돌아오는지 살피도록 성벽 위에 망꾼을 세워 두게 했다. 그러고는 정오가 지난 뒤에 그들은 말 위에 탄 볼품없는 작은 사내 하나를 보았고, 그 뒤에는 역시 말을 탄 귀부인인지 처녀인지 모를 여인이 있었으며, 그 뒤에는 큰 체구의 기사가 몸을 굽힌 채 머리를 낮고 슬프게 떨구고서, 부서지고 쓸모없는 갑옷을 걸치고 따라오고 있었다.\n\n그들이 성문에 가까이 오기 전에 망꾼 한 사람이 귀네비어에게 가서, 어떤 사람들이 보이며 그들이 어떤 모습을 하고 있는지를 아뢰었다. \"저들이 누구인지는 알지 못하겠나이다,\" 그가 말했다. \"하지만 나는 안다,\" 귀네비어가 말했다. \"이는 게라인트가 뒤쫓던 그 기사로다. 그리고 내 생각에 그가 제 발로 이곳에 오는 것은 아닐 것이다. 게라인트가 그를 따라잡아, 시녀가 당한 모욕을 끝까지 갚은 것이리라.\" 그러자 보라, 문지기 하나가 귀네비어가 있는 자리로 왔다. \"왕비님,\" 그가 말했다, \"성문에 기사 하나가 있사온데, 보기에 그토록 가련한 모습을 한 사람은 일찍이 본 적이 없나이다. 그가 걸친 갑옷은 비참하고 부서져 있으며, 그 위에는 갑옷 본래의 색보다 핏빛이 더 두드러져 보이옵니다.\" \"그의 이름을 아느냐?\" 그녀가 물었다. \"압니다,\" 그가 말했다. \"그는 자신이 누드의 아들 에데인이라 하옵니다.\" 그러자 그녀가 대답했다. \"나는 그를 알지 못한다.\"\n\n그리하여 귀네비어가 그를 맞으러 성문으로 나갔고, 그가 들어왔다. 비록 그가 무례한 난쟁이를 데리고 있긴 했으나, 귀네비어는 그가 처한 형편을 보고 가엾게 여겼다. 그때 에데인이 귀네비어에게 인사를 올렸다. \"하늘이 그대를 지켜 주기를,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"왕비님,\" 그가 말했다, \"에르빈의 아들이자 왕비님의 가장 훌륭하고 용맹한 신하인 게라인트가 안부를 전하나이다.\" \"그가 그대를 만났느냐?\" 그녀가 물었다. \"그렇습니다,\" 그가 말했다, \"그리고 그것은 제게 이로운 일이 아니었사옵니다. 허나 그것은 그의 잘못이 아니라 저의 잘못이었나이다, 왕비님. 게라인트는 왕비님께 정중히 안부를 전하며, 왕비님의 시녀가 난쟁이에게서 받은 모욕에 대해 왕비님의 뜻에 따르도록, 저를 이리로 오게 하였습니다.\" \"그렇다면 그가 어디에서 그대를 따라잡았느냐?\" \"지금은 카디프라 불리는 고을에서, 저희가 참새매를 두고 마상 창시합을 벌이며 겨루던 그 자리에서였습니다. 게라인트가 그 시합에서 창을 겨룬 것은 이니윌 백작의 딸인 처녀의 사랑을 증명하기 위함이었나이다. 그리하여 저희는 서로 맞붙었고, 그가 저를 이렇게, 왕비님께서 보시는 대로 만들어 놓았습니다.\" \"기사여,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"게라인트가 언제쯤 이곳에 이르리라 생각하느냐?\" \"내일이면, 왕비님, 그가 그 처녀와 함께 이곳에 이르리라 생각하옵니다.\"\n\n그때 아서가 그들에게로 왔다. 그러자 그가 아서에게 인사를 올렸고, 아서는 그를 오랫동안 바라보며 그의 그런 모습에 놀랐다. 그리고 그를 아는 듯하여, 그에게 물었다. \"그대는 누드의 아들 에데인인가?\" \"그렇습니다, 전하,\" 그가 말했다, \"그리고 저는 많은 고난을 만나 견디기 힘든 상처를 입었나이다.\" 그러고는 아서에게 자신의 모든 일을 이야기했다. \"그렇구나,\" 아서가 말했다, \"내가 들은 바로는 귀네비어가 그대에게 자비를 베푸는 것이 마땅하겠다.\" \"전하께서 바라시는 그 자비를,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"그에게 베풀겠나이다. 제게 모욕이 가해지는 것은 곧 전하 자신께 모욕이 가해지는 것과 같으니 말이옵니다.\" \"이렇게 하는 것이 가장 좋으리라,\" 아서가 말했다. \"이 사람이 살 수 있을지 알게 될 때까지 의술의 보살핌을 받게 하라. 그리고 그가 살아난다면, 궁정의 사람들이 가장 옳다고 판단하는 대로 보상을 치르게 하라. 그러나 그가 죽는다면, 에데인 같은 젊은이의 죽음은 처녀에게 가한 모욕의 대가로는 지나치게 무거우리라.\" \"그것이 제 마음에 듭니다,\" 귀네비어가 말했다. 그리고 아서는 모건 투드를 불러오게 했다. 그는 으뜸가는 의원이었다. \"누드의 아들 에데인을 데려가, 그를 위해 방을 마련하게 하고, 만일 내가 부상을 입었다면 내게 하듯이 그에게 의술의 도움을 베풀라. 그리고 그의 방에는 그를 괴롭히려는 자가 아무도 들어가지 못하게 하되, 그대와 그대의 제자들만이 그에게 치료약을 베풀게 하라.\" \"기꺼이 그리하겠나이다, 전하,\" 모건 투드가 말했다. 그때 집사장이 말했다. \"전하, 저 처녀는 어디로 보내는 것이 옳겠나이까?\" \"귀네비어와 그 시녀들에게 보내라,\" 그가 말했다. 그러자 집사장은 그녀를 그렇게 보냈다.\n\n\"그리고 그는 몸을 일으켜 아서의 궁정으로 말을 달렸고, 그곳에서 왕비는 그를 선뜻 용서하였네. 또한 젊은 몸이라 그는 스스로를 바꾸어 자라났으니, 제 죄와 그토록 닮아 보이던 그 죄, 아서의 조카 모드레드의 죄를 미워하기에 이르렀고, 마침내 큰 싸움터에서 왕을 위해 싸우다 쓰러졌네.\" —《에니드》\n\n이튿날 게라인트가 궁정을 향해 다가왔다. 그리고 귀네비어는 그가 불시에 당도하지 못하도록 성벽 위에 망꾼을 세워 두게 했다. 망꾼 하나가 귀네비어에게 왔다. \"왕비님,\" 그가 말했다, \"제 눈에 게라인트와 그와 함께 있는 처녀가 보이는 듯하옵니다. 그는 말 위에 있으나 평상복 차림이며, 처녀는 흰옷을 입은 듯, 아마포로 지은 옷을 걸친 것으로 보이옵니다.\" \"모든 여인들을 모으라,\" 귀네비어가 말했다, \"그리고 게라인트를 맞으러 나가, 그를 환영하며 기쁨을 빌어 주자.\" 그리하여 귀네비어가 게라인트와 그 처녀를 맞으러 나갔다. 게라인트가 귀네비어가 있는 자리에 이르렀을 때, 그는 그녀에게 인사를 올렸다. \"하늘이 그대를 번성케 하기를,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"그리고 그대를 환영하노라.\" \"왕비님,\" 그가 말했다, \"저는 왕비님의 뜻에 따라 왕비님께 보상을 얻어 드리기를 간절히 바랐나이다. 그리하여, 보소서, 여기 왕비님께서 복수를 이루시게 된 그 처녀가 있나이다.\" \"참으로,\" 귀네비어가 말했다, \"하늘의 환영이 그녀에게 임하기를. 그리고 우리가 그녀를 기쁘게 맞이하는 것이 마땅하도다.\" 그러고는 그들이 안으로 들어가 말에서 내렸다. 그리고 게라인트가 아서가 있는 자리로 가서 그에게 인사를 올렸다. \"하늘이 그대를 지켜 주기를,\" 아서가 말했다, \"그리고 하늘의 환영이 그대에게 임하기를. 그대가 누드의 아들 에데인을 무찔렀으니, 그대는 성공한 길을 걸어왔도다.\" \"저를 탓하지는 마소서,\" 게라인트가 말했다. \"저희가 벗이 되지 못한 것은 누드의 아들 에데인 자신의 오만함 때문이었나이다.\" \"자,\" 아서가 말했다, \"그대가 도전을 걸었다고 내가 들은 그 처녀는 어디에 있는가?\" \"귀네비어와 함께 그 방으로 갔나이다.\" 그러자 아서가 그 처녀를 보러 갔다. 그리고 아서와 그의 모든 동료들, 그의 온 궁정이 그 처녀를 보고 기뻐했다. 그리고 만일 그녀의 차림새가 그 아름다움에 어울렸더라면, 그녀보다 더 고운 처녀를 일찍이 본 적이 없으리라는 데 모두가 한마음이었다. 그리하여 아서는 그 처녀를 게라인트에게 내주었다. 그리고 두 사람 사이에 맺는 통상의 혼약이 게라인트와 그 처녀 사이에 맺어졌고, 귀네비어의 모든 의복 가운데 가장 빼어난 것이 그 처녀에게 주어졌다. 그렇게 차려입으니 그녀는 그녀를 바라보는 모든 이에게 아리땁고 우아하게 보였다. 그리고 그날과 그날 밤은 풍성한 음유의 가락과 넉넉한 술의 선물과 수많은 놀이로 보내졌다. 그리고 그들이 잠자리에 들 때가 되자 모두 잠자리로 갔다. 그리고 아서와 귀네비어의 침상이 있는 방에 게라인트와 에니드의 침상이 마련되었다. 그리고 그때부터 그녀는 그의 아내가 되었다. 그리고 이튿날 아서는 게라인트에게 권리를 주장하는 모든 이들을 넉넉한 선물로 만족시켰다. 그리고 그 처녀는 궁전에 거처를 정했고, 남녀를 막론하고 많은 동무가 있었으며, 브리튼 섬에서 그녀보다 더 존경받는 처녀는 없었다.\n\n그때 귀네비어가 말했다. \"내가 옳게 판단하였구나,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"사슴 머리에 관하여, 게라인트가 돌아올 때까지 그것을 누구에게도 주어서는 안 된다고 한 것 말이다. 그리고 보라, 여기 그것을 줄 마땅한 기회가 있도다. 그것을 이니윌의 딸이자 가장 빼어난 처녀인 에니드에게 주도록 하자. 그리고 나는 누구도 그것을 그녀에게 주는 것을 아까워하리라 믿지 않는다. 그녀와 이곳의 모든 사람 사이에는 오직 사랑과 우정만이 있을 따름이니 말이다.\" 이 말은 그들 모두에게서, 그리고 아서에게서도 큰 갈채를 받았다. 그리하여 사슴 머리는 에니드에게 주어졌다. 그리고 그로 인해 그녀의 명성은 더해 갔고, 그녀의 벗들은 전보다 더 많아졌다. 그리고 게라인트는 그때부터 사냥과 마상 창시합과 격렬한 겨룸을 사랑하게 되었으며, 그 모든 것에서 승리를 거두고 돌아왔다. 그리고 한 해가 가고, 두 해가 가고, 세 해째에도 그는 이렇게 나아가, 마침내 그의 명성이 온 왕국의 땅 위로 날아올랐다.\n\n그리고 어느 날, 아서가 우스크 강가의 카에를레온에서 궁정을 열고 있을 때였다. 보라, 그에게 사절들이 찾아왔으니, 지혜롭고 사려 깊으며 식견이 가득하고 말솜씨가 능란한 자들이었다. 그들이 아서에게 인사를 올렸다. \"하늘이 그대들을 번성케 하기를!\" 아서가 말했다. \"그런데 그대들은 어디에서 왔는가?\" \"저희는, 전하,\" 그들이 말했다, \"콘월에서 왔나이다. 저희는 전하의 숙부이신 쿠스텐닌의 아들 에르빈의 사절이며, 저희의 임무는 전하께 닿아 있나이다. 그분께서는 숙부가 조카에게 안부를 전하듯, 또 신하가 주군에게 안부를 전하듯 전하께 정중히 안부를 전하시나이다. 그리고 그분께서는 전하께 아뢰시기를, 당신께서 점점 무겁고 쇠약해지며 나이가 들어 가고 있다 하시옵니다. 그리고 이를 안 이웃의 족장들이 그분께 무례해지며, 그분의 땅과 재산을 탐내고 있나이다. 그리하여 그분께서는 전하께 간절히 청하시기를, 아들 게라인트가 그분께 돌아와 그분의 재산을 지키고 그 영지의 경계를 익히도록 허락하여 주시기를 바라시옵니다. 그리고 그분께서는 게라인트에게, 비록 마상 창시합에서 영광을 얻기는 하나 아무런 이득도 없는 그런 시합에서 청춘의 꽃과 한창때의 나날을 보내는 것보다는, 자신의 영지 경계를 지키는 데 그것을 쓰는 편이 더 나으리라 아뢰시옵니다.\"\n\n\"좋다,\" 아서가 말했다, \"가서 무장을 벗고 음식을 들며, 여독을 풀고 기운을 되찾으라. 그리고 그대들이 이곳을 떠나기 전에 답을 받게 되리라.\" 그리하여 그들은 먹으러 갔다. 그리고 아서는 게라인트를 자신에게서, 그리고 자신의 궁정에서 떠나보내는 것이 자신에게 견디기 힘든 일이 되리라 생각했다. 또한 그의 사촌의 아버지가 그렇게 할 수 없게 된 마당에, 자신의 영토와 그 경계를 지키러 가는 것을 그의 사촌이 가로막히는 것은 옳지 않다고 여겼다. 귀네비어와 그녀의 모든 여인들과 모든 시녀들의 슬픔과 아쉬움도 그에 못지않았으니, 그 처녀가 그들을 떠나리라는 두려움 때문이었다. 그리고 그날과 그날 밤은 풍성한 연회로 보내졌다. 그리고 아서는 게라인트에게 그 사절의 사연과, 콘월에서 사절들이 그에게 찾아온 까닭을 이야기했다. \"참으로,\" 게라인트가 말했다, \"그것이 제게 이로운 일이든 해로운 일이든, 전하, 이 사절의 일에 관해서는 전하의 뜻에 따르겠나이다.\" \"보라,\" 아서가 말했다, \"그대와 헤어지는 것이 내게는 슬픈 일이나, 내 충고는 그대가 그대 자신의 영토로 가서 살며 그 경계를 지키라는 것이다. 그리고 나의 충성스러운 자들 가운데서, 그리고 그대의 벗들과 무기를 함께한 동료들 가운데서 그대가 가장 아끼는 자들을 그대가 원하는 만큼 데려가 그대를 따르게 하라.\" \"하늘이 전하께 보답하시기를! 그리하겠나이다,\" 게라인트가 말했다. \"그대들 사이에서 무슨 이야기가,\" 귀네비어가 말했다, \"내 귀에 들리는 것입니까? 게라인트를 그의 고향으로 인도할 자들에 관한 것입니까?\" \"그렇소,\" 아서가 말했다. \"그렇다면 저도,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"저와 함께 있는 그 부인을 위한 동무들과 채비에 관해 헤아려 보아야 하겠습니다.\" \"그리하는 것이 좋겠소,\" 아서가 말했다.\n\n그리고 그날 밤 그들은 잠자리에 들었다. 이튿날 사절들은 떠나도록 허락받았고, 게라인트가 그들을 뒤따르리라는 말을 전해 들었다. 그리고 사흘째 되는 날 게라인트가 길을 나섰는데, 많은 이들이 그와 함께 갔다. 그와이르의 아들 가웨인, 아일랜드 왕의 아들 리오고네드, 부르고뉴 공작의 아들 온디아우, 프랑크인들의 통치자의 아들 그윌림, 브리타니 백작의 아들 호웰, 에브라우크의 아들 퍼시벌, 아서의 궁정 재판관 그위르, 베드라우드의 아들 베드위르, 키네르의 아들 카이, 프랑크인 오디아르, 그리고 누드의 아들 에데인이 그들이었다. 게라인트가 말했다. \"내 곁에 기사의 무리가 넉넉하리라 생각하노라.\" 그리하여 그들은 길을 나섰다. 세번 강을 향해 나아가는 그토록 훌륭한 무리는 일찍이 본 적이 없었다. 그리고 세번 강 건너편에는 쿠스텐닌의 아들 에르빈의 귀족들이, 그의 양아버지를 선두로 하여, 게라인트를 기쁘게 맞이하려 나와 있었다. 또한 궁정의 여인들 다수가 그의 어머니와 함께, 그의 아내이자 이니윌의 딸인 에니드를 맞이하러 나왔다. 그리고 온 궁정에 걸쳐, 또 온 나라에 걸쳐 게라인트로 인한 크나큰 기쁨과 환희가 있었으니, 그들이 그를 향해 품은 사랑의 크기 때문이요, 그가 그들 곁을 떠난 뒤로 얻은 명성의 크기 때문이며, 또한 그가 자신의 영토를 차지하고 그 경계를 지키러 돌아왔기 때문이었다. 그리하여 그들은 궁정에 이르렀다. 그리고 궁정에서 그들은 넉넉한 향응과 수많은 선물과 풍성한 술과 충분한 시중과 갖가지 놀이를 누렸다. 그리고 게라인트를 기리기 위해, 그 나라의 모든 으뜸가는 인물들이 그날 밤 그를 찾아오도록 초대되었다. 그리고 그들은 그날과 그날 밤을 더할 나위 없는 즐거움 속에서 보냈다. 그리고 이튿날 동틀 무렵 에르빈이 일어나 게라인트와 그를 따라온 고귀한 인물들을 불러들였다. 그리고 그가 게라인트에게 말했다. \"나는 쇠약하고 늙은 사람이로다. 그리고 내가 너와 나를 위해 이 영토를 지킬 수 있는 동안에는 그렇게 해 왔다. 허나 너는 젊고, 네 기력과 청춘의 꽃이 한창이로구나. 이제부터는 네가 네 재산을 지키도록 하여라.\" \"참으로,\" 게라인트가 말했다, \"제 뜻으로는 아버님께서 지금 영토에 대한 권한을 제 손에 넘겨주셔서는 안 되며, 또한 저를 아서의 궁정에서 떼어 놓으셔서도 안 되옵니다.\" \"네 손에 그것을 넘겨주겠노라,\" 에르빈이 말했다, \"그리고 오늘 너는 또한 네 백성들의 충성 서약을 받게 될 것이니라.\"\n\n그때 가웨인이 말했다. \"오늘은 청을 드릴 것이 있는 자들을 만족시켜 주시고, 내일 그대의 영토에 대한 충성 서약을 받으시는 편이 나으리다.\" 그리하여 청을 드릴 것이 있는 자들이 모두 한자리에 불려 모였다. 그리고 카디리아스가 그들에게 와서 그들의 청이 무엇인지 알아보았다. 그리고 저마다 자신이 바라는 바를 청했다. 그리고 아서의 수하들이 선물을 베풀기 시작했고, 곧이어 콘월 사람들이 와서 그들도 베풀었다. 그리고 저마다 선물을 베푸는 데 그토록 열심이었으므로 그들이 베푸는 데에 오래 걸리지 않았으며, 선물을 청하러 온 자들 가운데 만족하지 못한 채 떠난 이는 아무도 없었다. 그리고 그날과 그날 밤은 더할 나위 없는 즐거움 속에서 보내졌다.\n\n그리고 이튿날 동틀 무렵, 에르빈은 게라인트에게 청하기를, 자신이 와서 그들의 충성 서약을 받는 것이 그들에게 못마땅한지, 또 그들이 그에게 이의를 제기할 것이 있는지를 물어보도록 사람들을 보내라 했다. 그리하여 게라인트는 콘월 사람들에게 이를 묻기 위해 사절들을 보냈다. 그러자 그들은 모두 게라인트가 와서 자신들의 충성 서약을 받는 것이야말로 더없는 기쁨이요 영예라고 말했다. 그리하여 그는 그곳에 있는 자들의 충성 서약을 받았다. 그리고 그 이튿날 아서의 수하들이 떠나려 했다. \"아직 그대들이 떠나기에는 너무 이르오,\" 그가 말했다. \"내게 오기로 한 으뜸가는 사람들의 충성 서약을 다 받을 때까지 나와 함께 머무르시오.\" 그리하여 그들은 그가 그 일을 마칠 때까지 그와 함께 머물렀다. 그런 다음 그들은 아서의 궁정을 향해 길을 나섰다. 그리고 게라인트가 그들과 동행하러 갔고, 에니드도 디간위까지 함께 갔다. 그곳에서 그들은 헤어졌다. 그리고 부르고뉴 공작의 아들 온디아우가 게라인트에게 말했다. \"이제 가서 그대 영토의 가장 먼 곳까지 둘러보고, 그대 영지의 경계를 잘 살피시오. 그리고 그에 관해 어떤 어려움이 생기거든 그대의 동료들에게 사람을 보내시오.\" \"하늘이 그대에게 보답하시기를!\" 게라인트가 말했다. \"그리하겠소.\" 그리하여 게라인트는 자신의 영토 가장 먼 곳까지 나아갔다. 그리고 노련한 안내인들과 그 나라의 으뜸가는 사람들이 그와 함께 갔다. 그리고 그들이 그에게 보여 준 가장 먼 지점까지 그는 자신의 것으로 지켰다.\n\n————\n\n에르빈의 아들 게라인트 (계속)\n게라인트는 아서 왕의 궁정에 있을 때 하던 대로 마상 시합에 자주 참가했다. 그리하여 용맹하고 강력한 사내들과 교분을 맺었으며, 마침내 예전에 다른 곳에서 그러했던 것처럼 그곳에서도 큰 명성을 얻기에 이르렀다. 또한 그는 자신의 궁정과 동료들과 귀족들을 가장 훌륭한 말과 가장 훌륭한 무구로, 그리고 가장 좋고 가장 값진 보석으로 풍요롭게 했으며, 그의 명성이 왕국 전체의 얼굴 위로 날아갈 때까지 그치지 않았다.\n\n\"적의 채찍, 게라인트 앞에서 나는 거품으로 하얗게 물든 군마들을 보았노라, 그리고 전투의 함성 뒤에 무시무시한 격류를 보았노라.\" — 헨.\n\n사정이 이러함을 알게 되었을 때, 그는 안락과 쾌락을 사랑하기 시작했다. 그에게 맞설 만한 자가 아무도 없었기 때문이다. 그리고 그는 아내를 사랑했으며, 음유의 노래와 여흥과 더불어 궁궐에 머무는 것을 좋아했다. 그리하여 그는 아내의 방 안에 틀어박히기 시작했고, 그 밖의 어떤 것에서도 즐거움을 찾지 않았으니, 마침내 그는 사냥과 오락과 더불어 귀족들과의 우정마저 저버렸으며, 자신의 궁정에 있는 온 무리의 마음을 잃고 말았다. 그리고 그가 아내에 대한 사랑 때문에 그토록 완전히 그들과의 교제를 포기한 것을 두고, 궁궐의 거주자들 사이에서는 그에 관한 수군거림과 조롱이 일어났다.\n\n\"그들은 그에 관하여 비웃고 조롱하며 떠들어 대기 시작했으니, 그 사내다움이 모조리 사라져 한낱 아내에 대한 맹목적인 애정 속에 녹아 버린 군주에 대해 떠드는 것 같았다.\"\n\n이 소식이 에르빈에게 이르렀다. 그리고 에르빈은 이 일들을 듣고 나서 에니드에게 말을 건네어, 게라인트로 하여금 이렇게 행동하게 하고 그의 백성과 무리를 저버리게 한 것이 바로 그녀가 아니냐고 물었다. \"제가 아닙니다, 하늘에 고백하건대,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"제게는 이보다 더 가증스러운 것이 없습니다.\" 그러고도 그녀는 자신이 무엇을 해야 할지 알지 못했으니, 비록 이를 게라인트에게 털어놓기가 어려웠다고는 하나, 자신이 들은 바를 게라인트에게 경고하지 않고 듣기만 하는 것 또한 더 쉬운 일은 아니었기 때문이다. 그리하여 그녀는 몹시 슬퍼했다.\n\n여름철의 어느 아침, 그들은 침상 위에 있었고, 게라인트는 그 가장자리에 누워 있었다. 그리고 에니드는 유리 창문이 달린 방 안에서 잠을 이루지 못하고 있었으며, 해가 침상 위로 비치고 있었다. 옷이 그의 두 팔과 가슴에서 흘러내려 있었고, 그는 잠들어 있었다. 그러자 그녀는 그의 모습이 지닌 경이로운 아름다움을 바라보며 이렇게 말했다. \"아아! 이 두 팔과 이 가슴이 그 영광을, 그리고 한때 그토록 풍성하게 누렸던 무인의 명성을 잃게 한 것이 바로 나란 말인가!\" 그녀가 이 말을 하는 동안 눈에서 눈물이 떨어져 그의 가슴 위로 흘러내렸다. 그리고 그녀가 흘린 눈물과 그녀가 한 말이 그를 깨웠다. 또 다른 것이 그를 깨우는 데 일조했으니, 그것은 곧 그녀가 그를 생각하며 그렇게 말한 것이 아니라, 그녀가 그보다 다른 사내를 더 사랑하기 때문이며 다른 이와의 교제를 바라기 때문이라는 생각이었다. 그리하여 게라인트는 마음이 어지러워져 시종을 불렀다. 시종이 오자, \"어서 가서,\" 그가 말했다. \"내 말과 내 무구를 준비하여 채비를 갖추어라.\" 그러고 나서 에니드에게 말했다. \"그대도 일어나 채비를 갖추고, 그대의 말에도 마구를 갖추게 하라. 그리고 그대가 가진 것 중 가장 허름한 승마복을 입어라. 그리고 내게 화가 미칠지어다,\" 그가 말했다. \"그대가 한 말처럼 내가 그토록 완전히 힘을 잃었는지 알게 되기 전에는, 그대가 이곳으로 돌아온다면 말이다. 그리고 만일 그러하다면, 그때는 그대가 생각하던 그 사내와의 교제를, 그대가 바라던 그 교제를 찾기란 쉬운 일이 될 것이다.\" 그리하여 그녀는 일어나 가장 보잘것없는 옷을 입었다. \"저는 아무것도 모릅니다, 주군이시여,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"당신의 뜻을 말입니다.\" \"지금은 그대가 알 수도 없으리라,\" 그가 말했다.\n\n그러고 나서 게라인트는 에르빈을 만나러 갔다. \"주군이시여,\" 그가 말했다. \"저는 어떤 탐색에 나서려 하는데, 언제 돌아올 수 있을지 확실치 않습니다. 그러하오니 제가 돌아올 때까지 당신의 재산을 잘 살펴 주십시오.\" \"그리하마,\" 그가 말했다. \"허나 네가 그토록 갑작스레 떠난다는 것이 내게는 이상하구나. 그리고 누가 너와 함께 가겠느냐, 너는 혼자서 로에귀르 땅을 가로지를 만큼 강하지도 않은데?\" \"단 한 사람만이 저와 함께 갈 것입니다.\" \"하늘이 너를 인도하시기를, 내 아들아,\" 에르빈이 말했다. \"그리고 로에귀르에서 많은 이가 네게 따르기를 바라노라.\" 그러고 나서 게라인트는 자신의 말이 있는 곳으로 갔으니, 그것은 무겁고 빛나는 외국산 무구로 채비되어 있었다. 그리고 그는 에니드에게 말에 오르라 명하고, 앞서 나아가되 자신보다 한참 앞에 떨어져 가라고 했다. \"그리고 그대가 무엇을 보든, 나에 관하여 무엇을 듣든,\" 그가 말했다. \"그대는 돌아서지 마라. 그리고 내가 그대에게 말을 건네지 않는 한, 그대 또한 한마디도 하지 마라.\" 그리하여 그들은 길을 떠났다. 그리고 그는 가장 즐겁고 가장 많이 다니는 길을 택하지 않고, 가장 황량하며 도둑과 강도와 독을 품은 짐승들이 가장 들끓는 길을 택했다.\n\n그리고 그들은 큰길에 이르러 그 길을 따라가다가 광대한 숲을 보게 되었다. 그리고 그들은 무장한 기마병 넷이 그 숲에서 나오는 것을 보았다. 무장한 사내들은 그들을 보자 서로에게 말했다. \"여기 우리가 말 두 마리와 무구를, 그리고 귀부인 하나까지 차지할 좋은 기회가 있구나. 저토록 처량하고 무겁게 고개를 숙인 채 가는 외톨이 기사 하나를 상대로 이 일을 하기란 어렵지 않을 테니.\" 에니드는 이 말을 들었으나, 침묵하라고 일러 둔 게라인트에 대한 두려움 때문에 무엇을 해야 할지 알지 못했다. \"하늘의 복수가 제게 임하소서,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"다른 누구의 손이 아니라 그분의 손에 차라리 죽음을 받지 않으려 한다면 말입니다. 비록 그분이 저를 죽이신다 해도, 저는 그분에게 말하겠으니, 그분의 죽음을 목격하는 불행을 겪지 않기 위함입니다.\" 그리하여 그녀는 게라인트가 자신에게 가까이 올 때까지 기다렸다. \"주군이시여,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"당신에 관한 저 사내들의 말을 들으셨습니까?\" 그러자 그는 눈을 들어 노한 눈으로 그녀를 바라보았다. \"그대는 그저,\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 명한 대로 입을 다물고 있기만 하면 되었거늘. 나는 침묵을 바랄 뿐, 경고를 바라지 않는다. 그리고 비록 그대가 저 사내들의 손에 내가 패배하고 죽는 것을 보고 싶어 한다 해도, 나는 아무런 두려움도 느끼지 않는다.\" 그러자 그들 중 맨 앞의 자가 창을 겨누고 게라인트에게 달려들었다. 그리고 그는 그를 맞이했으니, 그것도 미약하게가 아니었다. 그는 찌르기를 자신의 옆으로 흘려보내는 한편, 그 기마병의 방패 한복판을 후려쳤다. 그 일격이 어찌나 강했던지 방패가 쪼개지고 갑옷이 부서졌으며, 게라인트의 창대가 한 큐빗 길이만큼 그의 몸을 꿰뚫고 지나가 그를 땅바닥으로 내동댕이쳤으니, 창의 길이만큼 그의 말 엉덩이 너머로 나가떨어졌다. 그러자 두 번째 기마병이 동료의 죽음에 분노하여 사납게 그를 공격했다. 그러나 게라인트는 단 한 번의 찌르기로 그 또한 거꾸러뜨리고, 앞서 한 것처럼 그를 죽였다. 그러자 세 번째 자가 그에게 덤벼들었고, 그는 마찬가지 방식으로 그를 죽였다. 그리고 네 번째 자 또한 그렇게 베어 버렸다. 이 모든 것을 보면서 처녀는 슬프고 비통했다. 게라인트는 말에서 내려, 자신이 죽인 사내들의 무구를 거두어 그들의 안장 위에 얹고, 그들의 말고삐를 한데 묶었다. 그러고는 다시 말에 올랐다. \"그대가 해야 할 일을 보라,\" 그가 말했다. \"이 네 마리 말을 데리고 그대 앞으로 몰아가며, 방금 내가 명한 대로 앞으로 나아가라. 그리고 내가 먼저 그대에게 말을 건네지 않는 한, 한마디도 내게 하지 마라. 그리고 하늘에 선언하노니,\" 그가 말했다. \"그대가 이렇게 하지 않는다면, 그것은 그대에게 화가 되리라.\" \"할 수 있는 데까지 하겠습니다, 주군이시여,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"당신의 뜻에 따라서요.\"\n\n그리하여 처녀는 그가 바란 대로 게라인트보다 앞서 나아갔다. 그리고 그토록 고귀한 처녀가 말들을 돌보느라 그토록 큰 수고를 겪는 것을 보는 일이, 그의 분노가 허락하는 한도 안에서 그를 비통하게 했다. 이윽고 그들은 숲에 이르렀으니, 그것은 깊고도 광대했으며, 그 숲에서 밤이 그들을 덮쳤다. \"아, 처녀여,\" 그가 말했다. \"앞으로 더 나아가려 애쓰는 것은 헛된 일이다.\" \"그럼, 주군이시여,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"당신이 바라시는 대로 하겠습니다.\" \"우리에게 가장 좋은 것은,\" 그가 대답했다. \"쉬면서 날이 밝기를 기다렸다가 여정을 이어 가는 것이리라.\" \"기꺼이 그리하겠습니다,\" 그녀가 말했다. 그리하여 그들은 그렇게 했다. 그는 직접 말에서 내린 뒤, 그녀를 말에서 내려 주었다. \"나는 피로 때문에 도무지 잠을 떨칠 수가 없구나,\" 그가 말했다. \"그러니 그대는 말들을 지키며 잠들지 마라.\" \"그리하겠습니다, 주군이시여,\" 그녀가 말했다. 그러고 나서 그는 갑옷을 입은 채 잠이 들었고, 그렇게 밤을 보냈으니, 그 계절에는 밤이 길지 않았다. 그리고 그녀는 날이 밝아 오는 것을 보자, 그가 깨어났는지 보려고 주위를 둘러보았는데, 그때 그가 깨어났다. 그러자 그는 일어나 그녀에게 말했다. \"말들을 데리고 나아가되, 어제와 마찬가지로 곧장 가라.\" 그리하여 그들은 숲을 떠나, 한쪽으로는 풀밭이 펼쳐지고 풀을 베는 자들이 그 풀밭에서 풀을 베고 있는 트인 들판에 이르렀다. 그리고 그들 앞에 강이 있어, 말들이 고개를 숙여 그 물을 마셨다. 그리고 그들은 가파른 비탈을 통해 강에서 올라왔는데, 거기서 목에 가방을 두른 호리호리한 젊은이를 만났다. 그들은 그 가방 안에 무언가 들어 있는 것을 보았으나, 그것이 무엇인지는 알지 못했다. 그리고 그는 손에 작은 푸른 물병을 들고 있었으며, 물병 주둥이 위에는 그릇이 하나 얹혀 있었다. 젊은이가 게라인트에게 인사했다. \"하늘이 너를 번성케 하시기를!\" 게라인트가 말했다. \"그리고 너는 어디서 오는 길이냐?\" \"저는,\" 그가 말했다. \"당신 앞에 놓인 도시에서 옵니다. 나으리,\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"제가 당신께서도 어디서 오시는지 여쭈어도 언짢지 않으시겠습니까?\" \"전혀 그렇지 않다. 나는 저쪽 숲을 지나 왔다.\" \"오늘 숲을 지나오신 것은 아니지요.\" \"그렇다,\" 그가 대답했다. \"우리는 어젯밤 숲속에 있었다.\" \"장담컨대,\" 젊은이가 말했다. \"어젯밤 그곳에서 당신의 처지는 그리 유쾌하지 않았을 테고, 고기도 마실 것도 없으셨겠지요.\" \"그래, 정말 그러했다,\" 그가 말했다. \"제 권유를 따르시어,\" 젊은이가 말했다. \"제게서 식사를 받으시겠습니까?\" \"어떤 종류의 식사 말이냐?\" 그가 물었다. \"저쪽 풀 베는 자들에게 보내는 아침 식사로, 빵과 고기와 포도주에 지나지 않습니다만, 나으리께서 원하신다면 그들은 그것을 조금도 받지 못할 것입니다.\" \"그리하겠다,\" 그가 말했다. \"하늘이 네게 그 보답을 내리시기를.\"\n\n그리하여 게라인트는 말에서 내렸고, 젊은이는 처녀를 말에서 내려 주었다. 그러고 나서 그들은 몸을 씻고 식사를 했다. 젊은이는 빵을 조각조각 자르고, 그들에게 마실 것을 주었으며, 그렇게 시중을 들었다. 그리고 그들이 식사를 마치자, 젊은이는 일어나 게라인트에게 말했다. \"나으리, 허락하신다면, 저는 이제 풀 베는 자들에게 줄 음식을 가지러 가겠습니다.\" \"먼저 마을로 가서,\" 게라인트가 말했다. \"네가 아는 가장 좋은 곳에, 그리고 말들에게 가장 편리한 곳에 내가 묵을 숙소를 잡아 다오. 그리고 네 봉사와 네 선물에 대한 보답으로, 어느 말이든 어느 무구든 네가 고르는 것을 가지거라.\" \"하늘이 당신께 보답하시기를, 나으리!\" 젊은이가 말했다. \"그리고 이것이라면 제가 당신께 베푼 것보다 훨씬 큰 봉사라도 충분히 갚을 만합니다.\" 그리하여 젊은이는 마을로 가서 자신이 아는 가장 좋고 가장 쾌적한 숙소를 잡았다. 그러고 나서 그는 말과 무구를 챙겨 궁궐로 가, 백작이 있는 곳으로 나아가 자신의 모든 일을 그에게 이야기했다. \"저는 이제 가겠습니다, 나으리,\" 그가 말했다. \"그 기사를 만나 그의 숙소로 안내하기 위해서요.\" \"기꺼이 가거라,\" 백작이 말했다. \"그리고 그가 그렇게 온다면, 이곳에서 참으로 기쁘게 맞아들이겠노라.\" 그리하여 젊은이는 게라인트를 만나러 가, 백작이 자신의 궁궐에서 그를 기꺼이 맞으리라고 그에게 전했다. 그러나 게라인트는 자신의 숙소로만 가려 했다. 그리고 그는 짚과 휘장이 넉넉히 갖추어진 훌륭한 방을 얻었고, 말들을 위해서도 넓고 편리한 자리를 얻었으며, 젊은이는 그것들을 위해 먹이를 넉넉히 마련해 주었다. 그들이 무장을 벗은 뒤, 게라인트는 에니드에게 이렇게 말했다. \"가서,\" 그가 말했다. \"방의 다른 쪽으로 가서, 이쪽 집안으로는 오지 마라. 그리고 그대가 원한다면 이 집의 여인을 그대에게 불러도 좋다.\" \"그리하겠습니다, 주군이시여,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"당신이 말씀하신 대로요.\" 그러자 그 집의 사내가 게라인트에게 와서 그를 환영했다. 그리고 그들이 먹고 마신 뒤, 게라인트는 잠이 들었고, 에니드 또한 그러했다.\n\n저녁이 되자, 보라, 백작이 그의 명예로운 기사 열둘을 데리고 게라인트를 방문하러 왔다. 그러자 게라인트는 일어나 그를 환영했다. 그러고 나서 그들 모두는 명예의 서열에 따라 자리에 앉았다. 백작은 게라인트와 이야기를 나누며 그의 여정의 목적을 물었다. \"제게는 그런 것이 없습니다,\" 그가 대답했다. \"모험을 찾고 제 마음 가는 대로 따르는 것 말고는요.\" 그러자 백작은 에니드에게 눈길을 던지더니, 그녀를 뚫어지게 바라보았다. 그리고 그는 그녀보다 더 아름답거나 더 어여쁜 처녀를 본 적이 없다고 생각했다. 그리하여 그는 자신의 온 생각과 애정을 그녀에게 쏟았다. 그러고는 게라인트에게 물었다. \"저쪽 처녀가 당신과 떨어져 있는 것이 보이니, 제가 가서 저 처녀와 이야기를 나누어도 좋겠습니까?\" \"기꺼이 좋습니다,\" 그가 말했다. 그리하여 백작은 처녀가 있는 곳으로 가서 그녀와 이야기를 나누었다. \"아! 처녀여,\" 그가 말했다. \"저쪽 사내와 함께 여행하는 것이 그대에게 즐거울 리 없겠지요.\" \"제게는 불쾌하지 않습니다,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그대를 시중들 젊은이도 처녀도 없지 않소,\" 그가 말했다. \"참으로,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"젊은이와 처녀의 시중을 받는 것보다 저쪽 사내를 따르는 것이 제게는 더 즐겁습니다.\" \"내가 그대에게 좋은 조언을 하겠소,\" 그가 말했다. \"그대가 나와 함께 산다면, 내 백작령 전부를 그대의 소유로 두겠소.\"\n\n\"에니드, 외로운 내 삶을 이끄는 길잡이 별, 에니드, 내 첫사랑이자 유일한 사랑이여.\" — 에니드.\n\n\"그리하지 않겠습니다, 하늘에 맹세코,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저쪽 사내가 제 정절을 처음으로 맹세한 분인데, 제가 그분에게 변심을 하겠습니까?\" \"그대가 틀렸소,\" 백작이 말했다. \"내가 저쪽 사내를 죽이면, 나는 내가 원하는 한 그대를 곁에 둘 수 있고, 그대가 더 이상 마음에 들지 않으면 그대를 내쫓을 수 있소. 허나 그대가 그대 자신의 선의로 나와 함께 간다면, 내가 살아 있는 한 우리의 결합이 계속되리라 단언하겠소.\" 그러자 그녀는 그의 그 말들을 곰곰이 생각하였고, 그의 청을 부추기는 것이 현명하리라 여겼다. \"그렇다면 보십시오, 족장이시여, 모든 비난으로부터 저를 구하려면 이렇게 하시는 것이 가장 마땅합니다. 내일 이곳으로 오시어, 제가 아무것도 모르는 것처럼 저를 데려가십시오.\" \"그리하겠소,\" 그가 말했다. 그리하여 그는 일어나 작별을 고하고, 수행원들과 함께 떠났다. 그리고 그녀는 백작과 나눈 대화를 그때 게라인트에게 조금도 이야기하지 않았으니, 그것이 그의 분노를 불러일으켜 그에게 불안과 근심을 끼칠까 두려웠기 때문이다.\n\n그리고 여느 때와 같은 시각에 그들은 잠자리에 들었다. 그리고 밤이 시작될 무렵 에니드는 잠깐 잠을 잤다. 그러다가 한밤중에 그녀는 일어나, 곧바로 입을 수 있도록 게라인트의 무구를 모두 한데 모아 두었다. 그리고 자신이 하려는 일이 두려웠음에도, 그녀는 게라인트의 침상 곁으로 가, 그에게 부드럽고 다정하게 말했다. \"주군이시여, 일어나 옷을 입으십시오. 이것이 백작이 제게 한 말이며, 저에 관한 그의 의도입니다.\" 그리하여 그녀는 게라인트에게 일어난 일을 모두 이야기했다. 그리고 비록 그가 그녀에게 노했으나, 그는 그 경고를 받아들여 옷을 입었다. 그리고 그녀는 그가 옷을 입을 수 있도록 빛을 비추려고 초에 불을 켰다. \"그 초는 거기 두고,\" 그가 말했다. \"이 집의 사내더러 이리 오라고 청하라.\" 그러자 그녀가 갔고, 집의 사내가 그에게 왔다. \"내가 네게 진 빚이 얼마인지 아느냐?\" 게라인트가 물었다. \"제게 지신 빚은 얼마 안 된다고 생각합니다.\" \"그 세 마리 말과 세 벌의 무구를 가지거라.\" \"하늘이 당신께 보답하시기를, 나으리,\" 그가 말했다. \"허나 저는 당신께 무구 한 벌의 값도 쓰지 않았습니다.\" \"바로 그 까닭에,\" 그가 말했다. \"너는 더 부유해질 것이다. 그런데 이제 나를 마을 밖으로 안내해 주겠느냐?\" \"기꺼이 그리하겠습니다,\" 그가 말했다. \"그리고 어느 방향으로 가실 작정이십니까?\" \"나는 들어온 길과는 다른 길로 마을을 떠나고자 한다.\" 그리하여 그 숙소의 사내는 그가 바라는 데까지 그와 동행했다. 그러고 나서 그는 처녀에게 자신보다 앞서 가라고 명했고, 그녀는 그렇게 하여 곧장 앞으로 나아갔으며, 그의 주인은 집으로 돌아갔다.\n\n그리고 게라인트와 처녀는 큰길을 따라 앞으로 나아갔다. 그리고 그렇게 여행하던 중, 그들은 자신들 가까이에서 몹시 큰 통곡 소리를 들었다. \"그대는 여기 머물러라,\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 가서 이 통곡의 까닭이 무엇인지 보고 오겠다.\" \"그리하겠습니다,\" 그녀가 말했다. 그러고 나서 그는 길 가까이에 있는 트인 빈터로 나아갔다. 그리고 그 빈터에서 그는 말 두 마리를 보았는데, 한 마리에는 남자용 안장이, 다른 한 마리에는 여자용 안장이 얹혀 있었다. 그리고 보라, 갑옷을 입은 채 죽어 누워 있는 기사가 하나 있었고, 승마복을 입은 젊은 처자가 그 위에서 슬피 울며 서 있었다. \"아, 부인,\" 게라인트가 말했다. \"무슨 일이 그대에게 닥쳤습니까?\" \"보십시오,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"저는 사랑하는 남편과 함께 이곳을 여행하고 있었는데, 보세요! 거인 셋이 우리에게 덮쳐 와, 세상에 아무런 까닭도 없이 그를 죽였습니다.\" \"그들은 여기서 어느 쪽으로 갔습니까?\" 게라인트가 말했다. \"저쪽 큰길로 갔습니다,\" 그녀가 대답했다. 그리하여 그는 에니드에게 돌아갔다. \"가라,\" 그가 말했다. \"저 아래에 있는 부인에게 가서, 내가 올 때까지 거기서 나를 기다려라.\" 그가 그렇게 하라고 명했을 때 그녀는 슬펐으나, 그럼에도 그녀는 듣기에 가여운 그 처자에게로 갔으며, 게라인트가 결코 돌아오지 못하리라 확신했다.\n\n한편 게라인트는 거인들을 뒤쫓아 따라잡았다. 그리고 그들 각자는 다른 사내 셋을 합친 것보다 키가 컸으며, 저마다 어깨에는 거대한 곤봉을 지고 있었다. 그러자 그는 그들 중 하나에게 돌진하여 자신의 창을 그 몸에 꿰뚫었다. 그리고 그것을 다시 뽑아내어, 그들 중 또 하나를 마찬가지로 꿰뚫었다. 그러나 세 번째 자가 그에게 돌아서서 곤봉으로 그를 후려쳤으니, 그 바람에 그의 방패가 쪼개지고 그의 어깨가 으스러졌다. 그러나 게라인트는 칼을 뽑아 그 거인의 정수리에 일격을 가했으니, 그것이 어찌나 매섭고 사납고 격렬했던지, 그의 머리와 목이 어깨까지 쪼개져 그가 죽어 쓰러졌다. 그리하여 게라인트는 그를 그렇게 두고 에니드에게로 돌아갔다. 그리고 그녀가 있는 곳에 이르렀을 때, 그는 말에서 죽은 듯이 떨어졌다. 에니드가 내지른 외침은 날카롭고 크고 가슴을 에는 것이었다. 그리고 그녀는 그가 쓰러진 곳으로 가서 그 위에 섰다. 그리고 그녀의 외침 소리에 리무르 백작과 그와 함께 여행하던 자들이 왔으니, 그녀의 비통한 울음이 그들을 길에서 끌어낸 것이었다. 그리고 백작이 에니드에게 말했다. \"아, 부인, 무슨 일이 그대에게 닥쳤습니까?\" \"아, 선량하신 분이여,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"제가 사랑해 온, 또 앞으로도 사랑할 유일한 사내가 죽임을 당했습니다.\" 그러자 그가 다른 여인에게 말했다. \"그리고 그대 슬픔의 까닭은 무엇이오?\" \"그들은 제 사랑하는 남편 또한 죽였습니다,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그리고 그들을 죽인 것은 누구였소?\" \"거인 몇이,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"제가 가장 사랑하던 이를 죽였고, 저 다른 기사가 그들을 뒤쫓아 갔다가, 당신이 보시는 그 꼴이 되어 돌아왔습니다.\" 백작은 죽은 그 기사를 묻게 했으나, 게라인트에게는 아직 얼마간 생명이 남아 있다고 생각했다. 그리하여 그가 아직 살아날지 보려고, 그는 게라인트를 자신의 방패 우묵한 곳에 담고 들것에 실어 함께 데려가게 했다. 그리고 두 처자는 궁정으로 갔다. 그들이 그곳에 이르자, 게라인트는 홀에 있는 탁자 앞의 작은 침상 위에 뉘어졌다. 그러고 나서 그들 모두는 여행복을 벗었고, 백작은 에니드에게도 그렇게 하여 다른 옷으로 갈아입으라고 간청했다. \"그리하지 않겠습니다, 하늘에 맹세코,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"아, 부인,\" 그가 말했다. \"이 일로 그토록 슬퍼하지 마시오.\" \"제가 달리 굴도록 설득하기란 어려울 것입니다,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저쪽 기사가 살든 죽든, 그대가 슬퍼할 필요가 없도록 내가 그대를 대하리다. 보시오, 좋은 백작령과 더불어 나 자신을 그대에게 바치겠소. 그러니 행복하고 기뻐하시오.\" \"저는 하늘에 선언합니다,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이제부터 제가 사는 동안 결코 기뻐하지 않으리라고요.\" \"자,\" 그가 말했다. \"그리고 드시오.\" \"아니요, 하늘에 맹세코, 그러지 않겠습니다.\" \"허나, 하늘에 맹세코, 그대는 그러할 것이오,\" 그가 말했다. 그리하여 그는 그녀의 뜻을 거슬러 그녀를 탁자로 데려가, 여러 번 먹으라고 청했다. \"하늘을 증인으로 부르겠습니다,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저쪽 들것 위의 사내가 마찬가지로 먹기 전에는 저도 먹지 않겠다고요.\" \"그대는 그것을 이룰 수 없소,\" 백작이 말했다. \"저쪽 사내는 이미 죽었으니.\" \"제가 이룰 수 있음을 증명하겠습니다,\" 그녀가 말했다. 그러자 그는 그녀에게 술잔을 내밀었다. \"이 잔을 드시오,\" 그가 말했다. \"그러면 그대의 마음이 바뀌게 될 것이오.\" \"제게 화가 미칠지어다,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"그분이 마시기 전에 제가 무엇이라도 마신다면요.\" \"참으로,\" 백작이 말했다. \"그대에게 부드럽게 대하는 것이 거칠게 대하는 것보다 나을 게 없구려.\" 그러고는 그녀의 귀싸대기를 한 대 갈겼다. 그러자 그녀는 크고 날카로운 비명을 내질렀고, 그녀의 비통한 울음은 전보다 훨씬 더 커졌으니, 게라인트가 살아 있었다면 감히 그녀를 이렇게 치지 못했으리라고 마음속으로 생각했기 때문이다. 그러나, 보라, 그녀의 외침 소리에 게라인트가 혼절에서 깨어나, 들것 위에 일어나 앉았다. 그리고 자신의 방패 우묵한 곳에서 칼을 찾아내어, 백작이 있는 곳으로 돌진해, 그의 정수리에 사납게 베고 매섭게 독을 품고 무자비하게 후려치는 일격을 가했으니, 그를 둘로 쪼개어 그의 칼이 탁자에 가로막힐 때까지 갈랐다. 그러자 모두가 식탁을 떠나 달아났다. 그리고 이는 산 자에 대한 두려움 때문이라기보다는, 죽은 사내가 일어나 그들을 죽이는 것을 보고 느낀 공포 때문이었다. 그리고 게라인트는 에니드를 바라보았고, 두 가지 까닭으로 비통했다. 하나는 에니드가 혈색과 평소의 모습을 잃은 것을 본 까닭이요, 다른 하나는 그녀가 옳았음을 알게 된 까닭이었다. \"부인,\" 그가 말했다. \"우리 말들이 어디 있는지 아시오?\" \"압니다, 주군이시여, 당신 말이 어디 있는지는요,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"허나 다른 말이 어디 있는지는 모릅니다. 당신 말은 저쪽 집에 있습니다.\" 그리하여 그는 그 집으로 가서 자신의 말을 끌어내어 올라타고, 에니드를 들어 올려 자신과 함께 말 위에 앉혔다. 그리고 그는 앞으로 말을 달렸다. 그리고 그들의 길은 두 산울타리 사이로 나 있었으며, 밤이 낮을 밀어내고 있었다. 그런데 보라! 그들은 자신들 뒤편, 자신들과 하늘 사이로 솟은 창자루들을 보았고, 말들이 짓밟는 소리와 한 무리가 다가오는 소음을 들었다. \"무언가 우리를 뒤쫓는 소리가 들리는구나,\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 그대를 산울타리 저편으로 옮겨 두겠다.\" 그리고 그는 그렇게 했다. 그리고 그러자, 보라, 한 기사가 그를 향해 박차를 가하며 달려와 창을 겨누었다. 에니드가 이것을 보고 외쳐 말했다. \"오 족장이시여, 당신이 누구시든, 죽은 사내를 죽여서 무슨 명성을 얻으시렵니까?\" \"오 하늘이여!\" 그가 말했다. \"게라인트인가?\" \"그렇습니다, 참으로,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그런데 당신은 누구십니까?\" \"나는 기페르트 페티트요,\" 그가 말했다. \"그대 남편의 동맹이며, 그대가 곤경에 처했다는 말을 들었기에 그대를 도우러 왔소. 여기서 가까운, 내 누이의 사위가 있는 궁정으로 나와 함께 갑시다. 그러면 그대는 이 왕국에서 가장 훌륭한 의술의 도움을 받을 것이오.\" \"기꺼이 그리하겠습니다,\" 게라인트가 말했다. 그리하여 에니드는 기페르트의 시종 중 한 사람의 말에 태워졌고, 그들은 남작의 궁궐로 나아갔다. 그리고 그들은 그곳에서 기쁘게 맞아들여졌으며, 환대와 보살핌을 받았다. 다음 날 아침 그들은 의원들을 찾으러 갔고, 오래지 않아 그들이 왔으며, 게라인트가 완전히 나을 때까지 그를 돌보았다. 그리고 게라인트가 치료를 받는 동안, 기페르트는 그의 무구를 수선하게 하여, 그것이 예전 못지않게 좋아질 때까지 손보게 했다. 그리고 그들은 그곳에서 한 달 보름을 머물렀다. 그러고 나서 그들은 헤어졌고, 게라인트는 자신의 영토로 향했으며, 그때부터 그는 번영을 누리며 다스렸고, 그의 무인다운 명성과 영광은 그때 이후로 그와 에니드 모두에게 영예와 명예와 더불어 오래도록 이어졌다.\n\n————\n\n# PWYLL, PRINCE OF DYVED\n\n옛날 옛적에 퓔은 그의 으뜸가는 궁전이 있는 나르베르스에 머물고 있었는데, 그곳에는 그를 위한 잔치가 마련되어 있었고, 수많은 사람들이 그와 함께하고 있었다. 첫 번째 식사를 마친 뒤 퓔은 일어나 거닐었다. 그는 궁전 위쪽에 있는, 고르세드 아르베르스라고 불리는 둔덕 꼭대기로 올라갔다. \"주군이시여.\" 궁정의 한 사람이 말했다. \"이 둔덕에는 특이한 점이 있으니, 누구든 그 위에 앉는 사람은 상처나 매를 입거나, 아니면 어떤 기이한 일을 보지 않고서는 그곳을 떠나지 못합니다.\" \"상처나 매를 입는 것은 두렵지 않다.\" 퓔이 말했다. \"허나 기이한 일이라면, 기꺼이 보고 싶구나. 그러니 나는 가서 저 둔덕 위에 앉으리라.\"\n\n그리하여 그는 둔덕 위에 앉았다. 그가 그곳에 앉아 있는 동안, 사람들은 한 귀부인이 커다란 순백의 말을 타고서, 빛나는 황금빛 옷을 두른 채, 둔덕에서 이어지는 큰길을 따라 오는 것을 보았다. \"나의 부하들이여.\" 퓔이 말했다. \"그대들 중에 저 귀부인을 아는 자가 있는가?\" \"없습니다, 주군이시여.\" 그들이 말했다. \"그대들 중 하나가 가서 그녀를 맞이하여, 그녀가 누구인지 우리가 알 수 있게 하라.\" 그러자 그들 중 한 사람이 일어났고, 그녀를 맞이하러 길로 나아가자 그녀는 지나쳐 갔다. 그는 걸어서 할 수 있는 한 빨리 뒤를 따랐으나, 그의 속도가 빠를수록 그녀는 그에게서 더 멀어졌다. 그녀를 따라가는 것이 아무 소용없음을 깨닫자, 그는 퓔에게 돌아와 이렇게 말했다. \"주군이시여, 세상 누구라도 걸어서 그녀를 따라가는 것은 부질없는 일입니다.\" \"참으로.\" 퓔이 말했다. \"궁전으로 가서, 그대가 본 가장 날랜 말을 골라, 그녀를 뒤쫓으라.\"\n\n그리하여 그는 말 한 마리를 잡아타고 앞으로 나아갔다. 그는 탁 트인 평평한 들판에 이르러 말에 박차를 가했다. 그러나 말을 재촉하면 할수록 그녀는 그에게서 더 멀어졌다. 그는 퓔이 있는 곳으로 돌아와 말했다. \"주군이시여, 누구라도 저 귀부인을 따라가는 것은 아무 소용이 없을 것입니다. 이 나라에서 이보다 빠른 말을 저는 알지 못하나, 그녀를 뒤쫓는 것은 소용이 없었습니다.\" \"참으로.\" 퓔이 말했다. \"여기에는 무슨 환술이 있는 것이 틀림없다. 궁전으로 가자.\" 그리하여 그들은 궁전으로 갔고, 그날을 보냈다.\n\n이튿날 그들은 식사를 하러 갈 때까지 즐겁게 시간을 보냈다. 식사가 끝나자 퓔이 말했다. \"어제 둔덕 꼭대기로 갔던 무리들은 어디 있는가?\" \"보십시오, 주군이시여, 저희가 여기 있습니다.\" 그들이 말했다. \"가자.\" 그가 말했다. \"둔덕으로 가서 그곳에 앉자. 그리고 그대는.\" 그가 자기 말을 돌보는 시동에게 말했다. \"내 말에 안장을 잘 얹어 서둘러 길로 끌고 오고, 내 박차도 함께 가져오라.\" 젊은이는 그대로 했다. 그리하여 그들은 가서 둔덕 위에 앉았다. 그곳에 앉은 지 얼마 지나지 않아, 그들은 그 귀부인이 같은 길을 따라, 같은 모습으로, 같은 걸음으로 오는 것을 보았다. \"젊은이여.\" 퓔이 말했다. \"저 귀부인이 오는 것이 보이는구나. 내 말을 다오.\" 그가 말에 오르기도 전에 그녀는 그를 지나쳐 갔다. 그는 그녀를 향해 방향을 돌려 뒤를 따랐다. 그는 말이 신나게 껑충거리며 달리게 하면서, 곧 그녀를 따라잡으리라 생각했다. 그러나 그는 처음보다 조금도 그녀에게 가까워지지 못했다. 그러자 그는 말을 최대한의 속도로 몰았으나, 그래도 소용이 없음을 깨달았다. 그때 퓔이 말했다. \"오, 처녀여, 그대가 가장 사랑하는 이를 위하여, 나를 위해 멈춰 주오.\" \"기꺼이 멈추겠어요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그리고 진작 그리 청하셨더라면 당신의 말에게도 더 나았을 거예요.\" 그리하여 처녀는 멈춰 섰고, 그녀는 얼굴을 가리고 있던 머리 장식의 일부를 뒤로 젖혔다. 그러자 그는 자신이 일찍이 보았던 모든 처녀와 모든 귀부인의 아름다움이 그녀의 아름다움에 비하면 아무것도 아니라고 생각했다. \"귀부인이여.\" 그가 말했다. \"그대가 온 뜻에 관하여 내게 말해 주겠소?\" \"말씀드리지요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저의 으뜸가는 소망은 당신을 뵙는 것이었어요.\" \"참으로.\" 퓔이 말했다. \"이는 그대가 올 수 있었던 가장 기쁜 소망이오. 그대가 누구인지 말해 주겠소?\" \"말씀드리겠어요, 주군이시여.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저는 헤베이드의 딸 리아논이며, 사람들은 제 뜻을 거슬러 제게 남편을 짝지어 주려 했어요. 그러나 저는 어떤 남편도 원치 않았으니, 그것은 당신을 향한 제 사랑 때문이었지요. 당신이 저를 물리치지 않는 한, 저는 아직 어떤 남편도 두지 않을 거예요. 그리고 저는 당신의 대답을 듣고자 이곳에 왔어요.\" \"하늘에 맹세코.\" 퓔이 말했다. \"보시오, 이것이 내 대답이오. 세상의 모든 귀부인과 처녀 가운데서 고를 수 있다면, 나는 그대를 고를 것이오.\" \"참으로.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"당신이 그러한 마음이시라면, 제가 다른 이에게 주어지기 전에 저와 만날 것을 약속하세요.\" \"내가 그렇게 할 수 있는 것이 빠를수록, 내게는 더 기쁜 일이오.\" 퓔이 말했다. \"그대가 원하는 곳이라면 어디서든, 나는 그대와 만나겠소.\" \"지금부터 한 해가 되는 오늘, 헤베이드의 궁전에서 저와 만나 주세요.\" \"기꺼이.\" 그가 말했다. \"이 약속을 지키겠소.\" 그리하여 그들은 헤어졌고, 그는 자기 무리에게로, 그리고 자기 가솔들에게로 돌아갔다. 그들이 그 처녀에 관하여 어떤 질문을 하든, 그는 늘 화제를 다른 일로 돌렸다.\n\n그때로부터 한 해가 지나자, 그는 기사 백 명을 무장시켜 자신과 함께 헤베이드의 궁전으로 가게 했다. 그는 궁전에 이르렀고, 그를 두고 큰 기쁨이 있었으니, 사람들이 많이 모여들었고, 크게 즐거워했으며, 그의 도착을 위한 성대한 준비가 있었다. 그리고 궁정 전체가 그의 명령 아래 놓였다.\n\n큰 방이 단장되었고, 그들은 식사를 하러 가서 이렇게 앉았다. 헤베이드는 퓔의 한쪽에, 리아논은 다른 쪽에 앉았고, 나머지는 모두 그들의 지위에 따라 앉았다. 그들은 먹고 잔치를 벌이며 서로 이야기를 나누었다. 식사가 끝난 뒤 흥겨운 술자리가 시작될 무렵, 키가 크고 적갈색 머리에, 왕족의 풍모를 지니고, 비단옷을 차려입은 젊은이가 들어왔다. 그가 큰 방으로 들어오자, 그는 퓔과 그 동료들에게 인사했다. \"하늘의 인사가 그대에게 있기를.\" 퓔이 말했다. \"이리 와서 앉으라.\" \"아닙니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"저는 청을 드리러 온 사람이니, 제 용무를 행하겠습니다.\" \"기꺼이 그리하라.\" 퓔이 말했다. \"주군이시여.\" 그가 말했다. \"제 용무는 당신께 드리는 것이니, 제가 온 것은 당신께 은혜 하나를 간청하기 위함입니다.\" \"그대가 내게 무엇을 청하든, 내 힘이 닿는 한, 그대는 그것을 얻으리라.\" \"아!\" 리아논이 말했다. \"어찌하여 그런 대답을 하셨나요?\" \"이분께서 이 귀족들 앞에서 그리 말씀하시지 않았습니까?\" 젊은이가 물었다. \"내 영혼이여.\" 퓔이 말했다. \"그대가 청하는 은혜가 무엇이오?\" \"제가 가장 사랑하는 귀부인이 오늘 밤 당신의 신부가 되기로 되어 있습니다. 저는 이곳에 있는 잔치와 연회와 더불어 그녀를 당신께 청하러 왔습니다.\" 퓔은 자신이 한 약속 때문에 말이 없었다. \"원하는 만큼 잠자코 계셔도 좋아요.\" 리아논이 말했다. \"그러나 당신처럼 제 지혜를 잘못 쓴 사람은 일찍이 없었어요.\" \"귀부인이여.\" 그가 말했다. \"나는 그가 누구인지 알지 못했소.\" \"보세요, 이 사람이 바로 제 뜻을 거슬러 사람들이 제게 주려 했던 사람이에요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그리고 그는 클루드의 아들 가울이니, 막강한 권세와 부를 지닌 사람이지요. 당신이 하신 말씀 때문에, 수치가 당신께 닥치지 않도록, 저를 그에게 주세요.\" \"귀부인이여.\" 그가 말했다. \"나는 그대의 대답을 이해하지 못하겠소. 결코 그대가 말하는 대로 할 수 없소.\" \"저를 그에게 주세요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그리고 저는 결코 그의 것이 되지 않도록 하겠어요.\" \"어떤 방법으로 그리되겠소?\" 퓔이 물었다. 그러자 그녀는 자신의 마음속에 있는 생각을 그에게 말했다. 그리고 그들은 오래도록 함께 이야기를 나누었다. 그때 가울이 말했다. \"주군이시여, 제 청에 대한 대답을 듣는 것이 마땅합니다.\" \"그대가 청한 것 가운데 내 힘으로 줄 수 있는 만큼은, 그대가 얻으리라.\" 퓔이 대답했다. \"내 영혼이여.\" 리아논이 가울에게 말했다. \"여기 있는 잔치와 연회로 말하자면, 저는 그것을 디베드의 사람들과, 우리와 함께 있는 가솔과 전사들에게 베풀었어요. 이것을 누구에게도 내어 줄 수는 없어요. 오늘 밤으로부터 한 해가 되는 날, 이 궁전에서 당신을 위한 연회가 마련될 것이고, 그때 제가 당신의 신부가 되겠어요.\"\n\n그리하여 가울은 자기 영지로 떠났고, 퓔 또한 디베드로 돌아갔다. 그리고 그들 둘은 헤베이드의 궁전에서 잔치가 열릴 때가 될 때까지 그 한 해를 보냈다. 그때 클루드의 아들 가울은 자신을 위해 마련된 잔치를 향해 길을 나섰다. 그는 궁전에 이르러, 그곳에서 기쁨 속에 맞아들여졌다. 디베드의 우두머리 퓔 또한 리아논이 명한 대로, 기사 백 명과 함께 과수원으로 왔다. 퓔은 거칠고 누더기 같은 옷을 입고, 발에는 크고 볼품없는 낡은 신을 신고 있었다. 식사 뒤의 술자리가 시작되었음을 알자, 그는 큰 방을 향해 갔다. 큰 방에 들어서자 그는 클루드의 아들 가울과 그 일행에게, 남자와 여자 모두에게 인사했다. \"하늘이 그대를 번성케 하기를.\" 가울이 말했다. \"그리고 정다운 인사가 그대에게 있기를!\" \"주군이시여.\" 그가 말했다. \"하늘이 당신께 보답하기를! 저는 당신께 드릴 용무가 있습니다.\" \"그대의 용무를 환영하노라. 그대가 내게 옳은 것을 청한다면, 그대는 그것을 기꺼이 얻으리라.\" \"마땅한 일입니다.\" 그가 대답했다. \"저는 다만 궁핍하여 청할 뿐이니, 제가 청하는 은혜는 당신이 보시는 이 작은 자루를 고기로 가득 채우는 것입니다.\" \"이치에 맞는 청이로다.\" 그가 말했다. \"기꺼이 그대는 그것을 얻으리라. 그에게 음식을 가져오라.\" 수많은 시종이 일어나 자루를 채우기 시작했다. 그러나 그들이 아무리 많이 넣어도, 그것은 처음보다 조금도 더 차지 않았다. \"내 영혼이여.\" 가울이 말했다. \"그대의 자루는 언제고 가득 차겠는가?\" \"하늘에 단언하건대, 가득 차지 않을 것입니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"거기 무엇을 넣든, 영지와 영토와 보물을 가진 누군가가 일어나 두 발로 자루 안의 음식을 밟고 서서, '충분히 넣었다'고 말하지 않는 한 말입니다.\" 그러자 리아논이 클루드의 아들 가울에게 말했다. \"어서 일어나세요.\" \"기꺼이 일어나리다.\" 그가 말했다. 그리하여 그는 일어나 두 발을 자루 안에 넣었다. 그러자 퓔은 자루의 가장자리를 끌어올려, 가울이 머리까지 그 안에 잠기게 했다. 그리고 그는 재빨리 그것을 닫고, 가죽끈에 매듭을 지어 묶고는 자기 뿔나팔을 불었다. 그러자 보라, 그의 기사들이 궁전으로 들이닥쳤다. 그들은 가울과 함께 온 무리를 모조리 붙잡아, 그의 감옥에 가두었다. 퓔은 누더기와 낡은 신과 너덜너덜한 차림을 벗어 던졌다. 그리고 들어오면서, 퓔의 기사들은 저마다 자루에 한 대씩 후려치며 물었다. \"여기 무엇이 있는가?\" \"오소리입니다.\" 그들이 말했다. 이런 식으로 그들은 저마다 발로, 혹은 막대기로 자루를 치며 놀았다. 그렇게 그들은 자루를 가지고 놀았다. 그리고 그때 '자루 속 오소리'라는 놀이가 처음으로 행해졌다.\n\n\"주군이시여.\" 자루 속의 사내가 말했다. \"제 말을 들어주신다면, 저는 자루 속에서 죽임을 당할 만한 자가 아닙니다.\" 헤베이드가 말했다. \"주군이시여, 그의 말이 옳습니다. 그대가 그의 말을 듣는 것이 마땅하니, 그는 이런 일을 당할 만하지 않습니다.\" \"참으로.\" 퓔이 말했다. \"그에 관하여 그대의 조언을 따르겠소.\" \"보세요, 그렇다면 이것이 제 조언이에요.\" 리아논이 말했다. \"당신은 지금 청원자들과 음유시인들을 만족시켜야 마땅한 처지에 계세요. 그가 당신을 대신해 그들에게 베풀게 하시고, 자신에게 행해진 일에 대해 결코 복수를 꾀하지 않겠다는 서약을 그에게서 받으세요. 그것으로 충분한 벌이 될 거예요.\" \"기꺼이 그리하겠습니다.\" 자루 속의 사내가 말했다. \"그리고 나는 기꺼이 그것을 받아들이겠소.\" 퓔이 말했다. \"이는 헤베이드와 리아논의 조언이니. 그대 자신의 보증인을 찾으라.\" \"그의 부하들이 그를 위해 답할 수 있게 될 때까지, 우리가 그의 보증인이 되겠소.\" 헤베이드가 말했다. 그리하여 그는 자루에서 풀려났고, 그의 신하들도 풀려났다. \"참으로, 주군이시여.\" 가울이 말했다. \"저는 크게 다쳤고, 멍이 많이 들었습니다. 허락하신다면, 저는 떠나겠습니다. 당신이 요구하시는 모든 일에 대해 저를 대신해 답할 귀족들을 제 대신 남겨 두겠습니다.\" \"기꺼이.\" 퓔이 말했다. \"그대는 그리해도 좋다.\" 그리하여 가울은 자기 영지로 갔다.\n\n큰 방은 퓔과 그 무리의 사람들을 위해, 그리고 궁전의 사람들을 위해서도 정돈되었고, 그들은 식탁으로 가서 앉았다. 그리고 그들이 한 해 전 그때에 앉았던 대로, 그들은 그날 밤에도 앉았다. 그들은 먹고 잔치를 벌였으며, 흥겹고 평온하게 밤을 보냈다. 잠자리에 들 때가 되자, 퓔과 리아논은 그들의 침소로 갔다.\n\n이튿날 동틀 무렵, \"나의 주군이시여.\" 리아논이 말했다. \"일어나셔서 음유시인들에게 선물을 베풀기 시작하세요. 오늘은 당신의 후한 베풂을 청하는 누구도 물리치지 마세요.\" \"기꺼이 그리하리다.\" 퓔이 말했다. \"오늘도, 그리고 잔치가 계속되는 날마다 그리하겠소.\" 그리하여 퓔은 일어나, 정숙히 하라고 선포하게 하고, 모든 청원자와 음유시인에게 그들이 바라는 선물을 보이고 가리키도록 청했다. 이것이 행해지자 잔치는 계속되었고, 그는 잔치가 이어지는 동안 누구도 거절하지 않았다. 잔치가 끝나자 퓔은 헤베이드에게 말했다. \"나의 주군이시여, 허락하신다면, 저는 내일 디베드로 떠나겠습니다.\" \"물론이오.\" 헤베이드가 말했다. \"하늘이 그대를 번성케 하기를! 리아논이 그대를 뒤따를 때도 정하시오.\" \"하늘에 맹세코.\" 퓔이 말했다. \"우리는 함께 이곳을 떠날 것입니다.\" \"그것을 원하시오, 주군이여?\" 헤베이드가 말했다. \"그렇습니다, 주군이시여.\" 퓔이 대답했다.\n\n이튿날 그들은 디베드를 향해 길을 나서, 나르베르스의 궁전으로 여정을 떠났으니, 그곳에는 그들을 위한 잔치가 마련되어 있었다. 그리고 그들에게로 그 땅의 으뜸가는 사람들과 가장 고귀한 귀부인들이 무수히 찾아왔고, 그들 가운데 리아논이 팔찌든 반지든 보석이든 값진 선물을 주지 않은 이가 하나도 없었다. 그리하여 그들은 그해와 그다음 해에 그 땅을 번영 속에 다스렸다.\n\n————\n\n# BRANWEN, THE DAUGHTER OF LLYR\n\n흘리르의 아들 벤디게이드 브란은 이 섬의 왕관을 쓴 임금이었으며, 런던의 왕관으로부터 높여진 이였다. 어느 날 오후 그는 아르두드위의 할레크에 있는 자신의 궁정에 머물고 있었다. 그는 할레크의 바위 위에 앉아 바다를 바라보았다. 그의 곁에는 형제인 흘리르의 아들 마나위단과, 어머니 쪽 형제인 니시엔과 에브니시엔, 그리고 임금 주위에 둘러 있기 마땅한 여러 귀족이 함께 있었다. 어머니 쪽 두 형제는 에우로스위드의 아들들이었는데, 그 가운데 한 젊은이는 선량하고 온순한 성품을 지녀, 친족 사이에 화해를 이루고, 분노가 절정에 달했을 때에도 가족을 서로 벗으로 만드는 이였으니, 이가 곧 니시엔이었다. 그러나 다른 하나는 두 형제가 가장 평화로울 때에 그들 사이에 다툼을 일으키는 이였다. 이렇게 앉아 있던 중, 그들은 아일랜드 남쪽에서 열세 척의 배가 자신들을 향해 오는 것을 보았다. 배들은 바람을 등에 업고 빠른 속도로 다가왔고, 순식간에 가까워졌다. \"멀리 배가 보이는구나.\" 임금이 말했다. \"빠르게 뭍을 향해 오고 있다. 궁정의 사람들에게 명하여 채비를 갖추고 가서 저들의 뜻을 알아 오게 하라.\" 그리하여 사람들이 채비를 갖추고 그들을 향해 내려갔다. 배가 가까이 온 것을 보았을 때, 그들은 그토록 잘 단장된 배는 일찍이 본 적이 없다고 확신했다. 배에는 아름다운 비단 깃발들이 걸려 있었다. 그런데 보라, 배 한 척이 다른 배들을 앞질렀고, 그들은 방패가 뱃전 위로 들어 올려진 것을, 그 방패의 끝이 위를 향해 평화의 표징을 이루고 있는 것을 보았다. 사람들이 이야기를 나누고자 가까이 다가갔다. 그러자 저쪽에서 작은 배들을 내려 뭍을 향해 다가왔다. 그들은 임금에게 인사를 올렸다. 임금은 그들의 머리 위 바위에 있던 자리에서 그들의 말을 들을 수 있었다. \"하늘이 그대들을 번성케 하기를,\" 그가 말했다. \"그리고 환영하노라! 이 배들은 누구의 것이며, 그대들 가운데 우두머리는 누구인가?\" \"주군이시여,\" 그들이 말했다. \"아일랜드의 임금 마톨크가 여기 계시며, 이 배들은 그분의 것입니다.\" \"어찌하여 오는가?\" 임금이 물었다. \"그리고 뭍에 오르려는가?\" \"그분은 주군께 구혼하러 오신 것이옵니다.\" 그들이 말했다. \"그리고 청을 들어주시지 않으면 뭍에 오르지 않으실 것입니다.\" \"그것이 무엇인가?\" 임금이 물었다. \"그분은 주군과 동맹을 맺기를 바라십니다.\" 그들이 말했다. \"그리고 흘리르의 딸 브란웬을 청하러 오셨으니, 주군께서 좋게 여기신다면 강대한 자들의 섬이 아일랜드와 동맹을 이루어 두 나라가 모두 더욱 강성해지기를 바라십니다.\" \"참으로,\" 임금이 말했다. \"그를 뭍에 오르게 하라. 그 일에 관하여 우리가 의논하겠노라.\" 이 대답이 마톨크에게 전해졌다. \"기꺼이 가겠노라.\" 그가 말했다. 그리하여 그가 뭍에 올랐고, 사람들이 그를 기쁘게 맞이했다. 그날 밤 궁정에는 그의 무리와 궁정의 무리가 어울려 큰 인파가 모였다. 이튿날 그들은 의논하였고, 브란웬을 마톨크에게 주기로 결의하였다. 그녀는 이 섬의 으뜸가는 세 귀부인 가운데 하나였으며, 세상에서 가장 어여쁜 처녀였다.\n\n그들은 그녀가 그의 신부가 될 곳으로 아베르프라우를 정했다. 그들은 그곳을 떠나 아베르프라우를 향해 무리를 이끌고 나아갔으니, 마톨크와 그의 무리는 배를 타고, 벤디게이드 브란과 그의 무리는 뭍으로 가, 마침내 아베르프라우에 이르렀다. 아베르프라우에서 그들은 잔치를 시작하고 자리에 앉았다. 그들이 앉은 모습은 이러하였다. 강대한 자들의 섬의 임금과 흘리르의 아들 마나위단이 한쪽에, 마톨크가 다른 쪽에, 그리고 흘리르의 딸 브란웬이 그의 곁에 앉았다. 그들은 집 안이 아니라 천막 아래 있었다. 어떤 집도 벤디게이드 브란을 담을 수 없었기 때문이다. 그들은 향연을 시작하여, 마시고 떠들며 이야기를 나누었다. 그리고 흥겨워하기보다 잠드는 것이 더 즐겁게 여겨지자 그들은 잠자리에 들었고, 브란웬은 마톨크의 신부가 되었다.\n\n이튿날 그들이 일어났고, 궁정의 모든 사람과 관리들이 채비를 갖추기 시작하여 말과 시종들을 정렬하였으니, 바다에 이르기까지 줄지어 세웠다.\n\n그런데 보라, 어느 날 앞서 말한 그 다툼을 좋아하는 사내 에브니시엔이 우연히 마톨크의 말들이 있는 곳에 와서, 이 말들이 누구의 것이냐고 물었다. \"그대의 누이 브란웬과 혼인한 아일랜드의 임금 마톨크의 말들입니다. 그분의 말들이지요.\" \"이런 식으로 그녀 같은 처녀를, 더구나 내 누이를, 내 동의도 없이 내주었단 말인가? 이보다 더 큰 모욕을 내게 줄 수는 없었으리라.\" 그가 말했다. 그러고는 그가 말들 밑으로 달려들어, 입술을 이빨 있는 데까지 잘라 내고, 귀를 머리에 닿도록 베어 내고, 꼬리를 등에 닿도록 잘라 내었다. 그리하여 그가 말들을 흉하게 만들어 쓸모없게 하였다.\n\n사람들이 이 소식을 마톨크에게 가져와, 말들이 흉하게 망가져 다시는 그 가운데 한 마리도 쓸모가 없게 되었다고 아뢰었다. \"참으로, 주군이시여,\" 한 사람이 말했다. \"이는 주군께 가하는 모욕이며, 그러한 뜻으로 행한 것입니다.\" \"참으로 내게 놀라운 일이로다. 만약 저들이 나를 모욕하려 하였다면, 어찌하여 저토록 지체 높고 친족들에게 그토록 사랑받는 처녀를 내게 주었단 말인가.\" \"주군이시여,\" 다른 사람이 말했다. \"사정이 이러함을 보셨으니, 주군께서 하실 일은 배로 돌아가는 것밖에 없습니다.\" 그러자 그는 자신의 배를 향해 떠났다.\n\n마톨크가 작별을 청하지도 않고 궁정을 떠난다는 소식이 벤디게이드 브란에게 전해졌고, 어찌하여 그러하느냐고 그에게 묻고자 사신들이 보내졌다. 가게 된 사신은 아나라우드의 아들 이디크와 헤베이드 히르였다. 이들이 그를 따라잡아, 무엇을 하려는지, 어찌하여 떠나는지를 물었다. \"참으로,\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 알았더라면 이곳에 오지 않았을 것이오. 나는 온전히 모욕을 당하였소. 여기서 내가 받은 것보다 더 나쁜 대접을 받은 이는 일찍이 없었소.\" \"참으로, 주군이시여, 궁정에 속한 그 누구의 뜻도, 의회에 속한 그 누구의 뜻도, 주군께서 이러한 모욕을 받게 하려는 것이 아니었습니다.\" 그들이 말했다. \"그리고 주군께서 모욕을 당하셨으니, 그 불명예는 주군보다 벤디게이드 브란께 더 큰 것입니다.\" \"참으로,\" 그가 말했다. \"나도 그리 생각하오. 그렇다 하더라도 그가 그 모욕을 되돌릴 수는 없소.\" 이 사람들은 그 대답을 가지고 벤디게이드 브란이 있는 곳으로 돌아와, 마톨크가 그들에게 준 답을 아뢰었다. \"참으로,\" 그가 말했다. \"그가 우리와 원수가 되어 떠나는 것을 막을 수단이 있다면, 우리가 쓰지 않을 방법은 없다.\" \"그렇다면, 주군이시여,\" 그들이 말했다. \"그에게 또 다른 사절을 보내십시오.\" \"그리하겠노라.\" 그가 말했다. \"일어서라, 흘리르의 아들 마나위단과 헤베이드 히르여, 그를 따라가 그에게 전하라. 망가진 말 한 마리마다 성한 말을 한 마리씩 줄 것이라고. 그뿐만 아니라 모욕에 대한 보상으로, 그 자신만큼 크고 높은 은 지팡이와, 그의 얼굴 너비만 한 황금 판을 줄 것이라고. 그리고 누가 이 일을 저질렀는지, 그것이 내 뜻에 반하여 행해진 일임을 그에게 보여 주라. 다만 그 일을 저지른 자는 내 형제이니, 그를 죽이기는 내게 어려운 일이라고. 그러니 그에게 와서 나를 만나게 하라.\" 그가 말했다. \"그가 바라는 어떤 방식으로든 우리가 화평을 이루리라.\"\n\n사절은 마톨크를 따라가, 이 모든 말을 우호적으로 전하였고, 그는 그것에 귀를 기울였다. \"여러분,\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 의논해 보겠소.\" 그리하여 그가 의회로 갔다. 의회에서 그들은, 만약 이를 거절한다면 그토록 큰 보상을 얻기는커녕 오히려 더 큰 수치를 당하기 쉬우리라고 헤아렸다. 그리하여 그들은 이를 받아들이기로 결의하고, 화평하게 궁정으로 돌아왔다.\n\n이윽고 정자와 천막이 회당 모양으로 정돈되었다. 그들은 식사하러 갔고, 잔치를 처음 시작했을 때 앉았던 그대로 거기에 앉았다. 마톨크와 벤디게이드 브란이 이야기를 나누기 시작했다. 그런데 보라, 이야기를 나누는 동안 벤디게이드 브란에게는 마톨크가 전처럼 그리 유쾌해 보이지 않았다. 그는 그 족장이 자신이 당한 잘못에 대한 보상이 적어 슬퍼하는 것이 아닐까 생각했다. \"오, 그대여,\" 벤디게이드 브란이 말했다. \"그대는 오늘 밤 평소처럼 유쾌하게 이야기하지 않는구려. 만약 그것이 보상이 적은 까닭이라면, 그대가 무엇이든 고르는 대로 거기에 더할 것이며, 내일 내가 말 값을 치르리다.\" \"주군이시여,\" 그가 말했다. \"하늘이 주군께 보답하시기를!\" \"그리고 내가 보상을 더하겠소.\" 벤디게이드 브란이 말했다. \"내가 그대에게 솥을 하나 주겠으니, 그 솥의 성질인즉, 그대의 사람 가운데 하나가 오늘 죽어 그 안에 던져지면, 이튿날 그는 가장 좋았을 때처럼 멀쩡해질 것이라오. 다만 말을 되찾지는 못할 것이오.\" 그러자 그가 크게 감사하였고, 그 일로 매우 기뻐하였다.\n\n그날 밤 그들은 바라는 만큼 이야기를 이어 나갔으며, 음유의 가락과 흥겨운 술자리를 즐겼다. 그리고 더 오래 앉아 있기보다 잠드는 것이 더 즐겁게 여겨지자 그들은 잠자리에 들었다. 이렇게 향연이 기쁨 속에 치러졌고, 그것이 끝나자 마톨크는 브란웬과 함께 아일랜드를 향해 길을 떠났다. 그들은 아베르 메네이에서 열세 척의 배로 떠나 아일랜드에 이르렀다. 아일랜드에서는 그들이 온 것을 두고 큰 기쁨이 있었다. 어떤 위대한 인물도, 어떤 고귀한 부인도 브란웬을 찾아오면, 그녀는 그들에게 떠나면서 지니고 가기에 떳떳할 만한 걸쇠나 반지나 왕가의 보석을 주지 않은 이가 없었다. 이러한 일들로 그녀는 그해를 큰 명성 속에 보냈으며, 영예와 우정을 누리며 즐겁게 시간을 보냈다. 그리고 때가 이르러 그녀에게 아들이 태어났으니, 그들이 그에게 지어 준 이름은 마톨크의 아들 그웨른이었다. 그들은 그 아이를 아일랜드에서 가장 훌륭한 이들이 있는 곳에 내보내 양육하게 하였다.\n\n그런데 보라, 두 번째 해에 마톨크가 웨일스에서 받은 모욕과, 그의 말 값으로 받은 보상을 두고 아일랜드에 소동이 일어났다. 그의 양형제들과 그에게 가장 가까운 자들이 그 일로 공공연히 그를 비난하였다. 그리고 그들이 그 치욕을 그에게 복수해 줄 때까지 그는 소동으로 인해 평안을 얻을 수 없었다. 그들이 행한 복수란, 브란웬을 그와 같은 방에서 쫓아내어 궁정을 위해 요리하게 하는 것이었고, 또 고기를 다 자른 백정으로 하여금 그녀에게 와서 날마다 그녀의 귀를 한 대씩 때리게 하는 것이었다. 그들은 그녀에게 그런 벌을 내렸다.\n\n\"참으로, 주군이시여,\" 그의 사람들이 마톨크에게 말했다. \"이제 배와 나룻배와 작은 가죽배가 웨일스로 가지 못하도록 금하시고, 웨일스에서 이리로 건너오는 자들은 가두어, 이 일이 그곳에 알려지지 않도록 그들이 되돌아가지 못하게 하소서.\" 그가 그리하였고, 이는 자그마치 삼 년 동안이나 그러하였다.\n\n브란웬은 반죽통 뚜껑 안에서 찌르레기 한 마리를 길러, 그것에게 말하는 법을 가르치고, 자기 오라비가 어떤 사람인지를 그 새에게 일러 주었다. 그리고 그녀는 자신의 비통함과 자신이 받은 멸시를 적은 편지를 써서, 그 편지를 새의 날개 뿌리에 매어 웨일스를 향해 보냈다. 새가 그 섬에 이르렀고, 어느 날 아르본의 카에르 세이온트에서 의논하고 있던 벤디게이드 브란을 발견하여, 그의 어깨에 내려앉아 깃털을 곤두세우니 편지가 보였고, 사람들은 그 새가 사람 손에 길러졌음을 알았다.\n\n이윽고 벤디게이드 브란이 편지를 받아 들여다보았다. 편지를 다 읽고 나자 그는 브란웬의 비통한 소식에 몹시 슬퍼하였다. 그리고 즉시 그는 섬을 한데 소집하고자 사신들을 보내기 시작했다. 그는 자신의 으뜸가는 사람 백마흔넷을 자기에게로 오게 하여, 누이가 견디고 있는 슬픔을 그들에게 호소하였다. 그리하여 그들이 의논하였다. 의논 끝에 그들은 아일랜드로 가기로, 그리고 일곱 사람을 제후로 남겨 두기로, 그 우두머리로 브란의 아들 카라도크를 두기로 결의하였다.\n\n벤디게이드 브란은 우리가 앞서 말한 무리와 함께 아일랜드를 향해 항해하였다. 바다를 건너는 거리는 멀지 않아, 그는 얕은 물에 이르렀다. 그때 마톨크의 돼지치기들이 바닷가에 있다가 마톨크에게로 왔다. \"주군이시여,\" 그들이 말했다. \"문안 올립니다.\" \"하늘이 그대들을 보호하시기를!\" 그가 말했다. \"무슨 소식이라도 있느냐?\" \"주군이시여,\" 그들이 말했다. \"놀라운 소식이 있사옵니다. 일찍이 나무 한 그루 본 적 없는 바다 위에서 숲을 보았나이다.\" \"이는 참으로 놀라운 일이로다.\" 그가 말했다. \"그 밖에 또 본 것이 있느냐?\" \"주군이시여, 보았나이다.\" 그들이 말했다. \"숲 곁에 거대한 산이 있어 움직이는데, 그 산 꼭대기에는 높은 능선이 있고, 그 능선 양쪽에 호수가 하나씩 있었습니다. 그리고 숲과 산과 이 모든 것이 움직였나이다.\" \"참으로,\" 그가 말했다. \"이에 관하여 무엇이든 알 수 있는 이는 브란웬밖에 없으리라.\"\n\n이윽고 사신들이 브란웬에게로 갔다. \"부인이시여,\" 그들이 말했다. \"이것이 무엇이라 생각하십니까?\" \"내가 받은 학대와 내 비통함을 듣고 이리로 온 강대한 자들의 섬의 사람들이오.\" \"바다 위에 보이는 그 숲은 무엇입니까?\" 그들이 물었다. \"배들의 활대와 돛대들이오.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"아아!\" 그들이 말했다. \"배 곁에 보이는 그 산은 무엇입니까?\" \"내 오라비 벤디게이드 브란이오.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"얕은 물에 이르러 뭍을 향해 걸어오고 있는 것이라오.\" \"양쪽에 호수가 하나씩 있는 그 높은 능선은 무엇입니까?\" \"이 섬을 바라보며 그가 노여워하고 있으니, 코 양쪽에 있는 그의 두 눈이 곧 능선 양쪽의 두 호수라오.\"\n\n아일랜드의 전사들과 으뜸가는 인물들이 황급히 한데 모여 의논하였다. \"주군이시여,\" 이웃들이 마톨크에게 말했다. \"이 한 가지 말고는 다른 방도가 없습니다. 브란웬에게 행해진 잘못과 멸시에 대한 보상으로, 그분의 누이 브란웬의 아들 그웨른에게 왕국을 주소서. 그리하면 그분이 주군과 화평하실 것입니다.\" 의논 끝에 나라가 멸망하지 않도록 이 전갈을 벤디게이드 브란에게 보내기로 결의하였다. 그리하여 이 화평이 이루어졌다. 마톨크는 벤디게이드 브란과 그의 무리를 위해 큰 집을 짓게 하였다. 그러자 무리가 그 집 안으로 들어왔다. 아일랜드 섬의 사람들이 한쪽으로 집에 들어왔고, 강대한 자들의 섬의 사람들이 다른 쪽으로 들어왔다. 그리고 그들이 자리에 앉자마자 그들 사이에 화목이 이루어졌으며, 주권이 그 소년에게 주어졌다. 화평이 맺어지자 벤디게이드 브란이 그 소년을 자기에게로 불렀고, 벤디게이드 브란에게서 소년은 마나위단에게로 갔다. 그를 본 모든 이가 그를 사랑하였다. 마나위단에게서 그 소년을 에우로스위드의 아들 니시엔이 불렀고, 소년은 다정하게 그에게로 갔다. \"어찌하여,\" 에브니시엔이 말했다. \"내 조카, 내 누이의 아들이 내게로 오지 않는가? 그가 아일랜드의 임금이 아니라 할지라도, 나는 기꺼이 그 아이를 어루만지고 싶다.\" \"기꺼이 그를 그대에게 가게 하라.\" 벤디게이드 브란이 말했다. 소년이 기꺼이 그에게로 갔다. \"하늘께 고백하건대,\" 에브니시엔이 마음속으로 말했다. \"내가 지금 이 순간 저지를 살육을 아무도 생각지 못하리라.\"\n\n그러고는 그가 일어나 소년을 안아 들었고, 집 안의 누구도 그를 붙잡기 전에 소년을 거꾸로 타오르는 불 속에 던져 넣었다. 브란웬은 자기 아들이 불 속에서 타는 것을 보자, 두 오라비 사이에 앉아 있던 자리에서 자신도 불 속으로 뛰어들려 하였다. 그러나 벤디게이드 브란이 한 손으로는 그녀를, 다른 손으로는 자신의 방패를 붙들었다. 이윽고 그들 모두가 집 안을 분주히 돌아다녔으니, 저마다 무장을 갖추는 가운데 어떤 무리도 한 집 안에서 그들이 일으킨 것만큼 큰 소동을 일으킨 적이 없었다. 그들 모두가 무기를 찾는 동안 벤디게이드 브란은 자기 방패와 어깨 사이에 브란웬을 받쳐 두었다. 그리하여 그들이 싸웠다.\n\n이윽고 아일랜드인들이 되살림의 솥 아래에 불을 지피고, 솥이 가득 찰 때까지 시신들을 그 안에 던져 넣었다. 그러자 이튿날 그들이 전과 다름없이 훌륭한 싸움꾼이 되어 나왔으니, 다만 말을 하지 못할 따름이었다. 에브니시엔은 강대한 자들의 섬의 사람들의 시신이 어디에서도 되살아나지 못하는 것을 보고, 마음속으로 말했다. \"아아! 비통하도다, 내가 강대한 자들의 섬의 사람들을 이토록 곤경에 빠뜨린 장본인이 되다니. 내가 거기서 벗어날 길을 찾지 못한다면 화가 내게 미치리라.\" 그러고는 그가 아일랜드인들의 시신 사이에 몸을 던졌다. 맨발의 아일랜드인 둘이 그에게 와서, 그를 아일랜드인 가운데 하나로 여기고 솥 안에 던져 넣었다. 그가 솥 안에서 몸을 쭉 뻗으니, 솥이 네 조각으로 갈라졌고, 그 자신의 심장 또한 터졌다.\n\n이 일로 인하여 강대한 자들의 섬의 사람들이 거둔 성과는 그러하였으나, 그들은 승리하지 못하였다. 그들 모두 가운데 오직 일곱 사람만이 빠져나왔고, 벤디게이드 브란 자신은 독화살에 발을 맞아 부상을 입었기 때문이다. 그때 빠져나온 사람들은 프리데리, 마나위단, 탈리에신, 그리고 다른 네 사람이었다.\n\n벤디게이드 브란은 그들에게 자신의 머리를 베라고 명하였다. \"그리고 내 머리를 가지고,\" 그가 말했다. \"런던의 화이트 마운트까지 가지고 가서, 얼굴이 프랑스를 향하도록 거기에 묻으라. 그것이 거기 놓여 있는 한, 어떤 적도 이 섬에 발을 들이지 못하리라.\" 그리하여 그들이 그의 머리를 베었고, 이 일곱 사람이 그것을 지니고 나아갔다. 브란웬이 그들과 더불어 여덟 번째였다. 그들이 아베르 알라우에 뭍으로 올라 쉬려고 앉았다. 브란웬은 그들을 알아볼 수 있을까 하여 아일랜드 쪽과 강대한 자들의 섬 쪽을 바라보았다. \"아아!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"비통하도다, 내가 태어났다는 것이. 나로 인하여 두 섬이 멸망하였구나.\" 그러고는 그녀가 신음을 토하니, 거기서 그녀의 심장이 터졌다. 그들은 그녀를 위해 네모진 무덤을 만들어, 알라우 강가에 그녀를 묻었다.\n\n이윽고 일곱 사람이 그 머리를 지니고 앞으로 나아갔다. 그들이 가는 동안, 보라, 한 무리의 남녀가 그들을 마주쳤다. \"무슨 소식이라도 있소?\" 마나위단이 말했다. \"없습니다.\" 그들이 말했다. \"다만 벨리의 아들 카스왈라운이 강대한 자들의 섬을 정복하여, 런던에서 왕관을 쓴 임금이 되었다는 것 말고는.\" \"브란의 아들 카라도크와, 이 섬에 그와 함께 남겨졌던 일곱 사람은 어찌 되었소?\" 그들이 물었다. \"카스왈라운이 그들을 덮쳐 여섯 사람을 죽였고, 카라도크는 그로 인한 슬픔으로 심장이 터졌습니다.\" 그리하여 일곱 사람이 런던을 향해 나아가, 벤디게이드 브란이 그들에게 명한 대로 그 머리를 화이트 마운트에 묻었다.\n\n————\n\n프윌과 리아논 사이에는 아들이 하나 있었으니, 그들은 그 아이를 프리데리라고 이름 지었다. 그리고 그 아이가 다 자랐을 무렵, 그의 아버지 프윌이 세상을 떠났다. 그리고 프리데리는 그윈 글로이의 딸 키크바와 혼인하였다.\n\n이제 마나위단이 아일랜드에서의 전쟁을 마치고 돌아왔는데, 자신의 사촌이 그의 모든 재산을 빼앗아 차지한 것을 알게 되었고, 큰 슬픔과 무거움이 그에게 닥쳤다. \"아아! 가련한 내 신세여!\" 그는 탄식하였다. \"나 말고는 집도 쉴 곳도 없는 자가 아무도 없구나.\" \"주군이시여,\" 프리데리가 말하였다. \"그토록 슬퍼하지 마소서. 당신의 사촌은 강한 자들의 섬의 왕이며, 비록 그가 당신께 잘못을 저질렀다 하나, 당신께서는 땅이나 재산을 두고 권리를 주장하신 적이 한 번도 없으셨습니다.\" \"그렇다,\" 그가 대답하였다. \"하지만 이 사람이 비록 내 사촌이라 할지라도, 내 형제 벤디게이드 브란의 자리에 누군가가 앉아 있는 것을 보는 일은 나를 슬프게 하니, 나는 그와 같은 거처에서 행복할 수가 없도다.\" \"다른 이의 충고를 따르시겠습니까?\" 프리데리가 말하였다. \"나는 충고가 필요하다,\" 그가 대답하였다. \"그 충고란 무엇이냐?\" \"일곱 개의 칸트레브가 제게 속해 있으며,\" 프리데리가 말하였다. \"그곳에는 제 어머니 리아논이 살고 계십니다. 제가 어머니를 당신께 드리고, 일곱 개의 칸트레브도 함께 드리겠습니다. 비록 당신께서 그 칸트레브 외에 아무 재산도 없으시다 한들, 그보다 더 아름다운 것을 가지실 수는 없을 것입니다. 당신과 리아논이 그것을 누리소서. 그리고 만일 어떤 재산을 바라신다 해도 이것을 업신여기지 않으실 것입니다.\" \"그렇지 않다, 족장이여,\" 그가 말하였다. \"하늘이 이 우정에 보답해 주기를! 내가 그대와 함께 가서 리아논을 찾고, 그대의 재산을 살펴보겠노라.\" \"잘 결정하셨습니다,\" 그가 대답하였다. \"그리고 제 생각으로는 당신께서는 그분보다 더 말을 잘하는 부인을 들어 보신 적이 없으실 것이며, 그분이 한창때이셨을 적에는 그분보다 더 아름다운 이가 없었습니다. 지금도 그분의 자태는 결코 보기 흉하지 않습니다.\"\n\n그들은 길을 떠났고, 그 여정이 아무리 길었다 한들, 마침내 디베드에 이르렀다. 그리고 리아논과 키크바가 그들을 위해 잔치를 마련하였다. 그때 마나위단과 리아논이 함께 앉아 이야기를 나누기 시작하였다. 그리고 그의 마음과 생각이 그녀를 향해 따뜻해졌으며, 그는 마음속으로 그녀보다 더 우아함과 아름다움으로 가득 찬 부인을 본 적이 없다고 여겼다. \"프리데리여,\" 그가 말하였다. \"나는 그대가 말한 대로 되기를 바라노라.\" \"그 말이란 무엇이었습니까?\" 리아논이 물었다. \"부인이시여,\" 프리데리가 말하였다. \"제가 당신을 흘르의 아들 마나위단의 아내로 드리겠다고 청하였습니다.\" \"그 일에 나는 기꺼이 따르겠노라,\" 리아논이 말하였다. \"나 역시 더없이 기쁘오,\" 마나위단이 말하였다. \"이토록 완전한 우정을 내게 보여 준 이에게 하늘이 보답하기를!\"\n\n그리고 잔치가 끝나기도 전에 그녀는 그의 신부가 되었다. 프리데리가 말하였다. \"여기서 나머지 잔치를 즐기십시오. 저는 잉글랜드로 가서 벨리의 아들 카스왈라운에게 신하의 예를 바치겠습니다.\" \"주군이시여,\" 리아논이 말하였다. \"카스왈라운은 켄트에 있습니다. 그러니 당신께서는 잔치에 머무르시며 그가 더 가까이 올 때까지 기다리셔도 됩니다.\" \"그리하겠소,\" 그가 대답하였다. 그리하여 그들은 잔치를 마쳤다. 그리고 그들은 디베드를 한 바퀴 돌며 사냥을 하고 즐거움을 누리기 시작하였다. 그리고 그들이 그 땅을 두루 다녀 보니, 그보다 더 살기 좋은 땅도, 더 나은 사냥터도, 더 풍성한 꿀과 물고기도 본 적이 없었다. 그리고 이 네 사람 사이의 우정이 어찌나 깊었던지, 그들은 밤에도 낮에도 서로 떨어지려 하지 않았다.\n\n그리고 이 모든 일이 한창일 때, 그는 옥스퍼드에 있는 카스왈라운에게로 가서 신하의 예를 바쳤다. 그리고 그곳에서 그는 명예로운 대접을 받았으며, 신하의 예를 바친 일로 크게 칭송을 받았다.\n\n그리고 그가 돌아온 뒤, 프리데리와 마나위단은 잔치를 벌이며 편안함과 즐거움을 누렸다. 그리고 그들은 나르베르스에서 잔치를 시작하였으니, 그곳이 으뜸가는 궁전이었기 때문이다. 그리고 그들이 첫 번째 식사를 마쳤을 때, 그들을 시중들던 이들이 식사를 하는 동안, 그들은 일어나 밖으로 나가 고르세드, 곧 나르베르스의 언덕으로 향하였고, 그들의 수행원들도 함께였다. 그리고 그들이 그렇게 앉아 있는데, 보라, 한 차례 천둥소리가 울렸으며, 그 천둥 폭풍의 격렬함과 함께, 보라! 안개가 내려앉았는데 어찌나 짙었던지 그들 가운데 누구도 서로를 볼 수 없었다. 그리고 안개가 걷힌 뒤에는 사방이 환하게 밝아졌다. 그리고 그들이 늘 가축과 무리와 거처를 보던 곳을 바라보니, 이제는 아무것도 보이지 않았다. 집도, 짐승도, 연기도, 불도, 사람도, 거처도 없었고, 다만 궁정의 건물들만이 텅 비고 황량하게 사람 없이 남아 있을 뿐, 그 안에는 사람도 짐승도 없었다. 그리고 진실로 그들의 모든 동료들이 그들에게서 사라져 버렸으니, 무슨 일이 그들에게 닥쳤는지 전혀 알지 못한 채, 오직 이 네 사람만이 남았다.\n\n\"하늘의 이름으로,\" 마나위단이 말하였다. \"궁정의 사람들과 내 모든 군사들은 다 어디로 갔는가? 가서 살펴보세.\"\n\n그리하여 그들은 성으로 갔으나 아무도 보지 못했고, 큰 방으로, 또 침소로 들어갔으나 거기에도 아무도 없었으며, 벌꿀술 저장고와 부엌에도 황량함밖에는 없었다. 그때 그들은 그 땅과 그들이 가진 모든 재산을 두루 다니기 시작하였다. 그리고 그들은 집들과 거처들을 찾아가 보았으나 야수들밖에는 아무것도 발견하지 못하였다. 그리고 그들이 잔치 음식과 모든 양식을 다 먹어 치웠을 때, 그들은 사냥하여 잡은 짐승과 야생 백조의 꿀로 끼니를 이었다.\n\n그리고 어느 날 아침 프리데리와 마나위단이 사냥을 하러 일어나, 개들을 정렬시키고 밖으로 나갔다. 그리고 개들 가운데 몇 마리가 그들 앞으로 달려가 가까이에 있는 덤불에 이르렀다. 그러나 그 덤불에 다다르자마자 개들은 황급히 물러나 사람들에게로 돌아왔으며, 그 털이 잔뜩 곤두서 있었다. \"저 덤불 가까이 가서,\" 프리데리가 말하였다. \"그 안에 무엇이 있는지 봅시다.\" 그리고 그들이 가까이 다가가니, 보라, 순백색의 멧돼지 한 마리가 덤불에서 일어났다. 그러자 사람들에게 부추김을 받은 개들이 그 멧돼지를 향해 돌진하였다. 그러나 멧돼지는 덤불을 떠나 사람들로부터 조금 물러나서는, 사람들이 가까이 올 때까지 물러서지 않고 개들에 맞서 버티고 섰다. 그리고 사람들이 다가오자, 멧돼지는 두 번째로 물러나 달아나기 시작하였다. 그러자 그들은 그 멧돼지를 쫓다가, 마침내 이전에는 돌 하나 건물 하나 본 적이 없던 곳에 갓 지어진 거대하고 드높은 성을 보게 되었다. 그리고 멧돼지는 재빨리 성안으로 달려 들어갔고, 개들도 그 뒤를 따랐다. 이제 멧돼지와 개들이 성안으로 들어가 버리자, 사람들은 이전에는 어떤 건물도 본 적이 없던 곳에 성이 있는 것을 보고 의아해하기 시작하였다. 그리고 그들은 고르세드 꼭대기에서 개들을 보고 들으려 애썼다. 그러나 그들이 거기 있는 동안에는 개 한 마리의 소리도, 개들에 관한 그 무엇도 듣지 못하였다.\n\n\"주군이시여,\" 프리데리가 말하였다. \"제가 성안으로 들어가 개들의 소식을 알아보겠습니다.\" \"진실로,\" 그가 대답하였다. \"그대가 이제껏 한 번도 본 적 없는 이 성안으로 들어가는 것은 어리석은 일일 것이다. 만일 그대가 내 충고를 따른다면, 그 안으로 들어가지 않을 것이다. 이 땅에 마법을 건 자가 누구든, 이 성이 여기에 있게 한 것이다.\" \"진실로,\" 프리데리가 대답하였다. \"저는 이대로 제 개들을 포기할 수가 없습니다.\" 그리고 마나위단이 그에게 베푼 온갖 충고에도 불구하고, 그는 끝내 성으로 들어갔다.\n\n그가 성안에 들어서니, 사람도 짐승도, 멧돼지도 개들도, 집도 거처도, 그 안에서 아무것도 보이지 않았다. 다만 성의 마룻바닥 한가운데에 대리석 세공으로 둘러싸인 샘 하나가 보였고, 그 샘 가장자리의 대리석 판 위에 황금 사발 하나가 놓여 있었으며, 허공으로부터 사슬들이 드리워져 있었는데 그 끝은 보이지 않았다.\n\n그리고 그는 그 황금의 아름다움과 사발의 정교한 세공에 크게 기뻐하였다. 그래서 그는 사발에 다가가 그것을 붙잡았다. 그리고 그가 그것을 붙잡자, 그의 두 손이 사발에 들러붙고, 그의 두 발이 사발이 놓인 판에 들러붙었다. 그리고 그의 모든 기쁨이 그를 떠나, 그는 한마디도 내뱉을 수 없게 되었다. 그리하여 그는 그렇게 서 있었다.\n\n그리고 마나위단은 그날이 거의 저물 때까지 그를 기다렸다. 그리고 저녁 늦게, 프리데리나 개들에 관한 소식을 결코 듣지 못하리라는 것을 확신하고는, 그는 궁전으로 돌아갔다. 그리고 그가 들어서자 리아논이 그를 바라보았다. \"그대의 동료와 그대의 개들은,\" 그녀가 말하였다. \"어디에 있습니까?\" \"보시오,\" 그가 대답하였다. \"내게 닥친 일이 이러하오.\" 그리고 그는 그 모든 것을 그녀에게 이야기하였다. \"그대는 못된 동료였으며,\" 리아논이 말하였다. \"좋은 동료를 잃었습니다.\" 그리고 그 말과 함께 그녀는 밖으로 나가, 그가 일러 준 방향을 따라 성을 향해 나아갔다. 성문은 열려 있는 것을 그녀가 발견하였다. 그녀는 조금도 겁먹지 않고 안으로 들어갔다. 그리고 그녀가 안으로 들어가니, 프리데리가 사발을 붙잡고 있는 것이 보였고, 그녀는 그에게로 다가갔다. \"오 나의 주군이시여,\" 그녀가 말하였다. \"여기서 무엇을 하고 계십니까?\" 그리고 그녀는 그와 함께 사발을 붙잡았다. 그러자 그녀가 그렇게 하니, 그녀의 두 손 또한 사발에 들러붙고, 그녀의 두 발이 판에 들러붙어, 그녀는 한마디도 내뱉을 수 없게 되었다. 그리고 그와 함께 밤이 되니, 보라! 그들 위로 천둥이 닥치고, 안개가 내려앉았다. 그리고 그러자 성이 사라졌고, 그들도 성과 함께 사라졌다.\n\n그윈 글로이의 딸 키크바는 궁전에 자신과 마나위단 외에는 아무도 없는 것을 보고는, 살든 죽든 개의치 않을 만큼 깊이 슬퍼하였다. 그리고 마나위단이 이를 보았다. \"그대가 잘못 생각하고 있소,\" 그가 말하였다. \"만일 나에 대한 두려움 때문에 그렇게 슬퍼한다면 말이오. 하늘을 증인으로 부르건대, 하늘이 그대가 이러하기를 바라는 한, 내가 그대에게 품을 우정보다 더 순수한 우정을 그대는 본 적이 없을 것이오. 그대에게 분명히 말하노니, 내가 젊음의 동틀 무렵에 있다 할지라도, 나는 프리데리에 대한 신의를 지킬 것이며, 그대에게도 그 신의를 지킬 것이오. 그러니 그대에게 두려움이 없기를.\" \"하늘이 당신께 보답하기를!\" 그녀가 말하였다. \"그리고 그것이 바로 제가 당신에 대해 헤아렸던 바입니다.\" 그리고 그 처녀는 그 말에 용기를 얻어 기뻐하였다.\n\n\"진실로, 부인이여,\" 마나위단이 말하였다. \"우리가 여기 머무는 것은 마땅치 않소. 우리는 개들을 잃었고, 먹을 것을 구할 수 없소. 잉글랜드로 갑시다. 거기서라면 우리가 살아갈 방도를 찾기가 가장 쉬울 것이오.\" \"기꺼이, 주군이시여,\" 그녀가 말하였다. \"그리하겠습니다.\" 그리고 그들은 함께 잉글랜드로 길을 떠났다.\n\n\"주군이시여,\" 그녀가 말하였다. \"어떤 생업을 따르시겠습니까? 어울리는 것을 택하소서.\" \"다른 것은 택하지 않겠소,\" 그가 대답하였다. \"다만 신발을 만드는 일을 하겠소.\" \"주군이시여,\" 그녀가 말하였다. \"그러한 생업은 당신처럼 고귀하게 태어난 분께는 어울리지 않습니다.\" \"그래도 나는 그 일을 따르겠소,\" 그가 말하였다. \"저는 그 일에 대해 아무것도 모릅니다,\" 키크바가 말하였다. \"하지만 나는 아오,\" 마나위단이 대답하였다. \"그리고 내가 그대에게 바느질을 가르쳐 주겠소. 우리는 가죽을 무두질하려 하지는 않을 것이고, 다 무두질된 가죽을 사서 그것으로 신발을 만들 것이오.\"\n\n그리하여 그들은 잉글랜드로 들어가, 헤리퍼드까지 이르렀다. 그리고 그들은 신발 만드는 일에 전념하였다. 그리고 그는 그 고을에서 구할 수 있는 가장 좋은 코도반 가죽을 사는 일부터 시작하였으며, 다른 것은 사지 않았다. 그리고 그는 그 고을에서 가장 솜씨 좋은 금세공인과 어울리며, 그로 하여금 신발에 다는 죔쇠를 만들게 하고 그 죔쇠에 금을 입히게 하였다. 그리고 그는 그것이 어떻게 만들어지는지를 눈여겨보아 그 방법을 익혔다. 그리하여 그는 황금 신발을 만든 세 사람 가운데 하나라 불린다. 그리고 그에게서 신발을 구할 수 있게 되자, 그 고을의 어느 제화공에게서도 신발 한 켤레, 양말 한 켤레 사는 이가 없었다. 그러나 제화공들이 자신들의 벌이가 줄어드는 것을 알아채자(마나위단이 일감을 마름질하면 키크바가 그것을 바느질하였기 때문이다), 그들은 함께 모여 의논하였고, 그들을 죽이기로 의견을 모았다. 그리고 그는 이에 대한 경고를 받았으니, 제화공들이 함께 그를 죽이기로 작당하였다는 것을 그는 전해 들었다.\n\n\"주군이시여,\" 키크바가 말하였다. \"어찌하여 이 촌놈들에게서 이런 일을 견디셔야 합니까?\" \"아니오,\" 그가 말하였다. \"우리는 디베드로 돌아갑시다.\" 그리하여 그들은 디베드를 향해 길을 떠났다.\n\n이제 마나위단은 디베드로 돌아가려고 길을 나설 때 밀 한 짐을 가지고 갔다. 그리고 그는 나르베르스를 향해 나아갔고, 거기서 살았다. 그리고 그가 나르베르스를, 그리고 프리데리와 리아논과 함께 사냥하곤 하던 그 땅들을 다시 보았을 때보다 더 기뻤던 적은 없었다. 그리고 그는 물고기를 잡고, 사슴이 숨은 곳에서 사슴을 사냥하는 일에 익숙해졌다. 그리고 그러고 나서 그는 얼마간의 땅을 일구기 시작하였으며, 한 뙈기의 밭을 갈고, 두 번째 밭을 갈고, 세 번째 밭을 갈았다. 그리고 세상의 어떤 밀도 그보다 더 잘 돋아난 적이 없었다. 그리고 세 뙈기의 밭이 완전한 성장으로 무성하게 자라났으며, 그보다 더 아름다운 밀을 본 사람은 아무도 없었다.\n\n그리하여 추수 때가 올 때까지 한 해의 절기들이 지나갔다. 그리고 그가 자신의 밭 한 뙈기를 살펴보러 갔는데, 보라, 그것이 익어 있었다. \"내일 이것을 거두어들이겠다,\" 그가 말하였다. 그리고 그날 밤 그는 나르베르스로 돌아갔고, 이튿날 잿빛 새벽녘에 그 밭을 거두러 갔다. 그리고 그가 거기 이르렀을 때, 그는 헐벗은 짚 외에는 아무것도 발견하지 못하였다. 밀 이삭은 하나하나 줄기에서 잘려 나가, 모든 이삭이 송두리째 실려 가 버렸고, 짚 외에는 아무것도 남아 있지 않았다. 그리고 이에 그는 크게 놀랐다.\n\n그러고 나서 그는 또 다른 밭을 살펴보러 갔는데, 보라, 그것 또한 익어 있었다. \"진실로,\" 그가 말하였다. \"이것을 내일 거두어들이겠다.\" 그리고 이튿날 그는 그것을 거둘 작정으로 왔다. 그리고 그가 거기 이르렀을 때, 그는 헐벗은 짚 외에는 아무것도 발견하지 못하였다. \"오 자비로운 하늘이여!\" 그가 탄식하였다. \"나의 파멸을 시작한 자가 누구든 그것을 마저 끝내고 있으며, 또한 나와 더불어 이 나라를 망쳐 놓았음을 나는 아노라.\"\n\n그러고 나서 그는 세 번째 밭을 살펴보러 갔다. 그리고 그가 거기 이르렀을 때, 그토록 고운 밀은 이제껏 본 적이 없었으며, 이것 또한 익어 있었다. \"내게 화가 미칠지어다,\" 그가 말하였다. \"오늘 밤 여기서 지켜보지 않는다면. 다른 곡식을 가져간 자가 누구든 이것을 가져가려고 똑같이 올 것이며, 그가 누구인지 내가 알아내고 말겠다.\" 그리고 그는 키크바에게 일어난 모든 일을 이야기하였다. \"진실로,\" 그녀가 말하였다. \"어찌하실 작정입니까?\" \"오늘 밤 그 밭을 지켜보겠소,\" 그가 말하였다. 그리고 그는 밭을 지키러 갔다.\n\n그리고 한밤중에 그는 밀밭 사이에서 무언가 움직이는 소리를 들었다. 그리고 그가 바라보니, 보라, 세상에서 가장 거대한 쥐 떼였는데, 헤아릴 수도 가늠할 수도 없을 만큼 많았다. 그리고 쥐들이 밭으로 길을 내며 들어와, 저마다 짚을 타고 올라 제 무게로 그것을 휘어 굽히고는 밀 이삭 하나를 잘라 내어 그것을 실어 가 버리고 줄기만 남길 때까지, 그는 그것이 무엇인지 알지 못하였다. 그리고 그는 거기서 쥐 한 마리가 붙어 있지 않은 짚 하나도 보지 못하였다. 그리고 쥐들은 이삭을 실은 채 모두 제 갈 길로 가 버렸다.\n\n격노와 분노에 차 그는 쥐들에게 달려들었다. 그러나 그것들이 각다귀나 공중의 새였다 한들 그가 따라잡지 못했을 것과 마찬가지로, 그는 한 마리도 따라잡을 수 없었다. 다만 한 마리만은 예외였으니, 그것은 굼떴음에도 어찌나 빨리 가던지 걸어가는 사람이 간신히 따라잡을 정도였다. 그리고 그는 이 한 마리를 쫓아가 붙잡았고, 그것을 자신의 장갑 속에 넣고는 끈으로 장갑의 입구를 묶어, 그것을 지닌 채 궁전으로 돌아왔다. 그리고 그는 키크바가 있는 큰 방으로 와서, 불을 피우고, 그 끈으로 장갑을 못에 걸어 두었다. \"거기 무엇을 가지고 계십니까, 주군이시여?\" 키크바가 말하였다. \"도둑이오,\" 그가 말하였다. \"나의 것을 훔치고 있던 것을 내가 잡았소.\" \"어떤 종류의 도둑이기에, 주군이시여, 당신의 장갑 속에 넣으실 수 있단 말입니까?\" 그녀가 말하였다. 그러자 그는 쥐들이 그가 보던 마지막 밭으로 어떻게 왔는지를 그녀에게 이야기하였다. \"그리고 그것들 가운데 한 마리가 다른 것들보다 덜 날쌔어, 지금 내 장갑 속에 들어 있소. 내일 그것을 목매달 것이오.\" \"나의 주군이시여,\" 그녀가 말하였다. \"이는 놀라운 일입니다. 하지만 당신처럼 위엄 있는 분이 이런 파충류 같은 것을 목매다는 일은 어울리지 않을 것입니다.\" \"내게 화가 미칠지어다,\" 그가 말하였다. \"만일 잡을 수만 있다면 그것들을 모조리 목매달지 않는다면 말이오. 그러니 내가 잡은 것은 목매달겠소.\" \"진실로, 주군이시여,\" 그녀가 말하였다. \"제가 이 파충류를 구해 줄 이유는 없습니다. 다만 당신께 불명예가 미치지 않도록 하려는 것 외에는요. 그러니 주군이시여, 뜻대로 하소서.\"\n\n그러고 나서 그는 그 쥐를 데리고 나르베르스의 언덕으로 갔다. 그리고 그는 언덕의 가장 높은 곳에 갈래진 기둥 두 개를 세웠다. 그리고 그가 이 일을 하고 있는데, 보라, 한 학생이 낡고 초라하고 누덕누덕한 옷차림으로 그에게 다가오는 것이 보였다. 그리고 그가 그곳에서 사람이나 짐승을 본 지가 이제 칠 년이 되었으니, 다만 함께 남아 있다가 그중 둘을 잃은 그 네 사람을 빼고는 아무도 본 적이 없었다.\n\n\"나의 주군이시여,\" 학생이 말하였다. \"당신께 좋은 날이 되기를.\" \"하늘이 그대를 번성케 하기를, 그리고 그대에게 나의 인사를 전하노라! 그리고 어디서 오는 길인가, 학생이여?\" 그가 물었다. \"저는 잉글랜드에서 노래하다가 오는 길입니다, 주군이시여. 그런데 어찌하여 물으십니까?\" \"지난 칠 년 동안,\" 그가 대답하였다. \"나는 여기서 외따로 떨어져 있는 네 사람과, 이 순간의 그대 말고는 아무도 본 적이 없기 때문이다.\" \"진실로, 주군이시여,\" 그가 말하였다. \"저는 이 땅을 지나 제 고장으로 가는 길입니다. 그런데 무슨 일을 하고 계십니까, 주군이시여?\" \"나의 것을 훔치다가 내가 잡은 도둑을 목매달고 있다,\" 그가 말하였다. \"그것이 어떤 종류의 도둑입니까?\" 학생이 물었다. \"당신의 손에 쥐 같은 것이 보입니다만, 당신 같은 신분의 분께서 이런 파충류를 만지는 일은 어울리지 않습니다. 그것을 풀어 주십시오.\" \"하늘에 맹세코, 나는 그것을 풀어 주지 않겠다,\" 그가 말하였다. \"나의 것을 훔치다가 내가 그것을 잡았으니, 나는 그것에 도둑의 형벌을 내릴 것이며, 그것을 목매달 것이다.\" \"주군이시여,\" 그가 말하였다. \"당신 같은 신분의 분이 이런 일을 하시는 것을 보느니, 차라리 제가 동냥으로 받은 일 파운드를 드릴 터이니, 그 파충류를 풀어 주십시오.\" \"나는 그것을 풀어 주지도 않을 것이며,\" 그가 말하였다. \"팔지도 않겠다.\" \"뜻대로 하소서, 주군이시여,\" 그가 대답하였다. \"저는 개의치 않습니다.\" 그리고 학생은 제 갈 길로 갔다.\n\n그리고 그가 갈래진 두 기둥 위에 가로대를 올려놓고 있는데, 보라, 한 사제가 마구를 두른 말을 타고 그에게 다가왔다. \"좋은 날이 되기를, 주군이시여,\" 그가 말하였다. \"하늘이 그대를 번성케 하기를!\" 마나위단이 말하였다. \"그대의 축복을.\" \"하늘의 축복이 그대에게 있기를! 그런데 무엇을 하고 계십니까, 주군이시여?\" \"나의 것을 훔치다가 내가 잡은 도둑을 목매달고 있다,\" 그가 말하였다. \"어떤 종류의 도둑입니까, 주군이시여?\" 그가 물었다. \"쥐 모양의,\" 그가 대답하였다. \"한 마리 짐승이다. 그것이 나의 것을 훔쳐 왔으니, 나는 그것에 도둑의 형벌을 내리는 중이다.\" \"주군이시여,\" 그가 말하였다. \"당신께서 이 파충류를 만지시는 것을 보느니, 차라리 제가 그 자유를 사겠습니다.\" \"하늘에 고백하건대, 나는 그것을 팔지도, 풀어 주지도 않겠다.\" \"사실, 주군이시여, 그것은 사들일 만한 값어치도 없습니다. 하지만 당신께서 이런 파충류를 만져 스스로를 더럽히시는 것을 보느니, 그것을 놓아주시도록 제가 삼 파운드를 드리겠습니다.\" \"하늘에 맹세코, 나는,\" 그가 말하였다. \"그것에 대해 어떤 값도 받지 않겠다. 마땅히 그래야 할 대로, 그것은 목매달릴 것이다.\" 그리고 사제는 제 갈 길로 갔다.\n\n그러고 나서 그는 쥐의 목에 끈으로 올가미를 씌웠다. 그리고 그가 그것을 끌어 올리려 하는데, 보라, 그는 짐말들과 시종들을 거느린 한 주교의 행렬을 보았다. 그리고 주교 자신이 그에게 다가왔다. 그리고 그는 일손을 멈추었다. \"주교님이시여,\" 그가 말하였다. \"당신의 축복을.\" \"하늘의 축복이 그대에게 있기를!\" 그가 말하였다. \"무슨 일을 하고 계십니까?\" \"나의 것을 훔치다가 내가 잡은 도둑을 목매달고 있습니다,\" 그가 말하였다. \"그대의 손에 보이는 것이 쥐가 아닙니까?\" \"그렇습니다,\" 그가 대답하였다. \"그리고 그것이 나의 것을 훔쳤습니다.\" \"허,\" 그가 말하였다. \"내가 이 파충류의 처형에 마침 당도하였으니, 그것을 그대에게서 속량하겠소. 그것의 값으로 그대에게 칠 파운드를 주겠소. 그것도 당신 같은 신분의 분이 이런 천한 파충류를 죽이는 것을 보느니 그러는 것이오. 그것을 풀어 주시오, 그러면 그 돈을 가질 것이오.\" \"하늘에 분명히 말하건대, 나는 그것을 풀어 주지 않겠습니다.\" \"이것으로 풀어 주지 않겠다면, 그것을 놓아주도록 내가 그대에게 현금 이십사 파운드를 주겠소.\" \"하늘에 맹세코, 나는 그 두 배를 준다 해도 그것을 놓아주지 않겠습니다,\" 그가 말하였다. \"이것으로 놓아주지 않겠다면, 이 들판에 그대가 보는 모든 말과, 일곱 짐의 짐과, 그것들을 실은 일곱 마리의 말을 그대에게 주겠소.\" \"하늘에 맹세코, 나는 그러지 않겠습니다,\" 그가 대답하였다. \"이것으로 그것을 놓아주지 않겠다면, 그대가 원하는 어떤 값으로든 그렇게 하시오.\" \"리아논과 프리데리가 풀려나기를 바랍니다,\" 그가 말하였다. \"그것은 그대가 가질 것이오,\" 그가 대답하였다. \"하늘에 맹세코, 나는 아직 쥐를 놓아주지 않겠습니다.\" \"그러면 무엇을 더 바라는가?\" \"디베드의 일곱 칸트레브에서 마법과 환영이 걷히기를 바랍니다.\" \"이것 또한 그대가 가질 것이오. 그러니 쥐를 풀어 주시오.\" \"하늘에 맹세코, 나는 그것을 풀어 주지 않겠습니다,\" 그가 말하였다. \"이 쥐가 누구인지 알기 전까지는요.\" \"그것은 나의 아내요.\" \"어찌하여 그것이 내게 왔습니까?\" \"그대의 것을 약탈하기 위해서요,\" 그가 대답하였다. \"나는 킬웨드의 아들 흘로이드이며, 디베드의 일곱 칸트레브에 마법을 건 것이 나요. 그리고 내가 그 마법을 건 것은, 내가 클루드의 아들 가울에게 품었던 우정에서, 그의 원수를 갚기 위함이었소. 그리고 나는 아우윈의 아들 프윌이 그에게 행하였던 〈자루 속 오소리〉 놀이에 대한 원수를 프리데리에게 갚았소. 그리고 그대가 그 땅에 와서 산다는 것이 알려지자, 나의 식솔들이 와서 자신들을 쥐로 변신시켜 그대의 곡식을 망치게 해 달라고 내게 간청하였소. 그리고 그들은 첫째 날 밤과 둘째 날 밤에 가서 그대의 두 곡식을 망쳤소. 그리고 셋째 날 밤에는 나의 아내와 궁정의 부인들이 내게 와서 자신들을 변신시켜 달라고 간청하였소. 그래서 내가 그들을 변신시켰소. 이제 그녀는 평소의 건강 상태가 아니오. 그리고 그녀가 평소의 건강 상태였다면, 그대는 그녀를 따라잡을 수 없었을 것이오. 하지만 이런 일이 일어나 그녀가 잡혔으니, 내가 그대에게 프리데리와 리아논을 돌려주고, 디베드에서 마법과 환영을 걷어 가겠소. 그러니 그녀를 풀어 주시오.\" \"나는 아직 그녀를 풀어 주지 않겠습니다.\" \"그러면 무엇을 더 바라는가?\" 그가 물었다. \"나는 디베드의 일곱 칸트레브에 더 이상 마법이 없으며, 이후로도 그것에 마법이 걸리지 않기를 바랍니다. 또한 이 일에 대한 복수가 프리데리에게도, 리아논에게도, 나에게도 결코 행해지지 않기를 바랍니다.\" \"이 모든 것을 그대가 가질 것이오. 그리고 진실로 그대는 이것을 청함에 있어 현명하게 처신하였소. 이 모든 화가 그대의 머리 위에 떨어졌을 것이오.\" \"그렇습니다,\" 그가 말하였다. \"그것이 두려웠기에 내가 이것을 요구한 것입니다.\" \"이제 나의 아내를 풀어 주시오.\" \"나는 그러지 않겠습니다,\" 그가 말하였다. \"프리데리와 리아논이 풀려나 내 곁에 있는 것을 볼 때까지는요.\" \"보시오, 여기 그들이 오고 있소,\" 그가 대답하였다.\n\n그리고 그러자, 보라, 프리데리와 리아논이었다. 그리고 그는 일어나 그들을 맞이하고, 그들에게 인사하며, 그들 곁에 앉았다. \"아, 족장이여, 이제 나의 아내를 풀어 주시오,\" 주교가 말하였다. \"그대는 청한 모든 것을 받지 않았소?\" \"기꺼이 그녀를 풀어 주겠습니다,\" 그가 말하였다. 그리고 그러자 그는 그녀를 풀어 주었다.\n\n그러고 나서 그는 마법의 지팡이로 그녀를 쳤고, 그녀는 이제껏 본 가운데 가장 아름다운 젊은 여인으로 다시 변하였다.\n\n\"그대의 땅을 둘러보시오,\" 그가 말하였다. \"그러면 그대는 그것이 모두 가장 좋았던 시절처럼 경작되고 사람들로 가득 찬 것을 볼 것이오.\" 그리고 그는 일어나 밖을 내다보았다. 그리고 그가 내다보았을 때, 그는 모든 땅이 경작되어 가축 떼와 거처로 가득 찬 것을 보았다.\n\n그리하여 마비노기의 이 부분이 끝난다.\n\n————\n\n앞의 이야기를 암시하는 다음의 언급은 시인 사우디가 1802년 6월 6일 자로 존 리크먼 님에게 보낸 편지에서 발견된다.\n\n\"당신은 마비노게온을 읽으실 텐데, 그것에 관하여 제가 당신께 이야기를 드렸어야 했습니다. 그 마지막 편에 나오는, 더없이 기이하고 아라비아풍인 쥐 이야기에는 동냥하는 학생에 대한 언급이 있는데, 그것이 연대를 짐작하는 데 도움이 됩니다. 그러나 킴리족은 그러한 이야기를 만들어 낼 수 있는 상상력을 대체 어디서 얻었을까요? 하늘에서 사슬에 매달려 늘어진 그 사발의 마법은 「아라비안 나이트」의 더없이 거친 정신을 담고 있습니다. 그러한 허구가 웨일스어로 존재한다는 것에 저는 완전히 경악하였습니다. 그것들은 로맨스의 기원에 대해 아무런 빛도 던져 주지 않으니, 모든 것이 우리가 그 용어로 의미하는 바와는 전혀 다르기 때문입니다. 하지만 그것들은 허구의 새로운 세계를 열어 줍니다. 그리고 만일 그 언어의 연대가 십이 세기나 십삼 세기쯤으로 정해진다면, 저는 그 신화적 실체가 훨씬 더 이른 시대의 것이라 생각하지 않을 수 없습니다. 아마도 최초의 정착민이나 정복자 가운데 누군가가 동방에서 가져온 것이겠지요.\"\n\n————\n\n# KILWICH AND OLWEN\n\n켈리돈 왕자의 아들 킬리드는 자신을 도와줄 아내를 얻고자 했는데, 그가 택한 아내는 안라우드 왕자의 딸 골레우디드였다. 그리고 두 사람이 부부가 된 뒤에 백성들은 그들에게 후사가 있기를 기도해 올렸다. 그리하여 백성들의 기도 덕분에 두 사람은 아들을 얻었으니, 그 이름을 킬위치라 하였다.\n\n이 일이 있은 뒤에 소년의 어머니인 안라우드 왕자의 딸 골레우디드가 병이 들었다. 그러자 그녀는 남편을 자기 곁으로 불러 이렇게 말했다. \"이 병으로 저는 죽게 될 것이고, 당신은 다른 아내를 얻으시겠지요. 본디 아내란 주님께서 내려 주시는 선물이오나, 당신이 아들에게 해를 끼친다면 그것은 옳지 못한 일이옵니다. 그러니 제 무덤에 두 송이 꽃이 핀 들장미가 돋는 것을 보시기 전까지는 다른 아내를 얻지 마시기를 당신께 당부드립니다.\" 그러자 그는 그녀에게 그리하겠노라 약속했다. 그러자 그녀는 무덤에 잡초가 자라지 않도록 해마다 무덤을 손질해 달라고 그에게 간청했다. 그리하여 왕비는 죽었다. 그리하여 왕은 매일 아침마다 시종을 보내어 무덤에 무엇이 돋아나는지 살피게 하였다. 그런데 일곱 해째가 저물 무렵에 그들은 왕비에게 했던 약속을 소홀히 하게 되었다.\n\n어느 날 왕은 사냥을 나갔다. 그리고 그는 무덤을 살펴보고, 또 자신이 아내를 얻을 때가 되었는지 알아보고자 매장지로 말을 달려갔다. 그리고 왕은 들장미를 보았다. 왕은 그것을 보자 어디서 아내를 구할 것인지 의논했다. 그의 한 고문이 말했다. \"전하께 잘 어울릴 아내를 제가 알고 있사옵니다. 도게드 왕의 아내이옵니다.\" 그리하여 그들은 그녀를 찾으러 가기로 결정했다. 그리고 그들은 그 왕을 죽이고 그의 아내를 데려왔다. 그리고 그들은 그 왕의 영지를 정복했다. 그리하여 그는 도게드 왕의 미망인, 곧 이스파다덴 펜카우르의 누이와 혼인했다.\n\n어느 날 그의 계모가 킬위치에게 말했다. \"네가 아내를 얻는 것이 좋겠구나.\" \"저는 아직 혼인할 나이가 아니옵니다.\" 젊은이가 대답했다. 그러자 그녀가 그에게 말했다. \"내 너에게 일러두건대, 이스파다덴 펜카우르의 딸 올웬을 얻기 전에는 어떤 아내도 너에게 어울리지 않는 것이 너의 운명이니라.\" 그러자 젊은이는 얼굴을 붉혔고, 비록 그 처녀를 한 번도 본 적이 없었음에도 그녀를 향한 사랑이 온몸으로 퍼져 나갔다. 그러자 그의 아버지가 그에게 물었다. \"아들아, 너에게 무슨 일이 생긴 것이며, 무엇이 너를 괴롭히는 것이냐?\" \"제 계모께서 이스파다덴 펜카우르의 딸 올웬을 얻기 전에는 제가 결코 아내를 얻지 못하리라 일러 주셨습니다.\" \"그것은 너에게 쉬운 일이 될 것이다.\" 그의 아버지가 대답했다. \"아서가 너의 사촌이니라. 그러니 아서에게 가서 머리카락을 자르고, 이 일을 그에게 청하여 은혜를 구하도록 하여라.\"\n\n그리하여 젊은이는 잿빛 얼룩이 진 머리를 가진, 네 해를 묵어 사지가 단단하고 조개 모양의 발굽을 가진 군마에 올라타 박차를 가하며 앞으로 나아갔다. 말의 머리에는 사슬로 엮은 황금 굴레가 씌워져 있었고, 그 등에는 값비싼 황금 안장이 얹혀 있었다. 그리고 젊은이의 손에는 날카롭고 잘 단련된 강철 촉을 단 은빛 창 두 자루가 들려 있었으니, 그 길이는 세 엘이었고, 그 날은 바람조차 베어 피를 흘리게 할 만큼 예리하였고, 유월의 이슬이 가장 무겁게 맺힐 무렵 갈대 잎 끝에서 떨어지는 이슬방울보다도 빨랐다. 그의 넓적다리에는 황금 자루가 달린 검이 차여 있었으니, 그 날은 도금되어 있었고, 하늘의 번개와 같은 빛을 띤 황금 십자가 상감으로 새겨져 있었다. 그의 전쟁용 뿔나팔은 상아로 만들어진 것이었다. 그의 앞에는 어깨에서 귀에까지 이르는 루비로 된 단단한 목걸이를 두른, 가슴이 흰 얼룩무늬 사냥개 두 마리가 있었다. 그리고 왼편에 있던 놈은 오른편으로 뛰어넘어 가고, 오른편에 있던 놈은 왼편으로 뛰어넘어 가면서, 마치 두 마리의 바다제비처럼 그의 주위에서 뛰놀았다. 그리고 그의 준마는 네 발굽으로 네 무더기의 흙을 차올렸으니, 그것은 마치 그의 머리 둘레에서, 때로는 위로 때로는 아래로 나는 네 마리의 제비와도 같았다. 그의 몸에는 네 귀퉁이가 진 자줏빛 천이 둘려 있었고, 그 귀퉁이마다 황금 사과가 달려 있었으며, 그 사과 하나하나는 암소 백 마리의 값어치가 있었다. 그리고 그의 신발과 등자에는 무릎에서 발끝까지 암소 삼백 마리의 값어치가 나가는 귀한 황금이 박혀 있었다. 그리고 그가 아서의 궁전 문을 향해 나아갈 때, 그의 준마의 걸음이 어찌나 가벼웠던지 그 발밑에서는 풀잎 하나 휘어지지 않았다.\n\n젊은이가 입을 열었다. \"문지기가 있는가?\" \"있소. 그리고 그대가 잠자코 있지 않는다면, 환대받지 못할 것이오. 나는 정월 초하루마다 아서의 문지기 노릇을 하는 자라오.\" \"문을 열어 주게.\" \"나는 열지 않겠소.\" \"어찌하여 열지 않는가?\" \"고기에는 칼이 꽂혀 있고, 뿔잔에는 술이 담겨 있으며, 아서의 홀에서는 흥겨운 잔치가 벌어지고 있소. 그리고 특권을 가진 나라의 왕자나 자기 솜씨를 가지고 오는 장인이 아니면 누구도 그 안에 들어갈 수 없소. 허나 그대의 개들과 말에게는 먹이가 마련될 것이며, 그대에게는 후추를 친 구운 고깃점과 감미로운 포도주와 흥겨운 노래가 마련될 것이오. 그리고 아서의 궁전 경내에 들어오지 못하는 이방인과 다른 나라의 자제들이 먹는 손님방에서, 그대에게는 쉰 사람 몫의 음식이 차려질 것이오. 그곳에서 그대는 궁정에서 아서와 함께 지내는 것보다 못한 대접을 받지 않을 것이오. 한 귀부인이 그대의 잠자리를 매만지고, 노래로 그대를 잠재워 줄 것이오. 그리고 내일 이른 아침, 오늘 이리로 온 무리에게 문이 열릴 때, 그대를 위해 먼저 그 문이 열릴 것이고, 그대는 아서의 홀에서 윗자리에서 아랫자리에 이르기까지 그대가 택하는 자리에 앉을 수 있을 것이오.\" 젊은이가 말했다. \"그리하지 않겠다. 그대가 문을 연다면 좋은 일이다. 그러나 그대가 문을 열지 않는다면, 나는 그대의 주군에게 치욕을 안기고 그대에게 악평을 가져다줄 것이다. 그리고 나는 바로 이 문 앞에서 일찍이 들어 본 적 없는 가장 끔찍한 세 차례의 함성을 지를 것이다.\" \"그대가 아서의 궁전의 법도를 거슬러 무슨 소란을 일으킨다 하더라도,\" 문지기 글레울위드가 말했다. \"내가 먼저 가서 아서와 이야기하기 전까지는 그대는 그 안에 들어가지 못할 것이오.\"\n\n그리하여 글레울위드는 홀 안으로 들어갔다. 그러자 아서가 그에게 말했다. \"문에서 무슨 소식이라도 가져왔느냐?\" \"제 인생의 절반이 지나갔고,\" 글레울위드가 말했다. \"전하의 인생의 절반도 지나갔습니다. 저는 전에 카에르 세와 아세에, 사크와 살라크에, 로토르와 포토르에 있었사옵고, 큰 인도와 작은 인도에도 가 보았으며, 유럽과 아프리카에도, 또 코르시카의 섬들에도 가 보았고, 전하께서 동방에서 그리스를 정복하실 때 그 자리에 있었사옵니다. 그곳에서 우리는 잘생긴 아홉 명의 지엄한 군주를 보았으나, 지금 문간에 있는 그 사람만큼 위엄을 갖춘 자는 일찍이 본 적이 없사옵니다.\" 그러자 아서가 말했다. \"걸어서 이곳에 들어왔다면, 달려서 돌아가거라. 네가 말한 그런 사람을 비바람 속에 세워 두는 것은 온당치 못한 일이다.\" 케이가 말했다. \"제 벗의 손을 두고 맹세하건대, 전하께서 제 충언을 따르신다면 그자 때문에 궁정의 법도를 깨뜨리지는 않으실 것입니다.\" \"그렇지 않다, 복된 케이여,\" 아서가 말했다. \"사람들이 우리를 찾아오는 것은 우리에게 명예로운 일이며, 우리의 예우가 클수록 우리의 명성과 영예와 영광 또한 그만큼 커질 것이다.\"\n\n그리하여 글레울위드는 문으로 가서 킬위치 앞에서 문을 열었다. 그리고 모두가 문 앞의 디딤대에서 말에서 내렸으나, 그는 말에서 내리지 않고 자신의 군마를 탄 채 안으로 들어갔다. 그러고는 이렇게 말했다. \"이 섬의 지엄한 통치자시여, 그대에게 인사를 올립니다. 그리고 이 인사가 가장 높은 자에게 못지않게 가장 낮은 자에게도 미치기를, 또한 그대의 손님들과 전사들과 족장들에게도 똑같이 미치기를 바랍니다. 모두가 그대 자신과 마찬가지로 온전히 이 인사를 받기를 바랍니다. 그리고 그대의 은총과 명성과 영광이 이 섬 전역에 걸쳐 온전하기를 바랍니다.\" \"그대에게도 인사를 보내노라,\" 아서가 말했다. \"내 전사 두 사람 사이에 앉으라. 그대 앞에는 악사들이 있을 것이고, 그대가 이곳에 머무는 동안에는 왕좌를 타고난 왕의 특권을 누릴 것이다. 그리고 내가 이 궁정의 방문객과 이방인에게 선물을 나누어 줄 때, 내가 시작할 무렵 그것들이 그대의 손에 먼저 들어갈 것이다.\" 젊은이가 말했다. \"저는 고기와 술을 축내려고 이곳에 온 것이 아닙니다. 그러나 제가 구하는 은혜를 얻는다면, 저는 그것을 그대에게 갚고 그대를 칭송할 것입니다. 허나 그것을 얻지 못한다면, 저는 그대의 명성이 미친 데까지 세상의 사방으로 그대를 헐뜯는 말을 가지고 나아갈 것입니다.\" 그러자 아서가 말했다. \"족장이여, 그대가 이곳에 머물지 않겠다 하니, 바람이 말리고 비가 적시고 해가 도는 곳, 또 바다가 에워싸고 땅이 펼쳐진 곳에 이르기까지, 그대의 혀가 무엇을 이름하든 그대는 그 은혜를 받게 될 것이다. 다만 나의 배 프리드웬과 나의 망토, 나의 검 칼리번, 나의 창 롱고미안트, 그리고 나의 아내 귀네비어만은 예외다. 하늘의 진리를 두고 맹세하건대, 그대는 그것을 기꺼이 얻을 것이니, 무엇이든 원하는 것을 이름하라.\" \"저는 그대가 제 머리카락을 축복해 주시기를 바랍니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"그것을 그대에게 허락하노라.\"\n\n그리하여 아서는 황금 빗과 고리가 은으로 된 가위를 들어 그의 머리카락을 빗어 주었다. 그리고 아서는 그가 누구인지 물었다. \"내 마음이 그대에게 따뜻해지는 것을 보니, 그대가 내 핏줄을 이은 자임을 알겠다. 그러니 그대가 누구인지 말해 보라.\" \"말씀드리겠습니다.\" 젊은이가 말했다. \"저는 킬위치, 켈리돈 왕자의 아들 킬리드의 아들이며, 제 어머니 골레우디드, 곧 안라우드 왕자의 딸의 소생입니다.\" \"그것은 참이로다,\" 아서가 말했다. \"그대는 나의 사촌이다. 그대가 무슨 은혜를 청하든, 그대의 혀가 무엇을 이름하든 그대는 그것을 받게 될 것이다.\" \"하늘의 진리와 그대 왕국의 신의로써 그것을 맹세해 주십시오.\" \"기꺼이 그대에게 맹세하노라.\" \"그러면 저는 그대께 청하옵건대, 이스파다덴 펜카우르의 딸 올웬을 제 아내로 얻게 해 주십시오. 그리고 이 은혜를 저는 그대의 전사들의 손에도 똑같이 구합니다. 저는 그것을 케이와 베드위르에게서 구하며, 또한 누드의 아들 그윈, 게레인트의 아들 가드위, 플레우두르 플람 왕자, 프랑스의 왕 이오나, 셀기의 아들 셀, 음유시인들의 우두머리 탈리에신, 에르빈의 아들 게레인트, 케이의 아들 가란윈, 베드위르의 아들 암렌, 올위드의 아들 올, 주교 베드윈, 우두머리 귀부인 귀네비어와 그녀의 누이 귀엔히와크, 우리엔의 딸 모르베드, 위풍당당한 처녀 그웬리안 데그, 변함없는 처녀 루드의 딸 크레이딜라드, 그리고 반인반수 킨벨린의 딸 에와에다에게서 구합니다.\" 킬리드의 아들 킬위치는 자신의 은혜를 얻고자 이 모든 이를 두고 간청했다.\n\n그러자 아서가 말했다. \"오 족장이여, 나는 그대가 말하는 처녀에 대해서도, 그녀의 친족에 대해서도 들은 바가 없으나, 기꺼이 그녀를 찾으러 전령들을 보내겠노라. 그녀를 찾을 시간을 내게 다오.\" 그러자 젊은이가 말했다. \"오늘 밤부터 한 해가 저무는 그날까지 기꺼이 그렇게 하도록 허락하겠습니다.\" 그리하여 아서는 그 처녀를 찾으러 자신의 영토 안 모든 땅으로 전령들을 보냈고, 한 해가 저물 무렵 아서의 전령들은 첫날과 마찬가지로 올웬에 대한 어떤 지식이나 소식도 얻지 못한 채 돌아왔다. 그러자 킬위치가 말했다. \"모두가 자신의 은혜를 받았으나, 저는 아직 제 은혜를 얻지 못했습니다. 저는 떠나겠으며, 그대의 명예를 가지고 떠나겠습니다.\" 그러자 케이가 말했다. \"성급한 족장이여! 그대가 아서를 책망하는 것인가? 우리와 함께 가자. 그리고 그대가 그 처녀가 이 세상에 존재하지 않음을 인정하거나, 우리가 그녀를 얻기 전까지 우리는 헤어지지 않을 것이다.\" 그러고 나서 케이가 일어섰다. 그리고 아서는 베드위르를 불렀으니, 그는 케이가 떠나는 어떤 모험에서도 결코 물러서는 법이 없는 자였다. 이 섬 전역에서 아서 한 사람을 제외하고는 누구도 그의 날쌤에 견줄 자가 없었고, 비록 그가 외팔이였으나 전장에서 그보다 더 빨리 피를 흘리게 할 수 있는 전사는 세 사람도 없었다.\n\n그리고 아서는 길잡이 킨델리그를 불렀다. \"그대도 이 원정에 족장과 함께 가도록 하라.\" 그는 한 번도 본 적 없는 땅에서도 자기 고향에서만큼이나 훌륭한 길잡이였기 때문이다.\n\n아서는 모든 언어를 아는 구르히르 그왈스타트를 불렀다.\n\n아서는 그위아르의 아들 가웨인을 불렀으니, 그는 자신이 찾아 떠난 모험을 이루지 않고는 결코 집으로 돌아오는 법이 없었기 때문이다.\n\n그리고 아서는 테이르그웨드의 아들 메네우를 불렀으니, 그들이 야만의 땅으로 들어갈 경우, 그가 그들에게 주문과 환영을 걸어 그들은 누구나 볼 수 있으되 누구도 그들을 보지 못하게 하기 위함이었다.\n\n그들은 광활한 평원에 이를 때까지 길을 갔는데, 그곳에서 그들은 세상의 성들 가운데 가장 아름다운 거대한 성을 보았다. 그리고 그들이 그 성 앞에 이르렀을 때, 그들은 거대한 양 떼를 보았다. 그리고 둔덕 꼭대기에는 한 목자가 양을 지키고 있었다. 그리고 그의 몸에는 가죽으로 만든 깔개가 둘려 있었고, 그의 곁에는 아홉 해를 묵은 군마보다 더 큰 털북숭이 맹견이 있었다.\n\n그러자 케이가 말했다. \"구르히르 그왈스타트, 그대가 가서 저기 있는 사람에게 인사를 건네라.\" \"케이,\" 그가 말했다. \"나는 그대 자신보다 더 멀리 가지 않기로 했소.\" \"그럼 함께 가세.\" 케이가 대답했다. 메네우가 말했다. \"그리로 가기를 두려워 마시오. 내가 저 개에게 주문을 걸어 아무도 해치지 못하게 하겠소.\" 그리하여 그들은 목자가 있는 둔덕으로 올라가 그에게 말했다. \"어떻게 지내시오, 목자여?\" \"당신들 못지않게 내게도 좋은 일이 있기를.\" \"그대가 지키는 양은 누구의 것이며, 저기 있는 성은 누구의 것이오?\" \"당신들은 정말 어리석구려! 이것이 이스파다덴 펜카우르의 성인 줄도 모르다니. 그리고 당신들은 누구요?\" \"우리는 아서가 보낸 사절단으로, 이스파다덴 펜카우르의 딸 올웬을 찾으러 왔소.\" \"오 사람들이여! 하늘의 자비가 당신들에게 깃들기를. 무슨 일이 있어도 그 일은 하지 마시오. 이 일로 이곳에 온 자치고 살아 돌아간 자는 아무도 없소.\" 그러고 나서 목자가 일어섰다. 그리고 그가 일어서자 킬위치가 그에게 황금 반지 하나를 주었다. 그리고 그는 집으로 가서 그 반지를 아내에게 주어 간수하게 했다. 그리고 그녀는 그것을 받자 이렇게 말했다. \"이 반지가 어디서 났나요? 당신은 본디 행운을 누리는 사람이 아닌데.\" \"여보, 이 반지의 주인을 당신은 오늘 저녁 이곳에서 보게 될 것이오.\" \"그게 누구인가요?\" 여인이 물었다. \"킬위치, 골레우디드, 곧 안라우드 왕자의 딸의 소생인 킬리드의 아들로, 올웬을 아내로 얻으러 온 자라오.\" 그리고 그 말을 들은 그녀는, 자기 누이의 아들인 조카가 자기에게 온다는 것에 기뻐했고, 또한 그 일로 이곳에 온 자치고 살아서 떠나는 이를 한 번도 본 적이 없었기에 슬퍼했다.\n\n그리하여 사람들은 목자의 집 문으로 나아갔다. 그리고 그녀가 그들의 발소리가 다가오는 것을 듣자, 기뻐하며 그들을 맞으러 달려 나왔다. 그리고 케이는 장작더미에서 통나무 하나를 낚아챘다. 그리고 그녀가 그들을 맞이하며 그들의 목을 끌어안으려 했다. 그러자 케이가 통나무를 그녀의 두 손 사이에 끼워 넣었고, 그녀가 그것을 어찌나 세게 쥐었던지 그것은 비틀린 똬리가 되고 말았다. \"오 여인이여,\" 케이가 말했다. \"그대가 나를 이렇게 쥐었더라면, 누구도 다시는 내게 애정을 품지 못했을 것이오. 이것은 고약한 사랑일 것이오.\" 그들은 집 안으로 들어가 대접을 받았고, 곧이어 모두 즐기러 밖으로 나갔다. 그러자 여인은 굴뚝 모퉁이 앞에 있던 돌 궤짝을 열었고, 그 안에서 노랗고 곱슬곱슬한 머리의 젊은이가 일어섰다. 구르히르가 말했다. \"이 젊은이를 숨겨 두다니 안타까운 일이오. 그에게 이렇게 닥친 것이 그 자신의 죄 때문이 아님을 나는 알겠소.\" \"이 아이는 그저 남은 자식일 뿐이라오.\" 여인이 말했다. \"이스파다덴 펜카우르가 내 아들 스물셋을 죽였고, 나는 이 아이에 대해서도 다른 아이들에 대해서와 마찬가지로 더는 희망을 품지 않는다오.\" 그러자 케이가 말했다. \"그를 내 동무로 삼게 하시오. 그리고 나도 그와 함께 죽지 않는 한 그는 죽지 않을 것이오.\" 그리고 그들은 음식을 먹었다. 그리고 여인이 그들에게 물었다. \"당신들은 무슨 용무로 이곳에 왔나요?\" \"우리는 이 젊은이를 위해 올웬을 찾으러 왔소.\" 그러자 여인이 말했다. \"하늘의 이름으로 청하건대, 성에서 아직 누구도 당신들을 보지 못했으니, 왔던 곳으로 다시 돌아가시오.\" \"하늘이 우리의 증인이니, 우리는 그 처녀를 보기 전까지 돌아가지 않을 것이오. 그녀가 사람들 눈에 띌 만큼 이곳에 온 적이 있소?\" \"그녀는 토요일마다 이곳에 와서 머리를 감는데, 머리를 감는 그릇에 자기 반지를 모두 두고 가며, 그것을 가지러 자기가 직접 오는 일도, 전령을 보내는 일도 결코 없다오.\" \"사람을 보내면 그녀가 이곳에 오겠소?\" \"하늘은 아시거니와 나는 내 영혼을 망치지도, 나를 믿는 자들을 배신하지도 않겠소. 당신들이 그녀를 해치지 않겠노라 내게 신의를 맹세하지 않는 한, 나는 그녀에게 사람을 보내지 않겠소.\" \"우리가 맹세하겠소.\" 그들이 말했다. 그리하여 전갈이 보내졌고, 그녀가 왔다.\n\n그 처녀는 불꽃 빛깔의 비단 옷을 입었고, 목에는 귀한 에메랄드와 루비가 박힌 붉은 황금 목걸이를 두르고 있었다. 그녀의 머리는 금작화 꽃보다도 더 노랬고, 그녀의 살결은 물결의 거품보다도 더 희었으며, 그녀의 손과 손가락은 풀밭 샘물의 물보라 속에 핀 바람꽃의 꽃송이보다도 더 고왔다. 잘 훈련된 매의 눈도 그녀의 눈보다 더 빛나지는 못했다. 그녀의 가슴은 흰 백조의 가슴보다도 더 눈처럼 희었고, 그녀의 뺨은 가장 붉은 장미보다도 더 붉었다. 그녀를 본 자는 누구나 그녀를 향한 사랑으로 가득 찼다. 그녀가 밟는 곳마다 흰 토끼풀이 네 잎씩 돋아났다. 그래서 그녀는 올웬이라 불렸다.\n\n그녀는 집 안으로 들어와 맨 앞자리 의자에서 킬위치의 곁에 앉았다. 그리고 그는 그녀를 보자마자 그녀를 알아보았다. 그리고 킬위치가 그녀에게 말했다. \"아! 처녀여, 그대가 바로 내가 사랑해 온 그 사람이오. 사람들이 그대와 나에 대해 험담하지 못하도록 나와 함께 떠납시다. 여러 날 동안 나는 그대를 사랑해 왔소.\" \"저는 그렇게 할 수 없어요. 저는 아버지의 뜻 없이는 떠나지 않겠노라 신의를 맹세했고, 아버지의 목숨은 제가 혼인하는 그때까지만 이어질 것이기 때문이에요. 무엇이 일어나야 하든, 그것은 일어나야만 하지요. 하지만 당신이 받아들이신다면 제가 충고를 드리겠어요. 가셔서 제 아버지께 저를 청하시고, 그분이 당신께 요구하시는 것은 무엇이든 들어주세요. 그러면 당신은 저를 얻으실 거예요. 하지만 그분께 무엇이든 거절하신다면 당신은 저를 얻지 못하실 것이며, 당신이 목숨을 부지하고 빠져나오신다면 그것만으로도 다행일 거예요.\" \"기회가 주어진다면 이 모든 것을 약속하겠소.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n그녀는 자기 방으로 돌아갔고, 그들은 모두 일어나 그녀를 따라 성으로 갔다. 그리고 그들은 아홉 개의 문에 있던 아홉 명의 문지기를 소리 없이 죽였다. 그리고 그들은 아홉 마리의 파수견을 한 마리도 짖지 못하게 하고 죽였다. 그리고 그들은 홀로 나아갔다.\n\n\"하늘과 사람의 인사가 그대에게 있기를, 이스파다덴 펜카우르여.\" 그들이 말했다. \"그래, 당신들은 무슨 일로 왔는가?\" \"우리는 켈리돈 왕자의 아들 킬리드의 아들 킬위치를 위해 그대의 딸 올웬을 청하러 왔소.\" \"내 시동들과 종들은 어디에 있느냐? 내 눈을 덮어 버린 두 눈썹 밑에 받침대를 받쳐 들어 올려라. 그래서 내 사위 될 자의 생김새를 좀 보자꾸나.\" 그러자 그들이 그렇게 했다. \"내일 이리로 다시 오라. 그러면 답을 주겠노라.\"\n\n그들이 나가려고 일어서자, 이스파다덴 펜카우르는 자기 곁에 놓여 있던 독을 바른 세 자루의 화살 가운데 하나를 집어 그들 뒤로 던졌다. 그러자 베드위르가 그것을 잡아 되던져 이스파다덴 펜카우르의 무릎을 꿰뚫어 심하게 상처를 입혔다. 그러자 그가 말했다. \"참으로 저주받을 무례한 사위로다! 저자의 무례함 때문에 나는 평생 더 절뚝거리며 걷게 될 것이며, 영영 낫지 못할 것이다. 이 독을 바른 쇠붙이가 등에에게 물린 것처럼 나를 아프게 하는구나. 이것을 벼린 대장장이와 이것을 만든 모루에 저주가 있기를! 어찌 이리도 날카로운가!\"\n\n그날 밤도 그들은 목자의 집에 거처를 정했다. 이튿날 동이 틀 무렵, 그들은 채비를 갖추어 성으로 나아가 홀로 들어갔다. 그러고는 말했다. \"이스파다덴 펜카우르여, 우리가 그대에게 치를 신부 지참금과 처녀 값을 헤아려 그대의 딸을 우리에게 주시오. 그리고 마찬가지로 그녀의 두 친족 여인에게도 치르겠소.\" 그러자 그가 말했다. \"그 아이의 네 명의 증조모와 네 명의 증조부가 아직 살아 있으니, 그들과 의논하는 것이 마땅하다.\" \"그렇게 하시오.\" 그들이 대답했다. \"우리는 식사를 하러 가겠소.\" 그들이 일어서자 그는 자기 곁에 놓인 두 번째 화살을 집어 그들 뒤로 던졌다. 그러자 가웨드의 아들 메네우가 그것을 잡아 되던져 그의 가슴 한복판에 상처를 입혔다. \"참으로 저주받을 무례한 사위로다!\" 그가 말했다. \"이 단단한 쇠붙이가 거머리에게 물린 것처럼 나를 아프게 하는구나. 이것을 달구던 화덕과 이것을 만든 대장장이에게 저주가 있기를! 어찌 이리도 날카로운가! 이제부터 나는 언덕을 오를 때마다 숨이 가빠지고 가슴이 아플 것이며, 종종 음식이 역겨워질 것이다.\" 그리고 그들은 식사를 하러 갔다.\n\n그리고 사흘째 되는 날 그들은 궁으로 돌아왔다. 그러자 이스파다덴 펜카우르가 그들에게 말했다. \"죽음을 원하지 않거든 다시는 내게 쏘지 마라. 내 시종들은 어디에 있느냐? 내 눈알을 덮어 버린 눈썹 밑에 받침대를 받쳐 들어 올려라. 그래서 내 사위 될 자의 생김새를 좀 보자꾸나.\" 그러자 그들이 일어섰고, 그들이 그렇게 하는 동안 이스파다덴 펜카우르는 세 번째 독화살을 집어 그들에게 던졌다. 그러자 킬위치가 그것을 잡아 힘차게 되던져 그의 눈알을 꿰뚫어 상처를 입혔다. \"참으로 저주받을 무례한 사위로다! 내가 살아 있는 한, 내 시력은 더 나빠질 것이다. 바람을 거슬러 갈 때마다 내 눈에서는 눈물이 날 것이며, 어쩌면 내 머리가 화끈거리고 초승달이 뜰 때마다 어지러울 것이다. 이 독을 바른 쇠붙이의 일격은 미친개에게 물린 것과 같구나. 이것을 벼린 불에 저주가 있기를!\" 그리고 그들은 식사를 하러 갔다.\n\n그리고 이튿날 그들은 다시 궁으로 와서 이렇게 말했다. \"지금 그대가 겪은 것과 같은, 아니 그보다 더한 상해와 해악과 고통을 원하지 않거든 다시는 우리에게 쏘지 마시오.\" 킬위치가 말했다. \"그대의 딸을 내게 주시오. 그대가 그녀를 주지 않는다면, 그대는 그녀로 인해 죽음을 맞을 것이오.\" \"내 딸을 구하는 자가 어디 있느냐? 내가 너를 볼 수 있도록 이리로 오너라.\" 그리하여 그들은 그를 그와 얼굴을 마주하도록 의자에 앉혔다.\n\n이스파다덴 펜카우르가 말했다. \"내 딸을 구하는 자가 너냐?\"\n\n\"바로 저올시다.\" 킬위치가 대답했다.\n\n\"네가 정당한 것 외에 다른 방식으로 나를 대하지 않겠다는 너의 맹세를 받아야겠다. 그리고 내가 이름하는 것을 얻게 되면, 너는 내 딸을 얻을 것이다.\"\n\n\"기꺼이 그것을 그대께 약속하겠소.\" 킬위치가 말했다. \"원하는 것을 이름하시오.\"\n\n\"그렇게 하지.\" 그가 말했다. \"저기 붉게 일군 땅이 보이느냐?\"\n\n\"보입니다.\"\n\n\"내가 이 처녀의 어머니를 처음 만났을 때, 그곳에 아홉 부셸의 아마 씨를 뿌렸으나 아직 흰 것도 검은 것도 하나 돋아나지 않았다. 나는 그 아마를 저기 새 땅에 뿌려야겠으니, 그것이 자라나면 네 혼례 날에 내 딸의 머리에 씌울 흰 두건을 만들 수 있도록 함이다.\"\n\n\"비록 그대는 쉽지 않으리라 여길지 모르나, 이것을 이루는 것은 제게 쉬운 일이 될 것이오.\"\n\n\"이것을 얻는다 하더라도, 네가 얻지 못할 것이 아직 있으니, 그날 밤 우리에게 연주해 줄 테이르투의 하프다. 어떤 사람이 그것이 연주하기를 원하면 그것은 저절로 연주하고, 그것이 멎기를 원하면 그것은 멎는다. 그런데 그는 제 뜻으로는 그것을 내주지 않을 것이며, 너는 그를 강제할 수도 없을 것이다.\"\n\n\"비록 그대는 쉽지 않으리라 여길지 모르나, 이것을 이루는 것은 제게 쉬운 일이 될 것이오.\"\n\n\"이것을 얻는다 하더라도, 네가 얻지 못할 것이 아직 있다. 나는 네가 내 사냥꾼으로 모드론의 아들 마본을 데려오기를 요구한다. 그는 태어난 지 사흘 밤 만에 어머니에게서 빼앗겼고, 그가 지금 어디에 있는지, 살았는지 죽었는지조차 알려져 있지 않다.\"\n\n\"비록 그대는 쉽지 않으리라 여길지 모르나, 이것을 이루는 것은 제게 쉬운 일이 될 것이오.\"\n\n\"이것을 얻는다 하더라도, 네가 얻지 못할 것이 아직 있으니, 암늑대 가스트 림히의 두 새끼다. 세상의 어떤 가죽끈도 그놈들을 붙들어 매지 못하나, 강도 딜루스 바르와우크의 수염으로 만든 가죽끈만은 예외다. 그리고 그 가죽끈은 그가 살아 있는 동안 그의 수염에서 뽑아낸 것이 아니면 아무 소용이 없다. 그가 살아 있는 동안에는 자기 몸에 그런 일이 행해지도록 두지 않을 것이며, 또 그가 죽은 뒤라면 그 가죽끈은 잘 부스러질 것이므로 아무 소용이 없을 것이다.\"\n\n\"비록 그대는 쉽지 않으리라 여길지 모르나, 이것을 이루는 것은 제게 쉬운 일이 될 것이오.\"\n\n\"이것을 얻는다 하더라도, 네가 얻지 못할 것이 아직 있으니, 거인 그웨르나크의 검이다. 그는 제 뜻으로는 그것을 내주지 않을 것이며, 너는 결코 그를 강제할 수 없을 것이다.\"\n\n\"비록 그대는 쉽지 않으리라 여길지 모르나, 이것을 이루는 것은 제게 쉬운 일이 될 것이오.\"\n\n\"이것을 얻는다 하더라도, 네가 얻지 못할 것이 아직 있다. 이것을 구하면서 너는 갖가지 어려움과 잠 못 이루는 밤들을 만날 것이며, 그것을 얻지 못한다면 너는 내 딸도 얻지 못할 것이다.\"\n\n\"제게는 말과 기사도가 있을 것이며, 제 주군이자 친족인 아서가 이 모든 것을 제게 구해 줄 것이오. 그리고 저는 그대의 딸을 얻을 것이며, 그대는 목숨을 잃을 것이오.\"\n\n\"가거라. 그리고 네가 이것들을 구하는 동안에는 내 딸의 음식이나 의복에 대한 책임은 네게 지우지 않겠다. 그리고 네가 이 모든 경이로운 일을 이루어 내거든, 너는 내 딸을 아내로 얻을 것이다.\"\n\n————\n\n그들은 그날 종일 길을 갔고, 저녁이 되자 거대한 성을 발견했으니, 그것은 세상에서 가장 큰 성이었다. 그런데 보라! 이 세상 사람 셋을 합한 것보다도 큰 검은 사내 하나가 성에서 나왔다. 그래서 그들은 그에게 말을 걸어 물었다. \"여보시오, 저것은 누구의 성이오?\" \"참으로 어리석은 자들이로구나, 그대들이여! 저것이 거인 그웨르나흐의 성임을 모르는 자는 이 세상에 없거늘.\" \"저 성에 들르는 손님이나 나그네는 어떤 대접을 받소?\" \"이보시오, 하늘이 그대를 보살피시기를! 저곳에서 살아 돌아온 손님은 아무도 없으며, 자기 기술을 지니고 오지 않은 자는 그 누구도 들어갈 수 없소.\"\n\n그러자 그들은 성문을 향해 나아갔다. 구르히르 그왈스타트가 말했다. \"문지기가 있소?\" \"있소. 어찌하여 부르시오?\" \"문을 여시오.\" \"열어 주지 않겠소.\" \"어찌하여 열어 주지 않겠다는 거요?\" \"고기에는 칼이 꽂혀 있고, 뿔잔에는 술이 가득하며, 거인 그웨르나흐의 연회장에서는 흥겨운 잔치가 벌어지고 있소. 자기 기술을 지니고 오는 장인이 아니라면, 오늘 밤 문은 열리지 않을 것이오.\" \"참으로, 문지기여,\" 그러자 카이가 말했다. \"나는 내 기술을 지니고 왔소.\" \"그대의 기술이 무엇이오?\" \"나는 세상에서 검을 가장 잘 가는 사람이오.\" \"내가 가서 이를 거인 그웨르나흐에게 전하고, 그대에게 답을 가져오겠소.\"\n\n그리하여 문지기가 안으로 들어가니, 그웨르나흐가 그에게 말했다. \"성문에서 소식이 있느냐?\" \"있습니다. 성문 앞에 들어오기를 바라는 일행이 있습니다.\" \"그들에게 무슨 기술을 지녔는지 물어보았느냐?\" \"물어보았습니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"그리고 그중 한 사람이 검을 가는 데 능숙하다고 말했습니다.\" \"그렇다면 그가 우리에게 필요하구나. 한동안 나는 내 검을 갈아 줄 자를 찾았으나, 아무도 찾지 못했다. 그자가 자기 기술을 지니고 왔다 하니, 들어오게 하라.\"\n\n그리하여 문지기가 돌아와 성문을 열었다. 그러자 카이가 홀로 들어가 거인 그웨르나흐에게 인사를 올렸다. 그리고 그웨르나흐의 맞은편에 그를 위한 의자가 놓였다. 그웨르나흐가 그에게 말했다. \"여보게, 그대에 관해 전해진 말이 사실인가, 그대가 검을 갈 줄 안다는 것이?\" \"나는 그것을 아주 잘할 줄 압니다.\" 카이가 답했다. 그러자 그웨르나흐의 검이 그에게 건네졌다. 카이는 겨드랑이 밑에서 푸른 숫돌을 꺼내, 검을 희게 갈기를 바라는지 푸르게 갈기를 바라는지 물었다. \"그대 보기에 좋을 대로 하게나, 아니면 그것이 그대 자신의 것이라면 하였을 대로 하게.\" 그러자 카이는 칼날의 절반을 갈아 그의 손에 쥐여 주었다. \"이것이 마음에 드십니까?\" 그가 물었다. \"내 영토 안의 모든 것을 다 준다 해도, 검 전체가 이와 같았으면 좋겠네. 그대 같은 사람이 동료 하나 없다는 것이 내게는 놀라운 일이로군.\" \"고귀하신 분이시여, 제게는 동료가 있습니다만, 그는 이 기술에는 능숙하지 못합니다.\" \"그가 누구인가?\" \"문지기를 내보내 주시면, 그를 알아볼 방도를 일러 드리겠습니다. 창끝이 그 자루를 떠나, 바람에서 피를 뽑아내고는, 다시 그 자루 위로 내려앉을 것입니다.\" 그러자 성문이 열리고, 베드위르가 들어왔다. 카이가 말했다. \"베드위르는 이 기술은 모르나, 매우 솜씨가 뛰어납니다.\"\n\n그리고 카이와 베드위르가 안으로 들어갔으므로, 밖에 있던 자들 사이에는 많은 이야기가 오갔다. 그러더니 그들과 함께 있던 한 젊은이, 곧 목동의 외아들 또한 안으로 들어갔다. 그는 나머지 모두를 들여보낼 방도를 꾀했으나, 그들은 몸을 숨긴 채 있었다.\n\n이제 검이 다 갈렸으므로, 카이는 거인 그웨르나흐의 손에 그것을 건네어 자기 솜씨가 마음에 드는지를 살피게 했다. 거인이 말했다. \"솜씨가 훌륭하구나. 나는 이에 만족한다.\" 카이가 말했다. \"그대의 검을 녹슬게 한 것은 검집입니다. 그것을 제게 주시면, 그 나무 옆판을 빼내고 새것을 끼우겠습니다.\" 그러고는 그에게서 검집을 받고, 다른 손에는 검을 들었다. 그리고 그는 마치 검을 검집에 넣으려는 듯이 거인 앞으로 다가가 마주 섰다. 그리고 그 검으로 거인의 머리를 내리쳐, 단칼에 그 목을 베어 버렸다. 그런 다음 그들은 성을 약탈하여, 거기서 원하는 재물과 보석을 가져갔다. 그리고 그들은 거인 그웨르나흐의 검을 지니고 아서의 궁정으로 돌아왔다.\n\n그리고 그들이 어떻게 일을 해냈는지 아서에게 이야기하자, 아서가 말했다. \"좋은 시작이로다.\" 그러고 나서 그들은 의논하여 말했다. \"이 경이로운 일들 가운데 다음으로 어느 것을 찾는 것이 우리에게 가장 나을까?\" \"가장 나은 것은,\" 한 사람이 말했다. \"모드론의 아들 마본을 찾는 것입니다. 그러나 그는 우리가 먼저 그의 친족인 아에르의 아들 에이도엘을 찾지 않으면 발견되지 않을 것입니다.\" 그러자 아서가 일어났고, 브리튼 섬의 전사들이 그와 함께 에이도엘을 찾으러 나섰다. 그들은 나아가 에이도엘이 갇혀 있는 글리비의 성에 이르렀다. 글리비는 자기 성의 꼭대기에 서서 말했다. \"아서여, 그대는 내게서 무엇을 바라는가, 이 요새에는 내게 남은 것이 아무것도 없고, 나는 여기서 기쁨도 즐거움도, 밀도 귀리도 없거늘? 그러니 내게 해를 끼치려 하지 말라.\" 아서가 말했다. \"내가 이곳에 온 것은 그대를 해치려는 것이 아니라, 그대와 함께 있는 죄수를 찾기 위함이다.\" \"내 죄수를 그대에게 주겠노라. 나는 그를 누구에게도 넘겨줄 생각이 없었으나, 그와 더불어 그대는 내 지지와 도움도 얻을 것이다.\"\n\n그의 부하들이 아서에게 말했다. \"주군이시여, 집으로 돌아가소서. 주군께서는 이처럼 하찮은 모험을 좇아 군대를 이끌고 나아가실 수 없습니다.\" 그러자 아서가 말했다. \"구르히르 그왈스타트여, 그대가 이 탐색에 나서는 것이 좋겠다. 그대는 모든 언어를 알고, 새와 짐승의 말에도 익숙하니 말이다. 에이도엘이여, 그대 또한 그대의 부하들과 함께 그대의 사촌을 찾으러 가야 마땅하다. 그리고 카이와 베드위르여, 그대들이 어떤 모험을 좇든, 나는 그대들이 그것을 이루어 내리라는 희망을 품는다. 나를 위해 이 모험을 이루어 다오.\"\n\n그들은 나아가 킬그우리의 검은새에게 이르렀다. 구르히르가 그 새에게 간절히 청하여 말했다. \"그대가 모드론의 아들 마본에 관해 무엇이라도 아는 것이 있다면 내게 말해 다오. 그는 태어난 지 사흘 밤 만에 그 어머니와 벽 사이에서 빼앗겨 갔다.\" 검은새가 답했다. \"내가 처음 이곳에 왔을 때, 이 자리에는 대장장이의 모루가 있었고, 그때 나는 어린 새였소. 그 이래로 그 모루에는 매일 저녁 내 부리로 쪼는 일 말고는 아무 일도 행해지지 않았소. 그런데도 이제는 그 모루가 개암 한 알만큼도 남지 않았소. 그러나 그 오랜 세월 동안 나는 그대가 묻는 사람에 관해 들어 본 적이 없소. 그럼에도 나는 아서께서 보내신 사절에게 마땅히 해야 할 바를 행하겠소. 나보다 먼저 만들어진 짐승의 무리가 있으니, 내가 그대들을 그들에게로 인도하겠소.\"\n\n그리하여 그들은 레딘브레의 수사슴이 있는 곳으로 나아갔다. \"레딘브레의 수사슴이여, 보라, 우리는 아서께서 보내신 사절로서 그대에게 왔노라. 우리는 그대보다 나이 많은 짐승에 관해서는 들어 본 적이 없기 때문이다. 말해 다오, 그대는 태어난 지 사흘 밤 만에 그 어머니에게서 빼앗겨 간 모드론의 아들 마본에 관해 무엇이라도 아는가?\" 수사슴이 말했다. \"내가 처음 이곳에 왔을 때, 내 주위에는 어린 떡갈나무 한 그루를 빼고는 나무 하나 없는 평원이 펼쳐져 있었소. 그 어린 나무가 자라 가지 백 개를 지닌 떡갈나무가 되었소. 그런데 그 떡갈나무는 그 후 죽어 버려, 이제는 시든 그루터기 말고는 아무것도 남지 않았소. 그날부터 지금까지 나는 이곳에 있었으나, 그대들이 묻는 사람에 관해서는 들어 본 적이 없소. 그럼에도, 아서께서 보내신 사절이니, 나보다 먼저 만들어진 짐승, 곧 세상에서 가장 나이 많고, 가장 많이 떠돌아다닌 짐승인 그웨른 아브위의 독수리가 있는 곳으로 그대들을 인도하겠소.\"\n\n구르히르가 말했다. \"그웨른 아브위의 독수리여, 우리는 아서께서 보내신 사절로서 그대에게 왔노라. 그대가 태어난 지 사흘 밤 만에 그 어머니에게서 빼앗겨 간 모드론의 아들 마본에 관해 무엇이라도 아는지 그대에게 묻고자 함이다.\" 독수리가 말했다. \"나는 아주 오랜 세월 동안 이곳에 있었소. 내가 처음 이곳에 왔을 때, 여기에는 바위가 하나 있어 그 꼭대기에서 매일 저녁 별을 쪼았소. 그런데 그것이 무너져 내려, 이제는 한 뼘 높이도 되지 않소. 그 모든 세월 동안 나는 이곳에 있었으나, 그대들이 묻는 사람에 관해서는 들어 본 적이 없소. 단 한 번, 내가 린 리우까지 먹이를 찾으러 갔을 때를 빼고는 말이오. 그곳에 이르러 나는 발톱을 연어 한 마리에 박았으니, 그가 오랫동안 내 먹이가 되어 주리라 여겼소. 그러나 그가 나를 물속으로 끌어들였고, 나는 가까스로 그에게서 벗어날 수 있었소. 그 후 나는 그와 화해했소. 그리고 나는 그의 등에서 물고기 작살 쉰 개를 뽑아내어 그를 덜어 주었소. 그가 그대들이 찾는 자에 관해 무엇인가 알지 못한다면, 나로서는 누가 알지 도무지 알 수 없소. 그렇지만 내가 그가 있는 곳으로 그대들을 인도하겠소.\"\n\n그리하여 그들은 그곳으로 갔다. 그리고 독수리가 말했다. \"린 리우의 연어여, 나는 아서께서 보내신 사절을 데리고 그대에게 왔노라. 그대가 태어난 지 사흘 밤 만에 그 어머니에게서 빼앗겨 간 모드론의 아들 마본에 관해 무엇이라도 아는지 그대에게 묻고자 함이다.\" \"내가 아는 만큼은 그대에게 말해 주겠소. 나는 밀물이 들 때마다 강을 거슬러 올라가, 글로스터의 성벽 가까이에 이르오. 그리고 그곳에서 나는 다른 어디서도 본 적 없는 부당한 일을 보았소. 그대들이 이를 믿을 수 있도록, 그대들 가운데 한 사람씩 내 두 어깨에 올라타시오.\" 그리하여 카이와 구르히르 그왈스타트가 연어의 두 어깨에 올라탔고, 그들은 나아가 감옥의 벽에 이르렀다. 그리고 그들은 지하 감옥에서 들려오는 크나큰 통곡과 한탄을 들었다. 구르히르가 말했다. \"이 돌집에서 한탄하는 자가 누구인가?\" \"아아! 나는 이곳에 갇힌 모드론의 아들 마본이오. 내 갇힘만큼 비참한 갇힘은 일찍이 없었소.\" \"그대는 금이나 은으로, 혹은 어떤 재물의 선물로, 아니면 전투와 싸움을 통해 풀려날 희망이 있는가?\" \"내가 얻을 수 있는 것이 무엇이든, 그것은 싸움을 통해서만 얻어질 것이오.\"\n\n그리하여 그들은 그곳을 떠나 아서에게 돌아가, 모드론의 아들 마본이 갇혀 있는 곳을 그에게 일러 주었다. 그러자 아서는 섬의 전사들을 불러 모았고, 그들은 마본이 갇혀 있는 글로스터까지 나아갔다. 카이와 베드위르가 물고기의 어깨에 올라탄 사이, 아서의 전사들은 성을 공격했다. 그리고 전사들 사이에서 싸움이 벌어지는 동안, 카이는 벽을 뚫고 지하 감옥으로 들어가 그 죄수를 등에 업고 데려 나왔다. 그리고 아서는 집으로 돌아왔고, 마본도 자유의 몸이 되어 그와 함께였다.\n\n어느 날 구르히르 그왈스타트가 산을 넘어 걷고 있을 때, 그는 통곡 소리와 비통한 울부짖음을 들었다. 그리고 그것을 듣자, 그는 앞으로 뛰어나가 그 소리를 향해 갔다. 그리고 그곳에 이르렀을 때, 그는 잔디 사이에서 불이 타오르고 있고, 개미집 하나가 그 불에 거의 둘러싸여 있는 것을 보았다. 그래서 그는 검을 뽑아, 개미집을 땅에 바싹 붙여 베어 냈고, 그리하여 개미집은 불에 타는 것을 면했다. 그러자 개미들이 그에게 말했다. \"우리에게서 하늘의 축복을 받으소서. 그리고 그 누구도 줄 수 없는 것을, 우리가 그대에게 드리겠나이다.\" 그러고는 그들이 이스파다덴 펜카우르가 킬위치에게 요구했던 아홉 부셸의 아마 씨를 가져왔는데, 그들은 절름발이 개미 한 마리가 밤이 되기 전에 가져온 아마 씨 한 알을 빼고는, 하나도 모자람 없이 가득 채운 분량을 가져왔다.\n\n그러자 아서가 말했다. \"이 경이로운 일들 가운데 다음으로 어느 것을 찾는 것이 우리에게 가장 나을까?\" \"암늑대 가스트 림히의 새끼 두 마리를 찾는 것이 가장 나을 것입니다.\"\n\n\"그 암늑대가 어디 있는지,\" 아서가 말했다. \"알려져 있는가?\" \"그것은 아베르 클레디프에 있습니다.\" 한 사람이 말했다. 그러자 아서는 아베르 클레디프의 트링가드의 집으로 가서, 그가 그곳에서 그 암늑대에 관해 들은 적이 있는지 물었다. \"그것은 자주 내 가축들을 죽였소. 그것은 저 아래 아베르 클레디프의 동굴 안에 있소.\"\n\n그리하여 아서는 자기 배 프리드웬을 타고 바다로 갔고, 다른 이들은 뭍으로 가서 그 암늑대를 사냥했다. 그리고 그들은 그 암늑대와 그 새끼 두 마리를 에워싸, 그들을 붙잡아 데려갔다.\n\n카이와 베드위르가 플린리몬 꼭대기의 봉화 돌무덤 위에, 일찍이 없던 가장 거센 바람 속에 앉아 있을 때, 그들은 주위를 둘러보다가 멀리서 큰 연기가 피어오르는 것을 보았다. 그러자 카이가 말했다. \"내 벗의 손에 걸고 말하건대, 저기 저것은 도둑의 불이로다.\" 그러고는 그들이 연기를 향해 서둘러 갔고, 그들이 그것에 아주 가까이 다가가니, 딜루스 바르와우크가 멧돼지 한 마리를 굽고 있는 것이 보였다. \"보라, 저기 저자가 아서에게서 달아난 가장 큰 도둑이로다.\" 베드위르가 카이에게 말했다. \"그대는 저자를 아는가?\" \"나는 저자를 안다.\" 카이가 답했다. \"저자는 딜루스 바르와우크요, 저기 그대가 보는 저자의 수염으로 만든 가죽끈이 아니고서는, 세상의 그 어떤 가죽끈도 가스트 림히의 새끼들을 매어 둘 수 없소. 게다가 그것조차도 그의 수염을 살아 있을 때, 나무 족집게로 뽑아내지 않으면 쓸모가 없소. 죽은 뒤에 뽑으면 부스러질 것이기 때문이오.\" \"이 일에 관해 우리가 어찌해야 좋겠는가?\" 베드위르가 말했다. \"저자가,\" 카이가 말했다. \"고기를 원하는 만큼 실컷 먹게 내버려 둡시다. 그러면 저자는 잠들 것이오.\" 그리고 그동안 그들은 나무 족집게를 만드는 일에 몰두했다. 그리고 카이가 그가 잠들었음을 확실히 알았을 때, 그는 그의 발밑에 구덩이를 파고, 그에게 사나운 일격을 가해 구덩이 속으로 밀어 넣었다. 그리고 그곳에서 그들은 나무 족집게로 그의 수염을 완전히 뽑아냈고, 그런 다음 그를 아주 죽여 버렸다. 그리고 그곳을 떠나, 그들은 딜루스 바르와우크의 수염으로 만든 가죽끈을 가지고 와서, 그것을 아서의 손에 건넸다.\n\n이렇게 하여 그들은 이스파다덴 펜카우르가 킬위치에게 요구했던 모든 경이로운 물건을 손에 넣었다. 그리고 그들은 길을 떠나, 그 경이로운 물건들을 그의 궁정으로 가져갔다. 킬위치가 이스파다덴 펜카우르에게 말했다. \"이제 그대의 딸은 제 것입니까?\" \"그 애는 그대의 것이오.\" 그가 말했다. \"그러나 그 일로 그대는 내게 감사할 필요가 없고, 그대를 위해 이를 이루어 낸 아서에게 감사해야 하오.\" 그러자 이스파다덴 펜카우르가 그 형제들을 죽인 바 있는 목동 쿠스텐닌의 아들 고레우가, 그의 머리채를 움켜잡고 망루까지 끌고 가, 그의 목을 베어, 그 머리를 성채의 말뚝 위에 꽂았다. 그런 다음 그들은 그의 성과 그의 보물을 차지했다. 그리고 그날 밤 올웬은 킬위치의 신부가 되었고, 그녀는 살아 있는 동안 내내 그의 아내로 지냈다.\n\n————\n\n그위드노 가란히르는 바다와 접한 영토인 그와엘로드의 군주였다. 그는 디비와 아베리스트위스 사이의 해변에, 자기 성에서 가까운 곳에 어량을 하나 가지고 있었는데, 그곳에서는 해마다 오월제 전야가 되면 백 파운드어치가 잡히곤 했다. 그위드노에게는 엘핀이라는 외아들이 있었으니, 그는 젊은이들 가운데 가장 불운하고 가장 궁핍한 자였다. 이는 그의 아버지를 몹시 슬프게 했으니, 그가 불길한 시각에 태어났다고 여겨졌기 때문이다. 그의 아버지는 자문단의 조언에 따라, 혹시라도 그에게 행운이 깃들지, 또한 세상에 첫발을 내디딜 밑천이라도 마련해 줄지 보고자 그해의 어량 거두기를 그에게 맡겼다. 그것은 사월 스무아흐렛날의 일이었다.\n\n이튿날 엘핀이 가서 보니, 어량에는 막대에 걸린 가죽 자루 하나밖에 아무것도 없었다. 그러자 어량지기가 엘핀에게 말했다. \"여태까지의 불운은 모두 이에 비하면 아무것도 아닙니다. 이제 도련님은 해마다 오월제 전야에 백 파운드어치를 거두어 들이던 이 어량의 효험을 망쳐 버리셨으니, 오늘 밤에는 그 안에 이 가죽 자루밖에 아무것도 없군요.\" \"그게 무슨 말이냐,\" 엘핀이 말했다. \"그 안에 백 파운드어치가 들어 있을지도 모르지 않느냐.\" 그리하여 그들은 가죽 자루를 들어 올렸고, 그것을 연 자는 일찍이 본 적 없는 더없이 아름다운 갓난아이의 이마를 보았다. 그가 말했다. \"보라, 빛나는 이마로다!\" (웨일스 말로 탈리에신이라 한다.) \"이 아이를 탈리에신이라 부르자,\" 엘핀이 말했다. 그러고는 자루를 두 팔에 안고, 자신의 불운을 한탄하면서 슬픈 마음으로 아이를 자기 뒤에 앉혔다. 그리고 앞서까지 빠른 걸음으로 달리던 말을 부드럽게 천천히 걷게 하여, 마치 세상에서 가장 편안한 의자에 앉은 듯이 아이를 부드럽게 실어 날랐다. 그러자 곧 아이가 위로의 노래를 지어 엘핀을 찬양했으니, 그 위로의 노래는 여기 보는 바와 같았다.\n\n\"어여쁜 엘핀이여, 한탄을 그치소서! 그위드노의 어량에서 오늘 밤만큼 큰 행운은 일찍이 없었나이다. 슬퍼한들 아무 소용없으니, 불행을 미리 두려워하느니 하느님을 믿는 편이 나으리라. 비록 나는 약하고 작으나, 거품 이는 바닷가에서, 환난의 날에 나는 연어 삼백 마리보다 그대에게 더 큰 보탬이 되리라.\"\n\n이것이 탈리에신이 처음으로 부른 시였으니, 어량의 소출을 잃은 데다, 더 나쁘게는 온 세상이 그것을 자신의 잘못과 불운 탓으로 여기리라는 비탄에 잠긴 엘핀을 위로하기 위함이었다. 그러자 엘핀이 그에게 사람인지 정령인지 무엇이냐고 물었다. 그러자 그가 이렇게 노래했다.\n\n\"나는 잘생긴 사람으로 빚어졌노라. 비록 작으나, 나는 크게 타고난 재능이 있노라. 어두운 가죽 자루 속에 던져져, 가없는 바다 위로 떠밀려 보내졌노라. 바다에서, 그리고 산에서 하느님은 복 받은 자에게 부를 가져다주시느니라.\"\n\n이윽고 엘핀은 자신의 아버지 그위드노의 집에 이르렀으니, 탈리에신도 그와 함께였다. 그위드노가 그에게 어량에서 많이 잡았느냐고 묻자, 그는 물고기보다 더 나은 것을 얻었다고 말했다. \"그게 무엇이냐?\" 그위드노가 물었다. \"음유시인입니다,\" 엘핀이 말했다. 그러자 그위드노가 말했다. \"아아! 그것이 네게 무슨 이로움이 되겠느냐?\" 그러자 탈리에신이 직접 대답하여 말했다. \"그는 어량이 일찍이 어르신께 끼친 이로움보다 더 큰 이로움을 그분께 끼칠 것입니다.\" 그위드노가 물었다. \"그처럼 작은 네가 말을 할 줄 안단 말이냐?\" 탈리에신이 그에게 대답했다. \"나는 어르신께서 내게 물으시는 것보다 더 잘 말할 줄 압니다.\" \"네가 무슨 말을 할 수 있는지 들어 보자,\" 그위드노가 말했다. 그러자 탈리에신이 노래했다.\n\n\"나는 세 번 태어났노라, 명상으로 그것을 아노라. 세상의 온갖 학문이 내 가슴속에 모여 있으니, 이는 내가 있었던 일과, 앞으로 있을 일을 알기 때문이로다.\"\n\n엘핀은 자신이 거둔 것을 아내에게 주었고, 아내는 그를 정성스럽고 사랑스럽게 길렀다. 그때부터 엘핀은 날이 갈수록 더욱더 부유해졌고, 왕의 사랑과 총애도 늘어 갔다. 탈리에신은 열세 살이 될 때까지 그곳에 머물렀는데, 그때 그위드노의 아들 엘핀은 성탄절 초대를 받아 그의 숙부 마엘간 그위네드에게로 갔다. 마엘간은 성탄절 무렵에 디가뉘 성에서 영적인 신분과 세속적인 신분 양쪽의 모든 귀족들과, 기사와 종자들로 이루어진 거대하고 빽빽한 무리를 모두 불러 모아 큰 잔치를 열고 있었다. 그때 한 사람이 일어나 말했다. \"온 세상에 마엘간만큼 위대한 왕이 있겠는가, 또는 그분만큼 하늘로부터 많은 선물을 받은 자가 있겠는가. 영혼의 온갖 능력은 말할 것도 없고, 풍채와 미모와 온유함과 힘까지 받으신 분이.\" 그리고 그들은 이와 더불어, 하늘이 다른 모든 것을 능가하는 한 가지 선물을 주셨다고 말했으니, 그것은 곧 그분의 왕비의 아름다움과 우아함과 지혜와 정숙함이었으며, 그 덕성은 온 왕국의 모든 귀부인과 고귀한 처녀들의 덕성을 능가한다는 것이었다. 그러면서 그들은 서로에게 물음을 던졌다. 누가 더 용감한 부하들을 거느렸는가? 누가 더 아름답거나 빠른 말과 사냥개를 가졌는가? 누가 마엘간보다 더 능란하거나 현명한 음유시인을 거느렸는가?\n\n그들이 모두 왕과 그의 선물에 대한 찬양을 끝냈을 때, 엘핀이 이런 식으로 입을 열게 되었다. \"참으로 왕에 견줄 수 있는 자는 왕밖에 없습니다. 그러나 만일 그가 왕이 아니라면, 나는 내 아내가 이 왕국의 어떤 귀부인 못지않게 정숙하다고 말하겠고, 또한 나는 왕의 모든 음유시인보다 더 능란한 음유시인을 거느리고 있다고 말하겠습니다.\" 머지않아 그의 동료들 가운데 몇몇이 엘핀의 온갖 자랑을 왕에게 일러바쳤다. 그러자 왕은 그의 아내의 덕성과 음유시인의 지혜에 관한 진실을 그가 증명할 수 있을 때까지 그를 견고한 감옥에 가두라고 명했다.\n\n이제 엘핀이 발에 두꺼운 사슬을 차고서 성의 탑에 갇히자(그가 왕족의 혈통이었던 까닭에 그것이 은사슬이었다고 한다), 이야기가 전하는 바에 따르면, 왕은 엘핀의 아내의 행실을 살피게 하고자 아들 룬을 보냈다. 그런데 룬은 세상에서 가장 파렴치한 사내였으니, 그가 말을 섞은 아내든 처녀든 험담의 대상이 되지 않은 자가 없었다. 룬이 엘핀의 아내에게 불명예를 안길 작정을 단단히 하고 서둘러 엘핀의 집으로 가는 동안, 탈리에신은 자신의 안주인에게 왕이 자신의 주인을 옥에 가두었으며, 룬이 그녀에게 불명예를 안기려고 서둘러 오고 있다는 것을 일러 주었다. 그래서 그는 안주인에게 부엌의 하녀 가운데 하나에게 자신의 옷을 입히게 했으니, 고귀한 부인은 기꺼이 그렇게 했고, 자신과 남편이 가진 가장 좋은 반지들로 그 하녀의 손을 가득 채워 주었다.\n\n이런 차림새로 탈리에신은 안주인으로 하여금 그 하녀를 안주인의 방 식탁에 앉혀 저녁 식사를 하게 했다. 그는 하녀를 안주인처럼 보이게 하고, 안주인을 하녀처럼 보이게 했다. 앞서 말한 대로 그들이 때맞춰 저녁 식사 자리에 앉았을 때, 룬이 불쑥 엘핀의 집에 당도했고, 하인들이 그를 알아보았기에 그는 기쁘게 맞아들여졌다. 그들은 그를 안주인의 방으로 데려갔는데, 안주인의 모습을 한 하녀가 저녁 식사 자리에서 일어나 그를 반갑게 맞이했다. 그런 다음 하녀는 다시 저녁 식사 자리에 앉았고, 룬도 그녀와 함께 앉았다. 그러자 룬은 여전히 안주인의 모습을 하고 있는 하녀와 농을 주고받기 시작했다. 그리고 이 이야기가 보여 주는 바에 따르면, 하녀는 몹시 취하여 잠이 들고 말았으니, 이야기가 전하기로는 룬이 술에 가루를 탄 까닭에 그녀가 너무도 깊이 잠들어, 그가 그녀의 손에서 새끼손가락을 잘라낼 때에도 전혀 느끼지 못했다고 한다. 그 손가락에는 엘핀의 인장 반지가 끼워져 있었으니, 그가 얼마 전에 정표로 아내에게 보낸 것이었다. 룬은 그 손가락과 반지를 증거로 삼아 왕에게 돌아왔으니, 그녀가 만취하여 잠든 사이에 깨어나지 않은 채로 그것을 그녀의 손에서 잘라냈음을 보이기 위함이었다.\n\n왕은 이 소식에 크게 기뻐하며 자문관들을 불러, 그들에게 처음부터 모든 이야기를 들려주었다. 그리고 엘핀을 감옥에서 끌어내 오게 하여, 그의 허풍을 꾸짖었다. 그는 이런 식으로 말했다. \"엘핀이여, 사내가 자기 눈으로 볼 수 있는 그 이상으로 아내의 덕성을 믿는 것은 어리석은 일임을 의심의 여지 없이 알아 두라. 그대 아내의 비열함을 그대가 확실히 알 수 있도록, 보라, 그대의 인장 반지가 끼워진 그녀의 손가락을. 이것은 지난밤 그녀가 만취하여 잠든 사이에 그녀의 손에서 잘라낸 것이다.\" 그러자 엘핀이 이렇게 말했다. \"황공하오나 위대하신 왕이시여, 저는 제 반지를 부인할 수 없으니, 그것은 많은 이가 아는 것입니다. 그러나 그 반지가 끼워진 손가락은 결코 제 아내의 손에 붙어 있던 것이 아님을 진실로 단언합니다. 진실로 그리고 확실히, 그 손가락에는 주목할 만한 세 가지가 있으니, 그 가운데 어느 것도 제 아내의 손가락 어느 것에도 결코 해당된 적이 없습니다. 세 가지 가운데 첫째는, 이 반지가 결코 제 아내의 엄지손가락에는 머물러 있지 못했으리라는 것을 제가 확실히 안다는 것이니, 반면에 보시다시피 이것을 잘라낸 손의 새끼손가락 마디 위로는 이 반지를 끼우기가 어렵습니다. 둘째는, 제 아내는 제가 그녀를 알게 된 이래로 잠자리에 들기 전에 손톱을 깎지 않은 채 토요일을 단 한 번도 그냥 넘긴 적이 없는데, 이 새끼손가락의 손톱은 한 달 동안 깎이지 않았음을 충분히 보실 수 있다는 것입니다. 셋째는, 진실로, 이 손가락이 나온 손은 손가락이 거기서 잘려 나가기 사흘 전 이내에 호밀 반죽을 이기고 있었다는 것인데, 전하께 장담하건대 제 아내는 제 아내가 된 이래로 호밀 반죽을 이긴 적이 결코 없습니다.\"\n\n왕은 엘핀이 자기 아내의 어짊에 관하여 그토록 완강하게 자신에게 맞서는 것에 몹시 노했다. 그래서 그는 그를 두 번째로 감옥에 보내라고 명하면서, 음유시인의 지혜와 아내의 덕성 양쪽에 관한 자신의 허풍이 참됨을 증명하기 전까지는 거기서 풀려나지 못하리라고 말했다.\n\n그러는 사이 그의 아내와 탈리에신은 엘핀의 집에서 즐겁게 지냈다. 탈리에신은 안주인에게 엘핀이 그들 때문에 옥에 갇혀 있음을 알렸으나, 그녀에게 기뻐하라고 일렀으니, 자신이 마엘간의 궁정으로 가서 주인을 풀어 주겠다는 것이었다. 그리하여 그는 안주인에게 작별을 고하고 마엘간의 궁정으로 갔다. 마엘간은 자신의 큰 홀에 자리하여 왕다운 위엄을 갖추고 식사를 하려던 참이었으니, 그 시절 왕과 군주들이 큰 잔치 때마다 으레 하던 관례였다. 탈리에신은 홀에 들어서자마자, 큰 축제 때 하사품이 선포될 무렵 음유시인과 악사들이 왕에게 봉사와 직분을 다하고자 오곤 하던 자리 가까이의 한적한 구석에 자리를 잡았다. 그리하여 음유시인과 전령들이 하사품을 외치고 왕의 권세와 힘을 선포하러 와서, 그가 웅크리고 있던 구석을 지나는 순간, 탈리에신은 그들을 향해 입술을 쑥 내밀고 손가락으로 입술을 튕기며 \"블레룸, 블레룸!\" 하고 소리를 냈다. 그들은 지나가면서 그에게 그다지 주의를 기울이지 않고 앞으로 나아가 왕 앞에 이르렀는데, 왕에게 평소처럼 몸을 굽혀 절을 하되 단 한 마디도 하지 않고, 입술을 쑥 내밀어 왕을 향해 우스꽝스러운 입 모양을 짓고는, 아이가 하는 것을 본 대로 손가락으로 입술을 튕기며 \"블레룸, 블레룸!\" 하고 소리를 냈다. 이 광경에 왕은 의아해하며, 그들이 술을 잔뜩 마시고 취했다고 속으로 여겼다. 그래서 그는 식탁에서 시중을 들던 귀족 하나에게 그들에게 가서 정신을 차리고, 자신들이 어디에 서 있으며 무엇을 하는 것이 마땅한지 헤아려 보라고 이르게 했다. 그 귀족은 기꺼이 그렇게 했다. 그러나 그들은 전과 마찬가지로 그 어리석은 짓을 그치지 않았다. 그러자 그는 두 번째로, 또 세 번째로 그들에게 사람을 보내어 홀에서 나가라고 일렀다. 마침내 왕은 종자 하나에게 그들 가운데 우두머리인 헤이닌 바르드에게 일격을 가하라고 명했다. 종자는 빗자루를 들어 그의 머리를 후려쳤고, 그러자 그는 자기 자리로 나자빠졌다. 그러자 그는 일어나 무릎을 꿇고, 이 잘못이 지식이 모자라서도, 취해서도 아니요, 홀 안에 있는 어떤 정령의 영향 때문임을 보이게 해 달라고 왕의 자비를 간청했다. 그는 이런 식으로 말했다. \"오, 존귀하신 왕이시여, 저희가 벙어리가 된 것은 술의 힘이나 너무 많이 마신 술 때문이 아니라, 저기 구석에 아이의 모습으로 앉아 있는 어떤 정령의 영향 때문임을 전하께 아뢰옵니다.\" 그러자 곧바로 왕은 종자에게 그를 데려오라고 명했고, 종자는 탈리에신이 앉아 있는 후미진 곳으로 가서 그를 왕 앞에 데려왔다. 왕이 그에게 무엇이며 어디서 왔느냐고 묻자, 그는 시로 왕에게 대답했다.\n\n\"나는 엘핀의 으뜸가는 우두머리 음유시인이요, 나의 고향은 여름 별들의 고장이로다. 나는 노아와 함께 방주에서 아시아에 있었고, 소돔과 고모라의 멸망을 보았으며, 로마가 세워질 때 나는 인도에 있었고, 이제 여기 트로이아의 잔존자들에게로 왔노라.\"\n\n왕과 그의 귀족들이 그 노래를 듣고서 크게 의아해했으니, 이는 그처럼 어린 소년에게서 그와 같은 것을 일찍이 들어 본 적이 없었기 때문이다. 왕은 그가 엘핀의 음유시인임을 알자, 그의 첫째가는 가장 현명한 음유시인 헤이닌에게 탈리에신에게 응수하여 그와 겨루라고 명했다. 그러나 그가 나아갔을 때, 그는 입술로 \"블레룸!\" 하고 소리를 내는 것밖에는 아무것도 할 수 없었다. 그가 스물네 음유시인의 나머지를 부르러 보냈을 때에도, 그들 모두 똑같이 했을 뿐 다른 아무것도 할 수 없었다. 마엘간이 소년 탈리에신에게 무슨 볼일이냐고 묻자, 그는 노래로 그에게 대답했다.\n\n\"그위드노의 아들 엘핀이 아르트로의 땅에 있으니, 그를 가르친 자를 찬양한 죄로 열세 개의 자물쇠로 갇혔도다. 그러므로 나, 탈리에신은, 서방 음유시인들의 우두머리로서, 황금 족쇄로부터 엘핀을 풀어 주리라.\"\n\n그러고는 그들에게 수수께끼를 노래했다.\n\n\"그대는 알아내 보라, 그것이 무엇인지를, 대홍수 이전부터 있던 강한 피조물, 살도 없고, 뼈도 없고, 힘줄도 없고, 피도 없고, 머리도 없고, 발도 없으며, 태초에 그러했던 것보다 더 늙지도 더 젊어지지도 않으리라. 보라, 바다가 어떻게 희게 일렁이는지, 그것이 처음 올 때, 그것이 남쪽에서 올 때, 그것이 해안을 칠 때. 그것은 들에도 있고, 숲에도 있으나, 눈으로는 그것을 알아챌 수 없도다. 한 존재가 그것을 마련하셨으니, 무시무시한 돌풍으로, 마엘간 그위네드에게 복수를 내리시고자 함이라.\n\n그가 이렇게 시를 노래하는 동안 거센 폭풍이 일어, 왕과 그의 모든 귀족들은 성이 그들의 머리 위로 무너지리라 여겼다. 왕은 사람들을 시켜 서둘러 엘핀을 지하 감옥에서 데려오게 하여, 그를 탈리에신 앞에 세웠다. 그러자 탈리에신이 곧바로 시 한 수를 노래하니, 사슬이 그의 발에서 풀려났다고 한다.\n\n그런 다음 탈리에신은 엘핀의 아내를 그들 앞에 데려와, 그녀에게 손가락이 하나도 모자라지 않음을 보였다. 이런 식으로 그는 주인을 감옥에서 풀어 주고, 안주인의 결백을 지켰으며, 음유시인들을 잠잠하게 하여 그들 가운데 어느 누구도 감히 한마디도 하지 못하게 했다. 엘핀은 더없이 기뻐했고, 탈리에신도 더없이 기뻐했다.\n브리튼 종족의 영웅 신화\n\n베오울프\n브리튼 종족의 영웅들의 이름 가운데 두드러진 것이 베오울프의 이름이니, 이는 영어를 쓰는 모든 사람들에게 매우 각별한 방식으로 호소한다. 그는 우리 영국 선조들이 대륙의 고향을 떠나 브리튼 섬들로 건너가기 이전에 품었던 이상을 그 이야기에서 엿볼 수 있는 유일한 영웅이기 때문이다.\n\n이 영웅은 소년 시절과 젊은 날에 수많은 힘의 위업으로 두각을 나타냈으나, 그가 널리 명성을 떨치게 된 것은 덴마크 왕 흐로스가르를 괴물 그렌델에게서 구해 낸 일을 통해서였다. 그렌델은 반은 괴물이요 반은 사람이었으며, 흐로스가르의 거처 부근의 늪지 요새에 보금자리를 두고 있었다. 그는 밤마다 헤오로트라 불리는 왕의 거대한 궁전에 몰래 숨어들어, 거기서 잠든 기사들을 때로는 한 번에 서른 명씩이나 죽였다. 베오울프는 엄선된 전사 무리의 선두에 서서 괴물을 치러 갔고, 끔찍한 싸움 끝에 그것을 죽였다. 이튿날 밤 그렌델의 어미가, 제 아들 못지않게 무시무시한 마귀가, 흐로스가르의 가장 담대한 가신 가운데 하나를 채어 갔다. 베오울프는 다시 한번 덴마크 왕을 돕기 위해 나서서, 늪 한가운데 진흙 호수 바닥에 있는 암컷 괴물의 소굴까지 그것을 뒤쫓아 가, 그의 훌륭한 검 흐룬팅과 자신의 우람한 두 팔로 그 바다 여인의 목을 부러뜨렸다.\n\n흐로스가르가 그에게 베푼 영예를 가득 안고 자신의 나라 예아트로 돌아온 베오울프는, 예아트랜드의 왕을 섬겨 그의 가장 신임받는 자문관이자 용사가 되었다. 여러 해가 지나 왕이 적의 손에 쓰러지자, 예아트족은 한마음으로 베오울프를 새 왕으로 뽑았다. 전사로서의 그의 명성은 나라를 침략에서 자유롭게 지켜 주었고, 정치가로서의 그의 지혜는 나라의 번영과 행복을 더해 주었다.\n\n그러나 베오울프 통치 오십 년째 되던 해, 거대한 불을 뿜는 용의 형상으로 큰 공포가 그 땅에 닥쳤다. 그 용은 밤이면 바위 속 굴에서 날아 나와 타오르는 숨결로 어둠을 밝히고, 입에서 나오는 불길로 집과 농가, 사람과 가축을 불태웠다. 자신의 백성이 고통받고 죽어 가는데도 어떤 전사도 감히 목숨을 걸고 이 죽음의 황폐로부터 나라를 구해 내려 하지 않는다는 소식이 베오울프에게 이르자, 늙은 왕은 방패와 검을 들고 자신의 마지막 싸움을 향해 나아갔다. 용의 동굴 입구에서 베오울프는 목청을 높여 굴의 무시무시한 수호자에게 격렬한 도전을 외쳤다. 용은 끔찍하게 포효하며 이글거리는 두 날개를 마주쳐 푸드덕거리고는 베오울프를 향해 돌진하여, 반은 날고 반은 뛰어올라 그에게 덤벼들었다. 그리하여 무시무시한 격투가 시작되었으니, 그것은 베오울프가 용의 비늘 갑옷을 꿰뚫어 치명상을 입히는 것으로 끝났으나, 아아! 그 자신도 적의 독 어금니에 목을 물려 상처를 입었고, 그것이 그의 죽음을 불러왔다. 그가 땅바닥에 쭉 뻗은 채 누워, 용과의 싸움을 도왔던 명예로운 전사 위글라프에게 머리를 받친 채, 베오울프는 위글라프의 손을 움켜쥐며 안간힘을 다해 말했다.\n\n\"이제 그대는 나라의 필요를 돌보아야 하리라. 나는 더 이상 여기 머물지 않으니, 운명이 나를 부르는도다! 그대는 내 전사들에게 일러, 나의 화장단이 사위거든 바닷가 벼랑 꼭대기에 나의 봉분을 높이 쌓게 하라. 하여 거센 물결 위로 거품 이는 용골을 멀리 모는 뱃사람들이 앞으로 그것을 베오울프의 봉분이라 부르게 하라. 그대는 와그문드 일족의 마지막 사람이로다! 위르드가 내 모든 혈족을, 그 모든 용감한 우두머리들을 휩쓸어 갔도다! 이제 나는 그들을 뒤따라야 하리라!\"\n\n이 마지막 말을 남기고, 위험을 찾아 나서는 데 용감하고 죽음과 운명을 의연히 마주하는 데 용감했던 예아트족의 왕은 쓰러져 숨을 거두었다. 그의 마지막 소망에 따라, 그의 부하들은 나무를 모아 벼랑 꼭대기에 쌓아 올렸다. 이 화장단 위에 베오울프의 시신을 누이고 재가 되도록 태웠다. 그런 다음, 바로 그 흐로네스네스의 벼랑 위에, 너르게 펼쳐지고 우뚝 솟은 거대한 봉분을 세웠으니, 그것은 이후로 베오울프의 봉분이라 알려지게 되었다.\n\n————\n\n# CUCHULAIN, CHAMPION OF IRELAND\n\n아일랜드의 용사 쿠훌린\n유럽의 모든 초기 문학 가운데, 대륙의 정반대 모퉁이에 자리하면서도 가장 놀랍도록 비슷한 특징을 보여 주는 둘이 있다. 그것은 그리스 문학과 아일랜드 문학으로, 두 문학의 유사성을 잘 보여 주는 아일랜드 용사 쿠훌린의 전설은 아킬레우스의 이야기와 너무도 닮아서, 이 영웅에게 \"아일랜드의 아킬레우스\"라는 칭호를 얻어 주었다. 참으로 무모한 용기, 두려움을 불러일으키는 위력, 자기 가치에 대한 자부심, 그리고 거침없는 언변에서, 아일랜드 영웅은 막강한 그리스 영웅과 전적으로 대등하다.\n\n쿠훌린은 얼스터 왕 코노르의 조카로, 그의 누이 데흐티레의 아들이었으며, 그의 아버지는 인간이 아니라 긴 손의 위대한 신 루였다고 한다. 쿠훌린은 코노르 왕이 손수 길렀는데, 그가 아직 소년이었을 때부터 그의 명성은 온 아일랜드에 퍼졌다. 그의 호전적인 행적은 젖먹이 또래의 아이가 아니라 노련한 전사의 그것이었으니, 쿠훌린이 열일곱 살이 될 무렵 그는 얼스터의 용사들 가운데 견줄 자가 없었다.\n\n쿠훌린이 큰 권능을 지닌 드루이드인 교활한 포갈의 딸 에머와 혼인하자, 부부는 코노르 왕의 보호 아래 얼스터의 수도 아마에 거처를 정했다. 이곳에는 한 우두머리가 있었으니, 그리스 지도자들 가운데 테르시테스가 그러했듯이, 독설가 브리크리우라는 자로서 분란 일으키기를 즐겼다. 머지않아 그는 얼스터의 영웅들 사이에 다툼을 부추길 계략을 꾸미고 있었는데, 그 영웅들 가운데 으뜸은 막강한 라이가레, 쿠훌린의 사촌 코날 케르나흐, 그리고 쿠훌린 자신이었다. 브리크리우는 코노르 왕의 궁정 사람들을 만찬에 초대하여, 누가 \"용사의 몫\"을 차지할지를 두고 다툼이 일어나도록 꾸몄으니, 그 일이 어찌나 성공적이었던지, 피비린내 나는 싸움을 피하고자 앞서 말한 세 영웅은 아일랜드 용사의 우두머리 자리에 대한 자신들의 주장을 코노트 왕 아일릴에게 맡겨 판가름하기로 했다.\n\n아일릴은 영웅들을 뜻밖의 시험에 들게 했다. 그들의 만찬이 따로 떨어진 방에 차려졌는데, 왕은 그 방으로 거대한 고양이의 형상을 한 세 마리의 마법 짐승을 들여보냈다. 그것들을 보자 라이가레와 코날은 식사 자리에서 일어나 서까래 사이로 기어 올라가 밤새도록 거기 머물렀다. 쿠훌린은 한 마리 고양이가 자신을 덮칠 때까지 기다렸다가, 검을 뽑아 그 괴물을 내리쳤다. 그것은 더 이상 싸울 기색을 보이지 않았고, 동이 트자 마법 짐승들은 사라졌다.\n\n라이가레와 코날이 이 시험은 공정치 못했다고 주장하자, 아일릴은 세 경쟁자를 케리의 쿠로이에게 보냈다. 그는 공정하고 현명한 사람으로, 마법과 주술로 영웅들 가운데 으뜸을 가려내기로 했다. 그들은 차례로 쿠로이의 성 밖에서 보초를 섰는데, 그곳에서 라이가레와 코날은 거대한 거인에게 제압당하고 말았다. 그 거인은 거대한 떡갈나무로 만든 창들을 내던졌고, 끝내는 그들을 담장 너머 안뜰로 내동댕이쳤다. 쿠훌린만이 홀로 거인을 막아 냈는데, 그러자 그는 다른 마법의 적들에게 공격을 받았다. 그 가운데에는 용 한 마리가 있었으니, 그것은 이웃한 호수에서 끔찍한 날개를 펴고 날아와 앞을 가로막는 모든 것을 집어삼킬 듯이 보였다. 쿠훌린은 그 놀라운 영웅의 도약으로 뛰어올라, 용의 입속으로, 목구멍 아래로 팔을 찔러 넣어 그 심장을 뜯어냈다. 괴물이 죽어 쓰러지자, 그는 그 비늘 덮인 머리를 베어 냈다.\n\n그런데도 쿠훌린의 적수들이 그의 우두머리 자격을 인정하려 하지 않자, 세 사람 모두 아마로 돌아가 쿠로이의 판결을 기다리라는 지시를 받았다. 그러던 어느 날 밤, 쿠훌린과 그의 사촌 코날을 제외한 얼스터의 영웅들이 모두 큰 홀에 있게 되었다. 그들이 신분 순서대로 앉아 있을 때, 거구에다 흉측한 외모를 하고, 게걸스러운 노란 눈을 한 무시무시한 낯선 자가 들어왔다. 그의 손에는 날이 예리하고 번쩍이는 거대한 도끼가 들려 있었다. 코노르 왕이 그곳에 온 용건을 묻자, 낯선 자가 대답했다.\n\n\"내 도끼를 보라! 오늘 이것을 움켜쥐는 자는 이것으로 내 머리를 벨 수 있으되, 다만 내가 같은 방식으로 내일 그자의 머리를 벨 수 있어야 한다. 만일 그대들에게 감히 나를 마주할 용사가 없다면, 나는 얼스터가 용기를 잃었고 명예를 잃었다고 말하리라.\"\n\n곧바로 라이가레가 그 도전을 받아들였다. 거인은 자기 머리를 받침대 위에 올려놓았고, 영웅은 단칼에 그것을 몸에서 베어 냈다. 그러자 거인은 일어나 머리와 도끼를 집어 들고는, 그렇게 머리 없이 홀에서 성큼성큼 걸어 나갔다. 그러나 이튿날 밤 그가 여전히 멀쩡한 모습으로 돌아와 라이가레의 약속 이행을 요구하자, 라이가레는 겁을 먹고 앞으로 나서지 못했다. 그러자 낯선 자는 얼스터의 사내들을 비웃었으니, 그들의 위대한 용사가 감히 약속을 지키지도, 자신이 가한 일격의 대가로 받아야 할 일격을 마주하지도 못한다는 것이었다.\n\n얼스터의 사내들은 더없이 부끄러웠으나, 그날 밤 그 자리에 있던 코날 케르나흐가 낯선 자와 새로이 약조를 맺었다. 그는 거인의 머리를 베는 일격을 가했으나, 이번에도 이튿날 저녁 거인이 멀쩡하고 온전한 모습으로 돌아오자, 그 용사는 어디서도 찾을 수 없었다. 이제 쿠훌린의 차례가 되었으니, 그는 다른 이들이 그러했듯이 단칼에 거인의 머리를 베어 냈다. 이튿날 코노르의 궁정 사람들은 쿠훌린이 어찌하는지 보려고 그를 지켜보았다. 그가 다른 이들처럼 실패했더라도 그들은 놀라지 않았을 것이며, 그 다른 이들은 이제 그 자리에 있었다. 그러나 용사는 실패하거나 물러설 기색을 조금도 보이지 않았다. 그는 슬픈 마음으로 자기 자리에 앉아 있다가, 그들이 기다리는 동안 한숨을 쉬며 코노르 왕에게 말했다. \"모든 것이 끝날 때까지 이 자리를 떠나지 마소서. 죽음이 제게 아주 확실히 다가오고 있으나, 저는 제 약조를 이행해야 합니다. 약속을 어기느니 차라리 죽는 편이 낫기 때문입니다.\"\n\n날이 저물 무렵, 낯선 자가 의기양양하게 홀로 성큼성큼 걸어 들어왔다.\n\n\"쿠훌린은 어디 있느냐?\" 그가 외쳤다.\n\n\"여기 있다,\" 하는 대답이 돌아왔다.\n\n\"아, 가엾은 녀석! 오늘 밤 네 말이 슬프구나. 죽음의 두려움이 네게 무겁게 드리워 있구나. 허나 적어도 너는 네 말을 지켰고 나를 저버리지 않았도다.\"\n\n젊은이는 자리에서 일어나, 거대한 도끼를 든 채 서 있는 그를 향해 나아가, 일격을 받고자 무릎을 꿇었다.\n\n얼스터의 영웅은 받침대 위에 머리를 올려놓았다. 그러나 거인은 만족하지 않았다. \"목을 더 길게 빼라,\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"나를 가지고 놀며 괴롭히는구나,\" 쿠훌린이 말했다. \"이제 어서 나를 죽여라. 나는 어젯밤 너를 기다리게 하지 않았다.\"\n\n그러나 그는 명령받은 대로 목을 길게 뺐고, 낯선 자는 도끼를 들어 올렸다. 도끼는 폭풍 속에 나무들이 쓰러질 때의 굉음처럼 홀의 서까래를 위로 뚫고 부딪쳤다. 도끼가 무시무시한 소리와 함께 내려오자, 모든 사람이 두려움에 떨며 쿠훌린을 바라보았다. 내려온 도끼는 그를 건드리지조차 않았으니, 그것은 무딘 쪽이 땅에 닿도록 내려왔고, 젊은이는 다친 데 없이 그곳에 무릎을 꿇고 있었다. 그에게 미소 지으며 도끼에 기대어 선 자는 무시무시하고 흉측한 낯선 자가 아니라, 마침내 판결을 내리러 온 케리의 쿠로이였다.\n\n\"일어나라, 쿠훌린,\" 쿠로이가 말했다. \"얼스터의 모든 영웅 가운데 용기와 충성과 진실에서 그대에 견줄 자가 없도다. 오늘 이후로 아일랜드 용사들의 우두머리 자격은 그대의 것이며, 모든 잔치에서 용사의 몫도 그대의 것이라. 그리고 그대의 아내에게는 얼스터의 모든 여인 가운데 으뜸의 자리를 내가 판결하노라. 이 판결에 감히 이의를 다는 자에게 화가 있으리라!\" 그러고는 쿠로이가 사라졌고, 전사들이 쿠훌린의 둘레에 모여, 모두 한목소리로 그를 온 아일랜드 용사들의 우두머리로 환호했으니, 그 칭호는 오늘날까지도 그에게 따라붙어 있다.\n\n이것은 아일랜드의 용사에 관해 전해지는 수많은 이야기 가운데 하나이니, 그의 용맹한 행적은 여러 쪽을 가득 채우고도 남으리라. 쿠훌린은 결국 전장에서 최후를 맞았으니, 그 싸움에서 그는 여느 때와 다름없는 용맹을 한껏 떨쳤으나, 그를 제압하기 위해 부당한 수단이 쓰였다.\n\n여러 세기에 걸쳐 웨일스와 잉글랜드에 아서는 대표적인 \"더없이 점잖고 완벽한 기사\"였다. 그와 마찬가지로, 잉글랜드의 자매 섬에서는 쿠훌린이 아일랜드 게일족의 가장 드높은 이상을 언제나 대변한다.\n\n————\n\n# HEREWARD THE WAKE\n\n파수꾼 헤리워드\n파수꾼(또는 \"경계하는 자\") 헤리워드에게서, 우리는 그 연대를 상당히 정확하게 확인할 수 있으면서도 그 이야기 속에는 모든 시대에 속하는 듯한 신화적 요소가 등장하는, 그런 영웅들 가운데 하나를 발견한다. 원시 종족의 민담은 거대한 보고이니, 한 민족은 거기서 이야기와 영웅적 행적을 골라 자기 민족의 영웅을 찬미할 수 있으되, 그 똑같은 이야기와 행적이 다른 민족과 다른 영웅을 위해 쓰인 적이 있다는 것은 개의치 않는다. 그리하여 넬슨이나 조지 워싱턴 못지않게 실재하고 실존했던 애국 영웅, 그가 죽은 지 사십 년 안에 그 행적이 산문과 운문으로 기록되었던 색슨족 헤리워드조차도, 이미 그때부터 전설과 신비의 구름에 둘러싸여, 그의 가문도, 그의 혼인도, 심지어 그의 죽음까지도 그 구름이 모호하게 가려 버렸던 것이다.\n\n간략히 말하자면, 헤리워드는 링컨셔 태생으로, 1070년 무렵 한창때였다. 그해에 그는 잉글랜드에 나타난 덴마크인 무리에 가담하여 피터버러를 공격하고 그곳 수도원을 약탈했으며, 이후 일리 섬으로 피신했다. 그곳에서 그는 정복왕 윌리엄에게 포위되었고, 끝내 그 노르만인에게 항복할 수밖에 없었다. 이리하여 그는 패배한 색슨 종족을 대변하게 되었고, 그의 이름은 색슨족이 가장 사랑하는 영웅의 이름으로 후세에 전해졌다. 그가 일리를 훌륭히 방어한 일로 인해, 사람들은 그가 끝내 윌리엄 공에게 항복한 것을 용서해 주었다. 그들은 자유민에게 본래 깃들어 있다고 여겨지는 온갖 덕성과, 영국인이 자랑으로 삼던 온갖 영광스러운 용맹을 그에게 돌렸으며, 마지막으로 그의 죽음을 필사적인 싸움의 후광으로 둘러싸, 그의 마지막 격전을 롱스보에서의 롤랑의 그것만큼이나 경이로운 것으로 만들었다. 롤랑이 노르만 봉건 기사도의 이상이라면, 헤리워드도 그에 못지않게 앵글로색슨의 굳건한 사내다움과 기사도의 이상이다.\n\n헤리워드가 젊은 시절에 겪은 한 모험에 대한 이야기는 그의 용맹에 관해 전해지는 이야기들의 본보기가 될 것이다. 콘월로 어쩔 수 없이 가게 되었을 때, 그는 작은 브리튼족 우두머리인 알레프 왕이 자신의 아름다운 딸을 무시무시한 픽트족 거인에게 약혼시켰다는 것을 알게 되었는데, 그렇게 하기 위해 왕은 딸과 아일랜드의 한 덴마크 왕의 아들인 워터퍼드의 시그트뤼그 왕자 사이의 혼약을 깨뜨렸다. 언제나 의협심이 강한 헤리워드는 거인에게 시비를 걸어 정정당당한 싸움에서 그를 죽였고, 그러자 왕은 그를 옥에 가두었다. 그러나 이튿날 밤, 풀려난 공주가 손을 써서 그 용감한 색슨인이 풀려나도록 하여, 그를 자신의 연인 시그트뤼그 왕자에게 부랴부랴 보냈다. 헤리워드는 숱한 모험 끝에 왕자에게 이르렀고, 왕자는 그 젊은 영웅과 함께 서둘러 콘월로 돌아갔다. 그러나 두 사람 모두에게 비통하게도, 그들은 도착해서야 공주가 사납고 거친 콘월의 영웅 하코에게 막 약혼했으며, 혼인 잔치가 바로 그날 열리기로 되어 있다는 것을 알게 되었다. 시그트뤼그는 곧바로 덴마크인 마흔 명의 무리를 알레프 왕에게 보내, 자신과 왕의 딸 사이의 혼약을 이행할 것을 요구하며, 만일 그것이 깨진다면 복수하겠다고 위협했다. 이 위협에 왕은 아무런 대답도 내놓지 않았고, 그들이 어떻게 맞아들여졌는지를 전하러 돌아온 덴마크인은 하나도 없었다.\n\n시그트뤼그는 왕의 명예를 믿고 아침까지 기다리려 했으나, 헤리워드는 음유시인으로 변장하여 혼인 잔치에 들어가는 것을 허락받았고, 그곳에서 그는 곧 아름다운 노래로 갈채를 받았다. 신랑 하코는 황홀해하며 그가 청하는 것은 무엇이든 들어주겠다고 했으나, 그는 다만 신부의 손으로 따라 주는 포도주 한 잔만을 청했다. 그녀가 그것을 그에게 가져오자, 그는 빈 잔에 혼약의 반지를, 그녀가 시그트뤼그에게 정표로 보냈던 그 반지를 던져 넣고 말했다. \"고맙소, 부인이여, 떠도는 음유시인에게 베풀어 주신 너그러움에 보답하고자 합니다. 그 마음 씀씀이의 정표를 담아, 전보다 더 값진 이 잔을 돌려 드리리다.\" 공주는 그를 바라보다가 잔 속을 들여다보고 자신의 반지를 발견했다. 그러고는 다시 바라보고서, 자신의 구원자를 알아보았고 구출이 가까이 왔음을 알았다.\n\n사람들이 잔치를 벌이는 동안 헤리워드는 귀를 기울이고 이야기를 나누면서, 마흔 명의 덴마크인이 포로로 잡혀 있으며, 이튿날 하코가 신부를 확실히 차지하게 되면 풀려나되, 다만 눈이 멀린 채 쫓겨나 쓸모없고 비참한 신세로 풀려나리라는 것을 알아냈다. 하코는 자신의 아름다운 신부를 데리고 자기 땅으로 돌아가려 하고 있었고, 헤리워드는 어떤 구출이든 성공하려면 행군 중에 시도해야 함을 알았다.\n\n젊은 색슨인은 시그트뤼그에게 돌아가 자신이 알아낸 모든 것을 일러 주었고, 덴마크인들은 하코가 포로들의 눈을 멀게 한 뒤 풀어 주기로 정한 골짜기에 매복을 계획했다. 모든 일이 헤리워드가 짜 놓은 그대로 실행되었다. 콘월 사람들은 덴마크인 포로들과 함께 먼저 공격받지 않고 지나갔다. 다음으로 하코가 왔으니, 말 없는 신부 곁에서 험상궂고 사납게 말을 타고 있었으며, 그는 자신의 성공에 의기양양해하고, 그녀는 구출의 기색이 없나 애타게 살피고 있었다. 그들이 지나갈 때 헤리워드가 은신처에서 뛰쳐나오며 외쳤다. \"저들에게 덤벼라, 덴마크인들이여, 그대들의 형제를 풀어 주어라!\" 그러고는 직접 하코를 쳐 쓰러뜨리고 그 머리를 베어 냈다. 짧은 격전이 있었으나, 곧 구출된 덴마크인들이 자신들의 구원자들을 도울 수 있게 되었고, 콘월의 호위병들은 모두 죽임을 당했다. 본래 하코의 대의에 그다지 열성이 없던 알레프 왕의 부하들은 달아났고, 덴마크인들이 그 들판의 주인으로 남았다.\n\n그러는 사이 시그트뤼그는 공주의 안전을 살펴 두었고, 이제 그녀를 자신과 헤리워드 사이에 세우고서 배까지 호위해 갔으니, 배는 곧 그들을 워터퍼드로, 그리고 행복한 혼례로 데려다주었다. 워터퍼드의 왕자와 공주는 헤리워드를 자신들의 구원자이자 가장 좋은 벗으로 언제나 받들었고, 감사하는 마음으로 그가 늘 자신들과 함께 살기를 바랐다. 그러나 영웅의 떠돌며 모험하는 기질은 그가 정착하는 것을 허락하지 않았고, 오히려 다른 나라들에서 무용을 떨치도록 그를 내몰았으니, 그곳에서 그는 누구에게도 뒤지지 않는 명성을 금세 얻었다.\n\n————\n\n# ROBIN HOOD\n\n로빈 후드\n앵글로색슨어의 가장 오래된 유산 가운데에는 로빈 후드와 그의 유쾌한 무법자들에 관한 노래와 전설이 있으니, 그것은 육백 년이 넘도록 어린이와 어른 모두를 매료해 왔다. 이 흥겨운 이야기들은 초서가 \"캔터베리 이야기\"를 쓰던 시절, 더 격식 차린 정밀한 오늘날의 인쇄본 책 대신에 음유시인과 필경사가 자리하던 그 시절로 거슬러 올라간다.\n\n로빈 후드가 실존 인물이었는지 아닌지에 대한 물음은 여러 해 동안 제기되어 왔으니, 이는 윌리엄 텔을 비롯하여 누구나 차라리 믿음으로 받아들이고 싶어 하는 이들에 대해 비슷한 물음이 제기되어 온 것과 마찬가지다. 그것은 박식한 이들이 옛 기록을 샅샅이 뒤지고 그 주제에 관해 책을 썼음에도, 짤막한 \"그렇다\"나 \"아니다\"로 답할 수 없는 물음이다. 일반적인 믿음에 따르면 로빈은 리처드 1세 치세의 무법자였으니, 그때 그는 셔우드 숲 깊은 곳에서 모두 뛰어난 궁수인 키 큰 사내 백 명을 자신이 빼앗은 노획물로 거두어 먹였다. 그러나 \"그는 어떤 여인도 핍박받거나 다른 식으로 괴롭힘당하도록 두지 않았으며, 가난한 자들의 재물은 아끼고, 수도원과 부유한 백작들의 저택에서 훔쳐 온 것으로 그들을 넉넉히 구제하였다.\" 그리하여 로빈은 평민들에게 엄청난 인기를 누렸다.\n\n이 인기는 우두머리에서부터 그의 강인한 무리의 모든 구성원에게로 미쳤다. \"로빈 후드와 그의 모든 훌륭한 자유민에게 신의 가호가 있기를\"이라는 구절은 많은 옛 발라드의 맺음말이다. 동료들보다 활을 더 잘 쏘는 영리한 궁수, 타격에 단련된 용감한 자유민, 그리고 어떤 어려움이 닥쳐도 벗에게 진실할 수 있는 사내는 모든 시대에 걸쳐 사랑받는 인물이었으니, 그들은 로빈 후드와 그의 유쾌한 무법자들의 모습으로 이상화되었다.\n\n초기 영국 시대의 이 그림 같은 인물에 관해 가장 잘 알려진 이야기 가운데 하나는, 월터 스콧 경이 \"아이반호\"에서 들려준 것으로, 리처드가 왕국에 없는 사이 존 왕자의 통치, 아니 실정(失政) 동안 벌어진 활쏘기 시합에 관한 것이다. 로빈 후드는 록슬리라는 가명으로, 애시비에서 열린 왕실 무술 대회에 장궁 쏘기 경기의 상을 노리는 경쟁자로서 대담하게 모습을 드러낸다. 경기에 참가한 여덟 명인가 열 명의 궁수 가운데, 그 수는 마침내 둘로 좁혀지는데, 바로 왕의 한 귀족을 섬기는 산지기 휴버트와, 록슬리, 곧 로빈 후드다. 휴버트가 마지막 솜씨 겨루기에서 먼저 쏘아, 화살을 과녁의 안쪽 고리 안에, 그러나 정확히 한가운데는 아닌 곳에 맞힌다.\n\n\"'바람을 고려하지 않았군, 휴버트,' 록슬리가 말했다. '그러지 않았다면 더 나은 사격이었을 텐데.'\n\n\"이렇게 말하고는, 조준에 뜸을 들이려는 기색은 조금도 보이지 않은 채, 록슬리는 정해진 자리로 걸어가, 과녁을 보지도 않은 듯 겉보기에는 무심하게 화살을 쏘았다. 화살이 시위를 떠나는 바로 그 순간에도 그는 거의 말을 하고 있었으나, 그 화살은 휴버트의 화살보다 한가운데를 표시한 흰 점에 2인치 더 가까이 과녁에 꽂혔다.\n\n\"'하늘의 빛에 걸고!' 존 왕자가 휴버트에게 말했다. '네가 저 떠돌이 불한당 놈에게 진다면, 너는 교수대에 매달려 마땅하다!'\n\n\"휴버트에게는 어떤 경우에나 통하는 한 가지 정해진 대꾸가 있었다. '전하께서 저를 매달려 하신다 해도,' 그가 말했다. '사람이란 제 힘껏 할 수 있을 뿐입니다. 그렇지만 저의 할아버님께서는 좋은 활을 당기셨고——'\n\n\"'네 할아비와 그 일족 모두에게 흉악한 마귀가 들러붙으라지!' 존이 말을 가로막았다. '쏘아라, 이놈, 네 힘껏 쏘아라, 그러지 않으면 네게 더 나쁜 일이 닥칠 것이다!'\n\n\"이렇게 다그침을 받자, 휴버트는 제자리로 돌아가, 적수에게서 받은 충고를 소홀히 하지 않고, 막 일어난 아주 가벼운 바람을 알맞게 고려하여, 어찌나 성공적으로 쏘았던지 그 화살이 과녁의 바로 한가운데에 꽂혔다.\n\n\"'휴버트 만세! 휴버트 만세!' 군중이 외쳤으니, 낯선 자보다 아는 사람에게 더 관심이 쏠린 까닭이었다. '과녁 한복판이다!——한복판이다!——휴버트 영원하라!'\n\n\"'저 사격은 자네가 더 낫게 할 수 없을 걸세, 록슬리,' 왕자가 비웃는 미소를 띠고 말했다.\n\n\"'그래도 저는 그의 화살에 금을 새겨 드리겠습니다,' 록슬리가 대답했다.\n\n\"그러고는 전보다 조금 더 조심하여 화살을 날리니, 그것은 적수의 화살 바로 위에 꽂혀, 그 화살을 산산조각으로 쪼개 버렸다. 둘러서 있던 사람들은 그의 놀라운 솜씨에 어찌나 경악했던지, 평소의 함성으로조차 놀라움을 터뜨리지 못했다. '이자는 틀림없이 악마이지, 살과 피로 된 사람이 아니다,' 자유민들이 서로에게 속삭였다. '이런 활 솜씨는 브리튼에서 처음으로 활시위가 당겨진 이래로 본 적이 없다.'\n\n\"'그러면 이제,' 록슬리가 말했다. '북부 지방에서 쓰이는 그런 과녁을 세우도록 전하의 윤허를 청합니다. 그리고 가장 사랑하는 어여쁜 처녀의 미소를 얻고자 그것에 한 발을 쏘아 보려는 용감한 자유민이라면 누구든 환영하겠습니다.'\"\n\n이에 록슬리는 길이 6피트에 사람 엄지손가락만큼 굵은 버드나무 가지를 세운다. 휴버트는 그런 활 솜씨 겨루기에 참가하는 영예를 사양할 수밖에 없으나, 그의 적수는 300피트 거리에서 그 가지를 손쉽게 쪼개고 상을 차지한다.\n\n\"존 왕자조차도 록슬리의 솜씨에 감탄하여 한순간 그 인물에 대한 미움을 잊었다. '이 스무 닢의 금화는,' 그가 말했다. '뿔나팔과 더불어 네가 정정당당히 얻은 것이니, 네 것이다. 만일 네가 우리 호위대의 자유민으로서 제복을 받고 우리를 섬기며 우리 곁에 머문다면, 우리는 그것을 쉰 닢으로 만들어 주겠다. 일찍이 그토록 강한 손이 활을 당기거나, 그토록 정확한 눈이 화살을 겨눈 적이 없었으니 말이다.'\"\n* 71: (원주에 해당하는 출처 표시.)\n\n그러나 록슬리는 왕자의 봉사에 들어가는 것이 자신에게는 불가능하다고 밝히고는, 자신의 상을 그 훌륭한 휴버트와 너그러이 나누고서, 좋은 푸른 숲의 빛과 그늘 사이에 있는 자신이 사랑하는 본거지로 다시 한번 물러난다.\n샤를마뉴 전설\n\n서론\n샤를마뉴와 그의 동료들에 관한 낭만적인 옛이야기의 기원을 탐구한 이들은, 샤를 마르텔의, 어쩌면 다른 샤를들의 행적까지도, 본래 샤를마뉴에게 속하는 행적과 민간 전승 속에서 뒤섞여 버렸다는 견해를 갖고 있다. 그것은 참으로 더없이 중대한 시대였으니, 만일 우리 독자들이 이제 우리가 펼쳐 보일 그 허구의 연대기를 읽어 나가기에 앞서, 그 시대의 실제 역사를 잠시 빠르게 훑어볼 인내심을 가져 준다면, 그 역사가 시인들의 이야기 못지않게 낭만적임을 알게 될 것이다.\n\n서기 600년부터 시작되는 그 세기에, 우리 구세주의 본향에 동쪽과 남쪽으로 접한 나라들은 아직 그분의 종교를 받아들이지 않은 상태였다. 아라비아는 태양과 달과 별을 숭배하던 고대 페르시아인의 종교와 비슷한 우상 숭배 종교의 본거지였다. 571년 메카에서 마호메트가 태어났고, 마흔 살에 그는 이곳에서 스스로를 하느님의 예언자라 선포했으니, 그리스도가 모세보다 위에 있었던 만큼이나 자신이 그리스도보다 위에 있는 존엄한 자라 했다. 더디게나마 상당수의 제자를 얻은 그는 자신의 종교를 퍼뜨리고자 무력에 호소했다. 그의 추종자들의 열정과 열의는 이웃 나라들의 약함에 힘입어, 그와 그의 후계자들이 아라비아의 지배와 마호메트의 종교를 동쪽으로 인더스 강에 이르기까지, 북쪽으로 페르시아와 소아시아에 걸쳐, 서쪽으로 이집트와 지중해 남쪽 연안에 걸쳐, 그리고 거기서 다시 에스파냐의 주요 지역에 걸쳐 펼치도록 해 주었다. 이 모든 일이 헤지라, 곧 마호메트가 메카에서 메디나로 달아난 일로부터 백 년 안에 이루어졌으니, 그 일은 622년에 일어났으며, 우리가 그리스도의 탄생으로부터 햇수를 헤아리듯 마호메트교도들이 시간을 헤아리는 기점이다.\n\n에스파냐로부터 사라센인(마호메트의 추종자들은 이렇게 불렸다)이 프랑스로 들어가는 길이 열려 있었으니, 만일 그 정복이 이루어졌다면 십중팔구 그 뒤를 이어 유럽의 나머지 전역의 정복이 따랐을 것이고, 그 결과 그리스도교가 지상에서 추방되었을 것이다. 왜냐하면 그 시절에는 우리가 지금 문명에서 가장 앞섰다고 여기는 나라들조차도 그리스도교를 보편적으로 신봉하지는 않았기 때문이다. 게르마니아와 브리튼, 덴마크, 러시아의 큰 부분은 여전히 이교를 믿거나 야만 상태에 있었다.\n\n그 무렵 프랑스에서는, 비록 왕의 칭호는 없었으나, 우리가 말한 저 이름난 샤를들 가운데 첫째인 샤를 마르텔, 곧 샤를마뉴의 할아버지가 다스리고 있었다. 에스파냐의 사라센인들은 712년과 718년에 프랑스로 침입했다가, 막대한 노획물을 가지고 물러갔다. 725년에는 당시 에스파냐의 사라센 총독이던 안베사가 대군을 이끌고 피레네 산맥을 넘어, 견고한 도시 카르카손을 급습하여 함락했다. 이 침입이 불러일으킨 공포가 어찌나 컸던지, 넓은 지역에 걸쳐 나라가 정복자에게 굴복했고, 그 속주의 마호메트교도 총독이 임명되어 나르본에 자리를 잡았다. 그러나 안베사는 한 전투에서 치명상을 입었고, 사라센인들은 이렇게 더 이상의 진격이 저지되어 나르본으로 물러갔다.\n\n732년 사라센인들은 압달라만의 지휘 아래 다시 프랑스를 침입하여, 가론 강 기슭으로 빠르게 진격하여 보르도를 포위했다. 도시는 강습으로 함락되어 병사들에게 내맡겨졌다. 침입자들은 여전히 앞으로 밀고 나아가, 오를레앙과 옥세르와 상스의 영지에 두루 퍼졌다. 그들의 선발대는 우두머리에게서 갑자기 소환되었으니, 그는 투르의 성 마르탱의 부유한 수도원에 대한 정보를 입수하고는 그것을 약탈하고 파괴하기로 마음먹었던 것이다.\n\n샤를은 이 모든 동안 사라센인들에게 맞서 아무것도 하지 않았으니, 그 까닭은 그들의 침입이 행해진 프랑스의 그 지역이 당시 그의 지배 아래 있지 않고, 아키텐이라는 이름의 독립 왕국을 이루고 있었으며, 그 왕은 외드였기 때문이다. 그러나 이제 샤를은 그 위험을 확신하게 되어 그것에 맞설 채비를 했다. 압달라만이 투르를 향해 진격하고 있을 때, 프랑크인의 군대를 이끌고 다가오는 샤를의 소식에 그는 유리한 전장을 차지하고자 푸아티에로 물러설 수밖에 없었다.\n\n샤를 마르텔은 자신의 영토 곳곳에서 전사들을 불러 모아, 일찍이 프랑스에서 보기 드물었던 그런 군대를 이끌고, 아마도 오를레앙에서 루아르 강을 건넜고, 아키텐 군대의 잔존 병력과 합류하여 732년 시월에 아랍인들을 시야에 두게 되었다. 사라센인들은 자신들이 이제 마주하게 될 무서운 적을 알아챈 듯했으니, 이 가공할 정복자들이 처음으로 머뭇거렸다. 두 군대는 어느 쪽도 감히 공격을 시작하지 못한 채 이레 동안 대치한 상태로 있었다. 그러나 마침내 압달라만이 전투의 신호를 내렸고, 사라센 군대의 거대한 무리가 맹렬히 프랑크인들에게 돌진했다. 그러나 북방 전사들의 두터운 전열은 바위처럼 버텼고, 사라센인들은 거의 하루 종일 그들에게 조금이라도 흠집을 내려는 헛된 시도에 힘을 소진했다. 마침내 오후 네 시 무렵, 압달라만이 프랑크인의 전열을 깨뜨리기 위한 새롭고 필사적인 시도를 준비하고 있을 때, 사라센인들의 후방에서 무시무시한 함성이 들려왔다. 그것은 외드 왕이었으니, 그는 자신의 아키텐인들과 함께 그들의 진영을 공격했고, 사라센 군대의 큰 부분이 자신들의 노획물을 지키고자 전장에서 우르르 빠져나갔다. 이 혼란의 순간에 프랑크인의 전열이 진격하여, 앞에 있던 전장을 휩쓸며 적들 사이에 끔찍한 살육을 가했다. 압달라만은 자신의 병사들을 다시 모으려고 필사적으로 애썼으나, 그 자신이 가장 용감한 장교들과 함께 그리스도교도들의 칼 아래 쓰러지자, 모든 질서가 사라졌고, 그의 군대의 잔존 병력은 자신들의 거대한 진영으로 피신했으니, 그곳은 외드와 그의 아키텐인들이 격퇴당했던 곳이었다. 이제 날이 저물었기에, 샤를은 어둠 속에서 진영을 공격하는 위험을 무릅쓰고 싶지 않아 군대를 물려, 아침에 전투를 재개할 셈으로 평원에서 밤을 보냈다.\n\n그리하여 날이 밝자 프랑크인들은 전투 대형을 갖추었으나, 적은 나타나지 않았다. 마침내 그들이 큰맘 먹고 사라센 진영에 다가가 보니, 그곳은 텅 비어 있었다. 침입자들은 밤을 틈타 퇴각을 시작했고, 이미 에스파냐로 돌아가는 길에 올라, 그들의 거대한 노획물을 뒤에 남겨 프랑크인들의 손에 떨어지게 한 것이었다.\n\n이것이 그 유명한 투르 전투로, 거기서 막대한 수의 사라센인이 죽임을 당했고, 프랑크인은 단 천오백 명만이 죽었다. 샤를은 이 승리의 결과로 마르텔(망치)이라는 별명을 얻었다.\n\n사라센인들은 이 모진 타격에도 불구하고 프랑스 남부에서 자신들의 입지를 계속 지켰다. 그러나 샤를 마르텔의 아들로서 아버지의 권력을 이어받아 왕의 칭호를 취한 피핀은 그들이 차지하고 있던 견고한 요새들을 차례로 빼앗았고, 759년에는 그들의 거점인 나르본을 함락함으로써 프랑스에서 그들 세력의 잔재를 소멸시켰다.\n\n샤를마뉴, 곧 샤를 대제는 768년에 아버지 피핀의 뒤를 이어 왕위에 올랐다. 이 군주는 수많은 낭만적 전설의 주인공이기는 하나, 허구에서보다 역사에서 더 위대하게 나타난다. 그를 전사로 보든 입법자로 보든, 학문의 후원자로 보든 야만 민족의 교화자로 보든, 그는 우리의 가장 뜨거운 찬탄을 받을 자격이 있다. 역사 속의 그는 그러하다. 그러나 옛이야기 작가들은 그를 흔히 나약하고 격정적이며, 음흉한 자문관들의 희생자요, 자신의 왕위 유지를 그 용맹에 기대고 있는 사나운 영주들에게 휘둘리는 인물로 그린다. 역사적 묘사가 의심할 바 없이 참된 것이니, 그것은 믿을 만한 기록으로 전해 내려오고, 그 시대의 사건들로 확증되기 때문이다. 그의 권세가 절정에 이르렀을 때, 프랑스 제국은 우리가 지금 프랑스, 게르마니아, 스위스, 네덜란드, 벨기에라 부르는 곳과 이탈리아의 큰 부분에 걸쳐 펼쳐져 있었다.\n\n800년에 샤를마뉴는 교황을 보호하고자 대군을 이끌고 갔던 로마에 머물던 중, 교황에 의해 서방의 황제로 대관되었다. 성탄절 날 샤를은, 마치 나머지 회중과 더불어 미사 거행에 참여하려는 것일 뿐인 양 성 베드로 성당에 들어섰다. 그가 제단에 다가가 기도하려고 몸을 굽히자, 교황이 앞으로 나아가 그의 머리에 금관을 씌웠다. 그러자 곧바로 로마 사람들이 외쳤다. \"하느님께서 대관하신 위대하고 평화로운 로마인의 황제, 존엄한 샤를께 생명과 승리가 있기를.\" 그러고 나서 교황은 그 앞에 엎드려, 고대 황제들의 시대에 확립된 관습에 따라 그에게 경의를 표했고, 그에게 성유를 발라 의식을 마무리했다.\n\n샤를마뉴의 전쟁은 주로 색슨족이라는 이름으로 지금 하노버와 네덜란드라 불리는 나라들에 살던 이교도 야만 민족을 상대로 한 것이었다. 그는 또한 에스파냐의 사라센인들을 상대로 원정을 이끌었으나, 사라센인들과의 전쟁은 옛이야기들이 주장하는 것처럼 프랑스에서가 아니라 에스파냐 땅에서 치러졌다. 그는 동부 피레네 산맥으로 에스파냐에 들어가, 바르셀로나와 팜플로나를 손쉽게 정복했다. 그러나 사라고사가 그에게 성문 열기를 거부하자, 샤를은 협상을 벌여 피레네 산맥을 다시 넘어가는 대가로 막대한 금을 받아들이는 것으로 끝을 맺었다.\n\n돌아오는 길에 그는 온 군대를 이끌고 엥기와 에노와 롱스보의 골짜기를 따라 산악의 협곡들을 지나 행군했다. 이 지역의 우두머리는 그가 진격할 때에는 군주국의 충실한 봉신으로서 샤를마뉴를 받들었으나, 이제 프랑크인들이 돌아오자, 그는 자신을 우두머리로 인정하는 거친 산사람들을 모두 불러 모았고, 그들은 군대가 그 아래로 지나가야 하는 산악의 고지를 점령했다. 군대의 본대는 아무런 방해도 만나지 않았고 위험의 기미도 받지 못했다. 그러나 상당히 뒤처진 데다 노획물에 발이 묶인 후위대는 롱스보의 고개에서 산사람들에게 압도당해 한 사람도 남김없이 죽임을 당했다. 이때 프랑크인 우두머리들 가운데 가장 용감한 몇몇이 목숨을 잃었는데, 그 가운데 브르타뉴 변경, 곧 국경 지대의 영주 롤랑, 곧 오를란도가 언급된다. 그의 이름은 후세에 유명해졌고, 롱스보의 참사와 롤랑의 죽음은 끝내 그 방대한 옛이야기의 순환에서 가장 이름난 일화가 되었다.\n\n이 일 이후로도 샤를마뉴의 군대와 사라센인들 사이에 적대적인 충돌이 있기는 했으나, 그것들은 사소한 것이었고, 대개는 에스파냐 땅에서였다. 그리하여 옛이야기 작가들의 이야기에 대한 역사적 근거는, 만일 우리가 더 이른 시대와 더 늦은 시대의 사건들이 샤를마뉴 자신의 시대의 사건들과 한데 합쳐졌다고 가정하지 않는 한, 빈약할 따름이다.\n\n그러나 오랫동안 진본으로 인정받았으며, 샤를마뉴 시대의 실존 인물인 랭스 대주교 튀르팽의 저작으로 여겨진, 사칭된 역사서가 하나 있다. 그 제목은 \"샤를 대제와 오를란도의 역사\"다. 그것은 이제 의심의 여지 없이, 어떤 잘 속고 양심 없는 수도사가, 잘 알려지고 저명한 인물에게 그 저작을 돌림으로써 자신의 옛이야기에 권위를 부여하려는 생각에서 만들어 낸 민간 전승 모음집으로 여겨진다. 그것은 그 사칭된 저자 튀르팽 주교를 이런 식으로 소개한다.\n\n\"랭스 대주교 튀르팽은 샤를 대제의 벗이자 비서로서, 성속의 문학에 두루 빼어나게 통달했고, 산문과 운문에 똑같이 어울리는 재능을 지녔으며, 가난한 자들의 옹호자요, 그 삶과 언행에서 하느님의 사랑을 받았으며, 황제 곁에서 사라센인들과 백병전으로 자주 싸웠으니, 그가 샤를 대제의 행적을 한 권의 책에 기록하며, 샤를과 그의 아들 루이 치하에서 우리 주의 해 830년까지 활약했다.\"\n\n튀르팽 대주교의 몇몇 장의 제목들은 그 역사서의 성격을 보여 줄 것이다. 그것들은 이러하다. \"저절로 무너진 팜플로나의 성벽에 관하여.\" \"창들이 자라난 성 파쿤두스의 전쟁에 관하여.\" (어떤 그리스도교도들이 저녁에 성 앞 땅바닥에 창들을 똑바로 꽂아 두었는데, 아침에 보니 그것들이 나무껍질과 가지로 덮여 있더라는 것이다.) \"어떻게 태양이 사흘 동안 멈추어 섰는가, 그리고 사천 명의 사라센인의 살육에 관하여.\"\n\n튀르팽의 역사서는 어쩌면 후대의 시인과 옛이야기 작가들이 샤를마뉴와 그의 팔라딘, 곧 동료들의 이름 둘레에 쌓아 올린 경이로운 모험들의 원천이었는지도 모른다. 그러나 아리오스토와 다른 이탈리아 시인들은 서로 다른 원천에서, 그리고 의심할 바 없이 흔히 자신들의 창작에서, 같은 영웅들에게 돌리는 수많은 다른 이야기를 길어 올렸으니, 그러면서 비록 그의 역사서에 그 흔적이 전혀 없는데도 자신들의 전거로 \"선량한 튀르팽\"을 인용하기를 주저하지 않았다. 그리고 그들 서술의 개연성 없음이, 아니 차라리 불가능함이 터무니없을수록, 그들은 더욱 정성스레 \"대주교\"를 인용하며, 대개는 그의 의심할 바 없는 진실성에 대한 자신들의 증언을 덧붙였다.\n\n샤를마뉴 동료들의 모험을 노래한 주요 이탈리아 시인들은 풀치, 보이아르도, 아리오스토다. 오를란도, 리날도, 아스톨포, 가노, 그리고 다른 이들의 성격은 모든 작품에서 똑같으나, 그들에게 돌려진 모험은 서로 다르다. 보이아르도는 우리에게 오를란도의 사랑을 들려주고, 아리오스토는 그의 좌절과 그로 인한 광기를, 풀치는 그의 죽음을 들려준다.\n\n덴마크인 오지에는 실존 인물이다. 역사는 그를 본래 덴마크 출신의 이교도이던 막강한 영주로, 그리스도교를 받아들이고 샤를마뉴 휘하에서 봉직했다고 묘사하는 점에서 옛이야기와 일치한다. 그는 황제에게 반기를 들었다가 추방되었다. 그는 훗날 샤를마뉴의 변변치 못한 후계자들의 치세에 프랑스를 약탈하던 노르만족 해적 무리 가운데 하나를 이끌었다. 한 옛 연대기 작가가 오지에의 입을 빌려 묘사한 샤를마뉴의 모습은 어찌나 생생한지, 우리는 그것을 옮겨 적고 싶은 마음이 든다. 샤를마뉴는 파비아 공략을 향해 진격하고 있었다. 롬바르드인의 왕 디디에가 자신이 피난처를 내주었던 오지에와 함께 그 도시 안에 있었다. 왕이 다가오고 있다는 것을 알게 되자 그들은 높은 탑에 올라, 거기서 들판을 멀리, 넓게 바라볼 수 있었다. \"그들은 먼저 다리우스나 율리우스 카이사르의 군대에나 어울릴 만한 공성 무기들이 다가오는 것을 보았다. '저기 샤를마뉴가 있다,' 디디에가 말했다. '아니오,' 오지에가 말했다. 다음으로 롬바르드인은 들판을 온통 메운 방대한 군사들을 보았다. '저 무리와 함께 샤를이 진격해 오는 것이 분명하다,' 왕이 말했다. '아직 아니오,' 오지에가 대답했다. '우리에게 무슨 희망이 있겠는가,' 왕이 다시 말했다. '만일 그가 저보다 더 큰 무리를 거느리고 온다면?' 마침내 샤를이 나타났으니, 그의 머리는 철 투구로, 두 손은 철 장갑으로, 가슴과 어깨는 철 흉갑으로 덮여 있었고, 왼손은 철 창을 쥐고, 오른손은 검을 거머쥐고 있었다. 군주 앞에서 가던 자들도, 그의 곁에서 행군하던 자들도, 그를 뒤따르던 자들도, 모두 비슷한 무장을 하고 있었다. 철이 들판과 길을 뒤덮었고, 철의 끝이 햇빛을 반사했다. 그토록 단단한 이 철을, 마음은 그보다 더 단단한 백성이 지니고 있었다. 무기의 번쩍임이 도시의 거리에 공포를 쏟아부었다.\"\n\n군사적 모습을 한 샤를마뉴의 이 그림은, 그에 상응하는 그의 \"평화의 면모\"의 그림이 없다면 미완성으로 남을 것이다. 근대의 가장 위대한 역사가 가운데 한 사람인 기조 씨는 샤를마뉴의 영광을, 야만의 어둠에서 홀연히 떠올라 못지않게 홀연히 봉건제의 어둠 속으로 사라진 찬란한 유성에 비유했다. 그러나 이 유성의 빛은 꺼지지 않았으니, 되살아나던 문명은 프랑크인의 위대한 황제에게 길이 이로운 많은 것을 빚졌다. 그의 다스리는 손길은 그 시대의 입법에서도, 법의 집행에서도 엿보인다. 그는 학문을 장려했고, 잠식해 오는 사나운 영주들에 맞서 유일하게 평화롭고 지적인 계층이던 성직자들을 옹호했으며, 자애로운 아버지로서 아들이든 딸이든 자녀들의 교육을 정성껏 보살폈다. 그의 학문 장려에 관해 우리는 몇 가지 자세한 것을 들려주고자 한다.\n\n그는 학식 있는 이들을 이탈리아와 다른 외국에서 데려오게 하여, 그동안\n\n앞선 시대의 혼란으로 무너진 것들을 회복하고자 했다. 그는 이런 학자들에게 후하게 보답했고, 그 가운데 몇 사람을 가까이 두면서 우정을 베풀어 예우했다. 그중 가장 유명한 인물은 잉글랜드 사람 알퀴누스로, 그가 남긴 저술들은 지금까지 전해지면서 그가 박식하고 지혜로운 사람이었음을 입증하고 있다. 그는 알퀴누스와 그와 비슷한 다른 사람들의 도움을 받아 학술원, 곧 왕립 학교를 설립했는데, 이 기관은 왕국의 모든 학교의 학업을 지도하는 역할을 맡았다. 카롤루스 자신도 다른 회원들과 동등한 자격으로 이 학술원의 일원이었다. 그는 회합에 참석했고, 학술원 회원으로서의 모든 의무를 다했다. 회원마다 고대의 어느 유명한 인물의 이름을 취했다. 알퀴누스는 자신을 호라티우스라 불렀고, 또 다른 이는 아우구스티누스의 이름을, 세 번째 사람은 핀다로스의 이름을 취했다. 시편을 외우고 있었으며 자신의 생각대로 〈하느님의 마음에 합당한 왕〉이 되기를 열망했던 카롤루스는, 학술원 동료들로부터 다윗이라는 이름을 받았다.\n\n외국에서 그를 향해 품었던 존경에 관한 흥미로운 증거는, 아라비아인의 칼리프, 곧 그 유명한 하룬 알 라시드가 그에게 보낸 사절단에서 확인할 수 있다. 이 군주는 성품과 행실에서 카롤루스와 적지 않게 닮은 인물이었다. 사절들은 다른 값진 선물들과 더불어 시계 하나를 가져왔는데, 이는 유럽에서 처음 보는 것이어서 모두의 찬탄을 자아냈다. 그것은 열두 개의 문이 달린 십이면체 건축물의 형상을 하고 있었다. 이 문들은 벽감을 이루고 있었으며, 각각의 벽감 안에는 시간을 나타내는 작은 조각상이 하나씩 들어 있었다. 정시가 되어 종이 울릴 때면, 종이 한 번 칠 때마다 문이 하나씩 열리는 것이 보였고, 그 문에서 친 횟수만큼의 작은 조각상들이 나와서 줄지어 뒤따르며 탑 둘레를 엄숙하게 행진했다. 시계의 움직임은 물에 의해 이루어졌고, 종을 치는 일은 시간의 수와 같은 수의 놋쇠 공이 같은 금속으로 된 심벌즈 위로 떨어지면서 이루어졌는데, 떨어지는 공의 수는 물의 배출에 따라 정해졌다. 물이 그릇 안에서 내려가면서 공이 빠져나갈 수 있게 했던 것이다.\n\n카롤루스의 뒤를 이은 사람은 그의 아들 루도비쿠스로, 선의를 품었으나 유약한 군주였으며, 그의 치세에 카롤루스가 세운 구조물은 빠르게 허물어지기 시작했다. 루도비쿠스의 뒤는 차례로 두 명의 카롤루스가 이었는데, 무능한 군주들이었다. 그들의 유약하고 흔히 폭압적이었던 행실이야말로, 의심할 바 없이 로맨스에서 카롤루스에게 돌려진 그런 성격의 사건들의 근원이다. 로맨스의 전설에 그토록 자주 등장하는, 카롤루스의 팔라딘들이 보이는 무법적이고 불복종하는 처신 또한 쇠퇴기 제국의 한 특징이었지, 카롤루스 시대의 특징은 아니었다.\n\n————\n\n# THE PEERS, OR PALADINS\n\n동료 기사들, 곧 팔라딘들\n카롤루스의 가장 명망 높은 열두 기사는 〈동료들[페어]〉이라 불렸는데, 이는 그들 사이에 지배하던 평등 때문이었다. 한편 그들에게 함께 부여된 〈팔라딘〉이라는 이름은, 그들이 궁전의 거주자이자 왕의 동반자임을 뜻한다. 로맨스 작가들은 늘 그들의 이름을 똑같이 들곤 하지만, 그중 가장 두드러진 이들을 다음과 같이 꼽을 수 있다. 카롤루스가 아끼는 조카 오를란도, 곧 롤랑(앞은 이탈리아식, 뒤는 프랑스식 이름이다), 오를란도의 사촌인 몬탈반의 리날도, 바이에른 공작 나모, 브르타뉴 왕 살로몬, 대주교 튀르팽, 잉글랜드의 아스톨포, 덴마크인 오지에, 마법사 말라지지, 그리고 오를란도의 벗 플로리스마르트 등이다. 때때로 팔라딘으로 거명되는 다른 이들도 있어서, 그 수를 엄격히 열둘로 한정할 수는 없다. 카롤루스 자신도 그중 하나로 헤아려야 하며, 마옌스의 가넬론, 곧 가노는 나머지 모두의 배신자이자 적이었음에도, 그의 술수에 완전히 농락당한 채 그를 신임한 군주에 의해 명단의 높은 자리에 올랐다.\n\n우리는 주요 동료 기사들 가운데 몇몇을 독자들에게 더 자세히 소개하고, 나머지는 이야기가 진행되는 가운데 등장할 때 스스로 소개하도록 남겨두려 한다. 오를란도부터 시작하기로 한다.\n오를란도\n\n밀롱, 곧 밀로네는 대단한 가문의 기사로 카롤루스와 먼 친척이었는데, 황제의 누이 베르타와 몰래 혼인한 까닭에 프랑스에서 추방되고 교황으로부터 파문당했다. 밀롱과 그의 아내는 걸인의 처지로 오랫동안 비참하게 도보로 떠돈 끝에 이탈리아의 수트리에 이르렀고, 그곳의 한 동굴로 피신했는데, 바로 그 동굴에서 오를란도가 태어났다. 그의 어머니는 그곳에 계속 머무르며 이웃 농민들의 동정에서 얻는 변변찮은 도움으로 연명했고, 밀롱은 명예와 재물을 좇아 이국 땅으로 떠났다. 오를란도는 농민의 아이들 틈에서 자라났으며, 힘과 사내다운 기품에서 그들 모두를 능가했다. 비슷한 또래의 동무들 가운데에는, 신분은 훨씬 높았으나, 그 고을 통치자의 아들 올리버가 있었다. 두 소년 사이에 불화가 일어 싸움으로 번졌고, 오를란도가 그 경쟁자를 흠씬 두들겼다. 하지만 이 일이 두 사람 사이에 우정이 싹트는 것을 막지는 못했으니, 그 우정은 평생토록 이어졌다.\n\n오를란도는 너무도 가난하여 때로는 반쯤 헐벗고 지냈다. 그가 소년들의 사랑을 받았던 까닭에, 어느 날 그중 넷이서 그에게 옷을 지어 주려고 천을 가져왔다. 둘은 흰 천을, 둘은 붉은 천을 가져왔는데, 바로 이 사정에서 오를란도는 자신의 문장, 곧 〈사분 문장〉을 취하게 되었다.\n\n카롤루스가 제관을 받으러 로마로 가는 길에 수트리에서 공개적으로 식사를 하던 때였다. 그날 오를란도와 그의 어머니는 먹을 것이 하나도 없었는데, 오를란도가 불쑥 왕의 일행과 마주쳤다가 풍성한 식량을 보고는, 시종들에게서 들고 갈 수 있는 만큼 잔뜩 빼앗아, 그들의 저항에도 아랑곳없이 무사히 빠져나왔다. 이 사건에 관해 들은 황제는 꿈에서 받은 어떤 계시가 떠올라, 그 소년을 뒤따르게 했다. 이 일은 세 기사가 맡아 했는데, 그들이 동굴 안으로 들어설 때 오를란도가 몽둥이를 들고 맞섰을 것이나, 그의 어머니가 그를 말렸다. 그녀가 누구인지 그녀로부터 듣게 되자, 그들은 그녀의 발치에 몸을 던지고 황제로부터 사면을 얻어 주겠노라 약속했다. 이는 손쉽게 이루어졌다. 오를란도는 황제의 총애를 받아 그와 함께 프랑스로 돌아왔고, 어찌나 두각을 나타냈던지 마침내 왕좌와 기독교의 가장 든든한 버팀목이 되었다.[72]\n롤랑과 페라구스\n\n# ROLAND AND FERRAGUS\n\n오를란도, 곧 롤랑은 특히 페라구스와의 싸움으로 명성을 떨쳤다. 페라구스는 거인이었으며, 게다가 그의 살갗은 어떤 칼도 자국을 낼 수 없을 만큼 뚫리지 않는 재질로 되어 있었다. 그 거인의 싸움 방식은 적수를 두 팔로 붙잡아, 그가 아무리 발버둥쳐도 들고 가 버리는 것이었다. 롤랑은 온갖 솜씨를 다해도 겨우 거인의 손아귀에서 벗어나는 것이 고작이었고, 칼로 그에게 상처를 입히려는 모든 노력은 헛수고였다. 오래 싸운 끝에 페라구스는 너무도 지쳐 휴전을 제안했고, 합의가 이루어지자 그는 드러누워 곧장 잠이 들었다. 그는 완전히 안심한 채 잠을 잤으니, 그런 상황에서 적수를 이용하는 것은 기사도의 모든 법도에 어긋나는 일이었기 때문이다. 그런데 페라구스가 베개가 없어 너무도 불편하게 누워 있는 까닭에, 오를란도는 그를 가엾이 여겨 매끄러운 돌 하나를 가져다 그의 머리 밑에 받쳐 주었다. 거인이 개운하게 한숨 자고 깨어나 오를란도가 한 일을 알아차리자, 그는 무척 고마워하는 듯하더니, 친근해져서는 그런 부류의 인물들이 으레 하는 허풍 섞인 투로 거리낌 없이 떠들었다. 그는 여러 이야기 가운데, 오를란도에게 칼로 자기를 죽이려 애쓸 필요가 없다고 했는데, 자기 몸은 이곳을 제외하고는 어느 부위도 상처를 입지 않기 때문이라고 했다. 그러면서 그는 자기 가슴 한복판의 급소에 손을 가져다 댔다. 이 정보 덕분에 오를란도는 싸움이 재개되었을 때, 거인이 가리켰던 바로 그 자리를 찔러 그에게 치명상을 입히는 데 성공했다. 기독교 진영의 환호는 대단했고, 황제와 그의 온 군대가 승리한 팔라딘에게 쏟아부은 찬사도 끝이 없었다.\n\n또 한번은 오를란도가 강력한 사라센 전사와 맞붙어, 승리의 전리품으로 그에게서 칼 두린다나를 빼앗았다. 이 이름난 무기는 한때 트로이아의 명망 높은 왕자 헥토르의 것이었다. 그것은 더없이 정교한 솜씨로 만들어졌고, 그 강도와 단련이 어찌나 뛰어났던지 세상의 어떤 갑옷도 그것을 당해 낼 수 없었다.\n올리버에게는 롤랑으로\n\n# A ROLAND FOR AN OLIVER\n\n몽글라브의 게랭은 카롤루스에게 신속한 빈[비엔]의 영주였다. 그는 군주와 다투었고, 카롤루스는 인근 지역을 약탈한 뒤 그의 도시를 포위했다. 게랭은 연로한 전사였으나, 자신을 지키는 일을 네 아들과 두 손자에게 의지했는데, 그들은 당대의 가장 용맹한 기사들 축에 들었다. 포위가 두 달 동안 이어진 뒤, 카롤루스는 에스파냐 왕 마르실리우스가 프랑스를 침공하여, 아무런 저항도 받지 않자 남부 지방으로 빠르게 진격하고 있다는 소식을 받았다. 이 소식에 카롤루스는 동료 기사들의 조언에 귀를 기울여, 게랭과의 분쟁을 하늘의 결정에 맡기기로 했으니, 곧 제비로 뽑은 양편 각각의 기사 한 명씩이 벌이는 일대일 결투로 정하기로 한 것이다. 이 제안은 게랭과 그의 아들들에게도 받아들일 만한 것이었다. 네 아들의 이름과, 빠지려 하지 않은 게랭 자신의 이름, 그리고 제비뽑기에 끼워 달라고 청한 두 손자의 이름이 투구 안에 담겼고, 거기서 올리버의 이름이 뽑혀 나왔다. 손자들 가운데 가장 어린 그에게 결투의 영예와 위험이 맡겨졌다. 그는 자기 가문의 명분을 지킬 자격이 있다고 여겨졌다는 데 기뻐 우쭐대며 그 결정을 기꺼이 받아들였다. 카롤루스 편에서는 롤랑이 지명된 투사였는데, 롤랑도 올리버도 자신의 적수가 누구일지 알지 못했다.\n\n그들은 론강의 한 섬에서 만났고, 양 진영의 전사들은 양쪽 강기슭에 늘어서서 싸움을 지켜보았다. 첫 격돌에서 두 자루의 창이 모두 산산이 부서졌으나, 두 기수 모두 미동도 없이 안장에 앉아 있었다. 그들은 말에서 내려 칼을 뽑았다. 그러자 너무도 호각인 싸움이 벌어져, 구경하던 이들은 어느 쪽이 이길지 도무지 짐작할 수 없었다. 두 시간이 넘도록 두 기사는 치고 막고, 찌르고 받아내기를 계속했는데, 어느 쪽도 지친 기색을 보이지 않았고, 방심하여 허를 찔리는 일도 없었다. 마침내 오를란도가 올리버의 방패를 맹렬히 내리쳐 두린다나를 방패 가장자리에 어찌나 깊이 박았던지 다시 빼낼 수 없게 되었고, 거의 같은 순간에 올리버는 오를란도의 흉갑을 어찌나 힘차게 찔렀던지 칼이 손잡이에서 뚝 부러지고 말았다. 이리하여 두 전사는 무기를 잃은 채 남겨졌다. 그들은 한순간도 멈추지 않고 서로에게 달려들어, 저마다 적수를 땅에 쓰러뜨리려 안간힘을 썼고, 그것이 여의치 않자 저마다 상대의 투구를 낚아채 벗기려 들었다. 둘 다 성공했고, 같은 순간 그들은 맨머리로 얼굴을 마주하고 섰으니, 롤랑은 올리버를, 올리버는 롤랑을 알아보았다. 한순간 그들은 가만히 서 있었다. 그리고 다음 순간, 두 팔을 벌린 채 서로의 품으로 달려들었다. \"내가 졌소.\" 오를란도가 말했다. \"내가 항복하오.\" 올리버가 말했다.\n\n강기슭의 사람들은 이 모든 광경을 어떻게 받아들여야 할지 알 수 없었다. 이내 그들은 조금 전까지 맞붙었던 두 사람이 손을 맞잡고 서 있는 것을 보았고, 싸움이 끝났음이 분명해졌다. 기사들이 그들 둘레로 몰려들어, 한목소리로 그들을 영광에서 대등한 자들이라 칭송했다. 혹여 싸움이 결판나지 않은 채 끝났다고 투덜대려는 이가 있었다면, 덴마크인 오지에의 목소리에 입을 다물었을 것이니, 그는 명예가 요구하는 모든 것이 다 이루어졌다고 큰 소리로 선포했으며, 누가 이의를 제기하든 그 판정을 지켜내겠노라 단언했다.\n\n게랭과 그의 아들들과의 분쟁은 결판나지 않은 채 남았고, 나흘간의 휴전이 맺어졌으며, 그동안 한편으로는 나모 공작의 노력으로, 다른 편으로는 올리버의 노력으로 화해가 이루어졌다. 카롤루스는 게랭과 그의 용맹한 가족을 거느리고 마르실리우스를 맞아 진격했으며, 마르실리우스는 서둘러 국경 너머로 퇴각했다.\n\n리날도\n리날도는 카롤루스의 누이 아야와 혼인한 에몽의 네 아들 가운데 하나였다. 따라서 리날도는 카롤루스의 조카이자 오를란도의 사촌이었다.\n\n리날도가 무기를 들 만한 나이가 되었을 무렵, 오를란도는 사라센인들을 상대로 한 무훈으로 이미 빛나는 이름을 얻고 있었으니, 카롤루스와 그의 용감한 기사들은 그 사라센인들을 프랑스에서 몰아낸 터였다. 오를란도의 명성은 리날도의 마음에 고귀한 경쟁심을 불러일으켰다. 영광을 좇고 싶은 열망에 차서, 그는 파리 인근 지방을 떠돌다가, 어느 날 한 나무 밑에서 완전히 마구를 갖추고 한 벌의 갑옷을 가득 실은 훌륭한 말 한 마리를 보았다. 리날도는 그 갑옷을 입고 말에 올랐으나, 칼은 취하지 않았다. 형제들과 함께 황제로부터 기사 작위의 영예를 받던 날, 그는 어느 이름난 기사에게서 칼을 빼앗기 전에는 결코 칼을 옆구리에 차지 않겠노라 맹세했던 것이다.\n\n리날도는 그토록 많은 모험으로 유명한 아르덴 숲으로 향했다. 그가 숲에 들어서기가 무섭게 세월의 무게에 허리가 굽은 한 노인을 만났고, 그에게서 그 숲에는 길들일 수 없는 야생마 한 마리가 출몰하여, 제 앞길을 가로막는 것은 무엇이든 부수고 뒤엎어 버린다는 말을 들었다. 그 말을 공격하기는커녕 마주치기만 해도 죽음을 면치 못한다고 그는 말했다. 리날도는 겁먹기는커녕 그 짐승과 싸우고 싶다는 가장 간절한 열망을 드러냈다. 이것이 바로 훗날 그토록 유명해진 말 바이아르였다. 이 말은 전에 갈리아의 아마디스의 것이었다. 그 영웅이 죽은 뒤, 말은 어느 마법사의 힘으로 마법에 걸린 채 붙들려 있었는데, 마법사는 이 마법을 풀 때가 오면, 아마디스의 혈통이면서 그에 못지않게 용감한 기사에게 길들여지리라 예언했다.\n\n이 놀라운 말을 얻으려면 힘이나 솜씨로 그를 굴복시켜야 했으니, 그가 쓰러지는 순간부터 순하고 다루기 쉬운 말이 될 것이었기 때문이다. 그가 늘 머무는 곳은 숲 가장자리의 한 동굴이었으나, 인간을 넘어서는 힘과 용기를 지니지 않은 자라면 누구든 그에게 다가가는 자에게 화가 미칠 것이었다. 이 말을 들려준 뒤 노인은 떠났다. 그는 사실 노인이 아니라 마법사 말라지지, 곧 리날도의 사촌으로, 이 젊은 기사의 모험을 돕기 위해 그가 마침맞게 발견한 말과 갑옷을 마련해 주었던 것이며, 이제는 세상에 둘도 없는 말을 손에 넣을 길로 그를 인도한 것이었다.\n\n리날도는 숲속으로 뛰어들어 여러 날 동안 바이아르를 찾아다녔으나, 그 흔적조차 발견하지 못했다. 어느 날 그는 한 사라센 기사와 마주쳤는데, 기사들 사이에서 흔히 그러하듯 처음에 싸움으로 맞닥뜨려 알게 되었다. 이졸리에라는 이름의 이 기사 역시 바이아르를 찾는 중이었다. 리날도가 그 격돌에서 이겼는데, 충격이 어찌나 심했던지 이졸리에는 오랫동안 정신을 잃고 있었다. 그가 정신을 차리고 다시 싸움을 벌이려 할 때, 마침 지나가던 한 농부(그가 바로 말라지지였다)가 그 무시무시한 말이 가까이 와 있다는 소식으로 그들을 가로막으며, 그를 굴복시키려면 두 사람의 모든 능력을 다 쏟아부어야 할 것이니 힘을 합치라고 조언했다.\n\n이제 벗이 된 리날도와 이졸리에는 함께 그 말을 공격하러 나아갔다. 그들은 바이아르를 발견하고는, 오랫동안 숲에 몸을 숨긴 채 그 힘과 아름다움에 감탄하며 서 있었다.\n\n빛나는 밤색 털빛에(그래서 바이아르라 불렸다), 이마에는 은빛 별이 박혀 있고, 뒷발은 희며, 몸은 날렵하고, 머리는 섬세하며, 떡 벌어진 가슴은 불룩한 근육으로 가득 찼고, 어깨는 넓고 두툼하며, 다리는 곧고 힘줄이 단단하며, 두툼한 갈기가 활처럼 휜 목 위로 흘러내린 그 말은, 바위든 덤불이든 나무든 아랑곳없이 숲을 헤치고 돌진하며, 제 앞길을 가로막는 것은 무엇이든 찢어발기고, 도전하듯 울부짖으며 달려왔다.\n\n그는 먼저 이졸리에를 발견하고 그에게 돌진했다. 기사는 창을 겨눈 채 그를 맞았으나, 사나운 짐승은 창을 부러뜨렸고, 그 때문에 그의 질주는 한순간도 늦춰지지 않았다. 에스파냐 사람은 날렵하게 옆으로 비켜 그 돌진하는 폭풍에게 길을 내주었다. 바이아르는 질주를 멈추고 다시 기사에게로 돌아섰으니, 기사는 이미 칼을 뽑은 터였다. 그가 칼을 뽑은 것은 그 말을 길들일 가망이 없다고 여겼기 때문이다. 그것은 불가능하다고 그는 확신했다.\n\n바이아르는 그에게 돌진하여, 이쪽저쪽으로 사납게 뒷발로 곧추섰다. 기사는 흰 별이 이마를 장식한 자리를 칼로 내리쳤으나, 헛되이 내리쳤을 뿐이어서, 자기가 너무 약하게 친 줄로만 알고 부끄러워했다. 그 말의 살갗이 어찌나 질긴지 아무리 날카로운 칼도 자국을 낼 수 없다는 것을 그는 알지 못했던 것이다.\n\n다시 한번 칼이 휙 소리를 내며 더 큰 힘으로 내리쳐졌고, 사나운 말은 그것을 느껴 일격에 머리를 수그렸으나, 다음 순간 어찌나 세게 들이받았던지 이교도는 정신을 잃고 죽은 듯 땅바닥에 쓰러졌다.\n\n이졸리에가 쓰러지는 것을 보고 그가 목숨을 잃은 줄로 여긴 리날도는 말을 향해 달려들어, 주먹으로 그 턱을 어찌나 세게 후려쳤던지 피가 말의 입가를 진홍빛으로 물들였다. 활을 떠난 화살보다도 빠르게 말이 그에게로 돌아서서, 이빨로 그의 팔을 물어뜯으려 했다.\n\n기사는 뒤로 물러섰다가, 다시 일격을 가하여 그의 이마를 내리쳤다. 바이아르는 돌아서서, 산이라도 부숴 버릴 만한 힘으로 두 발을 함께 내질렀다. 리날도는 방심하지 않고, 머리로 들이받든 발로 차든 그의 공격을 피해 냈다. 그는 둘 다 피하며 그의 옆에 바짝 붙어 있었으나, 발을 헛디딘 탓에 마침내 말의 발에 끔찍한 일격을 맞았고, 그 충격에 거의 정신을 잃을 뻔했다. 그런 일격을 한 번 더 맞았더라면 목숨을 잃었을 것이나, 말이 아무렇게나 발길질을 한 덕에 두 번째 일격은 리날도에게 닿지 않았고, 그는 잠깐 사이에 정신을 추슬렀다. 이렇게 싸움이 계속되다가, 어쩌다 바이아르의 발이 떡갈나무 가지 사이에 끼고 말았다. 리날도는 그것을 붙잡아, 온 힘과 기량을 다 쏟아부어 그를 땅에 메다꽂았다.\n\n바이아르가 땅에 닿자마자 그의 모든 분노는 가라앉았다. 더는 두려움의 대상이 아니라, 그는 온순하고 조용해졌으나, 그 온화함 속에도 위엄이 깃들어 있었다.\n\n팔라딘은 그 목을 토닥이고, 가슴을 쓰다듬고, 갈기를 매만져 주었고, 짐승은 울음소리를 내며 제 주인의 손길을 받는 것을 기뻐하는 기색을 보였다. 리날도는 이제 그가 완전히 굴복한 것을 보고, 다른 말에서 안장과 마구를 떼어다 그 노획물로 바이아르를 치장했다.\n\n리날도는 카롤루스 궁정의 가장 명망 높은 기사들 가운데 하나가 되었으니, 오를란도를 제외한다면 사실상 가장 명망 높은 기사였다. 그러나 그는 늘 황제의 명령에 마땅한 만큼 순종하지는 않았으며, 그가 저지른 잘못은 무엇이든 마간차 공작 간의 악의로 인해 으레 더 부풀려지곤 했으니, 간은 리날도와 그의 온 가문에 대한 배신자이자 적이었다.\n\n한번은 리날도가 카롤루스의 심한 노여움을 사 궁정에서 추방당한 적이 있었다. 다시 총애를 되찾을 가망이 도무지 보이지 않자, 그는 에스파냐로 가서 사라센 왕 이보를 섬기게 되었다. 그의 형제들인 알라르도와 리카르도, 리치아르데토도 그와 동행했고, 네 사람 모두 어찌나 충실하게 왕을 섬겼던지 그의 높은 총애를 받기에 이르렀다. 왕은 그들에게 프랑스와 에스파냐의 국경 산악 지대에 땅을 내주었고, 인근의 온 지역을 리날도의 권한 아래 두었다. 그 산악에는 대리석이 풍부했고, 왕이 일꾼들을 대 주어, 그들은 리날도를 위해 높은 성벽으로 둘러싸인, 거의 난공불락의 성을 지었다. 흰 돌로 지어져 대리석 곶의 마루 위에 자리 잡은 그 성은 별처럼 빛났고, 리날도는 그 성에 몬탈반이라는 이름을 붙였다. 이곳에 그는 자신의 벗들을 불러 모았는데, 그들 가운데 다수는 그 자신처럼 추방당한 사람들이었고, 그 고장 사람들은 성이 제공하는 보호에 대한 보답으로 그들에게 식량을 대 주었다. 그러나 리날도의 부하들 가운데 일부는 무법자였고, 때때로 식량이 넉넉하게 공급되지 않는 일도 있어서, 리날도와 그의 수비대는 받지 못한 것을 힘으로 빼앗는다는 나쁜 평판을 얻었다. 그래서 우리는 몬탈반이 때때로 약탈자들의 소굴로 일컬어지고, 그 수비대원들이 거지 같은 수비대라 불리는 것을 보게 된다.\n\n카롤루스의 노여움은 오래가지 않았고, 우리의 이야기가 시작되는 시점에 리날도와 그의 형제들은 황제의 총애를 완전히 되찾았으며, 사라센인 및 이교도들과 벌인 그의 모든 전쟁에서, 그의 어느 기사도 그들보다 더한 열의와 충성으로 그를 섬기지는 않았다.\n\n————\n\n# THE TOURNAMENT\n\n마상 시합\n때는 5월, 오순절 축일이었다. 카롤루스는 성대한 축연을 명하고, 자신의 팔라딘들과 왕가의 봉신들 외에도, 그때 파리에 머물던 모든 외지인을 기독교도든 사라센이든 가리지 않고 축연에 불러 모았다. 손님들 가운데에는 에스파냐에서 온 그란도니오 왕, 독수리 같은 눈을 지닌 사라센 페라우, 황제의 조카인 오를란도와 리날도, 나모 공작, 살아 있는 가장 잘생긴 사내인 잉글랜드의 아스톨포, 마법사 말라지지, 그리고 마간차의 가노가 있었으니, 가노는 그 교활한 배신자로, 황제에게 음모를 꾸미면서도 마치 그를 사랑하는 것처럼 믿게 만드는 재주를 지닌 자였다.\n\n카롤루스는 봉신들과 팔라딘들의 우두머리 자리에 높이 앉아, 그들의 수와 위세를 생각하며 흐뭇해했고, 모두가 둘러앉아 음악을 듣고 잔치를 즐기던 차에, 별안간 거대한 거인 넷이 그들 사이에 비할 데 없이 아름다운 한 귀부인을 데리고 단 한 명의 기사를 거느린 채 홀로 들어왔다. 그 자리에는 그녀가 나타나기 전까지 아름다워 보였던 귀부인이 많이 있었으나, 그녀가 나타난 뒤로는 그들 모두가 하찮아 보였다. 기독교 기사들은 저마다 그녀에게로 눈길을 돌렸고, 이교도들은 저마다 그녀 둘레로 몰려들었으며, 그녀는 돌 같은 마음도 움직일 만한 감미로움으로 황제에게 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"고매하신 주군이시여, 바다에서 바다로 메아리치는 당신의 훌륭하심과 이 기사들의 무용에 대한 명성에 힘입어, 세상 끝에서 당신을 뵈러 온 두 순례자가 그 고생을 헛되이 치르지는 않으리라는 희망을 품게 되었나이다. 그리고 저희를 이리로 이끈 동기를 밝히기에 앞서, 이 기사가 제 오라비 우베르토이며, 제가 그의 누이 안젤리카임을 알아 주소서. 오늘로 정해진 마상 시합 소식을 명성을 통해 들었기에, 제 오라비 되는 왕자께서 자신의 무용을 입증하고자 오셨나이다. 여기 모인 기사들 가운데 누구든 그와 시합에서 겨루기를 원하는 이가 있다면, 그는 소나무 샘 곁 멀린의 계단에서 한 사람씩 차례로 그들을 맞으리라 전하라 하셨나이다. 그리고 그의 조건은 이러하옵니다. 낙마하게 되는 기사는 누구도 싸움을 다시 시작할 수 없으며, 제 오라비의 포로로 남아야 하나이다. 그러나 만일 제 오라비가 쓰러진다면, 그는 저를 승자의 전리품으로 남겨 둔 채 이 나라를 떠나리이다.\"\n\n그런데 이 안젤리카와, 우베르토라 자칭했으나 실제 이름은 아르갈리아인 그녀의 오라비는, 카타이 왕 갈라프론의 자식들이었음을 밝혀 두어야 하겠다. 갈라프론은 기독교 군대를 멸하고자 그들을 보낸 것이었으니, 아르갈리아는 닿는 것은 무엇이든 어김없이 쓰러뜨리는 마법의 창으로 무장하고 있었고, 바람보다 빠른 마법의 피조물인 말을 타고 있었기 때문이다. 안젤리카 또한 모든 마법을 막아 주고, 입에 물면 지닌 자를 보이지 않게 만드는 반지를 가지고 있었다. 이리하여 아르갈리아는 감히 그와 맞서려는 기사라면 누구든 굴복시켜 포로로 잡으리라 기대되었고, 팔라딘들을 이 치명적인 시도로 유혹하는 데에는 안젤리카의 매력이 의지가 되었으며, 한편 그녀의 반지는 그녀에게 손쉬운 도주 수단을 마련해 줄 것이었다.\n\n안젤리카가 말을 마치자 그녀는 왕 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 그의 대답을 기다렸으며, 모두가 감탄하며 그녀를 응시했다. 특히 오를란도는 거역할 수 없이 그녀에게 이끌려, 몸을 떨며 안색이 변할 정도였다. 홀 안의 모든 기사가 똑같은 감정에 사로잡혔으니, 머리가 하얗게 센 늙은 나모 공작도, 카롤루스 자신도 예외가 아니었다.\n\n모두가 한동안 말없이 서서 그녀를 바라보는 기쁨에 넋을 잃고 있었다. 혈기 왕성한 젊은이 페라우는 거인들에게서 그녀를 낚아채 데려가고 싶은 마음을 가까스로 억눌렀고, 리날도는 불처럼 빨갛게 달아올랐으며, 한편 자신의 술법으로 그 낯선 여인이 진실을 말하고 있지 않음을 간파한 말라지지는, 그녀를 바라보며 낮게 중얼거렸다. \"절묘한 거짓 피조물이로구나! 이 일로 네게 이런 장난을 쳐 주마. 네 방문을 자랑할 거리가 하나도 남지 않게 될 게다.\"\n\n카롤루스는 그녀를 되도록 오래 자기 앞에 붙들어 두고자, 여러 가지 질문을 던질 때까지 승낙을 미루었고, 그녀가 그 모든 질문에 신중하게 대답하자, 그제야 도전은 받아들여졌다.\n\n그녀가 떠나자마자 말라지지는 자신의 책을 들여다보아, 우리가 설명한 대로, 비열한 이교도 왕 갈라프론의 음모 전부를 알아냈다. 그리하여 그는 그 처녀를 찾아내어 그녀의 계략을 좌절시키기로 마음먹었다. 그는 약속된 장소로 서둘러 갔고, 그곳에서 왕자와 그의 누이가 아름다운 천막 안에 잠들어 있는 것을 발견했으며, 한편 거인 넷이 망을 보고 있었다. 말라지지는 자신의 책을 꺼내 거기서 주문을 외웠고, 곧장 거인 넷은 깊은 잠에 빠졌다. 그는 칼을 뽑아 들고(그는 작위를 받은 기사였으므로) 살그머니 그 젊은 여인에게 다가가, 단숨에 그녀를 처치하려 했다. 그러나 그녀의 모습이 너무도 사랑스러워 보이는 까닭에, 자신의 주문이 그녀에게 걸려 있어 그녀가 깨어날 수 없으리라 믿었던 터라, 서두를 필요가 없다고 여기며 잠시 멈칫했다. 그런데 그녀가 끼고 있던 반지가 주문의 효력으로부터 그녀를 지켜 주었고, 어떤 작은 소리 때문인지 아니면 다른 무엇 때문인지, 그녀는 바로 그 순간 잠에서 깨어났다. 그녀는 크게 비명을 지르며 오라비에게로 달려가 그를 깨웠다. 마법에 대한 그녀의 지식의 도움으로, 그들은 마법사를 붙잡아 단단히 묶고, 그의 책을 빼앗아 그의 술법을 그 자신에게 되돌렸다. 그런 다음 그들은 한 무리의 악마를 불러내어, 자신들의 포로를 붙잡아 그의 큰 도시 알브라카에 있는 갈라프론 왕에게 데려가라 명했고, 악마들은 그대로 했으며, 그가 도착하자 그는 바다 밑 바위 속에 갇혔다.\n\n이런 일들이 벌어지는 동안 파리에서는 온통 소란이 일었으니, 오를란도가 멀린의 계단에서 그 모험을 맨 먼저 시도하겠다고 고집을 부렸기 때문이다. 이는 안젤리카를 노리는 다른 구혼자들의 반발을 샀고, 모두가 그의 우선권을 두고 다투었다. 이 소동은 늘 쓰이는 방편인 제비뽑기로 가라앉았는데, 첫 번째 차례는 아스톨포가 뽑았다. 사라센 페라우가 두 번째를, 그란도니오가 세 번째를 차지했다. 다음으로 베를링기에리와 오토가, 그다음에는 카롤루스 자신이 뽑았고, 그의 불운으로 인해 서른 명도 더 지난 뒤에야 분개한 오를란도의 차례가 왔다.\n\n첫 번째 제비를 뽑은 아스톨포는 잘생기고 용감하며 부유했다. 그러나 부주의 탓인지 솜씨가 모자란 탓인지, 그는 운 없는 시합꾼이어서 곧잘 낙마하곤 했는데, 이런 사고를 그는 더없이 좋은 기분으로 받아넘기며, 언제나 기꺼이 다시 말에 올라 운수를 만회하려 애썼으나, 대개는 그다지 나은 성과를 거두지 못했다.\n\n아스톨포는 차림새도 거동도 무척 화려하게 모험에 나섰는데, 아르갈리아와 맞붙자마자 곧장 안장에서 나가떨어졌다. 그는 모든 잘못을 운수 탓으로 돌리며 운수를 원망했으나, 안젤리카의 친절로 그의 아픈 심정이 다소 누그러졌으니, 그녀는 그의 젊음과 잘생긴 용모에 마음이 움직여 천막 안에서 자유로이 지내게 해 주고, 그를 온갖 친절과 존경으로 대접하도록 했다.\n\n격렬한 페라우가 다음으로 격돌의 차례를 맞았는데, 아스톨포 못지않게 빠르게 나가떨어졌다. 그러나 그는 자신의 불운을 그리 순순히 받아들이지 않았다. \"황제의 약속 따위가 내게 무슨 상관이냐?\" 하고 소리치며, 그는 칼을 들고 아르갈리아에게 달려들었고, 아르갈리아는 어쩔 수 없이 자신을 지키려 말에서 내려 칼을 뽑았으나, 싸움에서 어찌나 밀렸던지 항복의 신호를 보냈고, 몇 마디 말이 오간 뒤 자신의 누이에게 청혼하는 페라우의 제안에 귀를 기울였다. 그러나 그 미녀는 그토록 거칠고 사납게 생긴 자와 짝이 될 마음이 조금도 없었던지라, 그 제안에 어찌나 질겁했던지, 오라비에게 아르덴 숲에서 만나자고 황급히 이른 뒤, 마법의 반지를 써서 두 사람의 시야에서 사라졌다. 이를 본 아르갈리아는 자신의 빠른 말에 올라 같은 방향으로 내달렸다. 페라우가 그를 뒤쫓았고, 이렇게 홀로 남겨진 아스톨포는 자신의 창이 부러진 터라, 그 안에 어떤 보물이 깃들어 있는지도 모른 채 마법의 창을 제 것 대신 차지하여 시합으로 돌아왔다. 카롤루스는 그 귀부인과 그녀의 오라비가 떠난 것을 알고는, 본래 의도한 대로 시합을 진행하라 명했고, 그 시합에서 아스톨포는 마법의 창의 도움으로 자신에게 맞서는 모든 자를 낙마시켰으니, 상대들도 그 자신도 똑같이 놀라워했다.\n\n팔라딘 리날도는 페라우와 그 낯선 자의 싸움이 어찌 되었는지 알게 되자, 사랑과 조바심에 사로잡혀 그 아름다운 도망자를 뒤쫓아 말을 몰았다. 오를란도는 그가 사라진 것을 알아채고 마찬가지로 뒤따라 나섰으며, 마침내 세 사람 모두 아르덴 숲에서, 보이지 않는 그녀를 찾아 헤매게 되었다.\n\n그런데 이 숲에는 두 개의 샘이 있었는데, 하나는 현자 멀린이 만든 것으로, 그는 트리스트람과 아름다운 이주드를 위해 그것을 마련했다.[73] 이 샘에는 그 물을 한 모금 마시면, 마신 이가 느끼던 사랑을 잊게 되고, 심지어 전에 사랑하던 대상에 대한 혐오마저 일으키는 효능이 있었기 때문이다. 다른 샘은 정반대의 성질을 지니고 있어, 그 물을 한 모금 마시면 마신 뒤 처음 본 살아 있는 대상을 향한 사랑을 불러일으켰다. 리날도는 우연히 앞서 말한 샘에 이르러, 더위로 달아오른 터라 말에서 내려, 한 모금으로 갈증과 사랑을 함께 가셨다. 그는 전처럼 안젤리카를 사랑하기는커녕 마음속 깊이 그녀를 미워하게 되었고, 자신이 벌이고 있는 추적에 진절머리가 났으며, 말을 타느라 지친 데다 아늑하고 꽃이 만발한 후미진 곳을 찾아내자, 그곳에 드러누워 잠이 들었다.\n\n얼마 뒤 안젤리카가 왔는데, 그녀는 다른 방향에서 다가오다가 다른 샘을 발견하여 그곳에서 갈증을 가셨다. 그런 다음 다시 길을 가다가, 잠들어 있는 리날도와 마주쳤다. 사랑이 곧장 그녀를 사로잡았고, 그녀는 그 자리에 뿌리박힌 듯 서 있었다.\n\n둘레의 풀밭은 온통 은방울꽃과 들장미로 가득했다. 어찌할 바를 모르던 안젤리카는 마침내 그 꽃을 한 움큼 따서, 잠든 이의 얼굴 위에 하나씩 떨어뜨렸다. 그는 잠에서 깨어, 그녀가 누구인지를 보고는, 얼굴을 돌린 채 그녀의 인사를 받고, 다시 말에 올라 내달렸다. 그 아름다운 여인이 그를 뒤따르며 불러 보아도 헛일이었고, 자기가 무엇을 했기에 그토록 멸시받는지 물어보아도 헛일이었다. 리날도는 그녀를 절망 속에 남겨 둔 채 사라졌고, 그녀는 눈물을 흘리며 그가 잠들어 있던 자리로 돌아왔다. 그곳에서 이번에는 그녀가 직접 드러누워, 그가 누웠던 땅바닥을 끌어안았고, 피로와 슬픔에 겨워 잠이 들었다.\n\n안젤리카가 그렇게 누워 있는 동안, 운명은 오를란도를 바로 그곳으로 인도했다. 그녀가 잠든 모습은 어찌나 사랑스러웠던지 상상조차 할 수 없을 정도였으니, 말로 표현하기란 더더욱 불가능했다. 오를란도는 다른 세계로 옮겨진 사람처럼 멍하니 응시하며 서 있었다. \"내가 땅 위에 있는 것인가,\" 하고 그가 외쳤다, \"아니면 낙원에 있는 것인가? 분명 잠든 것은 나이고, 이것은 내 꿈이로구나.\"\n\n그러나 그의 꿈은 그가 조금도 바라지 않던 방식으로 꿈이 아님이 드러났다. 아르갈리아를 죽인 페라우가, 질투로 길길이 날뛰며 다가왔고, 싸움이 벌어져 잠든 여인을 깨웠다.\n\n자기가 본 광경에 겁먹은 그녀는 자신의 작은 말에게로 달려갔고, 두 싸움꾼이 서로에게 정신이 팔려 있는 사이에, 숲을 가로질러 달아났다. 두 투사는 한 전령이 그들을 가로막을 때까지 싸움을 계속했는데, 전령은 페라우에게 그의 군주인 마르실리우스 왕이 그의 도움을 절실히 필요로 하고 있으니 에스파냐로 돌아와 달라고 간청한다는 말을 전했다. 이에 페라우는 싸움을 멈추자고 제안했고, 안젤리카를 뒤쫓고 싶어 안달이 난 오를란도는 이에 동의했다. 한편 페라우는 전령과 함께 에스파냐로 떠났다.\n\n그 아름다운 도망자를 찾으려는 오를란도의 추적은 모두 헛수고였다. 안젤리카는 마법의 힘에 힘입어 자기 나라로 잽싸게 돌아갔다.\n\n그러나 리날도에 대한 생각은 그녀의 마음에서 떨쳐지지 않았고, 그녀는 말라지지를 풀어 주어, 가능하다면 그를 시켜 리날도가 자신에게 애정을 되돌려 주게끔 그의 마음을 얻기로 결심했다. 그리하여 그녀는 자신의 손으로 그의 족쇄를 풀어 그를 지하 감옥에서 자유롭게 해 주고, 그의 책을 돌려주면서, 리날도를 자신의 발치로 데려온다는 조건으로 그에게 넉넉한 명예와 보상을 약속했다.\n\n이에 따라 말라지지는 자신의 책의 도움으로 악마 하나를 불러내어 그 위에 올라타고 떠났다. 목적지에 다다른 그는 리날도를 꾀어 마법의 작은 배에 태웠는데, 그 배는 눈에 보이는 사공도 없이 그를 어느 섬으로 실어 갔으니, 그 섬에는 즐거운 성이라 불리는 건축물이 서 있었다. 섬 전체가 정원이었다. 서편, 바다에 가까운 곳에 그 궁전이 있었는데, 대리석으로 지어져 어찌나 맑고 윤이 났던지 둘레의 풍경을 비출 정도였다. 리날도는 뭍에 올라, 이내 한 귀부인을 만났고, 그녀는 그에게 안으로 들기를 청했다. 그 집은 안도 밖만큼이나 아름다워서, 하늘빛과 황금빛으로, 그리고 고귀한 그림들로 꾸며진 방들로 가득했다. 귀부인은 기사를 이야기들이 그려져 있고, 황금빛 머리 장식을 얹은 수정 기둥들을 통해 정원으로 트인 한 방으로 이끌었다. 그곳에서 그는 한 무리의 귀부인들을 발견했는데, 그중 셋은 화음을 맞춰 노래하고 있었고, 또 한 사람은 더없이 절묘하게 어우러지는 악기를 연주하고 있었으며, 나머지는 그들 둘레로 춤을 추고 있었다. 귀부인들은 그가 오는 것을 보고는 춤을 그를 둘러싸는 원무로 바꾸었고, 그런 다음 그중 한 사람이 더없이 감미로운 태도로 말했다. \"기사님, 식탁이 차려졌고, 연회의 시간이 왔나이다.\" 이 말과 함께, 여전히 춤을 추면서, 그들은 그를 방 앞 잔디밭을 가로질러, 황금 천과 고운 아마포가 깔린 식탁으로 이끌었으니, 그 식탁은 샘 곁의 다마스크 장미 정자 아래 차려져 있었다.\n\n그곳에는 이미 네 명의 귀부인이 앉아 있다가, 일어서서 리날도를 진주로 장식된 의자에 그들의 상석으로 앉혔다. 참으로 그는 놀라움을 금치 못했다. 더없이 정교한 음식들과, 빼어난 만큼이나 향기로운 포도주로 이루어진 연회가 보석으로 꾸민 잔에 담겨 펼쳐졌고, 연회가 끝나갈 무렵 멀리서 하프와 류트 소리가 들려왔는데, 귀부인들 가운데 한 사람이 기사의 귓가에 대고 말했다. \"이 집과 여기서 보시는 모든 것이 그대의 것이옵니다. 이는 오로지 그대만을 위해 지어졌고, 그것을 지은 이는 여왕이옵니다. 참으로 그대는 스스로를 행복하다 여기셔야 하리니, 그녀가 그대를 사랑하며, 그녀는 세상에서 가장 빼어난 미녀이기 때문이옵니다! 그녀의 이름은 안젤리카이옵니다.\"\n\n리날도는 그토록 혐오하던 그 이름을 듣는 순간 안색이 변하여 벌떡 일어섰고, 귀부인이 무슨 말을 하든 아랑곳없이 정원을 가로질러 뛰쳐나가, 자기가 뭍에 오른 곳에 이르기까지 한순간도 멈추지 않고 서둘렀다. 작은 배는 아직 물가에 있었다. 그는 배 안으로 뛰어들어 노를 저어 나아갔는데, 배 안에는 자기 말고는 아무도 보이지 않았다. 그가 배의 움직임을 다스려 보려 해도 헛일이었으니, 배는 마치 격분한 듯 내달려, 음울한 숲으로 뒤덮인 머나먼 물가에 이르렀다. 이곳에서 리날도는 조금 전에 물리쳤던 것과는 전혀 다른 종류의 마법에 둘러싸인 채, 함정 구덩이에 빠지고 말았다.\n\n그 구덩이는 알타리파라 불리는 성에 딸린 것이었는데, 그 성은 사람의 머리들이 매달려 있고 피로 붉게 칠해져 있었다. 팔라딘이 놀라움에 차서 그 광경을 바라보고 있을 때, 흉측한 노파 하나가 구덩이 가장자리에 나타나, 그가 어느 괴물에게 던져질 운명이라고 말했다. 그 괴물은 살아 있는 사람의 살을 공급받음으로써만 온 나라를 황폐하게 만드는 것을 막을 수 있는 짐승이었다. 리날도가 말했다. \"그렇다 치자. 다만 내가 지금 이대로 무장한 채 있을 수만 있다면, 나는 아무것도 두렵지 않다.\" 노파는 비웃으며 웃었다. 리날도는 밤새도록 구덩이 안에 있었고, 이튿날 아침 괴물이 굴을 튼 곳으로 끌려갔다. 그곳은 높은 벽으로 둘러싸인 안뜰이었다. 리날도는 그 짐승과 함께 갇혔고, 끔찍한 싸움이 벌어졌다. 리날도는 괴물의 비늘에 아무런 자국도 낼 수 없었던 반면, 괴물은 그 무시무시한 발톱으로 팔라딘에게서 판금과 사슬 갑옷을 찢어 냈다. 리날도는 자신의 마지막 순간이 왔다고 생각하기 시작했고, 빠져나갈 길이 있는지 보려고 사방과 위를 둘러보았다. 그는 벽에서 열 자쯤 되는 높이에 들보 하나가 튀어나와 있는 것을 발견하고는, 거의 기적과도 같은 도약으로 그것에 닿는 데 성공하여, 몸을 날려 그 위에 가로걸쳤다. 이곳에서 그는 몇 시간이고 앉아 있었는데, 그 흉측한 짐승은 끊임없이 그에게 닿으려 애썼다. 그러던 차에 별안간 새처럼 무언가가 공중을 가르며 다가오는 소리가 들리더니, 느닷없이 안젤리카가 직접 들보 끝에 내려앉았다. 그녀는 손에 무언가를 들고 그에게 내밀며, 사랑스러운 목소리로 그에게 말을 건넸다. 그러나 리날도는 그녀를 보는 순간 물러가라 명하며, 그녀가 베푸는 모든 도움을 거절했고, 마침내는 만일 그녀가 자기를 떠나지 않으면 괴물에게 몸을 던져 운명을 맞겠다고 단언했다. 안젤리카는 그를 언짢게 하느니 차라리 목숨을 잃겠노라 말하며 떠났다. 그러나 떠나기에 앞서 그녀는 미리 마련해 둔 밀랍 덩어리 하나를 괴물에게 던져 주고, 그 둘레에 올가미 매듭을 지은 밧줄을 펼쳐 놓았다. 짐승은 그 미끼를 물었고, 이빨이 밀랍으로 들러붙은 것을 알아채자, 펄쩍펄쩍 뛰며 분풀이를 하다가 이내 올가미에 얽혀, 몸부림칠수록 매듭을 단단히 조여, 사지를 거의 움직일 수 없게 되었다. 리날도는 기회를 엿보다가 그 등 위로 뛰어내려, 목을 끌어안고 졸랐으며, 짐승이 죽어 쓰러질 때까지 손아귀를 늦추지 않았다.\n\n또 하나의 난관이 남아 있었다. 벽은 어마어마하게 높았고, 그 안에서 유일하게 트인 곳이라고는 어찌나 튼튼한지 창살을 부술 수 없는 격자창 하나뿐이었다. 곤경에 처한 리날도는 안젤리카가 바닥에 남겨 둔 줄칼 하나를 발견했고, 그 도움으로 탈출을 이루어 냈다.\n\n그가 또 어떤 모험을 겪었는지는 다음 장에서 이야기하겠다.\n\n————\n\n# THE SIEGE OF ALBRACCA\n\n알브라카 공방전\n카롤루스가 전체 어전 회의와 대마상 시합을 열고 있던 바로 그 무렵, 그의 왕국은 한 강대한 군주에게 침공당했는데, 그 군주는 게다가 전투에서 어찌나 용맹하고 강했던지 아무도 그를 당해 낼 수 없었다. 그의 이름은 그라다소였고, 그의 왕국은 세리카네라 불렸다. 그런데 가장 위대하고 가장 부유한 자들이 흔히 가질 수 없는 것을 갈망하다가 이미 가진 것마저 잃곤 하듯이, 이 왕은 오를란도의 칼 두린다나와 리날도의 말 바이아르를 갖지 못하고서는 도무지 만족할 수 없었다. 이것들을 손에 넣으려고 그는 프랑스를 상대로 전쟁을 벌이기로 작정하고, 이를 위해 강대한 군대를 정렬시켰다.\n\n그는 에스파냐를 거쳐 진군했고, 그 나라의 왕 마르실리우스를 여러 차례의 전투에서 무찌른 뒤, 빠르게 프랑스로 진격해 들어왔다. 카롤루스는 마르실리우스가 사라센이며 자신의 적이었음에도, 공동의 위험을 고려하여 이 위급한 처지에서 그를 도울 필요가 있다고 느꼈고, 동료 기사들의 동의를 얻어 강력한 병력과 함께 리날도를 그라다소에게 맞서도록 파견했다.\n\n승부를 가리기 어려운 격전이 거듭되었고, 그라다소는 꾸준히 프랑스 안으로 진격했다. 그러나 자신의 목적을 이루고 싶어 안달이 난 그는, 리날도에게 도보로, 그리고 다음 조건으로 일대일 결투를 청했다. 만일 리날도가 이기면, 그라다소는 모든 포로를 풀어 주고 자기 나라로 돌아가기로 했다. 그러나 만일 그라다소가 그날 이기면, 그는 바이아르를 차지하기로 했다.\n\n도전은 받아들여졌고, 만일 말라지지의 술법이 없었더라면 결투가 벌어졌을 것이다. 말라지지는 마침 그때 안젤리카의 왕국에서 돌아온 참이었는데, 자신을 사랑하다 죽어 가는 그 아름다운 공주를 리날도가 호의로 바라보도록 만들겠다는 작정을 품고 있었다. 말라지지는 그라다소의 모습으로 변장하여 리날도를 군대에서 꾀어냈고, 짧은 다툼 끝에 그의 앞에서 달아나는 척했으니, 이로써 리날도는 그를 따라 한 배에 오르도록 유인되었고, 그 배에 실려 가 우리가 이미 이야기한 대로 갖가지 모험에 얽혀들게 되었다.\n\n리날도의 형제 리치아르데토의 지휘 아래 남겨진 군대는 곧 카롤루스와 그의 모든 동료 기사들과 합세했으나, 처참한 패주를 겪었고, 황제와 그의 많은 팔라딘들이 포로로 잡혔다. 그러나 그라다소는 자신의 승리를 함부로 이용하지 않았다. 그는 카롤루스의 손을 잡아 자기 곁에 앉히고, 자기는 오로지 명예를 위해 싸울 뿐이라고 말했다. 그는 황제가 자신에게 바이아르와 두린다나를 넘긴다는 조건으로 모든 정복을 포기했으니, 그 둘 다 황제의 봉신들의 소유물이었고, 그중 앞의 것은, 그의 주장에 따르면, 리날도가 약속대로 자신을 만나러 나오지 않은 탓에 이미 자신에게 몰수된 것이었다. 카롤루스는 이 조건에 선뜻 응했다.\n\n바이아르는 주인이 떠난 뒤 리치아르데토의 보호를 받아, 카롤루스가 없는 사이 아스톨포가 지휘하고 있던 파리로 돌려보내진 터였다. 아스톨포는 바이아르를 내놓으라고 보내온 전갈을 크게 분개하며 받았고, 전령을 통해 이렇게 답했다. \"나는 내 친족 리날도의 말을 싸움 없이는 내주지 않겠다. 그라다소가 그 말을 원한다면 와서 가져가 보라지. 나, 아스톨포는 들판에서 그를 맞을 준비가 되어 있다.\"\n\n그라다소는 이 대답을 그저 우습게만 여겼으니, 성공한 전사로서의 아스톨포의 명성이 그리 높지 않았던 까닭이며, 그라다소는 리날도와 맺었던 흥정을 그와 기꺼이 다시 맺었다. 이 조건으로 싸움이 벌어졌다. 아스톨포의 손에 들린 마법의 창은 새로운 기적을 일으켰고, 그라다소, 그 무시무시한 그라다소가 낙마하고 말았다.\n\n그는 약속을 지켜, 자신의 포로들을 풀어 주고, 군대를 자기 나라로 돌아가도록 행군시켰으나, 리날도에게서 그의 말을, 그리고 오를란도에게서 그의 칼을 빼앗기 전에는, 아니면 그 시도 끝에 목숨을 잃기 전에는 결코 쉬지 않겠노라 맹세를 새로이 했다.\n\n아스톨포에게 감사하는 마음으로 가득 찬 카롤루스는 그를 자기 곁에 두고 온갖 명예를 안겨 주고 싶어 했으나, 아스톨포는 리날도를 찾아 그의 말을 돌려주고자 했기에, 그런 작정으로 파리를 떠났다.\n\n————\n\n이제 우리의 이야기는 오를란도에게로 돌아간다. 우리는 그가 잠든 미녀를 보고 매혹되었던 데서 그를 떠나왔는데, 그러나 그녀는 그가 페라우와 싸움을 벌이는 사이에 그에게서 달아나 버렸다. 오랫동안 숲속 깊은 곳을 헤매며 그녀를 찾았으나 헛일이었던지라, 그는 그녀를 따라 그녀의 아버지의 궁정으로 가기로 결심했다. 그리하여 카롤루스의 진영을 떠나, 그는 오랫동안 동쪽 방향으로 여행하며, 혹여 그 도망자의 소식을 얻을 수 있을까 하여 곳곳에서 물어보았다. 숱한 모험을 겪은 끝에, 그는 어느 날 여러 길이 교차하는 곳에 이르러, 그곳에서 한 전령을 만나 소식을 물었다. 전령의 대답에 따르면, 자신은 안젤리카의 명을 받아 체르카시아 왕 사크리판트의 도움을 청하러 파견되었는데, 이는 그녀의 아버지 갈라프론을 위함이라 했다. 갈라프론은 타르타리아 왕 아그리칸에게 자신의 도시 알브라카에서 포위당해 있었던 것이다. 이 아그리칸은 그 처녀에게 청혼했다가 거절당한 자로, 이제는 무력으로 그녀를 쫓고 있었다. 이리하여 오를란도는 자신이 알브라카에서 하룻길 안에 와 있음을 알게 되었고, 이제 안젤리카를 만나리라 확신하며 온 힘을 다해 그녀의 도시로 나아갔다.\n\n이렇게 여행하다가 그는 한 다리에 이르렀는데, 그 아래로는 거품 이는 강이 흐르고 있었다. 이곳에서 한 처녀가 술잔 하나를 들고 그를 맞으며, 이 다리의 관례는 길손에게 잔을 권하는 것이라고 알려 주었다. 오를란도는 내미는 잔을 받아 그 안의 것을 마셨다. 그가 그렇게 하자마자 머리가 빙빙 돌더니, 자신이 떠나온 여행의 목적도, 다른 모든 것도 잊어버리게 되었다. 이 마법의 영향 아래 그는 처녀를 따라 웅장하고도 경이로운 궁전으로 들어갔다. 이곳에서 그는 자신도 모르고 서로도 모르는 많은 기사들과 함께 있게 되었으니, 그들 모두가 마신 망각의 잔만 아니었더라면 서로가 전우임을 알아보았을 것이었다.\n\n————\n\n리날도를 찾으러 길을 가던 아스톨포는, 늘 그렇듯 화려하게 차려입고 채비를 갖춘 채 체르카시아에 다다랐고, 그곳에서 그 나라의 왕 사크리판트의 지휘 아래 진을 친 대군을 발견했는데, 사크리판트는 안젤리카의 아버지 갈라프론을 지키러 그 군대를 이끌고 가는 중이었다. 사크리판트는 아스톨포와 그의 말의 모습에 크게 감탄하여 그에게 정중히 말을 건네며 자신의 휘하에 들이려 했으나, 최근의 승리로 우쭐해진 아스톨포는 그의 제안을 경멸하듯 거절하고 제 갈 길을 갔다. 사크리판트 왕은 그의 모습에 너무도 마음이 끌린 나머지 그를 그리 쉽게 보내 줄 수가 없어, 왕의 장신구들을 벗어 두고 그를 뒤쫓아 나섰다.\n\n아스톨포는 이튿날 길에서 한 낯선 기사를 만났는데, 그 이름은 숲의 탑의 영주 플로리스마르트 경으로, 기사들 가운데 가장 용감하고 가장 훌륭한 이들 축에 드는 사람이었으며, 길잡이로 젊고 아름답고 정숙한 처녀를 데리고 있었으니, 그가 애틋이 사랑하는 그 처녀의 이름은 플로르델리스였다. 아스톨포는 다가서며 그 기사에게 도전하여, 귀부인을 내놓든지 아니면 무력으로 자신의 권리를 지킬 채비를 하라고 했다. 플로리스마르트는 결투를 받아들였고, 두 기사가 격돌했다. 플로리스마르트는 낙마했고 그의 말은 쓰러져 죽었으나, 바이아르는 그 충격에도 아무런 상처를 입지 않았다.\n\n플로리스마르트는 자신의 치욕과 처녀의 비탄하는 모습에 어찌나 절망에 휩싸였던지, 칼을 뽑아 자신의 가슴에 찌르려 했다. 그러나 아스톨포가 그의 손을 붙들고는, 자기는 오로지 영광을 위해 겨루었을 뿐이며, 그에게 귀부인을 남겨 두는 것으로 만족한다고 말했다.\n\n플로리스마르트와 플로르델리스가 영원한 감사를 맹세하고 있을 때 사크리판트 왕이 다다랐는데, 그는 한 투사의 처녀를 다른 투사의 말과 무구만큼이나 탐내어, 그들에게 시합을 청했다. 아스톨포는 도전자를 맞아 단숨에 그를 쓰러뜨리고, 자신의 군마를 플로리스마르트에게 내주었으며, 왕은 도보로 자신의 군대로 돌아가게 내버려 두었다.\n\n두 벗은 길을 이어 갔고, 오래지 않아 플로르델리스는 자기가 아는 표지들을 통해 그들이 망각의 물가에 다가가고 있음을 알아채고는, 발길을 되돌리거나 길을 바꾸라고 권했다. 기사들은 이 말을 들으려 하지 않았고, 행군을 계속하다가 곧 오를란도가 포로로 잡혔던 그 다리에 다다랐다.\n\n다리의 처녀가 앞서처럼 마법의 잔을 들고 나타났으나, 미리 일러 받은 아스톨포는 그것을 경멸하며 물리쳤다. 그녀는 잔을 땅바닥에 내동댕이쳤고, 불길이 타올라 다리에 다가갈 수 없게 되었다. 같은 순간에 두 기사는 아는 이며 모르는 이며 여러 전사들의 습격을 받았으니, 그들은 아무것도 기억하지 못한 채 자신들의 감옥을 지키려고 맹목적으로 가담한 것이었다. 이들 가운데 오를란도가 있었는데, 그를 보자 아스톨포는 그 모든 자신감에도 불구하고 감히 그와 맞설 엄두를 내지 못하고, 돌아서서 달아났으니, 바이아르의 힘과 빠르기 덕에 탈출할 수 있었다.\n\n한편 플로리스마르트는 두려운 중과부적에 짓눌려, 어쩔 수 없이 필요에 굴복하여 그 요정의 관례를 따랐다. 그는 잔을 마시고 나머지 이들과 함께 포로로 남았다. 두 벗을 잃은 플로르델리스는 그 자리에서 물러나, 자신의 연인을 구해 내려는 지칠 줄 모르는 노력에 몸을 바쳤다. 아스톨포는 알브라카로 길을 갔는데, 아그리칸이 막 그곳을 포위하려는 참이었다. 그는 안젤리카에게 정중히 환대받아 그녀의 수비대에 이름을 올렸다. 두각을 나타내고 싶어 안달이 난 그는 어느 날 밤 홀로 출격하여 아그리칸의 진영에 다다랐고, 마법의 창으로 그의 전사들을 좌우로 낙마시켰다. 그러나 그는 이내 포위되어 중과부적에 몰렸고, 아그리칸의 포로가 되고 말았다.\n\n그러나 구원이 가까이 있었으니, 어느 날 시민들과 병사들이 성벽 너머로 몸을 내밀고 있을 때, 그들은 한 줄기 흙먼지 구름을 발견했고, 그 구름이 포위군의 진영을 향해 굴러오면서 그 속에서 기병들이 박차고 나오는 것이 보였다. 이는 사크리판트의 군대로 밝혀졌는데, 그 군대는 곧장 아그리칸의 군대를 공격하여, 그의 진영을 뚫고 포위된 도시로 가는 길을 내려 했다. 그러나 아그리칸은 아스톨포에게서 빼앗은 바이아르에 올라타 있었으되 황금 창은 갖추지 않았으니, 그 창의 효능을 그는 알지 못했던 것이며, 그럼에도 그는 놀라운 활약을 펼쳐, 느닷없이 들이닥친 기습에 무너졌던 자신의 흩어진 군대를 다시 결집시켰다. 사크리판트는 사크리판트대로 더없이 결사적인 무용으로 자신의 부하들을 북돋웠으니, 그의 용기를 더욱 부추기는 것으로 안젤리카의 모습이 있었는데, 그녀는 성벽 위에 모습을 드러냈던 것이다. 그곳에서 그녀는 두 지휘관 아그리칸과 사크리판트 사이의 일대일 결투를 지켜보았다. 이 결투에서 마침내 그녀의 수호자가 밀리는 듯 보였을 때, 체르카시아인들이 포위망을 깨뜨려 두 싸움꾼을 갈라놓았고, 그들은 그 혼란 속에 서로 떨어져 휩쓸려 갔다. 심한 상처를 입은 사크리판트는 그 혼란을 틈타 알브라카 안으로 달아났고, 그곳에서 안젤리카에게 정중히 맞아들여져 정성껏 간호받았다.\n\n싸움이 계속되어 마침내 체르카시아인들이 패주하게 되었고, 적의 전열과 도시 사이에 가로막힌 그들은 성벽 아래에서 피난처를 찾았다. 안젤리카는 도개교를 내리고 성문을 열어 도망자들을 들이도록 명했다. 이들과 함께, 무리 속에서 눈에 띄지 않은 아그리칸이 체르카시아인과 카타이인을 앞으로 몰아붙이며 성안으로 들어왔고, 내리닫이 쇠창살이 떨어지면서 그는 안에 갇히고 말았다.\n\n한동안 그가 불러일으킨 공포가 모든 맞서는 자들을 패주시켰으나, 마침내 그의 추종자들 가운데 그와 함께 안으로 들어온 자가 거의 또는 전혀 없다는 것이 알려지자, 도망자들은 다시 결집하여 사방에서 그를 에워쌌다. 이렇게 그가 겉보기에 막다른 궁지에 몰린 듯했을 때, 그는 그를 파멸로 위협하던 바로 그 상황 덕에 구원받았다. 사방에서 그에게 좁혀들던 안젤리카의 병사들이 자신들의 방어 진지를 버린 것이며, 한편 그 자신의 포위군은 성벽이 무너져 있던 한쪽으로 도시 안으로 들어온 것이다.\n\n이런 식으로 아그리칸은 구출되었고, 도시는 함락되었으며, 주민들은 칼날에 죽임을 당했다. 그러나 안젤리카는, 그녀의 수호자였던 몇몇 기사들과 함께, 그 가운데에는 사크리판트도 있었는데, 바위 위에 세워진 성채로 몸을 피했다.\n\n그 요새는 난공불락이었으나, 식량이 변변치 못했고 다른 필수품도 빈약하게 갖춰져 있었다. 이런 상황에서 안젤리카는 성채 안에 함께 봉쇄된 이들에게 자신이 도움을 구하러 나서겠다는 뜻을 알리고, 속히 돌아오겠다는 약속을 맹세한 뒤, 마법의 반지를 손가락에 끼고 길을 나섰다. 자신의 작은 말에 올라탄 처녀는 적의 전열을 지나, 동틀 무렵에는 그들의 진영에서 여러 마일 벗어나 있었다.\n\n마침 그녀의 길은 그 운명적인 망각의 다리 가까이로 그녀를 이끌었는데, 그곳에 다가가다가 그녀는 비통하게 울고 있는 한 처녀를 만났다. 그녀는 플로르델리스였으니, 우리가 이야기한 대로 그녀의 연인 플로리스마르트가 오를란도를 비롯한 많은 이들과 같은 운명을 맞아 잔의 마녀에게 희생되었던 것이다. 그녀는 안젤리카에게 자신이 겪은 일을 들려주며, 자신의 주군과 그의 동료들을 구해 낼 수 있도록 힘닿는 대로 도와 달라고 간청했다. 이에 따라 안젤리카는 기회를 엿보다가 반지의 도움을 받아, 새로운 희생자를 들이려고 문이 열렸을 때 들키지 않고 성안으로 미끄러져 들어갔다. 이곳에서 그녀는 자신의 부적을 한 번 갖다 대는 것으로 오를란도와 나머지 이들의 마법을 신속히 풀어 주었다. 그러나 플로리스마르트는 그곳에 없었다. 그는 더 강력한 마녀인 팔레리나에게 넘겨져, 여전히 갇혀 있었던 것이다. 안젤리카는 구출된 포로들에게 자신의 왕국을 되찾는 일을 도와 달라고 간청했고, 모두 함께 알브라카를 향해 떠났다.\n\n오를란도가 그의 동료들, 모두 합쳐 아홉이며 프랑스의 가장 용감한 기사들 축에 드는 이들과 함께 도착하자, 전쟁의 운세는 단번에 뒤바뀌었다. 그 위대한 팔라딘이 가는 곳마다 군기와 깃발이 그의 앞에 쓰러졌다. 아그리칸은 헛되이 자신의 군대를 다시 결집시키려 애썼다. 오를란도는 끊임없이 그의 정면에 버티고 서서, 그가 다른 누구에게도 신경 쓸 수 없게 만들었다. 마침내 타르타리아 왕은 한 가지 계략을 떠올렸다. 그는 말머리를 돌려, 절망에 빠져 달아나는 척했다. 오를란도는 그가 바라던 대로 그를 뒤쫓아 내달렸고, 아그리칸은 한 숲속의 푸른 곳에 이르기까지 달아났는데, 그곳에는 샘이 하나 있었다.\n\n그곳은 아름다웠고, 타르타리아 왕은 그 샘에서 기운을 차리려 말에서 내렸으나, 투구를 벗거나 갑옷의 어느 부분도 풀어 두지는 않았다. 오를란도가 곧 그의 뒤에 다다라 소리쳤다. \"그토록 대담하면서도 도망자라니! 어찌 단 한 사람의 팔에서 달아나 벗어날 수 있으리라 여겼는가?\"\n\n타르타리아 왕은 적을 보는 순간 안장에 뛰어올랐고, 팔라딘이 말을 마치자 부드러운 목소리로 말했다. \"의심할 바 없이 그대는 내가 여태껏 마주친 가장 빼어난 기사요. 만일 그대가 내 군대를 다시 결집하는 것을 가로막지 않겠다면, 나는 기꺼이 그대 자신을 위해 그대를 건드리지 않고 떠나고 싶소. 내가 달아나는 척한 것은 그대를 싸움터 밖으로 끌어내기 위함이었소. 그대가 한사코 싸우겠다면 나도 싸워 그대를 죽일 수밖에 없겠으나, 나는 하늘의 해를 두고 맹세컨대 차라리 그러고 싶지 않소. 그대의 죽음은 내게 무척 애석한 일이 될 것이오.\"\n\n오를란도 백작은 그토록 큰 무용에 연민을 느껴 이렇게 말했다. \"그대가 더 고결한 모습을 보일수록, 그대가 참된 믿음을 알지 못한 채 죽어 저세상에서 길을 잃으리라는 생각에 더욱 마음이 아프오. 그대에게 권하노니, 몸과 영혼을 한꺼번에 구하시오. 세례를 받고, 평안히 그대의 길을 가시오.\"\n\n아그리칸이 대답했다. \"그대가 팔라딘 오를란도가 아닌가 의심스럽소. 만일 그렇다면, 나는 낙원의 왕이 된다 해도 그대와 싸울 이 기회를 놓치고 싶지 않소. 저세상의 일에 관해서는 내게 더 말하지 마시오. 그대가 아무리 설교해도 헛일일 테니. 각자 제 몫이 있는 법, 칼이 심판이 되게 합시다.\"\n\n사라센은 칼을 뽑아 들고 대담하게 오를란도에게 다가섰고, 어찌나 끈질기고 어찌나 긴 싸움이 시작되었던지, 두 전사 저마다가 무용의 기적이었던 까닭에, 이야기에 따르면 그 싸움이 정오부터 밤까지 이어졌다고 한다. 그때 오를란도는 별들이 돋아나는 것을 보고 먼저 휴식을 제안했다.\n\n\"이제 어찌하면 좋겠소,\" 하고 그가 말했다, \"햇빛이 우리를 떠났으니 말이오?\"\n\n아그리칸은 선뜻 대답했다. \"이 풀밭에서 쉬다가, 동틀 녘에 싸움을 다시 시작합시다.\"\n\n이에 따라 휴식을 취했다. 저마다 자기 말을 매어 두고, 서로에게서 멀지 않은 곳에 풀밭에 몸을 뉘었으니, 마치 두 사람이 벗이라도 되는 양, 오를란도는 샘 곁에, 아그리칸은 소나무 아래에 누웠다. 맑고 아름다운 밤이었고, 잠을 청하기에 앞서 그들이 함께 이야기를 나누던 중, 기독교 세계의 투사가 하늘을 올려다보며 말했다. \"저것은 훌륭한 솜씨의 작품이오, 저 별들의 장관 말이오. 하느님께서 저 모든 것을 만드셨으니, 저 은빛 달도, 저 황금빛 별들도, 한낮의 빛도, 해도, 모두가 인류를 위함이라오.\"\n\n\"그대가 믿음의 문제를 이야기하고 싶어 하는 것을 알겠소,\" 하고 타르타리아 왕이 말했다. \"이제 그대에게 대뜸 말해 두는 편이 좋겠소만, 나는 그런 문제에 아무런 소양도 없고, 그 어떤 종류의 학식도 없소. 나는 어릴 적에 무엇 하나 배울 수가 없었소. 어찌나 그것이 싫었던지, 나를 가르치라고 맡겨진 자의 머리를 깨뜨려 버렸고, 그 일이 다른 이들에게도 어찌나 큰 효과를 미쳤던지, 그 뒤로는 아무도 감히 내게 책 한 권 보여 줄 엄두를 내지 못했소. 내 어린 시절은\n\n그러므로 시간은 마땅히 그래야 하듯 말타기와 사냥, 그리고 싸움을 익히는 데 보냈다. 신사가 온종일 책에 코를 박고 있은들 무슨 소용이 있겠는가? 기사에게는 무용을, 성직자에게는 설교를, 이것이 내 신조다.\"\n\n\"인정하지요.\" 오를란도가 대답했다. \"신사에게 무기가 첫째가는 고려 사항이라는 것은. 하지만 지식이 신사 자신을 욕되게 한다는 것은 결코 인정할 수 없습니다. 오히려 지식이란, 우리 앞에 펼쳐진 초원에 꽃이 그러하듯, 그가 이룬 다른 모든 성취를 더욱 아름답게 꾸며 주는 것입니다. 그리고 자신을 만든 분에 대한 앎으로 말하자면, 그것이 없는 사람은 나무토막이나 돌멩이, 혹은 짐승보다 나을 것이 없습니다. 또한 학문이 없이는 그 묵상의 깊이와 신성함을 제대로 느끼는 경지에 이를 수도 없는 것입니다.\"\n\n\"배웠든 안 배웠든,\" 아그리칸이 말했다. \"당신이 내게 불리한 주제로 말을 시키려 애쓰는 것보다는 더 교양 있게 처신할 수도 있을 텐데. 잠을 자고 싶다면 잘 자라고 인사하겠소. 하지만 이야기를 나누고 싶다면 싸움이나 아름다운 여인들에 관해 이야기하기를 권하오. 그리고 그건 그렇고, 부디 말해 주시오, 당신은 세상에 그토록 떠들썩한 이름을 떨치는 그 오를란도가 아니오? 그래, 도대체 무엇이 당신을 이런 곳까지 데려왔단 말이오? 한 번이라도 사랑에 빠진 적이 있소? 틀림없이 있었으리라 생각하오. 기사이면서 사랑에 빠진 적이 없다는 것은, 가슴속에 심장이 없는 사람과 같을 테니까.\"\n\n백작이 대답했다. \"오를란도, 그게 바로 나요. 그리고 나는 사랑에 빠져 있소. 사랑이 나로 하여금 모든 것을 버리게 했고, 이 머나먼 지방으로 데려왔소. 한마디로 다 말하자면, 내 마음은 갈라프론 왕의 딸의 손안에 있소. 당신은 그의 성과 영토를 차지하려고 불과 칼을 들고 그를 치러 왔지요. 그리고 나는 그를 돕기 위해 왔으니, 오직 그의 딸을 기쁘게 하고 그 아름다운 손을 얻으려는 것 말고는 세상의 어떤 목적도 없소. 나는 다른 무엇도 마음에 두지 않소.\"\n\n그런데 타타르의 왕 아그리칸이 적수가 이렇게 말하는 것을 듣고, 그가 정말로 오를란도이며 안젤리카를 사랑하고 있다는 것을 알게 되자, 그의 얼굴은 슬픔과 질투로 빛을 잃었다. 다만 어둠 때문에 그것이 보이지 않았을 뿐이다. 그의 심장은 너무도 격렬하게 뛰기 시작해서 그는 마치 죽을 것만 같았다. \"좋소.\" 그가 오를란도에게 말했다. \"우리는 날이 밝으면 싸우게 될 것이고, 둘 중 하나는 이 땅 위에 죽어 누워 있게 될 것이오. 내 당신에게 제안을 하나 하겠소. 아니, 간청이라 해야겠소. 같은 여인에 대한 내 사랑이 너무도 지나치니, 그녀를 내게 양보해 주기를 비오. 그러면 내 당신에게 고마움을 빚지고, 포위를 풀어 전쟁을 끝내겠소. 누군가가 그녀를 사랑하는데 내가 살아서 그것을 보아야 한다는 것을 나는 견딜 수가 없소. 그러니 어찌하여 우리 둘 중 누구라도 죽어야 한단 말이오? 그녀를 포기하시오. 아무도 그것을 알지 못할 것이오.\"\n\n\"나는 여태껏,\" 오를란도가 대답했다. \"내가 지키지 않을 약속은 한 번도 한 적이 없소. 그럼에도 당신에게 고백하건대, 만일 내가 그런 약속을 하고 심지어 그것을 지키겠노라 맹세한다 해도, 나는 지키지 못할 것이오. 당신은 차라리 내 몸에서 사지를 뜯어내고, 내 머리에서 두 눈을 뽑아 달라고 청하는 편이 나을 것이오. 나는 안젤리카를 사랑하기를 그만두느니 차라리 숨 그 자체 없이 사는 편이 낫소.\"\n\n아그리칸은 그가 말을 끝내도록 참아 줄 인내심조차 거의 없어, 한밤중이었음에도 미친 듯이 말 위로 뛰어올랐다. \"그녀를 포기하라.\" 그가 말했다. \"아니면 죽어라!\"\n\n오를란도는 그 이교도가 몸을 일으키는 것을 보고, 그가 사나움에 더해 배신까지 저지르지 않으리라고 확신할 수 없었기에, 그 못지않게 재빨리 말에 올라 결투에 임했다. \"결코,\" 그가 외쳤다. \"원한다 해도 나는 그녀를 포기할 수 없었을 것이오. 그리고 이제는 할 수 있다 해도 하지 않겠소. 당신은 이런 수단이 아닌 다른 방법으로 그녀를 찾아야 할 것이오.\"\n\n한밤중, 푸른 풀밭 위에서 그들의 말이 사납게 부딪쳤다. 달빛 아래 그들이 주고받는 타격은 잔혹하고 무시무시했다. 아그리칸은 분노에 차서 싸웠고, 오를란도는 더 냉정했다. 그리고 이제 싸움이 다섯 시간 넘게 이어져 날이 밝기 시작했을 때, 타타르의 왕은 이토록 큰 곤란을 겪게 된 것에 격분하여 적에게 상상을 초월할 만큼 날카롭고 격렬한 일격을 가했다. 그것은 마치 나무로 만들어진 양 방패를 두 동강 냈고, 오를란도는 운명에 의해 보호받았기에 피를 흘리지는 않았지만, 그 일격은 마치 그의 몸의 모든 관절을 어긋나게 한 듯 그를 뒤흔들고 멍들게 했다.\n\n그러나 그것은 오직 그의 몸뿐, 그의 영혼은 한 점도 흔들리지 않았다. 그 무사가 답례로 가한 일격이 어찌나 끔찍했던지, 방패뿐 아니라 아그리칸의 몸에 걸친 갑옷 한 조각 한 조각이 산산이 부서졌고, 갈비뼈 세 대가 동강 났다.\n\n타타르인은 사자처럼 울부짖으며 전보다 더 큰 격렬함으로 칼을 치켜들어, 그 무사가 여태껏 필멸의 인간에게서 받아 본 적 없는 일격을 그의 투구에 가했다. 한순간 그는 정신을 잃었다. 시야가 흐려지고, 귀가 울렸으며, 놀란 말은 달아나려고 몸을 돌렸다. 그가 안장에서 떨어지려는 찰나, 바로 그 떨어지는 동작이 그의 머리를 위로 젖혀 놓았고, 그리하여 그는 정신을 되찾았다.\n\n\"이 무슨 수치인가!\" 그는 생각했다. \"이래서야 어찌 다시 안젤리카를 마주 볼 낯이 있겠는가! 나는 이 자와 몇 시간이고 싸워 왔는데, 그는 하나뿐인 적이거늘, 그러고도 내가 오를란도라 자처하다니! 이 싸움이 조금이라도 더 길어진다면, 나는 수도원에 틀어박혀 두 번 다시 칼을 보지 않으리라.\"\n\n오를란도는 입술을 굳게 다물고 이를 갈며 중얼거렸다. 그의 코와 입에서는 숨 대신 불길이 뿜어 나온다고 여길 만했다. 그는 두 손으로 자신의 검 두린다나를 치켜들어, 아그리칸의 어깨 위로 어찌나 무시무시하게 내리쳤던지, 그것은 흉갑을 뚫고 허리뼈까지 갈랐을 뿐 아니라, 뼈와 쇠로 만들어졌음에도 안장의 앞테를 으스러뜨리고 사람과 말을 함께 땅에 쓰러뜨렸다.\n아그리칸은 잿빛처럼 창백해졌고, 죽음이 자신에게 닥쳤음을 느꼈다. 그는 부드러운 목소리로 오를란도를 가까이 오라 부르더니, 할 수 있는 한 가까스로 말했다. \"나는 십자가에서 죽으신 그분을 믿소. 내 정신이 사라지기 전에, 부디 저 샘물로 내게 세례를 베풀어 주시오. 나는 악한 삶을 살았으나, 죽음에 이르러서까지 하느님께 거역할 필요는 없겠지요. 세상의 다른 모든 이를 구원하러 오신 그분이 나를 구원해 주시기를!\" 그러고는 그 위대한 왕이, 그토록 오만하고 사나웠던 그가 눈물을 흘렸다.\n\n오를란도는 자신의 얼굴에도 눈물을 흘리며 재빨리 말에서 내렸다. 그는 그 왕을 두 팔로 다정하게 안아 올려, 샘에 둘려 있던 대리석 가장자리에 데려가 눕혔다. 그러고는 그와 한마음으로 진심으로 울며 용서를 구했고, 샘물로 그에게 세례를 베풀었으며, 무릎을 꿇고 두 손을 모아 그를 위해 하느님께 기도했다.\n\n이윽고 그는 멈추어 그를 바라보았다. 그의 얼굴빛이 변하고 온몸이 차갑게 식은 것을 알아차리자, 그는 그를 그곳 샘의 대리석 가장자리에, 무장을 갖춘 그대로, 칼을 옆구리에 차고 왕관을 머리에 쓴 채로 남겨 두었다.\n\n————\n\n# ADVENTURES OF RINALDO AND ORLANDO\n\n리날도와 오를란도의 모험\n우리는 리날도가 괴물을 물리친 뒤 알타리파 성을 떠나 걸어서 길을 가던 대목에서 그를 두고 왔다. 그는 곧 울고 있는 한 처녀를 만났는데, 슬픔의 까닭을 묻자, 그녀는 사악한 마녀에게 오를란도와 그 밖의 여러 사람과 함께 포로로 잡힌 자신의 연인을 구출하기 위해 싸워 줄 사람을 찾고 있다고 말했다. 그 처녀는 플로리스마트의 연인 플로르델리스였고, 리날도는 용맹으로든 기지로든 그 모험을 완수하리라 믿으며 도움을 약속했다. 플로르델리스는 리날도에게 자신의 말을 타라고 고집했고, 그는 그녀가 뒤에 함께 타는 조건으로 그러기로 했다.\n\n그들이 숲을 지나 말을 달리는 동안 이상한 소리가 들려왔고, 리날도는 처녀를 안심시키며 그 소리가 나는 쪽을 향해 나아갔다. 그는 곧 둥근 천장 같은 동굴 아래 거대한 곤봉을 손에 든 채 서 있는 거인을 보았는데, 그 모습은 가장 대담한 정신마저 두려움에 떨게 할 만했다. 동굴 옆에는 그리핀 한 마리가 사슬에 묶여 있었는데, 그것은 거인과 함께 그곳에 배치되어 한 마리 경이로운 말을 지키고 있었다. 그 말은 한때 아르갈리아의 것이었다. 이 말은 마법의 산물로, 활력과 속도와 자태에서 견줄 데가 없었으며, 곡식이나 풀 같은 동료 말들의 먹이를 함께 먹기를 마다하고 오직 공기만을 먹고 살았다. 그 이름은 라비칸이었다.\n\n이 경이로운 말은 주인 아르갈리아가 페라우에게 죽임을 당한 뒤, 자신이 자유로워진 것을 알고 고향인 동굴로 돌아왔고, 그곳에서 거인과 그리핀의 보호 아래 길러지고 있었다. 리날도가 다가가자 거인이 곤봉으로 그를 공격했다. 리날도는 거인의 타격을 막아 내고 답례로 한 방을 가했는데, 그의 가죽이 가장 질긴 것이 아니었더라면 그 일격으로 싸움은 끝났을 것이다. 그러나 거인은 부상을 입고도 달아나면서 그리핀을 풀어 놓았다. 이 괴물 새는 공중으로 높이 솟구쳤다가 거기서 리날도에게 덮쳐 내렸고, 그는 기회를 노려 그것에게 치명적인 상처를 입혔다. 그러나 그것은 또 한 번 날아오를 힘이 남아 있어 거듭 공격을 되풀이했고, 리날도는 할 수 있는 한 그것을 받아넘겼으며, 그동안 처녀는 옆에서 떨며 그 싸움을 지켜보았다.\n\n밤이 다가오면서 더욱 무시무시해진 싸움이 계속되자, 리날도는 그것을 끝내기 위해 필사적인 방책을 결심했다. 그는 상처로 인해 기절한 척 쓰러졌고, 그리핀이 바짝 다가들자 그것에게 일격을 가하여 날개 하나를 잘라 냈다. 그 짐승은 비록 쓰러지면서도 발톱으로 그를 단단히 움켜쥐어 강철판과 사슬갑옷을 뚫고 파고들었으나, 리날도는 극도의 필사적인 심정으로 칼을 휘둘러 마침내 그것을 파멸시켰다.\n\n이윽고 리날도는 동굴 안으로 들어가 거기서 마구를 모두 갖춘 그 경이로운 말을 발견했다. 그 말은 이마에 흰 별 무늬가 하나 있고 뒷발 하나가 흰 것을 제외하면 새까만 빛이었다. 속도로 말하면 견줄 데가 없었으나, 힘에서는 바야르에 미치지 못했다. 리날도는 라비칸에 올라 동굴 밖으로 나왔다.\n\n길을 가던 그는 아그리칸의 군대에서 도망쳐 나온 자를 만났는데, 그자가 안젤리카 편에서 싸우는 한 용사의 무용에 관해 어찌나 그럴듯하게 이야기하던지, 리날도는 그가 오를란도임에 틀림없다고 확신하게 되었다. 다만 그가 어떻게 포로 신세에서 풀려날 수 있었는지는 짐작할 수 없어 막막했다. 그는 호기심을 풀기 위해 그 싸움터로 가 보기로 결심했고, 플로르델리스도 오를란도와 함께 플로리스마트를 찾을 수 있으리라는 희망에 그와 동행하기로 했다.\n\n이런 일들이 벌어지는 동안, 아그리칸의 죽음으로 인해 타타르군은 온통 패주와 경악에 휩싸여 있었다. 갈라프론 왕이 바로 이때에 자신의 수도 알브라카를 구원하기 위한 군대를 이끌고 당도하여, 적진을 공격해 모든 것을 휩쓸었다. 리날도는 이제 싸움터에 다다라 무심한 구경꾼으로 지켜보고 있었는데, 그때 갈라프론에게 발각되었다. 왕은 라비칸이라는 말을 즉시 알아보았으니, 그것은 그가 불길한 사명을 띠고 아르갈리아를 파리로 보내면서 그에게 주었던 말이었다. 말의 기수가 아르갈리아의 살해자라는 생각에 사로잡힌 갈라프론은 리날도에게 말을 달려 온 힘을 다해 내리쳤다. 리날도도 그 일격에 복수하기를 더디 하지 않았으니, 만일 그의 부하들이 즉시 그를 둘러싸 두 사람을 떼어 놓지 않았더라면 왕은 위태로웠을 것이다.\n\n이리하여 리날도는 거의 자신의 선택과 무관하게 안젤리카의 적들 편에 서게 되었으나, 그것은 그에게 아무런 걱정거리가 되지 않았다. 미움의 샘에서 마신 물이 그의 마음을 그녀에게서 그토록 완전히 굳혀 놓았기 때문이다.\n\n여러 날이 잇따라 싸움이 계속되었으나 별다른 중요한 결과는 없었으니, 리날도는 안젤리카 편의 가장 용맹한 기사들과 맞붙어 그들을 차례로 물리쳤다. 마침내 그는 오를란도와 마주쳤고, 두 기사는 각자 택한 명분을 두고 서로를 호되게 비난하며 격렬한 싸움에 돌입했다. 오를란도는 리날도의 말 바야르에 타고 있었는데, 그 말은 우연히 아그리칸의 손에 들어갔다가 오를란도가 승리의 전리품으로 그에게서 빼앗은 것이었다. 바야르는 자신의 주인을 상대로 싸우려 하지 않았고, 오를란도가 그 대결에서 점차 불리해지고 있을 때, 갑자기 리날도는 그에 대한 사랑으로 그의 편에 서서 무장한 아스톨포가 수많은 적에게 호되게 시달리는 것을 보고, 오를란도를 내버려 두고 친구를 지키러 달려갔다. 밤이 되어 싸움은 다시 시작되지 못했으나, 다음 만남을 위한 결투 신청이 오가고 받아들여졌다.\n\n그러나 마음속으로 리날도를 그리며 한숨짓던 안젤리카는 그가 또다시 그토록 무시무시한 모험에 노출되기를 원치 않았다. 그녀는 오를란도에게 청을 하나 들어 달라고 부탁하며, 그가 자신의 분부를 따른다면 그의 것이 되겠노라 약속했다. 그의 승낙을 받자 그녀는 지체 없이 출발하여 마녀 팔레리나의 정원을 파괴하라고 명했으니, 그곳에는 수많은 용맹한 기사가 함정에 빠져 갇혀 있었다.\n\n오를란도는 자신의 말 브릴리아도로를 타고 떠났으며, 전날 보인 나쁜 행실 때문에 바야르는 면목을 잃은 채 남겨 두었다. 안젤리카는 리날도의 환심을 사려고 바야르를 그에게 보냈으나, 리날도는 그녀가 전에 베푼 모든 친절에 그러했듯 이 일에도 마음이 움직이지 않았다.\n\n리날도는 오를란도가 떠났다는 것을 알자, 플로리스마트의 연인의 간청에 응하여 자신의 약속을 이행하고 마녀의 힘에서 그녀의 연인을 구출할 채비를 했다. 이리하여 리날도와 오를란도 둘 다 같은 모험에 매이게 되었으나, 서로는 그것을 알지 못했다.\n\n팔레리나의 성은 강으로 보호되어 있었고, 그 강 위에는 다리가 놓여 있었는데, 한 흉포한 자가 그 다리를 지키며 오는 자마다 모두 싸움을 걸었다. 그의 힘이 어찌나 셌던지 그는 여태껏 모든 대결에서 이겨 왔으니, 여러 기사에게서 빼앗아 강가에 전리품으로 쌓아 놓은 무기들이 그 증거였다. 리날도는 그를 공격했으나 다른 이들과 마찬가지로 성과가 좋지 않았다. 다리지기가 쇠몽둥이로 그에게 어찌나 격렬한 일격을 가했던지 그는 땅에 쓰러지고 말았다. 그러나 그 악당이 그의 갑옷을 벗기려고 다가오자 리날도가 그를 붙잡았고, 다리지기는 빠져나오지 못한 채 리날도와 함께 호수로 뛰어들었으며, 두 사람은 모두 자취를 감추었다.\n\n한편 오를란도는 안젤리카에게 한 약속을 이행하기 위해 같은 모험을 찾아 길을 갔다. 숲을 지나가다 그는 빈틈없이 무장하고 말에 탄 한 기사가, 나무에 묶인 채 비통하게 우는 한 여인을 지키고 있는 것을 보았다. 오를란도가 그녀를 구하러 서둘러 갔으나, 그 기사는 그녀가 자신의 사악함으로 그런 운명을 자초한 것이니 끼어들지 말라고 권고했다. 그 증거로 그는 그녀에게 몇 가지 죄목을 들었다. 여인은 그것을 모두 부인했고, 오를란도는 그녀를 믿고 기사에게 도전하여 그를 쓰러뜨리고는, 여인을 풀어 주어 자신의 말 엉덩이에 태우고 떠났다.\n\n그들이 말을 달리는 동안 또 다른 처녀가 흰 조랑말을 타고 다가와, 오를란도에게 임박한 위험을 경고하며 그가 마녀의 정원 가까이에 있음을 알려 주었다. 오를란도는 그 소식에 기뻐하며 어떻게 하면 안으로 들어갈 수 있는지 알려 달라고 청했다. 그녀는 그 정원은 오직 해 뜰 무렵에만 들어갈 수 있다고 대답하며, 들어갈 수 있도록 일러 주었다. 그녀는 또한 그에게 책을 한 권 주었는데, 거기에는 정원과 그 안에 있는 모든 것이, 그리고 그 거짓 마녀의 궁전이 그려져 있었으니, 마녀는 자신이 매달려 있는 마법의 작업을 수행하기 위해 그곳에 칩거해 있었다. 그것은 마법에 걸린 물질조차 베어 낼 수 있는 검을 만드는 일이었다. 그 작업의 목적은, 처녀가 그에게 말하기를, 서방의 한 기사를 파멸시키려는 것이었으니, 그 기사의 이름은 오를란도로, 그가 자신의 정원을 무너뜨리러 오리라는 것을 마녀가 운명의 책에서 읽었던 것이다. 이렇게 그에게 일러 준 뒤 처녀는 떠났다.\n\n오를란도는 자신의 거사를 다음 날 아침까지 미룰 수밖에 없음을 알고, 이제 자리에 누워 곧 잠이 들었다. 그가 잠든 것을 보자, 그가 구해 주었던 비열한 여인은, 정부와 다시 만나기 위해 달아날 작정으로, 브릴리아도로에 올라 두린다나를 가지고 말을 달려 가 버렸다.\n\n오를란도가 깨어났을 때, 짐작할 수 있듯 도둑맞은 것을 알고 그의 분노는 컸다. 그러나 훌륭하고 충실한 기사답게 그는 자신의 거사에서 마음을 돌리지 않았다. 그는 칼을 대신할 거대한 느릅나무 가지 하나를 꺾어 냈고, 해가 떠오르자 정원 문으로 길을 잡았는데, 그곳에서는 용 한 마리가 망을 보고 있었다. 그는 거듭된 타격으로 그것을 죽이고 정원으로 들어갔으며, 정원 문은 그의 뒤에서 닫혀 후퇴를 가로막았다. 주위를 둘러보니 아름다운 샘 하나가 보였는데, 그 물이 넘쳐흘러 강을 이루었고, 샘 한가운데에는 형상 하나가 있어 그 이마에는 이렇게 새겨져 있었다.\n\n\"제비꽃과 장미에 물을 대는 이 시냇물은, 여기서부터 마법의 궁전으로 흘러간다.\"\n\n이 흐르는 시냇물의 둑을 따라가며, 매혹적인 정원의 즐거움에 빠져, 오를란도는 궁전에 다다랐다. 안으로 들어가니 흰옷을 입고 머리에 금관을 쓴 여주인이 마법의 검 표면에 자신의 모습을 비추어 보고 있었다. 오를란도는 그녀가 달아나기 전에 기습하여 그 무기를 빼앗고, 뒤로 흩날리는 그녀의 긴 머리채를 단단히 움켜쥔 채, 만일 포로들을 내놓고 자신이 빠져나갈 방도를 마련해 주지 않으면 당장 죽이겠다고 위협했다. 그러나 그녀는 굳은 결심으로 아무런 대답도 하지 않았고, 위협으로도 간청으로도 그녀를 움직일 수 없었던 오를란도는, 어쩔 수 없이 그녀를 너도밤나무에 묶어 두고 할 수 있는 한 자신의 탐색을 계속해야 했다.\n\n이윽고 그는 책을 떠올리고 그것을 참고하여, 남쪽으로 출구가 하나 있음을 알게 되었다. 그러나 거기에 이르려면 호수 하나를 건너야 했는데, 그 호수에는 세이렌이 살고 있어, 그 노래가 어찌나 황홀한지 듣는 자라면 누구든 도저히 거역할 수 없을 정도였다. 그러나 그의 책은 이 위험으로부터 자신을 지키는 법을 일러 주었다. 그 지시에 따라 그는 길을 가면서 사방에 돋아난 꽃을 잔뜩 따 모아 투구와 귀를 그것으로 채웠다. 그러고는 새가 노래하는 소리가 들리는지 귀를 기울였다. 새의 벌어진 부리와 부푼 목과 곤두선 깃털은 보였으나 한 가락의 소리도 들을 수 없자, 그는 자신의 방비에 만족하며 호수를 향해 나아갔다. 호수는 작았으나 깊었고, 어찌나 맑고 고요한지 눈으로 바닥까지 꿰뚫어 볼 수 있었다.\n\n그가 둑에 다다르자마자 물이 부글거리는 것이 보이더니, 세이렌이 못의 한가운데서 솟아올라 어찌나 달콤하게 노래했던지 새와 짐승이 떼 지어 물가로 몰려와 귀를 기울였다. 오를란도는 이 가운데 아무것도 듣지 못했으나, 그 마력에 굴복하는 척하며 둑 위에 쓰러졌다. 세이렌은 그를 파멸시킬 작정으로 물에서 나왔다. 오를란도는 그녀의 머리채를 움켜쥐었고, 그녀가 더욱 큰 소리로 노래하는 동안(노래가 그녀의 유일한 방어 수단이었다) 그 목을 잘랐다. 그러고는 책의 지시를 따라 그녀의 피로 자신의 온몸을 물들였다.\n\n이 부적으로 보호받으며 그는 마녀와 그 정원을 지키기 위해 배치된 모든 괴물을 차례로 마주쳐 물리쳤고, 마침내 자신이 마녀를 사로잡았던 그 자리로 다시 돌아왔으니, 마녀는 여전히 너도밤나무에 묶인 채로 있었다. 그러나 광경은 바뀌어 있었다. 정원은 사라졌고, 전에는 그토록 오만했던 팔레리나가 이제는 자비를 구하며, 많은 목숨이 자신의 목숨을 보존하는 데 달려 있다고 그를 안심시켰다. 오를란도는 그녀가 자기 포로들을 풀어 주겠다고 서약하는 조건으로 그녀의 목숨을 약속했다.\n\n그러나 이것은 쉬운 일이 아니었다. 포로들은 그녀의 손안에 있는 것이 아니라, 훨씬 더 강력한 마녀인 모르가나, 곧 호수의 귀부인의 손안에 있었으니, 그녀에게 맞선다는 생각만으로도 팔레리나는 두려움에 창백해졌다. 그녀는 그에게 그 거사의 위험을 일러 주며 모르가나의 거처로 그를 이끌었다. 거기에 다가가려면 그는 이미 그토록 많은 기사를 물리쳐 사로잡은, 그리고 마지막으로 리날도까지 사로잡은 그 무례한 다리지기와 맞서야 했다. 그는 아리다노라는 이름의 가장 흉포한 성품의 상놈이었다. 모르가나는 그에게 뚫리지 않는 갑옷을 마련해 주었고, 그를 그런 식으로 부여하여, 그의 힘이 언제나 맞붙은 상대의 힘에 비례하여 커지도록 했다. 그의 인내력이 어찌나 대단했던지 물속에서도 자유로이 숨을 쉴 수 있었으므로, 여태껏 그 대결에서 빠져나온 자가 아무도 없었다. 그리하여 그는 한 기사와 맞붙어 그와 함께 호수 바닥으로 가라앉았다가, 적의 무기를 의기양양하게 수면 위로 들고 돌아오곤 했다.\n\n팔레리나가 거듭 주의와 충고를 되풀이하는 동안, 오를란도는 그 악당이 차지한 다른 전리품들 사이에 리날도의 무기가 전리품 모양으로 세워져 있는 것을 보았고, 지난날의 다툼을 잊고 친구의 원수를 갚기로 결심했다. 그 길목에 다다르자 상놈이 길을 막으려 했고, 필사적인 대결이 벌어졌는데, 그동안 팔레리나는 달아났다. 상놈은 무기를 쓰는 대결에서 자신이 당해 낼 수 없음을 알고 자신의 독특한 재주에 의지하여, 적수를 붙잡고 그와 함께 호수로 뛰어들었다. 바닥에 다다랐을 때 오를란도는 자신이 또 다른 세계에, 마른 풀밭 위에 있음을 알게 되었으니, 머리 위에는 호수가 있어 그것을 뚫고 우리 태양의 빛이 비쳐 들었고, 물은 사방에서 수정 벽처럼 서 있었다. 여기서 싸움이 다시 시작되었고, 오를란도는 자신의 마법 검에서 여태껏 누구도 가져 본 적 없는 이점을 누렸다. 그것은 팔레리나에 의해 단련되어 어떤 마법도 그것에 맞서 효력을 발휘할 수 없었다. 이렇게 무장하고, 자신의 뛰어난 기량과 민첩함으로 적수의 힘을 상쇄하니, 오래지 않아 그는 그를 들판에 죽여 눕혔다.\n\n이윽고 오를란도는 서둘러 지상의 공기 속으로 돌아가려 했고, 물을 지나가니 그 앞에서 물이 길을 열어 주었으며(마법 검의 힘이 그러했다), 그는 곧 물가를 되찾아, 하늘이 별로 뒤덮여 있듯 보석으로 빼곡히 뒤덮인 들판에 있는 자신을 발견했다. 오를란도는 사방에 흩뿌려진 그 찬란한 보석을 하나라도 주워 자신의 거사를 늦추려는 유혹에 빠지지 않고 그 들판을 가로질렀다. 그는 이어서 나무가 심긴 꽃핀 풀밭으로 들어섰는데, 그곳은 과일과 꽃으로 뒤덮이고 상상할 수 있는 온갖 즐거움으로 가득했다.\n\n이 풀밭 한가운데에는 샘이 하나 있었고, 그 바로 곁에 모르가나가 잠들어 누워 있었다. 사랑스러운 용모의 귀부인으로, 흰색과 진홍색의 옷을 입고 있었으며, 이마에는 머리카락이 풍성했으나 뒤쪽으로는 머리카락이 거의 없었다.\n\n오를란도가 말없이 서서 그녀의 아름다움을 바라보고 있을 때, 한 목소리가 외치는 것이 들렸다. \"고운 성공을 바라거든, 저 요정의 앞머리를 붙잡아라.\" 그러나 그의 주의는 다른 대상에 사로잡혀 그 경고에 유념하지 않았다. 그는 갑자기 탑과 첨탑과 기둥의 행렬을, 발코니와 창문이 달린 궁전을, 나무가 늘어선 길게 뻗은 가로수길을, 한마디로 그가 여태껏 본 그 어떤 것도 능가하는 건축적 장려함의 광경을 보았다. 그가 말없이 놀라 바라보고 서 있는 동안, 그 광경은 서서히 녹아 사라져 버렸다.\n\n그가 놀라움에서 깨어났을 때 그는 다시 샘 쪽을 바라보았다. 요정은 깨어나 일어나서, 나뭇잎처럼 가벼이 샘 가장자리를 돌며 춤추고 있었으니, 그 발걸음을 이 노래에 맞추고 있었다.\n\n\"이 세상에서 부와 보물을 누리고자 하는 이, 명예와 기쁨과 영화와 가장 좋은 것을 바라는 이, 어서 내 이마에서 나부끼는 머리채를 붙잡으라, 그리하여 복을 받으라. 그러나 내미는 그 좋은 것을 멀리하지 말 것이며, 덧없는 축복을 붙잡기까지는 쉬지 말지니, 오늘 잃은 것은 내일 다시 구하여도 헛되며, 속아 넘어간 가련한 자는 슬픔 속에 남겨지리라.\"\n\n이렇게 노래를 부른 요정은 펄쩍 뛰어, 꽃핀 풀밭을 가로질러 높고 오를 수 없는 산 너머로 달아났다. 오를란도는 가시덤불과 바위를 헤치며 그녀를 뒤쫓았고, 그동안 하늘은 점차 흐려지더니, 마침내 그는 폭풍과 번개와 우박의 습격을 받았다.\n\n그가 이렇게 뒤쫓는 동안, 창백하고 야윈 한 여인이 채찍을 들고 동굴에서 나와, 그의 발걸음을 바짝 따라붙으며 힘찬 매질로 그를 후려쳤다. 그녀의 이름은 후회였고, 그녀는 신중함의 목소리에 따르기를 게을리하여, 할 수 있을 때에 요정 운명을 붙잡지 않은 자들을 벌하는 것이 자신의 소임이라고 그에게 말했다.\n\n이 징벌에 격분한 오를란도는 자신을 괴롭히는 자에게 몸을 돌렸으나, 차라리 바람을 친 것이나 다름없었다. 저항해 봐야 소용없음을 알고, 그는 요정 추격을 다시 시작하여 그녀에게 다가갔고, 흰색과 진홍색의 옷을 거듭 낚아채려 했으나 그것은 여전히 그의 손아귀를 빠져나갔다. 마침내 그녀가 한순간 고개를 돌리자, 그는 그 틈을 타 그녀의 앞머리를 붙잡았다. 순식간에 폭풍이 멎고 하늘이 맑아졌으며, 후회는 자신의 동굴로 물러갔다.\n\n이제 오를란도는 모르가나에게 그녀의 감옥 열쇠를 요구했고, 요정은 만족스러운 표정을 꾸미며 은으로 된 열쇠 하나를 내주면서, 그것을 쓸 때 조심하라고 일렀으니, 자물쇠를 부수면 자신과 모든 이를 피할 수 없는 파멸에 빠뜨리게 되리라는 것이었다. 이 주의는 백작에게 오랜 사색의 여지를 주었고, 그로 하여금 다음과 같은 점을 곰곰이 생각하게 했다.\n\n그 귀부인에게 끈질기게 매달리는 구혼자들 가운데 운명의 열쇠를 돌릴 줄 아는 이가 어찌 그리 적은가.\n\n오를란도는 여전히 요정의 앞머리를 단단히 붙잡은 채 감옥을 향해 나아갔고, 우려했던 화를 일으키지 않고 열쇠를 돌려 포로들을 풀어 주었다.\n\n이들 가운데에는 플로리스마트와 리날도, 그리고 프랑스의 가장 용맹한 기사들 여럿이 있었다. 모르가나는 사라졌고, 기사들은 오를란도의 인도 아래 그가 왔던 길을 되짚어갔다. 그들은 곧 보물의 들판에 다다랐다. 리날도는 이 막대한 부 가운데에 있는 자신을 발견하고, 몬탈반의 궁핍한 수비대를 떠올리며, 전리품의 일부를 차지하고 싶은 유혹을 이겨 낼 수 없었다. 특히 다이아몬드가 박힌 황금 사슬 하나는 그의 절제력으로는 너무 벅찼으니, 그는 오를란도의 만류에도 불구하고 그것을 집어 들고 가지고 가려 했다. 그런데 그가 문에 다가가자 거센 바람이 그를 붙잡아 뒤로 휘몰아쳤다. 이것이 두 번, 세 번 일어나자, 리날도는 마침내 친구들의 간청보다는 어쩔 수 없는 형편에 굴복하여 자신이 차지한 것을 내던졌다.\n\n그들은 곧 다리에 다다라 거침없이 건너편으로 건너갔고, 그곳에서 자신들의 무기로 장식된 전리품 더미를 발견했다. 여기서 기사들은 저마다 자기 것을 되찾았고, 무사들과 그 친구들을 제외한 모두는 각자의 뜻이나 의무가 이끄는 대로 흩어졌다. 구출된 기사들 가운데 하나인 덴마크인 두돈은 사촌들에게 자신이 샤를마뉴에게서 그들에게 보내는 사명을 수행하던 중 모르가나에게 사로잡혔노라고 알렸으니, 샤를마뉴는 그들에게 기독교 세계의 방어를 위해 돌아오라고 요청했던 것이다. 오를란도는 안젤리카에게 너무도 매혹되어 이 부름에 따를 수 없었고, 그를 떠나려 하지 않는 충실한 플로리스마트를 거느린 채 알브라카를 향해 돌아갔다. 리날도와 두돈, 이롤도와 프라실도, 그리고 다른 이들은 서쪽으로 길을 잡았다.\n\n————\n\n# THE INVASION OF FRANCE\n\n프랑스 침공\n\n아프리카의 왕 아그라만트는 자신의 봉신인 왕들을 소집하여 회의에서 숙의하게 했다. 그는 자신이 프랑스에게서 입은 손해를, 자신의 부친이 샤를마뉴와의 전투에서 쓰러졌음을, 그리고 자신이 어린 나이여서 여태껏 지난 패배의 오욕을 씻을 수 없었음을 그들에게 일깨웠다. 이제 그는 그들에게 프랑스로 전쟁을 가져갈 것을 제안했다. 그의 가장 현명한 고문인 소브리노는 그 일의 무모함을 들어 그 계획에 반대했다. 그러나 젊고 혈기왕성한 알제의 왕 로도몬트는 소브리노의 충고를 비열하고 비겁하다고 규탄하며, 자신은 그 거사를 위해 안달이 났노라고 선언했다. 가라만테스의 왕은 나이로 존경받고 예언의 지식으로 명성이 높았는데, 끼어들어 왕에게 이렇게 단언했다. 그런 시도는 반드시 실패할 것이니, 다만 왕이 먼저 한 젊은이를 자기편으로 끌어들일 수 있다면 모를 일이라고. 그 젊은이는 프랑스의 가장 강력한 기사들의 합당한 적수로 운명에 의해 점지된 자로, 트로이아의 헥토르의 직계 후손인 젊은 로제로였다. 이 왕자는 이제 카레나 산에 머무는 자였으니, 그곳에서 강력한 마법사인 그의 양부 아틀란테스가 그를 은둔시켜 두었던 것이다. 아틀란테스는 자신의 제자가 세상과 어울리도록 두면 자신에게서 잃게 되리라는 것을 자신의 술법으로 알아냈던 것이다. 아틀란테스의 마법을 깨뜨리고 로제로를 그 은둔지에서 끌어낼 단 하나의 수단이 있었다. 그것은 카타이의 공주 안젤리카가 지닌 반지로, 모든 마법에 맞서는 부적이었다. 만일 이 반지를 손에 넣을 수 있다면 만사가 잘될 것이요, 그것이 없다면 그 거사는 가망이 없었다.\n\n로도몬트는 늙은 예언자의 이 선언을 비웃었고, 만일 그 노왕이 세월의 무게에 짓눌려 자신의 예언을 재차 확언하는 바로 그 순간에 숨을 거두지 않았더라면, 그것은 아마 회의에서 별 무게도 갖지 못했을 것이다. 이 일이 회의에 어찌나 깊은 인상을 남겼던지, 로제로를 진영으로 끌어들이려는 노력을 기울일 때까지 전쟁을 미루기로 만장일치로 결의했다.\n\n이에 아그라만트 왕은 안젤리카의 반지를 손에 넣는 데 성공하는 자에게 한 왕국의 통치권을 상으로 주겠노라고 선포했다. 아프리카 전체에서 가장 교활한 도둑인 난쟁이 브루넬로가 그것을 손에 넣겠노라 나섰다.\n\n이 계획을 추진하기 위해 그는 안젤리카의 왕국으로 한껏 서둘러 가서, 포위군이 요새 앞에 진을 친 동안 알브라카의 성벽 아래에 다다랐다. 수비대의 주의가 아래에서 벌어지는 전투에 쏠려 있는 동안, 그는 성벽을 기어올라 아무도 모르게 공주에게 다가가, 그녀의 손가락에서 반지를 빼내어 들키지 않고 달아났다. 그는 바닷가로 서둘러 가, 막 출항하려는 배 한 척을 발견하고 올라타, 아프리카의 비세르타에 다다랐다. 여기서 그는 아틀란테스의 마법을 꺾고 로제로를 자신의 손안에 넣어 줄 그 부적을 애타게 기다리던 아그라만트를 만났다. 난쟁이는 왕 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 그에게 반지를 바쳤고, 그 사명의 성공에 기뻐한 아그라만트는 그 보답으로 그를 팅기타나의 왕으로 봉했다.\n\n이제 모두가 로제로를 찾아 나서기를 간절히 바랐다. 이에 따라 행렬이 떠났고, 때맞춰 카레나 산에 다다랐다.\n\n이 산기슭에는 비옥하고 숲이 우거진 평원이 있어 큰 강이 흐르며 적셔 주었고, 이 평원에서 산꼭대기에 있는 아름다운 정원이 바라다보였으니, 그곳에는 아틀란테스의 저택이 있었다. 그러나 전에는 보이지 않던 것을 드러내 주는 그 반지도, 이 낙원을 드러내 보이기는 했으되, 아그라만트나 그 부하들이 그 안으로 들어가게 해 주지는 못했다. 그 바위는 본래 어찌나 가파르고 매끄러운지, 브루넬로조차 그것을 기어오르려는 모든 시도에 실패했다. 그러나 그는 이 때문에 목적을 이루기를 단념하지는 않았으니, 아그라만트의 동의를 얻어, 모인 조신들과 기사들로 하여금 아래의 평원에서 마상 시합을 벌이게 했다. 이는 로제로를 그 요새에서 꾀어내려는 의도로 행한 것이었고, 그 책략은 성공을 거두었다.\n\n로제로는 시합에 참가했고, 아그라만트에게서 프론티노라는 훌륭한 말 한 마리와 화려한 검 한 자루를 선사받았다. 아그라만트에게서 그가 계획한 프랑스 침공에 관해 듣고, 그는 기꺼이 그 원정에 합류하기로 했다.\n\n한편 로도몬트는 아그라만트의 준비를 기다리기에는 너무 조급하여, 자신이 모을 수 있는 모든 병력을 거느리고 배에 올라, 프랑스 해안에 무사히 상륙하여 여러 차례 교전에서 기독교도들을 무찔렀다. 그러나 이에 앞서, 오를란도와 샤를마뉴의 다른 조카들의 적인 배신자 가노, 혹은 (때로 불리는 이름으로) 가넬론이, 에스파냐의 사라센 왕 마르실리우스와 반역의 내통을 맺고 그를 프랑스로 끌어들였다. 이에 고무된 마르실리우스는 군대를 이끌고 국경을 넘어 로도몬트와 합류했다. 두돈의 부름에 따른 리날도와 다른 기사들이 프랑스로 돌아가는 길에 나섰을 때 사정은 이러했다.\n\n그들이 헝가리의 부다에 다다랐을 때, 그 나라의 왕이 막 자신의 아들 오타키에로를 군대와 함께 샤를마뉴를 구원하러 보내려 하고 있음을 알게 되었다. 리날도가 당도한 것에 기뻐한 왕은 자신의 아들과 군대를 그의 지휘 아래 두었다. 때맞춰 군대는 프랑스 국경에 다다랐고, 롬바르디아의 왕 데시데리우스의 군대와 합쳐 프로방스로 쏟아져 내려갔다. 연합군이 이 화창한 지방을 행군한 지 며칠 되지 않아, 그들은 언덕 너머에서 북과 나팔이 요란하게 울리는 소리를 들었으니, 그것은 로도몬트가 이끄는 이교도들과 기독교군 사이의 충돌을 알리는 것이었다. 산 위에서 로도몬트의 무용을 목격한 리날도는, 자신의 군대를 친구들에게 맡기고 창을 겨눈 채 그를 향해 말을 달렸다. 그 기세는 거역할 수 없었고, 로도몬트는 말에서 떨어졌다. 그러나 리날도는 그 유리함을 이용하기를 꺼려, 언덕으로 다시 말을 달려 짐들 사이에 바야르를 안전히 둔 뒤, 걸어서 싸움을 끝내러 돌아왔다.\n\n이 사이에 전투는 전면전이 되었고, 헝가리군은 패주했으며, 돌아온 리날도는 오타키에로가 부상당하고 두돈이 포로로 잡힌 것을 알고 비통해했다. 싸움을 다시 시작하려고 로도몬트를 찾는 동안, 북과 나팔의 새로운 소리가 들려왔고, 샤를마뉴가 자신의 본대를 거느리고 전투 대형으로 진격해 오는 것이 보였다.\n\n이것을 본 로도몬트는 두돈의 말에 올라, 걸어 다니던 리날도를 내버려 두고 이 새로운 적을 맞으러 말을 달려 갔다.\n\n이때쯤 로제로를 거느린 아그라만트가 무사히 상륙하여 자신의 전 병력을 이끌고 로도몬트와 합류했다. 로제로는 자신을 떨칠 이 첫 기회를 열렬히 붙잡아, 가는 곳마다 공포를 퍼뜨리며, 프랑스의 가장 용맹한 기사들을 차례로 마주쳐 쓰러뜨렸다. 마침내 그는 리날도와 마주하게 되었으니, 리날도는 앞서 말했듯 로도몬트와의 싸움이 중단된 데다, 걸어 다니느라 그를 뒤쫓을 수 없어, 지난번의 적에게 돌아와 싸움을 끝내자고 외치고 있었다. 로제로 역시 걸어 다니고 있었는데, 그 기독교 기사가 그토록 싸움에 열심인 것을 보고, 자신이 그 지난번 적수를 대신하겠노라 자청했다. 리날도는 그 무어 왕자가 자신의 팔에 걸맞은 용사임을 한눈에 알아보고 기꺼이 그 도전을 받아들였다. 한동안 싸움은 굳세게 이어졌다. 그러나 이제 운명은 이교도 군대에 확연히 유리하게 기울었고, 샤를마뉴의 군대는 모든 곳에서 회복할 수 없는 혼란 속에 무너졌다. 두 결투자는 패주하는 자들과 추격하는 자들의 무리에 의해 갈라졌고, 리날도는 서둘러 자신의 말을 되찾으려 했다. 그러나 바야르는 혼란 통에 풀려나 있었고, 리날도는 그것을 따라 울창한 숲속으로 들어갔으니, 이리하여 그는 로제로와 완전히 갈라지게 되었다.\n\n로제로 역시 그 난전 속에서 자신의 말을 찾던 중, 두 전사가 사투를 벌이고 있는 곳에 이르렀다. 그들이 누구인지는 알지 못했으나, 하나는 이교도이고 다른 하나는 기독교도임은 분간할 수 있었다. 예의의 정신에 움직인 그는 그들에게 다가가 외쳤다. \"둘 중 그리스도를 섬기는 자는 멈추고 내가 할 말을 들으시오. 샤를의 군대가 패하여 달아나고 있으니, 그가 자신의 지휘관을 따르고자 한다면 지체할 겨를이 없소.\" 그 기독교 기사는 다름 아닌 브라다만테로, 무용에서 가장 뛰어난 기사들에 못지않은 여전사였는데, 그 소식에 아연실색하여 기꺼이 싸움을 결판내지 않은 채 두고 전장에서 물러나려 했다. 그러나 그녀의 적수인 로도몬트는 결코 동의하려 하지 않았다. 그의 무례함에 분개한 로제로는 그녀더러 떠나라고 고집하며, 자신이 로도몬트와의 다툼을 떠맡았다.\n\n양쪽 모두 완강하게 이어 가던 싸움은 브라다만테의 귀환으로 중단되었다. 그녀는 달아나는 자들을 따라잡을 수 없음을 알았고, 본래 자신에게 속한 싸움의 짐과 위험을 다른 이에게 맡기기를 꺼려, 싸움을 되찾으려고 돌아왔던 것이다. 그러나 그녀가 당도했을 때 자신의 용사는 적에게 일격을 가하여, 적으로 하여금 칼과 고삐를 둘 다 떨어뜨리게 만든 참이었다. 로제로는 적수의 무방비한 처지를 이용하기를 마다하여 자신의 말 위에 따로 떨어져 앉아 있었고, 그동안 로도몬트의 말은 정신이 멍하고 얼떨떨해진 기수를 태운 채 들판을 이리저리 내달렸다.\n\n브라다만테는 로제로에게 다가갔으니, 그러한 자제의 본보기를 보고 그의 무용에 한층 더 높은 평가를 품게 되었다. 그녀는 그에게 말을 건네며, 자신의 일에 끼어든 그를 적에게 노출된 채 두고 떠난 것을 사과하고, 자신의 군주에 대한 의무를 그 동기로 들어 변명했다. 그녀가 말하는 동안, 혼란에서 회복된 로도몬트가 그들에게 말을 달려 왔다. 그러나 그의 태도는 달라져 있었으니, 그는 \"이미 자신을 예의로써 정복한\" 자와 더 싸울 생각이 전혀 없다고 밝혔다. 이렇게 말하고는 적수를 떠나, 자신의 칼을 주워 들고 박차를 가해 시야에서 사라졌다.\n\n이제 브라다만테는 다시 전장에서 물러나고 싶어 했고, 로제로는 그녀의 성을 아직 알지 못한 채 그녀를 따라가겠노라 고집했다.\n\n길을 가던 그녀는 새로운 동행자의 이름과 신분을 물었고, 로제로는 자신의 나라와 가문을 그녀에게 알려 주었다. 그는 그녀에게 트로이아의 헥토르의 아들 아스티아낙스가 시칠리아의 메시나 왕국을 세웠다고 말했다. 그에게서 두 갈래가 갈라져 나와 두 명문 가문의 기원이 되었다. 한 갈래에서는 페핀과 샤를마뉴의 왕가가 나왔고, 다른 갈래에서는 이탈리아 레조의 가문이 나왔다. \"내가 나온 것은 그 레조의 가문이오.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"내 어머니는 전쟁의 우연으로 고향에서 쫓겨나, 내게 생명을 주다가 돌아가셨고, 나는 한 현명한 마법사의 보살핌을 받았으니, 그는 나를 사막과 사냥의 위험 속에서 무예의 위업을 익히도록 길렀소.\"\n\n이렇게 이야기를 마친 로제로는 동행자에게도 마찬가지로 예의로써 답해 주기를 청했고, 그녀는 거리낌 없이 자신이 클레르몽의 혈통이며, 그에게도 아마 명성이 알려졌을 리날도의 누이라고 대답했다. 로제로는 이 소식에 크게 마음이 움직여 그녀에게 투구를 벗어 달라고 청했고, 그녀의 얼굴이 드러나자 기쁨에 도취된 채로 있었다.\n\n이 응시에 빠져 있는 동안, 뜻밖의 위험이 그들을 덮쳤다. 퇴각하는 기독교도들을 가로막으려고 숲속에 배치되어 있던 한 무리가 매복에서 뛰쳐나와 두 사람에게 달려들었고, 투구를 벗고 있던 브라다만테는 머리에 부상을 입었다. 로제로는 이 공격에 격노했고, 브라다만테는 투구를 다시 쓰고 그와 함께 자신들의 적에게 신속한 복수를 가했다. 그들은 들판에서 적을 쓸어버렸으나, 추격 중에 서로 갈라지게 되었고, 추격을 그만둔 로제로는, 찾자마자 곧 잃어버린 그녀를 찾아 산과 골짜기를 헤매고 다녔다.\n\n이 탐색을 이어 가던 중 그는 두 기사와 마주쳤고, 그들과 합류하여 자신의 동행자를 찾는 일을 도와 달라고 청하며 그녀의 무기를 묘사했으나, 어떤 질투의 감정에서 그녀의 신분과 성별은 감추었다.\n\n그들이 합류한 것은 저녁이었고, 함께 밤새 말을 달려 아침이 막 밝아 오기 시작할 무렵, 두 낯선 자 가운데 하나가 로제로의 방패에 시선을 고정하더니, 무슨 권리로 트로이아의 문장을 달고 있느냐고 물었다. 로제로는 자신의 출신과 혈통을 밝힌 뒤, 이번에는 자신이 그 질문자에게 그가 달고 있는 헥토르의 문장에 대한 그의 권리를 따져 물었다. 낯선 자가 대답했다. \"내 이름은 만드리카르도, 타타르의 왕 아그리칸의 아들로, 오를란도가 비겁하게 죽인 그 왕이오. 비겁하게라고 말하는 것은, 정정당당한 싸움에서라면 그가 그러지 못했을 것이기 때문이오. 내가 프랑스로 온 것은 그를 찾아, 내 아버지의 원수를 갚고, 또 그에게서 두린다나를 빼앗기 위함이오. 그 유명한 검은 내 것이지 그의 것이 아니오.\" 기사들이 무슨 권리로 두린다나를 주장하느냐고 묻자, 만드리카르도는 이렇게 자신의 내력을 들려주었다.\n\n\"나는 아버지가 돌아가시기 전에는 거칠고 무모한 젊은이였소. 그 사건이 내 기력을 일깨워, 나를 복수를 찾아 나서게 했소. 오직 나 자신의 노력에만 성공을 빚지기로 작정하고, 나는 시종도 말도 무기도 없이 떠났소. 이렇게 홀로, 걸어서 여행하던 어느 날, 나는 샘 가까이에 친 천막을 하나 발견하고, 모험을 좇아 그 안으로 들어갔소. 거기서 나는 우아한 용모의 한 처녀를 만났는데, 그녀는 내 물음에 답하여, 그 샘은 한 요정의 작품으로, 그 요정의 성은 이웃 언덕 너머에 있으며, 거기서 그녀가 한 보물을 지키고 있는데, 많은 기사가 그것을 얻으려 했으나 헛되이, 그 시도에서 목숨이나 자유를 잃었다고 말했소. 그 보물은 트로이아의 왕자 헥토르의 갑옷이었으니, 헥토르는 아킬레우스가 비겁하게 죽인 자요. 부족한 것이라곤 그의 검 두린다나뿐이었는데, 이것은 펜테실레이아라는 여왕의 손에 들어갔다가, 그녀의 후손들을 거쳐 알몬테스에게 넘어갔고, 오를란도가 알몬테스를 죽이고 이리하여 그 검을 손에 넣게 된 것이오. 헥토르의 나머지 무기들은 아이네아스가 건져 가져갔고, 이 요정이 그에게서 봉사의 보답으로 그것을 받은 것이오. '만일 그것을 손에 넣을 용기가 있다면,' 처녀가 말했소. '내가 그대의 안내자가 되어 주겠소.'\"\n\n만드리카르도는 이어서 말하기를, 자신은 그 제안을 열렬히 받아들였고, 처녀에게서 말과 갑옷을 제공받아 자신의 거사에 나섰으며, 그 여인이 그와 동행했다고 했다.\n\n그들이 말을 달리는 동안 그녀는 그 탐색의 위험을 설명해 주었다. 그 갑옷은 한 용사가 지키고 있었으니, 그는 그 상을 노렸으나 실패한 수많은 모험가 가운데 하나로, 그들 모두는 요정에게 사로잡혀, 날마다 차례로 그 무기를 지키며 오는 자마다 모두 막아 내도록 강요받았다. 이렇게 이야기하며 그들은 성에 다다랐으니, 그것은 설화석고로 만들어져 금으로 덧입혀져 있었다. 그 앞 잔디밭에는 무장한 기사 하나가 말에 타고 앉아 있었는데, 그는 다름 아닌 세리카네의 왕 그라다소로, 프랑스로의 무모한 침입에 실패하고 고국으로 돌아가던 길에 그 요정의 힘에 빠져 그녀의 분부를 따르고 있던 자였다.\n만드리카르도는 그를 보자 면갑을 내리고 창을 겨누었다. 성의 용사도 그에 못지않게 채비를 갖추었고, 둘은 각자 적을 향해 박차를 가했다. 그들은 똑같은 힘으로 서로 맞부딪쳐 창을 산산이 부수었고, 다시 돌격하여 칼로 맞붙었다. 싸움은 길고 승부를 가늠하기 어려웠는데, 만드리카르도는 그것을 끝내기로 작정하고 그라다소에게 두 팔을 두르고 맞붙어 격투하여 둘 다 땅으로 쓰러졌다. 그러나 만드리카르도가 위에 떨어졌고, 그 유리함을 지켜 그라다소로 하여금 패배를 인정하게 만들었다. 이제 처녀가 끼어들어 승자에게 축하를 건네고, 패자도 할 수 있는 한 위로했다.\n\n만드리카르도와 처녀는 성문으로 나아갔는데, 그것은 지키는 자가 없었다. 안으로 들어가자 그들은 황금 벽기둥에 매달린 방패 하나를 보았다. 그 문장은 하늘빛 바탕에 흰 독수리였으니, 프리기아 종족의 꽃인 가니메데스를 채어 간 제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]의 새를 기리는 것이었다. 그 아래에는 다음과 같은 시구가 새겨져 있었다.\n\n\"헥토르처럼 강한 자가 아니라면 그 누구도 부정한 손으로 내 방패를 욕되게 하지 말라.\"\n\n처녀는 조랑말에서 내려, 몸을 땅에 굽혀 그 무기에 절을 올렸다. 타타르 왕도 똑같은 경의로 머리를 숙였다. 그러고는 방패를 향해 나아가 자신의 칼로 그것을 건드렸다. 그러자 지진이 땅을 뒤흔들었고, 그가 들어온 길이 닫혔다. 또 다른, 반대편의 문이 열려, 황금 줄기와 곡식이 빽빽이 들어선 들판을 드러냈다. 이에 처녀는 그에게, 자신의 앞에 있는 그 곡식을 베어 넘기고, 들판 한가운데 자란 나무 하나를 뿌리째 뽑는 것 말고는 빠져나갈 길이 없다고 말했다. 만드리카르도는 아무 대꾸 없이 칼로 곡식을 베기 시작했으나, 세 번도 채 내려치기 전에, 쓰러지는 줄기마다 즉시 어떤 독을 품거나 굶주린 짐승으로 변하여 그를 덮치려 채비하는 것을 보았다. 처녀의 가르침을 받은 그는 돌 하나를 집어 그 무리 가운데로 던졌다. 기이한 경이가 뒤따랐으니, 돌이 그 짐승들 사이에 떨어지자마자 그것들은 서로에게 분노를 돌려 서로를 갈가리 찢었기 때문이다.\n만드리카르도는 그 기적에 감탄하느라 멈추지 않고, 자신의 과업을 이루어 나무를 뽑으러 나아갔다. 그는 줄기를 둘러 끌어안고, 그것을 뿌리째 뽑아내려 힘껏 애를 썼다. 한 번씩 힘을 쓸 때마다 잎이 소나기처럼 떨어졌고, 그것은 즉시 맹금으로 변하여 그 기사를 공격하며, 끔찍한 비명을 지르면서 그의 얼굴에 날개를 퍼덕였다. 그러나 그는 이 새로운 성가심에도 굴하지 않고, 줄기가 자신의 힘에 굴복할 때까지 계속 잡아당겼다. 한바탕 바람과 천둥이 뒤따랐고, 매와 독수리들이 비명을 지르며 날아가 버렸다.\n\n그러나 이것들은 다만 새로운 적에게 자리를 내주었을 뿐이었다. 나무를 뽑아내며 생긴 구멍에서 사나운 뱀 한 마리가 나와, 만드리카르도에게 달려들어, 그를 거의 으스러뜨릴 만한 힘으로 그의 사지를 휘감았기 때문이다. 그러나 운명은 또다시 그의 편이 되어 주었으니, 그 괴물의 똬리 아래 몸부림치다가 그가 구멍 속으로 뒤로 넘어졌고, 그의 적은 그의 무게에 짓눌려 으스러졌다.\n\n만드리카르도는 어느 정도 회복되어 뱀이 죽은 것을 확인하자, 자신이 떨어진 곳을 살펴보기 시작했고, 자신이 값진 금속으로 입혀지고 한 점 타는 숯불로 밝혀진 지하실 안에 있음을 보았다. 한가운데에는 일종의 상아 관대가 있었고, 그 위에는 갑옷을 입은 기사처럼 보이는 것이 누워 있었으나, 그것은 사실 빈 전리품으로, 한때 헥토르의 것이었던 풍요롭고 값진 무기들로 이루어진 것이었으니, 부족한 것이라곤 그 검뿐이었다. 만드리카르도가 그 상을 바라보며 서 있을 때, 그의 뒤로 문이 열리더니, 아름다운 처녀의 무리가 춤추며 들어와, 갑옷을 한 조각씩 집어 들고 그를 방패가 매달린 곳으로 데려갔다. 거기서 그는 위풍당당하게 앉아 있는 성의 요정을 보았다. 그녀에 의해 그는 자신이 얻은 무기로 무장되었으니, 다만 먼저 오를란도에게서 빼앗아 헥토르의 무기를 손에 넣는 일을 완성할 두린다나 외에는 다른 어떤 칼도 차지 않겠노라 엄숙한 맹세를 한 뒤였다.\n\n————\n\n프랑스 침공 (계속)\n이야기를 마친 만드리카르도는 이제 로제로를 향해, 둘 중 누가 트로이아 기사의 상징을 달기에 가장 합당한지 무기로 결판내자고 제안했다.\n\n로제로는 자신의 적수에게 칼이 없다는 점을 보고 일어난 거리낌 외에는 이 제안에 다른 이의가 없었다. 만드리카르도는 그것이 장애가 될 필요는 없다고 고집했으니, 자신의 맹세가 두린다나를 손에 넣기 전에는 칼을 쓰지 못하도록 막고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n이 말이 떨어지기가 무섭게 이제 만드리카르도와 동행하던 그라다소가 새로운 적수로 나섰다. 그라다소는 두린다나에 대한 자신의 선차적 권리를 주장했으니, 그것을 얻으려고 그는 (처음에 이야기되었듯) 그 대담한 프랑스 침입에 나섰던 것이다. 이리하여 타타르와 세리카네의 왕들 사이에 다툼이 일었다. 다툼이 격해지는 동안, 한 처녀를 동반한 기사 하나가 그곳에 당도했고, 로제로가 그에게 다툼의 까닭을 들려주었다. 그 기사는 플로리스마트였고, 그의 동행자는 플로르델리스였다. 플로리스마트는 자신이 두린다나의 임자인 오를란도 앞으로 그들을 데려갈 수 있다고 알려 줌으로써 두 용사를 화해시키는 데 성공했다.\n\n그라다소와 만드리카르도는 플로리스마트와 동행하기 위해 기꺼이 휴전을 맺었고, 로제로도 뒤에 남으려 하지 않았다.\n\n탐색을 이어 가던 중 그들은 한 난쟁이를 만났는데, 그는 자신의 여주인을 위해 그들의 도움을 간청했으니, 그녀가 날개 달린 말에 탄 한 마법사에게 채여 갔다는 것이었다. 칼의 문제를 결판내지 않은 채 두기를 아무리 꺼린다 해도, 기사들로서는 이 호소를 뿌리칠 수 없었다. 그리하여 그들 가운데 둘, 그라다소와 로제로가 난쟁이와 동행했다. 만드리카르도는 오를란도를 찾는 일을 고집했고, 플로리스마트는 플로르델리스와 함께 샤를마뉴의 진영으로 길을 갔다.\n\n로제로를 길러 그에게 가장 따뜻한 애정을 품었던 마법사 아틀란테스는, 자신의 제자가 자신에게서 떨어져 나가, 우연히 그가 알게 된 그 고귀한 처녀 브라다만테의 영향으로 기독교 신앙으로 개종할 운명임을 자신의 술법으로 알고 있었다. 이 점에서 하늘의 뜻을 가로막으려는 생각으로, 그는 이제 로제로를 자신의 손아귀에 가두려고 온갖 술법을 펼쳤다. 부리는 악마들의 도움으로 그는 피레네 산맥의 오를 수 없는 높은 곳에 성 하나를 세웠고, 그것을 자신의 제자에게 즐거운 거처로 만들고자, 자신의 성 인근에 우연히 들어온 기사와 처녀 들을 숱하게 함정에 빠뜨려 그곳으로 데려왔다. 여기서, 일종의 관능의 낙원에서, 그들은 너무도 기꺼이 영광과 의무를 잊고, 나태한 향락 속에 시간을 보냈다.\n\n기사들을 자신의 손아귀로 꾀어들이려고 이제 난쟁이를 보낸 것은 바로 그 마법사였다.\n\n그러나 우리는 이제 리날도에게로 돌아가야 하니, 우리는 그를 로도몬트와의 싸움이 중단된 대목에서 두고 왔다. 지난번 적수를 찾아, 그들의 싸움을 결판내려는 일념으로, 그는 로도몬트가 갔으리라 짐작한 아르덴 숲으로 들어갔다. 이 탐색에 몰두해 있을 때, 그는 벌거벗은 채 춤추는 아름다운 아이의 환영에 놀랐으니, 그 아이만큼이나 아름다운 세 처녀가 함께 있었다. 그 광경에 넋을 잃고 감탄하고 있을 때 아이가 그에게 다가와, 그에게 장미와 백합을 한 움큼씩 던져 그를 말에서 떨어뜨렸다. 그가 떨어지자마자 춤추던 자들에게 붙잡혀, 그들에게 이리저리 끌려다니며 꽃으로 매질을 당하다가 기절하고 말았다. 그가 정신을 차리기 시작하자 그 무리 중 하나가 그에게 다가와, 그의 벌은 만물이 굽히는 그 힘에 대한 그의 반역의 결과라고, 그리고 그에게 가해진 상처를 치유할 단 하나의 치료법이 있으니, 그것은 사랑의 샘물을 마시는 것이라고 말했다. 그러고는 그를 떠났다.\n\n리날도는 쑤시고 기력이 빠진 채, 근처에 흐르는 샘으로 몸을 끌고 가, 갈증에 타들어 가던 터라 그 물을 게걸스럽게, 거의 무의식적으로 마셨는데, 그 물은 맛은 달았으나 마음에는 썼다. 거듭 들이켠 끝에 그는 기력과 기억을 되찾았고, 자신이 일찍이 안젤리카가 꽃비를 내려 그를 깨웠던, 그리고 그가 그녀의 호의를 멸시하며 달아났던 바로 그 자리에 있음을 알았다.\n\n이 장면을 떠올리자 곧이어 자신의 죄에 대한 자각이 따랐다. 그는 자신의 배은망덕을 비통하게 뉘우치며, 안젤리카의 나라로 서둘러 가 그녀의 발치에서 용서를 구하려는 생각으로 바야르에 뛰어올랐다.\n\n이제 우리의 발걸음을 되짚어, 무사들이 두돈에게서 침입자들을 물리치기 위해 프랑스로 돌아오라는 샤를마뉴의 부름을 전해 들었던 때로 돌아가 보자. 그들은 모두 그 명령에 따랐으나, 안젤리카에 대한 정념이 여전히 그를 그녀의 곁에 붙들어 둔 오를란도만은 예외였다. 알브라카 앞에 당도한 오를란도는 그곳이 빈틈없이 포위당해 있는 것을 보았다. 그러나 그는 성채 안으로 들어가, 자신의 출발 때부터 리날도와 나머지 사람들이 샤를마뉴를 도우러 떠나면서 그들과 헤어진 때까지의 자신의 모험을 안젤리카에게 들려주었다. 안젤리카는 답하여, 수비대의 고초와 포위군의 위세를 설명했고, 끝으로 오를란도에게 자신이 임박한 위험에서 벗어나도록 도와 프랑스로 호위해 달라고 간청했다. 리날도에 대한 사랑이 그녀의 숨은 동기임을 의심하지 못한 오를란도는 그 제안에 기꺼이 동의했고, 탈출이 결정되었다.\n\n요새에 등불을 켜 둔 채 그들은 해 질 무렵에 떠나, 적진을 무사히 지나갔다. 수많은 모험을 겪은 끝에 그들은 바닷가에 다다라, 프랑스로 가는 작은 배에 올랐다. 배는 무사히 당도했고, 여행자들은 프로방스에 내려 육로로 길을 갔다. 어느 날, 더위에 지친 그들은 아르덴 숲에서 햇볕을 피해 쉴 곳을 찾았는데, 우연이 안젤리카를 멸시의 샘으로 이끌었고, 그녀는 그 물을 게걸스럽게 마셨다. 거기서 나오던 백작과 처녀는 한 낯선 기사와 마주쳤다. 그는 다름 아닌 리날도로, 자신의 무정함에 대한 용서를 빌고 새로이 찾은 정념을 호소하려고 안젤리카를 찾아 막 순례를 떠나려던 참이었다. 놀라움과 기쁨이 처음에는 그에게서 말을 앗아 갔으나, 곧 정신을 차린 그는 기쁘게 그녀에게 인사하며, 그녀를 자신의 것이라 주장하고, 자신의 보호 아래 들어오라고 권했다. 그의 주제넘음은 안젤리카에게 멸시로써 물리쳐졌고, 자신의 권리를 침범당해 격노한 오를란도는 그에게 무기로 그들의 권리를 결판내자고 도전했다.\n\n뒤이어 벌어진 싸움에 겁먹은 안젤리카는 숲을 가로질러 한껏 달아나, 천막으로 뒤덮인 평원으로 나왔다. 이것은 샤를마뉴의 진영이었으니, 그는 마르실리우스에 맞서 진격한 군대를 지원하도록 예정된 예비군을 거느리고 있었다. 처녀의 이야기를 들은 샤를은 어렵사리 두 사촌을 떼어 놓은 뒤, 다툼의 원인인 안젤리카를 바이에른의 공작 나모의 보살핌에 맡기며, 임박한 전투에서 그녀를 가장 잘 받을 자격을 갖춘 자의 것이 되리라고 약속했다.\n\n그러나 이 계획과 희망은 좌절되었다. 기독교군은 모든 곳에서 패하여 사라센군에게서 달아났고, 두 연인 모두에게 무심했던 안젤리카는 날쌘 조랑말에 올라 숲속으로 뛰어들었으니, 두려움에도 불구하고 자유를 되찾은 것을 기뻐했다. 마침내 그녀는 부드러운 산들바람이 부는, 무성한 숲에 멈추었다. 그곳의 어린 나무들은 두 줄기 맑은 시냇물이 와서 물을 합쳐 흐르며 적셔 주었고, 그 시냇물은 듣기 좋은 속삭임을 자아냈다. 리날도에게서 멀리 떨어졌다고 믿고, 피로와 여름 더위에 지친 그녀는, 푸른 풀밭을 거의 가릴 만큼 빽빽이 꽃으로 뒤덮인 둑이 자신에게 내려 쉬라고 손짓하는 것을 기쁘게 바라보았다. 그녀는 조랑말에서 내려, 그것이 시냇가에 돋은 연한 풀로 기력을 회복하도록 풀어놓았다. 그러고는 이끼로 덮이고 장미와 산사나무 꽃으로 둘러싸인 아늑한 구석에서 고마운 휴식에 몸을 맡겼다.\n\n그녀가 오래 잠들지 않았을 때, 말 한 마리가 다가오는 소리에 잠을 깼다. 벌떡 일어난 그녀는 시냇가에 당도한 무장한 기사 하나를 보았다. 그가 두려워할 자인지 아닌지 알지 못한 그녀의 가슴은 불안으로 두근거렸다. 그녀는 누구인지 보려고 잎새를 옆으로 헤쳤으나, 자신을 드러낼까 두려워 숨조차 거의 쉬지 못했다. 곧 기사는 꽃핀 둑에 몸을 던지더니, 머리를 손에 괴고 깊은 상념에 빠져들었다. 그러고는 침묵에서 깨어나, 깊은 한숨에 뒤섞인 탄식을 쏟아내기 시작했다. 강물 같은 눈물이 그의 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸고, 그의 가슴은 감추어진 불꽃으로 괴로워하는 듯했다. \"아, 부질없는 회한이여!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"잔인한 운명이여! 다른 이들은 승리하는데, 나는 가망 없는 비참함을 견디는구나! 이토록 치욕스럽고 이토록 짓누르는 사슬을 차느니, 차라리 천 번이라도 목숨을 잃는 편이 나으리라!\"\n\n이때쯤 안젤리카는 그 낯선 자를 알아보았고, 그가 자신의 가장 훌륭한 구혼자들 가운데 하나인 키르카시아의 왕 사크리판트임을 알아차렸다. 이 왕자는 안젤리카를 따라 그녀의 나라에서부터, 바로 그 성문에서부터\n\n그날, 프랑스로 향했고, 그곳에서 그는 그녀가 팔라딘 오를란도의 보호 아래 있다는 것과, 황제가 자신의 조카들 가운데 그녀를 가장 받을 만한 자에게 무용의 상으로 그녀를 내리겠다는 칙령을 선포했다는 소식을 낙담한 채 들었다.\n\n사크리판트가 계속해서 한탄하는 동안, 그의 한숨에 언제나 대리석처럼 차갑게 맞서 왔던 안젤리카는 이 불행한 위기에 그의 도움을 이용하지 못할 까닭이 없다고 속으로 생각했다. 그를 결코 배우자로 받아들이지 않겠다고 굳게 마음먹고 있었으나, 그녀는 자신이 그에게 요구하는 봉사에 대한 보답으로 한 줄기 희망의 빛을 비춰 줄 필요가 있다고 느꼈다. 그녀는 갑자기, 마치 아르테미스[로마의 〈디아나〉]처럼 나무 그늘에서 걸어 나왔다. \"신들께서 그대를 지켜 주시고,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"나에 대한 모든 모진 생각을 그대에게서 멀리 거두어 주시기를!\" 그러고는 그녀는 아버지의 궁정에서 그와 헤어진 뒤로 자신에게 닥친 모든 일을, 그리고 어떻게 자신이 오를란도의 보호에 의지하여 포위된 도시에서 빠져나왔는지를 그에게 이야기했다. 바로 그 순간 누군가가 다가오는 듯이 말과 갑옷이 부딪치는 소리가 들렸다. 그리고 방해받은 데 분노한 사크리판트는 다시 투구를 쓰고 말에 올라타고는 창을 겨누어 잡았다. 그는 한 기사가 눈처럼 새하얀 띠와 깃털을 두르고 다가오는 것을 보았다. 사크리판트는 성난 눈으로 그를 바라보았고, 그가 아직 얼마간 떨어져 있을 때 결투를 청했다. 상대방은 그의 성난 어조에 대꾸할 마음이 일지 않아, 그저 방어 자세를 취했다. 두 사람의 말은 동시에 박차를 받고 폭풍 같은 기세로 서로에게 달려들었다. 두 사람의 방패는 각각 상대의 창에 꿰뚫렸으나, 오직 가슴받이의 단단한 강도만이 그들의 목숨을 구했다. 두 말 모두 충돌의 격렬함에 뒤로 물러났으나, 정체 모를 기사의 말은 박차가 닿자 곧 자세를 되찾았다. 사라센 왕의 말은 죽어 쓰러지면서 주인을 함께 깔아뭉갰다. 흰 기사는 적이 이런 처지에 빠진 것을 보고도 다시 싸움을 벌일 마음이 없어, 영광을 위해서는 이만하면 충분하다고 여기고 숲을 가로질러 제 갈 길을 갔으며, 사크리판트가 말에서 벗어나기도 전에 이미 1마일이나 떨어져 있었다.\n\n마치 자기 쟁기를 끌던 소들을 벼락이 내리쳐 죽이자 그 일격에 망연자실한 농부가 자신의 손실을 슬프게 바라보며 꼼짝 못 하고 서 있듯이, 사크리판트도 안젤리카를 자기 패배의 증인으로 둔 데 대한 굴욕에 어쩔 줄 모르고 압도되어 그렇게 서 있었다. 그는 신음하고 한숨지었으나, 그것은 멍든 상처의 고통 때문이라기보다는 그녀 앞에서 이런 꼴로 전락한 수치심 때문이었다. 공주는 그를 가엾이 여겨 할 수 있는 한 위로해 주었다. \"후회는 버리세요, 나리,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"이 사고는 오로지 그대 말이 약했던 탓에 일어난 것이니, 그 말은 이런 격돌보다는 휴식과 먹이가 더 필요했던 것이지요. 그대의 적도 이 일로 아무런 명예를 얻을 수 없을 것이니, 그가 두 번째 시도를 감행할 엄두도 내지 못하고 서둘러 떠나 버렸기 때문이지요.\" 그녀가 이렇게 사크리판트를 위로하는 동안, 그들은 가방과 뿔나팔을 든, 전령으로 보이는 사람이 다가오는 것을 알아차렸다. 그는 다가오자마자 사크리판트에게 말을 걸어, 흰 방패를 들고 투구에 흰 깃털을 단 기사가 이 길로 지나가는 것을 보았느냐고 물었다. \"정말이지 그를 너무 많이 보았소,\" 사크리판트가 말했다, \"나를 땅바닥에 쓰러뜨린 자가 바로 그자요. 그래도 적어도 그 기사가 누구인지는 그대에게서 알 수 있기를 바라오.\" \"그것은 쉽게 알려 드릴 수 있소,\" 그 사람이 말했다. \"그러니 아시오, 만일 그대가 쓰러졌다면, 그것은 아름다운 만큼 용감한 한 귀부인의 드높은 무용 탓이오. 그대에게서 승리의 영예를 빼앗은 이는 아름답고 이름 높은 브라다만테요.\" 이 말과 함께 전령은 제 갈 길로 말을 달려 갔고, 사크리판트는 그 어느 때보다 더 어쩔 줄 모르고 굴욕에 잠긴 채 남겨졌다. 그는 말없이 안젤리카의 말에 올라타, 그 귀부인을 뒤편 엉덩이 쪽에 태우고는 더 안전한 피난처를 찾아 떠났다. 그들이 채 2마일도 가기 전에 숲에서 새로운 소리가 들려왔고, 그들은 한 마리 늠름하고 힘센 말이 골짜기를 뛰어넘고 길을 가로막는 나뭇가지들을 헤치며 황금으로 장식된 화려한 마구를 갖춘 채 그들 앞에 나타나는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"덤불을 뚫고 보기 어려운 내 눈을 믿을 수 있다면,\" 안젤리카가 말했다, \"수풀을 저토록 힘차게 헤치며 달려오는 저 말은 바야르예요. 우리 둘이 한 마리 약한 짐승에 함께 타고 있는 지금, 어떻게 저 말이 우리에게 자기가 필요하다는 것을 아는 듯한지 놀라울 따름이에요.\" 사크리판트는 작은 말에서 내려 그 사나운 군마에게 다가가 고삐를 붙잡으려 했으나, 거만한 짐승은 그를 외면하고 대리석 벽이라도 산산조각 낼 만한 뒷발질을 한바탕 퍼부었다. 그러더니 바야르는 마치 충성스러운 개가 오랜 헤어짐 끝에 주인에게 다가가듯 부드럽고 다정한 태도로 안젤리카에게 다가갔다. 그것은 그가 알브라카에서 그녀가 자기를 어루만져 주고 심지어 먹이까지 주었던 것을 기억했기 때문이다. 그녀는 왼손으로 그 고삐를 잡고, 오른손으로 그 목을 토닥였다. 놀라운 지혜를 타고난 그 아름다운 짐승은 완전히 순종하는 듯 보였다. 사크리판트는 그 틈을 타 말 위로 뛰어올라 그 뛰는 동작을 다스렸고, 안젤리카는 작은 말의 엉덩이 자리를 떠나 다시 제자리에 올라앉았다.\n\n그러나 무기 소리가 들리는 쪽으로 눈을 돌리던 사크리판트는 리날도를 보았다. 그 영웅은 이제 안젤리카를 제 목숨보다 더 사랑하건만, 그녀는 겁 많은 두루미가 매를 피하듯 그를 피해 달아난다.\n\n안젤리카가 마셨던 그 샘은 이 아름다운 여왕에게 그러한 효력을 미쳤으니, 그녀는 괴로운 얼굴빛과 떨리는 목소리로 사크리판트에게 리날도가 다가오는 것을 기다리지 말고 자기와 함께 달아나자고 간청했다.\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 사크리판트가 말했다, \"내가 당신을 지켜 줄 능력이 있는지 당신이 의심할 만큼 내가 당신에게 그토록 하찮은 존재란 말이오? 알브라카의 전투를, 그리고 당신을 지키기 위해 내가 홀로 아그리칸과 그 기사 전부를 상대로 싸웠던 것을 잊었단 말이오?\"\n\n안젤리카는 어찌해야 할지 몰라 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 그러나 이미 리날도는 너무 가까이 와 있어 달아날 수 없었다. 그는 그 말을 알아보았기에 위협적으로 키르카시아의 왕에게 다가갔다.\n\n\"비열한 도둑놈,\" 그가 외쳤다, \"그 말에서 내려라, 그리고 감히 내 소유물을 훔친 죄로 마땅히 받아야 할 벌을 면하라. 또한 공주도 내 손에 넘겨라. 이토록 매혹적인 귀부인과 이토록 늠름한 군마를 그런 자의 손에 두는 것은 참으로 죄악일 테니.\"\n\n이렇게 모욕을 당한 데 분노한 키르카시아의 왕이 소리쳤다. \"거짓말이다, 악당아, 나에게 도둑이라는 이름을 붙이다니, 그 이름은 나보다 네 자신에게 더 어울린다. 이 귀부인의 아름다움과 이 말의 완벽함이 견줄 데 없다는 것은 사실이다. 그러니 덤벼라, 우리 둘 중에 누가 이들을 차지하기에 더 합당한지 가려 보자.\"\n\n이 말과 함께 키르카시아의 왕과 리날도는 온 힘을 다해 서로 맞붙었으니, 한 사람은 걸어서, 다른 한 사람은 말을 타고 싸웠다. 그렇다고 사라센 왕이 이로써 어떤 유리함을 얻었으리라고 여겨서는 안 된다. 말타기에 서투른 어린 시동이라도 이 노련한 기사보다 더 완전히 바야르를 다루지 못하지는 않았을 것이기 때문이다. 충성스러운 그 짐승은 제 주인을 너무도 사랑한 나머지 주인을 다치게 하려 하지 않았고, 사크리판트의 손길에는 도움도 복종도 거부했다. 사크리판트는 헛된 일격밖에 가하지 못했으니, 말은 그가 앞으로 가게 하려 하면 뒤로 물러나고, 머리를 떨구고 등을 활처럼 구부리며 다리로 걷어차 거의 기사를 안장에서 떨어뜨릴 지경이었다. 사크리판트는 그를 다스릴 수 없음을 알고 기회를 엿보다 안장 위로 몸을 일으켜 가볍게 땅으로 뛰어내렸다. 그러고는 말의 거추장스러움에서 벗어나 보다 대등한 조건으로 싸움을 다시 시작했다. 찌르고 막는 두 사람의 기량은 막상막하였다. 한 사람이 몸을 일으키면 다른 사람은 몸을 숙였다. 한 발을 단단히 디딘 채 몸을 돌리고 비틀며 타격을 가하거나 피했다. 마침내 리날도가 키르카시아인에게 몸을 던지며 너무도 무시무시한 일격을 가하니, 그의 훌륭한 검 푸스베르타가 사크리판트의 방패를 두 동강 내고 말았는데, 그 방패는 뼈로 만들고 잘 단련된 두꺼운 강철판으로 덮여 있었음에도 그러했다. 사라센인의 팔은 방어 수단을 잃었고, 그 일격에 거의 마비될 지경이었다. 안젤리카는 승리가 어느 쪽으로 기우는지를 알아차리고, 리날도의 전리품이 될 생각에 몸서리치며 더는 망설이지 않았다. 그녀는 말머리를 돌려 온 힘을 다해 달아났고, 가파른 비탈을 뒤덮은 둥근 자갈들에도 아랑곳없이 깊은 골짜기로 뛰어들었으니, 리날도가 뒤쫓아 오지나 않을까 하는 두려움에 떨면서였다. 이 골짜기 밑바닥에서 그녀는 흰 수염이 허리까지 흘러내리고 그 거룩한 모습이 신심을 보증하는 듯한 한 늙은 은자를 만났다.\n\n세월과 단식으로 쪼그라든 듯한 이 은자는 초라한 나귀를 타고 천천히 길을 가고 있었다. 두려움에 압도된 공주는 그에게 자기 목숨을 구해 달라고, 그리고 자기를 바다의 어느 항구로 인도하여 그곳에서 배를 타고 프랑스를 떠나 다시는 리날도라는 가증스러운 이름을 듣지 않게 해 달라고 간청했다.\n\n이 늙은 은자는 어느 정도 마법사이기도 했다. 그는 안젤리카를 위로하며 모든 위험에서 그녀를 보호하겠다고 약속했다. 그러고는 자기 자루를 열어 그 안에서 책 한 권을 꺼내 단 한 쪽을 읽었을 뿐인데, 그의 주문에 복종하는 한 도깨비가 일하는 농부의 모습으로 나타나 그의 명령을 청했다. 도깨비는 명령을 받고는, 두 기사가 여전히 싸움을 벌이고 있는 곳으로 몸을 옮겨, 대담하게 두 사람 사이로 끼어들었다.\n\n\"청컨대 말씀해 주시오,\" 그가 말했다, \"이 싸움에서 이기는 자에게 무슨 이득이 돌아오겠소? 그대들이 다투는 대상은 이미 처분되었소. 팔라딘 오를란도가 아무런 힘도 들이지 않고 아무런 저항도 받지 않은 채 지금 안젤리카 공주를 파리로 데려가고 있으니 말이오. 그대들은 어서 그들을 뒤쫓는 편이 나을 것이오. 그들이 파리에 닿으면 그대들은 다시는 그녀를 보지 못할 것이기 때문이오.\"\n\n이 말에 그 경쟁하던 두 전사가 어쩔 줄 모르고 망연자실하여, 자신들이 경쟁자에게 자기들을 누르고 승리할 절호의 기회를 내주고 있음을 말없이 인정하는 모습을 그대는 보았을 것이다. 리날도는 바야르에게 다가가 수치와 분노의 한숨을 내쉬며, 만일 오를란도를 따라잡는다면 그 심장을 뽑아내겠노라고 무시무시한 맹세를 했다. 그러고는 바야르에 올라 박차로 그 옆구리를 압박하며, 키르카시아의 왕을 숲속에 걸어서 남겨 둔 채 떠났다.\n\n그토록 오랫동안 누구도 제 고삐에 손조차 대지 못하게 하던 바야르가 마침내 리날도에게 순종한 것이 이상하게 보여서는 안 된다. 그 훌륭한 짐승은 거의 인간에 가까운 지혜를 지니고 있었으니, 그가 제 주인에게서 달아난 것은 오로지 주인을 안젤리카의 자취로 이끌어 그녀를 되찾게 하기 위함이었다. 그는 공주가 전투에서 달아나는 것을 보았고, 그때 리날도가 걸어서 싸움에 몰두해 있었으므로, 바야르는 자유로이 안젤리카의 흔적을 뒤쫓을 수 있었다. 이렇게 그는 주인이 다가오는 것을 허락하지 않으면서도 주인을 자기 뒤로 이끌어, 공주를 볼 수 있는 데까지 데려왔던 것이다. 그러나 이제 바야르는 자기 주인과 마찬가지로 도깨비의 거짓 소식에 속아, 평소대로 올라타게 하고 주인을 섬기는 데 순순히 따랐다. 분노에 들끓는 리날도는 그를 파리 쪽으로 달리게 했으니, 바야르의 속도가 바람을 앞질렀음에도 그의 바람에는 미치지 못할 만큼 더디게 느껴졌다. 오를란도와 맞붙고 싶은 조바심에 가득 차, 그는 그날 밤 잠에는 겨우 몇 시간만을 내주었다. 이튿날 이른 아침 그는 자기 앞에 그 큰 도시를 보았으니, 그 성벽 아래 황제 카를루스가 흩어진 자기 군대의 잔병들을 모아 두었던 곳이었다. 곧 사방에서 공격받으리라는 것을 내다본 황제는 옛 요새를 보수하게 하고, 넓고 깊은 해자로 둘러싸인 새 요새를 짓게 했다. 적을 상대로 전장을 지키려는 열망에 그는 새로운 동맹을 얻을 온갖 수단을 붙잡았다. 그는 새로운 진영을 꾸릴 만큼 충분한 원조를 잉글랜드로부터 받기를 바랐고, 리날도가 다시 합류하자마자 그를 사절로 뽑아 잉글랜드로 보내 지원군을 청하게 했다. 리날도는 이 임무가 전혀 마음에 들지 않았으나, 자기 마음에 가장 가까운 목적에 단 하루도 바칠 시간을 갖지 못한 채 황제의 명령에 복종했다. 그는 칼레로 서둘러 가서 한순간도 지체하지 않고 잉글랜드로 떠나는 배에 올랐으니, 자기 임무를 신속히 처리하고 어서 프랑스로 돌아오기를 간절히 바라면서였다.\n\n# BRADAMANTE AND ROGERO\n\n브라다만테와 로제로\n브라다만테, 곧 흰 깃털과 방패를 단 그 기사는, 그 갑작스러운 등장과 사크리판트와의 격돌에 대해 우리가 이미 이야기한 바 있거니와, 자신과 알게 된 지 거의 처음에 우연히 헤어진 로제로를 찾고 있었다. 사크리판트와 격돌한 뒤 브라다만테는 로제로와 다시 만나기를 바라며 숲을 가로질러 제 갈 길을 갔고, 마침내 어느 아름다운 샘가에 이르렀다.\n\n이 샘은 넓은 풀밭을 가로질러 흘렀다. 오래된 나무들이 그 위에 그늘을 드리웠고, 그 물의 감미로운 속삭임에 이끌린 나그네들이 그곳에 멈춰 몸을 식히곤 했다. 브라다만테는 그곳의 아름다움을 즐기려고 사방으로 눈길을 돌리다가, 한 나무 그늘 아래 가장 깊은 슬픔에 짓눌린 듯한 한 기사가 비스듬히 누워 있는 것을 보았다. 브라다만테는 그에게 말을 걸어 그 비탄의 까닭을 알려 달라고 청했다. \"아아! 나리,\" 그가 말했다, \"나는 젊고 매혹적인 한 벗을, 내 약혼한 아내를 한탄하고 있소. 그녀는 한 악당—차라리 그를 악마라 부르게 해 주오—에게 빼앗겼는데, 그자는 날개 달린 말을 타고 공중에서 내려와 그녀를 붙잡아 비명을 지르는 그녀를 자기 소굴로 데려갔소. 나는 내 말이 더는 나를 태우지 못할 때까지 바위를 넘고 골짜기를 가로질러 그들을 뒤쫓았으나, 이제 죽음만을 기다릴 뿐이오.\" 그는 덧붙이기를, 우연히 그곳에 이르렀던 두 기사가 이미 자기를 위해 헛된 시도를 했다고 말했다. 그들의 이름은 세리카네의 왕 그라다소와 무어인 로제로였다. 두 사람 모두 마법사의 술수에 굴복하여, 그가 산꼭대기에 자리한 난공불락의 성에 가두어 둔 포로들의 수에 더해졌다. 로제로의 이름이 언급되자 브라다만테는 기쁨에 움찔했으나, 자기 연인이 마법사의 올가미에 사로잡힌 포로라는 말을 듣고는 그 기쁨이 곧 반대의 감정으로 바뀌었다. \"기사 양반,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"절망에 자신을 내맡기지 마시오. 만일 그대가 그토록 한탄하는 여인을 가둔 그 성으로 나를 인도해 주기만 한다면, 오늘은 그대가 생각하는 것보다 더 행복한 날이 될 수 있을 것이오.\"\n\n기사가 대답했다. \"내 삶을 소중하게 만들던 모든 것을 잃은 마당에, 나로서는 이 일의 위험을 피할 까닭이 없으니, 그대가 청하는 대로 하겠소. 그러나 그대가 맞닥뜨려야 할 위험들을 미리 일러두는 바이오. 만일 그대가 쓰러진다면, 그것을 내 탓으로 돌리지 마시오.\"\n\n이렇게 말한 뒤 그들은 성으로 가는 길을 떠났으나, 진영에서 온 한 전령에게 따라잡혔다. 그 전령은 브라다만테를 찾아 그녀를 다시 군대로 불러오라는 명을 받고 보내진 것이었으니, 낙심한 그녀의 병력을 안심시키고 무어인들의 진격을 막아 내는 데 그녀의 존재가 필요했기 때문이었다.\n\n이름이 피나벨이었던 그 슬픔에 잠긴 기사는 이리하여 브라다만테가 클레르몽 가문의 후손이며, 그 가문과 자기 가문인 마옌스 가문 사이에는 오랜 반목이 있음을 알게 되었다. 이 순간부터 그 배신자는 오로지 브라다만테와의 동행에서 벗어날 방도만을 찾았으니, 그는 그녀에게서 자기에게 좋은 일이라곤 일어나지 않으리라, 오히려 자기 이름과 혈통이 그녀에게 알려진다면 치명적인 해를 입으리라고 두려워했기 때문이다. 그는 자기 자신의 비열한 잣대로 그녀를 헤아렸고, 자기 악행의 응보를 알고 있었기에 마땅한 보복을 받을까 두려워했던 것이다.\n\n브라다만테는 군대로 돌아오라는 소환에도 불구하고 자기 연인을 포로로 남겨 두기로 결심할 수 없어, 먼저 자신이 착수한 모험을 끝마치기로 마음먹었다. 피나벨이 길을 앞장서서, 그들은 마침내 한 숲에 이르렀는데, 그 한가운데에는 가파르고 바위투성이인 산이 솟아 있었다. 이제 브라다만테에게서 어떻게 달아날까 하는 것 말고는 아무 생각도 없던 피나벨은, 만일 눈에 보이는 범위 안에 밤을 지낼 피난처가 있다면 그것을 찾아내기 위해 시야를 넓히려고 산에 오르자고 제안했다. 이 핑계 아래 그는 브라다만테를 떠나 산비탈을 올라가다 바위틈에 이르렀고, 그 아래를 내려다보니 그것이 아래쪽에서는 널찍한 동굴로 넓어지는 것을 알아차렸다. 그동안 자기 안내자를 잃을까 두려웠던 브라다만테는 그의 발자취를 바짝 뒤따라가 동굴 입구에서 그와 다시 합류했다. 그러자 그 배신자는 그녀에게서 달아날 수 없음을 깨닫고 또 다른 계략을 꾸몄다. 그는 그녀에게 말하기를, 그녀가 다가오기 전에 동굴 안에서 젊고 아름다운 한 처녀를 보았는데, 그 화려한 옷차림이 그녀의 고귀한 출생을 알려 주었으며, 그녀가 눈물과 한탄으로 도움을 청했다고, 그리고 자기가 그녀를 구하러 내려가기도 전에 한 악당이 그녀를 붙잡아 동굴 깊숙한 곳으로 끌고 가 버렸다고 했다.\n\n진실하고 용감함으로 가득한 브라다만테는 이 마옌스 출신 배신자의 거짓말을 선뜻 믿었다. 그 처녀를 구하려는 열망에 그녀는 내려가기를 수월하게 할 수단을 찾아 둘러보았고, 가지를 넓게 펼친 커다란 느릅나무를 발견하고는 검으로 가장 큰 가지 하나를 잘라 내 그 틈으로 밀어 넣었다. 그녀는 피나벨에게 굵은 쪽 끝을 단단히 붙잡고 있으라 이르고는, 손으로 가지들을 움켜쥔 채 자신을 동굴 속으로 내려뜨렸다.\n\n그 배신자는 그녀가 이렇게 매달린 것을 보고 미소 지으며, \"그대는 뛰어내리기를 잘하시오?\" 하고 비웃듯 물으면서, 음흉한 환희에 차 가지를 놓아 버렸고, 브라다만테가 동굴 바닥으로 곤두박질치는 것을 보았다. \"그대의 일족 전부가 그대와 함께 거기 있었더라면,\" 그가 중얼거렸다, \"그래서 그대들 모두가 함께 죽어 버렸더라면.\"\n\n그러나 피나벨의 흉악한 계략은 이루어지지 않았다. 가지의 잔가지와 잎사귀들이 그 추락을 막아 주어, 브라다만테는 떨어지면서 충격에 기절하기는 했으나 심하게 다치지는 않았고, 다른 모험들을 위해 보존되었다.\n\n충격에서 회복되자마자 브라다만테는 사방으로 눈길을 돌렸고, 한 문을 발견하여 그것을 지나 첫 번째 것보다 더 크고 높은 두 번째 동굴로 들어섰다. 그것은 지하의 신전 같은 모습이었다. 더없이 순수한 설화석고 기둥들이 그것을 장식하며 지붕을 떠받쳤다. 한가운데에는 소박한 제단이 솟아 있었고, 설화석고 벽에 그 광채가 반사되는 등불 하나가 부드러운 빛을 사방에 던지고 있었다. 종교적 외경심에 사로잡힌 브라다만테는 제단으로 다가가 무릎을 꿇고, 자기 목숨의 보존자에게 기도와 감사를 쏟아 내며 그 힘의 보호를 간구했다. 그 순간 작은 문 하나가 열리더니 맨발에 옷자락과 머리카락을 흩날리는 한 여인이 그곳에서 나와 그녀의 이름을 부르며 이렇게 말을 건넸다. \"용감하고 너그러운 브라다만테여, 그대를 이곳으로 데려온 것은 위로부터의 힘임을 알라. 마지막으로 이승에 거하던 곳이 바로 이곳이었던 멀린의 영혼이 그대의 도착과 그대를 기다리는 운명을 내게 미리 알려 주었다. 이 유명한 동굴은,\" 그녀가 말을 이었다, \"마법사 멀린의 작품이며, 이곳에 그의 유골이 잠들어 있다. 이 현명하고 덕 있는 마법사가 어떻게 생을 마쳤는지는 그대도 분명 들었으리라. 멀린은 호수의 교활한 요정의 희생자가 되어, 그녀의 청에 운명적으로 응한 탓에 산 채로 제 무덤에 누웠으니, 그 배은망덕한 자가 그에게 건 주문에 저항할 힘이 없었던 것이다. 그 요정은 그가 사는 한 그를 그곳에 붙들어 두었다. 그의 영혼은 이곳을 맴돌며, 마지막 나팔 소리가 죽은 자들을 심판으로 불러낼 때까지 이곳을 떠나지 않을 것이다. 그는 자기 무덤에 다가오는 이들의 물음에 답하니, 그곳에서 어쩌면 그대도 그의 목소리를 듣는 영광을 누릴지 모른다.\"\n\n이 말과 자기 눈앞에 펼쳐진 광경들에 놀란 브라다만테는 자신이 깨어 있는지 잠들어 있는지 알 수 없었다. 어리둥절하면서도 겸손하게 그녀는 눈을 내리깔았고, 그녀의 얼굴에 붉은빛이 번졌다. \"아, 내가 무엇이기에,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"그토록 위대한 예언자가 나에게 말씀하기를 마다하지 않으신단 말인가!\" 그래도 그녀는 은밀한 만족감에 차 그 여사제를 따라갔고, 여사제는 그녀를 멀린의 무덤으로 인도했다. 이 무덤은 단단하고 불처럼 찬란하게 빛나는 일종의 돌로 만들어져 있었다. 그 돌에서 뿜어 나오는 광선만으로도 햇빛이 결코 스며들지 않는 그 끔찍한 곳을 밝히기에 충분했으나, 그 빛이 돌 자체의 어떤 인광의 효과인지, 아니면 그 위에 새겨진 수많은 부적과 주문의 효과인지는 나도 알지 못한다.\n\n브라다만테가 이 신성한 곳의 문턱을 넘자마자, 마법사의 영혼이 굳세고 또렷한 목소리로 그녀에게 인사했다. \"그대의 계획이 번창하기를, 오 순결하고 고귀한 처녀여, 영웅들의 미래의 어머니여, 이탈리아의 영광이여, 그리고 온 세상을 그들의 명성으로 채우도록 정해진 자여. 위대한 장수들, 이름 높은 기사들이 그대의 후손들 가운데 헤아려질 것이니, 그들은 교회를 지키고 그들의 조국에 옛 영화를 되찾아 줄 것이다. 아우구스투스만큼 현명한 군주들과 현자 누마 같은 이들이 황금시대를 다시 가져올 것이다.[75] 이 위대한 운명을 이루기 위해, 그대가 이름 높은 로제로와 혼인하도록 정해져 있다. 그러니 어서 그를 구하러 날아가, 그를 그대에게서 낚아채 지금 사슬로 묶어 두고 있는 그 배신자를 흙먼지 속에 쓰러뜨리라!\"\n\n멀린은 이 말과 함께 그치고는, 그 처녀에게 앞으로의 행로를 더 자세히 가르치는 일을 여사제 멜리사에게 맡겼다. \"내일,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"내가 그대를 로제로가 갇혀 있는 바위 위의 성으로 인도하겠다. 이 거친 숲을 지나 그대를 안내하기 전에는 그대 곁을 떠나지 않을 것이며, 그대가 길을 잘못 들 위험이 없도록 그대의 길을 일러 주겠다.\"\n\n이튿날 아침 멜리사는 브라다만테를 바위와 벼랑 사이로 인도하여, 거센 급류를 건너고 복잡한 협로를 가로질렀으며, 그동안 그녀가 자기 계획을 성공적으로 이루어 내는 데 필요한 정보들을 일러 주었다.\n\n\"힘으로는 뚫을 수 없는 그 성과 그자의 날개 달린 말이 그대의 노력을 좌절시킬 뿐 아니라, 그가 또한 너무도 눈부신 빛을 뿜어 그것을 바라보는 모든 이의 눈을 멀게 하는 방패를 지니고 있음도 알라. 눈을 감아 그것을 피할 수 있으리라고는 생각지 마라. 그렇게 하면 그대가 어떻게 그의 타격을 피하고, 그에게 그대의 타격을 느끼게 할 수 있겠는가? 그러나 내가 그대에게 취해야 할 올바른 방도를 가르쳐 주겠다.\n\n무어인 군주 아그라만트는 인도의 한 여왕에게서 훔친 반지를 지니고 있으니, 그것은 모든 마법을 무력하게 만드는 힘을 지녔다. 아그라만트는 로제로가 자기 전사들 누구보다도 자기에게 더 중요한 인물임을 알고 있어, 그를 그 마법사의 손아귀에서 구해 내기를 바라며, 그 목적으로 자기 종 가운데 가장 교활하고 명민한 브루넬로를, 그 놀라운 반지를 갖추어 보냈으니, 그자는 바로 지금 이 일에 골몰하여 가까이 와 있다. 그러나 아름다운 브라다만테여, 그대 미래의 배우자를 속박에서 구해 낼 영광을 그대 말고는 누구도 차지하지 못하기를 내가 바라므로, 성공의 방도를 일러 주는 동안 잘 들으라. 바닷가를 따라 나 있는 이 길을 따라가면 그대는 머잖아 한 여관에 이를 것이며, 그곳에 사라센인 브루넬로가 그대보다 조금 앞서 도착해 있을 것이다. 그대는 그를 쉽게 알아볼 것이니, 4피트도 안 되는 그 키, 균형이 맞지 않게 큰 머리, 사팔뜨기 눈, 납빛 얼굴, 더부룩한 수염과 맞붙은 짙은 눈썹으로 알 수 있을 것이다. 게다가 그의 옷차림, 곧 전령의 차림이 그대에게 그를 가리켜 줄 것이다.\n\n그대가 그와 대화를 나누기는 쉬울 것이니, 자신을 마법사와 싸우려는 한 기사라고 밝히되, 그 악당이 그대가 반지에 대해 무엇이든 알고 있다고 의심하게 해서는 안 된다. 그가 그대를 마법사의 성까지 안내하리라는 것을 나는 의심치 않는다. 그의 제안을 받아들이되, 성의 찬란한 둥근 지붕이 보이는 곳에 이르기까지는 그의 뒤에 머무르도록 조심하라. 그러고는 망설임 없이 그를 쳐 죽이라, 그 비열한 자는 동정받을 가치가 없으니, 그리고 그에게서 반지를 빼앗으라. 그러나 그가 그대의 의도를 눈치채게 해서는 안 되니, 반지를 입에 넣으면 그는 순식간에 보이지 않게 되어 그대의 눈에서 사라질 것이기 때문이다.\"\n\n이렇게 말하면서 현명한 멜리사와 아름다운 브라다만테는 보르도시 가까이에 이르렀으니, 그곳에서는 풍요롭고 너른 가론강이 제 물결의 조공을 바다에 쏟아붓고 있었다. 그들은 다정하게 포옹하며 헤어졌다. 오로지 자기 목적에만 마음을 쏟은 브라다만테는 여관에 도착하려고 서둘렀으며, 그곳에는 브루넬로가 그녀보다 단 몇 순간 앞서 도착해 있었다. 젊은 여걸은 어렵지 않게 그를 알아보았다. 그녀는 그에게 말을 걸어 몇 가지 가벼운 질문을 던졌고, 그는 능란한 거짓말로 대답했다. 브라다만테는 그녀대로 그에게 자기 성별과 종교와 조국과 자신이 비롯된 혈통을 감추었다. 그들이 함께 이야기를 나누는 동안, 여관 곳곳에서 갑자기 외치는 소리가 들려왔다. \"오 하늘의 여왕이시여!\" 브라다만테가 외쳤다, \"이 갑작스러운 소동의 까닭이 무엇일까?\" 그녀는 곧 그 까닭을 알게 되었다. 주인과 아이들과 하인들 모두가, 마치 혜성이나 큰 일식이라도 본 듯이 눈을 위로 치켜뜨고, 가능성의 한계를 넘어서는 듯한 어떤 경이를 바라보고 있었다. 그녀는 날개 달린 말 한 마리가, 화려한 갑옷을 입은 한 기사를 태운 채 빠른 비행으로 공중을 가르는 것을 또렷이 보았다. 이 기이한 군마의 날개는 넓게 펼쳐져 갖가지 빛깔의 깃털로 덮여 있었다. 그 기사의 광택 나는 갑옷이 그 깃털을 무지개 빛깔로 빛나게 했다. 잠깐 사이에 말과 기사는 산봉우리들 뒤로 사라졌다.\n\n\"저것은 마법사요,\" 주인이 말했다, \"저런 식으로 공중을 가로지르는 것이 자주 목격되는 마법사라오. 어떤 때는 마치 별들 사이에 있는 듯 높이 날고, 또 어떤 때는 땅 위를 스치듯 날지요. 그는 피레네산맥 꼭대기에 놀라운 성 하나를 지니고 있소. 많은 기사가 그를 공격하러 가 용기를 보였으나, 누구도 돌아온 적이 없으니, 그것으로 보아 그들이 목숨이나 자유를 잃은 것이 아닌가 두렵소.\"\n\n브라다만테가 주인에게 말했다. \"이 마법사의 성으로 나를 인도해 줄 안내자를 마련해 줄 수 있겠소?\" \"맹세코,\" 브루넬로가 끼어들며 말했다, \"그것은 헛되이 찾지 않으셔도 되오. 나에게 그것이 모두 글로 적혀 있으니, 내가 직접 그대를 안내하겠소.\" 브라다만테는 고마워하며 그를 자기 안내자로 받아들였다.\n\n주인에게는 처분할 만한 그런대로 쓸 만한 말 한 마리가 있어, 브라다만테는 그것을 흥정해 사들였고, 이튿날 동이 트는 첫 새벽에 그녀는 좁은 골짜기를 따라 길을 떠나면서, 사라센인 브루넬로가 앞장서 길을 인도하도록 신경 썼다.\n\n그들은 피레네산맥 꼭대기에 이르렀으니, 그곳에서는 프랑스와 스페인과 두 바다를 내려다볼 수 있었다. 이 높은 곳에서 그들은 다시 고된 길을 따라 깊은 골짜기로 내려갔다. 이 골짜기 한가운데에 외따로 솟은 산 하나가 있었는데, 거칠고 깎아지른 바위로 이루어져 있었으며, 그 꼭대기에는 청동 벽으로 둘러싸인 성이 있었다. 브루넬로가 말했다. \"저기가 그 마법사가 자기 포로들을 가두어 두는 요새요. 저곳에 오르려면 날개가 있어야 하니, 이 성의 주인에게는 날개 달린 말의 도움이 필요할 수밖에 없음을 쉽게 알 수 있소. 그는 이 성을 자기 감옥으로도, 자기 거처로도 쓰지요.\"\n\n충분히 일러 받은 브라다만테는 이제 반지를 손에 넣을 때가 왔음을 알았다. 그러나 그녀는 무방비한 사람을 죽이기로 결심할 수가 없었다. 그녀는 브루넬로가 알아차리기 전에 그를 붙잡아 나무에 묶고는, 그가 손가락 하나에 끼고 있던 반지를 빼앗았다. 그 음흉한 사라센인의 외침과 애원도 그녀의 마음을 움직이지 못했다. 그녀는 성이 서 있는 바위 발치로 나아가, 마법사를 싸움으로 끌어내려고 자기 뿔나팔을 불며 도전의 외침을 더했다.\n\n마법사는 지체 없이 날개 달린 말을 타고 모습을 드러냈다. 브라다만테는 그토록 무시무시하다고 묘사된 이 인물이 창도 곤봉도, 그 어떤 치명적인 무기도 지니고 있지 않은 것을 보고 기쁨이 섞인 놀라움에 사로잡혔다. 그는 다만 천으로 덮인 방패 하나를 팔에 두르고, 손에는 펼쳐진 책 한 권을 들고 있을 뿐이었다. 그 날개 달린 말로 말하자면, 거기에는 아무런 마법도 없었다. 그것은 리파이아산맥에 사는 어떤 종에 속하는 자연의 짐승이었다. 그리핀처럼 그것은 독수리의 머리에, 발톱으로 무장한 발과 깃털로 덮인 날개를 지녔으며, 몸의 나머지는 말의 것이었다. 이 기이한 짐승은 히포그리프라고 불린다.\n\n여걸은 마법사가 다가오자 격렬한 싸움의 온 기세를 다해 이쪽저쪽으로 내리치며 그를 공격했으나, 베는 것이라곤 허공뿐이었다. 그리고 이 거짓 공격이 한동안 이어진 뒤, 그녀는 마치 걸어서 더 효과적으로 싸우기를 바라는 듯 말에서 내렸다. 이제 마법사는 자기의 유일한 무기를 쓸 채비를 하니, 적의 정신을 빼앗아 굴복시키는 데 결코 실패한 적 없는 그 마법의 방패를 벗기는 것이었다. 자기 반지를 믿은 브라다만테는 적의 모든 움직임을 지켜보다가, 방패의 천이 벗겨지자 마치 방패의 광채에 압도된 척하며 땅바닥에 몸을 던졌으니, 실은 마법사가 말에서 내려 자기에게 다가오도록 유인하기 위함이었다.\n\n일은 그녀의 바람대로 되었다. 마법사는 그녀가 엎어진 것을 보자 자기 말을 땅에 내려앉히고는, 말에서 내려 방패를 안장 앞머리에 고정한 뒤 쓰러진 전사를 붙잡으려고 다가왔다. 그를 골똘히 지켜보던 브라다만테는 그가 가까이 다가온 것을 보자마자 벌떡 일어나 그를 힘껏 붙잡아 쓰러뜨리고는, 마법사가 자기에게 쓰려고 준비해 두었던 바로 그 사슬로 그를 단단히 묶었으니, 그는 어떠한 효과적인 저항도 할 수 없었다. 마법사는 절망의 어조로 외쳤다. \"내 목숨을 가져가오, 젊은이!\" 그러나 브라다만테는 그러한 소원을 들어줄 마음이 전혀 없었다. 그 마법사의 이름과, 그가 무슨 목적으로 그토록 정교하게 이 난공불락의 요새를 지었는지를 알고 싶었던 그녀는, 그에게 알려 달라고 명했다.\n\n\"아아!\" 마법사가 두 뺨에 눈물을 흘리며 대답했다, \"내가 이 성을 지은 것은 약탈품을 숨기기 위함도, 어떤 죄스러운 계획을 위함도 아니오. 그것은 오로지 내가 더없이 다정하게 사랑하는 한 젊은 기사의 목숨을 지키기 위함이었으니, 내 술법이 나에게 일러 주기를 그가 그리스도교도가 되도록, 그리고 그 직후에 가장 시커먼 배신으로 목숨을 잃도록 정해져 있다 하였소.\n\n로제로라는 이름의 이 젊은이는 기사들 가운데 가장 아름답고 가장 출중하오. 그를 어린 시절부터 길러 온 이가 바로 나, 불행한 아틀란테스요. 명예의 부름과 영광에 대한 갈망이 그를 나에게서 떼어 내, 자기 군주 아그라만트를 따라 프랑스 침공에 나서게 했고, 가장 다정한 부모보다도 더 로제로에게 헌신한 나는, 그를 위협하는 잔혹한 운명에서 구해 내려는 희망으로 그를 이 거처로 데려올 방도를 찾았소.\n\n이 목적을 위해 나는 그대에게 쓰려 했던 것과 같은 수단으로 그를 내 수중에 넣었으며, 그 같은 방법으로 수많은 기사와 귀부인들을 내 성에 모으는 데 성공했소. 내 뜻은 내 사랑하는 제자에게 그를 즐겁게 해 줄 벗을 마련해 주어 그의 마음이 전쟁과 영광의 일들로 향하지 않게 함으로써, 그의 사로잡힘을 가벼이 하려는 것이었소. 아아! 내 보살핌은 헛되었구려! 그래도, 청컨대, 내가 가진 다른 무엇이든 가져가되, 내 사랑하는 제자만은 살려 주오. 이 방패를 가지시오, 이 날개 달린 군마를 가지시오, 내 포로들 가운데 그대의 벗들을 찾아내거든 풀어 주시오, 원한다면 그들 모두를 풀어 주시오, 그러나 내게 사랑하는 로제로만은 남겨 주오. 만일 그마저 내게서 낚아채 가려거든, 내 목숨도 함께 가져가시오, 그러면 내 목숨은 더는 내게 보존할 가치가 없을 것이니.\"\n\n브라다만테가 대답했다. \"노인이여, 부질없는 애원으로 나를 움직이려 하지 마시오. 내가 요구하는 것이 바로 그 로제로의 자유요. 그대는 그를 이곳에 속박해 두고 나태한 쾌락에 빠뜨려, 그대가 내다본 운명에서 그를 구하려 하오. 부질없는 노인이여! 그대 자신의 운명도 내다보지 못했으면서 어찌 그의 운명을 내다볼 수 있단 말이오? 그대는 내게 그대의 목숨을 가져가라 하오. 아니오, 내 팔과 내 영혼은 그 청을 거부하오.\" 이렇게 말한 뒤 그녀는 마법사에게 앞장서서 자신을 성으로 인도하라고 명했다. 포로들은 풀려났으나, 그들 가운데 몇몇은 그렇게 끝나 버린 그 향락의 삶을 속으로 아쉬워했다. 브라다만테와 로제로는 기쁨에 겨워 서로를 맞이했다.\n\n그들은 산에서 내려와 격돌이 벌어졌던 곳에 이르렀다. 그곳에서 그들은 마법의 방패를 천에 싸인 채 안장 앞머리에 매단 히포그리프를 발견했다. 브라다만테가 고삐를 붙잡으러 나아가니, 히포그리프는 그녀가 다가오기를 기다리는 듯했으나, 그녀가 닿기 전에 날개를 펴 이웃한 언덕으로 날아가 버렸고, 같은 식으로 두 번째에도 그녀의 노력을 따돌렸다. 로제로와 풀려난 다른 기사들이 그를 붙잡으려고 들판과 언덕 꼭대기로 흩어졌고, 마침내 그 짐승은 로제로가 자기 고삐를 붙잡도록 허락했다. 두려움을 모르는 로제로는 망설임 없이 그 등으로 뛰어올라 박차를 느끼게 했고, 그것이 그 기개를 어찌나 돋우었던지 잠깐 달리던 그는 갑자기 날개를 펴 공중으로 솟아올랐다. 브라다만테는 재회의 바로 그 순간에 자기 연인이 자기에게서 낚아채여 가는 것을 보는 슬픔을 겪었다. 말을 다루는 기술을 알지 못하는 로제로는 그 비행을 통제할 수 없었다. 그는 자신이 산봉우리들 위로, 어디가 땅이고 어디가 물인지 거의 분간할 수 없을 만큼 높이 실려 가는 것을 깨달았다. 히포그리프는 비행을 서쪽으로 잡고는, 가장 상쾌하고 가장 알맞은 바람에 떠밀려 새로 의장을 갖춘 배가 물결을 가르듯 빠르게 공중을 갈랐다.\n\n# ASTOLPHO AND THE ENCHANTRESS\n\n아스톨포와 마녀\n로제로가 히포그리프의 등에 올라 떠난 그 긴 비행에서, 그는 어디로 가는지도 모른 채 땅과 바다 위로 실려 갔다. 그 짐승에 대해 어느 정도 통제력을 얻자마자 그는 그것을 가장 가까운 육지에 내려앉게 했다. 땅에 충분히 가까워졌을 때 로제로는 가볍게 그 등에서 뛰어내려 그 짐승을 도금양나무에 묶었다. 그 자리 가까이에는 삼나무와 종려나무로 둘러싸인 샘의 맑은 물이 흐르고 있었다. 로제로는 방패를 내려놓고 투구를 벗은 뒤, 신선한 공기를 기쁘게 들이마시고 샘물로 입술을 식혔다. 그가 한 그 비행을 생각하면 그가 극도로 지쳐 있었다는 것은 놀랄 일이 아니기 때문이다. 그가 막 휴식의 단맛을 맛보려던 참에, 그는 자기가 고삐로 도금양나무에 묶어 둔 히포그리프가 무언가에 놀라 벗어나려고 격렬하게 발버둥치는 것을 알아차렸다. 그 몸부림이 도금양나무를 어찌나 흔들었던지 그 아름다운 잎사귀들이 많이 뜯겨 나가 땅에 흩어졌다.\n\n타는 나무에서 나는 듯한 소리가 그 도금양나무에서 나오는 듯했으니, 처음에는 희미하고 또렷하지 않더니, 차츰 강해져 마침내 이렇게 말하는 목소리로 들리게 되었다. \"오 기사여, 만일 그대 마음의 다정함이 그대 외모의 아름다움에 걸맞다면, 청컨대 이 괴롭히는 짐승에게서 나를 풀어 주오. 나는 바깥의 고통이 내 신세에 더해지지 않아도 안으로 충분히 괴로워하고 있소.\"\n\n이 목소리의 첫 음절에 로제로는 곧바로 눈을 도금양나무로 돌려 그리로 서둘러 갔고, 그 목소리가 나무 자체에서 나오는 것을 알아차리고는 놀라움에 굳어 버렸다. 그는 곧바로 자기 말을 풀고, 놀라움과 후회로 얼굴이 붉어진 채 외쳤다. \"그대가 누구이든, 필멸의 존재이든 이 숲의 여신이든, 청컨대 나의 본의 아닌 잘못을 용서해 주오. 만일 이 단단한 껍질이 감정을 지닌 존재를 감싸고 있는 줄 짐작이라도 했더라면, 내가 이토록 아름다운 도금양나무를 이 군마의 횡포에 내맡길 수 있었겠소? 하늘과 대기의 감미로운 기운이 내가 끼친 해를 어서 회복시켜 주기를! 나로 말하자면, 내 마음의 주권자이신 여인을 두고 맹세하노니, 그대의 용서를 받기 위해 그대가 바라는 모든 일을 다 하겠소.\"\n\n이 말에 도금양나무는 뿌리에서 줄기까지 떠는 듯했고, 로제로는 마치 불 위에 놓인 통나무에서 스며 나오는 것 같은 눈물 같은 물기가 그 껍질을 따라 흘러내리는 것을 보았다. 그러고는 그것이 말했다.\n\n\"그대의 말이 풍기는 친절함이 나로 하여금 내가 한때 누구였는지, 그리고 어떤 불운으로 이 모습으로 바뀌었는지를 그대에게 밝히지 않을 수 없게 하오. 내 이름은 아스톨포였으니, 그 명성이 온 세상을 가득 채운 오를란도와 리날도의 사촌이었소. 나 자신도 프랑스의 가장 용감한 팔라딘들 가운데 하나로 꼽혔으며, 태생으로는 내 아버지 오토의 뒤를 이어 잉글랜드를 다스릴 권리를 지니고 있었소. 우리의 무력으로 프랑스의 위대한 황제를 돕도록 본국으로 부름받아, 리날도와 다른 여러 용감한 기사들과 함께 머나먼 동방에서 돌아오던 길에, 우리는 강력한 마녀 알치나가 바닷가에 성 하나를 소유하고 있는 곳에 이르렀소. 그녀는 낚시로 소일하려고 물가에 나와 있었고, 우리는 그녀가 자기 술법으로 낚싯바늘도 낚싯줄도 없이 물에서 무엇이든 마음대로 끌어 올리는 것을 구경하려고 걸음을 멈췄소.\n\n물가에서 멀지 않은 곳에 거대한 고래 한 마리가 너무도 넓고 미동도 없는 등을 드러내고 있어 마치 섬처럼 보였소. 알치나는 나에게 눈길을 고정하고는 나를 자기 손아귀에 넣을 궁리를 했소. 그녀가 우리에게 말하기를, '바로 지금이 바다에서 가장 어여쁜 인어가 매일 정해진 때에 저 섬 기슭으로 오는 시각이오. 그 인어는 너무도 감미롭게 노래하여 그 소리에 물결마저 더 잔잔히 흐른다오. 그 노래를 듣고 싶거든 나와 함께 그 인어가 머무는 곳으로 갑시다.' 그렇게 말하며 알치나는 그 물고기를 가리켰는데, 우리는 모두 그것을 섬이라 여겼소. 무모했던 나는 망설임 없이 그녀를 따라갔으니, 말을 헤엄쳐 건너가 그 물고기의 등에 올랐소. 리날도와 두돈이 헛되이 나에게 조심하라는 신호를 보냈으나, 알치나는 미소 지으며 나를 떠맡고 앞장서 갔소. 우리가 그 위에 오르자마자 고래는 큰 지느러미를 펼치며 움직이기 시작해 빠르게 물을 갈랐소. 그제야 나는 내 어리석음을 깨달았으나, 후회하기에는 너무 늦었소. 알치나는 내 분노를 달래며, 자기가 한 일은 나를 사랑하기 때문이었다고 공언했소. 머잖아 우리는 이 섬에 이르렀고, 처음에는 나를 내 신세에 만족하게 하고 내 나날을 행복하게 보내게 하려고 온갖 일이 행해졌소. 그러나 곧 알치나는 자기 정복에 싫증이 나 무관심해지더니, 나에게 진력이 났고, 마침내 나를 떼어 버리려고 나를 이 모습으로 바꾸어 버렸소. 그녀는 나보다 앞서 수많은 연인에게도 그리했으니, 그들 가운데 더러는 올리브나무로, 더러는 종려나무로, 더러는 삼나무로 만들고, 또 다른 이들은 샘이나 바위로, 심지어 들짐승으로 바꾸어 버렸소. 그러니 그대, 우연히 이 마법의 섬으로 오게 된 정중한 기사여, 그녀가 그대에 대해 힘을 얻지 못하도록 조심하오, 그러지 않으면 그대도 어쩌면 우리처럼 나무나 샘이나 바위가 되고 말 것이오.\"\n\n로제로는 이 이야기에 자기 놀라움을 표했다. 아스톨포는 그 섬의 태반이 알치나의 지배를 받고 있다고 덧붙였다. 그녀는 자기 자매 모르가나의 도움으로 또 다른 자매 로제스틸라에게서 그 세습 재산의 거의 전부를 빼앗는 데 성공했으니, 그 섬 전체가 본래 아버지의 유언에 따라 로제스틸라의 것이었기 때문이다. 그러나 로제스틸라는 절제 있고 현명했던 반면, 다른 두 자매는 거짓되고 음탕했다. 로제스틸라의 영토는 만(灣)과 산줄기로 그들의 영토와 갈라져 있었는데, 지금까지 그것만이 그 자매가 로제스틸라의 영토를 찬탈하는 것을 막아 왔다.\n\n아스톨포는 여기서 자기 이야기를 마쳤고, 그가 브라다만테의 사촌임을 안 로제로는 기꺼이 그를 구할 어떤 방도를 궁리하고 싶었으나, 그것이 자기 능력 밖의 일인지라, 할 수 있는 한 그를 위로하고는, 로제스틸라의 궁전으로 가는 길과 알치나의 궁전을 피하는 법을 일러 달라고 청했다. 아스톨포는 그에게 왼쪽 길을 택하라고 일러 주었으니, 그것은 비록 험하고 바위투성이였다. 그는 이 길이 심각한 장애들을 내놓으리라고, 곧 괴물들의 무리가 그의 통행을 막으리라고 경고했으니, 그것들은 알치나가 자기 신민들이 자기 영토에서 달아나는 것을 막으려고 술법으로 부리는 것들이었다. 로제로는 도금양나무에게 고마움을 표하고는 길을 떠날 채비를 했다.\n\n그는 처음에는 날개 달린 말에 올라 그 등에 탄 채 산을 오를까 생각했으나, 그를 다스릴 자기 능력이 너무도 미덥지 못해 또 한 번 공중을 비행하는 위험을 무릅쓰고 싶지 않았고, 게다가 먹을 것이 없어 거의 굶주린 상태였다. 그리하여 그는 말을 뒤에 이끌고 걸어서 길을 떠났는데, 그 길은 얼마간은 두 자매의 영토 양쪽으로 똑같이 이어져 있었다.\n\n그가 채 2마일도 더 나아가기 전에 그는 자기 앞에 알치나의 화려한 도시를 보았다. 그 도시는 황금 벽으로 둘러싸여 있어 하늘에 닿을 듯이 보였다. 어떤 이들은 그 벽이 진짜 황금이 아니라 단지 연금술의 산물일 뿐이라고 생각하는 줄 나도 안다. 그러나 그것은 중요치 않으니, 나는 그것을 황금이라 여기는 편이 좋다. 그것이 분명 황금처럼 빛났기 때문이다.\n\n넓고 평탄한 길이 도시의 성문으로 이어졌고, 이 길에서 또 하나의 길이 갈라져 나왔는데, 좁고 험한 그 길은 산악 지대로 이어졌다. 로제로는 망설임 없이 좁은 길을 택했으나, 그 길에 들어서자마자 그의 통행을 막는 한 무리의 떼거리에게 습격당했다.\n\n그대는 이 도깨비 무리만큼 우스꽝스럽고 기괴한 것을 일찍이 본 적이 없을 것이다. 그것들 가운데 더러는 목에서 발까지는 사람의 모습이었으나 머리는 원숭이나 고양이의 것이었고, 다른 것들은 말의 다리와 귀를 지녔으며, 대머리에 흉측한 늙은이와 늙은 여자들이 짐승의 텁수룩한 가죽을 반쯤 걸친 채 마치 제정신이 아닌 듯 이리저리 내달렸다. 하나는 고삐 없는 말을 타고 전속력으로 달렸고, 또 하나는 나귀나 소를 타고 느릿느릿 갔으며, 다른 것들은 민첩하기 그지없게 깡충깡충 뛰어다니며 제 동료들이 탄 짐승들의 꼬리와 갈기에 매달렸다. 어떤 것들은 뿔나팔을 불었고, 다른 것들은 술잔을 휘둘렀으며, 어떤 것들은 꼬챙이로, 어떤 것들은 쇠스랑으로 무장했다. 우두머리로 보이는 하나는 엄청나게 큰 배와 살찐 큰 머리를 지녔으며, 거북 한 마리를 타고 있었는데, 그 거북은 어느 한 방향도 지키지 못한 채 이리저리 뒤뚱거렸다.\n\n이 괴물들 가운데 하나가, 비록 목과 귀와 주둥이는 개의 것이었으나 사람의 모습에 어느 정도 가까웠던 그것이, 로제로에게 사납게 짖어 대며 그를 오른쪽으로 돌려 그 화려한 도시로 가는 길로 다시 들어서게 하려 했다. 그러나 그 용감한 기사는 외쳤다. \"이 검을 쓸 수 있는 한, 나는 그러지 않을 것이다.\" 그러고는 곧장 그것의 얼굴에 칼끝을 들이밀었다. 그 괴물이 창으로 그를 치려 했으나, 로제로가 너무 빨라, 그 몸에 검을 찔러 넣으니 칼끝이 그 등 뒤로 손 한 뼘만큼 튀어나왔다. 이제 분노를 마음껏 터뜨린 팔라딘은 그 천한 무리 사이에서 힘차게 칼을 휘둘러, 하나는 이빨까지, 또 하나는 허리띠까지 쪼개 버렸다. 그러나 그 무리가 어찌나 많은지, 그의 타격에도 불구하고 그를 너무도 바짝 에워싸는 통에, 길을 트려면 그는 브리아레오스만큼이나 많은 팔을 지녀야 했을 것이다.\n\n만일 로제로가 자기 안장 앞머리에 매달려 있던 마법사의 방패를 벗겼더라면, 그는 이 괴물 같은 패거리를 쉽게 무찔렀을 것이다. 그러나 어쩌면 그는 그것을 생각지 못했거나, 어쩌면 자기의 훌륭한 검 말고는 어디에서도 방어를 구하기를 원치 않았던 것이리라. 그의 당혹이 절정에 이른 그 순간, 그는 도시의 성문에서 두 젊은 미인이 나오는 것을 보았으니, 그들의 풍채와 옷차림이 그들의 신분과 고귀한 가문 출신임을 알려 주었다. 그들은 각자 일각수 한 마리를 타고 있었는데, 그 일각수의 흰빛은 흰담비의 것보다도 더 희었다. 그들은 로제로가 그토록 용맹하게 도깨비들을 상대로 싸우고 있던 풀밭으로 다가왔고, 도깨비들은 그들이 다가오자 모두 물러갔다. 그들은 가까이 와서 그 젊은 전사에게 손을 내밀었으니, 그의 두 뺨은 격렬한 운동과 수줍음의 홍조로 달아올라 있었다. 그들의 도움에 고마워한 그는 감사를 표했고, 그들을 거절할 마음이 없어 그들의 안내를 따라 도시의 성문으로 갔다.\n\n이 웅장하고 아름다운 입구는 모두 금강석으로 된 네 개의 거대한 기둥으로 이루어진 주랑(柱廊)으로 장식되어 있었다. 그것들이 진짜 금강석인지 인공의 것인지 나는 말할 수 없다. 그것들이 눈에는 금강석처럼 보였고, 그보다 더 화려하고 찬란할 수 없었으니, 그것이 무슨 상관이겠는가.\n\n문턱 위, 그리고 기둥들 사이에는 한 무리의 매혹적인 젊은 여인들이 보였는데, 그들은 함께 놀며 장난치고 있었다. 그들 모두가 달려 나와 로제로를 맞이하고는, 그를 궁전 안으로 인도했으니, 그 궁전은 마치 낙원 같아 보였다.\n\n우리는 이 거처를 그 이름으로 부를 만하였으니, 그곳에서는 시간이 헤아릴 수도 없이, 언제나 새로운 즐거움 속에서 흘러갔다. 포만이나 결핍, 무엇보다도 늙음이라는 생각조차 그곳 주민들의 마음에는 결코 들어선 적이 없었다. 그들은 사치와 환락 말고는 어떤 감각도 느끼지 못했고, 행복의 잔은 그들을 위해 끊임없이 흘러넘쳐 마를 줄 모르는 듯했다. 로제로가 도깨비들에게서 구출된 것을 빚진 그 두 젊은 처녀는 그를 자기 여주인의 거처로 인도했다. 아름다운 알치나가 나아와, 위엄과 정중함을 함께 갖춘 태도로 그를 맞이했다. 그녀의 온 궁정이 팔라딘을 에워싸고 그에게 더없이 아첨하는 환대를 베풀었다. 그 성은 그 웅장함보다 그곳에 사는 이들의 매력으로 더 감탄할 만했다. 그들은 남녀 양성으로, 아름다움과 젊음과 우아함에서 잘 어울렸으나, 이 매혹적인 무리 가운데서도 눈부신 알치나는 마치 해가 별들을 무색하게 하듯 빛났다. 그 젊은 전사는 매혹되었다. 그가 도금양나무에게서 들은 모든 것이 그에게는 한낱 비열한 중상으로만 보였다. 거짓과 배신이 미소와 순진한 진실의 모습 아래 자신을 가린다는 것을 어찌 그가 의심할 수 있었겠는가? 그는 아스톨포가 자기 운명을, 어쩌면 더 가혹한 벌을 마땅히 받았으리라고 의심치 않았다. 그는 그의 모든 이야기를 좌절한 마음과 복수에 대한 갈망에서 비롯된 것으로 여겼다. 그러나 우리는 로제로를 너무 가혹하게 비난해서는 안 되니, 그는 마법의 힘의 희생자였기 때문이다.\n\n그들이 식탁에 자리를 잡자마자, 조화로운 리라와 하프가 더없이 황홀한 가락으로 대기를 일깨웠다. 흥겨운 이야기들로 시(詩)의 매력이 더해졌고, 그 잔치의 화려함은 왕가의 식탁에 견주어도 손색이 없었을 것이다. 그 배신자 여인은 팔라딘을 매혹하여 그곳에 붙들어 둘 만한 그 무엇도 빠뜨리지 않았으니, 그에게 싫증이 나면 그를 다른 이들에게 했던 것처럼 변신시킬 작정이었다. 같은 식으로 뒤이은 나날들도 흘러갔다. 즐거운 운동 경기와 사냥과 춤, 혹은 전원의 유희가 시간을 빠르게 지나가게 했고, 한편으로는 목욕이나 잠의 상쾌함에 흥취를 더해 주었다.\n\n이리하여 로제로는 카를루스와 아그라만트가 제국을 두고 다투는 동안 안락과 사치의 삶을 살았다. 그러나 사랑스럽고 용감한 브라다만테가 로제로를 되찾으려는 희망으로, 아주 작은 가능성이라도 그녀를 부르는 곳이라면 어디로든 밤낮으로 자신의 불확실한 걸음을 옮기고 있는 동안, 나는 그와 함께 머물러 있을 수 없다.\n\n그러므로 나는 이렇게 말하겠다. 그녀는 들판에서도 도시에서도 그를 헛되이 찾은 끝에, 다음에는 어디로 발걸음을 향해야 할지 알지 못했다. 그녀는 로제로의 죽음을 염려하지는 않았다. 그러한 영웅의 죽음이라면 히다스페스강에서 서방의 가장 먼 강에 이르기까지 메아리쳤을 테니까. 그러나 그가 땅 위에 있는지 공중에 있는지 알지 못한 그녀는, 마지막 수단으로 멀린의 무덤이 든 동굴로 돌아가, 자기가 찾는 대상에 대한 어떤 확실한 인도를 그에게 청하기로 결심했다. 이 생각이 그녀의 마음을 차지하고 있던 동안, 현명한 마녀 멜리사가 갑자기 그녀 앞에 나타났다. 이 덕 있고 자비로운 마법사는 자기 주문으로 로제로가 자기 명예와 자기 군주를 잊은 채 쾌락과 나태 속에 시간을 보내고 있음을 알아냈다. 영웅이 되도록 태어난 자가 비루한 안락 속에 세월을 허비하고, 살아남은 이들의 기억 속에 더럽혀진 명성을 남긴다는 생각을 견딜 수 없었던 그녀는, 그를 덕의 길로 끌어내기 위해 강력한 조치를 써야 함을 깨달았다. 멜리사는 아틀란테스처럼 그 사랑스러운 팔라딘에 대한 애정에 눈멀지 않았으니, 아틀란테스는 오로지 로제로의 목숨을 보존하는 데만 골몰하여 그의 명성 따위는 전혀 개의치 않았던 것이다. 너무도 매혹적인 알치나의 섬으로 히포그리프를 인도한 술법이 바로 그 늙은 마법사의 것이었으니, 그는 그곳에서 자기가 아끼는 자가 명예를 잊고 영광에 대한 사랑을 버리기를 배우기를 바랐던 것이다.\n\n멜리사를 보자 브라다만테의 얼굴에 기쁨이 밝아 올랐고, 그녀의 가슴에 희망이 살아났다. 멜리사는 그녀에게 아무것도 숨기지 않고, 로제로가 알치나의 올가미에 빠져 있음을 이야기했다. 브라다만테는 비탄과 두려움에 잠겼으나, 친절한 마녀가 그녀를 달래고 그녀의 두려움을 흩어 주었으며, 며칠 안에 그 팔라딘을 그녀의 발치로 다시 데려오겠다고 약속했다.\n\n\"내 딸아,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"그대가 끼고 있는, 마법을 무찌르는 힘을 지닌 그 반지를 내게 다오. 그것의 도움으로 나는 거짓된 알치나가 로제로를 가두어 두고 있는 그 요새로 들어가, 그녀를 무찌르고 그를 풀어 주는 데 성공할 수 있으리라고 믿어 의심치 않는다.\" 브라다만테는 망설임 없이 그녀에게 반지를 건네며, 로제로를 위해 최선을 다해 달라고 부탁했다. 그러자 멜리사는 자기 술법으로 칠흑같이 검은, 다만 한 발만은 적갈색인 거대한 말 한 마리를 불러냈다. 이 짐승을 탄 그녀는 너무도 빠르게 달려, 이튿날 아침에는 알치나의 거처에 다다랐다.\n\n그녀는 이곳에서 자신을 늙은 마법사 아틀란테스와 완벽하게 닮은 모습으로 변신시켰으니, 자기 키에 한 뼘을 더하고 온몸을 부풀렸다. 자기 턱은 긴 수염으로 덮고, 온 얼굴에는 주름을 가득 새겼다. 그녀는 또한 그의 목소리와 태도도 흉내 내며, 로제로가 혼자 있을 기회를 엿보았다. 마침내 그녀는 그를 찾아냈으니, 그는 비단과 황금으로 된 화려한 겉옷을 입고, 목에는 보석 목걸이를 두르고, 한때 단련으로 그토록 거칠었던 두 팔은 팔찌로 장식하고 있었다. 그의 풍채와 모든 몸짓이 유약함을 드러냈고, 그는 로제로의 것이라곤 이름밖에 지니고 있지 않은 듯했으니, 그 마녀가 그에 대해 그만한 힘을 얻었던 것이다.\n\n자기의 옛 스승의 모습을 한 멜리사는 엄하고 진지한 얼굴로 그의 앞에 나섰다. \"그렇다면 이것이,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"내 모든 노고의 열매란 말이냐? 내가 너를 곰과 사자의 골수로 먹이고, 너에게 용을 제압하는 법을 가르치고, 헤라클레스처럼 어린 나이에 네 손아귀로 뱀을 목 졸라 죽이게 한 것이, 고작 내 모든 보살핌으로 너를 유약한 아도니스로 만들기 위함이었단 말이냐? 별들을, 아직 따뜻한 짐승들의 섬유 조직을 밤새워 지켜본 것이며, 내가 던진 점괘며, 내가 계산한 출생의 별자리들이, 네가 위대함을 위해 태어났다고 모두 거짓되이 가리켰단 말이냐? 네가 한 비열한 마녀의 노예가 되리라고 누가 믿을 수 있었겠느냐? 오 로제로여, 이 알치나를 알도록 배워라, 그녀의 술법을 이해하고 그것을 무력화하는 법을 배워라. 이 반지를 받아 네 손가락에 끼고, 그녀의 면전으로 돌아가, 그녀의 진짜 매력이 어떤 것인지 네 눈으로 직접 보라.\"\n\n이 말에 로제로는 어쩔 줄 모르고 부끄러워하며 눈을 땅에 떨구었고, 무어라 대답해야 할지 알지 못했다. 멜리사는 그 순간을 붙잡아 그의 손가락에 반지를 끼웠고, 팔라딘은 다시 본래의 자신으로 돌아왔다. 그에게 이 얼마나 청천벽력이었던가! 수치심에 압도된 그는 자기 스승의 눈길을 마주할 엄두를 내지 못했다. 마침내 그가 눈을 들었을 때, 그가 본 것은 그 거룩한 모습이 아니라, 이제 반지의 힘으로 자기 본래의 모습을 드러낸 여사제 멜리사였다. 그녀는 그를 구하러 온 동기들과, 브라다만테의 슬픔과 한탄과, 그를 찾는 그녀의 지칠 줄 모르는 노력에 대해 그에게 이야기했다. \"그 매혹적인 여전사가,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"그대에게 이 반지를 보냈으니, 그것은 모든 마법에 으뜸가는 해독제라오. 만일 자기 마음이 그대를 섬기는 데 더 큰 힘을 지녔더라면, 그녀는 내 손에 자기 마음을 들려 보냈을 것이오.\"\n\n멜리사가 더 말할 필요는 없었다. 알치나에 대한 로제로의 사랑은 한낱 마법의 작품에 지나지 않았던 까닭에, 마법이 거두어지자마자 사라졌고, 이제 그는 그녀에게서 그 악덕 말고는 더 이상 아무것도 보지 못하고, 그녀가 자기에게 안긴 수치심에 대한 원한만을 느끼며, 그만큼 강렬한 증오로 그녀를 미워했다.\n\n그가 알치나를 다시 보았을 때의 놀라움은 그의 분개에 못지않았다. 반지로 그녀의 마법으로부터 무장한 그는 그녀를 본래 모습대로, 곧 추함의 괴물로 보았다. 그녀의 모든 매력은 인공의 것이었고, 진정으로 보자면 차라리 기형이었다. 그녀는 사실 헤카베나 쿠마이의 시빌라보다도 더 늙었으나, 우리 시대가 잃어버린 것이 안타까운 어떤 술법이 그녀로 하여금 매혹적으로 보이게 하고, 젊음의 온갖 매력으로 자신을 치장하게 해 주었던 것이다. 로제로는 이제 이 모든 것을 보았으나, 멜리사의 조언에 따라 자기 놀라움을 감추고, 어떤 핑계를 대어 오래 방치해 두었던 자기 갑옷을 입고, 자기의 믿음직한 검 벨리사르다를 옆구리에 찼으며, 또한 천으로 덮인 아틀란테스의 방패도 챙겼다.\n\n그러고는 그녀의 의심을 사지 않은 채 알치나의 마구간에서 말 한 마리를 골랐다. 그러나 그는 멜리사의 조언에 따라 히포그리프는 남겨 두었으니, 멜리사가 그를 맡아 더 다루기 쉬운 상태로 길들이겠다고 약속했기 때문이다. 그가 택한 말은 아스톨포의 것이었던 라비칸이었다. 그는 멜리사에게 반지를 돌려주었다.\n\n로제로가 멀리 가기도 전에 그는 손목에 매를 앉히고 개 한 마리를 거느린 알치나의 사냥꾼 하나를 만났다. 그 사냥꾼은 힘센 말을 타고 있었으며, 다소 거만한 태도로 팔라딘에게 어디로 그토록 빨리 가느냐고 물으며 대담하게 다가왔다. 로제로는 멈추기도 대답하기도 마다했고, 그러자 그가 달아나려 하는 것이라 확신한 사냥꾼이 말했다. \"내 매로 내가 그대의 길을 멈추게 한다면 어떻겠소?\" 그렇게 말하며 그는 그 새를 날려 보냈는데, 그 빠르기는 라비칸조차 따를 수 없었다. 그러더니 사냥꾼은 자기 말에서 뛰어내렸고, 그 짐승은 입을 벌린 채 화살같이 빠르게 로제로를 뒤쫓았다. 사냥꾼 또한 바람이나 불이 그를 실어 나르는 듯이 달렸고, 개는 빠르기가 라비칸과 맞먹었다. 로제로는 달아나기가 불가능함을 깨닫고 멈춰 서서 자기를 뒤쫓는 자들을 마주했으나, 그의 검은 그러한 적들에게는 쓸모가 없었다. 그 무례한 사냥꾼은 말로 그를 공격하고, 자기가 지닌 유일한 무기인 채찍으로 그를 후려쳤으며, 개는 그의 발을 물어뜯었고, 말은 발굽으로 그에게 달려들었다. 동시에 매는 그의 머리와 라비칸의 머리 위를 날며 발톱과 날개로 그들을 공격했고, 그 통에 말이 겁에 질려 다루기 힘들어지기 시작했다. 그 순간 골짜기에 나팔과 심벌즈 소리가 울려 퍼졌고, 알치나가 추격에 나서도록 자기 군세를 모두 출동시켰음이 분명해졌다. 로제로는 한순간도 허비할 시간이 없음을 느꼈고, 다행히도 자기 목에 매달려 있던 아틀란테스의 방패를 떠올렸다. 그가 그것의 천을 벗기니 그 마력이 놀랍게 작용했다. 사냥꾼과 개와 말이 납작하게 쓰러졌고, 떨리는 매의 날개는 더 이상 그녀를 떠받칠 수 없어, 그녀는 정신을 잃고 땅에 떨어졌다. 그들의 성가심에서 벗어난 로제로는 그들을 혼절 상태로 남겨 둔 채 말을 달려 떠났다.\n\n한편 알치나는 모을 수 있는 모든 군세를 거느리고 추격에 나서 자기 궁전에서 뛰쳐나왔다. 뒤에 남겨진 멜리사는 그 기회를 틈타 반지로 보호받으며 모든 방을 샅샅이 뒤졌다. 그녀는 자기가 발견한 모든 부적과 주문을 하나하나 풀고, 봉인을 깨뜨리고, 형상들을 불태우고, 마법의 매듭들을 풀었다. 그곳에서 그녀는 들판을 가로질러 서둘러 가, 나무나 샘이나 돌이나 짐승으로 바뀐 희생자들의 마법을 풀어 주었고, 그들 모두가 자유를 되찾아 자기들의 구원자에게 영원한 감사를 맹세했다. 그들은 할 수 있는 한 서둘러 선한 로제스틸라의 영토로 달아났고, 그곳에서 다시 저마다의 집으로 떠나갔다.\n\n멜리사가 가장 먼저 풀어 준 이는 아스톨포였으니, 로제로가 특별히 그를 그녀의 보살핌에 부탁해 두었기 때문이다. 그녀는 그가 자기 무구를, 특히 한때 아르갈리아의 것이었던 그 귀중한 황금 머리 창을 되찾도록 도왔다. 마녀는 그와 함께 날개 달린 말에 올랐고, 얼마 지나지 않아 공중을 가로질러 로제스틸라의 성에 다다랐으며, 그곳에서 로제로가 곧 그들과 합류했다.\n[75] 황금시대: 신화에서 인류가 평화와 풍요 속에 살았다고 전하는 태초의 이상적 시대.\n\n이 거처에서 두 친구는 현명한 로게스틸라와 그녀의 덕망 높은 궁정과 더불어 즐겁고 유익한 교류를 나누며 짧은 기간을 보냈다. 그런 다음 각자 길을 떠났다. 로게로는 히포그리프와 반지, 그리고 방패를 챙겼고, 아스톨포는 황금 창을 들고 가장 빠른 준마인 라비칸에 올라탔다. 로게스틸라는 로게로에게 히포그리프를 다스리기에 알맞은 재갈과 고삐를 주었고, 아스톨포에게는 다른 무기가 모두 소용없을 때에만 불어야 하는 경이로운 힘을 지닌 뿔나팔을 주었다.\n\n오르크\n\n우리는 매혹적인 안젤리카가 자신을 두고 다투던 두 연인 사크리판트와 리날도에게서 달아나던 순간에, 그녀가 한 늙은 은자를 만났던 데서 이야기를 멈추었다. 우리는 그녀가 은자에게 청한 것이, 자신이 증오하는 리날도를 피하기 위해 프랑스와 유럽 자체를 떠나 해안에 이를 수 있는 방도를 마련해 달라는 것이었음을 보았다. 거짓 은자는 실은 비열한 마법사에 다름 아니었으며, 안젤리카의 이 계획을 돕는 일이 자신의 거짓 신들의 마음에 들지 않으리라는 것을 잘 알면서도, 그녀의 소원을 들어주는 척했다. 그는 자신의 술법으로 교활한 악마를 안에 들어가게 한 말 한 필을 그녀에게 내어 주었고, 안젤리카를 그 짐승에 태운 뒤 바다에 이르려면 어떤 길을 가야 할지 일러 주었다.\n\n안젤리카는 아무 의심 없이 길을 갔으나, 해안에 다다르자 악마는 그 짐승을 곧장 물속으로 몰아넣었다. 안젤리카가 그를 뭍으로 되돌리려 애썼으나 헛수고였다. 그는 계속 나아가다가 밤이 다가올 무렵, 짐을 진 채 모래 곶에 올라섰다.\n\n안젤리카는 자신이 이 끔찍한 고독 속에 홀로 버려졌음을 깨닫고, 마치 정신을 잃은 듯 두 손을 맞잡고 눈을 하늘로 향한 채 미동도 없이 있다가, 마침내 눈물을 폭포처럼 쏟아 내며 외쳤다. \"잔혹한 운명이여, 너는 아직도 나에 대한 분노를 다 쏟아 내지 못했단 말인가? 어떤 새로운 비참함으로 나를 몰아넣으려는 것이냐? 아아! 그러면 네 일을 끝내 다오! 나를 어느 사나운 짐승의 먹이로 내어 주든, 네가 택한 어떤 운명으로든 나를 끝장내 다오. 내 목숨과 내 비참함을 끝내 준다면 나는 너에게 감사하리라.\" 마침내 슬픔에 지친 그녀는 잠이 들어 모래 위에 엎드려 쓰러졌다.\n\n다음에 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 이야기하기에 앞서, 우리는 이 불행한 여인이 이제 던져진 곳이 어떤 장소였는지를 밝혀야 한다. 아일랜드 해안을 씻는 바다에 에부다라는 섬이 있었는데, 한때 수가 많았던 그곳 주민들은 프로테우스의 분노로 인해 점점 줄어 이제는 얼마 남지 않았다. 이 신은 옛날에 그 땅의 주민들에게서 받곤 했던 통상적인 예우가 어떤 식으로든 소홀해진 데에 노여워했고, 복수를 실행하기 위해 그들을 잡아먹을 오르크라 불리는 무시무시한 바다 괴물을 보냈다. 그 약탈의 공포가 얼마나 컸던지, 섬의 온 백성이 중심 도시에 틀어박혀 오로지 성벽만을 의지해 자신들을 지키려 했다. 이 곤경 속에서 그들은 신탁에 조언을 구했고, 그 나라에서 낼 수 있는 가장 아름다운 처녀를 바쳐 바다 괴물의 분노를 달래라는 지시를 받았다.\n\n그런데 마침 이 무시무시한 신탁이 선포되고, 땅에서 가장 아름다운 처녀들 가운데 괴물에게 바칠 한 사람을 찾으라는 치명적인 명령이 떨어진 바로 그날, 몇몇 선원들이 안젤리카가 있던 해변에 상륙하여, 잠들어 있는 그녀의 그 아름다움을 보았다.\n\n오, 눈먼 우연이여! 인간사에 미치는 너의 힘은 지나치게 큰 것을, 너는 그러면 서로 다른 군주들이 차지하기 위해 서로 무기를 들었던 그 매력을 끔찍한 괴물의 이빨에 내던질 수 있단 말인가? 아아! 사랑스러운 안젤리카는 저 잔혹한 섬사람들의 제물이 될 운명이었다. 여전히 잠든 채로 그녀는 에부다 사람들에게 결박당했고, 배 위로 옮겨진 뒤에야 자신의 처지를 알게 되었다. 바람이 돛을 채워 배를 항구로 빠르게 실어 갔으며, 그녀를 본 모든 이들은 그녀가 의심할 여지 없이 프로테우스 자신이 먹이로 삼고자 골라낸 제물이라는 데 의견을 같이했다. 자신의 잔혹한 운명에 대한 끔찍한 소식이 전해졌을 때 이 불행한 처녀가 지른 비명을, 죽음에 이르는 고뇌를, 하늘 자체에까지 퍼부은 원망을 누가 다 전할 수 있겠는가? 나는 할 수 없으니, 차라리 내 이야기의 더 행복한 대목으로 눈을 돌리고자 한다.\n\n로게로는 로게스틸라의 궁전을 떠나, 날아다니는 자신의 준마를 타고 산봉우리들보다 훨씬 높이 내달았으며, 멜리사가 준 고삐 덕분에 손쉽게 다룰 수 있는 히포그리프에 실려 서쪽으로 나아갔다. 브라다만테를 되찾으려는 마음이 간절했지만, 빠른 비행 가운데 지나치는 수많은 광대한 지역과 인구 많은 나라들의 광경에 기뻐하지 않을 수 없었다. 마침내 그는 잉글랜드 해안에 가까이 다가갔고, 마치 승리의 희망에 들떠 출정하려는 듯, 온갖 군사적 위풍을 갖춘 거대한 군대를 발견했다. 그는 그 광경에서 멀지 않은 곳에 히포그리프를 내려앉게 했고, 곧 호기심과 경이로움을 가눌 줄 모르는 구경꾼들, 곧 기사들과 병사들에게 둘러싸이게 되었다. 로게로는 자신의 물음에 대한 답으로, 눈앞의 저 훌륭한 군대가 그의 숙부인 샤를 왕의 사절로서 명망 높은 리날도가 올린 요청에 응하여, 프랑스 황제를 돕기 위해 떠나기로 정해진 군대임을 알게 되었다.\n\n이 무렵 잉글랜드 기사들의 호기심은 가만히 멈춰 있는 히포그리프를 보는 것으로 어느 정도 채워졌고, 로게로는 그들의 놀라움과 기쁨을 새로이 자아내고자 다시 그 짐승에 올라타, 옆구리에 박차를 가하여 유성 같은 속도로 공중에 솟아오르게 한 뒤, 여전히 서쪽을 향해 비행을 이끌어 마침내 아일랜드 해안이 보이는 데에 이르렀다. 여기서 그는 바다로 튀어나온 바위에 단단히 사슬로 묶인 채 홀로 있는, 아리따운 처녀로 보이는 이를 멀리서 보았다. 가까이 다가가 아름다운 공주 안젤리카임을 알아보았을 때 그의 놀라움이 어떠했겠는가! 그날 그녀는 끌려 나와 바위에 묶였고, 바다 괴물이 와서 자신을 잡아먹을 때까지 거기서 기다리게 되어 있었다. 로게로는 가까이 다가오며 외쳤다. \"어떤 잔인한 손이, 어떤 야만적인 영혼이, 어떤 가혹한 운명이 그대에게 저 사슬을 지웠단 말이오?\" 안젤리카는 처음에는 눈물의 폭포로만 답했고, 그것이 그녀의 유일한 응답이었다. 그러다가 떨리는 목소리로, 자신이 여기 내맡겨진 그 끔찍한 운명을 그에게 털어놓았다. 그녀가 말하는 동안 멀리 바다에서 무시무시한 포효가 들려왔다. 거대한 괴물이 곧 모습을 드러냈는데, 몸의 일부는 물결 위로 솟아 있고 일부는 물속에 잠겨 있었다. 두려움에 반쯤 죽은 안젤리카는 절망에 자신을 내맡겼다.\n\n로게로는 창을 겨눈 채 히포그리프에 박차를 가해 오르크를 향해 돌진하여, 그에게 일격을 가했다. 그 끔찍한 괴물은 자연이 낳은 그 무엇과도 같지 않았다. 그것은 그저 뒤척이고 비틀거리는 몸뚱이 하나의 덩어리였으며, 짐승다운 것이라고는 머리와 눈과 입뿐이었는데, 그 입에는 멧돼지의 것과 같은 엄니가 돋아 있었다. 로게로의 창이 그 두 눈 사이를 찔렀으나, 그 비늘은 바위나 쇠 못지않게 뚫리지 않았다. 기사는 첫 일격이 헛됨을 보고 두 번째 일격을 가하려 채비했다. 그 짐승은 물 위에 비친 히포그리프의 거대한 날개 그림자를 보고는 먹이를 버리고, 더 가까이 있는 듯 보이는 것을 붙잡으려 몸을 돌렸다. 로게로는 그 틈을 타, 살인적인 이빨에서 멀찍이 떨어지도록 조심하면서 그 몸의 여러 곳에 맹렬한 타격을 퍼부었으나, 비늘은 어떤 공격에도 끄떡없었다. 오르크는 꼬리로 물을 후려쳐 거품을 일으켰고, 그 거품이 로게로와 그의 준마를 뒤덮어, 기사는 자신이 물속에 있는지 공중에 있는지조차 거의 분간할 수 없었다. 그는 히포그리프의 날개가 물에 흠뻑 젖어 더는 자신을 떠받치지 못하게 될까 두려워지기 시작했다. 그 순간 로게로는 안장 앞머리에 걸린 마법의 방패를 떠올렸으나, 그 섬광에 안젤리카까지 눈이 멀까 하는 두려움에 그것을 쓰기를 망설였다. 그러다 그는 멜리사가 준 반지를 떠올렸으니, 그 위력은 얼마 전에 몸소 입증한 바였다. 그는 서둘러 안젤리카에게 다가가 그것을 그녀의 손가락에 끼워 주었다. 그런 다음 방패의 덮개를 벗겨, 그 빛나는 원반을 가증스러운 오르크의 얼굴 정면으로 돌렸다. 효과는 즉각적이었다. 괴물은 감각과 운동을 빼앗긴 채 바다 위에서 굴러, 등을 보이며 떠 있었다. 로게로는 이제 드러난 부위에 자신의 창을 시험해 보고 싶었으나, 안젤리카는 괴물이 되살아나기 전에 자신을 사슬에서 풀어 주는 데 한시도 지체하지 말 것을 그에게 애원했다. 그녀의 간청에 마음이 움직인 로게로는 서둘러 그렇게 했고, 그녀를 풀어 준 뒤 히포그리프에서 자신의 뒤에 태웠다. 그 짐승은 땅을 박차고 공중으로 솟구쳐 그 사이를 빠르게 내달았다. 로게로는 공주가 그 잔혹한 동요에서 벗어나 쉴 틈을 주려고, 곧 다시 땅을 찾아 브르타뉴 해안에 내려앉았다. 해안 가까이에는 새들의 노래가 울려 퍼지는 울창한 숲이 펼쳐져 있었다. 그 한가운데에는 맑은 물의 샘이 작은 풀밭의 잔디를 적시고 있었다. 가까이에는 완만한 언덕이 솟아 있었다. 로게로는 히포그리프를 풀밭에 내려앉게 하고는 말에서 내린 뒤, 안젤리카를 말에서 내려 주었다.\n\n격정의 첫 동요가 가라앉았을 때 안젤리카는 눈을 아래로 떨구다가, 자신의 손가락에 끼워진 그 귀한 반지를 보았다. 그 효력을 그녀는 잘 알고 있었으니, 그것은 바로 사라센인 브루넬로가 그녀에게서 빼앗아 갔던 그 반지였다. 그녀는 손가락에서 그것을 빼어 입에 넣었고, 우리가 말로 옮기는 것보다도 빠르게 그 무사의 시야에서 사라졌다.\n\n로게로는 미친 사람처럼 사방을 둘러보았으나, 곧 자신이 얼마 전 그녀의 손가락에 끼워 준 반지를 떠올렸다. 자신의 봉사에 이렇게 보답하는 배은망덕함에 충격을 받은 그가 외쳤다. \"은혜를 모르는 미인이여, 그러면 이것이 그대가 내게 하는 보답이오? 내 반지를 선물로 받느니 차라리 내게서 빼앗기를 택한단 말이오? 그대가 청하기만 했어도 나는 기꺼이 그것을 주었을 것이오.\" 이렇게 말하며 그는 마치 눈먼 사람처럼 두 팔을 뻗고 사방을 더듬으며, 시야에서 사라진 것을 손길로나마 되찾기를 바랐으나, 헛되이 찾았을 뿐이다. 그 잔인한 미인은 이미 멀리 가 있었다.\n\n자신을 구해 준 이에게 진 신세를 잘 알고 있었으나, 그녀에게 가장 먼저 필요한 것은 옷과 음식과 휴식이었다. 그녀는 곧 양치기의 오두막에 이르렀고, 눈에 띄지 않게 들어가 당장의 어려움을 덜어 줄 만한 것을 찾아냈다. 그 오두막에는 한 늙은 목자가 살고 있었는데, 그가 돌보는 것이라고는 한 무리의 암말 떼였다. 휴식으로 기운을 회복한 안젤리카는 그 무리에서 암말 한 마리를 골라 그 짐승에 올라탔고, 동방의 고향으로 돌아가고픈 마음이 다시 일어남을 느꼈다. 그 목적을 위해 그녀는 자신과 고향을 갈라놓는 저 광대한 지역들을 가로지르는 동안 오를란도나 사크리판트의 보호를 기꺼이 받았으리라. 그 둘 중 누구라도 만나기를 바라며 그녀는 길을 갔다.\n\n한편 로게로는 안젤리카를 다시 볼 수 없으리라는 절망에 빠진 채, 날개 달린 말을 두고 떠났던 나무로 돌아갔으나, 그 짐승이 고삐를 끊고 달아나 버린 것을 발견하는 분함을 맛보았다. 이 상실은 앞서의 실망에 더해져 그를 짜증에 휩싸이게 했다. 그는 슬프게도 자신의 무기를 거두어 방패를 어깨에 둘러메고, 눈에 띈 첫 길로 들어서, 이내 빽빽하고 넓게 펼쳐진 숲의 언저리 안에 자신이 있음을 알게 되었다.\n\n얼마쯤 나아갔을 때 그는 오른편에서 소리를 들었고, 주의 깊게 귀 기울여 무기 부딪치는 소리를 분간해 냈다. 그는 그 소리가 나는 곳을 향해 길을 잡았고, 죽기를 각오한 결투에 몰두한 두 전사를 발견했다. 그중 하나는 고귀하고 늠름한 풍채의 기사였고, 다른 하나는 사나운 거인이었다. 기사는 거인의 육중한 곤봉에 맞서 스스로를 지키며 더할 나위 없는 솜씨를 발휘하는 듯했으니, 그 타격을 피하거나 칼이나 방패로 막아 냈다. 로게로는 그 결투의 구경꾼으로 서 있었으니, 은밀한 감정이 그를 강하게 기사 편으로 기울게 했음에도 끼어들기를 스스로에게 허락하지 않았던 것이다. 마침내 그는 육중한 곤봉이 기사의 머리 위로 곧장 떨어지는 것을 비통하게 보았고, 기사는 그 일격에 굴복하여 엎어져 쓰러졌다. 거인은 그를 끝장내려 앞으로 뛰어들었고, 그 목적으로 투구의 끈을 풀었으니, 그때 로게로는 경악하며 브라다만테의 얼굴을 알아보았다. 그는 큰 소리로 \"멈춰라, 악당아!\" 하고 외치며 칼을 빼 들고 앞으로 뛰어들었다. 그러자 거인은 마치 또 다른 결투에 들어설 마음이 없는 듯, 브라다만테를 어깨에 둘러메고 그녀를 안은 채 숲속으로 달려갔다.\n\n로게로는 그를 뒤쫓아 뛰어들었으나, 거인의 긴 다리가 그를 너무도 빠르게 앞으로 실어 가서 그 무사는 그를 시야에 두기조차 힘들었다. 마침내 그들은 숲에서 빠져나왔고, 로게로는 앞에 대리석으로 지어진 화려한 궁전을 보았으니, 거장의 솜씨로 새겨진 조각들로 장식되어 있었다. 황금 문을 지나 이 건물 안으로 거인이 들어갔고, 로게로도 뒤따랐다. 그러나 둘러보니 거인도 브라다만테도 어디에서도 보이지 않았다. 그는 이 방 저 방을 뛰어다니며, 자신의 비겁한 적에게 돌아서서 자신을 맞으라고 큰 소리로 외쳤으나, 아무 응답도 얻지 못했고 거인이나 그 먹이를 다시는 한 번도 보지 못했다. 그 헛된 추격 가운데 그는 그들을 알아보지 못한 채 페라우, 플로리스마르트, 그라다소 왕, 오를란도, 그리고 그 밖의 많은 이들을 만났으니, 그들 모두는 자신처럼 이 마법의 성에 함정에 빠진 이들이었다. 그것은 로게로를 자신의 손아귀에 끌어들이고, 어떤 식으로든 자신의 안전을 위협할 만한 자들까지 잡아 두려는 마법사 아틀란테스의 새로운 책략이었다. 로게로가 브라다만테로 여긴 것은 한낱 환영에 불과했다. 그 매혹적인 여인은 멀리 떨어진 곳에서, 오래도록 그 도착을 기다려 온 자신의 로게로를 향한 근심으로 가득 차 있었다.\n\n황제는 그녀에게 마르세유의 도시와 수비대를 맡겼고, 그녀는 그 임무를 용맹과 분별로 이교도들에 맞서 지켜 냈다. 어느 날 멜리사가 그녀 앞에 불쑥 나타났다. 그녀의 물음을 앞질러 멜리사가 말했다. \"로게로 때문에 두려워하지 마시오. 그는 살아 있고, 여느 때와 다름없이 그대에게 충실하오. 다만 그는 자유를 잃었소. 그 잔혹한 마법사가 다시금 그를 포로로 만드는 데 성공했소. 그를 구하고자 한다면, 말에 올라 나를 따르시오.\" 그녀는 아틀란테스가 어떻게 로게로를 속였는지, 곧 그의 눈을 위험에 처한 그녀 자신의 환영으로 현혹했는지 그녀에게 일러 주었다. \"그대의 경우에도,\" 멜리사가 이어 말했다. \"그대가 숲을 뚫고 들어가 그 성에 다가가면, 그자의 술법도 그와 같을 것이오. 그대는 로게로를 본다고 여기겠으나, 실은 그 마법사 자신을 볼 뿐이오. 속지 말고, 그대의 칼을 그자의 몸에 찔러 넣으시오. 그리고 내 말을 믿으시오. 그자를 죽임으로써 그대는 로게로뿐 아니라 그와 더불어, 그 마법사의 술법이 자신들의 군주의 진영에서 빼낸 프랑스의 가장 용맹한 기사들 다수까지 되찾게 될 것이오.\"\n\n브라다만테는 즉시 무장하고 말에 올랐다. 멜리사는 들판과 숲을 지나 강행군으로 그녀를 이끌었으며, 자신의 청자가 가장 관심을 두는 주제에 관한 이야기로 길을 달랬다. 마침내 그들이 숲에 이르자, 멜리사는 다시 한 번 자신의 지침을 되풀이한 뒤, 마법사가 자신을 알아채고 경계하게 될까 두려워 작별을 고했다.\n\n브라다만테가 두 마일쯤 말을 달렸을 때, 갑자기 그녀의 눈에 비친 대로 로게로가 두 사나운 거인에게 호되게 몰리고 있는 것이 보였다. 그녀가 머뭇거리는 사이 그가 자신에게 도움을 청하는 목소리가 들려왔다. 그 순간 멜리사의 경고는 무게를 잃었다. 자신의 친절한 조언자의 신의와 진실함에 대한 갑작스러운 의심이 그녀의 마음을 스쳤다. \"내 눈과 귀를 믿어서는 안 된단 말인가?\" 그녀는 이렇게 말하며 그를 지키려 앞으로 돌진했다. 로게로는 거인들에게 쫓기며 달아났고, 브라다만테가 뒤따라, 그들과 함께 성문을 지났다. 그곳에 이르자 브라다만테는 미혹에서 풀려났으니, 거인도 기사도 보이지 않았던 것이다. 그녀는 자신이 포로가 되었음을 깨달았으나, 자신이 사랑하는 이의 갇힘을 함께 나눈다는 위안조차 알지 못했다. 그녀는 여러 모습의 남자들과 여자들을 보았으나, 그들 가운데 누구도 알아볼 수 없었고, 그녀에 관해서도 그들의 처지는 마찬가지였다. 저마다 다른 이들을 어떤 환상의 미혹 아래 보아, 거인이나 난쟁이, 심지어 네발짐승의 모습을 띠고 있었으니, 그들 사이에는 어떤 교제도 소통도 없었다.\n\n# ASTOLPHO’S ADVENTURES CONTINUED, AND ISABELLA’S BEGUN\n\n이어지는 아스톨포의 모험, 그리고 시작되는 이사벨라의 모험\n아스톨포가 잔혹한 알키나에게서 벗어나, 덕망 높은 로게스틸라의 왕국에 잠시 머문 뒤, 그는 고국으로 돌아가기를 바랐다. 로게스틸라는 그를 본토로 실어다 줄 자신의 함대 가운데 가장 좋은 배를 빌려주었다. 헤어지면서 그녀는 그에게 온갖 종류의 마법을 이겨 내는 비밀을 가르쳐 주는 놀라운 책 한 권을 주었고, 자신을 생각해서 그것을 늘 지니고 다니기를 청했다. 그녀는 또 다른 선물도 주었으니, 그것은 인간의 솜씨로 빚을 수 있는 그런 종류의 모든 것을 능가하는 것이었다. 그러나 겉보기에는 한낱 단순한 뿔나팔에 지나지 않았다.\n\n이 선물들로 보호받게 된 아스톨포는 선량한 요정에게 감사하고 그녀에게 작별을 고한 뒤, 프랑스로 돌아가는 길에 올랐다. 그의 항해는 순조로웠고, 바라던 항구에 이르자 그는 충실한 뱃사람들에게 작별을 고하고 육로로 여정을 이어 갔다. 산을 넘고 골짜기를 지나는 동안 그는 도적 떼와 사나운 짐승들, 독을 품은 뱀들을 자주 만났으나, 그저 자신의 뿔나팔을 불기만 하면 그들 모두를 달아나게 할 수 있었다.\n\n프랑스에 상륙하여 군대로 가는 길에 여러 지방을 가로지른 그는, 어느 날 숲을 건너다가 샘 곁에 이르러 물을 마시려 말에서 내렸다. 그가 샘에 몸을 굽히고 있는 동안, 한 젊은 시골뜨기가 덤불에서 튀어나와 라비칸에 올라타 달아났다. 그것은 마법사 아틀란테스의 새로운 술책이었다. 그 소리를 들은 아스톨포는 자신의 말을 잃는 것을 보기에 꼭 알맞게 고개를 돌렸고, 벌떡 일어나 그 도둑을 뒤쫓았다. 도둑은 자기 쪽에서는 말을 전속력으로 몰지 않고, 둘 다 숲을 빠져나올 때까지 그저 추격자의 시야에 머물러 있을 만큼만 달렸다. 그런 다음 라비칸과 그 기수는 가까이 서 있던 어느 성으로 몸을 피했다. 아스톨포가 뒤따라, 어려움 없이 성의 안뜰 안으로 들어가 기수와 그 말을 찾으려 둘러보았으나, 어느 쪽의 흔적도 볼 수 없었고, 물어볼 만한 사람도 보이지 않았다. 자신을 곤경에 빠뜨리려 마법이 쓰이고 있음을 의심한 그는 자신의 책을 떠올렸고, 그것을 살펴보고는 자신의 의심이 근거 있는 것임을 알아냈다. 그는 또한 어떤 길을 따라야 할지도 알게 되었다. 그는 문지방 노릇을 하는 돌을 들어 올리라는 지시를 받았으니, 그 아래에는 한 정령이 갇혀 있어 기꺼이 빠져나가 성을 누구나 드나들 수 있게 비워 둘 터였다. 아스톨포는 그 돌을 옆으로 들어내는 데 자신의 힘을 쏟았다. 그러자 마법사가 자신의 술법을 발휘했다. 성에는 포로들이 가득했는데, 마법사는 그들 모두에게 아스톨포가 어떤 거짓된 모습으로 보이도록 했으니, 어떤 이에게는 사나운 짐승으로, 어떤 이에게는 거인으로, 어떤 이에게는 맹금으로 보이게 했다. 그리하여 모두가 그를 덮쳤고, 만약 그가 자신의 뿔나팔을 떠올리지 않았더라면 이내 그를 끝장내고 말았을 것이다. 그가 한 차례 나팔을 불자마자, 그 끔찍한 경보 소리에 기사들과 그들과 더불어 마법사가, 마치 새 사냥꾼의 총소리에 비둘기 떼가 흩어지듯 달아났다. 그러자 아스톨포는 다시 돌에 힘을 쏟아 그것을 뒤집었다. 그 아랫면은 온통 마법의 문자로 새겨져 있었는데, 기사는 자신의 책이 일러 준 대로 그것을 지워 버렸다. 그가 그렇게 하자마자, 그 성은 성벽과 작은 탑들과 더불어 연기 속으로 사라졌다.\n\n풀려난 기사들과 귀부인들 가운데에는 로게로와 브라다만테 외에도 오를란도, 그라다소, 플로리스마르트, 그리고 그 밖의 많은 이들이 있었다. 뿔나팔 소리에 라비칸을 빼고는, 사람이며 준마며 너 나 할 것 없이 모두가 달아났으나, 아스톨포는 자신의 공포에도 불구하고 라비칸을 붙들었다. 소리가 그치자마자 로게로는 브라다만테를 알아보았으니, 그는 갇혀 있는 동안 날마다 그녀를 마주쳤으나 마법사의 술법으로 알아보지 못했던 것이다. 그들이 서로를 알아보고, 헤어진 이후 각자에게 일어난 모든 일을 서로 이야기하던 그 기쁨은 어떤 말로도 다 전할 수 없다. 로게로는 그 기회를 틈타 자신의 구애를 밀어붙였고, 단 하나의 장애, 곧 그들의 신앙의 차이만 아니었다면, 브라다만테가 바랄 수 있는 만큼이나 호의적임을 알게 되었다. \"만약 그가 자신을 아내로 얻고자 한다면,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그는 정식으로 자신의 아버지 에이몬 공작에게 자신을 청해야 하며, 자신의 거짓 예언자를 버리고 그리스도교도가 되어야 한다.\" 후자의 조처는 로게로가 자신만의 이유로 얼마 전부터 취하려고 작정해 온 것이었다. 그리하여 그는 그 조건을 기꺼이 받아들였고, 멀지 않은 곳에 탑들이 보이는 발롬브로사 수도원으로 곧장 가자고 제안했다. 그들은 그쪽으로 말머리를 돌렸고, 우리는 그들이 우리의 동행 없이 길을 찾아가도록 두고 떠나기로 한다.\n\n로게로가 게걸스러운 오르크로부터 안젤리카를 막 구해 낸 순간, 그 도도한 미인이 자신의 반지를 입에 넣고 시야에서 사라졌던 것을 내 독자들이 기억하는지 나는 모르겠다. 같은 때에 히포그리프는 고삐를 떨쳐 버리고 솟구쳐 올라 자신의 옛 주인에게 돌아가려 날아갔으니, 아주 자연스럽게도 늘 익숙하던 마구간으로 돌아간 것이었다. 여기서 아스톨포는 그를 발견하고 크게 기뻐했다. 그는 로게로가 그 짐승을 타는 것을 본 적이 있어 그 능력을 알고 있었고, 온 땅을 두루 날아다니며 공중의 항로에서 갖가지 나라와 백성들을 보고 싶어 했다. 그는 로게스틸라가 그 짐승을 어떻게 다루어야 하는지 일러 주는 것을 들었고, 그녀가 그 머리에 고삐를 맞추는 것을 보았다. 그리하여 그는 마구간에서 찾아낸 모든 고삐 가운데 알맞은 것을 골라낼 수 있었고, 라비칸의 안장을 히포그리프의 등에 얹으니, 그가 즉시 떠나는 것을 막을 만한 것은 아무것도 없어 보였다. 그러나 떠나기에 앞서 그는 라비칸을 안전한 곳에, 그리고 필요할 때 되찾을 수 있는 곳에 맡겨 둘 생각을 했다. 어디서 전령을 찾아야 할지 궁리하며 서 있는 동안, 그는 브라다만테가 다가오는 것을 보았다. 그 아리따운 여전사는 발롬브로사 수도원으로 가는 길에, 기사를 불러낸 때아닌 모험으로 인해 로게로와 헤어진 터였다. 그녀는 이제 몬탈반으로 돌아가는 길이었으니, 거기서 로게로와 만나기로 약속해 둔 참이었다. 그리하여 아스톨포는 자신의 아리따운 사촌 브라다만테에게 라비칸을, 그리고 그의 공중 여행에서는 짐만 될 뿐인 황금 창도 맡겼다. 브라다만테는 둘 다 떠맡았고, 아스톨포는 그녀에게 작별을 고하고 공중으로 솟아올랐다.\n\n아스톨포가 마법사의 성에서 풀어 준 이들 가운데에는 오를란도가 있었다. 우연의 인도를 따라 그 무사는 하루가 저물 무렵 어느 숲속에 있는 자신을 발견했고, 한 산기슭에 멈춰 섰다. 바위틈에서 새어 나오는 빛을 알아보고 놀란 그는 그 빛줄기에 이끌려 다가갔고, 깊은 동굴로 이어지는 산허리의 좁은 통로를 발견했다.\n\n오를란도는 자신의 말을 매어 두고, 통로를 막아선 덤불을 헤치며 바위에서 바위로 내려가 일종의 동굴에 이르렀다. 그 안으로 들어가서 그는 한 여인을 보았는데, 젊고 아리따웠으니, 그녀의 얼굴을 어지럽히는 비탄의 기색 너머로 그가 알아볼 수 있는 한에서 그러했다. 그녀의 유일한 동반자는 한 노파였는데, 젊은 동반자는 그 노파를 두려움과 분노로 바라보는 듯했다. 정중한 무사는 그 여인들에게 공손히 인사하고, 어떤 자의 야만으로 인해 그들이 이런 갇힘을 당하게 되었는지 알기를 청했다.\n\n젊은 여인이 흐느낌으로 자주 끊기는 목소리로 답했다.\n\n\"제 이야기가 저를 여기 가두어 두고 있는 저 야만적인 자에게서 더 가혹한 대우를 받게 하리라는 것을, 그리고 이 여인이 그것을 그자에게 어김없이 일러바치리라는 것을 잘 알고 있지만, 그래도 당신께 사실을 숨기지는 않겠습니다. 아! 어찌 제가 그자의 분노를 두려워하겠습니까? 그자가 제 목숨을 거둔다 한들, 저는 죽음보다 더 나은 무엇을 청할 수 있을지 모르겠습니다.\n\n제 이름은 이사벨라입니다. 저는 갈리시아 왕의 딸이지요, 아니 차라리 불운과 슬픔이 제 부모라고 말해야 할 것입니다. 젊고, 부유하고, 정숙하며, 차분한 성품을 지녀, 모든 것이 제 운명을 행복하게 하려 어우러진 듯했습니다. 아아! 그런데 저는 오늘 가난하고, 비천해지고, 비참하며, 어쩌면 더한 고난을 겪을 운명에 처한 제 자신을 봅니다. 제 아버지께서 바욘에서 마상 시합을 위해 명단을 열겠다고 알리신 지 한 해가 되었을 때, 사방에서 수많은 기사들이 우리 궁정에 모여들었습니다. 그들 가운데 스코틀랜드 왕의 아들 제르비노가 모든 결투에서 승리하여, 그 아름다움과 용맹으로 나머지 모두를 무색하게 했습니다. 갈리시아의 궁정을 떠나기 전에 그는 저와 혼인하고 싶다는 뜻을 밝혔고, 저는 그가 제 아버지인 왕께 제 손을 청하는 것에 동의했습니다. 그러나 저는 마호메트교도였고 제르비노는 그리스도교도였으니, 제 아버지께서는 승낙을 거절하셨습니다. 그 왕자는 프랑스 황제를 돕기 위해 정해진 군대의 지휘를 맡으라는 아버지의 부름을 받고 본국으로 불려 갔는데, 저를 설득하여 그와 비밀리에 혼인하고 그를 따라 스코틀랜드로 가게 했습니다. 그는 저를 맞이할 갤리선 한 척을 마련하게 하고, 육지와 바다 양쪽에서의 무훈으로 이름난 비스케이 사람 기사 오데릭을 그 지휘관으로 앉혔습니다. 정해진 날에 오데릭은 자신의 배를 제 아버지의 바닷가 별장으로 가져왔고, 저는 거기서 승선했습니다. 제 시종들 몇이 저를 따랐고, 그렇게 저는 제 고국을 떠났습니다.\n\n순풍을 받으며 항해하다가, 몇 시간 뒤에 우리는 사나운 폭풍에 휩싸였습니다. 우리가 돛을 모두 거두어들인 것도 소용없었으니, 우리는 바람에 떠밀려 곧장 바위투성이 해안으로 향했습니다. 다른 안전의 희망이 보이지 않자 오데릭은 저를 작은 배에 태우고, 자신도 부하 몇과 함께 따라 타서 뭍으로 향했습니다. 우리는 끝없는 위험을 헤치고 그곳에 다다랐고, 저는 단단한 땅을 발 밑에 느끼자마자 무릎을 꿇고 저를 지켜 주신 섭리에 진심 어린 감사를 쏟아 냈습니다.\n\n우리가 상륙한 해안은 사람이 살지 않는 듯했습니다. 우리를 비호해 줄 거처도, 더 후한 곳으로 우리를 이끌어 줄 길도 보이지 않았습니다. 높은 산 하나가 우리 앞에 솟아 있었는데, 그 기슭은 바다로 뻗어 있었습니다. 바로 여기서 그 파렴치한 오데릭은 제 눈물과 간청에도 불구하고 저를 해적 떼에게 팔아넘겼으니, 그들은 제가 자신들의 군주인 모로코의 술탄에게 받아들여질 만한 선물이 될 수도 있겠다고 여겼던 것입니다. 이 동굴이 그들의 소굴이며, 여기서 그들은 자신들의 편의에 맞게 저를 데려갈 때까지 이 여인의 감시 아래 저를 가두어 두고 있습니다.\"\n\n이사벨라가 자신의 이야기를 채 끝내기도 전에 한 무리의 무장한 자들이 동굴로 들어오기 시작했다. 오를란도 왕자를 본 한 사람이 나머지에게 말했다. \"이게 무슨 새인데, 우리가 덫조차 놓지 않고 잡았단 말이냐?\" 그러더니 오를란도에게 말을 걸었다. \"여보게, 자네가 그 멋진 갑옷과 윗옷을 입고 여기까지 와 주다니 참으로 친절하군그래, 바로 내가 원하던 것들이로세.\" \"그렇다면 그 값을 치르게 될 것이다.\" 오를란도가 말했다. 그리고 불 속에서 반쯤 타다 만 장작 하나를 움켜쥐어 그에게 던지니, 그것이 그자의 머리를 쳐서 바닥에 송장으로 뻗게 했다.\n\n동굴 한가운데에는 해적들의 식사에 쓰이는 육중한 탁자가 있었다. 오를란도는 그것을 들어 올려, 입구 쪽에 무리 지어 모여 선 도적들을 향해 내던졌다. 패거리의 절반이 머리와 사지가 부서진 채 엎어졌고, 나머지는 할 수 있는 한 날쌔게 달아났다.\n\n소굴과 그 안에 든 자들을 각자의 운명에 맡겨 둔 채, 오를란도는 이사벨라를 자신의 보호 아래 데리고 며칠 동안 아무런 모험도 만나지 않고 길을 갔다.\n\n어느 날 그들은 한 무리의 사람들이 다가오는 것을 보았는데, 그들은 손발이 묶인 한 포로를 마치 처형장으로 끌고 가듯 지키고 있는 듯했다. 그 포로는 고귀하고 순진해 보이는 젊은 기사였다. 그 무리는 마간차의 배신자 가문의 우두머리인 안셀모 백작의 휘장을 달고 있었다. 오를란도는 이사벨라에게 기다리라 이르고는, 이 행렬의 뜻을 물으러 앞으로 말을 달려 나갔다. 다가가서 그는 우두머리에게 그 포로가 누구이며 어떤 죄를 지었는지 물었다. 그 사내는 그 포로가 살인자로, 그자의 손에 안셀모 백작의 아들 피나벨이 비열하게 살해되었다고 답했다. 이 말에 포로가 외쳤다. \"나는 살인자가 아니며, 어떤 식으로도 그 젊은이의 죽음의 원인이 된 적이 없소.\" 마간차 가문 우두머리들의 잔혹하고 흉포한 성정을 알고 있던 오를란도는, 그 젊은이가 불의의 희생자임을 확신하는 데 더 이상의 것이 필요치 않았다. 그는 그 무리의 우두머리에게 자신의 희생자를 풀어 주라 명했고, 무례한 답을 듣자 창의 일격으로 그자를 땅에 내동댕이쳤다. 그런 다음 몇 차례 힘찬 타격으로 그 무리를 흩어 놓았으니, 가장 늦게 그 자리를 뜨는 자들에게 치명적인 흔적을 남겼다.\n\n그러고 나서 오를란도는 서둘러 그 포로를 풀어 주었고, 그 거짓 마간차인이 감히 차려입었던 그의 갑옷을 다시 입도록 도왔다. 그런 다음 그를 이사벨라에게로 데려갔으니, 그녀는 이제 그 현장으로 다가오는 참이었다. 이사벨라가 그에게서 자신의 남편 제르비노를 알아보고, 또 왕자가 물결에 휩쓸려 사라진 줄로 믿었던 그녀를 알아보았을 때의 그 기쁨과 놀라움을 우리가 어찌 그려 낼 수 있겠는가! 그들은 서로 끌어안고 기쁨에 겨워 울었다. 오를란도도 그들의 행복을 함께 나누며, 자신이 그 도구가 되었음을 스스로 기뻐했다. 공주는 제르비노에게 그 명망 높은 무사가 자신을 위해 한 일을 들려주었고, 왕자는 오를란도의 발치에 몸을 던지며, 자신의 목숨을 두 번이나 지켜 준 이로서 그에게 감사했다.\n\n이런 축하와 감사의 인사가 오가는 동안, 덤불 속의 소리 하나가 그들의 주의를 끌어, 두 기사로 하여금 투구를 조여 매고 경계 태세를 갖추게 했다. 이 방해의 원인이 무엇이었는지는 다른 장에서 적기로 한다.\n\n메도로\n\n이 무렵 프랑스는 끔찍한 사건들의 무대였다. 사라센인들과 그리스도교도들은 수많은 충돌 속에서 서로를 죽였다. 한번은 리날도가 이교도 대열을 공격하여 그들을 깨뜨려 흩어 놓다가, 마침내 그 갑옷이 (우연이었든 선택이었든, 그것은 중요치 않다) 오를란도의 문장을 달고 있는 한 기사와 맞서게 되었다. 그는 주마라의 젊고 용맹한 왕자 다르디넬이었고, 리날도는 그가 사방에 퍼뜨리는 살육으로 그를 알아보았다. \"아하,\" 그가 속으로 말했다. \"이 위험한 풀이 다 자라기 전에 뽑아 버리자.\"\n\n리날도가 나아가자, 무리가 그 앞에서 갈라졌으니, 그리스도교도들은 그의 칼이 거침없이 길을 내도록, 이교도들은 그 휘두름을 피하려 함이었다. 다르디넬과 그는 얼굴을 맞대고 섰다. 리날도가 사납게 외쳤다. \"젊은이여, 그 고귀한 방패를 네게 들려 준 자가 누구든, 너에게 위험한 선물을 준 것이다. 나는 네가 그 붉고 흰 사분 문장을 어떻게 지켜 내는지 보고 싶구나. 네가 그것을 나에게서 지켜 내지 못한다면, 오를란도가 그것을 요구할 때는 도대체 어떻게 하겠느냐?\" 다르디넬이 답했다. \"내가 지니고 있는 이 무구를 내가 지켜 낼 수 있으며, 그 위에 새로운 영광을 더할 수 있다는 것을 너는 알게 될 것이다. 목숨이 붙어 있는 한 누구도 이것을 내게서 빼앗지 못한다.\" 이 말을 하며 다르디넬은 칼을 치켜들고 리날도에게 돌진했다.\n\n리날도가 마치 사자가 어린 황소를 향하듯 왕자를 공격하러 나아가는 것을 보았을 때, 죽음에 이르는 공포의 한기가 사라센인들의 영혼을 채웠다. 첫 일격은 다르디넬의 손에서 나왔으나, 그 무기는 맘브리노의 투구에서 아무 소용 없이 튕겨 나왔다. 리날도가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"이제 내 타격이 더 효험 있는지 보여 주겠다.\" 이 말과 함께 그는 불운한 다르디넬의 가슴 한가운데를 찔렀다. 그 일격이 어찌나 맹렬했던지 그 잔혹한 무기는 몸을 꿰뚫고 등 뒤로 한 뼘 너비만큼 빠져나왔다. 이 상처를 통해 다르디넬의 생명이 그의 피와 함께 흘러나왔고, 그의 몸은 맥없이 땅에 쓰러졌다.\n\n지나가는 쟁기에 뿌리째 뽑힌 꽃이 시들어 고개를 떨구듯, 다르디넬도 죽음의 창백함으로 얼굴을 덮은 채 숨을 거두었고, 한 명망 높은 가문의 희망이 그와 함께 스러졌다.\n\n둑에 막혀 있던 물이 둑이 터지면 온 들판으로 흩어지듯, 무어인들도 더 이상 다르디넬의 본보기로 대열을 유지하지 못한 채 사방으로 달아났다. 리날도는 이런 손쉬운 승리들을 너무도 경멸하여 그들을 뒤쫓지 않았으니, 그는 오로지 용맹한 자들과의 싸움만을 바랐다. 동시에 다른 무사들은 무어인들을 끔찍하게 살육했다. 샤를 자신과 올리버, 귀도, 그리고 데인 사람 오지에가 사방에서 그들의 대열 속으로 죽음을 실어 날랐다.\n\n이교도들은 그 끔찍한 날에 한 사람도 남김없이 멸망할 운명인 듯 보였다. 그러나 현명한 왕 마르실리우스가 마침내 그 전면적인 패주에 약간의 질서를 부여했다. 그는 남은 군사들을 그러모아 한 부대로 편성하고, 제법 정연한 대오를 갖추어 자신의 진영으로 물러났다. 그 진영은 보루와 넓은 도랑으로 잘 방비되어 있었다. 도망자들은 그곳으로 서둘러 갔고, 무어인 군대의 남은 자들 전부가 차츰 거기로 모여들었다.\n\n황제는 어쩌면 그날 밤에 자신의 적을 완전히 무너뜨릴 수도 있었을 것이다. 그러나 이미 지친 자신의 군사들을 그토록 잘 방비된 진영에 대한 공격에 내모는 것이 분별 있는 일이라 여기지 않아, 그는 적을 자신의 군사들로 에워싸 정식 포위전을 펼칠 채비를 하는 것으로 만족했다. 밤 동안 무어인들은 자신들의 손실의 규모를 헤아릴 시간을 가졌다. 그들의 천막은 통곡으로 울려 퍼졌다. 이 전사는 형제를 잃고, 저 전사는 벗을 잃었으며, 많은 이들이 심한 상처로 고통받았고, 모두가 자신들에게 닥칠 운명에 떨었다.\n\n당시 인간의 역사에서 보기 드문 애착과 충심을 그때 입증한 두 젊은 무어인이 있었으니, 둘 다 미천한 신분이었다. 클로리단과 메도로는 자신들의 왕자 다르디넬을 따라 프랑스의 전쟁에 나선 이들이었다. 대담한 사냥꾼 클로리단은 힘과 민첩함을 겸비했다. 메도로는 한낱 젊은이로, 그 두 뺨은 아직 곱고 발그레했다. 모든 사라센인 가운데 그토록 많은 우아함과 아름다움을 한 몸에 지닌 이는 없었다. 그의 연한 머리칼은 검고 반짝이는 눈으로 한층 돋보였다. 두 벗은 함께 성벽에서 보초를 서고 있었다. 자정 무렵 그들은 깊은 비탄에 잠겨 그 광경을 바라보았다. 메도로는 눈에 눈물을 머금고 선량한 왕자 다르디넬에 대해 이야기했으며, 그의 시신이 장례의 예우도 받지 못한 채 들판에 내던져진다는 생각을 견딜 수 없었다. \"오 나의 벗이여,\" 그가 말했다. \"그러면 우리 왕자의 시신이 늑대와 까마귀의 먹이가 되어야 한단 말인가? 아아! 그분이 나를 얼마나 아끼셨는지를 떠올리면, 그분께 영예를 바치기 위해 내 목숨을 바친다 해도 내 본분 이상을 하는 것이 아니라는 생각이 드네. 사랑하는 벗이여, 나는 전장에서 그분의 시신을 찾아내어 묻어 드리고 싶네. 그리고 그들이 아마 모두 잠들어 있을 터이니, 들키지 않고 샤를 왕의 진영을 지나갈 수 있기를 바라네. 자네 클로리단은, 만약 내가 이 일에서 죽거든, 우리 왕자께 대한 감사와 충심이 나를 이끈 것이라고 나를 위해 말해 줄 수 있을 걸세.\"\n\n클로리단은 그 젊은이의 헌신의 이 증거에 놀라는 동시에 마음이 움직였다. 그는 그를 끔찍이 아꼈고, 오랜 시간 온갖 노력을 다해 그의 계획을 단념시키려 했으나, 메도로가 자신의 뜻을 이루거나 그 시도 중에 죽기로 작정했음을 알게 되었다.\n\n그의 뜻을 바꿀 수 없었던 클로리단이 말했다. \"자네와 함께 가겠네, 메도로. 그리고 이 갸륵한 일에서 자네를 돕겠네. 나는 명예에 견주면 목숨을 귀히 여기지 않으며, 설령 그렇다 한들, 사랑하는 벗이여, 자네 없이 내가 살 수 있으리라 생각하는가? 자네를 잃은 슬픔으로 죽느니, 차라리 우리 적들의 무기에 쓰러지는 편이 낫겠네.\"\n\n두 벗은 보초 임무에서 풀려나자 아무도 거느리지 않고 그리스도교도들의 진영으로 들어갔다. 그곳은 온통 고요했고, 모닥불은 사그라지고 있었으며, 사라센인들 쪽에서 무슨 시도가 있으리라는 두려움도 없어, 병사들은 피로나 술에 못 이겨 자신들의 무기와 장비 한가운데 땅바닥에 누워 마음 놓고 잠들어 있었다. 클로리단이 멈춰 서서 말했다. \"메도로, 나는 우리 왕자의 죽음에 대한 복수를 하지 않고는 이 진영을 떠나지 않겠네. 망을 보게, 아무도 우리를 기습하지 못하도록 경계하게. 나는 우리 적들의 대열을 가로질러 내 칼로 길을 내겠네.\" 이렇게 말하며 그는 알페우스가 잠든 천막으로 들어갔으니, 알페우스는 한 해 전에 샤를의 진영에 합류하여 자신이 위대한 의사이자 점성가라고 행세하던 자였다. 그러나 만약 그의 학문이 그에게 천수를 누리며 잠자리에서 평온히 죽으리라는 희망을 주었다면, 그것은 그를 속인 것이었으니, 그의 운명은 거의 경고도 없이 죽는 것이었다. 클로리단은 그의 심장을 칼로 꿰뚫었다. 그다음으로 늦도록 주사위 노름을 하던 그리스인 한 명과 게르만인 한 명이 뒤따랐으니, 그들이 노름을 조금만 더 이어 갔더라면 운이 좋았으련만, 그들은 이런 한 판을 결코 자신들의 패 가운데 셈하지 못했다. 클로리단은 다음으로 불운한 그릴론에게 이르렀으니, 그의 머리는 베개 위에 편안히 놓여 있었다. 그는 아마도 자신이 막 자리를 뜬 잔치 꿈을 꾸고 있었을 것이다. 클로리단이 그의 머리를 베자 피와 더불어 포도주가 흘러나왔으니 말이다.\n\n두 젊은 무어인은 샤를마뉴의 천막에까지도 뚫고 들어갈 수 있었을 것이다. 그러나 그를 둘러싸고 진을 친 무사들이 번갈아 망을 본다는 것을 알았고, 그들 모두가 잠들어 있을 리는 없다고 판단했기에, 너무 가까이 가기를 두려워했다. 그들은 또한 값진 노획물도 얻을 수 있었을 것이다. 그러나 오로지 자신들의 목적에만 몰두한 채 그들은 진영을 가로질러, 마침내 피로 물든 들판에 다다랐으니, 그곳에는 방패와 창과 칼이 미천한 자와 부유한 자, 평범한 병사와 왕자의 시신들, 말들과 핏물 웅덩이들 한가운데 흩어져 있었다. 이 끔찍한 살육의 광경은, 달이 그 어슴푸레한 빛의 도움을 빌려 주지 않았더라면, 동이 틀 때까지 그들이 찾는 것을 발견할 모든 희망을 앗아 갔을 것이다.\n\n메도로는 그 천체를 향해 눈을 들어 올리며 외쳤다. \"오 거룩한 여신이여, 우리 조상들이 세 가지 다른 모습으로 숭배해 온 분이여, 하늘과 땅과 저승에서 당신의 권능을 드러내시는 분이여, 숲의 짐승들을 쫓는 요정들 가운데 맨 앞에 보이시는 분이여, 비오니, 제 사랑하는 주인이 누워 계신 곳을 제가 볼 수 있게 해 주소서. 그리고 당신께서 보여 주시는 자비와 사랑의 행적의 본보기를 제 평생토록 따르게 해 주소서.\"\n\n우연이었든, 아니면 달이 메도로의 기도를 알아챈 것이었든, 구름이 걷히고 달빛이 대낮처럼 환하게 쏟아져 나왔다. 그 빛줄기는 다르디넬 왕자의 시신이 누운 자리를 유독 황금빛으로 물들이는 듯했고, 메도로는 눈물에 젖고 가슴에서 피를 흘리며, 그의 방패에 새겨진 붉고 흰 사분 문장으로 그를 알아보았다.\n\n눈물로 억눌린 신음과 짓눌린 목소리의 탄식으로 — 자신을 위해서가 아니었으니 그는 목숨 따위는 개의치 않았다, 다만 아직 끝맺지 못한 자신들의 경건한 본분을 누군가가 깨어나 방해할까 두려워서였다 — 그는 자신의 동무에게 둘이서 함께 다르디넬을 어깨에 메어, 그 사랑하는 유해의 짐을 나누어 지자고 제안했다.\n\n그 소중한 짐을 지고 빠른 걸음으로 행군하던 그들은 별들이 희미해지기 시작하고, 밤의 그늘이 곧 동이 트면 흩어지리라는 것을 알아챘다. 바로 그때, 그 지극한 용맹으로 도망자들을 뒤쫓아 진영에서 멀리 나아갔던 제르비노가 돌아오는 길에 그들이 있던 숲으로 들어섰다. 그의 수행원 가운데 몇몇 기사가 멀리서 그 두 의형제를 알아보았다. 클로리단이 그 무리를 보고는, 그들이 마치 노획물을 찾는 듯 들판으로 흩어지는 것을 보고, 메도로에게 시신을 내려놓고 각자 달아나 목숨을 구하자고 일렀다. 그는 메도로도 그러리라 생각하고 자신의 몫을 내려놓았다. 그러나 그 선량한 젊은이는 자신의 왕자를 너무도 깊이 사랑하여 그를 버리지 못하고, 클로리단이 달아나는 동안 혼자서나마 힘닿는 대로 계속 그 짐을 졌다. 가까이에는 야생 짐승 말고는 무엇도 들어가 본 적 없는 듯 우거진 숲의 한 구역이 있었다. 그 불운한 젊은이는 죽은 주인의 무게를 진 채 그 깊숙한 곳으로 뛰어들었다.\n\n클로리단은 자신이 적들을 따돌렸음을 알아챘을 때, 메도로가 자신과 함께 있지 않다는 것을 알게 되었다. \"아아!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"어찌 내가, 사랑하는 메도로여, 자네를 돌보지 않고 내 한 몸의 안전만 살피느라 그토록 나 자신을 잊을 수 있었단 말인가?\" 이렇게 말하며 그는 자신이 달아나 온 그곳을 향해 숲의 얽힌 길을 되짚어 갔다. 다가가면서 그는 말발굽 소리와 무장한 자들의 위협적인 목소리를 들었다. 곧 그는 메도로가 걸어서, 기사들에게 둘러싸여 있는 것을 보았다. 그들의 지휘관 제르비노가 그를 붙잡으라 명했다. 불행한 메도로는 이쪽으로 저쪽으로 몸을 돌리며 떡갈나무나 바위 뒤에 숨으려 했으나, 결코 내려놓으려 하지 않는 그 시신을 여전히 지고 있었다. 그를 어떻게 도와야 할지 몰랐으나 만약 죽어야 한다면 그와 함께 죽기로 결심한 클로리단은 화살 하나를 집어 활에 메겨 쏘아, 한 그리스도교도 기사의 가슴을 꿰뚫으니, 그자가 맥없이 말에서 떨어졌다. 다른 자들은 그 치명적인 화살이 어디서 날아왔는지 알아내려 이리저리 둘러보았다. 한 사람이 자신의 동료들에게 화살이 어느 방향에서 왔는지 물으려는데, 두 번째 화살을 목에 맞아 그 말이 막혔고, 이내 그 광경에 눈을 감았다. 제르비노는 자신의 두 동료의 죽음에 격분하여 메도로에게 달려들어, 그의 금빛 머리칼을 움켜쥐고 그를 죽이려 앞으로 끌어당겼다. 그러나 그토록 많은 젊음과 아름다움의 광경이 연민을 자아냈다. 그는 자신의 팔을 멈추었다. 그 젊은이가 애원하는 어조로 말했다. \"아아! 나리,\" 그가 말했다. \"당신께서 섬기시는 하느님의 이름으로 당신께 간청하오니, 제가 제 주인이신 왕자의 시신을 묻을 때까지는 제 목숨을 거두지 말아 주소서. 제가 당신께 다른 은혜를 청하리라 두려워 마소서. 목숨은 제게 귀하지 않으며, 이 거룩한 본분을 다하는 즉시 저는 죽음을 바랍니다. 그러니 저를 마음대로 하소서. 제 사지를 새와 짐승의 먹이로 던지셔도 좋으니, 다만 먼저 제 왕자를 묻게만 해 주소서.\" 메도로가 이 말을 어찌나 곱고 부드러운 기색으로 했던지 돌 같은 마음이라도 움직였을 것이다. 제르비노는 그 영혼 밑바닥까지 움직였다. 그가 막 자비의 말을 입에 올리려는 찰나, 한 잔혹한 부하가 자신의 지휘관에 대한 모든 존경을 잊고서 그 젊은 무어인의 가슴에 자신의 창을 찔러 넣었다. 제르비노는 그의 잔인무도함에 격노하여 그 비열한 자에게 복수하려 돌아섰으나, 그자는 황급히 달아나 몸을 피했다.\n\n메도로가 쓰러지는 것을 본 클로리단은 더 이상 자신을 억누를 수 없었다. 그는 자신이 숨어 있던 곳에서 뛰쳐나와 활을 내던지고, 칼을 손에 쥔 채 오로지 메도로의 복수와 그와 함께 죽기만을 바라는 듯했다. 한순간에 수많은 상처로 온몸이 꿰뚫린 그는 마지막 남은 힘을 짜내어 메도로에게 몸을 끌고 가, 그를 끌어안고 죽으려 했다. 기사들은 그들을 그대로 두고 제르비노에게로 돌아갔으니, 메도로를 죽인 자에 대한 그의 분노가 그를 그 자리에서 멀어지게 했던 것이다.\n\n클로리단은 죽었다. 그리고 피를 줄줄 흘리던 메도로가 죽음에 가까워지고 있을 때, 도움의 손길이 다다랐다.\n\n이 위태로운 순간에 한 젊은 처녀가 쓰러진 기사들에게 다가왔다. 그녀의 옷차림은 농가 처녀의 것이었으나, 그 기품은 고귀했고, 그 아름다움은 천상의 것이었다. 다정함과 선량함이 그녀의 사랑스러운 얼굴에 깃들어 있었다. 그녀는 다름 아닌 카타이의 공주 안젤리카였다.\n\n우리가 앞서 이야기한 대로 그 귀한 반지를 되찾았을 때, 안젤리카는 그 가치를 알았기에 그것이 부여하는 권능에 자긍심을 느꼈고, 두려움 없이 홀로 여행했으나, 자신의 방랑 가운데 오를란도 백작과 사크리판트의 보호를 구할 수밖에 없었던 적이 있었다는 데에 은밀한 부끄러움이 없지 않았다. 그녀는 또한 리날도와 혼인할 생각을 한 적이 있다는 것을 하나의 나약함으로 여겨 스스로를 책망했다. 요컨대 그녀의 자긍심은 살아 있는 어떤 남자도 자신의 손을 넘볼 자격이 없다고 스스로를 설득할 만큼 드높아졌다.\n\n상처 입은 젊은이의 모습에 연민으로 마음이 움직이고, 그 사연을 듣고서 눈물 짓게 된 그녀는, 식물의 효능과 치료의 기술이 공주들의 교육에까지도 일부를 이루던 인도에서 자신이 익힌 지식을 재빨리 기억해 냈다. 그 아름다운 여왕은 피의 흐름을 멎게 할 효험 있는 식물들을 캐러 이웃한 풀밭으로 달려갔다. 가는 길에 길 잃은 어린 암소를 찾아 말을 타고 있던 한 시골 사람을 만난 그녀는, 자신을 도와 그 상처 입은 사람을 더 안전한 피난처로 옮겨 보자고 그에게 청했다.\n\n안젤리카는 그 식물들을 두 돌 사이에서 짓이겨 마련한 뒤, 그것을 자신의 고운 손으로 메도로의 상처에 얹었다. 그 치료약은 곧 상처 입은 사람의 기력을 어느 정도 회복시켰으니, 그는 그 자리를 떠나기에 앞서 그들에게 자신의 벗과 왕자의 시신을 흙과 잔디로 덮게 했다. 그런 다음 자신을 구해 준 이들의 연민에 자신을 맡기며, 그는 양치기의 말에 자신을 태워 그 오두막으로 데려가도록 했다. 그것은 숲 가장자리에 자리 잡은 아늑한 농가로, 안락함과 넉넉함의 자취를 띠고 있었다. 그곳에서 양치기는 아내와 자식들과 더불어 살았다. 그곳에서 안젤리카는 메도로를 돌보았고, 그곳에서 그 아름다운 여왕의 헌신적인 보살핌으로 그의 처참한 상처가 아물었으며, 그는 온전한 건강을 되찾았다.\n\n오 리날도 백작이여, 오 사크리판트 왕이여! 그토록 많은 미덕과 그토록 큰 명성을 지닌 것이 그대들에게 무슨 소용이 있었던가? 그대들의 그 모든 드높은 공적에서 그대들은 어떤 이득을 얻었던가? 오 불운한 왕, 위대한 아그리칸이여! 그대가 되살아날 수 있다면, 미천한 출신의 한 젊은 병사를 위해 히멘의 멍에에 머리를 숙일 그녀에게서 자신이 거절당하는 것을 어찌 견딜 수 있겠는가? 그리고 그대 페라우여, 그리고 이 잔혹한 미인을 위해 백 번이나 목숨을 걸었던 그대들 수많은 다른 이들이여, 그녀가 그대들 모두를 미천한 메도로의 차지로 희생시키는 것을 보는 일이 그대들에게 얼마나 쓰라리겠는가!\n\n그곳, 한 양치기의 나지막한 지붕 아래에서, 이 도도한 여왕을 위해 히멘의 불꽃이 밝혀졌다. 그녀는 양치기의 아내를 어머니 대신으로, 양치기와 그 자식들을 증인으로 삼아, 행복한 메도로와 혼인했다.\n\n안젤리카는 혼인한 뒤, 아직 자신에게 남아 있던 나라들의 통치권을 메도로에게 물려주고자, 그와 함께 동방으로 가는 길에 올랐다. 그녀는 자신의 온갖 모험을 거치는 동안 오를란도 백작의 선물인, 보석으로 장식된 금팔찌 하나를 간직해 왔다. 그토록 많은 정성과 충심으로 자신을 섬겨 준 그 선량한 양치기와 그 아내에게 보답할 다른 무엇도 없었기에, 그녀는 그 팔찌를 자신의 팔에서 빼어 그들에게 주었고, 그런 다음 새로 혼인한 부부는 프랑스와 에스파냐를 가르는 저 산들을 향해 발걸음을 옮겼으니, 바르셀로나에서 자신들을 동방으로 가는 길에 태워다 줄 배 한 척을 기다릴 작정이었다.\n광기에 빠진 오를란도\n\n# ORLANDO MAD\n\n안젤리카를 잃은 오를란도는 자신의 깃 장식과 무구를 내려놓고, 자신의 절망을 드러내는 검은 갑옷 한 벌로 차려입었다. 이런 차림으로 그는 이교도들의 대열 가운데 얼마나 큰 살육을 벌였던지, 두 군대 모두가 그 낯선 기사의 위업에 놀랐다. 전투에 나서지 않았던 만드리카르도는 이 위업들에 대한 소문을 듣고, 그토록 칭송받는 그 기사의 용맹을 스스로 시험해 보기로 작정했다. 무사의 용맹으로 두 연인이 행복하게 재회한 뒤, 제르비노와 이사벨라, 그리고 그들의 은인 오를란도가 서로 축하의 말을 나누느라 여념이 없을 때, 그들의 대화에 끼어든 이가 바로 그였다.\n\n만드리카르도는 한순간 그 무리를 바라보고는, 오를란도에게 이런 말로 자신의 뜻을 밝혔다. \"그대가 내가 찾는 자임에 틀림없다. 열흘 넘게 나는 그대의 자취를 좇아왔다. 그대 위업의 명성이 나를 이리로 이끌었으니, 그대의 힘을 내 힘과 견주어 보고자 함이다. 그대의 깃 장식과 방패가 우리 군대 가운데 그토록 큰 살육을 퍼뜨린 바로 그 자임을 입증한다. 그러나 이런 표시들은 군더더기이니, 그대를 백 명 가운데서 본다 한들 나는 그 군인다운 풍채로 그대가 내가 찾는 자임을 알아볼 것이다.\"\n\n\"그대의 용기를 나는 존중한다.\" 오를란도가 말했다. \"그런 계획은 용맹하고 너그러운 영혼이 아니고서는 결코 솟아날 수 없는 것이다. 나를 보고자 하는 욕망이 그대를 이리로 데려온 것이라면, 가능하다면 나는 그대에게 내 가장 깊은 속마음을 보여 주고 싶다. 그대의 호기심을 채울 수 있도록 내 면갑을 들어 올리겠다. 그러나 그러고 나면, 그대 또한 내 용맹이 내 외양과 걸맞은지 시험해 보기를 바란다.\"\n\n\"덤벼라.\" 사라센인이 말했다. \"내 첫 번째 바람은 그대를 보고 아는 것이었으나, 두 번째 바람은 채워 주지 않겠다.\"\n\n오를란도는 만드리카르도를 살펴보다가, 그의 옆구리에 칼도 없고 안장 앞머리에 철퇴도 없는 것을 보고 놀랐다. \"그러면 그대는 무슨 무기를 지녔는가,\" 그가 말했다. \"그대의 창이 그대를 저버린다면?\"\n\n\"그 일은 걱정 말라.\" 만드리카르도가 말했다. \"나는 그대가 보는 것 말고는 다른 무기 없이도 많은 훌륭한 기사들을 물러서게 했다. 알아 두라, 나는 무사 오를란도가 지니고 다니는 저 이름난 두린다나를 되찾을 때까지는 결코 칼을 차지 않겠다고 맹세했다. 그 칼은 내가 입은 갑옷 한 벌에 속하는 것으로, 오직 그것만이 빠져 있다. 의심할 바 없이 그것은 도둑맞은 것이나, 그것이 어찌하여 오를란도의 손에 들어갔는지 나는 모른다. 그러나 그를 찾아내면 나는 그가 그 값을 톡톡히 치르게 하겠다. 내가 그를 더욱 애타게 찾는 것은, 그가 비열하게 살해한 내 아버지 아그리칸 왕의 죽음을 그의 피로 갚기 위함이다. 그가 비열한 술책으로 그리했음이 틀림없으니, 내 아버지 같은 전사를 정정당당한 싸움에서 굴복시키는 것은 그의 힘으로 될 일이 아니었기 때문이다.\"\n\n\"거짓말이다.\" 오를란도가 외쳤다. \"그리고 그리 말하는 자는 누구든 거짓말을 하는 것이다. 내가 바로 그대가 찾는 오를란도다. 그렇다, 내가 바로 그대의 아버지를 명예롭게 죽인 자다. 자, 여기 그 칼이 있다. 그대의 용기가 그것을 얻을 자격이 있다면 그대가 그것을 갖게 될 것이다. 비록 그것이 정당하게 내 것이지만, 나는 이 다툼에서 그것을 쓰지 않겠다. 보라, 나는 그것을 이 나무에 걸어 두겠다. 그대가 내 목숨을 앗아 간다면 그대가 그것의 주인이 될 것이요, 그렇지 못하면 안 될 것이다.\"\n\n이 말과 함께 오를란도는 두린다나를 빼어 가까이 있는 한 나무의 가지 하나에 걸었다.\n\n두 기사는 다 같이 똑같은 열기로 끓어오른 채, 반원을 그리며 말을 달려 나갔다가, 고삐를 풀어 놓고 서로를 향해 돌진하여 창으로 서로를 쳤다. 둘 다 미동도 없이 자리를 지켰다. 그들의 창 조각이 공중으로 날아갔고, 어느 쪽에게도 손에 쥔 부러진 조각 말고는 무기가 남지 않았다. 그리하여 쇠사슬 갑옷으로 무장한 그 두 기사는, 풀밭의 경계나 샘 하나의 소유를 두고 다투는 두 시골뜨기처럼 막대기로 싸워야 하는 처지가 되었다.\n\n이 몽둥이들은 그토록 억센 타격을 가하는 자들의 손에서 오래 멀쩡할 수 없었으니, 그들은 이내 맨주먹으로 싸우는 처지가 되었다. 그런 싸움은 타격을 받는 자보다 가하는 자에게 더 고통스러웠다. 그들은 다음으로 서로 부둥켜안고, 헤라클레스가 안타이오스에게 했듯이 저마다 자신의 적수를 힘껏 조였다. 오를란도보다 더 격분한 만드리카르도는 그 무사를 안장에서 떨어뜨리려 격렬히 애쓰다가, 자신의 말고삐를 놓치고 말았다. 더 침착한 오를란도가 그것을 알아챘다. 그는 한 손으로 만드리카르도를 막으면서, 다른 손으로 그 짐승의 고삐를 그 귀 위로 홱 벗겨 냈다. 사라센인이 있는 힘껏 오를란도를 끌어당겼으나, 오를란도의 두 넓적다리가 안장을 죔쇠처럼 붙들었다. 마침내 사라센인의 안간힘에 오를란도의 말 뱃대끈이 끊어졌고, 안장이 미끄러졌으며, 등자에 단단히 버틴 기사도 그것과 함께 미끄러져, 자신이 떨어진 것을 거의 의식하지도 못한 채 땅에 닿았다. 그의 갑옷이 떨어지며 내는 소리가 이제 고삐 없는 만드리카르도의 말을 놀라게 했다. 그 말은 나무도 바위도 험한 땅도 아랑곳하지 않고 전속력으로 내달리기 시작했다. 공포에 내몰린 그 말은 맹렬한 속도로 달려, 분노로 거의 제정신이 아닌 자신의 주인을 실어 갔으니, 주인은 소리치며 주먹으로 그 짐승을 때렸고 그로써 도리어 그 질주를 부추겼다. 그렇게 삼 마일 남짓 달린 끝에, 깊은 도랑 하나가 그들의 앞을 가로막았다. 말과 기수는 그 안으로 곤두박질쳐 떨어졌으니, 바닥에는 깃털 침대도 장미꽃도 깔려 있지 않았다. 그들은 처참하게 멍이 들었으나, 사지가 부러지는 일은 없이 벗어날 만큼은 운이 좋았다. 만드리카르도는 발을 딛자마자 그 말의 갈기를 사납게 움켜쥐었으나, 고삐가 없어 붙들 수가 없었다. 그는 고삐 노릇을 할 만한 무언가를 찾을 수 있을까 하여 주위를 둘러보았다. 바로 그때, 마침내 그를 도우려는 듯한 운명이, 길 잃은 자기 농장 말을 찾고 있던, 손에 고삐를 든 한 농부를 그 길로 데려왔다.\n\n오를란도는 자신의 말 뱃대끈을 재빨리 손본 뒤 다시 올라타, 사라센인이 돌아오기를 족히 한 시간이나 기다렸다. 그가 보이지 않자, 그는 그를 찾아 나서기로 작정했다. 그는 제르비노와 이사벨라에게 다정한 작별을 고했으니, 그들은 기꺼이 그를 따라가려 했으나, 용맹한 무사는 이를 결코 허락하지 않았다. 그는 방어자 노릇을 할지도 모를 벗을 동반하고 적을 찾아 나서는 것을 기사답지 못한 일로 여겼다. 그리하여 그는 그들에게, 만약 만드리카르도를 만나거든 자신의 뜻이 사흘간 이 근방에 머물다가 그런 다음 샤를마뉴의 진영으로 가는 것이라고 전해 달라 청한 뒤, 나무에서 두린다나를 내려 사라센인의 말이 향했던 방향으로 나아갔다. 그러나 그 짐승은 자신의 공포 말고는 인도해 줄 것이 없었기에 자신의 자취를 어찌나 되돌리고 어지럽혀 놓았던지, 오를란도는 그 수색에 이틀을 보낸 끝에 그 시도를 단념했다.\n\n그 무사가 꽃으로 아롱진 풀밭 사이를 굽이쳐 흐르는 시내의 쾌적한 둑에 다다른 것은 사흘째 날의 한낮 무렵이었다. 우듬지가 서로 맞닿아 정자를 이룬 높은 나무들이 그 샘에 그늘을 드리웠고, 그 잎새 사이로 부는 산들바람이 더위를 누그러뜨렸다. 이곳으로 양치기들이 갈증을 풀러, 그리고 한낮의 해를 피해 그늘을 즐기러 모여들곤 했다. 꽃향기로 그윽한 그 공기는 그들의 핏줄에 새로운 기운을 불어넣는 듯했다. 갑옷으로 덮여 있었음에도 오를란도는 그 영향을 느꼈다. 그는 모든 것이 휴식을 권하는 듯한 이 감미로운 정자에 멈춰 섰다. 그러나 그는 이보다 더 치명적인 피난처를 고를 수는 없었을 것이다. 그는 그곳에서 자신의 생애에서 가장 비참한 순간들을 보냈다.\n\n그는 주위를 둘러보며 그곳의 온갖 매력을 흐뭇하게 눈에 담았다. 그는 나무들 가운데 몇 그루에 글귀가 새겨져 있는 것을 보았다. 그는 가까이 다가가 그것을 읽었고, 그것들이 안젤리카라는 이름을 이루고 있음을 알았을 때 그의 놀라움이 어떠했겠는가! 더 나아가 그는 메도로의 이름이 그녀의 이름과 뒤섞여 있는 것을 발견했다. 그 무사는 자신이 꿈을 꾸는 것이라 여겼다. 그는 마치 날아오르려다 그 발이 그물에 걸린 새처럼, 망연자실한 사람마냥 서 있었다.\n\n오를란도는 시내의 물줄기를 따라가다, 산의 바위들이 일종의 동굴을 이루도록 굽어 있는 어느 굽이에 이르렀다. 담쟁이덩굴과 야생 포도덩굴의 뒤엉킨 줄기가 자연의 손으로 파인 이 후미진 곳의 입구를 휘장처럼 덮고 있었다.\n\n그 불행한 무사는 동굴로 들어서다가, 최근에 새겨진 듯한 글자들을 보았다. 그것은 메도로가 그 아름다운 여왕과의 행복한 혼인을 기려 지은 시구였다. 오를란도는 그 시구가 기리는 이가 어떤 다른 안젤리카임에 틀림없다고 스스로를 설득하려 애썼고, 메도로로 말하자면 그는 그 이름을 들어 본 적조차 없었다. 해는 이제 기울고 있었고, 오를란도는 다시 말에 올라 길을 갔다. 그는 곧 연기가 피어오르는 오두막 한 채의 지붕을 보았고, 개 짖는 소리와 소 우는 소리를 들었으며, 하룻밤의 피난처를 내어 줄 듯한 한 누추한 거처에 다다랐다. 그곳에 든 사람들은 그를 보자마자 서둘러 그를 시중들었다. 한 사람은 그의 말을, 다른 사람은 그의 방패와 흉갑을, 또 다른 사람은 그의 황금 박차를 받았다. 이 오두막은 바로 메도로가 깊이 부상당한 채 실려 왔던 그곳이었다.\n\n부상을 당했던 곳이었다. 그곳에서 안젤리카가 그를 간호했고, 이후 그와 혼인했던 것이다. 그곳에 살던 목동은 누구에게나 이 혼인의 이야기를 들려주기를 좋아했고, 곧 그 모든 자초지종을 비참한 처지의 오를란도에게 들려주었다.\n\n이야기를 마친 목동은 자리를 떴다가, 안젤리카가 그의 수고에 감사하며 기념으로 주었던 그 귀한 팔찌를 가지고 돌아왔다. 그것은 다름 아닌 오를란도 자신이 그녀에게 주었던 것이었다.\n\n이 마지막 일격은 흥분한 기사에게 결정타가 되었다. 미친 듯이 격분한 그는, 자신을 멸시한 그 배은망덕하고 잔인한 공주를 향해 외쳤다. 프랑스의 모든 기사들 가운데 가장 명성 높고 가장 굴복할 줄 모르는 자, 그토록 무시무시한 위험에서 그녀를 구해낸 자, 그녀를 위해 가장 끔찍한 싸움을 치른 자가 바로 자신이거늘, 그런 자신보다 한낱 젊은 사라센 사람을 더 좋아하다니! 고귀한 백작의 자존심은 깊은 상처를 입었다. 분노에 휩싸여 미쳐 날뛰며, 다스릴 수 없는 격분의 희생자가 된 그는 가장 끔찍한 비명을 지르며 숲 속으로 뛰어들었다.\n\n\"아니다, 아니야!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"나는 그들이 여기는 그런 사람이 아니다! 오를란도는 죽었어! 나는 그저 그 불행한 백작의 떠도는 유령일 뿐, 그 백작은 지금 지옥의 고통을 겪고 있단 말이다!\"\n\n오를란도는 발길 닿는 대로 밤새도록 숲을 헤매었고, 해가 뜰 무렵 그의 운명은 그를 어느 샘으로 이끌었다. 메도로가 그 치명적인 글귀를 새겨 놓았던 바로 그 샘이었다. 미쳐 날뛰던 기사는 그것을 두 번째로 보고는 격노하여 칼을 뽑아 바위에서 그 글귀를 깎아 내버렸다.\n\n불운한 동굴이여! 너는 더 이상 그늘과 서늘함으로 누구도 끌어들이지 못할 것이며, 너의 아치 아래 목동도 양 떼도 더 이상 품지 못하리라. 그리고 너, 맑고 깨끗한 샘이여, 너 또한 광란하는 오를란도의 분노를 피하지 못하리라! 그는 나뭇가지들, 뽑아낸 나무 둥치들, 부숴 낸 바위 조각들, 흙이 달라붙은 채 뿌리째 뽑은 풀들, 그리고 떼와 덤불을 샘 속으로 던져 넣어 샘을 틀어막고 그 물의 맑음을 망쳐 놓았다. 마침내 격렬한 노력에 지친 오를란도는 땀에 흠뻑 젖고 숨이 차서 헐떡이며 땅에 쓰러졌고, 거기 사흘 밤낮을 의식 없이 누워 있었다.\n\n나흘째 되는 날 그는 벌떡 일어나 무기를 움켜쥐었다. 그는 투구와 방패를 멀리 내던지고, 갑옷과 옷가지를 갈가리 찢어 그 조각들을 숲 곳곳에 흩뿌렸다. 요컨대 그는 광포한 미치광이가 되었다. 그 광기가 어찌나 심했던지 그는 자신의 칼조차 지니려 하지 않았다. 그러나 그가 놀라운 일들을 해내는 데는 두린다나도, 다른 무기도 필요치 않았다. 그의 엄청난 힘만으로 충분했다. 그의 강대한 팔로 한 번 비틀자 소나무 한 그루가 뿌리째 뽑혀 나왔다. 떡갈나무, 너도밤나무, 단풍나무, 그가 지나는 길에 마주치는 것은 무엇이든 똑같은 방식으로 쓰러졌다. 그 오래된 숲은 이내 사냥꾼이 그물을 치려고 덤불을 쳐낸 늪지의 가장자리처럼 헐벗게 되었다.\n목동들은 숲에서 들려오는 그 끔찍한 우지끈거리는 소리를 듣고, 양 떼를 버려 둔 채 이 예사롭지 않은 소란의 원인을 보러 달려왔다. 사나운 운명의 별 때문인지, 혹은 자신들의 죄 때문인지, 그들은 그곳으로 이끌려 왔다. 백작의 광포한 상태와 그 믿을 수 없는 힘을 본 그들은 그의 손길이 닿지 않는 곳으로 기꺼이 달아나고 싶었으나, 두려움에 정신을 잃고 말았다. 미치광이는 그들을 뒤쫓아 한 사람을 붙잡아, 마치 나무에서 잘 익은 사과를 따듯 손쉽게 그 팔다리를 갈가리 찢어 놓았다. 그는 또 다른 사람의 발을 잡아, 그를 곤봉처럼 휘둘러 세 번째 사람을 때려눕혔다. 목동들은 달아났으나, 만약 그가 바로 그 순간 그들을 내버려두고 같은 광기로 그들의 양 떼에게 달려들지 않았더라면 누구도 빠져나가기 어려웠을 것이다. 농부들은 쟁기와 써레를 내버리고 건물 지붕과 바위 꼭대기로 기어 올라갔는데, 떡갈나무와 소나무조차 믿지 못했다. 그들은 그런 높은 곳에서 불행한 오를란도의 광란하는 분노에 떨며 내려다보았다. 그의 주먹, 그의 이빨, 그의 손톱, 그의 발이 소와 양과 돼지를 붙잡고 부수고 찢었으니, 가장 빨리 달아난 것들만이 그에게서 벗어날 수 있었다.\n\n마침내 공포가 그의 앞에 있던 모든 것을 흩어 놓았을 때, 그는 주민들이 버리고 간 어느 오두막으로 들어갔고, 거기서 먹을 것으로 쓸 만한 것을 발견했다. 오랜 굶주림은 그에게 가장 게걸스러운 허기를 느끼게 했다. 그는 먹을 수 있는 것이라면 뿌리든, 도토리든, 빵이든, 날고기든 익힌 고기든 가리지 않고 닥치는 대로 움켜쥐어 마구 먹어 치웠다.\n\n거기서 다시 나온 광란하는 오를란도는 눈에 보이는 살아 있는 것이라면 사람이든 짐승이든 가리지 않고 뒤쫓았다. 어떤 때는 수사슴과 암사슴을 쫓았고, 어떤 때는 곰과 늑대를 덮쳐 맨손으로 죽이고 찢어 그 살을 먹어 치웠다.\n\n이렇게 그는 프랑스 곳곳을 이리저리 떠돌며 천 가지 방식으로 목숨을 위태롭게 했으나, 언제나 어떤 알 수 없는 섭리에 의해 치명적인 결말은 면하였다. 그러나 여기서 우리는 오를란도를 잠시 떠나, 그가 제르비노와 이사벨라와 헤어진 뒤 그들에게 무슨 일이 일어났는지 기록하고자 한다.\n\n왕자와 그의 아름다운 신부는 오를란도의 부탁대로 사흘 동안 전투가 벌어졌던 곳 가까이에서 기다렸다. 혹시 만드리카르도가 돌아오면, 오를란도가 어디서 그와 다시 만나려 하는지 알려주기 위해서였다. 그 기간이 끝나자 결말을 알고 싶은 마음에 그들은 오를란도의 자취를 따라갔고, 그 자취는 마침내 나무마다 안젤리카와 메도로의 이름이 새겨진 그 숲으로 그들을 이끌었다. 그들은 그 모든 글귀들이 어떻게 지워졌는지, 동굴이 어떻게 헝클어졌는지, 샘이 어떻게 쓰레기로 막혔는지 눈여겨보았다. 그러나 무엇보다도 그들을 놀라게 하고 비통하게 한 것은 풀밭에서 오를란도의 흉갑을 발견한 일이었고, 그 멀지 않은 곳에서 그의 투구, 곧 한때 이름 높은 알몬테스가 썼던 바로 그 투구를 발견한 일이었다.\n\n숲에서 말이 우는 소리를 듣고 제르비노는 그쪽으로 눈길을 돌렸고, 안장 앞테에 고삐가 아직 걸려 있는 브릴리아도로를 보았다. 그는 두린다나를 찾아 둘러보다가, 칼집도 없이 풀밭에 놓여 있는 그 유명한 칼을 발견했다. 그는 또한 오를란도의 다른 무기들과 옷가지의 조각들이 들판 사방에 흩어져 있는 것도 보았다. 제르비노와 이사벨라는 놀라움과 슬픔에 잠겨 어찌 생각해야 할지 알 수 없었으나, 그 참된 원인은 거의 짐작하지 못했다. 만약 그들이 무기나 옷 조각에서 핏자국을 발견했더라면 그가 죽임을 당했으리라 여겼겠지만, 핏자국은 전혀 없었다. 그들이 이 괴로운 불확실함에 잠겨 있을 때, 한 젊은 농부가 다가오는 것이 보였다. 그는 바위 꼭대기에서 그 장면을 지켜보았던 터라 아직 공포에서 벗어나지 못한 채, 그 슬픈 사건의 자초지종을 그들에게 모두 들려주었다.\n\n제르비노는 눈에 눈물을 가득 담고, 흩어진 무기들을 모두 정성스레 거두어 모았다. 이사벨라도 그 슬픈 임무를 돕고자 말에서 내렸다. 그들은 그 값진 갑옷의 조각들을 모두 모은 뒤, 그것들을 전리품처럼 소나무에 매달았다. 그리고 지나가는 누군가가 그것을 훼손하지 못하도록 제르비노는 나무껍질에 이렇게 경고를 새겼다. \"이것은 기사 오를란도의 무기다.\"\n\n이 경건한 일을 마친 그는 다시 말에 올랐고, 바로 그때 한 기사가 말을 타고 다가와 제르비노에게 그 전리품의 뜻을 말해 달라고 청했다. 왕자는 일이 일어난 그대로 사실을 들려주었다. 그러자 그 사라센 기사, 곧 만드리카르도는 기쁨에 가득 차서 앞으로 달려나가 칼을 움켜쥐며 말했다. \"내가 하는 일을 두고 누구도 나를 나무랄 수 없다. 이 칼은 내 것이니, 내 것은 어디서 찾든 가질 수 있다. 오를란도가 나를 상대로 이것을 지킬 엄두를 내지 못하고, 칼을 넘겨준 변명거리로 광기를 꾸며낸 것이 분명하다.\"\n\n제르비노는 격렬하게 외쳤다. \"그 칼에 손대지 마라. 싸움 없이 그것을 차지하리라 생각지 마라. 네가 두른 갑옷이 헥토르의 것이라는 말이 사실이라면, 너는 그것을 무용이 아니라 도둑질로 얻었으리라.\"\n\n곧바로 그들은 더없는 격렬함으로 서로에게 달려들었다. 빗발치듯 쏟아지는 일격에 공기가 울렸다. 제르비노는 능숙하고 민첩하여 한동안 두린다나의 일격을 잘 피해냈으나, 마침내 무시무시한 일격이 그의 목을 쳤다. 그는 말에서 떨어졌고, 타르타르의 왕은 승리의 전리품을 차지하고 말을 달려 떠났다.\n제르비노와 이사벨라\n\n# ZERBINO AND ISABELLA\n\n타르타르의 왕자가 칼을 가지고 떠나는 것을 보는 제르비노의 고통은 상처의 아픔을 넘어섰다. 그러나 이제 출혈로 기력이 너무 쇠하여 그는 쓰러진 자리에서 움직일 수조차 없었다. 어디로 가서 도움을 구해야 할지 모르는 이사벨라는 그저 그를 애도하며 자신의 잔인한 운명을 탓할 뿐이었다. 제르비노가 말했다. \"내가 그대를, 나의 가장 사랑하는 이를, 어딘가 안전한 거처에 남겨 둘 수만 있다면 죽는 것도 괴롭지 않으련만. 그러나 그대를 이렇게, 보호자도 없이 버려 두고 가야 하다니 참으로 슬프오.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"저를 두고 갈 생각 마세요, 사랑하는 이여. 우리 두 영혼은 갈라지지 않을 거예요. 이 칼이 당신을 따를 길을 제게 줄 테니까요.\" 제르비노의 마지막 말은 그녀에게 그런 생각을 떨쳐 버리고 살아남아 자신의 기억에 충실하라고 간청하는 것이었다. 이사벨라는 숱한 눈물을 흘리며, 목숨이 붙어 있는 한 그에게 충실하겠노라 약속했다.\n\n그가 숨을 거두자 이사벨라의 울음소리가 숲을 울려 퍼졌고, 한 경건한 은둔자의 귀에까지 닿았다. 그는 서둘러 그곳으로 왔다. 그는 하느님의 말씀이 주는 위안을 권하며 그녀를 달래고 진정시켰고, 마침내 그녀로 하여금 남은 생을 오로지 신앙에 바치는 것 외에는 아무것도 바라지 않게 만들었다.\n\n죽은 자신의 주인을 버려 두고 떠난다는 생각을 견딜 수 없었던 터라, 선량한 은둔자의 도움으로 시신은 말 위에 실려 가장 가까운 마을로 옮겨졌고, 거기서 길을 가는 동안 함께 운반하기에 알맞은 궤가 만들어졌다. 은둔자의 계획은 자신이 맡은 그녀를 며칠 거리에 있는 수도원으로 호위해 가는 것이었고, 이사벨라는 그곳에서 남은 생을 보내기로 결심했다. 그리하여 그들은 날마다 길을 떠났는데, 나라 안이 무장한 자들로 가득했으므로 가장 한적한 길을 골랐다. 어느 날 한 기사가 그들을 만나 길을 가로막았다. 그는 다름 아닌 알제의 왕 로도몬트였으니, 그는 도랄리체에게서 받은 대우에 분노가 가득한 채 막 아그라만트의 진영을 떠나온 참이었다. 그 사랑스러운 여인과 그녀의 경건한 동행, 그리고 검은 천을 두른 짐을 실은 그들의 말을 보고, 그는 그들이 어디로 가는 길인지 물었다. 이사벨라는 그에게 자신의 비통함과, 세상을 버리고 신앙에, 그리고 잃어버린 벗의 기억에 자신을 바치기로 한 결심을 말했다. 로도몬트는 이를 비웃으며, 그녀의 계획은 어리석은 것이라고, 그녀와 같은 미모는 묻어 둘 것이 아니라 누려야 할 것이라고, 그리고 죽은 연인 대신 자신이 그 이상으로 보상해 주겠노라고 말했다. 이 불경한 말을 꾸짖으려 즉시 끼어든 수도사는 입을 다물라는 명령을 받았고, 그래도 끈질기게 굴자 기사에게 붙들려 절벽 가장자리 너머로 내던져졌으니, 그는 바다로 떨어져 익사하고 말았다.\n\n로도몬트는 은둔자를 처치한 뒤, 공포에 넋이 나간 그 슬픈 여인에게 다시 다가가, 연인들이 쓰는 말투로 \"그녀가 자신의 바로 그 심장이요, 자신의 생명이요, 자신의 빛\"이라고 말했다. 그는 온갖 난폭함을 버린 채, 가까이 있는 자신의 거처로 함께 가 달라고 겸손히 간청했다. 그곳은 시국의 혼란으로 수도사들이 쫓겨난 폐허가 된 예배당이었고, 로도몬트가 차지하고 있던 곳이었다. 따르는 것 외에 달리 도리가 없는 이사벨라는 그를 뒤따랐는데, 가는 동안 어떻게 하면 그의 손아귀에서 벗어날 방도를 찾을 수 있을지, 그리고 목숨이 붙어 있는 한 그의 기억에 충실하겠노라 죽은 남편에게 한 맹세를 어떻게 지킬지 골똘히 궁리했다. 마침내 그녀가 말했다. \"나리, 만약 제가 이미 말씀드린 대로 가서 제 맹세와 제 뜻을 이루게 해 주신다면, 백 명의 여인의 마음보다 나리께 더 값진 것을 드리겠습니다. 저는 어떤 약초를 알고 있는데, 오는 길에 그것을 보았습니다. 제대로 조제하면 그 즙이 어찌나 강력한지, 살에 그것을 바르면 칼이나 불로도 뚫을 수 없게 됩니다. 이 약물을 저는 만들 수 있고, 나리께서 제 제안을 받아들이신다면 오늘 만들어 드리겠습니다. 그 효험을 보시면 나리께서는 온 유럽을 손에 넣는 것보다 그것을 더 귀하게 여기실 것입니다.\"\n\n이 말을 듣고 로도몬트는, 옛날의 아킬레우스처럼 자신을 만들어 줄 비결을 배우려는 열망에 사로잡혀, 청하는 것을 모두 선뜻 약속했다. 이사벨라는 알맞다고 여긴 약초들을 모아 끓이며 어떤 신비로운 표시와 말들을 곁들였고, 마침내 자신의 작업이 끝났음을 알렸다. 그리고 시험 삼아 그 효험을 자기 자신에게 시험해 보겠다고 제안했다. 그녀는 그 약물로 자신의 목과 가슴을 적신 뒤, 로도몬트에게 온 힘을 다해 내리쳐 그의 칼이 해를 입힐 힘이 있는지 보라고 청했다. 조제하는 동안 자주 포도주를 들이켜 자기가 무슨 짓을 하는지도 거의 분간하지 못하던 그 이교도는, 그 말에 칼을 뽑아 온 힘을 다해 그녀의 목을 가로질러 쳤고, 그 아름다운 머리는 눈처럼 흰 목과 가슴에서 잘려 나가 떨어졌다.\n\n거칠고 무정한 자였으나, 그 이교도 기사는 이 슬픈 결말을 비통하게 한탄했다. 그녀의 기억을 기리고자 그는 그녀의 헌신만큼이나 비할 데 없는 일을 하기로 결심했다. 그는 사방에서 일꾼들을 불러들여, 예배당을 둘러싸는 탑을 세우게 했으며, 그 안에 제르비노와 이사벨라의 유해를 안치했다. 가까이 흐르는 시냇물 위에는 폭이 채 두 야드도 안 되는 다리를 세웠는데, 난간도 손잡이도 두지 않았다. 탑 꼭대기에는 파수꾼을 두어, 어떤 길손이든 다리에 다가오면 주인에게 알리게 했다. 그러면 로도몬트가 뛰쳐나가, 다가오는 기사에게 다리 위에서 싸우자고 도전했으니, 그 위에서는 옆으로 조금만 발을 헛디뎌도 말 탄 자가 곤두박질쳐 시내로 빠지게 되어 있었다. 그는 정복한 기사들에게서 천 벌의 갑옷을 얻어 자신의 희생자와 그녀의 주인을 위한 전승비를 세우기 전까지는 이 다리를 지키겠노라 맹세했다.\n\n열흘 만에 다리가 세워졌고, 탑도 공사가 진행되고 있었다. 머지않아 많은 기사들이, 가장 짧은 길을 찾아서든 모험에 대한 욕망에 이끌려서든, 그 다리를 건너려 시도했다. 예외 없이 모두가 무기든 목숨이든, 혹은 둘 다를 잃었다. 어떤 이는 로도몬트의 창에 쓰러졌고, 어떤 이는 강물로 곤두박질쳤다. 어느 날 로도몬트가 서서 일꾼들을 재촉하고 있을 때, 마침 광기에 사로잡힌 오를란도가 그곳에 와서 다리로 다가왔다. 로도몬트가 그에게 소리쳤다. \"멈춰라, 이 촌놈아. 감히 저 다리에 발을 들여놓을 생각 마라. 저것은 너 같은 자를 위해 만든 것이 아니다!\" 오를란도는 아랑곳하지 않고 밀고 나아갔다. 바로 그때 한 정숙한 처녀가 말을 타고 다가왔다. 그녀는 자신의 플로리스마르트를 찾고 있던 플로르델리스였다. 그녀는 오를란도를 보았고, 그의 기이한 몰골에도 불구하고 그를 알아보았다. 자신의 명령이 거역당하는 데 익숙지 않은 로도몬트는 그 미치광이에게 손을 대어 강물에 던져 넣으려 했으나, 놀랍게도 그렇게 쉽사리 처리할 수 없는 자의 손아귀에 자신이 잡혀 있음을 깨달았다. \"어떻게 바보가 이런 힘을 가졌단 말인가?\" 그가 이를 갈며 으르렁거렸다. 플로르델리스는 멈추어 서서 결말을 지켜보았으니, 그곳에서는 이 두 막강한 전사가 서로를 다리에서 떨어뜨리려 안간힘을 쓰고 있었다. 마침내 오를란도가 적을 갑옷째 들어 올려 다리 너머로 던질 만큼 힘을 냈으나, 자신을 그에게서 떼어낼 만한 지혜는 없었기에 둘은 함께 떨어지고 말았다. 둘이 함께 수면을 치자 물보라가 높이 솟구쳤다. 여기서는 오를란도가 유리했다. 그는 벌거벗은 몸이었고 물고기처럼 헤엄칠 수 있었다. 그는 곧 강기슭에 닿았고, 칭찬도 비난도 개의치 않은 채 그 모험의 결말이 어찌 되는지 보려고 멈추지 않았다. 갑옷에 발이 묶인 로도몬트는 가까스로 강기슭으로 빠져나왔다. 그 사이 플로르델리스는 아무런 제지 없이 다리를 건넜다.\n\n성과 없이 오래 헤맨 끝에 그녀는 파리로 돌아왔고, 거기서 자신이 찾던 이를 발견했다. 플로리스마르트가 알브라카가 함락된 뒤 그곳으로 와 있었기 때문이다. 다시 만난 기쁨도, 오를란도의 비참한 처지에 관해 플로르델리스가 전한 소식에 플로리스마르트에게는 흐려지고 말았다. 그녀가 그를 마지막으로 본 것은 그가 로도몬트와 함께 시내로 떨어졌을 때였다. 오를란도를 형제처럼 사랑하던 플로리스마르트는 그 여인의 안내를 받아 즉시 길을 떠나 그를 찾아내어, 그의 상태에 맞는 치료를 받을 수 있는 곳으로 데려가기로 결심했다. 며칠 만에 그들은 타르타르의 왕이 여전히 다리를 지키고 있는 그곳에 이르렀다. 늘 하던 대로 도전과 응전이 이루어졌고, 두 기사는 다리 위에서 맞붙으러 말을 달렸다. 첫 격돌에서 두 말이 모두 쓰러졌고, 발을 다시 디딜 공간이 없었으므로 말과 기사가 함께 물속으로 떨어졌다. 시내의 수심을 알고 있던 로도몬트는 곧 뭍으로 올라왔으나, 플로리스마르트는 물살에 떠내려가 마침내 말이 발 디딜 곳을 거의 찾지 못하는 진흙 둑에 닿았다. 다리에서 싸움을 지켜보던 플로르델리스는 자신의 연인이 이 가련한 처지에 빠진 것을 보고 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"아! 로도몬트여, 당신이 죽어서도 기리는 그녀를 사랑하는 마음으로, 이 기사를 사랑하는 저를 가엾이 여겨 그를 죽이지 말아 주세요. 그가 자신의 갑옷을 그 무더기에 바치는 것으로 족하게 해 주세요. 그 어떤 것도 그의 갑옷보다 더 영광스럽지는 못할 테니까요.\" 그토록 잘 겨눈 그녀의 간청은, 비록 움직이기 어려운 것이었으나 그 이교도의 마음을 움직였고, 그는 기사가 뭍에 오르도록 도움을 주었다. 그러나 그는 그를 포로로 붙잡아 두었고, 그의 갑옷을 그 무더기에 더했다. 플로르델리스는 무거운 마음으로 가던 길을 떠났다.\n\n이제 우리는 로제로에게로 돌아가야 한다. 우리가 그와 헤어졌을 때, 그는 자신의 진로를 가로막은 한 모험에 얽혀 있었다. 그는 세례를 받아 브라다만테를 신부로 맞을 자격을 갖추려는 뜻으로 수도원을 향해 가던 중이었다. 가는 길에 그는 만드리카르도를 만났고, 헥토르의 표장을 두를 권리를 둘러싼 다툼이 다시 일어났다. 격렬한 논쟁 끝에 양측은 그 문제를 아그라만트 왕에게 맡기기로 합의했고, 그 목적으로 사라센 진영으로 향했다. 거기서 그들은 그라다소를 만났는데, 그 또한 만드리카르도와 다툼이 있었다. 이 전사는 오를란도의 칼을 주장하며, 그 칼이 주인에게 버림받은 것을 발견했다는 이유로 만드리카르도가 그것을 차지할 권리를 부인했다. 아그라만트 왕은 이 다툼들을 화해시키려 애썼으나 헛수고였고, 마침내 분쟁의 쟁점들을 한 차례의 결투로 해결하는 데 동의할 수밖에 없었다. 그 결투에서 만드리카르도는 다른 두 용사 가운데 한 사람과 맞붙기로 하였고, 그 사람에게 양측의 주장이 맡겨지게 되었다. 제비뽑기로 로제로가 그라다소의 주장과 자신의 주장을 함께 옹호하게 되었다. 이 중대한 결투를 위해 큰 준비가 이루어졌다. 정해진 날, 아그라만트와 전 군대가 지켜보는 가운데 결투가 벌어졌다. 로제로가 승리했다. 그리고 헥토르의 무기를 차지한 자이자, 오를란도에게 도전한 자이자, 제르비노를 죽인 자인 만드리카르도는 목숨을 잃었다. 그라다소는 두린다나를 상으로 받았으나, 그것이 자신의 무용이 아니라 남의 무용으로 얻어진 것이었기에 그의 눈에는 그 가치가 절반으로 떨어졌다.\n\n로제로는 승리했음에도 심한 부상을 입어, 아그라만트의 진영에서 여러 주 동안 무력하게 누워 있었다. 한편 브라다만테는 그가 늦어지는 까닭을 모른 채 몬탈반에서 그를 기다리고 있었다. 그는 보름 안에, 늦어도 스무날 안에 그곳으로 가겠노라 약속했는데, 그때쯤이면 사라센 사령관에 대한 자신의 의무에서 명예롭게 벗어날 수 있으리라 기대했던 것이다. 스무날이 지나고, 다시 한 달이 더 지나도 로제로는 오지 않았고, 그가 보이지 않는 까닭을 알려 줄 어떤 소식도 브라다만테에게 닿지 않았다. 그 기간이 끝날 무렵, 한 떠돌이 기사가 그 유명한 결투와 로제로의 부상에 관한 소식을 전했다. 그는 브라다만테를 더욱 불안하게 만드는 말을 덧붙였으니, 젊고 아름다운 여전사 마르피사가 그 부상당한 기사를 간호하고 있다는 것이었다. 그는 또한 로제로의 상처가 낫는 대로 그 두 사람이 혼인으로 맺어지리라고 온 군대가 기대하고 있다는 말도 덧붙였다.\n\n이 소식에 괴로워하던 브라다만테는, 비록 그것을 일부만 믿었으나, 즉시 가서 직접 확인하기로 결심했다. 그녀는 아스톨포가 자신에게 맡긴 말 라비칸에 올랐고, 그 놀라운 힘은 알지 못한 채 황금 창을 챙겼다. 이렇게 차림을 갖춘 그녀는 성을 떠나 파리와 사라센 진영을 향한 길로 들어섰다.\n\n로제로의 병중에 그를 향한 헌신으로 브라다만테의 질투를 그토록 자극했던 마르피사는, 사실 로제로의 쌍둥이 누이였다. 그녀는 로제로와 함께 갓난아이였을 때 마법사 아틀란테스의 보살핌을 받았으나, 아직 어린아이였을 적에 한 아랍 부족에게 도둑맞았다. 그들의 우두머리에게 입양된 그녀는 일찍이 마술(馬術)과 무예를 익혔고, 이 무렵 양 진영 전사들의 무용을 직접 보고 시험해 보겠다는 것 외에는 다른 뜻 없이 아그라만트의 진영에 와 있었으니, 그들의 명성이 온 세상에 울려 퍼지고 있었기 때문이다. 바로 그 앞선 결투의 순간에 도착한 그녀는, 로제로라는 이름과 자신이 알게 된 그의 이야기 몇 가지만으로도, 일대일 결투에서 승리하는 모습을 본 자가 자신의 오라비라는 생각을 떠올리기에 충분했다. 알아본 결과 두 사람은 자신들이 가까운 혈육임을 확인했고, 그 순간부터 마르피사는 새로 찾은, 그토록 사랑하는 오라비를 돌보는 데 자신을 바쳤다.\n\n그렇게 은거하던 동안 로제로는 늙은 아틀란테스에게서 자신들의 혈통에 관해 알게 된 바를 누이에게 알려 주었다. 그들의 아버지인 로제로는 기독교 기사였는데, 아프리카 술탄의 딸이자 아그라만트 왕의 누이인 갈라키엘라의 마음을 얻어 그녀를 기독교 신앙으로 개종시키고 은밀히 혼인했다. 딸의 혼인에 격노한 술탄은 사위를 추방하고, 딸을 그 갓난 자식들인 로제로와 마르피사와 함께 배에 태워 바람과 파도에 맡겨 죽게 했으나, 그들은 아틀란테스에 의해 그 운명에서 구해졌다. 이를 듣고 마르피사가 외쳤다. \"오라버니, 어찌 그토록 오랫동안 우리 부모님의 원수를 갚지 않은 채 두고, 게다가 그분들에게 그토록 몹쓸 짓을 한 폭군의 아들을 섬기기까지 한단 말이에요?\" 로제로는 자신이 온전한 진실을 안 것은 최근의 일이라고, 그것을 알았을 때는 이미 아그라만트와 함께 배에 오른 뒤였고 그에게서 기사 작위를 받은 뒤였노라고, 그래서 자신은 명예롭게 그의 깃발을 버리고 동시에 조상의 신앙으로 돌아갈 수 있는 알맞은 기회만을 기다리고 있노라고 대답했다. 마르피사는 이 결심을 기쁘게 맞이하며, 자신도 그와 함께 기독교 신앙을 받아들이겠다는 뜻을 밝혔다.\n\n우리가 브라다만테와 헤어진 것은, 그녀가 라비칸에 올라 아스톨포의 창으로 무장하고 로제로의 오랜 부재의 까닭을 알아내기로 작정한 채 말을 달려 나갔을 때였다. 어느 날, 말을 달리던 그녀는 한 처녀를 만났는데, 얼굴과 거동이 고왔으나 슬픔에 짓눌려 있었다. 그녀는 자신의 주인을 구하고 그 원수를 갚아 줄 용사를 멀리까지 찾아 헤매던 플로르델리스였다. 플로르델리스는 다가오는 전사를 눈여겨보았고, 겉모습으로 미루어 자신이 찾던 용사를 만났다고 생각했다. \"기사님,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"제 주인을 포로로 삼고 저를 이렇게 떠돌이요 탄원자로 만든, 사납고 잔인한 한 전사를 상대로 제 사정을 맡아 주실 만큼 대담하고 자비로우신가요?\" 그러고는 다리에서 일어났던 일들을 들려주었다. 고귀한 모험이라면 언제나 반갑게 여기던 브라다만테는 이를 선뜻 받아들였으니, 더욱이 자신의 어두운 예감 속에서 로제로가 영영 자신에게서 떠나 버린 것만 같았기 때문이다.\n\n이튿날 두 사람은 다리에 다다랐다. 파수꾼이 그들이 다가오는 것을 보고 주인에게 알렸고, 주인은 이에 갑옷을 차려입고 그들을 맞으러 나왔다. 여기서 그는 늘 하던 대로, 다가오는 전사에게 말과 무기를 무덤에 바치는 제물로 내놓으라고 요구했다. 브라다만테는 무슨 권리로 죄 없는 자에게 그의 죄에 대한 속죄를 시키느냐고 물으며 대답했다. \"네 목숨과 네 갑옷이야말로,\" 그녀가 덧붙였다. \"그녀의 무덤에 가장 어울리는 제물이며, 여인인 내가 그것을 취하기에 가장 어울리는 용사다.\" 그 말과 함께 그녀는 창을 겨누고 말에 박차를 가하며 격돌하러 달려 나갔다. 로도몬트 왕도 빠르게 다가왔다. 다리 위에서 짓밟는 소리가 천둥처럼 울렸다. 승부를 가르는 데는 한순간이면 족했다. 황금 창이 제 몫을 다하자, 마상 시합에서 그토록 명성 높던 그 사나운 무어인이 다리 위에 길게 뻗어 누웠다. \"이제 패자는 누구냐?\" 브라다만테가 말했다. 그러나 여인의 손에 자신이 쓰러졌다는 데 어안이 벙벙해진 로도몬트는 대답할 수 없었거나, 대답하려 하지 않았다. 말없이 침울하게 그는 몸을 일으켜 투구와 갑옷을 풀어 무덤에 내던졌고, 그러고는 시무룩하게 걸어서 그 자리를 떠났다. 그러나 떠나기에 앞서 그는 자신의 종자 하나에게 모든 포로들을 풀어 주라고 명했다. 그들은 아프리카로 보내져 있었다. 플로리스마르트 외에도 산소네트와 올리버가 있었으니, 이들은 오를란도를 찾아 그 길로 말을 달리다가 둘 다 차례로 그 격돌에서 쓰러졌던 것이다.\n\n브라다만테는 승리한 뒤 다시 길을 떠났고, 때가 되어 기독교 진영에 다다랐다. 거기서 그녀는 자신을 그토록 불안하게 했던 수수께끼에 대한 설명을 쉽게 얻었다. 로제로와 그의 아름답고 용감한 누이 마르피사는 그 지위와 공적으로 너무도 빛나는 인물들이었기에, 적진에서조차 자주 이야깃거리가 되지 않을 수 없었고, 브라다만테가 알고자 애쓰던 모든 것이 거의 묻지 않아도 그녀의 귀에 들어왔다.\n\n이제 우리는 그라다소에게로 돌아간다. 그는 로제로의 승리로 두린다나의 소유자가 되었다. 이제 그에게 남은 것은 리날도의 말을 찾는 일뿐이었다. 그리고 주고받은 도전은 그 전사와 아직 치러야 할 것이었으니, 그 도전이 말라지지의 술수로 중단되었던 까닭이다. 그라다소는 이제 리날도와 다시 만나기를 청했고, 리날도 쪽에서도 꺼리지 않았다. 그 결투는 바야르드를 차지하기 위한 것이었으므로, 두 기사는 말에서 내려 도보로 싸웠다. 싸움은 오래 이어졌다. 두린다나의 치명적인 일격을 잘 아는 리날도는 그 타격을 막거나 피하려고 온갖 재주를 다 부렸다. 그라다소는 힘껏 내리쳤으나, 그의 일격은 거의 다 허공에 흩어졌고, 설령 닿더라도 비스듬히 떨어져 별다른 해를 입히지 못했다.\n\n이렇게 그들은 서로의 눈을 노려보며 다른 것은 아무것도 보지 못한 채 오래도록 싸웠는데, 그때 어떤 기이한 소리에 어쩔 수 없이 그들의 주의가 쏠렸다. 그들은 돌아보았고, 선량한 바야르드가 괴물 같은 새 한 마리에게 공격당하는 것을 보았다. 어쩌면 그것은 새였을지도 모른다. 그렇게 보였으니 말이다. 그러나 그런 새가 언제 어디서 본 적이 있는지 나는 투르팽 말고는 어디서도 읽은 바가 없다. 나로서는 그것이 새가 아니라, 말라지지가 땅 밑에서 불러내어 그 싸움을 중단시킬 목적으로 그곳에 보낸 악귀였다고 믿고 싶다. 악귀였든 새였든, 그 괴물은 곧장 바야르드에게 날아들어 그 얼굴에 날개를 퍼덕였다. 그러자 군마는 풀려나 미친 듯이 들판을 가로질러 달렸고, 그 새에게 쫓겨 마침내 숲 속으로 뛰어들어 시야에서 사라졌다.\n\n리날도와 그라다소는 바야르드가 달아나는 것을 보고, 다툼의 대상인 그 말을 되찾을 때까지 싸움을 미루기로 합의했다. 그라다소는 자신의 군마에 올라 숲 속으로 들어간 바야르드의 발자국을 따라갔다. 그 어느 때보다 심란해진 리날도는 그 자리에 남았으니, 그라다소가 말을 찾으면 그것을 데리고 그곳으로 돌아오겠노라 약속했기 때문이다. 그는 오래 찾은 끝에 정말로 말을 찾아냈으니, 운 좋게도 말이 우는 소리를 들었던 것이다. 이리하여 그는 자기 나라에서 군대를 이끌고 와 프랑스를 침공한 두 가지 목표를 모두 손에 넣게 되었다. 그는 리날도를 두고 온 곳으로 바야르드를 도로 데려가겠다는 약속을 잊지 않았으나, 그저 이렇게 중얼거릴 뿐이었다. \"이제 이놈을 손에 넣었으니, 내가 이놈을 내줄 거라고 기대하는 자는 나를 잘 모르는 것이다. 리날도가 이 말을 원한다면, 내가 프랑스에서 이놈을 찾아 헤맸듯이 인도에서 이놈을 찾으라고 해라.\" 그러고는 자신의 배들이 정박해 있는 아를로 부지런히 떠나갔다. 그리고 자신의 야망의 두 목표인 말과 칼을 손에 넣은 채, 배를 타고 자기 나라로 떠나갔다.\n아비시니아의 아스톨포\n\n# ASTOLPHO IN ABYSSINIA\n\n우리가 모험심 강한 기사 아스톨포와 마지막으로 헤어졌을 때, 그는 세계 각국의 상공을 가로지르는 그 비행을 막 시작하던 참이었으니, 그 비행에서 그는 큰 즐거움을 기대하고 있었다. 독수리도 매도 아스톨포가 탄 히포그리프만큼 빠르게 날지는 못한다는 것을 우리 독자들은 알고 있다. 따라서 그 기사가 남동쪽으로 진로를 잡아, 큰 강 나일이 발원하는 아프리카의 그 지역 상공에 다다르기까지는 오래 걸리지 않았다. 여기서 그는 내려앉았고, 자신이 세나푸스가 다스리는 아비시니아 수도 근처에 있음을 알게 되었으니, 세나푸스의 부와 권세는 어마어마했다. 그의 궁전은 비할 데 없이 찬란했다. 성문의 빗장이며 경첩이며 자물쇠가 모두 순금이었으니, 사실 그 나라에서는 우리가 철을 쓰는 모든 용도에 이 금속을 쓴다. 금이 너무도 흔하여 그들은 장식용으로는 수정을 더 좋아하며, 모든 기둥이 수정으로 만들어져 있다. 루비, 에메랄드, 사파이어, 토파즈 등 갖가지 보석들이 장식 무늬로 박혀 있었고, 벽과 천장은 진주로 꾸며져 있었다.\n\n저 유명한 향유들이 자라는 곳이 바로 이 나라이니, 그 향유들은 길레아드라 불리는 유대의 그 지역에 몇몇 나무로만 남아 있다. 유럽에서 그토록 귀한 사향, 용연향, 그리고 수많은 수지(樹脂)들이 여기서는 제 고향 기후 속에 있다. 이집트의 술탄은 이 나라의 군주에게 막대한 조공을 바쳐, 나일의 수원을 끊지 않도록 매수한다고 한다. 군주가 그렇게 하기는 쉬운 일이어서, 강물을 다른 방향으로 흐르게 하면 이집트는 그 비옥함의 원천을 빼앗기게 될 것이기 때문이다.\n\n아스톨포가 그의 영토에 도착했을 무렵, 이 군주는 큰 고통에 빠져 있었다. 그 부와 그 나라의 진귀한 산물에도 불구하고, 그는 굶어 죽을 위험에 처해 있었다. 그는 하르피이아이라 불리는 흉측한 새 떼의 먹잇감이 되어 있었으니, 그 새들은 그가 식사하려고 앉을 때마다 덮쳐들어 발톱으로 모든 것을 낚아채고 찢고 흩뜨려, 그릇을 엎고 음식을 게걸스레 먹어 치우며, 남긴 것은 그 더러운 손길로 더럽혔다. 이 벌이 그 왕에게 내려진 까닭은, 그가 젊었을 적에 교만과 오만에 가득 차서, 나일이 그 수원을 끌어오는 산꼭대기에 자리한 지상 낙원을 군대를 이끌고 침범하려 했기 때문이라고들 했다. 이것이 그의 유일한 벌도 아니었다. 그는 눈이 멀게 되었다.\n\n아스톨포는 이 군주의 영토에 도착하자, 서둘러 그에게 경의를 표하러 갔다. 세나푸스 왕은 그를 정중하게 맞이하였고, 그의 도착을 기리는 성대한 잔치를 준비하라 명했다. 손님들이 식탁에 앉아 있는 동안, 아스톨포가 왕의 오른편 상석을 차지하고 있을 때, 하르피이아이의 끔찍한 비명이 공중에 들려왔고, 곧 그것들이 다가와 식탁 위를 맴돌며 접시에서 음식을 낚아채고 그 넓은 날개를 퍼덕여 모든 것을 엎어 놓았다. 손님들은 칼이며 가진 무기로 그것들을 쳐 보았으나 헛일이었고, 아스톨포도 칼을 뽑아 거듭 일격을 가했으나, 그것들의 몸이 마치 삼 부스러기로 만들어진 양 아무 효과도 없는 듯했다.\n\n마침내 아스톨포는 자신의 뿔나팔을 떠올렸다. 그는 먼저 왕과 손님들에게 귀를 막으라고 일러두었고, 그러고는 한바탕 나팔을 불었다. 그 소리에 질겁한 하르피이아이는 날개가 데려갈 수 있는 한 빠르게 날아 달아났다. 기사는 히포그리프에 올라 그것들을 뒤쫓으며, 가까이 다가갈 때마다 뿔나팔을 불었다. 그것들은 큰 산을 향해 날아갔는데, 그 산기슭에는 지하 세계의 입구로 여겨지는 동굴이 하나 있었다. 그 흉측한 새들은 마치 제 집으로 가듯 이리로 날아들었다. 그것들이 모두 그 깊숙한 곳으로 사라지는 것을 본 아스톨포는 그것들을 더 쫓지 않고, 내려앉아 거대한 바위들을 굴려 동굴 입구를 막고 그 안에 나뭇가지들을 쌓아, 그것들이 빠져나올 길을 완전히 막아 버렸다. 그리하여 그 새들이 그 뒤로 바깥 공기에서 목격되었다는 증거는 어디에도 없다.\n\n이 수고를 마친 뒤 아스톨포는 바위 틈에서 맑은 물이 솟아나는 샘에서 목욕하며 원기를 회복했다. 한동안 쉬고 나니, 그의 위로 우뚝 솟은 산을 올라 보고 싶은 간절한 욕망이 그를 사로잡았다. 히포그리프는 그를 재빨리 위로 실어 날라, 산꼭대기에 내려놓았는데, 그곳은 드넓은 평원이었다. 이 평원 한가운데에 찬란한 궁전이 솟아 있었는데, 그 벽이 어찌나 눈부시게 빛났던지 인간의 눈으로는 그 광경을 도저히 견딜 수 없을 정도였다. 아스톨포는 날개 달린 말을 그 건물 쪽으로 몰아, 이 축복받은 곳과 그 주변을 느긋이 살펴보는 동안 공중에 떠 있게 했다. 마치 자연과 기예가 서로 겨루어, 어느 쪽이 그곳을 더 아름답게 꾸밀 수 있는지 다툰 듯했다.\n\n아스톨포가 그 건물에 다가가자, 한 위엄 있는 노인이 그를 맞으러 나오는 것이 보였다. 이 인물은 눈처럼 흰 긴 옷을 입고 있었고, 어깨에는 자줏빛 망토를 둘러 땅까지 늘어뜨리고 있었다. 흰 수염이 허리께까지 내려왔고, 같은 빛깔의 머리카락이 어깨를 덮고 있었다. 그의 눈이 어찌나 빛났던지 아스톨포는 그가 천상의 거처에 사는 축복받은 자라고 확신하게 되었다.\n\n그 현인은, 존경의 뜻으로 말에서 내린 기사를 향해 자애롭게 미소 지으며 말했다. \"고귀한 기사여, 신의 뜻에 의해 그대가 지상 낙원으로 인도되었음을 알라. 그대가 샤를을 구할 방도를, 그리고 우리 거룩한 신앙의 영광을 떠받칠 방도를 배우도록 하는 것이 하늘의 뜻이 아니었다면, 그대의 필멸의 몸으로는 이 높은 곳을 오르고 이 축복의 자리에 이를 수 없었으리라. 나는 필요한 조언을 베풀 준비가 되어 있다. 그러나 시작하기에 앞서 우리의 거처에 온 그대를 환영하게 해 다오. 그대의 오랜 단식과 먼 여정으로 입맛이 좋아졌으리라 의심치 않는다.\"\n\n그 위엄 있는 노인의 모습은 기사를 경탄으로 채웠다. 그러나 그가 우리 주님의 사도들 가운데 한 사람, 곧 주님이 \"내가 올 때까지 그가 머물기를 원하노라\" 하고 말씀하신 그 사람임을 그에게서 알게 되자, 그의 놀라움은 가셨다.\n\n성 요한은 아스톨포를 데리고 자신의 동료들에게 다시 갔다. 이들은 족장 에녹과 예언자 엘리야였으니, 둘 다 아직 죽는 날을 보지 못한 채 우리 하계(下界)에서 옮겨져, 최후의 나팔이 울릴 때까지 영원한 봄의 기후 속, 평화와 기쁨의 영역에 살고 있었다.\n\n지상 낙원에 사는 이 세 거룩한 이들은 아스톨포를 더없이 친절하게 맞이하여, 그를 쾌적한 방으로 데려갔고, 히포그리프도 정성껏 돌보아 그에게 맞는 먹이를 주었다. 한편 기사에게는 더없이 맛있는 과일들을 내놓았으니, 그는 우리 최초의 조상들이 허락도 없이 그것을 따 먹은 죄를 너그러이 용서해 주고 싶은 마음이 들 정도였다.\n\n아스톨포는 이 훌륭한 과일들뿐 아니라 달콤한 잠으로도 기력을 회복하고는, 동틀 무렵 첫 햇살에 잠에서 깨어, 방을 나서자마자 자신을 찾아오는 사랑하는 사도를 만났다. 성 요한은 그의 손을 잡고, 과거와 미래에 관한 많은 것을 그에게 일러 주었다. 그 가운데 그는 이렇게 말했다. \"아들아, 지금 프랑스에서 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 일러 주마. 오를란도는, 태어날 때 필멸자 이상의 힘과 용기를 부여받은 그 빼어난 기사로서, 옛 삼손이 그러했듯 참된 신앙의 용사로 세워진 자이거늘, 기독교 진영이 그의 팔의 도움을 가장 필요로 할 때 그곳을 떠나 한 사라센 공주의 뒤를 쫓는 가장 비열한 배은망덕의 죄를 저질렀다. 그 공주는 그를 멸시하는데도 그는 그녀와 혼인하기를 바라 마지않았던 것이다. 그를 벌하고자 그의 이성이 거두어졌으니, 그리하여 그는 한 줄기 분별도 없이 산을 넘고 골짜기를 지나 벌거벗은 채 온 땅을 내달리고 있다. 그 벌의 기간은 석 달로 정해졌고, 그 시한이 거의 다 차 가는 터라, 오를란도의 이성을 되찾아 줄 방도를 우리에게서 배우도록 그대가 이리로 인도된 것이다. 다만 그대는 나와 함께 한 여정을 떠나야 할 것이며, 우리는 이 땅을 떠나 달에 올라야 하기까지 할 것이다. 그 기사의 광기를 치료할 약을 찾아야 할 곳이 바로 그 천체이기 때문이다. 나는 오늘 저녁, 달이 우리 머리 위에 떠오르는 대로 여정을 떠나자고 제안한다.\"\n\n해가 바다 아래로 가라앉고 달이 그 빛나는 원반을 드러내자마자, 그 거룩한 이는 자신이 별들 사이를 다닐 때 쓰던 마차를 끌어내게 했으니, 그것은 오래전 엘리야를 땅에서 위로 실어 올리는 데 쓰였던 바로 그 마차였다. 성인은 아스톨포를 자기 곁에 앉히고 고삐를 잡았으며, 준마들에게 신호를 보내자 그것들은 놀라운 속도로 두 사람을 위로 실어 날랐다.\n\n마침내 그들은 달의 거대한 대륙에 다다랐다. 그 표면은 광을 낸 강철 같았는데, 여기저기 녹처럼 그 광채를 흐리는 얼룩이 있었다. 그 기사는 모든 바다와 강을 거느린 지구가 저 멀리 하찮은 한 점에 지나지 않아 보이는 것을 보고 놀랐다.\n\n이 왕자는 자신에게 너무도 새로운 이 영역에서 강과 호수와 평원과 언덕과 골짜기를 발견했다. 많은 아름다운 도시와 성들이 그 풍경을 풍요롭게 했다. 그는 또한 광대한 숲들을 보았고, 그 안에서 뿔나팔 소리와 개 짖는 소리를 들었으니, 그것으로 미루어 그는 요정들이 사냥을 하고 있으리라 짐작했다.\n\n자신이 본 모든 것에 경이로 가득 찬 그 기사는, 성인의 인도를 받아 한 골짜기로 들어섰는데, 거기서 그는 사방에 흩어진 부(富)에 놀라 멍하니 섰다. 그럴 만도 했으니, 그 골짜기는 사람의 잘못으로든, 세월과 우연의 작용으로든 지상에서 잃어버린 것들을 받아 두는 곳이었기 때문이다. 우리가 여기서 왕국이나 보물을 말한다고는 아무도 생각지 말라. 그것들은 운명의 여신이 자신의 수레바퀴를 돌리며 베푸는 장난감일 뿐이다. 우리가 말하는 것은 그 여신이 주지도 빼앗지도 못하는 것들이다. 한때 그토록 찬란히 빛나다가 얼마 지나지 않아 더는 소식조차 들리지 않게 되는 명성이 그러한 것이다. 여기에는 또한 이룰 수 없는 것을 바라는 수없는 맹세와 기도, 연인들의 한숨과 눈물, 노름과 치장과 빈둥거림에 허비된 시간, 둔한 자들의 여가와 게으른 자들의 의도, 근거 없는 계획, 음모, 모략이 있었으니, 이런 것들과 그 비슷한 것들이 온 골짜기를 채우고 있었다.\n\n아스톨포는 자신이 본 것, 그리고 자신에게 그토록 기이해 보이는 것을 모두 이해하고 싶은 큰 욕망이 있었다. 그 가운데서 그는 부풀어 오른 방광들로 이루어진 큰 산을 보았는데, 거기서 알아들을 수 없는 소리들이 흘러나왔다. 성인은 그것들이 한때 세상의 경이였던 아시리아와 페르시아 왕들의 왕조들이며, 이제는 그 이름조차 거의 남지 않았다고 일러 주었다. 성인이 그에게 \"그대가 보는 저 은과 금으로 된 갈고리들은 모두 더 나은 것을 보답으로 얻으려는 바람에서 신하들이 군주들에게 바친 선물들이다\"라고 말하자 아스톨포는 웃지 않을 수 없었다. 성인은 또한 그에게 올가미가 숨겨진 꽃 화환들을 보여 주었으니, 이것들은 속이려는 뜻으로 한 아첨과 아부였다. 그러나 무엇보다도 우스꽝스러운 것은, 지저귀다가 허파가 터져 버린 수많은 메뚜기들의 광경이었다. 성인은 그에게, 이것들은 돈에 매수된 시인들이 권세 있는 자들에게 바친 소네트와 송시(頌詩)와 헌사라고 일러 주었다.\n\n그 기사는 엎질러진 우유의 호수처럼 보이는 것을 경이롭게 바라보았다. \"저것은,\" 성인이 말했다. \"겁에 질린 구두쇠들이 임종의 자리에서 베푼 자선이다.\" 그 골짜기에 담긴 것을 모두 이야기하자면 너무 길어질 것이다. 비열함, 허식, 거짓 미덕, 감춰진 악덕이 거기에 가득했다.\n\n그 가운데서 아스톨포는 자신이 잃어버린 숱한 날들과, 자신이 저질렀던, 그리고 다시는 떠올리고 싶지 않은 숱한 경솔한 행동들을 알아보았다. 그러나 그는 또한 그 숱한 잃어버린 것들 가운데, 사람들이 누구나 다 지니고 있다고 여기기 쉽고 굳이 빌어서 얻을 필요가 없다고 생각하는 한 가지가 아주 풍부하게 있는 것을 보았으니, 곧 양식(良識)이었다. 이 물건은 더없이 가볍고 쉽게 증발하는 액체의 형태로 나타났다. 그래서 그것은 단단히 봉인된 작은 병들에 담겨 보관되어 있었다. 그 가운데 하나에는 \"기사 오를란도의 분별\"이라는 딱지가 붙어 있었다.\n\n모든 병에 딱지가 붙어 있었고, 그 현인은 그 가운데 하나를 아스톨포의 손에 쥐어 주었는데, 그것이 자신의 것임을 그는 알아차렸다. 그것은 절반 이상 차 있었다. 그는 사람들 사이에서 지혜롭다고 통하는 많은 이들의 재기를 거의 다 담은 다른 병들이 거기 많이 있는 것을 보고 놀랐다. 아, 사람이 이성을 잃기란 얼마나 쉬운 일인가! 어떤 이들은 정념의 지배에 굴복하여 이성을 잃고, 어떤 이들은 부를 좇아 폭풍과 모래톱을 무릅쓰다가 잃으며, 어떤 이들은 권세 있는 자들의 약속을 지나치게 믿다가 잃고, 어떤 이들은 하찮은 것에 마음을 쏟다가 잃는다. 능히 짐작할 수 있듯이, 점성가, 발명가, 형이상학자, 그리고 무엇보다도 시인들의 재기를 담은 병들이 대체로 다른 모든 것들 가운데 가장 잘 차 있었다.\n\n아스톨포는 자신의 병을 집어 코에 대고 그것을 모두 들이마셨다. 투르팽은 그가 그 후로 오랫동안 누구나 바랄 만큼 지혜로웠다고 우리에게 단언한다. 다만 대주교는, 그 귀한 액체의 일부가 나중에 다시 병 속으로 돌아갔을까 염려할 만한 까닭이 있다고 덧붙인다. 그 기사는 오를란도에게 속한 병도 집어 들었다. 그것은 큰 병이었고, 가득 차 있었다.\n\n천체의 영역을 떠나기에 앞서 아스톨포는 한 강가에 있는 어느 건물로 인도되었다. 그는 비단, 아마포, 면, 양모 다발이 가득한 거대한 홀을 보게 되었다. 천 가지 갖가지 빛깔이, 밝거나 흐릿하거나 더러는 아주 검은 것이, 이 실타래들 가운데 섞여 있었다. 홀의 한쪽에서는 한 노파가 이 갖가지 다발들에서 실을 감아내는 일에 분주했다. 그녀가 한 타래를 다 끝내면 또 다른 늙은 부인이 그것을 받아 다른 것들과 함께 놓았고, 세 번째 부인은 자아 놓은 양털 가운데서 골라 알맞은 비율로 섞었다. 그 기사는 이 모든 것이 무엇을 뜻하는지 물었다. \"이 노파들은,\" 성인이 말했다. \"필멸자들의 생명을 잣고 재고 끊는 운명의 여신들이다. 저 실타래 가운데 하나에서 실이 늘어나 있는 동안에는 그만큼 그 필멸자가 햇빛을 누리지만, 자연과 죽음이 방심하지 않고 지켜보다가 실이 다 자아진 자들의 눈을 감긴다.\"\n\n각 실타래마다 금이나 은이나 철로 된 딱지가 붙어, 그것이 속한 사람의 이름이 적혀 있었다. 한 노인이, 나이의 무게에도 불구하고 활기차고 민첩해 보였는데, 쉴 새 없이 달려와 이 딱지들로 자기 앞치마를 채우고는, 레테라 불리는 강에 던져 넣으려 가져갔다. 강기슭에 이르자 그 노인은 앞치마를 털었고, 딱지들은 바닥으로 가라앉았다. 오직 적은 수만이 한동안 떠 있었으니, 천에 하나도 채 못 되었다. 수많은 새들, 매와 까마귀와 독수리들이 시끄러운 울음소리를 내며 강 위를 맴돌며 그 이름들 가운데 일부를 물에서 낚아채려 애썼으나, 그것들은 새들에게 너무 무거웠고, 얼마 지나지 않아 새들은 그것들을 망각의 강에 떨어뜨릴 수밖에 없었다. 그러나 눈처럼 흰 두 마리의 아름다운 백조가 그 이름들 가운데 몇몇을 거두어 강기슭으로 돌아왔는데, 거기서 한 사랑스러운 요정이 그 부리에서 그것들을 받아, 언덕 위에 자리한 신전으로 가져가, 〈불멸〉의 조각상이 서 있는 성스러운 기둥에 그것들을 영원토록 매달았다.\n\n아스톨포는 이 모든 것에 놀라, 안내자에게 그것을 설명해 달라고 청했다. 그가 대답했다. \"그 노인은 〈시간〉이다. 만약 그 노인이 그것들을 망각의 강에 빠뜨리지 않는다면, 딱지에 적힌 모든 이름은 불멸일 것이다. 어떤 이름들을 구하려고 헛된 애를 쓰는 저 시끄러운 새들은 아첨꾼들, 연금에 기대 사는 자들, 돈에 팔리는 운문 짓는 자들이니, 그들은 자기 후원자들의 보잘것없는 이름을 망각에서 구해 내려고 안간힘을 쓰지만 모두 헛일이다. 그들은 그 이름들을 잠시 동안 그 운명에서 지켜낼 수는 있어도, 머지않아 망각의 강이 그것들을 모두 삼켜 버리고야 만다.\n\n\"조화로운 가락으로 어떤 이름들을 〈영원한 기억〉의 신전으로 실어 나르는 저 백조들은 위대한 시인들이니, 그들은 불멸할 만하다고 여기는 이들의 이름을, 죽음보다 더한 망각에서 구해 낸다. 이런 백조는 드물다. 군주들로 하여금 그 참된 혈통을 알게 하여, 자신들의 시대에 우연히 나타날지 모르는 그런 자들을 정성껏 길러 내기를 게을리하지 않게 하라.\"\n아프리카의 전쟁\n\n# THE WAR IN AFRICA\n\n아스톨포가 그 귀한 작은 병을 가지고 땅으로 내려오자, 성 요한은 그에게 놀라운 효험을 지닌 한 식물을 보여 주며, 그것으로 아비시니아 왕의 눈을 만지기만 하면 그의 시력을 되찾아 줄 수 있다고 일러 주었다. \"그 중요한 봉사는,\" 성인이 말했다. \"그대가 그를 하르피이아이에게서 구해 준 일에 더하여, 그로 하여금 그대에게 군대를 내주어, 그것으로 아프리카인들의 배후를 공격하여 그들이 프랑스에서 돌아와 자기 나라를 지키지 않을 수 없게 만들도록 할 것이다.\" 성인은 또한 그에게, 대상(隊商)들이 흔히 움직이는 모래 기둥에 휩쓸리곤 하는 대사막을 어떻게 무사히 군대를 이끌고 건널 수 있는지도 가르쳐 주었다. 충분한 지침으로 든든해진 아스톨포는 다시 히포그리프에 올라, 성인에게 감사를 표하고 그의 축복을 받은 뒤, 평지로 날아 내려갔다.\n\n나일강의 물줄기를 따라가던 그는 곧 아비시니아의 수도에 다다라, 세나푸스와 다시 만났다. 자신을 하르피이아이에게서 구해 준 영웅의 목소리를 다시 듣자 왕의 기쁨은 컸다. 아스톨포는 지상 낙원에서 가져온 식물로 그의 눈을 만져, 그의 시력을 되찾아 주었다. 왕의 감사는 끝이 없었다. 그는 그에게 보답을 청하라고, 그것이 무엇이든 들어주겠노라 약속하며 간청했다. 아스톨포는 샤를마뉴를 도우러 갈 군대를 청했고, 왕은 그에게 십만 명을 내주었을 뿐 아니라, 자신이 직접 그들을 이끌겠다고 제안하기까지 했다.\n\n군대가 떠나기로 정해진 날의 전날 밤, 아스톨포는 날개 달린 말에 올라 한 산을 향해 날아갔는데, 거기서 사나운 남풍이 나오니, 그 바람의 입김은 누비아 사막의 모래를 일으켜 압도하는 구름처럼 그것들을 앞으로 휘몰아간다. 그 기사는 성 요한의 조언에 따라 가죽 자루를 하나 마련해 두었다가, 그 무시무시한 바람이 나오는 분출구 위에 그 입구를 벌린 채 솜씨 좋게 갖다 댔다. 동이 트는 첫 무렵 바람이 자신의 동굴에서 솟구쳐 나와 날마다의 진로를 다시 시작하려 했고, 그러다 자루 속에 갇혀 단단히 묶이고 말았다. 아스톨포는 자신의 노획물에 기뻐하며 군대로 돌아와, 그 선두에 서서 행군을 시작했다. 아비시니아인들은 자기 나라를 북아프리카의 여러 왕국과 갈라놓는 그 광대한 모래 벌판을 위험도 어려움도 없이 가로질렀으니, 완전히 사로잡힌 그 무시무시한 남풍에게는 촛불 하나 끌 만한 힘조차 남아 있지 않았기 때문이다.\n\n세나푸스는 기병을 전혀 댈 수 없는 것을 안타까워했으니, 그의 나라는 낙타와 코끼리는 풍부했으나 말은 없었기 때문이다. 이 어려움을 성인은 미리 내다보고, 그것을 해결할 방도를 아스톨포에게 가르쳐 두었다. 그는 이제 그 방도를 실행에 옮겼다. 광대한 평원과 바다가 내려다보이는 한 지점에 이르자, 그는 자신의 군대 가운데서 가장 잘 빠지고 가장 영리해 보이는 자들을 골랐다. 이들을 그는 평원과 맞닿은 높은 산기슭에 부대별로 정렬시켰고, 자신은 자신의 큰 계획을 실행하고자 정상으로 올라갔다. 거기서 그는 엄청난 양의 바위 조각과 자갈을 발견했다. 이것들을 그는 산비탈로 굴려 내렸는데, 놀랍게도 그것들은 굴러가면서 크기가 커졌고, 스스로 몸통과 다리와 목과 긴 얼굴을 만들어 냈다. 다음으로 그것들은 울기 시작했고, 껑충거렸으며, 평원 사방으로 내달렸다. 어떤 것은 적갈색이었고, 어떤 것은 회갈색이었고, 어떤 것은 얼룩빼기였고, 어떤 것은 밤색이었다. 산기슭의 군대는 이 새로 만들어진 말들을 잡으려 애썼고, 손쉽게 잡았으니, 그 기적이 인심 좋게도 모든 말에게 굴레와 안장까지 갖추어 준 덕분이었다. 이리하여 아스톨포는 갑자기 훌륭한 기병대를 갖추게 되었으니, (투르팽 대주교가 단언하는 바로는) 그 수가 팔만에 못지않았다. 이 군대로 아스톨포는 온 나라를 복종시켰고, 마침내 아그라만트의 수도 비제르타의 성벽 앞에 다다라, 그곳을 포위했다.\n\n이제 우리는 기독교군의 진영으로 돌아가야 한다. 그 진영은 아를 앞에 펼쳐져 있었으니, 사라센군은 리날도가 이끈 야습에서 패한 뒤 그 도시로 물러나 있었다. 아그라만트는 여기서 새로운 적인 아비시니아인들이 자기 나라를 침공했다는 소식을 받았고, 비제르타가 그들의 손에 떨어질 위험에 처했음을 알게 되었다. 그는 휘하 장수들과 상의하여, 샤를에게 사절을 보내, 양측에서 각각 한 명씩 두 전사의 결투로 모든 다툼을 가리자고 제안하기로 결정했다. 그 결투의 결과에 따라 어느 편이 다른 편에게 조공을 바칠지 정해지고, 전쟁은 그치게 될 것이었다. 아프리카에서 형세가 유리하게 돌아간 것을 듣지 못한 샤를마뉴는 이 제안에 선뜻 동의했고, 기독교 측에서는 리날도가 그 결투를 맡을 자로 뽑혔다.\n\n사라센군은 자신들의 용사로 로제로를 뽑았다. 로제로는 여전히 사라센 진영에 있었으나, 오직 명예 때문에 거기 머물러 있을 뿐이었다. 그의 마음은 브라다만테의 논변으로 기독교 신앙의 진리에 눈을 떴고, 그는 첫 번째 알맞은 기회가 오면 이교도의 편을 떠나 기독교 측에 합류하기로 결심해 둔 터였다. 그러나 옛 벗들이 곤경에 처해 있는 동안 그렇게 하는 것은 그의 명예가 허락지 않았다. 그리하여 그는 때가 무엇을 가져다줄지 기다리고 있었는데, 자신이 기독교군에 맞서 사라센군의 대의를 떠받칠 자로 뽑혔다는 것, 그리고 자신의 적수가 브라다만테의 오라비 리날도라는 발표에 소스라치게 놀랐다.\n\n로제로가 이 소식에 망연자실해 있는 동안, 브라다만테 또한 제안된 그 결투에 관해 듣고 더없이 깊은 고통을 느꼈다. 만약 로제로가 쓰러진다면, 그녀는 살아 있는 그 어떤 남자도 자신의 사랑을 받을 자격이 없다고 느꼈다. 그리고 다른 한편으로 만약 하늘이 자신이 뽑은 용사의 죽음으로 프랑스를 벌하기로 한다면, 브라다만테는 그토록 사랑하는 오라비를 잃고 슬퍼해야 할 것이며, 자신의 애정의 대상과도 그에 못지않게 완전히 갈라서게 될 것이었다.\n\n그 아름다운 여인이 이런 슬픈 상념에 잠겨 있을 때, 현명한 마법사 멜리사가 갑자기 그녀 앞에 나타났다. \"두려워 말아라, 내 딸아.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그대를 그토록 괴롭히는 이 결투를 막을 방도를 내가 찾아 주마.\"\n\n그러는 사이 리날도와 로제로는 결투를 위해 무기를 준비했다. 리날도에게 선택권이 있었고, 그는 도보로, 그리고 전투용 도끼와 단검 외에는 어떤 무기도 쓰지 않고 싸우기로 정했다. 정해진 장소는 샤를마뉴의 진영과 아를 성벽 사이의 평원이었다.\n\n이 기념할 만한 결투를 위해 정해진 날을 새벽이 알리자마자, 양측에서 전령들이 나와 결투장을 표시했다. 머지않아 아프리카군이 아그라만트를 선두로 도시에서 나오는 것이 보였다. 그의 찬란한 무기는 무어식으로 꾸며져 있었고, 그의 말은 이마에 흰 별이 박힌 적갈색이었다. 로제로가 그의 곁에서 행군했고, 사라센 진영의 가장 위대한 전사들 몇몇이 그를 수행하며 그의 갑옷과 무기의 여러 부분들을 받쳐 들고 있었다. 샤를마뉴 또한 자신의 보루에서 나와, 군대를 반원형으로 늘어세우고, 자신의 중신들과 기사들에게 둘러싸여 섰다. 그들 가운데 몇몇이 리날도의 갑옷 일부를 받쳐 들었는데, 저 유명한 덴마크 사람 오기에르가 리날도가 맘브리노에게서 빼앗은 투구를 받쳐 들었다. 바이에른의 나모 공작과 브르타뉴의 살로몬이, 이 경우를 위해 마련된, 무게가 똑같은 두 자루의 도끼를 받쳐 들었다.\n\n이어 결투의 조건을 모든 당사자가 더없이 엄숙하게 맹세했다. 만약 어느 한쪽에서 결투를 방해하려는 어떤 시도라도 한다면, 두 결투자 모두 그 방해를 저지른 편을 향해 무기를 돌리기로 합의했다. 그리고 두 군주는, 그런 경우 그 죄를 범한 편의 용사는 자신의 충성 서약에서 풀려나, 자유로이 자신의 무기를 다른 편으로 옮길 수 있다는 조건에도 동의했다.\n\n모든 준비가 끝나자 군주들과 그 수행원들은 각기 제 편으로 물러갔고, 두 용사만이 남겨졌다. 두 전사는 일정한 걸음으로 서로를 향해 나아가, 그 공간의 한가운데서 마주쳤다. 그들은 동시에 서로에게 달려들었고, 그들이 주고받는 일격에 공기가 울렸다. 전투용 도끼에서 불꽃이 튀었고, 그들이 무기를 다루는 속도는 보는 이들을 놀라게 했다. 자신의 적수가 약혼녀의 오라비임을 늘 잊지 않은 로제로는 치명적인 상처를 입힐 수가 없었다. 그는 다만 자신을 향해 겨눠지는 일격을 막아 내려고만 애썼다. 반면에 리날도는, 로제로를 아무리 높이 평가했어도 자신의 일격을 아끼지 않았으니, 자기 자신을 위해서, 그리고 자기 나라와 자기 신앙을 위해서 승리를 간절히 바랐기 때문이다.\n\n사라센군은 곧 자신들의 용사가 약하게 싸우고 있으며, 리날도에게서 받는 만큼의 일격을 그에게 돌려주지 못하고 있음을 알아챘다. 그의 열세가 너무도 뚜렷하여 아그라만트의 얼굴에는 불안과 수치가 역력했다. 이제껏 살았던 가장 예리한 마법사 가운데 하나인 멜리사는 이 순간을 틈타, 한동안 사라센 진영을 떠나 있던 그 거칠고 충동적인 전사 로도몬트의 모습으로 자신을 변장시켰다. 아그라만트에게 다가가 그녀가 말했다. \"전하, 어찌하여 프랑스에서 가장 가공할 전사를 상대하도록 경험 없는 젊은이를 뽑는 경솔함을 저지르셨나이까? 분명 전하께서는 전하의 무기의 명예도, 전하의 제국의 운명도 도외시하셨음에 틀림없습니다! 그러나 아직 늦지 않았습니다. 전하의 파멸을 불러올 것이 분명한 그 합의를 지체 없이 깨뜨리소서.\" 그렇게 말하고 그녀는 가까이 서 있던 군사들에게 말을 걸었다. \"벗들이여,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"나를 따르라. 내 인도를 받으면 그대들 누구나 저 약해 빠진 기독교도 스무 명을 능히 당해 낼 것이다.\" 아그라만트는 로도몬트가 다시 자기 곁에 돌아온 것을 보고 기뻐하며 동의를 표했고, 사라센군은 그 즉시 창을 겨누고 군마에 박차를 가하여 프랑스군을 향해 휩쓸어 내려갔다. 멜리사는 자신의 일이 성공한 것을 보고는 사라졌다.\n\n리날도와 로제로는 휴전이 깨어지고 두 군대가 전면전에 돌입한 것을 보고 자신들의 싸움을 멈추었다. 그들의 호전적인 분노는 단번에 그쳤고, 그들은 손을 맞잡고, 어느 편이 자신의 맹세를 지키지 않았는지가 분명히 밝혀지기 전까지는 어느 편을 위해서도 더는 싸우지 않기로 결심했다. 두 사람 모두, 이렇게 거짓되고 위약한 편을 영원히 버리겠다는 약속을 새로이 했다.\n\n그러는 사이, 기독교군은 처음의 놀란 순간이 지나자, 적의 배신에 대한 분노로 곱절이 된 용기를 가지고 사라센군과 맞섰다. 리날도의 아우이자 맞수인 〈야인〉 귀도, 올리버의 아들들인 그리퐁과 아퀼란트, 그리고 우리 이야기들에서 이미 그 이름이 기려진 수많은 다른 이들이 침공자들을 물리쳤고, 마침내 엄청난 살육 끝에 그들을 아를의 성벽 안으로 피신하지 않을 수 없게 만들었다.\n\n이제 우리는 오를란도에게로 돌아간다. 우리가 그에 관해 마지막으로 들은 것은 그가 미쳐 날뛰며, 분별 없는 광란 속에 천 가지 난폭한 짓을 저지르고 있을 때였다. 어느 날 그는 자신의 길을 가로막는 시냇가에 이르렀다. 그는 수달처럼 헤엄칠 수 있었으므로 그것을 헤엄쳐 건넜고, 건너편에서 한 농부가 자기 말에 물을 먹이고 있는 것을 보았다. 그는 농부의 저항에도 아랑곳없이 그 짐승을 붙잡아, 미친 듯한 속도로 말을 몰았으니, 마침내\n\n그는 에스파냐가 좁은 해협 하나만으로 아프리카와 갈라지는 해안에 다다랐다. 그가 도착한 바로 그 순간, 배 한 척이 해협을 건너기 위해 막 떠나가던 참이었다. 배에는 사람들이 가득 타고 있었는데, 그들은 잔을 손에 들고 순풍에 실려 가며 육지를 향해 즐겁게 작별을 고하는 듯이 보였다. 광기에 사로잡힌 오를란도는 그들에게 멈추어 자기를 태워 달라고 외쳤다. 하지만 그들은 미치광이를 일행에 끼워 줄 마음이 없었으므로 그를 거들떠보지도 않았다. 기사는 이러한 행동이 매우 무례하다고 여겼다. 그리하여 그는 채찍질을 거듭하여 말을 물속으로 몰아 배를 뒤쫓았다. 가엾은 짐승은 곧 머리만 물 위에 내놓을 지경이 되었다. 하지만 오를란도가 계속 앞으로 몰아대니, 불쌍한 말에게는 죽거나 아니면 헤엄쳐 아프리카까지 건너가는 것 외에는 다른 길이 남아 있지 않았다.\n\n이미 오를란도는 그 작은 배를 시야에서 놓치고 말았다. 거리와 바다의 너울이 배를 그의 눈에서 완전히 가려 버린 것이다. 그는 말을 계속 앞으로 몰았고, 마침내 말은 더 이상 버티지 못하고 그를 태운 채 가라앉았다. 오를란도는 조금도 동요하지 않고 억센 팔을 쭉 뻗어 입 앞으로 밀려드는 짠물을 내뿜으며 머리를 파도 위로 들어 올렸다. 다행히 파도는 거칠지 않았고, 수면을 흔드는 바람도 거의 없었다. 그렇지 않았더라면 무적의 오를란도도 그때 죽음을 맞이했을 것이다. 하지만 어리석은 자를 돌본다는 운명이 그를 이 위험에서 건져 내어 세우타의 해안에 무사히 데려다 놓았다. 그는 해안을 따라 거닐다가 마침내 아스톨포의 검은 군대가 진을 친 곳에 이르렀다.\n\n그런데 마침 이 무렵, 로도몬트가 다리에서 사로잡은 포로들을 가득 실은 배 한 척이 도착했는데, 아비시니아 군대가 와 있는 줄을 모르고 곧장 항구로 들어와 버렸다. 그리하여 당연히 포로와 그들을 사로잡았던 자들의 처지가 뒤바뀌어, 전자는 풀려나 온갖 환대를 받았고, 후자는 갤리선의 노예로 보내졌다. 아스톨포는 이렇게 그리스도교 기사들에게 둘러싸이게 되었고, 그와 그의 벗들이 서로 인사와 축하를 나누고 있을 때 진영에서 소란이 일어 그 소리가 시시각각 커지는 것 같았다.\n\n아스톨포와 그의 벗들은 무기를 집어 들고 말에 올라 소란이 일어난 쪽으로 달려갔다. 그 소동이 단 한 사람 때문에 일어난 것임을 보았을 때 그들의 놀라움이 어떠했을지 상상해 보라. 그자는 완전히 벌거벗었고 더러움과 풍상에 그을려 있었으나, 어찌나 무서운 힘과 광기를 지녔는지 자기에게 손을 대려는 자를 모조리 메다꽂았다.\n\n아스톨포와 두돈, 올리버, 플로리스마르트는 경악하며 그를 바라보았다. 그가 누구인지 알아보기란 쉽지 않았다. 거룩한 안내자에게 그의 처지를 미리 전해 들었던 아스톨포가 가장 먼저 그를 알아보았다. 기사들이 오를란도를 에워싸자 미치광이는 이 사람 저 사람에게 주먹을 휘둘렀는데, 만약 그들이 갑옷을 입지 않았거나 그가 무기라도 들고 있었더라면 그 일격에 목숨을 잃었을 것이다. 그나마 그 정도로 그쳐, 두돈과 아스톨포가 모래밭에 나뒹굴었다. 하지만 플로리스마르트가 뒤에서 그를 붙잡고, 산소네토와 또 다른 자가 그의 다리를 움켜쥐어, 마침내 그들은 밧줄로 그를 결박하는 데 성공했다. 그들은 그를 물가로 데려가 깨끗이 씻겼다. 그런 다음 아스톨포는 먼저 그의 입을 싸매어 코로만 숨을 쉴 수 있게 하고, 귀중한 약병을 가져와 마개를 뽑아 솜씨 좋게 그의 콧구멍 밑에 갖다 댔다. 그러자 선량한 오를란도는 그것을 단숨에 모조리 들이마셨다. 오, 놀라운 기적이여! 기사는 순식간에 모든 지각을 되찾았다. 그는 괴물들이 자기를 갈가리 찢으려 한다고 믿었던 괴로운 꿈에서 깨어난 사람처럼 느껴졌다. 그는 풀이 죽어 말없이 부끄러워하는 듯했다. 플로리스마르트와 올리버, 아스톨포가 그를 응시하는 동안, 그는 주위와 제 몸을 둘러보았다. 그는 자신이 벌거벗은 채 결박당하여 바닷가에 뉘어 있는 것을 보고 놀란 듯했다. 잠시 후 그는 벗들을 알아보고는 어찌나 다정한 어조로 말을 건넸던지, 그들은 서둘러 그를 풀어 주고 옷을 입혔다. 그런 다음 그들은 그를 위로하고, 그의 마음을 짓누르던 무게를 덜어 주며, 그가 빠졌던 비참한 처지를 잊게 하려고 애썼다.\n\n오를란도는 이성을 되찾으면서 카타이의 여왕을 향한 광적인 집착에서도 벗어났음을 알게 되었다. 이제 그의 마음은 그녀에 대한 기억으로부터, 어떤 빛나는 무공으로 명예를 되찾고자 하는 뜨거운 열망 외에는 아무런 영향도 받지 않았다. 아스톨포는 기꺼이 그에게 군대의 총지휘권을 넘겨주려 했으나, 오를란도는 그토록 많은 신세를 진 벗에게서 이번 원정의 영광을 빼앗으려 하지 않았다. 다만 모든 일에서 두 기사는 협력하여 의논을 함께 모았다. 그들은 비세르타 시를 총공격하기로 정하고 적당한 때만을 기다리고 있었는데, 그들의 계획은 새로운 사건들로 인해 중단되고 말았다.\n\n아그라만트는 휴전 위반에 뒤따른 피비린내 나는 전투를 치른 뒤, 자신의 세력이 너무도 약해져 프랑스에 머무르려 해 봐야 헛수고임을 깨달았다. 그리하여 그는 부하 가운데 가장 용맹하고 신임이 두터운 소브리노와 함께, 미리 남은 소수의 병력을 같은 방향으로 보내 둔 뒤 자기 나라로 돌아가기 위해 배에 올랐다. 아그라만트와 소브리노를 실은 배가 비세르타 앞에 진을 친 아스톨포의 군대가 있는 해안으로 다가갔는데, 너무 늦기 전에 이 사실을 알아챈 왕은 키잡이에게 동쪽으로 배를 몰아 이집트 왕의 보호를 구하라고 명했다. 그러나 날씨가 거칠어지자 그는 동행들의 조언을 받아들여 시칠리아와 아프리카 사이에 있는 어느 섬에 정박처를 찾았다. 그곳에서 그는 세리카네의 호전적인 왕 그라다소를 만났는데, 그라다소는 말 바야르와 검 두린다나를 손에 넣기 위해 프랑스에 왔다가 두 전리품을 모두 차지하고 자기 나라로 돌아가던 길이었다. 파리 성벽 아래에서 함께 싸운 전우였던 두 왕은 다정하게 서로를 끌어안았다. 그라다소는 아그라만트가 겪은 좌절을 안타까이 듣고서 자신의 군대와 몸을 그에게 바치겠노라고 했다. 그는 이집트에 도움을 청하는 것을 강력히 만류했다. \"위대한 폼페이우스를 기억하십시오.\" 그가 말했다. \"그 불길한 해안을 피하셔야 합니다. 제 계획은 이렇습니다.\" 그가 이어 말했다. \"저는 오를란도에게 일대일 결투를 신청할 생각입니다. 저의 이런 검과 군마를 가진 자에게라면, 그가 강철이나 청동으로 되어 있다 한들 저를 벗어날 수 없을 것입니다. 그가 제거되면 아비시니아인들을 몰아내는 데는 어려움이 없을 것입니다. 우리는 나일 강 건너편의 이슬람 민족들, 곧 아라비아인과 페르시아인과 칼데아인을 그들에게 맞서도록 일으킬 것이고, 그들은 곧 세나푸스로 하여금 제 영토를 지키기 위해 군대를 불러들이게 할 것입니다.\"\n\n아그라만트는 한 가지만 빼고 이 조언을 받아들였다. \"오를란도와 싸우는 것은 나의 몫이오.\" 그가 말했다. \"그 임무를 명예롭게 남에게 떠넘길 수는 없소.\"\n\n\"세 번째 방도를 택합시다.\" 노년의 전사 소브리노가 말했다. \"저 역시 그런 결투를 그저 구경꾼으로만 지켜보고 싶지는 않습니다. 종자 셋을 아프리카 해안으로 보내어 오를란도와 그의 전우 가운데 둘에게 이 람페두사 섬에서 우리 셋과 맞붙자고 도전하게 합시다.\"\n\n이 조언이 채택되어 종자 셋이 길을 떠났다. 그리고 이제 그들은 그리스도교 기사들 앞에 나타나 전갈을 전했다.\n\n오를란도는 기뻐하며 종자들에게 후한 선물을 내렸다. 그는 이미 그라다소를 찾아내어, 자기가 소유하고 있다고 들은 두린다나를 돌려놓도록 강요하기로 마음먹고 있던 터였다. 백작은 두 전우로 충직한 벗 플로리스마르트와 사촌 올리버를 골랐다.\n\n세 전사는 배에 올라 순풍을 타고 항해했고, 이튿날 아침이 되자 오른편으로 이 중대한 결투가 벌어질 섬이 보였다. 오를란도와 두 전우는 상륙하여 천막을 쳤다. 아그라만트는 그 맞은편에 자기 천막을 세웠다. 이튿날 아침, 새벽 여신이 지평선 가장자리를 밝히자마자 양쪽 진영의 전사들은 무장을 갖추고 말에 올랐다. 그들은 서로 마주 보고 자리를 잡아 창을 내리고 창받이에 걸친 다음, 말에 박차를 가하여 돌격해 들어갔다. 오를란도는 그라다소의 돌격을 맞받았다. 기사는 미동도 하지 않았으나, 그의 말은 바야르의 무시무시한 충격을 견디지 못했다. 말은 뒤로 물러서며 휘청거리다가 몇 걸음 뒤로 쓰러졌다. 오를란도는 말을 일으켜 세우려 했으나 헛수고임을 알고는 방패를 집어 들고 그 유명한 발리사르도를 뽑았다. 그동안 아그라만트와 용감한 올리버는 어느 쪽도 우세를 점하지 못했으나, 플로리스마르트는 소브리노 왕을 말에서 떨어뜨렸다. 적을 땅에 쓰러뜨린 그는 승세를 몰아가지 않고, 오를란도를 메다꽂은 그라다소를 공격하러 서둘러 갔다. 오를란도는 그가 그렇게 맞붙은 것을 보고 끼어들지 않고, 검을 치켜든 채 소브리노에게 달려들어 단 일격에 그의 의식과 움직임을 빼앗았다. 그가 죽었다고 여긴 오를란도는 다음으로 사랑하는 플로리스마르트를 돕기 위해 몸을 돌렸다. 그 용감한 기사는 말도 무기도 상대에게 미치지 못하여, 무시무시한 두린다나의 일격을 그저 받아넘기고 피할 수밖에 없었다. 오를란도는 그를 구하려고 서둘렀으나, 소브리노 왕의 말을 붙잡아 올라타느라 잠시 지체했다. 그것은 한순간에 불과했고, 그는 검을 치켜든 채 그라다소에게 달려들었다. 그라다소는 이 두 번째 적의 공격에도 조금도 당황하지 않고 호기롭게 도전을 외치며 검을 찔러 왔으나, 거리를 잘못 가늠한 탓에 그에게 거의 닿지 못하여 사슬 갑옷을 뚫지 못했다. 답례로 오를란도는 발리사르도로 일격을 가했는데, 그것이 떨어지며 얼굴과 가슴과 넓적다리를 베었고, 조금만 더 가까웠더라면 그를 두 동강 냈을 것이다. 이때쯤 소브리노는 심한 상처를 입긴 했어도 혼절에서 깨어나 다리로 몸을 일으켜, 어떻게 하면 벗들을 도울 수 있을지 살폈다. 아그라만트가 올리버에게 거세게 몰리는 것을 보고, 그는 올리버의 말 옆구리에 검을 찔러 넣었다. 말은 쓰러지며 주인을 짓눌렀고, 쓰러질 때 그의 다리를 휘감아 올리버는 빠져나올 수가 없었다. 플로리스마르트는 벗의 위기를 보고 말을 몰아 소브리노에게 달려들어 그를 쓰러뜨린 다음, 몸을 돌려 아그라만트를 막아 냈다. 둘의 실력은 어슷비슷했는데, 브리글리아도로에 올라탄 아그라만트가 변변찮은 말을 탄 플로리스마르트보다 유리하기는 했으나, 아그라만트는 올리버와 맞붙었을 때 심한 상처를 입은 터였다.\n\n오를란도와 그라다소의 격돌이 보여 준 격렬함은 그 무엇과도 견줄 수 없었다. 그라다소의 손에 들린 두린다나는 닿는 것은 무엇이든 두 동강 냈다. 그러나 그 무기의 일격이 자신을 어떤 위험에 빠뜨리는지 완벽히 알고 있던 오를란도의 솜씨가 어찌나 뛰어났던지, 그 검은 아직 그에게 상처를 입힐 만큼 제대로 닿지 못했다. 그러는 동안 그라다소는 여러 상처에서 피를 흘리고 있었고, 그의 분노와 무모함은 시시각각 커져 갔다. 절망에 빠진 그는 두 손으로 두린다나를 치켜들어, 오를란도의 투구를 정통으로 내리쳤는데, 그 일격이 어찌나 무시무시했던지 기사는 한순간 정신을 잃었다. 그는 고삐를 떨어뜨렸고, 겁에 질린 말은 그를 태운 채 들판을 내달렸다. 그라다소는 그를 뒤쫓으려고 몸을 돌렸으나, 바로 그 순간 플로리스마르트가 말에서 떨어뜨린 아그라만트에게 막 치명적인 일격을 가하려는 것을 보았다. 플로리스마르트가 승리를 마무리하는 데 온통 정신이 팔려 있는 사이, 그라다소는 그의 옆구리에 검을 찔러 넣었다. 플로리스마르트는 말에서 떨어져 자신의 피로 들판을 적셨다.\n\n오를란도는 마침 그 행위를 볼 수 있을 때 정신을 차렸다. 그의 가슴속에 분노가 컸는지 슬픔이 컸는지 나는 알 수 없다. 다만 그는 분노에 차 발리사르도를 움켜쥐었고, 그 첫 일격이 가장 가까이 있던 아그라만트에게 떨어져 그의 목을 어깨에서 베어 냈다. 이 광경을 본 그라다소는 처음으로 용기가 꺾이는 것을 느꼈고, 죽음을 예감하는 어두운 그림자가 그를 덮쳤다. 그가 미처 방어 자세를 채 갖추기도 전에 오를란도가 그에게 몸을 던져 치명적인 일격을 찔러 넣었다. 검은 그의 갈비뼈를 꿰뚫고 몸 반대편으로 한 뼘이나 삐져나왔다.\n\n이리하여 프랑스에서 가장 빛나는 기사의 검 아래 사라센 군대에서 가장 용맹한 전사가 쓰러졌다. 그러자 오를란도는 마치 자신의 승리를 대수롭지 않게 여기듯 가볍게 땅으로 뛰어내려, 사랑하는 벗 플로리스마르트에게 달려가 그를 끌어안고 눈물로 적셨다. 플로리스마르트는 아직 숨을 쉬고 있었다. 그는 작별의 몇 마디를 내뱉을 만큼 목소리를 가다듬을 수도 있었다. \"사랑하는 벗이여, 나를 잊지 말게. 나를 위해 기도해 주게. 그리고 오, 플로르델리스에게 형제가 되어 주게.\" 그는 그녀의 이름을 부르며 숨을 거두었다.\n\n슬픔에 잠긴 잠시가 지난 뒤 오를란도는 또 다른 전우와 방금 전의 적들을 살피러 몸을 돌렸다. 올리버는 자신의 말 무게에 짓눌린 채 누워 있었고, 그것에서 빠져나오려고 헛되이 몸부림쳤었다. 오를란도는 힘겹게 그를 끌어냈다. 그런 다음 소브리노를 땅에서 일으켜 자신의 종자에게 맡겼는데, 마치 그가 친형제라도 되는 양 다정하게 대했다. 이 무시무시한 전사는 쓰러진 적에게는 누구보다도 너그러운 사람이었기 때문이다. 그는 바야르와 브리글리아도로를, 패배한 기사들의 무기와 함께 취했다. 그들의 시신과 나머지 노획물은 그들의 시종들에게 돌려보냈다.\n\n그러나 전사들이 돌아오는 것을 보고도, 헤어졌다가 만날 때면 늘 그러하듯 플로리스마르트가 제 곁으로 달려오지 않는 것을 알았을 때 플로르델리스의 슬픔이 어떠했는지 그 누가 말할 수 있으랴. 그녀는 다른 이들의 표정을 보고 자신의 주인이 죽었음을 알아차렸다. 그 생각이 들자, 질문이 입술을 채 떠나기도 전에 그녀는 의식을 잃고 땅에 쓰러졌다. 생기가 돌아와 자신의 가장 무서운 두려움이 사실임을 알게 되었을 때, 그녀는 자기 없이 그를 떠나보낸 것을 두고 모질게 자책했다. \"그의 적이 그 비겁한 일격을 가했을 때 내가 한 번만 외쳤어도 그를 구할 수 있었을 텐데, 아니면 사이에 몸을 던져 그를 위해 내 보잘것없는 목숨을 바칠 수도 있었을 텐데. 설령 그러지는 못했더라도, 그의 마지막 말을 듣고 마지막 입맞춤이라도 해 줄 수 있었을 텐데.\" 그녀는 이렇게 한탄하며 위로받지 못했다.\n로제로와 브라다만테\n\n# ROGERO AND BRADAMANTE\n\n앞서 이야기한 대로 리날도와의 결투가 중단된 뒤, 로제로는 어떤 길을 택해야 할지 의혹에 사로잡혔다. 조약의 조건은 그것을 깨뜨린 아그라만트를 저버리고 충성을 샤를마뉴에게 옮기라고 요구했다. 브라다만테를 향한 사랑도 그를 같은 방향으로 불렀다. 하지만 곤경에 처한 자신의 군주이자 지휘관을 저버리려 하지 않는 마음이 이 길을 가로막았다. 그리하여 그는 아프리카로 가는 배에 올라 사라센 군대와 다시 합류하러 길을 떠났으나, 도중에 폭풍을 만나 배가 바위에 부딪히면서 발이 묶이고 말았다. 선원들은 작은 배에 옮겨 탔으나 그 배도 곧 파도에 잠겼고, 로제로는 나머지 사람들과 함께 목숨을 부지하려 헤엄칠 수밖에 없었다. 그러다 파도와 싸우는 동안 로제로는 그리스도교 신앙 고백을 그토록 오래 미룬 자신의 죄를 떠올리고, 만약 살아서 뭍에 닿게 된다면 더는 미루지 않고 세례를 받겠노라고 마음속으로 맹세했다. 그의 맹세는 들어졌고 응답을 받았다. 그는 무사히 해안에 다다랐으며, 상륙하자마자 바다가 내려다보이는 암자에 사는 경건한 은자의 도움과 구제를 받았다. 그는 먼저 그와 며칠을 함께 지내며 소박한 음식을 나누고 그리스도교 신앙의 교리를 배운 뒤, 그에게서 세례를 받았다.\n\n이런 일들이 벌어지고 있는 동안, 그라다소를 찾아 그에게서 바야르를 되찾으러 길을 떠났던 리날도는 도중에 아프리카에서 큰일이 벌어지고 있다는 소식을 듣고, 거기에 한몫 끼려고 그곳으로 향했다. 그가 도착했을 때는 이미 너무 늦어, 벗들과 함께 플로리스마르트의 죽음을 애도하고 이교도 기사들에게 거둔 승리를 함께 기뻐하는 것 외에는 할 일이 없었다. 자기네 왕이 죽자 아프리카인들은 싸움을 포기했고, 비세르타는 항복했으며, 그리스도교 기사들은 군대를 해산하고 고국으로 돌아가기만 하면 되었다. 아스톨포는 아비시니아 군대에 작별을 고하고, 그들을 전리품을 가득 실어 본국으로 돌려보냈다. 그러면서 바람을 가둔 자루를 그들에게 맡기는 것도 잊지 않았는데, 그 자루 덕분에 그들은 다시 위험 없이 모래사막을 건널 수 있었고, 자기 나라에 이를 때까지 자루를 풀지 않았다.\n\n이제 오를란도는 외과의의 보살핌이 절실했던 올리버, 그리고 그와 똑같은 정성을 받은 소브리노와 함께, 그리스도교의 땅에 묻기 위해 플로리스마르트의 시신을 싣고 빠른 배에 올라 시칠리아로 향했다. 리날도가 그들과 동행했고, 산소네토와 다른 그리스도교 지휘관들도 함께했다. 시칠리아에 다다른 그들은 종교의 온갖 의식을 갖추어, 그리고 플로리스마르트를 알았거나 그의 명성을 들었던 이들의 깊은 슬픔 속에 장례를 엄숙히 치렀다. 그런 다음 그들은 다시 마르세유를 향해 항로에 올랐다. 그러나 올리버의 상처는 나아지기는커녕 더 악화되었고, 그의 고통이 어찌나 벗들을 괴롭혔던지 그들은 어찌해야 할지 몰라 함께 의논했다. 그때 키잡이가 말했다. \"여기서 멀지 않은 곳에 거룩한 은자가 홀로 바다 한가운데에 사는 섬이 있습니다. 그의 조언이나 도움을 구해 헛되이 돌아간 사람은 없다고들 합니다. 그는 놀라운 치유를 행해 왔으니, 그 거룩한 분을 찾아가신다면 의심할 바 없이 저 기사를 낫게 해 주실 것입니다.\" 오를란도는 그쪽으로 배를 몰라고 명했고, 곧 작은 배는 그 외딴 바위 곁에 무사히 닿았다. 부상자는 작은 배로 내려져 선원들의 손에 실려 은자의 암자로 옮겨졌다. 그는 로제로가 난파 후 몸을 의탁했던 바로 그 은자였으니, 로제로가 그에게서 세례를 받고 지금도 그와 함께 머물며 거룩한 학문과 명상에 잠겨 있던 그 은자였다.\n\n거룩한 그는 오를란도와 나머지 사람들을 친절히 맞이하며 그들이 온 용건을 물었다. 그리스도교 신앙을 위해 싸우다가 안타까운 상처로 위태로운 처지에 빠진 자를 도우러 왔다는 말을 듣고, 그는 곧바로 치료에 나섰다. 그가 쓴 약은 단순했으나, 그의 기도가 거기에 힘을 보탰다. 기사는 곧 고통에서 벗어났고, 며칠 만에 그의 발은 완전히 건강을 되찾았다. 소브리노는 거룩한 수도자가 그 기적을 행하는 것을 보자마자 자신의 거짓 예언자를 내던지고, 뉘우치는 마음으로 참된 하느님을 받아들여 그의 손에 세례를 청했다. 은자는 그의 청을 들어주었고, 또한 기도로써 그의 건강을 회복시켰다. 그리하여 모든 그리스도교 기사들은 그의 개종을 올리버의 회복에 못지않게 기뻐했다. 누구보다도 로제로가 기쁨과 감사를 느꼈고, 날로 은총과 신앙이 깊어졌다.\n\n로제로의 명성은 모든 그리스도교 기사들에게 알려져 있었으나, 결투에서 그의 용맹을 증명해 본 리날도조차 그를 얼굴로는 알아보지 못했다. 소브리노가 그를 그들에게 소개했고, 그 용맹과 예의가 온 세상에 떨친 자가 더 이상 적이자 불신자가 아니라 참된 신앙으로 개종한 투사임을 알게 되자 모두 크게 기뻐했다. 모두 기사 주위로 몰려들었다. 한 사람은 그의 손을 움켜쥐었고, 또 한 사람은 그를 꼭 끌어안았다. 그러나 누구보다도 리날도가 그를 아꼈으니, 그가 누구보다도 그의 진가를 잘 알았기 때문이다.\n\n오래지 않아 로제로는 자신의 누이와 맺어지기를 바라는 희망을 벗에게 털어놓았고, 리날도는 그 제안에 흔쾌히 동의를 표했다. 그러나 기사가 알지 못하는 사정들이 바로 그때 그 성사를 가로막는 장애물이 되고 있었다.\n\n브라다만테의 아름다움과 가치에 대한 명성이 그리스 황제 콘스탄티누스의 귀에까지 들어가, 그는 사람을 보내어 자기 아들이자 영토의 후계자인 레오를 위해 샤를마뉴에게 그 조카딸의 손을 청했다. 그녀의 아버지인 에이몬 공작은 지금 부재중인 아들 리날도와 먼저 이야기를 나눌 때까지만 승낙을 미루어 두고 있었다.\n\n이제 전사들은 다시 항해에 나설 채비를 했다. 로제로는 자신에게 참된 신앙을 가르쳐 준 선량한 은자에게 다정히 작별을 고했다. 오를란도는 마땅히 그의 것인 말과 무기를 그에게 돌려주었으며, 자신이 여마법사에게서 손에 넣은 검 발리사르다에 대해서도 권리를 주장하지 않았다.\n\n은자는 그 무리에게 축복을 내렸고, 그들은 다시 배에 올랐다. 항해는 빨라서, 그들은 아주 곧 마르세유 항구에 도착했다.\n\n아스톨포는 군대를 해산한 뒤 히포그리프에 올라, 한 번의 비행으로 사르데냐로 날아갔고, 거기서 코르시카로, 다시 거기서 살짝 왼쪽으로 방향을 틀어 프로방스 위를 맴돌다가 마르세유 인근에 내려앉았다. 그곳에서 그는 거룩한 성인이 명한 대로 했다. 곧 히포그리프의 굴레를 벗겨, 다시는 안장이나 재갈에 시달리지 않도록 제 보금자리를 찾아가게 풀어 준 것이다. 뿔피리는 달을 방문한 이후로 그 놀라운 힘을 잃은 터였다.\n\n아스톨포는 오를란도, 리날도, 올리버, 소브리노, 로제로가 마르세유에 도착한 바로 그날 그곳에 닿았다. 샤를은 이미 사라센 왕들의 패배와 그에 따른 모든 사건의 소식을 들은 터였다. 용맹한 기사들이 다가온다는 것을 알자, 그는 가장 빼어난 귀족들 몇몇을 먼저 보내어 그들을 맞이하게 했고, 자신은 나머지 궁정의 왕들과 공작들과 중신들, 왕비, 그리고 아름답고 화려한 귀부인들의 무리를 거느리고 그들을 맞으러 아를에서 출발했다.\n\n서로 인사를 나누기가 무섭게 오를란도와 그의 벗들은 로제로를 앞으로 이끌어 황제에게 소개했다. 그들은 그가 그리스도교 전사 가운데 가장 이름난 이인 리사 공작 로제로의 아들로서, 불운한 운명으로 어릴 적에 도둑맞아 사라센인들 손에 거짓 신앙으로 길러졌으나, 이제 자비로운 섭리로 개종하여 그의 아버지가 한때 왕좌와 교회의 으뜸가는 투사들 사이에서 차지했던 자리를 채우러 돌아왔노라고 보증했다.\n\n로제로는 말에서 내려 황제 앞에 공손히 섰다. 샤를마뉴는 그에게 다시 말에 올라 자기 곁에서 나란히 가자고 했고, 자신의 무사 행렬이 보는 앞에서 그를 영예롭게 할 만한 것이라면 무엇 하나 빠뜨리지 않았다. 개선의 화려함과 축제의 환호 속에 일행은 도시로 돌아왔다. 거리는 화환으로 장식되었고, 집집마다 값진 융단을 내걸었으며, 발코니와 창마다 아름다운 귀부인과 처녀들의 손에서 꽃이 비처럼 정복자 무리 위로 쏟아졌다. 이렇게 환대를 받으며 위대한 황제는 왕궁에 이를 때까지 나아갔고, 그곳에서 그는 여러 날을 신하들과 함께 높은 홀에서 마상 시합과 향연과 춤과 노래 속에 잔치를 벌였다.\n\n리날도가 아버지 에이몬 공작에게 자기 누이를 로제로에게 약속했다고 말하자, 그 아버지는 그 말을 분노하며 들었으니, 누이가 그리스 황제의 아들과 맺어지기를 마음에 두고 있었기 때문이다. 그녀의 어머니인 베아트리체 부인 또한 브라다만테 본인에게 직접 호소하여, 작위도 영지도 없는 기사를 물리치고 그녀를 드넓은 동방의 황후로 만들어 줄 사람을 택하라고 했다. 그러나 브라다만테는 어머니의 간청을 거절하기에는 존경심이 가로막았으나, 마음이 거부하는 일을 하겠노라고 약속하지도 않고 그저 한숨으로만 답했다. 그러다 홀로 남게 되자 눈물을 마음껏 쏟았다.\n\n한편 로제로는 낯선 자가 감히 자기 신부를 빼앗으려 한다는 데 분개하여, 그리스 왕자를 찾아내어 죽음을 건 결투를 신청하기로 마음먹었다. 이러한 계획으로 그는 갑옷을 입었으나, 투구 장식과 문장은 바꾸어, 진홍빛 바탕에 흰 일각수를 그려 넣었다. 그는 믿을 만한 종자 하나를 골라, 자신을 로제로라 부르지 말라고 명한 뒤 길을 떠났다. 라인 강과 오스트리아의 여러 지방을 가로질러 헝가리로 들어선 그는 다뉴브 강의 물길을 따라가 마침내 베오그라드에 이르렀다. 그곳에서 그는 도시 앞에 황제의 깃발이 펼쳐지고, 군대로 북적이는 흰 막사들이 늘어선 것을 보았다. 황제 콘스탄티누스가 얼마 전 자신에게서 그 도시를 빼앗은 불가리아인들에게서 그것을 되찾으려고 포위 공격을 벌이고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n강 하나가 황제의 진영과 불가리아인들 사이로 흘렀는데, 로제로가 다가오던 순간 양쪽 진영에서 물을 길러 강가로 나온 자들 사이에 작은 충돌이 벌어진 참이었다. 그 싸움에서 그리스인들은 사 대 일로 우세하여 불가리아인들을 허둥지둥 패주시켰다. 이를 본 로제로는 오직 그리스 왕자에 대한 증오에 불타, 도망치는 무리 한가운데로 뛰어들어 달아나는 자들에게 큰 소리로 돌아서라고 외쳤다. 그는 먼저 화려한 갑옷을 입은 그리스 군대의 한 지휘관과 마주쳤는데, 그는 황제의 조카로서 친아들처럼 사랑받던 자였다. 로제로의 창은 방패와 갑옷을 꿰뚫어 그 전사를 숨이 끊긴 채 들판에 뉘었다. 또 한 사람, 또 한 사람이 그의 앞에 쓰러지자, 경악과 공포가 그리스인들의 진격을 막았고, 그동안 불가리아인들은 그 기사에게서 용기를 얻어 전열을 가다듬고 방향을 돌려 그리스 군대를 쫓았다. 이번에는 그리스인들이 달아날 차례였다. 이 갑작스러운 충돌이 일어났을 때 왕자 레오는 멀리 떨어져 있었으나, 자신이 차지한 높은 자리에서 뒤바뀐 전세가 모두 한 사람의 솜씨임을 또렷이 볼 수 있을 만큼은 가까웠고, 그것을 해낸 용맹과 무용에 감탄하지 않을 수 없었다. 그는 내걸린 문장을 보고 그 투사가 불가리아 군대 소속이 아니면서도 그들을 도왔음을 알았다. 그의 용맹 때문에 손해를 입었음에도 왕자는 그에게 악의를 품을 수 없었으니, 그의 감탄이 원한을 넘어섰기 때문이다. 이때쯤 그리스인들은 강에 다다라, 물을 건너거나 헤엄쳐 일부는 도망쳤으나 더 많은 자들이 불가리아인들의 손에 포로로 남았다. 로제로는 몇몇 포로에게서 레오가 강 하류 어느 지점에 있다는 것을 듣고, 그를 만날 생각으로 그쪽으로 말을 달렸으나, 그가 도착하기 전에 그리스 왕자는 이미 강 너머로 물러나 다리를 끊어 버린 뒤였다. 날이 저물어, 지친 로제로는 밤을 보낼 거처를 찾아 둘러보았다. 그는 어느 오두막에서 거처를 찾았고, 곧 휴식에 몸을 맡겼다. 그런데 방금 전 전투에서 로제로의 검을 가까스로 피했던 기사 하나가 마침 같은 오두막에서 거처를 찾았다가, 이 알 수 없는 기사의 갑옷을 알아보고는 그가 잠든 사이 쉽사리 방도를 마련하여 그를 결박했다. 그리고 이튿날 아침 사슬에 묶은 그를 끌고 가 황제에게 넘겼다. 황제는 그를 다시 자기 누이 테오도라에게 넘겼는데, 그녀는 로제로의 창에 첫 희생자가 된 젊은 기사의 어머니였다. 그녀는 자신의 복수를 만족시킬 만큼 충분히 고통스러운 죽음을 궁리해 낼 때까지 그를 지하 감옥에 던져 두었다.\n\n한편 브라다만테는 아버지와 어머니의 성화를 피하려고 샤를마뉴에게 한 가지 청을 올렸는데, 군주는 그것을 들어주겠노라고 왕의 말로 약속했다. 그것은 누구든 먼저 일대일 결투에서 자기를 이기지 않는 한 어느 누구와도 결혼하도록 강요받지 않겠다는 것이었다. 그리하여 황제는 이런 말로 마상 시합을 선포했다. \"에이몬 공작의 딸과 혼인하려는 자는 해가 뜰 때부터 질 때까지 검으로 그 부인과 겨루어야 한다. 그리고 그 시간 동안 패하지 않으면 그 부인은 그의 것이 되리라.\"\n\n에이몬 공작과 베아트리체 부인은 일이 돌아가는 형국에 몹시 분개하면서도, 마상 시합으로 정해진 날을 기다리기 위해 딸을 궁정으로 데려왔다. 브라다만테는 그곳에서 자기 마음이 바라는 이를 찾지 못하자, 그가 부재한 까닭이 무엇일지 의혹으로 괴로워했다. 온갖 상상 가운데 가장 고통스러운 것은, 그가 자기 부모가 둘의 결합을 반대하는 것을 알고 그들에 맞서 싸우기를 단념하여, 자기를 잊는 법을 배우러 떠나 버렸다는 것이었다. 그러나 오, 그녀의 약혼자가 그때 무엇을 견디고 있는지 그녀가 알았더라면 처녀의 슬픔이 얼마나 더 깊었으랴!\n\n그는 한 줄기 햇빛도 들지 않는 지하 감옥에 던져져, 사슬에 묶이고 가장 거친 음식조차 변변히 받지 못했다. 절망이 그의 마음을 사로잡고 그가 안식으로서 죽음을 갈망하게 된 것도 무리가 아니었다. 그러던 어느 밤(혹은 어느 낮이라 해도, 그에게는 둘 다 똑같이 어두웠으므로) 횃불의 불빛에 그는 깨어났고, 두 사람이 자신의 감방으로 들어오는 것을 보았다. 그것은 시종 하나를 거느린 레오 왕자였는데, 그는 전쟁터에서 자신이 보고 감탄했던 용맹한 기사의 비참한 운명을 알게 되자마자 찾아온 것이었다. \"기사여,\" 그가 말했다. \"나는 그대의 용맹에 어찌나 마음이 매였던지, 그대를 돕기 위해 기꺼이 내 자신의 안위를 무릅쓰는 사람이오.\" \"그대에게 무한한 감사를 드리오.\" 로제로가 답했다. \"그대가 주신 이 목숨, 그대가 부르면 충실히 되돌려 드리고, 언제든 그대를 위해 기꺼이 내걸겠노라 약속드리오.\" 그러자 왕자는 로제로에게 자신의 이름과 지위를 밝혔는데, 그 말을 듣자 서로 다투는 감정의 물결이 로제로를 거의 압도했다. 그는 풀려났고, 말과 무기를 돌려받았다.\n\n한편 누구든 브라다만테의 손을 얻으려는 자는 먼저 검과 창으로 그녀와 겨루어야 한다는 샤를 왕의 포고 소식이 전해졌다. 이 소식에 그리스 왕자는 얼굴이 창백해졌으니, 자신이 싸움에서 그녀의 적수가 못 됨을 알았기 때문이다. 혼자 곰곰이 생각하던 그는, 어떻게 하면 용맹 대신 꾀를 써서, 아직 그 이름조차 모르는 프랑스 기사를 시켜 자기 대신 결투를 치르게 할 수 있을지 깨달았다. 로제로는 그 제안을 극심한 고통 속에 들었다. 그러나 자기 목숨을 빚진 이의 첫 청을 거절하는 것은 죽음보다도 더 끔찍해 보였다. 그는 황급히 \"레오가 명하는 것이라면 무엇이든 하겠노라\"고 승낙했다. 그 뒤 쓰라린 후회가 그를 덮쳤다. 그러나 마음을 바꾸었음을 고백하느니 차라리 죽음이 반가울 지경이었다. 죽음만이 그의 유일한 해결책으로 보였다. 그러나 어떻게 죽는단 말인가? 때때로 그는 거짓으로만 저항하여 그녀의 검이 쉽게 닿게 할까 생각했으니, 그녀의 손이 무기를 이끈다면 그보다 더 행복한 죽음은 없을 터였기 때문이다. 그러나 그래서는 안 될 일이었다. 그리스 왕자를 위해 그 처녀를 얻어 주지 않는 한 그의 빚은 갚아지지 않을 테니 말이다. 그는 거짓이 아닌 진짜 대결을 벌이겠노라 약속한 터였다. 그렇다면 그는 약속을 지킬 것이고, 자신의 신의를 지키도록 그를 움직인 그 생각 외에는 모든 생각을 가슴에서 몰아내리라.\n\n젊은 왕자는 화려한 수행원을 거느리고 길을 떠났고, 로제로도 함께였다. 그들은 파리에 도착했으나, 레오는 도시 안으로 들어가지 않고 성벽 밖에 막사를 치고는 사절을 보내 자신의 도착을 샤를마뉴에게 알렸다. 군주는 기뻐하며 방문과 선물로 자신의 예의를 표했다. 왕자는 자신이 온 목적을 밝히고, 황제에게 자신의 청혼을 처리해 줄 것을 청했으니, \"싸움에서 자기보다 못한 어떤 군주와도 혼인하지 않겠다는 그 처녀를 내보내 달라. 그녀는 그의 신부가 되거나, 아니면 그가 그녀의 검 아래 스러질 것이다\"라고 했다.\n\n로제로는 결투로 정해진 날의 전날 밤을, 마치 이튿날 제 목숨으로 죗값을 치르도록 선고받은 죄인이 보내는 그런 밤처럼 보냈다. 그는 오직 검으로만, 그것도 걸어서 싸우기로 했으니, 그녀가 프론티노를 보면 그 말을 알아보리라는 것을 알았기 때문이다. 또한 발리사르다도 쓰지 않으려 했으니, 그 마법의 칼날 앞에서는 어떤 갑옷도 소용없을 터였기 때문이다. 그리고 자기가 쥘 검은 날카로움을 무디게 하려고 칼날을 잘 두드려 두었다. 그는 레오 왕자의 겉옷을 입고, 황금빛 쌍두 독수리가 그려진 그의 방패를 들었다. 왕자는 자신이 누구의 눈에도 띄지 않도록 조심했다.\n\n한편 브라다만테는 그 결투를 위해 사뭇 다르게 채비했다. 그녀는 언월도의 날을 무디게 하기는커녕 강철을 갈았고, 거기에 자신의 매서움을 불어넣고자 했다. 결투의 순간이 다가오자 그녀는 핏줄 속에 불이 타오르는 듯하여, 나팔 소리를 애타게 기다렸다. 신호가 울리자 그녀는 검을 뽑아 분노에 차 자신의 로제로에게 달려들었다. 그러나 잘 지어진 성벽이나 오랜 세월을 견딘 바위가 폭풍의 노여움에도 끄떡없이 서 있듯이, 트로이아의 헥토르가 한때 입었던 그 갑옷을 두른 로제로는 자신의 머리와 가슴과 옆구리로 휘몰아치는 일격들을 막아 냈다. 그의 방패와 투구와 흉갑에서 불꽃이 튀었다. 곧은 일격과 되받아치는 일격이, 높이 또 낮게 겨냥되어 오두막 지붕에 쏟아지는 우박처럼 빽빽하고 빠르게 떨어졌다. 그러나 로제로는 능숙한 방어로 그것들을 비껴 내거나, 갑옷이 확실히 막아 주는 곳으로 받아 냈으며, 오직 자신을 지키는 데만 마음을 쓸 뿐 되받아 칠 생각은 조금도 하지 않았다. 이렇게 시간이 흘러가고, 해가 서쪽으로 기울자 처녀는 절망하기 시작했다. 그러나 그럴수록 그녀의 분노는 더욱 커져, 마치 날이 거의 다 저물도록 일을 끝내지 못한 장인처럼 그녀는 노력을 배가했다. 오, 가엾은 처녀여! 그대가 죽이려는 이가 누구인지 안다면, 그대를 맞아 겨루는 그 기사가 그대의 생명줄이 통째로 매달린 바로 그 로제로임을 안다면, 그를 죽이느니 차라리 그대 자신을 죽일 터였으니, 그는 그대에게 목숨보다도 더 소중한 이였기 때문이다.\n\n그 기사를 그리스 왕자로 여긴 샤를 왕과 중신들은 그토록 대단한 힘과 솜씨가 펼쳐지는 것을, 그리고 그 기사가 그녀를 공격하지 않으면서도 자신을 방어하는 것을 보고 감탄으로 가득 차, 두 투사가 서로 잘 어울리는 적수이며 서로에게 합당하다고 선언했다.\n\n해가 지자 샤를마뉴는 결투를 끝내는 신호를 보냈고, 브라다만테는 레오 왕자에게 신부로 주어졌다. 로제로는 깊은 시름에 잠겨 자신의 막사로 돌아왔다. 그곳에서 레오는 그의 투구 끈을 풀고 그의 양 볼에 입을 맞추었다. \"이제부터,\" 그가 말했다. \"나를 그대 뜻대로 부리시오. 그대는 내 감사를 다 써 버릴 수 없을 테니.\" 로제로는 별 대꾸 없이, 입었던 표장을 벗어 놓고 일각수 문장을 다시 단 다음, 모든 이의 눈을 피해 서둘러 물러났다. 한밤중이 되자 그는 일어나 프론티노에 안장을 얹고 막사를 빠져나가, 말이 가고 싶어 하는 방향으로 나아갔다. 밤새도록 그는 쓰라린 비탄에 잠겨 말을 달리며, 자신의 고통을 덜어 줄 수 있는 유일한 것으로서 죽음을 불렀다. 마침내 그는 어느 숲에 들어서서 가장 깊은 후미진 곳까지 파고들었다. 그곳에서 그는 프론티노의 마구를 벗겨 가고 싶은 대로 떠돌게 두었다. 그런 다음 그는 땅바닥에 몸을 던지고는, 그의 외침을 들은 것이라곤 새와 짐승밖에 없었으나, 그것들마저 그의 부르짖음에 측은한 마음이 들 만큼 쓰라린 통곡을 쏟아 냈다.\n\n브라다만테 부인의 시름도 그에 못지않았으니, 그녀는 로제로 외에는 그 누구와도 혼인하느니 차라리 자신의 말을 어기고 친족과 궁정과 샤를마뉴 자신에게까지 맞서기로, 그리고 다른 방도가 없다면 죽기로 결심했다. 그러나 구원은 뜻밖의 곳에서 찾아왔다. 로제로의 누이 마르피사는 브라다만테에 못지않은 무용을 갖춘 여걸이었다. 그녀는 두 사람의 사랑을 속속들이 아는 절친한 벗이었고, 그들의 결합을 위협하는 위험을 보며 그들 자신 못지않은 시름을 느꼈다. \"그들은 이미 서로의 맹세로 맺어졌어요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그리고 하늘이 보기에 그 외에 또 무엇이 필요하단 말입니까?\" 이 생각으로 가득 차 그녀는 샤를마뉴 앞에 나아가, 그 처녀가 로제로에게 혼인하는 이들이 맹세하는 그 말을 건네는 것을 자신이 직접 목격했으며, 그 약조가 두 사람 사이에 어찌나 단단히 봉해졌던지 그들은 더 이상 자유롭지 못하여 서로를 저버리고 다른 배필을 맞을 수 없노라고 선언했다. 이 자신의 주장을 그녀는 레오 왕자든 다른 누구든 상대로 일대일 결투로써 증명하겠다고 했다.\n\n이에 몹시 곤혹스러워진 샤를마뉴는 브라다만테를 불러오게 하여, 대담한 마르피사가 선언한 바를 그녀에게 일렀다. 브라다만테는 그 진술을 부인하지도 시인하지도 않은 채, 고개를 떨구고 침묵을 지켰다. 에이몬 공작은 격분하여, 설령 그런 약조가 이루어졌다 한들 그것은 로제로가 세례를 받기 전에 이루어진 것이 틀림없으므로 무효라는 이유로 그 가짜 약조를 무시하려 했다. 그러나 리날도도, 선량한 오를란도도 그렇게 생각하지 않았다. 샤를마뉴가 어느 쪽으로 결정해야 할지 몰라 하던 차에 마르피사가 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"제 오라비가 살아 있는 한 그 누구도 처녀와 혼인할 수 없으니, 왕자가 로제로와 죽음을 건 결투로 맞붙어, 살아남는 자가 그녀를 신부로 맞게 하소서.\"\n\n이 말은 황제의 마음에 들었고, 왕자도 그것을 받아들였으니, 자신의 알려지지 않은 투사의 도움으로 그 싸움에서 반드시 이기리라 여겼기 때문이다. 그리하여 로제로에게 나타나 자신의 권리를 변호하라는 포고가 내려졌고, 레오 또한 그 나름으로 사방을 뒤져 일각수의 기사를 찾게 했다.\n\n한편 절망에 압도된 로제로는 음식도 입에 대지 않고 밤낮으로 숲속 땅바닥에 누워 죽음을 청하고 있었다. 이곳에서 그는 레오의 부하 가운데 한 사람에게 발견되었는데, 그가 자신을 옮기려는 온갖 시도에 저항하는 것을 본 그 부하는, 멀지 않은 곳에 있던 자기 주인에게 서둘러 달려가 그를 그 자리로 데려왔다. 다가가면서 레오는 그 기사의 절망이 사랑 때문임을 확신케 하는 말들을 들었으나, 그 사랑의 대상으로 그를 이끌어 줄 실마리는 아무것도 없었다. 왕자는 몸을 굽혀 울고 있는 전사를 끌어안고는, 더없이 다정한 어조로 말했다. \"부디 그대의 시름의 까닭을 숨기지 마시오. 인간에게 닥치는 그토록 절망적인 재앙 가운데 도무지 고칠 수 없는 것은 드무오. 그대가 내게서 슬픔을 감추려 하니 몹시 마음이 아프오. 나는 그 무엇으로도 풀 수 없는 인연으로 그대에게 매여 있는 사람이오. 그러니 그대의 슬픔을 내게 말하고, 재물이든 솜씨든 꾀든 힘이든 설득이든 그것으로 그대를 구할 수 있을지 내게 맡겨 보시오. 그래도 안 된다면, 모든 것을 다 해 보고도 헛수고로 끝난 뒤에 죽어도 늦지 않을 것이오.\"\n\n그가 어찌나 마음을 움직이는 어조로 말했던지 로제로는 응하지 않을 수 없었다. 그가 입을 열기까지는 한참이 걸렸다. 마침내 그가 말했다. \"나리, 제가 어떤 사람인지 아시게 되면, 나리께서도 저처럼 제가 죽는 것을 마땅하다 여기실 것이옵니다. 그러니 아소서, 저는 나리께서 그토록 미워할 까닭이 있는, 그리고 나리를 죽이려고 작정하여 나리의 부친의 궁정으로 찾아갈 만큼 나리를 미워했던 바로 그 로제로이옵니다. 제가 이리한 것은, 제게 약속된 신부가 나리께 빼앗기는 것을 차마 두고 볼 수 없었기 때문입니다. 그러나 사람이 꾀하면 하느님이 처분하시는 법, 절박한 위기에 잘 증명된 나리의 크나큰 도량이 제 굳은 결심을 어찌나 움직였던지, 저는 품었던 미움을 내려놓았을 뿐만 아니라 영원토록 나리의 벗이 되기로 마음먹었습니다. 그때 나리께서는 제게 브라다만테 부인을 얻어 달라 청하셨는데, 그것은 제게 제 심장과 영혼을 내놓으라 하는 것과 다름없었습니다. 제가 충실히 나리를 섬겼는지 아닌지는 나리께서 아실 것입니다. 그 부인은 나리의 것이니, 평화로이 그녀를 차지하소서. 다만 제게 그것을 살아서 보라고는 청하지 마소서. 차라리 제가 죽도록 두십시오. 저와 그녀 사이에는 제가 살아 있는 한 그녀가 다른 이와 합법적으로 혼인할 수 없도록 막는 맹세가 오갔기 때문입니다.\"\n\n이 말에 점잖은 레오는 어찌나 놀랐던지, 한동안 입술도 움직이지 않고 시선을 고정한 채 조각상처럼 말없이 서 있었다. 그리고 그 낯선 이가 로제로라는 발견은 그가 그에게 품었던 호의를 누그러뜨리기는커녕 오히려 키웠으니, 로제로가 겪는 고통에 대한 그의 시름이 자기 자신의 시름과 맞먹는 듯했다. 이런 까닭에서, 그리고 자신이 마땅히 황제의 아들답게 보이고자, 다른 일에서는 뒤질지언정 예의에서만은 뒤지지 않으려고, 그는 말했다. \"로제로, 그대의 비할 데 없는 용맹이 내 군대를 무찌르던 그날 그대가 로제로임을 내가 알았더라면, 그대의 덕이 나로 하여금 그대를 내 사람으로 만들게 했을 것이오. 적이 누구인지도 모르던 그때 그러했듯이 말이오. 그러면 나는 그 못지않게 기꺼이 그대를 테오도라의 지하 감옥에서 구해 냈을 것이오. 그때도 기꺼이 그리했을 것인데, 하물며 지금 그대가 내게 주려고 스스로에게서 빼앗으려는 그 선물을, 어찌 더더욱 기꺼이 돌려주지 않으리오. 그 처녀는 내게보다 그대에게 더 마땅한 사람이오. 그녀의 가치를 내가 안다 해도, 나는 그대 같은 기사를 괴롭게 하느니 차라리 그녀뿐 아니라 목숨까지도 기꺼이 포기하겠소.\"\n\n그는 같은 뜻으로 이것 말고도 많은 말을 했다. 마침내 로제로가 답했다. \"굽히겠습니다. 살아 있는 것에 만족하겠습니다. 그리하여 두 번째로 나리께 제 목숨을 빚지겠습니다.\"\n\n그러나 로제로가 왕궁으로 돌아갈 만큼 회복되기까지는 여러 날이 걸렸다. 왕궁에는 불가리아 제후들이 보낸 사절이 도착해, 일각수의 기사를 찾아 그에게 전투에서 쓰러진 자기네 왕을 대신하여 그 나라의 왕관을 바치려 하고 있었다.\n\n레오 왕자가 브라다만테와의 결투를 치를 때 입었던 찌그러진 갑옷을 그대로 두른 로제로의 손을 잡고 왕 앞에 나섰을 때, 사정은 이러했다. \"보소서,\" 그가 말했다. \"동틀 녘부터 해 질 녘까지 그 고된 결투를 버텨 낸 투사가 바로 여기 있나이다. 그가 싸움의 보상을 청하러 왔나이다.\" 샤를마뉴 왕과 그의 모든 중신은 경악하여 섰으니, 모두가 그리스 왕자 본인이 브라다만테와 싸웠다고 믿었기 때문이다. 그때 마르피사가 앞으로 나서며 말했다. \"로제로가 자기 권리를 주장하러 여기 없으니, 그의 누이인 제가 그의 송사를 맡아, 감히 그의 권리에 이의를 다는 자가 누구든 그에 맞서 그것을 지키겠나이다.\" 그녀가 이 말을 어찌나 분노와 경멸을 담아 했던지, 왕자는 더 이상 꾸미는 것이 현명하지 않다 여기고 로제로의 투구를 그의 이마에서 벗기며 말했다. \"여기 그가 있소!\" 마르피사의 놀라움과 기쁨을 그 누가 다 말할 수 있으랴! 그녀는 달려가 오라비의 목을 끌어안았고, 둘레로 몰려든 샤를마뉴와 리날도, 오를란도, 두돈, 그 밖의 사람들이 그를 끌어안고 그의 이마에 다정한 입맞춤을 누르도록 좀처럼 자리를 내주지 않았다. 그 기쁜 소식은 여러 전령에 의해 비밀의 방에서 슬퍼 누워 있던 브라다만테에게 빠르게 날아갔다. 그녀의 심장 둘레에 멎어 있던 피가 그 소식에 어찌나 빠르게 흘렀던지, 그녀는 기쁨으로 거의 죽을 뻔했다. 에이몬 공작과 베아트리체 부인도 더 이상 승낙을 거두지 않고, 그 용맹한 무리 모두의 앞에서 자기 딸을 용감한 로제로에게 약속했다.\n\n이제 불가리아 사절들이 다가와 로제로의 발치에 무릎 꿇고는, 자기네와 함께 본국으로 돌아가 줄 것을 간청했으니, 아드리아노폴리스에서 왕관과 홀(笏)이 그의 받아들임을 기다리고 있다고 했다. 레오 왕자도 자신의 설득을 그들의 설득에 보태며, 자기 부왕(父王)의 이름으로 그들 편의 평화가 회복되리라 약속했다. 로제로는 승낙했다. 그리고 사람들은, 그토록 두드러지게 그에게서 빛나던 미덕 가운데 그 무엇도, 장차 사위가 될 이가 군주로서 칭송받는 것을 들은 것만큼 로제로를 베아트리체 부인에게 잘 천거해 주지는 못했으리라고 짐작했다.\n론세스바예스 전투\n\n# THE BATTLE OF RONCESVALLES\n\n사라센인들을 프랑스에서 몰아낸 뒤, 샤를마뉴는 군대를 이끌고 에스파냐로 들어가, 지난 전쟁에서 아프리카 사라센인들 편을 든 죄로 그 나라의 왕 마르실리우스를 응징하고자 했다. 샤를마뉴는 모든 시도에서 성공하여, 마르실리우스에게 항복하고 프랑스에 조공을 바치도록 강요했다. 독자들은 가노, 달리 간 혹은 가넬론이라 불리는 자를 기억할 것이다. 우리는 앞선 어느 장에서 그를 샤를마뉴의 늙은 신하이자 오를란도와 리날도, 그리고 그들의 모든 벗에게 철천지원수인 자로 언급한 바 있다. 그는 나이가 비슷하고 오랫동안 가까이 지낸 덕에 샤를에게 큰 영향력을 지녔다. 그리고 그에게 좋은 자질이 없는 것도 아니어서, 용감하고 명민했으나, 시기심이 많고 거짓되며 음흉했다. 간은 샤를을 설득하여 자신을 사절로 마르실리우스에게 보내어 조공을 처리하게 했다. 그는 작별할 때 오를란도를 거듭거듭 끌어안으며, 다정하고 진실해 보이려고 어찌나 애를 썼던지, 그 위선이 늙은 군주를 빼고는 모두의 눈에 뻔히 드러났다. 그는 똑같은 다정함으로 올리버에게도 매달렸는데, 올리버는 그의 얼굴에 대고 경멸하듯 웃으며 속으로 생각했다. \"그대가 아무리 듣기 좋은 말을 늘어놓아도, 그대는 거짓을 말하고 있다.\" 그 자리에 있던 다른 모든 기사들도 같은 생각이었고, 그들은 황제에게 그만큼이나 말하며, 간을 결코 에스파냐인들에게 사절로 보내서는 안 된다고 덧붙였다. 그러나 샤를은 마음이 홀려 있었다.\n\n간은 마르실리우스에게 큰 영예로 맞아들여졌다. 왕은 자신의 영주들을 거느리고 그를 맞으러 사라고사에서 십오 마일 밖까지 나왔고, 환호 속에 그를 도시로 인도했다. 며칠 동안 무도회와 경기와 기사도 시범 외에는 아무것도 없었으니, 귀부인들은 프랑스 기사들의 머리 위로 꽃을 던졌고, 사람들은 \"프랑스! 몽주아와 성 디오니시오!\" 하고 외쳤다.\n\n첫 환영 의례가 끝난 뒤, 왕과 사절은 서로를 이해하기 시작했다. 어느 날 그들은 샘 가장자리의 정원에 함께 앉아 있었다. 물이 어찌나 맑고 잔잔했던지 둘레의 온갖 것을 비추었고, 그 자리는 신선한 바람에 떨리는 과실수들로 에워싸여 있었다. 거리낌 없는 듯 둘이 앉아 이야기를 나눌 때, 간은 왕의 얼굴을 정면으로 보지 않고도 물에 비친 그 표정을 볼 수 있어, 그에 맞추어 말을 조절했다. 마르실리우스도 그에 못지않게 능란하여, 간에게 말을 건네면서 그의 얼굴을 살폈다. 마르실리우스는 사절에게가 아니라 벗에게 하듯, 샤를이 자기 영토를 침범하여 입힌 손해를 한탄하는 것으로 말문을 열며, 샤를이 자기에게서 왕국을 빼앗아 오를란도에게 주려 한다고 비난했다. 그러다 마침내 그는, 그 야심 찬 기사가 죽기만 한다면 선량한 이들이 제 권리를 찾으리라는 자신의 믿음을 노골적으로 내뱉었다.\n\n간은 왕이 한 말의 설득력을 마지못해 인정할 수밖에 없다는 듯 한숨을 내쉬었다. 그러나 오래 자신을 억누르지 못하고 그는 의기양양한 사악함으로 빛나는 얼굴을 들며 외쳤다. \"그대가 하는 말은 한마디 한마디가 진실이오. 그는 죽어야 하오. 그리고 궁정에서 내게 그 더러운 일격을 가한 올리버도 죽어야 하오. 이런 모욕을 응징하는 것이 배신이란 말이오? 나는 모든 것을 계획해 두었소. 그들의 얼빠진 주인과 이미 모든 것을 정해 두었소. 오를란도는 조공을 받으러 그대의 국경으로, 론세스바예스로 올 것이오. 샤를은 산기슭에서 그를 기다릴 것이오. 오를란도는 작은 무리만 데려올 것이니, 그대는 그를 맞을 때 비밀리에 그대의 전군을 등 뒤에 두시오. 그를 에워싸시오. 그러면 그때 조공을 받는 자가 누구이겠소?\"\n\n이 새로운 유다가 이 말을 채 다 내뱉기도 전에, 그의 의기양양함이 자연의 낯빛이 바뀌는 것으로 중단되었다. 하늘이 갑자기 흐려지고 천둥과 번개가 쳤으며, 월계수 한 그루가 위에서 아래까지 둘로 쪼개졌고, 간이 앉아 있던 캐럽나무는 가룟 유다가 목매단 나무가 바로 그 종류라고 전해지는 그 나무인데, 그 꼬투리 하나를 그의 머리 위로 떨어뜨렸다.\n\n간과 마찬가지로 마르실리우스도 이 징조에 질겁했다. 그러나 점쟁이들을 불러 모으니, 그들은 월계수가 카이사르들의 후계자인 황제에게 그 징조를 돌리는 것이라고 결론지었다. 다만 그들 가운데 하나가, 자기는 유다의 나무가 무슨 뜻인지 모르겠다고 하며 어쩌면 사절이 그것을 설명할 수 있을지 모르겠다고 넌지시 말하여 간의 당혹감을 다시 일으켰다. 간은 분노로 자신의 분함을 달랬다. 사악함이 몸에 밴 습성이 다른 모든 고려를 압도했다. 그리하여 왕은 전군의 선두에 서서 론세스바예스로 진군할 채비를 했다.\n\n간은 샤를마뉴에게 편지를 써, 마르실리우스가 얼마나 겸손하고 순종적으로 조공을 오를란도의 손에 바치러 오고 있는지, 그리고 황제가 그를 중도까지 마중 나가 조공을 바친 뒤 자기 진영에서 그를 맞을 채비를 하는 것이 얼마나 훌륭한 일일지 적었다. 그는 조공과 거기 딸린 선물에 대한 화려한 설명도 덧붙였다. 선량한 황제는 답장을 써, 사절의 수고에 매우 흡족하며, 일이 자신이 바라던 대로 꼭 맞게 처리되었다고 했다. 그러나 그의 궁정은 여전히 의심을 품고 있었는데, 다만 간이 샤를을 론세스바예스 인근으로 끌어들이려는 속셈이 오를란도를 마르실리우스의 손에 죽게 한 다음 샤를마저 그의 손에 넘기려는 것임은 거의 짐작하지 못했다.\n\n그러나 오를란도는 자신의 주군이자 군주가 바라는 대로 했다. 그는 자기를 기다리는 잔혹한 음모는 꿈에도 모른 채, 적당한 규모의 전사 행렬을 거느리고 론세스바예스로 갔다. 그동안 간은 샤를 앞에서 거리낌 없고 태연한 모습을 보여 자기 음모의 성공을 굳히려고 서둘러 프랑스로 돌아갔다. 한편 마르실리우스는 확실에 확실을 더하려고, 무려 세 개의 군대를 론세스바예스의 고갯길로 끌어들였다. 그것들은 최악의 경우 차례로 기사를 덮쳐 수효로 그를 짓밟을 셈이었다. 그는 또한 간의 조언에 따라, 먼저 희생자들 앞에 차려 놓으려고 술과 좋은 음식을 무더기로 가져왔다. \"그것이,\" 그 배신자는 말했다. \"잔치 벌이는 자들이 무장을 풀게 하여 공격을 한층 효과적으로 만들 것이오. 그러나 한 가지 잊어서는 안 될 것이 있소.\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"내 아들 발드윈은 분명 오를란도와 함께 있을 것이오. 나를 위해 그 아이의 목숨을 지켜 주시오.\"\n\n\"내 몸에서 벗은 이 옷을 그에게 주겠소.\" 왕이 말했다. \"전투에서 그것을 입게 하고 두려워 말라 하시오. 내 병사들에게 그를 건드리지 말라 명할 것이오.\"\n\n간은 기뻐하며 프랑스로 떠났다. 그는 군주와 궁정 사람들을 빙 둘러 끌어안았는데, 마치 자기가 그들에게 축복밖에는 가져다준 것이 없는 사람인 양 굴었고, 늙은 왕은 다정함과 기쁨에 겨워 눈물을 흘렸다.\n\n\"무언가 잘못 돌아가고 있다, 아주 어두워 보인다.\" 선량한 마법사 말라지지가 생각했다. \"리날도가 여기 없는데, 그가 꼭 있어야 한다. 그가 어디 있는지, 그리고 리차르데토도 어디 있는지 알아내어, 한시바삐 그들을 불러와야겠다.\"\n\n말라지지는 자신의 술법으로 아스타로트라는 이름의, 현명하고 무섭고 잔인한 정령을 불러냈다. \"리날도에 대해 말해 다오, 그것도 사실대로.\" 말라지지가 그 정령에게 말했다. 악마는 기사를 매섭게 쏘아보며 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그 낯빛은 흐려지고 사나웠다.\n\n마법사는 한층 더 흐려진 낯빛으로 아스타로트에게 그 표정을 거두라 명하고, 더 노한 강압을 쓰겠다는 듯 손짓을 했다. 그러자 놀란 악마는 혀를 풀고 말했다. \"리날도에 대해 무엇을 알고 싶은지 말하지 않았소이다.\"\n\n\"그가 무엇을 하고 있었는지, 그리고 어디에 있는지 알고 싶다.\"\n\n\"그는 동에서 서로 세상을 정복하고 세례를 베풀고 있었소이다.\" 악마가 말했다. \"그리고 지금은 리차르데토와 함께 이집트에 있소이다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 간은 마르실리우스와 무엇을 꾸미고 있느냐?\" 말라지지가 물었다. \"그리고 거기서 무슨 일이 벌어질 것이냐?\"\n\n\"모르겠소이다.\" 악마가 말했다. \"그때 나는 간을 지켜보고 있지 않았고, 우리 타락한 정령들은 미래를 알지 못하오. 내가 알아챌 수 있는 것이라고는, 하늘의 징조와 혜성들로 보아 무언가 끔찍한 일이, 무언가 아주 기이하고 배신에 찬 피비린내 나는 일이 막 벌어지려 한다는 것뿐이오. 그리고 간을 위한 자리가 지옥에 마련되어 있다는 것뿐이오.\"\n\n\"사흘 안에,\" 마법사가 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"리날도와 리차르데토를 론세스바예스 고갯길로 데려오너라. 그리하면 내 다시는 너를 불러내지 않겠노라 이로써 약속하마.\"\n\n\"그들이 나를 믿고 따라오려 하지 않으면 어찌하오?\" 정령이 말했다.\n\n\"리날도의 말에 들어가, 그가 너를 믿든 안 믿든 그를 데려오너라.\"\n\n\"그리하겠소이다.\" 악마가 답했다.\n\n지진이 일더니 아스타로트가 사라졌다.\n\n이제 마르실리우스는 오를란도를 파멸시키기 위한 첫 움직임을 시작했으니, 자신의 봉신인 블랑샤르딘 왕을 술과 그 밖의 진미를 선물로 들려 그보다 앞서 보냈다. 절제할 줄 알면서도 정중한 영웅은 그것을 선의로 받아들여, 그 배신자가 바라던 대로 그것들을 나누어 주었다. 그런 다음 블랑샤르딘은 앞으로 나아가 샤를마뉴에게 인사를 올린다는 핑계로 돌아가, 자기 군주가 정해 준 자리인 제2군의 선두에 섰다. 오를란도에게 아들을 전투에서 잃은 팔세론 왕이 제1군을 이끌었고, 발루간테 왕이 제3군을 이끌었다. 마르실리우스는 그들에게 연설을 하여 자신의 계획을 알려 주었고, 그들이 자기가 보낸 옷으로 알아볼 수 있을, 그리고 그리스도교도 가운데 살려 두어야 할 유일한 자인 자기 벗 간의 아들에게 호의를 베풀어 달라고 부탁하는 것으로 끝맺었다.\n\n한편 이 간의 아들과, 불신자들을 믿지 못하여 어떻게든 오를란도와 함께 있고자 했던 몇몇 기사들은, 그 운명의 골짜기에서 영웅과 합류한 터였다. 그리하여 그 작은 그리스도교 무리는, 자기네 주군과 그 벗들의 어마어마한 용맹을 생각하건대 그냥 헐값에 팔려 갈 자들이 아니었다. 아아, 그리스도교 세계의 두 번째 벼락인 리날도는 사태를 맞을 시간에 그곳에 닿을 운명이 못 되었다. 기사들은 오를란도에게 배신을 경계하여 더 많은 병력을 불러오라고 헛되이 간청했다. 신앙의 투사의 위대한 마음은, 어쩔 수 없는 한 의심을 품으려 하지 않았다. 그는 쓸데없을지도 모르는 지원을 부르기를 거부했고, 또한 자신의 주군이 명한 것 외에는 아무것도 하지 않으려 했다. 그러면서도 그는 불안을 온전히 떨쳐 내지는 못했다. 위대하고 쾌활한 그의 마음에도 그림자가 드리워졌다. 그가 벗들을 대하는 얼굴과는 달리, 그들의 예감이 그를 어지럽혔다. 어쩌면 어떤 예지로 자신의 죽음이 다가옴을 느꼈는지도 모른다. 그러나 그는 그런 인상을 부추겨서는 안 된다고 여겼다. 게다가 시간이 촉박했다. 고대하던 조공의 순간이 임박했고, 사소한 정황들의 조합이 흔히 가장 큰 사건을 결정하는 법이다.\n\n마르실리우스 왕은 이튿날 아침 일찍 조공을 가지고 오기로 되어 있었고, 올리버는 아침 해와 함께 정찰하러 말을 달려, 멀리서 에스파냐 궁정의 평화로운 행렬을 알아볼 수 있을지 살폈다. 그는 가장 가까운 고지로 올라가, 그 꼭대기에서 이미 고갯길에 전열을 갖추고 있는 마르실리우스의 제1군을 보았다. \"오, 악마 같은 간.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"이것이 바로 네 수고의 결말이로구나!\" 올리버는 말에 박차를 가하여 산을 내달려 오를란도에게 돌아갔다.\n\n\"자,\" 영웅이 외쳤다. \"무슨 소식인가?\"\n\n\"나쁜 소식이오.\" 그의 사촌이 말했다. \"어제는 들으려 하지 않으셨던 그런 소식이오. 마르실리우스가 무장하고 여기 와 있고, 온 세상이 그와 함께요.\"\n\n기사들은 오를란도 주위로 몰려들어, 도움이 필요하다는 표시로 뿔피리를 불라고 간청했다. 그의 유일한 대답은 말에 올라 산소네토와 함께 산으로 올라가는 것이었다.\n\n그러나 그가 눈을 들어 자기 둘레에 있는 것을 보자마자, 그는 슬픔에 잠겨 돌아서서 론세스바예스를 내려다보며 말했다. \"오, 비참한 골짜기여! 오늘 네 안에 흘릴 피가 네 이름을 영원토록 물들이리라.\"\n\n오를란도의 작은 진영은 사라센인들에 대한 분노로 들끓었다. 그들은 더없이 조바심을 내며 무장을 갖추었다. 투구 끈을 매고 말에 오르는 것 외에는 아무것도 없었고, 그동안 선량한 대주교 튀르팽은 대열을 이리저리 누비며 그리스도의 전사들을 권면하고 격려했다. 오를란도와 그의 지휘관들은 잠시 의논하러 물러났다. 그는 슬픔에 그야말로 신음했고, 처음에는 한마디도 하지 못했으니, 자기 사람들을 론세스바예스에서 죽게 만든 것이 그토록 비참했기 때문이다. 그러다 그가 말했다. \"에스파냐 왕이 이런 악당일 줄 내 마음에 한 번이라도 떠올랐더라면, 그대들은 결코 오늘을 보지 않았을 것이다. 그는 나와 수천 가지 예의와 좋은 말을 주고받았다. 그래서 나는 우리가 전에 더 나쁜 원수였던 만큼, 이제 더 좋은 벗이 되었다고 여겼다. 나는 모든 인간이 좋은 기회가 있으면 이런 종류의 미덕을 발휘할 수 있다고 믿었다. 다만 제 용서한 이조차 결코 용서하지 못하는 그런 비열한 마음의 악당들만은 빼고 말이다. 그리고 나는 그가 그런 자들 가운데 하나라고는 생각지 않았다. 죽어야 한다면, 정직하고 용맹한 사내답게 죽자. 그리하여 우리에 대해, 죽은 것은 다만 우리의 몸뿐이었다는 말이 나오게 하자. 내가 뿔피리를 불지 않은 까닭은, 한편으로는 그것이 우리에게 어울리지 않는다고 여겼기 때문이고, 또 한편으로는 설령 우리 주군이 그 소리를 듣는다 해도 우리를 좀처럼 구할 수 없을 것이기 때문이다.\" 그리고 이 말과 함께 오를란도는 말 위로 뛰어오르며 외쳤다. \"사라센인들을 향해 진격하라!\" 그러나 그는 얼굴을 돌리기가 무섭게 쓰라리게 울며 말했다. \"오, 거룩한 동정녀여, 저 죄인 오를란도는 생각지 마시고, 부디 당신의 이 종들을 가엾이 여기소서!\"\n\n이제 어마어마한 흙먼지와 골짜기를 가득 채우며 밀려드는 끝없는 뿔피리와 작은북 소리와 함께, 이교도들의 제1군이\n\n군마들이 늠름하게 나타났고, 말들은 울부짖었으며, 수천 개의 군기가 공중에서 펄럭였다.\n팔세론[Falseron] 왕이 그들을 이끌었고, 휘하 장교들에게 이렇게 말했다. \"누구도 감히 오를란도[Orlando]에게 손을 대서는 안 된다. 그는 내 몫이다. 내 아들의 죽음에 대한 복수는 내 것이다. 우리 사이에 끼어드는 자는 내가 베어 버리겠다.\"\n\n\"자, 친구들이여.\" 오를란도가 말했다. \"각자 자기 한 몸을 지키고, 성 미카엘께서 우리 모두를 지켜 주시기를! 여기 모인 이들 가운데 완벽한 기사가 아닌 자는 하나도 없다.\" 그가 이렇게 말할 만도 했으니, 리날도[Rinaldo]와 리차르데토[Ricciardetto]만 빼고 온 프랑스의 정수가 그곳에 모여 있었다. 한 사람 한 사람이 가려 뽑힌 인재였으며, 모두 오를란도의 벗이자 변함없는 동료였다.\n\n그리하여 작은 부대와 거대한 군대의 지휘관들은 서로를 바라보며 마주 앉아 있다가, 큰 군대가 다가오자 서로의 상대를 골라냈고, 이윽고 기사들은 창을 겨누고 한동안 둘씩 둘씩 차례로 달려 나가 서로 맞부딪쳤다.\n\n가장 먼저 움직인 것은 아스톨포[Astolpho]였다. 그는 소리오의 아를로토[Arlotto]를 향해 달려가, 상대의 몸뚱이를 안장에서 밀쳐 내고 그 영혼을 저세상으로 보내 버렸다. 올리버[Oliver]는 말프리모[Malprimo]와 맞붙었고, 비록 그를 다치게 하는 일격을 받기는 했으나, 자신의 창을 말프리모의 심장 한가운데로 곧장 꿰뚫었다.\n\n팔세론은 이 일격에 기가 꺾였다. \"참으로.\" 그는 생각했다. \"이것은 놀라운 일이로다.\" 올리버는 사라센인들 사이로 밀고 들어가지는 못했으니, 그 상처가 너무 고통스러웠던 것이다. 그러나 이제 오를란도가 자신과 휘하의 온 무리를 움직이기 시작하니, 어떤 소란이 뒤따랐을지 가히 짐작할 만하다. 타격과 투구가 부딪쳐 울리는 소리는 마치 불카누스의 대장간이 활짝 열린 듯하였다. 팔세론은 오를란도가 그토록 맹렬하게 다가오는 것을 보고, 사슬을 끊고 뛰쳐나온 루키페르라고 여겼으며, 그를 오로지 자신의 몫으로 삼겠노라 작정했을 때와는 전혀 다른 마음이 되었다. 오히려 그는 자기 신들에게 몸을 의탁하고는, 보다 상서로운 복수의 때를 기다리려는 생각으로 발길을 돌렸다. 그러나 오를란도가 무시무시한 목소리로 그를 불러 세우며 말했다. \"오 이 배신자여! 묵은 다툼을 화해로 끝맺은 것이 고작 이런 결말을 위해서였느냐?\" 그러고는 오를란도가 어찌나 날쌘 분노와 더불어 동시에 놀라운 창술로 팔세론에게 달려들었던지, 비록 그 몸뚱이에 창을 박아 즉사시키고 다시 빼냈음에도 그 몸은 안장에서 미동도 하지 않았다. 영웅은 앞으로 내달리면서도 그토록 완벽한 일격의 결말을 보고 싶어 못 견뎌, 말머리를 돌려 그 송장을 검으로 건드리니, 그것이 그 즉시 떨어졌다!\n\n이교도들은 자기들의 지휘관이 죽은 것을 보자 어찌나 큰 공포가 그들을 덮쳤던지 팔라딘들에게 전장을 내주고 떠나려 하였으나, 그럴 수가 없었다. 마르실리우스[Marsilius]가 나머지 병력을 골짜기 둘레로 그물처럼 둘러쳤던 까닭에, 등을 돌려 보아야 헛일이었다. 오를란도는 그들 한복판으로 말을 몰았고, 그가 가는 곳마다 투구 위로 벼락이 떨어졌다. 올리버는 다시 난전에 뛰어들어 발터[Walter] 및 발드윈[Baldwin], 아비노[Avino]와 아볼리오[Avolio]와 함께 싸웠고, 한편 투르팽[Turpin] 대주교는 주교 지팡이를 창으로 바꿔 들고 새로운 무리를 산으로 쫓아냈다.\n\n그러나 수효를 헤아릴 수 없는 적들을 상대로 무엇을 할 수 있었으랴? 마르실리우스는 끊임없이 그들을 쏟아부었다. 팔라딘들은 수천에 맞선 한 줌에 지나지 않았다. 어찌하여 리날도와 리차르데토의 말들은 더디 오는가?\n\n말들이 더딘 것은 아니었으나, 운명이 마법보다 빨랐던 것이다. 아슈타로트[Ashtaroth]가 이집트에서 리날도 앞에 나타나, 자신의 용건을 전한 뒤, 그와 그의 종 입더러운놈[Foul-mouth]이 리날도와 리차르데토의 말 속으로 들어가니, 그 말들은 몸속의 악귀들과 더불어 울부짖고 콧김을 내뿜으며 펄쩍 뛰기 시작하더니, 마침내 공중을 날아 피라미드 위를 지나 사막을 가로질러, 마르실리우스가 세 번째 군대를 끌고 올 바로 그때 스페인과 전장에 다다랐다. 말을 탄 두 팔라딘은 사라센인들 한복판으로 곧장 떨어져, 그들 사이에서 어찌나 큰 살육을 벌였던지, 산에서 싸움을 내려다보던 마르실리우스는 자기 병사들이 서로 등을 돌려 싸우는 줄로 여겼다. 오를란도가 그것을 보고는, 자신의 사촌들이 틀림없으리라 짐작하고 그들을 향해 달려갔다. 바로 그 순간 올리버도 다가오니, 온 무리의 환희는 이루 말할 수 없었다. 황급히 몇 마디 설명을 주고받은 뒤, 그들은 다시 적에게 돌아서지 않을 수 없었으니, 그 수효는 도무지 끝이 없어 보였다.\n\n오를란도는 마르실리우스를 향해 피의 길을 내며 나아가다가, 한 젊은이의 머리를 내리쳤는데, 그 투구가 어찌나 단단했던지 일격을 견뎌 냈으나 동시에 벗겨져 날아갔다. 오를란도가 두 번째 일격을 가하려는데, 그 젊은이가 외쳤다. \"멈추시오! 당신은 내 아버지를 사랑했소. 나는 부야포르테[Bujaforte]요!\" 팔라딘은 부야포르테를 본 적이 없었으나, 그 아버지인 선량한 노인과 닮은 것을 알아보고 검을 떨구었다. \"오 부야포르테여.\" 그가 말했다. \"내 진정 그를 사랑했노라. 한데 그의 아들이 어찌하여 여기서 제 벗들과 맞서 싸우고 있느냐?\"\n\n부야포르테는 눈물 때문에 한동안 입을 떼지 못했다. 마침내 그가 말했다. \"나는 내 주군이자 영주이신 마르실리우스 때문에 여기 있을 수밖에 없소. 나는 싸우는 시늉만 했을 뿐, 그리스도교도는 한 명도 해치지 않았소. 배신이 당신 사방에 깔려 있소. 발드윈 자신도 마르실리우스가 준 조끼를 입고 있으니, 모두가 그를 그의 벗 간[Gan]의 아들임을 알아보고 해치지 않게 하려는 것이오.\"\n\n\"투구를 다시 쓰라.\" 오를란도가 말했다. \"그리고 지금까지 해 온 대로 행하라. 그대 아버지의 벗이 그 아들에게 결코 적이 되지는 않으리라.\"\n\n이윽고 영웅은 분노에 차서 발드윈을 찾으려 돌아섰는데, 마침 그 순간 발드윈이 다정한 표정으로 그를 향해 서둘러 다가오고 있었다.\n\n\"이상한 일입니다.\" 발드윈이 말했다. \"나는 할 수 있는 한 내 본분을 다했건만, 아무도 나를 향해 덤벼들지 않습니다. 좌우로 적들을 베어 넘겼는데도, 무엇 때문에 가장 사나운 이교도들이 나를 피하는지 도무지 알 수가 없습니다.\"\n\n\"그 조끼를 벗어라.\" 오를란도가 경멸하듯 말했다. \"그러면 알고 싶다면 곧 그 비밀을 알게 되리라. 그대 아버지가 우리를 마르실리우스에게 팔아넘겼다. 다만 그의 명예로운 아들만은 빼고 말이다.\"\n\n\"만약 내 아버지가.\" 발드윈이 격분하여 조끼를 찢어 던지며 말했다. \"그런 악당이었다면, 그리고 내가 죽음을 면한다면, 이 검을 그의 심장에 꽂아 넣으리라. 그러나 나는 배신자가 아니오, 오를란도여. 그리 말하다니 나를 욕보이는 것이오. 내가 불명예를 안고 살 수 있으리라 생각지 마시오.\"\n\n발드윈은 오를란도의 다음 말을 들으려고 기다리지도 않고 박차를 가하여 싸움터로 내달렸다. 오를란도는 자기가 한 말을 몹시 후회했으니, 그 젊은이가 절망에 빠진 것을 알아챘기 때문이다.\n\n이제 싸움은 그 어느 때보다도 격렬하게 타올랐다. 팔라딘 한 명에 이교도 스무 명이 쓰러졌으나, 그래도 팔라딘들도 쓰러져 갔다. 산소네토[Sansonetto]는 그란도니오[Grandonio]의 곤봉에 맞아 땅바닥에 나가떨어졌고, 다물리온의 발터는 어깨가 부러졌으며, 베를링기에리[Berlinghieri]와 오토네[Ottone]가 죽임을 당했고, 마침내 아스톨포도 쓰러지니, 오를란도는 그의 죽음에 복수하고자 그가 죽은 자리를 사라센인의 피로 된 호수로 만들어 버렸다. 불운한 부야포르테는 리날도와 마주쳤는데, 어찌하여 자기가 사라센 편에서 싸우는 것처럼 보이는지 미처 설명도 하기 전에 머리에 어찌나 모진 일격을 받았던지, 한마디 말도 내뱉지 못하고 쓰러졌다. 오를란도는 격렬한 싸움과 소란이 벌어지는 곳으로 길을 뚫고 나아가다가, 가슴에 창 두 자루가 박힌 가엾은 젊은이 발드윈, 곧 간의 아들을 발견했다. \"이제 나는 배신자가 아니오.\" 발드윈이 말했고, 그것이 그가 남긴 마지막 말이었다. 오를란도는 자기가 그의 죽음의 원인이 된 것을 몹시 슬퍼하여, 두 눈에서 눈물이 흘러내렸다. 마침내 올리버 자신도 쓰러졌다. 그는 제 피에 눈이 멀어, 오를란도를 알아보지 못한 채 그를 내리쳤던 것이다. \"이게 어찌 된 일인가, 사촌이여.\" 오를란도가 외쳤다. \"그대마저 적에게 넘어갔단 말인가?\" \"오 나의 주군이자 영주여.\" 상대가 외쳤다. \"용서를 비오. 나는 아무것도 보이지 않소. 나는 죽어 가고 있소. 어떤 배신자가 내 등을 찔렀소. 나를 사랑하거든, 내 말을 저들 한복판으로 끌고 가 주오, 내가 복수도 못 한 채 죽지 않도록 말이오.\"\n\n\"나도 머지않아 죽으리라.\" 오를란도가 말했다. \"오로지 고된 싸움과 비탄 때문에 말이다. 그러니 우리 함께 가세.\"\n\n오를란도는 사촌의 말을 가장 빽빽한 곳으로 끌고 갔는데, 죽어 가는 자와 지친 그 동료의 힘은 무시무시하였다. 그들은 거리 하나를 내며 싸움터를 빠져나왔고, 오를란도는 사촌을 천막으로 데려가서 말했다. \"내가 돌아올 때까지 잠시 기다리게. 저기 언덕에 가서 뿔나팔을 불고 오겠네.\"\n\n\"소용없네.\" 올리버가 말했다. \"내 영혼은 빠르게 떠나고 있으며, 자기 주님이자 구세주와 함께하기를 바라고 있다네.\"\n\n그는 더 말하고자 하였으나, 그 말은 꿈꾸는 자의 말처럼 어눌하게 흘러나왔고, 그렇게 그는 숨을 거두었다.\n\n오를란도는 그가 죽은 것을 보자 자기 혼자만 이 세상에 남은 듯한 심정이 들었고, 이 세상을 떠나는 데 아무 미련이 없었으되, 다만 그가 떠나기 전에 산기슭에 있는 샤를[Charles] 왕이 사정이 어떠한지 알아 주기를 바랐다. 그리하여 그는 뿔나팔을 집어 들어 세 번 불었으니, 어찌나 세게 불었던지 코와 입에서 피가 터져 나왔다. 투르팽은 세 번째 나팔 소리에 뿔나팔이 두 동강으로 갈라졌다고 전한다.\n\n싸움의 그 온갖 소음에도 불구하고, 뿔나팔 소리는 마치 저세상에서 들려오는 목소리처럼 그 위로 울려 퍼졌다. 그 소리에 새들이 죽어 떨어졌으며, 온 사라센 군대가 공포에 질려 물러섰다고 전한다. 그 소리가 다다랐을 때 샤를마뉴[Charlemagne]는 자기 궁정 한가운데 앉아 있었고, 간도 그 자리에 있었다. 그 소리를 가장 먼저 들은 것은 황제였다.\n\n\"저 소리가 들리는가?\" 그가 귀족들에게 말했다. \"내가 들은 것처럼 그대들도 저 뿔나팔 소리를 들었는가?\"\n\n이에 그들 모두 귀를 기울였고, 간은 가슴이 철렁 내려앉는 것을 느꼈다. 뿔나팔이 두 번째로 울렸다.\n\n\"이게 무슨 뜻인가?\" 샤를이 말했다.\n\n\"오를란도가 사냥을 하고 있는데.\" 간이 말했다. \"사슴이 잡힌 것이지요.\"\n\n그러나 뿔나팔이 세 번째로 울렸을 때, 그 소리가 어찌나 무섭도록 격렬했던지, 모두가 서로를 쳐다보더니, 이윽고 다들 분노에 차 간을 노려보았다. 샤를이 자리에서 일어섰다.\n\n\"이것은 사슴 사냥이 아니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"그 소리가 내 심장 깊은 곳까지 파고든다. 오 간이여! 오 간이여! 그대 때문에 내가 낯을 붉히는 것이 아니라, 나 자신 때문에 붉히노라. 오 더럽고 흉악한 악당이여! 경들이여, 이자를 잡아다가 깊은 옥에 가두라. 하느님께서 내가 이 날을 보도록 살려 두지 않으셨더라면!\"\n\n그러나 말로 따질 때가 아니었다. 그들은 그 배신자를 옥에 가두었고, 이윽고 샤를은 온 궁정을 거느리고 슬퍼하며 기도하며 롱스보[Roncesvalles]로 길을 떠났다.\n\n뿔나팔이 울린 것은 오후였고, 황제가 출발한 것은 그로부터 반 시간 뒤였다. 그동안 오를란도는, 아무리 가망 없을지라도 자기가 말 위에 앉아 있을 수 있는 한 본분을 다하고자 다시 싸움터로 돌아갔다. 마침내 그는 고된 싸움과 열병으로 자신의 최후가 다가옴을 느끼고는, 전에 갈증을 풀었던 샘으로 홀로 말을 몰았다. 그의 말은 그보다 더 지쳐 있어서, 주인이 내리자마자 그 짐승은 마치 작별을 고하려는 듯, \"당신을 안식의 자리로 모셔 왔습니다\" 하고 말하려는 듯 무릎을 꿇더니, 그의 발치에서 쓰러져 죽었다. 오를란도는 그가 죽었다고 믿고 싶지 않아 샘물을 떠다 끼얹었으나, 아무 소용이 없음을 알고는 마치 인간이었던 것처럼 그를 슬퍼하며 눈물을 흘리며 이름을 불렀고, 혹여 자기가 그를 부당하게 대한 일이 있었다면 용서를 빌었다. 이 말에 그 말이 눈을 조금 떠서 주인을 다정하게 바라보더니 이내 더는 움직이지 않았다고 전한다. 또한 오를란도가 그때 온 힘을 다하여 아름다운 검 두린다나[Durindana]로 곁에 있던 바위를 내리쳤다고도 전하는데, 강철을 산산조각 내어 적의 손에 떨어지지 않게 하려는 생각이었다. 그러나 바위는 점판암처럼 쪼개져 그 큰 틈이 이후로도 영영 남아 순례자들의 눈을 놀라게 하였건만, 검은 조금도 상하지 않은 채 남았다.\n\n이제 리날도와 리차르데토가 투르팽과 함께 다가왔으니, 그들은 사라센인들을 물리치고 와서 오를란도에게 전투에서 이겼노라 전하였다. 그러자 오를란도는 투르팽 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 자기 죄를 사하여 달라고 빌었고, 투르팽이 그에게 사죄를 베풀었다. 오를란도는 마치 십자가상을 보듯 자기 검의 자루에 두 눈을 고정하고는 그것을 끌어안았으며, 두 눈을 들어 올리니 천사처럼 거룩하게 변모한 존재 같았고, 머리를 숙이며 그 맑은 영혼을 내쉬었다.\n\n이제 샤를 왕과 그 귀족들이 다다랐다. 황제는 죽은 오를란도를 보자, 마치 무모한 젊은이라도 된 듯 말에서 몸을 던져 그 시신을 끌어안고 입 맞추며 말했다. \"그대를 축복하노라, 오를란도여. 그대의 온 생애를, 그대의 모든 것을, 그대가 한 모든 일을, 그리고 그대를 낳은 아버지를 축복하노라. 또한 그대를 죽음으로 몰아간 자들의 말을 믿은 것을 그대에게 용서받기를 구하노라. 그들은 응분의 대가를 치를 것이다, 오 사랑하는 이여! 그러나 참으로 살아 있는 이는 그대이며, 죽은 것보다 못한 자는 바로 나로다.\"\n\n롱스보의 들판은 황제의 눈에 끔찍하기 그지없었다. 과연 사라센인들은 패하여 달아났으나, 그의 팔라딘들은 둘만 빼고 모두 그곳에 죽어 쓰러져 있었고, 온 골짜기는 거대한 도살장처럼 피와 흙으로 짓밟히고 열기에 김을 내뿜으며 악취를 풍기고 있었다. 샤를은 경악과 고뇌로 심장 한가운데까지 떨었다. 그곳을 말없이 응시한 뒤, 그는 엄숙한 저주로 그곳을 저주하며, 그 안에도 그 둘레의 어떤 산에도 다시는 풀이 자라지 말고 어떤 종류의 씨앗도 돋지 말 것이며, 오직 하늘의 진노만이 그 위에 영원히 머물기를 바랐다.\n\n샤를과 그의 전사들은 사라센인들을 쫓아 스페인으로 들어갔다. 그들은 사라고사를 함락하여 불태웠고, 마르실리우스는 그가 간과 더불어 흉계를 꾸몄던 그 캐럽나무에 매달렸다. 그리고 간은 온 나라의 저주 속에 롱스보에서 교수되어 사지가 찢기는 형벌을 받았다.\n리날도와 바야르\n\n# RINALDO AND BAYARD\n\n샤를마뉴는 롱스보의 참사에서 그토록 많은 가장 용감한 전사들을 잃은 데 비탄에 잠겼고, 배신자 간 백작의 권고에 자신을 그토록 송두리째 내맡긴 자기 경솔함을 쓰라리게 자책했다. 그러나 그는 곧 비슷한 덫에 빠지고 말았으니, 자기 못난 아들 샤를로[Charlot]가 자신에게 지나친 영향력을 갖도록 내버려 두어, 그 아들이 제정신이라면 부끄러워하여 결코 저지르지 않았을 잔인하고 부당한 행위들로 그를 끊임없이 이끌었던 것이다. 리날도와 그 형제들은 이 오만한 젊은 왕자에게 사소한 무례를 저질렀다는 이유로 파리에서 달아나, 자기들의 성 몽탈방[Montalban]으로 피신하지 않을 수 없었다. 샤를이 그들을 붙잡을 수만 있다면 모조리 교수하겠노라 공공연히 말했기 때문이다. 그는 가장 용감한 기사들을 여럿 보내 그들을 체포하려 했으나, 모두 헛수고였다. 리날도가 그들의 시도를 좌절시키고 갑옷과 영광을 빼앗긴 채 돌려보내거나, 아니면 그를 만나 이야기를 나눈 뒤 그들이 돌아와 왕에게 그런 일에 자기들은 그의 도구가 될 수 없노라 고하곤 했다.\n\n마침내 샤를 자신이 대군을 일으켜, 그 팔라딘을 굴복시키고자 몸소 나섰다. 그는 몽탈방 주위의 온 고장을 짓밟아 식량 보급을 끊어 버렸고, 감히 밖으로 나오려 하는 자는 누구든 죽이겠노라 위협하니, 식량이 떨어져 수비대가 굴복하지 않을 수 없게 만들려는 속셈이었다.\n\n리날도의 자원은 너무도 바닥나 더는 버티는 것이 무의미해 보일 지경이 되었다. 그의 형제들은 작은 접전에서 포로로 붙잡혔고, 그들의 목숨을 구할 유일한 희망은 왕과 화친을 맺는 데 있었다.\n\n그리하여 그는 사자를 보내, 왕이 자기와 형제들의 목숨을 살려 준다면 자신과 성을 내어 주겠노라 제안했다. 사자가 떠나 있는 동안 리날도는 어떤 소식을 가져올지 안달이 나서, 그를 맞으러 말을 타고 나갔다. 그는 분별 있다 여겨지는 만큼 말을 달린 뒤, 숲에서 멈추어 내려서서는 바야르[Bayard]를 나무에 매어 두었다. 그러고는 앉아서 기다리다가 잠이 들었다. 그동안 바야르는 풀어져, 풀이 마음을 끄는 곳으로 어슬렁어슬렁 가 버렸다. 마침 그때 시골 사람 몇이 지나가다가 서로 말했다. \"이보게, 저것이 리날도가 타는 그 명마 바야르 아닌가? 저놈을 붙잡아 샤를 왕에게 데려가세. 우리 수고에 후하게 값을 쳐 주실 게야.\" 그들은 그렇게 했고, 왕은 그 노획물에 크게 기뻐하며 그들이 죽는 날까지 부유하게 살 만한 선물을 주었다.\n\n리날도가 깨어나 자기 말을 찾아 둘러보았으나 보이지 않자, 그는 신음하며 말했다. \"오 내가 태어난 불운한 시각이여! 운명이 어찌 이리 나를 괴롭히는가!\" 그는 어찌나 절망했던지 갑옷과 박차를 벗으며 말했다. \"바야르를 잃었거늘, 이런 것들이 내게 무슨 소용이랴?\" 그가 이렇게 한탄하며 서 있을 때, 덤불에서 한 사내가 나왔는데, 나이로 허리가 굽은 듯했다. 그는 긴 수염을 가슴까지 드리웠고, 눈썹이 두 눈을 거의 덮고 있었다. 그가 리날도에게 좋은 날 되시라 인사했다. 리날도가 고마움을 표하며 말했다. \"좋은 날이라곤 태어난 이래 거의 가져 본 적이 없소.\" 그러자 노인이 말했다. \"리날도 나리, 절망하셔서는 안 됩니다. 하느님께서 모든 일을 가장 좋은 쪽으로 돌려놓으실 테니까요.\" 리날도가 대답했다. \"내 고통은 너무 무거워 구원을 바랄 수가 없소. 왕이 내 형제들을 잡아다 죽이려 하오. 내 말 바야르로 그들을 구해 낼 작정이었건만, 내가 잠든 사이 어떤 도둑놈이 그놈을 훔쳐 가 버렸소.\" 노인이 대답했다. \"기도 가운데 나리와 형제분들을 기억하겠습니다. 저는 가난한 사람인데, 제게 무어라도 베푸실 것이 없으신지요?\" 리날도가 말했다. \"내게는 줄 것이 없소.\" 그러나 곧 자기 박차가 떠올랐다. 그는 그것을 거지에게 주며 말했다. \"자, 내 박차를 가지시오. 이것은 내 아버지 에몽[Aymon] 백작께서 나를 기사로 서임하셨을 때 어머니께서 처음 주신 선물이오. 이것이면 그대에게 십 파운드는 될 것이오.\"\n\n노인은 박차를 받아 자루에 넣고는 말했다. \"고귀하신 나리, 제게 주실 만한 것이 달리 또 없으신지요?\" 리날도가 대답했다. \"그대 지금 나를 놀리는 게요? 진실로 말하건대, 그토록 무력한 자를 때리는 것이 부끄럽지만 않다면, 더 나은 예의범절을 가르쳐 주었을 것이오.\" 노인이 말했다. \"참으로, 나리, 그리하셨다면 큰 죄를 짓는 것이옵니다. 제가 동냥했던 모든 이가 저를 때렸더라면, 저는 진작에 맞아 죽었을 것입니다. 저는 교회와 수도원에서, 그리고 할 수 있는 곳이면 어디서든 동냥을 청하니까요.\" \"그대 말이 옳소.\" 리날도가 대답했다. \"그대가 청하지 않으면 누구도 그대를 구제하지 않을 테지.\" 노인이 말했다. \"옳습니다, 고귀하신 나리. 그러니 청하옵건대 나누어 주실 것이 더 있거든 제게 주십시오.\" 리날도는 그에게 자기 망토를 주며 말했다. \"받으시오, 순례자여. 그리스도의 사랑으로 그대에게 주는 것이니, 하느님께서 내 형제들을 치욕스러운 죽음에서 구하시고, 내가 샤를 왕의 손아귀에서 벗어나도록 도우시기를.\"\n\n순례자는 망토를 받아 개켜서 자기 자루에 넣었다. 그러더니 세 번째로 리날도에게 말했다. \"나리, 기도 가운데 나리를 기억할 수 있도록 제게 주실 것이 더 남지 않았는지요?\" \"이 못된 것!\" 리날도가 외쳤다. \"그대 나를 놀림감으로 삼느냐?\" 그러고는 검을 빼어 그를 내리쳤으나, 노인은 지팡이로 그 일격을 막아 내며 말했다. \"리날도, 그대의 사촌 말라지지[Malagigi]를 죽이려는 게요?\" 리날도는 그 말을 듣자 손을 멈추고 노인을 미심쩍게 바라보았는데, 노인은 이제 변장을 벗어 던지니 과연 말라지지였다. \"사랑하는 사촌이여.\" 리날도가 말했다. \"부디 용서하시오. 그대를 알아보지 못했소. 하느님 다음으로 나의 믿음은 그대에게 있소. 부탁이니 내 형제들이 옥에서 빠져나오도록 도와주시오. 나는 말을 잃어, 그들에게 아무런 도움도 줄 수가 없소.\"\n말라지지가 대답했다. \"사촌 리날도여, 내 그대가 말을 되찾도록 해 주겠소. 그동안 그대는 내가 시키는 대로 해야 하오.\"\n\n이윽고 말라지지는 자루에서 긴 겉옷 하나를 꺼내, 리날도에게 갑옷 위에 입으라고 주었고, 구멍이 숭숭 난 모자 하나와 헌 신발 한 켤레를 신으라고 주었다. 그들은 매우 늙고 가난한 두 순례자처럼 보였다. 그러고서 그들이 숲에서 나오니, 얼마 지나지 않아 길을 따라 말을 타고 오는 수도사 넷이 보였다. 말라지지가 리날도에게 말했다. \"내가 가서 저 수도사들을 만나 무슨 소식이라도 알아낼 수 있는지 보겠소.\"\n\n말라지지는 수도사들에게서, 다가오는 축일에 궁정에 큰 인파가 모이리라는 것을 알아냈으니, 왕자가 전에 리날도의 소유였던 그 유명한 명마 바야르를 귀부인들에게 보여 주려 한다는 것이었다. \"뭐라고요!\" 순례자가 말했다. \"바야르가 거기 있단 말이오?\" \"그렇소.\" 수도사들이 대답했다. \"왕이 그 말을 샤를로에게 주었는데, 왕자가 그 말을 탄 다음, 왕은 리날도의 형제들에게 선고를 내려 그들을 교수할 작정이오.\" 그러자 말라지지가 수도사들에게 동냥을 청했으나, 그가 순례자의 옷을 벗어 던지고 자기 갑옷을 그들에게 보여 주기 전까지는 그들이 아무것도 주려 하지 않았다. 그제야 그들은 한편으로는 자비심에서, 한편으로는 두려움에서, 햇빛에 반짝이는 보석들로 장식된 황금 잔 하나를 그에게 주었다. 그러자 말라지지는 서둘러 리날도에게 돌아가, 자기가 알아낸 것을 그에게 전했다.\n\n축일 아침, 리날도와 말라지지는 경기가 열릴 장소에 다다랐다. 말라지지가 리날도에게 박차를 도로 주며 말했다. \"사촌이여, 박차를 차시오, 그대가 그것을 필요로 할 테니.\" \"내가 어찌 그것을 필요로 하겠소.\" 리날도가 말했다. \"말을 잃었거늘?\" 그래도 그는 말라지지가 일러 준 대로 했다.\n\n두 사람이 군중 틈에 끼어 들판 가장자리에 자리를 잡자, 궁정의 왕자들과 귀부인들이 모여들기 시작했다. 그들이 모두 모이자 왕도 왔고, 샤를로도 함께 왔는데, 그 곁에는 마부들이 명마 바야르를 이끌고 있었으니, 그들은 그 말을 안전하게 지키라는 명령을 단단히 받은 터였다. 왕은 구경꾼들의 둘레를 둘러보다가 말라지지와 리날도를 보았고, 그들이 가진 휘황한 잔을 눈여겨보고는 샤를로에게 말했다. \"보아라, 내 아들아, 저 두 순례자가 어찌나 빛나는 잔을 가졌는지. 백 두카트는 나가 보이는구나.\" \"참으로 그렇습니다.\" 샤를로가 말했다. \"가서 저들이 어디서 그것을 얻었는지 물어보지요.\" 그리하여 그들은 순례자들이 서 있는 곳으로 말을 몰았고, 샤를로는 그들 가까이에 바야르를 세웠다.\n\n말은 순례자들의 냄새를 맡더니 리날도를 알아보고는 주인에게 어리광을 부렸다. 왕이 말라지지에게 말했다. \"이보게, 그 아름다운 잔을 어디서 얻었는가?\" 말라지지가 대답했다. \"존귀하신 나리, 저는 교회와 수도원에서 십일 년간 동냥하여 모은 돈을 죄다 들여 이것을 샀습니다. 교황님께서 친히 이것을 축복하시어, 누구든 이것으로 먹거나 마시는 자는 자기 모든 죄를 사함받는 권능을 주셨습니다.\" 그러자 왕이 샤를로에게 말했다. \"내 아들아, 이들은 참으로 거룩한 사람들이로다. 말 못 하는 짐승조차 어찌 저들을 경배하는지 보아라.\"\n\n이윽고 왕이 말라지지에게 말했다. \"그대의 잔에서 한 모금 내게 주어, 내 죄가 씻기게 하라.\" 말라지지가 대답했다. \"고명하신 주군이시여, 언제든 나리를 거스른 모든 자를 용서하지 않으신다면, 저는 감히 그리할 수 없습니다. 그리스도께서 당신을 배신하고 십자가에 못 박은 모든 자를 용서하셨음을 아시지 않습니까.\" 왕이 대답했다. \"이보게, 그 말은 옳다. 그러나 리날도는 나를 너무도 심하게 거슬렀으니, 나는 그를 용서할 수 없고, 또 다른 자, 마법사 말라지지도 용서할 수 없다. 이 둘은 결코 다시는 내 왕국에서 살지 못할 것이다. 내가 그들을 붙잡으면 반드시 교수하겠다. 한데 말해 보라, 순례자여, 그대 곁에 선 저 사내는 누구인가?\" \"저자는 귀머거리에 벙어리에 장님입니다.\" 말라지지가 말했다. 그러자 왕이 다시 말했다. \"그대의 잔에서 내게 마시게 하여, 내 죄를 없애게 하라.\" 말라지지가 대답했다. \"주군이신 왕이시여, 여기 제 가엾은 아우가 있는데, 쉰 날 동안 듣지도 말하지도 보지도 못했습니다. 이 불행은 저희가 묵었던 어느 집에서 그에게 닥쳤는데, 그저께 저희가 한 지혜로운 여인을 만났더니, 그를 고칠 유일한 희망은 바야르를 타는 어떤 곳에 가서 그 말에 올라타 모는 것이라 일러 주었습니다. 그것이 무엇보다도 그에게 좋으리라 하였습니다.\" 그러자 왕이 말했다. \"이보게, 그대는 마침 알맞은 곳에 왔도다. 오늘 여기서 바야르를 탈 것이니 말이다. 그대의 잔에서 내게 한 모금 주면, 그대의 동행이 바야르에 오르게 해 주겠다.\" 말라지지는 이 말을 듣고 말했다. \"그리하겠습니다.\" 그러자 왕은 큰 경건함으로 숟가락을 들어 순례자의 잔에서 한 술을 떠내었으니, 그로써 자기 죄가 사함받으리라 믿었던 것이다.\n\n이 일이 끝나자 왕이 샤를로에게 말했다. \"아들아, 청컨대 이 병든 순례자를 네 말에 앉혀, 할 수 있거든 타게 해 주어라. 그리하면 그가 자기 모든 병약함에서 나을 것이니라.\" 샤를로가 대답했다. \"기꺼이 그리하겠습니다.\" 그렇게 말하며 그가 말에서 내리자, 하인들이 순례자를 팔로 받쳐 말에 오르도록 거들었다.\n\n리날도는 말에 오르자 두 발을 등자에 걸고 말했다. \"조금 타 보고 싶구나.\" 말라지지는 그가 말하는 것을 듣고 기뻐하는 듯하더니, 그에게 보고 듣는 것도 되느냐고 물었다. \"그렇소.\" 리날도가 말했다. \"나는 내 모든 병약함에서 나았소.\" 왕이 그 말을 듣고 투르팽 주교에게 말했다. \"주교 경, 우리는 이를 십자가와 깃발을 앞세운 행렬로 기념해야 하오. 이는 큰 기적이니 말이오.\"\n\n리날도는 자기가 면밀히 감시당하지 않는 것을 알아채고는, 말에게 말을 걸며 박차로 그를 건드렸다. 바야르는 자기 주인이 자기 위에 탄 것을 알고 빠른 걸음으로 내달리기 시작하여, 순식간에 멀찍이 가 버렸다. 말라지지는 크게 놀란 척했다. \"오 고귀하신 왕이자 주군이시여.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"제 가엾은 동행이 말에 끌려가고 있습니다. 그가 떨어져 목이 부러질 것입니다.\" 왕은 기사들에게 명하여 순례자를 뒤쫓아 데려오거나, 필요하다면 그를 도우라 하였다. 그들은 그리했으나 헛일이었다. 리날도는 그들 모두를 뒤로 따돌리고 줄곧 길을 달려 몽탈방에 다다랐다. 말라지지는 의심받지 않고 떠나도록 허락되어 제 갈 길을 갔으니, 자기 동료의 운명을 두고 슬피 한탄하며, 그가 틀림없이 산산조각 났으리라 여기는 척했다.\n\n말라지지는 멀리 가지 않고, 변장을 바꾸어 왕이 있는 곳으로 돌아가, 리날도의 형제들을 옥에서 빼내는 데 자기 최고의 재주를 부렸다. 그는 성공했고, 셋 모두 무사히 몽탈방에 이르렀으니, 형제들을 구하고 바야르를 되찾은 데 대한 리날도의 기쁨은 혀로는 다 이를 수 없을 정도였다.\n리날도의 죽음\n\n# DEATH OF RINALDO\n\n리날도의 성에서 식량이 부족하여 겪는 곤경은 포위의 압박 아래 날이 갈수록 더욱 심해졌다. 수비대는 어쩔 수 없이 자기들의 말을 잡아야 했으니, 말들이 먹어 치울 양식을 아끼는 동시에 그 고기를 식량으로 삼으려는 것이었다. 마침내 바야르만 빼고 모든 말이 잡히고 말았고, 리날도가 형제들에게 말했다. \"바야르를 잡아야겠다, 달리 먹을 것이 없으니.\" 그리하여 그들은 마구간으로 가서 바야르를 잡으려고 끌고 나왔다. 그러나 알라르도[Alardo]가 말했다. \"형님, 바야르를 조금만 더 살려 둡시다. 하느님께서 우리에게 무슨 일을 해 주실지 누가 알겠습니까?\"\n\n바야르는 이 말을 듣고 마치 사람인 양 알아듣고는, 자비를 구하려는 듯 무릎을 꿇었다. 리날도가 자기 말의 곤경을 보자 마음이 약해져, 그를 살려 두었다.\n\n바로 이때, 황제의 누이인 리날도의 어머니 아야[Aya]가 자기 아들들을 위해 탄원하고자 기사들과 귀부인들을 거느리고 진영에 왔다. 그녀는 왕 앞에 무릎을 꿇고, 리날도와 그 형제들을 용서해 달라 간청했다. 그러자 모든 중신과 기사가 그녀 편을 들어, 왕에게 그녀의 청을 들어주기를 간곡히 빌었다. 그러자 왕이 말했다. \"사랑하는 누이여, 그대는 선한 어머니의 도리를 다하는구려. 내 그대의 다정한 마음을 존중하여 그대의 간청에 따르겠소. 그대의 아들들이 내 뜻에 절대적으로 복종한다면, 내 그들의 목숨을 살려 주겠소.\"\n\n샤를로는 이 말을 듣고 왕에게 다가가 그의 귀에 대고 속삭였다. 그러자 왕이 자기 누이에게로 돌아서서 말했다. \"샤를로가 바야르를 가져야 하오. 내가 그 말을 그 아이에게 주었기 때문이오. 자, 가시오, 누이여, 그리고 내가 한 말을 리날도에게 전하시오.\"\n\n아야 부인은 이 말을 듣고 기뻐하며 마음속으로 하느님께 감사드리고는 말했다. \"고귀하신 왕이자 오라버니시여, 분부하신 대로 하겠습니다.\" 그리하여 그녀가 성으로 들어가니, 그녀의 아들들이 더없이 기쁘고 다정하게 그녀를 맞이했고, 그녀는 그들에게 왕의 제안을 전했다. 그러자 알라르도가 말했다. \"형님, 저는 바야르를 샤를로에게 주느니 차라리 왕의 적의를 사겠습니다. 그가 그 말을 죽일 것이라 믿기 때문입니다.\" 다른 형제들도 모두 같은 말을 했다. 리날도가 그들의 말을 듣고 말했다. \"사랑하는 아우들아, 말을 내어 줌으로써 우리 용서를 얻을 수 있다면, 그리하자. 우리 화친을 맺자, 우리는 왕의 힘에 맞설 수 없으니.\" 그러고는 어머니에게 가서, 왕이 그들을 용서하고 그들이 왕관과 위엄에 거슬러 행한 모든 일을 사하여 준다면, 그 말을 샤를로에게 줄 것이며 그 이상도 내어 주겠노라 전했다. 부인은 샤를에게 돌아가 아들들의 대답을 전했다.\n\n그리하여 왕과 에몽의 아들들 사이에 화친이 맺어지자, 형제들은 바야르를 데리고 성에서 나와 왕의 발 앞에 엎드려 용서를 빌었다. 왕은 그들에게 일어나라 명하고는, 그의 모든 고귀한 기사와 고문관들이 보는 앞에서 그들을 다시 총애 안에 받아들이니, 모두가, 특히 그들의 어머니인 아야 부인이 크게 기뻐했다. 이윽고 리날도는 명마 바야르를 끌어다 샤를로에게 주며 말했다. \"나의 주군이자 왕자시여, 이 말을 당신께 드리오니, 좋으실 대로 처분하소서.\" 샤를로는 약속된 대로 그를 받았다. 그러더니 그는 하인들에게 그를 다리로 끌고 가 물속에 던지게 했다. 바야르는 바닥까지 가라앉았으나, 곧 다시 수면으로 떠올라 헤엄쳤고, 리날도가 자기를 바라보는 것을 보고는 뭍으로 올라와 옛 주인에게 달려가, 마치 무엇을 헤아릴 줄 아는 것처럼 당당하게 그 곁에 서서, \"어찌하여 저를 이렇게 대하셨습니까?\" 하고 말하려는 듯하였다. 왕자가 그것을 보고 말했다. \"리날도, 그 말을 내게 다시 다오, 그놈은 죽어야 하니까.\" 리날도가 대답했다. \"나의 주군이자 왕자시여, 그는 두말할 것 없이 당신의 것입니다.\" 그러고는 그를 그에게 넘겨주었다. 그러자 왕자는 발마다 맷돌 하나씩을, 목에는 둘을 매달게 하고는, 다시 그를 물속에 던지게 했다. 바야르는 물속에서 발버둥 치며 주인을 올려다보더니, 돌들을 떨쳐 내고 리날도에게 돌아왔다. 알라르도가 그것을 보고 말했다. \"이제 형님이 그 말을 또 내어 준다면, 형님은 영영 치욕을 안게 될 것입니다.\" 그러나 리날도가 대답했다. \"아우야, 가만히 있어라. 내 말 한 마리의 목숨 때문에 다시 왕의 노여움을 돋우겠느냐?\" 그러자 알라르도가 말했다. \"아, 바야르여! 네 그 참된 사랑과 봉사에 우리가 어찌 이런 보답을 한단 말이냐!\" 리날도는 그 말을 왕자에게 다시 주며 말했다. \"나의 주군이여, 만약 그 말이 또 나온다면 나는 더는 그를 당신께 돌려 드릴 수 없으니, 내 가슴이 너무도 미어지기 때문입니다.\" 그러자 샤를로는 바야르에게 전처럼 돌을 매달게 하여 물속에 던지게 하고는, 리날도에게는 말이 자기를 볼 수 있는 곳에 서 있지 말라고 명했다. 바야르는 수면으로 떠오르자 물 밖으로 목을 빼고 주인을 찾아 두리번거렸으나, 그를 보지 못했다. 그러자 그는 바닥으로 가라앉았다.\n\n리날도는 바야르를 잃은 것이 어찌나 비통했던지, 평생토록 다시는 말을 타지 않고, 옆구리에 검도 차지 않으며, 은수자가 되겠노라 맹세했다. 그는 어느 황량한 숲으로 들어가기로 결심했으나, 먼저 자기 성으로 돌아가 자식들을 보고 각자에게 자기 영지의 몫을 정해 주기로 했다.\n\n그리하여 그는 왕과 형제들에게 작별을 고하고 몽탈방으로 돌아갔으며, 그의 형제들은 왕 곁에 남았다. 리날도는 자식들을 불러, 맏아들 에므리[Aymeric]를 기사로 삼고 그를 자기 성과 영지의 영주로 세웠다. 그는 나머지에게는 자기에게 있던 다른 재산을 나누어 주고, 그들 모두에게 입 맞추고 끌어안고는, 그들을 하느님께 의탁하고 무거운 마음으로 그들 곁을 떠났다.\n\n그가 멀리 가기 전에 한 숲으로 들어섰는데, 거기서 오래도록 세상을 등지고 지내 온 한 은수자를 만났다. 리날도가 그에게 인사하니, 은수자가 정중히 답하며 그가 누구이며 무슨 뜻을 품었는지 물었다. 리날도가 대답했다. \"수사님, 저는 죄 많은 삶을 살았습니다. 많은 폭력을 저질렀고, 많은 이를 죽였는데, 늘 옳은 명분에서 그런 것이 아니라 흔히 제 고집스러운 격정에 떠밀려 그리하였습니다. 또한 제 편을 들어 준 많은 벗의 죽음의 원인이 되었으니, 그들은 제가 옳다고 여겨서가 아니라 오직 저를 사랑하여 제 편을 들었던 것입니다. 이제 저는 제 모든 죄를 고백하고, 혹여 하느님의 자비가 저를 용서해 주신다면, 남은 평생을 속죄하며 살고자 왔습니다.\" 은수자가 말했다. \"벗이여, 그대가 큰 죄에 빠져 하느님의 계명을 어겼음을 알겠소. 그러나 그분의 자비는 그대의 죄보다 크니, 그대가 진심으로 뉘우치고 새 삶을 산다면, 그분이 지난 일을 용서해 주시리라는 희망이 아직 그대에게 있소.\" 그리하여 리날도는 위로를 얻고 말했다. \"스승님, 제가 스승님과 함께 머물며, 분부하시는 대로 하겠습니다.\" 은수자가 대답했다. \"뿌리와 풀이 그대의 음식이 될 것이오. 셔츠나 신발은 걸쳐서는 안 되오. 그대가 나와 함께 머문다면 그대의 몫은 가난과 결핍일 것이오.\" 리날도가 대답했다. \"그 모든 것을, 아니 그 이상도 기꺼이 견디겠습니다.\" 그리하여 그는 은수자와 함께 꼬박 삼 년을 지냈는데, 그 뒤로 그의 기력이 쇠하여 마치 죽을 것만 같아 보였다.\n\n어느 날 밤 은수자가 꿈을 꾸었는데, 하늘에서 들려오는 목소리가 그에게, 그의 동행이 지체 없이 성지로 가서 이교도에 맞서 싸워야 한다고 그에게 이르라 명하였다. 은수자는 그 목소리를 듣고 기뻐하며 리날도를 불러 말했다. \"벗이여, 하느님의 천사가 내게 명하여 그대에게 이르라 하셨으니, 그대는 지체 없이 예루살렘으로 가서, 이교도와의 싸움에서 우리 동료 그리스도교도를 도와야 하오.\" 그러자 리날도가 말했다. \"아! 스승님, 제가 어찌 그리할 수 있겠습니까? 다시는 말을 타지 않고 손에 검이나 창을 들지 않겠노라 맹세한 지 삼 년이 넘었습니다.\" 은수자가 대답했다. \"사랑하는 벗이여, 하느님께 순종하여, 천사가 명한 대로 하시오.\" \"그리하겠습니다.\" 리날도가 말했다. \"스승님, 저를 위해 기도해 주십시오, 하느님께서 저를 바른길로 인도하시도록.\" 그러고서 그는 떠나, 바닷가로 가서 배를 타고 시리아의 트리폴리에 이르렀다.\n\n그가 길을 가는 동안 기력이 그에게 돌아와, 마침내 그의 전성기 때와 맞먹게 되었다. 그리고 그는 결코 말에 오르지도, 손에 검을 들지도 않았으나, 순례자의 지팡이로 그리스도교도들의 군대에서 훌륭한 공을 세웠다. 하느님께서 기뻐하시어 그는 비록 숱한 전투에 임했으되 다치지 않고 화를 면했으며, 그의 용기는 병사들에게도 같은 용기를 불어넣었다. 마침내 사라센인들과 휴전이 맺어지자, 이제 늙고 쇠약해진 리날도는 죽기 전에 다시금 고국 땅을 보고자 하여, 배를 타고 프랑스로 항해해 갔다. 그는 도착하자 권세 있는 자들의 거처를 피하고, 자기가 알려지지 않은 미천한 사람들 틈에서 사는 편을 택했다. 그는 시골 일을 하고, 우유와 빵으로 연명하며, 물을 마시고, 그것으로 만족했다. 그가 그렇게 살던 중, 쾰른이라는 도시가 그곳에서 신앙을 위해 피를 쏟은 성인들의 유물과 유해 덕분에 가장 거룩하고 으뜸가는 도시라는 말을 들었다. 이것이 그로 하여금 그곳으로 가도록 이끌었다. 이 경건한 영웅은 쾰른에 도착하자 성 베드로 수도원으로 가서, 밤낮으로 기도에 전념하며 거룩한 삶을 살았다. 마침 그때 쾰른에 인접한 마을에 무서운 역병이 창궐했다. 많은 사람이 리날도에게 와서, 역병이 멎도록 자기들을 위해 기도해 달라 간청했다. 거룩한 사람은 간절히 기도하여, 주님께 그 백성에게서 역병을 거두어 달라 빌었고, 그의 기도가 응답받았다. 역병의 타격이 멎으니, 모든 사람이 거룩한 사람에게 감사하고 하느님을 찬양했다.\n\n그런데 이 무렵 쾰른에는 아길롤푸스[Agilolphus]라는 주교가 있었으니, 그는 지혜롭고 분별 있는 사람으로, 정결하고 은둔하는 삶을 살며 남들에게 좋은 본을 보였다. 이 주교가 성 베드로 성당을 짓는 일을 맡아, 인근의 모든 석공과 다른 일꾼들에게 쾰른으로 오면 일감과 품삯을 얻으리라 알렸다. 다른 이들 가운데 리날도도 왔는데, 그는 일꾼들 틈에서 일하며 보통 일꾼 네댓 명 몫 이상을 해냈다. 다른 이들이 저녁을 먹으러 갈 때 그는 돌과 회반죽을 날라, 그들이 하루 종일 쓸 만큼을 마련해 놓았다. 다른 이들이 잠자리에 들 때 그는 돌 위에 몸을 뉘었다. 그는 빵만 먹고 물만 마셨으며, 품삯으로는 하루에 한 푼만 받았다. 십장이 그에게 이름이 무엇이며 어디 출신이냐고 물었다. 그는 말하려 하지 않고, 아무 말 없이 자기 일에만 매달렸다. 사람들은 그를 성 베드로의 일꾼이라 불렀으니, 그가 그토록 일에 헌신했기 때문이다.\n\n감독관이 이 거룩한 사람의 부지런함을 보고는 다른 일꾼들의 게으름을 꾸짖으며 말했다. \"너희는 이 선한 사람보다 더 많은 품삯을 받으면서도, 그의 절반만큼도 일하지 않는구나.\" 이런 까닭에 다른 일꾼들은 리날도를 미워하여, 그를 죽이기로 은밀히 모의했다. 그들은 그가 밤마다 어느 성당에 가서 기도하고 적선하는 것을 관례로 삼고 있음을 알았다. 그리하여 그들은 그를 죽일 작정으로 매복하여 기다리기로 했다. 그가 그 자리에 이르자 그들은 그를 붙잡아 머리를 후려쳐 죽였다. 그러고는 그의 시신을 돌과 함께 자루에 넣어, 자루가 바닥으로 가라앉아 거기 감추어지기를 바라며 라인강에 던졌다. 그러나 하느님께서는 그렇게 되기를 바라지 않으시어, 그 자루가 수면에 떠올라 강둑으로 밀려 올라오게 하셨다. 그리고 거룩한 순교자의 영혼은 천사들에 의해 찬미의 노래와 함께 하늘로 인도되었다.\n\n그런데 그 무렵 도르트문트 사람들이 그리스도교 신앙으로 개종해 있었다. 그래서 그들은 쾰른의 주교에게 사람을 보내, 그 도시에 그토록 풍부히 있는 거룩한 유물 가운데 얼마를 자기들에게 달라 청했다. 그리하여 주교는 자기 성직자들을 불러 모아 이 청에 어떤 답을 줄지 의논했다. 그리고 방금 순교를 당한 그 거룩한 사람의 유해를 도르트문트 사람들에게 주기로 결정되었다.\n\n이제 그 유해를 관과 함께 수레에 실으니, 수레가 말도 사람의 도움도 없이 도르트문트를 향해 움직이기 시작하여, 오늘날 성 리날도 성당이 서 있는 자리에 이르기까지 멈추지 않았다. 주교와 그의 성직자들은 그 거룩한 사람에게 예를 표하고자 찬가를 부르며 삼 마일에 걸쳐 그를 따랐다. 그리고 성 리날도는 그 이후로 그곳의 수호성인이 되었으며, 전설에 나와 있듯이 하느님께서 그를 통해 많은 놀라운 일을 행하셨다.\n\n# HUON OF BORDEAUX\n\n보르도의 위옹\n샤를마뉴는 나이가 들어 가면서 통치의 짐이 해마다 더 무거워짐을 느끼다가, 마침내 자기의 고위 제후들과 중신들을 불러 모아, 제국과 프랑스의 왕좌를 자기 아들들인 샤를로와 루이[Lewis]에게 넘기고 양위할 뜻을 제안했다.\n\n황제는 자기 맏아들에게 분별없이 편애를 품고 있었다. 그는 제후들과 중신들이 샤를로를 유일한 군주로 청해 주기를 바랐을 것이다. 그러나 그 왕자는 거짓됨과 잔인함으로 워낙 악명이 높았던 탓에, 회의는 황제의 양위 제안에 한사코 반대하며, 그토록 영광스럽게 휘둘러 온 왕홀을 계속 쥐고 있어 주기를 간청했다.\n\n가늘롱[Ganelon]의 사촌이며 이제 마간차[Maganza] 가문의 사악한 분파의 우두머리가 된 오트빌의 아모리[Amaury]는 샤를로의 은밀한 추종자였으니, 그는 방탕한 품행과 못된 성정에서 샤를로를 빼닮은 자였다. 아모리는 기엔[Guienne] 가문에 대해 더없이 쓰라린 원한을 품고 있었으니, 그 가문의 선대 공작 세비누스[Sevinus]가 종종 그의 악행을 꾸짖었기 때문이다. 그는 이 기회를 틈타, 세비누스 공작이 그 어머니인 알리스[Alice] 공작부인의 보살핌 아래 남겨 둔 두 어린 자식에게 해를 끼치는 동시에, 샤를로의 부와 권력을 키워 줌으로써 그와의 사이에서 자기 잇속을 챙기려 했다. 이런 속셈으로 그는 왕자에게 새로운 생각 하나를 넌지시 일러 주었다.\n\n그는 제후들의 의견에 동의하는 척했다. 그는 샤를로를 왕좌에 앉히기에 앞서 그에게 부유한 속주 몇을 주어 통치 능력을 시험해 보는 것이 최선이라고, 또한 황제께서는 자기 영토의 어느 부분도 잃지 않고서 샤를로에게 기엔의 봉토 수여권을 줄 수 있다고 말했다. 세비누스가 죽은 지 칠 년이 지났건만, 그 아들인 젊은 공작이 아직 자기의 정당한 군주에게 마땅히 바쳐야 할 신종 서약을 하러 샤를마뉴의 궁정에 오지 않았기 때문이다.\n\n우리는 어느 경우에나 바이에른의 나모[Namo] 공작이 샤를마뉴에게 베푼 조언의 정의로움과 지혜로움에 자주 감탄할 기회를 가져 왔거니와, 그는 이번에도 아모리의 이기적인 조언을 분개하여 물리쳤다. 그는 황제에게 세비누스 자식들의 어린 나이와 그들의 작고한 아버지가 베푼 유익하고 영광스러운 공로들을 일깨우고, 샤를마뉴에게 기사 둘을 보르도의 공작부인에게 보내, 그녀의 두 아들을 황제의 궁정으로 불러들여 인사를 올리고 신종 서약을 하게 하자고 제안했다.\n\n샤를마뉴는 이 조언을 옳게 여겨, 두 기사를 보내 그 두 어린 왕자를 그 어머니에게 요청하게 했다. 공작부인은 두 기사가 다가온다는 소식을 듣자마자, 지체 높은 사람들을 보내 그들을 맞이하게 했다. 그리고 그들이 궁에 들어서자마자 그녀는 자기의 맏아들과 어린 아들, 위옹[Huon]과 지라르[Girard]를 데리고 그들 앞에 모습을 드러냈다. 사절들은 받은 영예와 후대에, 그리고 곁들여진 값진 선물들에 기뻐하며 아쉬운 마음으로 보르도를 떠났다. 그들은 돌아와 샤를마뉴에게, 젊은 공작 위옹이 그 용맹한 아버지의 발자취를 따르도록 태어난 듯하다고 아뢰며, 석 달 안에 기엔의 젊은 왕자들이 그의 궁정에 모습을 드러내리라 알렸다.\n\n공작부인은 그 짧은 사이에 아들들에게 마지막 가르침을 주었다. 위옹은 그것을 가슴에 새겨 받아들였고, 지라르는 그토록 어린 아이에게 기대할 수 있는 만큼은 거기에 귀를 기울였다.\n\n그들의 출발 준비가 갖추어지자, 공작부인은 그들을 다정히 끌어안고 하늘의 보살핌에 의탁하며, 가는 길에 그 유명한 클뤼니[Cluny] 수도원에 들러 그들 아버지의 형제인 수도원장을 찾아뵙도록 당부했다. 그 높은 지위에 걸맞은 이 수도원장은 선행을 베풀 기회를 결코 놓치지 않았으며, 온갖 미덕의 본을 보이고, 자기 본보기로써 덕을 매력 있는 것으로 만든 사람이었다.\n\n그는 더없는 성대함으로 조카들을 맞이했다. 그리고 자기의 임석이 샤를마뉴와의 사이에서 그들에게 얼마나 유익할지 잘 알고 있었으니 — 그는 샤를마뉴가 소중히 여기는 고문관이었던 것이다 — 그들과 함께 파리로 가는 길에 올랐다.\n\n아모리는 샤를마뉴의 두 사절이 보르도에서 받은 환대와, 젊은 왕자들이 황제의 궁정을 방문하기 위해 마련된 준비를 알게 되자, 샤를로에게 그의 호위병 한 무리를 자기에게 달라고 넌지시 청했으니, 그것으로 몽틀레리[Montlery] 숲에서 그 젊은이들을 매복하여 기다렸다가 죽임으로써, 그로써 왕자 샤를로에게 기엔 공작령을 차지하게 해 줄 작정이었다.\n\n배신과 폭력의 계략은 샤를로의 성정에 너무도 잘 들어맞았다. 그는 아모리의 제안을 받아들였을 뿐만 아니라, 거기에 한몫 끼겠다고 고집했다. 그들은 밤을 틈타 은밀히 나갔으니, 모두 검은 갑옷으로 무장한 수많은 수행원을 거느리고, 형제들이 지날 그 숲에 매복하려는 것이었다.\n\n둘 가운데 동생인 지라르는 말을 타고 가면서 눈앞에 나타나는 사냥감에 자기 매를 날려 보내며 즐기다가, 형과 클뤼니 수도원장보다 앞서 말을 달려 나가 있었다. 샤를로는 그가 홀로 무장도 없이 오는 것을 보고는, 그를 맞으러 나가 그에게 시비를 걸더니, 창 일격으로 그를 말에서 떨어뜨렸다. 지라르는 떨어지면서 비명을 질렀고, 위옹이 그것을 듣고 검 외에는 다른 무기도 없이 그를 지키려고 날아갔다. 그가 동생에게 다다라 보니, 그 상처에서 피가 흐르고 있었다. \"이 아이가 네게 무슨 짓을 했단 말이냐, 이 못된 놈!\" 그가 샤를로에게 외쳤다. \"스스로를 지킬 채비도 안 된 아이를 치다니 어찌 그리 비겁한가!\" \"내 명예를 걸고.\" 샤를로가 말했다. \"내 너에게도 똑같이 해 줄 작정이다. 알아 두어라, 나는 아르덴의 티에리[Thierry] 공작의 아들이며, 네 아비 세비누스가 그분에게서 성 셋을 빼앗았다. 나는 그분의 원수를 갚겠노라 맹세했으니, 너에게 도전한다.\" \"비겁한 놈.\" 위옹이 대답했다. \"나는 네 혈통에 깃든 비열함을 잘 안다. 티에리의 아들답게, 네 갑옷이 주는 이점을 마음껏 써라. 그러나 알아 두어라, 나는 너를 두려워하지 않는다.\" 이 말에 샤를로는 사악하게도 창을 겨누어 위옹에게 달려들었고, 위옹은 가까스로 자기 팔을 망토로 감쌀 틈밖에 없었다. 이 보잘것없는 방패로 그는 창의 일격을 받아 냈다. 창은 망토를 꿰뚫었으나 그의 몸은 빗나갔다. 그러자 위옹 경은 등자를 딛고 일어서며 샤를로에게 검으로 어찌나 무서운 일격을 가했던지, 투구가 두 동강으로 갈라지고 머리도 함께 쪼개졌다. 그 비겁한 왕자는 땅바닥에 쓰러져 죽었다.\n\n이제 위옹은 그 숲이 무장한 자들로 가득함을 알아챘다. 그는 자기 수행원들을 불렀고, 그들은 황급히 대열을 갖추었으나, 숲에서 그를 치러 나오는 자는 아무도 없었다. 샤를로가 쓰러지는 것을 본 아모리는 자기 몸을 위태롭게 할 마음이 없었다. 그리고 샤를마뉴가 자기 아들의 죽음에 복수하리라 확신하였기에, 지금 자기가 더 무엇을 해야 할 까닭이 없다고 보았다. 그는 위옹과 클뤼니 수도원장이 지라르의 상처를 싸매도록 내버려 두었고, 그들이 떠나 파리로 가는 길을 다시 잡는 것을 본 뒤, 샤를로의 시신을 거두어 말에 가로질러 얹고는 파리로 운반하게 하여, 위옹보다 네 시간 뒤에 그곳에 도착했다.\n\n클뤼니 수도원장은 자기 조카를 샤를마뉴에게 알현시켰으나, 위옹은 경의를 표하기를 삼가며, 자기에게 놓였던 매복에 대해 비통하게 호소했으니, 그것이 황제의 허락 없이는 있을 수 없는 일이라고 말했다. 샤를마뉴는 그 너그러운 영혼으로는 결코 받을 까닭이 없는 비난에 놀라, 자기 조카가 호소하는 바의 근거가 무엇이냐고 수도원장에게 다급히 물었다. 수도원장은 일어난 모든 일을 그에게 사실대로 고하며, 아르덴의 티에리의 아들이라 자칭하는 비겁한 기사가 지라르에게 상처를 입히고 무장도 없는 위옹에게 달려들었으나, 위옹이 그 힘과 용맹으로 그 배신자를 제압하여 들판에 죽어 쓰러뜨려 두었노라 아뢰었다.\n\n샤를마뉴는 그 파렴치한 티에리의 행위와 자기는 아무 관련이 없노라 분개하여 부인하고, 젊은 공작에게 그 승리를 축하한 뒤, 몸소 두 형제를 호화로운 거처로 안내하고, 지라르의 상처에 첫 치료가 베풀어지는 것을 지켜보고 머물렀으며, 바이에른의 나모 공작에게 두 형제를 맡겼으니, 나모는 세비누스 공작과 한때 전우였던지라 그 젊은이들을 거의 제 친아들처럼 여겼다.\n\n샤를마뉴가 그들 곁을 떠나자마자, 자기 방으로 돌아가던 그는 외침 소리를 듣고, 창문 너머로 방금 도착한 한 무리의 무장한 사람들을 보았다. 그는 한 죽은 기사를 말에 가로질러 실어 온 아모리를 알아보았고, 안뜰에 모인 사람들의 외침 가운데서 샤를로의 이름이 들려왔다.\n\n이 못난 아들에 대한 샤를의 편애는 그의 약점 가운데 하나였다. 그는 떨면서 안뜰로 내려가 아모리에게 달려가서는, 샤를로를 알아보고 비탄의 외침을 내질렀다. \"보르도의 위옹입니다.\" 배신자 아모리가 말했다. \"제가 미처 그를 지킬 새도 없이 그가 폐하의 아드님을 학살했습니다.\" 샤를마뉴는 이 말에 격분하여 검을 쥐고는, 자기 아들을 죽인 자의 심장에 그것을 꽂으려고 두 형제의 거처로 날아갔다. 나모 공작이 잠시 그의 손을 막아서는 동안, 샤를은 그에게 위옹이 받는 죄목이 무엇인지 말했다. \"그는 이 나라의 중신입니다.\" 나모가 말했다. \"또한 그가 죄가 있다 한들, 그가 여기 폐하의 손안에 있지 않습니까? 그리고 그를 사형에 처할 마땅한 심판관은 저희 중신들이 아닙니까? 그의 피로 폐하의 손을 더럽히지 마소서.\" 황제는 나모 공작의 지혜로 마음을 가라앉히고, 아모리를 자기 앞으로 불렀다. 중신들이 그의 증언을 듣고자 모였고, 배신자는 보르도의 위옹이 샤를로에게 스스로를 지킬 기회를 주지도 않고서, 그것도 자기 상대가 황제의 맏아들임을 알면서도 그 치명적인 일격을 가했노라 고발했다.\n\n클뤼니 수도원장은 아모리의 거짓 고발에 분개하여 나서며 말했다. \"성 베네딕투스를 두고 맹세하건대, 폐하, 저 배신자는 목구멍으로 거짓을 토하고 있습니다. 제 조카가 샤를로를 죽였다면, 그것은 자기 방어를 위함이었고, 자기 동생이 그에게 상처 입는 것을 본 뒤였으며, 또한 자기 적수가 왕자임을 모르는 채였습니다. 비록 제가 교회의 아들이오나.\" 선량한 수도원장이 덧붙였다. \"저는 제가 태생으로 기사임을 잊지 않았습니다. 만약 아모리가 감히 그 거짓을 고집한다면, 저는 제 몸으로써 그 거짓을 그에게 입증해 보일 것을 제안하며, 찬미가와 아침기도를 부르는 것보다 불충한 배신자를 벌하는 것이 더 나은 일을 하는 것이라 여길 것입니다.\"\n\n위옹은 이때까지 아모리의 시커먼 중상에 어이가 없어 잠자코 있었으나, 이제 앞으로 나서서 아모리를 향해 말했다. \"배신자여! 네가 내뱉은 거짓을 감히 무기로써 지켜 내겠느냐?\" 큰 무용을 지닌 기사 아모리는 위옹의 젊음과 가냘픈 체구를 업신여기며 주저 없이 자기 장갑을 내밀었고, 위옹이 그것을 움켜쥐었다. 그러고는 다시 중신들을 향해 그가 말했다. \"청컨대 제게 이 결투를 허락해 주십시오, 이보다 더 정당한 명분은 일찍이 없었으니 말입니다.\" 나모 공작과 나머지 사람들이 이 문제는 하늘의 심판에 맡겨야 한다고 결정하니, 결투가 명해졌고, 샤를마뉴는 마지못해 이에 동의했다. 젊은 공작은 다시 나모 공작에게 맡겨졌으며, 나모는 이튿날 아침 그에게 기사의 영예를 수여하고, 시험을 거친 갑옷과 더불어 흰 방패를 주었다. 클뤼니 수도원장은 조카에게서 그 태생에 걸맞은 기개를 보게 된 것을 기뻐하며 그를 끌어안고 축복을 베풀었으며, 왕의 관리들이 결투를 위한 결투장을 마련하는 동안, 그를 위해 기도하러 서둘러 생제르맹 성당으로 갔다.\n\n싸움은 길고도 끈질겼다. 위옹의 솜씨와 날렵함 덕분에 그는 사나운 아모리가 그에게 겨냥하는 무서운 일격들을 피할 수 있었다. 그러나 위옹은 여러 차례 자기 적수에게서 피를 끌어냈다. 그 효력이 배신자의 쇠해 가는 힘에서 드러나기 시작했고, 마침내 그는 말에서 몸을 던져 무릎을 꿇고 자비를 빌었다. \"저를 살려 주십시오.\" 그가 말했다. \"그러면 모든 것을 자백하겠습니다. 일어서도록 부축하여 저를 샤를마뉴께 데려가 주십시오.\" 용감하고 충성스러운 위옹은 이 말에 검을 왼팔 밑에 끼우고, 엎드린 자를 일으키려 오른손을 내밀었는데, 그자는 그 틈을 타 그의 옆구리를 찔렀다. 위옹의 갑옷이 그 일격을 막아 내어, 그는 가벼운 상처만 입었다. 이 비열한 짓에 격분하여 그는 자기의 완전한 무죄 입증을 위해 아모리의 자백이 얼마나 필요한지도 잊고는, 지체 없이 그에게 치명적인 일격을 가했다.\n\n나모 공작과 다른 중신들이 다가와, 아모리의 시신을 결투장 밖으로 끌어내게 하고, 위옹을 샤를마뉴에게 인도했다. 그러나 황제는 자기 아들의 죽음에 대한 원한과 비탄 외에는 아무것도 들으려 하지 않으며 만족하기를 거부했다. 그리고 위옹이 자기를 고발한 자에게서 고발을 철회시키는 데 성공하지 못했다는 구실로, 그의 영지를 몰수하고 그를 프랑스에서 영영 추방하기로 작정한 듯하였다. 나모 공작과 나머지 사람들이 오랫동안 간청한 뒤에야 비로소 그는 위옹에게 자기가 부과할 조건들 아래 그를 사면하는 데 동의했다.\n\n위옹이 다가가 황제 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 신종 서약을 바치며, 자기가 본의 아니게 그의 아들을 죽인 것에 대해 자비를 빌었다. 샤를마뉴는 위옹의 두 손을 자기 손안에 받아들이려 하지 않고, 다만 왕홀로 그를 건드리며 말했다. \"네 신종 서약을 받아들이고, 내 아들의 죽음을 네게 용서하노라. 그러나 오직 한 가지 조건에서다. 너는 즉시 술탄 가우디소[Gaudisso]의 궁정으로 가라. 그가 식사하러 앉아 있을 때 그 앞에 나아가라. 그의 가장 가까이에 앉아 있는, 네가 발견할 가장 지체 높은 손님의 목을 베어라. 그의 딸인 어여쁜 공주의 입에 세 번 입을 맞추어라. 그리고 술탄에게, 내게 바치는 공물의 표시로 그의 흰 수염 한 줌과 그의 입에서 어금니 넷을 요구하라.\"\n\n이 조건들은 온 회중에게서 웅성거림을 자아냈다. \"뭐라고요!\" 클뤼니 수도원장이 말했다. \"사라센 왕자에게 먼저 세례를 권하지도 않고 그를 살육하라니요?\" \"두 번째 조건은 그리 어렵지 않으나.\" 젊은 중신들이 말했다. \"위옹이 늙은 술탄에게 해야 하는 그 요구는 매우 무례하여, 얻어 내기가 어려울 것입니다.\"\n\n황제가 일단 어떤 일을 작정하고 나면 그 고집이 어떠한지는 익히 알려진 바이다. 위옹의 용기에는 불가능해 보이는 일이 없었다. \"그 조건들을 받아들이겠습니다.\" 그가 늙은 바이에른 공작의 중재를 잠재우며 말했다. \"주군이시여, 이 값을 치르고 저의 사면을 받아들이겠습니다. 저는 폐하의 봉신이자 프랑스의 중신으로서, 폐하의 명을 수행하러 가겠습니다.\"\n\n나모 공작과 클뤼니 수도원장은 샤를마뉴가 내린 선고를 조금도 누그러뜨릴 수 없게 되자, 젊은 공작을 데리고 나왔고, 공작은 곧장 자기의 원정에 나서기로 결심했다. 선량한 수도원장이 그에게서 얻어 낼 수 있었던 것이라곤, 그가 이 위험천만한 과업에 대비하여 먼저 로마로 가서 교황에게 경의를 표하라는 것뿐이었다. 교황은 위옹의 어머니인 알리스 공작부인의 형제였으니, 그에게서 사죄와 축복을 청하라는 것이었다. 위옹은 그러겠노라 약속하고, 곧바로 로마로 가는 길에 올랐다.\n\n보르도의 위옹 (이어서)\n\n위옹은 아펜니노산맥과 이탈리아를 가로질러 로마 근교에 다다랐고, 거기서 갑옷을 벗어 두고 순례자의 옷차림을 했다. 이런 차림으로 그는 교황 앞에 모습을 드러냈으며, 자기 죄를 남김없이 고백하고 나서야 비로소 자기가 그의 조카임을 밝혔다. \"아! 사랑하는 조카여.\" 교황 성하가 외쳤다. \"황제가 이미 부과한 것보다 더 가혹한 속죄를 내가 무엇으로 부과할 수 있겠느냐? 평안히 가거라, 내 아들아.\" 그가 그를 사죄하며 덧붙였다. \"내 지극히 높으신 분께 너를 위해 중재하러 가겠노라.\" 그러고는 조카를 자기 궁으로 이끌어, 로마의 모든 추기경과 제후들에게 그를 자기 누이 알리스 공작부인의 아들인 기엔의 공작으로 소개했다.\n\n위옹은 길을 떠날 때, 한곳에서 사흘 넘게 머물지 않겠노라 맹세를 한 바 있었다. 교황 성하는 이 사흘을 틈타 그에게 그리스도교의 영광을 위한 열의와 지극히 높으신 분의 보호에 대한 믿음을 불어넣었다. 그는 위옹에게 팔레스타인으로 배를 타고 가서 성묘를 참배하고, 거기서 아시아 내륙으로 떠나라고 조언했다.\n\n위옹은 교황 성하의 축복을 한껏 받고, 그 권고에 따라 팔레스타인으로 배를 타고 가서 도착하여, 더없는 경건함으로 성지들을 참배했다. 그러고는 떠나 동방을 향해 길을 잡았다.\n\n그러나 그 고장도 언어도 알지 못한 탓에, 그는 어느 숲에서 길을 잃고, 사흘 동안 사람 하나 보지 못한 채, 나무에서 찾아낸 꿀과 들과실로 연명하며 지냈다. 사흘째 되던 날, 바위투성이 협곡을 지나는 길을 찾던 그는, 누더기 옷을 걸치고 수염과 머리털이 가슴과 어깨를 덮은 한 사내를 보았다. 이 사내는 그를 보고 멈추어 그를 살피더니, 프랑스 기사의 무구와 거동을 알아보았다. 그는 즉시 다가와, 프랑스 남부의 말로 외쳤다. \"하느님께 찬미를! 동포의 얼굴을 보지 못하고 이 황야에서 십오 년을 보낸 끝에, 내가 정녕 내 고국의 기사를 보는 것인가?\"\n\n위옹은 그를 한층 더 기쁘게 해 주려고 투구를 풀고, 미소 띤 얼굴로 그에게 다가갔다. 상대는 처음보다 더 놀라며 그를 바라보았다. \"맙소사!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"이런 닮음이 일찍이 있었던가? 아, 고귀하신 나리.\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"청하옵건대 제게 말씀해 주십시오, 나리는 어느 나라, 어느 혈통에서 오셨습니까?\" \"내 것을 말하기 전에.\" 위옹이 대답했다. \"그대가 먼저 그대 자신의 것을 밝히기를 요구하오. 지금으로서는, 내가 그리스도교도이며 기엔에서 태어났다는 것을 아는 것으로 족하리다.\" \"아! 내 눈과 내 마음이 나를 속이지 않기를 하늘이 허락하시기를.\" 그 낯선 이가 외쳤다. \"제 이름은 셰라스맹[Sherasmin]이며, 보르도의 시장 기르[Guire]의 형제입니다. 저는 제 사랑하고 존귀한 주군 세비누스께서 목숨을 잃으신 그 전투에서 포로로 붙잡혔습니다. 삼 년 동안 저는 견뎌 냈습니다.\n\n노예 생활의 비참함을 맛보았다. 마침내 나는 사슬을 끊고 이 황야로 달아났으며, 그 후로 줄곧 이곳에서 홀로 연명해 왔다. 그대의 용모를 보니 어린 시절부터 그분이 돌아가실 때까지 내가 섬겼던, 내가 사랑하던 군주가 떠오른다.” 위옹은 아무런 대답도 하지 않고 다만 눈물을 글썽이며 노인을 끌어안았다. 그제야 셰라스맹은 자기가 두 팔로 끌어안고 있는 사람이 세비누스 공작의 아들임을 알게 되었다. 그는 위옹을 자기 오두막으로 데려가서, 그의 유일한 양식인 말린 과일과 꿀을 앞에 차려 놓았다.\n\n위옹은 셰라스맹에게 자신의 모험담을 들려주었고, 그 이야기를 듣던 셰라스맹은 눈물을 흘리며 감동했다. 그런 다음 위옹은 자신의 과업을 수행할 방도에 관하여 그와 상의했다. 셰라스맹은 성공이 불가능해 보인다는 사실을 주저 없이 털어놓았다. 그럼에도 불구하고 그는 결코 위옹을 저버리지 않겠노라고 엄숙히 맹세했다. 그가 능통한 사라센어는 그들이 황야를 떠나 사람들 틈에 섞이게 될 때 요긴하게 쓰일 터였다.\n\n그들은 홍해 쪽으로 길을 잡아 아라비아로 들어섰다. 그들이 가야 할 길은 셰라스맹이 온갖 공포로 가득하다고 묘사한 지역을 지나고 있었다. 그곳에는 요정들의 왕 오베론이 살고 있었는데, 그는 무모하게도 그 지역에 발을 들여놓는 기사들을 사로잡아 도깨비로 둔갑시켰다. 길을 다소 멀리 돌아가는 대가를 치르면 이 지역을 피해 갈 수도 있었다. 그러나 그 어떤 위험도 보르도의 위옹을 단념시키지는 못했다. 그리고 이제 다시 기사의 갑옷을 차려입은 용감한 셰라스맹은 마지못해 그와 함께 더 짧은 길의 위험을 나누기로 했다.\n\n그들은 어느 숲으로 들어섰고, 여러 방향으로 오솔길이 갈라지는 한 지점에 다다랐다. 그중 하나는 끝에 화려한 궁전이 서 있는 듯 보였는데, 그 도금한 지붕은 다이아몬드로 뒤덮인 눈부신 풍향계들로 장식되어 있었다. 화려한 전차 한 대가 궁전 문에서 나와, 마치 그들을 중간에서 맞이하려는 듯 위옹과 그의 동행을 향해 달려왔다. 왕자가 전차 안에서 본 것이라고는 다섯 살쯤 되어 보이는 아이 하나뿐이었는데, 매우 아름다웠고 보석으로 반짝이는 옷을 걸치고 있었다. 그 아이를 보자 셰라스맹의 공포는 극에 달했다. 그는 위옹의 말고삐를 붙잡아 말을 돌려세우고는 왕자를 황급히 끌고 가면서, 저 짓궂은 난쟁이와 멈추어 입씨름이라도 하게 되면 우리는 끝장이라고 단언했다. 난쟁이는 비록 아이처럼 보이지만 실은 나이도 많고 간계로 가득 차 있다는 것이었다. 위옹은 아무런 위협도 풍기지 않는 그 아름다운 난쟁이를 시야에서 놓치는 것이 아쉬웠다. 그럼에도 그는 온 힘을 다해 말을 몰아 가는 친구를 뒤따랐다. 이내 폭풍이 숲을 가로질러 으르렁대기 시작했고, 햇빛은 어두워졌으며, 그들은 가까스로 길을 더듬어 나아갔다. 이따금 그들은 어린아이 같은 목소리가 이렇게 말하는 것을 듣는 듯했다. “멈추시오, 위옹 공작. 내 말을 들으시오. 그대가 나를 피해 달아나 보았자 헛수고요!”\n\n셰라스맹은 그저 더 빨리 달아날 뿐이었고, 수도사들과 수녀들이 머무는 어느 수도원의 문에 다다라서야 비로소 멈추었다. 마침 그곳에서는 두 공동체가 한데 모여 종교 행렬을 이루고 있었다. 셰라스맹은 이토록 많은 성직자들과 신성한 깃발들 앞이라면 난쟁이의 악의로부터 안전하다고 느끼고는, 피난처를 청하기 위해 멈추어 서서 위옹도 말에서 내리게 했다. 그러나 바로 그 순간 난쟁이가 그들에게 합류하더니, 자기 목에 걸린 상아 뿔피리를 한 번 불었다. 그러자 곧바로 선량한 셰라스맹은 자기 의지와 무관하게 마치 어린 학생처럼 춤을 추기 시작했고, 죽을 것만 같다고 여기는 어느 늙은 수녀의 손을 붙잡고서 풀밭 위를 경쾌하게 누볐다. 다른 모든 수도사들과 수녀들도 한데 뒤섞여 그를 따라 추니, 일찍이 본 적 없는 가장 기묘한 춤판이 벌어졌다. 위옹만은 춤출 마음이 조금도 들지 않았다. 그러나 다른 이들의 우스꽝스러운 자세와 도약을 보고는 웃다가 거의 숨이 넘어갈 지경이었다.\n\n난쟁이는 위옹에게 다가와, 위옹의 모국어로, 다정한 목소리로 말했다. “기엔 공작이여, 어찌하여 나를 피하시오? 하늘의 이름으로 그대에게 간청하노니, 내게 말을 건네 주시오.” 위옹은 이토록 진지한 방식으로 자신에게 건네는 말을 듣고, 또한 어떤 악령도 감히 자신의 계략을 돕고자 거룩한 이름을 입에 올리지는 못한다는 것을 알기에 이렇게 대답했다. “경이시여, 그대가 누구이든, 나는 그대의 말을 듣고 답할 준비가 되어 있소.” 난쟁이가 말을 이었다. “위옹, 나의 벗이여, 나는 늘 그대의 가문을 사랑해 왔으며, 그대 또한 태어날 때부터 내게 소중한 존재였소. 그대가 내 숲에 들어섰을 때 지니고 있던 그 은혜로운 양심의 상태가, 설령 내가 그대에게 어떤 마법을 부리려 했다 하더라도 그대를 온갖 마법으로부터 지켜 주었소. 만일 이 수도사들과 수녀들이, 그리고 그대의 벗 셰라스맹조차도 그대만큼 순결한 양심을 지녔더라면 내 뿔피리가 그들을 춤추게 하지는 못했을 것이오. 하지만 늘 유혹자의 목소리에 귀를 막을 수 있는 수도사나 수녀가 어디 있겠소. 그리고 셰라스맹도 황야에서 종종 섭리의 권능을 의심했더랬소.”\n\n이 말을 듣고 위옹은 춤추던 이들이 기진맥진해 있는 것을 보았다. 그가 그들에게 자비를 베풀어 달라고 청하니 난쟁이가 이를 받아들였고, 뿔피리의 효력은 즉시 멈추었다. 수녀들은 자기 짝에게서 벗어나 옷매무새를 가다듬고는 서둘러 행렬에서 제자리를 되찾았다. 열기에 지치고 헐떡이며 두 다리로 서 있지도 못하던 셰라스맹은 풀밭에 몸을 던지고는 이렇게 입을 열었다. “내가 말하지 않았소—” 그가 성난 어조로 말을 계속하려 했으나, 난쟁이가 다가와 말했다. “셰라스맹, 어찌하여 그대는 섭리에 대고 불평했소? 어찌하여 나를 나쁘게 여겼소? 그대는 이 가벼운 벌을 받아 마땅하오. 하지만 나는 그대가 선량하고 충직한 사람임을 아오. 머지않아 알게 되겠지만, 나는 그대의 벗이 되어 줄 작정이오.” 이렇게 말하면서 그는 그에게 화려한 술잔 하나를 내밀었다. “이 잔에 십자가 표시를 그으시오.” 그가 말했다. “그러고 나서, 내가 나의 권능을 그대가 섬기는 신으로부터 얻었음을 믿으시오. 그대와 마찬가지로 나 또한 그분의 충실한 종이라오.” 셰라스맹이 그대로 하니, 그 순간 잔은 향긋한 포도주로 가득 찼다. 한 모금을 들이켜자 그의 사지에 기운이 되돌아왔고, 다시 젊어진 듯한 느낌이 들었다. 감사함에 겨워 그가 무릎을 꿇으니, 난쟁이가 그를 일으켜 세워 자기 곁에 앉게 하고는 이렇게 자신의 내력을 들려주기 시작했다.\n\n“율리우스 카이사르가 자기 군대에 합류하고자 바다를 건너던 중 폭풍을 만나 켈레아 섬으로 피신하게 되었는데, 그곳에는 요정 글로리안다가 살고 있었소. 나는 이 명망 높은 두 사람에게서 태어났소. 나는 내 부모 각각에게서 가장 감탄할 만한 것을 물려받았으니, 곧 아버지의 영웅적 자질과 어머니의 아름다움과 마법의 기예라오. 그러나 어머니의 심술궂은 자매 하나가 사소한 모욕에 앙갚음하느라, 내가 겨우 다섯 살이었을 때 자기 지팡이로 나를 건드리고는 더 이상 자라지 못하게 명했소. 그리고 내 어머니는 온 권능을 동원하고도 그 선고를 무효로 만들 수 없었소. 그리하여 나는 나이도 많고 경험도 풍부하건만 겉모습만은 줄곧 어린아이로 머물러 있게 된 것이오. 어머니에게서 물려받은 권능을 나는 때로는 내 자신의 즐거움을 위해 쓰지만, 언제나 정의를 펼치고 미덕에 보답하기 위해 사용한다오. 나는 그대를 도울 능력도 있고 의지도 있소, 기엔 공작이여. 그대가 이곳에 온 사명이 무엇인지를 내가 알고 있기 때문이오. 그대가 내 조언을 따른다면, 나는 그대에게 완벽한 성공과 아내가 될 아름다운 클라리문다를 예언하는 바이오.”\n\n이렇게 말하고 나서 그는 위옹에게 그 귀하고도 요긴한 잔을 내밀었으니, 그 잔은 선량한 사람이 손에 쥐면 저절로 채워지는 능력을 지니고 있었다. 그는 또한 자신의 아름다운 상아 뿔피리도 그에게 주면서 이렇게 말했다. “위옹, 그대가 이것을 부드럽게 불면, 그대도 보았다시피 듣는 이들을 춤추게 만들 것이오. 하지만 그대가 힘껏 불면, 내가 백 리외 떨어진 곳에 있더라도 그 소리를 듣고 그대를 구하러 날아오리니 염려 마시오. 다만 가장 절박한 경우가 아니라면 그런 식으로 불지 않도록 조심하시오.”\n\n오베론은 위옹에게 술탄 가우디소의 나라에 다다르려면 어떤 길을 잡아야 하는지를 일러 주었다. “그대는 그곳에 닿기 전에 큰 위험들을 만나게 될 것이오.” 그가 말했다. 그리고 눈에 눈물을 머금은 채 이렇게 덧붙였다. “게다가 나는 두렵소, 그대가 내 지시를 모든 일에서 다 따르지는 않으리라는 것이. 만약 그렇게 된다면 그대는 많은 재난을 겪게 될 것이오.” 그런 다음 그는 위옹과 셰라스맹을 끌어안고 그들을 떠났다.\n\n위옹과 그의 종자는 사람이 사는 곳에 다다르기까지 여러 날을 황야를 가로질러 여행했으며, 그동안 내내 그 경이로운 잔이 포도주뿐만 아니라 음식까지도 마련해 주어 그들을 지탱해 주었다. 마침내 그들은 어느 큰 도시에 이르렀다. 날이 저물어 가고 있을 무렵 그들은 그 변두리로 들어섰고, 사라센어에 완벽하게 능통한 셰라스맹이 하룻밤 묵을 만한 여관을 수소문했다. 그곳의 주요 인사 가운데 한 사람으로 보이는 어떤 이가, 점잖아 보이는 두 이방인이 이렇게 묻는 것을 보고 앞으로 나서서 자기 저택에 묵어가기를 청했다. 그들이 안으로 들어서니, 주인은 사라센인에게서 보리라고는 미처 생각지 못한 정중함으로 자기 집의 손님 대접을 했다. 그는 그들에게 커피와 셔벗을 내오게 했고, 모든 일이 대단히 예법에 맞게 진행되었다. 그러다가 하인 하나가 서툴게도 뜨거운 커피 잔을 주인의 다리에 엎질렀는데, 그러자 주인이 벌떡 일어나 아주 훌륭한 가스코뉴 사투리로 이렇게 외쳤다. “이런 빌어먹을! 이 멍청한 놈, 모스크 너머로 내던져 마땅한 놈 같으니!”\n\n위옹은 이렇게 자기 고향의 활기와 말씨가 무심결에 터져 나오는 것을 보고 웃음을 참을 수가 없었다. 손님들이 자기 말을 알아들으리라고는 전혀 생각지 못했던 주인은, 위옹이 자기 고향 사투리로 말을 건네자 깜짝 놀랐다. 곧 두 사람 사이에 신뢰가 형성되었으니, 특히 하인들이 물러간 뒤로는 더욱 그러했다. 자신의 정체가 들통났고 또 사라센인 행세를 하던 두 사람이 가론 강 유역 출신이라는 것을 안 주인은 그들을 끌어안고는, 자기가 기독교인이라는 사실을 털어놓았다. 오베론의 충고에서 신중함을 배운 위옹은 주인의 진심을 시험해 보고자, 요정 왕이 자기에게 준 잔을 옷에서 꺼내 빈 채로 주인에게 내밀었다. “훌륭한 잔이군요.” 그가 말했다. “허나 가득 차 있다면 더 좋아할 텐데요.” 그러자 곧바로 잔이 가득 찼다. 깜짝 놀란 주인은 감히 그것을 입에 대지 못했다. “마음껏 드시오, 친애하는 동향 사람이여.” 위옹이 말했다. “정직한 사람의 손에서만 저절로 채워지는 이 잔이 그대의 진실함을 증명해 주었소.” 주인은 더 이상 망설이지 않았고, 잔은 손에서 손으로 자유로이 오갔다. 잔이 오가는 동안 그들의 우애는 더욱 깊어졌고, 저마다 자신의 모험담을 들려주었다. 위옹의 이야기는 주인의 존경심을 갑절로 키웠으니, 그가 위옹에게서 자신의 정당한 군주를 알아보았기 때문이다. 한편 주인의 이야기는 다음과 같았다.\n\n“제 이름은 플로리아크입니다. 놀라움과 슬픔을 안고 들으셔야겠지만, 이 크고 강성한 도시는 세비누스 공작의 아우이자 공작님의 숙부 되시는 분이 다스리고 있습니다. 기엔 공작의 어린 아우가 동무들과 함께 해변에서 어느 해적들에게 납치되었다는 이야기를 분명 들으셨을 겁니다. 그때 저는 그분의 시동(侍童)이었는데, 우리는 그 해적들에게 끌려 바르바리로 갔고, 그곳에서 노예로 팔렸습니다. 바르바리의 군주는 우리를 자신의 군주인 술탄 가우디소에게 해마다 바치는 조공의 일부로 보냈습니다. 시종들의 아첨에 다소 우쭐해져 있던 공작님의 숙부께서는, 자신의 신분을 밝힘으로써 새 주인 앞에서 자기 위세를 높일 수 있으리라 여겼습니다. 진정한 이슬람교도답게 모든 기독교 군주를 증오하던 술탄은 그 순간부터 그분을 사라센 신앙으로 끌어들이려 애썼습니다. 그리고 너무도 잘 성공하고 말았지요. 공작님의 숙부께서는 산톤들의 술책과 술탄이 허락한 쾌락과 방종에 현혹되어, 배교라는 끔찍한 죄를 저지르고 말았습니다. 그분은 자신의 세례를 저버리고 마호메트교를 받아들이셨습니다. 그러자 가우디소는 그분에게 온갖 영예를 안기고, 자기 조카딸 하나와 혼인하게 한 뒤, 이 도시와 인근 지역을 다스리도록 보냈습니다. 공작님의 숙부께서는 소년 시절에 제게 품으셨던 그 우정을 한결같이 간직하고 계셨습니다. 그러나 그분의 온갖 다정함과 노력으로도 제 신앙을 저버리게 하지는 못했습니다. 어쩌면 그분은 그 설득에 맞서는 저를 속으로 존중하셨는지도, 어쩌면 언젠가 제가 당신을 본받도록 만들 수 있으리라 기대하셨는지도 모르겠습니다. 그분은 자신이 다스리는 이 도시로 저를 데려와 신임을 베푸셨고, 제가 그들의 신앙을 위해 보호하는 몇몇 기독교인을 부리도록 허락하고 계십니다.”\n\n“아!” 위옹이 외쳤다. “나를 그 죄 많은 숙부에게 데려가 주시오. 기엔 가문의 한 군주가 어찌 조상들의 신앙을 비겁하게 저버린 것을 부끄러워하지 않을 수 있겠소?”\n\n“아아!” 플로리아크가 대답했다. “저로서는 그분이 공작님의 질책에 부끄러움을 느끼지도, 그분의 혈통에 그토록 걸맞은 조카를 보고 기뻐하지도 않으리라 두렵습니다. 육욕에 짐승처럼 무뎌지고, 자신의 권력을 시샘하여 종종 잔혹하게 휘두르는 그분이라면, 오히려 공작님을 힘으로 붙들어 두거나 죽여 버릴 가능성이 더 큽니다.”\n\n“그렇다 하더라도 좋소.” 용감하고도 열렬한 위옹이 말했다. “이보다 더 나은 대의를 위해서라면 죽어도 여한이 없소. 그리고 나는 그대에게 청하노니, 내일 그분에게 나의 도착과 출생을 알린 다음 나를 그분에게 데려다주시오.” 플로리아크는 여전히 반대했지만, 위옹은 거절을 받아들이려 하지 않았고, 결국 그는 따르겠노라 약속했다.\n\n이튿날 아침 플로리아크는 총독을 알현하여 그의 조카 보르도의 위옹이 당도했음을, 그리고 그 왕자가 바로 그날 그의 궁정에 모습을 드러낼 작정임을 알렸다. 총독은 놀란 나머지 즉시 대답하지는 않았으나, 어떻게 할지 곧바로 마음을 정했다. 그는 플로리아크가 기독교인들과 자기 모국의 왕족들을 너무도 아끼는지라 그들 중 누구를 배신하는 일에는 가담하지 않으리라는 것을 알고 있었다. 그래서 그는 자기 집안의 맏이가 자기 궁정에 당도했다는 소식을 듣고 크게 기뻐하는 척했다. 그는 즉시 플로리아크를 보내 그를 데려오게 했고, 자기 궁전을 잔치 분위기로 꾸미게 했으며, 어전 회의를 소집하고, 몇 가지 은밀한 명령을 내린 뒤, 몸소 조카를 맞이하러 나가서 그를 본명과 직함 그대로 자기 궁정의 모든 고관들에게 소개했다.\n\n위옹은 자기 숙부가 보석 박힌 초승달 장식을 얹은 화려한 터번으로 이마를 두른 것을 보고 분노로 끓어올랐다. 천성이 솔직한 그는 음흉한 총독이 자신에게 아낌없이 퍼붓는 포옹을 고통스럽게 받아들였다. 한편 그의 배교를 질책할 적당한 순간을 찾을 수 있으리라는 희망 때문에, 그는 숙부가 자신에게 베풀게 한 그 영예들을 묵묵히 견뎌 냈다. 총독은 위옹과 단둘이 있게 될 기회를 교묘히 피하면서, 그를 자신의 정원과 궁전 곳곳으로 데리고 다니며 오전 내내를 보냈다. 마침내 만찬 시간이 가까워져 총독이 그의 손을 잡고 식당으로 이끌려 할 때, 위옹은 그 기회를 붙잡아 낮은 목소리로 그에게 말했다. “오 숙부님! 오 세비누스 공작의 아우이신 왕자님이시여! 제가 어떤 처지의 당신을 뵙게 되어 이토록 슬프고 부끄러워해야 한단 말입니까!” 총독은 감동한 척하며 그의 손을 꼭 쥐고는 그의 귀에 속삭였다. “조용히 하거라, 사랑하는 조카야. 내일 아침에 네 말을 충분히 들어 주마.” 이 말에 다소 위안을 얻은 위옹은 총독의 곁자리에 앉았다. 무프티, 몇몇 카디와 아가와 산톤이 나머지 자리를 채웠다. 셰라스맹도 그들과 함께 자리에 앉았다. 그러나 자기 손님들에게서 눈을 떼려 하지 않던 플로리아크는 선 채로 들락날락하면서 궁전 안에서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지를 살폈다. 그는 곧 무장한 사내 여럿이 식당과 이어진 통로와 대기실 사이로 슬그머니 움직이는 것을 알아챘다. 그가 본 것을 손님들에게 알리고자 안으로 들어가려던 참에, 식당 안에서 격렬한 소음과 소란이 들려왔다. 그 까닭은 이러했다.\n\n위옹과 셰라스맹은 첫 번째 요리가 제법 입에 맞아 왕성하게 먹었다. 그러나 그들 고향 사람들은 식사 때 물만 마시는 데 익숙하지 않았던지라, 위옹과 셰라스맹은 그러한 식습관이 그다지 마음에 들지 않아 서로를 마주 보았다. 위옹은 셰라스맹의 안달하는 모습에 소리 내어 웃었으나, 곧 자기도 똑같은 부족함을 느끼게 되자 오베론의 잔을 꺼내어 십자가 표시를 그었다. 잔이 가득 차자 그는 그것을 단숨에 들이켜고 셰라스맹에게 건넸고, 셰라스맹도 그를 따라 했다. 총독과 그의 고관들은 그 혐오스러운 표시를 보고 미간을 찌푸린 채 말없이 경악하여 앉아 있었다. 위옹은 그것을 못 본 척하고, 잔을 다시 가득 채워 숙부에게 건네며 말했다. “자, 우리와 함께하시지요, 친애하는 숙부님. 이건 훌륭한 보르도 포도주로, 숙부님께는 어머니의 젖과도 같은 술이랍니다.” 총독은 자기가 총애하는 술타나들과 더불어 종종 몰래 그리스와 시라즈의 포도주를 마셨지만, 사람들 앞에서는 결코 물 외에는 아무것도 마시지 않았다. 그는 오랫동안 자기 모국의 그 빼어난 포도주를 맛보지 못했던 터라, 지금 자기에게 건네진 것을 마시고 싶은 유혹에 몹시 사로잡혔다. 잔 속의 그것은 어찌나 환하게 빛나던지 황금마저도 무색할 지경이었다. 그가 손을 뻗어 찰랑찰랑 넘치는 잔을 받아들고 입술로 가져가자, 그 순간 잔은 말라붙어 사라져 버렸다. 위옹과 셰라스맹은 과연 가스코뉴 사람들답게 그의 경악하는 모습을 보고 웃음을 터뜨렸다. “기독교의 개들 같으니!” 그가 외쳤다. “감히 내 식탁에서 나를 모욕하는 게냐? 허나 내 곧 앙갚음하리라.” 이렇게 말하면서 그는 잔을 조카의 머리를 향해 던졌는데, 위옹은 그것을 왼손으로 받아 내는 동시에 다른 손으로 총독의 머리에서 초승달 장식이 달린 터번을 낚아채어 바닥에 내동댕이쳤다. 모든 사라센인들이 큰 소리로 외치며 식탁에서 벌떡 일어나 그 모욕에 앙갚음할 채비를 했다. 위옹과 셰라스맹은 방어 태세를 취하고, 자신들을 향해 겨누어진 언월도들을 칼로 맞받았다. 이 순간 식당 문이 열리며 병사들과 무장한 환관들이 무리 지어 쏟아져 들어와 위옹과 셰라스맹에 대한 공격에 가세했다. 왕자와 그의 종자는 넓은 선반인 찬장 위로 몸을 피해, 그곳에서 무리 지어 덤벼드는 자들을 막아 내며 가장 앞장선 자들에게 그 무모함의 대가를 톡톡히 치르게 했다. 그러나 더 많은 병력이 밀려들어 왔고, 보르도 포도주에 고무된 용감한 위옹은 농담의 묘미를 잃을 만큼 화가 난 것은 아니었던지라, 뿔피리를 부드럽게 한 가락 불었다. 그 소리가 들리기가 무섭게 그것은 싸우는 자들의 분노를 가라앉히고 그들을 춤추게 만들었다. 더 이상 공격받지 않게 된 위옹과 셰라스맹은 높은 곳에서 가장 기묘하고도 우스꽝스러운 광경을 내려다보았다. 이윽고 춤추는 소리를 들은 술타나들이 자기들의 호위병이 물러간 것을 알고 식당 안으로 들어와 춤추는 무리에 섞였다. 총애받는 술타나는 두 자 높이로 껑충껑충 뛰어오르는 젊은 산톤을 붙잡았는데, 곧 이 둘의 긴 옷자락이 서로 얽혀 그들을 넘어뜨렸다. 산톤의 수염이 술타나의 목걸이에 걸려 도무지 풀어낼 수가 없었다. 총독은 이러한 무람없는 짓을 결코 달가워하지 않아, 그 산톤에게 다가가려고 두 걸음을 내디뎠으나, 엎어져 있던 데르비시에 발이 걸려 바닥에 큰대자로 나뒹굴고 말았다. 춤은 추는 자들의 기운이 다할 때까지 계속되었고, 그들은 하나둘 쓰러져 속수무책으로 늘어졌다. 마침내 총독은, 자기를 쉬게만 해 준다면 무엇이든 양보하겠노라고 위옹에게 손짓으로 알렸다. 거래가 성립되었다. 총독은 위옹과 셰라스맹이 가던 길을 떠날 수 있도록 허락했고, 심지어 자기 영토를 무사히 통과하여 술탄 가우디소에게 다다를 수 있게 해 줄 반지까지 그들에게 주었다. 두 벗은 이 유리한 국면을 서둘러 이용하여, 플로리아크와 작별하고 여정을 이어 갔다.\n\n보르도의 위옹 (계속)\n\n위옹은 어머니의 궁정에서 많은 미녀들을 보았으나, 그의 마음이 사랑에 흔들린 적은 한 번도 없었다. 그에게는 명예가 연인이었으며, 그것을 좇느라 그는 더 부드러운 근심거리에 마음을 둘 겨를을 결코 갖지 못했다. 그토록 무감하던 마음이 꿈이라는 그토록 실체 없는 것에 처음으로 흔들렸다는 것은 기이한 일이나, 실제로 그러했다.\n\n숙부와의 모험을 겪은 다음 날, 여행자들이 어느 숲을 지나는 동안 밤이 그들을 덮쳤다. 동굴 하나가 그들에게 밤이슬을 피할 안식처를 내주었다. 마법의 잔이 그들의 저녁 식사를 마련해 주었으니, 원하기만 하면 포도주뿐만 아니라 더 든든한 음식까지도 내어 주는 것이 그 잔의 효능이었기 때문이다. 피로가 이내 그들을 깊은 잠에 빠뜨렸다. 나뭇잎들의 속삭임에 잠이 든 채 꽃들의 향기를 들이쉬며, 위옹은 일찍이 본 적 없을 만큼 아름다운 한 여인이 자기 위로 몸을 숙여 자기 입술에 입맞춤을 새기는 꿈을 꾸었다. 그녀를 끌어안으려 두 팔을 뻗는 순간 갑작스러운 한 줄기 바람이 그녀를 쓸어 가 버렸다.\n\n위옹은 애끓는 회한 속에서 잠을 깼다. 잠시 후 지나간 일이 한낱 꿈에 지나지 않았음을 깨닫고 다소 위안을 얻기는 했으나, 그의 당혹스러움과 슬픔은 셰라스맹의 눈을 피하지 못했다. 위옹은 자신의 충직한 종자에게 자기가 시름에 잠긴 까닭을 주저 없이 알렸으나, 그 보답으로 얻은 것이라고는 그런 일로 마음을 어지럽혔다는 셰라스맹의 놀림뿐이었다. 셰라스맹이 요정의 잔에서 한 모금 들이켜 보기를 권하니, 위옹은 그것을 시험해 보고 좋은 효험을 보았다.\n\n이른 새벽에 그들은 다시 길을 떠났다. 그들은 한낮이 될 때까지 여행했으나 서로 별다른 말을 나누지 않았다. 위옹은 자신의 꿈을 곱씹고 있었고, 셰라스맹의 생각은 꽃 만발한 가론 강 기슭에서 보낸 어린 시절로 날아갔다.\n\n그때 갑자기 그들은 고통에 찬 비명에 깜짝 놀랐고, 숲의 한 모퉁이를 돌아서니 어느 기사가 사나운 사자와 사투를 벌이며 궁지에 몰려 있는 곳에 이르렀다. 기사의 말은 죽어 쓰러져 있었고, 공포와 피로로 더 이상 저항할 힘을 완전히 잃은 기사에게는 한순간만 더 지나면 싸움이 끝장날 듯이 보였다. 그가 쓰러지고 사자의 앞발이 그의 위로 치켜올려지는 순간, 위옹의 칼이 내려친 일격이 그 괴물의 분노를 새로운 적에게로 돌려놓았다. 사자가 포효하니 숲이 진동했고, 사자가 덮칠 자세로 몸을 웅크리는 찰나에 위옹은 번개와 같은 속도로 그 옆구리에 칼을 찔러 넣었다. 사자는 죽음의 고통 속에서 벌판을 데굴데굴 굴렀다.\n\n그들은 기사를 땅에서 일으켜 세웠고, 셰라스맹이 서둘러 그에게 요정의 잔에서 한 모금 권했다. 포도주가 잔 가장자리까지 반짝이며 찼고, 그 전사는 그것을 들이켜려 입술을 내밀었으나, 술은 움츠러들어 그의 입술조차 적시지 못했다. 그는 분개의 외침과 함께 잔을 성난 듯 땅바닥에 내동댕이쳤다. 이 일은 어느 한쪽이 상대에게 더 호감을 갖게 하는 데 도움이 되지 못했고, 뒤이은 일은 더욱 고약했다. 위옹이 “기사여, 그대가 구원받은 것을 하느님께 감사하시오”라고 말하자, 그가 이렇게 대꾸했기 때문이다. “차라리 그대 자신과 마호메트께 감사하시오. 그분이 오늘 그대를 이끌어, 다름 아닌 히르카니아의 왕자에게 봉사하도록 하셨으니 말이오.”\n\n이 모독의 말을 듣고 위옹은 칼을 뽑아 그 악당에게 달려들었다. 그러나 조금 전에 그토록 분명한 무용을 목격한 터라 그와 맞붙을 마음이 거의 없던 그자는 달아나기 시작했다. 그는 위옹의 말로 달려가 가뿐히 그 등에 뛰어올라 옆구리에 박차를 가하더니, 쏜살같이 달려 시야에서 사라졌다.\n\n분통 터지는 일이었으나 어쩔 도리가 없었다. 왕자와 셰라스맹은 남은 말 한 마리의 도움을 받아 그럭저럭 여정을 이어 갔다. 마침내 저녁이 들 무렵, 그들은 바로 눈앞에 어느 큰 도시의 첨탑과 망루들을 발견했는데, 그곳이 그 유명한 바그다드 시임을 알아보았다.\n\n그곳 경내에 다다랐을 때 그들은 피로에 거의 기진맥진해 있었고, 어둠 속에서 어느 길로 가야 할지 몰라 막막하던 차에, 한 늙은 여인을 만나 기뻤다. 그 여인은 그들의 물음에 답하여 자기 오두막에서 마련할 수 있는 그런 잠자리를 내주겠노라 했다. 그들은 고맙게 그 제안을 받아들여 낮은 문 안으로 들어섰다. 마음씨 좋은 노부인은 자기 곳간에 있는 가장 좋은 먹을거리를, 즉 우유와 무화과와 복숭아를 부지런히 마련하면서, 매서운 바람이 자기 아몬드나무를 얼려 죽인 것을 몹시 안타까워했다.\n\n위옹 경은 평생 이토록 맛있는 음식을 먹어 본 적이 없다고 여겼다. 노부인은 손님들이 먹는 동안 이야기를 늘어놓았다. 그녀는 그들이 이튿날에 거행될 술탄의 딸 혼례를 기념하는 큰 잔치에 참석하러 온 것이 분명하다고 했다. 그들이 신랑이 누가 되느냐고 묻자 노부인은 “히르카니아의 왕자”라고 대답하고는, 이렇게 덧붙였다. “하지만 우리 공주님은 그를 미워하셔서, 그자와 혼인하느니 차라리 용과 혼인하시겠다고 하신답니다.” “그것을 어찌 아시오?” 위옹이 묻자, 노부인은 자기가 젖을 먹여 기른 양녀인 공주에게서 직접 들었노라고 그에게 알려 주었다. 위옹이 공주가 그토록 싫어하는 까닭을 묻자, 자기 수다가 이토록 큰 관심을 불러일으키는 것이 흐뭇했던 그 여인은 그것이 모두 어떤 꿈 때문이라고 대답했다. “꿈이라고요!” 위옹이 외쳤다. “그렇답니다, 꿈이지요. 공주님은 자기가 암사슴이 되고, 사냥꾼인 그 왕자가 자기를 뒤쫓아 거의 따라잡으려는 찰나에, 황금 수레를 타고 가는 아름다운 난쟁이 하나가 나타나는 꿈을 꾸셨답니다. 그 난쟁이 곁에는 노란 머리에 살결이 흰, 마치 이국에서 온 듯한 젊은이가 있었다지요. 공주님은 자기가 서 있는 곳에 그 수레가 멈추었고, 자기가 본디 모습을 되찾아 막 그 수레에 오르려는 순간, 갑자기 그것이 시야에서 사라지더니 그와 함께 난쟁이도 노란 머리의 젊은이도 자취를 감추는 꿈을 꾸셨답니다. 그러나 그 환영만은 공주님의 마음에서 사라지지 않았고, 그때부터 약혼자인 그 히르카니아 왕자가 공주님의 눈에 가증스럽게 비치게 되었지요. 그렇지만 공주님의 아버지인 술탄은 그런 까닭을 혼인을 막을 만한 충분한 이유로 결코 여기지 않으셨고, 자기 궁정과 인근 여러 나라의 많은 군주들 앞에서 혼례를 엄숙히 치를 시기로 이튿날을 지목하셨답니다. 공주님의 미모와 신랑의 화려함에 대한 명성이 그 군주들을 그 자리로 불러들였던 것이지요.”\n\n이 이야기가 위옹의 가슴속에 온갖 상념의 소용돌이를 불러일으켰으리라고 우리는 짐작할 수 있다. 섭리가 그를 이끌어 그의 행복한 성공으로 가는 길을 닦아 주고 있다는 것이 분명하지 않은가? 그날 밤 잠은 위옹의 눈을 일찍 찾아오지 않았다. 다만 그는 청춘 특유의 낙천적인 기질로, 자신의 기이한 경험이 어떻게 이어질지를 상상하며 공상에 잠겼다.\n\n이튿날, 자신의 운명을 결정짓는 날이라 여길 수밖에 없는 그날, 그는 샤를마뉴의 전갈을 전할 채비를 했다. 갑옷을 차려입고, 상아 뿔피리와 반지로 든든히 무장한 채, 그는 손님들이 연회에 모여 있을 때 가우디소의 궁전에 다다랐다. 그가 문에 다가서자, 모든 진실한 신자들은 들어오라고 외치는 목소리가 들렸다. 그리고 위옹은, 그 용감하고 충실한 위옹은, 조바심이 난 나머지 그 거짓된 명목 아래 안으로 들어서고 말았다. 그러나 그 관문을 통과하기가 무섭게 그는 자신의 비열함이 부끄러워졌고, 회한에 압도당했다. 자신의 잘못을 만회하고자 그는 두 번째 문으로 달려가 문지기에게 외쳤다. “이 불신자 개 같으니, 십자가에서 돌아가신 그분의 이름으로 명하노니, 내게 문을 열어라!” 즉시 백 개나 되는 무기의 끝이 그의 통행을 가로막았다. 그제야 위옹은 자기 숙부인 총독에게서 받은 반지를 처음으로 떠올렸다. 그는 그것을 내보이며 술탄 앞으로 안내해 줄 것을 요구했다. 호위 장교는 그 반지를 알아보고 공손히 절하며 그를 자유로이 들여보내 주었다. 같은 식으로 그는 다른 문들을 지나, 위대한 술탄이 자신의 속국 군주들과 함께 만찬을 들고 있는 호화로운 홀에 이르렀다. 반지를 본 시종장은 위옹을 홀의 상석으로 안내하여, 술탄과 그의 군주들에게 그를 샤를마뉴의 사신으로 소개했다. 왕족들 가까이에 그를 위한 자리가 마련되었다.\n\n위옹이 사자에게서 구해 주었던 바로 그 인물이자 아름다운 클라리문다의 정해진 신랑인 히르카니아의 왕자는 술탄의 오른편에 앉아 있었고, 공주는 그의 왼편에 앉아 있었다. 마침 위옹은 공주의 자리 가까이에 앉게 되었는데, 영접의 예가 끝나기가 무섭게 그는 그녀의 장밋빛 입술에 입맞춤을 새김으로써 샤를마뉴의 명을 서둘러 이행했고, 그런 다음 두 번째로 입맞춤을 했으니, 이번에는 명에 따른 것이 아니라 자신의 호의에 따른 것이었다. 히르카니아의 왕자가 외쳤다. “뻔뻔한 이교도 놈, 네 무례함의 대가를 받아라!” 그러면서 위옹에게 일격을 겨누었는데, 만일 그것이 명중했더라면 그의 사절 임무는 단숨에 끝장났을 것이다. 그러나 그 배은망덕한 자는 겨냥을 놓쳤고, 위옹은 그의 머리를 몸에서 베어 떨어뜨리는 일격으로 그의 모독과 배은망덕을 단번에 응징했다.\n\n이 모든 일이 너무도 갑작스레 벌어진지라 누구도 그것을 막으려 손을 들지 못했다. 그러나 이제 가우디소가 외쳤다. “저 살인자를 잡아라!” 위옹은 사방에서 포위되었으나, 그의 가공할 칼이 몰려드는 신하들의 무리를 막아 냈다. 그러나 그는 새로운 전투원들이 들어오는 것을 보았고, 그토록 많은 자들을 상대로 자기 자리를 지킬 수 있으리라 기대할 수 없었다. 그는 자신의 뿔피리를 떠올리고, 그것을 입술로 가져가 롤랑이 롱스보에서 불었던 것에 거의 맞먹을 만큼 우렁찬 가락을 불었다. 그러나 헛수고였다. 오베론은 그 소리를 들었다. 그러나 비록 한순간이나마 거짓 예언자를 믿는 신자 행세를 했다는, 위옹이 저지른 그 죄가 오베론으로 하여금 그를 도울 힘을 잃게 만들었던 것이다. 위옹은 자기가 버림받았음을 깨닫고 그 까닭을 의식하자 힘과 기력을 잃었고, 붙잡혀 사슬에 묶인 채 지하 감옥에 던져졌다. 그의 목숨은 한동안 살려 두었으나, 그것은 다만 그를 더 고통스러운 죽음을 위해 남겨 두려는 것이었다. 술탄은 그에게 굶주림과 절망의 온갖 고통을 맛보게 한 뒤 산 채로 가죽을 벗길 작정이었다.\n\n그러나 오베론 자신보다도 더 오래되고 더 강력한 마법사 하나가 용감한 위옹에게 관심을 기울였다. 그 마법사는 사랑이었다. 클라리문다 공주는 그 젊은 왕자에게 정해진 운명을 듣고 소스라쳤다. 그녀는 자기 가정교사의 도움을 받아 감옥지기를 자기편으로 끌어들이고는, 사랑하는 이의 사슬을 덜어 주고자 몸소 그를 찾아갔다. 그의 족쇄를 풀어 준 것은 그녀의 손이었고, 그가 그 후로 온전히 그녀에게 바친 목숨을 부지하게 해 줄 양식을 받은 것도 그녀에게서였다. 더없이 다정한 해명을 나눈 뒤 공주는 떠나면서 이튿날 다시 찾아오겠노라 약속했다.\n\n이튿날 그녀는 약속대로 찾아왔고, 다시 양식을 가져왔다. 이러한 방문은 꼬박 한 달 동안 이어졌다. 위옹은 너무도 훌륭한 교회의 아들이었던지라, 그 사랑스러운 공주가 사라센인이라는 사실을 잊지 않았고, 이러한 만남을 이용하여 그녀에게 참된 신앙을 가르쳤다. 사랑하는 이의 입술에서 흘러나올 때, 진리를 믿기란 얼마나 쉬운 일인가! 클라리문다는 머지않아 기독교 교리에 대한 자신의 온전한 믿음을 고백하고 세례받기를 원했다.\n\n한편 술탄은 자신의 죄수가 굶주림의 고통을 어찌 견디는지를 거듭 감옥지기에게 물었고, 죄수가 아직 그로 인해 별로 쇠약해지지 않았다는 것을 알고 놀랐다. 그가 잠시 뒤에 그 물음을 다시 던지자, 감옥지기는 죄수가 갑자기 죽어 동굴에 묻혔노라 대답했다. 술탄은 그 선고의 집행을 더 일찍 명하지 않은 것을 그저 후회할 따름이었다.\n\n이런 일들이 벌어지는 동안, 위옹의 마지막 모험에는 동행하지 않았으나 떠도는 소문으로 그 결과를 알게 된 충직한 셰라스맹은 자기 주인을 구할 무언가를 해 보겠다는 희망을 품고 궁정으로 찾아왔다. 그는 술탄에게 자신을 그의 조카 솔라리오라고 소개했다. 가우디소는 그를 친절히 맞아들였고, 모든 신하들이 그에게 온갖 관심을 쏟았다. 그는 곧 공주가 그 용감하지만 불운한 위옹을 어찌 여기는지를 알아낼 방도를 찾았고, 그녀에게 자기 정체를 밝히자 이내 둘 사이에 신뢰가 형성되었다. 클라리문다는 위옹의 탈출을 돕고 그와 함께 자기 아버지의 궁정을 떠나 샤를마뉴의 궁정으로 가는 데 흔쾌히 동의했다. 그들이 힘을 합쳐 거의 모든 준비를 마치고, 배 한 척을 은밀히 마련하여 도주를 위한 만반의 채비를 갖추었을 때, 뜻밖의 장애가 나타났다. 위옹 자신이 샤를마뉴의 명령을 이행하지 않은 채로 떠나기를 단호히 거부했던 것이다.\n\n셰라스맹은 절망에 빠졌다. 그는 가장 필요한 바로 그 위기의 순간에 도움을 거두어 버린 오베론의 변덕과 잔혹함을 쓰라리게 한탄했다. 그는 왕자에게, 명예를 위해 할 만큼 했으며 불가능한 일을 이루어야 할 의무까지 짊어질 수는 없다는 것을 납득시키고자 온갖 논거를 들어 간곡히 설득했다. 그러나 모두 헛일이었고, 그가 어찌할 바를 모르던 차에, 튀르크의 전제 정치 아래서 그토록 흔히 일어나는 그런 사건들 중 하나가 발생했다. 사자(使者) 하나가 술탄의 궁정에 당도했는데, 그는 자신의 군주이자 아라비아의 칼리프인 강대한 아그라파르의 반지를 지니고, 가우디소의 목에 채울 활시위를 가져왔다. 아무런 이유도 밝혀지지 않았다. 이러한 경우 칼리프의 뜻 외에는 그 어떤 것도 요구된 적이 없었다. 다만 사람들은 그 활시위를 가져온 자가 칼리프에게, 탐욕으로 잘 알려진 가우디소가 막대한 보물을 쌓아 두고도 그것을 자신의 군주와 마땅히 나누지 않았다고 설득하여, 그를 에미르 자리에서 갈아 치울 명령을 얻어 냈으리라고 짐작했다.\n\n가우디소의 시신은 개와 독수리의 먹이로 내던져졌을 터이나, 셰라스맹이 고인의 조카 자격으로 그것을 인수하여 점잖게 매장하도록 허락받았기에 그렇게 되지 않았다. 그는 그렇게 했으되, 다만 샤를마뉴의 명령에 따라 수염과 어금니를 챙긴 다음에야 그리했다.\n\n이제 연인들과 그들의 충직한 종자가 프랑스로 돌아가는 길에는 아무런 장애도 남아 있지 않았다. 그들은 도중에 로마를 거치며 배로 떠났는데, 그곳에서는 교황 성하 친히 자신의 조카인 보르도의 위옹 공작과 클라리문다 공주의 결합을 축복했다.\n\n그 후 머지않아 그들은 프랑스에 당도했고, 그곳에서 위옹은 자신의 전리품들을 샤를마뉴의 발치에 바쳤다. 그리고 황제의 총애를 되찾은 뒤, 자신의 어머니인 공작 부인과, 자신의 영지인 기엔 지방과 자신의 도시인 보르도의 충직한 신민들에게 자기와 자기 신부를 서둘러 선보였다. 그곳에서 그 부부는 환희에 찬 환영을 받았다.\n덴마크인 오지에\n\n# OGIER, THE DANE\n\n덴마크인 오지에는 이교도들에게서 덴마크를 빼앗아 그 나라 최초의 기독교 왕으로 다스린 조프루아의 아들이었다. 오지에가 태어나, 아직 세례받기 전에, 더없이 아름다운 여섯 명의 부인이 갑자기 그 아기의 방에 일제히 나타났다. 그들은 아기를 에워쌌고, 그중 가장 나이 들어 보이는 부인이 아기를 두 팔에 안고 입맞춘 뒤 그의 심장에 손을 얹었다. “너에게 주노라.” 그녀가 말했다. “네 시대의 가장 용맹한 전사가 되기를.” 그녀가 아기를 자기 자매에게 건네자, 그 자매가 말했다. “너에게 주노라, 네 용맹을 펼칠 풍성한 기회들을.” “자매여.” 세 번째 부인이 말했다. “당신은 이 아이에게 위험한 선물을 주었구려. 나는 이 아이에게 결코 패배하지 않을 것을 주노라.” 네 번째 자매는 아기의 두 눈과 입에 손을 얹으며 덧붙였다. “너에게 주노라, 남을 기쁘게 하는 재능을.” 다섯 번째 부인이 말했다. “이 모든 선물이 자칫 배신의 도구가 되지 않도록, 나는 너에게 주노라, 네가 불러일으킨 사랑에 보답할 줄 아는 감수성을.” 그러자 그 무리 가운데 가장 어리고 가장 아름다운 모르가나가 말했다. “매력적인 아이여, 나는 너를 나의 것으로 삼노라. 그리고 나는 너에게, 나의 섬 아발론으로 나를 찾아오기 전에는 결코 죽지 않을 것을 주노라.” 그런 다음 그녀는 아기에게 입맞추고 자매들과 함께 떠났다.\n\n그 후 왕은 아기를 세례반으로 데려가 오지에라는 이름으로 세례받게 했다.\n\n그의 교육에 있어서는, 그를 완벽한 기사의 기준으로 끌어올리고 영웅을 만드는 데 필요한 온갖 기예에 능통하게 만들기 위해 어느 것 하나 소홀히 하지 않았다.\n\n그가 열여섯 살에 채 이르기도 전에, 당대의 모든 군주들에 대해 권력을 확립해 둔 샤를마뉴는, 오지에의 아버지 조프루아가 황제이자 제국의 큰 봉토 중 하나인 덴마크의 종주에게 바쳐야 할 신종(臣從)의 예를 다하지 않았음을 떠올렸다. 그리하여 그는 덴마크 왕에게 이 신종의 예를 요구하고자 사절단을 보냈고, 오만한 어조로 표현된 거절을 받자, 그 요구를 강제하기 위해 군대를 보냈다. 조프루아는 저항에 실패한 끝에 따를 수밖에 없게 되었고, 자신의 진심을 보증하는 담보로 자신의 맏아들 오지에를 샤를에게 인질로 넘겨, 그의 궁정에서 양육받도록 했다. 그는 자기 아버지의 친구인 바이에른의 나모 공작에게 맡겨졌는데, 나모 공작은 그를 자기 친아들처럼 대했다.\n\n오지에는 나날이 더욱 잘생기고 더욱 사랑스럽게 자랐다. 그는 외모에서나 힘에서나 솜씨에서나 자기 동무인 모든 고귀한 젊은이들을 능가했다. 그는 모든 마상 시합에 빠짐없이 참석했고, 손위 기사들에게 정성을 다했으며, 그들을 본받고 싶은 조바심으로 가슴이 타올랐다. 그러나 그의 마음은 이따금 남몰래, 인질이라는 자신의 처지와 아버지에게 잊힌 듯한 자신의 신세에 맞서 들끓곤 했다.\n\n사실 덴마크 왕은 이 무렵 새로운 사랑에 빠져 있었다. 오지에의 어머니가 죽자 그는 두 번째 아내를 맞이하여 기용이라는 아들을 두었다. 새 왕비는 자기 남편에게 절대적인 권력을 휘둘렀으며, 만일 그가 오지에를 다시 본다면 기용보다 그를 더 아끼게 되리라 두려워하여, 그가 샤를마뉴에게 신종의 예를 바치는 일을 미루도록 교묘히 설득했으니, 이제 그 의식을 마지막으로 새로이 치른 이래 사 년이 흘러 버렸다. 이러한 태만에 격분한 샤를마뉴는, 자신이 덴마크 왕에게 보낸 새로운 소환에 대한 답을 받을 때까지 오지에를 더욱 엄중히 가두어 두었다.\n\n조프루아의 대답은 모욕적이고 도전적이었으며, 샤를마뉴의 분노는 극에 달했다. 그는 처음에는 자신의 인질인 오지에에게 분풀이를 하려 했다. 그러나 자기 제자에게 아버지와 같은 마음을 품고 있던 나모 공작의 간청에 따라, 오지에가 그를 자신의 주군으로 섬기겠노라 충성을 맹세하고 자신의 허락 없이는 궁정을 떠나지 않겠노라 약속한다면 그의 목숨을 살려 주기로 동의했다. 오지에는 이 조건을 받아들였고, 전에 누리던 모든 자유를 그대로 누리도록 허락받았다.\n\n황제는 즉시 무기를 들어 자신의 불복하는 봉신을 굴복시키려 했을 것이나, 다른 방향에서 교황 레오의 전갈에 불려 가게 되었으니, 그것은 그의 도움을 청하는 것이었다. 사라센인들이 로마 인근에 상륙하여 야니쿨룸 언덕을 점령하고는, 티베르 강을 건너 기독교 세계의 수도에 불과 칼을 들이댈 채비를 하고 있었던 것이다. 샤를마뉴는 주저 없이 교황의 간청에 응했다. 그는 신속히 군대를 소집하여 알프스를 넘고 이탈리아를 가로질러, 교황이 피신해 있던 견고한 요새 스폴레토에 다다랐다. 레오는 추기경들을 거느리고 그를 맞이하러 나아가, 마치 지난날 자기 아버지가 그러했듯 위급한 순간에 교황청을 지키러 온, 교황청의 저명한 보호자 피핀의 아들에게 바치듯 그에게 경의를 표했다. 샤를마뉴는 스폴레토에 이틀밖에 머물지 않았고, 이교도들이 로마를 손에 넣고 오래 버티지 못할 카피톨리움을 포위하고 있다는 것을 알게 되자, 신속히 그들을 공격하러 진군했다.\n\n군대의 전위는 나모 공작이 지휘했고, 오지에는 그의 종자로서 그를 시중들었다. 그는 아직 무기를 들지는 못했으니, 기사 서임을 받지 못했기 때문이다. 왕의 군기인 오리플람은 알로리라는 이름의 기사가 들었는데, 그는 자신이 그 영예에 걸맞지 않은 자임을 드러냈다. 나모 공작은 이교도들의 강력한 일대가 자기를 공격하려 진격해 오는 것을 보고 그들을 향해 돌격하라는 명을 내렸다. 오지에는 다른 젊은이들과 함께 후방에 남아, 싸움이 허락되지 않은 것을 몹시 안타까워했다. 이내 그는 알로리가 오리플람을 내리고 말머리를 돌려 달아나는 것을 보았다. 오지에는 그자를 젊은이들에게 가리키고는, 곤봉 하나를 집어 들고 알로리에게 달려들어 그를 말에서 쳐 떨어뜨렸다. 그런 다음 동무들과 함께 그의 무장을 벗기고 자신이 그 갑옷을 걸친 뒤, 오리플람을 치켜들고 그 못난 기사의 말에 올라타, 전열의 맨 앞으로 날듯이 달려가 나모 공작에게 합류하여 이교도들을 물리치고, 무너진 그들의 대열을 가로질러 오리플람을 끝까지 운반해 갔다. 공작은 자기가 그다지 높이 평가하지 않던 알로리가 그러는 줄로 알고, 그의 힘과 용맹에 깜짝 놀랐다. 오지에의 젊은 동무들도 그를 본받아 쓰러진 자들의 시신에서 갑옷을 마련해 걸쳤다. 그들은 오지에를 뒤따라 사라센인들의 대열에 죽음을 안겼고, 사라센인들은 혼란에 빠진 채 자기들의 본대로 물러났다.\n\n이제 나모 공작은 퇴각을 명했고, 오지에는 마지못해 그에 따랐는데, 그때 그들은 샤를마뉴가 자기들을 도우러 진격해 오는 것을 알아보았다. 이제 전투는 전면전이 되었고 그 어느 때보다도 무시무시해졌다. 샤를마뉴는 사라센인들의 지휘관 코르수블을 쓰러뜨리고, 그의 머리를 베어 내고자 자신의 그 유명한 칼 주아외즈를 뽑아 들었는데, 그때 두 명의 사라센 기사가 한꺼번에 그에게 덤벼들어, 하나는 그의 말을 죽이고 다른 하나는 황제를 모래밭에 쓰러뜨렸다. 그들은 그의 투구 위에 새겨진 독수리를 보고 그가 누구인지를 알아채고는, 그에게 마지막 일격을 가하려 황급히 말에서 내렸다. 황제의 목숨이 이토록 위태로웠던 적은 없었다. 그러나 그가 쓰러지는 것을 본 오지에가 그를 구하러 날아왔다. 그는 오리플람을 든 채로 거추장스러웠음에도 자기 말을 사라센인 가운데 하나에게 밀어붙여 그를 쓰러뜨렸고, 칼로 다른 하나에게 어찌나 세찬 일격을 가했던지 그자는 정신을 잃고 땅에 나뒹굴었다. 그런 다음 황제가 일어서도록 도와, 쓰러진 기사들 가운데 하나의 말에 그를 다시 태웠다. “용감하고 너그러운 알로리여!” 샤를이 외쳤다. “나는 그대에게 내 명예와 목숨을 빚졌소!” 오지에는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 다만 그는 샤를마뉴를 그를 구하러 날아온 수많은 기사들에게 에워싸인 채로 남겨 두고, 적의 가장 두터운 대열 속으로 뛰어들어, 젊은 전사들의 늠름한 무리를 거느리고 오리플람을 운반해 갔으니, 마침내 마호메트의 군기가 퇴각으로 돌아서고 이교도들이 자기들의 참호 속에서 안전을 구할 때까지 그리했다.\n\n그제야 선량한 튀르팽 대주교는 자신의 투구와 피 묻은 칼을 내려놓고(그는 이교도들을 베어 죽이는 동안만큼은 자신이 명백히 자기 의무의 선상에 있다고 늘 느꼈으므로), 자신의 주교관과 주교장을 들고 〈테 데움〉을 읊조렸다.\n\n이 순간 피와 먼지로 뒤덮인 오지에가 오리플람을 황제의 발치에 내려놓으러 왔다. 그의 뒤로는 키가 작은 전사들의 무리가 따라왔는데, 그들은 자기들에게 너무 무거운 갑옷을 짊어진 채 불편하게 걸었다. 오지에가 샤를마뉴의 발치에 무릎을 꿇자, 황제는 그를 알로리라 부르며 끌어안았고, 한편 튀르팽은 제단 높은 곳에서 온 힘을 다해 그를 축복했다. 그때 밀로네 백작의 아들이자 샤를마뉴의 조카인 젊은 오를란도가 더 이상 이 오해를 견디지 못하고, 자신의 투구를 내던지고 달려가 오지에의 투구 끈을 풀었으며, 다른 젊은이들도 자기 투구를 벗었다. 우리의 저자는 황제와 그의 중신들의 놀라움과 감탄과 애틋함을 이루 다 표현할 수 없노라고 말한다. 샤를은 오지에를 두 팔로 끌어안았고, 그 용감한 젊은이들의 행복한 아버지들은 기쁨의 눈물을 흘리며 자기 아들들을 껴안았다. 선량한 나모 공작이 앞으로 나서자, 샤를마뉴는 오지에를 그의 포옹에 내주었다. “내가 그대에게 얼마나 큰 빚을 졌는지!” 그가 말했다. “선량하고 지혜로운 벗이여, 내 분노를 억눌러 준 그대에게 말이오! 사랑하는 오지에여! 나는 그대에게 내 목숨을 빚졌소! 내 칼이 그대의 어깨를, 그대와 그대의 용감한 젊은 벗들의 어깨를 어서 닿고 싶어 펄쩍 뛰는구려.” 이렇게 말하면서 그는 그 유명한 칼 주아외즈를 뽑아, 오지에와 나머지가 자기 앞에 무릎을 꿇자 그들에게 기사 서임의 의식인 안수례를 베풀었다. 젊은 오를란도와 그의 사촌 올리비에는 황제 앞에서조차, 오지에의 목에 매달려, 옛 기사들에게 그토록 소중하고 신성하던 그 형제의 의를 그와 더불어 맹세하지 않을 수 없었다. 그러나 황제의 아들 샤를로는 오지에가 자신을 뒤덮은 그 영광을 보고, 가장 시커먼 질투와 증오를 품게 되었다.\n\n그날의 나머지와 그 이튿날은 군대의 환희 속에서 보내졌다. 튀르팽은 엄숙한 예배에서 젊은 기사들에게 하늘의 은총이 내리기를 빌고, 그들을 위해 마련된 흰 갑옷을 축복했다. 나모 공작은 그들에게 황금 박차를 선사했고, 샤를 친히 그들의 칼을 채워 주었다. 그러나 오지에를 위한 칼을 살펴보았을 때, 그의 놀라움이란 어떠했겠는가! 사랑이 가득한 요정 모르가나가 그것을 바꿔치기하여 자기가 마련한 칼로 대신 놓아 두는 재주를 부렸던 것이다. 그래서 샤를이 그것을 칼집에서 뽑자 강철 위에 이러한 말이 새겨져 나타났다. “나의 이름은 코르타나, 주아외즈와 뒤랭다나와 같은 강철, 같은 담금질로 벼린 것이라.” 샤를은 어떤 우월한 힘이 오지에의 운명을 지켜보고 있음을 알아챘다. 그는 아버지가 그러하듯 그를 사랑하겠노라 맹세했고, 오지에는 그에게 아들의 헌신을 약속했다. 만일 두 사람이 늘 자신들의 약속을 잊지 않고 있었더라면, 두 사람 모두에게 행복한 일이었으리라.\n\n사라센 군대가 자신들의 경악에서 채 회복되기도 전에, 샤를마뉴를 구할 때 오지에에게 쓰러진 기사들 가운데 하나였던 마우리타니아의 왕 카라위는 그에게 일대일 결투를 신청하기로 결심했다. 그러한 뜻에서 그는 자기 전갈을 직접 전하기로 마음먹고 전령의 복장을 차려입었다. 프랑스 기사들은 그의 풍채에 감탄하며 서로에게, 그는 전령이라기보다는 기사에 더 어울려 보인다고 말했다.\n\n카라위는 먼저 전투의 날에 오리플람을 들었던 그 기사에게 더없이 따뜻한 찬사를 보내는 것으로 말문을 열고는, 마우리타니아의 왕 카라위가 그 기사를 너무도 존경하는 나머지 그에게 결투를 신청한다는 말로 끝맺었다.\n\n오지에가 대답하려 일어서는데, 샤를로가 그의 말을 가로막으며, 마우리타니아 왕의 결투 신청은 포로의 신분으로 살아가는 봉신이 받아들이기에 마땅치 않다고 말했다. 그가 뜻한 것은 오지에로, 그는 그 무렵 자기 아버지를 대신하는 인질로 지내고 있었다. 오지에의 두 눈에서 불꽃이 일었으나, 황제의 면전이라 그는 말을 삼켰고, 샤를마뉴의 다정한 눈길에 마음을 가라앉혔다. 샤를마뉴는 성난 목소리로 말했다. “닥치거라, 샤를로! 내 왕비 베르타의 목숨을 걸고 말하노니, 내 목숨을 구한 자는 네 자신만큼이나 내게 소중하다. 오지에여.” 그가 말을 이었다. “그대는 더 이상 인질이 아니다. 전령이여! 그대의 주인에게 내 대답을 전하라, 내 궁정의 기사는 대등한 조건의 결투를 결코 거절하지 않노라고. 덴마크인 오지에가 그의 신청을 받아들이며, 나 자신이 그의 보증인이 되겠노라.\"\n\n카라위는 깊이 절하며 대답했다. “전하, 저는 전하만큼 위대한 군주의 마음가짐이라면 응당 그 높고 빛나는 명성에 걸맞으리라 확신하고 있었습니다. 전하의 대답을 제 주인께 전하겠습니다. 제가 알기로 그분은 전하를 흠모하시며, 마지못해 전하께 맞서 무기를 드시는 것입니다.” 그런 다음 그는 샤를로가 황제의 아들인 줄을 모른 채 그에게로 돌아서서 말을 이었다. “그리고 기사여, 그대로 말하자면, 만약 전투의 욕망이 그대를 불태운다면, 나는 마우리타니아 왕의 사촌인 사동으로부터, 그에게 결투의 영예를 베풀 어느 프랑스 기사에게든 똑같이 도전을 전하라는 분부를 받았소.”\n\n방금 받은 공개적인 질책에 분노와 굴욕으로 불타오른 샤를로는 주저 없이 자신의 결투 신청을 내밀었다. 카라위는 그것을 오지에의 것과 함께 받아들였고, 결투는 이튿날 양쪽 진영에서 똑같이 떨어져 있는, 숲으로 둘러싸인 풀밭에서 치르기로 합의되었다. 음흉한 샤를로는 가장 시커먼 배신을 꾀했다. 밤사이 그는 그 이름에 걸맞지 않으며 자기처럼 사나운 성품을 지닌 몇몇 기사를 그러모았다. 그는 그들에게 자신의 원한을 갚아 주겠노라 맹세하게 한 뒤, 그들을 검은 갑옷으로 무장시켜 숲속에 매복하도록 보냈으니, 무리 전체를 향해 공격하는 척하되 실은 오지에와 두 사라센인에게 가차 없이 손을 쓰라는 명령과 함께였다.\n\n동틀 무렵 사동과 카라위는 자기들의 창을 들 시동 둘만을 거느린 채 약속된 풀밭으로 향했다. 그리고 샤를로와 오지에도 그리로 갔으되, 서로 다른 길을 통해서였다. 오지에는 침착한 태도로 다가가 두 사라센 기사에게 정중히 인사하고는, 그들과 함께 결투의 조건을 정하는 일에 합류했다.\n\n이런 일이 진행되는 동안, 음흉한 샤를로는 뒤에 남아 자기 부하들에게 진격하라는 신호를 보냈다. 그 비겁한 무리가 숲에서 쏟아져 나와 세 기사를 에워쌌다. 세 사람 모두 그 공격에 똑같이 놀랐으나, 그들 가운데 누구도 다른 둘이 그 배신에 가담했으리라고는 의심하지 않았다. 공격이 자기들 모두에게 똑같이 가해지는 것을 보고, 그들은 힘을 합쳐 그것을 막아 내며, 가장 앞장선 공격자들이 흙먼지를 물도록 만들었다. 코르타나는 그것이 닿는 자마다 치명상을 입혔으나, 카라위의 칼은 그만한 담금질의 것이 아니어서 그의 손안에서 부러져 버렸다. 같은 순간 그의 말이 죽임을 당했고, 카라위는 무기도 없이, 쓰러진 자기 말에 뒤엉킨 채 넘어지고 말았다. 그것을 본 오지에가 그를 지키러 달려가 땅으로 뛰어내려 자신의 방패로 그 군주를 가려 주고, 쓰러진 악당들 가운데 하나의 칼을 그에게 건네주며 자기 말에 오르게 하려 했다. 그 순간 분노로 불타오른 샤를로가 자기 말을 오지에에게 밀어붙여 그를 쓰러뜨렸고, 자신의 창으로 그를 꿰뚫으려 했으나, 그 배신을 본 사동이 그에게 달려들어 그를 밀쳐 내지 않았더라면 그리되었을 것이다. 카라위는 오지에가 건네준 말에 가뿐히 뛰어올라, 다만 이렇게 외칠 틈밖에 없었다. “용감한 오지에여, 나는 더 이상 그대의 적이 아니오. 나는 그대에게 영원한 우정을 맹세하오.” 그때 그 배신을 알아챈 수많은 사라센 기사들이 다가오는 것이 보였고, 샤를로는 자기 부하들과 함께 숲속으로 몸을 피했다.\n\n다가오는 무리는, 오지에의 아버지 조프루아가 왕좌에서 몰아내어 사라센인들에게로 피신하지 않을 수 없게 만들었던 덴마크의 폐위된 왕 단느몽이 지휘하고 있었다. 그는 오지에가 누구인지를 알게 되자, 카라위와 사동의 간곡한 항변과 심지어 위협에도 아랑곳없이 즉각 그를 자신의 포로로 선언하고, 강력한 호위 아래 그를 사라센 진영으로 끌고 갔다. 이곳에서 그는 처음에는 더없이 혹독한 감금을 당했으나, 카라위와 사동이 그의 석방을 어찌나 격렬하게 주장하며 만약 그것이 받아들여지지 않으면 자기들 무기를 자기편에게로 돌리겠다고 위협하고, 단느몽 또한 그 못지않게 열렬히 그 조치에 반대하자, 사라센인들의 지휘관 코르수블은 절충안에 동의하여, 진영을 허락 없이 떠나지 않겠다는 그의 약속을 받고 오지에에게 진영 안에서의 자유를 허락했다.\n\n카라위는 이러한 부분적인 양보에 만족하지 않았다. 그는 이튿날 아침 도시를 떠나 샤를마뉴의 진영으로 나아가, 황제 앞으로 안내해 줄 것을 요구했다. 그의 앞에 이르렀을 때, 그는 말에서 내려 투구를 벗고 칼을 뽑아, 칼날을 잡고서 황제 앞에 무릎을 꿇으며 그것을 샤를마뉴에게 바쳤다.\n\n“저명한 군주시여.” 그가 말했다. “전하 앞에 있는 자를 보십시오, 마우리타니아 왕으로부터 전하의 기사들에게 결투를 신청해 온 그 전령이옵니다. 비겁한 늙은 왕 단느몽이 용감한 오지에를 포로로 사로잡고, 우리 장군을 설득하여 그를 내주기를 거부하게 만들었습니다. 저는 이 옹졸한 처사를 보상하고자, 저 자신, 마우리타니아의 왕 카라위를 전하의 포로로 바치러 왔습니다.”\n\n샤를마뉴는 그의 모든 중신들과 더불어 카라위의 도량에 감탄했다. 그는 그를 일으켜 세워 끌어안고, 그의 칼을 돌려주었다. “군주여.” 그가 말했다. “그대의 존재와, 그대가 내 기사들에게 보여 주는 빛나는 본보기가 오지에를 잃은 것을 내게 위로해 주는구려. 부디 그대가 우리의 거룩한 신앙을 받아들여 우리와 온전히 하나가 되기를 하느님께 비오.\" 나모 공작을 필두로 한 궁정의 모든 영주들이 마우리타니아의 왕에게 경의를 표했다. 다만 샤를로만은 배신자로 알아보일까 두려워 나타나지 않았다. 그러나 카라위의 마음은 너무도 고결하여, 그 아들의 배신을 일러바침으로써 샤를마뉴의 가슴을 찌르지는 않았다.\n\n한편 사라센 군대는 불화로 갈라져 있었다. 카라위의 병사들은 자기들의 왕이 포로로 남겨진 것을 두고 총사령관에게 거세게 항의했다. 그들은 심지어 대의를 저버리고 자기들 무기를 동맹들에게 돌리겠노라 위협하기까지 했다. 샤를마뉴는 포위를 맹렬히 밀어붙였고, 마침내 사라센 지휘관들은 도시를 버리고 자기들의 배로 물러나지 않을 수 없게 되었다. 휴전이 맺어졌고, 오지에는 카라위와 교환되었으며, 두 벗은 영원한 형제의 의를 맹세하며 서로를 끌어안았다. 교황은 자신의 영토에 복위되었고, 이탈리아가 평온해지자 샤를마뉴는 자신의 중신들과 그 종자들을 거느리고 프랑스로 돌아갔다.\n\n덴마크인 오지에 (계속)\n\n샤를마뉴는 덴마크 왕 조프루아가 신종의 예를 거부한 죄과를 잊지 않았고, 이제 복종을 강제할 채비를 했다. 그러나 이 위기의 순간에, 조프루아로부터 온 사절단이 그를 찾아와 자신의 잘못을 인정하며, 자기가 물리칠 수 없는 병력으로 자기 영토를 공격해 온 어느 침략군에 맞서 도움을 청했다. 샤를마뉴의 영혼은 너무도 위대하여 무자비할 수가 없었고, 그는 이 기회를 이용하여 오지에의 마음을 시험해 보았다. 오지에는 자기 아버지가 자신을 아무런 배려나 소식도 없이 십오 년 동안 포로로 내버려 둔 그 박정함을 뼈저리게 느껴 온 터였다. 샤를은 오지에에게, 아버지의 무관심에도 불구하고 그를 돕기 위해 군대를 이끌 마음이 있는지 물었다. 그가 대답했다. “아들은 죽음 외에 그 어떤 까닭으로도 아버지 돕는 일에서 결코 면제될 수 없습니다.” 샤를마뉴는 천 명의 기사로 이루어진 군대를 오지에의 지휘 아래 두었고, 그토록 빼어난 지도자 밑에서 진군하겠노라 자원하는 자들이 그 밖에도 무수히 많았다. 그는 자기 아버지를 구하러 날듯이 달려가 침략자들을 물리치고, 그들을 혼란에 빠뜨려 자기들 배로 내몰았다.\n그런 다음 오지에는 서둘러 수도로 향했으나, 도시에 가까워지자 모든 종이 조종(弔鐘)을 울리는 소리를 들었다. 그는 곧 그 까닭을 알게 되었으니, 그것은 조프루아 왕의 장례였다. 오지에는 자기 아버지를 한 번 더 끌어안고 그의 마지막 명을 듣도록 허락받지 못한 슬픔을 사무치게 느꼈다. 그러나 그는 자기 아버지가 자신을 왕위 계승자로 선포해 두었음을 알게 되었다. 그는 시신이 안치된 교회로 서둘러 가서, 무릎을 꿇고 눈물로 그 생기 없는 형해를 적셨다. 그 순간 천상의 빛이 사방으로 비치고, 한 천사의 목소리가 말했다. “오지에여, 그대의 왕관은 그대의 아우 기용에게 남기고, ‘덴마크인’이라는 칭호 외에는 다른 어떤 칭호도 지니지 말라. 그대의 운명은 영광스러우며, 다른 왕국들이 그대를 위해 마련되어 있노라.” 오지에는 그 신성한 명에 따랐다. 그는 자신의 계모에게 공손히 인사하고, 자기 아우를 끌어안으며, 샤를마뉴의 용맹한 기사들 가운데 헤아려지는 자신의 처지에 만족한다고 말하고는, 덴마크 왕관에 대한 모든 권리를 내려놓았다.\n\n오지에는 영광에 뒤덮인 채 샤를마뉴의 궁정으로 돌아왔고, 황제는 그의 이러한 충정의 증표에 감동하여 그에게 온갖 다정함을 쏟으며 거의 대등한 자처럼 그를 대했다.\n\n우리는 그 후 여러 해 동안의 오지에의 모험들에 대해서는 침묵하고 넘어가는데, 그동안 그가 유년기에 받았던 요정들의 선물은 그가 사랑에서나 전쟁에서나 모든 일에서 성공하게 함으로써 그 위력을 드러냈다. 그는 매혹적인 벨리센과 혼인하여, 어린 볼드윈의 아버지가 되었으니, 그 소년은 아버지의 힘과 용기와 어머니의 아름다움을 고스란히 물려받은 듯이 보였다. 그 아이가 어머니와 떨어질 만한 나이가 되었을 때, 오지에는 그를 궁정으로 데려가 샤를마뉴에게 선보였고, 황제는 그를 끌어안아 자기 휘하에 받아들였다. 나모 공작과 손위 기사들 모두에게는, 마치 오지에 자신의 젊은 시절 모습을 그 아이에게서 보는 듯하였고, 이러한 닮음이 그 소년에게 그들의 호의를 얻어 주었다. 심지어 샤를로조차 처음에는 그를 귀여워하는 듯이 보였으나, 얼마 지나지 않아 그가 알아챈 오지에와의 닮음이 도리어 그의 증오를 불러일으키는 결과를 낳았다.\n\n볼드윈은 샤를로에게 정성을 다했고, 도움이 될 기회를 한 번도 놓치지 않았다. 왕자는 장기 두기를 즐겼고, 장기를 잘 두는 볼드윈은 종종 그와 한판을 벌였다.\n\n어느 날 샤를로는 연달아 두 점을 잃고 약이 올랐다. 그는 볼드윈에게서 한 점을 따면 자신의 손실을 어느 정도 메울 수 있으리라 생각했으나, 볼드윈은 그가 자기가 놓은 덫에 걸려드는 것을 보고, “장군이오”라고 말하면서 가벼운 웃음을 참지 못했다. 샤를로는\n\n분노에 휩싸여 일어선 그는 화려하고 묵직한 체스판을 움켜쥐고 온 힘을 다해 발드윈의 머리에 내리쳤다. 발드윈은 쓰러졌고, 쓰러진 자리에서 숨을 거두었다.\n\n자신이 저지른 범죄에 겁을 먹은 데다 무시무시한 오지에의 복수가 두려웠던 샤를로는 궁전 안쪽에 몸을 숨겼다. 발드윈의 어린 동료 하나가 서둘러 오지에에게 사건을 알렸다. 그는 침실로 달려가 피투성이가 된 제 아이의 몸을 보았고, 샤를로가 그 일격을 가했다는 사실은 그에게 숨길 수 없었다. 분노에 사로잡힌 오지에는 궁전을 뒤지며 샤를로를 찾았다. 샤를로는 다른 어디에서도 안전하지 못하다고 느끼고는 샤를마뉴의 홀로 피신해, 나모 공작과 브르타뉴 공작 살로몬과 함께 식탁에 앉았다. 오지에는 칼을 빼 든 채로 황제의 식탁까지 그를 뒤쫓아 갔다. 술 따르는 시종이 그의 앞길을 막으려 하자, 그는 시종의 손에서 잔을 쳐 떨어뜨리며 그 내용물을 황제의 얼굴에 끼얹었다. 샤를마뉴는 격분하여 일어나 칼을 움켜쥐고 그의 가슴에 찔러 넣으려 했으나, 살로몬과 또 다른 영주가 그 사이에 몸을 던졌고, 한편 예부터 오지에에게 영향력을 지녀 온 나모가 그를 방 밖으로 끌어냈다. 이 폭거의 결과를 내다본 데다 오지에를 가엾이 여기고 마음속으로는 그를 두둔하던 나모는, 궁전의 호위병들이 그를 체포하기 전에 서둘러 그를 데리고 나가 말에 태워 파리를 떠나게 했다.\n\n샤를마뉴는 자신의 기사들을 한데 불러 모아, 온 힘을 다해 오지에를 체포해 마땅한 벌을 받게 하겠다는 맹세를 시켰다. 오지에는 한편으로 황제에게 전갈을 보내, 샤를로가 그 흉악한 범죄에 대해 벌을 받는다는 조건으로 자수하겠노라 제안했다. 황제는 어떤 조건도 들으려 하지 않았고, 대규모 병력을 이끌고 오지에를 추격하러 나섰다. 오지에는 다른 한편으로, 그를 지키겠노라 맹세한 많은 기사들의 열렬한 지지를 받았다. 싸움은 결정적인 성과 없이 오래도록 계속되었다. 오지에는 한 번 이상 황제를 자기 손아귀에 넣었으나, 그 우세를 이용하기를 마다하고 아무 조건 없이 그를 풀어 주었다. 그는 자신을 위한 용서를 간청하면서도, 동시에 샤를로에 대한 처벌을 요구했다. 그러나 샤를마뉴는 그토록 깊이 상처 입은 자를 달래기 위해 자신의 못난 아들을 처벌받게 하기에는, 너무도 눈멀게 그 아들을 아꼈다.\n\n마침내, 자신의 대의를 위해 벗들이 흘린 피에 괴로워하던 오지에는 자신의 작은 군대를 해산하고, 자신을 수행하고자 하는 이들에게서 슬그머니 빠져나와, 자신의 형제인 기용 공작과 다시 합류하고자 길을 떠났다. 가는 도중 아르덴 숲에 이르렀을 때, 오랜 여정에 지친 그는 외딴 골짜기의 싱그러움에 이끌려 잠시 쉬려고 자리에 누웠다. 그는 베이프로르의 안장을 풀고, 투구를 벗어 몸을 가볍게 한 뒤, 풀밭에 누워 방패를 베고 잠들었다.\n\n마침 그 무렵, 이따금 자신이 랭스의 대주교임을 떠올리던 튀르팽이 그 부근에서 자기 관할 아래 있는 교회들을 사목 방문하고 있었다. 그러나 프랑스 기사라는 그의 지위와, 그를 당대의 〈용맹한 기사들〉 가운데 하나로 꼽히게 만든 그의 무인다운 기상은, 그가 데리고 다니는 성직자만큼이나 많은 기사들의 수행 없이는 여행하지 못하게 했다. 그 가운데 한 사람이 목이 말랐는데, 오지에가 쉬고 있던 가장자리의 샘을 알고 있던 그는 그곳으로 말을 몰고 갔다가, 땅바닥에 뻗어 있는 한 기사의 모습에 놀랐다. 그는 서둘러 돌아가 대주교에게 알렸고, 대주교는 샘으로 다가가 오지에를 알아보았다.\n\n선량하고 너그러운 튀르팽의 첫 충동은, 더없이 따뜻한 정을 품고 있던 자신의 벗을 구하는 것이었다. 그러나 역시 오지에를 알아본 그의 부주교들과 기사들은, 황제가 그들 모두에게 강요한 맹세를 대주교에게 상기시켰다. 튀르팽은 자신의 맹세를 저버릴 수 없었다. 그러나 잠든 기사를 결박하도록 부하들에게 허락한 것은 신음 없이는 할 수 없는 일이었다. 대주교의 수행원들은 오지에의 말과 무기를 확보하고, 포로를 수아송에 있는 황제에게로 데려갔다.\n\n황제는 오지에의 본래 잘못에 더해, 그의 완강한 저항으로 인해 너무나 큰 원한을 품게 된 터라, 그를 즉각 처형하라고 명할 작정이었다. 그러나 선량한 나모 공작과 살로몬의 도움을 받은 튀르팽이 그를 위해 너무나 간절히 빌었으므로, 샤를마뉴는 참혹한 죽음만은 면해 주기로 했으나, 대주교의 관리 아래 엄중히 감금하라는 선고를 내렸다. 그러면서 그의 식사를 하루에 빵 사분의 일 덩이와 고기 한 점, 포도주 사분의 일 잔으로 엄격히 제한했다. 이런 식으로 그는 덴마크 왕을 비롯한 오지에의 강력한 벗들의 적의를 사지 않으면서도 그의 목숨을 속히 끝장내기를 바랐다. 그는 튀르팽에게 이 명령을 엄격히 따르겠다는 새로운 맹세를 받아 냈다.\n\n선량한 대주교는 오지에를 너무나 아꼈으므로, 그토록 빈약한 식사에 처해진다면 곧 잃고 말 것이 분명한 그의 목숨을 구할 어떤 방도를 궁리하지 않을 수 없었다. 오지에는 키가 7피트였고, 그에 걸맞은 식욕을 지녔던 것이다. 게다가 튀르팽은 오지에가 언제나 신앙을 전파하고 불신자들을 굴복시키는 데 열심이었던, 진정한 교회의 아들임을 기억했다. 그리하여 그는 자신이 한 맹세의 문구에서 벗어나지 않으면서도, 후대에 〈마음속 단서〉라 불리게 된 것을 이 경우에 실천하는 것이 정당하다고 느꼈다. 그가 생각해 낸 방법은 이러했다.\n\n날마다 아침이면 그는 포로에게 밀가루 두 부셸로 만든 빵 사분의 일 덩이를 공급하게 했고, 거기에 양이나 살진 송아지 사분의 일 마리를 더했으며, 포도주 사십 파인트가 담기는 잔을 만들게 하여 날마다 오지에에게 그 사분의 일을 허락했다.\n\n오지에의 감금은 오래 계속되었다. 샤를마뉴는 그가 여전히 버티고 있다는 소식을 이따금 들을 때마다 놀랐다. 그가 튀르팽에게 좀 더 자세히 물었을 때, 선량한 대주교는 자기 나름의 말 풀이에 의지하여, 자신이 포로에게 허락된 배급량 이상은 결코 주지 않았노라고 단호히 단언하기를 주저하지 않았다.\n\n오지에가 포로로 수아송에 끌려갔을 때, 생파롱의 수도원장이 그 훌륭한 말 베이프로르를 눈여겨보고는, 마침 샤를마뉴에게 청할 다른 청이 없던 차라 황제에게 그 말을 달라고 청하여 자기 수도원으로 데려갔다는 이야기를 우리는 깜빡 하지 못했다. 그는 새로 얻은 것을 시험해 보고 싶어 안달이 났고, 가마를 타고 산기슭에 도착하여 말이 그를 맞이하러 끌려오자, 그 위에 올라타 앞으로 나아갔다. 갑옷을 입은 오지에의 어마어마한 무게를 짊어지는 데 익숙했던 그 말은, 등에 긴 옷자락이 옆구리에 펄럭이는 수도원장의 가벼운 무게밖에 없음을 알아채자, 산의 가파른 비탈을 엄청난 도약으로 뛰어넘으며 달아나, 마침내 주아르 수녀원에 이르렀다. 그곳에서 수도원장은 이미 공포로 반쯤 죽은 채, 수녀원장과 그 수녀들이 보는 앞에서 땅바닥에 내동댕이쳐졌다. 멍들고 모욕당한 수도원장은 가엾은 베이프로르에게 분풀이를 했으니, 분노에 차서 그 말을, 수도원 근처에 짓고 있던 예배당을 위해 돌을 끌어 나르도록 일꾼들에게 넘기라고 명했다. 이렇게 하여, 제대로 먹지도 못하고 혹사당하며 자주 매를 맞으면서, 고귀한 말 베이프로르는 제 주인이 갇혀 있는 동안의 시간을 보냈다.\n\n그 감금은, 황제로 하여금 오지에를 풀어 주지 않을 수 없게 만든 몇 가지 중대한 사건이 없었더라면, 그의 목숨만큼이나 길었을 것이다.\n\n황제는 마우리타니아의 왕 카라우에가 오지에의 석방을 요구하러 오기 위해 군대를 소집하고 있다는 것, 덴마크 왕 기용이 자신의 모든 병력을 동원해 그 사업을 거들 채비를 갖추고 있다는 것, 그리고 무엇보다 나쁘게도, 아라비아의 술탄 브뤼이에가 이끄는 사라센인들이 가스코뉴에 상륙해 보르도를 함락하고 파리를 향해 전속력으로 진군하고 있다는 것을 동시에 알게 되었다.\n\n이제 샤를마뉴는 오지에의 도움이 자신에게 얼마나 절실한지를 느꼈다. 그러나 튀르팽과 나모와 살로몬의 진언에도 불구하고, 그는 오지에가 합당하다고 여길 만한 처벌에 샤를로를 내맡기는 데 차마 동의할 수 없었다. 게다가 그는 오지에가 감금과 오랜 절식으로 쇠약해져 기력도 힘도 없으리라고 믿었다.\n\n이 위기에 그는 브뤼이에로부터 전갈을 받았는데, 자신과 황제 또는 황제의 대전사 사이의 일대일 결투의 결과에 모든 것을 걸자고 제안하면서, 만일 패한다면 자신의 군대를 물리겠노라 약속하는 것이었다. 샤를마뉴는 기꺼이 그 도전을 받아들이고 싶었으나, 그의 고문들이 모두 반대했다. 그리하여 사자에게는 황제가 그 제안을 숙고할 시간을 가진 뒤 이튿날 답을 주겠노라 전해졌다.\n\n세 공작이 샤를마뉴를 설득하여 오지에를 사면하고, 이제 그를 도전하는 강대한 적과 싸우도록 그를 부르러 보내는 데 성공한 것은 바로 이 사이였다. 그러나 오지에를 설득하는 일은 결코 쉽지 않았다. 자신의 오랜 감금에 대한 생각과, 흉포한 샤를로의 일격에 자기 품에서 피 흘리며 죽어 간 아들에 대한 기억은, 그로 하여금 벗들의 간곡한 권유에 오래도록 저항하게 만들었다. 비록 영광이 그를 브뤼이에와 맞서도록 불렀고 그리스도교 세계의 안위가 신앙의 이 오만한 적의 파멸을 요구했으나, 오지에는 마침내 샤를로를 자기 손에 넘겨 자신이 합당하다고 여기는 대로 처분하게 한다는 조건으로만 굴복했다.\n\n조건은 가혹했으나 위험은 절박했다. 그리하여 샤를마뉴는 정의감을 되찾고, 격정적이지만 너그러운 오지에의 영혼에 대한 강한 신뢰를 품고서, 마침내 그 조건에 동의했다.\n\n오지에는 세 기사의 안내를 받아 샤를마뉴 앞으로 인도되었다. 자신의 말에 충실했던 황제는, 높은 영주들이 모여 있는 홀로 샤를로를 데려오게 하여, 그의 손을 묶고 머리를 드러내게 했다. 황제는 오지에가 다가오는 것을 보자 샤를로의 팔을 잡아 오지에에게로 이끌며 이렇게 말했다. \"죄인을 넘기노라. 그대가 합당하다고 여기는 대로 그를 처분하라.\" 오지에는 아무 대답도 하지 않고 샤를로의 머리채를 움켜쥐어 무릎을 꿇리고는, 다른 손으로 그 누구도 막을 수 없는 자신의 칼을 치켜들었다. 제 아들의 머리가 발치에 굴러떨어지는 것을 보리라 예상한 샤를마뉴는 눈을 감고 공포의 비명을 질렀다.\n\n오지에는 그것으로 충분히 했다. 다음 순간 그는 샤를로를 일으켜 세워 그의 결박을 끊고, 그의 입에 입을 맞춘 뒤, 서둘러 황제의 발치에 몸을 던졌다.\n\n다친 데 없는 아들과 자기 발치에 무릎 꿇은 오지에를 본 샤를마뉴의 놀라움과 기쁨은 무엇과도 견줄 수 없었다. 그는 오지에를 품에 끌어안고 눈물로 그를 적시며, 자신의 영주들에게 외쳤다. \"나는 이 순간 오지에가 나보다 더 위대하다는 것을 느끼노라.\" 샤를로로 말하자면, 그의 비천한 영혼은 죽음을 모면했다는 기쁨밖에 느끼지 못했다. 그는 예전 그대로의 모습으로 남아 있었으며, 몇 해 뒤에야 비로소, 앞선 어느 장에서 우리가 본 대로, 보르도의 위옹의 손에 마땅한 벌을 받았다.\n\n———— 덴마크인 오지에 (계속)\n\n샤를마뉴는 어느 정도 평정을 되찾고 나서, 오지에가 좋은 상태로 보이고 두 뺨에 건강한 혈색이 도는 것을 보고 놀랐다. 그는 대주교에게로 몸을 돌렸는데, 대주교는 그의 눈길을 마주하자 얼굴을 붉히지 않을 수 없었다. \"내 왕비 베르타의 머리에 두고 맹세하건대,\" 샤를마뉴가 말했다. \"오지에가 그대의 성에서 좋은 거처를 누렸구려, 대주교여. 허나 그럴수록 나는 그대에게 더욱 빚을 졌소.\" 모든 영주들이 웃으며 튀르팽을 놀려 댔으나, 그는 그저 이렇게 말할 뿐이었다. \"마음껏 웃으십시오, 영주들이여. 허나 저로서는, 오만한 사라센인에게 우리의 원수를 갚아 줄 저 팔이 충만한 기력을 되찾은 것을 보니 결코 유감스럽지 않습니다.\"\n\n샤를마뉴는 즉시 자신의 사자를 보내 도전을 받아들이고, 다음 다음 날을 결투의 날로 정했다. 오만하고 교활한 브뤼이에는 자신의 도전을 받아들인다는 답을 듣고 경멸하듯 비웃었으니, 그는 타고난 힘과 기량 외에 어떤 비책을 믿고 있었던 것이다. 그러나 그는 마호메트에 두고, 제안되고 합의된 대로 조건을 지키겠노라 맹세했다.\n\n이제 오지에는 자신의 갑옷을 요구했고, 그것은 최상의 상태로 그에게 전해졌으니, 선량한 튀르팽이 충실히 간직해 두었던 것이다. 그러나 이 일을 위해 말을 마련하는 일은 쉽지 않았다. 샤를마뉴는 자신의 군마 블랑샤르를 제외하고 마구간의 가장 좋은 말들을 끌어내게 했으나, 모두 헛일이었다. 오지에의 무게에 말들의 등이 땅바닥까지 휘었던 것이다. 이 곤란한 처지에서 대주교는 황제가 베이프로르를 생파롱의 수도원장에게 주었던 것을 기억해 내고, 그 말을 도로 청하러 급히 전령을 보냈다.\n\n수도사들은 모진 주인들이라, 수도원에서 일꾼들을 지휘하던 자는 수도원장의 명령을 너무나도 충실히 따랐던 것이다. 가엾은 베이프로르는 야위고 기운 없이, 그토록 오래 끌어야 했던 천한 짐수레의 마구에 쓸린 채 돌아왔다. 그는 고개를 떨구고 샤를마뉴 앞에서 무겁게 발을 디뎠다. 그러나 오지에의 목소리를 듣자 고개를 쳐들고 울부짖었으며, 두 눈이 번뜩이고, 땅을 차는 그 힘으로 예전의 열정을 드러냈다. 오지에가 그를 어루만지자 그 충직한 군마는 그 손길에 화답하는 듯했고, 오지에가 올라타자 베이프로르는 다시 제 주인을 태우게 된 것을 자랑스러워하며 젊은 시절의 온 기력을 다해 뛰어오르고 껑충거렸다.\n\n이제 더 부족한 것이 없게 되자, 샤를마뉴는 자신의 군대를 거느리고 파리 시에서 출진하여 몽마르트르 언덕을 점령했다. 그곳에서는 전투가 벌어질 생드니 평원이 한눈에 내려다보였다.\n\n정해진 날이 오자 나모 공작과 살로몬은 오지에의 입회인으로서 결투장으로 지정된 곳까지 그를 동행했고, 브뤼이에는 두 명의 저명한 에미르와 함께 반대편에 모습을 드러냈다.\n\n브뤼이에는 의기양양하여, 나아가면서 베이프로르의 행색을 두고 자기 벗들과 농담을 주고받았다. \"저것이 아틀라스 산의 골짜기에서 풀을 뜯던 그 어떤 말보다 뛰어난 마르슈발레와 맞붙으려 내세운 말이란 말인가?\" 그러나 이제 결투자들은 서로 만나 인사를 나눈 뒤, 전력으로 마주 달려오기 위해 따로 떨어졌다. 베이프로르는 평원 위를 날듯이 달려, 절반도 더 지난 지점에서 상대를 맞았다. 두 결투자의 창은 충돌과 함께 산산이 부서졌고, 브뤼이에는 거의 같은 순간에 오지에의 칼이 제 머리 위에서 번뜩이는 것을 보고 놀랐다. 그는 작은 방패로 그것을 막아 내고 오지에의 투구에 일격을 가했는데, 오지에는 더 정확히 겨누었거나 칼날의 단련이 더 잘 받쳐 준 또 다른 일격으로 되갚았으니, 그것은 브뤼이에의 투구 일부를 잘라 냈고 그와 함께 그의 귀와 뺨 일부를 베어 냈다. 피를 본 오지에는 곧바로 일격을 되풀이하지 않았고, 브뤼이에는 그 틈을 타 한쪽으로 말을 몰아 달아났다. 그는 달리면서 안장 앞고리에 걸려 있던 황금 항아리를 집어 그 내용물로 상처 입은 부위를 적셨다. 피는 즉시 멎었고, 귀와 살은 완전히 온전하게 되살아났으며, 덴마크인은 자신의 적수가 예전과 다름없이 멀쩡하게 결투장으로 돌아오는 것을 보고 놀랐다.\n\n브뤼이에는 그의 놀라움을 비웃었다. \"알아 두어라,\" 그가 말했다. \"나는 아리마태아의 요셉이, 너희가 숭배하는 십자가에 못 박힌 자의 몸에 발랐던 그 귀한 향유를 지니고 있노라. 내가 팔 하나를 잃는다 해도 이것 몇 방울이면 되살릴 수 있다. 나와 겨루는 것은 너에게 부질없는 일이다. 항복하라, 그러면 네가 건장한 사내로 보이니, 내 갤리선 한 척의 으뜸가는 노잡이로 삼아 주겠다.\"\n\n분노로 끓어오르면서도 오지에는 하늘의 도움을 간구하기를 잊지 않았다. \"오 주여!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"당신 이름의 적이, 그 모든 효험을 당신의 신성한 피에 빚지고 있는 것의 강력한 도움으로 이득을 보지 못하게 하소서.\" 이 말과 함께 그는 그 어느 때보다 더 큰 기력으로 다시 브뤼이에를 공격했다. 둘 다 무서운 일격을 가하며 참혹한 상처를 입혔으나, 오지에의 상처에서는 피가 흘렀고 브뤼이에는 향유를 발라 자신의 피를 멎게 했다. 불공평한 싸움에 절망한 오지에는 코르타나를 두 손으로 움켜쥐고 적에게 일격을 가했는데, 그것은 그의 작은 방패를 쪼개고 그와 함께 그의 팔을 잘라 냈다. 그러나 브뤼이에도 동시에 오지에에게 일격을 날렸으니, 그것은 오지에를 빗나가 베이프로르의 머리를 쳤고, 그 충직한 말은 쓰러지면서 제 주인을 함께 끌어내렸다. 브뤼이에는 땅으로 뛰어내려 자신의 팔을 주워 향유를 바를 시간이 있었다. 그러고는 오지에가 발을 채 추스르기도 전에, 그의 파멸을 마무리하려고 칼을 치켜든 채 앞으로 달려들었다.\n\n몽마르트르 언덕 위에서 용맹한 오지에가 이런 처지에 놓인 것을 본 샤를마뉴는 신음하며 섭리를 원망할 지경이었다. 그러나 선량한 튀르팽은 모세와 같은 믿음으로 두 팔을 치켜들어, 그 그리스도교 전사에게 하늘의 은총을 끌어내렸다.\n\n오지에는 재빨리 몸을 빼내어 그토록 맹렬하게 브뤼이에를 몰아붙였고, 마침내 귀한 향유가 안장 앞고리에 걸려 있는 그의 말로부터 그를 멀리 떼어 놓았다. 그리고 곧 샤를마뉴는 이제 완전히 우세를 점한 오지에가 적을 무릎 꿇리고 투구를 벗겨 낸 뒤, 한 번의 칼질로 그의 몸에서 머리를 베어 내는 것을 보았다.\n\n승리한 뒤 오지에는 마르슈발레를 붙잡아 그 등에 뛰어올라, 그 귀한 작은 병을 차지하게 되었으니, 거기서 몇 방울만으로도 그의 상처가 아물고 기력이 회복되었다. 브뤼이에의 포로였다가 이제 풀려난 프랑스 기사들이 자신들의 구출에 감사하려고 오지에 주위로 몰려들었다.\n\n샤를마뉴와 그의 귀족들은 일대일 결투에서 주의가 풀리자마자, 자신들이 있던 높은 자리에서 적진의 심상치 않은 동요를 알아챘다. 그들은 처음에는 그것을 적장의 죽음 탓으로 돌렸으나, 곧 무기 부딪치는 소리와 결투자들의 함성, 그리고 새로이 진군해 오는 깃발들이, 브뤼이에의 군대가 새로운 적의 공격을 받고 있다는 사실을 그들에게 드러냈다.\n\n황제의 짐작은 옳았다. 그것은 용맹한 마우리타니아의 카라우에로, 자신의 무기 형제인 오지에를 해방시키려 결심하고 군대와 함께 프랑스에 당도한 것이었다. 도착하여 정세가 바뀐 것을 알게 된 그는, 지휘관을 잃어 경악에 빠진 와중의 브뤼이에 군대를 공격함으로써, 황제에게 두드러진 도움을 베푸는 데 주저하지 않았다.\n\n오지에는 자기 벗의 깃발을 알아보고 마르슈발레에 뛰어올라 그의 공격을 돕고자 날아갔다. 샤를마뉴도 자신의 군대를 이끌고 뒤따랐다. 사라센 무리는 완강한 싸움 끝에 무조건 항복하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n오지에와 카라우에의 만남은, 그토록 정 깊은 두 벗이자 출중한 두 기사에게서 기대할 법한 그러한 것이었다. 샤를마뉴는 그들을 맞으러 나가 끌어안고는, 마우리타니아 왕을 자신의 오른편에, 오지에를 왼편에 두고 의기양양하게 파리로 돌아왔다. 그곳에서 황후 베르타와 그 궁정의 귀부인들이 그들에게 월계관을 씌워 주었고, 황제의 시종이자 비서인 현명하고 용감한 에긴하르트는 이 모든 위대한 사건들을 자신의 역사서에 기록했다.\n\n며칠 뒤 덴마크 왕 기용이 정예 기사들을 거느리고 프랑스에 당도하여, 샤를마뉴에게 사절을 보내 자신이 적으로서가 아니라, 당대 최고의 기사이자 그리스도교 세계의 수장인 그에게 경의를 표하러 왔노라 전했다. 샤를마뉴는 사절을 진심으로 맞이하고, 말에 올라 덴마크 왕을 맞으러 앞으로 나아갔다.\n\n이 위대한 군주들은 샤를의 궁정에 모여 함께 회의를 열었고, 연로하고 현명한 영주들도 그 자리에 부름을 받았다.\n\n덴마크와 마우리타니아의 연합군이 바다를 건너 사라센인들의 나라로 전쟁을 가져가기로, 그리고 천 명의 프랑스 기사가 덴마크인 오지에의 깃발 아래 늘어서기로 결정되었다. 오지에는 비록 왕은 아니었으나 다른 두 사람과 동등한 지위를 갖게 되었다.\n\n이 전쟁에서 오지에와 그의 동맹들이 수행한 빛나는 행적을 모두 기록할 지면은 우리에게 없다. 그들이 프톨레마이스와 유다의 사라센인들을 굴복시키고, 그 지역들을 하나의 왕국으로 세워 오지에의 머리에 왕관을 씌웠다는 것만 말해 두면 족할 것이다. 그러고서 기용과 카라우에는 각자의 영토로 돌아가고자 그를 떠났다. 오지에는 덴마크 기용의 아들 발터를 양자로 삼아 자신의 왕국을 이을 후계자로 정했다. 그는 그의 교육을 감독했고, 그 젊은 왕자가 자신의 보살핌에 값할 만하게 자라나는 것을 지켜보았다. 그러나 오지에는 자신의 지위에 따르는 온갖 영예에도 불구하고, 자식과 같은 존경과 애정을 품었던 샤를마뉴의 궁정과 나모 공작, 브르타뉴의 살로몬을 자주 그리워했다. 마침내 발터가 통치의 무게를 감당할 만큼 자란 것을 보고, 오지에는 은밀히 배 한 척을 마련하게 하여, 종자 한 사람만을 거느린 채 밤에 자신의 궁전을 떠나 프랑스로 돌아가고자 배에 올랐다.\n\n순풍에 밀린 배는 새처럼 빠르게 바다를 갈랐다. 그러나 갑자기 배는 항로에서 벗어나 더는 키를 따르지 않고, 바다로 뻗어 나온 검은 곶을 향해 빠르게 내달았다. 그것은 자석의 산이었고, 거리가 줄어들수록 그 끌어당기는 힘이 커져, 배는 마침내 화살 같은 빠르기로 그것을 향해 날아가, 그 바위투성이 기슭에 부딪혀 산산조각 났다. 오지에만이 홀로 목숨을 건져, 난파선 조각 하나에 의지해 기슭에 닿았다.\n\n오지에는 사람이 사는 흔적을 찾으며 그 땅 안쪽으로 나아갔으나 아무것도 찾지 못했다. 갑자기 그는 반짝이는 비늘로 덮인 두 마리의 괴물 같은 짐승과 마주쳤는데, 불을 내뿜는 말 한 마리가 그것들을 따르고 있었다. 오지에는 칼을 빼 들고 몸을 지킬 채비를 했다. 그러나 무시무시해 보이던 그 괴물들은 그를 공격하려는 시도를 하지 않았고, 파피용이라는 그 말은 무릎을 꿇고 오지에에게 제 등에 올라타기를 청하는 듯했다. 오지에는 그 모험을 끝까지 보는 것을 주저하지 않았다. 그가 파피용에 올라타자 말은 빠르게 달려, 곧 아름다운 풍경을 에워싸 감추고 있던 바위와 벼랑을 넘어갔다. 말은 계속 달려 어느 웅장한 궁전에 이르렀고, 오지에에게 그것을 감상할 틈도 주지 않은 채, 열주들로 장식된 큰 안뜰을 가로질러 한 정원으로 들어갔다. 그곳에서 말은 도금양 가로수 길을 헤치고 나아가다가 걸음을 멈추고, 샘가의 에나멜 같은 풀밭에 무릎을 꿇었다. 오지에는 말에서 내려 시냇가를 따라 몇 걸음 옮겼으나, 곧 한 젊은 미인을 만나 멈춰 서고 말았으니, 그녀는 화가들이 〈우미의 여신들〉을 그리는 그대로의 모습이었고, 거의 그들만큼이나 가볍게 차려입고 있었다. 바로 그 순간, 놀랍게도 그의 갑옷이 저절로 벗겨져 떨어졌다. 젊은 미인은 다정한 태도로 다가와 그의 머리에 꽃의 관을 씌웠다. 그 순간 덴마크의 영웅은 기억을 잃었다. 그의 전투들, 그의 영광, 샤를마뉴와 그의 궁정, 그 모두가 그의 마음에서 사라졌다. 그는 오직 모르가나만을 보았고, 영원히 그녀의 발치에서 한숨짓는 것 외에는 아무것도 바라지 않았다.\n\n오지에가 백 년 넘게 누린 그 모든 환락의 이야기는 줄여 두기로 한다. 시간은 흘러갔으나 그 흐름의 흔적은 남기지 않았다. 모르가나의 젊은 매력은 쇠하지 않았고, 오지에는 덜 은혜로운 필멸자들이 결코 받지 않을 수 없는, 나이 들어 감을 알리는 그런 경고를 전혀 받지 않았다. 어느 날 모르가나가 장난기 어린 순간에 그의 머리에서 관을 낚아채 버린 사고만 아니었더라면, 이 더없이 행복한 상태가 얼마나 오래 이어졌을지 알 수 없는 일이다. 그 순간 오지에는 기억을 되찾았고, 만족을 잃었다. 샤를마뉴와 자신의 친족과 벗들에 대한 기억이, 모르가나와 함께 보내는 시간들을 서글프게 했다. 그 요정은 슬픔에 잠겨 자기 연인의 달라진 표정을 지켜보았다. 마침내 그녀는 그에게서, 적어도 한동안은 샤를의 궁정을 다시 찾고 싶다는 고백을 끌어냈다. 그녀는 마지못해 동의했고, 자신의 손으로 그에게 갑옷을 다시 입혀 주었다. 파피용이 끌려 나왔고, 오지에는 그 위에 올라, 눈물짓는 모르가나에게 다정한 작별을 고하고는, 모르가나의 궁전을 바다의 경계와 갈라놓는 바위 띠를 빠른 속도로 가로질렀다.\n\n그가 올 때 그를 맞이했던 바다의 도깨비들이 기슭에서 그를 기다리고 있었다. 그 가운데 하나는 오지에를 제 등에 태우고, 다른 하나는 파피용 밑으로 들어가, 넓은 지느러미를 펼쳐 짧은 시간에 아발론 섬과 프랑스를 가르는 드넓은 공간을 가로질렀다. 그들은 오지에를 랑그도크 해안에 내려놓고는 바닷속으로 뛰어들어 사라졌다.\n\n오지에는 다시 파피용에 올라탔고, 말은 바다를 건넜던 것만큼이나 빠르게 그를 태우고 왕국을 가로질렀다. 그는 파리의 성벽 아래에 당도했는데, 생주느비에브의 높은 탑들이 그의 눈에 띄지 않았더라면 그곳을 거의 알아보지 못했을 것이다. 그는 곧장 샤를마뉴의 궁전으로 향했는데, 그곳은 완전히 다시 지어진 듯 보였다. 그의 놀라움은 극에 달했고, 호위병들과 시종들이 그의 물음에 답하는 말을 알아듣기 어렵다는 것을 알게 되자, 그리고 그들이 그가 건넨 말을 서로에게 설명하려 애쓰며 미소 짓는 것을 보자, 그의 놀라움은 한층 더 커졌다.\n이윽고 궁정으로 가던 몇몇 영주들의 주의가 그 광경에 끌렸고, 그들의 지위를 나타내는 표장을 알아본 오지에는 그들에게 말을 건네, 나모 공작과 살로몬이 아직도 황제의 궁정에 머무르고 있는지 물었다. 이 물음에 영주들은 어리둥절하여 서로를 바라보았다. 그리고 가장 나이 든 사람 하나가 나머지 사람들에게 말했다. \"이 기사가 내 종조부 덴마크인 오지에의 초상화를 어찌나 닮았는지.\" \"아! 친애하는 조카여, 내가 바로 덴마크인 오지에라네.\" 그가 말했다. 그리고 그는 모르가나와 함께 머무는 동안 시간이 얼마나 흘렀는지 자신이 거의 알지 못하리라고 그녀가 말해 주었던 것을 떠올렸다.\n\n그 어느 때보다 놀란 영주들은, 그를 당시 다스리던 군주, 위대한 위그 카페에게 안내하기로 했다.\n\n용맹한 오지에는 주저 없이 궁전에 들어섰다. 그러나 왕실의 홀에 이르렀을 때, 영주들이 그에게 프랑스 왕에게 경의를 표하라고 일렀는데, 그는 자신이 그토록 자주 샤를마뉴를 보았던 그 옥좌에 키가 작고 머리가 큰 한 사내가 앉아 있는 것을 보고 놀랐다. 그렇지만 그의 풍모는 고귀하고 무인다웠다. 샤를마뉴는 당대에 가장 키가 크고 가장 잘생긴 군주였던 것이다.\n\n오지에는 자신의 모험을 꾸밈없이 소박하게 이야기했다. 위그 카페는 좀처럼 그를 믿으려 하지 않았다. 그러나 오지에가 너무나 많은 증거와 정황을 떠올렸으므로, 마침내 그는 그 늙은 전사가 저 유명한 덴마크인 오지에임을 인정하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n왕은 오지에에게 그의 오랜 부재 동안 일어난 사건들을 알려 주었다. 샤를마뉴의 혈통이 끊겼다는 것, 새로운 왕조가 시작되었다는 것, 왕국의 오랜 적인 사라센인들이 여전히 골칫거리라는 것, 그리고 바로 그때에도 그 악당들의 군대가 샤르트르 시를 포위하고 있어, 자신이 며칠 안에 그곳을 구원하러 갈 참이라는 것이었다. 언제나 영광에 대한 사랑으로 불타던 오지에는 자신의 팔의 봉사를 자청했고, 그 명망 높은 군주는 이를 정중히 받아들이며 그를 왕비에게 데려갔다. 귀부인들의 새로운 장신구와 머리 장식을 보고 오지에의 놀라움은 곱절이 되었다. 그래도 그들이 이마 위에 높이 쌓아 올린 아름다운 머리카락과, 그토록 우아하게 흔들리는 사이사이 엮어 넣은 깃털은 그를 매료시키는 고귀한 분위기를 그들에게 부여했다. 그의 감탄은 더욱 커졌으니, 옛 황후 베르타 대신, 위엄 있는 풍모에 그 나이 또래의 우아함을, 그리고 모든 이의 마음을 사로잡기에 알맞은 솔직하고 매력적인 태도를 겸비한 젊은 왕비를 보았던 것이다. 오지에는 그 젊은 왕비에게 너무나 깊은 존경을 담아 인사했으므로, 많은 신하들이 그를 외국인으로, 혹은 적어도 파리에서 멀리 떨어진 곳에서 자라나 그들이 〈옛 궁정〉이라 부르던 시절의 예법을 간직한 어느 귀족으로 여겼다.\n\n자신의 남편으로부터, 그가 소개하는 이가 고대의 연대기에서 그 인상적인 공적을 자주 읽었던 저 유명한 덴마크인 오지에라는 것을 전해 들은 왕비의 놀라움은 극에 달했으니, 그의 품위 있는 태도와, 그의 얼굴에 깃든 생기와 심지어 젊음마저 알아채자 그 놀라움은 더욱 커졌다. 이 왕비는 너무나 총명하여 섣불리 믿지 않았다. 오직 증거만이 그녀의 동의를 끌어낼 수 있었다. 그래서 그녀는 그에게 샤를마뉴의 옛 궁정에 관해 많은 질문을 던졌고, 모든 의심을 거두게 할 만큼 유익하고 적절한 답을 들었다. 우리 자신의 역사의 모든 세부가 완벽하게 정확하고 믿을 만한 것은, 바로 그때 오지에가 자신의 공적에 관한 통속적인 이야기들에 가할 수 있었던 그 교정들 덕분이다.\n\n위그 카페 왕은 바로 그날 저녁 샤르트르 주민들로부터, 자신들이 포위군에게 심하게 시달리고 있다는 소식을 전하는 전령들을 받고, 오지에와 함께 서둘러 그들을 구원하러 가기로 결심했다.\n\n오지에는 그토록 자주 다른 일들을 처리했던 것처럼 신속하게 이 일을 마무리했다. 사라센인들이 감히 싸움을 걸어오자, 그는 그들의 가장 두터운 대열을 뚫고 오리플람므를 들고 나아갔다. 콧구멍에서 불을 내뿜는 파피용이 그들을 혼란에 빠뜨렸고, 그의 무적의 팔에 휘둘린 코르타나가 곧 그들의 패주를 마무리했다.\n\n사라센인들을 물리치고 승리한 왕은 덴마크의 영웅을 파리로 데리고 돌아왔고, 그곳에서 프랑스의 구원자는 자신의 용맹에 합당한 영예를 받았다. 오지에는 왕과 왕비의 총애에 붙들려 한동안 궁정에 머물렀다. 그러나 머지않아 그는 왕의 죽음을 목격하는 고통을 겪었다. 바로 그때, 왕비에게서 알아본 온갖 완덕에 감명받은 그는, 자신의 손을 청하는 다정한 경의를 표하지 않을 수 없었다. 왕비는 어쩌면 그것을 받아들였을지도 모른다. 그녀는 그 제안을 숙고하고자 자신의 대영주들의 회의까지 소집했던 것이다. 그런데 회의가 열리기로 한 전날, 오지에가 그녀의 발치에 무릎 꿇고 있던 순간, 그녀는 보이지 않는 손이 그의 이마에 씌워 놓은 황금의 관을 알아보았고, 순식간에 구름이 오지에를 감싸더니, 그는 그녀의 시야에서 영영 사라졌다. 자신이 본 것에 질투가 일었던 요정 모르가나가, 이제 자신의 힘을 되찾아 그를 데려가 아발론 섬에서 자신과 함께 살게 한 것이었다. 그곳에서 그는 브리튼의 위대한 아서 왕과 함께 여전히 살고 있으며, 그의 명망 높은 벗이 옛 통치를 다시 잇고자 돌아올 때면 틀림없이 그와 함께 돌아와 그의 승리를 나눌 것이다.\n각주\n\n-----\n\n[1] 워즈워스.\n\n[2] 괄호 안에 든 이름은 그리스식이며, 나머지는 로마식 또는 라틴식 이름이다.\n\n[3] 이 불일치는 로마인의 사투르누스를 그리스 신 크로노스(시간)와 동일시한 데서 비롯되는데, 크로노스는 시작이 있는 모든 것에 끝을 가져오므로 자기 자식을 삼킨다고 말할 수 있다.\n\n[4] 이 악기의 기원에서, \"껍데기\"라는 말이 흔히 \"리라\"와 같은 뜻으로, 또 비유적으로 음악과 시를 가리키는 데 쓰인다. 그래서 그레이는 「시의 진보」라는 송시에서 이렇게 말한다.\n\n\"오 기꺼운 영혼의 군주여, 감미롭고 장엄하게 숨 쉬는 가락의 어버이여, 매혹하는 껍데기여! 시무룩한 시름과 광란하는 정념이 그대의 부드러운 다스림을 듣는도다.\"\n\n[5] 파우나, 곧 보나 데아라 불린 여신도 있었다.\n\n[6] 순결과 정결의 여신. 그녀는 지상을 떠난 뒤 별들 사이에 놓여 처녀자리—처녀—라는 별자리가 되었다. 테미스(정의)가 아스트라이아의 어머니였다. 그녀는 한 쌍의 저울을 높이 치켜들고, 거기에 맞서는 양편의 주장을 다는 모습으로 그려진다.\n\n옛 시인들이 즐겨 품었던 생각은, 이 여신들이 언젠가 돌아와 황금시대를 되돌려 놓으리라는 것이었다. 어느 그리스도교 찬가, 곧 포프의 「메시아」에서도 이 생각이 나타난다.\n\n\"온갖 죄악이 그치고 옛 기만이 무너지리니, 돌아온 정의가 그 저울을 높이 치켜들고, 평화가 세상에 그 올리브 가지를 펼치며, 흰옷 입은 순결이 하늘에서 내려오리라.\"\n\n밀턴의 「예수 강탄 찬가」 14연과 15연도 보라.\n\n[7] 관용 표현을 보라.\n\n[8] 관용 표현을 보라.\n\n[9] 관용 표현을 보라.\n\n[10] 관용 표현을 보라.\n\n[11] 241면을 보라.\n\n[12] 여기서 묘사되는 것은 분명 우리가 아는 오늘날의 히아신스는 아니다. 아마도 붓꽃이나 참제비고깔, 혹은 팬지의 일종일 것이다.\n\n[13] 해바라기.\n\n[14] 무지개에 대한 이 정확한 묘사는 오비디우스의 글을 문자 그대로 옮긴 것이다.\n\n[15] 제임스 매킨토시 경은 이에 대해 이렇게 말한다. \"그대는 중국인이라 한들 나비의 화사한 빛깔을, 다음 시구—'그 벨벳 같은 솜털' 운운—보다 더 세밀하고 정확하게 그려 낼 수 있으리라 생각하는가?\"—「생애」, 제2권, 246면.\n\n[16] 관용 표현을 보라.\n\n[17] 헤카테는 신비로운 여신으로, 때로는 디아나와, 때로는 프로세르피나와 동일시되었다. 디아나가 밤의 달빛 어린 찬란함을 나타낸다면, 헤카테는 그 어둠과 공포를 나타낸다. 그녀는 마법과 주술의 여신으로, 밤이면 땅 위를 떠돈다고 믿어졌으며, 오직 개들만이 그녀를 알아보고 짖어 그 다가옴을 알렸다고 한다.\n\n[18] 알키데스, 헤라클레스의 다른 이름.\n\n[19] 이탈리아에서 가장 빼어난 조각품 가운데 하나인 바티칸의 「누운 아리아드네」가 바로 이 장면을 묘사한다. 그 사본 하나를 보스턴 아테나이움이 소장하여 현재 보스턴 미술관에 기탁해 두고 있다.\n\n[20] 프로테우스.\n\n[21] 아킬레우스가 불사신의 몸이었다는 이야기는 호메로스에게서는 찾아볼 수 없으며, 그의 서술과도 맞지 않는다. 아킬레우스가 정녕 상처 입지 않는 몸이었다면, 어찌 천상의 갑옷이 필요했겠는가?\n\n[22] 테니슨은 오이노네를 소재로 짧은 시를 지었으나, 이야기 가운데 가장 시적인 대목—상처 입은 파리스가 돌아오는 장면, 그녀의 매정함과 그 뒤의 뉘우침—은 빼놓았다.\n\n[23] 관용 표현을 보라.\n\n[24] 피로스가 외친 말, \"지금 이 순간이 요구하는 것은 그런 도움도 그런 수호자도 아니다\"는 하나의 격언이 되었다. 관용 표현을 보라.\n\n[25] 테니슨은 「연꽃 먹는 사람들」에서, 연꽃 열매가 자아낸다는 그 몽롱하고 나른한 느낌을 매혹적으로 그려 냈다.\n\n\"아, 얼마나 달콤할까, 흘러내리는 시내 소리를 들으며 반쯤 감은 눈으로 언제까지나 어렴풋한 잠에 빠져드는 듯한 그 느낌이! 저 높은 곳의 몰약나무를 떠나지 않는 호박빛 노을처럼 꿈꾸고 또 꿈꾸는 것이, 서로 속삭이는 말을 듣는 것이, 나날이 로토스 열매를 먹으며 바닷가에 이는 잔물결과 크림빛으로 굽이치는 부드러운 물보라를 바라보는 것이, 우리의 마음과 넋을 온전히 그 부드러운 우수의 기운에 내맡기는 것이, 어릴 적 낯익은 얼굴들을—이제는 풀 무덤에 덮이고, 놋쇠 항아리에 담긴 두 줌의 흰 재가 되어 버린 그 얼굴들을—추억 속에서 되새기고 되살려 사는 것이!\"\n\n[26] 관용 표현을 보라.\n\n[27] 관용 표현을 보라.\n\n[28] 관용 표현을 보라.\n\n[29] 관용 표현을 보라.\n\n[30] 관용 표현을 보라.\n\n[31] 관용 표현을 보라.\n\n[32] 관용 표현을 보라.\n\n[33] 시인은 여기서 말발굽 소리를 그 음률로 흉내 냈다고 여겨지는 유명한 시행 하나를 끼워 넣는다. 이렇게 옮길 수 있을 것이다. \"그리하여 준마들의 발굽이 네 굽으로 땅을 짓밟으며 울렸다.\"—관용 표현을 보라.\n\n[34] 관용 표현을 보라.\n\n[35] 이집트에는 비가 내리지 않으므로 풀은 \"소나기에 젖지 않으며\", 그 땅의 기름짐은 오로지 나일 강의 범람에 기댄다. 마지막 행에서 말하는 방주는, 이집트 신전 벽에 아직 남아 있는 그림들이 보여 주듯, 사제들이 종교 행렬에서 메고 다니던 것이었다. 이는 아마도 오시리스를 안치했던 궤를 나타낸 것으로 보인다.\n\n[36] 카우퍼의 번역은 덜 우아하지만 원문에 더 충실하다.\n\n\"그가 말을 마치자, 검은 눈썹 아래로 그 끄덕임이 확언인 양 내려졌다. 그 군주의 영원한 머리 둘레로 신령한 곱슬머리가 흔들렸고, 거대한 산이 흔들렸다.\"\n\n이 대목이 또 다른 유명한 번역본에서는 어떻게 나타나는지 독자들도 흥미롭게 볼 만하다. 이는 포프의 번역과 같은 시기에 티켈이라는 이름으로 나온 것으로, 많은 이들이 애디슨의 것으로 여겼던 탓에 애디슨과 포프 사이에 다툼이 벌어지는 빌미가 되었다.\n\n\"이렇게 말하며, 왕다운 그 이마를 아버지 신은 숙였다. 크고 검은 곱슬머리가 뒤로 위엄 있게 드리워져, 그 신의 준엄한 이마를 짙게 그늘지웠고, 올림포스는 그 전능한 끄덕임에 떨었다.\"\n\n[37] 그레이의 송가 「운명의 자매들」은 이 미신에 바탕을 둔 것이다.\n\n[38] 롱펠로의 시집에는 발두르의 죽음을 소재로 한 「테그네르의 드라파」라는 시가 실려 있다.\n\n[39] \"고귀한 브리튼인들은 대담한 트로이아인들에게서 태어났으니, 트로이노반트는 옛 트로이아의 식은 잿더미로 세워졌도다.\"—스펜서, 제3권, 제9곡, 38연.\n\n[40] \"베어 아래 묻히다.\" 관이나 상여처럼 그를 에워싼 무언가의 아래에 묻혔다는 뜻.\n\n[41] 글래스턴베리 수도원은 아리마태아 사람 요셉이 세웠다고 전해지며, 예로부터 아발로니아의 섬 또는 골짜기라 불리던 곳에 자리한다.\n\n테니슨은 「예술의 궁전」에서 요정 여왕이 아서를 구해 냈다는 전설을 이렇게 인유한다.\n\n\"혹은 신화 속 우서의 깊이 상처 입은 아들이, 완만히 펼쳐진 어느 푸른 들에 누워, 아발론 골짜기에서 졸고 있었으니, 눈물짓는 왕비들이 그를 지켜보았노라.\"\n\n[42] 귀네비어는 아서 왕비의 이름으로, 제니에브르 또는 제뉴라라고도 표기되며, 기사도 전승에 밝은 이라면 누구나 낯익은 이름이다. 단테가 프란체스카 다 리미니의 아름다운 삽화에서 인유하는 것이 바로 그녀와, 그녀의 진실한 기사 랜슬롯 경의 사연이다.\n\n[43] 이 이름은 프랑스어 로망스에서는 \"쾨\"라고 표기되며, 이는 \"수석 요리사\"를 뜻한다. 이는 이 말이 이름이 아니라 하나의 직함이었음을 암시하는 듯하나, 이 직함을 지녔던 인물이 다른 이름으로 불린 적은 없다. 그는 아서 궁정의 영웅들 가운데 유일하다고는 못해도 으뜸가는 익살스러운 인물이다. 그는 집사 겸 시종장으로, 요리장의 소임도 함께 맡았다. 로망스에서 그의 성격은 대체로 용맹과 익살이 뒤섞여 있어, 언제나 싸울 채비가 되어 있으나 대개는 지는 쪽이다. 또한 그는 빈정거리고 험한 말을 잘하여 그로 인해 곤란을 겪는 일이 잦다. 그런데도 아서는 그에게 애정을 품은 듯, 흔히 그의 조언을 따르는데, 그 조언은 대개 그르다.\n\n[44] 로망스 작가들은 아서 왕에게 여러 도시를 배정한다. 주된 것은 카얼레온, 카멜롯, 칼라일이다.\n\n카얼레온이라는 이름은 로마 지배 시절 한 군단의 주둔지였던 데서 유래한다. 라틴어 저술가들은 이를 \"우르브스 레기오눔\", 곧 \"군단들의 도시\"라 불렀다. 앞 낱말은 웨일스어로 도시를 뜻하는 \"카에르\"로 옮겨지고, 뒷 낱말은 \"레온\"으로 줄어들었다. 어스크 강은 오늘날 지리에도 그 이름이 남아 있으며, 그 강가에 카얼레온이라는 마을 혹은 도시가 있으나, 아서 궁정의 무대가 된 곳은 카디프로 여겨진다. 체스터 역시 웨일스어로는 카얼레온이라는 이름을 지니는데, \"성채\"를 뜻하는 라틴어 \"카스트라\"에서 나온 체스터라는 이름 자체가 군사 본부를 가리키는 명칭이기 때문이다.\n\n카멜롯은 윈체스터로 여겨진다.\n\n샬롯은 길퍼드다.\n\n헤이모의 항구는 사우샘프턴이다.\n\n칼라일은 스코틀랜드 접경 가까이에 지금도 그 이름을 지니고 있는 도시다. 그러나 이 이름은 이와 마찬가지로 군사 주둔지였던 다른 곳들에도 이따금 쓰인다.\n\n[45] \"사미트\", 일종의 견직물.\n\n[46] \"나스\"는 \"낫 워즈\"의 준말로, 현대어로는 \"있지 않았다\"이다. \"모첼 소로\"는 \"큰 슬픔\"이고, \"모로\"는 \"아침\"이다.\n\n[47] \"뉴팽글드\"—새것을 좋아하는.\n\n[48] \"루니스\", 매를 붙들어 매는 끈.\n\n[49] 어떤 악기.\n\n[50] \"신의는 기사도의 참된 초석이었다. 기사가 한번 말을 맹세하면 (아무리 경솔하게 한 것이라도) 어떤 대가를 치르더라도 지켜야 했다. 그리하여 기사가 탄원자에게 베푸는 '무조건의 은혜'는 신성한 의무가 되었다. 로망스에는 기사가 경솔하게 무조건의 은혜를 허락했다가 자신에게 지극히 불리한 무언가를 하거나 겪을 수밖에 없었던 사례가 무수히 나온다. 그러나 이렇듯 무조건의 약속을 고지식하게 지킨 기이한 사례는 로망스 속에만 있는 것이 아니다. 그 시대의 역사에도 그에 못지않게 난처하고 터무니없는 실제 사건들이 남아 있다.\"—스콧, 「트리스트람 경」 주.\n\n[51] \"페어스\"는 동료, \"슈스\"는 노고, \"리어스\"는 학식.\n\n[52] \"애글릿\", 끈 끝의 장식 쇠붙이나 술.\n\n[53] \"황금 위에 무늬 박고\" 운운, 황금빛 점이나 고리 무늬로 꾸미고 줄무늬로 고르게 가른 것. \"페일드\"(문장학 용어)는 줄무늬가 있다는 뜻.\n\n[54] 이 로망스가 쓰였던 시절, 궁정에는 흔히 어릿광대가 딸려 있었다. 어릿광대는 난쟁이 다음으로 귀하게 여겨지던 노리개였다. 그는 노란 두건을 쓴 흰옷을 입고, 손에는 방울 혹은 \"요술 방망이\"를 들고 다녔다. 어릿광대라 불렸어도, 그의 말은 흔히 무게 있게 받아들여져 마치 신탁 같은 뜻을 품은 것인 양 기억되곤 했다.\n\n[55] 이 낱말은 \"어부\"와 \"죄인\"을 함께 뜻한다.\n\n[56] 방 안에 푸른 골풀을 까는 관습은 카얼레온 어스크의 궁정만의 것이 결코 아니었다. 우리 조상들은 골풀을 몹시 아꼈으며, 이는 안락함뿐 아니라 사치의 필수 요소였던 듯하다. 바닥에 골풀을 까는 관습은 중세 잉글랜드와 프랑스에서 널리 행해졌음이 잘 알려져 있다.\n\n[57] 원문에서는 \"코드월\"이라는 낱말이 쓰이는데, 그 쓰임으로 보아 이는 분명 프랑스어의 \"코르두안\" 또는 \"코르도반\" 가죽을 가리키는 것으로, 그 가죽이 만들어지던 코르도바에서 이름을 얻었다. 영어 낱말 \"코드웨이너\"(제화공)도 여기서 나왔다.\n\n[58] 초기 브리튼 역사의 모든 인물 가운데, 이 이야기의 주인공만큼 흥미롭고 두드러진 자리를 차지하는 이도 드물다. 그의 아버지 유리엔은 지금의 컴벌랜드와 그 인접 지역을 아우르는 레게드의 군주였다. 그의 무용과 그가 누린 명망은 음유시인의 노래에 자주 오르내리는 주제였으며, 탈리에신이 지은 몇몇 활기찬 송가의 소재가 되기도 했다. 세 짝 시 가운데도 그에 관한 것이 하나 있으니, 이렇게 옮길 수 있다.\n\n\"아서 궁정에는 전투의 세 기사가 있었으니, 콘월의 백작 카드르, 호수의 랜슬롯 뒤 라크, 그리고 유리엔의 아들 오와인이라. 이들의 특징은 창이든 화살이든 칼이든 두려워 물러서지 않는다는 것이었다. 그리고 아서는 그들의 얼굴을 본 그날 전투에서 조금도 부끄러움을 느낀 적이 없었다. 그리하여 그들은 전투의 기사라 불렸다.\"\n\n[59] 6세기 이전까지 유럽인들이 쓰던 비단은 모두 세레스족—오늘날 부하라 사람들의 조상—을 통해 들여온 것이었으며, 그로부터 라틴어 이름 \"세리카\"가 나왔다. 551년에 이르러 두 수도사가 누에를 콘스탄티노폴리스로 들여왔으나, 비단 생산은 1130년까지 그리스 제국 안에만 머물러 있었다. 그해 시칠리아 왕 로제르가 십자군 원정에서 돌아오는 길에 아테네와 코린토스에서 장인들을 데려와 팔레르모에 자리 잡게 하였고, 그로부터 그 기술이 차츰 이탈리아 전역으로 퍼져 나갔다. 이 무렵 알려진 비단 직물의 종류로는 벨벳, 새틴(이는 \"사미트\"라 불렸다), 태피터(\"샌덜\" 혹은 \"센덜\"이라 불렸다)가 있었으며, 이들 모두 때로 금실과 은실로 수를 놓기도 했다.\n\n[60] 탈리에신의 시들 사이에 함께 실려 전하는 옛 시 한 편이 있으니, 「오와인 압 유리엔의 애가」라 불리며, 매우 아름답고 활기찬 대목을 여럿 담고 있다. 그 첫머리는 이러하다.\n\n\"오와인 압 유리엔의 넋이여, 그 주께서 그 절박함을 헤아려 주시기를! 레게드의 우두머리를 푸른 잔디가 덮는구나.\"\n\n이 애가가 이어지는 동안, 이 우두머리가 색슨족 원수들을 끊임없이 괴롭힌 그 전쟁을 노래하며 음유시인은 이렇게 외친다.\n\n\"잉글랜드가 눈 뜬 채로 잠들 수 있으랴!\"\n\n[61] 영주와 손님들이 식탁에 앉아 있는 동안 말을 탄 채 홀 안으로 들어오는 관습은 옛 로망스와 역사의 숱한 대목으로 예증할 수 있다. 그러나 초서의 아름답고도 끝나지 않은 이야기 「캄부스칸」에서 한 대목을 인용하는 것으로 충분하리라.\n\n\"셋째 코스가 끝난 뒤 이런 일이 일어났으니, 왕이 그 위엄 속에 앉아, 식탁 앞에서 흥겹게 악사들의 연주를 듣고 있을 때, 별안간 홀 문으로 청동 말을 탄 기사 하나가 들어왔는데, 손에는 큰 유리 거울을 들고, 엄지에는 금가락지를 끼고, 옆구리에는 뽑아 든 칼을 차고 있었다. 그는 높은 식탁까지 말을 몰아 올라갔다. 홀 안에서는 아무도 입을 열지 못하였으니, 이 기사가 놀라워, 늙은이나 젊은이나 모두 숨죽여 그를 지켜보았기 때문이다.\"\n\n[62] 옛 저술가들이 언제나 유리창을 두고 감탄해 마지않던 말투만 보아도, 다른 증거가 없다 한들, 그것이 우리 조상들의 집에서 얼마나 드문 사치품이었는지 넉넉히 알 수 있다. 유리창은 처음에는 교회 건축에만 오랫동안 국한되어 쓰였다. 유리는 14세기 이전에는 민가 건축에 쓰이지 않았다고 한다.\n\n[63] 로망스의 넓고도 다채로운 영역을 통틀어, 이니드—백작 이니울의 딸—만큼 소박하고도 진실한 인물을 찾기는 어려울 것이다. 그녀의 아름다움과 고귀한 품행이 두드러져, 그녀가 겪어야 했던 온갖 고초를 견뎌 낸 그 인내심과, 마침내 그녀가 넉넉히 받아 마땅한 승리를 이루어 낸 그 한결같음과 애정 가운데 어느 쪽에 더 감탄해야 할지 알기 어렵다.\n\n이니드의 성격은 이야기 내내 훌륭하게 지켜진다. 이는 그리셀다의 성격보다 덜 과장되어 더 자연스러운 만큼, 어쩌면 더 뭉클하기도 하다. 다만 그리셀다의 이야기에는 초서가 매력을 불어넣어 그 사연의 있을 법하지 않음을 잊게 만든다.\n\n[64] \"위대한 자들의 섬\"은 웨일스인들이 브리튼에 붙인 여러 이름 가운데 하나다.\n\n[65] 카락타쿠스.\n\n[66] 카시벨라우누스.\n\n[67] 런던 백탑 아래 묻힌 머리에 관한 이야기를 다룬 세 짝 시가 있으니, 이는 침략을 막는 부적으로 묻힌 것이었다. 아서는 자신의 힘만으로 이 섬을 지키기를 바라, 자랑스럽게도 그 머리를 도로 파냈다고 한다.\n\n[68] 크레이딜라드는 다름 아닌 셰익스피어의 코델리아이며, 그녀의 아버지인 리어 왕은 웨일스 전거에서는 흘리르 또는 흘루드라 불리며 뒤섞여 쓰인다. 옛 연대기들은 하나같이 그녀가 늙은 아버지에게 바친 헌신의 이야기를 전하지만, 그녀가 최후의 심판 날까지 아버지 곁에 머물도록 정해져 있는 한편, 요정들의 왕 귄 압 누드와 그위티르 압 그레이디올이 해마다 오월 초하루에 그녀를 두고 싸운다는 사실은 어느 연대기도 알아채지 못한 듯하다. 그리고 그때 승리를 거두는 자가 그녀를 신부로 얻게 된다.\n\n[69] 웨일스인들에게는 반쪽 사람을 소재로 한 우화가 있으니, 이는 버릇의 힘을 보여 주는 것으로 여겨진다. 이 우화에서 아서는 한 요정을 만나는데, 처음에는 작고 흐릿한 모습으로 나타나지만 가까이 다가올수록 몸집이 커져 마침내 반쪽 사람의 형상을 하고서 왕에게 씨름을 걸어 온다. 아서는 그 약함을 얕보고 겨루어 봐야 얻을 명예가 없다고 여겨 응하지 않고 대결을 미루는데, 마침내 그 반쪽 사람(버릇)이 너무도 강해져 그를 이기려면 온 힘을 다해야 할 지경에 이른다.\n\n[70] 로망스 작가들은 저마다의 여주인공들이 지닌 금빛 머리와 섬세한 살결을 몹시 흡족한 듯 즐겨 그린다. 이런 취향은 오랫동안 이어져, 머리칼을 밝게 만드는 것이 교육의 한 목표이기도 했다. 가발이 유행하게 되었을 때조차 모두 아마빛이었다. 갈리아인과 그들을 정복한 게르만족의 머리칼 빛깔도 그러하였다. 그들의 눈이 에스파냐와 이탈리아 이웃들의 거무스름한 미인들에 익숙해지기까지는 몇 세기가 걸렸다.\n\n[71] 「아이반호」, 제1권, 제13장.\n\n[72] 셰익스피어가 「뜻대로 하세요」에서 이와 비슷한 사건을 바로 이 출전에서 빌려 왔음이 분명하다. 그 극에 나오는 인물들의 이름—올랜도, 올리버, 롤런드—도 바로 그 점을 보여 준다.\n\n[73] 그들의 이야기는 「아서 왕과 그의 기사들」에서 보라.\n\n[74] 이는 시칠리아와 칼라브리아 사이 메시나 해협에서 실제로 나타난다고 하는 어떤 현상을 시적으로 묘사한 것이다. 이는 파타 모르가나, 곧 신기루라 불린다.\n\n[75] 이 예언은 아리오스토가 이 대목에서, 자기 고향 나라인 페라라 공국의 군주들, 곧 고귀한 에스테 가문에 찬사를 보내려고 끼워 넣은 것이다.";
if(typeof BODIES_SENT!=="undefined") BODIES_SENT["gb-56644"]=[[["THE religions of ancient Greece and Rome are extinct.", "고대 그리스와 로마의 종교는 이미 소멸했다."], ["The so-called divinities of Olympus have not a single worshipper among living men.", "이른바 올림포스의 신들은 오늘날 살아 있는 사람들 가운데 단 한 명의 숭배자도 거느리지 못하고 있다."], ["They belong now not to the department of theology, but to those of literature and taste.", "그들은 이제 신학의 영역이 아니라 문학과 취향의 영역에 속한다."], ["There they still hold their place, and will continue to hold it, for they are too closely connected with the finest productions of poetry and art, both ancient and modern, to pass into oblivion.", "거기서 그들은 여전히 제자리를 지키고 있으며, 앞으로도 계속 지켜 나갈 것이다. 왜냐하면 그들은 고대와 근대를 막론하고 가장 빼어난 시와 예술 작품들과 너무도 긴밀히 얽혀 있어, 망각 속으로 사라질 수 없기 때문이다."]], [["We propose to tell the stories relating to them which have come down to us from the ancients, and which are alluded to by modern poets, essayists, and orators.", "우리는 고대인들로부터 전해 내려와 근대의 시인과 수필가와 웅변가들이 인유(引喩)하는 그 신들에 관한 이야기를 들려주고자 한다."], ["Our readers may thus at the same time be entertained by the most charming fictions which fancy has ever created, and put in possession of information indispensable to every one who would read with intelligence the elegant literature of his own day.", "이로써 우리의 독자들은 상상력이 일찍이 빚어낸 가장 매혹적인 허구로 즐거움을 누리는 동시에, 자기 시대의 우아한 문학을 식견을 가지고 읽으려는 사람이라면 누구에게나 없어서는 안 될 지식을 갖추게 될 것이다."]], [["In order to understand these stories, it will be necessary to acquaint ourselves with the ideas of the structure of the universe which prevailed among the Greeks—the people from whom the Romans, and other nations through them, received their science and religion.", "이 이야기들을 이해하려면, 그리스인들 사이에 널리 퍼져 있던 우주의 구조에 관한 관념과 친숙해질 필요가 있다. 로마인들, 그리고 그들을 통하여 다른 여러 민족이 학문과 종교를 물려받은 원천이 바로 이 그리스인들이기 때문이다."]], [["The Greeks believed the earth to be flat and circular, their own country occupying the middle of it, the central point being either Mount Olympus, the abode of the gods, or Delphi, so famous for its oracle.", "그리스인들은 대지가 평평하고 둥글다고 믿었으며, 자신들의 나라가 그 한가운데를 차지하고, 그 중심점은 신들의 거처인 올림포스 산이거나 신탁으로 유명한 델포이라고 여겼다."]], [["The circular disk of the earth was crossed from west to east and divided into two equal parts by the Sea, as they called the Mediterranean, and its continuation the Euxine, the only seas with which they were acquainted.", "대지의 둥근 원반은 서쪽에서 동쪽으로 가로질러, 그들이 〈바다〉라고 부른 지중해와 그것에 이어지는 에욱세이노스[흑해]에 의해 두 개의 똑같은 부분으로 나뉘었는데, 이것이 그들이 알고 있던 유일한 바다였다."]], [["Around the earth flowed the River Ocean, its course being from south to north on the western side of the earth, and in a contrary direction on the eastern side.", "대지를 둘러싸고는 〈오케아노스 강〉이 흘렀으니, 그 물길은 대지의 서쪽에서는 남에서 북으로, 동쪽에서는 그 반대 방향으로 향했다."], ["It flowed in a steady, equable current, unvexed by storm or tempest.", "그것은 폭풍이나 풍랑에 시달리는 일 없이 한결같고 고른 흐름으로 흘렀다."], ["The sea, and all the rivers on earth, received their waters from it.", "바다와 대지의 모든 강은 그 물을 이 강에서 받았다."]], [["The northern portion of the earth was supposed to be inhabited by a happy race named the Hyperboreans, dwelling in everlasting bliss and spring beyond the lofty mountains whose caverns were supposed to send forth the piercing blasts of the north wind, which chilled the people of Hellas (Greece).", "대지의 북쪽 부분에는 히페르보레오이라 불리는 행복한 종족이 살고 있다고 여겨졌으니, 그들은 높이 솟은 산들 너머에서 영원한 지복과 봄 속에 거주했다. 그 산들의 동굴에서 헬라스[그리스] 사람들을 얼어붙게 하는 살을 에는 북풍이 뿜어 나온다고 여겨졌다."], ["Their country was inaccessible by land or sea.", "그들의 나라는 육로로도 바닷길로도 닿을 수 없었다."], ["They lived exempt from disease or old age, from toils and warfare.", "그들은 질병이나 노년, 고역과 전쟁에서 벗어나 살았다."], ["Moore has given us the “Song of a Hyperborean,” beginning", "무어는 우리에게 「히페르보레오이의 노래」를 남겼는데, 그것은 이렇게 시작된다."]], [["“I come from a land in the sun-bright deep, Where golden gardens glow, Where the winds of the north, becalmed in sleep, Their conch shells never blow.”", "\"나는 해 밝은 깊은 곳의 땅에서 왔노라, 황금빛 정원이 빛나는 곳, 북풍이 잠에 잠겨 고요해져 소라 나팔을 결코 불지 않는 곳에서.\""]], [["On the south side of the earth, close to the stream of Ocean, dwelt a people happy and virtuous as the Hyperboreans.", "대지의 남쪽, 오케아노스의 물줄기 가까이에는 히페르보레오이만큼이나 행복하고 덕스러운 종족이 살았다."], ["They were named the Æthiopians.", "그들은 아이티오피아인이라 불렸다."], ["The gods favored them so highly that they were wont to leave at times their Olympian abodes and go to share their sacrifices and banquets.", "신들은 그들을 무척이나 어여삐 여겨, 때때로 올림포스의 거처를 떠나 그들의 제사와 잔치에 동참하러 가곤 했다."]], [["On the western margin of the earth, by the stream of Ocean, lay a happy place named the Elysian Plain, whither mortals favored by the gods were transported without tasting of death, to enjoy an immortality of bliss.", "대지의 서쪽 가장자리, 오케아노스의 물줄기 곁에는 엘리시온 평원[엘리시움]이라 불리는 행복한 곳이 있었으니, 신들의 총애를 받은 필멸자들은 죽음을 맛보지 않은 채 그곳으로 옮겨져 지복의 불멸을 누렸다."], ["This happy region was also called the “Fortunate Fields,” and the “Isles of the Blessed.”", "이 행복한 지역은 〈축복받은 들판〉이라고도, 〈축복받은 자들의 섬〉이라고도 불렸다."]], [["We thus see that the Greeks of the early ages knew little of any real people except those to the east and south of their own country, or near the coast of the Mediterranean.", "이로써 우리는 초기 시대의 그리스인들이 자기 나라의 동쪽과 남쪽, 혹은 지중해 연안 가까이에 있는 종족을 제외하고는 실재하는 어떤 민족에 관해서도 거의 알지 못했음을 알 수 있다."], ["Their imagination meantime peopled the western portion of this sea with giants, monsters, and enchantresses; while they placed around the disk of the earth, which they probably regarded as of no great width, nations enjoying the peculiar favor of the gods, and blessed with happiness and longevity.", "한편 그들의 상상력은 이 바다의 서쪽 부분을 거인들과 괴물들과 마녀들로 채웠다. 또한 그다지 넓지 않다고 여겼을 법한 대지의 원반 둘레에는 신들의 각별한 총애를 받으며 행복과 장수를 누리는 여러 민족을 자리매김했다."]], [["The Dawn, the Sun, and the Moon were supposed to rise out of the Ocean, on the eastern side, and to drive through the air, giving light to gods and men.", "새벽과 태양과 달은 동쪽에서 오케아노스로부터 떠올라 하늘을 가로질러 달리면서 신들과 인간에게 빛을 준다고 여겨졌다."], ["The stars, also, except those forming the Wain or Bear, and others near them, rose out of and sank into the stream of Ocean.", "별들 또한 큰곰자리[북두]를 이루는 것들과 그 근처의 몇몇을 제외하고는 오케아노스의 물줄기에서 떠올랐다가 그 속으로 잠겼다."], ["There the sun-god embarked in a winged boat, which conveyed him round by the northern part of the earth, back to his place of rising in the east.", "그곳에서 태양신은 날개 달린 배에 올랐는데, 그 배는 그를 대지의 북쪽 부분을 돌아 동쪽에 있는 그의 떠오르는 자리로 도로 실어다 주었다."], ["Milton alludes to this in his “Comus”:", "밀턴은 「코머스」에서 이를 인유한다."]], [["“Now the gilded car of day His golden axle doth allay In the steep Atlantic stream, And the slope Sun his upward beam Shoots against the dusky pole, Pacing towards the other goal Of his chamber in the east.”", "\"이제 낮의 도금한 수레가 그 황금 굴대를 가라앉히노니 가파른 대서양의 물줄기 속으로, 비스듬한 태양은 그 솟구치는 빛살을 어두운 하늘 끝에 쏘아 올리며, 동쪽에 있는 그의 침실, 또 다른 종착점을 향해 걸음을 옮긴다.\""]], [["The abode of the gods was on the summit of Mount Olympus, in Thessaly.", "신들의 거처는 테살리아에 있는 올림포스 산의 정상이었다."], ["A gate of clouds, kept by the goddesses named the Seasons, opened to permit the passage of the Celestials to earth, and to receive them on their return.", "〈사계절〉이라 불리는 여신들이 지키는 구름의 문이 열려 천인(天人)들이 대지로 나아가는 것을 허락하고, 돌아올 때 그들을 맞아들였다."], ["The gods had their separate dwellings; but all, when summoned, repaired to the palace of Jupiter, as did also those deities whose usual abode was the earth, the waters, or the underworld.", "신들은 저마다 따로 거처를 두고 있었으나, 부름을 받으면 모두 제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]의 궁전으로 모여들었으며, 평소 거처가 대지나 물속, 혹은 저승인 신들 역시 그러했다."], ["It was also in the great hall of the palace of the Olympian king that the gods feasted each day on ambrosia and nectar, their food and drink, the latter being handed round by the lovely goddess Hebe.", "또한 올림포스 왕의 궁전 대전(大殿)에서 신들은 날마다 그들의 음식과 음료인 암브로시아와 넥타르로 잔치를 벌였으니, 후자는 사랑스러운 여신 헤베가 돌아가며 따라 주었다."], ["Here they conversed of the affairs of heaven and earth; and as they quaffed their nectar, Apollo, the god of music, delighted them with the tones of his lyre, to which the Muses sang in responsive strains.", "이곳에서 그들은 하늘과 땅의 일을 이야기했고, 넥타르를 들이켜는 동안 음악의 신 아폴론이 그의 리라 가락으로 그들을 즐겁게 하면, 무사 여신들이 화답하는 노래로 답했다."], ["When the sun was set, the gods retired to sleep in their respective dwellings.", "해가 지면 신들은 저마다의 거처로 물러가 잠들었다."]], [["The following lines from the “Odyssey” will show how Homer conceived of Olympus:", "「오디세이아」의 다음 시구는 호메로스가 올림포스를 어떻게 그렸는지 보여 줄 것이다."]], [["“So saying, Minerva, goddess azure-eyed, Rose to Olympus, the reputed seat Eternal of the gods, which never storms Disturb, rains drench, or snow invades, but calm The expanse and cloudless shines with purest day.", "\"그렇게 말하고서, 푸른 눈의 여신 미네르바는 신들의 영원한 거처로 일컬어지는 올림포스로 올라갔으니, 그곳은 폭풍도 어지럽히지 못하고, 비도 적시지 못하며, 눈도 범하지 못하고, 다만 고요히 그 넓은 하늘이 구름 한 점 없이 더없이 맑은 낮으로 빛난다."], ["There the inhabitants divine rejoice Forever.”", "그곳에서 신성한 거주자들은 영원히 기뻐하노라.\""], ["Cowper.", "쿠퍼."]], [["The robes and other parts of the dress of the goddesses were woven by Minerva and the Graces and everything of a more solid nature was formed of the various metals.", "여신들의 옷과 의복의 다른 부분들은 미네르바와 〈우아미의 여신〉들이 짜냈고, 좀 더 단단한 성질의 것은 모두 갖가지 금속으로 만들어졌다."], ["Vulcan was architect, smith, armorer, chariot builder, and artist of all work in Olympus.", "불카누스는 올림포스에서 건축가이자 대장장이, 무기 제작자, 전차 제작자이며, 온갖 일을 도맡은 장인이었다."], ["He built of brass the houses of the gods; he made for them the golden shoes with which they trod the air or the water, and moved from place to place with the speed of the wind, or even of thought.", "그는 신들의 집을 청동으로 지었고, 그들이 공기나 물 위를 디디며 바람의 속도로, 아니 심지어 생각의 속도로 이곳저곳을 옮겨 다니게 해 주는 황금 신발을 만들어 주었다."], ["He also shod with brass the celestial steeds, which whirled the chariots of the gods through the air, or along the surface of the sea.", "그는 또한 신들의 전차를 공중으로, 혹은 바다 표면을 따라 휘몰아 가는 천상의 준마들에게 청동 편자를 박았다."], ["He was able to bestow on his workmanship self-motion, so that the tripods (chairs and tables) could move of themselves in and out of the celestial hall.", "그는 자기 솜씨로 만든 것에 스스로 움직이는 힘을 부여할 수 있어, 세발솥(의자와 탁자)들이 저절로 천상의 대전을 드나들 수 있었다."], ["He even endowed with intelligence the golden handmaidens whom he made to wait on himself.", "그는 자신의 시중을 들도록 만든 황금 시녀들에게마저 지성을 부여했다."]], [["Jupiter, or Jove (Zeus[2]), though called the father of gods and men, had himself a beginning.", "제우스, 곧 유피테르(제우스)는 신들과 인간의 아버지로 일컬어지지만, 그 자신에게도 시작이 있었다."], ["Saturn (Cronos) was his father, and Rhea (Ops) his mother.", "사투르누스(크로노스)가 그의 아버지였고, 레아(옵스)가 그의 어머니였다."], ["Saturn and Rhea were of the race of Titans, who were the children of Earth and Heaven, which sprang from Chaos, of which we shall give a further account in our next chapter.", "사투르누스와 레아는 티탄족에 속했는데, 이들은 대지와 하늘의 자식이었으며, 대지와 하늘은 카오스에서 솟아났으니, 이에 관해서는 다음 장에서 좀 더 자세히 이야기하겠다."]], [["There is another cosmogony, or account of the creation, according to which Earth, Erebus, and Love were the first of beings.", "또 다른 우주 발생설, 곧 천지 창조에 관한 설명이 있는데, 그에 따르면 대지와 에레보스와 사랑이 최초의 존재였다."], ["Love (Eros) issued from the egg of Night, which floated on Chaos.", "사랑(에로스)은 카오스 위를 떠다니던 밤의 알에서 나왔다."], ["By his arrows and torch he pierced and vivified all things, producing life and joy.", "그는 자신의 화살과 횃불로 만물을 꿰뚫고 생기를 불어넣어, 생명과 기쁨을 빚어냈다."]], [["Saturn and Rhea were not the only Titans.", "사투르누스와 레아만이 유일한 티탄은 아니었다."], ["There were others, whose names were Oceanus, Hyperion, Iapetus, and Ophion, males; and Themis, Mnemosyne, Eurynome, females.", "다른 이들도 있었으니, 남신으로는 오케아노스, 히페리온, 이아페토스, 오피온이 있었고, 여신으로는 테미스, 므네모시네, 에우리노메가 있었다."], ["They are spoken of as the elder gods, whose dominion was afterwards transferred to others.", "그들은 더 오래된 신들로 일컬어지며, 그들의 지배권은 훗날 다른 신들에게 넘어갔다."], ["Saturn yielded to Jupiter, Oceanus to Neptune, Hyperion to Apollo.", "사투르누스는 제우스에게, 오케아노스는 넵투누스에게, 히페리온은 아폴론에게 자리를 내주었다."], ["Hyperion was the father of the Sun, Moon, and Dawn.", "히페리온은 태양과 달과 새벽의 아버지였다."], ["He is therefore the original sun-god, and is painted with the splendor and beauty which were afterwards bestowed on Apollo.", "그러므로 그는 본디 태양신으로서, 훗날 아폴론에게 부여된 광채와 아름다움을 지닌 모습으로 그려진다."]], [["“Hyperion’s curls, the front of Jove himself.”", "\"히페리온의 곱슬머리, 유피테르 그 자신의 이마.\""], ["Shakspeare.", "셰익스피어."]], [["Ophion and Eurynome ruled over Olympus till they were dethroned by Saturn and Rhea.", "오피온과 에우리노메는 사투르누스와 레아에게 폐위되기 전까지 올림포스를 다스렸다."], ["Milton alludes to them in “Paradise Lost.”", "밀턴은 「실낙원」에서 이들을 인유한다."], ["He says the heathens seem to have had some knowledge of the temptation and fall of man.", "그는 이교도들이 인간의 유혹과 타락에 관해 어느 정도 알고 있었던 듯하다고 말한다."]], [["“And fabled how the serpent, whom they called Ophion, with Eurynome, (the wide-Encroaching Eve perhaps,) had first the rule Of high Olympus, thence by Saturn driven.”", "\"그리고 꾸며 내기를, 그들이 오피온이라 부른 뱀이 에우리노메와 더불어 (어쩌면 널리 세력을 뻗친 이브일지도 모를 그녀와 더불어) 처음에 드높은 올림포스를 다스렸으나, 거기서 사투르누스에게 쫓겨났다 하더라.\""]], [["The representations given of Saturn are not very consistent; for on the one hand his reign is said to have been the golden age of innocence and purity, and on the other he is described as a monster who devoured his children.[3] Jupiter, however, escaped this fate, and when grown up espoused Metis (Prudence), who administered a draught to Saturn which caused him to disgorge his children.", "사투르누스에 관해 전해지는 묘사는 그다지 일관되지 않다. 한편으로 그의 치세는 순결과 순수의 황금시대였다고 일컬어지면서도, 다른 한편으로 그는 자기 자식들을 집어삼킨 괴물로 묘사되기 때문이다."], ["Jupiter, with his brothers and sisters, now rebelled against their father Saturn and his brothers the Titans; vanquished them, and imprisoned some of them in Tartarus, inflicting other penalties on others.", "그러나 제우스는 이 운명을 모면했으니, 장성한 뒤 메티스(지혜)와 혼인했고, 그녀는 사투르누스에게 약을 한 모금 먹여 그가 삼킨 자식들을 토해 내게 만들었다. 이제 제우스는 형제자매들과 더불어 그들의 아버지 사투르누스와 그 형제인 티탄족에게 반란을 일으켜, 그들을 무찌르고 일부는 타르타로스에 가두었으며, 다른 이들에게는 또 다른 형벌을 내렸다."], ["Atlas was condemned to bear up the heavens on his shoulders.", "아틀라스는 두 어깨로 하늘을 떠받치는 형벌을 받았다."]], [["On the dethronement of Saturn, Jupiter with his brothers Neptune (Poseidon) and Pluto (Dis) divided his dominions.", "사투르누스가 폐위되자, 제우스는 형제인 넵투누스(포세이돈), 플루토(디스)와 더불어 그의 영토를 나누었다."], ["Jupiter’s portion was the heavens, Neptune’s the ocean, and Pluto’s the realms of the dead.", "제우스의 몫은 하늘이었고, 넵투누스의 몫은 바다였으며, 플루토의 몫은 죽은 자들의 영역이었다."], ["Earth and Olympus were common property.", "대지와 올림포스는 공동의 소유였다."], ["Jupiter was king of gods and men.", "제우스는 신들과 인간의 왕이었다."], ["The thunder was his weapon, and he bore a shield called Ægis, made for him by Vulcan.", "천둥이 그의 무기였고, 그는 불카누스가 만들어 준 아이기스라는 방패를 지녔다."], ["The eagle was his favorite bird, and bore his thunderbolts.", "독수리가 그가 가장 아끼는 새였으며, 그의 벼락을 날랐다."]], [["Juno (Hera) was the wife of Jupiter, and queen of the gods.", "유노(헤라)는 제우스의 아내이자 신들의 여왕이었다."], ["Iris, the goddess of the rainbow, was her attendant and messenger.", "무지개의 여신 이리스가 그녀의 시중을 들며 전령 노릇을 했다."], ["The peacock was her favorite bird.", "공작이 그녀가 가장 아끼는 새였다."]], [["Vulcan (Hephæstos), the celestial artist, was the son of Jupiter and Juno.", "천상의 장인 불카누스(헤파이스토스)는 제우스와 유노의 아들이었다."], ["He was born lame, and his mother was so displeased at the sight of him that she flung him out of heaven.", "그는 절름발이로 태어났고, 그 어머니는 그의 모습을 보고 너무도 언짢아하여 그를 하늘 밖으로 내던졌다."], ["Other accounts say that Jupiter kicked him out for taking part with his mother in a quarrel which occurred between them.", "다른 설명에 따르면, 제우스와 유노 사이에 일어난 다툼에서 불카누스가 어머니 편을 들었기에 제우스가 그를 발로 차서 내쫓았다고 한다."], ["Vulcan’s lameness, according to this account, was the consequence of his fall.", "이 설명에 따르면 불카누스의 절름거림은 그 추락의 결과였다."], ["He was a whole day falling, and at last alighted in the island of Lemnos, which was thenceforth sacred to him.", "그는 꼬박 하루를 떨어져 마침내 렘노스 섬에 내려앉았는데, 그 섬은 그때부터 그에게 바쳐진 신성한 곳이 되었다. 밀턴은 「실낙원」"], ["Milton alludes to this story in “Paradise Lost,” Book I.:", "제1권에서 이 이야기를 인유한다."]], [["“. .", "\"."], [".", "."], ["From morn To noon he fell, from noon to dewy eve, A summer’s day; and with the setting sun Dropped from the zenith, like a falling star, On Lemnos, the Ægean isle.”", ". 아침부터 한낮까지 그는 떨어졌고, 한낮부터 이슬 맺힌 저녁까지, 여름날 하루를 다 채워 떨어졌으며, 지는 해와 함께 하늘 꼭대기에서 떨어지는 별처럼 곤두박질쳐 에게 해의 섬, 렘노스에 내려앉았다.\""]], [["Mars (Ares), the god of war, was the son of Jupiter and Juno.", "전쟁의 신 마르스(아레스)는 제우스와 유노의 아들이었다."]], [["Phœbus Apollo, the god of archery, prophecy, and music, was the son of Jupiter and Latona, and brother of Diana (Artemis).", "활쏘기와 예언과 음악의 신 포이보스 아폴론은 제우스와 라토나의 아들이며, 디아나(아르테미스)의 오라비였다."], ["He was god of the sun, as Diana, his sister, was the goddess of the moon.", "그의 누이 디아나가 달의 여신이었듯이, 그는 태양의 신이었다."]], [["Venus (Aphrodite), the goddess of love and beauty, was the daughter of Jupiter and Dione.", "사랑과 아름다움의 여신 베누스(아프로디테)는 제우스와 디오네의 딸이었다."], ["Others say that Venus sprang from the foam of the sea.", "다른 이들은 베누스가 바다의 거품에서 솟아났다고 말한다."], ["The zephyr wafted her along the waves to the Isle of Cyprus, where she was received and attired by the Seasons, and then led to the assembly of the gods.", "서풍이 그녀를 물결 위로 실어 키프로스 섬으로 데려갔고, 그곳에서 〈사계절〉 여신들이 그녀를 맞아 단장시킨 뒤 신들의 회합으로 인도했다."], ["All were charmed with her beauty, and each one demanded her for his wife.", "모두가 그녀의 아름다움에 매혹되어 저마다 그녀를 아내로 달라고 청했다."], ["Jupiter gave her to Vulcan, in gratitude for the service he had rendered in forging thunderbolts.", "제우스는 불카누스가 벼락을 벼려 준 공로에 대한 보답으로 그녀를 그에게 주었다."], ["So the most beautiful of the goddesses became the wife of the most ill-favored of gods.", "그리하여 여신들 가운데 가장 아름다운 이가 신들 가운데 가장 못생긴 이의 아내가 되었다."], ["Venus possessed an embroidered girdle called Cestus, which had the power of inspiring love.", "베누스는 케스토스라 불리는 수놓은 띠를 지녔는데, 그것은 사랑을 불러일으키는 힘이 있었다."], ["Her favorite birds were swans and doves, and the plants sacred to her were the rose and the myrtle.", "그녀가 가장 아끼는 새는 백조와 비둘기였고, 그녀에게 바쳐진 식물은 장미와 도금양이었다."]], [["Cupid (Eros), the god of love, was the son of Venus.", "사랑의 신 쿠피도(에로스)는 베누스의 아들이었다."], ["He was her constant companion; and, armed with bow and arrows, he shot the darts of desire into the bosoms of both gods and men.", "그는 그녀의 한결같은 동반자였으며, 활과 화살로 무장하고서 신들과 인간 모두의 가슴에 욕망의 화살을 쏘았다."], ["There was a deity named Anteros, who was sometimes represented as the avenger of slighted love, and sometimes as the symbol of reciprocal affection.", "안테로스라는 신도 있었는데, 그는 때로 무시당한 사랑을 갚아 주는 복수자로, 때로는 서로 주고받는 애정의 상징으로 그려졌다."], ["The following legend is told of him:", "그에 관해서는 다음과 같은 전설이 전해진다."]], [["Venus, complaining to Themis that her son Eros continued always a child, was told by her that it was because he was solitary, and that if he had a brother he would grow apace.", "베누스가 테미스에게 자기 아들 에로스가 늘 어린아이로만 머물러 있다고 하소연하자, 테미스는 그것이 그가 외톨이이기 때문이며, 만일 그에게 형제가 있다면 쑥쑥 자랄 것이라고 일러 주었다."], ["Anteros was soon afterwards born, and Eros immediately was seen to increase rapidly in size and strength.", "얼마 지나지 않아 안테로스가 태어났고, 곧바로 에로스가 몸집과 힘이 부쩍 자라나는 것이 보였다."]], [["Minerva (Pallas, Athene), the goddess of wisdom, was the offspring of Jupiter, without a mother.", "지혜의 여신 미네르바(팔라스, 아테네)는 어머니 없이 제우스에게서 난 자식이었다."], ["She sprang forth from his head completely armed.", "그녀는 완전히 무장한 채 그의 머리에서 솟아났다."], ["Her favorite bird was the owl, and the plant sacred to her the olive.", "그녀가 가장 아끼는 새는 올빼미였고, 그녀에게 바쳐진 식물은 올리브였다."]], [["Byron, in “Childe Harold,” alludes to the birth of Minerva thus:", "바이런은 「차일드 해럴드」에서 미네르바의 탄생을 이렇게 인유한다."]], [["“Can tyrants but by tyrants conquered be, And Freedom find no champion and no child, Such as Columbia saw arise, when she Sprang forth a Pallas, armed and undefiled?", "\"폭군은 폭군에게만 정복될 수 있는가, 자유는 콜룸비아가 보았던 것 같은, 무장한 채 더럽혀지지 않은 팔라스로 솟아났을 때의 그런 투사도 그런 자식도 찾지 못하는가?"], ["Or must such minds be nourished in the wild, Deep in the unpruned forest, ’midst the roar Of cataracts, where nursing Nature smiled On infant Washington?", "아니면 그런 정신들은 거친 들에서, 가지치기 않은 깊은 숲속에서, 길러 주는 자연이 어린 워싱턴에게 미소 지었던 폭포의 굉음 한가운데서 길러져야만 하는가?"], ["Has earth no more Such seeds within her breast, or Europe no such shore?”", "대지는 그 가슴속에 이제 그런 씨앗을 더 품지 못하고, 유럽에는 그런 기슭이 없는가?\""]], [["Mercury (Hermes) was the son of Jupiter and Maia.", "메르쿠리우스(헤르메스)는 제우스와 마이아의 아들이었다."], ["He presided over commerce, wrestling, and other gymnastic exercises, even over thieving, and everything, in short, which required skill and dexterity.", "그는 상업과 씨름을 비롯한 여러 체육 활동을 주관했으며, 심지어 도둑질에 이르기까지, 요컨대 솜씨와 재간을 필요로 하는 모든 일을 관장했다."], ["He was the messenger of Jupiter, and wore a winged cap and winged shoes.", "그는 제우스의 전령이었으며, 날개 달린 모자와 날개 달린 신발을 신었다."], ["He bore in his hand a rod entwined with two serpents, called the caduceus.", "그는 두 마리 뱀이 휘감긴 지팡이를 손에 들었으니, 그것을 카두케우스라 불렀다."]], [["Mercury is said to have invented the lyre.", "메르쿠리우스가 리라를 발명했다고 한다."], ["He found, one day, a tortoise, of which he took the shell, made holes in the opposite edges of it, and drew cords of linen through them, and the instrument was complete.", "어느 날 그는 거북 한 마리를 발견하고는 그 등딱지를 떼어 내어, 마주 보는 가장자리에 구멍을 뚫고 그 사이로 아마실을 꿰니, 악기가 완성되었다."], ["The cords were nine, in honor of the nine Muses.", "그 줄은 아홉 무사 여신을 기려 아홉 가닥이었다."], ["Mercury gave the lyre to Apollo, and received from him in exchange the caduceus.[4]", "메르쿠리우스는 그 리라를 아폴론에게 주고, 그 대가로 그에게서 카두케우스를 받았다."]], [["Ceres (Demeter) was the daughter of Saturn and Rhea.", "케레스(데메테르)는 사투르누스와 레아의 딸이었다."], ["She had a daughter named Proserpine (Persephone), who became the wife of Pluto, and queen of the realms of the dead.", "그녀에게는 프로세르피나(페르세포네)라는 딸이 있었는데, 그녀는 플루토의 아내이자 죽은 자들의 영역의 여왕이 되었다."], ["Ceres presided over agriculture.", "케레스는 농경을 주관했다."]], [["Bacchus (Dionysus), the god of wine, was the son of Jupiter and Semele.", "포도주의 신 바쿠스(디오니소스)는 제우스와 세멜레의 아들이었다."], ["He represents not only the intoxicating power of wine, but its social and beneficent influences likewise, so that he is viewed as the promoter of civilization, and a lawgiver and lover of peace.", "그는 포도주의 취하게 하는 힘뿐만 아니라 그 사교적이고 이로운 영향력까지도 나타내므로, 문명의 촉진자이자 입법자이며 평화를 사랑하는 자로 여겨진다."]], [["The Muses were the daughters of Jupiter and Mnemosyne (Memory).", "무사 여신들은 제우스와 므네모시네(기억)의 딸들이었다."], ["They presided over song, and prompted the memory.", "그들은 노래를 주관하고 기억을 일깨웠다."], ["They were nine in number, to each of whom was assigned the precedence over some particular department of literature, art, or science.", "그들은 모두 아홉이었으며, 저마다 문학과 예술, 혹은 학문의 특정한 영역에 대한 으뜸 자리를 맡았다."], ["Calliope was the muse of epic poetry, Clio of history, Euterpe of lyric poetry, Melpomene of tragedy, Terpsichore of choral dance and song, Erato of love poetry, Polyhymnia of sacred poetry, Urania of astronomy, Thalia of comedy.", "칼리오페는 서사시의 무사였고, 클리오는 역사의, 에우테르페는 서정시의, 멜포메네는 비극의, 테르프시코레는 합창 무용과 노래의, 에라토는 연애시의, 폴리힘니아는 종교시의, 우라니아는 천문학의, 탈리아는 희극의 무사였다."]], [["The Graces were goddesses presiding over the banquet, the dance, and all social enjoyments and elegant arts.", "〈우아미의 여신〉들은 잔치와 춤, 그리고 온갖 사교의 즐거움과 우아한 기예를 주관하는 여신들이었다."], ["They were three in number.", "그들은 모두 셋이었다."], ["Their names were Euphrosyne, Aglaia, and Thalia.", "그들의 이름은 에우프로시네, 아글라이아, 탈리아였다."]], [["Spenser describes the office of the Graces thus:", "스펜서는 〈우아미의 여신〉들의 소임을 이렇게 묘사한다."]], [["“These three on men all gracious gifts bestow Which deck the body or adorn the mind, To make them lovely or well-favored show; As comely carriage, entertainment kind, Sweet semblance, friendly offices that bind, And all the complements of courtesy; They teach us how to each degree and kind We should ourselves demean, to low, to high, To friends, to foes; which skill men call Civility.”", "\"이 셋은 사람들에게 온갖 우아한 선물을 베푸나니 몸을 꾸미거나 마음을 아름답게 하는 것들, 그들을 사랑스럽거나 호감 가게 보이게 하는 것들, 이를테면 단정한 몸가짐, 다정한 환대, 상냥한 자태, 사람을 묶어 주는 친절한 도리, 그리고 예의의 온갖 격식이라. 그들은 우리에게 가르치나니 온갖 신분과 부류, 낮은 이에게, 높은 이에게, 벗에게, 적에게 우리가 어떻게 처신해야 하는지를. 사람들은 이 재간을 예절이라 부르노라.\""]], [["The Fates were also three—Clotho, Lachesis, and Atropos.", "운명의 여신들 또한 셋이었으니, 클로토, 라케시스, 아트로포스였다."], ["Their office was to spin the thread of human destiny, and they were armed with shears, with which they cut it off when they pleased.", "그들의 소임은 인간 운명의 실을 잣는 것이었으며, 그들은 가위로 무장하여 마음 내키는 대로 그 실을 끊어 버렸다."], ["They were the daughters of Themis (Law), who sits by Jove on his throne to give him counsel.", "그들은 테미스(법)의 딸들이었는데, 테미스는 제우스에게 조언을 하기 위해 그의 옥좌 곁에 앉아 있다."]], [["The Erinnyes, or Furies, were three goddesses who punished by their secret stings the crimes of those who escaped or defied public justice.", "에리니에스, 곧 복수의 여신들은 공적 정의를 모면하거나 거역한 자들의 죄악을 그들만의 은밀한 가시로 벌하는 세 여신이었다."], ["The heads of the Furies were wreathed with serpents, and their whole appearance was terrific and appalling.", "복수의 여신들의 머리에는 뱀들이 화관처럼 휘감겨 있었고, 그들의 모습 전체가 무시무시하고 소름 끼쳤다."], ["Their names were Alecto, Tisiphone, and Megæra.", "그들의 이름은 알렉토, 티시포네, 메가이라였다."], ["They were also called Eumenides.", "그들은 에우메니데스라고도 불렸다."]], [["Nemesis was also an avenging goddess.", "네메시스 또한 복수의 여신이었다."], ["She represents the righteous anger of the gods, particularly towards the proud and insolent.", "그녀는 신들의 정당한 분노를, 특히 거만하고 오만한 자들을 향한 분노를 나타낸다."]], [["Pan was the god of flocks and shepherds.", "판은 가축 떼와 목자들의 신이었다."], ["His favorite residence was in Arcadia.", "그가 가장 좋아한 거처는 아르카디아였다."]], [["The Satyrs were deities of the woods and fields.", "사티로스들은 숲과 들판의 신들이었다."], ["They were conceived to be covered with bristly hair, their heads decorated with short, sprouting horns, and their feet like goats’ feet.", "그들은 뻣뻣한 털로 뒤덮여 있고, 머리에는 짧게 돋아난 뿔로 장식되었으며, 발은 염소의 발 같았다고 여겨졌다."]], [["Momus was the god of laughter, and Plutus the god of wealth.", "모모스는 웃음의 신이었고, 플루토스는 부(富)의 신이었다. 로마의 신들"]], [["# ROMAN DIVINITIES", "# ROMAN DIVINITIES"]], [["The preceding are Grecian divinities, though received also by the Romans.", "앞에서 든 신들은 그리스의 신들로, 로마인들에게도 받아들여진 것들이다."], ["Those which follow are peculiar to Roman mythology:", "다음에 드는 신들은 로마 신화에 고유한 것들이다."]], [["Saturn was an ancient Italian deity.", "사투르누스는 고대 이탈리아의 신이었다."], ["It was attempted to identify him with the Grecian god Cronos, and fabled that after his dethronement by Jupiter he fled to Italy, where he reigned during what was called the Golden Age.", "사람들은 그를 그리스의 신 크로노스와 동일시하려 했으며, 제우스에게 폐위된 뒤 그가 이탈리아로 달아나 그곳에서 이른바 황금시대 동안 다스렸다고 꾸며 냈다."], ["In memory of his beneficent dominion, the feast of Saturnalia was held every year in the winter season.", "그의 자애로운 치세를 기려 해마다 겨울철에 사투르날리아 축제가 열렸다."], ["Then all public business was suspended, declarations of war and criminal executions were postponed, friends made presents to one another, and the slaves were indulged with great liberties.", "그때에는 모든 공무가 중단되고, 선전포고와 사형 집행이 미뤄졌으며, 벗들이 서로 선물을 주고받고, 노예들에게는 큰 자유가 허락되었다."], ["A feast was given them at which they sat at table, while their masters served them, to show the natural equality of men, and that all things belonged equally to all, in the reign of Saturn.", "노예들에게 잔치가 베풀어져 그들이 식탁에 앉으면 그 주인들이 시중을 들었으니, 이는 인간의 천부적 평등을, 그리고 사투르누스의 치세에는 만물이 모두에게 똑같이 속했음을 보여 주기 위함이었다."]], [["Faunus,[5] the grandson of Saturn, was worshipped as the god of fields and shepherds, and also as a prophetic god.", "사투르누스의 손자인 파우누스는 들판과 목자들의 신으로, 또한 예언의 신으로 숭배되었다."], ["His name in the plural, Fauns, expressed a class of gamesome deities, like the Satyrs of the Greeks.", "복수형인 파우니라는 이름은 그리스의 사티로스들처럼 장난기 어린 신들의 한 부류를 가리켰다."]], [["Quirinus was a war god, said to be no other than Romulus, the founder of Rome, exalted after his death to a place among the gods.", "퀴리누스는 전쟁의 신으로, 로마의 건국자 로물루스가 죽은 뒤 신들 사이의 한 자리로 드높여진 것에 다름 아니라고 한다."]], [["Bellona, a war goddess.", "벨로나는 전쟁의 여신이었다."]], [["Terminus, the god of landmarks.", "테르미누스는 경계표의 신이었다."], ["His statue was a rude stone or post, set in the ground to mark the boundaries of fields.", "그의 상은 들판의 경계를 표시하기 위해 땅에 박아 둔 투박한 돌이나 기둥이었다."]], [["Pales, the goddess presiding over cattle and pastures.", "팔레스는 가축과 목초지를 주관하는 여신이었다."]], [["Pomona presided over fruit trees.", "포모나는 과일나무를 주관했다."]], [["Flora, the goddess of flowers.", "플로라는 꽃의 여신이었다."]], [["Lucina, the goddess of childbirth.", "루키나는 출산의 여신이었다."]], [["Vesta (the Hestia of the Greeks) was a deity presiding over the public and private hearth.", "베스타(그리스의 헤스티아)는 공적·사적 화로를 주관하는 신이었다."], ["A sacred fire, tended by six virgin priestesses called Vestals, flamed in her temple.", "베스탈리스라 불리는 여섯 처녀 사제가 돌보는 신성한 불이 그녀의 신전에서 타올랐다."], ["As the safety of the city was held to be connected with its conservation, the neglect of the virgins, if they let it go out, was severely punished, and the fire was rekindled from the rays of the sun.", "도시의 안위가 이 불의 보존과 결부되어 있다고 여겨졌으므로, 만일 처녀들이 그 불을 꺼뜨리는 태만을 범하면 가혹하게 벌받았고, 그 불은 햇빛에서 다시 지펴졌다."]], [["Liber is the Latin name of Bacchus; and Mulciber of Vulcan.", "리베르는 바쿠스의 라틴식 이름이고, 물키베르는 불카누스의 라틴식 이름이다."]], [["Janus was the porter of heaven.", "야누스는 하늘의 문지기였다."], ["He opens the year, the first month being named after him.", "그는 한 해를 여니, 첫 번째 달은 그의 이름을 따서 붙여졌다."], ["He is the guardian deity of gates, on which account he is commonly represented with two heads, because every door looks two ways.", "그는 성문을 지키는 수호신이었는데, 그런 까닭에 흔히 두 개의 머리를 지닌 모습으로 그려진다. 모든 문은 두 방향을 바라보기 때문이다."], ["His temples at Rome were numerous.", "로마에는 그의 신전이 많았다."], ["In war time the gates of the principal one were always open.", "전시에는 가장 중요한 신전의 문이 늘 열려 있었다."], ["In peace they were closed; but they were shut only once between the reign of Numa and that of Augustus.", "평화로울 때에는 닫혔으나, 누마의 치세와 아우구스투스의 치세 사이에 그 문이 닫힌 것은 단 한 번뿐이었다."]], [["The Penates were the gods who were supposed to attend to the welfare and prosperity of the family.", "페나테스는 가족의 안녕과 번영을 보살핀다고 여겨진 신들이었다."], ["Their name is derived from Penus, the pantry, which was sacred to them.", "그들의 이름은 그들에게 바쳐진 식료품 저장실인 페누스에서 유래했다."], ["Every master of a family was the priest to the Penates of his own house.", "모든 가장은 자기 집 페나테스의 사제였다."]], [["The Lares, or Lars, were also household gods, but differed from the Penates in being regarded as the deified spirits of mortals.", "라레스, 곧 라르 또한 가정의 신이었으나, 필멸자들의 신격화된 영혼으로 여겨졌다는 점에서 페나테스와 달랐다."], ["The family Lars were held to be the souls of the ancestors, who watched over and protected their descendants.", "가족의 라르는 후손을 지켜보고 보호하는 조상들의 혼령이라 여겨졌다."], ["The words Lemur and Larva more nearly correspond to our word Ghost.", "레무르와 라르바라는 말이 우리말의 〈유령〉이라는 낱말에 좀 더 가깝게 들어맞는다."]], [["The Romans believed that every man had his Genius, and every woman her Juno: that is, a spirit who had given them being, and was regarded as their protector through life.", "로마인들은 모든 남자에게 저마다의 게니우스가 있고 모든 여자에게 저마다의 유노가 있다고 믿었다. 곧, 그들에게 생명을 준 정령으로서, 평생 그들의 수호자로 여겨지는 존재였다."], ["On their birthdays men made offerings to their Genius, women to their Juno.", "생일이면 남자들은 자기 게니우스에게, 여자들은 자기 유노에게 제물을 바쳤다."]], [["A modern poet thus alludes to some of the Roman gods:", "근대의 한 시인은 로마의 몇몇 신들을 이렇게 인유한다."]], [["“Pomona loves the orchard, And Liber loves the vine, And Pales loves the straw-built shed Warm with the breath of kine; And Venus loves the whisper Of plighted youth and maid, In April’s ivory moonlight, Beneath the chestnut shade.”", "\"포모나는 과수원을 사랑하고, 리베르는 포도밭을 사랑하며, 팔레스는 소들의 숨결로 따뜻해진 짚으로 지은 헛간을 사랑하고, 베누스는 4월의 상아빛 달빛 아래, 밤나무 그늘 밑에서 언약을 맺은 젊은이와 처녀의 속삭임을 사랑한다.\""], ["—Macaulay, “Prophecy of Capys.” ————", "—매콜리, 「카피스의 예언」."]], [["N.B.—It is to be observed that in proper names the final e and es are to be sounded.", "비고. 고유명사에서 끝에 오는 e와 es는 소리 내어 발음해야 함에 유의해야 한다."], ["Thus Cybele and Penates are words of three syllables.", "따라서 키벨레와 페나테스는 세 음절로 된 낱말이다."], ["But Proserpine and Thebes are exceptions, and to be pronounced as English words.", "그러나 프로세르피네와 테베는 예외로, 영어 낱말처럼 발음해야 한다."], ["In the Index at the close of the volume we shall mark the accented syllable in all words which appear to require it. ————", "이 책 끝의 찾아보기에서 우리는 강세가 필요해 보이는 모든 낱말에 강세 음절을 표시할 것이다."]], [["# PROMETHEUS AND PANDORA", "# PROMETHEUS AND PANDORA"]], [["THE creation of the world is a problem naturally fitted to excite the liveliest interest of man, its inhabitant.", "세계의 창조란 그곳에 거주하는 인간으로서는 자연히 가장 생생한 흥미를 불러일으킬 수밖에 없는 문제다."], ["The ancient pagans, not having the information on the subject which we derive from the pages of Scripture, had their own way of telling the story, which is as follows:", "고대의 이교도들은 우리가 성서의 책장에서 얻는 그 주제에 관한 정보를 갖지 못했던 까닭에, 그들 나름의 방식으로 이 이야기를 전했는데, 그 내용은 다음과 같다."]], [["Before earth and sea and heaven were created, all things wore one aspect, to which we give the name of Chaos—a confused and shapeless mass, nothing but dead weight, in which, however, slumbered the seeds of things.", "땅과 바다와 하늘이 창조되기 이전, 만물은 하나의 모습을 하고 있었으니, 우리는 이것을 카오스[혼돈]라 부른다. 그것은 혼란스럽고 형태가 없는 덩어리, 죽은 무게에 지나지 않는 것이었으되, 그 안에는 만물의 씨앗이 잠들어 있었다."], ["Earth, sea, and air were all mixed up together; so the earth was not solid, the sea was not fluid, and the air was not transparent.", "땅과 바다와 공기가 모두 한데 뒤섞여 있었던 까닭에, 땅은 단단하지 않았고, 바다는 흐르지 않았으며, 공기는 투명하지 않았다."], ["God and Nature at last interposed, and put an end to this discord, separating earth from sea, and heaven from both.", "마침내 신과 자연이 개입하여 이 불화에 종지부를 찍었으니, 땅을 바다에서 갈라놓고, 하늘을 그 둘에서 갈라놓았다."], ["The fiery part, being the lightest, sprang up, and formed the skies; the air was next in weight and place.", "불의 부분은 가장 가벼운 까닭에 위로 솟구쳐 올라 하늘을 이루었고, 공기는 무게와 위치에서 그다음이었다."], ["The earth, being heavier, sank below; and the water took the lowest place, and buoyed up the earth.", "땅은 더 무거운 까닭에 아래로 가라앉았고, 물은 가장 낮은 자리를 차지하여 땅을 떠받쳤다."]], [["Here some god—it is not known which—gave his good offices in arranging and disposing the earth.", "여기서 어떤 신이 — 그가 누구인지는 알려져 있지 않다 — 땅을 정돈하고 배치하는 데에 그 호의를 베풀었다."], ["He appointed rivers and bays their places, raised mountains, scooped out valleys, distributed woods, fountains, fertile fields, and stony plains.", "그는 강과 만에 제자리를 지정해 주었고, 산을 솟아오르게 했으며, 골짜기를 파냈고, 숲과 샘과 비옥한 들판과 돌 많은 평원을 나누어 놓았다."], ["The air being cleared, the stars began to appear, fishes took possession of the sea, birds of the air, and four-footed beasts of the land.", "공기가 맑아지자, 별들이 나타나기 시작했고, 물고기는 바다를, 새는 공기를, 네발짐승은 땅을 차지하게 되었다."]], [["But a nobler animal was wanted, and Man was made.", "그러나 더 고귀한 동물이 필요했고, 그리하여 인간이 만들어졌다."], ["It is not known whether the creator made him of divine materials, or whether in the earth, so lately separated from heaven, there lurked still some heavenly seeds.", "창조자가 신성한 재료로 인간을 빚었는지, 아니면 하늘에서 갈라져 나온 지 얼마 되지 않은 땅에 여전히 어떤 천상의 씨앗들이 숨어 있었는지는 알려져 있지 않다."], ["Prometheus took some of this earth, and kneading it up with water, made man in the image of the gods.", "프로메테우스는 이 땅의 일부를 취하여 물과 함께 반죽해, 신들의 모습을 본떠 인간을 만들었다."], ["He gave him an upright stature, so that while all other animals turn their faces downward, and look to the earth, he raises his to heaven, and gazes on the stars.", "그는 인간에게 곧은 자세를 주었으니, 다른 모든 동물이 얼굴을 아래로 향하여 땅을 바라보는 동안, 인간은 얼굴을 하늘로 들어 별들을 응시하게 되었다."]], [["Prometheus was one of the Titans, a gigantic race, who inhabited the earth before the creation of man.", "프로메테우스는 티탄족 가운데 하나였으니, 그들은 인간이 창조되기 이전에 땅에 거주하던 거대한 종족이었다."], ["To him and his brother Epimetheus was committed the office of making man, and providing him and all other animals with the faculties necessary for their preservation.", "그와 그의 형제 에피메테우스에게는 인간을 만들고, 인간과 그 밖의 모든 동물에게 생존에 필요한 능력을 부여하는 임무가 맡겨졌다."], ["Epimetheus undertook to do this, and Prometheus was to overlook his work, when it was done.", "에피메테우스가 이 일을 떠맡았고, 프로메테우스는 일이 끝났을 때 그 작업을 살펴보기로 했다."], ["Epimetheus accordingly proceeded to bestow upon the different animals the various gifts of courage, strength, swiftness, sagacity; wings to one, claws to another, a shelly covering to a third, etc.", "이에 따라 에피메테우스는 여러 동물에게 용기와 힘과 빠르기와 영리함이라는 갖가지 선물을 베풀기 시작했으니, 어떤 동물에게는 날개를, 다른 동물에게는 발톱을, 또 다른 동물에게는 딱딱한 껍데기를 주는 식이었다."], ["But when man came to be provided for, who was to be superior to all other animals, Epimetheus had been so prodigal of his resources that he had nothing left to bestow upon him.", "그러나 다른 모든 동물보다 우월해야 할 인간을 위해 베풀 차례가 되었을 때, 에피메테우스는 이미 그 자원을 너무도 헤프게 써 버린 나머지 인간에게 줄 것이 아무것도 남아 있지 않았다."], ["In his perplexity he resorted to his brother Prometheus, who, with the aid of Minerva, went up to heaven, and lighted his torch at the chariot of the sun, and brought down fire to man.", "당황한 그는 그의 형제 프로메테우스에게 의지했고, 프로메테우스는 아테나[로마의 〈미네르바〉]의 도움을 받아 하늘로 올라가 태양의 마차에서 횃불을 밝혀, 인간에게 불을 가져다주었다."], ["With this gift man was more than a match for all other animals.", "이 선물로 인해 인간은 다른 모든 동물을 능히 능가하게 되었다."], ["It enabled him to make weapons wherewith to subdue them; tools with which to cultivate the earth; to warm his dwelling, so as to be comparatively independent of climate; and finally to introduce the arts and to coin money, the means of trade and commerce.", "불은 인간으로 하여금 동물을 굴복시킬 무기를 만들 수 있게 했고, 땅을 경작할 도구를 만들 수 있게 했으며, 거처를 따뜻하게 하여 기후에 비교적 구애받지 않게 했고, 마침내 여러 기예를 들여오고 화폐를 주조하여 교역과 상업의 수단을 갖추게 했다."]], [["Woman was not yet made.", "여자는 아직 만들어지지 않았다."], ["The story (absurd enough!) is that Jupiter made her, and sent her to Prometheus and his brother, to punish them for their presumption in stealing fire from heaven; and man, for accepting the gift.", "그 이야기(자못 터무니없다!)에 따르면, 제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]가 여자를 만들어, 하늘에서 불을 훔친 그 주제넘음을 벌하고자 프로메테우스와 그의 형제에게 보냈으며, 또한 그 선물을 받아들인 인간을 벌하고자 했다는 것이다."], ["The first woman was named Pandora.", "최초의 여자는 판도라라 불렸다."], ["She was made in heaven, every god contributing something to perfect her.", "그녀는 하늘에서 만들어졌으니, 신들이 저마다 무언가를 보태어 그녀를 완성했다."], ["Venus gave her beauty, Mercury persuasion, Apollo music, etc.", "아프로디테[로마의 〈베누스〉]는 그녀에게 아름다움을, 헤르메스[로마의 〈메르쿠리우스〉]는 설득력을, 아폴론은 음악을 주는 식이었다."], ["Thus equipped, she was conveyed to earth, and presented to Epimetheus, who gladly accepted her, though cautioned by his brother to beware of Jupiter and his gifts.", "이렇게 갖추어진 그녀는 땅으로 보내져 에피메테우스에게 바쳐졌고, 그는 제우스와 그의 선물을 경계하라는 형제의 충고를 받았음에도 기꺼이 그녀를 받아들였다."], ["Epimetheus had in his house a jar, in which were kept certain noxious articles, for which, in fitting man for his new abode, he had had no occasion.", "에피메테우스의 집에는 항아리가 하나 있었으니, 그 안에는 인간을 새로운 거처에 맞게 갖추는 과정에서 쓸 일이 없었던 어떤 해로운 것들이 담겨 있었다."], ["Pandora was seized with an eager curiosity to know what this jar contained; and one day she slipped off the cover and looked in.", "판도라는 이 항아리에 무엇이 들어 있는지 알고 싶은 간절한 호기심에 사로잡혔고, 어느 날 뚜껑을 살그머니 열어 안을 들여다보았다."], ["Forthwith there escaped a multitude of plagues for hapless man,—such as gout, rheumatism, and colic for his body, and envy, spite, and revenge for his mind,—and scattered themselves far and wide.", "그 즉시 가엾은 인간을 괴롭힐 수많은 재앙이 쏟아져 나왔으니 — 육체를 괴롭힐 통풍과 류머티즘과 복통이, 정신을 괴롭힐 질투와 악의와 복수심이 — 사방으로 멀리 흩어졌다."], ["Pandora hastened to replace the lid! but, alas!", "판도라는 서둘러 뚜껑을 다시 덮었으나, 아아!"], ["the whole contents of the jar had escaped, one thing only excepted, which lay at the bottom, and that was hope.", "항아리의 내용물은 단 하나만을 남기고 모조리 빠져나간 뒤였으니, 바닥에 남은 그 하나는 바로 희망이었다."], ["So we see at this day, whatever evils are abroad, hope never entirely leaves us; and while we have that, no amount of other ills can make us completely wretched.", "그리하여 오늘날 우리가 보듯, 어떤 악이 세상에 퍼져 있든 희망만은 결코 우리를 온전히 떠나지 않으며, 우리가 그것을 지니고 있는 한 다른 어떤 재앙도 우리를 완전히 비참하게 만들 수는 없다."]], [["Another story is that Pandora was sent in good faith, by Jupiter, to bless man; that she was furnished with a box, containing her marriage presents, into which every god had put some blessing.", "또 다른 이야기에 따르면, 판도라는 제우스가 인간을 축복하고자 선의로 보낸 것이며, 그녀에게는 혼인 선물이 담긴 상자가 주어졌는데, 그 안에는 신들이 저마다 어떤 축복을 넣어 두었다고 한다."], ["She opened the box incautiously, and the blessings all escaped, hope only excepted.", "그녀가 부주의하게 상자를 열자, 축복이 모두 빠져나갔고, 오직 희망만이 남았다."], ["This story seems more probable than the former; for how could hope, so precious a jewel as it is, have been kept in a jar full of all manner of evils, as in the former statement?", "이 이야기가 앞의 것보다 더 그럴듯해 보이는데, 어찌하여 희망처럼 그토록 귀한 보석이, 앞의 진술에서처럼 온갖 종류의 악으로 가득 찬 항아리 속에 담겨 있을 수 있었겠는가."]], [["The world being thus furnished with inhabitants, the first age was an age of innocence and happiness, called the Golden Age.", "이렇게 하여 세계에 거주자가 갖추어지자, 첫 번째 시대는 순수와 행복의 시대였으니, 이를 황금시대라 부른다."], ["Truth and right prevailed, though not enforced by law, nor was there any magistrate to threaten or punish.", "진리와 정의가 널리 행해졌으되, 법으로 강요된 것도 아니었고, 위협하거나 벌하는 어떤 관리가 있는 것도 아니었다."], ["The forest had not yet been robbed of its trees to furnish timbers for vessels, nor had men built fortifications round their towns.", "숲은 아직 배의 목재를 마련하느라 그 나무들을 빼앗긴 적이 없었고, 사람들도 도시 둘레에 요새를 쌓은 적이 없었다."], ["There were no such things as swords, spears, or helmets.", "칼이나 창이나 투구 따위의 것은 존재하지 않았다."], ["The earth brought forth all things necessary for man, without his labor in ploughing or sowing.", "땅은 인간이 밭을 갈거나 씨를 뿌리는 수고 없이도 인간에게 필요한 모든 것을 내주었다."], ["Perpetual spring reigned, flowers sprang up without seed, the rivers flowed with milk and wine, and yellow honey distilled from the oaks.", "영원한 봄이 다스렸고, 꽃은 씨앗 없이도 피어났으며, 강에는 젖과 포도주가 흘렀고, 떡갈나무에서는 노란 꿀이 방울방울 떨어졌다."]], [["Then succeeded the Silver Age, inferior to the golden, but better than that of brass.", "이어 은의 시대가 뒤따랐으니, 황금시대보다는 못하되 청동시대보다는 나았다."], ["Jupiter shortened the spring, and divided the year into seasons.", "제우스는 봄을 짧게 줄이고, 한 해를 계절들로 나누었다."], ["Then, first, men had to endure the extremes of heat and cold, and houses became necessary.", "그리하여 처음으로 인간은 더위와 추위의 극단을 견뎌야 했고, 집이 필요해졌다."], ["Caves were the first dwellings, and leafy coverts of the woods, and huts woven of twigs.", "동굴이 최초의 거처였고, 숲의 잎이 무성한 은신처와 잔가지를 엮어 만든 오두막이 그 뒤를 이었다."], ["Crops would no longer grow without planting.", "작물은 더 이상 심지 않으면 자라지 않게 되었다."], ["The farmer was obliged to sow the seed and the toiling ox to draw the plough.", "농부는 씨를 뿌려야 했고, 힘겹게 일하는 소는 쟁기를 끌어야 했다."]], [["Next came the Brazen Age, more savage of temper, and readier to the strife of arms, yet not altogether wicked.", "다음으로 청동시대가 왔으니, 기질이 더 거칠고 무력의 다툼에 더 쉽게 기울었으되, 아주 사악하지는 않았다."], ["The hardest and worst was the Iron Age.", "가장 모질고 가장 나빴던 것은 철의 시대였다."], ["Crime burst in like a flood; modesty, truth, and honor fled.", "죄악이 홍수처럼 터져 나왔고, 정숙과 진리와 명예가 달아났다."], ["In their places came fraud and cunning, violence, and the wicked love of gain.", "그 자리에 사기와 교활함과 폭력과 사악한 이득의 사랑이 들어섰다."], ["Then seamen spread sails to the wind, and the trees were torn from the mountains to serve for keels to ships, and vex the face of ocean.", "그리하여 뱃사람들이 바람에 돛을 펼쳤고, 나무들은 산에서 뽑혀 배의 용골 노릇을 하며 대양의 얼굴을 괴롭혔다."], ["The earth, which till now had been cultivated in common, began to be divided off into possessions.", "그때까지 공동으로 경작되던 땅이 사유지로 나뉘기 시작했다."], ["Men were not satisfied with what the surface produced, but must dig into its bowels, and draw forth from thence the ores of metals.", "사람들은 표면이 내주는 것에 만족하지 못하고, 그 내장을 파헤쳐 그 안에서 금속의 광석을 캐내야만 했다."], ["Mischievous iron, and more mischievous gold, were produced.", "해로운 철과, 그보다 더 해로운 금이 만들어졌다."], ["War sprang up, using both as weapons; the guest was not safe in his friend’s house; and sons-in-law and fathers-in-law, brothers and sisters, husbands and wives, could not trust one another.", "그 둘을 모두 무기로 쓰는 전쟁이 일어났으니, 손님은 친구의 집에서도 안전하지 못했고, 사위와 장인이, 형제와 자매가, 남편과 아내가 서로를 믿지 못했다."], ["Sons wished their fathers dead, that they might come to the inheritance; family love lay prostrate.", "아들들은 유산을 차지하고자 아비가 죽기를 바랐고, 가족의 사랑은 땅에 엎어졌다."], ["The earth was wet with slaughter, and the gods abandoned it, one by one, till Astræa[6] alone was left, and finally she also took her departure.", "땅은 살육으로 젖었고, 신들은 하나둘 그곳을 버렸으니, 마침내 아스트라이아 홀로 남았다가, 끝내 그녀마저 떠나갔다. * 아스트라이아: 정의의 여신."]], [["Jupiter, seeing this state of things, burned with anger.", "제우스는 이러한 사태를 보고 분노로 불타올랐다."], ["He summoned the gods to council.", "그는 신들을 회의에 소집했다."], ["They obeyed the call, and took the road to the palace of heaven.", "신들은 부름에 응하여, 하늘의 궁전으로 가는 길을 따라 나섰다."], ["The road, which any one may see in a clear night, stretches across the face of the sky, and is called the Milky Way.", "맑은 밤이면 누구나 볼 수 있는 그 길은 하늘의 얼굴을 가로질러 뻗어 있으며, 은하수라 불린다."], ["Along the road stand the palaces of the illustrious gods; the common people of the skies live apart, on either side.", "그 길을 따라 이름난 신들의 궁전이 늘어서 있고, 하늘의 평민들은 그 양옆에 따로 떨어져 산다."], ["Jupiter addressed the assembly.", "제우스는 회의에서 연설했다."], ["He set forth the frightful condition of things on the earth, and closed by announcing his intention to destroy the whole of its inhabitants, and provide a new race, unlike the first, who would be more worthy of life, and much better worshippers of the gods.", "그는 땅 위의 끔찍한 사태를 낱낱이 펼쳐 보였고, 끝으로 그곳의 거주자를 모조리 멸하고, 첫 번째와는 다른 새로운 종족, 곧 삶에 더 합당하고 신들을 훨씬 더 잘 섬길 종족을 마련하겠다는 그의 뜻을 알리며 끝맺었다."], ["So saying he took a thunderbolt, and was about to launch it at the world, and destroy it by burning; but recollecting the danger that such a conflagration might set heaven itself on fire, he changed his plan, and resolved to drown it.", "그렇게 말하며 그는 벼락을 집어 들어 세계를 향해 던져 불태워 멸하려 했으나, 그러한 큰불이 자칫 하늘 자체에 불을 붙일지도 모른다는 위험을 떠올리고는 계획을 바꾸어, 세계를 물에 잠기게 하기로 작정했다."], ["The north wind, which scatters the clouds, was chained up; the south was sent out, and soon covered all the face of heaven with a cloak of pitchy darkness.", "구름을 흩어 버리는 북풍은 사슬에 묶였고, 남풍이 풀려나와 곧 칠흑 같은 어둠의 외투로 하늘의 온 얼굴을 뒤덮었다."], ["The clouds, driven together, resound with a crash; torrents of rain fall; the crops are laid low; the year’s labor of the husbandman perishes in an hour.", "한데로 몰린 구름이 굉음을 내며 울렸고, 빗줄기가 억수로 쏟아졌으며, 작물이 쓰러졌고, 농부의 한 해 수고가 한 시간 만에 사라졌다."], ["Jupiter, not satisfied with his own waters, calls on his brother Neptune to aid him with his.", "제우스는 자신의 물만으로는 만족하지 못하고, 그의 형제 포세이돈[로마의 〈넵투누스〉]을 불러 그의 물로 도와 달라 했다."], ["He lets loose the rivers, and pours them over the land.", "포세이돈은 강들을 풀어놓아 땅 위로 쏟아부었다."], ["At the same time, he heaves the land with an earthquake, and brings in the reflux of the ocean over the shores.", "동시에 그는 지진으로 땅을 들어 올려, 대양의 역류를 해안 위로 끌어들였다."], ["Flocks, herds, men, and houses are swept away, and temples, with their sacred enclosures, profaned.", "가축 떼와 무리와 사람과 집이 휩쓸려 갔고, 신전은 그 성스러운 울타리와 더불어 더럽혀졌다."], ["If any edifice remained standing, it was overwhelmed, and its turrets lay hid beneath the waves.", "어떤 건물이 무너지지 않고 서 있더라도, 그것은 물에 잠겼고, 그 작은 탑들은 물결 아래 숨어 버렸다."], ["Now all was sea, sea without shore.", "이제 온통 바다였으니, 해안 없는 바다였다."], ["Here and there an individual remained on a projecting hill-top, and a few, in boats, pulled the oar where they had lately driven the plough.", "여기저기 튀어나온 언덕 꼭대기에 한 사람씩 남았고, 몇몇은 배를 타고서, 얼마 전까지 쟁기를 몰던 곳에서 노를 저었다."], ["The fishes swim among the tree-tops; the anchor is let down into a garden.", "물고기는 나무 꼭대기 사이를 헤엄쳤고, 닻은 정원 안으로 내려졌다."], ["Where the graceful lambs played but now, unwieldy sea calves gambol.", "방금 전까지 우아한 새끼 양들이 뛰놀던 곳에서, 둔중한 바다표범이 장난을 쳤다."], ["The wolf swims among the sheep, the yellow lions and tigers struggle in the water.", "늑대가 양 떼 사이를 헤엄쳤고, 누런 사자와 호랑이가 물속에서 허우적거렸다."], ["The strength of the wild boar serves him not, nor his swiftness the stag.", "멧돼지의 힘도 그에게 소용이 없었고, 사슴의 빠르기도 마찬가지였다."], ["The birds fall with weary wing into the water, having found no land for a resting-place.", "새들은 쉴 만한 땅을 찾지 못한 채 지친 날개로 물 위에 떨어졌다."], ["Those living beings whom the water spared fell a prey to hunger.", "물이 살려 둔 그 생명들은 굶주림의 먹이가 되었다."]], [["Parnassus alone, of all the mountains, overtopped the waves; and there Deucalion, and his wife Pyrrha, of the race of Prometheus, found refuge—he a just man, and she a faithful worshipper of the gods.", "모든 산 가운데 오직 파르나소스만이 물결 위로 솟아 있었고, 그곳에서 프로메테우스의 혈통인 데우칼리온과 그의 아내 피라가 피난처를 찾았으니 — 그는 의로운 사람이었고, 그녀는 신들을 충실히 섬기는 자였다."], ["Jupiter, when he saw none left alive but this pair, and remembered their harmless lives and pious demeanor, ordered the north winds to drive away the clouds, and disclose the skies to earth, and earth to the skies.", "제우스는 이 한 쌍 외에 살아남은 자가 아무도 없음을 보고, 그들의 해롭지 않은 삶과 경건한 처신을 떠올려, 북풍에게 구름을 몰아내어 땅에게 하늘을, 하늘에게 땅을 드러내라 명했다."], ["Neptune also directed Triton to blow on his shell, and sound a retreat to the waters.", "포세이돈 또한 트리톤에게 명하여 그의 소라고둥을 불어 물들에게 물러나라는 신호를 울리게 했다."], ["The waters obeyed, and the sea returned to its shores, and the rivers to their channels.", "물은 명에 따랐고, 바다는 그 해안으로, 강은 그 물길로 돌아갔다. 그러자 데우칼리온이 피라에게 이렇게 말했다."], ["Then Deucalion thus addressed Pyrrha: “O wife, only surviving woman, joined to me first by the ties of kindred and marriage, and now by a common danger, would that we possessed the power of our ancestor Prometheus, and could renew the race as he at first made it!", "\"오 아내여, 유일하게 살아남은 여인이여, 처음에는 혈연과 혼인의 끈으로, 이제는 함께 겪은 위험으로 나와 맺어진 그대여, 우리에게 우리의 선조 프로메테우스의 힘이 있어, 그가 처음 종족을 만들었던 것처럼 우리도 종족을 새로 만들 수 있다면 좋으련만!"], ["But as we cannot, let us seek yonder temple, and inquire of the gods what remains for us to do.”", "그러나 우리에게 그럴 힘이 없으니, 저기 저 신전을 찾아가, 우리가 무엇을 해야 할지 신들에게 여쭙도록 합시다.\""], ["They entered the temple, deformed as it was with slime, and approached the altar, where no fire burned.", "그들은 진흙으로 흉하게 더럽혀진 신전 안으로 들어가, 불이 타지 않는 제단으로 다가갔다."], ["There they fell prostrate on the earth, and prayed the goddess to inform them how they might retrieve their miserable affairs.", "그곳에서 그들은 땅에 엎드려, 그들의 비참한 처지를 어떻게 되돌릴 수 있을지 알려 달라고 여신에게 기도했다. 신탁이 답했다."], ["The oracle answered, “Depart from the temple with head veiled and garments unbound, and cast behind you the bones of your mother.”", "\"머리를 가리고 옷을 풀어 헤친 채 신전을 떠나, 너희 어머니의 뼈를 너희 등 뒤로 던져라.\" 그들은 그 말을 듣고 놀랐다."], ["They heard the words with astonishment.", "피라가 먼저 침묵을 깨뜨렸다."], ["Pyrrha first broke silence: “We cannot obey; we dare not profane the remains of our parents.”", "\"우리는 따를 수 없습니다. 우리는 감히 부모의 유해를 더럽힐 수 없습니다.\""], ["They sought the thickest shades of the wood, and revolved the oracle in their minds.", "그들은 숲의 가장 짙은 그늘을 찾아가, 그 신탁을 마음속에서 곱씹었다. 마침내 데우칼리온이 입을 열었다."], ["At length Deucalion spoke: “Either my sagacity deceives me, or the command is one we may obey without impiety.", "\"내 영민함이 나를 속이는 것이 아니라면, 그 명은 우리가 불경을 저지르지 않고도 따를 수 있는 것이오."], ["The earth is the great parent of all; the stones are her bones; these we may cast behind us; and I think this is what the oracle means.", "땅은 만물의 위대한 어머니요, 돌은 그녀의 뼈이니, 이것을 우리가 등 뒤로 던질 수 있을 것이며, 나는 이것이 신탁의 뜻이라 생각하오."], ["At least, it will do no harm to try.”", "적어도 한번 해 본다고 해될 것은 없소.\""], ["They veiled their faces, unbound their garments, and picked up stones, and cast them behind them.", "그들은 얼굴을 가리고 옷을 풀어 헤친 채 돌을 주워 등 뒤로 던졌다."], ["The stones (wonderful to relate) began to grow soft, and assume shape.", "그 돌들은(이야기하기에 놀라운 일이지만) 물러지기 시작하더니 형태를 갖추기 시작했다."], ["By degrees, they put on a rude resemblance to the human form, like a block half-finished in the hands of the sculptor.", "차츰 그것들은 조각가의 손에서 절반쯤 다듬어진 덩어리처럼, 인간의 형상을 어렴풋이 닮아 갔다."], ["The moisture and slime that were about them became flesh; the stony part became bones; the veins remained veins, retaining their name, only changing their use.", "그 주위에 있던 물기와 진흙은 살이 되었고, 돌의 부분은 뼈가 되었으며, 핏줄은 그 이름을 그대로 지닌 채 핏줄로 남았으되, 다만 그 쓰임만 바뀌었다."], ["Those thrown by the hand of the man became men, and those by the woman became women.", "남자의 손으로 던진 것은 남자가 되었고, 여자가 던진 것은 여자가 되었다."], ["It was a hard race, and well adapted to labor, as we find ourselves to be at this day, giving plain indications of our origin.", "그것은 모진 종족이었고, 노동에 잘 맞았으니, 오늘날 우리 자신이 그러하여 우리의 기원을 분명히 드러내고 있는 것과 같다."]], [["The comparison of Eve to Pandora is too obvious to have escaped Milton, who introduces it in Book IV. of “Paradise-Lost”:", "이브를 판도라에 견주는 것은 너무도 명백하여 밀턴이 놓칠 리 없었으니, 그는 『실낙원』 제4권에서 이를 끌어들이고 있다."]], [["“More lovely than Pandora, whom the gods Endowed with all their gifts; and O, too like In sad event, when to the unwiser son Of Japhet brought by Hermes, she insnared Mankind with her fair looks, to be avenged On him who had stole Jove’s authentic fire.”", "\"판도라보다도 더 사랑스러운 그녀, 신들이 그들의 온갖 선물로 채워 준 판도라, 아아, 그 슬픈 결말에서도 너무나 닮았으니, 헤르메스가 데려와 이아페토스의 더 어리석은 아들에게 보냈을 때, 그녀는 그 고운 자태로 인류를 옭아매어, 유피테르의 진정한 불을 훔친 자에게 앙갚음하였도다.\""]], [["Prometheus and Epimetheus were sons of Iapetus, which Milton changes to Japhet.", "프로메테우스와 에피메테우스는 이아페토스의 아들들이었는데, 밀턴은 이를 야벳[야펫]으로 바꾸어 적었다."]], [["Prometheus has been a favorite subject with the poets.", "프로메테우스는 시인들이 즐겨 다루는 소재였다."], ["He is represented as the friend of mankind, who interposed in their behalf when Jove was incensed against them, and who taught them civilization and the arts.", "그는 인류의 벗으로 그려지니, 유피테르가 인류에게 노여워했을 때 그들을 위해 나섰고, 그들에게 문명과 기예를 가르쳤다."], ["But as, in so doing, he transgressed the will of Jupiter, he drew down on himself the anger of the ruler of gods and men.", "그러나 그렇게 함으로써 그는 제우스의 뜻을 거슬렀던 까닭에, 신들과 인간의 지배자의 노여움을 자신에게 끌어들였다."], ["Jupiter had him chained to a rock on Mount Caucasus, where a vulture preyed on his liver, which was renewed as fast as devoured.", "제우스는 그를 카우카소스 산의 바위에 사슬로 묶었고, 그곳에서 독수리 한 마리가 그의 간을 쪼아 먹었는데, 그 간은 먹히는 즉시 다시 돋아났다."], ["This state of torment might have been brought to an end at any time by Prometheus, if he had been willing to submit to his oppressor; for he possessed a secret which involved the stability of Jove’s throne, and if he would have revealed it, he might have been at once taken into favor.", "이 고통의 상태는 프로메테우스가 그의 압제자에게 굴복하기만 한다면 언제든 끝날 수 있었으니, 그는 제우스의 옥좌의 안정과 관련된 비밀을 지니고 있었던 까닭에, 만일 그것을 밝히려 했다면 곧바로 총애를 받았을 것이다."], ["But that he disdained to do.", "그러나 그는 그렇게 하기를 경멸했다."], ["He has therefore become the symbol of magnanimous endurance of unmerited suffering, and strength of will resisting oppression.", "그리하여 그는 받아 마땅하지 않은 고통을 의연히 견디는 것과, 압제에 맞서는 의지의 굳셈을 상징하는 존재가 되었다."]], [["Byron and Shelley have both treated this theme.", "바이런과 셸리 모두 이 주제를 다룬 바 있다."], ["The following are Byron’s lines:", "다음은 바이런의 시구다."]], [["“Titan!", "\"티탄이여!"], ["to whose immortal eyes The sufferings of mortality, Seen in their sad reality, Were not as things that gods despise; What was thy pity’s recompense?", "그대의 불멸하는 눈에는 필멸하는 자들의 고통이, 그 슬픈 실상 그대로 보였으되, 신들이 업신여기는 것들로 보이지 않았도다. 그대의 연민이 받은 보답은 무엇이었던가?"], ["A silent suffering, and intense; The rock, the vulture, and the chain; All that the proud can feel of pain; The agony they do not show; The suffocating sense of woe.", "소리 없는, 그러나 격렬한 고통, 바위와 독수리와 사슬, 오만한 자가 느낄 수 있는 모든 아픔, 그들이 내보이지 않는 그 고뇌, 숨 막히는 비탄의 감각이었도다."]], [["“Thy godlike crime was to be kind; To render with thy precepts less The sum of human wretchedness, And strengthen man with his own mind.", "\"그대의 신과도 같은 죄는 친절을 베푼 것이었으니, 그대의 가르침으로 인간의 비참함의 총량을 덜어 주고, 인간을 그 자신의 정신으로 굳세게 한 것이었도다."], ["And, baffled as thou wert from high, Still, in thy patient energy In the endurance and repulse Of thine impenetrable spirit, Which earth and heaven could not convulse, A mighty lesson we inherit.”", "그리고 그대가 위로부터 좌절당했을지언정, 여전히, 그대의 참을성 있는 기력 속에서, 그대의 헤아릴 수 없는 정신의 인내와 거부 속에서, 땅도 하늘도 뒤흔들 수 없었던 그 정신 속에서, 우리는 위대한 교훈을 물려받았도다.\""]], [["Byron also employs the same allusion, in his “Ode to Napoleon Bonaparte”:", "바이런은 또한 그의 「나폴레옹 보나파르트에게 바치는 송가」에서도 같은 인유를 쓰고 있다."]], [["“Or, like the thief of fire from heaven, Wilt thou withstand the shock?", "\"아니면, 하늘에서 불을 훔친 그 도둑처럼, 그대는 그 충격을 견뎌 내려는가?"], ["And share with him—the unforgiven— His vulture and his rock?”", "그리고 그와 함께 — 용서받지 못한 그와 함께 — 그의 독수리와 그의 바위를 나누려는가?\""]], [["————", "————"]], [["# APOLLO AND DAPHNE—PYRAMUS AND THISBE—CEPHALUS AND PROCRIS", "# APOLLO AND DAPHNE—PYRAMUS AND THISBE—CEPHALUS AND PROCRIS"]], [["THE slime with which the earth was covered by the waters of the flood produced an excessive fertility, which called forth every variety of production, both bad and good.", "홍수의 물이 대지를 뒤덮은 뒤 남긴 진흙은 지나친 비옥함을 자아냈으니, 그것은 좋은 것이든 나쁜 것이든 온갖 종류의 생물을 불러내었다."], ["Among the rest, Python, an enormous serpent, crept forth, the terror of the people, and lurked in the caves of Mount Parnassus.", "그 가운데서도 거대한 뱀인 피톤이 기어 나와 사람들에게 공포가 되었으며, 파르나소스산의 동굴 속에 숨어 살았다."], ["Apollo slew him with his arrows—weapons which he had not before used against any but feeble animals, hares, wild goats, and such game.", "아폴론은 자신의 화살로 그것을 죽였는데, 그때까지 그 무기는 토끼나 들염소 같은 연약한 짐승이나 사냥감에게만 사용하던 것이었다."], ["In commemoration of this illustrious conquest he instituted the Pythian games, in which the victor in feats of strength, swiftness of foot, or in the chariot race was crowned with a wreath of beech leaves; for the laurel was not yet adopted by Apollo as his own tree.", "이 빛나는 승리를 기념하여 그는 피티아 경기를 창설하였으니, 여기서 힘이나 발의 빠름, 혹은 전차 경주에서 승리한 자는 너도밤나무 잎으로 엮은 화관을 받았다. 월계수가 아직 아폴론 자신의 나무로 채택되기 전이었기 때문이다."]], [["The famous statue of Apollo called the Belvedere represents the god after this victory over the serpent Python.", "벨베데레라고 불리는 저 유명한 아폴론 조각상은 뱀 피톤을 물리친 뒤의 신의 모습을 나타낸 것이다."], ["To this Byron alludes in his “Childe Harold,” iv., 161:", "바이런은 그의 『차일드 해럴드』 제4권 161연에서 이를 언급하고 있다."]], [["“. .", "\"……빗나가지 않는 활의 주인, 생명과 시와 빛의 신, 인간의 사지를 두르고, 그 이마는 싸움의 승리로 온통 빛나는 태양."], [". The lord of the unerring bow, The god of life, and poetry, and light, The Sun, in human limbs arrayed, and brow All radiant from his triumph in the fight.", "화살은 막 쏘아졌으니, 그 빛나는 살은 불멸자의 복수를 담았고, 그의 눈과 콧구멍에는 아름다운 경멸이, 또 힘과 위엄이 온전한 번개를 번뜩이며 스쳐 가니, 그 한 번의 눈길에 신성이 드러나는도다.\""], ["The shaft has just been shot; the arrow bright With an immortal’s vengeance; in his eye And nostril, beautiful disdain, and might And majesty flash their full lightnings by, Developing in that one glance the Deity.”", "아폴론과 다프네"]], [["# APOLLO AND DAPHNE", "# APOLLO AND DAPHNE"]], [["Daphne was Apollo’s first love.", "다프네는 아폴론의 첫사랑이었다."], ["It was not brought about by accident, but by the malice of Cupid.", "그것은 우연으로 이루어진 일이 아니라, 에로스[로마의 〈쿠피도〉]의 심술 때문이었다."], ["Apollo saw the boy playing with his bow and arrows; and being himself elated with his recent victory over Python, he said to him, “What have you to do with warlike weapons, saucy boy?", "아폴론은 그 소년이 활과 화살을 가지고 노는 것을 보았는데, 자신은 최근 피톤을 물리친 승리로 한껏 우쭐해져 그에게 말하였다. \"건방진 녀석아, 너 따위가 전쟁의 무기를 가지고 무엇을 하려느냐?"], ["Leave them for hands worthy of them.", "그것은 그럴 자격이 있는 손에게 맡겨 두어라."], ["Behold the conquest I have won by means of them over the vast serpent who stretched his poisonous body over acres of the plain!", "보아라, 넓디넓은 들판에 독을 품은 몸을 뻗치고 있던 저 거대한 뱀을 내가 이것으로 정복하지 않았느냐! 얘야, 너는 네 횃불로나 만족하여라."], ["Be content with your torch, child, and kindle up your flames, as you call them, where you will, but presume not to meddle with my weapons.”", "네가 말하는 그 불꽃을 어디든 네 마음대로 일으키되, 내 무기에는 감히 손대지 말아라.\" 아프로디테[로마의 〈베누스〉]의 아들은 이 말을 듣고 이렇게 응수하였다."], ["Venus’s boy heard these words, and rejoined, “Your arrows may strike all things else, Apollo, but mine shall strike you.”", "\"아폴론, 당신의 화살은 다른 모든 것을 맞힐지 몰라도, 내 화살은 당신을 맞히고 말 것이오.\""], ["So saying, he took his stand on a rock of Parnassus, and drew from his quiver two arrows of different workmanship, one to excite love, the other to repel it.", "그렇게 말하고 그는 파르나소스산의 바위 위에 올라서서 화살통에서 서로 다르게 만들어진 두 개의 화살을 꺼냈는데, 하나는 사랑을 불러일으키는 것이요, 다른 하나는 사랑을 물리치는 것이었다. 앞의 것은 황금으로 되어 끝이 날카로웠고, 뒤의 것은 무디며 끝이 납으로 되어 있었다."], ["The former was of gold and sharp pointed, the latter blunt and tipped with lead.", "그는 납으로 된 화살로 강의 신 페네이오스의 딸인 요정 다프네를 쏘았고, 황금 화살로는 아폴론의 심장을 꿰뚫었다."], ["With the leaden shaft he struck the nymph Daphne, the daughter of the river god Peneus, and with the golden one Apollo, through the heart.", "그러자 신은 즉시 그 처녀를 향한 사랑에 사로잡혔으나, 그녀는 사랑한다는 생각만으로도 혐오감을 느꼈다. 그녀의 기쁨은 숲속의 놀이와 사냥의 전리품에 있었다."], ["Forthwith the god was seized with love for the maiden, and she abhorred the thought of loving. Her delight was in woodland sports and in the spoils of the chase.", "많은 구혼자들이 그녀를 찾았으나 그녀는 그들 모두를 물리치며 숲속을 거닐었고, 에로스에 대해서도 결혼의 신 히멘에 대해서도 아무런 생각이 없었다."], ["Many lovers sought her, but she spurned them all, ranging the woods, and taking no thought of Cupid nor of Hymen.", "아버지는 종종 그녀에게 말하곤 했다. \"딸아, 너는 내게 사위를 안겨 주어야 하고, 손주를 안겨 주어야 한다.\""], ["Her father often said to her, “Daughter, you owe me a son-in-law; you owe me grandchildren.”", "그녀는 결혼이라는 생각을 죄악처럼 미워하며, 아름다운 얼굴을 온통 발갛게 붉힌 채 아버지의 목을 끌어안고 말하였다."], ["She, hating the thought of marriage as a crime, with her beautiful face tinged all over with blushes, threw her arms around her father’s neck, and said, “Dearest father, grant me this favor, that I may always remain unmarried, like Diana.”", "\"사랑하는 아버지, 제게 이 한 가지 청을 들어주세요. 아르테미스[로마의 〈디아나〉]처럼 언제까지나 결혼하지 않고 지내게 해 주세요.\""], ["He consented, but at the same time said, “Your own face will forbid it.”", "아버지는 이를 허락하였으나, 동시에 이렇게 말하였다. \"네 얼굴이 그것을 허락하지 않을 게다.\""]], [["Apollo loved her, and longed to obtain her; and he who gives oracles to all the world was not wise enough to look into his own fortunes.", "아폴론은 그녀를 사랑하여 그녀를 얻기를 갈망하였다. 하지만 온 세상에 신탁을 내리는 그도 자신의 운명을 들여다볼 만큼 현명하지는 못하였다."], ["He saw her hair flung loose over her shoulders, and said, “If so charming in disorder, what would it be if arranged?”", "그는 그녀의 머리카락이 어깨 위로 풀어 헤쳐진 것을 보고 말하였다. \"흐트러져 있어도 저토록 매혹적인데, 단정히 빗으면 어떠하겠는가?\""], ["He saw her eyes bright as stars; he saw her lips, and was not satisfied with only seeing them.", "그는 별처럼 빛나는 그녀의 눈을 보았고, 그녀의 입술을 보았으나 그저 보는 것만으로는 만족하지 못하였다."], ["He admired her hands and arms, naked to the shoulder, and whatever was hidden from view he imagined more beautiful still.", "그는 어깨까지 드러난 그녀의 손과 팔을 흠모하였으며, 눈에 보이지 않는 것은 더더욱 아름다우리라 상상하였다."], ["He followed her; she fled, swifter than the wind, and delayed not a moment at his entreaties.", "그가 뒤따르자 그녀는 바람보다도 빠르게 달아났고, 그의 간청에도 한순간도 멈추지 않았다."], ["“Stay,”", "\"멈추어라.\""], ["said he, “daughter of Peneus; I am not a foe.", "그가 말하였다. \"페네이오스의 딸이여, 나는 적이 아니다."], ["Do not fly me as a lamb flies the wolf, or a dove the hawk.", "새끼 양이 늑대를 피하듯, 비둘기가 매를 피하듯 나를 피해 달아나지 말아라."], ["It is for love I pursue you.", "내가 너를 쫓는 것은 사랑 때문이다."], ["You make me miserable, for fear you should fall and hurt yourself on these stones, and I should be the cause.", "네가 이 돌부리에 걸려 넘어져 다치기라도 할까, 그래서 그 원인이 내가 될까 두려워 나는 비참하구나."], ["Pray run slower, and I will follow slower.", "부디 천천히 달려라, 그러면 나도 천천히 뒤따르마."], ["I am no clown, no rude peasant.", "나는 어릿광대도 아니요, 무례한 시골뜨기도 아니다."], ["Jupiter is my father, and I am lord of Delphos and Tenedos, and know all things, present and future.", "제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]가 내 아버지이며, 나는 델포스와 테네도스의 주인으로, 현재와 미래의 모든 것을 안다."], ["I am the god of song and the lyre.", "나는 노래와 리라의 신이다."], ["My arrows fly true to the mark; but, alas!", "내 화살은 과녁을 정확히 맞힌다. 그러나 아아!"], ["an arrow more fatal than mine has pierced my heart!", "내 것보다 더 치명적인 화살이 내 심장을 꿰뚫었구나!"], ["I am the god of medicine, and know the virtues of all healing plants.", "나는 의술의 신으로 온갖 약초의 효능을 안다. 그러나 아아!"], ["Alas! I suffer a malady that no balm can cure!”", "어떤 향유로도 고칠 수 없는 병을 나는 앓고 있구나!\""]], [["The nymph continued her flight, and left his plea half uttered.", "요정은 계속 달아나 그의 호소를 절반밖에 듣지 못한 채 남겨 두었다."], ["And even as she fled she charmed him.", "그리고 달아나는 그 모습조차 그를 매혹하였다."], ["The wind blew her garments, and her unbound hair streamed loose behind her.", "바람이 그녀의 옷자락을 날리고, 묶지 않은 머리카락이 뒤로 풀려 흩날렸다."], ["The god grew impatient to find his wooings thrown away, and, sped by Cupid, gained upon her in the race.", "신은 자신의 구애가 헛되이 버려지는 것에 조바심이 났고, 에로스의 부추김을 받아 추격에서 그녀에게 점점 가까워졌다."], ["It was like a hound pursuing a hare, with open jaws ready to seize, while the feebler animal darts forward, slipping from the very grasp.", "그것은 마치 사냥개가 토끼를 쫓는 것과 같아서, 입을 벌리고 막 붙잡으려 하지만 더 약한 짐승은 앞으로 내달아 바로 그 손아귀에서 빠져나간다."], ["So flew the god and the virgin—he on the wings of love, and she on those of fear.", "신과 처녀도 그렇게 달렸으니, 그는 사랑의 날개를 달고, 그녀는 두려움의 날개를 달고 있었다."], ["The pursuer is the more rapid, however, and gains upon her, and his panting breath blows upon her hair.", "그러나 쫓는 자가 더 빨라 그녀에게 다가갔고, 그의 헐떡이는 숨결이 그녀의 머리카락에 닿았다."], ["Her strength begins to fail, and, ready to sink, she calls upon her father, the river god: “Help me, Peneus!", "그녀의 힘이 다하기 시작하더니, 막 쓰러지려는 순간 그녀는 아버지인 강의 신을 불렀다. \"도와주세요, 페네이오스여!"], ["open the earth to enclose me, or change my form, which has brought me into this danger!”", "땅을 열어 저를 가두거나, 저를 이 위험에 빠뜨린 이 모습을 바꾸어 주세요!\""], ["Scarcely had she spoken, when a stiffness seized all her limbs; her bosom began to be enclosed in a tender bark; her hair became leaves; her arms became branches; her foot stuck fast in the ground, as a root; her face became a tree-top, retaining nothing of its former self but its beauty.", "그녀가 채 말을 마치기도 전에 뻣뻣함이 그녀의 온 사지를 사로잡았다. 그녀의 가슴은 부드러운 나무껍질에 둘러싸이기 시작하였고, 머리카락은 잎사귀가 되었으며, 두 팔은 가지가 되었고, 발은 뿌리가 되어 땅에 단단히 박혔으며, 얼굴은 나무 꼭대기가 되어 그 본래의 모습 가운데 아름다움 외에는 아무것도 남기지 않았다."], ["Apollo stood amazed.", "아폴론은 놀라움에 사로잡혀 섰다."], ["He touched the stem, and felt the flesh tremble under the new bark.", "그가 줄기를 만지니 새 껍질 아래에서 살갗이 떨리는 것이 느껴졌다."], ["He embraced the branches, and lavished kisses on the wood.", "그가 가지를 끌어안고 나무에 입맞춤을 퍼부으니, 가지는 그의 입술을 피하여 움츠러들었다."], ["The branches shrank from his lips.", "\"그대가 내 아내가 될 수 없다면,\" 그가 말하였다."], ["“Since you cannot be my wife,”", "\"그대는 반드시 내 나무가 되리라."], ["said he, “you shall assuredly be my tree.", "나는 그대를 내 화관으로 쓰리라."], ["I will wear you for my crown; I will decorate with you my harp and my quiver; and when the great Roman conquerors lead up the triumphal pomp to the Capitol, you shall be woven into wreaths for their brows.", "그대로 내 하프와 화살통을 장식하리라. 그리고 위대한 로마의 정복자들이 카피톨리움으로 개선의 행렬을 이끌고 올라갈 때, 그대는 그들의 이마를 위한 화관으로 엮이리라."], ["And, as eternal youth is mine, you also shall be always green, and your leaf know no decay.”", "또 영원한 젊음이 내 것이듯, 그대 역시 언제나 푸르를 것이며, 그대의 잎은 시드는 법을 알지 못하리라.\""], ["The nymph, now changed into a Laurel tree, bowed its head in grateful acknowledgment.", "이제 월계수로 변한 요정은 감사의 마음으로 그 머리를 숙였다."]], [["That Apollo should be the god both of music and poetry will not appear strange, but that medicine should also be assigned to his province, may.", "아폴론이 음악과 시의 신이라는 것은 이상하게 여겨지지 않겠으나, 의술 또한 그의 영역으로 주어졌다는 것은 이상하게 보일 수 있다."], ["The poet Armstrong, himself a physician, thus accounts for it:", "시인 암스트롱은 자신 또한 의사였던바, 이를 다음과 같이 설명한다."]], [["“Music exalts each joy, allays each grief, Expels diseases, softens every pain; And hence the wise of ancient days adored One power of physic, melody, and song.”", "\"음악은 모든 기쁨을 드높이고 모든 슬픔을 달래며, 질병을 몰아내고 온갖 고통을 부드럽게 하나니, 그리하여 옛 시절의 현인들은 떠받들었도다, 의술과 가락과 노래의 한 권능을.\""]], [["The story of Apollo and Daphne is often alluded to by the poets.", "아폴론과 다프네의 이야기는 시인들에 의해 자주 언급된다."], ["Waller applies it to the case of one whose amatory verses, though they did not soften the heart of his mistress, yet won for the poet wide-spread fame:", "월러는 이를, 비록 그 연애시가 연인의 마음을 부드럽게 하지는 못하였으나 시인에게 널리 퍼진 명성을 안겨 준 어느 인물의 경우에 적용하고 있다."]], [["“Yet what he sung in his immortal strain, Though unsuccessful, was not sung in vain.", "\"허나 그가 불멸의 가락으로 노래한 것은, 비록 성공하지 못했으나, 헛되이 노래한 것이 아니었다."], ["All but the nymph that should redress his wrong, Attend his passion and approve his song.", "그의 잘못을 바로잡았어야 할 그 요정만 빼고, 모두가 그의 열정에 귀 기울이고 그의 노래를 칭송하였다."], ["Like Phœbus thus, acquiring unsought praise, He caught at love and filled his arms with bays.”", "이리하여 포이보스처럼, 구하지 않은 찬사를 얻으며, 그는 사랑을 붙잡으려다 두 팔에 월계수를 가득 안았다.\""]], [["The following stanza from Shelley’s “Adonais” alludes to Byron’s early quarrel with the reviewers:", "다음은 셸리의 『아도네이스』에 나오는 한 연으로, 바이런이 초기에 비평가들과 벌인 다툼을 가리킨다."]], [["“The herded wolves, bold only to pursue; The obscene ravens, clamorous o’er the dead; The vultures, to the conqueror’s banner true, Who feed where Desolation first has fed, And whose wings rain contagion: how they fled, When like Apollo, from his golden bow, The Pythian of the age one arrow sped And smiled!", "\"오로지 뒤쫓는 데에만 대담한 무리 진 늑대들, 주검 위에서 떠들어 대는 추악한 까마귀들, 정복자의 깃발에 충성하는 독수리들, 황폐가 먼저 먹은 자리에서 먹이를 취하며 그 날개가 역병을 퍼붓는 그것들이, 어찌 달아났던가, 아폴론처럼, 황금 활에서, 이 시대의 피티아가 화살 하나를 쏘아 보내며 미소 지었을 때!"], ["The spoilers tempt no second blow; They fawn on the proud feet that spurn them as they go.”", "약탈자들은 두 번째 일격을 청하지 못하고, 저들을 차 버리고 가는 그 도도한 발에 아첨하며 따른다.\" 피라모스와 티스베"]], [["# PYRAMUS AND THISBE", "# PYRAMUS AND THISBE"]], [["Pyramus was the handsomest youth, and Thisbe the fairest maiden, in all Babylonia, where Semiramis reigned.", "피라모스는 가장 잘생긴 청년이었고, 티스베는 세미라미스가 다스리던 온 바빌로니아에서 가장 아름다운 처녀였다."], ["Their parents occupied adjoining houses; and neighborhood brought the young people together, and acquaintance ripened into love.", "그들의 부모는 이웃한 집에서 살았으니, 이웃하여 지내다 보니 두 젊은이가 한데 모이게 되었고, 알고 지내던 사이가 사랑으로 무르익었다."], ["They would gladly have married, but their parents forbade.", "그들은 기꺼이 결혼하고자 하였으나 부모가 이를 금하였다."], ["One thing, however, they could not forbid—that love should glow with equal ardor in the bosoms of both.", "그러나 한 가지만은 부모도 금할 수 없었으니, 두 사람의 가슴속에서 사랑이 똑같이 뜨겁게 타오르는 것이었다."], ["They conversed by signs and glances, and the fire burned more intensely for being covered up.", "그들은 몸짓과 눈빛으로 마음을 주고받았으며, 그 불길은 가려져 있을수록 더욱 격렬하게 타올랐다."], ["In the wall that parted the two houses there was a crack, caused by some fault in the structure.", "두 집을 가르는 담벼락에는 구조상의 어떤 흠 때문에 생긴 틈이 하나 있었다."], ["No one had remarked it before, but the lovers discovered it.", "그전에는 아무도 그것을 눈여겨보지 않았으나, 두 연인이 그것을 발견하였다."], ["What will not love discover!", "사랑이 발견하지 못할 것이 무엇이랴!"], ["It afforded a passage to the voice; and tender messages used to pass backward and forward through the gap.", "그 틈은 목소리가 지나갈 길을 내주었으니, 다정한 말들이 그 틈을 통해 오가곤 하였다."], ["As they stood, Pyramus on this side, Thisbe on that, their breaths would mingle.", "그들이 서로 마주 서서, 피라모스는 이쪽에 티스베는 저쪽에 서면 두 사람의 숨결이 한데 섞이곤 하였다."], ["“Cruel wall,”", "\"잔인한 담이여,\""], ["they said, “why do you keep two lovers apart?", "그들은 말하였다. \"어찌하여 너는 두 연인을 갈라놓는가?"], ["But we will not be ungrateful.", "그러나 우리는 은혜를 모르지는 않으리라."], ["We owe you, we confess, the privilege of transmitting loving words to willing ears.”", "우리는 인정하노니, 사랑의 말을 기꺼이 들으려는 귀에 전해 주는 은혜를 우리는 네게 입었구나.\""], ["Such words they uttered on different sides of the wall; and when night came and they must say farewell, they pressed their lips upon the wall, she on her side, he on his, as they could come no nearer.", "그들은 담벼락 양쪽에서 이러한 말을 주고받았다. 그리고 밤이 와서 작별을 고해야 할 때면, 그들은 더는 가까이 다가갈 수 없었으므로 담벼락에 입술을 갖다 대었으니, 그녀는 그녀 쪽에서, 그는 그의 쪽에서 그러하였다."]], [["Next morning, when Aurora had put out the stars, and the sun had melted the frost from the grass, they met at the accustomed spot.", "이튿날 아침, 에오스[로마의 〈아우로라〉]가 별들을 거두고 해가 풀잎의 서리를 녹였을 때, 그들은 늘 만나던 곳에서 만났다."], ["Then, after lamenting their hard fate, they agreed that next night, when all was still, they would slip away from watchful eyes, leave their dwellings and walk out into the fields; and to insure a meeting, repair to a well-known edifice standing without the city’s bounds, called the Tomb of Ninus, and that the one who came first should await the other at the foot of a certain tree.", "그러고는 자신들의 모진 운명을 한탄한 뒤, 그들은 다음 날 밤 만물이 고요해지면 지켜보는 눈을 피해 빠져나와 집을 떠나 들판으로 걸어 나가기로 합의하였다. 그리고 만남을 확실히 하기 위해, 도시의 경계 밖에 서 있는 니노스의 무덤이라 불리는 잘 알려진 건축물로 가되, 먼저 도착한 이가 어떤 나무 아래에서 다른 이를 기다리기로 하였다."], ["It was a white mulberry tree, and stood near a cool spring.", "그것은 흰 뽕나무였으며, 시원한 샘 가까이에 서 있었다."], ["All was agreed on, and they waited impatiently for the sun to go down beneath the waters and night to rise up from them.", "모든 것이 합의되었고, 그들은 해가 물 밑으로 가라앉고 그 물에서 밤이 솟아오르기를 애타게 기다렸다."], ["Then cautiously Thisbe stole forth, unobserved by the family, her head covered with a veil, made her way to the monument and sat down under the tree.", "그리하여 티스베는 조심스럽게, 가족의 눈에 띄지 않은 채 머리에 베일을 쓰고 몰래 빠져나와 그 무덤으로 가서 나무 아래에 앉았다."], ["As she sat alone in the dim light of the evening she descried a lioness, her jaws reeking with recent slaughter, approaching the fountain to slake her thirst.", "저녁의 어스름한 빛 속에 홀로 앉아 있던 그녀는 암사자 한 마리가 갓 잡은 먹이로 입에서 김이 무럭무럭 나는 채로 갈증을 풀러 샘으로 다가오는 것을 발견하였다."], ["Thisbe fled at the sight, and sought refuge in the hollow of a rock.", "티스베는 그 광경에 달아나 바위의 움푹한 곳으로 몸을 피하였다."], ["As she fled she dropped her veil.", "달아나는 동안 그녀는 베일을 떨어뜨렸다."], ["The lioness after drinking at the spring turned to retreat to the woods, and seeing the veil on the ground, tossed and rent it with her bloody mouth.", "암사자는 샘에서 물을 마신 뒤 숲으로 물러나려 돌아서다가 땅에 떨어진 베일을 보고는 피 묻은 입으로 그것을 흔들어 찢어 놓았다."]], [["Pyramus, having been delayed, now approached the place of meeting.", "뒤늦게 출발한 피라모스는 이제 만나기로 한 장소에 다가갔다."], ["He saw in the sand the footsteps of the lion, and the color fled from his cheeks at the sight.", "그는 모래 위에서 사자의 발자국을 보았고, 그 광경에 두 뺨에서 핏기가 가셨다."], ["Presently he found the veil all rent and bloody.", "곧이어 그는 온통 찢기고 피 묻은 베일을 발견하였다."], ["“O hapless girl,”", "\"오, 가엾은 소녀여,\""], ["said he, “I have been the cause of thy death!", "그가 말하였다. \"내가 그대 죽음의 원인이 되었구나!"], ["Thou, more worthy of life than I, hast fallen the first victim. I will follow.", "나보다 더 살 자격이 있는 그대가 첫 번째 희생자로 쓰러졌으니, 내가 뒤따르리라."], ["I am the guilty cause, in tempting thee forth to a place of such peril, and not being myself on the spot to guard thee.", "이토록 위험한 곳으로 그대를 꾀어 내고서 정작 나 자신은 그대를 지키려 그 자리에 있지 못하였으니, 죄지은 원인은 바로 나로다."], ["Come forth, ye lions, from the rocks, and tear this guilty body with your teeth.”", "바위에서 나오너라, 사자들이여, 이 죄지은 몸을 너희 이빨로 찢어 발기어라.\""], ["He took up the veil, carried it with him to the appointed tree, and covered it with kisses and with tears.", "그는 베일을 집어 들어 약속한 나무로 가져가서는 입맞춤과 눈물로 그것을 적셨다."], ["“My blood also shall stain your texture,”", "\"내 피 또한 그대의 천을 물들이리라.\""], ["said he, and drawing his sword plunged it into his heart.", "그가 말하며 칼을 뽑아 자신의 심장에 찔러 넣었다."], ["The blood spurted from the wound, and tinged the white mulberries of the tree all red; and sinking into the earth reached the roots, so that the red color mounted through the trunk to the fruit.", "상처에서 피가 솟구쳐 나무의 흰 오디를 온통 붉게 물들였고, 땅속으로 스며들어 뿌리에 이르렀으니, 그 붉은 빛이 줄기를 타고 올라가 열매에까지 미쳤다."]], [["By this time Thisbe, still trembling with fear, yet wishing not to disappoint her lover, stepped cautiously forth, looking anxiously for the youth, eager to tell him the danger she had escaped.", "이 무렵 티스베는 여전히 두려움에 떨면서도 연인을 실망시키고 싶지 않아, 자신이 모면한 위험을 그에게 어서 알리고 싶은 마음에 그 청년을 애타게 찾으며 조심스럽게 걸어 나왔다."], ["When she came to the spot and saw the changed color of the mulberries she doubted whether it was the same place.", "그곳에 이르러 오디의 변한 빛깔을 보고 그녀는 이곳이 같은 장소인지 의심하였다."], ["While she hesitated she saw the form of one struggling in the agonies of death.", "그녀가 머뭇거리는 동안 그녀는 죽음의 고통 속에 몸부림치는 한 사람의 형상을 보았다."], ["She started back, a shudder ran through her frame as a ripple on the face of the still water when a sudden breeze sweeps over it.", "그녀는 흠칫 뒤로 물러섰고, 잔잔한 물 위에 갑작스러운 미풍이 스칠 때 이는 잔물결처럼 그녀의 온몸에 전율이 흘렀다."], ["But as soon as she recognized her lover, she screamed and beat her breast, embracing the lifeless body, pouring tears into its wounds, and imprinting kisses on the cold lips.", "그러나 자신의 연인임을 알아본 순간 그녀는 비명을 지르며 가슴을 치고, 싸늘한 시신을 끌어안고 그 상처에 눈물을 쏟으며 차가운 입술에 입을 맞추었다. \"오, 피라모스,\""], ["“O Pyramus,”", "그녀가 외쳤다."], ["she cried, “what has done this?", "\"무엇이 이런 짓을 하였나요?"], ["Answer me, Pyramus; it is your own Thisbe that speaks.", "대답해 주세요, 피라모스. 당신의 티스베가 말하고 있어요."], ["Hear me, dearest, and lift that drooping head!”", "들어 주세요, 사랑하는 이여, 그 늘어진 머리를 들어 주세요!\""], ["At the name of Thisbe Pyramus opened his eyes, then closed them again.", "티스베라는 이름에 피라모스는 눈을 떴다가 다시 감았다."], ["She saw her veil stained with blood and the scabbard empty of its sword.", "그녀는 자신의 베일이 피에 물든 것과 칼이 빠져나간 빈 칼집을 보았다."], ["“Thy own hand has slain thee, and for my sake,” she said.", "\"당신 자신의 손이, 그것도 나 때문에 당신을 죽였군요.\""], ["“I too can be brave for once, and my love is as strong as thine.", "그녀가 말하였다. \"나도 한 번쯤은 용감할 수 있고, 내 사랑도 당신의 사랑만큼 강하답니다."], ["I will follow thee in death, for I have been the cause; and death which alone could part us shall not prevent my joining thee.", "내가 그 원인이 되었으니, 나도 죽음 속에서 당신을 따르리라. 우리를 갈라놓을 수 있었던 것이 오직 죽음뿐이었으나, 그 죽음도 내가 당신과 함께하는 것을 막지는 못하리라."], ["And ye, unhappy parents of us both, deny us not our united request.", "그리고 우리 둘의 불행한 부모들이여, 우리의 하나 된 청을 거절하지 마세요."], ["As love and death have joined us, let one tomb contain us.", "사랑과 죽음이 우리를 하나로 묶었으니, 한 무덤이 우리를 함께 담게 해 주세요."], ["And thou, tree, retain the marks of slaughter.", "그리고 너, 나무여, 죽음의 자취를 간직하여라."], ["Let thy berries still serve for memorials of our blood.”", "네 열매가 우리 피의 기념물이 되어 길이 남게 하여라.\""], ["So saying she plunged the sword into her breast.", "그렇게 말하며 그녀는 칼을 자신의 가슴에 찔러 넣었다."], ["Her parents ratified her wish, the gods also ratified it.", "그녀의 부모는 그녀의 소원을 받아들였고, 신들 또한 그것을 받아들였다."], ["The two bodies were buried in one sepulchre, and the tree ever after brought forth purple berries, as it does to this day.", "두 사람의 몸은 한 무덤에 묻혔고, 그 나무는 그 후로 언제나 자줏빛 열매를 맺었으니, 오늘날에 이르기까지 그러하다."]], [["Moore, in the “Sylph’s Ball,” speaking of Davy’s Safety Lamp, is reminded of the wall that separated Thisbe and her lover:", "무어는 「실프의 무도회」에서 데이비의 안전등을 이야기하던 중, 티스베와 그 연인을 갈라놓았던 담벼락을 떠올린다."]], [["“O for that Lamp’s metallic gauze, That curtain of protecting wire, Which Davy delicately draws Around illicit, dangerous fire!", "\"오, 저 등불의 금속 그물망이여, 데이비가 섬세하게 둘러친 저 보호하는 철사의 장막이여, 금지된 위험한 불 둘레에 친 그것이여!"], ["The wall he sets ’twixt Flame and Air, (Like that which barred young Thisbe’s bliss,) Through whose small holes this dangerous pair May see each other, but not kiss.”", "그가 불꽃과 공기 사이에 세운 그 벽은, (어린 티스베의 기쁨을 가로막던 그 벽처럼,) 그 작은 구멍들을 통해 이 위험한 한 쌍이 서로를 볼 수는 있어도, 입맞출 수는 없게 하는도다.\""]], [["In Mickle’s translation of the “Lusiad” occurs the following allusion to the story of Pyramus and Thisbe, and the metamorphosis of the mulberries.", "미클이 번역한 『우스 루지아다스』에는 피라모스와 티스베의 이야기와 오디가 변모한 일에 대한 다음과 같은 언급이 나온다."], ["The poet is describing the Island of Love:", "시인은 사랑의 섬을 묘사하고 있다."]], [["“. . . here each gift Pomona’s hand bestows In cultured garden, free uncultured flows, The flavor sweeter and the hue more fair Than e’er was fostered by the hand of care. The cherry here in shining crimson glows, And stained with lovers’ blood, in pendent rows, The mulberries o’erload the bending boughs.”", "\"……이곳에서는 포모나의 손이 베푸는 온갖 선물이, 가꾼 정원에서처럼, 가꾸지 않아도 자유로이 흘러나오니, 그 향기는 더욱 달콤하고 그 빛깔은 더욱 고와, 정성스러운 손길이 길러 낸 그 어느 것보다도 낫도다. 이곳의 버찌는 빛나는 진홍빛으로 타오르고, 연인들의 피로 물든 채, 늘어진 줄을 이루어, 오디는 휘어진 가지를 가득 메우는도다.\""]], [["If any of our young readers can be so hard-hearted as to enjoy a laugh at the expense of poor Pyramus and Thisbe, they may find an opportunity by turning to Shakspeare’s play of the “Midsummer Night’s Dream,”", "만약 우리의 어린 독자들 가운데 누구라도, 가엾은 피라모스와 티스베를 비웃을 만큼 모진 마음을 지닌 이가 있다면, 셰익스피어의 희곡 『한여름 밤의 꿈』을 펼쳐 보는 것으로 그 기회를 찾을 수 있을 것이다."], ["where it is most amusingly burlesqued.", "거기에서 이 이야기는 더없이 우스꽝스럽게 익살로 패러디되어 있다."]], [["# CEPHALUS AND PROCRIS", "# CEPHALUS AND PROCRIS"]], [["Cephalus was a beautiful youth and fond of manly sports.", "케팔로스와 프로크리스\n케팔로스는 아름다운 청년으로 사내다운 운동을 좋아하였다. 그는 사냥을 하려고 동트기도 전에 일어나곤 하였다."], ["He would rise before the dawn to pursue the chase.", "에오스가 처음 밖을 내다보았을 때 그를 보고는 그와 사랑에 빠져 그를 몰래 데려갔다."], ["Aurora saw him when she first looked forth, fell in love with him, and stole him away.", "그러나 케팔로스는 자신이 지극히 사랑하는 매혹적인 아내와 갓 결혼한 참이었다. 그녀의 이름은 프로크리스였다."], ["But Cephalus was just married to a charming wife whom he devotedly loved. Her name was Procris.", "그녀는 사냥의 여신 아르테미스의 총애를 받았는데, 여신은 그녀에게 어떤 경쟁자보다도 빨리 달리는 개 한 마리와 결코 과녁을 빗나가지 않는 투창 한 자루를 주었다."], ["She was a favorite of Diana, the goddess of hunting, who had given her a dog which could outrun every rival, and a javelin which would never fail of its mark; and Procris gave these presents to her husband.", "프로크리스는 이 선물들을 남편에게 주었다. 케팔로스는 아내로 인해 너무나 행복하였으므로 에오스의 온갖 간청을 물리쳤고, 마침내 그녀는 못마땅해하며 그를 돌려보내며 말하였다."], ["Cephalus was so happy in his wife that he resisted all the entreaties of Aurora, and she finally dismissed him in displeasure, saying, “Go, ungrateful mortal, keep your wife, whom, if I am not much mistaken, you will one day be very sorry you ever saw again.”", "\"가거라, 은혜를 모르는 인간아, 네 아내를 지켜라. 그러나 내 짐작이 크게 틀리지 않다면, 너는 언젠가 그녀를 다시 본 것을 몹시 후회하게 되리라.\""]], [["Cephalus returned, and was as happy as ever in his wife and his woodland sports.", "케팔로스는 돌아와 여전히 아내와 숲속의 사냥으로 행복하였다."], ["Now it happened some angry deity had sent a ravenous fox to annoy the country; and the hunters turned out in great strength to capture it.", "그런데 마침 어느 노한 신이 사납게 날뛰는 여우 한 마리를 보내어 그 고장을 괴롭혔다. 그래서 사냥꾼들이 그것을 잡으려고 대거 나섰다."], ["Their efforts were all in vain; no dog could run it down; and at last they came to Cephalus to borrow his famous dog, whose name was Lelaps.", "그러나 그들의 노력은 모두 헛되었으니, 어떤 개도 그것을 따라잡지 못하였다. 결국 그들은 케팔로스를 찾아와 그의 유명한 개를 빌리려 하였는데, 그 개의 이름은 라일랍스였다."], ["No sooner was the dog let loose than he darted off, quicker than their eye could follow him.", "개를 풀어 놓자마자 그것은 그들의 눈이 따라갈 수 없을 만큼 빠르게 내달렸다."], ["If they had not seen his footprints in the sand they would have thought he flew.", "모래 위에 찍힌 발자국을 보지 못하였다면 그들은 그 개가 날아간 줄로 여겼을 것이다."], ["Cephalus and others stood on a hill and saw the race.", "케팔로스를 비롯한 이들은 언덕 위에 서서 그 추격을 지켜보았다."], ["The fox tried every art; he ran in a circle and turned on his track, the dog close upon him, with open jaws, snapping at his heels, but biting only the air.", "여우는 온갖 재주를 다 부렸으니, 원을 그리며 달리다가 제 발자국을 되짚어 돌아섰고, 개는 입을 벌린 채 그 뒤를 바짝 따라 발꿈치를 물어뜯으려 하였으나 허공만 물어뜯었다."], ["Cephalus was about to use his javelin, when suddenly he saw both dog and game stop instantly.", "케팔로스가 막 투창을 쓰려는 순간, 갑자기 그는 개와 사냥감이 동시에 멈춰 서는 것을 보았다."], ["The heavenly powers who had given both were not willing that either should conquer.", "둘 다를 내려 준 천상의 권능들이 어느 한쪽도 이기는 것을 바라지 않았던 것이다."], ["In the very attitude of life and action they were turned into stone.", "살아 움직이는 바로 그 자세 그대로 그들은 돌로 변하였다."], ["So lifelike and natural did they look, you would have thought, as you looked at them, that one was going to bark, the other to leap forward.", "너무나 살아 있는 듯 자연스러워 보였으므로, 그것들을 바라보노라면 하나는 막 짖으려 하고 다른 하나는 막 앞으로 뛰어오르려 하는 듯 여겨졌을 것이다."]], [["Cephalus, though he had lost his dog, still continued to take delight in the chase.", "케팔로스는 비록 개를 잃었으나 여전히 사냥에서 기쁨을 얻었다."], ["He would go out at early morning, ranging the woods and hills unaccompanied by any one, needing no help, for his javelin was a sure weapon in all cases.", "그는 이른 아침에 나가, 누구의 동행도 없이 숲과 언덕을 거닐었으니, 아무런 도움도 필요 없었다. 그의 투창이 어떤 경우에도 확실한 무기였기 때문이다."], ["Fatigued with hunting, when the sun got high he would seek a shady nook where a cool stream flowed, and, stretched on the grass, with his garments thrown aside, would enjoy the breeze.", "사냥에 지쳐 해가 높이 떠오르면 그는 시원한 시냇물이 흐르는 그늘진 곳을 찾아, 옷을 벗어 던지고 풀밭에 드러누워 산들바람을 즐기곤 하였다. 때때로 그는 소리 내어 말하곤 하였다."], ["Sometimes he would say aloud, “Come, sweet breeze, come and fan my breast, come and allay the heat that burns me.”", "\"오너라, 달콤한 산들바람이여, 와서 내 가슴에 부채질하여라, 와서 나를 태우는 이 열기를 식혀 다오.\""], ["Some one passing by one day heard him talking in this way to the air, and, foolishly believing that he was talking to some maiden, went and told the secret to Procris, Cephalus’s wife. Love is credulous.", "어느 날 곁을 지나가던 누군가가 그가 이렇게 허공에다 말하는 것을 듣고는, 그가 어떤 처녀에게 말을 거는 것이라 어리석게 믿고서, 가서 그 비밀을 케팔로스의 아내 프로크리스에게 일러 주었다."], ["Procris, at the sudden shock, fainted away.", "사랑은 쉽게 믿는 법이다."], ["Presently recovering, she said, “It cannot be true; I will not believe it unless I myself am a witness to it.”", "프로크리스는 그 갑작스러운 충격에 그만 정신을 잃고 쓰러졌다. 곧 정신을 차린 그녀는 말하였다."], ["So she waited, with anxious heart, till the next morning, when Cephalus went to hunt as usual.", "\"그럴 리 없어. 내가 직접 목격하지 않는 한 믿지 않으리라.\""], ["Then she stole out after him, and concealed herself in the place where the informer directed her.", "그리하여 그녀는 불안한 마음으로 이튿날 아침까지 기다렸고, 케팔로스가 여느 때처럼 사냥하러 갔다."], ["Cephalus came as he was wont when tired with sport, and stretched himself on the green bank, saying, “Come, sweet breeze, come and fan me; you know how I love you!", "그러자 그녀는 그를 뒤따라 몰래 빠져나가, 밀고자가 일러 준 장소에 몸을 숨겼다. 케팔로스는 사냥에 지치면 늘 그러하던 대로 와서 푸른 둑에 몸을 뉘며 말하였다."], ["you make the groves and my solitary rambles delightful.”", "\"오너라, 달콤한 산들바람이여, 와서 내게 부채질하여라."], ["He was running on in this way when he heard, or thought he heard, a sound as of a sob in the bushes.", "너는 내가 너를 얼마나 사랑하는지 알지! 너는 이 숲과 나의 외로운 거님을 즐겁게 해 주는구나.\""], ["Supposing it some wild animal, he threw his javelin at the spot.", "그가 이런 식으로 말을 이어 가는데, 덤불 속에서 흐느낌 같은 소리가 들리는 듯하였다."], ["A cry from his beloved Procris told him that the weapon had too surely met its mark.", "어떤 들짐승이려니 여기고 그는 그곳을 향해 투창을 던졌다. 사랑하는 프로크리스의 비명이 그 무기가 너무도 확실히 과녁을 맞혔음을 그에게 알렸다."], ["He rushed to the place, and found her bleeding, and with sinking strength endeavoring to draw forth from the wound the javelin, her own gift.", "그는 그곳으로 달려가, 그녀가 피를 흘리며 꺼져 가는 힘으로 자신의 선물이었던 투창을 상처에서 빼내려 애쓰고 있는 것을 발견하였다."], ["Cephalus raised her from the earth, strove to stanch the blood, and called her to revive and not to leave him miserable, to reproach himself with her death.", "케팔로스는 그녀를 땅에서 일으켜 피를 멎게 하려 애쓰며, 정신을 차려서 자신을 비참하게 버려 두지 말라고, 그녀의 죽음으로 자책하게 하지 말라고 그녀를 불렀다. 그녀는 힘없는 눈을 떠, 가까스로 이 몇 마디 말을 입에 담았다."], ["She opened her feeble eyes, and forced herself to utter these few words: “I implore you, if you have ever loved me, if I have ever deserved kindness at your hands, my husband, grant me this last request; do not marry that odious Breeze!”", "\"간청하건대, 당신이 일찍이 나를 사랑한 적이 있다면, 내가 일찍이 당신의 손길에서 다정함을 받을 자격이 있었다면, 여보, 이 마지막 청을 들어주세요. 그 가증스러운 산들바람과는 결혼하지 마세요!\""], ["This disclosed the whole mystery: but alas!", "이로써 모든 수수께끼가 드러났다. 그러나 아아!"], ["what advantage to disclose it now!", "이제 와서 그것을 밝힌들 무슨 소용이 있으랴!"], ["She died; but her face wore a calm expression, and she looked pityingly and forgivingly on her husband when he made her understand the truth.", "그녀는 죽었다. 그러나 그녀의 얼굴은 평온한 표정을 띠었고, 케팔로스가 진실을 깨닫게 해 주었을 때 그녀는 가엾어하며 용서하는 눈길로 남편을 바라보았다."]], [["Moore, in his “Legendary Ballads,” has one on Cephalus and Procris, beginning thus:", "무어는 그의 『전설 발라드』에서 케팔로스와 프로크리스에 관한 한 편을 실었는데, 이렇게 시작한다."]], [["“A hunter once in a grove reclined, To shun the noon’s bright eye, And oft he wooed the wandering wind To cool his brow with its sigh.", "\"한 사냥꾼이 일찍이 숲속에 몸을 뉘었네, 한낮의 밝은 눈을 피하려고, 그리고 자주 떠도는 바람에게 구애하였네, 그 한숨으로 제 이마를 식혀 달라고."], ["While mute lay even the wild bee’s hum, Nor breath could stir the aspen’s hair, His song was still, ‘Sweet Air, O come!’ While Echo answered, ‘Come, sweet Air!’”", "들벌의 윙윙거림조차 잠잠히 누웠을 때, 그 어떤 숨결도 사시나무의 머리칼을 흔들지 못할 때, 그의 노래는 한결같았네, '달콤한 바람이여, 오, 오라!' 메아리가 화답하는 동안, '오라, 달콤한 바람이여!'\""]], [["————", "————"]], [["# CHAPTER IV", "# CHAPTER IV"]], [["JUNO AND HER RIVALS, IO AND CALLISTO—DIANA AND ACTÆON—LATONA AND THE RUSTICS\nJUNO one day perceived it suddenly grow dark, and immediately suspected that her husband had raised a cloud to hide some of his doings that would not bear the light.", "어느 날 헤라[로마의 〈유노〉]는 갑자기 사방이 어두워지는 것을 알아채고는, 곧바로 자기 남편이 떳떳하지 못한 어떤 짓을 빛으로부터 가리려고 구름을 일으킨 것이 아닌가 의심했다."], ["She brushed away the cloud, and saw her husband on the banks of a glassy river, with a beautiful heifer standing near him.", "그녀가 구름을 걷어 내자, 거울처럼 맑은 강가에 서 있는 남편이 보였고, 그 곁에는 아름다운 어린 암소 한 마리가 서 있었다."], ["Juno suspected the heifer’s form concealed some fair nymph of mortal mould—as was, indeed the case; for it was Io, the daughter of the river god Inachus, whom Jupiter had been flirting with, and, when he became aware of the approach of his wife, had changed into that form.", "헤라는 그 암소의 모습 속에 필멸의 몸을 지닌 어여쁜 님프가 감추어져 있으리라고 의심했는데, 사실이 정말 그러했다. 그 암소는 강의 신 이나코스의 딸 이오였으니, 제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]가 그녀에게 추근거리던 차에 아내가 다가오는 것을 알아채고는 그런 모습으로 바꾸어 놓았던 것이다."]], [["Juno joined her husband, and noticing the heifer praised its beauty, and asked whose it was, and of what herd.", "헤라는 남편에게 다가가, 그 암소를 보고는 아름답다고 칭찬하면서 누구의 것이며 어느 무리에 속한 것이냐고 물었다."], ["Jupiter, to stop questions, replied that it was a fresh creation from the earth.", "제우스는 더 이상의 질문을 막으려고, 그것은 대지에서 갓 생겨난 것이라고 대답했다."], ["Juno asked to have it as a gift.", "헤라는 그것을 선물로 달라고 했다."], ["What could Jupiter do?", "제우스가 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가?"], ["He was loath to give his mistress to his wife; yet how refuse so trifling a present as a simple heifer?", "그는 자기 정부를 아내에게 넘겨주는 것이 내키지 않았으나, 그렇다고 한낱 어린 암소 같은 하찮은 선물을 어찌 거절한단 말인가?"], ["He could not, without exciting suspicion; so he consented.", "의심을 사지 않고서는 그럴 수가 없었기에, 그는 승낙하고 말았다."], ["The goddess was not yet relieved of her suspicions; so she delivered the heifer to Argus, to be strictly watched.", "그러나 여신은 아직 의심이 풀리지 않았기에, 그 암소를 아르고스에게 넘겨주어 엄중히 감시하도록 했다."]], [["Now Argus had a hundred eyes in his head, and never went to sleep with more than two at a time, so that he kept watch of Io constantly.", "그런데 아르고스는 머리에 백 개의 눈이 달려 있었고, 한 번에 두 개씩만 감고 잠을 잤으므로 이오를 끊임없이 지켜볼 수 있었다."], ["He suffered her to feed through the day, and at night tied her up with a vile rope round her neck.", "그는 낮 동안에는 이오가 풀을 뜯게 내버려 두었으나, 밤이 되면 흉한 밧줄로 목을 묶어 두었다."], ["She would have stretched out her arms to implore freedom of Argus, but she had no arms to stretch out, and her voice was a bellow that frightened even herself.", "이오는 아르고스에게 자유를 애원하려고 두 팔을 뻗으려 했지만, 뻗을 팔이 없었으며, 그녀의 목소리는 자신마저도 놀라게 하는 울음소리였다."], ["She saw her father and her sisters, went near them, and suffered them to pat her back, and heard them admire her beauty.", "그녀는 아버지와 자매들을 보고 그들에게 다가가, 그들이 자기 등을 쓰다듬게 내버려 두었으며, 그들이 자기 아름다움을 감탄하는 소리를 들었다."], ["Her father reached her a tuft of grass, and she licked the outstretched hand.", "아버지가 풀 한 다발을 내밀자, 그녀는 그 내민 손을 핥았다."], ["She longed to make herself known to him, and would have uttered her wish; but, alas!", "그녀는 자기가 누구인지 그에게 알리고 싶어 그 바람을 입 밖에 내려 했지만, 아아!"], ["words were wanting.", "말이 나오지 않았다."], ["At length she bethought herself of writing, and inscribed her name—it was a short one—with her hoof on the sand.", "마침내 그녀는 글씨를 떠올리고는, 자신의 이름을 — 짧은 이름이었다 — 발굽으로 모래 위에 새겼다."], ["Inachus recognized it, and discovering that his daughter, whom he had long sought in vain, was hidden under this disguise, mourned over her, and, embracing her white neck, exclaimed, “Alas!", "이나코스는 그것을 알아보았고, 오랫동안 헛되이 찾아 헤매던 딸이 이런 변장 아래 숨겨져 있음을 깨닫자, 그녀를 슬퍼하며 그 흰 목을 끌어안고 외쳤다."], ["my daughter, it would have been a less grief to have lost you altogether!”", "\"아아! 내 딸아, 너를 아주 잃어버렸더라면 차라리 덜 슬펐을 것을!\""], ["While he thus lamented, Argus, observing, came and drove her away, and took his seat on a high bank, from whence he could see all around in every direction.", "그가 이렇게 탄식하고 있을 때, 아르고스가 그것을 보고는 다가와 그녀를 몰아내고, 사방 어디든 두루 살필 수 있는 높은 둑 위에 자리를 잡고 앉았다."]], [["Jupiter was troubled at beholding the sufferings of his mistress, and calling Mercury told him to go and despatch Argus.", "제우스는 자기 정부의 고통을 보다 못해 헤르메스[로마의 〈메르쿠리우스〉]를 불러 아르고스를 처치하고 오라고 일렀다."], ["Mercury made haste, put his winged slippers on his feet, and cap on his head, took his sleep-producing wand, and leaped down from the heavenly towers to the earth.", "헤르메스는 서둘러 발에는 날개 달린 신을 신고 머리에는 모자를 쓴 다음, 잠을 불러오는 지팡이를 들고 하늘의 탑에서 땅으로 뛰어내렸다."], ["There he laid aside his wings, and kept only his wand, with which he presented himself as a shepherd driving his flock.", "그곳에서 그는 날개를 벗어 두고 지팡이만 챙긴 채, 양 떼를 모는 목동의 모습으로 나타났다."], ["As he strolled on he blew upon his pipes.", "그는 거닐면서 피리를 불었다."], ["These were what are called the Syrinx or Pandean pipes.", "그것은 이른바 시링크스, 곧 판의 피리라고 부르는 것이었다."], ["Argus listened with delight, for he had never seen the instrument before.", "아르고스는 그 악기를 전에 본 적이 없었기에 흥미롭게 귀를 기울였다."], ["“Young man,”", "\"젊은이,\""], ["said he, “come and take a seat by me on this stone.", "그가 말했다. \"이리 와서 이 돌 위에 내 곁에 앉게나."], ["There is no better place for your flocks to graze in than hereabouts, and here is a pleasant shade such as shepherds love.”", "자네 양 떼가 풀을 뜯기에 이만한 곳도 없거니와, 여기 목동들이 좋아하는 시원한 그늘도 있다네.\""], ["Mercury sat down, talked, and told stories till it grew late, and played upon his pipes his most soothing strains, hoping to lull the watchful eyes to sleep, but all in vain; for Argus still contrived to keep some of his eyes open though he shut the rest.", "헤르메스는 자리에 앉아 이야기를 나누며 날이 저물도록 여러 이야기를 들려주고는, 그 경계하는 눈들을 잠재우려는 마음으로 가장 마음을 누그러뜨리는 가락을 피리로 연주했다. 그러나 모두 헛일이었으니, 아르고스는 나머지 눈은 감으면서도 몇몇 눈은 여전히 뜨고 있을 줄 알았기 때문이다."]], [["Among other stories, Mercury told him how the instrument on which he played was invented.", "여러 이야기 가운데서, 헤르메스는 자기가 연주하는 그 악기가 어떻게 만들어졌는지를 그에게 들려주었다."], ["“There was a certain nymph, whose name was Syrinx, who was much beloved by the satyrs and spirits of the wood; but she would have none of them, but was a faithful worshipper of Diana, and followed the chase.", "\"시링크스라는 이름의 어떤 님프가 있었는데, 사티로스들과 숲의 정령들에게 무척이나 사랑을 받았다네. 하지만 그녀는 그들 중 누구도 거들떠보지 않고, 아르테미스[로마의 〈디아나〉]의 충실한 숭배자가 되어 사냥을 따라다녔지."], ["You would have thought it was Diana herself, had you seen her in her hunting dress, only that her bow was of horn and Diana’s of silver.", "사냥 복장을 한 그녀를 보았다면 아르테미스 본인이라고 여겼을 걸세, 다만 그녀의 활은 뿔로 만든 것이고 아르테미스의 활은 은으로 만든 것이라는 차이뿐이었지."], ["One day, as she was returning from the chase, Pan met her, told her just this, and added more of the same sort.", "어느 날 그녀가 사냥에서 돌아오는데 판이 그녀를 만나, 바로 이런 말을 건네고는 같은 식의 말을 더 늘어놓았다네."], ["She ran away, without stopping to hear his compliments, and he pursued till she came to the bank of the river, where he overtook her, and she had only time to call for help on her friends the water nymphs.", "그녀는 그의 찬사를 들을 새도 없이 달아났고, 그는 그녀가 강가에 이를 때까지 뒤쫓아 마침내 따라잡았는데, 그녀는 겨우 자기 벗인 물의 님프들에게 도움을 청할 시간밖에 없었지."], ["They heard and consented.", "님프들은 그 소리를 듣고 응해 주었다네."], ["Pan threw his arms around what he supposed to be the form of the nymph, and found he embraced only a tuft of reeds!", "판은 님프의 몸이라고 여긴 것을 두 팔로 끌어안았으나, 알고 보니 그저 갈대 한 다발을 껴안고 있었던 거야!"], ["As he breathed a sigh, the air sounded through the reeds, and produced a plaintive melody.", "그가 한숨을 내쉬자, 그 공기가 갈대 사이로 울려 구슬픈 가락을 빚어냈지. 그 신은 그 새로움과 음악의 감미로움에 매료되어 말했다네."], ["The god, charmed with the novelty and with the sweetness of the music, said, ‘Thus, then, at least, you shall be mine.’ And he took some of the reeds, and placing them together, of unequal lengths, side by side, made an instrument which he called Syrinx, in honor of the nymph.”", "'그렇다면 이렇게라도 너는 내 것이 되리라.' 그러고는 갈대 몇 대를 꺾어 서로 다른 길이로 나란히 붙여 악기를 만들고는, 그 님프를 기려 시링크스라고 이름 붙였다네.\""], ["Before Mercury had finished his story he saw Argus’s eyes all asleep.", "헤르메스가 이야기를 마치기도 전에, 그는 아르고스의 눈이 모두 잠든 것을 보았다."], ["As his head nodded forward on his breast, Mercury with one stroke cut his neck through, and tumbled his head down the rocks.", "그의 머리가 앞으로 떨궈져 가슴에 닿자, 헤르메스는 단칼에 그의 목을 베어 그 머리를 바위 아래로 굴려 떨어뜨렸다."], ["O hapless Argus!", "오, 가련한 아르고스여!"], ["the light of your hundred eyes is quenched at once!", "그대의 백 개 눈의 빛이 일순간에 꺼져 버렸도다!"], ["Juno took them and put them as ornaments on the tail of her peacock, where they remain to this day.", "헤라는 그 눈들을 거두어 자기 공작새 꽁지에 장식으로 달았으니, 그것들은 오늘날까지도 그곳에 남아 있다."]], [["But the vengeance of Juno was not yet satiated.", "그러나 헤라의 복수는 아직 가시지 않았다."], ["She sent a gadfly to torment Io, who fled over the whole world from its pursuit.", "그녀는 등에 한 마리를 보내 이오를 괴롭혔고, 이오는 그것의 추격을 피해 온 세상을 떠돌며 달아났다."], ["She swam through the Ionian sea, which derived its name from her, then roamed over the plains of Illyria, ascended Mount Hæmus, and crossed the Thracian strait, thence named the Bosphorus (cow-ford), rambled on through Scythia, and the country of the Cimmerians, and arrived at last on the banks of the Nile.", "그녀는 자기 이름을 딴 이오니아해를 헤엄쳐 건넌 다음, 일리리아의 평원을 헤매고, 하이모스산을 오르고, 트라케 해협을 건넜는데, 그곳은 그로 인해 보스포로스[암소의 여울]라는 이름을 얻었다. 그녀는 계속해서 스키티아와 킴메리아인들의 땅을 헤매다가, 마침내 나일강 기슭에 다다랐다."], ["At length Jupiter interceded for her, and upon his promising not to pay her any more attentions Juno consented to restore her to her form.", "마침내 제우스가 그녀를 위해 중재에 나섰고, 더 이상 그녀에게 관심을 두지 않겠다고 약속하자 헤라는 그녀를 본래의 모습으로 되돌리는 데 동의했다."], ["It was curious to see her gradually recover her former self.", "그녀가 차츰 예전의 자신을 되찾는 모습은 보기에 신기했다."], ["The coarse hairs fell from her body, her horns shrank up, her eyes grew narrower, her mouth shorter; hands and fingers came instead of hoofs to her forefeet; in fine there was nothing left of the heifer, except her beauty.", "거친 털이 몸에서 떨어져 나가고, 뿔이 오그라들고, 눈이 좁아지고, 입이 짧아졌으며, 앞다리의 발굽 대신 손과 손가락이 생겨났다. 요컨대 어린 암소의 모습은 그 아름다움 말고는 아무것도 남지 않았다."], ["At first she was afraid to speak, for fear she should low, but gradually she recovered her confidence and was restored to her father and sisters.", "처음에는 음매 소리가 날까 두려워 말하기를 겁냈으나, 차츰 자신감을 되찾고 아버지와 자매들에게 돌아갔다."]], [["In a poem dedicated to Leigh Hunt, by Keats, the following allusion to the story of Pan and Syrinx occurs:", "키츠가 리 헌트에게 헌정한 시에는 판과 시링크스 이야기에 대한 다음과 같은 인유가 나온다."]], [["“So did he feel who pulled the bough aside, That we might look into a forest wide, .", "\"나뭇가지를 옆으로 젖혀 우리로 하여금 드넓은 숲을 들여다보게 한 그도 그렇게 느꼈으리, ."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], ["Telling us how fair trembling Syrinx fled Arcadian Pan, with such a fearful dread.", "어여쁘게 떨던 시링크스가 아르카디아의 판에게서 그토록 두려운 공포에 사로잡혀 달아난 사연을 들려주면서."], ["Poor nymph—poor Pan—how he did weep to find Nought but a lovely sighing of the wind Along the reedy stream; a half-heard strain, Full of sweet desolation, balmy pain.”", "가엾은 님프여 — 가엾은 판이여 — 그가 발견한 것이라곤 갈대 우거진 시냇가를 따라 흐르는 바람의 사랑스러운 한숨뿐이었으니 얼마나 울었던가, 어렴풋이 들리는 가락, 감미로운 황량함과 향긋한 아픔으로 가득한 그 가락을.\""]], [["Callisto was another maiden who excited the jealousy of Juno, and the goddess changed her into a bear.", "칼리스토는 헤라의 질투를 불러일으킨 또 한 명의 처녀였으며, 여신은 그녀를 곰으로 바꾸어 놓았다."], ["“I will take away,”", "\"내가 빼앗아 버리리라,\""], ["said she, “that beauty with which you have captivated my husband.”", "여신이 말했다. \"네가 내 남편을 사로잡은 그 아름다움을.\""], ["Down fell Callisto on her hands and knees; she tried to stretch out her arms in supplication—they were already beginning to be covered with black hair.", "칼리스토는 두 손과 무릎을 땅에 떨어뜨리며 쓰러졌다. 그녀는 애원하려고 두 팔을 뻗으려 했으나 — 그 팔은 이미 검은 털로 덮이기 시작했다."], ["Her hands grew rounded, became armed with crooked claws, and served for feet; her mouth, which Jove used to praise for its beauty, became a horrid pair of jaws; her voice, which if unchanged would have moved the heart to pity, became a growl, more fit to inspire terror.", "그녀의 손은 둥글게 변하더니 굽은 발톱이 돋아나 발 노릇을 하게 되었고, 제우스가 그 아름다움을 칭찬하곤 하던 그녀의 입은 무시무시한 한 쌍의 턱이 되었으며, 변하지 않았더라면 가슴을 연민으로 움직였을 그녀의 목소리는 차라리 공포를 불러일으키기에 알맞은 으르렁거림이 되었다."], ["Yet her former disposition remained, and with continual groaning, she bemoaned her fate, and stood upright as well as she could, lifting up her paws to beg for mercy, and felt that Jove was unkind, though she could not tell him so.", "그러나 예전의 성품만은 남아 있어, 그녀는 끊임없이 신음하며 자기 운명을 슬퍼하고, 할 수 있는 한 똑바로 서서 두 앞발을 들어 자비를 빌었으며, 제우스가 매정하다는 것을 느꼈지만 그에게 그렇게 말할 수는 없었다."], ["Ah, how often, afraid to stay in the woods all night alone, she wandered about the neighborhood of her former haunts; how often, frightened by the dogs, did she, so lately a huntress, fly in terror from the hunters!", "아, 그녀는 밤새 숲속에 홀로 머무는 것이 두려워 예전에 자주 다니던 곳 부근을 얼마나 자주 헤매고 다녔던가. 얼마 전까지 사냥꾼이었던 그녀가 개들에게 겁먹고는 얼마나 자주 사냥꾼들로부터 두려워 달아났던가!"], ["Often she fled from the wild beasts, forgetting that she was now a wild beast herself; and, bear as she was, was afraid of the bears.", "그녀는 자신이 이제 야수가 되었다는 것을 잊은 채 야수들로부터 자주 도망쳤으며, 자신이 곰이면서도 곰들을 두려워했다."]], [["One day a youth espied her as he was hunting.", "어느 날 한 젊은이가 사냥을 하다가 그녀를 발견했다."], ["She saw him and recognized him as her own son, now grown a young man.", "그녀는 그를 보고는 이제 청년으로 자란 자기 아들임을 알아보았다."], ["She stopped and felt inclined to embrace him.", "그녀는 멈추어 서서 그를 끌어안고 싶은 마음이 들었다."], ["As she was about to approach, he, alarmed, raised his hunting spear, and was on the point of transfixing her, when Jupiter, beholding, arrested the crime, and snatching away both of them, placed them in the heavens as the Great and Little Bear.", "그녀가 막 다가가려 하자, 그는 놀라서 사냥 창을 치켜들고 그녀를 막 찌르려던 참이었는데, 제우스가 이를 보고는 그 죄악을 막고 두 사람을 모두 낚아채어 큰곰자리와 작은곰자리로 하늘에 올려놓았다."]], [["Juno was in a rage to see her rival so set in honor, and hastened to ancient Tethys and Oceanus, the powers of ocean, and in answer to their inquiries thus told the cause of her coming: “Do you ask why I, the queen of the gods, have left the heavenly plains and sought your depths?", "헤라는 자신의 연적이 그토록 영예로운 자리에 오른 것을 보고 격분하여, 바다의 권능인 태고의 테티스와 오케아노스에게 서둘러 갔다. 그들이 무슨 일이냐고 묻자 그녀는 자기가 온 까닭을 이렇게 말했다."], ["Learn that I am supplanted in heaven—my place is given to another.", "\"신들의 여왕인 내가 어찌하여 하늘의 평원을 떠나 그대들의 깊은 곳을 찾아왔는지 묻는가?"], ["You will hardly believe me; but look when night darkens the world, and you shall see the two of whom I have so much reason to complain exalted to the heavens, in that part where the circle is the smallest, in the neighborhood of the pole.", "내가 하늘에서 밀려났음을, 내 자리를 다른 이에게 빼앗겼음을 알아 두게나. 내 말을 좀처럼 믿기 어렵겠지만, 밤이 세상을 어둡게 할 때 보게나."], ["Why should any one hereafter tremble at the thought of offending Juno, when such rewards are the consequence of my displeasure?", "그러면 내가 그토록 한탄할 까닭이 있는 그 둘이 하늘로, 원이 가장 작은 그 부분, 곧 극(極) 부근으로 올려진 것을 보게 될 걸세. 내 노여움의 결과가 이런 상이라면, 앞으로 누가 헤라의 비위를 거스를까 두려워 떨겠는가?"], ["See what I have been able to effect!", "내가 무엇을 이룰 수 있었는지 보게나!"], ["I forbade her to wear the human form—she is placed among the stars!", "나는 그녀가 사람의 모습을 하는 것을 금했거늘 — 그녀는 별들 가운데 놓였구나!"], ["So do my punishments result—such is the extent of my power!", "내 벌이 이렇게 끝나니 — 내 권능이 이 정도로구나!"], ["Better that she should have resumed her former shape, as I permitted Io to do.", "차라리 이오에게 허락했듯이 그녀가 본래 모습을 되찾는 편이 나았을 것을."], ["Perhaps he means to marry her, and put me away!", "어쩌면 그는 그녀와 혼인하고 나를 내칠 작정인지도 모르지!"], ["But you, my foster-parents, if you feel for me, and see with displeasure this unworthy treatment of me, show it, I beseech you, by forbidding this guilty couple from coming into your waters.”", "그러나 그대들, 나를 길러 준 이들이여, 나를 가엾이 여기고 이 부당한 처사를 못마땅하게 본다면, 부디 이를 보여 주게나. 저 죄지은 한 쌍이 그대들의 물속으로 들어오지 못하도록 막아 줌으로써 말일세.\""], ["The powers of the ocean assented, and consequently the two constellations of the Great and Little Bear move round and round in heaven, but never sink, as the other stars do, beneath the ocean.", "바다의 권능들은 이에 응했고, 그리하여 큰곰자리와 작은곰자리의 두 별자리는 하늘에서 빙글빙글 돌면서도, 다른 별들처럼 결코 바다 아래로 가라앉지 않는다."]], [["Milton alludes to the fact that the constellation of the Bear never sets, when he says:", "밀턴은 곰자리가 결코 지지 않는다는 사실을 다음과 같이 언급한다."]], [["“Let my lamp at midnight hour Be seen in some high lonely tower, Where I may oft outwatch the Bear,”", "\"한밤중에 내 등불이 어느 높고 외로운 탑에서 보이게 하라, 그곳에서 나는 자주 곰자리보다 늦도록 지켜보리니,\""], ["etc.", "운운."]], [["And Prometheus, in J.", "그리고 J."], ["R.", "R."], ["Lowell’s poem, says:", "로웰의 시에 나오는 프로메테우스는 이렇게 말한다."]], [["“One after one the stars have risen and set, Sparkling upon the hoar frost of my chain; The Bear that prowled all night about the fold Of the North-star, hath shrunk into his den, Scared by the blithesome footsteps of the Dawn.”", "\"별들이 하나씩 차례로 떠올랐다 졌으니, 내 사슬의 흰 서리 위에서 반짝이면서. 밤새도록 북극성의 우리 주위를 어슬렁대던 곰자리는, 새벽의 발랄한 발소리에 놀라 제 굴 속으로 움츠러들었구나.\""]], [["The last star in the tail of the Little Bear is the Pole-star, called also the Cynosure.", "작은곰자리 꽁지의 마지막 별은 북극성으로, 키노수라라고도 불린다."], ["Milton says:", "밀턴은 이렇게 말한다."]], [["“Straight mine eye hath caught new pleasures While the landscape round it measures.", "\"곧장 내 눈은 새로운 즐거움을 붙잡았으니, 주위의 풍경을 두루 가늠하는 동안. ."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [". .", "."], [". .", "."], ["Towers and battlements it sees Bosomed high in tufted trees, Where perhaps some beauty lies The Cynosure of neighboring eyes.”", ". 탑과 성가퀴가 보이나니, 우거진 나무들 사이로 높이 안긴 채, 어쩌면 그곳에 어떤 미인이 누워 있어 이웃한 뭇 시선의 키노수라가 되리라.\""]], [["The reference here is both to the Pole-star as the guide of mariners, and to the magnetic attraction of the North.", "여기서의 언급은 뱃사람들의 길잡이가 되는 북극성과, 북녘의 자기적(磁氣的) 이끌림을 함께 가리킨다."], ["He calls it also the “Star of Arcady,” because Callisto’s boy was named Arcas, and they lived in Arcadia.", "그는 그것을 \"아르카디아의 별\"이라고도 부르는데, 이는 칼리스토의 아들 이름이 아르카스였고 그들이 아르카디아에 살았기 때문이다."], ["In “Comus,” the brother, benighted in the woods, says:", "「코머스」에서, 숲속에서 어둠에 갇힌 오라비는 이렇게 말한다."]], [["“. .", "\"."], [".", "."], ["Some gentle taper!", ". 다정한 촛불이여!"], ["Though a rush candle, from the wicker hole Of some clay habitation, visit us With thy long levelled rule of streaming light, And thou shalt be our star of Arcady, Or Tyrian Cynosure.”", "어느 진흙집의 버들 엮은 구멍에서 비치는 골풀 양초일지언정, 흘러나오는 빛의 그 길게 뻗은 자로 우리를 찾아오라, 그러면 그대는 우리의 아르카디아의 별, 아니면 티로스의 키노수라가 되리라.\""]], [["Thus in two instances we have seen Juno’s severity to her rivals; now let us learn how a virgin goddess punished an invader of her privacy.", "이렇듯 우리는 두 가지 사례에서 헤라가 연적들에게 얼마나 가혹했는지를 보았으니, 이제 한 처녀 여신이 자신의 사생활을 침범한 자를 어떻게 벌했는지 알아보기로 하자."]], [["It was midday, and the sun stood equally distant from either goal, when young Actæon, son of King Cadmus, thus addressed the youths who with him were hunting the stag in the mountains:", "한낮이었고, 해가 양쪽 끝에서 똑같이 떨어진 곳에 걸려 있을 때, 카드모스 왕의 아들인 젊은 악타이온은 함께 산에서 사슴을 사냥하던 젊은이들에게 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“Friends, our nets and our weapons are wet with the blood of our victims; we have had sport enough for one day, and to-morrow we can renew our labors.", "\"벗들이여, 우리의 그물과 무기는 잡은 짐승들의 피로 젖었네. 오늘 하루의 사냥은 이만하면 충분하니, 내일 다시 일을 시작하면 되네."], ["Now, while Phœbus parches the earth, let us put by our implements and indulge ourselves with rest.”", "자, 포이보스가 대지를 메마르게 하는 이때, 우리의 도구를 치워 두고 쉬면서 여유를 누리세.\""]], [["There was a valley thick enclosed with cypresses and pines, sacred to the huntress queen, Diana.", "사이프러스와 소나무가 빽빽이 둘러싼 골짜기가 하나 있었으니, 사냥의 여왕 아르테미스에게 바쳐진 곳이었다."], ["In the extremity of the valley was a cave, not adorned with art, but nature had counterfeited art in its construction, for she had turned the arch of its roof with stones as delicately fitted as if by the hand of man.", "골짜기 끝에는 동굴이 하나 있었는데, 인공으로 꾸민 것이 아니라 자연이 그 구조 속에서 인공을 흉내 낸 것이었다. 자연이 마치 사람의 손길로 다듬은 듯 정교하게 들어맞는 돌들로 그 지붕의 아치를 둥글게 빚어 놓았던 것이다."], ["A fountain burst out from one side, whose open basin was bounded by a grassy rim.", "한쪽에서는 샘이 솟아 나왔고, 그 열린 못은 풀이 우거진 가장자리로 둘려 있었다."], ["Here the goddess of the woods used to come when weary with hunting and lave her virgin limbs in the sparkling water.", "숲의 여신은 사냥에 지쳤을 때 이곳에 와서 반짝이는 물에 처녀의 사지를 씻곤 했다."]], [["One day, having repaired thither with her nymphs, she handed her javelin, her quiver, and her bow to one, her robe to another, while a third unbound the sandals from her feet.", "어느 날, 그녀는 님프들과 함께 그곳에 가서 자신의 투창과 화살통과 활을 한 님프에게, 겉옷을 다른 님프에게 건네주었고, 또 한 님프는 그녀의 발에서 샌들을 풀었다."], ["Then Crocale, the most skilful of them, arranged her hair, and Nephele, Hyale, and the rest drew water in capacious urns.", "그러고 나서 그들 가운데 가장 솜씨 좋은 크로칼레가 그녀의 머리를 매만졌고, 네펠레와 히알레를 비롯한 나머지 님프들은 넉넉한 항아리에 물을 길었다."], ["While the goddess was thus employed in the labors of the toilet, behold Actæon, having quitted his companions, and rambling without any especial object, came to the place, led thither by his destiny.", "여신이 이렇듯 몸단장의 일에 여념이 없을 때, 보라, 악타이온이 동료들을 떠나 별다른 목적 없이 헤매다가, 자신의 운명에 이끌려 그곳에 이르렀다."], ["As he presented himself at the entrance of the cave, the nymphs, seeing a man, screamed and rushed towards the goddess to hide her with their bodies.", "그가 동굴 입구에 모습을 드러내자, 한 남자를 본 님프들은 비명을 지르며 자기들 몸으로 여신을 가리려고 그녀에게 달려들었다."], ["But she was taller than the rest and overtopped them all by a head.", "그러나 여신은 다른 님프들보다 키가 커서 그들 모두보다 머리 하나만큼 솟아 있었다."], ["Such a color as tinges the clouds at sunset or at dawn came over the countenance of Diana thus taken by surprise.", "이렇게 불시에 들킨 아르테미스의 얼굴에는, 해 질 녘이나 동틀 무렵 구름을 물들이는 듯한 빛깔이 떠올랐다."], ["Surrounded as she was by her nymphs, she yet turned half away, and sought with a sudden impulse for her arrows.", "님프들에게 둘러싸여 있었으면서도, 그녀는 몸을 반쯤 돌리고는 갑작스러운 충동으로 화살을 찾았다."], ["As they were not at hand, she dashed the water into the face of the intruder, adding these words: “Now go and tell, if you can, that you have seen Diana unapparelled.”", "화살이 가까이에 없자, 그녀는 침입자의 얼굴에 물을 끼얹으며 이런 말을 덧붙였다. \"이제 가서 할 수 있거든 떠벌려 보아라, 옷을 걸치지 않은 아르테미스를 보았노라고.\""], ["Immediately a pair of branching stag’s horns grew out of his head, his neck gained in length, his ears grew sharp-pointed, his hands became feet, his arms long legs, his body was covered with a hairy spotted hide.", "그러자 즉시 가지처럼 뻗은 한 쌍의 사슴뿔이 그의 머리에서 돋아나고, 목은 길어지고, 귀는 뾰족해지고, 손은 발이 되고, 팔은 긴 다리가 되었으며, 몸은 털 난 얼룩 가죽으로 덮였다."], ["Fear took the place of his former boldness, and the hero fled.", "예전의 대담함 대신 두려움이 들어섰고, 그 영웅은 달아났다."], ["He could not but admire his own speed; but when he saw his horns in the water, “Ah, wretched me!”", "그는 자기 자신의 빠른 속도에 감탄하지 않을 수 없었으나, 물에 비친 자기 뿔을 보고는 \"아, 가련한 나여!\""], ["he would have said, but no sound followed the effort.", "하고 말하려 했지만, 그 애씀 끝에 아무 소리도 나오지 않았다."], ["He groaned, and tears flowed down the face which had taken the place of his own.", "그는 신음했고, 자기 얼굴을 대신한 그 얼굴 위로 눈물이 흘러내렸다."], ["Yet his consciousness remained.", "그러나 의식만은 그대로 남아 있었다."], ["What shall he do?—go home to seek the palace, or lie hid in the woods?", "무엇을 할 것인가? 집으로, 궁전으로 돌아갈 것인가, 아니면 숲속에 숨을 것인가?"], ["The latter he was afraid, the former he was ashamed, to do.", "뒤엣것은 두려웠고, 앞엣것은 부끄러웠다."], ["While he hesitated the dogs saw him.", "그가 망설이는 사이 개들이 그를 발견했다."], ["First Melampus, a Spartan dog, gave the signal with his bark, then Pamphagus, Dorceus, Lelaps, Theron, Nape, Tigris, and all the rest, rushed after him swifter than the wind.", "먼저 스파르타산 개 멜람푸스가 짖어서 신호를 보냈고, 이어 팜파고스, 도르케우스, 렐랍스, 테론, 나페, 티그리스를 비롯한 나머지 개들이 모두 바람보다 빠르게 그를 뒤쫓았다."], ["Over rocks and cliffs, through mountain gorges that seemed impracticable, he fled and they followed.", "바위와 절벽을 넘고, 도저히 지날 수 없을 것 같은 산골짜기를 가로질러 그는 달아났고 개들은 뒤따랐다."], ["Where he had often chased the stag and cheered on his pack, his pack now chased him, cheered on by his huntsmen.", "그가 자주 사슴을 쫓고 자기 사냥개 무리를 부추기던 그곳에서, 이제 그의 사냥개 무리가 그의 사냥꾼들에게 부추김을 받으며 그를 뒤쫓았다."], ["He longed to cry out, “I am Actæon; recognize your master!”", "그는 \"나는 악타이온이다, 너희 주인을 알아보아라!\""], ["but the words came not at his will.", "하고 외치고 싶었지만, 말이 그의 뜻대로 나오지 않았다."], ["The air resounded with the bark of the dogs.", "대기는 개들의 짖는 소리로 울렸다."], ["Presently one fastened on his back, another seized his shoulder.", "이윽고 한 마리가 그의 등에 달려들고, 다른 한 마리가 그의 어깨를 물었다."], ["While they held their master, the rest of the pack came up and buried their teeth in his flesh.", "그 둘이 주인을 붙잡고 있는 사이, 나머지 개들이 몰려와 그의 살에 이빨을 박았다."], ["He groaned,—not in a human voice, yet certainly not in a stag’s,—and falling on his knees, raised his eyes, and would have raised his arms in supplication, if he had had them.", "그는 신음했는데 — 사람의 목소리는 아니었으나, 그렇다고 분명 사슴의 목소리도 아니었다 — 무릎을 꿇으며 두 눈을 들어 올렸고, 팔이 있었더라면 애원하듯 두 팔을 들어 올렸을 것이다."], ["His friends and fellow-huntsmen cheered on the dogs, and looked everywhere for Actæon, calling on him to join the sport.", "그의 벗들이자 함께 사냥하던 이들은 개들을 부추기며, 사방으로 악타이온을 찾고 그에게 사냥에 합류하라고 불렀다."], ["At the sound of his name he turned his head, and heard them regret that he should be away.", "자기 이름이 불리자 그는 머리를 돌렸고, 그가 없는 것을 그들이 아쉬워하는 소리를 들었다."], ["He earnestly wished he was.", "그는 자기가 정말 그 자리에 없기를 간절히 바랐다."], ["He would have been well pleased to see the exploits of his dogs, but to feel them was too much.", "그는 자기 개들의 활약을 보았더라면 무척 기뻤겠으나, 그것을 몸으로 느끼는 것은 너무나 가혹했다."], ["They were all around him, rending and tearing; and it was not till they had torn his life out that the anger of Diana was satisfied.", "개들은 그를 온통 둘러싼 채 물어뜯고 찢었으며, 그들이 그의 목숨을 찢어 낸 뒤에야 비로소 아르테미스의 노여움이 풀렸다."]], [["In Shelley’s poem “Adonais” is the following allusion to the story of Actæon:", "셸리의 시 「아도네이스」에는 악타이온 이야기에 대한 다음과 같은 인유가 있다."]], [["“’Midst others of less note came one frail form, A phantom among men: companionless As the last cloud of an expiring storm, Whose thunder is its knell; he, as I guess, Had gazed on Nature’s naked loveliness, Actæon-like, and now he fled astray With feeble steps o’er the world’s wilderness; And his own Thoughts, along that rugged way, Pursued like raging hounds their father and their prey.”", "\"그다지 알려지지 않은 다른 이들 가운데 한 가냘픈 형상이 왔으니, 사람들 사이의 한 유령이었네, 벗 없이 홀로, 잦아드는 폭풍의 마지막 구름처럼, 그 천둥이 곧 그의 조종(弔鐘)이라네. 짐작건대 그는 자연의 벌거벗은 사랑스러움을 악타이온처럼 응시했었고, 이제 그는 갈 곳을 잃은 채 비틀거리는 걸음으로 세상의 황야를 헤매며 달아났으니, 그리고 그 자신의 〈생각들〉이, 그 험한 길을 따라 성난 사냥개들처럼 제 아비이자 제 먹이를 뒤쫓았네.\""], ["Stanza 31.", "제31연."]], [["The allusion is probably to Shelley himself.", "이 인유는 아마도 셸리 자신을 가리키는 것일 터이다."]], [["# LATONA AND THE RUSTICS", "# LATONA AND THE RUSTICS"]], [["Some thought the goddess in this instance more severe than was just, while others praised her conduct as strictly consistent with her virgin dignity.", "어떤 이들은 이 경우에 여신이 정당한 정도보다 더 가혹했다고 여겼고, 또 어떤 이들은 그녀의 처사가 처녀의 위엄에 엄격히 들어맞는다며 칭송했다."], ["As usual, the recent event brought older ones to mind, and one of the bystanders told this story: “Some countrymen of Lycia once insulted the goddess Latona, but not with impunity.", "으레 그렇듯, 최근의 사건이 더 오래된 이야기들을 떠올리게 했고, 곁에 있던 이들 가운데 하나가 이런 이야기를 들려주었다. \"리키아의 어떤 농부들이 언젠가 여신 레토[로마의 〈라토나〉]를 모욕했는데, 그러고도 벌을 면하지는 못했다네."], ["When I was young, my father, who had grown too old for active labors, sent me to Lycia to drive thence some choice oxen, and there I saw the very pond and marsh where the wonder happened.", "내가 젊었을 적에, 힘든 일을 하기에는 너무 늙으신 아버지께서 나를 리키아로 보내 그곳에서 좋은 소들을 몰고 오게 하셨는데, 거기서 나는 그 신기한 일이 일어났던 바로 그 못과 늪을 보았다네."], ["Near by stood an ancient altar, black with the smoke of sacrifice and almost buried among the reeds.", "그 가까이에는 제물의 연기로 검게 그을려 갈대 사이에 거의 묻히다시피 한 오래된 제단이 하나 서 있었지."], ["I inquired whose altar it might be, whether of Faunus or the Naiads, or some god of the neighboring mountain, and one of the country people replied, ‘No mountain or river god possesses this altar, but she whom royal Juno in her jealousy drove from land to land, denying her any spot of earth whereon to rear her twins.’ Bearing in her arms the infant deities, Latona reached this land, weary with her burden and parched with thirst.", "나는 그것이 누구의 제단인지, 파우누스의 것인지 나이아데스의 것인지, 아니면 이웃한 산의 어떤 신의 것인지 물었더니, 그 고장 사람 하나가 대답하기를, '이 제단은 산이나 강의 신이 차지한 것이 아니라, 왕비 헤라가 질투에 사로잡혀 이 땅에서 저 땅으로 내쫓으며 그녀의 쌍둥이를 기를 땅 한 뼘조차 주지 않았던 그 여신의 것이라오.' 갓난 신들을 두 팔에 안은 채, 레토는 그 짐에 지치고 갈증에 목이 타며 이 땅에 다다랐다네."], ["By chance she espied on the bottom of the valley this pond of clear water, where the country people were at work gathering willows and osiers.", "그녀는 우연히 골짜기 바닥에서 이 맑은 물의 못을 발견했는데, 그곳에서는 고장 사람들이 버드나무와 고리버들을 거두는 일을 하고 있었지."], ["The goddess approached, and kneeling on the bank would have slaked her thirst in the cool stream, but the rustics forbade her.", "여신이 다가가 둑에 무릎을 꿇고 그 시원한 물로 갈증을 풀려 하자, 농부들이 이를 막았다네. '어찌하여 내게 물을 주지 않는 것이오?'"], ["‘Why do you refuse me water?’ said she; ‘water is free to all.", "그녀가 말했지. '물은 누구에게나 자유로운 것이오."], ["Nature allows no one to claim as property the sunshine, the air, or the water.", "자연은 햇빛이나 공기나 물을 그 누구도 제 소유로 주장하도록 허락지 않소."], ["I come to take my share of the common blessing.", "나는 이 공통의 은혜에서 내 몫을 취하러 온 것이오."], ["Yet I ask it of you as a favor.", "그러나 나는 이를 호의로서 그대들에게 청하오."], ["I have no intention of washing my limbs in it, weary though they be, but only to quench my thirst.", "비록 지치긴 했으나 그 안에서 사지를 씻을 생각은 없고, 다만 갈증을 가시려는 것뿐이오."], ["My mouth is so dry that I can hardly speak.", "내 입이 어찌나 메말랐는지 말하기조차 힘드오."], ["A draught of water would be nectar to me; it would revive me, and I would own myself indebted to you for life itself.", "물 한 모금이 내게는 신주(神酒)와 같을 것이며, 나를 되살아나게 할 터이니, 그러면 나는 그대들에게 목숨 그 자체를 빚졌노라 인정하겠소."], ["Let these infants move your pity, who stretch out their little arms as if to plead for me;’ and the children, as it happened, were stretching out their arms.", "마치 나를 위해 애원하듯 작은 팔을 뻗고 있는 이 아기들을 보고 가엾이 여겨 주시오.' 그러자 마침 아기들이 작은 팔을 뻗고 있었다네."]], [["“Who would not have been moved with these gentle words of the goddess?", "\"여신의 이런 부드러운 말에 마음이 움직이지 않을 자가 어디 있겠는가?"], ["But these clowns persisted in their rudeness; they even added jeers and threats of violence if she did not leave the place.", "그러나 이 촌놈들은 무례함을 고집하며, 그녀가 그곳을 떠나지 않으면 폭력을 쓰겠다는 조롱과 위협까지 덧붙였지."], ["Nor was this all.", "이뿐만이 아니었다네."], ["They waded into the pond and stirred up the mud with their feet, so as to make the water unfit to drink.", "그들은 못으로 첨벙첨벙 걸어 들어가 발로 진흙을 휘저어 물을 마실 수 없게 만들어 버렸지."], ["Latona was so angry that she ceased to mind her thirst.", "레토는 어찌나 화가 났던지 갈증마저 잊고 말았다네."], ["She no longer supplicated the clowns, but lifting her hands to heaven exclaimed, ‘May they never quit that pool, but pass their lives there!’ And it came to pass accordingly.", "그녀는 더 이상 촌놈들에게 빌지 않고, 두 손을 하늘로 들어 올리며 외쳤지. '저들이 결코 저 못을 떠나지 못하고, 그곳에서 평생을 보내게 하소서!'"], ["They now live in the water, sometimes totally submerged, then raising their heads above the surface or swimming upon it.", "그러자 그대로 이루어졌다네. 그들은 이제 물속에서 살아가며, 때로는 온몸을 물에 담그고, 때로는 수면 위로 머리를 내밀거나 그 위를 헤엄친다네."], ["Sometimes they come out upon the bank, but soon leap back again into the water.", "때로는 둑으로 나오기도 하지만, 이내 다시 물속으로 뛰어들지."], ["They still use their base voices in railing, and though they have the water all to themselves, are not ashamed to croak in the midst of it.", "그들은 여전히 그 천한 목소리로 욕설을 일삼으며, 물을 통째로 차지하고 있으면서도 그 한가운데서 개굴개굴 우는 것을 부끄러워하지 않는다네."], ["Their voices are harsh, their throats bloated, their mouths have become stretched by constant railing, their necks have shrunk up and disappeared, and their heads are joined to their bodies.", "그들의 목소리는 거칠고, 목구멍은 부풀었으며, 입은 끊임없는 욕설로 늘어났고, 목은 오그라들어 사라졌으며, 머리는 몸뚱이에 붙어 버렸지."], ["Their backs are green, their disproportioned bellies white, and in short they are now frogs, and dwell in the slimy pool.”", "등은 초록빛이고, 어울리지 않게 큰 배는 흰빛이니, 요컨대 그들은 이제 개구리가 되어 그 진창의 못에서 살고 있다네.\""]], [["This story explains the allusion in one of Milton’s sonnets, “On the detraction which followed upon his writing certain treatises.”", "이 이야기는 밀턴의 소네트 가운데 하나인 「어떤 논고들을 쓴 뒤에 따라온 비방에 부쳐」에 나오는 인유를 설명해 준다."]], [["“I did but prompt the age to quit their clogs By the known laws of ancient liberty, When straight a barbarous noise environs me Of owls and cuckoos, asses, apes and dogs.", "\"나는 다만 옛 자유의 알려진 법으로써 이 시대더러 그 족쇄를 벗어 버리라 권했을 뿐인데, 그러자 곧 야만스러운 소음이 나를 에워쌌으니, 올빼미와 뻐꾸기, 당나귀, 원숭이, 개들의 소음이라."], ["As when those hinds that were transformed to frogs Railed at Latona’s twin-born progeny, Which after held the sun and moon in fee.”", "마치 개구리로 변한 저 촌놈들이 레토의 쌍둥이 자손에게 욕설을 퍼붓던 것과 같으니, 그 자손은 훗날 해와 달을 영지로 거느렸도다.\""]], [["The persecution which Latona experienced from Juno is alluded to in the story.", "레토가 헤라에게서 받은 박해가 이 이야기 속에 언급되어 있다."], ["The tradition was that the future mother of Apollo and Diana, flying from the wrath of Juno, besought all the islands of the Ægean to afford her a place of rest, but all feared too much the potent queen of heaven to assist her rival.", "전해 내려오는 바에 따르면, 장차 아폴론과 아르테미스의 어머니가 될 그녀는 헤라의 분노를 피해 달아나며 에게해의 모든 섬에 쉴 곳을 마련해 달라고 간청했으나, 모든 섬이 하늘의 강력한 여왕을 너무도 두려워한 나머지 그녀의 연적을 돕지 못했다."], ["Delos alone consented to become the birthplace of the future deities.", "오직 델로스만이 장차 태어날 신들의 탄생지가 되는 데 동의했다."], ["Delos was then a floating island; but when Latona arrived there, Jupiter fastened it with adamantine chains to the bottom of the sea, that it might be a secure resting-place for his beloved.", "델로스는 그때 떠다니는 섬이었으나, 레토가 그곳에 다다르자 제우스가 그것을 금강석 사슬로 바다 밑바닥에 붙들어 매어, 사랑하는 이를 위한 안전한 안식처가 되게 했다."], ["Byron alludes to Delos in his “Don Juan”:", "바이런은 「돈 후안」에서 델로스를 언급한다."]], [["“The isles of Greece!", "\"그리스의 섬들이여!"], ["the isles of Greece!", "그리스의 섬들이여!"], ["Where burning Sappho loved and sung, Where grew the arts of war and peace, Where Delos rose and Phœbus sprung!”", "불타는 사포가 사랑하고 노래하던 곳, 전쟁과 평화의 기예가 자라난 곳, 델로스가 솟아오르고 포이보스가 태어난 곳이여!\""], ["————", "————"]], [["PHAËTON was the son of Apollo and the nymph Clymene.", "파에톤은 아폴론과 님프 클리메네 사이에서 태어난 아들이었다."], ["One day a schoolfellow laughed at the idea of his being the son of the god, and Phaëton went in rage and shame and reported it to his mother.", "어느 날 학교 친구 하나가 그가 신의 아들이라는 말을 비웃자, 파에톤은 분노와 수치심에 사로잡혀 어머니에게 그 일을 알렸다."], ["“If,”", "\"어머니,\""], ["said he, “I am indeed of heavenly birth, give me, mother, some proof of it, and establish my claim to the honor.”", "그가 말했다. \"제가 정말로 하늘의 혈통을 타고났다면, 그 증거를 보여 주셔서 제가 그 영예를 주장할 수 있게 해 주세요.\""], ["Clymene stretched forth her hands towards the skies, and said, “I call to witness the Sun which looks down upon us, that I have told you the truth.", "클리메네는 두 손을 하늘을 향해 뻗으며 말했다. \"우리를 굽어보시는 저 태양을 증인으로 부르노니, 나는 네게 진실을 말했다."], ["If I speak falsely, let this be the last time I behold his light.", "만약 내가 거짓을 말한다면, 이것이 내가 그분의 빛을 보는 마지막 순간이 되게 하라."], ["But it needs not much labor to go and inquire for yourself; the land whence the Sun rises lies next to ours.", "하지만 직접 가서 물어보는 데는 그다지 많은 수고가 들지 않을 게다. 태양이 떠오르는 땅은 바로 우리 땅 곁에 있으니 말이다."], ["Go and demand of him whether he will own you as a son.”", "가서 그분께 너를 아들로 인정하실지 여쭈어 보아라.\""], ["Phaëton heard with delight.", "파에톤은 기뻐하며 그 말을 들었다."], ["He travelled to India, which lies directly in the regions of sunrise; and, full of hope and pride, approached the goal whence his parent begins his course.", "그는 해 뜨는 지역에 곧장 자리한 인도로 여행을 떠났고, 희망과 자긍심에 가득 차 아버지가 하루의 운행을 시작하는 그 출발점으로 다가갔다."]], [["The palace of the Sun stood reared aloft on columns, glittering with gold and precious stones, while polished ivory formed the ceilings, and silver the doors.", "태양의 궁전은 기둥들 위에 높이 솟아 황금과 보석으로 반짝였고, 천장은 매끈하게 다듬은 상아로, 문은 은으로 이루어져 있었다."], ["The workmanship surpassed the material;[7] for upon the walls Vulcan had represented earth, sea, and skies, with their inhabitants.", "그러나 솜씨는 재료를 능가했다.* 벽에는 헤파이스토스[로마의 〈불카누스〉]가 대지와 바다와 하늘을, 그리고 그곳에 사는 존재들을 새겨 놓았기 때문이다."], ["In the sea were the nymphs, some sporting in the waves, some riding on the backs of fishes, while others sat upon the rocks and dried their sea-green hair.", "바다에는 님프들이 있었으니, 어떤 이들은 파도 속에서 노닐고, 어떤 이들은 물고기의 등을 타고 있었으며, 또 어떤 이들은 바위 위에 앉아 바다처럼 푸른 머리카락을 말리고 있었다."], ["Their faces were not all alike, nor yet unlike,—but such as sisters’ ought to be.[8] The earth had its towns and forests and rivers and rustic divinities.", "그들의 얼굴은 모두 똑같지도, 그렇다고 서로 전혀 다르지도 않았으니, 자매들이 마땅히 그러해야 할 모습이었다. 대지에는 도시와 숲과 강과 시골의 신들이 있었다."], ["Over all was carved the likeness of the glorious heaven; and on the silver doors the twelve signs of the zodiac, six on each side.", "이 모든 것 위에는 찬란한 하늘의 형상이 새겨져 있었고, 은으로 된 문에는 황도십이궁의 별자리가 양쪽에 여섯씩 새겨져 있었다."]], [["Clymene’s son advanced up the steep ascent, and entered the halls of his disputed father.", "클리메네의 아들은 가파른 오르막을 올라가 다툼의 대상이 된 아버지의 전당으로 들어섰다."], ["He approached the paternal presence, but stopped at a distance, for the light was more than he could bear.", "그는 아버지가 계신 곳으로 다가갔으나, 그 빛이 견딜 수 있는 것 이상이었으므로 멀찍이 떨어진 곳에서 걸음을 멈추었다."], ["Phœbus, arrayed in a purple vesture, sat on a throne, which glittered as with diamonds.", "포이보스는 자줏빛 옷을 두르고, 다이아몬드처럼 번쩍이는 옥좌에 앉아 있었다."], ["On his right hand and his left stood the Day, the Month, and the Year, and, at regular intervals, the Hours.", "그의 좌우에는 〈하루〉와 〈한 달〉과 〈한 해〉가 서 있었고, 일정한 간격을 두고 〈시간〉들이 서 있었다."], ["Spring stood with her head crowned with flowers, and Summer, with garment cast aside, and a garland formed of spears of ripened grain, and Autumn, with his feet stained with grape-juice, and icy Winter, with his hair stiffened with hoar frost.", "〈봄〉은 머리에 꽃의 관을 쓰고 서 있었고, 〈여름〉은 옷을 벗어 던지고 익은 곡식 이삭으로 엮은 화환을 쓰고 있었으며, 〈가을〉은 발이 포도즙으로 물들어 있었고, 차가운 〈겨울〉은 머리카락이 서리로 뻣뻣하게 굳어 있었다."], ["Surrounded by these attendants, the Sun, with the eye that sees everything, beheld the youth dazzled with the novelty and splendor of the scene, and inquired the purpose of his errand.", "이 시종들에 둘러싸인 채, 모든 것을 보는 눈을 지닌 태양은 그 광경의 새로움과 찬란함에 눈이 부신 젊은이를 바라보며 무슨 일로 왔는지 물었다. 젊은이가 대답했다."], ["The youth replied, “O light of the boundless world, Phœbus, my father,—if you permit me to use that name,—give me some proof, I beseech you, by which I may be known as yours.”", "\"오, 끝없는 세상의 빛이시여, 포이보스여, 제 아버지시여 — 제가 그 이름을 쓰는 것을 허락하신다면 — 제가 당신의 아들임을 알 수 있도록, 간청하건대 어떤 증거를 보여 주십시오.\""], ["He ceased; and his father, laying aside the beams that shone all around his head, bade him approach, and embracing him, said, “My son, you deserve not to be disowned, and I confirm what your mother has told you.", "그가 말을 마치자, 그의 아버지는 머리 사방에서 빛나던 광선을 거두고 그를 가까이 오라 불러 끌어안으며 말했다. \"내 아들아, 너는 부인당할 만한 아이가 아니다."], ["To put an end to your doubts, ask what you will, the gift shall be yours.", "나는 네 어머니가 네게 한 말을 확인해 주마. 네 의심을 끝내기 위해, 무엇이든 원하는 것을 청하여라, 그 선물을 네게 주겠다."], ["I call to witness that dreadful lake, which I never saw, but which we gods swear by in our most solemn engagements.”", "내가 한 번도 본 적은 없으나 우리 신들이 가장 엄숙한 약속을 맺을 때 두고 맹세하는 저 무서운 호수를 증인으로 부르노라.\""], ["Phaëton immediately asked to be permitted for one day to drive the chariot of the sun.", "파에톤은 곧바로 하루 동안 태양의 마차를 몰게 해 달라고 청했다."], ["The father repented of his promise; thrice and four times he shook his radiant head in warning.", "아버지는 자신의 약속을 후회했고, 세 번 네 번 빛나는 머리를 가로저으며 경고했다."], ["“I have spoken rashly,”", "\"내가 경솔하게 말했구나,\""], ["said he; “this only request I would fain deny.", "그가 말했다. \"이 한 가지 청만은 거절하고 싶다."], ["I beg you to withdraw it.", "부디 거두어다오."], ["It is not a safe boon, nor one, my Phaëton, suited to your youth and strength.", "그것은 안전한 선물이 아니며, 내 아들 파에톤아, 네 젊음과 힘에 어울리는 것도 아니다."], ["Your lot is mortal, and you ask what is beyond a mortal’s power.", "네 운명은 필멸의 것인데, 너는 필멸자의 능력을 넘어서는 것을 청하고 있다."], ["In your ignorance you aspire to do that which not even the gods themselves may do.", "너는 무지한 탓에 신들 자신조차 할 수 없는 일을 하려 한다."], ["None but myself may drive the flaming car of day.", "나 말고는 그 누구도 하루의 불타는 마차를 몰 수 없다."], ["Not even Jupiter, whose terrible right arm hurls the thunderbolts.", "무서운 오른팔로 벼락을 던지는 제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]조차도 그러하다."], ["The first part of the way is steep, and such as the horses when fresh in the morning can hardly climb; the middle is high up in the heavens, whence I myself can scarcely, without alarm, look down and behold the earth and sea stretched beneath me.", "그 길의 첫 부분은 가파르니, 아침에 기운이 넘치는 말들조차 좀처럼 오르기 힘들 정도다. 한가운데는 하늘 높은 곳에 있어, 나 자신조차 두려움 없이는 아래를 굽어보며 내 발밑에 펼쳐진 대지와 바다를 바라볼 수 없다."], ["The last part of the road descends rapidly, and requires most careful driving.", "길의 마지막 부분은 가파르게 내려가니, 무엇보다 조심스러운 운전이 필요하다."], ["Tethys, who is waiting to receive me, often trembles for me lest I should fall headlong.", "나를 맞이하려 기다리는 테티스는 내가 곤두박질치지 않을까 나를 위해 자주 떤다."], ["Add to all this, the heaven is all the time turning round and carrying the stars with it.", "게다가 하늘은 내내 회전하면서 별들을 함께 데리고 돈다."], ["I have to be perpetually on my guard lest that movement, which sweeps everything else along, should hurry me also away.", "다른 모든 것을 휩쓸어 가는 그 움직임이 나마저 채어 가지 않도록 나는 끊임없이 경계해야 한다."], ["Suppose I should lend you the chariot, what would you do?", "가령 내가 네게 마차를 빌려 준다면, 너는 어떻게 하겠느냐?"], ["Could you keep your course while the sphere was revolving under you?", "천구가 네 아래에서 도는 동안 네가 길을 지킬 수 있겠느냐?"], ["Perhaps you think that there are forests and cities, the abodes of gods, and palaces and temples on the way.", "어쩌면 너는 길에 숲과 도시들, 신들의 거처와 궁전과 신전이 있으리라 생각할지도 모르겠다."], ["On the contrary, the road is through the midst of frightful monsters.", "그러나 그와는 반대로, 그 길은 무시무시한 괴물들 한가운데를 지나간다."], ["You pass by the horns of the Bull, in front of the Archer, and near the Lion’s jaws, and where the Scorpion stretches its arms in one direction and the Crab in another.", "너는 〈황소〉의 뿔 곁을 지나고, 〈궁수〉 앞을 지나며, 〈사자〉의 턱 가까이를, 그리고 〈전갈〉이 한 방향으로 팔을 뻗고 〈게〉가 다른 방향으로 팔을 뻗은 곳을 지나게 된다."], ["Nor will you find it easy to guide those horses, with their breasts full of fire that they breathe forth from their mouths and nostrils.", "또한 입과 콧구멍으로 내뿜는 불로 가슴이 가득한 그 말들을 부리는 일도 쉽지 않으리라."], ["I can scarcely govern them myself, when they are unruly and resist the reins.", "그것들이 다루기 힘들어져 고삐에 저항할 때면 나조차도 좀처럼 다스리지 못한다."], ["Beware, my son, lest I be the donor of a fatal gift; recall your request while yet you may.", "조심하여라, 내 아들아, 내가 치명적인 선물을 주는 자가 되지 않도록 말이다."], ["Do you ask me for a proof that you are sprung from my blood?", "아직 그럴 수 있을 때 네 청을 거두어라. 네가 내 피에서 났다는 증거를 청하느냐?"], ["I give you a proof in my fears for you.", "너를 향한 내 두려움 속에 그 증거를 주마."], ["Look at my face—I would that you could look into my breast, you would there see all a father’s anxiety.", "내 얼굴을 보아라 — 네가 내 가슴 속을 들여다볼 수 있다면, 거기서 아버지의 온갖 근심을 보게 되련만."], ["Finally,”", "끝으로,\""], ["he continued, “look round the world and choose whatever you will of what earth or sea contains most precious—ask it and fear no refusal.", "그가 이어 말했다. \"세상을 둘러보고 대지와 바다가 품은 가장 귀한 것 가운데 무엇이든 골라 보아라 — 그것을 청하면 거절을 두려워하지 않아도 된다."], ["This only I pray you not to urge.", "다만 이 한 가지만은 고집하지 말기를 빈다."], ["It is not honor, but destruction you seek.", "네가 구하는 것은 영예가 아니라 파멸이다."], ["Why do you hang round my neck and still entreat me?", "어찌하여 내 목에 매달려 여전히 간청하느냐?"], ["You shall have it if you persist,—the oath is sworn and must be kept,—but I beg you to choose more wisely.”", "네가 고집한다면 그것을 갖게 되리라 — 맹세는 이미 이루어졌으니 지켜야 한다 — 하지만 부디 더 현명하게 고르기를 바란다.\""]], [["He ended; but the youth rejected all admonition and held to his demand.", "그가 말을 마쳤다. 그러나 젊은이는 모든 충고를 물리치고 자신의 요구를 굽히지 않았다."], ["So, having resisted as long as he could, Phœbus at last led the way to where stood the lofty chariot.", "그리하여 할 수 있는 한 오래 버티던 포이보스는 마침내 높다란 마차가 서 있는 곳으로 그를 이끌었다."]], [["It was of gold, the gift of Vulcan; the axle was of gold, the pole and wheels of gold, the spokes of silver.", "그것은 헤파이스토스가 선물한 황금 마차였다. 굴대도 황금이었고, 끌채와 바퀴도 황금이었으며, 바큇살은 은이었다."], ["Along the seat were rows of chrysolites and diamonds which reflected all around the brightness of the sun.", "좌석을 따라 감람석과 다이아몬드가 줄지어 박혀 태양의 광채를 사방으로 반사했다."], ["While the daring youth gazed in admiration, the early Dawn threw open the purple doors of the east, and showed the pathway strewn with roses.", "대담한 젊은이가 감탄하며 바라보는 동안, 이른 〈새벽〉이 동쪽의 자줏빛 문을 활짝 열어젖히고 장미가 흩뿌려진 길을 드러냈다."], ["The stars withdrew, marshalled by the Day-star, which last of all retired also.", "별들은 〈샛별〉의 인도를 받아 물러갔고, 샛별 자신도 마지막으로 물러갔다."], ["The father, when he saw the earth beginning to glow, and the Moon preparing to retire, ordered the Hours to harness up the horses.", "아버지는 대지가 빛나기 시작하고 〈달〉이 물러갈 채비를 하는 것을 보자, 〈시간〉들에게 말들에 멍에를 메우라 명했다."], ["They obeyed, and led forth from the lofty stalls the steeds full fed with ambrosia, and attached the reins.", "그들은 명에 따라, 신들의 음식을 잔뜩 먹은 준마들을 높은 마구간에서 끌어내어 고삐를 채웠다."], ["Then the father bathed the face of his son with a powerful unguent, and made him capable of enduring the brightness of the flame.", "그러고 나서 아버지는 강력한 연고를 아들의 얼굴에 발라, 불꽃의 광채를 견딜 수 있게 했다. 그는 아들의 머리에 광선을 씌우고는, 불길한 한숨을 내쉬며 말했다."], ["He set the rays on his head, and, with a foreboding sigh, said, “If, my son, you will in this at least heed my advice, spare the whip and hold tight the reins.", "\"내 아들아, 적어도 이 일에서만큼은 내 충고를 따른다면, 채찍은 아끼고 고삐는 단단히 쥐어라. 그것들은 제 스스로 충분히 빨리 달리니, 어려운 일은 그것들을 붙드는 것이다."], ["They go fast enough of their own accord; the labor is to hold them in.", "너는 다섯 개의 둘레 사이를 곧장 지나는 직선 길을 택해서는 안 되고, 왼쪽으로 비껴가야 한다."], ["You are not to take the straight road directly between the five circles, but turn off to the left.", "가운데 띠의 경계 안에 머물면서 북쪽과 남쪽을 똑같이 피하여라. 바퀴 자국이 보일 테니, 그것이 너를 인도하는 데 도움이 될 것이다."], ["Keep within the limit of the middle zone, and avoid the northern and the southern alike.", "그리고 하늘과 대지가 저마다 알맞은 몫의 열기를 받도록, 너무 높이 가지 마라, 그러면 천상의 거처를 태우게 될 것이다."], ["You will see the marks of the wheels, and they will serve to guide you.", "그렇다고 너무 낮게도 가지 마라, 그러면 대지에 불을 지르게 될 것이다."], ["And, that the skies and the earth may each receive their due share of heat, go not too high, or you will burn the heavenly dwellings, nor too low, or you will set the earth on fire; the middle course is safest and best.[9] And now I leave you to your chance, which I hope will plan better for you than you have done for yourself.", "가운데 길이 가장 안전하고 가장 좋다. 그리고 이제 나는 너를 운에 맡기노니, 그 운이 네가 스스로를 위해 꾀한 것보다 더 잘 헤아려 주기를 바란다."], ["Night is passing out of the western gates and we can delay no longer.", "〈밤〉이 서쪽 문을 빠져나가고 있으니 우리는 더 지체할 수 없다."], ["Take the reins; but if at last your heart fails you, and you will benefit by my advice, stay where you are in safety, and suffer me to light and warm the earth.”", "고삐를 잡아라. 하지만 마지막에 네 마음이 약해져 내 충고를 따르고자 한다면, 안전하게 있는 그 자리에 머물러 내가 대지를 비추고 덥히도록 맡겨 두어라.\""], ["The agile youth sprang into the chariot, stood erect, and grasped the reins with delight, pouring out thanks to his reluctant parent.", "날렵한 젊은이는 마차로 뛰어올라 똑바로 서서 고삐를 기쁘게 거머쥐고는, 마지못해 허락한 아버지에게 감사를 쏟아 냈다."]], [["Meanwhile the horses fill the air with their snortings and fiery breath, and stamp the ground impatient.", "그러는 동안 말들은 콧김과 불타는 숨결로 공기를 채우고 초조하게 땅을 굴렀다."], ["Now the bars are let down, and the boundless plain of the universe lies open before them.", "이제 가로막이 내려지고, 우주의 끝없는 평원이 그들 앞에 활짝 펼쳐졌다."], ["They dart forward and cleave the opposing clouds, and outrun the morning breezes which started from the same eastern goal.", "그들은 앞으로 내달려 가로막는 구름을 가르고, 같은 동쪽 출발점에서 출발한 아침의 산들바람을 앞질렀다."], ["The steeds soon perceived that the load they drew was lighter than usual; and as a ship without ballast is tossed hither and thither on the sea, so the chariot, without its accustomed weight, was dashed about as if empty.", "준마들은 곧 자신들이 끄는 짐이 평소보다 가볍다는 것을 알아차렸다. 짐을 싣지 않은 배가 바다 위에서 이리저리 흔들리듯이, 익숙한 무게를 싣지 않은 마차도 빈 것처럼 마구 요동쳤다."], ["They rush headlong and leave the travelled road.", "말들은 곤두박질치듯 내달려 다니던 길을 벗어났다."], ["He is alarmed, and knows not how to guide them; nor, if he knew, has he the power.", "그는 놀라서 그것들을 어떻게 이끌어야 할지 몰랐고, 설령 안다 해도 그럴 힘이 없었다."], ["Then, for the first time, the Great and Little Bear were scorched with heat, and would fain, if it were possible, have plunged into the water; and the Serpent which lies coiled up round the north pole, torpid and harmless, grew warm, and with warmth felt its rage revive.", "그때 처음으로 〈큰곰〉과 〈작은곰〉이 열기에 그을렸고, 할 수만 있다면 물속에 뛰어들고 싶어 했다. 북극 둘레에 똬리를 틀고 무기력하게 누워 해를 끼치지 않던 〈뱀〉도 따뜻해졌고, 그 온기와 더불어 분노가 되살아나는 것을 느꼈다."], ["Boötes, they say, fled away, though encumbered with his plough, and all unused to rapid motion.", "사람들이 말하기를, 〈목동자리〉도 달아났다고 한다. 비록 쟁기에 매여 거추장스럽고 빠른 움직임에는 전혀 익숙하지 않았는데도 말이다."]], [["When hapless Phaëton looked down upon the earth, now spreading in vast extent beneath him, he grew pale and his knees shook with terror.", "불운한 파에톤이 이제 자기 발밑에 광대하게 펼쳐진 대지를 내려다보았을 때, 그는 창백해졌고 무릎이 공포로 떨렸다."], ["In spite of the glare all around him, the sight of his eyes grew dim.", "사방에서 쏟아지는 눈부신 빛에도 불구하고, 그의 눈앞은 흐릿해졌다."], ["He wished he had never touched his father’s horses, never learned his parentage, never prevailed in his request.", "그는 차라리 아버지의 말들을 결코 만지지 않았더라면, 자신의 혈통을 결코 알지 못했더라면, 자신의 청을 결코 관철하지 못했더라면 하고 바랐다."], ["He is borne along like a vessel that flies before a tempest, when the pilot can do no more and betakes himself to his prayers.", "그는 폭풍 앞에서 도망치는 배처럼 휩쓸려 갔으니, 그럴 때면 키잡이는 더 이상 손쓸 도리가 없어 기도에 의지할 따름이다."], ["What shall he do?", "그는 무엇을 해야 하는가?"], ["Much of the heavenly road is left behind, but more remains before.", "하늘 길의 많은 부분이 뒤에 남겨졌으나, 더 많은 부분이 앞에 남아 있다."], ["He turns his eyes from one direction to the other; now to the goal whence he began his course, now to the realms of sunset which he is not destined to reach.", "그는 이쪽저쪽으로 눈을 돌렸으니, 한 번은 자신이 운행을 시작한 출발점으로, 또 한 번은 자신이 도달할 운명이 아닌 해 지는 영역으로 향했다."], ["He loses his self-command, and knows not what to do,—whether to draw tight the reins or throw them loose; he forgets the names of the horses.", "그는 자제력을 잃고 무엇을 해야 할지 몰랐으니 — 고삐를 단단히 당겨야 할지 느슨하게 놓아야 할지 알 수 없었고, 말들의 이름도 잊어버렸다."], ["He sees with terror the monstrous forms scattered over the surface of heaven.", "그는 하늘 표면에 흩어져 있는 괴물 같은 형상들을 공포에 질려 바라보았다."], ["Here the Scorpion extended his two great arms, with his tail and crooked claws stretching over two signs of the zodiac.", "그곳에서 〈전갈〉이 두 개의 거대한 팔을 뻗고, 꼬리와 굽은 집게가 황도의 두 별자리에 걸쳐 펼쳐져 있었다."], ["When the boy beheld him, reeking with poison and menacing with his fangs, his courage failed, and the reins fell from his hands.", "소년이 독을 내뿜으며 송곳니로 위협하는 그것을 보았을 때, 그의 용기는 꺾였고 고삐가 그의 손에서 떨어졌다."], ["The horses, when they felt them loose on their backs, dashed headlong, and unrestrained went off into unknown regions of the sky, in among the stars, hurling the chariot over pathless places, now up in high heaven, now down almost to the earth.", "말들은 고삐가 등 위에서 느슨해진 것을 느끼자 곤두박질치듯 내달려, 제지받지 않은 채 하늘의 알 수 없는 영역으로, 별들 사이로 달려 들어갔고, 길도 없는 곳들 위로 마차를 내던지듯 몰아, 어느 때는 하늘 높이, 어느 때는 거의 대지에까지 내려왔다."], ["The moon saw with astonishment her brother’s chariot running beneath her own.", "달은 자기 오라비의 마차가 자기 마차 아래로 내달리는 것을 놀라움에 차서 바라보았다."], ["The clouds begin to smoke, and the mountain tops take fire; the fields are parched with heat, the plants wither, the trees with their leafy branches burn, the harvest is ablaze!", "구름은 연기를 뿜기 시작하고, 산꼭대기는 불이 붙었다. 들판은 열기에 메말랐고, 식물은 시들었으며, 잎이 무성한 가지를 단 나무들은 타올랐고, 곡식은 불길에 휩싸였다!"], ["But these are small things.", "그러나 이것들은 작은 일이었다."], ["Great cities perished, with their walls and towers; whole nations with their people were consumed to ashes!", "거대한 도시들이 성벽과 탑과 함께 멸망했고, 온 민족이 그 백성과 더불어 잿더미가 되었다!"], ["The forest-clad mountains burned, Athos and Taurus and Tmolus and Œte; Ida, once celebrated for fountains, but now all dry; the Muses’ mountain Helicon, and Hæmus; Ætna, with fires within and without, and Parnassus, with his two peaks, and Rhodope, forced at last to part with his snowy crown.", "숲으로 덮인 산들이 불탔으니, 아토스와 타우로스와 트몰로스와 오이테가 그러했고, 한때 샘들로 유명했으나 이제는 온통 메말라 버린 이데가 그러했으며, 무사 여신들의 산 헬리콘과 하이모스가 그러했고, 안팎으로 불을 품은 아이트나와 두 봉우리를 지닌 파르나소스, 그리고 마침내 눈 덮인 관을 잃어야 했던 로도페가 그러했다."], ["Her cold climate was no protection to Scythia, Caucasus burned, and Ossa and Pindus, and, greater than both, Olympus; the Alps high in air, and the Apennines crowned with clouds.", "차가운 기후도 스키티아를 지켜 주지 못했고, 카우카소스가 불탔으며, 오사와 핀도스가, 그리고 그 둘보다 더 큰 올림포스가 불탔고, 공중에 높이 솟은 알프스와 구름이 관처럼 덮인 아펜니노스가 불탔다."]], [["Then Phaëton beheld the world on fire, and felt the heat intolerable.", "그때 파에톤은 세상이 불타는 것을 보았고, 견딜 수 없는 열기를 느꼈다."], ["The air he breathed was like the air of a furnace and full of burning ashes, and the smoke was of a pitchy darkness.", "그가 들이쉬는 공기는 용광로의 공기와도 같았고 불타는 재로 가득했으며, 연기는 칠흑같이 어두웠다."], ["He dashed forward he knew not whither.", "그는 어디로 가는지도 모른 채 앞으로 내달렸다."], ["Then, it is believed, the people of Æthiopia became black by the blood being forced so suddenly to the surface, and the Libyan desert was dried up to the condition in which it remains to this day.", "그때 사람들이 믿기로는, 에티오피아의 사람들이 피가 너무도 갑작스럽게 표면으로 솟구쳐 검게 되었고, 리비아 사막은 오늘날까지 그대로 남아 있는 상태로 메말라 버렸다."], ["The Nymphs of the fountains, with dishevelled hair, mourned their waters, nor were the rivers safe beneath their banks: Tanais smoked, and Caicus, Xanthus, and Meander; Babylonian Euphrates and Ganges, Tagus with golden sands, and Caÿster where the swans resort.", "샘의 님프들은 머리를 풀어헤친 채 자신들의 물을 애도했고, 강들도 그 둑 아래에서 안전하지 못했다. 타나이스가 연기를 뿜었고, 카이코스와 크산토스와 마이안드로스도 그러했으며, 바빌론의 에우프라테스와 갠지스, 황금 모래를 품은 타구스, 그리고 백조들이 모여드는 카위스트로스도 그러했다."], ["Nile fled away and hid his head in the desert, and there it still remains concealed.", "나일은 달아나 사막에 머리를 감추었고, 그곳에 지금도 그것이 숨겨진 채 남아 있다."], ["Where he used to discharge his waters through seven mouths into the sea, there seven dry channels alone remained.", "그가 일곱 개의 어귀를 통해 바다로 물을 쏟아 내던 곳에는, 이제 일곱 개의 메마른 물길만이 남았다."], ["The earth cracked open, and through the chinks light broke into Tartarus, and frightened the king of shadows and his queen.", "대지가 갈라져 벌어졌고, 그 틈을 통해 빛이 타르타로스로 새어 들어가, 그림자들의 왕과 그 왕비를 놀라게 했다."], ["The sea shrank up.", "바다는 오그라들었다."], ["Where before was water, it became a dry plain; and the mountains that lie beneath the waves lifted up their heads and became islands.", "전에 물이 있던 곳은 메마른 평원이 되었고, 파도 아래 누워 있던 산들은 머리를 들어 올려 섬이 되었다."], ["The fishes sought the lowest depths, and the dolphins no longer ventured as usual to sport on the surface.", "물고기들은 가장 깊은 곳을 찾아갔고, 돌고래들도 더 이상 평소처럼 감히 표면에서 노닐려 하지 않았다."], ["Even Nereus, and his wife Doris, with the Nereids, their daughters, sought the deepest caves for refuge.", "네레우스와 그의 아내 도리스, 그리고 그들의 딸들인 네레이데스조차도 가장 깊은 동굴로 피신처를 찾아갔다."], ["Thrice Neptune essayed to raise his head above the surface, and thrice was driven back by the heat.", "포세이돈[로마의 〈넵투누스〉]은 세 번이나 표면 위로 머리를 들려고 애썼으나, 세 번 다 열기에 밀려 물러났다."], ["Earth, surrounded as she was by waters, yet with head and shoulders bare, screening her face with her hand, looked up to heaven, and with a husky voice called on Jupiter:", "대지는 비록 물에 둘러싸여 있었으나 머리와 어깨를 드러낸 채, 손으로 얼굴을 가리고 하늘을 우러러보며 쉰 목소리로 제우스를 불렀다."]], [["“O ruler of the gods, if I have deserved this treatment, and it is your will that I perish with fire, why withhold your thunderbolts?", "\"오, 신들의 통치자시여, 제가 이런 대접을 받아 마땅하고 제가 불에 멸망하는 것이 당신의 뜻이라면, 어찌하여 벼락을 거두고 계십니까?"], ["Let me at least fall by your hand.", "적어도 당신의 손에 쓰러지게 해 주십시오."], ["Is this the reward of my fertility, of my obedient service?", "이것이 제 비옥함에 대한, 제 순종하는 봉사에 대한 보답입니까?"], ["Is it for this that I have supplied herbage for cattle, and fruits for men, and frankincense for your altars?", "제가 가축에게 풀을, 사람에게 열매를, 당신의 제단에 유향을 바쳐 온 것이 이런 결과를 위해서였습니까? 그러나 제가 돌봄을 받을 가치가 없다 해도, 제 형제 오케아노스가 이런 운명을 받아 마땅할 무슨 짓을 했단 말입니까?"], ["But if I am unworthy of regard, what has my brother Ocean done to deserve such a fate?", "우리 둘 중 누구도 당신의 연민을 불러일으키지 못한다면, 부디 당신 자신의 하늘을 생각해 보십시오."], ["If neither of us can excite your pity, think, I pray you, of your own heaven, and behold how both the poles are smoking which sustain your palace, which must fall if they be destroyed.", "당신의 궁전을 떠받치는 두 극점이 어떻게 연기를 뿜고 있는지 보십시오. 그것들이 파괴되면 궁전도 무너질 것입니다."], ["Atlas faints, and scarce holds up his burden.", "아틀라스가 기진하여 제 짐을 좀처럼 떠받치지 못합니다."], ["If sea, earth, and heaven perish, we fall into ancient Chaos.", "바다와 대지와 하늘이 멸망한다면, 우리는 태고의 〈혼돈〉 속으로 떨어집니다."], ["Save what yet remains to us from the devouring flame.", "우리에게 아직 남은 것을 집어삼키는 불길로부터 구해 주십시오."], ["O, take thought for our deliverance in this awful moment!”", "오, 이 무서운 순간에 우리의 구원을 헤아려 주십시오!\""]], [["Thus spoke Earth, and overcome with heat and thirst, could say no more.", "이렇게 대지가 말했으나, 열기와 갈증에 압도되어 더 이상 말할 수 없었다."], ["Then Jupiter omnipotent, calling to witness all the gods, including him who had lent the chariot, and showing them that all was lost unless speedy remedy were applied, mounted the lofty tower from whence he diffuses clouds over the earth, and hurls the forked lightnings.", "그러자 전능하신 제우스는, 마차를 빌려 준 자를 포함하여 모든 신들을 증인으로 부르고, 신속한 처방이 가해지지 않으면 모든 것이 끝장이라는 것을 그들에게 보여 준 뒤, 대지 위에 구름을 펼치고 갈래진 번개를 내던지는 높은 탑에 올랐다."], ["But at that time not a cloud was to be found to interpose for a screen to earth, nor was a shower remaining unexhausted.", "그러나 그때는 대지를 가려 줄 가림막으로 끼어들 구름 한 점 찾을 수 없었고, 다 쏟아 내지 않고 남은 소나기 한 줄기 없었다."], ["He thundered, and brandishing a lightning bolt in his right hand launched it against the charioteer, and struck him at the same moment from his seat and from existence!", "그는 천둥을 울렸고, 오른손에 번개를 휘둘러 마차몰이를 향해 던져, 그를 좌석에서, 그리고 존재로부터 동시에 쳐 떨어뜨렸다!"], ["Phaëton, with his hair on fire, fell headlong, like a shooting star which marks the heavens with its brightness as it falls, and Eridanus, the great river, received him and cooled his burning frame.", "머리에 불이 붙은 파에톤은, 떨어지면서 그 광채로 하늘에 자국을 남기는 별똥별처럼 곤두박질쳤고, 거대한 강 에리다노스가 그를 받아들여 불타는 몸을 식혀 주었다."], ["The Italian Naiads reared a tomb for him, and inscribed these words upon the stone:", "이탈리아의 나이아데스가 그를 위해 무덤을 세우고, 돌 위에 이런 글귀를 새겼다."]], [["“Driver of Phœbus’ chariot. Phaëton, Struck by Jove’s thunder, rests beneath this stone.", "\"포이보스의 마차몰이꾼 파에톤이, 유피테르의 벼락에 맞아 이 돌 아래 잠들다."], ["He could not rule his father’s car of fire, Yet was it much so nobly to aspire.”[10]", "그는 아버지의 불의 마차를 다스리지 못했으나, 그토록 고귀하게 뜻을 품었음은 적지 않은 일이었네.\""]], [["His sisters, the Heliades, as they lamented his fate, were turned into poplar trees, on the banks of the river, and their tears, which continued to flow, became amber as they dropped into the stream.", "그의 누이들인 헬리아데스는 그의 운명을 슬퍼하다가 강둑의 포플러 나무로 변했고, 끊임없이 흐르던 그들의 눈물은 시냇물 속으로 떨어지면서 호박이 되었다."]], [["Milman, in his poem of “Samor,” makes the following allusion to Phaëton’s story:", "밀먼은 그의 시 「사모르」에서 파에톤의 이야기를 다음과 같이 암시한다."]], [["“As when the palsied universe aghast Lay . . . mute and still, When drove, so poets sing, the Sun-born youth Devious through Heaven’s affrighted signs his sire’s Ill-granted chariot. Him the Thunderer hurled From th’ empyrean headlong to the gulf Of the half-parched Eridanus, where weep Even now the sister trees their amber tears O’er Phaëton untimely dead.”", "\"마비된 우주가 경악하여 말없이 고요히 누워 있을 때처럼······ 시인들이 노래하듯, 태양에서 난 젊은이가 하늘의 겁먹은 별자리들을 가로질러 아버지가 잘못 허락한 마차를 빗나가게 몰았을 때. 〈천둥을 부리는 이〉가 그를 가장 높은 하늘에서 곤두박질쳐 반쯤 메마른 에리다노스의 심연으로 내던졌으니, 그곳에서 누이 나무들이 지금도 호박의 눈물을 흘리네 때 이르게 죽은 파에톤을 위하여.\""]], [["In the beautiful lines of Walter Savage Landor, descriptive of the Sea-shell, there is an allusion to the Sun’s palace and chariot.", "월터 새비지 랜더의 아름다운 시구, 〈바다 조개〉를 묘사한 대목에는 태양의 궁전과 마차에 대한 암시가 있다."], ["The water-nymph says:", "물의 님프는 이렇게 말한다."]], [["“. .", "\"······나는 진주빛 색조를 띤 구불구불한 조개들을 품고 있어요, 그리고 태양의 궁전 현관에서 광채를 빨아들인 것들도 품고 있지요, 그곳에서 멍에를 벗으면 그분의 마차 바퀴가 파도 한가운데에 멈춰 서지요."], [". I have sinuous shells of pearly hue Within, and things that lustre have imbibed In the sun’s palace porch, where when unyoked His chariot wheel stands midway on the wave.", "하나를 흔들어 보면 그것이 깨어나요, 그러면 그 매끄러운 입술을 귀 기울인 당신의 귀에 대 보세요, 그러면 그것은 자신의 장엄한 거처를 기억하고, 그곳의 대양이 속삭이듯 속삭인답니다.\""], ["Shake one and it awakens; then apply Its polished lip to your attentive ear, And it remembers its august abodes, And murmurs as the ocean murmurs there.” —Gebir, Book I.", "─「게비르」, 제1권."]], [["————", "────"]], [["# MIDAS—BAUCIS AND PHILEMON", "# MIDAS—BAUCIS AND PHILEMON"]], [["BACCHUS, on a certain occasion, found his old school-master and foster-father, Silenus, missing.", "디오니소스[로마의 〈바쿠스〉]는 어느 날 자신의 옛 스승이자 양아버지인 실레노스가 사라진 것을 알게 되었다."], ["The old man had been drinking, and in that state wandered away, and was found by some peasants, who carried him to their king, Midas.", "그 노인은 술을 마신 뒤 그 상태로 떠돌아다니다가 몇몇 농부에게 발견되었는데, 농부들은 그를 자기네 왕인 미다스에게 데려갔다."], ["Midas recognized him, and treated him hospitably, entertaining him for ten days and nights with an unceasing round of jollity.", "미다스는 그를 알아보고 정중하게 대접하여, 열흘 밤낮을 끊임없는 잔치로 융숭히 맞이했다."], ["On the eleventh day he brought Silenus back, and restored him in safety to his pupil.", "열하루째 되는 날 그는 실레노스를 데리고 돌아와, 무사히 그 제자에게 돌려보냈다."], ["Whereupon Bacchus offered Midas his choice of a reward, whatever he might wish.", "그러자 디오니소스는 미다스에게 무엇이든 원하는 것을 보답으로 고르라고 했다."], ["He asked that whatever he might touch should be changed into gold.", "미다스는 자신이 무엇을 만지든 그것이 황금으로 바뀌게 해 달라고 청했다."], ["Bacchus consented, though sorry that he had not made a better choice.", "디오니소스는 더 나은 선택을 하지 않은 것을 안타까워하면서도 그 청을 들어주었다."], ["Midas went his way, rejoicing in his new-acquired power, which he hastened to put to the test.", "미다스는 새로 얻은 능력에 기뻐하며 길을 떠났고, 서둘러 그것을 시험해 보았다."], ["He could scarce believe his eyes when he found a twig of an oak, which he plucked from the branch, become gold in his hand.", "그는 떡갈나무 가지에서 잔가지 하나를 꺾었는데 그것이 손안에서 황금으로 변하는 것을 보고 자기 눈을 믿을 수 없었다."], ["He took up a stone; it changed to gold.", "그가 돌멩이 하나를 집어 드니 그것도 황금이 되었다."], ["He touched a sod; it did the same.", "그가 잔디 한 떼를 건드리니 그것도 마찬가지였다."], ["He took an apple from the tree; you would have thought he had robbed the garden of the Hesperides.", "그가 나무에서 사과 하나를 따니, 누구라도 그가 헤스페리데스의 정원을 약탈했다고 여겼으리라."], ["His joy knew no bounds, and as soon as he got home, he ordered the servants to set a splendid repast on the table.", "그의 기쁨은 끝이 없었고, 집에 돌아오자마자 그는 하인들에게 식탁에 성대한 식사를 차리라고 명했다."], ["Then he found to his dismay that whether he touched bread, it hardened in his hand; or put a morsel to his lips, it defied his teeth.", "그런데 그는 낭패스럽게도 빵을 만지면 손안에서 빵이 딱딱해지고, 한 조각을 입술에 갖다 대면 이로 깨물 수가 없다는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["He took a glass of wine, but it flowed down his throat like melted gold.", "그가 포도주 한 잔을 들었으나, 그것은 녹은 황금처럼 목구멍으로 흘러내렸다."]], [["In consternation at the unprecedented affliction, he strove to divest himself of his power; he hated the gift he had lately coveted.", "전례 없는 이 고통에 경악한 그는 자신의 능력을 떨쳐 버리려 애썼다. 그는 얼마 전까지 그토록 탐내던 그 선물을 미워하게 되었다."], ["But all in vain; starvation seemed to await him.", "그러나 모든 것이 헛수고였으니, 굶주림이 그를 기다리는 듯했다."], ["He raised his arms, all shining with gold, in prayer to Bacchus, begging to be delivered from his glittering destruction.", "그는 온통 황금으로 번쩍이는 두 팔을 들어 디오니소스에게 기도하며, 이 반짝이는 파멸에서 자신을 구해 달라고 빌었다."], ["Bacchus, merciful deity, heard and consented.", "자비로운 신 디오니소스는 그 청을 듣고 들어주었다."], ["“Go,”", "\"가거라.\""], ["said he, “to the River Pactolus, trace the stream to its fountain-head, there plunge your head and body in, and wash away your fault and its punishment.”", "그가 말했다. \"팍톨로스 강으로 가서, 물줄기를 따라 그 발원지까지 거슬러 올라가, 거기서 머리와 몸을 물에 담그고, 너의 잘못과 그 벌을 함께 씻어 내거라.\""], ["He did so, and scarce had he touched the waters before the gold-creating power passed into them, and the river-sands became changed into gold, as they remain to this day.", "미다스는 그대로 했고, 물에 닿자마자 황금을 만들어 내는 능력이 그 물속으로 옮겨 가니, 강의 모래는 황금으로 변하여 오늘날까지도 그대로 남아 있다."]], [["Thenceforth Midas, hating wealth and splendor, dwelt in the country, and became a worshipper of Pan, the god of the fields.", "그때부터 미다스는 부와 화려함을 싫어하며 시골에 머물렀고, 들판의 신 판을 섬기는 자가 되었다."], ["On a certain occasion Pan had the temerity to compare his music with that of Apollo, and to challenge the god of the lyre to a trial of skill.", "어느 날 판은 자신의 음악을 아폴론의 음악에 견주는 무모함을 부려, 리라의 신에게 솜씨를 겨루자고 도전했다."], ["The challenge was accepted, and Tmolus, the mountain god, was chosen umpire.", "도전은 받아들여졌고, 산의 신 트몰로스가 심판으로 뽑혔다."], ["The senior took his seat, and cleared away the trees from his ears to listen.", "이 연장자는 자리에 앉아, 귀에서 나무들을 치워 들을 수 있게 했다."], ["At a given signal Pan blew on his pipes, and with his rustic melody gave great satisfaction to himself and his faithful follower Midas, who happened to be present.", "신호가 떨어지자 판은 자신의 피리를 불었고, 그 투박한 가락으로 자기 자신과 마침 그 자리에 있던 충실한 추종자 미다스를 크게 만족시켰다."], ["Then Tmolus turned his head toward the Sun-god, and all his trees turned with him.", "그러자 트몰로스가 태양신 쪽으로 머리를 돌리니, 그의 모든 나무도 그와 함께 돌아섰다."], ["Apollo rose, his brow wreathed with Parnassian laurel, while his robe of Tyrian purple swept the ground.", "아폴론이 일어섰는데, 그의 이마에는 파르나소스의 월계관이 둘려 있었고, 티로스산 자줏빛 옷자락은 땅을 쓸었다."], ["In his left hand he held the lyre, and with his right hand struck the strings.", "그는 왼손에 리라를 들고, 오른손으로 줄을 튕겼다."], ["Ravished with the harmony, Tmolus at once awarded the victory to the god of the lyre, and all but Midas acquiesced in the judgment.", "그 선율에 넋을 빼앗긴 트몰로스는 즉시 리라의 신에게 승리를 안겼고, 미다스를 제외한 모두가 그 판정에 동의했다."], ["He dissented, and questioned the justice of the award.", "그는 동의하지 않았고, 그 판정의 정당함에 이의를 제기했다."], ["Apollo would not suffer such a depraved pair of ears any longer to wear the human form, but caused them to increase in length, grow hairy, within and without, and movable on their roots; in short, to be on the perfect pattern of those of an ass.", "아폴론은 그토록 저열한 귀 한 쌍이 더 이상 인간의 모습을 지니는 것을 용납하지 않았으니, 그 귀들이 길게 늘어나고, 안팎으로 털이 자라며, 뿌리에서 움직일 수 있게 했다. 한마디로, 당나귀의 귀와 완전히 똑같은 모양이 되도록 한 것이다."]], [["Mortified enough was King Midas at this mishap; but he consoled himself with the thought that it was possible to hide his misfortune, which he attempted to do by means of an ample turban or head-dress.", "이 불행에 미다스 왕은 적잖이 굴욕을 느꼈지만, 자신의 불운을 감출 수 있으리라는 생각으로 스스로를 위로했다. 그는 넉넉한 터번 혹은 머리쓰개를 써서 그렇게 하려고 했다."], ["But his hairdresser of course knew the secret.", "그러나 그의 이발사만은 당연히 그 비밀을 알고 있었다."], ["He was charged not to mention it, and threatened with dire punishment if he presumed to disobey.", "이발사는 그 일을 입 밖에 내지 말라는 명을 받았으며, 감히 어기려 들면 끔찍한 벌을 받으리라는 위협도 함께 받았다."], ["But he found it too much for his discretion to keep such a secret; so he went out into the meadow, dug a hole in the ground, and stooping down, whispered the story, and covered it up.", "그러나 그는 그런 비밀을 지키는 것이 자신의 분별력으로는 너무 벅차다는 것을 알게 되었다. 그래서 그는 풀밭으로 나가 땅에 구멍을 파고, 몸을 굽혀 그 이야기를 속삭인 다음 흙으로 덮어 버렸다."], ["Before long a thick bed of reeds sprang up in the meadow, and as soon as it had gained its growth, began whispering the story, and has continued to do so, from that day to this, every time a breeze passes over the place.", "머지않아 그 풀밭에 갈대가 빽빽이 돋아났고, 다 자라자마자 그 이야기를 속삭이기 시작하여, 그날 이후 오늘날까지 그곳에 산들바람이 지날 때마다 그렇게 속삭여 오고 있다."]], [["The story of King Midas has been told by others with some variations.", "미다스 왕의 이야기는 다른 사람들에 의해 약간씩 변형되어 전해지기도 했다."], ["Dryden, in the “Wife of Bath’s Tale,” makes Midas’s queen the betrayer of the secret:", "드라이든은 「바스 여인의 이야기」에서 미다스의 왕비를 비밀을 누설한 자로 삼는다."]], [["“This Midas knew, and durst communicate To none but to his wife his ears of state.”", "\"이 미다스는 그것을 알았으나, 감히 털어놓을 수 있었던 것은 오직 아내에게뿐, 자신의 위엄 어린 귀에 관해서.\""]], [["Midas was king of Phrygia.", "미다스는 프리기아의 왕이었다."], ["He was the son of Gordius, a poor countryman, who was taken by the people and made king, in obedience to the command of the oracle, which had said that their future king should come in a wagon.", "그는 고르디오스의 아들이었는데, 고르디오스는 가난한 시골 사람이었으나 백성들에게 추대되어 왕이 되었으니, 이는 장차 그들의 왕이 수레를 타고 오리라고 했던 신탁의 명에 따른 것이었다."], ["While the people were deliberating, Gordius with his wife and son came driving his wagon into the public square.", "백성들이 논의하고 있는 동안, 고르디오스가 아내와 아들을 데리고 수레를 몰아 광장으로 들어왔던 것이다."]], [["Gordius, being made king, dedicated his wagon to the deity of the oracle, and tied it up in its place with a fast knot.", "왕이 된 고르디오스는 자신의 수레를 신탁의 신에게 바치고, 그것을 단단한 매듭으로 제자리에 묶어 두었다."], ["This was the celebrated Gordian knot, which, in after times it was said, whoever should untie should become lord of all Asia.", "이것이 그 유명한 고르디오스의 매듭으로, 훗날 누구든 이것을 푸는 자가 온 아시아의 주인이 되리라는 말이 전해졌다."], ["Many tried to untie it, but none succeeded, till Alexander the Great, in his career of conquest, came to Phrygia.", "많은 이들이 그것을 풀려 했으나 아무도 성공하지 못했는데, 마침내 알렉산드로스 대왕이 정복의 행로 가운데 프리기아에 이르렀다."], ["He tried his skill with as ill success as others, till growing impatient he drew his sword and cut the knot.", "그도 다른 이들 못지않게 실패만 거듭하며 솜씨를 시험하다가, 참다못해 칼을 뽑아 매듭을 잘라 버렸다."], ["When he afterwards succeeded in subjecting all Asia to his sway, people began to think that he had complied with the terms of the oracle according to its true meaning.", "훗날 그가 온 아시아를 자신의 지배 아래 복속시키는 데 성공하자, 사람들은 그가 신탁의 참뜻에 따라 그 조건을 충족시켰다고 여기기 시작했다. 바우키스와 필레몬"]], [["# BAUCIS AND PHILEMON", "# BAUCIS AND PHILEMON"]], [["On a certain hill in Phrygia stands a linden tree and an oak, enclosed by a low wall.", "프리기아의 어느 언덕에 보리수 한 그루와 떡갈나무 한 그루가 낮은 담장에 둘러싸여 서 있다."], ["Not far from the spot is a marsh, formerly good habitable land, but now indented with pools, the resort of fen-birds and cormorants.", "그곳에서 멀지 않은 데에 늪이 하나 있는데, 예전에는 사람이 살 만한 좋은 땅이었으나 지금은 못이 군데군데 패여, 늪새와 가마우지가 모여드는 곳이 되었다."], ["Once on a time Jupiter, in human shape, visited this country, and with him his son Mercury (he of the caduceus), without his wings.", "옛날 어느 때에 제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]가 인간의 모습으로 이 고장을 찾아왔는데, 그와 함께 그의 아들 헤르메스[로마의 〈메르쿠리우스〉], 곧 카두케우스를 지닌 신이 날개 없이 동행했다."], ["They presented themselves, as weary travellers, at many a door, seeking rest and shelter, but found all closed, for it was late, and the inhospitable inhabitants would not rouse themselves to open for their reception.", "그들은 지친 나그네의 모습으로 여러 집 문 앞에 나타나 쉴 곳과 머물 곳을 구했으나, 모든 문이 닫혀 있음을 보았으니, 때는 이미 늦었고 손님을 박대하는 그 주민들이 일어나 그들을 맞으려고 문을 열어 주지 않았기 때문이다."], ["At last a humble mansion received them, a small thatched cottage, where Baucis, a pious old dame, and her husband Philemon, united when young, had grown old together.", "마침내 한 초라한 거처가 그들을 받아 주었으니, 작은 초가집이었다. 그곳에는 경건한 노부인 바우키스와 그녀의 남편 필레몬이 젊어서 맺어져 함께 늙어 가고 있었다."], ["Not ashamed of their poverty, they made it endurable by moderate desires and kind dispositions.", "그들은 자신들의 가난을 부끄러워하지 않았고, 욕심을 줄이고 마음을 곱게 씀으로써 그 가난을 견딜 만한 것으로 만들었다."], ["One need not look there for master or for servant; they two were the whole household, master and servant alike.", "그곳에서는 주인이나 하인을 따로 찾을 필요가 없었으니, 그 두 사람이 온 식구로서 주인이자 하인이었다."], ["When the two heavenly guests crossed the humble threshold, and bowed their heads to pass under the low door, the old man placed a seat, on which Baucis, bustling and attentive, spread a cloth, and begged them to sit down.", "두 천상의 손님이 그 초라한 문지방을 넘으며 낮은 문 아래를 지나려고 고개를 숙이자, 노인은 자리 하나를 놓았고, 바우키스가 부지런히 마음을 써서 그 위에 천을 펴고는 그들에게 앉기를 청했다."], ["Then she raked out the coals from the ashes, and kindled up a fire, fed it with leaves and dry bark, and with her scanty breath blew it into a flame.", "그런 다음 그녀는 잿더미에서 숯을 긁어내어 불을 지피고, 나뭇잎과 마른 껍질로 불을 키웠으며, 가쁜 숨을 불어넣어 불꽃을 일으켰다."], ["She brought out of a corner split sticks and dry branches, broke them up, and placed them under the small kettle.", "그녀는 구석에서 쪼갠 막대기와 마른 가지를 꺼내 와 부러뜨려 작은 솥 밑에 놓았다."], ["Her husband collected some pot-herbs in the garden, and she shred them from the stalks, and prepared them for the pot.", "그녀의 남편은 텃밭에서 채소를 좀 거두어 왔고, 그녀는 그것을 줄기에서 다듬어 솥에 넣을 채비를 했다."], ["He reached down with a forked stick a flitch of bacon hanging in the chimney, cut a small piece, and put it in the pot to boil with the herbs, setting away the rest for another time.", "남편은 끝이 갈라진 막대기로 굴뚝에 매달린 베이컨 한 덩이를 내려, 작은 조각 하나를 잘라 채소와 함께 끓이려고 솥에 넣고, 나머지는 다음을 위해 따로 치워 두었다."], ["A beechen bowl was filled with warm water, that their guests might wash.", "너도밤나무로 만든 대접에는 따뜻한 물이 채워졌으니, 손님들이 씻을 수 있게 하기 위함이었다."], ["While all was doing, they beguiled the time with conversation.", "모든 일이 진행되는 동안, 그들은 이야기를 나누며 시간을 보냈다."]], [["On the bench designed for the guests was laid a cushion stuffed with sea-weed; and a cloth, only produced on great occasions, but ancient and coarse enough, was spread over that.", "손님들을 위해 마련된 의자에는 해초를 채운 방석이 놓였고, 큰일이 있을 때에만 꺼내는, 그러나 충분히 오래되고 거친 천 한 장이 그 위에 펼쳐졌다."], ["The old lady, with her apron on, with trembling hand set the table.", "노부인은 앞치마를 두른 채 떨리는 손으로 식탁을 차렸다."], ["One leg was shorter than the rest, but a piece of slate put under restored the level.", "다리 하나가 나머지보다 짧았으나, 그 밑에 석판 조각 하나를 받치니 수평이 잡혔다."], ["When fixed, she rubbed the table down with some sweet-smelling herbs.", "자리가 잡히자 그녀는 향기로운 풀로 식탁을 닦았다."], ["Upon it she set some of chaste Minerva’s olives, some cornel berries preserved in vinegar, and added radishes and cheese, with eggs lightly cooked in the ashes.", "그 위에 그녀는 정결한 아테나[로마의 〈미네르바〉]의 올리브 몇 알, 식초에 절인 산딸기 몇 알을 차렸고, 무와 치즈를 더했으며, 잿불에 살짝 익힌 달걀도 곁들였다."], ["All were served in earthen dishes, and an earthenware pitcher, with wooden cups, stood beside them.", "모든 것이 흙으로 빚은 그릇에 담겨 나왔고, 흙으로 만든 물병과 나무 잔이 그 곁에 놓였다."], ["When all was ready, the stew, smoking hot, was set on the table.", "모든 준비가 끝나자, 김이 모락모락 나는 뜨거운 스튜가 식탁에 올랐다."], ["Some wine, not of the oldest, was added; and for dessert, apples and wild honey; and over and above all, friendly faces, and simple but hearty welcome.", "가장 오래된 것은 아니나 포도주도 곁들여졌고, 후식으로는 사과와 야생 꿀이 나왔다. 그리고 그 모든 것을 넘어, 다정한 얼굴들과 소박하지만 진심 어린 환대가 있었다."]], [["Now while the repast proceeded, the old folks were astonished to see that the wine, as fast as it was poured out, renewed itself in the pitcher, of its own accord.", "식사가 진행되는 동안, 노부부는 포도주가 따라지는 족족 저절로 물병 안에서 다시 채워지는 것을 보고 놀랐다."], ["Struck with terror, Baucis and Philemon recognized their heavenly guests, fell on their knees, and with clasped hands implored forgiveness for their poor entertainment.", "두려움에 사로잡힌 바우키스와 필레몬은 자신들의 천상의 손님을 알아보고, 무릎을 꿇고 두 손을 맞잡아 변변찮은 대접을 용서해 달라고 빌었다."], ["There was an old goose, which they kept as the guardian of their humble cottage; and they bethought them to make this a sacrifice in honor of their guests.", "그들에게는 초라한 오두막의 파수꾼으로 기르던 늙은 거위 한 마리가 있었다. 그들은 이 거위를 손님들을 기리는 제물로 바치기로 생각했다."], ["But the goose, too nimble, with the aid of feet and wings, for the old folks, eluded their pursuit, and at last took shelter between the gods themselves.", "그러나 거위는 노부부에게는 너무 날렵하여, 발과 날개의 도움으로 그들의 추격을 피해 다니다가, 마침내 신들 자신의 사이로 피신했다."], ["They forbade it to be slain; and spoke in these words: “We are gods.", "신들은 거위를 죽이지 못하게 하고는 이렇게 말했다. \"우리는 신이다."], ["This inhospitable village shall pay the penalty of its impiety; you alone shall go free from the chastisement.", "손님을 박대하는 이 마을은 그 불경의 대가를 치르게 되리라. 너희만은 그 징벌에서 벗어나리라."], ["Quit your house, and come with us to the top of yonder hill.”", "너희 집을 떠나 우리와 함께 저 언덕 꼭대기로 오라.\""], ["They hastened to obey, and, staff in hand, labored up the steep ascent.", "그들은 서둘러 따랐고, 지팡이를 손에 쥐고 가파른 오르막을 힘겹게 올랐다."], ["They had reached to within an arrow’s flight of the top, when turning their eyes below, they beheld all the country sunk in a lake, only their own house left standing.", "그들이 꼭대기에서 화살이 닿을 만한 거리에 이르렀을 때, 아래로 눈을 돌리니 온 고장이 호수 아래 잠기고 오직 자신들의 집만이 서 있는 것이 보였다."], ["While they gazed with wonder at the sight, and lamented the fate of their neighbors, that old house of theirs was changed into a temple.", "그들이 그 광경을 경이로움에 잠겨 바라보며 이웃들의 운명을 탄식하고 있는 동안, 그들의 그 낡은 집은 신전으로 바뀌었다."], ["Columns took the place of the corner posts, the thatch grew yellow and appeared a gilded roof, the floors became marble, the doors were enriched with carving and ornaments of gold.", "모서리 기둥 자리에는 원주들이 들어섰고, 초가지붕은 노랗게 변해 황금빛 지붕으로 나타났으며, 바닥은 대리석이 되었고, 문은 조각과 황금 장식으로 화려해졌다. 그러자 제우스가 인자한 어조로 말했다."], ["Then spoke Jupiter in benignant accents: “Excellent old man, and woman worthy of such a husband, speak, tell us your wishes; what favor have you to ask of us?”", "\"훌륭한 노인이여, 그리고 그러한 남편에게 어울리는 부인이여, 말해 보라, 너희의 소원을 말해 보라. 우리에게 무슨 청을 하고 싶은가?\""], ["Philemon took counsel with Baucis a few moments; then declared to the gods their united wish.", "필레몬은 잠시 바우키스와 의논한 다음, 신들에게 두 사람이 함께 바라는 바를 밝혔다. \"저희는 이 당신의 신전의 사제이자 파수꾼이 되기를 청합니다."], ["“We ask to be priests and guardians of this your temple; and since here we have passed our lives in love and concord, we wish that one and the same hour may take us both from life, that I may not live to see her grave, nor be laid in my own by her.”", "그리고 저희가 여기서 사랑과 화목 속에 일생을 보냈으니, 똑같은 한 시각에 저희 둘을 한꺼번에 이 삶에서 데려가 주시기를 바랍니다. 그리하여 제가 살아남아 아내의 무덤을 보지 않게 하시고, 또 아내의 손으로 제 무덤에 누이지 않게 해 주소서.\""], ["Their prayer was granted.", "그들의 기도는 들어주어졌다."], ["They were the keepers of the temple as long as they lived.", "그들은 사는 동안 그 신전의 지킴이로 지냈다."], ["When grown very old, as they stood one day before the steps of the sacred edifice, and were telling the story of the place, Baucis saw Philemon begin to put forth leaves, and old Philemon saw Baucis changing in like manner.", "아주 늙었을 때, 어느 날 그들이 그 신성한 건물의 계단 앞에 서서 그곳의 내력을 이야기하고 있노라니, 바우키스는 필레몬이 잎을 내밀기 시작하는 것을 보았고, 늙은 필레몬은 바우키스가 똑같이 변해 가는 것을 보았다."], ["And now a leafy crown had grown over their heads, while exchanging parting words, as long as they could speak.", "그리고 이제 잎이 무성한 관이 그들의 머리 위로 자라났고, 그동안 그들은 말할 수 있는 한 작별의 말을 주고받았다."], ["“Farewell, dear spouse,”", "\"잘 가오, 사랑하는 이여.\""], ["they said, together, and at the same moment the bark closed over their mouths.", "그들은 함께 말했고, 바로 그 순간 나무껍질이 그들의 입 위로 닫혔다."], ["The Tyanean shepherd still shows the two trees, standing side by side, made out of the two good old people.", "티아나의 목동은 지금도 그 두 선량한 노부부로 만들어진 두 그루의 나무가 나란히 서 있는 것을 보여 준다."]], [["The story of Baucis and Philemon has been imitated by Swift, in a burlesque style, the actors in the change being two wandering saints, and the house being changed into a church, of which Philemon is made the parson.", "바우키스와 필레몬의 이야기는 스위프트가 익살스러운 문체로 모방한 바 있는데, 거기서는 변화를 겪는 이들이 떠돌이 성자 둘이고, 집은 교회로 바뀌며, 필레몬은 그 교회의 목사가 된다."], ["The following may serve as a specimen:", "다음 대목이 그 본보기가 될 만하다."]], [["“They scarce had spoke, when, fair and soft, The roof began to mount aloft; Aloft rose every beam and rafter; The heavy wall climbed slowly after. The chimney widened and grew higher, Became a steeple with a spire. The kettle to the top was hoist, And there stood fastened to a joist, But with the upside down, to show Its inclination for below; In vain, for a superior force, Applied at bottom, stops its course; Doomed ever in suspense to dwell, ’Tis now no kettle, but a bell. A wooden jack, which had almost Lost by disuse the art to roast, A sudden alteration feels. Increased by new intestine wheels; And, what exalts the wonder more, The number made the motion slower; The flier, though ’t had leaden feet, Turned round so quick you scarce could see ’t; But slackened by some secret power, Now hardly moves an inch an hour. The jack and chimney, near allied, Had never left each other’s side: The chimney to a steeple grown, The jack would not be left alone; But up against the steeple reared, Became a clock, and still adhered; And still its love to household cares By a shrill voice at noon declares, Warning the cook-maid not to burn That roast meat which it cannot turn; The groaning chair began to crawl, Like a huge snail, along the wall; There stuck aloft in public view, And with small change, a pulpit grew. A bedstead of the antique mode, Compact of timber many a load, Such as our ancestors did use, Was metamorphosed into pews, Which still their ancient nature keep By lodging folks disposed to sleep.”", "\"그들이 채 말을 마치기도 전에, 곱고도 부드럽게 지붕이 위로 솟아오르기 시작했네. 들보와 서까래가 모두 위로 솟았고, 무거운 벽이 천천히 그 뒤를 따라 기어올랐네. 굴뚝은 넓어지고 더 높이 자라나 첨탑을 인 종루가 되었네. 솥은 꼭대기까지 끌어올려져 들보에 단단히 매달려 섰는데, 위아래가 뒤집힌 채로, 아래를 향한 그 마음을 드러내었네. 헛되이도, 밑에서 가해진 더 강한 힘이 그 움직임을 막으니, 영영 매달려 지내도록 운명 지어져, 이제 그것은 솥이 아니라 종이 되었네. 오랜 쓰임 없음에 고기 굽는 재주를 거의 잃어버렸던 나무 회전구이틀은 돌연한 변화를 느꼈네. 새로운 안쪽 톱니바퀴들이 더해지고, 더욱 놀라운 것은, 그 수가 늘수록 움직임은 느려졌다는 것. 납으로 만든 발을 가졌건만 날개바퀴는 너무 빨리 돌아 눈으로 좇기 어려웠는데, 어떤 은밀한 힘에 누그러져 이제는 한 시간에 한 치도 겨우 움직이네. 회전구이틀과 굴뚝은 가까운 사이라 서로의 곁을 결코 떠난 적이 없었네. 굴뚝이 첨탑으로 자라나니, 회전구이틀은 홀로 남으려 하지 않고 첨탑에 기대어 곧추서서 시계가 되어 여전히 붙어 있었네. 그리고 살림살이에 대한 그 애정을 여전히 정오마다 날카로운 목소리로 알리며, 돌릴 수 없는 그 구이 고기를 태우지 말라고 부엌데기에게 일러 주네. 삐걱대는 의자는 기어가기 시작했네, 거대한 달팽이처럼 벽을 따라. 그리하여 높이 매달려 모두의 눈앞에 드러나, 작은 변화로 설교단이 되어 자라났네. 옛 양식의 침대틀은, 수레로 여러 짐의 목재를 들여 짠 것으로, 우리 조상들이 쓰던 그런 것이었는데, 교회 신도석으로 탈바꿈하였네. 그것들은 여전히 옛 본성을 지키고 있으니, 잠들고 싶어 하는 이들을 재워 줌으로써 그러하네.\""]], [["————", "────"]], [["# PROSERPINE—GLAUCUS AND SCYLLA", "# PROSERPINE—GLAUCUS AND SCYLLA"]], [["WHEN Jupiter and his brothers had defeated the Titans and banished them to Tartarus, a new enemy rose up against the gods.", "제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]와 그 형제들이 티탄족을 무찌르고 타르타로스로 추방했을 때, 신들에게 맞서 새로운 적이 일어났다."], ["They were the giants Typhon, Briareus, Enceladus, and others.", "그들은 거인족 티폰, 브리아레오스, 엔켈라도스와 그 밖의 무리였다."], ["Some of them had a hundred arms, others breathed out fire.", "이들 가운데 어떤 자는 팔이 백 개나 되었고, 또 어떤 자는 입에서 불을 뿜었다."], ["They were finally subdued and buried alive under Mount Ætna, where they still sometimes struggle to get loose, and shake the whole island with earthquakes.", "그들은 마침내 정복당해 에트나 산 아래 산 채로 매장되었는데, 지금도 이따금 빠져나오려고 몸부림을 쳐서 섬 전체를 지진으로 뒤흔든다."], ["Their breath comes up through the mountain, and is what men call the eruption of the volcano.", "그들의 숨결은 산을 통해 올라오는데, 사람들은 이것을 화산의 분화라고 부른다."]], [["The fall of these monsters shook the earth, so that Pluto was alarmed, and feared that his kingdom would be laid open to the light of day.", "이 괴물들의 추락은 대지를 뒤흔들었고, 그리하여 플루톤[로마의 〈디스〉]은 놀란 나머지 자신의 왕국이 햇빛에 드러나지나 않을까 두려워했다."], ["Under this apprehension, he mounted his chariot, drawn by black horses, and took a circuit of inspection to satisfy himself of the extent of the damage.", "이런 염려 속에서 그는 검은 말들이 끄는 전차에 올라타, 피해의 규모를 직접 확인하려고 두루 시찰에 나섰다."], ["While he was thus engaged, Venus, who was sitting on Mount Eryx playing with her boy Cupid, espied him, and said, “My son, take your darts with which you conquer all, even Jove himself, and send one into the breast of yonder dark monarch, who rules the realm of Tartarus.", "그가 이렇게 다니던 동안, 에릭스 산에 앉아 아들 에로스[로마의 〈쿠피도〉]와 놀고 있던 아프로디테[로마의 〈베누스〉]가 그를 발견하고 말했다. \"내 아들아, 네가 모든 것을 정복하는, 심지어 제우스마저 굴복시키는 그 화살을 들어, 저기 타르타로스의 영역을 다스리는 어두운 군주의 가슴에 하나 쏘아 보내거라."], ["Why should he alone escape?", "어찌하여 그자만 벗어나야 한단 말이냐?"], ["Seize the opportunity to extend your empire and mine.", "이 기회를 잡아 너와 나의 권세를 넓히도록 하여라."], ["Do you not see that even in heaven some despise our power?", "하늘에서조차 어떤 이들은 우리의 힘을 업신여기는 것을 너는 보지 못하느냐?"], ["Minerva the wise, and Diana the huntress, defy us; and there is that daughter of Ceres, who threatens to follow their example.", "지혜로운 아테나[로마의 〈미네르바〉]와 사냥꾼 아르테미스[로마의 〈디아나〉]가 우리에게 맞서고 있으며, 또 데메테르[로마의 〈케레스〉]의 저 딸도 그들의 본을 따르겠다고 위협하고 있다."], ["Now do you, if you have any regard for your own interest or mine, join these two in one.”", "자, 너에게 너 자신의 이익이나 나의 이익을 조금이라도 생각하는 마음이 있다면, 저 두 신을 하나로 맺어 다오.\""], ["The boy unbound his quiver, and selected his sharpest and truest arrow; then straining the bow against his knee, he attached the string, and, having made ready, shot the arrow with its barbed point right into the heart of Pluto.", "소년은 화살통을 끌러, 가장 날카롭고 가장 정확한 화살을 골라 들었다. 그러고는 무릎에 활을 대어 당겨 시위를 메긴 다음, 준비를 마치자 미늘 돋친 화살촉을 플루톤의 심장 한복판에 곧장 쏘아 박았다."]], [["In the vale of Enna there is a lake embowered in woods, which screen it from the fervid rays of the sun, while the moist ground is covered with flowers, and Spring reigns perpetual.", "엔나의 골짜기에는 숲에 둘러싸인 호수가 하나 있어, 작열하는 태양의 빛살로부터 가려져 있었고, 한편 습기 어린 땅은 꽃들로 뒤덮이고 봄이 영원히 군림했다."], ["Here Proserpine was playing with her companions, gathering lilies and violets, and filling her basket and her apron with them, when Pluto saw her, loved her, and carried her off.", "페르세포네[로마의 〈프로세르피나〉]는 이곳에서 동무들과 어울려 백합과 제비꽃을 따며, 바구니와 앞치마를 그것들로 가득 채우고 있었다. 그때 플루톤이 그녀를 보고는 사랑에 빠져 그녀를 채어 갔다."], ["She screamed for help to her mother and companions; and when in her fright she dropped the corners of her apron and let the flowers fall, childlike she felt the loss of them as an addition to her grief.", "그녀는 어머니와 동무들에게 도와 달라고 비명을 질렀다. 그리고 놀란 나머지 앞치마 자락을 놓쳐 꽃들이 쏟아져 내리자, 어린아이답게 그녀는 그 꽃을 잃은 일을 자기 슬픔에 보태어지는 것으로 느꼈다."], ["The ravisher urged on his steeds, calling them each by name, and throwing loose over their heads and necks his iron-colored reins.", "약탈자는 말들을 하나하나 이름을 부르며 재촉했고, 쇠빛 고삐를 그들의 머리와 목 위로 느슨히 던졌다."], ["When he reached the River Cyane, and it opposed his passage, he struck the river-bank with his trident, and the earth opened and gave him a passage to Tartarus.", "그가 키아네 강에 이르자 강물이 그의 길을 막았으므로, 그는 삼지창으로 강둑을 내리쳤고, 그러자 땅이 갈라져 타르타로스로 통하는 길을 그에게 내주었다."]], [["Ceres sought her daughter all the world over.", "데메테르는 세상 곳곳을 다니며 딸을 찾았다."], ["Bright-haired Aurora, when she came forth in the morning, and Hesperus when he led out the stars in the evening, found her still busy in the search.", "빛나는 머리채의 에오스[로마의 〈아우로라〉]가 아침에 나설 때에도, 헤스페로스가 저녁에 별들을 이끌고 나올 때에도, 그녀가 여전히 그 탐색에 바쁜 모습을 보았다."], ["But it was all unavailing.", "그러나 모든 것이 헛수고였다."], ["At length, weary and sad, she sat down upon a stone, and continued sitting nine days and nights, in the open air, under the sunlight and moonlight and falling showers.", "마침내 지치고 슬픔에 잠긴 그녀는 돌 위에 앉았고, 아흐레 낮과 아흐레 밤을 노천에서, 햇빛과 달빛과 쏟아지는 소나기 아래 계속 앉아 있었다. 그곳은 지금 엘레우시스 시가 서 있는 자리로, 그때에는 켈레오스라는 이름의 노인이 사는 곳이었다."], ["It was where now stands the city of Eleusis, then the home of an old man named Celeus.", "그는 들에 나가 도토리와 검은딸기, 그리고 불을 지필 나뭇가지를 줍고 있었다."], ["He was out in the field, gathering acorns and blackberries, and sticks for his fire.", "그의 어린 딸은 염소 두 마리를 집으로 몰고 있었는데, 노파의 모습으로 나타난 여신 곁을 지나면서 그녀에게 말했다. \"어머니,\""], ["His little girl was driving home their two goats, and as she passed the goddess, who appeared in the guise of an old woman, she said to her, “Mother,”—and the name was sweet to the ears of Ceres,—“why do you sit here alone upon the rocks?”", "이 호칭은 데메테르의 귀에 달콤하게 들렸다. \"어찌하여 여기 바위 위에 홀로 앉아 계신가요?\""], ["The old man also stopped, though his load was heavy, and begged her to come into his cottage, such as it was.", "노인 또한, 비록 그의 짐이 무거웠으나 걸음을 멈추고는, 누추하나마 자기 오두막으로 들어오시라고 청했다."], ["She declined, and he urged her.", "그녀가 사양하자 그는 거듭 권했다."], ["“Go in peace,”", "\"편히 가시고,\""], ["she replied, “and be happy in your daughter; I have lost mine.”", "그녀가 대답했다. \"그대의 딸로 인하여 행복하시기를."], ["As she spoke, tears—or something like tears, for the gods never weep—fell down her cheeks upon her bosom.", "나는 내 딸을 잃었답니다.\" 그녀가 이렇게 말하는 동안, 눈물이, 아니 눈물 비슷한 것이, 신들은 결코 울지 않으니, 그녀의 두 뺨을 타고 가슴 위로 흘러내렸다."], ["The compassionate old man and his child wept with her.", "인정 많은 노인과 그의 아이도 그녀와 함께 울었다. 그러더니 그가 말했다."], ["Then said he, “Come with us, and despise not our humble roof; so may your daughter be restored to you in safety.”", "\"우리와 함께 가십시다, 우리의 보잘것없는 지붕을 업신여기지 마십시오. 그리하면 그대의 딸도 무사히 그대에게 돌아올지 모릅니다.\""], ["“Lead on,”", "\"앞장서시오.\""], ["said she, “I cannot resist that appeal!”", "그녀가 말했다. \"그 청만은 거절할 수가 없군요!\""], ["So she rose from the stone and went with them.", "그리하여 그녀는 돌에서 일어나 그들과 함께 갔다."], ["As they walked he told her that his only son, a little boy, lay very sick, feverish, and sleepless.", "걸어가면서 그는 그녀에게 자신의 외아들인 어린 사내아이가 열에 시달리며 잠도 못 자고 몹시 앓아누워 있다고 말했다."], ["She stooped and gathered some poppies.", "그녀는 몸을 굽혀 양귀비 몇 송이를 꺾었다."], ["As they entered the cottage, they found all in great distress, for the boy seemed past hope of recovery.", "그들이 오두막에 들어서니, 아이가 회복할 가망이 없어 보였으므로 온 집안이 큰 시름에 잠겨 있는 것을 보았다."], ["Metanina, his mother, received her kindly, and the goddess stooped and kissed the lips of the sick child.", "아이의 어머니 메타니라는 그녀를 친절히 맞아들였고, 여신은 몸을 굽혀 병든 아이의 입술에 입을 맞추었다."], ["Instantly the paleness left his face, and healthy vigor returned to his body.", "그 즉시 아이의 얼굴에서 창백함이 가시고, 건강한 활기가 그 몸에 되돌아왔다."], ["The whole family were delighted—that is, the father, mother, and little girl, for they were all; they had no servants.", "온 가족이 기뻐했으니, 곧 아버지와 어머니와 어린 딸이었고, 그들이 식구의 전부였으니, 그들에게는 하인이 없었던 것이다."], ["They spread the table, and put upon it curds and cream, apples, and honey in the comb.", "그들은 상을 차려, 응유와 크림, 사과, 그리고 벌집째인 꿀을 그 위에 올렸다."], ["While they ate, Ceres mingled poppy juice in the milk of the boy.", "그들이 먹는 동안, 데메테르는 양귀비 즙을 아이의 우유에 섞었다."], ["When night came and all was still, she arose, and taking the sleeping boy, moulded his limbs with her hands, and uttered over him three times a solemn charm, then went and laid him in the ashes.", "밤이 되어 모든 것이 고요해지자, 그녀는 일어나 잠든 아이를 안아 두 손으로 그 사지를 빚고, 그 위에 세 번 엄숙한 주문을 외운 다음, 가서 아이를 잿불 속에 뉘었다."], ["His mother, who had been watching what her guest was doing, sprang forward with a cry and snatched the child from the fire.", "손님이 하는 일을 지켜보고 있던 아이의 어머니는 비명을 지르며 앞으로 달려들어 불 속에서 아이를 낚아챘다."], ["Then Ceres assumed her own form, and a divine splendor shone all around.", "그러자 데메테르는 본래의 모습으로 돌아왔고, 신성한 광채가 사방으로 빛났다."], ["While they were overcome with astonishment, she said, “Mother, you have been cruel in your fondness to your son.", "그들이 경악에 사로잡혀 있는 사이, 그녀가 말했다. \"어머니여, 그대는 아들에 대한 애정으로 그대 자신이 잔인했소."], ["I would have made him immortal, but you have frustrated my attempt.", "나는 이 아이를 불사의 존재로 만들려 했건만, 그대가 내 시도를 좌절시켰소."], ["Nevertheless, he shall be great and useful.", "그렇기는 하나, 이 아이는 위대하고 유익한 존재가 될 것이오."], ["He shall teach men the use of the plough, and the rewards which labor can win from the cultivated soil.”", "이 아이는 사람들에게 쟁기의 쓰임새와, 일구어진 흙에서 노동이 거둘 수 있는 보답을 가르치게 될 것이오.\""], ["So saying, she wrapped a cloud about her, and mounting her chariot rode away.", "이렇게 말하고서 그녀는 구름으로 몸을 감싸고, 전차에 올라타 떠나갔다."]], [["Ceres continued her search for her daughter, passing from land to land, and across seas and rivers, till at length she returned to Sicily, whence she at first set out, and stood by the banks of the River Cyane, where Pluto made himself a passage with his prize to his own dominions.", "데메테르는 딸을 찾는 일을 계속하여, 이 땅에서 저 땅으로, 바다와 강을 가로질러 다니다가, 마침내 그녀가 처음에 출발했던 시킬리아[시칠리아]로 돌아왔고, 플루톤이 자기 전리품을 데리고 자신의 영토로 통하는 길을 냈던 키아네 강의 둑 곁에 섰다."], ["The river nymph would have told the goddess all she had witnessed, but dared not, for fear of Pluto; so she only ventured to take up the girdle which Proserpine had dropped in her flight, and waft it to the feet of the mother.", "강의 님프는 자신이 목격한 모든 것을 여신에게 말해 주고 싶었으나, 플루톤이 두려워 감히 그러지 못했다. 그래서 그녀는 다만, 페르세포네가 끌려가던 길에 떨어뜨린 허리띠를 집어 들어, 어머니의 발치로 띄워 보내는 일만 감행했다."], ["Ceres, seeing this, was no longer in doubt of her loss, but she did not yet know the cause, and laid the blame on the innocent land.", "데메테르는 이것을 보고 더 이상 자기 딸을 잃었다는 사실을 의심하지 않게 되었으나, 아직 그 까닭은 알지 못했으므로 죄 없는 대지를 탓했다. \"은혜를 모르는 흙이여,\""], ["“Ungrateful soil,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “which I have endowed with fertility and clothed with herbage and nourishing grain, no more shall you enjoy my favors.”", "\"내가 비옥함을 부여하고 풀과 자양 가득한 곡식으로 옷 입혀 주었거늘, 너는 더 이상 나의 은총을 누리지 못하리라.\" 그러자 가축이 죽고, 쟁기는 고랑 속에서 부러지고, 씨앗은 싹이 트지 않았다."], ["Then the cattle died, the plough broke in the furrow, the seed failed to come up; there was too much sun, there was too much rain; the birds stole the seeds—thistles and brambles were the only growth.", "햇볕이 너무 많고, 비가 너무 많았다. 새들은 씨앗을 훔쳐 갔고, 엉겅퀴와 가시덤불만이 유일하게 자라났다."], ["Seeing this, the fountain Arethusa interceded for the land.", "이것을 본 샘의 님프 아레투사가 대지를 위해 중재에 나섰다. \"여신이여,\""], ["“Goddess,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “blame not the land; it opened unwillingly to yield a passage to your daughter.", "\"대지를 탓하지 마십시오. 대지는 당신의 따님이 지나갈 길을 마지못해 내주었던 것입니다."], ["I can tell you of her fate, for I have seen her.", "제가 따님의 운명을 말씀드릴 수 있으니, 제가 그분을 보았기 때문입니다."], ["This is not my native country; I came hither from Elis.", "이곳은 제 고향이 아닙니다. 저는 엘리스에서 이리로 왔습니다."], ["I was a woodland nymph, and delighted in the chase.", "저는 숲의 님프로서, 사냥을 즐겼습니다."], ["They praised my beauty, but I cared nothing for it, and rather boasted of my hunting exploits.", "사람들은 제 아름다움을 칭송했으나, 저는 그런 것에는 아무 관심도 없었고, 오히려 제 사냥 솜씨를 자랑하곤 했지요."], ["One day I was returning from the wood, heated with exercise, when I came to a stream silently flowing, so clear that you might count the pebbles on the bottom.", "어느 날 저는 운동으로 몸이 달아오른 채 숲에서 돌아오다가, 소리 없이 흐르는 시냇물에 이르렀는데, 어찌나 맑은지 바닥의 자갈을 셀 수 있을 정도였습니다."], ["The willows shaded it, and the grassy bank sloped down to the water’s edge.", "버드나무가 그 위에 그늘을 드리웠고, 풀 우거진 둑이 물가까지 비스듬히 내려가 있었습니다."], ["I approached, I touched the water with my foot.", "저는 가까이 다가가 발로 물을 건드려 보았습니다."], ["I stepped in knee-deep, and not content with that, I laid my garments on the willows and went in.", "무릎까지 들어가 보았는데, 그것으로도 만족하지 못해, 옷을 버드나무에 걸어 두고 물속으로 들어갔습니다."], ["While I sported in the water, I heard an indistinct murmur coming up as out of the depths of the stream; and made haste to escape to the nearest bank.", "제가 물속에서 노니는 동안, 시내의 깊은 곳에서 솟아오르는 듯한 어렴풋한 웅얼거림이 들려와, 저는 서둘러 가장 가까운 둑으로 피하려 했습니다. 그 목소리가 말했습니다."], ["The voice said, ‘Why do you fly, Arethusa?", "'어찌하여 그대는 달아나는가, 아레투사여? 나는 이 시내의 신 알페이오스다.'"], ["I am Alpheus, the god of this stream.’ I ran, he pursued; he was not more swift than I, but he was stronger, and gained upon me, as my strength failed.", "저는 달렸고, 그는 뒤쫓았습니다. 그가 저보다 빠르지는 않았으나 더 힘이 셌으므로, 제 기운이 빠져 갈수록 그는 저에게 다가왔습니다."], ["At last, exhausted, I cried for help to Diana.", "마침내 지친 저는 아르테미스에게 도와 달라고 외쳤습니다."], ["‘Help me, goddess!", "'도와주소서, 여신이여!"], ["help your votary!’ The goddess heard, and wrapped me suddenly in a thick cloud.", "당신을 섬기는 자를 도와주소서!' 여신께서 들으시고는, 저를 갑자기 짙은 구름으로 감싸 주셨습니다."], ["The river god looked now this way and now that, and twice came close to me, but could not find me.", "강의 신은 이쪽저쪽을 살피며, 두 번이나 제 곁까지 바싹 다가왔으나 저를 찾아내지 못했습니다. '아레투사여!"], ["‘Arethusa!", "아레투사여!'"], ["Arethusa!’ he cried.", "그가 외쳤습니다."], ["Oh, how I trembled,—like a lamb that hears the wolf growling outside the fold.", "아, 저는 어찌나 떨었던지, 우리 밖에서 늑대가 으르렁대는 소리를 듣는 어린 양 같았습니다."], ["A cold sweat came over me, my hair flowed down in streams; where my foot stood there was a pool.", "식은땀이 저를 뒤덮었고, 제 머리카락은 물줄기 되어 흘러내렸습니다. 제 발이 디딘 자리에는 웅덩이가 생겼습니다."], ["In short, in less time than it takes to tell it I became a fountain.", "요컨대, 이 일을 이야기하는 것보다도 짧은 사이에 저는 샘이 되었던 것입니다."], ["But in this form Alpheus knew me and attempted to mingle his stream with mine.", "그러나 이 모습으로도 알페이오스는 저를 알아보고 자기 물줄기를 제 물줄기와 섞으려 했습니다."], ["Diana cleft the ground, and I, endeavoring to escape him, plunged into the cavern, and through the bowels of the earth came out here in Sicily.", "아르테미스가 땅을 가르자, 저는 그를 피하려 애쓰며 그 동굴 속으로 뛰어들었고, 대지의 깊은 속을 지나 이곳 시킬리아로 솟아 나왔습니다."], ["While I passed through the lower parts of the earth, I saw your Proserpine.", "대지의 아래쪽을 지나는 동안, 저는 당신의 따님 페르세포네를 보았습니다."], ["She was sad, but no longer showing alarm in her countenance.", "그분은 슬픈 모습이었으나, 더 이상 얼굴에 놀란 기색을 드러내지는 않았습니다."], ["Her look was such as became a queen—the queen of Erebus; the powerful bride of the monarch of the realms of the dead.”", "그분의 모습은 여왕에게, 곧 에레보스의 여왕에게 어울리는 것이었으니, 죽은 자들의 영역을 다스리는 군주의 권세 있는 신부의 모습이었습니다.\""]], [["When Ceres heard this, she stood for a while like one stupefied; then turned her chariot towards heaven, and hastened to present herself before the throne of Jove.", "데메테르는 이 말을 듣고 한동안 넋이 나간 사람처럼 서 있었다. 그러더니 전차를 하늘 쪽으로 돌려, 서둘러 제우스의 옥좌 앞에 나아갔다."], ["She told the story of her bereavement, and implored Jupiter to interfere to procure the restitution of her daughter.", "그녀는 딸을 잃은 사연을 이야기하고, 제우스에게 나서서 딸을 돌려받게 해 달라고 간청했다."], ["Jupiter consented on one condition, namely, that Proserpine should not during her stay in the lower world have taken any food; otherwise, the Fates forbade her release.", "제우스는 한 가지 조건을 걸어 허락했으니, 곧 페르세포네가 저승에 머무는 동안 어떤 음식도 입에 대지 않았어야 한다는 것이었다. 그렇지 않다면 운명의 여신들이 그녀의 풀려남을 허락하지 않으리라는 것이었다."], ["Accordingly, Mercury was sent, accompanied by Spring, to demand Proserpine of Pluto.", "그리하여 헤르메스[로마의 〈메르쿠리우스〉]가 봄을 동반하여 플루톤에게 페르세포네를 내놓으라고 요구하러 파견되었다."], ["The wily monarch consented; but, alas!", "교활한 군주는 응했으나, 아아!"], ["the maiden had taken a pomegranate which Pluto offered her, and had sucked the sweet pulp from a few of the seeds.", "그 처녀는 플루톤이 그녀에게 건넨 석류를 받아, 그 씨앗 몇 알에서 달콤한 과육을 빨아먹은 뒤였다."], ["This was enough to prevent her complete release; but a compromise was made, by which she was to pass half the time with her mother, and the rest with her husband Pluto.", "이것만으로도 그녀의 완전한 풀려남을 가로막기에 충분했다. 그러나 한 가지 타협이 이루어져, 그녀는 그 시간의 절반을 어머니와 함께 보내고, 나머지를 남편 플루톤과 함께 보내기로 했다."]], [["Ceres allowed herself to be pacified with this arrangement, and restored the earth to her favor.", "데메테르는 이 조정에 마음을 누그러뜨리고, 대지에 다시 자신의 은총을 베풀었다."], ["Now she remembered Celeus and his family, and her promise to his infant son Triptolemus.", "이제 그녀는 켈레오스와 그 가족, 그리고 그의 어린 아들 트립톨레모스에게 했던 약속을 기억해 냈다."], ["When the boy grew up, she taught him the use of the plough, and how to sow the seed.", "소년이 자라나자, 그녀는 그에게 쟁기의 쓰임새와 씨앗 뿌리는 법을 가르쳤다."], ["She took him in her chariot, drawn by winged dragons, through all the countries of the earth, imparting to mankind valuable grains, and the knowledge of agriculture.", "그녀는 날개 달린 용들이 끄는 전차에 그를 태우고, 대지의 온갖 나라를 두루 다니며, 인류에게 값진 곡식과 농경의 지식을 전해 주었다."], ["After his return, Triptolemus built a magnificent temple to Ceres in Eleusis, and established the worship of the goddess, under the name of the Eleusinian mysteries, which, in the splendor and solemnity of their observance, surpassed all other religious celebrations among the Greeks.", "돌아온 뒤 트립톨레모스는 엘레우시스에 데메테르를 기리는 웅장한 신전을 세우고, 〈엘레우시스의 비의(秘儀)〉라는 이름으로 여신을 섬기는 예배를 확립했는데, 그 거행의 화려함과 장엄함에 있어 그리스인들 사이의 다른 모든 종교 행사를 능가했다."]], [["There can be little doubt of this story of Ceres and Proserpine being an allegory.", "이 데메테르와 페르세포네의 이야기가 하나의 우의(寓意)라는 점은 의심의 여지가 거의 없다."], ["Proserpine signifies the seed-corn which when cast into the ground lies there concealed—that is, she is carried off by the god of the underworld.", "페르세포네는 땅속에 던져져 거기 숨어 있는 씨앗 곡식을 뜻한다. 곧 그녀가 저승의 신에게 채어 가는 것이다."], ["It reappears—that is, Proserpine is restored to her mother.", "그것이 다시 모습을 드러낸다. 곧 페르세포네가 어머니에게 돌아오는 것이다."], ["Spring leads her back to the light of day.", "봄이 그녀를 다시 햇빛 속으로 이끌어 낸다."]], [["Milton alludes to the story of Proserpine in “Paradise Lost,”", "밀턴은 「실낙원」"], ["Book IV.:", "제4권에서 페르세포네의 이야기를 넌지시 언급한다."]], [["“. .", "\"."], [".", "."], ["Not that fair field Of Enna where Proserpine gathering flowers, Herself a fairer flower, by gloomy Dis Was gathered, which cost Ceres all that pain To seek her through the world,— .", ". 페르세포네가 꽃을 따다가, 그녀 자신이 더 고운 꽃이었거늘, 음울한 디스에게 꺾여 갔던 저 엔나의 아름다운 들판도, 그 때문에 데메테르가 그 모든 고난을 무릅쓰고 온 세상을 헤매며 딸을 찾았던 그곳도, ."], [".", "."], [".", "."], ["might with this Paradise Of Eden strive.”", "이 에덴의 낙원과는 견줄 수 없으리라.\""]], [["Hood, in his “Ode to Melancholy,” uses the same allusion very beautifully:", "후드는 그의 「우울에 부치는 송가」에서 같은 인유를 매우 아름답게 활용한다."]], [["“Forgive, if somewhile I forget, In woe to come the present bliss; As frighted Proserpine let fall Her flowers at the sight of Dis.”", "\"용서하라, 다가올 비탄 속에서 잠시 내가 지금의 행복을 잊는다 한들, 놀란 페르세포네가 디스를 보고서 제 꽃들을 떨어뜨렸던 것처럼.\""]], [["The River Alpheus does in fact disappear underground, in part of its course, finding its way through subterranean channels till it again appears on the surface.", "알페이오스 강은 실제로 그 물길의 일부에서 지하로 사라져, 지하의 수로를 통해 흐르다가 다시 지표로 솟아나곤 한다."], ["It was said that the Sicilian fountain Arethusa was the same stream, which, after passing under the sea, came up again in Sicily.", "시킬리아의 샘 아레투사가 바로 그 같은 물줄기로서, 바다 밑을 지난 뒤 시킬리아에서 다시 솟아난 것이라고들 했다."], ["Hence the story ran that a cup thrown into the Alpheus appeared again in Arethusa.", "그리하여 알페이오스에 던진 잔이 아레투사에서 다시 나타났다는 이야기가 전해졌다."], ["It is this fable of the underground course of Alpheus that Coleridge alludes to in his poem of “Kubla Khan”:", "콜리지가 그의 시 「쿠블라 칸」에서 넌지시 언급하는 것이 바로 이 알페이오스의 지하 물길에 관한 이야기다."]], [["“In Xanadu did Kubla Khan A stately pleasure-dome decree, Where Alph, the sacred river, ran Through caverns measureless to man, Down to a sunless sea.”", "\"재너두에서 쿠블라 칸은 장엄한 환락의 궁전을 지으라 명했으니, 그곳에는 성스러운 강 알프가 인간이 헤아릴 수 없는 동굴들을 지나 햇빛 없는 바다로 흘러내렸도다.\""]], [["In one of Moore’s juvenile poems he thus alludes to the same story, and to the practice of throwing garlands or other light objects on his stream to be carried downward by it, and afterwards reproduced at its emerging:", "무어는 그의 초기 시들 가운데 하나에서 같은 이야기를, 그리고 그의 물줄기에 화환이나 그 밖의 가벼운 물건을 던져 강을 따라 떠내려가게 한 뒤, 강이 솟아오르는 곳에서 그것이 다시 나타나게 하던 관습을 이렇게 넌지시 언급한다."]], [["“O my beloved, how divinely sweet Is the pure joy when kindred spirits meet!", "\"오 나의 사랑하는 이여, 한마음인 영혼들이 만날 때 그 순수한 기쁨은 얼마나 거룩하게 감미로운가!"], ["Like him the river god, whose waters flow, With love their only light, through caves below, Wafting in triumph all the flowery braids And festal rings, with which Olympic maids Have decked his current, as an offering meet To lay at Arethusa’s shining feet.", "강의 신처럼, 그의 물줄기는 사랑만을 유일한 빛으로 삼아 아래쪽 동굴들을 지나 흐르며, 올림피아의 처녀들이 그의 물길을 장식하여 아레투사의 빛나는 발치에 바치는 합당한 제물로 엮어 놓은 온갖 꽃 띠와 축제의 고리들을 의기양양하게 실어 나르는 그 신처럼."], ["Think, when he meets at last his fountain bride, What perfect love must thrill the blended tide!", "생각해 보라, 그가 마침내 샘이 된 그의 신부를 만날 때, 그 어우러진 물결을 전율케 하는 얼마나 온전한 사랑일지를!"], ["Each lost in each, till mingling into one, Their lot the same for shadow or for sun, A type of true love, to the deep they run.”", "서로가 서로에게 잠겨, 하나로 뒤섞일 때까지, 그림자 속에서든 햇빛 속에서든 그 운명이 한가지인 채로, 참된 사랑의 본보기로서, 그들은 깊은 바다로 흘러간다.\""]], [["The following extract from Moore’s “Rhymes on the Road” gives an account of a celebrated picture by Albano, at Milan, called a Dance of Loves:", "다음은 무어의 「길 위의 운율」에서 발췌한 것으로, 밀라노에 있는 알바노의 유명한 그림, 〈사랑들의 춤〉이라 불리는 작품을 그려 보인다."]], [["“’Tis for the theft of Enna’s flower from earth These urchins celebrate their dance of mirth, Round the green tree, like fays upon a heath;— Those that are nearest linked in order bright, Cheek after cheek, like rosebuds in a wreath; And those more distant showing from beneath The others’ wings their little eyes of light.", "\"엔나의 꽃을 대지에서 훔쳐 간 일을 기려 이 개구쟁이들은 흥겨운 춤을 펼치니, 푸른 나무를 빙 둘러, 황야 위의 요정들처럼. 가장 가까이 있는 이들은 밝게 차례차례 손을 맞잡아, 뺨에 뺨을 맞대니, 화관 속의 장미 봉오리 같고, 더 멀리 있는 이들은 다른 이들의 날개 아래로 그 작고 빛나는 두 눈을 내비친다."], ["While see!", "보라!"], ["among the clouds, their eldest brother, But just flown up, tells with a smile of bliss, This prank of Pluto to his charmed mother, Who turns to greet the tidings with a kiss.”", "구름 사이로, 이제 막 날아오른 그들의 맏형이, 더없는 기쁨의 미소를 띠고, 플루톤의 이 장난을 매혹된 어머니에게 전하니, 어머니는 그 소식을 입맞춤으로 반기려 돌아선다.\" 글라우코스와 스킬라"]], [["# GLAUCUS AND SCYLLA", "# GLAUCUS AND SCYLLA"]], [["Glaucus was a fisherman.", "글라우코스는 어부였다."], ["One day he had drawn his nets to land, and had taken a great many fishes of various kinds.", "어느 날 그는 그물을 뭍으로 끌어올려, 갖가지 종류의 물고기를 무척 많이 잡았다."], ["So he emptied his net, and proceeded to sort the fishes on the grass.", "그래서 그는 그물을 비우고는, 풀밭 위에 물고기를 종류별로 가려내기 시작했다."], ["The place where he stood was a beautiful island in the river, a solitary spot, uninhabited, and not used for pasturage of cattle, nor ever visited by any but himself.", "그가 서 있던 곳은 강 가운데 자리한 아름다운 섬으로, 외딴 곳이었고, 사람도 살지 않았으며, 가축을 풀어 먹이는 데도 쓰이지 않았고, 그 자신 말고는 누구도 찾는 일이 없었다."], ["On a sudden, the fishes, which had been laid on the grass, began to revive and move their fins as if they were in the water; and while he looked on astonished, they one and all moved off to the water, plunged in, and swam away.", "그런데 갑자기, 풀밭 위에 놓여 있던 물고기들이 마치 물속에 있기라도 한 듯 다시 살아나 지느러미를 움직이기 시작했다. 그가 놀라서 바라보는 동안, 물고기들은 하나도 빠짐없이 물 쪽으로 옮겨 가더니, 물속으로 뛰어들어 헤엄쳐 가 버렸다."], ["He did not know what to make of this, whether some god had done it or some secret power in the herbage. “What herb has such a power?”", "그는 이 일을 어떻게 받아들여야 할지 알 수 없었으니, 어떤 신이 한 일인지, 아니면 그 풀 속에 깃든 어떤 은밀한 힘 때문인지 알 수가 없었다."], ["he exclaimed; and gathering some of it, he tasted it.", "\"어떤 풀이 이런 힘을 지녔단 말인가?\" 그가 외쳤다."], ["Scarce had the juices of the plant reached his palate when he found himself agitated with a longing desire for the water.", "그러고는 그 풀을 조금 뜯어 맛을 보았다. 그 식물의 즙이 그의 입천장에 닿기가 무섭게, 그는 물을 향한 간절한 갈망에 사로잡힌 자신을 발견했다."], ["He could no longer restrain himself, but bidding farewell to earth, he plunged into the stream.", "그는 더 이상 자신을 억누를 수 없어, 뭍에 작별을 고하고는 물줄기 속으로 뛰어들었다."], ["The gods of the water received him graciously, and admitted him to the honor of their society.", "물의 신들은 그를 정중히 맞아들여, 자신들의 무리에 들 영예를 그에게 허락했다."], ["They obtained the consent of Oceanus and Tethys, the sovereigns of the sea, that all that was mortal in him should be washed away.", "그들은 바다의 주권자인 오케아노스와 테티스의 동의를 얻어, 그에게 있는 필멸의 것을 모조리 씻어 내게 했다."], ["A hundred rivers poured their waters over him.", "백 개의 강이 그 물을 그의 위에 쏟아부었다."], ["Then he lost all sense of his former nature and all consciousness.", "그러자 그는 자신의 옛 본성에 대한 모든 감각과 모든 의식을 잃었다."], ["When he recovered, he found himself changed in form and mind.", "정신을 되찾았을 때, 그는 자신이 모습과 마음에서 변해 있음을 알았다."], ["His hair was sea-green, and trailed behind him on the water; his shoulders grew broad, and what had been thighs and legs assumed the form of a fish’s tail.", "그의 머리카락은 바다처럼 푸르렀고, 물 위로 그의 뒤를 끌며 늘어졌다. 그의 어깨는 넓어졌고, 넓적다리와 다리였던 것은 물고기 꼬리의 형상을 띠었다."], ["The sea-gods complimented him on the change of his appearance, and he fancied himself rather a good-looking personage.", "바다의 신들은 변모한 그의 모습을 두고 그에게 찬사를 보냈고, 그는 자신이 제법 보기 좋은 인물이라고 여겼다."]], [["One day Glaucus saw the beautiful maiden Scylla, the favorite of the water-nymphs, rambling on the shore, and when she had found a sheltered nook, laving her limbs in the clear water.", "어느 날 글라우코스는 물의 님프들의 총애를 받는 아름다운 처녀 스킬라가 물가를 거닐다가, 아늑한 후미를 찾아내어 맑은 물에 사지를 담그는 것을 보았다."], ["He fell in love with her, and showing himself on the surface, spoke to her, saying such things as he thought most likely to win her to stay; for she turned to run immediately on the sight of him, and ran till she had gained a cliff overlooking the sea.", "그는 그녀에게 사랑에 빠져, 수면 위로 모습을 드러내고는, 그녀가 머물도록 마음을 끌기에 가장 알맞다고 여겨지는 말들을 그녀에게 건넸다. 그도 그럴 것이, 그녀는 그를 보자마자 곧장 달아나려 돌아섰고, 바다가 내려다보이는 절벽에 이를 때까지 달렸기 때문이다."], ["Here she stopped and turned round to see whether it was a god or a sea animal, and observed with wonder his shape and color.", "여기서 그녀는 걸음을 멈추고 돌아서서, 그것이 신인지 바다 짐승인지를 살피며, 그의 모습과 빛깔을 경이로운 눈길로 바라보았다."], ["Glaucus partly emerging from the water, and supporting himself against a rock, said, “Maiden, I am no monster, nor a sea animal, but a god; and neither Proteus nor Triton ranks higher than I.", "글라우코스는 물에서 일부만 솟아오른 채 바위에 몸을 기대고 말했다. \"처녀여, 나는 괴물도 아니요, 바다 짐승도 아니라 신이오."], ["Once I was a mortal, and followed the sea for a living; but now I belong wholly to it.”", "프로테우스도 트리톤도 나보다 높지 않소. 한때 나는 필멸의 존재로서 바다를 좇아 생계를 꾸렸으나, 이제는 온전히 바다에 속한 몸이오.\""], ["Then he told the story of his metamorphosis, and how he had been promoted to his present dignity, and added, “But what avails all this if it fails to move your heart?”", "그러고는 자신이 변모한 이야기와, 어떻게 지금의 지위에 오르게 되었는지를 들려준 다음, 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"그러나 이 모든 것이 그대의 마음을 움직이지 못한다면 무슨 소용이겠소?\""], ["He was going on in this strain, but Scylla turned and hastened away.", "그가 이런 어조로 계속 말을 이어 가려 했으나, 스킬라는 돌아서서 황급히 달아나 버렸다."]], [["Glaucus was in despair, but it occurred to him to consult the enchantress Circe.", "글라우코스는 절망에 빠졌으나, 마법사 키르케에게 의논해 보자는 생각이 떠올랐다."], ["Accordingly he repaired to her island—the same where afterwards Ulysses landed, as we shall see in one of our later stories.", "그리하여 그는 그녀의 섬으로 갔으니, 훗날 오디세우스가 상륙했던 바로 그 섬으로, 우리의 뒷이야기들 가운데 하나에서 보게 될 것이다. 서로 인사를 나눈 뒤 그가 말했다."], ["After mutual salutations, he said, “Goddess, I entreat your pity; you alone can relieve the pain I suffer.", "\"여신이여, 부디 저를 가엾이 여겨 주십시오. 제가 겪는 고통을 덜어 줄 수 있는 분은 당신뿐입니다."], ["The power of herbs I know as well as any one, for it is to them I owe my change of form.", "풀의 힘이라면 저도 누구 못지않게 잘 알고 있으니, 제 모습이 변한 것도 바로 그 풀 덕분이기 때문입니다."], ["I love Scylla.", "저는 스킬라를 사랑합니다."], ["I am ashamed to tell you how I have sued and promised to her, and how scornfully she has treated me.", "제가 어떻게 그녀에게 구애하고 약속했는지, 또 그녀가 저를 얼마나 경멸스럽게 대했는지를 말씀드리기조차 부끄럽습니다."], ["I beseech you to use your incantations, or potent herbs, if they are more prevailing, not to cure me of my love,—for that I do not wish,—but to make her share it and yield me a like return.”", "부디 당신의 주술을, 아니 더 효험이 있다면 강력한 약초를 써 주시기를 간청드립니다. 저를 제 사랑에서 낫게 해 달라는 것이 아니라, 그것은 제가 바라는 바가 아니니, 그녀로 하여금 제 사랑을 함께 나누어, 제게 똑같이 보답하게 해 주십사 하는 것입니다.\""], ["To which Circe replied, for she was not insensible to the attractions of the sea-green deity, “You had better pursue a willing object; you are worthy to be sought, instead of having to seek in vain.", "이에 키르케가 대답했으니, 그녀 또한 바다처럼 푸른 그 신의 매력에 무감하지는 않았던 것이다. \"당신은 차라리 기꺼이 응할 상대를 좇는 편이 낫겠습니다."], ["Be not diffident, know your own worth.", "당신은 헛되이 좇아다니기보다는 오히려 좇음을 받을 만한 분이니까요."], ["I protest to you that even I, goddess though I be, and learned in the virtues of plants and spells, should not know how to refuse you.", "주눅 들지 말고, 당신 자신의 가치를 아십시오. 단언하건대, 비록 내가 여신이고 약초와 주문의 효능에 정통하다 해도, 당신을 거절할 줄은 모를 것입니다."], ["If she scorns you scorn her; meet one who is ready to meet you half way, and thus make a due return to both at once.”", "그녀가 당신을 경멸한다면 당신도 그녀를 경멸하십시오. 중도에서 당신을 맞아 줄 준비가 된 이를 만나, 그리하여 두 사람 모두에게 한꺼번에 마땅한 보답을 하십시오.\""], ["To these words Glaucus replied, “Sooner shall trees grow at the bottom of the ocean, and sea-weed on the top of the mountains, than I will cease to love Scylla, and her alone.”", "이 말에 글라우코스가 대답했다. \"내가 스킬라를, 그녀만을 사랑하기를 그치느니, 차라리 바다 밑바닥에서 나무가 자라고 산꼭대기에서 해초가 자라는 편이 빠를 것이오.\""]], [["The goddess was indignant, but she could not punish him, neither did she wish to do so, for she liked him too well; so she turned all her wrath against her rival, poor Scylla.", "여신은 분개했으나, 그를 벌할 수 없었고, 그러기를 바라지도 않았으니, 그를 너무도 좋아했기 때문이다. 그래서 그녀는 자신의 온 분노를 연적인 가엾은 스킬라에게로 돌렸다."], ["She took plants of poisonous powers and mixed them together, with incantations and charms.", "그녀는 독성을 지닌 식물들을 가져다 한데 섞고, 주술과 주문을 곁들였다."], ["Then she passed through the crowd of gambolling beasts, the victims of her art, and proceeded to the coast of Sicily, where Scylla lived.", "그러고는 자신의 술법에 희생되어 뛰노는 짐승들의 무리를 지나, 스킬라가 사는 시킬리아 해안으로 나아갔다."], ["There was a little bay on the shore to which Scylla used to resort, in the heat of the day, to breathe the air of the sea, and to bathe in its waters.", "그 물가에는 스킬라가 한낮의 더위에 바다 공기를 쐬고 그 물에 멱을 감으러 즐겨 찾던 작은 만이 하나 있었다."], ["Here the goddess poured her poisonous mixture, and muttered over it incantations of mighty power.", "여기에 여신은 자신의 독성 어린 혼합물을 쏟아붓고, 그 위에 막강한 힘을 지닌 주술을 중얼거렸다."], ["Scylla came as usual and plunged into the water up to her waist.", "스킬라는 여느 때처럼 와서 허리까지 물속에 잠겼다."], ["What was her horror to perceive a brood of serpents and barking monsters surrounding her!", "그런데 뱀들과 짖어 대는 괴물들의 무리가 자기를 에워싸고 있는 것을 알아챘을 때 그녀의 공포가 어떠했겠는가!"], ["At first she could not imagine they were a part of herself, and tried to run from them, and to drive them away; but as she ran she carried them with her, and when she tried to touch her limbs, she found her hands touch only the yawning jaws of monsters.", "처음에 그녀는 그것들이 자기 몸의 일부라고는 상상도 하지 못한 채, 그것들로부터 달아나려 하고 그것들을 쫓아 버리려 했다. 그러나 달아날수록 그것들을 함께 데리고 다녔고, 자기 사지를 만져 보려 하면 손에 닿는 것은 오직 괴물들의 떡 벌린 아가리뿐이었다."], ["Scylla remained rooted to the spot.", "스킬라는 그 자리에 뿌리박힌 듯 머물러 있게 되었다."], ["Her temper grew as ugly as her form, and she took pleasure in devouring hapless mariners who came within her grasp.", "그녀의 성미는 그 모습만큼이나 흉악해졌고, 그녀는 자기 손아귀에 들어오는 가엾은 뱃사람들을 잡아먹는 데서 즐거움을 느꼈다."], ["Thus she destroyed six of the companions of Ulysses, and tried to wreck the ships of Æneas, till at last she was turned into a rock, and as such still continues to be a terror to mariners.", "이리하여 그녀는 오디세우스의 동료 여섯을 잡아 죽였고, 아이네아스의 배들을 난파시키려 했으며, 마침내 바위로 변했는데, 그런 모습으로 지금도 여전히 뱃사람들에게 공포의 대상이 되고 있다."]], [["Keats, in his “Endymion,” has given a new version of the ending of “Glaucus and Scylla.”", "키츠는 그의 「엔디미온」에서 \"글라우코스와 스킬라\"의 결말에 새로운 해석을 내놓았다."], ["Glaucus consents to Circe’s blandishments, till he by chance is witness to her transactions with her beasts.[11] Disgusted with her treachery and cruelty, he tries to escape from her, but is taken and brought back, when with reproaches she banishes him, sentencing him to pass a thousand years in decrepitude and pain.", "글라우코스는 키르케의 아첨에 응하다가, 우연히 그녀가 짐승들을 상대로 벌이는 짓을 목격하게 된다.[11] 그녀의 배신과 잔인함에 넌더리가 난 그는 그녀에게서 달아나려 하나, 붙잡혀 다시 끌려오고, 그러자 그녀는 그를 비난하며 추방하여, 천 년 동안 노쇠와 고통 속에 지내도록 선고한다."], ["He returns to the sea, and there finds the body of Scylla, whom the goddess has not transformed but drowned.", "그는 바다로 돌아가, 거기서 스킬라의 시신을 발견하는데, 여신이 그녀를 변신시킨 것이 아니라 물에 빠뜨려 죽인 것이었다."], ["Glaucus learns that his destiny is that, if he passes his thousand years in collecting all the bodies of drowned lovers, a youth beloved of the gods will appear and help him.", "글라우코스는 자신의 운명이 다음과 같음을 알게 된다. 곧 그가 자신의 천 년을 물에 빠져 죽은 연인들의 시신을 모두 거두어 모으는 데 보낸다면, 신들의 사랑을 받는 한 젊은이가 나타나 그를 도우리라는 것이었다."], ["Endymion fulfils this prophecy, and aids in restoring Glaucus to youth, and Scylla and all the drowned lovers to life.", "엔디미온이 이 예언을 이루어, 글라우코스를 젊음으로, 스킬라와 물에 빠져 죽은 모든 연인들을 생명으로 되돌리는 일을 돕는다."]], [["The following is Glaucus’s account of his feelings after his “sea-change”:", "다음은 자신의 \"바다의 변화\"를 겪은 뒤 느낀 감정에 관한 글라우코스의 이야기다."]], [["“I plunged for life or death.", "\"나는 삶이냐 죽음이냐를 걸고 뛰어들었다."], ["To interknit One’s senses with so dense a breathing stuff Might seem a work of pain; so not enough Can I admire how crystal-smooth it felt, And buoyant round my limbs.", "그토록 빽빽이 숨 쉬는 물질에 제 감각을 한데 엮는 일은 고통스러운 일로 여겨질 법도 했으나, 그러기는커녕 그것이 수정처럼 매끄럽게 느껴졌고, 내 사지를 떠받쳐 주던 그 부력을 나는 아무리 찬탄해도 모자라다."], ["At first I dwelt Whole days and days in sheer astonishment; Forgetful utterly of self-intent, Moving but with the mighty ebb and flow.", "처음에 나는 온전한 경이 속에 며칠이고 며칠이고 머물렀고, 나 자신의 의도는 까맣게 잊은 채, 오직 그 거대한 밀물과 썰물에 실려서만 움직였다."], ["Then like a new-fledged bird that first doth show His spreaded feathers to the morrow chill, I tried in fear the pinions of my will.", "그러다가 갓 깃털이 돋은 새가 처음으로 아침의 한기에 제 활짝 편 깃을 펼쳐 보이듯, 나는 두려운 마음으로 내 의지의 날개를 시험해 보았다."], ["’Twas freedom!", "그것은 자유였다!"], ["and at once I visited The ceaseless wonders of this ocean-bed,” etc.", "그리고 나는 곧장 이 바다 밑바닥의 그칠 줄 모르는 경이들을 찾아갔다.\""], ["—Keats.", "— 키츠."]], [["————", "————"]], [["# PYGMALION—DRYOPE—VENUS AND ADONIS—APOLLO AND HYACINTHUS", "# PYGMALION—DRYOPE—VENUS AND ADONIS—APOLLO AND HYACINTHUS"]], [["PYGMALION saw so much to blame in women that he came at last to abhor the sex, and resolved to live unmarried.", "피그말리온은 여인들에게서 비난할 점을 너무도 많이 보았던 까닭에, 마침내 그 성(性) 전체를 혐오하게 되었고, 결혼하지 않은 채로 살아가기로 결심했다."], ["He was a sculptor, and had made with wonderful skill a statue of ivory, so beautiful that no living woman came anywhere near it.", "그는 조각가였는데, 놀라운 솜씨로 상아 조각상을 하나 만들었고, 그것이 어찌나 아름다웠던지 살아 있는 어떤 여인도 거기에는 도무지 미치지 못했다."], ["It was indeed the perfect semblance of a maiden that seemed to be alive, and only prevented from moving by modesty.", "그것은 실로 살아 있는 듯한, 다만 수줍음 때문에 움직이지 못할 뿐인 처녀의 완벽한 형상이었다."], ["His art was so perfect that it concealed itself and its product looked like the workmanship of nature.", "그의 기술은 너무도 완벽하여 스스로를 감추었고, 그 산물은 자연의 솜씨처럼 보였다."], ["Pygmalion admired his own work, and at last fell in love with the counterfeit creation.", "피그말리온은 자기 자신의 작품을 찬탄했고, 마침내 그 인공의 피조물과 사랑에 빠지고 말았다."], ["Oftentimes he laid his hand upon it as if to assure himself whether it were living or not, and could not even then believe that it was only ivory.", "그는 마치 그것이 살아 있는지 아닌지 확인하려는 듯 자주 그 위에 손을 얹어 보았으나, 그러고도 그것이 한낱 상아일 뿐이라고는 도무지 믿을 수가 없었다."], ["He caressed it, and gave it presents such as young girls love,—bright shells and polished stones, little birds and flowers of various hues, beads and amber.", "그는 그것을 어루만졌고, 어린 소녀들이 좋아할 만한 선물들을 주었다. 빛나는 조개껍데기와 매끄러운 돌멩이, 작은 새들과 갖가지 빛깔의 꽃들, 구슬과 호박 같은 것들이었다."], ["He put raiment on its limbs, and jewels on its fingers, and a necklace about its neck.", "그는 그 사지에 옷을 입혔고, 손가락에는 보석을 끼웠으며, 목에는 목걸이를 걸어 주었다."], ["To the ears he hung earrings and strings of pearls upon the breast.", "귀에는 귀고리를 달았고, 가슴에는 진주 목걸이를 드리웠다."], ["Her dress became her, and she looked not less charming than when unattired.", "옷차림은 그녀에게 잘 어울렸고, 아무것도 걸치지 않았을 때 못지않게 매혹적으로 보였다."], ["He laid her on a couch spread with cloths of Tyrian dye, and called her his wife, and put her head upon a pillow of the softest feathers, as if she could enjoy their softness.", "그는 그녀를 티로스산 염료로 물들인 천을 깐 침상 위에 누이고, 그녀를 자신의 아내라 불렀으며, 마치 그녀가 그 부드러움을 즐길 수 있기라도 한 듯 가장 부드러운 깃털을 채운 베개 위에 그 머리를 뉘었다."]], [["The festival of Venus was at hand—a festival celebrated with great pomp at Cyprus.", "아프로디테[로마의 〈베누스〉]의 축제가 다가왔으니, 그것은 키프로스에서 성대하게 거행되던 축제였다."], ["Victims were offered, the altars smoked, and the odor of incense filled the air.", "제물이 바쳐졌고, 제단에서는 연기가 피어올랐으며, 향내가 공기를 가득 채웠다."], ["When Pygmalion had performed his part in the solemnities, he stood before the altar and timidly said, “Ye gods, who can do all things, give me, I pray you, for my wife”—he dared not say “my ivory virgin,”", "피그말리온은 의식에서 자기 몫을 다하고 나자, 제단 앞에 서서 머뭇거리며 말했다. \"무엇이든 하실 수 있는 신들이시여, 부디 제게 아내로 삼을 이를 주십시오.\""], ["but said instead—“one like my ivory virgin.”", "그는 차마 \"제 상아 처녀를\"이라고는 말하지 못하고, 그 대신 \"제 상아 처녀 같은 이를\"이라고 말했다."], ["Venus, who was present at the festival, heard him and knew the thought he would have uttered; and as an omen of her favor, caused the flame on the altar to shoot up thrice in a fiery point into the air.", "그 축제에 함께 자리해 있던 아프로디테는 그의 말을 들었고, 그가 입에 담고 싶어 했던 생각을 알아차렸다. 그리하여 그녀는 자신의 호의를 알리는 징조로, 제단의 불꽃이 세 번 불기둥처럼 공중으로 솟구치게 했다."], ["When he returned home, he went to see his statue, and leaning over the couch, gave a kiss to the mouth.", "그가 집으로 돌아와 자신의 조각상을 보러 가서, 침상 위로 몸을 굽혀 그 입에 입을 맞추었다."], ["It seemed to be warm.", "그것은 따뜻하게 느껴졌다."], ["He pressed its lips again, he laid his hand upon the limbs; the ivory felt soft to his touch and yielded to his fingers like the wax of Hymettus.", "그가 다시 그 입술을 누르고, 그 사지에 손을 얹어 보니, 상아는 손길에 부드럽게 느껴졌고 히메토스의 밀랍처럼 그의 손가락에 눌려 들어갔다."], ["While he stands astonished and glad, though doubting, and fears he may be mistaken, again and again with a lover’s ardor he touches the object of his hopes.", "그가 놀라움과 기쁨에 휩싸인 채, 그러면서도 의심하며, 혹시 자신이 잘못 안 것은 아닐까 두려워하며 서 있는 동안, 연인의 열정으로 거듭거듭 그 희망의 대상을 만져 보았다."], ["It was indeed alive!", "그것은 정녕 살아 있었다!"], ["The veins when pressed yielded to the finger and again resumed their roundness.", "핏줄은 눌리면 손가락에 따라 들어갔다가 다시 본래의 둥근 모양을 되찾았다."], ["Then at last the votary of Venus found words to thank the goddess, and pressed his lips upon lips as real as his own.", "그제야 비로소 아프로디테의 신봉자는 여신께 감사를 드릴 말을 찾아냈고, 제 입술만큼이나 진짜인 입술 위에 자신의 입술을 눌렀다."], ["The virgin felt the kisses and blushed, and opening her timid eyes to the light, fixed them at the same moment on her lover.", "처녀는 그 입맞춤을 느끼며 얼굴을 붉혔고, 수줍은 두 눈을 빛을 향해 떠서, 바로 그 순간 그것을 자기 연인에게 고정했다."], ["Venus blessed the nuptials she had formed, and from this union Paphos was born, from whom the city, sacred to Venus, received its name.", "아프로디테는 자신이 맺어 준 혼인을 축복했고, 이 결합에서 파포스가 태어났으니, 아프로디테에게 봉헌된 그 도시는 바로 그에게서 이름을 얻었다."]], [["Schiller, in his poem the “Ideals,” applies this tale of Pygmalion to the love of nature in a youthful heart.", "실러는 자신의 시 「이상」에서 이 피그말리온의 이야기를 젊은 가슴속의 자연에 대한 사랑에 빗대었다."], ["The following translation is furnished by a friend:", "다음 번역은 한 친구가 제공한 것이다."]], [["“As once with prayers in passion flowing, Pygmalion embraced the stone, Till from the frozen marble glowing, The light of feeling o’er him shone, So did I clasp with young devotion Bright nature to a poet’s heart; Till breath and warmth and vital motion Seemed through the statue form to dart.", "\"한때 정열로 넘쳐흐르는 기도와 더불어, 피그말리온이 그 돌을 끌어안았을 때, 얼어붙은 대리석에서 빛이 타올라, 감정의 빛이 그를 비추었듯이, 나 또한 젊은 헌신으로 끌어안았네, 빛나는 자연을 시인의 가슴에다. 마침내 숨결과 온기와 살아 있는 움직임이 그 조각상의 형상을 꿰뚫고 솟구치는 듯했네."]], [["“And then, in all my ardor sharing, The silent form expression found; Returned my kiss of youthful daring, And understood my heart’s quick sound.", "그리고 나의 온갖 열정을 함께 나누며, 그 말없는 형상은 표정을 찾아내었고, 젊은 대담함이 담긴 나의 입맞춤에 화답하며, 내 가슴의 빠른 고동을 알아들었네. 그리하여 그 빛나는 피조물이 나를 위해 살아났고, 은빛 시냇물은 노래로 가득 차올랐네."], ["Then lived for me the bright creation, The silver rill with song was rife; The trees, the roses shared sensation, An echo of my boundless life.”", "나무들도, 장미들도 감각을 함께 나누었으니, 나의 가없는 생명의 메아리였네.\" ―S."], ["—S.", "G."], ["G. B.", "B."]], [["Dryope and Iole were sisters.", "드리오페와 이올레는 자매였다."], ["The former was the wife of Andræmon, beloved by her husband, and happy in the birth of her first child.", "드리오페는 안드라이몬의 아내로, 남편의 사랑을 받았고, 첫아이를 낳아 행복했다."], ["One day the sisters strolled to the bank of a stream that sloped gradually down to the water’s edge, while the upland was overgrown with myrtles.", "어느 날 자매는 물가까지 완만하게 비탈진 시냇가로 거닐어 나갔는데, 그 위쪽 땅에는 도금양이 무성하게 우거져 있었다."], ["They were intending to gather flowers for forming garlands for the altars of the nymphs, and Dryope carried her child at her bosom, precious burden, and nursed him as she walked.", "그들은 님프들의 제단에 바칠 화환을 엮을 꽃을 따려던 참이었고, 드리오페는 소중한 짐인 자기 아이를 가슴에 안고서 걸으며 젖을 먹이고 있었다."], ["Near the water grew a lotus plant, full of purple flowers.", "물가에는 자줏빛 꽃이 가득한 로토스 나무 한 그루가 자라고 있었다."], ["Dryope gathered some and offered them to the baby, and Iole was about to do the same, when she perceived blood dropping from the places where her sister had broken them off the stem.", "드리오페가 몇 송이를 꺾어 아기에게 내밀었고, 이올레도 막 같은 일을 하려던 참이었는데, 그때 그녀는 자기 언니가 줄기에서 꽃을 꺾어 낸 자리에서 피가 떨어지는 것을 보았다."], ["The plant was no other than the nymph Lotis, who, running from a base pursuer, had been changed into this form.", "그 나무는 다름 아니라 비천한 추적자에게서 달아나다가 이 형상으로 바뀐 님프 로티스였다."], ["This they learned from the country people when it was too late.", "이 사실을 그들은 시골 사람들에게서 알게 되었으나, 그때는 이미 너무 늦은 뒤였다."]], [["Dryope, horror-struck when she perceived what she had done, would gladly have hastened from the spot, but found her feet rooted to the ground.", "드리오페는 자신이 저지른 일을 깨닫고 공포에 사로잡혀, 기꺼이 그 자리에서 서둘러 벗어나려 했으나, 자기 두 발이 땅에 뿌리내린 것을 알았다."], ["She tried to pull them away, but moved nothing but her upper limbs.", "그녀는 발을 빼내려 애썼지만, 상체밖에는 움직일 수가 없었다."], ["The woodiness crept upward, and by degrees invested her body.", "나무의 성질이 위로 기어올라, 차츰차츰 그녀의 몸을 뒤덮었다."], ["In anguish she attempted to tear her hair, but found her hands filled with leaves.", "괴로움에 못 이겨 그녀는 머리카락을 쥐어뜯으려 했으나, 두 손에 나뭇잎이 가득함을 알았다."], ["The infant felt his mother’s bosom begin to harden, and the milk cease to flow.", "아기는 어머니의 가슴이 굳어지기 시작하고 젖이 더는 흐르지 않는 것을 느꼈다."], ["Iole looked on at the sad fate of her sister, and could render no assistance.", "이올레는 언니의 슬픈 운명을 지켜보았으나, 아무런 도움도 줄 수 없었다."], ["She embraced the growing trunk, as if she would hold back the advancing wood, and would gladly have been enveloped in the same bark.", "그녀는 차오르는 나무 둥치를 끌어안았으니, 마치 뻗어 가는 나무를 붙들어 막으려는 듯했고, 기꺼이 같은 나무껍질에 함께 감싸이고 싶어 했다."], ["At this moment Andræmon, the husband of Dryope, with her father, approached; and when they asked for Dryope, Iole pointed them to the new-formed lotus.", "그 순간 드리오페의 남편 안드라이몬이 그녀의 아버지와 함께 다가왔다. 그들이 드리오페를 찾자, 이올레는 새로이 생겨난 로토스 나무를 가리켰다."], ["They embraced the trunk of the yet warm tree, and showered their kisses on its leaves.", "그들은 아직 온기가 남은 그 나무 둥치를 끌어안고, 그 잎사귀에 입맞춤을 퍼부었다."]], [["Now there was nothing left of Dryope but her face.", "이제 드리오페에게 남은 것이라곤 그 얼굴뿐이었다."], ["Her tears still flowed and fell on her leaves, and while she could she spoke.", "그녀의 눈물은 여전히 흘러 그 잎사귀 위로 떨어졌고, 말할 수 있는 동안 그녀는 입을 열었다."], ["“I am not guilty.", "\"저는 죄가 없습니다."], ["I deserve not this fate.", "저는 이런 운명을 받아 마땅하지 않습니다."], ["I have injured no one.", "저는 누구도 해친 적이 없습니다."], ["If I speak falsely, may my foliage perish with drought and my trunk be cut down and burned.", "만일 제가 거짓을 말한다면, 제 잎사귀가 가뭄으로 시들고 제 둥치가 베여 불태워지게 하소서. 이 아기를 데려가 유모에게 맡겨 주십시오."], ["Take this infant and give it to a nurse.", "자주 데려와 제 가지 아래에서 젖을 먹이고, 제 그늘에서 뛰놀게 해 주십시오."], ["Let it often be brought and nursed under my branches, and play in my shade; and when he is old enough to talk, let him be taught to call me mother, and to say with sadness, ‘My mother lies hid under this bark.’ But bid him be careful of river banks, and beware how he plucks flowers, remembering that every bush he sees may be a goddess in disguise.", "그리고 이 아이가 말할 만큼 자라거든, 저를 어머니라 부르도록, 그리고 슬픔에 잠겨 '저의 어머니는 이 나무껍질 아래 숨어 계십니다'라고 말하도록 가르쳐 주십시오. 다만 이 아이에게 강가를 조심하고, 꽃을 꺾을 때 마음을 쓰며, 눈에 보이는 덤불 하나하나가 모습을 감춘 여신일 수 있음을 기억하라고 일러 주십시오."], ["Farewell, dear husband, and sister, and father.", "잘 있어요, 사랑하는 남편이여, 그리고 동생이여, 아버지여."], ["If you retain any love for me, let not the axe wound me, nor the flocks bite and tear my branches.", "저를 향한 사랑이 조금이라도 남아 있거든, 도끼가 저를 상하게 하지 못하게 하고, 가축 떼가 제 가지를 물어뜯지 못하게 해 주십시오."], ["Since I cannot stoop to you, climb up hither and kiss me; and while my lips continue to feel, lift up my child that I may kiss him.", "제가 당신들에게 몸을 굽힐 수 없으니, 이리로 올라와 제게 입을 맞춰 주십시오. 그리고 제 입술이 아직 느낄 수 있는 동안, 제 아이를 들어 올려 제가 입 맞출 수 있게 해 주십시오."], ["I can speak no more, for already the bark advances up my neck, and will soon shoot over me.", "더는 말할 수가 없습니다. 어느새 나무껍질이 제 목까지 차올라, 곧 저를 온통 뒤덮을 것이기 때문입니다."], ["You need not close my eyes, the bark will close them without your aid.”", "제 눈을 감겨 주실 필요는 없습니다. 당신들의 손길 없이도 나무껍질이 그 눈을 감겨 줄 것입니다.\""], ["Then the lips ceased to move, and life was extinct; but the branches retained for some time longer the vital heat.", "그러더니 그 입술은 움직임을 멈추었고, 생명은 꺼지고 말았다. 그러나 가지들은 얼마 동안 더 생명의 온기를 간직하고 있었다."]], [["Keats, in “Endymion,” alludes to Dryope thus:", "키츠는 「엔디미온」에서 드리오페를 이렇게 언급한다."]], [["“She took a lute from which there pulsing came A lively prelude, fashioning the way In which her voice should wander.", "\"그녀는 류트를 집어 들었고, 거기서 고동치듯 경쾌한 전주가 흘러나와, 그녀의 목소리가 노닐어 갈 길을 빚어내었네."], ["’Twas a lay More subtle-cadenced, more forest-wild Than Dryope’s lone lulling of her child;”", "그것은 드리오페가 홀로 제 아이를 어르던 자장가보다도 더 미묘한 가락을 띤, 더 숲처럼 야성적인 노래였네.\""], ["etc.", "등등."]], [["# VENUS AND ADONIS", "# VENUS AND ADONIS"]], [["Venus, playing one day with her boy Cupid, wounded her bosom with one of his arrows.", "아프로디테가 어느 날 자기 아들 에로스[로마의 〈쿠피도〉]와 놀다가, 그 화살 하나에 가슴을 다치고 말았다."], ["She pushed him away, but the wound was deeper than she thought.", "그녀는 아들을 밀쳐 냈으나, 그 상처는 그녀가 생각했던 것보다 깊었다."], ["Before it healed she beheld Adonis, and was captivated with him.", "그것이 채 아물기도 전에 그녀는 아도니스를 보았고, 그에게 마음을 빼앗겼다."], ["She no longer took any interest in her favorite resorts—Paphos, and Cnidos, and Amathos, rich in metals.", "그녀는 이제 자신이 즐겨 찾던 곳들, 곧 파포스와 크니도스, 그리고 금속이 풍부한 아마토스에도 더는 흥미를 두지 않았다."], ["She absented herself even from heaven, for Adonis was dearer to her than heaven.", "그녀는 하늘마저 멀리하였으니, 아도니스가 그녀에게는 하늘보다 더 소중했기 때문이다."], ["Him she followed and bore him company.", "그녀는 그를 따라다니며 그와 동무가 되어 주었다."], ["She who used to love to recline in the shade, with no care but to cultivate her charms, now rambles through the woods and over the hills, dressed like the huntress Diana; and calls her dogs, and chases hares and stags, or other game that it is safe to hunt, but keeps clear of the wolves and bears, reeking with the slaughter of the herd.", "한때 그늘에 기대 누워 제 아름다움을 가꾸는 것 말고는 아무 근심도 없던 그녀가, 이제는 사냥꾼 아르테미스[로마의 〈디아나〉]처럼 차려입고서 숲을 가로지르고 언덕을 넘어 거닐었다. 그녀는 자기 개들을 부르며, 산토끼와 사슴이나, 사냥해도 안전한 다른 짐승을 쫓았으나, 가축 떼를 도륙한 피비린내를 풍기는 늑대와 곰은 멀리했다."], ["She charged Adonis, too, to beware of such dangerous animals.", "그녀는 아도니스에게도 그러한 위험한 짐승들을 조심하라고 단단히 일렀다."], ["“Be brave towards the timid,”", "\"겁 많은 것들에게는 용감하세요.\""], ["said she; “courage against the courageous is not safe.", "그녀가 말했다. \"용감한 것들을 상대로 한 용기는 안전하지 않답니다."], ["Beware how you expose yourself to danger and put my happiness to risk.", "스스로를 위험에 내맡겨 제 행복을 위태롭게 하지 않도록 조심하세요."], ["Attack not the beasts that Nature has armed with weapons.", "자연이 무기로 무장시켜 놓은 짐승들은 공격하지 마세요."], ["I do not value your glory so high as to consent to purchase it by such exposure.", "저는 당신의 영광을 그토록 높이 사서 그런 위험을 무릅쓰며 그것을 사들이는 데 동의할 만큼은 아니랍니다."], ["Your youth, and the beauty that charms Venus, will not touch the hearts of lions and bristly boars.", "당신의 젊음과 아프로디테를 매혹하는 그 아름다움도, 사자와 뻣뻣한 털의 멧돼지들의 마음은 움직이지 못해요."], ["Think of their terrible claws and prodigious strength!", "그것들의 무시무시한 발톱과 어마어마한 힘을 생각해 보세요!"], ["I hate the whole race of them.", "저는 그것들의 종족 전체를 미워한답니다."], ["Do you ask me why?”", "그 까닭을 제게 물으시나요?\""], ["Then she told him the story of Atalanta and Hippomenes, who were changed into lions for their ingratitude to her.", "그러더니 그녀는 자신에게 배은망덕했던 탓에 사자로 변해 버린 아탈란테와 히포메네스의 이야기를 그에게 들려주었다."]], [["Having given him this warning, she mounted her chariot drawn by swans, and drove away through the air.", "그에게 이렇게 경고를 한 뒤, 그녀는 백조들이 끄는 자기 수레에 올라 공중을 가르며 떠나갔다."], ["But Adonis was too noble to heed such counsels.", "그러나 아도니스는 그런 충고에 귀 기울이기에는 너무도 기개가 높았다."], ["The dogs had roused a wild boar from his lair, and the youth threw his spear and wounded the animal with a sidelong stroke.", "개들이 멧돼지 한 마리를 그 굴에서 몰아냈고, 젊은이는 자기 창을 던져 비스듬한 일격으로 그 짐승에게 상처를 입혔다."], ["The beast drew out the weapon with his jaws, and rushed after Adonis, who turned and ran; but the boar overtook him, and buried his tusks in his side, and stretched him dying upon the plain.", "짐승은 주둥이로 그 창을 뽑아내고서 아도니스를 향해 돌진했고, 아도니스는 몸을 돌려 달아났다. 그러나 멧돼지가 그를 따라잡아, 그 옆구리에 엄니를 박아 넣고는, 그를 들판 위에 죽어 가는 채로 뻗어 눕히고 말았다."]], [["Venus, in her swan-drawn chariot, had not yet reached Cyprus, when she heard coming up through mid-air the groans of her beloved, and turned her white-winged coursers back to earth.", "백조가 끄는 수레에 탄 아프로디테는 아직 키프로스에 이르지 못했는데, 그때 공중을 가로질러 올라오는 사랑하는 이의 신음을 듣고, 자신의 흰 날개 달린 준마들을 땅으로 되돌렸다."], ["As she drew near and saw from on high his lifeless body bathed in blood, she alighted and, bending over it, beat her breast and tore her hair.", "그녀가 가까이 다가가 높은 곳에서 피에 잠긴 그의 생기 없는 몸을 보고는 내려앉아, 그 위로 몸을 굽힌 채 가슴을 치고 머리카락을 쥐어뜯었다. 운명의 여신들을 책망하며 그녀가 말했다."], ["Reproaching the Fates, she said, “Yet theirs shall be but a partial triumph; memorials of my grief shall endure, and the spectacle of your death, my Adonis, and of my lamentations shall be annually renewed.", "\"그러나 그들의 승리는 절반에 그칠 뿐이리라. 내 슬픔을 기리는 표적은 길이 남을 것이며, 나의 아도니스여, 그대의 죽음과 나의 애통함의 광경은 해마다 새로이 되살아나리라."], ["Your blood shall be changed into a flower; that consolation none can envy me.”", "그대의 피는 한 송이 꽃으로 바뀔 것이니, 그 위안만은 누구도 내게서 시샘할 수 없으리라.\""], ["Thus speaking, she sprinkled nectar on the blood; and as they mingled, bubbles rose as in a pool on which raindrops fall, and in an hour’s time there sprang up a flower of bloody hue like that of the pomegranate.", "이렇게 말하며 그녀는 그 피 위에 넥타르를 뿌렸다. 그러자 그것들이 뒤섞이며, 빗방울이 떨어지는 웅덩이에서 그러하듯 거품이 일어올랐고, 한 시간이 지나자 석류꽃 같은 핏빛의 꽃 한 송이가 솟아났다."], ["But it is short-lived.", "그러나 그것은 수명이 짧다."], ["It is said the wind blows the blossoms open, and afterwards blows the petals away; so it is called Anemone, or Wind Flower, from the cause which assists equally in its production and its decay.", "바람이 불어 그 꽃송이를 피워 놓고, 이내 또 불어 그 꽃잎을 날려 버린다고 한다. 그리하여 그것은 아네모네, 곧 바람꽃이라 불리니, 그것을 피워 내는 데에도 또 시들게 하는 데에도 똑같이 거드는 그 원인에서 비롯한 이름이다."]], [["Milton alludes to the story of Venus and Adonis in his “Comus”:", "밀턴은 자신의 「코머스」에서 아프로디테와 아도니스의 이야기를 언급한다."]], [["“Beds of hyacinth and roses Where young Adonis oft reposes, Waxing well of his deep wound In slumber soft, and on the ground Sadly sits th’ Assyrian queen;”", "\"히아킨토스와 장미꽃 자리들, 젊은 아도니스가 자주 쉬어 가는 곳, 부드러운 잠결에 그 깊은 상처가 잘도 아물어 가는 곳, 그리고 땅바닥에는 슬픔에 잠긴 아시리아의 여왕이 앉아 있네.\""], ["etc.", "등등."]], [["# APOLLO AND HYACINTHUS", "# APOLLO AND HYACINTHUS"]], [["Apollo was passionately fond of a youth named Hyacinthus.", "아폴론은 히아킨토스라는 젊은이를 열렬히 사랑했다."], ["He accompanied him in his sports, carried the nets when he went fishing, led the dogs when he went to hunt, followed him in his excursions in the mountains, and neglected for him his lyre and his arrows.", "그는 히아킨토스가 노는 데 함께했고, 그가 낚시하러 갈 때면 그물을 들어 주었으며, 그가 사냥하러 갈 때면 개들을 이끌었고, 그가 산으로 나설 때면 그를 따라다녔으며, 그를 위해 자신의 리라와 화살을 소홀히 했다."], ["One day they played a game of quoits together, and Apollo, heaving aloft the discus, with strength mingled with skill, sent it high and far.", "어느 날 그들이 함께 원반던지기 놀이를 했는데, 아폴론은 솜씨가 어린 힘으로 원반을 높이 치켜들어 높고도 멀리 던졌다."], ["Hyacinthus watched it as it flew, and excited with the sport ran forward to seize it, eager to make his throw, when the quoit bounded from the earth and struck him in the forehead.", "히아킨토스는 그것이 날아가는 것을 지켜보다가, 놀이에 흥이 올라 그것을 붙잡으려고, 어서 제 차례에 던지고 싶은 마음에 앞으로 달려나갔다. 그때 원반이 땅에서 튀어 올라 그의 이마를 때렸다."], ["He fainted and fell.", "그는 정신을 잃고 쓰러졌다."], ["The god, as pale as himself, raised him and tried all his art to stanch the wound and retain the flitting life, but all in vain; the hurt was past the power of medicine.", "신은 그 자신만큼이나 창백해진 얼굴로 그를 일으켜, 상처의 피를 멎게 하고 사라져 가는 생명을 붙들고자 자신의 온갖 재주를 다 부려 보았으나, 모든 것이 헛되었다. 그 상처는 의술의 힘이 미치지 못하는 것이었다."], ["As when one has broken the stem of a lily in the garden it hangs its head and turns its flowers to the earth, so the head of the dying boy, as if too heavy for his neck, fell over on his shoulder.", "마치 누군가 정원에서 백합의 줄기를 꺾으면 그 꽃이 고개를 떨구고 꽃송이를 땅으로 돌리듯이, 죽어 가는 소년의 머리도 마치 그 목에 너무 무겁기라도 한 듯 어깨 위로 떨구어졌다. \"그대가 죽어 가는구나, 히아킨토스여.\""], ["“Thou diest, Hyacinth,”", "포이보스[아폴론]가 이렇게 말했다."], ["so spoke Phœbus, “robbed of thy youth by me.", "\"나로 말미암아 그대의 젊음을 빼앗긴 채로 말이다."], ["Thine is the suffering, mine the crime.", "그대에게는 고통이요, 내게는 죄로다."], ["Would that I could die for thee!", "내가 그대를 위해 죽을 수만 있다면!"], ["But since that may not be, thou shalt live with me in memory and in song.", "그러나 그럴 수가 없으니, 그대는 추억 속에서, 또 노래 속에서 나와 더불어 살아가리라."], ["My lyre shall celebrate thee, my song shall tell thy fate, and thou shalt become a flower inscribed with my regrets.”", "나의 리라가 그대를 기릴 것이며, 나의 노래가 그대의 운명을 들려줄 것이고, 그대는 나의 회한이 새겨진 한 송이 꽃이 되리라.\""], ["While Apollo spoke, behold the blood which had flowed on the ground and stained the herbage ceased to be blood; but a flower of hue more beautiful than the Tyrian sprang up, resembling the lily, if it were not that this is purple and that silvery white.[12] And this was not enough for Phœbus; but to confer still greater honor, he marked the petals with his sorrow, and inscribed “Ah!", "아폴론이 말하는 동안, 보라, 땅 위로 흘러 풀을 물들였던 그 피가 더는 피가 아니게 되었고, 티로스의 염료보다도 더 아름다운 빛깔의 꽃 한 송이가 솟아났으니, 이것은 자줏빛이요 백합은 은빛처럼 흰 것을 빼고는 백합을 닮은 꽃이었다.[12] 그러나 이것으로도 포이보스에게는 충분하지 않았다. 더 큰 영예를 부여하고자, 그는 그 꽃잎에 자신의 슬픔을 새겨, 오늘날에도 우리가 보는 대로 그 위에 \"아!"], ["ah!”", "아!\""], ["upon them as we see to this day.", "하는 글자를 적어 넣었다."], ["The flower bears the name of Hyacinthus, and with every returning spring revives the memory of his fate.", "그 꽃은 히아킨토스라는 이름을 지니며, 봄이 돌아올 때마다 그의 운명에 대한 기억을 되살린다."]], [["It was said that Zephyrus (the West wind), who was also fond of Hyacinthus and jealous of his preference of Apollo, blew the quoit out of its course to make it strike Hyacinthus.", "서풍인 제피로스 또한 히아킨토스를 좋아하여 그가 아폴론을 더 좋아하는 것을 질투한 나머지, 원반이 히아킨토스를 맞히도록 그 진로에서 벗어나게 불어 버렸다고 한다."], ["Keats alludes to this in his “Endymion,” where he describes the lookers-on at the game of quoits:", "키츠는 자신의 「엔디미온」에서 원반던지기 놀이의 구경꾼들을 묘사하는 대목에서 이를 언급한다."]], [["“Or they might watch the quoit-pitchers, intent On either side, pitying the sad death Of Hyacinthus, when the cruel breath Of Zephyr slew him; Zephyr penitent, Who now ere Phœbus mounts the firmament, Fondles the flower amid the sobbing rain.”", "\"혹은 그들은 원반 던지는 이들을 지켜보았으리, 양편에 마음을 쏟으며, 히아킨토스의 슬픈 죽음을 가엾이 여기며, 제피로스의 잔인한 숨결이 그를 죽이던 그때를. 이제 뉘우치는 제피로스는, 포이보스가 창공에 오르기 전에, 흐느끼는 빗속에서 그 꽃을 어루만지네.\""]], [["An allusion to Hyacinthus will also be recognized in Milton’s “Lycidas”:", "히아킨토스에 대한 언급은 밀턴의 「리시다스」에서도 알아볼 수 있다."]], [["“Like to that sanguine flower inscribed with woe.”", "\"비탄이 새겨진 저 핏빛 꽃처럼.\""], ["————", "————"]], [["CEYX was king of Thessaly, where he reigned in peace, without violence or wrong.", "케익스는 테살리아의 왕으로, 폭력이나 불의 없이 평화롭게 다스렸다."], ["He was son of Hesperus, the Day-star, and the glow of his beauty reminded one of his father.", "그는 낮의 별인 헤스페로스의 아들이었고, 그 빛나는 아름다움은 보는 이로 하여금 그의 아버지를 떠올리게 했다."], ["Halcyone, the daughter of Æolus, was his wife, and devotedly attached to him.", "아이올로스의 딸 할키오네는 그의 아내였으며, 그에게 헌신적으로 매달려 있었다."], ["Now Ceyx was in deep affliction for the loss of his brother, and direful prodigies following his brother’s death made him feel as if the gods were hostile to him.", "그런데 케익스는 동생을 잃은 슬픔에 깊이 빠져 있었으며, 동생의 죽음에 뒤이어 나타난 무시무시한 징조들 탓에 마치 신들이 자신에게 적의를 품은 것처럼 느껴졌다."], ["He thought best, therefore, to make a voyage to Carlos in Ionia, to consult the oracle of Apollo.", "그리하여 그는 이오니아의 카를로스로 항해하여 아폴론의 신탁을 구하는 것이 최선이라고 생각했다."], ["But as soon as he disclosed his intention to his wife Halcyone, a shudder ran through her frame, and her face grew deadly pale.", "그러나 그가 이 뜻을 아내 할키오네에게 밝히자마자, 그녀의 온몸에 전율이 일었고 얼굴은 죽은 듯이 창백해졌다."], ["“What fault of mine, dearest husband, has turned your affection from me?", "\"사랑하는 남편이시여, 제 어떤 잘못이 당신의 애정을 제게서 돌려놓았나요?"], ["Where is that love of me that used to be uppermost in your thoughts?", "늘 당신의 마음속 가장 윗자리를 차지하던 제게 대한 그 사랑은 어디로 갔나요?"], ["Have you learned to feel easy in the absence of Halcyone?", "할키오네가 없어도 마음이 편안하다는 것을 배우셨나요?"], ["Would you rather have me away?”", "차라리 제가 곁에 없기를 바라시나요?\""], ["She also endeavored to discourage him, by describing the violence of the winds, which she had known familiarly when she lived at home in her father’s house,—Æolus being the god of the winds, and having as much as he could do to restrain them.", "그녀는 또한 바람의 거셈을 묘사하며 그를 단념시키려 애썼다. 친정인 아버지의 집에서 살 적에 익히 알고 지냈던 바람의 모습을 그녀는 잘 알았으니, 아이올로스가 바람의 신이었고 그것들을 억누르는 데 온 힘을 다해야 했던 까닭이다."], ["“They rush together,”", "\"그것들이 한데 몰아쳐,\""], ["said she, “with such fury that fire flashes from the conflict.", "그녀가 말했다, \"그 격렬함에 부딪침으로 불꽃이 튈 정도예요."], ["But if you must go,”", "하지만 정 가셔야 한다면,\""], ["she added, “dear husband, let me go with you, otherwise I shall suffer not only the real evils which you must encounter, but those also which my fears suggest.”", "그녀가 덧붙였다, \"사랑하는 남편이시여, 저를 함께 데려가 주세요. 그러지 않으면 저는 당신이 마주할 실제의 재앙뿐 아니라, 제 두려움이 빚어내는 재앙까지도 겪게 될 거예요.\""]], [["These words weighed heavily on the mind of King Ceyx, and it was no less his own wish than hers to take her with him, but he could not bear to expose her to the dangers of the sea.", "이 말은 케익스 왕의 마음을 무겁게 짓눌렀고, 아내를 데려가고 싶은 것은 그녀의 바람 못지않은 그 자신의 바람이기도 했다. 그러나 그는 그녀를 바다의 위험에 내맡길 수는 없었다."], ["He answered, therefore, consoling her as well as he could, and finished with these words: “I promise, by the rays of my father the Day-star, that if fate permits I will return before the moon shall have twice rounded her orb.”", "그리하여 그는 할 수 있는 한 그녀를 위로하며 대답했고, 끝으로 이런 말을 했다. \"나의 아버지 낮의 별의 빛에 걸고 약속하오."], ["When he had thus spoken, he ordered the vessel to be drawn out of the shiphouse, and the oars and sails to be put aboard.", "운명이 허락한다면, 달이 두 번 그 둘레를 다 돌기 전에 돌아오리다.\" 이렇게 말하고 나서 그는 배를 선창에서 끌어내고, 노와 돛을 실으라고 명했다."], ["When Halcyone saw these preparations she shuddered, as if with a presentiment of evil.", "할키오네는 이 준비를 보자 불길한 예감에 사로잡힌 듯 몸을 떨었다."], ["With tears and sobs she said farewell, and then fell senseless to the ground.", "눈물과 흐느낌으로 작별을 고하고는, 그대로 정신을 잃고 땅에 쓰러졌다."]], [["Ceyx would still have lingered, but now the young men grasped their oars and pulled vigorously through the waves, with long and measured strokes.", "케익스는 여전히 머뭇거리고자 했지만, 이제 젊은이들은 노를 움켜쥐고 길고 고른 동작으로 힘차게 물살을 헤쳐 나갔다."], ["Halcyone raised her streaming eyes, and saw her husband standing on the deck, waving his hand to her.", "할키오네는 눈물이 흐르는 눈을 들어, 갑판 위에 서서 자신을 향해 손을 흔드는 남편을 바라보았다."], ["She answered his signal till the vessel had receded so far that she could no longer distinguish his form from the rest.", "그녀는 배가 너무 멀어져 그의 모습을 다른 사람들과 더 이상 구별할 수 없게 될 때까지 그의 신호에 답했다."], ["When the vessel itself could no more be seen, she strained her eyes to catch the last glimmer of the sail, till that too disappeared.", "배 자체가 더 이상 보이지 않게 되자, 그녀는 돛의 마지막 희미한 빛을 붙잡으려 눈을 부릅떴고, 마침내 그것마저 사라졌다."], ["Then, retiring to her chamber, she threw herself on her solitary couch.", "그제야 그녀는 방으로 물러나 외로운 침상에 몸을 던졌다."]], [["Meanwhile they glide out of the harbor, and the breeze plays among the ropes.", "한편 그들은 항구를 미끄러져 나갔고, 산들바람이 밧줄 사이로 노닐었다."], ["The seamen draw in their oars, and hoist their sails.", "뱃사람들은 노를 거두어들이고 돛을 올렸다."], ["When half or less of their course was passed, as night drew on, the sea began to whiten with swelling waves, and the east wind to blow a gale.", "항로의 절반쯤, 혹은 그보다 덜 지났을 무렵, 밤이 다가오면서 바다는 부풀어 오르는 파도로 하얗게 일기 시작했고, 동풍이 거센 바람을 몰아왔다."], ["The master gave the word to take in sail, but the storm forbade obedience, for such is the roar of the winds and waves his orders are unheard.", "선장이 돛을 거두라는 명령을 내렸으나, 폭풍이 복종을 가로막았으니, 바람과 파도의 포효가 너무도 커서 그의 명령이 들리지 않았던 까닭이다."], ["The men, of their own accord, busy themselves to secure the oars, to strengthen the ship, to reef the sail.", "사람들은 저마다 알아서 노를 단단히 매고, 배를 보강하고, 돛을 줄이는 일에 분주했다."], ["While they thus do what to each one seems best, the storm increases.", "그들이 저마다 최선이라 여기는 일을 하는 동안에도 폭풍은 거세어졌다."], ["The shouting of the men, the rattling of the shrouds, and the dashing of the waves, mingle with the roar of the thunder.", "사람들의 외침, 삭구의 덜컹거림, 파도의 부딪침이 천둥의 포효와 뒤섞였다."], ["The swelling sea seems lifted up to the heavens, to scatter its foam among the clouds; then sinking away to the bottom assumes the color of the shoal—a Stygian blackness.", "부풀어 오른 바다는 하늘까지 치솟아 그 거품을 구름 사이에 흩뿌리는 듯했고, 그러고는 바닥으로 가라앉으며 여울의 빛깔, 곧 스틱스의 검음을 띠었다."]], [["The vessel shares all these changes.", "배는 이 모든 변화를 함께 겪었다."], ["It seems like a wild beast that rushes on the spears of the hunters.", "그것은 사냥꾼들의 창을 향해 돌진하는 야수와도 같았다."], ["Rain falls in torrents, as if the skies were coming down to unite with the sea.", "마치 하늘이 내려와 바다와 하나가 되려는 듯, 비가 억수같이 쏟아졌다."], ["When the lightning ceases for a moment, the night seems to add its own darkness to that of the storm; then comes the flash, rending the darkness asunder, and lighting up all with a glare.", "번개가 잠시 멎으면, 밤은 폭풍의 어둠 위에 제 어둠을 더하는 듯했고, 그러고는 번개가 번쩍여 어둠을 갈가리 찢고 모든 것을 섬광으로 밝혔다."], ["Skill fails, courage sinks, and death seems to come on every wave.", "기술은 소용없었고, 용기는 꺾였으며, 파도마다 죽음이 닥쳐오는 듯했다."], ["The men are stupefied with terror.", "사람들은 공포에 얼이 빠졌다."], ["The thought of parents, and kindred, and pledges left at home, comes over their minds.", "집에 두고 온 부모와 친족과 약속들에 대한 생각이 그들의 마음을 스쳤다."], ["Ceyx thinks of Halcyone.", "케익스는 할키오네를 생각했다."], ["No name but hers is on his lips, and while he yearns for her, he yet rejoices in her absence.", "그의 입술에는 그녀의 이름밖에 없었고, 그녀를 그리워하면서도, 그녀가 곁에 없음을 기뻐했다."], ["Presently the mast is shattered by a stroke of lightning, the rudder broken, and the triumphant surge curling over looks down upon the wreck, then falls, and crushes it to fragments.", "이윽고 돛대가 번개를 맞아 산산조각 났고, 키가 부러졌으며, 의기양양하게 굽이쳐 오른 물결이 그 잔해를 내려다보다가 떨어져 내려 산산이 부수어 버렸다."], ["Some of the seamen, stunned by the stroke, sink, and rise no more; others cling to fragments of the wreck.", "뱃사람 가운데 몇몇은 그 충격에 정신을 잃고 가라앉아 다시는 떠오르지 못했고, 다른 이들은 난파선의 조각에 매달렸다."], ["Ceyx, with the hand that used to grasp the sceptre, holds fast to a plank, calling for help,—alas, in vain,—upon his father and his father-in-law.", "케익스는 일찍이 왕홀을 움켜쥐던 그 손으로 널빤지를 단단히 붙잡고, 그의 아버지와 장인을 향해 도움을 청했으나, 아아, 헛된 일이었다."], ["But oftenest on his lips was the name of Halcyone.", "그러나 그의 입술에 가장 자주 오른 것은 할키오네의 이름이었다."], ["To her his thoughts cling.", "그의 생각은 그녀에게 매달렸다."], ["He prays that the waves may bear his body to her sight, and that it may receive burial at her hands.", "그는 파도가 자신의 몸을 그녀의 눈앞으로 실어다 주기를, 그리하여 그녀의 손으로 묻히기를 기도했다."], ["At length the waters overwhelm him, and he sinks.", "마침내 물결이 그를 덮쳤고, 그는 가라앉았다."], ["The Day-star looked dim that night.", "그날 밤 낮의 별은 흐릿해 보였다."], ["Since it could not leave the heavens, it shrouded its face with clouds.", "하늘을 떠날 수 없었기에, 그 얼굴을 구름으로 가렸던 것이다."]], [["In the meanwhile Halcyone, ignorant of all these horrors, counted the days till her husband’s promised return.", "그동안 할키오네는 이 모든 참사를 알지 못한 채, 남편이 약속한 귀환의 날까지 날을 헤아렸다."], ["Now she gets ready the garments which he shall put on, and now what she shall wear when he arrives.", "이제 그녀는 그가 입을 옷을 마련하고, 또 그가 도착할 때 자신이 입을 옷을 준비했다."], ["To all the gods she offers frequent incense, but more than all to Juno.", "그녀는 모든 신들에게 자주 향을 올렸으나, 누구보다도 헤라[로마의 〈유노〉]에게 그러했다."], ["For her husband, who was no more, she prayed incessantly: that he might be safe; that he might come home; that he might not, in his absence, see any one that he would love better than her.", "이미 세상에 없는 남편을 위해 그녀는 쉴 새 없이 기도했다. 그가 무사하기를, 집으로 돌아오기를, 떨어져 있는 동안 자신보다 더 사랑할 만한 사람을 만나지 않기를."], ["But of all these prayers, the last was the only one destined to be granted.", "그러나 이 모든 기도 가운데 마지막 것만이 이루어질 운명이었다."], ["The goddess, at length, could not bear any longer to be pleaded with for one already dead, and to have hands raised to her altars that ought rather to be offering funeral rites.", "여신은 마침내 이미 죽은 자를 위해 간청받는 것을, 그리고 차라리 장례 의식을 올려야 마땅할 손이 자신의 제단을 향해 들어 올려지는 것을 더는 견딜 수 없었다."], ["So, calling Iris, she said, “Iris, my faithful messenger, go to the drowsy dwelling of Somnus, and tell him to send a vision to Halcyone in the form of Ceyx, to make known to her the event.”", "그리하여 이리스를 불러 말했다. \"이리스, 나의 충실한 전령이여, 졸음에 잠긴 솜누스의 거처로 가서, 케익스의 모습을 한 환영을 할키오네에게 보내어 그 일을 알리라고 일러라.\""]], [["Iris puts on her robe of many colors, and tingeing the sky with her bow, seeks the palace of the King of Sleep.", "이리스는 여러 빛깔의 옷을 두르고, 무지개로 하늘을 물들이며, 잠의 왕의 궁전을 찾아갔다."], ["Near the Cimmerian country, a mountain cave is the abode of the dull god Somnus.", "킴메리아 땅 가까이에 있는 한 산속 동굴이 둔한 신 솜누스의 거처였다."], ["Here Phœbus dares not come, either rising, at midday, or setting.", "이곳에는 포이보스[아폴론]가 떠오를 때도, 한낮에도, 질 때도 감히 들어오지 못했다."], ["Clouds and shadows are exhaled from the ground, and the light glimmers faintly.", "땅에서는 구름과 그림자가 피어올랐고, 빛은 희미하게 가물거렸다."], ["The bird of dawning, with crested head, never there calls aloud to Aurora, nor watchful dog, nor more sagacious goose disturbs the silence.", "볏 달린 머리를 한 새벽의 새는 그곳에서 결코 에오스[로마의 〈아우로라〉]를 향해 큰 소리로 울지 않았고, 경계하는 개도, 더 영리한 거위도 그 고요를 어지럽히지 않았다."], ["No wild beast, nor cattle, nor branch moved with the wind, nor sound of human conversation, breaks the stillness.", "어떤 야수도, 가축도, 바람에 흔들리는 나뭇가지도, 사람의 대화 소리도 그 정적을 깨뜨리지 않았다."], ["Silence reigns there; but from the bottom of the rock the River Lethe flows, and by its murmur invites to sleep.", "그곳에는 침묵이 지배했다. 다만 바위 밑바닥에서 레테 강이 흘렀고, 그 졸졸거림이 잠으로 이끌었다."], ["Poppies grow abundantly before the door of the cave, and other herbs, from whose juices Night collects slumbers, which she scatters over the darkened earth.", "동굴 문 앞에는 양귀비가 무성하게 자랐고, 또 다른 풀들도 자랐으니, 밤은 그것들의 즙에서 잠을 거두어 어두워진 대지 위에 흩뿌렸다."], ["There is no gate to the mansion, to creak on its hinges, nor any watchman; but in the midst a couch of black ebony, adorned with black plumes and black curtains.", "이 저택에는 돌쩌귀에서 삐걱대는 문도, 어떤 파수꾼도 없었다. 다만 한가운데에 검은 깃털과 검은 휘장으로 장식된 검은 흑단 침상이 놓여 있었다."], ["There the god reclines, his limbs relaxed with sleep.", "그곳에 신은 잠으로 사지를 늘어뜨린 채 기대어 있었다."], ["Around him lie dreams, resembling all various forms, as many as the harvest bears stalks, or the forest leaves, or the seashore sand grains.", "그의 주위에는 온갖 형상을 닮은 꿈들이 누워 있었는데, 그 수는 추수철의 곡식 줄기만큼이나, 숲의 나뭇잎만큼이나, 바닷가의 모래알만큼이나 많았다."]], [["As soon as the goddess entered and brushed away the dreams that hovered around her, her brightness lit up all the cave.", "여신이 들어서서 자기 주위를 떠도는 꿈들을 떨쳐 내자, 그 광채가 동굴 전체를 밝혔다. 신은 가까스로 눈을 뜨고, 이따금 턱을 가슴에 떨구다가, 마침내 제 몸을 흔들어 잠에서 벗어나, 팔을 괴고는 그녀의 용건을 물었다."], ["The god, scarce opening his eyes, and ever and anon dropping his beard upon his breast, at last shook himself free from himself, and leaning on his arm, inquired her errand,—for he knew who she was.", "그는 그녀가 누구인지 알았던 것이다. 그녀가 대답했다."], ["She answered, “Somnus, gentlest of the gods, tranquillizer of minds and soother of care-worn hearts, Juno sends you her commands that you despatch a dream to Halcyone, in the city of Trachine, representing her lost husband and all the events of the wreck.”", "\"솜누스여, 신들 가운데 가장 온화한 이, 마음을 가라앉히는 이, 근심에 지친 가슴을 달래는 이여, 헤라가 당신에게 명을 내리십니다. 트라키네 시의 할키오네에게 꿈을 보내어, 잃어버린 남편과 난파의 모든 일을 보여 주라고 하십니다.\""]], [["Having delivered her message, Iris hasted away, for she could not longer endure the stagnant air, and as she felt drowsiness creeping over her, she made her escape, and returned by her bow the way she came.", "전갈을 전하고 나서 이리스는 서둘러 떠났으니, 그 정체된 공기를 더는 견딜 수 없었던 까닭이다. 졸음이 자신을 덮쳐 오는 것을 느끼자, 그녀는 빠져나와 왔던 길을 따라 무지개를 타고 돌아갔다."], ["Then Somnus called one of his numerous sons,—Morpheus,—the most expert in counterfeiting forms, and in imitating the walk, the countenance, and mode of speaking, even the clothes and attitudes most characteristic of each.", "그러자 솜누스는 자신의 수많은 아들 가운데 하나인 모르페우스를 불렀다. 그는 형상을 흉내 내고, 걸음걸이와 표정과 말하는 방식, 심지어 각자에게 가장 특징적인 옷차림과 자세까지 모방하는 데 가장 능했다."], ["But he only imitates men, leaving it to another to personate birds, beasts, and serpents.", "그러나 그는 오직 사람만을 흉내 냈으며, 새와 짐승과 뱀을 흉내 내는 일은 다른 이에게 맡겼다."], ["Him they call Icelos; and Phantasos is a third, who turns himself into rocks, waters, woods, and other things without life.", "그를 이켈로스라 불렀고, 또 세 번째인 판타소스는 자신을 바위와 물과 숲, 그 밖의 생명 없는 것들로 바꾸었다."], ["These wait upon kings and great personages in their sleeping hours, while others move among the common people.", "이들은 왕과 위대한 인물들의 잠든 시간에 시중을 들었고, 다른 이들은 평민들 사이를 오갔다."], ["Somnus chose, from all the brothers, Morpheus, to perform the command of Iris; then laid his head on his pillow and yielded himself to grateful repose.", "솜누스는 형제들 가운데 모르페우스를 골라 이리스의 명을 수행하게 하고는, 베개에 머리를 누이고 달콤한 휴식에 몸을 맡겼다."]], [["Morpheus flew, making no noise with his wings, and soon came to the Hæmonian city, where, laying aside his wings, he assumed the form of Ceyx.", "모르페우스는 날개로 아무 소리도 내지 않고 날아, 이윽고 하이모니아의 도시에 이르렀다. 그곳에서 날개를 내려놓고 케익스의 모습을 취했다."], ["Under that form, but pale like a dead man, naked, he stood before the couch of the wretched wife.", "그 모습이되, 죽은 사람처럼 창백하고 벌거벗은 채로, 그는 가엾은 아내의 침상 앞에 섰다."], ["His beard seemed soaked with water, and water trickled from his drowned locks.", "그의 수염은 물에 흠뻑 젖은 듯했고, 물에 빠진 머리카락에서 물이 뚝뚝 떨어졌다."], ["Leaning over the bed, tears streaming from his eyes, he said, “Do you recognize your Ceyx, unhappy wife, or has death too much changed my visage?", "침대 위로 몸을 숙이고, 눈에서 눈물을 쏟으며 그가 말했다. \"불행한 아내여, 그대의 케익스를 알아보겠소, 아니면 죽음이 내 얼굴을 너무 많이 바꾸어 놓았소?"], ["Behold me, know me, your husband’s shade, instead of himself.", "나를 보시오, 나를 알아보시오. 그대 남편 그 자신이 아니라, 그의 망령이오."], ["Your prayers, Halcyone, availed me nothing.", "할키오네여, 그대의 기도는 내게 아무 소용이 없었소."], ["I am dead.", "나는 죽었소."], ["No more deceive yourself with vain hopes of my return.", "더 이상 내가 돌아오리라는 헛된 희망으로 그대 자신을 속이지 마시오."], ["The stormy winds sunk my ship in the Ægean Sea, waves filled my mouth while it called aloud on you.", "폭풍의 바람이 에게 해에서 내 배를 가라앉혔고, 내 입이 그대를 큰 소리로 부르는 동안 파도가 그 입을 채웠소."], ["No uncertain messenger tells you this, no vague rumor brings it to your ears.", "불확실한 전령이 그대에게 이를 알리는 것도 아니요, 막연한 소문이 그대의 귀에 전하는 것도 아니오."], ["I come in person, a shipwrecked man, to tell you my fate.", "난파당한 자인 내가 몸소 와서 내 운명을 그대에게 알리는 것이오."], ["Arise!", "일어나시오!"], ["give me tears, give me lamentations, let me not go down to Tartarus unwept.”", "내게 눈물을 주시오, 내게 애도를 주시오, 나로 하여금 애도받지 못한 채 타르타로스로 내려가지 않게 하시오.\""], ["To these words Morpheus added the voice, which seemed to be that of her husband; he seemed to pour forth genuine tears; his hands had the gestures of Ceyx.", "이 말에 모르페우스는 그녀 남편의 것인 듯한 목소리를 더했다. 그는 진짜 눈물을 쏟는 듯했고, 그의 손짓은 케익스의 것이었다."]], [["Halcyone, weeping, groaned, and stretched out her arms in her sleep, striving to embrace his body, but grasping only the air.", "할키오네는 흐느끼며 신음했고, 잠결에 팔을 뻗어 그의 몸을 끌어안으려 애썼으나, 붙잡은 것은 허공뿐이었다."], ["“Stay!”", "\"멈추세요!\""], ["she cried; “whither do you fly?", "그녀가 외쳤다. \"어디로 날아가시나요?"], ["let us go together.”", "함께 가요.\""], ["Her own voice awakened her.", "자신의 목소리가 그녀를 깨웠다."], ["Starting up, she gazed eagerly around, to see if he was still present, for the servants, alarmed by her cries, had brought a light.", "벌떡 일어나 그가 아직 거기 있는지 보려고 주위를 애타게 둘러보았으니, 그녀의 외침에 놀란 하인들이 등불을 가져온 참이었다."], ["When she found him not, she smote her breast and rent her garments.", "그를 찾을 수 없자 그녀는 가슴을 치고 옷을 찢었다."], ["She cares not to unbind her hair, but tears it wildly.", "머리를 풀 생각도 하지 않고 그것을 마구 쥐어뜯었다."], ["Her nurse asks what is the cause of her grief.", "유모가 슬픔의 까닭을 물었다. \"할키오네는 이제 없어요.\""], ["“Halcyone is no more,”", "그녀가 대답했다."], ["she answers, “she perished with her Ceyx.", "\"그녀는 자기 케익스와 함께 죽었어요."], ["Utter not words of comfort, he is shipwrecked and dead.", "위로의 말일랑 하지 마세요, 그는 난파되어 죽었어요."], ["I have seen him, I have recognized him.", "나는 그를 보았어요, 나는 그를 알아보았어요."], ["I stretched out my hands to seize him and detain him.", "그를 붙잡아 머물게 하려고 손을 뻗었어요."], ["His shade vanished, but it was the true shade of my husband.", "그의 망령은 사라졌지만, 그것은 내 남편의 참된 망령이었어요."], ["Not with the accustomed features, not with the beauty that was his, but pale, naked, and with his hair wet with sea-water, he appeared to wretched me.", "늘 보던 모습으로도, 그가 지녔던 아름다움으로도 아니라, 창백하고 벌거벗은 채, 바닷물에 젖은 머리로 가엾은 내 앞에 나타났어요."], ["Here, in this very spot, the sad vision stood,”—and she looked to find the mark of his footsteps.", "여기, 바로 이 자리에 그 슬픈 환영이 서 있었어요.\" 그러면서 그녀는 그의 발자국 흔적을 찾으려 살폈다."], ["“This it was, this that my presaging mind foreboded, when I implored him not to leave me, to trust himself to the waves.", "\"바로 이것이었어요, 내가 그에게 나를 떠나지 말라고, 파도에 자신을 맡기지 말라고 간청했을 때, 예감하는 내 마음이 미리 두려워했던 바로 그것 말이에요."], ["Oh, how I wish, since thou wouldst go, thou hadst taken me with thee!", "아, 정녕 가시려거든 나를 함께 데려가셨더라면 얼마나 좋았을까요!"], ["It would have been far better.", "그편이 훨씬 나았을 거예요."], ["Then I should have had no remnant of life to spend without thee, nor a separate death to die.", "그랬다면 나는 당신 없이 보낼 생명의 자투리도 남지 않았을 것이고, 따로 맞을 죽음도 없었을 거예요."], ["If I could bear to live and struggle to endure, I should be more cruel to myself than the sea has been to me.", "만일 내가 살아남아 견디려 애쓸 수 있다면, 나는 바다가 내게 그러했던 것보다 더 나 자신에게 잔인할 거예요."], ["But I will not struggle, I will not be separated from thee, unhappy husband.", "하지만 나는 애쓰지 않을래요, 나는 당신과 떨어지지 않을래요, 불행한 남편이여."], ["This time, at least, I will keep thee company.", "적어도 이번만은 내가 당신과 동행할게요."], ["In death, if one tomb may not include us, one epitaph shall; if I may not lay my ashes with thine, my name, at least, shall not be separated.”", "죽음에 이르러, 한 무덤이 우리를 함께 담을 수 없다면, 한 묘비명만은 그리할 거예요. 내 유골을 당신의 것과 함께 누일 수 없다 하더라도, 적어도 내 이름만은 떼어 놓이지 않을 거예요.\""], ["Her grief forbade more words, and these were broken with tears and sobs.", "슬픔이 더 이상의 말을 가로막았고, 이 말들마저 눈물과 흐느낌으로 끊어졌다."]], [["It was now morning.", "이제 아침이 되었다."], ["She went to the seashore, and sought the spot where she last saw him, on his departure.", "그녀는 바닷가로 가서, 그가 떠날 때 마지막으로 그를 보았던 자리를 찾았다."], ["“While he lingered here, and cast off his tacklings, he gave me his last kiss.”", "\"그가 여기서 머뭇거리며 닻줄을 풀 때, 그는 내게 마지막 입맞춤을 했지.\""], ["While she reviews every object, and strives to recall every incident, looking out over the sea, she descries an indistinct object floating in the water.", "그녀가 모든 사물을 되짚어 보며 모든 순간을 떠올리려 애쓰다가, 바다 너머를 내다보던 중, 물 위에 떠 있는 어렴풋한 물체를 발견했다."], ["At first she was in doubt what it was, but by degrees the waves bore it nearer, and it was plainly the body of a man.", "처음에는 그것이 무엇인지 알 수 없었으나, 점차 파도가 그것을 더 가까이 실어 왔고, 그것은 분명히 사람의 몸이었다."], ["Though unknowing of whom, yet, as it was of some shipwrecked one, she was deeply moved, and gave it her tears, saying, “Alas!", "누구인지는 몰랐으나, 어떤 난파당한 자의 것이었기에 그녀는 깊이 마음이 움직여 그것에 눈물을 바치며 말했다. \"아아!"], ["unhappy one, and unhappy, if such there be, thy wife!”", "불행한 이여, 그리고 만일 그런 이가 있다면, 불행한 것은 그대의 아내로다!\""], ["Borne by the waves, it came nearer.", "파도에 실려 그것은 점점 더 가까이 다가왔다."], ["As she more and more nearly views it, she trembles more and more.", "그녀가 점점 더 가까이 그것을 바라볼수록 그녀는 점점 더 떨었다."], ["Now, now it approaches the shore.", "이제, 이제 그것이 물가로 다가왔다."], ["Now marks that she recognizes appear.", "이제 그녀가 알아보는 표지들이 나타났다."], ["It is her husband!", "그것은 그녀의 남편이었다!"], ["Stretching out her trembling hands towards it, she exclaims, “O dearest husband, is it thus you return to me?”", "그것을 향해 떨리는 손을 뻗으며 그녀가 외쳤다. \"오 가장 사랑하는 남편이여, 이렇게 내게 돌아오시나요?\""]], [["There was built out from the shore a mole, constructed to break the assaults of the sea, and stem its violent ingress.", "물가에서 바다를 향해 방파제가 쌓여 있었으니, 바다의 공격을 막고 그 격렬한 침입을 막아 내려고 지은 것이었다."], ["She leaped upon this barrier and (it was wonderful she could do so) she flew, and striking the air with wings produced on the instant, skimmed along the surface of the water, an unhappy bird.", "그녀는 이 둑 위로 뛰어올랐고, 그러고는 (그녀가 그럴 수 있었다는 것이 놀라운 일이었다) 그녀는 날아올라, 그 순간 돋아난 날개로 공기를 치며, 가엾은 새가 되어 물 위를 스치듯 날았다."], ["As she flew, her throat poured forth sounds full of grief, and like the voice of one lamenting.", "날아가는 동안 그녀의 목구멍에서는 슬픔으로 가득한 소리, 애도하는 이의 목소리 같은 소리가 흘러나왔다."], ["When she touched the mute and bloodless body, she enfolded its beloved limbs with her new-formed wings, and tried to give kisses with her horny beak.", "그녀가 말없이 핏기 없는 그 몸에 닿았을 때, 그녀는 갓 돋아난 날개로 사랑하는 그 사지를 감쌌고, 뿔처럼 단단한 부리로 입맞춤을 하려 애썼다."], ["Whether Ceyx felt it, or whether it was only the action of the waves, those who looked on doubted, but the body seemed to raise its head.", "케익스가 그것을 느꼈는지, 아니면 그것이 단지 파도의 작용이었는지, 바라보던 이들은 의심했으나, 그 몸은 머리를 드는 듯했다."], ["But indeed he did feel it, and by the pitying gods both of them were changed into birds.", "그러나 그는 정말로 그것을 느꼈고, 가엾이 여긴 신들에 의해 두 사람 모두 새로 변했다."], ["They mate and have their young ones.", "그들은 짝을 짓고 새끼를 낳았다."], ["For seven placid days, in winter time, Halcyone broods over her nest, which floats upon the sea.", "겨울철, 이레 동안의 잔잔한 날에 할키오네는 바다 위에 떠 있는 둥지 위에서 알을 품는다."], ["Then the way is safe to seamen.", "그때면 뱃사람들에게 길이 안전하다."], ["Æolus guards the winds and keeps them from disturbing the deep.", "아이올로스는 바람을 지켜 깊은 바다를 어지럽히지 못하게 한다."], ["The sea is given up, for the time, to his grandchildren.", "그 동안 바다는 그의 손주들에게 내맡겨진다."]], [["The following lines from Byron’s “Bride of Abydos” might seem borrowed from the concluding part of this description, if it were not stated that the author derived the suggestion from observing the motion of a floating corpse:", "바이런의 「아비도스의 신부」에 나오는 다음 시구는, 작가가 떠다니는 시신의 움직임을 관찰하여 그 착상을 얻었다고 밝혀 두지 않았더라면, 이 묘사의 마무리 부분에서 빌려 온 것처럼 보일 법하다."]], [["“As shaken on his restless pillow, His head heaves with the heaving billow, That hand, whose motion is not life, Yet feebly seems to menace strife, Flung by the tossing tide on high, Then levelled with the wave . . .”", "\"쉴 줄 모르는 베개 위에서 흔들리듯, 그의 머리는 일렁이는 물결과 함께 일렁이고, 생명의 것이 아닌 그 손의 움직임은, 그래도 희미하게 싸움을 위협하는 듯, 출렁이는 조류에 높이 던져졌다가, 이윽고 물결과 같은 높이로 가라앉는다……\""]], [["Milton in his “Hymn on the Nativity,” thus alludes to the fable of the Halcyon:", "밀턴은 「그리스도 탄생의 찬가」에서 할키온의 이야기를 이렇게 암시한다."]], [["“But peaceful was the night Wherein the Prince of light His reign of peace upon the earth began; The winds with wonder whist Smoothly the waters kist Whispering new joys to the mild ocean, Who now hath quite forgot to rave While birds of calm sit brooding on the charmed wave.”", "\"그러나 평화로웠도다 그 밤은 빛의 왕자가 이 땅 위에 평화의 통치를 시작하셨던 밤은. 바람은 경이로움에 잠잠해지고 잔잔히 물결에 입맞추며 온화한 대양에 새로운 기쁨을 속삭였으니, 이제 그 바다는 사납게 날뛰기를 까맣게 잊었도다 고요의 새들이 마법에 걸린 물결 위에 앉아 알을 품는 동안에.\""]], [["Keats, also, in “Endymion,” says:", "키츠 또한 「엔디미온」에서 이렇게 말한다."]], [["“O magic sleep!", "\"오 마법 같은 잠이여!"], ["O comfortable bird That broodest o’er the troubled sea of the mind Till it is hushed and smooth.”", "오 위안을 주는 새여 어지러운 마음의 바다 위에서 알을 품는구나 그것이 잠잠해지고 잔잔해질 때까지.\""], ["————", "————"]], [["# VERTUMNUS AND POMONA", "# VERTUMNUS AND POMONA"]], [["THE Hamadryads were Wood-nymphs.", "하마드리아데스는 숲의 님프였다."], ["Pomona was of this class, and no one excelled her in love of the garden and the culture of fruit.", "포모나도 이 부류에 속했는데, 정원을 가꾸고 과실을 기르는 데 대한 애정에서는 누구도 그녀를 능가하지 못했다."], ["She cared not for forests and rivers, but loved the cultivated country, and trees that bear delicious apples.", "그녀는 숲이나 강에는 관심이 없었고, 사람이 일군 들판과 맛있는 사과가 열리는 나무를 사랑했다."], ["Her right hand bore for its weapon not a javelin, but a pruning-knife.", "그녀의 오른손에 들린 무기는 투창이 아니라 가지치기 칼이었다."], ["Armed with this, she busied herself at one time to repress the too luxuriant growths, and curtail the branches that straggled out of place; at another, to split the twig and insert therein a graft, making the branch adopt a nursling not its own.", "그 칼로 무장한 채 그녀는 어느 때에는 지나치게 무성하게 자란 가지를 솎아 내고, 제자리를 벗어나 제멋대로 뻗은 가지를 잘라 내느라 분주했으며, 또 어느 때에는 잔가지를 갈라 그 속에 접가지를 끼워 넣어 가지로 하여금 제 것이 아닌 어린 가지를 받아들이게 하였다."], ["She took care, too, that her favorites should not suffer from drought, and led streams of water by them, that the thirsty roots might drink.", "또한 그녀는 자기가 아끼는 나무들이 가뭄에 시달리지 않도록 정성을 기울였고, 목마른 뿌리가 물을 마실 수 있도록 그 곁으로 물줄기를 끌어 댔다."], ["This occupation was her pursuit, her passion; and she was free from that which Venus inspires.", "이 일이 그녀의 일과이자 열정이었다. 그리고 그녀는 아프로디테[로마의 〈베누스〉]가 불어넣는 정념으로부터 자유로웠다."], ["She was not without fear of the country people, and kept her orchard locked, and allowed not men to enter.", "그녀가 시골 사람들을 두려워하지 않은 것은 아니어서, 과수원에 자물쇠를 채우고 남자들이 들어오는 것을 허락하지 않았다."], ["The Fauns and Satyrs would have given all they possessed to win her, and so would old Sylvanus, who looks young for his years, and Pan, who wears a garland of pine leaves around his head.", "파우누스들과 사티로스들은 그녀의 마음을 얻기 위해 가진 것 전부라도 내놓았을 것이고, 나이에 비해 젊어 보이는 늙은 실바누스도, 머리에 소나무 잎으로 엮은 화관을 두른 판도 그랬을 것이다."], ["But Vertumnus loved her best of all; yet he sped no better than the rest.", "그러나 누구보다도 베르툼누스가 그녀를 깊이 사랑했다. 하지만 그도 다른 이들보다 나을 것이 없었다."], ["O how often, in the disguise of a reaper, did he bring her corn in a basket, and looked the very image of a reaper!", "오, 그는 얼마나 자주 추수꾼으로 변장하여 광주리에 곡식을 담아 그녀에게 가져갔던가. 그 모습은 영락없는 추수꾼이었다."], ["With a hay band tied round him, one would think he had just come from turning over the grass.", "건초 묶는 끈을 몸에 두른 그를 보면 누구라도 그가 방금 풀을 뒤집다 온 줄로 여겼을 것이다."], ["Sometimes he would have an ox-goad in his hand, and you would have said he had just unyoked his weary oxen.", "때로는 손에 소몰이 막대를 들고 있어, 사람들은 그가 지친 소들의 멍에를 막 벗긴 참이라고 말했을 것이다."], ["Now he bore a pruning-hook, and personated a vine-dresser; and again, with a ladder on his shoulder, he seemed as if he was going to gather apples.", "또 어떤 때에는 가지치기용 낫을 들고 포도밭 일꾼 행세를 했고, 다시 어깨에 사다리를 메고 사과를 따러 가는 듯 보였다."], ["Sometimes he trudged along as a discharged soldier, and again he bore a fishing-rod, as if going to fish.", "때로는 제대한 병사처럼 터덜터덜 걸었고, 또 어떤 때에는 낚시하러 가는 듯 낚싯대를 들었다."], ["In this way he gained admission to her again and again, and fed his passion with the sight of her.", "이런 방식으로 그는 거듭거듭 그녀에게 다가가는 것을 허락받았고, 그녀를 바라보는 것으로 자신의 정념을 달랬다."]], [["One day he came in the guise of an old woman, her gray hair surmounted with a cap, and a staff in her hand.", "어느 날 그는 노파의 모습으로 찾아왔다. 백발 위에 모자를 쓰고 손에는 지팡이를 들고 있었다."], ["She entered the garden and admired the fruit.", "그녀는 정원에 들어와 과실을 칭송했다."], ["“It does you credit, my dear,”", "\"참으로 자랑스러운 솜씨로구나, 얘야.\""], ["she said, and kissed her, not exactly with an old woman’s kiss.", "노파는 이렇게 말하며 그녀에게 입을 맞추었는데, 그것은 꼭 노파의 입맞춤이라고 할 수만은 없었다."], ["She sat down on a bank, and looked up at the branches laden with fruit which hung over her.", "노파는 둑에 걸터앉아 머리 위로 드리운, 과실이 주렁주렁 매달린 가지들을 올려다보았다."], ["Opposite was an elm entwined with a vine loaded with swelling grapes.", "맞은편에는 탱탱하게 익은 포도송이를 가득 단 포도 덩굴이 휘감긴 느릅나무가 서 있었다."], ["She praised the tree and its associated vine, equally.", "노파는 그 나무와 거기 어우러진 덩굴을 똑같이 칭찬했다. \"그렇지만,\""], ["“But,” said she, “if the tree stood alone, and had no vine clinging to it, it would have nothing to attract or offer us but its useless leaves.", "노파가 말했다, \"만일 저 나무가 홀로 서 있고 휘감긴 덩굴이 없었다면, 우리를 끌어당기거나 우리에게 베풀 것이라곤 쓸모없는 잎사귀밖에 없었을 게다."], ["And equally the vine, if it were not twined round the elm, would lie prostrate on the ground.", "마찬가지로 저 덩굴도 느릅나무를 휘감지 않았다면 땅바닥에 납작하게 누워 있었을 테고."], ["Why will you not take a lesson from the tree and the vine, and consent to unite yourself with some one?", "어찌하여 너는 저 나무와 덩굴에서 교훈을 얻어 누군가와 맺어지는 데 동의하지 않으려는 게냐? 나는 네가 그러기를 바란단다."], ["I wish you would. Helen herself had not more numerous suitors, nor Penelope, the wife of shrewd Ulysses.", "헬레네에게도 그토록 많은 구혼자가 없었고, 지혜로운 오디세우스의 아내 페넬로페에게도 그러지 못했지."], ["Even while you spurn them, they court you,—rural deities and others of every kind that frequent these mountains.", "네가 그들을 물리치는 동안에도, 그들은 너에게 구애하고 있단다. 이 산을 자주 찾는 들판의 신들이며 온갖 부류의 다른 이들이 말이다."], ["But if you are prudent and want to make a good alliance, and will let an old woman advise you,—who loves you better than you have any idea of,—dismiss all the rest and accept Vertumnus, on my recommendation.", "하지만 네가 사려 깊어 좋은 인연을 맺고자 한다면, 그리고 네가 짐작하는 것보다 훨씬 너를 사랑하는 이 늙은이의 충고를 받아들인다면, 다른 이들은 모두 물리치고 내 권유대로 베르툼누스를 받아들이려무나."], ["I know him as well as he knows himself.", "나는 그를 그 자신이 아는 만큼이나 잘 안단다."], ["He is not a wandering deity, but belongs to these mountains.", "그는 떠돌이 신이 아니라 이 산에 속한 신이지."], ["Nor is he like too many of the lovers nowadays, who love any one they happen to see; he loves you, and you only.", "또한 요즘의 너무나 많은 연인들처럼 눈에 띄는 누구든 사랑하는 그런 자가 아니란다. 그는 너를, 오직 너만을 사랑해."], ["Add to this, he is young and handsome, and has the art of assuming any shape he pleases, and can make himself just what you command him.", "게다가 그는 젊고 잘생겼으며, 마음먹은 대로 어떤 모습이든 취하는 재주가 있어서, 네가 명하는 그대로 제 모습을 바꿀 수 있단다."], ["Moreover, he loves the same things that you do, delights in gardening, and handles your apples with admiration.", "더욱이 그는 네가 좋아하는 것을 똑같이 좋아하고, 정원 가꾸기를 즐기며, 너의 사과를 감탄하며 다룬단다."], ["But now he cares nothing for fruits nor flowers, nor anything else, but only yourself.", "하지만 지금 그는 과실에도 꽃에도, 그 어떤 것에도 마음을 두지 않고 오직 너만을 생각하지."], ["Take pity on him, and fancy him speaking now with my mouth.", "그를 가엾이 여기고, 지금 그가 내 입을 빌려 말하고 있다고 여겨 보아라."], ["Remember that the gods punish cruelty, and that Venus hates a hard heart, and will visit such offences sooner or later.", "신들이 잔혹함을 벌하시고, 아프로디테가 모진 마음을 미워하여 그런 죄과를 머잖아 응징하신다는 것을 잊지 말거라."], ["To prove this, let me tell you a story, which is well known in Cyprus to be a fact; and I hope it will have the effect to make you more merciful.", "이를 증명하기 위해, 키프로스에서 실제 있었던 일로 널리 알려진 이야기를 하나 들려주마. 이 이야기가 너를 한결 자비롭게 만드는 효과를 내기를 바란단다."]], [["“Iphis was a young man of humble parentage, who saw and loved Anaxarete, a noble lady of the ancient family of Teucer.", "이피스는 미천한 가문에서 태어난 젊은이로, 테우크로스의 오랜 가문에 속한 고귀한 여인 아낙사레테를 보고 사랑하게 되었단다."], ["He struggled long with his passion, but when he found he could not subdue it, he came a suppliant to her mansion.", "그는 오래도록 자신의 정념과 싸웠지만, 도저히 그것을 억누를 수 없음을 알자 그녀의 저택으로 탄원하러 갔지."], ["First he told his passion to her nurse, and begged her as she loved her foster-child to favor his suit.", "처음에 그는 그녀의 유모에게 자신의 사랑을 털어놓고, 길러 낸 아이를 사랑하는 마음으로 자기 청을 도와 달라고 애원했단다."], ["And then he tried to win her domestics to his side.", "그다음에는 그녀의 하인들을 제 편으로 끌어들이려 했지."], ["Sometimes he committed his vows to written tablets, and often hung at her door garlands which he had moistened with his tears.", "때로는 글을 적은 서판에 자기 맹세를 새겼고, 자주 그녀의 문에 눈물로 적신 화관을 걸어 두었단다."], ["He stretched himself on her threshold, and uttered his complaints to the cruel bolts and bars.", "그녀의 문지방에 몸을 뉘고는, 비정한 빗장과 자물쇠를 향해 자신의 한탄을 쏟아 냈지."], ["She was deafer than the surges which rise in the November gale; harder than steel from the German forges, or a rock that still clings to its native cliff.", "그녀는 11월의 사나운 바람에 일어나는 파도보다도 귀가 먹었고, 게르만의 대장간에서 나온 강철보다도, 제가 솟은 절벽에 여전히 들러붙어 있는 바위보다도 단단했단다."], ["She mocked and laughed at him, adding cruel words to her ungentle treatment, and gave not the slightest gleam of hope.", "그녀는 그를 비웃고 조롱하며, 그 박정한 처사에 잔인한 말까지 덧붙였고, 한 줄기 희망의 빛조차 비춰 주지 않았지."]], [["“Iphis could not any longer endure the torments of hopeless love, and, standing before her doors, he spake these last words: ‘Anaxarete, you have conquered, and shall no longer have to bear my importunities.", "이피스는 더 이상 가망 없는 사랑의 고통을 견딜 수 없어, 그녀의 문 앞에 서서 이 마지막 말을 내뱉었단다. '아낙사레테여, 그대가 이겼소."], ["Enjoy your triumph!", "이제 더는 나의 성가신 졸음을 견딜 필요가 없으리다."], ["Sing songs of joy, and bind your forehead with laurel,—you have conquered!", "그대의 승리를 누리시오! 환희의 노래를 부르고, 이마에 월계관을 두르시오."], ["I die; stony heart, rejoice!", "그대가 이겼으니! 나는 죽소."], ["This at least I can do to gratify you and force you to praise me; and thus shall I prove that the love of you left me but with life.", "돌처럼 굳은 마음이여, 기뻐하시오! 적어도 이것만큼은 그대를 흡족하게 하고 그대로 하여금 나를 칭송하게 만들 수 있으리다."], ["Nor will I leave it to rumor to tell you of my death.", "또한 이로써 그대를 향한 사랑이 오직 목숨과 함께 나를 떠났음을 증명하리다."], ["I will come myself, and you shall see me die, and feast your eyes on the spectacle.", "게다가 내 죽음을 그대에게 전하는 일을 소문에 맡기지 않겠소. 내가 몸소 와서, 그대가 내 죽는 모습을 보게 하고, 그 광경으로 그대의 눈을 즐겁게 하리다."], ["Yet, O ye gods, who look down on mortal woes, observe my fate!", "그러나, 오, 인간의 비탄을 굽어보시는 신들이여, 나의 운명을 살피소서! 나는 다만 이것만을 청하나이다."], ["I ask but this: let me be remembered in coming ages, and add those years to my fame which you have reft from my life.’ Thus he said, and, turning his pale face and weeping eyes towards her mansion, he fastened a rope to the gatepost, on which he had often hung garlands, and putting his head into the noose, he murmured, ‘This garland at least will please you, cruel girl!’ and falling hung suspended with his neck broken.", "다가오는 세월에 내가 기억되게 하시고, 그대들이 내 목숨에서 앗아 간 그 햇수를 내 명성에 보태 주소서.' 그는 이렇게 말한 뒤, 창백한 얼굴과 눈물 어린 눈을 그녀의 저택 쪽으로 돌리고, 자기가 자주 화관을 걸어 두던 문기둥에 밧줄을 매고는, 그 올가미 안에 머리를 넣으며 중얼거렸지."], ["As he fell he struck against the gate, and the sound was as the sound of a groan.", "'이 화관만큼은 그대를 기쁘게 하리다, 잔인한 처녀여!'"], ["The servants opened the door and found him dead, and with exclamations of pity raised him and carried him home to his mother, for his father was not living.", "그러고는 떨어지며 목이 부러진 채 매달렸단다. 떨어지면서 그는 문에 부딪혔고, 그 소리는 신음 소리와도 같았지."], ["She received the dead body of her son, and folded the cold form to her bosom, while she poured forth the sad words which bereaved mothers utter.", "하인들이 문을 열어 그가 죽은 것을 발견하고는, 연민의 탄식을 내뱉으며 그를 들어 올려 어머니에게 데려갔단다. 그의 아버지는 살아 있지 않았기 때문이지."], ["The mournful funeral passed through the town, and the pale corpse was borne on a bier to the place of the funeral pile.", "어머니는 아들의 주검을 받아 들고, 그 차가운 몸을 가슴에 끌어안으며, 자식을 잃은 어머니가 토해 내는 슬픈 말들을 쏟아 냈단다."], ["By chance the home of Anaxarete was on the street where the procession passed, and the lamentations of the mourners met the ears of her whom the avenging deity had already marked for punishment.", "비통한 장례 행렬이 마을을 지나갔고, 창백한 시신은 상여에 실려 화장터로 옮겨졌지. 우연히도 아낙사레테의 집은 그 행렬이 지나가는 거리에 있었고, 조문객들의 통곡이 이미 복수의 신에게 벌받을 자로 점지된 그녀의 귀에 닿았단다."]], [["“‘Let us see this sad procession,’ said she, and mounted to a turret, whence through an open window she looked upon the funeral.", "'이 슬픈 행렬을 좀 보아야겠다.' 그녀는 이렇게 말하며 작은 탑으로 올라가, 열린 창을 통해 장례식을 내려다보았지."], ["Scarce had her eyes rested upon the form of Iphis stretched on the bier, when they began to stiffen, and the warm blood in her body to become cold.", "상여에 누인 이피스의 모습에 그녀의 눈길이 닿자마자, 그 눈이 굳어지기 시작했고, 몸속의 따뜻한 피가 차갑게 식어 가기 시작했단다."], ["Endeavoring to step back, she found she could not move her feet; trying to turn away her face, she tried in vain; and by degrees all her limbs became stony like her heart.", "뒤로 물러서려 했으나 발을 움직일 수 없음을 알았고, 얼굴을 돌리려 했으나 헛수고였으며, 차츰 그녀의 사지가 죄다 그 마음처럼 돌이 되어 갔지."], ["That you may not doubt the fact, the statue still remains, and stands in the temple of Venus at Salamis, in the exact form of the lady.", "네가 이 일을 의심하지 않도록 말해 두자면, 그 조각상은 지금도 살라미스의 아프로디테 신전에 그 여인의 모습 그대로 서 있단다."], ["Now think of these things, my dear, and lay aside your scorn and your delays, and accept a lover.", "그러니 이제 이런 것들을 헤아려 보거라, 얘야. 그리고 너의 멸시와 미적거림을 내려놓고 연인을 받아들이려무나."], ["So may neither the vernal frosts blight your young fruits, nor furious winds scatter your blossoms!”", "그리하면 봄철의 서리도 네 어린 과실을 시들게 하지 못할 것이며, 사나운 바람도 네 꽃들을 흩날리지 못하리라!\""]], [["When Vertumnus had spoken thus, he dropped the disguise of an old woman, and stood before her in his proper person, as a comely youth.", "베르툼누스는 이렇게 말하고 나서 노파의 변장을 벗어 던지고, 잘생긴 젊은이라는 제 본모습으로 그녀 앞에 섰다."], ["It appeared to her like the sun bursting through a cloud.", "그녀에게는 그것이 마치 구름을 뚫고 솟아오르는 태양 같아 보였다."], ["He would have renewed his entreaties, but there was no need; his arguments and the sight of his true form prevailed, and the Nymph no longer resisted, but owned a mutual flame.", "그는 다시 간청을 거듭하려 했으나 그럴 필요가 없었다. 그의 논변과 그 참모습의 광경이 효력을 발휘했고, 님프는 더 이상 저항하지 않고 서로 같은 불길에 휩싸였음을 인정했다."]], [["Pomona was the especial patroness of the Apple-orchard, and as such she was invoked by Phillips, the author of a poem on Cider, in blank verse.", "포모나는 사과 과수원의 각별한 수호신이었으므로, 무운시로 사과술에 관한 시를 지은 시인 필립스가 그녀를 불러 기렸다."], ["Thomson in the “Seasons” alludes to him:", "톰슨은 「사계절」에서 그를 이렇게 암시한다."]], [["“Phillips, Pomona’s bard, the second thou Who nobly durst, in rhyme-unfettered verse, With British freedom, sing the British song.”", "\"포모나의 시인 필립스여, 그대는 두 번째로 각운에 얽매이지 않은 시구로, 브리튼의 자유로써 브리튼의 노래를 부르기를 고결하게 감행한 이로다.\""]], [["But Pomona was also regarded as presiding over other fruits, and as such is invoked by Thomson:", "그러나 포모나는 다른 과실들도 주관하는 여신으로 여겨졌으니, 그러한 자격으로 톰슨이 그녀를 불러 기린다."]], [["“Bear me, Pomona, to thy citron groves, To where the lemon and the piercing lime, With the deep orange, glowing through the green, Their lighter glories blend.", "\"포모나여, 나를 그대의 시트론 숲으로 데려가 주오, 레몬과 알싸한 라임이, 초록을 뚫고 타오르는 짙은 오렌지와 더불어, 그 한결 가벼운 영광을 어우러 놓은 그곳으로."], ["Lay me reclined Beneath the spreading tamarind, that shakes, Fanned by the breeze, its fever-cooling fruit.”", "나를 비스듬히 누이소서 널리 가지를 뻗은 타마린드 아래에, 산들바람에 흔들려 열병을 식히는 그 열매를 떨구는 나무 아래에.\""], ["————", "————"]], [["# CUPID AND PSYCHE", "# CUPID AND PSYCHE"]], [["A CERTAIN king and queen had three daughters.", "어떤 왕과 왕비에게 세 딸이 있었다."], ["The charms of the two elder were more than common, but the beauty of the youngest was so wonderful that the poverty of language is unable to express its due praise.", "위의 두 딸도 보통 이상으로 아름다웠지만, 막내딸의 미모는 너무도 경이로워서 빈약한 언어로는 그에 합당한 찬사를 표현할 길이 없었다."], ["The fame of her beauty was so great that strangers from neighboring countries came in crowds to enjoy the sight, and looked on her with amazement, paying her that homage which is due only to Venus herself.", "그녀의 미모에 관한 명성이 얼마나 컸던지 이웃 나라에서 온 낯선 이들이 그 광경을 즐기려고 무리 지어 몰려와 경탄하며 그녀를 바라보았고, 오로지 아프로디테[로마의 〈베누스〉]에게만 바쳐야 마땅한 경배를 그녀에게 바쳤다."], ["In fact Venus found her altars deserted, while men turned their devotion to this young virgin.", "실제로 아프로디테는 자기 제단이 버려진 것을 알게 되었으니, 사람들이 이 젊은 처녀에게 정성을 돌린 까닭이었다."], ["As she passed along, the people sang her praises, and strewed her way with chaplets and flowers.", "그녀가 지나갈 때면 사람들은 그녀를 찬미하는 노래를 부르고, 그녀가 가는 길에 화관과 꽃을 흩뿌렸다."]], [["This perversion of homage due only to the immortal powers to the exaltation of a mortal gave great offence to the real Venus.", "오로지 불멸의 권능에게만 마땅한 경배를 한낱 필멸자를 떠받드는 데로 그릇되이 돌린 이 일은 진짜 아프로디테의 큰 노여움을 샀다."], ["Shaking her ambrosial locks with indignation, she exclaimed, “Am I then to be eclipsed in my honors by a mortal girl?", "그녀는 분개하여 신들의 향기로운 머리채를 흔들며 외쳤다. \"그렇다면 한낱 필멸의 계집아이에게 내 영예가 가려져야 한단 말이냐?"], ["In vain then did that royal shepherd, whose judgment was approved by Jove himself, give me the palm of beauty over my illustrious rivals, Pallas and Juno.", "제우스께서 친히 그 판단을 인정하신 저 왕가의 목자가 나의 빛나는 연적인 팔라스[아테나]와 헤라[로마의 〈유노〉]를 제치고 나에게 미의 영예를 안겨 준 것이 부질없는 일이었더란 말이냐."], ["But she shall not so quietly usurp my honors.", "하지만 저 계집아이가 그토록 조용히 내 영예를 가로채도록 두지는 않으리라."], ["I will give her cause to repent of so unlawful a beauty.”", "저 부당한 미모를 후회하도록 만들어 주겠다.\""]], [["Thereupon she calls her winged son Cupid, mischievous enough in his own nature, and rouses and provokes him yet more by her complaints. She points out Psyche to him and says, “My dear son, punish that contumacious beauty; give thy mother a revenge as sweet as her injuries are great; infuse into the bosom of that haughty girl a passion for some low, mean, unworthy being, so that she may reap a mortification as great as her present exultation and triumph.”", "그러고 나서 그녀는 날개 달린 아들 에로스[로마의 〈쿠피도〉]를 불렀으니, 그는 본디 천성부터 짓궂은 데다, 그녀의 하소연이 그를 더욱 부추기고 자극했다. 그녀는 그에게 프시케를 가리켜 보이며 말했다. \"사랑하는 내 아들아, 저 오만한 미모를 벌하여라. 네 어미가 입은 모욕이 큰 만큼 그에 못지않게 달콤한 복수를 안겨 다오. 저 거만한 계집아이의 가슴에 어떤 비천하고 미천하며 보잘것없는 자에 대한 정념을 불어넣어, 지금 누리는 우쭐함과 승리만큼이나 큰 굴욕을 거두게 하여라.\""]], [["Cupid prepared to obey the commands of his mother.", "에로스는 어머니의 분부를 따를 채비를 했다."], ["There are two fountains in Venus’s garden, one of sweet waters, the other of bitter.", "아프로디테의 정원에는 두 개의 샘이 있는데, 하나는 단물의 샘이고 다른 하나는 쓴물의 샘이었다."], ["Cupid filled two amber vases, one from each fountain, and suspending them from the top of his quiver, hastened to the chamber of Psyche, whom he found asleep.", "에로스는 호박으로 만든 병 두 개에 각각 한 샘에서 물을 채워 자기 화살통 꼭대기에 매달고, 프시케의 침실로 서둘러 갔는데, 가 보니 그녀는 잠들어 있었다."], ["He shed a few drops from the bitter fountain over her lips, though the sight of her almost moved him to pity; then touched her side with the point of his arrow.", "그는 쓴물 샘의 물을 몇 방울 그녀의 입술 위에 떨어뜨렸으나, 그녀를 보고 있노라니 거의 연민이 일어날 지경이었다. 그러고는 화살촉으로 그녀의 옆구리를 건드렸다."], ["At the touch she awoke, and opened eyes upon Cupid (himself invisible), which so startled him that in his confusion he wounded himself with his own arrow.", "그 닿음에 그녀가 깨어나 (그 자신은 보이지 않는) 에로스에게로 눈을 떴고, 이에 그는 어찌나 놀랐던지 당황한 나머지 제 화살에 스스로 상처를 입고 말았다."], ["Heedless of his wound, his whole thought now was to repair the mischief he had done, and he poured the balmy drops of joy over all her silken ringlets.", "그러나 그는 제 상처에는 아랑곳하지 않고, 오로지 자기가 저지른 해악을 바로잡을 생각뿐이어서, 그녀의 비단결 같은 곱슬머리 위에 온통 기쁨의 향기로운 물방울을 부었다."]], [["Psyche, henceforth frowned upon by Venus, derived no benefit from all her charms.", "이후로 아프로디테의 눈 밖에 난 프시케는 자신의 온갖 미모로부터 아무런 덕도 보지 못했다."], ["True, all eyes were cast eagerly upon her, and every mouth spoke her praises; but neither king, royal youth, nor plebeian presented himself to demand her in marriage.", "과연 모든 눈이 그녀를 열렬히 바라보았고, 모든 입이 그녀를 찬미했으나, 왕도 왕자도 평민도 그녀에게 청혼하려고 나서는 이가 없었다."], ["Her two elder sisters of moderate charms had now long been married to two royal princes; but Psyche, in her lonely apartment, deplored her solitude, sick of that beauty which, while it procured abundance of flattery, had failed to awaken love.", "보통 정도의 미모를 지닌 두 언니는 이미 오래전에 두 왕자와 혼인했으나, 프시케는 외로운 처소에서 자신의 고독을 한탄하며, 넘치는 아첨은 안겨 주었으되 사랑은 깨우지 못한 그 미모에 진절머리를 냈다."]], [["Her parents, afraid that they had unwittingly incurred the anger of the gods, consulted the oracle of Apollo, and received this answer: “The virgin is destined for the bride of no mortal lover.", "그녀의 부모는 자신들이 모르는 사이에 신들의 노여움을 산 것이 아닌가 두려워하여 아폴론의 신탁에 물었고, 이런 답을 받았다. \"그 처녀는 어떤 필멸의 연인의 신부가 될 운명이 아니로다."], ["Her future husband awaits her on the top of the mountain.", "그녀의 장래 남편이 산꼭대기에서 그녀를 기다리고 있느니라."], ["He is a monster whom neither gods nor men can resist.”", "그는 신도 인간도 대적할 수 없는 괴물이로다.\""]], [["This dreadful decree of the oracle filled all the people with dismay, and her parents abandoned themselves to grief.", "이 무시무시한 신탁의 선고는 온 백성을 경악에 빠뜨렸고, 그녀의 부모는 비탄에 몸을 내맡겼다. 그러나 프시케가 말했다."], ["But Psyche said, “Why, my dear parents, do you now lament me?", "\"사랑하는 부모님, 어찌하여 이제 와 저를 두고 슬퍼하십니까?"], ["You should rather have grieved when the people showered upon me undeserved honors, and with one voice called me a Venus.", "차라리 백성들이 제게 분에 넘치는 영예를 쏟아붓고 한목소리로 저를 아프로디테라 불렀을 때 슬퍼하셨어야지요."], ["I now perceive that I am a victim to that name.", "이제야 제가 그 이름의 희생양임을 깨닫습니다. 저는 따르겠습니다."], ["I submit. Lead me to that rock to which my unhappy fate has destined me.”", "제 불행한 운명이 저를 정해 놓은 그 바위로 저를 이끌어 주소서.\""], ["Accordingly, all things being prepared, the royal maid took her place in the procession, which more resembled a funeral than a nuptial pomp, and with her parents, amid the lamentations of the people, ascended the mountain, on the summit of which they left her alone, and with sorrowful hearts returned home.", "그리하여 만반의 준비가 갖추어지자, 왕가의 처녀는 혼례의 행렬이라기보다 차라리 장례 행렬에 가까운 그 대열에서 자기 자리를 잡았고, 부모와 함께 백성의 통곡 속에서 산을 올랐으며, 그 산꼭대기에서 사람들은 그녀를 홀로 남겨 두고 슬픈 마음으로 집으로 돌아갔다."]], [["While Psyche stood on the ridge of the mountain, panting with fear and with eyes full of tears, the gentle Zephyr raised her from the earth and bore her with an easy motion into a flowery dale.", "프시케가 두려움에 헐떡이며 눈에는 눈물이 가득한 채 산마루에 서 있을 때, 부드러운 서풍 제피로스가 그녀를 땅에서 들어 올려 편안한 움직임으로 꽃이 만발한 골짜기로 실어 갔다."], ["By degrees her mind became composed, and she laid herself down on the grassy bank to sleep.", "차츰 그녀의 마음은 가라앉았고, 그녀는 풀이 우거진 둑에 몸을 누이고 잠을 청했다."], ["When she awoke refreshed with sleep, she looked round and beheld near by a pleasant grove of tall and stately trees.", "잠으로 기운을 되찾고 깨어났을 때, 그녀는 주위를 둘러보다가 가까이에 크고 위엄 있는 나무들이 늘어선 쾌적한 숲을 보았다."], ["She entered it, and in the midst discovered a fountain, sending forth clear and crystal waters, and fast by, a magnificent palace whose august front impressed the spectator that it was not the work of mortal hands, but the happy retreat of some god.", "그녀가 그 안으로 들어가자, 한가운데에 맑고 수정 같은 물을 뿜어내는 샘이 있었고, 그 바로 곁에는 장려한 궁전이 있었으니, 그 위엄 있는 정면은 보는 이로 하여금 그것이 필멸의 손으로 빚은 것이 아니라 어느 신의 행복한 안식처라는 인상을 주었다."], ["Drawn by admiration and wonder, she approached the building and ventured to enter.", "경탄과 경이에 이끌려 그녀는 그 건물에 다가가 용기를 내어 안으로 들어갔다."], ["Every object she met filled her with pleasure and amazement.", "마주치는 모든 것이 그녀를 기쁨과 경탄으로 채웠다."], ["Golden pillars supported the vaulted roof, and the walls were enriched with carvings and paintings representing beasts of the chase and rural scenes, adapted to delight the eye of the beholder.", "황금 기둥들이 둥근 천장을 떠받치고 있었고, 벽들은 사냥감인 짐승들과 전원 풍경을 담은 조각과 회화로 장식되어 보는 이의 눈을 즐겁게 하기에 알맞았다."], ["Proceeding onward, she perceived that besides the apartments of state there were others filled with all manner of treasures, and beautiful and precious productions of nature and art.", "더 안으로 나아가니, 그녀는 의례를 위한 방들 외에도 온갖 종류의 보물과, 자연과 기예가 빚어낸 아름답고 진귀한 산물로 가득한 다른 방들이 있음을 알아차렸다."]], [["While her eyes were thus occupied, a voice addressed her, though she saw no one, uttering these words: “Sovereign lady, all that you see is yours.", "그녀의 눈이 이렇게 사로잡혀 있을 때, 비록 아무도 보이지 않았으나 어떤 목소리가 그녀에게 이런 말을 건넸다. \"여왕이신 마님, 보시는 모든 것이 마님의 것입니다."], ["We whose voices you hear are your servants and shall obey all your commands with our utmost care and diligence.", "마님께서 그 목소리를 듣고 계신 저희는 마님의 종이며, 온갖 정성과 부지런함으로 마님의 모든 분부를 받들겠나이다."], ["Retire, therefore, to your chamber and repose on your bed of down, and when you see fit repair to the bath.", "그러니 침실로 물러가 솜털 침상에서 쉬시고, 적당하다 여기실 때 목욕탕으로 가소서."], ["Supper awaits you in the adjoining alcove when it pleases you to take your seat there.”", "곁방에는 마님께서 자리에 드시기를 즐거이 원하실 때 저녁 식사가 기다리고 있나이다.\""]], [["Psyche gave ear to the admonitions of her vocal attendants, and after repose and the refreshment of the bath, seated herself in the alcove, where a table immediately presented itself, without any visible aid from waiters or servants, and covered with the greatest delicacies of food and the most nectareous wines.", "프시케는 목소리뿐인 시종들의 권유에 귀를 기울였고, 휴식을 취하고 목욕으로 기운을 차린 뒤 곁방에 자리를 잡으니, 곧바로 시중드는 자나 종의 손길이 보이지도 않는데 식탁이 눈앞에 차려졌으며, 거기에는 더없이 진귀한 음식과 더없이 감미로운 술이 가득했다."], ["Her ears too were feasted with music from invisible performers; of whom one sang, another played on the lute, and all closed in the wonderful harmony of a full chorus.", "그녀의 귀 또한 보이지 않는 연주자들의 음악으로 즐거움을 누렸으니, 그중 하나는 노래하고 다른 하나는 류트를 연주했으며, 모두가 어우러져 충만한 합창의 경이로운 조화로 끝맺었다."]], [["She had not yet seen her destined husband.", "그녀는 정해진 남편을 아직 보지 못했다."], ["He came only in the hours of darkness and fled before the dawn of morning, but his accents were full of love, and inspired a like passion in her.", "그는 오로지 어둠의 시간에만 찾아왔다가 아침의 동이 트기 전에 사라졌으나, 그의 말투는 사랑으로 가득하여 그녀에게도 그와 같은 정념을 불어넣었다."], ["She often begged him to stay and let her behold him, but he would not consent.", "그녀는 자주 그에게 머물러 자기에게 모습을 보여 달라고 간청했으나, 그는 동의하려 하지 않았다."], ["On the contrary he charged her to make no attempt to see him, for it was his pleasure, for the best of reasons, to keep concealed.", "오히려 그는 자기를 보려는 어떠한 시도도 하지 말라고 당부했으니, 더없이 마땅한 이유로 모습을 감추는 것이 그의 뜻이었던 까닭이다."], ["“Why should you wish to behold me?”", "\"어찌하여 그대는 나를 보고 싶어 하오?\""], ["he said; “have you any doubt of my love?", "그가 말했다. \"그대는 내 사랑을 의심하오?"], ["have you any wish ungratified?", "채워지지 않은 소망이라도 있소?"], ["If you saw me, perhaps you would fear me, perhaps adore me, but all I ask of you is to love me.", "만일 나를 본다면, 그대는 어쩌면 나를 두려워하거나, 어쩌면 숭배할지도 모르오. 그러나 내가 그대에게 바라는 모든 것은 나를 사랑해 달라는 것뿐이오."], ["I would rather you would love me as an equal than adore me as a god.”", "나는 그대가 나를 신으로 숭배하기보다 대등한 자로서 사랑해 주기를 바라오.\""]], [["This reasoning somewhat quieted Psyche for a time, and while the novelty lasted she felt quite happy.", "이 논리는 한동안 프시케를 어느 정도 진정시켰고, 그 새로움이 지속되는 동안 그녀는 꽤 행복했다."], ["But at length the thought of her parents, left in ignorance of her fate, and of her sisters, precluded from sharing with her the delights of her situation, preyed on her mind and made her begin to feel her palace as but a splendid prison.", "그러나 마침내 자신의 운명을 모른 채 남겨진 부모 생각과, 자기 처지의 기쁨을 함께 나누지 못하는 언니들 생각이 그녀의 마음을 갉아먹어, 그녀는 자신의 궁전을 한낱 화려한 감옥으로 느끼기 시작했다."], ["When her husband came one night, she told him her distress, and at last drew from him an unwilling consent that her sisters should be brought to see her.", "어느 밤 남편이 찾아왔을 때, 그녀는 그에게 자신의 괴로움을 털어놓았고, 마침내 언니들을 데려와 자기를 보게 해도 좋다는 마지못한 허락을 그에게서 끌어냈다."]], [["So, calling Zephyr, she acquainted him with her husband’s commands, and he, promptly obedient, soon brought them across the mountain down to their sister’s valley.", "그리하여 그녀는 제피로스를 불러 남편의 분부를 알렸고, 그는 즉시 순순히 따라 곧 언니들을 산 너머 동생의 골짜기로 데려왔다."], ["They embraced her and she returned their caresses.", "그들은 그녀를 끌어안았고 그녀도 그들의 포옹에 응했다."], ["“Come,”", "\"자,\""], ["said Psyche, “enter with me my house and refresh yourselves with whatever your sister has to offer.”", "프시케가 말했다. \"저와 함께 제 집으로 들어가셔서 동생이 내어 드릴 수 있는 무엇으로든 기운을 차리세요.\""], ["Then taking their hands she led them into her golden palace, and committed them to the care of her numerous train of attendant voices, to refresh them in her baths and at her table, and to show them all her treasures.", "그러고는 그들의 손을 잡고 자신의 황금 궁전으로 이끌었으며, 수많은 목소리 시종 무리에게 맡겨 목욕탕과 식탁에서 그들의 기운을 돋우고 자신의 온갖 보물을 보여 주도록 했다."], ["The view of these celestial delights caused envy to enter their bosoms, at seeing their young sister possessed of such state and splendor, so much exceeding their own.", "이 천상의 즐거움을 보자, 어린 동생이 자기들 것을 훨씬 능가하는 그런 위세와 화려함을 누리는 것을 보고 그들의 가슴에는 시샘이 일어났다."]], [["They asked her numberless questions, among others what sort of a person her husband was.", "그들은 그녀에게 수없이 많은 질문을 던졌는데, 그중에는 그녀의 남편이 어떤 사람이냐는 물음도 있었다."], ["Psyche replied that he was a beautiful youth, who generally spent the daytime in hunting upon the mountains.", "프시케는 그가 아름다운 젊은이로, 대개 낮에는 산에서 사냥을 하며 보낸다고 대답했다."], ["The sisters, not satisfied with this reply, soon made her confess that she had never seen him.", "언니들은 이 대답에 만족하지 않고, 곧 그녀가 그를 한 번도 본 적이 없음을 실토하게 만들었다."], ["Then they proceeded to fill her bosom with dark suspicions.", "그러고는 그녀의 가슴을 어두운 의심으로 채우기 시작했다."], ["“Call to mind,”", "\"기억해 봐,\""], ["they said, “the Pythian oracle that declared you destined to marry a direful and tremendous monster.", "그들이 말했다. \"네가 무섭고 끔찍한 괴물과 혼인할 운명이라고 선언한 피티아의 신탁을 말이야."], ["The inhabitants of this valley say that your husband is a terrible and monstrous serpent, who nourishes you for a while with dainties that he may by and by devour you.", "이 골짜기 사람들이 하는 말로는 네 남편이 무섭고 괴물 같은 뱀이라더구나. 그자가 한동안 너를 진미로 먹여 살리는 것은 머지않아 너를 잡아먹으려는 것이라더라."], ["Take our advice.", "우리 충고를 들어."], ["Provide yourself with a lamp and a sharp knife; put them in concealment that your husband may not discover them, and when he is sound asleep, slip out of bed, bring forth your lamp, and see for yourself whether what they say is true or not.", "등불 하나와 날카로운 칼 한 자루를 마련해서, 네 남편이 찾아내지 못하도록 감추어 두었다가, 그가 깊이 잠들거든 침대에서 살그머니 빠져나와 등불을 가져와서, 그들이 하는 말이 사실인지 아닌지 네 눈으로 직접 보아라."], ["If it is, hesitate not to cut off the monster’s head, and thereby recover your liberty.”", "만일 사실이거든, 망설이지 말고 그 괴물의 머리를 베어 그로써 네 자유를 되찾아라.\""]], [["Psyche resisted these persuasions as well as she could, but they did not fail to have their effect on her mind, and when her sisters were gone, their words and her own curiosity were too strong for her to resist.", "프시케는 이 설득에 할 수 있는 한 맞섰으나, 그것이 그녀의 마음에 영향을 미치지 않을 리 없었고, 언니들이 떠나자 그들의 말과 그녀 자신의 호기심이 너무도 강하여 그녀로서는 도저히 거스를 수 없었다."], ["So she prepared her lamp and a sharp knife, and hid them out of sight of her husband.", "그리하여 그녀는 등불과 날카로운 칼을 마련해 남편의 눈에 띄지 않게 숨겨 두었다."], ["When he had fallen into his first sleep, she silently rose and uncovering her lamp beheld not a hideous monster, but the most beautiful and charming of the gods, with his golden ringlets wandering over his snowy neck and crimson cheek, with two dewy wings on his shoulders, whiter than snow, and with shining feathers like the tender blossoms of spring.", "그가 첫잠에 빠져들자, 그녀는 조용히 일어나 등불의 덮개를 벗겼고, 흉측한 괴물이 아니라 신들 가운데 가장 아름답고 매혹적인 이를 보았으니, 그의 황금빛 곱슬머리는 눈처럼 흰 목과 진홍빛 뺨 위로 흘러내렸고, 어깨에는 눈보다 흰 이슬 맺힌 두 날개가 봄의 여린 꽃송이 같은 빛나는 깃털과 더불어 달려 있었다."], ["As she leaned the lamp over to have a nearer view of his face a drop of burning oil fell on the shoulder of the god, startled with which he opened his eyes and fixed them full upon her; then, without saying one word, he spread his white wings and flew out of the window.", "그의 얼굴을 더 가까이 보려고 등불을 기울이는 순간, 타는 기름 한 방울이 그 신의 어깨 위에 떨어졌고, 이에 깜짝 놀란 그는 눈을 떠 그녀를 정면으로 응시하더니, 한 마디도 하지 않고 자신의 흰 날개를 펼쳐 창밖으로 날아가 버렸다."], ["Psyche, in vain endeavoring to follow him, fell from the window to the ground.", "프시케는 그를 뒤따르려 했으나 헛되이 창에서 땅으로 떨어지고 말았다."], ["Cupid, beholding her as she lay in the dust, stopped his flight for an instant and said, “O foolish Psyche, is it thus you repay my love?", "에로스는 그녀가 흙먼지 속에 누운 것을 보고 잠시 비행을 멈추고 말했다. \"오, 어리석은 프시케여, 그대는 이렇게 내 사랑에 보답하는가?"], ["After having disobeyed my mother’s commands and made you my wife, will you think me a monster and cut off my head?", "내 어머니의 분부를 거역하면서까지 그대를 내 아내로 삼았건만, 그대는 나를 괴물로 여겨 내 머리를 베려 하다니? 하지만 가라."], ["But go; return to your sisters, whose advice you seem to think preferable to mine.", "그대의 언니들에게로 돌아가라. 그대는 내 충고보다 그들의 충고가 더 낫다고 여기는 듯하니."], ["I inflict no other punishment on you than to leave you forever.", "나는 그대를 영원히 떠나는 것 외에 다른 벌은 내리지 않겠다."], ["Love cannot dwell with suspicion.”", "사랑은 의심과 더불어 머무를 수 없는 법이다.\""], ["So saying, he fled away, leaving poor Psyche prostrate on the ground, filling the place with mournful lamentations.", "이렇게 말하고 그는 가엾은 프시케를 땅바닥에 엎드린 채로 남겨 두고 날아가 버렸으니, 그녀는 그 자리를 슬픈 통곡으로 채웠다."]], [["When she had recovered some degree of composure she looked around her, but the palace and gardens had vanished, and she found herself in the open field not far from the city where her sisters dwelt.", "그녀가 어느 정도 평정을 되찾고 주위를 둘러보았을 때, 궁전과 정원은 사라지고 없었으며, 그녀는 언니들이 사는 도시에서 그리 멀지 않은 허허벌판에 있는 자신을 발견했다."], ["She repaired thither and told them the whole story of her misfortunes, at which, pretending to grieve, those spiteful creatures inwardly rejoiced.", "그녀는 그곳으로 가서 자신의 불행에 관한 이야기를 모조리 들려주었는데, 그 심술궂은 것들은 겉으로는 슬퍼하는 체하면서 속으로는 기뻐했다."], ["“For now,”", "\"이제,\""], ["said they, “he will perhaps choose one of us.”", "그들이 말했다. \"어쩌면 그가 우리 중 하나를 택할 테니까.\""], ["With this idea, without saying a word of her intentions, each of them rose early the next morning and ascended the mountains, and having reached the top, called upon Zephyr to receive her and bear her to his lord; then leaping up, and not being sustained by Zephyr, fell down the precipice and was dashed to pieces.", "이런 생각으로, 자기 속셈은 한 마디도 입에 담지 않은 채, 그들 각자는 이튿날 아침 일찍 일어나 산을 올랐고, 꼭대기에 다다르자 제피로스에게 자기를 받아 그의 주인에게로 데려가 달라고 외쳤다. 그러고는 뛰어올랐으나 제피로스의 받침을 받지 못해 벼랑 아래로 떨어져 산산조각이 나고 말았다."]], [["Psyche meanwhile wandered day and night, without food or repose, in search of her husband.", "그동안 프시케는 음식도 휴식도 없이 밤낮으로 헤매며 남편을 찾았다."], ["Casting her eyes on a lofty mountain having on its brow a magnificent temple, she sighed and said to herself, “Perhaps my love, my lord, inhabits there,”", "이마에 장려한 신전을 인 높다란 산에 눈길을 던지며, 그녀는 한숨지으며 혼잣말을 했다. \"어쩌면 내 사랑, 내 주인이 저기에 사실지도.\""], ["and directed her steps thither.", "그러고는 그곳으로 발걸음을 향했다."]], [["She had no sooner entered than she saw heaps of corn, some in loose ears and some in sheaves, with mingled ears of barley.", "그녀가 안으로 들어서자마자 곡식 더미가 보였으니, 더러는 흩어진 이삭으로, 더러는 단으로 묶여 있었고, 보리 이삭이 뒤섞여 있었다."], ["Scattered about, lay sickles and rakes, and all the instruments of harvest, without order, as if thrown carelessly out of the weary reapers’ hands in the sultry hours of the day.", "여기저기에는 낫과 갈퀴, 그리고 추수의 온갖 연장이 어지러이 흩어져 있었으니, 마치 무더운 한낮에 지친 일꾼들의 손에서 아무렇게나 내던져진 것 같았다."]], [["This unseemly confusion the pious Psyche put an end to, by separating and sorting everything to its proper place and kind, believing that she ought to neglect none of the gods, but endeavor by her piety to engage them all in her behalf.", "이 보기 흉한 어수선함을, 경건한 프시케는 모든 것을 제 자리와 제 종류대로 갈라 정리함으로써 끝맺었으니, 그녀는 어떤 신도 소홀히 해서는 안 되며 오히려 자신의 경건함으로 그들 모두가 자기 편이 되도록 애써야 한다고 믿은 까닭이었다. 그 신전의 주인인 거룩한 데메테르[로마의 〈케레스〉]는 그녀가 그토록 경건히 일하는 것을 보고 그녀에게 이렇게 말했다."], ["The holy Ceres, whose temple it was, finding her so religiously employed, thus spoke to her: “O Psyche, truly worthy of our pity, though I cannot shield you from the frowns of Venus, yet I can teach you how best to allay her displeasure.", "\"오 프시케여, 참으로 우리의 연민을 받을 만하도다. 비록 내가 너를 아프로디테의 노여움으로부터 막아 줄 수는 없으나, 그녀의 노여움을 가장 잘 누그러뜨릴 방도는 가르쳐 줄 수 있노라."], ["Go, then, and voluntarily surrender yourself to your lady and sovereign, and try by modesty and submission to win her forgiveness, and perhaps her favor will restore you the husband you have lost.”", "그러니 가서, 너의 마님이자 주인이신 분께 스스로 너를 내맡기고, 겸손과 순종으로 그녀의 용서를 얻으려 애써 보아라. 그리하면 어쩌면 그녀의 은혜가 네게 잃어버린 남편을 되돌려 줄지도 모른다.\""]], [["Psyche obeyed the commands of Ceres and took her way to the temple of Venus, endeavoring to fortify her mind and ruminating on what she should say and how best propitiate the angry goddess, feeling that the issue was doubtful and perhaps fatal.", "프시케는 데메테르의 분부를 따라 아프로디테의 신전으로 향했으니, 그녀는 마음을 굳건히 하려 애쓰면서 무슨 말을 해야 할지, 또 노여워하는 여신을 어떻게 하면 가장 잘 달랠 수 있을지 곰곰이 생각했으나, 그 결말이 미덥지 않고 어쩌면 치명적일 수도 있음을 느꼈다."]], [["Venus received her with angry countenance.", "아프로디테는 노여운 낯빛으로 그녀를 맞았다."], ["“Most undutiful and faithless of servants,”", "\"가장 본분을 저버리고 가장 불충한 종아,\""], ["said she, “do you at last remember that you really have a mistress?", "그녀가 말했다. \"너는 마침내 네게 정말로 주인이 있다는 것을 기억하게 되었느냐?"], ["Or have you rather come to see your sick husband, yet laid up of the wound given him by his loving wife?", "아니면 차라리 사랑하는 아내가 입힌 상처로 아직 자리보전하고 있는 네 병든 남편을 보러 온 것이냐?"], ["You are so ill-favored and disagreeable that the only way you can merit your lover must be by dint of industry and diligence.", "너는 어찌나 박색이고 못마땅한지, 네 연인에게 어울리는 자가 될 길이라곤 오로지 근면과 부지런함뿐이로다."], ["I will make trial of your housewifery.”", "네 살림 솜씨를 시험해 보겠노라.\""], ["Then she ordered Psyche to be led to the storehouse of her temple, where was laid up a great quantity of wheat, barley, millet, vetches, beans, and lentils prepared for food for her pigeons, and said, “Take and separate all these grains, putting all of the same kind in a parcel by themselves, and see that you get it done before evening.”", "그러고는 자기 신전의 곳간으로 프시케를 데려가게 했으니, 그곳에는 그녀의 비둘기들을 먹이려고 마련해 둔 밀, 보리, 기장, 살갈퀴, 콩, 렌즈콩이 엄청나게 쌓여 있었으며, 그녀는 말했다. \"이 곡식을 모조리 갈라, 같은 종류끼리 따로따로 한데 모아 두되, 저녁이 되기 전에 일을 마치도록 하여라.\""], ["Then Venus departed and left her to her task.", "그러고 나서 아프로디테는 떠나며 그녀에게 그 일을 맡겨 두었다."]], [["But Psyche, in a perfect consternation at the enormous work, sat stupid and silent, without moving a finger to the inextricable heap.", "그러나 프시케는 그 엄청난 일에 완전히 넋이 나가, 그 풀 길 없는 더미에 손가락 하나 까딱하지 못한 채 멍하니 말없이 앉아 있었다."]], [["While she sat despairing, Cupid stirred up the little ant, a native of the fields, to take compassion on her.", "그녀가 절망하여 앉아 있는 동안, 에로스는 들판의 토박이인 작은 개미를 부추겨 그녀를 가엾이 여기게 했다."], ["The leader of the ant hill, followed by whole hosts of his six-legged subjects, approached the heap, and with the utmost diligence, taking grain by grain, they separated the pile, sorting each kind to its parcel; and when it was all done, they vanished out of sight in a moment.", "개미집의 우두머리가 여섯 다리 달린 신하들의 온 무리를 거느리고 그 더미에 다가와, 더없는 부지런함으로 알갱이 하나하나를 집어 그 무더기를 갈라, 종류마다 제 묶음으로 골라냈다. 그리고 그 일이 모두 끝나자, 그들은 순식간에 시야에서 사라졌다."]], [["Venus at the approach of twilight returned from the banquet of the gods, breathing odors and crowned with roses.", "아프로디테는 황혼 무렵 신들의 잔치에서 돌아왔으니, 향기를 내뿜으며 장미로 관을 쓰고 있었다. 그녀는 일이 끝난 것을 보고 외쳤다."], ["Seeing the task done, she exclaimed, “This is no work of yours, wicked one, but his, whom to your own and his misfortune you have enticed.”", "\"이것은 네 일솜씨가 아니라, 사악한 것아, 그자의 솜씨로구나. 너 자신과 그의 불행을 초래하면서까지 네가 꾀어낸 그자 말이다.\""], ["So saying, she threw her a piece of black bread for her supper and went away.", "이렇게 말하고 그녀는 저녁거리로 검은 빵 한 조각을 그녀에게 던져 주고 가 버렸다."]], [["Next morning Venus ordered Psyche to be called and said to her, “Behold yonder grove which stretches along the margin of the water.", "이튿날 아침 아프로디테는 프시케를 불러오게 하여 그녀에게 말했다. \"저 물가를 따라 펼쳐진 숲을 보아라."], ["There you will find sheep feeding without a shepherd, with golden-shining fleeces on their backs.", "그곳에 가면 목자도 없이 풀을 뜯는 양들이 있을 터인데, 그 등에는 황금빛으로 빛나는 양털이 덮여 있느니라."], ["Go, fetch me a sample of that precious wool gathered from every one of their fleeces.”", "가서, 그 양들 하나하나의 양털에서 거둔 그 진귀한 털의 표본을 내게 가져오너라.\""]], [["Psyche obediently went to the riverside, prepared to do her best to execute the command.", "프시케는 순순히 강가로 가, 그 분부를 이행하려고 최선을 다할 채비를 했다."], ["But the river god inspired the reeds with harmonious murmurs, which seemed to say, “O maiden, severely tried, tempt not the dangerous flood, nor venture among the formidable rams on the other side, for as long as they are under the influence of the rising sun, they burn with a cruel rage to destroy mortals with their sharp horns or rude teeth.", "그러나 강의 신이 갈대들에게 조화로운 속삭임을 불어넣었으니, 그 소리는 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. \"오 가혹한 시련을 겪는 처녀여, 위험한 물결을 함부로 건드리지 말고, 저편에 있는 무시무시한 숫양들 사이로 무모하게 들어가지 말지어다."], ["But when the noontide sun has driven the cattle to the shade, and the serene spirit of the flood has lulled them to rest, you may then cross in safety, and you will find the woolly gold sticking to the bushes and the trunks of the trees.”", "저들이 떠오르는 해의 기운 아래 있는 동안은 날카로운 뿔이나 거친 이빨로 필멸자들을 죽이려는 잔혹한 분노로 타오르는 까닭이다. 그러나 한낮의 해가 그 짐승들을 그늘로 몰아넣고, 잔잔한 물결의 평온한 정기가 그들을 잠재워 쉬게 하거든, 그때는 안전하게 건널 수 있으리니, 그대는 그 황금빛 양털이 덤불과 나무줄기에 들러붙어 있는 것을 보게 되리라.\""]], [["Thus the compassionate river god gave Psyche instructions how to accomplish her task, and by observing his directions she soon returned to Venus with her arms full of the golden fleece; but she received not the approbation of her implacable mistress, who said, “I know very well it is by none of your own doings that you have succeeded in this task, and I am not satisfied yet that you have any capacity to make yourself useful. But I have another task for you. Here, take this box and go your way to the infernal shades, and give this box to Proserpine and say, ‘My mistress Venus desires you to send her a little of your beauty, for in tending her sick son she has lost some of her own.’ Be not too long on your errand, for I must paint myself with it to appear at the circle of the gods and goddesses this evening.”", "이렇게 자비로운 강의 신은 프시케에게 어떻게 그 일을 이룰지 일러 주었고, 그녀는 그 지시를 따름으로써 곧 두 팔 가득 황금 양털을 안고 아프로디테에게로 돌아왔다. 그러나 그녀는 그 무자비한 마님의 칭찬을 받지 못했으니, 마님은 이렇게 말했다. \"이번 일에서 네가 성공한 것이 네 솜씨에서 비롯된 일이 결코 아님을 나는 익히 안다. 또한 나는 네게 너 자신을 쓸모 있게 만들 능력이 있다고 아직 만족하지 못하겠다. 하지만 네게 또 다른 일이 있다. 자, 이 상자를 받아 저승의 그늘로 가, 이 상자를 페르세포네[로마의 〈프로세르피나〉]에게 건네며 이렇게 말하여라. '제 마님 아프로디테께서 당신의 아름다움을 조금 보내 주시기를 바라십니다. 병든 아드님을 돌보시느라 당신 것을 얼마간 잃으셨거든요.' 네 심부름을 너무 오래 끌지 말지니, 오늘 저녁 신들과 여신들의 모임에 나가려면 내가 그것으로 단장을 해야 하느니라.\""]], [["Psyche was now satisfied that her destruction was at hand, being obliged to go with her own feet directly down to Erebus.", "프시케는 이제 자신의 파멸이 눈앞에 닥쳤음을 확신했으니, 제 발로 곧장 에레보스[저승]로 내려가야만 했던 까닭이다."], ["Wherefore, to make no delay of what was not to be avoided, she goes to the top of a high tower to precipitate herself headlong, thus to descend the shortest way to the shades below.", "그리하여 피할 수 없는 일을 지체하지 않으려고, 그녀는 거꾸로 몸을 던져 저 아래 그늘로 가장 빠른 길로 내려가려고 높은 탑 꼭대기로 올라갔다."], ["But a voice from the tower said to her, “Why, poor unlucky girl, dost thou design to put an end to thy days in so dreadful a manner?", "그러나 탑에서 어떤 목소리가 그녀에게 말했다. \"어찌하여, 가엾고 불운한 처녀여, 그대는 그토록 끔찍한 방식으로 그대의 목숨을 끝내려 하는가?"], ["And what cowardice makes thee sink under this last danger who hast been so miraculously supported in all thy former?”", "또한 그대는 지난 모든 위기에서 그토록 기적적으로 도움을 받았으면서, 어떤 비겁함으로 이 마지막 위험 앞에 무너지려 하는가?\""], ["Then the voice told her how by a certain cave she might reach the realms of Pluto, and how to avoid all the dangers of the road, to pass by Cerberus, the three-headed dog, and prevail on Charon, the ferryman, to take her across the black river and bring her back again.", "그러고 나서 그 목소리는 그녀가 어느 동굴을 통해 하데스[로마의 〈플루톤〉]의 영역에 이를 수 있는 방법과, 길의 온갖 위험을 피하는 방법, 머리 셋 달린 개 케르베로스 곁을 지나는 방법, 그리고 뱃사공 카론을 설득해 검은 강을 건너게 하고 다시 데려오게 하는 방법을 일러 주었다."], ["But the voice added, “When Proserpine has given you the box filled with her beauty, of all things this is chiefly to be observed by you, that you never once open or look into the box nor allow your curiosity to pry into the treasure of the beauty of the goddesses.”", "그러나 그 목소리는 덧붙였다. \"페르세포네가 자신의 아름다움을 채운 상자를 그대에게 건네거든, 무엇보다 그대가 명심해야 할 것은, 그 상자를 결코 한 번도 열거나 들여다보지 말 것이며, 여신들의 아름다움이라는 그 보물을 그대의 호기심이 엿보도록 결코 허락하지 말라는 것이다.\""]], [["Psyche, encouraged by this advice, obeyed it in all things, and taking heed to her ways travelled safely to the kingdom of Pluto.", "프시케는 이 충고에 힘을 얻어 모든 일에서 그것을 따랐고, 제 갈 길을 조심하여 무사히 하데스의 왕국에 다다랐다."], ["She was admitted to the palace of Proserpine, and without accepting the delicate seat or delicious banquet that was offered her, but contented with coarse bread for her food, she delivered her message from Venus.", "그녀는 페르세포네의 궁전에 받아들여졌고, 그녀에게 권해진 안락한 자리도 진미의 잔치도 받지 않은 채, 다만 거친 빵으로 끼니를 때우는 것으로 만족하며, 아프로디테의 전갈을 전했다."], ["Presently the box was returned to her, shut and filled with the precious commodity.", "이내 그 상자가 닫힌 채 그 진귀한 물건으로 채워져 그녀에게 되돌려졌다."], ["Then she returned the way she came, and glad was she to come out once more into the light of day.", "그러고서 그녀는 왔던 길로 되돌아갔으니, 다시 한 번 대낮의 빛 속으로 나오게 되어 기뻤다."]], [["But having got so far successfully through her dangerous task, a longing desire seized her to examine the contents of the box.", "그러나 위험한 일을 거기까지 무사히 해낸 마당에, 그 상자의 내용물을 살펴보고 싶은 간절한 욕망이 그녀를 사로잡았다."], ["“What,”", "\"어째서,\""], ["said she, “shall I, the carrier of this divine beauty, not take the least bit to put on my cheeks to appear to more advantage in the eyes of my beloved husband!”", "그녀가 말했다. \"이 신성한 아름다움을 나르는 내가, 사랑하는 내 남편의 눈에 더 돋보이도록 내 뺨에 바를 만큼 조금이라도 가져서는 안 된단 말인가!\""], ["So she carefully opened the box, but found nothing there of any beauty at all, but an infernal and truly Stygian sleep, which being thus set free from its prison, took possession of her, and she fell down in the midst of the road, a sleepy corpse without sense or motion.", "그리하여 그녀는 조심스레 상자를 열었으나, 거기에는 아름다움이라곤 도무지 들어 있지 않고, 다만 저승의, 참으로 스틱스 강 같은 잠이 들어 있어, 그것이 이렇게 감옥에서 풀려나 그녀를 사로잡았고, 그녀는 길 한가운데에 쓰러져, 감각도 움직임도 없는 잠든 시체가 되고 말았다."]], [["But Cupid, being now recovered from his wound, and not able longer to bear the absence of his beloved Psyche, slipping through the smallest crack of the window of his chamber which happened to be left open, flew to the spot where Psyche lay, and gathering up the sleep from her body closed it again in the box, and waked Psyche with a light touch of one of his arrows.", "그러나 이제 상처에서 회복된 에로스는 사랑하는 프시케와 떨어져 있는 것을 더는 견딜 수 없어, 마침 열려 있던 자기 침실 창문의 가장 작은 틈으로 빠져나와 프시케가 누워 있는 곳으로 날아갔고, 그녀의 몸에서 잠을 거두어 다시 상자에 가두고는, 자기 화살 하나로 가볍게 건드려 프시케를 깨웠다. \"또다시,\""], ["“Again,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “hast thou almost perished by the same curiosity.", "\"그대는 그 똑같은 호기심으로 하마터면 목숨을 잃을 뻔했구려."], ["But now perform exactly the task imposed on you by my mother, and I will take care of the rest.”", "하지만 이제 내 어머니께서 그대에게 맡기신 일을 정확히 이행하시오. 나머지는 내가 알아서 처리하리다.\""]], [["Then Cupid, as swift as lightning penetrating the heights of heaven, presented himself before Jupiter with his supplication.", "그러고 나서 에로스는 번개처럼 빠르게 하늘의 높은 곳을 꿰뚫고 제우스 앞에 나아가 자신의 탄원을 올렸다. 제우스는 호의로운 귀를 기울였고, 두 연인의 사정을 아프로디테에게 어찌나 간곡히 변호했던지 그녀의 동의를 얻어 냈다."], ["Jupiter lent a favoring ear, and pleaded the cause of the lovers so earnestly with Venus that he won her consent.", "이에 그는 헤르메스를 보내 프시케를 천상의 회합으로 데려오게 했고, 그녀가 도착하자 신들의 음식 암브로시아 한 잔을 건네며 말했다."], ["On this he sent Mercury to bring Psyche up to the heavenly assembly, and when she arrived, handing her a cup of ambrosia, he said, “Drink this, Psyche, and be immortal; nor shall Cupid ever break away from the knot in which he is tied, but these nuptials shall be perpetual.”", "\"이것을 마셔라, 프시케여, 그리고 불멸의 존재가 되어라. 에로스는 자기가 묶인 그 매듭에서 결코 빠져나가지 못할 것이며, 이 혼인은 영원할 것이니라.\""]], [["Thus Psyche became at last united to Cupid, and in due time they had a daughter born to them whose name was Pleasure.", "이리하여 프시케는 마침내 에로스와 맺어졌고, 때가 되어 그들에게 딸 하나가 태어났으니 그 이름은 〈환희〉였다."]], [["The fable of Cupid and Psyche is usually considered allegorical.", "에로스와 프시케의 우화는 흔히 우의적인 것으로 여겨진다."], ["The Greek name for a butterfly is Psyche, and the same word means the soul.", "나비를 가리키는 그리스어 이름이 프시케이며, 같은 낱말이 영혼을 뜻하기도 한다."], ["There is no illustration of the immortality of the soul so striking and beautiful as the butterfly, bursting on brilliant wings from the tomb in which it has lain, after a dull, grovelling, caterpillar existence, to flutter in the blaze of day and feed on the most fragrant and delicate productions of the spring.", "영혼의 불멸을 나비만큼 인상적이고 아름답게 보여 주는 예는 없으니, 나비는 둔하고 비천하게 기는 애벌레로 살다가, 그것이 누워 있던 무덤에서 찬란한 날개를 펼치며 솟아나, 대낮의 눈부신 빛 속에서 날갯짓하며 봄의 가장 향기롭고 가녀린 산물을 먹는 까닭이다."], ["Psyche, then, is the human soul, which is purified by sufferings and misfortunes, and is thus prepared for the enjoyment of true and pure happiness.", "그러므로 프시케는 인간의 영혼이니, 고난과 불행으로 정화되어, 그리하여 참되고 순수한 행복을 누릴 채비를 갖추게 되는 것이다."]], [["In works of art Psyche is represented as a maiden with the wings of a butterfly, along with Cupid, in the different situations described in the allegory.", "미술 작품에서 프시케는 나비의 날개를 단 처녀로, 에로스와 함께 그 우의에 묘사된 갖가지 상황 속에 표현된다."]], [["Milton alludes to the story of Cupid and Psyche in the conclusion of his “Comus”:", "밀턴은 자신의 「코머스」 결말부에서 에로스와 프시케의 이야기를 암시한다."]], [["“Celestial Cupid, her famed son, advanced, Holds his dear Psyche sweet entranced, After her wandering labors long, Till free consent the gods among Make her his eternal bride; And from her fair unspotted side Two blissful twins are to be born, Youth and Joy; so Jove hath sworn.”", "\"천상의 에로스, 그녀의 이름난 아들이 나아와, 오랜 방랑의 노고를 마친 뒤 감미로이 황홀경에 든 사랑하는 프시케를 품에 안으니, 이윽고 신들 사이의 거리낌 없는 동의가 그녀를 그의 영원한 신부로 삼는도다. 그리고 그녀의 곱고 티 없는 옆구리에서 축복받은 쌍둥이가 태어나리니, 〈청춘〉과 〈환희〉라, 제우스께서 그리 맹세하셨도다.\""]], [["The allegory of the story of Cupid and Psyche is well presented in the beautiful lines of T.", "에로스와 프시케 이야기의 우의는 T. K."], ["K. Harvey:", "하비의 아름다운 시구에 잘 드러나 있다."]], [["“They wove bright fables in the days of old, When reason borrowed fancy’s painted wings; When truth’s clear river flowed o’er sands of gold, And told in song its high and mystic things!", "\"그들은 옛 시절에 빛나는 우화를 지어냈으니, 이성이 공상의 채색된 날개를 빌려 오던 때요, 진리의 맑은 강물이 황금 모래 위로 흐르며, 그 높고 신비로운 것들을 노래로 들려주던 때라!"], ["And such the sweet and solemn tale of her The pilgrim heart, to whom a dream was given.", "그리고 그러한 것이 그녀의 감미롭고 엄숙한 이야기니, 한 꿈을 받은 순례하는 마음이여."], ["That led her through the world,—Love’s worshipper,— To seek on earth for him whose home was heaven!", "그 꿈이 그녀를 세상 너머로 이끌었으니—사랑의 숭배자라— 그 집이 하늘에 있는 이를 땅에서 찾으려 함이라!"]], [["“In the full city,—by the haunted fount,— Through the dim grotto’s tracery of spars,— ’Mid the pine temples, on the moonlit mount, Where silence sits to listen to the stars; In the deep glade where dwells the brooding dove, The painted valley, and the scented air, She heard far echoes of the voice of Love, And found his footsteps’ traces everywhere.", "\"북적이는 도시에서—유령 깃든 샘가에서— 어슴푸레한 동굴의 결정 무늬 사이로— 소나무 신전 가운데, 달빛 어린 산 위에서, 거기 침묵이 앉아 별들에 귀 기울이는 곳에서, 품은 비둘기가 깃든 깊은 숲속 빈터에서, 채색된 골짜기와 향기로운 대기 속에서, 그녀는 사랑의 목소리의 먼 메아리를 들었고, 어디서나 그의 발자취를 찾아내었네."]], [["“But nevermore they met!", "\"그러나 그들은 다시는 만나지 못했네!"], ["since doubts and fears, Those phantom shapes that haunt and blight the earth, Had come ’twixt her, a child of sin and tears, And that bright spirit of immortal birth; Until her pining soul and weeping eyes Had learned to seek him only in the skies; Till wings unto the weary heart were given, And she became Love’s angel bride in heaven!”", "의심과 두려움, 대지를 떠돌며 시들게 하는 저 허깨비의 형상들이 죄와 눈물의 자식인 그녀와, 불멸의 태생인 저 빛나는 정령 사이에 끼어든 까닭이라. 마침내 그녀의 애타는 영혼과 우는 두 눈이 오직 하늘에서만 그를 찾기를 배우게 되었고, 지친 마음에 날개가 주어져, 그녀는 하늘에서 사랑의 천사 신부가 되었네!\""]], [["The story of Cupid and Psyche first appears in the works of Apuleius, a writer of the second century of our era.", "에로스와 프시케 이야기는 우리 시대 2세기의 작가인 아풀레이우스의 작품에 처음 나타난다."], ["It is therefore of much more recent date than most of the legends of the Age of Fable.", "따라서 그것은 신화의 시대의 전설 대부분보다 훨씬 더 나중에 생겨난 것이다."], ["It is this that Keats alludes to in his “Ode to Psyche”:", "키츠가 자신의 「프시케에게 바치는 송가」에서 암시하는 것이 바로 이것이다."]], [["“O latest born and loveliest vision far Of all Olympus’ faded hierarchy!", "\"오 올림포스의 빛바랜 모든 신들의 위계 가운데 가장 늦게 태어난, 그리고 단연 가장 사랑스러운 환영이여! 사파이어빛 영역을 거느린 포이베의 별보다도, 하늘의 정겨운 반딧불 베스페르보다도 곱구나."], ["Fairer than Phœbe’s sapphire-regioned star Or Vesper, amorous glow-worm of the sky; Fairer than these, though temple thou hast none, Nor altar heaped with flowers; Nor virgin choir to make delicious moan Upon the midnight hours; No voice, no lute, no pipe, no incense sweet, From chain-swung censor teeming; No shrine, no grove, no oracle, no heat Of pale-mouthed prophet dreaming.”", "이 모든 것보다 곱건만, 그대에게는 신전이 없고, 꽃을 수북이 쌓은 제단도 없으며, 한밤의 시각에 감미로운 탄식을 자아낼 처녀들의 합창도 없구나. 목소리도, 류트도, 피리도, 사슬에 매달린 향로에서 피어오르는 감미로운 향연(香煙)도 없고, 사당도, 숲도, 신탁도, 창백한 입술로 꿈꾸는 예언자의 열기도 없구나.\""]], [["In Moore’s “Summer Fête” a fancy ball is described, in which one of the characters personated is Psyche—", "무어의 「여름 축제」에는 가장무도회가 묘사되는데, 거기서 분장한 인물 가운데 하나가 프시케다—"]], [["“. .", "\"."], [".", "."], ["not in dark disguise to-night Hath our young heroine veiled her light;— For see, she walks the earth, Love’s own.", ". 오늘 밤 우리의 젊은 여주인공은 어두운 변장으로 제 빛을 가리지 않았네— 보라, 그녀는 땅 위를 거니니, 사랑이 자기 것으로 삼은 이라."], ["His wedded bride, by holiest vow Pledged in Olympus, and made known To mortals by the type which now Hangs glittering on her snowy brow.", "더없이 거룩한 맹세로 올림포스에서 약속된 그의 혼인한 신부요, 이제 그녀의 눈처럼 흰 이마 위에 반짝이며 걸린 그 표지로써 필멸자들에게 알려진 이라."], ["That butterfly, mysterious trinket, Which means the soul, (though few would think it,) And sparkling thus on brow so white Tells us we’ve Psyche here to-night.”", "저 나비, 신비로운 장신구는 영혼을 뜻하니, (그리 여길 이 드물겠으나,) 그토록 흰 이마 위에 이렇게 반짝이며 오늘 밤 여기 프시케가 있음을 우리에게 일러 주네.\""]], [["————", "————"]], [["# CADMUS—THE MYRMIDONS", "# CADMUS—THE MYRMIDONS"]], [["JUPITER, under the disguise of a bull, had carried away Europa, the daughter of Agenor, king of Phœnicia.", "제우스가 황소로 변장하여 페니키아의 왕 아게노르의 딸 에우로페를 납치해 갔다."], ["Agenor commanded his son Cadmus to go in search of his sister, and not to return without her.", "아게노르는 아들 카드모스에게 누이를 찾아 나서되 누이 없이는 돌아오지 말라고 명령했다."], ["Cadmus went and sought long and far for his sister, but could not find her, and not daring to return unsuccessful, consulted the oracle of Apollo to know what country he should settle in.", "카드모스는 길을 떠나 멀리까지 오래도록 누이를 찾았으나 끝내 찾지 못했고, 성공하지 못한 채 돌아갈 엄두를 내지 못해 자신이 어느 나라에 정착해야 할지 알아보려고 아폴론의 신탁을 청했다."], ["The oracle informed him that he should find a cow in the field, and should follow her wherever she might wander, and where she stopped, should build a city and call it Thebes.", "신탁은 그가 들판에서 암소 한 마리를 발견할 것이며, 그 소가 어디로 떠돌든 따라가다가 소가 멈추는 곳에 도시를 세우고 그곳을 테바이라고 부르라고 일러 주었다."], ["Cadmus had hardly left the Castalian cave, from which the oracle was delivered, when he saw a young cow slowly walking before him.", "카드모스가 신탁이 내려진 카스탈리아의 동굴을 막 떠나자마자, 어린 암소 한 마리가 그의 앞을 천천히 걸어가는 것이 보였다."], ["He followed her close, offering at the same time his prayers to Phœbus.", "그는 그 소를 바짝 뒤따르면서 동시에 포이보스에게 기도를 올렸다."], ["The cow went on till she passed the shallow channel of Cephisus and came out into the plain of Panope.", "소는 케피소스의 얕은 물길을 건너 파노페 평원으로 나올 때까지 계속 걸어갔다."], ["There she stood still, and raising her broad forehead to the sky, filled the air with her lowings.", "그곳에서 소는 가만히 멈추어 서더니, 넓은 이마를 하늘로 쳐들고 울음소리로 공기를 가득 채웠다."], ["Cadmus gave thanks, and stooping down kissed the foreign soil, then lifting his eyes, greeted the surrounding mountains.", "카드모스는 감사를 올리고, 몸을 굽혀 낯선 땅에 입을 맞춘 뒤, 눈을 들어 주위를 둘러싼 산들에게 인사했다."], ["Wishing to offer a sacrifice to Jupiter, he sent his servants to seek pure water for a libation.", "그는 제우스에게 제물을 바치고자 하인들을 보내 헌주에 쓸 맑은 물을 구해 오게 했다."], ["Near by there stood an ancient grove which had never been profaned by the axe, in the midst of which was a cave, thick covered with the growth of bushes, its roof forming a low arch, from beneath which burst forth a fountain of purest water.", "가까이에는 도끼날에 한 번도 더럽혀진 적이 없는 오래된 숲이 있었는데, 그 한가운데에는 덤불이 무성하게 뒤덮인 동굴이 있어 그 지붕은 낮은 아치를 이루었고, 그 아래에서 더없이 맑은 물이 솟아 나오는 샘이 있었다."], ["In the cave lurked a horrid serpent with a crested head and scales glittering like gold.", "그 동굴 속에는 볏 달린 머리에 황금처럼 반짝이는 비늘을 가진 무시무시한 뱀 한 마리가 숨어 있었다."], ["His eyes shone like fire, his body was swollen with venom, he vibrated a triple tongue, and showed a triple row of teeth.", "그 눈은 불처럼 빛났고, 몸은 독으로 부풀어 있었으며, 세 갈래로 갈라진 혀를 날름거렸고, 세 줄로 늘어선 이빨을 드러내고 있었다."], ["No sooner had the Tyrians dipped their pitchers in the fountain, and the in-gushing waters made a sound, than the glittering serpent raised his head out of the cave and uttered a fearful hiss.", "티로스 사람들이 샘에 물항아리를 담그자마자, 그리고 물이 콸콸 흘러 들어오는 소리가 나자마자, 반짝이는 뱀이 동굴 밖으로 머리를 쳐들고 끔찍한 쉭쉭 소리를 냈다."], ["The vessels fell from their hands, the blood left their cheeks, they trembled in every limb.", "그들의 손에서 그릇이 떨어졌고, 뺨에서는 핏기가 가셨으며, 온몸의 사지가 떨렸다."], ["The serpent, twisting his scaly body in a huge coil, raised his head so as to overtop the tallest trees, and while the Tyrians from terror could neither fight nor fly, slew some with his fangs, others in his folds, and others with his poisonous breath.", "뱀은 비늘 덮인 몸을 거대한 똬리로 비틀어 가장 키 큰 나무들보다도 높이 머리를 쳐들었고, 티로스 사람들이 공포에 질려 싸우지도 달아나지도 못하는 사이에, 어떤 이들은 송곳니로, 어떤 이들은 똬리로 휘감아서, 또 어떤 이들은 독기 어린 숨결로 죽여 버렸다."]], [["Cadmus, having waited for the return of his men till midday, went in search of them.", "카드모스는 한낮이 되도록 부하들이 돌아오기를 기다리다가 그들을 찾아 나섰다."], ["His covering was a lion’s hide, and besides his javelin he carried in his hand a lance, and in his breast a bold heart, a surer reliance than either.", "그가 걸친 것은 사자 가죽이었고, 투창 외에도 손에는 긴 창을 들었으며, 가슴에는 그 어느 무기보다 더 든든하게 의지할 수 있는 담대한 심장을 품고 있었다."], ["When he entered the wood, and saw the lifeless bodies of his men, and the monster with his bloody jaws, he exclaimed, “O faithful friends, I will avenge you, or share your death.”", "그가 숲에 들어서서 부하들의 생기 없는 주검들과 피투성이 아가리를 한 괴물을 보았을 때, 그는 이렇게 외쳤다. \"오, 충실한 벗들이여, 내가 너희의 원수를 갚거나, 아니면 너희와 죽음을 함께하리라.\""], ["So saying he lifted a huge stone and threw it with all his force at the serpent.", "이렇게 말하고는 거대한 돌을 들어 온 힘을 다해 뱀에게 던졌다."], ["Such a block would have shaken the wall of a fortress, but it made no impression on the monster.", "그만한 돌덩이라면 요새의 성벽이라도 흔들었으련만, 괴물에게는 아무런 흔적도 남기지 못했다."], ["Cadmus next threw his javelin, which met with better success, for it penetrated the serpent’s scales, and pierced through to his entrails.", "카드모스는 다음으로 투창을 던졌고, 이번에는 더 나은 성공을 거두어 창이 뱀의 비늘을 뚫고 들어가 내장까지 꿰뚫었다."], ["Fierce with pain, the monster turned back his head to view the wound, and attempted to draw out the weapon with his mouth, but broke it off, leaving the iron point rankling in his flesh.", "고통으로 사나워진 괴물은 머리를 돌려 상처를 살펴보고는 입으로 무기를 뽑아내려 했으나, 창대를 부러뜨려 쇠 촉을 살 속에 박힌 채로 남겨 두고 말았다."], ["His neck swelled with rage, bloody foam covered his jaws, and the breath of his nostrils poisoned the air around.", "분노로 목이 부풀어 올랐고, 피 묻은 거품이 아가리를 뒤덮었으며, 콧구멍에서 나오는 숨결이 주변의 공기를 독으로 물들였다."], ["Now he twisted himself into a circle, then stretched himself out on the ground like the trunk of a fallen tree.", "이제 괴물은 몸을 둥글게 똬리 틀었다가, 다시 쓰러진 나무 둥치처럼 땅바닥에 길게 뻗었다."], ["As he moved onward, Cadmus retreated before him, holding his spear opposite to the monster’s opened jaws.", "괴물이 앞으로 다가오자 카드모스는 그 앞에서 물러서면서 괴물의 벌어진 아가리를 향해 창을 겨누었다."], ["The serpent snapped at the weapon and attempted to bite its iron point.", "뱀은 무기를 덥석 물고 쇠 촉을 깨물려 했다."], ["At last Cadmus, watching his chance, thrust the spear at a moment when the animal’s head thrown back came against the trunk of a tree, and so succeeded in pinning him to its side.", "마침내 카드모스는 기회를 노리다가, 짐승이 뒤로 젖힌 머리가 나무 둥치에 부딪치는 순간 창을 찔러 넣어, 마침내 괴물을 나무 옆구리에 못 박는 데 성공했다."], ["His weight bent the tree as he struggled in the agonies of death.", "괴물이 죽음의 고통 속에서 몸부림치자 그 무게에 나무가 휘었다."]], [["While Cadmus stood over his conquered foe, contemplating its vast size, a voice was heard (from whence he knew not, but he heard it distinctly) commanding him to take the dragon’s teeth and sow them in the earth.", "카드모스가 자신이 쓰러뜨린 적 위에 서서 그 어마어마한 크기를 바라보고 있을 때, 어디서 들려오는지는 알 수 없으나 분명하게 들리는 한 목소리가 그에게 그 용의 이빨을 뽑아 땅에 뿌리라고 명령했다. 그는 그 명에 따랐다."], ["He obeyed. He made a furrow in the ground, and planted the teeth, destined to produce a crop of men.", "그는 땅에 고랑을 내고, 사람이라는 작물을 자라게 할 운명을 지닌 그 이빨들을 심었다."], ["Scarce had he done so when the clods began to move, and the points of spears to appear above the surface.", "그가 그렇게 하자마자 흙덩이가 움직이기 시작하더니, 창끝이 땅 위로 모습을 드러냈다."], ["Next helmets with their nodding plumes came up, and next the shoulders and breasts and limbs of men with weapons, and in time a harvest of armed warriors.", "이어서 깃털 장식이 흔들리는 투구들이 솟아올랐고, 다음으로는 무기를 든 사람들의 어깨와 가슴과 사지가, 그리고 이윽고 무장한 전사들의 한 무리 수확물이 솟아났다."], ["Cadmus, alarmed, prepared to encounter a new enemy, but one of them said to him, “Meddle not with our civil war.”", "깜짝 놀란 카드모스는 새로운 적과 맞설 채비를 했으나, 그들 가운데 하나가 그에게 말했다. \"우리 내전에 끼어들지 마시오.\""], ["With that he who had spoken smote one of his earth-born brothers with a sword, and he himself fell pierced with an arrow from another.", "그렇게 말한 자는 땅에서 태어난 형제 하나를 칼로 내리쳤고, 자신은 또 다른 자가 쏜 화살에 꿰뚫려 쓰러졌다."], ["The latter fell victim to a fourth, and in like manner the whole crowd dealt with each other till all fell, slain with mutual wounds, except five survivors.", "화살을 쏜 자는 네 번째 자에게 희생되었고, 이런 식으로 무리 전체가 서로를 베어 가다가, 다섯 명의 생존자를 제외하고는 모두 서로의 상처에 쓰러져 죽었다."], ["One of these cast away his weapons and said, “Brothers, let us live in peace!”", "그중 하나가 무기를 내던지며 말했다. \"형제들이여, 우리 평화롭게 삽시다!\""], ["These five joined with Cadmus in building his city, to which they gave the name of Thebes.", "이 다섯 명은 카드모스와 합류하여 그의 도시를 세웠고, 그 도시에 테바이라는 이름을 붙였다."]], [["Cadmus obtained in marriage Harmonia, the daughter of Venus.", "카드모스는 아프로디테[로마의 〈베누스〉]의 딸 하르모니아를 아내로 맞이했다."], ["The gods left Olympus to honor the occasion with their presence, and Vulcan presented the bride with a necklace of surpassing brilliancy, his own workmanship.", "신들은 그 경사를 함께하여 기리고자 올림포스를 떠나 왔고, 헤파이스토스[로마의 〈불카누스〉]는 자신이 직접 만든, 더없이 눈부신 목걸이를 신부에게 선물했다."], ["But a fatality hung over the family of Cadmus in consequence of his killing the serpent sacred to Mars.", "그러나 카드모스가 아레스[로마의 〈마르스〉]에게 바쳐진 뱀을 죽인 탓에, 그의 가문에는 불운이 드리워져 있었다."], ["Semele and Ino, his daughters, and Actæon and Pentheus, his grandchildren, all perished unhappily, and Cadmus and Harmonia quitted Thebes, now grown odious to them, and emigrated to the country of the Enchelians, who received them with honor and made Cadmus their king.", "그의 딸들인 세멜레와 이노, 그의 외손자들인 악타이온과 펜테우스가 모두 불행하게 죽었고, 카드모스와 하르모니아는 이제 자신들에게 끔찍해진 테바이를 떠나 엔켈리스인들의 나라로 이주했는데, 그들은 두 사람을 정중히 맞이하여 카드모스를 자신들의 왕으로 삼았다. 그러나 자식들의 불행은 여전히 두 사람의 마음을 짓눌렀다."], ["But the misfortunes of their children still weighed upon their minds; and one day Cadmus exclaimed, “If a serpent’s life is so dear to the gods, I would I were myself a serpent.”", "그러던 어느 날 카드모스가 외쳤다. \"뱀의 목숨이 신들에게 그토록 소중하다면, 차라리 내가 직접 뱀이 되었으면 좋겠구나.\""], ["No sooner had he uttered the words than he began to change his form.", "그가 이 말을 내뱉기가 무섭게 그의 몸이 변하기 시작했다."], ["Harmonia beheld it and prayed to the gods to let her share his fate.", "하르모니아가 그것을 보고는 자기도 남편의 운명을 함께하게 해 달라고 신들에게 기도했다."], ["Both became serpents.", "두 사람은 모두 뱀이 되었다."], ["They live in the woods, but mindful of their origin, they neither avoid the presence of man nor do they ever injure any one.", "그들은 숲속에 살지만, 자신들의 본디 모습을 잊지 않고 있어, 사람의 곁을 피하지도 않으며 누구도 해치지 않는다."]], [["There is a tradition that Cadmus introduced into Greece the letters of the alphabet which were invented by the Phœnicians.", "카드모스가 페니키아인들이 발명한 알파벳 글자를 그리스에 들여왔다는 전승이 있다."], ["This is alluded to by Byron, where, addressing the modern Greeks, he says:", "바이런은 근대 그리스인들에게 이렇게 말하면서 이 일을 넌지시 언급한다."]], [["“You have the letters Cadmus gave, Think you he meant them for a slave?”", "\"그대들은 카드모스가 준 글자를 가지고 있으니, 그가 그것을 노예를 위해 마련했다고 생각하는가?\""]], [["Milton, describing the serpent which tempted Eve, is reminded of the serpents of the classical stories and says:", "밀턴은 이브를 유혹한 뱀을 묘사하면서 고전 이야기 속의 뱀들을 떠올리고는 이렇게 말한다."]], [[". .", "……\"그 모습은 보기 좋고 사랑스러웠으니, 그 후로 뱀의 종족 가운데 이보다 더 사랑스러운 것은 없었다."], [". “—pleasing was his shape, And lovely; never since of serpent kind Lovelier; not those that in Illyria changed Hermione and Cadmus, nor the god In Epidaurus.”", "일리리아에서 헤르미오네와 카드모스를 변신시킨 뱀들도, 에피다우로스의 그 신도 이만 못했다.\""]], [["For an explanation of the last allusion, see Oracle of Æsculapius, p. 298.", "이 마지막 인유에 대한 설명은 아스클레피오스[로마의 〈아이스쿨라피우스〉]의 신탁, 298면을 보라."]], [["# THE MYRMIDONS", "# THE MYRMIDONS"]], [["The Myrmidons were the soldiers of Achilles, in the Trojan war.", "미르미돈족\n미르미돈족은 트로이아 전쟁에서 아킬레우스의 병사들이었다."], ["From them all zealous and unscrupulous followers of a political chief are called by that name, down to this day.", "그들에게서 비롯하여, 어떤 정치 지도자의 열성적이고 양심을 가리지 않는 추종자들은 오늘날까지도 그 이름으로 불린다."], ["But the origin of the Myrmidons would not give one the idea of a fierce and bloody race, but rather of a laborious and peaceful one.", "그러나 미르미돈족의 기원은 사납고 피에 굶주린 종족이라는 인상보다는, 오히려 부지런하고 평화로운 종족이라는 인상을 떠올리게 한다."]], [["Cephalus, king of Athens, arrived in the island of Ægina to seek assistance of his old friend and ally Æacus, the king, in his war with Minos, king of Crete.", "아테나이의 왕 케팔로스는 크레타의 왕 미노스와 벌이는 전쟁에서 오랜 벗이자 동맹인 아이아코스 왕에게 도움을 청하고자 아이기나섬에 도착했다. 케팔로스는 더없이 친절한 환대를 받았고, 그가 바라던 도움도 흔쾌히 약속받았다."], ["Cephalus was most kindly received, and the desired assistance readily promised. “I have people enough,”", "\"나는 나 자신을 지키고도 당신에게 필요한 만큼의 병력을 내어 줄 수 있을 만큼 백성이 충분하오.\""], ["said Æacus, “to protect myself and spare you such a force as you need.”", "아이아코스가 말했다. \"그것을 보니 기쁘오.\""], ["“I rejoice to see it,”", "케팔로스가 대답했다."], ["replied Cephalus, “and my wonder has been raised, I confess, to find such a host of youths as I see around me, all apparently of about the same age.", "\"고백하건대, 내 주위에 보이는 이토록 많은 젊은이들이 하나같이 비슷한 나이로 보이니 놀라울 따름이오."], ["Yet there are many individuals whom I previously knew, that I look for now in vain.", "그런데 내가 전에 알고 지내던 이들이 많건만, 지금은 아무리 찾아도 보이지 않는구려."], ["What has become of them?”", "그들은 어찌 되었소?\""], ["Æacus groaned, and replied with a voice of sadness, “I have been intending to tell you, and will now do so, without more delay, that you may see how from the saddest beginning a happy result sometimes flows.", "아이아코스는 신음하더니 슬픔에 잠긴 목소리로 대답했다. \"당신에게 말하려고 하던 참이었으니, 이제 더 미루지 않고 이야기하겠소."], ["Those whom you formerly knew are now dust and ashes!", "그리하여 당신은 더없이 슬픈 시작에서 때로 행복한 결말이 흘러나오는 법을 보게 될 것이오."], ["A plague sent by angry Juno devastated the land.", "당신이 예전에 알던 이들은 이제 모두 먼지요 재가 되었소!"], ["She hated it because it bore the name of one of her husband’s female favorites.", "노한 헤라[로마의 〈유노〉]가 보낸 역병이 이 땅을 황폐하게 만들었소."], ["While the disease appeared to spring from natural causes we resisted it, as we best might, by natural remedies; but it soon appeared that the pestilence was too powerful for our efforts, and we yielded.", "그분은 이 땅이 자기 남편이 총애하던 여인들 가운데 하나의 이름을 따왔다는 이유로 이 땅을 미워하셨소. 그 병이 자연적인 원인에서 비롯된 것처럼 보이는 동안에는 우리도 자연적인 치료법으로 최선을 다해 그것에 맞섰소."], ["At the beginning the sky seemed to settle down upon the earth, and thick clouds shut in the heated air.", "그러나 곧 그 역병이 우리의 노력으로는 당해 낼 수 없을 만큼 강력하다는 것이 드러났고, 우리는 굴복하고 말았소."], ["For four months together a deadly south wind prevailed.", "처음에는 하늘이 땅 위로 내려앉는 듯하더니, 두꺼운 구름이 달아오른 공기를 가두었소."], ["The disorder affected the wells and springs; thousands of snakes crept over the land and shed their poison in the fountains.", "넉 달 동안 내리 죽음의 남풍이 기승을 부렸소. 그 병은 우물과 샘을 오염시켰고, 수천 마리의 뱀이 땅 위를 기어 다니며 샘에 독을 풀었소."], ["The force of the disease was first spent on the lower animals—dogs, cattle, sheep, and birds.", "그 병의 위력은 처음에는 하등 동물들에게 쏟아졌으니, 개와 소와 양과 새들이었소."], ["The luckless ploughman wondered to see his oxen fall in the midst of their work, and lie helpless in the unfinished furrow.", "가엾은 농부는 자기 황소들이 일하던 도중에 쓰러져 끝내지 못한 고랑에 무력하게 누워 있는 것을 보고 어리둥절해했소."], ["The wool fell from the bleating sheep, and their bodies pined away.", "매에 울던 양에게서는 털이 빠졌고, 그 몸뚱이는 야위어 갔소."], ["The horse, once foremost in the race, contested the palm no more, but groaned at his stall and died an inglorious death.", "한때 경주에서 가장 앞서 달리던 말은 더 이상 우승을 다투지 못하고, 마구간에서 신음하다가 영광 없는 죽음을 맞았소."], ["The wild boar forgot his rage, the stag his swiftness, the bears no longer attacked the herds.", "멧돼지는 사나움을 잊었고, 사슴은 날쌤을 잊었으며, 곰은 더 이상 가축 떼를 덮치지 않았소."], ["Everything languished; dead bodies lay in the roads, the fields, and the woods; the air was poisoned by them.", "모든 것이 시들어 갔으니, 주검들이 길과 들과 숲에 널브러져 있어 공기가 그것들로 오염되었소."], ["I tell you what is hardly credible, but neither dogs nor birds would touch them, nor starving wolves.", "좀처럼 믿기 어려운 일을 말하건대, 개도 새도, 굶주린 늑대조차도 그 주검에 손대려 하지 않았소."], ["Their decay spread the infection.", "그것들이 썩어 가며 전염병을 퍼뜨렸소."], ["Next the disease attacked the country people, and then the dwellers in the city.", "다음으로 그 병은 시골 사람들을 덮쳤고, 그다음에는 도시 거주민들을 덮쳤소."], ["At first the cheek was flushed, and the breath drawn with difficulty.", "처음에는 뺨이 벌겋게 달아오르고 숨쉬기가 힘들어졌소."], ["The tongue grew rough and swelled, and the dry mouth stood open with its veins enlarged and gasped for the air.", "혀가 거칠어지고 부어올랐으며, 마른 입이 핏줄이 부풀어 오른 채 벌어져 공기를 들이켜려 헐떡였소."], ["Men could not bear the heat of their clothes or their beds, but preferred to lie on the bare ground; and the ground did not cool them, but, on the contrary, they heated the spot where they lay.", "사람들은 옷이나 잠자리의 열기를 견디지 못하고 차라리 맨땅에 눕기를 좋아했소. 그러나 땅이 그들을 식혀 주기는커녕, 도리어 그들이 누운 자리를 그들이 달구어 놓았소."], ["Nor could the physicians help, for the disease attacked them also, and the contact of the sick gave them infection, so that the most faithful were the first victims.", "의사들도 도움이 되지 못했으니, 그 병이 의사들마저 덮쳐 환자와 접촉하면 그들에게도 전염되었기 때문이오. 그리하여 가장 충실한 자들이 가장 먼저 희생되었소."], ["At last all hope of relief vanished, and men learned to look upon death as the only deliverer from disease.", "마침내 회복에 대한 모든 희망이 사라졌고, 사람들은 죽음을 병에서 벗어나게 해 주는 유일한 구원자로 여기게 되었소."], ["Then they gave way to every inclination, and cared not to ask what was expedient, for nothing was expedient.", "그러자 그들은 온갖 욕망에 몸을 내맡겼고, 무엇이 이로운지 묻는 것조차 개의치 않았으니, 이로운 것이라곤 아무것도 없었기 때문이오."], ["All restraint laid aside, they crowded around the wells and fountains and drank till they died, without quenching thirst.", "모든 절제를 내던진 채 그들은 우물과 샘 주위에 몰려들어 죽을 때까지 물을 마셨으나 갈증을 가시게 하지는 못했소."], ["Many had not strength to get away from the water, but died in the midst of the stream, and others would drink of it notwithstanding.", "많은 이들이 물에서 빠져나올 기운조차 없어 물줄기 한가운데서 죽었고, 그래도 다른 이들은 그 물을 마셨소."], ["Such was their weariness of their sick beds that some would creep forth, and if not strong enough to stand, would die on the ground.", "병상에 대한 진저리가 어찌나 심했던지, 어떤 이들은 기어 나오다가 설 만한 기운이 없으면 땅바닥에서 죽었소."], ["They seemed to hate their friends, and got away from their homes, as if, not knowing the cause of their sickness, they charged it on the place of their abode.", "그들은 자기 벗들을 미워하는 듯했고, 마치 병의 원인을 알지 못한 채 그것을 자기 거처 탓으로 돌리기라도 하듯 집에서 빠져나갔소."], ["Some were seen tottering along the road, as long as they could stand, while others sank on the earth, and turned their dying eyes around to take a last look, then closed them in death.", "어떤 이들은 설 수 있는 동안에는 비틀거리며 길을 따라 걸어가는 모습이 보였고, 또 어떤 이들은 땅에 주저앉아 죽어 가는 눈으로 주위를 둘러보며 마지막 한 번을 바라본 뒤 죽음 속에서 그 눈을 감았소."]], [["“What heart had I left me, during all this, or what ought I to have had, except to hate life and wish to be with my dead subjects?", "\"이 모든 일을 겪는 동안 내게 무슨 심정이 남아 있었겠소, 또 죽은 내 백성들과 함께하기를 바라며 삶을 미워하는 것 말고 내게 어떤 심정이 있어야 했겠소?"], ["On all sides lay my people strewn like over-ripened apples beneath the tree, or acorns under the storm-shaken oak.", "사방에 내 백성들이 나무 아래 지나치게 익은 사과처럼, 혹은 폭풍에 흔들린 떡갈나무 아래 도토리처럼 흩어져 누워 있었소."], ["You see yonder a temple on the height.", "저기 높은 곳에 신전이 하나 보일 것이오."], ["It is sacred to Jupiter.", "그것은 제우스에게 바쳐진 것이오."], ["O how many offered prayers there, husbands for wives, fathers for sons, and died in the very act of supplication!", "오, 얼마나 많은 이들이 그곳에서 기도를 올렸던가, 남편들은 아내를 위해, 아버지들은 아들을 위해, 그러다가 바로 그 탄원의 행위 가운데 죽어 갔소!"], ["How often, while the priest made ready for sacrifice, the victim fell, struck down by disease without waiting for the blow!", "사제가 제사를 준비하는 동안에, 제물로 바칠 짐승이 일격을 기다리지도 않고 병에 쓰러진 일이 얼마나 잦았던가!"], ["At length all reverence for sacred things was lost.", "마침내 신성한 것들에 대한 온갖 경의도 사라지고 말았소."], ["Bodies were thrown out unburied, wood was wanting for funeral piles, men fought with one another for the possession of them.", "주검들이 묻히지 못한 채 내던져졌고, 화장용 장작이 모자라 사람들이 그것을 차지하려고 서로 싸웠소."], ["Finally there were none left to mourn; sons and husbands, old men and youths, perished alike unlamented.", "끝내는 슬퍼해 줄 이마저 남지 않았으니, 아들과 남편, 노인과 젊은이가 한결같이 애도받지 못한 채 죽어 갔소."]], [["“Standing before the altar I raised my eyes to heaven.", "\"제단 앞에 서서 나는 하늘로 눈을 들었소. '오, 제우스시여,'"], ["‘O Jupiter,’ I said, ‘if thou art indeed my father, and art not ashamed of thy offspring, give me back my people, or take me also away!’ At these words a clap of thunder was heard.", "나는 말했소. '당신이 정녕 제 아버지이시고, 당신의 자식을 부끄러워하지 않으신다면, 제 백성을 돌려주시거나, 아니면 저마저 데려가 주소서!'"], ["‘I accept the omen,’ I cried; ‘O may it be a sign of a favorable disposition towards me!’ By chance there grew by the place where I stood an oak with wide-spreading branches, sacred to Jupiter.", "이 말이 끝나자 천둥소리가 한 번 울렸소. '이 징조를 받아들이겠나이다,'"], ["I observed a troop of ants busy with their labor, carrying minute grains in their mouths and following one another in a line up the trunk of the tree.", "나는 외쳤소. '오, 부디 이것이 제게 호의를 베푸시려는 표시이기를!'"], ["Observing their numbers with admiration, I said, ‘Give me, O father, citizens as numerous as these, and replenish my empty city.’ The tree shook and gave a rustling sound with its branches, though no wind agitated them.", "마침 내가 서 있던 자리 곁에는 가지를 넓게 펼친 떡갈나무 한 그루가 자라고 있었는데, 제우스에게 바쳐진 것이었소. 나는 개미 떼가 부지런히 일하며 작은 알갱이를 입에 물고 나무 둥치를 따라 한 줄로 서로를 뒤따라 오르는 것을 보았소."], ["I trembled in every limb, yet I kissed the earth and the tree.", "그 수에 감탄하며 나는 말했소. '오, 아버지시여, 제게 이만큼이나 많은 시민을 주시어, 텅 빈 제 도시를 다시 채워 주소서.'"], ["I would not confess to myself that I hoped, yet I did hope.", "나무가 흔들리더니, 바람 한 점 가지를 흔들지 않는데도 가지에서 바스락거리는 소리가 났소."], ["Night came on and sleep took possession of my frame oppressed with cares.", "나는 온몸의 사지가 떨렸으나, 그래도 땅과 나무에 입을 맞추었소."], ["The tree stood before me in my dreams, with its numerous branches all covered with living, moving creatures.", "나는 내가 희망을 품고 있다는 것을 나 자신에게도 인정하려 하지 않았으나, 그래도 나는 희망을 품었소. 밤이 찾아오자, 근심에 짓눌린 내 몸을 잠이 사로잡았소."], ["It seemed to shake its limbs and throw down over the ground a multitude of those industrious grain-gathering animals, which appeared to gain in size, and grow larger and larger, and by and by to stand erect, lay aside their superfluous legs and their black color, and finally to assume the human form.", "꿈속에서 그 나무가 내 앞에 서 있었는데, 그 수많은 가지가 온통 살아 움직이는 생물들로 뒤덮여 있었소. 나무가 가지를 흔들어 그 부지런히 알갱이를 모으는 짐승들의 무리를 땅 위로 떨어뜨리는 듯했고, 그것들은 몸집이 불어나 점점 더 커지더니, 이윽고 똑바로 서서 남아도는 다리와 검은 빛깔을 벗어 버리고, 마침내 사람의 모습을 띠는 듯했소."], ["Then I awoke, and my first impulse was to chide the gods who had robbed me of a sweet vision and given me no reality in its place.", "그때 나는 잠에서 깨어났고, 내 첫 충동은 달콤한 환영을 빼앗아 가고 그 자리에 아무런 실체도 주지 않은 신들을 꾸짖는 것이었소."], ["Being still in the temple, my attention was caught by the sound of many voices without; a sound of late unusual to my ears.", "아직 신전 안에 있던 차에, 신전 밖에서 들려오는 수많은 목소리에 내 주의가 쏠렸소. 근래에 내 귀에는 낯선 소리였소."], ["While I began to think I was yet dreaming, Telamon, my son, throwing open the temple gates, exclaimed: ‘Father, approach, and behold things surpassing even your hopes!’ I went forth; I saw a multitude of men, such as I had seen in my dream, and they were passing in procession in the same manner.", "내가 아직도 꿈을 꾸고 있는 것이 아닌가 생각하기 시작하던 차에, 내 아들 텔라몬이 신전 문을 활짝 열어젖히며 외쳤소. '아버지, 가까이 오셔서, 아버지의 희망마저 능가하는 것들을 보소서!'"], ["While I gazed with wonder and delight they approached and kneeling hailed me as their king.", "나는 밖으로 나갔소. 꿈에서 보았던 것과 똑같은 사람들의 무리가 보였고, 그들은 똑같은 모양으로 줄지어 지나가고 있었소."], ["I paid my vows to Jove, and proceeded to allot the vacant city to the new-born race, and to parcel out the fields among them.", "내가 놀라움과 기쁨에 잠겨 바라보는 동안 그들은 다가와 무릎을 꿇고 나를 자신들의 왕으로 맞이했소. 나는 제우스께 서원을 갚고, 텅 빈 도시를 이 새로 태어난 종족에게 나누어 주고, 들판을 그들 사이에 갈라 주는 일에 착수했소."], ["I called them Myrmidons, from the ant (myrmex) from which they sprang.", "나는 그들이 솟아 나온 개미(미르멕스)를 따서 그들을 미르미돈족이라 불렀소."], ["You have seen these persons; their dispositions resemble those which they had in their former shape.", "당신은 이 사람들을 보았소. 그들의 기질은 예전 모습에서 지녔던 것을 그대로 닮았소."], ["They are a diligent and industrious race, eager to gain, and tenacious of their gains.", "그들은 부지런하고 근면한 종족이며, 얻는 데 열심이고 얻은 것을 굳게 지키오."], ["Among them you may recruit your forces.", "그들 가운데서 당신은 병력을 보충할 수 있을 것이오."], ["They will follow you to the war, young in years and bold in heart.”", "그들은 나이는 젊으나 마음은 담대하여 당신을 따라 전장으로 갈 것이오.\""]], [["This description of the plague is copied by Ovid from the account which Thucydides, the Greek historian, gives of the plague of Athens.", "이 역병 묘사는 그리스 역사가 투키디데스가 아테나이의 역병에 관해 전하는 기록에서 오비디우스가 베껴 온 것이다."], ["The historian drew from life, and all the poets and writers of fiction since his day, when they have had occasion to describe a similar scene, have borrowed their details from him.", "그 역사가는 실제 삶에서 끌어왔고, 그 이후의 모든 시인과 소설가들은 비슷한 장면을 묘사할 일이 있을 때면 그에게서 세부를 빌려 왔다."]], [["————", "————"]], [["# NISUS AND SCYLLA", "# NISUS AND SCYLLA"]], [["MINOS, king of Crete, made war upon Megara.", "크레타의 왕 미노스가 메가라를 상대로 전쟁을 일으켰다."], ["Nisus was king of Megara, and Scylla was his daughter.", "니소스는 메가라의 왕이었고, 스킬라는 그의 딸이었다."], ["The siege had now lasted six months and the city still held out, for it was decreed by fate that it should not be taken so long as a certain purple lock, which glittered among the hair of King Nisus, remained on his head.", "공성전은 이제 여섯 달째에 접어들었으나 도시는 여전히 버티고 있었으니, 이는 니소스 왕의 머리카락 사이에서 반짝이는 어떤 자줏빛 머리타래가 그의 머리에 붙어 있는 한 도시는 함락되지 않으리라는 운명의 정해진 바였기 때문이다."], ["There was a tower on the city walls, which overlooked the plain where Minos and his army were encamped.", "도시의 성벽에는 망루가 하나 있었는데, 그곳에서는 미노스와 그의 군대가 진을 친 평원이 내려다보였다."], ["To this tower Scylla used to repair, and look abroad over the tents of the hostile army.", "스킬라는 자주 이 망루로 올라가 적군의 천막들을 두루 살펴보곤 했다."], ["The siege had lasted so long that she had learned to distinguish the persons of the leaders.", "공성전이 워낙 오래 이어졌던 까닭에 그녀는 적장들의 모습을 분간할 수 있을 정도가 되었다."], ["Minos, in particular, excited her admiration.", "그중에서도 미노스가 유독 그녀의 흠모를 자아냈다."], ["Arrayed in his helmet, and bearing his shield, she admired his graceful deportment; if he threw his javelin skill seemed combined with force in the discharge; if he drew his bow Apollo himself could not have done it more gracefully.", "투구를 쓰고 방패를 든 그의 우아한 거동을 그녀는 찬탄했다. 그가 투창을 던지면 그 동작에는 힘과 더불어 솜씨가 어우러진 듯했고, 그가 활을 당기면 아폴론 자신이라 한들 그보다 더 우아하게 해내지는 못했을 것이다."], ["But when he laid aside his helmet, and in his purple robes bestrode his white horse with its gay caparisons, and reined in its foaming mouth, the daughter of Nisus was hardly mistress of herself; she was almost frantic with admiration.", "그러나 그가 투구를 벗어 놓고, 자줏빛 의복을 걸친 채 화려한 마구를 두른 백마에 올라타 거품 무는 입에 고삐를 죄었을 때, 니소스의 딸은 자기 자신을 거의 가눌 수가 없었으니, 흠모에 겨워 거의 정신이 나간 지경이었다."], ["She envied the weapon that he grasped, the reins that he held.", "그녀는 그가 움켜쥔 무기를, 그가 잡은 고삐를 부러워했다."], ["She felt as if she could, if it were possible, go to him through the hostile ranks; she felt an impulse to cast herself down from the tower into the midst of his camp, or to open the gates to him, or to do anything else, so only it might gratify Minos.", "그녀는 가능하기만 하다면 적진을 가로질러 그에게로 갈 수 있을 것만 같은 심정이었다. 그녀는 망루에서 그의 진영 한가운데로 몸을 던지거나, 그에게 성문을 열어 주거나, 그 밖에 무슨 일이든 오로지 미노스를 기쁘게 할 수만 있다면 해 버리고 싶은 충동에 사로잡혔다."], ["As she sat in the tower, she talked thus with herself: “I know not whether to rejoice or grieve at this sad war.", "망루에 앉아 그녀는 이렇게 혼잣말을 했다. \"이 슬픈 전쟁을 두고 기뻐해야 할지 슬퍼해야 할지 모르겠구나."], ["I grieve that Minos is our enemy; but I rejoice at any cause that brings him to my sight.", "미노스가 우리의 적이라는 사실은 슬프지만, 그를 내 눈앞에 데려다주는 어떤 까닭에 대해서는 기쁘다."], ["Perhaps he would be willing to grant us peace, and receive me as a hostage.", "어쩌면 그는 기꺼이 우리에게 평화를 베풀고, 나를 인질로 받아 줄지도 모른다."], ["I would fly down, if I could, and alight in his camp, and tell him that we yield ourselves to his mercy.", "할 수만 있다면 나는 날아 내려가 그의 진영에 내려앉아, 우리가 그의 자비에 우리 자신을 맡긴다고 그에게 말하고 싶구나."], ["But then, to betray my father! No!", "하지만 그렇게 되면 내 아버지를 배반하는 것이 아닌가!"], ["rather would I never see Minos again.", "안 된다! 차라리 다시는 미노스를 보지 않으리라."], ["And yet no doubt it is sometimes the best thing for a city to be conquered, when the conqueror is clement and generous.", "그러나 정복자가 너그럽고 관대하다면, 도시가 정복당하는 편이 때로는 최선일 수도 있음은 의심의 여지가 없다."], ["Minos certainly has right on his side.", "미노스는 분명 정의를 자기편에 두고 있다."], ["I think we shall be conquered; and if that must be the end of it, why should not love unbar the gates to him, instead of leaving it to be done by war?", "내 생각에 우리는 정복당하고 말 것이다. 그리고 만일 그것이 끝이어야 한다면, 어찌하여 전쟁이 그 일을 하도록 내버려 두는 대신, 사랑이 그에게 성문의 빗장을 풀어 주지 않겠는가?"], ["Better spare delay and slaughter if we can.", "할 수만 있다면 지연과 살육을 면하는 편이 낫다."], ["And O if any one should wound or kill Minos!", "아, 누군가 미노스를 상하게 하거나 죽이기라도 한다면!"], ["No one surely would have the heart to do it; yet ignorantly, not knowing him, one might.", "분명 그럴 마음을 먹을 자는 아무도 없으리라. 그러나 그를 알아보지 못한 채 무심코 그리할 수도 있겠지."], ["I will, I will surrender myself to him, with my country as a dowry, and so put an end to the war.", "나는, 나는 내 조국을 지참금으로 삼아 그에게 나 자신을 바치고, 그리하여 이 전쟁을 끝내리라."], ["But how?", "그러나 어떻게?"], ["The gates are guarded, and my father keeps the keys; he only stands in my way.", "성문은 지켜지고 있고, 열쇠는 내 아버지가 쥐고 있다. 오직 그분만이 내 앞을 막고 있구나."], ["O that it might please the gods to take him away!", "아, 신들께서 그분을 데려가 주신다면 좋으련만!"], ["But why ask the gods to do it?", "그러나 어찌하여 신들께 그 일을 청하겠는가?"], ["Another woman, loving as I do, would remove with her own hands whatever stood in the way of her love.", "나처럼 사랑에 빠진 다른 여인이라면, 제 사랑을 가로막는 것이 무엇이든 제 손으로 치워 버렸으리라."], ["And can any other woman dare more than I?", "그리고 어떤 다른 여인이 감히 나보다 더한 일을 할 수 있겠는가?"], ["I would encounter fire and sword to gain my object; but here there is no need of fire and sword.", "나는 내 목적을 이루기 위해서라면 불과 칼이라도 무릅쓰리라. 그러나 여기에는 불도 칼도 필요 없다."], ["I only need my father’s purple lock.", "내게 필요한 것은 오직 내 아버지의 자줏빛 머리타래뿐이다."], ["More precious than gold to me, that will give me all I wish.”", "내게는 황금보다도 더 귀한 그것이, 내가 바라는 모든 것을 가져다주리라.\""]], [["While she thus reasoned night came on, and soon the whole palace was buried in sleep.", "이렇게 궁리하는 동안 밤이 찾아왔고, 이윽고 온 궁궐이 잠 속에 묻혔다."], ["She entered her father’s bedchamber and cut off the fatal lock; then passed out of the city and entered the enemy’s camp.", "그녀는 아버지의 침실로 들어가 그 운명의 머리타래를 잘라 냈다. 그러고는 도시를 빠져나와 적진으로 들어갔다."], ["She demanded to be led to the king, and thus addressed him: “I am Scylla, the daughter of Nisus.", "그녀는 왕에게로 안내해 줄 것을 요구하고, 그에게 이렇게 말했다. \"저는 니소스의 딸 스킬라입니다."], ["I surrender to you my country and my father’s house.", "저는 제 조국과 제 아버지의 집을 당신께 바칩니다."], ["I ask no reward but yourself; for love of you I have done it.", "저는 당신 자신 외에 어떤 보답도 바라지 않습니다. 당신을 사랑하는 마음에서 이 일을 한 것입니다."], ["See here the purple lock!", "여기 이 자줏빛 머리타래를 보십시오!"], ["With this I give you my father and his kingdom.”", "이것과 더불어 저는 당신께 제 아버지와 그의 왕국을 드립니다.\""], ["She held out her hand with the fatal spoil.", "그녀는 그 운명의 전리품을 손에 내밀었다."], ["Minos shrunk back and refused to touch it.", "미노스는 뒷걸음질하며 그것에 손대기를 거부했다."], ["“The gods destroy thee, infamous woman,”", "\"신들께서 너를 멸하시기를, 이 파렴치한 여인아.\""], ["he exclaimed; “disgrace of our time!", "그가 외쳤다. \"우리 시대의 치욕이로다!"], ["May neither earth nor sea yield thee a resting-place!", "땅도 바다도 네게 쉴 자리를 내주지 않기를!"], ["Surely, my Crete, where Jove himself was cradled, shall not be polluted with such a monster!”", "분명, 제우스 자신이 요람에 누였던 나의 크레타가 그런 괴물로 더럽혀져서는 안 될 것이다!\""], ["Thus he said, and gave orders that equitable terms should be allowed to the conquered city, and that the fleet should immediately sail from the island.", "이렇게 말하고서 그는 정복당한 도시에 공정한 조건을 허락하라고, 또한 함대가 즉시 그 섬을 떠나 출항하라고 명령을 내렸다."]], [["Scylla was frantic.", "스킬라는 미친 듯이 부르짖었다."], ["“Ungrateful man,”", "\"은혜를 모르는 사내여,\""], ["she exclaimed, “is it thus you leave me?—me who have given you victory,—who have sacrificed for you parent and country!", "그녀가 외쳤다. \"이렇게 나를 버리고 가는가?"], ["I am guilty, I confess, and deserve to die, but not by your hand.”", "그대에게 승리를 안겨 준 나를, 그대를 위해 어버이와 조국을 희생한 나를!"], ["As the ships left the shore, she leaped into the water, and seizing the rudder of the one which carried Minos, she was borne along an unwelcome companion of their course.", "나는 죄를 지었으며, 그것을 인정하고, 죽어 마땅하지만, 그대의 손에 의해서는 아니다.\" 배들이 해안을 떠나가자, 그녀는 물속으로 뛰어들어 미노스를 태운 배의 키를 붙들었고, 그리하여 반갑지 않은 동행자가 되어 그들의 항로를 따라 끌려갔다."], ["A sea-eagle soaring aloft,—it was her father who had been changed into that form,—seeing her, pounced down upon her, and struck her with his beak and claws.", "높이 솟구쳐 날던 바다수리 한 마리가, 그것은 그 모습으로 변한 그녀의 아버지였는데, 그녀를 보고는 덮쳐 내려와 부리와 발톱으로 그녀를 후려쳤다."], ["In terror she let go the ship and would have fallen into the water, but some pitying deity changed her into a bird.", "두려움에 사로잡힌 그녀는 배를 놓치고 물속으로 떨어질 뻔했으나, 가엾이 여긴 어떤 신이 그녀를 새로 바꾸어 놓았다."], ["The sea-eagle still cherishes the old animosity; and whenever he espies her in his lofty flight you may see him dart down upon her, with beak and claws, to take vengeance for the ancient crime.", "바다수리는 여전히 옛 원한을 품고 있으니, 높이 날아오르다가 그녀를 발견할 때마다, 그가 부리와 발톱으로 그녀를 향해 내리꽂으며 그 오래된 죄에 복수하려는 광경을 그대는 볼 수 있을 것이다."]], [["# ECHO AND NARCISSUS", "# 제21장"]], [["Echo was a beautiful nymph, fond of the woods and hills, where she devoted herself to woodland sports.", "에코는 숲과 언덕을 좋아하는 아름다운 님프였으며, 그곳에서 숲속의 놀이에 몰두했다."], ["She was a favorite of Diana, and attended her in the chase.", "그녀는 아르테미스[로마의 〈디아나〉]의 총애를 받았고, 사냥에서 여신을 수행했다."], ["But Echo had one failing; she was fond of talking, and whether in chat or argument, would have the last word.", "그러나 에코에게는 한 가지 결점이 있었으니, 그녀는 말하기를 좋아하여, 한담을 나누든 논쟁을 벌이든 마지막 말을 차지하려 들었다."], ["One day Juno was seeking her husband, who, she had reason to fear, was amusing himself among the nymphs.", "어느 날 헤라[로마의 〈유노〉]가 남편을 찾고 있었는데, 그가 님프들 틈에서 즐기고 있으리라 의심할 만한 이유가 있었기 때문이다."], ["Echo by her talk contrived to detain the goddess till the nymphs made their escape.", "에코는 자신의 수다로 여신을 붙잡아 두는 데 성공하여, 그동안 님프들이 달아나도록 했다. 헤라가 이를 알아차렸을 때, 그녀는 에코에게 이런 말로 선고를 내렸다."], ["When Juno discovered it, she passed sentence upon Echo in these words: “You shall forfeit the use of that tongue with which you have cheated me, except for that one purpose you are so fond of—reply.", "\"너는 나를 속인 그 혀의 쓰임을 빼앗기리라. 다만 네가 그토록 좋아하는 그 한 가지 목적, 곧 대꾸하는 일에서만은 예외다."], ["You shall still have the last word, but no power to speak first.”", "너는 여전히 마지막 말을 차지하겠으나, 먼저 말할 능력은 없으리라.\""]], [["This nymph saw Narcissus, a beautiful youth, as he pursued the chase upon the mountains.", "이 님프는 산속에서 사냥을 쫓던 아름다운 청년 나르키소스를 보았다."], ["She loved him and followed his footsteps.", "그녀는 그를 사랑하여 그의 발자취를 뒤따랐다."], ["O how she longed to address him in the softest accents, and win him to converse!", "아, 가장 부드러운 어조로 그에게 말을 건네어 그를 대화로 이끌어 들이고 싶은 마음이 얼마나 간절했던가!"], ["but it was not in her power.", "그러나 그것은 그녀의 능력 밖이었다."], ["She waited with impatience for him to speak first, and had her answer ready.", "그녀는 그가 먼저 말해 주기를 애타게 기다리며, 대답을 준비해 두었다."], ["One day the youth, being separated from his companions, shouted aloud, “Who’s here?”", "어느 날 동료들과 떨어진 그 청년이 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"거기 누구 있느냐?\""], ["Echo replied, “Here.”", "에코가 대답했다. \"있느냐.\""], ["Narcissus looked around, but seeing no one called out, “Come.”", "나르키소스가 주위를 둘러보았으나 아무도 보이지 않자 다시 외쳤다. \"이리 오라.\""], ["Echo answered, “Come.”", "에코가 답했다. \"오라.\""], ["As no one came, Narcissus called again, “Why do you shun me?”", "아무도 오지 않자 나르키소스가 다시 불렀다. \"어찌하여 나를 피하느냐?\""], ["Echo asked the same question.", "에코도 같은 물음을 던졌다."], ["“Let us join one another,”", "\"우리 함께 어울리자.\""], ["said the youth.", "청년이 말했다."], ["The maid answered with all her heart in the same words, and hastened to the spot, ready to throw her arms about his neck.", "처녀는 온 마음을 담아 같은 말로 대답하고는, 그의 목을 끌어안을 준비를 한 채 그 자리로 달려갔다."], ["He started back, exclaiming, “Hands off!", "그는 뒷걸음질하며 외쳤다. \"손을 떼라!"], ["I would rather die than you should have me!”", "네가 나를 차지하느니 차라리 죽는 편이 낫겠다!\""], ["“Have me,”", "\"나를 차지하라.\""], ["said she; but it was all in vain.", "그녀가 말했다. 그러나 모두 헛일이었다."], ["He left her, and she went to hide her blushes in the recesses of the woods.", "그가 그녀를 떠나가자, 그녀는 붉어진 뺨을 숨기러 숲의 깊은 곳으로 들어갔다."], ["From that time forth she lived in caves and among mountain cliffs.", "그때부터 그녀는 동굴 속과 산속 벼랑 사이에서 살았다."], ["Her form faded with grief, till at last all her flesh shrank away.", "그녀의 몸은 슬픔으로 시들어 갔고, 마침내 그 살이 모두 오그라들어 사라졌다."], ["Her bones were changed into rocks and there was nothing left of her but her voice.", "그녀의 뼈는 바위로 바뀌었고, 그녀에게서 남은 것은 오직 목소리뿐이었다."], ["With that she is still ready to reply to any one who calls her, and keeps up her old habit of having the last word.", "그 목소리로 그녀는 지금도 자기를 부르는 누구에게나 대답할 준비가 되어 있으며, 마지막 말을 차지하는 옛 버릇을 이어 가고 있다."]], [["Narcissus’s cruelty in this case was not the only instance.", "이 일에서 나르키소스가 보인 매정함은 이것이 유일한 경우가 아니었다."], ["He shunned all the rest of the nymphs, as he had done poor Echo.", "그는 가엾은 에코에게 그러했듯이 다른 모든 님프들도 멀리했다."], ["One day a maiden who had in vain endeavored to attract him uttered a prayer that he might some time or other feel what it was to love and meet no return of affection.", "어느 날 그를 끌어들이려 헛되이 애쓰던 한 처녀가, 그도 언젠가는 사랑을 하되 그 애정에 아무런 보답도 받지 못하는 것이 무엇인지 느끼게 해 달라는 기도를 올렸다."], ["The avenging goddess heard and granted the prayer.", "복수의 여신이 그 기도를 듣고 들어주었다."]], [["There was a clear fountain, with water like silver, to which the shepherds never drove their flocks, nor the mountain goats resorted, nor any of the beasts of the forest; neither was it defaced with fallen leaves or branches; but the grass grew fresh around it, and the rocks sheltered it from the sun.", "은빛 같은 물이 솟는 맑은 샘이 하나 있었으니, 목동들은 그곳으로 가축을 몰지 않았고, 산양들도 찾아들지 않았으며, 숲의 어떤 짐승도 오지 않았다. 또한 그곳은 떨어진 잎이나 가지로 더럽혀지지도 않았으니, 다만 그 둘레로는 풀이 싱그럽게 자랐고, 바위들이 햇볕을 가려 주었다."], ["Hither came one day the youth, fatigued with hunting, heated and thirsty.", "어느 날 사냥에 지치고 몸이 달아오르고 목이 마른 그 청년이 이곳으로 왔다."], ["He stooped down to drink, and saw his own image in the water; he thought it was some beautiful water-spirit living in the fountain.", "그는 물을 마시려 몸을 굽혔다가 물속에 비친 자신의 모습을 보았다. 그는 그것이 샘 속에 사는 어떤 아름다운 물의 정령이라고 생각했다."], ["He stood gazing with admiration at those bright eyes, those locks curled like the locks of Bacchus or Apollo, the rounded cheeks, the ivory neck, the parted lips, and the glow of health and exercise over all.", "그는 그 빛나는 눈을, 디오니소스[로마의 〈바쿠스〉]나 아폴론의 머리타래처럼 곱슬곱슬한 머리카락을, 둥근 뺨을, 상아 같은 목을, 살짝 벌어진 입술을, 그리고 그 모든 것에 어린 건강과 운동의 발그레한 빛을 흠모하며 바라보고 서 있었다."], ["He fell in love with himself.", "그는 자기 자신과 사랑에 빠졌다."], ["He brought his lips near to take a kiss; he plunged his arms in to embrace the beloved object.", "그는 입을 맞추려 입술을 가까이 가져갔고, 사랑하는 대상을 끌어안으려 두 팔을 물속에 담갔다."], ["It fled at the touch, but returned again after a moment and renewed the fascination.", "그것은 손길이 닿자 달아났으나, 잠시 후 다시 돌아와 그 매혹을 새로이 했다."], ["He could not tear himself away; he lost all thought of food or rest, while he hovered over the brink of the fountain gazing upon his own image.", "그는 스스로를 떼어 놓을 수가 없었다. 그는 자신의 모습을 바라보며 샘가에 머무는 동안 음식이나 휴식에 대한 모든 생각을 잃어버렸다."], ["He talked with the supposed spirit: “Why, beautiful being, do you shun me?", "그는 그 정령이라 여긴 것과 이야기를 나누었다. \"어찌하여, 아름다운 존재여, 그대는 나를 피하는가?"], ["Surely my face is not one to repel you.", "분명 내 얼굴은 그대를 물리칠 그런 얼굴이 아니다."], ["The nymphs love me, and you yourself look not indifferent upon me.", "님프들은 나를 사랑하며, 그대 자신도 나를 무심하게 바라보지 않는다."], ["When I stretch forth my arms you do the same; and you smile upon me and answer my beckonings with the like.”", "내가 두 팔을 내밀면 그대도 그리하고, 그대는 내게 미소 짓고 내 손짓에 같은 손짓으로 답한다.\""], ["His tears fell into the water and disturbed the image.", "그의 눈물이 물속으로 떨어져 그 모습을 흩뜨렸다."], ["As he saw it depart, he exclaimed, “Stay, I entreat you!", "그것이 사라지는 것을 보자 그가 외쳤다. \"머물러 다오, 그대에게 간청하노라!"], ["Let me at least gaze upon you, if I may not touch you.”", "그대를 만질 수 없을지언정, 적어도 그대를 바라볼 수 있게 해 다오.\""], ["With this, and much more of the same kind, he cherished the flame that consumed him, so that by degrees he lost his color, his vigor, and the beauty which formerly had so charmed the nymph Echo.", "이런 말과 함께 그와 같은 종류의 수많은 말로, 그는 자신을 갉아먹는 그 불꽃을 어루만졌으며, 그리하여 점차 그 안색과 활력을, 그리고 예전에 님프 에코를 그토록 매혹했던 아름다움을 잃어 갔다."], ["She kept near him, however, and when he exclaimed, “Alas!", "그러나 그녀는 여전히 그의 곁에 머물렀고, 그가 \"아아!"], ["alas!”", "아아!\""], ["she answered him with the same words.", "하고 외치면 그녀도 같은 말로 그에게 답했다."], ["He pined away and died; and when his shade passed the Stygian river, it leaned over the boat to catch a look of itself in the waters.", "그는 시들어 죽고 말았으니, 그의 혼령이 스틱스의 강을 건널 때 그것은 물속에 비친 제 모습을 보려고 배 위로 몸을 기울였다."], ["The nymphs mourned for him, especially the water-nymphs; and when they smote their breasts Echo smote hers also.", "님프들이 그를 애도했으니, 특히 물의 님프들이 그러했다. 그리고 그들이 가슴을 칠 때 에코도 자기 가슴을 쳤다."], ["They prepared a funeral pile and would have burned the body, but it was nowhere to be found; but in its place a flower, purple within, and surrounded with white leaves, which bears the name and preserves the memory of Narcissus.", "그들은 장례용 장작더미를 마련하여 그 시신을 태우려 했으나, 시신은 어디에서도 찾을 수 없었다. 그러나 그 자리에는 안쪽이 자줏빛이고 흰 잎으로 둘러싸인 꽃 한 송이가 피어 있었으니, 그 꽃은 나르키소스의 이름을 지니고 그의 기억을 간직하고 있다."]], [["Milton alludes to the story of Echo and Narcissus in the Lady’s song in “Comus.”", "밀턴은 「코머스」에 나오는 〈귀부인〉의 노래에서 에코와 나르키소스의 이야기를 넌지시 끌어들인다."], ["She is seeking her brothers in the forest, and sings to attract their attention:", "그녀는 숲속에서 오라비들을 찾으며, 그들의 주의를 끌기 위해 노래한다."]], [["“Sweet Echo, sweetest nymph, that liv’st unseen Within thy aëry shell By slow Meander’s margent green, And in the violet-embroidered vale, Where the love-lorn nightingale Nightly to thee her sad song mourneth well; Canst thou not tell me of a gentle pair That likest thy Narcissus are?", "\"고운 에코여, 가장 고운 님프여, 그대는 보이지 않게 살고 있구나 그대의 공기 같은 껍질 속에서 느릿한 마이안드로스의 푸른 강기슭에서, 그리고 제비꽃으로 수놓인 골짜기에서, 사랑에 버림받은 나이팅게일이 밤마다 그대에게 제 슬픈 노래를 곱게 애도하는 그곳에서. 그대는 내게 일러 주지 못하는가, 그대의 나르키소스를 가장 닮은 어느 다정한 한 쌍을?"], ["O, if thou have Hid them in some flowery cave, Tell me but where, Sweet queen of parly, daughter of the sphere, So may’st thou be translated to the skies, And give resounding grace to all heaven’s harmonies.”", "아, 만일 그대가 그들을 어느 꽃 핀 동굴 속에 숨겨 두었거든, 다만 어디인지 일러 다오, 대화의 고운 여왕이여, 천구(天球)의 딸이여, 그리하면 그대는 하늘로 옮겨져 온 천상의 화음에 울려 퍼지는 우아함을 더하리라.\""]], [["Milton has imitated the story of Narcissus in the account which he makes Eve give of the first sight of herself reflected in the fountain:", "밀턴은 이브가 샘에 비친 자기 모습을 처음 보았던 일을 이야기하게 하는 대목에서 나르키소스의 이야기를 본떴다."]], [["“That day I oft remember when from sleep I first awaked, and found myself reposed Under a shade on flowers, much wondering where And what I was, whence thither brought, and how.", "\"잠에서 처음 깨어나 꽃 위 그늘 아래 쉬고 있는 나 자신을 발견하고서, 내가 어디에 있는지, 무엇인지, 어디서 그리로 데려와졌는지, 또 어떻게 그리되었는지 무척이나 의아해하던 그날을 나는 자주 떠올린다."], ["Not distant far from thence a murmuring sound Of waters issued from a cave, and spread Into a liquid plain, then stood unmoved Pure as the expanse of heaven; I thither went With unexperienced thought, and laid me down On the green bank, to look into the clear Smooth lake that to me seemed another sky.", "그곳에서 멀지 않은 곳에서 물이 졸졸 흐르는 소리가 동굴에서 흘러나와 펼쳐지더니 잔잔한 평원을 이루고는, 이윽고 하늘의 넓은 폭처럼 맑게 미동도 없이 멈추어 있었다. 나는 그리로 갔으니 아직 무엇도 겪어 보지 못한 생각으로, 그 푸른 둔덕에 몸을 누이고서, 내게는 또 하나의 하늘처럼 보였던 그 맑고 매끄러운 호수를 들여다보았다."], ["As I bent down to look, just opposite A shape within the watery gleam appeared, Bending to look on me.", "들여다보려 몸을 굽히자, 바로 맞은편 물빛 어른거림 속에 한 형상이 나타나, 나를 보려 몸을 굽혔다."], ["I started back; It started back; but pleased I soon returned, Pleased it returned as soon with answering looks Of sympathy and love.", "나는 뒷걸음질했고, 그것도 뒷걸음질했다. 그러나 기뻐하며 곧 돌아갔더니, 그것도 그만큼 기뻐하며 공감과 사랑의 화답하는 눈길로 이내 돌아왔다."], ["There had I fixed Mine eyes till now, and pined with vain desire, Had not a voice thus warned me: ‘What thou seest, What there thou seest, fair creature, is thyself;’”", "그곳에 나는 지금까지 내 눈을 붙박고서, 헛된 갈망에 시들었으리라, 한 목소리가 이렇게 나를 일깨우지 않았더라면. '그대가 보는 것은, 그곳에 그대가 보는 것은, 아름다운 피조물이여, 그대 자신이로다.'\""], ["etc.", "등등."], ["—Paradise Lost, Book IV.", "— 「실낙원」 제4권."]], [["No one of the fables of antiquity has been oftener alluded to by the poets than that of Narcissus.", "고대의 그 어떤 우화도 나르키소스의 이야기만큼 시인들에 의해 자주 인용된 것은 없다."], ["Here are two epigrams which treat it in different ways.", "여기 그것을 서로 다른 방식으로 다룬 두 편의 경구시가 있다."], ["The first is by Goldsmith:", "첫 번째 것은 골드스미스의 작품이다."]], [["“ON A BEAUTIFUL YOUTH, STRUCK BLIND BY LIGHTNING “Sure ’twas by Providence designed, Rather in pity than in hate, That he should be like Cupid blind, To save him from Narcissus’ fate.”", "\"벼락에 맞아 눈먼 한 아름다운 청년에 대하여\" \"분명 그것은 섭리가 마련한 것이었으니, 미움에서라기보다 차라리 가엾이 여김에서, 그가 쿠피도[에로스]처럼 눈멀게 된 것은, 나르키소스의 운명에서 그를 구하기 위함이었네.\""]], [["The other is by Cowper:", "다른 하나는 카우퍼의 작품이다."]], [["“ON AN UGLY FELLOW “Beware, my friend, of crystal brook Or fountain, lest that hideous hook, Thy nose, thou chance to see; Narcissus’ fate would then be thine, And self-detested thou would’st pine, As self-enamoured he.”", "\"한 못생긴 사내에 대하여\" \"벗이여, 수정 같은 시냇물이나 샘을 조심하게, 그 흉측한 갈고리, 곧 그대의 코를 우연히 보지 않도록. 그러면 나르키소스의 운명이 그대의 것이 되리니, 스스로에게 반한 그가 시들었듯이, 그대는 스스로를 혐오하며 시들어 가리라.\""]], [["Clytie was a water-nymph and in love with Apollo, who made her no return.", "클리티에는 물의 님프로 아폴론을 사랑했으나, 아폴론은 그녀에게 아무런 보답도 하지 않았다."], ["So she pined away, sitting all day long upon the cold ground, with her unbound tresses streaming over her shoulders.", "그리하여 그녀는 시들어 갔으니, 풀어헤친 머리타래를 두 어깨 위로 흘러내린 채, 차가운 땅 위에 온종일 앉아 있었다."], ["Nine days she sat and tasted neither food nor drink, her own tears and the chilly dew her only food.", "아흐레 동안 그녀는 앉아서 음식도 음료도 입에 대지 않았고, 자신의 눈물과 싸늘한 이슬만이 유일한 양식이었다."], ["She gazed on the sun when he rose, and as he passed through his daily course to his setting; she saw no other object, her face turned constantly on him.", "그녀는 해가 떠오를 때 그를 바라보았고, 그가 날마다 가는 길을 따라 저물 때까지도 그러했다. 그녀는 다른 어떤 것도 보지 않았으니, 얼굴을 끊임없이 그에게로 향한 채였다."], ["At last, they say, her limbs rooted in the ground, her face became a flower[13] which turns on its stem so as always to face the sun throughout its daily course; for it retains to that extent the feeling of the nymph from whom it sprang.", "마침내, 사람들이 말하기를, 그녀의 사지는 땅에 뿌리를 내렸고, 그녀의 얼굴은 한 송이 꽃[13]이 되었으니, 그 꽃은 줄기 위에서 돌며 해가 날마다 가는 길 내내 언제나 해를 향한다. 그것이 그만큼은 자신이 솟아난 그 님프의 마음을 간직하고 있기 때문이다."]], [["Hood, in his “Flowers,” thus alludes to Clytie:", "후드는 그의 「꽃들」에서 클리티에를 이렇게 넌지시 끌어들인다."]], [["“I will not have the mad Clytie, Whose head is turned by the sun; The tulip is a courtly quean, Whom therefore I will shun; The cowslip is a country wench, The violet is a nun;— But I will woo the dainty rose, The queen of every one.”", "\"나는 미친 클리티에를 갖지 않으리, 그 머리는 해에 의해 돌아가니. 튤립은 궁정의 요부라, 그러므로 나는 그것을 멀리하리. 카우슬립은 시골 처녀요, 제비꽃은 수녀라네. 그러나 나는 어여쁜 장미에게 구애하리, 모든 꽃의 여왕에게.\""]], [["The sunflower is a favorite emblem of constancy.", "해바라기는 한결같음을 나타내는 사랑받는 상징이다."], ["Thus Moore uses it:", "무어는 그것을 이렇게 쓴다."]], [["“The heart that has truly loved never forgets, But as truly loves on to the close; As the sunflower turns on her god when he sets The same look that she turned when he rose.”", "\"참되게 사랑한 적이 있는 마음은 결코 잊지 않으니, 끝까지 한결같이 사랑을 이어 가네. 해바라기가 제 신이 저물 때 그를 향해 돌리는 눈길이 그가 떠오를 때 돌렸던 그 눈길과 같듯이.\""]], [["# HERO AND LEANDER", "# HERO AND LEANDER"]], [["Leander was a youth of Abydos, a town of the Asian side of the strait which separates Asia and Europe.", "레안드로스는 아시아와 유럽을 가르는 해협의 아시아 쪽에 있는 도시 아비도스의 청년이었다."], ["On the opposite shore, in the town of Sestos, lived the maiden Hero, a priestess of Venus.", "맞은편 기슭, 세스토스라는 도시에는 아프로디테[로마의 〈베누스〉]의 여사제인 처녀 헤로가 살고 있었다."], ["Leander loved her, and used to swim the strait nightly to enjoy the company of his mistress, guided by a torch which she reared upon the tower for the purpose.", "레안드로스는 그녀를 사랑하여, 자신의 연인과 함께 있기 위해 밤마다 그 해협을 헤엄쳐 건너곤 했으니, 그녀가 그를 위해 망루에 높이 들어 올린 횃불의 인도를 받았다."], ["But one night a tempest arose and the sea was rough; his strength failed, and he was drowned.", "그러나 어느 날 밤 폭풍이 일어 바다가 거칠어졌다. 그의 힘이 다하여, 그는 물에 빠져 죽고 말았다."], ["The waves bore his body to the European shore, where Hero became aware of his death, and in her despair cast herself down from the tower into the sea and perished.", "파도가 그의 시신을 유럽 쪽 기슭으로 실어 갔고, 그곳에서 헤로는 그의 죽음을 알게 되자, 절망에 사로잡혀 망루에서 바다로 몸을 던져 목숨을 잃었다."]], [["The following sonnet is by Keats:", "다음 소네트는 키츠의 작품이다."]], [["“ON A PICTURE OF LEANDER “Come hither all sweet maidens soberly, Down looking aye, and with a chasten’d light Hid in the fringes of your eyelids white, And meekly let your fair hands joined be.", "\"레안드로스의 그림에 부쳐\" \"이리로 오라, 모든 고운 처녀들이여, 조용히, 언제나 눈길을 아래로 떨구고, 그대들의 흰 눈꺼풀 가장자리에 감춘 절제된 빛으로, 그리고 온순히 그대들의 고운 두 손을 맞잡으라."], ["As if so gentle that ye could not see, Untouch’d, a victim of your beauty bright, Sinking away to his young spirit’s night, Sinking bewilder’d ’mid the dreary sea.", "너무도 부드러워 그대들이 차마 볼 수 없는 듯이, 손도 대지 못한 채, 그대들의 빛나는 아름다움의 희생자가, 제 젊은 영혼의 밤 속으로 가라앉아 가는 것을, 음울한 바다 한가운데서 어찌할 바 모르며 가라앉는 것을."], ["’Tis young Leander toiling to his death.", "그것은 죽음을 향해 안간힘 쓰는 젊은 레안드로스라."], ["Nigh swooning he doth purse his weary lips For Hero’s cheek, and smiles against her smile.", "거의 정신을 잃을 듯, 그는 헤로의 뺨을 향해 지친 입술을 오므리고, 그녀의 미소에 미소로 답한다. 아, 끔찍한 꿈이여!"], ["O horrid dream!", "보라, 그의 몸이 어떻게 죽음처럼 무겁게 잠겨 드는지를."], ["see how his body dips Dead-heavy; arms and shoulders gleam awhile; He’s gone; up bubbles all his amorous breath!”", "팔과 어깨가 잠시 어른거리더니, 그는 사라졌도다. 그의 모든 사랑하던 숨결이 거품 되어 솟아오른다!\""]], [["The story of Leander’s swimming the Hellespont was looked upon as fabulous, and the feat considered impossible, till Lord Byron proved its possibility by performing it himself.", "레안드로스가 헬레스폰토스를 헤엄쳐 건넌 이야기는 꾸며 낸 것으로 여겨졌고, 그 위업은 불가능한 일로 간주되었으니, 바이런 경이 직접 그것을 해냄으로써 그 가능성을 입증하기 전까지는 그러했다."], ["In the “Bride of Abydos” he says,", "그는 「아비도스의 신부」에서 이렇게 말한다."]], [["“These limbs that buoyant wave hath borne.”", "\"이 사지를 떠받쳐 실어 나른 저 출렁이는 물결이여.\""]], [["The distance in the narrowest part is almost a mile, and there is a constant current setting out from the Sea of Marmora into the Archipelago.", "가장 좁은 부분의 거리는 거의 1마일에 이르며, 마르마라해에서 다도해[아르키펠라고스]로 끊임없이 흘러나가는 물길이 있다."], ["Since Byron’s time the feat has been achieved by others; but it yet remains a test of strength and skill in the art of swimming sufficient to give a wide and lasting celebrity to any one of our readers who may dare to make the attempt and succeed in accomplishing it.", "바이런의 시대 이후로 그 위업은 다른 이들에 의해서도 이루어졌다. 그러나 그것은 여전히 수영술에서의 힘과 솜씨를 가늠하는 시험으로 남아 있으니, 우리 독자들 가운데 감히 그 시도를 해 그것을 완수하는 데 성공하는 이가 있다면, 누구에게나 넓고도 오래도록 이어질 명성을 안겨 주기에 충분하다."]], [["In the beginning of the second canto of the same poem, Byron thus alludes to this story:", "같은 시의 둘째 칸토 첫머리에서 바이런은 이 이야기를 이렇게 넌지시 끌어들인다."]], [["“The winds are high on Helle’s wave, As on that night of stormiest water, When Love, who sent, forgot to save The young, the beautiful, the brave, The lonely hope of Sestos’ daughter.", "\"헬레의 물결 위에 바람이 높이 분다, 가장 사납게 물결치던 그날 밤처럼, 그날 사랑은, 그를 보냈으되, 구해 주기를 잊었으니, 그 젊고, 아름답고, 용감한 이를, 세스토스 딸의 외로운 희망을. 아, 망루의 횃불이 하늘을 따라 홀로 높이 타오르던 그때, 솟구치는 강풍과 부서지는 거품이, 그리고 비명 지르는 바닷새들이 그에게 돌아가라 일렀건만, 높이 뜬 구름과 아래의 조수가, 온갖 징조와 소리로 가지 말라 막았건만, 그는 보지 못했고, 들으려 하지 않았으니, 두려움을 예고하는 그 어떤 소리도 광경도."], ["O, when alone along the sky The turret-torch was blazing high, Though rising gale and breaking foam, And shrieking sea-birds warned him home; And clouds aloft and tides below, With signs and sounds forbade to go, He could not see, he would not hear Or sound or sight foreboding fear.", "그의 눈은 다만 저 사랑의 빛을 보았을 뿐이니, 그것은 그가 우러른 하늘의 유일한 별이었다. 그의 귀는 다만 헤로의 노래로 울렸으니, '그대 물결이여, 연인들을 오래 갈라놓지 말라.'"], ["His eye but saw that light of love, The only star it hailed above; His ear but rang with Hero’s song, ‘Ye waves, divide not lovers long.’ That tale is old, but love anew May nerve young hearts to prove as true.”", "그 이야기는 오래된 것이나, 사랑은 다시금 젊은 가슴들로 하여금 그처럼 참됨을 증명하도록 북돋우리라.\" [13] 해바라기."]], [["MINERVA, the goddess of wisdom, was the daughter of Jupiter.", "아테나, 곧 지혜의 여신은 제우스의 딸이었다."], ["She was said to have leaped forth from his brain, mature, and in complete armor.", "그녀는 다 자란 모습으로, 완전한 갑옷을 입은 채 아버지의 머릿속에서 뛰쳐나왔다고 전한다."], ["She presided over the useful and ornamental arts, both those of men—such as agriculture and navigation—and those of women,—spinning, weaving, and needlework.", "그녀는 유용한 기예와 장식적인 기예를 두루 주관했으니, 농업과 항해와 같은 남성의 기예와 더불어 길쌈, 직조, 바느질과 같은 여성의 기예까지도 관장했다."], ["She was also a warlike divinity; but it was defensive war only that she patronized, and she had no sympathy with Mars’s savage love of violence and bloodshed.", "그녀는 또한 전쟁의 신이기도 했다. 하지만 그녀가 보호한 것은 오로지 방어를 위한 전쟁뿐이었으니, 폭력과 유혈을 잔혹하게 즐기는 아레스[로마의 〈마르스〉]의 성향에는 전혀 공감하지 않았다."], ["Athens was her chosen seat, her own city, awarded to her as the prize of a contest with Neptune, who also aspired to it.", "아테나이는 그녀가 택한 자리, 곧 그녀 자신의 도시였는데, 이 도시 역시 탐냈던 포세이돈[로마의 〈넵투누스〉]과 벌인 경합의 상으로 그녀에게 주어진 것이었다."], ["The tale ran that in the reign of Cecrops, the first king of Athens, the two deities contended for the possession of the city.", "전하는 이야기에 따르면, 아테나이의 첫 번째 왕 케크롭스의 치세에 두 신이 이 도시의 소유권을 두고 다투었다고 한다."], ["The gods decreed that it should be awarded to that one who produced the gift most useful to mortals.", "신들은 필멸자에게 가장 유용한 선물을 만들어 내는 쪽에게 도시를 주기로 정했다."], ["Neptune gave the horse; Minerva produced the olive.", "포세이돈은 말을 주었고, 아테나는 올리브 나무를 내놓았다."], ["The gods gave judgment that the olive was the more useful of the two, and awarded the city to the goddess; and it was named after her, Athens, her name in Greek being Athene.", "신들은 둘 가운데 올리브가 더 유용하다고 판정하여 도시를 여신에게 주었고, 도시는 그녀의 이름을 따라 아테나이라 불렸으니, 그녀의 그리스식 이름이 아테네였기 때문이다."]], [["There was another contest, in which a mortal dared to come in competition with Minerva.", "또 다른 경합도 있었는데, 거기서는 한 필멸자가 감히 아테나와 겨루려 들었다."], ["That mortal was Arachne, a maiden who had attained such skill in the arts of weaving and embroidery that the nymphs themselves would leave their groves and fountains to come and gaze upon her work.", "그 필멸자는 아라크네라는 처녀로, 그녀는 직조와 자수의 기예에 너무나 뛰어난 솜씨를 익혀, 님프들조차 자기들의 숲과 샘을 떠나 그녀의 작품을 구경하러 올 정도였다."], ["It was not only beautiful when it was done, but beautiful also in the doing.", "그것은 완성되었을 때만 아름다운 것이 아니라, 만들어지는 과정마저도 아름다웠다."], ["To watch her, as she took the wool in its rude state and formed it into rolls, or separated it with her fingers and carded it till it looked as light and soft as a cloud, or twirled the spindle with skilful touch, or wove the web, or, after it was woven, adorned it with her needle, one would have said that Minerva herself had taught her.", "그녀가 거친 상태의 양털을 집어 두루마리 모양으로 만드는 모습이며, 손가락으로 그것을 나누고 빗질하여 구름처럼 가볍고 보드라워 보일 때까지 다듬는 모습이며, 능숙한 손길로 물레를 돌리거나 천을 짜는 모습, 또는 다 짠 뒤에 바늘로 장식을 더하는 모습을 지켜보노라면, 누구든 아테나가 직접 그녀를 가르친 것이라 말했으리라."], ["But this she denied, and could not bear to be thought a pupil even of a goddess.", "하지만 그녀는 이를 부인했고, 여신의 제자로조차 여겨지는 것을 견디지 못했다."], ["“Let Minerva try her skill with mine,”", "\"아테나더러 내 솜씨와 한번 겨뤄 보라고 하시오.\""], ["said she; “if beaten I will pay the penalty.”", "그녀는 말했다. \"지면 그 대가를 치르겠소.\""], ["Minerva heard this and was displeased.", "아테나는 이 말을 듣고 언짢아했다."], ["She assumed the form of an old woman and went and gave Arachne some friendly advice.", "그녀는 노파의 모습으로 변신하여 아라크네를 찾아가 친절한 충고를 건넸다."], ["“I have had much experience,”", "\"나는 많은 일을 겪어 왔다네.\""], ["said she, “and I hope you will not despise my counsel.", "그녀는 말했다. \"그러니 자네가 내 충고를 업신여기지 않기를 바라네."], ["Challenge your fellow-mortals as you will, but do not compete with a goddess.", "같은 필멸자들에게야 자네 마음껏 도전하게나, 하지만 여신과는 겨루지 말게."], ["On the contrary, I advise you to ask her forgiveness for what you have said, and as she is merciful perhaps she will pardon you.”", "오히려 나는 자네가 한 말에 대해 여신께 용서를 빌라고 권하겠네. 여신은 자비로우시니 어쩌면 자네를 용서해 주실지도 모르지.\""], ["Arachne stopped her spinning and looked at the old dame with anger in her countenance.", "아라크네는 길쌈을 멈추고 얼굴에 노기를 띤 채 노파를 바라보았다. \"그 충고는 간직해 두었다가.\""], ["“Keep your counsel,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “for your daughters or handmaids; for my part I know what I say, and I stand to it.", "\"당신 딸들이나 하녀들에게나 베푸시오. 나로 말하자면 내가 무슨 말을 하는지 잘 알고 있고, 그 말을 지킬 것이오."], ["I am not afraid of the goddess; let her try her skill, if she dare venture.” “She comes,”", "나는 여신이 두렵지 않으니, 감히 나설 용기가 있다면 그 솜씨를 겨뤄 보라고 하시오.\""], ["said Minerva; and dropping her disguise stood confessed.", "\"여기 그분이 오셨다.\" 아테나는 이렇게 말하며 변장을 벗고 본모습을 드러냈다."], ["The nymphs bent low in homage, and all the bystanders paid reverence.", "님프들은 깊이 허리를 굽혀 경의를 표했고, 곁에 있던 이들 모두가 공경을 바쳤다."], ["Arachne alone was unterrified.", "오직 아라크네만이 겁먹지 않았다."], ["She blushed, indeed; a sudden color dyed her cheek, and then she grew pale.", "물론 그녀도 얼굴을 붉히기는 했으니, 갑작스러운 홍조가 그녀의 뺨을 물들였다가 이내 창백해졌다."], ["But she stood to her resolve, and with a foolish conceit of her own skill rushed on her fate.", "하지만 그녀는 결심을 굽히지 않았고, 자기 솜씨에 대한 어리석은 자만으로 자기 운명을 향해 달려들었다."], ["Minerva forbore no longer nor interposed any further advice.", "아테나는 더 이상 참지도, 더 이상 충고를 내놓지도 않았다."], ["They proceed to the contest.", "두 사람은 경합에 들어갔다."], ["Each takes her station and attaches the web to the beam.", "저마다 자리를 잡고 베틀에 날실을 매었다."], ["Then the slender shuttle is passed in and out among the threads.", "그러자 가느다란 북이 실 사이를 들고 났다."], ["The reed with its fine teeth strikes up the woof into its place and compacts the web.", "가는 빗살이 달린 바디가 씨실을 제자리로 쳐 올려 천을 다져 갔다."], ["Both work with speed; their skilful hands move rapidly, and the excitement of the contest makes the labor light.", "둘 다 빠른 속도로 작업했으니, 능숙한 손이 재빠르게 움직였고, 경합의 흥분이 그 수고를 가볍게 만들었다."], ["Wool of Tyrian dye is contrasted with that of other colors, shaded off into one another so adroitly that the joining deceives the eye.", "티로스산 자줏빛으로 물들인 양털이 다른 빛깔의 양털과 어우러졌는데, 너무나 능란하게 서로 차츰 옅어지며 섞여 들어 그 이음매가 눈을 속일 정도였다."], ["Like the bow, whose long arch tinges the heavens, formed by sunbeams reflected from the shower,[14] in which, where the colors meet they seem as one, but at a little distance from the point of contact are wholly different.", "마치 소나기에 햇살이 반사되어 생겨난, 길게 휜 활 모양으로 하늘을 물들이는 무지개와도 같았으니, 빛깔들이 맞닿는 곳에서는 하나처럼 보이지만 맞닿은 지점에서 조금만 떨어지면 완전히 다른 빛깔이 된다."]], [["Minerva wrought on her web the scene of her contest with Neptune.", "아테나는 자기 천에 포세이돈과 벌인 경합 장면을 짜 넣었다."], ["Twelve of the heavenly powers are represented, Jupiter, with august gravity, sitting in the midst.", "열두 천상의 권능이 묘사되었는데, 제우스가 위엄 있는 근엄함으로 그 한가운데 앉아 있었다."], ["Neptune, the ruler of the sea, holds his trident, and appears to have just smitten the earth, from which a horse has leaped forth.", "바다의 지배자 포세이돈은 삼지창을 들고 막 대지를 내리친 듯한 모습이었고, 그 대지에서는 말 한 마리가 뛰쳐나와 있었다."], ["Minerva depicted herself with helmed head, her Ægis covering her breast.", "아테나는 자신을 투구 쓴 머리에 아이기스로 가슴을 가린 모습으로 그려 넣었다."], ["Such was the central circle; and in the four corners were represented incidents illustrating the displeasure of the gods at such presumptuous mortals as had dared to contend with them.", "가운데 원이 그러했고, 네 귀퉁이에는 감히 신들과 겨루려 든 오만한 필멸자들에 대한 신들의 노여움을 보여 주는 사건들이 묘사되어 있었다."], ["These were meant as warnings to her rival to give up the contest before it was too late.", "이는 자신의 경쟁자에게 너무 늦기 전에 경합을 포기하라는 경고로 의도된 것이었다."]], [["Arachne filled her web with subjects designedly chosen to exhibit the failings and errors of the gods.", "아라크네는 자기 천을 신들의 결점과 잘못을 드러내려는 의도로 고른 소재들로 채웠다."], ["One scene represented Leda caressing the swan, under which form Jupiter had disguised himself; and another, Danaë, in the brazen tower in which her father had imprisoned her, but where the god effected his entrance in the form of a golden shower.", "한 장면은 백조를 어루만지는 레다를 묘사했는데, 제우스가 바로 그 백조의 모습으로 변장했던 것이다. 또 다른 장면은 아버지가 가두어 둔 청동 탑 안에 있는 다나에를 그렸는데, 거기서 신은 황금 소나기의 모습으로 들어가는 데 성공했다."], ["Still another depicted Europa deceived by Jupiter under the disguise of a bull.", "또 다른 장면은 황소로 변장한 제우스에게 속아 넘어간 에우로페를 묘사했다."], ["Encouraged by the tameness of the animal Europa ventured to mount his back, whereupon Jupiter advanced into the sea and swam with her to Crete.", "그 짐승이 온순한 데 용기를 얻은 에우로페는 과감히 그 등에 올라탔는데, 그러자 제우스는 바다로 나아가 그녀를 태운 채 크레타까지 헤엄쳐 갔다."], ["You would have thought it was a real bull, so naturally was it wrought, and so natural the water in which it swam.", "그것이 어찌나 자연스럽게 짜여 있고 헤엄치는 물 또한 어찌나 자연스러웠던지, 누구든 그것을 진짜 황소라 여겼으리라."], ["She seemed to look with longing eyes back upon the shore she was leaving, and to call to her companions for help.", "그녀는 떠나가는 해안을 그리운 눈길로 돌아보며 동무들에게 도와 달라 외치는 듯했다."], ["She appeared to shudder with terror at the sight of the heaving waves, and to draw back her feet from the water.", "또한 일렁이는 파도를 보고는 공포에 몸서리치며 물에서 발을 끌어당기는 듯했다."]], [["Arachne filled her canvas with similar subjects, wonderfully well done, but strongly marking her presumption and impiety.", "아라크네는 자기 화폭을 이와 비슷한 소재들로 채웠는데, 솜씨는 놀랄 만큼 훌륭했으나 그녀의 오만과 불경을 뚜렷이 드러내는 것들이었다."], ["Minerva could not forbear to admire, yet felt indignant at the insult.", "아테나는 감탄하지 않을 수 없었으나, 그 모욕에 분노를 느꼈다."], ["She struck the web with her shuttle and rent it in pieces; she then touched the forehead of Arachne and made her feel her guilt and shame.", "그녀는 북으로 천을 쳐서 갈가리 찢어 버렸고, 그런 다음 아라크네의 이마를 건드려 죄책감과 수치심을 느끼게 했다."], ["She could not endure it and went and hanged herself.", "아라크네는 그것을 견디지 못하고 가서 목을 매어 버렸다."], ["Minerva pitied her as she saw her suspended by a rope.", "아테나는 밧줄에 매달린 그녀를 보고 가엾이 여겼다."], ["“Live,” she said, “guilty woman!", "\"살아라, 죄지은 여인이여!\""], ["and that you may preserve the memory of this lesson, continue to hang, both you and your descendants, to all future times.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"그리고 이 교훈의 기억을 간직하도록, 너와 네 후손들은 앞으로 영원토록 계속 매달려 있을지어다.\""], ["She sprinkled her with the juices of aconite, and immediately her hair came off, and her nose and ears likewise.", "그녀는 아라크네에게 투구꽃 즙을 뿌렸고, 그러자 즉시 그녀의 머리카락이 빠지고 코와 귀도 떨어져 나갔다."], ["Her form shrank up, and her head grew smaller yet; her fingers cleaved to her side and served for legs.", "그녀의 몸은 오그라들었고, 머리는 더욱 작아졌다. 손가락은 옆구리에 들러붙어 다리 노릇을 했다."], ["All the rest of her is body, out of which she spins her thread, often hanging suspended by it, in the same attitude as when Minerva touched her and transformed her into a spider.", "나머지는 모두 몸뚱이가 되었는데, 그녀는 그 몸에서 실을 자아내며, 아테나가 그녀를 건드려 거미로 바꾸어 놓았을 때와 똑같은 자세로 흔히 그 실에 매달려 있다."]], [["Spenser tells the story of Arachne in his “Muiopotmos,” adhering very closely to his master Ovid, but improving upon him in the conclusion of the story.", "스펜서는 그의 「뮈오포트모스」에서 아라크네 이야기를 들려주는데, 그의 스승 오비디우스를 매우 충실하게 따르면서도 이야기의 결말에서는 그를 능가한다."], ["The two stanzas which follow tell what was done after the goddess had depicted her creation of the olive tree:", "다음에 이어지는 두 연은 여신이 자신의 올리브 나무 창조 장면을 묘사한 뒤에 벌어진 일을 들려준다."]], [["“Amongst these leaves she made a Butterfly, With excellent device and wondrous slight, Fluttering among the olives wantonly, That seemed to live, so like it was in sight; The velvet nap which on his wings doth lie, The silken down with which his back is dight, His broad outstretched horns, his hairy thighs, His glorious colors, and his glistening eyes.”[15]", "\"이 잎새들 사이에 그녀는 나비 한 마리를 만들었으니, 빼어난 솜씨와 놀라운 정교함으로, 올리브 나무 사이를 즐거이 팔랑이는 그 모습이, 보기에 어찌나 똑같던지 살아 있는 듯하였다. 그 날개 위에 깔린 부드러운 솜털이며, 그 등을 덮은 비단 같은 솜털이며, 넓게 뻗은 더듬이며, 털 난 다리며, 그 찬란한 빛깔이며, 반짝이는 눈이며.\""]], [["“Which when Arachne saw, as overlaid And mastered with workmanship so rare, She stood astonied long, ne aught gainsaid; And with fast-fixed eyes on her did stare, And by her silence, sign of one dismayed, The victory did yield her as her share: Yet did she inly fret and felly burn, And all her blood to poisonous rancor turn.”", "\"아라크네가 이를 보았을 때, 그토록 보기 드문 솜씨에 짓눌리고 압도당하여, 그녀는 오래도록 망연히 서서 한마디도 못하였고, 그녀에게 눈길을 못 박은 채 빤히 바라보았으니, 낙담한 이의 표시인 그 침묵으로, 승리를 상대의 몫으로 내어 주고 말았다. 허나 속으로는 애를 태우며 사납게 타올랐고, 온몸의 피가 독한 원한으로 바뀌었다.\""]], [["And so the metamorphosis is caused by Arachne’s own mortification and vexation, and not by any direct act of the goddess.", "이리하여 그 변신은 여신의 어떤 직접적인 행위가 아니라 아라크네 자신의 굴욕과 분함으로 말미암아 일어난 것이 된다."]], [["The following specimen of old-fashioned gallantry is by Garrick:", "다음은 개릭이 지은, 옛 풍의 정중한 헌사의 한 본보기다."]], [["“UPON A LADY’S EMBROIDERY “Arachne once, as poets tell, A goddess at her art defied, And soon the daring mortal fell The hapless victim of her pride.", "\"어느 귀부인의 자수에 부쳐 \"지난날 아라크네는, 시인들이 전하듯이, 자신의 기예로 여신에게 도전하였으니, 그 당돌한 필멸자는 머지않아 제 오만의 가엾은 희생물로 쓰러졌네."], ["“O, then beware Arachne’s fate; Be prudent, Chloe, and submit, For you’ll most surely meet her hate, Who rival both her art and wit.”", "\"오, 그러니 아라크네의 운명을 경계하라. 신중하라, 클로에여, 그리고 굽힐지니, 그대는 반드시 그녀의 미움을 사게 되리라, 그녀의 기예와 재치를 모두 겨루는 자라면.\""]], [["Tennyson, in his “Palace of Art,” describing the works of art with which the palace was adorned, thus alludes to Europa:", "테니슨은 그의 「예술의 궁전」에서, 그 궁전을 장식한 예술 작품들을 묘사하며 에우로페를 이렇게 넌지시 언급한다."]], [["“. .", "\"."], [".", "."], ["sweet Europa’s mantle blew unclasped From off her shoulder, backward borne, From one hand drooped a crocus, one hand grasped The mild bull’s golden horn.”", ". 어여쁜 에우로페의 외투가 어깨에서 풀려 바람에 날려 뒤로 펄럭이고, 한 손에서는 크로커스가 늘어졌으며, 다른 한 손은 온순한 황소의 황금빛 뿔을 움켜쥐었네.\""]], [["In his “Princess” there is this allusion to Danaë:", "그의 「공주」에는 다나에를 이렇게 넌지시 언급하는 대목이 있다."]], [["“Now lies the earth all Danaë to the stars, And all thy heart lies open unto me.”", "\"이제 대지는 온통 별들을 향한 다나에처럼 누워 있고, 그대의 마음은 온통 내게 열려 있네.\""]], [["The fate of Arachne was noised abroad through all the country, and served as a warning to all presumptuous mortals not to compare themselves with the divinities.", "아라크네의 운명은 온 나라에 두루 알려졌고, 감히 자신을 신들과 견주지 말라는 경고로서 오만한 필멸자 모두에게 본보기가 되었다."], ["But one, and she a matron too, failed to learn the lesson of humility.", "하지만 한 사람만은, 그것도 부인의 몸으로, 그 겸손의 교훈을 배우지 못했다."], ["It was Niobe, the queen of Thebes.", "그가 바로 테바이의 여왕 니오베였다."], ["She had indeed much to be proud of; but it was not her husband’s fame, nor her own beauty, nor their great descent, nor the power of their kingdom that elated her.", "그녀에게는 실로 자랑할 것이 많았다. 하지만 그녀를 우쭐하게 한 것은 남편의 명성도, 자신의 미모도, 그들의 고귀한 혈통도, 그들 왕국의 권세도 아니었다."], ["It was her children; and truly the happiest of mothers would Niobe have been if only she had not claimed to be so.", "그것은 그녀의 자식들이었으니, 만약 그녀가 그렇다고 내세우지만 않았던들 니오베는 참으로 어머니들 가운데 가장 행복한 사람이었으리라."], ["It was on occasion of the annual celebration in honor of Latona and her offspring, Apollo and Diana,—when the people of Thebes were assembled, their brows crowned with laurel, bearing frankincense to the altars and paying their vows,—that Niobe appeared among the crowd.", "레토[로마의 〈라토나〉]와 그 자손인 아폴론과 아르테미스를 기리는 연례 축전이 열려, 테바이 백성이 모여 이마에 월계관을 쓰고 제단에 유향을 바치며 서원을 올리던 바로 그때, 니오베가 군중 사이에 나타났다."], ["Her attire was splendid with gold and gems, and her aspect beautiful as the face of an angry woman can be.", "그녀의 차림새는 황금과 보석으로 찬란했고, 그 모습은 성난 여인의 얼굴이 아름다울 수 있는 만큼이나 아름다웠다."], ["She stood and surveyed the people with haughty looks.", "그녀는 거만한 눈길로 백성을 둘러보며 서 있었다. \"이 무슨 어리석은 짓이냐.\""], ["“What folly,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “is this!—to prefer beings whom you never saw to those who stand before your eyes!", "\"너희가 한 번도 본 적 없는 존재들을, 눈앞에 서 있는 이보다 더 받든다니!"], ["Why should Latona be honored with worship, and none be paid to me?", "어찌하여 레토는 숭배를 받고, 내게는 아무것도 바쳐지지 않는단 말이냐?"], ["My father was Tantalus, who was received as a guest at the table of the gods; my mother was a goddess.", "내 아버지는 신들의 식탁에 손님으로 맞아들여진 탄탈로스였고, 내 어머니는 여신이었다."], ["My husband built and rules this city, Thebes, and Phrygia is my paternal inheritance.", "내 남편은 이 도시 테바이를 세우고 다스리며, 프리기아는 내 아버지에게서 물려받은 유산이다."], ["Wherever I turn my eyes I survey the elements of my power; nor is my form and presence unworthy of a goddess.", "내가 눈길을 돌리는 곳마다 내 권세의 바탕이 보이며, 내 자태와 풍모 또한 여신에 못지않다."], ["To all this let me add I have seven sons and seven daughters, and look for sons-in-law and daughters-in-law of pretensions worthy of my alliance.", "이 모든 것에 더하여, 내게는 아들 일곱과 딸 일곱이 있고, 내 가문과 인연 맺기에 합당한 사위들과 며느리들을 기대하고 있다."], ["Have I not cause for pride?", "내게 자랑할 까닭이 없겠느냐?"], ["Will you prefer to me this Latona, the Titan’s daughter, with her two children?", "너희는 단 두 자식뿐인 저 티탄의 딸 레토를 나보다 더 받들겠다는 것이냐?"], ["I have seven times as many.", "나는 그 일곱 곱절을 두었다."], ["Fortunate indeed am I, and fortunate I shall remain!", "나는 참으로 복받은 몸이요, 앞으로도 복받은 몸으로 남으리라!"], ["Will any one deny this?", "누가 이를 부인하겠느냐?"], ["My abundance is my security.", "내 풍요로움이 곧 내 안전이다."], ["I feel myself too strong for Fortune to subdue.", "나는 운명의 여신조차 굴복시킬 수 없을 만큼 스스로 강하다고 느낀다."], ["She may take from me much; I shall still have much left.", "그녀가 내게서 많은 것을 앗아 갈지라도, 내게는 여전히 많은 것이 남으리라."], ["Were I to lose some of my children, I should hardly be left as poor as Latona with her two only.", "설령 내가 자식 몇을 잃는다 해도, 단 둘뿐인 레토만큼 가난해지지는 않을 것이다."], ["Away with you from these solemnities,—put off the laurel from your brows,—have done with this worship!”", "이 엄숙한 의식에서 썩 물러가거라. 이마에서 월계관을 벗어 던지고, 이 숭배를 그만두어라!\""], ["The people obeyed, and left the sacred services uncompleted.", "백성은 복종하여, 신성한 예식을 끝맺지 못한 채 떠나갔다."]], [["The goddess was indignant.", "여신은 분노했다."], ["On the Cynthian mountain top where she dwelt she thus addressed her son and daughter: “My children, I who have been so proud of you both, and have been used to hold myself second to none of the goddesses except Juno alone, begin now to doubt whether I am indeed a goddess.", "그녀는 자신이 거처하는 킨토스 산꼭대기에서 아들과 딸에게 이렇게 말했다. \"내 자식들아, 너희 둘을 그토록 자랑스러워했고 오직 헤라 한 분을 제외하고는 어떤 여신에게도 뒤지지 않는다고 여겨 온 내가, 이제는 정녕 내가 여신인지 의심하기 시작했구나."], ["I shall be deprived of my worship altogether unless you protect me.”", "너희가 나를 지켜 주지 않는다면 나는 숭배를 송두리째 빼앗기고 말 것이다.\""], ["She was proceeding in this strain, but Apollo interrupted her.", "그녀가 이런 식으로 말을 이어 가는데, 아폴론이 그녀의 말을 가로막았다. \"더 말씀하지 마소서.\""], ["“Say no more,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he; “speech only delays punishment.”", "\"말은 그저 응징을 늦출 뿐입니다.\""], ["So said Diana also.", "아르테미스도 똑같이 말했다."], ["Darting through the air, veiled in clouds, they alighted on the towers of the city.", "구름에 몸을 가린 채 공중을 가르며 날아가, 그들은 그 도시의 탑들 위에 내려앉았다."], ["Spread out before the gates was a broad plain, where the youth of the city pursued their warlike sports.", "성문 앞에는 너른 들판이 펼쳐져 있었는데, 거기서 도시의 젊은이들이 전쟁 놀이를 하고 있었다."], ["The sons of Niobe were there with the rest,—some mounted on spirited horses richly caparisoned, some driving gay chariots.", "니오베의 아들들도 다른 이들과 함께 거기 있었으니, 더러는 화려하게 장식한 혈기 왕성한 말에 올라타 있었고, 더러는 멋들어진 전차를 몰고 있었다."], ["Ismenos, the first-born, as he guided his foaming steeds, struck with an arrow from above, cried out, “Ah me!”", "맏아들 이스메노스는 거품 무는 준마들을 몰다가, 위에서 날아온 화살에 맞고는 \"아아!\""], ["dropped the reins, and fell lifeless.", "하고 외치며 고삐를 떨어뜨리더니 숨을 거두며 쓰러졌다."], ["Another, hearing the sound of the bow,—like a boatman who sees the storm gathering and makes all sail for the port,—gave the reins to his horses and attempted to escape.", "또 한 아들은 활시위 소리를 듣고서, 마치 폭풍이 몰려오는 것을 보고 돛을 활짝 펼쳐 항구로 향하는 뱃사공처럼, 말들에게 고삐를 늦추어 달아나려 했다."], ["The inevitable arrow overtook him as he fled.", "하지만 그가 달아나는 사이에 피할 수 없는 화살이 그를 따라잡았다."], ["Two others, younger boys, just from their tasks, had gone to the playground to have a game of wrestling.", "더 어린 두 소년은 막 자기들 할 일을 마치고 씨름 한판을 하러 운동장에 나가 있었다."], ["As they stood breast to breast, one arrow pierced them both.", "그들이 가슴을 맞댄 채 서 있을 때, 화살 하나가 둘을 한꺼번에 꿰뚫었다."], ["They uttered a cry together, together cast a parting look around them, and together breathed their last.", "그들은 함께 비명을 지르고, 함께 마지막 눈길을 주위에 던지며, 함께 숨을 거두었다."], ["Alphenor, an elder brother, seeing them fall, hastened to the spot to render assistance, and fell stricken in the act of brotherly duty.", "손위 형인 알페노르는 그들이 쓰러지는 것을 보고 도우려 그 자리로 달려갔다가, 형제로서의 도리를 다하던 중에 화살에 맞아 쓰러졌다."], ["One only was left, Ilioneus.", "오직 한 사람, 일리오네우스만이 남았다."], ["He raised his arms to heaven to try whether prayer might not avail.", "그는 혹시 기도가 도움이 될까 하여 하늘을 향해 두 팔을 들어 올렸다."], ["“Spare me, ye gods!”", "\"신들이시여, 저를 살려 주소서!\""], ["he cried, addressing all, in his ignorance that all needed not his intercessions; and Apollo would have spared him, but the arrow had already left the string, and it was too late.", "그는 모두에게 호소하며 외쳤으니, 모두에게 빌 필요는 없다는 것을 모르는 탓이었다. 아폴론은 그를 살려 주려 했으나, 화살은 이미 시위를 떠난 뒤였고, 때는 너무 늦어 있었다."]], [["The terror of the people and grief of the attendants soon made Niobe acquainted with what had taken place.", "백성의 공포와 시중드는 이들의 비탄은 이내 벌어진 일을 니오베에게 알려 주었다."], ["She could hardly think it possible; she was indignant that the gods had dared and amazed that they had been able to do it.", "그녀는 그것이 일어날 수 있다는 것을 좀처럼 믿을 수 없었다. 신들이 감히 그런 짓을 했다는 데 분개했고, 그들이 그럴 능력이 있었다는 데 경악했다."], ["Her husband, Amphion, overwhelmed with the blow, destroyed himself.", "그녀의 남편 암피온은 그 타격에 짓눌려 스스로 목숨을 끊었다."], ["Alas!", "아아!"], ["how different was this Niobe from her who had so lately driven away the people from the sacred rites, and held her stately course through the city, the envy of her friends, now the pity even of her foes!", "얼마 전 신성한 예식에서 백성을 쫓아내고, 벗들의 부러움을 사며 도시를 위풍당당하게 거닐던 그 니오베가, 이제는 적들에게조차 동정을 받는 신세가 되었으니, 이 얼마나 달라진 모습인가!"], ["She knelt over the lifeless bodies, and kissed now one, now another of her dead sons.", "그녀는 숨이 끊긴 시신들 위에 무릎을 꿇고, 죽은 아들들에게 차례로 입을 맞추었다."], ["Raising her pallid arms to heaven, “Cruel Latona,”", "창백한 두 팔을 하늘로 들어 올리며 말했다."], ["said she, “feed full your rage with my anguish!", "\"잔인한 레토여, 내 비통함으로 네 분노를 실컷 채우려무나!"], ["Satiate your hard heart, while I follow to the grave my seven sons.", "내가 일곱 아들을 무덤까지 따라가는 동안, 네 모진 마음을 흡족하게 채우거라."], ["Yet where is your triumph?", "하지만 네 승리가 어디 있느냐?"], ["Bereaved as I am, I am still richer than you, my conqueror.”", "이렇게 자식을 빼앗긴 나일지라도, 나를 이긴 너보다는 여전히 더 부유하다.\""], ["Scarce had she spoken, when the bow sounded and struck terror into all hearts except Niobe’s alone.", "그녀가 말을 채 끝내기도 전에 활시위 소리가 울리며 모든 이의 가슴에 공포를 불러일으켰으나, 오직 니오베만은 예외였다."], ["She was brave from excess of grief.", "그녀는 비통함이 넘쳐 도리어 담대해져 있었다."], ["The sisters stood in garments of mourning over the biers of their dead brothers.", "딸들은 상복을 입은 채 죽은 오라비들의 관 곁에 서 있었다."], ["One fell, struck by an arrow, and died on the corpse she was bewailing.", "한 딸이 화살에 맞아 쓰러지더니, 자기가 애도하던 시신 위에서 숨을 거두었다."], ["Another, attempting to console her mother, suddenly ceased to speak, and sank lifeless to the earth.", "또 한 딸은 어머니를 위로하려다가, 갑자기 말을 멈추고는 숨이 끊겨 땅에 쓰러졌다."], ["A third tried to escape by flight, a fourth by concealment, another stood trembling, uncertain what course to take.", "셋째 딸은 달아나 몸을 피하려 했고, 넷째 딸은 몸을 숨겨 화를 면하려 했으며, 또 한 딸은 어느 쪽을 택해야 할지 몰라 떨며 서 있었다."], ["Six were now dead, and only one remained, whom the mother held clasped in her arms, and covered as it were with her whole body.", "이제 여섯이 죽고 오직 하나만 남았는데, 어머니는 그 아이를 두 팔로 끌어안고 마치 온몸으로 감싸듯 가렸다. \"이 하나만은, 그것도 막내만은 살려 다오!"], ["“Spare me one, and that the youngest!", "오, 그 많던 자식 가운데 이 하나만은 살려 다오!\""], ["O spare me one of so many!”", "그녀가 외쳤다."], ["she cried; and while she spoke, that one fell dead.", "그러나 그녀가 말하는 사이에 그 하나마저 죽어 쓰러졌다."], ["Desolate she sat, among sons, daughters, husband, all dead, and seemed torpid with grief.", "그녀는 아들들, 딸들, 남편이 모두 죽은 가운데 황량하게 앉아, 비탄으로 마비된 듯했다."], ["The breeze moved not her hair, no color was on her cheek, her eyes glared fixed and immovable, there was no sign of life about her.", "산들바람도 그녀의 머리카락을 흔들지 못했고, 뺨에는 핏기가 없었으며, 두 눈은 고정된 채 미동도 없이 노려보았고, 그녀에게는 살아 있다는 어떤 기색도 없었다."], ["Her very tongue cleaved to the roof of her mouth, and her veins ceased to convey the tide of life.", "그녀의 혀마저 입천장에 들러붙었고, 핏줄은 생명의 흐름을 실어 나르기를 멈추었다."], ["Her neck bent not, her arms made no gesture, her foot no step.", "목은 굽혀지지 않았고, 두 팔은 어떤 몸짓도 하지 못했으며, 발은 한 걸음도 떼지 못했다."], ["She was changed to stone, within and without.", "그녀는 안팎으로 모두 돌로 변했다."], ["Yet tears continued to flow; and borne on a whirlwind to her native mountain, she still remains, a mass of rock, from which a trickling stream flows, the tribute of her never-ending grief.", "그러나 눈물만은 계속 흘러내렸다. 그리하여 그녀는 회오리바람에 실려 고향의 산으로 옮겨졌고, 지금도 여전히 바위 덩어리로 남아, 거기서 졸졸 흐르는 시냇물이, 그 끝없는 비탄의 공물로서 흘러내리고 있다."]], [["The story of Niobe has furnished Byron with a fine illustration of the fallen condition of modern Rome:", "니오베 이야기는 바이런에게 몰락한 근대 로마의 처지를 빗댄 멋진 비유의 소재를 마련해 주었다."]], [["“The Niobe of nations!", "\"뭇 나라의 니오베여!"], ["there she stands, Childless and crownless in her voiceless woe; An empty urn within her withered hands, Whose holy dust was scattered long ago; The Scipios’ tomb contains no ashes now: The very sepulchres lie tenantless Of their heroic dwellers; dost thou flow, Old Tiber!", "저기 그녀가 서 있노라, 자식도 없고 왕관도 없이, 목소리 잃은 비탄에 잠겨, 시든 두 손에는 텅 빈 항아리를 들었는데, 그 거룩한 유골은 오래전에 흩어져 버렸도다. 스키피오 가문의 무덤에는 이제 한 줌 재도 없고, 무덤들조차 그 영웅적인 주인들을 잃은 채 텅 비어 있구나."], ["through a marble wilderness?", "오 늙은 티베리스여, 그대는 대리석 황무지를 가르며 흐르는가?"], ["Rise with thy yellow waves, and mantle her distress.”", "누런 물결로 솟구쳐 올라, 그녀의 비탄을 덮어 다오.\""], ["Childe Harold, IV. 79.", "「차일드 해럴드」, 제4편 79연."]], [["This affecting story has been made the subject of a celebrated statue in the imperial gallery of Florence.", "이 가슴 아픈 이야기는 피렌체의 황실 미술관에 있는 한 유명한 조각상의 소재가 되었다."], ["It is the principal figure of a group supposed to have been originally arranged in the pediment of a temple.", "그것은 본디 어느 신전의 박공에 배치되어 있었으리라 여겨지는 군상의 중심 인물이다."], ["The figure of the mother clasped by the arm of her terrified child is one of the most admired of the ancient statues.", "겁에 질린 자식의 팔에 안긴 어머니의 형상은 고대 조각상들 가운데 가장 찬탄받는 작품의 하나다."], ["It ranks with the Laocoön and the Apollo among the masterpieces of art.", "그것은 라오콘 군상이나 아폴론 상과 더불어 예술의 걸작으로 손꼽힌다."], ["The following is a translation of a Greek epigram supposed to relate to this statue:", "다음은 이 조각상에 관한 것으로 여겨지는 어느 그리스 경구시의 번역이다."]], [["“To stone the gods have changed her, but in vain; The sculptor’s art has made her breathe again.”", "\"신들이 그녀를 돌로 바꾸어 놓았으나, 헛된 일이었네. 조각가의 솜씨가 그녀에게 다시 숨결을 불어넣었으니.\""]], [["Tragic as is the story of Niobe, we cannot forbear to smile at the use Moore has made of it in “Rhymes on the Road”:", "니오베 이야기가 비극적이기는 하나, 무어가 「길 위의 시편」에서 그것을 다룬 방식에는 웃음을 참을 수 없다."]], [["“’Twas in his carriage the sublime Sir Richard Blackmore used to rhyme, And, if the wits don’t do him wrong, ’Twixt death and epics passed his time, Scribbling and killing all day long; Like Phœbus in his car at ease, Now warbling forth a lofty song, Now murdering the young Niobes.”", "\"고귀하신 리처드 블랙모어 경께서는 마차 안에서 곧잘 시를 짓곤 하셨으니, 재사들이 그분을 헐뜯는 것이 아니라면, 죽음과 서사시 사이에서 시간을 보내셨다네, 온종일 끄적이고 또 죽여 가면서. 마치 마차에 느긋이 앉은 포이보스처럼, 한편으로는 드높은 노래를 읊조리다가, 한편으로는 어린 니오베들을 죽여 가면서.\""]], [["Sir Richard Blackmore was a physician, and at the same time a very prolific and very tasteless poet, whose works are now forgotten, unless when recalled to mind by some wit like Moore for the sake of a joke.", "리처드 블랙모어 경은 의사였는데, 동시에 매우 다작하면서도 매우 무미건조한 시인이었다. 그의 작품들은 이제 잊혔으니, 무어 같은 재사가 농담거리로 떠올릴 때나 간혹 되살아날 뿐이다."]], [["————", "────"]], [["THE Grææ were three sisters who were gray-haired from their birth, whence their name.", "그라이아이는 태어날 때부터 머리가 잿빛이었던 세 자매로, 거기에서 그들의 이름이 비롯되었다."], ["The Gorgons were monstrous females with huge teeth like those of swine, brazen claws, and snaky hair.", "고르고네스는 돼지의 그것처럼 거대한 이빨과 청동 발톱, 그리고 뱀으로 된 머리카락을 지닌 흉측한 여인들이었다."], ["None of these beings make much figure in mythology except Medusa, the Gorgon, whose story we shall next advert to.", "이 존재들 가운데 신화에서 비중을 차지하는 것은 고르고네스 중 하나인 메두사뿐이며, 우리는 곧이어 그녀의 이야기를 다룰 것이다."], ["We mention them chiefly to introduce an ingenious theory of some modern writers, namely, that the Gorgons and Grææ were only personifications of the terrors of the sea, the former denoting the strong billows of the wide open main, and the latter the white-crested waves that dash against the rocks of the coast.", "우리가 이들을 언급하는 까닭은 주로 몇몇 근대 작가들이 내놓은 한 가지 기발한 학설을 소개하기 위해서다. 즉 고르고네스와 그라이아이는 바다의 공포를 의인화한 것에 지나지 않으며, 전자는 드넓은 외해의 거센 큰 물결을, 후자는 해안의 바위에 부딪쳐 흰 물마루를 이루는 파도를 가리킨다는 것이다."], ["Their names in Greek signify the above epithets.", "그리스어로 된 그들의 이름은 위에서 말한 별칭들을 뜻한다."]], [["# PERSEUS AND MEDUSA", "# PERSEUS AND MEDUSA"]], [["Perseus was the son of Jupiter and Danaë.", "페르세우스는 제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]와 다나에 사이에서 태어난 아들이었다."], ["His grandfather Acrisius, alarmed by an oracle which had told him that his daughter’s child would be the instrument of his death, caused the mother and child to be shut up in a chest and set adrift on the sea.", "그의 외할아버지 아크리시오스는 딸의 자식이 자기 죽음의 도구가 되리라는 신탁의 경고에 놀란 나머지, 어머니와 아이를 궤짝에 가두어 바다에 띄워 보내게 했다."], ["The chest floated towards Seriphus, where it was found by a fisherman who conveyed the mother and infant to Polydectes, the king of the country, by whom they were treated with kindness.", "궤짝은 세리포스 쪽으로 떠내려갔고, 거기서 한 어부가 그것을 발견하여 어머니와 아기를 그 나라의 왕인 폴리덱테스에게 데려갔으며, 왕은 그들을 친절히 대접했다."], ["When Perseus was grown up Polydectes sent him to attempt the conquest of Medusa, a terrible monster who had laid waste the country.", "페르세우스가 다 자라자 폴리덱테스는 그를 보내 메두사를 정복하게 했는데, 메두사는 그 나라를 황폐하게 만든 무시무시한 괴물이었다."], ["She was once a beautiful maiden whose hair was her chief glory, but as she dared to vie in beauty with Minerva, the goddess deprived her of her charms and changed her beautiful ringlets into hissing serpents.", "그녀는 한때 머리카락이 가장 큰 자랑이었던 아름다운 처녀였으나, 감히 아테나[로마의 〈미네르바〉]와 아름다움을 겨루려 했기에 여신은 그녀의 매력을 빼앗고 그 아름다운 곱슬머리를 쉭쉭거리는 뱀들로 바꾸어 버렸다."], ["She became a cruel monster of so frightful an aspect that no living thing could behold her without being turned into stone.", "그녀는 너무나 끔찍한 모습의 잔인한 괴물이 되어, 살아 있는 어떤 것도 돌로 변하지 않고서는 그녀를 바라볼 수 없었다."], ["All around the cavern where she dwelt might be seen the stony figures of men and animals which had chanced to catch a glimpse of her and had been petrified with the sight.", "그녀가 사는 동굴 주위 사방에는, 우연히 그녀를 흘끗 보았다가 그 광경에 돌이 되어 버린 사람과 짐승들의 석상이 보였다."], ["Perseus, favored by Minerva and Mercury, the former of whom lent him her shield and the latter his winged shoes, approached Medusa while she slept, and taking care not to look directly at her, but guided by her image reflected in the bright shield which he bore, he cut off her head and gave it to Minerva, who fixed it in the middle of her Ægis.", "페르세우스는 아테나와 헤르메스[로마의 〈메르쿠리우스〉]의 도움을 받았는데, 전자는 자기 방패를 빌려주었고 후자는 날개 달린 신발을 빌려주었다. 그는 메두사가 잠든 사이에 다가가, 그녀를 똑바로 바라보지 않도록 조심하며 자기가 든 빛나는 방패에 비친 그녀의 모습에 의지하여 그녀의 머리를 베었고, 그것을 아테나에게 바쳤으며, 여신은 그것을 자기 아이기스 한가운데에 박아 넣었다."]], [["Milton, in his “Comus,” thus alludes to the Ægis:", "밀턴은 그의 「코무스」에서 아이기스를 다음과 같이 암시한다."]], [["“What was that snaky-headed Gorgon-shield That wise Minerva wore, unconquered virgin, Wherewith she freezed her foes to congealed stone, But rigid looks of chaste austerity, And noble grace that dashed brute violence With sudden adoration and blank awe!”", "\"현명한 아테나가 둘렀던, 정복당하지 않은 처녀가 지녔던 저 뱀의 머리를 한 고르곤의 방패는 무엇이었던가, 그녀가 그것으로 적들을 얼어붙은 돌로 굳혀 버렸으니, 그것은 정숙하고 엄정한 굳은 표정이요, 난폭한 폭력을 갑작스러운 경배와 멍한 외경으로 꺾어 버린 고귀한 우아함이었도다!\""]], [["Armstrong, the poet of the “Art of Preserving Health,” thus describes the effect of frost upon the waters:", "「건강을 지키는 기술」의 시인 암스트롱은 서리가 물에 미치는 영향을 다음과 같이 묘사한다."]], [["“Now blows the surly North and chills throughout The stiffening regions, while by stronger charms Than Circe e’er or fell Medea brewed, Each brook that wont to prattle to its banks Lies all bestilled and wedged betwixt its banks, Nor moves the withered reeds . .", "\"이제 험한 북풍이 불어 굳어 가는 온 지역을 얼리는도다, 키르케가 일찍이 빚었거나 잔혹한 메데이아가 끓였던 것보다 더 강한 마법으로, 제 둑에 재잘대곤 하던 시냇물마다 온통 잠잠해진 채 둑 사이에 끼여 누워 있고, 시든 갈대조차 움직이지 않는도다…"], [". The surges baited by the fierce North-east, Tossing with fretful spleen their angry heads, E’en in the foam of all their madness struck To monumental ice.", "사나운 북동풍에 부추겨진 물결들이 성난 머리를 짜증스레 흔들어 대다가, 그 광기의 거품 한가운데서 그대로 기념비 같은 얼음으로 굳어 버렸도다."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [". .", "."], ["Such execution, So stern, so sudden, wrought the grisly aspect Of terrible Medusa, When wandering through the woods she turned to stone Their savage tenants; just as the foaming Lion Sprang furious on his prey, her speedier power Outran his haste, And fixed in that fierce attitude he stands Like Rage in marble!”", ". 그러한 처형을, 그토록 준엄하고 그토록 갑작스러운 처형을, 무시무시한 메두사의 소름 끼치는 모습이 이루어 냈으니, 그녀가 숲을 헤매며 그 사나운 거주자들을 돌로 바꾸었을 때, 거품을 문 사자가 제 먹이를 향해 사납게 덮쳐들던 바로 그 순간, 그녀의 더 빠른 힘이 그 서두름을 앞질러, 그 사나운 자세 그대로 멈춰 선 채 사자는 대리석에 새겨진 분노처럼 서 있도다!\""], ["—Imitations of Shakspeare.", "—「셰익스피어 모작」."]], [["# PERSEUS AND ATLAS", "# PERSEUS AND ATLAS"]], [["After the slaughter of Medusa, Perseus, bearing with him the head of the Gorgon, flew far and wide, over land and sea.", "메두사를 죽인 뒤 페르세우스는 고르곤의 머리를 지닌 채 육지와 바다를 가로질러 멀리, 그리고 넓게 날아갔다."], ["As night came on, he reached the western limit of the earth, where the sun goes down.", "밤이 다가오자 그는 해가 지는 대지의 서쪽 끝에 이르렀다."], ["Here he would gladly have rested till morning.", "거기서 그는 기꺼이 아침까지 쉬고 싶었다."], ["It was the realm of King Atlas, whose bulk surpassed that of all other men.", "그곳은 아틀라스 왕의 영토였는데, 그의 덩치는 다른 모든 사람을 능가했다."], ["He was rich in flocks and herds and had no neighbor or rival to dispute his state.", "그는 양 떼와 소 떼가 풍부했고, 그의 지위를 다툴 만한 이웃이나 경쟁자가 없었다."], ["But his chief pride was in his gardens, whose fruit was of gold, hanging from golden branches, half hid with golden leaves.", "그러나 그의 가장 큰 자랑은 정원이었으니, 그 열매는 황금으로 되어 있어 황금 잎에 반쯤 가려진 채 황금 가지에 매달려 있었다."], ["Perseus said to him, “I come as a guest.", "페르세우스는 그에게 말했다. \"저는 손님으로 왔습니다."], ["If you honor illustrious descent, I claim Jupiter for my father; if mighty deeds, I plead the conquest of the Gorgon.", "당신이 빛나는 혈통을 존중하신다면, 저는 제우스를 제 아버지로 내세웁니다. 당신이 위대한 공적을 높이 사신다면, 저는 고르곤을 정복한 일을 내세웁니다."], ["I seek rest and food.”", "저는 쉴 곳과 먹을 것을 구합니다.\""], ["But Atlas remembered that an ancient prophecy had warned him that a son of Jove should one day rob him of his golden apples.", "그러나 아틀라스는 언젠가 제우스의 아들이 자기에게서 황금 사과를 빼앗으리라고 경고한 옛 예언을 떠올렸다. 그래서 그는 대답했다."], ["So he answered, “Begone!", "\"썩 물러가라!"], ["or neither your false claims of glory nor parentage shall protect you;”", "그러지 않으면 너의 거짓된 영광의 주장도, 혈통도 너를 지켜 주지 못하리라.\""], ["and he attempted to thrust him out.", "그러고는 그를 밀쳐 내려 했다."], ["Perseus, finding the giant too strong for him, said, “Since you value my friendship so little, deign to accept a present;”", "페르세우스는 그 거인이 자기보다 너무 강하다는 것을 알고 말했다. \"당신이 제 우정을 그토록 하찮게 여기시니, 부디 선물 하나를 받아 주십시오.\""], ["and turning his face away, he held up the Gorgon’s head.", "그러고는 얼굴을 돌린 채 고르곤의 머리를 치켜들었다."], ["Atlas, with all his bulk, was changed into stone.", "아틀라스는 그 거대한 몸뚱이 전체가 돌로 변했다."], ["His beard and hair became forests, his arms and shoulders cliffs, his head a summit, and his bones rocks.", "그의 수염과 머리카락은 숲이 되었고, 그의 팔과 어깨는 절벽이, 그의 머리는 봉우리가, 그의 뼈는 바위가 되었다."], ["Each part increased in bulk till he became a mountain, and (such was the pleasure of the gods) heaven with all its stars rests upon his shoulders.", "각 부분이 점점 커져서 마침내 그는 산이 되었고, (그것이 신들의 뜻이었기에) 모든 별을 거느린 하늘이 그의 어깨 위에 얹히게 되었다."]], [["# THE SEA-MONSTER", "# THE SEA-MONSTER"]], [["Perseus, continuing his flight, arrived at the country of the Æthiopians, of which Cepheus was king.", "페르세우스는 비행을 계속하여 케페우스가 왕으로 있던 아이티오피아인들의 나라에 이르렀다."], ["Cassiopeia his queen, proud of her beauty, had dared to compare herself to the Sea-Nymphs, which roused their indignation to such a degree that they sent a prodigious sea-monster to ravage the coast.", "그의 왕비 카시오페이아는 자기 아름다움을 뽐낸 나머지 감히 자신을 바다의 님프들과 비교했고, 이것이 그들의 분노를 너무나 크게 자극하여, 그들은 해안을 황폐하게 만들 어마어마한 바다 괴물을 보냈다."], ["To appease the deities, Cepheus was directed by the oracle to expose his daughter Andromeda to be devoured by the monster.", "신들을 달래기 위해 케페우스는 신탁의 지시에 따라 자기 딸 안드로메다를 괴물에게 잡아먹히도록 내놓아야 했다."], ["As Perseus looked down from his aerial height he beheld the virgin chained to a rock, and waiting the approach of the serpent.", "페르세우스가 공중의 높은 곳에서 내려다보니, 처녀가 바위에 사슬로 묶인 채 그 뱀이 다가오기를 기다리고 있는 것이 보였다."], ["She was so pale and motionless that if it had not been for her flowing tears and her hair that moved in the breeze, he would have taken her for a marble statue.", "그녀는 너무도 창백하고 미동도 없어, 흘러내리는 눈물과 산들바람에 나부끼는 머리카락이 아니었더라면 그는 그녀를 대리석상으로 여겼을 것이다."], ["He was so startled at the sight that he almost forgot to wave his wings.", "그는 그 광경에 너무나 놀라 날개를 퍼덕이는 것조차 거의 잊을 뻔했다."], ["As he hovered over her he said, “O virgin, undeserving of those chains, but rather of such as bind fond lovers together, tell me, I beseech you, your name, and the name of your country, and why you are thus bound.”", "그녀의 위를 맴돌며 그는 말했다. \"오 처녀여, 그대는 이런 사슬에 묶일 만한 사람이 아니라 오히려 다정한 연인들을 함께 묶는 사슬에나 어울릴 사람이니, 부디 청하노니 그대의 이름과 그대 나라의 이름을, 그리고 어찌하여 이렇게 묶여 있는지를 말해 주오.\""], ["At first she was silent from modesty, and, if she could, would have hid her face with her hands; but when he repeated his questions, for fear she might be thought guilty of some fault which she dared not tell, she disclosed her name and that of her country, and her mother’s pride of beauty.", "처음에 그녀는 부끄러움에 입을 다물었고, 할 수만 있었다면 두 손으로 얼굴을 가렸을 것이다. 그러나 그가 질문을 거듭하자, 차마 말하지 못할 어떤 잘못을 저질렀다고 여겨질까 두려워 그녀는 자기 이름과 나라의 이름을, 그리고 어머니가 아름다움을 뽐낸 일을 털어놓았다."], ["Before she had done speaking, a sound was heard off upon the water, and the sea-monster appeared, with his head raised above the surface, cleaving the waves with his broad breast.", "그녀가 말을 채 마치기도 전에, 물 저편에서 소리가 들리더니 바다 괴물이 나타나, 머리를 수면 위로 쳐들고 넓은 가슴으로 파도를 가르며 다가왔다."], ["The virgin shrieked, the father and mother who had now arrived at the scene, wretched both, but the mother more justly so, stood by, not able to afford protection, but only to pour forth lamentations and to embrace the victim.", "처녀는 비명을 질렀고, 이제 그 자리에 당도한 아버지와 어머니는 둘 다 비참했으나 어머니가 더 마땅히 그러했으며, 그들은 곁에 서서 보호해 줄 수도 없이 그저 통곡을 쏟아 내고 제물이 된 딸을 끌어안을 따름이었다. 그때 페르세우스가 말했다."], ["Then spoke Perseus: “There will be time enough for tears; this hour is all we have for rescue.", "\"눈물을 흘릴 시간은 얼마든지 있을 것이오. 그러나 구해 낼 시간은 지금 이 순간뿐이오."], ["My rank as the son of Jove and my renown as the slayer of the Gorgon might make me acceptable as a suitor; but I will try to win her by services rendered, if the gods will only be propitious.", "제우스의 아들이라는 제 신분과 고르곤을 죽인 자라는 제 명성이라면 구혼자로서 받아들여질 만하겠지만, 신들이 호의를 베풀어만 주신다면 저는 제가 한 봉사로써 그녀를 얻고자 합니다."], ["If she be rescued by my valor, I demand that she be my reward.”", "만약 제 용맹으로 그녀가 구원받는다면, 저는 그녀를 제 보답으로 받기를 요구합니다.\""], ["The parents consent (how could they hesitate?) and promise a royal dowry with her.", "부모는 (어찌 망설일 수 있었겠는가?) 이에 동의하며 딸과 함께 왕가의 지참금까지 약속했다."]], [["And now the monster was within the range of a stone thrown by a skilful slinger, when with a sudden bound the youth soared into the air.", "그리고 이제 괴물이 솜씨 좋은 투석꾼이 던진 돌의 사정거리 안에 들어왔을 때, 청년은 갑자기 도약하여 공중으로 솟아올랐다."], ["As an eagle, when from his lofty flight he sees a serpent basking in the sun, pounces upon him and seizes him by the neck to prevent him from turning his head round and using his fangs, so the youth darted down upon the back of the monster and plunged his sword into its shoulder.", "마치 독수리가 높은 비행 중에 햇볕을 쬐고 있는 뱀을 보고 덮쳐, 뱀이 머리를 돌려 독니를 쓰지 못하도록 목을 움켜쥐듯이, 청년은 괴물의 등 위로 내리꽂혀 자기 검을 그 어깨에 박아 넣었다."], ["Irritated by the wound, the monster raised himself in the air, then plunged into the depth; then, like a wild boar surrounded by a pack of barking dogs, turned swiftly from side to side, while the youth eluded its attacks by means of his wings.", "상처에 자극받은 괴물은 공중으로 몸을 일으켰다가 깊은 물속으로 곤두박질쳤고, 그러고는 짖어 대는 개 떼에 둘러싸인 멧돼지처럼 이리저리 재빨리 몸을 틀었으며, 그동안 청년은 날개의 힘으로 그 공격을 피했다."], ["Wherever he can find a passage for his sword between the scales he makes a wound, piercing now the side, now the flank, as it slopes towards the tail.", "그는 비늘 사이로 검이 들어갈 만한 틈을 찾는 곳마다 상처를 내어, 지금은 옆구리를, 이제는 꼬리 쪽으로 비스듬히 이어지는 허리를 찔렀다."], ["The brute spouts from his nostrils water mixed with blood.", "그 짐승은 콧구멍에서 피가 섞인 물을 내뿜었다."], ["The wings of the hero are wet with it, and he dares no longer trust to them.", "영웅의 날개가 그것에 젖어, 그는 더는 날개를 믿을 수 없게 되었다."], ["Alighting on a rock which rose above the waves, and holding on by a projecting fragment, as the monster floated near he gave him a death stroke.", "그는 파도 위로 솟아오른 바위에 내려앉아 튀어나온 조각 하나를 붙잡고 있다가, 괴물이 가까이 떠올랐을 때 치명적인 일격을 가했다."], ["The people who had gathered on the shore shouted so that the hills reëchoed the sound.", "해안에 모여든 사람들이 어찌나 환호했던지 언덕들이 그 소리를 메아리쳤다."], ["The parents, transported with joy, embraced their future son-in-law, calling him their deliverer and the savior of their house, and the virgin, both cause and reward of the contest, descended from the rock.", "부모는 기쁨에 겨워 장래의 사위를 끌어안으며 그를 자신들의 구원자요 가문의 구세주라 불렀고, 이 싸움의 원인이자 보답이었던 처녀는 바위에서 내려왔다."]], [["Cassiopeia was an Æthiopian, and consequently, in spite of her boasted beauty, black; at least so Milton seems to have thought, who alludes to this story in his “Penseroso,”", "카시오페이아는 아이티오피아인이었으므로, 그녀가 뽐내던 아름다움에도 불구하고 피부가 검었다."], ["where he addresses Melancholy as the", "적어도 밀턴은 그렇게 생각했던 듯한데, 그는 「펜세로소」에서 이 이야기를 암시하며 우울을 다음과 같이 부른다."]], [["“. . . . goddess, sage and holy, Whose saintly visage is too bright To hit the sense of human sight, And, therefore, to our weaker view O’erlaid with black, staid Wisdom’s hue. Black, but such as in esteem Prince Memnon’s sister might beseem, Or that starred Æthiop queen that strove To set her beauty’s praise above The sea-nymphs, and their powers offended.”", "\"…현명하고 거룩한 여신이여, 그대의 성스러운 얼굴은 너무도 환하여 인간의 시야로는 감당할 수 없으니, 그러므로 우리의 약한 눈을 위해 검은빛으로, 차분한 지혜의 빛깔로 덮였도다. 검으나, 그 검음은 사람들의 존경 속에서 멤논 왕자의 누이에게나 어울릴 만한 것이거나, 아니면 자기 아름다움의 찬사를 바다의 님프들 위에 두려 하여 그들의 권능을 노엽게 했던 저 별이 된 아이티오피아 왕비에게나 어울릴 만한 것이로다.\""]], [["Cassiopeia is called “the starred Æthiop queen” because after her death she was placed among the stars, forming the constellation of that name.", "카시오페이아가 \"별이 된 아이티오피아 왕비\"라 불리는 까닭은, 죽은 뒤 그녀가 별들 사이에 놓여 그 이름의 별자리를 이루었기 때문이다."], ["Though she attained this honor, yet the Sea-Nymphs, her old enemies, prevailed so far as to cause her to be placed in that part of the heaven near the pole, where every night she is half the time held with her head downward, to give her a lesson of humility.", "비록 그녀가 이런 영예를 얻기는 했으나, 옛 원수였던 바다의 님프들이 끝내 그 뜻을 관철하여, 그녀를 하늘의 극에 가까운 부분에 놓이게 했으니, 거기서 그녀는 매일 밤 절반의 시간을 머리를 아래로 향한 채 매달려 겸손의 교훈을 얻게 되었다."]], [["Memnon was an Æthiopian prince, of whom we shall tell in a future chapter.", "멤논은 아이티오피아의 왕자였는데, 그에 관해서는 뒤의 어느 장에서 이야기할 것이다."]], [["# THE WEDDING FEAST", "# THE WEDDING FEAST"]], [["The joyful parents, with Perseus and Andromeda, repaired to the palace, where a banquet was spread for them, and all was joy and festivity.", "기뻐하던 부모는 페르세우스와 안드로메다와 함께 궁전으로 가서, 거기서 그들을 위해 연회를 베풀었고, 온통 기쁨과 잔치 분위기였다."], ["But suddenly a noise was heard of warlike clamor, and Phineus, the betrothed of the virgin, with a party of his adherents, burst in, demanding the maiden as his own.", "그러나 갑자기 호전적인 함성 소리가 들리더니, 처녀의 약혼자였던 피네우스가 자기 추종자 무리를 거느리고 들이닥쳐 그 처녀를 자기 것이라며 내놓으라고 요구했다."], ["It was in vain that Cepheus remonstrated—“You should have claimed her when she lay bound to the rock, the monster’s victim.", "케페우스가 항변했으나 헛된 일이었다. \"그대는 그녀가 괴물의 제물이 되어 바위에 묶여 있을 때 그녀를 요구했어야 했소."], ["The sentence of the gods dooming her to such a fate dissolved all engagements, as death itself would have done.”", "그녀를 그런 운명에 처하게 한 신들의 판결이 모든 약혼을 무효로 만들었으니, 그것은 죽음 그 자체가 그랬을 것과 같소.\""], ["Phineus made no reply, but hurled his javelin at Perseus, but it missed its mark and fell harmless.", "피네우스는 아무 대꾸도 없이 페르세우스에게 투창을 던졌으나, 그것은 빗나가 무해하게 떨어졌다."], ["Perseus would have thrown his in turn, but the cowardly assailant ran and took shelter behind the altar.", "페르세우스도 답으로 창을 던지려 했으나, 비겁한 공격자는 달아나 제단 뒤로 몸을 숨겼다."], ["But his act was a signal for an onset by his band upon the guests of Cepheus.", "그러나 그의 행동은 그의 무리가 케페우스의 손님들에게 달려드는 신호가 되었다."], ["They defended themselves and a general conflict ensued, the old king retreating from the scene after fruitless expostulations, calling the gods to witness that he was guiltless of this outrage on the rights of hospitality.", "그들은 스스로를 방어했고 전면적인 싸움이 벌어졌으니, 늙은 왕은 헛된 항의를 한 끝에 그 자리에서 물러나, 자신은 이 환대의 권리를 짓밟는 만행에 죄가 없음을 신들에게 증언해 달라고 외쳤다."]], [["Perseus and his friends maintained for some time the unequal contest; but the numbers of the assailants were too great for them, and destruction seemed inevitable, when a sudden thought struck Perseus,—“I will make my enemy defend me.”", "페르세우스와 그의 벗들은 한동안 이 불공평한 싸움을 버텨 냈으나, 공격자들의 수가 너무나 많아, 파멸이 불가피해 보였다."], ["Then with a loud voice he exclaimed, “If I have any friend here let him turn away his eyes!”", "그때 페르세우스에게 한 가지 생각이 번뜩였다. \"내 적이 나를 지키게 만들리라.\""], ["and held aloft the Gorgon’s head.", "그러고는 큰 소리로 외쳤다."], ["“Seek not to frighten us with your jugglery,”", "\"여기 내 벗이 있거든 눈을 돌릴지어다!\""], ["said Thescelus, and raised his javelin in act to throw, and became stone in the very attitude.", "그러고는 고르곤의 머리를 높이 치켜들었다. \"네 요술로 우리를 겁주려 들지 마라.\""], ["Ampyx was about to plunge his sword into the body of a prostrate foe, but his arm stiffened and he could neither thrust forward nor withdraw it.", "테스켈로스가 이렇게 말하며 던지려고 투창을 들어 올리다가, 바로 그 자세 그대로 돌이 되어 버렸다. 암피크스는 쓰러진 적의 몸에 검을 박으려던 참이었으나, 그의 팔이 굳어 버려 그는 검을 앞으로 찌를 수도 빼낼 수도 없었다."], ["Another, in the midst of a vociferous challenge, stopped, his mouth open, but no sound issuing.", "또 다른 자는 떠들썩하게 도전하던 중에 입을 벌린 채로 멈춰 버렸으나 아무 소리도 나오지 않았다."], ["One of Perseus’s friends, Aconteus, caught sight of the Gorgon and stiffened like the rest.", "페르세우스의 벗 중 하나인 아콘테우스는 고르곤을 흘끗 보고 다른 이들처럼 굳어 버렸다."], ["Astyages struck him with his sword, but instead of wounding, it recoiled with a ringing noise.", "아스티아게스가 검으로 그를 내리쳤으나, 상처를 내기는커녕 그것은 쟁그랑 소리를 내며 튕겨 나왔다."]], [["Phineus beheld this dreadful result of his unjust aggression, and felt confounded.", "피네우스는 자신의 부당한 침략이 빚은 이 끔찍한 결과를 보고 당황했다."], ["He called aloud to his friends, but got no answer; he touched them and found them stone.", "그는 벗들을 큰 소리로 불렀으나 아무 대답도 얻지 못했고, 그들을 만져 보니 돌이 되어 있었다."], ["Falling on his knees and stretching out his hands to Perseus, but turning his head away he begged for mercy.", "그는 무릎을 꿇고 페르세우스에게 두 손을 뻗으면서도 고개는 돌린 채 자비를 빌었다. \"모든 것을 가지시오.\""], ["“Take all,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “give me but my life.”", "\"다만 제 목숨만은 살려 주시오.\" \"비열한 겁쟁이여,\""], ["“Base coward,”", "페르세우스가 말했다."], ["said Perseus, “thus much I will grant you; no weapon shall touch you; moreover, you shall be preserved in my house as a memorial of these events.”", "\"이만큼은 내가 그대에게 허락하리라. 어떤 무기도 그대를 건드리지 않을 것이며, 게다가 그대는 이 사건들의 기념물로서 내 집에 보존되리라.\""], ["So saying, he held the Gorgon’s head to the side where Phineus was looking, and in the very form in which he knelt, with his hands outstretched and face averted, he became fixed immovably, a mass of stone!", "이렇게 말하며 그는 피네우스가 바라보고 있던 쪽으로 고르곤의 머리를 들이밀었고, 무릎을 꿇은 바로 그 모습 그대로, 두 손을 뻗고 얼굴을 돌린 채, 그는 미동도 없이 굳어 한 덩어리의 돌이 되어 버렸다!"]], [["The following allusion to Perseus is from Milman’s “Samor”:", "다음은 밀먼의 「사모르」에 나오는, 페르세우스에 관한 암시다."]], [["“As ’mid the fabled Libyan bridal stood Perseus in stern tranquillity of wrath, Half stood, half floated on his ankle-plumes Out-swelling, while the bright face on his shield Looked into stone the raging fray; so rose, But with no magic arms, wearing alone Th’ appalling and control of his firm look, The Briton Samor; at his rising awe Went abroad, and the riotous hall was mute.”", "\"옛이야기 속 리비아의 혼례 한가운데 페르세우스가 분노의 준엄한 평정으로 섰던 것처럼, 부풀어 오른 발목의 깃털 위에 반쯤 서고 반쯤 떠오른 채, 방패에 비친 그 빛나는 얼굴이 날뛰던 난투를 돌로 굳혀 버렸으니, 그처럼, 허나 아무런 마법의 무기도 없이, 오직 그 굳건한 표정의 위압과 통제만을 지닌 채, 브리튼인 사모르가 일어섰도다. 그가 일어서자 외경이 사방에 퍼지고, 소란하던 회당이 잠잠해졌도다.\""]], [["————", "————"]], [["GIANTS, SPHINX, PEGASUS AND CHIMÆRA, CENTAURS, GRIFFIN, AND PYGMIES\nMONSTERS, in the language of mythology, were beings of unnatural proportions or parts, usually regarded with terror, as possessing immense strength and ferocity, which they employed for the injury and annoyance of men.", "괴물이란 신화의 언어로 말하자면 그 비율이나 신체 부위가 자연스럽지 못한 존재들로서, 대개는 엄청난 힘과 흉포함을 지녔으며 그것을 인간에게 해를 끼치고 괴롭히는 데 사용했기에 두려움의 대상으로 여겨졌다."], ["Some of them were supposed to combine the members of different animals; such were the Sphinx and Chimæra; and to these all the terrible qualities of wild beasts were attributed, together with human sagacity and faculties.", "그중 일부는 서로 다른 동물의 신체 부위가 결합된 것으로 여겨졌는데, 스핑크스와 키마이라가 그러했다. 그리고 이들에게는 야수의 온갖 끔찍한 속성이 인간의 지혜와 능력과 함께 부여되었다."], ["Others, as the giants, differed from men chiefly in their size; and in this particular we must recognize a wide distinction among them.", "또 다른 괴물들, 이를테면 거인들은 주로 그 크기에서 인간과 달랐다. 그리고 바로 이 점에서 우리는 그들 사이의 커다란 차이를 인정하지 않을 수 없다."], ["The human giants, if so they may be called, such as the Cyclopes, Antæus, Orion, and others, must be supposed not to be altogether disproportioned to human beings, for they mingled in love and strife with them.", "인간 거인이라 부를 수 있다면 그러한 부류, 곧 키클로페스, 안타이오스, 오리온 등은 인간과 전혀 어울리지 않을 정도로 크지는 않았다고 보아야 하는데, 왜냐하면 이들은 인간과 더불어 사랑하고 다투며 어울렸기 때문이다."], ["But the superhuman giants, who warred with the gods, were of vastly larger dimensions.", "그러나 신들과 전쟁을 벌인 초인적인 거인들은 그 크기가 어마어마하게 더 컸다."], ["Tityus, we are told, when stretched on the plain, covered nine acres, and Enceladus required the whole of Mount Ætna to be laid upon him to keep him down.", "전하는 바에 따르면 티티오스는 평원에 누우면 아홉 에이커를 덮을 정도였고, 엔켈라도스를 눌러 두기 위해서는 아이트나 산 전체를 그 위에 얹어 놓아야 했다고 한다."]], [["We have already spoken of the war which the giants waged against the gods, and of its result.", "거인들이 신들을 상대로 벌인 전쟁과 그 결말에 관해서는 이미 이야기한 바 있다. 이 전쟁이 계속되는 동안 거인들은 만만찮은 적임을 입증했다."], ["While this war lasted the giants proved a formidable enemy.", "그들 가운데 일부는, 이를테면 브리아레오스처럼 백 개의 팔을 지녔고, 또 다른 일부는, 이를테면 티폰처럼 불을 내뿜었다."], ["Some of them, like Briareus, had a hundred arms; others, like Typhon, breathed out fire.", "한때 거인들은 신들에게 어찌나 큰 공포를 안겨 주었던지 신들은 이집트로 달아나 갖가지 모습으로 변신해 몸을 숨겼다."], ["At one time they put the gods to such fear that they fled into Egypt and hid themselves under various forms.", "제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]는 숫양의 모습을 취했는데, 그래서 나중에 이집트에서 굽은 뿔을 지닌 암몬 신으로 숭배받게 되었다."], ["Jupiter took the form of a ram, whence he was afterwards worshipped in Egypt as the god Ammon, with curved horns. Apollo became a crow, Bacchus a goat, Diana a cat, Juno a cow, Venus a fish, Mercury a bird.", "아폴론은 까마귀가, 디오니소스[로마의 〈바쿠스〉]는 염소가, 아르테미스[로마의 〈디아나〉]는 고양이가, 헤라[로마의 〈유노〉]는 암소가, 아프로디테[로마의 〈베누스〉]는 물고기가, 헤르메스[로마의 〈메르쿠리우스〉]는 새가 되었다."], ["At another time the giants attempted to climb up into heaven, and for that purpose took up the mountain Ossa and piled it on Pelion.[16] They were at last subdued by thunderbolts, which Minerva invented, and taught Vulcan and his Cyclopes to make for Jupiter.", "또 한번은 거인들이 하늘로 기어 올라가려고 시도했는데, 그 목적을 위해 오사 산을 들어올려 펠리온 산 위에 쌓아 올렸다.[16] 그들은 마침내 벼락에 의해 제압되었는데, 그 벼락은 아테나[로마의 〈미네르바〉]가 고안하여 헤파이스토스[로마의 〈불카누스〉]와 그의 키클로페스에게 제우스를 위해 만들도록 가르쳐 준 것이었다."]], [["# THE SPHINX", "# THE SPHINX"]], [["Laius, king of Thebes, was warned by an oracle that there was danger to his throne and life if his new-born son should be suffered to grow up.", "테바이의 왕 라이오스는 갓 태어난 아들이 자라도록 내버려 두면 자신의 왕좌와 목숨이 위태로워지리라는 신탁의 경고를 받았다."], ["He therefore committed the child to the care of a herdsman with orders to destroy him; but the herdsman, moved with pity, yet not daring entirely to disobey, tied up the child by the feet and left him hanging to the branch of a tree.", "그리하여 그는 아이를 한 목동에게 맡기며 죽여 버리라고 명령했다. 그러나 목동은 연민을 느낀 나머지, 그렇다고 명령을 완전히 거역할 엄두는 내지 못한 채, 아이의 두 발을 묶어 나뭇가지에 매달아 두었다."], ["In this condition the infant was found by a peasant, who carried him to his master and mistress, by whom he was adopted and called Œdipus, or Swollen-foot.", "이런 처지에 놓인 갓난아이를 한 농부가 발견하여 자신의 주인 부부에게 데려갔고, 그들은 아이를 양자로 들여 오이디푸스, 곧 〈부어오른 발〉이라 불렀다."]], [["Many years afterwards Laius being on his way to Delphi, accompanied only by one attendant, met in a narrow road a young man also driving in a chariot.", "여러 해가 지난 뒤 라이오스가 시종 하나만을 데리고 델포이로 가던 길에, 좁은 길에서 역시 전차를 몰고 오는 한 젊은이와 마주쳤다."], ["On his refusal to leave the way at their command the attendant killed one of his horses, and the stranger, filled with rage, slew both Laius and his attendant.", "길을 비키라는 그들의 명령에 젊은이가 따르지 않자 시종이 그의 말 한 마리를 죽였고, 분노에 찬 그 낯선 이는 라이오스와 그의 시종을 둘 다 살해했다."], ["The young man was Œdipus, who thus unknowingly became the slayer of his own father.", "그 젊은이가 바로 오이디푸스였으니, 그는 이렇게 자기도 모르는 사이에 제 아버지를 죽인 자가 되었다."]], [["Shortly after this event the city of Thebes was afflicted with a monster which infested the highroad.", "이 사건이 있은 지 얼마 지나지 않아 테바이 시는 큰길을 들끓게 하는 한 괴물 때문에 고통을 받았다."], ["It was called the Sphinx.", "그것은 스핑크스라 불렸다."], ["It had the body of a lion and the upper part of a woman.", "그것은 사자의 몸뚱이에 상반신은 여인의 모습이었다."], ["It lay crouched on the top of a rock, and arrested all travellers who came that way, proposing to them a riddle, with the condition that those who could solve it should pass safe, but those who failed should be killed.", "그것은 바위 꼭대기에 웅크리고 앉아 그 길로 오는 모든 나그네를 가로막고는, 그들에게 수수께끼를 내며 그것을 풀 수 있는 자는 무사히 지나가게 하되 풀지 못하는 자는 죽이겠다는 조건을 내걸었다."], ["Not one had yet succeeded in solving it, and all had been slain.", "아직 누구도 그것을 풀어내는 데 성공하지 못했고, 모두가 죽임을 당했다."], ["Œdipus was not daunted by these alarming accounts, but boldly advanced to the trial.", "오이디푸스는 이런 무시무시한 이야기에도 주눅 들지 않고 대담하게 시험에 나섰다. 스핑크스가 그에게 물었다."], ["The Sphinx asked him, “What animal is that which in the morning goes on four feet, at noon on two, and in the evening upon three?”", "\"아침에는 네 발로 걷고, 한낮에는 두 발로 걸으며, 저녁에는 세 발로 걷는 동물이 무엇이냐?\" 오이디푸스가 대답했다."], ["Œdipus replied, “Man, who in childhood creeps on hands and knees, in manhood walks erect, and in old age with the aid of a staff.”", "\"사람이다. 사람은 어린 시절에는 손과 무릎으로 기어다니고, 장년에는 똑바로 서서 걸으며, 노년에는 지팡이의 도움을 받아 걷는다.\""], ["The Sphinx was so mortified at the solving of her riddle that she cast herself down from the rock and perished.", "스핑크스는 자신의 수수께끼가 풀린 것에 너무도 분한 나머지 바위에서 몸을 던져 죽고 말았다."]], [["The gratitude of the people for their deliverance was so great that they made Œdipus their king, giving him in marriage their queen Jocasta.", "자신들을 구해 준 데 대한 백성들의 감사가 어찌나 컸던지 그들은 오이디푸스를 왕으로 삼고, 그들의 왕비 이오카스테를 그와 결혼시켰다."], ["Œdipus, ignorant of his parentage, had already become the slayer of his father; in marrying the queen he became the husband of his mother.", "자신의 혈통을 알지 못한 오이디푸스는 이미 제 아버지를 죽인 자가 되어 있었으며, 왕비와 결혼함으로써 제 어머니의 남편이 되었다."], ["These horrors remained undiscovered, till at length Thebes was afflicted with famine and pestilence, and the oracle being consulted, the double crime of Œdipus came to light.", "이 끔찍한 일들은 발각되지 않은 채로 있었으나, 마침내 테바이가 기근과 역병에 시달리게 되어 신탁에 물어보자 오이디푸스의 이중의 죄가 밝혀졌다."], ["Jocasta put an end to her own life, and Œdipus, seized with madness, tore out his eyes and wandered away from Thebes, dreaded and abandoned by all except his daughters, who faithfully adhered to him, till after a tedious period of miserable wandering he found the termination of his wretched life.", "이오카스테는 스스로 목숨을 끊었고, 광기에 사로잡힌 오이디푸스는 제 두 눈을 뽑아내고 테바이를 떠나 떠돌았는데, 그에게 충실히 매달린 딸들을 제외한 모든 이들에게 두려움의 대상이자 버림받은 자가 되었으며, 비참한 방랑의 지루한 세월을 보낸 끝에 그 불행한 삶의 종착점을 맞이했다."]], [["When Perseus cut off Medusa’s head, the blood sinking into the earth produced the winged horse Pegasus.", "페르세우스가 메두사의 머리를 베어 냈을 때, 땅속으로 스며든 그 피에서 날개 달린 말 페가소스가 생겨났다."], ["Minerva caught him and tamed him and presented him to the Muses.", "아테나가 그를 붙잡아 길들인 뒤 무사이[무사 여신들]에게 선물했다."], ["The fountain Hippocrene, on the Muses’ mountain Helicon, was opened by a kick from his hoof.", "무사이의 산인 헬리콘 산에 있는 히포크레네 샘은 그의 발굽이 한 번 차서 생겨난 것이었다."]], [["The Chimæra was a fearful monster, breathing fire.", "키마이라는 불을 내뿜는 무시무시한 괴물이었다."], ["The fore part of its body was a compound of the lion and the goat, and the hind part a dragon’s.", "그 몸의 앞부분은 사자와 염소가 결합된 것이었고, 뒷부분은 용의 것이었다."], ["It made great havoc in Lycia, so that the king, Iobates, sought for some hero to destroy it.", "그것이 리키아에서 큰 재앙을 일으키자, 그곳의 왕 이오바테스는 그것을 처치할 영웅을 찾았다."], ["At that time there arrived at his court a gallant young warrior, whose name was Bellerophon.", "바로 그 무렵 그의 궁정에 한 용맹한 젊은 전사가 도착했는데, 그의 이름은 벨레로폰이었다."], ["He brought letters from Prœtus, the son-in-law of Iobates, recommending Bellerophon in the warmest terms as an unconquerable hero, but added at the close a request to his father-in-law to put him to death.", "그는 이오바테스의 사위인 프로이토스가 보낸 편지를 가져왔는데, 그 편지는 벨레로폰을 천하무적의 영웅이라며 더없이 따뜻한 말로 추천하고 있었으나, 끝에 가서는 장인에게 그를 죽여 달라는 부탁을 덧붙이고 있었다."], ["The reason was that Prœtus was jealous of him, suspecting that his wife Antea looked with too much admiration on the young warrior.", "그 까닭은 프로이토스가 그를 질투했기 때문이니, 자신의 아내 안테이아가 그 젊은 전사를 지나치게 흠모의 눈길로 바라본다고 의심한 탓이었다."], ["From this instance of Bellerophon being unconsciously the bearer of his own death warrant, the expression “Bellerophontic letters” arose, to describe any species of communication which a person is made the bearer of, containing matter prejudicial to himself.", "벨레로폰이 자기도 모르게 자신의 사형 선고문을 지니고 다닌 이 사례에서, 〈벨레로폰의 편지〉라는 표현이 생겨났는데, 이는 어떤 이가 자신에게 해로운 내용이 담긴 글을 자기도 모른 채 전달하게 되는 온갖 종류의 전언을 가리키는 말이다."]], [["Iobates, on perusing the letters, was puzzled what to do, not willing to violate the claims of hospitality, yet wishing to oblige his son-in-law.", "이오바테스는 그 편지를 읽고 어찌해야 할지 난감해했으니, 환대의 도리를 저버리고 싶지도 않았으나 사위의 뜻을 들어주고 싶기도 했던 것이다."], ["A lucky thought occurred to him, to send Bellerophon to combat with the Chimæra.", "그러던 차에 그에게 한 가지 묘안이 떠올랐는데, 벨레로폰을 키마이라와 싸우도록 보내는 것이었다."], ["Bellerophon accepted the proposal, but before proceeding to the combat consulted the soothsayer Polyidus, who advised him to procure if possible the horse Pegasus for the conflict.", "벨레로폰은 그 제안을 받아들였으나, 싸움에 나서기 전에 예언자 폴리이도스에게 자문을 구했고, 그는 가능하다면 그 싸움을 위해 페가소스라는 말을 구해 두라고 조언했다."], ["For this purpose he directed him to pass the night in the temple of Minerva.", "그 목적을 위해 그는 벨레로폰에게 아테나의 신전에서 하룻밤을 보내라고 일러 주었다."], ["He did so, and as he slept Minerva came to him and gave him a golden bridle.", "벨레로폰이 그대로 하고 잠들어 있는 동안 아테나가 그에게 다가와 황금 굴레를 주었다."], ["When he awoke the bridle remained in his hand.", "그가 깨어나 보니 그 굴레가 손에 남아 있었다."], ["Minerva also showed him Pegasus drinking at the well of Pirene, and at sight of the bridle the winged steed came willingly and suffered himself to be taken.", "아테나는 또한 그에게 페이레네 샘에서 물을 마시고 있는 페가소스를 보여 주었고, 굴레를 본 그 날개 달린 준마는 순순히 다가와 잡히기를 자청했다."], ["Bellerophon mounted him, rose with him into the air, soon found the Chimæra, and gained an easy victory over the monster.", "벨레로폰이 그 위에 올라타 함께 하늘로 솟아오르자, 이내 키마이라를 찾아내어 그 괴물을 손쉽게 무찔렀다."]], [["After the conquest of the Chimæra Bellerophon was exposed to further trials and labors by his unfriendly host, but by the aid of Pegasus he triumphed in them all, till at length Iobates, seeing that the hero was a special favorite of the gods, gave him his daughter in marriage and made him his successor on the throne.", "키마이라를 정복한 뒤에도 벨레로폰은 우호적이지 않은 그 주인 때문에 더 많은 시험과 노역에 시달렸으나, 페가소스의 도움으로 그 모든 것에서 승리를 거두었고, 마침내 이오바테스는 그 영웅이 신들의 각별한 총애를 받는 자임을 알아보고 자신의 딸을 그와 결혼시켜 왕위의 후계자로 삼았다."], ["At last Bellerophon by his pride and presumption drew upon himself the anger of the gods; it is said he even attempted to fly up into heaven on his winged steed, but Jupiter sent a gadfly which stung Pegasus and made him throw his rider, who became lame and blind in consequence.", "그러나 끝내 벨레로폰은 자신의 오만과 자만으로 인해 신들의 분노를 스스로 불러들였다. 전하는 바에 따르면 그는 심지어 자신의 날개 달린 준마를 타고 하늘로 날아오르려 시도하기까지 했으나, 제우스가 등에 한 마리를 보내어 페가소스를 쏘게 하니 페가소스가 그 등에 탄 자를 내던졌고, 그 결과 벨레로폰은 절름발이에 장님이 되었다."], ["After this Bellerophon wandered lonely through the Aleian field, avoiding the paths of men, and died miserably.", "그 후 벨레로폰은 인간의 길을 피하며 알레이온 평원을 외로이 떠돌다가 비참하게 죽었다."]], [["Milton alludes to Bellerophon in the beginning of the seventh book of “Paradise Lost”:", "밀턴은 『실낙원』 제7권의 첫머리에서 벨레로폰을 언급한다."]], [["“Descend from Heaven, Urania, by that name If rightly thou art called, whose voice divine Following above the Olympian hill I soar, Above the flight of Pegasean wing.", "\"하늘에서 내려오소서, 우라니아여, 그 이름이 당신을 옳게 부르는 것이라면, 그 신성한 목소리를 좇아 나는 올림포스 언덕 위로 솟아오르며, 페가소스의 날갯짓보다 더 높이 날았나이다. 당신에게 이끌려, 나는 하늘들의 하늘로 감히 올라갔으니, 한낱 지상의 손님으로서, 천상의 공기를 들이마셨나이다 (당신이 누그러뜨려 주신 덕분에)."], ["Upled by thee, Into the Heaven of Heavens I have presumed, An earthly guest, and drawn empyreal air (Thy tempering); with like safety guided down Return me to my native element; Lest from this flying steed unreined (as once Bellerophon, though from a lower sphere), Dismounted on the Aleian field I fall, Erroneous there to wander and forlorn.”", "마찬가지로 안전하게 다시 아래로 인도받아 내 본래의 자리로 돌려보내소서. 행여 이 고삐 풀린 날개 달린 준마에서 (일찍이 벨레로폰이 그러했듯, 비록 그는 더 낮은 곳에서였으나), 알레이온 평원에 내동댕이쳐져 떨어질까 두려우니, 그곳에서 길을 잃고 버림받은 채 헤맬까 두렵나이다.\""]], [["Young, in his “Night Thoughts,” speaking of the sceptic, says:", "영은 그의 『밤의 상념』에서 회의론자를 두고 이렇게 말한다."]], [["“He whose blind thought futurity denies, Unconscious bears, Bellerophon, like thee His own indictment; he condemns himself.", "\"그 눈먼 생각이 내세를 부정하는 자는, 벨레로폰이여, 그대처럼 자기도 모르게 제 죄상을 지니고 다니나니, 그는 스스로를 단죄한다. 그의 가슴을 읽는 자는 불멸의 삶을 읽거나, 아니면 그곳에서 자연이 제 자식들을 속이려 거짓을 적어 놓았음을 읽으리라."], ["Who reads his bosom reads immortal life, Or nature there, imposing on her sons, Has written fables; man was made a lie.” Vol.", "인간은 거짓으로 지어졌느니.\""], ["II., p. 12.", "제2권, 12쪽."]], [["Pegasus, being the horse of the Muses, has always been at the service of the poets.", "페가소스는 무사이의 말이었던 만큼 늘 시인들을 위해 봉사해 왔다."], ["Schiller tells a pretty story of his having been sold by a needy poet and put to the cart and the plough.", "실러는 페가소스가 어느 가난한 시인에게 팔려가 수레와 쟁기에 매이게 된 사연을 아름다운 이야기로 들려준다."], ["He was not fit for such service, and his clownish master could make nothing of him.", "페가소스는 그런 일에 적합하지 않았고, 그 어리석은 주인은 도무지 그를 부릴 수가 없었다."], ["But a youth stepped forth and asked leave to try him.", "그러나 한 젊은이가 나서서 그를 한번 다루어 보겠다고 청했다."], ["As soon as he was seated on his back the horse, which had appeared at first vicious, and afterwards spirit-broken, rose kingly, a spirit, a god, unfolded the splendor of his wings, and soared towards heaven.", "그가 등에 올라타자마자, 처음에는 사나워 보였다가 나중에는 기가 꺾인 듯했던 그 말이 왕처럼, 하나의 정령처럼, 하나의 신처럼 일어서더니, 찬란한 날개를 펼치고 하늘을 향해 솟아올랐다."], ["Our own poet Longfellow also records an adventure of this famous steed in his “Pegasus in Pound.”", "우리의 시인 롱펠로 역시 그의 『우리에 갇힌 페가소스』에서 이 유명한 준마의 모험을 기록하고 있다."]], [["Shakspeare alludes to Pegasus in “Henry IV.,” where Vernon describes Prince Henry:", "셰익스피어는 『헨리 4세』에서 페가소스를 언급하는데, 버넌이 헨리 왕자를 묘사하는 대목이다."]], [["“I saw young Harry, with his beaver on, His cuishes on his thighs, gallantly armed, Rise from the ground like feathered Mercury, And vaulted with such ease into his seat, As if an angel dropped down from the clouds, To turn and wind a fiery Pegasus, And witch the world with noble horsemanship.”", "\"나는 보았네, 젊은 해리가 투구를 쓰고, 넓적다리에 정강이받이를 두른 채 늠름하게 무장하고, 날개 달린 메르쿠리우스처럼 땅에서 일어나, 구름에서 천사가 내려와 불같은 페가소스를 돌리고 휘몰며 고귀한 마술(馬術)로 세상을 홀리려는 듯, 그토록 수월하게 안장 위로 뛰어오르는 것을.\""]], [["# THE CENTAURS", "# THE CENTAURS"]], [["These monsters were represented as men from the head to the loins, while the remainder of the body was that of a horse.", "이 괴물들은 머리에서 허리까지는 인간의 모습이었으나, 나머지 몸은 말의 것으로 묘사되었다."], ["The ancients were too fond of a horse to consider the union of his nature with man’s as forming a very degraded compound, and accordingly the Centaur is the only one of the fancied monsters of antiquity to which any good traits are assigned.", "고대인들은 말을 너무도 아꼈던 까닭에 말의 본성을 인간의 본성과 결합하는 것을 그리 천박한 조합으로 여기지 않았으며, 따라서 켄타우로스는 고대의 상상 속 괴물들 가운데 어떤 좋은 특성이 부여된 유일한 존재다."], ["The Centaurs were admitted to the companionship of man, and at the marriage of Pirithous with Hippodamia they were among the guests.", "켄타우로스족은 인간의 벗으로 받아들여졌으며, 페이리토오스와 히포다메이아의 결혼식에서도 그들은 손님들 가운데 끼어 있었다."], ["At the feast Eurytion, one of the Centaurs, becoming intoxicated with the wine, attempted to offer violence to the bride; the other Centaurs followed his example, and a dreadful conflict arose in which several of them were slain.", "잔치에서 켄타우로스족 가운데 하나인 에우리티온이 술에 취해 신부에게 폭력을 가하려 들자, 다른 켄타우로스들도 그의 본을 따랐고, 그리하여 끔찍한 싸움이 벌어져 그들 가운데 여럿이 죽임을 당했다."], ["This is the celebrated battle of the Lapithæ and Centaurs, a favorite subject with the sculptors and poets of antiquity.", "이것이 바로 그 유명한 라피타이족과 켄타우로스족의 싸움으로, 고대의 조각가들과 시인들이 즐겨 다룬 소재였다."]], [["But not all the Centaurs were like the rude guests of Pirithous.", "그러나 모든 켄타우로스가 페이리토오스의 무례한 손님들 같지는 않았다."], ["Chiron was instructed by Apollo and Diana, and was renowned for his skill in hunting, medicine, music, and the art of prophecy.", "케이론은 아폴론과 아르테미스에게 가르침을 받았으며, 사냥과 의술과 음악, 그리고 예언술에 능한 것으로 이름이 높았다."], ["The most distinguished heroes of Grecian story were his pupils.", "그리스 이야기에 등장하는 가장 뛰어난 영웅들이 그의 제자였다."], ["Among the rest the infant Æsculapius was intrusted to his charge by Apollo, his father.", "그중에서도 갓난아이 아스클레피오스는 그의 아버지 아폴론에 의해 케이론의 보살핌에 맡겨졌다."], ["When the sage returned to his home bearing the infant, his daughter Ocyroe came forth to meet him, and at sight of the child burst forth into a prophetic strain (for she was a prophetess), foretelling the glory that he was to achieve.", "그 현자가 갓난아이를 안고 집으로 돌아오자 그의 딸 오키로에가 그를 맞으러 나왔고, 아이를 보자마자 예언의 가락을 터뜨려(그녀는 예언녀였던 것이다) 그 아이가 이룩하게 될 영광을 예언했다."], ["Æsculapius when grown up became a renowned physician, and even in one instance succeeded in restoring the dead to life.", "아스클레피오스는 자라나서 이름난 의사가 되었으며, 심지어 한번은 죽은 자를 되살리는 데 성공하기까지 했다."], ["Pluto resented this, and Jupiter, at his request, struck the bold physician with lightning, and killed him, but after his death received him into the number of the gods.", "하데스[로마의 〈플루톤〉]가 이를 못마땅하게 여기자, 제우스는 그의 청에 따라 그 대담한 의사를 벼락으로 내리쳐 죽였으나, 그가 죽은 뒤에는 신들의 무리에 받아들였다."]], [["Chiron was the wisest and justest of all the Centaurs, and at his death Jupiter placed him among the stars as the constellation Sagittarius.", "케이론은 모든 켄타우로스 가운데 가장 지혜롭고 가장 의로운 자였으며, 그가 죽자 제우스는 그를 별들 사이에 두어 궁수자리라는 별자리로 삼았다."]], [["# THE PYGMIES", "# THE PYGMIES"]], [["The Pygmies were a nation of dwarfs, so called from a Greek word which means the cubit or measure of about thirteen inches, which was said to be the height of these people.", "피그미족은 난쟁이들의 종족으로, 약 13인치쯤 되는 길이의 단위인 큐빗을 뜻하는 그리스어 단어에서 그 이름이 비롯되었는데, 이것이 바로 그 종족의 키였다고 전해진다."], ["They lived near the sources of the Nile, or according to others, in India.", "그들은 나일 강의 발원지 근처에, 혹은 다른 이들에 따르면 인도에 살았다."], ["Homer tells us that the cranes used to migrate every winter to the Pygmies’ country, and their appearance was the signal of bloody warfare to the puny inhabitants, who had to take up arms to defend their cornfields against the rapacious strangers.", "호메로스는 두루미들이 겨울마다 피그미족의 나라로 옮겨 가곤 했다고 우리에게 전하는데, 그 두루미들이 나타나는 것은 그 자그마한 주민들에게 피비린내 나는 전쟁의 신호였으니, 그들은 탐욕스러운 침입자들로부터 자신들의 밀밭을 지키기 위해 무기를 들어야만 했다."], ["The Pygmies and their enemies the Cranes form the subject of several works of art.", "피그미족과 그들의 적인 두루미는 여러 미술 작품의 소재가 되었다."]], [["Later writers tell of an army of Pygmies which finding Hercules asleep made preparations to attack him, as if they were about to attack a city.", "후대의 작가들은 잠들어 있는 헤라클레스를 발견한 피그미족의 한 부대가 마치 도시를 공격하려는 듯 그를 칠 준비를 했다는 이야기를 들려준다."], ["But the hero, awaking, laughed at the little warriors, wrapped some of them up in his lion’s skin, and carried them to Eurystheus.", "그러나 잠에서 깨어난 영웅은 그 자그마한 전사들을 보고 웃으며, 그들 가운데 일부를 자신의 사자 가죽으로 둘둘 말아 에우리스테우스에게 가져갔다."]], [["Milton uses the Pygmies for a simile, “Paradise Lost,” Book I.:", "밀턴은 『실낙원』 제1권에서 피그미족을 직유에 사용한다."]], [["“. . . like that Pygmæan race Beyond the Indian mount, or fairy elves Whose midnight revels by a forest side, Or fountain, some belated peasant sees (Or dreams he sees), while overhead the moon Sits arbitress, and nearer to the earth Wheels her pale course; they on their mirth and dance Intent, with jocund music charm his ear. At once with joy and fear his heart rebounds.”", "\"……저 인도 너머 산 저편의 피그미족 무리처럼, 혹은 요정 엘프들처럼, 숲가나 샘가에서 벌이는 그들의 한밤중 잔치를 때늦은 어느 농부가 바라보는 (혹은 본다고 꿈꾸는) 동안, 머리 위에서는 달이 심판자로 앉아 땅에 더 가까이 그 창백한 행로를 굴리니, 그들은 흥겨움과 춤에 몰두하여 유쾌한 가락으로 그의 귀를 홀린다. 이내 그의 심장은 기쁨과 두려움으로 두근거린다.\""]], [["# THE GRIFFIN, OR GRYPHON", "# THE GRIFFIN, OR GRYPHON"]], [["The Griffin is a monster with the body of a lion, the head and wings of an eagle, and back covered with feathers.", "그리핀은 사자의 몸뚱이에 독수리의 머리와 날개를 지녔으며, 등은 깃털로 덮인 괴물이다."], ["Like birds it builds its nest, and instead of an egg lays an agate therein.", "그것은 새들처럼 둥지를 틀고, 그 속에 알 대신 마노(瑪瑙)를 낳는다."], ["It has long claws and talons of such a size that the people of that country make them into drinking-cups.", "그것은 긴 발톱과 갈고리발톱을 지녔는데, 그 크기가 어찌나 큰지 그 나라 사람들은 그것으로 술잔을 만든다."], ["India was assigned as the native country of the Griffins.", "인도가 그리핀의 본고장으로 정해졌다."], ["They found gold in the mountains and build their nests of it, for which reason their nests were very tempting to the hunters, and they were forced to keep vigilant guard over them.", "그것들은 산에서 황금을 발견하여 그것으로 둥지를 짓는데, 그런 까닭에 그 둥지는 사냥꾼들에게 몹시 탐나는 것이어서, 그것들은 둥지를 부지런히 지켜야만 했다."], ["Their instinct led them to know where buried treasures lay, and they did their best to keep plunderers at a distance.", "그것들은 본능에 따라 묻힌 보물이 어디 있는지 알아냈고, 약탈자들을 멀리 떼어 놓으려고 온 힘을 다했다."], ["The Arimaspians, among whom the Griffins flourished, were a one-eyed people of Scythia.", "그리핀이 번성하던 곳의 주민인 아리마스포이족은 스키티아에 사는 외눈박이 종족이었다."]], [["Milton borrows a simile from the Griffins, “Paradise Lost,” Book II.:", "밀턴은 『실낙원』 제2권에서 그리핀에게서 직유 하나를 빌려 온다."]], [["“As when a Gryphon through the wilderness, With winged course, o’er hill and moory dale, Pursues the Arimaspian who by stealth Hath from his wakeful custody purloined His guarded gold,”", "\"그리핀 한 마리가 황야를 가로질러, 날개 달린 행로로, 언덕과 황량한 골짜기를 넘어, 그 깨어 있는 감시를 틈타 몰래 제가 지키던 황금을 훔쳐 간 아리마스포이족을 뒤쫓듯이.\" 등등."], ["etc. ————", "————"]], [["# THE GOLDEN FLEECE", "# THE GOLDEN FLEECE"]], [["IN very ancient times there lived in Thessaly a king and queen named Athamas and Nephele.", "아주 먼 옛날에 테살리아에는 아타마스와 네펠레라는 이름의 왕과 왕비가 살고 있었다."], ["They had two children, a boy and a girl.", "두 사람에게는 아들과 딸, 둘의 자녀가 있었다."], ["After a time Athamas grew indifferent to his wife, put her away, and took another.", "그러나 얼마 후 아타마스는 아내에게 무관심해져 그녀를 내쫓고 다른 여인을 맞아들였다."], ["Nephele suspected danger to her children from the influence of the stepmother, and took measures to send them out of her reach.", "네펠레는 새어머니의 영향으로 자기 자녀들에게 위험이 닥칠 것을 염려하여 그들을 그녀의 손이 닿지 않는 곳으로 보내기 위한 방책을 마련했다."], ["Mercury assisted her, and gave her a ram with a golden fleece, on which she set the two children, trusting that the ram would convey them to a place of safety.", "헤르메스[로마의 〈메르쿠리우스〉]가 그녀를 도와 황금 양털을 지닌 숫양 한 마리를 주었고, 그녀는 그 숫양이 두 아이를 안전한 곳으로 데려다 주리라 믿고 그 위에 두 아이를 태웠다."], ["The ram vaulted into the air with the children on his back, taking his course to the East, till when crossing the strait that divides Europe and Asia, the girl, whose name was Helle, fell from his back into the sea, which from her was called the Hellespont,—now the Dardanelles.", "숫양은 아이들을 등에 태운 채 공중으로 솟아올라 동쪽을 향해 나아갔는데, 유럽과 아시아를 가르는 해협을 건너던 중 헬레라는 이름의 소녀가 숫양의 등에서 바다로 떨어졌으며, 그 바다는 그녀의 이름을 따서 헬레스폰토스라고 불리게 되었으니, 지금의 다르다넬스 해협이다."], ["The ram continued his career till he reached the kingdom of Colchis, on the eastern shore of the Black Sea, where he safely landed the boy Phryxus, who was hospitably received by Æetes, king of the country.", "숫양은 계속 나아가 흑해 동쪽 기슭에 자리한 콜키스 왕국에 이르렀고, 그곳에 소년 프릭소스를 무사히 내려놓았으며, 그 나라의 왕인 아이에테스는 그를 융숭하게 맞아들였다."], ["Phryxus sacrificed the ram to Jupiter, and gave the Golden Fleece to Æetes, who placed it in a consecrated grove, under the care of a sleepless dragon.", "프릭소스는 그 숫양을 제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]에게 제물로 바치고 황금 양털을 아이에테스에게 주었으며, 아이에테스는 그것을 신성한 숲 속에 두어 잠들지 않는 용의 보호를 받게 했다."]], [["There was another kingdom in Thessaly near to that of Athamas, and ruled over by a relative of his.", "테살리아에는 아타마스의 왕국에 가까운 또 다른 왕국이 있었는데, 그의 친척이 다스리고 있었다."], ["The king Æson, being tired of the cares of government, surrendered his crown to his brother Pelias on condition that he should hold it only during the minority of Jason, the son of Æson.", "아이손 왕은 통치의 근심에 지친 나머지 자기 왕관을 동생 펠리아스에게 넘겨주되, 아이손의 아들 이아손이 미성년인 동안에만 그것을 보유한다는 조건을 달았다."], ["When Jason was grown up and came to demand the crown from his uncle, Pelias pretended to be willing to yield it, but at the same time suggested to the young man the glorious adventure of going in quest of the Golden Fleece, which it was well known was in the kingdom of Colchis, and was, as Pelias pretended, the rightful property of their family.", "이아손이 성장하여 숙부에게 와서 왕관을 요구하자, 펠리아스는 그것을 기꺼이 내어줄 듯이 꾸미면서도, 동시에 그 젊은이에게 황금 양털을 찾으러 떠나는 영광스러운 모험을 권했는데, 그 양털은 콜키스 왕국에 있음이 익히 알려져 있었고, 펠리아스가 주장하기로는 본디 그들 가문의 정당한 소유물이었다."], ["Jason was pleased with the thought, and forthwith made preparations for the expedition.", "이아손은 그 생각에 흡족해하며 곧바로 원정 준비에 들어갔다."], ["At that time the only species of navigation known to the Greeks consisted of small boats or canoes hollowed out from trunks of trees, so that when Jason employed Argus to build him a vessel capable of containing fifty men, it was considered a gigantic undertaking.", "그 시절 그리스인들에게 알려진 항해의 유일한 형태란 나무줄기를 파내어 만든 작은 배나 카누뿐이었으니, 이아손이 아르고스를 고용하여 쉰 명을 태울 수 있는 배를 짓게 했을 때 그것은 어마어마한 사업으로 여겨졌다."], ["It was accomplished, however, and the vessel named “Argo,” from the name of the builder.", "그러나 그 일은 완수되었고, 배는 만든 이의 이름을 따서 〈아르고〉라 불렸다."], ["Jason sent his invitation to all the adventurous young men of Greece, and soon found himself at the head of a band of bold youths, many of whom afterwards were renowned among the heroes and demigods of Greece.", "이아손은 그리스의 모험심 강한 젊은이들 모두에게 초대장을 보냈고, 곧 대담한 젊은이들의 무리를 이끄는 우두머리가 되었는데, 그들 가운데 많은 이가 훗날 그리스의 영웅과 반신반인 사이에서 이름을 떨치게 되었다."], ["Hercules, Theseus, Orpheus, and Nestor were among them.", "헤라클레스, 테세우스, 오르페우스, 네스토르도 그들 가운데 있었다."], ["They are called the Argonauts, from the name of their vessel.", "이들은 그들의 배 이름을 따서 아르고나우타이[아르고호 선원들]라고 불린다."]], [["The “Argo” with her crew of heroes left the shores of Thessaly and having touched at the Island of Lemnos, thence crossed to Mysia and thence to Thrace.", "영웅들을 태운 〈아르고〉호는 테살리아의 기슭을 떠나 렘노스 섬에 들렀다가, 거기서 미시아로 건너갔고, 다시 트라키아로 갔다."], ["Here they found the sage Phineus, and from him received instruction as to their future course.", "이곳에서 그들은 현자 피네우스를 만나 앞으로의 항로에 관한 가르침을 받았다."], ["It seems the entrance of the Euxine Sea was impeded by two small rocky islands, which floated on the surface, and in their tossings and heavings occasionally came together, crushing and grinding to atoms any object that might be caught between them.", "에욱세이노스[흑해]의 입구는 작은 바위섬 둘에 가로막혀 있었던 듯한데, 그 섬들은 수면 위를 떠다니다가 출렁이고 솟구치는 가운데 이따금 서로 맞부딪쳐, 그 사이에 끼이는 무엇이든 짓이겨 가루로 만들어 버렸다."], ["They were called the Symplegades, or Clashing Islands.", "그 섬들은 심플레가데스, 곧 맞부딪치는 섬들이라 불렸다."], ["Phineus instructed the Argonauts how to pass this dangerous strait.", "피네우스는 아르고나우타이에게 이 위험한 해협을 통과하는 방법을 일러 주었다."], ["When they reached the islands they let go a dove, which took her way between the rocks, and passed in safety, only losing some feathers of her tail.", "그들은 그 섬들에 이르자 비둘기 한 마리를 풀어놓았는데, 비둘기는 바위들 사이로 길을 잡아 무사히 통과하였고 꽁지깃 몇 개만을 잃었을 뿐이었다."], ["Jason and his men seized the favorable moment of the rebound, plied their oars with vigor, and passed safe through, though the islands closed behind them, and actually grazed their stern.", "이아손과 그의 부하들은 바위가 되튀는 순간의 호기를 놓치지 않고 힘차게 노를 저어 무사히 빠져나갔으니, 비록 섬들이 그들의 뒤에서 맞닫혀 실제로 고물을 스치기는 했어도 그러했다."], ["They now rowed along the shore till they arrived at the eastern end of the sea, and landed at the kingdom of Colchis.", "이제 그들은 바다의 동쪽 끝에 다다를 때까지 기슭을 따라 노를 저어 가서 콜키스 왕국에 상륙했다."]], [["Jason made known his message to the Colchian king, Æetes, who consented to give up the golden fleece if Jason would yoke to the plough two fire-breathing bulls with brazen feet, and sow the teeth of the dragon which Cadmus had slain, and from which it was well known that a crop of armed men would spring up, who would turn their weapons against their producer.", "이아손은 콜키스의 왕 아이에테스에게 자기 용건을 알렸고, 왕은 이아손이 청동 발굽을 지니고 불을 내뿜는 황소 두 마리에게 쟁기를 메우고, 카드모스가 죽인 용의 이빨을 뿌린다는 조건을 충족시키면 황금 양털을 내어주겠노라고 동의했다. 그 이빨에서는 무장한 군사들이 솟아나리라는 것이 익히 알려져 있었고, 그들은 자기를 만들어 낸 자에게 무기를 겨눌 것이었다."], ["Jason accepted the conditions, and a time was set for making the experiment.", "이아손은 그 조건을 받아들였고, 그 시도를 행할 날이 정해졌다."], ["Previously, however, he found means to plead his cause to Medea, daughter of the king.", "그러나 그에 앞서 그는 왕의 딸 메데이아에게 자기 사정을 호소할 방도를 찾아냈다."], ["He promised her marriage, and as they stood before the altar of Hecate, called the goddess to witness his oath.", "그는 그녀에게 혼인을 약속했고, 두 사람이 헤카테의 제단 앞에 섰을 때 그 여신을 자기 맹세의 증인으로 불렀다."], ["Medea yielded, and by her aid, for she was a potent sorceress, he was furnished with a charm, by which he could encounter safely the breath of the fire-breathing bulls and the weapons of the armed men.", "메데이아는 마음이 기울었고, 그녀가 강력한 마법사였던 까닭에 그녀의 도움으로 그는 부적 하나를 얻어, 그것으로 불을 내뿜는 황소들의 숨결과 무장한 군사들의 무기를 무사히 맞설 수 있게 되었다."]], [["At the time appointed, the people assembled at the grove of Mars, and the king assumed his royal seat, while the multitude covered the hill-sides.", "정해진 때가 되자 사람들은 아레스[로마의 〈마르스〉]의 숲에 모였고, 왕은 자기 옥좌에 앉았으며, 군중은 언덕 비탈을 뒤덮었다."], ["The brazen-footed bulls rushed in, breathing fire from their nostrils that burned up the herbage as they passed.", "청동 발굽의 황소들이 콧구멍에서 불길을 내뿜으며 달려드니 그 불길은 지나는 자리마다 풀을 태워 버렸다."], ["The sound was like the roar of a furnace, and the smoke like that of water upon quick-lime.", "그 소리는 용광로의 포효 같았고, 연기는 생석회에 물을 부었을 때 피어오르는 것과 같았다."], ["Jason advanced boldly to meet them.", "이아손은 대담하게 그것들을 맞으러 나아갔다."], ["His friends, the chosen heroes of Greece, trembled to behold him.", "그의 벗들, 곧 그리스에서 뽑힌 영웅들은 그를 바라보며 몸을 떨었다."], ["Regardless of the burning breath, he soothed their rage with his voice, patted their necks with fearless hand, and adroitly slipped over them the yoke, and compelled them to drag the plough.", "그는 타오르는 숨결에도 아랑곳없이 목소리로 그것들의 분노를 달래고, 두려움 없는 손으로 그 목덜미를 쓰다듬었으며, 능숙하게 멍에를 씌워 쟁기를 끌게 했다."], ["The Colchians were amazed; the Greeks shouted for joy.", "콜키스인들은 경악했고, 그리스인들은 기뻐 환호했다."], ["Jason next proceeded to sow the dragon’s teeth and plough them in.", "이아손은 다음으로 용의 이빨을 뿌리고 쟁기로 갈아 묻었다."], ["And soon the crop of armed men sprang up, and, wonderful to relate!", "그러자 곧 무장한 군사들의 무리가 솟아났는데, 놀랍게도!"], ["no sooner had they reached the surface than they began to brandish their weapons and rush upon Jason.", "그들은 땅 위로 올라오기가 무섭게 무기를 휘두르며 이아손에게 달려들었다."], ["The Greeks trembled for their hero, and even she who had provided him a way of safety and taught him how to use it, Medea herself, grew pale with fear.", "그리스인들은 자기네 영웅을 위해 떨었고, 그에게 안전의 길을 마련해 주고 그 사용법을 가르쳐 준 메데이아조차도 두려움에 새파랗게 질렸다."], ["Jason for a time kept his assailants at bay with his sword and shield, till, finding their numbers overwhelming, he resorted to the charm which Medea had taught him, seized a stone and threw it in the midst of his foes.", "이아손은 한동안 칼과 방패로 공격자들을 막아 냈으나, 그 수가 압도적임을 깨닫고 메데이아가 가르쳐 준 부적을 써서 돌 하나를 집어 적들 한가운데로 던졌다."], ["They immediately turned their arms against one another, and soon there was not one of the dragon’s brood left alive.", "그들은 즉시 서로에게 무기를 겨누었고, 곧 용의 종자들 가운데 살아남은 자는 하나도 없게 되었다."], ["The Greeks embraced their hero, and Medea, if she dared, would have embraced him too.", "그리스인들은 자기네 영웅을 끌어안았고, 메데이아도 감히 그럴 수 있었다면 그를 끌어안았을 것이다."]], [["It remained to lull to sleep the dragon that guarded the fleece, and this was done by scattering over him a few drops of a preparation which Medea had supplied.", "이제 남은 일은 양털을 지키는 용을 잠재우는 것이었으니, 이는 메데이아가 마련해 준 조제약 몇 방울을 그 위에 뿌림으로써 이루어졌다."], ["At the smell he relaxed his rage, stood for a moment motionless, then shut those great round eyes, that had never been known to shut before, and turned over on his side, fast asleep.", "그 냄새를 맡자 용은 분노를 누그러뜨리며 잠시 꼼짝 않고 서 있다가, 일찍이 한 번도 감긴 적이 없던 그 크고 둥근 눈을 감고 옆으로 몸을 돌리더니 깊이 잠들어 버렸다."], ["Jason seized the fleece and with his friends and Medea accompanying, hastened to their vessel before Æetes the king could arrest their departure, and made the best of their way back to Thessaly, where they arrived safe, and Jason delivered the fleece to Pelias, and dedicated the “Argo”", "이아손은 양털을 움켜쥐고 벗들과 메데이아를 동반하여, 아이에테스 왕이 그들의 출발을 막기 전에 서둘러 배로 달려가 테살리아로 돌아가는 길을 재촉했으며, 그곳에 무사히 도착하여 이아손은 양털을 펠리아스에게 넘겨주고 〈아르고〉호를 포세이돈[로마의 〈넵투누스〉]에게 봉헌했다."], ["to Neptune. What became of the fleece afterwards we do not know, but perhaps it was found after all, like many other golden prizes, not worth the trouble it had cost to procure it.", "그 양털이 그 후 어찌 되었는지는 우리도 알지 못하나, 어쩌면 그것은 다른 많은 황금 전리품과 마찬가지로, 결국 그것을 손에 넣느라 치른 수고에 값하지 못하는 것이었음이 드러났을지도 모른다."]], [["This is one of those mythological tales, says a late writer, in which there is reason to believe that a substratum of truth exists, though overlaid by a mass of fiction.", "후대의 한 저술가가 말하기를, 이것은 그러한 신화적 이야기 가운데 하나로서, 비록 한 무더기의 허구에 뒤덮여 있기는 하나 그 밑바탕에 진실이 깔려 있다고 믿을 만한 근거가 있는 부류에 속한다고 한다."], ["It probably was the first important maritime expedition, and like the first attempts of the kind of all nations, as we know from history, was probably of a half-piratical character.", "그것은 아마도 최초의 중요한 해상 원정이었을 터인데, 역사를 통해 우리가 알고 있듯이 모든 민족의 그러한 첫 시도가 그러하였듯이, 아마도 반쯤 해적질에 가까운 성격을 띠었을 것이다."], ["If rich spoils were the result it was enough to give rise to the idea of the golden fleece.", "만일 풍부한 약탈물이 그 결과였다면, 그것만으로도 황금 양털이라는 발상을 불러일으키기에 족했을 것이다."]], [["Another suggestion of a learned mythologist, Bryant, is that it is a corrupt tradition of the story of Noah and the ark.", "박학한 신화학자 브라이언트가 내놓은 또 다른 견해는, 이것이 노아와 방주 이야기가 변질되어 전해진 전승이라는 것이다."], ["The name “Argo” seems to countenance this, and the incident of the dove is another confirmation.", "〈아르고〉라는 이름이 이를 뒷받침하는 듯하며, 비둘기 일화 역시 또 하나의 방증이 된다."]], [["Pope, in his “Ode on St. Cecilia’s Day,” thus celebrates the launching of the ship “Argo,” and the power of the music of Orpheus, whom he calls the Thracian:", "포프는 그의 「성 케킬리아 축일에 부치는 송가」에서 〈아르고〉호의 진수와, 그가 트라키아 사람이라 부르는 오르페우스의 음악이 지닌 힘을 이렇게 기린다."]], [["“So when the first bold vessel dared the seas, High on the stern the Thracian raised his strain, While Argo saw her kindred trees Descend from Pelion to the main.", "\"그리하여 최초의 대담한 배가 바다에 맞섰을 때, 트라키아 사람은 고물 높이 서서 가락을 돋우었고, 아르고호는 제 일가붙이 나무들이 펠리온 산에서 바다로 내려오는 것을 보았네."], ["Transported demigods stood round, And men grew heroes at the sound.”", "황홀에 빠진 반신반인들이 둘러섰고, 그 소리에 사람들은 영웅이 되었네.\""]], [["In Dyer’s poem of “The Fleece” there is an account of the ship “Argo” and her crew, which gives a good picture of this primitive maritime adventure:", "다이어의 시 「양털」에는 〈아르고〉호와 그 선원들에 관한 기록이 있는데, 이 원초적 해상 모험의 모습을 잘 그려 낸다."]], [["“From every region of Ægea’s shore The brave assembled; those illustrious twins Castor and Pollux; Orpheus, tuneful bard; Zetes and Calais, as the wind in speed; Strong Hercules and many a chief renowned.", "\"아이게해의 기슭 곳곳에서 용맹한 이들이 모여들었으니, 저 빛나는 쌍둥이 카스토르와 폴리데우케스, 가락 고운 음유시인 오르페우스, 바람처럼 빠른 제테스와 칼라이스, 힘센 헤라클레스와 이름난 숱한 우두머리들. 깊은 이올코스의 모래 기슭에 그들이 모여들어, 갑옷을 번쩍이며 공훈을 열망하였네."], ["On deep Iolcos’ sandy shore they thronged, Gleaming in armor, ardent of exploits; And soon, the laurel cord and the huge stone Uplifting to the deck, unmoored the bark; Whose keel of wondrous length the skilful hand Of Argus fashioned for the proud attempt; And in the extended keel a lofty mast Upraised, and sails full swelling; to the chiefs Unwonted objects.", "이윽고 월계수 밧줄과 거대한 돌을 갑판 위로 들어 올려 배의 닻줄을 풀었으니, 그 배의 놀랍도록 긴 용골은 솜씨 좋은 아르고스의 손이 저 자랑스러운 시도를 위해 빚어낸 것이었네. 그리고 길게 뻗은 용골에는 높다란 돛대가 세워지고 돛은 가득 부풀었으니, 우두머리들에게는 낯선 물건이었네."], ["Now first, now they learned Their bold steerage over ocean wave, Led by the golden stars, as Chiron’s art Had marked the sphere celestial,” etc.", "이제 처음으로, 처음으로 그들은 바다 물결 위를 헤쳐 나가는 대담한 키잡이 노릇을 배웠으니, 케이론의 솜씨가 천구를 표시해 둔 그대로 황금빛 별들의 인도를 받으며,\"등등."]], [["Hercules left the expedition at Mysia, for Hylas, a youth beloved by him, having gone for water, was laid hold of and kept by the nymphs of the spring, who were fascinated by his beauty.", "헤라클레스는 미시아에서 원정대를 떠났으니, 그가 사랑하던 소년 힐라스가 물을 길러 갔다가 그 미모에 매혹된 샘의 님프들에게 붙들려 그곳에 머물게 되었기 때문이다."], ["Hercules went in quest of the lad, and while he was absent the “Argo” put to sea and left him.", "헤라클레스는 그 소년을 찾으러 갔는데, 그가 자리를 비운 사이 〈아르고〉호는 바다로 나아가 그를 두고 떠났다."], ["Moore, in one of his songs, makes a beautiful allusion to this incident:", "무어는 자기 노래 가운데 하나에서 이 일화를 아름답게 암시한다."]], [["“When Hylas was sent with his urn to the fount, Through fields full of light and heart full of play, Light rambled the boy over meadow and mount, And neglected his task for the flowers in the way.", "\"힐라스가 물동이를 들고 샘으로 보내어졌을 때, 빛으로 가득한 들판을 지나 놀이로 가득한 마음으로, 소년은 풀밭과 언덕을 가벼이 거닐며, 길가의 꽃들에 빠져 제 일을 잊고 말았네."], ["“Thus many like me, who in youth should have tasted The fountain that runs by Philosophy’s shrine, Their time with the flowers on the margin have wasted, And left their light urns all as empty as mine.”", "그리하여 나와 같은 많은 이가, 젊은 날에 철학의 성소 곁을 흐르는 샘물을 맛보았어야 했건만, 가장자리의 꽃들에 시간을 헛되이 흘려보내고, 제 가벼운 물동이를 내 것처럼 텅 빈 채로 남겨 두었네.\""]], [["Amid the rejoicings for the recovery of the Golden Fleece, Jason felt that one thing was wanting, the presence of Æson, his father, who was prevented by his age and infirmities from taking part in them.", "황금 양털을 되찾은 데 대한 기쁨이 한창인 가운데, 이아손은 한 가지가 빠져 있음을 느꼈으니, 바로 그의 아버지 아이손이 그 자리에 없다는 것이었다. 아이손은 늙고 쇠약하여 그 기쁨에 함께하지 못했다."], ["Jason said to Medea, “My spouse, would that your arts, whose power I have seen so mighty for my aid, could do me one further service, take some years from my life and add them to my father’s.”", "이아손이 메데이아에게 말했다. \"내 아내여, 그 위력이 나를 돕는 데 그토록 강력함을 내가 보아 온 그대의 마법으로, 내게 한 가지 봉사를 더 베풀어 주었으면 하오."], ["Medea replied, “Not at such a cost shall it be done, but if my art avails me, his life shall be lengthened without abridging yours.”", "내 목숨에서 몇 해를 떼어 내 아버지의 목숨에 보태 주오.\" 메데이아가 대답했다."], ["The next full moon she issued forth alone, while all creatures slept; not a breath stirred the foliage, and all was still.", "\"그런 대가를 치르고 그리할 수는 없습니다. 그러나 제 마법이 효험을 발한다면, 당신의 목숨을 줄이지 않고도 그분의 목숨을 늘려 드리겠습니다.\""], ["To the stars she addressed her incantations, and to the moon; to Hecate,[17] the goddess of the underworld, and to Tellus the goddess of the earth, by whose power plants potent for enchantment are produced.", "다음 보름달이 뜨자 그녀는 만물이 잠든 사이에 홀로 밖으로 나섰다. 한 점 바람도 나뭇잎을 흔들지 않았고, 모든 것이 고요했다."], ["She invoked the gods of the woods and caverns, of mountains and valleys, of lakes and rivers, of winds and vapors.", "그녀는 별들에게, 그리고 달에게 주문을 외었고, 저승의 여신 헤카테에게, 또 마법에 강력한 효력을 지닌 식물들을 그 권능으로 자라나게 하는 대지의 여신 텔루스에게 주문을 외었다."], ["While she spoke the stars shone brighter, and presently a chariot descended through the air, drawn by flying serpents.", "그녀는 숲과 동굴의 신들, 산과 골짜기의 신들, 호수와 강의 신들, 바람과 안개의 신들을 불러내었다. 그녀가 말하는 동안 별들은 더욱 밝게 빛났고, 이윽고 나는 뱀들이 끄는 수레 한 대가 공중에서 내려왔다."], ["She ascended it, and borne aloft made her way to distant regions, where potent plants grew which she knew how to select for her purpose.", "그녀는 거기에 올라타 높이 실려 먼 지방으로 길을 잡았으니, 그곳에는 그녀가 자기 목적에 맞게 고를 줄 아는 강력한 식물들이 자라고 있었다."], ["Nine nights she employed in her search, and during that time came not within the doors of her palace nor under any roof, and shunned all intercourse with mortals.", "그녀는 아흐레 밤을 그 탐색에 들였고, 그동안 자기 궁전의 문 안에도, 어떤 지붕 아래에도 들지 않았으며, 모든 인간과의 교류를 피했다. * 헤카테: 저승의 여신."]], [["She next erected two altars, the one to Hecate, the other to Hebe, the goddess of youth, and sacrificed a black sheep, pouring libations of milk and wine.", "다음으로 그녀는 제단 둘을 쌓았는데, 하나는 헤카테에게, 다른 하나는 젊음의 여신 헤베에게 바치는 것이었으며, 검은 양 한 마리를 제물로 바치고 우유와 포도주를 헌주로 부었다."], ["She implored Pluto and his stolen bride that they would not hasten to take the old man’s life.", "그녀는 하데스[로마의 〈플루토〉]와 그가 납치해 온 신부에게 노인의 목숨을 서둘러 거두어 가지 말아 달라고 간청했다."], ["Then she directed that Æson should be led forth, and having thrown him into a deep sleep by a charm, had him laid on a bed of herbs, like one dead.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 아이손을 불러오게 하여, 주문으로 그를 깊은 잠에 빠뜨린 뒤 죽은 사람처럼 약초 침상 위에 눕혔다."], ["Jason and all others were kept away from the place, that no profane eyes might look upon her mysteries.", "이아손과 그 밖의 모든 이는 그곳에서 멀리 떨어져 있게 했으니, 신성치 못한 눈이 그녀의 신비를 들여다보지 못하게 하기 위함이었다."], ["Then, with streaming hair, she thrice moved round the altars, dipped flaming twigs in the blood, and laid them thereon to burn.", "그런 다음 그녀는 머리카락을 풀어 헤친 채 제단 둘레를 세 번 돌고, 불타는 잔가지들을 피에 적셔 그 위에 올려놓아 타게 했다."], ["Meanwhile the caldron with its contents was got ready.", "그러는 사이 안에 든 것과 함께 가마솥이 준비되었다."], ["In it she put magic herbs, with seeds and flowers of acrid juice, stones from the distant east, and sand from the shore of all-surrounding ocean; hoar frost, gathered by moonlight, a screech owl’s head and wings, and the entrails of a wolf.", "그녀는 그 안에 마법의 약초들을 넣었는데, 쓰디쓴 즙을 지닌 씨앗과 꽃들, 먼 동방에서 가져온 돌들, 사방을 에워싼 대양의 기슭에서 가져온 모래, 달빛 아래 모은 흰 서리, 부엉이의 머리와 날개, 그리고 늑대의 내장도 함께 넣었다."], ["She added fragments of the shells or tortoises, and the liver of stags,—animals tenacious of life,—and the head and beak of a crow, that outlives nine generations of men.", "거기에 거북의 등딱지 조각들과, 끈질긴 생명력을 지닌 짐승인 수사슴의 간을 더했고, 사람의 아홉 세대를 살아 넘긴다는 까마귀의 머리와 부리도 더했다."], ["These with many other things “without a name”", "이것들에 더해 \"이름조차 없는\""], ["she boiled together for her purposed work, stirring them up with a dry olive branch; and behold!", "다른 많은 것을 함께 넣어 그녀는 자기가 의도한 일을 위해 끓였으며, 마른 올리브 가지로 그것들을 휘저었다. 그런데 보라!"], ["the branch when taken out instantly became green, and before long was covered with leaves and a plentiful growth of young olives; and as the liquor boiled and bubbled, and sometimes ran over, the grass wherever the sprinklings fell shot forth with a verdure like that of spring.", "그 가지를 꺼내자 곧바로 푸르러졌고, 머지않아 잎과 어린 올리브가 무성하게 뒤덮였다. 그리고 그 액체가 끓어 거품을 일으키며 이따금 넘쳐흘렀는데, 그 방울이 떨어진 곳마다 풀이 봄철과 같은 푸름으로 돋아났다."]], [["Seeing that all was ready, Medea cut the throat of the old man and let out all his blood, and poured into his mouth and into his wound the juices of her caldron.", "모든 준비가 갖추어진 것을 보고, 메데이아는 노인의 목을 베어 그 피를 모조리 흘려보낸 뒤, 그의 입과 상처에 가마솥의 즙을 부어 넣었다."], ["As soon as he had completely imbibed them, his hair and beard laid by their whiteness and assumed the blackness of youth; his paleness and emaciation were gone; his veins were full of blood, his limbs of vigor and robustness.", "그가 그것을 완전히 빨아들이자마자, 그의 머리카락과 수염은 흰빛을 벗고 젊음의 검은빛을 띠었으며, 창백함과 야윔은 사라졌고, 혈관은 피로 가득 찼으며, 사지에는 활력과 건장함이 돌았다."], ["Æson is amazed at himself, and remembers that such as he now is, he was in his youthful days, forty years before.", "아이손은 스스로에게 놀라며, 사십 년 전 자기 젊은 날에 지금의 자기와 같은 모습이었음을 떠올렸다."]], [["Medea used her arts here for a good purpose, but not so in another instance, where she made them the instruments of revenge.", "메데이아는 이곳에서는 자기 마법을 선한 목적에 썼으나, 다른 경우에는 그렇지 않았으니, 그때는 그것을 복수의 도구로 삼았다."], ["Pelias, our readers will recollect, was the usurping uncle of Jason, and had kept him out of his kingdom.", "독자들은 펠리아스가 이아손의 왕위를 찬탈한 숙부로서, 그를 자기 왕국에서 내쫓아 둔 자임을 기억할 것이다."], ["Yet he must have had some good qualities, for his daughters loved him, and when they saw what Medea had done for Æson, they wished her to do the same for their father.", "그러나 그에게도 얼마간 좋은 자질이 있었음이 분명하니, 그의 딸들이 그를 사랑했고, 메데이아가 아이손에게 베푼 일을 보고는 그녀가 자기 아버지에게도 같은 일을 해 주기를 바랐기 때문이다."], ["Medea pretended to consent, and prepared her caldron as before.", "메데이아는 응하는 척하며 전과 같이 자기 가마솥을 마련했다."], ["At her request an old sheep was brought and plunged into the caldron.", "그녀의 요청에 따라 늙은 양 한 마리가 끌려와 가마솥에 던져졌다."], ["Very soon a bleating was heard in the kettle, and when the cover was removed, a lamb jumped forth and ran frisking away into the meadow.", "곧 솥 안에서 양 우는 소리가 들렸고, 뚜껑을 열자 새끼 양 한 마리가 뛰쳐나와 깡충거리며 풀밭으로 달아났다."], ["The daughters of Pelias saw the experiment with delight, and appointed a time for their father to undergo the same operation.", "펠리아스의 딸들은 그 시연을 기뻐하며 보고, 자기 아버지가 같은 시술을 받을 날을 정했다."], ["But Medea prepared her caldron for him in a very different way.", "그러나 메데이아는 그를 위해서는 자기 가마솥을 아주 다르게 마련했다."], ["She put in only water and a few simple herbs.", "그녀는 그 안에 물과 몇 가지 평범한 약초만을 넣었다."], ["In the night she with the sisters entered the bed chamber of the old king, while he and his guards slept soundly under the influence of a spell cast upon them by Medea.", "밤이 되자 그녀는 자매들과 함께 늙은 왕의 침실로 들어갔는데, 왕과 그의 호위병들은 메데이아가 그들에게 건 주문의 영향으로 깊이 잠들어 있었다."], ["The daughters stood by the bedside with their weapons drawn, but hesitated to strike, till Medea chid their irresolution.", "딸들은 무기를 빼어 든 채 침상 곁에 섰으나, 차마 내려치지 못하고 망설였고, 마침내 메데이아가 그들의 우유부단함을 꾸짖었다."], ["Then turning away their faces, and giving random blows, they smote him with their weapons.", "그러자 그들은 얼굴을 돌린 채 마구잡이로 내려쳐 자기네 무기로 그를 쳤다."], ["He, starting from his sleep, cried out, “My daughters, what are you doing?", "그는 잠에서 깨어나 소리쳤다. \"내 딸들아, 너희가 무엇을 하는 것이냐?"], ["Will you kill your father?”", "너희 아비를 죽이려느냐?\""], ["Their hearts failed them and their weapons fell from their hands, but Medea struck him a fatal blow, and prevented his saying more.", "그들의 가슴은 무너져 내렸고 무기는 손에서 떨어졌으나, 메데이아가 그에게 치명상을 입혀 그가 더 말하지 못하게 했다."]], [["Then they placed him in the caldron, and Medea hastened to depart in her serpent-drawn chariot before they discovered her treachery, or their vengeance would have been terrible.", "그러고 나서 그들은 그를 가마솥에 넣었고, 메데이아는 그들이 자기의 배신을 알아채기 전에 뱀이 끄는 수레를 타고 서둘러 떠났으니, 그러지 않았다면 그들의 복수가 끔찍했을 것이다."], ["She escaped, however, but had little enjoyment of the fruits of her crime.", "그러나 그녀는 무사히 달아났으되, 자기 죄의 열매를 거의 누리지 못했다."], ["Jason, for whom she had done so much, wishing to marry Creusa, princess of Corinth, put away Medea.", "그녀가 그토록 많은 일을 해 준 이아손이 코린토스의 공주 크레우사와 혼인하고자 메데이아를 내쫓았기 때문이다."], ["She, enraged at his ingratitude, called on the gods for vengeance, sent a poisoned robe as a gift to the bride, and then killing her own children, and setting fire to the palace, mounted her serpent-drawn chariot and fled to Athens, where she married King Ægeus, the father of Theseus, and we shall meet her again when we come to the adventures of that hero.", "그녀는 그의 배은망덕에 격분하여 신들에게 복수를 빌었고, 독을 묻힌 예복을 신부에게 선물로 보냈으며, 그런 다음 자기 자식들을 죽이고 궁전에 불을 지른 뒤, 뱀이 끄는 수레에 올라 아테네로 달아났다. 그곳에서 그녀는 테세우스의 아버지인 아이게우스 왕과 혼인했으니, 우리는 그 영웅의 모험에 이르렀을 때 그녀를 다시 만나게 될 것이다."]], [["The incantations of Medea will remind the reader of those of the witches in “Macbeth.”", "메데이아의 주문은 독자에게 「맥베스」에 나오는 마녀들의 주문을 떠오르게 할 것이다."], ["The following lines are those which seem most strikingly to recall the ancient model:", "다음 시구들은 그 고대의 본보기를 가장 인상 깊게 되살리는 듯하다."]], [["“Round about the caldron go; In the poisoned entrails throw.", "\"가마솥 둘레를 돌아라. 독 묻은 내장을 던져 넣어라."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], ["Fillet of a fenny snake In the caldron boil and bake; Eye of newt and toe of frog, Wool of bat and tongue of dog, Adder’s fork and blind-worm’s sting, Lizard’s leg and howlet’s wing: .", "늪에 사는 뱀의 토막을 가마솥에 끓여 익혀라. 영원의 눈알에 개구리의 발가락, 박쥐의 털에 개의 혀, 살무사의 갈래 혀에 발 없는 도마뱀의 침, 도마뱀의 다리에 올빼미의 날개, ."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], ["Maw of ravening salt-sea shark, Root of hemlock digged in the dark,”", "게걸스러운 짠 바다 상어의 위, 어둠 속에서 캐낸 독미나리 뿌리,\"등등. —「맥베스」"], ["etc. —Macbeth, Act IV., Scene 1", "제4막 제1장\n그리고 또한 이러하다."]], [["And again:\nMacbeth.—What is’t you do?", "맥베스. — 너희가 하는 것이 무엇이냐?"], ["Witches.—A deed without a name.", "마녀들. — 이름조차 없는 짓이옵니다."]], [["There is another story of Medea almost too revolting for record even of a sorceress, a class of persons to whom both ancient and modern poets have been accustomed to attribute every degree of atrocity.", "메데이아에 관해서는 마법사를 두고 기록하기에도 너무나 끔찍한 또 다른 이야기가 있는데, 마법사란 고금의 시인들이 온갖 정도의 잔혹함을 으레 덧씌워 온 부류의 인물들이다."], ["In her flight from Colchis she had taken her young brother Absyrtus with her.", "콜키스에서 달아날 때 그녀는 어린 동생 압시르토스를 데리고 갔다."], ["Finding the pursuing vessels of Æetes gaining upon the Argonauts, she caused the lad to be killed and his limbs to be strewn over the sea.", "아이에테스의 추격선들이 아르고나우타이를 따라잡고 있음을 알아채자, 그녀는 그 소년을 죽이게 하여 그 사지를 바다 위에 흩뿌리게 했다."], ["Æetes on reaching the place found these sorrowful traces of his murdered son; but while he tarried to collect the scattered fragments and bestow upon them an honorable interment, the Argonauts escaped.", "아이에테스는 그곳에 이르러 살해된 아들의 이 비통한 흔적들을 발견했으나, 그가 흩어진 조각들을 거두어 명예로운 매장을 치러 주느라 지체하는 사이 아르고나우타이는 달아났다."]], [["In the poems of Campbell will be found a translation of one of the choruses of the tragedy of “Medea,” where the poet Euripides has taken advantage of the occasion to pay a glowing tribute to Athens, his native city.", "캠벨의 시집에는 비극 「메데이아」의 합창 가운데 하나를 옮긴 것이 실려 있는데, 거기서 시인 에우리피데스는 그 기회를 빌려 자기 고향 도시 아테네에 열렬한 찬사를 바쳤다."], ["It begins thus:", "그것은 이렇게 시작한다."]], [["“O haggard queen!", "\"오 초췌한 여왕이여!"], ["to Athens dost thou guide Thy glowing chariot, steeped in kindred gore; Or seek to hide thy damned parricide Where peace and justice dwell for evermore?”", "그대는 핏줄의 피에 흠뻑 젖은 그 이글거리는 수레를 몰아 아테네로 향하는가. 아니면 평화와 정의가 영원토록 깃든 곳에 그대의 저주받은 혈육 살해를 숨기려 하는가?\""], ["————", "————"]], [["# MELEAGER AND ATALANTA", "# MELEAGER AND ATALANTA"]], [["ONE of the heroes of the Argonautic expedition was Meleager, son of Œneus and Althea, king and queen of Calydon.", "아르고호 원정의 영웅 가운데 한 사람이 멜레아그로스였으니, 그는 칼리돈의 왕과 왕비인 오이네우스와 알타이아의 아들이었다."], ["Althea, when her son was born, beheld the three destinies, who, as they spun their fatal thread, foretold that the life of the child should last no longer than a brand then burning upon the hearth.", "알타이아는 아들을 낳았을 때 세 운명의 여신을 보았는데, 그들은 숙명의 실을 잣는 동안 이 아이의 생명이 화롯불 위에서 타고 있는 장작개비보다 더 오래가지 못할 것이라고 예언했다."], ["Althea seized and quenched the brand, and carefully preserved it for years, while Meleager grew to boyhood, youth, and manhood.", "알타이아는 그 장작개비를 붙잡아 불을 끄고는, 멜레아그로스가 소년기와 청년기를 지나 장년에 이르도록 여러 해에 걸쳐 그것을 정성껏 간직했다."], ["It chanced, then, that Œneus, as he offered sacrifices to the gods, omitted to pay due honors to Diana; and she, indignant at the neglect, sent a wild boar of enormous size to lay waste the fields of Calydon.", "그런데 마침 오이네우스가 신들에게 제물을 바칠 때 아르테미스[로마의 〈디아나〉]에게 마땅히 바쳐야 할 경의를 빠뜨리는 일이 있었고, 여신은 그 소홀함에 분노하여 어마어마하게 큰 멧돼지 한 마리를 보내 칼리돈의 들판을 짓밟게 했다."], ["Its eyes shone with blood and fire, its bristles stood like threatening spears, its tusks were like those of Indian elephants.", "그 두 눈은 피와 불처럼 빛났고, 그 뻣뻣한 털은 위협하는 창처럼 곤두섰으며, 그 엄니는 인도 코끼리의 그것과도 같았다."], ["The growing corn was trampled, the vines and olive trees laid waste, the flocks and herds were driven in wild confusion by the slaughtering foe.", "자라나던 곡식은 짓밟혔고, 포도나무와 올리브나무는 쑥대밭이 되었으며, 양 떼와 소 떼는 살육을 일삼는 적 앞에서 어지러이 흩어져 내몰렸다."], ["All common aid seemed vain; but Meleager called on the heroes of Greece to join in a bold hunt for the ravenous monster.", "흔한 도움으로는 모두 헛된 듯 보였다. 그리하여 멜레아그로스는 그리스의 영웅들을 불러 이 게걸스러운 괴물을 잡으려는 대담한 사냥에 함께하자고 청했다."], ["Theseus and his friend Pirithous, Jason, Peleus, afterwards the father of Achilles, Telamon the father of Ajax, Nestor, then a youth, but who in his age bore arms with Achilles and Ajax in the Trojan war,—these and many more joined in the enterprise.", "테세우스와 그의 벗 페이리토오스, 이아손, 훗날 아킬레우스의 아버지가 된 펠레우스, 아이아스의 아버지 텔라몬, 그리고 당시에는 젊은이였으나 노년에 이르러 트로이아 전쟁에서 아킬레우스 및 아이아스와 함께 무기를 든 네스토르, 이들을 비롯한 수많은 이가 그 일에 합세했다."], ["With them came Atalanta, the daughter of Iasius, king of Arcadia.", "그들과 더불어 아르카디아의 왕 이아시오스의 딸 아탈란테가 왔다."], ["A buckle of polished gold confined her vest, an ivory quiver hung on her left shoulder, and her left hand bore the bow.", "윤이 나는 황금 죔쇠가 그녀의 옷을 여미었고, 상아 화살통이 그녀의 왼쪽 어깨에 걸려 있었으며, 왼손에는 활을 들고 있었다."], ["Her face blent feminine beauty with the best graces of martial youth.", "그녀의 얼굴은 여인의 아름다움과 무인다운 젊은이의 더없는 기품을 한데 아울렀다."], ["Meleager saw and loved.", "멜레아그로스는 그녀를 보고 사랑에 빠졌다."]], [["But now already they were near the monster’s lair.", "그런데 그들은 어느새 괴물의 굴 가까이에 이르렀다."], ["They stretched strong nets from tree to tree; they uncoupled their dogs, they tried to find the footprints of their quarry in the grass.", "그들은 나무에서 나무로 튼튼한 그물을 펼쳤고, 개들을 풀어놓았으며, 풀숲에서 사냥감의 발자국을 찾으려 했다."], ["From the wood was a descent to marshy ground.", "숲에서 늪지로 내려가는 비탈이 있었다."], ["Here the boar, as he lay among the reeds, heard the shouts of his pursuers, and rushed forth against them.", "여기서 갈대 사이에 누워 있던 멧돼지가 추격자들의 함성을 듣고는 그들을 향해 뛰쳐나왔다."], ["One and another is thrown down and slain.", "하나둘씩 쓰러지고 목숨을 잃었다."], ["Jason throws his spear, with a prayer to Diana for success; and the favoring goddess allows the weapon to touch, but not to wound, removing the steel point of the spear in its flight.", "이아손이 성공을 비는 기도를 아르테미스에게 올리며 창을 던졌으나, 호의를 베푼 여신은 그 무기가 닿기는 하되 상처를 입히지는 못하게 하여, 날아가는 도중에 창끝의 쇠를 거두어 버렸다."], ["Nestor, assailed, seeks and finds safety in the branches of a tree.", "네스토르는 공격을 받자 나무의 가지들 속에서 안전을 찾아 그리로 피했다."], ["Telamon rushes on, but stumbling at a projecting root, falls prone.", "텔라몬은 돌진했으나 튀어나온 나무뿌리에 발이 걸려 앞으로 고꾸라졌다."], ["But an arrow from Atalanta at length for the first time tastes the monster’s blood.", "그러나 마침내 아탈란테의 화살 한 대가 처음으로 괴물의 피를 맛보았다."], ["It is a slight wound, but Meleager sees and joyfully proclaims it.", "가벼운 상처였으나 멜레아그로스는 그것을 보고 기뻐하며 외쳐 알렸다."], ["Anceus, excited to envy by the praise given to a female, loudly proclaims his own valor, and defies alike the boar and the goddess who had sent it; but as he rushes on, the infuriated beast lays him low with a mortal wound.", "여인에게 주어진 칭찬에 시기심이 인 안카이오스는 자기 자신의 용맹을 큰 소리로 떠벌리며, 멧돼지와 그것을 보낸 여신을 한꺼번에 비웃었다. 그러나 그가 돌진하자 격노한 짐승은 그에게 치명상을 입혀 그를 쓰러뜨렸다."], ["Theseus throws his lance, but it is turned aside by a projecting bough.", "테세우스가 창을 던졌으나 튀어나온 나뭇가지에 빗나가고 말았다."], ["The dart of Jason misses its object, and kills instead one of their own dogs.", "이아손의 투창은 목표를 빗맞히고 도리어 자기네 개 한 마리를 죽였다."], ["But Meleager, after one unsuccessful stroke, drives his spear into the monster’s side, then rushes on and despatches him with repeated blows.", "그러나 멜레아그로스는 한 차례 헛손질을 한 뒤, 자기 창을 괴물의 옆구리에 박아 넣고는 돌진하여 거듭 내리쳐 그것을 끝장냈다."]], [["Then rose a shout from those around; they congratulated the conqueror, crowding to touch his hand.", "그러자 둘러선 이들에게서 함성이 일었다. 그들은 승리자에게 축하의 말을 건네며 그의 손을 만지려고 몰려들었다."], ["He, placing his foot upon the head of the slain boar, turned to Atalanta and bestowed on her the head and the rough hide which were the trophies of his success.", "멜레아그로스는 쓰러진 멧돼지의 머리에 발을 올리고는 아탈란테에게로 돌아서서, 자기 성공의 전리품인 그 머리와 거친 가죽을 그녀에게 내려주었다."], ["But at this, envy excited the rest to strife.", "그러나 이를 두고 시기심이 나머지 사람들을 다툼으로 몰아갔다."], ["Plexippus and Toxeus, the brothers of Meleager’s mother, beyond the rest opposed the gift, and snatched from the maiden the trophy she had received.", "멜레아그로스의 어머니 쪽 형제인 플렉시포스와 톡세우스가 누구보다도 앞장서 그 선물에 반대하며, 처녀가 받은 전리품을 빼앗았다."], ["Meleager, kindling with rage at the wrong done to himself, and still more at the insult offered to her whom he loved, forgot the claims of kindred, and plunged his sword into the offenders’ hearts.", "멜레아그로스는 자기에게 가해진 부당함에 분노가 타올랐고, 자기가 사랑하는 여인에게 가해진 모욕에는 더욱 분노하여, 혈육의 정을 잊고 검을 휘둘러 그 무뢰한들의 심장에 박아 넣었다."]], [["As Althea bore gifts of thankfulness to the temples for the victory of her son, the bodies of her murdered brothers met her sight.", "알타이아가 아들의 승리를 기리는 감사의 예물을 신전으로 가져갈 때, 살해당한 형제들의 시신이 그녀의 눈에 들어왔다."], ["She shrieks, and beats her breast, and hastens to change the garments of rejoicing for those of mourning.", "그녀는 비명을 지르고 가슴을 쳤으며, 기쁨의 옷을 슬픔의 옷으로 서둘러 갈아입었다."], ["But when the author of the deed is known, grief gives way to the stern desire of vengeance on her son.", "그러나 그 일을 저지른 자가 누구인지 알려지자, 슬픔은 아들에게 복수하려는 모진 욕망에 자리를 내주었다."], ["The fatal brand, which once she rescued from the flames, the brand which the destinies had linked with Meleager’s life, she brings forth, and commands a fire to be prepared.", "한때 불길에서 건져 낸 그 숙명의 장작개비, 운명의 여신들이 멜레아그로스의 생명과 엮어 놓은 그 장작개비를 그녀는 꺼내 들고 불을 피우라고 명했다."], ["Then four times she essays to place the brand upon the pile; four times draws back, shuddering at the thought of bringing destruction on her son.", "그러고는 네 번이나 그 장작개비를 장작더미 위에 올리려 했으나, 네 번이나 아들에게 파멸을 가져온다는 생각에 몸서리치며 물러섰다."], ["The feelings of the mother and the sister contend within her.", "어머니의 마음과 누이의 마음이 그녀 안에서 다투었다."], ["Now she is pale at the thought of the proposed deed, now flushed again with anger at the act of her son.", "한순간 그녀는 자기가 작정한 행위를 떠올리며 핏기가 가셨다가, 다시 한순간 아들의 행위에 분노가 치밀어 얼굴이 붉어졌다."], ["As a vessel, driven in one direction by the wind, and in the opposite by the tide, the mind of Althea hangs suspended in uncertainty.", "바람에 한쪽으로 떠밀리고 조류에 그 반대쪽으로 떠밀리는 배처럼, 알타이아의 마음은 갈피를 잡지 못한 채 매달려 있었다."], ["But now the sister prevails above the mother, and she begins as she holds the fatal wood: “Turn, ye Furies, goddesses of punishment!", "그러나 이제 누이가 어머니를 이기게 되니, 그녀는 그 숙명의 나무를 쥐고서 이렇게 말하기 시작했다. \"돌아보소서, 복수의 여신들이여, 징벌의 여신들이여!"], ["turn to behold the sacrifice I bring!", "돌아보시어 내가 바치는 제물을 보소서!"], ["Crime must atone for crime.", "죄는 죄로 갚아야 합니다."], ["Shall Œneus rejoice in his victor son, while the house of Thestius is desolate?", "오이네우스는 승리한 아들을 두고 기뻐하는데, 테스티오스의 집안은 황폐해져야 한단 말입니까?"], ["But, alas!", "그러나, 아아!"], ["to what deed am I borne along?", "나는 무슨 짓을 향해 떠밀려 가고 있는가?"], ["Brothers forgive a mother’s weakness!", "형제들이여, 어미의 나약함을 용서하시오!"], ["my hand fails me.", "내 손이 말을 듣지 않는구나."], ["He deserves death, but not that I should destroy him.", "그 아이는 죽어 마땅하나, 내가 그 아이를 죽이는 것은 마땅치 않다."], ["But shall he then live, and triumph, and reign over Calydon, while you, my brothers, wander unavenged among the shades?", "그러나 그러면 그 아이는 살아남아 의기양양하게 칼리돈을 다스리고, 너희, 내 형제들은 원수도 갚지 못한 채 망령들 사이를 떠돌란 말이냐? 안 된다!"], ["No! thou hast lived by my gift; die, now, for thine own crime.", "너는 내가 준 선물로 살아왔으니, 이제 네 죄로 인하여 죽어라."], ["Return the life which twice I gave thee, first at thy birth, again when I snatched this brand from the flames.", "두 번 너에게 준 생명을 돌려다오. 처음은 네가 태어났을 때요, 다음은 내가 이 장작개비를 불길에서 낚아챘을 때다."], ["O that thou hadst then died!", "아, 그때 네가 죽었더라면!"], ["Alas!", "아아!"], ["evil is the conquest; but, brothers, ye have conquered.”", "이 승리는 사악하구나. 그러나, 형제들이여, 너희가 이겼다.\""], ["And, turning away her face, she threw the fatal wood upon the burning pile.", "그러고는 얼굴을 돌리며, 그녀는 그 숙명의 나무를 타오르는 장작더미 위에 던졌다."]], [["It gave, or seemed to give, a deadly groan.", "그것은 죽음의 신음을 토했다, 혹은 토하는 듯했다."], ["Meleager, absent and unknowing of the cause, felt a sudden pang.", "멀리 떨어져 있어 그 까닭을 알지 못하던 멜레아그로스는 문득 격통을 느꼈다."], ["He burns, and only by courageous pride conquers the pain which destroys him.", "그는 불타올랐고, 오직 용감한 긍지로써 자신을 무너뜨리는 그 고통을 이겨 냈다."], ["He mourns only that he perishes by a bloodless and unhonored death.", "그는 다만 피도 흘리지 않고 영예도 없는 죽음으로 스러진다는 것을 슬퍼했다."], ["With his last breath he calls upon his aged father, his brother, and his fond sisters, upon his beloved Atalanta, and upon his mother, the unknown cause of his fate.", "마지막 숨을 거두며 그는 늙은 아버지와 형제와 다정한 누이들을, 사랑하는 아탈란테를, 그리고 알지 못한 채 자기 운명의 원인이 된 어머니를 불렀다."], ["The flames increase, and with them the pain of the hero.", "불길이 거세지자 그와 더불어 영웅의 고통도 거세졌다."], ["Now both subside; now both are quenched.", "이제 둘 다 잦아들었다. 이제 둘 다 꺼졌다."], ["The brand is ashes, and the life of Meleager is breathed forth to the wandering winds.", "장작개비는 재가 되었고, 멜레아그로스의 생명은 떠도는 바람 속으로 흩어져 사라졌다."]], [["Althea, when the deed was done, laid violent hands upon herself.", "알타이아는 그 일을 마치고 나서 제 손으로 제 목숨을 끊었다."], ["The sisters of Meleager mourned their brother with uncontrollable grief; till Diana, pitying the sorrows of the house that once had aroused her anger, turned them into birds.", "멜레아그로스의 누이들은 걷잡을 수 없는 슬픔으로 오라비를 애도했고, 마침내 아르테미스는 한때 자기 분노를 불러일으켰던 그 집안의 비탄을 가엾이 여겨 그들을 새로 바꾸어 놓았다."]], [["The innocent cause of so much sorrow was a maiden whose face you might truly say was boyish for a girl, yet too girlish for a boy.", "이토록 많은 슬픔의 죄 없는 원인이 된 이는 한 처녀였으니, 그 얼굴은 소녀치고는 사내아이 같다 할 만하면서도 사내아이치고는 너무도 소녀다웠다고 할 수 있을 것이다."], ["Her fortune had been told, and it was to this effect: “Atalanta, do not marry; marriage will be your ruin.”", "그녀의 운명은 이미 점쳐져 있었는데, 그 내용은 이러했다. \"아탈란테여, 결혼하지 마라."], ["Terrified by this oracle, she fled the society of men, and devoted herself to the sports of the chase.", "결혼은 너의 파멸이 되리라.\" 이 신탁에 겁을 먹은 그녀는 남자들과 어울리기를 멀리하고 사냥의 즐거움에 자신을 바쳤다."], ["To all suitors (for she had many) she imposed a condition which was generally effectual in relieving her of their persecutions,—“I will be the prize of him who shall conquer me in the race; but death must be the penalty of all who try and fail.”", "모든 구혼자에게(그녀에게는 구혼자가 많았기 때문이다) 그녀는 대개 그들의 성가신 구애에서 벗어나는 데 효험이 있는 조건을 내걸었으니, \"나는 달리기에서 나를 이기는 자의 차지가 되리라. 그러나 시도했다가 실패하는 모든 이에게는 죽음이 그 대가가 되어야 한다\"는 것이었다."], ["In spite of this hard condition some would try.", "이토록 가혹한 조건에도 불구하고 더러는 시도하려 들었다."], ["Hippomenes was to be judge of the race.", "히포메네스가 그 달리기의 심판을 맡기로 되어 있었다."], ["“Can it be possible that any will be so rash as to risk so much for a wife?”", "\"아내 하나를 얻겠다고 그토록 큰 위험을 무릅쓸 만큼 무모한 자가 과연 있을 수 있단 말인가?\""], ["said he.", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["But when he saw her lay aside her robe for the race, he changed his mind, and said, “Pardon me, youths, I knew not the prize you were competing for.”", "그러나 그녀가 달리기를 위해 겉옷을 벗어 놓는 것을 보자 그는 생각을 바꾸고 이렇게 말했다. \"젊은이들이여, 용서하시오."], ["As he surveyed them he wished them all to be beaten, and swelled with envy of any one that seemed at all likely to win.", "나는 그대들이 다투고 있는 상이 무엇인지 몰랐소.\" 그는 그들을 둘러보며 그들 모두가 패하기를 바랐고, 조금이라도 이길 듯한 자가 있으면 시기심으로 가슴이 부풀어 올랐다."], ["While such were his thoughts, the virgin darted forward.", "그가 이런 생각에 잠겨 있는 동안 처녀가 앞으로 내달렸다."], ["As she ran she looked more beautiful than ever.", "그녀는 달리는 모습이 어느 때보다 아름다워 보였다."], ["The breezes seemed to give wings to her feet; her hair flew over her shoulders, and the gay fringe of her garment fluttered behind her.", "산들바람이 그녀의 발에 날개를 달아 주는 듯했고, 머리카락은 어깨 너머로 흩날렸으며, 옷자락의 화사한 술이 그녀의 등 뒤에서 펄럭였다."], ["A ruddy hue tinged the whiteness of her skin, such as a crimson curtain casts on a marble wall.", "진홍빛 휘장이 대리석 벽에 드리우는 듯한 발그레한 빛이 그녀의 흰 살결을 물들였다."], ["All her competitors were distanced, and were put to death without mercy.", "모든 경쟁자가 뒤처졌고, 가차 없이 죽임을 당했다."], ["Hippomenes, not daunted by this result, fixing his eyes on the virgin, said, “Why boast of beating those laggards?", "히포메네스는 이 결과에 주눅 들지 않고 처녀에게 눈길을 고정한 채 말했다. \"저 굼뜬 자들을 이긴 것을 어찌 자랑하시오?"], ["I offer myself for the contest.”", "내가 그 겨룸에 나 자신을 내놓겠소.\""], ["Atalanta looked at him with a pitying countenance, and hardly knew whether she would rather conquer him or not.", "아탈란테는 가엾어하는 낯빛으로 그를 바라보았고, 그를 이기는 편이 나을지 아닐지 좀처럼 가늠하지 못했다."], ["“What god can tempt one so young and handsome to throw himself away?", "\"어떤 신이 이토록 젊고 잘생긴 이를 부추겨 제 목숨을 내던지게 한단 말인가?"], ["I pity him, not for his beauty (yet he is beautiful), but for his youth.", "나는 그가 가엾다. 그 아름다움 때문이 아니라(하긴 그는 아름답지만), 그 젊음 때문이다."], ["I wish he would give up the race, or if he will be so mad, I hope he may outrun me.”", "그가 이 겨룸을 단념했으면 좋으련만, 만일 그토록 미친 짓을 하려 든다면, 부디 그가 나를 앞질렀으면.\""], ["While she hesitates, revolving these thoughts, the spectators grow impatient for the race, and her father prompts her to prepare.", "이런 생각을 곱씹으며 그녀가 망설이는 동안, 구경꾼들은 달리기를 기다리다 조바심을 냈고, 그녀의 아버지는 그녀에게 준비하라고 재촉했다."], ["Then Hippomenes addressed a prayer to Venus: “Help me, Venus, for you have led me on.”", "그러자 히포메네스는 아프로디테[로마의 〈베누스〉]에게 기도를 올렸다. \"나를 도우소서, 아프로디테여, 당신께서 나를 여기까지 이끄셨으니.\""], ["Venus heard and was propitious.", "아프로디테는 듣고 호의를 베풀었다."]], [["In the garden of her temple, in her own island of Cyprus, is a tree with yellow leaves and yellow branches and golden fruit.", "그녀의 섬 키프로스에 있는 그녀의 신전 정원에는 누런 잎과 누런 가지와 황금 열매가 달린 나무 한 그루가 있다."], ["Hence she gathered three golden apples, and, unseen by any one else, gave them to Hippomenes, and told him how to use them.", "거기서 그녀는 황금 사과 세 개를 따서, 아무도 보지 못하게 히포메네스에게 건네고는 그것을 어떻게 쓸지 일러 주었다."], ["The signal is given; each starts from the goal and skims over the sand.", "신호가 떨어졌다. 둘은 각기 출발선에서 내달려 모래 위를 스쳐 달렸다."], ["So light their tread, you would almost have thought they might run over the river surface or over the waving grain without sinking.", "그 발걸음이 어찌나 가벼운지, 강물 위나 물결치는 곡식 위를 가라앉지 않고 달릴 수 있으리라 생각했을 정도였다."], ["The cries of the spectators cheered Hippomenes,—“Now, now, do your best!", "구경꾼들의 외침이 히포메네스를 북돋웠다. \"지금이야, 지금, 힘껏 달려라!"], ["haste, haste!", "서둘러, 서둘러!"], ["you gain on her!", "따라잡고 있다!"], ["relax not!", "늦추지 마라!"], ["one more effort!”", "한 번만 더 힘을 내라!\""], ["It was doubtful whether the youth or the maiden heard these cries with the greater pleasure.", "이 외침을 젊은이가 더 즐거이 들었는지 처녀가 더 즐거이 들었는지는 분간하기 어려웠다."], ["But his breath began to fail him, his throat was dry, the goal yet far off.", "그러나 그의 숨이 가빠지기 시작했고, 목은 말랐으며, 결승점은 아직 멀었다."], ["At that moment he threw down one of the golden apples.", "그 순간 그는 황금 사과 하나를 던져 떨어뜨렸다."], ["The virgin was all amazement.", "처녀는 온통 놀라움에 휩싸였다."], ["She stopped to pick it up.", "그녀는 그것을 줍느라 멈춰 섰다."], ["Hippomenes shot ahead.", "히포메네스가 앞으로 쏜살같이 나아갔다."], ["Shouts burst forth from all sides.", "사방에서 함성이 터져 나왔다."], ["She redoubled her efforts, and soon overtook him.", "그녀는 갑절로 힘을 냈고, 이내 그를 따라잡았다."], ["Again he threw an apple.", "그가 다시 사과 하나를 던졌다."], ["She stopped again, but again came up with him.", "그녀는 또 멈춰 섰으나, 다시 그를 따라잡았다."], ["The goal was near; one chance only remained.", "결승점이 가까웠고, 단 한 번의 기회만이 남아 있었다. \"이제, 여신이여,\""], ["“Now, goddess,”", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “prosper your gift!”", "\"당신의 선물을 도와주소서!\""], ["and threw the last apple off at one side.", "그러고는 마지막 사과를 한쪽으로 던졌다."], ["She looked at it, and hesitated; Venus impelled her to turn aside for it.", "그녀는 그것을 바라보며 망설였다. 아프로디테가 그녀를 부추겨 그것을 향해 비켜서게 했다."], ["She did so, and was vanquished.", "그녀는 그렇게 했고, 패하고 말았다."], ["The youth carried off his prize.", "젊은이는 자기 상을 차지하여 데려갔다."]], [["But the lovers were so full of their own happiness that they forgot to pay due honor to Venus; and the goddess was provoked at their ingratitude.", "그러나 두 연인은 자신들의 행복에 흠뻑 빠진 나머지 아프로디테에게 마땅한 경의를 바치는 것을 잊고 말았다. 여신은 그들의 배은망덕에 노여워했다."], ["She caused them to give offence to Cybele.", "그녀는 그들이 키벨레의 심기를 거스르게 만들었다."], ["That powerful goddess was not to be insulted with impunity.", "그 강대한 여신은 모욕을 당하고도 그대로 넘어갈 분이 아니었다. 그녀는 그들에게서 인간의 모습을 거두어, 그들 자신의 성품을 닮은 짐승으로 바꾸어 놓았다."], ["She took from them their human form and turned them into animals of characters resembling their own: of the huntress-heroine, triumphing in the blood of her lovers, she made a lioness, and of her lord and master a lion, and yoked them to her car, where they are still to be seen in all representations, in statuary or painting, of the goddess Cybele.", "연인들의 피를 보고 의기양양하던 그 여전사 영웅을 그녀는 암사자로 만들었고, 그녀의 주인이자 남편을 사자로 만들어, 자기 수레에 멍에를 지웠다. 그리하여 여신 키벨레를 조각이나 그림으로 나타낸 모든 표현 속에서 그들은 지금도 볼 수 있다."]], [["Cybele is the Latin name of the goddess called by the Greeks Rhea and Ops.", "키벨레는 그리스인들이 레아 또는 옵스라 부르던 여신의 라틴어 이름이다."], ["She was the wife of Cronos and mother of Zeus.", "그녀는 크로노스의 아내이자 제우스의 어머니였다."], ["In works of art she exhibits the matronly air which distinguishes Juno and Ceres.", "예술 작품 속에서 그녀는 헤라[로마의 〈유노〉]와 데메테르[로마의 〈케레스〉]를 특징짓는 부인다운 기품을 드러낸다."], ["Sometimes she is veiled, and seated on a throne with lions at her side, at other times riding in a chariot drawn by lions.", "어떤 때는 너울을 쓴 채 양옆에 사자를 거느리고 옥좌에 앉아 있고, 또 어떤 때는 사자들이 끄는 수레를 타고 있다."], ["She wears a mural crown, that is, a crown whose rim is carved in the form of towers and battlements.", "그녀는 성벽관(城壁冠)을 쓰는데, 이는 그 테두리가 탑과 흉벽 모양으로 새겨진 관이다."], ["Her priests were called Corybantes.", "그녀의 사제들은 코리반테스라 불렸다."]], [["Byron, in describing the city of Venice, which is built on a low island in the Adriatic Sea, borrows an illustration from Cybele:", "바이런은 아드리아 해의 나지막한 섬 위에 세워진 도시 베네치아를 묘사하면서 키벨레에서 비유를 빌려 온다."]], [["“She looks a sea-Cybele fresh from ocean, Rising with her tiara of proud towers At airy distance, with majestic motion, A ruler of the waters and their powers.”", "\"그녀는 대양에서 갓 떠오른 바다의 키벨레처럼 보이나니, 당당한 탑들의 보관(寶冠)을 쓰고 아득한 거리에서 위엄 있는 몸짓으로 떠오르는, 물과 그 권능을 다스리는 통치자로다.\""], ["—Childe Harold, IV.", "─〈차일드 해럴드〉, 제4편."]], [["In Moore’s “Rhymes on the Road,” the poet, speaking of Alpine scenery, alludes to the story of Atalanta and Hippomenes thus:", "무어의 〈길 위의 시편〉에서 시인은 알프스의 풍경을 이야기하며 아탈란테와 히포메네스의 이야기를 이렇게 넌지시 언급한다."]], [["“Even here, in this region of wonders, I find That light-footed Fancy leaves Truth far behind, Or at least, like Hippomenes, turns her astray By the golden illusions he flings in her way.”", "\"이 경이의 고장에서조차, 나는 깨닫노라 발 가벼운 공상이 진실을 저만치 뒤에 남겨 두고 가는 것을, 아니면 적어도, 히포메네스처럼, 진실의 앞길에 던지는 황금빛 환영(幻影)으로 진실을 옆길로 빠지게 하는 것을.\""], ["————", "————"]], [["HERCULES was the son of Jupiter and Alcmena.", "헤라클레스는 제우스와 알크메네 사이에서 태어난 아들이었다."], ["As Juno was always hostile to the offspring of her husband by mortal mothers, she declared war against Hercules from his birth.", "헤라[로마의 〈유노〉]는 자기 남편이 필멸의 어머니들에게서 얻은 자식들에게 늘 적대적이었기에, 헤라클레스가 태어난 순간부터 그를 상대로 전쟁을 선포했다."], ["She sent two serpents to destroy him as he lay in his cradle, but the precocious infant strangled them with his own hands.", "그녀는 그가 요람에 누워 있을 때 두 마리의 뱀을 보내 그를 죽이려 했으나, 조숙한 이 아기는 제 손으로 그것들을 목 졸라 죽였다."], ["He was, however, by the arts of Juno rendered subject to Eurystheus and compelled to perform all his commands.", "그렇지만 그는 헤라의 술책에 의해 에우리스테우스에게 예속된 신세가 되어, 그의 모든 명령을 수행하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Eurystheus enjoined upon him a succession of desperate adventures, which are called the “Twelve Labors of Hercules.”", "에우리스테우스는 그에게 일련의 무모한 모험들을 명했는데, 이것이 이른바 \"헤라클레스의 열두 가지 과업\"이라 불리는 것이다."], ["The first was the fight with the Nemean lion.", "첫 번째는 네메아의 사자와 벌인 싸움이었다."], ["The valley of Nemea was infested by a terrible lion.", "네메아 골짜기는 무시무시한 사자 한 마리에게 시달리고 있었다."], ["Eurystheus ordered Hercules to bring him the skin of this monster.", "에우리스테우스는 헤라클레스에게 이 괴물의 가죽을 가져오라고 명했다."], ["After using in vain his club and arrows against the lion, Hercules strangled the animal with his hands.", "헤라클레스는 곤봉과 화살을 그 사자에게 써 보았으나 헛수고였고, 결국 제 손으로 그 짐승을 목 졸라 죽였다."], ["He returned carrying the dead lion on his shoulders; but Eurystheus was so frightened at the sight of it and at this proof of the prodigious strength of the hero, that he ordered him to deliver the account of his exploits in future outside the town.", "그는 죽은 사자를 어깨에 메고 돌아왔다. 그러나 에우리스테우스는 그 광경과 이 영웅의 어마어마한 힘을 입증하는 증거에 얼마나 겁을 먹었던지, 앞으로는 그의 공적에 관한 보고를 성 밖에서 전하라고 명했다."]], [["His next labor was the slaughter of the Hydra.", "그의 다음 과업은 히드라를 도살하는 것이었다."], ["This monster ravaged the country of Argos, and dwelt in a swamp near the well of Amymone.", "이 괴물은 아르고스 땅을 황폐하게 만들었고, 아미모네 샘 근처의 늪에 살고 있었다."], ["This well had been discovered by Amymone when the country was suffering from drought, and the story was that Neptune, who loved her, had permitted her to touch the rock with his trident, and a spring of three outlets burst forth.", "이 샘은 그 땅이 가뭄에 시달리고 있을 때 아미모네가 발견한 것이었는데, 전하는 이야기에 따르면 그녀를 사랑한 포세이돈[로마의 〈넵투누스〉]이 자신의 삼지창으로 바위를 건드리도록 허락하자, 출구가 세 갈래인 샘물이 솟구쳐 나왔다고 한다."], ["Here the Hydra took up his position, and Hercules was sent to destroy him.", "이곳에 히드라가 자리를 잡았고, 헤라클레스는 그것을 죽이기 위해 보내졌다."], ["The Hydra had nine heads, of which the middle one was immortal.", "히드라는 머리가 아홉 개였는데, 그 가운데 하나는 불사의 것이었다."], ["Hercules struck off its heads with his club, but in the place of the head knocked off, two new ones grew forth each time.", "헤라클레스는 곤봉으로 그 머리들을 쳐서 떨어뜨렸으나, 떨어뜨린 머리 자리에는 그때마다 새로운 머리 두 개가 자라났다."], ["At length with the assistance of his faithful servant Iolaus, he burned away the heads of the Hydra, and buried the ninth or immortal one under a huge rock.", "마침내 그는 충실한 종 이올라오스의 도움을 받아 히드라의 머리들을 불로 지져 없앴고, 아홉 번째 곧 불사의 머리는 거대한 바위 아래 묻어 버렸다."]], [["Another labor was the cleaning of the Augean stables.", "또 다른 과업은 아우게이아스의 외양간을 청소하는 것이었다."], ["Augeas, king of Elis, had a herd of three thousand oxen, whose stalls had not been cleansed for thirty years.", "엘리스의 왕 아우게이아스는 삼천 마리의 소 떼를 거느리고 있었는데, 그 우리는 삼십 년 동안 한 번도 청소된 적이 없었다."], ["Hercules brought the rivers Alpheus and Peneus through them, and cleansed them thoroughly in one day.", "헤라클레스는 알페이오스 강과 페네이오스 강을 그 사이로 끌어들여, 단 하루 만에 그곳을 말끔히 청소해 냈다."]], [["His next labor was of a more delicate kind.", "그의 다음 과업은 좀 더 미묘한 종류의 것이었다."], ["Admeta, the daughter of Eurystheus, longed to obtain the girdle of the queen of the Amazons, and Eurystheus ordered Hercules to go and get it.", "에우리스테우스의 딸 아드메테는 아마조네스 여왕의 허리띠를 손에 넣고 싶어 했고, 에우리스테우스는 헤라클레스에게 가서 그것을 가져오라고 명했다."], ["The Amazons were a nation of women.", "아마조네스는 여인들로 이루어진 민족이었다."], ["They were very warlike and held several flourishing cities.", "그들은 무척 호전적이었고 번성하는 여러 도시를 거느리고 있었다."], ["It was their custom to bring up only the female children; the boys were either sent away to the neighboring nations or put to death.", "여자아이들만 길러 내는 것이 그들의 관습이었고, 사내아이들은 이웃 민족들에게 보내 버리거나 죽여 버렸다."], ["Hercules was accompanied by a number of volunteers, and after various adventures at last reached the country of the Amazons.", "헤라클레스는 다수의 자원자들을 거느리고 떠났으며, 여러 모험을 거친 끝에 마침내 아마조네스의 나라에 다다랐다."], ["Hippolyta, the queen, received him kindly, and consented to yield him her girdle, but Juno, taking the form of an Amazon, went and persuaded the rest that the strangers were carrying off their queen.", "여왕 히폴리테는 그를 친절히 맞이하고 자기 허리띠를 그에게 내주기로 했으나, 헤라가 아마조네스 한 사람의 모습을 하고 나타나 나머지 여인들에게 그 이방인들이 자기네 여왕을 납치해 가려 한다고 부추겼다."], ["They instantly armed and came in great numbers down to the ship.", "그들은 즉시 무장하고 큰 무리를 이루어 배가 있는 곳으로 몰려 내려왔다."], ["Hercules, thinking that Hippolyta had acted treacherously, slew her, and taking her girdle made sail homewards.", "헤라클레스는 히폴리테가 음모를 꾸민 것이라 여겨 그녀를 죽이고, 그녀의 허리띠를 빼앗아 고향으로 돛을 올렸다."]], [["Another task enjoined him was to bring to Eurystheus the oxen of Geryon, a monster with three bodies, who dwelt in the island Erytheia (the red), so called because it lay at the west, under the rays of the setting sun.", "그에게 부과된 또 다른 임무는 게리온의 소 떼를 에우리스테우스에게 가져오는 것이었다. 게리온은 몸이 셋인 괴물로서, 에리테이아[붉은] 섬에 살고 있었는데, 그 섬이 서쪽에 지는 해의 빛살 아래 자리 잡고 있다 하여 그렇게 불렸다."], ["This description is thought to apply to Spain, of which Geryon was king.", "이 묘사는 에스파냐를 가리키는 것으로 여겨지는데, 게리온은 그곳의 왕이었다."], ["After traversing various countries, Hercules reached at length the frontiers of Libya and Europe, where he raised the two mountains of Calpe and Abyla, as monuments of his progress, or, according to another account, rent one mountain into two and left half on each side, forming the straits of Gibraltar, the two mountains being called the Pillars of Hercules.", "여러 나라를 가로지른 끝에 헤라클레스는 마침내 리비아와 에우로페[유럽]의 경계에 다다랐고, 그곳에 자신이 지나온 자취를 기리는 기념물로서 칼페와 아빌라라는 두 산을 세웠다. 또 다른 이야기에 따르면, 그는 산 하나를 둘로 쪼개어 절반씩 양쪽에 남겨 둠으로써 지브롤터 해협을 이루었으며, 그 두 산을 헤라클레스의 기둥이라 불렀다고 한다."], ["The oxen were guarded by the giant Eurytion and his two-headed dog, but Hercules killed the giant and his dog and brought away the oxen in safety to Eurystheus.", "그 소 떼는 거인 에우리티온과 머리가 둘 달린 그의 개가 지키고 있었으나, 헤라클레스는 그 거인과 개를 죽이고 소 떼를 무사히 에우리스테우스에게 가져왔다."]], [["The most difficult labor of all was getting the golden apples of the Hesperides, for Hercules did not know where to find them.", "모든 과업 가운데 가장 어려운 것은 헤스페리데스의 황금 사과를 손에 넣는 일이었으니, 헤라클레스는 그것을 어디서 찾아야 할지 알지 못했던 것이다."], ["These were the apples which Juno had received at her wedding from the goddess of the Earth, and which she had intrusted to the keeping of the daughters of Hesperus, assisted by a watchful dragon.", "이 사과들은 헤라가 결혼식 때 대지의 여신에게서 받은 것으로, 그녀는 이를 헤스페로스의 딸들이 지키도록 맡겨 두었고, 경계심 많은 용이 그들을 거들었다."], ["After various adventures Hercules arrived at Mount Atlas in Africa.", "여러 모험을 거친 끝에 헤라클레스는 아프리카의 아틀라스 산에 다다랐다."], ["Atlas was one of the Titans who had warred against the gods, and after they were subdued, Atlas was condemned to bear on his shoulders the weight of the heavens.", "아틀라스는 신들에 맞서 싸운 티탄들 가운데 하나였는데, 그들이 제압된 뒤 아틀라스는 어깨에 하늘의 무게를 짊어지도록 선고받았다."], ["He was the father of the Hesperides, and Hercules thought might, if any one could, find the apples and bring them to him.", "그는 헤스페리데스의 아버지였으므로, 만일 누군가가 그 사과를 찾아 가져다줄 수 있다면 바로 그일 것이라고 헤라클레스는 생각했다."], ["But how to send Atlas away from his post, or bear up the heavens while he was gone?", "그러나 어떻게 아틀라스를 그 자리에서 떠나보내며, 또 그가 떠나 있는 동안 하늘을 떠받칠 수 있단 말인가?"], ["Hercules took the burden on his own shoulders, and sent Atlas to seek the apples.", "헤라클레스는 그 짐을 제 어깨에 떠메고, 아틀라스를 보내어 사과를 찾아오게 했다."], ["He returned with them, and though somewhat reluctantly, took his burden upon his shoulders again, and let Hercules return with the apples to Eurystheus.", "아틀라스는 사과를 가지고 돌아왔으며, 다소 마지못한 것이기는 했으나 다시 자기 짐을 어깨에 떠메고, 헤라클레스가 그 사과를 에우리스테우스에게 가져가도록 놓아 주었다."]], [["Milton, in his “Comus,” makes the Hesperides the daughters of Hesperus and nieces of Atlas:", "밀턴은 자신의 작품 「코머스」에서 헤스페리데스를 헤스페로스의 딸들이자 아틀라스의 조카딸들로 그린다."]], [["“. .", "\"."], [".", "."], ["amidst the gardens fair Of Hesperus and his daughters three, That sing about the golden tree.”", ". 헤스페로스와 그의 세 딸들의 아름다운 정원 한가운데, 그들은 황금 나무 둘레에서 노래하네.\""]], [["The poets, led by the analogy of the lovely appearance of the western sky at sunset, viewed the west as a region of brightness and glory.", "서쪽 하늘이 해 질 녘에 보여 주는 사랑스러운 모습에서 유추하여, 시인들은 서쪽을 광명과 영광의 영역으로 보았다."], ["Hence they placed in it the Isles of the Blest, the ruddy Isle Erythea, on which the bright oxen of Geryon were pastured, and the Isle of the Hesperides.", "그리하여 그들은 그곳에 축복받은 자들의 섬들과, 게리온의 빛나는 소 떼가 풀을 뜯던 붉은 에리테아 섬과, 헤스페리데스의 섬을 두었다."], ["The apples are supposed by some to be the oranges of Spain, of which the Greeks had heard some obscure accounts.", "어떤 이들은 그 사과들이 에스파냐의 오렌지였으리라 추측하는데, 그리스인들은 그것에 관해 어렴풋한 이야기를 들었던 것이다."]], [["A celebrated exploit of Hercules was his victory over Antæus.", "헤라클레스의 이름난 무훈 가운데 하나는 안타이오스를 누른 승리였다."], ["Antæus, the son of Terra, the Earth, was a mighty giant and wrestler, whose strength was invincible so long as he remained in contact with his mother Earth.", "대지의 여신 테라의 아들 안타이오스는 강력한 거인이자 씨름꾼이었는데, 그의 힘은 어머니인 대지와 맞닿아 있는 한 누구도 당할 수 없었다."], ["He compelled all strangers who came to his country to wrestle with him, on condition that if conquered (as they all were) they should be put to death.", "그는 자기 나라에 오는 모든 이방인에게 자신과 씨름하도록 강요했으며, 패배할 경우(그들은 모두 패배했다) 죽임을 당해야 한다는 조건이었다. 헤라클레스는 그와 맞붙었는데, 그를 내동댕이쳐 보아야 소용이 없음을 깨달았다."], ["Hercules encountered him, and finding that it was of no avail to throw him, for he always rose with renewed strength from every fall, he lifted him up from the earth and strangled him in the air.", "그가 넘어질 때마다 늘 새로운 힘을 얻어 다시 일어났던 것이다. 그래서 헤라클레스는 그를 땅에서 들어 올려 공중에서 목 졸라 죽였다."]], [["Cacus was a huge giant, who inhabited a cave on Mount Aventine, and plundered the surrounding country.", "카쿠스는 거대한 거인으로, 아벤티누스 산의 동굴에 살면서 주변 지방을 약탈했다."], ["When Hercules was driving home the oxen of Geryon, Cacus stole part of the cattle, while the hero slept.", "헤라클레스가 게리온의 소 떼를 몰고 집으로 돌아가던 길에, 영웅이 잠든 사이 카쿠스는 그 소들의 일부를 훔쳤다."], ["That their footprints might not serve to show where they had been driven, he dragged them backward by their tails to his cave; so their tracks all seemed to show that they had gone in the opposite direction.", "그 발자국이 소들이 어디로 몰려갔는지를 드러내지 않도록, 그는 소들의 꼬리를 잡고 뒷걸음질로 끌어 자기 동굴로 데려갔다. 그리하여 그 자취는 온통 소들이 반대 방향으로 갔음을 가리키는 듯 보였다."], ["Hercules was deceived by this stratagem, and would have failed to find his oxen, if it had not happened that in driving the remainder of the herd past the cave where the stolen ones were concealed, those within began to low, and were thus discovered.", "헤라클레스는 이 술책에 속아 넘어가 자기 소들을 찾지 못할 뻔했으나, 마침 남은 소 떼를 몰고 훔친 소들이 숨겨진 동굴 곁을 지나갈 때 안에 있던 소들이 울기 시작했고, 그렇게 하여 발각되었다."], ["Cacus was slain by Hercules.", "카쿠스는 헤라클레스에게 죽임을 당했다."]], [["The last exploit we shall record was bringing Cerberus from the lower world.", "우리가 기록할 마지막 무훈은 저승에서 케르베로스를 데려온 일이다."], ["Hercules descended into Hades, accompanied by Mercury and Minerva.", "헤라클레스는 헤르메스[로마의 〈메르쿠리우스〉]와 아테나[로마의 〈미네르바〉]를 거느리고 하데스로 내려갔다."], ["He obtained permission from Pluto to carry Cerberus to the upper air, provided he could do it without the use of weapons; and in spite of the monster’s struggling, he seized him, held him fast, and carried him to Eurystheus, and afterwards brought him back again.", "그는 무기를 쓰지 않고서 해낼 수 있다는 조건 아래, 케르베로스를 지상으로 데려가도 좋다는 허락을 하데스의 신 플루톤에게서 얻었다. 그리고 그 괴물이 발버둥 쳤음에도, 그는 그것을 붙잡아 단단히 움켜쥐고 에우리스테우스에게 데려갔다가, 나중에 다시 데려다 놓았다."], ["When he was in Hades he obtained the liberty of Theseus, his admirer and imitator, who had been detained a prisoner there for an unsuccessful attempt to carry off Proserpine.", "그가 하데스에 있을 때, 그는 자신의 찬미자이자 모방자인 테세우스를 풀어 주었으니, 테세우스는 페르세포네[로마의 〈프로세르피나〉]를 납치하려다 실패한 죄로 그곳에 죄수로 붙들려 있던 터였다."]], [["Hercules in a fit of madness killed his friend Iphitus, and was condemned for this offence to become the slave of Queen Omphale for three years.", "헤라클레스는 광기가 발작하여 자신의 벗 이피토스를 죽였고, 이 죄로 삼 년 동안 옴팔레 여왕의 노예가 되는 벌을 받았다."], ["While in this service the hero’s nature seemed changed.", "이 봉사를 하는 동안 영웅의 본성은 변한 듯 보였다."], ["He lived effeminately, wearing at times the dress of a woman, and spinning wool with the hand-maidens of Omphale, while the queen wore his lion’s skin.", "그는 여자처럼 나약하게 살았으며, 때로는 여인의 옷을 걸치고 옴팔레의 시녀들과 함께 양털을 자아냈고, 그동안 여왕은 그의 사자 가죽을 두르고 있었다."], ["When this service was ended he married Dejanira and lived in peace with her three years.", "이 봉사가 끝나자 그는 데이아네이라와 결혼하여 삼 년 동안 그녀와 평화롭게 살았다."], ["On one occasion as he was travelling with his wife, they came to a river, across which the Centaur Nessus carried travellers for a stated fee.", "한번은 그가 아내와 함께 여행하던 중 어느 강에 이르렀는데, 그 강은 켄타우로스인 네소스가 정해진 삯을 받고 나그네들을 건네주던 곳이었다."], ["Hercules himself forded the river, but gave Dejanira to Nessus to be carried across.", "헤라클레스 자신은 강을 걸어서 건넜으나, 데이아네이라는 네소스에게 맡겨 건너게 했다."], ["Nessus attempted to run away with her, but Hercules heard her cries and shot an arrow into the heart of Nessus.", "네소스가 그녀를 데리고 달아나려 하자, 헤라클레스는 그녀의 비명을 듣고 네소스의 심장에 화살을 쏘았다."], ["The dying Centaur told Dejanira to take a portion of his blood and keep it, as it might be used as a charm to preserve the love of her husband.", "죽어 가는 켄타우로스는 데이아네이라에게, 자기 피의 일부를 받아 간직해 두라고, 그것이 남편의 사랑을 지켜 주는 부적으로 쓰일 수 있을 것이라고 일러 주었다."]], [["Dejanira did so and before long fancied she had occasion to use it.", "데이아네이라는 그대로 했고, 머지않아 그것을 쓸 일이 생겼다고 여겼다."], ["Hercules in one of his conquests had taken prisoner a fair maiden, named Iole, of whom he seemed more fond than Dejanira approved.", "헤라클레스는 자신의 정복 가운데 한 곳에서 이올레라는 아름다운 처녀를 사로잡았는데, 데이아네이라가 좋게 보아 넘길 수 있는 정도보다 그녀를 더 좋아하는 것처럼 보였다."], ["When Hercules was about to offer sacrifices to the gods in honor of his victory, he sent to his wife for a white robe to use on the occasion.", "헤라클레스가 자신의 승리를 기려 신들에게 제물을 바치려 할 즈음, 그는 그 자리에서 쓸 흰옷을 보내 달라고 아내에게 사람을 보냈다."], ["Dejanira, thinking it a good opportunity to try her love-spell, steeped the garment in the blood of Nessus.", "데이아네이라는 자신의 사랑의 주술을 시험해 볼 좋은 기회라고 여겨, 그 옷을 네소스의 피에 적셨다."], ["We are to suppose she took care to wash out all traces of it, but the magic power remained, and as soon as the garment became warm on the body of Hercules the poison penetrated into all his limbs and caused him the most intense agony.", "우리는 그녀가 피의 흔적을 모두 씻어 내려 애썼으리라 짐작해야겠으나, 그 주술의 힘은 그대로 남아 있었고, 그 옷이 헤라클레스의 몸에서 따뜻해지자마자 독이 그의 온 사지로 스며들어 그에게 더없이 격렬한 고통을 안겼다."], ["In his frenzy he seized Lichas, Who had brought him the fatal robe, and hurled him into the sea.", "그는 광란에 사로잡혀, 그 치명적인 옷을 가져온 리카스를 붙잡아 바다로 내던졌다."], ["He wrenched off the garment, but it stuck to his flesh, and with it he tore away whole pieces of his body.", "그는 그 옷을 잡아 뜯었으나, 옷은 그의 살에 들러붙어, 그것과 함께 그의 몸뚱이 살점이 통째로 떨어져 나갔다."], ["In this state he embarked on board a ship and was conveyed home.", "이런 상태로 그는 배에 올라 집으로 옮겨졌다."], ["Dejanira, on seeing what she had unwittingly done, hung herself.", "데이아네이라는 자신이 영문도 모른 채 저지른 일을 보고는 목매어 죽었다."], ["Hercules, prepared to die, ascended Mount Œta, where he built a funeral pile of trees, gave his bow and arrows to Philoctetes, and laid himself down on the pile, his head resting on his club, and his lion’s skin spread over him.", "헤라클레스는 죽을 채비를 하고 오이테 산에 올라, 그곳에 나무로 화장용 장작더미를 쌓고 자신의 활과 화살을 필록테테스에게 주었으며, 머리는 곤봉에 받치고 사자 가죽을 몸 위에 덮은 채 장작더미 위에 누웠다."], ["With a countenance as serene as if he were taking his place at a festal board he commanded Philoctetes to apply the torch.", "마치 잔칫상에 자리를 잡으려는 듯 평온한 얼굴로, 그는 필록테테스에게 횃불을 갖다 대라고 명했다."], ["The flames spread apace and soon invested the whole mass.", "불길은 빠르게 번져 곧 그 더미 전체를 휘감았다."]], [["Milton thus alludes to the frenzy of Hercules:", "밀턴은 헤라클레스의 광란을 이렇게 암시한다."]], [["“As when Alcides,[18] from Œchalia crowned With conquest, felt the envenomed robe, and tore, Through pain, up by the roots Thessalian pines And Lichas from the top of Œta threw Into the Euboic Sea.”", "\"알키데스가, 오이칼리아에서 정복으로 관을 쓰고, 독 묻은 옷을 느꼈을 때, 고통에 못 이겨 테살리아의 소나무들을 뿌리째 뽑아 올렸고 리카스를 오이테 산꼭대기에서 에우보이아 바다로 내던졌던 것처럼.\""]], [["The gods themselves felt troubled at seeing the champion of the earth so brought to his end.", "신들조차 대지의 투사가 그처럼 최후를 맞는 것을 보고 마음 아파했다."], ["But Jupiter with cheerful countenance thus addressed them: “I am pleased to see your concern, my princes, and am gratified to perceive that I am the ruler of a loyal people, and that my son enjoys your favor.", "그러나 제우스는 밝은 얼굴로 그들에게 이렇게 말했다. \"나는 그대들의 근심을 보니 기쁘고, 내가 충실한 백성의 통치자임을, 그리고 내 아들이 그대들의 호의를 누리고 있음을 알게 되어 흐뭇하오."], ["For although your interest in him arises from his noble deeds, yet it is not the less gratifying to me.", "그대들이 그에게 품은 관심이 그의 고귀한 행적에서 비롯된 것이라 해도, 그것이 내게 흐뭇하지 않은 것은 아니오."], ["But now I say to you, Fear not.", "그러나 이제 나는 그대들에게 이르노니, 두려워 마시오."], ["He who conquered all else is not to be conquered by those flames which you see blazing on Mount Œta.", "다른 모든 것을 정복한 그가, 그대들이 오이테 산에서 타오르는 것을 보는 저 불길에 정복당하지는 않을 것이오."], ["Only his mother’s share in him can perish; what he derived from me is immortal.", "다만 그 어머니에게서 받은 몫만이 스러질 수 있을 뿐, 내게서 물려받은 것은 불멸이오."], ["I shall take him, dead to earth, to the heavenly shores, and I require of you all to receive him kindly.", "나는 대지에 대해서는 죽은 그를 하늘의 기슭으로 데려가겠으니, 그대들 모두 그를 친절히 맞이해 주기를 청하오."], ["If any of you feel grieved at his attaining this honor, yet no one can deny that he has deserved it.”", "그가 이 영예에 이르는 것을 슬퍼하는 이가 그대들 가운데 있다 하더라도, 그가 그것을 받을 자격이 있다는 것은 누구도 부정할 수 없을 것이오.\""], ["The gods all gave their assent; Juno only heard the closing words with some displeasure that she should be so particularly pointed at, yet not enough to make her regret the determination of her husband.", "신들은 모두 동의했다. 헤라만이 그 끝맺는 말이 자기를 그토록 콕 집어 가리킨다는 데 다소 언짢음을 느끼며 들었으나, 그렇다고 남편의 결심을 후회하게 할 만큼은 아니었다."], ["So when the flames had consumed the mother’s share of Hercules, the diviner part, instead of being injured thereby, seemed to start forth with new vigor, to assume a more lofty port and a more awful dignity.", "그리하여 불길이 헤라클레스의 어머니에게서 받은 몫을 다 태워 버리자, 보다 신성한 부분은 그로 인해 손상되기는커녕 오히려 새로운 활력을 띠고 솟아오르는 듯하였고, 더욱 드높은 풍모와 더욱 경외로운 위엄을 갖추는 듯하였다."], ["Jupiter enveloped him in a cloud, and took him up in a four-horse chariot to dwell among the stars.", "제우스는 그를 구름으로 감싸, 네 마리 말이 끄는 전차에 태워 별들 사이에서 살도록 데려갔다."], ["As he took his place in heaven, Atlas felt the added weight.", "그가 하늘에서 제자리를 잡자, 아틀라스는 무게가 더해진 것을 느꼈다."]], [["Juno, now reconciled to him, gave him her daughter Hebe in marriage.", "이제 그와 화해한 헤라는 자기 딸 헤베를 그에게 아내로 주었다."]], [["The poet Schiller, in one of his pieces called the “Ideal and Life,” illustrates the contrast between the practical and the imaginative in some beautiful stanzas, of which the last two may be thus translated:", "시인 실러는 「이상과 삶」이라는 자신의 작품 가운데 하나에서, 실제적인 것과 상상적인 것 사이의 대조를 아름다운 시연(詩聯)으로 그려 내는데, 그 마지막 두 연을 이렇게 옮길 수 있다."]], [["“Deep degraded to a coward’s slave, Endless contests bore Alcides brave, Through the thorny path of suffering led; Slew the Hydra, crushed the lion’s might, Threw himself, to bring his friend to light, Living, in the skiff that bears the dead.", "\"비겁한 자의 노예로 깊이 굴러떨어져, 용맹한 알키데스는 끝없는 싸움을 견뎠고, 고난의 가시밭길을 헤쳐 나갔네. 히드라를 죽이고, 사자의 힘을 으스러뜨렸으며, 벗을 빛으로 데려오고자, 산 몸으로 죽은 자들을 실어 나르는 작은 배에 몸을 던졌네."], ["All the torments, every toil of earth Juno’s hatred on him could impose, Well he bore them, from his fated birth To life’s grandly mournful close.", "대지의 온갖 고통, 온갖 노역, 헤라의 증오가 그에게 지울 수 있었던 그 모두를, 그는 정해진 출생에서부터 장엄하고도 비통한 삶의 끝에 이르도록 잘 견뎠네."]], [["“Till the god, the earthly part forsaken, From the man in flames asunder taken, Drank the heavenly ether’s purer breath.", "\"마침내 그 신은, 지상의 부분을 버리고, 불길 속에서 인간으로부터 떨어져 나와, 더 순수한 천상의 정기를 들이마셨네."], ["Joyous in the new unwonted lightness, Soared he upwards to celestial brightness, Earth’s dark heavy burden lost in death.", "낯설고도 새로운 가벼움에 기뻐하며, 그는 천상의 광명을 향해 솟구쳐 올랐으니, 대지의 어둡고 무거운 짐은 죽음 속에 잃었네."], ["High Olympus gives harmonious greeting To the hall where reigns his sire adored; Youth’s bright goddess, with a blush at meeting, Gives the nectar to her lord.”", "드높은 올림포스는 그의 흠모받는 아버지가 다스리는 전당에 화음으로 인사를 보내고, 젊음의 빛나는 여신은, 마주침에 얼굴 붉히며, 자신의 주인에게 넥타르를 건네네.\" ―S."], ["—S.", "G."], ["G. B.", "B."]], [["# HEBE AND GANYMEDE", "# HEBE AND GANYMEDE"]], [["Hebe, the daughter of Juno, and goddess of youth, was cup-bearer to the gods.", "헤라의 딸이자 젊음의 여신인 헤베는 신들의 술 따르는 시종이었다."], ["The usual story is that she resigned her office on becoming the wife of Hercules.", "흔히 전하는 이야기로는 그녀가 헤라클레스의 아내가 되면서 그 직분을 내려놓았다고 한다."], ["But there is another statement which our countryman Crawford, the sculptor, has adopted in his group of Hebe and Ganymede, now in the Athenæum gallery.", "그러나 또 다른 설명이 있는데, 우리나라 사람인 조각가 크로퍼드가 지금 아테나이움 화랑에 있는 그의 군상 「헤베와 가니메데스」에서 이를 채택한 바 있다."], ["According to this, Hebe was dismissed from her office in consequence of a fall which she met with one day when in attendance on the gods.", "그에 따르면 헤베는 어느 날 신들의 시중을 들던 중에 넘어지는 일을 당한 까닭에 그 직분에서 물러나게 되었다."], ["Her successor was Ganymede, a Trojan boy, whom Jupiter, in the disguise of an eagle, seized and carried off from the midst of his playfellows on Mount Ida, bore up to heaven, and installed in the vacant place.", "그녀의 뒤를 이은 것은 트로이아의 소년 가니메데스였는데, 제우스가 독수리로 변장하여 이다 산에서 놀이 동무들 한가운데 있던 그를 낚아채 데려가, 하늘로 올려 그 빈자리에 앉혔던 것이다."]], [["Tennyson, in his “Palace of Art,” describes among the decorations on the walls a picture representing this legend:", "테니슨은 자신의 「예술의 궁전」에서, 벽을 꾸민 장식들 가운데 이 전설을 그린 그림 하나를 묘사한다."]], [["“There, too, flushed Ganymede, his rosy thigh Half buried in the eagle’s down, Sole as a flying star shot through the sky Above the pillared town.”", "\"거기 또한, 발그레한 가니메데스가, 그의 장밋빛 넓적다리를 독수리의 솜털 속에 반쯤 묻은 채, 기둥 늘어선 도시 위로 하늘을 가로질러 쏜살같이 나는 별처럼 홀로 있네.\""]], [["And in Shelley’s “Prometheus” Jupiter calls to his cup-bearer thus:", "그리고 셸리의 「프로메테우스」에서 제우스는 자신의 술 따르는 시종을 이렇게 부른다."]], [["“Pour forth heaven’s wine, Idæan Ganymede, And let it fill the Dædal cups like fire.”", "\"하늘의 술을 따라 부어라, 이다 산의 가니메데스여, 그리하여 다이달로스의 잔들을 불처럼 가득 채워라.\""]], [["The beautiful legend of the “Choice of Hercules” may be found in the “Tatler,”", "「헤라클레스의 선택」이라는 아름다운 전설은 「태틀러」"], ["No. 97.", "제97호에서 찾아볼 수 있다."]], [["————", "────"]], [["THESEUS was the son of Ægeus, king of Athens, and of Æthra, daughter of the king of Trœzen.", "테세우스는 아테네의 왕 아이게우스와 트로이젠 왕의 딸 아이트라 사이에서 태어난 아들이었다."], ["He was brought up at Trœzen, and when arrived at manhood was to proceed to Athens and present himself to his father.", "그는 트로이젠에서 양육되었고, 장성하여 어른이 되면 아테네로 가서 아버지 앞에 자기를 내보일 예정이었다."], ["Ægeus on parting from Æthra, before the birth of his son, placed his sword and shoes under a large stone and directed her to send his son to him when he became strong enough to roll away the stone and take them from under it.", "아이게우스는 아들이 태어나기 전에 아이트라와 작별하면서 자신의 칼과 신발을 커다란 바위 밑에 넣어 두고는, 아들이 그 바위를 굴려 치우고 그 밑에서 그것들을 꺼낼 수 있을 만큼 자라거든 자기에게 보내라고 일렀다."], ["When she thought the time had come, his mother led Theseus to the stone, and he removed it with ease and took the sword and shoes.", "때가 되었다고 여겨졌을 때, 그의 어머니는 테세우스를 그 바위로 데려갔고, 그는 손쉽게 그것을 치우고 칼과 신발을 꺼냈다."], ["As the roads were infested with robbers, his grandfather pressed him earnestly to take the shorter and safer way to his father’s country—by sea; but the youth, feeling in himself the spirit and the soul of a hero, and eager to signalize himself like Hercules, with whose fame all Greece then rang, by destroying the evil-doers and monsters that oppressed the country, determined on the more perilous and adventurous journey by land.", "길마다 도적들이 들끓었으므로, 외조부는 아버지의 나라로 가는 더 짧고 안전한 길을, 곧 바닷길을 택하라고 그에게 간곡히 권했다. 하지만 자신 안에 영웅의 기상과 혼이 깃들어 있음을 느낀 그 젊은이는, 당시 온 그리스가 그 명성으로 떠들썩했던 헤라클레스처럼, 나라를 짓누르던 악행을 일삼는 자들과 괴물들을 무찔러 스스로 이름을 떨치기를 갈망하여, 더 위험하고 모험으로 가득한 육로 여행을 결심했다."]], [["His first day’s journey brought him to Epidaurus, where dwelt a man named Periphetes, a son of Vulcan.", "첫날의 여정 끝에 그는 에피다우로스에 이르렀는데, 그곳에는 헤파이스토스[로마의 〈불카누스〉]의 아들인 페리페테스라는 사내가 살고 있었다."], ["This ferocious savage always went armed with a club of iron, and all travellers stood in terror of his violence.", "이 흉포한 야만인은 언제나 쇠몽둥이로 무장하고 다녔으므로, 모든 나그네가 그의 난폭함을 두려워했다."], ["When he saw Theseus approach he assailed him, but speedily fell beneath the blows of the young hero, who took possession of his club and bore it ever afterwards as a memorial of his first victory.", "그는 테세우스가 다가오는 것을 보자 그를 덮쳤으나, 이내 젊은 영웅의 타격 아래 쓰러졌고, 영웅은 그 몽둥이를 차지하여 그 후로 줄곧 자신의 첫 승리를 기리는 기념물로 지니고 다녔다."]], [["Several similar contests with the petty tyrants and marauders of the country followed, in all of which Theseus was victorious.", "그 나라의 소소한 폭군들과 약탈자들을 상대로 한 비슷한 싸움이 여러 차례 이어졌고, 그 모두에서 테세우스는 승리를 거두었다."], ["One of these evil-doers was called Procrustes, or the Stretcher.", "이 악행을 일삼는 자들 가운데 하나는 프로크루스테스, 곧 〈잡아 늘이는 자〉라고 불렸다."], ["He had an iron bedstead, on which he used to tie all travellers who fell into his hands.", "그는 쇠로 된 침상을 갖고 있었는데, 자기 손에 걸려든 나그네들을 모두 거기에 묶곤 했다."], ["If they were shorter than the bed, he stretched their limbs to make them fit it; if they were longer than the bed, he lopped off a portion.", "그들이 침상보다 짧으면 사지를 잡아 늘여 침상에 맞추었고, 침상보다 길면 일부를 잘라 냈다."], ["Theseus served him as he had served others.", "테세우스는 그가 다른 이들에게 했던 그대로 그를 다루었다."]], [["Having overcome all the perils of the road, Theseus at length reached Athens, where new dangers awaited him.", "길의 온갖 위험을 다 이겨 낸 끝에 테세우스는 마침내 아테네에 다다랐는데, 그곳에서는 새로운 위험들이 그를 기다리고 있었다."], ["Medea, the sorceress, who had fled from Corinth after her separation from Jason, had become the wife of Ægeus, the father of Theseus.", "이아손과 갈라선 뒤 코린토스에서 달아났던 마녀 메데이아가 테세우스의 아버지 아이게우스의 아내가 되어 있었던 것이다."], ["Knowing by her arts who he was, and fearing the loss of her influence with her husband if Theseus should be acknowledged as his son, she filled the mind of Ægeus with suspicions of the young stranger, and induced him to present him a cup of poison; but at the moment when Theseus stepped forward to take it, the sight of the sword which he wore discovered to his father who he was, and prevented the fatal draught.", "그녀는 자신의 술법으로 그가 누구인지 알아채고는, 테세우스가 아들로 인정받으면 남편에 대한 자신의 영향력을 잃을까 두려워하여, 아이게우스의 마음에 그 젊은 이방인에 대한 의심을 가득 채워 넣었고, 그에게 독이 든 잔을 권하도록 부추겼다. 그러나 테세우스가 그것을 받으려고 앞으로 나서는 순간, 그가 차고 있던 칼이 눈에 띄어 아버지에게 그가 누구인지를 드러내 주었고, 이로써 치명적인 한 모금을 막아 주었다."], ["Medea, detected in her arts, fled once more from deserved punishment, and arrived in Asia, where the country afterwards called Medea received its name from her.", "술법이 발각된 메데이아는 또다시 마땅한 벌을 피해 달아나 아시아에 이르렀는데, 그 후 그곳의 나라는 그녀의 이름을 따 메데이아라 불리게 되었다."], ["Theseus was acknowledged by his father, and declared his successor.", "테세우스는 아버지에게 인정받았고, 그 후계자로 선포되었다."]], [["The Athenians were at that time in deep affliction, on account of the tribute which they were forced to pay to Minos, king of Crete.", "당시 아테네 사람들은 크레타의 왕 미노스에게 바칠 수밖에 없었던 공물 때문에 깊은 고통에 잠겨 있었다."], ["This tribute consisted of seven youths and seven maidens, who were sent every year to be devoured by the Minotaur, a monster with a bull’s body and a human head.", "이 공물은 일곱 명의 젊은이와 일곱 명의 처녀로 이루어져 있었는데, 이들은 해마다 미노타우로스에게 잡아먹히도록 보내졌다. 미노타우로스는 황소의 몸에 사람의 머리를 가진 괴물이었다."], ["It was exceedingly strong and fierce, and was kept in a labyrinth constructed by Dædalus, so artfully contrived that whoever was enclosed in it could by no means find his way out unassisted.", "그것은 지극히 강하고 사나웠으며, 다이달로스가 지은 미궁 안에 갇혀 있었다. 그 미궁은 어찌나 교묘하게 만들어졌던지, 그 안에 갇힌 자는 누구든 도움 없이는 결코 빠져나갈 길을 찾지 못했다."], ["Here the Minotaur roamed, and was fed with human victims.", "이곳에서 미노타우로스는 돌아다니며 사람 제물을 먹이로 받았다."]], [["Theseus resolved to deliver his countrymen from this calamity, or to die in the attempt.", "테세우스는 이 재앙으로부터 자기 동족을 구해 내거나, 아니면 그 시도 중에 죽기로 결심했다."], ["Accordingly, when the time of sending off the tribute came, and the youths and maidens were, according to custom, drawn by lot to be sent, he offered himself as one of the victims, in spite of the entreaties of his father.", "그리하여 공물을 보낼 때가 오고, 관례에 따라 보낼 젊은이들과 처녀들을 제비뽑기로 정하게 되자, 그는 아버지의 간곡한 만류에도 불구하고 자신을 제물 가운데 하나로 내놓았다."], ["The ship departed under black sails, as usual, which Theseus promised his father to change for white, in case of his returning victorious.", "배는 여느 때처럼 검은 돛을 달고 떠났는데, 테세우스는 승리하여 돌아올 경우 그것을 흰 돛으로 바꾸겠노라 아버지에게 약속했다."], ["When they arrived in Crete, the youths and maidens were exhibited before Minos; and Ariadne, the daughter of the king, being present, became deeply enamored of Theseus, by whom her love was readily returned.", "그들이 크레타에 이르자 젊은이들과 처녀들은 미노스 앞에 내보여졌다. 그 자리에 있던 왕의 딸 아리아드네는 테세우스에게 깊이 반하게 되었고, 그 사랑은 그에게서 선뜻 화답받았다."], ["She furnished him with a sword, with which to encounter the Minotaur, and with a clew of thread by which he might find his way out of the labyrinth.", "그녀는 미노타우로스와 맞서 싸우라고 그에게 칼을 마련해 주었고, 또 미궁에서 빠져나올 길을 찾도록 실타래 하나를 건네주었다."], ["He was successful, slew the Minotaur, escaped from the labyrinth, and taking Ariadne as the companion of his way, with his rescued companions sailed for Athens.", "그는 성공하여 미노타우로스를 죽이고 미궁에서 벗어났으며, 아리아드네를 길동무로 삼아, 구해 낸 동료들과 함께 아테네를 향해 배를 띄웠다."], ["On their way they stopped at the island of Naxos, where Theseus abandoned Ariadne, leaving her asleep.[19] His excuse for this ungrateful treatment of his benefactress was that Minerva appeared to him in a dream and commanded him to do so.", "가는 길에 그들은 낙소스섬에 들렀는데, 그곳에서 테세우스는 아리아드네가 잠든 사이에 그녀를 버리고 떠났다.[19] 자신의 은인을 이처럼 배은망덕하게 대한 데 대한 그의 변명은, 아테나[로마의 〈미네르바〉]가 꿈에 나타나 그렇게 하라고 명했다는 것이었다."]], [["On approaching the coast of Attica, Theseus forgot the signal appointed by his father, and neglected to raise the white sails, and the old king, thinking his son had perished, put an end to his own life.", "아티카의 해안에 다가가면서 테세우스는 아버지가 정해 둔 신호를 잊고서 흰 돛을 올리는 일을 소홀히 했고, 늙은 왕은 아들이 죽은 줄로 알고 스스로 목숨을 끊었다."], ["Theseus thus became king of Athens.", "이리하여 테세우스는 아테네의 왕이 되었다."]], [["One of the most celebrated of the adventures of Theseus is his expedition against the Amazons.", "테세우스의 모험 가운데 가장 유명한 것 하나는 아마조네스를 상대로 한 원정이다."], ["He assailed them before they had recovered from the attack of Hercules, and carried off their queen Antiope.", "그는 그들이 헤라클레스의 공격에서 미처 회복되기 전에 그들을 덮쳐, 그들의 여왕 안티오페를 데려왔다."], ["The Amazons in their turn invaded the country of Athens and penetrated into the city itself; and the final battle in which Theseus overcame them was fought in the very midst of the city.", "이번에는 아마조네스가 아테네의 나라로 쳐들어와 도시 한복판까지 파고들었고, 테세우스가 그들을 무찌른 마지막 전투는 바로 도시 한가운데에서 벌어졌다."], ["This battle was one of the favorite subjects of the ancient sculptors, and is commemorated in several works of art that are still extant.", "이 전투는 고대 조각가들이 즐겨 다룬 주제 가운데 하나였으며, 지금도 전해지는 여러 예술 작품에 기념되어 있다."]], [["The friendship between Theseus and Pirithous was of a most intimate nature, yet it originated in the midst of arms.", "테세우스와 페이리토오스 사이의 우정은 더없이 친밀한 것이었으나, 그것은 무기를 맞댄 가운데서 비롯되었다. 페이리토오스는 마라톤 평원으로 쳐들어와 아테네 왕의 가축 떼를 몰고 갔다."], ["Pirithous had made an irruption into the plain of Marathon, and carried off the herds of the king of Athens.", "테세우스는 그 약탈자들을 물리치러 나섰다. 페이리토오스는 그를 보는 순간 감탄에 사로잡혔다."], ["Theseus went to repel the plunderers.", "그는 화평의 표시로 손을 내밀며 외쳤다."], ["The moment Pirithous beheld him, he was seized with admiration; he stretched out his hand as a token of peace, and cried, “Be judge thyself—what satisfaction dost thou require?”", "\"그대 스스로 판가름하라. 어떤 보상을 바라는가?\""], ["“Thy friendship,”", "\"그대의 우정이오.\""], ["replied the Athenian, and they swore inviolable fidelity.", "아테네인이 대답했고, 두 사람은 깨뜨릴 수 없는 신의를 맹세했다."], ["Their deeds corresponded to their professions, and they ever continued true brothers in arms.", "그들의 행적은 그들의 다짐과 들어맞았으며, 그들은 언제까지나 진정한 전우로 남았다."], ["Each of them aspired to espouse a daughter of Jupiter.", "둘은 저마다 제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]의 딸을 아내로 맞기를 열망했다."], ["Theseus fixed his choice on Helen, then but a child, afterwards so celebrated as the cause of the Trojan war, and with the aid of his friend he carried her off.", "테세우스는 당시 아직 어린아이였던 헬레네를 점찍었는데, 그녀는 훗날 트로이아 전쟁의 원인으로 그토록 유명해진 여인이었고, 그는 친구의 도움을 받아 그녀를 데려왔다."], ["Pirithous aspired to the wife of the monarch of Erebus; and Theseus, though aware of the danger, accompanied the ambitious lover in his descent to the under-world.", "페이리토오스는 에레보스[저승]의 군주의 아내를 열망했고, 테세우스는 그 위험을 알면서도 야심 찬 연인을 따라 저승으로 내려갔다."], ["But Pluto seized and set them on an enchanted rock at his palace gate, where they remained till Hercules arrived and liberated Theseus, leaving Pirithous to his fate.", "그러나 하데스[로마의 〈플루토〉]는 그들을 붙잡아 자기 궁전 문 앞의 마법에 걸린 바위에 앉혀 두었고, 그들은 헤라클레스가 와서 테세우스를 풀어 줄 때까지 그곳에 머물렀으며, 페이리토오스는 자신의 운명에 내맡겨졌다."]], [["After the death of Antiope, Theseus married Phædra, daughter of Minos, king of Crete.", "안티오페가 죽은 뒤, 테세우스는 크레타의 왕 미노스의 딸 파이드라와 혼인했다."], ["Phædra saw in Hippolytus, the son of Theseus, a youth endowed with all the graces and virtues of his father, and of an age corresponding to her own.", "파이드라는 테세우스의 아들 히폴리토스에게서 그 아버지의 온갖 우아함과 덕을 두루 갖추었으며 자신과 또래인 젊은이를 보았다."], ["She loved him, but he repulsed her advances, and her love was changed to hate.", "그녀는 그를 사랑했으나 그가 그녀의 구애를 물리치자, 그 사랑은 미움으로 바뀌었다."], ["She used her influence over her infatuated husband to cause him to be jealous of his son, and he imprecated the vengeance of Neptune upon him.", "그녀는 사랑에 눈먼 남편에 대한 영향력을 이용해 그가 제 아들을 질투하도록 만들었고, 그는 포세이돈[로마의 〈넵투누스〉]의 복수가 아들에게 내리기를 빌었다."], ["As Hippolytus was one day driving his chariot along the shore, a sea-monster raised himself above the waters, and frightened the horses so that they ran away and dashed the chariot to pieces.", "어느 날 히폴리토스가 해안을 따라 전차를 몰고 있을 때, 바다 괴물 하나가 물 위로 솟아올라 말들을 놀라게 했고, 말들은 달아나다가 전차를 산산조각 냈다."], ["Hippolytus was killed, but by Diana’s assistance Æsculapius restored him to life.", "히폴리토스는 죽고 말았으나, 아르테미스[로마의 〈디아나〉]의 도움으로 아스클레피오스가 그를 되살려 냈다."], ["Diana removed Hippolytus from the power of his deluded father and false stepmother, and placed him in Italy under the protection of the nymph Egeria.", "아르테미스는 히폴리토스를 속아 넘어간 아버지와 거짓된 계모의 손에서 빼내어, 이탈리아에 있는 님프 에게리아의 보호 아래 두었다."]], [["Theseus at length lost the favor of his people, and retired to the court of Lycomedes, king of Scyros, who at first received him kindly, but afterwards treacherously slew him.", "테세우스는 마침내 백성의 신망을 잃고서, 스키로스의 왕 리코메데스의 궁정으로 물러났는데, 리코메데스는 처음에는 그를 친절히 맞아 주었으나 나중에는 그를 배신하여 죽였다."], ["In a later age the Athenian general Cimon discovered the place where his remains were laid, and caused them to be removed to Athens, where they were deposited in a temple called the Theseum, erected in honor of the hero.", "후대에 이르러 아테네의 장군 키몬이 그의 유해가 묻힌 곳을 찾아내어 그것을 아테네로 옮겨 오게 했고, 그 유해는 그 영웅을 기려 세운 테세이온이라는 신전에 안치되었다."]], [["The queen of the Amazons whom Theseus espoused is by some called Hippolyta.", "테세우스가 아내로 맞은 아마조네스의 여왕을 어떤 이들은 히폴리타라고 부른다."], ["That is the name she bears in Shakspeare’s “Midsummer Night’s Dream,”—the subject of which is the festivities attending the nuptials of Theseus and Hippolyta.", "그것은 셰익스피어의 「한여름 밤의 꿈」에서 그녀가 지니는 이름인데, 이 작품의 주제는 테세우스와 히폴리타의 혼례에 따르는 축제다."]], [["Mrs. Hemans has a poem on the ancient Greek tradition that the “Shade of Theseus” appeared strengthening his countrymen at the battle of Marathon.", "헤먼스 부인은, 마라톤 전투에서 〈테세우스의 망령〉이 나타나 동족을 북돋워 주었다는 고대 그리스의 전승을 두고 시 한 편을 썼다."]], [["Theseus is a semi-historical personage.", "테세우스는 반쯤은 역사적인 인물이다."], ["It is recorded of him that he united the several tribes by whom the territory of Attica was then possessed into one state, of which Athens was the capital.", "그에 관해서는, 당시 아티카 영토를 차지하고 있던 여러 부족을 하나의 국가로 통합하고, 그 수도를 아테네로 삼았다고 기록되어 있다."], ["In commemoration of this important event, he instituted the festival of Panathenæa, in honor of Minerva, the patron deity of Athens.", "이 중대한 사건을 기념하여 그는 아테네의 수호신 아테나를 기리는 판아테나이아 축제를 창설했다."], ["This festival differed from the other Grecian games chiefly in two particulars.", "이 축제는 주로 두 가지 점에서 다른 그리스의 경기들과 달랐다."], ["It was peculiar to the Athenians, and its chief feature was a solemn procession in which the Peplus, or sacred robe of Minerva, was carried to the Parthenon, and suspended before the statue of the goddess.", "그것은 아테네인들에게만 고유한 것이었고, 그 핵심 행사는 아테나의 신성한 옷인 페플로스를 파르테논으로 받들어 가서 여신상 앞에 걸어 두는 엄숙한 행렬이었다."], ["The Peplus was covered with embroidery, worked by select virgins of the noblest families in Athens.", "페플로스는 아테네에서 가장 고귀한 가문 출신으로 뽑힌 처녀들이 손수 짠 자수로 뒤덮여 있었다."], ["The procession consisted of persons of all ages and both sexes.", "행렬은 온갖 연령과 양쪽 성별의 사람들로 이루어졌다."], ["The old men carried olive branches in their hands, and the young men bore arms.", "노인들은 손에 올리브 가지를 들었고, 젊은이들은 무기를 들었다."], ["The young women carried baskets on their heads, containing the sacred utensils, cakes, and all things necessary for the sacrifices.", "젊은 여인들은 머리에 바구니를 이었는데, 그 안에는 신성한 제기와 과자, 그리고 제사에 필요한 온갖 것들이 담겨 있었다."], ["The procession formed the subject of the bas-reliefs which embellished the outside of the temple of the Parthenon.", "이 행렬은 파르테논 신전 바깥을 장식한 부조의 주제가 되었다."], ["A considerable portion of these sculptures is now in the British Museum among those known as the “Elgin marbles.”", "이 조각들 가운데 상당 부분이 지금은 〈엘긴 대리석〉으로 알려진 작품들에 섞여 대영 박물관에 소장되어 있다."]], [["# OLYMPIC AND OTHER GAMES", "# OLYMPIC AND OTHER GAMES"]], [["It seems not inappropriate to mention here the other celebrated national games of the Greeks.", "여기서 그리스인들의 다른 유명한 국민적 경기들을 언급하는 것도 부적절하지는 않을 듯하다."], ["The first and most distinguished were the Olympic, founded, it was said, by Jupiter himself.", "첫째이자 가장 이름난 것은 올림피아 경기였는데, 제우스가 친히 창설했다고 전해진다."], ["They were celebrated at Olympia in Elis.", "이 경기는 엘리스의 올림피아에서 거행되었다."], ["Vast numbers of spectators flocked to them from every part of Greece, and from Asia, Africa, and Sicily.", "헤아릴 수 없이 많은 구경꾼들이 그리스 각지에서, 또 아시아와 아프리카와 시켈리아[시칠리아]에서 그곳으로 몰려들었다."], ["They were repeated every fifth year in midsummer, and continued five days.", "이 경기는 한여름에 다섯 해마다 거듭 열렸고, 닷새 동안 이어졌다."], ["They gave rise to the custom of reckoning time and dating events by Olympiads.", "이로부터 올림피아드를 단위로 시간을 헤아리고 사건의 연대를 매기는 관습이 생겨났다."], ["The first Olympiad is generally considered as corresponding with the year 776 B.C.", "첫 번째 올림피아드는 일반적으로 기원전 776년에 해당하는 것으로 여겨진다."], ["The Pythian games were celebrated in the vicinity of Delphi, the Isthmian on the Corinthian isthmus, the Nemean at Nemea, a city of Argolis.", "피티아 경기는 델포이 부근에서, 이스트미아 경기는 코린토스 지협에서, 네메아 경기는 아르골리스의 도시 네메아에서 거행되었다."]], [["The exercises in these games were of five sorts: running, leaping, wrestling, throwing the quoit, and hurling the javelin, or boxing.", "이 경기들의 종목은 다섯 가지였다. 곧 달리기, 뛰기, 씨름, 원반던지기, 그리고 창던지기 혹은 권투였다."], ["Besides these exercises of bodily strength and agility, there were contests in music, poetry, and eloquence.", "이러한 신체의 힘과 민첩함을 겨루는 종목들 외에도, 음악과 시와 웅변을 겨루는 경연이 있었다."], ["Thus these games furnished poets, musicians, and authors the best opportunities to present their productions to the public, and the fame of the victors was diffused far and wide.", "그리하여 이 경기들은 시인과 음악가와 작가들에게 자기 작품을 대중 앞에 선보일 더없이 좋은 기회를 마련해 주었고, 승리자들의 명성은 멀리 널리 퍼져 나갔다."]], [["The labyrinth from which Theseus escaped by means of the clew of Ariadne was built by Dædalus, a most skilful artificer.", "테세우스가 아리아드네의 실타래 덕분에 빠져나온 미궁은 더없이 솜씨 좋은 장인 다이달로스가 지은 것이었다."], ["It was an edifice with numberless winding passages and turnings opening into one another, and seeming to have neither beginning nor end, like the river Mæander, which returns on itself, and flows now onward, now backward, in its course to the sea.", "그것은 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 굽이진 통로와 모퉁이가 서로 맞물려 열려 있는 건축물로, 마치 제 자신에게로 되돌아와 바다로 흘러가는 길에 때로는 앞으로, 때로는 뒤로 흐르는 마이안드로스강처럼, 시작도 끝도 없어 보였다."], ["Dædalus built the labyrinth for King Minos, but afterwards lost the favor of the king, and was shut up in a tower.", "다이달로스는 미노스 왕을 위해 그 미궁을 지었으나, 그 후 왕의 신망을 잃고 탑에 갇혔다."], ["He contrived to make his escape from his prison, but could not leave the island by sea, as the king kept strict watch on all the vessels, and permitted none to sail without being carefully searched.", "그는 궁리 끝에 감옥에서 빠져나오는 데는 성공했지만, 왕이 모든 배를 철저히 감시하여 꼼꼼히 수색하지 않고서는 어떤 배도 출항을 허락하지 않았으므로, 바닷길로는 섬을 떠날 수가 없었다."], ["“Minos may control the land and sea,” said Dædalus, “but not the regions of the air.", "\"미노스가 땅과 바다는 다스릴 수 있을지언정 하늘의 영역은 다스리지 못한다.\""], ["I will try that way.”", "다이달로스가 말했다."], ["So he set to work to fabricate wings for himself and his young son Icarus.", "\"나는 그 길로 가 보겠다.\" 그리하여 그는 자신과 어린 아들 이카로스를 위해 날개를 만드는 일에 착수했다."], ["He wrought feathers together, beginning with the smallest and adding larger, so as to form an increasing surface.", "그는 가장 작은 깃털부터 시작하여 점점 큰 것을 더해 가며 깃털들을 한데 엮어, 차츰 넓어지는 면을 이루도록 했다."], ["The larger ones he secured with thread and the smaller with wax, and gave the whole a gentle curvature like the wings of a bird.", "큰 깃털들은 실로 동여매고 작은 것들은 밀랍으로 붙였으며, 그 전체에 새의 날개처럼 부드러운 곡선을 주었다."], ["Icarus, the boy, stood and looked on, sometimes running to gather up the feathers which the wind had blown away, and then handling the wax and working it over with his fingers, by his play impeding his father in his labors.", "어린 이카로스는 곁에 서서 구경하다가, 이따금 바람에 날려 간 깃털들을 주우러 달려가기도 하고, 또 밀랍을 만지작거리며 손가락으로 주물러 대기도 하면서, 그 장난으로 일에 몰두한 아버지를 방해했다."], ["When at last the work was done, the artist, waving his wings, found himself buoyed upward, and hung suspended, poising himself on the beaten air.", "마침내 일이 다 끝나자, 장인은 날개를 퍼덕이며 자신의 몸이 위로 떠오르는 것을 느꼈고, 두드린 공기 위에 몸을 가눈 채 공중에 매달려 있었다."], ["He next equipped his son in the same manner, and taught him how to fly, as a bird tempts her young ones from the lofty nest into the air.", "이어서 그는 같은 방식으로 아들에게도 날개를 채워 주고, 마치 어미 새가 새끼들을 높은 둥지에서 공중으로 꾀어내듯이 그에게 나는 법을 가르쳤다. 날아오를 채비가 모두 갖추어지자 그가 말했다."], ["When all was prepared for flight he said, “Icarus, my son, I charge you to keep at a moderate height, for if you fly too low the damp will clog your wings, and if too high the heat will melt them.", "\"내 아들 이카로스야, 너에게 당부하노니 적당한 높이를 지켜라. 너무 낮게 날면 습기가 날개를 무겁게 할 것이고, 너무 높이 날면 열기가 날개를 녹여 버릴 것이다."], ["Keep near me and you will be safe.”", "내 곁에 가까이 있으면 안전할 것이다.\""], ["While he gave him these instructions and fitted the wings to his shoulders, the face of the father was wet with tears, and his hands trembled.", "이렇게 그에게 일러 주며 어깨에 날개를 채워 주는 동안, 아버지의 얼굴은 눈물로 젖었고 두 손은 떨렸다."], ["He kissed the boy, not knowing that it was for the last time.", "그는 그것이 마지막인 줄도 모르고 아들에게 입을 맞추었다."], ["Then rising on his wings, he flew off, encouraging him to follow, and looked back from his own flight to see how his son managed his wings.", "그러고는 날개로 떠올라 날아가며 아들에게 뒤따르라고 격려했고, 자신의 비행 중에 뒤를 돌아보아 아들이 날개를 어떻게 다루는지 살폈다."], ["As they flew the ploughman stopped his work to gaze, and the shepherd leaned on his staff and watched them, astonished at the sight, and thinking they were gods who could thus cleave the air.", "그들이 날아갈 때 밭갈이하던 농부는 하던 일을 멈추고 바라보았고, 양치기는 지팡이에 몸을 기댄 채 그들을 지켜보면서, 그 광경에 놀라 저렇게 공기를 가르는 것들은 신이 아닐까 생각했다."]], [["They passed Samos and Delos on the left and Lebynthos on the right, when the boy, exulting in his career, began to leave the guidance of his companion and soar upward as if to reach heaven.", "그들은 사모스와 델로스를 왼편에, 레빈토스를 오른편에 두고 지나쳤는데, 그때 비행에 우쭐해진 소년은 길잡이인 동행에게서 벗어나기 시작하더니 마치 하늘에 닿으려는 듯 위로 솟구쳤다."], ["The nearness of the blazing sun softened the wax which held the feathers together, and they came off.", "이글거리는 태양이 가까워지자 깃털들을 한데 붙들고 있던 밀랍이 물러져, 깃털들이 떨어져 나갔다."], ["He fluttered with his arms, but no feathers remained to hold the air.", "그는 두 팔을 퍼덕였으나, 공기를 붙들어 줄 깃털은 더 이상 남아 있지 않았다."], ["While his mouth uttered cries to his father it was submerged in the blue waters of the sea, which thenceforth was called by his name.", "그의 입이 아버지를 부르는 외침을 토해 내는 동안, 그 입은 바다의 푸른 물결 속에 잠겨 버렸고, 그 바다는 그 후로 그의 이름으로 불리게 되었다."], ["His father cried, “Icarus, Icarus, where are you?”", "아버지가 외쳤다. \"이카로스야, 이카로스야, 어디에 있느냐?\""], ["At last he saw the feathers floating on the water, and bitterly lamenting his own arts, he buried the body and called the land Icaria in memory of his child.", "마침내 그는 물 위에 떠다니는 깃털들을 보았고, 자신의 솜씨를 쓰라리게 한탄하며 아들의 시신을 묻고는 그 땅을 자기 아이를 기려 이카리아라 불렀다."], ["Dædalus arrived safe in Sicily, where he built a temple to Apollo, and hung up his wings, an offering to the god.", "다이달로스는 무사히 시켈리아에 다다라 그곳에 아폴론을 위한 신전을 짓고, 자신의 날개를 신에게 바치는 봉헌물로 걸어 두었다."]], [["Dædalus was so proud of his achievements that he could not bear the idea of a rival.", "다이달로스는 자신의 업적을 어찌나 자랑스러워했던지 경쟁자가 있다는 생각을 견디지 못했다."], ["His sister had placed her son Perdix under his charge to be taught the mechanical arts.", "그의 누이가 아들 페르딕스를 그의 손에 맡겨 기계 기술을 배우게 했다."], ["He was an apt scholar and gave striking evidences of ingenuity.", "그는 재주 있는 제자였고 놀라운 재능의 증거를 여러 차례 보여 주었다."], ["Walking on the seashore he picked up the spine of a fish.", "바닷가를 거닐다가 그는 물고기의 등뼈를 주웠다."], ["Imitating it, he took a piece of iron and notched it on the edge, and thus invented the saw.", "그것을 본떠 쇳조각 하나를 가져다가 가장자리에 톱니를 새겨, 이리하여 톱을 발명했다."], ["He put two pieces of iron together, connecting them at one end with a rivet, and sharpening the other ends, and made a pair of compasses.", "그는 쇳조각 두 개를 한쪽 끝에서 못으로 이어 붙이고 다른 끝들을 뾰족하게 갈아 컴퍼스를 만들었다."], ["Dædalus was so envious of his nephew’s performances that he took an opportunity, when they were together one day on the top of a high tower, to push him off.", "다이달로스는 조카의 솜씨를 어찌나 시샘했던지, 어느 날 둘이 높은 탑 꼭대기에 함께 있을 때를 틈타 그를 밀어 떨어뜨렸다."], ["But Minerva, who favors ingenuity, saw him falling, and arrested his fate by changing him into a bird called after his name, the Partridge.", "그러나 재주를 아끼는 아테나가 그가 떨어지는 것을 보고는, 그를 그의 이름을 딴 자고새라는 새로 바꾸어 그 운명을 막아 주었다."], ["This bird does not build his nest in the trees, nor take lofty flights, but nestles in the hedges, and mindful of his fall, avoids high places.", "이 새는 나무에 둥지를 틀지도 않고 높이 날아오르지도 않으며, 산울타리 속에 깃들고 자신이 떨어졌던 일을 잊지 않아 높은 곳을 피한다."]], [["The death of Icarus is told in the following lines by Darwin:", "이카로스의 죽음은 다윈이 다음과 같은 시구로 노래하고 있다."]], [["“. .", "\"……녹아내리는 밀랍과 풀려난 실과 더불어 가엾은 이카로스는 믿지 못할 날개를 타고 가라앉았네."], [".", "겁에 질린 공기를 곤두박질쳐 뚫고 내려가니, 사지는 뒤틀리고 머리카락은 흐트러졌네. 흩어진 깃털은 물결 위에서 춤추었고, 슬퍼하는 네레이데스가 그의 물속 무덤을 꾸며 주었네."], ["with melting wax and loosened strings Sunk hapless Icarus on unfaithful wings; Headlong he rushed through the affrighted air, With limbs distorted and dishevelled hair; His scattered plumage danced upon the wave, And sorrowing Nereids decked his watery grave; O’er his pale corse their pearly sea-flowers shed, And strewed with crimson moss his marble bed; Struck in their coral towers the passing bell, And wide in ocean tolled his echoing knell.”", "그의 창백한 주검 위에 진주 같은 바다꽃을 뿌리고, 그의 대리석 침상에 진홍빛 이끼를 깔아 주었네. 산호 탑에서 조종을 울리니, 그 메아리치는 만가가 너른 바다에 울려 퍼졌네.\""]], [["# CASTOR AND POLLUX", "# CASTOR AND POLLUX"]], [["Castor and Pollux were the offspring of Leda and the Swan, under which disguise Jupiter had concealed himself.", "카스토르와 폴리데우케스는 레다와 백조 사이에서 난 자식들이었는데, 제우스는 그 백조의 모습으로 자신을 숨겼던 것이다."], ["Leda gave birth to an egg from which sprang the twins.", "레다는 알 하나를 낳았고, 거기서 쌍둥이가 태어났다."], ["Helen, so famous afterwards as the cause of the Trojan war, was their sister.", "훗날 트로이아 전쟁의 원인으로 그토록 유명해진 헬레네가 그들의 누이였다."]], [["When Theseus and his friend Pirithous had carried off Helen from Sparta, the youthful heroes Castor and Pollux, with their followers, hastened to her rescue.", "테세우스와 그의 친구 페이리토오스가 스파르타에서 헬레네를 데려갔을 때, 젊은 영웅 카스토르와 폴리데우케스는 추종자들을 거느리고 누이를 구하러 서둘러 달려갔다."], ["Theseus was absent from Attica and the brothers were successful in recovering their sister.", "테세우스는 아티카를 떠나 있었으므로, 두 형제는 누이를 되찾는 데 성공했다."]], [["Castor was famous for taming and managing horses, and Pollux for skill in boxing.", "카스토르는 말을 길들이고 다루는 데 이름났고, 폴리데우케스는 권투 솜씨로 이름났다."], ["They were united by the warmest affection and inseparable in all their enterprises.", "그들은 더없이 따뜻한 애정으로 맺어져 있었으며 모든 일에서 떼어 놓을 수 없는 사이였다."], ["They accompanied the Argonautic expedition.", "그들은 아르고호 원정에 함께했다."], ["During the voyage a storm arose, and Orpheus prayed to the Samothracian gods, and played on his harp, whereupon the storm ceased and stars appeared on the heads of the brothers.", "항해 도중 폭풍이 일자, 오르페우스가 사모트라케의 신들에게 기도하며 하프를 켰는데, 그러자 폭풍이 멎고 두 형제의 머리 위에 별들이 나타났다."], ["From this incident, Castor and Pollux came afterwards to be considered the patron deities of seamen and voyagers, and the lambent flames, which in certain states of the atmosphere play round the sails and masts of vessels, were called by their names.", "이 일로부터 카스토르와 폴리데우케스는 그 후 뱃사람과 항해자의 수호신으로 여겨지게 되었고, 대기가 어떤 상태에 있을 때 배의 돛과 돛대 둘레에 너울거리는 불꽃을 그들의 이름으로 부르게 되었다."]], [["After the Argonautic expedition, we find Castor and Pollux engaged in a war with Idas and Lynceus.", "아르고호 원정 이후, 우리는 카스토르와 폴리데우케스가 이다스 및 린케우스와 전쟁을 벌이는 것을 본다."], ["Castor was slain, and Pollux, inconsolable for the loss of his brother, besought Jupiter to be permitted to give his own life as a ransom for him.", "카스토르는 살해되었고, 형제를 잃고 위로받지 못한 폴리데우케스는 형제의 몸값으로 자신의 목숨을 내놓도록 허락해 달라고 제우스에게 간청했다."], ["Jupiter so far consented as to allow the two brothers to enjoy the boon of life alternately, passing one day under the earth and the next in the heavenly abodes.", "제우스는 두 형제가 번갈아 가며 삶의 은혜를 누리도록, 곧 하루는 땅 밑에서, 다음 하루는 하늘의 거처에서 지내도록 허락하는 데까지 동의했다."], ["According to another form of the story, Jupiter rewarded the attachment of the brothers by placing them among the stars as Gemini the Twins.", "이야기의 또 다른 형태에 따르면, 제우스는 형제의 우애에 보답하여 그들을 별들 사이에 〈쌍둥이자리〉인 게미니로 두었다."]], [["They received divine honors under the name of Dioscuri (sons of Jove).", "그들은 디오스쿠로이(제우스의 아들들)라는 이름으로 신적인 영예를 받았다."], ["They were believed to have appeared occasionally in later times, taking part with one side or the other, in hard-fought fields, and were said on such occasions to be mounted on magnificent white steeds.", "그들은 후대에 이르러 격전이 벌어진 전장에서 이따금 나타나 어느 한쪽 편을 들었다고 믿어졌으며, 그러한 때에는 으리으리한 흰 군마에 올라타 있었다고 전해진다."], ["Thus in the early history of Rome they are said to have assisted the Romans at the battle of Lake Regillus, and after the victory a temple was erected in their honor on the spot where they appeared.", "그리하여 로마 초기 역사에서 그들은 레길루스호 전투에서 로마인들을 도왔다고 하며, 승리를 거둔 뒤 그들이 나타난 바로 그 자리에 그들을 기리는 신전이 세워졌다."]], [["Macaulay, in his “Lays of Ancient Rome,” thus alludes to the legend:", "매콜리는 「고대 로마의 노래」에서 이 전설을 이렇게 암시하고 있다."]], [["“So like they were, no mortal Might one from other know; White as snow their armor was, Their steeds were white as snow.", "\"그들이 어찌나 닮았던지, 어떤 필멸자도 하나를 다른 하나와 구별하지 못하였네. 그들의 갑옷은 눈처럼 희었고, 그들의 군마도 눈처럼 희었네."], ["Never on earthly anvil Did such rare armor gleam, And never did such gallant steeds Drink of an earthly stream.", "지상의 모루에서는 그토록 진귀한 갑옷이 빛난 적 없었고, 그토록 늠름한 군마가 지상의 시내에서 물을 마신 적도 없었네. ."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [". .", "."], [". .", "."], ["“Back comes the chief in triumph Who in the hour of fight Hath seen the great Twin Brethren In harness on his right.", ". \"싸움의 순간에 위대한 쌍둥이 형제가 갑옷을 입고 제 오른편에 선 것을 본 우두머리는 개선하여 돌아오네."], ["Safe comes the ship to haven, Through billows and through gales, If once the great Twin Brethren Sit shining on the sails.”", "위대한 쌍둥이 형제가 한 번 돛 위에 빛나며 앉기만 하면, 배는 물결을 헤치고 풍랑을 뚫고 무사히 항구에 다다르네.\""]], [["————", "————"]], [["BACCHUS was the son of Jupiter and Semele. Juno, to gratify her resentment against Semele, contrived a plan for her destruction. Assuming the form of Beroë, her aged nurse, she insinuated doubts whether it was indeed Jove himself who came as a lover. Heaving a sigh, she said, “I hope it will turn out so, but I can’t help being afraid. People are not always what they pretend to be. If he is indeed Jove, make him give some proof of it. Ask him to come arrayed in all his splendors, such as he wears in heaven. That will put the matter beyond a doubt.” Semele was persuaded to try the experiment. She asks a favor, without naming what it is. Jove gives his promise, and confirms it with the irrevocable oath, attesting the river Styx, terrible to the gods themselves. Then she made known her request. The god would have stopped her as she spake, but she was too quick for him. The words escaped, and he could neither unsay his promise nor her request. In deep distress he left her and returned to the upper regions. There he clothed himself in his splendors, not putting on all his terrors, as when he overthrew the giants, but what is known among the gods as his lesser panoply. Arrayed in this, he entered the chamber of Semele. Her mortal frame could not endure the splendors of the immortal radiance. She was consumed to ashes.", "바쿠스—아리아드네\n바쿠스"]], [["Jove took the infant Bacchus and gave him in charge to the Nysæan nymphs, who nourished his infancy and childhood, and for their care were rewarded by Jupiter by being placed, as the Hyades, among the stars. When Bacchus grew up he discovered the culture of the vine and the mode of extracting its precious juice; but Juno struck him with madness, and drove him forth a wanderer through various parts of the earth. In Phrygia the goddess Rhea cured him and taught him her religious rites, and he set out on a progress through Asia, teaching the people the cultivation of the vine. The most famous part of his wanderings is his expedition to India, which is said to have lasted several years. Returning in triumph, he undertook to introduce his worship into Greece, but was opposed by some princes, who dreaded its introduction on account of the disorders and madness it brought with it.", "바쿠스는 유피테르와 세멜레 사이에서 태어난 아들이었다. 유노는 세멜레를 향한 자신의 원한을 풀고자, 그녀를 파멸시킬 계략을 꾸몄다. 세멜레의 늙은 유모인 베로에의 모습으로 변신한 그녀는, 과연 연인으로 찾아온 이가 정말로 유피테르 본인인지 의심을 불어넣었다. 한숨을 내쉬며 유노는 이렇게 말했다. \"정말 그러기를 바라지만, 나로서는 두려운 마음을 떨칠 수가 없구나. 사람이란 늘 자기가 내세우는 그대로인 건 아니니 말이다. 그분이 정말 유피테르라면, 그 증거를 보여 달라고 하려무나. 하늘에서 두르는 온갖 광휘를 갖춘 모습으로 와 달라고 청하거라. 그러면 그 일은 의심할 여지 없이 분명해질 게다.\" 세멜레는 이 시험을 해 보도록 설득당했다. 그녀는 무엇인지 밝히지 않은 채 한 가지 소원을 청했다. 유피테르는 약속을 해 주었고, 신들 자신도 두려워하는 스틱스 강을 두고, 결코 거둘 수 없는 맹세로 그것을 확약했다. 그러자 세멜레는 자신의 청을 밝혔다. 신은 그녀가 말하는 동안 막으려 했으나, 그녀가 너무 빨랐다. 말은 이미 새어 나왔고, 그는 자신의 약속도 그녀의 청도 거둘 수 없었다. 깊은 고뇌에 잠긴 채 그는 그녀 곁을 떠나 위쪽 영역으로 돌아갔다. 그곳에서 그는 자신의 광휘를 걸쳤으되, 거인들을 쓰러뜨릴 때처럼 온갖 무서운 위용을 다 갖추지는 않고, 신들 사이에서 그의 작은 위엄으로 알려진 것만을 둘렀다. 그렇게 차려입은 채 그는 세멜레의 방으로 들어섰다. 그녀의 필멸의 육신은 불멸의 광채가 뿜는 광휘를 견딜 수 없었다. 그녀는 한 줌 재로 타 버렸다. 유피테르는 갓난아기 바쿠스를 데려다 니사의 님프들에게 맡겨 길렀고, 님프들은 그의 영아기와 유년기를 보살펴 키웠으며, 그 보살핌의 대가로 유피테르는 그들을 히아데스로 삼아 별들 사이에 두어 보답하였다. 바쿠스는 자라나 포도 재배법과 그 귀한 즙을 짜내는 방법을 발견하였다. 그러나 유노가 그를 광기로 후려쳐, 그를 떠돌이로 만들어 땅의 여러 곳을 헤매게 하였다. 프리기아에서 여신 레아가 그를 치유하고 자신의 종교 의식을 가르쳐 주었고, 그는 아시아 전역을 두루 돌며 사람들에게 포도 재배법을 가르치는 여정에 나섰다. 그의 방랑 가운데 가장 유명한 부분은 인도 원정으로, 이 원정은 여러 해에 걸쳐 이어졌다고 전한다. 의기양양하게 돌아온 그는 자신의 숭배를 그리스에 들여오려 하였으나, 그것이 함께 가져오는 무질서와 광기를 두려워한 몇몇 제후들의 반대에 부딪혔다."]], [["As he approached his native city Thebes, Pentheus the king, who had no respect for the new worship, forbade its rites to be performed.", "그가 자신의 고향 도시 테바이에 다가갔을 때, 새로운 숭배에 아무런 경의도 품지 않았던 왕 펜테우스는 그 의식의 거행을 금하였다."], ["But when it was known that Bacchus was advancing, men and women, but chiefly the latter, young and old, poured forth to meet him and to join his triumphal march.", "그러나 바쿠스가 다가오고 있다는 것이 알려지자, 남자도 여자도, 그러나 주로 여자들이, 젊은이도 늙은이도 그를 맞이하고 그의 개선 행렬에 합류하고자 쏟아져 나왔다."]], [["Mr. Longfellow in his “Drinking Song” thus describes the march of Bacchus:", "롱펠로 씨는 그의 「술의 노래」에서 바쿠스의 행렬을 이렇게 묘사한다."]], [["“Fauns with youthful Bacchus follow; Ivy crowns that brow, supernal As the forehead of Apollo, And possessing youth eternal.", "\"파우누스들이 젊은 바쿠스를 뒤따르니, 담쟁이덩굴 관이 그 이마를 둘렀구나, 아폴론의 이마처럼 드높고 거룩하며, 영원한 젊음을 지녔도다."]], [["“Round about him fair Bacchantes, Bearing cymbals, flutes and thyrses, Wild from Naxian groves of Zante’s Vineyards, sing delirious verses.”", "\"그를 둘러싸고 아름다운 박코스의 무녀들이, 심벌즈와 피리와 튀르소스를 들고서, 잔테의 낙소스 숲, 포도밭에서 거칠게 뛰쳐나와, 황홀한 노래를 부르네.\""]], [["It was in vain Pentheus remonstrated, commanded, and threatened.", "펜테우스가 항의하고 명령하고 위협한 것은 모두 헛일이었다."], ["“Go,”", "\"가서,\""], ["said he to his attendants, “seize this vagabond leader of the rout and bring him to me.", "그는 시종들에게 말했다. \"저 떠돌이 무리의 우두머리를 붙잡아 내 앞으로 끌고 오너라."], ["I will soon make him confess his false claim of heavenly parentage and renounce his counterfeit worship.”", "내 곧 그가 하늘의 혈통을 거짓으로 주장한다는 것을 자백하게 하고, 그 가짜 숭배를 버리게 하리라.\""], ["It was in vain his nearest friends and wisest counsellors remonstrated and begged him not to oppose the god.", "그의 가장 가까운 벗들과 가장 지혜로운 조언자들이 항의하며 그 신에게 맞서지 말라고 간청한 것도 헛일이었다."], ["Their remonstrances only made him more violent.", "그들의 항의는 오히려 그를 더욱 격렬하게 만들 뿐이었다."]], [["But now the attendants returned whom he had despatched to seize Bacchus.", "그런데 이제 그가 바쿠스를 붙잡으라고 보냈던 시종들이 돌아왔다."], ["They had been driven away by the Bacchanals, but had succeeded in taking one of them prisoner, whom, with his hands tied behind him, they brought before the king.", "그들은 박코스의 무리에게 쫓겨났으나, 그중 한 사람을 사로잡는 데에는 성공하여, 손을 등 뒤로 묶은 채 왕 앞으로 끌고 왔다."], ["Pentheus, beholding him with wrathful countenance, said, “Fellow!", "펜테우스는 노여운 얼굴로 그를 바라보며 말했다. \"이놈!"], ["you shall speedily be put to death, that your fate may be a warning to others; but though I grudge the delay of your punishment, speak, tell us who you are, and what are these new rites you presume to celebrate.”", "너는 곧 죽임을 당할 것이니, 네 운명이 남들에게 본보기가 되리라. 허나 네 처벌을 미루는 것이 아깝긴 하다만, 말하라, 네가 누구인지, 그리고 네가 감히 거행하려는 이 새로운 의식이 무엇인지 우리에게 일러라.\""]], [["The prisoner, unterrified, responded, “My name is Acetes; my country is Mæonia; my parents were poor people, who had no fields or flocks to leave me, but they left me their fishing rods and nets and their fisherman’s trade.", "붙잡힌 자는 겁먹지 않고 대답했다. \"제 이름은 아케테스이며, 제 고향은 마이오니아입니다."], ["This I followed for some time, till growing weary of remaining in one place, I learned the pilot’s art and how to guide my course by the stars.", "제 부모는 가난한 사람들이라 제게 물려줄 밭도 가축도 없었고, 다만 낚싯대와 그물, 그리고 어부의 생업을 물려주었습니다. 저는 한동안 그 일을 따랐으나, 한곳에 머무는 것이 싫증나면서 키잡이의 기술과 별을 보고 항로를 잡는 법을 익혔습니다."], ["It happened as I was sailing for Delos we touched at the island of Dia and went ashore.", "마침 제가 델로스로 항해하던 중에 우리는 디아 섬에 닿아 뭍에 올랐습니다."], ["Next morning I sent the men for fresh water, and myself mounted the hill to observe the wind; when my men returned bringing with them a prize, as they thought, a boy of delicate appearance, whom they had found asleep.", "이튿날 아침 저는 부하들을 보내 신선한 물을 길어 오게 하고, 저 자신은 바람을 살피러 언덕에 올랐습니다. 그때 부하들이 그들 생각으로는 노획물이라며 한 소년을 데리고 돌아왔는데, 곱상한 외모의 그 소년은 잠든 채로 발견된 것이었습니다."], ["They judged he was a noble youth, perhaps a king’s son, and they might get a liberal ransom for him.", "그들은 그가 고귀한 젊은이, 어쩌면 왕의 아들일 거라 짐작하고, 그를 데려가면 후한 몸값을 받을 수 있으리라 여겼습니다."], ["I observed his dress, his walk, his face.", "저는 그의 옷차림과 걸음걸이와 얼굴을 살폈습니다."], ["There was something in them which I felt sure was more than mortal.", "거기에는 필멸의 인간을 넘어서는 무언가가 분명히 있다고 저는 확신했습니다. 저는 부하들에게 말했습니다."], ["I said to my men, ‘What god there is concealed in that form I know not, but some one there certainly is.", "'저 모습 속에 어떤 신이 숨어 있는지는 모르겠으나, 누군가 분명히 깃들어 있다. 너그러운 신이시여, 우리가 당신께 저지른 폭행을 용서하시고, 우리의 일에 성공을 내려 주소서.'"], ["Pardon us, gentle deity, for the violence we have done you, and give success to our undertakings.’ Dictys, one of my best hands for climbing the mast and coming down by the ropes, and Melanthus, my steersman, and Epopeus, the leader of the sailor’s cry, one and all exclaimed, ‘Spare your prayers for us.’ So blind is the lust of gain!", "돛대를 오르내리고 밧줄을 타고 내려오는 데 가장 솜씨 좋은 디크티스와, 키잡이 멜란투스와, 뱃사람의 구령을 이끄는 에포페우스가, 너 나 할 것 없이 모두 외쳤습니다. '우리를 위한 기도는 그만두시오.'"], ["When they proceeded to put him on board I resisted them.", "이익을 향한 욕심이란 이토록 눈을 멀게 하는 것입니다! 그들이 소년을 배에 태우려 하자 저는 그들에게 맞섰습니다."], ["‘This ship shall not be profaned by such impiety,’ said I.", "'이 배가 그런 불경으로 더럽혀져서는 안 된다,' 제가 말했습니다."], ["‘I have a greater share in her than any of you.’ But Lycabas, a turbulent fellow, seized me by the throat and attempted to throw me overboard, and I scarcely saved myself by clinging to the ropes.", "'이 배에 대한 내 몫이 너희 누구보다도 크다.' 그러나 난폭한 자 리카바스가 제 목을 움켜쥐고 저를 배 밖으로 던지려 했고, 저는 가까스로 밧줄에 매달려 목숨을 건졌습니다."], ["The rest approved the deed.", "나머지 자들은 그 짓을 두둔했습니다."]], [["“Then Bacchus (for it was indeed he), as if shaking off his drowsiness, exclaimed, ‘What are you doing with me? What is this fighting about? Who brought me here? Where are you going to carry me?’ One of them replied, ‘Fear nothing; tell us where you wish to go and we will take you there.’ ‘Naxos is my home,’ said Bacchus; ‘take me there and you shall be well rewarded.’ They promised so to do, and told me to pilot the ship to Naxos. Naxos lay to the right, and I was trimming the sails to carry us there, when some by signs and others by whispers signified to me their will that I should sail in the opposite direction, and take the boy to Egypt to sell him for a slave. I was confounded and said, ‘Let some one else pilot the ship;’ withdrawing myself from any further agency in their wickedness. They cursed me, and one of them, exclaiming, ‘Don’t flatter yourself that we depend on you for our safety,’ took my place as pilot, and bore away from Naxos.", "\"그때 바쿠스가 (정말로 그였습니다) 마치 졸음을 떨쳐 내듯 외쳤습니다. '나에게 무슨 짓을 하는 것이냐? 이 다툼은 무엇 때문이냐? 누가 나를 이리로 데려왔느냐? 너희는 나를 어디로 데려가려는 것이냐?' 그들 중 하나가 대답했습니다. '아무것도 두려워 마라. 어디로 가고 싶은지 말하면 그곳으로 데려다주마.' '낙소스가 내 고향이다,' 바쿠스가 말했습니다. '나를 그곳으로 데려다주면 너희에게 후히 보답하리라.' 그들은 그리하겠다고 약속하고는, 저더러 배를 낙소스로 몰라고 일렀습니다. 낙소스는 오른편에 있었고, 저는 우리를 그리로 데려가도록 돛을 다루고 있었는데, 그때 어떤 자들은 손짓으로, 또 어떤 자들은 속삭임으로, 제가 반대 방향으로 배를 몰아 그 소년을 이집트로 데려가 노예로 팔아 버리기를 바라는 그들의 뜻을 제게 전했습니다. 저는 어찌할 바를 몰라 말했습니다. '누구든 다른 사람이 배를 몰아라.' 그러고는 그들의 악행에 더 이상 가담하지 않으려 물러섰습니다. 그들은 저를 저주하였고, 그중 하나가 '우리가 안전을 너에게 의지한다고 우쭐대지 마라' 하고 외치며 제 자리를 차지하여 키를 잡고는, 낙소스에서 멀어져 갔습니다."]], [["“Then the god, pretending that he had just become aware of their treachery, looked out over the sea and said in a voice of weeping, ‘Sailors, these are not the shores you promised to take me to; yonder island is not my home.", "\"그러자 신은 자신이 그들의 배신을 막 알아챈 척하며, 바다 너머를 내다보고는 울먹이는 목소리로 말했습니다. '뱃사람들이여, 이곳은 너희가 나를 데려다주겠다 약속한 해안이 아니다."], ["What have I done that you should treat me so?", "저기 저 섬은 내 고향이 아니다."], ["It is small glory you will gain by cheating a poor boy.’ I wept to hear him, but the crew laughed at both of us, and sped the vessel fast over the sea.", "너희가 나를 이리 대하다니, 내가 무슨 짓을 했단 말이냐? 가엾은 소년 하나를 속여서 너희가 얻을 영광이란 보잘것없으리라.'"], ["All at once—strange as it may seem, it is true,—the vessel stopped, in the mid sea, as fast as if it was fixed on the ground.", "저는 그 말을 듣고 눈물을 흘렸으나, 선원들은 우리 둘을 비웃으며 배를 바다 위로 빠르게 몰았습니다."], ["The men, astonished, pulled at their oars, and spread more sail, trying to make progress by the aid of both, but all in vain.", "그때 갑자기—이상하게 들릴지 모르나, 이것은 사실입니다—배가 바다 한가운데에서, 마치 땅에 박힌 듯이 단단히 멈춰 섰습니다."], ["Ivy twined round the oars and hindered their motion, and clung to the sails, with heavy clusters of berries.", "사람들은 놀라 노를 젓고 돛을 더 펼쳐, 그 둘의 힘으로 나아가려 애썼으나 모두 헛일이었습니다. 담쟁이덩굴이 노를 휘감아 그 움직임을 막았고, 무거운 열매송이를 단 채 돛에 들러붙었습니다."], ["A vine, laden with grapes, ran up the mast, and along the sides of the vessel.", "포도를 가득 단 덩굴이 돛대를 타고 올라, 배의 양쪽 옆을 따라 뻗어 갔습니다."], ["The sound of flutes was heard and the odor of fragrant wine spread all around.", "피리 소리가 들려왔고, 향기로운 포도주 내음이 사방에 퍼졌습니다."], ["The god himself had a chaplet of vine leaves, and bore in his hand a spear wreathed with ivy.", "신 자신은 포도 잎으로 엮은 화관을 쓰고, 손에는 담쟁이덩굴을 감은 창을 들고 있었습니다."], ["Tigers crouched at his feet, and forms of lynxes and spotted panthers played around him.", "호랑이들이 그의 발치에 웅크렸고, 스라소니와 점박이 표범의 형상이 그를 둘러싸고 노닐었습니다. 사람들은 공포에, 혹은 광기에 사로잡혔습니다."], ["The men were seized with terror or madness; some leaped overboard; others preparing to do the same beheld their companions in the water undergoing a change, their bodies becoming flattened and ending in a crooked tail.", "어떤 자들은 배 밖으로 뛰어내렸고, 또 같은 짓을 하려던 다른 자들은 물속에 있는 동료들이 변해 가는 것을, 몸이 납작해지고 구부러진 꼬리로 끝나는 것을 보았습니다. 한 사람이 외쳤습니다."], ["One exclaimed, ‘What miracle is this!’ and as he spoke his mouth widened, his nostrils expanded, and scales covered all his body.", "'이게 무슨 기적이란 말이냐!' 그리고 그가 말하는 동안 그의 입이 넓어지고, 콧구멍이 벌어지고, 비늘이 온몸을 뒤덮었습니다."], ["Another, endeavoring to pull the oar, felt his hands shrink up and presently to be no longer hands but fins; another, trying to raise his arms to a rope, found he had no arms, and curving his mutilated body, jumped into the sea.", "또 한 사람은 노를 당기려다 손이 오그라들더니 이내 손이 아닌 지느러미가 되는 것을 느꼈습니다. 또 다른 자는 밧줄을 향해 팔을 들어 올리려다 자기에게 팔이 없음을 깨닫고는, 잘린 몸을 구부려 바다로 뛰어들었습니다."], ["What had been his legs became the two ends of a crescent-shaped tail.", "그의 다리였던 것이 초승달 모양 꼬리의 두 끝이 되었습니다."], ["The whole crew became dolphins and swam about the ship, now upon the surface, now under it, scattering the spray, and spouting the water from their broad nostrils.", "선원 전체가 돌고래가 되어 배 주위를 헤엄쳤는데, 어떤 때는 수면 위로, 어떤 때는 그 아래로 오가며 물보라를 흩뿌리고, 넓은 콧구멍으로 물을 내뿜었습니다. 스무 명 가운데 저 혼자만 남았습니다."], ["Of twenty men I alone was left.", "두려움에 떠는 제게 신은 용기를 북돋아 주었습니다."], ["Trembling with fear, the god cheered me.", "'두려워 마라,' 그가 말했습니다."], ["‘Fear not,’ said he; ‘steer towards Naxos.’ I obeyed, and when we arrived there, I kindled the altars and celebrated the sacred rites of Bacchus.”", "'낙소스를 향해 키를 잡아라.' 저는 그 말을 따랐고, 우리가 그곳에 다다랐을 때 저는 제단에 불을 지피고 바쿠스의 신성한 의식을 거행했습니다.\""]], [["Pentheus here exclaimed, “We have wasted time enough on this silly story.", "여기서 펜테우스가 외쳤다. \"우리가 이 어리석은 이야기에 시간을 충분히 허비했다."], ["Take him away and have him executed without delay.”", "저놈을 끌어내어 지체 없이 처형하라.\""], ["Acetes was led away by the attendants and shut up fast in prison; but while they were getting ready the instruments of execution the prison doors came open of their own accord and the chains fell from his limbs, and when they looked for him he was nowhere to be found.", "아케테스는 시종들에게 끌려가 옥에 단단히 갇혔다. 그러나 그들이 처형 도구를 준비하는 동안, 옥문이 저절로 열리고 그의 사지에서 쇠사슬이 떨어져 나갔으며, 그들이 그를 찾았을 때 그는 어디에서도 보이지 않았다."]], [["Pentheus would take no warning, but instead of sending others, determined to go himself to the scene of the solemnities.", "펜테우스는 아무런 경고도 받아들이지 않고, 다른 이들을 보내는 대신 직접 그 제전의 현장으로 가기로 결심했다."], ["The mountain Citheron was all alive with worshippers, and the cries of the Bacchanals resounded on every side.", "키타이론 산은 온통 숭배자들로 들끓었고, 박코스의 무리가 지르는 외침이 사방에서 울려 퍼졌다."], ["The noise roused the anger of Pentheus as the sound of a trumpet does the fire of a war-horse.", "그 소음은, 나팔 소리가 군마의 투지를 돋우듯, 펜테우스의 분노를 돋우었다."], ["He penetrated through the wood and reached an open space where the chief scene of the orgies met his eyes.", "그는 숲을 헤치고 들어가 탁 트인 공터에 이르렀고, 그곳에서 광란의 제전의 주된 광경이 그의 눈에 들어왔다. 바로 그 순간 여자들이 그를 보았다."], ["At the same moment the women saw him; and first among them his own mother, Agave, blinded by the god, cried out, “See there the wild boar, the hugest monster that prowls in these woods!", "그리고 그들 가운데 가장 먼저, 신에 의해 눈이 먼 그의 친어머니 아가베가 소리쳤다. \"저기 멧돼지를 보아라, 이 숲을 어슬렁대는 가장 거대한 괴물이로다!"], ["Come on, sisters!", "자, 가자, 자매들이여!"], ["I will be the first to strike the wild boar.”", "내가 가장 먼저 저 멧돼지를 치리라.\""], ["The whole band rushed upon him, and while he now talks less arrogantly, now excuses himself, and now confesses his crime and implores pardon, they press upon him and wound him.", "무리 전체가 그에게 달려들었고, 그가 이제는 덜 오만하게 말하고, 이제는 변명하며, 이제는 자신의 죄를 자백하고 용서를 빌었으나, 그들은 그를 짓누르며 상처를 입혔다."], ["In vain he cries to his aunts to protect him from his mother.", "어머니로부터 자신을 지켜 달라고 이모들에게 외쳤으나 헛일이었다."], ["Autonoë seized one arm, Ino the other, and between them he was torn to pieces, while his mother shouted, “Victory!", "아우토노에가 한쪽 팔을, 이노가 다른 쪽 팔을 붙잡았고, 그 사이에서 그는 갈가리 찢겼으며, 그의 어머니는 외쳤다. \"승리다!"], ["Victory!", "승리다!"], ["we have done it; the glory is ours!”", "우리가 해냈다, 영광은 우리의 것이다!\""]], [["So the worship of Bacchus was established in Greece.", "이렇게 하여 바쿠스의 숭배가 그리스에 자리 잡았다."]], [["There is an allusion to the story of Bacchus and the mariners in Milton’s “Comus,” at line 46.", "바쿠스와 뱃사람들의 이야기에 대한 암시는 밀턴의 「코머스」"], ["The story of Circe will be found in", "46행에서 찾아볼 수 있다. 키르케의 이야기는"]], [["# Chapter XXIX.", "# Chapter XXIX."]], [["“Bacchus that first from out the purple grapes Crushed the sweet poison of misused wine, After the Tuscan mariners transformed, Coasting the Tyrrhene shore as the winds listed On Circe’s island fell (who knows not Circe, The daughter of the Sun?", "맨 처음 자줏빛 포도알에서 그릇되이 쓰인 술의 달콤한 독을 짜낸 디오니소스는, 토스카나의 뱃사람들을 변모시킨 뒤, 바람이 이끄는 대로 티레네 해안을 따라 항해하다 키르케의 섬에 이르렀으니 (태양의 딸 키르케를 그 누가 모르랴?"], ["whose charmed cup Whoever tasted lost his upright shape, And downward fell into a grovelling swine).”", "그녀의 마법의 잔을 맛본 자는 누구든 곧은 형상을 잃고 땅을 기는 돼지로 굴러떨어졌노라)."]], [["We have seen in the story of Theseus how Ariadne, the daughter of King Minos, after helping Theseus to escape from the labyrinth, was carried by him to the island of Naxos and was left there asleep, while the ungrateful Theseus pursued his way home without her.", "테세우스 이야기에서 우리는, 미노스 왕의 딸 아리아드네가 테세우스를 도와 미궁에서 빠져나오게 한 뒤, 그에게 이끌려 낙소스 섬으로 갔다가 잠든 채 그곳에 버려졌으며, 은혜를 모르는 테세우스는 그녀를 남겨 둔 채 홀로 귀향길을 재촉했음을 이미 보았다."], ["Ariadne, on waking and finding herself deserted, abandoned herself to grief.", "잠에서 깨어나 홀로 버려진 것을 안 아리아드네는 슬픔에 몸을 내맡겼다."], ["But Venus took pity on her, and consoled her with the promise that she should have an immortal lover, instead of the mortal one she had lost.", "그러나 아프로디테[로마의 〈베누스〉]가 그녀를 가엾이 여겨, 잃어버린 필멸의 연인 대신 불멸의 연인을 얻으리라는 약속으로 그녀를 위로해 주었다."]], [["The island where Ariadne was left was the favorite island of Bacchus, the same that he wished the Tyrrhenian mariners to carry him to, when they so treacherously attempted to make prize of him.", "아리아드네가 버려진 섬은 디오니소스가 가장 아끼던 섬으로, 티레네의 뱃사람들이 음험하게 그를 사로잡으려 했을 때 그가 데려다 달라고 했던 바로 그 섬이었다."], ["As Ariadne sat lamenting her fate, Bacchus found her, consoled her, and made her his wife.", "아리아드네가 자신의 운명을 한탄하며 앉아 있을 때, 디오니소스가 그녀를 발견하여 위로하고는 자신의 아내로 삼았다."], ["As a marriage present he gave her a golden crown, enriched with gems, and when she died, he took her crown and threw it up into the sky.", "결혼 선물로 그는 보석으로 장식된 황금 왕관을 주었으며, 그녀가 죽자 그 왕관을 거두어 하늘로 던져 올렸다."], ["As it mounted the gems grew brighter and were turned into stars, and preserving its form Ariadne’s crown remains fixed in the heavens as a constellation, between the kneeling Hercules and the man who holds the serpent.", "왕관이 솟아오르는 동안 보석들은 더욱 밝게 빛나 별로 변하였고, 그 형상을 고스란히 간직한 아리아드네의 왕관은 무릎 꿇은 헤라클레스와 뱀을 쥔 사내 사이에서 별자리가 되어 하늘에 붙박이게 되었다."]], [["Spenser alludes to Ariadne’s crown, though he has made some mistakes in his mythology.", "스펜서는 아리아드네의 왕관을 언급하지만, 그의 신화 서술에는 몇 가지 오류가 있다."], ["It was at the wedding of Pirithous, and not Theseus, that the Centaurs and Lapithæ quarrelled.", "켄타우로스족과 라피타이족이 다툰 것은 테세우스의 결혼식이 아니라 페이리토오스의 결혼식에서였다."]], [["“Look how the crown which Ariadne wore Upon her ivory forehead that same day That Theseus her unto his bridal bore, Then the bold Centaurs made that bloody fray With the fierce Lapiths which did them dismay; Being now placed in the firmament, Through the bright heaven doth her beams display, And is unto the stars an ornament, Which round about her move in order excellent.” ————", "보라, 아리아드네가 쓴 왕관을, 테세우스가 그녀를 신부로 맞이하던 바로 그날 그녀의 상아빛 이마 위에 얹혔던 그 왕관을, 그때 담대한 켄타우로스족은 사나운 라피타이족과 피비린내 나는 싸움을 벌여 그들을 떨게 하였으나, 이제 창공에 자리 잡아 밝은 하늘을 가로질러 그 빛살을 펼치며, 뭇 별들에게 하나의 장식이 되었으니, 별들은 그 둘레를 더없이 정연하게 돌고 있도다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXII", "# CHAPTER XXII"]], [["THE RURAL DEITIES—ERISICHTHON—RHŒCUS—THE WATER DEITIES—CAMENÆ—WINDS\nPAN, the god of woods and fields, of flocks and shepherds, dwelt in grottos, wandered on the mountains and in valleys, and amused himself with the chase or in leading the dances of the nymphs.", "판은 숲과 들판, 가축 떼와 목자들의 신으로, 동굴에서 거처하다가 산과 골짜기를 떠돌고, 사냥을 하거나 님프들의 춤을 이끌며 즐겼다."], ["He was fond of music, and as we have seen, the inventor of the syrinx, or shepherd’s pipe, which he himself played in a masterly manner.", "그는 음악을 좋아했고, 앞서 보았듯이 시링크스, 곧 목동의 피리를 발명한 자였으며, 그 자신이 그것을 능숙하게 연주했다."], ["Pan, like other gods who dwelt in forests, was dreaded by those whose occupations caused them to pass through the woods by night, for the gloom and loneliness of such scenes dispose the mind to superstitious fears.", "판은 숲에 거처하던 다른 신들과 마찬가지로, 밤에 숲을 지나가야 하는 일에 종사하는 자들에게 두려움의 대상이었다. 그런 풍경의 음울함과 적막함이 마음을 미신적인 공포로 기울게 만들기 때문이다."], ["Hence sudden fright without any visible cause was ascribed to Pan, and called a Panic terror.", "그리하여 눈에 보이는 원인 없이 갑작스레 찾아드는 놀람은 판의 탓으로 돌려졌고, 〈패닉의 공포〉라 불렸다."]], [["As the name of the god signifies all, Pan came to be considered a symbol of the universe and personification of Nature; and later still to be regarded as a representative of all the gods and of heathenism itself.", "판이라는 신의 이름이 〈전부〉를 뜻하므로, 판은 우주의 상징이자 자연의 화신으로 여겨지게 되었고, 더 나중에는 모든 신들과 이교 그 자체를 대표하는 자로 간주되기에 이르렀다."]], [["Sylvanus and Faunus were Latin divinities, whose characteristics are so nearly the same as those of Pan that we may safely consider them as the same personage under different names.", "실바누스와 파우누스는 라틴의 신들로, 그 특성이 판의 그것과 거의 같으므로, 우리는 그들을 다른 이름으로 불리는 동일한 존재로 보아도 무방하다."]], [["The wood-nymphs, Pan’s partners in the dance, were but one class of nymphs.", "판의 춤 상대였던 숲의 님프들은 님프 가운데 한 부류에 불과했다."], ["There were beside them the Naiads, who presided over brooks and fountains, the Oreads, nymphs of mountains and grottos, and the Nereids, sea-nymphs.", "그 밖에도 시내와 샘을 관장하는 나이아데스, 산과 동굴의 님프인 오레아데스, 바다의 님프인 네레이데스가 있었다."], ["The three last named were immortal, but the wood-nymphs, called Dryads or Hamadryads, were believed to perish with the trees which had been their abode and with which they had come into existence.", "마지막에 든 세 부류는 불멸이었으나, 드리아데스 혹은 하마드리아데스라 불린 숲의 님프들은 그들의 거처였으며 그들과 함께 생겨난 나무가 죽으면 함께 스러지는 것으로 믿어졌다."], ["It was therefore an impious act wantonly to destroy a tree, and in some aggravated cases were severely punished, as in the instance of Erisichthon, which we are about to record.", "그러므로 함부로 나무를 베어 없애는 것은 불경한 짓이었고, 어떤 가중된 경우에는 가혹하게 벌해졌으니, 이제 우리가 기록하려는 에리시크톤의 사례가 바로 그러하다."]], [["Milton in his glowing description of the early creation, thus alludes to Pan as the personification of Nature:", "밀턴은 태초의 창조를 묘사한 그 찬란한 대목에서, 자연의 화신으로서의 판을 이렇게 언급한다."]], [["“. . . Universal Pan, Knit with the Graces and the Hours in dance, Led on the eternal spring.”", "\"……만유의 판이, 카리테스, 〈사계절〉 여신들과 손잡고 춤추며, 영원한 봄을 이끌었도다.\""]], [["And describing Eve’s abode:", "또한 이브의 거처를 묘사하며 이렇게 노래한다."]], [["“. . . In shadier bower, More sacred or sequestered, though but feigned, Pan or Sylvanus never slept, nor nymph Nor Faunus haunted.” —Paradise Lost, B. IV.", "\"……더 그늘진 정자, 더 신성하고 더 외진 그곳을, 비록 꾸며 낸 것일망정, 판도 실바누스도 결코 잠든 적 없고, 님프도 파우누스도 깃든 적 없었도다.\" ─『실낙원』, 제4권."]], [["It was a pleasing trait in the old Paganism that it loved to trace in every operation of nature the agency of deity.", "옛 이교 신앙의 한 가지 흐뭇한 특징은, 자연의 모든 작용 속에서 신의 손길을 더듬어 찾기를 즐겼다는 점이다."], ["The imagination of the Greeks peopled all the regions of earth and sea with divinities, to whose agency it attributed those phenomena which our philosophy ascribes to the operation of the laws of nature.", "그리스인들의 상상력은 땅과 바다의 온갖 영역을 신들로 가득 채웠고, 우리의 철학이 자연 법칙의 작용으로 돌리는 저 현상들을 그 신들의 손길에 돌렸다."], ["Sometimes in our poetical moods we feel disposed to regret the change, and to think that the heart has lost as much as the head has gained by the substitution.", "때때로 우리는 시적인 기분에 잠길 때, 그 변화를 아쉬워하고, 그러한 대체로써 머리가 얻은 만큼 가슴이 잃었다고 여기는 마음이 들기도 한다."], ["The poet Wordsworth thus strongly expresses this sentiment:", "시인 워즈워스는 이러한 정서를 이렇게 힘차게 표현한다."]], [["“. .", "\"……위대한 신이여, 나는 차라리 낡아 빠진 신조에 길러진 이교도가 되고 싶나이다."], [". Great God, I’d rather be A Pagan, suckled in a creed outworn, So might I, standing on this pleasant lea, Have glimpses that would make me less forlorn; Have sight of Proteus rising from the sea, And hear old Triton blow his wreathed horn.”", "그리하면 이 즐거운 풀밭에 서서, 나를 덜 외롭게 해 줄 광경들을 얼핏 볼 수 있으리니, 바다에서 솟아오르는 프로테우스를 보고, 늙은 트리톤이 휘감긴 뿔나팔 부는 소리를 들으리라.\""]], [["Schiller, in his poem “Die Götter Griechenlands,” expresses his regret for the overthrow of the beautiful mythology of ancient times in a way which has called forth an answer from a Christian poet, Mrs.", "실러는 그의 시 「그리스의 신들」에서 옛 시대의 아름다운 신화가 무너진 것을 아쉬워하는 마음을 표현했고, 이는 기독교 시인 E."], ["E. Barrett Browning, in her poem called “The Dead Pan.”", "바렛 브라우닝 여사의 응답을 불러일으켰으니, 「죽은 판」이라는 그녀의 시가 그것이다."], ["The two following verses are a specimen:", "다음 두 연이 그 한 본보기이다."]], [["“By your beauty which confesses Some chief Beauty conquering you, By our grand heroic guesses Through your falsehood at the True, We will weep not!", "\"그대들을 정복하는 어떤 으뜸가는 〈아름다움〉을 고백하는 그대들의 아름다움에 의하여, 그대들의 거짓을 꿰뚫어 〈참됨〉에 이르는 우리의 웅대하고 영웅적인 짐작들에 의하여, 우리는 울지 않으리라!"], ["earth shall roll Heir to each god’s aureole, And Pan is dead.", "대지는 굴러 저마다의 신의 후광을 물려받을 것이며, 판은 죽었도다."], ["“Earth outgrows the mythic fancies Sung beside her in her youth; And those debonaire romances Sound but dull beside the truth.", "대지는 그 젊음의 시절 곁에서 불려진 신화적 환상들을 자라나 넘어서노라. 그리고 저 정중한 옛이야기들은 참됨 곁에서는 한갓 따분하게 들릴 뿐이라."], ["Phœbus’ chariot course is run!", "포이보스의 전차는 그 길을 다하였도다!"], ["Look up, poets, to the sun!", "시인들이여, 태양을 우러러보라!"], ["Pan, Pan is dead.”", "판은, 판은 죽었도다.\""]], [["These lines are founded on an early Christian tradition that when the heavenly host told the shepherds at Bethlehem of the birth of Christ, a deep groan, heard through all the isles of Greece, told that the great Pan was dead, and that all the royalty of Olympus was dethroned and the several deities were sent wandering in cold and darkness.", "이 시구들은 어느 초기 기독교 전승에 바탕을 둔 것이다. 곧 천상의 무리가 베들레헴의 목자들에게 그리스도의 탄생을 알렸을 때, 그리스의 온 섬들을 두루 울리는 깊은 신음 소리가 위대한 판이 죽었음을, 그리고 올림포스의 모든 왕권이 폐위되어 여러 신들이 추위와 어둠 속을 떠돌도록 내쫓겼음을 알렸다는 것이다."], ["So Milton in his “Hymn on the Nativity”:", "밀턴 또한 그의 「예수 강탄 찬가」에서 이렇게 노래한다."]], [["“The lonely mountains o’er, And the resounding shore, A voice of weeping heard and loud lament; From haunted spring and dale, Edged with poplar pale, The parting Genius is with sighing sent; With flower-enwoven tresses torn, The nymphs in twilight shade of tangled thickets mourn.”", "\"외로운 산들 너머로, 그리고 메아리치는 해변에는, 울음소리와 드높은 비탄이 들리었도다. 창백한 포플러로 가장자리 두른 신령 깃든 샘과 골짜기로부터, 떠나가는 정령이 한숨과 함께 보내어지고, 꽃 엮은 머리채를 쥐어뜯으며, 님프들이 뒤엉킨 덤불의 어스름 그늘에서 애곡하도다.\" 에리시크톤"]], [["Erisichthon was a profane person and a despiser of the gods.", "에리시크톤은 불경한 자이자 신들을 업신여기는 자였다."], ["On one occasion he presumed to violate with the axe a grove sacred to Ceres.", "한번은 그가 감히 데메테르[로마의 〈케레스〉]에게 바쳐진 숲을 도끼로 더럽히려 들었다."], ["There stood in this grove a venerable oak so large that it seemed a wood in itself, its ancient trunk towering aloft, whereon votive garlands were often hung and inscriptions carved expressing the gratitude of suppliants to the nymph of the tree.", "이 숲에는 그 자체로 하나의 숲처럼 보일 만큼 거대한 유서 깊은 떡갈나무 한 그루가 서 있었으니, 그 오래된 둥치가 높이 솟아 있어, 그 위에는 봉헌의 화환이 자주 내걸리고 나무의 님프에게 바치는 탄원자들의 감사를 새긴 비문이 새겨지곤 했다."], ["Often had the Dryads danced round it hand in hand.", "드리아데스도 손에 손을 잡고 그 둘레에서 자주 춤을 추었다."], ["Its trunk measured fifteen cubits round, and it overtopped the other trees as they overtopped the shrubbery.", "그 둥치의 둘레는 열다섯 큐빗에 이르렀고, 다른 나무들이 덤불 위로 솟은 만큼이나 그 나무는 다른 나무들 위로 우뚝 솟아 있었다."], ["But for all that, Erisichthon saw no reason why he should spare it and he ordered his servants to cut it down.", "그러나 그 모든 것에도 불구하고 에리시크톤은 그것을 아껴야 할 까닭을 찾지 못하고, 하인들에게 그것을 베어 넘기라 명령했다."], ["When he saw them hesitate he snatched an axe from one, and thus impiously exclaimed: “I care not whether it be a tree beloved of the goddess or not; were it the goddess herself it should come down if it stood in my way.”", "그들이 머뭇거리는 것을 보자 그는 한 사람에게서 도끼를 낚아채더니, 이렇게 불경하게 외쳤다. \"이것이 여신의 사랑을 받는 나무이든 아니든 나는 개의치 않는다."], ["So saying, he lifted the axe and the oak seemed to shudder and utter a groan.", "설령 이것이 여신 자신이라 해도, 내 앞길을 막아선다면 쓰러져야 하리라.\""], ["When the first blow fell upon the trunk blood flowed from the wound.", "그렇게 말하며 그는 도끼를 치켜들었고, 떡갈나무는 부르르 떨며 신음을 토하는 듯했다."], ["All the bystanders were horror-struck, and one of them ventured to remonstrate and hold back the fatal axe.", "둥치에 첫 도끼질이 떨어지자 그 상처에서 피가 흘러나왔다. 곁에 서 있던 모든 이들이 공포에 질렸고, 그 가운데 한 사람이 감히 나서서 그를 말리며 그 치명적인 도끼를 붙들었다."], ["Erisichthon, with a scornful look, said to him, “Receive the reward of your piety;”", "에리시크톤은 경멸하는 눈빛으로 그에게 말했다. \"네 경건함의 보답을 받아라.\""], ["and turned against him the weapon which he had held aside from the tree, gashed his body with many wounds, and cut off his head.", "그러고는 나무에게서 거두었던 그 무기를 그자에게 돌려, 그의 몸을 여러 군데 베어 상처를 내고는 목을 베어 버렸다."], ["Then from the midst of the oak came a voice, “I who dwell in this tree am a nymph beloved of Ceres, and dying by your hands forewarn you that punishment awaits you.”", "그러자 떡갈나무 한가운데서 목소리가 흘러나왔다. \"이 나무에 거처하는 나는 데메테르의 사랑을 받는 님프이니, 그대의 손에 죽어 가면서 그대에게 미리 이르노라, 응징이 그대를 기다리고 있느니라.\""], ["He desisted not from his crime, and at last the tree, sundered by repeated blows and drawn by ropes, fell with a crash and prostrated a great part of the grove in its fall.", "그러나 그는 자신의 죄악을 멈추지 않았고, 마침내 그 나무는 거듭된 도끼질에 쪼개지고 밧줄에 끌려, 요란한 소리와 함께 쓰러지며 넘어지는 결에 숲의 큰 부분을 깔아뭉갰다."]], [["The Dryads in dismay at the loss of their companion and at seeing the pride of the forest laid low, went in a body to Ceres, all clad in garments of mourning, and invoked punishment upon Erisichthon.", "드리아데스는 동무를 잃은 것과 숲의 자랑이 쓰러진 것에 망연자실하여, 모두 상복을 입고 떼 지어 데메테르에게 가서 에리시크톤에게 응징을 내려 달라고 빌었다."], ["She nodded her assent, and as she bowed her head the grain ripe for harvest in the laden fields bowed also.", "여신은 고개를 끄덕여 응낙했고, 그녀가 머리를 숙이자 곡식이 가득한 들판에서 추수를 앞두고 익은 낟알들도 함께 고개를 숙였다."], ["She planned a punishment so dire that one would pity him, if such a culprit as he could be pitied,—to deliver him over to Famine.", "여신은 너무도 끔찍한 응징을 꾸몄으니, 만일 그자 같은 죄인을 가엾이 여길 수 있다면 누구라도 그를 가엾이 여길 만한 것이었다. 곧 그를 〈굶주림〉에게 넘겨주는 것이었다."], ["As Ceres herself could not approach Famine, for the Fates have ordained that these two goddesses shall never come together, she called an Oread from her mountain and spoke to her in these words: “There is a place in the farthest part of ice-clad Scythia, a sad and sterile region without trees and without crops.", "데메테르 자신은 〈굶주림〉에게 다가갈 수 없었으니, 운명의 여신들이 이 두 여신은 결코 함께 만나지 못하도록 정해 두었기 때문이라, 여신은 산에서 오레아스 하나를 불러 이렇게 일렀다. \"얼음으로 뒤덮인 스키티아의 가장 먼 곳에, 나무도 없고 곡식도 없는 슬프고 메마른 지역이 있느니라."], ["Cold dwells there, and Fear and Shuddering, and Famine.", "거기에는 〈추위〉와 〈공포〉와 〈전율〉과 〈굶주림〉이 거처하느니라."], ["Go and tell the last to take possession of the bowels of Erisichthon.", "가서 마지막의 그것에게 에리시크톤의 뱃속을 차지하라고 일러라."], ["Let not abundance subdue her, nor the power of my gifts drive her away.", "풍요로움도 그것을 누그러뜨리지 못하게 하고, 내 선물의 힘도 그것을 몰아내지 못하게 하라."], ["Be not alarmed at the distance” (for Famine dwells very far from Ceres), “but take my chariot.", "그 거리에 놀라지 말고(〈굶주림〉은 데메테르에게서 아주 멀리 거처하기 때문이다), 내 전차를 타고 가라."], ["The dragons are fleet and obey the rein, and will take you through the air in a short time.”", "그 용들은 날래고 고삐에 순종하니, 너를 짧은 시간에 공중으로 데려다 줄 것이니라.\""], ["So she gave her the reins, and she drove away and soon reached Scythia.", "그렇게 여신은 그녀에게 고삐를 건넸고, 그녀는 전차를 몰아 떠나 이내 스키티아에 다다랐다."], ["On arriving at Mount Caucasus she stopped the dragons and found Famine in a stony field, pulling up with teeth and claws the scanty herbage.", "카우카소스 산에 이르자 그녀는 용들을 멈춰 세우고는, 돌투성이 들판에서 이빨과 발톱으로 메마른 풀포기를 뜯어내고 있는 〈굶주림〉을 발견했다."], ["Her hair was rough, her eyes sunk, her face pale, her lips blanched, her jaws covered with dust, and her skin drawn tight, so as to show all her bones.", "그녀의 머리카락은 거칠었고, 두 눈은 움푹 꺼졌으며, 얼굴은 창백하고, 입술은 핏기가 없었으며, 턱은 먼지로 덮여 있었고, 살갗은 팽팽하게 당겨져 온갖 뼈마디가 다 드러나 보였다."], ["As the Oread saw her afar off (for she did not dare to come near), she delivered the commands of Ceres; and, though she stopped as short a time as possible, and kept her distance as well as she could, yet she began to feel hungry, and turned the dragons’ heads and drove back to Thessaly.", "오레아스는 멀리서 그녀를 바라보며(감히 가까이 다가가지는 못했기에) 데메테르의 명령을 전했고, 비록 되도록 짧게 머물고 되도록 거리를 유지했음에도, 그녀는 배가 고파지기 시작하여, 용들의 머리를 돌려 테살리아로 몰고 돌아갔다."]], [["Famine obeyed the commands of Ceres and sped through the air to the dwelling of Erisichthon, entered the bedchamber of the guilty man, and found him asleep.", "〈굶주림〉은 데메테르의 명령에 따라 공중을 가로질러 에리시크톤의 거처로 달려가, 그 죄인의 침실로 들어가서, 그가 잠들어 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["She enfolded him with her wings and breathed herself into him, infusing her poison into his veins.", "그녀는 날개로 그를 감싸 안고 자신을 그의 몸 안으로 불어넣어, 그의 핏줄 속에 독을 주입했다."], ["Having discharged her task, she hastened to leave the land of plenty and returned to her accustomed haunts.", "임무를 마치자 그녀는 풍요의 땅을 서둘러 떠나 자신이 익숙한 처소로 돌아갔다."], ["Erisichthon still slept, and in his dreams craved food, and moved his jaws as if eating.", "에리시크톤은 여전히 잠들어 있었고, 꿈속에서 음식을 갈망하며, 먹기라도 하듯 턱을 움직였다."], ["When he awoke, his hunger was raging.", "잠에서 깨어났을 때 그의 허기는 사납게 날뛰었다."], ["Without a moment’s delay he would have food set before him, of whatever kind earth, sea, or air produces; and complained of hunger even while he ate.", "그는 한순간도 지체하지 않고 땅과 바다와 하늘이 내는 온갖 종류의 음식을 자기 앞에 차리게 했고, 먹는 와중에도 배고프다고 불평했다."], ["What would have sufficed for a city or a nation, was not enough for him.", "한 도시나 한 나라를 먹이기에 족할 만한 것도 그에게는 충분하지 않았다."], ["The more he ate the more he craved.", "먹으면 먹을수록 그는 더 갈망했다."], ["His hunger was like the sea, which receives all the rivers, yet is never filled; or like fire, that burns all the fuel that is heaped upon it, yet is still voracious for more.", "그의 허기는 모든 강물을 받아들이고도 결코 차오르지 않는 바다와 같았고, 혹은 위에 쌓인 모든 땔감을 태우고도 여전히 더 게걸스레 탐하는 불길과 같았다."]], [["His property rapidly diminished under the unceasing demands of his appetite, but his hunger continued unabated.", "그의 재산은 그칠 줄 모르는 식욕의 요구 아래 빠르게 줄어들었으나, 그의 허기는 조금도 수그러들지 않았다."], ["At length he had spent all and had only his daughter left, a daughter worthy of a better parent.", "마침내 그는 모든 것을 다 써 버리고 딸 하나만 남게 되었으니, 더 나은 부모에게 어울릴 만한 딸이었다."], ["Her too he sold.", "그는 그 딸마저 팔아 버렸다."], ["She scorned to be the slave of a purchaser and as she stood by the seaside raised her hands in prayer to Neptune.", "딸은 사들인 자의 노예가 되는 것을 떳떳치 못하게 여겨, 바닷가에 서서 포세이돈[로마의 〈넵투누스〉]에게 두 손 들어 기도했다."], ["He heard her prayer, and though her new master was not far off and had his eye upon her a moment before, Neptune changed her form and made her assume that of a fisherman busy at his occupation.", "그가 그녀의 기도를 들었고, 새 주인이 멀지 않은 곳에서 조금 전까지 그녀를 눈여겨보고 있었음에도, 포세이돈은 그녀의 모습을 바꾸어 자기 일에 분주한 어부의 모습을 띠게 했다."], ["Her master, looking for her and seeing her in her altered form, addressed her and said, “Good fisherman, whither went the maiden whom I saw just now, with hair dishevelled and in humble garb, standing about where you stand?", "주인은 그녀를 찾다가 바뀐 모습의 그녀를 보고는, 그녀에게 말을 걸어 이렇게 물었다. \"착한 어부여, 머리를 풀어 헤치고 초라한 차림으로, 바로 그대가 선 그 자리쯤에 서 있던 처녀를 내가 방금 보았는데, 그 처녀가 어디로 갔소?"], ["Tell me truly; so may your luck be good and not a fish nibble at your hook and get away.”", "사실대로 말해 주오. 그리하면 그대에게 운수가 좋아 물고기 한 마리도 미끼만 물고 달아나는 일이 없으리다.\""], ["She perceived that her prayer was answered and rejoiced inwardly at hearing herself inquired of about herself.", "그녀는 자신의 기도가 응답받았음을 알아차리고, 제 자신에 관해 질문받는 것을 들으며 속으로 기뻐했다. 그녀는 이렇게 대답했다."], ["She replied, “Pardon me, stranger, but I have been so intent upon my line that I have seen nothing else; but I wish I may never catch another fish if I believe any woman or other person except myself to have been hereabouts for some time.”", "\"용서하시오, 나그네여, 나는 낚싯줄에 너무 골몰해 있어 다른 것은 아무것도 보지 못했소. 그러나 한동안 이 근처에 나 말고 다른 어떤 여인이나 다른 사람이 있었다고 믿느니, 차라리 다시는 물고기 한 마리도 못 잡았으면 하오.\""], ["He was deceived and went his way, thinking his slave had escaped.", "그는 속아 넘어가, 자신의 노예가 달아났다고 여기며 제 갈 길을 갔다."], ["Then she resumed her own form.", "그러자 그녀는 본디 제 모습으로 돌아왔다."], ["Her father was well pleased to find her still with him, and the money too that he got by the sale of her; so he sold her again.", "그녀의 아비는 딸이 여전히 자기 곁에 남아 있는 것과 딸을 팔아 얻은 돈마저 함께 손에 든 것을 매우 흡족해하며, 다시 그녀를 팔았다."], ["But she was changed by the favor of Neptune as often as she was sold, now into a horse, now a bird, now an ox, and now a stag,—got away from her purchasers and came home.", "그러나 그녀는 팔릴 때마다 포세이돈의 은총으로 모습이 바뀌어, 어떤 때는 말로, 어떤 때는 새로, 어떤 때는 황소로, 또 어떤 때는 수사슴으로 변해, 사들인 자들에게서 빠져나와 집으로 돌아왔다."], ["By this base method the starving father procured food; but not enough for his wants, and at last hunger compelled him to devour his limbs, and he strove to nourish his body by eating his body, till death relieved him from the vengeance of Ceres.", "굶주린 아비는 이 비열한 방법으로 음식을 마련했으나, 그것으로도 그의 욕구를 채우기에는 충분치 않았고, 마침내 허기에 떠밀려 자신의 사지를 뜯어 먹게 되었으니, 제 몸을 먹어 제 몸을 기르려 애쓰다가, 죽음이 그를 데메테르의 응징에서 풀어 줄 때까지 그러하였다."]], [["The Hamadryads could appreciate services as well as punish injuries.", "로이코스\n하마드리아데스는 입은 해를 응징할 줄 알았던 만큼이나 받은 도움도 알아볼 줄 알았다."], ["The story of Rhœcus proves this.", "로이코스의 이야기가 이를 입증한다."], ["Rhœcus, happening to see an oak just ready to fall, ordered his servants to prop it up.", "로이코스는 마침 막 쓰러지려는 떡갈나무 한 그루를 보고는, 하인들에게 그것을 떠받쳐 놓으라고 명령했다."], ["The nymph, who had been on the point of perishing with the tree, came and expressed her gratitude to him for having saved her life and bade him ask what reward he would.", "그 나무와 함께 막 스러질 참이었던 님프가 찾아와, 자기 목숨을 구해 준 그에게 고마움을 표하며 어떤 보답이든 청해 보라고 했다."], ["Rhœcus boldly asked her love and the nymph yielded to his desire.", "로이코스는 대담하게 그녀의 사랑을 청했고, 님프는 그의 바람을 들어주었다."], ["She at the same time charged him to be constant and told him that a bee should be her messenger and let him know when she would admit his society.", "그와 동시에 그녀는 그에게 변함없이 한결같으라고 당부하며, 벌 한 마리가 자신의 전령이 되어, 그녀가 그와 함께할 자리를 허락할 때를 알려 주리라고 일렀다."], ["One time the bee came to Rhœcus when he was playing at draughts and he carelessly brushed it away.", "한번은 로이코스가 장기를 두고 있을 때 벌이 찾아왔는데, 그는 무심히 그것을 손짓으로 쫓아 버렸다."], ["This so incensed the nymph that she deprived him of sight.", "이에 님프가 어찌나 분노했던지 그에게서 시력을 앗아 가 버렸다."]], [["Our countryman, J.", "우리 동향인 J. R."], ["R. Lowell, has taken this story for the subject of one of his shorter poems.", "로웰은 이 이야기를 자신의 비교적 짧은 시 가운데 하나의 소재로 삼았다."], ["He introduces it thus:", "그는 이렇게 그 시를 시작한다."]], [["“Hear now this fairy legend of old Greece, As full of freedom, youth and beauty still, As the immortal freshness of that grace Carved for all ages on some Attic frieze.”", "\"이제 옛 그리스의 이 요정 전설을 들어 보라, 어느 아티카의 프리즈에 만대를 위해 새겨진 저 우아함의 불멸의 싱그러움처럼, 여전히 자유와 젊음과 아름다움으로 가득한 그 이야기를.\" 물의 신들"]], [["# THE WATER DEITIES", "# THE WATER DEITIES"]], [["Oceanus and Tethys were the Titans who ruled over the watery element.", "오케아노스와 테티스는 물의 원소를 다스린 티탄들이었다."], ["When Jove and his brothers overthrew the Titans and assumed their power, Neptune and Amphitrite succeeded to the dominion of the waters in place of Oceanus and Tethys.", "제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]와 그 형제들이 티탄들을 무너뜨리고 그 권력을 차지했을 때, 포세이돈과 암피트리테가 오케아노스와 테티스를 대신하여 물의 통치권을 이어받았다. 포세이돈"]], [["Neptune was the chief of the water deities.", "포세이돈은 물의 신들의 우두머리였다."], ["The symbol of his power was the trident, or spear with three points, with which he used to shatter rocks, to call forth or subdue storms, to shake the shores and the like.", "그 권능의 상징은 삼지창, 곧 세 갈래로 끝이 뾰족한 창이었으니, 그는 그것으로 바위를 부수고, 폭풍을 불러일으키거나 가라앉히고, 해안을 뒤흔들곤 했다."], ["He created the horse and was the patron of horse races.", "그는 말을 창조했고 경마의 수호신이었다."], ["His own horses had brazen hoofs and golden manes.", "그 자신의 말들은 청동의 발굽과 황금의 갈기를 지녔다."], ["They drew his chariot over the sea, which became smooth before him, while the monsters of the deep gambolled about his path.", "그것들이 그의 전차를 끌어 바다 위를 달리면, 바다는 그의 앞에서 잔잔해졌고, 그러는 동안 심해의 괴물들이 그의 길 주위에서 뛰놀았다. 암피트리테"]], [["Amphitrite was the wife of Neptune.", "암피트리테는 포세이돈의 아내였다."], ["She was the daughter of Nereus and Doris, and the mother of Triton.", "그녀는 네레우스와 도리스의 딸이자 트리톤의 어머니였다."], ["Neptune, to pay his court to Amphitrite, came riding on a dolphin.", "포세이돈은 암피트리테에게 구애하기 위해 돌고래를 타고 왔다."], ["Having won her he rewarded the dolphin by placing him among the stars.", "그녀를 얻자 그는 돌고래를 별들 사이에 두어 그 보답을 했다. 네레우스와 도리스"]], [["# NEREUS AND DORIS", "# NEREUS AND DORIS"]], [["Nereus and Doris were the parents of the Nereids, the most celebrated of whom were Amphitrite, Thetis, the mother of Achilles, and Galatea, who was loved by the Cyclops Polyphemus.", "네레우스와 도리스는 네레이데스의 부모였으니, 그 가운데 가장 이름난 이들은 암피트리테, 아킬레우스의 어머니인 테티스, 그리고 키클롭스 폴리페모스의 사랑을 받은 갈라테이아였다. 네레우스는 그 지혜와 진실과 정의를 사랑하는 마음으로 두드러져, 그리하여 〈장로〉라 불렸다."], ["Nereus was distinguished for his knowledge and his love of truth and justice, whence he was termed an elder; the gift of prophecy was also assigned to him.", "예언의 능력 또한 그에게 주어진 것이었다. 트리톤과 프로테우스"]], [["# TRITON AND PROTEUS", "# TRITON AND PROTEUS"]], [["Triton was the son of Neptune and Amphitrite, and the poets make him his father’s trumpeter.", "트리톤은 포세이돈과 암피트리테의 아들로, 시인들은 그를 그 아버지의 나팔수로 삼는다."], ["Proteus was also a son of Neptune.", "프로테우스 역시 포세이돈의 아들이었다."], ["He, like Nereus, is styled a sea-elder for his wisdom and knowledge of future events.", "그는 네레우스와 마찬가지로 그 지혜와 앞날의 일에 대한 지식으로 인해 바다의 〈장로〉라 일컬어진다."], ["His peculiar power was that of changing his shape at will.", "그의 독특한 능력은 자신의 모습을 마음대로 바꾸는 것이었다. 테티스"]], [["Thetis, the daughter of Nereus and Doris, was so beautiful that Jupiter himself sought her in marriage; but having learned from Prometheus the Titan that Thetis should bear a son who should grow greater than his father, Jupiter desisted from his suit and decreed that Thetis should be the wife of a mortal.", "네레우스와 도리스의 딸인 테티스는 너무도 아름다워 제우스 자신이 그녀에게 혼인을 청했다. 그러나 티탄 프로메테우스에게서 테티스가 제 아비보다 더 위대하게 자라날 아들을 낳으리라는 것을 알게 되자, 제우스는 구애를 단념하고 테티스가 필멸자의 아내가 되도록 정했다."], ["By the aid of Chiron the Centaur, Peleus succeeded in winning the goddess for his bride and their son was the renowned Achilles.", "켄타우로스족인 키론의 도움으로 펠레우스는 그 여신을 신부로 얻는 데 성공했고, 그들의 아들이 바로 저 이름난 아킬레우스였다."], ["In our chapter on the Trojan war it will appear that Thetis was a faithful mother to him, aiding him in all difficulties, and watching over his interests from the first to the last.", "트로이아 전쟁을 다룬 우리의 장에서 드러나겠지만, 테티스는 그에게 신실한 어머니여서, 온갖 어려움 속에서 그를 돕고 처음부터 끝까지 그의 이익을 지켜 주었다."]], [["Ino, the daughter of Cadmus and wife of Athamas, flying from her frantic husband with her little son Melicertes in her arms, sprang from a cliff into the sea.", "레우코테아와 팔라이몬\n카드모스의 딸이자 아타마스의 아내인 이노는, 미쳐 날뛰는 남편에게서 어린 아들 멜리케르테스를 품에 안고 달아나다가, 벼랑에서 바다로 몸을 던졌다."], ["The gods, out of compassion, made her a goddess of the sea, under the name of Leucothea, and him a god, under that of Palæmon.", "신들은 가엾이 여겨 그녀를 레우코테아라는 이름의 바다의 여신으로 삼고, 그를 팔라이몬이라는 이름의 신으로 삼았다."], ["Both were held powerful to save from shipwreck and were invoked by sailors.", "두 신 모두 난파에서 사람을 구하는 데 강력한 힘을 지닌 것으로 여겨져 뱃사람들의 기원을 받았다."], ["Palæmon was usually represented riding on a dolphin.", "팔라이몬은 대개 돌고래를 탄 모습으로 그려졌다."], ["The Isthmian games were celebrated in his honor.", "이스트미아 경기는 그를 기려 열렸다."], ["He was called Portunus by the Romans, and believed to have jurisdiction of the ports and shores.", "그는 로마인들에게 포르투누스라 불렸고, 항구와 해안을 관장하는 것으로 믿어졌다."]], [["Milton alludes to all these deities in the song at the conclusion of “Comus”:", "밀턴은 「코머스」의 결말부 노래에서 이 모든 신들을 언급한다."]], [["“. . . Sabrina fair, Listen and appear to us, In name of great Oceanus; By the earth-shaking Neptune’s mace, And Tethys’ grave, majestic pace, By hoary Nereus’ wrinkled look, And the Carpathian wizard’s hook,[20] By scaly Triton’s winding shell, And old soothsaying Glaucus’ spell, By Leucothea’s lovely hands, And her son who rules the strands. By Thetis’ tinsel-slippered feet, And the songs of Sirens sweet;” etc.", "\"……아리따운 사브리나여, 귀 기울여 우리에게 모습을 드러내라, 위대한 오케아노스의 이름으로, 대지를 뒤흔드는 포세이돈의 철퇴와, 테티스의 장중하고 위엄 있는 걸음걸이로, 백발의 네레우스의 주름진 얼굴과, 카르파토스 마법사의 갈고리로,[20] 비늘 덮인 트리톤의 휘감긴 소라와, 예언 잘하는 늙은 글라우코스의 주문으로, 레우코테아의 어여쁜 두 손과, 물가를 다스리는 그 아들로, 테티스의 번쩍이는 신 신은 발과, 세이레네스의 감미로운 노래로.\" 등등."]], [["Armstrong, the poet of the “Art of preserving Health,” under the inspiration of Hygeia, the goddess of health, thus celebrates the Naiads.", "「건강 보존의 기술」의 시인 암스트롱은, 건강의 여신 히게이아의 영감을 받아 나이아데스를 이렇게 기린다."], ["Pæon is a name both of Apollo and Æsculapius.", "파이온은 아폴론과 아스클레피오스 둘 다의 이름이다."]], [["“Come, ye Naiads!", "\"오라, 나이아데스여!"], ["to the fountains lead!", "샘으로 인도하라!"], ["Propitious maids!", "자비로운 처녀들이여!"], ["the task remains to sing Your gifts (so Pæon, so the powers of Health Command), to praise your crystal element.", "그대들의 선물을 노래하는 일이 남았으니(파이온이, 〈건강〉의 권능이 그리 명하노라), 그대들의 수정 같은 원소를 찬미하는 일이라."], ["O comfortable streams!", "오 위안을 주는 시냇물이여!"], ["with eager lips And trembling hands the languid thirsty quaff New life in you; fresh vigor fills their veins.", "갈망하는 입술과 떨리는 손으로 기진하고 목마른 이들이 그대 안에서 새 생명을 들이켜고, 신선한 활기가 그 핏줄을 채우네. 시골의 옛 시대는 더 따뜻한 잔을 알지 못했고, 인류의 조상들도 더 따뜻한 것을 찾지 않았네."], ["No warmer cups the rural ages knew, None warmer sought the sires of humankind; Happy in temperate peace their equal days Felt not the alternate fits of feverish mirth And sick dejection; still serene and pleased, Blessed with divine immunity from ills, Long centuries they lived; their only fate Was ripe old age, and rather sleep than death.”", "온화한 평화 속에 행복하여 그 한결같은 나날은 열에 들뜬 환락과 병든 의기소침의 번갈아 드는 발작을 느끼지 못했고, 늘 평온하고 흡족하여 온갖 재앙에서 벗어나는 신성한 면제를 누리며, 긴 세월을 살았네. 그들의 유일한 운명은 무르익은 노년이었고, 죽음이라기보다 차라리 잠이었네.\""]], [["By this name the Latins designated the Muses, but included under it also some other deities, principally nymphs of fountains.", "카메나이\n라틴인들은 이 이름으로 무사 여신들을 가리켰으나, 그 안에 다른 몇몇 신들, 주로 샘의 님프들도 포함시켰다."], ["Egeria was one of them, whose fountain and grotto are still shown.", "에게리아도 그 가운데 하나였으니, 그녀의 샘과 동굴은 지금도 사람들에게 보여진다."], ["It was said that Numa, the second king of Rome, was favored by this nymph with secret interviews, in which she taught him those lessons of wisdom and of law which he imbodied in the institutions of his rising nation.", "로마의 두 번째 왕인 누마가 이 님프의 은총을 입어 은밀한 만남을 가졌으며, 그 자리에서 그녀가 그에게 지혜와 법의 가르침을 일러 주었고, 그는 그것을 떠오르는 나라의 제도에 담아냈다고 전해진다."], ["After the death of Numa the nymph pined away and was changed into a fountain.", "누마가 죽은 뒤 님프는 시름에 잠겨 야위어 가다가 샘으로 변했다."]], [["Byron, in “Childe Harold,”", "바이런은 「차일드 해럴드」"], ["Canto IV., thus alludes to Egeria and her grotto:", "제4편에서 에게리아와 그녀의 동굴을 이렇게 언급한다."]], [["“Here didst thou dwell, in this enchanted cover, Egeria!", "\"여기 그대 거처했도다, 이 마법에 걸린 은신처에, 에게리아여!"], ["all thy heavenly bosom beating For the far footsteps of thy mortal lover; The purple midnight veiled that mystic meeting With her most starry canopy;”", "그대의 천상의 가슴은 온통 그대 필멸의 연인의 머나먼 발걸음에 두근거렸도다. 자줏빛 한밤이 그 가장 별 많은 차일로 그 신비로운 만남을 가렸도다.\""], ["etc.", "등등."]], [["Tennyson, also, in his “Palace of Art,” gives us a glimpse of the royal lover expecting the interview:", "테니슨 또한 「예술의 궁전」에서, 그 만남을 기다리는 왕가의 연인을 우리에게 얼핏 보여 준다."]], [["“Holding one hand against his ear, To list a footfall ere he saw The wood-nymph, stayed the Tuscan king to hear Of wisdom and of law.”", "\"한 손을 귀에 갖다 댄 채, 숲의 님프를 보기에 앞서 그 발소리를 들으려, 토스카나의 왕은 멈춰 서서 귀 기울였도다, 지혜와 법에 관하여.\" 바람들"]], [["# THE WINDS", "# THE WINDS"]], [["When so many less active agencies were personified, it is not to be supposed that the winds failed to be so.", "그토록 많은 덜 활동적인 작용들조차 의인화되었으니, 바람들이 그러하지 못했으리라고는 생각할 수 없다."], ["They were Boreas or Aquilo, the north wind; Zephyrus or Favonius, the west; Notus or Auster, the south; and Eurus, the east.", "그들은 북풍인 보레아스 혹은 아퀼로, 서풍인 제피로스 혹은 파보니우스, 남풍인 노토스 혹은 아우스테르, 그리고 동풍인 에우로스였다."], ["The first two have been chiefly celebrated by the poets, the former as the type of rudeness, the latter of gentleness.", "앞의 둘이 주로 시인들에게 기려졌으니, 전자는 거칢의 전형으로, 후자는 부드러움의 전형으로 그러했다."], ["Boreas loved the nymph Orithyia, and tried to play the lover’s part, but met with poor success.", "보레아스는 님프 오리티이아를 사랑하여 연인의 역할을 해 보려 했으나, 변변찮은 성공밖에 거두지 못했다."], ["It was hard for him to breathe gently, and sighing was out of the question.", "그에게는 부드럽게 숨 쉬는 것이 힘겨운 일이었고, 한숨짓는 것은 더더욱 가당치 않은 일이었다."], ["Weary at last of fruitless endeavors, he acted out his true character, seized the maiden and carried her off.", "마침내 헛된 시도에 지친 그는 자신의 본래 성품을 그대로 드러내어, 그 처녀를 붙잡아 채어 갔다."], ["Their children were Zetes and Calais, winged warriors, who accompanied the Argonautic expedition, and did good service in an encounter with those monstrous birds the Harpies.", "그들의 자식이 제테스와 칼라이스이니, 날개 달린 전사들로서 아르고호 원정에 동행하여, 저 흉측한 새들인 하르피이아이와의 싸움에서 큰 공을 세웠다."]], [["Zephyrus was the lover of Flora.", "제피로스는 플로라의 연인이었다."], ["Milton alludes to them in “Paradise Lost,” where he describes Adam waking and contemplating Eve still asleep.", "밀턴은 「실낙원」에서 아담이 잠에서 깨어 아직 잠들어 있는 이브를 바라보는 장면을 묘사하며 그들을 언급한다."]], [["“. .", "\"……그는 옆으로 몸을 반쯤 일으켜 기댄 채, 다정한 사랑의 눈빛으로, 그녀 위로 넋을 잃고 몸을 굽혀, 깨어 있을 때나 잠들어 있을 때나 남다른 우아함을 뿜어내는 아름다움을 바라보았네."], [". He on his side Leaning half raised, with looks of cordial love, Hung over her enamored, and beheld Beauty which, whether waking or asleep, Shot forth peculiar graces; then with voice, Mild as when Zephyrus on Flora breathes, Her hand soft touching, whispered thus: ‘Awake!", "그러고는, 제피로스가 플로라에게 숨결을 불어넣을 때처럼 부드러운 목소리로, 그녀의 손을 살며시 어루만지며, 이렇게 속삭였네."], ["My fairest, my espoused, my latest found, Heaven’s last, best gift, my ever-new delight.’”", "'깨어나오! 나의 가장 아리따운 이, 나의 배필, 내가 가장 늦게 찾은 이, 하늘이 준 마지막이자 가장 좋은 선물, 나의 영원히 새로운 기쁨이여.'\""]], [["Dr. Young, the poet of the “Night Thoughts,” addressing the idle and luxurious, says:", "「밤의 상념」의 시인 영 박사는, 게으르고 사치스러운 자들에게 이렇게 말한다."]], [["“Ye delicate! who nothing can support (Yourselves most insupportable) for whom The winter rose must blow, . . . . . . and silky soft Favonius breathe still softer or be chid!”", "\"그대 여린 이들이여! 아무것도 견뎌 내지 못하는(그러면서 그대 자신은 더없이 견디기 힘든) 이들이여, 그대들을 위하여 겨울의 장미가 피어나야만 하고, …… ……그리고 비단처럼 부드러운 파보니우스가 더욱 부드럽게 숨 쉬거나, 아니면 꾸지람을 들어야만 하는도다!\""]], [["————", "────"]], [["# CHAPTER XXIII", "# CHAPTER XXIII"]], [["ACHELOUS AND HERCULES—ADMETUS AND ALCESTIS—ANTIGONE—PENELOPE\nTHE river-god Achelous told the story of Erisichthon to Theseus and his companions, whom he was entertaining at his hospitable board, while they were delayed on their journey by the overflow of his waters.", "강의 신 아켈로오스는 자신의 환대하는 식탁에서 테세우스와 그 일행을 대접하던 중에 에리시크톤의 이야기를 들려주었는데, 이들은 강물이 불어나는 바람에 여행길이 지체되어 그곳에 머무르고 있었다."], ["Having finished his story, he added, “But why should I tell of other persons’ transformations when I myself am an instance of the possession of this power?", "이야기를 마치고 나서 그는 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"그런데 내가 직접 이 능력을 지닌 본보기인데, 어찌하여 남들의 변신 이야기를 늘어놓겠는가?"], ["Sometimes I become a serpent, and sometimes a bull, with horns on my head.", "나는 때로 뱀이 되기도 하고, 때로는 머리에 뿔이 돋은 황소가 되기도 한다."], ["Or I should say I once could do so; but now I have but one horn, having lost one.”", "아니, 한때 그럴 수 있었다고 말해야 옳겠다. 지금은 뿔 하나를 잃어 하나밖에 남지 않았으니 말이다.\""], ["And here he groaned and was silent.", "그러고는 신음하더니 입을 다물었다."]], [["Theseus asked him the cause of his grief, and how he lost his horn.", "테세우스가 그에게 슬픔의 까닭과 어떻게 뿔을 잃었는지를 물었다. 그러자 강의 신은 이렇게 대답했다."], ["To which question the river-god replied as follows: “Who likes to tell of his defeats?", "\"누가 자신의 패배를 이야기하고 싶어 하겠는가? 그래도 나는 주저하지 않고 내 패배를 들려주겠다."], ["Yet I will not hesitate to relate mine, comforting myself with the thought of the greatness of my conqueror, for it was Hercules.", "나를 이긴 자가 위대한 인물이라는 생각으로 스스로를 위로하면서 말이다. 그자는 바로 헤라클레스였다."], ["Perhaps you have heard of the fame of Dejanira, the fairest of maidens, whom a host of suitors strove to win.", "그대들도 아마 데이아네이라의 명성을 들어 보았을 것이다. 처녀들 가운데 가장 아름다웠던 그녀를 차지하려고 수많은 구혼자들이 다투었다."], ["Hercules and myself were of the number, and the rest yielded to us two.", "헤라클레스와 나도 그 가운데 끼어 있었고, 나머지는 우리 둘에게 양보했다."], ["He urged in his behalf his descent from Jove and his labors by which he had exceeded the exactions of Juno, his stepmother.", "그는 자신을 위해 제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]의 후손이라는 점과, 계모인 헤라[로마의 〈유노〉]가 강요한 과업을 능가하여 이룬 자신의 공적들을 내세웠다."], ["I, on the other hand, said to the father of the maiden, ‘Behold me, the king of the waters that flow through your land.", "반면에 나는 처녀의 아버지에게 이렇게 말했다. '나를 보십시오."], ["I am no stranger from a foreign shore, but belong to the country, a part of your realm.", "나는 당신의 땅을 흐르는 강물의 왕입니다. 나는 이방의 해안에서 온 낯선 자가 아니라 이 나라에 속한 자, 당신 왕국의 일부입니다."], ["Let it not stand in my way that royal Juno owes me no enmity nor punishes me with heavy tasks.", "왕녀이신 헤라께서 나에게 적의를 품지 않고 무거운 과업으로 벌하지 않는다고 해서, 그것이 나에게 불리하게 작용해서는 안 됩니다. 저자로 말하자면, 제우스의 아들이라고 뽐내고 있으나, 그것은 거짓 주장이거나, 설령 사실이라 해도 그에게는 수치스러운 일입니다."], ["As for this man, who boasts himself the son of Jove, it is either a false pretence, or disgraceful to him if true, for it cannot be true except by his mother’s shame.’ As I said this Hercules scowled upon me, and with difficulty restrained his rage.", "그것이 사실일 수 있는 길이란 오로지 그 어머니의 부끄러움을 통해서뿐이니 말입니다.' 내가 이렇게 말하자 헤라클레스는 나를 노려보며 가까스로 분노를 억눌렀다."], ["‘My hand will answer better than my tongue,’ said he.", "'내 혀보다는 내 손이 더 나은 대답을 하리라.' 그가 말했다."], ["‘I yield to you the victory in words, but trust my cause to the strife of deeds.’ With that he advanced towards me, and I was ashamed, after what I had said, to yield.", "'말로는 그대에게 승리를 양보하겠으나, 내 주장은 행동의 다툼에 맡기겠다.' 그렇게 말하면서 그가 나를 향해 다가왔고, 나는 앞서 그런 말을 해 놓은 터라 물러서기가 부끄러웠다."], ["I threw off my green vesture and presented myself for the struggle.", "나는 푸른 옷을 벗어 던지고 싸움에 임했다."], ["He tried to throw me, now attacking my head, now my body.", "그는 나를 쓰러뜨리려고 때로는 내 머리를, 때로는 내 몸을 공격했다."], ["My bulk was my protection, and he assailed me in vain.", "내 거대한 몸집이 나를 지켜 주어 그의 공격은 헛수고였다."], ["For a time we stopped, then returned to the conflict.", "한동안 우리는 멈추었다가 다시 싸움으로 돌아갔다."], ["We each kept our position, determined not to yield, foot to foot, I bending over him, clenching his hand in mine, with my forehead almost touching his.", "우리는 각자 자리를 지키며 물러서지 않으려고 발과 발을 맞대었고, 나는 그의 위로 몸을 굽혀 그의 손을 내 손으로 움켜쥐고 내 이마를 거의 그의 이마에 맞닿을 정도로 가져갔다."], ["Thrice Hercules tried to throw me off, and the fourth time he succeeded, brought me to the ground, and himself upon my back.", "헤라클레스는 세 번이나 나를 떼어 내려 했고, 네 번째에 성공하여 나를 땅에 쓰러뜨리고는 자신이 내 등 위에 올라탔다."], ["I tell you the truth, it was as if a mountain had fallen on me.", "진실로 말하건대, 마치 산 하나가 내 위에 무너져 내린 것 같았다."], ["I struggled to get my arms at liberty, panting and reeking with perspiration.", "나는 헐떡이며 땀에 젖어 김을 내뿜으면서 팔을 자유롭게 빼내려고 안간힘을 썼다."], ["He gave me no chance to recover, but seized my throat.", "그는 내가 회복할 틈을 주지 않고 내 목을 움켜쥐었다."], ["My knees were on the earth and my mouth in the dust.", "내 무릎은 땅에 닿았고 내 입은 흙먼지 속에 처박혔다."]], [["“Finding that I was no match for him in the warrior’s art, I resorted to others and glided away in the form of a serpent.", "\"전사의 기술로는 그의 적수가 되지 못함을 깨닫고, 나는 다른 방법에 의지하여 뱀의 모습으로 미끄러져 빠져나갔다."], ["I curled my body in a coil and hissed at him with my forked tongue.", "나는 몸을 똬리로 감고 갈라진 혀로 그에게 쉭쉭거렸다. 그는 이것을 보고 비웃으며 말했다."], ["He smiled scornfully at this, and said, ‘It was the labor of my infancy to conquer snakes.’ So saying he clasped my neck with his hands.", "'뱀을 정복하는 것은 내 갓난 시절의 과업이었다.' 그렇게 말하면서 그는 두 손으로 내 목을 움켜쥐었다."], ["I was almost choked, and struggled to get my neck out of his grasp.", "나는 거의 목이 졸려 그의 손아귀에서 목을 빼내려고 발버둥쳤다."], ["Vanquished in this form, I tried what alone remained to me and assumed the form of a bull.", "이 모습으로도 패하자, 나는 내게 남은 단 하나의 방법을 시도하여 황소의 모습을 취했다."], ["He grasped my neck with his arm, and dragging my head down to the ground, overthrew me on the sand.", "그는 팔로 내 목을 휘감고는 내 머리를 땅으로 끌어내려 모래밭에 나를 쓰러뜨렸다."], ["Nor was this enough.", "그것으로도 모자랐다."], ["His ruthless hand rent my horn from my head.", "그의 무자비한 손이 내 머리에서 뿔을 뜯어냈다."], ["The Naiades took it, consecrated it, and filled it with fragrant flowers.", "나이아데스가 그 뿔을 가져다 봉헌하고 향기로운 꽃들로 가득 채웠다."], ["Plenty adopted my horn and made it her own, and called it ‘Cornucopia.’”", "풍요의 여신이 내 뿔을 받아들여 자기 것으로 삼고는 그것을 '코르누코피아[풍요의 뿔]'라고 불렀다.\""]], [["The ancients were fond of finding a hidden meaning in their mythological tales.", "고대인들은 자신들의 신화 이야기 속에서 숨은 의미를 찾기를 좋아했다."], ["They explain this fight of Achelous with Hercules by saying Achelous was a river that in seasons of rain overflowed its banks.", "그들은 아켈로오스와 헤라클레스의 이 싸움을 이렇게 설명한다. 즉 아켈로오스는 비가 오는 철이면 둑을 넘쳐흐르던 강이었다는 것이다."], ["When the fable says that Achelous loved Dejanira, and sought a union with her, the meaning is that the river in its windings flowed through part of Dejanira’s kingdom.", "신화에서 아켈로오스가 데이아네이라를 사랑하여 그녀와의 결합을 구했다고 말할 때, 그 뜻은 그 강이 굽이쳐 흐르면서 데이아네이라 왕국의 일부를 지나갔다는 것이다."], ["It was said to take the form of a snake because of its winding, and of a bull because it made a brawling or roaring in its course.", "그 강이 뱀의 모습을 취했다고 한 것은 그 강이 구불구불 굽이졌기 때문이고, 황소의 모습을 취했다고 한 것은 그 강이 흐르는 동안 요란하게 으르렁거리는 소리를 냈기 때문이다."], ["When the river swelled, it made itself another channel.", "강물이 불어나면 또 다른 물길을 냈다."], ["Thus its head was horned.", "그래서 그 머리에 뿔이 돋았다는 것이다."], ["Hercules prevented the return of these periodical overflows by embankments and canals; and therefore he was said to have vanquished the river-god and cut off his horn.", "헤라클레스는 둑과 운하로 이 주기적인 범람의 재발을 막았다. 그래서 그가 강의 신을 정복하고 그 뿔을 잘라 냈다고 한 것이다."], ["Finally, the lands formerly subject to overflow, but now redeemed, became very fertile, and this is meant by the horn of plenty.", "마침내 예전에는 범람에 시달렸으나 이제는 되찾은 그 땅이 매우 비옥해졌으니, 이것이 바로 풍요의 뿔이 뜻하는 바다."]], [["There is another account of the origin of the Cornucopia.", "코르누코피아의 기원에 관해서는 또 다른 이야기가 있다."], ["Jupiter at his birth was committed by his mother Rhea to the care of the daughters of Melisseus, a Cretan king.", "제우스는 태어날 때 어머니 레아에 의해 크레타의 왕 멜리세우스의 딸들의 보살핌에 맡겨졌다."], ["They fed the infant deity with the milk of the goat Amalthea.", "그들은 아말테이아라는 암염소의 젖으로 갓난 신을 길렀다."], ["Jupiter broke off one of the horns of the goat and gave it to his nurses, and endowed it with the wonderful power of becoming filled with whatever the possessor might wish.", "제우스는 그 염소의 뿔 하나를 부러뜨려 자신의 유모들에게 주고는, 그것을 지닌 자가 원하는 무엇으로든 가득 채워지는 놀라운 힘을 부여했다."]], [["The name of Amalthea is also given by some writers to the mother of Bacchus.", "아말테이아라는 이름은 어떤 작가들에 의해 디오니소스[로마의 〈바쿠스〉]의 어머니에게도 붙여진다. 밀턴이 「실낙원」"], ["It is thus used by Milton, “Paradise Lost,” Book IV.:", "제4권에서 그렇게 쓰고 있다."]], [["“. .", "\"."], [".", "."], ["That Nyseian isle, Girt with the river Triton, where old Cham, Whom Gentiles Ammon call, and Libyan Jove, Hid Amalthea and her florid son, Young Bacchus, from his stepdame Rhea’s eye.”", ". 그 니세이아의 섬, 트리톤 강에 둘러싸인 곳, 그곳에서 늙은 함은, 이방인들이 암몬이라, 또 리비아의 유피테르라 부르는 그가, 아말테이아와 그녀의 발그레한 아들, 어린 디오니소스를 그의 계모 레아의 눈을 피해 숨겼노라.\""]], [["# ADMETUS AND ALCESTIS", "# ADMETUS AND ALCESTIS"]], [["Æsculapius, the son of Apollo, was endowed by his father with such skill in the healing art that he even restored the dead to life.", "아폴론의 아들 아스클레피오스는 아버지에게서 치유의 기술에 관해 어찌나 뛰어난 솜씨를 부여받았던지 죽은 자를 되살려 내기까지 했다."], ["At this Pluto took alarm, and prevailed on Jupiter to launch a thunderbolt at Æsculapius.", "이에 하데스[로마의 〈플루톤〉]가 놀라 제우스를 설득하여 아스클레피오스에게 벼락을 내리치게 했다."], ["Apollo was indignant at the destruction of his son, and wreaked his vengeance on the innocent workmen who had made the thunderbolt.", "아폴론은 자기 아들이 죽임을 당한 것에 분개하여, 그 벼락을 만든 죄 없는 일꾼들에게 복수를 퍼부었다."], ["These were the Cyclopes, who have their workshop under Mount Ætna, from which the smoke and flames of their furnaces are constantly issuing.", "이들은 키클로페스로, 아이트나 산 아래에 작업장을 두고 있어 그곳에서는 그들의 화로에서 나오는 연기와 불길이 끊임없이 솟아오른다."], ["Apollo shot his arrows at the Cyclopes, which so incensed Jupiter that he condemned him as a punishment to become the servant of a mortal for the space of one year.", "아폴론이 키클로페스에게 화살을 쏘았고, 이에 제우스가 어찌나 격노했던지 그 벌로 그를 일 년 동안 한 필멸자의 종이 되라고 선고했다."], ["Accordingly Apollo went into the service of Admetus, king of Thessaly, and pastured his flocks for him on the verdant banks of the river Amphrysos.", "그리하여 아폴론은 테살리아의 왕 아드메토스의 종이 되어, 암프리소스 강의 푸른 둑에서 그를 위해 가축 떼를 쳤다."]], [["Admetus was a suitor, with others, for the hand of Alcestis, the daughter of Pelias, who promised her to him who should come for her in a chariot drawn by lions and boars.", "아드메토스는 다른 이들과 더불어 펠리아스의 딸 알케스티스에게 구혼한 자였는데, 펠리아스는 사자들과 멧돼지들이 끄는 전차를 타고 그녀를 데리러 오는 자에게 그녀를 주겠노라고 약속했다."], ["This task Admetus performed by the assistance of his divine herdsman, and was made happy in the possession of Alcestis.", "아드메토스는 자신의 신성한 목자의 도움으로 이 과업을 해냈고, 알케스티스를 얻어 행복을 누렸다."], ["But Admetus fell ill, and being near to death, Apollo prevailed on the Fates to spare him on condition that some one would consent to die in his stead.", "그러나 아드메토스가 병이 들어 죽음에 가까워지자, 아폴론은 운명의 여신들을 설득하여 누군가가 그를 대신해 죽기로 동의한다는 조건으로 그를 살려 주게 했다."], ["Admetus, in his joy at this reprieve, thought little of the ransom, and perhaps remembering the declarations of attachment which he had often heard from his courtiers and dependents fancied that it would be easy to find a substitute.", "아드메토스는 이렇게 죽음을 면하게 된 것에 기뻐하여 그 대가에 대해서는 별로 생각하지 않았으며, 어쩌면 신하들과 부하들에게서 자주 들어 온 충성의 맹세를 떠올리면서 대신할 자를 찾기가 쉬우리라 여겼는지도 모른다."], ["But it was not so.", "그러나 그렇지 않았다."], ["Brave warriors, who would willingly have perilled their lives for their prince, shrunk from the thought of dying for him on the bed of sickness; and old servants who had experienced his bounty and that of his house from their childhood up, were not willing to lay down the scanty remnant of their days to show their gratitude.", "왕을 위해서라면 기꺼이 목숨을 걸었을 용감한 전사들도 병상에서 그를 대신해 죽는다는 생각 앞에서는 움츠러들었다. 또한 어릴 적부터 그와 그 가문의 은덕을 입어 온 늙은 종들도 자신들의 얼마 남지 않은 여생을 내놓아 감사를 표하려 하지 않았다."], ["Men asked, “Why does not one of his parents do it?", "사람들은 물었다. \"어찌하여 그의 부모 가운데 한 사람이 그리하지 않는가?"], ["They cannot in the course of nature live much longer, and who can feel like them the call to rescue the life they gave from an untimely end?”", "그들은 자연의 이치상 그리 오래 살 수 없으니, 그들만큼 자기들이 준 생명을 때 이른 종말에서 구해 내야 한다는 부름을 절실히 느낄 수 있는 자가 누구겠는가?\""], ["But the parents, distressed though they were at the thought of losing him, shrunk from the call.", "그러나 부모는 그를 잃는다는 생각에 비통해하면서도 그 부름 앞에서 움츠러들었다."], ["Then Alcestis, with a generous self-devotion, proffered herself as the substitute.", "그러자 알케스티스가 너그러운 자기희생으로 자신을 대신할 자로 내놓았다."], ["Admetus, fond as he was of life, would not have submitted to receive it at such a cost; but there was no remedy.", "아드메토스는 그만큼 삶을 아꼈으나, 그런 대가를 치르고 삶을 받아들이려 하지는 않았을 것이다. 그러나 다른 방도가 없었다."], ["The condition imposed by the Fates had been met, and the decree was irrevocable.", "운명의 여신들이 내건 조건은 충족되었고, 그 결정은 돌이킬 수 없었다."], ["Alcestis sickened as Admetus revived, and she was rapidly sinking to the grave.", "아드메토스가 되살아남에 따라 알케스티스는 병들었고, 그녀는 빠르게 무덤으로 가라앉고 있었다."]], [["Just at this time Hercules arrived at the palace of Admetus, and found all the inmates in great distress for the impending loss of the devoted wife and beloved mistress.", "바로 이때 헤라클레스가 아드메토스의 궁전에 당도하여, 헌신적인 아내이자 사랑받는 안주인을 곧 잃게 되리라는 생각에 온 집안 식구들이 깊은 슬픔에 빠져 있는 것을 보았다."], ["Hercules, to whom no labor was too arduous, resolved to attempt her rescue.", "어떤 과업도 너무 힘겹다고 여기지 않는 헤라클레스는 그녀를 구해 내기로 결심했다."], ["He went and lay in wait at the door of the chamber of the dying queen, and when Death came for his prey, he seized him and forced him to resign his victim.", "그는 죽어 가는 왕비의 침실 문 앞에 가서 매복했고, 죽음의 신이 자신의 먹이를 가지러 오자 그를 붙잡아 자신의 희생물을 내놓도록 강요했다."], ["Alcestis recovered, and was restored to her husband.", "알케스티스는 회복되어 남편에게 돌아왔다."]], [["Milton alludes to the story of Alcestis in his Sonnet “on his deceased wife”:", "밀턴은 「세상을 떠난 자기 아내에 관하여」라는 소네트에서 알케스티스의 이야기를 언급한다."]], [["“Methought I saw my late espoused saint Brought to me like Alcestis from the grave, Whom Jove’s great son to her glad husband gave, Rescued from death by force, though pale and faint.”", "\"내가 보았노라, 얼마 전 혼인한 나의 성녀가 무덤에서 알케스티스처럼 내게로 돌아오는 것을, 제우스의 위대한 아들이 기쁨에 찬 남편에게 돌려준 그녀, 창백하고 기력 없으나, 힘으로 죽음에서 구해 낸 그녀를.\""]], [["J. R.", "J."], ["Lowell has chosen the “Shepherd of King Admetus”", "R. 로웰은 「아드메토스 왕의 목자」를 짧은 시의 주제로 택했다."], ["for the subject of a short poem. He makes that event the first introduction of poetry to men.", "그는 그 사건을 시가 인간에게 처음으로 소개된 일로 그린다."]], [["“Men called him but a shiftless youth, In whom no good they saw, And yet unwittingly, in truth, They made his careless words their law.", "\"사람들은 그를 한낱 쓸모없는 젊은이라 불렀으니, 그에게서 아무런 좋은 점도 보지 못했네. 그러나 자기들도 모르는 사이에, 진실로, 그들은 그의 무심한 말들을 자기들의 법으로 삼았네."], ["“And day by day more holy grew Each spot where he had trod, Till after-poets only knew Their first-born brother was a god.”", "\"그리고 나날이 더욱 거룩해졌네, 그가 발 디딘 곳마다. 마침내 후세의 시인들만이 알게 되었네, 그들의 맏형이 신이었음을.\""]], [["A large proportion both of the interesting persons and of the exalted acts of legendary Greece belongs to the female sex.", "전설 속 그리스의 흥미로운 인물들과 고귀한 행적들 가운데 상당수가 여성에게 속한다."], ["Antigone was as bright an example of filial and sisterly fidelity as was Alcestis of connubial devotion.", "알케스티스가 부부간의 헌신의 본보기였듯이, 안티고네는 자식으로서, 또 누이로서의 충실함의 빛나는 본보기였다."], ["She was the daughter of Œdipus and Jocasta, who with all their descendants were the victims of an unrelenting fate, dooming them to destruction.", "그녀는 오이디푸스와 이오카스테의 딸이었는데, 이들은 모든 후손과 더불어 가차 없는 운명의 희생자가 되어 파멸의 선고를 받았다."], ["Œdipus in his madness had torn out his eyes, and was driven forth from his kingdom Thebes, dreaded and abandoned by all men, as an object of divine vengeance.", "오이디푸스는 광기에 사로잡혀 자신의 눈을 뽑아내고는, 신의 복수의 대상으로서 모든 사람에게 두려움과 버림을 받으며 자신의 왕국 테베에서 쫓겨났다."], ["Antigone, his daughter, alone shared his wanderings and remained with him till he died, and then returned to Thebes.", "그의 딸 안티고네만이 홀로 그의 방랑을 함께하며 그가 죽을 때까지 곁에 머물렀고, 그 뒤 테베로 돌아왔다."]], [["Her brothers, Eteocles and Polynices, had agreed to share the kingdom between them, and reign alternately year by year.", "그녀의 오라비들인 에테오클레스와 폴리네이케스는 왕국을 둘이서 나누어 해마다 번갈아 다스리기로 합의했다."], ["The first year fell to the lot of Eteocles, who, when his time expired, refused to surrender the kingdom to his brother.", "첫해는 에테오클레스의 몫이 되었는데, 그는 자기 차례가 끝나자 동생에게 왕국을 넘겨주기를 거부했다."], ["Polynices fled to Adrastus, king of Argos, who gave him his daughter in marriage, and aided him with an army to enforce his claim to the kingdom.", "폴리네이케스는 아르고스의 왕 아드라스토스에게로 도망쳤고, 아드라스토스는 그에게 자기 딸을 아내로 주고 군대로 도와 왕국에 대한 그의 권리를 관철하게 했다."], ["This led to the celebrated expedition of the “Seven against Thebes,” which furnished ample materials for the epic and tragic poets of Greece.", "이로 인해 저 유명한 \"테베를 공격한 일곱 장수\"의 원정이 일어났으니, 이는 그리스의 서사 시인들과 비극 시인들에게 풍부한 소재를 제공했다."]], [["Amphiaraus, the brother-in-law of Adrastus, opposed the enterprise, for he was a soothsayer, and knew by his art that no one of the leaders except Adrastus would live to return.", "아드라스토스의 처남인 암피아라오스는 그 거사에 반대했다. 그는 예언자였기에 자신의 기술로 아드라스토스를 제외한 어떤 장수도 살아 돌아오지 못하리라는 것을 알았기 때문이다."], ["But Amphiaraus, on his marriage to Eriphyle, the king’s sister, had agreed that whenever he and Adrastus should differ in opinion, the decision should be left to Eriphyle.", "그러나 암피아라오스는 왕의 누이 에리필레와 혼인할 때, 자신과 아드라스토스의 의견이 엇갈릴 때면 언제나 그 결정을 에리필레에게 맡기기로 합의해 두었다."], ["Polynices, knowing this, gave Eriphyle the collar of Harmonia, and thereby gained her to his interest.", "이를 안 폴리네이케스는 에리필레에게 하르모니아의 목걸이를 주어 그녀를 자기편으로 끌어들였다."], ["This collar or necklace was a present which Vulcan had given to Harmonia on her marriage with Cadmus, and Polynices had taken it with him on his flight from Thebes.", "이 목걸이는 헤파이스토스[로마의 〈불카누스〉]가 하르모니아와 카드모스의 혼인 때 그녀에게 준 선물이었는데, 폴리네이케스가 테베에서 도망칠 때 그것을 가지고 나갔던 것이다."], ["Eriphyle could not resist so tempting a bribe, and by her decision the war was resolved on, and Amphiaraus went to his certain fate.", "에리필레는 그토록 솔깃한 뇌물을 뿌리치지 못했고, 그녀의 결정으로 전쟁이 결정되었으며, 암피아라오스는 자신의 정해진 운명을 향해 나아갔다."], ["He bore his part bravely in the contest, but could not avert his destiny.", "그는 싸움에서 자기 몫을 용감히 해냈으나, 자신의 숙명을 피할 수는 없었다."], ["Pursued by the enemy, he fled along the river, when a thunderbolt launched by Jupiter opened the ground, and he, his chariot, and his charioteer were swallowed up.", "적에게 쫓겨 강을 따라 달아나던 중에, 제우스가 던진 벼락이 땅을 갈라놓아 그와 그의 전차와 마부가 함께 삼켜졌다."]], [["It would not be in place here to detail all the acts of heroism or atrocity which marked the contest; but we must not omit to record the fidelity of Evadne as an offset to the weakness of Eriphyle.", "이 싸움을 수놓은 영웅적인 행적이나 잔혹한 행적을 모두 여기서 자세히 늘어놓는 것은 적절하지 않을 것이다. 그러나 에리필레의 나약함에 대비되는 본보기로서 에우아드네의 충실함은 기록하지 않을 수 없다."], ["Capaneus, the husband of Evadne, in the ardor of the fight declared that he would force his way into the city in spite of Jove himself.", "에우아드네의 남편 카파네우스는 싸움의 열기 속에서 제우스 그 자신이 막아서더라도 도성 안으로 밀고 들어가겠노라고 선언했다."], ["Placing a ladder against the wall he mounted, but Jupiter, offended at his impious language, struck him with a thunderbolt.", "그는 성벽에 사다리를 걸고 올라갔으나, 그 불경한 말에 노한 제우스가 벼락으로 그를 쳤다."], ["When his obsequies were celebrated, Evadne cast herself on his funeral pile and perished.", "그의 장례가 치러질 때, 에우아드네는 그의 화장용 장작더미 위로 몸을 던져 목숨을 끊었다."]], [["Early in the contest Eteocles consulted the soothsayer Tiresias as to the issue.", "싸움 초기에 에테오클레스는 그 결말에 관해 예언자 테이레시아스에게 물었다."], ["Tiresias in his youth had by chance seen Minerva bathing.", "테이레시아스는 젊은 시절에 우연히 아테나[로마의 〈미네르바〉]가 목욕하는 것을 본 적이 있었다."], ["The goddess in her wrath deprived him of his sight, but afterwards relenting gave him in compensation the knowledge of future events.", "여신은 노하여 그의 시력을 빼앗았으나, 나중에 마음이 누그러져 그 보상으로 그에게 미래의 일을 아는 능력을 주었다."], ["When consulted by Eteocles, he declared that victory should fall to Thebes if Menœceus, the son of Creon, gave himself a voluntary victim.", "에테오클레스가 묻자, 그는 크레온의 아들 메노이케우스가 스스로를 제물로 바치면 승리가 테베에 돌아가리라고 선언했다."], ["The heroic youth, learning the response, threw away his life in the first encounter.", "그 영웅적인 젊은이는 그 답을 듣고 첫 교전에서 자신의 목숨을 내던졌다."]], [["The siege continued long, with various success.", "포위는 엎치락뒤치락하며 오래 이어졌다."], ["At length both hosts agreed that the brothers should decide their quarrel by single combat.", "마침내 양쪽 군대는 두 형제가 일대일 결투로 그들의 다툼을 결판내야 한다는 데 합의했다."], ["They fought and fell by each other’s hands.", "그들은 싸웠고 서로의 손에 쓰러졌다."], ["The armies then renewed the fight, and at last the invaders were forced to yield, and fled, leaving their dead unburied.", "그러자 양쪽 군대가 다시 싸움을 벌였고, 결국 침략자들이 물러설 수밖에 없게 되어, 자기들의 죽은 자들을 묻지 못한 채 달아났다."], ["Creon, the uncle of the fallen princes, now become king, caused Eteocles to be buried with distinguished honor, but suffered the body of Polynices to lie where it fell, forbidding every one on pain of death to give it burial.", "쓰러진 왕자들의 숙부인 크레온이 이제 왕이 되어, 에테오클레스는 각별한 영예로 매장하게 했으나, 폴리네이케스의 시신은 쓰러진 자리에 그대로 두게 하고는, 누구든 그것을 매장하면 죽음으로 다스리겠다며 매장을 금했다."]], [["Antigone, the sister of Polynices, heard with indignation the revolting edict which consigned her brother’s body to the dogs and vultures, depriving it of those rites which were considered essential to the repose of the dead.", "폴리네이케스의 누이 안티고네는 자기 오라비의 시신을 개들과 독수리들에게 내맡겨, 죽은 자의 안식에 필수적인 것으로 여겨지던 의례를 박탈하는 그 혐오스러운 칙령을 분개하며 들었다."], ["Unmoved by the dissuading counsel of an affectionate but timid sister, and unable to procure assistance, she determined to brave the hazard, and to bury the body with her own hands.", "애정 깊으나 소심한 자매의 만류하는 충고에도 흔들리지 않고, 또 도움을 구할 수도 없게 되자, 그녀는 위험을 무릅쓰고 자신의 손으로 시신을 묻기로 결심했다."], ["She was detected in the act, and Creon gave orders that she should be buried alive, as having deliberately set at naught the solemn edict of the city.", "그녀는 그 일을 하다가 발각되었고, 크레온은 그녀가 도성의 엄숙한 칙령을 일부러 무시한 것이라 하여 산 채로 매장하라는 명을 내렸다."], ["Her lover, Hæmon, the son of Creon, unable to avert her fate, would not survive her, and fell by his own hand.", "그녀의 연인이자 크레온의 아들인 하이몬은 그녀의 운명을 막을 수 없게 되자 그녀보다 더 살려 하지 않고 제 손으로 목숨을 끊었다."]], [["Antigone forms the subject of two fine tragedies of the Grecian poet Sophocles. Mrs.", "안티고네는 그리스 시인 소포클레스의 두 편의 빼어난 비극의 주제를 이룬다."], ["Jameson, in her “Characteristics of Women,” has compared her character with that of Cordelia, in Shakspeare’s “King Lear.”", "제임슨 부인은 그녀의 「여성의 특성」에서 안티고네의 성격을 셰익스피어의 「리어 왕」에 나오는 코딜리아의 성격과 비교했다."], ["The perusal of her remarks cannot fail to gratify our readers.", "그녀의 견해를 읽어 보면 우리 독자들은 틀림없이 흡족할 것이다."]], [["The following is the lamentation of Antigone over Œdipus, when death has at last relieved him from his sufferings:", "다음은 죽음이 마침내 오이디푸스를 그 고통에서 벗어나게 했을 때, 안티고네가 그를 두고 부르는 애도의 노래다."]], [["“Alas!", "\"아아!"], ["I only wished I might have died With my poor father; wherefore should I ask For longer life?", "나는 그저 가엾은 아버지와 함께 죽을 수 있었기를 바랐을 뿐. 어찌하여 내가 더 긴 삶을 구하겠는가?"], ["O, I was fond of misery with him; E’en what was most unlovely grew beloved When he was with me.", "오, 나는 그분과 함께라면 비참함조차 사랑했네. 가장 사랑스럽지 못한 것조차 사랑스러워졌으니 그분이 나와 함께 계셨을 때는."], ["O my dearest father, Beneath the earth now in deep darkness hid, Worn as thou wert with age, to me thou still Wast dear, and shalt be ever.”", "오 나의 가장 사랑하는 아버지, 이제 땅 밑 깊은 어둠 속에 숨으신 분, 세월에 그토록 지치셨으나, 나에게는 여전히 소중하셨고, 영원히 소중하시리라.\""], ["—Francklin’s Sophocles.", "─프랭클린이 옮긴 소포클레스."]], [["Penelope is another of those mythic heroines whose beauties were rather those of character and conduct than of person.", "페넬로페는 또 한 사람의 신화적 여걸로, 그 아름다움이 외모보다는 성품과 행실에 있었던 인물이다."], ["She was the daughter of Icarius, a Spartan prince.", "그녀는 스파르타의 왕자 이카리오스의 딸이었다."], ["Ulysses, king of Ithaca, sought her in marriage, and won her, over all competitors.", "이타카의 왕 오디세우스가 그녀에게 구혼하여, 모든 경쟁자를 제치고 그녀를 얻었다."], ["When the moment came for the bride to leave her father’s house, Icarius, unable to bear the thoughts of parting with his daughter, tried to persuade her to remain with him, and not accompany her husband to Ithaca.", "신부가 친정을 떠날 순간이 오자, 이카리오스는 딸과 헤어진다는 생각을 견디지 못하고 그녀에게 자기 곁에 머물면서 남편을 따라 이타카로 가지 말라고 설득하려 했다."], ["Ulysses gave Penelope her choice, to stay or go with him.", "오디세우스는 페넬로페에게 머물지 자신과 함께 갈지를 스스로 택하게 했다."], ["Penelope made no reply, but dropped her veil over her face.", "페넬로페는 아무 대답도 하지 않고 자기 얼굴 위로 베일을 드리웠다."], ["Icarius urged her no further, but when she was gone erected a statue to Modesty on the spot where they parted.", "이카리오스는 더는 그녀를 졸라 대지 않았으나, 그녀가 떠난 뒤 그들이 헤어진 그 자리에 정숙의 여신상을 세웠다."]], [["Ulysses and Penelope had not enjoyed their union more than a year when it was interrupted by the events which called Ulysses to the Trojan war.", "오디세우스와 페넬로페는 부부의 연을 일 년도 채 누리지 못한 채, 오디세우스를 트로이아 전쟁으로 불러낸 사건들로 인해 그 결합이 중단되었다."], ["During his long absence, and when it was doubtful whether he still lived, and highly improbable that he would ever return, Penelope was importuned by numerous suitors, from whom there seemed no refuge but in choosing one of them for her husband.", "그의 오랜 부재 동안, 그가 아직 살아 있는지조차 의심스럽고 결코 돌아오지 못할 것이 거의 확실해 보일 무렵, 페넬로페는 수많은 구혼자들에게 시달렸으니, 그들 가운데 하나를 남편으로 택하는 것 말고는 빠져나갈 길이 없어 보였다."], ["Penelope, however, employed every art to gain time, still hoping for Ulysses’ return.", "그러나 페넬로페는 여전히 오디세우스의 귀환을 바라며 시간을 벌기 위해 온갖 방책을 썼다."], ["One of her arts of delay was engaging in the preparation of a robe for the funeral canopy of Laertes, her husband’s father.", "그녀가 쓴 지연책 가운데 하나는 남편의 아버지 라에르테스의 장례용 덮개로 쓸 옷을 짜는 일에 매달리는 것이었다."], ["She pledged herself to make her choice among the suitors when the robe was finished.", "그녀는 그 옷이 다 완성되면 구혼자들 가운데에서 택하겠노라고 맹세했다."], ["During the day she worked at the robe, but in the night she undid the work of the day.", "낮 동안에는 옷을 짰으나, 밤이면 낮에 짠 것을 도로 풀어 헤쳤다."], ["This is the famous Penelope’s web, which is used as a proverbial expression for anything which is perpetually doing but never done.", "이것이 바로 그 유명한 페넬로페의 베틀로, 끊임없이 행해지나 결코 끝나지 않는 일을 가리키는 속담의 표현으로 쓰인다."], ["The rest of Penelope’s history will be told when we give an account of her husband’s adventures.", "페넬로페에 관한 나머지 이야기는 그녀의 남편의 모험을 설명할 때 들려주기로 하겠다."]], [["————", "————"]], [["# CHAPTER XXIV", "# CHAPTER XXIV"]], [["ORPHEUS AND EURYDICE—ARISTÆUS—AMPHION—LINUS—THAMYRIS—MARSYAS—MELAMPUS—MUSÆUS\nORPHEUS was the son of Apollo and the Muse Calliope.", "오르페우스는 아폴론과 무사 칼리오페 사이에서 난 아들이었다."], ["He was presented by his father with a Lyre and taught to play upon it, which he did to such perfection that nothing could withstand the charm of his music.", "그는 아버지에게서 리라를 선물로 받았고, 그것을 연주하는 법을 배워 너무도 완벽한 경지에 이르렀기에, 그 어떤 것도 그의 음악이 지닌 매력을 거스를 수 없었다."], ["Not only his fellow-mortals but wild beasts were softened by his strains, and gathering round him laid by their fierceness, and stood entranced with his lay.", "그와 같은 필멸자 동료들뿐만 아니라 사나운 짐승들마저 그의 가락에 마음이 누그러져, 그의 주위에 모여들어 사나움을 내려놓고 그의 노래에 넋을 잃은 채 서 있었다."], ["Nay, the very trees and rocks were sensible to the charm.", "아니, 나무와 바위까지도 그 매력을 느꼈다."], ["The former crowded round him and the latter relaxed somewhat of their hardness, softened by his notes.", "나무들은 그의 주위로 빽빽이 모여들었고, 바위들은 그의 가락에 누그러져 그 단단함을 다소 풀어 놓았다."]], [["Hymen had been called to bless with his presence the nuptials of Orpheus with Eurydice; but though he attended, he brought no happy omens with him.", "히멘이 초대를 받아, 오르페우스와 에우리디케의 혼례에 임석하여 축복을 내리기로 되어 있었다. 하지만 그는 참석하기는 했어도, 어떤 행복한 징조도 함께 가져오지 않았다."], ["His very torch smoked and brought tears into their eyes.", "그의 횃불마저 연기를 피워 두 사람의 눈에 눈물이 맺히게 했다."], ["In coincidence with such prognostics, Eurydice, shortly after her marriage, while wandering with the nymphs, her companions, was seen by the shepherd Aristæus, who was struck with her beauty and made advances to her.", "그런 불길한 조짐과 맞아떨어지듯, 에우리디케는 결혼한 지 얼마 지나지 않아 동무인 님프들과 함께 거닐던 중, 목동 아리스타이오스의 눈에 띄었다. 그는 그녀의 아름다움에 반해 그녀에게 다가갔다."], ["She fled, and in flying trod upon a snake in the grass, was bitten in the foot, and died.", "그녀는 도망치다가 풀밭에서 뱀을 밟았고, 발을 물려 죽고 말았다."], ["Orpheus sang his grief to all who breathed the upper air, both gods and men, and finding it all unavailing resolved to seek his wife in the regions of the dead.", "오르페우스는 천상의 공기를 들이쉬는 모든 이들에게, 신이든 인간이든 가리지 않고 자신의 슬픔을 노래로 불렀으나, 그 모든 것이 헛되다는 것을 깨닫고는 죽은 자들의 영역에서 아내를 찾기로 마음먹었다."], ["He descended by a cave situated on the side of the promontory of Tænarus and arrived at the Stygian realm.", "그는 타이나로스 곶 옆에 자리한 동굴을 통해 내려가 스틱스 강의 영역에 다다랐다."], ["He passed through crowds of ghosts and presented himself before the throne of Pluto and Proserpine.", "그는 망령들의 무리를 헤치고 나아가 플루톤과 페르세포네의 옥좌 앞에 모습을 드러냈다. 리라에 맞춰 말을 노래로 풀어내며 그는 이렇게 읊었다."], ["Accompanying the words with the lyre, he sung, “O deities of the underworld, to whom all we who live must come, hear my words, for they are true.", "\"오, 저승의 신들이시여, 살아 있는 우리 모두가 마침내 찾아와야 할 곳을 다스리시는 분들이시여, 제 말을 들어 주소서, 제 말은 진실이기 때문입니다."], ["I come not to spy out the secrets of Tartarus, nor to try my strength against the three-headed dog with snaky hair who guards the entrance.", "저는 타르타로스의 비밀을 엿보러 온 것도 아니요, 뱀 같은 머리털을 지닌 채 입구를 지키는 머리 셋 달린 개에게 제 힘을 시험해 보려고 온 것도 아닙니다."], ["I come to seek my wife, whose opening years the poisonous viper’s fang has brought to an untimely end.", "저는 제 아내를 찾으러 왔습니다. 한창 피어나던 그녀의 세월을 독을 품은 살무사의 송곳니가 때 이르게 끝장내고 말았습니다."], ["Love has led me here, Love, a god all powerful with us who dwell on the earth, and, if old traditions say true, not less so here.", "사랑이 저를 이리로 이끌었으니, 사랑이란 지상에 사는 우리에게 전능한 신이며, 옛 전승이 참되다면 이곳에서도 그 힘이 결코 덜하지 않을 것입니다."], ["I implore you by these abodes full of terror, these realms of silence and uncreated things, unite again the thread of Eurydice’s life.", "공포로 가득한 이 거처들에 걸고, 침묵과 아직 빚어지지 않은 것들의 이 영역들에 걸고 간청하오니, 에우리디케의 생명의 실을 다시 이어 주소서."], ["We all are destined to you, and sooner or later must pass to your domain.", "우리는 모두 그대들에게 정해진 몸이니, 머잖아 또는 언젠가는 그대들의 영토로 넘어가야 합니다."], ["She too, when she shall have filled her term of life, will rightly be yours.", "그녀 역시 제 수명을 다 채우고 나면 마땅히 그대들의 것이 될 것입니다."], ["But till then grant her to me, I beseech you.", "하지만 그때까지는 그녀를 제게 내어 주시기를 비나이다."], ["If you deny me I cannot return alone; you shall triumph in the death of us both.”", "그대들이 저를 거절하신다면 저는 홀로 돌아갈 수 없으니, 그대들은 우리 두 사람의 죽음으로 승리를 거두게 될 것입니다.\""]], [["As he sang these tender strains, the very ghosts shed tears.", "그가 이 애절한 가락을 노래하자, 망령들마저 눈물을 흘렸다."], ["Tantalus, in spite of his thirst, stopped for a moment his efforts for water, Ixion’s wheel stood still, the vulture ceased to tear the giant’s liver, the daughters of Danaüs rested from their task of drawing water in a sieve, and Sisyphus sat on his rock to listen.", "탄탈로스는 갈증에도 불구하고 물을 향한 몸부림을 잠시 멈추었고, 익시온의 수레바퀴는 멈춰 섰으며, 독수리는 거인의 간을 쪼는 것을 그쳤고, 다나오스의 딸들은 체로 물을 긷는 일을 쉬었으며, 시시포스는 그의 바위에 걸터앉아 귀를 기울였다."], ["Then for the first time, it is said, the cheeks of the Furies were wet with tears.", "그때 처음으로, 전하는 바에 따르면, 푸리아이의 뺨이 눈물로 젖었다고 한다."], ["Proserpine could not resist, and Pluto himself gave way.", "페르세포네도 마음이 흔들렸고, 플루톤마저 양보했다."], ["Eurydice was called.", "에우리디케가 불려 왔다."], ["She came from among the new-arrived ghosts, limping with her wounded foot.", "그녀는 새로 도착한 망령들 사이에서, 상처 입은 발을 절뚝거리며 걸어 나왔다."], ["Orpheus was permitted to take her away with him on one condition, that he should not turn around to look at her till they should have reached the upper air.", "오르페우스는 한 가지 조건을 걸고 그녀를 데려가도 좋다는 허락을 받았으니, 바로 천상의 공기에 다다르기 전까지는 돌아서서 그녀를 바라보아서는 안 된다는 것이었다."], ["Under this condition they proceeded on their way, he leading, she following, through passages dark and steep, in total silence, till they had nearly reached the outlet into the cheerful upper world, when Orpheus, in a moment of forgetfulness, to assure himself that she was still following, cast a glance behind him, when instantly she was borne away.", "이 조건 아래 두 사람은 길을 나아갔고, 그가 앞장서고 그녀가 뒤따르며, 어둡고 가파른 통로들을 완전한 침묵 속에서 지나, 마침내 즐거운 천상의 세계로 나서는 출구에 거의 다다랐을 무렵이었다. 그때 오르페우스가 깜빡 잊은 한순간, 그녀가 여전히 뒤따르고 있는지 스스로 확인하려고 뒤를 한 번 흘끗 돌아보았고, 그러자마자 그녀는 끌려가 버렸다."], ["Stretching out their arms to embrace each other, they grasped only the air!", "두 사람은 서로를 끌어안으려고 팔을 뻗었으나, 손에 잡힌 것은 허공뿐이었다!"], ["Dying now a second time, she yet cannot reproach her husband, for how can she blame his impatience to behold her?", "두 번째로 죽음을 맞으면서도, 그녀는 남편을 탓할 수가 없었으니, 자신을 보고 싶어 한 그의 조바심을 어찌 나무랄 수 있겠는가?"], ["“Farewell,”", "\"안녕히,\""], ["she said, “a last farewell,”—and was hurried away, so fast that the sound hardly reached his ears.", "그녀가 말했다, \"마지막 작별이에요.\" 그러고는 너무도 빨리 끌려가 버려, 그 소리가 그의 귀에 가까스로 닿았다."]], [["Orpheus endeavored to follow her, and besought permission to return and try once more for her release; but the stern ferryman repulsed him and refused passage.", "오르페우스는 그녀를 뒤따르려 애썼고, 다시 돌아가 한 번 더 그녀의 석방을 시도하게 해 달라고 간청했으나, 무뚝뚝한 뱃사공은 그를 밀쳐 내며 건너가는 것을 거부했다."], ["Seven days he lingered about the brink, without food or sleep; then bitterly accusing of cruelty the powers of Erebus, he sang his complaints to the rocks and mountains, melting the hearts of tigers and moving the oaks from their stations.", "그는 이레 동안 먹지도 자지도 않은 채 강가를 서성였다. 그러고는 에레보스의 신들이 무자비하다고 쓰라리게 책망하며, 바위와 산을 향해 자신의 한탄을 노래로 불러, 호랑이들의 마음을 녹이고 떡갈나무들을 제자리에서 움직이게 했다."], ["He held himself aloof from womankind, dwelling constantly on the recollection of his sad mischance.", "그는 여인들을 멀리하고, 자신의 슬픈 불운에 대한 기억에 끊임없이 잠겨 지냈다."], ["The Thracian maidens tried their best to captivate him, but he repulsed their advances.", "트라키아의 처녀들이 그를 사로잡으려고 갖은 애를 썼으나, 그는 그들의 접근을 물리쳤다."], ["They bore with him as long as they could; but finding him insensible one day, excited by the rites of Bacchus, one of them exclaimed, “See yonder our despiser!”", "처녀들은 견딜 수 있는 한 그를 참아 주었으나, 어느 날 그가 무덤덤한 것을 보고는, 디오니소스의 의식에 흥분한 그들 가운데 하나가 외쳤다. \"저기 우리를 멸시하는 자가 있다!\""], ["and threw at him her javelin.", "그러고는 그를 향해 투창을 던졌다."], ["The weapon, as soon as it came within the sound of his lyre, fell harmless at his feet.", "그 무기는 그의 리라 소리가 미치는 범위에 들어서자마자, 아무 해도 끼치지 못한 채 그의 발치에 떨어졌다."], ["So did also the stones that they threw at him.", "그들이 그에게 던진 돌멩이들도 마찬가지였다."], ["But the women raised a scream and drowned the voice of the music, and then the missiles reached him and soon were stained with his blood.", "하지만 여인들이 비명을 질러 음악의 소리를 묻어 버리자, 그제야 날아온 것들이 그에게 가닿았고, 이내 그의 피로 물들었다."], ["The maniacs tore him limb from limb, and threw his head and his lyre into the river Hebrus, down which they floated, murmuring sad music, to which the shores responded a plaintive symphony.", "그 광란하는 여인들은 그의 팔다리를 갈가리 찢어, 그의 머리와 리라를 헤브로스 강에 던져 넣었다. 그것들은 강을 따라 떠내려가며 구슬픈 음악을 흥얼거렸고, 강기슭은 애처로운 화음으로 화답했다."], ["The Muses gathered up the fragments of his body and buried them at Libethra, where the nightingale is said to sing over his grave more sweetly than in any other part of Greece.", "무사이는 그의 몸의 조각들을 거두어 리베트라에 묻었으니, 그곳에서는 나이팅게일이 그의 무덤 위에서 그리스의 다른 어느 곳에서보다 더 감미롭게 노래한다고 전해진다."], ["His lyre was placed by Jupiter among the stars.", "그의 리라는 유피테르에 의해 별들 사이에 놓였다."], ["His shade passed a second time to Tartarus, where he sought out his Eurydice and embraced her with eager arms.", "그의 혼령은 두 번째로 타르타로스로 건너갔고, 그곳에서 에우리디케를 찾아내어 간절한 팔로 그녀를 끌어안았다."], ["They roam the happy fields together now, sometimes he leading, sometimes she; and Orpheus gazes as much as he will upon her, no longer incurring a penalty for a thoughtless glance.", "그들은 이제 행복한 들판을 함께 거니는데, 때로는 그가 앞장서고 때로는 그녀가 앞장서며, 오르페우스는 마음껏 그녀를 바라보아도 이제 더는 무심한 시선에 대한 벌을 받지 않는다."]], [["The story of Orpheus has furnished Pope with an illustration of the power of music, for his “Ode for St. Cecilia’s Day.”", "오르페우스의 이야기는 포프에게 그의 \"성 케칠리아 축일의 송가\"를 위한 음악의 힘에 관한 예시를 제공했다."], ["The following stanza relates the conclusion of the story:", "다음 연은 이야기의 결말을 노래한다."]], [["“But soon, too soon the lover turns his eyes; Again she falls, again she dies, she dies!", "\"하지만 곧, 너무도 곧 연인은 그의 눈길을 돌리니, 그녀는 다시 쓰러지고, 다시 죽고, 또 죽는구나!"], ["How wilt thou now the fatal sisters move?", "이제 그대 어찌 운명의 자매들을 움직이려는가?"], ["No crime was thine, if ’tis no crime to love.", "그대에게는 죄가 없었으니, 사랑하는 것이 죄가 아니라면."], ["Now under hanging mountains, Beside the falls of fountains, Or where Hebrus wanders, Rolling in meanders, All alone, He makes his moan, And calls her ghost, Forever, ever, ever lost!", "이제 매달린 산 아래에서, 샘물이 떨어지는 곳 곁에서, 혹은 헤브로스가 굽이쳐 구비를 따라 흘러가는 곳에서, 홀로 외로이, 그는 비탄을 토하며, 그녀의 혼령을 부르노라, 영원히, 영원히, 영원히 잃은 그녀를!"], ["Now with furies surrounded, Despairing, confounded, He trembles, he glows, Amidst Rhodope’s snows.", "이제 푸리아이에 둘러싸여, 절망하고 혼미한 채, 그는 떨고, 그는 타오르노라, 로도페의 눈밭 한가운데서."], ["See, wild as the winds o’er the desert he flies; Hark!", "보라, 사막을 가로지르는 바람처럼 거칠게 그는 내달리고, 들으라!"], ["Hæmus resounds with the Bacchanals’ cries; Ah, see, he dies!", "하이모스가 바카날의 외침으로 메아리치는구나. 아, 보라, 그가 죽는구나!"], ["Yet even in death Eurydice he sung, Eurydice still trembled on his tongue: Eurydice the woods Eurydice the floods Eurydice the rocks and hollow mountains rung.”", "하나 죽음 속에서도 그는 에우리디케를 노래했으니, 에우리디케가 여전히 그의 혀끝에서 떨렸도다. 에우리디케 하고 숲이, 에우리디케 하고 강물이, 에우리디케 하고 바위와 텅 빈 산들이 울려 퍼졌도다.\""]], [["The superior melody of the nightingale’s song over the grave of Orpheus is alluded to by Southey in his “Thalaba”:", "오르페우스의 무덤 위에서 나이팅게일의 노래가 한층 빼어나다는 사실은 사우디가 그의 \"탈라바\"에서 넌지시 언급한 바 있다."]], [["“Then on his ear what sounds Of harmony arose!", "\"그때 그의 귀에 어떤 화음의 소리가 솟아올랐던가!"], ["Far music and the distance-mellowed song From bowers of merriment; The waterfall remote; The murmuring of the leafy groves; The single nightingale Perched in the rosier by, so richly toned, That never from that most melodious bird Singing a love song to his brooding mate, Did Thracian shepherd by the grave Of Orpheus hear a sweeter melody, Though there the spirit of the sepulchre All his own power infuse, to swell The incense that he loves.”", "먼 음악과 거리로 무르익은 노래가 흥겨운 나무 그늘에서 들려오고, 아득한 폭포 소리, 잎 무성한 숲의 속삭임, 장미 덤불에 앉은 한 마리 나이팅게일은 어찌나 풍성한 가락을 내던지, 알을 품은 짝에게 사랑 노래를 부르는 그 가장 선율 고운 새의 노랫소리보다, 오르페우스의 무덤 곁에서 트라키아의 목동이 들은 더 감미로운 가락은 없었으리라, 비록 그곳에서 무덤의 정령이 자신의 온갖 힘을 불어넣어, 그가 아끼는 향연을 부풀린다 하더라도.\""]], [["Man avails himself of the instincts of the inferior animals for his own advantage.", "인간은 자신의 이로움을 위해 하등 동물들의 본능을 이용한다."], ["Hence sprang the art of keeping bees.", "양봉의 기술이 여기서 비롯되었다."], ["Honey must first have been known as a wild product, the bees building their structures in hollow trees or holes in the rocks, or any similar cavity that chance offered.", "꿀은 처음에는 야생의 산물로 알려졌을 것이 틀림없으니, 벌들은 속이 빈 나무나 바위의 구멍, 또는 우연히 마련된 비슷한 빈 공간에 그들의 구조물을 지었다."], ["Thus occasionally the carcass of a dead animal would be occupied by the bees for that purpose.", "그리하여 때로는 죽은 동물의 사체가 그러한 목적으로 벌들에게 차지되기도 했다."], ["It was no doubt from some such incident that the superstition arose that the bees were engendered by the decaying flesh of the animal; and Virgil, in the following story, shows how this supposed fact may be turned to account for renewing the swarm when it has been lost by disease or accident:", "죽은 짐승의 썩어 가는 살에서 벌들이 생겨난다는 미신이 일어난 것은 의심할 여지 없이 이러한 사건에서 비롯되었다. 그리고 베르길리우스는 다음 이야기에서, 질병이나 사고로 벌떼를 잃었을 때 그것을 되살리는 데 이 추정된 사실을 어떻게 활용할 수 있는지를 보여 준다."]], [["Aristæus, who first taught the management of bees, was the son of the water-nymph Cyrene.", "벌을 다루는 법을 처음 가르친 아리스타이오스는 물의 님프 키레네의 아들이었다."], ["His bees had perished, and he resorted for aid to his mother.", "그의 벌들이 죽어 버리자, 그는 도움을 구하러 어머니에게 의지했다."], ["He stood at the river-side and thus addressed her: “O mother, the pride of my life is taken from me!", "그는 강가에 서서 어머니에게 이렇게 호소했다. \"오 어머니, 제 삶의 자랑거리가 제게서 떠나가 버렸습니다!"], ["I have lost my precious bees.", "제 소중한 벌들을 잃었습니다."], ["My care and skill have availed me nothing, and you my mother have not warded off from me the blow of misfortune.”", "제 정성과 솜씨도 제게 아무 소용이 없었고, 어머니마저 제게서 불운의 타격을 막아 주지 못하셨습니다.\""], ["His mother heard these complaints as she sat in her palace at the bottom of the river, with her attendant nymphs around her.", "그의 어머니는 강 밑바닥의 궁전에 앉아, 시중드는 님프들을 곁에 두고 이러한 한탄을 들었다."], ["They were engaged in female occupations, spinning and weaving, while one told stories to amuse the rest.", "그들은 실을 잣고 천을 짜는 여인의 일에 매여 있었고, 그동안 하나가 나머지를 즐겁게 해 주려고 이야기를 들려주고 있었다."], ["The sad voice of Aristæus interrupting their occupation, one of them put her head above the water and seeing him, returned and gave information to his mother, who ordered that he should be brought into her presence.", "아리스타이오스의 슬픈 목소리가 그들의 일을 가로막자, 그들 가운데 하나가 물 위로 머리를 내밀고 그를 보고는, 되돌아가 그의 어머니에게 알렸고, 어머니는 그를 자신의 앞으로 데려오라고 명했다."], ["The river at her command opened itself and let him pass in, while it stood curled like a mountain on either side.", "그녀의 명에 따라 강물이 스스로 갈라져 그를 들여보냈으니, 강물은 양옆으로 산처럼 굽이쳐 솟았다."], ["He descended to the region where the fountains of the great rivers lie; he saw the enormous receptacles of waters and was almost deafened with the roar, while he surveyed them hurrying off in various directions to water the face of the earth.", "그는 큰 강들의 샘이 자리한 영역으로 내려갔다. 그는 어마어마한 물의 저장소들을 보았고, 그것들이 대지의 표면을 적시려고 사방으로 황급히 흩어져 가는 모습을 둘러보는 동안 그 굉음에 거의 귀가 먹먹할 지경이었다."], ["Arriving at his mother’s apartment, he was hospitably received by Cyrene and her nymphs, who spread their table with the richest dainties.", "어머니의 거처에 다다르자, 그는 키레네와 그녀의 님프들에게 융숭한 대접을 받았으니, 그들은 더없이 풍성한 진미로 식탁을 차렸다."], ["They first poured out libations to Neptune, then regaled themselves with the feast, and after that Cyrene thus addressed him: “There is an old prophet named Proteus, who dwells in the sea and is a favorite of Neptune, whose herd of sea-calves he pastures.", "그들은 먼저 넵투누스에게 헌주를 올린 다음, 그 잔치를 마음껏 즐겼고, 그 뒤에 키레네가 그에게 이렇게 말했다. \"프로테우스라는 이름의 늙은 예언자가 있는데, 그는 바다에 살며 넵투누스의 총애를 받아 그의 바다표범 떼를 친단다."], ["We nymphs hold him in great respect, for he is a learned sage and knows all things, past, present, and to come.", "우리 님프들은 그를 크게 존경하니, 그는 박식한 현인이며 과거와 현재와 미래의 모든 것을 알고 있기 때문이지."], ["He can tell you, my son, the cause of the mortality among your bees, and how you may remedy it.", "그는 네게, 내 아들아, 네 벌들 사이에 죽음이 닥친 원인과 네가 그것을 어떻게 바로잡을 수 있는지를 일러 줄 수 있을 게다."], ["But he will not do it voluntarily, however you may entreat him.", "하지만 네가 아무리 간청해도 그는 자진해서는 그리해 주지 않을 게야."], ["You must compel him by force.", "너는 그를 힘으로 강제해야 한다."], ["If you seize him and chain him, he will answer your questions in order to get released, for he cannot by all his arts get away if you hold fast the chains.", "네가 그를 붙잡아 사슬로 묶으면, 그는 풀려나기 위해 네 물음에 차례차례 답할 게다. 네가 사슬을 단단히 붙들고 있으면 그는 온갖 재주를 부려도 빠져나갈 수 없기 때문이지."], ["I will carry you to his cave, where he comes at noon to take his midday repose.", "내가 너를 그의 동굴로 데려다주마. 그는 정오에 그곳으로 와서 한낮의 휴식을 취한단다."], ["Then you may easily secure him.", "그러면 너는 손쉽게 그를 사로잡을 수 있을 게야."], ["But when he finds himself captured, his resort is to a power he possesses of changing himself into various forms.", "하지만 그는 자신이 붙잡혔음을 알게 되면, 자신이 지닌 능력에 의지해 여러 모습으로 변신할 것이다."], ["He will become a wild boar or a fierce tiger, a scaly dragon or lion with yellow mane.", "그는 멧돼지나 사나운 호랑이, 비늘 돋친 용이나 누런 갈기를 지닌 사자가 될 게다."], ["Or he will make a noise like the crackling of flames or the rush of water, so as to tempt you to let go the chain, when he will make his escape.", "또는 불길이 타닥거리는 소리나 물이 솟구치는 소리를 내어, 네가 사슬을 놓도록 꾀어내고는 그 틈에 달아날 게야."], ["But you have only to keep him fast bound, and at last when he finds all his arts unavailing, he will return to his own figure and obey your commands.”", "하지만 너는 그저 그를 단단히 묶어 두기만 하면 되니, 마침내 그가 자신의 온갖 재주가 헛되다는 것을 알게 되면, 제 본래 모습으로 돌아와 네 명령에 따를 게다.\""], ["So saying she sprinkled her son with fragrant nectar, the beverage of the gods, and immediately an unusual vigor filled his frame, and courage his heart, while perfume breathed all around him.", "이렇게 말하며 그녀는 아들에게 향기로운 넥타르, 곧 신들의 음료를 뿌렸고, 그러자 곧바로 예사롭지 않은 활력이 그의 몸을 가득 채우고 용기가 그의 마음을 채웠으며, 향기가 그의 주위에 온통 감돌았다."]], [["The nymph led her son to the prophet’s cave and concealed him among the recesses of the rocks, while she herself took her place behind the clouds.", "님프는 아들을 예언자의 동굴로 데려가 바위틈에 숨겨 두었고, 그녀 자신은 구름 뒤에 자리를 잡았다."], ["When noon came and the hour when men and herds retreat from the glaring sun to indulge in quiet slumber, Proteus issued from the water, followed by his herd of sea-calves which spread themselves along the shore.", "정오가 되어, 사람도 가축도 이글거리는 태양을 피해 조용한 잠에 잠기는 시각이 오자, 프로테우스가 물속에서 나왔고, 그를 뒤따라 바다표범 떼가 해안을 따라 흩어졌다."], ["He sat on the rock and counted his herd; then stretched himself on the floor of the cave and went to sleep.", "그는 바위에 앉아 자신의 떼를 헤아렸고, 그런 다음 동굴 바닥에 몸을 뉘여 잠이 들었다."], ["Aristæus hardly allowed him to get fairly asleep before he fixed the fetters on him and shouted aloud.", "아리스타이오스는 그가 채 깊이 잠들기도 전에 그에게 족쇄를 채우고 큰 소리로 고함을 질렀다."], ["Proteus, waking and finding himself captured, immediately resorted to his arts, becoming first a fire, then a flood, then a horrible wild beast, in rapid succession.", "프로테우스는 잠에서 깨어나 자신이 붙잡혔음을 알고는, 곧장 자신의 재주에 의지하여, 빠르게 잇달아 처음에는 불이 되고, 다음에는 홍수가 되고, 그다음에는 끔찍한 들짐승이 되었다."], ["But finding all would not do, he at last resumed his own form and addressed the youth in angry accents: “Who are you, bold youth, who thus invade my abode, and what do you want of me?”", "하지만 모든 것이 소용없음을 알고는, 마침내 제 본래 모습을 되찾아 성난 어조로 젊은이에게 말했다. \"대담한 젊은이여, 이렇게 내 거처를 침범하는 너는 누구이며, 내게서 무엇을 바라느냐?\""], ["Aristæus replied, “Proteus, you know already, for it is needless for any one to attempt to deceive you.", "아리스타이오스가 대답했다. \"프로테우스여, 그대는 이미 알고 있으니, 누구든 그대를 속이려 드는 것은 부질없는 일이기 때문이오."], ["And do you also cease your efforts to elude me.", "그러니 그대도 나를 따돌리려는 노력을 그치시오."], ["I am led hither by divine assistance, to know from you the cause of my misfortune and how to remedy it.”", "나는 신의 도움에 이끌려 이리로 왔으니, 내 불운의 원인과 그것을 바로잡는 법을 그대에게서 알기 위함이오.\" 이 말에 예언자는 그를 향해 잿빛 눈을 꿰뚫는 듯한 시선으로 고정하고는 이렇게 말했다."], ["At these words the prophet, fixing on him his gray eyes with a piercing look, thus spoke: “You receive the merited reward of your deeds, by which Eurydice met her death, for in flying from you she trod upon a serpent, of whose bite she died.", "\"너는 네 행위의 마땅한 대가를 치르는 것이다. 그 행위로 인해 에우리디케가 죽음을 맞았으니, 그녀는 너를 피해 달아나다가 뱀을 밟았고, 그 뱀에게 물려 죽었기 때문이다."], ["To avenge her death, the nymphs, her companions, have sent this destruction to your bees.", "그녀의 죽음을 복수하기 위해, 그녀의 동무인 님프들이 네 벌들에게 이 파멸을 보낸 것이다."], ["You have to appease their anger, and thus it must be done: Select four bulls, of perfect form and size, and four cows of equal beauty, build four altars to the nymphs, and sacrifice the animals, leaving their carcasses in the leafy grove.", "너는 그들의 분노를 달래야 하니, 이렇게 해야 한다. 완벽한 모양과 크기를 지닌 황소 네 마리와 그에 못지않게 아름다운 암소 네 마리를 골라, 님프들에게 제단 네 개를 쌓고, 그 짐승들을 제물로 바치며, 그 사체들을 잎 무성한 숲에 남겨 두어라."], ["To Orpheus and Eurydice you shall pay such funeral honors as may allay their resentment.", "오르페우스와 에우리디케에게는 그들의 원한을 가라앉힐 만한 장례의 예를 올려라."], ["Returning after nine days, you will examine the bodies of the cattle slain and see what will befall.”", "아흐레가 지난 뒤에 돌아와, 죽인 가축들의 몸뚱이를 살펴보면 무슨 일이 벌어질지 알게 될 것이다.\""], ["Aristæus faithfully obeyed these directions.", "아리스타이오스는 이 지시를 충실히 따랐다."], ["He sacrificed the cattle, he left their bodies in the grove, he offered funeral honors to the shades of Orpheus and Eurydice; then returning on the ninth day he examined the bodies of the animals, and, wonderful to relate!", "그는 가축들을 제물로 바쳤고, 그 몸뚱이를 숲에 남겨 두었으며, 오르페우스와 에우리디케의 혼령에게 장례의 예를 올렸다. 그런 다음 아흐레째 되는 날에 돌아와 그 짐승들의 몸뚱이를 살펴보니, 놀랍게도!"], ["a swarm of bees had taken possession of one of the carcasses and were pursuing their labors there as in a hive.", "벌떼 한 무리가 사체 가운데 하나를 차지하고는, 마치 벌집에서처럼 그곳에서 일을 이어 가고 있었다."]], [["In “The Task,” Cowper alludes to the story of Aristæus, when speaking of the ice-palace built by the Empress Anne of Russia.", "쿠퍼는 \"과제\"에서, 러시아의 여제 안나가 지은 얼음 궁전을 이야기하면서 아리스타이오스의 이야기를 넌지시 언급한다."], ["He has been describing the fantastic forms which ice assumes in connection with waterfalls, etc.:", "그는 폭포 따위와 관련하여 얼음이 취하는 기이한 형상들을 묘사하던 참이었다."]], [["“Less worthy of applause though more admired Because a novelty, the work of man, Imperial mistress of the fur-clad Russ, Thy most magnificent and mighty freak, The wonder of the north.", "\"그보다 더 찬탄을 받지만 갈채를 받을 자격은 덜한, 새로운 것이기에 그러한, 인간의 작품이여, 털옷 두른 러시아인의 황실 여주인이여, 그대의 더없이 장엄하고 위풍당당한 변덕, 북방의 경이로다."], ["No forest fell When thou wouldst build, no quarry sent its stores T’ enrich thy walls; but thou didst hew the floods And make thy marble of the glassy wave.", "그대가 지으려 할 때 숲이 베어 넘어진 것도 아니요, 채석장이 그 보물을 내어 그대의 벽을 풍성히 한 것도 아니라, 그대는 강물을 깎아 유리 같은 물결로 그대의 대리석을 빚었도다."], ["In such a palace Aristæus found Cyrene, when he bore the plaintive tale Of his lost bees to her maternal ear.”", "그러한 궁전에서 아리스타이오스는 키레네를 찾아내었으니, 그가 잃어버린 벌들의 가련한 사연을 어머니의 귀에 전하던 그때였다.\""]], [["Milton also appears to have had Cyrene and her domestic scene in his mind when he describes to us Sabrina, the nymph of the river Severn, in the Guardian-spirit’s Song in “Comus”:", "밀턴 또한 \"코머스\"에서 수호 정령의 노래로 세번 강의 님프 사브리나를 우리에게 묘사할 때, 키레네와 그녀의 거처 정경을 마음에 두고 있었던 것으로 보인다."]], [["“Sabrina fair!", "\"아름다운 사브리나여!"], ["Listen where thou art sitting Under the glassy, cool, translucent wave In twisted braids of lilies knitting The loose train of thy amber-dropping hair; Listen for dear honor’s sake, Goddess of the silver lake!", "그대 앉아 있는 곳에서 들으라, 유리처럼 맑고 서늘하며 투명한 물결 아래에서, 나리꽃을 비틀어 엮은 머리띠로 호박빛 방울 듣는 그대 풀어 헤친 머리채를 매면서. 소중한 명예를 위하여 들으라, 은빛 호수의 여신이여!"], ["Listen and save.”", "들으라, 그리고 구하라.\""]], [["The following are other celebrated mythical poets and musicians, some of whom were hardly inferior to Orpheus himself:", "다음은 그 밖의 이름난 신화 속 시인과 음악가들로서, 그들 가운데 일부는 오르페우스 자신에게도 거의 뒤지지 않았다."]], [["Amphion was the son of Jupiter and Antiope, queen of Thebes.", "암피온은 유피테르와 테바이의 왕비 안티오페 사이에서 난 아들이었다."], ["With his twin brother Zethus he was exposed at birth on Mount Cithæron, where they grew up among the shepherds, not knowing their parentage.", "그는 쌍둥이 형제 제토스와 함께 태어나자마자 키타이론 산에 버려졌고, 그곳에서 자신들의 혈통도 모른 채 목동들 틈에서 자랐다."], ["Mercury gave Amphion a lyre and taught him to play upon it, and his brother occupied himself in hunting and tending the flocks.", "메르쿠리우스가 암피온에게 리라를 주고 그것을 연주하는 법을 가르쳤으며, 그의 형제는 사냥을 하고 가축을 돌보는 일에 몰두했다."], ["Meanwhile Antiope, their mother, who had been treated with great cruelty by Lycus, the usurping king of Thebes, and by Dirce, his wife, found means to inform her children of their rights and to summon them to her assistance.", "그러는 동안 그들의 어머니 안티오페는 테바이의 왕위를 찬탈한 리코스와 그의 아내 디르케에게 모진 학대를 받아 오던 터라, 자식들에게 그들의 권리를 알리고 자신을 도우러 오라고 부를 방도를 찾아냈다."], ["With a band of their fellow-herdsmen they attacked and slew Lycus, and tying Dirce by the hair of her head to a bull, let him drag her till she was dead.", "그들은 함께 가축을 치던 동료들로 무리를 이루어 리코스를 공격해 죽였고, 디르케는 그 머리채를 황소에 묶어, 그녀가 죽을 때까지 황소가 끌고 다니게 했다."], ["Amphion, having become king of Thebes, fortified the city with a wall.", "테바이의 왕이 된 암피온은 성벽으로 도시를 둘렀다."], ["It is said that when he played on his lyre the stones moved of their own accord and took their places in the wall.", "그가 리라를 연주하면 돌들이 저절로 움직여 성벽 안의 제자리를 잡았다고 전해진다."]], [["See Tennyson’s poem of “Amphion” for an amusing use made of this story.", "이 이야기를 흥미롭게 활용한 예로는 테니슨의 시 \"암피온\"을 보라."]], [["Linus was the instructor of Hercules in music, but having one day reproved his pupil rather harshly, he roused the anger of Hercules, who struck him with his lyre and killed him.", "리노스는 헤라클레스에게 음악을 가르친 스승이었으나, 어느 날 제자를 다소 가혹하게 나무란 일이 헤라클레스의 분노를 자아냈고, 헤라클레스는 그를 리라로 내리쳐 죽이고 말았다."]], [["An ancient Thracian bard, who in his presumption challenged the Muses to a trial of skill, and being overcome in the contest, was deprived by them of his sight.", "트라키아의 옛 음유시인으로, 그는 오만하게도 무사이에게 솜씨를 겨루자고 도전했다가, 그 겨룸에서 패하여 그들에게 시력을 빼앗겼다."], ["Milton alludes to him with other blind bards, when speaking of his own blindness, “Paradise Lost,”", "밀턴은 자신의 실명에 관해 이야기하며 다른 눈먼 음유시인들과 함께 그를 넌지시 언급한다. \"실낙원\""], ["Book III., 35.", "제3권 35행이다."]], [["Minerva invented the flute, and played upon it to the delight of all the celestial auditors; but the mischievous urchin Cupid having dared to laugh at the queer face which the goddess made while playing, Minerva threw the instrument indignantly away, and it fell down to earth, and was found by Marsyas.", "미네르바가 피리를 발명하여, 천상의 모든 청중을 기쁘게 하며 그것을 연주했다. 하지만 짓궂은 개구쟁이 쿠피도가 감히 그 여신이 연주하는 동안 짓는 우스꽝스러운 표정을 비웃자, 미네르바는 분개하여 그 악기를 멀리 내던졌고, 그것은 땅으로 떨어져 마르시아스에게 발견되었다."], ["He blew upon it, and drew from it such ravishing sounds that he was tempted to challenge Apollo himself to a musical contest.", "그가 그것을 불자 너무도 황홀한 소리가 흘러나왔기에, 그는 아폴론에게마저 음악 겨루기를 도전하고 싶은 유혹에 사로잡혔다."], ["The god of course triumphed, and punished Marsyas by flaying him alive.", "물론 신이 승리를 거두었고, 마르시아스에게는 산 채로 가죽을 벗기는 벌을 내렸다."]], [["Melampus was the first mortal endowed with prophetic powers.", "멜람푸스는 예언의 능력을 부여받은 최초의 필멸자였다."], ["Before his house there stood an oak tree containing a serpent’s nest.", "그의 집 앞에는 뱀의 둥지가 든 떡갈나무 한 그루가 서 있었다."], ["The old serpents were killed by the servants, but Melampus took care of the young ones and fed them carefully.", "늙은 뱀들은 하인들에게 죽임을 당했으나, 멜람푸스는 새끼들을 돌보며 정성껏 먹였다."], ["One day when he was asleep under the oak the serpents licked his ears with their tongues.", "어느 날 그가 떡갈나무 아래에서 잠들어 있을 때, 뱀들이 혀로 그의 귀를 핥았다."], ["On awaking he was astonished to find that he now understood the language of birds and creeping things.", "깨어나 보니 그는 이제 새들과 기는 것들의 말을 알아듣게 되었음을 알고 놀랐다."], ["This knowledge enabled him to foretell future events, and he became a renowned soothsayer.", "이 능력 덕분에 그는 앞일을 예언할 수 있게 되었고, 이름난 점쟁이가 되었다."], ["At one time his enemies took him captive and kept him strictly imprisoned.", "한번은 그의 적들이 그를 사로잡아 엄중히 가두었다."], ["Melampus in the silence of the night heard the woodworms in the timbers talking together, and found out by what they said that the timbers were nearly eaten through and the roof would soon fall in.", "멜람푸스는 밤의 고요 속에서 목재 속의 나무좀들이 서로 주고받는 말을 들었고, 그들이 하는 말로 미루어 목재가 거의 다 갉아 먹혀 머잖아 지붕이 무너지리라는 것을 알아냈다."], ["He told his captors and demanded to be let out, warning them also.", "그는 자신을 가둔 자들에게 이를 일러 주며 풀어 달라고 요구하고, 그들에게도 경고했다."], ["They took his warning, and thus escaped destruction, and rewarded Melampus and held him in high honor.", "그들은 그의 경고를 받아들여 파멸을 면했고, 멜람푸스에게 보답하며 그를 높이 받들었다."]], [["A semi-mythological personage who was represented by one tradition to be the son of Orpheus.", "전설이 절반쯤 섞인 인물로, 어느 전승에서는 오르페우스의 아들로 일컬어졌다."], ["He is said to have written sacred poems and oracles.", "그는 신성한 시와 신탁을 지었다고 전해진다."], ["Milton couples his name with that of Orpheus in his “Il Penseroso”:", "밀턴은 자신의 \"사색하는 사람\"에서 그의 이름을 오르페우스의 이름과 나란히 둔다."]], [["“But O, sad virgin, that thy power Might raise Musæus from his bower, Or bid the soul of Orpheus sing Such notes as warbled to the string, Drew iron tears down Pluto’s cheek, And made Hell grant what love did seek.” ————", "\"하나 오, 슬픈 처녀여, 그대의 힘이 무사이오스를 그의 나무 그늘에서 일으켜 세우거나, 오르페우스의 영혼더러 그러한 가락을 노래하게 하여, 그것이 현(絃)에 어우러져 떨려 나와, 플루톤의 뺨에 무쇠 같은 눈물을 흘리게 하고, 사랑이 구하던 것을 지옥이 허락하게 했으면.\""]], [["THE poets whose adventures compose this chapter were real persons some of whose works yet remain, and their influence on poets who succeeded them is yet more important than their poetical remains.", "이 장의 모험을 이루는 시인들은 실존 인물이었으며, 그들의 작품 가운데 일부는 오늘날까지도 남아 있다. 그리고 후대의 시인들에게 끼친 그들의 영향력은 그들이 남긴 시작품 자체보다도 더욱 중요하다."], ["The adventures recorded of them in the following stories rest on the same authority as other narratives of the “Age of Fable,” that is, of the poets who have told them.", "다음 이야기들에 기록된 그들의 모험은 「신화의 시대」의 다른 서사들과 같은 권위, 즉 그것을 들려준 시인들의 권위에 의존하고 있다."], ["In their present form, the first two are translated from the German, Arion from Schlegel, and Ibycus from Schiller.", "지금의 형태로 보자면, 앞의 두 이야기는 독일어에서 번역한 것으로, 아리온은 슐레겔에게서, 이비코스는 실러에게서 가져온 것이다. 아리온"]], [["Arion was a famous musician, and dwelt in the court of Periander, king of Corinth, with whom he was a great favorite.", "아리온은 이름난 음악가였고, 코린토스의 왕 페리안드로스의 궁정에 거처하면서 그의 크나큰 총애를 받았다."], ["There was to be a musical contest in Sicily, and Arion longed to compete for the prize.", "시켈리아[시칠리아]에서 음악 경연이 열릴 예정이었고, 아리온은 그 상을 두고 겨루기를 간절히 바랐다."], ["He told his wish to Periander, who besought him like a brother to give up the thought.", "그가 자신의 소망을 페리안드로스에게 말하자, 왕은 형제처럼 그 생각을 접으라고 간청했다."], ["“Pray stay with me,”", "\"부디 나와 함께 머물러 주게.\""], ["he said, “and be contented.", "왕이 말했다. \"그리고 만족하게나."], ["He who strives to win may lose.”", "이기려 애쓰는 자는 잃을 수도 있는 법이라네.\""], ["Arion answered, “A wandering life best suits the free heart of a poet.", "아리온이 대답했다. \"떠도는 삶이야말로 시인의 자유로운 마음에 가장 잘 어울리는 것입니다."], ["The talent which a god bestowed on me, I would fain make a source of pleasure to others.", "신께서 제게 내려 주신 재능을 저는 기꺼이 다른 이들에게 기쁨의 원천으로 삼고 싶습니다."], ["And if I win the prize, how will the enjoyment of it be increased by the consciousness of my widespread fame!”", "그리고 만약 제가 상을 탄다면, 저의 드넓은 명성을 자각하는 일로 그 기쁨이 얼마나 더 커지겠습니까!\""], ["He went, won the prize, and embarked with his wealth in a Corinthian ship for home.", "그는 떠났고, 상을 탔으며, 자신의 재물과 함께 코린토스 배에 올라 고향으로 향했다."], ["On the second morning after setting sail, the wind breathed mild and fair.", "출항한 뒤 둘째 날 아침, 바람은 부드럽고 순하게 불었다. \"오 페리안드로스여.\""], ["“O Periander,”", "그가 외쳤다."], ["he exclaimed, “dismiss your fears!", "\"당신의 두려움을 떨치소서!"], ["Soon shall you forget them in my embrace.", "머지않아 제 품에 안겨 그것을 잊으시게 될 것입니다."], ["With what lavish offerings will we display our gratitude to the gods, and how merry will we be at the festal board!”", "우리는 얼마나 아낌없는 제물을 바쳐 신들께 감사를 드러낼 것이며, 잔칫상 앞에서 얼마나 즐거워할 것인지요!\""], ["The wind and sea continued propitious.", "바람과 바다는 계속해서 순조로웠다."], ["Not a cloud dimmed the firmament.", "구름 한 점 창공을 흐리지 않았다."], ["He had not trusted too much to the ocean—but he had to man.", "그는 바다를 지나치게 믿지는 않았으나, 사람은 지나치게 믿었던 것이다."], ["He overheard the seamen exchanging hints with one another, and found they were plotting to possess themselves of his treasure.", "그는 뱃사람들이 서로 은밀히 신호를 주고받는 것을 엿듣게 되었고, 그들이 자신의 보물을 차지하려 음모를 꾸미고 있음을 알아챘다."], ["Presently they surrounded him loud and mutinous, and said, “Arion, you must die!", "이윽고 그들은 시끄럽고 반항적으로 그를 둘러싸고 말했다. \"아리온, 너는 죽어야 한다!"], ["If you would have a grave on shore, yield yourself to die on this spot; but if otherwise, cast yourself into the sea.”", "만약 뭍에 무덤을 갖고자 한다면 이 자리에서 죽음에 몸을 맡겨라. 그러나 그것이 싫다면 바다로 몸을 던져라.\""], ["“Will nothing satisfy you but my life?”", "\"내 목숨 말고는 너희를 만족시킬 것이 없단 말이냐?\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Take my gold, and welcome.", "\"내 황금을 가져가거라, 기꺼이 주겠다."], ["I willingly buy my life at that price.”", "나는 그 값으로 기꺼이 내 목숨을 사겠다.\" \"안 된다, 안 돼."], ["“No, no; we cannot spare you.", "우리는 너를 살려 둘 수 없다."], ["Your life would be too dangerous to us.", "너의 목숨은 우리에게 너무도 위험하다."], ["Where could we go to escape from Periander, if he should know that you had been robbed by us?", "만약 페리안드로스가 네가 우리에게 약탈당했음을 알게 된다면, 우리가 어디로 가야 그의 손길을 피할 수 있겠느냐?"], ["Your gold would be of little use to us, if on returning home, we could never more be free from fear.”", "고향에 돌아가 영영 두려움에서 벗어날 수 없다면, 네 황금이 우리에게 무슨 소용이 있겠느냐.\" \"그렇다면.\""], ["“Grant me, then,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “a last request, since nought will avail to save my life, that I may die, as I have lived, as becomes a bard.", "\"내 목숨을 구할 길이 전혀 없으니, 마지막 청을 하나 들어다오. 내가 살아온 대로, 음유시인답게 죽게 해다오."], ["When I shall have sung my death song, and my harp-strings shall have ceased to vibrate, then I will bid farewell to life, and yield uncomplaining to my fate.”", "내가 죽음의 노래를 다 부르고 나서 내 하프 줄이 더는 울리지 않게 되면, 그때 나는 삶에 작별을 고하고 불평 없이 내 운명에 몸을 맡기겠다.\" 다른 청들과 마찬가지로 이 기도 또한 무시되었을 터였다."], ["This prayer, like the others, would have been unheeded,—they thought only of their booty,—but to hear so famous a musician, that moved their rude hearts.", "그들은 오직 자신들의 약탈물만을 생각하고 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 그토록 이름난 음악가의 노래를 듣는다는 것, 그것이 그들의 거친 마음을 움직였다."], ["“Suffer me,”", "\"한 가지 더.\""], ["he added, “to arrange my dress.", "그가 덧붙였다. \"내 옷매무새를 다듬게 해다오."], ["Apollo will not favor me unless I be clad in my minstrel garb.”", "음유시인의 복장을 갖추지 않으면 아폴론께서 나를 돌보지 않으실 것이다.\""]], [["He clothed his well-proportioned limbs in gold and purple fair to see, his tunic fell around him in graceful folds, jewels adorned his arms, his brow was crowned with a golden wreath, and over his neck and shoulders flowed his hair perfumed with odors.", "그는 균형 잡힌 사지를 보기에 아름다운 황금과 자줏빛 옷으로 감쌌고, 그의 겉옷은 우아한 주름을 이루며 그의 몸을 따라 흘러내렸으며, 보석들이 그의 두 팔을 장식하고, 그의 이마에는 황금 화관이 씌워졌으며, 그의 목과 어깨 위로는 향유로 향기로운 머리카락이 흘러내렸다."], ["His left hand held the lyre, his right the ivory wand with which he struck its chords.", "그의 왼손은 리라를 쥐었고, 오른손은 그 줄을 켜는 상아 채를 쥐었다."], ["Like one inspired, he seemed to drink the morning air and glitter in the morning ray.", "그는 영감에 사로잡힌 자처럼 아침 공기를 들이마시며 아침 햇살 속에서 반짝이는 듯 보였다."], ["The seamen gazed with admiration.", "뱃사람들은 감탄하며 바라보았다."], ["He strode forward to the vessel’s side and looked down into the deep blue sea.", "그는 뱃전으로 성큼 걸어 나가 짙푸른 바다를 내려다보았다."], ["Addressing his lyre, he sang, “Companion of my voice, come with me to the realm of shades.", "그는 자신의 리라에 대고 노래했다. \"내 목소리의 동반자여, 나와 함께 그림자의 나라로 가자."], ["Though Cerberus may growl, we know the power of song can tame his rage.", "케르베로스가 으르렁댄다 한들, 우리는 노래의 힘이 그 분노를 길들일 수 있음을 안다."], ["Ye heroes of Elysium, who have passed the darkling flood,—ye happy souls, soon shall I join your band.", "그대들, 엘리시온의 영웅들이여, 어둑한 강물을 건너간 그대들이여, 그대 행복한 영혼들이여, 머지않아 나는 그대들의 무리에 끼어들리라."], ["Yet can ye relieve my grief?", "그러나 그대들이 내 슬픔을 덜어 줄 수 있겠는가?"], ["Alas, I leave my friend behind me.", "아아, 나는 내 벗을 뒤에 남겨 두고 가는구나."], ["Thou, who didst find thy Eurydice, and lose her again as soon as found; when she had vanished like a dream, how didst thou hate the cheerful light!", "그대여, 그대는 그대의 에우리디케를 찾았으나 찾자마자 다시 잃지 않았던가. 그녀가 꿈처럼 사라졌을 때, 그대는 그 환한 빛을 얼마나 미워했던가!"], ["I must away, but I will not fear.", "나는 떠나야 하지만, 두려워하지 않으리라."], ["The gods look down upon us.", "신들께서 우리를 굽어보신다."], ["Ye who slay me unoffending, when I am no more, your time of trembling shall come.", "죄 없는 나를 죽이는 그대들이여, 내가 사라지고 나면 그대들이 떨어야 할 때가 오리라."], ["Ye Nereids, receive your guest, who throws himself upon your mercy!”", "그대 네레이데스여, 그대들의 자비에 몸을 던지는 손님을 받아다오!\""], ["So saying, he sprang into the deep sea.", "이렇게 말하고서 그는 깊은 바다로 뛰어들었다."], ["The waves covered him, and the seamen held on their way, fancying themselves safe from all danger of detection.", "파도가 그를 덮었고, 뱃사람들은 온갖 발각의 위험에서 안전하다 여기며 가던 길을 갔다."]], [["But the strains of his music had drawn round him the inhabitants of the deep to listen, and Dolphins followed the ship as if chained by a spell.", "그러나 그의 음악 가락은 깊은 바다의 거주자들을 그의 주위로 불러 모아 귀 기울이게 했고, 돌고래들이 마법에 묶이기라도 한 듯 배를 뒤따랐다."], ["While he struggled in the waves, a Dolphin offered him his back, and carried him mounted thereon safe to shore.", "그가 파도 속에서 허우적거릴 때, 돌고래 한 마리가 자신의 등을 내어 주었고, 그를 등에 태워 무사히 뭍으로 실어다 주었다."], ["At the spot where he landed, a monument of brass was afterwards erected upon the rocky shore, to preserve the memory of the event.", "그가 상륙한 바로 그 자리에, 그 사건의 기억을 보존하기 위해 훗날 바위투성이 해안 위에 청동 기념비가 세워졌다."]], [["When Arion and the dolphin parted, each to his own element, Arion thus poured forth his thanks: “Farewell, thou faithful, friendly fish!", "아리온과 돌고래가 각자 자신의 터전으로 헤어질 때, 아리온은 이렇게 감사를 쏟아 냈다. \"잘 가거라, 충실하고 다정한 물고기여!"], ["Would that I could reward thee; but thou canst not wend with me, nor I with thee.", "내가 너에게 보답할 수 있다면 좋으련만, 너는 나와 함께 갈 수 없고, 나 또한 너와 함께 갈 수 없구나."], ["Companionship we may not have.", "우리는 동행할 수가 없는 것이다."], ["May Galatea, queen of the deep, accord thee her favor, and thou, proud of the burden, draw her chariot over the smooth mirror of the deep.”", "부디 깊은 바다의 여왕 갈라테이아가 너에게 그 은총을 베풀어 주시기를, 그리고 그 짐을 자랑스레 여기는 네가 매끄러운 거울 같은 바다 위로 그녀의 마차를 끌게 되기를.\""]], [["Arion hastened from the shore, and soon saw before him the towers of Corinth.", "아리온은 해안에서 서둘러 떠났고, 곧 자기 앞에 코린토스의 탑들이 보였다."], ["He journeyed on, harp in hand, singing as he went, full of love and happiness, forgetting his losses, and mindful only of what remained, his friend and his lyre.", "그는 손에 하프를 들고 사랑과 행복에 가득 차 노래하며 길을 갔으니, 자신이 잃은 것은 잊고 오직 남은 것, 곧 자신의 벗과 자신의 리라만을 마음에 두었다."], ["He entered the hospitable halls, and was soon clasped in the embrace of Periander.", "그는 환대의 전당으로 들어섰고, 곧 페리안드로스의 품에 안겼다."], ["“I come back to thee, my friend,”", "\"내가 그대에게 돌아왔소, 나의 벗이여.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“The talent which a god bestowed has been the delight of thousands, but false knaves have stripped me of my well-earned treasure; yet I retain the consciousness of wide spread fame.”", "\"신께서 내려 주신 재능은 수천 사람의 기쁨이 되었으나, 거짓된 악당들이 내가 정당하게 얻은 보물을 앗아 갔소. 그래도 나는 드넓은 명성을 자각하는 것만은 간직하고 있소.\""], ["Then he told Periander all the wonderful events that had befallen him, who heard him with amazement.", "그러고서 그는 자신에게 닥쳤던 그 모든 놀라운 사건을 페리안드로스에게 들려주었고, 왕은 경악하며 그 이야기를 들었다."], ["“Shall such wickedness triumph?”", "\"그런 악행이 승리해도 된단 말인가?\""], ["said he.", "왕이 말했다."], ["“Then in vain is power lodged in my hands.", "\"그렇다면 내 손에 쥐어진 권력은 헛된 것이로다."], ["That we may discover the criminals, you must remain here in concealment, and so they will approach without suspicion.”", "우리가 그 범인들을 찾아낼 수 있도록, 그대는 여기 숨어 있어야 하네. 그래야 그들이 의심 없이 다가올 것이니.\""], ["When the ship arrived in the harbor, he summoned the mariners before him.", "배가 항구에 들어오자, 왕은 뱃사람들을 자기 앞으로 불러들였다."], ["“Have you heard anything of Arion?”", "\"아리온에 대해 무슨 소식이라도 들은 것이 있느냐?\""], ["he inquired.", "왕이 물었다."], ["“I anxiously look for his return.”", "\"나는 그의 귀환을 애타게 기다리고 있다.\""], ["They replied, “We left him well and prosperous in Tarentum.”", "그들이 대답했다. \"저희는 그가 타렌툼에서 건강하고 잘 지내는 것을 보고 떠났습니다.\""], ["As they said these words, Arion stepped forth and faced them.", "그들이 이 말을 하는 동안, 아리온이 앞으로 나서서 그들을 마주했다."], ["His well-proportioned limbs were arrayed in gold and purple fair to see, his tunic fell around him in graceful folds, jewels adorned his arms, his brow was crowned with a golden wreath, and over his neck and shoulders flowed his hair perfumed with odors; his left hand held the lyre, his right the ivory wand with which he struck its chords.", "그의 균형 잡힌 사지는 보기에 아름다운 황금과 자줏빛 옷으로 차려입혀져 있었고, 그의 겉옷은 우아한 주름을 이루며 그의 몸을 따라 흘러내렸으며, 보석들이 그의 두 팔을 장식하고, 그의 이마에는 황금 화관이 씌워졌으며, 그의 목과 어깨 위로는 향유로 향기로운 머리카락이 흘러내렸다. 그의 왼손은 리라를 쥐었고, 오른손은 그 줄을 켜는 상아 채를 쥐었다."], ["They fell prostrate at his feet, as if a lightning bolt had struck them.", "그들은 벼락이라도 맞은 듯 그의 발치에 엎드렸다."], ["“We meant to murder him, and he has become a god.", "\"우리는 그를 죽이려 했는데, 그가 신이 되었구나."], ["O Earth, open and receive us!”", "오 대지여, 입을 벌려 우리를 삼켜다오!\""], ["Then Periander spoke.", "그러자 페리안드로스가 말했다."], ["“He lives, the master of the lay!", "\"그는 살아 있다, 노래의 명인이!"], ["Kind Heaven protects the poet’s life.", "자비로운 하늘이 시인의 목숨을 지켜 주었다."], ["As for you, I invoke not the spirit of vengeance; Arion wishes not your blood.", "너희에 관해서는, 나는 복수의 정령을 불러들이지 않으리라. 아리온은 너희의 피를 바라지 않는다."], ["Ye slaves of avarice, begone!", "탐욕의 노예들이여, 물러가라!"], ["Seek some barbarous land, and never may aught beautiful delight your souls!”", "어딘가 야만의 땅을 찾아가, 어떤 아름다운 것도 결코 너희 영혼을 기쁘게 하지 않기를!\""]], [["Spenser represents Arion, mounted on his dolphin, accompanying the train of Neptune and Amphitrite:", "스펜서는 아리온이 자신의 돌고래에 올라탄 채 넵투누스와 암피트리테의 행렬을 뒤따르는 모습을 그려 낸다."]], [["“Then was there heard a most celestial sound Of dainty music which did next ensue, And, on the floating waters as enthroned, Arion with his harp unto him drew The ears and hearts of all that goodly crew; Even when as yet the dolphin which him bore Through the Ægean Seas from pirates’ view, Stood still, by him astonished at his lore, And all the raging seas for joy forgot to roar.”", "\"그때 들려온 것은 더없이 천상적인 소리, 뒤이어 흘러나온 정묘한 음악이었으니, 넘실대는 물결 위에 보좌라도 차지한 듯, 아리온은 자신의 하프로 그 아름다운 무리 모두의 귀와 마음을 자기에게로 끌어당겼다. 그를 태우고 해적들의 눈을 피해 에게 해를 건너던 그 돌고래마저도 그의 가락에 놀라 가만히 멈추어 섰고, 날뛰던 바다도 기쁨에 겨워 으르렁대기를 잊었다.\""]], [["Byron, in his “Childe Harold,”", "바이런은 「차일드 해럴드」"], ["Canto II., alludes to the story of Arion, when, describing his voyage, he represents one of the seamen making music to entertain the rest:", "제2편에서 아리온의 이야기를 암시하는데, 자신의 항해를 묘사하면서 뱃사람 가운데 하나가 나머지 사람들을 즐겁게 하려고 음악을 연주하는 모습을 그려 낸다."]], [["“The moon is up; by Heaven a lovely eve!", "\"달이 떴다. 하늘에 맹세코, 사랑스러운 저녁이로다!"], ["Long streams of light o’er dancing waves expand; Now lads on shore may sigh and maids believe; Such be our fate when we return to land!", "긴 빛줄기가 춤추는 물결 위로 펼쳐지고, 이제 뭍의 젊은이들은 한숨짓고 처녀들은 믿으리니, 우리가 뭍으로 돌아갈 때 우리의 운명도 그러하기를!"], ["Meantime some rude Arion’s restless hand Wakes the brisk harmony that sailors love; A circle there of merry listeners stand, Or to some well-known measure featly move Thoughtless as if on shore they still were free to rove.”", "그러는 사이 어느 거친 아리온의 쉼 없는 손이 뱃사람들이 좋아하는 흥겨운 가락을 깨워 내고, 거기 즐거운 청중들이 둥글게 둘러서거나, 익숙한 가락에 맞추어 가뿐히 몸을 움직이니, 아직도 뭍에서 자유로이 거니는 듯 시름이 없구나.\" 이비코스"]], [["In order to understand the story of Ibycus which follows it is necessary to remember, first, that the theatres of the ancients were immense fabrics capable of containing from ten to thirty thousand spectators, and as they were used only on festival occasions, and admission was free to all, they were usually filled.", "이어지는 이비코스의 이야기를 이해하려면, 첫째로 고대인들의 극장이 만 명에서 삼만 명에 이르는 관객을 수용할 수 있는 거대한 건축물이었다는 점을 기억해 둘 필요가 있다. 그리고 그것은 오직 축제 때에만 사용되었고 모든 이에게 입장이 무료였으므로, 대개는 꽉 들어찼다."], ["They were without roofs and open to the sky, and the performances were in the daytime.", "그것은 지붕이 없어 하늘을 향해 열려 있었고, 공연은 대낮에 이루어졌다."], ["Secondly, the appalling representation of the Furies is not exaggerated in the story.", "둘째로, 복수의 여신들[푸리아이]의 소름 끼치는 묘사는 이 이야기에서 결코 과장된 것이 아니다."], ["It is recorded that Æschylus, the tragic poet, having on one occasion represented the Furies in a chorus of fifty performers, the terror of the spectators was such that many fainted and were thrown into convulsions, and the magistrates forbade a like representation for the future.", "비극 시인 아이스킬로스가 한번은 쉰 명의 연기자로 이루어진 합창대로 복수의 여신들을 무대에 올렸을 때, 관객들의 공포가 어찌나 컸던지 많은 이들이 기절하고 경련을 일으켰으며, 이에 행정관들이 앞으로 그와 같은 공연을 금했다고 전한다."]], [["Ibycus, the pious poet, was on his way to the chariot races and musical competitions held at the Isthmus of Corinth, which attracted all of Grecian lineage.", "경건한 시인 이비코스는 코린토스 지협에서 열리는 전차 경주와 음악 경연을 향해 길을 가던 중이었으니, 그 행사는 그리스 혈통을 지닌 모든 이를 불러 모으는 것이었다."], ["Apollo had bestowed on him the gift of song, the honeyed lips of the poet, and he pursued his way with lightsome step, full of the god.", "아폴론은 그에게 노래의 재능, 곧 시인의 꿀처럼 달콤한 입술을 내려 주었고, 그는 신에 가득 차 가벼운 걸음으로 길을 갔다."], ["Already the towers of Corinth crowning the height appeared in view, and he had entered with pious awe the sacred grove of Neptune.", "어느덧 높은 언덕을 왕관처럼 두른 코린토스의 탑들이 눈에 들어왔고, 그는 경건한 외경심을 품고 넵투누스의 신성한 숲으로 들어섰다."], ["No living object was in sight, only a flock of cranes flew overhead taking the same course as himself in their migration to a southern clime.", "살아 있는 것이라곤 눈에 띄지 않았고, 오직 두루미 떼만이 남쪽 지방으로 이주하느라 그와 같은 방향을 취하며 머리 위로 날아갈 뿐이었다."], ["“Good luck to you, ye friendly squadrons,”", "\"행운이 함께하기를, 그대 다정한 무리여.\""], ["he exclaimed, “my companions from across the sea.", "그가 외쳤다. \"바다 건너에서 온 나의 동반자들이여."], ["I take your company for a good omen.", "나는 그대들의 동행을 좋은 징조로 받아들이겠다."], ["We come from far and fly in search of hospitality.", "우리는 먼 곳에서 와서 환대를 찾아 날아가는구나."], ["May both of us meet that kind reception which shields the stranger guest from harm!”", "우리 둘 다, 나그네 손님을 해악으로부터 지켜 주는 저 따뜻한 영접을 받게 되기를!\""]], [["He paced briskly on, and soon was in the middle of the wood.", "그는 활기차게 걸음을 옮겼고, 곧 숲 한가운데에 이르렀다."], ["There suddenly, at a narrow pass, two robbers stepped forth and barred his way.", "그곳에서 별안간, 좁은 길목에서 두 명의 강도가 나서서 그의 길을 가로막았다."], ["He must yield or fight.", "그는 굴복하거나 싸워야만 했다."], ["But his hand, accustomed to the lyre, and not to the strife of arms, sank powerless.", "그러나 리라에는 익숙하되 무기를 들고 다투는 일에는 익숙하지 않은 그의 손은 무력하게 늘어졌다."], ["He called for help on men and gods, but his cry reached no defender’s ear.", "그는 사람들과 신들에게 도움을 청했으나, 그의 외침은 어떤 수호자의 귀에도 닿지 못했다."], ["“Then here must I die,”", "\"그렇다면 나는 여기서 죽어야 하는구나.\""], ["said he, “in a strange land, unlamented, cut off by the hand of outlaws, and see none to avenge my cause.”", "그가 말했다. \"낯선 땅에서, 애도해 줄 이도 없이, 무법자의 손에 목숨이 끊겨, 내 원한을 갚아 줄 이 하나 보지 못한 채로.\""], ["Sore wounded, he sank to the earth, when hoarse screamed the cranes overhead.", "깊은 상처를 입고 그가 땅에 쓰러질 때, 머리 위에서 두루미들이 쉰 소리로 울었다."], ["“Take up my cause, ye cranes,”", "\"내 원한을 떠맡아 다오, 그대 두루미들이여.\""], ["he said, “since no voice but yours answers to my cry.”", "그가 말했다. \"그대들의 목소리 말고는 내 외침에 답하는 것이 없으니.\""], ["So saying he closed his eyes in death.", "이렇게 말하고서 그는 죽음 속에 눈을 감았다."]], [["The body, despoiled and mangled, was found, and though disfigured with wounds, was recognized by the friend in Corinth who had expected him as a guest.", "벌거벗겨지고 난도질당한 그의 시신이 발견되었고, 비록 상처로 흉하게 일그러져 있었으나, 코린토스에서 그를 손님으로 기다리던 벗이 그를 알아보았다."], ["“Is it thus I find you restored to me?”", "\"이런 모습으로 그대를 되찾는단 말인가?\""], ["he exclaimed.", "그가 외쳤다."], ["“I who hoped to entwine your temples with the wreath of triumph in the strife of song!”", "\"노래의 겨룸에서 그대의 관자놀이를 승리의 화관으로 휘감아 주기를 바랐던 내가!\""]], [["The guests assembled at the festival heard the tidings with dismay.", "축제에 모인 손님들은 그 소식을 듣고 경악했다."], ["All Greece felt the wound, every heart owned its loss.", "온 그리스가 그 상처를 느꼈고, 모든 가슴이 그 상실을 제 것으로 받아들였다."], ["They crowded round the tribunal of the magistrates, and demanded vengeance on the murderers and expiation with their blood.", "그들은 행정관들의 법정 주위로 몰려들어 살인자들에 대한 복수와 그들의 피로 치르는 속죄를 요구했다."]], [["But what trace or mark shall point out the perpetrator from amidst the vast multitude attracted by the splendor of the feast?", "그러나 잔치의 화려함에 이끌려 모여든 그 방대한 군중 가운데서, 어떤 흔적이나 표적이 범인을 가려낼 수 있단 말인가?"], ["Did he fall by the hands of robbers or did some private enemy slay him?", "그는 강도들의 손에 쓰러진 것인가, 아니면 어떤 사사로운 원수가 그를 죽인 것인가?"], ["The all-discerning sun alone can tell, for no other eye beheld it.", "모든 것을 꿰뚫어 보는 태양만이 그것을 말해 줄 수 있으니, 다른 어떤 눈도 그것을 보지 못했기 때문이다."], ["Yet not improbably the murderer even now walks in the midst of the throng, and enjoys the fruits of his crime, while vengeance seeks for him in vain.", "그러나 어쩌면 그 살인자가 바로 지금도 군중 한가운데를 거닐며 자기 범행의 열매를 누리고 있을 법한데, 한편 복수는 헛되이 그를 찾고 있는 것이다."], ["Perhaps in their own temple’s enclosure he defies the gods, mingling freely in this throng of men that now presses into the amphitheatre.", "어쩌면 그는 자신들의 신전 경내에서 신들을 우롱하며, 지금 원형 극장 안으로 밀려드는 이 사람들의 무리에 거리낌 없이 섞여 있을지도 모른다."]], [["For now crowded together, row on row, the multitude fill the seats till it seems as if the very fabric would give way.", "이제 줄줄이 빽빽이 모여든 군중이 좌석을 가득 메우니, 마치 그 건축물 자체가 무너져 내릴 것만 같았다."], ["The murmur of voices sounds like the roar of the sea, while the circles widening in their ascent rise tier on tier, as if they would reach the sky.", "목소리들의 웅성거림은 바다의 포효처럼 들렸고, 위로 올라갈수록 넓어지는 원형의 단들은 마치 하늘에 닿으려는 듯 층층이 솟아올랐다."]], [["And now the vast assemblage listens to the awful voice of the chorus personating the Furies, which in solemn guise advances with measured step, and moves around the circuit of the theatre.", "이제 그 방대한 군중은 복수의 여신들로 분한 합창대의 무시무시한 목소리에 귀를 기울이니, 그것은 엄숙한 차림으로 박자를 맞춘 걸음을 옮기며 나아와 극장의 둘레를 돌았다."], ["Can they be mortal women who compose that awful group, and can that vast concourse of silent forms be living beings?", "저 무시무시한 무리를 이루는 것이 과연 필멸의 여인들일 수 있단 말인가, 그리고 저 방대하게 모인 말 없는 형상들이 과연 살아 있는 존재일 수 있단 말인가?"]], [["The choristers, clad in black, bore in their fleshless hands torches blazing with a pitchy flame.", "검은 옷을 입은 합창대원들은 살 없는 손에 역청 같은 불꽃을 타오르게 하는 횃불을 들었다."], ["Their cheeks were bloodless, and in place of hair writhing and swelling serpents curled around their brows.", "그들의 뺨에는 핏기가 없었고, 머리카락 대신 꿈틀거리며 부풀어 오르는 뱀들이 그들의 이마를 휘감고 있었다."], ["Forming a circle, these awful beings sang their hymns, rending the hearts of the guilty, and enchaining all their faculties.", "둥근 원을 이루어 이 무시무시한 존재들은 그들의 찬가를 불렀으니, 죄지은 자들의 마음을 찢고 그들의 모든 기능을 옭아맸다."], ["It rose and swelled, overpowering the sound of the instruments, stealing the judgment, palsying the heart, curdling the blood.", "그 노래는 솟아오르고 부풀어 올라 악기들의 소리를 압도하며, 판단력을 앗아 가고, 가슴을 마비시키며, 피를 얼어붙게 했다."]], [["“Happy the man who keeps his heart pure from guilt and crime!", "\"제 마음을 죄와 악행으로부터 순결하게 지키는 자는 복되도다!"], ["Him we avengers touch not; he treads the path of life secure from us.", "복수자인 우리는 그를 건드리지 않으니, 그는 우리로부터 안전하게 삶의 길을 걷는다."], ["But woe! woe!", "그러나 화로다!"], ["to him who has done the deed of secret murder.", "화로다! 은밀한 살인의 행위를 저지른 자에게는!"], ["We the fearful family of Night fasten ourselves upon his whole being.", "우리, 밤의 무시무시한 가족은 그의 온 존재에 들러붙는다."], ["Thinks he by flight to escape us?", "그는 달아나면 우리를 피할 수 있다고 생각하는가?"], ["We fly still faster in pursuit, twine our snakes around his feet, and bring him to the ground.", "우리는 더욱 빠르게 뒤쫓아 날아가, 그의 두 발에 우리의 뱀을 휘감아 그를 땅에 거꾸러뜨린다."], ["Unwearied we pursue; no pity checks our course; still on and on, to the end of life, we give him no peace nor rest.”", "우리는 지칠 줄 모르고 뒤쫓으며, 어떤 연민도 우리의 추격을 멈추지 못한다. 끝없이 나아가, 삶의 끝까지, 우리는 그에게 평안도 안식도 주지 않는다.\""], ["Thus the Eumenides sang, and moved in solemn cadence, while stillness like the stillness of death sat over the whole assembly as if in the presence of superhuman beings; and then in solemn march completing the circuit of the theatre, they passed out at the back of the stage.", "이렇게 에우메니데스는 노래하며 엄숙한 가락에 맞추어 움직였고, 한편 죽음의 정적과도 같은 고요가 마치 초인적인 존재들 앞에 있기라도 한 듯 온 군중 위에 내려앉았다. 그러고 나서 엄숙한 행진으로 극장의 둘레를 다 돌고서, 그들은 무대 뒤편으로 빠져나갔다."]], [["Every heart fluttered between illusion and reality, and every breast panted with undefined terror, quailing before the awful power that watches secret crimes and winds unseen the skein of destiny.", "모든 가슴은 환상과 현실 사이에서 떨렸고, 모든 가슴은 은밀한 범죄를 지켜보며 보이지 않게 운명의 실타래를 감는 그 무시무시한 힘 앞에 움츠러들어 막연한 공포로 두근거렸다."], ["At that moment a cry burst forth from one of the uppermost benches—“Look!", "바로 그 순간, 가장 높은 좌석 가운데 하나에서 외침이 터져 나왔다. \"보게!"], ["look!", "보게!"], ["comrade, yonder are the cranes of Ibycus!”", "친구여, 저기 이비코스의 두루미들이 있네!\""], ["And suddenly there appeared sailing across the sky a dark object which a moment’s inspection showed to be a flock of cranes flying directly over the theatre.", "그리고 별안간 하늘을 가로질러 떠가는 어두운 물체가 나타났으니, 잠시 살펴보니 극장 바로 위를 날아가는 두루미 떼였다. \"이비코스라고?"], ["“Of Ibycus! did he say?”", "저자가 그렇게 말했나?\""], ["The beloved name revived the sorrow in every breast.", "그 사랑하는 이름은 모든 가슴에 슬픔을 되살려 놓았다."], ["As wave follows wave over the face of the sea, so ran from mouth to mouth the words, “Of Ibycus!", "바다 위로 물결이 물결을 뒤따르듯, 입에서 입으로 그 말이 퍼져 나갔다. \"이비코스라고!"], ["him whom we all lament, whom some murderer’s hand laid low!", "우리 모두가 애도하는 그 사람, 어느 살인자의 손에 쓰러진 그 사람 말인가!"], ["What have the cranes to do with him?”", "두루미들이 그와 무슨 상관이란 말인가?\""], ["And louder grew the swell of voices, while like a lightning’s flash the thought sped through every heart, “Observe the power of the Eumenides!", "그리고 목소리의 너울은 더욱 커져 갔고, 한편 번개의 섬광처럼 한 생각이 모든 가슴을 꿰뚫고 지나갔다. \"에우메니데스의 힘을 보라!"], ["The pious poet shall be avenged!", "경건한 시인의 원한이 갚아지리라!"], ["the murderer has informed against himself.", "살인자가 스스로 자기를 고발했구나."], ["Seize the man who uttered that cry and the other to whom he spoke!”", "저 외침을 내뱉은 자와, 그가 말을 건넨 다른 자를 붙잡아라!\""]], [["The culprit would gladly have recalled his words, but it was too late.", "그 죄인은 기꺼이 자신의 말을 도로 거두고 싶었으나, 이미 너무 늦었다."], ["The faces of the murderers, pale with terror, betrayed their guilt.", "공포로 창백해진 살인자들의 얼굴이 그들의 죄를 드러냈다."], ["The people took them before the judge, they confessed their crime, and suffered the punishment they deserved.", "사람들은 그들을 재판관 앞으로 데려갔고, 그들은 자신들의 범행을 자백했으며, 마땅히 받아야 할 벌을 받았다. 시모니데스"]], [["Simonides was one of the most prolific of the early poets of Greece, but only a few fragments of his compositions have descended to us.", "시모니데스는 그리스 초기 시인들 가운데 가장 다작한 이들 중 하나였으나, 그의 작품은 단편 몇 조각만이 우리에게 전해져 내려왔다."], ["He wrote hymns, triumphal odes, and elegies.", "그는 찬가와 승리의 송가, 그리고 비가를 지었다."], ["In the last species of composition he particularly excelled.", "마지막 종류의 작품에서 그는 특히 뛰어났다."], ["His genius was inclined to the pathetic, and none could touch with truer effect the chords of human sympathy. The “Lamentation of Danaë,”", "그의 천재성은 비애에 기울어 있었고, 인간적 공감의 현을 그보다 더 진실한 효과로 건드릴 수 있는 이는 없었다."], ["the most important of the fragments which remain of his poetry, is based upon the tradition that Danaë and her infant son were confined by order of her father, Acrisius, in a chest and set adrift on the sea.", "그의 시 가운데 남아 있는 단편 중 가장 중요한 「다나에의 비탄」은, 다나에와 그녀의 어린 아들이 그녀의 아버지 아크리시오스의 명에 따라 궤짝에 갇혀 바다 위에 띄워졌다는 전승에 바탕을 두고 있다."], ["The chest floated towards the island of Seriphus, where both were rescued by Dictys, a fisherman, and carried to Polydectes, king of the country, who received and protected them.", "그 궤짝은 세리포스 섬을 향해 떠갔고, 그곳에서 둘은 어부 딕티스에게 구조되어 그 나라의 왕 폴리덱테스에게로 옮겨졌으니, 왕은 그들을 받아들여 보호해 주었다."], ["The child, Perseus, when grown up became a famous hero, whose adventures have been recorded in a previous chapter.", "그 아이 페르세우스는 자라나 이름난 영웅이 되었으며, 그의 모험은 앞선 장에 기록되어 있다."]], [["Simonides passed much of his life at the courts of princes, and often employed his talents in panegyric and festal odes, receiving his reward from the munificence of those whose exploits he celebrated.", "시모니데스는 생애의 많은 시간을 군주들의 궁정에서 보냈고, 종종 자신의 재능을 찬미의 시와 축제의 송가에 쏟으며, 자신이 그 위업을 기린 이들의 후한 베풂에서 그 보답을 받았다."], ["This employment was not derogatory, but closely resembles that of the earliest bards, such as Demodocus, described by Homer, or of Homer himself, as recorded by tradition.", "이 일은 천한 것이 아니었으니, 호메로스가 묘사한 데모도코스 같은 가장 이른 시기의 음유시인들의 일이나, 전승에 기록된 대로의 호메로스 자신의 일과 매우 흡사했다."]], [["On one occasion, when residing at the court of Scopas, king of Thessaly, the prince desired him to prepare a poem in celebration of his exploits, to be recited at a banquet.", "한번은 테살리아의 왕 스코파스의 궁정에 머물 때, 그 군주는 자신의 위업을 기리는 시를 지어 연회에서 낭송하게 해 달라고 그에게 청했다."], ["In order to diversify his theme, Simonides, who was celebrated for his piety, introduced into his poem the exploits of Castor and Pollux.", "자신의 주제에 변화를 주기 위해, 경건함으로 이름난 시모니데스는 자신의 시 속에 카스토르와 폴리데우케스의 위업을 끌어들였다."], ["Such digressions were not unusual with the poets on similar occasions, and one might suppose an ordinary mortal might have been content to share the praises of the sons of Leda.", "그러한 여담은 비슷한 자리에서 시인들에게 드문 일이 아니었으며, 평범한 필멸자라면 레다의 아들들과 찬사를 나누는 것에 만족했으리라 짐작할 만하다. 그러나 허영은 까다로운 법이다."], ["But vanity is exacting; and as Scopas sat at his festal board among his courtiers and sycophants, he grudged every verse that did not rehearse his own praises.", "스코파스가 자신의 신하들과 아첨꾼들 가운데에서 잔칫상 앞에 앉아, 자신에 대한 찬사를 늘어놓지 않는 모든 구절을 못마땅하게 여겼던 것이다."], ["When Simonides approached to receive the promised reward Scopas bestowed but half the expected sum, saying, “Here is payment for my portion of thy performance; Castor and Pollux will doubtless compensate thee for so much as relates to them.”", "시모니데스가 약속된 보답을 받으러 다가가자, 스코파스는 기대했던 액수의 절반만을 내주며 말했다. \"여기 네 공연 중 내 몫에 대한 값이 있다."], ["The disconcerted poet returned to his seat amidst the laughter which followed the great man’s jest.", "카스토르와 폴리데우케스가 분명 그들에게 관련된 만큼은 네게 보답해 줄 것이다.\" 당황한 시인은 그 위인의 농담에 뒤따른 웃음 속에서 자기 자리로 돌아갔다."], ["In a little time he received a message that two young men on horseback were waiting without and anxious to see him.", "얼마 지나지 않아 그는 말을 탄 두 젊은이가 밖에서 그를 애타게 만나려 기다리고 있다는 전갈을 받았다."], ["Simonides hastened to the door, but looked in vain for the visitors.", "시모니데스는 서둘러 문으로 갔으나, 그 방문객들을 찾아도 보이지 않았다."], ["Scarcely, however, had he left the banqueting hall when the roof fell in with a loud crash, burying Scopas and all his guests beneath the ruins.", "그러나 그가 연회장을 떠나기가 무섭게, 지붕이 요란한 굉음과 함께 무너져 내려 스코파스와 그의 모든 손님들을 그 잔해 아래 파묻어 버렸다."], ["On inquiring as to the appearance of the young men who had sent for him, Simonides was satisfied that they were no other than Castor and Pollux themselves.", "자신을 부르러 왔던 그 젊은이들의 모습에 대해 물어보고서, 시모니데스는 그들이 다름 아닌 카스토르와 폴리데우케스 바로 그들이었음을 확신하게 되었다."]], [["Sappho was a poetess who flourished in a very early age of Greek literature.", "사포\n사포는 그리스 문학의 매우 이른 시기에 활동한 여류 시인이었다."], ["Of her works few fragments remain, but they are enough to establish her claim to eminent poetical genius.", "그녀의 작품은 단편 몇 조각만이 남아 있으나, 그것만으로도 빼어난 시적 천재성에 대한 그녀의 자격을 입증하기에 충분하다."], ["The story of Sappho commonly alluded to is that she was passionately in love with a beautiful youth named Phaon, and failing to obtain a return of affection she threw herself from the promontory of Leucadia into the sea, under a superstition that those who should take that “Lover’s-leap” would, if not destroyed, be cured of their love.", "흔히 언급되는 사포의 이야기는, 그녀가 파온이라는 이름의 아름다운 젊은이를 열렬히 사랑했으나 그 사랑의 보답을 얻지 못하자, 저 \"연인의 도약\"을 감행하는 자들은, 죽지 않는다면 그 사랑이 치유되리라는 미신에 따라, 레우카디아의 곶에서 바다로 몸을 던졌다는 것이다."]], [["Byron alludes to the story of Sappho in “Childe Harold,”", "바이런은 「차일드 해럴드」"], ["Canto II.:", "제2편에서 사포의 이야기를 암시한다."]], [["“Childe Harold sailed and passed the barren spot Where sad Penelope o’erlooked the wave, And onward viewed the mount, not yet forgot, The lover’s refuge and the Lesbian’s grave.", "\"차일드 해럴드는 항해하여 저 황량한 곳을 지나쳤으니, 슬픈 페넬로페가 물결을 굽어보던 그곳이라, 그리고 앞으로 나아가며 아직 잊히지 않은 그 산, 연인의 피난처이자 레스보스 여인의 무덤을 바라보았다."], ["Dark Sappho!", "어두운 사포여!"], ["could not verse immortal save That breast imbued with such immortal fire?", "불멸의 시구로도 구할 수 없었던가, 그토록 불멸의 불꽃이 깃든 그 가슴을?"], ["“’Twas on a Grecian autumn’s gentle eve Childe Harold hailed Leucadia’s cape afar;”", "\"그리스의 어느 부드러운 가을 저녁이었으니 차일드 해럴드는 멀리 레우카디아의 곶을 반겼다.\""], ["etc.", "등등."]], [["Those who wish to know more of Sappho and her “leap”", "사포와 그녀의 \"도약\"에 대해 더 알고자 하는 이는 「스펙테이터」"], ["are referred to the “Spectator,” Nos.", "제223호와 제229호를 참조하기 바란다."], ["223 and 229. See also Moore’s “Evenings in Greece.”", "무어의 「그리스에서의 저녁」도 함께 보라."]], [["————", "————"]], [["# DIANA AND ENDYMION", "# DIANA AND ENDYMION"]], [["ENDYMION was a beautiful youth who fed his flock on Mount Latmos.", "엔디미온은 라트모스 산에서 양 떼를 치던 아름다운 청년이었다."], ["One calm, clear night Diana, the moon, looked down and saw him sleeping.", "어느 고요하고 맑은 밤, 달의 여신 아르테미스[로마의 〈디아나〉]가 아래를 굽어보다가 잠들어 있는 그를 보았다."], ["The cold heart of the virgin goddess was warmed by his surpassing beauty, and she came down to him, kissed him, and watched over him while he slept.", "처녀신의 차가운 마음은 그의 빼어난 아름다움에 데워졌고, 그녀는 그에게 내려와 입을 맞추고는 그가 잠든 사이 그를 지켜보았다."]], [["Another story was that Jupiter bestowed on him the gift of perpetual youth united with perpetual sleep.", "또 다른 이야기에 따르면 제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]가 그에게 영원한 젊음과 영원한 잠을 함께 누리는 선물을 내렸다고 한다."], ["Of one so gifted we can have but few adventures to record.", "이렇게 선물을 받은 자에 관해서는 기록할 만한 모험이 거의 없을 수밖에 없다."], ["Diana, it was said, took care that his fortunes should not suffer by his inactive life, for she made his flock increase, and guarded his sheep and lambs from the wild beasts.", "전하는 말로는 아르테미스가 그의 무위(無爲)한 삶 때문에 그의 살림이 곤궁해지지 않도록 보살펴 주었다고 한다. 그녀가 그의 양 떼를 불려 주었고, 그의 양과 새끼 양들을 들짐승들로부터 지켜 주었기 때문이다."]], [["The story of Endymion has a peculiar charm from the human meaning which it so thinly veils.", "엔디미온의 이야기는 그것이 워낙 얇게 가리고 있는 인간적인 의미 때문에 독특한 매력을 지닌다."], ["We see in Endymion the young poet, his fancy and his heart seeking in vain for that which can satisfy them, finding his favorite hour in the quiet moonlight, and nursing there beneath the beams of the bright and silent witness the melancholy and the ardor which consumes him.", "우리는 엔디미온에게서 젊은 시인을 본다. 그의 상상과 마음은 그들을 만족시켜 줄 무언가를 헛되이 찾아 헤매고, 고요한 달빛 속에서 가장 사랑하는 시간을 발견하며, 밝고도 말 없는 증인의 빛줄기 아래에서 그를 태우는 우수와 열정을 키운다."], ["The story suggests aspiring and poetic love, a life spent more in dreams than in reality, and an early and welcome death.—S.", "이 이야기는 동경하는 시적인 사랑을, 현실보다는 꿈 속에서 더 많이 보낸 삶을, 그리고 이르되 반가운 죽음을 떠올리게 한다.—S. G."], ["G. B.", "B."]], [["The “Endymion” of Keats is a wild and fanciful poem, containing some exquisite poetry, as this, to the moon:", "키츠의 「엔디미온」은 거칠고 환상적인 시로, 달을 향한 다음 구절처럼 더없이 빼어난 시구를 담고 있다."]], [["“. .", "“……잠든 소들이 그대의 광휘 속에 누워 신성한 들판을 꿈꾸네."], [". The sleeping kine Couched in thy brightness dream of fields divine.", "헤아릴 수 없는 산들이 솟고, 또 솟아오르네, 그대 눈길의 축성(祝聖)을 갈망하면서."], ["Innumerable mountains rise, and rise, Ambitious for the hallowing of thine eyes, And yet thy benediction passeth not One obscure hiding-place, one little spot Where pleasure may be sent; the nested wren Has thy fair face within its tranquil ken;”", "그러나 그대의 축복은 빠뜨리지 않네 어느 어두운 은신처 하나, 작은 자리 하나, 기쁨이 깃들 만한 그곳도. 둥지 튼 굴뚝새는 그 고요한 시야 안에 그대의 고운 얼굴을 담고 있나니.”"], ["etc., etc.", "등등."]], [["Dr. Young, in the “Night Thoughts,” alludes to Endymion thus:", "영 박사는 「밤의 상념」에서 엔디미온을 이렇게 언급한다."]], [["“. .", "“……이 상념들은, 오 밤이여, 그대의 것."], [". These thoughts, O night, are thine; From thee they came like lovers’ secret sighs, While others slept.", "그것들은 연인의 은밀한 한숨처럼 그대에게서 왔도다, 다른 이들이 잠든 사이에."], ["So Cynthia, poets feign, In shadows veiled, soft, sliding from her sphere, Her shepherd cheered, of her enamoured less Than I of thee.”", "그렇게 킨티아가, 시인들이 꾸며 내듯, 그늘에 가려져, 부드럽게, 자신의 천구에서 미끄러져 내려와, 자신의 목동을 위로했네, 그를 향한 그녀의 사랑이 그대를 향한 나의 사랑만은 못했을지라도.”"]], [["Fletcher, in the “Faithful Shepherdess,” tells:", "플레처는 「충실한 양치기 여인」에서 이렇게 노래한다."]], [["“How the pale Phœbe, hunting in a grove, First saw the boy Endymion, from whose eyes She took eternal fire that never dies; How she conveyed him softly in a sleep, His temples bound with poppy, to the steep Head of old Latmos, where she stoops each night, Gilding the mountain with her brother’s light, To kiss her sweetest.”", "“창백한 포이베가 어느 숲에서 사냥하다가, 어떻게 처음 소년 엔디미온을 보았는지를, 그의 눈에서 결코 꺼지지 않는 영원한 불을 어떻게 그녀가 가져갔는지를, 어떻게 그녀가 그를 잠결에 부드럽게 옮겨, 그의 관자놀이를 양귀비로 감싸, 오래된 라트모스의 가파른 봉우리로 데려갔는지를, 그곳으로 그녀는 밤마다 몸을 굽혀, 제 오라비의 빛으로 산을 금빛으로 물들이며, 가장 사랑스러운 이에게 입 맞추러 내려오네.”"]], [["Orion was the son of Neptune.", "오리온은 포세이돈[로마의 〈넵투누스〉]의 아들이었다."], ["He was a handsome giant and a mighty hunter.", "그는 잘생긴 거인이자 강력한 사냥꾼이었다."], ["His father gave him the power of wading through the depths of the sea, or, as others say, of walking on its surface.", "그의 아버지는 그에게 바다의 깊은 곳을 헤치고 걸어 다니는 힘을, 또는 어떤 이들의 말로는 바다 표면 위를 걸어 다니는 힘을 주었다."]], [["Orion loved Merope, the daughter of Œnopion, king of Chios, and sought her in marriage.", "오리온은 키오스의 왕 오이노피온의 딸 메로페를 사랑하여 그녀에게 청혼하였다."], ["He cleared the island of wild beasts, and brought the spoils of the chase as presents to his beloved; but as Œnopion constantly deferred his consent, Orion attempted to gain possession of the maiden by violence.", "그는 섬에서 들짐승들을 몰아내고, 사냥의 노획물을 사랑하는 이에게 선물로 가져다 바쳤다. 그러나 오이노피온이 끊임없이 승낙을 미루자, 오리온은 폭력으로 그 처녀를 차지하려 하였다."], ["Her father, incensed at this conduct, having made Orion drunk, deprived him of his sight and cast him out on the seashore.", "이런 행동에 격분한 그녀의 아버지는 오리온을 취하게 만든 뒤 그의 시력을 빼앗고는 바닷가에 내던졌다."], ["The blinded hero followed the sound of a Cyclops’ hammer till he reached Lemnos, and came to the forge of Vulcan, who, taking pity on him, gave him Kedalion, one of his men, to be his guide to the abode of the sun.", "눈먼 영웅은 키클롭스의 망치 소리를 따라가다가 마침내 렘노스에 다다랐고, 헤파이스토스[로마의 〈불카누스〉]의 대장간에 이르렀다. 헤파이스토스는 그를 가엾이 여겨 자신의 일꾼 가운데 하나인 케달리온을 붙여 주어 태양신의 거처로 가는 길잡이로 삼게 하였다."], ["Placing Kedalion on his shoulders, Orion proceeded to the east, and there meeting the sun-god, was restored to sight by his beam.", "오리온은 케달리온을 어깨에 얹고 동쪽으로 나아갔고, 그곳에서 태양신을 만나 그 빛줄기로 다시 시력을 되찾았다."]], [["After this he dwelt as a hunter with Diana, with whom he was a favorite, and it is even said she was about to marry him.", "이후 그는 사냥꾼으로서 아르테미스와 함께 지냈는데, 그녀에게 무척 총애를 받았으며, 심지어 그녀가 그와 혼인하려 했다는 말까지 전한다."], ["Her brother was highly displeased and often chid her, but to no purpose.", "그녀의 오라비는 몹시 못마땅해하며 자주 그녀를 나무랐으나 아무 소용이 없었다."], ["One day, observing Orion wading through the sea with his head just above the water, Apollo pointed it out to his sister and maintained that she could not hit that black thing on the sea.", "어느 날 오리온이 머리만 물 위로 내놓은 채 바다를 헤치며 걸어가는 것을 본 아폴론은 그것을 누이에게 가리키며, 바다 위의 저 검은 것을 그녀가 맞히지 못할 것이라고 단언하였다."], ["The archer-goddess discharged a shaft with fatal aim.", "활의 여신은 치명적인 겨냥으로 화살을 쏘았다."], ["The waves rolled the dead body of Orion to the land, and bewailing her fatal error with many tears, Diana placed him among the stars, where he appears as a giant, with a girdle, sword, lion’s skin, and club.", "파도가 오리온의 주검을 뭍으로 밀어 올렸고, 자신의 치명적인 실수를 많은 눈물로 슬퍼하던 아르테미스는 그를 별들 사이에 놓았다. 그곳에서 그는 띠와 칼과 사자 가죽과 곤봉을 갖춘 거인의 모습으로 나타난다."], ["Sirius, his dog, follows him, and the Pleiads fly before him.", "그의 개 시리우스가 그를 뒤따르고, 플레이아데스가 그의 앞에서 달아난다."]], [["The Pleiads were daughters of Atlas, and nymphs of Diana’s train.", "플레이아데스는 아틀라스의 딸들이자 아르테미스를 따르던 님프들이었다."], ["One day Orion saw them and became enamoured and pursued them.", "어느 날 오리온이 그들을 보고 반하여 뒤를 쫓았다."], ["In their distress they prayed to the gods to change their form, and Jupiter in pity turned them into pigeons, and then made them a constellation in the sky.", "곤경에 처한 그들은 신들에게 자신들의 모습을 바꿔 달라고 빌었고, 제우스는 가엾이 여겨 그들을 비둘기로 바꾸었다가 다시 하늘의 별자리로 만들었다. 그들의 수는 일곱이었으나 보이는 별은 여섯뿐이다."], ["Though their number was seven, only six stars are visible, for Electra, one of them, it is said left her place that she might not behold the ruin of Troy, for that city was founded by her son Dardanus.", "그들 가운데 하나인 엘렉트라가 트로이아의 멸망을 보지 않으려고 제 자리를 떠났다고 전하기 때문이다. 그 도시가 그녀의 아들 다르다노스에 의해 세워졌던 까닭이다."], ["The sight had such an effect on her sisters that they have looked pale ever since.", "그 광경이 그녀의 자매들에게 미친 영향이 어찌나 컸던지 그 이후로 그들은 줄곧 창백한 빛을 띠고 있다."]], [["Mr. Longfellow has a poem on the “Occultation of Orion.”", "롱펠로 씨는 「오리온의 엄폐(掩蔽)」라는 시를 지었다."], ["The following lines are those in which he alludes to the mythic story.", "다음 구절들은 그가 이 신화 이야기를 언급한 대목이다."], ["We must premise that on the celestial globe Orion is represented as robed in a lion’s skin and wielding a club.", "미리 일러 두어야 할 것은, 천구 위에서 오리온이 사자 가죽을 두르고 곤봉을 휘두르는 모습으로 그려진다는 점이다."], ["At the moment the stars of the constellation, one by one, were quenched in the light of the moon, the poet tells us", "그 별자리의 별들이 하나하나 달빛 속에 잦아드는 순간을 시인은 우리에게 이렇게 들려준다."]], [["“Down fell the red skin of the lion Into the river at his feet.", "“사자의 붉은 가죽이 떨어져 내려 그의 발치 강물 속으로 빠졌네."], ["His mighty club no longer beat The forehead of the bull; but he Reeled as of yore beside the sea, When blinded by Œnopion He sought the blacksmith at his forge, And climbing up the narrow gorge, Fixed his blank eyes upon the sun.”", "그의 거대한 곤봉은 더 이상 황소의 이마를 두드리지 않았고, 그는 옛날처럼 바닷가에서 비틀거렸네, 오이노피온에게 눈멀었을 적, 대장장이를 그의 대장간에서 찾고, 좁은 협곡을 기어올라, 텅 빈 눈을 태양에 고정했던 그때처럼.”"]], [["Tennyson has a different theory of the Pleiads:", "테니슨은 플레이아데스에 대해 다른 견해를 펼친다."]], [["“Many a night I saw the Pleiads, rising through the mellow shade, Glitter like a swarm of fire-flies tangled in a silver braid.”", "“수많은 밤 나는 보았네, 플레이아데스가 부드러운 어스름을 뚫고 떠올라, 은빛 댕기에 뒤엉킨 반딧불 떼처럼 반짝이는 것을.”"], ["—Locksley Hall.", "—「록슬리 홀」."]], [["Byron alludes to the lost Pleiad:", "바이런은 잃어버린 플레이아드를 이렇게 언급한다."]], [["“Like the lost Pleiad seen no more below.”", "“더 이상 저 아래에서 보이지 않는, 잃어버린 플레이아드처럼.”"]], [["See also Mrs. Hemans’s verses on the same subject.", "같은 주제를 다룬 히먼스 부인의 시구도 참조하라."]], [["# AURORA AND TITHONUS", "# AURORA AND TITHONUS"]], [["The goddess of the Dawn, like her sister the Moon, was at times inspired with the love of mortals.", "새벽의 여신은 그 자매인 달의 여신처럼 때때로 필멸자들을 향한 사랑에 빠졌다."], ["Her greatest favorite was Tithonus, son of Laomedon, king of Troy.", "그녀가 가장 총애한 이는 트로이아의 왕 라오메돈의 아들 티토노스였다."], ["She stole him away, and prevailed on Jupiter to grant him immortality; but, forgetting to have youth joined in the gift, after some time she began to discern, to her great mortification, that he was growing old.", "그녀는 그를 몰래 데려와 제우스를 설득해 그에게 불사(不死)를 내려 주게 하였다. 그러나 그 선물에 젊음을 함께 더하는 것을 잊었던 탓에, 얼마 지나지 않아 그녀는 크게 낙심하면서 그가 늙어 가고 있음을 알아차리기 시작하였다."], ["When his hair was quite white she left his society; but he still had the range of her palace, lived on ambrosial food, and was clad in celestial raiment.", "그의 머리카락이 온통 하얗게 세자 그녀는 그와 함께하기를 그만두었다. 그래도 그는 여전히 그녀의 궁전을 자유로이 거닐었고, 신들의 음식을 먹고 살았으며, 하늘의 옷을 걸쳤다."], ["At length he lost the power of using his limbs, and then she shut him up in his chamber, whence his feeble voice might at times be heard.", "마침내 그는 팔다리를 쓰는 힘마저 잃었고, 그러자 그녀는 그를 방 안에 가두었으니, 그곳에서 때때로 그의 가냘픈 목소리가 들리곤 하였다."], ["Finally she turned him into a grasshopper.", "결국 그녀는 그를 베짱이로 바꾸었다."]], [["Memnon was the son of Aurora and Tithonus.", "멤논은 에오스[로마의 〈아우로라〉]와 티토노스의 아들이었다."], ["He was king of the Æthiopians, and dwelt in the extreme east, on the shore of Ocean.", "그는 아이티오피아인들의 왕으로서 머나먼 동쪽, 오케아노스의 기슭에 살았다."], ["He came with his warriors to assist the kindred of his father in the war of Troy.", "그는 자신의 전사들을 거느리고 아버지의 친족들을 도와 트로이아 전쟁에 참전하러 왔다."], ["King Priam received him with great honors, and listened with admiration to his narrative of the wonders of the ocean shore.", "프리아모스 왕은 그를 큰 예우로 맞이하였고, 그가 들려주는 대양 기슭의 경이로운 이야기에 감탄하며 귀를 기울였다."]], [["The very day after his arrival, Memnon, impatient of repose, led his troops to the field.", "도착한 바로 다음 날, 휴식을 견디지 못한 멤논은 자신의 군대를 이끌고 전장으로 나아갔다."], ["Antilochus, the brave son of Nestor, fell by his hand, and the Greeks were put to flight, when Achilles appeared and restored the battle.", "네스토르의 용맹한 아들 안틸로코스가 그의 손에 쓰러졌고, 그리스인들은 패주하였다. 그때 아킬레우스가 나타나 전세를 되돌렸다."], ["A long and doubtful contest ensued between him and the son of Aurora; at length victory declared for Achilles, Memnon fell, and the Trojans fled in dismay.", "그와 에오스의 아들 사이에 길고도 승부를 알 수 없는 싸움이 이어졌다. 마침내 승리는 아킬레우스의 편으로 기울었고, 멤논은 쓰러졌으며, 트로이아인들은 혼비백산하여 달아났다."]], [["Aurora, who from her station in the sky had viewed with apprehension the danger of her son, when she saw him fall, directed his brothers, the Winds, to convey his body to the banks of the river Esepus in Paphlagonia.", "하늘의 자리에서 아들의 위험을 근심스레 지켜보던 에오스는 그가 쓰러지는 것을 보자, 그의 형제들인 바람들에게 그의 주검을 파플라고니아의 에세포스 강 기슭으로 옮기라고 명하였다."], ["In the evening Aurora came, accompanied by the Hours and the Pleiads, and wept and lamented over her son.", "저녁이 되자 에오스가 〈사계절〉 여신들과 플레이아데스를 거느리고 와서 아들을 두고 울며 슬퍼하였다."], ["Night, in sympathy with her grief, spread the heaven with clouds; all nature mourned for the offspring of the Dawn.", "밤은 그녀의 슬픔에 공감하여 하늘을 구름으로 뒤덮었고, 온 자연이 새벽의 여신의 자식을 애도하였다."], ["The Æthiopians raised his tomb on the banks of the stream in the grove of the Nymphs, and Jupiter caused the sparks and cinders of his funeral pile to be turned into birds, which, dividing into two flocks, fought over the pile till they fell into the flame.", "아이티오피아인들은 님프들의 숲, 그 강 기슭에 그의 무덤을 세웠고, 제우스는 그의 화장(火葬) 장작더미에서 튄 불꽃과 잿불을 새들로 바꾸어 놓았다. 그 새들은 두 무리로 갈라져 장작더미 위에서 싸우다가 불길 속으로 떨어졌다."], ["Every year at the anniversary of his death they return and celebrate his obsequies in like manner.", "해마다 그의 기일이 되면 그 새들이 돌아와 같은 방식으로 그의 장례를 기린다."], ["Aurora remains inconsolable for the loss of her son.", "에오스는 아들을 잃은 슬픔에서 끝내 위안을 얻지 못한다."], ["Her tears still flow, and may be seen at early morning in the form of dew-drops on the grass.", "그녀의 눈물은 지금도 흐르며, 이른 아침이면 풀잎 위에 맺힌 이슬방울의 모습으로 볼 수 있다."]], [["Unlike most of the marvels of ancient mythology, there still exist some memorials of this.", "고대 신화의 여느 경이로운 이야기들과 달리, 이 이야기에 관해서는 아직도 몇 가지 기념물이 남아 있다."], ["On the banks of the river Nile, in Egypt, are two colossal statues, one of which is said to be the statue of Memnon.", "이집트의 나일 강 기슭에 거대한 조각상 둘이 서 있는데, 그 가운데 하나가 멤논의 조각상이라고 전한다."], ["Ancient writers record that when the first rays of the rising sun fall upon this statue a sound is heard to issue from it, which they compare to the snapping of a harp-string.", "고대의 저술가들은, 떠오르는 태양의 첫 햇살이 이 조각상에 닿으면 거기서 어떤 소리가 새어 나오는데, 그들이 그것을 하프 줄이 끊어지는 소리에 견주었다고 기록하였다."], ["There is some doubt about the identification of the existing statue with the one described by the ancients, and the mysterious sounds are still more doubtful.", "현재 남아 있는 조각상이 고대인들이 묘사한 그 조각상과 같은 것인지에 대해서는 다소 의문이 있으며, 그 신비로운 소리에 대해서는 더욱 의심스럽다."], ["Yet there are not wanting some modern testimonies to their being still audible.", "그러나 그 소리가 지금도 들린다는 근대의 증언이 없는 것은 아니다."], ["It has been suggested that sounds produced by confined air making its escape from crevices or caverns in the rocks may have given some ground for the story.", "바위의 틈새나 동굴에 갇혀 있던 공기가 빠져나가며 내는 소리가 그 이야기에 어느 정도 근거를 제공했으리라는 설명도 제기되었다."], ["Sir Gardner Wilkinson, a late traveller, of the highest authority, examined the statue itself, and discovered that it was hollow, and that “in the lap of the statue is a stone, which on being struck emits a metallic sound, that might still be made use of to deceive a visitor who was predisposed to believe its powers.”", "최근의 여행가로서 가장 권위 있는 가드너 윌킨슨 경은 그 조각상을 직접 살펴보고는 그것이 속이 비어 있음을 발견하였으며, 또한 “조각상의 무릎에는 돌 하나가 놓여 있는데, 그것을 두드리면 금속성의 소리가 나니, 그 힘을 믿으려는 마음이 앞선 방문객을 속이는 데 지금도 쓰일 만하다”라고 하였다."]], [["The vocal statue of Memnon is a favorite subject of allusion with the poets.", "목소리를 내는 멤논의 조각상은 시인들이 즐겨 인유하는 소재다."], ["Darwin, in his “Botanic Garden,” says:", "다윈은 「식물의 정원」에서 이렇게 노래한다."]], [["“So to the sacred Sun in Memnon’s fane Spontaneous concords choired the matin strain; Touched by his orient beam responsive rings The living lyre and vibrates all its strings; Accordant aisles the tender tones prolong, And holy echoes swell the adoring song.”", "“그리하여 멤논의 신전에서 신성한 태양을 향해 저절로 어우러진 화음이 아침의 노래를 합창했네. 그의 동방의 빛줄기에 닿아 화답하며 울리니 살아 있는 칠현금이 그 모든 줄을 떨게 하네. 어우러진 회랑들이 그 여린 가락을 길게 늘이고, 거룩한 메아리가 경배의 노래를 부풀리네.”"]], [["# ACIS AND GALATEA", "# ACIS AND GALATEA"]], [["Scylla was a fair virgin of Sicily, a favorite of the Sea-Nymphs.", "스킬라는 시켈리아의 아름다운 처녀로, 바다의 님프들에게 사랑받는 존재였다."], ["She had many suitors, but repelled them all, and would go to the grotto of Galatea, and tell her how she was persecuted.", "그녀에게는 많은 구혼자가 있었지만 모두 물리쳤으며, 갈라테이아의 동굴로 찾아가서는 자신이 어떻게 시달리고 있는지를 털어놓곤 했다. 어느 날 여신은 스킬라가 자신의 머리를 매만져 주는 동안 그 이야기에 귀를 기울였고, 그런 다음 이렇게 대답했다."], ["One day the goddess, while Scylla dressed her hair, listened to the story, and then replied, “Yet, maiden, your persecutors are of the not ungentle race of men, whom, if you will, you can repel; but I, the daughter of Nereus, and protected by such a band of sisters, found no escape from the passion of the Cyclops but in the depths of the sea;”", "\"하지만 처녀여, 그대를 괴롭히는 자들은 그래도 그리 거칠지 않은 인간 종족이니, 마음만 먹으면 그대는 그들을 물리칠 수 있다. 그러나 네레우스의 딸이요 이토록 많은 자매들의 보호를 받는 나로서는, 키클롭스의 정념에서 벗어날 길을 오로지 바다의 깊은 곳에서밖에 찾지 못하였다.\""], ["and tears stopped her utterance, which when the pitying maiden had wiped away with her delicate finger, and soothed the goddess, “Tell me, dearest,”", "그러자 눈물이 솟아 그녀의 말을 가로막았고, 가엾이 여긴 처녀가 가녀린 손가락으로 그 눈물을 닦아 주며 여신을 달랜 뒤 이렇게 말했다. \"가장 사랑하는 분이시여, 그대가 슬퍼하는 까닭을 말씀해 주십시오.\""], ["said she, “the cause of your grief.”", "그러자 갈라테이아가 말하였다."], ["Galatea then said, “Acis was the son of Faunus and a Naiad.", "\"아키스는 파우누스와 어느 나이아스 사이에서 난 아들이었다."], ["His father and mother loved him dearly, but their love was not equal to mine.", "그의 아버지와 어머니는 그를 끔찍이 사랑하였지만, 그들의 사랑조차 나의 사랑에는 미치지 못하였다."], ["For the beautiful youth attached himself to me alone, and he was just sixteen years old, the down just beginning to darken his cheeks.", "그 아름다운 젊은이는 오직 나에게만 마음을 두었으며, 그때 그는 갓 열여섯 살로 두 뺨에 솜털이 막 거뭇해지기 시작할 무렵이었다."], ["As much as I sought his society, so much did the Cyclops seek mine; and if you ask me whether my love for Acis or my hatred of Polyphemus was the stronger, I cannot tell you; they were in equal measure.", "내가 그의 곁을 찾기를 갈망한 만큼이나 키클롭스는 나의 곁을 찾기를 갈망하였으니, 만일 그대가 내게 아키스를 향한 사랑과 폴리페모스를 향한 미움 가운데 어느 쪽이 더 강하였느냐고 묻는다면 나는 대답할 수 없을 것이다."], ["O Venus, how great is thy power!", "둘은 똑같은 무게였다."], ["this fierce giant, the terror of the woods, whom no hapless stranger escaped unharmed, who defied even Jove himself, learned to feel what love was, and, touched with a passion for me, forgot his flocks and his well-stored caverns.", "오 아프로디테[로마의 〈베누스〉]여, 그대의 권능은 얼마나 위대한가! 숲의 공포요, 어떤 가엾은 나그네도 무사히 빠져나가지 못하게 한 자요, 제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉] 그 자신마저도 거역하던 이 사나운 거인이 사랑이 무엇인지를 느끼는 법을 배웠고, 나를 향한 정념에 사로잡혀 자신의 가축 떼와 잘 채워 둔 동굴마저 잊고 말았다."], ["Then for the first time he began to take some care of his appearance, and to try to make himself agreeable; he harrowed those coarse locks of his with a comb, and mowed his beard with a sickle, looked at his harsh features in the water, and composed his countenance.", "그제야 처음으로 그는 자신의 외양을 조금이나마 가꾸기 시작하였고, 스스로를 호감 가게 보이려 애썼다. 그 거친 머리칼을 빗으로 갈아엎듯 다듬고, 낫으로 수염을 베어 내고, 물에 비친 자신의 험상궂은 얼굴을 들여다보며 표정을 가다듬었다."], ["His love of slaughter, his fierceness and thirst of blood prevailed no more, and ships that touched at his island went away in safety.", "살육을 즐기는 마음, 그의 사나움과 피에 대한 갈증은 더 이상 기승을 부리지 못하였고, 그의 섬에 들른 배들도 무사히 떠나갔다."], ["He paced up and down the seashore, imprinting huge tracks with his heavy tread, and, when weary, lay tranquilly in his cave.", "그는 바닷가를 오르내리며 무거운 발걸음으로 거대한 자국을 남겼고, 지치면 동굴에 조용히 누웠다."]], [["“There is a cliff which projects into the sea, which washes it on either side.", "\"바다 쪽으로 튀어나와 양옆이 물에 씻기는 벼랑이 하나 있었다."], ["Thither one day the huge Cyclops ascended, and sat down while his flocks spread themselves around.", "어느 날 그 거대한 키클롭스는 그곳으로 올라가, 가축 떼가 주위에 흩어져 풀을 뜯는 동안 자리를 잡고 앉았다."], ["Laying down his staff, which would have served for a mast to hold a vessel’s sail, and taking his instrument compacted of numerous pipes, he made the hills and the waters echo the music of his song.", "배의 돛을 지탱하는 돛대로 써도 좋을 만한 자신의 지팡이를 내려놓고는, 수많은 갈대 피리를 엮어 만든 악기를 집어 들어, 언덕과 물결이 그의 노랫소리를 메아리치게 하였다."], ["I lay hid under a rock by the side of my beloved Acis, and listened to the distant strain.", "나는 사랑하는 아키스의 곁에서 바위 밑에 몸을 숨긴 채 그 아득한 가락에 귀를 기울였다."], ["It was full of extravagant praises of my beauty, mingled with passionate reproaches of my coldness and cruelty.", "그 노래는 내 아름다움을 향한 과장된 찬사로 가득하였고, 거기에 나의 냉담함과 잔인함을 향한 격정적인 원망이 뒤섞여 있었다."]], [["“When he had finished he rose up, and, like a raging bull that cannot stand still, wandered off into the woods.", "\"노래를 마치자 그는 자리에서 일어나, 가만히 서 있지 못하는 성난 황소처럼 숲속으로 방황하며 떠나갔다. 아키스와 나는 더 이상 그를 마음에 두지 않았는데, 그러던 차에 별안간 그가 우리가 앉아 있는 모습이 한눈에 보이는 곳에 이르렀다."], ["Acis and I thought no more of him, till on a sudden he came to a spot which gave him a view of us as we sat.", "'내 너희를 보았다.' 그가 외쳤다."], ["‘I see you,’ he exclaimed, ‘and I will make this the last of your love-meetings.’ His voice was a roar such as an angry Cyclops alone could utter.", "'이것이 너희의 마지막 밀회가 되게 해 주마.' 그 목소리는 성난 키클롭스만이 낼 수 있는 포효였다."], ["Ætna trembled at the sound.", "아이트네[에트나 화산]가 그 소리에 떨었다."], ["I, overcome with terror, plunged into the water.", "나는 공포에 사로잡혀 물속으로 뛰어들었다. 아키스는 돌아서서 도망치며 울부짖었다."], ["Acis turned and fled, crying, ‘Save me, Galatea, save me, my parents!’ The Cyclops pursued him, and tearing a rock from the side of the mountain hurled it at him.", "'저를 구해 주소서, 갈라테이아여, 저를 구해 주소서, 부모님이여!' 키클롭스는 그를 뒤쫓아 산허리에서 바위를 뜯어내어 그에게 던졌다."], ["Though only a corner of it touched him, it overwhelmed him.", "비록 그 모서리 한 귀퉁이만이 그에게 닿았을 뿐이나, 그 바위는 그를 짓눌러 버렸다."]], [["“All that fate left in my power I did for Acis.", "\"운명이 내 손에 남겨 둔 모든 것을 나는 아키스를 위하여 다하였다."], ["I endowed him with the honors of his grandfather, the river-god.", "나는 그에게 그의 외조부인 강의 신이 누리던 영예를 부여하였다."], ["The purple blood flowed out from under the rock, but by degrees grew paler and looked like the stream of a river rendered turbid by rains, and in time it became clear.", "자줏빛 피가 바위 아래에서 흘러나왔으나, 차츰 옅어지더니 비로 흐려진 강물의 흐름처럼 보이게 되었고, 이윽고 맑아졌다."], ["The rock cleaved open, and the water, as it gushed from the chasm, uttered a pleasing murmur.”", "바위가 쩍 갈라져 열렸으며, 그 틈에서 솟아 나오는 물이 듣기 좋은 속삭임 소리를 냈다.\""]], [["Thus Acis was changed into a river, and the river retains the name of Acis.", "이리하여 아키스는 강으로 변하였고, 그 강은 지금도 아키스라는 이름을 간직하고 있다."]], [["Dryden, in his “Cymon and Iphigenia,” has told the story of a clown converted into a gentleman by the power of love, in a way that shows traces of kindred to the old story of Galatea and the Cyclops.", "드라이든은 그의 「키몬과 이피게네이아」에서 사랑의 힘으로 시골뜨기가 신사로 바뀌는 이야기를 들려주었는데, 그 방식에는 갈라테이아와 키클롭스의 옛이야기와 닮은 흔적이 엿보인다."]], [["“What not his father’s care nor tutor’s art Could plant with pains in his unpolished heart, The best instructor, Love, at once inspired, As barren grounds to fruitfulness are fired.", "\"그의 아버지의 보살핌도 스승의 재주도 그 거친 마음에 애써 심어 줄 수 없던 것을, 가장 뛰어난 스승인 사랑이 단숨에 불어넣었으니, 메마른 땅이 불길에 닿아 비옥해지듯 하였다."], ["Love taught him shame, and shame with love at strife Soon taught the sweet civilities of life.”", "사랑은 그에게 수치를 가르쳤고, 수치는 사랑과 다투며 이내 삶의 그 다정한 예절을 가르쳐 주었다.\""], ["————", "————"]], [["# THE TROJAN WAR", "# THE TROJAN WAR"]], [["MINERVA was the goddess of wisdom, but on one occasion she did a very foolish thing; she entered into competition with Juno and Venus for the prize of beauty.", "미네르바[로마의 〈미네르바〉]는 지혜의 여신이었으나, 한번은 매우 어리석은 짓을 저질렀다. 그녀가 헤라[로마의 〈유노〉] 및 아프로디테[로마의 〈베누스〉]와 더불어 아름다움의 상을 두고 다툼에 끼어든 것이다."], ["It happened thus: At the nuptials of Peleus and Thetis all the gods were invited with the exception of Eris, or Discord.", "사연인즉 이러하였다. 펠레우스와 테티스의 혼인 잔치에 모든 신이 초대를 받았으나, 오직 에리스[불화의 여신]만은 예외였다."], ["Enraged at her exclusion, the goddess threw a golden apple among the guests, with the inscription, “For the fairest.”", "자신이 제외된 것에 분노한 여신은 손님들 사이로 황금 사과 하나를 던졌는데, 거기에는 \"가장 아름다운 이에게\"라는 글귀가 새겨져 있었다."], ["Thereupon Juno, Venus, and Minerva each claimed the apple.", "그러자 헤라와 아프로디테와 미네르바가 저마다 그 사과가 자기 것이라고 주장하였다."], ["Jupiter, not willing to decide in so delicate a matter, sent the goddesses to Mount Ida, where the beautiful shepherd Paris was tending his flocks, and to him was committed the decision.", "제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]는 이처럼 미묘한 문제를 직접 판가름하고 싶지 않았으므로, 여신들을 이데 산으로 보냈다. 그곳에서는 아름다운 목동 파리스가 양 떼를 돌보고 있었으니, 결정은 그에게 맡겨졌다."], ["The goddesses accordingly appeared before him.", "이리하여 여신들은 그의 앞에 나타났다."], ["Juno promised him power and riches, Minerva glory and renown in war, and Venus the fairest of women for his wife, each attempting to bias his decision in her own favor.", "헤라는 권세와 부를, 미네르바는 전쟁에서의 영광과 명성을, 아프로디테는 가장 아름다운 여인을 아내로 주겠노라 약속하여, 저마다 그의 판단을 자기 쪽으로 기울게 하려 하였다."], ["Paris decided in favor of Venus and gave her the golden apple, thus making the two other goddesses his enemies.", "파리스는 아프로디테의 손을 들어 주고 그녀에게 황금 사과를 건넸으니, 이로써 다른 두 여신을 적으로 돌리고 말았다."], ["Under the protection of Venus, Paris sailed to Greece, and was hospitably received by Menelaus, king of Sparta.", "아프로디테의 보호 아래 파리스는 그리스로 배를 띄웠고, 스파르타의 왕 메넬라오스에게서 후한 대접을 받았다."], ["Now Helen, the wife of Menelaus, was the very woman whom Venus had destined for Paris, the fairest of her sex.", "그런데 메넬라오스의 아내 헬레네야말로 아프로디테가 파리스에게 점지해 둔 바로 그 여인, 곧 여인들 가운데 가장 아름다운 이였다."], ["She had been sought as a bride by numerous suitors, and before her decision was made known, they all, at the suggestion of Ulysses, one of their number, took an oath that they would defend her from all injury and avenge her cause if necessary.", "그녀는 수많은 구혼자들이 신붓감으로 탐낸 여인이었는데, 그녀의 선택이 알려지기 전에 그들은 모두, 자기들 가운데 하나였던 오디세우스의 제안에 따라, 그녀를 어떠한 해악으로부터도 지키고 필요하다면 그녀의 일을 위해 복수하겠노라 맹세하였다."], ["She chose Menelaus, and was living with him happily when Paris became their guest.", "그녀는 메넬라오스를 택하였고, 파리스가 그들의 손님이 되었을 때 그와 더불어 행복하게 살고 있었다."], ["Paris, aided by Venus, persuaded her to elope with him, and carried her to Troy, whence arose the famous Trojan war, the theme of the greatest poems of antiquity, those of Homer and Virgil.", "파리스는 아프로디테의 도움을 받아 그녀를 설득해 함께 달아나게 하였고, 그녀를 트로이아로 데려갔으니, 여기에서 그 유명한 트로이아 전쟁이 일어났다. 이는 호메로스와 베르길리우스의 작품들, 곧 고대 최고의 서사시들이 다룬 주제이다."]], [["Menelaus called upon his brother chieftains of Greece to fulfil their pledge, and join him in his efforts to recover his wife.", "메넬라오스는 그리스의 동료 족장들에게 그들이 한 맹세를 지켜, 아내를 되찾으려는 자신의 노력에 동참할 것을 호소하였다."], ["They generally came forward, but Ulysses, who had married Penelope, and was very happy in his wife and child, had no disposition to embark in such a troublesome affair.", "그들은 대체로 앞으로 나섰으나, 페넬로페와 혼인하여 아내와 자식과 더불어 매우 행복하게 지내던 오디세우스는 그처럼 성가신 일에 뛰어들 마음이 없었다."], ["He therefore hung back and Palamedes was sent to urge him.", "그리하여 그는 뒤로 물러서 있었고, 그를 재촉하기 위해 팔라메데스가 파견되었다."], ["When Palamedes arrived at Ithaca Ulysses pretended to be mad.", "팔라메데스가 이타케에 당도하자 오디세우스는 미친 척하였다."], ["He yoked an ass and an ox together to the plough and began to sow salt.", "그는 나귀와 소를 함께 쟁기에 메우고 소금을 뿌리기 시작하였다."], ["Palamedes, to try him, placed the infant Telemachus before the plough, whereupon the father turned the plough aside, showing plainly that he was no madman, and after that could no longer refuse to fulfil his promise.", "팔라메데스는 그를 시험하기 위하여 어린 텔레마코스를 쟁기 앞에 놓았는데, 그러자 아비는 쟁기를 옆으로 돌려, 자신이 미치광이가 아님을 분명히 드러내고 말았으니, 그 뒤로는 더 이상 약속을 지키기를 거부할 수 없었다."], ["Being now himself gained for the undertaking, he lent his aid to bring in other reluctant chiefs, especially Achilles.", "이제 스스로 이 일에 가담하게 된 그는, 마지못해하는 다른 족장들을, 특히 아킬레우스를 끌어들이는 데 힘을 보탰다."], ["This hero was the son of that Thetis at whose marriage the apple of Discord had been thrown among the goddesses.", "이 영웅은 혼인 잔치에서 불화의 사과가 여신들 사이로 던져졌던 바로 그 테티스의 아들이었다."], ["Thetis was herself one of the immortals, a sea-nymph, and knowing that her son was fated to perish before Troy if he went on the expedition, she endeavored to prevent his going.", "테티스 자신은 불사의 존재이자 바다의 님프였는데, 자기 아들이 만약 이 원정에 나선다면 트로이아 앞에서 죽을 운명임을 알았으므로, 그가 가는 것을 막으려 애썼다."], ["She sent him away to the court of King Lycomedes, and induced him to conceal himself in the disguise of a maiden among the daughters of the king.", "그녀는 그를 리코메데스 왕의 궁정으로 보내, 처녀의 옷차림으로 변장하여 왕의 딸들 틈에 숨어 지내도록 하였다."], ["Ulysses, hearing he was there, went disguised as a merchant to the palace and offered for sale female ornaments, among which he had placed some arms.", "오디세우스는 그가 그곳에 있다는 말을 듣고, 상인으로 변장하여 궁전으로 가서 여인용 장신구를 팔겠노라 내놓았는데, 그 가운데 무기 몇 점을 끼워 두었다."], ["While the king’s daughters were engrossed with the other contents of the merchant’s pack, Achilles handled the weapons and thereby betrayed himself to the keen eye of Ulysses, who found no great difficulty in persuading him to disregard his mother’s prudent counsels and join his countrymen in the war.", "왕의 딸들이 상인 보따리의 다른 물건들에 정신이 팔려 있는 동안, 아킬레우스는 무기를 만지작거렸고, 이로써 오디세우스의 날카로운 눈에 자신의 정체를 드러내고 말았다. 오디세우스는 그가 어머니의 신중한 충고를 저버리고 동포들과 함께 전쟁에 나서도록 설득하는 데 별 어려움이 없었다."]], [["Priam was king of Troy, and Paris, the shepherd and seducer of Helen, was his son.", "프리아모스는 트로이아의 왕이었고, 목동이자 헬레네를 꾄 파리스는 그의 아들이었다."], ["Paris had been brought up in obscurity, because there were certain ominous forebodings connected with him from his infancy that he would be the ruin of the state.", "파리스는 어려서부터 그가 나라의 파멸이 되리라는 어떤 불길한 예언이 따라다녔던 까닭에 이름 없이 자랐다."], ["These forebodings seemed at length likely to be realized, for the Grecian armament now in preparation was the greatest that had ever been fitted out.", "이 예언들은 마침내 실현될 것처럼 보였으니, 지금 준비되고 있는 그리스의 군세는 일찍이 갖추어진 어느 것보다도 거대하였기 때문이다."], ["Agamemnon, king of Mycenæ, and brother of the injured Menelaus, was chosen commander-in-chief.", "미케네의 왕이자 피해를 입은 메넬라오스의 형인 아가멤논이 총사령관으로 선출되었다."], ["Achilles was their most illustrious warrior.", "아킬레우스는 그들 가운데 가장 빛나는 전사였다."], ["After him ranked Ajax, gigantic in size and of great courage, but dull of intellect; Diomede, second only to Achilles in all the qualities of a hero; Ulysses, famous for his sagacity; and Nestor, the oldest of the Grecian chiefs, and one to whom they all looked up for counsel.", "그다음으로는 몸집이 거대하고 용맹은 대단하나 지혜가 둔한 아이아스, 영웅의 온갖 자질에서 아킬레우스에 버금가는 디오메데스, 명민함으로 이름난 오디세우스, 그리고 그리스 족장들 가운데 가장 나이가 많아 모두가 조언을 구하며 우러러보던 네스토르가 자리하였다."], ["But Troy was no feeble enemy.", "그러나 트로이아도 결코 만만한 적은 아니었다."], ["Priam, the king, was now old, but he had been a wise prince and had strengthened his state by good government at home and numerous alliances with his neighbors.", "왕 프리아모스는 이제 늙었으나, 일찍이 지혜로운 군주였으며 안으로는 선정을 펴고 밖으로는 이웃들과 수많은 동맹을 맺어 나라를 튼튼히 하였다."], ["But the principal stay and support of his throne was his son Hector, one of the noblest characters painted by heathen antiquity.", "그러나 그의 왕좌를 받치는 으뜸가는 기둥이자 버팀목은 그의 아들 헥토르였으니, 이는 이교 시대의 고대가 그려 낸 가장 고귀한 인물 가운데 하나였다."], ["He felt, from the first, a presentiment of the fall of his country, but still persevered in his heroic resistance, yet by no means justified the wrong which brought this danger upon her.", "그는 처음부터 조국의 멸망을 예감하였으나, 그럼에도 영웅다운 저항을 끝까지 이어 갔으며, 그러면서도 이 위험을 조국에 불러들인 잘못을 결코 두둔하지는 않았다."], ["He was united in marriage with Andromache, and as a husband and father his character was not less admirable than as a warrior.", "그는 안드로마케와 혼인하였는데, 남편이자 아버지로서의 그의 인품은 전사로서의 그것에 못지않게 칭송할 만하였다."], ["The principal leaders on the side of the Trojans, besides Hector, were Æneas and Deiphobus, Glaucus and Sarpedon.", "트로이아 편의 주요 지도자들로는 헥토르 외에 아이네아스와 데이포보스, 글라우코스와 사르페돈이 있었다."]], [["After two years of preparation the Greek fleet and army assembled in the port of Aulis in Bœotia.", "이 년에 걸친 준비 끝에 그리스의 함대와 군대가 보이오티아의 아울리스 항구에 집결하였다."], ["Here Agamemnon in hunting killed a stag which was sacred to Diana, and the goddess in return visited the army with pestilence, and produced a calm which prevented the ships from leaving the port.", "이곳에서 아가멤논은 사냥을 하다가 아르테미스[로마의 〈디아나〉]에게 바쳐진 사슴 한 마리를 죽였고, 여신은 그 보복으로 군대에 역병을 내렸으며, 또한 바람을 잠재워 배들이 항구를 떠나지 못하게 하였다."], ["Calchas, the soothsayer, thereupon announced that the wrath of the virgin goddess could only be appeased by the sacrifice of a virgin on her altar, and that none other but the daughter of the offender would be acceptable.", "그러자 예언자 칼카스는, 처녀 여신의 노여움은 오직 그 제단 위에 처녀 하나를 제물로 바쳐야만 가라앉힐 수 있으며, 잘못을 저지른 자의 딸이 아니고서는 다른 누구도 받아들여지지 않으리라고 알렸다."], ["Agamemnon, however reluctant, yielded his consent, and the maiden Iphigenia was sent for under the pretence that she was to be married to Achilles.", "아가멤논은 비록 마지못한 일이었으나 동의하였고, 처녀 이피게네이아가 아킬레우스와 혼인하게 되었다는 구실로 불려 왔다."], ["When she was about to be sacrificed the goddess relented and snatched her away, leaving a hind in her place, and Iphigenia, enveloped in a cloud, was carried to Tauris, where Diana made her priestess of her temple.", "그녀가 막 제물로 바쳐지려 할 때 여신은 마음이 누그러져 그녀를 낚아채 가고 그 자리에 암사슴 한 마리를 남겨 두었으며, 이피게네이아는 구름에 싸인 채 타우리스로 옮겨져, 그곳에서 아르테미스가 그녀를 자기 신전의 여사제로 삼았다."]], [["Tennyson, in his “Dream of Fair Women,” makes Iphigenia thus describe her feelings at the moment of sacrifice:", "테니슨은 그의 「아름다운 여인들의 꿈」에서 이피게네이아가 제물로 바쳐지는 순간에 느낀 심정을 다음과 같이 묘사하고 있다."]], [["“I was cut off from hope in that sad place, Which yet to name my spirit loathes and fears; My father held his hand upon his face; I, blinded by my tears,\n“Still strove to speak; my voice was thick with sighs, As in a dream.", "\"나는 그 슬픈 곳에서 모든 희망으로부터 끊겨 있었으니, 그 이름을 부르는 것조차 내 영혼은 꺼리고 두려워하노라. 내 아버지는 손으로 얼굴을 가렸고, 나는 눈물에 눈이 멀어, \"그래도 말하려 애썼으나, 내 목소리는 한숨에 막혔으니, 마치 꿈속과도 같았노라."], ["Dimly I could descry The stern black-bearded kings, with wolfish eyes, Waiting to see me die.", "어슴푸레 나는 보았네, 이리 같은 눈을 한, 검은 수염의 준엄한 왕들이, 내가 죽는 것을 지켜보려 기다리는 모습을."]], [["“The tall masts quivered as they lay afloat, The temples and the people and the shore; One drew a sharp knife through my tender throat Slowly,—and—nothing more.”", "\"높다란 돛대들이 물 위에 떠 흔들렸고, 신전들과 사람들과 해안도 그러하였네. 누군가 날카로운 칼을 내 여린 목에 천천히 그었노라, 그리고 그것으로 끝, 더는 아무것도 없었네.\""]], [["The wind now proving fair the fleet made sail and brought the forces to the coast of Troy.", "이제 바람이 순조로워지자 함대는 돛을 올려 병력을 트로이아의 해안으로 실어 갔다."], ["The Trojans came to oppose their landing, and at the first onset Protesilaus fell by the hand of Hector.", "트로이아인들이 그들의 상륙을 막으러 나왔고, 첫 충돌에서 프로테실라오스가 헥토르의 손에 쓰러졌다."], ["Protesilaus had left at home his wife, Laodamia, who was most tenderly attached to him.", "프로테실라오스는 고향에 그를 더없이 애틋하게 사랑하는 아내 라오다메이아를 남겨 두었다."], ["When the news of his death reached her she implored the gods to be allowed to converse with him only three hours.", "그의 죽음을 알리는 소식이 그녀에게 닿자, 그녀는 단 세 시간만이라도 그와 이야기를 나눌 수 있게 해 달라고 신들에게 간청하였다."], ["The request was granted.", "그 청은 받아들여졌다."], ["Mercury led Protesilaus back to the upper world, and when he died a second time Laodamia died with him.", "헤르메스[로마의 〈메르쿠리우스〉]가 프로테실라오스를 이승으로 데려왔고, 그가 두 번째로 죽었을 때 라오다메이아도 그와 함께 죽었다."], ["There was a story that the nymphs planted elm trees round his grave which grew very well till they were high enough to command a view of Troy, and then withered away, while fresh branches sprang from the roots.", "님프들이 그의 무덤 둘레에 느릅나무를 심었는데, 그 나무들이 트로이아가 내려다보일 만큼 자라자 시들어 버렸고, 그 뿌리에서 새 가지들이 돋아났다는 이야기도 전한다."]], [["Wordsworth has taken the story of Protesilaus and Laodamia for the subject of a poem.", "워즈워스는 프로테실라오스와 라오다메이아의 이야기를 시의 주제로 삼았다."], ["It seems the oracle had declared that victory should be the lot of that party from which should fall the first victim to the war.", "신탁이 선언하기를, 전쟁의 첫 희생자가 나오는 편에게 승리가 돌아가리라 하였던 모양이다."], ["The poet represents Protesilaus, on his brief return to earth, as relating to Laodamia the story of his fate:", "시인은 프로테실라오스가 잠시 이승으로 돌아왔을 때, 라오다메이아에게 자신의 운명에 얽힌 사연을 들려주는 것으로 그리고 있다."]], [["“‘The wished-for wind was given; I then revolved The oracle, upon the silent sea; And if no worthier led the way, resolved That of a thousand vessels mine should be The foremost prow impressing to the strand,— Mine the first blood that tinged the Trojan sand. “‘Yet bitter, ofttimes bitter was the pang When of thy loss I thought, beloved wife! On thee too fondly did my memory hang, And on the joys we shared in mortal life, The paths which we had trod,—these fountains, flowers; My new planned cities and unfinished towers.", "\"'바라던 바람이 불어왔노라. 그때 나는 고요한 바다 위에서 신탁을 곱씹었노라. 그리고 나보다 더 합당한 자가 앞장서지 않는다면, 천 척의 배 가운데 내 배가 맨 먼저 그 뱃머리를 해안에 들이박으리라 결심하였네, 내 피가 트로이아의 모래를 처음으로 물들이리라고. \"'허나 사랑하는 아내여, 그대를 잃을 생각을 하면 그 아픔은 쓰라리고, 또 쓰라렸노라! 내 기억은 그대에게 너무도 애틋하게 매달렸고, 또 우리가 필멸의 삶에서 함께 누린 기쁨들, 우리가 거닐던 길들, 이 샘물들과 꽃들, 내가 새로이 세우려던 도시들과 미처 짓다 만 탑들에도. \"'허나 만약 머뭇거림이 적으로 하여금 외치게 한다면, \"보라, 저들이 떨고 있도다! 진영은 거만하건만, 그 수 가운데 누구도 감히 죽으려 하지 않는구나!\"라고, 나는 영혼으로 그 치욕을 쓸어 버렸노라. 그때 옛 약함이 되살아났으나, 드높은 생각이 행동으로 구현되어 나의 떠남을 이루었노라.' . . . . . . ."]], [["“‘But should suspense permit the foe to cry, “Behold they tremble!", "\"."], ["haughty their array, Yet of their number no one dares to die?”", ". ."], ["In soul I swept the indignity away: Old frailties then recurred: but lofty thought In act embodied my deliverance wrought.’", "헬레스폰토스의 기슭에 (이런 믿음이 전해져 왔으니) 그녀가 그를 위해 죽은 그 사내의 무덤에서 뾰족한 나무 한 무리가 오랜 세월 자라났으니, 그리고 일리온의 성벽이 그 눈 아래 들어올 만큼 그 키가 자라날 때마다, 그 나무들의 높은 우듬지는 그 광경에 시들어 갔으니, 자라남과 마름이 끊임없이 갈마들었노라!\""]], [[". . . . . . .", "「일리아스」"]], [["“. .", "전쟁은 구 년 동안 결정적인 결말 없이 이어졌다."], [". upon the side Of Hellespont (such faith was entertained) A knot of spiry trees for ages grew From out the tomb of him for whom she died; And ever when such stature they had gained That Ilium’s walls were subject to their view, The trees’ tall summits withered at the sight, A constant interchange of growth and blight!”", "그러던 중 그리스 측의 대의에 치명적일 듯한 사건이 일어났으니, 곧 아킬레우스와 아가멤논 사이의 다툼이었다."], ["The war continued without decisive results for nine years.", "호메로스의 위대한 서사시 「일리아스」가 시작되는 것이 바로 이 지점이다."], ["Then an event occurred which seemed likely to be fatal to the cause of the Greeks, and that was a quarrel between Achilles and Agamemnon.", "그리스인들은 트로이아를 함락하지는 못하였으나, 그 인근의 동맹 도시들을 빼앗았는데, 전리품을 나누는 과정에서 아폴론의 사제 크리세스의 딸인 크리세이스라는 여인 포로가 아가멤논의 몫으로 떨어졌다."], ["It is at this point that the great poem of Homer, “The Iliad,” begins.", "크리세스는 자기 직무의 성스러운 표지를 지니고 와서 딸을 풀어 달라고 간청하였다."], ["The Greeks, though unsuccessful against Troy, had taken the neighboring and allied cities, and in the division of the spoil a female captive, by name Chryseis, daughter of Chryses, priest of Apollo, had fallen to the share of Agamemnon.", "아가멤논은 거절하였다. 그러자 크리세스는 그리스인들이 그 노획물을 내놓을 수밖에 없도록 그들을 괴롭혀 달라고 아폴론에게 빌었다."], ["Chryses came bearing the sacred emblems of his office, and begged the release of his daughter.", "아폴론은 자기 사제의 기도를 들어주어 그리스 진영에 역병을 보냈다."], ["Agamemnon refused. Thereupon Chryses implored Apollo to afflict the Greeks till they should be forced to yield their prey.", "그리하여 신들의 노여움을 어떻게 가라앉히고 역병을 물리칠지 의논하기 위한 회의가 소집되었다."], ["Apollo granted the prayer of his priest, and sent pestilence into the Grecian camp. Then a council was called to deliberate how to allay the wrath of the gods and avert the plague.", "아킬레우스는 그들의 불행이 아가멤논이 크리세이스를 내놓지 않은 데서 비롯된 것이라며 대담하게 그에게 책임을 돌렸다."], ["Achilles boldly charged their misfortunes upon Agamemnon as caused by his withholding Chryseis. Agamemnon, enraged, consented to relinquish his captive, but demanded that Achilles should yield to him in her stead Briseis, a maiden who had fallen to Achilles’ share in the division of the spoil.", "아가멤논은 격분하여 자기 포로를 내놓는 데 동의하였으나, 그 대신 아킬레우스가 전리품을 나눌 때 자기 몫으로 떨어진 처녀 브리세이스를 자기에게 넘기라고 요구하였다."], ["Achilles submitted, but forthwith declared that he would take no further part in the war.", "아킬레우스는 순순히 따랐으나, 곧장 자신은 더 이상 전쟁에 가담하지 않겠노라 선언하였다."], ["He withdrew his forces from the general camp and openly avowed his intention of returning home to Greece.", "그는 전체 진영에서 자기 병력을 거두어들이고, 그리스로 돌아가겠다는 뜻을 공공연히 밝혔다."]], [["The gods and goddesses interested themselves as much in this famous war as the parties themselves.", "신들과 여신들은 이 유명한 전쟁에 당사자들 못지않게 깊은 관심을 기울였다."], ["It was well known to them that fate had decreed that Troy should fall, at last, if her enemies should persevere and not voluntarily abandon the enterprise.", "트로이아의 적들이 끝까지 버티고 제 발로 이 일을 포기하지 않는다면 마침내 트로이아가 함락되리라는 것이 운명에 의해 정해져 있음을, 그들은 잘 알고 있었다."], ["Yet there was room enough left for chance to excite by turns the hopes and fears of the powers above who took part with either side.", "그러나 어느 한쪽 편을 든 천상의 권능들의 기대와 두려움을 번갈아 자극할 만한 우연의 여지는 여전히 충분히 남아 있었다."], ["Juno and Minerva, in consequence of the slight put upon their charms by Paris, were hostile to the Trojans; Venus for the opposite cause favored them.", "헤라와 미네르바는 파리스가 자기들의 아름다움을 무시한 탓에 트로이아인들에게 적대적이었고, 아프로디테는 그와 반대되는 까닭에 그들을 편들었다."], ["Venus enlisted her admirer Mars on the same side, but Neptune favored the Greeks.", "아프로디테는 자기를 흠모하는 아레스[로마의 〈마르스〉]를 같은 편으로 끌어들였으나, 포세이돈[로마의 〈넵투누스〉]은 그리스인들을 편들었다."], ["Apollo was neutral, sometimes taking one side, sometimes the other, and Jove himself, though he loved the good King Priam, yet exercised a degree of impartiality; not, however, without exceptions.", "아폴론은 중립이어서 때로는 이쪽을, 때로는 저쪽을 들었고, 제우스 자신은 비록 선량한 왕 프리아모스를 사랑하였으나 어느 정도 공정함을 지켰는데, 다만 예외가 없지는 않았다."]], [["Thetis, the mother of Achilles, warmly resented the injury done to her son.", "아킬레우스의 어머니 테티스는 자기 아들이 입은 모욕에 격하게 분노하였다."], ["She repaired immediately to Jove’s palace and besought him to make the Greeks repent of their injustice to Achilles by granting success to the Trojan arms.", "그녀는 곧장 제우스의 궁전으로 가서, 트로이아의 무력에 승리를 안겨 줌으로써 그리스인들이 아킬레우스에게 저지른 불의를 뉘우치게 해 달라고 그에게 간청하였다."], ["Jupiter consented, and in the battle which ensued the Trojans were completely successful.", "제우스는 동의하였고, 뒤이은 전투에서 트로이아인들은 완전한 승리를 거두었다."], ["The Greeks were driven from the field and took refuge in their ships.", "그리스인들은 전장에서 쫓겨나 자기들의 배 안으로 피신하였다."]], [["Then Agamemnon called a council of his wisest and bravest chiefs.", "그러자 아가멤논은 가장 지혜롭고 용감한 족장들을 불러 회의를 열었다."], ["Nestor advised that an embassy should be sent to Achilles to persuade him to return to the field; that Agamemnon should yield the maiden, the cause of the dispute, with ample gifts to atone for the wrong he had done.", "네스토르는 아킬레우스에게 사절을 보내 그를 설득하여 전장으로 돌아오게 하고, 아가멤논은 분쟁의 원인이 된 그 처녀를 넘겨주되 자신이 저지른 잘못을 보상할 넉넉한 선물을 함께 보내자고 권하였다."], ["Agamemnon consented, and Ulysses, Ajax, and Phœnix were sent to carry to Achilles the penitent message.", "아가멤논이 동의하니, 오디세우스와 아이아스와 포이닉스가 아킬레우스에게 뉘우침의 전갈을 전하러 파견되었다."], ["They performed that duty, but Achilles was deaf to their entreaties.", "그들은 그 임무를 수행하였으나, 아킬레우스는 그들의 간청에 귀를 닫았다."], ["He positively refused to return to the field, and persisted in his resolution to embark for Greece without delay.", "그는 전장으로 돌아가기를 단호히 거부하였고, 지체 없이 그리스로 떠나겠다는 결심을 굽히지 않았다."]], [["The Greeks had constructed a rampart around their ships, and now instead of besieging Troy they were in a manner besieged themselves, within their rampart.", "그리스인들은 자기들의 배 둘레에 방벽을 쌓아 두었는데, 이제 그들은 트로이아를 포위하기는커녕 도리어 자기들의 방벽 안에 갇혀 포위당한 형국이 되었다."], ["The next day after the unsuccessful embassy to Achilles, a battle was fought, and the Trojans, favored by Jove, were successful, and succeeded in forcing a passage through the Grecian rampart, and were about to set fire to the ships.", "아킬레우스에게 보낸 사절이 실패한 다음 날 전투가 벌어졌고, 제우스의 가호를 받은 트로이아인들이 승리하여 그리스의 방벽을 뚫고 길을 내는 데 성공하였으며, 막 배에 불을 지르려 하였다."], ["Neptune, seeing the Greeks so pressed, came to their rescue.", "포세이돈은 그리스인들이 그처럼 몰리는 것을 보고 그들을 구하러 왔다."], ["He appeared in the form of Calchas the prophet, encouraged the warriors with his shouts, and appealed to each individually till he raised their ardor to such a pitch that they forced the Trojans to give way.", "그는 예언자 칼카스의 모습으로 나타나 외침으로 전사들을 격려하고 한 사람 한 사람에게 호소하여, 마침내 그들의 투지를 한껏 끌어올려 트로이아인들을 물러서게 만들었다."], ["Ajax performed prodigies of valor, and at length encountered Hector.", "아이아스는 놀라운 용맹을 펼쳤고, 마침내 헥토르와 맞닥뜨렸다."], ["Ajax shouted defiance, to which Hector replied, and hurled his lance at the huge warrior.", "아이아스가 도전의 함성을 지르자 헥토르도 이에 응수하며 그 거대한 전사를 향해 창을 던졌다."], ["It was well aimed and struck Ajax, where the belts that bore his sword and shield crossed each other on the breast.", "창은 잘 겨냥되어 아이아스의 가슴에서 칼과 방패를 멘 두 띠가 엇갈리는 곳을 맞혔다."], ["The double guard prevented its penetrating and it fell harmless.", "이중의 보호막이 창이 뚫고 들어가는 것을 막아 창은 아무 해도 없이 떨어졌다."], ["Then Ajax, seizing a huge stone, one of those that served to prop the ships, hurled it at Hector.", "그러자 아이아스는 배를 받치는 데 쓰이던 커다란 돌 하나를 집어 헥토르를 향해 던졌다."], ["It struck him in the neck and stretched him on the plain.", "돌은 그의 목을 맞혀 그를 들판에 쓰러뜨렸다."], ["His followers instantly seized him and bore him off, stunned and wounded.", "그의 부하들이 곧장 그를 붙들어, 정신을 잃고 부상당한 그를 들쳐 메고 물러갔다."]], [["While Neptune was thus aiding the Greeks and driving back the Trojans, Jupiter saw nothing of what was going on, for his attention had been drawn from the field by the wiles of Juno.", "포세이돈이 이처럼 그리스인들을 돕고 트로이아인들을 물리치는 동안, 제우스는 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 전혀 알지 못하였으니, 헤라의 술책에 그의 주의가 전장에서 돌려졌기 때문이다."], ["That goddess had arrayed herself in all her charms, and to crown all had borrowed of Venus her girdle, called “Cestus,” which had the effect to heighten the wearer’s charms to such a degree that they were quite irresistible.", "그 여신은 자신의 온갖 매력으로 단장하였고, 거기에 더하여 아프로디테에게서 \"케스토스\"라 불리는 띠를 빌렸는데, 그것은 두른 자의 매력을 도저히 거역할 수 없을 만큼 한껏 돋우는 효력이 있었다."], ["So prepared, Juno went to join her husband, who sat on Olympus watching the battle.", "이렇게 단장한 헤라는 올림포스에 앉아 전투를 지켜보고 있던 남편에게로 갔다."], ["When he beheld her she looked so charming that the fondness of his early love revived, and, forgetting the contending armies and all other affairs of state, he thought only of her and let the battle go as it would.", "제우스가 그녀를 바라보니 그녀가 어찌나 매혹적이던지, 젊은 날 사랑의 애틋함이 되살아나, 그는 맞싸우는 군대들도 다른 모든 국사도 잊고 오직 그녀만을 생각하며 전투가 되어 가는 대로 내버려 두었다."]], [["But this absorption did not continue long, and when, upon turning his eyes downward, he beheld Hector stretched on the plain almost lifeless from pain and bruises, he dismissed Juno in a rage, commanding her to send Iris and Apollo to him.", "그러나 이 도취는 오래 가지 않았으니, 그가 눈길을 아래로 돌려 헥토르가 아픔과 타박상으로 거의 숨이 끊긴 채 들판에 쓰러져 있는 것을 보았을 때, 그는 노하여 헤라를 물리치고 이리스와 아폴론을 자기에게 보내라고 명하였다."], ["When Iris came he sent her with a stern message to Neptune, ordering him instantly to quit the field.", "이리스가 오자 그는 그녀를 시켜 포세이돈에게 준엄한 전갈을 보내, 그에게 당장 전장을 떠나라고 명령하였다."], ["Apollo was despatched to heal Hector’s bruises and to inspirit his heart.", "아폴론은 헥토르의 타박상을 치유하고 그의 마음에 기운을 불어넣으라고 파견되었다."], ["These orders were obeyed with such speed that, while the battle still raged, Hector returned to the field and Neptune betook himself to his own dominions.", "이 명령들은 어찌나 빨리 이행되었던지, 전투가 아직 한창인 가운데 헥토르가 전장으로 돌아왔고 포세이돈은 자신의 영역으로 물러갔다."]], [["An arrow from Paris’s bow wounded Machaon, son of Æsculapius, who inherited his father’s art of healing, and was therefore of great value to the Greeks as their surgeon, besides being one of their bravest warriors.", "파리스의 활에서 날아온 화살 하나가 아스클레피오스의 아들 마카온을 맞혔다. 그는 아버지의 치유 기술을 물려받았기에 그리스인들에게 외과의로서 큰 가치가 있었으며, 또한 그들 가운데 가장 용감한 전사 중 하나이기도 하였다."], ["Nestor took Machaon in his chariot and conveyed him from the field.", "네스토르가 마카온을 자기 전차에 태워 전장에서 데리고 나왔다."], ["As they passed the ships of Achilles, that hero, looking out over the field, saw the chariot of Nestor and recognized the old chief, but could not discern who the wounded chief was.", "그들이 아킬레우스의 배 곁을 지날 때, 들판을 내다보고 있던 그 영웅은 네스토르의 전차를 보고 그 노족장을 알아보았으나, 부상당한 자가 누구인지는 분간하지 못하였다."], ["So calling Patroclus, his companion and dearest friend, he sent him to Nestor’s tent to inquire.", "그리하여 그는 자기 동무이자 가장 소중한 벗인 파트로클로스를 불러, 네스토르의 막사로 가서 알아보고 오라고 보냈다."]], [["Patroclus, arriving at Nestor’s tent, saw Machaon wounded, and having told the cause of his coming would have hastened away, but Nestor detained him, to tell him the extent of the Grecian calamities.", "파트로클로스는 네스토르의 막사에 이르러 마카온이 부상당한 것을 보았고, 자신이 온 까닭을 말한 뒤 서둘러 돌아가려 하였으나, 네스토르가 그를 붙들어 그리스인들이 겪은 재앙이 얼마나 큰지를 일러 주었다. 그는 또한 트로이아로 떠날 무렵 아킬레우스와 그가 각자의 아버지로부터 서로 다른 당부를 받았던 일도 일깨워 주었으니, 아킬레우스는 영광의 가장 높은 봉우리를 갈망하라는 것이었고, 파트로클로스는 손위인 만큼 벗을 지켜보며 그의 미숙함을 이끌어 주라는 것이었다."], ["He reminded him also how, at the time of departing for Troy, Achilles and himself had been charged by their respective fathers with different advice: Achilles to aspire to the highest pitch of glory, Patroclus, as the elder, to keep watch over his friend, and to guide his inexperience.", "네스토르가 말하였다. \"지금이야말로 그런 영향력을 발휘할 때일세."], ["“Now,” said Nestor, “is the time for such influence.", "신들이 허락하신다면, 자네가 그를 우리 공동의 대의로 되돌려 놓을 수도 있을 것이네."], ["If the gods so please, thou mayest win him back to the common cause; but if not let him at least send his soldiers to the field, and come thou, Patroclus, clad in his armor, and perhaps the very sight of it may drive back the Trojans.”", "허나 그렇지 못하더라도 적어도 그가 자기 병사들이나마 전장에 보내게 하고, 파트로클로스, 자네가 그의 갑옷을 입고 나가게나. 어쩌면 그 모습만으로도 트로이아인들을 물러서게 할 수 있을지 모르니.\""]], [["Patroclus was strongly moved with this address, and hastened back to Achilles, revolving in his mind all he had seen and heard.", "파트로클로스는 이 말에 크게 감동하여, 자신이 보고 들은 모든 것을 마음속으로 곱씹으며 서둘러 아킬레우스에게 돌아왔다."], ["He told the prince the sad condition of affairs at the camp of their late associates: Diomede, Ulysses, Agamemnon, Machaon, all wounded, the rampart broken down, the enemy among the ships preparing to burn them, and thus to cut off all means of return to Greece.", "그는 한때 동료였던 이들의 진영이 처한 비참한 상황을 그 영웅에게 전하였다. 디오메데스, 오디세우스, 아가멤논, 마카온이 모두 부상당하였고, 방벽은 무너졌으며, 적은 배들 사이에 들어와 그것에 불을 질러 그리스로 돌아갈 모든 길을 끊으려 하고 있다는 것이었다."], ["While they spoke the flames burst forth from one of the ships.", "그들이 이야기를 나누는 동안 배 한 척에서 불길이 치솟았다."], ["Achilles, at the sight, relented so far as to grant Patroclus his request to lead the Myrmidons (for so were Achilles’ soldiers called) to the field, and to lend him his armor, that he might thereby strike more terror into the minds of the Trojans.", "그 광경을 본 아킬레우스는 마음이 누그러져, 파트로클로스가 미르미돈족(아킬레우스의 병사들은 그렇게 불렸다)을 이끌고 전장으로 나가게 해 달라는 청을 들어주고, 또 그가 트로이아인들의 마음에 더 큰 두려움을 심을 수 있도록 자기 갑옷을 빌려주기에 이르렀다."], ["Without delay the soldiers were marshalled, Patroclus put on the radiant armor and mounted the chariot of Achilles, and led forth the men ardent for battle.", "지체 없이 병사들이 대오를 갖추었고, 파트로클로스는 빛나는 갑옷을 걸치고 아킬레우스의 전차에 올라, 싸움에 불타는 병사들을 이끌고 나아갔다."], ["But before he went, Achilles strictly charged him that he should be content with repelling the foe.", "그러나 그가 떠나기 전에 아킬레우스는 그에게 적을 물리치는 것으로 만족하라고 엄히 당부하였다."], ["“Seek not,”", "그가 말하였다."], ["said he, “to press the Trojans without me, lest thou add still more to the disgrace already mine.”", "\"나 없이 트로이아인들을 몰아붙이려 하지 말게. 그리하여 이미 내게 씌워진 치욕에 더 보태지 않도록 하게나.\""], ["Then exhorting the troops to do their best he dismissed them full of ardor to the fight.", "그러고 나서 그는 병사들에게 최선을 다하라 격려하며 싸움의 열의에 가득 찬 그들을 떠나보냈다."]], [["Patroclus and his Myrmidons at once plunged into the contest where it raged hottest; at the sight of which the joyful Grecians shouted and the ships reëchoed the acclaim.", "파트로클로스와 그의 미르미돈족은 싸움이 가장 격렬한 곳으로 곧장 뛰어들었다. 그 광경에 기뻐한 그리스인들이 함성을 질렀고 배들이 그 환호를 되울렸다."], ["The Trojans, at the sight of the well-known armor, struck with terror, looked everywhere for refuge.", "트로이아인들은 그 낯익은 갑옷을 보고 두려움에 사로잡혀 사방으로 피할 곳을 찾았다."], ["First those who had got possession of the ship and set it on fire left and allowed the Grecians to retake it and extinguish the flames.", "먼저 배를 차지하여 불을 질렀던 자들이 떠나면서 그리스인들이 그 배를 되찾아 불길을 끄게 하였다."], ["Then the rest of the Trojans fled in dismay.", "그러자 나머지 트로이아인들도 겁에 질려 달아났다."], ["Ajax, Menelaus, and the two sons of Nestor performed prodigies of valor.", "아이아스와 메넬라오스와 네스토르의 두 아들은 놀라운 용맹을 펼쳤다."], ["Hector was forced to turn his horses’ heads and retire from the enclosure, leaving his men entangled in the fosse to escape as they could.", "헥토르는 말머리를 돌려 그 울타리에서 물러설 수밖에 없었고, 자기 부하들이 참호에 얽혀 제 살길대로 빠져나오도록 내버려 두었다."], ["Patroclus drove them before him, slaying many, none daring to make a stand against him.", "파트로클로스는 그들을 앞으로 몰아대며 많은 자를 죽였으니, 누구도 감히 그에게 맞서지 못하였다."]], [["At last Sarpedon, son of Jove, ventured to oppose himself in fight to Patroclus.", "마침내 제우스의 아들 사르페돈이 감히 나서서 파트로클로스와 싸움을 벌였다."], ["Jupiter looked down upon him and would have snatched him from the fate which awaited him, but Juno hinted that if he did so it would induce all others of the inhabitants of heaven to interpose in like manner whenever any of their offspring were endangered; to which reason Jove yielded.", "제우스는 그를 내려다보며 그를 기다리는 운명에서 낚아채 가고자 하였으나, 헤라가 만약 그가 그리한다면 천상의 다른 모든 거주자들도 자기 자손이 위험에 처할 때마다 똑같이 끼어들게 될 것이라고 일러 주었고, 제우스는 그 이치에 따랐다."], ["Sarpedon threw his spear, but missed Patroclus, but Patroclus threw his with better success.", "사르페돈이 창을 던졌으나 파트로클로스를 빗맞혔고, 파트로클로스가 던진 창은 더 정확하였다."], ["It pierced Sarpedon’s breast and he fell, and, calling to his friends to save his body from the foe, expired.", "그것은 사르페돈의 가슴을 꿰뚫었고, 그는 쓰러져 자기 벗들에게 자기 시신을 적에게서 지켜 달라고 외치며 숨을 거두었다."], ["Then a furious contest arose for the possession of the corpse.", "그러자 그 시신을 차지하려는 격렬한 다툼이 일어났다."], ["The Greeks succeeded and stripped Sarpedon of his armor; but Jove would not allow the remains of his son to be dishonored, and by his command Apollo snatched from the midst of the combatants the body of Sarpedon and committed it to the care of the twin brothers Death and Sleep, by whom it was transported to Lycia, the native land of Sarpedon, where it received due funeral rites.", "그리스인들이 이겨 사르페돈의 갑옷을 벗겨 냈으나, 제우스는 자기 아들의 유해가 욕보이는 것을 허락하지 않았으니, 그의 명에 따라 아폴론이 싸우는 자들 한가운데서 사르페돈의 시신을 낚아채 죽음과 잠이라는 쌍둥이 형제에게 맡겼고, 그들이 그것을 사르페돈의 고향 땅 리키아로 옮겨, 그곳에서 마땅한 장례 의식이 치러졌다."]], [["Thus far Patroclus had succeeded to his utmost wish in repelling the Trojans and relieving his countrymen, but now came a change of fortune.", "여기까지 파트로클로스는 트로이아인들을 물리치고 동포들을 구해 내려는 자신의 바람을 더할 나위 없이 이루었으나, 이제 운수가 뒤바뀌었다."], ["Hector, borne in his chariot, confronted him.", "전차에 탄 헥토르가 그와 맞섰다."], ["Patroclus threw a vast stone at Hector, which missed its aim, but smote Cebriones, the charioteer, and knocked him from the car.", "파트로클로스가 헥토르를 향해 거대한 돌을 던졌는데, 겨냥은 빗나갔으나 마부 케브리오네스를 맞혀 그를 수레에서 떨어뜨렸다."], ["Hector leaped from the chariot to rescue his friend, and Patroclus also descended to complete his victory.", "헥토르는 자기 벗을 구하려 전차에서 뛰어내렸고, 파트로클로스 또한 승리를 마무리하려 내려섰다."], ["Thus the two heroes met face to face.", "이리하여 두 영웅이 얼굴을 맞대고 마주 섰다."], ["At this decisive moment the poet, as if reluctant to give Hector the glory, records that Phœbus took part against Patroclus.", "이 결정적인 순간에 시인은, 마치 헥토르에게 영광을 주기를 꺼리는 듯, 포이보스[아폴론]가 파트로클로스에 맞서 가담하였음을 기록하고 있다."], ["He struck the helmet from his head and the lance from his hand.", "아폴론은 그의 머리에서 투구를 쳐서 떨어뜨리고 그의 손에서 창을 떨어뜨렸다."], ["At the same moment an obscure Trojan wounded him in the back, and Hector, pressing forward, pierced him with his spear.", "바로 그 순간 어느 이름 없는 트로이아인이 그의 등을 찔렀고, 헥토르가 앞으로 밀고 들어와 그를 창으로 꿰뚫었다."], ["He fell mortally wounded.", "그는 치명상을 입고 쓰러졌다."]], [["Then arose a tremendous conflict for the body of Patroclus, but his armor was at once taken possession of by Hector, who retiring a short distance divested himself of his own armor and put on that of Achilles, then returned to the fight.", "그러자 파트로클로스의 시신을 둘러싸고 엄청난 격투가 벌어졌으나, 그의 갑옷은 곧장 헥토르의 차지가 되었다. 헥토르는 잠시 물러나 자기 갑옷을 벗고 아킬레우스의 갑옷을 걸친 다음 다시 싸움으로 돌아왔다."], ["Ajax and Menelaus defended the body, and Hector and his bravest warriors struggled to capture it.", "아이아스와 메넬라오스가 시신을 지켰고, 헥토르와 그의 가장 용감한 전사들이 그것을 빼앗으려 안간힘을 썼다."], ["The battle raged with equal fortunes, when Jove enveloped the whole face of heaven with a dark cloud.", "싸움이 어느 쪽에도 기울지 않고 격렬하게 이어지던 그때, 제우스가 온 하늘을 어두운 구름으로 뒤덮었다."], ["The lightning flashed, the thunder roared, and Ajax, looking round for some one whom he might despatch to Achilles to tell him of the death of his friend, and of the imminent danger that his remains would fall into the hands of the enemy, could see no suitable messenger.", "번개가 번쩍이고 천둥이 울렸으니, 아이아스는 아킬레우스에게 보내어 그의 벗의 죽음과 그 유해가 적의 손에 떨어질 임박한 위험을 알릴 누군가를 찾아 둘러보았으나, 마땅한 전령을 찾을 수 없었다."], ["It was then that he exclaimed in those famous lines so often quoted,", "그가 그토록 자주 인용되는 저 유명한 시구로 부르짖은 것은 바로 그때였다."]], [["“Father of heaven and earth!", "\"하늘과 땅의 아버지시여! 당신께서 아카이아의 군대를 어둠에서 건져 주소서."], ["deliver thou Achaia’s host from darkness; clear the skies; Give day; and, since thy sovereign will is such, Destruction with it; but, O, give us day.”", "하늘을 맑게 하시고, 빛을 주소서. 그리고 당신의 지엄한 뜻이 그러하다면, 그와 더불어 파멸도 함께 주소서."], ["—Cowper.", "허나, 오, 우리에게 빛만은 주소서.\" ─카우퍼."]], [["Or, as rendered by Pope,", "또는 포프가 옮긴 대로는 이러하다."]], [["“.", "\"."], [".", "."], [".", "."], ["Lord of earth and air!", "땅과 하늘의 주여! 오 왕이시여!"], ["O king!", "오 아버지시여!"], ["O father!", "제 비천한 기도를 들으소서!"], ["hear my humble prayer!", "이 구름을 흩으시고, 하늘의 빛을 되돌려 주소서. 제게 볼 수 있게만 해 주시면 아이아스는 더 바라지 않나이다."], ["Dispel this cloud, the light of heaven restore; Give me to see and Ajax asks no more; If Greece must perish we thy will obey, But let us perish in the face of day.”", "그리스가 멸하여야 한다면 우리는 당신의 뜻에 따르겠나이다. 허나 우리로 하여금 대낮을 마주하고 멸하게 하소서.\""]], [["Jupiter heard the prayer and dispersed the clouds.", "제우스는 그 기도를 듣고 구름을 흩어 버렸다."], ["Then Ajax sent Antilochus to Achilles with the intelligence of Patroclus’s death, and of the conflict raging for his remains.", "그러자 아이아스는 안틸로코스를 아킬레우스에게 보내 파트로클로스의 죽음과, 그의 유해를 두고 벌어지는 격투의 소식을 전하게 하였다."], ["The Greeks at last succeeded in bearing off the body to the ships, closely pursued by Hector and Æneas and the rest of the Trojans.", "그리스인들은 마침내 헥토르와 아이네아스와 나머지 트로이아인들의 바짝 추격을 받으면서도 그 시신을 배까지 옮기는 데 성공하였다."]], [["Achilles heard the fate of his friend with such distress that Antilochus feared for a while that he would destroy himself.", "아킬레우스는 자기 벗의 운명을 전해 듣고 어찌나 비통해하던지, 안틸로코스는 한동안 그가 스스로 목숨을 끊지나 않을까 두려워하였다."], ["His groans reached the ears of his mother, Thetis, far down in the deeps of ocean where she abode, and she hastened to him to inquire the cause.", "그의 신음 소리는 그가 머무는 깊디깊은 대양 속 그의 어머니 테티스의 귀에까지 닿았고, 그녀는 그 까닭을 묻고자 그에게로 서둘러 왔다."], ["She found him overwhelmed with self-reproach that he had indulged his resentment so far, and suffered his friend to fall a victim to it.", "그녀는 그가, 자신의 분노에 그토록 빠져들어 벗이 그 분노의 희생물로 쓰러지게 둔 일에 대한 자책에 짓눌려 있는 것을 보았다."], ["But his only consolation was the hope of revenge.", "그러나 그의 유일한 위안은 복수의 희망이었다."], ["He would fly instantly in search of Hector.", "그는 당장 헥토르를 찾아 달려 나가려 하였다."], ["But his mother reminded him that he was now without armor, and promised him, if he would but wait till the morrow, she would procure for him a suit of armor from Vulcan more than equal to that he had lost.", "그러나 그의 어머니는 그가 지금 갑옷이 없는 처지임을 일깨우며, 만약 그가 다음 날 아침까지만 기다려 준다면 헤파이스토스[로마의 〈불카누스〉]에게서 그가 잃은 것보다 한층 뛰어난 갑옷 한 벌을 구해 오겠노라 약속하였다."], ["He consented, and Thetis immediately repaired to Vulcan’s palace.", "그가 동의하자 테티스는 즉시 헤파이스토스의 궁전으로 갔다."], ["She found him busy at his forge making tripods for his own use, so artfully constructed that they moved forward of their own accord when wanted, and retired again when dismissed.", "그녀는 그가 자기가 쓸 세발솥을 만드느라 화로에서 분주한 것을 보았는데, 그것은 어찌나 정교하게 만들어졌던지 필요할 때면 저절로 앞으로 움직여 나오고 물러나라 하면 다시 물러나는 것이었다."], ["On hearing the request of Thetis, Vulcan immediately laid aside his work and hastened to comply with her wishes.", "테티스의 청을 듣자 헤파이스토스는 곧장 하던 일을 내려놓고 서둘러 그녀의 바람을 들어주었다."], ["He fabricated a splendid suit of armor for Achilles, first a shield adorned with elaborate devices, then a helmet crested with gold, then a corselet and greaves of impenetrable temper, all perfectly adapted to his form, and of consummate workmanship.", "그는 아킬레우스를 위하여 화려한 갑옷 한 벌을 만들었으니, 먼저 정교한 무늬로 장식된 방패를, 다음으로 황금 깃을 단 투구를, 그다음으로 어떤 것도 뚫지 못할 강도로 단련된 흉갑과 정강이받이를 지었는데, 모두 그의 몸에 완벽하게 들어맞고 더없이 뛰어난 솜씨로 빚어진 것이었다."], ["It was all done in one night, and Thetis, receiving it, descended with it to earth, and laid it down at Achilles’ feet at the dawn of day.", "이 모든 것이 하룻밤 사이에 이루어졌고, 테티스는 그것을 받아 그것을 들고 땅으로 내려와, 동이 틀 무렵 아킬레우스의 발치에 그것을 내려놓았다."]], [["The first glow of pleasure that Achilles had felt since the death of Patroclus was at the sight of this splendid armor.", "파트로클로스가 죽은 이래 아킬레우스가 처음으로 느낀 기쁨의 빛은 이 화려한 갑옷을 본 순간이었다."], ["And now, arrayed in it, he went forth into the camp, calling all the chiefs to council.", "그리고 이제 그것을 걸친 채 그는 진영으로 나아가 모든 족장을 회의에 불러 모았다."], ["When they were all assembled he addressed them.", "그들이 모두 모이자 그는 그들에게 연설하였다."], ["Renouncing his displeasure against Agamemnon and bitterly lamenting the miseries that had resulted from it, he called on them to proceed at once to the field.", "아가멤논에 대한 노여움을 거두고 그로부터 비롯된 참화를 쓰라리게 한탄하며, 그는 그들에게 당장 전장으로 나아가자고 호소하였다."], ["Agamemnon made a suitable reply, laying all the blame on Ate, the goddess of discord; and thereupon complete reconcilement took place between the heroes.", "아가멤논도 합당한 대답을 하여 모든 잘못을 불화의 여신 아테에게 돌렸고, 이에 두 영웅 사이에 완전한 화해가 이루어졌다."]], [["Then Achilles went forth to battle inspired with a rage and thirst for vengeance that made him irresistible.", "그러고 나서 아킬레우스는 그를 거역할 수 없는 존재로 만드는 분노와 복수의 갈망에 사로잡혀 싸움터로 나아갔다."], ["The bravest warriors fled before him or fell by his lance.", "가장 용감한 전사들도 그 앞에서 달아나거나 그의 창에 쓰러졌다."], ["Hector, cautioned by Apollo, kept aloof; but the god, assuming the form of one of Priam’s sons, Lycaon, urged Æneas to encounter the terrible warrior.", "헥토르는 아폴론의 경고를 받고 멀찍이 떨어져 있었으나, 그 신은 프리아모스의 아들 리카온의 모습을 빌려 아이네아스에게 그 무서운 전사와 맞서라고 부추겼다."], ["Æneas, though he felt himself unequal, did not decline the combat.", "아이네아스는 자신이 그에 미치지 못함을 느끼면서도 싸움을 마다하지 않았다."], ["He hurled his spear with all his force against the shield the work of Vulcan.", "그는 온 힘을 다하여 헤파이스토스의 솜씨인 그 방패를 향해 창을 던졌다."], ["It was formed of five metal plates; two were of brass, two of tin, and one of gold.", "그것은 다섯 겹의 금속판으로 이루어져 있었으니, 둘은 청동이요, 둘은 주석이요, 하나는 황금이었다."], ["The spear pierced two thicknesses, but was stopped in the third.", "창은 두 겹을 뚫었으나 세 번째에서 멈추었다."], ["Achilles threw his with better success.", "아킬레우스가 던진 창은 더 정확하였다."], ["It pierced through the shield of Æneas, but glanced near his shoulder and made no wound.", "그것은 아이네아스의 방패를 꿰뚫었으나 그의 어깨 곁을 스쳐 상처를 입히지는 못하였다."], ["Then Æneas seized a stone, such as two men of modern times could hardly lift, and was about to throw it, and Achilles, with sword drawn, was about to rush upon him, when Neptune, who looked out upon the contest, moved with pity for Æneas, who he saw would surely fall a victim if not speedily rescued, spread a cloud between the combatants, and lifting Æneas from the ground, bore him over the heads of warriors and steeds to the rear of the battle.", "그러자 아이네아스는 요즘 사람 둘이 겨우 들어 올릴 만한 돌을 집어 막 던지려 하였고, 아킬레우스도 칼을 빼 들고 막 그에게 달려들려 하였는데, 그때 그 싸움을 지켜보던 포세이돈이, 신속히 구해 내지 않으면 아이네아스가 틀림없이 희생물이 되리라는 것을 보고 그에 대한 연민에 움직여, 싸우는 두 사람 사이에 구름을 펼치고 아이네아스를 땅에서 들어 올려, 전사들과 군마들의 머리 위로 그를 옮겨 전장의 후방에 내려놓았다."], ["Achilles, when the mist cleared away, looked round in vain for his adversary, and acknowledging the prodigy, turned his arms against other champions.", "안개가 걷히자 아킬레우스는 자기 적수를 찾아 헛되이 둘러보았고, 그 기이한 일을 인정하며 다른 용사들에게로 무기를 돌렸다."], ["But none dared stand before him, and Priam looking down from the city walls beheld his whole army in full flight towards the city.", "그러나 누구도 감히 그 앞에 서지 못하였고, 성벽에서 내려다보던 프리아모스는 자기 온 군대가 도성을 향해 황급히 달아나는 것을 보았다."], ["He gave command to open wide the gates to receive the fugitives, and to shut them as soon as the Trojans should have passed, lest the enemy should enter likewise.", "그는 도망쳐 오는 자들을 받아들이도록 성문을 활짝 열되, 적이 함께 들어오지 못하도록 트로이아인들이 다 지나가는 즉시 닫으라고 명하였다."], ["But Achilles was so close in pursuit that that would have been impossible if Apollo had not, in the form of Agenor, Priam’s son, encountered Achilles for a while, then turned to fly, and taken the way apart from the city.", "그러나 아킬레우스가 어찌나 바짝 뒤쫓던지, 만약 아폴론이 프리아모스의 아들 아게노르의 모습을 빌려 잠시 아킬레우스와 맞서다가 이내 달아나는 척하며 도성에서 벗어난 길로 빠지지 않았더라면 그것은 불가능하였을 것이다."], ["Achilles pursued and had chased his supposed victim far from the walls, when Apollo disclosed himself, and Achilles, perceiving how he had been deluded, gave up the chase.", "아킬레우스는 그를 뒤쫓아 자기가 노린 희생물을 성벽에서 멀리까지 몰아갔는데, 그때 아폴론이 자신의 정체를 드러냈고, 아킬레우스는 자기가 어떻게 속았는지를 깨닫고 추격을 그만두었다."]], [["But when the rest had escaped into the town Hector stood without determined to await the combat.", "그러나 나머지가 모두 도성 안으로 피해 들어간 뒤에도 헥토르는 싸움을 기다리기로 마음먹고 밖에 서 있었다."], ["His old father called to him from the walls and begged him to retire nor tempt the encounter.", "그의 늙은 아버지가 성벽에서 그를 불러, 물러나 그 대결을 무릅쓰지 말라고 애원하였다."], ["His mother, Hecuba, also besought him to the same effect, but all in vain. “How can I,”", "그의 어머니 헤카베도 같은 뜻으로 그에게 간청하였으나, 모두 헛일이었다."], ["said he to himself, “by whose command the people went to this day’s contest, where so many have fallen, seek safety for myself against a single foe?", "그는 혼자 중얼거렸다. \"내 명령으로 사람들이 오늘의 싸움에 나아가 그토록 많은 이가 쓰러졌거늘, 내 어찌 단 하나의 적을 피해 나 자신의 안위를 구한단 말인가?"], ["But what if I offer him to yield up Helen and all her treasures and ample of our own beside?", "허나 만약 내가 그에게 헬레네와 그녀의 모든 보물에 더하여 우리 자신의 것까지 넉넉히 내주겠다고 제안한다면?"], ["Ah, no!", "아, 아니다!"], ["it is too late.", "이미 너무 늦었다."], ["He would not even hear me through, but slay me while I spoke.”", "그는 내 말을 끝까지 듣지도 않고, 내가 말하는 동안 나를 베어 버릴 것이다.\""], ["While he thus ruminated, Achilles approached, terrible as Mars, his armor flashing lightning as he moved.", "그가 이렇게 곱씹는 동안, 아킬레우스가 아레스처럼 무섭게, 움직일 때마다 갑옷이 번개처럼 번쩍이며 다가왔다."], ["At that sight Hector’s heart failed him and he fled.", "그 광경에 헥토르는 가슴이 내려앉아 달아났다."], ["Achilles swiftly pursued.", "아킬레우스가 날쌔게 뒤쫓았다."], ["They ran, still keeping near the walls, till they had thrice encircled the city.", "그들은 줄곧 성벽 가까이 붙어 달려, 세 번이나 도성을 돌았다."], ["As often as Hector approached the walls Achilles intercepted him and forced him to keep out in a wider circle.", "헥토르가 성벽에 다가갈 때마다 아킬레우스가 그를 가로막아 더 넓은 원을 그리며 밖으로 돌게 만들었다."], ["But Apollo sustained Hector’s strength and would not let him sink in weariness.", "그러나 아폴론이 헥토르의 힘을 떠받쳐 그가 지쳐 쓰러지지 않게 하였다."], ["Then Pallas, assuming the form of Deiphobus, Hector’s bravest brother, appeared suddenly at his side.", "그때 팔라스[미네르바]가 헥토르의 가장 용감한 아우 데이포보스의 모습을 빌려 갑자기 그의 곁에 나타났다."], ["Hector saw him with delight, and thus strengthened stopped his flight and turned to meet Achilles.", "헥토르는 기뻐하며 그를 보았고, 이렇게 힘을 얻어 달아나기를 멈추고 돌아서서 아킬레우스를 맞이하였다."], ["Hector threw his spear, which struck the shield of Achilles and bounded back.", "헥토르가 창을 던지니 그것은 아킬레우스의 방패에 맞아 튕겨 나왔다."], ["He turned to receive another from the hand of Deiphobus, but Deiphobus was gone.", "그가 데이포보스의 손에서 또 다른 창을 받으려 돌아섰으나, 데이포보스는 사라지고 없었다."], ["Then Hector understood his doom and said, “Alas!", "그제야 헥토르는 자신의 운명을 깨닫고 말하였다. \"아아!"], ["it is plain this is my hour to die!", "이제 내가 죽을 때가 온 것이 분명하구나!"], ["I thought Deiphobus at hand, but Pallas deceived me, and he is still in Troy.", "데이포보스가 곁에 있는 줄 알았건만, 팔라스가 나를 속였고, 그는 아직 트로이아 안에 있구나."], ["But I will not fall inglorious.”", "허나 나는 영광 없이 쓰러지지는 않으리라.\""], ["So saying he drew his falchion from his side and rushed at once to combat.", "이렇게 말하며 그는 옆구리에서 칼을 빼 들고 단숨에 싸움으로 달려들었다."], ["Achilles, secured behind his shield, waited the approach of Hector.", "방패 뒤에 몸을 숨긴 아킬레우스는 헥토르가 다가오기를 기다렸다."], ["When he came within reach of his spear, Achilles choosing with his eye a vulnerable part where the armor leaves the neck uncovered, aimed his spear at that part and Hector fell, death-wounded, and feebly said, “Spare my body!", "그가 창이 닿을 거리에 들어오자, 아킬레우스는 갑옷이 목을 가리지 못하고 드러나는 취약한 부위를 눈으로 골라, 그곳을 향해 창을 겨누었다. 헥토르는 죽음의 상처를 입고 쓰러져 힘없이 말하였다."], ["Let my parents ransom it, and let me receive funeral rites from the sons and daughters of Troy.”", "\"내 몸을 아껴 주시오! 내 부모가 그것을 되사게 하고, 내가 트로이아의 아들딸들에게서 장례 의식을 받게 해 주시오.\""], ["To which Achilles replied, “Dog, name not ransom nor pity to me, on whom you have brought such dire distress.", "이에 아킬레우스가 대답하였다. \"개 같은 놈아, 그토록 끔찍한 고통을 내게 안겨 준 주제에 내게 몸값이니 연민이니 입에 올리지 마라."], ["No!", "안 된다!"], ["trust me, naught shall save thy carcass from the dogs.", "내 말을 믿어라, 그 무엇도 네 시체를 개들에게서 구해 내지 못하리라."], ["Though twenty ransoms and thy weight in gold were offered, I would refuse it all.”", "스무 배의 몸값과 네 무게만큼의 황금을 내놓는다 해도, 나는 그 모두를 거절하리라.\""]], [["So saying he stripped the body of its armor, and fastening cords to the feet tied them behind his chariot, leaving the body to trail along the ground.", "이렇게 말하며 그는 시신에서 갑옷을 벗기고, 발에 끈을 묶어 그것을 자기 전차 뒤에 매달아 시신이 땅바닥에 끌리게 두었다."], ["Then mounting the chariot he lashed the steeds and so dragged the body to and fro before the city.", "그러고 나서 전차에 올라 군마들을 채찍질하여, 도성 앞에서 시신을 이리저리 끌고 다녔다."], ["What words can tell the grief of King Priam and Queen Hecuba at this sight!", "이 광경을 본 프리아모스 왕과 헤카베 왕비의 슬픔을 무슨 말로 다 전할 수 있으랴!"], ["His people could scarce restrain the old king from rushing forth.", "그의 백성들은 그 늙은 왕이 밖으로 뛰쳐나가려는 것을 가까스로 막았다."], ["He threw himself in the dust and besought them each by name to give him way.", "그는 흙먼지 속에 몸을 던지고, 그들 하나하나의 이름을 부르며 길을 비켜 달라고 애원하였다."], ["Hecuba’s distress was not less violent.", "헤카베의 비탄도 그에 못지않게 격렬하였다."], ["The citizens stood round them weeping.", "시민들은 그들을 둘러싸고 울었다."], ["The sound of the mourning reached the ears of Andromache, the wife of Hector, as she sat among her maidens at work, and anticipating evil she went forth to the wall.", "그 애도의 소리는, 시녀들 틈에 앉아 일을 하던 헥토르의 아내 안드로마케의 귀에까지 닿았고, 불길함을 예감한 그녀는 성벽으로 나아갔다."], ["When she saw the sight there presented, she would have thrown herself headlong from the wall, but fainted and fell into the arms of her maidens.", "그곳에 펼쳐진 광경을 보았을 때, 그녀는 성벽에서 곤두박질쳐 몸을 던지려 하였으나, 정신을 잃고 시녀들의 품에 쓰러졌다."], ["Recovering, she bewailed her fate, picturing to herself her country ruined, herself a captive, and her son dependent for his bread on the charity of strangers.", "정신을 차린 그녀는 자신의 운명을 한탄하며, 조국이 멸망하고 자신은 포로가 되며 아들은 낯선 이들의 자비에 빵을 의지하게 될 모습을 마음속에 그렸다."]], [["When Achilles and the Greeks had taken their revenge on the killer of Patroclus they busied themselves in paying due funeral rites to their friend.", "아킬레우스와 그리스인들이 파트로클로스를 죽인 자에게 복수를 마치자, 그들은 자기 벗에게 마땅한 장례 의식을 치르는 데 분주하였다."], ["A pile was erected, and the body burned with due solemnity; and then ensued games of strength and skill, chariot races, wrestling, boxing, and archery.", "장작더미가 세워지고, 시신은 엄숙하게 화장되었다. 그리고 뒤이어 힘과 기술을 겨루는 경기들, 곧 전차 경주와 씨름과 권투와 활쏘기가 펼쳐졌다."], ["Then the chiefs sat down to the funeral banquet and after that retired to rest.", "그러고 나서 족장들은 장례 연회에 둘러앉았고, 그 뒤에 쉬러 물러갔다."], ["But Achilles neither partook of the feast nor of sleep.", "그러나 아킬레우스는 잔치에도 잠에도 들지 않았다."], ["The recollection of his lost friend kept him awake, remembering their companionship in toil and dangers, in battle or on the perilous deep.", "잃어버린 벗에 대한 회상이 그를 깨어 있게 하였으니, 그는 수고와 위험 속에서, 전투에서나 험난한 바다 위에서 함께한 그들의 우정을 떠올렸다."], ["Before the earliest dawn he left his tent, and joining to his chariot his swift steeds, he fastened Hector’s body to be dragged behind.", "가장 이른 새벽이 오기 전에 그는 자기 막사를 떠나, 날쌘 군마들을 전차에 매고 헥토르의 시신을 뒤에 끌리도록 묶었다."], ["Twice he dragged him around the tomb of Patroclus, leaving him at length stretched in the dust.", "두 번 그는 파트로클로스의 무덤 둘레로 그를 끌고 다니다가, 마침내 그를 흙먼지 속에 뻗어 둔 채 내버려 두었다."], ["But Apollo would not permit the body to be torn or disfigured with all this abuse, but preserved it free from all taint or defilement.", "그러나 아폴론은 이 온갖 모욕에도 시신이 찢기거나 일그러지는 것을 허락하지 않고, 그것을 어떤 더럽힘이나 흠집도 없이 온전하게 지켜 주었다."]], [["While Achilles indulged his wrath in thus disgracing brave Hector, Jupiter in pity summoned Thetis to his presence.", "아킬레우스가 이처럼 용맹한 헥토르를 욕보이며 분노를 풀고 있는 동안, 제우스는 가엾이 여겨 테티스를 자기 앞으로 불러들였다."], ["He told her to go to her son and prevail on him to restore the body of Hector to his friends.", "그는 그녀에게 자기 아들에게 가서 헥토르의 시신을 그의 친지들에게 돌려주도록 설득하라고 일렀다."], ["Then Jupiter sent Iris to King Priam to encourage him to go to Achilles and beg the body of his son.", "그러고 나서 제우스는 이리스를 프리아모스 왕에게 보내, 그가 아킬레우스에게 가서 자기 아들의 시신을 청하도록 격려하게 하였다."], ["Iris delivered her message, and Priam immediately prepared to obey.", "이리스가 그 전갈을 전하자 프리아모스는 즉시 따를 채비를 하였다."], ["He opened his treasuries and took out rich garments and cloths, with ten talents in gold and two splendid tripods and a golden cup of matchless workmanship.", "그는 자기 보고를 열어 값진 의복과 천들을, 황금 열 탈란톤과 화려한 세발솥 두 개, 그리고 견줄 데 없는 솜씨로 빚은 황금 잔 하나를 함께 꺼냈다."], ["Then he called to his sons and bade them draw forth his litter and place in it the various articles designed for a ransom to Achilles.", "그러고 나서 그는 아들들을 불러, 자기 수레를 끌어내어 아킬레우스에게 몸값으로 바칠 갖가지 물건을 그 안에 실으라고 명하였다."], ["When all was ready, the old king with a single companion as aged as himself, the herald Idæus, drove forth from the gates, parting there with Hecuba, his queen, and all his friends, who lamented him as going to certain death.", "모든 준비가 끝나자, 늙은 왕은 자신만큼이나 나이 든 동무 하나, 곧 전령 이다이오스만을 데리고 성문 밖으로 수레를 몰았으니, 그곳에서 그의 왕비 헤카베와 모든 친지와 작별하였는데, 그들은 그가 틀림없는 죽음을 향해 가는 것이라 여기며 그를 위해 한탄하였다."]], [["But Jupiter, beholding with compassion the venerable king, sent Mercury to be his guide and protector.", "그러나 제우스는 그 존경할 만한 왕을 연민으로 굽어보며, 헤르메스를 보내 그의 안내자이자 보호자가 되게 하였다."], ["Mercury, assuming the form of a young warrior, presented himself to the aged couple, and while at the sight of him they hesitated whether to fly or yield, the god approached, and grasping Priam’s hand offered to be their guide to Achilles’ tent.", "헤르메스는 젊은 전사의 모습을 빌려 그 노부부 앞에 나타났는데, 그를 보고 그들이 달아날지 머무를지 망설이는 사이 그 신이 다가와 프리아모스의 손을 잡고, 그들을 아킬레우스의 막사까지 안내하겠노라 제안하였다."], ["Priam gladly accepted his offered service, and he, mounting the carriage, assumed the reins and soon conveyed them to the tent of Achilles.", "프리아모스가 그 베풀어진 도움을 기꺼이 받아들이자, 그는 수레에 올라 고삐를 잡고 곧 그들을 아킬레우스의 막사로 데려갔다."], ["Mercury’s wand put to sleep all the guards, and without hinderance he introduced Priam into the tent where Achilles sat, attended by two of his warriors.", "헤르메스의 지팡이가 모든 보초를 잠재웠고, 그는 아무런 방해도 받지 않고 프리아모스를 막사 안으로 들였다. 그곳에는 두 전사의 시중을 받으며 아킬레우스가 앉아 있었다."], ["The old king threw himself at the feet of Achilles, and kissed those terrible hands which had destroyed so many of his sons.", "늙은 왕은 아킬레우스의 발치에 몸을 던지고, 그토록 많은 자기 아들을 죽인 저 무서운 손에 입을 맞추었다."], ["“Think, O Achilles,”", "그가 말하였다."], ["he said, “of thy own father, full of days like me, and trembling on the gloomy verge of life.", "\"오 아킬레우스여, 나처럼 살날이 다하여 삶의 어두운 가장자리에서 떨고 있는 그대 자신의 아버지를 생각해 보시오."], ["Perhaps even now some neighbor chief oppresses him and there is none at hand to succor him in his distress.", "어쩌면 지금 이 순간에도 어느 이웃 족장이 그를 짓누르고 있건만 그 곤경에 그를 구해 줄 이가 곁에 없을지도 모르오."], ["Yet doubtless knowing that Achilles lives he still rejoices, hoping that one day he shall see thy face again.", "그래도 그는 분명 아킬레우스가 살아 있음을 알고, 언젠가 다시 그대의 얼굴을 보게 되리라는 희망으로 여전히 기뻐하고 있을 것이오."], ["But no comfort cheers me, whose bravest sons, so late the flower of Ilium, all have fallen.", "허나 나에게는 어떤 위안도 없으니, 얼마 전까지 일리온의 꽃이었던 나의 가장 용감한 아들들이 모두 쓰러졌소."], ["Yet one I had, one more than all the rest the strength of my age, whom, fighting for his country, thou hast slain.", "그래도 내게 하나가 있었으니, 다른 모든 자식보다 더한, 내 노년의 힘이었던 그 아이를, 조국을 위해 싸우던 그를 그대가 죽였소."], ["I come to redeem his body, bringing inestimable ransom with me.", "나는 그 몸을 되찾으러 왔으니, 헤아릴 수 없는 몸값을 가지고 왔소."], ["Achilles!", "아킬레우스여!"], ["reverence the gods!", "신들을 경외하시오!"], ["recollect thy father!", "그대의 아버지를 떠올리시오!"], ["for his sake show compassion to me!”", "그분을 위하여 내게 연민을 베풀어 주시오!\""], ["These words moved Achilles, and he wept; remembering by turns his absent father and his lost friend.", "이 말이 아킬레우스의 마음을 움직여 그는 눈물을 흘렸으니, 멀리 있는 자기 아버지와 잃어버린 벗을 번갈아 떠올렸기 때문이다. 프리아모스의 은빛 머리카락과 수염에 연민이 일어, 그는 그를 땅에서 일으키며 이렇게 말하였다."], ["Moved with pity of Priam’s silver locks and beard, he raised him from the earth, and thus spake: “Priam, I know that thou hast reached this place conducted by some god, for without aid divine no mortal even in his prime of youth had dared the attempt.", "\"프리아모스여, 나는 그대가 어느 신의 인도를 받아 이곳에 이르렀음을 아오. 신의 도움 없이는 한창때의 젊은이라 할지라도 어떤 필멸자도 감히 그런 시도를 하지 못하였을 것이기 때문이오."], ["I grant thy request, moved thereto by the evident will of Jove.”", "제우스의 뜻이 분명함에 움직여, 나는 그대의 청을 들어주겠소.\""], ["So saying he arose, and went forth with his two friends, and unloaded of its charge the litter, leaving two mantles and a robe for the covering of the body, which they placed on the litter, and spread the garments over it, that not unveiled it should be borne back to Troy.", "이렇게 말하며 그는 일어나 자기 두 벗과 함께 밖으로 나가 수레에 실린 짐을 부리고, 시신을 덮을 외투 두 벌과 겉옷 한 벌을 남겨 두었다. 그것들을 수레에 놓고 그 위에 옷들을 펼쳐, 시신이 가려지지 않은 채로 트로이아로 옮겨지지 않게 하였다."], ["Then Achilles dismissed the old king with his attendants, having first pledged himself to allow a truce of twelve days for the funeral solemnities.", "그러고 나서 아킬레우스는, 먼저 장례 의식을 위하여 열이틀 동안의 휴전을 허락하겠노라 다짐한 뒤, 그 늙은 왕을 시종과 함께 떠나보냈다."]], [["As the litter approached the city and was descried from the walls, the people poured forth to gaze once more on the face of their hero.", "수레가 도성에 다가와 성벽에서 그것이 보이자, 사람들은 자기들의 영웅의 얼굴을 다시 한번 바라보려 쏟아져 나왔다."], ["Foremost of all, the mother and the wife of Hector came, and at the sight of the lifeless body renewed their lamentations.", "누구보다 앞서 헥토르의 어머니와 아내가 왔고, 숨이 끊긴 그 시신을 보고 그들의 비탄을 새로이 터뜨렸다."], ["The people all wept with them, and to the going down of the sun there was no pause or abatement of their grief.", "백성들도 모두 그들과 함께 울었으니, 해가 질 때까지 그들의 슬픔에는 멈춤도 누그러짐도 없었다."]], [["The next day preparations were made for the funeral solemnities.", "이튿날 장례 의식을 위한 준비가 갖추어졌다."], ["For nine days the people brought wood and built the pile, and on the tenth they placed the body on the summit and applied the torch; while all Troy thronging forth encompassed the pile.", "아흐레 동안 사람들은 나무를 날라 장작더미를 쌓았고, 열흘째 되는 날 그 꼭대기에 시신을 올리고 횃불을 댔으니, 그동안 온 트로이아가 쏟아져 나와 그 장작더미를 에워쌌다."], ["When it had completely burned, they quenched the cinders with wine, collected the bones and placed them in a golden urn, which they buried in the earth, and reared a pile of stones over the spot.", "그것이 완전히 타고 나자, 그들은 포도주로 그 잿불을 끄고 뼈를 모아 황금 항아리에 담아 땅에 묻었으며, 그 자리 위에 돌무더기를 쌓아 올렸다."]], [["“Such honors Ilium to her hero paid, And peaceful slept the mighty Hector’s shade.”", "\"일리온은 그 영웅에게 이런 예를 바쳤으니, 위대한 헥토르의 혼령은 평온히 잠들었노라.\""], ["—Pope.", "─포프."]], [["————", "────"]], [["# THE FALL OF TROY", "# THE FALL OF TROY"]], [["THE story of the Iliad ends with the death of Hector, and it is from the Odyssey and later poems that we learn the fate of the other heroes.", "일리아스의 이야기는 헥토르의 죽음으로 끝나며, 다른 영웅들의 운명을 우리가 알게 되는 것은 오디세이아와 그 이후의 시들을 통해서다."], ["After the death of Hector, Troy did not immediately fall, but receiving aid from new allies still continued its resistance.", "헥토르가 죽은 뒤에도 트로이아는 곧바로 함락되지 않았으며, 새로운 동맹들의 도움을 받아 여전히 저항을 이어 갔다."], ["One of these allies was Memnon, the Æthiopian prince, whose story we have already told.", "이 동맹들 가운데 하나가 에티오피아의 왕자 멤논이었으니, 그의 이야기는 우리가 이미 들려준 바 있다."], ["Another was Penthesilea, queen of the Amazons, who came with a band of female warriors.", "또 다른 동맹은 아마조네스의 여왕 펜테실레이아였는데, 그녀는 여전사들의 무리를 이끌고 왔다."], ["All the authorities attest their valor and the fearful effect of their war cry.", "모든 전거가 그들의 용맹과 그 전투의 함성이 자아낸 무시무시한 효과를 입증한다."], ["Penthesilea slew many of the bravest warriors, but was at last slain by Achilles.", "펜테실레이아는 가장 용맹한 전사들 가운데 많은 이를 죽였으나, 마침내 아킬레우스의 손에 쓰러졌다."], ["But when the hero bent over his fallen foe, and contemplated her beauty, youth, and valor, he bitterly regretted his victory.", "그러나 이 영웅이 쓰러진 적 위로 몸을 굽혀 그녀의 아름다움과 젊음과 용맹을 들여다보았을 때, 그는 자신의 승리를 쓰라리게 후회했다."], ["Thersites, an insolent brawler and demagogue, ridiculed his grief, and was in consequence slain by the hero.", "오만한 싸움꾼이자 선동가인 테르시테스가 그의 슬픔을 비웃었고, 그 결과 이 영웅의 손에 죽임을 당했다."]], [["Achilles by chance had seen Polyxena, daughter of King Priam, perhaps on the occasion of the truce which was allowed the Trojans for the burial of Hector.", "아킬레우스는 우연히 프리아모스 왕의 딸 폴릭세네를 본 적이 있었으니, 아마도 헥토르의 장례를 위해 트로이아인들에게 허락되었던 휴전 때였을 것이다."], ["He was captivated with her charms, and to win her in marriage agreed to use his influence with the Greeks to grant peace to Troy.", "그는 그녀의 매력에 사로잡혔고, 그녀를 아내로 얻기 위해 그리스인들에게 자신의 영향력을 행사하여 트로이아에 평화를 허락하도록 하기로 합의했다."], ["While in the temple of Apollo, negotiating the marriage, Paris discharged at him a poisoned arrow, which, guided by Apollo, wounded Achilles in the heel, the only vulnerable part about him.", "아폴론의 신전에서 혼인을 협상하던 중, 파리스가 그를 향해 독화살을 쏘았으니, 그 화살은 아폴론의 인도를 받아 아킬레우스의 발뒤꿈치를 맞혔다. 그곳은 그의 몸에서 유일하게 상처를 입을 수 있는 부분이었다."], ["For Thetis his mother had dipped him when an infant in the river Styx, which made every part of him invulnerable except the heel by which she held him.[21]", "그의 어머니 테티스가 그를 갓난아기였을 때 스틱스 강에 담갔던 까닭에, 그를 붙잡고 있던 발뒤꿈치만을 제외하고 그의 온몸이 상처를 입지 않게 되었기 때문이다.[21]"]], [["The body of Achilles so treacherously slain was rescued by Ajax and Ulysses.", "이처럼 비열하게 죽임을 당한 아킬레우스의 시신은 아이아스와 오디세우스에 의해 구출되었다."], ["Thetis directed the Greeks to bestow her son’s armor on the hero who of all the survivors should be judged most deserving of it.", "테티스는 그리스인들에게 자기 아들의 갑옷을 살아남은 자들 가운데 가장 그것을 받을 만하다고 판단되는 영웅에게 수여하도록 지시했다."], ["Ajax and Ulysses were the only claimants; a select number of the other chiefs were appointed to award the prize.", "아이아스와 오디세우스만이 그것을 요구했고, 다른 우두머리들 가운데 선발된 일정한 수의 사람들이 상을 판정하도록 임명되었다."], ["It was awarded to Ulysses, thus placing wisdom before valor; whereupon Ajax slew himself.", "상은 오디세우스에게 돌아갔으니, 이는 용맹보다 지혜를 앞세운 것이었다. 이에 아이아스는 스스로 목숨을 끊었다."], ["On the spot where his blood sank into the earth a flower sprang up, called the hyacinth, bearing on its leaves the first two letters of the name of Ajax, Ai, the Greek for “woe.”", "그의 피가 땅속으로 스며든 자리에서 한 송이 꽃이 솟아났으니, 그것은 히아킨토스라 불리며, 그 잎사귀에는 아이아스 이름의 첫 두 글자인 〈아이(Ai)〉가 새겨져 있었는데, 이는 그리스어로 \"비통\"을 뜻한다."], ["Thus Ajax is a claimant with the boy Hyacinthus for the honor of giving birth to this flower.", "이렇듯 아이아스는 이 꽃을 탄생시킨 영예를 두고 소년 히아킨토스와 다투는 자가 되었다."], ["There is a species of Larkspur which represents the hyacinth of the poets in preserving the memory of this event, the Delphinium Ajacis—Ajax’s Larkspur.", "이 사건의 기억을 간직하여 시인들의 히아킨토스를 대신하는 제비고깔의 한 종류가 있으니, 그것은 델피니움 아약키스, 곧 아이아스의 제비고깔이다."]], [["It was now discovered that Troy could not be taken but by the aid of the arrows of Hercules.", "이제 트로이아가 헤라클레스의 화살들의 도움 없이는 함락될 수 없다는 사실이 밝혀졌다."], ["They were in possession of Philoctetes, the friend who had been with Hercules at the last and lighted his funeral pyre.", "그 화살들은 필록테테스가 가지고 있었으니, 그는 헤라클레스가 마지막 순간에 함께 있어 주었으며 그의 장작더미에 불을 붙여 준 친구였다."], ["Philoctetes had joined the Grecian expedition against Troy, but had accidentally wounded his foot with one of the poisoned arrows, and the smell from his wound proved so offensive that his companions carried him to the isle of Lemnos and left him there.", "필록테테스는 트로이아 원정에 그리스 편으로 합류했으나, 우연히 그 독화살들 가운데 하나로 자기 발에 상처를 입었고, 그 상처에서 나는 냄새가 너무도 역겨워 동료들이 그를 렘노스 섬으로 데려가 그곳에 버려두었다."], ["Diomed was now sent to induce him to rejoin the army.", "이제 디오메데스가 그를 설득하여 군대에 다시 합류시키도록 파견되었다."], ["He succeeded.", "그는 성공했다."], ["Philoctetes was cured of his wound by Machaon, and Paris was the first victim of the fatal arrows.", "필록테테스는 마카온에게 상처를 치료받았고, 파리스가 그 치명적인 화살들의 첫 번째 희생자가 되었다."], ["In his distress Paris bethought him of one whom in his prosperity he had forgotten.", "고통 속에서 파리스는 자신이 번영하던 시절에 잊고 지냈던 한 사람을 떠올렸다."], ["This was the nymph Œnone, whom he had married when a youth, and had abandoned for the fatal beauty Helen.", "그녀는 요정 오이노네였으니, 그가 젊은 시절에 아내로 맞았다가 치명적인 미녀 헬레네를 위해 버렸던 여인이었다."], ["Œnone, remembering the wrongs she had suffered, refused to heal the wound, and Paris went back to Troy and died.", "오이노네는 자신이 겪었던 부당함을 기억하고 상처를 치료해 주기를 거부했고, 파리스는 트로이아로 돌아가 죽었다."], ["Œnone quickly repented, and hastened after him with remedies, but came too late, and in her grief hung herself.[22]", "오이노네는 곧 후회하고 치료약을 들고 그의 뒤를 서둘러 쫓았으나 너무 늦게 도착했고, 슬픔에 잠겨 스스로 목을 매었다.[22]"]], [["There was in Troy a celebrated statue of Minerva called the Palladium.", "트로이아에는 팔라디온이라 불리는 미네르바의 유명한 조각상이 있었다."], ["It was said to have fallen from heaven, and the belief was that the city could not be taken so long as this statue remained within it.", "그것은 하늘에서 떨어졌다고 전해졌으며, 이 조각상이 도시 안에 남아 있는 한 도시는 함락될 수 없다는 믿음이 있었다."], ["Ulysses and Diomed entered the city in disguise and succeeded in obtaining the Palladium, which they carried off to the Grecian camp.", "오디세우스와 디오메데스는 변장을 하고 도시로 들어가 팔라디온을 손에 넣는 데 성공했고, 그것을 그리스 진영으로 가지고 나왔다."]], [["But Troy still held out, and the Greeks began to despair of ever subduing it by force, and by advice of Ulysses resolved to resort to stratagem.", "그러나 트로이아는 여전히 버텼고, 그리스인들은 힘으로 그것을 굴복시키는 일에 절망하기 시작했으며, 오디세우스의 조언에 따라 계략에 의지하기로 결정했다."], ["They pretended to be making preparations to abandon the siege, and a portion of the ships were withdrawn and lay hid behind a neighboring island.", "그들은 포위를 풀고 떠날 채비를 하는 척했고, 함선들의 일부를 빼내어 이웃한 섬 뒤에 숨겨 두었다."], ["The Greeks then constructed an immense wooden horse, which they gave out was intended as a propitiatory offering to Minerva, but in fact was filled with armed men.", "그런 다음 그리스인들은 거대한 목마를 만들었으니, 그들은 그것이 미네르바에게 바치는 화해의 제물이라고 공언했으나, 실은 무장한 병사들로 가득 차 있었다."], ["The remaining Greeks then betook themselves to their ships and sailed away, as if for a final departure.", "남은 그리스인들은 함선으로 가서 마치 최후의 출발이라도 하듯 배를 띄워 떠나갔다."], ["The Trojans, seeing the encampment broken up and the fleet gone, concluded the enemy to have abandoned the siege.", "트로이아인들은 진영이 헐리고 함대가 사라진 것을 보고는 적이 포위를 단념했다고 결론지었다."], ["The gates were thrown open, and the whole population issued forth rejoicing at the long-prohibited liberty of passing freely over the scene of the late encampment.", "성문이 활짝 열렸고, 온 백성이 그토록 오랫동안 금지되었던, 지난날 진영이 있던 자리를 자유로이 거니는 해방감에 기뻐하며 밖으로 쏟아져 나왔다."], ["The great horse was the chief object of curiosity.", "그 거대한 목마가 호기심의 주된 대상이 되었다."], ["All wondered what it could be for.", "모두가 그것이 무엇에 쓰이는 것일까 궁금해했다."], ["Some recommended to take it into the city as a trophy; others felt afraid of it.", "어떤 이들은 그것을 전리품으로 도시 안에 들여놓자고 권했고, 다른 이들은 그것을 두려워했다."]], [["While they hesitate, Laocoön, the priest of Neptune exclaims, “What madness, citizens, is this?", "그들이 망설이고 있을 때, 넵투누스의 사제 라오코온이 외쳤다. \"시민들이여, 이 무슨 광기란 말인가?"], ["Have you not learned enough of Grecian fraud to be on your guard against it?", "그대들은 그리스인들의 기만을 충분히 겪고도 아직 그것을 경계할 줄 모른단 말인가?"], ["For my part, I fear the Greeks even when they offer gifts.”[23] So saying he threw his lance at the horse’s side.", "나로 말하자면, 나는 그리스인들이 선물을 바칠 때조차 그들이 두렵다.\"[23] 이렇게 말하며 그는 목마의 옆구리에 창을 던졌다."], ["It struck, and a hollow sound reverberated like a groan.", "창이 박히자 텅 빈 소리가 신음처럼 울려 퍼졌다."], ["Then perhaps the people might have taken his advice and destroyed the fatal horse and all its contents; but just at that moment a group of people appeared, dragging forward one who seemed a prisoner and a Greek.", "그때 백성들은 어쩌면 그의 조언을 받아들여 그 치명적인 목마와 그 안에 든 모든 것을 파괴했을지도 모른다. 그러나 바로 그 순간 한 무리의 사람들이 나타나, 포로인 듯하고 그리스인인 듯한 한 사람을 앞으로 끌고 왔다."], ["Stupefied with terror, he was brought before the chiefs, who reassured him, promising that his life should be spared on condition of his returning true answers to the questions asked him.", "공포에 질려 얼이 빠진 그는 우두머리들 앞으로 끌려 나왔고, 그들은 그를 안심시키며, 그에게 던지는 질문에 진실한 대답을 하는 조건으로 목숨을 살려 주겠다고 약속했다."], ["He informed them that he was a Greek, Sinon by name, and that in consequence of the malice of Ulysses he had been left behind by his countrymen at their departure.", "그는 자신이 그리스인이며 이름은 시논이라고 알렸고, 오디세우스의 악의 탓에 동족들이 떠날 때 뒤에 남겨졌다고 했다."], ["With regard to the wooden horse, he told them that it was a propitiatory offering to Minerva, and made so huge for the express purpose of preventing its being carried within the city; for Calchas the prophet had told them that if the Trojans took possession of it they would assuredly triumph over the Greeks.", "목마에 관해서 그는, 그것이 미네르바에게 바치는 화해의 제물이며, 도시 안으로 들여가지 못하게 하려는 명백한 목적에서 그토록 거대하게 만들어졌다고 그들에게 말했다. 예언자 칼카스가 그들에게 이르기를, 만약 트로이아인들이 그것을 손에 넣으면 그들이 틀림없이 그리스인들을 이기리라고 했기 때문이라는 것이었다."], ["This language turned the tide of the people’s feelings and they began to think how they might best secure the monstrous horse and the favorable auguries connected with it, when suddenly a prodigy occurred which left no room to doubt.", "이 말은 백성들의 감정의 물결을 돌려놓았고, 그들이 어떻게 하면 이 거대한 목마와 그것에 결부된 길한 조짐을 가장 잘 확보할 수 있을까 궁리하기 시작했을 때, 갑자기 한 가지 기적이 일어나 의심의 여지를 남기지 않았다."], ["There appeared, advancing over the sea, two immense serpents.", "바다 위로 다가오는 두 마리의 거대한 뱀이 나타났던 것이다."], ["They came upon the land, and the crowd fled in all directions.", "그것들이 뭍에 올라오자 군중은 사방으로 흩어져 달아났다."], ["The serpents advanced directly to the spot where Laocoön stood with his two sons.", "뱀들은 라오코온이 두 아들과 함께 서 있는 곳을 향해 곧장 나아갔다."], ["They first attacked the children, winding round their bodies and breathing their pestilential breath in their faces.", "그것들은 먼저 아이들을 공격하여 그들의 몸을 휘감고 역병 같은 숨결을 그들의 얼굴에 내뿜었다."], ["The father, attempting to rescue them, is next seized and involved in the serpents’ coils.", "아이들을 구하려던 아버지도 곧 붙잡혀 뱀들의 똬리에 휘감겼다."], ["He struggles to tear them away, but they overpower all his efforts and strangle him and the children in their poisonous folds.", "그는 그것들을 떼어 내려 몸부림쳤으나, 뱀들은 그의 온갖 노력을 압도하고 독을 품은 똬리로 그와 아이들을 졸라 죽였다."], ["This event was regarded as a clear indication of the displeasure of the gods at Laocoön’s irreverent treatment of the wooden horse, which they no longer hesitated to regard as a sacred object, and prepared to introduce with due solemnity into the city.", "이 사건은 라오코온이 목마를 불경하게 다룬 데 대한 신들의 노여움을 분명히 드러낸 것으로 여겨졌고, 그리하여 사람들은 더 이상 망설이지 않고 목마를 신성한 물건으로 간주하여 마땅한 엄숙함을 갖추어 도시 안으로 들여올 채비를 했다."], ["This was done with songs and triumphal acclamations, and the day closed with festivity.", "이 일은 노래와 승리의 환호 속에서 이루어졌으며, 그날은 축제로 마무리되었다."], ["In the night the armed men who were enclosed in the body of the horse, being let out by the traitor Sinon, opened the gates of the city to their friends, who had returned under cover of the night.", "밤이 되자 목마의 몸통 안에 갇혀 있던 무장한 병사들이 배신자 시논에 의해 풀려나, 밤의 어둠을 틈타 돌아온 동료들에게 도시의 성문을 열어 주었다."], ["The city was set on fire; the people, overcome with feasting and sleep, put to the sword, and Troy completely subdued.", "도시에는 불이 질러졌고, 잔치와 잠에 빠져 있던 백성들은 칼날에 베였으며, 트로이아는 완전히 굴복하고 말았다."]], [["One of the most celebrated groups of statuary in existence is that of Laocoön and his children in the embrace of the serpents.", "현존하는 가장 유명한 조각상 무리 가운데 하나가 뱀들에게 휘감긴 라오코온과 그의 아이들을 묘사한 것이다."], ["A cast of it is owned by the Boston Athenæum; the original is in the Vatican at Rome.", "그 복제품을 보스턴 아테나이움이 소장하고 있으며, 원작은 로마의 바티칸에 있다."], ["The following lines are from the “Childe Harold” of Byron:", "다음 시구는 바이런의 「차일드 해럴드」에서 가져온 것이다."]], [["“Now turning to the Vatican go see Laocoön’s torture dignifying pain; A father’s love and mortal’s agony With an immortal’s patience blending;—vain The struggle!", "\"이제 바티칸으로 향하여 가서 보라 고통을 위엄 있게 만드는 라오코온의 괴로움을. 아버지의 사랑과 필멸자의 고뇌가 불멸자의 인내와 어우러지나니, 헛되도다 그 몸부림이여!"], ["vain against the coiling strain And gripe and deepening of the dragon’s grasp The old man’s clinch; the long envenomed chain Rivets the living links; the enormous asp Enforces pang on pang and stifles gasp on gasp.”", "휘감아 죄어 오는 힘과 조이며 깊어지는 용의 손아귀에 맞선 노인의 움켜쥠은 헛되도다. 길게 독을 품은 사슬이 살아 있는 고리들을 단단히 죄고, 그 거대한 살무사는 고통 위에 고통을 더하며 숨결 위에 숨결을 막는도다.\""]], [["The comic poets will also occasionally borrow a classical allusion.", "희극 시인들 또한 이따금 고전적 인유를 빌려 오곤 한다."], ["The following is from Swift’s “Description of a City Shower”:", "다음은 스위프트의 「도시의 소나기 묘사」에서 가져온 것이다."]], [["“Boxed in a chair the beau impatient sits, While spouts run clattering o’er the roof by fits, And ever and anon with frightful din The leather sounds; he trembles from within.", "\"가마에 갇힌 멋쟁이는 안달하며 앉아 있고, 그동안 지붕 위로 빗물받이가 간간이 요란하게 쏟아지며, 이따금 무시무시한 소음과 함께 가죽이 울려 대니, 그는 안에서 떨고 있도다."], ["So when Troy chairmen bore the wooden steed Pregnant with Greeks impatient to be freed, (Those bully Greeks, who, as the moderns do, Instead of paying chairmen, run them through); Laocoön struck the outside with a spear, And each imprisoned champion quaked with fear.”", "그 옛날 트로이아의 가마꾼들이 목마를 메었을 때처럼, 풀려나기를 안달하는 그리스인들을 잉태한 그 목마를, (그 으스대는 그리스인들이란, 오늘날의 사람들이 그러하듯, 가마꾼에게 삯을 치르는 대신 칼로 꿰뚫어 버리는 자들이라). 라오코온이 그 바깥을 창으로 내려치자, 갇혀 있던 용사들이 저마다 두려움에 떨었도다.\""]], [["King Priam lived to see the downfall of his kingdom and was slain at last on the fatal night when the Greeks took the city.", "프리아모스 왕은 자기 왕국의 몰락을 지켜볼 만큼 오래 살았고, 마침내 그리스인들이 도시를 함락한 그 운명의 밤에 죽임을 당했다."], ["He had armed himself and was about to mingle with the combatants, but was prevailed on by Hecuba, his aged queen, to take refuge with herself and his daughters as a suppliant at the altar of Jupiter.", "그는 스스로 무장하고 전투원들 사이에 끼어들려 했으나, 늙은 왕비 헤카베의 설득에 따라 그녀와 자기 딸들과 함께 유피테르의 제단에 탄원자로서 피신했다."], ["While there, his youngest son Polites, pursued by Pyrrhus, the son of Achilles, rushed in wounded, and expired at the feet of his father; whereupon Priam, overcome with indignation, hurled his spear with feeble hand against Pyrrhus,[24] and was forthwith slain by him.", "그곳에 있을 때, 그의 막내아들 폴리테스가 아킬레우스의 아들 피로스에게 쫓겨 상처를 입은 채 뛰어들었다가 아버지의 발치에서 숨을 거두었다. 이에 프리아모스는 분노에 사로잡혀 쇠약한 손으로 피로스를 향해 창을 던졌으나,[24] 곧 그의 손에 죽임을 당했다."]], [["Queen Hecuba and her daughter Cassandra were carried captives to Greece.", "헤카베 왕비와 그녀의 딸 카산드라는 포로가 되어 그리스로 끌려갔다."], ["Cassandra had been loved by Apollo, and he gave her the gift of prophecy; but afterwards offended with her, he rendered the gift unavailing by ordaining that her predictions should never be believed.", "카산드라는 아폴론의 사랑을 받았고, 그는 그녀에게 예언의 능력을 선물로 주었다. 그러나 후에 그녀에게 노하여, 그녀의 예언이 결코 믿어지지 않도록 정함으로써 그 선물을 쓸모없게 만들었다."], ["Polyxena, another daughter, who had been loved by Achilles, was demanded by the ghost of that warrior, and was sacrificed by the Greeks upon his tomb.", "또 다른 딸 폴릭세네는 아킬레우스의 사랑을 받았던 까닭에 그 전사의 망령이 그녀를 요구했고, 그리스인들에 의해 그의 무덤 위에서 제물로 바쳐졌다."]], [["# MENELAUS AND HELEN", "# MENELAUS AND HELEN"]], [["Our readers will be anxious to know the fate of Helen, the fair but guilty occasion of so much slaughter.", "우리 독자들은 그토록 많은 살육의 아름답되 죄 많은 빌미가 되었던 헬레네의 운명을 알고 싶어 할 것이다."], ["On the fall of Troy Menelaus recovered possession of his wife, who had not ceased to love him, though she had yielded to the might of Venus and deserted him for another.", "트로이아가 함락되자 메넬라오스는 아내를 되찾았으니, 그녀는 베누스의 위력에 굴복하여 그를 버리고 다른 사람에게로 갔으되 그를 사랑하기를 그친 적은 없었다."], ["After the death of Paris she aided the Greeks secretly on several occasions, and in particular when Ulysses and Diomed entered the city in disguise to carry off the Palladium.", "파리스가 죽은 뒤로 그녀는 여러 차례 은밀히 그리스인들을 도왔으며, 특히 오디세우스와 디오메데스가 팔라디온을 가져가려고 변장하여 도시에 들어왔을 때 그러했다."], ["She saw and recognized Ulysses, but kept the secret and even assisted them in obtaining the image.", "그녀는 오디세우스를 알아보았으나 비밀을 지켰을 뿐 아니라, 그들이 그 조각상을 손에 넣도록 도와주기까지 했다."], ["Thus she became reconciled to her husband, and they were among the first to leave the shores of Troy for their native land.", "이렇게 하여 그녀는 남편과 화해했고, 그들은 트로이아의 해안을 떠나 고국으로 향한 첫 사람들 가운데 들었다."], ["But having incurred the displeasure of the gods they were driven by storms from shore to shore of the Mediterranean, visiting Cyprus, Phœnicia, and Egypt.", "그러나 신들의 노여움을 산 까닭에 그들은 폭풍에 떠밀려 지중해의 이 해안 저 해안으로 표류했고, 키프로스와 페니키아와 이집트를 거쳤다."], ["In Egypt they were kindly treated and presented with rich gifts, of which Helen’s share was a golden spindle and a basket on wheels.", "이집트에서 그들은 친절한 대접을 받고 풍성한 선물을 받았는데, 헬레네의 몫은 황금 물레와 바퀴 달린 바구니였다."], ["The basket was to hold the wool and spools for the queen’s work.", "그 바구니는 왕비의 일감인 양털과 실패를 담는 것이었다."]], [["Dyer, in his poem of the “Fleece,” thus alludes to this incident:", "다이어는 그의 시 「양털」에서 이 일화를 이렇게 언급한다."]], [["“. .", "\"."], [".", "."], ["many yet adhere To the ancient distaff, at the bosom fixed, Casting the whirling spindle as they walk.", ". 여전히 많은 이들이 가슴에 고정한 옛 실톳대를 고수하여, 걸으면서 빙빙 도는 물렛가락을 던지나니."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], ["This was of old, in no inglorious days, The mode of spinning, when the Egyptian prince A golden distaff gave that beauteous nymph, Too beauteous Helen; no uncourtly gift.”", "이것이 옛날, 영광스럽지 않다 할 수 없는 시절에, 실을 잣던 방식이었으니, 그때 이집트의 왕이 그 아리따운 요정에게 황금 실톳대를 주었으되, 너무도 아리따운 헬레네에게라, 무례하지 않은 선물이었도다.\""]], [["Milton also alludes to a famous recipe for an invigorating draught, called Nepenthe, which the Egyptian queen gave to Helen:", "밀턴 또한 이집트의 왕비가 헬레네에게 주었던, 네펜테라 불리는 기운을 북돋우는 음료의 유명한 비방을 언급한다."]], [["“Not that Nepenthes which the wife of Thone In Egypt gave to Jove-born Helena, Is of such power to stir up joy as this, To life so friendly or so cool to thirst.”", "\"토네의 아내가 이집트에서 제우스에게서 난 헬레네에게 주었던 그 네펜테스도, 이것만큼 기쁨을 불러일으키는 힘은 지니지 못했으니, 이토록 생명에 다정하거나 갈증에 시원하지는 못했도다.\""], ["—Comus.", "—「코머스」."]], [["Menelaus and Helen at length arrived in safety at Sparta, resumed their royal dignity, and lived and reigned in splendor; and when Telemachus, the son of Ulysses, in search of his father, arrived at Sparta, he found Menelaus and Helen celebrating the marriage of their daughter Hermione to Neoptolemus, son of Achilles.", "메넬라오스와 헬레네는 마침내 무사히 스파르타에 도착하여 왕의 위엄을 되찾았고, 영화 속에서 살며 다스렸다. 그리고 오디세우스의 아들 텔레마코스가 아버지를 찾아 스파르타에 이르렀을 때, 그는 메넬라오스와 헬레네가 자기들의 딸 헤르미오네와 아킬레우스의 아들 네오프톨레모스의 혼인을 축하하고 있는 것을 보았다."]], [["# AGAMEMNON, ORESTES, AND ELECTRA", "# AGAMEMNON, ORESTES, AND ELECTRA"]], [["Agamemnon, the general-in-chief of the Greeks, the brother of Menelaus, and who had been drawn into the quarrel to avenge his brother’s wrongs, not his own, was not so fortunate in the issue.", "그리스인들의 총사령관이자 메넬라오스의 형제이며, 자신의 부당함이 아니라 형제의 부당함을 갚기 위해 그 싸움에 끌려들었던 아가멤논은 그 결말이 그다지 운이 좋지 못했다."], ["During his absence his wife Clytemnestra had been false to him, and when his return was expected, she with her paramour, Ægisthus, laid a plan for his destruction, and at the banquet given to celebrate his return, murdered him.", "그가 자리를 비운 동안 그의 아내 클리타임네스트라가 그에게 부정을 저질렀고, 그의 귀환이 예상되자 그녀는 정부(情夫) 아이기스토스와 함께 그를 파멸시킬 계략을 꾸몄으며, 그의 귀환을 축하하기 위해 베푼 연회에서 그를 살해했다."]], [["It was intended by the conspirators to slay his son Orestes also, a lad not yet old enough to be an object of apprehension, but from whom, if he should be suffered to grow up, there might be danger.", "음모자들은 그의 아들 오레스테스도 죽이려 했으니, 그는 아직 우려의 대상이 될 만큼 자라지는 않았으나, 만약 그가 자라도록 내버려 둔다면 위험이 생길 수 있는 소년이었다."], ["Electra, the sister of Orestes, saved her brother’s life by sending him secretly away to his uncle Strophius, King of Phocis.", "오레스테스의 누이 엘렉트라는 그를 은밀히 그의 외숙부이자 포키스의 왕인 스트로피오스에게로 보내어 동생의 목숨을 구했다."], ["In the palace of Strophius Orestes grew up with the king’s son Pylades, and formed with him that ardent friendship which has become proverbial.", "스트로피오스의 궁전에서 오레스테스는 왕의 아들 필라데스와 함께 자랐고, 그와 더불어 속담으로까지 굳어진 그 뜨거운 우정을 맺었다."], ["Electra frequently reminded her brother by messengers of the duty of avenging his father’s death, and when grown up he consulted the oracle of Delphi, which confirmed him in his design.", "엘렉트라는 사자(使者)들을 통해 아버지의 죽음을 갚아야 할 의무를 동생에게 자주 일깨워 주었고, 다 자란 오레스테스가 델피의 신탁에 물으니, 신탁은 그의 결심을 확고히 해 주었다."], ["He therefore repaired in disguise to Argos, pretending to be a messenger from Strophius, who had come to announce the death of Orestes, and brought the ashes of the deceased in a funeral urn.", "그리하여 그는 변장을 하고 아르고스로 향했으니, 오레스테스의 죽음을 알리러 온 스트로피오스의 사자인 척하며, 죽은 자의 재를 장례용 항아리에 담아 가져왔다."], ["After visiting his father’s tomb and sacrificing upon it, according to the rites of the ancients, he made himself known to his sister Electra, and soon after slew both Ægisthus and Clytemnestra.", "그는 아버지의 무덤을 찾아가 옛사람들의 의식에 따라 그 위에 제물을 바친 뒤, 누이 엘렉트라에게 자신의 정체를 밝혔고, 머지않아 아이기스토스와 클리타임네스트라를 모두 죽였다."]], [["This revolting act, the slaughter of a mother by her son, though alleviated by the guilt of the victim and the express command of the gods, did not fail to awaken in the breasts of the ancients the same abhorrence that it does in ours.", "이 끔찍한 행위, 곧 아들이 어머니를 살해한 일은, 비록 희생자의 죄과와 신들의 명백한 명령으로 완화되기는 했으나, 옛사람들의 가슴속에 오늘날 우리가 느끼는 것과 똑같은 혐오를 불러일으키지 않을 수 없었다."], ["The Eumenides, avenging deities, seized upon Orestes, and drove him frantic from land to land.", "복수의 여신들인 에우메니데스가 오레스테스를 사로잡아 그를 이 땅 저 땅으로 미쳐 날뛰게 했다."], ["Pylades accompanied him in his wanderings and watched over him.", "필라데스는 그의 방랑에 동행하며 그를 보살폈다."], ["At length, in answer to a second appeal to the oracle, he was directed to go to Tauris in Scythia, and to bring thence a statue of Diana which was believed to have fallen from heaven.", "마침내, 신탁에 두 번째로 호소한 끝에, 그는 스키티아의 타우리스로 가서 하늘에서 떨어졌다고 믿어지는 디아나의 조각상을 그곳에서 가져오라는 지시를 받았다."], ["Accordingly Orestes and Pylades went to Tauris, where the barbarous people were accustomed to sacrifice to the goddess all strangers who fell into their hands.", "이에 따라 오레스테스와 필라데스는 타우리스로 갔으니, 그곳의 야만적인 백성들은 손아귀에 들어온 모든 이방인을 여신에게 제물로 바치는 관습이 있었다."], ["The two friends were seized and carried bound to the temple to be made victims.", "두 친구는 붙잡혀 결박당한 채 제물로 바쳐지기 위해 신전으로 끌려갔다."], ["But the priestess of Diana was no other than Iphigenia, the sister of Orestes, who, our readers will remember, was snatched away by Diana at the moment when she was about to be sacrificed.", "그러나 디아나의 여사제는 다름 아닌 오레스테스의 누이 이피게네이아였으니, 우리 독자들이 기억하다시피 그녀는 제물로 바쳐지려던 바로 그 순간에 디아나에게 채여 사라졌던 것이다."], ["Ascertaining from the prisoners who they were, Iphigenia disclosed herself to them, and the three made their escape with the statue of the goddess, and returned to Mycenæ.", "포로들에게서 그들이 누구인지를 알아낸 이피게네이아는 그들에게 자신의 정체를 밝혔고, 세 사람은 여신의 조각상을 가지고 함께 달아나 미케네로 돌아왔다."]], [["But Orestes was not yet relieved from the vengeance of the Erinyes.", "그러나 오레스테스는 아직 에리니에스의 복수에서 벗어나지 못했다."], ["At length he took refuge with Minerva at Athens.", "마침내 그는 아테네에서 미네르바에게 피신했다."], ["The goddess afforded him protection, and appointed the court of Areopagus to decide his fate.", "여신은 그를 보호해 주었고, 그의 운명을 결정하도록 아레오파고스 법정을 마련했다."], ["The Erinyes brought forward their accusation, and Orestes made the command of the Delphic oracle his excuse.", "에리니에스는 자신들의 고발을 제기했고, 오레스테스는 델피 신탁의 명령을 자기 변명으로 내세웠다."], ["When the court voted and the voices were equally divided, Orestes was acquitted by the command of Minerva.", "법정이 표결을 했는데 표가 똑같이 갈리자, 오레스테스는 미네르바의 명령으로 무죄를 선고받았다."]], [["Byron, in “Childe Harold,”", "바이런은 「차일드 해럴드」"], ["Canto IV., alludes to the story of Orestes:", "제4편에서 오레스테스의 이야기를 언급한다."]], [["“O thou who never yet of human wrong Left the unbalanced scale, great Nemesis!", "\"오, 인간의 부당함에 대하여 결코 저울을 한쪽으로 기울인 적 없는 그대, 위대한 네메시스여!"], ["Thou who didst call the Furies from the abyss, And round Orestes bade them howl and hiss, For that unnatural retribution,—just, Had it but been from hands less near,—in this, Thy former realm, I call thee from the dust!”", "심연에서 복수의 여신들을 불러내어 오레스테스를 에워싸고 울부짖고 쉭쉭거리게 한 그대여, 그 비정한 응보를 위하여, 그것이 다만 덜 가까운 손에서 비롯되었더라면 정당했을 그 응보를 위하여, 이곳, 그대의 옛 영토에서, 나는 그대를 티끌 속에서 불러내노라!\""]], [["One of the most pathetic scenes in the ancient drama is that in which Sophocles represents the meeting of Orestes and Electra, on his return from Phocis.", "고대 극에서 가장 비통한 장면 가운데 하나는 소포클레스가 묘사한, 오레스테스가 포키스에서 돌아왔을 때 그와 엘렉트라가 만나는 장면이다."], ["Orestes, mistaking Electra for one of the domestics, and desirous of keeping his arrival a secret till the hour of vengeance should arrive, produces the urn in which his ashes are supposed to rest.", "오레스테스는 엘렉트라를 하인들 가운데 하나로 잘못 알고, 또 복수의 시간이 닥칠 때까지 자신의 도착을 비밀로 하고자 하여, 자기 재가 담겨 있다고 여겨지는 항아리를 내놓는다."], ["Electra, believing him to be really dead, takes the urn and, embracing it, pours forth her grief in language full of tenderness and despair.", "엘렉트라는 그가 정말로 죽은 줄로 믿고 그 항아리를 받아 끌어안으며, 다정함과 절망으로 가득 찬 언어로 자신의 슬픔을 쏟아 낸다."]], [["Milton, in one of his sonnets, says:", "밀턴은 그의 소네트 가운데 하나에서 이렇게 말한다."]], [["“. .", "\"."], [".", "."], ["The repeated air Of sad Electra’s poet had the power To save the Athenian walls from ruin bare.”", ". 슬픈 엘렉트라의 시인이 거듭 부른 그 노래는 아테네의 성벽이 황량한 폐허로 무너지지 않도록 구할 힘을 지녔도다.\""]], [["This alludes to the story that when, on one occasion, the city of Athens was at the mercy of her Spartan foes, and it was proposed to destroy it, the thought was rejected upon the accidental quotation, by some one, of a chorus of Euripides.", "이는, 언젠가 아테네 도시가 스파르타 적들의 처분에 맡겨져 그것을 파괴하자는 제안이 나왔을 때, 누군가가 우연히 에우리피데스의 합창 한 대목을 인용하자 그 생각이 철회되었다는 이야기를 언급한 것이다."]], [["The facts relating to the city of Troy are still unknown to history.", "트로이아 도시에 관한 사실들은 아직도 역사에 알려져 있지 않다."], ["Antiquarians have long sought for the actual city and some record of its rulers.", "골동품 연구가들은 오랫동안 실제 도시와 그 통치자들에 관한 기록을 찾아왔다."], ["The most interesting explorations were those conducted about 1890 by the German scholar, Henry Schliemann, who believed that at the mound of Hissarlik, the traditional site of Troy, he had uncovered the ancient capital.", "가장 흥미로운 발굴은 1890년경 독일 학자 하인리히 슐리만이 수행한 것이었는데, 그는 트로이아의 전통적인 위치인 히사를리크의 언덕에서 자신이 그 고대의 수도를 드러냈다고 믿었다."], ["Schliemann excavated down below the ruins of three or four settlements, each revealing an earlier civilization, and finally came upon some royal jewels and other relics said to be “Priam’s Treasure.”", "슐리만은 서너 개의 정착지의 폐허 아래까지 파 내려갔으니, 그 하나하나가 더 이른 시대의 문명을 드러냈고, 마침내 \"프리아모스의 보물\"이라 일컬어지는 몇몇 왕가의 보석과 다른 유물들을 발견했다."], ["Scholars are by no means agreed as to the historic value of these discoveries.", "이 발견들의 역사적 가치에 관해서는 학자들의 의견이 결코 일치하지 않는다."]], [["————", "————"]], [["# CHAPTER XXIX", "# CHAPTER XXIX"]], [["ADVENTURES OF ULYSSES—THE LOTUS-EATERS—CYCLOPES—CIRCE—SIRENS—SCYLLA AND CHARYBDIS—CALYPSO\nTHE romantic poem of the Odyssey is now to engage our attention.", "이제 우리의 관심을 끄는 것은 낭만적 서사시 『오디세이아』다."], ["It narrates the wanderings of Ulysses (Odysseus in the Greek language) in his return from Troy to his own kingdom Ithaca.", "이 작품은 오디세우스[그리스어로는 오디세우스, 라틴어로는 율리시스]가 트로이아에서 자기 왕국 이타카로 돌아오는 동안 겪은 방랑을 이야기한다."]], [["From Troy the vessels first made land at Ismarus, city of the Ciconians, where, in a skirmish with the inhabitants, Ulysses lost six men from each ship.", "트로이아를 떠난 함선들은 처음에 키코네스족의 도시 이스마로스에 닿았는데, 그곳에서 주민들과 벌인 작은 충돌로 오디세우스는 배마다 여섯 명씩의 부하를 잃었다."], ["Sailing thence, they were overtaken by a storm which drove them for nine days along the sea till they reached the country of the Lotus-eaters.", "그곳을 떠나 항해하던 중 폭풍을 만나 아흐레 동안 바다 위로 떠밀려 다니다가 마침내 로토스를 먹는 사람들의 나라에 이르렀다."], ["Here, after watering, Ulysses sent three of his men to discover who the inhabitants were.", "이곳에서 물을 보충한 뒤, 오디세우스는 주민들이 누구인지 알아보려고 부하 세 명을 보냈다."], ["These men on coming among the Lotus-eaters were kindly entertained by them, and were given some of their own food, the lotus plant, to eat.", "로토스를 먹는 사람들 사이로 간 이 부하들은 그들에게 친절히 대접받았고, 그들 자신의 양식인 로토스 열매를 먹어 보라고 받았다."], ["The effect of this food was such that those who partook of it lost all thoughts of home and wished to remain in that country.", "이 음식의 효과는 그것을 먹은 사람은 누구든 고향 생각을 모조리 잃어버리고 그 나라에 남고 싶어 하게 만드는 것이었다."], ["It was by main force that Ulysses dragged these men away, and he was even obliged to tie them under the benches of the ships.[25]", "오디세우스가 이 부하들을 끌고 나올 수 있었던 것은 오로지 완력 덕분이었고, 심지어 그는 그들을 배의 노 젓는 자리 아래에 묶어 두기까지 해야 했다.[25]"]], [["They next arrived at the country of the Cyclopes.", "다음으로 그들은 키클로페스의 나라에 도착했다."], ["The Cyclopes were giants, who inhabited an island of which they were the only possessors.", "키클로페스는 어떤 섬에 살던 거인들로, 그 섬의 유일한 주인이었다."], ["The name means “round eye,” and these giants were so called because they had but one eye, and that placed in the middle of the forehead.", "그 이름은 〈둥근 눈〉을 뜻하는데, 이 거인들에게는 눈이 하나뿐이고 그것이 이마 한가운데에 박혀 있었기 때문에 그렇게 불렸다."], ["They dwelt in caves and fed on the wild productions of the island and on what their flocks yielded, for they were shepherds.", "그들은 동굴에 살며 섬의 야생 산물과 자기 가축이 내어 주는 것을 먹고 살았으니, 목동이었기 때문이다."], ["Ulysses left the main body of his ships at anchor, and with one vessel went to the Cyclopes’ island to explore for supplies.", "오디세우스는 함선의 대부분을 정박지에 닻을 내린 채 두고, 배 한 척만으로 키클로페스의 섬으로 가서 식량을 구할 만한 곳을 살폈다."], ["He landed with his companions, carrying with them a jar of wine for a present, and coming to a large cave they entered it, and finding no one within examined its contents.", "그는 동료들과 함께 선물로 쓸 포도주 한 단지를 들고 상륙했고, 어느 커다란 동굴에 이르러 그 안으로 들어갔다. 안에 아무도 없는 것을 보고 그 내용물을 살펴보았다."], ["They found it stored with the richest of the flock, quantities of cheese, pails and bowls of milk, lambs and kids in their pens, all in nice order.", "동굴에는 가축 중에서도 가장 좋은 것들이 가득했고, 치즈가 잔뜩 쌓여 있었으며, 우유를 담은 통과 그릇이 있었고, 우리 안에는 새끼 양과 새끼 염소들이 있었는데 모두 잘 정돈되어 있었다."], ["Presently arrived the master of the cave, Polyphemus, bearing an immense bundle of firewood, which he threw down before the cavern’s mouth.", "곧 동굴의 주인 폴리페모스가 거대한 장작 묶음을 지고 와서, 그것을 동굴 입구 앞에 내던졌다."], ["He then drove into the cave the sheep and goats to be milked, and, entering, rolled to the cave’s mouth an enormous rock, that twenty oxen could not draw.", "그러고는 젖을 짜려고 양과 염소를 동굴 안으로 몰아넣고, 자기도 들어와서는 황소 스무 마리로도 끌지 못할 만한 어마어마한 바위를 굴려 동굴 입구를 막았다."], ["Next he sat down and milked his ewes, preparing a part for cheese, and setting the rest aside for his customary drink.", "다음으로 그는 자리에 앉아 암양들의 젖을 짜서, 일부는 치즈로 만들고 나머지는 평소의 음료로 따로 두었다."], ["Then, turning round his great eye, he discerned the strangers, and growled out to them, demanding who they were, and where from.", "그런 다음 그 커다란 눈을 굴리다가 낯선 자들을 발견하고는, 그들에게 으르렁대며 누구이며 어디서 왔는지 따져 물었다."], ["Ulysses replied most humbly, stating that they were Greeks, from the great expedition that had lately won so much glory in the conquest of Troy; that they were now on their way home, and finished by imploring his hospitality in the name of the gods.", "오디세우스는 더없이 겸손하게 대답하여, 자신들은 그리스인이며 최근 트로이아 정복으로 큰 영광을 얻은 그 대원정에서 돌아오는 길이고, 지금 고향으로 향하는 중이라고 말했다. 그리고 신들의 이름으로 그의 환대를 간청하며 말을 맺었다."], ["Polyphemus deigned no answer, but reaching out his hand seized two of the Greeks, whom he hurled against the side of the cave, and dashed out their brains.", "폴리페모스는 대답할 가치도 없다는 듯 아무 말도 하지 않고, 손을 뻗어 그리스인 둘을 붙잡아 동굴 벽에 메다꽂아 그 머리를 으깨 버렸다."], ["He proceeded to devour them with great relish, and having made a hearty meal, stretched himself out on the floor to sleep.", "그는 그들을 아주 맛있게 먹어 치우기 시작했고, 든든히 식사를 마치고는 바닥에 몸을 뻗고 잠을 잤다."], ["Ulysses was tempted to seize the opportunity and plunge his sword into him as he slept, but recollected that it would only expose them all to certain destruction, as the rock with which the giant had closed up the door was far beyond their power to remove, and they would therefore be in hopeless imprisonment.", "오디세우스는 이 기회를 잡아 잠든 그에게 칼을 찔러 넣고 싶은 충동을 느꼈지만, 그렇게 하면 모두가 확실한 파멸에 노출될 뿐이라는 것을 떠올렸다. 거인이 입구를 막아 둔 바위는 그들의 힘으로는 도저히 치울 수 없는 것이어서, 그렇게 되면 그들은 희망 없는 갇힌 처지가 될 것이기 때문이었다."], ["Next morning the giant seized two more of the Greeks, and despatched them in the same manner as their companions, feasting on their flesh till no fragment was left.", "이튿날 아침 거인은 그리스인 두 명을 더 붙잡아, 그 동료들에게 했던 것과 똑같은 방식으로 처치하고는 한 조각도 남기지 않을 때까지 그 살을 먹어 치웠다."], ["He then moved away the rock from the door, drove out his flocks, and went out, carefully replacing the barrier after him.", "그런 다음 입구에서 바위를 치우고 가축들을 몰아 밖으로 나가면서, 자기 뒤로 그 장벽을 다시 조심스럽게 제자리에 돌려놓았다."], ["When he was gone Ulysses planned how he might take vengeance for his murdered friends, and effect his escape with his surviving companions.", "그가 떠난 뒤 오디세우스는 살해당한 친구들의 원수를 어떻게 갚을지, 그리고 살아남은 동료들과 함께 어떻게 탈출할지 궁리했다."], ["He made his men prepare a massive bar of wood cut by the Cyclops for a staff, which they found in the cave.", "그는 부하들을 시켜, 키클롭스가 지팡이로 쓰려고 잘라 둔, 동굴 안에서 발견한 묵직한 나무 막대를 준비하게 했다."], ["They sharpened the end of it, and seasoned it in the fire, and hid it under the straw on the cavern floor.", "그들은 그 끝을 뾰족하게 깎아 불에 그슬려 단단하게 만든 뒤, 동굴 바닥의 짚 아래에 숨겨 두었다."], ["Then four of the boldest were selected, with whom Ulysses joined himself as a fifth.", "그런 다음 가장 대담한 네 명이 뽑혔고, 오디세우스가 다섯 번째로 그들과 함께했다."], ["The Cyclops came home at evening, rolled away the stone and drove in his flock as usual.", "저녁이 되자 키클롭스가 집으로 돌아와 평소처럼 돌을 굴려 치우고 가축을 안으로 몰아넣었다."], ["After milking them and making his arrangements as before, he seized two more of Ulysses’ companions and dashed their brains out, and made his evening meal upon them as he had on the others.", "젖을 짜고 전과 같이 일을 처리한 뒤, 그는 오디세우스의 동료 두 명을 더 붙잡아 그 머리를 으깨고, 다른 자들에게 했던 것처럼 그들을 저녁 끼니로 삼았다."], ["After he had supped, Ulysses approaching him handed him a bowl of wine, saying, “Cyclops, this is wine; taste and drink after thy meal of men’s flesh.”", "식사를 마친 뒤 오디세우스가 그에게 다가가 포도주 한 그릇을 건네며 말했다. \"키클롭스여, 이것은 포도주요."], ["He took and drank it, and was hugely delighted with it, and called for more.", "사람 고기 식사 뒤에 한번 맛보고 마셔 보시오.\""], ["Ulysses supplied him once again, which pleased the giant so much that he promised him as a favor that he should be the last of the party devoured.", "그는 그것을 받아 마시고는 크게 기뻐하며 더 달라고 청했다. 오디세우스가 한 번 더 따라 주자 거인은 무척 흡족해하며, 호의로서 그를 일행 중 마지막으로 잡아먹겠다고 약속했다."], ["He asked his name, to which Ulysses replied, “My name is Noman.”", "거인이 그의 이름을 묻자, 오디세우스가 대답했다. \"내 이름은 아무도아니라요.\""]], [["After his supper the giant lay down to repose, and was soon sound asleep.", "저녁 식사 뒤 거인은 쉬려고 누웠고, 곧 깊이 잠들었다."], ["Then Ulysses with his four select friends thrust the end of the stake into the fire till it was all one burning coal, then poising it exactly above the giant’s only eye, they buried it deeply into the socket, twirling it round as a carpenter does his auger.", "그러자 오디세우스는 뽑힌 네 친구와 함께 막대 끝을 불 속에 밀어 넣어 온통 타는 숯이 될 때까지 달구었고, 그것을 거인의 하나뿐인 눈 바로 위에 정확히 겨눈 다음, 목수가 송곳을 돌리듯 빙빙 돌려 가며 그 눈구멍 속에 깊이 박아 넣었다."], ["The howling monster with his outcry filled the cavern, and Ulysses with his aids nimbly got out of his way and concealed themselves in the cave.", "울부짖는 괴물의 비명이 동굴을 가득 채웠고, 오디세우스와 그 조력자들은 재빨리 그의 손길을 피해 동굴 안에 몸을 숨겼다."], ["He, bellowing, called aloud on all the Cyclopes dwelling in the caves around him, far and near.", "그는 고함을 지르며, 멀고 가까운 주위 동굴에 사는 모든 키클로페스를 큰 소리로 불렀다."], ["They on his cry flocked round the den, and inquired what grievous hurt had caused him to sound such an alarm and break their slumbers.", "그들은 그의 외침을 듣고 굴 주위로 몰려와, 그가 무슨 끔찍한 상처를 입었기에 그토록 경보를 울려 자기들의 잠을 깨우느냐고 물었다. 그가 대답했다."], ["He replied, “O friends, I die, and Noman gives the blow.”", "\"오 친구들이여, 나는 죽어 가는데, 아무도아니라가 그 일격을 가했소.\""], ["They answered, “If no man hurts thee it is the stroke of Jove, and thou must bear it.”", "그들이 대답했다. \"아무도 그대를 해치지 않았다면 그것은 제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]의 일격이니, 그대는 그것을 견뎌야 하오.\""], ["So saying, they left him groaning.", "그렇게 말하고 그들은 신음하는 그를 남겨 둔 채 떠나갔다."]], [["Next morning the Cyclops rolled away the stone to let his flock out to pasture, but planted himself in the door of the cave to feel of all as they went out, that Ulysses and his men should not escape with them.", "이튿날 아침 키클롭스는 가축을 풀밭으로 내보내려고 돌을 굴려 치웠으나, 오디세우스와 그 부하들이 가축에 섞여 도망치지 못하도록 동굴 입구에 자리를 잡고 나가는 짐승들을 모두 더듬어 보았다."], ["But Ulysses had made his men harness the rams of the flock three abreast, with osiers which they found on the floor of the cave.", "그러나 오디세우스는 부하들을 시켜, 동굴 바닥에서 찾아낸 버들가지로 가축의 숫양들을 셋씩 나란히 묶게 해 두었다."], ["To the middle ram of the three one of the Greeks suspended himself, so protected by the exterior rams on either side.", "셋 중 가운데 숫양에 그리스인 한 명이 매달려, 양옆의 바깥쪽 두 숫양에 의해 가려지게 했다."], ["As they passed, the giant felt of the animals’ backs and sides, but never thought of their bellies; so the men all passed safe, Ulysses himself being on the last one that passed.", "그들이 지나갈 때 거인은 짐승들의 등과 옆구리를 더듬었으나, 그 배를 살펴볼 생각은 전혀 하지 못했다. 그리하여 부하들은 모두 무사히 빠져나갔고, 오디세우스 자신은 맨 마지막으로 지나가는 짐승에 매달려 있었다."], ["When they had got a few paces from the cavern, Ulysses and his friends released themselves from their rams, and drove a good part of the flock down to the shore to their boat.", "동굴에서 몇 걸음 벗어나자 오디세우스와 그 친구들은 숫양에서 풀려나, 가축의 상당 부분을 해안의 배까지 몰고 갔다."], ["They put them aboard with all haste, then pushed off from the shore, and when at a safe distance Ulysses shouted out, “Cyclops, the gods have well requited thee for thy atrocious deeds.", "그들은 서둘러 가축을 배에 실은 뒤 해안에서 배를 밀어냈고, 안전한 거리에 이르자 오디세우스가 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"키클롭스여, 신들께서 그대의 잔악한 행위에 마땅한 보답을 내리셨다."], ["Know it is Ulysses to whom thou owest thy shameful loss of sight.”", "그대가 수치스럽게 시력을 잃은 것은 바로 오디세우스 덕분임을 알아라.\""], ["The Cyclops, hearing this, seized a rock that projected from the side of the mountain, and rending it from its bed, he lifted it high in the air, then exerting all his force, hurled it in the direction of the voice.", "이 말을 들은 키클롭스는 산허리에서 튀어나온 바위를 붙잡아 그 자리에서 뜯어내더니, 그것을 높이 공중으로 들어 올린 다음 온 힘을 다해 목소리가 난 방향으로 던졌다."], ["Down came the mass, just clearing the vessel’s stern.", "거대한 덩어리가 떨어지면서 배의 고물을 아슬아슬하게 비껴갔다."], ["The ocean, at the plunge of the huge rock, heaved the ship towards the land, so that it barely escaped being swamped by the waves.", "거대한 바위가 풍덩 떨어지자 바다가 솟구쳐 배를 뭍 쪽으로 밀어붙였고, 배는 가까스로 파도에 삼켜지는 것을 면했다."], ["When they had with the utmost difficulty pulled off shore, Ulysses was about to hail the giant again, but his friends besought him not to do so.", "그들이 천신만고 끝에 해안에서 멀어지자 오디세우스가 다시 거인에게 소리치려 했으나, 친구들이 그러지 말라고 간청했다."], ["He could not forbear, however, letting the giant know that they had escaped his missile, but waited till they had reached a safer distance than before.", "그러나 그는 그들이 그 투척물을 피해 달아났다는 것을 거인에게 알리지 않고는 견딜 수 없었으니, 다만 전보다 더 안전한 거리에 이를 때까지 기다렸다."], ["The giant answered them with curses, but Ulysses and his friends plied their oars vigorously, and soon regained their companions.", "거인은 저주로 그들에게 응답했지만, 오디세우스와 그 친구들은 힘차게 노를 저어 곧 동료들에게 되돌아갔다."]], [["Ulysses next arrived at the island of Æolus.", "다음으로 오디세우스는 아이올로스의 섬에 이르렀다."], ["To this monarch Jupiter had intrusted the government of the winds, to send them forth or retain them at his will.", "이 왕에게 제우스는 바람을 다스리는 일을 맡겨, 제 뜻대로 바람을 내보내거나 붙잡아 두게 했다."], ["He treated Ulysses hospitably, and at his departure gave him, tied up in a leathern bag, with a silver string, such winds as might be hurtful and dangerous, commanding fair winds to blow the barks towards their country.", "그는 오디세우스를 후하게 대접했고, 그가 떠날 때 해롭고 위험할 만한 바람들을 가죽 자루에 담아 은실로 묶어 주면서, 순풍에게는 그 배들을 그들의 고향 쪽으로 불어 주라고 명령했다."], ["Nine days they sped before the wind, and all that time Ulysses had stood at the helm, without sleep.", "아흐레 동안 그들은 바람을 타고 달렸고, 그 내내 오디세우스는 잠도 자지 않고 키를 잡고 서 있었다."], ["At last quite exhausted he lay down to sleep.", "마침내 완전히 지친 그가 누워 잠들었다."], ["While he slept, the crew conferred together about the mysterious bag, and concluded it must contain treasures given by the hospitable king Æolus to their commander.", "그가 잠든 사이 선원들은 그 신비로운 자루에 대해 함께 수군대다가, 그것이 환대해 준 왕 아이올로스가 자기들의 지휘관에게 준 보물을 담고 있는 것이 틀림없다고 결론지었다."], ["Tempted to secure some portion for themselves, they loosed the string, when immediately the winds rushed forth.", "그 일부를 자기들 몫으로 챙기고 싶은 유혹에 빠진 그들이 실을 풀자, 곧바로 바람들이 쏟아져 나왔다."], ["The ships were driven far from their course, and back again to the island they had just left.", "배들은 항로에서 멀리 밀려나, 방금 떠나온 바로 그 섬으로 도로 떠밀려 갔다."], ["Æolus was so indignant at their folly that he refused to assist them further, and they were obliged to labor over their course once more by means of their oars.", "아이올로스는 그들의 어리석음에 너무도 분개하여 더 이상 도와주기를 거부했고, 그들은 다시 한번 노를 저어 힘겹게 항로를 헤쳐 나가야 했다."]], [["Their next adventure was with the barbarous tribe of Læstrygonians.", "다음 모험은 야만적인 라이스트리고네스족과의 일이었다."], ["The vessels all pushed into the harbor, tempted by the secure appearance of the cove, completely land-locked; only Ulysses moored his vessel without.", "함선들은 모두, 완전히 뭍으로 둘러싸여 안전해 보이는 작은 만의 모습에 이끌려 항구 안으로 들어갔다. 오직 오디세우스만이 자기 배를 그 바깥에 매어 두었다."], ["As soon as the Læstrygonians found the ships completely in their power they attacked them, heaving huge stones which broke and overturned them, and with their spears despatched the seamen as they struggled in the water.", "라이스트리고네스족은 배들이 완전히 자기들의 손아귀에 들어왔음을 알자마자 그것들을 공격하여, 거대한 돌을 던져 배를 부수고 뒤집었으며, 물속에서 허우적대는 선원들을 창으로 처치했다."], ["All the vessels with their crews were destroyed, except Ulysses’ own ship, which had remained outside, and finding no safety but in flight, he exhorted his men to ply their oars vigorously, and they escaped.", "바깥에 남아 있던 오디세우스 자신의 배를 제외하고는 배와 선원이 모두 파멸했고, 도망치는 것 말고는 안전을 찾을 수 없게 된 그는 부하들에게 힘차게 노를 저으라고 독려했고, 그들은 가까스로 빠져나갔다."]], [["With grief for their slain companions mixed with joy at their own escape, they pursued their way till they arrived at the Ææan isle, where Circe dwelt, the daughter of the sun.", "살해당한 동료들에 대한 슬픔에 자신들이 탈출했다는 기쁨이 뒤섞인 채, 그들은 길을 재촉하여 마침내 아이아이아 섬에 이르렀다. 그곳에는 태양의 딸 키르케가 살고 있었다."], ["Landing here, Ulysses climbed a hill, and gazing round saw no signs of habitation except in one spot at the centre of the island, where he perceived a palace embowered with trees.", "이곳에 상륙한 오디세우스는 언덕에 올라 사방을 둘러보았으나, 섬 한가운데의 한 지점을 제외하고는 사람이 사는 흔적이 보이지 않았다. 그 지점에는 나무들에 둘러싸인 궁전이 보였다."], ["He sent forward one-half of his crew, under the command of Eurylochus, to see what prospect of hospitality they might find.", "그는 어떤 환대를 기대할 수 있을지 알아보려고, 에우릴로코스의 지휘 아래 선원의 절반을 앞서 보냈다."], ["As they approached the palace, they found themselves surrounded by lions, tigers, and wolves, not fierce, but tamed by Circe’s art, for she was a powerful magician.", "그들이 궁전에 다가가자 사방을 사자와 호랑이와 늑대가 에워쌌는데, 사납지 않고 키르케의 기술로 길들여져 있었다. 그녀가 강력한 마법사였기 때문이다."], ["All these animals had once been men, but had been changed by Circe’s enchantments into the forms of beasts.", "이 짐승들은 모두 한때 사람이었으나 키르케의 마법으로 짐승의 모습으로 변한 것이었다."], ["The sounds of soft music were heard from within, and a sweet female voice singing.", "안에서는 부드러운 음악 소리와 달콤한 여인의 노랫소리가 들려왔다."], ["Eurylochus called aloud and the goddess came forth and invited them in; they all gladly entered except Eurylochus, who suspected danger.", "에우릴로코스가 큰 소리로 부르자 여신이 나와 그들을 안으로 청했다. 위험을 의심한 에우릴로코스만 빼고는 모두가 기꺼이 들어갔다."], ["The goddess conducted her guests to a seat, and had them served with wine and other delicacies.", "여신은 손님들을 자리로 안내하여 포도주와 그 밖의 진미를 대접하게 했다."], ["When they had feasted heartily, she touched them one by one with her wand, and they became immediately changed into swine, in “head, body, voice, and bristles,” yet with their intellects as before.", "그들이 든든히 먹고 마시자, 그녀는 지팡이로 그들을 하나씩 건드렸고, 그들은 즉시 〈머리와 몸과 목소리와 뻣뻣한 털〉까지 돼지로 변했으나, 지성만은 전과 같았다."], ["She shut them in her sties and supplied them with acorns and such other things as swine love.", "그녀는 그들을 우리에 가두고 도토리와 그 밖에 돼지가 좋아하는 것들을 먹였다."]], [["Eurylochus hurried back to the ship and told the tale.", "에우릴로코스는 배로 서둘러 돌아가 그 이야기를 전했다."], ["Ulysses thereupon determined to go himself, and try if by any means he might deliver his companions.", "그러자 오디세우스는 직접 가서 어떻게든 동료들을 구해 낼 수 있을지 시도해 보기로 결심했다."], ["As he strode onward alone, he met a youth who addressed him familiarly, appearing to be acquainted with his adventures.", "홀로 성큼성큼 나아가던 그는 한 젊은이를 만났는데, 그 젊은이는 마치 그의 모험을 다 알고 있는 듯 친근하게 말을 걸어왔다."], ["He announced himself as Mercury, and informed Ulysses of the arts of Circe, and of the danger of approaching her.", "그는 자신을 헤르메스[로마의 〈메르쿠리우스〉]라고 밝히고, 오디세우스에게 키르케의 기술과 그녀에게 다가가는 일의 위험을 일러 주었다."], ["As Ulysses was not to be dissuaded from his attempt, Mercury provided him with a sprig of the plant Moly, of wonderful power to resist sorceries, and instructed him how to act.", "오디세우스가 그 시도를 단념하려 하지 않자, 헤르메스는 그에게 마법에 맞서는 놀라운 힘을 지닌 몰뤼라는 식물의 가지를 건네주고, 어떻게 행동해야 할지 가르쳐 주었다."], ["Ulysses proceeded, and reaching the palace was courteously received by Circe, who entertained him as she had done his companions, and after he had eaten and drank, touched him with her wand, saying, “Hence, seek the sty and wallow with thy friends.”", "오디세우스가 나아가 궁전에 이르자 키르케는 그를 정중히 맞이하여 그의 동료들에게 했던 대로 대접했고, 그가 먹고 마신 뒤에 지팡이로 그를 건드리며 말했다. \"썩 물러가, 우리를 찾아가 네 친구들과 함께 뒹굴어라.\""], ["But he, instead of obeying, drew his sword and rushed upon her with fury in his countenance.", "그러나 그는 복종하는 대신, 칼을 뽑아 얼굴에 분노를 띤 채 그녀에게 달려들었다."], ["She fell on her knees and begged for mercy.", "그녀는 무릎을 꿇고 자비를 빌었다."], ["He dictated a solemn oath that she would release his companions and practise no further harm against him or them; and she repeated it, at the same time promising to dismiss them all in safety after hospitably entertaining them.", "그는 그녀가 동료들을 풀어 주고 자신이나 그들에게 더 이상 해를 끼치지 않겠다는 엄숙한 맹세를 하도록 명령했고, 그녀는 그것을 따라 외우면서 동시에, 그들을 후히 대접한 뒤에 모두 무사히 보내 주겠다고 약속했다."], ["She was as good as her word.", "그녀는 약속을 어김없이 지켰다."], ["The men were restored to their shapes, the rest of the crew summoned from the shore, and the whole magnificently entertained day after day, till Ulysses seemed to have forgotten his native land, and to have reconciled himself to an inglorious life of ease and pleasure.", "부하들은 본래의 모습을 되찾았고, 나머지 선원들도 해안에서 불려 왔으며, 모두가 날마다 성대히 대접받았다. 그리하여 오디세우스는 고국을 잊은 듯, 안일과 쾌락의 영광 없는 삶에 안주해 버린 것 같았다."]], [["At length his companions recalled him to nobler sentiments, and he received their admonition gratefully.", "마침내 동료들이 그에게 더 고귀한 마음을 일깨워 주었고, 그는 그들의 충고를 고맙게 받아들였다."], ["Circe aided their departure, and instructed them how to pass safely by the coast of the Sirens.", "키르케는 그들의 출발을 도왔고, 세이레네스의 해안을 무사히 지나는 법을 가르쳐 주었다."], ["The Sirens were sea-nymphs who had the power of charming by their song all who heard them, so that the unhappy mariners were irresistibly impelled to cast themselves into the sea to their destruction.", "세이레네스는 바다의 님프들로, 그 노래로 듣는 자를 모두 흘리는 힘을 지니고 있어서, 불행한 뱃사람들이 거역할 수 없는 충동에 이끌려 스스로 바다에 몸을 던져 파멸하게 만들었다."], ["Circe directed Ulysses to fill the ears of his seamen with wax, so that they should not hear the strain; and to cause himself to be bound to the mast, and his people to be strictly enjoined, whatever he might say or do, by no means to release him till they should have passed the Sirens’ island.", "키르케는 오디세우스에게, 선원들이 그 노랫가락을 듣지 못하도록 그들의 귀를 밀랍으로 막고, 자신은 돛대에 묶이게 하되, 자기가 무슨 말이나 행동을 하더라도 세이레네스의 섬을 지날 때까지는 결코 풀어 주지 말라고 부하들에게 엄히 일러 두라고 지시했다."], ["Ulysses obeyed these directions.", "오디세우스는 이 지시를 따랐다."], ["He filled the ears of his people with wax, and suffered them to bind him with cords firmly to the mast.", "그는 부하들의 귀를 밀랍으로 막고, 그들이 자기를 밧줄로 돛대에 단단히 묶도록 했다."], ["As they approached the Sirens’ island, the sea was calm, and over the waters came the notes of music so ravishing and attractive that Ulysses struggled to get loose, and by cries and signs to his people begged to be released; but they, obedient to his previous orders, sprang forward and bound him still faster.", "그들이 세이레네스의 섬에 다가가자 바다는 잔잔했고, 물 위로 너무도 황홀하고 매혹적인 음악 가락이 들려와 오디세우스는 풀려나려 몸부림치며 부하들에게 소리와 손짓으로 풀어 달라고 애원했다. 그러나 그들은 그가 미리 내린 명령에 복종하여 앞으로 달려들어 그를 더욱 단단히 묶었다."], ["They held on their course, and the music grew fainter till it ceased to be heard, when with joy Ulysses gave his companions the signal to unseal their ears, and they relieved him from his bonds.", "그들은 항로를 유지했고, 음악은 점점 희미해지다가 더 이상 들리지 않게 되었으며, 그제야 오디세우스는 기쁜 마음으로 동료들에게 귀를 막은 것을 떼라는 신호를 보냈고, 그들은 그를 결박에서 풀어 주었다."]], [["The imagination of a modern poet, Keats, has discovered for us the thoughts that passed through the brains of the victims of Circe, after their transformation.", "근대 시인 키츠의 상상력은 키르케의 희생자들이 변신한 뒤 그들의 머릿속을 스쳐 간 생각을 우리에게 드러내 주었다."], ["In his “Endymion” he represents one of them, a monarch in the guise of an elephant, addressing the sorceress in human language, thus:", "그는 자신의 작품 「엔디미온」에서 그들 중 하나, 곧 코끼리의 모습을 한 어느 왕이 인간의 언어로 마녀에게 이렇게 호소하는 모습을 그렸다."]], [["“I sue not for my happy crown again; I sue not for my phalanx on the plain; I sue not for my lone, my widowed wife; I sue not for my ruddy drops of life, My children fair, my lovely girls and boys; I will forget them; I will pass these joys, Ask nought so heavenward; so too—too high; Only I pray, as fairest boon, to die; To be delivered from this cumbrous flesh, From this gross, detestable, filthy mesh, And merely given to the cold, bleak air. Have mercy, goddess! Circe, feel my prayer!”", "\"나는 내 행복한 왕관을 되돌려 달라 간청하지 않노라. 나는 평원의 내 군대를 간청하지 않노라. 나는 외로이 홀로된 내 아내를 간청하지 않노라. 나는 붉은 생명의 핏방울을 간청하지 않노라. 내 어여쁜 아이들, 사랑스러운 딸들과 아들들을. 나는 그들을 잊으리라. 이 기쁨들을 흘려보내리라. 그토록 천국 같은 것은 아무것도 청하지 않으리. 너무도—너무도 높으니. 오직 나는, 가장 아름다운 은혜로서, 죽기를 기도하노라. 이 거추장스러운 육신에서 벗어나기를, 이 조잡하고 혐오스럽고 더러운 그물에서 벗어나, 그저 차갑고 황량한 대기에 내맡겨지기를. 자비를 베푸소서, 여신이여! 키르케여, 내 기도를 헤아리소서!\""]], [["# SCYLLA AND CHARYBDIS", "# SCYLLA AND CHARYBDIS"]], [["Ulysses had been warned by Circe of the two monsters Scylla and Charybdis.", "오디세우스는 키르케에게서 두 괴물 스킬라와 카립디스에 대한 경고를 들은 바 있었다."], ["We have already met with Scylla in the story of Glaucus, and remember that she was once a beautiful maiden and was changed into a snaky monster by Circe.", "우리는 글라우코스의 이야기에서 이미 스킬라를 만났고, 그녀가 한때 아름다운 처녀였으나 키르케에 의해 뱀 같은 괴물로 변했다는 것을 기억한다."], ["She dwelt in a cave high up on the cliff, from whence she was accustomed to thrust forth her long necks (for she had six heads), and in each of her mouths to seize one of the crew of every vessel passing within reach.", "그녀는 절벽 높은 곳의 동굴에 살면서, 그곳에서 자신의 긴 목들을(머리가 여섯이었으므로) 쑥 내밀어, 닿는 거리 안으로 지나가는 배마다 그 입 하나하나로 선원을 한 명씩 낚아채곤 했다."], ["The other terror, Charybdis, was a gulf, nearly on a level with the water.", "또 다른 공포인 카립디스는 거의 수면과 같은 높이에 있는 소용돌이였다."], ["Thrice each day the water rushed into a frightful chasm, and thrice was disgorged.", "하루에 세 번씩 물이 무시무시한 심연 속으로 휘몰려 들어갔다가, 세 번씩 토해 내어졌다."], ["Any vessel coming near the whirlpool when the tide was rushing in must inevitably be ingulfed; not Neptune himself could save it.", "조수가 휘몰려 들어올 때 그 소용돌이 가까이 다가오는 배는 무엇이든 어김없이 삼켜지고 말았으니, 포세이돈[로마의 〈넵투누스〉] 자신도 그것을 구할 수 없었다."]], [["On approaching the haunt of the dread monsters, Ulysses kept strict watch to discover them.", "이 무시무시한 괴물들의 소굴에 다가가면서 오디세우스는 그들을 발견하려고 면밀히 경계했다."], ["The roar of the waters as Charybdis ingulfed them, gave warning at a distance, but Scylla could nowhere be discerned.", "카립디스가 물을 삼킬 때 나는 물소리는 멀리서부터 경고를 주었으나, 스킬라는 어디서도 분간할 수 없었다."], ["While Ulysses and his men watched with anxious eyes the dreadful whirlpool, they were not equally on their guard from the attack of Scylla, and the monster, darting forth her snaky heads, caught six of his men, and bore them away, shrieking, to her den.", "오디세우스와 그 부하들이 불안한 눈으로 그 무서운 소용돌이를 지켜보는 사이, 그들은 스킬라의 공격에 대해서는 그만큼 경계하지 못했고, 괴물이 그 뱀 같은 머리들을 쏜살같이 내밀어 부하 여섯을 붙잡아, 비명을 지르는 그들을 자기 굴로 끌고 가 버렸다."], ["It was the saddest sight Ulysses had yet seen; to behold his friends thus sacrificed and hear their cries, unable to afford them any assistance.", "그것은 오디세우스가 그때까지 본 가장 슬픈 광경이었다. 친구들이 그렇게 제물로 바쳐지는 것을 지켜보며 그들의 비명을 들으면서도 아무런 도움도 줄 수 없었기 때문이다."]], [["Circe had warned him of another danger.", "키르케는 그에게 또 다른 위험을 경고해 두었다."], ["After passing Scylla and Charybdis the next land he would make was Thrinakia, an island whereon were pastured the cattle of Hyperion, the Sun, tended by his daughters Lampetia and Phaëthusa.", "스킬라와 카립디스를 지나면 그가 다음으로 닿게 될 땅은 트리나키아라는 섬인데, 그곳에는 태양 히페리온의 소 떼가 방목되어 그의 딸 람페티에와 파에투사가 돌보고 있었다."], ["These flocks must not be violated, whatever the wants of the voyagers might be.", "항해자들의 필요가 무엇이든 간에 이 가축들을 침범해서는 안 되었다."], ["If this injunction were transgressed destruction was sure to fall on the offenders.", "만약 이 명령을 어기면 어김없이 범한 자들에게 파멸이 닥칠 것이었다."]], [["Ulysses would willingly have passed the island of the Sun without stopping, but his companions so urgently pleaded for the rest and refreshment that would be derived from anchoring and passing the night on shore, that Ulysses yielded.", "오디세우스는 멈추지 않고 태양의 섬을 지나치고 싶었으나, 동료들이 닻을 내려 해안에서 하룻밤을 보내며 얻을 휴식과 원기 회복을 너무도 간절히 청하는 바람에 오디세우스가 양보했다."], ["He bound them, however, with an oath that they would not touch one of the animals of the sacred flocks and herds, but content themselves with what provision they yet had left of the supply which Circe had put on board.", "그러나 그는 그들에게, 신성한 가축과 소 떼 가운데 어느 한 마리도 건드리지 않고, 키르케가 배에 실어 준 식량 중에 아직 남은 것으로 만족하겠다는 맹세를 시켰다."], ["So long as this supply lasted the people kept their oath, but contrary winds detained them at the island for a month, and after consuming all their stock of provisions, they were forced to rely upon the birds and fishes they could catch.", "이 식량이 남아 있는 동안에는 사람들이 맹세를 지켰으나, 역풍이 그들을 한 달이나 섬에 붙잡아 두었고, 비축한 식량을 모두 먹어 치운 뒤에는 잡을 수 있는 새와 물고기에 의존할 수밖에 없었다."], ["Famine pressed them, and at length one day, in the absence of Ulysses, they slew some of the cattle, vainly attempting to make amends for the deed by offering from them a portion to the offended powers.", "기근이 그들을 압박했고, 마침내 어느 날 오디세우스가 없는 사이에 그들은 소 몇 마리를 잡아, 그 일부를 노여워하는 신들에게 바침으로써 그 행위를 보상하려 했으나 헛된 일이었다."], ["Ulysses, on his return to the shore, was horror-struck at perceiving what they had done, and the more so on account of the portentous signs which followed.", "해안으로 돌아온 오디세우스는 그들이 저지른 일을 알아채고 공포에 사로잡혔으며, 뒤따른 불길한 징조 때문에 더욱 그러했다."], ["The skins crept on the ground, and the joints of meat lowed on the spits while roasting.", "가죽들이 땅 위를 기어 다녔고, 꼬챙이에 꿰여 구워지던 고깃점들이 음매 하고 울었다."]], [["The wind becoming fair they sailed from the island.", "바람이 순해지자 그들은 섬을 떠나 항해했다."], ["They had not gone far when the weather changed, and a storm of thunder and lightning ensued.", "멀리 가지 못했을 때 날씨가 바뀌어 천둥과 번개를 동반한 폭풍이 일었다."], ["A stroke of lightning shattered their mast, which in its fall killed the pilot.", "번개 한 줄기가 돛대를 산산조각 냈고, 쓰러진 돛대가 키잡이를 죽였다."], ["At last the vessel itself came to pieces.", "마침내 배 자체가 부서져 버렸다."], ["The keel and mast floating side by side, Ulysses formed of them a raft, to which he clung, and, the wind changing, the waves bore him to Calypso’s island.", "용골과 돛대가 나란히 떠다니자 오디세우스는 그것으로 뗏목을 만들어 매달렸고, 바람이 바뀌면서 파도가 그를 칼립소의 섬으로 실어 갔다."], ["All the rest of the crew perished.", "나머지 선원들은 모두 죽고 말았다."]], [["The following allusion to the topics we have just been considering is from Milton’s “Comus,”", "방금 우리가 살펴본 주제들에 대한 다음 인유는 밀턴의 「코머스」"], ["line 252:", "252행에서 가져온 것이다."]], [["“. .", "\"."], [".", "."], ["I have often heard My mother Circe and the Sirens three, Amidst the flowery-kirtled Naiades, Culling their potent herbs and baneful drugs, Who as they sung would take the prisoned soul And lap it in Elysium.", ". 나는 자주 들었노라 내 어머니 키르케와 세이레네스 셋이, 꽃무늬 치마를 두른 나이아데스들 사이에서, 저들의 강력한 약초와 해로운 독초를 캐며 노래할 때면, 갇힌 영혼을 사로잡아 엘리시온에 감싸 두는 것을."], ["Scylla wept, And chid her barking waves into attention, And fell Charybdis murmured soft applause.”", "스킬라는 울었고, 짖어 대는 제 파도를 꾸짖어 귀 기울이게 했으며, 무서운 카립디스는 부드러운 갈채를 중얼거렸노라.\""]], [["Scylla and Charybdis have become proverbial, to denote opposite dangers which beset one’s course.", "스킬라와 카립디스는 사람의 진로를 위협하는 양쪽의 위험을 가리키는 속담이 되었다."], ["See Proverbial Expressions.", "「속담 표현」을 보라."]], [["Calypso was a sea-nymph, which name denotes a numerous class of female divinities of lower rank, yet sharing many of the attributes of the gods.", "칼립소는 바다의 님프였는데, 이 이름은 신분이 낮으면서도 신들의 여러 속성을 함께 지닌, 수많은 여성 신격의 한 부류를 가리킨다."], ["Calypso received Ulysses hospitably, entertained him magnificently, became enamoured of him, and wished to retain him forever, conferring on him immortality.", "칼립소는 오디세우스를 후히 맞이하여 성대히 대접했고, 그에게 반하여 그에게 불사를 내려 주고 영원히 붙잡아 두기를 바랐다."], ["But he persisted in his resolution to return to his country and his wife and son.", "그러나 그는 자기 나라와 아내와 아들에게로 돌아가겠다는 결심을 굽히지 않았다."], ["Calypso at last received the command of Jove to dismiss him.", "마침내 칼립소는 그를 보내 주라는 제우스의 명령을 받았다."], ["Mercury brought the message to her, and found her in her grotto, which is thus described by Homer:", "헤르메스가 그 전갈을 그녀에게 가져왔고, 그녀가 자기 동굴에 있는 것을 발견했다. 그 동굴을 호메로스는 이렇게 묘사한다."]], [["“A garden vine, luxuriant on all sides, Mantled the spacious cavern, cluster-hung Profuse; four fountains of serenest lymph, Their sinuous course pursuing side by side, Strayed all around, and everywhere appeared Meadows of softest verdure, purpled o’er With violets; it was a scene to fill A god from heaven with wonder and delight.”", "\"정원의 포도 덩굴이 사방에 무성하게 그 너른 동굴을 뒤덮어, 송이송이 풍성하게 늘어졌고, 더없이 맑은 샘물 넷이 구불구불한 길을 나란히 따라 흐르며 온 사방으로 퍼져 나갔고, 어디에나 더없이 부드러운 초록빛 풀밭이 펼쳐져 제비꽃으로 보랏빛을 띠었으니, 그것은 하늘의 신마저도 놀라움과 기쁨으로 채울 만한 광경이었노라.\""]], [["Calypso with much reluctance proceeded to obey the commands of Jupiter.", "칼립소는 몹시 마지못해하면서 제우스의 명령에 따르기 시작했다."], ["She supplied Ulysses with the means of constructing a raft, provisioned it well for him, and gave him a favoring gale.", "그녀는 오디세우스에게 뗏목을 만들 수단을 마련해 주고, 식량을 넉넉히 실어 주었으며, 순풍을 보내 주었다."], ["He sped on his course prosperously for many days, till at length, when in sight of land, a storm arose that broke his mast, and threatened to rend the raft asunder.", "그는 여러 날 동안 순조롭게 항로를 나아갔으나, 마침내 육지가 보일 무렵 폭풍이 일어 그의 돛대를 부러뜨리고 뗏목을 산산이 찢어 놓을 듯 위협했다."], ["In this crisis he was seen by a compassionate sea-nymph, who in the form of a cormorant alighted on the raft, and presented him a girdle, directing him to bind it beneath his breast, and if he should be compelled to trust himself to the waves, it would buoy him up and enable him by swimming to reach the land.", "이 위기에 한 자비로운 바다의 님프가 그를 보고, 가마우지의 모습으로 뗏목에 내려앉아 그에게 띠를 건네주며, 그것을 가슴 아래에 두르라고, 그리고 만약 파도에 몸을 맡길 수밖에 없게 되더라도 그것이 그를 떠받쳐 헤엄쳐 육지에 닿을 수 있게 해 줄 것이라고 일러 주었다."]], [["Fenelon, in his romance of “Telemachus,” has given us the adventures of the son of Ulysses in search of his father.", "페늘롱은 그의 소설 「텔레마코스」에서 아버지를 찾아 나선 오디세우스의 아들의 모험을 우리에게 들려주었다."], ["Among other places at which he arrived, following on his father’s footsteps, was Calypso’s isle, and, as in the former case, the goddess tried every art to keep him with her, and offered to share her immortality with him.", "아버지의 발자취를 따라 그가 다다른 여러 곳 가운데 칼립소의 섬이 있었는데, 앞서와 마찬가지로 여신은 온갖 기술을 동원해 그를 곁에 붙잡아 두려 했고, 자신의 불사를 그와 나누어 갖겠다고 제안했다."], ["But Minerva, who in the shape of Mentor accompanied him and governed all his movements, made him repel her allurements, and when no other means of escape could be found, the two friends leaped from a cliff into the sea, and swam to a vessel which lay becalmed off shore.", "그러나 멘토르의 모습으로 그를 동행하며 그의 모든 행동을 인도하던 아테나[로마의 〈미네르바〉]가 그에게 그녀의 유혹을 물리치게 했고, 다른 탈출의 방도를 찾을 수 없게 되자 두 친구는 절벽에서 바다로 뛰어들어, 해안에서 바람이 잔잔해 멈춰 있던 배까지 헤엄쳐 갔다."], ["Byron alludes to this leap of Telemachus and Mentor in the following stanza:", "바이런은 텔레마코스와 멘토르의 이 도약을 다음 연에서 인유한다."]], [["“But not in silence pass Calypso’s isles, The sister tenants of the middle deep; There for the weary still a haven smiles, Though the fair goddess long has ceased to weep, And o’er her cliffs a fruitless watch to keep For him who dared prefer a mortal bride.", "\"그러나 칼립소의 섬들을 침묵 속에 지나치지 말라, 한가운데 바다의 자매처럼 깃든 그 거처들을. 그곳에는 지친 자들을 위해 아직도 항구가 미소 짓나니, 비록 아름다운 여신은 오래전에 울기를 그쳤고, 필멸의 신부를 감히 더 좋아한 그를 위해 제 절벽 위에서 헛된 망보기를 그쳤을지라도."], ["Here too his boy essayed the dreadful leap, Stern Mentor urged from high to yonder tide; While thus of both bereft the nymph-queen doubly sighed.”", "이곳에서 그의 아들도 그 무서운 도약을 시도했으니, 준엄한 멘토르가 높은 곳에서 저 아래 물결로 재촉했노라. 그리하여 둘을 다 잃은 님프 여왕은 갑절로 탄식했노라.\""]], [["————", "————"]], [["ULYSSES clung to the raft while any of its timbers kept together, and when it no longer yielded him support, binding the girdle around him, he swam.", "오디세우스는 뗏목의 목재가 그나마 붙어 있는 동안에는 거기에 매달려 있었으나, 더 이상 그것이 자신을 떠받쳐 주지 못하게 되자 허리띠를 몸에 동여매고는 헤엄을 쳤다."], ["Minerva smoothed the billows before him and sent him a wind that rolled the waves towards the shore.", "아테나[로마의 〈미네르바〉]가 그의 앞에 놓인 큰 물결을 잔잔하게 만들고, 파도를 해안 쪽으로 밀어 보내는 바람을 보내 주었다."], ["The surf beat high on the rocks and seemed to forbid approach; but at length finding calm water at the mouth of a gentle stream, he landed, spent with toil, breathless and speechless and almost dead.", "부서지는 파도가 바위에 높이 부딪치며 접근을 가로막는 듯했다. 그러나 마침내 그는 잔잔한 물이 흐르는 어느 온화한 시내의 어귀를 발견해 그곳에 상륙했는데, 고생에 지쳐 숨도 제대로 쉬지 못하고 말 한마디 내뱉지 못한 채 거의 죽은 것이나 다름없었다."], ["After some time, reviving, he kissed the soil, rejoicing, yet at a loss what course to take.", "얼마 후 기운을 회복한 그는 기뻐하며 땅에 입을 맞추었으나, 어떤 길로 나아가야 할지 막막하기만 했다."], ["At a short distance he perceived a wood, to which he turned his steps.", "가까운 곳에 숲이 보이기에 그는 그쪽으로 발걸음을 옮겼다."], ["There, finding a covert sheltered by intermingling branches alike from the sun and the rain, he collected a pile of leaves and formed a bed, on which he stretched himself, and heaping the leaves over him, fell asleep.", "그곳에서 그는 햇빛과 빗물을 함께 막아 줄 만큼 가지가 서로 얽혀 가려진 은신처를 찾아내고, 나뭇잎을 한 무더기 모아 잠자리를 만든 다음, 그 위에 몸을 뻗고 누워 나뭇잎을 자신의 몸 위로 끌어 덮고는 잠이 들었다."]], [["The land where he was thrown was Scheria, the country of the Phæacians.", "그가 떠밀려 온 땅은 스케리아, 곧 파이아케스족의 나라였다."], ["These people dwelt originally near the Cyclopes; but being oppressed by that savage race, they migrated to the isle of Scheria, under the conduct of Nausithoüs, their king.", "이 사람들은 본래 키클로페스 근처에 살고 있었으나, 그 야만적인 종족에게 시달리다 못해 그들의 왕 나우시토오스의 인솔 아래 스케리아 섬으로 이주해 온 것이었다."], ["They were, the poet tells us, a people akin to the gods, who appeared manifestly and feasted among them when they offered sacrifices, and did not conceal themselves from solitary wayfarers when they met them.", "시인이 우리에게 전하는 바에 따르면, 그들은 신들과 가까운 종족이어서, 그들이 제물을 바칠 때면 신들이 모습을 분명히 드러내고 그들 사이에서 함께 잔치를 벌였으며, 홀로 길을 가는 나그네와 마주칠 때에도 자신들의 모습을 숨기지 않았다고 한다."], ["They had abundance of wealth and lived in the enjoyment of it undisturbed by the alarms of war, for as they dwelt remote from gain-seeking man, no enemy ever approached their shores, and they did not even require to make use of bows and quivers.", "그들은 풍부한 재물을 지니고 있었고, 전쟁의 위협에 시달리는 일 없이 그것을 누리며 살았다. 이익을 좇는 인간들로부터 멀리 떨어져 살았던 까닭에 그 어떤 적도 그들의 해안에 다가오는 일이 없었고, 따라서 그들은 활과 화살통조차 쓸 필요가 없었다."], ["Their chief employment was navigation.", "그들의 주된 일거리는 항해였다."], ["Their ships, which went with the velocity of birds, were endued with intelligence; they knew every port and needed no pilot.", "새와 같은 속도로 나아가는 그들의 배는 지성을 갖추고 있었으니, 모든 항구를 알았고 키잡이도 필요로 하지 않았다."], ["Alcinoüs, the son of Nausithoüs, was now their king, a wise and just sovereign, beloved by his people.", "나우시토오스의 아들 알키노오스가 이제 그들의 왕이었으며, 그는 슬기롭고 공정한 군주로서 백성에게 사랑을 받고 있었다."]], [["Now it happened that the very night on which Ulysses was cast ashore on the Phæacian island, and while he lay sleeping on his bed of leaves, Nausicaa, the daughter of the king, had a dream sent by Minerva, reminding her that her wedding-day was not far distant, and that it would be but a prudent preparation for that event to have a general washing of the clothes of the family.", "그런데 오디세우스가 파이아케스족의 섬에 떠밀려 와 나뭇잎으로 만든 잠자리에 누워 잠들어 있던 바로 그날 밤, 왕의 딸 나우시카가 아테나가 보낸 꿈을 꾸었다. 그 꿈은 그녀의 혼인날이 머지않았으니 그날을 대비해 집안의 옷가지를 모두 빨아 두는 것이야말로 사려 깊은 준비가 될 것이라고 일깨워 주었다."], ["This was no slight affair, for the fountains were at some distance, and the garments must be carried thither.", "이는 결코 사소한 일이 아니었으니, 샘이 다소 멀리 떨어져 있어 옷가지를 그곳까지 운반해야 했기 때문이다."], ["On awaking, the princess hastened to her parents to tell them what was on her mind; not alluding to her wedding-day, but finding other reasons equally good.", "잠에서 깨어난 공주는 자기 마음에 있는 바를 알리고자 서둘러 부모에게로 갔는데, 혼인날에 대해서는 언급하지 않고 그에 못지않게 그럴듯한 다른 이유들을 내세웠다."], ["Her father readily assented and ordered the grooms to furnish forth a wagon for the purpose.", "그녀의 아버지는 흔쾌히 동의하며 그 일에 쓸 수레를 마련하라고 마부들에게 명했다."], ["The clothes were put therein, and the queen mother placed in the wagon, likewise, an abundant supply of food and wine.", "옷가지를 수레에 실었고, 왕비인 어머니는 그 수레에 음식과 포도주도 넉넉히 실어 주었다."], ["The princess took her seat and plied the lash, her attendant virgins following her on foot.", "공주는 자리에 앉아 채찍을 휘둘렀고, 시중드는 처녀들이 걸어서 그 뒤를 따랐다."], ["Arrived at the river-side, they turned out the mules to graze, and unlading the carriage, bore the garments down to the water, and working with cheerfulness and alacrity soon despatched their labor.", "강가에 다다른 그들은 노새들을 풀어 풀을 뜯게 하고, 수레의 짐을 내려 옷가지를 물가로 가져갔으며, 명랑하고 민첩하게 일하여 곧 그 노고를 마쳤다."], ["Then having spread the garments on the shore to dry, and having themselves bathed, they sat down to enjoy their meal; after which they rose and amused themselves with a game of ball, the princess singing to them while they played.", "그런 다음 옷을 해안에 펼쳐 말리고 자신들도 목욕을 한 뒤 둘러앉아 식사를 즐겼다. 식사가 끝나자 그들은 일어나 공놀이를 하며 즐겼는데, 그들이 노는 동안 공주가 노래를 불러 주었다."], ["But when they had refolded the apparel and were about to resume their way to the town, Minerva caused the ball thrown by the princess to fall into the water, whereat they all screamed and Ulysses awaked at the sound.", "그러나 그들이 옷을 다시 개어 마을로 돌아갈 채비를 하던 차에, 아테나가 공주가 던진 공을 물속에 빠지게 만들었고, 그러자 모두가 비명을 질렀으며 오디세우스는 그 소리에 잠에서 깨어났다."]], [["Now we must picture to ourselves Ulysses, a shipwrecked mariner, but a few hours escaped from the waves, and utterly destitute of clothing, awaking and discovering that only a few bushes were interposed between him and a group of young maidens whom, by their deportment and attire, he discovered to be not mere peasant girls, but of a higher class.", "이제 우리는 오디세우스의 모습을 떠올려 보아야 한다. 그는 난파를 당한 뱃사람으로, 불과 몇 시간 전에야 파도에서 벗어났을 뿐 옷이라고는 전혀 걸치지 못한 처지였는데, 잠에서 깨어나 보니 자신과 한 무리의 젊은 처녀들 사이에 고작 덤불 몇 그루만이 가로놓여 있을 따름이었다."], ["Sadly needing help, how could he yet venture, naked as he was, to discover himself and make his wants known?", "더구나 그는 그녀들의 거동과 차림새로 보아 단순한 시골 처녀들이 아니라 더 높은 신분의 여인들임을 알아차렸다."], ["It certainly was a case worthy of the interposition of his patron goddess Minerva, who never failed him at a crisis.", "절실히 도움이 필요한 처지였으나, 알몸인 그가 어찌 감히 자신의 모습을 드러내고 자신의 곤궁함을 알릴 수 있었겠는가?"], ["Breaking off a leafy branch from a tree, he held it before him and stepped out from the thicket.", "이는 분명 그의 수호 여신인 아테나가 개입할 만한 상황이었고, 그녀는 위기의 순간에 결코 그를 저버린 적이 없었다."], ["The virgins at sight of him fled in all directions, Nausicaa alone excepted, for her Minerva aided and endowed with courage and discernment.", "그는 나무에서 잎이 무성한 가지 하나를 꺾어 자기 앞을 가리고는 덤불에서 걸어 나왔다. 그를 본 처녀들은 사방으로 달아났으나, 나우시카만은 예외였으니, 아테나가 바로 그녀를 도와 용기와 분별력을 불어넣어 주었기 때문이다."], ["Ulysses, standing respectfully aloof, told his sad case, and besought the fair object (whether queen or goddess he professed he knew not) for food and clothing.", "오디세우스는 정중하게 멀찍이 떨어져 서서 자신의 가련한 처지를 이야기하고, 그 아름다운 여인에게(그녀가 왕비인지 여신인지 자신은 알지 못하겠노라고 그는 고백했다) 음식과 옷을 베풀어 달라고 간청했다."], ["The princess replied courteously, promising present relief and her father’s hospitality when he should become acquainted with the facts.", "공주는 정중하게 대답하며, 당장의 구호를 약속하고 또 사정을 알게 되면 자기 아버지가 손님을 후하게 대접하리라고 약속했다."], ["She called back her scattered maidens, chiding their alarm, and reminding them that the Phæacians had no enemies to fear.", "그녀는 흩어진 시녀들을 다시 불러 모아 그들의 놀란 모습을 나무라며, 파이아케스족에게는 두려워할 만한 적이 없음을 일깨워 주었다."], ["This man, she told them, was an unhappy wanderer, whom it was a duty to cherish, for the poor and stranger are from Jove.", "그녀는 그들에게 이르기를, 이 사람은 불행한 방랑자이니 마땅히 보살펴 주어야 하며, 가난한 자와 나그네는 제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]께서 보내신 이들이라고 했다."], ["She bade them bring food and clothing, for some of her brother’s garments were among the contents of the wagon.", "그녀는 그들에게 음식과 옷을 가져오게 했으니, 수레에 실린 물건들 가운데에는 그녀 오라비의 옷가지도 들어 있었기 때문이다."], ["When this was done, and Ulysses, retiring to a sheltered place, had washed his body free from the sea-foam, clothed and refreshed himself with food, Pallas dilated his form and diffused grace over his ample chest and manly brows.", "이렇게 일이 끝나고, 오디세우스가 가려진 곳으로 물러가 자기 몸에서 바닷물 거품을 씻어 내고 옷을 입고 음식으로 기운을 차리자, 팔라스[아테나]가 그의 몸을 더욱 크게 만들고 그의 넓은 가슴과 사내다운 이마에 우아함을 흘려 넣어 주었다."]], [["The princess, seeing him, was filled with admiration, and scrupled not to say to her damsels that she wished the gods would sent her such a husband.", "그를 본 공주는 감탄으로 가득 차, 신들께서 자신에게 이런 남편을 보내 주셨으면 좋겠다고 시녀들에게 거리낌 없이 말했다."], ["To Ulysses she recommended that he should repair to the city, following herself and train so far as the way lay through the fields; but when they should approach the city she desired that he would no longer be seen in her company, for she feared the remarks which rude and vulgar people might make on seeing her return accompanied by such a gallant stranger.", "그녀는 오디세우스에게 도성으로 가라고 권하며, 들판을 지나는 길까지는 자신과 그 일행을 따라오라고 했다. 그러나 도성에 가까워지면 더 이상 자기와 동행하는 모습을 보이지 말아 달라고 했으니, 자신이 그토록 늠름한 낯선 사내를 데리고 돌아오는 것을 보고 무례하고 천박한 자들이 입에 올릴지도 모를 말을 두려워했기 때문이다."], ["To avoid which she directed him to stop at a grove adjoining the city, in which were a farm and garden belonging to the king.", "이를 피하기 위해 그녀는 그에게 도성에 인접한 작은 숲에서 멈추라고 일러 주었는데, 그곳에는 왕에게 속한 농장과 정원이 있었다."], ["After allowing time for the princess and her companions to reach the city, he was then to pursue his way thither, and would be easily guided by any he might meet to the royal abode.", "공주와 그 동행들이 도성에 다다를 만한 시간을 둔 다음, 그가 그쪽으로 길을 나서면, 마주치는 누구에게든 쉽게 안내를 받아 왕의 거처에 이를 수 있으리라고 했다."]], [["Ulysses obeyed the directions and in due time proceeded to the city, on approaching which he met a young woman bearing a pitcher forth for water.", "오디세우스는 그 지시에 따랐고, 때가 되자 도성으로 나아갔다. 도성에 가까워졌을 때 그는 물을 길으러 항아리를 들고 나오는 젊은 여인을 만났다."], ["It was Minerva, who had assumed that form.", "그 여인은 바로 그 모습으로 변장한 아테나였다."], ["Ulysses accosted her and desired to be directed to the palace of Alcinoüs the king.", "오디세우스는 그녀에게 말을 걸어 알키노오스 왕의 궁전으로 가는 길을 안내해 달라고 청했다."], ["The maiden replied respectfully, offering to be his guide; for the palace, she informed him, stood near her father’s dwelling.", "처녀는 정중하게 대답하며 그의 안내자가 되어 주겠다고 했으니, 그녀가 그에게 일러 준 바에 따르면 궁전은 자기 아버지의 거처 가까이에 있었다."], ["Under the guidance of the goddess, and by her power enveloped in a cloud which shielded him from observation, Ulysses passed among the busy crowd, and with wonder observed their harbor, their ships, their forum (the resort of heroes), and their battlements, till they came to the palace, where the goddess, having first given him some information of the country, king, and people he was about to meet, left him.", "여신의 인도를 받으며, 또한 그녀의 힘으로 남의 눈에 띄지 않게 가려 주는 구름에 싸인 채, 오디세우스는 분주한 군중 사이를 지나갔고, 그들의 항구와 배들, 그들의 광장(영웅들이 모여드는 곳)과 그들의 성벽을 경탄하며 바라보다가 마침내 궁전에 이르렀다. 그곳에서 여신은 그가 곧 만나게 될 나라와 왕과 백성에 관해 몇 가지를 먼저 일러 준 뒤 그를 떠났다."], ["Ulysses, before entering the courtyard of the palace, stood and surveyed the scene.", "오디세우스는 궁전의 안뜰로 들어서기 전에 멈추어 서서 그 광경을 둘러보았다."], ["Its splendor astonished him.", "그 화려함이 그를 놀라게 했다."], ["Brazen walls stretched from the entrance to the interior house, of which the doors were gold, the doorposts silver, the lintels silver ornamented with gold.", "청동으로 된 벽이 입구에서부터 안채에 이르기까지 뻗어 있었는데, 그 문은 황금이었고 문기둥은 은이었으며, 상인방은 은에 황금으로 장식되어 있었다."], ["On either side were figures of mastiffs wrought in gold and silver, standing in rows as if to guard the approach.", "양쪽으로는 황금과 은으로 빚어진 마스티프 개들의 형상이, 마치 그 길목을 지키는 듯 줄지어 서 있었다."], ["Along the walls were seats spread through all their length with mantles of finest texture, the work of Phæacian maidens.", "벽을 따라서는 그 길이 내내 자리들이 놓여 있었고, 그 위에는 파이아케스족 처녀들이 짠 가장 고운 천으로 된 덮개들이 펼쳐져 있었다."], ["On these seats the princes sat and feasted, while golden statues of graceful youths held in their hands lighted torches which shed radiance over the scene.", "이 자리에 왕족들이 앉아 잔치를 벌였으며, 우아한 젊은이들을 본뜬 황금 조각상들이 손에 불 켜진 횃불을 들고 그 광경 위로 빛을 비추었다."], ["Full fifty female menials served in household offices, some employed to grind the corn, others to wind off the purple wool or ply the loom.", "무려 쉰 명에 이르는 여종들이 집안일을 돌보고 있었으니, 더러는 곡식을 빻고 더러는 자줏빛 양털을 감거나 베틀을 부지런히 놀렸다."], ["For the Phæacian women as far exceeded all other women in household arts as the mariners of that country did the rest of mankind in the management of ships.", "그도 그럴 것이 파이아케스족의 여인들이 집안일 솜씨에서 다른 모든 여인을 능가하는 정도는, 그 나라의 뱃사람들이 배를 다루는 솜씨에서 나머지 인류를 능가하는 정도와 같았기 때문이다."], ["Without the court a spacious garden lay, four acres in extent.", "안뜰 밖으로는 넉넉한 정원이 펼쳐져 있었는데, 그 넓이가 네 에이커에 달했다."], ["In it grew many a lofty tree, pomegranate, pear, apple, fig, and olive.", "그 안에는 석류나무, 배나무, 사과나무, 무화과나무, 올리브나무 등 키 큰 나무가 숱하게 자라고 있었다."], ["Neither winter’s cold nor summer’s drought arrested their growth, but they flourished in constant succession, some budding while others were maturing.", "겨울의 추위도 여름의 가뭄도 그 성장을 막지 못하여, 어떤 것은 싹을 틔우는 동안 다른 것은 무르익으면서 끊임없이 잇따라 무성하게 자라났다."], ["The vineyard was equally prolific.", "포도밭도 그에 못지않게 풍요로웠다."], ["In one quarter you might see the vines, some in blossom, some loaded with ripe grapes, and in another observe the vintagers treading the wine press.", "한쪽에서는 어떤 포도덩굴은 꽃을 피우고 어떤 것은 잘 익은 포도를 잔뜩 매달고 있는 것을 볼 수 있었고, 다른 쪽에서는 포도 따는 이들이 포도즙 짜는 틀을 밟고 있는 것을 볼 수 있었다."], ["On the garden’s borders flowers of all hues bloomed all the year round, arranged with neatest art.", "정원의 가장자리에는 온갖 빛깔의 꽃들이 더없이 정교한 솜씨로 배치되어 한 해 내내 피어 있었다."], ["In the midst two fountains poured forth their waters, one flowing by artificial channels over all the garden, the other conducted through the courtyard of the palace, whence every citizen might draw his supplies.", "그 한가운데에서는 두 개의 샘이 물을 쏟아 내고 있었는데, 하나는 인공 수로를 통해 정원 곳곳으로 흘렀고, 다른 하나는 궁전의 안뜰을 가로질러 인도되어 그곳에서 모든 시민이 물을 길어 갈 수 있었다."]], [["Ulysses stood gazing in admiration, unobserved himself, for the cloud which Minerva spread around him still shielded him.", "오디세우스는 자신은 남의 눈에 띄지 않은 채 감탄하며 서서 바라보고 있었으니, 아테나가 그의 둘레에 펼쳐 놓은 구름이 여전히 그를 가려 주고 있었기 때문이다."], ["At length, having sufficiently observed the scene, he advanced with rapid step into the hall where the chiefs and senators were assembled, pouring libation to Mercury, whose worship followed the evening meal.", "마침내 그 광경을 충분히 살펴본 그는 빠른 걸음으로 영주들과 원로들이 모여 있는 큰 방으로 나아갔는데, 그들은 저녁 식사 뒤에 이어지던 헤르메스[로마의 〈메르쿠리우스〉] 숭배에 따라 그 신에게 헌주를 따르고 있었다."], ["Just then Minerva dissolved the cloud and disclosed him to the assembled chiefs.", "바로 그때 아테나가 구름을 흩어 버려 그를 모여 있는 영주들 앞에 드러냈다."], ["Advancing to the place where the queen sat, he knelt at her feet and implored her favor and assistance to enable him to return to his native country.", "왕비가 앉아 있는 자리로 나아간 그는 그녀의 발치에 무릎을 꿇고, 자신이 고국으로 돌아갈 수 있도록 호의와 도움을 베풀어 달라고 간청했다."], ["Then withdrawing, he seated himself in the manner of suppliants, at the hearth side.", "그러고는 물러나 탄원하는 자의 방식대로 화롯가에 앉았다."]], [["For a time none spoke.", "한동안 아무도 입을 열지 않았다."], ["At last an aged statesman, addressing the king, said, “It is not fit that a stranger who asks our hospitality should be kept waiting in suppliant guise, none welcoming him.", "마침내 한 나이 든 정치가가 왕에게 말했다. \"우리의 환대를 청하는 나그네를, 아무도 맞이하지 않은 채 탄원하는 모습 그대로 기다리게 하는 것은 옳지 않습니다."], ["Let him therefore be led to a seat among us and supplied with food and wine.”", "그러니 그를 우리 사이의 한 자리로 이끌고 음식과 포도주를 베풀어 주시지요.\""], ["At these words the king rising gave his hand to Ulysses and led him to a seat, displacing thence his own son to make room for the stranger.", "이 말에 왕은 일어나 오디세우스에게 손을 내밀어 한 자리로 이끌었는데, 이 낯선 이에게 자리를 내주고자 자기 아들을 그곳에서 옮기게 했다."], ["Food and wine were set before him and he ate and refreshed himself.", "음식과 포도주가 그의 앞에 차려졌고, 그는 그것을 먹으며 기운을 차렸다."]], [["The king then dismissed his guests, notifying them that the next day he would call them to council to consider what had best be done for the stranger.", "이윽고 왕은 손님들을 돌려보내며, 이튿날 이 낯선 이를 위해 무엇을 하는 것이 가장 좋을지 의논하고자 그들을 회의에 부르겠노라고 알렸다."]], [["When the guests had departed and Ulysses was left alone with the king and queen, the queen asked him who he was and whence he came, and (recognizing the clothes which he wore as those which her maidens and herself had made) from whom he received those garments.", "손님들이 떠나고 오디세우스가 왕과 왕비와 더불어 단둘이 남게 되자, 왕비는 그에게 그가 누구이며 어디서 왔는지를 물었고, 또한 (그가 입고 있는 옷이 자기 시녀들과 자신이 손수 만든 것임을 알아보고는) 누구에게서 그 옷가지를 받았는지를 물었다."], ["He told them of his residence in Calypso’s isle and his departure thence; of the wreck of his raft, his escape by swimming, and of the relief afforded by the princess.", "그는 칼립소의 섬에서 머문 일과 그곳을 떠나온 일, 자기 뗏목이 부서진 일, 헤엄쳐서 살아난 일, 그리고 공주가 베풀어 준 구원에 대해 이야기했다."], ["The parents heard approvingly, and the king promised to furnish a ship in which his guest might return to his own land.", "부모는 흡족하게 그 이야기를 들었고, 왕은 손님이 자기 나라로 돌아갈 수 있도록 배 한 척을 마련해 주겠노라고 약속했다."]], [["The next day the assembled chiefs confirmed the promise of the king.", "이튿날 모여든 영주들은 왕의 약속을 확정해 주었다."], ["A bark was prepared and a crew of stout rowers selected, and all betook themselves to the palace, where a bounteous repast was provided.", "작은 배 한 척이 준비되고 건장한 노잡이들로 선원이 선발되었으며, 모두가 궁전으로 발길을 옮겨 그곳에서 풍성한 잔치가 베풀어졌다."], ["After the feast the king proposed that the young men should show their guest their proficiency in manly sports, and all went forth to the arena for games of running, wrestling, and other exercises.", "잔치가 끝나자 왕은 젊은이들이 손님에게 사내다운 운동에서의 능숙한 솜씨를 보여 주자고 제안했고, 모두가 달리기와 씨름을 비롯한 여러 경기를 위해 경기장으로 나아갔다."], ["After all had done their best, Ulysses being challenged to show what he could do, at first declined, but being taunted by one of the youths, seized a quoit of weight far heavier than any of the Phæacians had thrown, and sent it farther than the utmost throw of theirs.", "모두가 자기 솜씨를 다 보인 뒤 오디세우스도 자신이 할 수 있는 바를 보이라는 요청을 받았는데, 처음에는 사양했으나 젊은이들 가운데 한 사람에게서 조롱을 받자 파이아케스족 누구도 던진 적이 없을 만큼 훨씬 무거운 원반을 집어 들어 그들의 가장 먼 던지기보다 더 멀리 날려 보냈다."], ["All were astonished, and viewed their guest with greatly increased respect.", "모두가 놀라며 한층 더 커진 존경의 눈으로 손님을 바라보았다."]], [["After the games they returned to the hall, and the herald led in Demodocus, the blind bard,—", "경기가 끝나자 그들은 큰 방으로 돌아왔고, 전령이 눈먼 음유시인 데모도코스를 인도해 들였다."]], [["“. . . Dear to the Muse, Who yet appointed him both good and ill, Took from him sight, but gave him strains divine.”", "\"······무사 여신들에게 사랑받는 이여, 여신은 그에게 좋은 것과 나쁜 것을 함께 정해 주어, 그에게서 시력은 앗아 갔으나 신성한 가락은 베풀어 주었네.\""]], [["He took for his theme the “Wooden Horse,” by means of which the Greeks found entrance into Troy.", "그는 그리스인들이 트로이아 안으로 들어가는 길을 찾아내게 해 준 〈목마〉를 자기 노래의 주제로 삼았다."], ["Apollo inspired him, and he sang so feelingly the terrors and the exploits of that eventful time that all were delighted, but Ulysses was moved to tears.", "아폴론이 그에게 영감을 불어넣어 주었고, 그가 그 파란만장했던 시절의 공포와 위업을 어찌나 절절하게 노래했던지 모두가 기뻐했으나, 오디세우스는 눈물을 흘릴 만큼 마음이 움직였다."], ["Observing which, Alcinoüs, when the song was done, demanded of him why at the mention of Troy his sorrows awaked.", "이를 알아챈 알키노오스는 노래가 끝나자 그에게, 어찌하여 트로이아라는 말을 듣고 슬픔이 깨어났는지를 물었다."], ["Had he lost there a father, or brother, or any dear friend?", "그가 거기서 아버지나 형제, 혹은 어느 소중한 친구를 잃기라도 했단 말인가?"], ["Ulysses replied by announcing himself by his true name, and at their request, recounted the adventures which had befallen him since his departure from Troy.", "오디세우스는 자신의 진짜 이름을 밝혀 자기가 누구인지 알리는 것으로 대답했고, 그들의 요청에 따라 트로이아를 떠난 이후 자신에게 일어난 모험들을 낱낱이 이야기했다."], ["This narrative raised the sympathy and admiration of the Phæacians for their guest to the highest pitch.", "이 이야기는 손님을 향한 파이아케스족의 공감과 감탄을 더없이 높이 끌어올렸다."], ["The king proposed that all the chiefs should present him with a gift, himself setting the example.", "왕은 모든 영주가 그에게 선물을 바치자고 제안하며 몸소 그 본을 보였다."], ["They obeyed, and vied with one another in loading the illustrious stranger with costly gifts.", "그들은 그 말을 따라 서로 다투어 그 이름난 낯선 이에게 값진 선물을 가득 안겨 주었다."]], [["The next day Ulysses set sail in the Phæacian vessel, and in a short time arrived safe at Ithaca, his own island.", "이튿날 오디세우스는 파이아케스족의 배에 올라 항해에 나섰고, 얼마 지나지 않아 무사히 자기 섬인 이타케에 다다랐다."], ["When the vessel touched the strand he was asleep.", "배가 물가에 닿았을 때 그는 잠들어 있었다."], ["The mariners, without waking him, carried him on shore, and landed with him the chest containing his presents, and then sailed away.", "뱃사람들은 그를 깨우지 않고 그를 뭍으로 옮겨 그가 받은 선물이 든 궤짝과 함께 그를 해안에 내려놓은 다음 배를 저어 떠나갔다."]], [["Neptune was so displeased at the conduct of the Phæacians in thus rescuing Ulysses from his hands that on the return of the vessel to port he transformed it into a rock, right opposite the mouth of the harbor.", "포세이돈[로마의 〈넵투누스〉]은 파이아케스족이 이렇듯 오디세우스를 자기 손아귀에서 구해 낸 처사에 몹시 노하여, 그 배가 항구로 돌아오자 항구 어귀 바로 맞은편에서 그것을 바위로 둔갑시켜 버렸다."]], [["Homer’s description of the ships of the Phæacians has been thought to look like an anticipation of the wonders of modern steam navigation.", "파이아케스족의 배에 대한 호메로스의 묘사는 근대 증기 항해의 경이를 미리 내다본 것처럼 여겨져 왔다."], ["Alcinoüs says to Ulysses:", "알키노오스는 오디세우스에게 이렇게 말한다."]], [["“Say from what city, from what regions tossed, And what inhabitants those regions boast?", "\"말하라, 그대가 어느 도성에서, 어느 고장에서 떠밀려 왔는지, 또 그 고장에는 어떤 거민들이 자랑스레 살고 있는지를."], ["So shalt thou quickly reach the realm assigned, In wondrous ships, self-moved, instinct with mind; No helm secures their course, no pilot guides; Like man intelligent they plough the tides, Conscious of every coast and every bay That lies beneath the sun’s all-seeing ray.”", "그리하면 그대는 머지않아 정해진 나라에 가닿으리라, 스스로 움직이며 마음을 깃들인 경이로운 배를 타고서. 그 배는 어떤 키도 그 항로를 잡아 주지 않고, 어떤 키잡이도 이끌지 않으나, 지각 있는 사람처럼 물결을 가르며 나아가, 해의 모든 것을 살피는 빛살 아래 펼쳐진 모든 해안과 모든 만을 낱낱이 알고 있노라.\""], ["—Odyssey, Book VIII.", "─〈오디세이아〉, 제8권."]], [["Lord Carlisle, in his “Diary in the Turkish and Greek Waters,” thus speaks of Corfu, which he considers to be the ancient Phæacian island:", "칼라일 경은 그의 「터키 및 그리스 해역 일기」에서, 자신이 고대 파이아케스족의 섬으로 여기는 코르푸에 대해 다음과 같이 말한다."]], [["“The sites explain the ‘Odyssey.’ The temple of the sea-god could not have been more fitly placed, upon a grassy platform of the most elastic turf, on the brow of a crag commanding harbor, and channel, and ocean.", "\"그곳의 지형들은 〈오디세이아〉를 설명해 준다. 바다의 신의 신전이, 항구와 해협과 대양을 굽어보는 벼랑 끝, 더없이 탄력 있는 잔디가 깔린 풀밭의 단(壇) 위에 자리한 것보다 더 어울리게 놓일 수는 없었으리라."], ["Just at the entrance of the inner harbor there is a picturesque rock with a small convent perched upon it, which by one legend is the transformed pinnace of Ulysses.", "안쪽 항구의 어귀 바로 그 자리에는 그림 같은 바위 하나가 있고 그 위에 작은 수도원이 얹혀 있는데, 어느 전설에 따르면 이것이 바로 둔갑한 오디세우스의 거룻배라고 한다."]], [["“Almost the only river in the island is just at the proper distance from the probable site of the city and palace of the king, to justify the princess Nausicaa having had resort to her chariot and to luncheon when she went with the maidens of the court to wash their garments.”", "\"이 섬에서 거의 유일한 강은, 왕의 도성과 궁전이 있었으리라 짐작되는 자리로부터 꼭 알맞은 거리에 놓여 있어, 나우시카 공주가 궁정의 처녀들과 함께 옷을 빨러 갈 때에 수레를 동원하고 점심을 챙겨 갔던 일을 충분히 납득하게 해 준다.\""]], [["# FATE OF THE SUITORS", "# FATE OF THE SUITORS"]], [["Ulysses had now been away from Ithaca for twenty years, and when he awoke he did not recognize his native land.", "오디세우스는 이제 이타케를 떠나 있은 지 스무 해가 되었으므로, 잠에서 깨어났을 때 그는 자기 고국 땅을 알아보지 못했다."], ["Minerva appeared to him in the form of a young shepherd, informed him where he was, and told him the state of things at his palace.", "아테나가 젊은 목동의 모습으로 그에게 나타나 그가 어디에 있는지를 일러 주고, 그의 궁전에서 벌어지고 있는 사정을 들려주었다."], ["More than a hundred nobles of Ithaca and of the neighboring islands had been for years suing for the hand of Penelope, his wife, imagining him dead, and lording it over his palace and people, as if they were owners of both.", "이타케와 이웃 섬들의 귀족 백 명이 넘는 자들이, 그가 죽었으리라 짐작하고는 여러 해 동안 그의 아내 페넬로페의 손길을 구해 왔으며, 마치 자기들이 궁전과 백성 둘 다의 주인이라도 되는 양 그것들 위에 군림하고 있다는 것이었다."], ["That he might be able to take vengeance upon them, it was important that he should not be recognized.", "그가 그들에게 복수할 수 있으려면, 자신의 정체를 들키지 않는 것이 중요했다."], ["Minerva accordingly metamorphosed him into an unsightly beggar, and as such he was kindly received by Eumæus, the swine-herd, a faithful servant of his house.", "그리하여 아테나는 그를 흉한 거지로 둔갑시켰고, 그런 모습으로 그는 자기 집안의 충직한 종인 돼지치기 에우마이오스에게 친절히 맞아들여졌다."]], [["Telemachus, his son, was absent in quest of his father.", "그의 아들 텔레마코스는 아버지를 찾아 떠나 있어 자리에 없었다."], ["He had gone to the courts of the other kings, who had returned from the Trojan expedition.", "그는 트로이아 원정에서 돌아온 다른 왕들의 궁정으로 가 있었다."], ["While on the search, he received counsel from Minerva to return home.", "찾아 헤매던 중에 그는 아테나에게서 집으로 돌아가라는 조언을 받았다."], ["He arrived and sought Eumæus to learn something of the state of affairs at the palace before presenting himself among the suitors.", "그는 돌아오자 구혼자들 사이에 모습을 드러내기에 앞서 궁전의 사정을 좀 알아보고자 에우마이오스를 찾아갔다."], ["Finding a stranger with Eumæus, he treated him courteously, though in the garb of a beggar, and promised him assistance.", "에우마이오스의 곁에 낯선 이가 있는 것을 보고, 그는 비록 거지의 행색을 하고 있었음에도 정중하게 그를 대하며 도움을 약속했다."], ["Eumæus was sent to the palace to inform Penelope privately of her son’s arrival, for caution was necessary with regard to the suitors, who, as Telemachus had learned, were plotting to intercept and kill him.", "에우마이오스는 페넬로페에게 그녀의 아들이 돌아왔음을 은밀히 알리고자 궁전으로 보내졌으니, 구혼자들에 관한 한 신중을 기할 필요가 있었기 때문이다. 텔레마코스가 알아낸 바에 따르면 그들은 그를 가로막아 죽이려는 음모를 꾸미고 있었던 것이다."], ["When Eumæus was gone, Minerva presented herself to Ulysses, and directed him to make himself known to his son.", "에우마이오스가 떠나자 아테나가 오디세우스 앞에 모습을 드러내어 그에게 아들에게 정체를 밝히라고 일렀다."], ["At the same time she touched him, removed at once from him the appearance of age and penury, and gave him the aspect of vigorous manhood that belonged to him.", "동시에 그녀는 그를 어루만져 단번에 그에게서 늙고 궁핍한 모습을 거두어 내고, 본래 그에게 속해 있던 정정한 장년의 풍모를 되돌려 주었다."], ["Telemachus viewed him with astonishment, and at first thought he must be more than mortal.", "텔레마코스는 놀라움에 차서 그를 바라보았고, 처음에는 그가 필멸의 인간 이상의 존재임이 틀림없다고 생각했다."], ["But Ulysses announced himself as his father, and accounted for the change of appearance by explaining that it was Minerva’s doing.", "그러나 오디세우스는 자신이 그의 아버지임을 밝히고, 모습이 바뀐 것은 아테나가 한 일이라고 설명했다."]], [["“. . . Then threw Telemachus His arms around his father’s neck and wept. Desire intense of lamentation seized On both; soft murmurs uttering, each indulged His grief.”", "\"······그러자 텔레마코스는 두 팔로 아버지의 목을 끌어안고 울었다. 북받치는 비탄의 욕망이 둘 모두를 사로잡으니, 나직한 흐느낌을 내뱉으며 저마다 제 슬픔에 몸을 맡겼다.\""]], [["The father and son took counsel together how they should get the better of the suitors and punish them for their outrages.", "아버지와 아들은 어떻게 하면 구혼자들을 제압하고 그들의 횡포를 벌할 수 있을지를 함께 의논했다."], ["It was arranged that Telemachus should proceed to the palace and mingle with the suitors as formerly; that Ulysses should also go as a beggar, a character which in the rude old times had different privileges from what we concede to it now.", "텔레마코스는 궁전으로 나아가 예전처럼 구혼자들 사이에 섞이고, 오디세우스 또한 거지의 행색으로 가기로 정해졌으니, 거지라는 신분은 거칠던 옛 시절에는 오늘날 우리가 그것에 부여하는 것과는 다른 권리를 누렸다."], ["As traveller and storyteller, the beggar was admitted in the halls of chieftains, and often treated like a guest; though sometimes, also, no doubt, with contumely.", "나그네이자 이야기꾼으로서 거지는 영주들의 큰 방에 받아들여졌고, 흔히 손님처럼 대접받기도 했다. 물론 때로는 모욕을 받는 일도 의심할 여지 없이 있기는 했다."], ["Ulysses charged his son not to betray, by any display of unusual interest in him, that he knew him to be other than he seemed, and even if he saw him insulted, or beaten, not to interpose otherwise than he might do for any stranger.", "오디세우스는 아들에게, 자신에 대한 어떤 별난 관심도 내비쳐서 그가 겉모습과는 다른 사람임을 알고 있다는 사실을 드러내지 말라고 단단히 일렀으며, 설령 자신이 모욕을 당하거나 매를 맞는 것을 보더라도 여느 낯선 이를 위해 나설 만한 정도 이상으로는 끼어들지 말라고 했다."], ["At the palace they found the usual scene of feasting and riot going on.", "궁전에 다다른 그들은 잔치와 소란이 여느 때처럼 벌어지고 있는 광경을 보았다."], ["The suitors pretended to receive Telemachus with joy at his return, though secretly mortified at the failure of their plots to take his life.", "구혼자들은 텔레마코스가 돌아온 것을 기쁘게 맞이하는 체했으나, 그의 목숨을 빼앗으려던 음모가 실패한 데 속으로는 분해하고 있었다."], ["The old beggar was permitted to enter, and provided with a portion from the table.", "늙은 거지는 안으로 들어오는 것이 허락되었고, 식탁에서 한몫의 음식을 받았다."], ["A touching incident occurred as Ulysses entered the courtyard of the palace.", "오디세우스가 궁전의 안뜰로 들어설 때 한 가지 가슴 뭉클한 일이 일어났다."], ["An old dog lay in the yard almost dead with age, and seeing a stranger enter, raised his head, with ears erect.", "늙은 개 한 마리가 마당에 누워 나이가 들 대로 들어 거의 죽어 가고 있었는데, 낯선 이가 들어오는 것을 보고는 귀를 쫑긋 세우며 머리를 들었다."], ["It was Argus, Ulysses’ own dog, that he had in other days often led to the chase.", "그것은 오디세우스 자신의 개인 아르고스로, 지난날 그가 흔히 사냥에 데리고 다니던 개였다."]], [["“. . . Soon as he perceived Long-lost Ulysses nigh, down fell his ears Clapped close, and with his tail glad sign he gave Of gratulation, impotent to rise, And to approach his master as of old. Ulysses, noting him, wiped off a tear Unmarked. . . . Then his destiny released Old Argus, soon as he had lived to see Ulysses in the twentieth year restored.”", "\"······오래도록 잃었던 오디세우스가 가까이 온 것을 알아채자마자, 그 개는 귀를 납작하게 늘어뜨리고 꼬리를 흔들어 반가운 인사를 보냈으나, 일어설 힘이 없어 예전처럼 주인에게 다가가지는 못했다. 그를 알아본 오디세우스는 남몰래 눈물 한 방울을 닦았다. ······그러자 운명이 늙은 아르고스를 풀어 주었으니, 그가 스무 해째에 돌아온 오디세우스를 살아서 보기에 이른 바로 그때였다.\""]], [["As Ulysses sat eating his portion in the hall, the suitors began to exhibit their insolence to him.", "오디세우스가 큰 방에 앉아 자기 몫의 음식을 먹고 있을 때, 구혼자들이 그에게 무례함을 부리기 시작했다."], ["When he mildly remonstrated, one of them raised a stool and with it gave him a blow.", "그가 점잖게 항의하자 그들 가운데 하나가 의자를 들어 그것으로 그를 후려쳤다."], ["Telemachus had hard work to restrain his indignation at seeing his father so treated in his own hall, but remembering his father’s injunctions, said no more than what became him as master of the house, though young, and protector of his guests.", "텔레마코스는 자기 아버지가 자기 집 안에서 그런 대접을 받는 것을 보고 분노를 억누르느라 무진 애를 썼으나, 아버지의 당부를 떠올리고는, 비록 젊으나 집안의 주인이자 손님들의 보호자로서 자기에게 어울리는 정도 이상은 말하지 않았다."]], [["Penelope had protracted her decision in favor of either of her suitors so long that there seemed to be no further pretence for delay.", "페넬로페는 자기 구혼자들 가운데 누구 하나를 택하는 결정을 어찌나 오래도록 미루어 왔던지, 이제 더 이상 지체할 핑계가 남아 있지 않은 듯했다."], ["The continued absence of her husband seemed to prove that his return was no longer to be expected.", "남편이 계속 돌아오지 않는 것은 그의 귀환을 더는 기대할 수 없음을 입증하는 것 같았다."], ["Meanwhile her son had grown up, and was able to manage his own affairs.", "그러는 사이 그녀의 아들은 다 자라나 자기 일을 스스로 처리할 수 있게 되었다."], ["She therefore consented to submit the question of her choice to a trial of skill among the suitors.", "그리하여 그녀는 자신의 선택이라는 문제를 구혼자들 사이의 솜씨 겨루기에 부치는 데 동의했다."], ["The test selected was shooting with the bow.", "선택된 시합은 활쏘기였다."], ["Twelve rings were arranged in a line, and he whose arrow was sent through the whole twelve was to have the queen for his prize.", "열두 개의 고리를 한 줄로 늘어놓고, 그 열두 개를 모조리 화살로 꿰뚫는 자가 왕비를 상으로 차지하게 되어 있었다."], ["A bow that one of his brother heroes had given to Ulysses in former times was brought from the armory, and with its quiver full of arrows was laid in the hall.", "지난날 동료 영웅 가운데 한 사람이 오디세우스에게 준 활 하나를 무기고에서 가져와, 화살이 가득 든 화살통과 함께 큰 방에 놓아두었다."], ["Telemachus had taken care that all other weapons should be removed, under pretence that in the heat of competition there was danger, in some rash moment, of putting them to an improper use.", "텔레마코스는 다른 무기는 모조리 치워 두도록 미리 손을 써 두었는데, 겨루기가 한창 달아오를 때 어느 무모한 순간에 그것들을 그릇되게 쓸 위험이 있다는 핑계를 댔다."]], [["All things being prepared for the trial, the first thing to be done was to bend the bow in order to attach the string.", "시합을 위한 모든 준비가 갖추어지자, 가장 먼저 해야 할 일은 시위를 메기 위해 활을 휘는 것이었다."], ["Telemachus endeavored to do it, but found all his efforts fruitless; and modestly confessing that he had attempted a task beyond his strength, he yielded the bow to another.", "텔레마코스가 그것을 해 보려 했으나 온갖 애를 써도 헛수고임을 알았고, 자기 힘에 부치는 일을 시도했노라고 겸손히 인정하며 활을 다른 이에게 넘겼다."], ["He tried it with no better success, and, amidst the laughter and jeers of his companions, gave it up.", "그 사람도 별로 나을 것 없는 솜씨로 시도하다가, 동료들의 웃음과 야유 속에 포기하고 말았다."], ["Another tried it and another; they rubbed the bow with tallow, but all to no purpose; it would not bend.", "또 다른 이가 시도하고 또 다른 이가 시도했으며, 그들은 활에 짐승 기름을 문질러 보기도 했으나 모두 헛일이었으니, 활은 도무지 휘어지지 않았다."], ["Then spoke Ulysses, humbly suggesting that he should be permitted to try; for, said he, “beggar as I am, I was once a soldier, and there is still some strength in these old limbs of mine.”", "그러자 오디세우스가 입을 열어, 자신에게도 한번 해 보게 해 달라고 겸손히 청하며 말했다. \"거지인 이 몸이오나, 저도 한때는 군인이었고, 이 늙은 팔다리에도 아직 얼마간의 힘은 남아 있습니다.\""], ["The suitors hooted with derision, and commanded to turn him out of the hall for his insolence.", "구혼자들은 비웃으며 야유를 보냈고, 무례하다 하여 그를 방에서 내쫓으라고 명했다."], ["But Telemachus spoke up for him, and, merely to gratify the old man, bade him try.", "그러나 텔레마코스가 그를 두둔하며, 그저 노인의 마음을 흡족하게 해 주려는 것일 뿐이라며 그에게 해 보라고 일렀다."], ["Ulysses took the bow, and handled it with the hand of a master.", "오디세우스는 활을 받아 들고 명수의 손길로 그것을 다루었다."], ["With ease he adjusted the cord to its notch, then fitting an arrow to the bow he drew the string and sped the arrow unerring through the rings.", "그는 손쉽게 시위를 홈에 메기고, 이어 화살 하나를 활에 메겨 시위를 당기더니, 그 화살을 빗나감 없이 고리들 사이로 쏘아 보냈다."]], [["Without allowing them time to express their astonishment, he said, “Now for another mark!”", "그들에게 놀라움을 표할 겨를도 주지 않고 그가 말했다. \"이제 또 다른 과녁을!\""], ["and aimed direct at the most insolent one of the suitors.", "그러고는 구혼자들 가운데 가장 무례한 자를 똑바로 겨누었다."], ["The arrow pierced through his throat and he fell dead.", "화살이 그자의 목을 꿰뚫었고 그는 쓰러져 죽었다."], ["Telemachus, Eumæus, and another faithful follower, well armed, now sprang to the side of Ulysses.", "텔레마코스와 에우마이오스, 그리고 또 한 사람의 충직한 부하가 단단히 무장을 한 채 이제 오디세우스의 곁으로 뛰어왔다."], ["The suitors, in amazement, looked round for arms, but found none, neither was there any way of escape, for Eumæus had secured the door.", "구혼자들은 경악하여 무기를 찾아 두리번거렸으나 아무것도 찾지 못했고, 달아날 길도 없었으니 에우마이오스가 문을 막아 두었기 때문이다. 오디세우스는 그들을 오래도록 의구심에 빠뜨려 두지 않았다."], ["Ulysses left them not long in uncertainty; he announced himself as the long-lost chief, whose house they had invaded, whose substance they had squandered, whose wife and son they had persecuted for ten long years; and told them he meant to have ample vengeance.", "그는 자신이 바로 오래도록 잃었던 그 영주임을 밝히니, 그들이 그의 집을 침범하고 그의 재산을 탕진했으며 그의 아내와 아들을 열 해라는 긴 세월 동안 핍박해 왔다고 했다. 그리고 자신은 그들에게 흡족하리만치 복수할 작정이라고 그들에게 일렀다."], ["All were slain, and Ulysses was left master of his palace and possessor of his kingdom and his wife.", "모두가 죽임을 당했고, 오디세우스는 자기 궁전의 주인이자 자기 왕국과 아내의 주인으로 남게 되었다."]], [["Tennyson’s poem of “Ulysses” represents the old hero, after his dangers past and nothing left but to stay at home and be happy, growing tired of inaction and resolving to set forth again in quest of new adventures:", "테니슨의 시 「율리시스」는 늙은 영웅을 그려 보이는데, 위험들이 지나가고 이제 집에 머물러 행복을 누리는 일밖에 남지 않게 되자 그는 무위에 싫증을 느껴 새로운 모험을 찾아 다시 길을 나서기로 결심한다."]], [["“. .", "\"······오라, 나의 벗들이여, 더 새로운 세계를 찾아 나서기에 너무 늦지 않았노라."], [". Come, my friends, ’Tis not too late to seek a newer world.", "배를 밀어내고, 차례로 자리에 앉아 울리는 물이랑을 두드려라."], ["Push off, and sitting well in order smite The sounding furrows; for my purpose holds To sail beyond the sunset, and the baths Of all the western stars, until I die.", "내 뜻은 굳건하니 해 지는 곳 너머로, 그리고 모든 서녘 별들이 멱 감는 곳 너머로 나아가, 죽는 그날까지 항해하리라."], ["It may be that the gulfs will wash us down; It may be we shall touch the Happy Isles, And see the great Achilles whom we knew;”", "어쩌면 깊은 바다가 우리를 휩쓸어 가라앉힐지도 모르고, 어쩌면 우리가 행복의 섬들에 가닿아, 우리가 알던 저 위대한 아킬레우스를 보게 될지도 모르리라.\""], ["etc.", "운운."]], [["————", "────"]], [["WE have followed one of the Grecian heroes, Ulysses, in his wanderings on his return home from Troy, and now we propose to share the fortunes of the remnant of the conquered people, under their chief Æneas, in their search for a new home, after the ruin of their native city.", "우리는 그리스의 영웅 가운데 한 사람인 오디세우스를 따라가며, 그가 트로이아에서 고향으로 돌아오기까지의 방랑을 살펴보았다. 이제 우리는 패배한 백성의 남은 무리가 그들의 우두머리 아이네아스의 인솔 아래, 조국이 멸망한 뒤 새로운 거처를 찾아 떠난 운명을 함께 나누어 보고자 한다."], ["On that fatal night when the wooden horse disgorged its contents of armed men, and the capture and conflagration of the city were the result, Æneas made his escape from the scene of destruction, with his father, and his wife, and young son.", "목마가 그 속에 품었던 무장한 병사들을 토해 내고, 그 결과 도시가 함락되어 불길에 휩싸이던 그 운명적인 밤에, 아이네아스는 아버지와 아내와 어린 아들을 데리고 파괴의 현장에서 빠져나왔다."], ["The father, Anchises, was too old to walk with the speed required, and Æneas took him upon his shoulders.", "아버지 안키세스는 너무 늙어 필요한 속도로 걸을 수 없었으므로, 아이네아스는 그를 어깨에 들쳐 멨다."], ["Thus burdened, leading his son and followed by his wife, he made the best of his way out of the burning city; but, in the confusion, his wife was swept away and lost.", "그렇게 짐을 진 채 아들의 손을 이끌고 아내를 뒤따르게 하면서, 그는 불타는 도시를 빠져나오려 안간힘을 썼다. 그러나 그 혼란 속에서 그의 아내는 휩쓸려 사라지고 말았다."]], [["On arriving at the place of rendezvous, numerous fugitives, of both sexes, were found, who put themselves under the guidance of Æneas.", "집결지에 도착하니 남녀를 막론한 수많은 피난민이 모여 있었고, 그들은 아이네아스의 인도에 따르기로 했다. 준비에 몇 달이 소요되었고, 마침내 그들은 배에 올랐다."], ["Some months were spent in preparation, and at length they embarked.", "그들은 먼저 인접한 트라키아 해안에 상륙하여 도시를 세우려 준비했으나, 아이네아스는 어떤 불길한 전조에 가로막혔다."], ["They first landed on the neighboring shores of Thrace, and were preparing to build a city, but Æneas was deterred by a prodigy.", "제물을 바치려고 그는 덤불 하나에서 잔가지 몇 개를 꺾었다. 그러자 놀랍게도 상처 입은 부분에서 피가 떨어졌다."], ["Preparing to offer sacrifice, he tore some twigs from one of the bushes.", "그가 다시 그 행동을 되풀이하자, 땅에서 어떤 목소리가 그에게 외쳤다."], ["To his dismay the wounded part dropped blood.", "\"나를 해치지 마시오, 아이네아스."], ["When he repeated the act a voice from the ground cried out to him, “Spare me, Æneas; I am your kinsman, Polydore, here murdered with many arrows, from which a bush has grown, nourished with my blood.”", "나는 당신의 친족 폴리도로스이며, 여기서 수많은 화살에 찔려 살해당했소. 그 화살들에서 덤불이 자라났고, 그것은 내 피로 자란 것이오.\""], ["These words recalled to the recollection of Æneas that Polydore was a young prince of Troy, whom his father had sent with ample treasures to the neighboring land of Thrace, to be there brought up, at a distance from the horrors of war.", "이 말은 아이네아스에게 폴리도로스가 트로이아의 젊은 왕자였음을 떠올리게 했다. 그의 아버지는 전쟁의 참상에서 멀리 떨어진 곳에서 그를 양육시키려고, 막대한 보물과 함께 그를 인접한 트라키아 땅으로 보냈던 것이다."], ["The king to whom he was sent had murdered him and seized his treasures.", "그러나 그가 보내진 곳의 왕은 그를 살해하고 그의 보물을 차지해 버렸다."], ["Æneas and his companions, considering the land accursed by the stain of such a crime, hastened away.", "아이네아스와 그의 동료들은 그런 죄악의 오점으로 저주받은 땅이라 여기고 서둘러 그곳을 떠났다."]], [["They next landed on the island of Delos, which was once a floating island, till Jupiter fastened it by adamantine chains to the bottom of the sea.", "그들은 다음으로 델로스 섬에 상륙했다. 이 섬은 한때 떠다니는 섬이었으나, 제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]가 단단한 사슬로 그것을 바다 밑바닥에 붙들어 매었다."], ["Apollo and Diana were born there, and the island was sacred to Apollo.", "아폴론과 아르테미스[로마의 〈디아나〉]가 그곳에서 태어났으며, 그 섬은 아폴론에게 바쳐진 신성한 곳이었다."], ["Here Æneas consulted the oracle of Apollo, and received an answer, ambiguous as usual,—“Seek your ancient mother; there the race of Æneas shall dwell, and reduce all other nations to their sway.”", "여기서 아이네아스는 아폴론의 신탁에 물었고, 늘 그러하듯 모호한 답을 받았다."], ["The Trojans heard with joy and immediately began to ask one another, “Where is the spot intended by the oracle?”", "\"그대의 옛 어머니를 찾으라. 그곳에서 아이네아스의 후손이 거하며, 다른 모든 민족을 그들의 지배 아래 굴복시키리라.\""], ["Anchises remembered that there was a tradition that their forefathers came from Crete and thither they resolved to steer.", "트로이아인들은 기쁨에 차서 이 말을 듣고는 곧바로 서로에게 묻기 시작했다. \"신탁이 가리키는 곳이 어디인가?\""], ["They arrived at Crete and began to build their city, but sickness broke out among them, and the fields that they had planted failed to yield a crop.", "안키세스는 자신들의 선조가 크레타에서 왔다는 전승이 있음을 기억해 냈고, 그들은 그곳으로 뱃머리를 돌리기로 결심했다."], ["In this gloomy aspect of affairs Æneas was warned in a dream to leave the country and seek a western land, called Hesperia, whence Dardanus, the true founder of the Trojan race, had originally migrated.", "그들은 크레타에 도착하여 도시를 세우기 시작했으나, 무리 사이에 병이 돌았고, 그들이 일군 밭은 수확을 내지 못했다. 일이 이렇듯 암울한 양상을 보이던 차에, 아이네아스는 꿈속에서 이 나라를 떠나 헤스페리아라 불리는 서쪽 땅을 찾으라는 경고를 받았다."], ["To Hesperia, now called Italy, therefore, they directed their future course, and not till after many adventures and the lapse of time sufficient to carry a modern navigator several times round the world, did they arrive there.", "그곳은 트로이아 종족의 진정한 시조인 다르다노스가 본래 이주해 온 땅이었다. 따라서 그들은 이제 이탈리아라 불리는 헤스페리아로 앞으로의 항로를 잡았으나, 수많은 모험을 거치고 근대의 항해자라면 세계를 여러 바퀴 돌고도 남을 만큼의 세월이 흐른 뒤에야 비로소 그곳에 도착할 수 있었다."]], [["Their first landing was at the island of the Harpies.", "그들이 처음 상륙한 곳은 하르피이아이의 섬이었다."], ["These were disgusting birds with the heads of maidens, with long claws and faces pale with hunger.", "이들은 처녀의 머리를 한 역겨운 새들로, 긴 발톱을 지녔으며 굶주림으로 얼굴이 창백했다."], ["They were sent by the gods to torment a certain Phineus, whom Jupiter had deprived of his sight, in punishment of his cruelty; and whenever a meal was placed before him the Harpies darted down from the air and carried it off.", "그들은 신들에 의해 어떤 피네우스를 괴롭히도록 보내졌는데, 제우스는 그의 잔혹함을 벌하고자 그의 시력을 앗아 간 바 있었다. 그의 앞에 음식이 놓이기만 하면 하르피이아이가 공중에서 쏜살같이 내려와 그것을 채어 가곤 했다."], ["They were driven away from Phineus by the heroes of the Argonautic expedition, and took refuge in the island where Æneas now found them.", "그들은 아르고호 원정대의 영웅들에게 쫓겨 피네우스에게서 물러났고, 이제 아이네아스가 그들을 발견한 그 섬으로 피신해 있었던 것이다."]], [["When they entered the port the Trojans saw herds of cattle roaming over the plain.", "항구에 들어서니 트로이아인들은 들판을 누비는 소 떼를 보았다."], ["They slew as many as they wished and prepared for a feast.", "그들은 원하는 만큼 소를 잡아 잔치를 준비했다."], ["But no sooner had they seated themselves at the table than a horrible clamor was heard in the air, and a flock of these odious harpies came rushing down upon them, seizing in their talons the meat from the dishes and flying away with it.", "그러나 그들이 식탁에 자리를 잡자마자 공중에서 무시무시한 소란이 들려왔고, 이 가증스러운 하르피이아이의 무리가 그들에게 달려들어 발톱으로 접시의 고기를 움켜쥐고는 그것을 들고 날아가 버렸다."], ["Æneas and his companions drew their swords and dealt vigorous blows among the monsters, but to no purpose, for they were so nimble it was almost impossible to hit them, and their feathers were like armor impenetrable to steel.", "아이네아스와 그의 동료들은 칼을 빼 들고 그 괴물들에게 힘껏 일격을 가했으나 아무런 소용이 없었으니, 그들이 어찌나 날쌔던지 맞히기가 거의 불가능했고, 그들의 깃털은 강철도 뚫지 못하는 갑옷과도 같았다. 그들 중 하나가 인근의 벼랑에 내려앉아 비명처럼 외쳤다."], ["One of them, perched on a neighboring cliff, screamed out, “Is it thus, Trojans, you treat us innocent birds, first slaughter our cattle and then make war on ourselves?”", "\"트로이아인들이여, 죄 없는 우리 새들을 이렇게 대접하느냐. 먼저 우리 소들을 도살하고 이제는 우리 자신에게 전쟁을 걸다니?\""], ["She then predicted dire sufferings to them in their future course, and having vented her wrath flew away.", "그러고는 그들에게 앞날의 항로에서 끔찍한 고난이 닥치리라 예언했고, 분노를 다 쏟아 낸 뒤 날아가 버렸다."], ["The Trojans made haste to leave the country, and next found themselves coasting along the shore of Epirus.", "트로이아인들은 서둘러 그 나라를 떠났고, 다음으로 에페이로스 해안을 따라 항해하게 되었다."], ["Here they landed, and to their astonishment learned that certain Trojan exiles, who had been carried there as prisoners, had become rulers of the country.", "여기서 그들은 상륙했고, 놀랍게도 포로로 끌려온 어떤 트로이아 망명자들이 그 나라의 통치자가 되었음을 알게 되었다."], ["Andromache, the widow of Hector, became the wife of one of the victorious Grecian chiefs, to whom she bore a son.", "헥토르의 미망인 안드로마케는 승리한 그리스의 우두머리 가운데 한 사람의 아내가 되어 그에게 아들을 낳아 주었다."], ["Her husband dying, she was left regent of the country, as guardian of her son, and had married a fellow-captive, Helenus, of the royal race of Troy.", "그녀의 남편이 죽자, 그녀는 아들의 후견인으로서 나라의 섭정으로 남았고, 함께 포로로 잡혀 온 트로이아 왕가 출신의 헬레노스와 혼인했다."], ["Helenus and Andromache treated the exiles with the utmost hospitality, and dismissed them loaded with gifts.", "헬레노스와 안드로마케는 망명자들을 더없는 환대로 맞이했으며, 선물을 잔뜩 안겨 떠나보냈다."]], [["From hence Æneas coasted along the shore of Sicily and passed the country of the Cyclopes.", "이곳에서 아이네아스는 시켈리아[시칠리아] 해안을 따라 항해하며 키클로페스의 나라를 지났다."], ["Here they were hailed from the shore by a miserable object, whom by his garments, tattered as they were, they perceived to be a Greek.", "여기서 그들은 해안에서 어떤 비참한 인물의 부름을 받았는데, 비록 누더기가 되었으나 그 옷차림으로 보아 그가 그리스인임을 알아챘다."], ["He told them he was one of Ulysses’s companions, left behind by that chief in his hurried departure.", "그는 자신이 오디세우스의 동료 가운데 한 사람이며, 그 우두머리가 황급히 떠나면서 자신을 뒤에 남겨 두었노라 말했다."], ["He related the story of Ulysses’s adventure with Polyphemus, and besought them to take him off with them as he had no means of sustaining his existence where he was but wild berries and roots, and lived in constant fear of the Cyclopes.", "그는 폴리페모스와 얽힌 오디세우스의 모험담을 들려주었고, 자신을 함께 데려가 달라 간청했다. 그곳에서는 야생 열매와 풀뿌리 말고는 목숨을 부지할 방도가 없었고, 키클로페스에 대한 끊임없는 두려움 속에서 살아왔기 때문이다."], ["While he spoke Polyphemus made his appearance; a terrible monster, shapeless, vast, whose only eye had been put out.[26] He walked with cautious steps, feeling his way with a staff, down to the sea-side, to wash his eye-socket in the waves.", "그가 말하는 동안 폴리페모스가 모습을 드러냈다. 형체를 알 수 없고 거대한, 그 하나뿐인 눈마저 멀어 버린 끔찍한 괴물이었다.[26] 그는 지팡이로 길을 더듬으며 조심스러운 걸음으로 바닷가로 내려와, 물결에 자신의 눈구멍을 씻으려 했다."], ["When he reached the water, he waded out towards them, and his immense height enabled him to advance far into the sea, so that the Trojans, in terror, took to their oars to get out of his way.", "물에 이르자 그는 그들 쪽으로 걸어 들어왔고, 그 엄청난 키 덕분에 바다 깊숙이까지 나아갈 수 있었으므로, 트로이아인들은 두려움에 휩싸여 그를 피하고자 노에 매달렸다."], ["Hearing the oars, Polyphemus shouted after them, so that the shores resounded, and at the noise the other Cyclopes came forth from their caves and woods and lined the shore, like a row of lofty pine trees.", "노 젓는 소리를 듣고 폴리페모스가 그들을 향해 고함을 질러 대니 해안이 온통 울려 퍼졌고, 그 소리에 다른 키클로페스가 동굴과 숲에서 나와 우뚝 솟은 소나무들이 늘어선 듯 해안을 따라 줄지어 섰다."], ["The Trojans plied their oars and soon left them out of sight.", "트로이아인들은 노를 힘껏 저어 곧 그들을 시야에서 벗어나게 했다."]], [["Æneas had been cautioned by Helenus to avoid the strait guarded by the monsters Scylla and Charybdis.", "아이네아스는 헬레노스에게서 괴물 스킬라와 카립디스가 지키는 해협을 피하라는 경고를 받은 바 있었다."], ["There Ulysses, the reader will remember, had lost six of his men, seized by Scylla while the navigators were wholly intent upon avoiding Charybdis.", "독자들이 기억하듯, 그곳에서 오디세우스는 부하 여섯을 잃었으니, 항해자들이 카립디스를 피하는 데 온통 정신이 팔린 사이에 스킬라에게 채여 갔던 것이다."], ["Æneas, following the advice of Helenus, shunned the dangerous pass and coasted along the island of Sicily.", "아이네아스는 헬레노스의 조언을 따라 그 위험한 길목을 피하고 시켈리아 섬을 따라 항해했다."]], [["Juno, seeing the Trojans speeding their way prosperously towards their destined shore, felt her old grudge against them revive, for she could not forget the slight that Paris had put upon her, in awarding the prize of beauty to another.", "헤라[로마의 〈유노〉]는 트로이아인들이 운명으로 정해진 해안을 향해 순조롭게 나아가는 것을 보고는, 그들에 대한 오랜 원한이 되살아남을 느꼈다. 파리스가 아름다움의 상을 다른 이에게 안겨 그녀를 모욕한 일을 잊을 수 없었기 때문이다."], ["In heavenly minds can such resentments dwell![27] Accordingly she hastened to Æolus, the ruler of the winds,—the same who supplied Ulysses with favoring gales, giving him the contrary ones tied up in a bag.", "천상의 마음에 그런 원한이 깃들 수 있단 말인가![27] 그리하여 그녀는 바람들의 통치자 아이올로스에게로 서둘러 갔으니, 그는 오디세우스에게 순풍을 베풀고 역풍은 자루에 묶어 주었던 바로 그 신이었다."], ["Æolus obeyed the goddess and sent forth his sons, Boreas, Typhon, and the other winds, to toss the ocean.", "아이올로스는 여신의 뜻에 따라 그의 아들들인 보레아스와 티폰을 비롯한 다른 바람들을 내보내 바다를 뒤흔들게 했다."], ["A terrible storm ensued and the Trojan ships were driven out of their course towards the coast of Africa.", "무시무시한 폭풍이 일어났고, 트로이아의 배들은 항로에서 벗어나 아프리카 해안 쪽으로 떠밀려 갔다."], ["They were in imminent danger of being wrecked, and were separated, so that Æneas thought that all were lost except his own.", "그들은 난파의 위기에 처하여 뿔뿔이 흩어졌고, 그리하여 아이네아스는 자신의 배를 제외한 나머지가 모두 사라졌다고 여겼다."]], [["At this crisis, Neptune, hearing the storm raging, and knowing that he had given no orders for one, raised his head above the waves, and saw the fleet of Æneas driving before the gale.", "이 위기의 순간에, 포세이돈[로마의 〈넵투누스〉]은 폭풍이 거세게 몰아치는 소리를 듣고, 자신이 그런 명령을 내린 바 없음을 알기에 물결 위로 머리를 들었다가, 아이네아스의 함대가 강풍에 떠밀리는 것을 보았다."], ["Knowing the hostility of Juno, he was at no loss to account for it, but his anger was not the less at this interference in his province.", "헤라의 적의를 아는지라 그 까닭을 헤아리는 데 어려움이 없었으나, 자신의 영역에 가해진 이러한 간섭에 그의 분노는 조금도 덜하지 않았다."], ["He called the winds and dismissed them with a severe reprimand.", "그는 바람들을 불러 호된 질책과 함께 돌려보냈다."], ["He then soothed the waves, and brushed away the clouds from before the face of the sun.", "그런 다음 물결을 잠재우고, 태양의 얼굴 앞을 가린 구름을 쓸어 냈다."], ["Some of the ships which had got on the rocks he pried off with his own trident, while Triton and a sea-nymph, putting their shoulders under others, set them afloat again.", "바위에 걸린 배 몇 척은 자신의 삼지창으로 들어 올려 떼어 냈고, 한편 트리톤과 바다의 요정은 다른 배들 밑에 어깨를 받쳐 다시 띄워 주었다."], ["The Trojans, when the sea became calm, sought the nearest shore, which was the coast of Carthage, where Æneas was so happy as to find that one by one the ships all arrived safe, though badly shaken.", "바다가 잔잔해지자 트로이아인들은 가장 가까운 해안을 찾았으니, 그곳은 카르타고 해안이었다. 거기서 아이네아스는 비록 심하게 흔들리기는 했어도 배들이 하나하나 모두 무사히 도착한 것을 알고 더없이 기뻐했다."]], [["Waller, in his “Panegyric to the Lord Protector” (Cromwell), alludes to this stilling of the storm by Neptune:", "월러는 「호국경(크롬웰)에게 바치는 송시」에서 포세이돈이 폭풍을 잠재운 이 일을 다음과 같이 인유한다."]], [["“Above the waves, as Neptune showed his face, To chide the winds and save the Trojan race, So has your Highness, raised above the rest, Storms of ambition tossing us repressed.”", "\"물결 위로 포세이돈이 얼굴을 드러내어 바람을 꾸짖고 트로이아 종족을 구하였듯, 다른 모든 이 위로 드높이 오르신 전하께서는 우리를 뒤흔들던 야망의 폭풍을 잠재우셨도다.\""]], [["Carthage, where the exiles had now arrived, was a spot on the coast of Africa opposite Sicily, where at that time a Tyrian colony under Dido, their queen, were laying the foundations of a state destined in later ages to be the rival of Rome itself.", "망명자들이 이제 다다른 카르타고는, 시켈리아 맞은편 아프리카 해안의 한 지점이었다. 그곳에서는 당시 그들의 여왕 디도가 이끄는 티로스의 식민지가, 훗날 로마 그 자체의 맞수가 될 운명을 지닌 한 국가의 토대를 닦고 있었다."], ["Dido was the daughter of Belus, king of Tyre, and sister of Pygmalion, who succeeded his father on the throne.", "디도는 티로스의 왕 벨로스의 딸이자, 아버지의 뒤를 이어 왕위에 오른 피그말리온의 누이였다."], ["Her husband was Sichæus, a man of immense wealth, but Pygmalion, who coveted his treasures, caused him to be put to death.", "그녀의 남편은 막대한 부를 지닌 시카이오스였는데, 그의 보물을 탐낸 피그말리온이 그를 죽음에 이르게 했다."], ["Dido, with a numerous body of friends and followers, both men and women, succeeded in effecting their escape from Tyre, in several vessels, carrying with them the treasures of Sichæus.", "디도는 남녀를 막론한 수많은 벗과 추종자들을 거느리고, 여러 척의 배에 시카이오스의 보물을 싣고 티로스를 탈출하는 데 성공했다."], ["On arriving at the spot which they selected as the seat of their future home, they asked of the natives only so much land as they could enclose with a bull’s hide.", "그들이 미래의 거처로 삼고자 택한 지점에 도착했을 때, 그녀는 원주민들에게 황소 한 마리의 가죽으로 둘러쌀 수 있을 만큼의 땅만을 청했다."], ["When this was readily granted, she caused the hide to be cut into strips, and with them enclosed a spot on which she built a citadel, and called it Byrsa (a hide).", "이것이 선뜻 허락되자, 그녀는 그 가죽을 가느다란 끈으로 잘라, 그것으로 한 지점을 에워싸고 그 위에 성채를 세운 뒤 그것을 비르사(가죽)라 불렀다."], ["Around this fort the city of Carthage rose, and soon became a powerful and flourishing place.", "이 요새를 중심으로 카르타고 도시가 일어섰고, 곧 강력하고 번창하는 곳이 되었다."]], [["Such was the state of affairs when Æneas with his Trojans arrived there.", "아이네아스가 그의 트로이아인들과 함께 그곳에 도착했을 때의 사정이 이러했다."], ["Dido received the illustrious exiles with friendliness and hospitality.", "디도는 그 저명한 망명자들을 다정함과 환대로 맞이했다."], ["“Not unacquainted with distress,”", "\"고난을 모르지 않기에,\""], ["she said, “I have learned to succor the unfortunate.”[28] The queen’s hospitality displayed itself in festivities at which games of strength and skill were exhibited.", "그녀는 말했다. \"나는 불행한 이들을 도울 줄 알게 되었습니다.\"[28] 여왕의 환대는 힘과 기예의 경기가 펼쳐지는 잔치로 드러났다."], ["The strangers contended for the palm with her own subjects, on equal terms, the queen declaring that whether the victor were “Trojan or Tyrian should make no difference to her.”[29] At the feast which followed the games, Æneas gave at her request a recital of the closing events of the Trojan history and his own adventures after the fall of the city.", "이방인들은 그녀 자신의 백성들과 대등한 조건으로 우승을 다투었고, 여왕은 승자가 \"트로이아인이든 티로스인이든 자신에게는 아무런 차이가 없다\"고 선언했다.[29] 경기에 뒤이은 잔치에서, 아이네아스는 여왕의 청에 따라 트로이아 역사의 마지막 사건들과 도시가 함락된 뒤 자신이 겪은 모험을 이야기해 들려주었다."], ["Dido was charmed with his discourse and filled with admiration of his exploits.", "디도는 그의 이야기에 매료되어 그의 위업에 감탄으로 가득 찼다."], ["She conceived an ardent passion for him, and he for his part seemed well content to accept the fortunate chance which appeared to offer him at once a happy termination of his wanderings, a home, a kingdom, and a bride.", "그녀는 그에게 뜨거운 연정을 품었고, 그 또한 자신의 방랑에 행복한 종지부를 찍어 줄 거처와 왕국과 신부를 한꺼번에 안겨 주는 듯 보이는 이 다행스러운 기회를 기꺼이 받아들이는 듯했다."], ["Months rolled away in the enjoyment of pleasant intercourse, and it seemed as if Italy and the empire destined to be founded on its shores were alike forgotten.", "즐거운 교류 속에 몇 달이 흘러갔고, 이탈리아도 그 해안에 세워질 운명인 제국도 똑같이 잊힌 듯했다."], ["Seeing which, Jupiter despatched Mercury with a message to Æneas recalling him to a sense of his high destiny, and commanding him to resume his voyage.", "이를 본 제우스는 헤르메스[로마의 〈메르쿠리우스〉]를 보내어 아이네아스에게 그의 드높은 운명을 일깨우고 항해를 다시 시작하라 명하는 전갈을 전하게 했다."]], [["Æneas parted from Dido, though she tried every allurement and persuasion to detain him.", "아이네아스는 디도와 작별했으나, 그녀는 그를 붙잡으려 온갖 유혹과 설득을 다했다."], ["The blow to her affection and her pride was too much for her to endure, and when she found that he was gone, she mounted a funeral pile which she had caused to be erected, and having stabbed herself was consumed with the pile.", "그녀의 사랑과 자존심에 가해진 타격은 견디기에 너무도 컸으니, 그가 떠나 버린 것을 알게 되자 그녀는 자신이 세우게 한 화장용 장작더미 위에 올라, 스스로를 찔러 그 장작더미와 함께 불타 사라졌다."], ["The flames rising over the city were seen by the departing Trojans, and, though the cause was unknown, gave to Æneas some intimation of the fatal event.", "도시 위로 치솟는 불길은 떠나가는 트로이아인들의 눈에 들어왔고, 비록 그 까닭은 알 수 없었으나, 아이네아스에게 그 치명적인 사건에 대한 어떤 예감을 안겨 주었다."]], [["The following epigram we find in “Elegant Extracts”:", "다음의 경구는 「우아한 발췌집」에서 찾아볼 수 있다."]], [["# FROM THE LATIN", "# FROM THE LATIN"]], [["“Unhappy, Dido, was thy fate In first and second married state!", "라틴어에서 \"불행하여라, 디도여, 그대의 운명은 첫 번째와 두 번째 혼인의 처지에서 그러하였도다!"], ["One husband caused thy flight by dying, Thy death the other caused by flying.”", "한 남편은 죽음으로 그대를 도망치게 하였고, 다른 이는 떠남으로 그대의 죽음을 불렀도다.\""]], [["After touching at the island of Sicily, where Acestes, a prince of Trojan lineage, bore sway, who gave them a hospitable reception, the Trojans reëmbarked, and held on their course for Italy.", "트로이아 혈통의 왕자 아케스테스가 다스리는 시켈리아 섬에 들러 그에게서 환대를 받은 뒤, 트로이아인들은 다시 배에 올라 이탈리아를 향한 항로를 이어 갔다."], ["Venus now interceded with Neptune to allow her son at last to attain the wished-for goal and find an end of his perils on the deep.", "이제 아프로디테[로마의 〈베누스〉]는 포세이돈에게 청하여, 마침내 자신의 아들이 바라 마지않던 목표에 다다라 깊은 바다에서 겪는 위험에 종지부를 찍게 해 달라 간청했다."], ["Neptune consented, stipulating only for one life as a ransom for the rest.", "포세이돈은 이에 응하되, 다만 나머지 모두를 위한 몸값으로 한 목숨만을 요구했다."], ["The victim was Palinurus, the pilot.", "그 희생자는 키잡이 팔리누루스였다."], ["As he sat watching the stars, with his hand on the helm, Somnus sent by Neptune approached in the guise of Phorbas and said: “Palinurus, the breeze is fair, the water smooth, and the ship sails steadily on her course.", "그가 손을 키에 얹은 채 별을 지켜보며 앉아 있을 때, 포세이돈이 보낸 솜누스가 포르바스의 모습으로 다가와 말했다. \"팔리누루스여, 산들바람은 순조롭고 물결은 잔잔하며 배는 제 항로를 따라 꾸준히 나아가고 있소."], ["Lie down awhile and take needful rest.", "잠시 누워 필요한 휴식을 취하시오."], ["I will stand at the helm in your place.”", "내가 그대 대신 키 앞에 서겠소.\" 팔리누루스가 대답했다."], ["Palinurus replied, “Tell me not of smooth seas or favoring winds,—me who have seen so much of their treachery.", "\"잔잔한 바다나 순풍 따위는 내게 말하지 마시오. 나는 그것들의 배신을 너무도 많이 보아 왔소."], ["Shall I trust Æneas to the chances of the weather and the winds?”", "내가 아이네아스를 날씨와 바람의 변덕에 맡길 성싶소?\""], ["And he continued to grasp the helm and to keep his eyes fixed on the stars.", "그러고는 그는 계속 키를 붙들고 별에 시선을 고정한 채 있었다."], ["But Somnus waved over him a branch moistened with Lethæan dew, and his eyes closed in spite of all his efforts.", "그러나 솜누스가 레테의 이슬에 적신 가지를 그의 머리 위로 흔들자, 그의 두 눈은 온갖 안간힘에도 불구하고 감기고 말았다."], ["Then Somnus pushed him overboard and he fell; but keeping his hold upon the helm, it came away with him.", "그러자 솜누스가 그를 뱃전 너머로 밀어 떨어뜨렸으나, 그가 키를 붙들고 있었던 탓에 키도 그와 함께 떨어져 나갔다."], ["Neptune was mindful of his promise and kept the ship on her track without helm or pilot, till Æneas discovered his loss, and, sorrowing deeply for his faithful steersman, took charge of the ship himself.", "포세이돈은 자신의 약속을 잊지 않고, 키도 키잡이도 없이 배를 항로에 머무르게 했으니, 아이네아스가 그를 잃은 것을 알아채고 충실한 키잡이를 깊이 애도하며 손수 배를 맡을 때까지 그러했다."]], [["There is a beautiful allusion to the story of Palinurus in Scott’s “Marmion,”", "스콧의 「마미온」 제1편 서두에는 팔리누루스의 이야기를 아름답게 인유한 대목이 있다."], ["Introduction to Canto I., where the poet, speaking of the recent death of William Pitt, says:", "거기서 시인은 최근 세상을 떠난 윌리엄 피트를 두고 이렇게 말한다."]], [["“O, think how, to his latest day, When death just hovering claimed his prey, With Palinure’s unaltered mood, Firm at his dangerous post he stood; Each call for needful rest repelled, With dying hand the rudder held, Till in his fall, with fateful sway, The steerage of the realm gave way.”", "\"오, 생각하라, 그의 마지막 날까지 죽음이 막 그 먹이를 노리며 맴돌 때, 팔리누루스의 변함없는 기개로, 그가 위태로운 자리에 굳건히 섰음을. 필요한 휴식을 청하는 매번의 부름을 물리치고, 죽어 가는 손으로 키를 붙들었음을, 그러다 그가 쓰러지면서, 운명의 흔들림으로 나라의 키잡이 자리가 무너졌음을.\""]], [["The ships at last reached the shores of Italy, and joyfully did the adventurers leap to land.", "배들은 마침내 이탈리아 해안에 다다랐고, 모험가들은 기쁨에 차서 뭍으로 뛰어내렸다."], ["While his people were employed in making their encampment Æneas sought the abode of the Sibyl.", "그의 백성들이 야영지를 마련하는 데 여념이 없는 동안, 아이네아스는 시빌라의 거처를 찾아갔다."], ["It was a cave connected with a temple and grove, sacred to Apollo and Diana.", "그곳은 아폴론과 아르테미스에게 바쳐진 신전과 숲에 잇닿은 동굴이었다."], ["While Æneas contemplated the scene, the Sibyl accosted him.", "아이네아스가 그 광경을 바라보고 있을 때, 시빌라가 그에게 말을 걸었다."], ["She seemed to know his errand, and under the influence of the deity of the place, burst forth in a prophetic strain, giving dark intimations of labors and perils through which he was destined to make his way to final success.", "그녀는 그의 용건을 아는 듯했고, 그곳을 다스리는 신의 영향 아래 예언의 어조로 터져 나오듯, 그가 마침내 성공에 이르기까지 헤쳐 나가야 할 노고와 위험에 대한 어두운 암시를 던졌다."], ["She closed with the encouraging words which have become proverbial: “Yield not to disasters, but press onward the more bravely.”[30] Æneas replied that he had prepared himself for whatever might await him.", "그녀는 격언이 된 다음의 격려의 말로 끝맺었다. \"재난에 굴하지 말고, 더욱 용감하게 앞으로 나아가라.\"[30] 아이네아스는 자신을 기다리는 무엇에든 대비가 되어 있노라 대답했다."], ["He had but one request to make.", "그에게는 단 한 가지 청이 있었다."], ["Having been directed in a dream to seek the abode of the dead in order to confer with his father, Anchises, to receive from him a revelation of his future fortunes and those of his race, he asked her assistance to enable him to accomplish the task.", "꿈속에서 아버지 안키세스와 의논하기 위해, 그로부터 자신과 자기 종족의 앞날에 대한 계시를 받기 위해 죽은 자들의 거처를 찾으라는 지시를 받은 터라, 그는 그 과업을 이룰 수 있도록 그녀의 도움을 청했다. 시빌라가 대답했다."], ["The Sibyl replied, “The descent to Avernus is easy: the gate of Pluto stands open night and day; but to retrace one’s steps and return to the upper air, that is the toil, that the difficulty.”[31] She instructed him to seek in the forest a tree on which grew a golden branch.", "\"아베르누스로 내려가는 것은 쉽소. 하데스[로마의 〈플루톤〉]의 문은 밤낮으로 열려 있으니까."], ["This branch was to be plucked off and borne as a gift to Proserpine, and if fate was propitious it would yield to the hand and quit its parent trunk, but otherwise no force could rend it away.", "그러나 발걸음을 되돌려 위쪽의 대기로 돌아오는 것, 그것이 바로 수고요, 그것이 바로 어려움이오.\"[31] 그녀는 숲에서 황금 가지가 달린 나무 한 그루를 찾으라고 일러 주었다. 이 가지는 꺾어서 페르세포네[로마의 〈프로세르피나〉]에게 바칠 선물로 가져가야 했으니, 운명이 호의를 베푼다면 그것은 손에 순순히 잡혀 모체인 줄기에서 떨어져 나올 것이나, 그렇지 않다면 어떤 힘으로도 그것을 떼어 낼 수 없었다."], ["If torn away, another would succeed.[32]", "떼어 내고 나면 또 다른 가지가 그 자리를 이을 것이었다.[32]"]], [["Æneas followed the directions of the Sibyl.", "아이네아스는 시빌라의 지시를 따랐다."], ["His mother, Venus, sent two of her doves to fly before him and show him the way, and by their assistance he found the tree, plucked the branch, and hastened back with it to the Sibyl.", "그의 어머니 아프로디테는 자신의 비둘기 두 마리를 보내어 그의 앞에서 날며 길을 안내하게 했고, 그것들의 도움으로 그는 나무를 찾아 가지를 꺾었으며, 그것을 들고 서둘러 시빌라에게로 돌아왔다."]], [["————", "————"]], [["# THE INFERNAL REGIONS", "# THE INFERNAL REGIONS"]], [["AS at the commencement of our series we have given the pagan account of the creation of the world, so as we approach its conclusion we present a view of the regions of the dead, depicted by one of their most enlightened poets, who drew his doctrines from their most esteemed philosophers.", "우리가 이 연작의 첫머리에서 세계의 창조에 관한 이교도의 설명을 소개했던 것과 마찬가지로, 이제 그 끝맺음에 다가서면서 죽은 자들의 영역을 한 폭의 그림처럼 펼쳐 보이려 한다. 그것을 묘사한 이는 이교도들 가운데 가장 깨우친 시인의 한 사람으로, 그들이 가장 존경했던 철학자들로부터 그 가르침을 길어 올린 인물이다."], ["The region where Virgil locates the entrance to this abode is perhaps the most strikingly adapted to excite ideas of the terrific and preternatural of any on the face of the earth.", "베르길리우스가 이 거처로 들어가는 입구를 위치시킨 지역은, 어쩌면 지상 어디에서도 찾아보기 힘들 만큼 무시무시하고 초자연적인 관념을 불러일으키기에 가장 적합한 곳일 것이다."], ["It is the volcanic region near Vesuvius, where the whole country is cleft with chasms, from which sulphurous flames arise, while the ground is shaken with pent-up vapors, and mysterious sounds issue from the bowels of the earth.", "그곳은 베수비오 화산 인근의 화산 지대로, 온 들판이 갈라진 틈으로 쪼개져 있고, 그 틈에서 유황불이 솟아오르며, 갇혀 있던 수증기가 땅을 뒤흔들고, 땅속 깊은 곳에서 알 수 없는 소리가 흘러나오는 곳이다."], ["The lake Avernus is supposed to fill the crater of an extinct volcano.", "아베르누스 호수는 사화산의 분화구를 메우고 있는 것으로 여겨진다."], ["It is circular, half a mile wide, and very deep, surrounded by high banks, which in Virgil’s time were covered with a gloomy forest.", "그것은 둥글고, 너비가 반 마일이며, 매우 깊고, 높은 둑으로 둘러싸여 있는데, 베르길리우스의 시대에는 이 둑이 음울한 숲으로 뒤덮여 있었다."], ["Mephitic vapors rise from its waters, so that no life is found on its banks, and no birds fly over it.", "호수의 물에서는 독기 어린 수증기가 피어올라, 둑에는 아무런 생명도 깃들지 못하고, 어떤 새도 그 위를 날지 못한다."], ["Here, according to the poet, was the cave which afforded access to the infernal regions, and here Æneas offered sacrifices to the infernal deities, Proserpine, Hecate, and the Furies.", "시인에 따르면 바로 이곳에 저승의 영역으로 들어가는 동굴이 있었으며, 바로 이곳에서 아이네아스가 저승의 신들, 곧 페르세포네[로마의 〈프로세르피나〉]와 헤카테와 복수의 여신들에게 제물을 바쳤다."], ["Then a roaring was heard in the earth, the woods on the hill-tops were shaken, and the howling of dogs announced the approach of the deities.", "그러자 땅속에서 우르릉거리는 소리가 들려왔고, 언덕 꼭대기의 숲이 흔들렸으며, 개들이 짖어 대는 소리가 신들이 다가옴을 알렸다."], ["“Now,”", "\"이제,\""], ["said the Sibyl, “summon up your courage, for you will need it.”", "하고 시빌라가 말했다. \"그대의 용기를 끌어모으시오."], ["She descended into the cave, and Æneas followed.", "그것이 필요할 터이니.\" 그녀가 동굴로 내려가자 아이네아스가 그 뒤를 따랐다."], ["Before the threshold of hell they passed through a group of beings who are enumerated as Griefs and avenging Cares, pale Diseases and melancholy Age, Fear and Hunger that tempt to crime, Toil, Poverty, and Death,—forms horrible to view.", "지옥의 문턱에 이르기 전, 그들은 한 무리의 존재들 사이를 지나갔으니, 그것들은 슬픔과 복수심에 불타는 근심, 창백한 질병과 우울한 노령, 죄악으로 이끄는 공포와 굶주림, 고역과 빈곤과 죽음으로 헤아려지는 것들이었다. 모두가 보기에 끔찍한 형상이었다."], ["The Furies spread their couches there, and Discord, whose hair was of vipers tied up with a bloody fillet.", "복수의 여신들은 그곳에 자기네 침상을 펴 놓았고, 머리카락이 살무사로 되어 피 묻은 띠로 묶여 있는 불화의 여신도 있었다."], ["Here also were the monsters, Briareus, with his hundred arms, Hydras hissing, and Chimæras breathing fire.", "이곳에는 또한 괴물들이 있었으니, 백 개의 팔을 가진 브리아레오스와, 쉭쉭거리는 히드라들과, 불을 내뿜는 키마이라들이었다."], ["Æneas shuddered at the sight, drew his sword and would have struck, but the Sibyl restrained him.", "아이네아스는 그 광경에 몸서리치며 칼을 빼어 내리치려 했으나, 시빌라가 그를 만류했다."], ["They then came to the black river Cocytus, where they found the ferryman, Charon, old and squalid, but strong and vigorous, who was receiving passengers of all kinds into his boat, magnanimous heroes, boys and unmarried girls, as numerous as the leaves that fall at autumn, or the flocks that fly southward at the approach of winter.", "이윽고 그들은 검은 강 코키토스에 이르렀으며, 그곳에서 뱃사공 카론을 만났다. 그는 늙고 추레했으나 억세고 정정한 자로, 온갖 종류의 승객들을 자기 배에 받아들이고 있었으니, 도량 넓은 영웅들이며 소년들이며 시집가지 않은 처녀들이, 가을에 떨어지는 나뭇잎처럼, 또는 겨울이 다가옴에 따라 남쪽으로 날아가는 새 떼처럼 수없이 많았다."], ["They stood pressing for a passage and longing to touch the opposite shore.", "그들은 건너가기를 다투며 맞은편 기슭에 닿기를 갈망하고 있었다."], ["But the stern ferryman took in only such as he chose, driving the rest back.", "그러나 엄혹한 뱃사공은 자기가 고른 자들만을 태우고, 나머지는 모두 되돌려 보냈다. 아이네아스는 그 광경에 의아해하며 시빌라에게 물었다."], ["Æneas, wondering at the sight, asked the Sibyl, “Why this discrimination?”", "\"어찌하여 이렇게 가려 뽑는 것이오?\" 그녀가 대답했다."], ["She answered, “Those who are taken on board the bark are the souls of those who have received due burial rites; the host of others who have remained unburied are not permitted to pass the flood, but wander a hundred years, and flit to and fro about the shore, till at last they are taken over.”", "\"배에 태워지는 자들은 마땅한 매장의 의례를 치른 자들의 혼령이오. 매장되지 못한 채 남아 있는 나머지 무리는 강을 건너는 것이 허락되지 않아, 백 년 동안 떠돌며 기슭을 이리저리 헤매다가, 마침내 건너편으로 옮겨지는 것이오.\""], ["Æneas grieved at recollecting some of his own companions who had perished in the storm.", "아이네아스는 폭풍 속에서 죽은 자기 동료들 가운데 몇몇을 떠올리며 슬퍼했다."], ["At that moment he beheld Palinurus, his pilot, who fell overboard and was drowned.", "그 순간 그는 배 밖으로 떨어져 물에 빠져 죽은 자기 키잡이 팔리누루스를 보았다."], ["He addressed him and asked him the cause of his misfortune.", "그는 그에게 말을 건네며 불행의 까닭을 물었다."], ["Palinurus replied that the rudder was carried away, and he, clinging to it, was swept away with it.", "팔리누루스는 키가 휩쓸려 떨어져 나갔고, 거기에 매달려 있던 자기도 키와 함께 휩쓸려 갔다고 대답했다."], ["He besought Æneas most urgently to extend to him his hand and take him in company to the opposite shore.", "그는 아이네아스에게 손을 내밀어 자기를 맞은편 기슭으로 함께 데려가 달라고 간절히 애원했다."], ["But the Sibyl rebuked him for the wish thus to transgress the laws of Pluto; but consoled him by informing him that the people of the shore where his body had been wafted by the waves should be stirred up by prodigies to give it due burial, and that the promontory should bear the name of Cape Palinurus, which it does to this day.", "그러나 시빌라는 그가 그렇게 플루톤의 율법을 어기려 한다고 꾸짖었다. 다만 그녀는, 그의 시신이 파도에 떠밀려 닿은 기슭의 사람들이 갖가지 전조에 의해 마음이 움직여 그것을 마땅히 매장하게 될 것이며, 그 곶이 팔리누루스 곶이라는 이름을 지니게 될 것이라고 일러 주어 그를 위로했으니, 그 이름은 오늘날까지도 그렇게 불린다."], ["Leaving Palinurus consoled by these words, they approached the boat.", "이 말로 위로받은 팔리누루스를 뒤로하고 그들은 배에 다가갔다."], ["Charon, fixing his eyes sternly upon the advancing warrior, demanded by what right he, living and armed, approached that shore.", "카론은 앞으로 나아오는 전사에게 매서운 눈길을 고정한 채, 살아 있고 무장을 한 그가 무슨 권리로 그 기슭에 다가오느냐고 따져 물었다."], ["To which the Sibyl replied that they would commit no violence, that Æneas’s only object was to see his father, and finally exhibited the golden branch, at sight of which Charon’s wrath relaxed, and he made haste to turn his bark to the shore, and receive them on board.", "이에 시빌라는 그들이 아무런 폭력도 저지르지 않을 것이며, 아이네아스의 유일한 목적은 자기 아버지를 만나는 것이라고 대답하고는, 마침내 황금 가지를 내보였다. 그것을 보자 카론의 노여움이 누그러졌고, 그는 서둘러 자기 배를 기슭으로 돌려 그들을 태웠다."], ["The boat, adapted only to the light freight of bodiless spirits, groaned under the weight of the hero.", "형체 없는 혼령들의 가벼운 짐만을 싣도록 만들어진 그 배는 영웅의 무게에 신음했다."], ["They were soon conveyed to the opposite shore.", "그들은 곧 맞은편 기슭으로 옮겨졌다."], ["There they were encountered by the three-headed dog, Cerberus, with his necks bristling with snakes.", "그곳에서 그들은 목에 뱀들이 곤두선 머리가 셋 달린 개 케르베로스와 마주쳤다."], ["He barked with all his three throats till the Sibyl threw him a medicated cake which he eagerly devoured, and then stretched himself out in his den and fell asleep.", "그것이 세 목구멍으로 한껏 짖어 대자, 시빌라가 약을 탄 과자를 던져 주었고, 케르베로스는 그것을 게걸스레 삼키고는 자기 굴에 길게 드러누워 잠이 들었다."], ["Æneas and the Sibyl sprang to land.", "아이네아스와 시빌라는 뭍으로 뛰어내렸다."], ["The first sound that struck their ears was the wailing of young children, who had died on the threshold of life, and near to these were they who had perished under false charges.", "그들의 귀를 처음으로 울린 소리는, 삶의 문턱에서 죽어 간 어린아이들의 울음이었고, 그 곁에는 거짓된 고발로 목숨을 잃은 자들이 있었다."], ["Minos presides over them as judge, and examines the deeds of each.", "미노스가 그들을 다스리는 재판관으로 군림하며, 저마다의 행적을 살핀다."], ["The next class was of those who had died by their own hand, hating life and seeking refuge in death.", "그다음 부류는 삶을 미워하여 죽음에서 피난처를 구하고 제 손으로 목숨을 끊은 자들이었다."], ["O how willingly would they now endure poverty, labor, and any other infliction, if they might but return to life!", "오, 그들은 이제 다시 삶으로 돌아갈 수만 있다면 빈곤이든 고역이든 그 어떤 고난이든 얼마나 기꺼이 견뎌 내려 하겠는가!"], ["Next were situated the regions of sadness, divided off into retired paths, leading through groves of myrtle.", "그다음에는 슬픔의 영역이 자리하고 있었으니, 외딴 오솔길로 나뉘어 도금양 숲 사이로 이어져 있었다."], ["Here roamed those who had fallen victims to unrequited love, not freed from pain even by death itself.", "이곳에는 보답받지 못한 사랑의 제물이 된 자들이 떠돌았으니, 죽음 그 자체로도 그 고통에서 풀려나지 못한 자들이었다."], ["Among these, Æneas thought he descried the form of Dido, with a wound still recent.", "그 가운데서 아이네아스는 아직도 상처가 생생한 디도의 형상을 알아본 것 같았다."], ["In the dim light he was for a moment uncertain, but approaching, perceived it was indeed herself.", "어슴푸레한 빛 속에서 그는 한순간 긴가민가했으나, 가까이 다가가 보고는 그것이 정녕 그녀임을 알아차렸다."], ["Tears fell from his eyes, and he addressed her in the accents of love.", "그의 눈에서 눈물이 흘렀고, 그는 사랑의 어조로 그녀에게 말을 건넸다."], ["“Unhappy Dido!", "\"불행한 디도여!"], ["was then the rumor true that you had perished?", "그렇다면 그대가 목숨을 잃었다는 소문이 참이었단 말이오?"], ["and was I, alas! the cause?", "그리고, 아아, 내가 그 까닭이었단 말이오?"], ["I call the gods to witness that my departure from you was reluctant, and in obedience to the commands of Jove; nor could I believe that my absence would cost you so dear.", "내가 그대를 떠난 것이 마음 내키지 않은 일이었으며 유피테르의 명령에 따른 것이었음을, 나는 신들을 증인 삼아 맹세하오. 내가 떠나는 것이 그대에게 그토록 값비싼 대가를 치르게 하리라고는 도저히 믿을 수 없었소."], ["Stop, I beseech you, and refuse me not a last farewell.”", "부디 멈추어, 마지막 작별 인사만이라도 내게 거절하지 말아 주오.\""], ["She stood for a moment with averted countenance, and eyes fixed on the ground, and then silently passed on, as insensible to his pleadings as a rock.", "그녀는 한순간 얼굴을 돌린 채 눈을 땅에 고정하고 서 있다가, 바위처럼 그의 호소에 아무런 감응도 없이 말없이 지나가 버렸다."], ["Æneas followed for some distance; then, with a heavy heart, rejoined his companion and resumed his route.", "아이네아스는 한동안 그 뒤를 따라가다가, 무거운 마음으로 동행에게 돌아와 다시 길을 갔다."]], [["They next entered the fields where roam the heroes who have fallen in battle.", "그들은 다음으로 전투에서 쓰러진 영웅들이 떠도는 들판으로 들어섰다."], ["Here they saw many shades of Grecian and Trojan warriors.", "이곳에서 그들은 수많은 그리스와 트로이아 전사들의 혼령을 보았다."], ["The Trojans thronged around him, and could not be satisfied with the sight.", "트로이아인들은 그의 주위로 몰려들어, 아무리 그를 보아도 만족할 줄을 몰랐다."], ["They asked the cause of his coming, and plied him with innumerable questions.", "그들은 그가 온 까닭을 물으며 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 질문을 퍼부었다."], ["But the Greeks, at the sight of his armor glittering through the murky atmosphere, recognized the hero, and filled with terror turned their backs and fled, as they used to do on the plains of Troy.", "그러나 그리스인들은 음침한 대기 사이로 번쩍이는 그의 갑옷을 보고는 그 영웅을 알아보았고, 공포에 사로잡혀 등을 돌리고 달아났으니, 트로이아의 들판에서 늘 그러했던 것과 같았다."]], [["Æneas would have lingered long with his Trojan friends, but the Sibyl hurried him away.", "아이네아스는 트로이아의 벗들과 오래도록 머물고 싶었으나, 시빌라가 그를 재촉해 떼어 놓았다."], ["They next came to a place where the road divided, the one leading to Elysium, the other to the regions of the condemned.", "그들은 다음으로 길이 갈라지는 곳에 이르렀으니, 한 갈래는 엘리시온[엘리시움]으로 이어지고, 다른 한 갈래는 단죄받은 자들의 영역으로 이어져 있었다."], ["Æneas beheld on one side the walls of a mighty city, around which Phlegethon rolled its fiery waters.", "아이네아스는 한쪽에서 거대한 도시의 성벽을 보았는데, 그 둘레로는 플레게톤이 불타는 물결을 굽이치며 흐르고 있었다."], ["Before him was the gate of adamant that neither gods nor men can break through.", "그의 앞에는 신들도 인간들도 뚫을 수 없는 강철의 문이 있었다."], ["An iron tower stood by the gate, on which Tisiphone, the avenging Fury, kept guard.", "문 곁에는 무쇠 탑이 서 있었고, 그 위에서 복수의 여신 티시포네가 망을 보고 있었다."], ["From the city were heard groans, and the sound of the scourge, the creaking of iron, and the clanking of chains.", "도시에서는 신음 소리와 채찍질 소리, 쇠가 삐걱거리는 소리, 사슬이 절그럭거리는 소리가 들려왔다."], ["Æneas, horror-struck, inquired of his guide what crimes were those whose punishments produced the sounds he heard?", "아이네아스는 공포에 사로잡혀 안내자에게, 자기가 듣는 저 소리를 자아내는 형벌을 받는 자들은 어떤 죄를 지은 것이냐고 물었다."], ["The Sibyl answered, “Here is the judgment hall of Rhadamanthus, who brings to light crimes done in life, which the perpetrator vainly thought impenetrably hid.", "시빌라가 대답했다. \"이곳은 라다만토스의 재판정으로, 그는 죄를 저지른 자가 헛되이 빈틈없이 감추었다고 여긴 생전의 죄들을 환히 밝혀내오."], ["Tisiphone applies her whip of scorpions, and delivers the offender over to her sister Furies.”", "티시포네는 전갈 채찍을 휘둘러, 죄인을 자기 자매인 복수의 여신들에게 넘겨주오.\""], ["At this moment with horrid clang the brazen gates unfolded, and Æneas saw within a Hydra with fifty heads guarding the entrance.", "그 순간 끔찍한 굉음과 함께 청동 문이 활짝 열렸고, 아이네아스는 그 안에서 머리가 쉰 개 달린 히드라가 입구를 지키고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["The Sibyl told him that the gulf of Tartarus descended deep, so that its recesses were as far beneath their feet as heaven was high above their heads.", "시빌라는 그에게, 타르타로스의 심연이 깊디깊게 내려가, 그 가장 깊은 구석은 하늘이 그들의 머리 위로 높은 만큼이나 그들의 발밑으로 까마득하다고 일러 주었다. 이 구렁의 바닥에는 신들에 맞서 싸운 티탄 일족이 엎드려 있다."], ["In the bottom of this pit, the Titan race, who warred against the gods, lie prostrate; Salmoneus, also, who presumed to vie with Jupiter, and built a bridge of brass over which he drove his chariot that the sound might resemble thunder, launching flaming brands at his people in imitation of lightning, till Jupiter struck him with a real thunderbolt, and taught him the difference between mortal weapons and divine.", "또한 살모네우스도 그곳에 있으니, 그는 감히 유피테르와 겨루려 들어 청동 다리를 놓고 그 위로 전차를 몰아 그 소리가 천둥과 비슷하게 들리도록 했으며, 번개를 흉내 내어 백성들에게 불타는 횃불을 던졌다. 그러다 유피테르가 진짜 벼락으로 그를 내리쳐, 필멸자의 무기와 신의 무기가 어떻게 다른지를 가르쳤다."], ["Here, also, is Tityus, the giant, whose form is so immense that as he lies he stretches over nine acres, while a vulture preys upon his liver, which as fast as it is devoured grows again, so that his punishment will have no end.", "이곳에는 또한 거인 티티오스가 있으니, 그 몸뚱이가 어찌나 거대한지 드러누우면 아홉 에이커에 걸쳐 뻗어 있고, 독수리 한 마리가 그의 간을 쪼아 먹는데, 먹히는 족족 다시 자라나, 그의 형벌은 끝이 없을 것이다."]], [["Æneas saw groups seated at tables loaded with dainties, while near by stood a Fury who snatched away the viands from their lips as fast as they prepared to taste them.", "아이네아스는 진수성찬이 가득한 식탁 앞에 앉은 무리들을 보았는데, 그 곁에는 복수의 여신 하나가 서 있다가, 그들이 맛보려고 들 때마다 입술에서 음식을 낚아채 갔다."], ["Others beheld suspended over their heads huge rocks, threatening to fall, keeping them in a state of constant alarm.", "또 다른 자들은 머리 위에 거대한 바위가 매달려 금방이라도 떨어질 듯 위협하는 것을 보며, 끊임없는 두려움에 사로잡혀 있었다."], ["These were they who had hated their brothers, or struck their parents, or defrauded the friends who trusted them, or who, having grown rich, kept their money to themselves, and gave no share to others; the last being the most numerous class.", "이들은 제 형제를 미워했거나, 제 부모를 때렸거나, 자기를 믿은 벗들을 속였거나, 부자가 되고도 그 재물을 제 것으로만 움켜쥐고 남에게 한 몫도 나누지 않은 자들이었으니, 마지막 부류가 가장 많았다."], ["Here also were those who had violated the marriage vow, or fought in a bad cause, or failed in fidelity to their employers.", "이곳에는 또한 혼인의 맹세를 저버린 자들, 옳지 못한 명분을 위해 싸운 자들, 자기를 고용한 이들에게 충실하지 못한 자들도 있었다."], ["Here was one who had sold his country for gold, another who perverted the laws, making them say one thing to-day and another to-morrow.", "이곳에는 황금을 받고 제 나라를 팔아넘긴 자가 있었고, 또 다른 자는 율법을 왜곡하여 오늘은 이렇게 내일은 저렇게 말하도록 만든 자도 있었다."]], [["Ixion was there, fastened to the circumference of a wheel ceaselessly revolving; and Sisyphus, whose task was to roll a huge stone up to a hill-top, but when the steep was well-nigh gained, the rock, repulsed by some sudden force, rushed again headlong down to the plain.", "익시온이 그곳에 있었으니, 쉴 새 없이 도는 수레바퀴 둘레에 묶여 있었다. 또 시시포스도 있었으니, 그의 일은 거대한 바위를 언덕 꼭대기까지 굴려 올리는 것이었으나, 가파른 정상에 거의 다다랐을 때, 어떤 갑작스러운 힘에 떠밀린 바위가 다시 곤두박질쳐 평지로 굴러떨어지는 것이었다."], ["Again he toiled at it, while the sweat bathed all his weary limbs, but all to no effect.", "그는 다시금 그것에 매달려 애쓰니, 땀이 그의 지친 사지를 흠뻑 적셨으나, 모두가 헛수고였다."], ["There was Tantalus, who stood in a pool, his chin level with the water, yet he was parched with thirst, and found nothing to assuage it; for when he bowed his hoary head, eager to quaff, the water fled away, leaving the ground at his feet all dry.", "그곳에는 탄탈로스가 있었으니, 그는 못 속에 서서 턱이 수면과 나란할 정도였으나, 갈증으로 타들어 가면서도 그것을 달랠 것을 찾지 못했다. 마시려고 백발의 머리를 숙일 때마다 물이 달아나, 그의 발치의 땅을 온통 메마르게 남겨 두었기 때문이다."], ["Tall trees laden with fruit stooped their heads to him, pears, pomegranates, apples, and luscious figs; but when with a sudden grasp he tried to seize them winds whirled them high above his reach.", "열매를 가득 매단 키 큰 나무들이 그를 향해 머리를 숙였으니, 배며 석류며 사과며 달콤한 무화과였다. 그러나 그가 느닷없이 손을 뻗어 그것들을 움켜잡으려 들면, 바람이 그것들을 손이 닿지 않을 만큼 높이 휘몰아 갔다."]], [["The Sibyl now warned Æneas that it was time to turn from these melancholy regions and seek the city of the blessed.", "이윽고 시빌라는 아이네아스에게, 이 음울한 영역에서 발길을 돌려 축복받은 자들의 도시를 찾아갈 때가 되었다고 일러 주었다."], ["They passed through a middle tract of darkness, and came upon the Elysian fields, the groves where the happy reside.", "그들은 어둠의 중간 지대를 가로질러, 행복한 자들이 머무는 숲, 곧 엘리시온의 들판에 다다랐다."], ["They breathed a freer air, and saw all objects clothed in a purple light.", "그들은 더 자유로운 공기를 들이마셨고, 모든 사물이 자줏빛에 감싸여 있는 것을 보았다."], ["The region has a sun and stars of its own.", "그 지역에는 그 자체의 해와 별들이 있었다."], ["The inhabitants were enjoying themselves in various ways, some in sports on the grassy turf, in games of strength or skill, others dancing or singing.", "그곳의 거주자들은 갖가지 방식으로 즐거움을 누리고 있었으니, 더러는 풀밭 잔디에서 힘이나 솜씨를 겨루는 경기로, 더러는 춤추거나 노래하며 흥겨워했다."], ["Orpheus struck the chords of his lyre, and called forth ravishing sounds.", "오르페우스는 자기 리라의 줄을 켜며 황홀한 소리를 불러냈다."], ["Here Æneas saw the founders of the Trojan state, magnanimous heroes who lived in happier times.", "이곳에서 아이네아스는 트로이아라는 나라를 세운 시조들, 더 행복한 시절에 살았던 도량 넓은 영웅들을 보았다."], ["He gazed with admiration on the war chariots and glittering arms now reposing in disuse.", "그는 이제 쓰임을 잃고 쉬고 있는 전차들과 번쩍이는 무구들을 감탄에 차 바라보았다."], ["Spears stood fixed in the ground, and the horses, unharnessed, roamed over the plain.", "창들은 땅에 꽂혀 있었고, 멍에를 벗은 말들은 들판을 누비고 다녔다."], ["The same pride in splendid armor and generous steeds which the old heroes felt in life, accompanied them here.", "옛 영웅들이 생전에 화려한 갑옷과 늠름한 군마에 대해 느꼈던 것과 똑같은 긍지가, 이곳에서도 그들과 함께하고 있었다."], ["He saw another group feasting and listening to the strains of music.", "그는 또 다른 무리가 잔치를 벌이며 음악의 가락에 귀 기울이고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["They were in a laurel grove, whence the great river Po has its origin, and flows out among men.", "그들은 월계수 숲에 있었으니, 그곳에서 큰 강 포가 발원하여 인간들 사이로 흘러나간다."], ["Here dwelt those who fell by wounds received in their country’s cause, holy priests also, and poets who have uttered thoughts worthy of Apollo, and others who have contributed to cheer and adorn life by their discoveries in the useful arts, and have made their memory blessed by rendering service to mankind.", "이곳에는 제 나라의 대의를 위해 입은 상처로 쓰러진 자들이 머물렀고, 거룩한 사제들도, 아폴론에 어울릴 만한 생각을 읊은 시인들도, 그리고 유용한 기예에서 이룬 발견으로 삶을 북돋우고 꾸미는 데 이바지하여 인류에게 봉사함으로써 자기 기억을 축복받게 한 자들도 있었다."], ["They wore snow-white fillets about their brows.", "그들은 이마에 눈처럼 흰 띠를 두르고 있었다."], ["The Sibyl addressed a group of these, and inquired where Anchises was to be found.", "시빌라는 이들 가운데 한 무리에게 말을 건네어, 안키세스를 어디에서 찾을 수 있느냐고 물었다."], ["They were directed where to seek him, and soon found him in a verdant valley, where he was contemplating the ranks of his posterity, their destinies and worthy deeds to be achieved in coming times.", "그들은 그를 찾아갈 곳을 일러 주었고, 그들은 곧 푸르른 골짜기에서 그를 찾아냈으니, 그곳에서 그는 자기 후손들의 대열과 그들의 운명, 다가올 시대에 이루어질 그들의 훌륭한 행적을 헤아려 보고 있었다."], ["When he recognized Æneas approaching, he stretched out both hands to him, while tears flowed freely.", "그는 아이네아스가 다가오는 것을 알아보자, 두 손을 그에게 내밀었고, 눈물이 거침없이 흘러내렸다. \"마침내 왔구나,\""], ["“Have you come at last,”", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “long expected, and do I behold you after such perils past?", "\"오래도록 기다렸거니와, 그 많은 위험을 다 겪고 난 너를 내가 다시 보는구나?"], ["O my son, how have I trembled for you as I have watched your career!”", "오, 내 아들아, 네 앞길을 지켜보며 내가 너를 위해 얼마나 마음을 졸였는지!\""], ["To which Æneas replied, “O father!", "이에 아이네아스가 대답했다. \"오, 아버지!"], ["your image was always before me to guide and guard me.”", "아버지의 모습이 늘 제 앞에 있어 저를 이끌고 지켜 주었습니다.\""], ["Then he endeavored to enfold his father in his embrace, but his arms enclosed only an unsubstantial image.", "그러고 나서 그는 아버지를 품에 끌어안으려 했으나, 그의 두 팔은 실체 없는 형상만을 감싸 안았을 뿐이다."]], [["Æneas perceived before him a spacious valley, with trees gently waving to the wind, a tranquil landscape, through which the river Lethe flowed.", "아이네아스는 자기 앞에 드넓은 골짜기가 펼쳐져 있음을 알아보았으니, 나무들이 바람에 부드럽게 흔들리는 고요한 풍경이었고, 그 사이로 레테 강이 흐르고 있었다."], ["Along the banks of the stream wandered a countless multitude, numerous as insects in the summer air.", "강기슭을 따라 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 무리가 떠돌고 있었으니, 여름 공기 속의 벌레 떼만큼이나 수가 많았다."], ["Æneas, with surprise, inquired who were these.", "아이네아스는 놀라며 이들이 누구냐고 물었다."], ["Anchises answered, “They are souls to which bodies are to be given in due time.", "안키세스가 대답했다. \"이들은 때가 되면 육신을 부여받게 될 혼령들이다."], ["Meanwhile they dwell on Lethe’s bank, and drink oblivion of their former lives.”", "그동안 이들은 레테의 기슭에 머물며, 자기네 옛 삶에 대한 망각을 들이마신다.\""], ["“O father!”", "\"오, 아버지!\""], ["said Æneas, “is it possible that any can be so in love with life as to wish to leave these tranquil seats for the upper world?”", "하고 아이네아스가 말했다. \"이 고요한 거처를 떠나 위쪽 세계로 가기를 바랄 만큼 삶을 사랑하는 자가 있다는 것이 가당키나 한 일입니까?\""], ["Anchises replied by explaining the plan of creation.", "안키세스는 창조의 섭리를 설명하여 대답했다."], ["The Creator, he told him, originally made the material of which souls are composed of the four elements, fire, air, earth, and water, all which when united took the form of the most excellent part, fire, and became flame.", "그가 그에게 일러 주기를, 창조주는 본디 혼령을 이루는 재료를 불과 공기와 흙과 물이라는 네 원소로 만들었으며, 이것들이 결합하면 가장 빼어난 부분인 불의 형태를 띠어 불꽃이 되었다고 했다."], ["This material was scattered like seed among the heavenly bodies, the sun, moon, and stars.", "이 재료는 씨앗처럼 천체들, 곧 해와 달과 별들 사이에 흩뿌려졌다."], ["Of this seed the inferior gods created man and all other animals, mingling it with various proportions of earth, by which its purity was alloyed and reduced.", "이 씨앗으로 하급 신들이 인간과 그 밖의 모든 동물을 지어내되, 그것을 갖가지 비율의 흙과 섞었으니, 그로 인해 그 순수함이 합금처럼 흐려지고 떨어뜨려졌다."], ["Thus, the more earth predominates in the composition the less pure is the individual; and we see men and women with their full-grown bodies have not the purity of childhood.", "그리하여 그 구성에서 흙이 더 우세할수록 그 개체는 덜 순수하며, 우리는 다 자란 육신을 가진 남자와 여자가 어린 시절의 순수함을 지니지 못함을 본다."], ["So in proportion to the time which the union of body and soul has lasted is the impurity contracted by the spiritual part.", "그러므로 육신과 혼령의 결합이 지속된 시간에 비례하여, 그 영적인 부분이 더럽혀지는 것이다."], ["This impurity must be purged away after death, which is done by ventilating the souls in the current of winds, or merging them in water, or burning out their impurities by fire.", "이 더러움은 죽은 뒤에 씻어 내야 하니, 그것은 바람의 흐름 속에서 혼령들을 바람 쐬게 하거나, 물에 잠기게 하거나, 불로 그 더러움을 태워 없앰으로써 이루어진다."], ["Some few, of whom Anchises intimates that he is one, are admitted at once to Elysium, there to remain.", "안키세스가 자기도 그중 하나라고 넌지시 비친 소수의 몇몇은, 곧바로 엘리시온으로 받아들여져 그곳에 머물게 된다."], ["But the rest, after the impurities of earth are purged away, are sent back to life endowed with new bodies, having had the remembrance of their former lives effectually washed away by the waters of Lethe.", "그러나 나머지는, 흙의 더러움이 씻겨 나간 뒤에, 새 육신을 부여받아 다시 삶으로 돌려보내지는데, 이들은 레테의 물로 옛 삶에 대한 기억이 말끔히 씻겨 나간 상태이다."], ["Some, however, there still are, so thoroughly corrupted, that they are not fit to be intrusted with human bodies, and these are made into brute animals, lions, tigers, cats, dogs, monkeys, etc.", "그러나 그 가운데는 여전히 너무도 철저히 타락하여 인간의 육신을 맡기기에 합당치 못한 자들이 있으니, 이들은 짐승으로, 곧 사자며 호랑이며 고양이며 개며 원숭이 따위로 만들어진다."], ["This is what the ancients called Metempsychosis, or the transmigration of souls; a doctrine which is still held by the natives of India, who scruple to destroy the life even of the most insignificant animal, not knowing but it may be one of their relations in an altered form.", "이것이 옛사람들이 메템프시코시스, 곧 혼령의 윤회라 일컬은 것이다. 이 교리는 오늘날에도 인도의 토착민들이 받드는 바이니, 그들은 가장 하찮은 동물의 목숨이라도 차마 끊지 못하는데, 그것이 모습을 바꾼 자기 친족 가운데 하나일지도 모른다고 여기기 때문이다."]], [["Anchises, having explained so much, proceeded to point out to Æneas individuals of his race, who were hereafter to be born, and to relate to him the exploits they should perform in the world.", "안키세스는 이만큼 설명하고 나서, 아이네아스에게 앞으로 태어날 자기 혈통의 사람들을 하나하나 가리켜 보이며, 그들이 세상에서 펼칠 위업을 이야기해 주었다."], ["After this he reverted to the present, and told his son of the events that remained to him to be accomplished before the complete establishment of himself and his followers in Italy.", "그런 다음 그는 현재로 돌아와, 자기 아들에게, 그와 그의 추종자들이 이탈리아에 완전히 자리 잡기까지 그가 이루어야 할 일들을 일러 주었다."], ["Wars were to be waged, battles fought, a bride to be won, and in the result a Trojan state founded, from which should rise the Roman power, to be in time the sovereign of the world.", "전쟁을 치러야 하고, 싸움을 벌여야 하며, 신붓감을 얻어야 하고, 그 결과로 트로이아라는 나라가 세워져, 그로부터 로마의 권세가 일어나 머지않아 세계의 주인이 되리라는 것이었다."]], [["Æneas and the Sibyl then took leave of Anchises, and returned by some short cut, which the poet does not explain, to the upper world.", "이윽고 아이네아스와 시빌라는 안키세스에게 작별을 고하고, 시인이 설명하지 않은 어떤 지름길을 통해 위쪽 세계로 돌아왔다. 엘리시온"]], [["Virgil, we have seen, places his Elysium under the earth, and assigns it for a residence to the spirits of the blessed.", "우리가 보았듯이, 베르길리우스는 자기 엘리시온을 땅 밑에 두고, 그것을 축복받은 자들의 혼령이 머무는 거처로 정했다."], ["But in Homer Elysium forms no part of the realms of the dead.", "그러나 호메로스에게서 엘리시온은 죽은 자들의 영역에 속하지 않는다."], ["He places it on the west of the earth, near Ocean, and describes it as a happy land, where there is neither snow, nor cold, nor rain, and always fanned by the delightful breezes of Zephyrus.", "그는 그것을 땅의 서쪽, 오케아노스 가까이에 두고, 눈도 추위도 비도 없으며 언제나 제피로스의 상쾌한 산들바람이 부채질하는 행복한 땅으로 묘사한다."], ["Hither favored heroes pass without dying and live happy under the rule of Rhadamanthus.", "이곳으로 은총받은 영웅들이 죽지 않고 건너와, 라다만토스의 다스림 아래 행복하게 산다."], ["The Elysium of Hesiod and Pindar is in the Isles of the Blessed, or Fortunate Islands, in the Western Ocean.", "헤시오도스와 핀다로스의 엘리시온은 서쪽 대양에 있는 축복받은 자들의 섬, 곧 행운의 섬들에 있다."], ["From these sprang the legend of the happy island Atlantis.", "여기에서 행복한 섬 아틀란티스의 전설이 비롯되었다."], ["This blissful region may have been wholly imaginary, but possibly may have sprung from the reports of some storm-driven mariners who had caught a glimpse of the coast of America.", "이 더없이 행복한 지역은 온전히 상상의 산물이었을 수도 있으나, 어쩌면 폭풍에 떠밀린 어떤 뱃사람들이 아메리카의 해안을 언뜻 보았던 일에 관한 보고에서 비롯되었을지도 모른다."]], [["J. R.", "J."], ["Lowell, in one of his shorter poems, claims for the present age some of the privileges of that happy realm.", "R. 로웰은 자기 단시 가운데 하나에서, 현재의 시대를 위해 그 행복한 영역의 특권 몇 가지를 주장한다."], ["Addressing the Past, he says:", "〈과거〉를 향해 그는 이렇게 말한다."]], [["“Whatever of true life there was in thee, Leaps in our age’s veins.", "\"네 안에 깃들어 있던 참된 삶이 무엇이었든, 그것은 우리 시대의 핏줄 속에서 뛰노느니라."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], ["Here, ’mid the bleak waves of our strife and care, Float the green ‘Fortunate Isles,’ Where all thy hero-spirits dwell and share Our martyrdoms and toils.", "이곳, 우리의 다툼과 근심의 황량한 물결 한가운데, 푸른 〈행운의 섬들〉이 떠 있으니, 거기에 네 영웅의 혼령들이 모두 깃들어 우리의 순교와 노고를 함께 나누느니라."], ["The present moves attended With all of brave and excellent and fair That made the old time splendid.”", "현재의 순간은 옛 시절을 찬란하게 했던 용맹하고 빼어나고 아름다운 모든 것을 거느리고 나아가느니라.\""]], [["Milton also alludes to the same fable in “Paradise Lost,”", "밀턴 또한 「실낙원」"], ["Book III., 1. 568:", "제3권 568행에서 같은 우화를 넌지시 언급한다."]], [["“Like those Hesperian gardens famed of old, Fortunate fields and groves and flowery vales, Thrice happy isles.”", "\"옛적에 이름난 저 헤스페리데스의 동산처럼, 행운의 들판과 숲과 꽃 피는 골짜기들, 세 곱절 행복한 섬들.\""]], [["And in Book II. he characterizes the rivers of Erebus according to the meaning of their names in the Greek language:", "그리고 제2권에서 그는 에레보스의 강들을 그 이름이 그리스어로 지니는 뜻에 따라 묘사한다."]], [["“Abhorred Styx, the flood of deadly hate, Sad Acheron of sorrow black and deep; Cocytus named of lamentation loud Heard on the rueful stream; fierce Phlegethon Whose waves of torrent fire inflame with rage. Far off from these a slow and silent stream, Lethe, the river of oblivion, rolls Her watery labyrinth, whereof who drinks Forthwith his former state and being forgets, Forgets both joy and grief, pleasure and pain.”", "\"치 떨리는 스틱스, 죽음 같은 증오의 강물, 슬픔에 잠긴 아케론, 검고 깊은 비탄의 강. 처량한 강물 위로 들려오는 통곡에서 이름 얻은 코키토스. 사나운 플레게톤, 그 격류 같은 불의 물결은 분노로 타오른다. 이것들에서 멀리 떨어져, 느리고 고요한 강물, 망각의 강 레테가 그 물결의 미궁을 굽이쳐 흐르니, 그 물을 마시는 자는 곧 자기의 옛 처지와 존재를 잊고, 기쁨도 슬픔도, 쾌락도 고통도 모두 잊는다.\" 시빌라"]], [["# THE SIBYL", "# THE SIBYL"]], [["As Æneas and the Sibyl pursued their way back to earth, he said to her, “Whether thou be a goddess or a mortal beloved of the gods, by me thou shalt always be held in reverence.", "아이네아스와 시빌라가 지상으로 돌아가는 길을 더듬어 가는 동안, 그가 그녀에게 말했다. \"그대가 여신이든, 아니면 신들의 사랑을 받는 필멸자이든, 나는 그대를 언제나 경외하며 받들 것이오."], ["When I reach the upper air I will cause a temple to be built to thy honor, and will myself bring offerings.”", "내가 위쪽 대기에 이르면 그대의 명예를 기려 신전을 짓게 하고, 내 손수 제물을 바치겠소.\" \"나는 여신이 아니오,\""], ["“I am no goddess,”", "하고 시빌라가 말했다."], ["said the Sibyl; “I have no claim to sacrifice or offering.", "\"나는 희생제물이나 봉헌물을 받을 자격이 없소. 나는 필멸자요."], ["I am mortal; yet if I could have accepted the love of Apollo I might have been immortal.", "그래도 아폴론의 사랑을 받아들일 수 있었다면 나는 불멸의 몸이 되었을지도 모르오."], ["He promised me the fulfilment of my wish, if I would consent to be his.", "그는 내가 자기 사람이 되기로 응한다면 내 소원을 이루어 주겠다고 약속했소. 나는 모래를 한 줌 쥐어 내밀며 말했소."], ["I took a handful of sand, and holding it forth, said, ‘Grant me to see as many birthdays as there are sand grains in my hand.’ Unluckily I forgot to ask for enduring youth.", "'내 손에 든 모래알의 수만큼 많은 생일을 보게 해 주소서.' 안타깝게도 나는 시들지 않는 젊음을 청하는 것을 잊고 말았소."], ["This also he would have granted, could I have accepted his love, but offended at my refusal, he allowed me to grow old.", "내가 그의 사랑을 받아들일 수 있었다면 그것도 그가 들어주었을 터이나, 내가 거절한 데 노여워하여 그는 내가 늙어 가도록 내버려 두었소."], ["My youth and youthful strength fled long ago.", "내 젊음과 젊은 날의 기운은 오래전에 사라졌소."], ["I have lived seven hundred years, and to equal the number of the sand grains I have still to see three hundred springs and three hundred harvests.", "나는 칠백 년을 살아왔으니, 모래알의 수를 채우려면 아직 삼백 번의 봄과 삼백 번의 추수를 더 보아야 하오."], ["My body shrinks up as years increase, and in time, I shall be lost to sight, but my voice will remain, and future ages will respect my sayings.”", "해가 거듭될수록 내 몸은 쪼그라들어, 머지않아 나는 눈에 보이지 않게 되겠지만, 내 목소리는 남을 것이며, 후세는 내 말을 존중할 것이오.\""]], [["These concluding words of the Sibyl alluded to her prophetic power.", "시빌라의 이 맺음말은 그녀의 예언 능력을 가리킨 것이었다."], ["In her cave she was accustomed to inscribe on leaves gathered from the trees the names and fates of individuals.", "그녀는 자기 동굴에서, 나무들에서 따 모은 잎사귀에 개개인의 이름과 운명을 적어 두곤 했다."], ["The leaves thus inscribed were arranged in order within the cave, and might be consulted by her votaries.", "이렇게 적은 잎사귀들은 동굴 안에 차례대로 정돈되어, 그녀를 찾는 신봉자들이 살펴볼 수 있었다."], ["But if perchance at the opening of the door the wind rushed in and dispersed the leaves the Sibyl gave no aid to restoring them again, and the oracle was irreparably lost.", "그러나 문이 열리는 순간 행여 바람이 밀려 들어와 잎사귀들을 흩어 놓으면, 시빌라는 그것들을 다시 추스르는 데 아무런 도움도 주지 않았고, 그리하여 그 신탁은 돌이킬 수 없이 사라져 버렸다."]], [["The following legend of the Sibyl is fixed at a later date.", "시빌라에 관한 다음의 전설은 그보다 후대에 자리한다."], ["In the reign of one of the Tarquins there appeared before the king a woman who offered him nine books for sale.", "타르퀴니우스 가운데 한 사람이 다스리던 시절, 한 여인이 왕 앞에 나타나 책 아홉 권을 팔겠다고 내놓았다."], ["The king refused to purchase them, whereupon the woman went away and burned three of the books, and returning offered the remaining books for the same price she had asked for the nine.", "왕이 사기를 거절하자, 그 여인은 물러가서 책 세 권을 불태웠고, 다시 돌아와 남은 책들을 아홉 권에 대해 청했던 것과 똑같은 값에 내놓았다."], ["The king again rejected them; but when the woman, after burning three books more, returned and asked for the three remaining the same price which she had before asked for the nine, his curiosity was excited, and he purchased the books.", "왕이 또다시 그것들을 물리치자, 여인은 책 세 권을 더 불태운 뒤 돌아와, 남은 세 권을 두고 앞서 아홉 권에 청했던 것과 똑같은 값을 요구했다. 그러자 왕의 호기심이 일었고, 그는 그 책들을 사들였다."], ["They were found to contain the destinies of the Roman state.", "그 책들에는 로마라는 나라의 운명이 담겨 있는 것으로 드러났다."], ["They were kept in the temple of Jupiter Capitolinus, preserved in a stone chest, and allowed to be inspected only by especial officers appointed for that duty, who, on great occasions, consulted them and interpreted their oracles to the people.", "그것들은 유피테르 카피톨리누스 신전에 돌로 만든 궤 속에 보관되었으며, 그 일을 위해 특별히 임명된 관리들만이 살펴보도록 허락되었으니, 그들은 큰일이 있을 때마다 그것들을 참조하여 그 신탁을 백성에게 풀이해 주었다."]], [["There were various Sibyls; but the Cumæan Sibyl, of whom Ovid and Virgil write, is the most celebrated of them.", "시빌라는 여럿이 있었으나, 오비디우스와 베르길리우스가 글로 쓴 쿠마이의 시빌라가 그 가운데 가장 이름난 자이다."], ["Ovid’s story of her life protracted to one thousand years may be intended to represent the various Sibyls as being only reappearances of one and the same individual.", "그녀의 생애가 천 년이나 길게 늘어났다는 오비디우스의 이야기는, 여러 시빌라가 실은 하나의 동일한 인물이 다시 나타난 것일 뿐임을 나타내려는 뜻이었는지도 모른다."]], [["Young, in the “Night Thoughts,” alludes to the Sibyl.", "영은 「밤의 상념」에서 시빌라를 넌지시 언급한다."], ["Speaking of Worldly Wisdom, he says:", "〈세속의 지혜〉에 대해 말하며 그는 이렇게 적는다."]], [["“If future fate she plans ’tis all in leaves, Like Sibyl, unsubstantial, fleeting bliss; At the first blast it vanishes in air.", "\"그것이 다가올 운명을 헤아린대도 모두가 잎사귀에 적힐 뿐, 시빌라처럼 실체 없고 덧없는 행복이라, 첫 바람이 한 번 불면 공기 속으로 스러진다."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], ["As worldly schemes resemble Sibyl’s leaves, The good man’s days to Sibyl’s books compare, The price still rising as in number less.”", "세속의 계략이 시빌라의 잎사귀를 닮았듯, 선한 이의 나날은 시빌라의 책들에 견줄 만하니, 그 수가 줄어들수록 값은 더욱 높아만 가더라.\""]], [["# CAMILLA—EVANDER—NISUS AND EURYALUS—MEZENTIUS—TURNUS", "# CAMILLA—EVANDER—NISUS AND EURYALUS—MEZENTIUS—TURNUS"]], [["ÆNEAS, having parted from the Sibyl and rejoined his fleet, coasted along the shores of Italy and cast anchor in the mouth of the Tiber.", "시빌라와 작별하고 자신의 함대로 돌아간 아이네아스는 이탈리아 해안을 따라 항해하다가 티베리스 강 어귀에 닻을 내렸다."], ["The poet, having brought his hero to this spot, the destined termination of his wanderings, invokes his Muse to tell him the situation of things at that eventful moment.", "시인은 자신의 영웅을 방랑의 정해진 종착지인 이곳까지 데려왔으니, 이 중대한 순간의 형세를 들려 달라며 무사 여신을 부른다."], ["Latinus, third in descent from Saturn, ruled the country.", "사투르누스의 삼대 후손인 라티누스가 이 나라를 다스리고 있었다."], ["He was now old and had no male descendant, but had one charming daughter, Lavinia, who was sought in marriage by many neighboring chiefs, one of whom, Turnus, king of the Rutulians, was favored by the wishes of her parents.", "그는 이제 늙었고 아들이 없었으나, 라비니아라는 매력적인 딸 하나를 두고 있었다. 라비니아에게는 이웃의 여러 족장들이 청혼해 왔는데, 그중 하나인 루툴리족의 왕 투르누스가 그녀 부모의 바람에 들어맞는 인물이었다."], ["But Latinus had been warned in a dream by his father Faunus, that the destined husband of Lavinia should come from a foreign land.", "그러나 라티누스는 꿈속에서 아버지 파우누스로부터, 라비니아의 정해진 남편은 외국 땅에서 오게 되리라는 경고를 받았다."], ["From that union should spring a race destined to subdue the world.", "그 결합으로부터 세계를 정복할 운명을 지닌 종족이 태어나리라는 것이었다."]], [["Our readers will remember that in the conflict with the Harpies one of those half-human birds had threatened the Trojans with dire sufferings.", "우리 독자들은 하르피이아이와의 싸움에서 그 반인반조의 새들 가운데 하나가 트로이아인들에게 끔찍한 고난을 예언했음을 기억할 것이다."], ["In particular she predicted that before their wanderings ceased they should be pressed by hunger to devour their tables.", "특히 그녀는 방랑이 끝나기 전에 그들이 굶주림에 쫓겨 자신들의 식탁마저 먹게 되리라고 예고했었다."], ["This portent now came true; for as they took their scanty meal, seated on the grass, the men placed their hard biscuit on their laps, and put thereon whatever their gleanings in the woods supplied.", "이제 그 전조가 실현되었다. 그들이 풀밭에 앉아 변변찮은 식사를 하면서, 사람들이 딱딱한 비스킷을 무릎에 올려놓고 그 위에 숲에서 채집한 것이라면 무엇이든 얹어 먹었기 때문이다."], ["Having despatched the latter they finished by eating the crusts.", "위에 얹은 것을 다 먹고 나자 그들은 마지막으로 빵 껍질까지 먹어 치웠다."], ["Seeing which, the boy Iulus said playfully, “See, we are eating our tables.”", "그것을 본 소년 이울루스가 장난스레 말했다. \"보세요, 우리가 우리 식탁을 먹고 있어요.\""], ["Æneas caught the words and accepted the omen.", "아이네아스는 그 말을 듣고 그것을 전조로 받아들였다."], ["“All hail, promised land!”", "\"약속의 땅이여, 만세!\""], ["he exclaimed, “this is our home, this our country.”", "그가 외쳤다. \"이곳이 우리의 거처요, 이곳이 우리의 나라다.\""], ["He then took measures to find out who were the present inhabitants of the land, and who their rulers.", "그러고 나서 그는 이 땅의 현재 주민이 누구이며 그 통치자가 누구인지 알아보기 위한 조치를 취했다."], ["A hundred chosen men were sent to the village of Latinus, bearing presents and a request for friendship and alliance.", "선발된 백 명이 선물과 더불어 우호와 동맹을 청하는 뜻을 지니고 라티누스의 마을로 파견되었다."], ["They went and were favorably received.", "그들은 그곳으로 가서 호의적인 영접을 받았다."], ["Latinus immediately concluded that the Trojan hero was no other than the promised son-in-law announced by the oracle.", "라티누스는 곧 그 트로이아 영웅이야말로 신탁이 예고한 약속의 사위가 틀림없다고 결론지었다."], ["He cheerfully granted his alliance and sent back the messengers mounted on steeds from his stables, and loaded with gifts and friendly messages.", "그는 기꺼이 동맹을 허락하고, 자기 마구간의 말에 사절들을 태워 선물과 우호의 전갈을 가득 실어 돌려보냈다."]], [["Juno, seeing things go thus prosperously for the Trojans, felt her old animosity revive, summoned Alecto from Erebus, and sent her to stir up discord.", "헤라[로마의 〈유노〉]는 트로이아인들에게 이렇듯 일이 순조롭게 풀리는 것을 보고 묵은 적의가 되살아남을 느꼈으니, 에레보스에서 알렉토를 불러내어 불화를 일으키도록 보냈다."], ["The Fury first took possession of the queen, Amata, and roused her to oppose in every way the new alliance.", "복수의 여신은 먼저 왕비 아마타를 사로잡아, 그 새로운 동맹에 온갖 방법으로 반대하도록 부추겼다."], ["Alecto then speeded to the city of Turnus, and assuming the form of an old priestess, informed him of the arrival of the foreigners and of the attempts of their prince to rob him of his bride.", "그런 다음 알렉토는 투르누스의 도시로 서둘러 가서, 늙은 여사제의 모습을 하고는 이방인들이 도착한 사실과 그들의 군주가 그의 신부를 빼앗으려 한다는 것을 알렸다."], ["Next she turned her attention to the camp of the Trojans.", "다음으로 그녀는 트로이아인들의 진영으로 주의를 돌렸다."], ["There she saw the boy Iulus and his companions amusing themselves with hunting.", "그곳에서 그녀는 소년 이울루스와 그 동료들이 사냥으로 즐기고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["She sharpened the scent of the dogs, and led them to rouse up from the thicket a tame stag, the favorite of Silvia, the daughter of Tyrrheus, the king’s herdsman.", "그녀는 개들의 후각을 예민하게 만들어, 왕의 목자인 티르레우스의 딸 실비아가 아끼는 길든 수사슴을 덤불에서 몰아내게 했다."], ["A javelin from the hand of Iulus wounded the animal, and he had only strength left to run homewards, and died at his mistress’s feet.", "이울루스의 손에서 날아간 투창이 그 짐승에게 상처를 입혔고, 사슴은 가까스로 남은 힘으로 집으로 달려가 여주인의 발치에서 죽었다."], ["Her cries and tears roused her brothers and the herdsmen, and they, seizing whatever weapons came to hand, furiously assaulted the hunting party.", "그녀의 울부짖음과 눈물에 그녀의 형제들과 목자들이 분노하여, 손에 잡히는 무기란 무기는 모두 집어 들고 사냥하던 무리를 맹렬히 공격했다."], ["These were protected by their friends, and the herdsmen were finally driven back with the loss of two of their number.", "이들은 동료들의 보호를 받았고, 결국 목자들은 두 명을 잃고 격퇴되었다."]], [["These things were enough to rouse the storm of war, and the queen, Turnus, and the peasants all urged the old king to drive the strangers from the country.", "이 일들은 전쟁의 폭풍을 일으키기에 충분했으니, 왕비와 투르누스와 농부들이 모두 늙은 왕에게 이방인들을 나라에서 몰아내라고 다그쳤다."], ["He resisted as long as he could, but, finding his opposition unavailing, finally gave way and retreated to his retirement.", "그는 할 수 있는 한 버텼으나, 자신의 반대가 소용없음을 깨닫고는 마침내 굴복하여 은거지로 물러났다."]], [["# OPENING THE GATES OF JANUS", "# OPENING THE GATES OF JANUS"]], [["It was the custom of the country, when war was to be undertaken, for the chief magistrate, clad in his robes of office, with solemn pomp to open the gates of the temple of Janus, which were kept shut as long as peace endured.", "이 나라의 관습에 따르면, 전쟁을 일으키게 될 때면 최고 행정관이 관복을 차려입고 엄숙한 의식과 함께 야누스 신전의 문을 여는 것이 관례였는데, 이 문은 평화가 이어지는 동안에는 닫혀 있었다."], ["His people now urged the old king to perform that solemn office, but he refused to do so.", "이제 백성들이 늙은 왕에게 그 엄숙한 의식을 거행하라고 다그쳤으나, 그는 거부했다."], ["While they contested, Juno herself, descending from the skies, smote the doors with irresistible force, and burst them open.", "그들이 다투는 사이에 헤라가 친히 하늘에서 내려와 거역할 수 없는 힘으로 그 문을 내리쳐 부서뜨려 활짝 열어젖혔다."], ["Immediately the whole country was in a flame.", "곧바로 온 나라가 불길에 휩싸였다."], ["The people rushed from every side breathing nothing but war.", "백성들이 사방에서 몰려들어 오직 전쟁만을 외쳐 댔다."]], [["Turnus was recognized by all as leader; others joined as allies, chief of whom was Mezentius, a brave and able soldier, but of detestable cruelty.", "투르누스가 모두에게 지도자로 인정받았고, 다른 이들이 동맹으로 가세했는데, 그 우두머리는 메젠티우스였다. 그는 용맹하고 유능한 군인이었으나 가증스러울 만큼 잔인했다."], ["He had been the chief of one of the neighboring cities, but his people drove him out.", "그는 이웃 도시들 가운데 하나의 우두머리였으나 백성들이 그를 쫓아냈다."], ["With him was joined his son Lausus, a generous youth, worthy of a better sire.", "그와 함께한 이는 그의 아들 라우수스로, 그보다 나은 아비를 둘 만한 너그러운 젊은이였다."]], [["Camilla, the favorite of Diana, a huntress and warrior, after the fashion of the Amazons, came with her band of mounted followers, including a select number of her own sex, and ranged herself on the side of Turnus.", "아르테미스가 아끼는 카밀라는 아마존족의 방식을 따르는 여자 사냥꾼이자 전사로, 자기와 같은 여성들 가운데 선발된 자들을 포함한 기마 추종자들의 무리를 거느리고 와서 투르누스의 편에 섰다."], ["This maiden had never accustomed her fingers to the distaff or the loom, but had learned to endure the toils of war, and in speed to outstrip the wind.", "이 처녀는 손가락을 실패나 베틀에 길들인 적이 없었고, 다만 전쟁의 노고를 견디는 법과 바람보다 빠르게 달리는 법을 배웠다."], ["It seemed as if she might run over the standing corn without crushing it, or over the surface of the water without dipping her feet.", "그녀는 곡식을 짓밟지 않고 그 위를 달릴 수 있을 것 같았고, 발을 적시지 않고 물 위를 스쳐 지날 수 있을 것만 같았다."], ["Camilla’s history had been singular from the beginning.", "카밀라의 내력은 처음부터 남달랐다."], ["Her father, Metabus, driven from his city by civil discord, carried with him in his flight his infant daughter.", "그녀의 아버지 메타부스는 내란으로 자기 도시에서 쫓겨나, 달아나는 길에 갓난 딸을 데리고 갔다."], ["As he fled through the woods, his enemies in hot pursuit, he reached the bank of the river Amazenus, which, swelled by rains, seemed to debar a passage.", "그가 숲을 가로질러 달아나는데 적들이 바짝 뒤쫓았고, 그는 아마제누스 강기슭에 이르렀으니, 강물은 비로 불어나 건널 길을 막은 듯했다."], ["He paused for a moment, then decided what to do.", "그는 잠시 멈췄다가 어떻게 할지 결심했다."], ["He tied the infant to his lance with wrappers of bark, and poising the weapon in his upraised hand thus addressed Diana: “Goddess of the woods!", "그는 나무껍질 띠로 갓난아이를 자기 창에 묶고, 그 무기를 치켜든 손에 가늠하며 아르테미스에게 이렇게 아뢰었다. \"숲의 여신이여!"], ["I consecrate this maid to you;”", "이 아이를 당신께 바칩니다.\""], ["then hurled the weapon with its burden to the opposite bank.", "그러고는 짐을 실은 그 무기를 맞은편 기슭으로 던졌다."], ["The spear flew across the roaring water.", "창은 울부짖는 물살을 가로질러 날아갔다."], ["His pursuers were already upon him, but he plunged into the river and swam across, and found the spear, with the infant safe on the other side.", "추격자들은 이미 그에게 들이닥쳤으나, 그는 강물에 뛰어들어 헤엄쳐 건너서, 갓난아이를 무사히 실은 창을 맞은편에서 찾아냈다."], ["Thenceforth he lived among the shepherds and brought up his daughter in woodland arts.", "그때부터 그는 목자들 사이에서 살며 딸을 숲의 기예 속에서 길렀다."], ["While a child she was taught to use the bow and throw the javelin.", "아이일 적에 그녀는 활을 쓰고 투창을 던지는 법을 배웠다."], ["With her sling she could bring down the crane or the wild swan.", "투석기로 그녀는 두루미나 들고니를 떨어뜨릴 수 있었다."], ["Her dress was a tiger’s skin.", "그녀의 옷은 호랑이 가죽이었다."], ["Many mothers sought her for a daughter-in-law, but she continued faithful to Diana and repelled the thought of marriage.", "많은 어머니들이 그녀를 며느리로 삼고자 했으나, 그녀는 아르테미스에게 충실하여 혼인이라는 생각을 물리쳤다."]], [["Such were the formidable allies that ranged themselves against Æneas.", "이러한 자들이 아이네아스에 맞서 늘어선 만만찮은 동맹이었다."], ["It was night and he lay stretched in sleep on the bank of the river under the open heavens.", "밤이었고, 그는 탁 트인 하늘 아래 강기슭에 몸을 뻗고 잠들어 있었다."], ["The god of the stream, Father Tiber, seemed to raise his head above the willows and to say, “O goddess-born, destined possessor of the Latin realms, this is the promised land, here is to be your home, here shall terminate the hostility of the heavenly powers, if only you faithfully persevere.", "강의 신, 티베리스 아버지가 버드나무 위로 머리를 들어 올리고 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. \"오 여신에게서 난 자여, 라티움 왕국의 정해진 주인이여, 이곳이 약속의 땅이며, 여기가 그대의 거처가 될 곳이요, 그대가 오직 충실히 견뎌 내기만 한다면 하늘의 권능들이 품은 적의도 여기서 끝나리라."], ["There are friends not far distant.", "멀지 않은 곳에 벗들이 있노라."], ["Prepare your boats and row up my stream; I will lead you to Evander, the Arcadian chief, he has long been at strife with Turnus and the Rutulians, and is prepared to become an ally of yours.", "그대의 배를 준비하여 내 물길을 거슬러 노를 저어라. 내가 그대를 아르카디아의 족장 에우안데르에게 인도하리니, 그는 오랫동안 투르누스와 루툴리족과 다투어 왔으며 그대의 동맹이 될 채비가 되어 있노라."], ["Rise!", "일어나라!"], ["offer your vows to Juno, and deprecate her anger.", "헤라에게 서약을 바쳐 그 노여움을 누그러뜨려라."], ["When you have achieved your victory then think of me.”", "그대가 승리를 이루거든 그때 나를 생각하라.\""], ["Æneas woke and paid immediate obedience to the friendly vision.", "아이네아스는 잠에서 깨어 그 우호적인 환영에 곧바로 따랐다."], ["He sacrificed to Juno, and invoked the god of the river and all his tributary fountains to lend their aid.", "그는 헤라에게 제물을 바치고, 강의 신과 그에게 흘러드는 모든 샘에게 도움을 빌었다."], ["Then for the first time a vessel filled with armed warriors floated on the stream of the Tiber.", "그러자 처음으로 무장한 전사들을 가득 실은 배가 티베리스 강물 위에 떠올랐다."], ["The river smoothed its waves, and bade its current flow gently, while, impelled by the vigorous strokes of the rowers, the vessels shot rapidly up the stream.", "강은 그 물결을 잔잔히 가라앉히고 물살이 부드럽게 흐르도록 했으니, 노 젓는 이들의 힘찬 노질에 떠밀려 배들은 강을 거슬러 빠르게 나아갔다."]], [["About the middle of the day they came in sight of the scattered buildings of the infant town, where in after times the proud city of Rome grew, whose glory reached the skies.", "한낮 무렵 그들은 갓 생겨난 마을의 띄엄띄엄 흩어진 건물들이 보이는 곳에 이르렀으니, 훗날 이곳에서 영광이 하늘에 닿는 자랑스러운 도시 로마가 자라나게 된다."], ["By chance the old king, Evander, was that day celebrating annual solemnities in honor of Hercules and all the gods.", "마침 그날 늙은 왕 에우안데르는 헤라클레스와 모든 신들을 기리는 연례 제전을 거행하고 있었다."], ["Pallas, his son, and all the chiefs of the little commonwealth stood by.", "그의 아들 팔라스와 이 작은 공동체의 모든 족장들이 곁에 서 있었다."], ["When they saw the tall ship gliding onward near the wood, they were alarmed at the sight, and rose from the tables.", "그들은 숲 가까이로 미끄러져 오는 큰 배를 보고 그 광경에 놀라 식탁에서 일어났다."], ["But Pallas forbade the solemnities to be interrupted, and seizing a weapon, stepped forward to the river’s bank.", "그러나 팔라스는 제전이 중단되는 것을 막고, 무기를 집어 들고 강기슭으로 앞장서 나아갔다."], ["He called aloud, demanding who they were, and what their object.", "그는 큰 소리로 그들이 누구이며 무슨 목적으로 왔는지 따져 물었다."], ["Æneas, holding forth an olive-branch, replied, “We are Trojans, friends to you, and enemies to the Rutulians.", "아이네아스는 올리브 가지를 내밀며 대답했다. \"우리는 트로이아인이며, 그대들에게는 벗이요 루툴리족에게는 적이오."], ["We seek Evander, and offer to join our arms with yours.”", "우리는 에우안데르를 찾아왔으며, 우리의 무기를 그대들의 무기에 합치고자 하오.\""], ["Pallas, in amaze at the sound of so great a name, invited them to land, and when Æneas touched the shore he seized his hand, and held it long in friendly grasp.", "그토록 위대한 이름의 울림에 놀란 팔라스는 그들에게 상륙하기를 청했고, 아이네아스가 기슭에 닿자 그의 손을 잡아 오래도록 우정 어린 악수로 쥐고 있었다."], ["Proceeding through the wood, they joined the king and his party and were most favorably received.", "숲을 지나 나아가 그들은 왕과 그 일행에 합류하여 더할 나위 없이 호의적인 영접을 받았다."], ["Seats were provided for them at the tables, and the repast proceeded.", "식탁에 그들을 위한 자리가 마련되었고, 잔치가 이어졌다."]], [["# INFANT ROME", "# INFANT ROME"]], [["When the solemnities were ended all moved towards the city.", "제전이 끝나자 모두 도시를 향해 움직였다."], ["The king, bending with age, walked between his son and Æneas, taking the arm of one or the other of them, and with much variety of pleasing talk shortening the way.", "나이로 허리가 굽은 왕은 아들과 아이네아스 사이에서 걸으며 둘 중 한쪽의 팔을 번갈아 잡았고, 갖가지 흥겨운 이야기로 길을 짧게 했다."], ["Æneas with delight looked and listened, observing all the beauties of the scene, and learning much of heroes renowned in ancient times.", "아이네아스는 기쁜 마음으로 보고 들으며, 그 풍경의 온갖 아름다움을 살피고, 옛 시절에 이름 높았던 영웅들에 관해 많은 것을 알게 되었다."], ["Evander said, “These extensive groves were once inhabited by fauns and nymphs, and a rude race of men who sprang from the trees themselves, and had neither laws nor social culture.", "에우안데르가 말했다. \"이 드넓은 숲은 한때 파우누스들과 님프들, 그리고 나무 자체에서 솟아나 법도 사회적 교양도 없던 거친 인간 종족이 살던 곳이오."], ["They knew not how to yoke the cattle nor raise a harvest, nor provide from present abundance for future want; but browsed like beasts upon the leafy boughs, or fed voraciously on their hunted prey.", "그들은 소에 멍에를 메우는 법도, 수확을 거두는 법도, 지금의 풍요에서 훗날의 궁핍에 대비하는 법도 알지 못했고, 다만 짐승처럼 잎이 무성한 가지를 뜯거나 사냥한 먹이를 게걸스레 먹을 따름이었소."], ["Such were they when Saturn, expelled from Olympus by his sons, came among them and drew together the fierce savages, formed them into society, and gave them laws.", "자식들에게 올림포스에서 쫓겨난 사투르누스가 그들에게 와서 그 사나운 야만인들을 한데 모아 사회를 이루게 하고 법을 내렸을 때, 그들은 그러한 처지였소."], ["Such peace and plenty ensued that men ever since have called his reign the golden age; but by degrees far other times succeeded, and the thirst of gold and the thirst of blood prevailed.", "그리하여 평화와 풍요가 뒤따랐기에 그 후로 사람들은 그의 치세를 황금시대라 불러 왔소. 그러나 점차 사뭇 다른 시절이 뒤를 이었고, 황금에 대한 갈증과 피에 대한 갈증이 만연했소."], ["The land was a prey to successive tyrants, till fortune and resistless destiny brought me hither, an exile from my native land, Arcadia.”", "이 땅은 잇따른 폭군들의 먹잇감이 되었으니, 마침내 운명과 거역할 수 없는 숙명이 내 고향 아르카디아에서 추방된 나를 이곳으로 데려왔소.\""]], [["Having thus said, he showed him the Tarpeian rock, and the rude spot then overgrown with bushes where in after times the Capitol rose in all its magnificence.", "이렇게 말하고 나서 그는 타르페이아 바위와, 훗날 카피톨리움이 그 온갖 장려함으로 솟아오르게 되나 당시에는 덤불로 뒤덮인 그 거친 자리를 그에게 보여 주었다."], ["He next pointed to some dismantled walls, and said, “Here stood Janiculum, built by Janus, and there Saturnia, the town of Saturn.”", "다음으로 그는 허물어진 성벽 몇 곳을 가리키며 말했다. \"여기에는 야누스가 세운 야니쿨룸이 있었고, 저기에는 사투르누스의 도시인 사투르니아가 있었소.\""], ["Such discourse brought them to the cottage of poor Evander, whence they saw the lowing herds roaming over the plain where now the proud and stately Forum stands.", "이러한 이야기를 나누며 그들은 가난한 에우안데르의 오두막에 이르렀으니, 그곳에서 그들은 지금 자랑스럽고 위풍당당한 포룸이 서 있는 평원 위로 음매 우는 가축 떼가 거니는 것을 보았다."], ["They entered, and a couch was spread for Æneas, well stuffed with leaves, and covered with the skin of a Libyan bear.", "그들은 안으로 들어갔고, 아이네아스를 위해 침상이 펼쳐졌는데, 잎으로 두둑이 채우고 리비아 곰의 가죽으로 덮은 것이었다."]], [["Next morning, awakened by the dawn and the shrill song of birds beneath the eaves of his low mansion, old Evander rose.", "이튿날 아침, 새벽빛과 그의 나지막한 집 처마 밑 새들의 날카로운 노래에 잠을 깬 늙은 에우안데르가 일어났다."], ["Clad in a tunic, and a panther’s skin thrown over his shoulders, with sandals on his feet and his good sword girded to his side, he went forth to seek his guest.", "튜닉을 입고 표범 가죽을 어깨에 두르고, 발에는 샌들을 신고 옆구리에는 자신의 훌륭한 검을 차고서 그는 손님을 찾으러 나섰다."], ["Two mastiffs followed him, his whole retinue and body guard.", "마스티프 두 마리가 그를 따랐으니, 이것이 그의 모든 수행원이자 호위였다."], ["He found the hero attended by his faithful Achates, and, Pallas soon joining them, the old king spoke thus:", "그는 충실한 아카테스가 시중드는 영웅을 발견했고, 팔라스도 이내 그들과 합류하자 늙은 왕이 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“Illustrious Trojan, it is but little we can do in so great a cause.", "\"고명한 트로이아인이여, 이토록 큰 대의에 우리가 할 수 있는 일은 미미하기 그지없소."], ["Our state is feeble, hemmed in on one side by the river, on the other by the Rutulians.", "우리 나라는 미약하여, 한편은 강에, 다른 한편은 루툴리족에 둘러싸여 있소."], ["But I propose to ally you with a people numerous and rich, to whom fate has brought you at the propitious moment.", "그러나 나는 그대를 수가 많고 부유한 백성과 동맹 맺게 하려 하니, 운명이 그대를 길한 때에 이리로 데려온 것이오."], ["The Etruscans hold the country beyond the river.", "에트루리아인들이 강 너머의 땅을 차지하고 있소."], ["Mezentius was their king, a monster of cruelty, who invented unheard-of torments to gratify his vengeance.", "메젠티우스가 그들의 왕이었으니, 잔인함의 괴물이었고 자신의 복수심을 채우려 전대미문의 고문들을 고안해 냈소."], ["He would fasten the dead to the living, hand to hand and face to face, and leave the wretched victims to die in that dreadful embrace.", "그는 죽은 자를 산 자에게, 손과 손을, 얼굴과 얼굴을 맞대어 붙들어 매고는, 그 비참한 희생자들을 그 끔찍한 포옹 속에서 죽게 내버려 두었소."], ["At length the people cast him out, him and his house.", "마침내 백성들이 그를, 그와 그의 집안을 내쫓았소."], ["They burned his palace and slew his friends.", "그들은 그의 궁전을 불태우고 그의 벗들을 죽였소."], ["He escaped and took refuge with Turnus, who protects him with arms.", "그는 달아나 투르누스에게 몸을 의탁했으니, 투르누스가 무력으로 그를 비호하고 있소."], ["The Etruscans demand that he shall be given up to deserved punishment, and would ere now have attempted to enforce their demand; but their priests restrain them, telling them that it is the will of heaven that no native of the land shall guide them to victory, and that their destined leader must come from across the sea.", "에트루리아인들은 그를 마땅한 벌을 받도록 넘기라고 요구하며, 진작에 그 요구를 무력으로 관철하려 했을 것이오. 그러나 그들의 사제들이 그들을 만류하기를, 이 땅의 어떤 토박이도 그들을 승리로 이끌어서는 안 되며 그들의 정해진 지도자는 바다 건너에서 와야 하는 것이 하늘의 뜻이라 하오."], ["They have offered the crown to me, but I am too old to undertake such great affairs, and my son is native-born, which precludes him from the choice.", "그들은 내게 왕관을 바쳤으나, 나는 그토록 큰일을 떠맡기에는 너무 늙었고, 내 아들은 이 땅에서 태어난 까닭에 선택에서 제외되오."], ["You, equally by birth and time of life, and fame in arms, pointed out by the gods, have but to appear to be hailed at once as their leader.", "그대는 가문으로 보나 나이로 보나 무용의 명성으로 보나, 신들이 지목한 자이니, 모습을 드러내기만 하면 곧 그들의 지도자로 환영받을 것이오."], ["With you I will join Pallas, my son, my only hope and comfort.", "그대와 함께 나는 내 아들이자 내 유일한 희망이요 위안인 팔라스를 보내겠소."], ["Under you he shall learn the art of war, and strive to emulate your great exploits.”", "그대 밑에서 그는 전쟁의 기술을 배우고 그대의 위대한 위업을 본받고자 힘쓸 것이오.\""]], [["Then the king ordered horses to be furnished for the Trojan chiefs, and Æneas, with a chosen band of followers and Pallas accompanying, mounted and took the way to the Etruscan city,[33] having sent back the rest of his party in the ships.", "그러고 나서 왕은 트로이아 족장들을 위해 말을 마련하라 명했고, 아이네아스는 선발된 추종자 무리와 동행하는 팔라스와 함께 말에 올라 에트루리아 도시로 가는 길을 잡았으며, 나머지 일행은 배에 태워 돌려보냈다."], ["Æneas and his band safely arrived at the Etruscan camp and were received with open arms by Tarchon and his countrymen.", "아이네아스와 그의 무리는 무사히 에트루리아 진영에 다다라 타르콘과 그의 동포들에게서 두 팔 벌린 환영을 받았다. * 에트루리아 도시: 아이네아스가 향한 곳."]], [["# NISUS AND EURYALUS", "# NISUS AND EURYALUS"]], [["In the meanwhile Turnus had collected his bands and made all necessary preparations for the war.", "그러는 사이에 투르누스는 자신의 부대를 모아 전쟁에 필요한 모든 준비를 갖추었다."], ["Juno sent Iris to him with a message inciting him to take advantage of the absence of Æneas and surprise the Trojan camp.", "헤라는 이리스를 그에게 보내, 아이네아스가 자리를 비운 틈을 타 트로이아 진영을 기습하라고 부추기는 전갈을 전했다."], ["Accordingly the attempt was made, but the Trojans were found on their guard, and having received strict orders from Æneas not to fight in his absence, they lay still in their intrenchments, and resisted all the efforts of the Rutulians to draw them into the field.", "그리하여 그 시도가 이루어졌으나, 트로이아인들은 경계를 늦추지 않고 있었으니, 자신이 없는 동안에는 싸우지 말라는 아이네아스의 엄명을 받았던 터라, 그들은 보루 안에 가만히 머물며 자신들을 들판으로 끌어내려는 루툴리족의 온갖 시도에 맞섰다."], ["Night coming on, the army of Turnus, in high spirits at their fancied superiority, feasted and enjoyed themselves, and finally stretched themselves on the field and slept secure.", "밤이 오자, 자기들이 우세하다고 여겨 사기가 한껏 오른 투르누스의 군대는 잔치를 벌이며 즐기다가, 마침내 들판에 몸을 뻗고 마음 놓고 잠들었다."]], [["In the camp of the Trojans things were far otherwise.", "트로이아인들의 진영에서는 사정이 사뭇 달랐다."], ["There all was watchfulness and anxiety and impatience for Æneas’s return.", "그곳은 온통 경계와 불안과 아이네아스의 귀환을 향한 초조함뿐이었다."], ["Nisus stood guard at the entrance of the camp, and Euryalus, a youth distinguished above all in the army for graces of person and fine qualities, was with him.", "니수스가 진영 어귀에서 보초를 서고 있었고, 군대에서 누구보다 빼어난 용모와 훌륭한 자질로 두드러진 젊은이 에우리알루스가 그와 함께 있었다."], ["These two were friends and brothers in arms.", "이 둘은 벗이자 무기를 같이하는 형제였다."], ["Nisus said to his friend, “Do you perceive what confidence and carelessness the enemy display?", "니수스가 자기 벗에게 말했다. \"적들이 얼마나 자만하고 부주의한지 자네 눈에 보이는가?"], ["Their lights are few and dim, and the men seem all oppressed with wine or sleep.", "그들의 불빛은 적고 희미하며, 사내들은 모두 술이나 잠에 짓눌린 것 같네."], ["You know how anxiously our chiefs wish to send to Æneas, and to get intelligence from him.", "자네도 알다시피 우리 족장들이 아이네아스에게 사람을 보내 그분의 소식을 얻기를 얼마나 애타게 바라는지 말일세."], ["Now, I am strongly moved to make my way through the enemy’s camp and to go in search of our chief.", "지금 나는 적진을 뚫고 나아가 우리 족장을 찾아 나서고 싶은 마음이 강하게 이네."], ["If I succeed, the glory of the deed will be reward enough for me, and if they judge the service deserves anything more, let them pay it to you.”", "내가 성공한다면 그 공적의 영광만으로도 내게는 충분한 보답이 될 것이고, 만일 그들이 그 봉사가 그 이상의 무언가에 값한다고 여긴다면 그것을 자네에게 치르게 하면 되네.\""]], [["Euryalus, all on fire with the love of adventure, replied, “Would you, then, Nisus, refuse to share your enterprise with me?", "모험을 향한 애착으로 온통 불타오른 에우리알루스가 대답했다. \"그렇다면 니수스, 자네는 자네의 거사를 나와 함께하기를 마다하겠다는 건가?"], ["And shall I let you go into such danger alone?", "그리고 내가 자네 혼자 그런 위험 속으로 들어가게 내버려 두겠는가?"], ["Not so my brave father brought me up, nor so have I planned for myself when I joined the standard of Æneas, and resolved to hold my life cheap in comparison with honor.”", "내 용맹한 아버지가 나를 그리 기르지 않으셨고, 내가 아이네아스의 깃발 아래 들어와 명예에 비하면 내 목숨을 하찮게 여기리라 다짐했을 때 나 자신을 위해 그리 계획하지도 않았네.\" 니수스가 대답했다."], ["Nisus replied, “I doubt it not, my friend; but you know the uncertain event of such an undertaking, and whatever may happen to me, I wish you to be safe.", "\"벗이여, 나도 그것을 의심하지 않네. 그러나 자네도 알다시피 그런 거사의 결말은 불확실하니, 내게 무슨 일이 닥치든 나는 자네가 무사하기를 바라네."], ["You are younger than I and have more of life in prospect.", "자네는 나보다 젊고 앞날이 더 많이 남아 있네."], ["Nor can I be the cause of such grief to your mother, who has chosen to be here in the camp with you rather than stay and live in peace with the other matrons in Acestes’ city.”", "또한 나는 자네 어머니께 그런 슬픔을 끼치는 까닭이 될 수 없으니, 그분은 아케스테스의 도시에서 다른 부인들과 평화롭게 지내기보다 여기 진영에서 자네와 함께 있기를 택하신 분이 아닌가.\""], ["Euryalus replied, “Say no more.", "에우리알루스가 대답했다. \"더는 말하지 말게."], ["In vain you seek arguments to dissuade me.", "나를 단념시킬 논거를 찾아 봐야 헛일이네."], ["I am fixed in the resolution to go with you.", "나는 자네와 함께 가기로 마음을 굳혔네."], ["Let us lose no time.”", "시간을 허비하지 마세.\""], ["They called the guard, and committing the watch to them, sought the general’s tent.", "그들은 보초들을 불러 경계를 맡기고 장군의 막사를 찾아갔다."], ["They found the chief officers in consultation, deliberating how they should send notice to Æneas of their situation.", "그들은 수석 장교들이 모여, 자신들의 처지를 어떻게 아이네아스에게 알릴지 의논하고 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["The offer of the two friends was gladly accepted, themselves loaded with praises and promised the most liberal rewards in case of success.", "두 벗의 제안은 기쁘게 받아들여졌으니, 그들 자신은 찬사를 한 몸에 받고 성공할 경우 더없이 후한 보상을 약속받았다."], ["Iulus especially addressed Euryalus, assuring him of his lasting friendship.", "특히 이울루스가 에우리알루스에게 말을 건네며 그에게 변함없는 우정을 다짐했다."], ["Euryalus replied, “I have but one boon to ask.", "에우리알루스가 대답했다. \"제게는 청할 은혜가 하나뿐입니다."], ["My aged mother is with me in the camp.", "제 늙으신 어머니가 저와 함께 이 진영에 계십니다."], ["For me she left the Trojan soil, and would not stay behind with the other matrons at the city of Acestes.", "저를 위해 어머니는 트로이아 땅을 떠나셨고, 아케스테스의 도시에서 다른 부인들과 함께 남으려 하지 않으셨습니다."], ["I go now without taking leave of her.", "저는 이제 어머니께 작별 인사도 드리지 않고 떠납니다."], ["I could not bear her tears nor set at nought her entreaties.", "어머니의 눈물을 차마 견딜 수 없고, 그 애원을 저버릴 수도 없습니다."], ["But do thou, I beseech you, comfort her in her distress.", "그러니 부디 그분이 비탄에 잠기시거든 위로해 주시기를 간청드립니다."], ["Promise me that and I shall go more boldly into whatever dangers may present themselves.”", "제게 그것을 약속해 주신다면, 저는 어떤 위험이 닥치든 더욱 담대하게 나아가겠습니다.\""], ["Iulus and the other chiefs were moved to tears, and promised to do all his request.", "이울루스와 다른 족장들은 눈물을 흘리며 그의 청을 모두 들어주겠노라 약속했다."], ["“Your mother shall be mine,”", "\"그대의 어머니는 내 어머니가 될 것이오.\""], ["said Iulus, “and all that I have promised to you shall be made good to her, if you do not return to receive it.”", "이울루스가 말했다. \"그리고 내가 그대에게 약속한 모든 것은, 만일 그대가 돌아와 받지 못하게 되더라도 그분께 어김없이 베풀어질 것이오.\""]], [["The two friends left the camp and plunged at once into the midst of the enemy.", "두 벗은 진영을 떠나 곧장 적들 한가운데로 뛰어들었다."], ["They found no watch, no sentinels posted, but, all about, the sleeping soldiers strewn on the grass and among the wagons.", "그들은 보초도, 배치된 파수꾼도 보지 못했고, 다만 사방에 잠든 병사들이 풀밭과 짐수레 사이에 흩어져 있을 따름이었다."], ["The laws of war at that early day did not forbid a brave man to slay a sleeping foe, and the two Trojans slew, as they passed, such of the enemy as they could without exciting alarm.", "그 먼 옛날의 전쟁 법도는 용감한 자가 잠든 적을 죽이는 것을 금하지 않았으므로, 두 트로이아인은 지나는 길에 경보를 울리지 않고 죽일 수 있는 적들은 죽였다."], ["In one tent Euryalus made prize of a helmet brilliant with gold and plumes.", "한 막사에서 에우리알루스는 금과 깃털로 번쩍이는 투구를 전리품으로 챙겼다."], ["They had passed through the enemy’s ranks without being discovered, but now suddenly appeared a troop directly in front of them, which, under Volscens, their leader, were approaching the camp.", "그들은 발각되지 않고 적의 대열을 지나왔으나, 이제 갑자기 그들 바로 앞에 한 무리가 나타났으니, 그들의 지휘관 볼스켄스 휘하로 진영을 향해 다가오고 있었다."], ["The glittering helmet of Euryalus caught their attention, and Volscens hailed the two, and demanded who and whence they were.", "에우리알루스의 번쩍이는 투구가 그들의 눈길을 끌었고, 볼스켄스가 그 둘을 불러 세워 누구이며 어디서 왔는지 따져 물었다."], ["They made no answer, but plunged into the wood.", "그들은 아무 대답도 하지 않고 숲속으로 뛰어들었다."], ["The horsemen scattered in all directions to intercept their flight.", "기마병들이 그들의 도주를 가로막으려 사방으로 흩어졌다."], ["Nisus had eluded pursuit and was out of danger, but Euryalus being missing he turned back to seek him.", "니수스는 추격을 따돌리고 위험에서 벗어났으나, 에우리알루스가 보이지 않자 그를 찾으러 되돌아갔다."], ["He again entered the wood and soon came within sound of voices.", "그는 다시 숲으로 들어가 이내 사람들의 목소리가 들리는 곳에 이르렀다."], ["Looking through the thicket he saw the whole band surrounding Euryalus with noisy questions.", "덤불 사이로 내다보니 그 무리 전체가 에우리알루스를 에워싸고 시끄럽게 캐묻고 있었다."], ["What should he do?", "그는 어찌해야 할까?"], ["how extricate the youth, or would it be better to die with him.", "어떻게 젊은이를 빼낼 것인가, 아니면 차라리 그와 함께 죽는 편이 나을까."]], [["Raising his eyes to the moon, which now shone clear, he said, “Goddess!", "이제 환하게 빛나는 달을 향해 눈을 들어 올리며 그가 말했다. \"여신이여!"], ["favor my effort!”", "제 노력을 도우소서!\""], ["and aiming his javelin at one of the leaders of the troop, struck him in the back and stretched him on the plain with a deathblow.", "그러고는 그 무리의 지휘관들 가운데 하나를 겨누어 투창을 던졌으니, 그 등을 맞혀 치명상으로 평원에 쓰러뜨렸다."], ["In the midst of their amazement another weapon flew and another of the party fell dead.", "그들이 경악하는 가운데 또 하나의 무기가 날아가 무리 가운데 또 한 명이 죽어 쓰러졌다."], ["Volscens, the leader, ignorant whence the darts came, rushed sword in hand upon Euryalus.", "지휘관 볼스켄스는 그 화살이 어디서 날아오는지 알지 못한 채, 검을 손에 들고 에우리알루스에게 달려들었다."], ["“You shall pay the penalty of both,”", "\"네가 둘 모두의 대가를 치르리라.\""], ["he said, and would have plunged the sword into his bosom, when Nisus, who from his concealment saw the peril of his friend, rushed forward exclaiming, “’Twas I, ’twas I; turn your swords against me, Rutulians, I did it; he only followed me as a friend.”", "그가 말하고는 검을 그의 가슴에 찔러 넣으려 하는데, 숨어서 자기 벗의 위급함을 본 니수스가 앞으로 달려 나오며 외쳤다. \"그것은 나였소, 그것은 나였소."], ["While he spoke the sword fell, and pierced the comely bosom of Euryalus.", "루툴리족이여, 그대들의 검을 내게 겨누시오, 내가 한 일이오. 그는 다만 벗으로서 나를 따랐을 뿐이오.\""], ["His head fell over on his shoulder, like a flower cut down by the plough.", "그가 말하는 사이에 검이 떨어져, 에우리알루스의 어여쁜 가슴을 꿰뚫었다."], ["Nisus rushed upon Volscens and plunged his sword into his body, and was himself slain on the instant by numberless blows.", "그의 머리가 어깨 위로 떨어졌으니, 마치 쟁기에 베인 꽃과도 같았다. 니수스는 볼스켄스에게 달려들어 그의 몸에 검을 찔러 넣고는, 그 자신도 곧바로 헤아릴 수 없는 칼질에 쓰러져 죽었다."]], [["Æneas, with his Etrurian allies, arrived on the scene of action in time to rescue his beleaguered camp; and now the two armies being nearly equal in strength, the war began in good earnest.", "에트루리아 동맹군을 거느린 아이네아스는 포위된 자신의 진영을 구할 만한 때에 전장에 다다랐다. 이제 두 군대가 힘에서 거의 대등해지자 전쟁이 본격적으로 시작되었다."], ["We cannot find space for all the details, but must simply record the fate of the principal characters whom we have introduced to our readers.", "그 모든 세부를 다 담을 자리는 없으니, 우리가 독자들에게 소개해 온 주요 인물들의 운명만을 적어 두기로 한다."], ["The tyrant Mezentius, finding himself engaged against his revolting subjects, raged like a wild beast.", "폭군 메젠티우스는 자신이 반란을 일으킨 백성들을 상대로 싸우게 된 것을 알고 야수처럼 날뛰었다."], ["He slew all who dared to withstand him, and put the multitude to flight wherever he appeared.", "그는 감히 자기에게 맞서는 자는 모두 죽였고, 그가 나타나는 곳마다 무리를 패주시켰다."], ["At last he encountered Æneas, and the armies stood still to see the issue.", "마침내 그는 아이네아스와 마주쳤고, 군대들은 그 결말을 보려고 멈춰 섰다."], ["Mezentius threw his spear, which striking Æneas’s shield glanced off and hit Anthor.", "메젠티우스가 창을 던졌으나, 아이네아스의 방패를 맞고 빗겨 나가 안토르를 맞혔다."], ["He was a Grecian by birth, who had left Argos, his native city, and followed Evander into Italy.", "그는 그리스 태생으로, 고향 도시 아르고스를 떠나 에우안데르를 따라 이탈리아로 온 자였다."], ["The poet says of him with simple pathos which has made the words proverbial, “He fell, unhappy, by a wound intended for another, looked up at the skies, and dying remembered sweet Argos.”[34] Æneas now in turn hurled his lance.", "시인은 그에 대해, 그 말을 격언으로 만든 소박한 비애를 담아 이렇게 말한다. \"그는 가엾게도 남을 겨눈 상처로 쓰러져 하늘을 올려다보며, 죽어 가면서 정다운 아르고스를 떠올렸노라.\""], ["It pierced the shield of Mezentius, and wounded him in the thigh.", "이제 아이네아스가 차례로 자신의 창을 던졌다. 그것은 메젠티우스의 방패를 꿰뚫어 그의 넓적다리에 상처를 입혔다."], ["Lausus, his son, could not bear the sight, but rushed forward and interposed himself, while the followers pressed round Mezentius and bore him away.", "그의 아들 라우수스는 그 광경을 차마 견디지 못하고 앞으로 달려 나와 몸을 가로막았으며, 그사이 추종자들이 메젠티우스를 에워싸고 그를 데리고 빠져나갔다."], ["Æneas held his sword suspended over Lausus and delayed to strike, but the furious youth pressed on and he was compelled to deal the fatal blow.", "아이네아스는 검을 라우수스 위로 치켜든 채 내려치기를 미루었으나, 격분한 젊은이가 거세게 밀고 들어오는 통에 어쩔 수 없이 치명적인 일격을 가하고 말았다."], ["Lausus fell, and Æneas bent over him in pity.", "라우수스가 쓰러지자 아이네아스는 연민에 차서 그 위로 몸을 굽혔다."], ["“Hapless youth,”", "\"가엾은 젊은이여,\""], ["he said, “what can I do for you worthy of your praise?", "그가 말했다. \"그대의 칭송에 값할 무엇을 내가 그대에게 해 줄 수 있겠는가?"], ["Keep those arms in which you glory, and fear not but that your body shall be restored to your friends, and have due funeral honors.”", "그대가 자랑으로 삼는 그 무구들은 간직하라, 그리고 그대의 주검이 그대의 벗들에게 돌려져 마땅한 장례의 예우를 받게 되리니 염려하지 말라.\""], ["So saying, he called the timid followers and delivered the body into their hands.", "이렇게 말하고 그는 겁에 질린 추종자들을 불러 그 주검을 그들의 손에 넘겼다."]], [["Mezentius meanwhile had been borne to the riverside, and washed his wound.", "그사이 메젠티우스는 강가로 옮겨져 상처를 씻었다."], ["Soon the news reached him of Lausus’s death, and rage and despair supplied the place of strength.", "이내 라우수스의 죽음을 알리는 소식이 그에게 닿았고, 분노와 절망이 힘을 대신했다."], ["He mounted his horse and dashed into the thickest of the fight, seeking Æneas.", "그는 말에 올라 싸움이 가장 치열한 곳으로 돌진하여 아이네아스를 찾았다."], ["Having found him he rode round him in a circle, throwing one javelin after another, while Æneas stood fenced with his shield, turning every way to meet them.", "그를 찾아내자 그는 아이네아스 둘레를 빙빙 돌며 투창을 잇따라 던졌고, 아이네아스는 방패로 몸을 가린 채 사방으로 돌아서며 그것들을 막아 냈다."], ["At last, after Mezentius had three times made the circuit, Æneas threw his lance directly at the horse’s head.", "마침내 메젠티우스가 세 차례 원을 그리며 돌고 난 뒤, 아이네아스가 말의 머리를 정통으로 겨누어 창을 던졌다."], ["It pierced his temples and he fell, while a shout from both armies rent the skies.", "창은 말의 관자놀이를 꿰뚫었고, 말이 쓰러지자 양쪽 군대에서 터져 나온 함성이 하늘을 갈랐다."], ["Mezentius asked no mercy, but only that his body might be spared the insults of his revolted subjects, and be buried in the same grave with his son.", "메젠티우스는 자비를 청하지 않았고, 다만 자신의 주검이 반란을 일으킨 백성들의 모욕을 면하고 아들과 같은 무덤에 묻히기만을 청했다."], ["He received the fatal stroke not unprepared, and poured out his life and his blood together.", "그는 치명적인 일격을 각오 없이 받지 않았으며, 자신의 목숨과 피를 함께 쏟아 냈다."]], [["# PALLAS, CAMILLA, TURNUS", "# PALLAS, CAMILLA, TURNUS"]], [["While these things were doing in one part of the field, in another Turnus encountered the youthful Pallas.", "전장의 한쪽에서 이러한 일들이 벌어지는 동안, 다른 한쪽에서는 투르누스가 젊은 팔라스와 마주쳤다."], ["The contest between champions so unequally matched could not be doubtful.", "그토록 기울어진 두 용사 사이의 다툼은 의심할 여지가 없었다."], ["Pallas bore himself bravely, but fell by the lance of Turnus.", "팔라스는 용감히 싸웠으나 투르누스의 창에 쓰러졌다."], ["The victor almost relented when he saw the brave youth lying dead at his feet, and spared to use the privilege of a conqueror in despoiling him of his arms.", "승자는 그 용감한 젊은이가 자기 발치에 죽어 누운 것을 보고 거의 마음이 누그러져, 그의 무구를 벗겨 내는 정복자의 권리를 행사하기를 삼갔다."], ["The belt only, adorned with studs and carvings of gold, he took and clasped round his own body.", "다만 금으로 된 장식 단추와 조각으로 꾸민 허리띠 하나만을 취해 자기 몸에 둘렀다."], ["The rest he remitted to the friends of the slain.", "나머지는 죽은 자의 벗들에게 돌려보냈다."]], [["After the battle there was a cessation of arms for some days to allow both armies to bury their dead.", "전투가 끝난 뒤 며칠 동안 양쪽 군대가 죽은 자들을 묻을 수 있도록 휴전이 있었다."], ["In this interval Æneas challenged Turnus to decide the contest by single combat, but Turnus evaded the challenge.", "이 사이에 아이네아스는 투르누스에게 일대일 결투로 승부를 가리자고 도전했으나, 투르누스는 그 도전을 피했다."], ["Another battle ensued, in which Camilla, the virgin warrior, was chiefly conspicuous.", "또 한 차례의 전투가 이어졌으니, 그 전투에서 처녀 전사 카밀라가 특히 두드러졌다."], ["Her deeds of valor surpassed those of the bravest warriors, and many Trojans and Etruscans fell pierced with her darts or struck down by her battle-axe.", "그녀의 무용은 가장 용맹한 전사들의 그것을 능가했으며, 많은 트로이아인과 에트루리아인이 그녀의 화살에 꿰뚫리거나 그녀의 전투용 도끼에 쓰러졌다."], ["At last an Etruscan named Aruns, who had watched her long, seeking for some advantage, observed her pursuing a flying enemy whose splendid armor offered a tempting prize.", "마침내 아룬스라는 이름의 에트루리아인이, 오래도록 그녀를 지켜보며 어떤 빈틈을 노리던 차에, 그녀가 화려한 무구로 탐나는 전리품을 안긴 채 달아나는 적을 뒤쫓는 것을 보았다."], ["Intent on the chase she observed not her danger, and the javelin of Aruns struck her and inflicted a fatal wound.", "추격에 정신이 팔려 그녀는 자신의 위험을 알아채지 못했고, 아룬스의 투창이 그녀를 맞혀 치명상을 입혔다."], ["She fell and breathed her last in the arms of her attendant maidens.", "그녀는 쓰러져 시중드는 처녀들의 품에서 마지막 숨을 거두었다."], ["But Diana, who beheld her fate, suffered not her slaughter to be unavenged.", "그러나 그녀의 최후를 지켜본 아르테미스는 그 죽음이 복수 없이 끝나도록 두지 않았다."], ["Aruns, as he stole away, glad, but frightened, was struck by a secret arrow, launched by one of the nymphs of Diana’s train, and died ignobly and unknown.", "아룬스가 기뻐하면서도 두려워하며 몰래 빠져나가는 사이, 아르테미스의 시종 님프들 가운데 하나가 날린 은밀한 화살에 맞아, 그는 비천하게 이름도 없이 죽었다."]], [["At length the final conflict took place between Æneas and Turnus.", "마침내 아이네아스와 투르누스 사이의 최후의 결전이 벌어졌다."], ["Turnus had avoided the contest as long as he could, but at last, impelled by the ill success of his arms and by the murmurs of his followers, he braced himself to the conflict.", "투르누스는 할 수 있는 한 그 싸움을 피해 왔으나, 마침내 자기 군대의 패전과 추종자들의 불평에 떠밀려 결전에 임할 각오를 다졌다."], ["It could not be doubtful.", "그 결말은 의심할 여지가 없었다."], ["On the side of Æneas were the expressed decree of destiny, the aid of his goddess-mother at every emergency, and impenetrable armor fabricated by Vulcan, at her request, for her son.", "아이네아스의 편에는 명백히 선포된 운명의 명령과, 위급할 때마다 그를 돕는 여신 어머니의 도움과, 그녀의 청에 따라 헤파이스토스[로마의 〈불카누스〉]가 그 아들을 위해 만든 뚫을 수 없는 무구가 있었다."], ["Turnus, on the other hand, was deserted by his celestial allies, Juno having been expressly forbidden by Jupiter to assist him any longer.", "반면에 투르누스는 천상의 동맹들에게 버림받았으니, 헤라는 제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]로부터 더는 그를 돕지 말라는 명백한 금령을 받았던 것이다."], ["Turnus threw his lance, but it recoiled harmless from the shield of Æneas.", "투르누스가 창을 던졌으나, 그것은 아이네아스의 방패에 맞고 아무런 해도 입히지 못한 채 튕겨 나왔다."], ["The Trojan hero then threw his, which penetrated the shield of Turnus, and pierced his thigh.", "그러자 트로이아 영웅이 자신의 창을 던졌으니, 그것은 투르누스의 방패를 뚫고 그의 넓적다리를 꿰뚫었다."], ["Then Turnus’s fortitude forsook him and he begged for mercy; and Æneas would have given him his life, but at the instant his eye fell on the belt of Pallas, which Turnus had taken from the slaughtered youth.", "그러자 투르누스의 굳센 의지가 그를 저버렸고 그는 자비를 빌었다. 아이네아스는 그에게 목숨을 살려 주려 했으나, 바로 그 순간 그의 눈길이 팔라스의 허리띠에 닿았으니, 투르누스가 살해당한 젊은이에게서 빼앗은 것이었다."], ["Instantly his rage revived, and exclaiming, “Pallas immolates thee with this blow,” he thrust him through with his sword.", "곧바로 그의 분노가 되살아났고, \"팔라스가 이 일격으로 너를 제물 삼노라\"라고 외치며 그는 자신의 검으로 그를 꿰뚫었다."]], [["Here the poem of the “Æneid” closes, and we are left to infer that Æneas, having triumphed over his foes, obtained Lavinia for his bride.", "여기서 『아이네이스』의 시는 끝나니, 우리는 아이네아스가 적들을 이긴 끝에 라비니아를 신부로 얻었으리라 짐작하게 된다."], ["Tradition adds that he founded his city, and called it after her name, Lavinium.", "전승은 거기에 덧붙이기를, 그가 자신의 도시를 세워 그녀의 이름을 따 라비니움이라 불렀다고 한다."], ["His son Iulus founded Alba Longa, which was the birthplace of Romulus and Remus and the cradle of Rome itself.", "그의 아들 이울루스는 알바 롱가를 세웠으니, 그곳은 로물루스와 레무스의 출생지이자 로마 그 자체의 요람이었다."]], [["There is an allusion to Camilla in those well-known lines of Pope, in which, illustrating the rule that “the sound should be an echo to the sense,” he says:", "카밀라에 관한 암시는 포프의 저 널리 알려진 시구들 속에 있으니, \"소리는 뜻의 메아리가 되어야 한다\"는 규칙을 예시하며 그는 이렇게 말한다."]], [["“When Ajax strives some rock’s vast weight to throw, The line too labors and the words move slow.", "\"아이아스가 어떤 바위의 거대한 무게를 던지려 애쓸 때면, 시행 또한 애를 쓰고 낱말들이 더디게 움직인다."], ["Not so when swift Camilla scours the plain, Flies o’er th’ unbending corn or skims along the main.”", "날랜 카밀라가 평원을 누빌 때는 그렇지 않으니, 굽지 않는 곡식 위를 날고 바다 위를 스쳐 지난다.\""], ["—Essay on Criticism.", "—『비평론』"]], [["————", "————"]], [["THE teachings of Anchises to Æneas, respecting the nature of the human soul, were in conformity with the doctrines of the Pythagoreans.", "앙키세스가 아이네아스에게 인간 영혼의 본성에 관해 가르친 내용은 피타고라스학파의 학설과 일치하는 것이었다."], ["Pythagoras (born five hundred and forty years B.C.) was a native of the island of Samos, but passed the chief portion of his life at Crotona in Italy.", "피타고라스(기원전 540년 출생)는 사모스섬 태생이었으나, 생애의 대부분을 이탈리아의 크로토나에서 보냈다."], ["He is therefore sometimes called “the Samian,” and sometimes “the philosopher of Crotona.”", "따라서 그는 때로 \"사모스 사람\"이라 불리기도 하고, 때로 \"크로토나의 철학자\"라 불리기도 한다."], ["When young he travelled extensively, and it is said visited Egypt, where he was instructed by the priests in all their learning, and afterwards journeyed to the East, and visited the Persian and Chaldean Magi, and the Brahmins of India.", "젊은 시절 그는 널리 여행을 다녔는데, 전하는 바에 따르면 이집트를 방문하여 그곳 사제들에게 그들의 모든 학문을 배웠고, 그 후 동방으로 떠나 페르시아와 칼데아의 마기, 그리고 인도의 브라만들을 찾아갔다고 한다."]], [["At Crotona, where he finally established himself, his extraordinary qualities collected round him a great number of disciples.", "마침내 정착하게 된 크로토나에서, 그의 비범한 자질은 수많은 제자들을 그의 주위로 끌어모았다."], ["The inhabitants were notorious for luxury and licentiousness, but the good effects of his influence were soon visible.", "그곳 주민들은 사치와 방탕으로 악명이 높았으나, 그의 영향이 미친 좋은 결과는 이내 눈에 띄게 나타났다."], ["Sobriety and temperance succeeded.", "절제와 검약이 그 자리를 대신하게 된 것이다."], ["Six hundred of the inhabitants became his disciples and enrolled themselves in a society to aid each other in the pursuit of wisdom, uniting their property in one common stock for the benefit of the whole.", "주민들 가운데 육백 명이 그의 제자가 되어, 지혜의 추구에서 서로를 돕기 위한 한 결사에 이름을 올렸고, 모두의 이익을 위해 그들의 재산을 하나의 공동 자산으로 합쳤다."], ["They were required to practise the greatest purity and simplicity of manners.", "그들은 행실에 있어 더없는 순결과 소박함을 실천하도록 요구받았다."], ["The first lesson they learned was silence; for a time they were required to be only hearers.", "그들이 처음 배운 가르침은 〈침묵〉이었으니, 한동안 그들은 오로지 듣는 자가 되어야만 했다."], ["“He [Pythagoras] said so” (Ipse dixit), was to be held by them as sufficient, without any proof.", "\"그분[피타고라스]께서 그렇게 말씀하셨다\"(입세 딕시트)는 말은, 어떠한 증명도 없이 그들에게 충분한 것으로 여겨졌다."], ["It was only the advanced pupils, after years of patient submission, who were allowed to ask questions and to state objections.", "여러 해에 걸친 인내로운 순종을 거친 뒤에야, 오직 진전된 제자들만이 질문을 하고 이견을 제기하는 것이 허락되었다."]], [["Pythagoras considered numbers as the essence and principle of all things, and attributed to them a real and distinct existence; so that, in his view, they were the elements out of which the universe was constructed.", "피타고라스는 〈수〉를 만물의 본질이자 원리로 여겼으며, 수에 실재하고 뚜렷한 존재성을 부여했다. 따라서 그의 견해로는, 수란 우주가 그것으로부터 구성된 요소들이었다."], ["How he conceived this process has never been satisfactorily explained.", "그가 이 과정을 어떻게 생각했는지는 결코 만족스럽게 설명된 바가 없다."], ["He traced the various forms and phenomena of the world to numbers as their basis and essence.", "그는 세계의 다양한 형태와 현상을 그 토대이자 본질로서의 수에까지 거슬러 올라가 추적했다."], ["The “Monad” or unit he regarded as the source of all numbers.", "\"모나드\", 곧 〈하나〉를 그는 모든 수의 근원으로 보았다."], ["The number Two was imperfect, and the cause of increase and division.", "수 〈둘〉은 불완전한 것이며, 증가와 분할의 원인이었다."], ["Three was called the number of the whole because it had a beginning, middle, and end.", "〈셋〉은 시작과 중간과 끝을 지니고 있으므로 전체의 수라 불렸다."], ["Four, representing the square, is in the highest degree perfect; and Ten, as it contains the sum of the four prime numbers, comprehends all musical and arithmetical proportions, and denotes the system of the world.", "정사각형을 나타내는 〈넷〉은 가장 높은 정도로 완전하며, 〈열〉은 처음 네 수의 합을 담고 있으므로 모든 음악적·산술적 비례를 포괄하고 세계의 체계를 나타낸다."]], [["As the numbers proceed from the monad, so he regarded the pure and simple essence of the Deity as the source of all the forms of nature.", "수가 모나드로부터 나오는 것처럼, 그는 신성의 순수하고 단순한 본질을 자연의 모든 형태의 근원으로 여겼다."], ["Gods, demons, and heroes are emanations of the Supreme, and there is a fourth emanation, the human soul.", "신들과 정령들과 영웅들은 지고자(至高者)로부터 흘러나온 것이며, 거기에 더해 네 번째 유출물, 곧 인간의 영혼이 있다."], ["This is immortal, and when freed from the fetters of the body passes to the habitation of the dead, where it remains till it returns to the world, to dwell in some other human or animal body, and at last, when sufficiently purified, it returns to the source from which it proceeded.", "이 영혼은 불멸하며, 육체의 족쇄로부터 벗어나면 죽은 자들의 거처로 옮겨가, 다시 이 세계로 돌아와 또 다른 인간이나 동물의 몸에 깃들 때까지 그곳에 머문다. 그리고 마침내 충분히 정화되었을 때, 자신이 비롯된 근원으로 되돌아간다."], ["This doctrine of the transmigration of souls (metempsychosis), which was originally Egyptian and connected with the doctrine of reward and punishment of human actions, was the chief cause why the Pythagoreans killed no animals.", "본디 이집트에서 유래하여 인간 행위에 대한 상벌의 교리와 결부된 이 영혼 전생(轉生)의 학설(메템프시코시스)이야말로, 피타고라스학파가 어떠한 동물도 죽이지 않은 주된 까닭이었다."], ["Ovid represents Pythagoras addressing his disciples in these words: “Souls never die, but always on quitting one abode pass to another.", "오비디우스는 피타고라스가 제자들에게 다음과 같은 말로 이야기하는 모습을 그린다. \"영혼은 결코 죽지 않으며, 언제나 하나의 거처를 떠날 때 또 다른 거처로 옮겨간다."], ["I myself can remember that in the time of the Trojan war I was Euphorbus, the son of Panthus, and fell by the spear of Menelaus.", "나 자신은 트로이아 전쟁 때에 내가 판투스의 아들 에우포르보스였고 메넬라오스의 창에 쓰러졌던 일을 기억할 수 있다."], ["Lately being in the temple of Juno, at Argos, I recognized my shield hung up there among the trophies.", "얼마 전 아르고스에 있는 헤라[로마의 〈유노〉]의 신전에 갔을 때, 나는 그곳 전리품들 가운데 걸려 있는 내 방패를 알아보았다."], ["All things change, nothing perishes.", "만물은 변하되, 어떤 것도 소멸하지 않는다."], ["The soul passes hither and thither, occupying now this body, now that, passing from the body of a beast into that of a man, and thence to a beast’s again.", "영혼은 이리저리 옮겨다니며 지금은 이 몸을, 지금은 저 몸을 차지하고, 짐승의 몸에서 사람의 몸으로 건너갔다가 다시 짐승의 몸으로 옮겨간다."], ["As wax is stamped with certain figures, then melted, then stamped anew with others, yet is always the same wax, so the soul, being always the same, yet wears, at different times, different forms.", "밀랍에 어떤 형상이 찍혔다가 녹여지고, 다시 다른 형상으로 새로이 찍히지만 언제나 같은 밀랍인 것처럼, 영혼도 언제나 같은 것이면서 서로 다른 때에 서로 다른 형태를 걸치는 것이다."], ["Therefore, if the love of kindred is not extinct in your bosoms, forbear, I entreat you, to violate the life of those who may haply be your own relatives.”", "그러므로 만일 혈육에 대한 사랑이 그대들의 가슴속에서 꺼지지 않았다면, 부디 간청하노니, 어쩌면 그대들 자신의 친족일 수도 있는 자들의 생명을 해치는 일을 삼가라.\""]], [["Shakspeare, in the “Merchant of Venice,” makes Gratiano allude to the metempsychosis, where he says to Shylock:", "셰익스피어는 「베니스의 상인」에서 그라티아노로 하여금 영혼 전생을 암시하게 하는데, 그가 샤일록에게 하는 말은 이러하다."]], [["“Thou almost mak’st me waver in my faith, To hold opinion with Pythagoras, That souls of animals infuse themselves Into the trunks of men; thy currish spirit Governed a wolf; who hanged for human slaughter Infused his soul in thee; for thy desires Are wolfish, bloody, starved and ravenous.”", "\"당신은 거의 내 믿음을 흔들어 놓아, 피타고라스의 견해를 품게 만드는구나, 짐승의 영혼이 사람의 몸뚱이 속으로 스며든다는 그 견해를. 당신의 개 같은 영혼은 한 마리 늑대를 다스렸으니, 사람을 죽인 죄로 목매달린 그 늑대가 제 영혼을 당신에게 불어넣었구나. 당신의 욕망은 늑대처럼 잔혹하고, 피에 굶주리고, 게걸스럽기 때문이지.\""]], [["The relation of the notes of the musical scale to numbers, whereby harmony results from vibrations in equal times, and discord from the reverse, led Pythagoras to apply the word “harmony” to the visible creation, meaning by it the just adaptation of parts to each other.", "음계의 음들과 수의 관계, 곧 같은 시간 간격의 진동에서 조화가 생겨나고 그 반대에서 불협화음이 생겨난다는 사실은, 피타고라스로 하여금 \"조화\"라는 말을 눈에 보이는 피조물 전체에 적용하도록 이끌었으니, 그가 그 말로써 뜻한 바는 부분들이 서로에게 알맞게 들어맞음이었다."], ["This is the idea which Dryden expresses in the beginning of his “Song for St. Cecilia’s Day”:", "이것이 바로 드라이든이 「성 케킬리아 축일을 위한 노래」의 첫머리에서 표현한 관념이다."]], [["“From harmony, from heavenly harmony This everlasting frame began; From harmony to harmony Through all the compass of the notes it ran, The Diapason closing full in Man.”", "\"조화로부터, 천상의 조화로부터 이 영원한 골격이 비롯되었네. 조화에서 조화로 음들의 온 음역을 두루 거쳐 그것은 흘러갔으니, 옥타브 화음은 인간 안에서 충만하게 닫혔네.\""]], [["In the centre of the universe (he taught) there was a central fire, the principle of life.", "그가 가르친 바에 따르면, 우주의 중심에는 생명의 원리인 중심의 불이 있었다."], ["The central fire was surrounded by the earth, the moon, the sun, and the five planets.", "이 중심의 불은 지구와 달과 태양, 그리고 다섯 행성으로 둘러싸여 있었다."], ["The distances of the various heavenly bodies from one another were conceived to correspond to the proportions of the musical scale.", "여러 천체들이 서로에게서 떨어진 거리는 음계의 비례에 상응하는 것으로 여겨졌다."], ["The heavenly bodies, with the gods who inhabited them, were supposed to perform a choral dance round the central fire, “not without song.”", "천체들은 그것들에 거주하는 신들과 더불어 \"노래가 없지 않게\" 중심의 불 주위를 합창의 춤을 추며 도는 것으로 생각되었다."], ["It is this doctrine which Shakspeare alludes to when he makes Lorenzo teach astronomy to Jessica in this fashion:", "셰익스피어가 로렌초로 하여금 다음과 같은 방식으로 제시카에게 천문학을 가르치게 할 때 암시하는 것이 바로 이 학설이다."]], [["“Look, Jessica, see how the floor of heaven Is thick inlaid with patines of bright gold!", "\"보라, 제시카, 하늘의 바닥이 빛나는 황금 접시들로 얼마나 빽빽이 박혀 있는지를!"], ["There’s not the smallest orb that thou behold’st But in his motion like an angel sings, Still quiring to the young-eyed cherubim; Such harmony is in immortal souls!", "그대가 바라보는 가장 작은 천구라도 그 운행 속에서 천사처럼 노래하지 않는 것이 없으니, 여전히 어린 눈의 케루빔에게 합창으로 화답하네. 그러한 조화가 불멸의 영혼들 안에 있도다!"], ["But whilst this muddy vesture of decay Doth grossly close it in we cannot hear it.”", "허나 이 진흙으로 빚어진 썩어질 옷이 그것을 둔하게 가두고 있는 동안, 우리는 그 소리를 들을 수 없네.\""], ["—Merchant of Venice.", "─「베니스의 상인」."]], [["The spheres were conceived to be crystalline or glassy fabrics arranged over one another like a nest of bowls reversed.", "이 천구들은 수정이나 유리로 된 구조물로서, 마치 엎어 놓은 그릇들을 포개 놓은 것처럼 서로 위에 겹겹이 배열되어 있는 것으로 생각되었다."], ["In the substance of each sphere one or more of the heavenly bodies was supposed to be fixed, so as to move with it.", "각각의 천구의 실체 안에는 하나 또는 그 이상의 천체가 박혀서 그 천구와 함께 움직이는 것으로 여겨졌다."], ["As the spheres are transparent we look through them and see the heavenly bodies which they contain and carry round with them.", "천구들은 투명하므로, 우리는 그것들을 꿰뚫어 보면서 그 안에 담겨 함께 돌고 있는 천체들을 바라보게 된다."], ["But as these spheres cannot move on one another without friction, a sound is thereby produced which is of exquisite harmony, too fine for mortal ears to recognize.", "그러나 이 천구들은 마찰 없이는 서로 위에서 움직일 수 없으므로, 그로 인해 하나의 소리가 생겨나는데, 그것은 더없이 절묘한 조화의 소리여서 필멸자의 귀로는 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 미묘하다."], ["Milton, in his “Hymn on the Nativity,” thus alludes to the music of the spheres:", "밀턴은 「그리스도 강탄의 아침에 부치는 찬가」에서 천구의 음악을 다음과 같이 암시한다."]], [["“Ring out, ye crystal spheres!", "\"울려 퍼져라, 그대 수정의 천구들이여!"], ["Once bless our human ears (If ye have power to charm our senses so); And let your silver chime Move in melodious time, And let the base of Heaven’s deep organ blow; And with your ninefold harmony Make up full concert with the angelic symphony.”", "한 번 우리 인간의 귀를 축복하라, (그대들에게 우리 감각을 그토록 매혹할 힘이 있다면.) 그대들의 은빛 종소리를 선율 고운 박자로 흐르게 하라, 또한 천국의 깊은 오르간의 저음을 울려라. 그리하여 그대들의 아홉 겹 조화로 천사들의 교향과 더불어 완전한 합주를 이루어라.\""]], [["Pythagoras is said to have invented the lyre.", "피타고라스는 리라를 발명했다고 전해진다."], ["Our own poet Longfellow, in “Verses to a Child,” thus relates the story:", "우리 시인 롱펠로는 「어느 아이에게 부치는 시」에서 그 이야기를 이렇게 들려준다."]], [["“As great Pythagoras of yore, Standing beside the blacksmith’s door, And hearing the hammers as they smote The anvils with a different note, Stole from the varying tones that hung Vibrant on every iron tongue, The secret of the sounding wire, And formed the seven-chorded lyre.”", "\"옛날 위대한 피타고라스가 대장간 문 옆에 서서, 망치들이 저마다 다른 음으로 모루를 내리치는 소리를 듣고는, 쇠로 된 모든 혀에 떨리며 매달려 있던 그 변화하는 음들로부터, 울리는 현(弦)의 비밀을 훔쳐내어, 일곱 줄의 리라를 만들었네.\""]], [["See also the same poet’s “Occultation of Orion”—", "같은 시인의 「오리온의 엄폐(掩蔽)」도 함께 보라."]], [["“The Samian’s great Æolian lyre.”", "\"사모스 사람의 위대한 아이올로스의 리라.\""]], [["# SYBARIS AND CROTONA", "# SYBARIS AND CROTONA"]], [["Sybaris, a neighboring city to Crotona, was as celebrated for luxury and effeminacy as Crotona for the reverse.", "크로토나의 이웃 도시 시바리스는, 크로토나가 그 반대의 것으로 유명했던 만큼이나 사치와 유약함으로 이름을 떨쳤다."], ["The name has become proverbial.", "그 이름은 속담처럼 쓰이게 되었다."], ["J. R.", "J."], ["Lowell uses it in this sense in his charming little poem “To the Dandelion”:", "R. 로웰은 그의 매혹적인 짧은 시 「민들레에게」에서 그 이름을 이러한 뜻으로 사용한다."]], [["“Not in mid June the golden cuirassed bee Feels a more summer-like, warm ravishment In the white lily’s breezy tent (His conquered Sybaris) than I when first From the dark green thy yellow circles burst.”", "\"유월 한복판에 황금 갑옷을 두른 벌이 하얀 백합의 산들바람 부는 천막(그가 정복한 시바리스) 안에서 느끼는 그 여름다운 따스한 황홀도, 어두운 초록 사이로 그대 노란 동그라미가 처음 터져 나올 때 내가 느끼는 것보다 더하지는 않으리.\""]], [["A war arose between the two cities, and Sybaris was conquered and destroyed.", "두 도시 사이에 전쟁이 일어나, 시바리스는 정복되어 파괴되었다."], ["Milo, the celebrated athlete, led the army of Crotona.", "이름난 운동선수 밀로가 크로토나의 군대를 이끌었다."], ["Many stories are told of Milo’s vast strength, such as his carrying a heifer of four years old upon his shoulders and afterwards eating the whole of it in a single day.", "밀로의 엄청난 힘에 관해서는 여러 이야기가 전해지는데, 이를테면 네 살배기 어린 암소를 어깨에 메고 다니다가 그 후 그것을 단 하루 만에 통째로 먹어 치웠다는 이야기 같은 것이다. 그의 죽음의 방식은 이렇게 전해진다."], ["The mode of his death is thus related: As he was passing through a forest he saw the trunk of a tree which had been partially split open by wood-cutters, and attempted to rend it further; but the wood closed upon his hands and held him fast, in which state he was attacked and devoured by wolves.", "어느 숲을 지나가다가 그는 나무꾼들이 일부 쪼개 놓은 나무 둥치를 보고는 그것을 더 갈라 보려 했다. 그러나 나무가 그의 두 손을 옥죄며 그를 단단히 붙들었고, 그러한 상태에서 그는 늑대들의 습격을 받아 잡아먹혔다."]], [["Byron, in his “Ode to Napoleon Bonaparte,” alludes to the story of Milo:", "바이런은 「나폴레옹 보나파르트에게 부치는 송가」에서 밀로의 이야기를 암시한다."]], [["“He who of old would rend the oak Deemed not of the rebound; Chained by the trunk he vainly broke, Alone, how looked he round!”", "\"옛날 참나무를 쪼개려던 그 사내는 되튀어 올 힘을 생각지 못했네. 헛되이 쪼개려던 그 둥치에 묶인 채, 홀로, 그는 어떤 눈길로 주위를 둘러보았던가!\""]], [["# EGYPTIAN DEITIES", "# EGYPTIAN DEITIES"]], [["The Egyptians acknowledged as the highest deity Amun, afterwards called Zeus, or Jupiter Ammon.", "이집트인들은 최고의 신으로 아문을 받들었으니, 그는 뒷날 제우스, 곧 유피테르 암몬이라 불렸다."], ["Amun manifested himself in his word or will, which created Kneph and Athor, of different sexes. From Kneph and Athor proceeded Osiris and Isis.", "아문은 자신의 말씀 또는 의지를 통해 자신을 드러냈고, 그 말씀이 서로 다른 성(性)을 지닌 크네프와 아토르를 창조했다."], ["Osiris was worshipped as the god of the sun, the source of warmth, life, and fruitfulness, in addition to which he was also regarded as the god of the Nile, who annually visited his wife, Isis (the Earth), by means of an inundation.", "크네프와 아토르로부터 오시리스와 이시스가 나왔다. 오시리스는 따스함과 생명과 풍요의 근원인 태양의 신으로 숭배되었으며, 그에 더하여 해마다 범람을 통해 자신의 아내 이시스(대지)를 찾아가는 나일강의 신으로도 여겨졌다."], ["Serapis or Hermes is sometimes represented as identical with Osiris, and sometimes as a distinct divinity, the ruler of Tartarus and god of medicine.", "세라피스 또는 헤르메스는 때로는 오시리스와 동일한 존재로 묘사되고, 때로는 타르타로스의 지배자이자 의술의 신인 별개의 신격으로 묘사된다."], ["Anubis is the guardian god, represented with a dog’s head, emblematic of his character of fidelity and watchfulness.", "아누비스는 수호신으로, 충실함과 경계심이라는 그의 성격을 상징하여 개의 머리를 한 모습으로 그려진다."], ["Horus or Harpocrates was the son of Osiris.", "호루스, 곧 하르포크라테스는 오시리스의 아들이었다."], ["He is represented seated on a Lotus flower, with his finger on his lips, as the god of Silence.", "그는 연꽃 위에 앉아 손가락을 입술에 댄 모습으로 그려지는데, 침묵의 신이기 때문이다."]], [["In one of Moore’s “Irish Melodies”", "무어의 「아일랜드 선율」"], ["is an allusion to Harpocrates:", "가운데 하나에 하르포크라테스를 암시하는 대목이 있다."]], [["“Thyself shall, under some rosy bower, Sit mute, with thy finger on thy lip; Like him, the boy, who born among The flowers that on the Nile-stream blush, Sits ever thus,—his only song To Earth and Heaven, ‘Hush all, hush!’”", "\"그대 자신은 어느 장밋빛 정자(亭子) 아래에서, 손가락을 입술에 댄 채 말없이 앉으리라. 나일강 물결 위에 붉게 피어나는 꽃들 사이에서 태어난 그 소년처럼, 언제나 그렇게 앉아 있는 그 소년처럼─그의 유일한 노래는 대지와 하늘을 향한, '모두 쉿, 쉿!'이라네.\""]], [["# MYTH OF OSIRIS AND ISIS", "# MYTH OF OSIRIS AND ISIS"]], [["Osiris and Isis were at one time induced to descend to the earth to bestow gifts and blessings on its inhabitants.", "오시리스와 이시스는 한때 지상으로 내려와 그곳 주민들에게 선물과 축복을 베풀도록 이끌렸다."], ["Isis showed them first the use of wheat and barley, and Osiris made the instruments of agriculture and taught men the use of them, as well as how to harness the ox to the plough.", "이시스는 먼저 그들에게 밀과 보리의 쓰임새를 보여 주었고, 오시리스는 농경의 도구들을 만들어 사람들에게 그 사용법을 가르쳐 주었으며, 황소를 쟁기에 매는 법도 일러 주었다."], ["He then gave men laws, the institution of marriage, a civil organization, and taught them how to worship the gods.", "그런 다음 그는 사람들에게 법률과 혼인 제도와 시민 조직을 주었고, 신들을 경배하는 법을 가르쳤다."], ["After he had thus made the valley of the Nile a happy country, he assembled a host with which he went to bestow his blessings upon the rest of the world.", "이렇게 하여 나일강 골짜기를 행복한 나라로 만든 뒤, 그는 한 무리의 군대를 모아 세계의 나머지 지역에도 자신의 축복을 베풀고자 떠났다."], ["He conquered the nations everywhere, but not with weapons, only with music and eloquence.", "그는 이르는 곳마다 여러 민족을 정복했는데, 무기로써가 아니라 오직 음악과 웅변으로써 그리하였다."], ["His brother Typhon saw this, and filled with envy and malice sought during his absence to usurp his throne.", "그의 형제 티폰이 이를 보고는 시기와 악의에 가득 차, 그가 자리를 비운 동안 그의 왕좌를 찬탈하려 꾀했다."], ["But Isis, who held the reins of government, frustrated his plans.", "그러나 통치의 고삐를 쥐고 있던 이시스가 그의 계획을 좌절시켰다."], ["Still more embittered, he now resolved to kill his brother.", "더욱 원한에 사무친 그는 이제 자신의 형제를 죽이기로 결심했다."], ["This he did in the following manner: Having organized a conspiracy of seventy-two members, he went with them to the feast which was celebrated in honor of the king’s return.", "그는 다음과 같은 방식으로 이를 실행했다. 일흔두 명의 음모자를 규합한 다음, 그들과 함께 왕의 귀환을 기리어 열린 잔치에 갔다."], ["He then caused a box or chest to be brought in, which had been made to fit exactly the size of Osiris, and declared that he would give that chest of precious wood to whosoever could get into it.", "그러고는 오시리스의 몸 크기에 꼭 들어맞도록 만들어진 상자, 곧 궤를 들여오게 한 뒤, 그 안에 들어갈 수 있는 자에게 그 값진 나무로 만든 궤를 주겠노라고 선언했다."], ["The rest tried in vain, but no sooner was Osiris in it than Typhon and his companions closed the lid and flung the chest into the Nile.", "나머지 사람들은 헛되이 시도했으나, 오시리스가 그 안에 들어가자마자 티폰과 그의 동료들은 뚜껑을 닫아 그 궤를 나일강에 던져 버렸다."], ["When Isis heard of the cruel murder she wept and mourned, and then with her hair shorn, clothed in black and beating her breast, she sought diligently for the body of her husband.", "이시스는 이 잔혹한 살해 소식을 듣고 울며 슬퍼했고, 그런 다음 머리카락을 자르고 검은 옷을 입고 가슴을 치면서, 남편의 시신을 부지런히 찾아다녔다."], ["In this search she was materially assisted by Anubis, the son of Osiris and Nephthys.", "이 수색에서 그녀는 오시리스와 네프티스의 아들 아누비스의 큰 도움을 받았다."], ["They sought in vain for some time; for when the chest, carried by the waves to the shores of Byblos, had become entangled in the reeds that grew at the edge of the water, the divine power that dwelt in the body of Osiris imparted such strength to the shrub that it grew into a mighty tree, enclosing in its trunk the coffin of the god.", "한동안 그들은 헛되이 찾아 헤맸으니, 그 궤가 물결에 실려 비블로스 해안에 이르러 물가에 자라는 갈대에 걸려 멈추었을 때, 오시리스의 몸 안에 깃든 신성한 힘이 그 관목에 어찌나 큰 기운을 불어넣었던지, 그것이 자라나 거대한 나무가 되어 그 둥치 안에 신의 관을 품게 되었던 것이다."], ["This tree with its sacred deposit was shortly after felled, and erected as a column in the palace of the king of Phœnicia.", "이 나무는 신성한 유물을 품은 채 얼마 지나지 않아 베어져, 페니키아 왕의 궁전에 기둥으로 세워졌다."], ["But at length by the aid of Anubis and the sacred birds, Isis ascertained these facts, and then went to the royal city.", "그러나 마침내 아누비스와 신성한 새들의 도움으로 이시스는 이러한 사실들을 알아내고, 그 왕도(王都)로 갔다."], ["There she offered herself at the palace as a servant, and being admitted, threw off her disguise and appeared as a goddess, surrounded with thunder and lightning.", "그곳에서 그녀는 궁전에 하녀로 자신을 내놓아 받아들여진 다음, 변장을 벗어 던지고 천둥과 번개에 둘러싸인 여신의 모습으로 나타났다."], ["Striking the column with her wand she caused it to split open and give up the sacred coffin.", "자신의 지팡이로 그 기둥을 내리쳐 그것을 쪼개어 신성한 관을 내놓게 했다."], ["This she seized and returned with it, and concealed it in the depth of a forest, but Typhon discovered it, and cutting the body into fourteen pieces scattered them hither and thither.", "그녀는 이것을 거두어 가지고 돌아와 어느 숲 깊은 곳에 감추었으나, 티폰이 그것을 발견하고는 시신을 열네 토막으로 잘라 이리저리 흩뿌렸다."], ["After a tedious search, Isis found thirteen pieces, the fishes of the Nile having eaten the other.", "지루한 수색 끝에 이시스는 열세 토막을 찾아냈으나, 나머지 한 토막은 나일강의 물고기들이 먹어 버린 뒤였다."], ["This she replaced by an imitation of sycamore wood, and buried the body at Philæ, which became ever after the great burying place of the nation, and the spot to which pilgrimages were made from all parts of the country.", "그녀는 이것을 무화과나무로 만든 모조품으로 대신하고, 필라이에 시신을 묻었으니, 그곳은 그 후로 줄곧 이 나라의 위대한 매장지가 되었고, 온 나라 각지에서 순례가 이루어지는 장소가 되었다."], ["A temple of surpassing magnificence was also erected there in honor of the god, and at every place where one of his limbs had been found minor temples and tombs were built to commemorate the event.", "그곳에는 또한 더없이 장엄한 신전이 그 신을 기리어 세워졌고, 그의 사지(四肢) 가운데 하나가 발견된 곳마다 그 일을 기념하여 작은 신전과 무덤들이 지어졌다."], ["Osiris became after that the tutelar deity of the Egyptians.", "그 후 오시리스는 이집트인들의 수호신이 되었다."], ["His soul was supposed always to inhabit the body of the bull Apis, and at his death to transfer itself to his successor.", "그의 영혼은 언제나 황소 아피스의 몸 안에 깃들어 있으며, 그 황소가 죽으면 그 후계자에게로 옮겨가는 것으로 여겨졌다."]], [["Apis, the Bull of Memphis, was worshipped with the greatest reverence by the Egyptians.", "멤피스의 황소 아피스는 이집트인들에게 더없는 경외로써 숭배되었다."], ["The individual animal who was held to be Apis was recognized by certain signs.", "아피스로 받들어지는 그 한 마리의 짐승은 특정한 표징들로 식별되었다."], ["It was requisite that he should be quite black, have a white square mark on the forehead, another, in the form of an eagle, on his back, and under his tongue a lump somewhat in the shape of a scarabæus or beetle.", "그 황소는 완전히 검은빛이어야 하고, 이마에는 흰 사각형 표지가, 등에는 독수리 모양의 또 다른 표지가 있어야 하며, 혀 밑에는 풍뎅이 또는 딱정벌레 형상에 가까운 혹이 있어야 했다."], ["As soon as a bull thus marked was found by those sent in search of him, he was placed in a building facing the east, and was fed with milk for four months.", "그를 찾으러 보내진 자들이 이렇게 표징을 지닌 황소를 발견하자마자, 그 황소는 동쪽을 향한 한 건물에 놓여 넉 달 동안 젖을 먹였다."], ["At the expiration of this term the priests repaired at new moon, with great pomp, to his habitation and saluted him Apis.", "이 기간이 끝나면 사제들은 초승달이 뜰 때 대단한 위엄을 갖추어 그의 거처로 가서 그를 아피스라 칭송했다."], ["He was placed in a vessel magnificently decorated and conveyed down the Nile to Memphis, where a temple, with two chapels and a court for exercise, was assigned to him.", "그는 화려하게 장식된 배에 실려 나일강을 따라 멤피스로 옮겨졌으며, 그곳에는 두 개의 예배당과 운동을 위한 마당을 갖춘 신전이 그에게 배정되었다."], ["Sacrifices were made to him, and once every year, about the time when the Nile began to rise, a golden cup was thrown into the river, and a grand festival was held to celebrate his birthday.", "그에게는 제물이 바쳐졌고, 해마다 한 번씩 나일강이 불어나기 시작할 무렵이면 황금 잔 하나를 강물에 던졌으며, 그의 생일을 기리어 성대한 축제가 열렸다."], ["The people believed that during this festival the crocodiles forgot their natural ferocity and became harmless.", "사람들은 이 축제 동안에는 악어들이 타고난 흉포함을 잊고 해를 끼치지 않게 된다고 믿었다."], ["There was, however, one drawback to his happy lot: he was not permitted to live beyond a certain period, and if, when he had attained the age of twenty-five years, he still survived, the priests drowned him in the sacred cistern and then buried him in the temple of Serapis.", "그러나 그의 행복한 처지에는 한 가지 결함이 있었으니, 그는 일정한 기간을 넘겨 살도록 허락되지 않았던 것이다. 만일 그가 스물다섯 살에 이르렀는데도 여전히 살아 있으면, 사제들은 그를 신성한 저수조에 빠뜨려 죽인 다음 세라피스의 신전에 묻었다."], ["On the death of this bull, whether it occurred in the course of nature or by violence, the whole land was filled with sorrow and lamentations, which lasted until his successor was found.", "이 황소가 죽으면, 그것이 자연의 순리로 일어난 것이든 폭력으로 일어난 것이든, 온 땅이 슬픔과 비탄으로 가득 찼고, 그 비탄은 그의 후계자가 발견될 때까지 이어졌다."]], [["We find the following item in one of the newspapers of the day:", "당대의 어느 신문에서 우리는 다음과 같은 기사를 발견한다."]], [["“The Tomb of Apis.—The excavations going on at Memphis bid fair to make that buried city as interesting as Pompeii.", "\"아피스의 무덤.─멤피스에서 진행 중인 발굴은 그 묻혔던 도시를 폼페이만큼이나 흥미로운 곳으로 만들 듯하다."], ["The monster tomb of Apis is now open, after having lain unknown for centuries.”", "수백 년 동안 알려지지 않은 채 묻혀 있던 아피스의 거대한 무덤이 이제 열렸다.\""]], [["Milton, in his “Hymn on the Nativity,” alludes to the Egyptian deities, not as imaginary beings, but as real demons, put to flight by the coming of Christ.", "밀턴은 「그리스도 강탄의 아침에 부치는 찬가」에서 이집트의 신들을 상상의 존재가 아니라 그리스도의 강림으로 쫓겨난 실재하는 악령들로 보아 암시한다."]], [["“The brutish gods of Nile as fast, Isis and Horus and the dog Anubis haste.", "\"나일강의 짐승 같은 신들도 황급히, 이시스와 호루스와 개 머리 아누비스가 서둘러 달아나네."], ["Nor is Osiris seen In Memphian grove or green Trampling the unshowered[35] grass with lowings loud; Nor can he be at rest Within his sacred chest; Nought but profoundest hell can be his shroud.", "오시리스의 모습도 보이지 않으니, 멤피스의 숲에서도 푸른 들에서도 비 한 번 내리지 않은 풀을 큰 울음소리와 함께 짓밟던 그 모습이. 그는 안식할 수도 없으니, 제 신성한 궤 안에서도 마찬가지라."], ["In vain with timbrel’d anthems dark The sable-stoled sorcerers bear his worshipped ark.”", "가장 깊은 지옥 말고는 그 무엇도 그의 수의(壽衣)가 될 수 없네. 헛되이 어두운 소고(小鼓)의 찬가를 울리며 검은 옷 두른 술사들이 그의 숭배받던 궤를 떠메고 가네.\""]], [["Isis was represented in statuary with the head veiled, a symbol of mystery.", "이시스는 조각상에서 머리를 베일로 가린 모습으로 표현되었으니, 신비의 상징이었다. 테니슨이 「모드」"], ["It is this which Tennyson alludes to in “Maud,” IV., 8:", "제4편 8연에서 암시하는 것이 바로 이것이다."]], [["“For the drift of the Maker is dark, an Isis hid by the veil,”", "\"창조주의 의도는 어두우니, 베일에 가려진 이시스라네.\""], ["etc.", "등등."]], [["Oracle was the name used to denote the place where answers were supposed to be given by any of the divinities to those who consulted them respecting the future.", "오라클이란, 미래에 관해 신들에게 자문을 구하는 자들에게 어느 신격이 답을 주는 것으로 여겨진 장소를 가리키는 데 쓰인 이름이었다."], ["The word was also used to signify the response which was given.", "이 말은 또한 그렇게 주어진 응답을 가리키는 데에도 쓰였다."]], [["The most ancient Grecian oracle was that of Jupiter at Dodona.", "가장 오래된 그리스의 신탁소는 도도나에 있던 제우스의 신탁소였다."], ["According to one account, it was established in the following manner: Two black doves took their flight from Thebes in Egypt.", "한 전승에 따르면, 그것은 다음과 같은 방식으로 세워졌다고 한다. 검은 비둘기 두 마리가 이집트의 테바이에서 날아올랐다."], ["One flew to Dodona in Epirus, and alighting in a grove of oaks, it proclaimed in human language to the inhabitants of the district that they must establish there an oracle of Jupiter.", "한 마리는 에페이로스의 도도나로 날아가 참나무 숲에 내려앉아, 그 지역 주민들에게 인간의 언어로 그곳에 제우스의 신탁소를 세워야 한다고 선포했다."], ["The other dove flew to the temple of Jupiter Ammon in the Libyan Oasis, and delivered a similar command there.", "다른 한 마리는 리비아 사막의 오아시스에 있는 유피테르 암몬의 신전으로 날아가, 그곳에서도 비슷한 명령을 전했다."], ["Another account is, that they were not doves, but priestesses, who were carried off from Thebes in Egypt by the Phœnicians, and set up oracles at the Oasis and Dodona.", "또 다른 전승에 따르면, 그들은 비둘기가 아니라 여사제들이었는데, 페니키아인들에게 이집트 테바이에서 끌려가 오아시스와 도도나에 신탁소를 세웠다고 한다."], ["The responses of the oracle were given from the trees, by the branches rustling in the wind, the sounds being interpreted by the priests.", "신탁소의 응답은 나무들로부터, 곧 바람에 흔들리는 가지들의 소리를 통해 주어졌고, 그 소리를 사제들이 해석했다."]], [["But the most celebrated of the Grecian oracles was that of Apollo at Delphi, a city built on the slopes of Parnassus in Phocis.", "그러나 그리스의 신탁소들 가운데 가장 이름난 것은 포키스의 파르나소스 비탈에 세워진 도시 델포이에 있던 아폴론의 신탁소였다."]], [["It had been observed at a very early period that the goats feeding on Parnassus were thrown into convulsions when they approached a certain long deep cleft in the side of the mountain.", "아주 이른 시기에 사람들은 파르나소스에서 풀을 뜯던 염소들이 산허리에 난 어떤 길고 깊은 틈에 다가가면 경련에 사로잡힌다는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["This was owing to a peculiar vapor arising out of the cavern, and one of the goatherds was induced to try its effects upon himself.", "이는 그 동굴에서 솟아오르는 독특한 증기 때문이었는데, 한 염소지기가 그것이 자신에게 어떤 작용을 하는지 시험해 보도록 이끌렸다."], ["Inhaling the intoxicating air, he was affected in the same manner as the cattle had been, and the inhabitants of the surrounding country, unable to explain the circumstance, imputed the convulsive ravings to which he gave utterance while under the power of the exhalations to a divine inspiration.", "그 도취시키는 공기를 들이마시자, 그는 가축들이 그러했던 것과 같은 방식으로 영향을 받았고, 주위 지역의 주민들은 이 일을 설명하지 못한 채, 그가 그 증기의 힘 아래에서 내뱉는 경련에 찬 헛소리를 신의 영감 탓으로 돌렸다."], ["The fact was speedily circulated widely, and a temple was erected on the spot.", "이 사실은 이내 널리 퍼졌고, 그 자리에 신전이 세워졌다."], ["The prophetic influence was at first variously attributed to the goddess Earth, to Neptune, Themis, and others, but it was at length assigned to Apollo, and to him alone.", "예언의 영향력은 처음에는 대지의 여신, 넵투누스, 테미스를 비롯한 여러 신들에게 다양하게 돌려졌으나, 마침내 아폴론에게, 오직 그에게만 돌려졌다."], ["A priestess was appointed whose office it was to inhale the hallowed air, and who was named the Pythia.", "그 신성한 공기를 들이마시는 일을 맡은 여사제가 임명되었으니, 그녀는 피티아라 불렸다."], ["She was prepared for this duty by previous ablution at the fountain of Castalia, and being crowned with laurel was seated upon a tripod similarly adorned, which was placed over the chasm whence the divine afflatus proceeded.", "그녀는 카스탈리아 샘에서 미리 몸을 씻어 이 임무를 위한 준비를 했고, 월계관을 쓴 채 마찬가지로 장식된 삼각대 위에 앉았는데, 그 삼각대는 신의 영기(靈氣)가 솟아오르는 그 틈 위에 놓여 있었다."], ["Her inspired words while thus situated were interpreted by the priests.", "이렇게 자리한 채 그녀가 영감에 차서 내뱉는 말들을 사제들이 해석했다."]], [["# ORACLE OF TROPHONIUS", "# ORACLE OF TROPHONIUS"]], [["Besides the oracles of Jupiter and Apollo, at Dodona and Delphi, that of Trophonius in Bœotia was held in high estimation.", "도도나와 델포이에 있던 제우스와 아폴론의 신탁소들 외에도, 보이오티아에 있던 트로포니오스의 신탁소가 높이 존중되었다."], ["Trophonius and Agamedes were brothers.", "트로포니오스와 아가메데스는 형제였다."], ["They were distinguished architects, and built the temple of Apollo at Delphi, and a treasury for King Hyrieus.", "그들은 뛰어난 건축가로서, 델포이에 아폴론의 신전을 짓고 히리에우스 왕을 위해 보물 창고를 지었다."], ["In the wall of the treasury they placed a stone, in such a manner that it could be taken out; and by this means, from time to time, purloined the treasure.", "그들은 그 창고의 벽에 빼낼 수 있도록 돌 하나를 끼워 두었고, 이 방법으로 때때로 보물을 훔쳤다."], ["This amazed Hyrieus, for his locks and seals were untouched, and yet his wealth continually diminished.", "이 일은 히리에우스를 어리둥절하게 했으니, 그의 자물쇠와 봉인은 손대지 않은 그대로였는데도 그의 재물이 끊임없이 줄어들었기 때문이다."], ["At length he set a trap for the thief and Agamedes was caught.", "마침내 그는 그 도둑을 잡으려고 덫을 놓았고, 아가메데스가 걸려들었다."], ["Trophonius, unable to extricate him, and fearing that when found he would be compelled by torture to discover his accomplice, cut off his head.", "트로포니오스는 그를 빼낼 수 없는 데다, 그가 발각되면 고문을 받아 공범을 발설하도록 강요당할 것을 두려워하여, 그의 머리를 잘랐다."], ["Trophonius himself is said to have been shortly afterwards swallowed up by the earth.", "트로포니오스 자신도 그 직후에 땅에 삼켜졌다고 전해진다."]], [["The oracle of Trophonius was at Lebadea in Bœotia.", "트로포니오스의 신탁소는 보이오티아의 레바데이아에 있었다."], ["During a great drought the Bœotians, it is said, were directed by the god at Delphi to seek aid of Trophonius at Lebadea.", "큰 가뭄이 들었을 때, 보이오티아인들은 델포이의 신으로부터 레바데이아의 트로포니오스에게 도움을 구하라는 지시를 받았다고 한다."], ["They came thither, but could find no oracle.", "그들은 그곳에 이르렀으나 신탁소를 찾을 수 없었다."], ["One of them, however, happening to see a swarm of bees, followed them to a chasm in the earth, which proved to be the place sought.", "그러나 그들 가운데 한 사람이 우연히 한 무리의 벌 떼를 보고 그것들을 따라가 땅에 난 틈에 이르렀는데, 그곳이 바로 찾던 장소로 밝혀졌다."]], [["Peculiar ceremonies were to be performed by the person who came to consult the oracle.", "신탁을 구하러 온 사람은 독특한 의식을 치러야 했다."], ["After these preliminaries, he descended into the cave by a narrow passage.", "이 예비 절차를 마친 뒤, 그는 좁은 통로를 통해 동굴 안으로 내려갔다."], ["This place could be entered only in the night.", "이곳은 오직 밤에만 들어갈 수 있었다."], ["The person returned from the cave by the same narrow passage, but walking backwards.", "그 사람은 같은 좁은 통로를 통해 동굴에서 돌아왔으나, 뒷걸음질로 걸어 나왔다."], ["He appeared melancholy and dejected; and hence the proverb which was applied to a person low-spirited and gloomy, “He has been consulting the oracle of Trophonius.”", "그는 우울하고 풀이 죽은 모습이었으니, 이로부터 기운 없고 침울한 사람에게 적용되는 \"그가 트로포니오스의 신탁소에 다녀왔다\"라는 속담이 생겨났다."]], [["There were numerous oracles of Æsculapius, but the most celebrated one was at Epidaurus.", "아스클레피오스의 신탁소는 여럿 있었으나, 가장 이름난 것은 에피다우로스에 있었다."], ["Here the sick sought responses and the recovery of their health by sleeping in the temple.", "이곳에서 병든 자들은 신전에서 잠을 잠으로써 응답과 건강의 회복을 구했다."], ["It has been inferred from the accounts that have come down to us that the treatment of the sick resembled what is now called Animal Magnetism or Mesmerism.", "우리에게 전해 내려온 기록들로부터, 병든 자들에 대한 그 치료가 오늘날 동물 자기(磁氣) 또는 메스머리즘이라 불리는 것과 비슷했으리라고 추정되어 왔다."]], [["Serpents were sacred to Æsculapius, probably because of a superstition that those animals have a faculty of renewing their youth by a change of skin.", "뱀은 아스클레피오스에게 신성한 것이었으니, 아마도 그 동물들이 허물을 벗음으로써 젊음을 되찾는 능력을 지녔다는 미신 때문이었을 것이다."], ["The worship of Æsculapius was introduced into Rome in a time of great sickness, and an embassy sent to the temple of Epidaurus to entreat the aid of the god.", "아스클레피오스 숭배는 큰 역병이 돌던 시기에 로마에 도입되었으며, 신의 도움을 간청하고자 에피다우로스의 신전에 사절단이 파견되었다."], ["Æsculapius was propitious, and on the return of the ship accompanied it in the form of a serpent.", "아스클레피오스는 호의를 베풀어, 배가 돌아올 때 뱀의 모습으로 그 배에 동행했다."], ["Arriving in the river Tiber, the serpent glided from the vessel and took possession of an island in the river, and a temple was there erected to his honor.", "티베리스강에 이르자, 그 뱀은 배에서 미끄러져 나와 강 가운데의 한 섬을 차지했고, 그곳에 그를 기리는 신전이 세워졌다."]], [["# ORACLE OF APIS", "# ORACLE OF APIS"]], [["At Memphis the sacred bull Apis gave answer to those who consulted him by the manner in which he received or rejected what was presented to him.", "멤피스에서는 신성한 황소 아피스가, 자신에게 바쳐진 것을 받아들이느냐 거부하느냐 하는 방식으로 자문을 구하는 자들에게 답을 주었다."], ["If the bull refused food from the hand of the inquirer it was considered an unfavorable sign, and the contrary when he received it.", "만일 그 황소가 질문하는 자의 손에서 먹이를 거부하면 그것은 불길한 징조로 여겨졌고, 그것을 받아들이면 그 반대로 여겨졌다."]], [["It has been a question whether oracular responses ought to be ascribed to mere human contrivance or to the agency of evil spirits.", "신탁의 응답이 단순한 인간의 술책에 돌려져야 하는지, 아니면 악령의 작용에 돌려져야 하는지는 하나의 논쟁거리가 되어 왔다."], ["The latter opinion has been most general in past ages.", "지난 시대에는 후자의 견해가 가장 널리 퍼져 있었다."], ["A third theory has been advanced since the phenomena of Mesmerism have attracted attention, that something like the mesmeric trance was induced in the Pythoness, and the faculty of clairvoyance really called into action.", "메스머리즘의 현상이 주목을 끌게 된 이래로 제3의 이론이 제기되었으니, 곧 피티아에게 메스머의 가수(假睡) 상태와 비슷한 무언가가 유도되어, 천리안(千里眼)의 능력이 실제로 발동되었다는 것이다."]], [["Another question is as to the time when the Pagan oracles ceased to give responses.", "또 다른 문제는 이교의 신탁소들이 응답을 그친 시점에 관한 것이다."], ["Ancient Christian writers assert that they became silent at the birth of Christ, and were heard no more after that date.", "고대 그리스도교 저술가들은 신탁소들이 그리스도의 탄생과 함께 침묵하게 되었으며, 그날 이후로는 더 이상 들리지 않게 되었다고 주장한다."], ["Milton adopts this view in his “Hymn on the Nativity,” and in lines of solemn and elevated beauty pictures the consternation of the heathen idols at the advent of the Saviour:", "밀턴은 「그리스도 강탄의 아침에 부치는 찬가」에서 이 견해를 받아들여, 엄숙하고 고결한 아름다움을 지닌 시구로 구세주의 강림 앞에서 이교 우상들이 느끼는 경악을 그려 낸다."]], [["“The oracles are dumb; No voice or hideous hum Rings through the arched roof in words deceiving.", "\"신탁소들은 말을 잃었네. 어떤 목소리도, 소름 끼치는 웅얼거림도 아치형 지붕을 통해 속이는 말로 울려 퍼지지 않네."], ["Apollo from his shrine Can no more divine, With hollow shriek the steep of Delphos leaving.", "아폴론은 자신의 성소에서 더 이상 점칠 수 없으니, 공허한 비명과 함께 델포스의 비탈을 떠나가네."], ["No nightly trance or breathed spell Inspires the pale-eyed priest from the prophetic cell.”", "어떤 밤의 황홀경도, 내쉬어진 주문(呪文)도 예언의 골방에서 창백한 눈의 사제에게 영감을 주지 못하네.\""]], [["In Cowper’s poem of “Yardley Oak” there are some beautiful mythological allusions.", "쿠퍼의 시 「야들리의 참나무」에는 아름다운 신화적 암시들이 몇 가지 들어 있다."], ["The former of the two following is to the fable of Castor and Pollux; the latter is more appropriate to our present subject.", "다음 두 대목 가운데 앞의 것은 카스토르와 폴리데우케스의 이야기를 가리키고, 뒤의 것은 우리의 현재 주제에 더 어울린다."], ["Addressing the acorn he says:", "도토리를 향해 그는 이렇게 말한다."]], [["“Thou fell’st mature; and in the loamy clod, Swelling with vegetative force instinct, Didst burst thine egg, as theirs the fabled Twins Now stars; two lobes protruding, paired exact; A leaf succeeded and another leaf, And, all the elements thy puny growth Fostering propitious, thou becam’st a twig.", "\"너는 무르익어 떨어졌고, 그 기름진 흙덩이 속에서 초목의 힘으로 부풀어 본능에 차, 네 알을 터뜨렸으니, 이제 별이 된 그 전설의 쌍둥이가 제 알을 터뜨렸듯이. 두 떡잎이 정확히 짝을 이루어 돋아났고, 한 잎이 뒤따르고 또 한 잎이 뒤따랐으며, 온 자연의 요소들이 네 가냘픈 성장을 호의롭게 길러 주어, 너는 한 가지가 되었네."], ["Who lived when thou wast such?", "네가 그러했을 때 누가 살아 있었던가?"], ["O, couldst thou speak, As in Dodona once thy kindred trees Oracular, I would not curious ask The future, best unknown, but at thy mouth Inquisitive, the less ambiguous past.”", "오, 네가 말할 수 있다면, 옛날 도도나에서 네 동족 나무들이 신탁을 내렸던 것처럼. 나는 알지 못하는 편이 나은 미래를 캐묻고 싶지는 않으나, 네 입에 대고 덜 모호한 과거를 호기심으로 물으려네.\""]], [["Tennyson, in his “Talking Oak,” alludes to the oaks of Dodona in these lines:", "테니슨은 「말하는 참나무」에서 다음 시구로 도도나의 참나무들을 암시한다."]], [["“And I will work in prose and rhyme, And praise thee more in both Than bard has honored beech or lime, Or that Thessalian growth In which the swarthy ring-dove sat And mystic sentence spoke;”", "\"그리고 나는 산문과 운문으로 일하며, 그 둘로 그대를 더욱 기리리라, 어느 시인이 너도밤나무나 보리수를 기린 것보다도, 또는 거무스름한 산비둘기가 앉아 신비로운 말을 내뱉던 그 테살리아의 나무를 기린 것보다도.\""], ["etc.", "등등."]], [["Byron alludes to the oracle of Delphi where, speaking of Rousseau, whose writings he conceives did much to bring on the French revolution, he says:", "바이런은 델포이의 신탁소를 암시하는데, 그 글이 프랑스 혁명을 불러오는 데 큰 몫을 했다고 그가 생각하는 루소를 두고 이렇게 말한다."]], [["“For then he was inspired, and from him came, As from the Pythian’s mystic cave of yore, Those oracles which set the world in flame, Nor ceased to burn till kingdoms were no more.”", "\"그때 그는 영감을 받았으니, 그에게서 나왔네, 옛날 피티아의 신비로운 동굴에서 나왔듯이, 세계를 불길에 휩싸이게 한 그 신탁들이, 왕국들이 더 이상 존재하지 않게 될 때까지 타오르기를 그치지 않았던 그 신탁들이.\""]], [["# CHAPTER XXXV", "# CHAPTER XXXV"]], [["ORIGIN OF MYTHOLOGY—STATUES OF GODS AND GODDESSES—POETS OF MYTHOLOGY\nHAVING reached the close of our series of stories of Pagan mythology, an inquiry suggests itself.", "우리는 이교 신화 이야기들의 연속을 끝맺는 지점에 이르렀고, 이제 한 가지 의문이 자연스럽게 떠오른다. \"이 이야기들은 어디에서 비롯되었는가?"], ["“Whence came these stories? Have they a foundation in truth, or are they simply dreams of the imagination?”", "이것들은 진실에 토대를 두고 있는가, 아니면 단순히 상상력이 빚어낸 꿈에 불과한가?\""], ["Philosophers have suggested various theories on the subject; and 1.", "철학자들은 이 주제에 관하여 여러 이론을 제시해 왔는데, 그 첫 번째는 1."], ["The Scriptural theory; according to which all mythological legends are derived from the narratives of Scripture, though the real facts have been disguised and altered.", "성서적 이론이다. 이 이론에 따르면 모든 신화적 전설은 성서의 서술에서 유래한 것이지만, 다만 실제 사실이 위장되고 변형되었다는 것이다."], ["Thus Deucalion is only another name for Noah, Hercules for Samson, Arion for Jonah, etc.", "그리하여 데우칼리온은 노아의 또 다른 이름일 뿐이며, 헤라클레스는 삼손, 아리온은 요나 등에 해당한다고 본다."], ["Sir Walter Raleigh, in his “History of the World,”", "월터 롤리 경은 그의 「세계사」에서 이렇게 말한다."], ["says, “Jubal, Tubal, and Tubal-Cain were Mercury, Vulcan, and Apollo, inventors of Pasturage, Smithing, and Music.", "\"유발, 투발, 투발카인은 곧 메르쿠리우스, 불카누스, 아폴론으로, 각각 목축과 대장일과 음악의 창시자였다."], ["The Dragon which kept the golden apples was the serpent that beguiled Eve.", "황금 사과를 지키던 용은 이브를 꾄 뱀이었다."], ["Nimrod’s tower was the attempt of the Giants against Heaven.”", "니므롯의 탑은 천상을 향한 거인들의 시도였다.\""], ["There are doubtless many curious coincidences like these, but the theory cannot without extravagance be pushed so far as to account for any great proportion of the stories.", "이와 같은 흥미로운 일치점이 적지 않은 것은 분명하지만, 이 이론을 지나치게 밀어붙여 이야기들의 큰 비중을 설명하려 하면 무리가 따른다."]], [["2.", "2."], ["The Historical theory; according to which all the persons mentioned in mythology were once real human beings, and the legends and fabulous traditions relating to them are merely the additions and embellishments of later times.", "역사적 이론이다. 이 이론에 따르면 신화에 언급된 모든 인물은 한때 실재했던 인간이었으며, 그들에 관한 전설과 황당무계한 전승은 후대에 덧붙고 윤색된 것에 불과하다는 것이다."], ["Thus the story of Æolus, the king and god of the winds, is supposed to have risen from the fact that Æolus was the ruler of some islands in the Tyrrhenian Sea, where he reigned as a just and pious king, and taught the natives the use of sails for ships, and how to tell from the signs of the atmosphere the changes of the weather and the winds.", "그리하여 바람의 왕이자 신인 아이올로스의 이야기는, 아이올로스가 티레니아해의 어느 섬들을 다스린 통치자로서 정의롭고 경건한 왕으로 군림하며 원주민들에게 배의 돛 사용법과 대기의 징후를 보고 날씨와 바람의 변화를 알아내는 법을 가르쳤다는 사실에서 비롯되었다고 본다."], ["Cadmus, who, the legend says, sowed the earth with dragon’s teeth, from which sprang a crop of armed men, was in fact an emigrant from Phœnicia, and brought with him into Greece the knowledge of the letters of the alphabet, which he taught to the natives.", "또한 전설이 말하기를 땅에 용의 이빨을 뿌려 거기에서 무장한 사람들이 솟아났다고 하는 카드모스는, 실은 페니키아에서 온 이주자로서 알파벳 글자에 관한 지식을 그리스로 가지고 들어와 원주민들에게 가르쳤다는 것이다."], ["From these rudiments of learning sprung civilization, which the poets have always been prone to describe as a deterioration of man’s first estate, the Golden Age of innocence and simplicity.", "이러한 학문의 초보로부터 문명이 싹텄으니, 시인들은 줄곧 이 문명을 인간의 첫 상태, 곧 순결과 소박함의 황금시대가 타락한 것으로 묘사하는 경향이 있었다."]], [["3.", "3."], ["The Allegorical theory supposes that all the myths of the ancients were allegorical and symbolical, and contained some moral, religious, or philosophical truth or historical fact, under the form of an allegory, but came in process of time to be understood literally.", "우의적 이론이다. 이 이론은 고대인의 모든 신화가 우의적이고 상징적이며, 우의의 형식 아래 어떤 도덕적, 종교적, 혹은 철학적 진리나 역사적 사실을 담고 있었으나, 세월이 흐르면서 글자 그대로 받아들여지게 되었다고 가정한다."], ["Thus Saturn, who devours his own children, is the same power whom the Greeks called Cronos (Time), which may truly be said to destroy whatever it has brought into existence.", "그리하여 제 자식을 잡아먹는 사투르누스는 그리스인들이 크로노스[시간]라 부른 바로 그 힘과 같으니, 시간이란 자신이 존재케 한 모든 것을 파괴한다고 진실로 말할 수 있다."], ["The story of Io is interpreted in a similar manner.", "이오의 이야기도 비슷한 방식으로 해석된다."], ["Io is the moon, and Argus the starry sky, which, as it were, keeps sleepless watch over her.", "이오는 달이고, 아르고스는 별이 총총한 하늘로서, 이를테면 잠들지 않고 달을 감시한다."], ["The fabulous wanderings of Io represent the continual revolutions of the moon, which also suggested to Milton the same idea.", "이오의 황당무계한 방랑은 달의 끊임없는 운행을 나타내며, 이는 밀턴에게도 같은 착상을 떠올리게 했다."]], [["“To behold the wandering moon Riding near her highest noon, Like one that had been led astray In the heaven’s wide, pathless way.”", "\"방황하는 달을 바라보네 가장 높은 정오 가까이 떠가는, 마치 길을 잃은 자가 하늘의 넓고 길 없는 길을 헤매듯이.\""], ["—Il Penseroso.", "—「생각에 잠긴 사람」"]], [["4.", "4."], ["The Physical theory; according to which the elements of air, fire, and water were originally the objects of religious adoration, and the principal deities were personifications of the powers of nature.", "자연적 이론이다. 이 이론에 따르면 공기, 불, 물의 요소가 본래 종교적 숭배의 대상이었으며, 주요 신들은 자연의 힘을 의인화한 것이었다."], ["The transition was easy from a personification of the elements to the notion of supernatural beings presiding over and governing the different objects of nature.", "요소들을 의인화하는 데에서 자연의 갖가지 대상을 주재하고 다스리는 초자연적 존재라는 관념으로 옮겨가는 일은 쉬웠다."], ["The Greeks, whose imagination was lively, peopled all nature with invisible beings, and supposed that every object, from the sun and sea to the smallest fountain and rivulet, was under the care of some particular divinity.", "상상력이 활발했던 그리스인들은 온 자연을 보이지 않는 존재들로 가득 채웠으며, 태양과 바다에서 가장 작은 샘과 시냇물에 이르기까지 모든 대상이 어떤 특정한 신의 보살핌 아래 있다고 여겼다."], ["Wordsworth, in his “Excursion,” has beautifully developed this view of Grecian mythology:", "워즈워스는 그의 「소요」에서 그리스 신화에 대한 이러한 견해를 아름답게 펼쳐 보였다."]], [["“In that fair clime the lonely herdsman, stretched On the soft grass through half a summer’s day, With music lulled his indolent repose; And, in some fit of weariness, if he, When his own breath was silent, chanced to hear A distant strain far sweeter than the sounds Which his poor skill could make, his fancy fetched Even from the blazing chariot of the Sun A beardless youth who touched a golden lute, And filled the illumined groves with ravishment.", "\"그 아름다운 풍토에서 외로운 목자는, 한여름 한나절을 부드러운 풀밭에 길게 누워, 음악으로 나른한 휴식을 달랬네. 그러다 어느 권태로운 순간, 제 숨소리가 잠잠해졌을 때, 그가 우연히 제 보잘것없는 솜씨로 낼 수 있는 소리보다 훨씬 감미로운 먼 가락을 듣게 되면, 그의 공상은 저 태양의 불타는 수레에서까지 황금 류트를 타며 빛나는 숲을 황홀로 가득 채우는 수염 없는 젊은이를 데려왔네."], ["The mighty hunter, lifting up his eyes Toward the crescent Moon, with grateful heart Called on the lovely Wanderer who bestowed That timely light to share his joyous sport; And hence a beaming goddess with her nymphs Across the lawn and through the darksome grove (Not unaccompanied with tuneful notes By echo multiplied from rock or cave) Swept in the storm of chase, as moon and stars Glance rapidly along the clouded heaven When winds are blowing strong.", "힘센 사냥꾼은 초승달을 향해 눈을 들어, 감사하는 마음으로 그 사랑스러운 방랑자를 불렀으니, 그녀가 제 즐거운 사냥을 함께하라고 때맞춰 빛을 베풀었음이라. 그리하여 빛나는 여신이 그녀의 님프들과 더불어 잔디밭을 가로질러 어두운 숲을 지나 (바위나 동굴에서 메아리에 거듭 울려 퍼지는 가락 어린 음표들과 더불어 결코 동행 없지 않게) 사냥의 폭풍 속을 휩쓸고 지나갔으니, 마치 달과 별이 바람이 거세게 불 때 구름 낀 하늘을 따라 재빨리 스쳐 가듯 했네."], ["The Traveller slaked His thirst from rill or gushing fount, and thanked The Naiad.", "나그네는 시냇물이나 솟구치는 샘에서 갈증을 풀고 나이아스에게 감사했네."], ["Sunbeams upon distant hills Gliding apace with shadows in their train, Might with small help from fancy, be transformed Into fleet Oreads sporting visibly.", "먼 언덕 위의 햇살이 그림자를 거느린 채 빠르게 미끄러져 가는 것은 공상의 작은 도움만 받아도, 눈에 보이게 뛰노는 날랜 오레아데스로 변모할 수 있었네."], ["The Zephyrs, fanning, as they passed, their wings, Lacked not for love fair objects whom they wooed With gentle whisper.", "제피로스들은 지나가며 날개를 부치다가, 부드러운 속삭임으로 구애할 사랑스러운 대상이 없는 법이 없었네."], ["Withered boughs grotesque, Stripped of their leaves and twigs by hoary age, From depth of shaggy covert peeping forth In the low vale, or on steep mountain side; And sometimes intermixed with stirring horns Of the live deer, or goat’s depending beard; These were the lurking Satyrs, a wild brood Of gamesome deities; or Pan himself, That simple shepherd’s awe-inspiring god.”", "늙은 세월에 잎과 잔가지를 벗긴 기괴하게 시든 가지들이, 낮은 골짜기나 가파른 산비탈의 텁수룩한 덤불 깊은 곳에서 빠끔히 내다보고, 때로는 살아 있는 사슴의 움직이는 뿔이나 염소의 늘어진 수염과 뒤섞이면, 이것들은 숨어 있는 사티로스들, 곧 장난기 어린 신들의 거친 무리였네. 혹은 판 자신이었으니, 저 소박한 목자의 경외를 자아내는 신이었네.\""]], [["All the theories which have been mentioned are true to a certain extent.", "지금까지 언급한 모든 이론은 어느 정도까지는 참이다."], ["It would therefore be more correct to say that the mythology of a nation has sprung from all these sources combined than from any one in particular.", "따라서 한 민족의 신화는 어느 한 가지 원천에서 나왔다기보다, 이 모든 원천이 결합하여 생겨났다고 말하는 편이 더 정확할 것이다."], ["We may add also that there are many myths which have arisen from the desire of man to account for those natural phenomena which he cannot understand; and not a few have had their rise from a similar desire of giving a reason for the names of places and persons.", "또한 덧붙이건대, 인간이 이해할 수 없는 자연 현상을 설명하고자 하는 욕구에서 생겨난 신화가 많으며, 장소와 인물의 이름에 까닭을 부여하려는 비슷한 욕구에서 비롯된 것도 적지 않다. 신들의 조각상"]], [["# STATUES OF THE GODS", "# STATUES OF THE GODS"]], [["To adequately represent to the eye the ideas intended to be conveyed to the mind under the several names of deities was a task which called into exercise the highest powers of genius and art.", "여러 신들의 이름 아래 마음에 전하고자 했던 관념을 눈앞에 충분히 재현하는 일은, 천재성과 예술의 최고 역량을 발휘하게 하는 과업이었다."], ["Of the many attempts four have been most celebrated, the first two known to us only by the descriptions of the ancients, the others still extant and the acknowledged masterpieces of the sculptor’s art.", "수많은 시도 가운데 네 가지가 가장 유명한데, 처음 두 가지는 고대인들의 묘사를 통해서만 우리에게 알려져 있고, 나머지 둘은 여전히 현존하며 조각 예술의 공인된 걸작이다. 올림피아의 유피테르"]], [["# THE OLYMPIAN JUPITER", "# THE OLYMPIAN JUPITER"]], [["The statue of the Olympian Jupiter by Phidias was considered the highest achievement of this department of Grecian art.", "페이디아스가 만든 올림피아의 유피테르 조각상은 그리스 예술의 이 분야에서 가장 높은 성취로 여겨졌다."], ["It was of colossal dimensions, and was what the ancients called “chryselephantine;”", "그것은 거대한 규모였으며, 고대인들이 \"황금상아상(黃金象牙像)\"이라 부르던 것, 곧 상아와 황금으로 이루어진 것이었다."], ["that is, composed of ivory and gold; the parts representing flesh being of ivory laid on a core of wood or stone, while the drapery and other ornaments were of gold.", "살을 나타내는 부분은 나무나 돌로 된 심에 상아를 입혔고, 옷자락과 그 밖의 장식은 황금으로 만들었다."], ["The height of the figure was forty feet, on a pedestal twelve feet high.", "상의 높이는 12미터였고, 받침대는 높이가 3.6미터였다."], ["The god was represented seated on his throne.", "신은 자신의 옥좌에 앉은 모습으로 표현되었다."], ["His brows were crowned with a wreath of olive, and he held in his right hand a sceptre, and in his left a statue of Victory.", "그의 이마에는 올리브 화관이 씌워졌고, 오른손에는 홀(笏)을, 왼손에는 승리의 여신 니케 상을 들고 있었다."], ["The throne was of cedar, adorned with gold and precious stones.", "옥좌는 삼나무로 만들어졌으며, 황금과 보석으로 장식되어 있었다."]], [["The idea which the artist essayed to embody was that of the supreme deity of the Hellenic (Grecian) nation, enthroned as a conqueror, in perfect majesty and repose, and ruling with a nod the subject world.", "예술가가 구현하고자 했던 관념은 헬레네스[그리스] 민족의 지고한 신이 정복자로서 옥좌에 앉아, 완전한 위엄과 평정 속에서 고갯짓 하나로 자신에게 종속된 세계를 다스리는 모습이었다. 페이디아스는 자신이 호메로스가 「일리아스」"], ["Phidias avowed that he took his idea from the representation which Homer gives in the first book of the “Iliad,” in the passage thus translated by Pope:", "제1권에서 보여 준 묘사에서 그 착상을 얻었다고 공언했는데, 그 구절은 포프가 다음과 같이 옮긴 것이다."]], [["“He spoke and awful bends his sable brows, Shakes his ambrosial curls and gives the nod, The stamp of fate and sanction of the god.", "\"그가 말하고 위엄 있게 검은 눈썹을 굽히니, 신령한 곱슬머리를 흔들며 고갯짓하였으니, 그것은 운명의 봉인이요 신의 재가였네."], ["High heaven with reverence the dread signal took, And all Olympus to the centre shook.”[36]", "드높은 하늘은 그 두려운 신호를 경외로 받아들였고, 온 올림포스가 중심까지 뒤흔들렸네.\"[36]\n파르테논의 미네르바"]], [["# THE MINERVA OF THE PARTHENON", "# THE MINERVA OF THE PARTHENON"]], [["This was also the work of Phidias.", "이것 또한 페이디아스의 작품이었다."], ["It stood in the Parthenon, or temple of Minerva at Athens.", "그것은 파르테논, 곧 아테나이의 미네르바 신전에 세워져 있었다."], ["The goddess was represented standing.", "여신은 서 있는 모습으로 표현되었다."], ["In one hand she held a spear, in the other a statue of Victory.", "한 손에는 창을, 다른 손에는 승리의 여신 니케 상을 들고 있었다."], ["Her helmet, highly decorated, was surmounted by a Sphinx.", "화려하게 장식된 그녀의 투구 꼭대기에는 스핑크스가 얹혀 있었다."], ["The statue was forty feet in height, and, like the Jupiter, composed of ivory and gold.", "조각상은 높이가 12미터였으며, 유피테르 상과 마찬가지로 상아와 황금으로 이루어졌다."], ["The eyes were of marble, and probably painted to represent the iris and pupil.", "눈은 대리석으로 만들어졌고, 아마도 홍채와 동공을 나타내도록 색칠되었을 것이다."], ["The Parthenon, in which this statue stood, was also constructed under the direction and superintendence of Phidias.", "이 조각상이 서 있던 파르테논 역시 페이디아스의 지휘와 감독 아래 건립되었다."], ["Its exterior was enriched with sculptures, many of them from the hand of Phidias.", "그 외부는 조각으로 풍성하게 꾸며졌으며, 그 가운데 많은 것이 페이디아스의 손에서 나왔다."], ["The Elgin marbles, now in the British Museum, are a part of them.", "지금 대영박물관에 있는 엘긴 대리석상들이 그 일부이다."]], [["Both the Jupiter and Minerva of Phidias are lost, but there is good ground to believe that we have, in several extant statues and busts, the artist’s conceptions of the countenances of both.", "페이디아스의 유피테르와 미네르바는 모두 소실되었으나, 현존하는 여러 조각상과 흉상에서 두 신의 얼굴에 대한 예술가의 구상을 우리가 지니고 있다고 믿을 만한 충분한 근거가 있다."], ["They are characterized by grave and dignified beauty, and freedom from any transient expression, which in the language of art is called repose.", "그것들은 장중하고 위엄 있는 아름다움, 그리고 어떤 일시적 표정으로부터 자유로운 것이 특징인데, 이를 예술 용어로 평정(平靜)이라 부른다. 메디치의 베누스"]], [["# THE VENUS DE’ MEDICI", "# THE VENUS DE’ MEDICI"]], [["The Venus of the Medici is so called from its having been in the possession of the princes of that name in Rome when it first attracted attention, about two hundred years ago.", "메디치의 베누스는 약 이백 년 전 처음 주목을 끌었을 때 로마에서 그 이름을 지닌 군주들의 소유였던 데에서 그렇게 불린다."], ["An inscription on the base records it to be the work of Cleomenes, an Athenian sculptor of 200 B.C., but the authenticity of the inscription is doubtful.", "받침대에 새겨진 명문(銘文)에는 이것이 기원전 200년 아테나이의 조각가 클레오메네스의 작품이라 기록되어 있으나, 그 명문의 진위는 의심스럽다."], ["There is a story that the artist was employed by public authority to make a statue exhibiting the perfection of female beauty, and to aid him in his task the most perfect forms the city could supply were furnished him for models.", "한 이야기에 따르면, 예술가가 공적 권위에 의해 여성미의 완벽함을 보여 주는 조각상을 만들도록 고용되었고, 그 과업을 돕고자 도시가 제공할 수 있는 가장 완벽한 형태들이 모델로 그에게 주어졌다고 한다."], ["It is this which Thomson alludes to in his “Summer”:", "톰슨이 그의 「여름」에서 암시하는 것이 바로 이것이다."]], [["“So stands the statue that enchants the world; So bending tries to veil the matchless boast, The mingled beauties of exulting Greece.”", "\"이렇게 세상을 매혹하는 조각상이 서 있네. 이렇게 몸을 굽혀 견줄 데 없는 자랑을 가리려 하니, 환희에 찬 그리스의 뒤섞인 아름다움들을.\""]], [["Byron also alludes to this statue.", "바이런 또한 이 조각상을 암시한다."], ["Speaking of the Florence Museum, he says:", "그는 피렌체 박물관에 관하여 이렇게 말한다."]], [["“There, too, the goddess loves in stone, and fills The air around with beauty;”", "\"거기에도, 여신이 돌 속에서 사랑하며, 주위의 공기를 아름다움으로 채우네.\""], ["etc.", "등등."]], [["And in the next stanza,", "그리고 다음 연에서는,"]], [["“Blood, pulse, and breast confirm the Dardan shepherd’s prize.”", "\"피와 맥박과 가슴이 다르다니아 목자의 상(賞)을 확증하네.\""]], [["See this last allusion explained in Chapter XXVII.", "이 마지막 암시의 설명은 제27장을 보라. 벨베데레의 아폴론"]], [["# THE APOLLO BELVEDERE", "# THE APOLLO BELVEDERE"]], [["The most highly esteemed of all the remains of ancient sculpture is the statue of Apollo, called the Belvedere, from the name of the apartment of the Pope’s palace at Rome in which it was placed.", "고대 조각의 모든 유물 가운데 가장 높이 평가받는 것은 벨베데레라 불리는 아폴론 조각상으로, 이는 그것이 놓였던 로마 교황 궁전의 방 이름에서 따온 것이다."], ["The artist is unknown.", "예술가는 알려져 있지 않다."], ["It is supposed to be a work of Roman art, of about the first century of our era.", "이것은 우리 시대 약 1세기경의 로마 예술 작품으로 추정된다."], ["It is a standing figure, in marble, more than seven feet high, naked except for the cloak which is fastened around the neck and hangs over the extended left arm.", "그것은 대리석으로 된 서 있는 상으로, 높이가 2미터가 넘으며, 목에 둘러 매어 뻗은 왼팔 위로 늘어진 망토를 빼고는 벌거벗고 있다."], ["It is supposed to represent the god in the moment when he has shot the arrow to destroy the monster Python.", "이것은 신이 괴물 피톤을 죽이려고 화살을 쏜 순간을 나타내는 것으로 추정된다(제3장을 보라)."], ["(See Chapter III.) The victorious divinity is in the act of stepping forward.", "승리를 거둔 신은 앞으로 발을 내딛는 동작을 취하고 있다."], ["The left arm, which seems to have held the bow, is outstretched, and the head is turned in the same direction.", "활을 들고 있었던 듯한 왼팔은 뻗어 있고, 머리도 같은 방향으로 돌려져 있다."], ["In attitude and proportion the graceful majesty of the figure is unsurpassed.", "자세와 비례에 있어 이 상의 우아한 위엄은 어디에도 뒤지지 않는다."], ["The effect is completed by the countenance, where on the perfection of youthful godlike beauty there dwells the consciousness of triumphant power.", "그 효과는 얼굴에서 완성되니, 거기에는 젊고 신과 같은 아름다움의 완벽함 위에 승리를 거둔 힘에 대한 자각이 깃들어 있다."]], [["# THE DIANA A LA BICHE", "# THE DIANA A LA BICHE"]], [["The Diana of the Hind, in the palace of the Louvre, may be considered the counterpart to the Apollo Belvedere.", "사슴과 함께 있는 디아나\n루브르 궁전에 있는 사슴의 디아나는 벨베데레의 아폴론에 대응하는 작품으로 볼 수 있다."], ["The attitude much resembles that of the Apollo, the sizes correspond and also the style of execution.", "그 자세는 아폴론의 것과 매우 비슷하고, 크기도 일치하며 제작 양식 또한 그러하다."], ["It is a work of the highest order, though by no means equal to the Apollo.", "그것은 최고 수준의 작품이나, 결코 아폴론에 견줄 만하지는 않다."], ["The attitude is that of hurried and eager motion, the face that of a huntress in the excitement of the chase.", "자세는 서두르며 열중한 움직임의 것이고, 얼굴은 사냥의 흥분에 사로잡힌 여자 사냥꾼의 것이다."], ["The left hand is extended over the forehead of the Hind, which runs by her side, the right arm reaches backward over the shoulder to draw an arrow from the quiver.", "왼손은 그녀 곁을 달리는 사슴의 이마 위로 뻗어 있고, 오른팔은 어깨 너머로 뒤로 뻗어 화살통에서 화살을 뽑으려 하고 있다."]], [["# THE POETS OF MYTHOLOGY", "# THE POETS OF MYTHOLOGY"]], [["Homer, from whose poems of the “Iliad” and “Odyssey”", "신화의 시인들\n우리가 트로이아 전쟁과 그리스인들의 귀환을 다룬 장들의 주요 부분을 가져온 「일리아스」와 「오디세이아」의 시인 호메로스는, 그가 노래한 영웅들 못지않게 신화적인 인물이다."], ["we have taken the chief part of our chapters of the Trojan war and the return of the Grecians, is almost as mythical a personage as the heroes he celebrates. The traditionary story is that he was a wandering minstrel, blind and old, who travelled from place to place singing his lays to the music of his harp, in the courts of princes or the cottages of peasants, and dependent upon the voluntary offerings of his hearers for support.", "전해 내려오는 이야기에 따르면 그는 눈멀고 늙은 떠돌이 음유시인으로, 군주들의 궁정이나 농부들의 오두막을 이곳저곳 떠돌며 자신의 하프 가락에 맞추어 노래를 부르고, 그 부양은 청중의 자발적인 헌금에 의존했다고 한다."], ["Byron calls him “The blind old man of Scio’s rocky isle,” and a well-known epigram, alluding to the uncertainty of the fact of his birthplace, says:", "바이런은 그를 \"키오스의 바위섬에 사는 눈먼 노인\"이라 부르며, 그의 출생지에 관한 사실의 불확실함을 암시하는 잘 알려진 경구는 이렇게 말한다."]], [["“Seven wealthy towns contend for Homer dead, Through which the living Homer begged his bread.”", "\"일곱 개의 부유한 도시가 죽은 호메로스를 두고 다투나, 살아 있는 호메로스는 그 도시들에서 빵을 구걸하였네.\""]], [["These seven were Smyrna, Scio, Rhodes, Colophon, Salamis, Argos, and Athens.", "이 일곱 도시는 스미르나, 키오스, 로도스, 콜로폰, 살라미스, 아르고스, 그리고 아테나이였다."]], [["Modern scholars have doubted whether the Homeric poems are the work of any single mind.", "근대 학자들은 호메로스의 시들이 과연 어느 한 사람의 마음에서 나온 작품인지 의심해 왔다."], ["This arises from the difficulty of believing that poems of such length could have been committed to writing at so early an age as that usually assigned to these, an age earlier than the date of any remaining inscriptions or coins, and when no materials capable of containing such long productions were yet introduced into use.", "이는 보통 이 시들에 부여되는 그처럼 이른 시대, 곧 현존하는 어떤 비문이나 주화보다도 앞서며, 그토록 긴 작품을 담아낼 만한 재료가 아직 쓰이지 않았던 시대에, 그처럼 긴 시들이 글로 기록될 수 있었다고 믿기 어렵기 때문에 생겨난다."], ["On the other hand it is asked how poems of such length could have been handed down from age to age by means of the memory alone.", "다른 한편으로는, 그처럼 긴 시들이 어떻게 오직 기억만으로 대대로 전해질 수 있었겠느냐는 물음이 제기된다."], ["This is answered by the statement that there was a professional body of men, called Rhapsodists, who recited the poems of others, and whose business it was to commit to memory and rehearse for pay the national and patriotic legends.", "이에 대한 답은, 음송시인이라 불리는 전문적인 집단이 있어 다른 이들의 시를 암송하였으며, 민족적이고 애국적인 전설을 기억해 두었다가 보수를 받고 낭송하는 것이 그들의 일이었다는 것이다."]], [["The prevailing opinion of the learned, at this time, seems to be that the framework and much of the structure of the poems belong to Homer, but that there are numerous interpolations and additions by other hands.", "오늘날 학자들 사이에 우세한 견해는, 시들의 골격과 구조의 많은 부분이 호메로스에게 속하지만, 다른 사람들의 손으로 끼워 넣어지고 덧붙여진 부분이 무수히 많다는 것으로 보인다."]], [["The date assigned to Homer, on the authority of Herodotus, is 850 B.C.", "헤로도토스의 권위에 따라 호메로스에게 부여된 연대는 기원전 850년이다. 베르길리우스"]], [["Virgil, called also by his surname, Maro, from whose poem of the “Æneid” we have taken the story of Æneas, was one of the great poets who made the reign of the Roman emperor Augustus so celebrated, under the name of the Augustan age.", "베르길리우스는 그의 성(姓)인 마로로도 불리는데, 우리가 그의 시 「아이네이스」에서 아이네아스의 이야기를 가져온 그는, 로마 황제 아우구스투스의 치세를 아우구스투스 시대라는 이름으로 그토록 유명하게 만든 위대한 시인들 가운데 하나였다."], ["Virgil was born in Mantua in the year 70 B.C.", "베르길리우스는 기원전 70년 만토바에서 태어났다."], ["His great poem is ranked next to those of Homer, in the highest class of poetical composition, the Epic.", "그의 위대한 시는 호메로스의 작품들에 버금가는 것으로, 시 작법의 최고 부류인 서사시에 든다."], ["Virgil is far inferior to Homer in originality and invention, but superior to him in correctness and elegance.", "베르길리우스는 독창성과 창의에서 호메로스에 훨씬 못 미치지만, 정확성과 우아함에서는 그를 능가한다."], ["To critics of English lineage Milton alone of modern poets seems worthy to be classed with these illustrious ancients.", "영국 혈통의 비평가들에게는 근대 시인 가운데 오직 밀턴만이 이 빛나는 고대인들과 한자리에 놓일 만한 인물로 여겨진다."], ["His poem of “Paradise Lost,” from which we have borrowed so many illustrations, is in many respects equal, in some superior, to either of the great works of antiquity.", "우리가 그토록 많은 인용을 빌려 온 그의 시 「실낙원」은 여러 면에서 고대의 두 위대한 작품 어느 쪽과도 대등하며, 어떤 면에서는 그것들을 능가한다."], ["The following epigram of Dryden characterizes the three poets with as much truth as it is usual to find in such pointed criticism:", "다음에 인용한 드라이든의 경구는, 그런 날카로운 비평에서 흔히 찾을 수 있는 만큼의 진실을 담아 세 시인을 특징짓는다."]], [["“Three poets in three different ages born, Greece, Italy, and England did adorn.", "\"밀턴에 부쳐 \"세 시대에 태어난 세 시인이 그리스와 이탈리아와 잉글랜드를 빛냈네."], ["The first in loftiness of soul surpassed, The next in majesty, in both the last.", "첫째는 영혼의 드높음에서 뛰어났고, 둘째는 위엄에서, 마지막은 그 둘 다에서."], ["The force of nature could no further go; To make a third she joined the other two.”", "자연의 힘은 더 나아갈 수 없었으니, 셋째를 만들려고 앞의 둘을 합쳤네.\""]], [["From Cowper’s “Table Talk”:", "쿠퍼의 「탁상담화」에서."]], [["“Ages elapsed ere Homer’s lamp appeared, And ages ere the Mantuan swan was heard.", "\"호메로스의 등불이 나타나기까지 여러 시대가 흘렀고, 만토바의 백조가 들리기까지 다시 여러 시대가 흘렀네."], ["To carry nature lengths unknown before, To give a Milton birth, asked ages more.", "이전엔 알지 못하던 길이로 자연을 끌어올려, 밀턴을 낳기까지는 더 많은 시대가 필요했네."], ["Thus genius rose and set at ordered times, And shot a dayspring into distant climes, Ennobling every region that he chose; He sunk in Greece, in Italy he rose, And, tedious years of Gothic darkness past, Emerged all splendor in our isle at last.", "그리하여 천재는 정해진 때에 떠오르고 졌으며, 먼 풍토에 새벽빛을 쏘아 보내어, 그가 택한 모든 지역을 고귀하게 하였네. 그는 그리스에서 졌다가 이탈리아에서 떠올랐고, 고딕 암흑의 지루한 세월이 지난 뒤, 마침내 우리 섬에서 온통 찬란하게 솟아올랐네."], ["Thus lovely Halcyons dive into the main, Then show far off their shining plumes again.”", "그리하여 사랑스러운 물총새들이 바다로 잠겨 들었다가, 저 멀리서 다시 그 빛나는 깃털을 보이는도다.\""]], [["often alluded to in poetry by his other name of Naso, was born in the year 43 B.C.", "오비디우스는,\n시에서 흔히 그의 다른 이름인 나소로 암시되는데, 기원전 43년에 태어났다."], ["He was educated for public life and held some offices of considerable dignity, but poetry was his delight, and he early resolved to devote himself to it.", "그는 공직 생활을 위한 교육을 받아 상당히 높은 몇몇 관직을 지냈으나, 시가 그의 기쁨이었기에 일찍이 거기에 자신을 바치기로 결심하였다."], ["He accordingly sought the society of the contemporary poets, and was acquainted with Horace and saw Virgil, though the latter died when Ovid was yet too young and undistinguished to have formed his acquaintance.", "그리하여 그는 당대 시인들과의 교제를 추구하여 호라티우스와 친분을 맺고 베르길리우스를 만났는데, 다만 베르길리우스는 오비디우스가 아직 너무 어리고 이름이 알려지지 않아 친교를 맺기 어려웠던 무렵에 세상을 떠났다."], ["Ovid spent an easy life at Rome in the enjoyment of a competent income.", "오비디우스는 넉넉한 수입을 누리며 로마에서 안락한 삶을 보냈다."], ["He was intimate with the family of Augustus, the emperor, and it is supposed that some serious offence given to some member of that family was the cause of an event which reversed the poet’s happy circumstances and clouded all the latter portion of his life.", "그는 황제 아우구스투스의 가문과 가까웠는데, 그 가문의 누군가에게 끼친 어떤 중대한 잘못이, 시인의 행복한 처지를 뒤엎고 그의 생애 후반 전체를 어둡게 한 사건의 원인이었으리라 추정된다."], ["At the age of fifty he was banished from Rome, and ordered to betake himself to Tomi, on the borders of the Black Sea.", "쉰 살의 나이에 그는 로마에서 추방되어 흑해 변경의 토미로 가라는 명을 받았다."], ["Here, among the barbarous people and in a severe climate, the poet, who had been accustomed to all the pleasures of a luxurious capital and the society of his most distinguished contemporaries, spent the last ten years of his life, worn out with grief and anxiety.", "이곳에서, 야만적인 사람들 사이에서 혹독한 기후 속에, 호사스러운 수도의 온갖 즐거움과 가장 저명한 동시대인들의 교제에 익숙했던 시인은, 슬픔과 근심에 시달리며 생애의 마지막 십 년을 보냈다."], ["His only consolation in exile was to address his wife and absent friends, and his letters were all poetical. Though these poems (the “Trista”", "유배지에서 그의 유일한 위안은 아내와 멀리 있는 벗들에게 글을 띄우는 것이었으며, 그의 편지는 모두 시로 되어 있었다."], ["and “Letters from Pontus”) have no other topic than the poet’s sorrows, his exquisite taste and fruitful invention have redeemed them from the charge of being tedious, and they are read with pleasure and even with sympathy.", "비록 이 시들(「트리스티아」와 「폰토스에서 온 편지」)이 시인 자신의 슬픔 말고는 다른 주제를 다루지 않으나, 그의 빼어난 취향과 풍부한 창의가 그것들을 지루하다는 비난에서 구해 내었으니, 사람들은 즐겁게, 심지어 공감마저 느끼며 그것들을 읽는다."]], [["The two great works of Ovid are his “Metamorphoses” and his “Fasti.”", "오비디우스의 두 위대한 작품은 「변신 이야기」와 「축제력」이다."], ["They are both mythological poems, and from the former we have taken most of our stories of Grecian and Roman mythology.", "둘 다 신화적인 시이며, 우리는 앞의 것에서 그리스와 로마 신화에 관한 이야기 대부분을 가져왔다."], ["A late writer thus characterizes these poems:", "후대의 한 작가는 이 시들을 이렇게 평한다."]], [["“The rich mythology of Greece furnished Ovid, as it may still furnish the poet, the painter, and the sculptor, with materials for his art.", "\"그리스의 풍요로운 신화는, 오늘날에도 시인과 화가와 조각가에게 그러하듯, 오비디우스에게 예술의 재료를 제공하였다."], ["With exquisite taste, simplicity, and pathos he has narrated the fabulous traditions of early ages, and given to them that appearance of reality which only a master hand could impart.", "빼어난 취향과 소박함과 비애로써 그는 먼 옛 시대의 황당무계한 전승을 서술하고, 거기에 오직 거장의 손만이 부여할 수 있는 그 실재감을 입혔다."], ["His pictures of nature are striking and true; he selects with care that which is appropriate; he rejects the superfluous; and when he has completed his work, it is neither defective nor redundant.", "그의 자연 묘사는 인상적이면서도 진실하니, 그는 적절한 것을 정성껏 골라내고 군더더기를 버려서, 작품을 완성했을 때 그것은 부족하지도 넘치지도 않는다."], ["The ‘Metamorphoses’ are read with pleasure by youth, and are re-read in more advanced age with still greater delight.", "「변신 이야기」는 젊은이가 즐겁게 읽고, 더 나이 든 시절에 다시 읽으면 한층 더 큰 기쁨을 준다."], ["The poet ventured to predict that his poem would survive him, and be read wherever the Roman name was known.”", "시인은 자신의 시가 자신보다 오래 살아남아, 로마라는 이름이 알려진 곳이라면 어디서나 읽히리라고 감히 예언하였다.\""]], [["The prediction above alluded to is contained in the closing lines of the “Metamorphoses,” of which we give a literal translation below:", "위에서 암시한 예언은 「변신 이야기」의 마지막 행들에 담겨 있는데, 아래에 그 직역을 싣는다."]], [["“And now I close my work, which not the ire Of Jove, nor tooth of time, nor sword, nor fire Shall bring to nought.", "\"이제 나는 내 작품을 마치노니, 이는 유피테르의 진노도, 세월의 이빨도, 칼도, 불도 무너뜨리지 못하리라."], ["Come when it will that day Which o’er the body, not the mind, has sway, And snatch the remnant of my life away, My better part above the stars shall soar, And my renown endure forevermore.", "몸은 다스리되 마음은 다스리지 못하는 그날이, 언제든 와서 내 남은 생을 앗아 간다 해도, 나의 더 나은 부분은 별들 위로 솟아오를 것이며, 나의 명성은 영원토록 이어지리라."], ["Where’er the Roman arms and arts shall spread, There by the people shall my book be read; And, if aught true in poet’s visions be, My name and fame have immortality.”", "로마의 무력과 학예가 퍼지는 곳이라면 어디서나, 그곳 사람들이 내 책을 읽으리라. 그리고 시인의 환상에 참된 것이 조금이라도 있다면, 나의 이름과 명성은 불멸을 누리리라.\""]], [["————", "————"]], [["# MODERN MONSTERS", "# MODERN MONSTERS"]], [["THERE is a set of imaginary beings which seem to have been the successors of the “Gorgons, Hydras, and Chimeras dire”", "세상에는 일군의 상상 속 존재들이 있는데, 이것들은 옛 미신에 등장하는 \"고르고네스와 히드라들과 무시무시한 키마이라들\"의 후계자처럼 보인다."], ["of the old superstitions, and, having no connection with the false gods of Paganism, to have continued to enjoy an existence in the popular belief after Paganism was superseded by Christianity.", "또한 이교의 거짓 신들과는 아무런 관련이 없었던 까닭에, 이교가 그리스도교에 의해 밀려난 뒤에도 민간의 믿음 속에서 계속 존속할 수 있었던 것처럼 보인다."], ["They are mentioned perhaps by the classical writers, but their chief popularity and currency seem to have been in more modern times.", "어쩌면 이것들은 고전 작가들에 의해 언급되었을지도 모르지만, 그것들이 널리 알려지고 회자된 것은 오히려 더 근대에 들어와서인 듯하다."], ["We seek our accounts of them not so much in the poetry of the ancients as in the old natural history books and narrations of travellers.", "우리는 이것들에 관한 이야기를 고대인의 시에서보다는 오래된 자연사 서적이나 여행자들의 기록에서 찾는다."], ["The accounts which we are about to give are taken chiefly from the Penny Cyclopedia.", "앞으로 우리가 소개할 이야기들은 주로 「페니 백과사전」에서 가져온 것이다."]], [["Ovid tells the story of the Phœnix as follows: “Most beings spring from other individuals; but there is a certain kind which reproduces itself.", "오비디우스는 불사조의 이야기를 다음과 같이 전한다. \"대부분의 존재는 다른 개체로부터 태어난다."], ["The Assyrians call it the Phœnix.", "그러나 스스로를 재생산하는 한 종류가 있다."], ["It does not live on fruit or flowers, but on frankincense and odoriferous gums.", "아시리아인들은 이것을 불사조라고 부른다. 이 새는 열매나 꽃을 먹지 않고, 유향과 향기로운 수지(樹脂)를 먹고 산다."], ["When it has lived five hundred years, it builds itself a nest in the branches of an oak, or on the top of a palm tree.", "오백 년을 살고 나면, 이 새는 떡갈나무 가지나 종려나무 꼭대기에 둥지를 짓는다."], ["In this it collects cinnamon, and spikenard, and myrrh, and of these materials builds a pile on which it deposits itself, and dying, breathes out its last breath amidst odors.", "그 안에 계피와 감송향과 몰약을 모으고, 이 재료들로 더미를 쌓아 그 위에 스스로 자리를 잡고, 죽음을 맞이하며 향기 속에서 마지막 숨을 내쉰다."], ["From the body of the parent bird, a young Phœnix issues forth, destined to live as long a life as its predecessor.", "이 어미 새의 몸에서 어린 불사조가 솟아나오는데, 그것은 자기 선조와 마찬가지로 오랜 생애를 살도록 정해져 있다."], ["When this has grown up and gained sufficient strength, it lifts its nest from the tree (its own cradle and its parent’s sepulchre), and carries it to the city of Heliopolis in Egypt, and deposits it in the temple of the Sun.”", "이 어린 새가 자라나 충분한 힘을 얻으면, 자기 요람이자 어버이의 무덤이었던 둥지를 나무에서 들어 올려, 이집트의 헬리오폴리스 시로 가져가, 태양의 신전에 안치한다.\""]], [["Such is the account given by a poet.", "이것이 한 시인이 전하는 이야기다."], ["Now let us see that of a philosophic historian.", "이제 어느 철학적 역사가의 이야기를 살펴보자."], ["Tacitus says, “In the consulship of Paulus Fabius (A.D.", "타키투스는 이렇게 적었다."], ["34) the miraculous bird known to the world by the name of the Phœnix, after disappearing for a series of ages, revisited Egypt.", "\"파울루스 파비우스의 집정관 재임기(서기 34년)에, 불사조라는 이름으로 세상에 알려진 기적의 새가 여러 시대에 걸쳐 자취를 감추었다가 다시 이집트를 찾아왔다."], ["It was attended in its flight by a group of various birds, all attracted by the novelty, and gazing with wonder at so beautiful an appearance.”", "이 새가 날아오를 때에는 갖가지 새들이 무리지어 뒤따랐는데, 모두가 이 진기함에 이끌려 그토록 아름다운 모습을 경이로운 눈으로 바라보았다.\""], ["He then gives an account of the bird, not varying materially from the preceding, but adding some details.", "그런 다음 그는 이 새에 관한 이야기를 전하는데, 앞의 이야기와 본질적으로 다르지 않으나 몇 가지 세부를 덧붙인다."], ["“The first care of the young bird as soon as fledged, and able to trust to his wings, is to perform the obsequies of his father.", "\"어린 새가 깃털이 다 나서 제 날개를 믿고 날 수 있게 되자마자 가장 먼저 하는 일은 자기 아비의 장례를 치르는 것이다."], ["But this duty is not undertaken rashly.", "그러나 이 임무를 경솔하게 떠맡지는 않는다."], ["He collects a quantity of myrrh, and to try his strength makes frequent excursions with a load on his back.", "어린 새는 상당량의 몰약을 모으고, 자기 힘을 시험하고자 그 짐을 등에 진 채 자주 비행을 떠난다."], ["When he has gained sufficient confidence in his own vigor, he takes up the body of his father and flies with it to the altar of the Sun, where he leaves it to be consumed in flames of fragrance.”", "자신의 기력에 충분한 자신감을 얻으면, 어린 새는 아비의 몸을 들어 올려 태양의 제단으로 날아가, 향기로운 불꽃 속에서 사그라지도록 그것을 그곳에 남겨 둔다.\""], ["Other writers add a few particulars.", "다른 작가들은 몇 가지 세부를 덧붙인다."], ["The myrrh is compacted in the form of an egg, in which the dead Phœnix is enclosed.", "몰약은 알 모양으로 빚어지고, 죽은 불사조는 그 안에 봉인된다."], ["From the mouldering flesh of the dead bird a worm springs, and this worm, when grown large, is transformed into a bird.", "죽은 새의 썩어 가는 살에서 한 마리 벌레가 솟아나며, 이 벌레가 크게 자라면 한 마리 새로 변모한다."], ["Herodotus describes the bird, though he says, “I have not seen it myself, except in a picture.", "헤로도토스는 이 새를 묘사하는데, 그러면서도 이렇게 말한다. \"나는 그림 속에서가 아니면 직접 본 적이 없다."], ["Part of his plumage is gold-colored, and part crimson; and he is for the most part very much like an eagle in outline and bulk.”", "깃털의 일부는 금빛이고 일부는 진홍빛이며, 윤곽과 크기는 대체로 독수리와 매우 흡사하다.\""]], [["The first writer who disclaimed a belief in the existence of the Phœnix was Sir Thomas Browne, in his “Vulgar Errors,”", "불사조의 존재에 대한 믿음을 처음으로 부정한 작가는 토머스 브라운 경으로, 1646년에 펴낸 「세속의 오류들」에서였다. 몇 년 뒤에 알렉산더 로스가 그에게 반박했는데, 불사조가 그토록 좀처럼 모습을 드러내지 않는다는 이의에 답하여 그는 이렇게 말한다."], ["published in 1646.", "\"본능이 그 새에게 만물의 폭군인 인간의 길목을 피하라고 가르친다."], ["He was replied to a few years later by Alexander Ross, who says, in answer to the objection of the Phœnix so seldom making his appearance, “His instinct teaches him to keep out of the way of the tyrant of the creation, man, for if he were to be got at, some wealthy glutton would surely devour him, though there were no more in the world.”", "만일 잡힐 수 있다면, 세상에 단 한 마리밖에 남지 않았다 한들 어느 부유한 대식가가 분명 그것을 먹어 치우고 말 테니 말이다.\""]], [["Dryden in one of his early poems has this allusion to the Phœnix:", "드라이든은 자신의 초기 시 한 편에서 불사조를 이렇게 인유한다."]], [["“So when the new-born Phœnix first is seen, Her feathered subjects all adore their queen, And while she makes her progress through the East, From every grove her numerous train’s increased; Each poet of the air her glory sings, And round him the pleased audience clap their wings.”", "\"그리하여 갓 태어난 불사조가 처음 모습을 드러내면, 깃털 달린 신민들 모두가 제 여왕을 흠모하고, 여왕이 동방을 가로질러 나아가는 동안, 숲마다 그 수많은 행렬이 불어나며, 창공의 시인들마다 그 영광을 노래하고, 기뻐하는 청중은 그 둘레에서 날개를 친다.\""]], [["Milton, in “Paradise Lost,” Book V., compares the angel Raphael descending to earth to a Phœnix:", "밀턴은 「실낙원」제5권에서 천사 라파엘이 지상으로 내려오는 모습을 불사조에 견준다."]], [["“.", "\". ."], [".", "."], [".", "저 아래로, 날개 펴고 곧장 그가 서둘러 내려오니, 광막한 천공을 가로질러 세계와 세계 사이를 한결같은 날개로 항해하고, 이제는 극지의 바람을 타다가, 다시 재빠른 날갯짓으로 풍성한 대기를 부쳐 가른다."], ["Down thither, prone in flight He speeds, and through the vast ethereal sky Sails between worlds and worlds, with steady wing, Now on the polar winds, then with quick fan Winnows the buxom air; till within soar Of towering eagles, to all the fowls he seems A Phœnix, gazed by all; as that sole bird When, to enshrine his relics in the sun’s Bright temple, to Egyptian Thebes he flies.”", "마침내 높이 솟구치는 독수리들의 비상권 안에 이르니, 모든 새들에게 그는 불사조로 보이고, 모두가 그를 바라본다. 마치 제 유해를 태양의 빛나는 신전에 안치하고자 이집트의 테베로 날아가는 그 외로운 새처럼.\""]], [["# THE COCKATRICE, OR BASILISK", "# THE COCKATRICE, OR BASILISK"]], [["This animal was called the king of the serpents.", "이 짐승은 뱀들의 왕이라 불렸다."], ["In confirmation of his royalty, he was said to be endowed with a crest, or comb upon the head, constituting a crown.", "그 왕권을 입증하듯, 이 짐승은 머리 위에 볏 또는 도가머리를 지니고 있어 그것이 왕관을 이루었다고 한다."], ["He was supposed to be produced from the egg of a cock hatched under toads or serpents.", "이 짐승은 수탉의 알이 두꺼비나 뱀의 품에서 부화하여 생겨난다고 여겨졌다. 이 짐승에는 여러 종류가 있었다."], ["There were several species of this animal.", "한 종류는 가까이 다가가는 것은 무엇이든 태워 버렸다."], ["One species burned up whatever they approached; a second were a kind of wandering Medusa’s heads, and their look caused an instant horror which was immediately followed by death.", "두 번째 종류는 일종의 떠도는 메두사의 머리와 같아서, 그 눈길을 받으면 곧바로 공포에 사로잡히고 그 즉시 죽음이 뒤따랐다. 셰익스피어의 희곡 「리처드 3세」에서, 앤 부인은 자기 눈을 칭찬하는 리처드의 말에 대답하며 이렇게 말한다."], ["In Shakspeare’s play of “Richard the Third,” Lady Anne, in answer to Richard’s compliment on her eyes, says, “Would they were basilisk’s, to strike thee dead!”", "\"그 눈이 바실리스크의 눈이어서 당신을 쳐 죽일 수 있다면 좋으련만!\""]], [["The basilisks were called kings of serpents because all other serpents and snakes, behaving like good subjects, and wisely not wishing to be burned up or struck dead, fled the moment they heard the distant hiss of their king, although they might be in full feed upon the most delicious prey, leaving the sole enjoyment of the banquet to the royal monster.", "바실리스크가 뱀들의 왕이라 불린 것은, 다른 모든 뱀과 구렁이가 선량한 신민처럼 처신하여, 현명하게도 태워지거나 쳐 죽임을 당하기를 원치 않은 까닭에, 비록 더없이 맛난 먹이를 한창 포식하고 있는 중이라 할지라도, 멀리서 제 왕의 쉭쉭거리는 소리가 들리는 순간 도망쳐 버리고, 그 잔치의 즐거움을 오롯이 왕다운 그 괴물에게 남겨 두었기 때문이다."]], [["The Roman naturalist Pliny thus describes him: “He does not impel his body, like other serpents, by a multiplied flexion, but advances lofty and upright.", "로마의 박물학자 플리니우스는 이 짐승을 이렇게 묘사한다. \"이것은 다른 뱀들처럼 몸을 여러 차례 굽혀서 나아가는 것이 아니라, 높다랗게 곧추세운 채 나아간다."], ["He kills the shrubs, not only by contact, but by breathing on them, and splits the rocks, such power of evil is there in him.”", "이것은 접촉뿐 아니라 입김만으로도 관목을 죽이고, 바위를 쪼개니, 그토록 강한 사악함의 힘이 그 안에 있다.\""], ["It was formerly believed that if killed by a spear from on horseback the power of the poison conducted through the weapon killed not only the rider, but the horse also.", "옛날에는 만일 이 짐승을 말 탄 자가 창으로 죽이면, 그 무기를 따라 전달된 독의 힘이 기수뿐 아니라 말까지도 죽인다고 믿었다."], ["To this Lucan alludes in these lines:", "루카누스는 이 시구에서 그것을 인유한다."]], [["“What though the Moor the basilisk hath slain, And pinned him lifeless to the sandy plain, Up through the spear the subtle venom flies, The hand imbibes it, and the victor dies.”", "\"무어인이 바실리스크를 죽이고 모래벌판에 그 주검을 꿰어 박은들 무슨 소용인가, 그 미묘한 독은 창을 타고 위로 솟아올라, 손이 그것을 빨아들이니, 승자도 죽고 만다.\""]], [["Such a prodigy was not likely to be passed over in the legends of the saints.", "이런 경이로운 존재가 성인들의 전설에서 그냥 지나쳐졌을 리 없다."], ["Accordingly we find it recorded that a certain holy man, going to a fountain in the desert, suddenly beheld a basilisk.", "그리하여 우리는 다음과 같은 기록을 발견한다. 어느 거룩한 사람이 사막의 샘으로 가다가 문득 바실리스크 한 마리를 보았다."], ["He immediately raised his eyes to heaven, and with a pious appeal to the Deity laid the monster dead at his feet.", "그는 곧바로 눈을 들어 하늘을 우러르고, 경건하게 신께 호소하여 그 괴물을 자기 발치에 쓰러뜨려 죽였다."]], [["These wonderful powers of the basilisk are attested by a host of learned persons, such as Galen, Avicenna, Scaliger, and others.", "바실리스크의 이 놀라운 능력은 갈레노스, 아비센나, 스칼리게르 등 수많은 박식한 인물들이 증언한다."], ["Occasionally one would demur to some part of the tale while he admitted the rest.", "더러는 나머지는 인정하면서도 이야기의 어느 부분에는 이의를 제기하곤 했다."], ["Jonston, a learned physician, sagely remarks, “I would scarcely believe that it kills with its look, for who could have seen it and lived to tell the story?”", "박식한 의사였던 욘스톤은 현명하게도 이렇게 말한다. \"그것이 눈길로 죽인다는 것은 좀처럼 믿을 수 없다."], ["The worthy sage was not aware that those who went to hunt the basilisk of this sort took with them a mirror, which reflected back the deadly glare upon its author, and by a kind of poetical justice slew the basilisk with his own weapon.", "그것을 보고도 살아남아 그 이야기를 전할 수 있는 자가 어디 있겠는가?\" 이 훌륭한 현인은, 이런 종류의 바실리스크를 사냥하러 가는 자들이 거울을 지니고 가서, 그 죽음의 눈빛을 그 주인에게 도로 반사시켜, 일종의 시적인 정의로써 바실리스크를 제 무기로 죽인다는 사실을 알지 못했던 것이다."]], [["But what was to attack this terrible and unapproachable monster?", "그렇다면 이 무시무시하고 가까이할 수 없는 괴물을 공격할 수 있는 것은 무엇이었을까?"], ["There is an old saying that “everything has its enemy”—and the cockatrice quailed before the weasel.", "\"모든 것에는 그 천적이 있다\"는 옛말이 있는데, 바실리스크는 족제비 앞에서 움츠러들었다."], ["The basilisk might look daggers, the weasel cared not, but advanced boldly to the conflict.", "바실리스크가 단검 같은 눈길을 던진들, 족제비는 아랑곳하지 않고 대담하게 싸움에 나섰다."], ["When bitten, the weasel retired for a moment to eat some rue, which was the only plant the basilisks could not wither, returned with renewed strength and soundness to the charge, and never left the enemy till he was stretched dead on the plain.", "물리면 족제비는 잠시 물러나 운향(芸香)을 조금 뜯어 먹었는데, 운향은 바실리스크가 시들게 할 수 없는 유일한 식물이었다. 그런 다음 새로워진 힘과 온전한 몸으로 돌아와 다시 덤벼들었고, 적이 벌판에 쭉 뻗어 죽을 때까지 결코 그를 놓아주지 않았다."], ["The monster, too, as if conscious of the irregular way in which he came into the world, was supposed to have a great antipathy to a cock; and well he might, for as soon as he heard the cock crow he expired.", "또한 이 괴물은 자기가 세상에 태어난 정상적이지 못한 방식을 의식하기라도 한 듯, 수탉에게 큰 반감을 지녔다고 여겨졌다. 그럴 만도 한 것이, 수탉이 우는 소리를 듣자마자 이 괴물은 숨을 거두었기 때문이다."]], [["The basilisk was of some use after death.", "바실리스크는 죽은 뒤에도 어느 정도 쓸모가 있었다."], ["Thus we read that its carcass was suspended in the temple of Apollo, and in private houses, as a sovereign remedy against spiders, and that it was also hung up in the temple of Diana, for which reason no swallow ever dared enter the sacred place.", "그리하여 우리는 그 주검이 아폴론의 신전과 개인의 집에 거미를 막는 으뜸가는 묘약으로 매달렸으며, 또한 아르테미스[로마의 〈디아나〉]의 신전에도 매달렸고, 그 까닭에 어떤 제비도 감히 그 신성한 장소에 들어오지 못했다는 기록을 본다."]], [["The reader will, we apprehend, by this time have had enough of absurdities, but still we can imagine his anxiety to know what a cockatrice was like.", "독자는 이쯤 되면 이런 터무니없는 이야기들을 충분히 들었으리라 짐작한다. 그러나 그래도 우리는 독자가 바실리스크의 생김새가 어떠했는지 알고 싶어 안달하는 모습을 상상할 수 있다."], ["The following is from Aldrovandus, a celebrated naturalist of the sixteenth century, whose work on natural history, in thirteen folio volumes, contains with much that is valuable a large proportion of fables and inutilities.", "다음은 16세기의 저명한 박물학자 알드로반디의 글에서 가져온 것인데, 자연사에 관한 그의 저작은 이절판 열세 권에 달하며, 값진 내용도 많지만 우화와 무익한 이야기도 큰 비중을 차지한다."], ["In particular he is so ample on the subject of the cock and the bull that from his practice, all rambling, gossiping tales of doubtful credibility are called cock and bull stories.", "특히 그는 수탉과 황소에 관해 어찌나 장황하게 썼던지, 그의 그런 습관에서 비롯하여 신빙성이 의심스러운 온갖 두서없고 수다스러운 이야기를 가리켜 〈수탉과 황소 이야기〉라 부르게 되었다."], ["Aldrovandus, however, deserves our respect and esteem as the founder of a botanic garden, and as a pioneer in the now prevalent custom of making scientific collections for purposes of investigation and research.", "그렇지만 알드로반디는 식물원의 창설자로서, 또한 오늘날 널리 퍼진 관습, 곧 조사와 연구를 목적으로 과학적 수집을 행하는 일의 선구자로서 우리의 존경과 흠모를 받을 만하다."]], [["Shelley, in his “Ode to Naples,” full of the enthusiasm excited by the intelligence of the proclamation of a Constitutional Government at Naples, in 1820, thus uses an allusion to the basilisk:", "셸리는 1820년 나폴리에서 입헌 정부의 선포 소식에 자극받은 열정으로 가득한 「나폴리에 바치는 송시」에서 바실리스크를 다음과 같이 인유한다."]], [["“What though Cimmerian anarchs dare blaspheme Freedom and thee?", "\"킴메리아의 폭군들이 감히 자유와 그대를 모독한들 무슨 상관인가?"], ["a new Actæon’s error Shall theirs have been,—devoured by their own hounds!", "그들의 잘못은 새로운 악타이온의 잘못이 되리라, 제 사냥개들에게 잡아먹히고 마는!"], ["Be thou like the imperial basilisk, Killing thy foe with unapparent wounds!", "그대는 제왕다운 바실리스크가 되어, 보이지 않는 상처로 그대의 적을 죽이라!"], ["Gaze on oppression, till at that dread risk, Aghast she pass from the earth’s disk.", "압제를 응시하라, 그 무서운 위험 앞에서 압제가 경악하여 이 땅의 표면에서 사라질 때까지."], ["Fear not, but gaze,—for freemen mightier grow, And slaves more feeble, gazing on their foe.”", "두려워 말고 응시하라. 자유인은 더욱 강해지고, 노예는 제 적을 응시할수록 더욱 약해지니.\""]], [["# THE UNICORN", "# THE UNICORN"]], [["Pliny, the Roman naturalist, out of whose account of the unicorn most of the modern unicorns have been described and figured, records it as “a very ferocious beast, similar in the rest of its body to a horse, with the head of a deer, the feet of an elephant, the tail of a boar, a deep, bellowing voice, and a single black horn, two cubits in length, standing out in the middle of its forehead.”", "로마의 박물학자 플리니우스는 일각수에 관한 이야기를 남겼는데, 근대의 일각수 대부분은 그의 이 기록에서 묘사되고 그려졌다."], ["He adds that “it cannot be taken alive;”", "그는 일각수를 이렇게 기록한다."], ["and some such excuse may have been necessary in those days for not producing the living animal upon the arena of the amphitheatre.", "\"지극히 사나운 짐승으로, 몸의 나머지는 말과 비슷하되, 사슴의 머리, 코끼리의 발, 멧돼지의 꼬리에, 깊고 우렁찬 울음소리를 내며, 이마 한가운데에 길이 두 큐빗의 검은 외뿔 하나가 솟아 있다.\" 그는 \"이것은 산 채로 잡을 수 없다\"고 덧붙이는데, 당시에 원형 경기장의 무대에 이 살아 있는 짐승을 내놓지 못하는 데 대해서는 그런 식의 변명이 필요했을지도 모른다."]], [["The unicorn seems to have been a sad puzzle to the hunters, who hardly knew how to come at so valuable a piece of game.", "일각수는 사냥꾼들에게 몹시 골치 아픈 수수께끼였던 듯한데, 그들은 그토록 값진 사냥감에 어떻게 접근해야 할지 거의 알지 못했다."], ["Some described the horn as movable at the will of the animal, a kind of small sword, in short, with which no hunter who was not exceedingly cunning in fence could have a chance.", "어떤 이들은 그 뿔이 짐승의 뜻대로 움직인다고, 요컨대 일종의 작은 검과 같아서, 검술에 지극히 능란하지 않은 사냥꾼은 도무지 승산이 없다고 묘사했다."], ["Others maintained that all the animal’s strength lay in its horn, and that when hard pressed in pursuit, it would throw itself from the pinnacle of the highest rocks horn foremost, so as to pitch upon it, and then quietly march off not a whit the worse for its fall.", "또 어떤 이들은 그 짐승의 모든 힘이 뿔에 있어서, 추격에 몰리면 가장 높은 바위 꼭대기에서 뿔을 앞세워 몸을 던져 그 뿔로 착지하고, 그런 다음 떨어진 충격에도 조금도 손상 없이 유유히 걸어가 버린다고 주장했다."]], [["But it seems they found out how to circumvent the poor unicorn at last.", "그러나 마침내 그들은 가엾은 일각수를 함정에 빠뜨리는 방법을 알아낸 듯하다."], ["They discovered that it was a great lover of purity and innocence, so they took the field with a young virgin, who was placed in the unsuspecting admirer’s way.", "그들은 이 짐승이 순결과 순수함을 무척 사랑한다는 사실을 발견했고, 그리하여 한 어린 처녀를 데리고 들판에 나가 아무것도 모르는 그 흠모자의 길목에 그녀를 세워 두었다."], ["When the unicorn spied her, he approached with all reverence, couched beside her, and laying his head in her lap, fell asleep.", "일각수는 처녀를 발견하자 온갖 경의를 표하며 다가와 그녀 곁에 엎드려, 그 무릎에 제 머리를 누이고 잠이 들었다."], ["The treacherous virgin then gave a signal, and the hunters made in and captured the simple beast.", "그러면 배신자 처녀가 신호를 보내고, 사냥꾼들이 들이닥쳐 그 순진한 짐승을 사로잡았다."]], [["Modern zoölogists, disgusted as they well may be with such fables as these, disbelieve generally the existence of the unicorn.", "근대의 동물학자들은 이런 우화들에 당연히 진저리를 내며, 일각수의 존재를 대체로 믿지 않는다."], ["Yet there are animals bearing on their heads a bony protuberance more or less like a horn, which may have given rise to the story.", "그렇지만 머리에 뿔과 어느 정도 비슷한 뼈로 된 돌기를 지닌 동물들이 있어, 이것이 이 이야기를 낳았을 수 있다."], ["The rhinoceros horn, as it is called, is such a protuberance, though it does not exceed a few inches in height, and is far from agreeing with the descriptions of the horn of the unicorn.", "이른바 코뿔소의 뿔이 그런 돌기인데, 다만 높이가 몇 인치를 넘지 않으며, 일각수의 뿔에 관한 묘사와는 거리가 멀다."], ["The nearest approach to a horn in the middle of the forehead is exhibited in the bony protuberance on the forehead of the giraffe; but this also is short and blunt, and is not the only horn of the animal, but a third horn, standing in front of the two others.", "이마 한가운데에 있는 뿔에 가장 가까운 것은 기린의 이마에 있는 뼈로 된 돌기인데, 이것 또한 짧고 뭉툭하며, 그 짐승의 유일한 뿔이 아니라 다른 두 뿔 앞쪽에 자리한 세 번째 뿔이다."], ["In fine, though it would be presumptuous to deny the existence of a one-horned quadruped other than the rhinoceros, it may be safely stated that the insertion of a long and solid horn in the living forehead of a horse-like or deer-like animal is as near an impossibility as anything can be.", "요컨대 코뿔소 외에 외뿔 달린 네발짐승의 존재를 부정하는 것은 주제넘은 일이겠으나, 말이나 사슴을 닮은 짐승의 살아 있는 이마에 길고 단단한 뿔이 박혀 있다는 것은 도저히 불가능에 가까운 일이라고 안전하게 말할 수 있다."]], [["# THE SALAMANDER", "# THE SALAMANDER"]], [["The following is from the “Life of Benvenuto Cellini,” an Italian artist of the sixteenth century, written by himself: “When I was about five years of age, my father, happening to be in a little room in which they had been washing, and where there was a good fire of oak burning, looked into the flames and saw a little animal resembling a lizard, which could live in the hottest part of that element. Instantly perceiving what it was, he called for my sister and me, and after he had shown us the creature, he gave me a box on the ear. I fell a-crying, while he, soothing me with caresses, spoke these words: ‘My dear child, I do not give you that blow for any fault you have committed, but that you may recollect that the little creature you see in the fire is a salamander; such a one as never was beheld before to my knowledge.’ So saying he embraced me, and gave me some money.”", "다음은 16세기 이탈리아의 예술가 벤베누토 첼리니가 스스로 쓴 「벤베누토 첼리니의 생애」에서 가져온 것이다. \"내가 다섯 살쯤 되었을 때, 아버지가 마침 빨래를 하던 작은 방에 계셨는데, 그곳에는 떡갈나무 장작불이 잘 타오르고 있었다. 아버지가 불꽃을 들여다보니, 도마뱀을 닮은 작은 짐승 한 마리가 그 불기운의 가장 뜨거운 곳에서도 살 수 있는 듯 보였다. 그것이 무엇인지 곧바로 알아채신 아버지는 나와 누이를 부르셨고, 우리에게 그 짐승을 보여 주신 다음, 내 따귀를 한 대 때리셨다. 내가 울음을 터뜨리자, 아버지는 나를 어루만지며 달래시고는 이런 말씀을 하셨다. '얘야, 네가 무슨 잘못을 저질렀다고 그 매를 때린 것이 아니란다. 다만 네가 불 속에서 보는 그 작은 짐승이 살라만드라라는 것을 기억하라고 그런 것이다. 내가 아는 한 일찍이 본 적이 없는 그런 짐승이지.' 이렇게 말씀하시며 아버지는 나를 껴안고 돈을 좀 주셨다.\""]], [["It seems unreasonable to doubt a story of which Signor Cellini was both an eye and ear witness.", "첼리니 씨가 직접 눈으로 보고 귀로 들은 증인이었던 이야기를 의심하는 것은 부당해 보인다."], ["Add to which the authority of numerous sage philosophers, at the head of whom are Aristotle and Pliny, affirms this power of the salamander.", "게다가 아리스토텔레스와 플리니우스를 필두로 하는 수많은 현명한 철학자들의 권위가 살라만드라의 이 능력을 단언한다."], ["According to them, the animal not only resists fire, but extinguishes it, and when he sees the flame charges it as an enemy which he well knows how to vanquish.", "그들에 따르면, 이 짐승은 불에 저항할 뿐 아니라 불을 끄기까지 하며, 불꽃을 보면 자기가 능히 무찌를 줄 아는 적을 대하듯 그것을 향해 돌진한다."]], [["That the skin of an animal which could resist the action of fire should be considered proof against that element is not to be wondered at.", "불의 작용에 저항할 수 있는 짐승의 가죽이라면 그 불에도 끄떡없으리라 여겨지는 것은 놀랄 일이 아니다."], ["We accordingly find that a cloth made of the skin of salamanders (for there really is such an animal, a kind of lizard) was incombustible, and very valuable for wrapping up such articles as were too precious to be intrusted to any other envelopes.", "그리하여 우리는 살라만드라(실제로 그런 짐승, 곧 일종의 도마뱀이 있다)의 가죽으로 만든 천이 불에 타지 않으며, 다른 어떤 덮개에도 맡길 수 없을 만큼 귀한 물건을 싸 두는 데 매우 값지게 쓰였다는 사실을 발견한다."], ["These fire-proof cloths were actually produced, said to be made of salamander’s wool, though the knowing ones detected that the substance of which they were composed was asbestos, a mineral, which is in fine filaments capable of being woven into a flexible cloth.", "이 불에 타지 않는 천은 실제로 만들어졌으며, 살라만드라의 털로 만든 것이라고 했으나, 사정에 밝은 이들은 그것을 이루는 물질이 석면임을 간파했다. 석면은 가는 섬유 형태로 되어 있어 유연한 천으로 짤 수 있는 광물이다."]], [["The foundation of the above fables is supposed to be the fact that the salamander really does secrete from the pores of his body a milky juice, which when he is irritated is produced in considerable quantity, and would doubtless, for a few moments, defend the body from fire.", "위 우화들의 근거는, 살라만드라가 실제로 제 몸의 모공에서 우윳빛 액체를 분비한다는 사실로 짐작된다. 이 액체는 짐승이 자극을 받으면 상당한 양으로 나오며, 의심할 바 없이 잠시 동안은 그 몸을 불로부터 지켜 줄 것이다."], ["Then it is a hibernating animal, and in winter retires to some hollow tree or other cavity, where it coils itself up and remains in a torpid state till the spring again calls it forth.", "게다가 이것은 겨울잠을 자는 짐승이라서, 겨울이면 속이 빈 나무나 다른 구멍 속으로 들어가 몸을 둘둘 말고, 봄이 다시 그것을 불러낼 때까지 마비된 상태로 머문다."], ["It may therefore sometimes be carried with the fuel to the fire, and wake up only time enough to put forth all its faculties for its defence.", "그러므로 이것은 이따금 땔감과 함께 불 속으로 옮겨질 수 있고, 제 모든 능력을 발휘하여 스스로를 지킬 만큼만 겨우 때맞추어 깨어날 수도 있다."], ["Its viscous juice would do good service, and all who profess to have seen it, acknowledge that it got out of the fire as fast as its legs could carry it; indeed, too fast for them ever to make prize of one, except in one instance, and in that one the animal’s feet and some parts of its body were badly burned.", "그 끈적한 액체가 큰 도움이 될 것이며, 그것을 보았다고 자처하는 모든 이들은 그것이 다리가 옮겨 줄 수 있는 한 가장 빠르게 불 속에서 빠져나갔다고 인정한다. 실로 너무 빨라서, 단 한 차례를 제외하고는 한 마리도 잡을 수 없었으며, 그 한 차례에도 짐승의 발과 몸 일부는 심하게 그을렸다."]], [["Dr. Young, in the “Night Thoughts,” with more quaintness than good taste, compares the sceptic who can remain unmoved in the contemplation of the starry heavens to a salamander unwarmed in the fire:", "영 박사는 「밤의 상념」에서, 그다지 고상하다고는 할 수 없는 기발함으로, 별이 빛나는 하늘을 바라보면서도 아무런 감동 없이 머무는 회의론자를 불 속에서도 데워지지 않는 살라만드라에 견준다."]], [["“An undevout astronomer is mad!", "\"신앙심 없는 천문학자는 미친 것이다!"], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], ["“O, what a genius must inform the skies!", "\"오, 저 하늘에 깃든 것은 얼마나 위대한 천재인가!"], ["And is Lorenzo’s salamander-heart Cold and untouched amid these sacred fires?”", "그런데 로렌초의 살라만드라 같은 심장은 이 신성한 불길 속에서도 차갑고 무감각하단 말인가?\""]], [["————", "————"]], [["# CHAPTER XXXVII", "# CHAPTER XXXVII"]], [["EASTERN MYTHOLOGY—ZOROASTER—HINDU MYTHOLOGY—CASTES—BUDDHA—GRAND LAMA\nOUR knowledge of the religion of the ancient Persians is principally derived from the Zendavesta, or sacred books of that people.", "우리가 고대 페르시아인의 종교에 관해 아는 바는 주로 그들의 경전인 「젠다베스타」에서 유래한다."], ["Zoroaster was the founder of their religion, or rather the reformer of the religion which preceded him.", "조로아스터는 그들 종교의 창시자, 아니 오히려 그에 앞선 종교를 개혁한 인물이었다."], ["The time when he lived is doubtful, but it is certain that his system became the dominant religion of Western Asia from the time of Cyrus (550 B.C.) to the conquest of Persia by Alexander the Great.", "그가 살았던 시기는 분명하지 않으나, 그의 체계가 키루스의 시대(기원전 550년)부터 알렉산드로스 대왕의 페르시아 정복에 이르기까지 서아시아의 지배적 종교가 되었다는 것은 확실하다."], ["Under the Macedonian monarchy the doctrines of Zoroaster appear to have been considerably corrupted by the introduction of foreign opinions, but they afterwards recovered their ascendency.", "마케도니아 왕조 치하에서 조로아스터의 교리는 외래 사상의 유입으로 상당히 변질된 것으로 보이나, 이후 다시 우위를 회복하였다."]], [["Zoroaster taught the existence of a supreme being, who created two other mighty beings and imparted to them as much of his own nature as seemed good to him.", "조로아스터는 한 지고의 존재가 있다고 가르쳤는데, 그 존재는 또 다른 두 강력한 존재를 창조하고 자신의 본성 가운데 자신이 합당하다고 여긴 만큼을 그들에게 나누어 주었다."], ["Of these, Ormuzd (called by the Greeks Oromasdes) remained faithful to his creator, and was regarded as the source of all good, while Ahriman (Arimanes) rebelled, and became the author of all evil upon the earth.", "이 가운데 오르무즈드(그리스인은 오로마스데스라 불렀다)는 창조자에게 충실하여 모든 선의 근원으로 간주되었으나, 아흐리만(아리마네스)은 반역하여 지상의 모든 악의 장본인이 되었다."], ["Ormuzd created man and supplied him with all the materials of happiness; but Ahriman marred this happiness by introducing evil into the world, and creating savage beasts and poisonous reptiles and plants.", "오르무즈드는 인간을 창조하고 그에게 행복에 필요한 온갖 재료를 마련해 주었으나, 아흐리만은 세상에 악을 들여오고 사나운 짐승과 독을 품은 파충류며 식물을 만들어 냄으로써 이 행복을 손상시켰다."], ["In consequence of this, evil and good are now mingled together in every part of the world, and the followers of good and evil—the adherents of Ormuzd and Ahriman—carry on incessant war.", "그 결과 이제 악과 선은 세상 곳곳에서 뒤섞여 있고, 선과 악을 따르는 자들, 곧 오르무즈드와 아흐리만의 추종자들은 끊임없는 전쟁을 이어 간다."], ["But this state of things will not last forever.", "그러나 이러한 상태가 영원히 지속되지는 않을 것이다."], ["The time will come when the adherents of Ormuzd shall everywhere be victorious, and Ahriman and his followers be consigned to darkness forever.", "오르무즈드의 추종자들이 어디서나 승리하고, 아흐리만과 그의 추종자들이 영원히 어둠에 처해지는 때가 올 것이다."]], [["The religious rites of the ancient Persians were exceedingly simple.", "고대 페르시아인의 종교 의례는 지극히 소박하였다."], ["They used neither temples, altars, nor statues, and performed their sacrifices on the tops of mountains.", "그들은 신전도, 제단도, 조각상도 쓰지 않았으며, 산꼭대기에서 제사를 올렸다."], ["They adored fire, light, and the sun as emblems of Ormuzd, the source of all light and purity, but did not regard them as independent deities.", "그들은 불과 빛과 태양을 모든 빛과 순수함의 근원인 오르무즈드의 상징으로 숭배하였으나, 그것들을 독립된 신격으로 여기지는 않았다."], ["The religious rites and ceremonies were regulated by the priests, who were called Magi.", "종교적 예식과 의례는 마기라 불리는 사제들이 관장하였다."], ["The learning of the Magi was connected with astrology and enchantment, in which they were so celebrated that their name was applied to all orders of magicians and enchanters.", "마기의 학문은 점성술과 주술과 관련되어 있었는데, 그 방면에서 워낙 이름이 높았던 까닭에 그들의 이름은 온갖 부류의 마법사와 주술사를 가리키는 말로 쓰이게 되었다."]], [["Wordsworth thus alludes to the worship of the Persians:", "워즈워스는 페르시아인의 숭배를 이렇게 노래하였다."]], [["“. . . the Persian,—zealous to reject Altar and Image, and the inclusive walls And roofs of temples built by human hands,— The loftiest heights ascending, from their tops, With myrtle-wreathed Tiara on his brows, Presented sacrifice to Moon and Stars, And to the Winds and mother Elements, And the whole circle of the Heavens, for him A sensitive existence and a God.” —Excursion, Book IV.", "“……페르시아인은—제단과 형상을, 그리고 사람의 손으로 지은 신전의 둘러싼 벽과 지붕을 열렬히 물리치고는— 가장 높은 봉우리에 올라, 그 꼭대기에서, 이마에 도금양 화관을 두른 채, 달과 별들에게, 그리고 바람과 어머니 같은 원소들에게, 또한 하늘의 온 둘레에게 제사를 바쳤으니, 그에게 그것은 살아 숨 쉬는 존재이자 신이었다.” —「소요(逍遙)」, 제4권."]], [["In “Childe Harold” Byron speaks thus of the Persian worship:", "바이런은 「차일드 해럴드」에서 페르시아인의 숭배를 이렇게 말한다."]], [["“Not vainly did the early Persian make His altar the high places and the peak Of earth-o’er-gazing mountains, and thus take A fit and unwalled temple, there to seek The Spirit, in whose honor shrines are weak, Upreared of human hands.", "“초기 페르시아인이 자신의 제단을 높은 곳과 대지를 굽어보는 산봉우리로 삼은 것은 헛된 일이 아니었으니, 그리하여 그는 벽 없는 알맞은 신전을 얻어, 거기서 신을 찾았던 것이다. 사람의 손으로 세운 성소가 그 영광 앞에서 미약한 그 신을."], ["Come and compare Columns and idol-dwellings, Goth or Greek, With Nature’s realms of worship, earth and air, Nor fix on fond abodes to circumscribe thy prayer.”", "와서 견주어 보라, 고트풍이든 그리스풍이든, 기둥과 우상의 거처를, 자연의 숭배의 영역인 대지와 대기와. 그대의 기도를 가둘 정겨운 거처에 매이지 말라.”"], ["III., 91.", "제3편, 91연."]], [["The religion of Zoroaster continued to flourish even after the introduction of Christianity, and in the third century was the dominant faith of the East, till the rise of the Mahometan power and the conquest of Persia by the Arabs in the seventh century, who compelled the greater number of the Persians to renounce their ancient faith.", "조로아스터의 종교는 그리스도교가 들어온 뒤에도 번성을 이어 갔고, 3세기에는 동방의 지배적 신앙이었으나, 마호메트 세력이 일어나고 7세기에 아랍인이 페르시아를 정복하자, 그들은 대다수의 페르시아인에게 옛 신앙을 버리도록 강요하였다."], ["Those who refused to abandon the religion of their ancestors fled to the deserts of Kerman and to Hindustan, where they still exist under the name of Parsees, a name derived from Pars, the ancient name of Persia.", "조상의 종교를 버리기를 거부한 자들은 케르만의 사막과 힌두스탄으로 달아났으며, 그곳에서 그들은 오늘날까지도 파르시라는 이름으로 존속하고 있는데, 이 이름은 페르시아의 옛 이름인 파르스에서 유래하였다."], ["The Arabs call them Guebers, from an Arabic word signifying unbelievers.", "아랍인은 그들을 게베르라 부르는데, 이는 불신자를 뜻하는 아랍어에서 나온 말이다."], ["At Bombay the Parsees are at this day a very active, intelligent, and wealthy class.", "봄베이에서 파르시는 오늘날 매우 활동적이고 지적이며 부유한 계층을 이룬다."], ["For purity of life, honesty, and conciliatory manners, they are favorably distinguished.", "삶의 청정함과 정직함, 그리고 온화한 처신으로 그들은 좋은 평판을 얻고 있다."], ["They have numerous temples to Fire, which they adore as the symbol of the divinity.", "그들은 불을 모시는 신전을 여럿 두고 있으며, 그 불을 신성의 상징으로 숭배한다."]], [["The Persian religion makes the subject of the finest tale in Moore’s “Lalla Rookh,”", "페르시아의 종교는 무어의 「랄라 루크」 가운데 가장 아름다운 이야기인 「불을 섬기는 자들」의 소재가 된다."], ["the “Fire Worshippers.” The Gueber chief says,", "게베르족 우두머리는 이렇게 말한다."]], [["“Yes!", "“그렇다!"], ["I am of that impious race, Those slaves of Fire, that morn and even Hail their creator’s dwelling-place Among the living lights of heaven; Yes!", "나는 그 불경한 족속, 불의 종들 가운데 하나이니, 아침저녁으로 하늘의 살아 있는 빛들 사이에 깃든 창조자의 거처에 인사를 올린다. 그렇다!"], ["I am of that outcast crew To Iran and to vengeance true, Who curse the hour your Arabs came To desecrate our shrines of flame, And swear before God’s burning eye, To break our country’s chains or die.”", "나는 그 버림받은 무리에 속하니, 이란에, 그리고 복수에 충실하여, 그대의 아랍인들이 와서 우리의 불꽃 성소를 더럽힌 그 시각을 저주하며, 신의 불타는 눈앞에서 맹세하노라, 우리 나라의 사슬을 끊지 못하면 죽으리라고.”"]], [["# HINDU MYTHOLOGY", "# HINDU MYTHOLOGY"]], [["The religion of the Hindus is professedly founded on the Vedas.", "힌두인의 종교는 명목상 베다에 근거를 둔다."], ["To these books of their scripture they attach the greatest sanctity, and state that Brahma himself composed them at the creation.", "그들은 자신들의 이 경전에 더없는 신성함을 부여하며, 천지창조 때 브라마 자신이 그것을 지었다고 말한다."], ["But the present arrangement of the Vedas is attributed to the sage Vyasa, about five thousand years ago.", "그러나 현재 전하는 베다의 편제는 약 오천 년 전 현인 비아사의 것으로 여겨진다."]], [["The Vedas undoubtedly teach the belief of one supreme God.", "베다는 의심할 여지 없이 유일한 지고신에 대한 믿음을 가르친다."], ["The name of this deity is Brahma.", "이 신격의 이름은 브라마이다."], ["His attributes are represented by the three personified powers of creation, preservation, and destruction, which under the respective names of Brahma, Vishnu, and Siva form the Trimurti or triad of principal Hindu gods.", "그의 속성은 창조와 보존과 파괴라는 세 가지 의인화된 권능으로 나타나는데, 각기 브라마, 비슈누, 시바라는 이름 아래 트리무르티, 곧 힌두 주신(主神)의 삼위를 이룬다."], ["Of the inferior gods the most important are: 1.", "하위 신들 가운데 가장 중요한 것은 다음과 같다."], ["Indra, the god of heaven, of thunder, lightning, storm, and rain; 2.", "첫째, 하늘과 천둥과 번개와 폭풍과 비의 신 인드라."], ["Agni, the god of fire; 3.", "둘째, 불의 신 아그니."], ["Yama, the god of the infernal regions; 4.", "셋째, 저승의 신 야마."], ["Surya, the god of the sun.", "넷째, 태양의 신 수리야."]], [["Brahma is the creator of the universe, and the source from which all the individual deities have sprung, and into which all will ultimately be absorbed.", "브라마는 우주의 창조자이며, 모든 개별 신격이 솟아난 근원이자 종국에는 모두가 흡수되어 들어갈 그곳이다."], ["“As milk changes to curd, and water to ice, so is Brahma variously transformed and diversified, without aid of exterior means of any sort.”", "“우유가 응유로 바뀌고 물이 얼음으로 바뀌듯이, 브라마는 어떠한 외부의 수단도 없이 갖가지로 변모하고 다양해진다.”"], ["The human soul, according to the Vedas, is a portion of the supreme ruler, as a spark is of the fire.", "베다에 따르면 인간의 영혼은, 마치 불에서 튄 불티가 그러하듯, 지고의 통치자의 한 부분이다."]], [["Vishnu occupies the second place in the triad of the Hindus, and is the personification of the preserving principle.", "비슈누는 힌두인의 삼위 가운데 두 번째 자리를 차지하며, 보존의 원리를 의인화한 존재이다."], ["To protect the world in various epochs of danger, Vishnu descended to the earth in different incarnations, or bodily forms, which descents are called Avatars.", "위기에 처한 여러 시대에 세상을 보호하기 위하여, 비슈누는 갖가지 화신, 곧 육신의 형상으로 지상에 내려왔는데, 이 강림을 아바타르라 부른다."], ["They are very numerous, but ten are more particularly specified.", "그 수는 매우 많으나, 그중 열 가지가 특히 자세히 지목된다."], ["The first Avatar was as Matsya, the Fish, under which form Vishnu preserved Manu, the ancestor of the human race, during a universal deluge.", "첫 번째 아바타르는 물고기인 마트시아였으니, 이 형상으로 비슈누는 온 세상을 덮친 대홍수 동안 인류의 조상 마누를 보호하였다."], ["The second Avatar was in the form of a Tortoise, which form he assumed to support the earth when the gods were churning the sea for the beverage of immortality, Amrita.", "두 번째 아바타르는 거북의 형상이었는데, 신들이 불사의 음료 암리타를 얻으려 바다를 휘저을 때 대지를 떠받치고자 이 형상을 취하였다."]], [["We may omit the other Avatars, which were of the same general character, that is, interpositions to protect the right or to punish wrong-doers, and come to the ninth, which is the most celebrated of the Avatars of Vishnu, in which he appeared in the human form of Krishna, an invincible warrior, who by his exploits relieved the earth from the tyrants who oppressed it.", "나머지 아바타르들은 같은 성격, 곧 정의를 지키거나 악행을 저지른 자를 벌하려는 개입의 사례이므로 생략하고, 비슈누의 아바타르 가운데 가장 이름난 아홉 번째로 넘어갈 수 있다. 여기서 그는 무적의 전사 크리슈나라는 인간 형상으로 나타났으니, 그는 자신의 위업으로 대지를 짓누르던 폭군들로부터 세상을 구원하였다."]], [["Buddha is by the followers of the Brahmanical religion regarded as a delusive incarnation of Vishnu, assumed by him in order to induce the Asuras, opponents of the gods, to abandon the sacred ordinances of the Vedas, by which means they lost their strength and supremacy.", "부처는 브라마교 신앙의 추종자들에 의해 비슈누의 기만적 화신으로 간주된다. 비슈누가 신들의 적인 아수라들로 하여금 베다의 신성한 율법을 저버리도록 꾀하려고 이 화신을 취하였으며, 그 수단으로 아수라들은 자신들의 힘과 우위를 잃었다는 것이다."]], [["Kalki is the name of the tenth Avatar, in which Vishnu will appear at the end of the present age of the world to destroy all vice and wickedness, and to restore mankind to virtue and purity.", "칼키는 열 번째 아바타르의 이름으로, 비슈누는 현세의 시대가 끝날 무렵 이 모습으로 나타나 온갖 악덕과 사악함을 멸하고 인류를 미덕과 순결로 되돌릴 것이다."]], [["Siva is the third person of the Hindu triad.", "시바는 힌두 삼위의 세 번째 존재이다."], ["He is the personification of the destroying principle.", "그는 파괴의 원리를 의인화한 존재이다."], ["Though the third name, he is, in respect to the number of his worshippers and the extension of his worship, before either of the others.", "비록 세 번째 이름이긴 하나, 그를 숭배하는 자의 수와 그 숭배의 범위로 보면 그는 다른 둘 가운데 어느 쪽보다도 앞선다."], ["In the Puranas (the scriptures of the modern Hindu religion) no allusion is made to the original power of this god as a destroyer; that power not being to be called into exercise till after the expiration of twelve millions of years, or when the universe will come to an end; and Mahadeva (another name for Siva) is rather the representative of regeneration than of destruction.", "푸라나(현대 힌두교의 경전)에서는 파괴자로서의 이 신의 본래 권능에 대해 아무런 언급이 없는데, 그 권능은 천이백만 년이 지난 뒤, 곧 우주가 종말에 이를 때라야 비로소 발휘되기 때문이다. 그리하여 마하데바(시바의 또 다른 이름)는 파괴라기보다는 오히려 재생의 표상이라 하겠다."]], [["The worshippers of Vishnu and Siva form two sects, each of which proclaims the superiority of its favorite deity, denying the claims of the other, and Brahma, the creator, having finished his work, seems to be regarded as no longer active, and has now only one temple in India, while Mahadeva and Vishnu have many.", "비슈누의 숭배자들과 시바의 숭배자들은 두 종파를 이루는데, 저마다 자기가 받드는 신격의 우월함을 내세우며 다른 쪽의 주장을 부인한다. 창조자 브라마는 자신의 일을 마쳤으므로 더 이상 활동하지 않는 것으로 여겨지는 듯하며, 이제 인도에 신전을 단 하나만 두고 있는 데 반해, 마하데바와 비슈누는 여럿을 두고 있다."], ["The worshippers of Vishnu are generally distinguished by a greater tenderness for life, and consequent abstinence from animal food, and a worship less cruel than that of the followers of Siva.", "비슈누의 숭배자들은 대체로 생명을 더 가엾이 여기고, 그에 따라 동물성 음식을 삼가며, 시바 추종자들의 숭배보다 덜 잔혹한 숭배를 행하는 것으로 구별된다."]], [["Whether the worshippers of Juggernaut are to be reckoned among the followers of Vishnu or Siva, our authorities differ.", "자간나트의 숭배자들을 비슈누의 추종자로 보아야 할지 시바의 추종자로 보아야 할지에 관해서는 우리의 전거들이 엇갈린다."], ["The temple stands near the shore, about three hundred miles south-west of Calcutta.", "그 신전은 캘커타에서 남서쪽으로 약 삼백 마일 떨어진 해안 가까이에 서 있다."], ["The idol is a carved block of wood, with a hideous face, painted black, and a distended blood-red mouth.", "우상은 깎아 만든 나무토막으로, 흉측한 얼굴은 검게 칠해져 있고 핏빛으로 벌어진 입을 하고 있다."], ["On festival days the throne of the image is placed on a tower sixty feet high, moving on wheels.", "축제일이면 이 형상의 좌대가 바퀴로 움직이는 높이 육십 피트의 탑 위에 놓인다."], ["Six long ropes are attached to the tower, by which the people draw it along.", "탑에는 여섯 가닥의 긴 밧줄이 매여 있어 사람들이 그것으로 탑을 끌고 간다."], ["The priests and their attendants stand round the throne on the tower, and occasionally turn to the worshippers with songs and gestures.", "사제들과 그 시중꾼들은 탑 위의 좌대를 둘러싸고 서서, 이따금 숭배자들을 향해 노래와 몸짓을 한다."], ["While the tower moves along numbers of the devout worshippers throw themselves on the ground, in order to be crushed by the wheels, and the multitude shout in approbation of the act, as a pleasing sacrifice to the idol.", "탑이 나아가는 동안 독실한 숭배자들 가운데 다수가 바퀴에 깔리려고 땅바닥에 몸을 내던지며, 군중은 그 행위가 우상에게 바치는 흡족한 제물이라며 환호로 그것을 추인한다."], ["Every year, particularly at two great festivals in March and July, pilgrims flock in crowds to the temple.", "해마다, 특히 3월과 7월의 두 큰 축제 때에는 순례자들이 무리 지어 신전으로 몰려든다."], ["Not less than seventy or eighty thousand people are said to visit the place on these occasions, when all castes eat together.", "이러한 때에는 이곳을 찾는 사람이 적어도 칠팔만 명에 이른다고 하며, 그때만큼은 모든 카스트가 함께 음식을 먹는다."]], [["The division of the Hindus into classes or castes, with fixed occupations, existed from the earliest times.", "힌두인을 정해진 직업을 가진 계급, 곧 카스트로 나누는 일은 아주 먼 옛날부터 있었다."], ["It is supposed by some to have been founded upon conquest, the first three castes being composed of a foreign race, who subdued the natives of the country and reduced them to an inferior caste.", "어떤 이들은 그것이 정복에서 비롯되었다고 보는데, 처음의 세 카스트는 외래 종족으로 이루어져 그들이 그 땅의 토착민을 정복하여 하위 카스트로 떨어뜨렸다는 것이다."], ["Others trace it to the fondness of perpetuating, by descent from father to son, certain offices or occupations.", "또 어떤 이들은 특정 직무나 직업을 아비에게서 아들로 대물림하여 영속시키려는 애착에서 그 기원을 찾는다."]], [["The Hindu tradition gives the following account of the origin of the various castes: At the creation Brahma resolved to give the earth inhabitants who should be direct emanations from his own body.", "힌두인의 전승은 여러 카스트의 기원에 대해 다음과 같이 전한다. 천지창조 때 브라마는 자신의 몸에서 직접 발산되어 나온 거주자들을 대지에 주기로 결심하였다."], ["Accordingly from his mouth came forth the eldest born, Brahma (the priest), to whom he confided the four Vedas; from his right arm issued Shatriya (the warrior), and from his left, the warrior’s wife.", "그리하여 그의 입에서 맏아들 브라만(사제)이 나왔으니, 브라마는 그에게 네 베다를 맡겼다. 그의 오른팔에서는 샤트리야(전사)가, 왼팔에서는 그 전사의 아내가 나왔다."], ["His thighs produced Vaissyas, male and female (agriculturists and traders), and lastly from his feet sprang Sudras (mechanics and laborers).", "그의 넓적다리는 남녀 바이샤(농사꾼과 상인)를 낳았고, 마지막으로 그의 발에서 수드라(직공과 노동자)가 솟아났다."]], [["The four sons of Brahma, so significantly brought into the world, became the fathers of the human race, and heads of their respective castes.", "이토록 의미심장하게 세상에 나온 브라마의 네 아들은 인류의 조상이자 각 카스트의 우두머리가 되었다."], ["They were commanded to regard the four Vedas as containing all the rules of their faith, and all that was necessary to guide them in their religious ceremonies.", "그들은 네 베다를 자기 신앙의 모든 규율과 종교 의례에서 그들을 인도할 모든 것을 담은 책으로 여기라는 명을 받았다."], ["They were also commanded to take rank in the order of their birth, the Brahmans uppermost, as having sprung from the head of Brahma.", "또한 그들은 태어난 순서대로 서열을 정하되, 브라만이 브라마의 머리에서 솟아난 까닭에 맨 위에 서라는 명도 받았다."]], [["A strong line of demarcation is drawn between the first three castes and the Sudras.", "처음의 세 카스트와 수드라 사이에는 뚜렷한 경계선이 그어져 있다."], ["The former are allowed to receive instruction from the Vedas, which is not permitted to the Sudras.", "앞의 셋은 베다로부터 가르침을 받는 것이 허용되나, 수드라에게는 허용되지 않는다."], ["The Brahmans possess the privilege of teaching the Vedas, and were in former times in exclusive possession of all knowledge.", "브라만은 베다를 가르치는 특권을 지니며, 옛날에는 모든 지식을 독차지하고 있었다."], ["Though the sovereign of the country was chosen from the Shatriya class, also called Rajputs, the Brahmans possessed the real power, and were the royal counsellors, the judges and magistrates of the country; their persons and property were inviolable; and though they committed the greatest crimes, they could only be banished from the kingdom.", "그 나라의 군주는 라지푸트라고도 불리는 샤트리야 계급에서 선택되었으나, 실제 권력은 브라만이 쥐고 있었으니, 그들은 왕의 고문이자 그 나라의 재판관이며 행정관이었다. 그들의 신체와 재산은 침범할 수 없었고, 비록 가장 큰 죄를 저지른다 하여도 왕국에서 추방되는 것이 고작이었다."], ["They were to be treated by sovereigns with the greatest respect, for “a Brahman, whether learned or ignorant, is a powerful divinity.”", "군주들은 그들을 더없는 존경으로 대해야 했으니, “브라만은 학식이 있든 무지하든 강력한 신격”이기 때문이었다."]], [["When the Brahman arrives at years of maturity it becomes his duty to marry.", "브라만이 장성한 나이에 이르면 혼인하는 것이 그의 의무가 된다."], ["He ought to be supported by the contributions of the rich, and not to be obliged to gain his subsistence by any laborious or productive occupation.", "그는 부유한 자들의 헌납으로 부양되어야 하며, 어떤 고된 일이나 생산적인 직업으로 생계를 꾸려야 할 처지에 놓여서는 안 된다."], ["But as all the Brahmans could not be maintained by the working classes of the community, it was found necessary to allow them to engage in productive employments.", "그러나 모든 브라만을 공동체의 노동 계급이 부양할 수는 없었으므로, 그들이 생산적인 일에 종사하는 것을 허용할 수밖에 없었다."]], [["We need say little of the two intermediate classes, whose rank and privileges may be readily inferred from their occupations.", "중간의 두 계급에 대해서는 별로 말할 것이 없으니, 그들의 지위와 특권은 그들의 직업에서 쉽게 미루어 알 수 있다."], ["The Sudras or fourth class are bound to servile attendance on the higher classes, especially the Brahmans, but they may follow mechanical occupations and practical arts, as painting and writing, or become traders or husbandmen.", "네 번째 계급인 수드라는 상위 계급, 특히 브라만에게 노예처럼 시중드는 일에 매여 있으나, 그림이나 글쓰기 같은 기계적 직업과 실용적 기예에 종사하거나 상인이나 농부가 될 수도 있다."], ["Consequently they sometimes grow rich, and it will also sometimes happen that Brahmans become poor.", "그 결과 그들은 때로 부유해지기도 하며, 또한 브라만이 가난해지는 일도 더러 일어난다."], ["That fact works its usual consequence, and rich Sudras sometimes employ poor Brahmans in menial occupations.", "그 사실은 으레 따르는 결과를 낳아, 부유한 수드라가 가난한 브라만을 비천한 일에 부리기도 한다."]], [["There is another class lower even than the Sudras, for it is not one of the original pure classes, but springs from an unauthorized union of individuals of different castes.", "수드라보다도 더 낮은 또 하나의 계급이 있는데, 이는 본래의 순수한 계급에 속하지 않고 서로 다른 카스트에 속한 개인들의 허락되지 않은 결합에서 생겨난 것이다."], ["These are the Pariahs, who are employed in the lowest services and treated with the utmost severity.", "이들이 바로 파리아로, 가장 천한 일에 부려지며 더없이 가혹한 대우를 받는다."], ["They are compelled to do what no one else can do without pollution.", "그들은 다른 누구도 더럽혀지지 않고서는 할 수 없는 일을 하도록 강요받는다."], ["They are not only considered unclean themselves, but they render unclean everything they touch.", "그들은 그들 자신이 부정하다고 여겨질 뿐만 아니라, 그들이 손대는 모든 것을 부정하게 만든다."], ["They are deprived of all civil rights, and stigmatized by particular laws regulating their mode of life, their houses, and their furniture.", "그들은 일체의 시민적 권리를 빼앗기며, 그들의 생활 방식과 집과 가구를 규제하는 특별한 법으로 낙인찍힌다."], ["They are not allowed to visit the pagodas or temples of the other castes, but have their own pagodas and religious exercises.", "그들은 다른 카스트의 파고다나 신전을 찾는 것이 허용되지 않고, 자신들만의 파고다와 종교 행사를 갖는다."], ["They are not suffered to enter the houses of the other castes; if it is done incautiously or from necessity, the place must be purified by religious ceremonies.", "그들은 다른 카스트의 집에 들어가는 것이 용납되지 않으며, 부주의하게 또는 부득이하게 그런 일이 일어나면 그 자리는 종교 의례로 정화되어야 한다."], ["They must not appear at public markets, and are confined to the use of particular wells, which they are obliged to surround with bones of animals, to warn others against using them.", "그들은 공공의 시장에 나타나서는 안 되며, 특정한 우물만 쓰도록 제한되는데, 다른 이들이 그 우물을 쓰지 않도록 경고하기 위해 그 둘레에 짐승의 뼈를 둘러놓아야 한다."], ["They dwell in miserable hovels, distant from cities and villages, and are under no restrictions in regard to food, which last is not a privilege, but a mark of ignominy, as if they were so degraded that nothing could pollute them.", "그들은 도시와 마을에서 멀리 떨어진 비참한 오두막에 살며, 음식에 관해서는 아무런 제약을 받지 않는데, 이것은 특권이 아니라 치욕의 표지이니, 마치 그들이 너무도 비천하여 무엇으로도 더럽혀질 것이 없는 것처럼 여겨지는 까닭이다."], ["The three higher castes are prohibited entirely the use of flesh.", "상위의 세 카스트는 고기를 먹는 것이 전적으로 금지된다."], ["The fourth is allowed to use all kinds except beef, but only the lowest caste is allowed every kind of food without restriction.", "네 번째 카스트는 쇠고기를 제외한 모든 종류를 먹는 것이 허용되나, 오직 가장 낮은 카스트만이 어떠한 음식이든 제약 없이 먹는 것이 허용된다."]], [["Buddha, whom the Vedas represent as a delusive incarnation of Vishnu, is said by his followers to have been a mortal sage, whose name was Gautama, called also by the complimentary epithets of Sakyasinha, the Lion, and Buddha, the Sage.", "베다가 비슈누의 기만적 화신으로 묘사하는 부처는, 그 추종자들에 따르면 본디 가우타마라는 이름의 인간 현인이었다고 하는데, 그는 사자(獅子)를 뜻하는 사키아신하와 현인을 뜻하는 부처라는 칭송의 별칭으로도 불린다."]], [["By a comparison of the various epochs assigned to his birth, it is inferred that he lived about one thousand years before Christ.", "그의 탄생에 부여된 여러 시기를 견주어 보면, 그는 그리스도보다 약 천 년 전에 살았던 것으로 추정된다."]], [["He was the son of a king; and when in conformity to the usage of the country he was, a few days after his birth, presented before the altar of a deity, the image is said to have inclined its head as a presage of the future greatness of the new-born prophet.", "그는 한 왕의 아들이었다. 그 나라의 관습에 따라 태어난 지 며칠 뒤 한 신격의 제단 앞에 바쳐졌을 때, 그 형상이 새로 태어난 예언자의 장차 이루어질 위대함을 예고하듯 머리를 숙였다고 한다."], ["The child soon developed faculties of the first order, and became equally distinguished by the uncommon beauty of his person.", "그 아이는 곧 으뜸가는 자질을 드러냈고, 보기 드문 용모의 아름다움으로도 똑같이 두드러졌다."], ["No sooner had he grown to years of maturity than he began to reflect deeply on the depravity and misery of mankind, and he conceived the idea of retiring from society and devoting himself to meditation.", "장성한 나이에 이르자마자 그는 인류의 타락과 비참함을 깊이 성찰하기 시작하였고, 세상에서 물러나 명상에 몸을 바치겠다는 생각을 품었다."], ["His father in vain opposed this design.", "그의 아버지는 이 뜻을 막으려 하였으나 헛수고였다."], ["Buddha escaped the vigilance of his guards, and having found a secure retreat, lived for six years undisturbed in his devout contemplations.", "부처는 호위병들의 경계를 벗어나, 안전한 은신처를 찾아 육 년 동안 방해받지 않고 경건한 사색에 잠겨 지냈다."], ["At the expiration of that period he came forward at Benares as a religious teacher.", "그 기간이 끝나자 그는 베나레스에 종교적 스승으로 나섰다."], ["At first some who heard him doubted of the soundness of his mind; but his doctrines soon gained credit, and were propagated so rapidly that Buddha himself lived to see them spread all over India.", "처음에는 그의 말을 들은 어떤 이들이 그의 정신이 온전한지 의심하였으나, 그의 교리는 이내 신망을 얻었고 워낙 빠르게 퍼져 나가, 부처 자신이 그것이 인도 전역에 퍼지는 것을 살아서 보았다."], ["He died at the age of eighty years.", "그는 여든 살의 나이로 세상을 떠났다."]], [["The Buddhists reject entirely the authority of the Vedas, and the religious observances prescribed in them and kept by the Hindus.", "불교도는 베다의 권위와, 거기에 규정되어 힌두인이 지키는 종교적 의례를 전적으로 배격한다."], ["They also reject the distinction of castes, and prohibit all bloody sacrifices, and allow animal food.", "그들은 또한 카스트의 구별을 배격하고, 일체의 피 흘리는 제사를 금하며, 동물성 음식을 허용한다."], ["Their priests are chosen from all classes; they are expected to procure their maintenance by perambulation and begging, and among other things it is their duty to endeavor to turn to some use things thrown aside as useless by others, and to discover the medicinal power of plants.", "그들의 사제는 모든 계급에서 선택되며, 떠돌며 동냥하여 생계를 마련하도록 기대되고, 다른 무엇보다 남들이 쓸모없다며 내버린 것을 어떻게든 쓸모 있게 만들고 식물의 약효를 찾아내는 일이 그들의 의무이다."], ["But in Ceylon three orders of priests are recognized; those of the highest order are usually men of high birth and learning, and are supported at the principal temples, most of which have been richly endowed by the former monarchs of the country.", "그러나 실론에서는 세 등급의 사제가 인정되는데, 가장 높은 등급에 속한 자들은 대개 가문이 좋고 학식이 높은 이들로, 주요 신전에서 부양을 받으며, 그 신전 대부분은 그 나라의 옛 군주들이 풍족하게 기진(寄進)한 것이다."]], [["For several centuries after the appearance of Buddha, his sect seems to have been tolerated by the Brahmans, and Buddhism appears to have penetrated the peninsula of Hindustan in every direction, and to have been carried to Ceylon, and to the eastern peninsula.", "부처가 나타난 뒤 여러 세기 동안 그의 종파는 브라만들의 묵인을 받았던 것으로 보이며, 불교는 힌두스탄 반도 곳곳으로 사방으로 스며들었고, 실론과 동쪽 반도로도 전해진 것으로 보인다."], ["But afterwards it had to endure in India a long-continued persecution, which ultimately had the effect of entirely abolishing it in the country where it had originated, but to scatter it widely over adjacent countries.", "그러나 그 뒤 인도에서는 오래도록 이어진 박해를 견뎌야 했으니, 그 박해는 끝내 그것이 발생한 바로 그 나라에서 그것을 완전히 없애 버리는 결과를 낳았으나, 한편으로는 그것을 인접한 여러 나라로 널리 흩뿌리는 결과도 낳았다."], ["Buddhism appears to have been introduced into China about the year 65 of our era.", "불교는 우리 기원으로 65년경에 중국으로 도입된 것으로 보인다."], ["From China it was subsequently extended to Corea, Japan, and Java.", "중국으로부터 그것은 이후 고려와 일본과 자바로 퍼져 나갔다."]], [["# THE GRAND LAMA", "# THE GRAND LAMA"]], [["It is a doctrine alike of the Brahminical Hindus and of the Buddhist sect that the confinement of the human soul, an emanation of the divine spirit, in a human body, is a state of misery, and the consequence of frailties and sins committed during former existences.", "신성한 정신의 발산물인 인간의 영혼이 인간의 육체에 갇혀 있는 것은 비참한 상태이며, 이전의 생존 동안 저지른 약점과 죄과의 결과라는 것은 브라만교 힌두인과 불교 종파 양쪽이 한가지로 지니는 교리이다."], ["But they hold that some few individuals have appeared on this earth from time to time, not under the necessity of terrestrial existence, but who voluntarily descended to the earth to promote the welfare of mankind.", "그러나 그들은, 지상의 생존을 겪어야 할 필요가 없으면서도 인류의 안녕을 도모하기 위해 스스로 지상에 내려온 소수의 사람들이 때때로 이 땅에 나타났다고 믿는다."], ["These individuals have gradually assumed the character of reappearances of Buddha himself, in which capacity the line is continued till the present day, in the several Lamas of Thibet, China, and other countries where Buddhism prevails.", "이러한 사람들은 차츰 부처 자신이 다시 나타난 존재라는 성격을 띠게 되었고, 그러한 자격으로 그 계보는 오늘날까지 이어져, 티베트와 중국, 그리고 불교가 성행하는 그 밖의 나라들의 여러 라마로 계속되고 있다."], ["In consequence of the victories of Gengis Khan and his successors, the Lama residing in Thibet was raised to the dignity of chief pontiff of the sect.", "칭기즈칸과 그 후계자들의 승리의 결과로, 티베트에 머무는 라마는 그 종파의 으뜸 사제의 지위로 격상되었다."], ["A separate province was assigned to him as his own territory, and besides his spiritual dignity he became to a limited extent a temporal monarch.", "그에게는 그 자신의 영토로 따로 한 지방이 주어졌고, 그는 영적 위엄에 더하여 제한된 범위에서나마 세속의 군주가 되었다."], ["He is styled the Dalai Lama.", "그는 달라이 라마라 일컬어진다."]], [["The first Christian missionaries who proceeded to Thibet were surprised to find there in the heart of Asia a pontifical court and several other ecclesiastical institutions resembling those of the Roman Catholic church.", "티베트로 나아간 초기 그리스도교 선교사들은 아시아의 한복판에서 로마 가톨릭 교회의 그것을 닮은 교황청 같은 궁정과 그 밖의 여러 교회 제도를 발견하고는 놀랐다."], ["They found convents for priests and nuns; also processions and forms of religious worship, attended with much pomp and splendor; and many were induced by these similarities to consider Lamaism as a sort of degenerated Christianity.", "그들은 사제와 수녀를 위한 수도원을 발견하였고, 또한 큰 위엄과 화려함이 곁들여진 행렬과 종교 예배의 형식도 발견하였다. 많은 이들이 이러한 유사함에 이끌려 라마교를 일종의 타락한 그리스도교로 여기게 되었다."], ["It is not improbable that the Lamas derived some of these practices from the Nestorian Christians, who were settled in Tartary when Buddhism was introduced into Thibet.", "라마들이 이러한 관행 가운데 일부를, 티베트에 불교가 도입되던 무렵 타타르에 정착해 있던 네스토리우스파 그리스도교도에게서 끌어왔을 가능성도 없지 않다."]], [["# PRESTER JOHN", "# PRESTER JOHN"]], [["An early account, communicated probably by travelling merchants, of a Lama or spiritual chief among the Tartars, seems to have occasioned in Europe the report of a Presbyter or Prester John, a Christian pontiff resident in Upper Asia. The Pope sent a mission in search of him, as did also Louis IX.", "타타르인 사이에 라마, 곧 영적 우두머리가 있다는 것에 관해, 아마도 떠도는 상인들이 전했을 어떤 초기의 소식이, 유럽에서 상부 아시아에 거주하는 그리스도교 교황 프레스비테르 또는 프레스터 존에 관한 소문을 불러일으킨 듯하다."], ["of France, some years later, but both missions were unsuccessful, though the small communities of Nestorian Christians, which they did find, served to keep up the belief in Europe that such a personage did exist somewhere in the East.", "교황은 그를 찾아 사절을 보냈고, 몇 년 뒤 프랑스의 루이 9세도 그러하였으나, 두 사절단 모두 성과를 거두지 못하였다. 다만 그들이 실제로 찾아낸 작은 규모의 네스토리우스파 그리스도교도 공동체들이 동방 어딘가에 그러한 인물이 실재한다는 믿음을 유럽에서 이어 가게 하는 데 한몫을 하였다."], ["At last in the fifteenth century, a Portuguese traveller, Pedro Covilham, happening to hear that there was a Christian prince in the country of the Abessines (Abyssinia), not far from the Red Sea, concluded that this must be the true Prester John.", "마침내 15세기에 포르투갈의 여행가 페드로 코빌량이, 홍해에서 멀지 않은 아비시니인(아비시니아)의 나라에 한 그리스도교 군주가 있다는 말을 우연히 듣고는, 이가 분명 진짜 프레스터 존일 것이라고 결론지었다."], ["He accordingly went thither, and penetrated to the court of the king, whom he calls Negus.", "그리하여 그는 그리로 가서, 자신이 네구스라 부르는 그 왕의 궁정까지 들어갔다. 밀턴은 「실낙원」"], ["Milton alludes to him in “Paradise Lost,” Book XI., where, describing Adam’s vision of his descendants in their various nations and cities, scattered over the face of the earth, he says,—", "제11권에서 그를 언급하는데, 거기서 그는 아담이 대지의 표면에 흩어진 갖가지 민족과 도시 속에서 살아가는 그 후손들을 환영으로 보는 장면을 묘사하며 이렇게 말한다."]], [["“. . . Nor did his eyes not ken Th’ empire of Negus, to his utmost port, Ercoco, and the less maritime kings, Mombaza and Quiloa and Melind.”", "“……그의 눈은 또한 알아보았으니 네구스의 제국을, 그 가장 먼 항구 에르코코에 이르기까지, 그리고 더 작은 바닷가 왕들, 몸바자와 퀼로아와 멜린드를.”"]], [["————", "————"]], [["# NORTHERN MYTHOLOGY", "# NORTHERN MYTHOLOGY"]], [["THE stories which have engaged our attention thus far relate to the mythology of southern regions.", "지금까지 우리의 관심을 끌었던 이야기들은 남방 지역의 신화에 관한 것이었다."], ["But there is another branch of ancient superstitions which ought not to be entirely overlooked, especially as it belongs to the nations from which we, through our English ancestors, derive our origin.", "그러나 고대의 미신 가운데에는 결코 완전히 간과해서는 안 될 또 하나의 갈래가 있으니, 특히 그것은 우리가 잉글랜드인 선조들을 통해 그 기원을 물려받은 여러 민족에 속한 것이기 때문이다."], ["It is that of the northern nations, called Scandinavians, who inhabited the countries now known as Sweden, Denmark, Norway, and Iceland.", "이는 곧 스칸디나비아인이라 불리는 북방 민족들의 신화로서, 그들은 오늘날 스웨덴, 덴마크, 노르웨이, 아이슬란드로 알려진 나라들에 거주했다."], ["These mythological records are contained in two collections called the Eddas, of which the oldest is in poetry and dates back to the year 1056, the more modern or prose Edda being of the date of 1640.", "이 신화적 기록들은 에다라고 불리는 두 묶음의 문헌에 담겨 있는데, 그중 더 오래된 것은 운문으로 되어 있으며 그 연대가 1056년까지 거슬러 올라가고, 더 근래의 것 곧 산문 에다는 1640년의 것이다."]], [["According to the Eddas there was once no heaven above nor earth beneath, but only a bottomless deep, and a world of mist in which flowed a fountain.", "에다에 따르면 한때는 위로 하늘도 없고 아래로 땅도 없었으며, 오직 바닥 없는 심연과, 그 안에 샘이 흐르는 안개의 세계만이 있었다."], ["Twelve rivers issued from this fountain, and when they had flowed far from their source, they froze into ice, and one layer accumulating over another, the great deep was filled up.", "이 샘에서 열두 줄기의 강이 흘러나왔고, 그 강들이 발원지에서 멀리 흘러갔을 때 얼음으로 얼어붙었으며, 한 켜 위에 또 한 켜가 쌓여 그 거대한 심연이 가득 메워졌다."]], [["Southward from the world of mist was the world of light.", "안개의 세계에서 남쪽으로는 빛의 세계가 있었다."], ["From this flowed a warm wind upon the ice and melted it.", "그곳에서 따뜻한 바람이 얼음 위로 불어와 얼음을 녹였다."], ["The vapors rose in the air and formed clouds, from which sprang Ymir, the Frost giant and his progeny, and the cow Audhumbla, whose milk afforded nourishment and food to the giant.", "수증기가 공중으로 솟아올라 구름을 이루었고, 그 구름에서 서리 거인 이미르와 그 자손이, 그리고 암소 아우둠블라가 생겨났는데, 이 암소의 젖은 거인에게 자양분과 양식을 주었다."], ["The cow got nourishment by licking the hoar frost and salt from the ice.", "암소는 얼음에서 서리와 소금을 핥아 자양분을 얻었다."], ["While she was one day licking the salt stones there appeared at first the hair of a man, on the second day the whole head, and on the third the entire form endowed with beauty, agility, and power.", "어느 날 그 암소가 소금기 어린 돌을 핥고 있을 때, 처음에는 한 사람의 머리카락이 나타났고, 이튿날에는 머리 전체가, 사흘째에는 아름다움과 민첩함과 힘을 갖춘 온전한 형상이 드러났다."], ["This new being was a god, from whom and his wife, a daughter of the giant race, sprang the three brothers Odin, Vili, and Ve.", "이 새로운 존재는 한 신이었으며, 그와 그의 아내인 거인족의 한 딸에게서 오딘, 빌리, 베 세 형제가 태어났다."], ["They slew the giant Ymir, and out of his body formed the earth, of his blood the seas, of his bones the mountains, of his hair the trees, of his skull the heavens, and of his brain clouds, charged with hail and snow.", "그들은 거인 이미르를 죽였고, 그 몸으로 땅을 빚었으며, 그 피로 바다를, 그 뼈로 산을, 그 머리카락으로 나무를, 그 두개골로 하늘을, 그 뇌로는 우박과 눈을 머금은 구름을 만들었다."], ["Of Ymir’s eyebrows the gods formed Midgard (mid earth), destined to become the abode of man.", "신들은 이미르의 눈썹으로 미드가르드[가운데 땅]를 빚었으니, 이는 장차 인간의 거처가 될 운명이었다."]], [["Odin then regulated the periods of day and night and the seasons by placing in the heavens the sun and moon and appointing to them their respective courses.", "그런 다음 오딘은 하늘에 해와 달을 두고 그것들에게 각자의 운행을 정해 줌으로써 낮과 밤의 시기와 계절을 다스렸다."], ["As soon as the sun began to shed its rays upon the earth, it caused the vegetable world to bud and sprout.", "해가 땅 위로 그 빛을 비추기 시작하자마자, 식물의 세계가 싹트고 움텄다."], ["Shortly after the gods had created the world they walked by the side of the sea, pleased with their new work, but found that it was still incomplete, for it was without human beings.", "신들이 세상을 창조한 직후, 그들은 자신들의 새로운 작품에 흡족해하며 바닷가를 거닐었으나, 그것이 아직 완전하지 못함을 알았으니, 거기에 인간이 없었기 때문이다."], ["They therefore took an ash tree and made a man out of it, and they made a woman out of an elder, and called the man Aske and the woman Embla.", "그리하여 그들은 물푸레나무 한 그루를 가져다 그것으로 남자를 만들었고, 딱총나무로 여자를 만들었으며, 남자를 아스케라 부르고 여자를 엠블라라 불렀다."], ["Odin then gave them life and soul, Vili reason and motion, and Ve bestowed upon them the senses, expressive features, and speech.", "그런 다음 오딘은 그들에게 생명과 영혼을 주었고, 빌리는 이성과 운동을, 베는 감각과 풍부한 표정과 언어를 베풀어 주었다."], ["Midgard was then given them as their residence, and they became the progenitors of the human race.", "미드가르드가 그들의 거처로 주어졌으며, 그들은 인류의 시조가 되었다."]], [["The mighty ash tree Ygdrasill was supposed to support the whole universe.", "거대한 물푸레나무 위그드라실은 온 우주를 떠받친다고 여겨졌다."], ["It sprang from the body of Ymir, and had three immense roots, extending one into Asgard (the dwelling of the gods), the other into Jotunheim (the abode of the giants), and the third to Niffleheim (the regions of darkness and cold).", "그것은 이미르의 몸에서 솟아났으며, 세 개의 어마어마한 뿌리를 지녔으니, 하나는 아스가르드[신들의 거처]로, 또 하나는 요툰헤임[거인들의 거처]으로, 셋째는 니플헤임[어둠과 추위의 영역]으로 뻗어 있었다."], ["By the side of each of these roots is a spring, from which it is watered.", "이 뿌리들 각각의 곁에는 샘이 하나씩 있어 거기서 물을 댄다."], ["The root that extends into Asgard is carefully tended by the three Norns, goddesses, who are regarded as the dispensers of fate.", "아스가르드로 뻗은 뿌리는 운명을 관장하는 자로 여겨지는 세 여신, 곧 노른들이 정성껏 돌본다."], ["They are Urdur (the past), Verdandi (the present), Skuld (the future).", "그들은 우르두르[과거], 베르단디[현재], 스쿨드[미래]이다."], ["The spring at the Jotunheim side is Ymir’s well, in which wisdom and wit lie hidden, but that of Niffleheim feeds the adder Nidhogge (darkness), which perpetually gnaws at the root.", "요툰헤임 쪽의 샘은 이미르의 우물로서, 그 안에는 지혜와 기지가 감추어져 있고, 니플헤임의 샘은 끊임없이 뿌리를 갉아먹는 독사 니드호게[어둠]를 기른다."], ["Four harts run across the branches of the tree and bite the buds; they represent the four winds.", "네 마리의 수사슴이 그 나무의 가지를 가로질러 달리며 새싹을 깨문다. 그들은 사방의 바람을 나타낸다."], ["Under the tree lies Ymir, and when he tries to shake off its weight the earth quakes.", "나무 아래에는 이미르가 누워 있어, 그가 그 무게를 떨쳐 내려 할 때면 땅이 흔들린다."]], [["Asgard is the name of the abode of the gods, access to which is only gained by crossing the bridge Bifrost (the rainbow).", "아스가르드는 신들의 거처를 일컫는 이름으로, 그곳으로 들어가는 길은 오직 비프로스트[무지개]라는 다리를 건너야만 얻을 수 있다."], ["Asgard consists of golden and silver palaces, the dwellings of the gods, but the most beautiful of these is Valhalla, the residence of Odin.", "아스가르드는 황금과 은으로 된 궁전들, 곧 신들의 거처로 이루어져 있는데, 그중에서도 가장 아름다운 것은 발할라, 곧 오딘의 거처이다."], ["When seated on his throne he overlooks all heaven and earth.", "오딘은 자신의 옥좌에 앉아 온 하늘과 땅을 굽어본다."], ["Upon his shoulders are the ravens Hugin and Munin, who fly every day over the whole world, and on their return report to him all they have seen and heard.", "그의 양어깨에는 까마귀 후긴과 무닌이 앉아 있어, 날마다 온 세상을 날아다니다가 돌아와서는 보고 들은 모든 것을 그에게 고한다."], ["At his feet lie his two wolves, Geri and Freki, to whom Odin gives all the meat that is set before him, for he himself stands in no need of food.", "그의 발치에는 두 마리 늑대 게리와 프레키가 누워 있는데, 오딘은 자기 앞에 차려진 모든 고기를 그들에게 주니, 그 자신은 음식이 필요 없기 때문이다."], ["Mead is for him both food and drink.", "벌꿀술이 그에게는 음식이자 음료이다."], ["He invented the Runic characters, and it is the business of the Norns to engrave the runes of fate upon a metal shield.", "그는 룬 문자를 고안했으며, 운명의 룬을 금속 방패에 새기는 것은 노른들의 일이다."], ["From Odin’s name, spelt Woden, as it sometimes is, came Wednesday, the name of the fourth day of the week.", "오딘의 이름은 더러 보덴이라고도 적히는데, 거기서 일주일의 넷째 날 이름인 수요일이 비롯되었다."]], [["Odin is frequently called Alfadur (All-father), but this name is sometimes used in a way that shows that the Scandinavians had an idea of a deity superior to Odin, uncreated and eternal.", "오딘은 흔히 알파두르[만물의 아버지]라 불리는데, 다만 이 이름이 쓰이는 방식을 보면 스칸디나비아인들이 오딘보다 우월하며 창조되지 않고 영원한 어떤 신성에 대한 관념을 지니고 있었음을 알 수 있는 경우도 있다."]], [["# OF THE JOYS OF VALHALLA", "# OF THE JOYS OF VALHALLA"]], [["Valhalla is the great hall of Odin, wherein he feasts with his chosen heroes, all those who have fallen bravely in battle, for all who die a peaceful death are excluded.", "발할라는 오딘의 거대한 전당으로, 그는 거기서 자신이 선택한 영웅들, 곧 전투에서 용맹하게 쓰러진 모든 자들과 더불어 향연을 베푼다. 평온하게 죽음을 맞은 자는 모두 배제되기 때문이다."], ["The flesh of the boar Schrimnir is served up to them, and is abundant for all.", "그들에게는 멧돼지 슈림니르의 고기가 차려지는데, 모두에게 넉넉하다."], ["For although this boar is cooked every morning, he becomes whole again every night.", "이 멧돼지는 아침마다 요리되지만 밤마다 다시 온전해지기 때문이다."], ["For drink the heroes are supplied abundantly with mead from the she-goat Heidrum.", "마실 것으로는 암염소 헤이드룬에게서 나온 벌꿀술이 영웅들에게 넉넉히 공급된다."], ["When the heroes are not feasting they amuse themselves with fighting.", "영웅들은 향연을 벌이지 않을 때면 싸움으로 즐긴다."], ["Every day they ride out into the court or field and fight until they cut each other in pieces.", "날마다 그들은 뜰이나 들판으로 말을 타고 나가 서로를 갈가리 베어 죽일 때까지 싸운다."], ["This is their pastime; but when meal time comes they recover from their wounds and return to feast in Valhalla.", "이것이 그들의 소일거리이다. 그러나 식사 때가 오면 그들은 상처에서 회복되어 다시 발할라로 돌아와 향연을 즐긴다."]], [["# THE VALKYRIE", "# THE VALKYRIE"]], [["The Valkyrie are warlike virgins, mounted upon horses and armed with helmets and spears.", "발키리에는 호전적인 처녀들로, 말에 올라 투구와 창으로 무장하고 있다."], ["Odin, who is desirous to collect a great many heroes in Valhalla to be able to meet the giants in a day when the final contest must come, sends them down to every battlefield to make choice of those who shall be slain.", "오딘은 마침내 닥쳐올 최후의 결전이 벌어지는 날에 거인들과 맞설 수 있도록 발할라에 수많은 영웅을 모으고자 하여, 그들을 모든 전장으로 내려보내 죽임을 당할 자들을 고르게 한다."], ["The Valkyrie are his messengers, and their name means “Choosers of the slain.”", "발키리에는 그의 사자(使者)이며, 그 이름의 뜻은 \"전사자(戰死者)를 고르는 자들\"이다."], ["When they ride forth on their errand, their armor sheds a strange flickering light, which flashes up over the northern skies, making what men call the “Aurora Borealis,”", "그들이 임무를 띠고 말을 달려 나아갈 때면 그 갑옷이 기이하게 가물거리는 빛을 발하여 북방의 하늘 위로 번쩍이는데, 이것이 사람들이 \"아우로라 보레알리스\""], ["or “Northern Lights.”[37]", "곧 \"북극광\"이라 부르는 것이다.[37]"]], [["# OF THOR AND THE OTHER GODS", "# OF THOR AND THE OTHER GODS"]], [["Thor, the thunderer, Odin’s eldest son, is the strongest of gods and men, and possesses three very precious things.", "천둥신이며 오딘의 맏아들인 토르는 신들과 인간들 가운데 가장 강한 자로서, 매우 귀중한 세 가지 물건을 지니고 있다."], ["The first is a hammer, which both the Frost and the Mountain giants know to their cost, when they see it hurled against them in the air, for it has split many a skull of their fathers and kindred.", "첫째는 망치인데, 서리 거인과 산의 거인들은 그것이 공중에서 자신들을 향해 던져지는 것을 볼 때 호된 대가로 그 위력을 안다. 그것이 그들의 조상과 친족의 두개골을 숱하게 쪼개 놓았기 때문이다."], ["When thrown, it returns to his hand of its own accord.", "던져진 뒤에는 저절로 그의 손으로 되돌아온다."], ["The second rare thing he possesses is called the belt of strength.", "그가 지닌 둘째 진귀한 물건은 힘의 허리띠라 불린다."], ["When he girds it about him his divine might is doubled.", "그것을 몸에 두르면 그의 신성한 위력이 곱절이 된다."], ["The third, also very precious, is his iron gloves, which he puts on whenever he would use his mallet efficiently.", "셋째 역시 매우 귀중한 것으로, 그의 쇠장갑이니, 그는 자신의 망치를 효과적으로 쓰고자 할 때면 언제나 그것을 낀다."], ["From Thor’s name is derived our word Thursday.", "토르의 이름에서 우리의 단어인 목요일이 비롯되었다."]], [["Frey is one of the most celebrated of the gods.", "프레이는 신들 가운데 가장 이름난 자 중 하나이다."], ["He presides over rain and sunshine and all the fruits of the earth.", "그는 비와 햇빛, 그리고 대지의 온갖 결실을 주관한다."], ["His sister Freya is the most propitious of the goddesses.", "그의 누이 프레이야는 여신들 가운데 가장 자비로운 이이다."], ["She loves music, spring, and flowers, and is particularly fond of the Elves (fairies).", "그녀는 음악과 봄과 꽃을 사랑하며, 특히 엘프[요정]들을 무척 좋아한다."], ["She is very fond of love ditties, and all lovers would do well to invoke her.", "그녀는 사랑의 노래를 매우 즐기니, 모든 연인은 그녀에게 기원하는 것이 좋을 것이다."]], [["Bragi is the god of poetry, and his song records the deeds of warriors.", "브라기는 시(詩)의 신으로, 그의 노래는 전사들의 공적을 기록한다."], ["His wife, Iduna, keeps in a box the apples which the gods, when they feel old age approaching, have only to taste of to become young again.", "그의 아내 이두나는 한 상자 안에 사과를 간직하고 있는데, 신들은 노년이 다가옴을 느낄 때면 그 사과를 맛보기만 해도 다시 젊어진다."]], [["Heimdall is the watchman of the gods, and is therefore placed on the borders of heaven to prevent the giants from forcing their way over the bridge Bifrost (the rainbow).", "헤임달은 신들의 파수꾼으로, 그러므로 거인들이 비프로스트[무지개] 다리를 억지로 건너오지 못하도록 하늘의 경계에 배치되어 있다."], ["He requires less sleep than a bird, and sees by night as well as by day a hundred miles around him.", "그는 새보다도 잠을 덜 자며, 낮이나 밤이나 똑같이 자기 주위 백 마일을 본다."], ["So acute is his ear that no sound escapes him, for he can even hear the grass grow and the wool on a sheep’s back.", "그의 귀가 어찌나 예민한지 어떤 소리도 그를 비껴가지 못하니, 그는 풀이 자라는 소리와 양 등에 양털이 자라는 소리까지 들을 수 있다."]], [["# OF LOKI AND HIS PROGENY", "# OF LOKI AND HIS PROGENY"]], [["There is another deity who is described as the calumniator of the gods and the contriver of all fraud and mischief.", "신들을 헐뜯는 자이며 온갖 기만과 해악을 꾸미는 자로 묘사되는 또 하나의 신이 있다. 그의 이름은 로키이다."], ["His name is Loki. He is handsome and well made, but of a very fickle mood and most evil disposition.", "그는 잘생기고 균형 잡힌 몸을 지녔으나, 매우 변덕스러운 기질에 더없이 사악한 성품을 지녔다."], ["He is of the giant race, but forced himself into the company of the gods, and seems to take pleasure in bringing them into difficulties, and in extricating them out of the danger by his cunning, wit, and skill.", "그는 거인족이지만 신들의 무리에 억지로 끼어들었으며, 그들을 곤경에 빠뜨렸다가 자신의 교활함과 기지와 솜씨로 그 위험에서 빠져나오게 하는 데서 즐거움을 얻는 듯하다."], ["Loki has three children.", "로키에게는 세 자식이 있다."], ["The first is the wolf Fenris, the second the Midgard serpent, the third Hela (Death).", "첫째는 늑대 펜리르이고, 둘째는 미드가르드의 뱀이며, 셋째는 헬라[죽음]이다."], ["The gods were not ignorant that these monsters were growing up, and that they would one day bring much evil upon gods and men.", "신들은 이 괴물들이 자라나고 있으며 언젠가 신들과 인간들에게 많은 해악을 끼치리라는 것을 모르지 않았다."], ["So Odin deemed it advisable to send one to bring them to him.", "그리하여 오딘은 그들을 자기에게로 데려오도록 하나를 보내는 것이 현명하다고 여겼다."], ["When they came he threw the serpent into that deep ocean by which the earth is surrounded.", "그들이 오자 그는 뱀을 땅을 둘러싼 저 깊은 대양 속으로 던져 넣었다."], ["But the monster had grown to such an enormous size that holding his tail in his mouth he encircles the whole earth.", "그러나 그 괴물은 어찌나 어마어마한 크기로 자랐던지 제 꼬리를 입에 문 채로 온 땅을 휘감았다."], ["Hela he cast into Niffleheim, and gave her power over nine worlds or regions, into which she distributes those who are sent to her; that is, all who die of sickness or old age.", "헬라는 니플헤임으로 내던지고, 그녀에게 아홉 세계 곧 아홉 영역을 다스릴 권한을 주었으니, 그녀는 그곳에 자기에게 보내진 자들, 곧 병이나 노환으로 죽은 모든 자들을 분배한다."], ["Her hall is called Elvidner.", "그녀의 전당은 엘비드네르라 불린다."], ["Hunger is her table, Starvation her knife, Delay her man, Slowness her maid, Precipice her threshold, Care her bed, and Burning Anguish forms the hangings of the apartments.", "굶주림이 그녀의 식탁이요, 기아가 그녀의 칼이요, 지연이 그녀의 종이요, 굼뜸이 그녀의 하녀요, 벼랑이 그녀의 문턱이요, 근심이 그녀의 침상이며, 타오르는 고뇌가 그 방의 휘장을 이룬다."], ["She may easily be recognized, for her body is half flesh color and half blue, and she has a dreadfully stern and forbidding countenance.", "그녀는 쉽게 알아볼 수 있으니, 그 몸이 반은 살색이고 반은 푸른빛이며, 무섭도록 준엄하고 험상궂은 얼굴을 하고 있기 때문이다."]], [["The wolf Fenris gave the gods a great deal of trouble before they succeeded in chaining him.", "늑대 펜리르는 신들이 그를 묶는 데 성공하기까지 그들에게 큰 골칫거리를 안겼다."], ["He broke the strongest fetters as if they were made of cobwebs.", "그는 가장 튼튼한 족쇄도 마치 거미줄로 만든 것인 양 끊어 버렸다."], ["Finally the gods sent a messenger to the mountain spirits, who made for them the chain called Gleipnir.", "마침내 신들은 산의 정령들에게 사자를 보냈고, 그들은 신들을 위해 글레이프니르라 불리는 사슬을 만들었다."], ["It is fashioned of six things, viz., the noise made by the footfall of a cat, the beards of women, the roots of stones, the breath of fishes, the nerves (sensibilities) of bears, and the spittle of birds.", "그것은 여섯 가지로 빚어졌으니, 곧 고양이의 발소리, 여인들의 수염, 돌의 뿌리, 물고기의 숨결, 곰의 신경[감각], 그리고 새의 침이다."], ["When finished it was as smooth and soft as a silken string.", "완성되었을 때 그것은 비단실처럼 매끄럽고 부드러웠다."], ["But when the gods asked the wolf to suffer himself to be bound with this apparently slight ribbon, he suspected their design, fearing that it was made by enchantment.", "그러나 신들이 늑대에게 겉보기에 가녀린 이 끈으로 자신을 묶게 하라고 청하자, 그는 그것이 마법으로 만들어진 것이 아닐까 두려워하며 그들의 의도를 의심했다."], ["He therefore only consented to be bound with it upon condition that one of the gods put his hand in his (Fenris’s) mouth as a pledge that the band was to be removed again.", "그리하여 그는 신들 가운데 하나가 그 끈이 다시 풀릴 것이라는 담보로 자기(펜리르)의 입 안에 손을 넣는다는 조건에서만 그것으로 묶이는 데 동의했다."], ["Tyr (the god of battles) alone had courage enough to do this.", "오직 티르[전투의 신]만이 이를 할 만한 용기를 지니고 있었다."], ["But when the wolf found that he could not break his fetters, and that the gods would not release him, he bit off Tyr’s hand, and he has ever since remained one-handed.", "그러나 늑대는 자신이 족쇄를 끊을 수 없고 신들이 자기를 풀어 주지 않으리라는 것을 알자 티르의 손을 물어뜯었으며, 티르는 그 뒤로 줄곧 외팔이로 남았다."]], [["# HOW THOR PAID THE MOUNTAIN GIANT HIS WAGES", "# HOW THOR PAID THE MOUNTAIN GIANT HIS WAGES"]], [["Once on a time, when the gods were constructing their abodes and had already finished Midgard and Valhalla, a certain artificer came and offered to build them a residence so well fortified that they should be perfectly safe from the incursions of the Frost giants and the giants of the mountains.", "옛적 어느 때에, 신들이 자신들의 거처를 짓고 있어 이미 미드가르드와 발할라를 완성했을 무렵, 어떤 장인이 와서 서리 거인과 산의 거인들의 침입으로부터 완벽하게 안전하도록 견고하게 요새화된 거처를 지어 주겠노라 제안했다."], ["But he demanded for his reward the goddess Freya, together with the sun and moon.", "그러나 그는 그 보수로 여신 프레이야와 더불어 해와 달을 요구했다."], ["The gods yielded to his terms, provided he would finish the whole work himself without any one’s assistance, and all within the space of one winter.", "신들은 그가 누구의 도움도 없이 혼자서, 그것도 한 겨울 동안에 그 일 전부를 끝내야 한다는 조건으로 그의 요구를 받아들였다."], ["But if anything remained unfinished on the first day of summer he should forfeit the recompense agreed on.", "그러나 여름의 첫날에 무엇 하나라도 끝내지 못한 채 남아 있으면 그는 약속된 보수를 잃어야 한다는 것이었다."], ["On being told these terms the artificer stipulated that he should be allowed the use of his horse Svadilfari, and this by the advice of Loki was granted to him.", "이 조건을 듣고 장인은 자신의 말 스바딜파리를 부릴 수 있게 해 달라고 요구했고, 로키의 조언에 따라 이것이 그에게 허락되었다."], ["He accordingly set to work on the first day of winter, and during the night let his horse draw stone for the building.", "그리하여 그는 겨울 첫날에 일을 시작했고, 밤이면 자신의 말로 건축용 돌을 끌어오게 했다."], ["The enormous size of the stones struck the gods with astonishment, and they saw clearly that the horse did one-half more of the toilsome work than his master.", "그 돌들의 어마어마한 크기에 신들은 깜짝 놀랐고, 그 말이 주인보다 절반은 더 많은 고된 일을 해내는 것을 똑똑히 보았다."], ["Their bargain, however, had been concluded, and confirmed by solemn oaths, for without these precautions a giant would not have thought himself safe among the gods, especially when Thor should return from an expedition he had then undertaken against the evil demons.", "그러나 그들의 거래는 이미 맺어졌고, 엄숙한 맹세로 확정되어 있었으니, 이러한 조치가 없었더라면 거인은 신들 가운데서, 특히 토르가 그때 사악한 악령들을 치러 떠난 원정에서 돌아오면 자신이 안전하리라 여기지 못했을 것이기 때문이다."]], [["As the winter drew to a close, the building was far advanced, and the bulwarks were sufficiently high and massive to render the place impregnable.", "겨울이 끝나 갈 무렵 건축은 크게 진척되었고, 성벽은 그곳을 난공불락으로 만들기에 충분할 만큼 높고 육중했다."], ["In short, when it wanted but three days to summer, the only part that remained to be finished was the gateway.", "요컨대 여름까지 사흘밖에 남지 않았을 때, 끝내야 할 부분은 오로지 성문뿐이었다."], ["Then sat the gods on their seats of justice and entered into consultation, inquiring of one another who among them could have advised to give Freya away, or to plunge the heavens in darkness by permitting the giant to carry away the sun and the moon.", "그러자 신들은 자신들의 심판석에 앉아 의논에 들어가, 그들 가운데 누가 프레이야를 내주거나, 거인이 해와 달을 가져가게 허락하여 하늘을 어둠에 잠기게 하자고 조언했는지 서로에게 물었다."]], [["They all agreed that no one but Loki, the author of so many evil deeds, could have given such bad counsel, and that he should be put to a cruel death if he did not contrive some way to prevent the artificer from completing his task and obtaining the stipulated recompense.", "그들은 그토록 많은 악행의 장본인인 로키 말고는 누구도 그런 나쁜 조언을 했을 리 없으며, 만약 그가 장인이 그 일을 완성하여 약속된 보수를 얻는 것을 막을 무슨 방도를 짜내지 못한다면 그는 잔혹한 죽음을 당해야 한다는 데 모두 동의했다."], ["They proceeded to lay hands on Loki, who in his fright promised upon oath that, let it cost him what it would, he would so manage matters that the man should lose his reward.", "그들이 로키에게 손을 대려 하자, 그는 겁에 질려 어떤 대가를 치르더라도 그 사내가 보수를 잃게끔 일을 처리하겠노라고 맹세코 약속했다."], ["That very night when the man went with Svadilfari for building stone, a mare suddenly ran out of a forest and began to neigh.", "바로 그날 밤, 그 사내가 스바딜파리와 함께 건축용 돌을 가지러 갔을 때, 암말 한 마리가 숲에서 갑자기 달려나와 울기 시작했다."], ["The horse thereat broke loose and ran after the mare into the forest, which obliged the man also to run after his horse, and thus between one and another the whole night was lost, so that at dawn the work had not made the usual progress.", "그러자 그 말은 고삐를 끊고 암말을 따라 숲속으로 달려갔고, 이 때문에 사내 또한 자신의 말을 쫓아 달려가야 했으며, 그렇게 이리저리 하는 사이 밤을 통째로 허비하여 동틀 무렵에는 일이 여느 때만큼 진척되지 못했다."], ["The man, seeing that he must fail of completing his task, resumed his own gigantic stature, and the gods now clearly perceived that it was in reality a mountain giant who had come amongst them.", "사내는 자신이 일을 완성하지 못하리라는 것을 알자 본래의 거대한 모습으로 돌아왔고, 신들은 이제 그가 실은 자신들 사이로 끼어든 산의 거인이었음을 똑똑히 깨달았다."], ["Feeling no longer bound by their oaths, they called on Thor, who immediately ran to their assistance, and lifting up his mallet, paid the workman his wages, not with the sun and moon, and not even by sending him back to Jotunheim, for with the first blow he shattered the giant’s skull to pieces and hurled him headlong into Niffleheim.", "더 이상 자신들의 맹세에 매이지 않게 된 그들은 토르를 불렀고, 토르는 즉시 그들을 도우러 달려와 자신의 망치를 치켜들고 그 일꾼에게 품삯을 치렀으니, 해와 달로 치른 것도 아니요, 심지어 그를 요툰헤임으로 돌려보낸 것도 아니라, 첫 일격에 거인의 두개골을 산산조각 내고 그를 곤두박질치게 하여 니플헤임으로 내던졌다."]], [["# THE RECOVERY OF THE HAMMER", "# THE RECOVERY OF THE HAMMER"]], [["Once upon a time it happened that Thor’s hammer fell into the possession of the giant Thrym, who buried it eight fathoms deep under the rocks of Jotunheim.", "옛적 어느 때에 토르의 망치가 거인 트림의 손에 들어가는 일이 있었는데, 그는 그것을 요툰헤임의 바위 아래 여덟 길 깊이로 파묻었다."], ["Thor sent Loki to negotiate with Thrym, but he could only prevail so far as to get the giant’s promise to restore the weapon if Freya would consent to be his bride.", "토르는 로키를 보내 트림과 교섭하게 했으나, 로키는 프레이야가 그의 신부가 되는 데 동의한다면 그 무기를 돌려주겠다는 거인의 약속을 얻어 내는 것까지만 해낼 수 있었다."], ["Loki returned and reported the result of his mission, but the goddess of love was quite horrified at the idea of bestowing her charms on the king of the Frost giants.", "로키는 돌아와 자신의 사명의 결과를 보고했으나, 사랑의 여신은 자신의 매력을 서리 거인의 왕에게 바친다는 생각에 몹시 끔찍해했다."], ["In this emergency Loki persuaded Thor to dress himself in Freya’s clothes and accompany him to Jotunheim.", "이 위급한 상황에서 로키는 토르를 설득하여 프레이야의 옷을 입고 자신과 함께 요툰헤임으로 가게 했다."], ["Thrym received his veiled bride with due courtesy, but was greatly surprised at seeing her eat for her supper eight salmons and a full grown ox, besides other delicacies, washing the whole down with three tuns of mead.", "트림은 베일을 쓴 신부를 정중한 예로 맞이했으나, 그녀가 저녁으로 연어 여덟 마리와 다 자란 소 한 마리에다 그 밖의 진미들까지 먹어 치우고는 그 모든 것을 벌꿀술 세 통으로 들이켜는 것을 보고 크게 놀랐다."], ["Loki, however, assured him that she had not tasted anything for eight long nights, so great was her desire to see her lover, the renowned ruler of Jotunheim.", "그러나 로키는, 그녀가 자신의 연인이며 요툰헤임의 이름난 통치자를 보고픈 열망이 어찌나 컸던지 여드레 밤이 다하도록 아무것도 입에 대지 못했노라고 그를 안심시켰다."], ["Thrym had at length the curiosity to peep under his bride’s veil, but started back in affright and demanded why Freya’s eyeballs glistened with fire.", "마침내 트림은 호기심에 신부의 베일 밑을 들여다보았으나, 깜짝 놀라 뒤로 물러나며 어찌하여 프레이야의 눈동자가 불처럼 번득이느냐고 물었다."], ["Loki repeated the same excuse and the giant was satisfied.", "로키가 똑같은 핑계를 되풀이하자 거인은 만족했다."], ["He ordered the hammer to be brought in and laid on the maiden’s lap.", "그는 망치를 들여와 처녀의 무릎 위에 놓도록 명했다."], ["Thereupon Thor threw off his disguise, grasped his redoubted weapon, and slaughtered Thrym and all his followers.", "그러자 토르는 변장을 벗어 던지고 자신의 두려운 무기를 움켜쥐어, 트림과 그의 모든 부하를 죽여 버렸다."]], [["Frey also possessed a wonderful weapon, a sword which would of itself spread a field with carnage whenever the owner desired it.", "프레이 또한 놀라운 무기를 지니고 있었으니, 그것은 주인이 원할 때면 언제나 저절로 들판을 살육으로 뒤덮는 검이었다."], ["Frey parted with this sword, but was less fortunate than Thor and never recovered it.", "프레이는 이 검을 내주었으나, 토르만큼 운이 좋지 못하여 결코 그것을 되찾지 못했다."], ["It happened in this way: Frey once mounted Odin’s throne, from whence one can see over the whole universe, and looking round saw far off in the giant’s kingdom a beautiful maid, at the sight of whom he was struck with sudden sadness, insomuch that from that moment he could neither sleep, nor drink, nor speak.", "일은 이러했다. 프레이가 언젠가 오딘의 옥좌에 올랐는데, 그곳에서는 온 우주를 굽어볼 수 있어, 둘러보던 중 멀리 거인의 왕국에 있는 아름다운 처녀를 보았고, 그 모습에 그는 별안간 슬픔에 사로잡혀 그 순간부터 잠도 잘 수 없고 마시지도 못하며 말도 할 수 없게 되었다."], ["At last Skirnir, his messenger, drew his secret from him, and undertook to get him the maiden for his bride, if he would give him his sword as a reward.", "마침내 그의 사자 스키르니르가 그에게서 비밀을 알아냈고, 만약 보수로 자신의 검을 준다면 그 처녀를 신부로 데려와 주겠노라고 나섰다."], ["Frey consented and gave him the sword, and Skirnir set off on his journey and obtained the maiden’s promise that within nine nights she would come to a certain place and there wed Frey.", "프레이는 동의하여 그에게 검을 주었고, 스키르니르는 길을 떠나 아흐레 밤 안에 어느 정해진 곳으로 와서 프레이와 혼인하겠다는 처녀의 약속을 얻어 냈다."], ["Skirnir having reported the success of his errand, Frey exclaimed:", "스키르니르가 자신의 임무가 성공했음을 보고하자, 프레이는 이렇게 외쳤다."]], [["“Long is one night, Long are two nights, But how shall I hold out three?", "\"하룻밤도 길거니와, 이틀 밤도 길거늘, 사흘 밤을 내 어찌 견디리?"], ["Shorter hath seemed A month to me oft Than of this longing time the half.”", "한 달이 내게는 이 그리움의 절반보다 더 짧게 여겨진 적이 흔하였거늘.\""]], [["So Frey obtained Gerda, the most beautiful of all women, for his wife, but he lost his sword.", "이리하여 프레이는 모든 여인 가운데 가장 아름다운 게르다를 아내로 얻었으나, 자신의 검은 잃었다."]], [["This story, entitled “Skirnir For,” and the one immediately preceding it, “Thrym’s Quida,” will be found poetically told in Longfellow’s “Poets and Poetry of Europe.”", "\"스키르니르의 여정\"이라는 제목의 이 이야기와, 그 바로 앞에 놓인 \"트림의 노래\"는 롱펠로의 「유럽의 시인과 시」에 시적으로 서술되어 있는 것을 찾아볼 수 있다."]], [["————", "————"]], [["# THOR’S VISIT TO JOTUNHEIM, THE GIANT’S COUNTRY", "# THOR’S VISIT TO JOTUNHEIM, THE GIANT’S COUNTRY"]], [["ONE day the god Thor, with his servant Thialfi, and accompanied by Loki, set out on a journey to the giant’s country.", "어느 날 신 토르는 자신의 종 티알피와 함께, 그리고 로키를 동반하여, 거인의 나라를 향해 여행을 떠났다."], ["Thialfi was of all men the swiftest of foot.", "티알피는 모든 사람 가운데 발이 가장 빠른 자였다."], ["He bore Thor’s wallet, containing their provisions.", "그는 식량이 담긴 토르의 자루를 짊어졌다."], ["When night came on they found themselves in an immense forest, and searched on all sides for a place where they might pass the night, and at last came to a very large hall, with an entrance that took the whole breadth of one end of the building.", "밤이 되자 그들은 어느 거대한 숲속에 있는 자신들을 발견하고, 하룻밤을 지낼 만한 곳을 사방으로 찾아 헤매다가, 마침내 매우 큰 회당에 이르렀는데, 그 입구는 건물 한쪽 끝의 너비를 통째로 차지할 만큼 넓었다."], ["Here they lay down to sleep, but towards midnight were alarmed by an earthquake which shook the whole edifice.", "이곳에서 그들은 누워 잠을 청했지만, 한밤중이 되자 건물 전체를 뒤흔드는 지진에 놀라고 말았다."], ["Thor, rising up, called on his companions to seek with him a place of safety.", "토르는 몸을 일으켜 동료들에게 자신과 함께 안전한 곳을 찾자고 외쳤다."], ["On the right they found an adjoining chamber, into which the others entered, but Thor remained at the doorway with his mallet in his hand, prepared to defend himself, whatever might happen.", "오른쪽에서 그들은 잇대어 있는 방을 하나 발견했고, 다른 이들은 그 안으로 들어갔으나, 토르는 무슨 일이 일어나든 자신을 지킬 채비를 갖추고 망치를 손에 든 채 문간에 머물렀다."], ["A terrible groaning was heard during the night, and at dawn of day Thor went out and found lying near him a huge giant, who slept and snored in the way that had alarmed them so.", "밤새 무시무시한 신음 소리가 들렸고, 동이 틀 무렵 토르가 밖으로 나가 보니 가까이에 거대한 거인 하나가 누워 있었는데, 그가 잠을 자며 코를 고는 소리가 바로 그들을 그토록 놀라게 했던 것이었다."], ["It is said that for once Thor was afraid to use his mallet, and as the giant soon waked up, Thor contented himself with simply asking his name.", "한번은 토르도 자신의 망치를 쓰기를 두려워했다고 전해지는데, 거인이 곧 잠에서 깨어나자, 토르는 단지 그의 이름을 묻는 것으로 만족했다."]], [["“My name is Skrymir,”", "\"내 이름은 스크리미르요.\""], ["said the giant, “but I need not ask thy name, for I know that thou art the god Thor.", "거인이 말했다. \"하지만 당신의 이름은 물을 필요가 없으니, 당신이 신 토르라는 것을 내가 알고 있기 때문이오."], ["But what has become of my glove?”", "그런데 내 장갑은 어디로 갔을까?\""], ["Thor then perceived that what they had taken overnight for a hall was the giant’s glove, and the chamber where his two companions had sought refuge was the thumb.", "그제야 토르는 자신들이 간밤에 회당으로 여겼던 것이 거인의 장갑이었으며, 두 동료가 피신했던 방은 그 엄지손가락 부분이었음을 깨달았다."], ["Skrymir then proposed that they should travel in company, and Thor consenting, they sat down to eat their breakfast, and when they had done, Skrymir packed all the provisions into one wallet, threw it over his shoulder, and strode on before them, taking such tremendous strides that they were hard put to it to keep up with him.", "이어서 스크리미르는 함께 여행하자고 제안했고, 토르가 이에 동의하자, 그들은 앉아서 아침을 먹었으며, 식사를 마치자 스크리미르는 모든 식량을 자루 하나에 챙겨 넣고는 그것을 어깨에 걸치고서 앞장서서 성큼성큼 걸었는데, 그 걸음이 어찌나 엄청나던지 그들은 그를 따라가기가 몹시 힘겨웠다."], ["So they travelled the whole day, and at dusk Skrymir chose a place for them to pass the night in under a large oak tree.", "그렇게 하루 종일 여행을 했고, 해 질 무렵 스크리미르는 그들이 큰 떡갈나무 아래에서 하룻밤을 지낼 곳을 골랐다."], ["Skrymir then told them he would lie down to sleep.", "그러고 나서 스크리미르는 자신은 누워서 자겠다고 그들에게 말했다."], ["“But take ye the wallet,”", "\"그러나 자루는 그대들이 가지시오.\""], ["he added, “and prepare your supper.”", "그가 덧붙였다. \"그리고 저녁을 지어 드시오.\""]], [["Skrymir soon fell asleep and began to snore strongly; but when Thor tried to open the wallet, he found the giant had tied it up so tight he could not untie a single knot.", "스크리미르는 곧 잠이 들어 거세게 코를 골기 시작했다. 그런데 토르가 자루를 열려고 해 보니, 거인이 어찌나 단단히 묶어 놓았던지 매듭 하나조차 풀 수 없었다."], ["At last Thor became wroth, and grasping his mallet with both hands he struck a furious blow on the giant’s head.", "마침내 토르는 분노가 치밀어, 두 손으로 망치를 움켜쥐고는 거인의 머리를 사납게 내리쳤다."], ["Skrymir, awakening, merely asked whether a leaf had not fallen on his head, and whether they had supped and were ready to go to sleep.", "스크리미르는 잠에서 깨어나 그저 나뭇잎 하나가 머리에 떨어진 것이 아니냐고, 그리고 저녁을 먹고 잘 채비가 되었느냐고 물을 뿐이었다."], ["Thor answered that they were just going to sleep, and so saying went and laid himself down under another tree.", "토르는 막 잠자리에 들려던 참이라고 대답하고는, 그렇게 말하면서 다른 나무 아래로 가서 누웠다."], ["But sleep came not that night to Thor, and when Skrymir snored again so loud that the forest reëchoed with the noise, he arose, and grasping his mallet launched it with such force at the giant’s skull that it made a deep dint in it.", "그러나 그날 밤 토르에게는 잠이 오지 않았고, 스크리미르가 다시 어찌나 크게 코를 골던지 숲이 그 소리로 울릴 지경이 되자, 토르는 몸을 일으켜 망치를 움켜쥐고 거인의 머리통을 향해 어찌나 힘껏 던졌던지 거기에 깊은 자국이 패였다."], ["Skrymir, awakening, cried out, “What’s the matter?", "스크리미르는 잠에서 깨어 외쳤다. \"무슨 일이지?"], ["Are there any birds perched on this tree?", "이 나무에 새들이라도 앉아 있는 건가?"], ["I felt some moss from the branches fall on my head.", "가지에서 이끼가 좀 떨어져 내 머리에 떨어진 것 같군."], ["How fares it with thee, Thor?”", "토르, 그대는 어떻게 지내고 있소?\""], ["But Thor went away hastily, saying that he had just then awoke, and that as it was only midnight, there was still time for sleep.", "그러나 토르는 자신은 막 잠에서 깬 참이며, 아직 한밤중에 불과하니 잘 시간이 충분히 남았다고 말하면서 황급히 자리를 떴다."], ["He, however, resolved that if he had an opportunity of striking a third blow, it should settle all matters between them.", "그렇지만 그는 만일 세 번째로 내리칠 기회가 생긴다면, 그것으로 둘 사이의 모든 일을 결판내리라 마음먹었다."], ["A little before daybreak he perceived that Skrymir was again fast asleep, and again grasping his mallet, he dashed it with such violence that it forced its way into the giant’s skull up to the handle.", "동이 트기 조금 전, 그는 스크리미르가 다시 깊이 잠들어 있는 것을 알아채고, 또다시 망치를 움켜쥐고는 어찌나 맹렬히 내리쳤던지 망치가 자루까지 거인의 머리통을 파고들었다."], ["But Skrymir sat up, and stroking his cheek said, “An acorn fell on my head.", "그러나 스크리미르는 몸을 일으켜 앉아 자기 뺨을 쓰다듬으며 말했다. \"도토리 하나가 내 머리에 떨어졌군."], ["What!", "아니!"], ["Art thou awake, Thor?", "토르, 그대는 깨어 있소?"], ["Methinks it is time for us to get up and dress ourselves; but you have not now a long way before you to the city called Utgard.", "이제 우리가 일어나 채비를 차릴 때가 된 것 같구려. 하지만 이제 그대들이 우트가르드라는 도시까지 가는 길은 멀지 않소."], ["I have heard you whispering to one another that I am not a man of small dimensions; but if you come to Utgard you will see there many men much taller than I.", "그대들이 서로 내가 작은 몸집의 사내가 아니라고 속닥이는 소리를 들었소만, 우트가르드에 가 보면 거기에서 나보다 훨씬 키 큰 사람들을 많이 보게 될 것이오."], ["Wherefore, I advise you, when you come there, not to make too much of yourselves, for the followers of Utgard-Loki will not brook the boasting of such little fellows as you are.", "그러니 충고하건대, 거기에 도착하거든 너무 우쭐대지 마시오. 우트가르드로키의 부하들은 그대들처럼 작은 친구들이 허풍 떠는 것을 참지 못할 테니 말이오."], ["You must take the road that leads eastward, mine lies northward, so we must part here.”", "그대들은 동쪽으로 난 길을 택해야 하고, 내 길은 북쪽으로 나 있으니, 우리는 여기서 헤어져야겠소.\""]], [["Hereupon he threw his wallet over his shoulders and turned away from them into the forest, and Thor had no wish to stop him or to ask for any more of his company.", "이 말과 함께 그는 자루를 어깨에 걸치고는 그들에게서 돌아서서 숲속으로 들어갔고, 토르는 그를 붙들거나 더 이상 그의 동행을 청하고 싶은 마음이 없었다."]], [["Thor and his companions proceeded on their way, and towards noon descried a city standing in the middle of a plain.", "토르와 그 동료들은 가던 길을 계속 나아갔고, 정오 무렵 평원 한가운데에 서 있는 도시 하나를 발견했다."], ["It was so lofty that they were obliged to bend their necks quite back on their shoulders in order to see to the top of it.", "그 도시는 어찌나 높던지 꼭대기를 보려면 목을 어깨 위로 한껏 젖혀야만 했다."], ["On arriving they entered the city, and seeing a large palace before them with the door wide open, they went in, and found a number of men of prodigious stature, sitting on benches in the hall.", "도착하자 그들은 도시로 들어섰고, 앞에 큰 궁전이 문을 활짝 연 채 서 있는 것을 보고는 안으로 들어가, 회당의 의자에 앉아 있는 거대한 체구의 사람들 여럿을 발견했다."], ["Going further, they came before the king, Utgard-Loki, whom they saluted with great respect.", "더 안으로 들어가자, 그들은 왕 우트가르드로키 앞에 이르렀고, 그에게 정중히 예를 표했다."], ["The king, regarding them with a scornful smile, said, “If I do not mistake me, that stripling yonder must be the god Thor.”", "왕은 경멸 어린 미소를 지으며 그들을 바라보고 말했다. \"내가 잘못 본 것이 아니라면, 저기 저 풋내기가 분명 신 토르일 테지.\""], ["Then addressing himself to Thor, he said, “Perhaps thou mayst be more than thou appearest to be.", "그러고는 토르에게 말을 건네며 이렇게 말했다. \"어쩌면 그대는 보기보다는 대단한 자일지도 모르지."], ["What are the feats that thou and thy fellows deem yourselves skilled in, for no one is permitted to remain here who does not, in some feat or other, excel all other men?”", "그대와 그대의 동료들이 스스로 능하다고 여기는 재주는 무엇인가? 이곳에서는 어떤 재주든 다른 모든 사람을 능가하지 못하는 자는 머무는 것이 허락되지 않으니 말이다.\""]], [["“The feat that I know,”", "\"내가 아는 재주는,\""], ["said Loki, “is to eat quicker than any one else, and in this I am ready to give a proof against any one here who may choose to compete with me.”", "로키가 말했다, \"그 누구보다도 빨리 먹는 것이오. 그리고 여기서 나와 겨루기를 원하는 자가 누구든, 그자를 상대로 그 솜씨를 입증할 채비가 되어 있소.\""]], [["“That will indeed be a feat,”", "\"네가 장담한 것을 정말로 해낸다면, 그것은 과연 대단한 재주일 테지.\""], ["said Utgard-Loki, “if thou performest what thou promisest, and it shall be tried forthwith.”", "우트가르드로키가 말했다. \"그러니 당장 시험해 보도록 하자.\""]], [["He then ordered one of his men who was sitting at the farther end of the bench, and whose name was Logi, to come forward and try his skill with Loki.", "그러고 나서 그는 의자 맨 끝에 앉아 있던, 이름이 로기인 부하 하나를 불러 나오게 하여 로키와 그 솜씨를 겨루게 했다."], ["A trough filled with meat having been set on the hall floor, Loki placed himself at one end, and Logi at the other, and each of them began to eat as fast as he could, until they met in the middle of the trough.", "고기로 가득 찬 구유가 회당 바닥에 놓이자, 로키는 한쪽 끝에, 로기는 다른 쪽 끝에 자리를 잡고, 각자 구유 한가운데에서 만날 때까지 최대한 빨리 먹기 시작했다."], ["But it was found that Loki had only eaten the flesh, while his adversary had devoured both flesh and bone, and the trough to boot.", "그러나 알고 보니 로키는 살코기만 먹은 데 반해, 그의 상대는 살코기와 뼈를 모두 먹어 치웠고, 구유까지 먹어 버린 것이었다."], ["All the company therefore adjudged that Loki was vanquished.", "그리하여 모든 좌중은 로키가 패했다고 판정했다."]], [["Utgard-Loki then asked what feat the young man who accompanied Thor could perform.", "이어서 우트가르드로키는 토르를 따라온 젊은이는 어떤 재주를 부릴 수 있느냐고 물었다."], ["Thialfi answered that he would run a race with any one who might be matched against him.", "티알피는 자신과 맞붙는 그 누구와도 달리기 경주를 하겠다고 대답했다."], ["The king observed that skill in running was something to boast of, but if the youth would win the match he must display great agility.", "왕은 달리기 솜씨는 자랑할 만한 것이라고 말하면서도, 만일 그 젊은이가 시합에서 이기려면 대단한 민첩함을 보여야 할 것이라고 했다."], ["He then arose and went with all who were present to a plain where there was good ground for running on, and calling a young man named Hugi, bade him run a match with Thialfi.", "그러고 나서 그는 일어나 그 자리에 있던 모든 이들과 함께, 달리기에 좋은 땅이 있는 평원으로 가서, 후기라는 젊은이를 불러 티알피와 시합을 벌이라고 명했다."], ["In the first course Hugi so much outstripped his competitor that he turned back and met him not far from the starting place.", "첫 번째 주로에서 후기는 경쟁자를 어찌나 크게 앞질렀던지, 되돌아와 출발점에서 멀지 않은 곳에서 그를 만났다."], ["Then they ran a second and a third time, but Thialfi met with no better success.", "이어서 그들은 두 번째, 세 번째로 달렸으나, 티알피는 더 나은 성과를 거두지 못했다."]], [["Utgard-Loki then asked Thor in what feats he would choose to give proofs of that prowess for which he was so famous.", "이어서 우트가르드로키는 토르에게, 그가 그토록 유명한 그 무용을 어떤 재주로써 입증하고자 하느냐고 물었다."], ["Thor answered that he would try a drinking-match with any one.", "토르는 누구와든 술 마시기 시합을 해 보겠다고 대답했다."], ["Utgard-Loki bade his cup-bearer bring the large horn which his followers were obliged to empty when they had trespassed in any way against the law of the feast.", "우트가르드로키는 술 따르는 자에게, 자신의 부하들이 연회의 규칙을 어떤 식으로든 어겼을 때 비워야 하는 큰 뿔잔을 가져오라고 명했다."], ["The cupbearer having presented it to Thor, Utgard-Loki said, “Whoever is a good drinker will empty that horn at a single draught, though most men make two of it, but the most puny drinker can do it in three.”", "술 따르는 자가 그것을 토르에게 내밀자, 우트가르드로키가 말했다. \"술 잘 마시는 자라면 누구든 저 뿔잔을 단숨에 비울 테지만, 대부분의 사람은 두 번에 나누어 비우고, 아무리 술이 약한 자라도 세 번이면 비운다네.\""]], [["Thor looked at the horn, which seemed of no extraordinary size though somewhat long; however, as he was very thirsty, he set it to his lips, and without drawing breath, pulled as long and as deeply as he could, that he might not be obliged to make a second draught of it; but when he set the horn down and looked in, he could scarcely perceive that the liquor was diminished.", "토르는 뿔잔을 바라보았는데, 다소 길긴 했으나 별스럽게 큰 크기로 보이지는 않았다. 그러나 그는 몹시 목이 말랐기에, 그것을 입술에 대고 두 번째로 들이켜지 않아도 되도록 숨도 쉬지 않고 최대한 길고 깊게 들이켰다. 하지만 뿔잔을 내려놓고 들여다보니, 술이 줄어든 것을 거의 알아볼 수 없었다."]], [["After taking breath, Thor went to it again with all his might, but when he took the horn from his mouth, it seemed to him that he had drunk rather less than before, although the horn could now be carried without spilling.", "숨을 고른 뒤 토르는 다시 온 힘을 다해 그것에 달려들었으나, 입에서 뿔잔을 떼어 보니 아까보다 오히려 덜 마신 것 같았다. 다만 이제는 뿔잔을 쏟지 않고 들고 다닐 정도는 되었다."]], [["“How now, Thor?” said Utgard-Loki; “thou must not spare thyself; if thou meanest to drain the horn at the third draught thou must pull deeply; and I must needs say that thou wilt not be called so mighty a man here as thou art at home if thou showest no greater prowess in other feats than methinks will be shown in this.”", "\"이게 어찌 된 일인가, 토르?\" 우트가르드로키가 말했다. \"그대는 몸을 아껴서는 안 되네. 세 번째 들이켜서 그 뿔잔을 비울 작정이라면 깊이 들이켜야 할 것이야. 그리고 내가 꼭 말해 두어야겠는데, 만일 그대가 다른 재주에서도 이 정도밖에 보여 주지 못한다면, 그대는 여기서는 고향에서만큼 대단한 사내로 불리지는 못할 것이네.\""]], [["Thor, full of wrath, again set the horn to his lips, and did his best to empty it; but on looking in found the liquor was only a little lower, so he resolved to make no further attempt, but gave back the horn to the cupbearer.", "분노로 가득 찬 토르는 다시 뿔잔을 입술에 대고 그것을 비우려 최선을 다했으나, 들여다보니 술은 조금 더 줄어들었을 뿐이었다. 그리하여 그는 더 이상 시도하지 않기로 마음먹고 뿔잔을 술 따르는 자에게 돌려주었다."]], [["“I now see plainly,” said Utgard-Loki, “that thou art not quite so stout as we thought thee: but wilt thou try any other feat, though methinks thou art not likely to bear any prize away with thee hence.”", "\"이제 분명히 알겠군.\" 우트가르드로키가 말했다. \"그대는 우리가 생각했던 만큼 그리 대단한 사내가 아니로구나. 그러나 다른 재주를 시험해 보겠는가? 비록 여기서는 어떤 상도 받아 가지 못할 것 같다만.\""]], [["“What new trial hast thou to propose?”", "\"그대가 제안할 새로운 시험은 무엇인가?\""], ["said Thor.", "토르가 말했다."]], [["“We have a very trifling game here,”", "\"여기 우리에게는 아주 사소한 놀이가 하나 있는데,\""], ["answered Utgard-Loki, “in which we exercise none but children.", "우트가르드로키가 대답했다, \"아이들이나 하는 것이지."], ["It consists in merely lifting my cat from the ground; nor should I have dared to mention such a feat to the great Thor if I had not already observed that thou art by no means what we took thee for.”", "그저 내 고양이를 바닥에서 들어 올리기만 하면 되는 것이라네. 그대가 우리가 여겼던 그런 사내가 결코 아니라는 것을 이미 알아챈 터가 아니었다면, 위대한 토르에게 감히 그런 재주를 입에 올리지도 못했을 것이야.\""]], [["As he finished speaking, a large gray cat sprang on the hall floor.", "그가 말을 마치자, 커다란 잿빛 고양이 한 마리가 회당 바닥으로 뛰어들었다."], ["Thor put his hand under the cat’s belly and did his utmost to raise him from the floor, but the cat, bending his back, had, notwithstanding all Thor’s efforts, only one of his feet lifted up, seeing which Thor made no further attempt.", "토르는 고양이의 배 밑으로 손을 넣어 그것을 바닥에서 들어 올리려 안간힘을 썼으나, 고양이는 등을 구부린 채, 토르의 온갖 애에도 불구하고 발 하나만 들렸을 뿐이었고, 이를 본 토르는 더 이상 시도하지 않았다."]], [["“This trial has turned out,”", "\"이 시험은,\""], ["said Utgard-Loki, “just as I imagined it would.", "우트가르드로키가 말했다, \"내가 짐작했던 그대로 되었군."], ["The cat is large, but Thor is little in comparison to our men.”", "고양이는 크지만, 토르는 우리 사람들에 비하면 작은 자로다.\""]], [["“Little as ye call me,”", "\"그대들이 나를 작다고 부르지만,\""], ["answered Thor, “let me see who among you will come hither now I am in wrath and wrestle with me.”", "토르가 대답했다, \"이제 내가 분이 났으니, 그대들 가운데 누가 여기로 나와 나와 씨름을 하겠는지 어디 한번 봅시다.\""]], [["“I see no one here,”", "\"여기에는,\""], ["said Utgard-Loki, looking at the men sitting on the benches, “who would not think it beneath him to wrestle with thee; let somebody, however, call hither that old crone, my nurse Elli, and let Thor wrestle with her if he will.", "우트가르드로키가 의자에 앉아 있는 사람들을 바라보며 말했다, \"그대와 씨름하는 것을 제 격에 못 미친다고 여기지 않을 자가 하나도 없어 보이는군. 그렇지만 누군가 저 늙은 노파, 내 유모 엘리를 이리로 불러오게 하여, 토르가 원한다면 그녀와 씨름을 하게 하라."], ["She has thrown to the ground many a man not less strong than this Thor is.”", "그녀는 이 토르 못지않게 힘센 사내들을 숱하게 땅바닥에 메다꽂은 적이 있느니라.\""]], [["A toothless old woman then entered the hall, and was told by Utgard-Loki to take hold of Thor.", "그러자 이가 빠진 늙은 여인 하나가 회당으로 들어왔고, 우트가르드로키는 그녀에게 토르를 붙잡으라고 일렀다."], ["The tale is shortly told.", "이야기는 짧게 끝난다."], ["The more Thor tightened his hold on the crone the firmer she stood.", "토르가 그 노파를 더욱 단단히 붙잡을수록, 그녀는 더욱 굳건히 버티고 섰다."], ["At length after a very violent struggle Thor began to lose his footing, and was finally brought down upon one knee.", "마침내 몹시 격렬한 다툼 끝에 토르는 발이 휘청거리기 시작했고, 결국 한쪽 무릎이 꺾여 주저앉고 말았다."], ["Utgard-Loki then told them to desist, adding that Thor had now no occasion to ask any one else in the hall to wrestle with him, and it was also getting late; so he showed Thor and his companions to their seats, and they passed the night there in good cheer.", "그러자 우트가르드로키는 그들에게 그만두라고 이르면서, 이제 토르는 회당의 다른 누구에게도 씨름을 청할 필요가 없게 되었으며, 또한 시간도 늦어 가고 있다고 덧붙였다. 그리하여 그는 토르와 그 동료들을 자리로 안내했고, 그들은 그곳에서 흥겹게 하룻밤을 보냈다."]], [["The next morning, at break of day, Thor and his companions dressed themselves and prepared for their departure.", "이튿날 아침, 동이 틀 무렵, 토르와 그 동료들은 옷을 차려입고 떠날 채비를 했다."], ["Utgard-Loki ordered a table to be set for them, on which there was no lack of victuals or drink.", "우트가르드로키는 그들을 위해 상을 차리라고 명했는데, 거기에는 음식이며 술이 부족함이 없었다."], ["After the repast Utgard-Loki led them to the gate of the city, and on parting asked Thor how he thought his journey had turned out, and whether he had met with any men stronger than himself.", "식사를 마친 뒤 우트가르드로키는 그들을 도시의 성문까지 데려다주었고, 헤어지면서 토르에게 이번 여행이 어떻게 되었다고 생각하는지, 그리고 자신보다 더 힘센 사람들을 만나기는 했는지를 물었다."], ["Thor told him that he could not deny but that he had brought great shame on himself.", "토르는 자신이 스스로에게 큰 수치를 안겼다는 것을 부인할 수 없다고 그에게 말했다."], ["“And what grieves me most,”", "\"그리고 무엇보다 나를 가장 괴롭게 하는 것은,\""], ["he added, “is that ye will call me a person of little worth.”", "그가 덧붙였다, \"그대들이 나를 보잘것없는 자라고 부를 것이라는 점이오.\""]], [["“Nay,”", "\"아니,\""], ["said Utgard-Loki, “it behooves me to tell thee the truth, now thou art out of the city, which so long as I live and have my way thou shalt never enter again.", "우트가르드로키가 말했다, \"이제 그대가 도시를 벗어났으니 그대에게 진실을 말해 주는 것이 마땅하겠소. 그대는, 내가 살아 있고 내 뜻대로 하는 한, 이 도시에 두 번 다시 발을 들이지 못할 것이오."], ["And, by my troth, had I known beforehand that thou hadst so much strength in thee, and wouldst have brought me so near to a great mishap, I would not have suffered thee to enter this time.", "그리고 진실로 말하건대, 만일 그대에게 그토록 큰 힘이 있고, 그대가 나를 그토록 큰 화로 몰아갈 뻔하리라는 것을 미리 알았더라면, 이번에 그대가 들어오는 것을 결코 허락하지 않았을 것이오. 그러니 알아 두시오, 나는 줄곧 환영으로 그대를 속여 왔소."], ["Know then that I have all along deceived thee by my illusions; first in the forest, where I tied up the wallet with iron wire so that thou couldst not untie it.", "먼저 숲에서, 나는 자루를 쇠줄로 묶어 그대가 풀 수 없게 했소. 그 뒤 그대는 망치로 나를 세 번 내리쳤소."], ["After this thou gavest me three blows with thy mallet; the first, though the least, would have ended my days had it fallen on me, but I slipped aside and thy blows fell on the mountain, where thou wilt find three glens, one of them remarkably deep.", "그 첫 번째 일격은, 가장 약한 것이었으되, 만일 내게 떨어졌더라면 내 목숨을 끊어 놓았을 것이오. 하지만 나는 옆으로 비켜섰고, 그대의 일격은 산에 떨어졌으니, 그대는 거기서 세 개의 협곡을 보게 될 것이며, 그중 하나는 유난히 깊을 것이오."], ["These are the dints made by thy mallet.", "그것들이 바로 그대의 망치가 낸 자국이라오."], ["I have made use of similar illusions in the contests you have had with my followers.", "나는 그대가 내 부하들과 벌인 시합들에서도 비슷한 환영을 써먹었소."], ["In the first, Loki, like hunger itself, devoured all that was set before him, but Loki was in reality nothing else than Fire, and therefore consumed not only the meat, but the trough which held it.", "첫째로, 로키는 굶주림 그 자체처럼 자기 앞에 놓인 모든 것을 먹어 치웠으나, 실은 그 로기는 다름 아닌 불이었기에, 고기뿐만 아니라 그것을 담은 구유까지 먹어 치운 것이오. 티알피가 달리기로 겨룬 후기는 생각이었으니, 티알피로서는 그것과 보조를 맞추기란 불가능했소."], ["Hugi, with whom Thialfi contended in running, was Thought, and it was impossible for Thialfi to keep pace with that.", "그대가 그대 차례에 뿔잔을 비우려 했을 때, 그대는, 진실로, 어찌나 놀라운 일을 해냈던지, 내가 직접 보지 않았더라면 결코 믿지 못했을 것이오."], ["When thou in thy turn didst attempt to empty the horn, thou didst perform, by my troth, a deed so marvellous that had I not seen it myself I should never have believed it.", "그 뿔잔의 한쪽 끝은 바다에 닿아 있었는데, 그대는 그것을 알지 못했소. 그러나 그대가 해변에 가 보면 그대의 들이킴으로 바다가 얼마나 줄어들었는지 알게 될 것이오."], ["For one end of that horn reached the sea, which thou wast not aware of, but when thou comest to the shore thou wilt perceive how much the sea has sunk by thy draughts.", "그대는 고양이를 들어 올림으로써 그 못지않게 놀라운 재주를 부렸소. 그리고 솔직히 말하면, 우리는 고양이의 발 하나가 바닥에서 떨어지는 것을 보았을 때 모두가 겁에 질리고 말았소."], ["Thou didst perform a feat no less wonderful by lifting up the cat, and to tell thee the truth, when we saw that one of his paws was off the floor, we were all of us terror-stricken, for what thou tookest for a cat was in reality the Midgard serpent that encompasseth the earth, and he was so stretched by thee that he was barely long enough to enclose it between his head and tail.", "그대가 고양이로 여겼던 것은 실은 대지를 둘러싸고 있는 미드가르드 뱀이었고, 그것은 그대로 인해 어찌나 늘어났던지 머리와 꼬리 사이에 대지를 가까스로 두를 만큼밖에 길이가 남지 않았기 때문이오. 그대가 엘리와 씨름한 것 또한 더없이 놀라운 재주였소."], ["Thy wrestling with Elli was also a most astonishing feat, for there was never yet a man, nor ever will be, whom Old Age, for such in fact was Elli, will not sooner or later lay low.", "노년 — 실은 엘리가 바로 그것이었소 — 이 머지않아 쓰러뜨리지 않을 사람은 지금까지 없었고, 앞으로도 결코 없을 것이기 때문이오. 그러나 이제 우리가 헤어지려 하니, 그대에게 한마디 일러두겠소."], ["But now, as we are going to part, let me tell thee that it will be better for both of us if thou never come near me again, for shouldst thou do so, I shall again defend myself by other illusions, so that thou wilt only lose thy labor and get no fame from the contest with me.”", "그대가 두 번 다시 내게 가까이 오지 않는 편이 우리 둘 모두에게 더 나을 것이오. 만일 그대가 그리한다면, 나는 또다시 다른 환영으로 나를 지킬 터이니, 그대는 그저 헛수고만 하고 나와의 시합에서 아무런 명성도 얻지 못할 것이오.\""]], [["On hearing these words Thor in a rage laid hold of his mallet and would have launched it at him, but Utgard-Loki had disappeared, and when Thor would have returned to the city to destroy it, he found nothing around him but a verdant plain.", "이 말을 듣자 토르는 노여움에 차서 망치를 움켜쥐고는 그를 향해 던지려 했으나, 우트가르드로키는 이미 사라지고 없었고, 토르가 그 도시를 파괴하려고 되돌아가려 하자, 그의 주위에는 푸르른 평원 말고는 아무것도 보이지 않았다."]], [["————", "————"]], [["# CHAPTER XL", "# CHAPTER XL"]], [["THE DEATH OF BALDUR—THE ELVES—RUNIC LETTERS—ICELAND—TEUTONIC MYTHOLOGY—NIBELUNGEN LIED\nBALDUR the Good, having been tormented with terrible dreams indicating that his life was in peril, told them to the assembled gods, who resolved to conjure all things to avert from him the threatened danger.", "선량한 발두르는 자신의 목숨이 위태롭다는 것을 암시하는 무서운 꿈에 시달린 끝에, 이를 모여 있던 신들에게 이야기했다. 신들은 그를 위협하는 위험을 물리치기 위해 만물에게 주문을 걸기로 결의했다."], ["Then Frigga, the wife of Odin, exacted an oath from fire and water, from iron and all other metals, from stones, trees, diseases, beasts, birds, poisons, and creeping things, that none of them would do any harm to Baldur.", "그러자 오딘의 아내 프리가는 불과 물에게서, 쇠와 그 밖의 모든 금속에게서, 돌과 나무와 질병과 짐승과 새와 독과 기어 다니는 것들에게서, 어느 것도 발두르를 해치지 않겠노라는 맹세를 받아 냈다."], ["Odin, not satisfied with all this, and feeling alarmed for the fate of his son, determined to consult the prophetess Angerbode, a giantess, mother of Fenris, Hela, and the Midgard serpent.", "오딘은 이 모든 것으로도 만족하지 못하고 아들의 운명을 염려한 나머지, 펜리스와 헬라와 미드가르드 뱀의 어미인 여거인이자 예언자인 앙게르보데에게 묻기로 결심했다."], ["She was dead, and Odin was forced to seek her in Hela’s dominions.", "그녀는 이미 죽은 몸이었으므로, 오딘은 헬라의 영토로 그녀를 찾아갈 수밖에 없었다."], ["This Descent of Odin forms the subject of Gray’s fine ode beginning,—", "이 오딘의 하강은 그레이의 빼어난 송시(頌詩)의 주제가 되었는데, 그 시는 이렇게 시작한다."]], [["“Uprose the king of men with speed And saddled straight his coal-black steed.”", "\"사람들의 왕이 서둘러 일어나 석탄처럼 검은 준마에 곧장 안장을 얹었네.\""]], [["But the other gods, feeling that what Frigga had done was quite sufficient, amused themselves with using Baldur as a mark, some hurling darts at him, some stones, while others hewed at him with their swords and battle-axes; for do what they would, none of them could harm him.", "그러나 다른 신들은 프리가가 한 일로 충분하다고 여겨, 발두르를 과녁 삼아 즐겼다. 어떤 이들은 그에게 화살을 던지고, 어떤 이들은 돌을 던지고, 또 어떤 이들은 칼과 전투용 도끼로 그를 내려쳤다."], ["And this became a favorite pastime with them and was regarded as an honor shown to Baldur.", "무슨 짓을 하더라도 그 누구도 그를 해칠 수 없었기 때문이다. 그리하여 이는 그들이 즐겨 하는 소일거리가 되었고, 발두르에게 표하는 영예로 간주되었다."], ["But when Loki beheld the scene he was sorely vexed that Baldur was not hurt.", "그러나 로키가 그 광경을 보고서는 발두르가 다치지 않는 것에 몹시 분개했다. 그래서 그는 여인의 모습으로 변신하여, 프리가의 거처인 펜살리르로 갔다."], ["Assuming, therefore, the shape of a woman, he went to Fensalir, the mansion of Frigga.", "그 여신은 그 가짜 여인을 보고서, 신들이 모임에서 무엇을 하고 있는지 아느냐고 물었다. 여인은 그들이 발두르에게 화살과 돌을 던지지만 그를 해치지 못하고 있다고 대답했다."], ["That goddess, when she saw the pretended woman, inquired of her if she knew what the gods were doing at their meetings.", "\"그렇지.\" 프리가가 말했다."], ["She replied that they were throwing darts and stones at Baldur, without being able to hurt him. “Ay,”", "\"돌이든 막대기든 그 무엇도 발두르를 해칠 수 없네."], ["said Frigga, “neither stones, nor sticks, nor anything else can hurt Baldur, for I have exacted an oath from all of them.”", "내가 그 모두에게서 맹세를 받아 냈기 때문이지.\" \"무엇이라고요?\""], ["“What,”", "여인이 외쳤다."], ["exclaimed the woman, “have all things sworn to spare Baldur?”", "\"만물이 발두르를 살려 두겠노라 맹세했다는 말씀이십니까?\" \"만물이 그러했네.\""], ["“All things,”", "프리가가 대답했다."], ["replied Frigga, “except one little shrub that grows on the eastern side of Valhalla, and is called Mistletoe, and which I thought too young and feeble to crave an oath from.”", "\"다만 발할라의 동쪽에서 자라는, 겨우살이라 불리는 작은 관목 하나만은 예외였네. 너무 어리고 연약하여 맹세를 요구할 것까지는 없다고 여겼기 때문이지.\""]], [["As soon as Loki heard this he went away, and resuming his natural shape, cut off the mistletoe, and repaired to the place where the gods were assembled.", "로키는 이 말을 듣자마자 자리를 떠나, 본래의 모습으로 돌아와 겨우살이를 잘라 들고는 신들이 모여 있는 곳으로 향했다. 거기서 그는 호두르가 눈이 멀어 놀이에 끼지 못한 채 한쪽에 떨어져 서 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["There he found Hodur standing apart, without partaking of the sports, on account of his blindness, and going up to him, said, “Why dost thou not also throw something at Baldur?”", "그에게 다가가 이렇게 말했다. \"그대는 어찌하여 발두르에게 무엇이든 던지지 않는가?\""]], [["“Because I am blind,”", "\"나는 눈이 멀어,\""], ["answered Hodur, “and see not where Baldur is, and have, moreover, nothing to throw.”", "호두르가 대답했다. \"발두르가 어디 있는지 보이지 않고, 게다가 던질 것도 없기 때문이오.\""]], [["“Come, then,”", "\"그러면 이리 오게.\" 로키가 말했다."], ["said Loki, “do like the rest, and show honor to Baldur by throwing this twig at him, and I will direct thy arm towards the place where he stands.”", "\"다른 이들처럼 이 가지를 발두르에게 던져 그에게 영예를 표하게. 내가 그대의 팔을 그가 서 있는 곳으로 인도해 주겠네.\""]], [["Hodur then took the mistletoe, and under the guidance of Loki, darted it at Baldur, who, pierced through and through, fell down lifeless.", "그리하여 호두르는 겨우살이를 받아 들고, 로키의 인도를 받아 발두르를 향해 그것을 던졌다. 발두르는 온몸이 꿰뚫려 숨이 끊어진 채 쓰러졌다."], ["Surely never was there witnessed, either among gods or men, a more atrocious deed than this.", "신들 사이에서든 사람들 사이에서든 이보다 더 흉악한 짓은 일찍이 목격된 적이 없었음이 분명하다."], ["When Baldur fell, the gods were struck speechless with horror, and then they looked at each other, and all were of one mind to lay hands on him who had done the deed, but they were obliged to delay their vengeance out of respect for the sacred place where they were assembled.", "발두르가 쓰러지자 신들은 공포에 사로잡혀 말을 잃었고, 그런 다음 서로를 바라보며 모두 한마음으로 그 짓을 저지른 자에게 손을 대려 했으나, 자신들이 모여 있는 신성한 장소를 존중하여 복수를 미룰 수밖에 없었다."], ["They gave vent to their grief by loud lamentations.", "그들은 큰 소리로 통곡하며 슬픔을 토해 냈다."], ["When the gods came to themselves, Frigga asked who among them wished to gain all her love and good will. “For this,”", "신들이 정신을 차리자 프리가는 그들 가운데 누가 자신의 온갖 사랑과 호의를 얻고 싶은지 물었다."], ["said she, “shall he have who will ride to Hel and offer Hela a ransom if she will let Baldur return to Asgard.”", "\"헬에게 가서 헬라가 발두르를 아스가르드로 돌려보내 준다면 그 대가로 몸값을 바치겠노라 제안하는 자가,\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["Whereupon Hermod, surnamed the Nimble, the son of Odin, offered to undertake the journey.", "\"그 사랑과 호의를 얻으리라.\" 이에 오딘의 아들이며 〈날쌘 자〉라는 별명을 지닌 헤르모드가 그 여정을 맡겠노라 나섰다."], ["Odin’s horse, Sleipnir, which has eight legs and can outrun the wind, was then led forth, on which Hermod mounted and galloped away on his mission.", "그러자 다리가 여덟이고 바람보다도 빨리 달리는 오딘의 말 슬레이프니르가 끌려 나왔고, 헤르모드는 거기에 올라타 자신의 사명을 향해 질주해 떠났다."], ["For the space of nine days and as many nights he rode through deep glens so dark that he could not discern anything, until he arrived at the river Gyoll, which he passed over on a bridge covered with glittering gold.", "그는 아흐레 낮과 그만큼의 밤 동안, 아무것도 분간할 수 없을 만큼 어두운 깊은 협곡들을 지나 말을 달려, 마침내 기욜 강에 다다랐다. 그는 반짝이는 황금으로 덮인 다리 위로 강을 건넜다."], ["The maiden who kept the bridge asked him his name and lineage, telling him that the day before five bands of dead persons had ridden over the bridge, and did not shake it as much as he alone.", "그 다리를 지키던 처녀가 그에게 이름과 혈통을 물으며, 전날에는 죽은 자들의 다섯 무리가 그 다리를 건넜으나 그가 홀로 건널 때만큼 다리가 흔들리지는 않았다고 말했다. \"그런데,\""], ["“But,”", "그녀가 덧붙였다."], ["she added, “thou hast not death’s hue on thee; why then ridest thou here on the way to Hel?”", "\"그대에게는 죽음의 빛이 보이지 않는군요. 그런데 어찌하여 그대는 헬로 가는 길에 이리 말을 달리고 있나요?\""]], [["“I ride to Hel,” answered Hermod, “to seek Baldur.", "\"나는 발두르를 찾으러 헬로 가오.\""], ["Hast thou perchance seen him pass this way?”", "헤르모드가 대답했다. \"혹시 그가 이 길을 지나가는 것을 보았소?\""]], [["She replied, “Baldur hath ridden over Gyoll’s bridge, and yonder lieth the way he took to the abodes of death.”", "그녀가 대답했다. \"발두르는 기욜의 다리를 건너갔습니다. 그가 죽음의 거처로 간 길이 저쪽에 있습니다.\""]], [["Hermod pursued his journey until he came to the barred gates of Hel.", "헤르모드는 헬의 빗장 걸린 문에 이를 때까지 여정을 계속했다."], ["Here he alighted, girthed his saddle tighter, and remounting clapped both spurs to his horse, who cleared the gate by a tremendous leap without touching it.", "여기서 그는 말에서 내려 안장을 더 단단히 조이고는 다시 올라타 말에 두 박차를 가했다. 말은 엄청난 도약으로 문에 닿지도 않은 채 그것을 넘었다."], ["Hermod then rode on to the palace, where he found his brother Baldur occupying the most distinguished seat in the hall, and passed the night in his company.", "그런 다음 헤르모드는 궁전까지 말을 달려가, 그곳 홀에서 가장 영예로운 자리를 차지하고 있는 형제 발두르를 발견했고, 그와 함께 하룻밤을 보냈다."], ["The next morning he besought Hela to let Baldur ride home with him, assuring her that nothing but lamentations were to be heard among the gods.", "이튿날 아침 그는 헬라에게 발두르가 자신과 함께 집으로 돌아가게 해 달라고 간청하며, 신들 사이에서는 통곡 외에는 들리는 것이 없다고 단언했다."], ["Hela answered that it should now be tried whether Baldur was so beloved as he was said to be.", "헬라는 발두르가 사람들이 말하는 것처럼 정말로 사랑받는지 이제 시험해 보겠노라 대답했다. \"따라서,\""], ["“If, therefore,”", "그녀가 덧붙였다."], ["she added, “all things in the world, both living and lifeless, weep for him, then shall he return to life; but if any one thing speak against him or refuse to weep, he shall be kept in Hel.”", "\"세상 만물이, 산 것이든 죽은 것이든 그를 위해 운다면 그는 다시 살아 돌아갈 것이다. 그러나 어느 하나라도 그를 거슬러 말하거나 울기를 거부한다면, 그는 헬에 머무르게 될 것이다.\""]], [["Hermod then rode back to Asgard and gave an account of all he had heard and witnessed.", "그리하여 헤르모드는 아스가르드로 돌아가 자신이 듣고 본 모든 것을 보고했다."]], [["The gods upon this despatched messengers throughout the world to beg everything to weep in order that Baldur might be delivered from Hel.", "이에 신들은 발두르가 헬에서 풀려날 수 있도록 만물에게 울어 달라고 청하기 위해 온 세상으로 사자들을 파견했다."], ["All things very willingly complied with this request, both men and every other living being, as well as earths, and stones, and trees, and metals, just as we have all seen these things weep when they are brought from a cold place into a hot one.", "만물은 이 청에 기꺼이 응했다. 사람들도, 다른 모든 살아 있는 것들도, 또한 흙과 돌과 나무와 금속도 모두 그러했는데, 마치 우리가 이런 것들을 차가운 곳에서 뜨거운 곳으로 옮길 때 그것들이 우는 것을 보아 온 그대로였다."], ["As the messengers were returning, they found an old hag named Thaukt sitting in a cavern, and begged her to weep Baldur out of Hel.", "사자들이 돌아오던 길에, 그들은 타우크트라는 이름의 늙은 노파가 동굴 안에 앉아 있는 것을 발견하고는 발두르가 헬에서 나올 수 있도록 울어 달라고 청했다."], ["But she answered,", "그러나 그녀는 이렇게 대답했다."]], [["“Thaukt will wail With dry tears Baldur’s bale-fire.", "\"타우크트는 마른 눈물로 발두르의 화장(火葬) 불을 위해 곡하리라."], ["Let Hela keep her own.”", "헬라가 제 것을 차지하게 하라.\""]], [["It was strongly suspected that this hag was no other than Loki himself, who never ceased to work evil among gods and men.", "이 노파는 다름 아닌 로키 자신일 것이라는 의심이 강하게 일었으니, 그는 신들과 사람들 사이에서 악을 행하기를 결코 멈추지 않았던 것이다."], ["So Baldur was prevented from coming back to Asgard.[38]", "그리하여 발두르는 아스가르드로 돌아오지 못하게 되었다.[38]"]], [["# THE FUNERAL OF BALDUR", "# THE FUNERAL OF BALDUR"]], [["The gods took up the dead body and bore it to the seashore where stood Baldur’s ship “Hringham,” which passed for the largest in the world.", "신들은 죽은 시신을 들어 올려 바닷가로 옮겼는데, 그곳에는 세상에서 가장 크다고 여겨지던 발두르의 배 〈흐링감〉이 있었다."], ["Baldur’s dead body was put on the funeral pile, on board the ship, and his wife Nanna was so struck with grief at the sight that she broke her heart, and her body was burned on the same pile as her husband’s.", "발두르의 시신은 배에 실린 장작더미 위에 놓였고, 그의 아내 난나는 그 광경에 슬픔이 사무친 나머지 심장이 터져 버려, 그녀의 시신도 남편의 것과 같은 장작더미 위에서 불태워졌다."], ["There was a vast concourse of various kinds of people at Baldur’s obsequies.", "발두르의 장례식에는 온갖 종류의 사람들이 어마어마하게 모여들었다."], ["First came Odin accompanied by Frigga, the Valkyrie, and his ravens; then Frey in his car drawn by Gullinbursti, the boar; Heimdall rode his horse Gulltopp, and Freya drove in her chariot drawn by cats.", "먼저 오딘이 프리가와 발키리에와 그의 까마귀들을 거느리고 왔다. 그다음에 프레이가 멧돼지 굴린부르스티가 끄는 수레를 타고 왔고, 헤임달은 자신의 말 굴토프를 타고 왔으며, 프레이야는 고양이들이 끄는 자신의 전차를 몰고 왔다."], ["There were also a great many Frost giants and giants of the mountain present.", "서리 거인들과 산의 거인들도 많이 참석했다."], ["Baldur’s horse was led to the pile fully caparisoned and consumed in the same flames with his master.", "발두르의 말은 온전히 마구를 갖춘 채 장작더미로 끌려와 주인과 같은 불길 속에서 불살라졌다."]], [["But Loki did not escape his deserved punishment.", "그러나 로키는 마땅한 벌을 면치 못했다."], ["When he saw how angry the gods were, he fled to the mountain, and there built himself a hut with four doors, so that he could see every approaching danger.", "그는 신들이 얼마나 노했는지를 보고서 산으로 달아나, 그곳에 문이 넷 달린 오두막을 지어 다가오는 온갖 위험을 볼 수 있게 했다."], ["He invented a net to catch the fishes, such as fishermen have used since his time.", "그는 물고기를 잡기 위한 그물을 고안했는데, 그 시절 이후로 어부들이 써 온 것과 같은 것이었다."], ["But Odin found out his hidingplace and the gods assembled to take him.", "그러나 오딘은 그의 은신처를 알아냈고, 신들은 그를 붙잡으려 모여들었다."], ["He, seeing this, changed himself into a salmon, and lay hid among the stones of the brook.", "그는 이를 보고 자신을 연어로 변신시켜 시냇물의 돌 사이에 숨었다."], ["But the gods took his net and dragged the brook, and Loki, finding he must be caught, tried to leap over the net; but Thor caught him by the tail and compressed it, so that salmons ever since have had that part remarkably fine and thin.", "그러나 신들은 그의 그물을 가져다 시냇물을 훑었고, 로키는 붙잡히게 될 것임을 깨닫고는 그물을 뛰어넘으려 했다. 그러나 토르가 그의 꼬리를 붙잡아 눌렀고, 그리하여 연어들은 그 이후로 그 부위가 유난히 가늘고 곱게 되었다."], ["They bound him with chains and suspended a serpent over his head, whose venom falls upon his face drop by drop.", "신들은 그를 사슬로 묶고 그의 머리 위에 뱀을 매달아 두었는데, 그 독이 그의 얼굴 위로 한 방울씩 떨어졌다."], ["His wife Siguna sits by his side and catches the drops as they fall, in a cup; but when she carries it away to empty it, the venom falls upon Loki, which makes him howl with horror, and twist his body about so violently that the whole earth shakes, and this produces what men call earthquakes.", "그의 아내 시구나가 그의 곁에 앉아 떨어지는 독방울을 잔에 받았다. 그러나 그녀가 그것을 비우려 가져갈 때면 독이 로키 위로 떨어졌고, 그러면 그는 공포에 질려 울부짖으며 온몸을 어찌나 격렬하게 비트는지 온 대지가 흔들렸으니, 이것이 사람들이 지진이라 부르는 것을 낳는다."]], [["# THE ELVES", "# THE ELVES"]], [["The Edda mentions another class of beings, inferior to the gods, but still possessed of great power; these were called Elves.", "에다는 신들보다는 못하지만 그래도 큰 권능을 지닌 또 다른 부류의 존재들을 언급한다. 이들은 엘프라 불렸다."], ["The white spirits, or Elves of Light, were exceedingly fair, more brilliant than the sun, and clad in garments of a delicate and transparent texture.", "흰 정령들, 곧 빛의 엘프들은 더없이 아름답고 태양보다도 눈부셨으며, 섬세하고 투명한 직물로 된 옷을 입었다."], ["They loved the light, were kindly disposed to mankind, and generally appeared as fair and lovely children.", "그들은 빛을 사랑했고 인간에게 호의를 품었으며, 대개 아름답고 사랑스러운 아이들의 모습으로 나타났다."], ["Their country was called Alfheim, and was the domain of Freyr, the god of the sun, in whose light they were always sporting.", "그들의 나라는 알프헤임이라 불렸으며, 태양의 신 프레이르의 영토였다. 그들은 늘 그 빛 속에서 즐거이 노닐었다."]], [["The Black or Night Elves were a different kind of creatures.", "검은 엘프, 곧 밤의 엘프들은 다른 종류의 피조물이었다."], ["Ugly, long-nosed dwarfs, of a dirty brown color, they appeared only at night, for they avoided the sun as their most deadly enemy, because whenever his beams fell upon any of them they changed them immediately into stones.", "추하고 코가 긴 난쟁이들로, 지저분한 갈색을 띠었으며, 밤에만 나타났다. 그들은 태양을 가장 무서운 적으로 여겨 피했는데, 태양의 빛이 그들 가운데 누구에게든 닿으면 그 즉시 돌로 변해 버렸기 때문이다."], ["Their language was the echo of solitudes, and their dwelling-places subterranean caves and clefts.", "그들의 언어는 고독의 메아리였으며, 그들의 거처는 땅 밑의 동굴과 틈새였다."], ["They were supposed to have come into existence as maggots produced by the decaying flesh of Ymir’s body, and were afterwards endowed by the gods with a human form and great understanding.", "그들은 이미르의 시신이 썩은 살에서 생겨난 구더기로 존재하게 되었다가, 후에 신들로부터 인간의 형상과 위대한 지력을 부여받았다고 여겨졌다."], ["They were particularly distinguished for a knowledge of the mysterious powers of nature, and for the runes which they carved and explained.", "그들은 특히 자연의 신비로운 힘에 대한 지식과, 자신들이 새겨 풀이하던 룬 문자로 이름이 높았다."], ["They were the most skilful artificers of all created beings, and worked in metals and in wood.", "그들은 창조된 모든 존재 가운데 가장 솜씨 좋은 장인들이었으며, 금속과 나무를 다루어 일했다."], ["Among their most noted works were Thor’s hammer, and the ship “Skidbladnir,”", "그들의 가장 이름난 작품들 가운데에는 토르의 망치와, 그들이 프레이르에게 선사한 배 〈스키드블라드니르〉가 있었다."], ["which they gave to Freyr, and which was so large that it could contain all the deities with their war and household implements, but so skillfully was it wrought that when folded together it could be put into a side pocket.", "그 배는 모든 신을 그들의 전쟁 도구와 살림 도구와 함께 다 실을 수 있을 만큼 컸으나, 어찌나 솜씨 좋게 만들어졌던지 접어 두면 옆 주머니에 넣을 수 있을 정도였다."]], [["# RAGNAROK, THE TWILIGHT OF THE GODS", "# RAGNAROK, THE TWILIGHT OF THE GODS"]], [["It was a firm belief of the northern nations that a time would come when all the visible creation, the gods of Valhalla and Niffleheim, the inhabitants of Jotunheim, Alfheim, and Midgard, together with their habitations, would be destroyed.", "북방 민족들은 보이는 모든 피조물, 곧 발할라와 니플헤임의 신들, 요툰헤임과 알프헤임과 미드가르드의 거주자들이 그들의 거처와 더불어 멸망할 때가 오리라고 굳게 믿었다."], ["The fearful day of destruction will not, however, be without its forerunners.", "그러나 그 무서운 멸망의 날에 전조가 없지는 않을 것이다."], ["First will come a triple winter, during which snow will fall from the four corners of the heavens, the frost be very severe, the wind piercing, the weather tempestuous, and the sun impart no gladness.", "먼저 세 겹의 겨울이 닥쳐올 것이니, 그동안 눈이 하늘의 네 귀퉁이에서 쏟아지고, 서리가 몹시 매서우며, 바람이 살을 에고, 날씨가 사납고, 태양이 아무런 기쁨도 주지 못할 것이다."], ["Three such winters will pass away without being tempered by a single summer.", "단 한 차례의 여름으로도 누그러지는 일 없이 그런 겨울이 세 번 지나갈 것이다."], ["Three other similar winters will then follow, during which war and discord will spread over the universe.", "그런 다음 그와 비슷한 또 다른 세 번의 겨울이 뒤따를 것이니, 그동안 전쟁과 불화가 온 우주에 퍼질 것이다."], ["The earth itself will be frightened and begin to tremble, the sea leave its basin, the heavens tear asunder, and men perish in great numbers, and the eagles of the air feast upon their still quivering bodies.", "대지 자체가 두려움에 떨기 시작하고, 바다가 그 그릇을 벗어나며, 하늘이 갈라지고, 사람들이 수없이 죽어 나가고, 하늘의 독수리들이 아직도 꿈틀거리는 시신들을 포식할 것이다."], ["The wolf Fenris will now break his bands, the Midgard serpent rise out of her bed in the sea, and Loki, released from his bonds, will join the enemies of the gods.", "이제 늑대 펜리스가 자신을 묶은 사슬을 끊고, 미드가르드 뱀이 바다 밑 잠자리에서 솟아오르며, 묶여 있던 데서 풀려난 로키가 신들의 적에 가담할 것이다."], ["Amidst the general devastation the sons of Muspelheim will rush forth under their leader Surtur, before and behind whom are flames and burning fire.", "만물이 황폐해지는 가운데 무스펠헤임의 아들들이 그들의 지도자 수르투르의 인솔 아래 돌진해 나올 것이니, 그의 앞뒤로는 불길과 타오르는 불이 따른다."], ["Onward they ride over Bifrost, the rainbow bridge, which breaks under the horses’ hoofs.", "그들은 무지개다리 비프로스트 위로 말을 달리는데, 다리는 말발굽 아래 무너진다."], ["But they, disregarding its fall, direct their course to the battlefield called Vigrid.", "그러나 그들은 다리가 무너지는 것에 아랑곳하지 않고 비그리드라 불리는 전장으로 향한다."], ["Thither also repair the wolf Fenris, the Midgard serpent, Loki with all the followers of Hela, and the Frost giants.", "그곳으로 늑대 펜리스와 미드가르드 뱀, 헬라의 모든 추종자를 거느린 로키, 그리고 서리 거인들도 모여든다."]], [["Heimdall now stands up and sounds the Giallar horn to assemble the gods and heroes for the contest.", "이제 헤임달이 일어서서 신들과 영웅들을 싸움에 소집하기 위해 걀라르 뿔나팔을 분다."], ["The gods advance, led on by Odin, who engages the wolf Fenris, but falls a victim to the monster, who is, however, slain by Vidar, Odin’s son.", "신들은 오딘을 앞세워 나아가고, 오딘은 늑대 펜리스와 맞붙으나 그 괴물의 희생물이 되고 만다. 그러나 그 괴물은 오딘의 아들 비다르에게 죽임을 당한다."], ["Thor gains great renown by killing the Midgard serpent, but recoils and falls dead, suffocated with the venom which the dying monster vomits over him.", "토르는 미드가르드 뱀을 죽여 크나큰 명성을 얻으나, 죽어 가는 괴물이 자신에게 뿜어낸 독에 질식하여 뒷걸음치다 쓰러져 죽는다."], ["Loki and Heimdall meet and fight till they are both slain.", "로키와 헤임달은 마주쳐 싸우다 둘 다 죽임을 당한다."], ["The gods and their enemies having fallen in battle, Surtur, who has killed Freyr, darts fire and flames over the world, and the whole universe is burned up.", "신들과 그 적들이 전투에서 쓰러진 뒤, 프레이르를 죽인 수르투르가 온 세상에 불과 불길을 쏘아 댔고, 온 우주가 불타 버린다."], ["The sun becomes dim, the earth sinks into the ocean, the stars fall from heaven, and time is no more.", "태양이 흐려지고, 대지가 대양 속으로 가라앉으며, 별들이 하늘에서 떨어지고, 시간은 더 이상 존재하지 않는다."]], [["After this Alfadur (the Almighty) will cause a new heaven and a new earth to arise out of the sea.", "이후에 알파두르[전능자]가 새 하늘과 새 땅을 바다에서 솟아오르게 할 것이다."], ["The new earth filled with abundant supplies will spontaneously produce its fruits without labor or care.", "풍성한 양식으로 가득한 새 땅은 수고나 보살핌 없이도 저절로 그 열매를 맺을 것이다."], ["Wickedness and misery will no more be known, but the gods and men will live happily together.", "사악함과 비참함은 더 이상 알려지지 않을 것이며, 신들과 사람들이 함께 행복하게 살아갈 것이다."]], [["# RUNIC LETTERS", "# RUNIC LETTERS"]], [["One cannot travel far in Denmark, Norway, or Sweden without meeting with great stones of different forms, engraven with characters called Runic, which appear at first sight very different from all we know.", "덴마크나 노르웨이나 스웨덴에서는 멀리 가지 않아도 룬이라 불리는 문자가 새겨진 갖가지 형태의 큰 돌들을 만나게 되는데, 이 문자는 첫눈에 우리가 아는 모든 것과는 사뭇 달라 보인다."], ["The letters consist almost invariably of straight lines, in the shape of little sticks either singly or put together.", "그 글자들은 거의 예외 없이 곧은 선들로 이루어져 있어, 작은 막대기를 하나씩 혹은 여럿 모아 놓은 모양을 하고 있다."], ["Such sticks were in early times used by the northern nations for the purpose of ascertaining future events.", "그런 막대기들은 일찍이 북방 민족들이 미래의 일들을 알아내기 위한 용도로 썼던 것이다."], ["The sticks were shaken up, and from the figures that they formed a kind of divination was derived.", "막대기들을 흔든 다음, 그것들이 이루는 형상에서 일종의 점복(占卜)을 끌어냈다."]], [["The Runic characters were of various kinds.", "룬 문자에는 여러 종류가 있었다."], ["They were chiefly used for magical purposes.", "그것들은 주로 마법의 목적에 쓰였다."], ["The noxious, or, as they called them, the bitter runes, were employed to bring various evils on their enemies; the favorable averted misfortune.", "해로운 룬, 곧 그들이 〈쓰라린〉 룬이라 부르던 것은 적들에게 갖가지 재앙을 가져오는 데 쓰였고, 이로운 룬은 불행을 물리쳤다."], ["Some were medicinal, others employed to win love, etc.", "어떤 것은 의약에 쓰였고, 또 어떤 것은 사랑을 얻는 데 쓰이는 등이었다."], ["In later times they were frequently used for inscriptions, of which more than a thousand have been found.", "후대에는 비문에 자주 쓰였는데, 그런 비문이 천 점 넘게 발견되었다."], ["The language is a dialect of the Gothic, called Norse, still in use in Iceland.", "그 언어는 노르드어라 불리는 고트어의 한 방언으로, 아이슬란드에서는 아직도 쓰인다."], ["The inscriptions may therefore be read with certainty, but hitherto very few have been found which throw the least light on history.", "따라서 그 비문들은 확실하게 읽어 낼 수 있으나, 지금까지 역사에 조금이라도 빛을 던져 주는 것은 거의 발견되지 않았다."], ["They are mostly epitaphs on tombstones.", "대부분은 묘비에 새겨진 비명(碑銘)이다."]], [["Gray’s ode on the “Descent of Odin” contains an allusion to the use of Runic letters for incantation:", "그레이의 송시 〈오딘의 하강〉에는 룬 문자를 주술에 사용하는 것에 관한 인유가 담겨 있다."]], [["“Facing to the northern clime, Thrice he traced the Runic rhyme; Thrice pronounced, in accents dread, The thrilling verse that wakes the dead, Till from out the hollow ground Slowly breathed a sullen sound.”", "\"북녘 하늘을 마주하고서 그는 룬의 운(韻)을 세 번 그렸네. 무서운 어조로 세 번 읊었네, 죽은 자를 깨우는 전율의 시구를. 마침내 텅 빈 땅속에서 음울한 소리가 천천히 새어 나왔네.\""]], [["# THE SKALDS", "# THE SKALDS"]], [["The Skalds were the bards and poets of the nation, a very important class of men in all communities in an early stage of civilization.", "스칼드는 그 나라의 음유시인이자 시인으로, 문명의 초기 단계에 있는 모든 공동체에서 매우 중요한 부류의 사람들이었다."], ["They are the depositaries of whatever historic lore there is, and it is their office to mingle something of intellectual gratification with the rude feasts of the warriors, by rehearsing, with such accompaniments of poetry and music as their skill can afford, the exploits of their heroes living or dead.", "그들은 존재하는 온갖 역사적 전승의 보관자였으며, 자신들의 솜씨가 허락하는 한의 시와 음악을 곁들여 살아 있거나 죽은 영웅들의 위업을 읊음으로써, 전사들의 거친 향연에 지적인 즐거움을 어느 정도 섞어 넣는 것이 그들의 소임이었다."], ["The compositions of the Skalds were called Sagas, many of which have come down to us, and contain valuable materials of history, and a faithful picture of the state of society at the time to which they relate.", "스칼드의 작품은 사가라 불렸으며, 그 가운데 다수가 우리에게 전해져 내려와 귀중한 역사 자료와, 그것이 다루는 시대의 사회상에 대한 충실한 그림을 담고 있다."]], [["The Eddas and Sagas have come to us from Iceland.", "에다와 사가는 아이슬란드에서 우리에게 전해져 왔다."], ["The following extract from Carlyle’s lectures on “Heroes and Hero Worship” gives an animated account of the region where the strange stories we have been reading had their origin.", "칼라일의 강연 〈영웅과 영웅 숭배〉에서 발췌한 다음 글은, 우리가 읽어 온 기이한 이야기들이 비롯된 지역에 대한 생생한 서술을 들려준다."], ["Let the reader contrast it for a moment with Greece, the parent of classical mythology:", "독자는 잠시 그곳을 고전 신화의 모태인 그리스와 대조해 보기 바란다."]], [["“In that strange island, Iceland,—burst up, the geologists say, by fire from the bottom of the sea, a wild land of barrenness and lava, swallowed many months of every year in black tempests, yet with a wild, gleaming beauty in summer time, towering up there stern and grim in the North Ocean, with its snow yokuls [mountains], roaring geysers [boiling springs], sulphur pools, and horrid volcanic chasms, like the waste, chaotic battlefield of Frost and Fire,—where, of all places, we least looked for literature or written memorials,—the record of these things was written down.", "\"저 기이한 섬 아이슬란드는 지질학자들의 말에 따르면 바다 밑바닥에서 불길이 치솟아 솟아오른 것으로, 메마름과 용암의 거친 땅이며, 해마다 여러 달 동안 검은 폭풍에 삼켜져 있으나 여름철이면 거칠고 어른거리는 아름다움을 띠고, 그 위로 눈 덮인 요쿨[산]과 포효하는 게이세르[끓는 샘]와 유황 못과 끔찍한 화산의 틈을 거느린 채 북녘 대양에 준엄하고 험상궂게 솟아 있으니, 마치 서리와 불의 황량하고 혼돈한 전장 같다. 무엇보다 우리가 문학이나 글로 된 기록을 가장 기대하기 어려웠던 그곳에서, 이런 것들의 기록이 적혀 내려왔던 것이다."], ["On the seaboard of this wild land is a rim of grassy country, where cattle can subsist, and men by means of them and of what the sea yields; and it seems they were poetic men these, men who had deep thoughts in them and uttered musically their thoughts.", "이 거친 땅의 해안가에는 풀이 자라는 띠 같은 지대가 있어, 거기서 가축이 살아갈 수 있고, 사람들은 그 가축과 바다가 내어 주는 것으로 살아갈 수 있다. 그리고 이들은 시인다운 사람들이었던 듯하다."], ["Much would be lost had Iceland not been burst up from the sea, not been discovered by the Northmen!”", "마음속에 깊은 사색을 품고 그 사색을 음악처럼 토해 낸 사람들이었던 것이다. 아이슬란드가 바다에서 솟아오르지 않았더라면, 노르드인들에게 발견되지 않았더라면, 잃어버렸을 것이 참으로 많았으리라!\""]], [["# TEUTONIC MYTHOLOGY", "# TEUTONIC MYTHOLOGY"]], [["In the mythology of Germany proper, the name of Odin appears as Wotan; Freya and Frigga are regarded as one and the same divinity, and the gods are in general represented as less warlike in character than those in the Scandinavian myths.", "독일 본토의 신화에서는 오딘의 이름이 보탄으로 나타난다. 프레이야와 프리가는 하나의 동일한 신성으로 간주되며, 신들은 대체로 스칸디나비아 신화의 신들보다 덜 호전적인 성격으로 그려진다."], ["As a whole, however, Teutonic mythology runs along almost identical lines with that of the northern nations.", "그러나 전체로 보면 게르만 신화는 북방 민족들의 신화와 거의 동일한 노선을 따른다."], ["The most notable divergence is due to modifications of the legends by reason of the difference in climatic conditions.", "가장 두드러진 차이는 기후 조건의 차이로 인해 전설들이 변형된 데에서 비롯된다."], ["The more advanced social condition of the Germans is also apparent in their mythology.", "게르만족의 보다 진보된 사회 상태 또한 그들의 신화에 드러난다."]], [["# THE NIBELUNGEN LIED", "# THE NIBELUNGEN LIED"]], [["One of the oldest myths of the Teutonic race is found in the great national epic of the Nibelungen Lied, which dates back to the prehistoric era when Wotan, Frigga, Thor, Loki, and the other gods and goddesses were worshipped in the German forests.", "게르만 종족의 가장 오래된 신화 가운데 하나는 위대한 국민 서사시 〈니벨룽겐의 노래〉에서 발견되는데, 이는 보탄과 프리가와 토르와 로키, 그리고 그 밖의 신들과 여신들이 게르만의 숲에서 숭배되던 선사시대까지 거슬러 올라간다."], ["The epic is divided into two parts, the first of which tells how Siegfried, the youngest of the kings of the Netherlands, went to Worms, to ask in marriage the hand of Kriemhild, sister of Günther, King of Burgundy.", "이 서사시는 두 부분으로 나뉘는데, 그 첫째 부분은 네덜란드 왕들 가운데 가장 어린 지크프리트가 부르군트의 왕 군터의 누이 크림힐트에게 청혼하러 보름스로 간 이야기를 들려준다."], ["While he was staying with Günther, Siegfried helped the Burgundian king to secure as his wife Brunhild, queen of Issland.", "군터의 곁에 머무는 동안 지크프리트는 부르군트 왕이 이슬란트의 여왕 브룬힐트를 아내로 얻도록 도왔다."], ["The latter had announced publicly that he only should be her husband who could beat her in hurling a spear, throwing a huge stone, and in leaping.", "브룬힐트는 창던지기와 거대한 돌 던지기, 그리고 멀리뛰기에서 자신을 이기는 자만이 자신의 남편이 되리라고 공공연히 선언해 둔 터였다."], ["Siegfried, who possessed a cloak of invisibility, aided Günther in these three contests, and Brunhild became his wife.", "투명 망토를 지닌 지크프리트는 이 세 가지 겨루기에서 군터를 도왔고, 브룬힐트는 군터의 아내가 되었다."], ["In return for these services, Günther gave Siegfried his sister Kriemhild in marriage.", "이러한 봉사의 대가로 군터는 자신의 누이 크림힐트를 지크프리트와 혼인시켰다."]], [["After some time had elapsed, Siegfried and Kriemhild went to visit Günther, when the two women fell into a dispute about the relative merits of their husbands.", "얼마간의 세월이 흐른 뒤, 지크프리트와 크림힐트가 군터를 방문했을 때, 두 여인은 각자의 남편이 지닌 우열을 놓고 다툼에 빠졌다."], ["Kriemhild, to exalt Siegfried, boasted that it was to the latter that Günther owed his victories and his wife.", "크림힐트는 지크프리트를 추켜세우려고, 군터가 자신의 승리들과 아내를 얻은 것은 바로 그 지크프리트 덕분이라고 떠벌렸다."], ["Brunhild, in great anger, employed Hagan, liegeman of Günther, to murder Siegfried.", "브룬힐트는 크게 노하여 군터의 봉신 하간을 시켜 지크프리트를 살해하게 했다."], ["In the epic Hagan is described as follows:", "서사시에서 하간은 다음과 같이 묘사된다."]], [["“Well-grown and well-compacted was that redoubted guest; Long were his legs and sinewy, and deep and broad his chest; His hair, that once was sable, with gray was dashed of late; Most terrible his visage, and lordly was his gait.” —Nibelungen Lied, stanza 1789.", "\"그 가공할 손님은 잘 자라고 단단히 다져진 몸이었네. 다리는 길고 힘줄이 불거졌으며, 가슴은 깊고 넓었네. 한때 검었던 그의 머리칼은 근래 회색이 섞였네. 그 얼굴은 더없이 무시무시했고, 걸음걸이는 위엄이 있었네.\" — 〈니벨룽겐의 노래〉, 1789연."]], [["This Achilles of German romance stabbed Siegfried between the shoulders, as the unfortunate King of the Netherlands was stooping to drink from a brook during a hunting expedition.", "게르만 로맨스의 이 아킬레우스는, 불운한 네덜란드 왕이 사냥 도중 시냇물을 마시려 몸을 숙이고 있을 때 그의 양 어깨 사이를 찔렀다."]], [["The second part of the epic relates how, thirteen years later, Kriemhild married Etzel, King of the Huns.", "서사시의 둘째 부분은, 십삼 년 뒤에 크림힐트가 훈족의 왕 에첼과 혼인한 이야기를 전한다."], ["After a time, she invited the King of Burgundy, with Hagan and many others, to the court of her husband.", "얼마 후 그녀는 부르군트의 왕을 하간과 그 밖의 많은 이와 더불어 자기 남편의 궁정으로 초대했다."], ["A fearful quarrel was stirred up in the banquet hall, which ended in the slaughter of all the Burgundians but Günther and Hagan.", "연회장에서 무서운 다툼이 일어났고, 그것은 군터와 하간을 제외한 모든 부르군트인의 살육으로 끝났다."], ["These two were taken prisoners and given to Kriemhild, who with her own hand cut off the heads of both.", "이 두 사람은 사로잡혀 크림힐트에게 넘겨졌고, 그녀는 자신의 손으로 두 사람의 목을 잘랐다."], ["For this bloody act of vengeance Kriemhild was herself slain by Hildebrand, a magician and champion, who in German mythology holds a place to an extent corresponding to that of Nestor in the Greek mythology.", "이 피비린내 나는 복수의 행위로 인해 크림힐트 자신도 힐데브란트에게 죽임을 당했는데, 그는 마법사이자 투사로서 게르만 신화에서 그리스 신화의 네스토르에 어느 정도 상응하는 자리를 차지한다."]], [["# THE NIBELUNGEN HOARD", "# THE NIBELUNGEN HOARD"]], [["This was a mythical mass of gold and precious stones which Siegfried obtained from the Nibelungs, the people of the north whom he had conquered and whose country he had made tributary to his own kingdom of the Netherlands.", "이것은 지크프리트가 니벨룽족에게서 얻은 황금과 보석의 신화적인 무더기였다. 니벨룽족은 그가 정복하여 그 나라를 자신의 왕국인 네덜란드에 조공을 바치게 만든 북방의 백성이었다."], ["Upon his marriage, Siegfried gave the treasure to Kriemhild as her wedding portion.", "혼인할 때 지크프리트는 그 보물을 신부의 몫으로 크림힐트에게 주었다."], ["After the murder of Siegfried, Hagan seized it and buried it secretly beneath the Rhine at Lochham, intending to recover it at a future period.", "지크프리트가 살해된 뒤 하간이 그것을 차지하여 훗날 되찾을 작정으로 로흐함의 라인 강 밑에 몰래 묻었다."], ["The hoard was lost forever when Hagan was killed by Kriemhild.", "하간이 크림힐트에게 죽임을 당했을 때 그 보물은 영영 사라졌다."], ["Its wonders are thus set forth in the poem:", "그 경이로움은 시에서 이렇게 그려진다."]], [["“’Twas as much as twelve huge wagons in four whole nights and days Could carry from the mountain down to the salt sea bay; Though to and fro each wagon thrice journeyed every day.", "\"거대한 수레 열두 대가 꼬박 나흘 낮과 밤 동안 산에서 짠 바닷가 만(灣)까지 실어 날라야 할 만큼이었네. 수레마다 날마다 세 번씩 오가는데도 그러하였네."], ["“It was made up of nothing but precious stones and gold; Were all the world bought from it, and down the value told, Not a mark the less would there be left than erst there was, I ween.”", "그것은 오로지 보석과 황금으로만 이루어져 있었네. 온 세상을 그것으로 사들이고 그 값을 셈하여 치른다 해도, 이전에 있던 데서 단 한 마르크도 줄지 않으리라, 내 짐작하건대.\""], ["—Nibelungen Lied, XIX.", "— 〈니벨룽겐의 노래〉, 19장."]], [["Whoever possessed the Nibelungen hoard were termed Nibelungers.", "니벨룽겐의 보물을 차지하는 자는 누구든 니벨룽족이라 불렸다."], ["Thus at one time certain people of Norway were so called.", "그리하여 한때 노르웨이의 어떤 백성이 그렇게 불렸다."], ["When Siegfried held the treasure he received the title “King of the Nibelungers.”", "지크프리트가 그 보물을 지녔을 때 그는 \"니벨룽족의 왕\"이라는 칭호를 얻었다."]], [["# WAGNER’S NIBELUNGEN RING", "# WAGNER’S NIBELUNGEN RING"]], [["Though Richard Wagner’s music-drama of the Nibelungen Ring bears some resemblance to the ancient German epic, it is a wholly independent composition and was derived from various old songs and sagas, which the dramatist wove into one great harmonious story.", "리하르트 바그너의 악극 〈니벨룽의 반지〉는 옛 게르만 서사시와 어느 정도 닮은 데가 있기는 하지만, 전적으로 독립된 작품으로, 갖가지 옛 노래와 사가에서 끌어와 극작가가 하나의 위대하고 조화로운 이야기로 엮어 낸 것이다."], ["The principal source was the Volsunga Saga, while lesser parts were taken from the Elder Edda and the Younger Edda, and others from the Nibelungen Lied, the Ecklenlied, and other Teutonic folklore.", "주된 출전은 〈볼숭 사가〉였으며, 비교적 작은 부분들은 〈옛 에다〉와 〈젊은 에다〉에서, 또 다른 부분들은 〈니벨룽겐의 노래〉와 〈에클렌리트〉, 그리고 그 밖의 게르만 민담에서 취해졌다."]], [["In the drama there are at first only four distinct races,—the gods, the giants, the dwarfs, and the nymphs.", "이 극에는 처음에 오직 네 가지 뚜렷한 종족만이 있으니, 곧 신들과 거인들과 난쟁이들과 님프들이다."], ["Later, by a special creation, there come the valkyrie and the heroes.", "후에 특별한 창조를 통해 발키리에와 영웅들이 등장한다."], ["The gods are the noblest and highest race, and dwell first in the mountain meadows, later in the palace of Valhalla on the heights.", "신들은 가장 고귀하고 드높은 종족으로, 처음에는 산속의 초원에서 살다가 후에는 높은 봉우리 위의 궁전 발할라에서 산다."], ["The giants are a great and strong race, but lack wisdom; they hate what is noble, and are enemies of the gods; they dwell in caves near the earth’s surface.", "거인들은 위대하고 강한 종족이지만 지혜가 모자라며, 고귀한 것을 미워하고 신들의 적이다. 그들은 땅 표면 가까운 동굴에서 산다."], ["The dwarfs, or nibelungs, are black uncouth pigmies, hating the good, hating the gods; they are crafty and cunning, and dwell in the bowels of the earth.", "난쟁이들, 곧 니벨룽족은 검고 거칠게 생긴 난쟁이들로, 선한 것을 미워하고 신들을 미워하며, 교활하고 간교하여 땅속 깊은 곳에서 산다."], ["The nymphs are pure, innocent creatures of the water.", "님프들은 순수하고 죄 없는 물의 피조물이다."], ["The valkyrie are daughters of the gods, but mingled with a mortal strain; they gather dead heroes from the battle-fields and carry them to Valhalla.", "발키리에는 신들의 딸이지만 인간의 혈통이 섞여 있으며, 전장에서 죽은 영웅들을 거두어 발할라로 데려간다."], ["The heroes are children of the gods, but also mingled with a mortal strain; they are destined to become at last the highest race of all, and to succeed the gods in the government of the world.", "영웅들은 신들의 자손이지만 역시 인간의 혈통이 섞여 있으며, 마침내 모든 종족 가운데 가장 드높은 종족이 되어 세상의 통치에서 신들을 잇도록 운명 지어져 있다."]], [["The principal gods are Wotan, Loki, Donner, and Froh.", "주요한 신들은 보탄과 로키와 도너와 프로다."], ["The chief giants are Fafner and Fasolt, brothers.", "으뜸가는 거인들은 형제인 파프너와 파졸트이다."], ["The chief dwarfs are Alberich and Mime, brothers, and later Hagan, son of Alberich.", "으뜸가는 난쟁이들은 형제인 알베리히와 미메, 그리고 후에 알베리히의 아들 하간이다."], ["The chief nymphs are the Rhine-daughters, Flosshilda, Woglinda, and Wellgunda.", "으뜸가는 님프들은 라인의 딸들인 플로스힐다와 보글린다와 벨군다이다."], ["There are nine Valkyrie, of whom Brunhild is the leading one.", "발키리에는 아홉이 있는데, 그 가운데 브룬힐트가 으뜸가는 자이다."]], [["Wagner’s story of the Ring may be summarized as follows:", "바그너의 〈반지〉 이야기는 다음과 같이 요약할 수 있다."]], [["A hoard of gold exists in the depths of the Rhine, guarded by the innocent Rhine-maidens.", "라인 강 깊은 곳에 황금 무더기가 있어, 죄 없는 라인의 처녀들이 그것을 지키고 있다."], ["Alberich, the dwarf, forswears love to gain this gold.", "난쟁이 알베리히가 이 황금을 얻고자 사랑을 버린다."], ["He makes it into a magic ring.", "그는 그것으로 마법의 반지를 만든다."], ["It gives him all power, and he gathers by it a vast amount of treasures.", "그 반지는 그에게 온갖 권능을 주고, 그는 그것으로 막대한 양의 보물을 그러모은다."]], [["Meanwhile Wotan, chief of the gods, has engaged the giants to build for him a noble castle, Valhalla, from whence to rule the world, promising in payment Freya, goddess of youth and love.", "한편 신들의 우두머리 보탄은 세상을 다스릴 거처로 삼을 고귀한 성 발할라를 지어 달라고 거인들과 계약을 맺고, 그 대가로 젊음과 사랑의 여신 프레이야를 주기로 약속했다."], ["But the gods find they cannot spare Freya, as they are dependent on her for their immortal youth.", "그러나 신들은 자신들의 불멸의 젊음이 프레이야에게 달려 있는지라 그녀를 내어 줄 수 없음을 알게 된다."], ["Loki, called upon to provide a substitute, tells of Alberich’s magic ring and other treasure.", "대신할 것을 마련하라는 요구를 받은 로키는 알베리히의 마법 반지와 그 밖의 보물에 대해 이야기한다."], ["Wotan goes with Loki, and they steal the ring and the golden hoard from Alberich, who curses the ring and lays the curse on all who shall henceforth possess it.", "보탄은 로키와 함께 가서 알베리히에게서 반지와 황금 무더기를 훔치고, 알베리히는 그 반지를 저주하며 이후로 그것을 지니는 모든 자에게 저주를 내린다."], ["The gods give the ring and the treasure to the giants as a substitute for Freya.", "신들은 프레이야 대신 그 반지와 보물을 거인들에게 준다."], ["The curse at once begins.", "저주는 즉시 시작된다."], ["One giant, Fafner, kills his brother to get all, and transforms himself into a dragon to guard his wealth.", "거인 가운데 하나인 파프너는 모든 것을 차지하려고 형제를 죽이고, 자신의 재물을 지키려 스스로를 용으로 변신시킨다."], ["The gods enter Valhalla over the rainbow bridge.", "신들은 무지개다리를 건너 발할라로 들어간다."], ["This ends the first part of the drama, called the Rhine-Gold.", "이로써 〈라인의 황금〉이라 불리는 극의 첫째 부분이 끝난다."]], [["The second part, the Valkyrie, relates how Wotan still covets the ring.", "둘째 부분 〈발키리에〉는, 보탄이 여전히 그 반지를 탐낸다는 이야기를 전한다."], ["He cannot take it himself, for he has given his word to the giants.", "그는 거인들에게 약속을 했기에 스스로 그것을 빼앗을 수 없다."], ["He stands or falls by his word.", "그는 자신의 약속으로 흥하기도 망하기도 하는 것이다."], ["So he devises an artifice to get the ring.", "그래서 그는 반지를 손에 넣을 책략을 꾸민다."], ["He will get a hero-race to work for him and recover the ring and the treasures.", "그는 자신을 위해 일하며 반지와 보물을 되찾아 줄 영웅의 종족을 얻으려 한다."], ["Siegmund and Sieglinda are twin children of this new race.", "지크문트와 지크린데가 이 새 종족의 쌍둥이 아이들이다."], ["Sieglinda is carried off as a child and is forced into marriage with Hunding.", "지크린데는 어린 시절 납치되어 훈딩과의 혼인을 강요당한다."], ["Siegmund comes, and unknowingly breaks the law of marriage, but wins Nothung, the great sword, and a bride.", "지크문트가 와서 자기도 모르게 혼인의 법을 어기지만, 위대한 검 노퉁과 신부를 얻는다."], ["Brunhild, chief of the Valkyrie, is commissioned by Wotan at the instance of Fricka, goddess of marriage, to slay him for his sin.", "발키리에의 우두머리 브룬힐트는 혼인의 여신 프리카의 청에 따라, 지크문트의 죄를 물어 그를 죽이라는 명을 보탄에게서 받는다."], ["She disobeys and tries to save him, but Hunding, helped by Wotan, slays him.", "그녀는 명을 거역하고 그를 구하려 하지만, 보탄의 도움을 받은 훈딩이 그를 죽인다."], ["Sieglinda, however, about to bear the free hero, to be called Siegfried, is saved by Brunhild, and hid in the forest.", "그러나 장차 지크프리트라 불릴 자유로운 영웅을 잉태하고 있던 지크린데는 브룬힐트에게 구원받아 숲속에 숨겨진다."], ["Brunhild herself is punished by being made a mortal woman.", "브룬힐트 자신은 인간 여인이 되는 벌을 받는다."], ["She is left sleeping on the mountains with a wall of fire around her which only a hero can penetrate.", "그녀는 영웅만이 뚫을 수 있는 불의 벽에 둘러싸인 채 산 위에 잠든 채로 남겨진다."]], [["The drama continues with the story of Siegfried, which opens with a scene in the smithy between Mime the dwarf and Siegfried.", "극은 지크프리트의 이야기로 이어지는데, 그것은 난쟁이 미메와 지크프리트 사이의 대장간 장면으로 열린다."], ["Mime is welding a sword, and Siegfried scorns him.", "미메는 검을 벼리고 있고, 지크프리트는 그를 경멸한다."], ["Mime tells him something of his mother, Sieglinda, and shows him the broken pieces of his father’s sword.", "미메는 그에게 그의 어머니 지크린데에 대해 조금 일러 주고, 그의 아버지의 부러진 검 조각들을 보여 준다."], ["Wotan comes and tells Mime that only one who has no fear can remake the sword.", "보탄이 와서, 두려움을 모르는 자만이 그 검을 다시 벼릴 수 있다고 미메에게 말한다."], ["Now Siegfried knows no fear and soon remakes the sword Nothung.", "그런데 지크프리트는 두려움을 모르는지라 곧 검 노퉁을 다시 벼려 낸다."], ["Wotan and Alberich come to where the dragon Fafner is guarding the ring.", "보탄과 알베리히는 용 파프너가 반지를 지키고 있는 곳으로 온다."], ["They both long for it, but neither can take it.", "둘 다 그것을 갈망하지만, 어느 쪽도 그것을 빼앗을 수 없다."], ["Soon Mime comes bringing Siegfried with the mighty sword.", "곧 미메가 위대한 검을 지닌 지크프리트를 데리고 온다."], ["Fafner comes out, but Siegfried slays him.", "파프너가 나오지만, 지크프리트가 그를 죽인다."], ["Happening to touch his lips with the dragon’s blood, he understands the language of the birds.", "어쩌다 용의 피가 입술에 닿자, 그는 새들의 언어를 알아듣게 된다. 새들은 그에게 반지에 대해 일러 준다."], ["They tell him of the ring.", "그는 가서 그것을 손에 넣는다."], ["He goes and gets it. Siegfried now has possession of the ring, but it is to bring him nothing of happiness, only evil.", "이제 지크프리트가 반지를 차지하게 되었으나, 그것은 그에게 어떤 행복도 가져다주지 않고 오직 재앙만을 가져올 것이다."], ["It is to curse love and finally bring death.", "그것은 사랑을 저주하고 마침내 죽음을 불러올 것이다."], ["The birds also tell him of Mime’s treachery.", "새들은 또한 그에게 미메의 배신을 일러 준다."], ["He slays Mime.", "그는 미메를 죽인다."], ["He longs for some one to love.", "그는 사랑할 누군가를 갈망한다."], ["The birds tell him of the slumbering Brunnhilda, whom he finds and marries.", "새들은 그에게 잠들어 있는 브룬힐다에 대해 일러 주고, 그는 그녀를 찾아내어 혼인한다."]], [["The Dusk of the Gods portrays at the opening the three norns or fates weaving and measuring the thread of destiny.", "〈신들의 황혼〉은 그 첫머리에 운명의 실을 잣고 재는 세 노른, 곧 운명의 여신들을 그린다."], ["It is the beginning of the end.", "이는 종말의 시작이다."], ["The perfect pair, Siegfried and Brunhild, appear in all the glory of their life, splendid ideals of manhood and womanhood.", "완벽한 한 쌍인 지크프리트와 브룬힐트가 그들 삶의 온갖 영광 속에서, 남성다움과 여성다움의 찬란한 이상으로 나타난다."], ["But Siegfried goes out into the world to achieve deeds of prowess.", "그러나 지크프리트는 무용(武勇)의 위업을 이루러 세상으로 나아간다."], ["He gives her the Nibelungen ring to keep as a pledge of his love till his return.", "그는 돌아올 때까지 자신의 사랑의 정표로 간직하라며 그녀에게 니벨룽겐의 반지를 준다."], ["Meanwhile Alberich also has begotten a son, Hagan, to achieve for him the possession of the ring.", "한편 알베리히 또한 자신을 위해 반지를 손에 넣어 줄 아들 하간을 낳았다."], ["He is partly of the Gibichung race, and works through Günther and Gutrune, half-brother and half-sister to him.", "하간은 일부는 기비흥 종족의 피를 지녔으며, 자신의 이복형제와 이복누이인 군터와 구트루네를 통해 일을 꾸민다."], ["They beguile Siegfried to them, give him a magic draught which makes him forget Brunhild and fall in love with Gutrune.", "그들은 지크프리트를 자기들에게로 꾀어 들여, 그에게 브룬힐트를 잊고 구트루네와 사랑에 빠지게 만드는 마법의 음료를 먹인다."], ["Under this same spell, he offers to bring Brunhild for wife to Günther.", "같은 마법에 걸린 채 그는 브룬힐트를 군터의 아내로 데려오겠노라 제안한다."], ["Now is Valhalla full of sorrow and despair.", "이제 발할라는 슬픔과 절망으로 가득하다."], ["The gods fear the end.", "신들은 종말을 두려워한다."], ["Wotan murmurs, “O that she would give back the ring to the Rhine.”", "보탄은 \"아, 그녀가 그 반지를 라인 강에 돌려주기만 한다면\" 하고 중얼거린다."], ["But Brunhild will not give it up,—it is now her pledge of love.", "그러나 브룬힐트는 그것을 내놓으려 하지 않으니, 이제 그것은 그녀의 사랑의 정표인 것이다."], ["Siegfried comes, takes the ring, and Brunhild is now brought to the Rhine castle of the Gibichungs, but Siegfried under the spell does not love her.", "지크프리트가 와서 반지를 빼앗고, 브룬힐트는 이제 기비흥 종족의 라인 강 성으로 끌려오지만, 마법에 걸린 지크프리트는 그녀를 사랑하지 않는다."], ["She is to be wedded to Günther.", "그녀는 군터와 혼인하게 될 처지이다."], ["She rises in wrath and denounces Siegfried.", "그녀는 분노하여 일어서서 지크프리트를 규탄한다."], ["But at a hunting banquet Siegfried is given another magic draught, remembers all, and is slain by Hagan by a blow in the back, as he calls on Brunhild’s name in love.", "그러나 사냥 연회에서 지크프리트는 또 다른 마법의 음료를 받아 마시고 모든 것을 기억해 내며, 사랑에 잠겨 브룬힐트의 이름을 부르던 그 순간 등에 일격을 맞아 하간에게 죽임을 당한다."], ["Then comes the end.", "그리하여 종말이 온다."], ["The body of Siegfried is burned on a funeral pyre, a grand funeral march is heard, and Brunhild rides into the flames and sacrifices herself for love’s sake; the ring goes back to the Rhine-daughters; and the old world—of the gods of Valhalla, of passion and sin—is burnt up with flames, for the gods have broken moral law, and coveted power rather than love, gold rather than truth, and therefore must perish.", "지크프리트의 시신은 장작더미 위에서 불태워지고, 장엄한 장송 행진곡이 울려 퍼지며, 브룬힐트는 불길 속으로 말을 달려 들어가 사랑을 위해 자신을 희생한다. 반지는 라인의 딸들에게 되돌아가고, 발할라의 신들과 정념과 죄로 이루어진 옛 세계는 불길에 타 버린다."], ["They pass, and a new era, the reign of love and truth, has begun.", "신들이 도덕의 법을 어기고 사랑보다 권력을, 진실보다 황금을 탐했으므로 멸망해야 마땅한 것이다. 그들은 스러지고, 새로운 시대, 곧 사랑과 진실의 치세가 시작되었다."]], [["Those who wish to study the differences in the legends of the Nibelungen Lied and the Nibelungen Ring, and the way in which Wagner used this ancient material, are referred to Professor W.", "〈니벨룽겐의 노래〉와 〈니벨룽의 반지〉의 전설들 사이의 차이, 그리고 바그너가 이 옛 소재를 사용한 방식을 연구하고자 하는 이들에게는, W. C."], ["C. Sawyer’s book on “Teutonic Legends in the Nibelungen Lied and the Nibelungen Ring,”", "소여 교수의 저서 〈니벨룽겐의 노래와 니벨룽의 반지에 나타난 게르만 전설〉을 권한다."], ["where the matter is treated in full detail.", "거기서 이 문제가 충분히 상세하게 다루어진다."], ["For a very thorough and clear analysis of the Ring as Wagner gives it, with a study of the musical motifs, probably nothing is better for general readers than the volume “The Epic of Sounds,” by Freda Winworth.", "바그너가 제시한 대로의 〈반지〉를 음악적 주제들에 대한 연구와 더불어 매우 철저하고 명료하게 분석한 것으로는, 일반 독자에게는 아마도 프레다 윈워스의 〈소리의 서사시〉라는 책보다 나은 것이 없을 것이다."], ["The more scholarly work of Professor Lavignac is indispensable for the student of Wagner’s dramas.", "라비냐크 교수의 보다 학구적인 저작은 바그너의 악극을 공부하는 이에게 없어서는 안 될 것이다."], ["There is much illuminating comment on the sources and materials in “Legends of the Wagner Drama”", "J."], ["by J.", "L."], ["L. Weston.", "웨스턴의 〈바그너 악극의 전설〉에는 그 출전과 소재에 관한 밝혀 주는 바가 많은 해설이 담겨 있다."]], [["————", "————"]], [["THE Druids were the priests or ministers of religion among the ancient Celtic nations in Gaul, Britain, and Germany.", "드루이드는 갈리아와 브리타니아, 게르마니아에 살던 고대 켈트 민족 사이에서 종교의 사제 또는 봉사자였다."], ["Our information respecting them is borrowed from notices in the Greek and Roman writers, compared with the remains of Welsh and Gaelic poetry still extant.", "그들에 관해 우리가 아는 바는 그리스와 로마 저술가들이 남긴 기록을, 오늘날까지 전해지는 웨일스와 게일의 시가 유물과 비교하여 얻은 것이다."]], [["The Druids combined the functions of the priest, the magistrate, the scholar, and the physician.", "드루이드는 사제와 행정관, 학자, 그리고 의사의 기능을 한데 겸하였다."], ["They stood to the people of the Celtic tribes in a relation closely analogous to that in which the Brahmans of India, the Magi of Persia, and the priests of the Egyptians stood to the people respectively by whom they were revered.", "그들이 켈트 부족의 사람들에 대해 가졌던 관계는, 인도의 브라만이나 페르시아의 마기, 이집트의 사제들이 저마다 그들을 숭배하던 사람들에 대해 가졌던 관계와 매우 흡사한 것이었다."]], [["The Druids taught the existence of one god, to whom they gave a name “Be’ al,” which Celtic antiquaries tell us means “the life of everything,”", "드루이드는 단 하나의 신이 존재한다고 가르쳤으며, 그 신에게 〈베알(Be’al)〉이라는 이름을 붙였다."], ["or “the source of all beings,” and which seems to have affinity with the Phœnician Baal.", "켈트 고고학자들의 말에 따르면 이 이름은 〈모든 것의 생명〉, 혹은 〈모든 존재의 근원〉을 뜻하며, 페니키아의 바알과 친연성이 있는 것으로 보인다."], ["What renders this affinity more striking is that the Druids as well as the Phœnicians identified this, their supreme deity, with the Sun.", "이 친연성을 한층 두드러지게 만드는 것은, 드루이드가 페니키아인과 마찬가지로 자신들의 이 최고신을 〈태양〉과 동일시했다는 점이다."], ["Fire was regarded as a symbol of the divinity.", "불은 신성의 상징으로 여겨졌다."], ["The Latin writers assert that the Druids also worshipped numerous inferior gods.", "라틴 저술가들은 드루이드가 그밖에도 수많은 하급 신을 숭배했다고 주장한다."]], [["They used no images to represent the object of their worship, nor did they meet in temples or buildings of any kind for the performance of their sacred rites.", "그들은 숭배의 대상을 나타내기 위해 어떠한 형상도 사용하지 않았으며, 신성한 의식을 거행하기 위해 신전이나 그 어떤 종류의 건물에 모이지도 않았다."], ["A circle of stones (each stone generally of vast size), enclosing an area of from twenty feet to thirty yards in diameter, constituted their sacred place.", "돌들이 이룬 원(저마다의 돌은 대개 거대한 크기였다)이 지름 20피트에서 30야드에 이르는 구역을 에워쌌으니, 이것이 그들의 신성한 장소를 이루었다."], ["The most celebrated of these now remaining is Stonehenge, on Salisbury Plain, England.", "오늘날까지 남아 있는 이런 유적 가운데 가장 유명한 것은 잉글랜드 솔즈베리 평원에 있는 스톤헨지다."]], [["These sacred circles were generally situated near some stream, or under the shadow of a grove or widespreading oak.", "이 신성한 원들은 대개 어떤 시냇가 근처, 혹은 작은 숲이나 널리 가지를 펼친 떡갈나무의 그늘 아래에 자리 잡았다."], ["In the centre of the circle stood the Cromlech or altar, which was a large stone, placed in the manner of a table upon other stones set up on end.", "원의 한가운데에는 크롬렉, 곧 제단이 서 있었는데, 그것은 커다란 돌을 세워 둔 다른 돌들 위에 탁자처럼 얹어 놓은 것이었다."], ["The Druids had also their high places, which were large stones or piles of stones on the summits of hills.", "드루이드에게는 또한 그들만의 높은 장소가 있었으니, 언덕 꼭대기에 놓인 커다란 돌이나 돌무더기가 그것이었다."], ["These were called Cairns, and were used in the worship of the deity under the symbol of the sun.", "이런 것들은 케언이라 불렸으며, 태양의 상징 아래 신을 숭배하는 데 쓰였다."]], [["That the Druids offered sacrifices to their deity there can be no doubt.", "드루이드가 자신들의 신에게 제물을 바쳤다는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없다."], ["But there is some uncertainty as to what they offered, and of the ceremonies connected with their religious services we know almost nothing.", "그러나 그들이 무엇을 바쳤는지는 다소 불분명하며, 그들의 종교 의례에 결부된 의식에 관해서는 우리가 거의 아무것도 알지 못한다."], ["The classical (Roman) writers affirm that they offered on great occasions human sacrifices; as for success in war or for relief from dangerous diseases.", "고전기(로마)의 저술가들은 그들이 큰일이 있을 때, 이를테면 전쟁의 승리를 위해서나 위험한 질병에서 벗어나기 위해 인간 제물을 바쳤다고 단언한다."], ["Cæsar has given a detailed account of the manner in which this was done.", "카이사르는 이 일이 어떤 방식으로 행해졌는지 상세히 기록해 두었다."], ["“They have images of immense size, the limbs of which are framed with twisted twigs and filled with living persons.", "\"그들에게는 거대한 크기의 형상들이 있는데, 그 사지는 비틀어 엮은 잔가지로 짜였으며 그 안에는 살아 있는 사람들이 채워진다."], ["These being set on fire, those within are encompassed by the flames.”", "여기에 불을 붙이면 안에 있는 자들은 불길에 둘러싸인다.\""], ["Many attempts have been made by Celtic writers to shake the testimony of the Roman historians to this fact, but without success.", "켈트의 저술가들은 이 사실에 관한 로마 사가들의 증언을 흔들어 보려고 여러 차례 시도했으나, 끝내 성공하지 못했다."]], [["The Druids observed two festivals in each year.", "드루이드는 해마다 두 차례의 축제를 지켰다."], ["The former took place in the beginning of May, and was called Beltane or “fire of God.”", "첫 번째 축제는 5월 초에 열렸으며, 벨테인 곧 〈신의 불〉이라 불렸다."], ["On this occasion a large fire was kindled on some elevated spot, in honor of the sun, whose returning beneficence they thus welcomed after the gloom and desolation of winter.", "이때에는 어느 높은 곳에 큰 불을 피워 태양을 기렸는데, 겨울의 어둠과 황량함이 지난 뒤 다시 돌아오는 태양의 은혜를 이렇게 맞이한 것이다."], ["Of this custom a trace remains in the name given to Whitsunday in parts of Scotland to this day.", "이 풍습의 자취는 오늘날까지도 스코틀랜드 일부 지방에서 성령강림절에 붙이는 이름에 남아 있다."], ["Sir Walter Scott uses the word in the “Boat Song” in the “Lady of the Lake”:", "월터 스콧 경은 「호수의 여인」에 실린 〈뱃노래〉에서 이 말을 쓰고 있다."]], [["“Ours is no sapling, chance sown by the fountain, Blooming at Beltane in winter to fade;”", "\"우리는 샘가에 우연히 씨가 뿌려진 어린나무가 아니니, 벨테인에 피어났다가 겨울에 시들지 않으리.\""], ["etc.", "운운."]], [["The other great festival of the Druids was called “Samh’in,” or “fire of peace,”", "드루이드의 또 다른 큰 축제는 〈삼인(Samh’in)〉 곧 〈평화의 불〉이라 불렸으며, 만성절 전야(11월 1일)에 열렸으니, 스코틀랜드 고지대에서는 지금까지도 이 명칭이 남아 있다."], ["and was held on Hallow-eve (first of November), which still retains this designation in the Highlands of Scotland.", "이때에 드루이드는 그들 교단의 사법 기능을 수행하기 위해 그 지역의 가장 중심이 되는 곳에 엄숙히 모여 회의를 열었다."], ["On this occasion the Druids assembled in solemn conclave, in the most central part of the district, to discharge the judicial functions of their order.", "공적인 것이든 사적인 것이든 모든 문제, 사람이나 재산에 대한 모든 범죄가 이때 그들 앞에 판결을 위해 회부되었다."], ["All questions, whether public or private, all crimes against person or property, were at this time brought before them for adjudication.", "이러한 사법적 행위에는 어떤 미신적 관습이 결부되었으니, 특히 신성한 불을 피우는 일이 그러했다."], ["With these judicial acts were combined certain superstitious usages, especially the kindling of the sacred fire, from which all the fires in the district, which had been beforehand scrupulously extinguished, might be relighted.", "그 지역의 모든 불은 미리 빈틈없이 꺼 두었다가 이 불에서 다시 점화될 수 있었다."], ["This usage of kindling fires on Hallow-eve lingered in the British islands long after the establishment of Christianity.", "만성절 전야에 불을 피우는 이 관습은 그리스도교가 확립된 뒤에도 오래도록 브리튼 제도에 남아 있었다."]], [["Besides these two great annual festivals, the Druids were in the habit of observing the full moon, and especially the sixth day of the moon.", "이 두 차례의 큰 연례 축제 말고도 드루이드는 보름달, 특히 달의 여섯째 날을 지키는 습관이 있었다."], ["On the latter they sought the Mistletoe, which grew on their favorite oaks, and to which, as well as to the oak itself, they ascribed a peculiar virtue and sacredness.", "이날 그들은 자신들이 아끼는 떡갈나무에서 자라는 겨우살이를 찾았는데, 떡갈나무 그 자체뿐 아니라 이 겨우살이에도 특별한 효험과 신성함이 깃들어 있다고 여겼다."], ["The discovery of it was an occasion of rejoicing and solemn worship. “They call it,”", "그것을 발견하는 일은 기쁨과 엄숙한 예배의 계기가 되었다."], ["says Pliny, “by a word in their language, which means ‘heal-all,’ and having made solemn preparation for feasting and sacrifice under the tree, they drive thither two milk-white bulls, whose horns are then for the first time bound.", "플리니우스는 이렇게 말한다. \"그들은 그것을 자기네 언어로 〈만병통치〉를 뜻하는 말로 부르는데, 나무 아래에서 잔치와 제사를 위해 엄숙한 준비를 마친 다음, 그곳으로 젖처럼 흰 황소 두 마리를 끌고 온다."], ["The priest then, robed in white, ascends the tree, and cuts off the mistletoe with a golden sickle.", "그 황소들의 뿔은 그때 처음으로 묶인다. 그러고 나서 흰옷을 입은 사제가 나무에 올라가 황금 낫으로 겨우살이를 베어 낸다."], ["It is caught in a white mantle, after which they proceed to slay the victims, at the same time praying that God would render his gift prosperous to those to whom he had given it.”", "그것을 흰 외투로 받아 내고는, 이어서 제물을 죽이는데, 그와 동시에 신이 그 선물을 받은 자들에게 그 선물이 번성하게 해 주시기를 기도한다.\""], ["They drink the water in which it has been infused, and think it a remedy for all diseases.", "그들은 그것을 우려낸 물을 마시며, 그것을 모든 질병의 치료약이라 여긴다."], ["The mistletoe is a parasitic plant, and is not always nor often found on the oak, so that when it is found it is the more precious.", "겨우살이는 기생 식물이라 떡갈나무에서 늘, 혹은 흔히 발견되는 것은 아니다. 그러므로 그것이 발견되면 그만큼 더 귀하게 여겨진다."]], [["The Druids were the teachers of morality as well as of religion.", "드루이드는 종교뿐 아니라 도덕의 교사이기도 했다."], ["Of their ethical teaching a valuable specimen is preserved in the Triads of the Welsh Bards, and from this we may gather that their views of moral rectitude were on the whole just, and that they held and inculcated many very noble and valuable principles of conduct.", "그들의 윤리적 가르침에 관해서는 웨일스 음유시인들의 트리어드에 귀중한 본보기가 보존되어 있다. 이로부터 우리는 도덕적 올바름에 관한 그들의 견해가 대체로 정당했으며, 그들이 매우 고결하고 값진 여러 행동의 원칙을 견지하고 가르쳤음을 짐작할 수 있다."], ["They were also the men of science and learning of their age and people.", "그들은 또한 그 시대와 민족 가운데서 학문과 지식을 갖춘 자들이었다."], ["Whether they were acquainted with letters or not has been disputed, though the probability is strong that they were, to some extent.", "그들이 문자를 알았는지 여부는 논쟁거리였으나, 어느 정도까지는 알았을 가능성이 높다."], ["But it is certain that they committed nothing of their doctrine, their history, or their poetry to writing.", "그러나 그들이 자신들의 교리나 역사, 혹은 시 가운데 그 무엇도 글로 적어 두지 않았다는 것만은 확실하다."], ["Their teaching was oral, and their literature (if such a word may be used in such a case) was preserved solely by tradition.", "그들의 가르침은 입으로 전해졌으며, 그들의 문학(이런 경우에 이 말을 써도 좋다면)은 오로지 구전으로 보존되었다."], ["But the Roman writers admit that “they paid much attention to the order and laws of nature, and investigated and taught to the youth under their charge many things concerning the stars and their motions, the size of the world and the lands, and concerning the might and power of the immortal gods.”", "그러나 로마 저술가들은 \"그들이 자연의 질서와 법칙에 많은 주의를 기울였으며, 자기네가 맡은 젊은이들에게 별과 그 운행, 세계와 대지의 크기, 그리고 불멸하는 신들의 위력과 권능에 관한 많은 것을 탐구하여 가르쳤다\"고 인정한다."]], [["Their history consisted in traditional tales, in which the heroic deeds of their forefathers were celebrated.", "그들의 역사는 전승되는 이야기들로 이루어졌으니, 그 속에서 선조들의 영웅적 행적이 기려졌다."], ["These were apparently in verse, and thus constituted part of the poetry as well as the history of the Druids.", "이 이야기들은 보아하니 운문이었던 것 같으며, 그리하여 드루이드의 역사이자 시의 일부를 이루었다."], ["In the poems of Ossian we have, if not the actual productions of Druidical times, what may be considered faithful representations of the songs of the Bards.", "오시안의 시에서 우리는, 비록 드루이드 시대의 실제 작품은 아닐지라도, 음유시인들의 노래를 충실히 재현한 것으로 간주할 만한 것을 만나게 된다."]], [["The Bards were an essential part of the Druidical hierarchy.", "음유시인들은 드루이드 위계 조직의 필수적인 일부였다."], ["One author, Pennant, says, “The Bards were supposed to be endowed with powers equal to inspiration.", "한 저술가, 페넌트는 이렇게 말한다. \"음유시인들은 영감에 맞먹는 능력을 부여받은 자들로 여겨졌다."], ["They were the oral historians of all past transactions, public and private.", "그들은 공적인 것이든 사적인 것이든 지난 모든 일을 입으로 전하는 사가였다."], ["They were also accomplished genealogists,”", "그들은 또한 뛰어난 계보학자이기도 했다.\""], ["etc.", "운운."]], [["Pennant gives a minute account of the Eisteddfods or sessions of the Bards and minstrels, which were held in Wales for many centuries, long after the Druidical priesthood in its other departments became extinct.", "페넌트는 음유시인과 악사들의 모임인 아이스테드보드에 관해 자세한 설명을 남겼는데, 이 모임은 드루이드 사제직이 그 다른 분야에서 소멸한 뒤로도 오래도록 여러 세기에 걸쳐 웨일스에서 열렸다."], ["At these meetings none but Bards of merit were suffered to rehearse their pieces, and minstrels of skill to perform.", "이 모임에서는 오로지 실력 있는 음유시인만이 자기 작품을 읊을 수 있었으며, 솜씨 있는 악사만이 연주할 수 있었다."], ["Judges were appointed to decide on their respective abilities, and suitable degrees were conferred.", "저마다의 능력을 판가름할 심사관이 임명되었고, 합당한 등급이 수여되었다."], ["In the earlier period the judges were appointed by the Welsh princes, and after the conquest of Wales, by commission from the kings of England.", "더 이른 시기에는 심사관이 웨일스 군주들에 의해 임명되었고, 웨일스가 정복된 뒤에는 잉글랜드 왕들의 위임을 받아 임명되었다."], ["Yet the tradition is that Edward I., in revenge for the influence of the Bards in animating the resistance of the people to his sway, persecuted them with great cruelty.", "그러나 전해 오는 이야기에 따르면, 에드워드 1세는 음유시인들이 자신의 지배에 맞서는 백성들의 저항을 북돋운 영향력에 앙심을 품고 그들을 가혹하게 박해했다고 한다."], ["This tradition has furnished the poet Gray with the subject of his celebrated ode, the “Bard.”", "이 전승은 시인 그레이에게 그의 유명한 송가 〈음유시인〉의 소재를 제공했다."]], [["There are still occasional meetings of the lovers of Welsh poetry and music, held under the ancient name.", "지금도 웨일스의 시와 음악을 사랑하는 이들이 옛 이름을 내걸고 간간이 모임을 갖는다."], ["Among Mrs. Hemans’ poems is one written for an Eisteddfod, or meeting of Welsh Bards, held in London, May 22, 1822.", "헤먼스 부인의 시 가운데 1822년 5월 22일 런던에서 열린 아이스테드보드, 곧 웨일스 음유시인들의 모임을 위해 쓴 작품이 있다."], ["It begins with a description of the ancient meeting, of which the following lines are a part:", "그것은 옛 모임에 대한 묘사로 시작되는데, 다음 시행들이 그 일부다."]], [["“. .", "\"."], [".", "."], ["midst the eternal cliffs, whose strength defied The crested Roman in his hour of pride; And where the Druid’s ancient cromlech frowned, And the oaks breathed mysterious murmurs round, There thronged the inspired of yore!", ". 영원한 절벽들 한가운데, 그 굳셈은 교만의 시절에 투구 쓴 로마인마저 물리쳤으니, 드루이드의 옛 크롬렉이 험상궂게 내려다보고, 떡갈나무들이 사방에 신비로운 속삭임을 불어넣던 그곳, 그곳에 옛적의 영감받은 자들이 모여들었네!"], ["on plain or height, In the sun’s face, beneath the eye of light, And baring unto heaven each noble head, Stood in the circle, where none else might tread.”", "평원에서나 높은 곳에서나, 태양의 얼굴 앞에서, 빛의 눈길 아래, 저마다 고귀한 머리를 하늘 향해 드러내며, 그 누구도 발 디딜 수 없는 원 안에 그들은 섰네.\""]], [["The Druidical system was at its height at the time of the Roman invasion under Julius Cæsar.", "드루이드 체계는 율리우스 카이사르가 이끈 로마의 침공 무렵에 절정에 이르렀다."], ["Against the Druids, as their chief enemies, these conquerors of the world directed their unsparing fury.", "이 세계의 정복자들은 자신들의 으뜸가는 적인 드루이드를 향해 가차 없는 분노를 쏟아부었다."], ["The Druids, harassed at all points on the mainland, retreated to Anglesey and Iona, where for a season they found shelter and continued their now dishonored rites.", "사방에서 시달리던 드루이드는 본토에서 앵글시와 이오나로 물러났으니, 그곳에서 한동안 피난처를 얻어 이제는 명예를 잃은 자신들의 의식을 이어 갔다."]], [["The Druids retained their predominance in Iona and over the adjacent islands and mainland until they were supplanted and their superstitions overturned by the arrival of St.", "드루이드는 이오나와 그 인접한 섬들 및 본토에서 우위를 지켜 갔으나, 고지대의 사도 성 콜룸바가 다다르면서 그 자리를 빼앗기고 그들의 미신이 뒤엎이게 되었다."], ["Columba, the apostle of the Highlands, by whom the inhabitants of that district were first led to profess Christianity.", "그 지역의 주민들이 처음으로 그리스도교를 신앙하게 된 것은 콜룸바에 의해서였다."]], [["One of the smallest of the British Isles, situated near a rugged and barren coast, surrounded by dangerous seas, and possessing no sources of internal wealth, Iona has obtained an imperishable place in history as the seat of civilization and religion at a time when the darkness of heathenism hung over almost the whole of Northern Europe.", "브리튼 제도 가운데 가장 작은 섬의 하나로, 험준하고 메마른 해안 가까이 자리 잡았으며, 위험한 바다에 둘러싸이고 내부의 부의 원천이라고는 지니지 못했음에도, 이오나는 이교의 어둠이 북유럽 거의 전역에 드리워져 있던 시대에 문명과 종교의 본거지로서 역사 속에 사라지지 않는 자리를 얻었다."], ["Iona or Icolmkill is situated at the extremity of the island of Mull, from which it is separated by a strait of half a mile in breadth, its distance from the mainland of Scotland being thirty-six miles.", "이오나 곧 아이콜름킬은 멀섬의 끄트머리에 자리 잡고 있는데, 그 섬과는 너비 반 마일의 해협으로 갈라져 있으며, 스코틀랜드 본토로부터의 거리는 36마일이다."]], [["Columba was a native of Ireland, and connected by birth with the princes of the land.", "콜룸바는 아일랜드 태생으로, 출생으로 보아 그 땅의 군주들과 연이 닿아 있었다."], ["Ireland was at that time a land of gospel light, while the western and northern parts of Scotland were still immersed in the darkness of heathenism.", "당시 아일랜드는 복음의 빛이 비치는 땅이었으나, 스코틀랜드의 서부와 북부는 여전히 이교의 어둠에 잠겨 있었다."], ["Columba with twelve friends landed on the island of Iona in the year of our Lord 563, having made the passage in a wicker boat covered with hides.", "콜룸바는 열두 명의 벗과 함께 주후 563년에 이오나섬에 상륙했는데, 가죽을 씌운 버들 배로 그 항해를 마쳤다."], ["The Druids who occupied the island endeavored to prevent his settling there, and the savage nations on the adjoining shores incommoded him with their hostility, and on several occasions endangered his life by their attacks.", "섬을 차지하고 있던 드루이드는 그가 그곳에 자리 잡는 것을 막으려 했고, 인접한 해안의 야만 민족들은 적의를 품고 그를 괴롭혔으며, 여러 차례 그를 공격하여 목숨까지 위태롭게 했다."], ["Yet by his perseverance and zeal he surmounted all opposition, procured from the king a gift of the island, and established there a monastery of which he was the abbot.", "그러나 그는 끈기와 열성으로 모든 반대를 이겨 냈고, 왕으로부터 그 섬을 선물로 얻어 그곳에 수도원을 세우고 그 원장이 되었다."], ["He was unwearied in his labors to disseminate a knowledge of the Scriptures throughout the Highlands and islands of Scotland, and such was the reverence paid him that though not a bishop, but merely a presbyter and monk, the entire province with its bishops was subject to him and his successors.", "그는 스코틀랜드의 고지대와 섬들 전역에 성서의 지식을 퍼뜨리는 일에 지칠 줄 몰랐으며, 그에게 바쳐진 존경이 어찌나 깊었던지, 그가 주교가 아니라 한낱 장로이자 수도사에 지나지 않았음에도 그 지방 전체가 주교들과 더불어 그와 그의 후계자들에게 복속하였다."], ["The Pictish monarch was so impressed with a sense of his wisdom and worth that he held him in the highest honor, and the neighboring chiefs and princes sought his counsel and availed themselves of his judgment in settling their disputes.", "픽트족 군주는 그의 지혜와 인품에 깊이 감명받아 그를 더없이 높이 받들었으며, 인근의 족장과 군주들은 그의 조언을 구하고 분쟁을 가라앉히는 데 그의 판단을 활용했다."]], [["When Columba landed on Iona he was attended by twelve followers whom he had formed into a religious body of which he was the head.", "콜룸바가 이오나에 상륙했을 때 그를 수행한 것은 열두 명의 추종자였으니, 그는 이들을 자신이 우두머리로 있는 하나의 종교 단체로 만들었다."], ["To these, as occasion required, others were from time to time added, so that the original number was always kept up.", "여기에는 필요에 따라 때때로 다른 이들이 더해졌으므로, 본래의 인원수는 늘 유지되었다."], ["Their institution was called a monastery and the superior an abbot, but the system had little in common with the monastic institutions of later times.", "그들의 조직은 수도원이라 불렸고 그 윗사람은 원장이라 불렸으나, 이 체계는 후대의 수도원 제도와 공통된 점이 거의 없었다."], ["The name by which those who submitted to the rule were known was that of Culdees, probably from the Latin “cultores Dei”—worshippers of God.", "그 규율을 따른 자들에게 붙은 이름은 컬디였는데, 아마도 라틴어 〈쿨토레스 데이(cultores Dei)〉 곧 〈신을 숭배하는 자들〉에서 온 듯하다."], ["They were a body of religious persons associated together for the purpose of aiding each other in the common work of preaching the gospel and teaching youth, as well as maintaining in themselves the fervor of devotion by united exercises of worship.", "그들은 복음을 전하고 젊은이를 가르치는 공동의 일에서 서로를 돕고, 또한 함께 예배하는 수행을 통해 자신들 안에 신앙의 열정을 지켜 나가기 위해 함께 모인 종교적 사람들의 무리였다."], ["On entering the order certain vows were taken by the members, but they were not those which were usually imposed by monastic orders, for of these, which are three,—celibacy, poverty, and obedience.—the Culdees were bound to none except the third.", "이 교단에 들어갈 때 단원들은 어떤 서약을 했으나, 그것은 수도회들이 흔히 부과하던 것이 아니었다. 그 서약은 셋, 곧 독신과 청빈, 순종인데, 컬디는 이 가운데 세 번째 것을 제외하고는 어느 것에도 매이지 않았다."], ["To poverty they did not bind themselves; on the contrary they seem to have labored diligently to procure for themselves and those dependent on them the comforts of life.", "청빈에는 스스로를 묶지 않았으니, 도리어 그들은 자신과 자신에게 딸린 이들을 위해 삶의 안락을 마련하고자 부지런히 일했던 것으로 보인다."], ["Marriage also was allowed them, and most of them seem to have entered into that state.", "결혼 또한 그들에게 허락되었으며, 그들 대다수는 그 상태로 들어갔던 것으로 보인다."], ["True, their wives were not permitted to reside with them at the institution, but they had a residence assigned to them in an adjacent locality.", "사실 그들의 아내는 그 시설에서 그들과 함께 거주하는 것이 허락되지 않았으나, 인근의 한 장소에 거처가 배정되었다."], ["Near Iona there is an island which still bears the name of “Eilen nam ban,” women’s island, where their husbands seem to have resided with them, except when duty required their presence in the school or the sanctuary.", "이오나 가까이에는 지금도 〈에일렌 남 반(Eilen nam ban)〉 곧 여인의 섬이라는 이름을 지닌 섬이 있는데, 그들의 남편들은 학교나 성소에 그들의 임무가 요구할 때를 빼고는 그곳에서 아내와 함께 거주했던 것으로 보인다."]], [["Campbell, in his poem of “Reullura,” alludes to the married monks of Iona:", "캠벨은 그의 시 「레울루라」에서 이오나의 기혼 수도사들을 넌지시 가리킨다."]], [["“. .", "\"."], [".", "."], ["The pure Culdees Were Albyn’s earliest priests of God, Ere yet an island of her seas By foot of Saxon monk was trod, Long ere her churchmen by bigotry Were barred from holy wedlock’s tie.", ". 순결한 컬디는 앨빈에서 가장 이른 신의 사제였으니, 아직 그 바다의 어느 섬도 색슨 수도사의 발에 밟히기 전, 그 땅의 성직자들이 편협한 신앙으로 거룩한 혼인의 인연에서 가로막히기 한참 전이었네."], ["’Twas then that Aodh, famed afar, In Iona preached the word with power, And Reullura, beauty’s star, Was the partner of his bower.”", "그때에 멀리까지 이름난 아오드가 이오나에서 권능으로 말씀을 전했고, 아름다움의 별 레울루라가 그의 거처를 함께하는 짝이었네.\""]], [["In one of his “Irish Melodies,”", "무어는 그의 「아일랜드 선율」"], ["Moore gives the legend of St. Senanus and the lady who sought shelter on the island, but was repulsed:", "가운데 하나에서 성 세나누스와, 섬에서 피난처를 구하려다 쫓겨난 여인의 전설을 들려준다."]], [["“O, haste and leave this sacred isle, Unholy bark, ere morning smile; For on thy deck, though dark it be, A female form I see; And I have sworn this sainted sod Shall ne’er by woman’s foot be trod.”", "\"오, 서둘러 이 거룩한 섬을 떠나라, 부정한 배여, 아침이 미소 짓기 전에. 네 갑판 위로, 비록 어둠에 잠겼어도, 여인의 모습이 내게 보이는구나. 그리고 나는 맹세했노라, 이 성스러운 흙은 결코 여인의 발에 밟히지 않으리라고.\""]], [["In these respects and in others the Culdees departed from the established rules of the Romish church, and consequently were deemed heretical.", "이런 점들에서, 또 다른 점들에서 컬디는 로마 교회의 확립된 규율에서 벗어났으며, 그리하여 이단으로 여겨졌다."], ["The consequence was that as the power of the latter advanced that of the Culdees was enfeebled.", "그 결과 로마 교회의 세력이 자라남에 따라 컬디의 세력은 약해졌다."], ["It was not, however, till the thirteenth century that the communities of the Culdees were suppressed and the members dispersed.", "그러나 컬디의 공동체가 억압되고 단원들이 흩어진 것은 13세기에 이르러서였다."], ["They still continued to labor as individuals, and resisted the inroads of Papal usurpation as they best might till the light of the Reformation dawned on the world.", "그들은 그 뒤로도 개인으로서 계속 일했으며, 종교개혁의 빛이 세상에 동틀 때까지 교황권의 침탈에 맞서 할 수 있는 한 저항했다."]], [["Iona, from its position in the western seas, was exposed to the assaults of the Norwegian and Danish rovers by whom those seas were infested, and by them it was repeatedly pillaged, its dwellings burned, and its peaceful inhabitants put to the sword.", "이오나는 서쪽 바다에 자리 잡은 까닭에, 그 바다를 들끓게 하던 노르웨이와 덴마크 약탈자들의 습격에 노출되었으며, 그들에게 거듭 약탈당하고 거처가 불태워졌으며 평화로운 주민들이 칼날에 쓰러졌다."], ["These unfavorable circumstances led to its gradual decline, which was expedited by the subversion of the Culdees throughout Scotland.", "이런 불리한 상황은 그 섬의 점진적인 쇠락을 불러왔고, 그 쇠락은 스코틀랜드 전역에 걸친 컬디의 전복으로 한층 빨라졌다."], ["Under the reign of Popery the island became the seat of a nunnery, the ruins of which are still seen.", "교황 지배의 시대에 그 섬은 수녀원의 본거지가 되었는데, 그 폐허는 지금도 볼 수 있다."], ["At the Reformation, the nuns were allowed to remain, living in community, when the abbey was dismantled.", "종교개혁 때, 수도원이 헐릴 적에 수녀들은 공동체를 이루어 살며 머물도록 허락받았다."]], [["Iona is now chiefly resorted to by travellers on account of the numerous ecclesiastical and sepulchral remains which are found upon it.", "이오나는 오늘날 그 위에서 발견되는 수많은 교회와 무덤 유적 덕분에 주로 여행자들이 찾는 곳이 되었다."], ["The principal of these are the Cathedral or Abbey Church and the Chapel of the Nunnery.", "그 가운데 으뜸가는 것은 대성당 곧 수도원 교회와 수녀원의 부속 예배당이다."], ["Besides these remains of ecclesiastical antiquity, there are some of an earlier date, and pointing to the existence on the island of forms of worship and belief different from those of Christianity.", "이러한 교회 고대 유물 말고도 더 이른 시기의 것들이 있으니, 그 섬에 그리스도교와는 다른 예배와 신앙의 형태가 존재했음을 가리킨다."], ["These are the circular Cairns which are found in various parts, and which seem to have been of Druidical origin.", "이것들은 여러 곳에서 발견되는 둥근 케언으로, 드루이드에서 비롯된 것으로 보인다."], ["It is in reference to all these remains of ancient religion that Johnson exclaims, “That man is little to be envied whose patriotism would not gain force upon the plains of Marathon, or whose piety would not grow warmer amid the ruins of Iona.”", "존슨이 \"마라톤 평원에서 애국심이 더 큰 힘을 얻지 못할 사람, 혹은 이오나의 폐허 가운데서 경건함이 더 뜨거워지지 못할 사람은 부러워할 것이 거의 없는 자다\"라고 외친 것은 바로 이 고대 종교의 온갖 유물을 두고 한 말이었다."]], [["In the “Lord of the Isles” Scott beautifully contrasts the church on Iona with the cave of Staffa, opposite:", "스콧은 「섬들의 군주」에서 이오나의 교회를, 그 맞은편에 있는 스타파의 동굴과 아름답게 견주어 보인다."]], [["“Nature herself, it seemed, would raise A minister to her Maker’s praise!", "\"자연이 친히, 그리 보였으니, 세우려 했네 그 창조주를 기리는 한 성소를! 더 하찮은 쓰임을 위해서가 아니라네, 그 기둥들이 솟구치고 그 아치들이 휘어진 것은."], ["Not for a meaner use ascend Her columns, or her arches bend; Nor of a theme less solemn tells That mighty surge that ebbs and swells, And still between each awful pause, From the high vault an answer draws, In varied tone, prolonged and high, That mocks the organ’s melody; Nor doth its entrance front in vain To old Iona’s holy fane, That Nature’s voice might seem to say, Well hast thou done, frail child of clay!", "덜 엄숙한 주제를 노래함도 아니라네, 썰물과 밀물로 굽이치는 저 거대한 물결이. 그 무서운 멈춤 사이마다 여전히, 높은 천장으로부터 응답을 끌어내니, 가지가지 가락으로, 길게 끌며 드높이, 오르간의 선율을 무색하게 한다네."], ["Thy humble powers that stately shrine Tasked high and hard—but witness mine!”", "그 입구가 옛 이오나의 거룩한 신전을 마주 보고 있음도 헛되지 않으니, 자연의 목소리가 이렇게 말하는 듯하네, 너는 참으로 잘 해냈구나, 진흙으로 빚어진 가녀린 자식이여! 네 보잘것없는 힘이 저 당당한 성소를 높고 어렵게 부려 냈도다 — 허나 내 것을 보라!\""]], [["ON the decline of the Roman power, about five centuries after Christ, the countries of Northern Europe were left almost destitute of a national government.", "로마의 세력이 쇠퇴하던 무렵, 그러니까 그리스도 이후 약 5세기경에, 북부 유럽의 여러 나라는 국가적 통치 체제를 거의 갖추지 못한 상태로 남게 되었다. 크고 작은 권세를 지닌 수많은 우두머리들이, 각자가 자신의 지배권을 강제할 수 있는 범위 안에서 지역적 통치를 행했고, 이따금 그 우두머리들이 공통의 목적을 위해 결속하기도 했다."], ["Numerous chiefs, more or less powerful, held local sway, as far as each could enforce his dominion, and occasionally those chiefs would unite for a common object; but, in ordinary times, they were much more likely to be found in hostility to one another.", "그러나 평상시에는 서로 적대하는 모습을 훨씬 더 자주 보였다. 이러한 상황에서 사회의 하층 계급이 누려야 할 권리는 모든 공격자의 처분에 내맡겨져 있었으니, 우두머리들의 무법적 권세에 대한 어떤 제어가 없었다면 사회가 야만 상태로 다시 떨어졌으리라는 점은 분명하다."], ["In such a state of things the rights of the humbler classes of society were at the mercy of every assailant; and it is plain that, without some check upon the lawless power of the chiefs, society must have relapsed into barbarism.", "그러한 제어는, 첫째로 우두머리들 자신의 경쟁 관계에서 찾을 수 있었으니, 서로에 대한 시기심이 그들로 하여금 서로를 억제하게 했다. 둘째로는 교회의 영향에서 찾을 수 있었으니, 교회는 순수한 동기에서든 이기적인 동기에서든 온갖 명분을 내세워 약자의 보호를 위해 개입하기로 서약한 바였다."], ["Such checks were found, first, in the rivalry of the chiefs themselves, whose mutual jealousy made them restraints upon one another; secondly, in the influence of the Church, which, by every motive, pure or selfish, was pledged to interpose for the protection of the weak; and lastly, in the generosity and sense of right which, however crushed under the weight of passion and selfishness, dwell naturally in the heart of man.", "마지막으로는, 격정과 이기심의 무게에 짓눌려 있을지언정 인간의 마음속에 본래 깃들어 있는 관대함과 옳음에 대한 감각에서 찾을 수 있었다. 바로 이 마지막 원천에서 기사도가 솟아났으니, 그것은 영웅적 인격의 이상을 빚어내었다."], ["From this last source sprang Chivalry, which framed an ideal of the heroic character, combining invincible strength and valor, justice, modesty, loyalty to superiors, courtesy to equals, compassion to weakness, and devotedness to the Church; an ideal which, if never met with in real life, was acknowledged by all as the highest model for emulation.", "그 이상은 무적의 힘과 용맹, 정의, 겸손, 윗사람에 대한 충성, 동등한 이에 대한 예의, 약자에 대한 연민, 그리고 교회에 대한 헌신을 한데 아우른 것이었다. 그것은 비록 실제 삶에서는 결코 마주칠 수 없었을지라도, 모두가 본받아야 할 최고의 본보기로 인정하던 이상이었다."]], [["The word “Chivalry” is derived from the French “cheval,”", "\"기사도\"라는 말은 프랑스어로 말[馬]을 뜻하는 슈발(cheval)에서 유래했다."], ["a horse. The word “knight,”", "본래는 소년이나 시종을 뜻하던 \"기사(knight)\"라는 말은, 무기를 지닐 특권을 인정받은 젊은이에게 특별히 적용되었다."], ["which originally meant boy or servant, was particularly applied to a young man after he was admitted to the privilege of bearing arms.", "이 특권은 오로지 가문과 재산을 갖춘 젊은이에게만 주어졌으니, 일반 대중은 무기를 갖추지 못했기 때문이다."], ["This privilege was conferred on youths of family and fortune only, for the mass of the people were not furnished with arms.", "따라서 기사란 말을 탄 전사요, 신분이 있는 자이거나 어떤 신분 있는 자를 섬기고 그 부양을 받는 자였다."], ["The knight then was a mounted warrior, a man of rank, or in the service and maintenance of some man of rank, generally possessing some independent means of support, but often relying mainly on the gratitude of those whom he served for the supply of his wants, and often, no doubt, resorting to the means which power confers on its possessor.", "대개는 독립적인 생계 수단을 어느 정도 갖추고 있었으나, 흔히 자신이 섬기는 자들의 감사에 주로 의존하여 필요한 것을 충당했고, 또한 분명 권세가 그 소유자에게 부여하는 수단에 의지하는 일도 잦았다."]], [["In time of war the knight was, with his followers, in the camp of his sovereign, or commanding in the field, or holding some castle for him.", "전시에는 기사가 자기 추종자들과 함께 군주의 진영에 머물거나, 전장에서 지휘하거나, 군주를 위해 어떤 성을 지켰다."], ["In time of peace he was often in attendance at his sovereign’s court, gracing with his presence the banquets and tournaments with which princes cheered their leisure.", "평화로운 시기에는 흔히 군주의 궁정에 시중을 들며, 군주들이 여가를 즐기던 연회와 마상 시합에 자신의 존재로 빛을 더했다. 혹은 모험을 찾아 온 나라를 누비며, 잘못을 바로잡고 권리를 지키는 일에 공공연히 헌신했고, 때로는 신앙이나 사랑의 어떤 서약을 이행하기 위함이었다."], ["Or he was traversing the country in quest of adventure, professedly bent on redressing wrongs and enforcing rights, sometimes in fulfilment of some vow of religion or of love.", "이렇게 떠돌아다니는 기사들을 편력 기사라 불렀다. 그들은 귀족의 성에서 환영받는 손님이었으니, 그들의 존재가 그 외딴 거처의 따분함을 활기차게 해 주었기 때문이다."], ["These wandering knights were called knights-errant; they were welcome guests in the castles of the nobility, for their presence enlivened the dulness of those secluded abodes, and they were received with honor at the abbeys, which often owed the best part of their revenues to the patronage of the knights; but if no castle or abbey or hermitage were at hand their hardy habits made it not intolerable to them to lie down, supperless, at the foot of some wayside cross, and pass the night.", "또한 그들은 수도원에서도 존경으로 맞이되었으니, 수도원은 흔히 그 수입의 가장 좋은 부분을 기사들의 후원에 빚지고 있었다. 그러나 가까이에 성도 수도원도 은둔처도 없을 때면, 그들의 굳센 습관 덕분에 어느 길가 십자가 발치에 저녁도 못 먹은 채 누워 밤을 지새우는 일도 견디지 못할 바가 아니었다."]], [["It is evident that the justice administered by such an instrumentality must have been of the rudest description.", "이러한 수단으로 집행되는 정의가 가장 거친 종류일 수밖에 없었으리라는 점은 명백하다."], ["The force whose legitimate purpose was to redress wrongs might easily be perverted to inflict them.", "잘못을 바로잡는 것을 정당한 목적으로 삼던 힘이, 잘못을 저지르는 데 손쉽게 변질될 수도 있었던 것이다."], ["Accordingly, we find in the romances, which, however fabulous in facts, are true as pictures of manners, that a knightly castle was often a terror to the surrounding country; that is, dungeons were full of oppressed knights and ladies, waiting for some champion to appear to set them free, or to be ransomed with money; that hosts of idle retainers were ever at hand to enforce their lord’s behests, regardless of law and justice; and that the rights of the unarmed multitude were of no account.", "그리하여 우리는 로맨스들에서, 비록 사실에 있어서는 허황될지언정 풍속의 묘사로서는 진실한 그 이야기들에서, 한 기사의 성이 흔히 주변 지방의 공포의 대상이었음을 보게 된다. 다시 말해, 지하 감옥에는 억압받는 기사와 귀부인들이 가득하여 자신들을 풀어 줄 어떤 투사가 나타나기를, 혹은 돈으로 몸값이 치러지기를 기다리고 있었으며, 한 무리의 빈둥거리는 가신들이 법과 정의를 아랑곳하지 않고 영주의 명령을 강제하려 늘 곁에 대기하고 있었고, 무장하지 않은 다수의 권리는 아무런 가치도 없었다."], ["This contrariety of fact and theory in regard to chivalry will account for the opposite impressions which exist in men’s minds respecting it.", "기사도에 관한 이러한 사실과 이론의 모순은, 그것을 두고 사람들의 마음속에 존재하는 상반된 인상을 설명해 줄 것이다."], ["While it has been the theme of the most fervid eulogium on the one part, it has been as eagerly denounced on the other.", "한편에서는 더없이 뜨거운 찬사의 주제가 되어 왔으나, 다른 한편에서는 그에 못지않게 열렬히 비난받아 왔다."], ["On a cool estimate, we cannot but see reason to congratulate ourselves that it has given way in modern times to the reign of law, and that the civil magistrate, if less picturesque, has taken the place of the mailed champion.", "냉정하게 따져 보면, 근대에 이르러 기사도가 법의 지배에 자리를 내주었다는 것, 그리고 비록 덜 그림 같을지라도 갑옷 입은 투사 대신 민간의 행정관이 그 자리를 차지했다는 것을 우리는 스스로 다행으로 여기지 않을 수 없다."]], [["# THE TRAINING OF A KNIGHT", "# THE TRAINING OF A KNIGHT"]], [["The preparatory education of candidates for knighthood was long and arduous.", "기사 후보자들의 예비 교육은 길고도 고된 것이었다."], ["At seven years of age the noble children were usually removed from their father’s house to the court or castle of their future patron, and placed under the care of a governor, who taught them the first articles of religion, and respect and reverence for their lords and superiors, and initiated them in the ceremonies of a court.", "일곱 살이 되면 귀족의 자제들은 대개 아버지의 집을 떠나 장차 자기를 후원할 사람의 궁정이나 성으로 옮겨져, 한 교육관의 보살핌 아래 맡겨졌다. 교육관은 그들에게 신앙의 첫 조항들과 영주와 윗사람에 대한 존경과 경의를 가르쳤고, 궁정의 예식에 입문시켰다."], ["They were called pages, valets, or varlets, and their office was to carve, to wait at table, and to perform other menial services, which were not then considered humiliating.", "그들은 시동(侍童), 시종, 또는 종자(從者)라 불렸으며, 그 임무는 고기를 썰고, 식탁에서 시중을 들고, 그 밖의 천한 일들을 수행하는 것이었으나, 당시에는 그것이 굴욕적인 일로 여겨지지 않았다."], ["In their leisure hours they learned to dance and play on the harp, were instructed in the mysteries of woods and rivers, that is, in hunting, falconry, and fishing, and in wrestling, tilting with spears, and performing other military exercises on horseback.", "여가 시간에는 춤추는 법과 하프 켜는 법을 배웠고, \"숲\"과 \"강\"의 비법, 곧 사냥과 매사냥과 낚시를 익혔으며, 또한 씨름과 창 겨루기, 그리고 말 위에서 행하는 그 밖의 군사 훈련을 배웠다."], ["At fourteen the page became an esquire, and began a course of severer and more laborious exercises.", "열네 살이 되면 시동은 종자(esquire)가 되어, 더 혹독하고 더 고된 훈련의 과정에 들어섰다."], ["To vault on a horse in heavy armor; to run, to scale walls, and spring over ditches, under the same encumbrance; to wrestle, to wield the battle-axe for a length of time, without raising the visor or taking breath; to perform with grace all the evolutions of horsemanship,—were necessary preliminaries to the reception of knighthood, which was usually conferred at twenty-one years of age, when the young man’s education was supposed to be completed.", "무거운 갑옷을 입은 채 말 위에 뛰어오르는 일, 같은 짐을 진 채로 달리고 성벽을 기어오르고 도랑을 뛰어넘는 일, 면갑을 들어 올리거나 숨을 돌리지 않고 오랫동안 씨름하고 전투용 도끼를 휘두르는 일, 그리고 마술(馬術)의 온갖 동작을 우아하게 해내는 일, 이런 것들이 기사 작위를 받기 위한 필수적인 예비 과정이었다. 기사 작위는 대개 스물한 살에 수여되었으니, 그 무렵이면 젊은이의 교육이 완성된 것으로 여겨졌다."], ["In the meantime, the esquires were no less assiduously engaged in acquiring all those refinements of civility which formed what was in that age called courtesy.", "그동안 종자들은 그 시대에 \"예의\"라 불리던 것을 이루는 온갖 세련된 교양을 익히는 데에도 그에 못지않게 부지런히 힘썼다."], ["The same castle in which they received their education was usually thronged with young persons of the other sex, and the page was encouraged, at a very early age, to select some lady of the court as the mistress of his heart, to whom he was taught to refer all his sentiments, words, and actions.", "그들이 교육받던 바로 그 성에는 대개 이성(異性)의 젊은이들도 가득했으니, 시동은 아주 어린 나이부터 궁정의 어떤 귀부인을 자기 마음의 연인으로 고르도록 장려되었고, 자신의 모든 감정과 말과 행동을 그녀에게 바치도록 가르침을 받았다."], ["The service of his mistress was the glory and occupation of a knight, and her smiles, bestowed at once by affection and gratitude, were held out as the recompense of his well-directed valor.", "자기 연인을 섬기는 일이 기사의 영광이요 소임이었으며, 애정과 감사에서 동시에 베풀어지는 그녀의 미소가 그의 올바로 발휘된 용맹에 대한 보상으로 제시되었다."], ["Religion united its influence with those of loyalty and love, and the order of knighthood, endowed with all the sanctity and religious awe that attended the priesthood, became an object of ambition to the greatest sovereigns.", "종교는 충성과 사랑의 영향에 그 영향을 더했고, 사제직에 따르던 온갖 신성함과 종교적 외경을 갖춘 기사단은 가장 위대한 군주들에게도 야망의 대상이 되었다."]], [["The ceremonies of initiation were peculiarly solemn.", "입회 의식은 유난히 엄숙했다."], ["After undergoing a severe fast, and spending whole nights in prayer, the candidate confessed, and received the sacrament.", "혹독한 단식을 견디고 여러 날 밤을 기도로 지새운 뒤, 후보자는 고해하고 성찬을 받았다."], ["He then clothed himself in snow-white garments, and repaired to the church, or the hall, where the ceremony was to take place, bearing a knightly sword suspended from his neck, which the officiating priest took and blessed, and then returned to him.", "그런 다음 눈처럼 흰 옷을 입고, 의식이 열릴 교회나 큰 방으로 나아갔는데, 그때 목에 기사의 검을 매달고 있었으니, 집전하는 사제가 그것을 받아 축복한 뒤 그에게 다시 돌려주었다."], ["The candidate then, with folded arms, knelt before the presiding knight, who, after some questions about his motives and purposes in requesting admission, administered to him the oaths, and granted his request.", "이어 후보자는 팔짱을 끼고 주관하는 기사 앞에 무릎을 꿇었으며, 그 기사는 입회를 청하는 동기와 목적에 관해 몇 가지를 물은 뒤 그에게 서약을 시키고 그의 청을 허락했다."], ["Some of the knights present, sometimes even ladies and damsels, handed to him in succession the spurs, the coat of mail, the hauberk, the armlet and gauntlet, and lastly he girded on the sword.", "그 자리에 있던 몇몇 기사들이, 때로는 귀부인과 처녀들까지도, 그에게 차례로 박차와 사슬 갑옷, 호버크, 팔 보호구와 손갑(手甲)을 건넸으며, 마지막으로 그는 검을 허리에 찼다."], ["He then knelt again before the president, who, rising from his seat, gave him the “accolade,” which consisted of three strokes, with the flat of a sword, on the shoulder or neck of the candidate, accompanied by the words: “In the name of God, of St.", "그런 다음 그는 다시 주관자 앞에 무릎을 꿇었고, 주관자는 자리에서 일어나 그에게 \"어콜레이드\"를 베풀었으니, 그것은 검의 평평한 면으로 후보자의 어깨나 목을 세 번 두드리는 것으로, 다음과 같은 말이 곁들여졌다."], ["Michael, and St. George, I make thee a knight; be valiant, courteous, and loyal!”", "\"하느님과 성 미카엘과 성 게오르기우스의 이름으로, 그대를 기사로 삼노라."], ["Then he received his helmet, his shield, and spear; and thus the investiture ended.", "용맹하고, 예의 바르며, 충성스러울지어다!\" 그런 다음 그는 투구와 방패와 창을 받았으니, 이로써 작위 수여가 끝났다."]], [["# FREEMEN, VILLAINS, SERFS, AND CLERKS", "# FREEMEN, VILLAINS, SERFS, AND CLERKS"]], [["The other classes of which society was composed were, first, freemen, owners of small portions of land independent, though they sometimes voluntarily became the vassals of their more opulent neighbors, whose power was necessary for their protection.", "사회를 이루던 다른 계급들로는, 첫째 자유민이 있었으니, 이들은 작은 토지를 소유한 독립적인 사람들이었다. 다만 이들은 때때로 자발적으로 더 부유한 이웃의 봉신이 되기도 했으니, 그 이웃의 권세가 자신들의 보호에 필요했기 때문이다."], ["The other two classes, which were much the most numerous, were either serfs or villains, both of which were slaves.", "다른 두 계급은 훨씬 더 수가 많았는데, 농노이거나 예농(隸農)이었으며, 둘 다 노예였다."]], [["The serfs were in the lowest state of slavery.", "농노는 노예 상태의 가장 낮은 단계에 있었다."], ["All the fruits of their labor belonged to the master whose land they tilled, and by whom they were fed and clothed.", "그들의 노동의 모든 결실은, 그들이 경작하던 땅의 주인에게 속했으니, 주인이 그들을 먹이고 입혔다."]], [["The villains were less degraded.", "예농은 덜 비참했다."], ["Their situation seems to have resembled that of the Russian peasants at this day.", "그들의 처지는 오늘날 러시아 농민의 처지와 비슷했던 것으로 보인다."], ["Like the serfs, they were attached to the soil, and were transferred with it by purchase; but they paid only a fixed rent to the landlord, and had a right to dispose of any surplus that might arise from their industry.", "농노와 마찬가지로 그들도 토지에 매여 있었고, 토지가 매매될 때 함께 넘겨졌다. 그러나 그들은 지주에게 정해진 소작료만 냈고, 자신들의 근면에서 생겨난 잉여가 있으면 그것을 처분할 권리가 있었다."]], [["The term “clerk” was of very extensive import.", "\"성직자(clerk)\"라는 말은 매우 넓은 의미를 지녔다."], ["It comprehended, originally, such persons only as belonged to the clergy, or clerical order, among whom, however, might be found a multitude of married persons, artisans or others.", "본래는 성직자, 곧 성직 계급에 속한 사람들만을 가리켰으나, 그 가운데에는 기혼자와 장인(匠人)을 비롯한 여러 사람이 많이 섞여 있을 수 있었다."], ["But in process of time a much wider rule was established; every one that could read being accounted a clerk or clericus, and allowed the “benefit of clergy,” that is, exemption from capital and some other forms of punishment, in case of crime.", "그러나 시간이 흐르면서 훨씬 더 폭넓은 규칙이 자리 잡아, 글을 읽을 수 있는 사람은 누구나 성직자(clericus)로 간주되어 \"성직자의 특전\"을, 곧 죄를 지었을 경우 사형과 그 밖의 몇몇 형벌을 면제받는 혜택을 누리게 되었다."]], [["The splendid pageant of a tournament between knights, its gaudy accessories and trappings, and its chivalrous regulations, originated in France.", "기사들 사이의 마상 시합이라는 화려한 구경거리, 그 현란한 부속물과 장식, 그리고 그 기사도적인 규칙은 프랑스에서 비롯되었다."], ["Tournaments were repeatedly condemned by the Church, probably on account of the quarrels they led to, and the often fatal results.", "마상 시합은 교회로부터 거듭 비난받았는데, 아마도 그것이 불러일으키던 다툼과 흔히 목숨까지 잃게 되는 결과 때문이었을 것이다."], ["The “joust,” or “just,”", "\"주스트(joust)\""], ["was different from the tournament.", "곧 마상 창 시합은 마상 시합과는 달랐다."], ["In these, knights fought with their lances, and their object was to unhorse their antagonists; while the tournaments were intended for a display of skill and address in evolutions, and with various weapons, and greater courtesy was observed in the regulations.", "주스트에서는 기사들이 창을 들고 싸웠고, 그 목적은 상대를 말에서 떨어뜨리는 것이었다. 반면 마상 시합은 여러 동작과 갖가지 무기를 다루는 기량과 솜씨를 펼쳐 보이기 위한 것이었고, 그 규칙에서는 더 큰 예의가 지켜졌다."], ["By these it was forbidden to wound the horse, or to use the point of the sword, or to strike a knight after he had raised his vizor, or unlaced his helmet.", "이 규칙에 따라 말을 다치게 하거나, 검의 끝을 쓰거나, 기사가 면갑을 들어 올리거나 투구 끈을 풀어헤친 뒤에 그를 치는 것은 금지되었다."], ["The ladies encouraged their knights in these exercises; they bestowed prizes, and the conqueror’s feats were the theme of romance and song.", "귀부인들은 이런 시합에서 자기 기사들을 격려했으니, 그들이 상을 내렸고, 승자의 무용은 로맨스와 노래의 주제가 되었다."], ["The stands overlooking the ground, of course, were varied in the shapes of towers, terraces, galleries, and pensile gardens, magnificently decorated with tapestry, pavilions, and banners.", "경기장을 내려다보는 관람석은 물론 탑, 테라스, 회랑, 매달린 정원 따위의 갖가지 모양으로 다양했으며, 융단과 천막과 깃발로 화려하게 꾸며져 있었다."], ["Every combatant proclaimed the name of the lady whose servant d’amour he was.", "모든 출전자는 자신이 사랑의 종복으로 섬기는 귀부인의 이름을 외쳤다."], ["He was wont to look up to the stand, and strengthen his courage by the sight of the bright eyes that were raining their influence on him from above.", "그는 으레 관람석을 올려다보며, 위에서 그에게 영향을 비처럼 쏟아붓는 빛나는 눈동자를 바라봄으로써 자신의 용기를 북돋우곤 했다."], ["The knights also carried favors, consisting of scarfs, veils, sleeves, bracelets, clasps,—in short, some piece of female habiliment,—attached to their helmets, shields, or armor.", "기사들은 또한 \"정표\"를 지녔으니, 그것은 스카프, 베일, 소매, 팔찌, 죔쇠 따위, 한마디로 여성 복식의 어떤 조각이었으며, 자신의 투구나 방패나 갑옷에 매달려 있었다."], ["If, during the combat, any of these appendages were dropped or lost the fair donor would at times send her knight new ones, especially if pleased with his exertions.", "만약 싸움 도중에 이 부착물 가운데 어느 것이 떨어지거나 잃어버려지면, 그 아름다운 증여자는 때때로 자기 기사에게 새것을 보내 주었으니, 특히 그의 분투에 흡족했을 때에 그러했다."]], [["# MAIL ARMOR", "# MAIL ARMOR"]], [["Mail armor, of which the hauberk is a species, and which derived its name from maille, a French word for mesh, was of two kinds, plate or scale mail, and chain mail.", "사슬 갑옷은, 호버크가 그 한 종류이며 그물코를 뜻하는 프랑스어 마유(maille)에서 그 이름이 나왔는데, 두 종류가 있었으니 판금 또는 비늘 갑옷과 사슬 갑옷이었다."], ["It was originally used for the protection of the body only, reaching no lower than the knees.", "본래는 몸통만 보호하기 위해 쓰였으며 무릎 아래로는 내려오지 않았다."], ["It was shaped like a carter’s frock, and bound round the waist by a girdle.", "짐수레꾼의 작업복 같은 모양이었고, 허리에 띠를 둘러 졸라맸다."], ["Gloves and hose of mail were afterwards added, and a hood, which, when necessary, was drawn over the head, leaving the face alone uncovered.", "사슬로 된 장갑과 각반이 나중에 더해졌고, 두건도 더해졌으니, 필요할 때면 머리 위로 끌어 올려 얼굴만 드러내었다."], ["To protect the skin from the impression of the iron network of the chain mail, a quilted lining was employed, which, however, was insufficient, and the bath was used to efface the marks of the armor.", "사슬 갑옷의 쇠 그물이 피부에 자국을 남기지 않도록 누비 안감을 댔으나, 그것으로는 충분치 않아 갑옷이 남긴 자국을 지우기 위해 목욕을 했다."]], [["The hauberk was a complete covering of double chain mail.", "호버크는 이중 사슬 갑옷으로 온몸을 완전히 덮는 것이었다."], ["Some hauberks opened before, like a modern coat; others were closed like a shirt.", "어떤 호버크는 현대의 외투처럼 앞이 트여 있었고, 어떤 것은 셔츠처럼 막혀 있었다."]], [["The chain mail of which they were composed was formed by a number of iron links, each link having others inserted into it, the whole exhibiting a kind of network, of which (in some instances at least) the meshes were circular, with each link separately riveted.", "그것을 이루던 사슬 갑옷은 수많은 쇠고리로 만들어졌는데, 각각의 고리에 다른 고리들이 끼워져, 전체가 일종의 그물 모양을 이루었으며, (적어도 어떤 경우에는) 그 그물코가 둥글었고 고리 하나하나가 따로 대갈못으로 박혀 있었다."]], [["The hauberk was proof against the most violent blow of a sword; but the point of a lance might pass through the meshes, or drive the iron into the flesh.", "호버크는 검의 가장 격렬한 일격도 막아 내었다. 그러나 창끝은 그물코를 뚫고 들어가거나 쇠를 살 속으로 박아 넣을 수도 있었다."], ["To guard against this, a thick and well-stuffed doublet was worn underneath, under which was commonly added an iron breastplate.", "이를 막기 위해 그 아래에 두껍고 속을 잘 채운 더블릿을 입었으며, 그 안에는 흔히 쇠 가슴받이를 더 덧대었다."], ["Hence the expression “to pierce both plate and mail,” so common in the earlier poets.", "그래서 초기 시인들에게 그토록 흔한 \"판금과 사슬을 둘 다 꿰뚫는다\"는 표현이 생겨난 것이다."]], [["Mail armor continued in general use till about the year 1300, when it was gradually supplanted by plate armor, or suits consisting of pieces or plates of solid iron, adapted to the different parts of the body.", "사슬 갑옷은 1300년 무렵까지 널리 쓰이다가, 점차 판금 갑옷에 자리를 내주었으니, 판금 갑옷이란 단단한 쇳조각이나 판으로 이루어져 몸의 여러 부위에 맞춘 갑주였다."]], [["Shields were generally made of wood, covered with leather, or some similar substance.", "방패는 대개 나무로 만들어 가죽이나 그와 비슷한 재료로 덮었다."], ["To secure them, in some sort, from being cut through by the sword, they were surrounded with a hoop of metal.", "검에 베여 잘리는 것을 어느 정도 막기 위해, 방패 둘레에 금속 테를 둘렀다."]], [["The helmet was composed of two parts: the headpiece, which was strengthened within by several circles of iron, and the visor, which, as the name implies, was a sort of grating to see through, so contrived as, by sliding in a groove, or turning on a pivot, to be raised or lowered at pleasure.", "투구는 두 부분으로 이루어졌다. 하나는 머리통을 덮는 부분으로, 안쪽을 여러 개의 쇠 고리로 보강했다."], ["Some helmets had a further improvement called a bever, from the Italian bevere, to drink.", "다른 하나는 면갑으로, 그 이름이 뜻하듯 들여다볼 수 있는 일종의 살창이었으며, 홈을 따라 미끄러지거나 굴대를 중심으로 돌려서 마음대로 올리고 내릴 수 있도록 고안되었다. 어떤 투구에는 \"비버(bever)\"라 불리는 한 가지 개량 장치가 더 있었으니, 그 이름은 마신다는 뜻의 이탈리아어 베베레(bevere)에서 나왔다."], ["The ventayle, or “air-passage,” is another name for this.", "\"공기 통로\"를 뜻하는 벤테일(ventayle)은 이것의 또 다른 이름이다."]], [["To secure the helmet from the possibility of falling, or of being struck off, it was tied by several laces to the meshes of the hauberk; consequently, when a knight was overthrown it was necessary to undo these laces before he could be put to death; though this was sometimes effected by lifting up the skirt of the hauberk, and stabbing him in the belly.", "투구가 떨어지거나 쳐서 벗겨질 가능성을 막기 위해, 그것을 여러 가닥의 끈으로 호버크의 그물코에 묶어 두었다. 따라서 한 기사가 쓰러지면, 그를 죽이기에 앞서 이 끈들을 풀어야 했다."], ["The instrument of death was a small dagger, worn on the right side.", "다만 때로는 호버크 자락을 들어 올려 배를 찌름으로써 죽이기도 했다. 죽음에 쓰던 도구는 오른쪽 옆구리에 차던 작은 단검이었다."]], [["In ages when there were no books, when noblemen and princes themselves could not read, history or tradition was monopolized by the story-tellers.", "책이 없던 시대, 귀족과 군주들 자신조차 글을 읽지 못하던 시대에는, 역사나 전승이 이야기꾼들에게 독점되어 있었다."], ["They inherited, generation after generation, the wondrous tales of their predecessors, which they retailed to the public with such additions of their own as their acquired information supplied them with.", "그들은 선조들의 경이로운 이야기들을 대대로 물려받아, 자신이 얻은 정보가 보태 주는 만큼 거기에 자기 나름의 보탬을 더하여 사람들에게 전했다."], ["Anachronisms became of course very common, and errors of geography, of locality, of manners, equally so.", "시대착오는 물론 매우 흔해졌고, 지리와 장소와 풍속의 오류도 마찬가지로 흔해졌다."], ["Spurious genealogies were invented, in which Arthur and his knights, and Charlemagne and his paladins, were made to derive their descent from Æneas, Hector, or some other of the Trojan heroes.", "가짜 족보가 지어내어져, 아서 왕과 그의 기사들, 그리고 샤를마뉴와 그의 용장들이 아이네아스나 헥토르, 혹은 그 밖의 어떤 트로이아 영웅의 후손으로 만들어졌다."]], [["With regard to the derivation of the word “Romance,” we trace it to the fact that the dialects which were formed in Western Europe, from the admixture of Latin with the native languages, took the name of Langue Romaine.", "\"로맨스\"라는 말의 유래에 관해서는, 라틴어와 토착 언어가 뒤섞여 서유럽에서 형성된 방언들이 랑그 로멘(Langue Romaine)이라는 이름을 얻었다는 사실로 거슬러 올라가게 된다."], ["The French language was divided into two dialects.", "프랑스어는 두 방언으로 나뉘었다."], ["The river Loire was their common boundary.", "루아르 강이 그 공통의 경계였다."], ["In the provinces to the south of that river the affirmative, yes, was expressed by the word oc; in the north it was called oil (oui); and hence Dante has named the southern language langue d’oc, and the northern langue d’oil.", "그 강 남쪽의 지방들에서는 긍정의 \"예\"를 오크(oc)라는 말로 나타냈고, 북쪽에서는 그것을 오일(oil, 곧 oui)이라 했다. 그래서 단테는 남쪽 언어를 랑그 도크(langue d’oc)라, 북쪽 언어를 랑그 도일(langue d’oil)이라 이름 붙였다."], ["The latter, which was carried into England by the Normans, and is the origin of the present French, may be called the French Romane; and the former the Provençal, or Provencial Romane, because it was spoken by the people of Provence and Languedoc, southern provinces of France.", "노르만인들에 의해 잉글랜드로 들어와 오늘날의 프랑스어의 기원이 된 후자는 프랑스 로만어라 부를 수 있고, 전자는 프로방스 로만어라 부를 수 있으니, 그것은 프랑스 남부 지방인 프로방스와 랑그도크의 사람들이 쓰던 말이었기 때문이다."]], [["These dialects were soon distinguished by very opposite characters.", "이 방언들은 곧 서로 매우 상반된 성격으로 구별되었다."], ["A soft and enervating climate, a spirit of commerce encouraged by an easy communication with other maritime nations, the influx of wealth, and a more settled government, may have tended to polish and soften the diction of the Provencials, whose poets, under the name of Troubadours, were the masters of the Italians, and particularly of Petrarch.", "부드럽고 사람을 나른하게 하는 기후, 다른 해양 국가들과의 손쉬운 교류로 장려된 상업 정신, 부의 유입, 그리고 더 안정된 통치는, 프로방스 사람들의 어법을 다듬고 부드럽게 하는 데 이바지했을 것이다. 그들의 시인들은 트루바두르라는 이름으로 이탈리아 시인들의, 특히 페트라르카의 스승이 되었다."], ["Their favorite pieces were Sirventes (satirical pieces), love-songs, and Ténsons, which last were a sort of dialogue in verse between two poets, who questioned each other on some refined points of loves’ casuistry.", "그들이 즐겨 지은 작품은 시르방트(풍자시), 사랑 노래, 그리고 텐송이었으니, 마지막 것은 두 시인 사이의 일종의 운문 대화로, 그들은 사랑의 결의론(決疑論)의 어떤 섬세한 점들에 관해 서로 묻고 답했다."], ["It seems the Provencials were so completely absorbed in these delicate questions as to neglect and despise the composition of fabulous histories of adventure and knighthood, which they left in a great measure to the poets of the northern part of the kingdom, called Trouveurs.", "프로방스 사람들은 이런 미묘한 문제에 너무나 완전히 빠져든 나머지, 모험과 기사도에 관한 허구의 이야기들을 짓는 일은 소홀히 하고 멸시했던 듯하며, 그것을 대체로 트루베르라 불리던 왕국 북부의 시인들에게 맡겨 두었다."]], [["At a time when chivalry excited universal admiration, and when all the efforts of that chivalry were directed against the enemies of religion, it was natural that literature should receive the same impulse, and that history and fable should be ransacked to furnish examples of courage and piety that might excite increased emulation.", "기사도가 만인의 찬탄을 불러일으키고, 그 기사도의 온갖 노력이 종교의 적들을 향하던 시대에, 문학이 같은 자극을 받는 것은, 그리고 한층 더 큰 본받음을 불러일으킬 만한 용기와 경건의 본보기를 마련하고자 역사와 우화를 샅샅이 뒤지는 것은 자연스러운 일이었다."], ["Arthur and Charlemagne were the two heroes selected for this purpose.", "아서 왕과 샤를마뉴가 이 목적을 위해 선택된 두 영웅이었다."], ["Arthur’s pretensions were that he was a brave, though not always a successful warrior; he had withstood with great resolution the arms of the infidels, that is to say of the Saxons, and his memory was held in the highest estimation by his countrymen, the Britons, who carried with them into Wales, and into the kindred country of Armorica, or Brittany, the memory of his exploits, which their national vanity insensibly exaggerated, till the little prince of the Silures (South Wales) was magnified into the conqueror of England, of Gaul, and of the greater part of Europe.", "아서 왕의 자격은 그가 비록 늘 성공하지는 못했을지언정 용감한 전사였다는 데 있었다. 그는 이교도들, 곧 색슨인들의 군세에 큰 결의로 맞섰고, 그의 기억은 그의 동족인 브리튼인들에게 더없이 높은 존경으로 간직되었다."], ["His genealogy was gradually carried up to an imaginary Brutus, and to the period of the Trojan war, and a sort of chronicle was composed in the Welsh, or Armorican language, which, under the pompous title of the “History of the Kings of Britain,”", "그들은 웨일스로, 그리고 그와 동족인 나라 아르모리카, 곧 브르타뉴로 그의 공적의 기억을 지니고 들어갔으며, 그들의 민족적 허영심이 그것을 부지중에 부풀린 끝에, 실루레스(남웨일스)의 작은 군주가 잉글랜드와 갈리아, 그리고 유럽 대부분의 정복자로 과장되기에 이르렀다."], ["was translated into Latin by Geoffrey of Monmouth, about the year 1150. The Welsh critics consider the material of the work to have been an older history, written by St.", "그의 족보는 점차 가공의 브루투스에게로, 그리고 트로이아 전쟁의 시기에까지 거슬러 올라갔으며, 웨일스어 혹은 아르모리카어로 일종의 연대기가 지어져, \"브리튼 왕들의 역사\"라는 거창한 제목 아래 1150년 무렵 몬머스의 제프리에 의해 라틴어로 번역되었다."], ["Talian, Bishop of St. Asaph, in the seventh century.", "웨일스의 비평가들은 이 저작의 바탕이 7세기에 성 아사프의 주교 성 탈리안이 쓴 더 오래된 역사서였다고 본다."]], [["As to Charlemagne, though his real merits were sufficient to secure his immortality, it was impossible that his holy wars against the Saracens should not become a favorite topic for fiction.", "샤를마뉴로 말하자면, 그의 실제 공적만으로도 그의 불멸을 보장하기에 충분했으나, 사라센인들에 맞선 그의 \"성전(聖戰)\"이 허구의 인기 있는 소재가 되지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Accordingly, the fabulous history of these wars was written, probably towards the close of the eleventh century, by a monk, who, thinking it would add dignity to his work to embellish it with a contemporary name, boldly ascribed it to Turpin, who was Archbishop of Rheims about the year 773.", "그리하여 이 전쟁들에 관한 허구의 역사가, 아마도 11세기 말엽에, 한 수도사에 의해 쓰였으니, 그는 동시대의 이름으로 자신의 저작을 꾸미면 거기에 권위가 더해지리라 여겨, 그것을 대담하게도 773년 무렵 랭스의 대주교였던 튀르팽의 작품으로 돌렸다."]], [["These fabulous chronicles were for a while imprisoned in languages of local only or of professional access.", "이 허구의 연대기들은 한동안 지역에서만, 혹은 특정 직업의 사람들만 접근할 수 있는 언어 속에 갇혀 있었다."], ["Both Turpin and Geoffrey might indeed be read by ecclesiastics, the sole Latin scholars of those times, and Geoffrey’s British original would contribute to the gratification of Welshmen; but neither could become extensively popular till translated into some language of general and familiar use.", "튀르팽과 제프리는 사실 당시 유일한 라틴어 학자였던 성직자들이 읽을 수 있었고, 제프리의 브리튼어 원전은 웨일스인들을 흡족하게 하는 데 이바지했을 것이다. 그러나 어느 쪽도 일반적으로 두루 쓰이는 친숙한 언어로 번역되기까지는 널리 대중적이 될 수 없었다."], ["The Anglo-Saxon was at that time used only by a conquered and enslaved nation; the Spanish and Italian languages were not yet formed; the Norman French alone was spoken and understood by the nobility in the greater part of Europe, and therefore was a proper vehicle for the new mode of composition.", "당시 앵글로색슨어는 정복당해 노예가 된 한 민족만이 쓰던 말이었고, 스페인어와 이탈리아어는 아직 형성되지 않았으며, 오직 노르만 프랑스어만이 유럽 대부분의 지역에서 귀족들에 의해 말해지고 이해되었으니, 따라서 그것이 이 새로운 양식의 글쓰기에 알맞은 매개체였다."]], [["That language was fashionable in England before the Conquest, and became, after that event, the only language used at the court of London.", "그 언어는 정복 이전부터 잉글랜드에서 유행했고, 정복 이후에는 런던 궁정에서 쓰이는 유일한 언어가 되었다."], ["As the various conquests of the Normans, and the enthusiastic valor of that extraordinary people, had familiarized the minds of men with the most marvellous events, their poets eagerly seized the fabulous legends of Arthur and Charlemagne, translated them into the language of the day, and soon produced a variety of imitations.", "노르만인들의 갖가지 정복과, 그 비범한 민족의 열광적인 용맹이 사람들의 마음을 더없이 경이로운 사건들에 익숙하게 만들어 놓았던 만큼, 그들의 시인들은 아서 왕과 샤를마뉴의 허구의 전설들을 열심히 붙잡아 당대의 언어로 옮겼고, 곧 갖가지 모방작을 내놓았다."], ["The adventures attributed to these monarchs, and to their distinguished warriors, together with those of many other traditionary or imaginary heroes, composed by degrees that formidable body of marvellous histories which, from the dialect in which the most ancient of them were written, were called “Romances.”", "이 군주들과 그들의 이름난 전사들에게 돌려진 모험들이, 그리고 전승에서 전해지거나 상상으로 지어낸 다른 많은 영웅들의 모험들과 더불어, 점차 저 어마어마한 경이로운 이야기들의 덩어리를 이루었으니, 그 가운데 가장 오래된 것들이 쓰인 방언에서 따와 \"로맨스\"라 불리게 되었다."]], [["# METRICAL ROMANCES", "# METRICAL ROMANCES"]], [["The earliest form in which romances appear is that of a rude kind of verse.", "로맨스가 나타나는 가장 이른 형태는 일종의 거친 운문이었다."], ["In this form it is supposed they were sung or recited at the feasts of princes and knights in their baronial halls.", "이 형태로, 그것이 군주와 기사들의 향연에서 그들의 영주 저택의 큰 방에서 노래되거나 낭송되었던 것으로 여겨진다."], ["The following specimen of the language and style of Robert de Beauvais, who flourished in 1257, is from Sir Walter Scott’s “Introduction to the Romance of Sir Tristrem”:", "1257년에 활동한 로베르 드 보베의 언어와 문체를 보여 주는 다음의 표본은, 월터 스콧 경의 \"트리스트렘 경 로맨스 서론\"에서 가져온 것이다."]], [["“Ne voil pas emmi dire, Ici diverse la matyere, Entre ceus qui solent cunter, E de le cunte Tristran parler.”", "\"Ne voil pas emmi dire, Ici diverse la matyere, Entre ceus qui solent cunter, E de le cunte Tristran parler.\""], ["“I will not say too much about it, So diverse is the matter, Among those who are in the habit of telling And relating the story of Tristran.”", "\"나는 그것에 관해 너무 많이 말하지 않으련다, 그 소재가 너무나 갖가지인 까닭에, 트리스트란의 이야기를 들려주고 이야기하는 데 익숙한 자들 사이에서.\""]], [["This is a specimen of the language which was in use among the nobility of England, in the ages immediately after the Norman conquest.", "이것은 노르만 정복 직후의 시대에 잉글랜드 귀족들 사이에서 쓰이던 언어의 표본이다."], ["The following is a specimen of the English that existed at the same time, among the common people.", "다음은 같은 시기에 일반 백성들 사이에 존재하던 영어의 표본이다."], ["Robert de Brunne, speaking of his Latin and French authorities, says:", "로베르 드 브룬은 자신의 라틴어와 프랑스어 전거들을 두고 이렇게 말한다."]], [["“Als thai haf wryten and sayd Haf I alle in myn Inglis layd, In symple speche as I couthe, That is lightest in manne’s mouthe.", "\"그들이 써 두고 말해 둔 그대로 나는 그 모두를 내 잉글랜드어로 담았네, 내가 할 수 있는 대로 소박한 말로, 사람의 입에 가장 가벼운 말로."], ["Alle for the luf of symple men, That strange Inglis cannot ken.”", "오로지 소박한 사람들의 사랑을 위하여, 낯선 잉글랜드어를 알지 못하는 이들을 위하여.\""]], [["The “strange Inglis” being the language of the previous specimen.", "여기서 \"낯선 잉글랜드어\"란 앞의 표본에 나온 언어를 가리킨다."]], [["It was not till toward the end of the thirteenth century that the prose romances began to appear.", "산문 로맨스가 나타나기 시작한 것은 13세기 말엽에 이르러서였다."], ["These works generally began with disowning and discrediting the sources from which in reality they drew their sole information.", "이 저작들은 대개, 실제로는 자신들이 정보를 끌어온 유일한 출처였던 것을 부인하고 깎아내리는 것으로 시작했다."], ["As every romance was supposed to be a real history, the compilers of those in prose would have forfeited all credit if they had announced themselves as mere copyists of the minstrels.", "모든 로맨스가 실제 역사로 여겨졌던 만큼, 산문으로 쓴 이들의 편찬자들은 자신이 음유 시인들을 그저 베껴 적은 자에 불과하다고 밝혔다면 모든 신뢰를 잃었을 것이다."], ["On the contrary, they usually state that, as the popular poems upon the matter in question contain many “lesings,” they had been induced to translate the real and true history of such or such a knight from the original Latin or Greek, or from the ancient British or Armorican authorities, which authorities existed only in their own assertion.", "도리어 그들은 으레 이렇게 진술했으니, 문제의 소재에 관한 인기 있는 시들에는 \"거짓말\"이 많이 들어 있는 까닭에, 자신은 이러저러한 기사의 실제이며 참된 역사를 본래의 라틴어나 그리스어에서, 혹은 고대 브리튼어나 아르모리카어 전거에서 번역하지 않을 수 없었다는 것이었으나, 그러한 전거들은 오직 그들 자신의 주장 속에만 존재했다."]], [["A specimen of the style of the prose romances may be found in the following extract from one of the most celebrated and latest of them, the “Morte d’Arthur” of Sir Thomas Mallory, of the date of 1485.", "산문 로맨스의 문체를 보여 주는 표본은, 그것들 가운데 가장 유명하고 가장 나중에 나온 것의 하나인, 1485년에 나온 토머스 맬러리 경의 \"아서 왕의 죽음\"에서 다음과 같이 발췌한 데서 찾을 수 있다."], ["From this work much of the contents of this volume has been drawn, with as close an adherence to the original style as was thought consistent with our plan of adapting our narrative to the taste of modern readers.", "이 저작에서 이 책의 내용 가운데 많은 부분을 끌어왔으니, 우리의 서술을 현대 독자의 취향에 맞춘다는 우리의 계획에 어긋나지 않는다고 여겨지는 한도 안에서, 원래의 문체에 되도록 가깝게 따랐다."]], [["“It is notoyrly knowen thorugh the vnyuersal world that there been ix worthy and the best that ever were.", "\"온 세상에 두루 널리 알려진 바, 일찍이 있었던 자들 가운데 아홉 명의 훌륭하고 가장 뛰어난 자가 있었다."], ["That is to wete thre paynyms, three Jewes, and three crysten men.", "곧 세 명의 이교도, 세 명의 유대인, 그리고 세 명의 기독교인이 그들이다."], ["As for the paynyms, they were tofore the Incarnacyon of Cryst whiche were named, the fyrst Hector of Troye; the second Alysaunder the grete, and the thyrd Julyus Cezar, Emperour of Rome, of whome thystoryes ben wel kno and had.", "이교도로 말하자면, 그들은 그리스도의 강생 이전의 자들로, 첫째는 트로이아의 헥토르라 불렸고, 둘째는 알렉산드로스 대왕, 셋째는 로마 황제 율리우스 카이사르였으니, 그들에 관한 이야기는 잘 알려지고 전해지고 있다."], ["And as for the thre Jewes whyche also were tofore thyncarnacyon of our Lord, of whome the fyrst was Duc Josue, whyche brought the chyldren of Israhel into the londe of beheste; the second Dauyd, kyng of Jherusalem, and the thyrd Judas Machabeus; of these thre the byble reherceth al theyr noble hystoryes and actes.", "그리고 또한 우리 주님의 강생 이전의 자들이었던 세 명의 유대인으로 말하자면, 그 첫째는 이스라엘의 자손들을 약속의 땅으로 이끈 지도자 여호수아였고, 둘째는 예루살렘의 왕 다윗이었으며, 셋째는 유다 마카베오였으니, 이 셋에 관하여는 성서가 그들의 온갖 고귀한 이야기와 행적을 들려준다."], ["And sythe the sayd Incarnacyon haue ben the noble crysten men stalled and admytted thorugh the vnyuersal world to the nombre of the ix beste and worthy, of whome was fyrst the noble Arthur, whose noble actes I purpose to wryte in this person book here folowyng.", "그리고 앞서 말한 강생 이후로, 온 세상에서 아홉 명의 가장 뛰어나고 훌륭한 자의 수에 들어가도록 세워지고 받아들여진 고귀한 기독교인들이 있었으니, 그 첫째가 고귀한 아서 왕으로, 그의 고귀한 행적을 나는 여기 이어지는 이 책에 쓰고자 한다."], ["The second was Charlemayn, or Charles the grete, of whome thystorye is had in many places both in frensshe and englysshe, and the thyrd and last was Godefray of boloyn.”", "둘째는 샤를마뉴, 곧 샤를 대제로, 그에 관한 이야기는 프랑스어로든 영어로든 여러 곳에 전해지며, 셋째이자 마지막은 부용의 고드프루아였다.\""]], [["————", "————"]], [["# THE MYTHICAL HISTORY OF ENGLAND", "# THE MYTHICAL HISTORY OF ENGLAND"]], [["THE illustrious poet, Milton, in his “History of England,” is the author whom we chiefly follow in this chapter.", "저명한 시인 밀턴이 그의 「영국사」에서 보여 준 서술이, 이 장에서 우리가 주로 따르는 전거이다."]], [["According to the earliest accounts, Albion, a giant, and son of Neptune, a contemporary of Hercules, ruled over the island, to which he gave his name.", "가장 오래된 기록에 따르면, 거인이자 넵투누스의 아들이며 헤라클레스와 동시대를 살았던 알비온이 이 섬을 다스렸고, 그는 자신의 이름을 그 섬에 주었다."], ["Presuming to oppose the progress of Hercules in his western march, he was slain by him.", "헤라클레스가 서쪽으로 진군하는 것을 감히 가로막으려 들었다가, 알비온은 그의 손에 죽임을 당하고 말았다."]], [["Another story is that Histion, the son of Japhet, the son of Noah, had four sons, Francus, Romanus, Alemannus, and Britto, from whom descended the French, Roman, German, and British people.", "또 다른 이야기에 따르면, 노아의 아들 야벳의 아들 히스티온에게는 네 아들 프란쿠스, 로마누스, 알레만누스, 브리토가 있었으며, 이들로부터 각각 프랑스인, 로마인, 게르만인, 브리튼인이 비롯되었다고 한다."]], [["Rejecting these and other like stories, Milton gives more regard to the story of Brutus, the Trojan, which, he says, is supported by “descents of ancestry long continued, laws and exploits not plainly seeming to be borrowed or devised, which on the common belief have wrought no small impression; defended by many, denied utterly by few.”", "밀턴은 이러한 이야기들과 그와 비슷한 다른 이야기들을 물리치고, 트로이아인 브루투스의 이야기에 더 큰 무게를 두는데, 그의 말에 따르면 이 이야기는 \"오래도록 이어진 조상의 계보, 빌려 오거나 꾸며 낸 것으로는 분명히 보이지 않는 법률과 위업으로 뒷받침되며, 이는 통상의 믿음 속에서 적지 않은 인상을 남겼고, 많은 이들이 옹호하며 이를 전적으로 부인하는 이는 거의 없다\"고 한다."], ["The principal authority is Geoffrey of Monmouth, whose history, written in the twelfth century, purports to be a translation of a history of Britain brought over from the opposite shore of France, which, under the name of Brittany, was chiefly peopled by natives of Britain who, from time to time, emigrated thither, driven from their own country by the inroads of the Picts and Scots.", "주된 전거는 몬머스의 제프리이며, 12세기에 쓰인 그의 역사서는 프랑스 맞은편 해안에서 건너온 브리튼의 역사서를 번역한 것이라 일컬어진다. 브르타뉴라는 이름으로 불리던 그 땅은, 픽트족과 스코트족의 침입에 떠밀려 제 나라를 떠나 때때로 그리로 이주해 온 브리튼 원주민들이 주로 살던 곳이었다."], ["According to this authority, Brutus was the son of Silvius, and he of Ascanius, the son of Æneas, whose flight from Troy and settlement in Italy are narrated in “Stories of Gods and Heroes.”", "이 전거에 따르면, 브루투스는 실비우스의 아들이고, 실비우스는 아스카니우스의 아들이며, 아스카니우스는 아이네아스의 아들이니, 아이네아스가 트로이아에서 도망쳐 이탈리아에 정착한 이야기는 「신들과 영웅들의 이야기」에 서술되어 있다."]], [["Brutus, at the age of fifteen, attending his father to the chase, unfortunately killed him with an arrow.", "브루투스는 열다섯 살 되던 해에 아버지를 따라 사냥에 나섰다가, 불행히도 화살로 그를 죽이고 말았다."], ["Banished therefor by his kindred, he sought refuge in that part of Greece where Helenus, with a band of Trojan exiles, had become established.", "이 일로 친족에게 추방당한 그는, 헬레노스가 트로이아 망명자들의 무리와 함께 자리를 잡은 그리스의 한 지역으로 피신했다."], ["But Helenus was now dead and the descendants of the Trojans were oppressed by Pandrasus, the king of the country.", "그러나 헬레노스는 이제 죽고 없었고, 트로이아인의 후손들은 그 나라의 왕 판드라소스에게 억압을 받고 있었다."], ["Brutus, being kindly received among them, so throve in virtue and in arms as to win the regard of all the eminent of the land above all others of his age.", "그들 가운데서 따뜻하게 받아들여진 브루투스는 덕과 무예에서 크게 자라나, 그 땅의 모든 명사들로부터 또래의 누구보다도 높은 존경을 얻게 되었다."], ["In consequence of this the Trojans not only began to hope, but secretly to persuade him to lead them the way to liberty.", "그 결과 트로이아인들은 희망을 품기 시작했을 뿐만 아니라, 은밀히 그에게 자유로 이르는 길을 이끌어 달라고 설득하기에 이르렀다."], ["To encourage them, they had the promise of help from Assaracus, a noble Greek youth, whose mother was a Trojan.", "그들에게 용기를 북돋아 준 것은, 어머니가 트로이아인이었던 고귀한 그리스 청년 아사라코스의 도움 약속이었다."], ["He had suffered wrong at the hands of the king, and for that reason the more willingly cast in his lot with the Trojan exiles.", "그는 왕의 손에 부당한 일을 당한 적이 있었던 까닭에, 한층 기꺼이 트로이아 망명자들과 운명을 함께하였다."]], [["Choosing a fit opportunity, Brutus with his countrymen withdrew to the woods and hills, as the safest place from which to expostulate, and sent this message to Pandrasus: “That the Trojans, holding it unworthy of their ancestors to serve in a foreign land, had retreated to the woods, choosing rather a savage life than a slavish one.", "적당한 기회를 골라, 브루투스는 동포들과 함께 숲과 산으로 물러났으니, 그곳이야말로 항변을 하기에 가장 안전한 자리였다. 그러고는 판드라소스에게 이런 전갈을 보냈다."], ["If that displeased him, then, with his leave, they would depart to some other country.”", "\"트로이아인들은 이국땅에서 종노릇하는 것이 제 조상들에게 합당치 않은 일이라 여겨 숲으로 물러났으며, 종살이하는 삶보다 차라리 야만의 삶을 택하였다."], ["Pandrasus, not expecting so bold a message from the sons of captives, went in pursuit of them, with such forces as he could gather, and met them on the banks of the Achelous, where Brutus got the advantage, and took the king captive.", "만일 그것이 그의 뜻에 거슬린다면, 그의 허락을 얻어 다른 어느 나라로든 떠나가겠노라.\" 포로의 자식들에게서 그토록 대담한 전갈이 오리라고는 예상치 못했던 판드라소스는, 끌어모을 수 있는 만큼의 병력을 거느리고 그들을 추격하여, 아켈로오스 강가에서 그들과 맞닥뜨렸다."], ["The result was, that the terms demanded by the Trojans were granted; the king gave his daughter Imogen in marriage to Brutus, and furnished shipping, money, and fit provision for them all to depart from the land.", "그곳에서 브루투스가 우세를 점하여 왕을 사로잡았다. 그 결과 트로이아인들이 요구한 조건이 받아들여졌으니, 왕은 딸 이모겐을 브루투스에게 아내로 주고, 그들 모두가 그 땅을 떠날 수 있도록 선박과 돈과 알맞은 식량을 마련해 주었다."]], [["The marriage being solemnized, and shipping from all parts got together, the Trojans, in a fleet of no less than three hundred and twenty sail, betook themselves to the sea.", "혼례가 치러지고, 사방에서 선박이 모이자, 트로이아인들은 무려 삼백스무 척에 이르는 함대를 이루어 바다로 나섰다."], ["On the third day they arrived at a certain island, which they found destitute of inhabitants, though there were appearances of former habitation, and among the ruins a temple of Diana.", "사흘째 되는 날, 그들은 어떤 섬에 다다랐는데, 그곳은 주민이 없었으나 예전에 사람이 살았던 흔적이 보였으며, 그 폐허 가운데에는 디아나의 신전이 있었다."], ["Brutus, here performing sacrifice at the shrine of the goddess, invoked an oracle for his guidance, in these lines:", "브루투스는 그곳에서 여신의 제단에 제물을 바치며, 자신을 인도해 달라는 신탁을 다음과 같은 시구로 청하였다."]], [["“Goddess of shades, and huntress, who at will Walk’st on the rolling sphere, and through the deep; On thy third realm, the earth, look now, and tell What land, what seat of rest, thou bidd’st me seek; What certain seat where I may worship thee For aye, with temples vowed and virgin choirs.”", "\"그늘의 여신이며 사냥의 여신이여, 뜻대로 구르는 천구 위를 거니시고 깊은 바다를 누비시는 이여, 당신의 세 번째 영토인 이 대지를 굽어보시어 일러 주소서, 어느 땅, 어느 안식의 자리를 찾으라 명하시는지를, 제가 영원토록 당신을 섬길 수 있는 확고한 자리, 서원한 신전과 처녀들의 합창대로써 받들 그곳이 어디인지를.\""]], [["To whom, sleeping before the altar, Diana in a vision thus answered:", "그러자 제단 앞에서 잠든 그에게, 디아나가 환영 속에서 이렇게 답하였다."]], [["“Brutus!", "\"브루투스여! 저 멀리 서쪽, 드넓은 대양 가운데, 갈리아의 영토 너머에 한 땅이 누워 있으니, 바다에 둘러싸인 그곳에는 옛적에 거인들이 살았으되, 이제는 비어 있어 네 백성에게 어울리노라."], ["far to the west, in the ocean wide, Beyond the realm of Gaul, a land there lies, Seagirt it lies, where giants dwelt of old; Now, void, it fits thy people: thither bend Thy course; there shalt thou find a lasting seat; There to thy sons another Troy shall rise, And kings be born of thee, whose dreaded might Shall awe the world, and conquer nations bold.”", "그리로 뱃길을 돌리라. 거기서 너는 영구한 자리를 찾으리니, 그곳에서 네 아들들에게 또 하나의 트로이아가 일어서고, 네게서 왕들이 태어나, 그 두려운 위세가 온 세상을 떨게 하며 담대한 민족들을 정복하리라.\""]], [["Brutus, guided now, as he thought, by divine direction, sped his course towards the west, and, arriving at a place on the Tyrrhene sea, found there the descendants of certain Trojans who, with Antenor, came into Italy, of whom Corineus was the chief.", "이제 자신이 신의 인도를 받는다고 여긴 브루투스는 서쪽을 향해 뱃길을 재촉했고, 티레눔 해의 어느 곳에 이르러, 안테노르와 함께 이탈리아로 들어온 어떤 트로이아인들의 후손을 거기서 발견했는데, 그들의 우두머리는 코리네우스였다. 이들이 일행에 합류했고, 함선들은 항해를 이어 가 프랑스의 루아르 강 어귀에 다다랐다."], ["These joined company, and the ships pursued their way till they arrived at the mouth of the river Loire, in France, where the expedition landed, with a view to a settlement, but were so rudely assaulted by the inhabitants that they put to sea again, and arrived at a part of the coast of Britain, now called Devonshire, where Brutus felt convinced that he had found the promised end of his voyage, landed his colony, and took possession.", "그곳에서 원정대는 정착할 뜻으로 상륙했으나, 주민들의 거친 공격을 받아 다시 바다로 나섰고, 지금은 데번셔라 불리는 브리튼 해안의 한 지역에 이르렀다. 그곳에서 브루투스는 자신이 항해의 약속된 종착지를 찾았음을 확신하여, 자신의 식민단을 상륙시키고 그 땅을 차지하였다."]], [["The island, not yet Britain, but Albion, was in a manner desert and inhospitable, occupied only by a remnant of the giant race whose excessive force and tyranny had destroyed the others.", "아직 브리튼이 아니라 알비온이라 불리던 그 섬은, 말하자면 황량하고 사람을 받아들이지 않는 땅이었으니, 지나친 힘과 폭정으로 동족을 멸한 거인족의 잔당만이 차지하고 있었다."], ["The Trojans encountered these and extirpated them, Corineus, in particular, signalizing himself by his exploits against them; from whom Cornwall takes its name, for that region fell to his lot, and there the hugest giants dwelt, lurking in rocks and caves, till Corineus rid the land of them.", "트로이아인들은 이들과 맞서 싸워 그들을 뿌리 뽑았는데, 특히 코리네우스는 그들을 상대로 한 위업으로 두드러진 명성을 떨쳤다. 콘월이라는 이름은 그에게서 비롯된 것이니, 그 지역이 그의 몫으로 떨어졌고, 또 거기에는 가장 거대한 거인들이 바위와 동굴 속에 숨어 살고 있었는데, 코리네우스가 그 땅에서 그들을 없애 버렸기 때문이다."]], [["Brutus built his capital city, and called it Trojanova (New Troy), changed in time to Trinovantus, now London;[39] and, having governed the isle twenty-four years, died, leaving three sons, Locrine, Albanact and Camber.", "브루투스는 자신의 수도를 세우고 그곳을 트로야노바(새 트로이아)라 불렀는데, 이것이 세월이 흘러 트리노반툼으로 바뀌었고, 지금의 런던이 되었다.[39] 그는 그 섬을 스물네 해 동안 다스리다가 세상을 떠나면서, 세 아들 로크린, 알바낙트, 캄베르를 남겼다."], ["Locrine had the middle part, Camber the west, called Cambria from him, and Albanact Albania, now Scotland.", "로크린은 가운데 지역을, 캄베르는 서쪽을 차지했는데 그곳은 그의 이름을 따 캄브리아라 불렸으며, 알바낙트는 알바니아 곧 지금의 스코틀랜드를 차지했다."], ["Locrine was married to Guendolen, the daughter of Corineus, but having seen a fair maid named Estrildis, who had been brought captive from Germany, he became enamoured of her, and had by her a daughter, whose name was Sabra.", "로크린은 코리네우스의 딸 구엔돌렌과 혼인했으나, 게르마니아에서 포로로 끌려온 에스트릴디스라는 어여쁜 처녀를 보고 그녀에게 반하여, 그녀에게서 사브라라는 이름의 딸을 얻었다."], ["This matter was kept secret while Corineus lived, but after his death Locrine divorced Guendolen, and made Estrildis his queen.", "이 일은 코리네우스가 살아 있는 동안에는 비밀에 부쳐졌으나, 그가 죽은 뒤 로크린은 구엔돌렌과 이혼하고 에스트릴디스를 왕비로 삼았다."], ["Guendolen, all in rage, departed to Cornwall, where Madan, her son, lived, who had been brought up by Corineus, his grandfather.", "구엔돌렌은 격분하여 콘월로 떠났는데, 그곳에는 외할아버지 코리네우스의 손에 자란 그녀의 아들 마단이 살고 있었다."], ["Gathering an army of her father’s friends and subjects, she gave battle to her husband’s forces and Locrine was slain.", "그녀는 아버지의 벗들과 신민들로 군대를 모아 남편의 군대에 싸움을 걸었고, 로크린은 죽임을 당했다."], ["Guendolen caused her rival, Estrildis, with her daughter Sabra, to be thrown into the river, from which cause the river thenceforth bore the maiden’s name, which by length of time is now changed into Sabrina or Severn.", "구엔돌렌은 연적 에스트릴디스를 그 딸 사브라와 함께 강물에 던지게 했으니, 그 까닭에 그 강은 그때부터 그 처녀의 이름을 지니게 되었고, 오랜 세월이 흐르면서 지금은 사브리나, 곧 세번으로 바뀌었다."], ["Milton alludes to this in his address to the rivers,—", "밀턴은 강들에게 바치는 글에서 이를 다음과 같이 언급하였다."]], [["“Severn swift, guilty of maiden’s death”;—", "\"빠른 세번이여, 처녀의 죽음에 죄 있는 강이여.\""]], [["and in his “Comus” tells the story with a slight variation, thus:", "또한 그의 「코머스」에서는 이 이야기를 조금 다르게 다음과 같이 들려준다."]], [["“There is a gentle nymph not far from hence, That with moist curb sways the smooth Severn stream; Sabrina is her name, a virgin pure: Whilom she was the daughter of Locrine, That had the sceptre from his father, Brute, She, guiltless damsel, flying the mad pursuit Of her enragéd step-dame, Guendolen, Commended her fair innocence to the flood, That stayed her flight with his cross-flowing course The water-nymphs that in the bottom played, Held up their pearléd wrists and took her in, Bearing her straight to aged Nereus’ hall, Who, piteous of her woes, reared her lank head, And gave her to his daughters to imbathe In nectared lavers strewed with asphodel, And through the porch and inlet of each sense Dropped in ambrosial oils till she revived, And underwent a quick, immortal change, Made goddess of the river,” etc.", "\"여기서 멀지 않은 곳에 한 고운 님프가 있으니, 촉촉한 고삐로 매끄러운 세번 물줄기를 다스린다. 사브리나가 그녀의 이름이요, 순결한 처녀라. 옛적에 그녀는 로크린의 딸이었으니, 로크린은 제 아비 브루투스에게서 왕홀을 물려받았다. 죄 없는 이 처녀는, 격분한 계모 구엔돌렌의 미친 추격을 피해 달아나다가, 제 고운 결백함을 강물에 맡기었더라. 강물은 가로질러 흐르는 물길로 그녀의 도주를 멈추게 하니, 바닥에서 노닐던 물의 님프들이 진주빛 손목을 들어 그녀를 받아 들고는, 곧장 늙은 네레우스의 전당으로 데려갔다. 네레우스는 그녀의 슬픔을 가엾이 여겨 시든 머리를 들어 올리고, 제 딸들에게 맡겨 그녀를 목욕시키게 하니, 아스포델을 흩뿌린 넥타르의 대야에서였다. 그러고는 감각마다의 문과 입구를 통하여 암브로시아의 기름을 떨구니 마침내 그녀가 되살아나, 재빠른 불멸의 변신을 겪고는 강의 여신이 되었더라.\" 운운."]], [["If our readers ask when all this took place, we must answer, in the first place, that mythology is not careful of dates; and next, that, as Brutus was the great-grandson of Æneas, it must have been not far from a century subsequent to the Trojan war, or about eleven hundred years before the invasion of the island by Julius Cæsar.", "이 모든 일이 언제 일어났느냐고 우리 독자들이 묻는다면, 우선 신화는 연대에 관해 그리 꼼꼼하지 않다고 답해야 하겠으며, 다음으로 브루투스가 아이네아스의 증손이었으니 그 일은 트로이아 전쟁으로부터 한 세기쯤 뒤, 곧 율리우스 카이사르가 이 섬을 침공하기 약 천백 년 전이었으리라고 답해야 하겠다."], ["This long interval is filled with the names of princes whose chief occupation was in warring with one another.", "이 긴 세월은 서로 전쟁하는 것이 주된 일이었던 군주들의 이름으로 채워져 있다."], ["Some few, whose names remain connected with places, or embalmed in literature, we will mention.", "그 가운데 이름이 어느 장소와 연관되어 남아 있거나 문학 속에 길이 보존된 몇몇을 우리는 언급하고자 한다."]], [["Bladud built the city of Bath, and dedicated the medicinal waters to Minerva.", "블라두드는 배스라는 도시를 세우고, 그 약효 있는 물을 미네르바에게 바쳤다."], ["He was a man of great invention, and practised the arts of magic, till, having made him wings to fly, he fell down upon the temple of Apollo, in Trinovant, and so died, after twenty years’ reign.", "그는 발명에 능한 사람이어서 마법의 기술을 부렸는데, 마침내 날아오를 날개를 자신에게 만들어 붙였다가 트리노반트에 있는 아폴론 신전 위로 떨어져, 스무 해의 치세 끝에 그렇게 죽고 말았다."]], [["Leir, who next reigned, built Leicester, and called it after his name.", "다음으로 다스린 레이르는 레스터를 세우고 자신의 이름을 따 그렇게 불렀다."], ["He had no male issue, but only three daughters.", "그에게는 아들이 없고 오로지 세 딸만 있었다."], ["When grown old he determined to divide his kingdom among his daughters, and bestow them in marriage.", "늙어서 그는 왕국을 딸들에게 나누어 주고 그들을 혼인시키기로 결심했다."], ["But first, to try which of them loved him best, he determined to ask them solemnly in order, and judge of the warmth of their affection by their answers.", "그러나 먼저, 누가 자기를 가장 사랑하는지를 시험하려고, 차례로 엄숙히 물어 그 대답으로 애정의 깊이를 가늠하기로 했다."], ["Goneril, the eldest, knowing well her father’s weakness, made answer that she loved him “above her soul.”", "만딸 고너릴은 아버지의 약점을 잘 알고 있었으므로, 자신은 그를 \"제 영혼보다 더\" 사랑한다고 대답했다."], ["“Since thou so honorest my declining age,”", "\"네가 그토록 내 저무는 노년을 받든다 하니,\""], ["said the old man, “to thee and to thy husband I give the third part of my realm.”", "노인이 말했다. \"너와 네 남편에게 내 왕국의 삼분의 일을 주겠노라.\""], ["Such good success for a few words soon uttered was ample instruction to Regan, the second daughter, what to say.", "몇 마디 쉽게 내뱉은 말로 이런 큰 성공을 거둔 것은, 둘째 딸 리건에게 무어라 말해야 할지를 충분히 일러 주었다."], ["She therefore to the same question replied that “she loved him more than all the world beside;”", "그리하여 그녀는 같은 물음에 자신은 \"이 세상 어느 것보다도 더\""], ["and so received an equal reward with her sister.", "아버지를 사랑한다고 답하여, 언니와 똑같은 보상을 받았다."], ["But Cordeilla, the youngest, and hitherto the best beloved, though having before her eyes the reward of a little easy soothing, and the loss likely to attend plain-dealing, yet was not moved from the solid purpose of a sincere and virtuous answer, and replied: “Father, my love towards you is as my duty bids.", "그러나 가장 어리고 그때까지 가장 사랑받던 코르데일라는, 약간의 손쉬운 아첨에 따르는 보상과 솔직히 말하는 데 따를 손실을 눈앞에 두고도, 진실하고 덕 있는 대답이라는 굳은 뜻에서 흔들리지 않고 이렇게 답했다. \"아버님, 아버님을 향한 제 사랑은 제 도리가 명하는 그대로입니다."], ["They who pretend beyond this flatter.”", "이를 넘어선다고 내세우는 이들은 아첨하는 것입니다.\""], ["When the old man, sorry to hear this, and wishing her to recall these words, persisted in asking, she still restrained her expressions so as to say rather less than more than the truth.", "노인은 이 말을 듣고 서운하여 그 말을 거두기를 바라며 계속 캐물었으나, 그녀는 여전히 표현을 자제하여 진실보다 더 말하기보다는 차라리 덜 말하였다. 그러자 레이르는 격분하여 터뜨렸다."], ["Then Leir, all in a passion, burst forth: “Since thou hast not reverenced thy aged father like thy sisters, think not to have any part in my kingdom or what else I have;”—and without delay, giving in marriage his other daughters, Goneril to the Duke of Albany, and Regan to the Duke of Cornwall, he divides his kingdom between them, and goes to reside with his eldest daughter, attended only by a hundred knights.", "\"네가 네 언니들처럼 늙은 아비를 받들지 않았으니, 내 왕국에서든 내가 가진 그 밖의 무엇에서든 한 몫이라도 받으리라고는 생각지 마라.\" 그러고는 지체 없이 다른 두 딸을 혼인시켜, 고너릴은 올버니 공작에게, 리건은 콘월 공작에게 주고, 그 둘 사이에 왕국을 나누었으며, 오로지 기사 백 명만을 거느리고 만딸과 함께 거처하러 갔다."], ["But in a short time his attendants, being complained of as too numerous and disorderly, are reduced to thirty.", "그러나 얼마 지나지 않아 그의 수행원들이 너무 많고 무질서하다는 불평이 일어 서른 명으로 줄어들었다."], ["Resenting that affront, the old king betakes him to his second daughter; but she, instead of soothing his wounded pride, takes part with her sister, and refuses to admit a retinue of more than five.", "이 모욕에 분개한 노왕은 둘째 딸에게 몸을 의탁했으나, 그녀는 상처 입은 그의 자존심을 달래기는커녕 언니 편을 들어, 다섯 명을 넘는 수행원은 받아들이기를 거부했다."], ["Then back he returns to the other, who now will not receive him with more than one attendant.", "그러자 그는 다시 만딸에게 돌아갔는데, 이번에는 한 명을 넘는 수행원과 함께라면 그를 받아들이려 하지 않았다."], ["Then the remembrance of Cordeilla comes to his thoughts, and he takes his journey into France to seek her, with little hope of kind consideration from one whom he had so injured, but to pay her the last recompense he can render,—confession of his injustice.", "그제야 코르데일라에 대한 기억이 그의 생각에 떠올라, 그는 그녀를 찾아 프랑스로 길을 떠났으니, 자신이 그토록 모질게 대한 이에게서 다정한 배려를 기대할 희망은 거의 없었으나, 그녀에게 자신이 베풀 수 있는 마지막 보답, 곧 자신의 불의를 고백하기 위함이었다."], ["When Cordeilla is informed of his approach, and of his sad condition, she pours forth true filial tears.", "코르데일라는 아버지가 다가오고 있으며 그가 처한 가련한 처지를 전해 듣고는, 참된 효녀의 눈물을 쏟았다."], ["And, not willing that her own or others’ eyes should see him in that forlorn condition, she sends one of her trusted servants to meet him, and convey him privately to some comfortable abode, and to furnish him with such state as befitted his dignity.", "그리고 자신의 눈이든 다른 이의 눈이든 그 비참한 처지에 놓인 아버지를 보게 하고 싶지 않아, 믿을 만한 종 하나를 보내 그를 맞이하게 하고, 은밀히 편안한 거처로 모셔 그 위엄에 걸맞은 격식을 갖추어 드리게 했다."], ["After which Cordeilla, with the king her husband, went in state to meet him, and, after an honorable reception, the king permitted his wife, Cordeilla, to go with an army and set her father again upon his throne.", "그런 뒤에 코르데일라는 자신의 남편인 왕과 함께 격식을 갖추어 아버지를 맞이하러 나갔으며, 정중한 영접을 받은 뒤, 왕은 아내 코르데일라가 군대를 거느리고 가서 그녀의 아버지를 다시 왕좌에 앉히도록 허락했다."], ["They prospered, subdued the wicked sisters and their consorts, and Leir obtained the crown and held it three years.", "그들은 일을 잘 이루어 사악한 언니들과 그 배우자들을 굴복시켰고, 레이르는 왕관을 되찾아 삼 년 동안 다스렸다."], ["Cordeilla succeeded him and reigned five years; but the sons of her sisters, after that, rebelled against her, and she lost both her crown and life.", "코르데일라가 그를 이어 다섯 해 동안 다스렸으나, 그 뒤로 언니들의 아들들이 그녀에게 반란을 일으켰고, 그녀는 왕관과 목숨을 모두 잃고 말았다."]], [["Shakspeare has chosen this story as the subject of his tragedy of “King Lear,” varying its details in some respects.", "셰익스피어는 이 이야기를 자신의 비극 「리어 왕」의 소재로 골라, 몇 가지 점에서 그 세부를 바꾸었다."], ["The madness of Leir, and the ill success of Cordeilla’s attempt to reinstate her father, are the principal variations, and those in the names will also be noticed.", "레이르의 광기와, 아버지를 복위시키려던 코르데일라의 시도가 실패로 끝나는 것이 주된 변형이며, 이름에서의 차이 또한 눈에 띌 것이다."], ["Our narrative is drawn from Milton’s “History;” and thus the reader will perceive that the story of Leir has had the distinguished honor of being told by the two acknowledged chiefs of British literature.", "우리의 서술은 밀턴의 「영국사」에서 끌어온 것이니, 이로써 독자는 레이르의 이야기가 영국 문학의 공인된 두 거장에 의해 이야기되는 두드러진 영예를 누렸음을 알아차릴 것이다."]], [["# FERREX AND PORREX", "# FERREX AND PORREX"]], [["Ferrex and Porrex were brothers, who held the kingdom after Leir.", "페렉스와 포렉스는 형제로서, 레이르의 뒤를 이어 왕국을 차지했다."], ["They quarrelled about the supremacy, and Porrex expelled his brother, who, obtaining aid from Suard, king of the Franks, returned and made war upon Porrex.", "그들은 패권을 두고 다투었고, 포렉스가 형을 내쫓았으나, 형은 프랑크족의 왕 수아르에게서 도움을 얻어 돌아와 포렉스에게 전쟁을 걸었다."], ["Ferrex was slain in battle and his forces dispersed.", "페렉스는 전투에서 죽임을 당하고 그의 군대는 흩어졌다."], ["When their mother came to hear of her son’s death, who was her favorite, she fell into a great rage, and conceived a mortal hatred against the survivor.", "그들의 어머니가 자신이 가장 아끼던 아들의 죽음을 전해 듣고는 크나큰 분노에 사로잡혀, 살아남은 아들에게 사무치는 증오를 품게 되었다."], ["She took, therefore, her opportunity when he was asleep, fell upon him, and, with the assistance of her women, tore him in pieces.", "그리하여 그녀는 그가 잠든 틈을 노려 그에게 달려들어, 시녀들의 도움을 받아 그를 갈가리 찢어 버렸다."], ["This horrid story would not be worth relating, were it not for the fact that it has furnished the plot for the first tragedy which was written in the English language. It was entitled “Gorboduc,”", "이 끔찍한 이야기는, 영어로 쓰인 최초의 비극에 줄거리를 제공했다는 사실이 아니었다면 옮길 만한 값어치가 없었을 것이다."], ["but in the second edition “Ferrex and Porrex,” and was the production of Thomas Sackville, afterwards Earl of Dorset, and Thomas Norton, a barrister.", "그 작품은 「고르보두크」라는 제목이었으나, 재판에서는 「페렉스와 포렉스」가 되었으며, 훗날 도싯 백작이 된 토머스 색빌과 법정 변호사 토머스 노턴의 작품이었다."], ["Its date was 1561.", "그것이 쓰인 해는 1561년이었다."]], [["# DUNWALLO MOLMUTIUS", "# DUNWALLO MOLMUTIUS"]], [["This is the next name of note.", "이것이 그다음으로 주목할 만한 이름이다."], ["Molmutius established the Molmutine laws, which bestowed the privilege of sanctuary on temples, cities, and the roads leading to them, and gave the same protection to ploughs, extending a religious sanction to the labors of the field.", "몰무티우스는 몰무티네 법을 제정했는데, 이 법은 신전과 도시와 그리로 이어지는 길에 성역의 특권을 부여하고, 쟁기에도 같은 보호를 베풀어, 들판의 노동에까지 종교적 보장을 미치게 했다. 셰익스피어는 「심벨린」"], ["Shakspeare alludes to him in “Cymbeline,” Act III., Scene 1:", "제3막 제1장에서 그를 다음과 같이 언급한다."]], [["“. .", "\"."], [".", "."], ["Molmutius made our laws; Who was the first of Britain which did put His brows within a golden crown, and called Himself a king.”", ". 몰무티우스가 우리의 법을 만들었으니, 그는 황금 왕관을 제 이마에 두르고 스스로를 왕이라 일컬은 브리튼 최초의 사람이었다.\""]], [["# BRENNUS AND BELINUS,", "# BRENNUS AND BELINUS,"]], [["the sons of Molmutius, succeeded him.", "몰무티우스의 아들들인 브렌누스와 벨리누스가 그의 뒤를 이었다."], ["They quarrelled, and Brennus was driven out of the island, and took refuge in Gaul, where he met with such favor from the king of the Allobroges that he gave him his daughter in marriage, and made him his partner on the throne.", "그들은 다투었고, 브렌누스는 섬에서 쫓겨나 갈리아로 피신했다. 그곳에서 그는 알로브로게스족의 왕에게 큰 호의를 입어, 왕은 그에게 자신의 딸을 아내로 주고 그를 왕좌의 동반자로 삼았다."], ["Brennus is the name which the Roman historians give to the famous leader of the Gauls who took Rome in the time of Camillus.", "브렌누스는 로마의 역사가들이 카밀루스 시대에 로마를 함락시킨 저 유명한 갈리아인 지도자에게 붙인 이름이다."], ["Geoffrey of Monmouth claims the glory of the conquest for the British prince, after he had become king of the Allobroges.", "몬머스의 제프리는 그 정복의 영광이 알로브로게스족의 왕이 된 뒤의 이 브리튼 왕에게 돌아간다고 주장한다."]], [["After Belinus and Brennus there reigned several kings of little note, and then came Elidure.", "벨리누스와 브렌누스의 뒤로는 이렇다 할 만하지 않은 몇몇 왕이 다스렸고, 그다음에 엘리두레가 나왔다."], ["Arthgallo, his brother, being king, gave great offence to his powerful nobles, who rose against him, deposed him, and advanced Elidure to the throne.", "그의 형 아르트갈로가 왕으로 있을 때, 그는 자신의 권세 있는 귀족들의 큰 노여움을 샀고, 그들이 들고일어나 그를 폐위시키고 엘리두레를 왕좌에 올렸다."], ["Arthgallo fled, and endeavored to find assistance in the neighboring kingdoms to reinstate him, but found none.", "아르트갈로는 도망쳐, 자신을 복위시키고자 이웃 왕국들에서 도움을 구하려 했으나 아무 데서도 얻지 못했다."], ["Elidure reigned prosperously and wisely.", "엘리두레는 번영을 누리며 지혜롭게 다스렸다."], ["After five years’ possession of the kingdom, one day, when hunting, he met in the forest his brother, Arthgallo, who had been deposed.", "왕국을 차지한 지 다섯 해째 되던 어느 날, 그는 사냥을 하다가 숲에서 폐위된 형 아르트갈로를 만났다."], ["After long wandering, unable longer to bear the poverty to which he was reduced, he had returned to Britain, with only ten followers, designing to repair to those who had formerly been his friends.", "오랜 방랑 끝에, 자신이 처한 가난을 더는 견딜 수 없게 된 형은, 예전에 자신의 벗이었던 이들을 찾아갈 작정으로 오로지 열 명의 부하만을 거느리고 브리튼으로 돌아와 있었던 것이다."], ["Elidure, at the sight of his brother in distress, forgetting all animosities, ran to him, and embraced him.", "곤경에 처한 형을 보자 엘리두레는 모든 원한을 잊고 그에게 달려가 그를 껴안았다."], ["He took Arthgallo home with him, and concealed him in the palace.", "그는 아르트갈로를 자기 집으로 데려가 궁궐 안에 숨겨 두었다."], ["After this he feigned himself sick, and, calling his nobles about him, induced them, partly by persuasion, partly by force, to consent to his abdicating the kingdom, and reinstating his brother on the throne.", "그런 뒤에 그는 병든 척하며 귀족들을 곁으로 불러, 일부는 설득으로 일부는 강압으로, 자신이 왕위를 내려놓고 형을 다시 왕좌에 앉히는 데 동의하도록 이끌었다."], ["The agreement being ratified, Elidure took the crown from his own head, and put it on his brother’s head.", "이 합의가 확정되자, 엘리두레는 자기 머리에서 왕관을 벗어 형의 머리에 씌웠다."], ["Arthgallo after this reigned ten years, well and wisely, exercising strict justice towards all men.", "아르트갈로는 그 뒤로 열 해 동안 훌륭하고 지혜롭게 다스리며, 만인에게 엄정한 정의를 베풀었다."]], [["He died, and left the kingdom to his sons, who reigned with various fortunes, but were not long-lived, and left no offspring, so that Elidure was again advanced to the throne, and finished the course of his life in just and virtuous actions, receiving the name of the pious, from the love and admiration of his subjects.", "그가 죽으며 왕국을 아들들에게 남기니, 그들은 갖가지 운명 속에서 다스렸으나 오래 살지 못했고 자손을 남기지 못했다. 그리하여 엘리두레가 다시 왕좌에 올라, 정의롭고 덕 있는 행실로 생애를 마쳤으며, 신민들의 사랑과 흠모로 인하여 경건한 자라는 이름을 얻었다."]], [["Wordsworth has taken the story of Artegal and Elidure for the subject of a poem, which is No. 2 of “Poems founded on the Affections.”", "워즈워스는 아테갈과 엘리두레의 이야기를 한 편의 시의 소재로 삼았는데, 그것은 「애정에 바탕을 둔 시편」의 제2번이다."]], [["After Elidure, the Chronicle names many kings, but none of special note, till we come to Lud, who greatly enlarged Trinovant, his capital, and surrounded it with a wall.", "엘리두레의 뒤로 연대기는 여러 왕의 이름을 들지만, 특별히 주목할 만한 이는 없다가, 마침내 루드에 이른다. 그는 자신의 수도 트리노반트를 크게 넓히고 그 둘레에 성벽을 둘렀다."], ["He changed its name, bestowing upon it his own, so that henceforth it was called Lud’s town, afterwards London.", "그는 도시의 이름을 바꾸어 자신의 이름을 붙였으니, 그때부터 그곳은 루드의 도시라 불렸고, 훗날 런던이 되었다."], ["Lud was buried by the gate of the city called after him Ludgate.", "루드는 자신의 이름을 따 루드게이트라 불린 도시의 성문 곁에 묻혔다."], ["He had two sons, but they were not old enough at the time of their father’s death to sustain the cares of government, and therefore their uncle, Caswallaun, or Cassibellaunus, succeeded to the kingdom.", "그에게는 두 아들이 있었으나, 아버지가 죽을 무렵 그들은 통치의 짐을 떠맡기에는 아직 나이가 차지 않았으므로, 그들의 숙부 카스왈라운, 곧 카시벨라우누스가 왕위를 이었다."], ["He was a brave and magnificent prince, so that his fame reached to distant countries.", "그는 용맹하고 위풍당당한 군주여서, 그의 명성이 먼 나라들에까지 이르렀다."]], [["About this time it happened (as is found in the Roman histories) that Julius Cæsar, having subdued Gaul, came to the shore opposite Britain.", "이 무렵에 (로마의 역사서에 나오듯이) 갈리아를 굴복시킨 율리우스 카이사르가 브리튼 맞은편 해안에 이르는 일이 있었다."], ["And having resolved to add this island also to his conquests, he prepared ships and transported his army across the sea, to the mouth of the River Thames.", "그리고 이 섬마저 자신의 정복지에 더하기로 결심한 그는, 함선을 마련하여 군대를 바다 건너 템스 강 어귀로 실어 날랐다."], ["Here he was met by Cassibellaun with all his forces, and a battle ensued, in which Nennius, the brother of Cassibellaun, engaged in single combat with Cæsar.", "여기서 그는 자신의 전군을 거느린 카시벨라운과 맞닥뜨려 전투가 벌어졌는데, 그 가운데 카시벨라운의 동생 네니우스가 카이사르와 일대일로 맞붙었다."], ["After several furious blows given and received, the sword of Cæsar stuck so fast in the shield of Nennius that it could not be pulled out, and the combatants being separated by the intervention of the troops Nennius remained possessed of this trophy.", "사납게 주고받은 몇 차례의 가격 끝에, 카이사르의 칼이 네니우스의 방패에 너무도 단단히 박혀 빠지지 않게 되었고, 군대가 끼어들어 둘이 갈라지게 되자 네니우스는 이 전리품을 차지하게 되었다."], ["At last, after the greater part of the day was spent, the Britons poured in so fast that Cæsar was forced to retire to his camp and fleet.", "마침내 하루의 대부분이 지났을 무렵, 브리튼인들이 어찌나 거세게 밀려들었던지 카이사르는 자신의 진지와 함대로 물러날 수밖에 없었다."], ["And finding it useless to continue the war any longer at that time, he returned to Gaul.", "그리고 그때 더는 전쟁을 이어 가는 것이 소용없음을 깨닫고 갈리아로 돌아갔다."]], [["Shakspeare alludes to Cassibellaunus, in “Cymbeline”:", "셰익스피어는 「심벨린」에서 카시벨라우누스를 다음과 같이 언급한다."]], [["“The famed Cassibelan, who was once at point (O giglot fortune!) to master Cæsar’s sword, Made Lud’s town with rejoicing fires bright, And Britons strut with courage.”", "\"이름 높은 카시벨란은, 한때 (오, 변덕스러운 운명이여!) 카이사르의 칼을 제압할 뻔하여, 루드의 도시를 기쁨의 횃불로 환히 밝히고 브리튼인들로 하여금 용기로 으스대게 하였다.\""]], [["# KYMBELINUS, OR CYMBELINE", "# KYMBELINUS, OR CYMBELINE"]], [["Cæsar, on a second invasion of the island, was more fortunate, and compelled the Britons to pay tribute.", "카이사르는 두 번째로 이 섬을 침공했을 때 더 큰 행운을 얻어, 브리튼인들에게 조공을 바치도록 강요했다."], ["Cymbeline, the nephew of the king, was delivered to the Romans as a hostage for the faithful fulfilment of the treaty, and, being carried to Rome by Cæsar, he was there brought up in the Roman arts and accomplishments.", "왕의 조카 심벨린은 그 조약이 충실히 이행되리라는 볼모로 로마인들에게 넘겨졌고, 카이사르에게 이끌려 로마로 가서 그곳에서 로마의 기예와 교양을 익히며 자랐다."], ["Being afterwards restored to his country, and placed on the throne, he was attached to the Romans, and continued through all his reign at peace with them.", "훗날 제 나라로 돌아와 왕좌에 오른 그는 로마인들에게 호의를 품었고, 치세 내내 그들과 평화를 유지했다."], ["His sons, Guiderius and Arviragus, who made their appearance in Shakspeare’s play of “Cymbeline,” succeeded their father, and, refusing to pay tribute to the Romans, brought on another invasion.", "셰익스피어의 희곡 「심벨린」에 등장하는 그의 아들들 기데리우스와 아르비라구스가 아버지의 뒤를 이었는데, 그들은 로마인들에게 조공 바치기를 거부하여 또 한 번의 침공을 불러왔다."], ["Guiderius was slain, but Arviragus afterward made terms with the Romans, and reigned prosperously many years.", "기데리우스는 죽임을 당했으나, 아르비라구스는 훗날 로마인들과 화친을 맺고 여러 해 동안 번영을 누리며 다스렸다."]], [["The next event of note is the conquest and colonization of Armorica, by Maximus, a Roman general, and Conan, lord of Miniadoc or Denbigh-land, in Wales.", "다음으로 주목할 만한 사건은, 로마 장군 막시무스와 웨일스의 미니아도크 곧 덴비 땅의 영주 코난에 의한 아르모리카의 정복과 식민이다."], ["The name of the country was changed to Brittany, or Lesser Britain; and so completely was it possessed by the British colonists, that the language became assimilated to that spoken in Wales, and it is said that to this day the peasantry of the two countries can understand each other when speaking their native language.", "그 나라의 이름은 브르타뉴, 곧 작은 브리튼으로 바뀌었으며, 브리튼 식민자들이 어찌나 완전히 그곳을 차지했던지 그 언어가 웨일스에서 쓰이는 말과 비슷해졌고, 오늘날까지도 두 나라의 농민들이 제 고유의 언어로 말할 때 서로 알아들을 수 있다고 한다."]], [["The Romans eventually succeeded in establishing themselves in the island, and after the lapse of several generations they became blended with the natives so that no distinction existed between the two races.", "로마인들은 마침내 이 섬에 자리를 잡는 데 성공했으며, 여러 세대가 흐르는 동안 원주민들과 뒤섞여 두 종족 사이에 아무런 구별도 남지 않게 되었다."], ["When at length the Roman armies were withdrawn from Britain, their departure was a matter of regret to the inhabitants, as it left them without protection against the barbarous tribes, Scots, Picts, and Norwegians, who harassed the country incessantly.", "마침내 로마 군대가 브리튼에서 철수했을 때, 그 떠남은 주민들에게 아쉬운 일이었으니, 끊임없이 나라를 괴롭히던 야만의 부족들, 곧 스코트족과 픽트족과 노르웨이인들에 맞설 보호 없이 그들을 남겨 두었기 때문이다."], ["This was the state of things when the era of King Arthur began.", "아서 왕의 시대가 시작된 것은 바로 이러한 형편에서였다."]], [["The adventure of Albion, the giant, with Hercules is alluded to by Spenser, “Faery Queene,”", "거인 알비온이 헤라클레스와 겪은 모험은 스펜서의 「선녀 여왕」"], ["Book IV., Canto xi:", "제4권 제11편에서 언급되어 있다."]], [["“For Albion the son of Neptune was; Who for the proof of his great puissance, Out of his Albion did on dry foot pass Into old Gaul that now is cleped France, To fight with Hercules, that did advance To vanquish all the world with matchless might: And there his mortal part by great mischance Was slain.”", "\"알비온은 넵투누스의 아들이었으니, 그의 크나큰 힘을 입증하고자 제 알비온을 떠나 마른 발로 건너가 이제는 프랑스라 불리는 옛 갈리아로 들어가 헤라클레스와 싸우려 하였더라. 헤라클레스는 견줄 데 없는 위력으로 온 세상을 정복하려 나섰던 터라, 거기서 그의 필멸의 육신은 큰 불운으로 죽임을 당하였더라.\""], ["————", "————"]], [["MERLIN was the son of no mortal father, but of an Incubus, one of a class of beings not absolutely wicked, but far from good, who inhabit the regions of the air.", "멀린은 필멸의 아버지에게서 태어난 것이 아니라, 인쿠부스의 아들이었다. 인쿠부스란 절대적으로 사악하지는 않으나 결코 선하지도 않은 부류의 존재로, 대기의 영역에 거하는 자들이었다."], ["Merlin’s mother was a virtuous young woman, who, on the birth of her son, intrusted him to a priest, who hurried him to the baptismal fount, and so saved him from sharing the lot of his father, though he retained many marks of his unearthly origin.", "멀린의 어머니는 정숙한 젊은 여인이었는데, 아들이 태어나자 그를 한 사제에게 맡겼고, 사제는 서둘러 그를 세례반으로 데려갔다. 그리하여 멀린은 아버지의 운명을 함께하는 일을 면했으나, 그래도 이 세상의 것이 아닌 출생의 흔적을 여럿 지니고 있었다."]], [["At this time Vortigern reigned in Britain.", "이 무렵 브리튼에서는 보르티게른이 다스리고 있었다."], ["He was a usurper, who had caused the death of his sovereign, Moines, and driven the two brothers of the late king, whose names were Uther and Pendragon, into banishment.", "그는 찬탈자로서, 자기 군주였던 모이네스를 죽음에 이르게 하고, 선왕의 두 형제, 곧 우테르와 펜드래건이라는 자들을 추방한 인물이었다."], ["Vortigern, who lived in constant fear of the return of the rightful heirs of the kingdom, began to erect a strong tower for defence.", "보르티게른은 왕국의 정당한 후계자들이 돌아오지 않을까 늘 두려워하며 살았던 까닭에, 방어를 위해 견고한 탑을 세우기 시작했다."], ["The edifice, when brought by the workmen to a certain height, three times fell to the ground, without any apparent cause.", "그런데 이 건축물은 일꾼들이 일정한 높이까지 쌓아 올리면, 뚜렷한 까닭도 없이 세 번이나 땅으로 무너져 내렸다."], ["The king consulted his astrologers on this wonderful event, and learned from them that it would be necessary to bathe the corner-stone of the foundation with the blood of a child born without a mortal father.", "왕은 이 기이한 사건을 두고 점성술사들에게 자문을 구했고, 그들에게서 필멸의 아버지 없이 태어난 아이의 피로 토대의 주춧돌을 적셔야 한다는 답을 들었다."]], [["In search of such an infant, Vortigern sent his messengers all over the kingdom, and they by accident discovered Merlin, whose lineage seemed to point him out as the individual wanted.", "보르티게른은 그러한 아이를 찾아 왕국 곳곳으로 사자들을 보냈고, 그들은 우연히 멀린을 발견했다. 멀린의 혈통이 바로 그가 찾던 그 사람임을 가리키는 듯했다."], ["They took him to the king; but Merlin, young as he was, explained to the king the absurdity of attempting to rescue the fabric by such means, for he told him the true cause of the instability of the tower was its being placed over the den of two immense dragons, whose combats shook the earth above them.", "그들은 그를 왕에게 데려갔다. 그러나 멀린은 비록 어렸음에도, 그런 방법으로 건물을 구하려는 시도가 얼마나 터무니없는지를 왕에게 설명했다."], ["The king ordered his workmen to dig beneath the tower, and when they had done so they discovered two enormous serpents, the one white as milk, the other red as fire.", "그는 탑이 불안정한 진짜 원인이 탑 아래 거대한 용 두 마리의 굴이 있는 데 있으며, 그것들이 싸울 때마다 그 위의 대지가 흔들리는 것이라고 일러 주었다. 왕은 일꾼들에게 탑 아래를 파라고 명했고, 그들이 그렇게 하자 거대한 뱀 두 마리가 모습을 드러냈다."], ["The multitude looked on with amazement, till the serpents, slowly rising from their den, and expanding their enormous folds, began the combat, when every one fled in terror, except Merlin, who stood by clapping his hands and cheering on the conflict.", "하나는 젖빛처럼 희었고, 다른 하나는 불처럼 붉었다. 군중은 놀라움에 차서 지켜보았고, 마침내 뱀들이 굴에서 천천히 솟아올라 그 거대한 몸을 펼치며 싸움을 시작하자, 멀린만을 제외하고는 모두가 공포에 질려 달아났다."], ["The red dragon was slain, and the white one, gliding through a cleft in the rock, disappeared.", "멀린은 그 곁에 서서 손뼉을 치며 싸움을 응원했다. 붉은 용은 죽임을 당했고, 흰 용은 바위 틈새로 미끄러져 들어가 사라졌다."]], [["These animals typified, as Merlin afterwards explained, the invasion of Uther and Pendragon, the rightful princes, who soon after landed with a great army.", "이 짐승들은, 멀린이 훗날 설명한 바에 따르면, 정당한 왕자들인 우테르와 펜드래건의 침공을 상징하는 것이었다. 그 두 사람은 얼마 지나지 않아 대군을 이끌고 상륙했다."], ["Vortigern was defeated, and afterwards burned alive in the castle he had taken such pains to construct.", "보르티게른은 패배했고, 그가 그토록 애써 세운 성에서 산 채로 불태워졌다."], ["On the death of Vortigern, Pendragon ascended the throne.", "보르티게른이 죽은 뒤 펜드래건이 왕좌에 올랐다."], ["Merlin became his chief adviser, and often assisted the king by his magical arts.", "멀린은 그의 으뜸가는 조언자가 되었으며, 종종 자신의 마법으로 왕을 도왔다."]], [["“Merlin, who knew the range of all their arts, Had built the King his havens, ships and halls.”", "\"멀린은 온갖 마법의 영역을 모두 알고 있어, 왕을 위해 항구와 배와 궁전을 지어 주었네.\""], ["—Vivian.", "— 「비비안」"]], [["Among other endowments, he had the power of transforming himself into any shape he pleased.", "다른 여러 재능 가운데서도 멀린은 자기가 원하는 어떤 모습으로든 변신할 수 있는 힘을 지니고 있었다."], ["At one time he appeared as a dwarf, at others as a damsel, a page, or even a greyhound or a stag.", "어느 때에는 난쟁이로 나타났고, 다른 때에는 처녀로, 시동으로, 심지어 그레이하운드나 수사슴으로도 나타났다."], ["This faculty he often employed for the service of the king, and sometimes also for the diversion of the court and the sovereign.", "그는 이 능력을 종종 왕을 섬기는 데 썼고, 때로는 궁정과 군주를 즐겁게 하는 데에도 썼다."]], [["Merlin continued to be a favorite counsellor through the reigns of Pendragon, Uther, and Arthur, and at last disappeared from view, and was no more found among men, through the treachery of his mistress, Viviane, the Fairy, which happened in this wise.", "멀린은 펜드래건과 우테르, 그리고 아서의 치세를 통틀어 총애받는 조언자였으나, 마침내 사람들의 시야에서 사라져 다시는 인간들 사이에서 발견되지 않았다. 이는 그의 연인이었던 요정 비비안의 배신 때문이었으며, 그 일은 다음과 같이 일어났다."]], [["Merlin, having become enamoured of the fair Viviane, the Lady of the Lake, was weak enough to impart to her various important secrets of his art, being impelled by fatal destiny, of which he was at the same time fully aware.", "멀린은 호수의 여인 아름다운 비비안에게 반한 나머지, 어리석게도 자기 마법의 여러 중요한 비밀들을 그녀에게 전해 주었다. 이는 그가 거역할 수 없는 운명에 떠밀린 탓이었으나, 그 자신도 그 운명을 동시에 충분히 알고 있었다."], ["The lady, however, was not content with his devotion, unbounded as it seems to have been, but “cast about,”", "그러나 그 여인은, 끝이 없어 보일 만큼 헌신적인 그의 사랑에도 만족하지 못하고, 로맨스가 우리에게 전하는 바에 따르면, 어떻게 하면 \"그를 영영 붙들어 둘\" 수 있을지 \"궁리하기\""], ["the Romance tells us, how she might “detain him for evermore,”", "시작했다. 그러던 어느 날 그녀는 그에게 이렇게 말을 건넸다."], ["and one day addressed him in these terms: “Sir, I would that we should make a fair place and a suitable, so contrived by art and by cunning that it might never be undone, and that you and I should be there in joy and solace.”", "\"기사님, 저는 우리가 아름답고 알맞은 장소 하나를 마련했으면 합니다. 기예와 솜씨로 지어 결코 허물어지지 않게 하고, 당신과 제가 그 안에서 기쁨과 위안을 누렸으면 합니다.\""], ["“My lady,”", "\"여인이여,\" 멀린이 말했다."], ["said Merlin, “I will do all this.”", "\"이 모든 것을 내가 해 드리리다.\" \"기사님,\""], ["“Sir,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “I would not have you do it, but you shall teach me, and I will do it, and then it will be more to my mind.”", "\"저는 당신이 그것을 하시는 걸 바라지 않습니다. 다만 제게 가르쳐 주시면, 제가 그것을 하겠습니다."], ["“I grant you this,”", "그러면 더욱 제 마음에 들 것입니다.\" \"그대에게 그리 하도록 허락하겠소.\""], ["said Merlin.", "멀린이 말했다."], ["Then he began to devise, and the damsel put it all in writing.", "그리하여 그는 궁리하기 시작했고, 처녀는 그것을 모두 글로 적었다."], ["And when he had devised the whole, then had the damsel full great joy, and showed him greater semblance of love than she had ever before made, and they sojourned together a long while.", "그가 모든 것을 궁리해 내자, 처녀는 더없이 큰 기쁨에 차서, 일찍이 보인 적 없는 더한 사랑의 모습을 그에게 드러냈다. 그리고 두 사람은 오랫동안 함께 머물렀다."], ["At length it fell out that, as they were going one day hand in hand through the forest of Brécéliande, they found a bush of white-thorn, which was laden with flowers; and they seated themselves under the shade of this white-thorn, upon the green grass, and Merlin laid his head upon the damsel’s lap, and fell asleep.", "마침내 어느 날, 그들이 손을 맞잡고 브레셀리앙드 숲을 거닐던 차에, 꽃이 흐드러지게 핀 산사나무 한 그루를 발견했다. 그들은 그 산사나무 그늘 아래 푸른 풀밭에 앉았고, 멀린은 처녀의 무릎에 머리를 누이고 잠이 들었다."], ["Then the damsel rose, and made a ring with her wimple round the bush, and round Merlin, and began her enchantments, such as he himself had taught her; and nine times she made the ring, and nine times she made the enchantment, and then she went and sat down by him, and placed his head again upon her lap.", "그러자 처녀가 일어나 자기 머릿수건으로 그 나무와 멀린을 둘러 고리를 만들고는, 멀린 자신이 그녀에게 가르쳐 준 그 주문을 외기 시작했다. 아홉 번 고리를 만들고 아홉 번 주문을 걸었으며, 그런 다음 그의 곁으로 가서 앉아 그의 머리를 다시 자기 무릎 위에 올려놓았다."]], [["“And a sleep Fell upon Merlin more like death, so deep Her finger on her lips; then Vivian rose, And from her brown-locked head the wimple throws, And takes it in her hand and waves it over The blossomed thorn tree and her sleeping lover.", "\"그리고 잠이 멀린에게 내렸으니, 죽음과도 같이 깊었다. 그녀의 손가락은 입술에 닿았고, 비비안이 일어나 갈색 머리 두른 머리에서 머릿수건을 벗어 손에 쥐고는 그것을 흔들었다."], ["Nine times she waved the fluttering wimple round, And made a little plot of magic ground.”", "꽃 핀 산사나무와 잠든 연인 위로. 아홉 번 그녀는 펄럭이는 머릿수건을 둘러 흔들었고, 작은 마법의 땅 한 자락을 만들어 냈다.\""], ["—Matthew Arnold.", "— 매슈 아널드"]], [["And when he awoke, and looked round him, it seemed to him that he was enclosed in the strongest tower in the world, and laid upon a fair bed.", "그가 깨어나 주위를 둘러보았을 때, 그는 세상에서 가장 견고한 탑 안에 갇혀 아름다운 침상 위에 누워 있는 듯했다. 그러자 그가 그 여인에게 말했다."], ["Then said he to the dame: “My lady, you have deceived me, unless you abide with me, for no one hath power to unmake this tower but you alone.”", "\"여인이여, 그대가 나와 함께 머물지 않는 한, 그대는 나를 속인 것이오. 그대 말고는 어느 누구도 이 탑을 허물 힘이 없으니 말이오.\""], ["She then promised she would be often there, and in this she held her covenant with him.", "그러자 그녀는 자주 그곳에 있겠노라 약속했고, 그 점에서는 그와 맺은 언약을 지켰다."], ["And Merlin never went out of that tower where his Mistress Viviane had enclosed him; but she entered and went out again when she listed.", "그리고 멀린은 자기 연인 비비안이 가둔 그 탑에서 결코 나오지 못했으나, 그녀는 마음 내킬 때마다 드나들었다."]], [["After this event Merlin was never more known to hold converse with any mortal but Viviane, except on one occasion.", "이 일이 있은 뒤로 멀린이 비비안 말고 다른 어떤 필멸자와 이야기를 나누었다고 알려진 적은 단 한 번을 제외하고는 없었다."], ["Arthur, having for some time missed him from his court, sent several of his knights in search of him, and, among the number, Sir Gawain, who met with a very unpleasant adventure while engaged in this quest.", "아서는 한동안 궁정에서 그가 보이지 않자, 여러 기사를 보내 그를 찾게 했는데, 그 가운데 가웨인 경도 있었다. 가웨인은 이 탐색에 나섰다가 매우 불쾌한 모험을 겪었다."], ["Happening to pass a damsel on his road, and neglecting to salute her, she revenged herself for his incivility by transforming him into a hideous dwarf.", "길에서 한 처녀를 우연히 지나치며 그녀에게 인사하기를 소홀히 했더니, 그녀가 그 무례함에 앙갚음하여 그를 흉측한 난쟁이로 변신시켰던 것이다."], ["He was bewailing aloud his evil fortune as he went through the forest of Brécéliande, when suddenly he heard the voice of one groaning on his right hand; and, looking that way, he could see nothing save a kind of smoke, which seemed like air, and through which he could not pass.", "그가 브레셀리앙드 숲을 지나며 제 불운을 소리 높여 한탄하고 있을 때, 문득 오른편에서 누군가 신음하는 목소리가 들렸다. 그쪽을 바라보니, 공기처럼 보이며 뚫고 지날 수 없는 일종의 연기 외에는 아무것도 보이지 않았다."], ["Merlin then addressed him from out the smoke, and told him by what misadventure he was imprisoned there.", "그러자 멀린이 그 연기 속에서 그에게 말을 건네며, 자기가 어떤 불운으로 그곳에 갇히게 되었는지 일러 주었다. \"아, 기사여!\""], ["“Ah, sir!”", "그가 덧붙였다."], ["he added, “you will never see me more, and that grieves me, but I cannot remedy it; I shall never more speak to you, nor to any other person, save only my mistress.", "\"그대는 다시는 나를 보지 못할 것이오. 그것이 나를 슬프게 하나, 나로서는 어찌할 도리가 없소."], ["But do thou hasten to King Arthur, and charge him from me to undertake, without delay, the quest of the Sacred Graal.", "나는 다시는 그대에게도, 내 연인 말고 다른 어느 누구에게도 말하지 못할 것이오. 다만 그대는 어서 아서 왕에게로 가서, 내 뜻을 전하여 지체 없이 성배(聖杯)의 탐색에 나서도록 명하라 하시오."], ["The knight is already born, and has received knighthood at his hands, who is destined to accomplish this quest.”", "이 탐색을 이루어 낼 운명을 지닌 기사는 이미 태어났고, 왕의 손에서 기사 서임을 받았소.\""], ["And after this he comforted Gawain under his transformation, assuring him that he should speedily be disenchanted; and he predicted to him that he should find the king at Carduel, in Wales, on his return, and that all the other knights who had been on like quest would arrive there the same day as himself.", "그러고 나서 그는 변신한 가웨인을 위로하며, 그가 곧 마법에서 풀려나리라고 장담했다. 또한 그는 가웨인이 돌아가는 길에 웨일스의 카르두엘에서 왕을 만나게 될 것이며, 같은 탐색에 나섰던 다른 모든 기사들도 그와 같은 날 그곳에 이르리라고 예언했다."], ["And all this came to pass as Merlin had said.", "그리고 이 모든 일은 멀린이 말한 그대로 이루어졌다."]], [["Merlin is frequently introduced in the tales of chivalry, but it is chiefly on great occasions, and at a period subsequent to his death, or magical disappearance.", "멀린은 기사도 이야기에 자주 등장하지만, 그것은 주로 큰 사건이 일어날 때, 그리고 그가 죽거나 마법으로 사라진 뒤의 시기에 한해서다."], ["In the romantic poems of Italy, and in Spenser, Merlin is chiefly represented as a magical artist.", "이탈리아의 낭만 서사시와 스펜서의 작품에서 멀린은 주로 마법 기예가로 그려진다."], ["Spenser represents him as the artificer of the impenetrable shield and other armor of Prince Arthur (“Faery Queene,”", "스펜서는 그를 아서 왕자의 뚫리지 않는 방패와 그 밖의 갑옷을 만든 장인으로(「요정 여왕」"], ["Book I., Canto vii.), and of a mirror, in which a damsel viewed her lover’s shade. The Fountain of Love, in the “Orlando Innamorata,”", "제1권 제7곡), 또 어느 처녀가 자기 연인의 모습을 들여다본 거울의 제작자로 그린다."], ["is described as his work; and in the poem of “Ariosto” we are told of a hall adorned with prophetic paintings, which demons had executed in a single night, under the direction of Merlin.", "「사랑에 빠진 오를란도」에 나오는 사랑의 샘은 그의 솜씨로 묘사되며, 「아리오스토」의 시에서는 멀린의 지휘 아래 악마들이 단 하룻밤 만에 완성했다는, 예언적 그림들로 장식된 어느 홀의 이야기가 전해진다."]], [["The following legend is from Spenser’s “Faery Queene,”", "다음 전설은 스펜서의 「요정 여왕」"], ["Book III., Canto iii.:", "제3권 제3곡에서 가져온 것이다."]], [["CAER-MERDIN, OR CAERMARTHEN (IN WALES), MERLIN’S TOWER, AND THE IMPRISONED FIENDS.", "카이르메르딘, 곧 카이르마던(웨일스 소재), 멀린의 탑, 그리고 갇힌 마귀들."]], [["“Forthwith themselves disguising both, in straunge And base attire, that none might them bewray, To Maridunum, that is now by chaunge Of name Caer-Merdin called, they took their way: There the wise Merlin whylome wont (they say) To make his wonne, low underneath the ground In a deep delve, far from the view of day, That of no living wight he mote be found, Whenso he counselled with his sprights encompassed round.", "\"곧바로 두 사람은 낯설고 허름한 옷차림으로 제 모습을 감추어, 누구도 알아보지 못하게 하고, 이름이 바뀌어 이제는 카이르메르딘이라 불리는 마리두눔으로 길을 떠났다. 그곳에서 현명한 멀린은 (사람들 말로는) 일찍이 거처를 마련하곤 했다는데, 땅속 깊이 낮 빛이 닿지 않는 깊은 굴속에서였다. 살아 있는 어느 누구에게도 발견되지 않으려 함이었으니, 그가 제 정령들에 둘러싸여 의논을 나눌 적에 말이다."]], [["“And if thou ever happen that same way To travel, go to see that dreadful place; It is a hideous hollow cave (they say) Under a rock that lies a little space From the swift Barry, tombling down apace Amongst the woody hills of Dynevor; But dare not thou, I charge, in any case, To enter into that same baleful bower, For fear the cruel fiends should thee unwares devour.", "\"그리고 그대가 언젠가 그 길로 여행할 일이 생기거든, 가서 그 무시무시한 곳을 보라. 그것은 (사람들 말로는) 흉측하게 텅 빈 동굴로, 디네보르의 숲 우거진 언덕들 사이로 급히 쏟아져 내리는 빠른 배리강에서 얼마 안 떨어진 바위 아래 자리하고 있다. 허나, 내 그대에게 이르노니, 어떤 일이 있어도 그 흉흉한 거처 안으로 들어서지는 말라. 잔혹한 마귀들이 그대를 불시에 집어삼킬까 두려우니."]], [["“But standing high aloft, low lay thine ear, And there such ghastly noise of iron chains And brazen cauldrons thou shalt rumbling hear, Which thousand sprites with long enduring pains Do toss, that it will stun thy feeble brains; And oftentimes great groans, and grievous stounds, When too huge toil and labor them constrains; And oftentimes loud strokes and ringing sounds From under that deep rock most horribly rebounds.", "\"다만 높이 우뚝 서서 귀를 낮게 기울이라. 그러면 그곳에서 쇠사슬과 놋쇠 가마솥이 부딪치는 무시무시한 소리가 우르릉 들려오리니, 오랜 세월 고통에 시달리는 수천의 정령들이 그것들을 내던지는 소리라, 그대의 연약한 머릿속을 어지럽힐 것이다. 또 이따금 큰 신음과 비통한 비명이 들리리니, 너무도 거대한 노역과 고역이 그들을 짓누를 때이며, 또 이따금 요란한 타격음과 쟁쟁 울리는 소리가 저 깊은 바위 밑에서 더없이 끔찍하게 되울려 나온다."]], [["“The cause some say is this.", "\"그 까닭을 두고 어떤 이들은 이렇게 말한다."], ["A little while Before that Merlin died, he did intend A brazen wall in compas to compile About Caermerdin, and did it commend Unto these sprites to bring to perfect end; During which work the Lady of the Lake, Whom long he loved, for him in haste did send; Who, thereby forced his workmen to forsake, Them bound till his return their labor not to slack.", "멀린이 죽기 얼마 전, 그는 카이르메르딘 둘레에 놋쇠 벽을 빙 둘러 쌓아 올리려 마음먹었고, 그 일을 이 정령들에게 맡겨 완성토록 명했다. 그 일을 하는 동안, 그가 오래도록 사랑했던 호수의 여인이 그를 급히 불러들였고, 그리하여 그는 일꾼들을 떠나도록 할 수밖에 없었으되, 그들에게 자기가 돌아올 때까지 노역을 늦추지 말라 묶어 두었다."]], [["“In the mean time, through that false lady’s train, He was surprised, and buried under beare,[40] Ne ever to his work returned again; Nathless those fiends may not their work forbear, So greatly his commandëment they fear; But there do toil and travail day and night, Until that brazen wall they up do rear.", "\"그러는 사이, 그 거짓된 여인의 술책에 빠져 그는 불시에 사로잡혀 관 아래 묻혔고,[40] 다시는 제 일로 돌아오지 못했다. 그럼에도 저 마귀들은 그 일을 멈추지 못하니, 그의 명령을 그토록 크게 두려워하는 까닭이다."], ["For Merlin had in magic more insight Than ever him before or after living wight.”", "그리하여 그들은 밤낮으로 애쓰며 고역에 시달리는데, 저 놋쇠 벽을 다 쌓아 올릴 때까지 그러하리라. 멀린은 그 전에도 그 후에도 살았던 어느 누구보다도 마법에 더 깊은 통찰을 지니고 있었기 때문이다.\""]], [["————", "————"]], [["WE shall begin our history of King Arthur by giving those particulars of his life which appear to rest on historical evidence; and then proceed to record those legends concerning him which form the earliest portion of British literature.", "햐 아서 왕에 대한 우리의 이야기는, 역사적 증거에 근거한다고 여겨지는 그의 생애에 관한 세세한 사실들을 먼저 제시하는 것으로 시작할 것이다. 그런 다음 그에 관한 전설들, 곧 브리튼 문학의 가장 이른 시기를 이루는 그 전설들을 기록하는 것으로 나아갈 것이다."]], [["Arthur was a prince of the tribe of Britons called Silures, whose country was South Wales, the son of Uther, named Pendragon, a title given to an elective sovereign, paramount over the many kings of Britain.", "아서는 실루레스라고 불리던 브리튼족 부족의 군주였는데, 그 부족의 영토는 남웨일스였으며, 그는 펜드래곤이라고 불린 우서의 아들이었다. 펜드래곤이란 브리튼의 수많은 왕들 위에 군림하는 선출직 군주에게 부여된 칭호였다."], ["He appears to have commenced his martial career about the year 500, and was raised to the Pendragonship about ten years later.", "그는 서기 500년경에 무인으로서의 경력을 시작한 것으로 보이며, 그로부터 약 10년 뒤에 펜드래곤의 지위에 올랐다."], ["He is said to have gained twelve victories over the Saxons.", "그는 색슨족을 상대로 열두 번의 승리를 거두었다고 전한다."], ["The most important of them was that of Badon, by some supposed to be Bath, by others Berkshire.", "그 가운데 가장 중요한 것이 바돈 전투였는데, 어떤 이들은 그곳을 바스로, 또 어떤 이들은 버크셔로 추정한다."], ["This was the last of his battles with the Saxons, and checked their progress so effectually, that Arthur experienced no more annoyance from them, and reigned in peace, until the revolt of his nephew Modred, twenty years later, which led to the fatal battle of Camlan, in Cornwall, in 542.", "이것이 색슨족과 벌인 그의 마지막 전투였으며, 그들의 진격을 어찌나 효과적으로 막아 냈던지 아서는 더 이상 그들에게 시달리지 않았고, 20년 뒤 그의 조카 모드레드가 반란을 일으킬 때까지 평화롭게 다스렸다. 그 반란은 542년에 콘월의 캄란에서 벌어진 치명적인 전투로 이어졌다."], ["Modred was slain, and Arthur, mortally wounded, was conveyed by sea to Glastonbury, where he died, and was buried.", "모드레드는 죽임을 당했고, 아서는 치명상을 입은 채 바닷길로 글래스턴베리에 옮겨졌으며, 그곳에서 숨을 거두고 묻혔다."], ["Tradition preserved the memory of the place of his interment within the abbey, as we are told by Giraldus Cambrensis, who was present when the grave was opened by command of Henry II.", "전설은 그가 매장된 장소가 그 수도원 안에 있다는 기억을 보존해 왔는데, 이는 기랄두스 캄브렌시스가 우리에게 전해 주는 바와 같다."], ["about 1150, and saw the bones and sword of the monarch, and a leaden cross let into his tombstone, with the inscription in rude Roman letters, “Here lies buried the famous King Arthur, in the island Avalonia.”", "그는 1150년경 헨리 2세의 명령으로 무덤이 열렸을 때 그 자리에 있었으며, 그 군주의 유골과 검을 보았고, 그의 묘비에 박혀 있던 납 십자가도 보았는데, 거기에는 투박한 로마 문자로 \"여기 저 유명한 아서 왕이 아발로니아섬에 묻혀 있노라\"라는 글귀가 새겨져 있었다."], ["This story has been elegantly versified by Warton.", "이 이야기는 워턴에 의해 우아하게 운문으로 옮겨진 바 있다."], ["A popular traditional belief was long entertained among the Britons, that Arthur was not dead, but had been carried off to be healed of his wounds in Fairy-land, and that he would reappear to avenge his countrymen and reinstate them in the sovereignty of Britain.", "브리튼족 사이에서는 오랫동안 한 가지 통속적인 전설적 믿음이 품어져 왔으니, 곧 아서가 죽은 것이 아니라 그의 상처를 치유받기 위해 요정의 나라로 옮겨졌으며, 다시 나타나 자기 동족의 원수를 갚고 그들에게 브리튼의 주권을 되찾아 주리라는 것이었다."], ["In Warton’s “Ode” a bard relates to King Henry the traditional story of Arthur’s death, and closes with these lines.", "워턴의 \"송시\"에서는 한 음유시인이 헨리 왕에게 아서의 죽음에 관한 전설을 들려주며 다음과 같은 시구로 끝맺는다."]], [["“Yet in vain a paynim foe Armed with fate the mighty blow: For when he fell, the Elfin queen, All in secret and unseen, O’er the fainting hero threw Her mantle of ambrosial blue, And bade her spirits bear him far, In Merlin’s agate-axled car, To her green isle’s enamelled steep, Far in the navel of the deep.", "\"그러나 이교도 적이 운명을 무기 삼아 저 강력한 일격을 가한 것은 헛된 일이었으니, 그가 쓰러질 때, 요정의 여왕이 은밀하게 아무도 모르게, 기절한 영웅 위에 신들의 음식 같은 푸른 망토를 던지고, 자신의 정령들에게 그를 멀리 데려가라 명하니, 멀린의 마노 굴대를 단 수레에 태워, 깊은 바다의 한가운데 멀리 자리한 그녀의 푸른 섬, 그 윤기 도는 비탈로 향하였노라."], ["O’er his wounds she sprinkled dew From flowers that in Arabia grew.", "그의 상처 위에 그녀는 이슬을 뿌렸으니, 아라비아에서 자라난 꽃들에서 거둔 이슬이었노라."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], ["There he reigns a mighty king, Thence to Britain shall return, If right prophetic rolls I learn, Borne on victory’s spreading plume, His ancient sceptre to resume, His knightly table to restore, And brave the tournaments of yore.”", "그곳에서 그는 강대한 왕으로 군림하며, 그곳에서 브리튼으로 돌아오리라, 내가 예언의 두루마리를 옳게 읽었다면, 승리의 펼쳐진 깃털 위에 실려, 그의 오랜 왕홀을 되찾고, 그의 기사들의 원탁을 복원하며, 옛적의 마상 시합을 다시 펼치리라.\""]], [["After this narration another bard came forward who recited a different story:", "이 이야기가 끝난 뒤 또 다른 음유시인이 나서서 다른 이야기를 읊었다."]], [["“When Arthur bowed his haughty crest, No princess veiled in azure vest Snatched him, by Merlin’s powerful spell, In groves of golden bliss to dwell; But when he fell, with winged speed, His champions, on a milk-white steed, From the battle’s hurricane, Bore him to Joseph’s towered fane,[41] In the fair vale of Avalon; There, with chanted orison And the long blaze of tapers clear, The stoled fathers met the bier; Through the dim aisles, in order dread Of martial woe, the chief they led, And deep entombed in holy ground, Before the altar’s solemn bound.”", "\"아서가 그 도도한 머리를 숙였을 때, 푸른 옷을 두른 어떤 공주도 멀린의 강력한 주문으로 그를 낚아채 황금빛 축복의 숲에 살게 하지 않았노라. 다만 그가 쓰러졌을 때, 날개 단 듯 빠르게 그의 용사들이, 젖빛처럼 흰 군마에 태워, 전투의 폭풍 속에서 그를 들어 올려, 아름다운 아발론 골짜기에 자리한 요셉의 탑 솟은 성소로 옮겼노라.[41] 그곳에서, 영창하는 기도와 맑은 촛불들의 길게 타오르는 불빛 속에서, 제의를 걸친 신부들이 상여를 맞이하였노라. 어슴푸레한 회랑을 지나, 군사적 비탄의 엄숙한 행렬을 이루어, 그들은 그 수령을 인도하였고, 제단의 장엄한 경계 앞, 거룩한 땅속 깊이 그를 안장하였노라.\""]], [["It must not be concealed that the very existence of Arthur has been denied by some.", "아서의 존재 자체가 어떤 이들에 의해 부정되어 왔다는 사실을 숨겨서는 안 될 것이다."], ["Milton says of him: “As to Arthur, more renowned in songs and romances than in true stories, who he was, and whether ever any such reigned in Britain, hath been doubted heretofore, and may again, with good reason.”", "밀턴은 그에 대하여 이렇게 말한다. \"아서로 말하자면, 참된 역사에서보다 노래와 로맨스에서 더 이름난 인물이거니와, 그가 누구였는지, 그리고 과연 그러한 인물이 브리튼을 다스린 적이 있었는지는 지금까지도 의심받아 왔으며, 충분한 이유로 다시금 의심받을 만하다.\""], ["Modern critics, however, admit that there was a prince of this name, and find proof of it in the frequent mention of him in the writings of the Welsh bards.", "그러나 근대의 비평가들은 이러한 이름의 군주가 있었음을 인정하며, 웨일스 음유시인들의 글에 그가 자주 언급된다는 점에서 그 증거를 찾는다."], ["But the Arthur of romance, according to Mr. Owen, a Welsh scholar and antiquarian, is a mythological person.", "그렇지만 웨일스 학자이자 골동품 연구가인 오언 씨에 따르면, 로맨스의 아서는 신화적인 인물이다."], ["“Arthur,”", "그는 이렇게 말한다."], ["he says, “is the Great Bear, as the name literally implies (Arctos, Arcturus), and perhaps this constellation, being so near the pole, and visibly describing a circle in a small space, is the origin of the famous Round Table.”", "\"아서란 곧 큰곰자리이니, 그 이름이 글자 그대로 의미하는 바이다(아르크토스, 아르크투루스). 그리고 어쩌면 이 별자리가 극에 너무 가까이 있어 좁은 공간 안에서 눈에 띄게 원을 그리는 까닭에, 저 유명한 원탁의 기원이 된 것인지도 모른다.\""]], [["# KING ARTHUR", "# KING ARTHUR"]], [["Constans, king of Britain, had three sons, Moines, Ambrosius, otherwise called Uther, and Pendragon.", "브리튼의 왕 콘스탄스에게는 세 아들이 있었으니, 모이네스와, 달리 우서라고도 불린 암브로시우스와, 펜드래곤이었다."], ["Moines, soon after his accession to the crown, was vanquished by the Saxons, in consequence of the treachery of his seneschal, Vortigern, and growing unpopular, through misfortune, he was killed by his subjects, and the traitor Vortigern chosen in his place.", "모이네스는 왕위에 오른 지 얼마 지나지 않아 자신의 가신장 보티건의 배신으로 색슨족에게 패하였고, 불운을 통해 인기를 잃게 되자 신하들의 손에 죽임을 당했으며, 반역자 보티건이 그 자리에 선택되었다."]], [["Vortigern was soon after defeated in a great battle by Uther and Pendragon, the surviving brothers of Moines and Pendragon ascended the throne.", "보티건은 그 뒤 곧 모이네스의 살아남은 두 형제인 우서와 펜드래곤에게 큰 전투에서 패하였고, 펜드래곤이 왕위에 올랐다."]], [["This prince had great confidence in the wisdom of Merlin, and made him his chief adviser.", "이 군주는 멀린의 지혜를 크게 신뢰하여 그를 자신의 으뜸가는 조언자로 삼았다."], ["About this time a dreadful war arose between the Saxons and Britons.", "이 무렵 색슨족과 브리튼족 사이에 무서운 전쟁이 일어났다."], ["Merlin obliged the royal brothers to swear fidelity to each other, but predicted that one of them must fall in the first battle.", "멀린은 두 왕족 형제에게 서로 충실할 것을 맹세하게 하였으나, 그 가운데 하나가 첫 전투에서 쓰러지리라고 예언하였다."], ["The Saxons were routed, and Pendragon, being slain, was succeeded by Uther, who now assumed in addition to his own name the appellation of Pendragon.", "색슨족은 패주하였고, 펜드래곤은 죽임을 당하여, 우서가 그 뒤를 이었는데, 그는 이제 자신의 이름에 더하여 펜드래곤이라는 칭호를 함께 취하였다."]], [["Merlin still continued a favorite counsellor.", "멀린은 여전히 총애받는 조언자로 남아 있었다."], ["At the request of Uther he transported by magic art enormous stones from Ireland, to form the sepulchre of Pendragon.", "우서의 요청에 따라 그는 마법의 기술로 아일랜드에서 거대한 돌들을 옮겨와 펜드래곤의 무덤을 짓게 하였다."], ["These stones constitute the monument now called Stonehenge, on Salisbury plain.", "이 돌들이 바로 지금 솔즈베리 평원에 있는 스톤헨지라고 불리는 기념물을 이룬다."]], [["Merlin next proceeded to Carlisle to prepare the Round Table, at which he seated an assemblage of the great nobles of the country.", "멀린은 이어 칼라일로 가서 원탁을 마련하였고, 그 둘레에 나라의 대귀족들을 한자리에 모아 앉혔다."], ["The companions admitted to this high order were bound by oath to assist each other at the hazard of their own lives, to attempt singly the most perilous adventures, to lead, when necessary, a life of monastic solitude, to fly to arms at the first summons, and never to retire from battle till they had defeated the enemy, unless night intervened and separated the combatants.", "이 고귀한 기사단에 받아들여진 동료들은 맹세로써 다음을 지킬 의무가 있었다. 곧 자기 목숨을 걸고서라도 서로를 돕고, 가장 위험한 모험에 홀로 나서며, 필요할 때에는 수도사처럼 고독한 삶을 살고, 처음 부름이 있으면 곧장 무장하여 달려가며, 밤이 끼어들어 싸우는 자들을 갈라놓는 경우가 아니라면 적을 물리치기 전에는 결코 전투에서 물러나지 않는 것이었다."]], [["Soon after this institution, the king invited all his barons to the celebration of a great festival, which he proposed holding annually at Carlisle.", "이 제도가 세워진 지 얼마 지나지 않아, 왕은 모든 영주들을 큰 축제의 거행에 초대하였는데, 그는 이 축제를 칼라일에서 해마다 열고자 하였다."]], [["As the knights had obtained the sovereign’s permission to bring their ladies along with them, the beautiful Igerne accompanied her husband, Gorlois, Duke of Tintadel, to one of these anniversaries.", "기사들이 자기 부인들을 함께 데려와도 좋다는 군주의 허락을 얻었으므로, 아름다운 이거네는 자신의 남편이자 틴타델의 공작인 고를로이스를 따라 이 기념 축제 가운데 한 자리에 함께하였다."], ["The king became deeply enamoured of the duchess, and disclosed his passion; but Igerne repelled his advances, and revealed his solicitations to her husband.", "왕은 그 공작 부인에게 깊이 반하여 자신의 연정을 털어놓았으나, 이거네는 그의 구애를 물리치고 그가 한 간청을 자기 남편에게 알렸다."], ["On hearing this, the duke instantly removed from court with Igerne, and without taking leave of Uther.", "이를 듣고 공작은 즉시 이거네를 데리고 궁정을 떠났는데, 우서에게 작별 인사도 하지 않았다."], ["The king complained to his council of this want of duty, and they decided that the duke should be summoned to court, and, if refractory, should be treated as a rebel.", "왕은 이러한 도리의 결여에 대해 자신의 자문회의에 하소연하였고, 그들은 공작을 궁정으로 소환하되 만일 그가 따르지 않으면 반역자로 다스려야 한다고 결정하였다."], ["As he refused to obey the citation, the king carried war into the estates of his vassal and besieged him in the strong castle of Tintadel.", "공작이 소환에 응하기를 거부하자, 왕은 자기 봉신의 영지에 전쟁을 일으켜 틴타델의 견고한 성에서 그를 포위하였다."], ["Merlin transformed the king into the likeness of Gorlois, and enabled him to have many stolen interviews with Igerne.", "멀린은 왕을 고를로이스의 모습으로 변신시켜, 그가 이거네와 여러 차례 몰래 만남을 가질 수 있게 하였다."], ["At length the duke was killed in battle and the king espoused Igerne.", "마침내 공작이 전투에서 죽임을 당하자, 왕은 이거네와 혼인하였다."]], [["From this union sprang Arthur, who succeeded his father, Uther, upon the throne.", "이 결합에서 아서가 태어났으니, 그는 자신의 아버지 우서의 뒤를 이어 왕위에 올랐다."]], [["# ARTHUR CHOSEN KING", "# ARTHUR CHOSEN KING"]], [["Arthur, though only fifteen years old at his father’s death, was elected king, at a general meeting of the nobles.", "아서는 아버지가 죽었을 때 겨우 열다섯 살이었으나, 귀족들의 전체 회의에서 왕으로 선출되었다."], ["It was not done without opposition, for there were many ambitious competitors.", "이 일이 반대 없이 이루어진 것은 아니었으니, 야심을 품은 경쟁자들이 많았기 때문이다."]], [["“For while he linger’d there A doubt that ever smoulder’d in the hearts Of those great Lords and Barons of his realm Flash’d forth and into war: for most of these Made head against him, crying, ‘Who is he That he should rule us?", "\"그가 그곳에 머무르는 동안 그의 왕국의 저 위대한 영주들과 남작들의 가슴속에서 줄곧 연기처럼 피어오르던 의심이 불꽃처럼 터져 나와 전쟁으로 번졌으니, 이들 대부분이 그에게 맞서 머리를 들고 외쳤기 때문이라, '저자가 누구이기에 우리를 다스린단 말인가?"], ["who hath proven him King Uther’s son?", "누가 그를 입증하였는가 우서 왕의 아들이라고?"], ["for lo!", "보라!"], ["we look at him, And find nor face nor bearing, limbs nor voice, Are like to those of Uther whom we knew.”", "우리가 그를 살펴보아도 얼굴도 풍채도, 사지도 목소리도, 우리가 알던 우서의 그것과 닮은 데가 없도다.'\""], ["—Coming of Arthur.", "─「아서의 도래」."]], [["But Bishop Brice, a person of great sanctity, on Christmas eve addressed the assembly, and represented that it would well become them, at that solemn season, to put up their prayers for some token which should manifest the intentions of Providence respecting their future sovereign.", "그러나 큰 거룩함을 지닌 인물인 브라이스 주교가 성탄절 전야에 그 모임에 연설하여, 이 엄숙한 시기에 그들이 앞으로 모실 군주에 관한 섭리의 뜻을 드러내 줄 어떤 징표를 구하는 기도를 올리는 것이 마땅하리라고 역설하였다."], ["This was done, and with such success, that the service was scarcely ended when a miraculous stone was discovered before the church door, and in the stone was firmly fixed a sword, with the following words engraven on its hilt:", "이 일이 행해졌고, 어찌나 성공적이었던지 예배가 채 끝나기도 전에 교회 문 앞에서 기적의 돌이 발견되었는데, 그 돌에는 검 한 자루가 단단히 박혀 있었으며, 그 검의 자루에는 다음과 같은 글귀가 새겨져 있었다."]], [["“I am hight Escalibore, Unto a king fair tresore.”", "\"내 이름은 엑스칼리보어, 어느 왕에게 어울리는 귀한 보물이라.\""]], [["Bishop Brice, after exhorting the assembly to offer up their thanksgiving for this signal miracle, proposed a law, that whoever should be able to draw out the sword from the stone, should be acknowledged as sovereign of the Britons; and his proposal was decreed by general acclamation.", "브라이스 주교는 이 뚜렷한 기적에 감사를 올리라고 모임에 권한 뒤, 누구든 그 돌에서 검을 뽑아낼 수 있는 자가 브리튼족의 군주로 인정받게 하자는 법을 제안하였고, 그의 제안은 모두의 환호로써 의결되었다."], ["The tributary kings of Uther, and the most famous knights, successively put their strength to the proof, but the miraculous sword resisted all their efforts.", "우서에게 조공을 바치던 왕들과 가장 이름난 기사들이 차례로 힘을 시험해 보았으나, 그 기적의 검은 그들 모두의 노력에 끄떡도 하지 않았다."], ["It stood till Candlemas; it stood till Easter, and till Pentecost, when the best knights in the kingdom usually assembled for the annual tournament.", "검은 성촉절까지 그대로 있었고, 부활절까지도, 또 나라에서 가장 뛰어난 기사들이 해마다 마상 시합을 위해 모이곤 하던 오순절까지도 그대로 있었다."], ["Arthur, who was at that time serving in the capacity of squire to his foster-brother, Sir Kay, attended his master to the lists.", "그때 아서는 자신의 의형제인 케이 경의 종자로 봉사하고 있었는데, 자기 주인을 따라 시합장에 나갔다."], ["Sir Kay fought with great valor and success, but had the misfortune to break his sword, and sent Arthur to his mother for a new one.", "케이 경은 큰 용맹과 성공으로 싸웠으나, 불운하게도 자신의 검을 부러뜨리고 말아, 아서를 어머니에게 보내어 새 검을 가져오게 하였다."], ["Arthur hastened home, but did not find the lady; but having observed near the church a sword, sticking in a stone, he galloped to the place, drew out the sword with great ease, and delivered it to his master.", "아서는 서둘러 집으로 갔으나 그 부인을 찾지 못하였다. 다만 교회 가까이에 한 자루의 검이 돌에 박혀 있는 것을 보고는, 그 자리로 말을 달려가 아주 쉽게 검을 뽑아내어 자기 주인에게 건넸다."], ["Sir Kay would willingly have assumed to himself the distinction conferred by the possession of the sword, but when, to confirm the doubters, the sword was replaced in the stone he was utterly unable to withdraw it, and it would yield a second time to no hand but Arthur’s.", "케이 경은 그 검의 소유가 부여하는 영예를 기꺼이 자기 것으로 삼고자 하였으나, 의심하는 자들을 납득시키려고 검을 다시 돌에 꽂아 두었을 때 그는 도무지 그것을 뽑을 수가 없었고, 검은 두 번째에도 아서의 손 외에는 어떤 손에도 굴복하지 않았다."], ["Thus decisively pointed out by Heaven as their king, Arthur was by general consent proclaimed as such, and an early day appointed for his solemn coronation.", "이렇게 하늘이 결정적으로 그를 왕으로 지목하였으므로, 아서는 모두의 동의로 그러한 왕임이 선포되었고, 그의 엄숙한 대관식을 위한 이른 날이 정해졌다."]], [["Immediately after his election to the crown, Arthur found himself opposed by eleven kings and one duke, who with a vast army were actually encamped in the forest of Rockingham.", "왕위에 선출된 직후, 아서는 열한 명의 왕과 한 명의 공작이 자신에게 맞서고 있음을 알게 되었는데, 그들은 막대한 군대를 거느리고 실로 로킹엄 숲에 진을 치고 있었다."], ["By Merlin’s advice Arthur sent an embassy to Brittany, to solicit the aid of King Ban and King Bohort, two of the best knights in the world.", "멀린의 조언에 따라 아서는 브르타뉴로 사절을 보내어, 세상에서 가장 뛰어난 두 기사인 반 왕과 보호르트 왕의 도움을 청하였다."], ["They accepted the call, and with a powerful army crossed the sea, landing at Portsmouth, where they were received with great rejoicing.", "그들은 그 부름을 받아들여, 강력한 군대를 거느리고 바다를 건너 포츠머스에 상륙하였으며, 그곳에서 큰 기쁨으로 환영받았다."], ["The rebel kings were still superior in numbers; but Merlin, by a powerful enchantment, caused all their tents to fall down at once, and in the confusion Arthur with his allies fell upon them and totally routed them.", "반란을 일으킨 왕들은 여전히 수에서 우세하였으나, 멀린이 강력한 마법으로 그들의 모든 천막을 한꺼번에 무너뜨렸고, 그 혼란을 틈타 아서가 자기 동맹들과 함께 그들을 덮쳐 완전히 패주시켰다."]], [["After defeating the rebels, Arthur took the field against the Saxons.", "반란군을 물리친 뒤, 아서는 색슨족을 상대로 출정하였다."], ["As they were too strong for him unaided, he sent an embassy to Armorica, beseeching the assistance of Hoel, who soon after brought over an army to his aid.", "그들은 혼자 힘으로 맞서기에는 너무 강하였으므로, 그는 아르모리카로 사절을 보내어 호엘의 도움을 간청하였고, 호엘은 곧 군대를 이끌고 건너와 그를 도왔다."], ["The two kings joined their forces, and sought the enemy, whom they met, and both sides prepared for a decisive engagement.", "두 왕은 군세를 합쳐 적을 찾아 나섰고, 마침내 적과 마주쳤으며, 양편 모두 결정적인 교전을 준비하였다."], ["“Arthur himself,” as Geoffrey of Monmouth relates, “dressed in a breastplate worthy of so great a king, places on his head a golden helmet engraved with the semblance of a dragon.", "몬머스의 제프리가 전하는 바에 따르면, \"아서 자신은 그토록 위대한 왕에게 걸맞은 흉갑을 두르고, 머리에는 용의 형상을 새긴 황금 투구를 얹는다."], ["Over his shoulders he throws his shield called Priwen, on which a picture of the Holy Virgin constantly recalled her to his memory.", "어깨에는 프리웬이라 불리는 방패를 둘러메는데, 그 위에는 성모의 그림이 그려져 있어 늘 그분을 그의 기억에 떠올리게 한다."], ["Girt with Caliburn, a most excellent sword, and fabricated in the isle of Avalon, he graces his right hand with the lance named Ron.", "아발론섬에서 만들어진 가장 빼어난 검 칼리번을 허리에 차고, 오른손에는 론이라 이름 붙은 창으로 위엄을 더한다."], ["This was a long and broad spear, well contrived for slaughter.”", "이것은 길고 폭이 넓은 창으로, 살육에 알맞게 잘 만들어진 것이었다.\""], ["After a severe conflict, Arthur, calling on the name of the Virgin, rushes into the midst of his enemies, and destroys multitudes of them with the formidable Caliburn, and puts the rest to flight.", "격렬한 충돌 끝에, 아서는 성모의 이름을 부르며 적들의 한복판으로 돌진하여, 저 무시무시한 칼리번으로 수많은 적을 베어 쓰러뜨리고 나머지는 달아나게 하였다."], ["Hoel, being detained by sickness, took no part in this battle.", "호엘은 병으로 발이 묶여 이 전투에 참여하지 못하였다."]], [["This is called the victory of Mount Badon, and, however disguised by fable, it is regarded by historians as a real event.", "이것이 바돈산의 승리라고 불리는 것인데, 비록 전설로 윤색되어 있기는 하나, 역사가들은 이를 실제로 있었던 사건으로 여긴다."]], [["The feats performed by Arthur at the battle of Badon Mount are thus celebrated in Drayton’s verse:", "아서가 바돈산 전투에서 이룬 위업은 드레이턴의 시에서 이렇게 기려진다."]], [["“They sung how he himself at Badon bore, that day, When at the glorious goal his British sceptre lay; Two daies together how the battel stronglie stood; Pendragon’s worthie son, who waded there in blood, Three hundred Saxons slew with his owne valiant hand.”", "\"그들은 노래하였노라, 그가 그날 바돈에서 몸소 떨쳐 일어선 일을, 영광스러운 결승점에 그의 브리튼 왕홀이 놓였던 그날을, 이틀에 걸쳐 어찌나 그 전투가 굳세게 버텼던지, 펜드래곤의 훌륭한 아들이, 그곳에서 피 속을 헤치고 나아가, 제 용맹한 손으로 삼백 명의 색슨족을 베어 쓰러뜨린 일을.\""], ["—Song IV.", "─「제4가」."]], [["Merlin had planned for Arthur a marriage with the daughter of King Laodegan of Carmalide.", "멀린은 아서를 위해 카르말리데의 왕 라오데간의 딸과의 혼인을 계획해 두었다."], ["By his advice Arthur paid a visit to the court of that sovereign, attended only by Merlin and by thirty-nine knights whom the magician had selected for that service.", "그의 조언에 따라 아서는 그 군주의 궁정을 방문하였는데, 오직 멀린과, 그 마법사가 이 임무를 위해 골라 둔 서른아홉 명의 기사만을 거느리고 갔다."], ["On their arrival they found Laodegan and his peers sitting in council, endeavoring, but with small prospect of success, to devise means of resisting the impending attack of Ryence, king of Ireland, who, with fifteen tributary kings and an almost innumerable army, had nearly surrounded the city.", "그들이 도착하였을 때, 라오데간과 그의 중신들이 자문회의를 열고 앉아, 임박한 공격에 맞설 방도를 궁리하고 있었으나 성공할 가망은 거의 없었다. 그 공격을 가하려는 자는 아일랜드의 왕 리언스였는데, 그는 조공을 바치는 열다섯 왕과 거의 헤아릴 수 없는 군대를 거느리고 그 도시를 거의 에워싸고 있었다."], ["Merlin, who acted as leader of the band of British knights, announced them as strangers, who came to offer the king their services in his wars; but under the express condition that they should be at liberty to conceal their names and quality until they should think proper to divulge them.", "브리튼 기사단의 지휘자 노릇을 하던 멀린은 그들을, 왕의 전쟁에서 그를 도와 봉사하고자 찾아온 이방인들이라고 소개하였다. 다만 그들이 스스로 밝히는 것이 마땅하다고 여길 때까지는 자신들의 이름과 신분을 감출 자유를 누린다는 명백한 조건을 붙였다."], ["These terms were thought very strange, but were thankfully accepted, and the strangers, after taking the usual oath to the king, retired to the lodging which Merlin had prepared for them.", "이러한 조건은 매우 이상하게 여겨졌으나 감사히 받아들여졌고, 그 이방인들은 왕에게 으레 하는 맹세를 한 뒤, 멀린이 그들을 위해 마련해 둔 숙소로 물러갔다."]], [["A few days after this, the enemy, regardless of a truce into which they had entered with King Laodegan, suddenly issued from their camp and made an attempt to surprise the city.", "이로부터 며칠 뒤, 적은 라오데간 왕과 맺은 휴전을 아랑곳하지 않고, 느닷없이 진영에서 쏟아져 나와 그 도시를 기습하려 하였다."], ["Cleodalis, the king’s general, assembled the royal forces with all possible despatch.", "왕의 장군 클레오달리스는 되도록 신속하게 왕의 군대를 소집하였다."], ["Arthur and his companions also flew to arms, and Merlin appeared at their head, bearing a standard on which was emblazoned a terrific dragon.", "아서와 그의 동료들 또한 무장하여 달려 나왔으며, 멀린이 무시무시한 용이 그려진 군기를 들고 그들의 선두에 나타났다."], ["Merlin advanced to the gate, and commanded the porter to open it, which the porter refused to do, without the king’s order.", "멀린은 성문으로 나아가 문지기에게 문을 열라고 명하였으나, 문지기는 왕의 명령 없이는 그리할 수 없다며 거부하였다."], ["Merlin thereupon took up the gate, with all its appurtenances of locks, bars, bolts, etc., and directed his troops to pass through, after which he replaced it in perfect order.", "그러자 멀린은 자물쇠와 빗장, 걸쇠 따위의 부속물들이 달린 그대로 성문 자체를 통째로 들어 올려, 자기 부대를 그리로 통과하게 한 뒤, 그것을 완벽하게 제자리에 도로 갖다 놓았다."], ["He then set spurs to his horse and dashed, at the head of his little troop, into a body of two thousand pagans.", "그러고는 자기 말에 박차를 가하여, 그 작은 부대의 선두에 서서 이천 명의 이교도 무리 속으로 돌진하였다."], ["The disparity of numbers being so enormous, Merlin cast a spell upon the enemy, so as to prevent their seeing the small number of their assailants; notwithstanding which the British knights were hard pressed.", "수의 차이가 워낙 엄청났으므로, 멀린은 적에게 주문을 걸어 자신들을 공격하는 자들의 수가 적다는 것을 보지 못하게 하였다. 그럼에도 브리튼 기사들은 거센 압박을 받았다."], ["But the people of the city, who saw from the walls this unequal contest, were ashamed of leaving the small body of strangers to their fate, so they opened the gate and sallied forth.", "그러나 성벽에서 이 불공평한 싸움을 지켜보던 도시 사람들은, 그 작은 무리의 이방인들을 운명에 내맡겨 두는 것을 부끄럽게 여겨, 성문을 열고 쏟아져 나왔다."], ["The numbers were now more nearly equal, and Merlin revoked his spell, so that the two armies encountered on fair terms.", "이제 수가 한결 비슷해지자, 멀린은 자신의 주문을 거두어, 두 군대가 공정한 조건에서 맞붙게 하였다."], ["Where Arthur, Ban, Bohort, and the rest fought the king’s army had the advantage; but in another part of the field the king himself was surrounded and carried off by the enemy.", "아서와 반, 보호르트, 그리고 나머지 사람들이 싸우는 곳에서는 왕의 군대가 우세하였으나, 전장의 다른 한쪽에서는 왕 자신이 적에게 둘러싸여 사로잡혀 가고 말았다."], ["The sad sight was seen by Guenever, the fair daughter of the king, who stood on the city wall and looked at the battle.", "이 슬픈 광경을, 성벽 위에 서서 전투를 바라보던 왕의 아름다운 딸 귀네비어가 보았다."], ["She was in dreadful distress, tore her hair, and swooned away.", "그녀는 끔찍한 비탄에 빠져 머리를 쥐어뜯으며 기절하고 말았다."]], [["But Merlin, aware of what passed in every part of the field, suddenly collected his knights, led them out of the battle, intercepted the passage of the party who were carrying away the king, charged them with irresistible impetuosity, cut in pieces or dispersed the whole escort, and rescued the king.", "그러나 전장의 구석구석에서 일어나는 일을 알고 있던 멀린은, 갑자기 자기 기사들을 모아 전투에서 이끌고 나와, 왕을 끌고 가던 무리의 길목을 가로막고, 막을 수 없는 맹렬함으로 그들에게 돌진하여, 호위대 전체를 베어 토막 내거나 흩어 버리고 왕을 구해 냈다."], ["In the fight Arthur encountered Caulang, a giant fifteen feet high, and the fair Guenever, who had already began to feel a strong interest in the handsome young stranger, trembled for the issue of the contest.", "그 싸움에서 아서는 키가 열다섯 피트나 되는 거인 콜랑과 맞붙었는데, 이미 그 잘생긴 젊은 이방인에게 강한 관심을 느끼기 시작했던 아름다운 귀네비어는 그 대결의 결과를 두고 떨었다."], ["But Arthur, dealing a dreadful blow on the shoulder of the monster, cut through his neck so that his head hung over on one side, and in this condition his horse carried him about the field, to the great horror and dismay of the Pagans.", "그러나 아서는 그 괴물의 어깨에 무서운 일격을 가하여, 그 목을 베어 머리가 한쪽으로 늘어지게 만들었고, 이런 꼴이 된 채로 그의 말이 그를 태우고 전장을 이리저리 돌아다니니, 이교도들은 크나큰 공포와 경악에 빠졌다."], ["Guenever could not refrain from expressing aloud her wish that the gentle knight, who dealt with giants so dexterously, were destined to become her husband, and the wish was echoed by her attendants.", "귀네비어는, 거인들을 그토록 능란하게 다루는 그 점잖은 기사가 자신의 남편이 될 운명이기를 바란다고 소리 내어 말하지 않을 수 없었고, 그녀의 시녀들도 그 바람을 따라 되뇌었다."], ["The enemy soon turned their backs and fled with precipitation, closely pursued by Laodegan and his allies.", "적은 곧 등을 돌려 황급히 달아났고, 라오데간과 그의 동맹들이 바짝 뒤를 쫓았다."]], [["After the battle Arthur was disarmed and conducted to the bath by the princess Guenever, while his friends were attended by the other ladies of the court.", "전투가 끝난 뒤 아서는 무장을 벗고 귀네비어 공주의 인도로 욕탕으로 안내되었으며, 그의 벗들은 궁정의 다른 귀부인들의 시중을 받았다."], ["After the bath the knights were conducted to a magnificent entertainment, at which they were diligently served by the same fair attendants.", "목욕을 마친 뒤 기사들은 호화로운 향연으로 안내되었는데, 그곳에서도 같은 아름다운 시녀들의 정성스러운 시중을 받았다."], ["Laodegan, more and more anxious to know the name and quality of his generous deliverers, and occasionally forming a secret wish that the chief of his guests might be captivated by the charms of his daughter, appeared silent and pensive, and was scarcely roused from his reverie by the banters of his courtiers.", "라오데간은 자신을 너그럽게 구해 준 이들의 이름과 신분을 점점 더 알고 싶어졌고, 이따금 자기 손님들의 우두머리가 자기 딸의 매력에 사로잡히기를 바라는 은밀한 소원을 품기도 하면서, 말이 없고 수심에 잠긴 듯하였으며, 신하들의 농담에도 좀처럼 그 상념에서 깨어나지 못하였다."], ["Arthur, having had an opportunity of explaining to Guenever his great esteem for her merit, was in the joy of his heart, and was still further delighted by hearing from Merlin the late exploits of Gawain at London, by means of which his immediate return to his dominions was rendered unnecessary, and he was left at liberty to protract his stay at the court of Laodegan.", "아서는 귀네비어에게 그녀의 미덕에 대한 자신의 크나큰 존경을 설명할 기회를 얻은 터라 마음속으로 기뻐하였으며, 멀린에게서 가웨인이 런던에서 최근에 거둔 공적들을 전해 듣고는 더욱 흐뭇해하였다. 그 덕분에 그가 곧장 자신의 영토로 돌아갈 필요가 없어졌고, 라오데간의 궁정에서 머무름을 늘릴 자유가 생겼기 때문이다."], ["Every day contributed to increase the admiration of the whole court for the gallant strangers, and the passion of Guenever for their chief; and when at last Merlin announced to the king that the object of the visit of the party was to procure a bride for their leader, Laodegan at once presented Guenever to Arthur, telling him that, whatever might be his rank, his merit was sufficient to entitle him to the possession of the heiress of Carmalide.", "날이 갈수록 그 용감한 이방인들에 대한 온 궁정의 찬탄과, 그들의 우두머리를 향한 귀네비어의 연정은 더해 갔다. 그리고 마침내 멀린이 왕에게 이 일행이 방문한 목적이 자신들의 지도자를 위해 신부를 구하는 데 있다고 알리자, 라오데간은 곧바로 귀네비어를 아서에게 내어 주며, 그의 신분이 무엇이든 그의 미덕만으로도 카르말리데의 상속녀를 차지할 자격이 충분하다고 말하였다."]], [["“And could he find a woman in her womanhood As great as he was in his manhood— The twain together might change the world.”", "\"그리고 그가 한 여인을 찾을 수 있다면, 그 여인다움에 있어 그가 그 남자다움에 있어 위대한 만큼이나 위대한 여인을─ 그 둘이 함께라면 세상을 바꿀 수 있으리라.\""], ["—Guinevere.", "─「귀네비어」."]], [["Arthur accepted the lady with the utmost gratitude, and Merlin then proceeded to satisfy the king of the rank of his son-in-law; upon which Laodegan, with all his barons, hastened to do homage to their lawful sovereign, the successor of Uther Pendragon.", "아서는 더할 나위 없는 감사로 그 여인을 받아들였고, 멀린은 이어 왕에게 자기 사위의 신분을 납득시켰다. 그러자 라오데간은 모든 남작들과 더불어 자신들의 정당한 군주, 곧 우서 펜드래곤의 후계자에게 서둘러 충성을 바쳤다."], ["The fair Guenever was then solemnly betrothed to Arthur, and a magnificent festival was proclaimed, which lasted seven days.", "그리하여 아름다운 귀네비어는 아서와 엄숙하게 약혼하였고, 이레 동안 이어지는 호화로운 축제가 선포되었다."], ["At the end of that time, the enemy appearing again with renewed force, it became necessary to resume military operations.[42]", "그 기간이 끝날 무렵 적이 새로이 군세를 되살려 다시 나타났으므로, 군사 작전을 재개하는 것이 불가피해졌다.[42]"]], [["We must now relate what took place at and near London, while Arthur was absent from his capital.", "이제 우리는 아서가 자신의 수도를 비운 동안 런던과 그 인근에서 일어난 일을 이야기해야 한다."], ["At this very time a band of young heroes were on their way to Arthur’s court, for the purpose of receiving knighthood from him.", "바로 이 무렵 한 무리의 젊은 영웅들이 아서에게서 기사 작위를 받기 위해 그의 궁정으로 가는 길이었다."], ["They were Gawain and his three brothers, nephews of Arthur, sons of King Lot, and Galachin, another nephew, son of King Nanters.", "그들은 아서의 조카이자 로트 왕의 아들인 가웨인과 그의 세 형제, 그리고 또 다른 조카이자 난터스 왕의 아들인 갈라킨이었다."], ["King Lot had been one of the rebel chiefs whom Arthur had defeated, but he now hoped by means of the young men to be reconciled to his brother-in-law.", "로트 왕은 아서가 패배시킨 반란 수령 가운데 하나였으나, 이제 이 젊은이들을 통해 자기 처남과 화해하기를 바랐다."], ["He equipped his sons and his nephew with the utmost magnificence, giving them a splendid retinue of young men, sons of earls and barons, all mounted on the best horses, with complete suits of choice armor.", "그는 자기 아들들과 조카를 더없이 호화롭게 채비시켜, 백작과 남작의 아들들인 젊은이들로 이루어진 화려한 수행단을 딸려 보냈는데, 모두가 가장 좋은 말에 올라 정선된 갑옷을 온전히 갖추고 있었다."], ["They numbered in all seven hundred, but only nine had yet received the order of knighthood; the rest were candidates for that honor, and anxious to earn it by an early encounter with the enemy.", "그들은 모두 합쳐 칠백 명이었으나, 아직 기사 작위를 받은 자는 아홉 명뿐이었다. 나머지는 그 영예의 후보자들로서, 적과의 이른 교전을 통해 그것을 얻기를 갈망하였다."], ["Gawain, the leader, was a knight of wonderful strength; but what was most remarkable about him was that his strength was greater at certain hours of the day than at others.", "그 지도자인 가웨인은 놀라운 힘을 지닌 기사였는데, 그에 관하여 가장 두드러진 점은 그의 힘이 하루 가운데 어떤 시각에는 다른 때보다 더 강해진다는 것이었다."], ["From nine o’clock till noon his strength was doubled, and so it was from three to evensong; for the rest of the time it was less remarkable, though at all times surpassing that of ordinary men.", "오전 아홉 시부터 정오까지 그의 힘은 곱절이 되었고, 오후 세 시부터 저녁 기도 시각까지도 그러하였다. 나머지 시간에는 덜 두드러졌으나, 그때에도 늘 보통 사람들의 힘을 능가하였다."]], [["After a march of three days they arrived in the vicinity of London, where they expected to find Arthur and his court, and very unexpectedly fell in with a large convoy belonging to the enemy, consisting of numerous carts and wagons, all loaded with provisions, and escorted by three thousand men, who had been collecting spoil from all the country round.", "사흘간 행군한 끝에 그들은 런던 인근에 이르렀는데, 그곳에서 아서와 그의 궁정을 만나리라 기대하였으나, 뜻밖에도 적에게 속한 커다란 수송대와 마주쳤다. 그것은 식량을 가득 실은 수많은 짐수레와 마차로 이루어져 있었고, 사방의 온 고장에서 노략질을 거두어 온 삼천 명의 병사들이 호위하고 있었다."], ["A single charge from Gawain’s impetuous cavalry was sufficient to disperse the escort and recover the convoy, which was instantly despatched to London.", "가웨인의 맹렬한 기병대가 한 차례 돌격하는 것만으로도 그 호위대를 흩어 버리고 수송대를 되찾기에 충분하였으며, 그 수송대는 즉시 런던으로 보내졌다."], ["But before long a body of seven thousand fresh soldiers advanced to the attack of the five princes and their little army.", "그러나 얼마 지나지 않아 칠천 명의 새로운 병력이 다섯 왕족과 그들의 작은 군대를 공격하려고 진격해 왔다."], ["Gawain, singling out a chief named Choas, of gigantic size, began the battle by splitting him from the crown of the head to the breast.", "가웨인은 거대한 몸집의 코아스라는 수령을 골라내어, 그를 정수리부터 가슴까지 갈라 버리는 것으로 전투를 시작하였다."], ["Galachin encountered King Sanagran, who was also very huge, and cut off his head.", "갈라킨은 역시 몸집이 매우 거대한 사나그란 왕과 맞붙어 그의 목을 베었다."], ["Agrivain and Gahariet also performed prodigies of valor.", "아그리바인과 가하리에 또한 놀라운 용맹의 위업을 펼쳤다."], ["Thus they kept the great army of assailants at bay, though hard pressed, till of a sudden they perceived a strong body of the citizens advancing from London, where the convoy which had been recovered by Gawain had arrived, and informed the mayor and citizens of the danger of their deliverer.", "이렇게 그들은 거센 압박을 받으면서도 그 거대한 공격군을 막아 냈는데, 그러다가 문득 런던에서 강력한 시민들의 무리가 진격해 오는 것을 보았다. 가웨인이 되찾아 보낸 수송대가 그곳에 도착하여, 시장과 시민들에게 자신들을 구해 준 이가 위험에 처했음을 알린 것이었다."], ["The arrival of the Londoners soon decided the contest.", "런던 시민들의 도착으로 싸움은 곧 판가름이 났다."], ["The enemy fled in all directions, and Gawain and his friends, escorted by the grateful citizens, entered London, and were received with acclamations.", "적은 사방으로 달아났고, 가웨인과 그의 벗들은 감사하는 시민들의 호위를 받으며 런던으로 들어가, 환호 속에 맞아들여졌다."]], [["————", "────"]], [["ARTHUR (Continued)\nAFTER the great victory of Mount Badon, by which the Saxons were for the time effectually put down, Arthur turned his arms against the Scots and Picts, whom he routed at Lake Lomond, and compelled to sue for mercy.", "배든 산에서의 대승으로 색슨족이 한동안 완전히 진압되자, 아서는 군대를 돌려 스코트족과 픽트족을 향해 진격했고, 로몬드 호수에서 그들을 패주시켜 자비를 구걸하게 만들었다."], ["He then went to York to keep his Christmas, and employed himself in restoring the Christian churches which the Pagans had rifled and overthrown.", "그런 다음 그는 요크로 가서 성탄절을 보냈으며, 이교도들이 약탈하고 무너뜨린 그리스도교 교회들을 복구하는 일에 힘썼다."], ["The following summer he conquered Ireland, and then made a voyage with his fleet to Iceland, which he also subdued.", "이듬해 여름에는 아일랜드를 정복했고, 이어서 함대를 이끌고 아이슬란드로 항해하여 그곳마저 굴복시켰다."], ["The kings of Gothland and of the Orkneys came voluntarily and made their submission, promising to pay tribute.", "고틀란드와 오크니의 왕들은 자진해서 와서 복종을 맹세하고 조공을 바치겠노라 약속했다."], ["Then he returned to Britain, where, having established the kingdom, he dwelt twelve years in peace.", "그 뒤 그는 브리튼으로 돌아왔으며, 왕국을 확고히 다진 다음 열두 해 동안 평화롭게 지냈다."]], [["During this time he invited over to him all persons whatsoever that were famous for valor in foreign nations, and augmented the number of his domestics, and introduced such politeness into his court as people of the remotest countries thought worthy of their imitation.", "이 기간에 그는 외국에서 무용으로 이름난 사람들을 가리지 않고 모두 자신에게로 불러들였고, 자신의 가신 수를 늘렸으며, 궁정에 그러한 세련됨을 도입하여 아주 먼 나라의 백성들조차 본받을 만하다고 여기게 되었다."], ["So that there was not a nobleman who thought himself of any consideration unless his clothes and arms were made in the same fashion as those of Arthur’s knights.", "그리하여 자기 옷과 무기가 아서 휘하 기사들의 것과 똑같은 양식으로 만들어지지 않으면, 자신이 조금이라도 존중받을 만하다고 여기는 귀족은 한 사람도 없을 정도였다."]], [["Finding himself so powerful at home, Arthur began to form designs for extending his power abroad.", "자신이 국내에서 이토록 강성하다는 것을 알게 되자, 아서는 자신의 힘을 외국으로 확장하려는 계획을 세우기 시작했다."], ["So, having prepared his fleet, he first attempted Norway, that he might procure the crown of it for Lot, his sister’s husband.", "그래서 그는 함대를 준비한 다음, 먼저 노르웨이를 공략하고자 했으니, 누이의 남편인 로트에게 그 나라의 왕관을 마련해 주기 위함이었다."], ["Arthur landed in Norway, fought a great battle with the king of that country, defeated him, and pursued the victory till he had reduced the whole country under his dominion, and established Lot upon the throne.", "아서는 노르웨이에 상륙하여 그 나라 왕과 큰 싸움을 벌여 그를 무찔렀으며, 그 승세를 몰아 온 나라를 자신의 통치 아래 복속시킬 때까지 추격했고, 로트를 왕좌에 앉혔다."], ["Then Arthur made a voyage to Gaul and laid siege to the city of Paris.", "그런 다음 아서는 갈리아로 항해하여 파리 시를 포위했다."], ["Gaul was at that time a Roman province, and governed by Flollo, the Tribune.", "갈리아는 그 무렵 로마의 속주였으며, 호민관 플롤로가 다스리고 있었다."], ["When the siege of Paris had continued a month, and the people began to suffer from famine, Flollo challenged Arthur to single combat, proposing to decide the conquest of the province in that way.", "파리의 포위가 한 달간 이어지고 백성들이 기근에 시달리기 시작하자, 플롤로는 아서에게 일대일 결투를 청하면서, 그런 방식으로 속주의 지배권을 결정짓자고 제안했다."], ["Arthur gladly accepted the challenge, and slew his adversary in the contest, upon which the citizens surrendered the city to him.", "아서는 기꺼이 그 도전을 받아들였고, 겨룸 끝에 적수를 베어 죽였으며, 그러자 시민들은 그에게 도시를 내주었다."], ["After the victory Arthur divided his army into two parts, one of which he committed to the conduct of Hoel, whom he ordered to march into Aquitaine, while he with the other part should endeavor to subdue the other provinces.", "승리한 뒤 아서는 자기 군대를 둘로 나누어, 그 하나는 호엘의 지휘에 맡겨 아키타니아로 진군하라 명했고, 자신은 나머지 군대를 거느리고 다른 속주들을 정복하려 했다."], ["At the end of nine years, in which time all the parts of Gaul were entirely reduced, Arthur returned to Paris, where he kept his court, and, calling an assembly of the clergy and people, established peace and the just administration of the laws in that kingdom.", "갈리아의 모든 지역이 완전히 복속되기까지 아홉 해가 걸렸으며, 그 끝에 아서는 파리로 돌아와 그곳에서 궁정을 열었고, 성직자와 백성들의 회의를 소집하여 그 왕국에 평화와 공정한 법의 집행을 확립했다."], ["Then he bestowed Normandy upon Bedver, his butler, and the province of Andegavia upon Kay, his steward,[43] and several other provinces upon his great men that attended him.", "그런 다음 그는 노르망디를 식료품 시종인 베드베르에게, 안데가비아 속주를 집사인 케이에게 하사했고, 그 밖의 여러 속주를 자신을 수행하던 대인들에게 나누어 주었다."], ["And, having settled the peace of the cities and countries, he returned back in the beginning of spring to Britain.", "그리고 도시와 지방의 평화를 정착시킨 다음, 봄이 시작될 무렵 브리튼으로 돌아왔다."]], [["Upon the approach of the feast of Pentecost, Arthur, the better to demonstrate his joy after such triumphant successes, and for the more solemn observation of that festival, and reconciling the minds of the princes that were now subject to him, resolved during that season to hold a magnificent court, to place the crown upon his head, and to invite all the kings and dukes under his subjection to the solemnity.", "성령강림 축일이 다가오자, 아서는 그토록 의기양양한 성공을 거둔 뒤의 기쁨을 더욱 잘 드러내고, 그 축일을 한층 엄숙하게 지키며, 이제 자신에게 복속된 제후들의 마음을 화합시키기 위하여, 그 절기 동안 성대한 궁정을 열어 자신의 머리에 왕관을 얹고, 자신에게 복속된 모든 왕과 공작을 그 의식에 초대하기로 결심했다."], ["And he pitched upon Caerleon, the City of Legions, as the proper place for his purpose.", "그리고 그는 자신의 목적에 알맞은 장소로 군단의 도시 카에를레온을 골랐다."], ["For, besides its great wealth above the other cities,[44] its situation upon the river Usk, near the Severn sea, was most pleasant and fit for so great a solemnity.", "다른 도시들보다 큰 부를 지녔다는 점 외에도, 세번 해 가까이 어스크 강가에 자리한 그 위치가 더없이 쾌적하고 그토록 큰 의식에 알맞았기 때문이다."], ["For on one side it was washed by that noble river, so that the kings and princes from the countries beyond the seas might have the convenience of sailing up to it.", "한쪽에서는 그 고귀한 강이 도시를 적셔, 바다 건너 여러 나라의 왕과 제후들이 배를 타고 거슬러 올라오는 편의를 누릴 수 있었다."], ["On the other side the beauty of the meadows and groves, and magnificence of the royal palaces, with lofty gilded roofs that adorned it, made it even rival the grandeur of Rome.", "다른 한쪽에서는 초원과 숲의 아름다움, 그리고 도시를 장식하는 높다란 금빛 지붕을 갖춘 왕궁의 장려함이 어우러져 로마의 웅대함과도 견줄 만했다."], ["It was also famous for two churches, whereof one was adorned with a choir of virgins, who devoted themselves wholly to the service of God, and the other maintained a convent of priests.", "또한 그곳은 두 교회로도 유명했는데, 그 하나는 온전히 하느님을 섬기는 데 헌신하는 처녀들의 성가대로 꾸며져 있었고, 다른 하나는 사제들의 수도원을 거느리고 있었다."], ["Besides, there was a college of two hundred philosophers, who, being learned in astronomy and the other arts, were diligent in observing the courses of the stars, and gave Arthur true predictions of the events that would happen.", "그 밖에도 그곳에는 이백 명의 철학자로 이루어진 학원이 있었으니, 이들은 천문학과 그 밖의 학예에 정통하여 별들의 운행을 부지런히 관측했고, 아서에게 앞으로 일어날 사건들에 관한 참된 예언을 일러 주었다."], ["In this place, therefore, which afforded such delights, were preparations made for the ensuing festival.", "그리하여 이처럼 온갖 즐거움을 안겨 주는 이곳에서 다가오는 축제를 위한 준비가 갖추어졌다."]], [["Ambassadors were then sent into several kingdoms, to invite to court the princes both of Gaul and of the adjacent islands.", "이어서 갈리아와 인근 섬들의 제후들을 궁정으로 초대하기 위해 여러 왕국으로 사신이 파견되었다."], ["Accordingly there came Augusel, king of Albania, now Scotland, Cadwallo, king of Venedotia, now North Wales, Sater, king of Demetia, now South Wales; also the archbishops of the metropolitan sees, London and York, and Dubricius, bishop of Caerleon, the City of Legions.", "그리하여 알바니아, 곧 지금의 스코틀랜드의 왕 아우구셀, 베네도티아, 곧 지금의 북웨일스의 왕 카드월로, 데메티아, 곧 지금의 남웨일스의 왕 사테르가 왔다. 또한 런던과 요크의 대주교좌의 대주교들, 그리고 군단의 도시 카에를레온의 주교 두브리키우스도 왔다."], ["This prelate, who was primate of Britain, was so eminent for his piety that he could cure any sick person by his prayers.", "브리튼의 수석 주교였던 이 고위 성직자는 신심이 어찌나 두터운지 기도만으로도 어떤 병자든 고칠 수 있었다."], ["There were also the counts of the principal cities, and many other worthies of no less dignity.", "그 밖에 주요 도시의 백작들과, 그에 못지않은 위엄을 지닌 여러 명사도 있었다."]], [["From the adjacent islands came Guillamurius, king of Ireland, Gunfasius, king of the Orkneys, Malvasius, king of Iceland, Lot, king of Norway, Bedver, the butler, Duke of Normandy, Kay, the sewer, Duke of Andegavia; also the twelve peers of Gaul, and Hoel, Duke of the Armorican Britons, with his nobility, who came with such a train of mules, horses, and rich furniture as it is difficult to describe.", "인근 섬들에서는 아일랜드의 왕 길라무리우스, 오크니의 왕 군파시우스, 아이슬란드의 왕 말바시우스, 노르웨이의 왕 로트, 노르망디 공작이자 식료품 시종인 베드베르, 안데가비아 공작이자 음식 시종인 케이가 왔다. 또한 갈리아의 열두 중신과, 아르모리카의 브리튼인의 공작 호엘이 자신의 귀족들과 함께 왔는데, 이들은 노새와 말, 호화로운 마구의 행렬을 어찌나 거느리고 왔던지 말로 다 표현하기 어려울 정도였다."], ["Besides these there remained no prince of any consideration on this side of Spain who came not upon this invitation.", "이들 외에도 에스파냐 이쪽 편에서는 이 초대에 응하지 않은, 조금이라도 존중받을 만한 제후가 한 사람도 없었다."], ["And no wonder, when Arthur’s munificence, which was celebrated over the whole world, made him beloved by all people.", "그것도 무리는 아니었으니, 온 세상에 두루 알려진 아서의 아량이 그를 만백성에게서 사랑받게 만든 까닭이었다."]], [["When all were assembled upon the day of the solemnity the archbishops were conducted to the palace, in order to place the crown upon the king’s head.", "의식이 거행될 그날 모두가 모이자, 대주교들이 왕의 머리에 왕관을 얹기 위해 궁궐로 인도되었다."], ["Then Dubricius, inasmuch as the court was held in his diocese, made himself ready to celebrate the office.", "그러자 궁정이 자신의 교구 안에서 열렸으므로, 두브리키우스가 그 직무를 거행할 채비를 갖추었다."], ["As soon as the king was invested with his royal habiliments he was conducted in great pomp to the metropolitan church, having four kings, viz., of Albania, Cornwall, Demetia, and Venedotia, bearing four golden swords before him.", "왕은 왕의 예복을 갖추어 입자마자 대성당으로 성대한 행렬과 함께 인도되었으니, 알바니아, 콘월, 데메티아, 베네도티아의 네 왕이 그의 앞에서 네 자루의 황금 칼을 받들어 나아갔다."], ["On another part was the queen, dressed out in her richest ornaments, conducted by the archbishops and bishops to the Church of Virgins; the four queens, also, of the kings last mentioned, bearing before her four white doves, according to ancient custom.", "한편 다른 쪽에서는 가장 화려한 장신구로 단장한 왕비가 대주교와 주교들의 인도를 받아 처녀들의 교회로 나아갔으며, 앞서 언급한 왕들의 네 왕비도 옛 관습에 따라 그녀 앞에서 네 마리의 흰 비둘기를 받들어 나아갔다."], ["When the whole procession was ended so transporting was the harmony of the musical instruments and voices, whereof there was a vast variety in both churches, that the knights who attended were in doubt which to prefer, and therefore crowded from the one to the other by turns, and were far from being tired of the solemnity, though the whole day had been spent in it.", "행렬이 모두 끝났을 때, 두 교회에서 갖가지로 어우러진 악기와 노랫소리의 화음이 어찌나 사람을 황홀케 했던지, 수행하던 기사들은 어느 쪽을 더 좋아해야 할지 갈피를 잡지 못해 번갈아 이 교회에서 저 교회로 몰려다녔으며, 하루를 온통 거기에 쏟았는데도 그 의식에 조금도 싫증을 내지 않았다."], ["At last, when divine service was over at both churches, the king and queen put off their crowns, and, putting on their lighter ornaments, went to the banquet.", "마침내 두 교회에서 미사가 끝나자, 왕과 왕비는 왕관을 벗고 더 가벼운 장신구로 갈아입은 다음 연회로 갔다."], ["When they had all taken their seats according to precedence, Kay, the sewer, in rich robes of ermine, with a thousand young noblemen all in like manner clothed in rich attire, served up the dishes.", "모두가 서열에 따라 자리에 앉자, 음식 시종인 케이가 호화로운 흰담비 모피 예복을 걸치고, 마찬가지로 호화로운 차림을 한 천 명의 젊은 귀족과 함께 음식을 차려 올렸다."], ["From another part Bedver, the butler, was followed by the same number of attendants, who waited with all kinds of cups and drinking-vessels.", "다른 쪽에서는 식료품 시종인 베드베르가 같은 수의 시종을 거느리고 따랐는데, 이들은 온갖 종류의 잔과 술그릇을 받들고 시중을 들었다."], ["And there was food and drink in abundance, and everything was of the best kind, and served in the best manner.", "그리고 음식과 술이 넘쳐났으며, 모든 것이 최상품이었고 최상의 방식으로 차려졌다."], ["For at that time Britain had arrived at such a pitch of grandeur that in riches, luxury, and politeness it far surpassed all other kingdoms.", "그 무렵 브리튼은 그토록 장려한 경지에 이르러, 부와 호화로움과 세련됨에서 다른 모든 왕국을 훨씬 능가했던 것이다."]], [["As soon as the banquets were over they went into the fields without the city to divert themselves with various sports, such as shooting with bows and arrows, tossing the pike, casting of heavy stones and rocks, playing at dice, and the like, and all these inoffensively, and without quarrelling.", "연회가 끝나자마자 그들은 도시 밖 들판으로 나가 갖가지 놀이로 흥을 돋우었으니, 활과 화살로 쏘기, 창 던지기, 무거운 돌과 바위 던지기, 주사위 놀이 따위였으며, 이 모든 것을 다툼 없이 무탈하게 즐겼다."], ["In this manner were three days spent, and after that they separated, and the kings and noblemen departed to their several homes.", "이렇게 사흘을 보낸 뒤 그들은 헤어졌고, 왕들과 귀족들은 저마다의 고향으로 떠나갔다."]], [["After this Arthur reigned five years in peace.", "이후 아서는 다섯 해 동안 평화롭게 다스렸다."], ["Then came ambassadors from Lucius Tiberius, Procurator under Leo, Emperor of Rome, demanding tribute.", "그러던 어느 날 로마 황제 레오 휘하의 대리관 루키우스 티베리우스가 보낸 사신들이 와서 조공을 요구했다."], ["But Arthur refused to pay tribute, and prepared for war.", "그러나 아서는 조공을 바치기를 거부하고 전쟁을 준비했다."], ["As soon as the necessary dispositions were made he committed the government of his kingdom to his nephew Modred and to Queen Guenever, and marched with his army to Hamo’s Port, where the wind stood fair for him.", "필요한 채비를 마치자마자 그는 왕국의 통치를 조카 모드레드와 왕비 귀네비어에게 맡기고, 군대를 이끌고 하모 항구로 행군했는데, 그곳에는 바람이 그에게 알맞게 불고 있었다."], ["The army crossed over in safety, and landed at the mouth of the river Barba.", "군대는 무사히 바다를 건너 바르바 강 어귀에 상륙했다."], ["And there they pitched their tents to wait the arrival of the kings of the islands.", "그리고 그곳에 천막을 치고 섬들의 왕들이 도착하기를 기다렸다."]], [["As soon as all the forces were arrived Arthur marched forward to Augustodunum, and encamped on the banks of the river Alba.", "모든 병력이 도착하자마자 아서는 아우구스토두눔으로 진군하여 알바 강 기슭에 진을 쳤다."], ["Here repeated battles were fought, in all which the Britons, under their valiant leaders, Hoel, Duke of Armorica, and Gawain, nephew to Arthur, had the advantage.", "이곳에서 여러 차례 전투가 벌어졌는데, 그 모든 싸움에서 용맹한 지휘관 아르모리카 공작 호엘과 아서의 조카 가웨인이 이끄는 브리튼인이 우세를 점했다."], ["At length Lucius Tiberius determined to retreat, and wait for the Emperor Leo to join him with fresh troops.", "마침내 루키우스 티베리우스는 후퇴하여 황제 레오가 새로운 병력을 거느리고 합류하기를 기다리기로 결심했다."], ["But Arthur, anticipating this event, took possession of a certain valley, and closed up the way of retreat to Lucius, compelling him to fight a decisive battle, in which Arthur lost some of the bravest of his knights and most faithful followers.", "그러나 아서는 이를 미리 내다보고 어느 골짜기를 차지하여 루키우스의 퇴로를 막았으며, 그로 하여금 결전을 치를 수밖에 없게 만들었으니, 이 싸움에서 아서는 자신의 가장 용맹한 기사들과 가장 충실한 추종자들 가운데 몇몇을 잃었다."], ["But on the other hand Lucius Tiberius was slain, and his army totally defeated.", "그러나 다른 한편으로 루키우스 티베리우스는 죽임을 당했고, 그의 군대는 완전히 패주했다."], ["The fugitives dispersed over the country, some to the by-ways and woods, some to cities and towns, and all other places where they could hope for safety.", "도망자들은 온 들판으로 흩어졌으니, 더러는 샛길과 숲으로, 더러는 도시와 마을로, 또 안전을 바랄 수 있는 온갖 다른 곳으로 달아났다."]], [["Arthur stayed in those parts till the next winter was over, and employed his time in restoring order and settling the government.", "아서는 다음 겨울이 지날 때까지 그 지방에 머물면서 질서를 회복하고 통치를 안정시키는 일에 시간을 보냈다."], ["He then returned into England, and celebrated his victories with great splendor.", "그런 다음 그는 잉글랜드로 돌아와 자신의 승리를 크나큰 영광으로 기렸다."]], [["Then the king stablished all his knights, and to them that were not rich he gave lands, and charged them all never to do outrage nor murder, and always to flee treason; also, by no means to be cruel, but to give mercy unto him that asked mercy, upon pain of forfeiture of their worship and lordship; and always to do ladies, damosels, and gentlewomen service, upon pain of death.", "이어서 왕은 자신의 모든 기사를 봉했으며, 부유하지 못한 자들에게는 영지를 내려 주었고, 그들 모두에게 결코 횡포를 부리거나 살인을 저지르지 말 것이며, 늘 반역을 멀리하라고 명했다. 또한 어떤 일이 있어도 잔혹하게 굴지 말고, 자비를 구하는 자에게는 자비를 베풀 것이며, 이를 어기면 자신의 명예와 영주권을 잃으리라 명했다."], ["Also that no man take battle in a wrongful quarrel, for no law, nor for any world’s goods.", "또한 부인과 처녀와 귀부인에게는 늘 봉사할 것이며, 이를 어기면 죽음을 면치 못하리라 명했다."], ["Unto this were all the knights sworn of the Table Round, both old and young.", "또한 어떤 사람도 그릇된 다툼을 위해, 또 어떤 법이나 세상의 어떤 재물을 위해서도 싸움에 나서지 말라 명했다. 이 맹세를 원탁의 기사들은 늙은이든 젊은이든 모두가 했다."], ["And at every year were they sworn at the high feast of Pentecost.", "그리고 해마다 성령강림의 큰 축일에 그들은 이 맹세를 새로이 했다."]], [["# KING ARTHUR SLAYS THE GIANT OF ST. MICHAEL’S MOUNT", "# KING ARTHUR SLAYS THE GIANT OF ST. MICHAEL’S MOUNT"]], [["While the army was encamped in Brittany, awaiting the arrival of the kings, there came a countryman to Arthur, and told him that a giant, whose cave was on a neighboring mountain, called St.", "군대가 왕들의 도착을 기다리며 브르타뉴에 진을 치고 있을 무렵, 한 시골 사람이 아서에게 와서 이렇게 일렀다."], ["Michael’s Mount, had for a long time been accustomed to carry off the children of the peasants to devour them.", "성 미카엘 산이라 불리는 인근 산에 동굴을 둔 거인 하나가 오래도록 농부의 아이들을 잡아다 잡아먹는 버릇이 있었다는 것이었다."], ["“And now he hath taken the Duchess of Brittany, as she rode with her attendants, and hath carried her away in spite of all they could do.”", "\"그런데 이제 그자가 브르타뉴 공작부인이 시종들과 함께 말을 타고 가던 것을 붙잡아, 그들이 아무리 애써도 막지 못한 채 끌고 가 버렸습니다.\" \"이보게,\""], ["“Now, fellow,”", "아서 왕이 말했다."], ["said King Arthur, “canst thou bring me there where this giant haunteth?”", "\"그 거인이 출몰한다는 곳으로 나를 데려갈 수 있겠는가?\" \"예, 분명히 데려갈 수 있습니다.\""], ["“Yea, sure,”", "그 선량한 사람이 말했다."], ["said the good man; “lo, yonder where thou seest two great fires, there shalt thou find him, and more treasure than I suppose is in all France beside.”", "\"보십시오, 저쪽에 큰 불 두 무더기가 보이는 곳, 거기서 그자를 찾으실 수 있을 것이며, 제 짐작으로는 프랑스 전역에 있는 것보다 더 많은 보물을 거기서 보실 것입니다.\""], ["Then the king called to him Sir Bedver and Sir Kay, and commanded them to make ready horse and harness for himself and them; for after evening he would ride on pilgrimage to St. Michael’s Mount.", "그러자 왕은 베드베르 경과 케이 경을 불러, 자신과 그들을 위해 말과 마구를 채비하라 명했으니, 저녁이 지나면 성 미카엘 산으로 순례를 떠나려 함이었다."]], [["So they three departed, and rode forth till they came to the foot of the mount.", "그리하여 그들 셋은 길을 떠나, 산기슭에 이를 때까지 말을 달렸다."], ["And there the king commanded them to tarry, for he would himself go up into that mount.", "그곳에서 왕은 그들에게 기다리라고 명했으니, 자신이 직접 그 산에 오르려 함이었다."], ["So he ascended the hill till he came to a great fire, and there he found an aged woman sitting by a new-made grave, making great sorrow.", "그리하여 그는 큰 불이 있는 곳에 이를 때까지 산을 올랐고, 거기서 갓 만들어진 무덤가에 앉아 크게 슬퍼하는 늙은 여인 하나를 보았다."], ["Then King Arthur saluted her, and demanded of her wherefore she made such lamentation; to whom she answered: “Sir knight, speak low, for yonder is a devil, and if he hear thee speak, he will come and destroy thee.", "그러자 아서 왕은 그녀에게 인사하고, 어찌하여 그토록 애통해하는지 물었다. 그녀가 답하여 말했다."], ["For ye cannot make resistance to him, he is so fierce and so strong.", "\"기사님, 나직이 말씀하십시오. 저쪽에 악마가 있어, 만약 그자가 당신 말소리를 들으면 와서 당신을 멸하고 말 것입니다."], ["He hath murdered the Duchess, which here lieth, who was the fairest of all the world, wife to Sir Hoel, Duke of Brittany.”", "그자가 어찌나 사납고 강한지, 당신은 그자에게 맞설 수 없을 것입니다. 그자는 여기 누워 있는 공작부인을 살해했는데, 그녀는 온 세상에서 가장 아름다운 분이셨고 브르타뉴 공작 호엘 경의 부인이셨습니다.\""], ["“Dame,”", "\"부인,\""], ["said the king, “I come from the noble conqueror, King Arthur, to treat with that tyrant.”", "왕이 말했다. \"나는 고귀한 정복자 아서 왕에게서 와서 저 폭군과 담판하려는 것이오.\""], ["“Fie on such treaties,”", "\"그런 담판이라니 가당치도 않습니다.\""], ["said she; “he setteth not by the king, nor by no man else.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"그자는 왕도, 다른 어떤 사람도 안중에 두지 않습니다.\""], ["“Well,”", "\"좋소,\""], ["said Arthur, “I will accomplish my message for all your fearful words.”", "아서가 말했다. \"당신의 두려운 말에도 불구하고 나는 내 사명을 이루겠소.\""], ["So he went forth by the crest of the hill, and saw where the giant sat at supper, gnawing on the limb of a man, and baking his broad limbs at the fire, and three fair damsels lying bound, whose lot it was to be devoured in their turn.", "그리하여 그는 산마루를 따라 나아가, 거인이 저녁을 먹으며 한 사람의 다리를 물어뜯고, 자신의 넓적한 사지를 불에 굽고 있는 광경을 보았으며, 차례대로 잡아먹힐 운명에 처한 아름다운 처녀 셋이 묶인 채 누워 있는 것을 보았다."], ["When King Arthur beheld that, he had great compassion on them, so that his heart bled for sorrow.", "아서 왕은 그것을 보고 그들에게 크나큰 연민을 느껴, 슬픔으로 가슴이 미어졌다."], ["Then he hailed the giant, saying, “He that all the world ruleth give thee short life and shameful death.", "그런 다음 그는 거인을 향해 외쳐 말했다. \"온 세상을 다스리시는 분께서 너에게 짧은 목숨과 치욕스러운 죽음을 내리시기를."], ["Why hast thou murdered this Duchess?", "어찌하여 이 공작부인을 살해했느냐?"], ["Therefore come forth, for this day thou shalt die by my hand.”", "그러니 나오너라, 오늘 너는 내 손에 죽으리라.\""], ["Then the giant started up, and took a great club, and smote at the king, and smote off his coronal; and then the king struck him in the belly with his sword, and made a fearful wound.", "그러자 거인이 벌떡 일어나 커다란 곤봉을 집어 들고 왕을 후려쳐, 그의 관을 쳐서 떨어뜨렸다. 그러자 왕은 칼로 그의 배를 찔러 무시무시한 상처를 입혔다."], ["Then the giant threw away his club, and caught the king in his arms, so that he crushed his ribs.", "그러자 거인은 곤봉을 내던지고 두 팔로 왕을 붙잡아, 그의 갈비뼈를 으스러뜨렸다."], ["Then the three maidens kneeled down and prayed for help and comfort for Arthur.", "그러자 세 처녀가 무릎을 꿇고 아서에게 도움과 위안이 있기를 기도했다."], ["And Arthur weltered and wrenched, so that he was one while under, and another time above.", "아서는 뒹굴고 몸을 비틀었으니, 한순간에는 밑에 깔렸다가 또 한순간에는 위로 올라왔다."], ["And so weltering and wallowing they rolled down the hill, and ever as they weltered Arthur smote him with his dagger; and it fortuned they came to the place where the two knights were.", "이렇게 뒹굴고 엎치락뒤치락하며 그들은 산을 굴러 내려갔으며, 굴러 내려가는 동안에도 아서는 단검으로 줄곧 그자를 찔렀다. 그러다가 그들은 두 기사가 있는 곳에 이르게 되었다."], ["And when they saw the king fast in the giant’s arms they came and loosed him.", "그리고 그들은 왕이 거인의 두 팔에 단단히 붙들린 것을 보고는, 다가가 그를 풀어 주었다."], ["Then the king commanded Sir Kay to smite off the giant’s head, and to set it on the truncheon of a spear, and fix it on the barbican, that all the people might see and behold it.", "그러자 왕은 케이 경에게 거인의 머리를 베어 창자루에 꿰어, 외성 망루 위에 꽂아 모든 백성이 그것을 보고 바라볼 수 있게 하라 명했다."], ["This was done, and anon it was known through all the country, wherefor the people came and thanked the king.", "이렇게 행해졌고, 이내 그 소식이 온 나라에 알려져, 백성들이 와서 왕에게 감사했다. 그러자 왕이 말했다."], ["And he said, “Give your thanks to God; and take ye the giant’s spoil and divide it among you.”", "\"그대들의 감사는 하느님께 드리시오. 그리고 거인의 노획물을 가져다 그대들끼리 나누시오.\""], ["And King Arthur caused a church to be builded on that hill, in honor of St. Michael.", "그리고 아서 왕은 성 미카엘을 기려 그 산에 교회를 짓게 했다."]], [["# KING ARTHUR GETS A SWORD FROM THE LADY OF THE LAKE", "# KING ARTHUR GETS A SWORD FROM THE LADY OF THE LAKE"]], [["One day King Arthur rode forth, and on a sudden he was ware of three churls chasing Merlin, to have slain him.", "어느 날 아서 왕이 말을 타고 나갔다가, 문득 무지렁이 세 사람이 멀린을 죽이려고 뒤쫓는 것을 알아챘다."], ["And the king rode unto them and bade them, “Flee, churls!”", "왕은 그들에게 말을 달려가 명했다. \"달아나라, 이 무지렁이들아!\""], ["Then were they afraid when they saw a knight, and fled.", "그러자 그들은 기사를 보고 겁을 먹어 달아났다. \"오, 멀린,\""], ["“O Merlin,”", "아서가 말했다."], ["said Arthur, “here hadst thou been slain, for all thy crafts, had I not been by.”", "\"내가 곁에 없었더라면, 그대의 온갖 재주에도 불구하고 그대는 여기서 죽임을 당했을 것이네.\" \"아니,\""], ["“Nay,”", "멀린이 말했다."], ["said Merlin, “not so, for I could save myself if I would; but thou art more near thy death than I am.”", "\"그렇지 않습니다. 제가 원하기만 했다면 스스로를 구할 수 있었습니다."], ["So, as they went thus talking, King Arthur perceived where sat a knight on horseback, as if to guard the pass.", "그러나 당신은 저보다 죽음에 더 가까이 계십니다.\" 그리하여 그들이 이렇게 이야기를 나누며 가는데, 아서 왕은 어느 기사가 마치 길목을 지키려는 듯 말 위에 앉아 있는 것을 알아챘다."], ["“Sir knight,”", "\"기사여,\""], ["said Arthur, “for what cause abidest thou here?”", "아서가 말했다. \"무슨 까닭으로 여기 머물러 있는가?\""], ["Then the knight said, “There may no knight ride this way unless he just with me, for such is the custom of the pass.”", "그러자 그 기사가 말했다. \"나와 겨루지 않고서는 어떤 기사도 이 길을 지날 수 없으니, 그것이 이 길목의 관습이오.\""], ["“I will amend that custom,”", "\"내가 그 관습을 바로잡겠다.\""], ["said the king.", "왕이 말했다."], ["Then they ran together, and they met so hard that their spears were shivered.", "그러자 그들은 마주 달려, 어찌나 세차게 부딪쳤던지 두 사람의 창이 산산조각 났다."], ["Then they drew their swords and fought a strong battle, with many great strokes.", "그런 다음 그들은 칼을 빼어 들고 수많은 큰 타격을 주고받으며 격렬한 싸움을 벌였다. 그러나 마침내 그 기사의 칼이 아서 왕의 칼을 둘로 쪼개 버렸다."], ["But at length the sword of the knight smote King Arthur’s sword in two pieces.", "그러자 그 기사가 아서에게 말했다. \"그대는 내 손안에 있으니, 살리든 죽이든 내게 달렸다."], ["Then said the knight unto Arthur, “Thou art in my power, whether to save thee or slay thee, and unless thou yield thee as overcome and recreant, thou shalt die.”", "그러니 그대가 패배한 비겁자임을 인정하지 않는다면, 그대는 죽으리라.\" \"죽음으로 말할 것 같으면,\""], ["“As for death,”", "아서 왕이 말했다. \"그것이 오거든 기꺼이 맞으리라."], ["said King Arthur, “welcome be it when it cometh; but to yield me unto thee as recreant, I will not.”", "그러나 그대에게 비겁자로서 항복하는 일은 결코 하지 않겠다.\" 그런 다음 그는 그 기사에게 달려들어 허리를 붙잡아 메다꽂았다."], ["Then he leapt upon the knight, and took him by the middle and threw him down; but the knight was a passing strong man, and anon he brought Arthur under him, and would have razed off his helm to slay him.", "그러나 그 기사는 더없이 강한 사내인지라, 이내 아서를 자기 밑에 깔고 투구를 벗겨 그를 죽이려 했다. 그러자 멀린이 말했다."], ["Then said Merlin, “Knight, hold thy hand, for this knight is a man of more worship than thou art aware of.”", "\"기사여, 손을 멈추시오. 이분은 그대가 짐작하는 것보다 훨씬 더 존귀한 분이오.\""], ["“Why, who is he?”", "\"어찌하여, 이분이 누구요?\""], ["said the knight.", "기사가 말했다."], ["“It is King Arthur.”", "\"아서 왕이시오.\""], ["Then would he have slain him for dread of his wrath, and lifted up his sword to slay him; and therewith Merlin cast an enchantment on the knight, so that he fell to the earth in a great sleep.", "그러자 그는 왕의 진노가 두려워 그를 죽이려고 칼을 들어 올렸다. 그러자 멀린이 그 기사에게 마법을 걸어, 그가 깊은 잠에 빠져 땅에 쓰러지게 했다."], ["Then Merlin took up King Arthur, and set him on his horse.", "그런 다음 멀린은 아서 왕을 일으켜 말 위에 앉혔다."], ["“Alas!”", "\"아아!\""], ["said Arthur, “what hast thou done, Merlin?", "아서가 말했다. \"그대가 무슨 짓을 했는가, 멀린?"], ["hast thou slain this good knight by thy crafts?”", "그대의 재주로 이 훌륭한 기사를 죽였단 말인가?\""], ["“Care ye not,”", "\"걱정 마십시오.\""], ["said Merlin; “he is wholer than ye be.", "멀린이 말했다. \"그는 당신보다 더 멀쩡합니다."], ["He is only asleep, and will wake in three hours.”", "그는 그저 잠들었을 뿐이며, 세 시간 뒤에는 깨어날 것입니다.\""]], [["Then the king and he departed, and went till they came to a hermit, that was a good man and a great leech.", "그런 다음 왕과 그는 길을 떠나, 어느 은수자에게 이를 때까지 갔으니, 그는 선량한 사람이요 뛰어난 의원이었다."], ["So the hermit searched all his wounds, and applied good salves; and the king was there three days, and then were his wounds well amended, that he might ride and go.", "그리하여 그 은수자는 왕의 상처를 모두 살펴 좋은 고약을 발라 주었고, 왕은 그곳에서 사흘을 머물렀으며, 그러자 상처가 잘 아물어 그가 말을 타고 거동할 수 있게 되었다."], ["So they departed, and as they rode Arthur said, “I have no sword.”", "그리하여 그들은 길을 떠났는데, 말을 달리며 아서가 말했다. \"내게는 칼이 없네.\""], ["“No matter,”", "\"괜찮습니다.\""], ["said Merlin; “hereby is a sword that shall be yours.”", "멀린이 말했다. \"이 근처에 당신 것이 될 칼이 있습니다.\""], ["So they rode till they came to a lake, which was a fair water and broad.", "그리하여 그들은 어느 호수에 이를 때까지 말을 달렸으니, 그것은 맑고 드넓은 물이었다."], ["And in the midst of the lake Arthur was aware of an arm clothed in white samite,[45] that held a fair sword in the hand.", "그리고 호수 한가운데서 아서는 흰 명주를 두른 팔 하나가 손에 아름다운 칼을 쥐고 있는 것을 알아챘다."], ["“Lo!”", "\"보십시오!\""], ["said Merlin, “yonder is that sword that I spake of.", "멀린이 말했다. \"저쪽에 제가 말씀드린 그 칼이 있습니다."], ["It belongeth to the Lady of the Lake, and, if she will, thou mayest take it; but if she will not, it will not be in thy power to take it.”", "그것은 호수의 귀부인의 것이니, 그녀가 원한다면 당신이 그것을 가질 수 있을 것입니다. 그러나 그녀가 원하지 않는다면, 그것을 가지는 일은 당신의 힘으로 어쩔 수 없을 것입니다.\""]], [["So Sir Arthur and Merlin alighted from their horses, and went into a boat.", "그리하여 아서 경과 멀린은 말에서 내려 배에 올랐다."], ["And when they came to the sword that the hand held Sir Arthur took it by the handle and took it to him, and the arm and the hand went under the water.", "그리고 손이 쥐고 있는 칼에 이르자 아서 경은 그 손잡이를 잡아 자신에게로 가져왔고, 그러자 팔과 손은 물 아래로 사라졌다."]], [["Then they returned unto the land and rode forth.", "그런 다음 그들은 뭍으로 돌아와 말을 달려 나아갔다."], ["And Sir Arthur looked on the sword and liked it right well.", "그리고 아서 경은 그 칼을 바라보며 무척 흡족해했다."]], [["So they rode unto Caerleon, whereof his knights were passing glad.", "그리하여 그들은 카에를레온으로 말을 달려갔고, 그곳의 기사들은 무척 기뻐했다."], ["And when they heard of his adventures they marvelled that he would jeopard his person so alone.", "그리고 그들은 왕의 모험에 관해 듣고는, 그가 그토록 홀로 제 몸을 위험에 내맡긴 것을 의아하게 여겼다."], ["But all men of worship said it was a fine thing to be under such a chieftain as would put his person in adventure as other poor knights did.", "그러나 명예를 아는 모든 이는, 다른 가난한 기사들이 그러하듯 제 몸을 모험에 내맡기는 그러한 우두머리를 모시는 것이 훌륭한 일이라고 말했다."]], [["————", "————"]], [["# SIR GAWAIN", "# SIR GAWAIN"]], [["SIR GAWAIN was nephew to King Arthur, by his sister Morgana, married to Lot, king of Orkney, who was by Arthur made king of Norway.", "가웨인 경은 아서 왕의 조카로, 오크니의 왕 로트와 혼인한 누이 모르가나에게서 난 아들이었으며, 로트는 아서에 의해 노르웨이의 왕이 되었다."], ["Sir Gawain was one of the most famous knights of the Round Table, and is characterized by the romancers as the sage and courteous Gawain. To this Chaucer alludes in his “Squiere’s Tale,”", "가웨인 경은 원탁의 기사들 가운데 가장 이름난 이들 중 하나였으며, 로맨스 작가들은 그를 가리켜 현명하고 예의 바른 가웨인이라 일컬었다."], ["where the strange knight “salueth” all the court", "초서도 자신의 「향사의 이야기」에서 이를 넌지시 언급하는데, 그곳에서 한 낯선 기사가 궁정의 모든 이에게 인사를 올린다."]], [["“With so high reverence and observance, As well in speeche as in countenance, That Gawain, with his olde curtesie, Though he were come agen out of faërie, Ne coude him not amenden with a word.”", "\"그토록 드높은 경의와 예법으로, 말씨로나 거동으로나 한결같이 정중하여, 가웨인이 그 오랜 예의로써, 설령 그가 요정 나라에서 다시 돌아왔다 한들, 한 마디도 더 보탤 데가 없었으리.\""]], [["Gawain’s brothers were Agrivain, Gahariet, and Gareth.", "가웨인의 형제들은 아그리바인, 가하리에트, 그리고 가레스였다."]], [["# SIR GAWAIN’S MARRIAGE", "# SIR GAWAIN’S MARRIAGE"]], [["Once upon a time King Arthur held his court in merry Carlisle, when a damsel came before him and craved a boon.", "옛날 옛적에 아서 왕이 흥겨운 칼라일에서 궁정을 열고 있을 때, 한 처녀가 그의 앞에 나와 청을 올렸다."], ["It was for vengeance upon a caitiff knight, who had made her lover captive and despoiled her of her lands.", "그것은 어느 비열한 기사에게 복수를 해달라는 것으로, 그자가 그녀의 연인을 사로잡고 그녀의 영지를 약탈하였다는 것이었다."], ["King Arthur commanded to bring him his sword, Excalibar, and to saddle his steed, and rode forth without delay to right the lady’s wrong.", "아서 왕은 자신의 검 엑스칼리버를 가져오고 군마에 안장을 얹으라 명하고는, 지체 없이 그 처녀의 억울함을 바로잡고자 말을 달려 나아갔다."], ["Ere long he reached the castle of the grim baron, and challenged him to the conflict.", "머지않아 그는 그 무시무시한 영주의 성에 이르러, 그에게 결투를 청하였다."], ["But the castle stood on magic ground, and the spell was such that no knight could tread thereon but straight his courage fell and his strength decayed.", "그러나 그 성은 마법의 땅 위에 서 있었으니, 어떠한 기사라도 그곳에 발을 들이는 순간 곧바로 용기가 꺾이고 힘이 쇠하게 되는 주문이 걸려 있었다."], ["King Arthur felt the charm, and before a blow was struck, his sturdy limbs lost their strength, and his head grew faint.", "아서 왕도 그 마력을 느꼈으니, 일격을 주고받기도 전에 그 굳센 사지가 힘을 잃고, 머리가 아득해졌다."], ["He was fain to yield himself prisoner to the churlish knight, who refused to release him except upon condition that he should return at the end of a year, and bring a true answer to the question, “What thing is it which women most desire?”", "그는 무례한 기사에게 포로로 항복할 수밖에 없었는데, 그 기사는 한 가지 조건이 아니면 그를 풀어주려 하지 않았다. 그것은 일 년이 다 차는 날 다시 돌아와, \"여자들이 가장 바라는 것이 무엇인가?\"라는 물음에 참된 답을 가져오는 것이었으며, 만일 그러지 못하면 자신과 그 영지를 내놓아야 한다는 것이었다."], ["or in default thereof surrender himself and his lands.", "아서 왕은 그 조건을 받아들이고, 정한 날에 돌아오겠노라 맹세하였다."], ["King Arthur accepted the terms, and gave his oath to return at the time appointed.", "그 한 해 동안 왕은 동으로도 달리고 서로도 달리며, 만나는 모든 이에게 여자라면 누구나 가장 바라는 것이 무엇이냐고 물었다."], ["During the year the king rode east, and he rode west, and inquired of all whom he met what thing it is which all women most desire. Some told him riches; some, pomp and state; some, mirth; some, flattery; and some, a gallant knight.", "어떤 이는 재물이라 하였고, 어떤 이는 영화와 권세라 하였으며, 어떤 이는 즐거움이라 하였고, 어떤 이는 아첨이라 하였으며, 또 어떤 이는 늠름한 기사라 하였다."], ["But in the diversity of answers he could find no sure dependence.", "그러나 그 갖가지 대답 가운데서 그는 확실히 믿을 만한 것을 찾을 수가 없었다."], ["The year was well-nigh spent, when one day, as he rode thoughtfully through a forest, he saw sitting beneath a tree a lady of such hideous aspect that he turned away his eyes, and when she greeted him in seemly sort, made no answer.", "어느덧 한 해가 거의 다 저물어갈 무렵, 어느 날 그가 깊은 생각에 잠긴 채 숲을 지나가는데, 한 나무 아래에 너무도 흉측한 모습의 여인이 앉아 있는 것을 보았다. 그가 눈길을 돌려버리고, 그녀가 점잖게 인사를 건네는데도 아무 대답을 하지 않았다."], ["“What wight art thou,”", "\"그대는 어떤 사람이기에,\""], ["the lady said, “that will not speak to me?", "여인이 말하였다. \"내게 말을 걸지 아니하는가?"], ["It may chance that I may resolve thy doubts, though I be not fair of aspect.”", "비록 내 생김새가 곱지는 않으나, 어쩌면 그대의 의문을 풀어줄 수 있을지도 모르오.\""], ["“If thou wilt do so,”", "\"그대가 그리해 준다면,\""], ["said King Arthur, “choose what reward thou wilt, thou grim lady, and it shall be given thee.”", "아서 왕이 말하였다. \"무시무시한 여인이여, 그대가 원하는 보답을 고르시오, 그러면 그것을 주리다.\""], ["“Swear me this upon thy faith,”", "\"그대의 신의를 걸고 내게 이를 맹세하시오,\""], ["she said, and Arthur swore it.", "그녀가 말하니, 아서가 그리 맹세하였다."], ["Then the lady told him the secret, and demanded her reward, which was that the king should find some fair and courtly knight to be her husband.", "그러자 여인은 그에게 그 비밀을 일러주고는 자신의 보답을 요구하였으니, 그것은 왕이 어느 곱고 예의 바른 기사를 찾아 자신의 남편으로 삼아 달라는 것이었다."]], [["King Arthur hastened to the grim baron’s castle and told him one by one all the answers which he had received from his various advisers, except the last, and not one was admitted as the true one.", "아서 왕은 그 무시무시한 영주의 성으로 서둘러 가서, 여러 조언자들에게서 들은 모든 대답을 하나하나 일러주었으나, 다만 마지막 것만은 빼놓았는데, 그 어느 것도 참된 답으로 인정받지 못하였다."], ["“Now yield thee, Arthur,”", "\"이제 항복하라, 아서여,\""], ["the giant said, “for thou hast not paid thy ransom, and thou and thy lands are forfeited to me.”", "거인이 말하였다. \"그대는 몸값을 치르지 못하였으니, 그대와 그대의 영지는 내게 몰수되었도다.\""], ["Then King Arthur said:", "그러자 아서 왕이 말하였다."]], [["“Yet hold thy hand, thou proud baron, I pray thee hold thy hand, And give me leave to speak once more, In rescue of my land.", "\"잠깐 손을 멈추시오, 오만한 영주여, 부디 그 손을 멈추기를 청하노라, 그리고 내 영지를 구하고자 한 번만 더 말하게 하여 다오."], ["This morn as I came over a moor, I saw a lady set, Between an oak and a green holly, All clad in red scarlett.", "오늘 아침 황야를 건너오다가, 나는 한 여인이 앉아 있음을 보았으니, 참나무와 푸른 호랑가시나무 사이에, 온통 진홍빛 비단을 두르고 있었노라."], ["She says all women would have their will, This is their chief desire; Now yield, as thou art a baron true, That I have paid my hire.”", "그녀가 말하기를 모든 여자는 제 뜻대로 하기를 바란다 하니, 이것이 그들의 으뜸가는 소망이로다. 이제 그대가 참된 영주라면 인정하라, 내가 내 값을 다 치렀음을.\""]], [["“It was my sister that told thee this,”", "\"네게 이것을 일러준 자는 바로 내 누이로다,\""], ["the churlish baron exclaimed.", "무례한 영주가 외쳤다."], ["“Vengeance light on her!", "\"그년에게 복수가 내리기를!"], ["I will some time or other do her as ill a turn.”", "언젠가 반드시 그년에게도 똑같이 못된 짓을 갚아주리라.\""]], [["King Arthur rode homeward, but not light of heart, for he remembered the promise he was under to the loathly lady to give her one of his young and gallant knights for a husband.", "아서 왕은 집으로 말을 달렸으나, 마음이 가볍지는 못하였으니, 자신이 그 끔찍한 여인에게 젊고 늠름한 기사 하나를 남편으로 주기로 한 약속이 떠올랐기 때문이었다."], ["He told his grief to Sir Gawain, his nephew, and he replied, “Be not sad, my lord, for I will marry the loathly lady.”", "그가 자신의 시름을 조카 가웨인 경에게 털어놓으니, 가웨인이 대답하였다. \"슬퍼하지 마소서, 폐하, 그 끔찍한 여인과는 제가 혼인하겠나이다.\""], ["King Arthur replied:", "아서 왕이 대답하였다."]], [["“Now nay, now nay, good Sir Gawaine, My sister’s son ye be; The loathly lady’s all too grim, And all too foule for thee.”", "\"안 되네, 안 되네, 착한 가웨인 경이여, 그대는 내 누이의 아들이 아닌가. 그 끔찍한 여인은 너무도 흉측하고, 그대에게는 너무도 추하다네.\""]], [["But Gawain persisted, and the king at last, with sorrow of heart, consented that Gawain should be his ransom.", "그러나 가웨인이 고집을 굽히지 않으니, 왕은 마침내 마음 아프게도 가웨인이 자신의 몸값이 되는 것을 승낙하였다."], ["So one day the king and his knights rode to the forest, met the loathly lady, and brought her to the court.", "그리하여 어느 날 왕과 그의 기사들이 숲으로 말을 달려, 그 끔찍한 여인을 만나 궁정으로 데려왔다."], ["Sir Gawain stood the scoffs and jeers of his companions as he best might, and the marriage was solemnized, but not with the usual festivities.", "가웨인 경은 동료들의 비웃음과 조롱을 애써 견뎌냈으며, 혼례가 치러졌으나, 여느 때와 같은 잔치는 없었다."], ["Chaucer tells us:", "초서는 우리에게 이렇게 전한다."]], [["“.", "\"."], [".", "."], [".", "."], ["There was no joye ne feste at alle; There n’ as but hevinesse and mochel sorwe, For prively he wed her on the morwe, And all day after hid him as an owle, So we was him his wife loked so foule!”[46]", "거기엔 아무런 기쁨도 잔치도 없었으니, 오직 무거움과 크나큰 슬픔만이 있었네. 이튿날 그가 남몰래 그녀와 혼인하고는, 온종일 올빼미처럼 자신을 숨겼으니, 제 아내가 그토록 추하게 보였던 탓이라네!\"[46]"]], [["When night came, and they were alone together, Sir Gawain could not conceal his aversion; and the lady asked him why he sighed so heavily, and turned away his face.", "밤이 되어 둘만이 함께 있게 되자, 가웨인 경은 자신의 혐오를 감출 수가 없었다. 그러자 여인이 그에게 어찌하여 그토록 무겁게 한숨을 쉬며 얼굴을 돌리느냐고 물었다."], ["He candidly confessed it was on account of three things, her age, her ugliness, and her low degree.", "그는 솔직하게 그것이 세 가지 까닭, 곧 그녀의 나이와 추함과 미천한 신분 때문이라고 고백하였다."], ["The lady, not at all offended, replied with excellent arguments to all his objections.", "여인은 조금도 언짢아하지 않고, 그의 모든 불평에 더없이 훌륭한 논리로 대답하였다."], ["She showed him that with age is discretion, with ugliness security from rivals, and that all true gentility depends, not upon the accident of birth, but upon the character of the individual.", "그녀는 그에게 나이에는 분별이 따르고, 추함에는 연적의 걱정이 없는 안심이 따르며, 모든 참된 고귀함이란 태생의 우연이 아니라 그 사람의 됨됨이에 달려 있음을 일깨워 주었다."]], [["Sir Gawain made no reply; but, turning his eyes on his bride, what was his amazement to perceive that she wore no longer the unseemly aspect that had so distressed him.", "가웨인 경은 아무 대답도 하지 못하였다. 그러나 신부에게로 눈길을 돌리니, 그녀가 더 이상 자신을 그토록 괴롭히던 흉한 모습을 하고 있지 않음을 알아채고는 그 얼마나 놀랐던가."], ["She then told him that the form she had worn was not her true form, but a disguise imposed upon her by a wicked enchanter, and that she was condemned to wear it until two things should happen: one, that she should obtain some young and gallant knight to be her husband.", "그러자 그녀는 그에게, 자신이 두르고 있던 그 모습이 본래의 모습이 아니라 어느 사악한 마법사가 그녀에게 씌운 변장이었으며, 두 가지 일이 일어나기 전까지는 그 모습으로 지내야 할 운명에 처해 있었다고 일러주었다. 그 한 가지는 어느 젊고 늠름한 기사를 남편으로 얻는 것이었다."], ["This having been done, one-half of the charm was removed.", "이것이 이루어졌으니, 그 마법의 절반은 풀린 셈이었다."], ["She was now at liberty to wear her true form for half the time, and she bade him choose whether he would have her fair by day, and ugly by night, or the reverse.", "이제 그녀는 시간의 절반은 본래의 모습으로 지낼 수 있게 되었기에, 그에게 자신을 낮에는 곱게 하고 밤에는 추하게 할지, 아니면 그 반대로 할지를 고르라고 청하였다."], ["Sir Gawain would fain have had her look her best by night, when he alone would see her, and show her repulsive visage, if at all, to others.", "가웨인 경은 자신만이 그녀를 보게 되는 밤에 그녀가 가장 아름다운 모습이기를, 그리고 만일 그 흉한 얼굴을 누군가에게 보여야 한다면 차라리 다른 이들에게나 보이기를 바랐다."], ["But she reminded him how much more pleasant it would be to her to wear her best looks in the throng of knights and ladies by day.", "그러나 그녀는 그에게, 낮에 기사들과 귀부인들이 무리지은 가운데서 가장 아름다운 모습을 하고 있는 편이 자신에게 얼마나 더 기쁜 일이겠느냐고 일깨워 주었다."], ["Sir Gawain yielded, and gave up his will to hers.", "가웨인 경은 양보하여, 자신의 뜻을 그녀의 뜻에 넘겨주었다."], ["This alone was wanting to dissolve the charm.", "마법을 풀기 위해 모자랐던 것은 오직 이것뿐이었다."], ["The lovely lady now with joy assured him that she should change no more, but as she now was, so would she remain by night as well as by day.", "이제 그 사랑스러운 여인은 기쁨에 차서 그에게, 자신이 더는 변하지 않을 것이며, 지금의 모습 그대로 밤에도 낮에도 한결같이 있으리라고 확약하였다."]], [["“Sweet blushes stayned her rud-red cheek, Her eyen were black as sloe, The ripening cherrye swelled her lippe, And all her neck was snow.", "\"고운 홍조가 그녀의 발그레한 뺨을 물들이고, 그녀의 두 눈은 자두처럼 검었으며, 무르익은 앵두가 그녀의 입술을 부풀리고, 그녀의 목덜미는 온통 눈빛이었네."], ["Sir Gawain kist that ladye faire Lying upon the sheete, And swore, as he was a true knight, The spice was never so swete.”", "가웨인 경은 그 고운 여인에게 자리에 누운 채로 입을 맞추고는, 참된 기사로서 맹세하기를, 이토록 달콤한 향기는 일찍이 없었노라 하였네.\""]], [["The dissolution of the charm which had held the lady also released her brother, the “grim baron,” for he too had been implicated in it.", "그 여인을 옭아매던 마법이 풀리자 그녀의 오라비인 \"무시무시한 영주\"도 함께 풀려났으니, 그 역시 그 마법에 얽혀 있었던 까닭이다."], ["He ceased to be a churlish oppressor, and became a gallant and generous knight as any at Arthur’s court.", "그는 더 이상 무례한 압제자가 아니게 되었으며, 아서 왕의 궁정에 있는 어느 누구 못지않게 늠름하고 너그러운 기사가 되었다."]], [["————", "————"]], [["CARADOC was the son of Ysenne, the beautiful niece of Arthur.", "카라도크는 아서 왕의 아름다운 조카 이센의 아들이었다."], ["He was ignorant who his father was, till it was discovered in the following manner: When the youth was of proper years to receive the honors of knighthood, King Arthur held a grand court for the purpose of knighting him.", "그는 자기 아버지가 누구인지 모르고 있었는데, 그것이 밝혀진 경위는 다음과 같다. 이 젊은이가 기사 작위를 받을 만한 나이가 되었을 때, 아서 왕은 그에게 기사 작위를 내리기 위해 성대한 궁정 회의를 열었다."], ["On this occasion a strange knight presented himself, and challenged the knights of Arthur’s court to exchange blow for blow with him.", "이 자리에 낯선 기사 한 사람이 나타나, 아서 왕의 궁정에 모인 기사들에게 자기와 일격을 주고받자고 도전했다. 그가 내건 제안은 이러했다."], ["His proposal was this—to lay his neck on a block for any knight to strike, on condition that, if he survived the blow, the knight should submit in turn to the same experiment.", "자기 목을 받침대 위에 올려놓을 테니 어느 기사든 내리쳐 보라, 다만 자기가 그 일격을 견디고 살아남으면 이번에는 그 기사가 똑같은 시련을 받아야 한다는 조건이었다."], ["Sir Kay, who was usually ready to accept all challenges, pronounced this wholly unreasonable, and declared that he would not accept it for all the wealth in the world.", "평소에는 어떤 도전이든 기꺼이 받아들이던 케이 경마저도 이것은 전혀 사리에 맞지 않는다고 단언하며, 온 세상의 재물을 다 준대도 받아들이지 않겠다고 선언했다. 그리고 그 기사가 시행할 칼을 내밀었으나 아무도 감히 받아들지 못했다."], ["And when the knight offered his sword, with which the operation was to be performed, no person ventured to accept it, till Caradoc, growing angry at the disgrace which was thus incurred by the Round Table, threw aside his mantle and took it.", "마침내 카라도크가 원탁이 이렇게 치욕을 당하는 데에 분노하여, 외투를 벗어 던지고 그 칼을 받아 들었다. \"그대는 가장 뛰어난 기사의 한 사람으로서 이 일을 하려는 것인가?\""], ["“Do you do this as one of the best knights?”", "낯선 기사가 물었다. \"아니오.\""], ["said the stranger.", "그가 대답했다."], ["“No,” he replied, “but as one of the most foolish.”", "\"다만 가장 어리석은 자의 한 사람으로서요.\""], ["The stranger lays his head upon the block, receives a blow which sends it rolling from his shoulders, walks after it, picks it up, replaces it with great success, and says he will return when the court shall be assembled next year, and claim his turn.", "낯선 기사는 받침대 위에 머리를 올려놓았고, 일격을 받아 머리가 어깨에서 굴러떨어졌으나, 그는 머리를 쫓아가 주워서 보란 듯이 도로 제자리에 붙인 뒤, 내년에 궁정이 다시 모일 때 돌아와 자기 차례를 청구하겠노라고 말했다."], ["When the anniversary arrived, both parties were punctual to their engagement.", "그 일 주년이 돌아오자, 양쪽 모두 약속을 어김없이 지켰다."], ["Great entreaties were used by the king and queen, and the whole court, in behalf of Caradoc, but the stranger was inflexible.", "왕과 왕비, 그리고 온 궁정이 카라도크를 위해 간곡히 사정했으나, 낯선 기사는 뜻을 굽히지 않았다."], ["The young knight laid his head upon the block, and more than once desired him to make an end of the business, and not keep him longer in so disagreeable a state of expectation.", "젊은 기사는 받침대 위에 머리를 올려놓고, 어서 일을 끝내어 이토록 견디기 어려운 기다림 속에 자기를 더 오래 붙들어 두지 말라고 거듭 청했다."], ["At last the stranger strikes him gently with the side of the sword, bids him rise, and reveals to him the fact that he is his father, the enchanter Eliaures, and that he gladly owns him for a son, having proved his courage and fidelity to his word.", "마침내 낯선 기사는 칼의 옆면으로 그를 가볍게 치고는, 일어나라 이르며 한 가지 사실을 밝혔다. 자기가 그의 아버지인 마법사 엘리아우레스이며, 아들의 용기와 약속에 대한 신의를 확인했으니 기꺼이 그를 아들로 인정하겠다는 것이었다."]], [["But the favor of enchanters is short-lived and uncertain.", "그러나 마법사들의 호의란 오래가지 못하고 변덕스러운 법이다."], ["Eliaures fell under the influence of a wicked woman, who, to satisfy her pique against Caradoc, persuaded the enchanter to fasten on his arm a serpent, which remained there sucking at his flesh and blood, no human skill sufficing either to remove the reptile or alleviate the torments which Caradoc endured.", "엘리아우레스는 어느 사악한 여인의 영향에 빠지고 말았는데, 그 여인은 카라도크에 대한 앙심을 풀고자 마법사를 꾀어 그의 팔에 뱀 한 마리를 붙여 놓게 했다. 그 뱀은 그 자리에 머무르며 그의 살과 피를 빨아먹었고, 그 어떤 인간의 솜씨로도 그 파충류를 떼어 내지도, 카라도크가 겪는 고통을 덜어 주지도 못했다."]], [["Caradoc was betrothed to Guimier, sister to his bosom friend, Cador, and daughter to the king of Cornwall.", "카라도크는 그의 막역한 벗 카도르의 누이이자 콘월 왕의 딸인 기미에르와 약혼한 사이였다."], ["As soon as they were informed of his deplorable condition, they set out for Nantes, where Caradoc’s castle was, that Guimier might attend upon him.", "그들은 그의 가련한 처지를 알게 되자마자, 기미에르가 그를 간호할 수 있도록 카라도크의 성이 있는 낭트로 길을 떠났다."], ["When Caradoc heard of their coming, his first emotion was that of joy and love.", "카라도크는 그들이 온다는 소식을 듣고, 처음에는 기쁨과 사랑의 감정에 젖었다."], ["But soon he began to fear that the sight of his emaciated form, and of his sufferings, would disgust Guimier; and this apprehension became so strong, that he departed secretly from Nantes, and hid himself in a hermitage.", "그러나 곧 그는 야윈 자기 몸과 고통받는 모습을 보면 기미에르가 정나미가 떨어지지 않을까 두려워하기 시작했다. 이 염려가 너무도 강해진 나머지, 그는 몰래 낭트를 떠나 어느 은둔처에 몸을 숨겼다."], ["He was sought far and near by the knights of Arthur’s court, and Cador made a vow never to desist from the quest till he should have found him.", "아서 왕 궁정의 기사들이 사방으로 그를 찾아 나섰고, 카도르는 그를 찾기 전에는 결코 이 탐색을 멈추지 않겠노라 맹세했다."], ["After long wandering, Cador discovered his friend in the hermitage, reduced almost to a skeleton, and apparently near his death.", "오랜 방랑 끝에 카도르는 거의 해골만 남고 죽음이 임박해 보이는 자기 벗을 은둔처에서 찾아냈다."], ["All other means of relief having already been tried in vain, Cador at last prevailed on the enchanter Eliaures to disclose the only method which could avail for his rescue.", "다른 모든 구제 방법은 이미 헛되이 시도된 뒤였으므로, 카도르는 마침내 마법사 엘리아우레스를 설득하여 그를 구해 낼 수 있는 유일한 방법을 알아냈다."], ["A maiden must be found, his equal in birth and beauty, and loving him better than herself, so that she would expose herself to the same torment to deliver him.", "출신과 미모에서 그와 대등하고, 자기 자신보다 그를 더 사랑하여, 그를 구하기 위해 똑같은 고통에 스스로를 내맡길 처녀를 찾아야 한다는 것이었다."], ["Two vessels were then to be provided, the one filled with sour wine, and the other with milk.", "그런 다음 그릇 두 개를 마련하되, 하나는 시큼한 포도주로, 다른 하나는 젖으로 채워야 했다."], ["Caradoc must enter the first, so that the wine should reach his neck, and the maiden must get into the other, and, exposing her bosom upon the edge of the vessel, invite the serpent to forsake the withered flesh of his victim for this fresh and inviting food.", "카라도크는 포도주가 목까지 차오르도록 첫 번째 그릇에 들어가고, 처녀는 다른 그릇에 들어가 가슴을 그릇 가장자리에 드러낸 채, 뱀에게 시들어 버린 희생자의 살을 버리고 이 싱싱하고 먹음직한 먹이로 옮겨 오라고 꾀어야 했다."], ["The vessels were to be placed three feet apart, and as the serpent crossed from one to the other, a knight was to cut him in two.", "두 그릇은 석 자 간격으로 놓아야 했고, 뱀이 한 그릇에서 다른 그릇으로 건너갈 때 한 기사가 그것을 둘로 베어야 했다."], ["If he failed in his blow, Caradoc would indeed be delivered, but it would be only to see his fair champion suffering the same cruel and hopeless torment. The sequel may be easily foreseen.", "만약 그 일격이 빗나가면 카라도크는 분명 구원받겠지만, 그것은 다만 자기를 위해 나선 아름다운 여인이 똑같이 잔인하고 가망 없는 고통을 겪는 모습을 지켜보기 위함일 뿐이었다."], ["Guimier willingly exposed herself to the perilous adventure, and Cador, with a lucky blow, killed the serpent.", "그 뒤의 일은 쉽게 짐작할 수 있다."], ["The arm in which Caradoc had suffered so long recovered its strength, but not its shape, in consequence of which he was called Caradoc Briefbras, Caradoc of the Shrunken Arm.", "기미에르는 기꺼이 그 위험한 모험에 스스로를 내맡겼고, 카도르는 운 좋은 일격으로 뱀을 죽였다."], ["Caradoc and Guimier are the hero and heroine of the ballad of the “Boy and the Mantle,” which follows:", "카라도크가 그토록 오래 고통받았던 팔은 힘을 되찾았으나 그 모양만은 회복되지 않았으니, 그 까닭으로 그는 카라도크 브리에브라, 곧 줄어든 팔의 카라도크라 불리게 되었다."]], [["“In Carlisle dwelt King Arthur, A prince of passing might, And there maintained his Table Round, Beset with many a knight. “And there he kept his Christmas, With mirth and princely cheer, When lo! a strange and cunning boy Before him did appear.", "카라도크와 기미에르는 다음에 이어지는 발라드 \"소년과 외투\"의 주인공이다."]], [["“A kirtle and a mantle This boy had him upon, With brooches, rings, and ouches, Full daintily bedone.", "\"소년과 외투 \"칼라일에 아서 왕이 사셨으니, 비할 데 없는 권세의 군주라, 그곳에 원탁을 두시어, 숱한 기사들이 둘러앉았네."]], [["“He had a sash of silk About his middle meet; And thus with seemly curtesie He did King Arthur greet:\n“‘God speed thee, brave King Arthur.", "\"그곳에서 성탄절을 보내셨으니, 흥겨움과 군왕다운 환대 가운데, 그런데 보라! 낯설고 영리한 소년 하나가 그분 앞에 나타났네."], ["Thus feasting in thy bower, And Guenever, thy goodly queen, That fair and peerless flower.", "\"겉옷과 외투를 이 소년은 걸쳤으니, 브로치와 반지와 장신구로, 더없이 곱게 꾸몄네."]], [["“‘Ye gallant lords and lordlings, I wish you all take heed, Lest what ye deem a blooming rose Should prove a cankered weed.’\n“Then straightway from his bosom A little wand he drew; And with it eke a mantle, Of wondrous shape and hue.", "\"비단 띠 하나를 허리에 알맞게 둘렀고, 그리하여 점잖은 예의로 아서 왕께 인사를 올렸네. \"'안녕하소서, 용맹하신 아서 왕이여. 이렇듯 그대 누각에서 잔치를 벌이시고, 또한 그대의 어진 왕비 귀네비어여, 그 아름답고 견줄 데 없는 꽃이여. \"'그대 늠름한 영주와 귀공자들이여, 바라건대 모두 마음에 새기소서, 그대들이 활짝 핀 장미로 여기는 것이 썩어 든 잡초로 드러날까 하노니.'"]], [["“Now have thou here, King Arthur, Have this here of me, And give unto thy comely queen, All shapen as you see.", "\"그러더니 곧장 품속에서 작은 지팡이 하나를 꺼냈고, 그것과 더불어 외투 하나도 꺼냈으니, 놀라운 모양과 빛깔의 것이었네."], ["“‘No wife it shall become, That once hath been to blame.’ Then every knight in Arthur’s court Sly glanced at his dame.", "\"이제 받으소서, 아서 왕이여, 이것을 제게서 받으시어, 그대의 어여쁜 왕비께 주소서, 보시는 대로 지어진 이것을."]], [["“And first came Lady Guenever, The mantle she must try.", "\"'한 번이라도 허물 있던 아내에게는 이것이 어울리지 않으리.'"], ["This dame she was new-fangled,[47] And of a roving eye.", "그러자 아서 궁정의 기사마다 슬며시 제 부인을 곁눈질했네."], ["“When she had taken the mantle, And all with it was clad, From top to toe it shivered down, As though with shears beshred.", "\"맨 먼저 귀네비어 왕비가 나왔으니, 외투를 입어 보아야 했네. 이 부인은 새것을 좋아하고, 눈길이 헤픈 이였네."]], [["“One while it was too long, Another while too short, And wrinkled on her shoulders, In most unseemly sort.", "\"그녀가 외투를 받아 그것으로 온몸을 두르자, 위에서 아래까지 후들후들 떨려 내렸으니, 마치 가위로 갈가리 찢긴 듯했네."], ["“Now green, now red it seemed, Then all of sable hue; ‘Beshrew me,’ quoth King Arthur, ‘I think thou be’st not true!’", "\"어느새 너무 길었다가, 또 어느새 너무 짧아졌고, 그녀의 어깨 위에 주름이 잡혔으니, 더없이 보기 흉한 꼴이었네."]], [["“Down she threw the mantle, No longer would she stay; But, storming like a fury, To her chamber flung away. “She cursed the rascal weaver, That had the mantle wrought; And doubly cursed the froward imp Who thither had it brought.", "\"이제 초록빛이었다가, 이제 붉은빛이더니, 그러더니 온통 검은빛이 되었네. '내 신세를 한탄하노라.' 아서 왕이 말했네. '내 보기엔 그대 정숙하지 못한 듯하구나!' \"그녀는 외투를 벗어 던지고, 더는 머무르려 하지 않았으니, 분노의 화신처럼 사납게 날뛰며, 제 방으로 휙 가 버렸네."]], [["“‘I had rather live in deserts, Beneath the greenwood tree, Than here, base king, among thy grooms The sport of them and thee.’\n“Sir Kay called forth his lady, And bade her to come near: ‘Yet dame, if thou be guilty, I pray thee now forbear.’", "\"그녀는 외투를 지은 못된 직조공을 저주했고, 그것을 이리 가져온 고약한 꼬마를 갑절로 저주했네. \"'차라리 황야에서 살리라, 푸른 숲 나무 아래에서, 여기 비천한 왕이여, 그대 마부들 틈에서 그들과 그대의 놀림감이 되느니.'"]], [["“This lady, pertly giggling, With forward step came on, And boldly to the little boy With fearless face is gone.", "\"케이 경이 제 부인을 불러내어, 가까이 오라 명했네. '하지만 부인, 그대 죄가 있거든, 이제 그만두기를 청하오.'"], ["“When she had taken the mantle, With purpose for to wear, It shrunk up to her shoulder, And left her back all bare.", "\"이 부인은 새치름히 킥킥대며, 당돌한 걸음으로 나아가더니, 그 작은 소년에게 거리낌 없이 두려움 없는 얼굴로 다가갔네."]], [["“Then every merry knight, That was in Arthur’s court, Gibed and laughed and flouted, To see that pleasant sport.", "\"그녀가 외투를 받아 입어 보려 하자, 그것은 어깨까지 오그라들어, 그녀의 등을 온통 드러냈네."], ["“Down she threw the mantle, No longer bold or gay, But, with a face all pale and wan, To her chamber slunk away.", "\"그러자 아서 궁정에 있던 흥겨운 기사들은 저마다, 그 재미난 구경거리를 보고 비웃고 깔깔대며 조롱했네."]], [["“Then forth came an old knight A pattering o’er his creed, And proffered to the little boy Five nobles to his meed:\n“‘And all the time of Christmas Plum-porridge shall be thine, If thou wilt let my lady fair Within the mantle shine.’", "\"그녀는 외투를 벗어 던지고, 더는 당돌하지도 흥겹지도 못한 채, 온통 창백하고 핏기 없는 얼굴로, 제 방으로 슬그머니 가 버렸네. \"그러자 늙은 기사 하나가 나왔으니, 신조를 중얼중얼 외며, 그 작은 소년에게 금화 다섯 닢을 사례로 내밀었네."]], [["“A saint his lady seemed, With step demure and slow, And gravely to the mantle With mincing face doth go.", "\"'또한 성탄절 내내 자두 죽을 그대에게 주리니, 내 고운 부인이 그 외투 속에서 빛나도록 해 준다면 말이다.'"], ["“When she the same had taken That was so fine and thin, It shrivelled all about her, And showed her dainty skin.", "\"그의 부인은 성녀인 양했으니, 얌전하고 느린 걸음으로, 엄숙하게 외투를 향해 새침한 얼굴로 나아갔네."]], [["“Ah! little did her mincing, Or his long prayers bestead; She had no more hung on her Than a tassel and a thread.", "\"그토록 곱고 얇은 그것을 그녀가 받아 들자, 그녀 온몸을 따라 오그라들어, 그 고운 살갗을 드러냈네."], ["“Down she threw the mantle, With terror and dismay, And with a face of scarlet To her chamber hied away.", "\"아! 그녀의 새침함도, 그의 긴 기도도 도움이 되지 못했으니, 그녀에게 걸린 것이라곤 술과 실 한 오라기뿐이었네."]], [["“Sir Cradock called his lady, And bade her to come near: ‘Come win this mantle, lady, And do me credit here:\n“‘Come win this mantle, lady, For now it shall be thine, If thou hast never done amiss, Since first I made thee mine.’", "\"그녀는 외투를 벗어 던지고, 두려움과 당혹 속에서, 진홍빛 얼굴로 제 방으로 황급히 가 버렸네. \"크라도크 경이 제 부인을 불러, 가까이 오라 명했네. '이 외투를 차지하시오, 부인, 그리고 여기서 내 체면을 세워 주오. \"'이 외투를 차지하시오, 부인, 이제 이것은 그대 것이 되리니, 내가 그대를 처음 아내로 삼은 뒤로 그대 잘못한 일이 없거든 말이오.'"]], [["“The lady, gently blushing, With modest grace came on; And now to try the wondrous charm Courageously is gone.", "\"부인은 조용히 얼굴을 붉히며, 정숙한 자태로 나아갔고, 이제 그 놀라운 마법을 시험하고자 용감하게 나아갔네."], ["“When she had ta’en the mantle, And put it on her back, About the hem it seemed To wrinkle and to crack.", "\"그녀가 외투를 받아 등에 걸치자, 가장자리 둘레가 주름지고 갈라지는 듯했네."]], [["“‘Lie still,’ she cried, ‘O mantle! And shame me not for naught; I’ll freely own whate’er amiss Or blameful I have wrought. “‘Once I kissed Sir Cradock Beneath the greenwood tree; Once I kissed Sir Cradock’s mouth, Before he married me.’", "\"'가만있거라.' 그녀가 외쳤네. '오 외투여! 공연히 나를 욕보이지 말라. 내 무슨 잘못이나 허물을 저질렀든 기꺼이 털어놓으리라. \"'한번은 크라도크 경에게 입 맞췄으니, 푸른 숲 나무 아래에서, 한번은 크라도크 경의 입에 입 맞췄으니, 그가 나와 혼인하기 전이었네.' \"그녀가 이렇게 죄를 고백하고, 가장 큰 허물을 털어놓자, 외투는 곧 그녀에게 어울렸으니, 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 곱게 맞았네. \"더없이 화려하고 빛깔 고와, 황금처럼 반짝이며 빛났으니, 아서 궁정의 기사들은 너나없이 그녀를 크게 찬탄했네.\""]], [["“When she had thus her shriven, And her worst fault had told, The mantle soon became her, Right comely as it should.", "이 발라드는 멧돼지 머리와 술 마시는 뿔잔으로써 행해진 같은 종류의 시험을 두 차례 더 들려주는데, 그 두 가지 모두에서 결과는 첫 번째와 똑같이 크라도크 경과 그 부인에게 유리하게 나타났다."], ["“Most rich and fair of color, Like gold it glittering shone, And much the knights in Arthur’s court Admired her every one.”", "그러고는 다음과 같이 끝맺는다."]], [["The ballad goes on to tell of two more trials of a similar kind, made by means of a boar’s head and a drinking horn, in both of which the result was equally favorable with the first to Sir Cradock and his lady.", "\"이리하여 멧돼지 머리와 뿔잔과 외투가 이 어여쁜 부부의 보상이 되었네."], ["It then concludes as follows:\n“Thus boar’s head, horn, and mantle Were this fair couple’s meed; And all such constant lovers, God send them well to speed.”", "또한 이토록 변함없는 모든 연인들에게, 하느님께서 복을 내려 주시기를.\" —퍼시의 「유물집」."], ["—Percy’s Reliques.", "————"]], [["# LAUNCELOT OF THE LAKE", "# LAUNCELOT OF THE LAKE"]], [["KING BAN, of Brittany, the faithful ally of Arthur was attacked by his enemy Claudas, and after a long war saw himself reduced to the possession of a single fortress, where he was besieged by his enemy.", "브르타뉴의 왕 반은 아서의 충실한 동맹이었는데, 그의 적 클라우다스의 공격을 받았고, 오랜 전쟁 끝에 단 하나의 요새만을 차지한 처지로 전락하여 그곳에서 적에게 포위되었다."], ["In this extremity he determined to solicit the assistance of Arthur, and escaped in a dark night, with his wife Helen and his infant son Launcelot, leaving his castle in the hands of his seneschal, who immediately surrendered the place to Claudas.", "이 절박한 상황에서 그는 아서의 도움을 청하기로 결심하고, 어느 어두운 밤에 아내 헬렌과 갓난 아들 란셀롯을 데리고 탈출하면서 성을 자신의 집사장에게 맡겼는데, 이 집사장은 곧바로 클라우다스에게 성을 넘겨주고 말았다."], ["The flames of his burning citadel reached the eyes of the unfortunate monarch during his flight and he expired with grief.", "불운한 군주는 도주하던 중에 자신의 성채가 불타오르는 화염을 보았고, 슬픔에 못 이겨 숨을 거두었다."], ["The wretched Helen, leaving her child on the brink of a lake, flew to receive the last sighs of her husband, and on returning perceived the little Launcelot in the arms of a nymph, who, on the approach of the queen, threw herself into the lake with the child.", "비참한 처지의 헬렌은 호숫가에 아이를 남겨 둔 채 남편의 마지막 숨결을 받으려 달려갔다가, 돌아와 보니 어린 란셀롯이 어느 님프의 품에 안겨 있는 것을 발견했다. 그 님프는 왕비가 다가오자 아이를 안은 채 호수 속으로 몸을 던졌다."], ["This nymph was Viviane, mistress of the enchanter Merlin, better known by the name of the Lady of the Lake.", "이 님프는 마법사 멀린의 연인 비비안으로, 흔히 〈호수의 여인〉이라는 이름으로 더 잘 알려진 인물이었다."], ["Launcelot received his appellation from having been educated at the court of this enchantress, whose palace was situated in the midst, not of a real, but, like the appearance which deceives the African traveller, of an imaginary lake, whose deluding resemblance served as a barrier to her residence.", "란셀롯이라는 호칭은 이 마법사의 궁정에서 자랐다는 데서 비롯되었는데, 그 궁전은 진짜 호수가 아니라 아프리카의 여행자를 속이는 신기루처럼 가상의 호수 한가운데에 자리하고 있었고, 그 사람을 홀리는 듯한 호수의 모습이 그녀가 거처하는 곳의 방벽 노릇을 했다."], ["Here she dwelt not alone, but in the midst of a numerous retinue, and a splendid court of knights and damsels.", "그녀는 이곳에서 홀로 지낸 것이 아니라, 수많은 시종들과 기사와 처녀들로 이루어진 화려한 궁정 한가운데에서 살았다."]], [["The queen, after her double loss, retired to a convent, where she was joined by the widow of Bohort, for this good king had died of grief on hearing of the death of his brother Ban.", "왕비는 이중의 상실을 겪은 뒤 어느 수녀원으로 물러났는데, 그곳에서 보호트의 미망인과 합류하게 되었다. 이 선량한 왕 보호트가 자신의 동생 반의 죽음 소식을 듣고 슬픔에 못 이겨 세상을 떠났기 때문이다."], ["His two sons, Lionel and Bohort, were rescued by a faithful knight, and arrived in the shape of greyhounds at the palace of the lake, where, having resumed their natural form, they were educated along with their cousin Launcelot.", "그의 두 아들 라이오넬과 보호트는 충실한 한 기사에 의해 구출되었고, 그레이하운드의 모습이 되어 호수의 궁전에 다다랐는데, 그곳에서 본래의 모습을 되찾은 뒤 사촌 란셀롯과 함께 양육되었다."]], [["The fairy, when her pupil had attained the age of eighteen, conveyed him to the court of Arthur for the purpose of demanding his admission to the honor of knighthood; and at the first appearance of the youthful candidate the graces of his person, which were not inferior to his courage and skill in arms, made an instantaneous and indelible impression on the heart of Guenever, while her charms inspired him with an equally ardent and constant passion.", "요정은 자신의 제자가 열여덟 살이 되었을 때, 기사 서임의 영예를 청하고자 그를 아서의 궁정으로 데려갔다. 그리고 이 젊은 후보자가 처음 모습을 드러내자, 그의 용기와 무예 솜씨에 못지않은 외모의 우아함이 귀네비어의 마음에 즉각적이고도 지울 수 없는 인상을 남겼고, 한편 그녀의 매력은 그에게 그에 못지않게 뜨겁고 한결같은 열정을 불러일으켰다."], ["The mutual attachment of these lovers exerted, from that time forth, an influence over the whole history of Arthur.", "두 연인의 상호 애착은 그때부터 아서의 모든 이야기에 걸쳐 영향을 미쳤다."], ["For the sake of Guenever, Launcelot achieved the conquest of Northumberland, defeated Gallehaut, King of the Marches, who afterwards became his most faithful friend and ally, exposed himself in numberless encounters, and brought hosts of prisoners to the feet of his sovereign.", "귀네비어를 위하여 란셀롯은 노섬벌랜드를 정복했고, 변경의 왕 갈레호를 물리쳤는데, 갈레호는 훗날 그의 가장 충실한 친구이자 동맹이 되었다. 또한 란셀롯은 수없이 많은 격전에 몸을 던졌고, 수많은 포로 무리를 자신의 군주의 발 앞에 데려왔다."]], [["# SIR LAUNCELOT", "# SIR LAUNCELOT"]], [["After King Arthur was come from Rome into England all the knights of the Table Round resorted unto him and made him many justs and tournaments.", "아서 왕이 로마에서 잉글랜드로 돌아온 뒤, 원탁의 모든 기사들이 그에게 모여들어 수많은 마상 시합과 토너먼트를 열었다."], ["And in especial Sir Launcelot of the Lake in all tournaments and justs and deeds of arms, both for life and death, passed all other knights, and was never overcome, except it were by treason or enchantment; and he increased marvellously in worship, wherefore Queen Guenever had him in great favor, above all other knights.", "그 가운데서도 특히 〈호수의〉 란셀롯 경은 모든 토너먼트와 마상 시합, 그리고 생사를 건 무예 겨룸에서 다른 모든 기사들을 능가했고, 배반이나 마법에 의하지 않고는 결코 패하는 일이 없었다. 그리하여 그의 명예는 놀랍도록 높아졌고, 그 까닭에 귀네비어 왕비는 다른 모든 기사들보다 그를 크게 총애했다."], ["And for certain he loved the queen again above all other ladies; and for her he did many deeds of arms, and saved her from peril, through his noble chivalry.", "그리고 그 역시 다른 모든 귀부인들보다 왕비를 사랑했음이 분명했다. 그는 왕비를 위해 수많은 무예를 펼쳤고, 자신의 고귀한 기사도로써 그녀를 위험에서 구해 냈다."], ["Thus Sir Launcelot rested him long with play and game, and then he thought to prove himself in strange adventures; so he bade his nephew, Sir Lionel, to make him ready,—“for we two will seek adventures.”", "이렇게 란셀롯 경은 오랫동안 유희와 오락 속에서 쉬었으나, 이윽고 낯선 모험 속에서 자신을 시험해 보고자 마음먹었다. 그리하여 그는 조카인 라이오넬 경에게 채비를 갖추라 일렀다."], ["So they mounted on their horses, armed at all sights, and rode into a forest, and so into a deep plain.", "\"우리 둘이 모험을 찾아 나설 것이다.\" 그래서 그들은 말에 올라 완전 무장을 갖추고 한 숲속으로 들어갔으며, 거기서 깊은 평원으로 나아갔다."], ["And the weather was hot about noon, and Sir Launcelot had great desire to sleep.", "정오 무렵 날씨가 무더웠고, 란셀롯 경은 몹시 잠이 오고 싶었다. 그때 라이오넬 경이 산울타리 옆에 서 있는 큰 사과나무 한 그루를 발견하고 말했다."], ["Then Sir Lionel espied a great apple-tree that stood by a hedge, and he said: “Brother, yonder is a fair shadow—there may we rest us and our horses.”", "\"형님, 저기 좋은 그늘이 있습니다. 저기서 우리와 우리의 말이 쉴 수 있겠습니다.\""], ["“It is well said,”", "\"옳은 말이다.\""], ["replied Sir Launcelot.", "란셀롯 경이 대답했다."], ["So they there alighted, and Sir Launcelot laid him down, and his helm under his head, and soon was asleep passing fast.", "그래서 그들은 그곳에 내려섰고, 란셀롯 경은 투구를 베개 삼아 머리에 받치고 누웠으며, 곧 아주 깊이 잠들었다."], ["And Sir Lionel waked while he slept.", "그리고 라이오넬 경은 그가 자는 동안 깨어 있었다."], ["And presently there came three knights riding as fast as ever they might ride, and there followed them but one knight.", "이윽고 기사 셋이 있는 힘껏 말을 몰아 달려왔고, 그 뒤를 단 한 명의 기사가 쫓아왔다."], ["And Sir Lionel thought he never saw so great a knight before.", "라이오넬 경은 그토록 거대한 기사를 일찍이 본 적이 없다고 생각했다."], ["So within a while this great knight overtook one of those knights, and smote him so that he fell to the earth.", "그러더니 잠시 후 이 거대한 기사가 세 기사 중 하나를 따라잡아 가격하니, 그 기사가 땅에 나뒹굴었다."], ["Then he rode to the second knight and smote him, and so he did to the third knight.", "그러고는 두 번째 기사에게 달려가 가격했고, 세 번째 기사에게도 똑같이 했다."], ["Then he alighted down and bound all the three knights fast with their own bridles.", "그런 다음 그는 말에서 내려 세 기사를 모두 그들 자신의 고삐로 단단히 묶었다."], ["When Sir Lionel saw him do thus, he thought to assay him, and made him ready silently, not to awake Sir Launcelot, and rode after the strong knight, and bade him turn.", "라이오넬 경은 그가 이렇게 하는 것을 보고 그를 시험해 보고자 했고, 란셀롯 경을 깨우지 않으려고 조용히 채비를 갖춘 다음 그 힘센 기사를 뒤쫓아 가서 돌아서라고 명했다. 그러자 상대는 라이오넬 경을 어찌나 세게 가격했던지, 말과 사람이 모두 땅에 쓰러지고 말았다."], ["And the other smote Sir Lionel so hard that horse and man fell to the earth; and then he alighted down and bound Sir Lionel, and threw him across his own horse; and so he served them all four, and rode with them away to his own castle.", "그러고는 그가 말에서 내려 라이오넬 경을 묶어 자신의 말 위에 가로질러 얹었다. 이렇게 그는 네 사람 모두를 그렇게 처치한 뒤, 그들을 데리고 자신의 성으로 떠나갔다."], ["And when he came there he put them in a deep prison, in which were many more knights in great distress.", "그리고 그곳에 다다르자 그들을 깊은 감옥에 가두었는데, 그 안에는 큰 고통에 처한 다른 기사들도 많았다."]], [["Now while Sir Launcelot lay under the apple-tree sleeping, there came by him four queens of great estate.", "자, 란셀롯 경이 사과나무 아래에서 잠들어 있는 동안, 그 곁으로 신분이 높은 네 왕비가 지나갔다."], ["And that the heat should not grieve them, there rode four knights about them, and bare a cloth of green silk on four spears, betwixt them and the sun.", "그리고 무더위가 그들을 괴롭히지 않도록 네 기사가 그들 주위를 따라 달리며 네 자루의 창에 초록색 비단 천을 걸어 그들과 햇빛 사이를 가렸다."], ["And the queens rode on four white mules.", "그리고 왕비들은 네 마리의 흰 노새를 타고 갔다."]], [["Thus as they rode they heard by them a great horse grimly neigh.", "이렇게 말을 타고 가던 중에 그들은 곁에서 큰 말 한 마리가 사납게 우는 소리를 들었다."], ["Then they were aware of a sleeping knight, that lay all armed under an apple-tree; and as the queens looked on his face, they knew it was Sir Launcelot.", "그래서 사과나무 아래에 완전 무장을 한 채 잠들어 있는 한 기사를 알아차렸다. 왕비들이 그 얼굴을 들여다보니, 그가 란셀롯 경임을 알아보았다."], ["Then they began to strive for that knight, and each one said she would have him for her love.", "그러자 그들은 그 기사를 두고 다투기 시작했고, 저마다 자신이 그를 연인으로 삼겠다고 말했다. \"다투지 맙시다.\""], ["“We will not strive,”", "아서 왕의 누이인 모르가나 르 페이가 말했다."], ["said Morgane le Fay, that was King Arthur’s sister, “for I will put an enchantment upon him, that he shall not wake for six hours, and we will take him away to my castle; and then when he is surely within my hold, I will take the enchantment from him, and then let him choose which of us he will have for his love.”", "\"내가 그에게 마법을 걸어 여섯 시간 동안 깨어나지 못하게 할 것이니, 우리가 그를 내 성으로 데려갑시다. 그런 다음 그가 확실히 내 손안에 들어왔을 때 마법을 풀어 줄 것이고, 그러면 그가 우리 중에 누구를 연인으로 삼을지 직접 고르도록 합시다.\""], ["So the enchantment was cast upon Sir Launcelot.", "그리하여 란셀롯 경에게 마법이 걸렸다."], ["And then they laid him upon his shield, and bare him so on horseback between two knights, and brought him unto the castle and laid him in a chamber, and at night they sent him his supper.", "그런 다음 그들은 그를 방패 위에 눕히고 두 기사 사이에 끼워 말에 실어 나르더니, 성으로 데려가 한 방에 눕혔고, 밤이 되자 그에게 저녁 식사를 보냈다."]], [["And on the morning came early those four queens, richly dight, and bade him good morning, and he them again.", "그리고 다음 날 아침 일찍 그 네 왕비가 화려하게 차려입고 와서 그에게 잘 잤느냐고 인사했고, 그도 그들에게 인사를 되돌렸다. \"기사여,\""], ["“Sir knight,”", "그들이 말했다. \"그대는 우리의 포로임을 알아 두어야 한다."], ["they said, “thou must understand thou art our prisoner; and we know thee well, that thou art Sir Launcelot of the Lake, King Ban’s son, and that thou art the noblest knight living.", "우리는 그대가 누구인지 잘 안다. 그대는 〈호수의〉 란셀롯 경, 반 왕의 아들이며, 살아 있는 기사 가운데 가장 고귀한 기사이다."], ["And we know well that there can no lady have thy love but one, and that is Queen Guenever; and now thou shalt lose her for ever, and she thee; and therefore it behooveth thee now to choose one of us.", "또 우리는 어떤 귀부인도 그대의 사랑을 차지할 수 없으며 오직 한 사람, 곧 귀네비어 왕비만이 그러할 수 있다는 것도 잘 안다. 그러나 이제 그대는 그녀를 영원히 잃을 것이고, 그녀 또한 그대를 잃을 것이다."], ["I am the Queen Morgane le Fay, and here is the Queen of North Wales, and the Queen of Eastland, and the Queen of the Isles.", "그러니 이제 그대는 우리 중에 하나를 골라야 마땅하다. 나는 모르가나 르 페이 왕비이고, 여기 이분은 북웨일스의 왕비, 또 이분은 동방의 왕비, 그리고 이분은 〈섬들〉의 왕비이다."], ["Now choose one of us which thou wilt have, for if thou choose not, in this prison thou shalt die.”", "자, 이제 우리 중에 그대가 갖고자 하는 자를 하나 고르라. 만일 고르지 않는다면, 그대는 이 감옥에서 죽게 될 것이다.\""], ["“This is a hard case,”", "\"이것은 가혹한 일입니다.\" 란셀롯 경이 말했다."], ["said Sir Launcelot, “that either I must die, or else choose one of you; yet had I liever to die in this prison with worship, than to have one of you for my paramour, for ye be false enchantresses.”", "\"내가 죽거나, 아니면 당신들 중 하나를 골라야 하다니. 그러나 나는 거짓된 마법사인 당신들 중 하나를 정부로 삼느니, 차라리 이 감옥에서 명예롭게 죽는 편이 낫겠습니다.\""], ["“Well,”", "\"그래,\""], ["said the queens, “is this your answer, that ye will refuse us.”", "왕비들이 말했다. \"이것이 그대의 대답인가, 우리를 거절하겠다는 것인가?\""], ["“Yea, on my life it is,”", "\"그렇소, 내 목숨을 걸고 그러하오.\""], ["said Sir Launcelot.", "란셀롯 경이 말했다."], ["Then they departed, making great sorrow.", "그러자 그들은 크게 슬퍼하며 물러갔다."]], [["Then at noon came a damsel unto him with his dinner, and asked him, “What cheer?”", "그러더니 정오에 한 처녀가 그에게 식사를 가지고 와서 물었다. \"기분이 어떠신지요?\""], ["“Truly, fair damsel,”", "\"정말로, 아름다운 처녀여,\""], ["said Sir Launcelot, “never so ill.”", "란셀롯 경이 말했다. \"이보다 나쁠 수가 없소.\""], ["“Sir,”", "\"기사님,\""], ["said she, “if you will be ruled by me, I will help you out of this distress.", "그녀가 말했다. \"제 말을 따르신다면, 제가 이 곤경에서 벗어나도록 도와 드리겠습니다."], ["If ye will promise me to help my father on Tuesday next, who hath made a tournament betwixt him and the king of North Wales; for last Tuesday my father lost the field.”", "다음 화요일에 제 아버님을 도와 주시겠다고 약속하신다면 말입니다. 아버님께서 북웨일스의 왕과 마상 시합을 벌이기로 하셨는데, 지난 화요일에 아버님께서 패하셨거든요.\""], ["“Fair maiden,”", "\"아름다운 아가씨여,\" 란셀롯 경이 말했다."], ["said Sir Launcelot, “tell me what is your father’s name, and then will I give you an answer.”", "\"그대 아버님의 이름이 무엇인지 말해 주오. 그러면 대답해 주겠소.\""], ["“Sir knight,”", "\"기사님,\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["she said, “my father is King Bagdemagus.”", "\"제 아버님은 바그데마구스 왕이십니다.\" \"내 그분을 잘 아오.\""], ["“I know him well,”", "란셀롯 경이 말했다."], ["said Sir Launcelot, “for a noble king and a good knight; and, by the faith of my body, I will be ready to do your father and you service at that day.”", "\"고귀한 왕이자 훌륭한 기사이지. 내 몸의 신의를 걸고, 그날 그대 아버님과 그대를 위해 봉사할 채비를 갖추겠소.\""]], [["So she departed, and came on the next morning early and found him ready, and brought him out of twelve locks, and brought him to his own horse, and lightly he saddled him, and so rode forth.", "그리하여 그녀는 물러갔고, 다음 날 아침 일찍 와서 그가 준비되어 있는 것을 보고는, 열두 개의 자물쇠를 풀어 그를 데리고 나와 그의 말이 있는 곳으로 안내했다. 그는 가뿐히 말에 안장을 얹고 길을 떠났다."]], [["And on the Tuesday next he came to a little wood where the tournament should be.", "그리고 다음 화요일에 그는 마상 시합이 열릴 작은 숲에 다다랐다."], ["And there were scaffolds and holds, that lords and ladies might look on, and give the prize.", "그곳에는 영주들과 귀부인들이 지켜보며 상을 줄 수 있도록 관람대와 자리가 마련되어 있었다."], ["Then came into the field the king of North Wales, with eightscore helms, and King Badgemagus came with fourscore helms.", "이윽고 북웨일스의 왕이 백육십 명의 투구를 거느리고 들판으로 들어왔고, 바그데마구스 왕은 팔십 명의 투구를 거느리고 들어왔다."], ["And then they couched their spears, and came together with a great dash, and there were overthrown at the first encounter twelve of King Bagdemagus’s party and six of the king of North Wales’s party, and King Bagdemagus’s party had the worse.", "그러더니 그들은 창을 겨누고 큰 충돌과 함께 맞부딪쳤는데, 첫 번째 격돌에서 바그데마구스 왕 편의 열두 명과 북웨일스 왕 편의 여섯 명이 나가떨어졌고, 바그데마구스 왕 편이 더 불리해졌다."]], [["With that came Sir Launcelot of the Lake, and thrust in with his spear in the thickest of the press; and he smote down five knights ere he held his hand; and he smote down the king of North Wales, and he brake his thigh in that fall.", "그때 〈호수의〉 란셀롯 경이 창을 들고 가장 빽빽한 무리 한가운데로 돌진했다. 그는 손을 멈추기 전에 다섯 기사를 쳐서 쓰러뜨렸다."], ["And then the knights of the king of North Wales would just no more; and so the gree was given to King Bagdemagus.", "그리고 북웨일스의 왕을 쳐서 쓰러뜨렸는데, 왕은 그 낙마로 허벅지가 부러졌다. 그러자 북웨일스 왕의 기사들은 더 이상 시합하려 하지 않았고, 그리하여 승리의 영예는 바그데마구스 왕에게 돌아갔다."]], [["And Sir Launcelot rode forth with King Bagdemagus unto his castle; and there he had passing good cheer, both with the king and with his daughter.", "그리고 란셀롯 경은 바그데마구스 왕과 함께 그의 성으로 갔다. 그곳에서 그는 왕과 그의 딸 모두에게서 더없이 융숭한 환대를 받았다."], ["And on the morn he took his leave, and told the king he would go and seek his brother, Sir Lionel, that went from him when he slept.", "그리고 이튿날 아침 그는 작별을 고하면서, 자신이 잠든 사이 떠나가 버린 형제 라이오넬 경을 찾아 나서겠다고 왕에게 말했다."], ["So he departed, and by adventure he came to the same forest where he was taken sleeping.", "그리하여 그는 떠났고, 모험을 따라가다가 그가 잠든 채 붙잡혔던 바로 그 숲에 다다랐다."], ["And in the highway he met a damsel riding on a white palfrey, and they saluted each other.", "그리고 큰길에서 흰 승용마를 탄 한 처녀를 만났고, 그들은 서로 인사를 나누었다."], ["“Fair damsel,”", "\"아름다운 처녀여,\""], ["said Sir Launcelot, “know ye in this country any adventures?”", "란셀롯 경이 말했다. \"이 고장에 무슨 모험거리가 있는지 아오?\""], ["“Sir knight,”", "\"기사님,\""], ["said the damsel, “here are adventures near at hand, if thou durst pursue them.”", "처녀가 말했다. \"감히 뒤쫓을 용기가 있으시다면, 여기 가까이에 모험거리가 있습니다.\""], ["“Why should I not prove adventures?”", "\"내가 어찌 모험을 시험해 보지 않겠소?\" 란셀롯 경이 말했다."], ["said Sir Launcelot, “since for that cause came I hither.”", "\"바로 그 까닭에 내가 여기에 왔으니 말이오.\" \"기사님,\""], ["“Sir,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “hereby dwelleth a knight that will not be overmatched for any man I know, except thou overmatch him.", "\"이 근처에 한 기사가 사는데, 제가 아는 어떤 사람도 그를 당해 내지 못합니다. 기사님이 그를 능가하지 못하신다면 말이지요."], ["His name is Sir Turquine, and, as I understand, he is a deadly enemy of King Arthur, and he has in his prison good knights of Arthur’s court, threescore and more, that he hath won with his own hands.”", "그의 이름은 투르킨 경이며, 제가 알기로 그는 아서 왕의 철천지원수입니다. 그는 자신의 손으로 직접 사로잡은 아서 궁정의 훌륭한 기사 예순 명 남짓을 자기 감옥에 가두어 두고 있습니다.\""], ["“Damsel,”", "\"처녀여,\""], ["said Launcelot, “I pray you bring me unto this knight.”", "란셀롯이 말했다. \"부디 나를 그 기사에게로 데려다 주오.\""], ["So she told him, “Hereby, within this mile, is his castle, and by it on the left hand is a ford for horses to drink of, and over that ford there groweth a fair tree, and on that tree hang many shields that good knights wielded aforetime, that are now prisoners; and on the tree hangeth a basin of copper and latten, and if thou strike upon that basin thou shalt hear tidings.”", "그러자 그녀가 그에게 일러 주었다. \"여기서 일 마일 안쪽에 그의 성이 있고, 그 옆 왼편에는 말들이 물을 마시는 여울이 있으며, 그 여울 너머에 아름다운 나무 한 그루가 자라고 있습니다."], ["And Sir Launcelot departed, and rode as the damsel had shown him, and shortly he came to the ford, and the tree where hung the shields and the basin.", "그 나무에는 예전에 훌륭한 기사들이 휘둘렀으나 지금은 포로가 된 자들의 방패가 여럿 걸려 있고, 또 그 나무에는 구리와 놋쇠로 된 대야가 하나 걸려 있는데, 그 대야를 두드리시면 소식을 듣게 되실 겁니다.\" 그리하여 란셀롯 경은 떠나서 처녀가 일러 준 대로 말을 달렸고, 이내 여울과 방패들과 대야가 걸린 나무에 다다랐다."], ["And among the shields he saw Sir Lionel’s and Sir Hector’s shields, besides many others of knights that he knew.", "그리고 방패들 가운데서 그는 라이오넬 경과 헥터 경의 방패를, 그 밖에도 그가 아는 여러 기사들의 방패를 보았다."]], [["Then Sir Launcelot struck on the basin with the butt of his spear; and long he did so, but he saw no man.", "그러자 란셀롯 경은 창 자루로 대야를 두드렸다. 오랫동안 그렇게 했으나 아무도 보이지 않았다."], ["And at length he was ware of a great knight that drove a horse before him, and across the horse there lay an armed knight bounden.", "그러다 마침내 한 거대한 기사가 말 한 마리를 앞세우고 몰아오는 것을 알아차렸는데, 그 말 위에는 무장한 기사 하나가 묶인 채 가로놓여 있었다."], ["And as they came near, Sir Launcelot thought he should know the captive knight.", "그들이 가까이 다가오자 란셀롯 경은 그 사로잡힌 기사가 누구인지 알 것 같다고 생각했다."], ["Then Sir Launcelot saw that it was Sir Gaheris, Sir Gawain’s brother, a knight of the Table Round.", "이윽고 란셀롯 경은 그가 가웨인 경의 형제이며 원탁의 기사인 가헤리스 경임을 알아보았다."], ["“Now, fair knight,”", "\"자, 훌륭한 기사여,\""], ["said Sir Launcelot, “put that wounded knight off the horse, and let him rest awhile, and let us two prove our strength.", "란셀롯 경이 말했다. \"그 부상당한 기사를 말에서 내려 잠시 쉬게 하고, 우리 둘이 힘을 겨루어 보세."], ["For, as it is told me, thou hast done great despite and shame unto knights of the Round Table, therefore now defend thee.”", "내가 듣기로 그대는 원탁의 기사들에게 큰 모욕과 수치를 안겨 주었다 하니, 그러므로 이제 그대 자신을 방어하라.\""], ["“If thou be of the Table Round,”", "\"그대가 원탁의 일원이라면,\""], ["said Sir Turquine, “I defy thee and all thy fellowship.”", "투르킨 경이 말했다. \"내 그대와 그대의 모든 동료에게 도전하노라.\""], ["“That is overmuch said,”", "\"그것은 지나친 말이다.\""], ["said Sir Launcelot.", "란셀롯 경이 말했다."]], [["Then they put their spears in the rests, and came together with their horses as fast as they might run.", "그러자 그들은 창을 받침대에 걸고, 있는 힘껏 달릴 수 있는 만큼 말을 몰아 맞부딪쳤다."], ["And each smote the other in the middle of their shields, so that their horses fell under them, and the knights were both staggered; and as soon as they could clear their horses they drew out their swords and came together eagerly, and each gave the other many strong strokes, for neither shield nor harness might withstand their strokes.", "그리고 저마다 상대의 방패 한가운데를 가격하니, 그들의 말이 밑에 깔려 쓰러졌고, 두 기사 모두 비틀거렸다. 그러나 말에서 몸을 빼내자마자 그들은 칼을 뽑아 들고 맹렬히 맞붙어 서로에게 강한 일격을 수없이 가했는데, 방패도 갑옷도 그 일격을 견뎌 내지 못했다."], ["So within a while both had grimly wounds, and bled grievously.", "그리하여 잠시 후 둘 다 끔찍한 상처를 입고 심하게 피를 흘렸다."], ["Then at the last they were breathless both, and stood leaning upon their swords.", "그러다 마침내 둘 다 숨이 차서 칼에 기댄 채 서 있었다. \"자, 친구여,\""], ["“Now, fellow,”", "투르킨 경이 말했다."], ["said Sir Turquine, “thou art the stoutest man that ever I met with, and best breathed; and so be it thou be not the knight that I hate above all other knights, the knight that slew my brother, Sir Carados, I will gladly accord with thee; and for thy love I will deliver all the prisoners that I have.”", "\"그대는 내가 일찍이 만난 사람 가운데 가장 강인하고 숨이 가장 긴 자이다. 그러니 그대가 내가 다른 모든 기사보다도 미워하는 그 기사, 곧 내 형제 카라도스 경을 죽인 그 기사만 아니라면, 내 기꺼이 그대와 화해할 것이며, 그대에 대한 호의로 내가 가둔 모든 포로를 풀어 주겠노라.\""]], [["“What knight is he that thou hatest so above others?”", "\"그대가 다른 누구보다도 그토록 미워하는 그 기사가 누구인가?\""], ["“Truly,”", "\"정말로,\""], ["said Sir Turquine, “his name is Sir Launcelot of the Lake.”", "투르킨 경이 말했다. \"그의 이름은 〈호수의〉 란셀롯 경이다.\""], ["“I am Sir Launcelot of the Lake, King Ban’s son of Benwick, and very knight of the Table Round; and now I defy thee do thy best.”", "\"내가 바로 〈호수의〉 란셀롯 경, 벤위크의 반 왕의 아들이며, 진정한 원탁의 기사이다. 자, 이제 내 그대에게 도전하노니, 최선을 다하라.\""], ["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["said Sir Turquine, “Launcelot, thou art to me the most welcome that ever was knight; for we shall never part till the one of us be dead.”", "투르킨 경이 말했다. \"란셀롯, 그대야말로 일찍이 어떤 기사보다도 내게 가장 반가운 자로다."], ["And then they hurtled together like two wild bulls, rashing and lashing with their swords and shields, so that sometimes they fell, as it were, headlong.", "우리 둘 중 하나가 죽기 전에는 결코 헤어지지 못할 것이니.\" 그러더니 그들은 두 마리 성난 황소처럼 맞부딪쳐, 칼과 방패를 휘두르고 내리치며 때로는 마치 곤두박질치듯 쓰러지기도 했다."], ["Thus they fought two hours and more, till the ground where they fought was all bepurpled with blood.", "이렇게 그들은 두 시간 넘도록 싸웠고, 마침내 그들이 싸우던 땅이 온통 피로 검붉게 물들었다."]], [["Then at the last Sir Turquine waxed sore faint, and gave somewhat aback, and bare his shield full low for weariness.", "그러다 끝내 투르킨 경이 몹시 기진맥진하여 다소 뒤로 물러서면서 지친 나머지 방패를 한껏 낮추어 들었다."], ["That spied Sir Launcelot, and leapt then upon him fiercely as a lion, and took him by the beaver of his helmet, and drew him down on his knees.", "란셀롯 경이 그것을 눈치채고는 사자처럼 사납게 그에게 달려들어 그의 투구 면갑을 붙잡고 무릎을 꿇리며 끌어내렸다."], ["And he raised off his helm, and smote his neck in sunder.", "그러고는 투구를 벗겨 내고 그의 목을 베어 두 동강 냈다."]], [["And Sir Gaheris, when he saw Sir Turquine slain, said, “Fair lord, I pray you tell me your name, for this day I say ye are the best knight in the world, for ye have slain this day in my sight the mightiest man and the best knight except you that ever I saw.” “Sir, my name is Sir Launcelot du Lac, that ought to help you of right for King Arthur’s sake, and in especial for Sir Gawain’s sake, your own dear brother. Now I pray you, that ye go into yonder castle, and set free all the prisoners ye find there, for I am sure ye shall find there many knights of the Table Round, and especially my brother Sir Lionel. I pray you greet them all from me, and tell them I bid them take there such stuff as they find; and tell my brother to go unto the court and abide me there, for by the feast of Pentecost I think to be there; but at this time I may not stop, for I have adventures on hand.” So he departed, and Sir Gaheris rode into the castle, and took the keys from the porter, and hastily opened the prison door and let out all the prisoners. There was Sir Kay, Sir Brandeles, and Sir Galynde, Sir Bryan, and Sir Alyduke, Sir Hector de Marys, and Sir Lionel, and many more. And when they saw Sir Gaheris they all thanked him, for they thought, because he was wounded, that he had slain Sir Turquine. “Not so,” said Sir Gaheris; “it was Sir Launcelot that slew him, right worshipfully; I saw it with mine eyes.”", "가헤리스 경은 투르킨 경이 죽임당한 것을 보고 말했다. \"훌륭하신 분이여, 부디 당신의 이름을 알려 주십시오. 오늘 제가 보기에 당신은 세상에서 가장 훌륭한 기사이십니다. 오늘 당신은 제 눈앞에서, 당신을 빼고는 제가 일찍이 본 적 없는 가장 강하고 가장 훌륭한 기사를 죽이셨으니 말입니다.\" \"기사여, 내 이름은 〈호수의〉 란셀롯이오. 아서 왕을 위해서, 또 특히 그대의 친형제인 가웨인 경을 위해서 마땅히 그대를 도와야 하지요. 자, 부디 그대는 저 성으로 들어가 거기 있는 모든 포로를 풀어 주오. 분명 그곳에서 원탁의 기사들을 많이 발견하게 될 것이며, 특히 내 형제 라이오넬 경도 있을 것이오. 부디 나를 대신해 그들 모두에게 인사를 전해 주고, 그곳에서 찾아낸 물건은 무엇이든 가져가라고 내가 일렀다고 전해 주오. 그리고 내 형제에게는 궁정으로 가서 그곳에서 나를 기다리라고 전해 주오. 나는 성령강림절 축일까지 그곳에 가 있을 생각이오. 그러나 지금은 멈출 수 없으니, 처리해야 할 모험이 있기 때문이오.\" 그리하여 그는 떠났고, 가헤리스 경은 성으로 말을 몰고 들어가 문지기에게서 열쇠를 받아 서둘러 감옥 문을 열고 모든 포로를 풀어 주었다. 그곳에는 케이 경, 브랜델스 경, 갈린드 경, 브라이언 경, 알리듀크 경, 〈습지의〉 헥터 경, 그리고 라이오넬 경과 그 밖에 여러 기사가 있었다. 그리고 그들은 가헤리스 경을 보자 모두 그에게 감사했는데, 그가 부상을 입은 것을 보고 그가 투르킨 경을 죽인 줄로 여겼기 때문이다. \"그렇지 않소.\" 가헤리스 경이 말했다. \"그를 죽인 것은 란셀롯 경이오. 참으로 영예롭게 말이오. 내 두 눈으로 똑똑히 보았소.\""]], [["Sir Launcelot rode till at nightfall he came to a fair castle, and therein he found an old gentlewoman, who lodged him with good-will, and there he had good cheer for him and his horse.", "란셀롯 경은 밤이 되도록 말을 달려 어느 아름다운 성에 다다랐고, 그 안에서 한 늙은 귀부인을 만났는데, 그녀가 기꺼이 그를 묵게 해 주었으며, 그곳에서 그와 그의 말은 융숭한 대접을 받았다."], ["And when time was, his host brought him to a fair chamber over the gate to his bed.", "그리고 때가 되자 주인은 그를 문 위의 아름다운 방으로 데려가 잠자리에 들게 했다."], ["Then Sir Launcelot unarmed him, and set his harness by him, and went to bed, and anon he fell asleep.", "그러자 란셀롯 경은 무장을 풀어 곁에 갑옷을 두고 잠자리에 들었으며, 이내 잠이 들었다. 그리고 얼마 지나지 않아 누군가가 말을 타고 와서 몹시 다급하게 문을 두드렸다."], ["And soon after, there came one on horseback and knocked at the gate in great haste; and when Sir Launcelot heard this, he arose and looked out of the window, and saw by the moonlight three knights riding after that one man, and all three lashed on him with their swords, and that one knight turned on them knightly again and defended himself.", "란셀롯 경이 이 소리를 듣고 일어나 창밖을 내다보니, 달빛 아래 세 기사가 한 사람을 뒤쫓으며 달려오는 것이 보였고, 셋 모두 칼로 그를 내리쳤으며, 그 한 기사는 기사답게 다시 그들을 향해 돌아서서 자신을 방어하고 있었다. \"정말로,\""], ["“Truly,”", "란셀롯 경이 말했다."], ["said Sir Launcelot, “yonder one knight will I help, for it is shame to see three knights on one.”", "\"저기 저 한 기사를 내가 도와야겠다. 세 기사가 한 사람을 치는 꼴은 보기에 수치스러우니.\""], ["Then he took his harness and went out at the window by a sheet down to the four knights; and he said aloud, “Turn you knights unto me, and leave your fighting with that knight.”", "그러고는 갑옷을 챙겨 시트를 타고 창밖으로 내려가 네 기사가 있는 곳에 이르렀다. 그리고 큰 소리로 말했다."], ["Then the knights left Sir Kay, for it was he they were upon, and turned unto Sir Launcelot, and struck many great strokes at Sir Launcelot, and assailed him on every side.", "\"기사들아, 그 기사와 싸우는 것을 멈추고 나를 향해 돌아서라.\" 그러자 기사들은 케이 경을 내버려 두었는데, 그가 바로 그들이 공격하던 자였다."], ["Then Sir Kay addressed him to help Sir Launcelot, but he said, “Nay, sir, I will none of your help; let me alone with them.”", "그러고는 란셀롯 경에게로 돌아서서 그에게 강한 일격을 수없이 날리며 사방에서 그를 공격했다. 그때 케이 경이 란셀롯 경을 도우려 나서자, 그가 말했다."], ["So Sir Kay suffered him to do his will, and stood one side.", "\"아니오, 기사여, 그대의 도움은 필요 없소. 저들은 나에게 맡겨 두시오.\""], ["And within six strokes Sir Launcelot had stricken them down.", "그래서 케이 경은 그가 뜻대로 하도록 두고 한쪽에 비켜섰다. 그리고 여섯 번의 일격 안에 란셀롯 경은 그들을 쳐서 쓰러뜨렸다."]], [["Then they all cried, “Sir knight, we yield us unto you.”", "그러자 그들 모두가 외쳤다. \"기사여, 우리가 당신께 항복합니다.\""], ["“As to that,”", "\"그것에 대해서라면,\""], ["said Sir Launcelot, “I will not take your yielding unto me.", "란셀롯 경이 말했다. \"나는 그대들이 내게 항복하는 것을 받지 않겠다."], ["If so be ye will yield you unto Sir Kay the Seneschal, I will save your lives, but else not.”", "만일 그대들이 집사장 케이 경에게 항복하겠다면, 내 그대들의 목숨을 살려 주겠지만, 그렇지 않으면 안 된다.\""], ["“Fair knight,”", "\"훌륭한 기사여,\""], ["then they said, “we will do as thou commandest us.”", "그러자 그들이 말했다. \"당신이 명하시는 대로 하겠습니다.\""], ["“Then shall ye,”", "\"그러면 그대들은,\""], ["said Sir Launcelot, “on Whitsunday next, go unto the court of King Arthur, and there shall ye yield you unto Queen Guenever, and say that Sir Kay sent you thither to be her prisoners.”", "란셀롯 경이 말했다. \"다음 성령강림절에 아서 왕의 궁정으로 가서 귀네비어 왕비께 항복하고, 케이 경이 그대들을 그녀의 포로로 그곳에 보냈다고 아뢰라.\""], ["“Sir,”", "\"기사여,\""], ["they said, “it shall be done, by the faith of our bodies;”", "그들이 말했다. \"우리 몸의 신의를 걸고 그리하겠습니다.\""], ["and then they swore, every knight upon his sword.", "그러고는 기사마다 자신의 칼에 대고 맹세했다."], ["And so Sir Launcelot suffered them to depart.", "그리하여 란셀롯 경은 그들이 떠나가도록 허락했다."]], [["On the morn Sir Launcelot rose early and left Sir Kay sleeping; and Sir Launcelot took Sir Kay’s armor, and his shield, and armed him, and went to the stable and took his horse, and so he departed.", "이튿날 아침 란셀롯 경은 일찍 일어나, 잠든 케이 경을 남겨 둔 채 케이 경의 갑옷과 방패를 가져다 자신이 무장하고, 마구간으로 가서 그의 말을 끌어내어 길을 떠났다. 그러고 얼마 뒤 케이 경이 일어나 보니 란셀롯 경이 보이지 않았다."], ["Then soon after arose Sir Kay, and missed Sir Launcelot.", "그리고 그가 자신의 갑옷과 말을 가져간 것을 알아차렸다."], ["And then he espied that he had taken his armor and his horse.", "\"자, 내 신의를 걸고 잘 알겠노라.\""], ["“Now, by my faith, I know well,”", "케이 경이 말했다."], ["said Sir Kay, “that he will grieve some of King Arthur’s knights, for they will deem that it is I, and will be bold to meet him.", "\"그가 아서 왕의 기사들 중 몇몇을 곤란하게 만들겠구나. 그들이 그를 나로 여기고 대담하게 그와 맞서려 할 테니."], ["But by cause of his armor I am sure I shall ride in peace.”", "하지만 그의 갑옷 덕분에 나는 분명 평온히 말을 달릴 수 있겠지.\""], ["Then Sir Kay thanked his host and departed.", "그러고서 케이 경은 주인에게 감사하고 떠났다."]], [["Sir Launcelot rode in a deep forest, and there he saw four knights, under an oak, and they were of Arthur’s court.", "란셀롯 경은 깊은 숲속으로 말을 달리다가, 한 떡갈나무 아래에서 네 기사를 보았는데, 그들은 아서의 궁정 사람들이었다."], ["There was Sir Sagramour le Desirus, and Hector de Marys, and Sir Gawain, and Sir Uwaine.", "그곳에는 〈갈망하는 자〉 사그라모르 경, 〈습지의〉 헥터 경, 가웨인 경, 그리고 우와인 경이 있었다."], ["As they spied Sir Launcelot they judged by his arms it had been Sir Kay.", "그들은 란셀롯 경을 알아보고는 그의 무구로 미루어 케이 경이라고 판단했다."], ["“Now, by my faith,”", "\"자, 내 신의를 걸고,\""], ["said Sir Sagramour, “I will prove Sir Kay’s might;”", "사그라모르 경이 말했다. \"내가 케이 경의 무력을 시험해 보겠소.\""], ["and got his spear in his hand, and came towards Sir Launcelot.", "그러고는 손에 창을 들고 란셀롯 경을 향해 달려왔다."], ["Therewith Sir Launcelot couched his spear against him, and smote Sir Sagramour so sore that horse and man fell both to the earth.", "그러자 란셀롯 경은 그를 향해 창을 겨누어 사그라모르 경을 어찌나 세게 가격했던지, 말과 사람이 모두 땅에 나뒹굴었다."], ["Then said Sir Hector, “Now shall ye see what I may do with him.”", "그때 헥터 경이 말했다. \"이제 내가 그에게 무엇을 할 수 있는지 보시오.\""], ["But he fared worse than Sir Sagramour, for Sir Launcelot’s spear went through his shoulder and bare him from his horse to the ground.", "그러나 그는 사그라모르 경보다 더 험한 꼴을 당했으니, 란셀롯 경의 창이 그의 어깨를 꿰뚫어 그를 말에서 땅으로 떨어뜨렸기 때문이다. \"내 신의를 걸고,\""], ["“By my faith,”", "우와인 경이 말했다."], ["said Sir Uwaine, “yonder is a strong knight, and I fear he hath slain Sir Kay, and taken his armor.”", "\"저기 저자는 강한 기사로구나. 그가 케이 경을 죽이고 그의 갑옷을 빼앗은 것이 아닐까 두렵다.\""], ["And therewith Sir Uwaine took his spear in hand, and rode toward Sir Launcelot; and Sir Launcelot met him on the plain and gave him such a buffet that he was staggered, and wist not where he was.", "그러고는 우와인 경이 손에 창을 들고 란셀롯 경을 향해 말을 몰았고, 란셀롯 경은 평원에서 그를 맞아 어찌나 세게 후려쳤던지, 그가 비틀거리며 자신이 어디에 있는지조차 알지 못했다."], ["“Now see I well,”", "\"이제 내 잘 알겠다.\""], ["said Sir Gawain, “that I must encounter with that knight.”", "가웨인 경이 말했다. \"내가 저 기사와 맞서야 하겠구나.\""], ["Then he adjusted his shield, and took a good spear in his hand, and Sir Launcelot knew him well.", "그러고는 방패를 고쳐 잡고 손에 좋은 창을 들었는데, 란셀롯 경은 그를 잘 알아보았다."], ["Then they let run their horses with all their mights, and each knight smote the other in the middle of his shield.", "그러더니 그들은 있는 힘껏 말을 달리게 했고, 두 기사는 저마다 상대의 방패 한가운데를 가격했다."], ["But Sir Gawain’s spear broke, and Sir Launcelot charged so sore upon him that his horse fell over backward.", "그러나 가웨인 경의 창은 부러졌고, 란셀롯 경이 어찌나 세게 그에게 돌진했던지 그의 말이 뒤로 나자빠졌다."], ["Then Sir Launcelot passed by smiling with himself, and he said, “Good luck be with him that made this spear, for never came a better into my hand.”", "그러자 란셀롯 경은 혼자 미소 지으며 지나가면서 말했다. \"이 창을 만든 자에게 행운이 있기를."], ["Then the four knights went each to the other and comforted one another.", "이보다 더 좋은 창이 내 손에 들어온 적이 없으니.\" 그러자 네 기사가 저마다 서로에게 다가가 서로를 위로했다."], ["“What say ye to this adventure,”", "\"이 모험을 어찌 생각하오?\""], ["said Sir Gawain, “that one spear hath felled us all four?”", "가웨인 경이 말했다. \"창 하나가 우리 넷을 모두 쓰러뜨렸으니 말이오.\""], ["“I dare lay my head it is Sir Launcelot,”", "\"내 목을 걸고 그자는 란셀롯 경이오.\""], ["said Sir Hector; “I know it by his riding.”", "헥터 경이 말했다. \"그가 말 타는 모습을 보고 알았소.\""]], [["And Sir Launcelot rode through many strange countries, till by fortune he came to a fair castle; and as he passed beyond the castle he thought he heard two bells ring.", "그리고 란셀롯 경은 낯선 여러 고장을 거쳐 말을 달리다가, 우연히 어느 아름다운 성에 다다랐다. 그가 성을 지나쳐 가는데 종 두 개가 울리는 소리가 들리는 듯했다."], ["And then he perceived how a falcon came flying over his head, toward a high elm; and she had long lunys[48] about her feet, and she flew unto the elm to take her perch, and the lunys got entangled in the bough; and when she would have taken her flight, she hung by the legs fast, and Sir Launcelot saw how she hung, and beheld the fair falcon entangled, and he was sorry for her.", "그러더니 매 한 마리가 그의 머리 위로 날아 높은 느릅나무를 향해 가는 것을 알아차렸다. 매는 발에 긴 끈을 매단 채 느릅나무로 날아가 가지에 앉으려 했는데, 그 끈이 나뭇가지에 엉켜 버렸다."], ["Then came a lady out of the castle and cried aloud, “O Launcelot, Launcelot, as thou art the flower of all knights, help me to get my hawk; for if my hawk be lost, my lord will slay me, he is so hasty.”", "매가 다시 날아오르려 했을 때 그것은 다리가 단단히 걸려 매달리고 말았고, 란셀롯 경은 그것이 매달려 있는 것을 보고 그 아름다운 매가 엉켜 있는 것을 바라보며 가엾이 여겼다. 그때 한 귀부인이 성에서 나와 큰 소리로 외쳤다."], ["“What is your lord’s name?”", "\"오 란셀롯, 란셀롯, 그대가 모든 기사의 꽃이거든, 내 매를 되찾도록 도와 주오. 내 매를 잃으면 내 주인이 나를 죽일 것이오."], ["said Sir Launcelot.", "그분은 성미가 어찌나 급하신지.\""], ["“His name is Sir Phelot, a knight that belongeth to the king of North Wales.”", "\"그대 주인의 이름이 무엇이오?\" 란셀롯 경이 말했다."], ["“Well, fair lady, since ye know my name, and require me of knighthood to help you, I will do what I may to get your hawk; and yet in truth I am an ill climber, and the tree is passing high, and few boughs to help me.”", "\"그분의 이름은 펠롯 경으로, 북웨일스의 왕에게 속한 기사입니다.\" \"좋소, 아름다운 부인이여, 그대가 내 이름을 알고 기사도를 들어 도와 달라 청하니, 내 그대의 매를 되찾기 위해 할 수 있는 일을 하겠소."], ["And therewith Sir Launcelot alighted and tied his horse to the tree, and prayed the lady to unarm him.", "허나 사실 나는 나무를 잘 오르지 못하고, 이 나무는 무척 높은 데다 나를 도울 가지도 거의 없구려.\" 그러고서 란셀롯 경은 말에서 내려 자신의 말을 나무에 매어 두고, 부인에게 자신의 무장을 풀어 달라고 청했다."], ["And when he was unarmed, he put off his jerkin, and with might and force he clomb up to the falcon, and tied the lunys to a rotten bough, and threw the hawk down with it; and the lady got the hawk in her hand.", "그리고 무장을 풀자 윗옷마저 벗어 던지고는, 온 힘을 다하여 매가 있는 곳까지 기어올라 끈을 썩은 가지에 묶어 그 가지째로 매를 아래로 던졌다. 그러자 부인은 매를 손에 받아 들었다."], ["Then suddenly there came out of the castle her husband, all armed, and with his naked sword in his hand, and said, “O Knight Launcelot, now have I got thee as I would,”", "그때 갑자기 그녀의 남편이 완전 무장을 한 채 손에 칼을 빼 들고 성에서 나와 말했다. \"오 기사 란셀롯, 이제 내가 바라던 대로 그대를 손에 넣었노라.\""], ["and stood at the boll of the tree to slay him.", "그러고는 그를 죽이려고 나무 밑동에 버티고 섰다."], ["“Ah, lady!”", "\"아, 부인!\""], ["said Sir Launcelot, “why have ye betrayed me?”", "란셀롯 경이 말했다. \"어찌하여 나를 배신했소?\""], ["“She hath done,”", "\"그녀는,\" 펠롯 경이 말했다."], ["said Sir Phelot, “but as I commanded her; and therefore there is none other way but thine hour is come, and thou must die.”", "\"내가 명한 대로 했을 뿐이다. 그러므로 다른 길은 없으니, 그대의 시각이 다하였고, 그대는 죽어야 한다.\""], ["“That were shame unto thee,”", "\"그것은 그대에게 수치가 될 일이오.\""], ["said Sir Launcelot; “thou an armed knight to slay a naked man by treason.”", "란셀롯 경이 말했다. \"무장한 기사가 맨몸의 사람을 배반으로 죽이다니.\""], ["“Thou gettest none other grace,”", "\"그대에게 다른 자비는 없다.\""], ["said Sir Phelot, “and therefore help thyself if thou canst.”", "펠롯 경이 말했다. \"그러니 할 수 있거든 스스로를 구하라.\""], ["“Alas!”", "\"아아!\""], ["said Sir Launcelot, “that ever a knight should die weaponless!”", "란셀롯 경이 말했다. \"기사가 무기 없이 죽어야 하다니!\""], ["And therewith he turned his eyes upward and downward; and over his head he saw a big bough leafless, and he brake it off from the trunk.", "그러고는 위아래로 눈길을 돌렸다. 그리고 머리 위에 잎이 없는 큰 가지 하나를 보고 그것을 줄기에서 꺾어 냈다."], ["And then he came lower, and watched how his own horse stood; and suddenly he leapt on the further side of his horse from the knight.", "그런 다음 더 아래로 내려와 자신의 말이 어디에 서 있는지 살피더니, 갑자기 기사로부터 먼 쪽으로 말을 사이에 두고 뛰어내렸다."], ["Then Sir Phelot lashed at him eagerly, meaning to have slain him.", "그러자 펠롯 경이 그를 죽이려고 맹렬히 내리쳤다."], ["But Sir Launcelot put away the stroke, with the big bough, and smote Sir Phelot therewith on the side of the head, so that he fell down in a swoon to the ground.", "그러나 란셀롯 경은 그 큰 가지로 일격을 막아 내고, 그것으로 펠롯 경의 머리 옆을 후려쳐 그가 정신을 잃고 땅에 쓰러지게 했다."], ["Then Sir Launcelot took his sword out of his hand and struck his head from the body.", "그러고는 란셀롯 경이 그의 손에서 칼을 빼앗아 그의 목을 몸에서 베어 냈다."], ["Then said the lady, “Alas!", "그러자 부인이 말했다. \"아아!"], ["why hast thou slain my husband?”", "어찌하여 내 남편을 죽였소?\""], ["“I am not the cause,”", "\"내 탓이 아니오.\""], ["said Sir Launcelot, “for with falsehood ye would have slain me, and now it is fallen on yourselves.”", "란셀롯 경이 말했다. \"그대들이 거짓으로 나를 죽이려 했으니, 이제 그것이 그대들 자신에게 떨어진 것이오.\""], ["Thereupon Sir Launcelot got all his armor, and put it upon him hastily, for fear of more resort, for the knight’s castle was so nigh.", "그러고서 란셀롯 경은 자신의 갑옷을 모두 챙겨 서둘러 몸에 둘렀는데, 기사의 성이 그토록 가까이 있어 더 많은 무리가 몰려올까 두려웠기 때문이다."], ["And as soon as he might, he took his horse and departed, and thanked God he had escaped that adventure.", "그리고 되도록 빨리 말을 끌어내어 떠났고, 그 모험에서 벗어난 것을 하느님께 감사했다."]], [["And two days before the feast of Pentecost, Sir Launcelot came home; and the king and all the court were passing glad of his coming.", "그리고 성령강림절 축일 이틀 전에 란셀롯 경은 집으로 돌아왔다. 그리고 왕과 온 궁정이 그의 귀환을 더없이 기뻐했다."], ["And when Sir Gawain, Sir Uwaine, Sir Sagramour, and Sir Hector de Marys saw Sir Launcelot in Sir Kay’s armor then they wist well it was he that smote them down, all with one spear.", "그리고 가웨인 경, 우와인 경, 사그라모르 경, 그리고 〈습지의〉 헥터 경은 케이 경의 갑옷을 입은 란셀롯 경을 보고는, 단 하나의 창으로 자신들 모두를 쓰러뜨린 자가 바로 그였음을 잘 알게 되었다."], ["Then there was laughing and merriment among them; and from time to time came all the knights that Sir Turquine had prisoners, and they all honored and worshipped Sir Launcelot.", "그러자 그들 사이에 웃음과 즐거움이 넘쳐흘렀다. 그리고 투르킨 경이 포로로 잡았던 모든 기사들이 차례차례 돌아와 모두가 란셀롯 경을 기리고 떠받들었다."], ["Then Sir Gaheris said, “I saw all the battle from the beginning to the end,”", "그때 가헤리스 경이 말했다. \"내가 그 싸움을 처음부터 끝까지 보았소.\""], ["and he told King Arthur all how it was.", "그러고는 아서 왕에게 그 모든 일이 어떠했는지 이야기했다."], ["Then Sir Kay told the king how Sir Launcelot had rescued him, and how he “made the knights yield to me, and not to him.”", "그러자 케이 경이 왕에게, 란셀롯 경이 어떻게 자신을 구해 주었으며, 어떻게 \"그가 기사들로 하여금 그가 아니라 내게 항복하게 했는지\" 이야기했다."], ["And there they were, all three, and confirmed it all.", "그리고 그곳에 그 셋이 모두 있어 그 모든 것을 확인해 주었다."], ["“And, by my faith,”", "\"그리고 내 신의를 걸고,\""], ["said Sir Kay, “because Sir Launcelot took my harness and left me his, I rode in peace, and no man would have to do with me.”", "케이 경이 말했다. \"란셀롯 경이 내 갑옷을 가져가고 자기 것을 남겨 둔 덕분에 나는 평온히 말을 달렸고, 아무도 나와 겨루려 하지 않았소.\""]], [["And so at that time Sir Launcelot had the greatest name of any knight of the world, and most was he honored of high and low.", "그리하여 그 무렵 란셀롯 경은 세상의 어느 기사보다도 큰 명성을 얻었고, 높은 자든 낮은 자든 그를 더없이 떠받들었다."]], [["# THE ADVENTURE OF THE CART", "# THE ADVENTURE OF THE CART"]], [["IT befell in the month of May, Queen Guenever called to her knights of the Table Round, and gave them warning that early upon the morrow she would ride a-maying into the woods and fields beside Westminster; “and I warn you that there be none of you but he be well horsed, and that ye all be clothed in green, either silk or cloth; and I shall bring with me ten ladies, and every knight shall have a lady behind him, and every knight shall have a squire and two yeoman, and all well horsed.”", "때는 5월이었다. 왕비 귀네비어는 원탁의 기사들을 불러 모아, 다음 날 아침 일찍 웨스트민스터 곁의 숲과 들판으로 오월맞이 나들이를 가겠다고 알렸다. \"그리고 그대들에게 당부하노니, 누구든 좋은 말을 갖추고, 모두 비단이든 천이든 초록빛 옷을 차려입도록 하라. 나는 시녀 열 명을 데려갈 터이니, 기사마다 한 사람의 시녀를 뒤에 태우고, 또 기사마다 종자 한 명과 향사 두 명을 거느리되, 모두 좋은 말을 갖추도록 하라.\""]], [["“For thus it chanced one morn when all the court, Green-suited, but with plumes that mock’d the May, Had been, their wont, a-maying.”", "\"어느 아침 온 궁정 사람들이 이렇게 되었으니, 초록빛 옷을 차려입고, 오월을 흉내 낸 깃털을 꽂은 채, 그들의 관례대로 오월맞이를 다녀온 것이었다.\""], ["—Guinevere.", "─「귀네비어」."]], [["So they made them ready; and these were the names of the knights: Sir Kay the Seneschal, Sir Agrivaine, Sir Brandiles, Sir Sagramour le Desirus, Sir Dodynas le Sauvage, Sir Ozanna, Sir Ladynas, Sir Persant of Inde, Sir Ironside, and Sir Pelleas; and these ten knights made them ready, in the freshest manner, to ride with the queen.", "그리하여 그들은 채비를 갖추었다. 함께 나선 기사들의 이름은 이러하였다."], ["So upon the morn they took their horses with the queen, and rode a-maying in woods and meadows, as it pleased them, in great joy and delight.", "가령(家令) 케이 경, 아그리바인 경, 브랜다일스 경, 사그라모르 르 데시루스 경, 도디나스 르 사바주 경, 오자나 경, 라디나스 경, 인드의 페르상 경, 아이언사이드 경, 그리고 펠레아스 경이었다. 이 열 명의 기사가 더없이 산뜻한 차림으로 채비를 갖추어 왕비와 함께 말을 달리려 하였다."], ["Now there was a knight named Maleagans, son to King Brademagus, who loved Queen Guenever passing well, and so had he done long and many years.", "그리하여 이튿날 아침 그들은 왕비와 더불어 말에 올라, 마음 내키는 대로 숲과 풀밭을 누비며 큰 기쁨과 즐거움 속에 오월맞이를 다녔다. 그런데 말레아간스라는 이름의 기사가 있었으니, 그는 브래더매거스 왕의 아들로, 왕비 귀네비어를 지극히 사모하였고, 그 마음이 여러 해에 걸쳐 오래도록 이어져 왔다."], ["Now this knight, Sir Maleagans, learned the queen’s purpose, and that she had no men of arms with her but the ten noble knights all arrayed in green for maying; so he prepared him twenty men of arms, and a hundred archers, to take captive the queen and her knights.", "이 기사 말레아간스 경은 왕비의 계획을 알게 되었으며, 왕비가 오월맞이를 위해 모두 초록빛으로 차려입은 열 명의 고귀한 기사 외에는 무장한 자들을 거느리지 않았다는 것도 알게 되었다. 그리하여 그는 무장한 자 스무 명과 궁수 백 명을 갖추어, 왕비와 그 기사들을 사로잡으려 하였다."]], [["“In the merry month of May, In a morn at break of day, With a troop of damsels playing, The Queen, forsooth, went forth a-maying.”", "\"즐거운 오월의 한 달 가운데, 날이 막 밝아 오는 어느 아침, 한 무리의 시녀들과 노닐며, 왕비는 참으로 오월맞이를 나섰도다.\""], ["—Old Song.", "─「옛 노래」."]], [["So when the queen had mayed, and all were bedecked with herbs, mosses, and flowers in the best manner and freshest, right then came out of a wood Sir Maleagans with eightscore men well harnessed, and bade the queen and her knights yield them prisoners.", "그리하여 왕비가 오월맞이를 마치고, 모두가 가장 보기 좋고 산뜻한 모양으로 풀과 이끼와 꽃으로 단장을 하였을 바로 그때, 한 숲에서 말레아간스 경이 잘 무장한 백예순 명의 부하를 거느리고 나타나, 왕비와 그 기사들에게 포로로 항복하라고 명하였다."], ["“Traitor knight,”", "\"반역의 기사여,\""], ["said Queen Guenever, “what wilt thou do?", "하고 왕비 귀네비어가 말하였다. \"그대는 무엇을 하려는 것인가?"], ["Wilt thou shame thyself?", "스스로 욕되게 하려는가?"], ["Bethink thee how thou art a king’s son, and a knight of the Table Round, and how thou art about to dishonor all knighthood and thyself?”", "그대가 왕의 아들이요 원탁의 기사임을, 그리고 모든 기사도와 그대 자신을 욕보이려 하고 있음을 생각해 보라.\" \"어찌 되든 좋소,\""], ["“Be it as it may,”", "하고 말레아간스 경이 말하였다."], ["said Sir Maleagans, “know you well, madam, I have loved you many a year and never till now could I get you to such advantage as I do now; and therefore I will take you as I find you.”", "\"잘 아시오, 부인, 나는 여러 해 동안 그대를 사모해 왔으나 이제껏 지금처럼 그대를 손에 넣을 좋은 기회를 얻지 못하였소. 그러니 지금 마주한 그대로 그대를 데려가겠소.\""], ["Then the ten knights of the Round Table drew their swords, and the other party run at them with their spears, and the ten knights manfully abode them, and smote away their spears.", "그러자 원탁의 열 기사가 칼을 빼어 들었고, 상대편은 창을 들고 그들에게 달려들었으며, 열 기사는 사내답게 맞서 그 창들을 쳐내었다. 이윽고 그들은 칼을 맞대고 싸우다가 여럿이 땅바닥에 쓰러졌다."], ["Then they lashed together with swords till several were smitten to the earth.", "그리하여 왕비는 자기 기사들이 이토록 비참하게 짓눌리어 마침내 죽임을 당할 수밖에 없음을 보고, 가엾고 슬픈 마음에 부르짖었다. \"말레아간스 경, 내 고귀한 기사들을 죽이지 말라."], ["So when the queen saw her knights thus dolefully oppressed, and needs must be slain at the last, then for pity and sorrow she cried, “Sir Maleagans, slay not my noble knights and I will go with you, upon this covenant, that they be led with me wheresoever thou leadest me.”", "그러면 내가 그대와 함께 가겠으니, 다만 한 가지 약조하라. 그대가 나를 어디로 데려가든 이들도 나와 함께 데려가도록 하라.\""], ["“Madame,”", "\"부인,\""], ["said Maleagans, “for your sake they shall be led with you into my own castle, if that ye will be ruled, and ride with me.”", "하고 말레아간스가 말하였다. \"그대를 위하여 이들도 그대와 함께 내 성으로 데려가리다."], ["Then Sir Maleagans charged them all that none should depart from the queen, for he dreaded lest Sir Launcelot should have knowledge of what had been done.", "다만 그대가 순순히 따라 나와 함께 말을 달린다면 말이오.\" 그러고 나서 말레아간스 경은 누구도 왕비 곁을 떠나지 못하도록 모두에게 엄히 명하였으니, 이는 란슬롯 경이 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 알게 될까 두려워한 까닭이었다."]], [["Then the queen privily called unto her a page of her chamber that was swiftly horsed, to whom she said, “Go thou when thou seest thy time, and bear this ring unto Sir Launcelot, and pray him as he loveth me, that he will see me and rescue me.", "이윽고 왕비는 발 빠른 말을 가진 침소의 시동 하나를 은밀히 불러 이렇게 일렀다. \"때를 보아 떠나, 이 반지를 란슬롯 경에게 전하고, 그가 나를 사랑한다면 부디 나를 만나 구해 달라고 청하라."], ["And spare not thy horse,”", "그리고 말을 아끼지 말라,\""], ["said the queen, “neither for water nor for land.”", "하고 왕비가 말하였다. \"물길이든 뭍길이든 가리지 말라.\""], ["So the child espied his time, and lightly he took his horse with the spurs and departed as fast as he might.", "그리하여 그 아이는 때를 엿보아, 날래게 말에 박차를 가하여 있는 힘껏 떠났다."], ["And when Sir Maleagans saw him so flee, he understood that it was by the queen’s commandment for to warn Sir Launcelot.", "말레아간스 경은 그가 이렇게 달아나는 것을 보고, 그것이 란슬롯 경에게 알리려는 왕비의 분부임을 알아차렸다."], ["Then they that were best horsed chased him, and shot at him, but the child went from them all.", "그러자 가장 좋은 말을 탄 자들이 그를 쫓아 활을 쏘았으나, 그 아이는 모두에게서 빠져나갔다."], ["Then Sir Maleagans said to the queen, “Madam, ye are about to betray me, but I shall arrange for Sir Launcelot that he shall not come lightly at you.”", "그러자 말레아간스 경이 왕비에게 말하였다. \"부인, 그대는 나를 배신하려 하나, 나는 란슬롯 경이 쉽사리 그대에게 이르지 못하도록 손을 써 두겠소.\""], ["Then he rode with her and them all to his castle, in all the haste that they might.", "그러고 나서 그는 왕비와 그 무리 모두를 데리고 있는 힘을 다해 서둘러 자기 성으로 말을 달렸다."], ["And by the way Sir Maleagans laid in ambush the best archers that he had to wait for Sir Launcelot.", "그리고 가는 길에 말레아간스 경은 자기가 거느린 가장 뛰어난 궁수들을 매복시켜 란슬롯 경을 기다리게 하였다."], ["And the child came to Westminster and found Sir Launcelot and told his message and delivered him the queen’s ring.", "그 아이는 웨스트민스터에 이르러 란슬롯 경을 찾아 전갈을 전하고 왕비의 반지를 건네었다."], ["“Alas!”", "\"아아!\""], ["said Sir Launcelot, “now am I shamed for ever, unless I may rescue that noble lady.”", "하고 란슬롯 경이 말하였다. \"이제 저 고귀한 부인을 구해 내지 못한다면 나는 영영 욕됨을 면치 못하리라.\""], ["Then eagerly he asked his armor and put it on him, and mounted his horse and rode as fast as he might; and men say he took the water at Westminster Bridge, and made his horse swim over Thames unto Lambeth.", "그러고 나서 그는 다급히 갑옷을 가져오게 하여 입고, 말에 올라 있는 힘껏 달렸다. 사람들이 전하기를, 그는 웨스트민스터 다리에서 물에 뛰어들어 말을 헤엄치게 하여 템스 강을 건너 램버스에 이르렀다고 한다."], ["Then within a while he came to a wood where was a narrow way; and there the archers were laid in ambush.", "이윽고 얼마 지나지 않아 그는 좁은 길이 난 어느 숲에 이르렀고, 그곳에 궁수들이 매복해 있었다."], ["And they shot at him and smote his horse so that he fell.", "그들이 그에게 활을 쏘아 그의 말을 맞히니, 말이 쓰러졌다."], ["Then Sir Launcelot left his horse and went on foot, but there lay so many ditches and hedges betwixt the archers and him that he might not meddle with them.", "그러자 란슬롯 경은 말을 버리고 걸어서 나아갔으나, 궁수들과 그 사이에는 도랑과 울타리가 하도 많이 가로놓여 있어 그들과 맞붙을 수가 없었다. \"아아!"], ["“Alas! for shame,”", "부끄러운 일이로다,\""], ["said Sir Launcelot, “that ever one knight should betray another!", "하고 란슬롯 경이 말하였다. \"한 기사가 다른 기사를 배신하다니!"], ["but it is an old saw, a good man is never in danger, but when he is in danger of a coward.”", "허나 옛말에 이르기를, 선한 사람은 결코 위험에 처하지 아니하나, 다만 비겁한 자로 인한 위험에는 처할 수 있다 하였다.\""], ["Then Sir Launcelot went awhile and he was exceedingly cumbered by his armor, his shield, and his spear, and all that belonged to him.", "그러고 나서 란슬롯 경은 한동안 걸었으나, 갑옷과 방패와 창과 그 밖에 몸에 지닌 온갖 것 때문에 몹시 거추장스러웠다."], ["Then by chance there came by him a cart that came thither to fetch wood.", "그때 마침 나무를 실으러 그곳으로 온 수레 한 대가 그의 곁을 지나갔다."]], [["Now at this time carts were little used except for carrying offal and for conveying criminals to execution.", "이 무렵에 수레란 짐승의 내장 따위를 나르거나 죄인을 형장으로 실어 가는 데 외에는 거의 쓰이지 않았다."], ["But Sir Launcelot took no thought of anything but the necessity of haste for the purpose of rescuing the queen; so he demanded of the carter that he should take him in and convey him as speedily as possible for a liberal reward.", "그러나 란슬롯 경은 왕비를 구해야 한다는 급한 마음 외에는 아무것도 헤아리지 않았다. 그리하여 그는 수레꾼에게 자기를 태워 후한 삯을 줄 터이니 되도록 빨리 데려가 달라고 청하였다."], ["The carter consented, and Sir Launcelot placed himself in the cart and only lamented that with much jolting he made but little progress.", "수레꾼이 응낙하자, 란슬롯 경은 수레에 몸을 실었으나, 몹시 덜컹거리는 통에 길이 좀처럼 나아가지 않음을 한탄할 따름이었다."], ["Then it happened Sir Gawain passed by and seeing an armed knight travelling in that unusual way he drew near to see who it might be.", "그때 마침 가웨인 경이 지나가다가, 무장한 기사가 그토록 별난 모양으로 가는 것을 보고는 그가 누구인지 보려고 가까이 다가왔다."], ["Then Sir Launcelot told him how the queen had been carried off, and how, in hastening to her rescue, his horse had been disabled and he had been compelled to avail himself of the cart rather than give up his enterprise.", "그러자 란슬롯 경은 왕비가 어떻게 끌려갔는지, 그리고 그 구원을 위해 서두르던 중에 말이 못 쓰게 되어 자기 일을 단념하느니 차라리 수레의 신세를 질 수밖에 없었던 사정을 그에게 일러 주었다. 그러자 가웨인 경이 말하였다."], ["Then Sir Gawain said, “Surely it is unworthy of a knight to travel in such sort;”", "\"기사가 그런 모양으로 다니는 것은 참으로 어울리지 아니하오.\""], ["but Sir Launcelot heeded him not.", "그러나 란슬롯 경은 그의 말에 마음 쓰지 않았다."]], [["At nightfall they arrived at a castle and the lady thereof came out at the head of her damsels to welcome Sir Gawain.", "날이 저물 무렵 그들은 어느 성에 이르렀고, 그 성의 부인이 시녀들을 거느리고 앞장서 나와 가웨인 경을 맞이하였다."], ["But to admit his companion, whom she supposed to be a criminal, or at least a prisoner, it pleased her not; however, to oblige Sir Gawain, she consented.", "그러나 죄인이거나 적어도 포로라고 여겨지는 그의 일행을 들이는 일은 그녀에게 마뜩잖았으나, 가웨인 경을 위하여 응낙하였다."], ["At supper Sir Launcelot came near being consigned to the kitchen and was only admitted to the lady’s table at the earnest solicitation of Sir Gawain.", "저녁 식사 때 란슬롯 경은 하마터면 부엌으로 보내질 뻔하였고, 가웨인 경의 간곡한 청 덕분에야 비로소 부인의 식탁에 앉을 수 있었다."], ["Neither would the damsels prepare a bed for him.", "시녀들 또한 그를 위해 잠자리를 마련해 주려 하지 않았다."], ["He seized the first he found unoccupied and was left undisturbed.", "그는 비어 있는 잠자리를 처음 눈에 띄는 대로 차지하였고, 누구의 방해도 받지 않은 채 그대로 두어졌다."]], [["Next morning he saw from the turrets of the castle a train accompanying a lady, whom he imagined to be the queen.", "이튿날 아침 그는 성의 망루에서 어떤 부인을 호위하며 가는 한 무리를 보았는데, 그 부인이 왕비이리라 짐작하였다."], ["Sir Gawain thought it might be so, and became equally eager to depart.", "가웨인 경도 그럴 법하다 여겨, 마찬가지로 떠나기를 서둘렀다."], ["The lady of the castle supplied Sir Launcelot with a horse and they traversed the plain at full speed.", "성의 부인이 란슬롯 경에게 말 한 필을 내어 주니, 두 사람은 들판을 전속력으로 가로질렀다."], ["They learned from some travellers whom they met, that there were two roads which led to the castle of Sir Maleagans.", "그들은 도중에 만난 몇몇 나그네에게서, 말레아간스 경의 성으로 이르는 길이 둘 있다는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["Here therefore the friends separated.", "그리하여 이곳에서 두 벗은 헤어졌다."], ["Sir Launcelot found his way beset with obstacles, which he encountered successfully, but not without much loss of time.", "란슬롯 경은 자기 앞길이 온갖 장애로 막혀 있음을 알았으나, 적지 않은 시간을 허비하기는 하였어도 그것들을 무사히 헤쳐 나갔다."], ["As evening approached he was met by a young and sportive damsel, who gayly proposed to him a supper at her castle.", "저녁이 다가올 무렵 그는 젊고 쾌활한 시녀 하나를 만났는데, 그녀는 명랑하게 자기 성에서 저녁을 들고 가라고 권하였다."], ["The knight, who was hungry and weary, accepted the offer, though with no very good grace.", "굶주리고 지쳐 있던 그 기사는 그리 흔쾌한 기색은 아니었으나 그 청을 받아들였다."], ["He followed the lady to her castle and ate voraciously of her supper, but was quite impenetrable to all her amorous advances.", "그는 부인을 따라 그녀의 성으로 가서 저녁을 게걸스레 먹었으나, 그녀의 온갖 사랑의 수작에는 조금도 마음이 움직이지 않았다. 그런데 갑자기 정경이 바뀌더니, 그는 여섯 명의 사나운 악당에게 습격을 당하였다."], ["Suddenly the scene changed and he was assailed by six furious ruffians, whom he dealt with so vigorously that most of them were speedily disabled, when again there was a change and he found himself alone with his fair hostess, who informed him that she was none other than his guardian fairy, who had but subjected him to tests of his courage and fidelity.", "그가 어찌나 힘차게 맞섰던지 대부분이 이내 못 쓰게 되었을 때, 또다시 정경이 바뀌어 그는 그 아름다운 안주인과 단둘이 있게 되었다. 그녀는 자기가 다름 아닌 그의 수호 요정이며, 다만 그의 용기와 충절을 시험해 본 것일 따름이라고 알려 주었다."], ["The next day the fairy brought him on his road, and before parting gave him a ring, which she told him would by its changes of color disclose to him all enchantments, and enable him to subdue them.", "이튿날 그 요정은 그를 길로 데려다주었고, 헤어지기에 앞서 반지 하나를 주며, 그 반지가 색이 변함으로써 그에게 온갖 마법을 드러내 보여, 그것들을 제압할 수 있게 해 주리라고 일러 주었다."]], [["Sir Launcelot pursued his journey, without being much incommoded except by the taunts of travellers, who all seemed to have learned, by some means, his disgraceful drive in the cart.", "란슬롯 경은 여정을 이어 갔으나, 나그네들의 조롱 외에는 별로 시달릴 것이 없었다. 그 나그네들은 어떤 경로로인지 모두 그가 수레를 타고 갔던 수치스러운 일을 알게 된 듯하였다."], ["One, more insolent than the rest, had the audacity to interrupt him during dinner, and even to risk a battle in support of his pleasantry.", "그 가운데 누구보다도 무례한 자 하나가 뻔뻔스럽게도 식사 도중에 그를 가로막더니, 제 농지거리를 두둔하느라 싸움까지도 마다하지 않았다."], ["Launcelot, after an easy victory, only doomed him to be carted in his turn.", "란슬롯은 손쉽게 이긴 뒤, 그자에게 이번에는 제가 수레에 실리는 벌을 내리는 것으로 그쳤다."]], [["At night he was received at another castle, with great apparent hospitality, but found himself in the morning in a dungeon, and loaded with chains.", "밤에 그는 또 다른 성에서 겉으로는 극진한 환대를 받았으나, 아침이 되자 사슬에 묶인 채 지하 감옥에 갇혀 있는 자신을 발견하였다."], ["Consulting his ring, and finding that this was an enchantment, he burst his chains, seized his armor in spite of the visionary monsters who attempted to defend it, broke open the gates of the tower, and continued his journey.", "반지를 들여다보아 이것이 마법임을 알아차린 그는 사슬을 끊고, 그것을 지키려 드는 허깨비 괴물들에도 아랑곳없이 갑옷을 챙겨 들고, 탑의 문을 부수어 열고는 여정을 이어 갔다."], ["At length his progress was checked by a wide and rapid torrent, which could only be passed on a narrow bridge, on which a false step would prove his destruction.", "마침내 그의 길은 넓고 거센 급류에 가로막혔으니, 그것은 좁은 다리로만 건널 수 있었고, 그 위에서 한 발만 잘못 디뎌도 목숨을 잃을 판이었다."], ["Launcelot, leading his horse by the bridle, and making him swim by his side, passed over the bridge, and was attacked as soon as he reached the bank by a lion and a leopard, both of which he slew, and then, exhausted and bleeding, seated himself on the grass, and endeavored to bind up his wounds, when he was accosted by Brademagus, the father of Maleagans, whose castle was then in sight, and at no great distance.", "란슬롯은 고삐를 쥐어 말을 끌고 곁에서 헤엄치게 하며 다리를 건넜는데, 강기슭에 닿자마자 사자 한 마리와 표범 한 마리에게 습격을 당하였다. 그는 두 마리를 모두 죽이고, 이내 기진하여 피를 흘리며 풀밭에 앉아 상처를 싸매려 하였다."], ["This king, no less courteous than his son was haughty and insolent, after complimenting Sir Launcelot on the valor and skill he had displayed in the perils of the bridge and the wild beasts, offered him his assistance, and informed him that the queen was safe in his castle, but could only be rescued by encountering Maleagans.", "그때 말레아간스의 아비인 브래더매거스가 그에게 다가와 말을 건네었으니, 그의 성은 그곳에서 보이는 곳, 그리 멀지 않은 데에 있었다. 이 왕은 그 아들이 거만하고 무례한 것과는 달리 못지않게 정중하여, 다리와 사나운 짐승들의 위험 속에서 란슬롯 경이 보여 준 용맹과 솜씨를 칭송한 뒤 그에게 도움을 베풀겠노라 하였고, 왕비가 자기 성에 무사히 있으나 다만 말레아간스와 맞서 싸워야만 구해 낼 수 있다고 일러 주었다."], ["Launcelot demanded the battle for the next day, and accordingly it took place, at the foot of the tower, and under the eyes of the fair captive.", "란슬롯은 그 이튿날 결투를 청하였고, 그리하여 그 일은 탑 아래에서, 아름다운 사로잡힌 부인의 눈앞에서 벌어졌다."], ["Launcelot was enfeebled by his wounds, and fought not with his usual spirit, and the contest for a time was doubtful; till Guenever exclaimed, “Ah, Launcelot!", "란슬롯은 상처로 쇠약해져 평소의 기백으로 싸우지 못하였고, 한동안 승부를 가늠하기 어려웠다. 그러다가 귀네비어가 부르짖었다."], ["my knight, truly have I been told that thou art no longer worthy of me!”", "\"아, 란슬롯! 나의 기사여, 그대가 더는 나에게 어울리는 사람이 아니라고 들은 것이 참말이로구나!\""], ["These words instantly revived the drooping knight; he resumed at once his usual superiority, and soon laid at his feet his haughty adversary.", "이 말은 이내 기운을 잃었던 기사를 되살려 놓았다. 그는 단숨에 평소의 우세를 되찾아, 오만한 적수를 곧 제 발치에 쓰러뜨렸다."]], [["He was on the point of sacrificing him to his resentment, when Guenever, moved by the entreaties of Brademagus, ordered him to withhold the blow, and he obeyed.", "그가 막 원한을 풀려고 그를 죽이려는 찰나에, 귀네비어가 브래더매거스의 간청에 마음이 움직여 그 일격을 거두라고 명하니, 그는 따랐다."], ["The castle and its prisoners were now at his disposal.", "이제 성과 그 안의 포로들은 그의 손안에 있었다."], ["Launcelot hastened to the apartment of the queen, threw himself at her feet, and was about to kiss her hand, when she exclaimed, “Ah, Launcelot!", "란슬롯은 왕비의 거처로 서둘러 가서 그 발치에 몸을 던지고 막 그 손에 입 맞추려 하였다. 그때 그녀가 부르짖었다."], ["why do I see thee again, yet feel thee to be no longer worthy of me, after having been disgracefully drawn about the country in a—”", "\"아, 란슬롯! 어찌하여 내가 다시 그대를 보면서도, 그대가 수치스럽게 나라 안 곳곳을 수레에 실려 끌려다닌 뒤로는 더는 나에게 어울리는 사람이 아니라고 느끼는 것인가…….\""], ["She had not time to finish the phrase, for her lover suddenly started from her, and, bitterly lamenting that he had incurred the displeasure of his sovereign lady, rushed out of the castle, threw his sword and his shield to the right and left, ran furiously into the woods, and disappeared.", "그녀는 그 말을 미처 끝맺지 못하였으니, 그녀의 연인이 별안간 그녀에게서 몸을 일으켜, 자신이 섬기는 부인의 노여움을 산 것을 비통히 한탄하며 성 밖으로 뛰쳐나가, 칼과 방패를 좌우로 내던지고 사납게 숲속으로 달려 들어가 사라져 버린 까닭이었다."]], [["It seems that the story of the abominable cart, which haunted Launcelot at every step, had reached the ears of Sir Kay, who had told it to the queen, as a proof that her knight must have been dishonored.", "란슬롯을 걸음마다 따라다니던 그 가증스러운 수레 이야기가 케이 경의 귀에까지 들어간 모양이었고, 케이 경이 그것을 왕비에게 일러바치며 그녀의 기사가 욕을 당하였음이 틀림없다는 증거로 삼은 듯하였다."], ["But Guenever had full leisure to repent the haste with which she had given credit to the tale.", "그러나 귀네비어는 그 이야기를 그토록 성급히 믿어 버린 것을 두고두고 뉘우칠 겨를을 갖게 되었다."], ["Three days elapsed, during which Launcelot wandered without knowing where he went, till at last he began to reflect that his mistress had doubtless been deceived by misrepresentation, and that it was his duty to set her right.", "사흘이 지나도록 란슬롯은 제가 어디로 가는지도 모른 채 헤매다가, 마침내 자기가 섬기는 부인이 필시 잘못 전해진 말에 속은 것이며, 그녀의 오해를 바로잡는 것이 자기 본분임을 헤아리기 시작하였다."], ["He therefore returned, compelled Maleagans to release his prisoners, and, taking the road by which they expected the arrival of Sir Gawain, had the satisfaction of meeting him the next day; after which the whole company proceeded gayly towards Camelot.", "그리하여 그는 돌아가 말레아간스로 하여금 포로들을 풀어 주게 하였고, 가웨인 경의 도착을 기다리던 길로 접어들어, 이튿날 그를 만나는 기쁨을 누렸다. 그러고 나서 일행 모두는 즐겁게 카멜롯을 향해 나아갔다."]], [["————", "────"]], [["# THE LADY OF SHALOTT", "# THE LADY OF SHALOTT"]], [["KING ARTHUR proclaimed a solemn tournament to be held at Winchester.", "아서 왕은 윈체스터에서 성대한 마상 시합을 열겠노라고 선포했다."], ["The king, not less impatient than his knights for this festival, set off some days before to superintend the preparations, leaving the queen with her court at Camelot.", "왕은 그의 기사들 못지않게 이 축제를 기다리는 마음이 간절했던 까닭에, 며칠 앞서 출발해 준비를 감독하면서 왕비와 그 궁정 사람들은 카멜롯에 남겨 두었다."], ["Sir Launcelot, under pretence of indisposition, remained behind also.", "랜슬롯 경은 몸이 좋지 못하다는 핑계로 역시 뒤에 남았다."], ["His intention was to attend the tournament in disguise; and having communicated his project to Guenever, he mounted his horse, set off without any attendant, and, counterfeiting the feebleness of age, took the most unfrequented road to Winchester, and passed unnoticed as an old knight who was going to be a spectator of the sports.", "그의 의도는 변장을 한 채 시합에 참가하려는 것이었다. 그래서 그는 자신의 계획을 귀네비어에게 알린 뒤, 말에 올라 수행원 하나 없이 출발했고, 늙은이의 쇠약함을 가장하여 가장 인적이 드문 길을 골라 윈체스터로 향했으며, 시합을 구경하러 가는 어느 늙은 기사처럼 아무에게도 주목받지 않고 지나갔다."], ["Even Arthur and Gawain, who happened to behold him from the windows of a castle under which he passed, were the dupes of his disguise.", "그가 지나가는 성의 창문에서 우연히 그를 바라본 아서와 가웨인조차 그의 변장에 속아 넘어갔다. 하지만 한 가지 사고가 그의 정체를 드러내고 말았다."], ["But an accident betrayed him. His horse happened to stumble, and the hero, forgetting for a moment his assumed character, recovered the animal with a strength and agility so peculiar to himself, that they instantly recognized the inimitable Launcelot.", "그의 말이 그만 비틀거렸는데, 영웅은 한순간 자신이 가장한 배역을 잊은 채 그만의 특유한 힘과 민첩함으로 그 짐승을 다시 세웠고, 그 덕에 그들은 단번에 흉내 낼 수 없는 랜슬롯을 알아보았다."], ["They suffered him, however, to proceed on his journey without interruption, convinced that his extraordinary feats of arms must discover him at the approaching festival.", "그러나 그들은 다가오는 축제에서 그의 비범한 무예가 반드시 그의 정체를 드러내리라 확신했으므로, 그가 방해받지 않고 여정을 계속하도록 내버려 두었다."]], [["In the evening Launcelot was magnificently entertained as a stranger knight at the neighboring castle of Shalott.", "저녁에 랜슬롯은 인근의 샬롯 성에서 낯선 기사로서 융숭한 대접을 받았다."], ["The lord of this castle had a daughter of exquisite beauty, and two sons lately received into the order of knighthood, one of whom was at that time ill in bed, and thereby prevented from attending the tournament, for which both brothers had long made preparation.", "이 성의 영주에게는 빼어난 미모를 지닌 딸 하나와, 최근에 기사 작위를 받은 두 아들이 있었는데, 그중 하나는 그때 병이 나 자리에 누워 있어 마상 시합에 참가하지 못하게 된 처지였으니, 두 형제는 오래전부터 그 시합을 준비해 왔던 것이다."], ["Launcelot offered to attend the other, if he were permitted to borrow the armor of the invalid, and the lord of Shalott, without knowing the name of his guest, being satisfied from his appearance that his son could not have a better assistant in arms, most thankfully accepted the offer.", "랜슬롯은 병든 아들의 갑옷을 빌려 입을 수만 있다면 다른 아들을 수행하겠노라 제안했고, 샬롯의 영주는 손님의 이름을 알지 못한 채로도 그 풍모만 보고 자기 아들이 이보다 더 나은 무예의 조력자를 얻을 수 없으리라 흡족해하며 그 제안을 진심으로 고맙게 받아들였다."], ["In the meantime the young lady, who had been much struck by the first appearance of the stranger knight, continued to survey him with increased attention, and, before the conclusion of supper, became so deeply enamoured of him, that after frequent changes of color, and other symptoms which Sir Launcelot could not possibly mistake, she was obliged to retire to her chamber, and seek relief in tears.", "그러는 동안 낯선 기사의 첫인상에 크게 마음을 빼앗긴 젊은 처녀는 점점 더 주의를 기울여 그를 살폈고, 저녁 식사가 끝나기도 전에 그를 너무도 깊이 연모하게 된 나머지, 여러 차례 얼굴빛이 변하고 또 랜슬롯 경이 도저히 잘못 알아볼 수 없는 다른 징후들을 드러내다가, 끝내 자기 방으로 물러나 눈물 속에서 위안을 구할 수밖에 없었다."], ["Sir Launcelot hastened to convey to her, by means of her brother, the information that his heart was already disposed of, but that it would be his pride and pleasure to act as her knight at the approaching tournament.", "랜슬롯 경은 그녀의 오라비를 통해, 자기 마음은 이미 다른 데로 정해졌으나 다가오는 시합에서 그녀의 기사로서 활약하는 것은 자신의 긍지이자 기쁨이 되리라는 뜻을 서둘러 전했다."], ["The lady, obliged to be satisfied with that courtesy, presented him her scarf to be worn at the tournament.", "그 예의에 만족할 수밖에 없었던 처녀는 시합에서 두르라며 자신의 스카프를 그에게 건넸다."]], [["Launcelot set off in the morning with the young knight, who, on their approaching Winchester, carried him to the castle of a lady, sister to the lord of Shalott, by whom they were hospitably entertained.", "랜슬롯은 아침에 그 젊은 기사와 함께 출발했는데, 윈체스터에 가까워지자 젊은 기사는 그를 샬롯 영주의 누이인 어느 귀부인의 성으로 데려갔고, 그들은 그곳에서 후한 대접을 받았다."], ["The next day they put on their armor, which was perfectly plain and without any device, as was usual to youths during the first year of knighthood, their shields being only painted red, as some color was necessary to enable them to be recognized by their attendants.", "이튿날 그들은 갑옷을 입었는데, 그 갑옷은 기사 작위를 받은 첫해의 젊은이들에게 으레 그러하듯 아무런 문양도 없이 완전히 단순한 것이었으며, 다만 방패만은 붉게 칠해져 있었으니, 수행원들이 그들을 알아볼 수 있으려면 어떤 색이든 필요했기 때문이다."], ["Launcelot wore on his crest the scarf of the maid of Shalott, and, thus equipped, proceeded to the tournament, where the knights were divided into two companies, the one commanded by Sir Galehaut, the other by King Arthur.", "랜슬롯은 투구 장식에 샬롯의 처녀가 준 스카프를 둘렀고, 그렇게 차림을 갖추고 시합장으로 나아갔는데, 그곳에서 기사들은 두 패로 나뉘어 한쪽은 갈레호 경이, 다른 한쪽은 아서 왕이 지휘하고 있었다."], ["Having surveyed the combat for a short time from without the lists, and observed that Sir Galehaut’s party began to give way, they joined the press and attacked the royal knights, the young man choosing such adversaries as were suited to his strength, while his companion selected the principal champions of the Round Table, and successively overthrew Gawain, Bohort, and Lionel.", "그들은 시합장 밖에서 잠시 싸움을 살피다가 갈레호 경 편이 밀리기 시작하는 것을 보고는, 무리 속으로 뛰어들어 왕의 기사들을 공격했으니, 젊은이는 제 힘에 걸맞은 상대들을 골랐고, 그의 동행은 원탁의 주요 용사들을 골라 가웨인과 보호트와 라이오넬을 차례로 거꾸러뜨렸다."], ["The astonishment of the spectators was extreme, for it was thought that no one but Launcelot could possess such invincible force; yet the favor on his crest seemed to preclude the possibility of his being thus disguised, for Launcelot had never been known to wear the badge of any but his sovereign lady.", "구경꾼들의 놀라움은 더할 나위 없었으니, 그토록 무적의 힘을 지닐 수 있는 자는 랜슬롯 말고는 없다고 여겨졌기 때문이다. 그러나 그의 투구 장식에 달린 정표는 그가 그렇게 변장했을 가능성을 배제하는 듯했으니, 랜슬롯이 자신의 군주이신 귀부인 외에는 그 누구의 표식도 단 적이 없었던 까닭이다."], ["At length Sir Hector, Launcelot’s brother, engaged him, and, after a dreadful combat, wounded him dangerously in the head, but was himself completely stunned by a blow on the helmet, and felled to the ground; after which the conqueror rode off at full speed, attended by his companion.", "마침내 랜슬롯의 형제인 헥터 경이 그와 맞붙어, 무시무시한 격투 끝에 그의 머리에 위험한 상처를 입혔으나, 그 자신도 투구에 일격을 맞아 완전히 정신을 잃고 땅에 쓰러지고 말았다. 그 뒤 승자는 동행을 거느린 채 전속력으로 말을 달려 떠났다."]], [["They returned to the castle of Shalott, where Launcelot was attended with the greatest care by the good earl, by his two sons, and, above all, by his fair daughter, whose medical skill probably much hastened the period of his recovery.", "그들은 샬롯 성으로 돌아왔고, 그곳에서 랜슬롯은 선량한 백작과 그의 두 아들, 그리고 무엇보다도 그 아름다운 딸의 지극한 보살핌을 받았는데, 그녀의 의술은 아마도 그의 회복기를 크게 앞당겼을 것이다."], ["His health was almost completely restored, when Sir Hector, Sir Bohort, and Sir Lionel, who, after the return of the court to Camelot, had undertaken the quest of their relation, discovered him walking on the walls of the castle.", "그의 건강이 거의 완전히 회복되었을 무렵, 궁정이 카멜롯으로 돌아간 뒤 친족을 찾아 나섰던 헥터 경과 보호트 경과 라이오넬 경이 성벽 위를 거니는 그를 발견했다."], ["Their meeting was very joyful; they passed three days in the castle amidst constant festivities, and bantered each other on the events of the tournament.", "그들의 만남은 무척이나 기뻤다. 그들은 끊임없는 잔치 속에서 사흘을 성에서 보내며 시합에서 있었던 일들을 두고 서로 농을 주고받았다."], ["Launcelot, though he began by vowing vengeance against the author of his wound, yet ended by declaring that he felt rewarded for the pain by the pride he took in witnessing his brother’s extraordinary prowess.", "랜슬롯은 처음에는 자신에게 상처를 입힌 자에게 복수하겠노라 맹세하며 말문을 열었으나, 끝에 가서는 자기 형제의 비범한 무용을 목격한 데서 느낀 긍지로써 그 고통의 보상을 받았노라 선언하였다."], ["He then dismissed them with a message to the queen, promising to follow immediately, it being necessary that he should first take a formal leave of his kind hosts, as well as of the fair maid of Shalott.", "그러고 나서 그는 왕비에게 전할 전언을 맡겨 그들을 떠나보내며, 곧 뒤따르겠노라 약속했으니, 자신을 친절히 맞아 준 주인들에게는 물론이고 샬롯의 아름다운 처녀에게도 먼저 격식을 갖추어 작별을 고해야 했기 때문이다."]], [["The young lady, after vainly attempting to detain him by her tears and solicitations, saw him depart without leaving her any ground for hope.", "젊은 처녀는 눈물과 간청으로 그를 붙들어 보려 헛되이 애쓴 끝에, 그가 자신에게 어떤 희망의 근거도 남기지 않고 떠나가는 모습을 바라보았다."]], [["It was early summer when the tournament took place; but some months had passed since Launcelot’s departure, and winter was now near at hand.", "마상 시합이 열린 것은 초여름이었다. 그러나 랜슬롯이 떠난 뒤로 몇 달이 흘렀고, 이제 겨울이 가까이 다가와 있었다."], ["The health and strength of the Lady of Shalott had gradually sunk, and she felt that she could not live apart from the object of her affections.", "샬롯의 귀부인은 건강과 기력이 차츰 쇠하였고, 자기가 사랑하는 이와 떨어져서는 살아갈 수 없으리라 느꼈다."], ["She left the castle, and descending to the river’s brink placed herself in a boat, which she loosed from its moorings, and suffered to bear her down the current toward Camelot.", "그녀는 성을 떠나 강기슭으로 내려가 한 척의 배에 몸을 실었으며, 그 배를 매어 둔 줄을 풀어 물살에 자신을 맡긴 채 카멜롯을 향해 떠내려가도록 두었다."]], [["One morning, as Arthur and Sir Lionel looked from the window of the tower, the walls of which were washed by a river, they descried a boat richly ornamented, and covered with an awning of cloth of gold, which appeared to be floating down the stream without any human guidance.", "어느 날 아침, 아서와 라이오넬 경이 강물이 그 벽을 씻어 내리는 탑의 창문에서 내다보고 있을 때, 그들은 화려하게 장식되고 금실로 짠 천막으로 덮인 배 한 척이 사람의 인도라곤 전혀 없이 강물을 따라 떠내려오는 것을 발견했다."], ["It struck the shore while they watched it, and they hastened down to examine it.", "그들이 지켜보는 사이 배는 기슭에 닿았고, 그들은 그것을 살피러 서둘러 내려갔다."], ["Beneath the awning they discovered the dead body of a beautiful woman, in whose features Sir Lionel easily recognized the lovely maid of Shalott.", "천막 아래에서 그들은 한 아름다운 여인의 주검을 발견했는데, 라이오넬 경은 그 이목구비에서 어렵지 않게 샬롯의 사랑스러운 처녀를 알아보았다."], ["Pursuing their search, they discovered a purse richly embroidered with gold and jewels, and within the purse a letter, which Arthur opened, and found addressed to himself and all the knights of the Round Table, stating that Launcelot of the Lake, the most accomplished of knights and most beautiful of men, but at the same time the most cruel and inflexible, had by his rigor produced the death of the wretched maiden, whose love was no less invincible than his cruelty.", "더 살펴 나가던 그들은 금실과 보석으로 화려하게 수놓인 지갑 하나를 발견했고, 그 지갑 안에서 편지 한 통을 찾아냈으니, 아서가 그것을 펼쳐 보자 자신과 원탁의 모든 기사에게 부쳐진 것으로, 호수의 랜슬롯, 곧 기사들 가운데 가장 출중하고 사내들 가운데 가장 아름다우나 동시에 가장 잔인하고 완고한 그가, 그 모진 태도로써 불쌍한 처녀의 죽음을 불러왔다는 내용이었으며, 그녀의 사랑은 그의 잔인함 못지않게 무적이었노라 적혀 있었다."], ["The king immediately gave orders for the interment of the lady with all the honors suited to her rank, at the same time explaining to the knights the history of her affection for Launcelot, which moved the compassion and regret of all.", "왕은 즉시 그 신분에 합당한 모든 예우를 갖추어 귀부인을 매장하라 명했고, 동시에 기사들에게 그녀가 랜슬롯을 사모해 온 내력을 설명해 주었으니, 이는 모든 이의 연민과 안타까움을 자아냈다."]], [["————", "————"]], [["Tennyson has chosen the story of the “Lady of Shalott”", "테니슨은 \"샬롯의 귀부인\" 이야기를 한 편의 시의 소재로 삼았다."], ["for the subject of a poem. The catastrophe is told thus:", "그 비극적 결말은 이렇게 노래된다."]], [["“Under tower and balcony, By garden-wall and gallery, A gleaming shape she floated by, A corse between the houses high, Silent into Camelot.", "\"탑과 발코니 아래로, 정원 담장과 회랑을 지나, 어슴푸레 빛나는 형상이 흘러갔네, 높다란 집들 사이로 한 구의 주검이, 말없이 카멜롯 안으로."], ["Out upon the wharfs they came, Knight and burgher, lord and dame, And round the prow they read her name, ‘The Lady of Shalott.’", "부두 위로 사람들이 몰려나왔네, 기사와 시민, 영주와 귀부인이, 뱃머리를 둘러싸고 그 이름을 읽었네, '샬롯의 귀부인'이라고."]], [["“Who is this?", "\"이가 누구인가?"], ["and what is here?", "여기 무슨 일인가?"], ["And in the lighted palace near Died the sound of royal cheer; And they crossed themselves for fear, All the knights at Camelot.", "가까운 불 밝힌 궁전 안에서는 왕가의 환호 소리가 잦아들었네. 그리고 그들은 두려워 성호를 그었네, 카멜롯의 모든 기사들이."], ["But Launcelot mused a little space; He said, ‘She has a lovely face; God in his mercy lend her grace, The Lady of Shalott.’”", "그러나 랜슬롯은 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가, 이렇게 말했네, '아름다운 얼굴이로구나. 하느님이 자비로이 그녀에게 은총을 베푸시기를, 샬롯의 귀부인에게.'\""]], [["————", "————"]], [["# QUEEN GUENEVER’S PERIL", "# QUEEN GUENEVER’S PERIL"]], [["IT happened at this time that Queen Guenever was thrown into great peril of her life.", "이 무렵에 귀네비어 왕비는 목숨이 위태로운 커다란 위기에 빠지게 되었다."], ["A certain squire who was in her immediate service, having some cause of animosity to Sir Gawain, determined to destroy him by poison, at a public entertainment.", "왕비를 가까이에서 모시던 어느 종자가 가웨인 경에게 모종의 원한을 품고 있었던 까닭에, 공개 연회에서 독으로 그를 죽이기로 작정했다."], ["For this purpose he concealed the poison in an apple of fine appearance, which he placed on the top of several others, and put the dish before the queen, hoping that, as Sir Gawain was the knight of greatest dignity, she would present the apple to him.", "이를 위해 그는 겉모양이 고운 사과 하나에 독을 감추어 다른 여러 사과 위에 얹어 두고는 그 접시를 왕비 앞에 올렸으니, 가웨인 경이 가장 지체 높은 기사인 만큼 왕비가 그에게 사과를 건네리라 기대했던 것이다."], ["But it happened that a Scottish knight of high distinction, who arrived on that day, was seated next to the queen, and to him as a stranger she presented the apple, which he had no sooner eaten than he was seized with dreadful pain, and fell senseless.", "그런데 마침 그날 도착한, 지체가 높은 어느 스코틀랜드 기사가 왕비의 곁에 앉아 있었던 까닭에, 왕비는 손님인 그에게 사과를 건넸고, 그는 그것을 먹자마자 끔찍한 고통에 사로잡혀 정신을 잃고 쓰러졌다."], ["The whole court was, of course, thrown into confusion; the knights rose from table, darting looks of indignation at the wretched queen, whose tears and protestations were unable to remove their suspicions.", "물론 궁정 전체가 큰 혼란에 빠졌다. 기사들은 식탁에서 일어나 가엾은 왕비에게 분노의 시선을 던졌으나, 왕비의 눈물과 항변으로도 그들의 의심을 거둘 수는 없었다."], ["In spite of all that could be done the knight died, and nothing remained but to order a magnificent funeral and monument for him, which was done.", "온갖 손을 써 보았으나 그 기사는 끝내 죽었고, 남은 일이라고는 그를 위해 성대한 장례와 기념비를 마련하는 것뿐이었으며, 그렇게 했다."]], [["Some time after Sir Mador, brother of the murdered knight, arrived at Arthur’s court in quest of him.", "얼마 뒤 살해된 기사의 형제인 마도르 경이 그를 찾아 아서 왕의 궁정에 도착했다."], ["While hunting in the forest he by chance came to the spot where the monument was erected, read the inscription, and returned to court determined on immediate and signal vengeance.", "숲에서 사냥을 하던 중 그는 우연히 기념비가 세워진 자리에 이르렀고, 그 비문을 읽고는 즉각적이고 본보기가 될 복수를 결심한 채 궁정으로 돌아왔다."], ["He rode into the hall, loudly accused the queen of treason, and insisted on her being given up for punishment, unless she should find by a certain day a knight hardy enough to risk his life in support of her innocence.", "그는 큰 홀로 말을 몰고 들어와 왕비를 반역죄로 큰소리로 고발했으며, 정해진 날까지 왕비의 결백을 지키기 위해 목숨을 걸 만큼 담대한 기사를 찾아내지 못한다면 왕비를 처벌받도록 내어 줄 것을 강력히 요구했다."], ["Arthur, powerful as he was, did not dare to deny the appeal, but was compelled with a heavy heart to accept it, and Mador sternly took his departure, leaving the royal couple plunged in terror and anxiety.", "아서 왕은 그토록 강력한 권세를 지녔음에도 그 요구를 거절할 엄두를 내지 못했고, 무거운 마음으로 이를 받아들일 수밖에 없었다. 마도르는 단호하게 자리를 떠났으며, 왕과 왕비는 공포와 불안 속에 빠진 채 남겨졌다."]], [["During all this time Launcelot was absent, and no one knew where he was.", "이 모든 일이 벌어지는 동안 란슬롯은 자리를 비우고 있었으며, 그가 어디에 있는지 아무도 알지 못했다."], ["He fled in anger from his fair mistress, upon being reproached by her with his passion for the Lady of Shalott, which she had hastily inferred from his wearing her scarf at the tournament.", "그는 자신이 마상 시합에서 샬롯의 여인의 스카프를 두르고 있던 것을 보고 왕비가 성급히 짐작하여, 그가 그 여인을 향해 연정을 품었다며 책망하자, 분노한 채 아리따운 연인 곁을 떠났던 것이다."], ["He took up his abode with a hermit in the forest, and resolved to think no more of the cruel beauty, whose conduct he thought must flow from a wish to get rid of him.", "그는 숲속의 어느 은자와 함께 거처를 정하고는, 자신을 떼어 내려는 바람에서 비롯되었으리라 여겨지는 행동을 한 그 무정한 미녀를 다시는 생각하지 않기로 마음먹었다."], ["Yet calm reflection had somewhat cooled his indignation, and he had begun to wish, though hardly able to hope, for a reconciliation when the news of Sir Mador’s challenge fortunately reached his ears.", "그러나 차분히 돌이켜 보는 사이에 그의 분노도 어느 정도 식었고, 거의 바랄 엄두를 내지는 못했으나 화해를 바라기 시작하던 차에, 마침 마도르 경의 도전 소식이 그의 귀에 들어왔다."], ["The intelligence revived his spirits, and he began to prepare with the utmost cheerfulness for a contest which, if successful, would insure him at once the affection of his mistress and the gratitude of his sovereign.", "그 소식은 그의 기운을 되살렸고, 그는 더없이 흔쾌한 마음으로 결투를 준비하기 시작했다. 그 결투에서 승리한다면 연인의 애정과 군주의 감사를 단번에 얻을 수 있을 터였다."]], [["The sad fate of the Lady of Shalott had ere this completely acquitted Launcelot in the queen’s mind of all suspicion of his fidelity, and she lamented most grievously her foolish quarrel with him, which now, at her time of need, deprived her of her most efficient champion.", "이 무렵 샬롯의 여인의 슬픈 운명은 란슬롯의 충정에 대한 왕비의 모든 의심을 완전히 풀어 준 터였고, 왕비는 그와의 어리석은 다툼을 더없이 비통하게 한탄했다. 그 다툼으로 인해 정작 도움이 절실한 때에 가장 든든한 옹호자를 잃고 말았기 때문이다."]], [["As the day appointed by Sir Mador was fast approaching, it became necessary that she should procure a champion for her defence; and she successively adjured Sir Hector, Sir Lionel, Sir Bohort, and Sir Gawain to undertake the battle.", "마도르 경이 정한 날이 빠르게 다가오는 만큼, 왕비는 자신을 변호해 줄 투사를 마련해야만 했다. 그리하여 왕비는 헥토르 경, 라이오넬 경, 보호트 경, 가웨인 경에게 차례로 그 싸움을 맡아 달라고 간청했다."], ["She fell on her knees before them, called heaven to witness her innocence of the crime alleged against her, but was sternly answered by all that they could not fight to maintain the innocence of one whose act, and the fatal consequence of it, they had seen with their own eyes.", "왕비는 그들 앞에 무릎을 꿇고는 자신에게 씌워진 죄에 결백함을 하늘이 증언해 달라 호소했으나, 자신들의 두 눈으로 그 행위와 그 치명적인 결과를 직접 보았으니 그 결백을 지키기 위해 싸울 수는 없다는 단호한 대답만을 모두에게서 들었다."], ["She retired, therefore, dejected and disconsolate; but the sight of the fatal pile on which, if guilty, she was doomed to be burned, exciting her to fresh effort, she again repaired to Sir Bohort, threw herself at his feet, and piteously calling on him for mercy, fell into a swoon.", "그리하여 왕비는 낙담하고 비탄에 잠긴 채 물러났다. 그러나 만일 죄가 있다면 자신이 불태워질 운명인 그 끔찍한 장작더미를 바라보자 새로운 힘이 솟구쳤고, 왕비는 다시 보호트 경에게로 가 그의 발치에 몸을 던지고는 가엾이 자비를 구하며 정신을 잃고 쓰러졌다."], ["The brave knight was not proof against this.", "용맹한 기사도 이에는 버티지 못했다."], ["He raised her up, and hastily promised that he would undertake her cause, if no other or better champion should present himself.", "그는 왕비를 일으켜 세우고는, 다른 더 나은 투사가 나서지 않는다면 자신이 그 일을 떠맡겠노라 서둘러 약속했다. 그런 다음 그는 벗들을 불러 모아 자신의 결심을 알렸다."], ["He then summoned his friends, and told them his resolution; and as a mortal combat with Sir Mador was a most fearful enterprise, they agreed to accompany him in the morning to the hermitage in the forest, where he proposed to receive absolution from the hermit, and to make his peace with Heaven before he entered the lists.", "마도르 경과의 목숨을 건 결투는 더없이 두려운 일이었던 까닭에, 그들은 이튿날 아침 그와 함께 숲속의 은자의 거처로 가기로 했다. 그곳에서 그는 결투장에 들어서기에 앞서 은자에게서 죄 사함을 받고 하늘과 화평을 이루고자 했던 것이다."], ["As they approached the hermitage, they espied a knight riding in the forest, whom they at once recognized as Sir Launcelot.", "은자의 거처에 가까워졌을 때, 그들은 숲에서 말을 타고 가는 어느 기사를 발견했고, 그가 다름 아닌 란슬롯 경임을 곧바로 알아보았다."], ["Overjoyed at the meeting, they quickly, in answer to his questions, confirmed the news of the queen’s imminent danger, and received his instructions to return to court, to comfort her as well as they could, but to say nothing of his intention of undertaking her defence, which he meant to do in the character of an unknown adventurer.", "만남에 크게 기뻐한 그들은 그의 물음에 답하여 왕비가 임박한 위험에 처해 있다는 소식을 서둘러 확인해 주었으며, 궁정으로 돌아가 힘닿는 대로 왕비를 위로하되 자신이 왕비를 변호하려는 뜻에 대해서는 아무 말도 하지 말라는 그의 당부를 받았다. 그는 정체를 알 수 없는 모험가의 모습으로 그 일을 해낼 작정이었다."]], [["On their return to the castle they found that mass was finished, and had scarcely time to speak to the queen before they were summoned into the hall to dinner.", "그들이 성으로 돌아오니 미사가 이미 끝나 있었고, 왕비에게 미처 말을 건넬 겨를도 없이 그들은 만찬을 위해 큰 홀로 불려 들어갔다."], ["A general gloom was spread over the countenances of all the guests.", "손님들 모두의 얼굴에 짙은 우울이 드리워 있었다."], ["Arthur himself was unable to conceal his dejection, and the wretched Guenever, motionless and bathed in tears, sat in trembling expectation of Sir Mador’s appearance.", "아서 왕조차 낙담을 감추지 못했고, 가엾은 귀네비어는 미동도 없이 눈물에 젖은 채 마도르 경이 나타나기를 떨면서 기다리고 있었다."], ["Nor was it long ere he stalked into the hall, and with a voice of thunder, rendered more impressive by the general silence, demanded instant justice on the guilty party.", "머지않아 그가 큰 홀로 성큼성큼 걸어 들어왔고, 좌중의 적막 속에 한층 더 위압적으로 울려 퍼지는 우레 같은 목소리로 죄인에 대한 즉각적인 심판을 요구했다."], ["Arthur replied with dignity, that little of the day was yet spent, and that perhaps a champion might yet be found capable of satisfying his thirst for battle.", "아서 왕은 위엄 있게 답하기를, 아직 하루가 얼마 지나지 않았으니 어쩌면 그의 싸움에 대한 갈망을 채워 줄 투사가 나타날지도 모른다고 했다."], ["Sir Bohort now rose from table, and shortly returning in complete armor, resumed his place, after receiving the embraces and thanks of the king, who now began to resume some degree of confidence.", "이때 보호트 경이 식탁에서 일어났다가 이내 완전한 갑주를 갖추고 돌아와, 왕의 포옹과 감사를 받은 뒤 자리에 다시 앉았다. 왕은 이제 어느 정도 자신감을 되찾기 시작했다."], ["Sir Mador, growing impatient, again repeated his denunciations of vengeance, and insisted that the combat should no longer be postponed.", "마도르 경은 점점 초조해지더니 복수의 다짐을 다시금 거듭하며, 결투를 더는 미루지 말 것을 강력히 요구했다."]], [["In the height of the debate there came riding into the hall a knight mounted on a black steed, and clad in black armor, with his visor down, and lance in hand.", "논쟁이 한창 무르익을 무렵, 검은 군마에 올라 검은 갑주를 두르고 면갑을 내린 채 손에 창을 든 기사 하나가 말을 몰아 큰 홀로 들어왔다. \"기사여,\""], ["“Sir,”", "왕이 말했다."], ["said the king, “is it your will to alight and partake of our cheer?”", "\"말에서 내려 우리와 함께 음식을 들 마음이 있는가?\" \"아니옵니다, 폐하.\""], ["“Nay, sir,”", "그가 답했다."], ["he replied; “I come to save a lady’s life.", "\"저는 한 여인의 목숨을 구하러 왔습니다."], ["The queen hath ill bestowed her favors, and honored many a knight, that in her hour of need she should have none to take her part.", "왕비께서는 그릇되이 은총을 베푸시어 수많은 기사를 영예롭게 하셨거늘, 정작 위난의 때에 그분의 편을 들 자가 하나도 없다니요."], ["Thou that darest accuse her of treachery, stand forth, for to-day shalt thou need all thy might.”", "왕비를 반역죄로 감히 고발한 그대여, 앞으로 나서라. 오늘 그대는 그대의 온 힘을 다 써야 할 것이니.\""]], [["Sir Mador, though surprised, was not appalled by the stern challenge and formidable appearance of his antagonist, but prepared for the encounter.", "마도르 경은 놀라기는 했으나, 상대의 단호한 도전과 위풍당당한 모습에도 겁먹지 않고 결투를 준비했다."], ["At the first shock both were unhorsed.", "첫 충돌에 두 사람 모두 말에서 떨어졌다."], ["They then drew their swords, and commenced a combat which lasted from noon till evening, when Sir Mador, whose strength began to fail, was felled to the ground by Launcelot, and compelled to sue for mercy.", "이어 그들은 칼을 뽑아 정오부터 저녁까지 이어진 결투를 시작했고, 마침내 힘이 다하기 시작한 마도르 경이 란슬롯에게 땅바닥에 쓰러져 자비를 구할 수밖에 없게 되었다."], ["The victor, whose arm was already raised to terminate the life of his opponent, instantly dropped his sword, courteously lifted up the fainting Sir Mador, frankly confessing that he had never before encountered so formidable an enemy.", "이미 적의 목숨을 끊고자 팔을 치켜들었던 승자는 곧바로 칼을 떨구고는 정중하게 정신을 잃은 마도르 경을 일으켜 세우며, 일찍이 이토록 가공할 적을 만나 본 적이 없노라 솔직히 고백했다."], ["The other, with similar courtesy, solemnly renounced all further projects of vengeance for his brother’s death; and the two knights, now become fast friends, embraced each other with the greatest cordiality.", "상대 또한 같은 예의로써, 형제의 죽음에 대한 모든 복수의 계획을 엄숙히 단념했다. 그리하여 이제 막역한 벗이 된 두 기사는 더없이 진심 어린 마음으로 서로를 끌어안았다."], ["In the meantime Arthur, having recognized Sir Launcelot, whose helmet was now unlaced, rushed down into the lists, followed by all his knights, to welcome and thank his deliverer.", "그러는 사이에 아서 왕은 이제 투구의 끈이 풀린 란슬롯 경을 알아보고는, 자신을 구해 준 이를 맞이하고 그에게 감사하고자 모든 기사를 거느린 채 결투장으로 달려 내려갔다."], ["Guenever swooned with joy, and the place of combat suddenly exhibited a scene of the most tumultuous delight.", "귀네비어는 기쁨에 겨워 정신을 잃었고, 결투의 장소는 별안간 더없이 떠들썩한 환희의 광경으로 바뀌었다."]], [["The general satisfaction was still further increased by the discovery of the real culprit.", "진짜 죄인이 밝혀지면서 모두의 만족은 한층 더 커졌다."], ["Having accidentally incurred some suspicion, he confessed his crime, and was publicly punished in the presence of Sir Mador.", "그자는 우연히 모종의 의심을 사게 되었던 까닭에 자신의 죄를 자백했고, 마도르 경이 보는 앞에서 공개적으로 처벌받았다."]], [["The court now returned to the castle, which, with the title of “La Joyeuse Garde” bestowed upon it in memory of the happy event, was conferred on Sir Launcelot by Arthur, as a memorial of his gratitude.", "이제 궁정은 성으로 돌아갔고, 아서 왕은 그 행복한 사건을 기리는 뜻에서 그 성에 \"라 주아이외즈 가르드\"라는 이름을 붙여 자신의 감사의 표시로 란슬롯 경에게 하사했다."]], [["————", "————"]], [["# TRISTRAM AND ISOUDE", "# TRISTRAM AND ISOUDE"]], [["MELIADUS was king of Leonois, or Lionesse, a country famous in the annals of romance, which adjoined the kingdom of Cornwall, but has now disappeared from the map, having been, it is said, overwhelmed by the ocean.", "멜리아두스는 레오노이스, 즉 리오네스의 왕이었다. 이 나라는 로맨스의 연대기에서 유명한 곳으로, 콘월 왕국과 인접해 있었으나 지금은 지도에서 사라졌으니, 전하는 바에 따르면 바다에 잠겨 버렸다고 한다."], ["Meliadus was married to Isabella, sister of Mark, king of Cornwall.", "멜리아두스는 콘월의 왕 마크의 누이인 이사벨라와 혼인했다."], ["A fairy fell in love with him, and drew him away by enchantment while he was engaged in hunting.", "한 요정이 그를 사랑하게 되어, 그가 사냥에 열중하고 있는 동안 마법을 부려 그를 멀리 데려가 버렸다."], ["His queen set out in quest of him, but was taken ill on her journey, and died, leaving an infant son, whom, from the melancholy circumstances of his birth, she called Tristram.", "그의 왕비는 그를 찾아 길을 나섰으나, 도중에 병이 들어 세상을 떠나며 갓난 아들 하나를 남겼다. 왕비는 그 아이가 태어난 슬픈 정황 때문에 아들을 트리스트람이라 불렀다."]], [["Gouvernail, the queen’s squire, who had accompanied her, took charge of the child, and restored him to his father, who had at length burst the enchantments of the fairy, and returned home.", "왕비를 수행했던 종자 구베르나일이 그 아이를 맡아 길렀고, 마침내 요정의 마법을 깨뜨리고 집으로 돌아온 아버지에게 아이를 데려다주었다."]], [["Meliadus after seven years married again, and the new queen, being jealous of the influence of Tristram with his father, laid plots for his life, which were discovered by Gouvernail, who in consequence fled with the boy to the court of the king of France, where Tristram was kindly received, and grew up improving in every gallant and knightly accomplishment, adding to his skill in arms the arts of music and of chess.", "멜리아두스는 칠 년 뒤에 재혼했는데, 새 왕비는 트리스트람이 아버지에게 끼치는 영향을 시기하여 그의 목숨을 노리는 음모를 꾸몄다. 이를 알아챈 구베르나일은 그 결과 소년을 데리고 프랑스 왕의 궁정으로 달아났다."], ["In particular, he devoted himself to the chase and to all woodland sports, so that he became distinguished above all other chevaliers of the court for his knowledge of all that relates to hunting.", "그곳에서 트리스트람은 따뜻한 환대를 받았고, 온갖 용맹하고 기사다운 소양을 갈고닦으며 자라났으니, 무예에 더해 음악과 체스의 기예까지 익혔다. 특히 그는 사냥과 온갖 숲속의 유희에 몰두하여, 사냥에 관한 모든 지식에서 궁정의 다른 어떤 기사보다도 빼어난 인물이 되었다."], ["No wonder that Belinda, the king’s daughter, fell in love with him; but as he did not return her passion, she, in a sudden impulse of anger, excited her father against him, and he was banished the kingdom.", "왕의 딸 벨린다가 그를 사랑하게 된 것도 무리는 아니었다. 그러나 그가 그녀의 연정에 응답하지 않자, 그녀는 갑작스러운 분노에 휩싸여 아버지를 충동질해 그에게 맞서게 했고, 그는 왕국에서 추방되고 말았다."], ["The princess soon repented of her act, and in despair destroyed herself, having first written a most tender letter to Tristram, sending him at the same time a beautiful and sagacious dog, of which she was very fond, desiring him to keep it as a memorial of her.", "공주는 곧 자신의 행동을 후회했고, 절망에 빠져 스스로 목숨을 끊었다. 그녀는 먼저 트리스트람에게 더없이 애틋한 편지를 써 보내며, 동시에 자신이 무척 아끼던 아름답고 영리한 개 한 마리를 보내어, 그것을 자신을 기리는 정표로 간직해 달라고 청했다."], ["Meliadus was now dead, and as his queen, Tristram’s stepmother, held the throne, Gouvernail was afraid to carry his pupil to his native country, and took him to Cornwall, to his uncle Mark, who gave him a kind reception.", "멜리아두스는 이제 세상을 떠난 뒤였고, 그의 왕비, 곧 트리스트람의 계모가 왕좌를 차지하고 있었으므로, 구베르나일은 자신의 제자를 그의 고국으로 데려가기가 두려워 그를 콘월의 숙부 마크에게로 데려갔고, 마크는 그를 친절히 맞아 주었다."]], [["King Mark resided at the castle of Tintadel, already mentioned in the history of Uther and Igerne.", "마크 왕은 틴타델 성에 거처하고 있었으니, 이 성은 우테르와 이게르네의 이야기에서 이미 언급된 바 있다."], ["In this court Tristram became distinguished in all the exercises incumbent on a knight; nor was it long before he had an opportunity of practically employing his valor and skill.", "이 궁정에서 트리스트람은 기사에게 요구되는 온갖 수련에서 두각을 나타냈고, 그의 용맹과 무예를 실제로 발휘할 기회는 오래지 않아 찾아왔다."], ["Moraunt, a celebrated champion, brother to the queen of Ireland, arrived at the court, to demand tribute of King Mark.", "아일랜드 왕비의 동생이자 이름난 용사인 모라운트가 마크 왕에게 조공을 요구하러 궁정에 당도한 것이다."], ["The knights of Cornwall are in ill repute in romance for their cowardice, and they exhibited it on this occasion.", "콘월의 기사들은 로맨스에서 비겁하기로 악명이 높았는데, 이번 일에서도 그 면모를 드러냈다."], ["King Mark could find no champion who dared to encounter the Irish knight, till his nephew Tristram, who had not yet received the honors of knighthood, craved to be admitted to the order, offering at the same time to fight the battle of Cornwall against the Irish champion.", "마크 왕은 이 아일랜드 기사와 맞서려는 용사를 한 명도 찾지 못했다. 그러던 차에 아직 기사 서임을 받지 못했던 조카 트리스트람이 기사단에 받아 주기를 간청하며, 동시에 아일랜드 용사에 맞서 콘월을 위해 싸우겠다고 자청했다."], ["King Mark assented with reluctance; Tristram received the accolade, which conferred knighthood upon him, and the place and time were assigned for the encounter.", "마크 왕은 마지못해 승낙했고, 트리스트람은 기사의 작위를 부여하는 서임을 받았으며, 결투의 장소와 시각이 정해졌다."]], [["Without attempting to give the details of this famous combat, the first and one of the most glorious of Tristram’s exploits, we shall only say that the young knight, though severely wounded, cleft the head of Moraunt, leaving a portion of his sword in the wound.", "트리스트람의 무훈 가운데 첫 번째이자 가장 영광스러운 위업의 하나인 이 유명한 결투의 세세한 내용을 모두 전하려 하지는 않겠으나, 다만 이렇게만 말해 두기로 한다. 젊은 기사는 비록 심한 상처를 입었지만 모라운트의 머리를 갈라, 칼의 일부를 그 상처 속에 남겨 놓았다."], ["Moraunt, half dead with his wound and the disgrace of his defeat, hastened to hide himself in his ship, sailed away with all speed for Ireland, and died soon after arriving in his own country.", "상처와 패배의 치욕으로 반쯤 죽은 모라운트는 서둘러 자신의 배에 몸을 숨기고 전속력으로 아일랜드를 향해 달아났으나, 고국에 도착한 직후에 숨을 거두었다."]], [["The kingdom of Cornwall was thus delivered from its tribute.", "이렇게 하여 콘월 왕국은 조공의 부담에서 벗어났다."], ["Tristram, weakened by loss of blood, fell senseless.", "트리스트람은 피를 많이 흘려 기력을 잃고 정신을 잃은 채 쓰러졌다."], ["His friends flew to his assistance.", "벗들이 그를 구하러 달려왔다."], ["They dressed his wounds, which in general healed readily; but the lance of Moraunt was poisoned, and one wound which it made yielded to no remedies, but grew worse day by day.", "그들은 그의 상처를 치료했고, 상처는 대체로 쉽게 아물었다. 그러나 모라운트의 창에는 독이 묻어 있어, 그 창이 낸 한 상처는 어떤 약에도 듣지 않고 날이 갈수록 더 악화되어 갔다."], ["The surgeons could do no more.", "의원들은 더 이상 손을 쓸 수가 없었다."], ["Tristram asked permission of his uncle to depart, and seek for aid in the kingdom of Loegria (England).", "트리스트람은 숙부에게 떠나도록 허락해 달라고 청하며, 로에그리아[잉글랜드] 왕국에서 도움을 구하고자 했다."], ["With his consent he embarked, and after tossing for many days on the sea, was driven by the winds to the coast of Ireland.", "숙부의 동의를 얻어 그는 배에 올랐고, 여러 날을 바다 위에서 표류한 끝에 바람에 떠밀려 아일랜드 해안에 닿았다."], ["He landed, full of joy and gratitude that he had escaped the peril of the sea; took his rote,[49] and began to play.", "그는 바다의 위험에서 벗어났다는 기쁨과 감사로 가득 차 뭍에 내려, 자신의 로테를 들고 연주하기 시작했다."], ["It was a summer evening, and the king of Ireland and his daughter, the beautiful Isoude, were at a window which overlooked the sea.", "때는 여름 저녁이었고, 아일랜드 왕과 그의 딸인 아름다운 이주드는 바다가 내려다보이는 창가에 있었다."], ["The strange harper was sent for, and conveyed to the palace, where, finding that he was in Ireland, whose champion he had lately slain, he concealed his name, and called himself Tramtris.", "그 낯선 하프 연주자는 부름을 받아 궁전으로 안내되었는데, 자신이 얼마 전에 그 용사를 죽인 바로 그 아일랜드에 있음을 알게 되자, 그는 이름을 숨기고 자신을 트람트리스라 칭했다."], ["The queen undertook his cure, and by a medicated bath gradually restored him to health.", "왕비가 그의 치료를 맡아, 약을 넣은 목욕으로 차츰차츰 그를 건강하게 회복시켰다."], ["His skill in music and in games occasioned his being frequently called to court, and he became the instructor of the princess Isoude in minstrelsy and poetry, who profited so well under his care, that she soon had no equal in the kingdom, except her instructor.", "음악과 놀이에 능한 그의 솜씨 덕분에 그는 자주 궁정에 불려 갔고, 공주 이주드에게 음유시와 시작(詩作)을 가르치는 스승이 되었다. 공주는 그의 가르침 아래 크게 정진하여, 곧 스승을 제외하고는 왕국에서 견줄 자가 없게 되었다."]], [["At this time a tournament was held, at which many knights of the Round Table, and others, were present.", "* 로테: 중세의 현악기로, 하프와 비슷한 일종의 류트. 이 무렵 한 차례 마상 시합이 열렸는데, 원탁의 기사를 비롯해 많은 기사들이 참석했다."], ["On the first day a Saracen prince, named Palamedes, obtained the advantage over all.", "첫째 날, 팔라메데스라는 이름의 사라센 왕자가 모든 이를 압도했다."], ["They brought him to the court, and gave him a feast, at which Tristram, just recovering from his wound, was present.", "사람들은 그를 궁정으로 데려가 잔치를 베풀었는데, 상처에서 막 회복된 트리스트람도 그 자리에 참석했다."], ["The fair Isoude appeared on this occasion in all her charms.", "이 자리에서 아름다운 이주드는 온갖 매력을 한껏 드러냈다."], ["Palamedes could not behold them without emotion, and made no effort to conceal his love.", "팔라메데스는 그 모습을 보며 마음이 흔들리지 않을 수 없었고, 자신의 사랑을 감추려 들지도 않았다."], ["Tristram perceived it, and the pain he felt from jealousy taught him how dear the fair Isoude had already become to him.", "트리스트람은 이를 알아챘고, 질투에서 느끼는 고통은 아름다운 이주드가 이미 자신에게 얼마나 소중한 존재가 되었는지를 그에게 일깨워 주었다."]], [["Next day the tournament was renewed.", "이튿날 시합이 다시 열렸다."], ["Tristram, still feeble from his wound, rose during the night, took his arms, and concealed them in a forest near the place of the contest, and, after it had begun, mingled with the combatants.", "트리스트람은 여전히 상처로 쇠약했으나, 밤중에 일어나 무기를 챙겨 경기장 근처의 숲에 숨겨 두었고, 시합이 시작된 뒤 싸우는 이들 틈에 섞여 들었다."], ["He overthrew all that encountered him, in particular Palamedes, whom he brought to the ground with a stroke of his lance, and then fought him hand to hand, bearing off the prize of the tourney.", "그는 자신과 맞선 모든 이를 쓰러뜨렸으니, 특히 팔라메데스를 창 한 방으로 땅에 쓰러뜨린 뒤 그와 맨손으로 겨루어 시합의 승리를 거머쥐었다. 그러나 그렇게 무리한 탓에 상처가 다시 벌어졌다."], ["But his exertions caused his wound to reopen; he bled fast, and in this sad state, yet in triumph, they bore him to the palace.", "피가 쉴 새 없이 흘렀고, 이 가련한 처지에서, 그러나 승리를 거둔 채로, 사람들은 그를 궁전으로 옮겼다."], ["The fair Isoude devoted herself to his relief with an interest which grew more vivid day by day; and her skilful care soon restored him to health.", "아름다운 이주드는 날이 갈수록 더욱 깊어지는 관심을 기울이며 그의 간호에 헌신했고, 그녀의 능숙한 보살핌은 곧 그를 건강하게 회복시켰다."]], [["It happened one day that a damsel of the court, entering the closet where Tristram’s arms were deposited, perceived that a part of the sword had been broken off.", "어느 날, 궁정의 한 시녀가 트리스트람의 무기가 보관된 작은 방에 들어갔다가, 칼의 일부가 부러져 나간 것을 알아챘다."], ["It occurred to her that the missing portion was like that which was left in the skull of Moraunt, the Irish champion.", "그녀는 그 사라진 조각이 아일랜드 용사 모라운트의 두개골에 박혀 있던 조각과 닮았다는 생각이 들었다."], ["She imparted her thought to the queen, who compared the fragment taken from her brother’s wound with the sword of Tristram, and was satisfied that it was part of the same, and that the weapon of Tristram was that which reft her brother’s life.", "그녀가 이 생각을 왕비에게 전하자, 왕비는 동생의 상처에서 빼낸 조각을 트리스트람의 칼과 맞추어 보고는, 그것이 같은 칼의 일부이며 트리스트람의 무기가 바로 자기 동생의 목숨을 앗아 간 그 무기임을 확신했다."], ["She laid her griefs and resentment before the king, who satisfied himself with his own eyes of the truth of her suspicions.", "왕비는 자신의 슬픔과 원한을 왕 앞에 털어놓았고, 왕은 자신의 두 눈으로 직접 그녀의 의심이 사실임을 확인했다."], ["Tristram was cited before the whole court, and reproached with having dared to present himself before them after having slain their kinsman.", "트리스트람은 온 궁정 앞에 불려 나와, 자신들의 혈족을 죽이고도 감히 그들 앞에 나타났다는 비난을 받았다."], ["He acknowledged that he had fought with Moraunt to settle the claim for tribute, and said that it was by force of winds and waves alone that he was thrown on their coast.", "그는 조공 요구를 매듭짓기 위해 모라운트와 싸운 것을 인정했고, 오직 바람과 물결의 힘만으로 그들의 해안에 떠밀려 온 것이라고 말했다."], ["The queen demanded vengeance for the death of her brother; the fair Isoude trembled and grew pale, but a murmur rose from all the assembly that the life of one so handsome and so brave should not be taken for such a cause, and generosity finally triumphed over resentment in the mind of the king.", "왕비는 동생의 죽음에 대한 복수를 요구했다. 아름다운 이주드는 몸을 떨며 창백해졌으나, 이토록 잘생기고 용감한 자의 목숨을 그런 이유로 빼앗아서는 안 된다는 웅성거림이 온 좌중에서 일었고, 마침내 왕의 마음속에서 너그러움이 원한을 이겨 냈다."], ["Tristram was dismissed in safety, but commanded to leave the kingdom without delay, and never to return thither under pain of death.", "트리스트람은 무사히 풀려났으나, 지체 없이 왕국을 떠나며 죽음을 각오하지 않는 한 다시는 그곳으로 돌아오지 말라는 명령을 받았다."], ["Tristram went back, with restored health, to Cornwall.", "트리스트람은 건강을 되찾아 콘월로 돌아갔다."]], [["King Mark made his nephew give him a minute recital of his adventures.", "마크 왕은 조카에게 자신의 모험담을 낱낱이 들려 달라고 했다."], ["Tristram told him all minutely; but when he came to speak of the fair Isoude he described her charms with a warmth and energy such as none but a lover could display.", "트리스트람은 그에게 모든 것을 소상히 이야기했는데, 아름다운 이주드에 대해 말할 차례가 되자 오로지 연인만이 드러낼 수 있는 열기와 활기로 그녀의 매력을 묘사했다."], ["King Mark was fascinated with the description, and, choosing a favorable time, demanded a boon[50] of his nephew, who readily granted it.", "마크 왕은 그 묘사에 매혹되었고, 적당한 때를 골라 조카에게 한 가지 청을 들어 달라고 요구했으며, 조카는 선뜻 이를 허락했다. 왕은 그에게 거룩한 성유물에 손을 얹고 자신의 명을 따르겠노라 맹세하게 했다."], ["The king made him swear upon the holy reliques that he would fulfil his commands.", "그런 다음 마크는 그에게 아일랜드로 가서, 자신을 위해 아름다운 이주드를 콘월의 왕비로 맞아 오라고 명했다."], ["Then Mark directed him to go to Ireland, and obtain for him the fair Isoude to be queen of Cornwall.", "* 청: 중세 기사도에서 상대가 무조건 들어주기로 약속한 부탁을 가리킨다."]], [["Tristram believed it was certain death for him to return to Ireland; and how could he act as ambassador for his uncle in such a cause?", "트리스트람은 아일랜드로 돌아가는 것이 자신에게 틀림없는 죽음이라 믿었다. 더구나 어떻게 그런 일로 숙부의 사절 노릇을 할 수 있단 말인가?"], ["Yet, bound by his oath, he hesitated not for an instant.", "그러나 맹세에 매인 몸이었으므로 그는 한순간도 망설이지 않았다."], ["He only took the precaution to change his armor.", "다만 갑옷을 바꾸어 입는 조심성만은 발휘했다."], ["He embarked for Ireland; but a tempest drove him to the coast of England, near Camelot, where King Arthur was holding his court, attended by the knights of the Round Table, and many others, the most illustrious in the world.", "그는 아일랜드를 향해 배에 올랐으나, 폭풍이 그를 카멜롯 가까운 잉글랜드 해안으로 떠밀어 보냈다. 그곳에서는 아서 왕이 원탁의 기사들과, 그 밖에 세상에서 가장 빼어난 수많은 이들을 거느리고 궁정을 열고 있었다."]], [["Tristram kept himself unknown.", "트리스트람은 정체를 감추었다."], ["He took part in many justs; he fought many combats, in which he covered himself with glory.", "그는 여러 차례 마상 창시합에 나섰고, 수많은 결투에서 싸워 영광으로 자신을 빛냈다."], ["One day he saw among those recently arrived the king of Ireland, father of the fair Isoude.", "어느 날 그는 최근에 도착한 이들 가운데 아름다운 이주드의 아버지인 아일랜드 왕이 있는 것을 보았다."], ["This prince, accused of treason against his liege sovereign, Arthur, came to Camelot to free himself from the charge.", "이 왕은 자신의 군주인 아서에게 반역을 꾀했다는 혐의를 받고, 그 혐의를 벗고자 카멜롯으로 온 것이었다."], ["Blaanor, one of the most redoubtable warriors of the Round Table, was his accuser, and Argius, the king, had neither youthful vigor nor strength to encounter him.", "원탁에서 가장 무시무시한 전사 중 하나인 블라아노르가 그를 고발한 자였는데, 아르기우스 왕은 그와 맞설 젊은 혈기도 힘도 없었다."], ["He must therefore seek a champion to sustain his innocence.", "따라서 그는 자신의 결백을 입증해 줄 용사를 구해야만 했다."], ["But the knights of the Round Table were not at liberty to fight against one another, unless in a quarrel of their own.", "그러나 원탁의 기사들은 자신들끼리의 다툼이 아니고서는 서로 싸울 수 없었다."], ["Argius heard of the great renown of the unknown knight; he also was witness of his exploits.", "아르기우스는 그 정체 모를 기사의 드높은 명성을 전해 들었고, 또한 그의 무훈을 직접 목격하기도 했다."], ["He sought him, and conjured him to adopt his defence, and on his oath declared that he was innocent of the crime of which he was accused.", "그는 그를 찾아가, 자신의 변호를 맡아 달라고 간청하며, 자신은 고발당한 죄에 결백하다고 맹세코 선언했다."], ["Tristram readily consented, and made himself known to the king, who on his part promised to reward his exertions, if successful, with whatever gift he might ask.", "트리스트람은 선뜻 응낙하고 왕에게 자신의 정체를 밝혔으며, 왕은 그 대가로, 만일 성공한다면 그가 청하는 무엇이든 선물로 주겠다고 약속했다."]], [["Tristram fought with Blaanor, and overthrew him, and held his life in his power.", "트리스트람은 블라아노르와 싸워 그를 쓰러뜨렸고, 그의 목숨을 자신의 손아귀에 쥐었다."], ["The fallen warrior called on him to use his right of conquest, and strike the fatal blow.", "쓰러진 전사는 그에게 정복의 권리를 행사하여 치명적인 일격을 가하라고 청했다."], ["“God forbid,” said Tristram, “that I should take the life of so brave a knight!”", "\"이토록 용감한 기사의 목숨을 내가 빼앗다니, 하느님께서 결코 그런 일을 허락지 않으시리라!\""], ["He raised him up and restored him to his friends.", "트리스트람이 말했다. 그는 그를 일으켜 세워 그의 벗들에게 돌려보냈다."], ["The judges of the field decided that the king of Ireland was acquitted of the charge against him, and they led Tristram in triumph to his tent.", "결투의 판관들은 아일랜드 왕이 그에게 씌워진 혐의를 벗었다고 판정했고, 트리스트람을 의기양양하게 그의 천막으로 이끌어 갔다."], ["King Argius, full of gratitude, conjured Tristram to accompany him to his kingdom.", "감사로 가득 찬 아르기우스 왕은 트리스트람에게 자신의 왕국으로 함께 가 달라고 간청했다."], ["They departed together, and arrived in Ireland; and the queen, forgetting her resentment for her brother’s death, exhibited to the preserver of her husband’s life nothing but gratitude and good-will.", "그들은 함께 떠나 아일랜드에 도착했고, 왕비는 동생의 죽음에 대한 원한을 잊은 채, 남편의 목숨을 구해 준 이에게 오로지 감사와 호의만을 보였다."]], [["How happy a moment for Isoude, who knew that her father had promised his deliverer whatever boon he might ask!", "아버지가 자신의 구원자에게 청하는 무엇이든 주겠노라 약속했음을 알고 있던 이주드에게 그 순간은 얼마나 행복했던가!"], ["But the unhappy Tristram gazed on her with despair, at the thought of the cruel oath which bound him.", "그러나 불행한 트리스트람은 자신을 옭아맨 잔혹한 맹세를 떠올리며 절망에 잠겨 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["His magnanimous soul subdued the force of his love.", "그의 도량 넓은 영혼이 사랑의 힘을 억눌렀다."], ["He revealed the oath which he had taken, and with trembling voice demanded the fair Isoude for his uncle.", "그는 자신이 한 맹세를 밝히고, 떨리는 목소리로 숙부를 위해 아름다운 이주드를 청했다."]], [["Argius consented, and soon all was prepared for the departure of Isoude.", "아르기우스는 이를 승낙했고, 이주드의 출발을 위한 모든 채비가 곧 갖추어졌다."], ["Brengwain, her favorite maid of honor, was to accompany her.", "그녀가 가장 아끼는 시녀 브렝그웨인이 그녀를 수행하기로 했다."], ["On the day of departure the queen took aside this devoted attendant, and told her that she had observed that her daughter and Tristram were attached to one another, and that to avert the bad effects of this inclination she had procured from a powerful fairy a potent philter (love-draught), which she directed Brengwain to administer to Isoude and to King Mark on the evening of their marriage.", "출발하는 날, 왕비는 이 충직한 시녀를 따로 불러, 자신이 딸과 트리스트람이 서로 정이 들었음을 눈치챘다고 말하며, 이 연정의 나쁜 결과를 막기 위해 강력한 요정에게서 효험 있는 미약(媚藥)을 구해 왔노라 일렀다. 그러고는 브렝그웨인에게 혼례 날 저녁에 이주드와 마크 왕에게 그것을 마시게 하라고 지시했다."]], [["Isoude and Tristram embarked together.", "이주드와 트리스트람은 함께 배에 올랐다."], ["A favorable wind filled the sails, and promised them a fortunate voyage.", "순풍이 돛을 채워 그들에게 순조로운 항해를 약속했다."], ["The lovers gazed upon one another, and could not repress their sighs.", "두 연인은 서로를 바라보며 한숨을 억누르지 못했다."], ["Love seemed to light up all his fires on their lips, as in their hearts.", "사랑은 그들의 가슴속에서처럼 그들의 입술 위에서도 온갖 불길을 밝히는 듯했다."], ["The day was warm; they suffered from thirst.", "날이 더웠고, 그들은 갈증으로 괴로워했다."], ["Isoude first complained.", "이주드가 먼저 갈증을 호소했다."], ["Tristram descried the bottle containing the love-draught, which Brengwain had been so imprudent as to leave in sight.", "트리스트람은 브렝그웨인이 경솔하게도 눈에 띄는 곳에 놓아둔, 미약이 든 병을 발견했다."], ["He took it, gave some of it to the charming Isoude, and drank the remainder himself.", "그는 그것을 집어 들어 매혹적인 이주드에게 얼마간 건네고, 나머지는 자신이 마셨다."], ["The dog Houdain licked the cup.", "개 우댕이 잔을 핥았다."], ["The ship arrived in Cornwall, and Isoude was married to King Mark.", "배는 콘월에 도착했고, 이주드는 마크 왕과 혼인했다."], ["The old monarch was delighted with his bride, and his gratitude to Tristram was unbounded.", "늙은 군주는 자신의 신부에 흠뻑 빠졌고, 트리스트람에 대한 그의 감사는 끝이 없었다."], ["He loaded him with honors, and made him chamberlain of his palace, thus giving him access to the queen at all times.", "그는 그에게 온갖 영예를 안겨 주고 그를 자신의 궁전 시종장으로 삼았으니, 이로써 그는 언제든 왕비에게 드나들 수 있게 되었다."]], [["In the midst of the festivities of the court which followed the royal marriage, an unknown minstrel one day presented himself, bearing a harp of peculiar construction.", "왕의 혼례에 뒤이은 궁정의 축연이 한창이던 어느 날, 정체 모를 한 음유시인이 모습을 드러냈는데, 그는 독특하게 만들어진 하프를 지니고 있었다."], ["He excited the curiosity of King Mark by refusing to play upon it till he should grant him a boon.", "그는 마크 왕이 한 가지 청을 들어주기 전에는 그것을 연주하지 않겠다고 버티어 왕의 호기심을 자극했다."], ["The king having promised to grant his request, the minstrel, who was none other than the Saracen knight, Sir Palamedes, the lover of the fair Isoude, sung to the harp a lay, in which he demanded Isoude as the promised gift.", "왕이 그의 청을 들어주겠노라 약속하자, 다름 아닌 사라센 기사이자 아름다운 이주드를 사모하는 팔라메데스 경이었던 그 음유시인은, 하프에 맞추어 노래를 부르며 약속된 선물로 이주드를 요구했다. 마크 왕은 기사도의 법도에 따라 그 청을 거절할 수 없었다."], ["King Mark could not by the laws of knighthood withhold the boon.", "부인은 말에 태워졌고, 의기양양한 연인에게 끌려갔다."], ["The lady was mounted on her horse, and led away by her triumphant lover.", "트리스트람이 그때 자리에 없었으며, 그들이 떠난 뒤에야 돌아왔다는 것은 굳이 말할 필요도 없으리라."], ["Tristram, it is needless to say, was absent at the time, and did not return until their departure.", "무슨 일이 벌어졌는지를 듣자 그는 자신의 로테를 움켜쥐고 해변으로 달려갔는데, 그곳에는 이미 이주드와 그녀의 새 주인이 배에 올라 있었다."], ["When he heard what had taken place he seized his rote, and hastened to the shore, where Isoude and her new master had already embarked.", "트리스트람이 로테를 연주하자, 그 소리가 이주드의 귀에 닿아 그녀는 깊이 감동했고, 이에 팔라메데스 경은 그 정체 모를 악사를 보고자 그녀와 함께 다시 뭍으로 돌아왔다."], ["Tristram played upon his rote, and the sound reached the ears of Isoude, who became so deeply affected, that Sir Palamedes was induced to return with her to land, that they might see the unknown musician.", "트리스트람은 기회를 엿보다가 부인의 말고삐를 붙잡고 그녀와 함께 숲속으로 뛰어들면서, 자신의 연적에게 \"하프로 얻은 것을 로테로 잃었구려\" 하고 비웃듯 일러 주었다."], ["Tristram watched his opportunity, seized the lady’s horse by the bridle, and plunged with her into the forest, tauntingly informing his rival that “what he had got by the harp he had lost by the rote.”", "팔라메데스가 뒤쫓아 와 막 결투가 시작되려 했으니, 그 결과는 이 용맹한 두 기사 가운데 하나에게 치명적일 수밖에 없었다. 그러나 이주드가 둘 사이로 끼어들어 팔라메데스에게 말했다."], ["Palamedes pursued, and a combat was about to commence, the result of which must have been fatal to one or other of these gallant knights; but Isoude stepped between them, and, addressing Palamedes, said, “You tell me that you love me; you will not then deny me the request I am about to make?”", "\"그대는 나를 사랑한다고 하니, 내가 이제 하려는 청을 거절하지는 않겠지요?\" \"부인이여,\""], ["“Lady,”", "그가 대답했다."], ["he replied, “I will perform your bidding.”", "\"그대의 분부대로 하겠소이다.\" \"그렇다면,\""], ["“Leave, then,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “this contest, and repair to King Arthur’s court, and salute Queen Guenever from me; tell her that there are in the world but two ladies, herself and I, and two lovers, hers and mine; and come thou not in future in any place where I am.”", "\"이 싸움을 그치고 아서 왕의 궁정으로 가서, 나를 대신해 귀네비어 왕비께 인사를 전하세요. 세상에는 두 여인, 곧 그분과 나뿐이며, 두 연인, 곧 그분의 연인과 나의 연인뿐이라고 전해 주세요."], ["Palamedes burst into tears.", "그리고 앞으로는 내가 있는 어떤 곳에도 그대는 오지 마세요.\" 팔라메데스는 눈물을 터뜨렸다."], ["“Ah, lady,”", "\"아, 부인이여,\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “I will obey you; but I beseech you that you will not for ever steel your heart against me.”", "\"그대의 뜻에 따르겠소. 다만 부디 영원히 그대의 마음을 내게 굳게 닫아 버리지는 마시기를 간청하오.\""], ["“Palamedes,”", "\"팔라메데스,\""], ["she replied, “may I never taste of joy again if I ever quit my first love.”", "그녀가 대답했다. \"내가 만일 첫사랑을 저버린다면, 다시는 기쁨을 맛보지 못하게 되기를.\""], ["Palamedes then went his way.", "그리하여 팔라메데스는 자신의 길을 떠나갔다."], ["The lovers remained a week in concealment, after which Tristram restored Isoude to her husband, advising him in future to reward minstrels in some other way.", "두 연인은 한 주일을 숨어 지냈고, 그 뒤 트리스트람은 이주드를 그녀의 남편에게 돌려보내며, 앞으로는 음유시인에게 다른 방식으로 보상하라고 조언했다."]], [["The king showed much gratitude to Tristram, but in the bottom of his heart he cherished bitter jealousy of him.", "왕은 트리스트람에게 큰 감사를 표했으나, 마음 깊은 곳에서는 그에 대한 쓰라린 질투를 품고 있었다."], ["One day Tristram and Isoude were alone together in her private chamber.", "어느 날 트리스트람과 이주드가 그녀의 사실(私室)에 단둘이 있었다."], ["A base and cowardly knight of the court, named Andret, spied them through a keyhole.", "안드레라는 이름의 비열하고 비겁한 궁정 기사가 열쇠 구멍으로 그들을 엿보았다."], ["They sat at a table of chess, but were not attending to the game.", "그들은 체스판을 앞에 두고 앉아 있었으나, 정작 놀이에는 마음을 두고 있지 않았다."], ["Andret brought the king, having first raised his suspicions, and placed him so as to watch their motions.", "안드레는 먼저 왕의 의심을 불러일으킨 다음 왕을 데려와, 그들의 거동을 지켜볼 수 있는 자리에 세웠다."], ["The king saw enough to confirm his suspicions, and he burst into the apartment with his sword drawn, and had nearly slain Tristram before he was put on his guard.", "왕은 자신의 의심을 굳히기에 충분한 것을 보았고, 칼을 빼어 든 채 방 안으로 뛰어들어, 트리스트람이 미처 경계하기도 전에 하마터면 그를 베어 버릴 뻔했다."], ["But Tristram avoided the blow, drew his sword, and drove before him the cowardly monarch, chasing him through all the apartments of the palace, giving him frequent blows with the flat of his sword, while he cried in vain to his knights to save him.", "그러나 트리스트람은 칼날을 피해 자신의 칼을 빼어, 비겁한 군주를 앞으로 몰아세우며 궁전의 온 방들을 가로질러 그를 쫓았다. 그러는 내내 칼의 옆면으로 그를 거듭 후려쳤고, 왕은 헛되이 자신의 기사들에게 구해 달라 외쳤다."], ["They were not inclined, or did not dare, to interpose in his behalf. ————", "그들은 그를 위해 나설 마음이 없었거나, 감히 끼어들지 못했다."]], [["A proof of the great popularity of the tale of Sir Tristram is the fact that the Italian poets, Boiardo and Ariosto, have founded upon it the idea of the two enchanted fountains, which produced the opposite effects of love and hatred.", "트리스트람 경의 이야기가 누린 크나큰 인기를 입증하는 사실 하나는, 이탈리아 시인 보이아르도와 아리오스토가 이 이야기에 근거하여, 사랑과 미움이라는 상반된 효과를 일으키는 두 마법의 샘이라는 착상을 빚어냈다는 점이다."], ["Boiardo thus describes the fountain of hatred:", "보이아르도는 미움의 샘을 이렇게 묘사한다."]], [["“Fair was that, fountain, sculptured all of gold, With alabaster sculptured, rich and rare; And in its basin clear thou might’st behold The flowery marge reflected fresh and fair.", "\"아름다웠어라, 그 샘은, 온통 황금으로 새겨지고, 설화석고로 새겨져, 풍요롭고 진귀하였네. 그 맑은 샘물 안에서 그대는 보았으리, 꽃 핀 가장자리가 싱그럽고 곱게 비친 모습을."], ["Sage Merlin framed the font,—so legends bear,— When on fair Isoude doated Tristram brave, That the good errant knight, arriving there, Might quaff oblivion in the enchanted wave, And leave his luckless love, and ’scape his timeless grave.", "현자 멀린이 그 샘을 빚었다고 전설은 전하나니, 용맹한 트리스트람이 아름다운 이주드를 사모하던 그때, 훌륭한 편력 기사가 그곳에 당도하면 마법의 물결 속에서 망각을 들이켜, 가망 없는 사랑을 버리고 때 이른 무덤을 면하게 하려 함이었네."]], [["“But ne’er the warrior’s evil fate allowed His steps that fountain’s charmed verge to gain.", "\"그러나 그 전사의 사악한 운명은 결코 허락지 않았으니, 그의 발길이 그 샘의 마법 어린 가장자리에 닿는 것을."], ["Though restless, roving on adventure proud, He traversed oft the land and oft the main.”", "쉴 새 없이, 자랑스러운 모험을 좇아 떠돌며, 그는 뭍을 누비고 또 바다를 가로질렀건만.\""], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [". .", "."], [". .", "."], ["————", ". ."]], [["TRISTRAM AND ISOUDE (Continued)\nAFTER this affair Tristram was banished from the kingdom, and Isoude shut up in a tower, which stood on the bank of a river.", "이 사건이 있은 후 트리스트람은 왕국에서 추방당했고, 이소우데는 강가에 서 있는 탑 안에 갇히고 말았다."], ["Tristram could not resolve to depart without some further communication with his beloved; so he concealed himself in the forest, till at last he contrived to attract her attention, by means of twigs which he curiously peeled, and sent down the stream under her window.", "트리스트람은 사랑하는 여인과 좀 더 이야기를 나누지 못한 채로는 차마 떠날 수 없었다. 그래서 그는 숲속에 몸을 숨기고 있다가, 마침내 잔가지의 껍질을 정교하게 벗겨 그녀의 창문 아래로 강물에 흘려보내는 방법으로 그녀의 주의를 끄는 데 성공했다."], ["By this means many secret interviews were obtained.", "이런 방법으로 두 사람은 여러 차례 은밀한 만남을 가질 수 있었다."], ["Tristram dwelt in the forest, sustaining himself by game, which the dog Houdain ran down for him; for this faithful animal was unequalled in the chase, and knew so well his master’s wish for concealment, that, in the pursuit of his game, he never barked.", "트리스트람은 숲속에 머물면서 개 후댕이 잡아 오는 사냥감으로 연명했다. 이 충직한 짐승은 사냥 솜씨가 누구에게도 뒤지지 않았고, 또한 주인이 몸을 숨기고자 한다는 것을 너무도 잘 알고 있어서, 사냥감을 쫓을 때에도 결코 짖는 법이 없었다."], ["At length Tristram departed, but left Houdain with Isoude, as a remembrancer of him.", "마침내 트리스트람은 떠났으나, 자신을 기억해 줄 정표로 후댕을 이소우데에게 남겨 두었다."]], [["Sir Tristram wandered through various countries, achieving the most perilous enterprises, and covering himself with glory, yet unhappy at the separation from his beloved Isoude.", "트리스트람 경은 여러 나라를 떠돌며 가장 위험천만한 일들을 해내고 영광으로 자신을 뒤덮었으나, 사랑하는 이소우데와 헤어진 처지가 못내 불행하기만 했다."], ["At length King Mark’s territory was invaded by a neighboring chieftain, and he was forced to summon his nephew to his aid.", "그러던 중에 마크 왕의 영토가 이웃 족장의 침략을 받았고, 왕은 어쩔 수 없이 조카에게 도움을 청해야 했다."], ["Tristram obeyed the call, put himself at the head of his uncle’s vassals, and drove the enemy out of the country.", "트리스트람은 부름에 응해, 숙부의 가신들을 이끌고 적을 나라 밖으로 몰아냈다."], ["Mark was full of gratitude, and Tristram, restored to favor and to the society of his beloved Isoude, seemed at the summit of happiness.", "마크는 감사한 마음으로 가득했고, 총애를 되찾고 사랑하는 이소우데와의 교제도 회복한 트리스트람은 행복의 정점에 이른 듯했다."], ["But a sad reverse was at hand.", "그러나 슬픈 반전이 가까이 다가와 있었다."]], [["Tristram had brought with him a friend named Pheredin, son of the king of Brittany.", "트리스트람은 페레딘이라는 친구를 데리고 왔는데, 브르타뉴 왕의 아들이었다."], ["This young knight saw Queen Isoude, and could not resist her charms.", "이 젊은 기사는 이소우데 왕비를 보고는 그 매력에 저항할 수 없었다."], ["Knowing the love of his friend for the queen, and that that love was returned, Pheredin concealed his own, until his health failed, and he feared he was drawing near his end.", "친구가 왕비를 사랑하고 있으며 그 사랑이 보답을 받고 있다는 것을 알았기에, 페레딘은 자신의 마음을 숨겼으나, 마침내 건강이 무너지고 자신이 죽음에 가까워졌다고 여기게 되었다."], ["He then wrote to the beautiful queen that he was dying for love of her.", "그러자 그는 아름다운 왕비에게 그녀를 사랑하는 까닭에 죽어 간다고 편지를 썼다."]], [["The gentle Isoude, in a moment of pity for the friend of Tristram, returned him an answer so kind and compassionate that it restored him to life.", "상냥한 이소우데는 트리스트람의 친구를 향한 일순간의 연민에서, 그를 다시 살려 낼 만큼 다정하고 측은한 답장을 보냈다."], ["A few days afterwards Tristram found this letter.", "며칠 뒤 트리스트람이 이 편지를 발견했다."], ["The most terrible jealousy took possession of his soul; he would have slain Pheredin, who with difficulty made his escape.", "가장 끔찍한 질투가 그의 영혼을 사로잡았다. 그는 페레딘을 죽이려 했으나, 페레딘은 간신히 몸을 피했다."], ["Then Tristram mounted his horse, and rode to the forest, where for ten days he took no rest nor food.", "그러고 나서 트리스트람은 말에 올라 숲으로 달려갔고, 그곳에서 열흘 동안 쉬지도 먹지도 않았다."], ["At length he was found by a damsel lying almost dead by the brink of a fountain.", "마침내 그는 한 처녀에 의해, 샘가에 거의 죽은 듯이 누워 있는 상태로 발견되었다."], ["She recognized him, and tried in vain to rouse his attention.", "처녀는 그를 알아보고 그의 주의를 끌어 보려 했으나 헛수고였다."], ["At last recollecting his love for music she went and got her harp, and played thereon.", "결국 처녀는 그가 음악을 사랑한다는 것을 떠올리고는, 가서 자신의 하프를 가져와 연주했다. 트리스트람은 망연한 상태에서 깨어났다."], ["Tristram was roused from his reverie; tears flowed; he breathed more freely; he took the harp from the maiden, and sung this lay, with a voice broken with sobs:\n“Sweet I sang in former days, Kind love perfected my lays: Now my art alone displays The woe that on my being preys.", "눈물이 흘렀다. 그는 한결 편하게 숨을 쉬었고, 처녀에게서 하프를 받아 들고는 흐느낌으로 갈라진 목소리로 이 노래를 불렀다."]], [["“Charming love, delicious power, Worshipped from my earliest hour, Thou who life on all dost shower, Love!", "\"지난날 나는 감미롭게 노래했네, 다정한 사랑이 내 노래를 완성해 주었지. 이제 나의 기예는 오직 내 존재를 갉아먹는 비통만을 드러낼 뿐이라네."], ["my life thou dost devour.", "매혹의 사랑이여, 황홀한 권능이여, 내 가장 어린 시절부터 받들어 온 그대여, 만물 위에 생명을 쏟아붓는 그대여, 사랑이여! 그대는 내 생명을 삼키는구나."], ["“In death’s hour I beg of thee, Isoude, dearest enemy, Thou who erst couldst kinder be, When I’m gone, forget not me.", "죽음의 시각에 나 그대에게 비노니, 이소우데여, 가장 사랑스러운 원수여, 한때는 더 다정할 수 있었던 그대여, 내가 떠난 뒤에도 나를 잊지 말아 다오. 내 무덤 위로 지나는 이들이 내가 낮이 누워 있는 동안 자주 읽으리라, '사랑에 있어 트리스트람과 견줄 자 아무도 없었으나, 그녀는 그를 죽게 내버려 두었노라.'\""]], [["“On my gravestone passers-by Oft will read, as low I lie, ‘Never wight in love could vie With Tristram, yet she let him die.’”", "트리스트람은 노래를 마치고 나서, 그것을 옮겨 적어 처녀에게 건네며, 부디 왕비에게 전해 달라고 간곡히 부탁했다."]], [["Tristram, having finished his lay, wrote it off and gave it to the damsel, conjuring her to present it to the queen.", "한편 이소우데 왕비는 트리스트람이 떠난 일로 위로받을 길이 없었다."], ["Meanwhile Queen Isoude was inconsolable at the absence of Tristram. She discovered that it was caused by the fatal letter which she had written to Pheredin.", "그녀는 그 원인이 자신이 페레딘에게 써 보낸 그 치명적인 편지에 있었음을 알아냈다."], ["Innocent, but in despair at the sad effects of her letter, she wrote another to Pheredin, charging him never to see her again.", "죄가 없으면서도 자기 편지가 빚은 슬픈 결과에 절망한 그녀는, 페레딘에게 다시는 자신을 만나지 말라는 또 한 통의 편지를 써 보냈다."], ["The unhappy lover obeyed this cruel decree.", "불행한 연인은 이 잔혹한 명령에 따랐다."], ["He plunged into the forest, and died of grief and love in a hermit’s cell.", "그는 숲속으로 뛰어들었고, 어느 은자의 암자에서 슬픔과 사랑으로 죽고 말았다."]], [["Isoude passed her days in lamenting the absence and unknown fate of Tristram.", "이소우데는 트리스트람이 떠나 알 길 없는 운명에 처한 것을 한탄하며 나날을 보냈다."], ["One day her jealous husband, having entered her chamber unperceived, overheard her singing the following lay:", "어느 날 질투에 사로잡힌 남편이 들키지 않은 채 그녀의 방에 들어왔다가, 그녀가 다음과 같은 노래를 부르는 것을 엿듣게 되었다."]], [["“My voice to piteous wail is bent, My harp to notes of languishment; Ah, love!", "\"내 목소리는 애처로운 비탄으로 기울고, 내 하프는 시름에 잠긴 가락으로 기우는구나."], ["delightsome days be meant For happier wights, with hearts content.", "아, 사랑이여! 즐거운 나날이란 마음 흡족한 더 행복한 이들을 위한 것이려니."], ["“Ah, Tristram!", "아, 트리스트람이여!"], ["far away from me, Art thou from restless anguish free?", "나에게서 멀리 떨어진 그대, 그대는 그칠 줄 모르는 고뇌에서 벗어나 있는가?"], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["couldst thou so one moment be, From her who so much loveth thee?”", "그대를 그토록 사랑하는 여인에게서 한순간이라도 그대가 그럴 수 있겠는가?\""]], [["The king hearing these words burst forth in a rage; but Isoude was too wretched to fear his violence.", "이 말을 들은 왕은 분노로 터져 나왔다. 그러나 이소우데는 그의 폭력을 두려워하기에는 너무도 비참한 처지였다."], ["“You have heard me,”", "\"당신은 제 노래를 들으셨군요.\""], ["she said; “I confess it all.", "그녀가 말했다. \"모든 것을 고백하겠어요."], ["I love Tristram, and always shall love him.", "저는 트리스트람을 사랑하며, 앞으로도 늘 사랑할 것입니다."], ["Without doubt he is dead, and died for me.", "그분은 틀림없이 죽었어요, 저를 위해 죽었지요."], ["I no longer wish to live.", "저는 더 이상 살고 싶지 않습니다."], ["The blow that shall finish my misery will be most welcome.”", "제 고통을 끝내 줄 일격이라면 더없이 반가울 거예요.\""]], [["The king was moved at the distress of the fair Isoude, and perhaps the idea of Tristram’s death tended to allay his wrath.", "왕은 아름다운 이소우데의 비탄에 마음이 움직였고, 어쩌면 트리스트람이 죽었으리라는 생각이 그의 분노를 누그러뜨리는 데 도움이 되었는지도 모른다."], ["He left the queen in charge of her women, commanding them to take especial care lest her despair should lead her to do harm to herself.", "그는 왕비를 시녀들에게 맡기면서, 그녀가 절망한 나머지 스스로를 해치는 일이 없도록 각별히 살피라고 명했다."]], [["Tristram meanwhile, distracted as he was, rendered a most important service to the shepherds by slaying a gigantic robber named Taullas, who was in the habit of plundering their flocks and rifling their cottages.", "그러는 동안 트리스트람은 그처럼 제정신이 아닌 상태에서도, 양 떼를 약탈하고 오두막을 털곤 하던 타울라스라는 거대한 도적을 죽임으로써 목자들에게 더없이 중요한 봉사를 했다."], ["The shepherds, in their gratitude to Tristram, bore him in triumph to King Mark to have him bestow on him a suitable reward.", "목자들은 트리스트람에게 감사하여, 그가 마땅한 보상을 받게 하고자 그를 의기양양하게 마크 왕에게 데려갔다."], ["No wonder Mark failed to recognize in the half-clad, wild man, before him his nephew Tristram; but grateful for the service the unknown had rendered he ordered him to be well taken care of, and gave him in charge to the queen and her women.", "옷을 반쯤만 걸친 채 앞에 선 야생인이 조카 트리스트람임을 마크가 알아보지 못한 것도 무리는 아니었다. 그러나 이 낯선 자가 행한 봉사에 감사한 마음으로 왕은 그를 잘 보살피라 명하고, 왕비와 그 시녀들에게 그를 맡겼다."], ["Under such care Tristram rapidly recovered his serenity and his health, so that the romancer tells us he became handsomer than ever.", "이러한 보살핌 속에서 트리스트람은 평정과 건강을 빠르게 되찾았으니, 이야기꾼의 말로는 그가 그 어느 때보다 잘생겨졌다고 한다."], ["King Mark’s jealousy revived with Tristram’s health and good looks, and, in spite of his debt of gratitude so lately increased, he again banished him from the court.", "마크 왕의 질투는 트리스트람의 건강과 준수한 외모와 더불어 되살아났고, 그토록 최근에 더 깊어진 감사의 빚에도 불구하고, 왕은 그를 다시 궁정에서 추방했다."]], [["Sir Tristram left Cornwall, and proceeded into the land of Loegria (England) in quest of adventures.", "트리스트람 경은 콘월을 떠나, 모험을 찾아 로에그리아[잉글랜드] 땅으로 나아갔다."], ["One day he entered a wide forest.", "어느 날 그는 드넓은 숲으로 들어섰다."], ["The sound of a little bell showed him that some inhabitant was near.", "작은 종소리가 누군가 거주하는 이가 가까이 있음을 알려 주었다."], ["He followed the sound, and found a hermit, who informed him that he was in the forest of Arnantes, belonging to the fairy Viviane, the Lady of the Lake, who, smitten with love for King Arthur, had found means to entice him to this forest, where by enchantments she held him a prisoner, having deprived him of all memory of who and what he was.", "그가 소리를 따라가니 한 은자가 있었는데, 은자는 그에게 이곳이 아르낭트 숲으로, 호수의 여인인 요정 비비안에게 속한 곳이라고 알려 주었다. 비비안은 아서 왕을 향한 사랑에 사로잡혀, 그를 이 숲으로 꾀어들일 방도를 찾아냈고, 마법으로 그를 포로로 붙들어 두면서 자신이 누구이며 무엇인지에 관한 기억을 모두 앗아 갔다는 것이었다."], ["The hermit informed him that all the knights of the Round Table were out in search of the king, and that he (Tristram) was now in the scene of the most grand and important adventures.", "은자는 그에게 원탁의 기사들이 모두 왕을 찾아 나섰으며, 그(트리스트람)는 이제 가장 위대하고 중요한 모험의 무대에 들어와 있다고 일러 주었다."]], [["This was enough to animate Tristram in the search.", "이것만으로도 트리스트람이 그 탐색에 의욕을 불태우기에 충분했다."], ["He had not wandered far before he encountered a knight of Arthur’s court, who proved to be Sir Kay the Seneschal, who demanded of him whence he came.", "그는 멀리 가기도 전에 아서 궁정의 한 기사와 마주쳤는데, 그는 다름 아닌 시종장 케이 경으로 밝혀졌고, 케이는 트리스트람에게 어디서 왔느냐고 물었다."], ["Tristram answering, “From Cornwall,” Sir Kay did not let slip the opportunity of a joke at the expense of the Cornish knight.", "트리스트람이 \"콘월에서 왔소\"라고 대답하자, 케이 경은 콘월 기사를 두고 농담할 기회를 놓치지 않았다."], ["Tristram chose to leave him in his error, and even confirmed him in it; for meeting some other knights Tristram declined to just with them.", "트리스트람은 그가 오해하도록 내버려 두었고, 심지어 그 오해를 더 굳혀 주기까지 했다. 다른 기사들 몇몇을 만났을 때에도 트리스트람은 그들과 겨루기를 사양했던 것이다."], ["They spent the night together at an abbey, where Tristram submitted patiently to all their jokes.", "그들은 어느 수도원에서 밤을 함께 보냈는데, 그곳에서 트리스트람은 그들의 온갖 농담을 묵묵히 참아 냈다."], ["The Seneschal gave the word to his companions that they should set out early next day, and intercept the Cornish knight on his way, and enjoy the amusement of seeing his fright when they should insist on running a tilt with him.", "시종장은 동료들에게, 이튿날 일찍 출발하여 길에서 콘월 기사를 가로막고, 함께 창 시합을 하자고 우길 때 그가 겁에 질리는 꼴을 구경하는 재미를 누리자고 일러두었다."], ["Tristram next morning found himself alone; he put on his armor, and set out to continue his quest.", "이튿날 아침 트리스트람은 혼자 남은 자신을 발견했다. 그는 갑옷을 차려입고 탐색을 계속하러 길을 나섰다."], ["He soon saw before him the Seneschal and the three knights, who barred the way, and insisted on a just.", "곧 그는 앞쪽에서 시종장과 세 기사가 길을 가로막고 서서 시합을 하자고 우기는 것을 보았다."], ["Tristram excused himself a long time; at last he reluctantly took his stand.", "트리스트람은 한참 동안 사양하다가, 마침내 마지못해 자세를 잡았다."], ["He encountered them, one after the other, and overthrew them all four, man and horse, and then rode off, bidding them not to forget their friend, the knight of Cornwall.", "그는 그들과 차례차례 맞붙어 넷 모두를 사람과 말 가릴 것 없이 쓰러뜨린 다음, 자신들의 벗인 콘월 기사를 잊지 말라고 이르고는 말을 달려 가 버렸다."]], [["Tristram had not ridden far when he met a damsel, who cried out, “Ah, my lord!", "트리스트람이 얼마 가지 않았을 때, 한 처녀를 만났는데, 처녀가 소리쳤다. \"아, 나리!"], ["hasten forward, and prevent a horrid treason!”", "어서 서둘러 가셔서, 끔찍한 배신을 막아 주세요!\""], ["Tristram flew to her assistance, and soon reached a spot where he beheld a knight, whom three others had borne to the ground, and were unlacing his helmet in order to cut off his head.", "트리스트람은 그녀를 돕고자 날듯이 달려갔고, 곧 한 기사가 다른 세 기사에게 땅에 쓰러뜨려져, 목을 베려고 투구의 끈이 풀리고 있는 광경을 보게 되었다."]], [["Tristram flew to the rescue, and slew with one stroke of his lance one of the assailants.", "트리스트람은 구하러 날듯이 달려가, 창 한 번을 휘둘러 공격자 가운데 하나를 죽였다."], ["The knight, recovering his feet, sacrificed another to his vengeance, and the third made his escape.", "발을 다시 딛고 일어선 그 기사는 또 하나를 자기 복수의 제물로 삼았고, 세 번째 자는 달아났다."], ["The rescued knight then raised the visor of his helmet, and a long white beard fell down upon his breast.", "구출된 기사가 투구의 면갑을 들어 올리자, 길고 흰 수염이 가슴 위로 흘러내렸다."], ["The majesty and venerable air of this knight made Tristram suspect that it was none other than Arthur himself, and the prince confirmed his conjecture.", "이 기사의 위엄과 존엄한 풍모를 보고 트리스트람은 그가 다름 아닌 아서 본인이 아닐까 의심하게 되었고, 군주는 그의 추측을 확인해 주었다."], ["Tristram would have knelt before him, but Arthur received him in his arms, and inquired his name and country; but Tristram declined to disclose them, on the plea that he was now on a quest requiring secrecy.", "트리스트람이 그 앞에 무릎을 꿇으려 하자 아서는 그를 두 팔로 받아 안고는 이름과 나라를 물었다. 그러나 트리스트람은 지금 비밀을 요하는 탐색 중이라는 이유를 들어 밝히기를 사양했다."], ["At this moment the damsel who had brought Tristram to the rescue darted forward, and, seizing the king’s hand, drew from his finger a ring, the gift of the fairy, and by that act dissolved the enchantment.", "바로 이 순간, 트리스트람을 구조에 나서게 했던 처녀가 앞으로 달려 나와 왕의 손을 붙잡고, 그의 손가락에서 요정이 준 반지를 빼냈으니, 그 행위로 마법이 풀렸다."], ["Arthur, having recovered his reason and his memory, offered to Tristram to attach him to his court, and to confer honors and dignities upon him; but Tristram declined all, and only consented to accompany him till he should see him safe in the hands of his knights.", "이성과 기억을 되찾은 아서는 트리스트람에게 자신의 궁정에 머무르며 영예와 작위를 받으라고 권했다. 그러나 트리스트람은 모두 사양하고, 다만 왕이 자신의 기사들의 손에 안전하게 맡겨지는 것을 볼 때까지만 동행하기로 했다."], ["Soon after, Hector de Marys rode up, and saluted the king, who on his part introduced him to Tristram as one of the bravest of his knights.", "곧이어 헥토르 데 마리스가 말을 달려와 왕에게 인사하자, 왕은 그를 트리스트람에게 자신의 가장 용감한 기사 가운데 하나로 소개했다."], ["Tristram took leave of the king and his faithful follower, and continued his quest.", "트리스트람은 왕과 그 충직한 신하에게 작별을 고하고, 탐색을 계속했다."]], [["We cannot follow Tristram through all the adventures which filled this epoch of his history.", "우리는 그의 생애에서 이 시기를 가득 채운 모든 모험을 두루 따라갈 수는 없다."], ["Suffice it to say, he fulfilled on all occasions the duty of a true knight, rescuing the oppressed, redressing wrongs, abolishing evil customs, and suppressing injustice, thus by constant action endeavoring to lighten the pains of absence from her he loved.", "다만 이렇게만 말해 두면 충분하리라. 그는 모든 경우에 참된 기사의 의무를 다하여, 억압받는 이들을 구하고, 잘못을 바로잡고, 악습을 없애고, 불의를 억눌렀으니, 이렇듯 끊임없는 행동으로 사랑하는 여인과 떨어져 있는 고통을 덜고자 애썼다."], ["In the meantime Isoude, separated from her dear Tristram, passed her days in languor and regret.", "그러는 동안 사랑하는 트리스트람과 헤어진 이소우데는 무기력과 회한 속에서 나날을 보냈다."], ["At length she could no longer resist the desire to hear some news of her lover.", "마침내 그녀는 연인의 소식을 듣고 싶은 갈망을 더는 억누를 수 없었다."], ["She wrote a letter, and sent it by one of her damsels, niece of her faithful Brengwain.", "그녀는 편지를 써서, 자신의 충직한 브렝귄의 조카인 시녀 가운데 하나를 시켜 보냈다."], ["One day Tristram, weary with his exertions, had dismounted and laid himself down by the side of a fountain and fallen asleep.", "어느 날 트리스트람은 노고에 지쳐 말에서 내려 샘가에 몸을 누이고 잠들어 있었다."], ["The damsel of Queen Isoude arrived at the same fountain, and recognized Passebreul, the horse of Tristram, and presently perceived his master asleep.", "이소우데 왕비의 시녀가 같은 샘에 다다랐다가, 트리스트람의 말 파스브뢸을 알아보았고, 이내 잠들어 있는 그 주인을 알아차렸다."], ["He was thin and pale, showing evident marks of the pain he suffered in separation from his beloved.", "그는 야위고 창백했으니, 사랑하는 여인과 헤어져 겪는 고통의 자취가 또렷이 드러나 있었다."], ["She awakened him, and gave him the letter which she bore, and Tristram enjoyed the pleasure, so sweet to a lover, of hearing from and talking about the object of his affections.", "시녀는 그를 깨워 가지고 온 편지를 건넸고, 트리스트람은 연인에게 더없이 달콤한 즐거움, 곧 사모하는 대상의 소식을 듣고 그에 관해 이야기하는 즐거움을 누렸다."], ["He prayed the damsel to postpone her return till after the magnificent tournament which Arthur had proclaimed should have taken place, and conducted her to the castle of Persides, a brave and loyal knight, who received her with great consideration.", "그는 시녀에게, 아서가 선포한 성대한 마상 시합이 열린 뒤로 돌아가는 것을 미루어 달라고 청했고, 용감하고 충직한 기사 페르시데스의 성으로 그녀를 데려갔으며, 페르시데스는 그녀를 크게 배려하여 맞이했다."]], [["Tristram conducted the damsel of Queen Isoude to the tournament, and had her placed in the balcony among the ladies of the queen.", "트리스트람은 이소우데 왕비의 시녀를 마상 시합으로 데려가, 왕비의 귀부인들 사이 발코니에 그녀를 앉혀 두었다."]], [["“He glanced and saw the stately galleries, Dame, damsel, each through worship of their Queen White-robed in honor of the stainless child, And some with scatter’d jewels, like a bank Of maiden snow mingled with sparks of fire.", "\"그가 흘끗 보니 위풍당당한 회랑들이 있어, 귀부인이며 처녀들이 저마다 자기네 왕비를 흠모하여 티 없는 아이를 기리며 흰옷을 입었고, 더러는 흩뿌려진 보석들로 단장하여, 마치 불꽃이 섞여든 처녀의 눈 같은 둑처럼 보였다."], ["He looked but once, and veiled his eyes again.”", "그는 단 한 번 보고는, 다시 눈을 가렸다.\""], ["—The Last Tournament.", "─「마지막 마상 시합」."]], [["He then joined the tourney.", "그러고 나서 그는 시합에 참가했다."], ["Nothing could exceed his strength and valor.", "그의 힘과 용맹은 무엇으로도 능가할 수 없었다."], ["Launcelot admired him, and by a secret presentiment declined to dispute the honor of the day with a knight so gallant and so skilful.", "란슬롯은 그를 찬탄했고, 어떤 은밀한 예감에서, 그토록 늠름하고 노련한 기사와 그날의 영예를 두고 다투기를 사양했다."], ["Arthur descended from the balcony to greet the conqueror; but the modest and devoted Tristram, content with having borne off the prize in the sight of the messenger of Isoude, made his escape with her, and disappeared.", "아서는 승자를 맞이하려 발코니에서 내려왔다. 그러나 겸손하고 헌신적인 트리스트람은 이소우데의 사자가 지켜보는 가운데 상을 거머쥔 것으로 만족하여, 그녀와 함께 빠져나가 자취를 감추었다."]], [["The next day the tourney recommenced.", "이튿날 시합이 다시 시작되었다."], ["Tristram assumed different armor, that he might not be known; but he was soon detected by the terrible blows that he gave.", "트리스트람은 정체가 드러나지 않도록 다른 갑옷을 둘렀다. 그러나 그가 휘두르는 무시무시한 일격으로 이내 정체가 드러나고 말았다."], ["Arthur and Guenever had no doubt that it was the same knight who had borne off the prize of the day before.", "아서와 귀네버는 그가 전날 상을 거머쥔 바로 그 기사임을 의심하지 않았다."], ["Arthur’s gallant spirit was roused.", "아서의 늠름한 기개가 들끓었다."], ["After Launcelot of the Lake and Sir Gawain he was accounted the best knight of the Round Table.", "그는 호수의 란슬롯과 가웨인 경 다음으로 원탁에서 가장 뛰어난 기사로 꼽혔다."], ["He went privately and armed himself, and came into the tourney in undistinguished armor.", "그는 남몰래 가서 무장을 갖추고는, 식별할 수 없는 갑옷을 입고 시합에 들어섰다."], ["He ran a just with Tristram, whom he shook in his seat; but Tristram, who did not know him, threw him out of the saddle.", "그는 트리스트람과 시합을 벌여 그를 안장에서 흔들리게 했으나, 그가 누군지 몰랐던 트리스트람은 그를 안장에서 떨어뜨렸다."], ["Arthur recovered himself, and content with having made proof of the stranger knight bade Launcelot finish the adventure, and vindicate the honor of the Round Table.", "아서는 몸을 추슬렀고, 낯선 기사의 실력을 시험해 본 것으로 만족하여, 란슬롯에게 이 모험을 마무리하고 원탁의 영예를 지켜 내라 명했다."], ["Sir Launcelot, at the bidding of the monarch, assailed Tristram, whose lance was already broken in former encounters.", "란슬롯 경은 군주의 명에 따라 트리스트람을 공격했는데, 트리스트람의 창은 앞선 싸움들에서 이미 부러져 있었다."], ["But the law of this sort of combat was that the knight after having broken his lance must fight with his sword, and must not refuse to meet with his shield the lance of his antagonist.", "그러나 이런 종류의 결투에는 한 가지 법이 있었으니, 기사는 창이 부러진 뒤에는 칼로 싸워야 하며, 상대의 창을 방패로 막기를 거부해서는 안 된다는 것이었다."], ["Tristram met Launcelot’s charge upon his shield, which that terrible lance could not fail to pierce.", "트리스트람은 란슬롯의 돌격을 방패로 받았는데, 그 무시무시한 창이 방패를 뚫지 못할 리 없었다."], ["It inflicted a wound upon Tristram’s side, and, breaking, left the iron in the wound.", "창은 트리스트람의 옆구리에 상처를 입히고는, 부러지면서 그 쇠끝을 상처 안에 남겼다."], ["But Tristram also with his sword smote so vigorously on Launcelot’s casque that he cleft it, and wounded his head.", "그러나 트리스트람 또한 칼로 란슬롯의 투구를 어찌나 힘차게 내리쳤던지, 투구를 쪼개고 그의 머리에 상처를 입혔다."], ["The wound was not deep, but the blood flowed into his eyes, and blinded him for a moment, and Tristram, who thought himself mortally wounded, retired from the field.", "상처는 깊지 않았으나, 피가 눈으로 흘러들어 한순간 그의 눈을 멀게 했고, 자신이 치명상을 입었다고 여긴 트리스트람은 시합장에서 물러났다."], ["Launcelot declared to the king that he had never received such a blow in his life before.", "란슬롯은 평생 그런 일격을 받아 본 적이 없다고 왕에게 말했다."]], [["Tristram hastened to Gouvernail, his squire, who drew forth the iron, bound up the wound, and gave him immediate ease.", "트리스트람은 자신의 종자 구베르나일에게 서둘러 갔고, 종자는 쇠끝을 빼내어 상처를 싸매 주어 그를 곧바로 편하게 해 주었다."], ["Tristram after the tournament kept retired in his tent, but Arthur, with the consent of all the knights of the Round Table, decreed him the honors of the second day.", "트리스트람은 시합이 끝난 뒤 천막 안에 틀어박혀 있었으나, 아서는 원탁 모든 기사들의 동의를 얻어 그에게 둘째 날의 영예를 내렸다."], ["But it was no longer a secret that the victor of the two days was the same individual, and Gouvernail, being questioned, confirmed the suspicions of Launcelot and Arthur that it was no other than Sir Tristram of Leonais, the nephew of the king of Cornwall.", "그러나 이틀의 승자가 동일 인물이라는 것은 더 이상 비밀이 아니었고, 질문을 받은 구베르나일은 그가 다름 아닌 콘월 왕의 조카, 레오나이의 트리스트람 경임을 확인해 줌으로써, 란슬롯과 아서의 의심을 굳혀 주었다."]], [["King Arthur, who desired to reward his distinguished valor, and knew that his Uncle Mark had ungratefully banished him, would have eagerly availed himself of the opportunity to attach Tristram to his court,—all the knights of the Round Table declaring with acclamation that it would be impossible to find a more worthy companion.", "그의 빼어난 용맹에 보답하고자 하고, 그의 숙부 마크가 그를 배은망덕하게 추방했음을 알고 있던 아서 왕은, 이 기회를 기꺼이 이용하여 트리스트람을 자신의 궁정에 머무르게 하려 했다. 원탁의 기사들 모두가, 이보다 더 합당한 동료를 찾기란 불가능하리라고 환호로 선언했다."], ["But Tristram had already departed in search of adventures, and the damsel of Queen Isoude returned to her mistress.", "그러나 트리스트람은 이미 모험을 찾아 떠난 뒤였고, 이소우데 왕비의 시녀는 자기 여주인에게로 돌아갔다."]], [["# SIR TRISTRAM’S BATTLE WITH SIR LAUNCELOT", "# SIR TRISTRAM’S BATTLE WITH SIR LAUNCELOT"]], [["SIR TRISTRAM rode through a forest and saw ten men fighting, and one man did battle against nine.", "트리스트람 경은 어느 숲을 지나가다가 열 명의 사내가 싸우는 것을 보았는데, 한 사람이 아홉 명을 상대로 싸우고 있었다."], ["So he rode to the knights and cried to them, bidding them cease their battle, for they did themselves great shame, so many knights to fight against one.", "그래서 그는 그 기사들에게로 말을 달려가 싸움을 그치라고 외치며, 이렇게 많은 기사가 한 사람을 상대로 싸우는 것은 스스로에게 크나큰 수치를 안기는 일이라고 명했다."], ["Then answered the master of the knights (his name was Sir Breuse sans Pitie, who was at that time the most villanous knight living): “Sir knight, what have ye to do to meddle with us?", "그러자 기사들의 우두머리가 대답했는데, 그의 이름은 브뢰즈 상 피티에 경으로, 당시 살아 있는 가장 비열한 기사였다. \"기사 양반, 우리 일에 끼어들어 무엇을 어쩌겠다는 거요?"], ["If ye be wise depart on your way as you came, for this knight shall not escape us.”", "현명하다면 왔던 길로 그냥 떠나시오, 이 기사는 우리 손아귀를 벗어나지 못할 테니.\""], ["“That were pity,”", "\"그것은 안타까운 일이오.\""], ["said Sir Tristram, “that so good a knight should be slain so cowardly; therefore I warn you I will succor him with all my puissance.”", "트리스트람 경이 말했다. \"이토록 훌륭한 기사가 이토록 비겁하게 살해당하다니, 그러므로 경고하건대 나는 내 온 힘을 다해 그를 도우리다.\""]], [["Then Sir Tristram alighted off his horse, because they were on foot, that they should not slay his horse.", "그러자 트리스트람 경은 자기 말에서 내렸는데, 저들이 걸어서 싸우고 있었으므로 자신의 말이 죽임을 당하지 않도록 하기 위함이었다."], ["And he smote on the right hand and on the left so vigorously that well-nigh at every stroke he struck down a knight.", "그리고 그는 오른쪽으로 왼쪽으로 어찌나 힘차게 내리쳤던지, 거의 한 번 칠 때마다 기사를 한 명씩 쓰러뜨릴 정도였다."], ["At last they fled, with Breuse sans Pitie, into the tower, and shut Sir Tristram without the gate.", "마침내 그들은 브뢰즈 상 피티에와 더불어 탑 안으로 달아나, 트리스트람 경을 문밖에 가두어 버렸다."], ["Then Sir Tristram returned back to the rescued knight, and found him sitting under a tree, sore wounded.", "그러자 트리스트람 경은 구해 준 기사에게로 되돌아가, 그가 나무 아래에 앉아 심하게 다쳐 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["“Fair knight,”", "\"고귀한 기사여,\""], ["said he, “how is it with you?”", "그가 말했다. \"그대는 어떠하오?\""], ["“Sir knight,”", "\"기사 양반,\""], ["said Sir Palamedes, for he it was, “I thank you of your great goodness, for ye have rescued me from death.”", "팔라메데스 경이 말했는데, 바로 그였기 때문이다, \"그대의 크나큰 선의에 감사하오, 그대가 나를 죽음에서 구해 주었으니.\""], ["“What is your name?”", "\"그대의 이름은 무엇이오?\""], ["said Sir Tristram.", "트리스트람 경이 말했다."], ["He said, “My name is Sir Palamedes.”", "그가 말했다. \"내 이름은 팔라메데스 경이오.\""], ["“Say ye so?”", "\"그렇게 말하는가?\""], ["said Sir Tristram; “now know that thou art the man in the world that I most hate; therefore make thee ready, for I will do battle with thee.”", "트리스트람 경이 말했다. \"이제 알라, 그대는 이 세상에서 내가 가장 미워하는 사람이로다, 그러니 채비를 하라, 내 그대와 싸우려 하노라.\""], ["“What is your name?”", "\"그대의 이름은 무엇이오?\""], ["said Sir Palamedes.", "팔라메데스 경이 말했다."], ["“My name is Sir Tristram, your mortal enemy.”", "\"내 이름은 트리스트람 경, 그대의 불구대천의 원수로다.\""], ["“It may be so,”", "\"그럴지도 모르오.\""], ["said Sir Palamedes; “but you have done overmuch for me this day, that I should fight with you.", "팔라메데스 경이 말했다. \"허나 오늘 그대는 내게 너무 많은 것을 베풀었으니, 내 어찌 그대와 싸우겠소."], ["Moreover, it will be no honor for you to have to do with me, for you are fresh and I am wounded.", "더구나 나와 겨루는 것은 그대에게 명예가 되지 못하오, 그대는 멀쩡하고 나는 부상을 입었으니."], ["Therefore, if you will needs have to do with me, assign me a day, and I shall meet you without fail.”", "그러니 굳이 나와 겨루고자 한다면, 내게 날을 정해 주시오, 그러면 내 어김없이 그대를 만나리다.\""], ["“You say well,”", "\"그대의 말이 옳소.\""], ["said Sir Tristram; “now I assign you to meet me in the meadow by the river of Camelot, where Merlin set the monument.”", "트리스트람 경이 말했다. \"이제 그대에게 카멜롯 강가의 풀밭에서 나를 만날 것을 정하노니, 멀린이 기념비를 세운 그곳이오.\""], ["So they were agreed.", "그리하여 두 사람은 합의했다."], ["Then they departed and took their ways diverse.", "그러고는 헤어져 각기 다른 길로 갔다."], ["Sir Tristram passed through a great forest into a plain, till he came to a priory, and there he reposed him with a good man six days.", "트리스트람 경은 큰 숲을 지나 평원으로 들어섰고, 마침내 어느 수도원에 다다라 그곳에서 어진 사람과 함께 엿새 동안 쉬었다."]], [["Then departed Sir Tristram, and rode straight into Camelot to the monument of Merlin, and there he looked about him for Sir Palamedes.", "그러고 나서 트리스트람 경은 떠나, 곧장 카멜롯의 멀린의 기념비로 말을 달려가, 그곳에서 팔라메데스 경을 찾아 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["And he perceived a seemly knight, who came riding against him all in white, with a covered shield.", "그러자 그는 온통 흰 차림에 가린 방패를 든 늠름한 기사 하나가 자기를 향해 말을 달려오는 것을 알아보았다."], ["When he came nigh Sir Tristram said aloud, “Welcome, sir knight, and well and truly have you kept your promise.”", "그가 가까이 오자 트리스트람 경이 큰 소리로 말했다. \"환영하오, 기사 양반, 그대는 약속을 잘 그리고 진실하게 지켰소이다.\""], ["Then they made ready their shields and spears, and came together with all the might of their horses, so fiercely, that both the horses and the knights fell to the earth.", "그러고는 두 사람은 방패와 창을 갖추고, 말이 낼 수 있는 온 힘을 다해 서로 맞부딪쳤는데, 어찌나 사납던지 말과 기사가 다 함께 땅에 나뒹굴었다."], ["And as soon as they might they quitted their horses, and struck together with bright swords as men of might, and each wounded the other wonderfully sore, so that the blood ran out upon the grass.", "그리고 일어날 수 있게 되자마자 그들은 말을 버리고, 힘센 사내들답게 번쩍이는 검으로 서로 맞부딪쳐, 저마다 상대에게 놀랍도록 심한 상처를 입혀, 피가 풀밭 위로 흘러내렸다."], ["Thus they fought for the space of four hours and never one would speak to the other one word.", "이렇게 그들은 네 시간 동안 싸우면서도 어느 한쪽도 다른 쪽에게 한 마디 말도 건네지 않았다."], ["Then at last spake the white knight, and said, “Sir, thou fightest wonderful well, as ever I saw a knight; therefore, if it please you, tell me your name.”", "그러다가 마침내 흰 기사가 입을 열어 말했다. \"기사 양반, 그대는 놀랍도록 잘 싸우오, 내 일찍이 본 어떤 기사 못지않게, 그러니 괜찮다면 그대의 이름을 말해 주시오.\""], ["“Why dost thou ask my name?”", "\"어찌하여 내 이름을 묻는가?\""], ["said Sir Tristram; “art thou not Sir Palamedes?”", "트리스트람 경이 말했다. \"그대는 팔라메데스 경이 아니오?\""], ["“No, fair knight,”", "\"아니오, 고귀한 기사여,\""], ["said he, “I am Sir Launcelot of the Lake.”", "그가 말했다. \"나는 호수의 란슬롯 경이오.\""], ["“Alas!”", "\"아아!\""], ["said Sir Tristram, “what have I done?", "트리스트람 경이 말했다. \"내가 무슨 짓을 한 것인가?"], ["for you are the man of the world that I love best.”", "그대는 이 세상에서 내가 가장 사랑하는 사람이거늘.\""], ["“Fair knight,”", "\"고귀한 기사여,\""], ["said Sir Launcelot, “tell me your name.”", "란슬롯 경이 말했다. \"그대의 이름을 말해 주시오.\""], ["“Truly,”", "\"참으로,\""], ["said he, “my name is Sir Tristram de Lionesse.”", "그가 말했다. \"내 이름은 리오네스의 트리스트람 경이오.\""], ["“Alas!", "\"아아!"], ["alas!”", "아아!\""], ["said Sir Launcelot, “what adventure has befallen me!”", "란슬롯 경이 말했다. \"내게 무슨 변고가 닥친 것인가!\""], ["And therewith Sir Launcelot kneeled down and yielded him up his sword; and Sir Tristram kneeled down and yielded him up his sword; and so either gave other the degree.", "그러면서 란슬롯 경은 무릎을 꿇고 자기 검을 그에게 바쳤고, 트리스트람 경도 무릎을 꿇고 자기 검을 그에게 바쳤으니, 이렇게 서로가 상대에게 영예를 돌렸다."], ["And then they both went to the stone, and sat them down upon it and took off their helms and each kissed the other a hundred times.", "그러고는 두 사람 모두 그 돌로 가서, 그 위에 앉아 투구를 벗고 서로 백 번씩 입을 맞추었다."], ["And then anon they rode toward Camelot, and on the way they met with Sir Gawain and Sir Gaheris, that had made promise to Arthur never to come again to the court till they had brought Sir Tristram with them.", "그러고 나서 곧 그들은 카멜롯을 향해 말을 달렸는데, 가는 길에 가웨인 경과 가헤리스 경을 만났으니, 이들은 트리스트람 경을 데려오기 전까지는 결코 궁정으로 돌아오지 않겠다고 아서에게 약속했던 터였다."]], [["“Return again,”", "\"되돌아가게.\""], ["said Sir Launcelot, “for your quest is done; for I have met with Sir Tristram.", "란슬롯 경이 말했다. \"그대들의 탐색은 끝났네, 내가 트리스트람 경을 만났으니."], ["Lo, here he is in his own person.”", "보게, 여기 그가 몸소 있도다.\""], ["Then was Sir Gawain glad, and said to Sir Tristram, “Ye are welcome.”", "그러자 가웨인 경은 기뻐하며 트리스트람 경에게 말했다. \"환영하오.\""], ["With this came King Arthur, and when he wist there was Sir Tristram, he ran unto him, and took him by the hand, and said, “Sir Tristram, ye are as welcome as any knight that ever came to this court.”", "이때 아서 왕이 다가왔고, 트리스트람 경이 거기 있음을 알아채자 그에게로 달려가 그의 손을 잡고 말했다. \"트리스트람 경, 그대는 일찍이 이 궁정에 온 어떤 기사 못지않게 환영받는 사람이오.\""], ["Then Sir Tristram told the king how he came thither for to have had to do with Sir Palamedes, and how he had rescued him from Sir Breuse sans Pitie and the nine knights.", "그러자 트리스트람 경은 왕에게, 자신이 팔라메데스 경과 겨루려고 이곳에 왔던 경위와, 어떻게 그를 브뢰즈 상 피티에와 아홉 기사로부터 구해 냈는지를 이야기했다."], ["Then King Arthur took Sir Tristram by the hand, and went to the Table Round, and Queen Guenever came, and many ladies with her, and all the ladies said with one voice, “Welcome, Sir Tristram.”", "그러자 아서 왕은 트리스트람 경의 손을 잡고 원탁으로 갔고, 귀네비어 왕비가 많은 귀부인을 거느리고 다가왔으며, 모든 귀부인이 한목소리로 말했다. \"환영하오, 트리스트람 경.\""], ["“Welcome,”", "\"환영하오.\""], ["said the knights.", "기사들이 말했다."], ["“Welcome,”", "\"환영하오.\""], ["said Arthur, “for one of the best of knights, and the gentlest of the world, and the man of most worship; for of all manner of hunting thou bearest the prize, and of all measures of blowing thou art the beginning, and of all the terms of hunting and hawking ye are the inventor, and of all instruments of music ye are the best skilled; therefore, gentle knight,”", "아서가 말했다. \"가장 훌륭한 기사 가운데 하나이며, 세상에서 가장 점잖고, 가장 명예로운 사람이여, 온갖 종류의 사냥에서 그대는 으뜸의 영예를 지녔으며, 온갖 뿔피리 부는 법에서 그대는 시초이며, 사냥과 매사냥의 온갖 용어에서 그대는 창안자이며, 온갖 악기에서 그대는 가장 빼어난 솜씨를 지녔으니, 그러므로 점잖은 기사여,\""], ["said Arthur, “ye are welcome to this court.”", "아서가 말했다. \"그대는 이 궁정에서 환영받는 사람이오.\""], ["And then King Arthur made Sir Tristram knight of the Table Round with great nobley and feasting as can be thought.", "그러고 나서 아서 왕은 가히 상상할 수 있는 가장 큰 위엄과 잔치를 베풀어 트리스트람 경을 원탁의 기사로 삼았다."]], [["# SIR TRISTRAM AS A SPORTSMAN", "# SIR TRISTRAM AS A SPORTSMAN"]], [["Tristram is often alluded to by the Romancers as the great authority and model in all matters relating to the chase.", "트리스트람은 로맨스 작가들에 의해 사냥에 관한 모든 일에서 위대한 권위자이자 본보기로 자주 언급된다."], ["In the “Faery Queene,” Tristram, in answer to the inquiries of Sir Calidore, informs him of his name and parentage, and concludes:", "「선녀 여왕」에서 트리스트람은 칼리도어 경의 물음에 답하여 자신의 이름과 혈통을 알려 주고, 이렇게 끝맺는다."]], [["“All which my days I have not lewdly spent, Nor spilt the blossom of my tender years In idlesse; but, as was convenient, Have trained been with many noble feres In gentle thewes, and such like seemly leers;[51] ’Mongst which my most delight hath always been To hunt the salvage chace, amongst my peers, Of all that rangeth in the forest green, Of which none is to me unknown that yet was seen.", "\"이 모든 날을 나는 음란하게 보내지 않았고, 내 어린 시절의 꽃송이를 게으름 속에 흩뿌리지도 않았으며, 마땅한 바대로, 많은 고귀한 동무와 더불어 점잖은 품행과 그와 같은 의젓한 학식 안에서 길러졌으니,[51] 그중에서도 나의 가장 큰 즐거움은 언제나 내 또래들과 더불어 야생의 사냥감을 쫓는 것이었으니, 푸른 숲을 누비는 모든 것 가운데, 그중에 이제껏 보인 것으로 내게 알려지지 않은 것은 없도다."]], [["“Ne is there hawk which mantleth on her perch, Whether high towering or accosting low, But I the measure of her flight do search, And all her prey, and all her diet know.", "\"제 횃대 위에서 날개를 펼치는 매도, 높이 솟구치든 낮게 다가들든, 나는 그 비행의 가늠을 살피지 못하는 것이 없고, 그 모든 먹이며, 그 모든 식성을 알도다."], ["Such be our joys, which in these forests grow.”", "이것이 이 숲에서 자라난 우리의 기쁨이로다.\""], ["————", "————"]], [["# THE ROUND TABLE", "# THE ROUND TABLE"]], [["THE famous enchanter, Merlin, had exerted all his skill in fabricating the Round Table.", "저 유명한 마법사 멀린은 원탁을 만드는 데 자신의 온갖 기예를 쏟아부었다."], ["Of the seats which surrounded it he had constructed thirteen, in memory of the thirteen Apostles.", "그는 원탁을 빙 둘러싸는 자리를 열셋 만들었는데, 이는 열세 사도를 기리기 위함이었다."], ["Twelve of these seats only could be occupied, and they only by knights of the highest fame; the thirteenth represented the seat of the traitor Judas.", "이 가운데 오직 열두 자리만이 채워질 수 있었으니, 그나마도 가장 명성 높은 기사들만이 앉을 수 있었다. 열세 번째 자리는 배신자 유다의 자리를 뜻했다."], ["It remained always empty.", "그 자리는 언제나 비어 있었다."], ["It was called the perilous seat, ever since a rash and haughty Saracen knight had dared to place himself in it, when the earth opened and swallowed him up.", "그것은 〈위험한 자리〉라 불렸으니, 일찍이 어느 무모하고 거만한 사라센 기사가 감히 거기에 앉았다가 땅이 갈라져 그를 삼켜 버린 이래로 그렇게 불렸다."]], [["“In our great hall there stood a vacant chair, Fashion’d by Merlin ere he past away, And carven with strange figures; and in and out The figures, like a serpent, ran a scroll Of letters in a tongue no man could read.", "“우리의 큰 홀에는 빈 의자 하나가 놓여 있었으니, 멀린이 세상을 떠나기 전에 만들어 놓은 것으로, 기이한 형상들이 새겨져 있었다. 그 형상들 사이로 뱀처럼 구불구불, 어떤 사람도 읽을 수 없는 언어로 쓰인 글자들의 두루마리가 흘러 다녔다."], ["And Merlin call’d it ‘The Siege perilous,’ Perilous for good and ill; ‘for there,’ he said, ‘No man could sit but he should lose himself.’”", "멀린은 그것을 〈위험한 자리〉라 불렀으니, 선한 자에게도 악한 자에게도 위험하다 하였다. ‘그 자리에는,’ 그가 말하기를, ‘누구도 앉을 수 없으되, 앉는다면 제 자신을 잃으리라.’”"], ["—The Holy Grail.", "—『성배(聖杯)』."]], [["A magic power wrote upon each seat the name of the knight who was entitled to sit in it.", "어떤 마법의 힘이 각 자리마다 거기에 앉을 자격이 있는 기사의 이름을 새겨 넣었다."], ["No one could succeed to a vacant seat unless he surpassed in valor and glorious deeds the knight who had occupied it before him; without this qualification he would be violently repelled by a hidden force.", "그 누구도 자신보다 앞서 그 자리를 차지했던 기사를 용맹과 영광스러운 무훈에서 능가하지 못한다면 빈자리를 이어받을 수 없었다. 이 자격을 갖추지 못한 자는 보이지 않는 힘에 의해 거칠게 튕겨 나갔다."], ["Thus proof was made of all those who presented themselves to replace any companions of the order who had fallen.", "이리하여 쓰러진 기사단의 동료를 대신하고자 나선 모든 이들이 시험을 받았다."]], [["One of the principal seats, that of Moraunt of Ireland, had been vacant ten years, and his name still remained over it ever since the time when that distinguished champion fell beneath the sword of Sir Tristram.", "주요한 자리 가운데 하나인 아일랜드의 모런트의 자리는 십 년 동안이나 비어 있었으며, 저 빼어난 용사가 트리스트람 경의 칼 아래 쓰러진 그때 이후로 그의 이름이 여전히 그 위에 남아 있었다."], ["Arthur now took Tristram by the hand and led him to that seat.", "이제 아서가 트리스트람의 손을 잡고 그 자리로 이끌었다."], ["Immediately the most melodious sounds were heard, and exquisite perfumes filled the place; the name of Moraunt disappeared, and that of Tristram blazed forth in light.", "그러자 곧바로 더없이 감미로운 소리가 들려오고, 그윽한 향기가 그곳을 가득 채웠다. 모런트의 이름은 사라지고, 트리스트람의 이름이 빛으로 환히 타올랐다."], ["The rare modesty of Tristram had now to be subjected to a severe task; for the clerks charged with the duty of preserving the annals of the Round Table attended, and he was required by the law of his order to declare what feats of arms he had accomplished to entitle him to take that seat.", "트리스트람의 보기 드문 겸손함은 이제 가혹한 시험을 치러야 했으니, 원탁의 연대기를 보존하는 임무를 맡은 서기들이 입회했고, 그는 기사단의 법도에 따라 그 자리에 앉을 자격을 부여할 만한 어떤 무공을 이루었는지 밝혀야 했다."], ["This ceremony being ended, Tristram received the congratulations of all his companions.", "이 의식이 끝나자 트리스트람은 모든 동료들의 축하를 받았다."], ["Sir Launcelot and Guenever took the occasion to speak to him of the fair Isoude, and to express their wish that some happy chance might bring her to the kingdom of Loegria.", "랜슬롯 경과 귀네비어는 이 기회를 빌려 그에게 아름다운 이졸데에 관한 이야기를 나누었고, 어떤 행복한 우연이 그녀를 로에그리아 왕국으로 데려와 주기를 바란다고 말했다."]], [["While Tristram was thus honored and caressed at the court of King Arthur, the most gloomy and malignant jealousy harassed the soul of Mark.", "트리스트람이 이처럼 아서 왕의 궁정에서 존경받고 환대받는 동안, 더없이 음울하고 악의에 찬 질투가 마크의 영혼을 괴롭혔다."], ["He could not look upon Isoude without remembering that she loved Tristram, and the good fortune of his nephew goaded him to thoughts of vengeance.", "그는 이졸데를 바라볼 때마다 그녀가 트리스트람을 사랑한다는 것을 떠올리지 않을 수 없었고, 조카의 행운은 그를 복수의 상념으로 몰아갔다."], ["He at last resolved to go disguised into the kingdom of Loegria, attack Tristram by stealth, and put him to death.", "그는 마침내 변장한 채 로에그리아 왕국으로 들어가 트리스트람을 몰래 습격하여 죽이기로 결심했다."], ["He took with him two knights, brought up in his court, who he thought were devoted to him; and, not willing to leave Isoude behind, named two of her maidens to attend her, together with her faithful Brengwain, and made them accompany him.", "그는 자기 궁정에서 길러져 자신에게 충성하리라 여긴 두 기사를 데리고 갔다. 그리고 이졸데를 뒤에 남겨 두기를 원치 않았기에, 그녀를 시중들 시녀 둘과 충직한 브렝귄을 지목하여 함께 동행하게 했다."]], [["Having arrived in the neighborhood of Camelot, Mark imparted his plan to his two knights, but they rejected it with horror; nay, more, they declared that they would no longer remain in his service; and left him, giving him reason to suppose that they should repair to the court to accuse him before Arthur.", "카멜롯 인근에 다다른 마크는 두 기사에게 자신의 계획을 털어놓았으나, 그들은 그것을 끔찍하게 여기며 거부했다. 아니, 그뿐 아니라 더 이상 그의 휘하에 머물지 않겠노라 선언하고는 그를 떠나 버렸다."], ["It was necessary for Mark to meet and rebut their accusation; so, leaving Isoude in an abbey, he pursued his way alone to Camelot.", "그러면서 자신들이 궁정으로 가서 아서 앞에 그를 고발하리라 짐작하게끔 만들었다. 마크로서는 그들과 맞서 그 고발에 반박할 필요가 있었으므로, 이졸데를 한 수도원에 남겨 둔 채 홀로 카멜롯을 향해 길을 갔다."]], [["Mark had not ridden far when he encountered a party of knights of Arthur’s court, and would have avoided them, for he knew their habit of challenging to a just every stranger knight whom they met.", "마크는 멀리 말을 달리기도 전에 아서 궁정의 기사 한 무리와 마주쳤고, 그들을 피하려 했다. 그들이 마주치는 모든 낯선 기사에게 마상 시합을 청하는 습관이 있음을 알고 있었기 때문이다."], ["But it was too late.", "그러나 이미 늦었다."], ["They had seen his armor, and recognized him as a Cornish knight, and at once resolved to have some sport with him.", "그들은 그의 갑옷을 보고 그가 콘월의 기사임을 알아차렸으며, 곧바로 그를 데리고 한바탕 놀아 보기로 작정했다."], ["It happened they had with them Daguenet, King Arthur’s fool, who, though deformed and weak of body, was not wanting in courage.", "마침 그들 가운데에는 아서 왕의 어릿광대 다그네가 있었는데, 그는 몸이 비틀어지고 허약했으나 용기만큼은 모자라지 않았다."], ["The knights as Mark approached laid their plan that Daguenet should personate Sir Launcelot of the Lake, and challenge the Cornish knight.", "마크가 다가오자 기사들은 다그네에게 호수의 랜슬롯 경 행세를 시켜 그 콘월 기사에게 도전하게 하기로 계획을 짰다."], ["They equipped him in armor belonging to one of their number who was ill, and sent him forward to the cross-road to defy the strange knight.", "그들은 일행 가운데 병이 난 한 사람의 갑옷을 다그네에게 입혀, 그 낯선 기사에게 결투를 청하도록 갈림길로 내보냈다."], ["Mark, who saw that his antagonist was by no means formidable in appearance, was not disinclined to the combat; but when the dwarf rode towards him, calling out that he was Sir Launcelot of the Lake, his fears prevailed, he put spurs to his horse, and rode away at full speed, pursued by the shouts and laughter of the party.", "마크는 자신의 상대가 겉모습으로는 결코 두려울 것 없는 자임을 보고 결투를 마다하지 않으려 했다. 그러나 그 난쟁이가 자신은 호수의 랜슬롯 경이라 외치며 말을 몰아 다가오자, 두려움이 그를 사로잡았고, 그는 말에 박차를 가해 일행의 함성과 웃음소리에 쫓기며 전속력으로 달아나 버렸다."]], [["Meanwhile Isoude, remaining at the abbey with her faithful Brengwain, found her only amusement in walking occasionally in a forest adjoining the abbey.", "한편 충직한 브렝귄과 함께 수도원에 머물던 이졸데는, 이따금 수도원에 잇닿은 숲속을 거니는 것에서만 유일한 위안을 찾았다."], ["There, on the brink of a fountain girdled with trees, she thought of her love, and sometimes joined her voice and her harp in lays reviving the memory of its pains or pleasures.", "거기, 나무들에 둘러싸인 샘가에서 그녀는 자신의 사랑을 생각하였고, 때로는 그 사랑의 고통이나 기쁨의 기억을 되살리는 노래에 자신의 목소리와 하프를 곁들였다."], ["One day the caitiff knight, Breuse the Pitiless, heard her voice, concealed himself, and drew near.", "어느 날 비열한 기사인 무자비한 브루즈가 그녀의 목소리를 듣고는 몸을 숨긴 채 가까이 다가왔다."], ["She sang:", "그녀는 이렇게 노래했다."]], [["“Sweet silence, shadowy bower, and verdant lair, Ye court my troubled spirit to repose, Whilst I, such dear remembrance rises there, Awaken every echo with my woes.", "“감미로운 고요여, 그늘진 정자여, 푸르른 안식처여, 그대들은 내 어지러운 마음을 쉬게 하라 손짓하건만, 나는, 그토록 애틋한 추억이 거기 솟아오르매, 온갖 메아리를 내 슬픔으로 일깨우는구나."], ["“Within these woods, by nature’s hand arrayed, A fountain springs, and feeds a thousand flowers; Ah!", "“자연의 손길이 단장한 이 숲속에, 샘 하나 솟아나 수천 송이 꽃을 기르나니, 아!"], ["how my groans do all its murmurs aid!", "내 신음이 어찌 그 모든 졸졸거림을 거드는가!"], ["How my sad eyes do swell it with their showers!", "내 슬픈 두 눈이 어찌 그 눈물의 빗줄기로 샘을 불리는가!"]], [["“What doth my knight the while?", "“그동안 나의 기사는 무엇을 하고 있을까?"], ["to him is given A double meed; in love and arms’ emprise, Him the Round Table elevates to heaven!", "그에게는 두 갑절의 보상이 주어졌으니, 사랑에서도 무예의 무훈에서도, 원탁이 그를 하늘로 드높이는구나!"], ["Tristram!", "트리스트람이여!"], ["ah me!", "아아!"], ["he hears not Isoude’s cries.”", "그는 이졸데의 외침을 듣지 못하네.”"]], [["Breuse the Pitiless, who like most other caitiffs had felt the weight of Tristram’s arm, and hated him accordingly, at hearing his name breathed forth by the beautiful songstress, impelled by a double impulse, rushed forth from his concealment and laid hands on his victim.", "무자비한 브루즈는, 여느 비열한 자들과 마찬가지로 트리스트람의 팔심을 겪어 본 적이 있어 그를 그만큼 미워하던 터라, 그 아름다운 가인(歌人)의 입에서 그 이름이 흘러나오는 것을 듣고 이중의 충동에 떠밀려 숨었던 곳에서 뛰쳐나와 제 먹잇감에게 손을 댔다."], ["Isoude fainted, and Brengwain filled the air with her shrieks.", "이졸데는 기절했고, 브렝귄은 비명으로 허공을 가득 채웠다."], ["Breuse carried Isoude to the place where he had left his horse; but the animal had got away from his bridle, and was at some distance.", "브루즈는 이졸데를 자기 말을 매어 둔 곳으로 데려갔다. 그러나 그 짐승은 고삐를 풀고 달아나 얼마간 떨어진 곳에 가 있었다."], ["He was obliged to lay down his fair burden, and go in pursuit of his horse.", "그는 어쩔 수 없이 그 아름다운 짐을 내려놓고 말을 뒤쫓아 가야 했다."], ["Just then a knight came up, drawn by the cries of Brengwain, and demanded the cause of her distress.", "바로 그때 브렝귄의 외침에 이끌린 한 기사가 다가와 그녀의 슬픔의 까닭을 물었다."], ["She could not speak, but pointed to her mistress lying insensible on the ground.", "그녀는 말을 하지 못한 채, 땅바닥에 정신을 잃고 누워 있는 자기 여주인을 가리킬 뿐이었다."]], [["Breuse had by this time returned, and the cries of Brengwain, renewed at seeing him, sufficiently showed the stranger the cause of the distress.", "그사이 브루즈는 돌아와 있었고, 그를 보고 다시 터져 나온 브렝귄의 외침은 그 낯선 이에게 슬픔의 까닭을 충분히 알려 주었다."], ["Tristram spurred his horse towards Breuse, who, not unprepared, ran to the encounter.", "트리스트람은 브루즈를 향해 말에 박차를 가했고, 브루즈 또한 대비하고 있던 터라 맞서 달려들었다."], ["Breuse was unhorsed, and lay motionless, pretending to be dead; but when the stranger knight left him to attend to the distressed damsels, he mounted his horse, and made his escape.", "브루즈는 말에서 떨어져 죽은 척하며 꼼짝 않고 누워 있었다. 그러나 낯선 기사가 그를 내버려 두고 슬픔에 잠긴 여인들을 돌보러 가자, 그는 제 말에 올라타 달아나 버렸다."]], [["The knight now approached Isoude, gently raised her head, drew aside the golden hair which covered her countenance, gazed thereon for an instant, uttered a cry, and fell back insensible.", "이제 그 기사는 이졸데에게 다가가 그녀의 머리를 부드럽게 받쳐 들고, 얼굴을 덮은 금빛 머리카락을 옆으로 쓸어내린 뒤, 한순간 그것을 바라보다가 외마디 소리를 지르며 정신을 잃고 뒤로 쓰러졌다."], ["Brengwain came; her cares soon restored her mistress to life, and they then turned their attention to the fallen warrior.", "브렝귄이 다가왔고, 그녀의 보살핌으로 여주인은 곧 생기를 되찾았다. 그러고는 두 사람이 쓰러진 용사에게로 관심을 돌렸다."], ["They raised his visor, and discovered the countenance of Sir Tristram.", "그들이 그의 투구 가리개를 들어 올리자, 트리스트람 경의 얼굴이 드러났다."], ["Isoude threw herself on the body of her lover, and bedewed his face with her tears.", "이졸데는 연인의 몸 위에 자신을 던져 그의 얼굴을 눈물로 적셨다."], ["Their warmth revived the knight, and Tristram on awaking found himself in the arms of his dear Isoude.", "그 따스함이 기사를 되살아나게 했고, 트리스트람은 깨어나 보니 자신이 사랑하는 이졸데의 품 안에 있었다."]], [["It was the law of the Round Table that each knight after his admission should pass the next ten days in quest of adventures, during which time his companions might meet him in disguised armor and try their strength with him.", "원탁의 법도란, 각 기사가 입단한 뒤 이후 열흘 동안 모험을 찾아 떠나야 한다는 것이었으며, 그동안 동료들이 변장한 갑옷 차림으로 그와 마주쳐 무예를 겨루어 볼 수 있었다."], ["Tristram had now been out seven days, and in that time had encountered many of the best knights of the Round Table, and acquitted himself with honor.", "트리스트람은 이제 이레째 밖에 나와 있었으니, 그동안 원탁의 가장 빼어난 기사들 여럿과 맞붙어 명예롭게 처신해 냈다."], ["During the remaining three days, Isoude remained at the abbey, under his protection, and then set out with her maidens, escorted by Sir Tristram, to rejoin King Mark at the court of Camelot.", "남은 사흘 동안 이졸데는 그의 보호 아래 수도원에 머물렀고, 그런 다음 트리스트람 경의 호위를 받으며 시녀들과 함께 카멜롯 궁정에 있는 마크 왕에게 돌아가고자 길을 떠났다."]], [["This happy journey was one of the brightest epochs in the lives of Tristram and Isoude.", "이 행복한 여정은 트리스트람과 이졸데의 생애에서 가장 빛나는 시절 가운데 하나였다."], ["He celebrated it by a lay upon the harp in a peculiar measure, to which the French give the name of Triolet.", "그는 프랑스인들이 〈트리올레〉라 이름 붙인 독특한 운율에 맞추어 하프로 노래를 지어 이를 기렸다."]], [["“With fair Isoude, and with love, Ah!", "“아름다운 이졸데와 더불어, 그리고 사랑과 더불어, 아!"], ["how sweet the life I lead!", "내가 누리는 삶은 얼마나 감미로운가!"], ["How blest for ever thus to rove, With fair Isoude, and with love!", "이렇듯 영원토록 떠도는 것은 얼마나 복된가, 아름다운 이졸데와 더불어, 그리고 사랑과 더불어!"], ["As she wills, I live and move, And cloudless days to days succeed: With fair Isoude, and with love, Ah!", "그녀가 원하는 대로 나는 살고 또 움직이니, 구름 한 점 없는 날들이 날들에 잇따르는구나. 아름다운 이졸데와 더불어, 그리고 사랑과 더불어, 아!"], ["how sweet the life I lead!", "내가 누리는 삶은 얼마나 감미로운가!"]], [["“Journeying on from break of day, Feel you not fatigued, my fair?", "“동틀 녘부터 길을 떠나 나아가는데, 그대는 지치지 않았는가, 내 어여쁜 이여?"], ["Yon green turf invites to play; Journeying on from day to day, Ah!", "저 푸른 풀밭이 노닐자 손짓하는구나. 날마다 길을 떠나 나아가는 동안, 아!"], ["let us to that shade away, Were it but to slumber there!", "저 그늘로 떠나가자꾸나, 설령 거기서 잠시 잠들기 위함일지라도!"], ["Journeying on from break of day, Feel you not fatigued, my fair?”", "동틀 녘부터 길을 떠나 나아가는데, 그대는 지치지 않았는가, 내 어여쁜 이여?”"]], [["They arrived at Camelot, where Sir Launcelot received them most cordially.", "그들은 카멜롯에 다다랐고, 랜슬롯 경이 더없이 정성스레 그들을 맞이했다."], ["Isoude was introduced to King Arthur and Queen Guenever, who welcomed her as a sister.", "이졸데는 아서 왕과 귀네비어 왕비에게 소개되었으며, 두 사람은 그녀를 자매처럼 환대했다."], ["As King Mark was held in arrest under the accusation of the two Cornish knights, Queen Isoude could not rejoin her husband, and Sir Launcelot placed his castle of La Joyeuse Garde at the disposal of his friends, who there took up their abode.", "마크 왕이 두 콘월 기사의 고발로 인해 구금되어 있었으므로, 이졸데 왕비는 남편에게 돌아갈 수 없었다. 랜슬롯 경은 자신의 성 라 주아유즈 가르드를 벗들이 마음껏 쓰도록 내어 주었고, 그들은 거기에 거처를 정했다."]], [["King Mark, who found himself obliged to confess the truth of the charge against him, or to clear himself by combat with his accusers, preferred the former, and King Arthur, as his crime had not been perpetrated, remitted the penalty, only enjoining upon him, under pain of his signal displeasure, to lay aside all thoughts of vengeance against his nephew.", "마크 왕은 자신을 향한 고발이 사실임을 자백하거나, 아니면 고발자들과의 결투로 결백을 입증해야 하는 처지에 놓이자 전자를 택했다. 아서 왕은 그 죄가 실제로 저질러지지는 않았으므로 처벌을 면해 주되, 다만 자신의 크나큰 분노를 사지 않으려거든 조카에 대한 복수의 생각을 모두 버리라고 그에게 엄히 명했다."], ["In the presence of the king and his court all parties were formally reconciled; Mark and his queen departed for their home, and Tristram remained at Arthur’s court.", "왕과 그 궁정 앞에서 모든 당사자들은 격식을 갖추어 화해했다. 마크와 그의 왕비는 고향으로 떠났고, 트리스트람은 아서의 궁정에 남았다."]], [["————", "————"]], [["# SIR PALAMEDES", "# SIR PALAMEDES"]], [["WHILE Sir Tristram and the fair Isoude abode yet at La Joyeuse Garde, Sir Tristram rode forth one day, without armor, having no weapon but his spear and his sword.", "트리스트람 경과 어여쁜 이주[이주드]가 아직 라 주아외즈 가르드에 머물고 있을 무렵, 어느 날 트리스트람 경은 갑옷을 걸치지 않고 창과 검 외에는 아무 무기도 지니지 않은 채 말을 타고 길을 나섰다."], ["And as he rode he came to a place where he saw two knights in battle, and one of them had gotten the better and the other lay overthrown.", "그렇게 말을 달리다가 그는 두 기사가 싸우고 있는 곳에 이르렀는데, 한 사람은 우세를 점했고 다른 한 사람은 쓰러져 누워 있었다. 우세한 쪽 기사는 팔라메데스 경이었다."], ["The knight who had the better was Sir Palamedes.", "팔라메데스 경은 트리스트람 경을 알아보고는 외쳤다."], ["When Sir Palamedes knew Sir Tristram, he cried out, “Sir Tristram, now we be met, and ere we depart we will redress our old wrongs.”", "\"트리스트람 경, 이제 우리가 만났으니, 헤어지기 전에 우리의 묵은 원한을 풀어 보세.\" \"그 일이라면,\""], ["“As for that,”", "트리스트람 경이 말했다."], ["said Sir Tristram, “there never yet was Christian man that might make his boast that I ever fled from him, and thou that art a Saracen shalt never say that of me.”", "\"지금껏 그리스도교도 가운데 내가 자기에게서 달아났노라고 자랑할 수 있었던 자는 없었거니와, 사라센인인 그대 또한 나에 대해 결코 그런 말을 하지 못할 것이오.\" 그러고는 트리스트람 경이 말을 달리게 하여 온 힘을 다해 곧장 팔라메데스 경에게 달려들어 그를 향해 창을 부러뜨렸다."], ["And therewith Sir Tristram made his horse to run, and with all his might came straight upon Sir Palamedes, and broke his spear upon him.", "그런 다음 그는 검을 빼어 팔라메데스 경의 투구를 향해 여섯 차례 큰 일격을 가했다. 팔라메데스 경은 트리스트람 경이 갑옷을 걸치지 않았음을 보고 그의 무모함과 크나큰 어리석음에 놀라며 속으로 이렇게 말했다."], ["Then he drew his sword and struck at Sir Palamedes six great strokes, upon his helm.", "\"내가 그와 맞붙어 그를 죽인다면, 나는 어디를 가든 수치를 면치 못하리라.\""], ["Sir Palamedes saw that Sir Tristram had not his armor on, and he marvelled at his rashness and his great folly; and said to himself, “If I meet and slay him, I am shamed wheresoever I go.”", "그러자 트리스트람 경이 소리쳐 말했다. \"이 비겁한 기사여, 어찌하여 나와 싸우려 하지 않는가?"], ["Then Sir Tristram cried out and said, “Thou coward knight, why wilt thou not do battle with me?", "그대의 온갖 악의를 내가 모조리 견뎌 내리라는 것을 의심하지 마시오.\" \"아, 트리스트람 경!\""], ["for have thou no doubt I shall endure all thy malice.”", "팔라메데스 경이 말했다. \"수치심 때문에 내가 그대와 싸울 수 없음을 그대도 알지 않소."], ["“Ah, Sir Tristram!”", "그대는 지금 맨몸이고 나는 무장을 했으니 말이오."], ["said Sir Palamedes, “thou knowest I may not fight with thee for shame; for thou art here naked, and I am armed; now I require that thou answer me a question that I shall ask you.”", "그러니 내가 그대에게 한 가지 묻는 물음에 답해 주기를 청하오.\" \"그것이 무엇인지 말해 보시오.\""], ["“Tell me what it is,”", "트리스트람 경이 말했다."], ["said Sir Tristram.", "\"이렇게 가정해 봅시다.\""], ["“I put the case,”", "팔라메데스가 말했다."], ["said Palamedes, “that you were well armed, and I naked as ye be; what would you do to me now, by your true knighthood?”", "\"그대는 온전히 무장을 했고, 나는 지금 그대처럼 맨몸이라고 합시다. 그렇다면 그대는 진정한 기사도에 비추어 이제 내게 어떻게 하겠소?\""], ["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["said Sir Tristram, “now I understand thee well, Sir Palamedes; and, as God bless me, what I shall say shall not be said for fear that I have of thee.", "트리스트람 경이 말했다. \"이제 그대의 뜻을 잘 알겠소, 팔라메데스 경."], ["But if it were so, thou shouldest depart from me, for I would not have to do with thee.”", "그리고 하느님께 맹세코, 내가 하려는 말은 그대에 대한 두려움 때문에 하는 말이 아니오. 만일 그러한 처지라면 그대는 내게서 떠나가야 할 것이오."], ["“No more will I with thee,”", "나는 그대와 싸움을 벌이지 않을 것이기 때문이오.\" \"나 또한 그대와 싸우지 않으리다.\""], ["said Sir Palamedes, “and therefore ride forth on thy way.”", "팔라메데스 경이 말했다. \"그러니 그대의 길로 말을 달려 가시오.\""], ["“As for that, I may choose,”", "\"그 일이라면 내가 택할 수 있는 바요.\""], ["said Sir Tristram, “either to ride or to abide.", "트리스트람 경이 말했다. \"떠나든 머무르든 말이오."], ["But, Sir Palamedes, I marvel at one thing,—that thou art so good a knight, yet that thou wilt not be christened.”", "하지만 팔라메데스 경, 나는 한 가지 일에 놀라움을 금치 못하오. 그대는 그토록 훌륭한 기사이면서도 세례를 받으려 하지 않는다는 점이오.\""], ["“As for that,”", "\"그 일이라면,\" 팔라메데스 경이 말했다."], ["said Sir Palamedes, “I may not yet be christened, for a vow which I made many years ago; yet in my heart I believe in our Saviour and his mild mother, Mary; but I have yet one battle to do, and when that is done I will be christened, with a good will.”", "\"나는 아직 세례를 받을 수 없소. 여러 해 전에 맺은 한 가지 서원 때문이오."], ["“By my head,”", "하지만 내 마음속으로는 우리의 구세주와 그분의 온화하신 어머니 마리아를 믿고 있소."], ["said Sir Tristram, “as for that one battle, thou shalt seek it no longer; for yonder is a knight, whom you have smitten down.", "다만 나는 아직 치러야 할 싸움이 한 가지 남아 있으니, 그것을 끝내고 나면 기꺼운 마음으로 세례를 받겠소.\" \"내 머리를 걸고 말하건대,\""], ["Now help me to be clothed in his armor, and I will soon fulfil thy vow.”", "트리스트람 경이 말했다. \"그 한 가지 싸움이라면 더 이상 찾아 헤맬 필요가 없소."], ["“As ye will,”", "저기 그대가 쓰러뜨린 기사가 있지 않소."], ["said Sir Palamedes, “so shall it be.”", "자, 그의 갑옷을 입도록 나를 도와주시오. 내가 곧 그대의 서원을 이루어 주리다.\""], ["So they rode both unto that knight that sat on a bank; and Sir Tristram saluted him, and he full weary saluted him again.", "\"그대의 뜻대로,\" 팔라메데스 경이 말했다."], ["“Sir,”", "\"그리하리다.\""], ["said Sir Tristram, “I pray you to lend me your whole armor; for I am unarmed, and I must do battle with this knight.”", "그리하여 두 사람은 둑 위에 앉아 있는 그 기사에게 함께 말을 타고 갔다. 트리스트람 경이 그에게 인사를 건네니, 잔뜩 지친 그가 다시 인사로 답했다."], ["“Sir,”", "\"기사여,\""], ["said the hurt knight, “you shall have it, with a right good will.”", "트리스트람 경이 말했다. \"청컨대 그대의 갑옷 일체를 내게 빌려 주시오."], ["Then Sir Tristram unarmed Sir Galleron, for that was the name of the hurt knight, and he as well as he could helped to arm Sir Tristram.", "나는 무장을 갖추지 못했는데, 이 기사와 싸움을 벌여야 하기 때문이오.\" \"기사여,\""], ["Then Sir Tristram mounted upon his own horse, and in his hand he took Sir Galleron’s spear.", "부상당한 기사가 말했다. \"기꺼운 마음으로 그것을 드리리다.\""], ["Thereupon Sir Palamedes was ready, and so they came hurling together, and each smote the other in the midst of their shields.", "그리하여 트리스트람 경은 그 부상당한 기사의 갑옷을 벗겼는데, 그 기사의 이름은 갈레론 경이었으며, 갈레론 경은 할 수 있는 한 힘껏 트리스트람 경의 무장을 도왔다. 그러고 나서 트리스트람 경은 자신의 말에 올라타 손에 갈레론 경의 창을 쥐었다."], ["Sir Palamedes’ spear broke, and Sir Tristram smote down the horse.", "이에 팔라메데스 경도 채비를 갖추었고, 그리하여 두 사람은 서로를 향해 맹렬히 달려들어 각자 상대의 방패 한가운데를 내리쳤다."], ["Then Sir Palamedes leapt from his horse, and drew out his sword.", "팔라메데스 경의 창은 부러졌고, 트리스트람 경은 말을 쓰러뜨렸다. 그러자 팔라메데스 경은 말에서 뛰어내려 검을 빼어 들었다."], ["That saw Sir Tristram, and therewith he alighted and tied his horse to a tree.", "트리스트람 경이 그것을 보고는 자신도 말에서 내려 말을 나무에 매어 놓았다. 그런 다음 두 사람은 두 마리 들짐승처럼 맞붙어 서로를 후려쳤으며, 그렇게 두 시간이 넘도록 싸웠다."], ["Then they came together as two wild beasts, lashing the one on the other, and so fought more than two hours; and often Sir Tristram smote such strokes at Sir Palamedes that he made him to kneel, and Sir Palamedes broke away Sir Tristram’s shield, and wounded him.", "트리스트람 경은 자주 팔라메데스 경에게 강한 일격을 가하여 그를 무릎 꿇게 했고, 팔라메데스 경은 트리스트람 경의 방패를 깨뜨리고 그에게 상처를 입혔다. 그러자 트리스트람 경은 가늠할 수 없을 만큼 노하여 팔라메데스 경에게 돌진해 어깨를 꿰뚫어 몹시 심한 상처를 입혔고, 운 좋게도 팔라메데스 경의 검을 그의 손에서 쳐내었다."], ["Then Sir Tristram was wroth out of measure, and he rushed to Sir Palamedes and wounded him passing sore through the shoulder, and by fortune smote Sir Palamedes’ sword out of his hand.", "만일 팔라메데스 경이 검을 주우려고 몸을 숙였다면 트리스트람 경이 그를 죽였을 것이다. 그러자 팔라메데스 경은 서서 비통하기 그지없는 마음으로 자신의 검을 바라보았다."], ["And if Sir Palamedes had stooped for his sword Sir Tristram had slain him.", "\"자,\" 트리스트람 경이 말했다."], ["Then Sir Palamedes stood and beheld his sword with a full sorrowful heart. “Now,”", "\"오늘 그대가 나를 유리한 처지에 두었듯, 이제 나는 그대를 유리한 처지에 두게 되었소."], ["said Sir Tristram, “I have thee at a vantage, as thou hadst me to-day; but it shall never be said, in court, or among good knights, that Sir Tristram did slay any knight that was weaponless; therefore take thou thy sword, and let us fight this battle to the end.”", "하지만 트리스트람 경이 무기 없는 기사를 죽였노라는 말은 궁정에서든 훌륭한 기사들 사이에서든 결코 나오지 않을 것이오. 그러니 그대의 검을 집어 들고, 이 싸움을 끝까지 겨뤄 봅시다.\""], ["Then spoke Sir Palamedes to Sir Tristram: “I have no wish to fight this battle any more.", "그러자 팔라메데스 경이 트리스트람 경에게 말했다. \"나는 이 싸움을 더 이상 하고 싶지 않소."], ["The offence that I have done unto you is not so great but that, if it please you, we may be friends.", "내가 그대에게 저지른 잘못은 그다지 크지 않으니, 그대가 원한다면 우리는 벗이 될 수도 있소. 내가 잘못을 저지른 까닭은 모두 왕비 라 벨 이주[이주드]를 향한 사랑 때문이며, 나는 그분이 여인들 가운데 견줄 데 없는 분임을 감히 주장하는 바요."], ["All that I have offended is for the love of the queen, La Belle Isoude, and I dare maintain that she is peerless among ladies; and for that offence ye have given me many grievous and sad strokes, and some I have given you again.", "그 잘못으로 인해 그대는 내게 무겁고 비통한 일격을 여러 차례 가했고, 나 또한 그대에게 몇 차례 되돌려 주었소. 그러니 청컨대, 나의 주군 트리스트람 경이여, 내가 그대에게 저지른 모든 잘못을 용서해 주시고, 오늘 나를 가장 가까운 교회로 데려가 주시오."], ["Wherefore I require you, my lord Sir Tristram, forgive me all that I have offended you, and this day have me unto the next church; and first I will be clean confessed, and after that see you that I be truly baptized, and then we will ride together unto the court of my lord, King Arthur, so that we may be there at the feast of Pentecost.”", "먼저 나는 깨끗이 고해를 하고, 그런 다음 그대가 내가 참되게 세례 받는 것을 지켜봐 주시오. 그러고 나면 우리 함께 나의 주군이신 아서 왕의 궁정으로 말을 달려, 오순절 축연에 맞춰 그곳에 다다를 수 있을 것이오.\""], ["“Now take your horse,”", "\"이제 그대의 말에 오르시오.\""], ["said Sir Tristram, “and as you have said, so shall it be done.”", "트리스트람 경이 말했다. \"그대가 말한 대로 그리될 것이오.\""], ["So they took their horses, and Sir Galleron rode with them.", "그리하여 두 사람은 말에 올랐고, 갈레론 경도 그들과 함께 말을 달렸다."], ["When they came to the church of Carlisle, the bishop commanded to fill a great vessel with water; and when he had hallowed it, he then confessed Sir Palamedes clean, and christened him, and Sir Tristram and Sir Galleron were his godfathers.", "그들이 칼라일의 교회에 이르자, 주교는 커다란 그릇에 물을 가득 채우라 명했다. 그리고 그 물을 축성한 다음, 그는 팔라메데스 경에게서 깨끗이 고해를 받고 그에게 세례를 베풀었으며, 트리스트람 경과 갈레론 경이 그의 대부가 되어 주었다."], ["Then soon after they departed, and rode towards Camelot, where the noble King Arthur and Queen Guenever were keeping a court royal.", "그러고 나서 얼마 지나지 않아 그들은 그곳을 떠나 카멜롯을 향해 말을 달렸는데, 그곳에서는 고귀한 아서 왕과 왕비 귀네비어가 왕실 궁정을 열고 있었다."], ["And the king and all the court were glad that Sir Palamedes was christened.", "왕과 온 궁정은 팔라메데스 경이 세례를 받은 것을 기뻐했다."], ["Then Sir Tristram returned again to La Joyeuse Garde, and Sir Palamedes went his way.", "그런 다음 트리스트람 경은 다시 라 주아외즈 가르드로 돌아갔고, 팔라메데스 경은 자신의 길을 떠났다."]], [["Not long after these events Sir Gawain returned from Brittany, and related to King Arthur the adventure which befell him in the forest of Breciliande, how Merlin had there spoken to him, and enjoined him to charge the king to go without delay upon the quest of the Holy Greal.", "이러한 일들이 있은 지 오래지 않아 가웨인 경이 브르타뉴에서 돌아와, 브레실리앙드 숲에서 그에게 일어난 모험을 아서 왕에게 이야기했다. 그곳에서 멀린이 그에게 말을 건넸으며, 왕에게 지체 없이 성배를 찾는 탐색에 나서도록 명하라고 당부했다는 것이었다."], ["While King Arthur deliberated Tristram determined to enter upon the quest, and the more readily, as it was well known to him that this holy adventure would, if achieved, procure him the pardon of all his sins.", "아서 왕이 숙고하는 동안 트리스트람 경은 그 탐색에 나서기로 마음을 정했는데, 이 거룩한 모험을 이루어 낸다면 자신의 모든 죄를 사면받게 되리라는 것을 잘 알고 있었기에 더욱 기꺼이 나서고자 했다."], ["He immediately departed for the kingdom of Brittany, hoping there to obtain from Merlin counsel as to the proper course to pursue to insure success.", "그는 즉시 브르타뉴 왕국을 향해 떠났으니, 그곳에서 멀린으로부터 성공을 확실히 하기 위해 따라야 할 올바른 방도에 관한 조언을 얻기를 바랐던 것이다."]], [["————", "————"]], [["# SIR TRISTRAM", "# SIR TRISTRAM"]], [["ON arriving in Brittany Tristram found King Hoel engaged in a war with a rebellious vassal, and hard pressed by his enemy.", "브르타뉴에 도착한 트리스트람은 호엘 왕이 반란을 일으킨 봉신과 전쟁을 벌이고 있으며, 적에게 심하게 몰리고 있음을 알게 되었다."], ["His best knights had fallen in a late battle, and he knew not where to turn for assistance.", "그의 가장 뛰어난 기사들은 얼마 전 전투에서 쓰러졌고, 그는 어디서 도움을 구해야 할지 알지 못했다."], ["Tristram volunteered his aid.", "트리스트람은 자진하여 도움을 자청했다."], ["It was accepted; and the army of Hoel, led by Tristram, and inspired by his example, gained a complete victory.", "그 제안은 받아들여졌고, 트리스트람이 이끌고 그의 본보기에 고무된 호엘의 군대는 완전한 승리를 거두었다."], ["The king, penetrated by the most lively sentiments of gratitude, and having informed himself of Tristram’s birth, offered him his daughter in marriage.", "가장 생생한 감사의 감정에 사무친 왕은 트리스트람의 출생을 알아본 뒤, 그에게 자기 딸을 아내로 주겠다고 제안했다."], ["The princess was beautiful and accomplished, and bore the same name with the Queen of Cornwall; but this one is designated by the Romancers as Isoude of the White Hands, to distinguish her from Isoude the Fair.", "공주는 아름답고 재주가 뛰어났으며, 콘월의 왕비와 같은 이름을 지니고 있었다. 그러나 이쪽은 로맨스 작가들에 의해 〈하얀 손의 이소우데〉라 불려, 〈아름다운 이소우데〉와 구별되었다."]], [["How can we describe the conflict that agitated the heart of Tristram?", "트리스트람의 마음을 뒤흔든 갈등을 우리가 어찌 묘사할 수 있겠는가?"], ["He adored the first Isoude, but his love for her was hopeless, and not unaccompanied by remorse.", "그는 첫 번째 이소우데를 흠모했지만, 그녀를 향한 그의 사랑은 가망 없는 것이었고, 회한이 따르지 않는 것도 아니었다."], ["Moreover, the sacred quest on which he had now entered demanded of him perfect purity of life.", "게다가 그가 이제 막 들어선 신성한 탐색은 그에게 완벽하게 순결한 삶을 요구했다."], ["It seemed as if a happy destiny had provided for him in the charming princess Isoude of the White Hands the best security for all his good resolutions.", "행복한 운명이 매혹적인 공주 〈하얀 손의 이소우데〉를 통해 그의 모든 선한 결심을 지킬 가장 든든한 보호막을 마련해 준 것처럼 보였다."], ["This last reflection determined him.", "이 마지막 생각이 그를 결심하게 했다."], ["They were married, and passed some months in tranquil happiness at the court of King Hoel.", "두 사람은 결혼했고, 호엘 왕의 궁정에서 몇 달을 평온한 행복 속에 보냈다."], ["The pleasure which Tristram felt in his wife’s society increased day by day.", "아내와 함께 있으며 트리스트람이 느끼는 기쁨은 날마다 커져 갔다."], ["An inward grace seemed to stir within him from the moment when he took the oath to go on the quest of the Holy Greal; it seemed even to triumph over the power of the magic love-potion.", "그가 성배를 찾아 떠나겠다고 맹세한 순간부터 어떤 내적인 은총이 그의 안에서 움직이는 듯했다. 그것은 마법의 사랑의 묘약이 지닌 힘마저 이겨 내는 것처럼 보였다."]], [["The war, which had been quelled for a time, now burst out anew.", "한동안 진압되었던 전쟁이 이제 다시 터져 나왔다."], ["Tristram as usual was foremost in every danger.", "트리스트람은 늘 그렇듯 모든 위험의 선봉에 섰다."], ["The enemy was worsted in successive conflicts, and at last shut himself up in his principal city.", "적은 잇따른 싸움에서 패했고, 마침내 자신의 본거지 도시에 틀어박혔다."], ["Tristram led on the attack of the city.", "트리스트람은 그 도시에 대한 공격을 이끌었다."], ["As he mounted a ladder to scale the walls he was struck on the head by a fragment of rock, which the besieged threw down upon him.", "그가 성벽을 기어오르려고 사다리에 올랐을 때, 포위당한 자들이 그에게 던진 바위 조각이 머리를 쳤다."], ["It bore him to the ground, where he lay insensible.", "그것은 그를 땅으로 쓰러뜨렸고, 그는 의식을 잃은 채 누워 있었다."]], [["As soon as he recovered consciousness he demanded to be carried to his wife.", "의식을 회복하자마자 그는 아내에게로 옮겨 달라고 요청했다."], ["The princess, skilled in the art of surgery, would not suffer any one but herself to touch her beloved husband.", "외과술에 능한 공주는 자신을 제외한 그 누구도 사랑하는 남편에게 손대는 것을 허락하지 않으려 했다."], ["Her fair hands bound up his wounds; Tristram kissed them with gratitude, which began to grow into love.", "그녀의 고운 두 손이 그의 상처를 싸맸고, 트리스트람은 감사의 마음으로 그 손에 입을 맞추었으며, 그 감사는 사랑으로 자라나기 시작했다."], ["At first the devoted cares of Isoude seemed to meet with great success; but after a while these flattering appearances vanished, and, in spite of all her care, the malady grew more serious day by day.", "처음에는 이소우데의 헌신적인 보살핌이 큰 성공을 거두는 듯했다. 그러나 얼마 지나지 않아 이 그럴듯한 조짐은 사라졌고, 그녀의 온갖 정성에도 불구하고 병세는 날이 갈수록 심각해져 갔다."]], [["In this perplexity, an old squire of Tristram’s reminded his master that the princess of Ireland, afterwards queen of Cornwall, had once cured him under circumstances quite as discouraging.", "이런 곤혹스러운 상황에서, 트리스트람의 늙은 시종 하나가 주인에게 일깨워 주기를, 훗날 콘월의 왕비가 된 아일랜드의 공주가 지금만큼이나 절망적인 상황에서 한때 그를 낫게 한 적이 있다고 했다."], ["He called Isoude of the White Hands to him, told her of his former cure, added that he believed that the Queen Isoude could heal him, and that he felt sure that she would come to his relief, if sent for.", "그는 〈하얀 손의 이소우데〉를 불러 자신이 예전에 치유받았던 일을 들려주고, 이소우데 왕비라면 자신을 낫게 할 수 있으리라 믿으며, 사람을 보내면 그녀가 자신을 구하러 와 줄 것이 틀림없다고 덧붙였다."]], [["Isoude of the White Hands consented that Gesnes, a trusty man and skilful navigator, should be sent to Cornwall.", "〈하얀 손의 이소우데〉는 믿음직하고 항해에 능숙한 사람인 게스네스를 콘월로 보내는 데 동의했다."], ["Tristram called him, and, giving him a ring, “Take this,”", "트리스트람은 그를 불러 반지를 건네며 이렇게 말했다."], ["he said, “to the Queen of Cornwall.", "\"이것을 콘월의 왕비에게 가져가게."], ["Tell her that Tristram, near to death, demands her aid.", "죽음에 가까운 트리스트람이 그녀의 도움을 청한다고 전하게."], ["If you succeed in bringing her with you, place white sails to your vessel on your return, that we may know of your success when the vessel first heaves in sight.", "그녀를 데려오는 데 성공하거든, 돌아올 때 배에 흰 돛을 달게. 그러면 배가 처음 시야에 들어올 때 우리가 자네의 성공을 알 수 있도록 말이네."], ["But if Queen Isoude refuses, put on black sails; they will be the presage of my impending death.”", "그러나 만약 이소우데 왕비가 거절하거든 검은 돛을 달게. 그것은 다가오는 내 죽음의 전조가 될 걸세.\""]], [["Gesnes performed his mission successfully. King Mark happened to be absent from his capital, and the queen readily consented to return with the bark to Brittany. Gesnes clothed his vessel in the whitest of sails, and sped his way back to Brittany.", "ii., Sir Calidore encounters in the forest a young hunter, whom he"]], [["Meantime the wound of Tristram grew more desperate day by day. His strength, quite prostrated, no longer permitted him to be carried to the seaside daily, as had been his custom from the first moment when it was possible for the bark to be on the way homeward. He called a young damsel, and gave her in charge to keep watch in the direction of Cornwall, and to come and tell him the color of the sails of the first vessel she should see approaching. When Isoude of the White Hands consented that the queen of Cornwall should be sent for, she had not known all the reasons which she had for fearing the influence which renewed intercourse with that princess might have on her own happiness. She had now learned more, and felt the danger more keenly. She thought, if she could only keep the knowledge of the queen’s arrival from her husband, she might employ in his service any resources which her skill could supply, and still avert the dangers which she apprehended. When the vessel was seen approaching, with its white sails sparkling in the sun, the damsel, by command of her mistress, carried word to Tristram that the sails were black.", "thus describes:"]], [["Tristram, penetrated with inexpressible grief, breathed a profound sigh, turned away his face, and said, “Alas, my beloved! we shall never see one another again!” Then he commended himself to God, and breathed his last. The death of Tristram was the first intelligence which the queen of Cornwall heard on landing. She was conducted almost senseless into the chamber of Tristram, and expired holding him in her arms.", "“Him steadfastly he marked, and saw to be"]], [["Tristram, before his death, had requested that his body should be sent to Cornwall, and that his sword, with a letter he had written, should be delivered to King Mark. The remains of Tristram and Isoude were embarked in a vessel, along with the sword, which was presented to the king of Cornwall.", "A goodly youth of amiable grace, Yet but a slender slip, that scarce did see Yet seventeen yeares, but tall and faire of face, That sure he deem’d him to be of noble race."], ["He was melted with tenderness when he saw the weapon which slew Moraunt of Ireland,—which had so often saved his life, and redeemed the honor of his kingdom. In the letter Tristram begged pardon of his uncle, and related the story of the amorous draught.", "All in a woodman’s jacket he was clad Of Lincoln greene, belayd with silver lace; And on his head an hood with aglets sprad, And by his side his hunter’s horne he hanging had."]], [["Mark ordered the lovers to be buried in his own chapel. From the tomb of Tristram there sprung a vine, which went along the walls, and descended into the grave of the queen. It was cut down three times, but each time sprung up again more vigorous than before, and this wonderful plant has ever since shaded the tombs of Tristram and Isoude.", "“Buskins he wore of costliest cordwayne,"]], [["————\nSpenser introduces Sir Tristram in his “Faery Queene.” In Book VI.,", "Pinckt upon gold, and paled part per part, As then the guize was for each gentle swayne; In his right hand he held a trembling dart, Whose fellow he before had sent apart; And in his left he held a sharp bore-speare, With which he wont to launch the salvage hart Of many a lyon, and of many a beare, That first unto his hand in chase did happen neare.”"]], [["Canto ii., Sir Calidore encounters in the forest a young hunter, whom he thus describes:", "제2편에서 칼리도어 경[Sir Calidore]은 숲에서 젊은 사냥꾼 하나를 만나는데, 그를 이렇게 묘사한다."]], [["“Him steadfastly he marked, and saw to be A goodly youth of amiable grace, Yet but a slender slip, that scarce did see Yet seventeen yeares; but tall and faire of face, That sure he deemed him borne of noble race.", "“그는 그 젊은이를 가만히 눈여겨보았으니, 사랑스럽고 우아한 한 어여쁜 청년이었다. 허나 아직은 가냘픈 가지에 지나지 않아, 열일곱 해도 채 넘기지 못한 듯하였으되, 키 크고 얼굴 고와 분명 귀한 혈통이라 여겼다."], ["All in a woodman’s jacket he was clad Of Lincoln greene, belayed with silver lace; And on his head an hood with aglets[52] sprad, And by his side his hunter’s horne he hanging had.", "온몸에는 사냥꾼의 웃옷을 걸쳤으니, 링컨산 초록 빛깔에 은빛 끈을 둘렀고, 머리에는 술 장식 흩뿌린 두건을 썼으며,[52] 그 곁에는 사냥꾼의 뿔피리를 매달고 있었다."]], [["“Buskins he wore of costliest cordawayne, Pinckt upon gold, and paled part per part,[53] As then the guize was for each gentle swayne.", "“가장 값진 코르도바 가죽으로 지은 장화를 신었으니, 황금 위에 무늬 박고 조각조각 줄지어 둘렀는데,[53] 그때는 점잖은 젊은이라면 다들 그런 차림이었다."], ["In his right hand he held a trembling dart, Whose fellow he before had sent apart; And in his left he held a sharp bore-speare, With which he wont to launch the salvage heart Of many a lyon, and of many a beare, That first unto his hand in chase did happen neare.”", "오른손에는 떨리는 투창 하나를 들고 있었으니, 그 짝은 이미 앞서서 던져 보냈음이라, 왼손에는 날카로운 멧돼지 창을 쥐었으니, 그 창으로 그는 거친 심장을 찌르곤 하였다. 뒤쫓는 길에 그의 손길 가까이 처음으로 다가온, 숱한 사자와 숱한 곰의 심장을 말이다.”"]], [["————", "————"]], [["THE father and two elder brothers of Perceval had fallen in battle or tournaments, and hence, as the last hope of his family, his mother retired with him into a solitary region, where he was brought up in total ignorance of arms and chivalry.", "페르세발의 아버지와 두 형은 전투나 마상 시합에서 목숨을 잃었고, 그리하여 그의 어머니는 가문의 마지막 희망인 그를 데리고 외딴 고장으로 물러났으며, 그곳에서 그는 무기와 기사도에 관해 전혀 알지 못한 채 자라났다. 그에게는 \"작은 스코틀랜드 창\""], ["He was allowed no weapon but “a lyttel Scots spere,” which was the only thing of all “her lordes faire gere”", "외에는 어떤 무기도 허락되지 않았는데, 그것이야말로 그의 어머니가 \"남편의 훌륭한 장비\""], ["that his mother carried to the wood with her.", "중에서 유일하게 숲으로 가지고 들어온 물건이었다."], ["In the use of this he became so skilful, that he could kill with it not only the animals of the chase for the table, but even birds on the wing.", "페르세발은 이 창을 다루는 데 무척이나 능숙해져서, 그것으로 식탁에 올릴 사냥감을 잡는 것은 물론이요, 날아가는 새까지 떨어뜨릴 수 있었다."], ["At length, however, Perceval was roused to a desire of military renown by seeing in the forest five knights who were in complete armor.", "그러던 어느 날, 페르세발은 숲속에서 완전한 갑주를 갖춘 다섯 명의 기사를 보고는 군사적 명성에 대한 욕망에 사로잡혔다."], ["He said to his mother, “Mother, what are those yonder?”", "그는 어머니에게 물었다. \"어머니, 저기 저들은 무엇인가요?\""], ["“They are angels, my son,” said she.", "\"저들은 천사들이란다, 아들아.\""], ["“By my faith, I will go and become an angel with them.”", "어머니가 말했다. \"맹세코 저는 가서 저들과 함께 천사가 되겠어요.\""], ["And Perceval went to the road and met them.", "그리하여 페르세발은 길로 나아가 그들을 만났다. \"착한 젊은이여, 말해 보게.\""], ["“Tell me, good lad,”", "그들 중 하나가 말했다."], ["said one of them, “sawest thou a knight pass this way either to-day or yesterday?”", "\"오늘이나 어제 이 길로 지나가는 기사를 보지 못했는가?\" \"기사가 무엇인지 저는 모릅니다.\""], ["“I know not,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “what a knight is.”", "\"나와 같은 자가 기사라네.\""], ["“Such an one as I am,”", "기사가 말했다."], ["said the knight. “If thou wilt tell me what I ask thee, I will tell thee what thou askest me.”", "\"내가 묻는 것에 답해 준다면, 그대가 묻는 것에 답해 주겠네.\""], ["“Gladly will I do so,”", "\"기꺼이 그리하지요.\""], ["said Sir Owain, for that was the knight’s name.", "오웨인 경이 말했으니, 그것이 그 기사의 이름이었다."], ["“What is this?”", "\"이것은 무엇입니까?\""], ["demanded Perceval, touching the saddle.", "페르세발이 안장을 만지며 물었다."], ["“It is a saddle,” said Owain.", "\"그것은 안장이라네.\""], ["Then he asked about all the accoutrements which he saw upon the men and the horses, and about the arms, and what they were for, and how they were used.", "오웨인이 말했다. 그러고 나서 그는 사람과 말 위에서 본 온갖 장비에 대해, 그리고 무기에 대해, 그것들이 무엇에 쓰이며 어떻게 사용되는지 물었다."], ["And Sir Owain showed him all those things fully.", "오웨인 경은 그 모든 것을 그에게 빠짐없이 보여 주었다."], ["And Perceval in return gave him such information as he had.", "그리고 페르세발도 답례로 자기가 아는 바를 그에게 일러 주었다."]], [["Then Perceval returned to his mother, and said to her, “Mother, those were not angels, but honorable knights.”", "그런 다음 페르세발은 어머니에게 돌아와 말했다. \"어머니, 저들은 천사가 아니라 명예로운 기사들이었어요.\""], ["Then his mother swooned away.", "그러자 그의 어머니는 정신을 잃고 쓰러졌다."], ["And Perceval went to the place where they kept the horses that carried firewood and provisions for the castle, and he took a bony, piebald horse, which seemed to him the strongest of them.", "페르세발은 성에 쓸 장작과 식량을 나르는 말들을 두는 곳으로 가서, 그 가운데 가장 튼튼해 보이는 깡마르고 얼룩진 말 한 마리를 골랐다."], ["And he pressed a pack into the form of a saddle, and with twisted twigs he imitated the trappings which he had seen upon the horses.", "그리고 짐 보따리를 안장 모양으로 눌러 만들고, 비틀어 엮은 잔가지로 말 위에서 보았던 마구를 흉내 내었다."], ["When he came again to his mother, the countess had recovered from her swoon.", "그가 다시 어머니에게 왔을 때, 백작 부인은 실신에서 깨어나 있었다."], ["“My son,”", "\"아들아.\""], ["said she, “desirest thou to ride forth?”", "어머니가 말했다. \"말을 타고 길을 떠나고 싶으냐?\""], ["“Yes, with thy leave,”", "\"예, 허락해 주신다면요.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Go forward, then,”", "\"그렇다면 떠나거라.\" 어머니가 말했다."], ["she said, “to the court of Arthur, where there are the best and the noblest and the most bountiful of men, and tell him thou art Perceval, the son of Pelenore, and ask of him to bestow knighthood on thee.", "\"아서의 궁정으로 가거라. 그곳에는 가장 훌륭하고 가장 고귀하며 가장 너그러운 자들이 있으니, 네가 펠레노어의 아들 페르세발이라 밝히고, 그에게 기사 작위를 내려 달라 청하여라."], ["And whenever thou seest a church, repeat there thy pater-noster; and if thou see meat and drink, and hast need of them, thou mayest take them.", "그리고 교회를 볼 때마다 그곳에서 주기도문을 외우거라. 또 고기와 마실 것을 보고 그것이 필요하거든 가져도 좋다."], ["If thou hear an outcry of one in distress, proceed toward it, especially if it be the cry of a woman, and render her what service thou canst.", "곤경에 처한 자의 부르짖음을 듣거든, 특히 그것이 여인의 비명이거든 그쪽으로 나아가, 네가 할 수 있는 봉사를 베풀어라."], ["If thou see a fair jewel, win it, for thus shalt thou acquire fame; yet freely give it to another, for thus thou shalt obtain praise.", "아름다운 보석을 보거든 그것을 손에 넣어라, 그리하면 명성을 얻을 것이다. 그러나 그것을 아낌없이 남에게 주어라, 그리하면 칭송을 얻을 것이다."], ["If thou see a fair woman, pay court to her, for thus thou wilt obtain love.”", "아름다운 여인을 보거든 그녀에게 구애하여라, 그리하면 사랑을 얻을 것이다.\""]], [["After this discourse Perceval mounted the horse and taking a number of sharp-pointed sticks in his hand he rode forth.", "이러한 가르침을 마친 뒤 페르세발은 말에 올라타, 끝이 뾰족한 막대기 여러 개를 손에 들고 길을 떠났다."], ["And he rode far in the woody wilderness without food or drink.", "그는 먹을 것도 마실 것도 없이 숲이 우거진 황야를 멀리 달려갔다."], ["At last he came to an opening in the wood where he saw a tent, and as he thought it might be a church he said his pater-noster to it.", "마침내 그는 숲속의 빈터에 이르러 천막 하나를 보았고, 그것이 교회일지도 모른다고 여겨 그것을 향해 주기도문을 외웠다."], ["And he went towards it; and the door of the tent was open.", "그리고 그쪽으로 다가갔는데, 천막의 문이 열려 있었다."], ["And Perceval dismounted and entered the tent.", "페르세발은 말에서 내려 천막 안으로 들어갔다."], ["In the tent he found a maiden sitting, with a golden frontlet on her forehead and a gold ring on her hand.", "천막 안에서 그는 이마에 황금 머리띠를 두르고 손에 금반지를 낀 채 앉아 있는 처녀를 발견했다. 페르세발이 말했다."], ["And Perceval said, “Maiden, I salute you, for my mother told me whenever I met a lady I must respectfully salute her.”", "\"처녀여, 인사드립니다. 어머니께서 숙녀를 만나거든 반드시 정중히 인사하라 일러 주셨기 때문입니다.\""], ["Perceiving in one corner of the tent some food, two flasks full of wine, and some boar’s flesh roasted, he said, “My mother told me, whenever I saw meat and drink to take it.”", "천막 한구석에 음식과 포도주가 가득 찬 두 개의 병, 그리고 구운 멧돼지 고기가 있는 것을 알아채고 그가 말했다. \"어머니께서 고기와 마실 것을 보거든 가지라 일러 주셨습니다.\""], ["And he ate greedily, for he was very hungry.", "그러고는 몹시 배가 고팠던 터라 게걸스레 먹었다. 처녀가 말했다."], ["The maiden said, “Sir, thou hadst best go quickly from here, for fear that my friends should come, and evil should befall you.”", "\"기사님, 여기서 어서 떠나시는 편이 좋겠습니다. 제 친구들이 와서 당신에게 화가 미칠까 두렵습니다.\""], ["But Perceval said, “My mother told me wheresoever I saw a fair jewel to take it,”", "그러나 페르세발은 말했다. \"어머니께서 아름다운 보석을 보거든 어디서든 가지라 일러 주셨습니다.\""], ["and he took the gold ring from her finger, and put it on his own; and he gave the maiden his own ring in exchange for hers; then he mounted his horse and rode away.", "그러고는 그녀의 손가락에서 금반지를 빼어 자기 손가락에 끼고, 그 대신 자신의 반지를 그녀에게 주었다. 그런 다음 말에 올라 길을 떠났다."]], [["Perceval journeyed on till he arrived at Arthur’s court.", "페르세발은 계속 나아가 마침내 아서의 궁정에 도착했다."], ["And it so happened that just at that time an uncourteous knight had offered Queen Guenever a gross insult.", "그런데 마침 그때, 무례한 기사 하나가 왕비 귀네비어에게 심각한 모욕을 가한 일이 있었다."], ["For when her page was serving the queen with a golden goblet, this knight struck the arm of the page and dashed the wine in the queen’s face and over her stomacher.", "그녀의 시동이 왕비에게 황금 술잔을 받들어 올리고 있을 때, 이 기사가 시동의 팔을 쳐서 포도주를 왕비의 얼굴과 가슴받이 위로 끼얹었던 것이다. 그러고 나서 그는 말했다."], ["Then he said, “If any have boldness to avenge this insult to Guenever, let him follow me to the meadow.”", "\"귀네비어에게 가한 이 모욕을 갚을 만큼 담대한 자가 있거든, 나를 따라 초원으로 나오너라.\""], ["So the knight took his horse and rode to the meadow, carrying away the golden goblet.", "그리하여 기사는 말을 타고 황금 술잔을 가지고서 초원으로 갔다."], ["And all the household hung down their heads and no one offered to follow the knight to take vengeance upon him.", "그러자 온 궁정 사람들이 고개를 떨구었고, 어느 누구도 그 기사를 따라가 복수하겠다고 나서지 않았다."], ["For it seemed to them that no one would have ventured on so daring an outrage unless he possessed such powers, through magic or charms, that none could be able to punish him.", "그들에게는, 마법이나 주술을 통해 그 누구도 처벌할 수 없는 그러한 힘을 지니지 않고서야 어느 누구도 그토록 대담한 폭거를 감행하지 못했으리라 여겨졌기 때문이다."], ["Just then, behold, Perceval entered the hall upon the bony, piebald horse, with his uncouth trappings.", "바로 그때, 보라, 페르세발이 그 깡마르고 얼룩진 말에 볼품없는 마구를 갖추고서 큰 방으로 들어섰다."], ["In the centre of the hall stood Kay the Seneschal.", "방 한가운데에는 집사장 케이가 서 있었다."], ["“Tell me, tall man,”", "\"큰 사람이여, 말해 주시오.\""], ["said Perceval, “is that Arthur yonder?”", "페르세발이 말했다. \"저기 저분이 아서요?\""], ["“What wouldst thou with Arthur?”", "\"아서에게 무슨 볼일인가?\""], ["asked Kay.", "케이가 물었다."], ["“My mother told me to go to Arthur and receive knighthood from him.”", "\"어머니께서 아서에게 가서 그분에게 기사 작위를 받으라 일러 주셨습니다.\""], ["“By my faith,”", "\"맹세코.\""], ["said he, “thou art all too meanly equipped with horse and with arms.”", "그가 말했다. \"그대는 말이며 무기며 너무도 초라하게 갖추었구나.\""], ["Then all the household began to jeer and laugh at him.", "그러자 온 궁정 사람들이 그를 향해 야유하고 비웃기 시작했다."], ["But there was a certain damsel who had been a whole year at Arthur’s court, and had never been known to smile.", "그런데 아서의 궁정에 꼬박 일 년을 머무는 동안 단 한 번도 미소를 지은 적이 없다고 알려진 한 처녀가 있었다."], ["And the king’s fool[54] had said that this damsel would not smile till she had seen him who would be the flower of chivalry.", "왕의 어릿광대는 이 처녀가 기사도의 꽃이 될 자를 보기 전에는 미소 짓지 않으리라고 말한 바 있었다."], ["Now this damsel came up to Perceval and told him, smiling, that if he lived he would be one of the bravest and best of knights.", "그런데 이제 이 처녀가 페르세발에게 다가와 미소를 지으며, 그가 살아남는다면 가장 용감하고 가장 훌륭한 기사 중 하나가 될 것이라 말했다. \"참으로.\""], ["“Truly,”", "케이가 말했다."], ["said Kay, “thou art ill taught to remain a year at Arthur’s court, with choice of society, and smile on no one, and now before the face of Arthur and all his knights to call such a man as this the flower of knighthood;”", "\"그대는 가르침을 잘못 받았구나. 아서의 궁정에서 일 년을 머물며 골라 어울릴 사람이 많은데도 누구에게도 미소 짓지 않더니, 이제 아서와 그의 모든 기사들 앞에서 이런 자를 두고 기사도의 꽃이라 부르다니.\""], ["and he gave her a box on the ear, that she fell senseless to the ground.", "그러고는 그녀의 뺨을 후려쳐, 그녀는 정신을 잃고 바닥에 쓰러졌다. 그런 다음 케이가 페르세발에게 말했다."], ["Then said Kay to Perceval, “Go after the knight who went hence to the meadow, overthrow him and recover the golden goblet, and possess thyself of his horse and arms, and thou shalt have knighthood.”", "\"여기서 초원으로 나간 기사를 뒤따라가, 그를 쓰러뜨리고 황금 술잔을 되찾아라. 그리고 그의 말과 무기를 차지하면, 그대는 기사 작위를 얻으리라.\""], ["“I will do so, tall man,”", "\"그리하지요, 큰 사람이여.\""], ["said Perceval.", "페르세발이 말했다."], ["So he turned his horse’s head toward the meadow.", "그리하여 그는 말 머리를 초원 쪽으로 돌렸다."], ["And when he came there, the knight was riding up and down, proud of his strength and valor and noble mien.", "그가 그곳에 이르렀을 때, 그 기사는 자신의 힘과 용맹과 고귀한 풍채에 자만하여 이리저리 말을 몰고 있었다. \"말해 보아라.\""], ["“Tell me,”", "기사가 말했다."], ["said the knight, “didst thou see any one coming after me from the court?”", "\"궁정에서 누군가 나를 뒤따라오는 것을 보았느냐?\" \"그곳에 있던 큰 사람이.\""], ["“The tall man that was there,”", "페르세발이 말했다."], ["said Perceval, “told me to come and overthrow thee, and to take from thee the goblet and thy horse and armor for myself.”", "\"나더러 와서 너를 쓰러뜨리고, 너에게서 술잔과 말과 갑주를 빼앗아 내 것으로 삼으라 했다.\" \"닥쳐라!\""], ["“Silence!”", "기사가 말했다."], ["said the knight; “go back to the court, and tell Arthur either to come himself, or to send some other to fight with me; and unless he do so quickly, I will not wait for him.”", "\"궁정으로 돌아가, 아서더러 직접 오든지 아니면 나와 싸울 다른 자를 보내라 일러라. 그가 서둘러 그리하지 않으면, 나는 그를 기다리지 않겠다.\""], ["“By my faith,”", "\"맹세코.\""], ["said Perceval, “choose thou whether it shall be willingly or unwillingly, for I will have the horse and the arms and the goblet.”", "페르세발이 말했다. \"기꺼이 내어 줄지 마지못해 내어 줄지는 네가 택하여라."], ["Upon this the knight ran at him furiously, and struck him a violent blow with the shaft of his spear, between the neck and the shoulder.", "나는 그 말과 갑주와 술잔을 가질 것이니.\" 이 말에 기사는 사납게 그에게 달려들어, 창대로 그의 목과 어깨 사이를 세차게 후려쳤다."], ["“Ha, ha, lad!”", "\"하, 하, 이놈!\""], ["said Perceval, “my mother’s servants were not used to play with me in this wise; so thus will I play with thee.”", "페르세발이 말했다. \"어머니의 하인들도 나와 이런 식으로 놀지는 않았다."], ["And he threw at him one of his sharp-pointed sticks, and it struck him in the eye, and came out at the back of his head, so that he fell down lifeless.", "그러니 나도 너와 이렇게 놀아 주마.\" 그러고는 끝이 뾰족한 막대기 하나를 그에게 던졌고, 그것이 그의 눈에 박혀 뒤통수로 빠져나오니, 기사는 숨을 거두고 쓰러졌다."]], [["“Verily,”", "\"참으로.\""], ["said Sir Owain, the son of Urien, to Kay the Seneschal, “thou wast ill-advised to send that madman after the knight, for he must either be overthrown or flee, and either way it will be a disgrace to Arthur and his warriors; therefore will I go to see what has befallen him.”", "우리엔의 아들 오웨인 경이 집사장 케이에게 말했다. \"그대가 저 미치광이를 기사에게 보낸 것은 잘못된 판단이오."], ["So Sir Owain went to the meadow, and he found Perceval trying in vain to get the dead knight’s armor off, in order to clothe himself with it.", "그는 쓰러지거나 달아나거나 둘 중 하나일 터인데, 어느 쪽이든 아서와 그의 전사들에게 수치가 될 것이오. 그러니 내가 가서 그에게 무슨 일이 일어났는지 보겠소.\""], ["Sir Owain unfastened the armor, and helped Perceval to put it on, and taught him how to put his foot in the stirrup, and use the spur; for Perceval had never used stirrup nor spur, but rode without saddle, and urged on his horse with a stick.", "그리하여 오웨인 경이 초원으로 가 보니, 페르세발이 죽은 기사의 갑주를 벗겨 자기가 입으려고 헛되이 애쓰고 있었다. 오웨인 경은 갑주를 풀어 주고, 페르세발이 그것을 입는 것을 도왔으며, 발을 등자에 끼우고 박차를 쓰는 법을 가르쳐 주었다."], ["Then Owain would have had him return to the court to receive the praise that was his due; but Perceval said, “I will not come to the court till I have encountered the tall man that is there, to revenge the injury he did to the maiden.", "페르세발은 등자도 박차도 써 본 적이 없어, 안장 없이 말을 타고 막대기로 말을 몰아 왔던 것이다. 그러자 오웨인은 그가 마땅히 받아야 할 칭송을 받도록 궁정으로 돌아가게 하려 했다."], ["But take thou the goblet to Queen Guenever, and tell King Arthur that, wherever I am, I will be his vassal, and will do him what profit and service I can.”", "그러나 페르세발은 말했다. \"그곳에 있던 큰 사람을 만나 그가 처녀에게 가한 모욕을 갚기 전에는 궁정으로 가지 않겠소."], ["And Sir Owain went back to the court, and related all these things to Arthur and Guenever, and to all the household.", "그러나 그대가 이 술잔을 왕비 귀네비어에게 가져다 드리고, 아서 왕께는 내가 어디에 있든 그의 가신이 되어 내가 할 수 있는 이로움과 봉사를 다하겠다고 전해 주시오.\" 그리하여 오웨인 경은 궁정으로 돌아가, 이 모든 일을 아서와 귀네비어, 그리고 온 궁정 사람들에게 전했다."]], [["And Perceval rode forward.", "페르세발은 다시 말을 타고 나아갔다."], ["And he came to a lake on the side of which was a fair castle, and on the border of the lake he saw a hoary-headed man sitting upon a velvet cushion, and his attendants were fishing in the lake.", "그는 한쪽에 아름다운 성이 서 있는 호수에 이르렀고, 호숫가에서 벨벳 방석 위에 앉아 있는 백발의 노인을 보았는데, 그의 시종들은 호수에서 낚시를 하고 있었다."], ["When the hoary-headed man beheld Perceval approaching, he arose and went into the castle.", "백발의 노인은 페르세발이 다가오는 것을 보자 일어나 성안으로 들어갔다."], ["Perceval rode to the castle, and the door was open, and he entered the hall.", "페르세발은 말을 타고 성으로 갔고, 문이 열려 있어 큰 방으로 들어섰다."], ["And the hoary-headed man received Perceval courteously, and asked him to sit by him on the cushion.", "백발의 노인은 페르세발을 정중히 맞이하며, 자기 곁의 방석에 앉으라 청했다."], ["When it was time the tables were set, and they went to meat.", "때가 되자 식탁이 차려졌고, 그들은 식사를 하러 갔다."], ["And when they had finished their meat the hoary-headed man asked Perceval if he knew how to fight with the sword.", "식사를 마친 뒤 백발의 노인은 페르세발에게 검으로 싸울 줄 아느냐고 물었다. \"모릅니다.\""], ["“I know not,”", "페르세발이 말했다."], ["said Perceval, “but were I to be taught, doubtless I should.”", "\"허나 가르쳐 주신다면, 분명 익힐 수 있을 것입니다.\" 그러자 백발의 노인이 그에게 말했다."], ["And the hoary-headed man said to him, “I am thy uncle, thy mother’s brother; I am called King Pecheur.[55] Thou shalt remain with me a space, in order to learn the manners and customs of different countries, and courtesy and noble bearing.", "\"나는 그대의 외숙, 곧 그대 어머니의 오라비다. 사람들은 나를 페쇠르 왕이라 부른다."], ["And this do thou remember, if thou seest aught to cause thy wonder, ask not the meaning of it; if no one has the courtesy to inform thee, the reproach will not fall upon thee, but upon me that am thy teacher.”", "그대는 여러 나라의 예절과 관습, 그리고 예의와 고귀한 처신을 배우기 위해 한동안 나와 함께 머물거라. 그리고 이것을 명심하여라."], ["While Perceval and his uncle discoursed together, Perceval beheld two youths enter the hall bearing a golden cup and a spear of mighty size, with blood dropping from its point to the ground.", "그대를 놀라게 할 만한 무언가를 보더라도 그 뜻을 묻지 말아라. 누구도 친절히 알려 주지 않더라도, 그 책망은 그대가 아니라 그대의 스승인 나에게 돌아갈 것이다.\""], ["And when all the company saw this they began to weep and lament. But for all that, the man did not break off his discourse with Perceval.", "페르세발과 외숙이 함께 이야기를 나누는 동안, 페르세발은 두 젊은이가 황금 잔과 거대한 창을 들고 큰 방으로 들어오는 것을 보았는데, 그 창끝에서는 피가 바닥으로 방울방울 떨어지고 있었다."], ["And as he did not tell him the meaning of what he saw, he forebore to ask him concerning it.", "그리고 그곳의 모든 사람들이 이를 보고는 울며 탄식하기 시작했다."], ["Now the cup that Perceval saw was the Sangreal, and the spear the sacred spear; and afterwards King Pecheur removed with those sacred relics into a far country. .", "그러나 그 모든 것에도 노인은 페르세발과의 이야기를 멈추지 않았다."], [". .", "그리고 노인이 그가 본 것의 뜻을 일러 주지 않았으므로, 그도 그것에 관해 묻기를 삼갔다."], [". .", "그런데 페르세발이 본 그 잔은 성배였고, 그 창은 신성한 창이었다."], [". .", "후에 페쇠르 왕은 그 신성한 성물들을 가지고 먼 나라로 떠나갔다."]], [["One evening Perceval entered a valley, and came to a hermit’s cell; and the hermit welcomed him gladly, and there he spent the night.", "어느 날 저녁 페르세발은 어느 골짜기로 들어가 은자의 처소에 이르렀다. 은자는 그를 기쁘게 맞이했고, 그는 그곳에서 하룻밤을 보냈다."], ["And in the morning he arose, and when he went forth, behold! a shower of snow had fallen in the night, and a hawk had killed a wild-fowl in front of the cell.", "그리고 아침에 그가 일어나 밖으로 나가 보니, 보라, 밤사이 눈이 한바탕 내렸고, 매 한 마리가 처소 앞에서 들새를 잡아 죽여 놓았다."], ["And the noise of the horse had scared the hawk away, and a raven alighted on the bird.", "그런데 말이 내는 소리에 매가 놀라 날아가 버리자, 까마귀 한 마리가 그 새 위에 내려앉았다."], ["And Perceval stood and compared the blackness of the raven and the whiteness of the snow and the redness of the blood to the hair of the lady that best he loved, which was blacker than jet, and to her skin, which was whiter than the snow, and to the two red spots upon her cheeks, which were redder than the blood upon the snow.", "페르세발은 가만히 서서, 까마귀의 검음과 눈의 흼과 피의 붉음을, 그가 가장 사랑하는 여인의 머리카락 — 흑옥보다 검은 — 과 그녀의 살결 — 눈보다 흰 — 과 그녀의 두 뺨에 어린 두 점의 붉은 빛 — 눈 위의 피보다 붉은 — 에 견주어 보았다."]], [["Now Arthur and his household were in search of Perceval, and by chance they came that way.", "그런데 아서와 그의 궁정 사람들은 페르세발을 찾아 나서 있었고, 우연히 그 길로 들어서게 되었다. \"그대들은 아는가.\""], ["“Know ye,”", "아서가 말했다."], ["said Arthur, “who is the knight with the long spear that stands by the brook up yonder?”", "\"저 위쪽 시냇가에 긴 창을 들고 서 있는 저 기사가 누구인지?\""], ["“Lord,”", "\"주군이시여.\""], ["said one of them, “I will go and learn who he is.”", "그들 중 하나가 말했다. \"제가 가서 그가 누구인지 알아보겠습니다.\""], ["So the youth came to the place where Perceval was, and asked him what he did thus, and who he was.", "그리하여 그 젊은이가 페르세발이 있는 곳으로 와, 그에게 어찌하여 그러고 있는지, 또 그가 누구인지 물었다."], ["But Perceval was so intent upon his thought that he gave him no answer.", "그러나 페르세발은 자신의 생각에 너무도 골똘히 잠겨 그에게 아무런 대답도 하지 않았다."], ["Then the youth thrust at Perceval with his lance; and Perceval turned upon him, and struck him to the ground.", "그러자 젊은이가 창으로 페르세발을 찔렀고, 페르세발은 그에게 몸을 돌려 그를 땅바닥에 쓰러뜨렸다."], ["And when the youth returned to the king, and told how rudely he had been treated, Sir Kay said, “I will go myself.”", "그리고 젊은이가 왕에게 돌아가 자기가 얼마나 거칠게 당했는지 고하자, 케이 경이 말했다. \"내가 직접 가겠소.\""], ["And when he greeted Perceval, and got no answer, he spoke to him rudely and angrily.", "그가 페르세발에게 인사를 건넸으나 아무런 대답을 얻지 못하자, 그는 무례하고 성난 어조로 그에게 말을 걸었다."], ["And Perceval thrust at him with his lance, and cast him down so that he broke his arm and his shoulder-blade.", "그러자 페르세발은 창으로 그를 찔러 내동댕이쳤고, 그는 팔과 어깨뼈가 부러졌다."], ["And while he lay thus stunned his horse returned back at a wild and prancing pace.", "그가 그렇게 정신을 잃고 쓰러져 있는 동안, 그의 말은 사납게 껑충거리며 돌아가 버렸다."]], [["Then said Sir Gawain, surnamed the Golden-Tongued, because he was the most courteous knight in Arthur’s court: “It is not fitting that any should disturb an honorable knight from his thought unadvisedly; for either he is pondering some damage that he has sustained, or he is thinking of the lady whom best he loves.", "그러자 아서의 궁정에서 가장 예의 바른 기사였기에 황금 혀라는 별명을 가진 가웨인 경이 말했다. \"명예로운 기사를 분별없이 그의 생각에서 흔들어 놓는 것은 마땅하지 않습니다."], ["If it seem well to thee, lord, I will go and see if this knight has changed from his thought, and if he has, I will ask him courteously to come and visit thee.”", "그는 자신이 입은 어떤 손해를 곱씹고 있거나, 아니면 그가 가장 사랑하는 여인을 생각하고 있을 것이기 때문입니다. 주군께서 좋으시다면, 제가 가서 이 기사가 생각에서 벗어났는지 살피고, 만약 그렇다면 그에게 정중히 청하여 주군을 뵈러 오게 하겠습니다.\""]], [["And Perceval was resting on the shaft of his spear, pondering the same thought, and Sir Gawain came to him, and said: “If I thought it would be as agreeable to thee as it would be to me, I would converse with thee.", "페르세발은 창대에 기댄 채 여전히 그 생각에 잠겨 있었고, 가웨인 경이 그에게 다가와 말했다. \"그대에게도 나에게도 똑같이 흡족한 일이리라 여겨진다면, 그대와 이야기를 나누고 싶습니다."], ["I have also a message from Arthur unto thee, to pray thee to come and visit him.", "또한 나는 아서로부터 그대에게 전할 전갈을 지니고 있으니, 와서 그를 뵈어 달라 청하는 것입니다."], ["And two men have been before on this errand.”", "그리고 이 심부름으로 두 사람이 앞서 다녀갔습니다.\""], ["“That is true,”", "\"그건 사실입니다.\""], ["said Perceval; “and uncourteously they came.", "페르세발이 말했다. \"그리고 그들은 무례하게 왔습니다."], ["They attacked me, and I was annoyed thereat.”", "그들이 나를 공격했고, 나는 그것에 짜증이 났습니다.\""], ["Then he told him the thought that occupied his mind, and Gawain said, “This was not an ungentle thought, and I should marvel if it were pleasant for thee to be drawn from it.”", "그러고는 자신의 마음을 사로잡고 있던 생각을 그에게 들려주자, 가웨인이 말했다. \"그것은 점잖지 못한 생각이 아니었으니, 그대를 거기서 끌어내는 것이 그대에게 즐거운 일이라면 나는 놀랄 것입니다.\""], ["Then said Perceval, “Tell me, is Sir Kay in Arthur’s court?”", "그러자 페르세발이 말했다. \"말해 주시오, 케이 경이 아서의 궁정에 있습니까?\""], ["“He is,”", "\"있습니다.\""], ["said Gawain; “and truly he is the knight who fought with thee last.”", "가웨인이 말했다. \"그리고 참으로 그가 그대와 마지막으로 싸운 그 기사입니다.\""], ["“Verily,”", "\"참으로.\""], ["said Perceval, “I am not sorry to have thus avenged the insult to the smiling maiden.”", "페르세발이 말했다. \"미소 짓던 처녀에게 가한 모욕을 이렇게 갚게 되어 유감스럽지 않습니다.\""], ["Then Perceval told him his name, and said, “Who art thou?”", "그러고 나서 페르세발은 그에게 자신의 이름을 일러 주고는 말했다. \"그대는 누구입니까?\""], ["And he replied, “I am Gawain.”", "그가 대답했다. \"나는 가웨인입니다.\""], ["“I am right glad to meet thee,”", "\"그대를 만나게 되어 참으로 기쁩니다.\""], ["said Perceval, “for I have everywhere heard of thy prowess and uprightness; and I solicit thy fellowship.”", "페르세발이 말했다. \"그대의 용맹과 올곧음에 관해서는 어디서나 들어 왔기 때문입니다."], ["“Thou shalt have it, by my faith; and grant me thine,”", "그대의 우정을 청합니다.\" \"맹세코 그대는 그것을 얻을 것이니, 그대 또한 나에게 우정을 베풀어 주시오.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Gladly will I do so,”", "\"기꺼이 그리하지요.\""], ["answered Perceval.", "페르세발이 대답했다."]], [["So they went together to Arthur, and saluted him.", "그리하여 그들은 함께 아서에게 가서 그에게 인사를 올렸다."]], [["“Behold, lord,”", "\"보십시오, 주군이시여.\""], ["said Gawain, “him whom thou hast sought so long.”", "가웨인이 말했다. \"오래도록 찾으셨던 그자입니다.\""], ["“Welcome unto thee, chieftain,”", "\"그대에게 환영을 보내노라, 우두머리여.\""], ["said Arthur.", "아서가 말했다."], ["And hereupon there came the queen and her handmaidens, and Perceval saluted them.", "그러자 이때 왕비와 그녀의 시녀들이 다가왔고, 페르세발은 그들에게 인사를 올렸다."], ["And they were rejoiced to see him, and bade him welcome.", "그들은 그를 보고 기뻐하며 환영의 인사를 건넸다."], ["And Arthur did him great honor and respect and they returned towards Caerleon.", "아서는 그에게 큰 영예와 경의를 베풀었고, 그들은 카에를레온을 향해 돌아갔다."]], [["————", "————"]], [["# THE SANGREAL, OR HOLY GRAAL", "# THE SANGREAL, OR HOLY GRAAL"]], [["THE Sangreal was the cup from which our Saviour drank at his last supper.", "상그레알은 우리 구세주께서 최후의 만찬에서 마셨던 잔이었다."], ["He was supposed to have given it to Joseph of Arimathea, who carried it to Europe, together with the spear with which the soldier pierced the Saviour’s side.", "구세주는 이 잔을 아리마태아의 요셉에게 주셨던 것으로 여겨지며, 요셉은 그것을 군인이 구세주의 옆구리를 찔렀던 창과 함께 유럽으로 가져왔다."], ["From generation to generation, one of the descendants of Joseph of Arimathea had been devoted to the guardianship of these precious relics; but on the sole condition of leading a life of purity in thought, word, and deed.", "세대를 거듭하며 아리마태아의 요셉의 후손들 가운데 한 사람이 이 귀중한 성물의 수호를 위해 헌신해 왔다. 다만 생각과 말과 행동에서 순결한 삶을 사는 것을 유일한 조건으로 삼았다."], ["For a long time the Sangreal was visible to all pilgrims, and its presence conferred blessings upon the land in which it was preserved.", "오랜 세월 동안 상그레알은 모든 순례자에게 보이는 것이었고, 그것이 자리한 땅에는 그 존재로 인해 축복이 내렸다."], ["But at length one of those holy men to whom its guardianship had descended so far forgot the obligation of his sacred office as to look with unhallowed eye upon a young female pilgrim whose robe was accidentally loosened as she knelt before him.", "그러나 마침내 그 수호의 임무를 물려받은 거룩한 사람들 가운데 하나가, 자기 앞에 무릎 꿇은 한 젊은 여성 순례자의 옷이 우연히 풀어졌을 때, 신성한 직무의 의무를 잊고 부정한 눈으로 그녀를 바라보고 말았다."], ["The sacred lance instantly punished his frailty, spontaneously falling upon him, and inflicting a deep wound.", "신성한 창은 즉시 그의 나약함을 벌하여, 저절로 그에게 떨어져 깊은 상처를 입혔다."], ["The marvellous wound could by no means be healed, and the guardian of the Sangreal was ever after called “Le Roi Pescheur,”—The Sinner King.", "그 불가사의한 상처는 어떤 방법으로도 치유될 수 없었고, 상그레알의 수호자는 그 이후로 줄곧 \"르 루아 페쇠르(Le Roi Pescheur)\", 곧 죄지은 왕이라 불렸다."], ["The Sangreal withdrew its visible presence from the crowds who came to worship, and an iron age succeeded to the happiness which its presence had diffused among the tribes of Britain.", "상그레알은 경배하러 온 무리에게서 그 보이는 존재를 거두었고, 그 존재가 브리튼의 부족들 사이에 퍼뜨려 주었던 행복의 자리에는 철의 시대가 뒤를 이었다."]], [["“But then the times Grew to such evil that the Holy cup Was caught away to heaven and disappear’d.”", "\"하지만 그때 시대는 너무도 악해져서 그 거룩한 잔은 하늘로 채여 올라가 사라지고 말았네.\""], ["—The Holy Grail.", "─「거룩한 성배」."]], [["We have told in the history of Merlin how that great prophet and enchanter sent a message to King Arthur by Sir Gawain, directing him to undertake the recovery of the Sangreal, informing him at the same time that the knight who should accomplish that sacred quest was already born, and of a suitable age to enter upon it.", "우리는 멀린의 이야기에서, 그 위대한 예언자이자 마법사가 가웨인 경을 통해 아서 왕에게 전갈을 보내, 상그레알을 되찾는 일을 떠맡으라 지시하고, 동시에 그 거룩한 탐색을 이루어 낼 기사가 이미 태어났으며 그 일에 나서기에 알맞은 나이가 되었음을 알린 사연을 들려준 바 있다. 가웨인 경이 그 전갈을 전했고, 왕은 그 과업을 어떻게 가장 잘 이룰지 마음속으로 골똘히 궁리하고 있었다."], ["Sir Gawain delivered his message, and the king was anxiously revolving in his mind how best to achieve the enterprise, when, at the vigil of Pentecost, all the fellowship of the Round Table being met together at Camelot, as they sat at meat, suddenly there was heard a clap of thunder, and then a bright light burst forth, and every knight, as he looked on his fellow, saw him, in seeming, fairer than ever before.", "그러던 중 성령강림절 전야에, 원탁의 모든 동료들이 카멜롯에 함께 모여 식탁에 앉아 있을 때, 갑자기 천둥소리가 한 번 울리더니 환한 빛이 터져 나왔고, 모든 기사가 동료를 바라보니 그가 전보다 더 아름다워 보였다. 온 홀이 감미로운 향기로 가득 찼고, 모든 기사 앞에는 저마다 가장 좋아하는 음식과 음료가 놓였다."], ["All the hall was filled with sweet odors, and every knight had such meat and drink as he best loved.", "그때 흰 비단으로 덮인 거룩한 성배가 홀 안으로 들어왔는데, 아무도 그것을 볼 수 없었으며, 그것은 홀을 가로질러 지나가더니 갑자기 사라져 버렸다."], ["Then there entered into the hall the Holy Graal, covered with white samite, so that none could see it, and it passed through the hall suddenly, and disappeared.", "이 동안 누구도 한마디 말이 없었으나, 말할 숨을 되찾았을 때 아서 왕이 말했다. \"참으로 우리는 오늘 주께서 우리에게 보여 주신 것에 대해 크게 감사드려야 마땅하다.\""], ["During this time no one spoke a word, but when they had recovered breath to speak King Arthur said, “Certainly we ought greatly to thank the Lord for what he hath showed us this day.”", "그러자 가웨인 경이 일어나, 열두 달 하고도 하루 동안 상그레알을 찾을 것이며, 만일 그렇게 이룰 수 있다면 그것을 보기 전에는 돌아오지 않겠노라 맹세했다. 원탁의 기사들이 가웨인 경이 그렇게 말하는 것을 듣고는, 그들 대부분이 일어나 같은 맹세를 했다."], ["Then Sir Gawain rose up, and made a vow that for twelve months and a day he would seek the Sangreal, and not return till he had seen it, if so he might speed.", "아서 왕이 이를 듣고 크게 언짢아했으니, 그들이 자신들의 맹세를 거스를 수 없음을 잘 알았기 때문이다. \"아아!\""], ["When they of the Round Table heard Sir Gawain say so, they arose, the most part of them, and vowed the same.", "그가 가웨인 경에게 말했다. \"그대들은 그 맹세와 약속으로 나를 거의 죽인 셈이오."], ["When King Arthur heard this, he was greatly displeased, for he knew well that they might not gainsay their vows. “Alas!”", "그대들은 세상 어느 왕국에서도 함께 모인 것을 본 적 없는 가장 훌륭한 동료들을 내게서 앗아 갔으니 말이오."], ["said he to Sir Gawain, “you have nigh slain me with the vow and promise that ye have made, for ye have bereft me of the fairest fellowship that ever were seen together in any realm of the world; for when they shall depart hence, I am sure that all shall never meet more in this world.”", "그대들이 이곳을 떠나면, 모두가 이 세상에서 다시는 함께 만나지 못하리라는 것을 나는 확신하오.\""]], [["# SIR GALAHAD", "# SIR GALAHAD"]], [["At that time there entered the hall a good old man, and with him he brought a young knight, and these words he said: “Peace be with you, fair lords.” Then the old man said unto King Arthur, “Sir, I bring you here a young knight that is of kings’ lineage, and of the kindred of Joseph of Arimathea, being the son of Dame Elaine, the daughter of King Pelles, king of the foreign country.” Now the name of the young knight was Sir Galahad, and he was the son of Sir Launcelot du Lac; but he had dwelt with his mother, at the court of King Pelles, his grandfather, till now he was old enough to bear arms, and his mother had sent him in the charge of a holy hermit to King Arthur’s court. Then Sir Launcelot beheld his son, and had great joy of him. And Sir Bohort told his fellows, “Upon my life, this young knight shall come to great worship.” The noise was great in all the court, so that it came to the queen. And she said, “I would fain see him, for he must needs be a noble knight, for so is his father.” And the queen and her ladies all said that he resembled much unto his father; and he was seemly and demure as a dove, with all manner of good features, that in the whole world men might not find his match. And King Arthur said, “God make him a good man, for beauty faileth him not, as any that liveth.”", "그때 한 선량한 노인이 홀 안으로 들어왔는데, 그는 젊은 기사 한 사람을 데리고 있었고, 이렇게 말했다. \"평화가 그대들과 함께하기를, 고귀한 경들이여.\" 그러고는 노인이 아서 왕에게 말했다. \"전하, 제가 여기 왕가의 혈통이며 아리마태아의 요셉의 친족인 한 젊은 기사를 데려왔습니다. 이 자는 이방 나라의 왕인 펠레스 왕의 딸 일레인 부인의 아들입니다.\" 그런데 이 젊은 기사의 이름은 갈라하드 경이었고, 그는 호수의 랜슬롯 경의 아들이었다. 다만 그는 무기를 들 만한 나이가 될 때까지 외할아버지인 펠레스 왕의 궁정에서 어머니와 함께 지냈으며, 그의 어머니가 거룩한 은수자에게 맡겨 그를 아서 왕의 궁정으로 보낸 것이었다. 그때 랜슬롯 경이 자기 아들을 바라보고 그로 인해 큰 기쁨을 누렸다. 또 보호트 경이 동료들에게 말했다. \"내 목숨을 걸고, 이 젊은 기사는 큰 명예에 이를 것이오.\" 온 궁정에 떠들썩한 소리가 컸으므로 그 소문이 왕비에게까지 이르렀다. 그러자 왕비가 말했다. \"나도 기꺼이 그를 보고 싶구나. 그의 아버지가 그러하니, 그 또한 필시 고귀한 기사일 것이다.\" 왕비와 그 시녀들이 모두 그가 아버지를 무척 닮았다고 말했다. 그는 비둘기처럼 어여쁘고 단정했으며, 온갖 좋은 풍채를 갖추어, 온 세상을 통틀어 그와 견줄 자를 찾을 수 없을 정도였다. 아서 왕이 말했다. \"하느님께서 그를 선한 사람으로 만들어 주시기를. 살아 있는 어느 누구에게도 뒤지지 않을 만큼 그에게는 아름다움이 모자라지 않으니 말이오.\""]], [["Then the hermit led the young knight to the Siege Perilous; and he lifted up the cloth, and found there letters that said, “This is the seat of Sir Galahad, the good knight;”", "그러고는 은수자가 그 젊은 기사를 〈위험한 자리〉로 이끌었다. 그가 천을 걷어 내자 거기에는 \"이것은 선한 기사 갈라하드 경의 자리이다\"라고 적힌 글자가 있었고, 그는 그를 그 자리에 앉혔다."], ["and he made him sit in that seat.", "원탁의 모든 기사가 갈라하드 경이 그 자리에 무사히 앉아 있는 것을 보고 크게 경탄하며 말했다."], ["And all the knights of the Round Table marvelled greatly at Sir Galahad, seeing him sit securely in that seat, and said, “This is he by whom the Sangreal shall be achieved, for there never sat one before in that seat without being mischieved.”", "\"이 자가 바로 상그레알을 이루어 낼 분이다. 이전에 이 자리에 앉고도 화를 입지 않은 자가 없었으니 말이다.\""]], [["On the next day the king said, “Now, at this quest of the Sangreal shall all ye of the Round Table depart, and never shall I see you again altogether; therefore I will that ye all repair to the meadow of Camelot, for to just and tourney yet once more before ye depart.”", "이튿날 왕이 말했다. \"이제 이 상그레알의 탐색에 그대들 원탁의 기사 모두가 떠날 것이니, 다시는 그대들을 다 함께 보지 못하리라."], ["But all the meaning of the king was to see Sir Galahad proved.", "그러므로 나는 그대들이 모두 카멜롯의 들판으로 가서, 떠나기 전에 한 번 더 마상 창 시합과 무술 시합을 벌이기를 바라오.\""], ["So then were they all assembled in the meadow.", "그러나 왕의 모든 뜻은 갈라하드 경의 역량을 시험하는 데 있었다."], ["Then Sir Galahad, by request of the king and queen, put on his harness and his helm, but shield would he take none for any prayer of the king.", "그리하여 그들은 모두 들판에 모였다. 갈라하드 경은 왕과 왕비의 청에 따라 갑옷과 투구를 갖추었으나, 왕이 아무리 청해도 방패만은 들려 하지 않았다."], ["And the queen was in a tower, with all her ladies, to behold that tournament.", "왕비는 시녀들을 모두 거느리고 탑 위에서 그 시합을 지켜보았다. 갈라하드 경이 들판 한가운데로 말을 몰고 나아갔다."], ["Then Sir Galahad rode into the midst of the meadow; and there he began to break spears marvellously, so that all men had wonder of him, for he surmounted all knights that encountered with him, except two, Sir Launcelot and Sir Perceval.", "그곳에서 그는 놀랍도록 창을 부러뜨리기 시작했으니, 모든 이가 그를 보고 경탄했다. 그가 맞붙는 모든 기사를 압도했기 때문이었는데, 다만 두 사람, 랜슬롯 경과 퍼시벌 경만은 예외였다."]], [["“So many knights, that all the people cried, And almost burst the barriers in their heat, Shouting ‘Sir Galahad and Sir Perceval!’”", "\"수많은 기사들, 그리하여 모든 사람들이 외쳐 대며 열기에 차서 차단대를 거의 부수다시피 하고, '갈라하드 경과 퍼시벌 경!'을 소리쳤네.\""], ["—Sir Galahad.", "─「갈라하드 경」."]], [["Then the king, at the queen’s request, made him to alight, and presented him to the queen; and she said, “Never two men resembled one another more than he and Sir Launcelot, and therefore it is no marvel that he is like him in prowess.”", "그러자 왕이 왕비의 청에 따라 그를 말에서 내리게 하여 왕비에게 데려갔다. 왕비가 말했다. \"그와 랜슬롯 경보다 더 서로 닮은 두 사람은 결코 없었으니, 그가 무용에서도 그를 닮은 것은 조금도 이상한 일이 아니로다.\""]], [["Then the king and the queen went to the minster, and the knights followed them.", "그러고는 왕과 왕비가 성당으로 갔고, 기사들이 그들을 뒤따랐다."], ["And after the service was done they put on their helms and departed, and there was great sorrow.", "예배가 끝난 뒤 그들은 투구를 쓰고 떠났는데, 큰 슬픔이 일었다."], ["They rode through the streets of Camelot, and there was weeping of the rich and poor; and the king turned away, and might not speak for weeping.", "그들이 카멜롯의 거리를 지나갈 때 부자와 빈자가 모두 울었고, 왕은 고개를 돌린 채 울음 때문에 말을 잇지 못했다."], ["And so they departed, and every knight took the way that him best liked.", "그리하여 그들은 떠났고, 모든 기사가 저마다 가장 마음에 드는 길을 택했다."]], [["Sir Galahad rode forth without shield, and rode four days, and found no adventure.", "갈라하드 경은 방패 없이 말을 몰고 나아가, 나흘을 달렸으나 아무런 모험도 만나지 못했다."], ["And on the fourth day he came to a white abbey; and there he was received with great reverence, and led to a chamber.", "나흘째 되는 날 그는 한 흰 수도원에 이르렀다. 그곳에서 그는 큰 존경을 받으며 맞이되어 한 방으로 안내되었다."], ["He met there two knights, King Bagdemagus and Sir Uwaine, and they made of him great solace.", "그는 거기서 두 기사, 바그데마구스 왕과 우와인 경을 만났고, 그들은 그를 크게 위로해 주었다."], ["“Sirs,”", "\"경들이여,\""], ["said Sir Galahad, “what adventure brought you hither?”", "갈라하드 경이 말했다. \"무슨 모험이 그대들을 이곳으로 이끌었소?\""], ["“Sir,”", "\"경이여,\""], ["said they, “it is told us that within this place is a shield, which no man may bear unless he be worthy; and if one unworthy should attempt to bear it, it shall surely do him a mischief.”", "그들이 말했다. \"이 안에는 방패가 하나 있는데, 합당한 자가 아니면 누구도 그것을 들 수 없으며, 만일 합당치 못한 자가 그것을 들려 하면 반드시 그에게 화가 미친다고 들었소.\""], ["Then King Bagdemagus said, “I fear not to bear it, and that shall ye see to-morrow.”", "그러자 바그데마구스 왕이 말했다. \"나는 그것을 드는 일이 두렵지 않으니, 그것을 그대들이 내일 보게 될 것이오.\""]], [["So on the morrow they arose, and heard mass; then King Bagdemagus asked where the adventurous shield was.", "그리하여 이튿날 그들은 일어나 미사를 드렸고, 바그데마구스 왕이 그 모험의 방패가 어디 있는지 물었다."], ["Anon a monk led him behind an altar, where the shield hung, as white as snow; but in the midst there was a red cross.", "곧 한 수도사가 그를 제단 뒤로 이끌었는데, 그곳에 눈처럼 흰 방패가 걸려 있었으며, 그 한가운데에는 붉은 십자가가 있었다. 바그데마구스 왕은 그 방패를 들고 성당 밖으로 나왔다."], ["Then King Bagdemagus took the shield, and bare it out of the minster; and he said to Sir Galahad, “If it please you, abide here till ye know how I shall speed.”", "그러고는 갈라하드 경에게 말했다. \"괜찮다면, 내가 어찌 되는지 그대가 알게 될 때까지 여기 머물러 주시오.\""]], [["Then King Bagdemagus and his squire rode forth; and when they had ridden a mile or two, they saw a goodly knight come towards them, in white armor, horse and all; and he came as fast as his horse might run, with his spear in the rest; and King Bagdemagus directed his spear against him, and broke it upon the white knight, but the other struck him so hard that he broke the mails, and thrust him through the right shoulder, for the shield covered him not, and so he bare him from his horse. Then the white knight turned his horse and rode away.", "그러고는 바그데마구스 왕과 그의 종자가 말을 몰고 나아갔다. 그들이 한두 마장쯤 달렸을 때, 말과 갑옷이 온통 흰, 한 늠름한 기사가 그들을 향해 다가오는 것을 보았다. 그는 창을 받침에 끼운 채 말이 달릴 수 있는 한 빠르게 다가왔다. 바그데마구스 왕은 자기 창을 그를 향해 겨누어 흰 기사에게 부딪쳐 부러뜨렸으나, 상대는 그를 어찌나 세게 쳤는지 그의 사슬 갑옷을 부수고 오른쪽 어깨를 꿰뚫었으니, 방패가 그를 가려 주지 못한 탓이었고, 그리하여 그를 말에서 떨어뜨렸다. 그러고는 흰 기사가 말 머리를 돌려 떠나갔다."]], [["Then the squire went to King Bagdemagus, and asked him whether he were sore wounded or not.", "그러자 종자가 바그데마구스 왕에게 가서 그가 심하게 다쳤는지 아닌지를 물었다."], ["“I am sore wounded,”", "\"나는 심하게 다쳤다,\""], ["said he, “and full hardly shall I escape death.”", "그가 말했다. \"죽음을 면하기가 무척 어렵겠구나.\""], ["Then the squire set him on his horse, and brought him to an abbey; and there he was taken down softly, and unarmed, and laid in a bed, and his wound was looked to, for he lay there long, and hardly escaped with his life.", "그러자 종자가 그를 말에 태워 한 수도원으로 데려갔다. 그곳에서 그는 조심스레 내려져 갑옷을 벗고 침상에 뉘어졌으며, 그의 상처가 치료되었으니, 그는 오래도록 거기 누워 있다가 가까스로 목숨을 건졌다."], ["And the squire brought the shield back to the abbey.", "그리고 종자는 그 방패를 수도원으로 도로 가져왔다."]], [["The next day Sir Galahad took the shield, and within a while he came to the hermitage, where he met the white knight, and each saluted the other courteously.", "이튿날 갈라하드 경이 그 방패를 들었고, 얼마 지나지 않아 그는 은수처에 이르러 거기서 흰 기사를 만났는데, 두 사람은 서로 정중히 인사를 나누었다. \"경이여,\""], ["“Sir,”", "갈라하드 경이 말했다."], ["said Sir Galahad, “can you tell me the marvel of the shield?”", "\"그대는 이 방패의 불가사의를 내게 일러 줄 수 있겠소?\" \"경이여,\""], ["“Sir,”", "흰 기사가 말했다. \"그 방패는 옛적에 고귀한 기사 아리마태아의 요셉의 것이었소."], ["said the white knight, “that shield belonged of old to the gentle knight, Joseph of Arimathea; and when he came to die he said, ‘Never shall man bear this shield about his neck but he shall repent it, unto the time that Sir Galahad the good knight bear it, the last of my lineage, the which shall do many marvellous deeds.’”", "그가 죽음에 이르렀을 때 이렇게 말했소. '내 혈통의 마지막인 선한 기사 갈라하드 경이 이것을 들 때까지는, 어떤 사람도 이 방패를 목에 걸고 다니되 그것을 후회하지 않을 자가 없으리라."], ["And then the white knight vanished away.", "그는 수많은 불가사의한 일을 행할 것이다.'\" 그러고는 흰 기사가 사라져 버렸다."]], [["# SIR GAWAIN", "# SIR GAWAIN"]], [["After Sir Gawain departed, he rode many days, both toward and forward, and at last he came to the abbey where Sir Galahad took the white shield.", "가웨인 경은 떠난 뒤로 여러 날을 이리저리 오가며 말을 달렸고, 마침내 갈라하드 경이 흰 방패를 들었던 그 수도원에 이르렀다."], ["And they told Sir Gawain of the marvellous adventure that Sir Galahad had done.", "사람들은 갈라하드 경이 행한 불가사의한 모험을 가웨인 경에게 들려주었다. \"참으로,\""], ["“Truly,”", "가웨인 경이 말했다."], ["said Sir Gawain, “I am not happy that I took not the way that he went, for, if I may meet with him, I will not part from him lightly, that I may partake with him all the marvellous adventures which he shall achieve.”", "\"내가 그가 간 길로 가지 못한 것이 기쁘지 않구나. 만일 그를 만날 수 있다면, 그가 이루어 낼 모든 불가사의한 모험에 함께하기 위해 그를 쉬이 떠나지 않으련만.\""], ["“Sir,”", "\"경이여,\""], ["said one of the monks, “he will not be of your fellowship.”", "수도사 가운데 한 사람이 말했다. \"그는 그대와 한 무리가 되지 않을 것이오.\""], ["“Why?”", "\"어찌하여?\""], ["said Sir Gawain.", "가웨인 경이 말했다."], ["“Sir,”", "\"경이여,\""], ["said he, “because ye be sinful, and he is blissful.”", "그가 말했다. \"그대는 죄가 많고, 그는 복되기 때문이오.\""], ["Then said the monk, “Sir Gawain, thou must do penance for thy sins.”", "그러고는 수도사가 말했다. \"가웨인 경이여, 그대는 자기 죄를 위해 속죄를 행해야 하오.\""], ["“Sir, what penance shall I do?”", "\"경이여, 내가 무슨 속죄를 행해야 하오?\" \"내가 일러 줄 그런 것을 하시오,\""], ["“Such as I will show,”", "선한 사람이 말했다. \"아니오,\""], ["said the good man. “Nay,”", "가웨인 경이 말했다."], ["said Sir Gawain, “I will do no penance, for we knights adventurous often suffer great woe and pain.”", "\"나는 아무런 속죄도 하지 않겠소. 우리 모험하는 기사들은 흔히 큰 비탄과 고통을 겪으니 말이오.\""], ["“Well,”", "\"그러시오,\""], ["said the good man; and he held his peace.", "선한 사람이 말하고는 입을 다물었다."], ["And Sir Gawain departed.", "그리고 가웨인 경은 떠났다."]], [["Now it happened, not long after this, that Sir Gawain and Sir Hector rode together, and they came to a castle where was a great tournament.", "그런데 이 일이 있고 오래지 않아, 가웨인 경과 헥터 경이 함께 말을 달리다가, 큰 마상 시합이 열리고 있는 한 성에 이르게 되었다."], ["And Sir Gawain and Sir Hector joined themselves to the party that seemed the weaker, and they drove before them the other party.", "가웨인 경과 헥터 경은 더 약해 보이는 편에 가담하여 다른 편을 앞으로 몰아붙였다."], ["Then suddenly came into the lists a knight, bearing a white shield with a red cross, and by adventure he came by Sir Gawain, and he smote him so hard that he clave his helm and wounded his head, so that Sir Gawain fell to the earth.", "그때 갑자기 붉은 십자가가 그려진 흰 방패를 든 한 기사가 시합장으로 들어왔는데, 우연히 그가 가웨인 경 곁을 지나며 그를 어찌나 세게 쳤는지 투구를 쪼개고 머리에 상처를 입혀, 가웨인 경이 땅에 쓰러졌다."], ["When Sir Hector saw that, he knew that the knight with the white shield was Sir Galahad, and he thought it no wisdom to abide him, and also for natural love, that he was his uncle.", "헥터 경이 그것을 보고는 흰 방패를 든 기사가 갈라하드 경임을 알았고, 그에게 맞서는 것은 지혜롭지 못하다고 여겼는데, 또한 그가 자신의 조카라는 천륜의 정 때문이기도 했다."], ["Then Sir Galahad retired privily, so that none knew where he had gone.", "그러자 갈라하드 경은 아무도 그가 어디로 갔는지 모르게 슬며시 물러갔다."], ["And Sir Hector raised up Sir Gawain, and said, “Sir, me seemeth your quest is done.”", "헥터 경이 가웨인 경을 일으켜 세우며 말했다. \"경이여, 내가 보기에 그대의 탐색은 끝난 것 같소.\""], ["“It is done,”", "\"끝났소,\""], ["said Sir Gawain; “I shall seek no further.”", "가웨인 경이 말했다. \"나는 더는 찾지 않겠소.\""], ["Then Gawain was borne into the castle, and unarmed, and laid in a rich bed, and a leech found to search his wound.", "그러고는 가웨인 경이 성안으로 옮겨져 갑옷을 벗고 호화로운 침상에 뉘어졌으며, 그의 상처를 살필 의사가 불려 왔다."], ["And Sir Gawain and Sir Hector abode together, for Sir Hector would not away till Sir Gawain were whole.", "그리고 가웨인 경과 헥터 경은 함께 머물렀으니, 헥터 경이 가웨인 경이 다 나을 때까지 떠나려 하지 않았기 때문이다."]], [["————", "────"]], [["# SIR LAUNCELOT", "# SIR LAUNCELOT"]], [["THE SANGREAL (Continued)\nSIR LAUNCELOT rode overthwart and endlong in a wide forest, and held no path but as wild adventure led him.", "랜슬롯 경은 너른 숲을 가로지르고 또 세로질러 말을 달렸으되, 정해진 길을 따르지 않고 오로지 거친 모험이 이끄는 대로 나아갔다."]], [["“My golden spurs now bring to me, And bring to me my richest mail, For to-morrow I go over land and sea In search of the Holy, Holy Grail.", "나에게 황금 박차를 가져다 다오, 그리고 나에게 가장 값진 갑옷을 가져다 다오, 내일이면 나는 뭍과 바다를 건너 거룩하고 거룩한 성배를 찾아 떠날 터이니."], ["Shall never a bed for me be spread, Nor shall a pillow be under my head, Till I begin my vow to keep.", "나를 위하여 잠자리는 펼쳐지지 않으리, 내 머리 밑에 베개도 놓이지 않으리, 내 맹세를 지키기 시작하기 전까지는."], ["Here on the rushes will I sleep, And perchance there may come a vision true Ere day create the world anew.”", "여기 골풀 위에서 나는 잠들겠노라, 그리고 어쩌면 참된 환영이 찾아올지도 모르리, 날이 밝아 세상을 새로이 빚어내기 전에."], ["—Lowell’s Holy Grail.", "─로웰, 「성배」."]], [["And at last he came to a stone cross.", "마침내 그는 어느 돌 십자가에 이르렀다."], ["Then Sir Launcelot looked round him, and saw an old chapel.", "그러자 랜슬롯 경은 주위를 둘러보았고, 낡은 예배당 하나를 보았다."], ["So he tied his horse to a tree, and put off his shield, and hung it upon a tree; and then he went into the chapel, and looked through a place where the wall was broken.", "그리하여 그는 말을 나무에 매고, 방패를 풀어 나무에 걸어 두었다. 그러고 나서 예배당 안으로 들어가, 벽이 허물어진 곳을 통하여 안을 들여다보았다."], ["And within he saw a fair altar, full richly arrayed with cloth of silk; and there stood a fair candlestick, which bare six great candles, and the candlestick was of silver.", "안에는 비단 천으로 더없이 화려하게 꾸며진 아름다운 제단이 보였다. 그리고 그곳에는 여섯 자루의 큰 초를 받친 아름다운 촛대가 서 있었고, 그 촛대는 은으로 만들어진 것이었다."], ["When Sir Launcelot saw this sight, he had a great wish to enter the chapel, but he could find no place where he might enter.", "랜슬롯 경은 이 광경을 보고 예배당 안으로 들어가고 싶은 마음이 간절하였으나, 들어갈 만한 곳을 찾을 수 없었다."], ["Then was he passing heavy and dismayed.", "그리하여 그는 몹시 마음이 무겁고 낙담하였다."], ["And he returned and came again to his horse, and took off his saddle and his bridle, and let him pasture; and unlaced his helm, and ungirded his sword, and laid him down to sleep upon his shield before the cross.", "그는 발길을 돌려 다시 말에게로 와서, 안장과 굴레를 벗기고 풀을 뜯게 하였다. 그리고 투구의 끈을 풀고 검을 허리에서 끌러, 십자가 앞에서 방패를 베고 누워 잠을 청하였다."]], [["And as he lay, half waking and half sleeping, he saw come by him two palfreys, both fair and white, which bare a litter, on which lay a sick knight.", "그가 반쯤 깨고 반쯤 잠든 채로 누워 있을 때, 그의 곁으로 두 마리 작은 말이 지나가는 것이 보였는데, 둘 다 아름답고 흰빛이었으며, 가마 하나를 메고 있었고, 그 위에는 병든 기사 한 사람이 누워 있었다."], ["And when he was nigh the cross, he there abode still.", "그가 십자가 가까이 이르렀을 때, 그 자리에 멈추어 머물렀다."], ["And Sir Launcelot heard him say, “O sweet Lord, when shall this sorrow leave me, and when shall the holy vessel come by me whereby I shall be healed?”", "그리고 랜슬롯 경은 그가 이렇게 말하는 것을 들었다. \"오, 자비로우신 주여, 언제쯤 이 고통이 저를 떠나오리이까, 그리고 언제쯤 저를 낫게 하실 그 거룩한 그릇이 제 곁에 오리이까?\""], ["And thus a great while complained the knight, and Sir Launcelot heard it.", "이렇게 그 기사는 오랫동안 탄식하였고, 랜슬롯 경은 그것을 들었다."], ["Then Sir Launcelot saw the candlestick, with the lighted tapers, come before the cross, but he could see nobody that brought it.", "그때 랜슬롯 경은 불 켜진 가느다란 초들을 꽂은 촛대가 십자가 앞으로 다가오는 것을 보았으나, 그것을 가져오는 자는 아무도 보이지 않았다. 또한 은으로 된 쟁반 하나와 산그레알의 거룩한 그릇이 다가왔다."], ["Also there came a salver of silver and the holy vessel of the Sangreal; and therewithal the sick knight sat him upright, and held up both his hands, and said, “Fair, sweet Lord, which is here within the holy vessel, take heed to me, that I may be whole of this great malady.”", "그러자 그와 동시에 병든 기사가 몸을 일으켜 두 손을 들고 말하였다. \"아름답고 자비로우신 주여, 이 거룩한 그릇 안에 계신 분이시여, 저를 굽어살피시어, 이 크나큰 병에서 온전히 낫게 하소서.\""], ["And therewith, upon his hands and upon his knees, he went so nigh that he touched the holy vessel and kissed it.", "그러고 나서 그는 두 손과 두 무릎으로 기어가, 마침내 그 거룩한 그릇에 닿아 입을 맞추었다."], ["And anon he was whole.", "그러자 곧바로 그는 온전히 나았다."], ["Then the holy vessel went into the chapel again, with the candlestick and the light, so that Sir Launcelot wist not what became of it.", "그런 다음 그 거룩한 그릇은 촛대와 그 빛과 함께 다시 예배당 안으로 들어갔고, 랜슬롯 경은 그것이 어찌 되었는지 알지 못하였다."]], [["Then the sick knight rose up and kissed the cross; and anon his squire brought him his arms and asked his lord how he did.", "그러자 병든 기사가 일어나 십자가에 입을 맞추었다. 그리고 곧 그의 종자가 그에게 무구를 가져와, 주인에게 어떠하시냐고 물었다."], ["“I thank God right heartily,”", "\"진심으로 하느님께 감사하노라.\""], ["said he, “for, through the holy vessel, I am healed.", "그가 말하였다. \"이 거룩한 그릇을 통하여 내가 나았으니."], ["But I have great marvel of this sleeping knight, who hath had neither grace nor power to awake during the time that the holy vessel hath been here present.”", "허나 나는 이 잠든 기사가 몹시 기이하구나. 거룩한 그릇이 이곳에 머무는 동안 깨어날 은총도 힘도 얻지 못하였으니 말이다.\""], ["“I dare it right well say,”", "\"감히 분명하게 말씀드리건대,\""], ["said the squire, “that this same knight is stained with some manner of deadly sin, whereof he was never confessed.”", "종자가 말하였다. \"이 기사야말로 어떤 종류의 치명적인 죄로 더럽혀져 있되, 그것을 결코 고백한 적이 없는 자입니다.\""], ["So they departed.", "그리하여 그들은 떠나갔다."]], [["Then anon Sir Launcelot waked, and set himself upright, and bethought him of what he had seen and whether it were dreams or not.", "그러자 곧 랜슬롯 경이 깨어나 몸을 일으켜 앉았고, 자기가 본 것이 무엇이었는지, 그것이 꿈이었는지 아닌지를 곰곰이 생각하였다."], ["And he was passing heavy, and wist not what to do.", "그는 몹시 마음이 무거웠고, 어찌해야 할지를 알지 못하였다."], ["And he said: “My sin and my wretchedness hath brought me into great dishonor.", "그리고 그가 말하였다. \"내 죄와 내 비루함이 나를 크나큰 불명예에 빠뜨렸도다."], ["For when I sought worldly adventures and worldly desires, I ever achieved them, and had the better in every place, and never was I discomfited in any quarrel, were it right or wrong.", "내가 세속의 모험과 세속의 욕망을 좇았을 때에는 언제나 그것들을 이루었고, 어느 곳에서나 우위를 점하였으며, 옳든 그르든 어떤 싸움에서도 결코 패한 적이 없었노라."], ["And now I take upon me the adventure of holy things, I see and understand that mine old sin hindereth me, so that I had no power to stir nor to speak when the holy blood appeared before me.”", "그런데 이제 거룩한 일의 모험을 떠맡고 보니, 나의 오랜 죄가 나를 가로막아, 거룩한 피가 내 앞에 나타났을 때 움직일 힘도 말할 힘도 없었음을 보고 또 깨닫노라.\""], ["So thus he sorrowed till it was day, and heard the fowls of the air sing.", "이렇게 그는 날이 밝을 때까지 슬퍼하였고, 하늘의 새들이 노래하는 소리를 들었다."], ["Then was he somewhat comforted.", "그러자 그는 다소 위로를 받았다."]], [["Then he departed from the cross into the forest.", "그런 다음 그는 십자가를 떠나 숲속으로 들어갔다."], ["And there he found a hermitage, and a hermit therein, who was going to mass.", "그리고 그곳에서 은수처 하나와, 그 안에 미사를 드리러 가던 은수자 한 사람을 발견하였다."], ["So when mass was done Sir Launcelot called the hermit to him, and prayed him for charity to hear his confession.", "그리하여 미사가 끝나자 랜슬롯 경은 그 은수자를 불러, 자비를 베풀어 자기 고해를 들어 달라고 청하였다."], ["“With a good will,”", "\"기꺼이 그리하겠소.\""], ["said the good man.", "그 선량한 이가 말하였다."], ["And then he told that good man all his life, and how he had loved a queen unmeasurably many years.", "그러고 나서 그는 그 선량한 이에게 자기의 온 생애를 이야기하였고, 어떻게 자기가 어느 왕비를 여러 해 동안 한없이 사랑하였는지를 털어놓았다."], ["“And all my great deeds of arms that I have done I did the most part for the queen’s sake, and for her sake would I do battle, were it right or wrong, and never did I battle all only for God’s sake, but for to win worship, and to cause me to be better beloved; and little or naught I thanked God for it.", "\"그리고 내가 행한 그 모든 위대한 무공도, 대부분은 그 왕비를 위하여 한 것이었고, 그분을 위하여서라면 옳든 그르든 싸움에 나섰으며, 오로지 하느님만을 위하여 싸운 적은 한 번도 없었고, 다만 명예를 얻고자, 그리고 더욱 사랑받고자 하였을 따름이오. 그것에 대하여 하느님께 감사한 마음은 거의 없거나 아예 없었소."], ["I pray you counsel me.”", "부디 나에게 가르침을 주시오.\""]], [["“I will counsel you,”", "\"내 그대에게 가르침을 주리다.\""], ["said the hermit, “if ye will insure me that ye will never come in that queen’s fellowship as much as ye may forbear.”", "은수자가 말하였다. \"만일 그대가 힘닿는 한 그 왕비와의 사귐에 결코 다시 들지 않겠노라 나에게 다짐한다면 말이오.\""], ["And then Sir Launcelot promised the hermit, by his faith, that he would no more come in her company.", "그러자 랜슬롯 경은 자기 신앙을 걸고, 다시는 그분과 어울리지 않겠노라고 은수자에게 약속하였다."], ["“Look that your heart and your mouth accord,”", "\"그대의 마음과 그대의 입이 일치하도록 살피시오.\""], ["said the good man, “and I shall insure you that ye shall have more worship than ever ye had.”", "그 선량한 이가 말하였다. \"그리하면 내 그대에게 다짐하건대, 그대는 일찍이 누렸던 것보다 더 큰 명예를 누리게 되리다.\""]], [["Then the good man enjoined Sir Launcelot such penance as he might do, and he assailed Sir Launcelot and made him abide with him all that day.", "그러고 나서 그 선량한 이는 랜슬롯 경에게 그가 행할 수 있는 만큼의 고행을 부과하였고, 랜슬롯 경에게 청하여 그날 온종일 자기와 함께 머물게 하였다."], ["And Sir Launcelot repented him greatly.", "그리고 랜슬롯 경은 크게 뉘우쳤다."]], [["# SIR PERCEVAL", "# SIR PERCEVAL"]], [["Sir Perceval departed and rode till the hour of noon; and he met in a valley about twenty men of arms.", "페르세발 경은 길을 떠나 정오 무렵까지 말을 달렸다. 그러다가 어느 골짜기에서 무장한 사람 스무 명가량과 마주쳤다."], ["And when they saw Sir Perceval, they asked him whence he was; and he answered: “Of the court of King Arthur.”", "그들은 페르세발 경을 보자 그에게 어디서 왔느냐고 물었다. 그가 대답하였다."], ["Then they cried all at once, “Slay him.”", "\"아서 왕의 궁정에서 왔노라.\" 그러자 그들이 일제히 외쳤다."], ["But Sir Perceval smote the first to the earth, and his horse upon him.", "\"저자를 죽여라.\" 그러나 페르세발 경은 맨 앞의 사내를 땅에 쳐서 쓰러뜨렸고, 그의 말까지 그 위로 넘어뜨렸다."], ["Then seven of the knights smote upon his shield all at once, and the remnant slew his horse, so that he fell to the earth.", "그러자 일곱 기사가 한꺼번에 그의 방패를 내리쳤고, 나머지가 그의 말을 죽여, 그가 땅에 쓰러지게 하였다."], ["So had they slain him or taken him, had not the good knight Sir Galahad, with the red cross, come there by adventure.", "그리하여 만일 붉은 십자가를 단 선량한 기사 갤러해드 경이 모험길에 그곳을 지나가지 않았더라면, 그들은 그를 죽이거나 사로잡았을 것이다."], ["And when he saw all the knights upon one, he cried out, “Save me that knight’s life.”", "갤러해드 경은 모든 기사가 한 사람에게 달려드는 것을 보고 외쳤다. \"저 기사의 목숨을 살려 다오.\""], ["Then he rode toward the twenty men of arms as fast as his horse might drive, with his spear in the rest, and smote the foremost horse and man to the earth.", "그러고 나서 그는 창을 창받침에 대고, 말이 내달을 수 있는 한 빠르게 스무 명의 무장한 사람들을 향하여 달려, 맨 앞의 말과 사람을 땅에 쳐 쓰러뜨렸다. 그리고 창이 부러지자 그는 검에 손을 대어 오른쪽과 왼쪽을 내리쳤으니, 그 광경이 가히 놀라울 따름이었다."], ["And when his spear was broken, he set his hand to his sword, and smote on the right hand and on the left, that it was marvel to see; and at every stroke he smote down one, or put him to rebuke, so that they would fight no more, but fled to a thick forest, and Sir Galahad followed them.", "매번 휘두를 때마다 그는 한 사람씩 쳐 쓰러뜨리거나 물러나게 하였고, 마침내 그들은 더는 싸우려 하지 않고 울창한 숲으로 달아났으며, 갤러해드 경은 그들을 뒤쫓았다. 페르세발 경은 그가 그렇게 그들을 쫓는 것을 보고, 자기 말이 죽은 것을 크게 슬퍼하였다."], ["And when Sir Perceval saw him chase them so, he made great sorrow that his horse was slain.", "그리고 그는 그가 갤러해드 경임을 잘 알았다. 그리하여 그가 큰 소리로 외쳤다."], ["And he wist well it was Sir Galahad.", "\"아, 아름다운 기사여, 멈추시오, 그리고 내가 그대에게 감사를 드리게 해 주시오."], ["Then he cried aloud, “Ah, fair knight, abide, and suffer me to do thanks unto thee; for right well have ye done for me.”", "그대가 나를 위하여 참으로 훌륭히 싸워 주었으니.\" 그러나 갤러해드 경은 어찌나 빨리 말을 달렸던지 마침내 그의 시야에서 사라지고 말았다."], ["But Sir Galahad rode so fast that at last he passed out of his sight.", "페르세발 경은 그가 돌아서려 하지 않는 것을 보고 말하였다. \"이제 나는 참으로 비참한 자요, 다른 모든 기사 가운데 가장 불행한 자로구나.\""], ["When Sir Perceval saw that he would not turn, he said, “Now am I a very wretch, and most unhappy above all other knights.”", "그리하여 그는 슬픔에 잠긴 채 밤이 될 때까지 그날 온종일 머물렀다. 그러고 나서 그는 기진하여 몸을 누이고 한밤중까지 잠들었다."], ["So in his sorrow he abode all that day till it was night; and then he was faint, and laid him down and slept till midnight; and then he awaked and saw before him a woman, who said unto him, “Sir Perceval, what dost thou here?”", "그러다가 그는 깨어나 자기 앞에 한 여인이 서 있는 것을 보았는데, 그 여인이 그에게 말하였다. \"페르세발 경이여, 그대는 여기서 무엇을 하고 있는가?\""], ["He answered, “I do neither good, nor great ill.”", "그가 대답하였다. \"나는 좋은 일도, 큰 나쁜 일도 하고 있지 않소.\""], ["“If thou wilt promise me,”", "\"만일 그대가,\" 그 여인이 말하였다."], ["said she, “that thou wilt fulfil my will when I summon thee, I will lend thee my own horse, which shall bear thee whither thou wilt.”", "\"내가 그대를 부를 때 내 뜻을 이루어 주겠노라고 나에게 약속한다면, 나는 그대에게 내 말을 빌려주리니, 그 말이 그대가 가고자 하는 곳 어디로든 그대를 데려다 주리라.\""], ["Sir Perceval was glad of her proffer, and insured her to fulfil all her desire.", "페르세발 경은 그 여인의 제안을 기뻐하며, 그 여인의 모든 바람을 이루어 주겠노라고 다짐하였다."], ["“Then abide me here, and I will go fetch you a horse.”", "\"그러면 여기서 나를 기다리시오, 내가 가서 그대에게 말 한 마리를 가져오리다.\""], ["And so she soon came again, and brought a horse with her that was inky black.", "그리하여 그 여인은 곧 다시 와서, 먹빛처럼 검은 말 한 마리를 데려왔다."], ["When Perceval beheld that horse he marvelled, it was so great and so well apparelled.", "페르세발은 그 말을 보고 경탄하였으니, 어찌나 크고 어찌나 잘 차려 입혀졌던지 그러하였다."], ["And he leapt upon him and took no heed of himself.", "그리고 그는 자기 자신을 돌아보지도 않고 그 위로 뛰어올랐다."], ["And he thrust him with his spurs, and within an hour and less he bare him four days’ journey thence, until he came to a rough water, which roared, and his horse would have borne him into it.", "그가 박차를 가하자, 한 시간도 채 못 되어 그 말은 그를 그곳에서 나흘 길이나 데려갔고, 마침내 그는 으르렁대며 흐르는 거친 물가에 이르렀는데, 그의 말은 그를 그 물속으로 몰고 들어가려 하였다."], ["And when Sir Perceval came nigh the brim and saw the water so boisterous he doubted to overpass it.", "페르세발 경이 물가에 가까이 이르러 물이 그토록 사납게 날뛰는 것을 보고는 그것을 건너기를 주저하였다."], ["And then he made the sign of the cross on his forehead.", "그러고 나서 그는 자기 이마에 십자 표시를 그었다."], ["When the fiend felt him so charged, he shook off Sir Perceval, and went into the water crying and roaring; and it seemed unto him that the water burned.", "그 마귀는 그가 그렇게 십자가로 무장한 것을 느끼자, 페르세발 경을 떨쳐 버리고 울부짖고 으르렁대며 물속으로 들어갔으며, 그에게는 그 물이 불타는 것처럼 보였다."], ["Then Sir Perceval perceived it was a fiend that would have brought him unto his perdition.", "그제야 페르세발 경은 그것이 자기를 멸망으로 이끌려 한 마귀였음을 알아차렸다."], ["Then he commended himself unto God, and prayed our Lord to keep him from all such temptations; and so he prayed all that night till it was day.", "그리하여 그는 자신을 하느님께 의탁하며, 우리 주께 자기를 그러한 모든 유혹으로부터 지켜 주시기를 기도하였다. 그리고 그는 날이 밝을 때까지 그날 밤 내내 그렇게 기도하였다."], ["Then he saw that he was in a wild place, that was closed with the sea nigh all about.", "그제야 그는 자기가 거의 사방이 바다로 둘러싸인 황량한 곳에 있음을 보았다."], ["And Sir Perceval looked forth over the sea, and saw a ship come sailing towards him; and it came and stood still under the rock.", "그리고 페르세발 경이 바다 너머를 바라보니, 배 한 척이 자기를 향하여 항해해 오는 것이 보였다. 그 배는 다가와 바위 아래에 가만히 멈추어 섰다."], ["And when Sir Perceval saw this, he hied him thither, and found the ship covered with silk; and therein was a lady of great beauty, and clothed so richly that none might be better.", "페르세발 경은 이것을 보고 서둘러 그곳으로 갔고, 비단으로 덮인 배를 발견하였다. 그리고 그 안에는 더없이 아름다운 귀부인이 있었는데, 그보다 더 화려할 수 없을 만큼 호화롭게 차려입고 있었다."]], [["And when she saw Sir Perceval, she saluted him, and Sir Perceval returned her salutation.", "그 귀부인은 페르세발 경을 보자 그에게 인사하였고, 페르세발 경도 그 인사에 답하였다."], ["Then he asked her of her country and her lineage.", "그러고 나서 그는 그 여인에게 고향과 가문을 물었다."], ["And she said, “I am a gentlewoman that am disinherited, and was once the richest woman of the world.”", "그러자 그 여인이 말하였다. \"나는 상속권을 빼앗긴 귀부인으로, 한때는 세상에서 가장 부유한 여인이었나이다.\""], ["“Damsel,”", "\"아가씨여,\""], ["said Sir Perceval, “who hath disinherited you?", "페르세발 경이 말하였다. \"누가 그대의 상속권을 빼앗았소?"], ["for I have great pity of you.”", "내 그대를 크게 가엾이 여기노라.\""], ["“Sir,”", "\"기사님,\""], ["said she, “my enemy is a great and powerful lord, and aforetime he made much of me, so that of his favor and of my beauty I had a little pride more than I ought to have had.", "그 여인이 말하였다. \"제 원수는 위대하고 강력한 군주인데, 예전에는 저를 무척 아꼈으니, 그분의 총애와 제 아름다움으로 인하여 저는 마땅한 정도보다 조금 더 자만하게 되었나이다."], ["Also I said a word that pleased him not.", "또한 저는 그분의 마음에 들지 않는 말을 한마디 하였습니다."], ["So he drove me from his company and from mine heritage.", "그리하여 그분은 저를 자기 곁에서, 그리고 제 유산에서 내쫓았나이다."], ["Therefore I know no good knight nor good man, but I get him on my side if I may.", "그러므로 저는 선량한 기사나 선량한 사내를 알게 되면, 할 수만 있다면 제 편으로 끌어들이옵니다."], ["And for that I know that thou art a good knight, I beseech thee to help me.”", "그리고 그대가 선량한 기사임을 알기에, 부디 저를 도와주시기를 간청하나이다.\""]], [["Then Sir Perceval promised her all the help that he might, and she thanked him.", "그러자 페르세발 경은 자기가 베풀 수 있는 모든 도움을 그 여인에게 약속하였고, 그 여인은 그에게 감사하였다."]], [["And at that time the weather was hot, and she called to her a gentlewoman, and bade her bring forth a pavilion.", "그리고 그때 날씨가 무더웠으므로, 그 여인은 한 시녀를 불러 천막을 가져오라고 일렀다."], ["And she did so, and pitched it upon the gravel.", "그러자 시녀가 그리하여, 자갈밭 위에 천막을 쳤다."], ["“Sir,”", "\"기사님,\""], ["said she, “now may ye rest you in this heat of the day.”", "그 여인이 말하였다. \"이제 이 한낮의 더위 속에서 쉬소서.\""], ["Then he thanked her, and she put off his helm and his shield, and there he slept a great while.", "그러자 그는 그 여인에게 감사하였고, 여인은 그의 투구와 방패를 벗겨 주었으며, 그곳에서 그는 한참 동안 잠을 잤다."], ["Then he awoke, and asked her if she had any meat, and she said yea, and so there was set upon the table all manner of meats that he could think on.", "그러다가 그는 깨어나 그 여인에게 먹을 것이 있느냐고 물었고, 여인은 그렇다고 대답하였으며, 그리하여 식탁 위에는 그가 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 음식이 차려졌다."], ["Also he drank there the strongest wine that ever he drank, and therewith he was a little chafed more than he ought to be.", "또한 그는 그곳에서 일찍이 마셔 본 가장 독한 술을 마셨고, 그로 인하여 마땅한 정도보다 조금 더 달아올랐다. 그러면서 그는 그 귀부인을 바라보았는데, 일찍이 본 가장 아름다운 사람이라고 여겼다."], ["With that he beheld the lady, and he thought she was the fairest creature that ever he saw.", "그러고 나서 페르세발 경은 그 여인에게 사랑을 청하며, 자기 사람이 되어 달라고 간청하였다."], ["And then Sir Perceval proffered her love, and prayed her that she would be his.", "그러자 그 여인은 어느 정도 그를 거절하였으니, 그가 자기에게 더욱 애타게 매달리도록 하기 위함이었고, 그는 끊임없이 그 여인에게 사랑을 간청하였다."], ["Then she refused him in a manner, for the cause he should be the more ardent on her, and ever he ceased not to pray her of love.", "그리고 그 여인은 그가 잔뜩 달아오른 것을 보고 말하였다. \"페르세발 경이여, 잘 들으소서."], ["And when she saw him well enchafed, then she said, “Sir Perceval, wit you well I shall not give ye my love, unless you swear from henceforth you will be my true servant, and do no thing but that I shall command you.", "저는 그대에게 제 사랑을 주지 않겠나이다. 그대가 지금부터 저의 충실한 종이 되어, 제가 명하는 것 외에는 아무 일도 하지 않겠노라 맹세하지 않는 한은 말입니다."], ["Will you insure me this, as ye be a true knight?”", "진정한 기사로서 이를 제게 다짐하시겠나이까?\" \"그리하리다,\""], ["“Yea,”", "그가 말하였다."], ["said he, “fair lady, by the faith of my body.”", "\"아름다운 여인이여, 내 몸의 신앙을 걸고.\""], ["And as he said this, by adventure and grace, he saw his sword lie on the ground naked, in whose pommel was a red cross, and the sign of the crucifix thereon.", "그리고 그가 이 말을 하는 순간, 우연이자 은총으로, 그는 자기 검이 칼집 없이 땅에 놓여 있는 것을 보았는데, 그 손잡이 머리에는 붉은 십자가가, 그리고 그 위에 십자가에 못 박히신 분의 표징이 있었다."], ["Then he made the sign of the cross on his forehead, and therewith the pavilion shrivelled up, and changed into a smoke and a black cloud.", "그러자 그는 자기 이마에 십자 표시를 그었고, 그와 동시에 천막은 오그라들어 연기와 검은 구름으로 변하였다."], ["And the damsel cried aloud, and hasted into the ship, and so she went with the wind roaring and yelling that it seemed all the water burned after her.", "그리고 그 아가씨는 큰 소리로 외치며 서둘러 배로 들어갔고, 그리하여 그 여인은 으르렁대고 울부짖는 바람과 함께 떠나갔으니, 그 여인의 뒤로 온 바닷물이 불타는 것처럼 보였다."], ["Then Sir Perceval made great sorrow, and called himself a wretch, saying, “How nigh was I lost!”", "그러자 페르세발 경은 크게 슬퍼하며 자신을 비참한 자라 일컫고 말하였다. \"내가 얼마나 멸망에 가까웠던가!\""], ["Then he took his arms, and departed thence.", "그러고 나서 그는 자기 무구를 챙겨, 그곳을 떠나갔다."]], [["————", "────"]], [["# SIR BOHORT", "# SIR BOHORT"]], [["THE SANGREAL (Continued)\nWHEN Sir Bohort departed from Camelot he met with a religious man, riding upon an ass; and Sir Bohort saluted him.", "보호트 경이 카멜롯을 떠난 뒤, 그는 당나귀를 타고 가는 어느 수도자를 만났다. 보호트 경이 그에게 인사를 건넸다."], ["“What are ye?”", "\"그대는 누구신가?\""], ["said the good man.", "그 선한 사람이 물었다."], ["“Sir,”", "\"선생님,\""], ["said Sir Bohort, “I am a knight that fain would be counselled in the quest of the Sangreal.”", "보호트 경이 말했다. \"저는 성배를 찾는 일에 관해 기꺼이 조언을 구하고자 하는 기사입니다.\""], ["So rode they both together till they came to a hermitage; and there he prayed Sir Bohort to dwell that night with him.", "두 사람은 함께 말을 타고 어느 은수처에 이를 때까지 갔다. 그곳에서 그는 보호트 경에게 그날 밤을 자기와 함께 머물기를 청했다."], ["So he alighted, and put away his armor, and prayed him that he might be confessed.", "그리하여 보호트 경은 말에서 내려 갑옷을 벗어 두고, 고해를 할 수 있게 해 달라고 청했다."], ["And they went both into the chapel, and there he was clean confessed.", "두 사람은 함께 예배당으로 들어갔고, 그곳에서 그는 정결하게 고해를 마쳤다."], ["And they ate bread and drank water together.", "그리고 그들은 함께 빵을 먹고 물을 마셨다."], ["“Now,”", "\"이제,\""], ["said the good man, “I pray thee that thou eat none other till thou sit at the table where the Sangreal shall be.”", "그 선한 사람이 말했다. \"내 그대에게 청하노니, 성배가 놓일 식탁에 그대가 앉을 때까지는 다른 어떤 것도 먹지 마시오.\""], ["“Sir,”", "\"선생님,\""], ["said Sir Bohort, “but how know ye that I shall sit there?”", "보호트 경이 말했다. \"그런데 제가 그곳에 앉게 되리란 것을 어찌 아십니까?\""], ["“Yea,”", "\"그렇소,\""], ["said the good man, “that I know well; but there shall be few of your fellows with you.”", "그 선한 사람이 말했다. \"그것을 나는 잘 알고 있소."], ["Then said Sir Bohort, “I agree me thereto.”", "다만 그대와 함께할 동료는 몇 되지 않을 것이오.\" 그러자 보호트 경이 말했다."], ["And the good man when he had heard his confession found him in so pure a life and so stable that he marvelled thereof.", "\"그 말씀에 따르겠습니다.\" 그리고 그 선한 사람은 그의 고해를 듣고 나서, 그가 그토록 정결하고 그토록 굳건한 삶을 살고 있음을 알고는 그것을 경탄해 마지않았다."]], [["On the morrow, as soon as the day appeared, Sir Bohort departed thence, and rode into a forest unto the hour of midday.", "이튿날 날이 밝자마자 보호트 경은 그곳을 떠나 한낮이 되도록 어느 숲속을 달렸다. 그리고 그곳에서 그는 놀라운 모험을 맞이했다."], ["And there befell him a marvellous adventure.", "두 갈래로 갈라지는 길목에서 그는 두 명의 기사를 만났는데, 그들은 그의 형 리오넬 경을 발가벗긴 채 튼튼한 말에 묶어 끌고 가고 있었다."], ["For he met, at the parting of two ways, two knights that led Sir Lionel, his brother, all naked, bound upon a strong hackney, and his hands bound before his breast; and each of them held in his hand thorns wherewith they went beating him, so that he was all bloody before and behind; but he said never a word, but, as he was great of heart, he suffered all that they did to him as though he had felt none anguish.", "형의 두 손은 가슴 앞에 결박되어 있었고, 그들은 저마다 손에 가시나무를 들고 그를 후려치고 있어, 형의 앞과 뒤는 온통 피투성이였다. 그러나 형은 한마디도 하지 않았다."], ["Sir Bohort prepared to rescue his brother.", "마음이 큰 사람이었기에, 마치 아무 고통도 느끼지 못하는 것처럼 그들이 자기에게 하는 모든 일을 견디고 있었다. 보호트 경은 형을 구하려고 채비했다."], ["But he looked on the other side of him, and saw a knight dragging along a fair gentlewoman, who cried out, “Saint Mary!", "그러나 그가 다른 쪽으로 눈을 돌리자, 한 기사가 아리따운 귀부인 한 사람을 끌고 가고 있었다. 그 여인은 \"성모 마리아여!"], ["succor your maid!”", "당신의 종을 구원하소서!\" 하고 외치고 있었다."], ["And when she saw Sir Bohort, she called to him, and said, “By the faith that ye owe to knighthood, help me!”", "그리고 보호트 경을 보자, 여인은 그를 불러 말했다. \"기사도에 마땅히 지키셔야 할 신의를 두고 비오니, 저를 도와주소서!\""], ["When Sir Bohort heard her say thus he had such sorrow that he wist not what to do.", "보호트 경은 여인이 이렇게 말하는 것을 듣고 어찌해야 할지 알 수 없을 만큼 큰 시름에 잠겼다. \"내 형을 그대로 둔다면 형은 틀림없이 죽임을 당할 터인데, 그것은 온 세상을 준다 해도 내가 바라지 않는 일이다."], ["“For if I let my brother be he must be slain, and that would I not for all the earth; and if I help not the maid I am shamed for ever.”", "그러나 이 여인을 돕지 않는다면 나는 영원히 수치를 입게 될 것이다.\" 그러고는 눈을 들어 올리며 울면서 말했다."], ["Then lift he up his eyes and said, weeping, “Fair Lord, whose liegeman I am, keep Sir Lionel, my brother, that none of these knights slay him, and for pity of you, and our Lady’s sake, I shall succor this maid.”", "\"제가 충성을 바치는 어지신 주님이시여, 제 형 리오넬 경을 지켜 주시어 저 기사들 중 누구도 형을 죽이지 못하게 하소서. 그리하면 저는 주님을 향한 측은함과 성모님을 위하여 이 여인을 구하겠나이다.\""]], [["Then he cried out to the knight, “Sir knight, lay your hand off that maid, or else ye be but dead.”", "그러고는 그 기사에게 소리쳤다. \"기사여, 그 여인에게서 손을 떼시오."], ["Then the knight set down the maid, and took his shield, and drew out his sword.", "그러지 않으면 그대는 죽은 목숨이오.\" 그러자 그 기사는 여인을 내려놓고 방패를 잡고 검을 빼들었다."], ["And Sir Bohort smote him so hard that it went through his shield and habergeon, on the left shoulder, and he fell down to the earth.", "보호트 경은 그를 어찌나 세게 내리쳤던지 그 일격이 방패와 사슬갑옷을 뚫고 왼쪽 어깨에 박혔고, 그는 땅바닥에 쓰러졌다."], ["Then came Sir Bohort to the maid, “Ye be delivered of this knight this time.”", "그러자 보호트 경이 여인에게 다가왔다. \"이번에는 그대가 이 기사에게서 벗어났소.\""], ["“Now,”", "\"이제,\""], ["said she, “I pray you lead me there where this knight took me.”", "여인이 말했다. \"부디 저를 이 기사가 저를 끌어온 그곳으로 데려다주소서.\""], ["“I shall gladly do it,”", "\"기꺼이 그리하리다.\""], ["said Sir Bohort.", "보호트 경이 말했다."], ["So he took the horse of the wounded knight, and set the gentlewoman upon it, and brought her there where she desired to be.", "그리하여 그는 상처 입은 기사의 말을 끌어와 그 위에 귀부인을 태우고, 여인이 가고자 하는 곳으로 데려다주었다. 그리고 그곳에서 그는 여인을 찾고 있던 열두 명의 기사를 만났다."], ["And there he found twelve knights seeking after her; and when she told them how Sir Bohort had delivered her, they made great joy, and besought him to come to her father, a great lord, and he should be right welcomed.", "여인이 그들에게 보호트 경이 자기를 어떻게 구해 주었는지를 일러 주자, 그들은 크게 기뻐하며, 위대한 영주인 여인의 아버지에게로 와 달라고 간청했다. 그러면 그가 마땅히 환대를 받으리라는 것이었다."], ["“Truly,”", "\"참으로,\""], ["said Sir Bohort, “that may not be; for I have a great adventure to do.”", "보호트 경이 말했다. \"그럴 수는 없소."], ["So he commended them to God and departed.", "내게는 행해야 할 큰 모험이 있기 때문이오.\" 그리하여 그는 그들을 하느님께 맡기고 떠났다."]], [["Then Sir Bohort rode after Sir Lionel, his brother, by the trace of their horses.", "그러고 나서 보호트 경은 말발굽 자국을 따라 형 리오넬 경의 뒤를 쫓았다."], ["Thus he rode seeking, a great while.", "이렇게 그는 오랫동안 찾아 헤매며 말을 달렸다."], ["Then he overtook a man clothed in a religious clothing, who said, “Sir Knight, what seek ye?”", "그러다가 그는 수도자의 옷을 입은 한 사람을 따라잡았는데, 그 사람이 말했다. \"기사여, 무엇을 찾고 계시오?\""], ["“Sir,”", "\"선생님,\""], ["said Sir Bohort, “I seek my brother, that I saw within a little space beaten of two knights.”", "보호트 경이 말했다. \"저는 제 형을 찾고 있습니다."], ["“Ah, Sir Bohort, trouble not thyself to seek for him, for truly he is dead.”", "조금 전 두 기사에게 매를 맞고 있는 것을 보았습니다.\" \"아, 보호트 경, 형을 찾으려고 애쓰지 마시오."], ["Then he showed him a new-slain body, lying in a thick bush; and it seemed him that it was the body of Sir Lionel.", "그는 정녕 죽었기 때문이오.\" 그러고는 빽빽한 덤불 속에 누워 있는, 갓 살해당한 시신 하나를 그에게 보여 주었다."], ["And then he made such sorrow that he fell to the ground in a swoon, and lay there long.", "그것은 보호트 경에게 리오넬 경의 시신으로 보였다. 그러자 그는 너무도 큰 시름에 빠져 정신을 잃고 땅바닥에 쓰러졌고, 그곳에 오래도록 누워 있었다."], ["And when he came to himself again, he said, “Fair brother, since the fellowship of you and me is sundered, shall I never have joy again; and now He that I have taken for my Master, He be my help!”", "그리고 다시 정신을 차렸을 때, 그가 말했다. \"어진 형이여, 그대와 나의 동기간이 끊어졌으니, 나는 다시는 기쁨을 누리지 못하리라."], ["And when he had said thus he took up the body in his arms, and put it upon the horse.", "이제 내가 주님으로 모신 그분께서 나를 도와주시기를!\" 이렇게 말한 뒤 그는 두 팔로 시신을 들어 올려 말 위에 얹었다."], ["And then he said to the man, “Canst thou tell me the way to some chapel, where I may bury this body?”", "그리고 그 사람에게 말했다. \"이 시신을 묻을 수 있는 예배당으로 가는 길을 일러 줄 수 있겠소?\""], ["“Come on,”", "\"이리 오시오,\""], ["said the man, “here is one fast by.”", "그 사람이 말했다. \"바로 가까이에 하나 있소.\""], ["And so they rode till they saw a fair tower, and beside it a chapel.", "그리하여 그들은 아리따운 탑 하나가 보일 때까지 말을 달렸고, 그 옆에는 예배당이 있었다."], ["Then they alighted both, and put the body into a tomb of marble.", "그러자 두 사람은 모두 말에서 내려 시신을 대리석 무덤에 안치했다."]], [["Then Sir Bohort commended the good man unto God, and departed.", "그러고 나서 보호트 경은 그 선한 사람을 하느님께 맡기고 떠났다. 그는 온종일 말을 달려 어느 노부인의 집에 묵었다."], ["And he rode all that day, and harbored with an old lady.", "그리고 이튿날 그는 어느 골짜기에 있는 성으로 말을 달렸고, 그곳에서 한 시종을 만났다. \"말해 보오,\""], ["And on the morrow he rode unto the castle in a valley, and there he met with a yeoman.", "보호트 경이 말했다. \"혹 어떤 모험에 관해 아는 것이 있소?\""], ["“Tell me,”", "\"기사 어른,\" 그가 말했다."], ["said Sir Bohort, “knowest thou of any adventure?” “Sir,”", "\"이 성 아래에서 크고 놀라운 마상 시합이 열릴 것입니다.\""], ["said he, “here shall be, under this castle, a great and marvellous tournament.”", "그러자 보호트 경은, 성배를 찾는 길에 나선 동료들 가운데 누구라도 만날 수 있을까 하여 그곳에 가 볼 생각이었다. 그래서 그는 숲의 어귀에 있는 어느 은수처로 향했다."], ["Then Sir Bohort thought to be there, if he might meet with any of the fellowship that were in quest of the Sangreal; so he turned to a hermitage that was on the border of the forest.", "그곳에 다다랐을 때, 그는 예배당 문 어귀에 온통 무장한 채 앉아 있는 형 리오넬 경을 발견했다. 보호트 경은 형을 보고 크게 기뻐하며 말에서 내려 말했다."], ["And when he was come hither, he found there Sir Lionel his brother, who sat all armed at the entry of the chapel door.", "\"어진 형이여, 언제 이곳에 오셨소?\" 리오넬 경은 그를 보자마자 말했다."], ["And when Sir Bohort saw him, he had great joy, and he alighted off his horse, and said, “Fair brother, when came ye hither?”", "\"아, 보호트, 거짓된 시늉일랑 하지 마라. 너로 인해 나는 죽임을 당할 뻔했다."], ["As soon as Sir Lionel saw him he said, “Ah, Sir Bohort, make ye no false show, for, as for you, I might have been slain, for ye left me in peril of death to go succor a gentlewoman; and for that misdeed I now assure you but death, for ye have right well deserved it.”", "네가 귀부인을 구하러 가느라 나를 죽음의 위험 속에 내버려 두었기 때문이다. 그 악행의 대가로 나는 이제 네게 죽음 외에는 아무것도 약속하지 않겠다."], ["When Sir Bohort perceived his brother’s wrath he kneeled down to the earth and cried him mercy, holding up both his hands, and prayed him to forgive him.", "너는 마땅히 그것을 받아야 하기 때문이다.\" 보호트 경은 형의 분노를 알아차리고 땅에 무릎을 꿇고 두 손을 들어 올리며 자비를 빌었고, 자기를 용서해 달라고 간청했다."], ["“Nay,”", "\"아니다,\""], ["said Sir Lionel, “thou shalt have but death for it, if I have the upper hand; therefore leap upon thy horse and keep thyself, and if thou do not I will run upon thee there as thou standest on foot, and so the shame shall be mine, and the harm thine, but of that I reck not.”", "리오넬 경이 말했다. \"내가 우위를 점한다면 너는 그 대가로 죽음만을 얻게 되리라."], ["When Sir Bohort saw that he must fight with his brother or else die, he wist not what to do.", "그러니 말 위에 뛰어올라 너 자신을 지켜라. 그러지 않으면 네가 발로 선 채 그대로 있는 그곳을 향해 내가 달려들 것이다."], ["Then his heart counselled him not so to do, inasmuch as Sir Lionel was his elder brother, wherefore he ought to bear him reverence.", "그리하면 수치는 내 몫이요 해악은 네 몫이 되겠지만, 그런 것은 내 알 바 아니다.\" 보호트 경은 형과 싸우든지 아니면 죽든지 해야만 한다는 것을 알았으나, 어찌해야 할지 알 수 없었다."], ["Yet kneeled he down before Sir Lionel’s horse’s feet, and said, “Fair brother, have mercy upon me and slay me not.”", "그의 마음은 그리하지 말라고 일렀으니, 리오넬 경은 그의 형이었으므로 마땅히 공경을 바쳐야 했기 때문이다."], ["But Sir Lionel cared not, for the fiend had brought him in such a will that he should slay him.", "그래도 그는 리오넬 경의 말발굽 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 말했다. \"어진 형이여, 부디 저를 가엾이 여기시어 죽이지 마소서.\""], ["When he saw that Sir Bohort would not rise to give him battle, he rushed over him, so that he smote him with his horse’s feet to the earth, and hurt him sore, that he swooned of distress.", "그러나 리오넬 경은 아랑곳하지 않았으니, 악마가 그의 마음을 동생을 죽이려는 쪽으로 몰아갔기 때문이다. 보호트 경이 일어나 싸우려 하지 않는 것을 보자, 그는 동생을 향해 돌진하여 말발굽으로 그를 땅바닥에 내리쳤고, 그를 심하게 다치게 하여 동생은 괴로움에 정신을 잃었다."], ["When Sir Lionel saw this he alighted from his horse for to have smitten off his head; and so he took him by the helm, and would have rent it from his head.", "리오넬 경은 이를 보고 동생의 목을 베려고 말에서 내렸다. 그러고는 투구를 잡고 동생의 머리에서 그것을 벗겨내려 했다."], ["But it happened that Sir Colgrevance, a knight of the Round Table, came at that time thither, as it was our Lord’s will; and then he beheld how Sir Lionel would have slain his brother, and he knew Sir Bohort, whom he loved right well.", "그러나 마침 그때, 우리 주님의 뜻에 따라 원탁의 기사인 콜그레반스 경이 그곳에 이르렀다. 그리고 그는 리오넬 경이 동생을 죽이려는 것을 보았고, 자기가 무척이나 아끼던 보호트 경을 알아보았다."]], [["Then leapt he down from his horse and took Sir Lionel by the shoulders, and drew him strongly back from Sir Bohort, and said, “Sir Lionel, will ye slay your brother?”", "그러자 그는 말에서 뛰어내려 리오넬 경의 어깨를 붙잡고 보호트 경에게서 힘껏 뒤로 끌어당기며 말했다. \"리오넬 경, 그대는 형제를 죽이려 하시오?\""], ["“Why,”", "\"어찌하여,\""], ["said Sir Lionel, “will ye stay me?", "리오넬 경이 말했다. \"그대는 나를 막으려는 것이오?"], ["If ye interfere in this I will slay you, and him after.”", "그대가 이 일에 끼어든다면 내 그대를 죽이고, 그다음에 저놈을 죽이겠소.\""], ["Then he ran upon Sir Bohort, and would have smitten him; but Sir Colgrevance ran between them, and said, “If ye persist to do so any more, we two shall meddle together.”", "그러고는 보호트 경을 향해 달려들어 내리치려 했으나, 콜그레반스 경이 두 사람 사이로 뛰어들어 말했다. \"그대가 더 이상 그리한다면, 우리 둘이 맞붙게 될 것이오.\""], ["Then Sir Lionel defied him, and gave him a great stroke through the helm.", "그러자 리오넬 경은 그에게 도전하며 투구를 뚫는 강한 일격을 가했다."], ["Then he drew his sword, for he was a passing good knight, and defended himself right manfully.", "그러자 그는 검을 빼들었으니, 그는 빼어나게 훌륭한 기사였으므로 사내답게 자신을 지켜냈다."], ["So long endured the battle, that Sir Bohort rose up all anguishly, and beheld Sir Colgrevance, the good knight, fight with his brother for his quarrel.", "싸움이 어찌나 오래 이어졌던지, 보호트 경은 온통 괴로워하며 몸을 일으켜, 훌륭한 기사 콜그레반스 경이 자기를 위한 싸움에서 형과 맞서 싸우는 것을 바라보게 되었다."], ["Then was he full sorry and heavy, and thought that if Sir Colgrevance slew him that was his brother he should never have joy, and if his brother slew Sir Colgrevance the shame should ever be his.", "그러자 그는 한없이 슬프고 무거운 마음에 잠겨, 만일 콜그레반스 경이 자기 형을 죽인다면 자기는 결코 기쁨을 누리지 못하리라, 그리고 형이 콜그레반스 경을 죽인다면 그 수치는 영원히 자기 몫이 되리라 생각했다."]], [["Then would he have risen for to have parted them, but he had not so much strength to stand on his feet; so he staid so long that Sir Colgrevance had the worse; for Sir Lionel was of great chivalry and right hardy.", "그러고는 그가 일어나 두 사람을 떼어 놓으려 했으나, 발로 설 만한 힘조차 없었다. 그리하여 그가 그토록 오래 머뭇거리는 사이에 콜그레반스 경이 열세에 몰렸으니, 리오넬 경은 무용이 뛰어나고 무척이나 용맹했기 때문이다."], ["Then cried Sir Colgrevance, “Ah, Sir Bohort, why come ye not to bring me out of peril of death, wherein I have put me to succor you?”", "그러자 콜그레반스 경이 외쳤다. \"아, 보호트 경, 어찌하여 나를 죽음의 위험에서 구하러 오지 않으시오?"], ["With that, Sir Lionel smote off his helm and bore him to the earth.", "나는 그대를 돕고자 이 위험에 빠졌건만.\" 그 순간 리오넬 경이 그의 투구를 쳐서 떨어뜨리고 그를 땅에 쓰러뜨렸다."], ["And when he had slain Sir Colgrevance he ran upon his brother as a fiendly man, and gave him such a stroke that he made him stoop.", "그리고 콜그레반스 경을 죽이고 나서, 그는 악마에 들린 사람처럼 동생에게 달려들어 어찌나 세게 내리쳤던지 동생을 휘청거리게 만들었다. 겸손함으로 가득한 동생은 그에게 빌었다."], ["And he that was full of humility prayed him, “for God’s sake leave this battle, for if it befell, fair brother, that I slew you, or ye me, we should be dead of that sin.”", "\"하느님을 위하여 이 싸움을 그만두소서. 어진 형이여, 만일 제가 형을 죽이거나, 혹은 형이 저를 죽이는 일이 벌어진다면 우리는 그 죄로 인해 죽은 목숨이 될 것입니다.\""], ["“Pray ye not me for mercy,”", "\"내게 자비를 구하지 마라.\""], ["said Sir Lionel.", "리오넬 경이 말했다."], ["Then Sir Bohort, all weeping, drew his sword, and said, “Now God have mercy upon me, though I defend my life against my brother.”", "그러자 보호트 경은 온통 울면서 검을 빼들고 말했다. \"이제 하느님께서 저를 가엾이 여기소서."], ["With that Sir Bohort lifted up his sword, and would have smitten his brother.", "비록 제가 형에게서 제 목숨을 지킨다 할지라도.\" 그 말과 함께 보호트 경은 검을 들어 올려 형을 내리치려 했다."], ["Then he heard a voice that said, “Flee, Sir Bohort, and touch him not.”", "그때 그는 한 목소리가 이렇게 말하는 것을 들었다."], ["Right so alighted a cloud between them, in the likeness of a fire and a marvellous flame, so that they both fell to the earth, and lay there a great while in a swoon.", "\"달아나라, 보호트 경, 그에게 손대지 마라.\" 바로 그때 두 사람 사이로 구름 하나가 불길과 놀라운 화염의 형상으로 내려앉아, 그들 둘 다 땅바닥에 쓰러져 오래도록 정신을 잃고 누워 있었다."], ["And when they came to themselves, Sir Bohort saw that his brother had no harm; and he was right glad, for he dread sore that God had taken vengeance upon him.", "그리고 정신을 차렸을 때, 보호트 경은 형이 아무런 해도 입지 않았음을 보았다. 그는 무척이나 기뻤으니, 하느님께서 형에게 복수하셨을까 몹시 두려워했기 때문이다."], ["Then Sir Lionel said to his brother, “Brother, forgive me, for God’s sake, all that I have trespassed against you.”", "그러자 리오넬 경이 동생에게 말했다. \"아우야, 하느님을 위하여 내가 네게 저지른 모든 잘못을 용서해 다오.\""], ["And Sir Bohort answered, “God forgive it thee, and I do.”", "그러자 보호트 경이 대답했다. \"하느님께서 형을 용서하시기를, 저도 용서합니다.\""]], [["With that Sir Bohort heard a voice say, “Sir Bohort, take thy way anon, right to the sea, for Sir Perceval abideth thee there.”", "그 말과 함께 보호트 경은 한 목소리가 이렇게 말하는 것을 들었다. \"보호트 경, 지체 없이 곧장 바다로 가라."], ["So Sir Bohort departed, and rode the nearest way to the sea.", "그곳에서 퍼시벌 경이 그대를 기다리고 있느니라.\""], ["And at last he came to an abbey that was nigh the sea.", "그리하여 보호트 경은 길을 떠나 바다로 가는 가장 가까운 길로 말을 달렸다."], ["That night he rested him there, and in his sleep there came a voice unto him and bade him go to the sea-shore.", "그리고 마침내 그는 바다 가까이에 있는 어느 수도원에 이르렀다. 그날 밤 그는 그곳에서 쉬었는데, 잠결에 한 목소리가 그에게 다가와 바닷가로 가라고 명했다."], ["He started up, and made a sign of the cross on his forehead, and armed himself, and made ready his horse and mounted him, and at a broken wall he rode out, and came to the sea-shore.", "그는 벌떡 일어나 이마에 십자가 표시를 긋고, 무장을 갖춘 다음 말을 채비하여 올라탔고, 무너진 성벽을 통해 밖으로 나가 바닷가에 이르렀다."], ["And there he found a ship, covered all with white samite.", "그리고 그곳에서 그는 온통 흰 명주로 덮인 배 한 척을 발견했다."], ["And he entered into the ship; but it was anon so dark that he might see no man, and he laid him down and slept till it was day.", "그는 배에 올랐다. 그러나 곧 너무나 어두워져 사람을 알아볼 수 없었으므로, 그는 몸을 누이고 날이 밝을 때까지 잠을 잤다."], ["Then he awaked, and saw in the middle of the ship a knight all armed, save his helm.", "그러고 나서 잠에서 깨어, 배 한가운데에 투구만 벗은 채 온통 무장한 기사 하나가 있는 것을 보았다."], ["And then he knew it was Sir Perceval de Galis, and each made of other right great joy.", "그리고 그는 그가 갈리스의 퍼시벌 경임을 알아보았고, 둘은 서로를 보고 무척이나 크게 기뻐했다."], ["Then said Sir Perceval, “We lack nothing now but the good knight Sir Galahad.”", "그러자 퍼시벌 경이 말했다. \"이제 우리에게 부족한 것은 훌륭한 기사 갈라하드 경뿐이오.\""]], [["SIR LAUNCELOT (Resumed)\nIt befell upon a night Sir Launcelot arrived before a castle, which was rich and fair.", "어느 날 밤, 란슬롯 경은 풍요롭고 아름다운 어느 성 앞에 다다랐다. 그곳에는 바다를 향해 열린 뒷문이 하나 있었는데, 아무도 지키지 않고 열려 있었으며 다만 사자 두 마리가 입구를 지키고 있었다."], ["And there was a postern that was opened toward the sea, and was open without any keeping, save two lions kept the entry; and the moon shined clear.", "그리고 달이 밝게 빛나고 있었다. 이윽고 란슬롯 경은 한 목소리가 이렇게 말하는 것을 들었다."], ["Anon Sir Launcelot heard a voice that said, “Launcelot, enter into the castle, where thou shalt see a great part of thy desire.”", "\"란슬롯, 성안으로 들어가라. 그러면 네가 바라는 것의 큰 부분을 보게 되리라.\""], ["So he went unto the gate, and saw the two lions; then he set hands to his sword, and drew it.", "그리하여 그가 문으로 다가가 두 마리 사자를 보았을 때, 그는 검에 손을 대어 그것을 빼들었다. 그러자 갑자기 마치 일격을 당한 듯 팔에 어찌나 심한 충격이 가해졌던지 검이 손에서 떨어졌고, 그는 한 목소리가 이렇게 말하는 것을 들었다."], ["Then there came suddenly as it were a stroke upon the arm, so sore that the sword fell out of his hand, and he heard a voice that said, “O man of evil faith, wherefore believest thou more in thy armor than in thy Maker?”", "\"오, 믿음이 약한 자여, 어찌하여 너는 너를 지으신 분보다 네 갑옷을 더 믿느냐?\" 그러자 란슬롯 경이 말했다."], ["Then said Sir Launcelot, “Fair Lord, I thank thee of thy great mercy, that thou reprovest me of my misdeed; now see I well that thou holdest me for thy servant.”", "\"어지신 주님이시여, 저의 악행을 꾸짖어 주시는 그 크나큰 자비에 감사드리나이다. 이제 저는 주님께서 저를 당신의 종으로 여기심을 잘 알겠나이다.\""], ["Then he made a cross on his forehead, and came to the lions; and they made semblance to do him harm, but he passed them without hurt, and entered into the castle, and he found no gate nor door but it was open.", "그러고는 그는 이마에 십자가를 긋고 사자들에게로 다가갔다. 사자들은 그에게 해를 끼치려는 듯 보였으나, 그는 다친 데 없이 그들을 지나쳐 성안으로 들어갔다."], ["But at the last he found a chamber whereof the door was shut; and he set his hand thereto, to have opened it, but he might not.", "그리고 그는 열려 있지 않은 문이나 출입구를 하나도 찾지 못했다. 그러나 마침내 그는 문이 닫혀 있는 방 하나를 발견했다."], ["Then he listened, and heard a voice which sung so sweetly that it seemed none earthly thing; and the voice said, “Joy and honor be to the Father of heaven.”", "그가 그것을 열려고 손을 대었으나 열 수 없었다. 그러자 그는 귀를 기울였고, 한 목소리가 어찌나 감미롭게 노래하던지 도무지 이 세상 것 같지 않은 노래를 들었다."], ["Then Sir Launcelot kneeled down before the chamber, for well he wist that there was the Sangreal in that chamber.", "그 목소리는 이렇게 말했다. \"기쁨과 영광이 하늘에 계신 아버지께 있으라.\""], ["Then said he, “Fair, sweet Lord, if ever I did anything that pleased thee, for thy pity show me something of that which I seek.”", "그러자 란슬롯 경은 그 방 앞에 무릎을 꿇었으니, 그 방 안에 성배가 있음을 잘 알았기 때문이다. 그러고는 그가 말했다."], ["And with that he saw the chamber door open, and there came out a great clearness, that the house was as bright as though all the torches of the world had been there.", "\"어질고 자애로우신 주님이시여, 만일 제가 주님을 기쁘게 한 일을 한 번이라도 행하였다면, 주님의 측은하심으로 제가 찾는 것 가운데 무엇이라도 보여 주소서.\" 그러자 그는 방문이 열리고, 그 안에서 큰 광채가 나와 집안이 마치 세상의 모든 횃불이 그곳에 다 모인 듯 환해지는 것을 보았다."], ["So he came to the chamber door, and would have entered; and anon a voice said unto him, “Stay, Sir Launcelot, and enter not.”", "그리하여 그는 방문으로 다가가 들어가려 했다. 그러자 곧 한 목소리가 그에게 말했다."], ["And he withdrew him back, and was right heavy in his mind.", "\"멈추어라, 란슬롯 경, 들어가지 마라.\" 그는 뒤로 물러섰고, 마음이 몹시 무거웠다."], ["Then looked he in the midst of the chamber, and saw a table of silver, and the holy vessel, covered with red samite, and many angels about it; whereof one held a candle of wax burning, and another held a cross, and the ornaments of the altar.", "그러고는 그가 방 한가운데를 들여다보니, 은으로 된 식탁과 붉은 명주로 덮인 거룩한 그릇이 있었고, 그 주위에 많은 천사들이 있었다. 그중 하나는 타오르는 밀랍 초를, 또 하나는 십자가와 제단의 장식물들을 들고 있었다."]], [["“O, yet methought I saw the Holy Grail, All pall’d in crimson samite, and around Great angels, awful shapes, and wings and eyes.”", "\"오, 그래도 나는 성배를 보았노라. 온통 진홍빛 명주에 둘러싸이고, 그 둘레에는 위대한 천사들, 경외로운 형상들, 날개와 눈들이 있었네.\""], ["—The Holy Grail.", "— 성배."]], [["Then for very wonder and thankfulness Sir Launcelot forgot himself and he stepped forward and entered the chamber.", "그러자 란슬롯 경은 순전한 경탄과 감사에 사로잡혀 자신을 잊고 앞으로 발을 내디뎌 방으로 들어갔다."], ["And suddenly a breath that seemed intermixed with fire smote him so sore in the visage that therewith he fell to the ground, and had no power to rise.", "그러자 갑자기 불길이 섞인 듯한 숨결이 그의 얼굴을 어찌나 세게 후려쳤던지, 그는 그 때문에 땅에 쓰러져 일어날 힘조차 없었다."], ["Then felt he many hands about him, which took him up and bare him out of the chamber, without any amending of his swoon, and left him there, seeming dead to all the people.", "그러고는 그는 자기를 둘러싼 많은 손길을 느꼈는데, 그 손들이 그를 들어 올려 방 밖으로 내가, 정신을 잃은 채로 그곳에 두었으니, 모든 사람들 보기에 그는 죽은 듯했다."], ["So on the morrow, when it was fair daylight, and they within were arisen, they found Sir Launcelot lying before the chamber door.", "그리하여 이튿날 환한 대낮이 되어 성안의 사람들이 일어났을 때, 그들은 란슬롯 경이 방문 앞에 누워 있는 것을 발견했다. 그들은 그를 살펴보고 그에게 생명이 남아 있는지 알아보려고 맥을 짚었다."], ["And they looked upon him and felt his pulse, to know if there were any life in him.", "그리고 그들은 그에게서 생명을 발견했으나, 그는 일어서지도, 몸의 어느 부분 하나 움직이지도 못했다."], ["And they found life in him, but he might neither stand nor stir any member that he had.", "그리하여 그들은 그를 데려가 어느 방으로 옮겨, 모든 사람들에게서 멀리 떨어진 침상에 눕혔고, 그는 그곳에 여러 날을 누워 있었다."], ["So they took him and bare him into a chamber, and laid him upon a bed, far from all folk, and there he lay many days.", "그러자 한 사람은 그가 살아 있다고 했고, 다른 이들은 아니라고 했다. 그러나 한 노인이 말했다."], ["Then the one said he was alive, and the others said nay.", "\"그는 그대들 가운데 가장 힘센 자만큼이나 생명이 가득하오."], ["But said an old man, “He is as full of life as the mightiest of you all, and therefore I counsel you that he be well kept till God bring him back again.”", "그러니 하느님께서 그를 다시 데려오실 때까지 잘 보살피시기를 권하오.\" 그리고 스물나흘이 지나자 그가 눈을 떴다."], ["And after twenty-four days he opened his eyes; and when he saw folk he made great sorrow, and said, “Why have ye wakened me?", "그리고 사람들을 보자 그는 크게 슬퍼하며 말했다. \"어찌하여 그대들은 나를 깨웠는가?"], ["for I was better at ease than I am now.”", "나는 지금보다 한결 편안했거늘.\" \"무엇을 보셨소?\""], ["“What have ye seen?”", "그를 둘러싼 사람들이 말했다."], ["said they about him.", "\"나는 보았소,\""], ["“I have seen,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “great marvels that no tongue can tell, and more than any heart can think.”", "\"어떤 혀로도 이를 수 없고, 어떤 마음으로도 헤아릴 수 없는 큰 경이를.\" 그러자 그들이 말했다."], ["Then they said, “Sir, the quest of the Sangreal is achieved right now in you, and never shall ye see more of it than ye have seen.”", "\"기사여, 성배의 탐색은 바로 지금 그대 안에서 이루어졌으니, 그대는 이미 본 것보다 더한 것은 결코 보지 못하리다.\" \"나는 하느님께 감사드리오,\""], ["“I thank God,”", "란슬롯 경이 말했다."], ["said Sir Launcelot, “of his great mercy, for that I have seen, for it sufficeth me.”", "\"그 크나큰 자비로 내가 본 것에 대하여. 그것으로 내게는 족하오.\""], ["Then he rose up and clothed himself; and when he was so arrayed they marvelled all, for they knew it was Sir Launcelot the good knight.", "그러고는 그가 일어나 옷을 입었다. 그가 그렇게 차려입자 모두가 경탄했으니, 그가 훌륭한 기사 란슬롯 경임을 알았기 때문이다."], ["And after four days he took his leave of the lord of the castle, and of all the fellowship that were there, and thanked them for their great labor and care of him.", "그리고 나흘 뒤 그는 성주와 그곳에 있던 모든 동료들에게 작별을 고하고, 자기를 위해 베푼 그들의 큰 수고와 보살핌에 감사했다."], ["Then he departed, and turned to Camelot, where he found King Arthur and Queen Guenever; but many of the knights of the Round Table were slain and destroyed, more than half.", "그러고는 그가 떠나 카멜롯으로 향했고, 그곳에서 그는 아서 왕과 귀네비어 왕비를 만났다. 그러나 원탁의 기사들 가운데 많은 이가, 절반이 넘는 이들이 살해되고 멸망한 뒤였다."], ["Then all the court was passing glad of Sir Launcelot; and he told the king all his adventures that had befallen him since he departed.", "그러자 온 궁정이 란슬롯 경을 보고 한없이 기뻐했다. 그리고 그는 떠난 뒤 자기에게 일어난 모든 모험을 왕에게 들려주었다."]], [["# SIR GALAHAD", "# SIR GALAHAD"]], [["Now, when Sir Galahad had rescued Perceval from the twenty knights, he rode into a vast forest, wherein he abode many days.", "자, 갈라하드 경은 퍼시벌을 스무 명의 기사로부터 구해 낸 뒤, 어느 광대한 숲속으로 말을 달려 그곳에서 여러 날을 지냈다."], ["Then he took his way to the sea, and it befell him that he was benighted in a hermitage.", "그러고는 바다로 향하는 길을 잡았는데, 그러던 중 어느 은수처에서 밤을 맞게 되었다."], ["And the good man was glad when he saw he was a knight-errant.", "그 선한 사람은 그가 편력 기사임을 보고 기뻐했다."], ["And when they were at rest, there came a gentlewoman knocking at the door; and the good man came to the door to wit what she would.", "그리고 그들이 쉬고 있을 때, 한 귀부인이 문을 두드리며 왔다. 그 선한 사람이 그녀가 무엇을 원하는지 알아보려고 문으로 나갔다."], ["Then she said, “I would speak with the knight which is with you.”", "그러자 그녀가 말했다. \"당신과 함께 있는 기사와 이야기를 나누고 싶습니다.\""], ["Then Galahad went to her, and asked her what she would.", "그러자 갈라하드가 그녀에게로 가서 무엇을 원하는지 물었다. \"갈라하드 경,\""], ["“Sir Galahad,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “I will that ye arm you, and mount upon your horse, and follow me; for I will show you the highest adventure that ever knight saw.”", "\"원컨대 무장을 갖추시고 말에 올라 저를 따라오십시오. 어떤 기사도 일찍이 본 적 없는 가장 높은 모험을 보여드리겠습니다.\""], ["Then Galahad armed himself and commended himself to God, and bade the damsel go before, and he would follow where she led.", "그러자 갈라하드는 무장을 갖추고 자신을 하느님께 의탁한 뒤, 그 처녀에게 앞장서라 명하고, 그녀가 인도하는 곳을 따라가겠노라 했다."]], [["So she rode as fast as her palfrey might bear her, till she came to the sea; and there they found the ship where Sir Bohort and Sir Perceval were, who cried from the ship, “Sir Galahad, you are welcome; we have waited you long.”", "그리하여 그녀는 자기 말이 실어 나를 수 있는 한 빠르게 달려 바닷가에 이르렀다. 그곳에서 그들은 보호트 경과 퍼시벌 경이 타고 있는 배를 발견했고, 두 사람은 배에서 외쳤다."], ["And when he heard them, he asked the damsel who they were.", "\"갈라하드 경, 어서 오시오. 우리는 그대를 오래 기다렸소.\""], ["“Sir,”", "그가 그들의 말을 듣고는 처녀에게 그들이 누구인지 물었다. \"기사 어른,\""], ["said she, “leave your horse here, and I shall leave mine, and we will join ourselves to their company.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"그대의 말은 이곳에 두고, 제 말도 두겠습니다."], ["So they entered into the ship, and the two knights received them both with great joy.", "그리고 우리는 그들 일행에 합류할 것입니다.\" 그리하여 그들은 배에 올랐고, 두 기사는 둘 다를 큰 기쁨으로 맞이했다."], ["For they knew the damsel, that she was Sir Perceval’s sister.", "그들은 그 처녀가 퍼시벌 경의 누이임을 알고 있었기 때문이다. 그러자 바람이 일어 그날과 이튿날 내내 그들을 바다 너머로 몰아갔고, 마침내 배는 한없이 크고 놀라운 두 바위 사이에 다다랐다."], ["Then the wind arose and drove them through the sea all that day and the next, till the ship arrived between two rocks, passing great and marvellous; but there they might not land, for there was a whirlpool; but there was another ship, and upon it they might go without danger.", "그러나 그곳에는 소용돌이가 있어 상륙할 수 없었다. 그런데 또 다른 배 한 척이 있어, 그 위에 오르면 위험 없이 갈 수 있었다."], ["“Go we thither,”", "\"저리로 갑시다.\""], ["said the gentlewoman, “and there we shall see adventures, for such is our Lord’s will.”", "귀부인이 말했다. \"그곳에서 우리는 모험을 보게 될 것이니, 그것이 우리 주님의 뜻이기 때문입니다.\""], ["Then Sir Galahad blessed him, and entered therein, and then next the gentlewoman, and then Sir Bohort and Sir Perceval.", "그러자 갈라하드 경은 성호를 긋고 그 안으로 들어갔고, 그다음으로 귀부인이, 그다음으로 보호트 경과 퍼시벌 경이 들어갔다. 그리고 그들이 배에 올랐을 때, 그곳에서 은으로 된 식탁과, 붉은 명주로 덮인 성배를 발견했다."], ["And when they came on board they found there the table of silver, and the Sangreal, which was covered with red samite.", "그들은 그것에 크게 경배를 올렸고, 갈라하드 경은 우리 주님께 오래도록 기도했으니, 자기가 이 세상을 떠나기를 청하는 그때에 그리될 수 있게 해 달라는 것이었다. 그러자 한 목소리가 그에게 말했다."], ["And they made great reverence thereto, and Sir Galahad prayed a long time to our Lord, that at what time he should ask to pass out of this world he should do so; and a voice said to him, “Galahad, thou shalt have thy request; and when thou askest the death of they body, thou shalt have it, and then shalt thou find the life of thy soul.”", "\"갈라하드, 너는 네 청을 이루리라. 그리고 네가 네 육신의 죽음을 청할 때, 너는 그것을 얻고, 그때 너는 네 영혼의 생명을 찾으리라.\""]], [["And anon the wind drove them across the sea, till they came to the city of Sarras.", "그리고 곧 바람이 그들을 바다 너머로 몰아가, 마침내 그들은 사라스라는 도시에 이르렀다."], ["Then took they out of the ship the table of silver, and Sir Perceval and Sir Bohort took it before, and Sir Galahad came behind, and right so they went to the city.", "그러자 그들은 은으로 된 식탁을 배에서 내렸고, 퍼시벌 경과 보호트 경이 앞에서 그것을 들고 갈라하드 경이 뒤따랐으며, 그렇게 그들은 도시로 들어갔다."], ["And at the gate of the city they saw an old man, a cripple.", "그리고 도시의 성문에서 그들은 한 노인을, 절름발이 한 사람을 보았다."]], [["“And Sir Launfal said, ‘I behold in thee An image of Him who died on the tree.", "\"그리고 란팔 경이 말했네. '나는 그대에게서 보노라, 나무 위에서 돌아가신 그분의 형상을."], ["Thou also hast had thy crown of thorns, Thou also hast had the world’s buffets and scorns; And to thy life were not denied The wounds in thy hands and feet and side.", "그대 또한 가시관을 쓰셨고, 그대 또한 세상의 매질과 조롱을 받으셨으며, 그대의 생애에 거절되지 않았네, 그대의 두 손과 두 발과 옆구리의 상처들이. 온유하신 마리아의 아드님이시여, 저를 알아주소서."], ["Mild Mary’s son, acknowledge me; Behold, through Him I give to thee!’”", "보소서, 그분을 통하여 제가 그대에게 드리나이다!'\""], ["—Lowell’s Holy Grail.", "— 로웰의 성배."]], [["Then Galahad called him, and bade him help to bear this heavy thing.", "그러자 갈라하드가 그를 불러, 이 무거운 것을 옮기는 일을 도와 달라고 청했다."], ["“Truly,”", "\"참으로,\""], ["said the old man, “it is ten years since I could not go but with crutches.”", "노인이 말했다. \"내가 목발 없이는 걷지 못한 지 십 년이 되었소.\""], ["“Care thou not,”", "\"염려치 마시오,\""], ["said Sir Galahad, “but arise up, and show thy good will.”", "갈라하드 경이 말했다. \"다만 일어나서 그대의 선한 뜻을 보이시오.\""], ["Then the old man rose up, and assayed, and found himself as whole as ever he was; and he ran to the table, and took one part with Sir Galahad.", "그러자 노인이 일어나 애써 보니, 자기 몸이 전과 다름없이 온전해진 것을 알았다. 그리고 그는 식탁으로 달려가 갈라하드 경과 함께 한쪽을 들었다."]], [["When they came to the city it chanced that the king was just dead, and all the city was dismayed, and wist not who might be their king.", "그들이 도시에 이르렀을 때, 마침 왕이 막 세상을 떠나 온 도시가 당황에 빠져, 누가 자기들의 왕이 될 수 있을지 알지 못하던 참이었다."], ["Right so, as they were in counsel, there came a voice among them, and bade them choose the youngest knight of those three to be their king.", "바로 그때, 그들이 의논하고 있는 중에 그들 가운데로 한 목소리가 들려와, 그 세 기사 중 가장 젊은 기사를 왕으로 택하라고 명했다."], ["So they made Sir Galahad king, by all the assent of the city.", "그리하여 그들은 도시 전체의 동의로 갈라하드 경을 왕으로 삼았다."], ["And when he was made king, he commanded to make a chest of gold and of precious stones to hold the holy vessel.", "그리고 그가 왕이 되자, 그는 거룩한 그릇을 담을 함을 금과 보석으로 만들라고 명했다."], ["And every day the three companions would come before it and make their prayers.", "그리고 날마다 세 동료는 그 앞에 나아와 기도를 올리곤 했다."]], [["Now at the year’s end, and the same day of the year that Sir Galahad received the crown, he got up early, and, with his fellows, came to where the holy vessel was; and they saw one kneeling before it that had about him a great fellowship of angels; and he called Sir Galahad, and said, “Come, thou servant of the Lord, and thou shalt see what thou hast much desired to see.”", "한 해가 끝날 무렵, 갈라하드 경이 왕관을 받은 바로 그날, 그는 일찍 일어나 동료들과 함께 거룩한 그릇이 있는 곳으로 갔다. 그리고 그들은 그 앞에 무릎을 꿇은 한 사람을 보았는데, 그 둘레에는 천사들의 큰 무리가 있었다."], ["And Sir Galahad’s mortal flesh trembled right hard when he began to behold the spiritual things.", "그가 갈라하드 경을 불러 말했다. \"오라, 주님의 종이여, 그러면 그대가 그토록 보기를 갈망하던 것을 보게 되리라.\""], ["Then said the good man, “Now wottest thou who I am?”", "그리하여 갈라하드 경이 영적인 것들을 바라보기 시작하자 그의 필멸하는 육신이 몹시 심하게 떨렸다."], ["“Nay,” said Sir Galahad.", "그러자 그 선한 사람이 말했다."], ["“I am Joseph of Arimathea, whom our Lord hath sent here to thee, to bear thee fellowship.”", "\"이제 내가 누구인지 알겠는가?\" \"모르겠나이다.\""], ["Then Sir Galahad held up his hands toward heaven, and said, “Now, blessed Lord, would I not longer live, if it might please thee.”", "갈라하드 경이 말했다. \"나는 아리마태아의 요셉이니, 우리 주님께서 그대에게 벗이 되어 주라고 나를 이곳 그대에게로 보내셨노라.\""], ["And when he had said these words, Sir Galahad went to Sir Perceval and to Sir Bohort and kissed them, and commended them to God.", "그러자 갈라하드 경이 하늘을 향해 두 손을 들어 올리며 말했다. \"이제, 복되신 주님이시여, 만일 주님께서 기뻐하신다면 저는 더 이상 살기를 원치 않나이다.\""], ["And then he kneeled down before the table, and made his prayers, and suddenly his soul departed, and a great multitude of angels bare his soul up to heaven, so as the two fellows could well behold it.", "그리고 이 말을 마치자, 갈라하드 경은 퍼시벌 경과 보호트 경에게로 가서 그들에게 입을 맞추고, 그들을 하느님께 맡겼다. 그러고는 그가 식탁 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 기도를 올리니, 갑자기 그의 영혼이 떠났고, 천사들의 큰 무리가 그의 영혼을 하늘로 들어 올려, 두 동료가 그것을 똑똑히 바라볼 수 있었다."], ["Also they saw come from heaven a hand, but they saw not the body; and the hand came right to the vessel and bare it up to heaven.", "또한 그들은 하늘에서 손 하나가 내려오는 것을 보았으나, 그 몸은 보지 못했다. 그 손은 곧장 그릇으로 와서 그것을 하늘로 들어 올렸다."], ["Since then was there never one so hardy as to say that he had seen the Sangreal on earth any more.", "그 후로는 누구도 다시는 이 땅에서 성배를 보았노라 감히 말할 만큼 담대한 자가 없었다."]], [["————", "────"]], [["# SIR AGRIVAIN’S TREASON", "# SIR AGRIVAIN’S TREASON"]], [["WHEN Sir Perceval and Sir Bohort saw Sir Galahad dead they made as much sorrow as ever did two men.", "퍼시벌 경과 보호트 경은 갈라하드 경이 죽은 것을 보았을 때, 일찍이 어떤 두 사람도 보인 적 없을 만큼 깊은 슬픔에 잠겼다."], ["And if they had not been good men they might have fallen into despair.", "만약 그들이 선량한 사람이 아니었다면 절망에 빠지고 말았을 것이다."], ["As soon as Sir Galahad was buried Sir Perceval retired to a hermitage out of the city, and took a religious clothing; and Sir Bohort was always with him, but did not change his secular clothing, because he purposed to return to the realm of Loegria.", "갈라하드 경이 묻히자마자 퍼시벌 경은 도시 밖의 어느 은수처로 물러나 수도자의 옷을 입었다. 보호트 경은 늘 그와 함께 있었으나 세속의 옷을 갈아입지는 않았으니, 로에그리아 왕국으로 돌아갈 작정이었기 때문이다."], ["Thus a year and two months lived Sir Perceval in the hermitage a full holy life, and then passed out of this world, and Sir Bohort buried him by his sister and Sir Galahad.", "이렇게 퍼시벌 경은 은수처에서 일 년 두 달 동안 더없이 거룩한 삶을 살다가 이 세상을 떠났고, 보호트 경은 그를 누이와 갈라하드 경의 곁에 묻었다."], ["Then Sir Bohort armed himself and departed from Sarras, and entered into a ship, and sailed to the kingdom of Loegria, and in due time arrived safe at Camelot, where the king was.", "그런 다음 보호트 경은 무장을 갖추고 사라스를 떠나 배에 올라 로에그리아 왕국으로 항해하여, 마침내 무사히 카멜롯에 도착했으니, 그곳에 왕이 있었다."], ["Then was there great joy made of him in the whole court, for they feared he had been dead.", "그러자 온 궁정이 그를 두고 크게 기뻐했으니, 그들은 그가 죽은 줄로만 알았던 것이다."], ["Then the king made great clerks to come before him, that they should chronicle of the high adventures of the good knights.", "그리하여 왕은 학식 높은 서기들을 불러들여 선량한 기사들의 드높은 모험을 기록하게 했다."], ["And Sir Bohort told him of the adventures that had befallen him, and his two fellows, Sir Perceval and Sir Galahad.", "그리고 보호트 경은 자신과 두 동료 퍼시벌 경, 갈라하드 경에게 일어났던 모험을 이야기했다."], ["And Sir Launcelot told the adventures of the Sangreal that he had seen.", "또 란슬롯 경은 자신이 본 성배의 모험을 이야기했다."], ["All this was made in great books, and put up in the church at Salisbury.", "이 모든 것이 큰 책으로 만들어져 솔즈베리의 교회에 봉안되었다."]], [["So King Arthur and Queen Guenever made great joy of the remnant that were come home, and chiefly of Sir Launcelot and Sir Bohort.", "이렇게 아서 왕과 귀네비어 왕비는 고향으로 돌아온 자들을, 그중에서도 특히 란슬롯 경과 보호트 경을 두고 크게 기뻐했다. 그러나 란슬롯 경은 다시 귀네비어 왕비를 찾기 시작했고, 탐색 길에서 했던 약속을 잊고 말았다."], ["Then Sir Launcelot began to resort unto Queen Guenever again, and forgot the promise that he made in the quest; so that many in the court spoke of it, and in especial Sir Agrivain, Sir Gawain’s brother, for he was ever open-mouthed.", "그리하여 궁정 안에서 많은 이가 그 일을 입에 올렸으니, 특히 가웨인 경의 동생 아그리베인 경이 그러했다. 그는 늘 입이 가벼웠기 때문이다."], ["So it happened Sir Gawain and all his brothers were in King Arthur’s chamber, and then Sir Agrivain said thus openly, “I marvel that we all are not ashamed to see and to know so noble a knight as King Arthur so to be shamed by the conduct of Sir Launcelot and the queen.”", "그러던 어느 날 가웨인 경과 그 형제들이 모두 아서 왕의 방에 있었는데, 그때 아그리베인 경이 이렇게 드러내 놓고 말했다. \"아서 왕처럼 고귀한 기사가 란슬롯 경과 왕비의 행실로 인해 이토록 수치를 당하는 것을 보면서도 우리가 모두 부끄러워하지 않으니, 참으로 놀랄 일이오.\""], ["Then spoke Sir Gawain, and said, “Brother, Sir Agrivain, I pray you and charge you move not such matters any more before me, for be ye assured I will not be of your counsel.”", "그러자 가웨인 경이 말했다. \"아우 아그리베인 경, 청컨대, 또한 명하건대, 내 앞에서 다시는 그런 일을 꺼내지 말게."], ["“Neither will we,”", "분명히 말하건대 나는 자네 편에 서지 않을 것이니.\" \"우리도 그렇소.\""], ["said Sir Gaheris and Sir Gareth.", "가헤리스 경과 가레스 경이 말했다. \"그럼 내가 하지요.\""], ["“Then will I,”", "모드레드 경이 말했다."], ["said Sir Modred.", "\"자네라면 의심치 않네.\""], ["“I doubt you not,”", "가웨인 경이 말했다."], ["said Sir Gawain, “for to all mischief ever were ye prone; yet I would that ye left all this, for I know what will come of it.”", "\"자네는 늘 온갖 해악에 기울었으니. 그러나 나는 자네가 이 모든 일에서 손을 떼었으면 하네, 그 끝에 무엇이 올지를 내가 알기 때문일세.\""]], [["“Modred’s narrow foxy face, Heart-hiding smile, and gray persistent eye: Henceforward, too, the Powers that tend the soul To help it from the death that cannot die, And save it even in extremes, began To vex and plague.”", "\"모드레드의 좁고 여우 같은 얼굴, 마음을 감추는 미소, 잿빛으로 끈질긴 눈. 이제부터 영혼을 보살펴 죽을 수 없는 죽음에서 구하고 극한에서마저 지켜 주려는 힘들이 괴롭히고 들볶기 시작했다.\""], ["—Guinevere.", "─〈귀네비어〉"]], [["“Fall of it what fall may,”", "\"그 끝에 무엇이 오든 상관없소.\""], ["said Sir Agrivain, “I will disclose it to the king.”", "아그리베인 경이 말했다. \"나는 이 일을 왕께 아뢸 것이오.\""], ["With that came to them King Arthur.", "그러는 사이 아서 왕이 그들에게로 왔다."], ["“Now, brothers, hold your peace,”", "\"자, 형제들, 입을 다무시오.\""], ["said Sir Gawain.", "가웨인 경이 말했다."], ["“We will not,”", "\"그러지 않겠소.\""], ["said Sir Agrivain.", "아그리베인 경이 말했다."], ["Then said Sir Gawain, “I will not hear your tales nor be of your counsel.”", "그러자 가웨인 경이 말했다. \"나는 자네 이야기를 듣지도 않을 것이며, 자네 편에 서지도 않을 것일세.\""], ["“No more will I,”", "\"나도 더는 그러지 않겠소.\""], ["said Sir Gareth and Sir Gaheris, and therewith they departed, making great sorrow.", "가레스 경과 가헤리스 경이 말했고, 그러면서 그들은 크나큰 슬픔에 잠겨 자리를 떠났다."]], [["Then Sir Agrivain told the king all that was said in the court of the conduct of Sir Launcelot and the queen, and it grieved the king very much.", "그러자 아그리베인 경은 란슬롯 경과 왕비의 행실에 관해 궁정에서 오간 말을 모두 왕에게 아뢰었고, 그 말에 왕은 몹시 비통해했다."], ["But he would not believe it to be true without proof.", "그러나 왕은 증거 없이는 그것이 사실이라 믿으려 하지 않았다."], ["So Sir Agrivain laid a plot to entrap Sir Launcelot and the queen, intending to take them together unawares.", "그리하여 아그리베인 경은 란슬롯 경과 왕비를 함정에 빠뜨릴 계략을 꾸몄으니, 그 둘을 방심한 틈에 한자리에서 붙잡을 작정이었다."], ["Sir Agrivain and Sir Modred led a party for this purpose, but Sir Launcelot escaped from them, having slain Sir Agrivain and wounded Sir Modred.", "아그리베인 경과 모드레드 경이 이 목적으로 한 무리를 이끌었으나, 란슬롯 경은 아그리베인 경을 죽이고 모드레드 경에게 상처를 입힌 뒤 그들에게서 벗어났다."], ["Then Sir Launcelot hastened to his friends, and told them what had happened, and withdrew with them to the forest; but he left spies to bring him tidings of whatever might be done.", "그런 다음 란슬롯 경은 벗들에게로 서둘러 가서 무슨 일이 있었는지 일러 주고는 그들과 함께 숲으로 물러났다. 다만 그는 무슨 일이 벌어지든 소식을 전해 줄 첩자들을 남겨 두었다."]], [["So Sir Launcelot escaped, but the queen remained in the king’s power, and Arthur could no longer doubt of her guilt.", "이렇게 란슬롯 경은 벗어났으나 왕비는 왕의 손아귀에 남게 되었고, 아서 왕은 더 이상 그녀의 죄를 의심할 수 없었다. 그 시절의 법은 이러했으니, 그 신분이나 지위가 어떠하든 그런 죄를 저지른 자는 화형에 처해 죽어야 했다."], ["And the law was such in those days that they who committed such crimes, of what estate or condition soever they were, must be burned to death, and so it was ordained for Queen Guenever.", "그리하여 귀네비어 왕비에게도 그렇게 명해졌다. 그러자 아서 왕이 가웨인 경에게 말했다."], ["Then said King Arthur to Sir Gawain, “I pray you make you ready, in your best armor, with your brethren, Sir Gaheris and Sir Gareth, to bring my queen to the fire, there to receive her death.”", "\"청컨대 가장 좋은 갑옷을 입고, 그대의 형제 가헤리스 경과 가레스 경과 함께 채비를 갖추어, 나의 왕비를 불길로 데려가 그곳에서 죽음을 받게 하시오.\" \"아니 됩니다, 더없이 고귀하신 주군이시여.\""], ["“Nay, my most noble lord,”", "가웨인 경이 말했다. \"그 일만은 결코 하지 않겠나이다."], ["said Sir Gawain, “that will I never do; for know thou well, my heart will never serve me to see her die, and it shall never be said that I was of your counsel in her death.”", "똑똑히 아소서, 제 마음이 그녀가 죽는 것을 차마 보지 못할 것이며, 제가 그녀의 죽음에 가담했다는 말은 결코 나오지 않을 것입니다.\" 그러자 왕은 가헤리스 경과 가레스 경에게 그곳에 있으라 명했고, 그들은 말했다."], ["Then the king commanded Sir Gaheris and Sir Gareth to be there, and they said, “We will be there, as ye command us, sire, but in peaceable wise, and bear no armor upon us.”", "\"명하신 대로 그곳에 있겠나이다, 폐하. 다만 평온한 차림으로, 갑옷은 몸에 걸치지 않겠나이다.\""]], [["So the queen was led forth, and her ghostly father was brought to her to shrive her, and there was weeping and wailing of many lords and ladies.", "이렇게 왕비가 끌려 나왔고, 그녀를 고해 받게 하려고 영적 아버지가 그녀에게로 인도되었으며, 그곳에는 많은 영주와 귀부인의 울음과 통곡이 있었다."], ["And one went and told Sir Launcelot that the queen was led forth to her death.", "그리고 누군가 가서 왕비가 죽음을 향해 끌려 나왔다고 란슬롯 경에게 일렀다."], ["Then Sir Launcelot and the knights that were with him fell upon the troop that guarded the queen, and dispersed them, and slew all who withstood them.", "그러자 란슬롯 경과 그와 함께 있던 기사들은 왕비를 지키던 무리를 덮쳐 그들을 흩어 버리고, 맞서는 자들을 모두 베어 버렸다."], ["And in the confusion Sir Gareth and Sir Gaheris were slain, for they were unarmed and defenceless.", "그 혼란 속에서 가레스 경과 가헤리스 경도 죽임을 당했으니, 그들은 무장하지 않아 몸을 지킬 수 없었기 때문이다."], ["And Sir Launcelot carried away the queen to his castle of La Joyeuse Garde.", "그리고 란슬롯 경은 왕비를 자신의 성 라 주아유즈 가르드로 데려갔다."]], [["Then there came one to Sir Gawain and told him how that Sir Launcelot had slain the knights and carried away the queen.", "그때 누군가 가웨인 경에게로 와서 란슬롯 경이 기사들을 죽이고 왕비를 데려간 일을 일러 주었다. \"오 주여, 제 형제들을 지켜 주소서!\""], ["“O Lord, defend my brethren!”", "가웨인 경이 말했다. \"진실로,\""], ["said Sir Gawain.", "그 사람이 말했다."], ["“Truly,” said the man, “Sir Gareth and Sir Gaheris are slain.”", "\"가레스 경과 가헤리스 경께서 죽임을 당하셨나이다.\""], ["“Alas!”", "\"아아!\""], ["said Sir Gawain, “now is my joy gone.”", "가웨인 경이 말했다. \"이제 내 기쁨이 사라졌구나.\""], ["And then he fell down and swooned, and long he lay there as he had been dead.", "그러고는 그는 쓰러져 정신을 잃었고, 마치 죽은 사람처럼 오래도록 그곳에 누워 있었다."]], [["When he arose out of his swoon Sir Gawain ran to the king, crying, “O King Arthur, mine uncle, my brothers are slain.”", "가웨인 경은 혼절에서 깨어나자 왕에게로 달려가 부르짖었다. \"오 아서 왕이시여, 제 외숙이시여, 제 형제들이 죽임을 당했나이다.\""], ["Then the king wept and he both.", "그러자 왕도 그도 함께 눈물을 흘렸다."], ["“My king, my lord, and mine uncle,”", "\"제 임금이시며, 제 주군이시며, 제 외숙이시여,\""], ["said Sir Gawain, “bear witness now that I make you a promise that I shall hold by my knighthood, and from this day I will never fail Sir Launcelot until the one of us have slain the other.", "가웨인 경이 말했다. \"이제 증인이 되어 주소서, 제가 기사의 명예를 걸고 약속을 드리나니, 오늘부터 우리 둘 중 하나가 다른 하나를 죽이기 전까지 저는 결코 란슬롯 경을 놓아주지 않겠나이다."], ["I will seek Sir Launcelot throughout seven kings’ realms, but I shall slay him or he shall slay me.”", "일곱 왕의 영토를 두루 헤매며 란슬롯 경을 찾을 것이며, 제가 그를 죽이든 그가 저를 죽이든 할 것입니다.\""], ["“Ye shall not need to seek him,”", "\"그를 찾을 필요는 없을 것이오.\" 왕이 말했다."], ["said the king, “for as I hear, Sir Launcelot will abide me and you in the Joyeuse Garde; and much people draweth unto him, as I hear say.”", "\"내가 듣기로 란슬롯 경은 주아유즈 가르드에서 나와 그대를 기다릴 것이라 하며, 또한 듣건대 많은 사람이 그에게로 모여든다 하오.\" \"그 말씀은 믿을 만하옵니다.\""], ["“That may I believe,”", "가웨인 경이 말했다."], ["said Sir Gawain; “but, my lord, summon your friends, and I will summon mine.”", "\"그러나 주군이시여, 폐하의 벗들을 부르소서, 그러면 저도 제 벗들을 부르겠나이다.\""], ["“It shall be done,”", "\"그리하겠소.\""], ["said the king.", "왕이 말했다."], ["So then the king sent letters and writs throughout all England, both in the length and breadth, to summon all his knights.", "그리하여 왕은 잉글랜드 전역에, 그 길이와 너비를 가리지 않고 모든 기사를 불러 모으는 서한과 영장을 보냈다."], ["And unto Arthur drew many knights, dukes, and earls, so that he had a great host.", "그러자 아서에게로 많은 기사와 공작과 백작이 모여들었으니, 그는 큰 군세를 갖추게 되었다."], ["Thereof heard Sir Launcelot, and collected all whom he could; and many good knights held with him, both for his sake and for the queen’s sake.", "란슬롯 경도 그 소식을 듣고 모을 수 있는 자들을 모두 모았다. 많은 선량한 기사가 그를 위해서, 또 왕비를 위해서 그의 편에 섰다."], ["But King Arthur’s host was too great for Sir Launcelot to abide him in the field; and he was full loath to do battle against the king.", "그러나 아서 왕의 군세가 너무도 커서 란슬롯 경은 들판에서 그를 맞아 싸울 수 없었고, 그는 왕을 상대로 싸우는 것을 몹시 꺼렸다."], ["So Sir Launcelot drew him to his strong castle, with all manner of provisions.", "그리하여 란슬롯 경은 온갖 양식을 갖추고 자신의 견고한 성으로 물러났다."], ["Then came King Arthur with Sir Gawain, and laid siege all about La Joyeuse Garde, both the town and the castle; but in no wise would Sir Launcelot ride out of his castle, neither suffer any of his knights to issue out, until many weeks were past.", "그러자 아서 왕이 가웨인 경과 함께 와서 라 주아유즈 가르드를, 그 마을과 성을 빙 둘러 포위했다. 그러나 란슬롯 경은 여러 주가 지나도록 결코 성 밖으로 말을 몰아 나오지 않았고, 자신의 기사 누구도 밖으로 나가게 두지 않았다."]], [["Then it befell upon a day in harvest-time, Sir Launcelot looked over the wall, and spoke aloud to King Arthur and Sir Gawain, “My lords both, all is in vain that ye do at this siege, for here ye shall win no worship, but only dishonor; for if I list to come out, and my good knights, I shall soon make an end of this war.”", "그러던 어느 추수철의 하루, 란슬롯 경이 성벽 너머를 내다보며 아서 왕과 가웨인 경에게 큰 소리로 말했다."], ["“Come forth,”", "\"두 분 주군이시여, 이 포위로 하시는 모든 일이 헛되옵니다."], ["said Arthur, “if thou darest, and I promise thee I shall meet thee in the midst of the field.”", "여기서 두 분은 영예를 얻지 못하고 오직 치욕만 얻으실 뿐이니, 만약 제가 제 선량한 기사들과 함께 나서고자 한다면 곧 이 전쟁을 끝장낼 것이옵니다.\" \"나오라.\""], ["“God forbid me,”", "아서가 말했다. \"그럴 배짱이 있다면."], ["said Sir Launcelot, “that I should encounter with the most noble king that made me knight.”", "내 약속하건대 들판 한가운데서 그대를 맞으리라.\" \"하느님께서 막아 주소서,\""], ["“Fie upon thy fair language,”", "란슬롯 경이 말했다. \"저를 기사로 만들어 주신 더없이 고귀하신 왕과 맞붙는 일만은.\""], ["said the king, “for know thou well I am thy mortal foe, and ever will be to my dying day.”", "\"그 입에 발린 말은 집어치워라.\" 왕이 말했다."], ["And Sir Gawain said, “What cause hadst thou to slay my brother, Sir Gaheris, who bore no arms against thee, and Sir Gareth, whom thou madest knight, and who loved thee more than all my kin?", "\"똑똑히 알아 두라, 나는 그대의 철천지원수이며, 죽는 날까지 늘 그러할 것이다.\" 그리고 가웨인 경이 말했다."], ["Therefore know thou well I shall make war to thee all the while that I may live.”", "\"그대에게 맞서 무기 한번 들지 않은 내 형제 가헤리스 경을, 그리고 그대가 기사로 만들어 주었고 내 모든 혈족보다 그대를 더 사랑했던 가레스 경을 죽일 무슨 까닭이 있었느냐? 그러니 똑똑히 알아 두라, 나는 살아 있는 한 그대에게 전쟁을 벌일 것이다.\""]], [["When Sir Bohort, and Sir Hector de Marys, and Sir Lionel heard this outcry, they called to them Sir Palamedes, and Sir Saffire his brother, and Sir Lawayn, with many more, and all went to Sir Launcelot.", "보호트 경과 헥터 디 마리스 경, 그리고 라이오넬 경이 이 외침을 듣고는 팔라메데스 경과 그 형제 새파이어 경, 라웨인 경, 그 밖의 많은 이를 불러 모두 함께 란슬롯 경에게로 갔다. 그리고 그들이 말했다."], ["And they said, “My lord, Sir Launcelot, we pray you, if you will have our service keep us no longer within these walls, for know well all your fair speech and forbearance will not avail you.”", "\"주군이신 란슬롯 경이시여, 청컨대 우리의 봉사를 원하신다면 더 이상 우리를 이 성벽 안에 가두지 마소서. 똑똑히 아소서, 당신의 그 모든 고운 말과 인내는 당신께 아무 소용이 없을 것입니다.\""], ["“Alas!”", "\"아아!\""], ["said Sir Launcelot, “to ride forth and to do battle I am full loath.”", "란슬롯 경이 말했다. \"말을 몰아 나가 싸우는 일을 나는 몹시 꺼리노라.\""], ["Then he spake again unto the king and Sir Gawain, and willed them to keep out of the battle; but they despised his words.", "그러고는 그는 다시 왕과 가웨인 경에게 말하여 그들에게 싸움에서 빠져 있으라고 권했으나, 그들은 그의 말을 업신여겼다."], ["So then Sir Launcelot’s fellowship came out of the castle in full good array.", "그리하여 란슬롯 경의 무리가 한껏 좋은 진용을 갖추어 성에서 나왔다."], ["And always Sir Launcelot charged all his knights, in any wise, to save King Arthur and Sir Gawain.", "그리고 란슬롯 경은 언제나 모든 기사에게 어떤 일이 있어도 아서 왕과 가웨인 경만은 살려 두라고 당부했다."]], [["Then came forth Sir Gawain from the king’s host and offered combat, and Sir Lionel encountered with him, and there Sir Gawain smote Sir Lionel through the body, that he fell to the earth as if dead.", "그때 가웨인 경이 왕의 군세에서 나와 결투를 청했고, 라이오넬 경이 그와 맞붙었는데, 거기서 가웨인 경이 라이오넬 경의 몸을 꿰뚫어 그가 죽은 듯이 땅에 쓰러졌다. 그러자 큰 싸움이 벌어져 많은 사람이 죽임을 당했다."], ["Then there began a great conflict, and much people were slain; but ever Sir Launcelot did what he might to save the people on King Arthur’s party, and ever King Arthur followed Sir Launcelot to slay him; but Sir Launcelot suffered him, and would not strike again.", "그러나 란슬롯 경은 늘 아서 왕 편의 사람들을 살리려 애썼고, 아서 왕은 늘 란슬롯 경을 뒤쫓아 그를 죽이려 했다. 그러나 란슬롯 경은 왕을 참아 주며 되받아치려 하지 않았다."], ["Then Sir Bohort encountered with King Arthur, and smote him down; and he alighted and drew his sword, and said to Sir Launcelot, “Shall I make an end of this war?”", "그때 보호트 경이 아서 왕과 맞붙어 그를 쳐서 쓰러뜨렸다. 그는 말에서 내려 칼을 뽑고 란슬롯 경에게 말했다."], ["for he meant to have slain King Arthur.", "\"제가 이 전쟁을 끝내 버릴까요?\" 아서 왕을 죽이려는 뜻이었다."], ["“Not so,”", "\"그러지 말라.\""], ["said Sir Launcelot, “touch him no more, for I will never see that most noble king that made me knight either slain or shamed;”", "란슬롯 경이 말했다. \"그에게 더는 손대지 말라."], ["and therewith Sir Launcelot alighted off his horse, and took up the king, and horsed him again, and said thus: “My lord Arthur, for God’s love, cease this strife.”", "나를 기사로 만들어 주신 더없이 고귀하신 왕이 죽임을 당하거나 수치를 당하는 것을 나는 결코 보지 않을 것이니.\" 그러면서 란슬롯 경은 말에서 내려 왕을 일으켜 다시 말에 태우고 이렇게 말했다."], ["And King Arthur looked upon Sir Launcelot, and the tears burst from his eyes, thinking on the great courtesy that was in Sir Launcelot more than in any other man; and therewith the king rode his way.", "\"주군 아서시여, 하느님의 사랑을 위해 이 다툼을 멈추소서.\" 그러자 아서 왕은 란슬롯 경을 바라보았고, 그 어떤 사람보다도 란슬롯 경에게 깃든 크나큰 예의를 생각하며 두 눈에서 눈물을 쏟았다."], ["Then anon both parties withdrew to repose them, and buried the dead.", "그러면서 왕은 말을 몰아 떠나갔다. 이윽고 양편은 곧 물러나 휴식을 취하며 죽은 자들을 묻었다."]], [["But the war continued, and it was noised abroad through all Christendom, and at last it was told afore the pope; and he, considering the great goodness of King Arthur, and of Sir Launcelot, called unto him a noble clerk, which was the Bishop of Rochester, who was then in his dominions, and sent him to King Arthur, charging him that he take his queen, dame Guenever, unto him again, and make peace with Sir Launcelot.", "그러나 전쟁은 계속되었고, 그 소문이 온 그리스도교 세계에 퍼졌으며, 마침내 교황에게까지 전해졌다. 교황은 아서 왕과 란슬롯 경의 크나큰 선량함을 헤아려, 당시 자신의 영토에 있던 고귀한 성직자 로체스터의 주교를 불러 그를 아서 왕에게 보냈으니, 왕이 왕비 귀네비어 부인을 다시 자기 곁으로 받아들이고 란슬롯 경과 화평할 것을 명하였다."]], [["So, by means of this bishop, peace was made for the space of one year; and King Arthur received back the queen, and Sir Launcelot departed from the kingdom with all his knights, and went to his own country.", "이렇게 이 주교의 주선으로 일 년 동안의 화평이 이루어졌다. 아서 왕은 왕비를 다시 받아들였고, 란슬롯 경은 모든 기사와 함께 왕국을 떠나 자신의 고향으로 갔다."], ["So they shipped at Cardiff, and sailed unto Benwick, which some men call Bayonne.", "그리하여 그들은 카디프에서 배에 올라 벤위크로 항해했으니, 어떤 이들은 그곳을 바욘이라 부른다."], ["And all the people of those lands came to Sir Launcelot, and received him home right joyfully.", "그 땅의 모든 백성이 란슬롯 경에게로 와서 그를 더없이 기쁘게 맞아들였다."], ["And Sir Launcelot stablished and garnished all his towns and castles, and he greatly advanced all his noble knights, Sir Lionel and Sir Bohort, and Sir Hector de Marys, Sir Blamor, Sir Lawayne, and many others, and made them lords of lands and castles; till he left himself no more than any one of them.", "그리고 란슬롯 경은 자신의 모든 마을과 성을 정비하고 갖추었으며, 라이오넬 경과 보호트 경, 헥터 디 마리스 경, 블라모르 경, 라웨인 경, 그 밖의 많은 이를 크게 높여 그들을 땅과 성의 영주로 삼았으니, 마침내 자신에게는 그들 누구보다도 더 많은 것을 남기지 않았다."]], [["“Then Arthur made vast banquets, and strange knights From the four winds came in: and each one sat, Tho’ served with choice from air, land, stream and sea, Oft in mid-banquet measuring with his eyes His neighbor’s make and might.”", "\"그러자 아서는 거대한 잔치를 열었고, 낯선 기사들이 사방의 바람을 타고 모여들었다. 그리하여 저마다 앉아서, 하늘과 땅과 강과 바다에서 가려 뽑은 진미를 받으면서도, 잔치 한복판에서 곧잘 제 눈으로 이웃의 됨됨이와 힘을 가늠하곤 했다.\""], ["—Pelleas and Ettarre.", "─〈펠레아스와 에타르〉"]], [["But when the year was passed, King Arthur and Sir Gawain came with a great host, and landed upon Sir Launcelot’s lands, and burned and wasted all that they might overrun.", "그러나 그 한 해가 지나자, 아서 왕과 가웨인 경이 큰 군세를 거느리고 와서 란슬롯 경의 영토에 상륙하여, 휩쓸 수 있는 모든 것을 불사르고 황폐하게 만들었다. 그러자 보호트 경이 말했다."], ["Then spake Sir Bohort and said, “My lord, Sir Launcelot, give us leave to meet them in the field, and we shall make them rue the time that ever they came to this country.”", "\"주군이신 란슬롯 경이시여, 저희가 들판에서 그들을 맞아 싸우도록 허락해 주소서. 그러면 그들이 이 나라에 온 것을 두고두고 후회하게 만들겠나이다.\""], ["Then said Sir Launcelot, “I am full loath to ride out with my knights for shedding of Christian blood; so we will yet a while keep our walls, and I will send a messenger unto my lord Arthur, to propose a treaty; for better is peace than always war.”", "그러자 란슬롯 경이 말했다. \"그리스도교도의 피를 흘리는 일이기에 나는 기사들을 거느리고 말을 몰아 나가기를 몹시 꺼리노라."], ["So Sir Launcelot sent forth a damsel, and a dwarf with her, requiring King Arthur to leave his warring upon his lands; and so she started on a palfrey, and the dwarf ran by her side.", "그러니 우리는 얼마간 더 성벽을 지킬 것이며, 나는 주군 아서께 사자를 보내어 조약을 제안하겠노라. 늘 전쟁만 하는 것보다는 화평이 나으니.\""], ["And when she came to the pavilion of King Arthur, she alighted, and there met her a gentle knight, Sir Lucan, the butler, and said, “Fair damsel, come ye from Sir Launcelot du Lac?”", "그리하여 란슬롯 경은 한 처녀와 그 곁에 난쟁이 하나를 딸려 보내어, 아서 왕에게 자신의 영토에 대한 전쟁을 멈출 것을 청했다. 처녀는 작은 말을 타고 길을 나섰고, 난쟁이는 그 곁에서 달렸다."], ["“Yea, sir,”", "처녀가 아서 왕의 천막에 이르러 말에서 내리자, 그곳에서 점잖은 기사인 시종장 루칸 경이 그녀를 맞으며 말했다. \"어여쁜 처녀여, 란슬롯 두 라크 경에게서 왔는가?\""], ["she said, “I come hither to speak with the king.”", "\"그러하옵니다, 기사님.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["“Alas!”", "\"왕께 말씀드리러 왔나이다.\" \"아아!\""], ["said Sir Lucan, “my lord Arthur would be reconciled to Sir Launcelot, but Sir Gawain will not suffer him.”", "루칸 경이 말했다. \"주군 아서께서는 란슬롯 경과 화해하고자 하시나, 가웨인 경이 이를 허락지 않으신다오.\""], ["And with this Sir Lucan led the damsel to the king, where he sat with Sir Gawain, to hear what she would say.", "그러면서 루칸 경은 처녀를 왕에게로 인도했으니, 그곳에 왕이 가웨인 경과 함께 앉아 그녀가 무슨 말을 할지 들으려 했다. 그리하여 그녀가 사연을 말하자, 왕의 두 눈에서 눈물이 흘러내렸다."], ["So when she had told her tale, the tears ran out of the king’s eyes; and all the lords were forward to advise the king to be accorded with Sir Launcelot, save only Sir Gawain; and he said, “My lord, mine uncle, what will ye do?", "그리고 가웨인 경 하나만을 제외하고는 모든 영주가 앞다투어 왕에게 란슬롯 경과 화합하라고 권했다. 가웨인 경이 말했다."], ["Will you now turn back, now you are so far advanced upon your journey?", "\"주군이시며 제 외숙이시여, 어찌하시려나이까? 이토록 멀리 길을 떠나오시고도 이제 와서 돌아서시렵니까?"], ["If ye do all the world will speak shame of you.”", "그리하신다면 온 세상이 폐하를 두고 수치라 말할 것입니다.\" \"아니오.\""], ["“Nay,”", "아서 왕이 말했다. \"나는 그대가 권하는 대로 하겠소."], ["said King Arthur, “I will do as ye advise me; but do thou give the damsel her answer, for I may not speak to her for pity.”", "다만 처녀에게는 그대가 답을 주시오. 가엾어서 내가 그녀에게 말을 할 수 없으니.\""]], [["Then said Sir Gawain, “Damsel, say ye to Sir Launcelot, that it is waste labor to sue to mine uncle for peace, and say that I, Sir Gawain, send him word that I promise him, by the faith I owe unto God and to knighthood, I shall never leave him till he have slain me or I him.” So the damsel returned; and when Sir Launcelot had heard this answer the tears ran down his cheeks.", "그러자 가웨인 경이 말했다. \"처녀여, 란슬롯 경에게 전하라. 내 외숙께 화평을 구하는 것은 헛수고이며, 나 가웨인 경이 이렇게 전한다고 하라. 하느님과 기사도에 진 신의를 걸고 약속하건대, 그가 나를 죽이거나 내가 그를 죽이기 전까지 나는 결코 그를 놓아주지 않으리라.\" 이렇게 처녀는 돌아갔고, 란슬롯 경이 이 답을 듣자 두 뺨으로 눈물이 흘러내렸다."]], [["Then it befell on a day Sir Gawain came before the gates, armed at all points, and cried with a loud voice, “Where art thou now, thou false traitor, Sir Launcelot?", "그러던 어느 날 가웨인 경이 온몸을 무장하고 성문 앞에 와서 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"이 거짓된 배신자 란슬롯 경아, 지금 어디 있느냐?"], ["Why hidest thou thyself within holes and walls like a coward?", "어찌하여 비겁자처럼 굴과 성벽 안에 숨어 있느냐? 이제 나오라, 이 배신자 기사야, 내 세 형제의 죽음을 네 몸뚱이에 갚아 주리라.\""], ["Look out now, thou traitor knight, and I will avenge upon thy body the death of my three brethren.”", "란슬롯 경과 그 곁에 있던 기사들이 이 모든 말을 들었고, 그들이 그에게 말했다. \"란슬롯 경이시여, 이제는 기사답게 자신을 지키셔야 하나이다."], ["All this language heard Sir Launcelot, and the knights which were about him; and they said to him, “Sir Launcelot, now must ye defend you like a knight, or else be shamed for ever, for you have slept overlong and suffered overmuch.”", "그러지 않으면 영원히 수치를 당하실 것이니, 너무 오래 잠자코 계셨고 너무 많이 참으셨나이다.\" 그러자 란슬롯 경이 아서 왕에게 큰 소리로 말했다."], ["Then Sir Launcelot spake on high unto King Arthur, and said, “My lord Arthur, now I have forborne long, and suffered you and Sir Gawain to do what ye would, and now must I needs defend myself, inasmuch as Sir Gawain hath appealed me of treason.”", "\"주군 아서시여, 이제 저는 오래 참아 왔고, 폐하와 가웨인 경이 하고자 하시는 대로 두었나이다. 그러나 이제는 부득이 제 몸을 지켜야 하니, 가웨인 경이 저를 반역으로 고발하였기 때문입니다.\""], ["Then Sir Launcelot armed him and mounted upon his horse, and the noble knights came out of the city, and the host without stood all apart; and so the covenant was made that no man should come near the two knights, nor deal with them, till one were dead or yielded.", "그러고는 란슬롯 경이 무장하고 말에 올랐으며, 고귀한 기사들이 도시에서 나왔고, 바깥의 군세는 모두 멀찍이 물러섰다. 그리하여 둘 중 하나가 죽거나 항복하기 전까지는 누구도 두 기사에게 가까이 가거나 끼어들지 않기로 약조가 맺어졌다."]], [["Then Sir Launcelot and Sir Gawain departed a great way asunder, and then they came together with all their horses’ might, and each smote the other in the middle of their shields, but neither of them was unhorsed, but their horses fell to the earth.", "그런 다음 란슬롯 경과 가웨인 경은 서로 멀찍이 떨어졌다가, 말을 한껏 내달려 서로에게로 다가와 저마다 상대의 방패 한가운데를 쳤다. 그러나 둘 중 누구도 말에서 떨어지지 않았고, 다만 그들의 말이 땅에 쓰러졌다."], ["And then they leapt from their horses, and drew their swords, and gave many sad strokes, so that the blood burst out in many places.", "그러자 그들은 말에서 뛰어내려 칼을 뽑고 수많은 매서운 칼질을 주고받았으니, 여러 군데서 피가 솟구쳤다."], ["Now Sir Gawain had this gift from a holy man, that every day in the year, from morning to noon, his strength was increased threefold, and then it fell again to its natural measure.", "그런데 가웨인 경은 어느 거룩한 사람에게서 이런 능력을 받았으니, 한 해의 매일 아침부터 정오까지 그의 힘이 세 배로 늘어났다가 다시 본래의 크기로 줄어드는 것이었다."], ["Sir Launcelot was aware of this, and therefore, during the three hours that Sir Gawain’s strength was at the height, Sir Launcelot covered himself with his shield, and kept his might in reserve.", "란슬롯 경은 이를 알고 있었기에, 가웨인 경의 힘이 절정에 이르는 세 시간 동안 방패로 몸을 가리고 자신의 힘을 비축해 두었다."], ["And during that time Sir Gawain gave him many sad brunts, that all the knights that looked on marvelled how Sir Launcelot might endure them.", "그동안 가웨인 경이 그에게 수많은 매서운 일격을 가했으니, 지켜보던 모든 기사가 란슬롯 경이 어찌 그것을 견디는지 놀라워했다."], ["Then, when it was past noon, Sir Gawain had only his own might; and when Sir Launcelot felt him so brought down he stretched himself up, and doubled his strokes, and gave Sir Gawain such a buffet that he fell down on his side; and Sir Launcelot drew back and would strike no more.", "그러다 정오가 지나자 가웨인 경에게는 자신의 본래 힘만 남았고, 란슬롯 경은 그가 그렇게 약해진 것을 느끼자 몸을 곧추세워 칼질을 곱절로 늘려, 가웨인 경에게 일격을 가하니 그가 옆으로 쓰러졌다. 그러자 란슬롯 경은 뒤로 물러나 더는 치려 하지 않았다."], ["“Why withdrawest thou, false traitor?”", "\"어찌하여 물러서느냐, 거짓된 배신자야?\" 그때 가웨인 경이 말했다."], ["then said Sir Gawain; “now turn again and slay me, for if thou leave me thus when I am whole again, I shall do battle with thee again.”", "\"이제 다시 돌아서서 나를 죽여라. 네가 나를 이렇게 두면, 내가 다시 멀쩡해졌을 때 너와 또 싸울 것이다.\""], ["“I shall endure you, sir, by God’s grace,”", "\"하느님의 은총으로 그대를 참아 주겠소, 기사여.\""], ["said Sir Launcelot, “but know thou well Sir Gawain, I will never smite a felled knight.”", "란슬롯 경이 말했다. \"그러나 똑똑히 아시오, 가웨인 경."], ["And so Sir Launcelot went into the city, and Sir Gawain was borne into King Arthur’s pavilion, and his wounds were looked to.", "나는 쓰러진 기사는 결코 치지 않을 것이오.\" 그리하여 란슬롯 경은 도시로 들어갔고, 가웨인 경은 아서 왕의 천막으로 옮겨져 그의 상처가 치료되었다."]], [["Thus the siege endured, and Sir Gawain lay helpless near a month; and when he was near recovered came tidings unto King Arthur that made him return with all his host to England.", "이렇게 포위는 이어졌고, 가웨인 경은 거의 한 달 동안 꼼짝 못 하고 누워 있었다. 그리고 그가 거의 회복되었을 무렵, 아서 왕에게 한 소식이 전해졌으니, 그로 인해 왕은 온 군세를 거느리고 잉글랜드로 돌아가게 되었다."]], [["————", "————"]], [["# MORTE D’ARTHUR", "# MORTE D’ARTHUR"]], [["SIR MODRED was left ruler of all England, and he caused letters to be written, as if from beyond sea, that King Arthur was slain in battle.", "모드레드 경은 잉글랜드 전역의 통치자로 남아, 마치 바다 너머에서 온 것인 양 아서 왕이 전투에서 죽었다는 내용의 편지가 쓰이도록 했다."], ["So he called a Parliament, and made himself be crowned king; and he took the queen Guenever, and said plainly that he would wed her, but she escaped from him and took refuge in the Tower of London.", "그리하여 그는 의회를 소집해 스스로 왕으로 즉위했다. 또한 그는 왕비 귀네비어를 차지하고는 그녀와 혼인하겠노라고 노골적으로 말했으나, 그녀는 그에게서 달아나 런던탑으로 피신했다."], ["And Sir Modred went and laid siege about the Tower of London, and made great assaults thereat, but all might not avail him.", "그러자 모드레드 경은 그곳으로 가서 런던탑을 포위하고 거센 공격을 퍼부었으나, 그 무엇도 그에게 도움이 되지 못했다."], ["Then came word to Sir Modred that King Arthur had raised the siege of Sir Launcelot, and was coming home.", "그때 모드레드 경에게, 아서 왕이 란슬롯 경에 대한 포위를 풀고 본국으로 돌아오고 있다는 전갈이 당도했다. 그러자 모드레드 경은 나라 안의 모든 영주를 소집했다."], ["Then Sir Modred summoned all the barony of the land; and much people drew unto Sir Modred, and said they would abide with him for better and for worse; and he drew a great host to Dover, for there he heard say that King Arthur would arrive.", "또한 수많은 사람들이 모드레드 경에게로 모여들어, 좋든 나쁘든 그와 함께하겠노라고 말했다. 그리하여 그는 도버로 대군을 이끌고 갔으니, 그곳으로 아서 왕이 도착하리라는 말을 들었기 때문이었다."]], [["“I hear the steps of Modred in the west, And with him many of thy people, and knights Once thine, whom thou hast loved, but grosser grown Than heathen, spitting at their vows and thee.”", "\"나는 서쪽에서 모드레드의 발소리를 듣노라, 그와 함께 그대의 백성도 많이 따르며, 한때 그대의 기사였던 자들, 그대가 사랑하였으나 이제 이교도보다 더 천박해져, 자신들의 맹세와 그대에게 침을 뱉는 자들이로다.\""], ["—The Passing of Arthur.", "— 「아서의 떠남」."]], [["And as Sir Modred was at Dover with his host, came King Arthur, with a great number of ships and galleys, and there was Sir Modred awaiting upon the landing.", "그리고 모드레드 경이 군대를 거느리고 도버에 있을 때, 아서 왕이 수많은 배와 갤리선을 이끌고 당도했으니, 모드레드 경은 그 상륙 지점에서 기다리고 있었다."], ["Then was there launching of great boats and small, full of noble men of arms, and there was much slaughter of gentle knights on both parts.", "그리하여 크고 작은 배들이 무장한 고귀한 사람들로 가득 찬 채 진수되었고, 양편 모두에서 고결한 기사들의 살육이 크게 일어났다."], ["But King Arthur was so courageous, there might no manner of knights prevent him to land, and his knights fiercely followed him; and so they landed, and put Sir Modred aback so that he fled, and all his people.", "그러나 아서 왕은 어찌나 용맹했던지, 그 어떤 기사도 그의 상륙을 막을 수 없었으며, 그의 기사들도 맹렬히 그를 뒤따랐다. 그리하여 그들은 상륙해 모드레드 경을 뒤로 밀어붙였으니, 그가 달아나고 그의 모든 백성도 달아났다."], ["And when the battle was done, King Arthur commanded to bury his people that were dead.", "그리고 전투가 끝나자 아서 왕은 죽은 자기 백성들을 매장하라고 명했다."], ["And then was noble Sir Gawain found, in a great boat, lying more than half dead.", "그때 고귀한 가웨인 경이 큰 배 안에서, 반쯤 죽은 채 누워 있는 것이 발견되었다."], ["And King Arthur went to him, and made sorrow out of measure.", "아서 왕이 그에게로 가서 한없는 슬픔에 잠겼다. \"외숙부님,\""], ["“Mine uncle,”", "가웨인 경이 말했다."], ["said Sir Gawain, “know thou well my death-day is come, and all is through mine own hastiness and wilfulness, for I am smitten upon the old wound which Sir Launcelot gave me, of which I feel I must die.", "\"제 임종의 날이 왔음을 잘 알아 주십시오. 이 모든 것은 저 자신의 성급함과 고집 때문이니, 란슬롯 경이 제게 입혔던 옛 상처를 다시 맞았고, 그 때문에 제가 죽으리라는 것을 느낍니다."], ["And had Sir Launcelot been with you as of old, this war had never begun, and of all this I am the cause.”", "그리고 란슬롯 경이 옛날처럼 폐하와 함께 있었더라면 이 전쟁은 결코 시작되지 않았을 것이며, 이 모든 일의 원인이 바로 저입니다.\""], ["Then Sir Gawain prayed the king to send for Sir Launcelot, and to cherish him above all other knights.", "그러고 나서 가웨인 경은 왕에게 란슬롯 경을 불러들이고 그를 다른 모든 기사보다 더 아껴 달라고 청했다."], ["And so at the hour of noon Sir Gawain yielded up his spirit, and then the king bade inter him in a chapel within Dover Castle; and there all men may see the skull of him, and the same wound is seen that Sir Launcelot gave him in battle.", "그리하여 정오 무렵 가웨인 경은 숨을 거두었고, 왕은 그를 도버 성 안의 한 예배당에 안장하라고 명했다. 그곳에서는 누구든 그의 두개골을 볼 수 있고, 란슬롯 경이 전투에서 그에게 입힌 바로 그 상처도 보인다."]], [["Then was it told the king that Sir Modred had pitched his camp upon Barrendown; and the king rode thither, and there was a great battle betwixt them, and King Arthur’s party stood best, and Sir Modred and his party fled unto Canterbury.", "그때 왕에게, 모드레드 경이 배런다운에 진을 쳤다는 말이 전해졌다. 왕이 그곳으로 말을 달려가니 양편 사이에 큰 전투가 벌어졌고, 아서 왕의 무리가 가장 우세했으며, 모드레드 경과 그의 무리는 캔터베리로 달아났다."]], [["And there was a day assigned betwixt King Arthur and Sir Modred that they should meet upon a down beside Salisbury, and not far from the sea-side, to do battle yet again.", "그리고 아서 왕과 모드레드 경 사이에는, 솔즈베리 인근, 바닷가에서 멀지 않은 어느 구릉에서 다시 한 번 전투를 벌이기로 날이 정해졌다. 그리고 밤에 왕이 잠들었을 때, 그는 기이한 꿈을 꾸었다."], ["And at night, as the king slept, he dreamed a wonderful dream.", "그에게는 정말로 가웨인 경이, 아리따운 여인들을 여럿 거느리고 자기에게 다가오는 것처럼 보였다. 아서 왕이 그를 보고 말했다."], ["It seemed him verily that there came Sir Gawain unto him, with a number of fair ladies with him.", "\"어서 오너라, 내 누이의 아들아. 나는 네가 죽은 줄로만 알았는데, 이제 네가 살아 있는 것을 보니 내 기쁨이 크구나."], ["And when King Arthur saw him, he said, “Welcome, my sister’s son; I weened thou hadst been dead; and now I see thee alive great is my joy.", "그런데, 오, 아리따운 조카야, 너와 함께 이리로 온 이 여인들은 누구냐?\" \"전하,\""], ["But, O fair nephew, what be these ladies that hither be come with you?”", "가웨인 경이 말했다. \"이들은 모두 제가 살아 있는 사람이었을 때 그들을 위해 싸웠던 여인들입니다."], ["“Sir,” said Sir Gawain, “all these be ladies for whom I have fought when I was a living man; and because I did battle for them in righteous quarrel they have given me grace to bring me hither unto you to warn you of your death, if ye fight to-morrow with Sir Modred.", "그리고 제가 의로운 다툼에서 그들을 위해 싸웠기에, 그들이 제게 은총을 베풀어 저를 이리로 데려와 전하께 경고하게 하였으니, 만일 전하께서 내일 모드레드 경과 싸우신다면 전하의 죽음을 맞이하시리라는 것입니다."], ["Therefore take ye treaty, and proffer you largely for a month’s delay; for within a month shall come Sir Launcelot and all his noble knights, and rescue you worshipfully, and slay Sir Modred and all that hold with him.”", "그러니 전하께서는 화친을 맺으시고, 한 달의 유예를 위해 넉넉히 양보하십시오. 한 달 안에 란슬롯 경과 그의 모든 고귀한 기사들이 와서 전하를 명예롭게 구하고, 모드레드 경과 그를 따르는 모든 자를 무찌를 것이기 때문입니다.\""], ["And then Sir Gawain and all the ladies vanished.", "그러고 나서 가웨인 경과 모든 여인들이 사라졌다."], ["And anon the king called to fetch his noble lords and wise bishops unto him.", "그러자 곧 왕은 고귀한 영주들과 지혜로운 주교들을 자기에게로 불러오게 했다."], ["And when they were come, the king told them his vision, and what Sir Gawain had told him.", "그들이 오자 왕은 자기가 본 환영과 가웨인 경이 자기에게 한 말을 들려주었다."], ["Then the king sent Sir Lucan, the butler, and Sir Bedivere, with two bishops, and charged them in any wise to take a treaty for a month and a day with Sir Modred.", "그러고 나서 왕은 시종장 루칸 경과 베디비어 경을 두 주교와 함께 보내며, 무슨 수를 써서라도 모드레드 경과 한 달 하고도 하루의 화친을 맺으라고 명했다."], ["So they departed, and came to Sir Modred; and so, at the last, Sir Modred was agreed to have Cornwall and Kent during Arthur’s life, and all England after his death.", "그리하여 그들은 떠나 모드레드 경에게 갔다. 그리하여 마침내 모드레드 경은 아서가 살아 있는 동안에는 콘월과 켄트를 갖고, 그가 죽은 뒤에는 잉글랜드 전역을 갖는 데에 합의했다."]], [["“Sir Modred; he the nearest to the king, His nephew, ever like a subtle beast Lay couchant with his eyes upon the throne, Ready to spring, waiting a chance.”", "\"모드레드 경, 그는 왕에게 가장 가까운 자, 그의 조카, 늘 음흉한 짐승처럼 두 눈으로 왕좌를 노리며 웅크려 누워, 기회를 기다리며 덤벼들 채비를 하던 자였다.\""], ["—Guinevere.", "— 「기네비어」."]], [["Then was it agreed that King Arthur and Sir Modred should meet betwixt both their hosts, and each of them should bring fourteen persons, and then and there they should sign the treaty.", "그러고 나서, 아서 왕과 모드레드 경이 양편 군대 사이에서 만나되, 각자 열네 사람씩을 데려와 그 자리에서 화친에 서명하기로 합의되었다. 그리고 아서 왕과 그의 기사들이 나아갈 채비를 했을 때, 그는 자기 군대 전체에게 이렇게 경고했다."], ["And when King Arthur and his knights were prepared to go forth, he warned all his host, “If so be ye see any sword drawn, look ye come on fiercely, and slay whomsoever withstandeth, for I in no wise trust that traitor, Sir Modred.”", "\"만일 칼 한 자루라도 뽑히는 것을 보거든, 너희는 맹렬히 달려나와 맞서는 자는 누구든 베어 버려라. 나는 저 배신자 모드레드 경을 결코 믿지 않기 때문이다.\""], ["In like wise Sir Modred warned his host.", "모드레드 경도 마찬가지로 자기 군대에 경고했다."], ["So they met, and were agreed and accorded thoroughly.", "그리하여 그들은 만나, 빈틈없이 합의하고 화목하게 되었다."], ["And wine was brought, and they drank.", "그리고 포도주가 들어와 그들이 마셨다."], ["Right then came an adder out of a little heath-bush, and stung a knight on the foot.", "바로 그때, 작은 히스 덤불에서 살무사 한 마리가 나와 어느 기사의 발을 물었다."], ["And when the knight felt him sting, he looked down and saw the adder, and then he drew his sword to slay the adder, and thought of no other harm.", "그 기사가 물린 것을 느끼고 아래를 내려다보니 살무사가 보였고, 그래서 그는 살무사를 죽이려고 칼을 뽑았을 뿐 다른 해의를 품지는 않았다."], ["And when the host on both sides saw that sword drawn, they blew trumpets and horns, and shouted greatly.", "그런데 양편 군대가 그 뽑힌 칼을 보자, 나팔과 뿔피리를 불며 크게 외쳤다."], ["And King Arthur took his horse, and rode to his party, saying, “Alas, this unhappy day!”", "그리고 아서 왕은 말에 올라 자기 무리에게 달려가며 말했다. \"아, 이 불행한 날이여!\""], ["And Sir Modred did in like wise.", "모드레드 경도 마찬가지로 했다."], ["And never was there a more doleful battle in Christian land.", "그리하여 그리스도교 땅에서 일찍이 그보다 더 비통한 전투는 없었다."], ["And ever King Arthur rode throughout the battle, and did full nobly, as a worthy king should, and Sir Modred that day did his devoir, and put himself in great peril.", "아서 왕은 줄곧 전장을 누비며, 가치 있는 왕이 마땅히 해야 할 바대로 더없이 고귀하게 싸웠고, 모드레드 경도 그날 자기 임무를 다하며 큰 위험에 몸을 던졌다."], ["And thus they fought all the long day, till the most of all the noble knights lay dead upon the ground.", "그리하여 그들은 긴 하루 내내 싸웠고, 마침내 모든 고귀한 기사 대부분이 땅 위에 죽어 누웠다."], ["Then the king looked about him, and saw of all his host were left alive but two knights, Sir Lucan, the butler, and Sir Bedivere, his brother, and they were full sore wounded.", "그때 왕이 주위를 둘러보니, 자기 군대 전체에서 살아남은 자는 단 두 기사뿐이었으니, 시종장 루칸 경과 그의 형제 베디비어 경이었고, 그들은 심한 상처를 입고 있었다."]], [["Then King Arthur saw where Sir Modred leaned upon his sword among a great heap of dead men.", "그때 아서 왕은 모드레드 경이 죽은 자들의 큰 무더기 사이에서 자기 칼에 기대어 있는 곳을 보았다. \"이제 내 창을 다오.\""], ["“Now give me my spear,”", "아서가 루칸 경에게 말했다."], ["said Arthur unto Sir Lucan; “for yonder I espy the traitor that hast wrought all this woe.”", "\"저기 이 모든 비통함을 빚어낸 그 배신자가 보이는구나.\" \"전하, 그를 내버려 두십시오.\""], ["“Sir, let him be,”", "루칸 경이 말했다."], ["said Sir Lucan; “for if ye pass this unhappy day, ye shall be right well revenged upon him.", "\"전하께서 이 불행한 날을 넘기신다면, 마땅히 그에게 제대로 복수하실 것입니다."], ["Remember what the sprite of Sir Gawain told you, and leave off now, for ye have won the field; and if ye leave off now this evil day of destiny is past.”", "가웨인 경의 혼령이 전하께 한 말을 기억하시고, 이제 그만두십시오. 전하께서 전장을 차지하셨으니, 지금 그만두신다면 이 운명의 사악한 날도 지나갈 것입니다.\""], ["“Betide me life, betide me death,”", "\"살든 죽든,\" 아서 왕이 말했다."], ["said King Arthur, “he shall not now escape my hands.”", "\"그는 이제 내 손아귀를 벗어나지 못한다.\""], ["Then the king took his spear in both hands, and ran toward Sir Modred, crying, “Traitor, now is thy death-day come.”", "그러고 나서 왕은 두 손으로 창을 잡고 모드레드 경을 향해 달려가며 외쳤다. \"배신자야, 이제 네 임종의 날이 왔다.\""], ["And there King Arthur smote Sir Modred under the shield, with a thrust of his spear through the body.", "그리하여 아서 왕은 방패 아래로 모드레드 경을 찔러, 그 몸을 창으로 꿰뚫었다."], ["And when Sir Modred felt that he had his death-wound, with the might that he had he smote King Arthur, with his sword holden in both his hands, on the side of the head, that the sword pierced the helmet and the brain-pan; and then Sir Modred fell stark dead upon the earth.", "그리고 모드레드 경은 자기가 치명상을 입은 것을 느끼자, 남은 힘을 다해 두 손으로 잡은 칼로 아서 왕의 머리 옆쪽을 내리쳤으니, 그 칼이 투구와 두개골을 꿰뚫었다. 그러고 나서 모드레드 경은 땅 위에 완전히 죽어 쓰러졌다."], ["And the noble Arthur fell in a swoon to the earth.", "그리고 고귀한 아서는 정신을 잃고 땅에 쓰러졌다."], ["And Sir Lucan, the butler, and Sir Bedivere raised him up, and gently led him betwixt them both to a little chapel not far from the sea-side.", "그러자 시종장 루칸 경과 베디비어 경이 그를 일으켜, 둘이서 부축하여 바닷가에서 멀지 않은 작은 예배당으로 조심스레 데려갔다."], ["And when the king was there he thought him well eased.", "그리고 왕은 그곳에 이르자 한결 편안해졌다고 여겼다."], ["Then heard they people cry in the field.", "그때 그들은 들판에서 사람들이 외치는 소리를 들었다."], ["And Sir Lucan went to see what that cry betokened; and he saw by the moonlight that pillers and robbers were come to rob the dead.", "루칸 경이 그 외침이 무엇을 뜻하는지 보러 갔다. 그리하여 그는 달빛 속에서, 약탈자와 도적들이 죽은 자들을 약탈하러 온 것을 보았다."], ["And he returned, and said to the king, “By my rede, it is best that we bring you to some town.”", "그래서 그는 돌아와 왕에게 말했다. \"제 생각으로는, 전하를 어느 마을로 모셔 가는 것이 가장 좋겠습니다.\""], ["“I would it were so,”", "\"그리하면 좋겠다.\""], ["said the king.", "왕이 말했다."], ["And when the king tried to go he fainted.", "그리고 왕이 가려고 하니 그는 기절했다."], ["Then Sir Lucan took up the king on the one part, and Sir Bedivere on the other part; and in the lifting, Sir Lucan fell in a swoon to the earth, for he was grievously wounded.", "그러자 루칸 경이 한쪽에서, 베디비어 경이 다른 쪽에서 왕을 들어 올렸다. 그런데 들어 올리던 중에 루칸 경이 정신을 잃고 땅에 쓰러졌으니, 그가 심한 상처를 입었기 때문이었다."], ["And then the noble knight’s heart burst.", "그러고 나서 그 고귀한 기사의 심장이 터졌다."], ["And when the king awoke he beheld Sir Lucan how he lay foaming at the mouth, and speechless.", "왕이 깨어나 보니, 루칸 경이 입에 거품을 물고 말도 못한 채 누워 있는 것이 보였다. \"아아!\""], ["“Alas!”", "왕이 말했다."], ["said the king, “this is to me a full heavy sight, to see this noble duke so die for my sake; for he would have holpen me that had more need of help than I, and he would not complain, his heart was so set to help me.”", "\"이 고귀한 공작이 나 때문에 이렇게 죽는 것을 보는 것은 내게 더없이 무거운 광경이로다. 나보다 도움이 더 필요했던 그가 오히려 나를 도우려 했고, 불평하지 않으려 했으니, 그의 마음이 그토록 나를 돕는 데에 쏠려 있었기 때문이다.\""], ["Then Sir Bedivere wept for his brother.", "그러자 베디비어 경이 자기 형제를 위해 울었다."], ["“Leave this mourning and weeping,”", "\"이 애도와 눈물을 멈추어라.\" 왕이 말했다."], ["said the king, “for wilt thou well, if I might live myself, the death of Sir Lucan would grieve me evermore; but my time hieth fast.", "\"잘 알아 두어라, 만일 내가 살아남을 수 있다 해도, 루칸 경의 죽음은 영원히 나를 슬프게 할 것이다. 그러나 나의 때가 빠르게 다가오는구나."], ["Therefore,”", "그러니,\" 아서가 베디비어 경에게 말했다."], ["said Arthur unto Sir Bedivere, “take thou Excalibar, my good sword, and go with it to yonder water-side; and when thou comest there I charge thee throw my sword in that water, and come again and tell me what thou there seest.”", "\"내 좋은 칼 엑스칼리버를 가지고 저기 물가로 가거라. 그리고 그곳에 이르거든 내 칼을 저 물에 던지고, 다시 돌아와 네가 그곳에서 본 것을 내게 말하라고 명하노라.\""], ["“My lord,”", "\"전하,\""], ["said Sir Bedivere, “your commandment shall be done.”", "베디비어 경이 말했다. \"분부대로 하겠나이다.\""], ["So Sir Bedivere departed, and by the way he beheld that noble sword, that the pommel and the haft were all of precious stones; and then he said to himself, “If I throw this rich sword into the water no good shall come thereof, but only harm and loss.”", "그리하여 베디비어 경은 떠났는데, 가는 길에 그 고귀한 칼을 보니 자루 끝과 손잡이가 온통 보석으로 되어 있었다. 그래서 그는 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다."], ["And then Sir Bedivere hid Excalibar under a tree.", "\"이 값진 칼을 물에 던진다면 아무 좋은 일도 생기지 않고 오직 해와 손실만 있으리라.\""], ["And so, as soon as he might, he came again to the king.", "그러고 나서 베디비어 경은 엑스칼리버를 어느 나무 아래 숨겼다. 그리하여 그는 되도록 빨리 다시 왕에게 돌아왔다."], ["“What sawest thou there?”", "\"그곳에서 무엇을 보았느냐?\""], ["said the king.", "왕이 말했다."], ["“Sir,”", "\"전하,\""], ["he said, “I saw nothing.”", "그가 말했다. \"아무것도 보지 못했나이다.\""], ["“Alas!", "\"아아!"], ["thou hast deceived me,”", "네가 나를 속였구나.\""], ["said the king.", "왕이 말했다."], ["“Go thou lightly again, and as thou love me, spare not to throw it in.”", "\"지체 없이 다시 가서, 나를 사랑하거든 주저 말고 그것을 던져 넣어라.\""], ["Then Sir Bedivere went again, and took the sword in his hand to throw it; but again it beseemed him but sin and shame to throw away that noble sword, and he hid it away again, and returned, and told the king he had done his commandment.", "그러자 베디비어 경은 다시 가서, 그것을 던지려고 칼을 손에 들었다. 그러나 다시금 그 고귀한 칼을 내버리는 것이 죄이자 수치로만 여겨졌기에, 그는 그것을 다시 숨기고 돌아와, 왕에게 분부대로 했노라고 말했다."], ["“What sawest thou there?”", "\"그곳에서 무엇을 보았느냐?\""], ["said the king.", "왕이 말했다."], ["“Sir,”", "\"전하,\""], ["he said, “I saw nothing but waters deep and waves wan.”", "그가 말했다. \"깊은 물과 창백한 물결밖에는 보지 못했나이다.\""], ["“Ah, traitor untrue!”", "\"아, 진실치 못한 배신자여!\""], ["said King Arthur, “now hast thou betrayed me twice.", "아서 왕이 말했다. \"이제 네가 나를 두 번이나 배신했구나."], ["And yet thou art named a noble knight, and hast been lief and dear to me.", "그러고도 너는 고귀한 기사로 불리며, 내게 사랑스럽고 소중한 자였다."], ["But now go again, and do as I bid thee, for thy long tarrying putteth me in jeopardy of my life.”", "그러나 이제 다시 가서, 내가 명하는 대로 하라. 네가 오래 머뭇거리는 것이 내 목숨을 위태롭게 하고 있다.\""], ["Then Sir Bedivere went to the sword, and lightly took it up, and went to the water-side, and he bound the girdle about the hilt, and then he threw the sword as far into the water as he might.", "그러자 베디비어 경은 칼이 있는 곳으로 가서, 그것을 가볍게 집어 들고 물가로 가, 손잡이에 띠를 묶고는, 칼을 물속으로 던질 수 있는 한 멀리 던졌다."], ["And there came an arm and a hand out of the water, and met it, and caught it, and shook it thrice and brandished it, and then vanished away the hand with the sword in the water.", "그러자 물속에서 팔과 손이 나와 그것을 받아 잡고는, 세 번 흔들어 휘두른 다음, 칼을 든 그 손이 물속으로 사라졌다."]], [["Then Sir Bedivere came again to the king, and told him what he saw.", "그러고 나서 베디비어 경은 다시 왕에게 돌아와, 자기가 본 것을 말했다. \"나를 여기서 데려가 다오.\""], ["“Help me hence,”", "왕이 말했다."], ["said the king, “for I fear I have tarried too long.”", "\"내가 너무 오래 지체한 것 같아 두렵구나.\" 그러자 베디비어 경이 왕을 등에 업고 그 물가로 갔다."], ["Then Sir Bedivere took the king on his back, and so went with him to that water-side; and when they came there, even fast by the bank there rode a little barge with many fair ladies in it, and among them was a queen; and all had black hoods, and they wept and shrieked when they saw King Arthur.", "그곳에 이르니, 바로 물가 가까이에 작은 거룻배 한 척이 떠 있었는데, 그 안에는 아리따운 여인들이 많이 타고 있었고, 그들 가운데에는 한 왕비가 있었다. 모두가 검은 두건을 쓰고 있었으며, 아서 왕을 보자 울며 비명을 질렀다."]], [["“Now put me in the barge,”", "\"이제 나를 거룻배에 태워 다오.\""], ["said the king.", "왕이 말했다."], ["And there received him three queens with great mourning, and in one of their laps King Arthur laid his head.", "그러자 그곳에서 세 왕비가 큰 애도와 함께 그를 맞이했고, 그들 가운데 한 사람의 무릎에 아서 왕은 머리를 뉘었다."], ["And the queen said, “Ah, dear brother, why have ye tarried so long?", "그러자 그 왕비가 말했다. \"아, 사랑하는 형제여, 어찌하여 그토록 오래 지체하셨나이까?"], ["Alas!", "아아!"], ["this wound on your head hath caught overmuch cold.”", "머리에 입은 이 상처가 너무 많은 냉기를 머금었나이다.\""], ["And then they rowed from the land, and Sir Bedivere beheld them go from him.", "그러고 나서 그들이 뭍에서 노를 저어 나아가자, 베디비어 경은 그들이 자기에게서 멀어져 가는 것을 바라보았다."], ["Then he cried: “Ah, my lord Arthur, will ye leave me here alone among mine enemies?”", "그러자 그가 외쳤다. \"아, 나의 주군 아서여, 저를 여기 적들 사이에 홀로 남겨 두려 하시나이까?\""], ["“Comfort thyself,”", "\"스스로 위안을 삼아라.\""], ["said the king, “for in me is no further help; for I will to the Isle of Avalon, to heal me of my grievous wound.”", "왕이 말했다. \"내게는 더 이상 도움이 없으니, 나는 나의 깊은 상처를 치유하기 위해 아발론 섬으로 가려 한다.\""], ["And as soon as Sir Bedivere had lost sight of the barge, he wept and wailed; then he took the forest, and went all that night, and in the morning he was ware of a chapel and a hermitage.", "그리고 베디비어 경은 거룻배가 시야에서 사라지자, 울며 통곡했다. 그러고 나서 그는 숲으로 들어가 밤새도록 걸었고, 아침에 한 예배당과 은수처를 알아보았다."]], [["Then went Sir Bedivere thither; and when he came into the chapel, he saw where lay an hermit on the ground, near a tomb that was newly graven.", "그러자 베디비어 경이 그곳으로 갔다. 그가 예배당에 들어서니, 새로 새긴 무덤 곁의 땅바닥에 한 은수자가 누워 있는 것이 보였다."], ["“Sir,”", "\"어르신,\""], ["said Sir Bedivere, “what man is there buried that ye pray so near unto?”", "베디비어 경이 말했다. \"그토록 가까이에서 기도드리는 그곳에 묻힌 자는 어떤 사람입니까?\""], ["“Fair son,”", "\"착한 아들이여,\""], ["said the hermit, “I know not verily.", "은수자가 말했다. \"나도 정말 알지 못하오."], ["But this night there came a number of ladies, and brought hither one dead, and prayed me to bury him.”", "다만 이 밤에 여인들이 여럿 와서, 죽은 자 한 사람을 이리로 데려와 매장해 달라고 내게 청했소.\""], ["“Alas!”", "\"아아!\""], ["said Sir Bedivere, “that was my lord, King Arthur.”", "베디비어 경이 말했다. \"그분이 나의 주군, 아서 왕이셨구나.\""], ["Then Sir Bedivere swooned; and when he awoke he prayed the hermit he might abide with him, to live with fasting and prayers.", "그러고 나서 베디비어 경은 정신을 잃었다. 깨어났을 때 그는 은수자에게, 자기가 그와 함께 머물며 단식과 기도로 살게 해 달라고 청했다."], ["“Ye are welcome,”", "\"그대를 환영하오.\""], ["said the hermit.", "은수자가 말했다."], ["So there bode Sir Bedivere with the hermit; and he put on poor clothes, and served the hermit full lowly in fasting and in prayers.", "그리하여 베디비어 경은 은수자와 함께 머물렀다. 그리고 그는 초라한 옷을 걸치고, 단식과 기도 속에서 더없이 겸손하게 은수자를 섬겼다."]], [["Thus of Arthur I find never more written in books that be authorized, nor more of the very certainty of his death; but thus was he led away in a ship, wherein were three queens; the one was King Arthur’s sister, Queen Morgane le Fay; the other was Viviane, the Lady of the Lake; and the third was the queen of North Galis.", "이리하여 아서에 관해서는, 권위 있는 책들 속에서 더 이상 쓰인 바를 나는 찾지 못하며, 그의 죽음의 명확한 진상에 관해서도 더는 알 수 없다. 그러나 이렇게 그는 배에 실려 떠나갔으니, 그 배 안에는 세 왕비가 있었다."], ["And this tale Sir Bedivere, knight of the Table Round, made to be written.", "한 사람은 아서 왕의 누이인 모르가나 르 페이 왕비요, 다른 한 사람은 호수의 여인 비비안이요, 셋째는 노스 갈리스의 왕비였다. 그리고 이 이야기는 원탁의 기사 베디비어 경이 글로 쓰이게 한 것이다."]], [["Yet some men say that King Arthur is not dead, but hid away into another place, and men say that he shall come again and reign over England.", "그러나 어떤 이들은 아서 왕이 죽지 않고 다른 곳에 숨겨졌으며, 그가 다시 돌아와 잉글랜드를 다스리리라고 말한다."], ["But many say that there is written on his tomb this verse:", "그러나 많은 이들은 그의 무덤에 이 시구가 쓰여 있다고 말한다."]], [["“Hie jacet Arthurus, Rex quondam, Rexque futurus.”", "\"히크 야케트 아르투루스, 렉스 콴담, 렉스퀘 푸투루스.\""], ["Here Arthur lies, King once and King to be.", "여기 아서가 누워 있도다, 한때의 왕이요 장차 올 왕이여."]], [["And when Queen Guenever understood that King Arthur was slain, and all the noble knights with him, she stole away, and five ladies with her; and so she went to Almesbury, and made herself a nun, and ware white clothes and black, and took great penance as ever did sinful lady, and lived in fasting, prayers, and alms-deeds.", "그리고 귀네비어 왕비는 아서 왕이 그와 함께한 모든 고귀한 기사들과 더불어 죽었음을 알게 되자, 다섯 여인을 데리고 몰래 빠져나갔다. 그리하여 그녀는 앰즈베리로 가서 수녀가 되었고, 희고 검은 옷을 입었으며, 죄지은 여인이 일찍이 했던 그 어떤 고행보다 큰 고행을 행하고, 단식과 기도와 자선 속에서 살았다."], ["And there she was abbess and ruler of the nuns.", "그리고 그곳에서 그녀는 수녀들의 수녀원장이자 지도자가 되었다."]], [["“And when she came to Almesbury she spake There to the nuns, and said, ‘Mine enemies Pursue me, but, O peaceful Sisterhood, Receive, and yield me sanctuary, nor ask Her name to whom ye yield it, till her time To tell you:’ and her beauty, grace and power Wrought as a charm upon them, and they spared To ask it.”", "\"그리고 그녀는 앰즈베리에 이르러 그곳 수녀들에게 말하기를, '내 적들이 나를 쫓는구나. 그러나, 오, 평화로운 자매들이여, 나를 받아들여 성역을 베풀어 다오, 그리고 그것을 베푸는 그 여인의 이름은, 그녀가 그대들에게 말할 때가 올 때까지 묻지 말아 다오.'"], ["—Guinevere.", "그리고 그녀의 아름다움과 우아함과 위엄이 그들에게 마법처럼 작용하니, 그들은 그것을 묻기를 삼갔다.\" — 「기네비어」."]], [["Now turn we from her, and speak of Sir Launcelot of the Lake.", "이제 우리는 그녀에게서 눈을 돌려, 호수의 란슬롯 경에 관해 이야기하자."]], [["When Sir Launcelot heard in his country that Sir Modred was crowned king of England, and made war against his own uncle, King Arthur, then was Sir Launcelot wroth out of measure, and said to his kinsmen: “Alas, that double traitor, Sir Modred!", "란슬롯 경은 자기 나라에서, 모드레드 경이 잉글랜드의 왕으로 즉위해 자기 외숙부인 아서 왕에게 전쟁을 일으켰다는 소식을 들었을 때, 한없는 분노에 사로잡혀 자기 친족들에게 말했다. \"아, 저 이중의 배신자 모드레드 경이여!"], ["now it repenteth me that ever he escaped out of my hands.”", "이제 나는 그가 내 손아귀를 빠져나간 것이 후회스럽구나.\" 그러고 나서 란슬롯 경과 그의 동료들은 잉글랜드로 건너가기 위해 배와 갤리선을 갖추어 온갖 서두름으로 채비를 했다."], ["Then Sir Launcelot and his fellows made ready in all haste, with ships and galleys, to pass into England; and so he passed over till he came to Dover, and there he landed with a great army.", "그리하여 그는 바다를 건너 도버에 이르렀고, 그곳에서 대군을 이끌고 상륙했다. 그때 란슬롯 경은 아서 왕이 죽었다는 말을 들었다."], ["Then Sir Launcelot was told that King Arthur was slain.", "\"아아!\" 란슬롯 경이 말했다."], ["“Alas!”", "\"이것은 내게 일찍이 닥친 가장 무거운 소식이로다.\""], ["said Sir Launcelot, “this is the heaviest tidings that ever came to me.”", "그러고 나서 그는 왕들과 공작들과 영주들과 기사들을 불러 모아 이렇게 말했다. \"나의 아리따운 영주들이여, 나와 함께 이 나라로 와 준 그대들 모두에게 감사하오."], ["Then he called the kings, dukes, barons, and knights, and said thus: “My fair lords, I thank you all for coming into this country with me, but we came too late, and that shall repent me while I live.", "그러나 우리는 너무 늦게 왔고, 그것이 내가 사는 동안 나를 후회하게 할 것이오. 그러나 일이 이렇게 되었으니,\""], ["But since it is so,”", "란슬롯 경이 말했다. \"나는 직접 말을 달려 나의 여인, 귀네비어 왕비를 찾으려 하오."], ["said Sir Launcelot, “I will myself ride and seek my lady, Queen Guenever, for I have heard say she hath fled into the west; therefore ye shall abide me here fifteen days, and if I come not within that time, then take your ships and your host, and depart into your country.”", "그녀가 서쪽으로 달아났다는 말을 들었기 때문이오. 그러니 그대들은 이곳에서 보름 동안 나를 기다리되, 만일 그 안에 내가 돌아오지 않거든, 그대들의 배와 군대를 이끌고 그대들의 나라로 떠나시오.\""]], [["So Sir Launcelot departed and rode westerly, and there he sought many days; and at last he came to a nunnery, and was seen of Queen Guenever as he walked in the cloister; and when she saw him she swooned away. And when she might speak she bade him to be called to her. And when Sir Launcelot was brought to her she said: “Sir Launcelot, I require thee and beseech thee, for all the love that ever was betwixt us, that thou never see me more, but return to thy kingdom and take thee a wife, and live with her with joy and bliss; and pray for me to my Lord, that I may get my soul’s health.” “Nay, madam,” said Sir Launcelot, “wit you well that I shall never do; but the same destiny that ye have taken you to will I take me unto, for to please and serve God.” And so they parted, with tears and much lamentation; and the ladies bare the queen to her chamber, and Sir Launcelot took his horse and rode away, weeping.", "그리하여 란슬롯 경은 떠나 서쪽으로 말을 달려, 그곳에서 여러 날 동안 찾아 헤맸다. 그리고 마침내 그는 한 수녀원에 이르렀으니, 회랑을 거니는 그를 귀네비어 왕비가 보았다. 그녀는 그를 보자 정신을 잃고 쓰러졌다. 그리고 말을 할 수 있게 되자 그를 자기에게로 불러오라고 명했다. 란슬롯 경이 그녀에게로 인도되자 그녀가 말했다. \"란슬롯 경, 일찍이 우리 사이에 있었던 그 모든 사랑을 걸고, 그대에게 요구하고 간청하노니, 그대는 다시는 나를 보지 말고, 그대의 왕국으로 돌아가 아내를 맞이하여, 기쁨과 축복 속에서 그녀와 함께 사시오. 그리고 나를 위해 나의 주님께 기도하여, 내가 내 영혼의 구원을 얻게 해 주시오.\" \"아니오, 부인,\" 란슬롯 경이 말했다. \"잘 아시오, 나는 결코 그리하지 않을 것이오. 그대가 택한 바로 그 운명을 나 또한 택하여, 하느님을 기쁘게 하고 섬기겠소.\" 그리하여 그들은 눈물과 큰 비탄 속에서 헤어졌다. 그리고 여인들이 왕비를 그녀의 방으로 데려갔고, 란슬롯 경은 자기 말에 올라 울며 떠나갔다."]], [["And at last Sir Launcelot was ware of a hermitage and a chapel, and then he heard a little bell ring to mass; and thither he rode and alighted, and tied his horse to the gate, and heard mass.", "그리고 마침내 란슬롯 경은 한 은수처와 예배당을 알아보았고, 그때 미사를 알리는 작은 종이 울리는 소리를 들었다. 그래서 그는 그곳으로 말을 달려가 내려서, 자기 말을 문에 매어 두고 미사를 들었다."], ["And he that sang the mass was the hermit with whom Sir Bedivere had taken up his abode; and Sir Bedivere knew Sir Launcelot, and they spake together after mass.", "그리고 미사를 노래하던 자는 바로 베디비어 경이 거처를 정했던 그 은수자였다. 그리고 베디비어 경은 란슬롯 경을 알아보았고, 그들은 미사가 끝난 뒤 함께 이야기를 나누었다."], ["But when Sir Bedivere had told his tale, Sir Launcelot’s heart almost burst for sorrow.", "그런데 베디비어 경이 자기 이야기를 들려주자, 란슬롯 경의 심장은 슬픔으로 거의 터질 듯했다."], ["Then he kneeled down, and prayed the hermit to shrive him, and besought that he might be his brother.", "그러고 나서 그는 무릎을 꿇고, 은수자에게 자기 죄를 사하여 달라고 기도하며, 자기가 그의 형제가 되게 해 달라고 간청했다."], ["Then the hermit said, “I will gladly;”", "그러자 은수자가 말했다. \"기꺼이 그리하겠소.\""], ["and then he put a habit upon Sir Launcelot, and there he served God day and night, with prayers and fastings.", "그러고 나서 그는 란슬롯 경에게 수도복을 입혔고, 그곳에서 란슬롯 경은 기도와 단식으로 밤낮없이 하느님을 섬겼다."]], [["And the great host abode at Dover till the end of the fifteen days set by Sir Launcelot, and then Sir Bohort made them to go home again to their own country; and Sir Bohort, Sir Hector de Marys, Sir Blamor, and many others, took on them to ride through all England to seek Sir Launcelot.", "그리고 대군은 란슬롯 경이 정한 보름이 끝날 때까지 도버에 머물렀고, 그러고 나서 보호트 경이 그들을 다시 자기들의 나라로 돌아가게 했다. 그리고 보호트 경, 마리스의 헥토르 경, 블라모르 경, 그 밖의 많은 이들은 란슬롯 경을 찾기 위해 잉글랜드 전역을 말 달려 찾아 나서기로 했다."], ["So Sir Bohort by fortune rode until he came to the same chapel where Sir Launcelot was; and when he saw Sir Launcelot in that manner of clothing he prayed the hermit that he might be in that same.", "그리하여 보호트 경은 운명에 이끌려 말을 달리다, 란슬롯 경이 있던 바로 그 예배당에 이르렀다. 그리고 그는 란슬롯 경이 그런 차림의 옷을 입고 있는 것을 보고, 은수자에게 자기도 그와 똑같이 되게 해 달라고 기도했다."], ["And so there was an habit put upon him, and there he lived in prayers and fasting.", "그리하여 그에게도 수도복이 입혀졌고, 그곳에서 그는 기도와 단식 속에서 살았다."], ["And within half a year came others of the knights, their fellows, and took such a habit as Sir Launcelot and Sir Bohort had.", "그리고 반년 안에 그들의 동료인 다른 기사들이 와서, 란슬롯 경과 보호트 경이 입은 것과 같은 수도복을 걸쳤다."], ["Thus they endured in great penance six years.", "이리하여 그들은 육 년 동안 큰 고행 속에서 견뎌 냈다."]], [["And upon a night there came a vision to Sir Launcelot, and charged him to haste toward Almesbury, and “by the time thou come there, thou shalt find Queen Guenever dead.”", "그리고 어느 밤 란슬롯 경에게 환영이 찾아와, 그에게 앰즈베리로 서둘러 가라고 명하며 이렇게 일렀다. \"네가 그곳에 이를 즈음, 너는 귀네비어 왕비가 죽어 있는 것을 보게 되리라.\""], ["Then Sir Launcelot rose up early and told the hermit thereof.", "그러자 란슬롯 경은 일찍 일어나 그것을 은수자에게 알렸다."], ["Then said the hermit, “It were well that ye disobey not this vision.”", "그러자 은수자가 말했다. \"이 환영을 거역하지 않는 것이 좋겠소.\""], ["And Sir Launcelot took his seven companions with him, and on foot they went from Glastonbury to Almesbury, which is more than thirty miles.", "그리하여 란슬롯 경은 일곱 동료를 데리고, 글래스턴베리에서 앰즈베리까지 걸어서 갔으니, 그 거리는 삼십 마일이 넘었다."], ["And when they were come to Almesbury, they found that Queen Guenever died but half an hour before.", "그리고 그들이 앰즈베리에 이르렀을 때, 귀네비어 왕비가 불과 반 시간 전에 죽었음을 알게 되었다."], ["Then Sir Launcelot saw her visage, but he wept not greatly, but sighed.", "그때 란슬롯 경은 그녀의 얼굴을 보았으나, 크게 울지는 않고 한숨을 지었다."], ["And so he did all the observance of the service himself, both the “dirige” at night, and at morn he sang mass.", "그리하여 그는 밤에 드리는 위령 기도와 아침에 노래하는 미사까지, 장례의 모든 의식을 직접 행했다."], ["And there was prepared an horse-bier, and Sir Launcelot and his fellows followed the bier on foot from Almesbury until they came to Glastonbury; and she was wrapped in cered clothes, and laid in a coffin of marble.", "그리고 말이 끄는 영구가 마련되었고, 란슬롯 경과 그의 동료들은 앰즈베리에서 글래스턴베리에 이르기까지 걸어서 영구를 뒤따랐다. 그리고 그녀는 밀랍 입힌 천에 싸여 대리석 관에 안치되었다."], ["And when she was put in the earth Sir Launcelot swooned, and lay long as one dead.", "그리고 그녀가 땅속에 묻히자 란슬롯 경은 정신을 잃고, 죽은 자처럼 오래 누워 있었다."]], [["And Sir Launcelot never after ate but little meat, nor drank; but continually mourned.", "그리고 란슬롯 경은 그 후로 음식을 거의 먹지도 마시지도 않고, 줄곧 애도하기만 했다. 그리고 육 주 안에 란슬롯 경은 병이 들었다."], ["And within six weeks Sir Launcelot fell sick; and he sent for the hermit and all his true fellows, and said, “Sir hermit, I pray you give me all my rights that a Christian man ought to have.”", "그래서 그는 은수자와 자기의 모든 진실한 동료들을 불러 말했다. \"은수자 어르신, 그리스도교인이라면 마땅히 받아야 할 모든 예식을 제게 베풀어 주시기를 청합니다.\""], ["“It shall not need,”", "\"그럴 필요 없소.\" 은수자와 그의 모든 동료들이 말했다."], ["said the hermit and all his fellows; “it is but heaviness of your blood, and to-morrow morn you shall be well.”", "\"이것은 단지 그대 피의 무거움일 뿐이니, 내일 아침이면 그대는 나을 것이오.\" \"나의 아리따운 영주들이여,\""], ["“My fair lords,”", "란슬롯 경이 말했다."], ["said Sir Launcelot, “my careful body will into the earth; I have warning more than now I will say; therefore give me my rights.”", "\"내 근심에 찬 몸은 땅속으로 들어가려 하오. 나는 지금 말하려는 것 이상의 경고를 받았소."], ["So when he was houseled and aneled, and had all that a Christian man ought to have, he prayed the hermit that his fellows might bear his body to Joyous Garde.", "그러니 내게 예식을 베풀어 주시오.\" 그리하여 그가 성체를 받고 종부 성사를 받아, 그리스도교인이 마땅히 받아야 할 모든 것을 받고 나자, 그는 은수자에게 자기 동료들이 자기 시신을 조이어스 가드로 운구하게 해 달라고 기도했다."], ["(Some men say it was Alnwick, and some say it was Bamborough.) “It repenteth me sore,”", "(어떤 이들은 그곳이 앤윅이라 말하고, 어떤 이들은 뱀버러라고 말한다.) \"내게 몹시 후회스러운 일이오.\""], ["said Sir Launcelot, “but I made a vow aforetime that in Joyous Garde I would be buried.”", "란슬롯 경이 말했다. \"그러나 나는 일찍이, 조이어스 가드에 묻히겠노라고 서원했소.\""], ["Then there was weeping and wringing of hands among his fellows.", "그러자 그의 동료들 사이에서 울음과 손을 쥐어짜는 비탄이 일었다. 그리고 그날 밤 란슬롯 경은 죽었다."], ["And that night Sir Launcelot died; and when Sir Bohort and his fellows came to his bedside the next morning they found him stark dead; and he lay as if he had smiled, and the sweetest savor all about him that ever they knew.", "그리고 이튿날 아침 보호트 경과 그의 동료들이 그의 침상에 이르러 보니, 그가 완전히 죽어 있는 것을 발견했다. 그리고 그는 마치 미소 짓고 있는 듯이 누워 있었고, 그의 주위에는 일찍이 그들이 알았던 그 어떤 향기보다 가장 감미로운 향기가 감돌았다."]], [["And they put Sir Launcelot into the same horse-bier that Queen Guenever was laid in, and the hermit and they altogether went with the body till they came to Joyous Garde.", "그리고 그들은 란슬롯 경을, 귀네비어 왕비가 안치되었던 바로 그 말이 끄는 영구에 뉘었고, 은수자와 그들 모두가 시신과 함께 가서 조이어스 가드에 이르렀다."], ["And there they laid his corpse in the body of the quire, and sang and read many psalms and prayers over him.", "그리고 그곳에서 그들은 그의 유해를 성가대석 한가운데에 안치하고, 그를 위해 많은 시편과 기도문을 노래하고 읽었다."], ["And ever his visage was laid open and naked, that all folks might behold him.", "그리고 그의 얼굴은 줄곧 드러난 채 가리지 않았으니, 모든 사람이 그를 바라볼 수 있게 하기 위함이었다."], ["And right thus, as they were at their service, there came Sir Hector de Maris, that had seven years sought Sir Launcelot, his brother, through all England, Scotland and Wales.", "그리고 바로 이렇게, 그들이 예배를 드리고 있을 때, 칠 년 동안 잉글랜드와 스코틀랜드와 웨일스 전역에서 자기 형제 란슬롯 경을 찾아 헤맸던 마리스의 헥토르 경이 왔다."], ["And when Sir Hector heard such sounds in the chapel of Joyous Garde he alighted and came into the quire.", "그리고 헥토르 경은 조이어스 가드의 예배당에서 그런 소리가 나는 것을 듣고, 말에서 내려 성가대석으로 들어왔다."], ["And all they knew Sir Hector.", "그리고 그들 모두 헥토르 경을 알아보았다."], ["Then went Sir Bohort, and told him how there lay Sir Launcelot, his brother, dead.", "그때 보호트 경이 가서, 그의 형제 란슬롯 경이 그곳에 죽어 누워 있다고 그에게 일렀다."], ["Then Sir Hector threw his shield, his sword, and helm from him.", "그러자 헥토르 경은 자기 방패와 칼과 투구를 내던졌다."], ["And when he beheld Sir Launcelot’s visage it were hard for any tongue to tell the doleful complaints he made for his brother.", "그리고 그는 란슬롯 경의 얼굴을 보고서, 그 어떤 혀로도 그가 자기 형제를 위해 토해 낸 비통한 탄식을 다 전하기 어려울 정도였다."], ["“Ah, Sir Launcelot!”", "\"아, 란슬롯 경이여!\""], ["he said, “there thou liest.", "그가 말했다. \"그대가 거기 누워 있구려."], ["And now I dare to say thou wert never matched of none earthly knight’s hand.", "그리고 이제 나는 감히 말하노니, 그대는 일찍이 그 어떤 지상의 기사의 손으로도 맞상대 된 적이 없었소. 그리고 그대는 일찍이 방패를 든 가장 예의 바른 기사였소."], ["And thou wert the courteousest knight that ever bare shield; and thou wert the truest friend to thy lover that ever bestrode horse; and thou wert the truest lover, of a sinful man, that ever loved woman; and thou wert the kindest man that ever struck with sword.", "그리고 그대는 일찍이 말에 올라탄 가장 진실한 벗이었소. 그리고 그대는 일찍이 죄 많은 사람으로서 여인을 사랑한 가장 진실한 연인이었소."], ["And thou wert the goodliest person that ever came among press of knights.", "그리고 그대는 일찍이 칼을 휘두른 가장 친절한 사람이었소. 그리고 그대는 일찍이 기사들의 무리 가운데 끼었던 가장 훌륭한 사람이었소."], ["And thou wert the meekest man, and the gentlest, that ever ate in hall among ladies.", "그리고 그대는 일찍이 여인들 사이에서 홀에서 식사한 가장 온순하고 가장 정중한 사람이었소."], ["And thou wert the sternest knight to thy mortal foe that ever put spear in the rest.”", "그리고 그대는 일찍이 창을 창받침에 걸친, 자기 철천지원수에게 가장 준엄한 기사였소.\""], ["Then there was weeping and dolor out of measure.", "그러자 그곳에서 한없는 울음과 비탄이 일었다."], ["Thus they kept Sir Launcelot’s corpse fifteen days, and then they buried it with great devotion.", "이리하여 그들은 란슬롯 경의 유해를 보름 동안 지켰고, 그러고 나서 큰 경건함으로 그를 매장했다."]], [["Then they went back with the hermit to his hermitage.", "그러고 나서 그들은 은수자와 함께 그의 은수처로 돌아갔다."], ["And Sir Bedivere was there ever still hermit to his life’s end.", "그리고 베디비어 경은 그곳에서 생애가 끝날 때까지 줄곧 은수자로 머물렀다."], ["And Sir Bohort, Sir Hector, Sir Blamor, and Sir Bleoberis went into the Holy Land.", "그리고 보호트 경, 헥토르 경, 블라모르 경, 블레오베리스 경은 성지로 갔다."], ["And these four knights did many battles upon the miscreants, the Turks; and there they died upon a Good Friday, as it pleased God.", "그리고 이 네 기사는 이교도인 튀르크인들을 상대로 많은 전투를 벌였다. 그리고 그곳에서 그들은, 하느님의 뜻에 따라, 어느 성금요일에 죽었다."]], [["Thus endeth this noble and joyous book, entitled “La Morte d’Arthur;”", "이리하여 「라 모르트 다르튀르」라는 제목의 이 고귀하고 즐거운 책이 끝난다."], ["notwithstanding it treateth of the birth, life, and acts of the said King Arthur, and of his noble Knights of the Round Table, their marvellous enquests and adventures, the achieving of the Sangreal, and, in the end, le Morte d’Arthur, with the dolorous death and departing out of this world of them all.", "그럼에도 이 책은 앞서 말한 아서 왕과 그의 고귀한 원탁의 기사들의 탄생과 생애와 행적, 그들의 경이로운 탐색과 모험, 성배의 성취, 그리고 마침내 라 모르트 다르튀르, 곧 그들 모두의 비통한 죽음과 이 세상을 떠남을 다룬다."], ["Which book was reduced into English by Sir Thomas Mallory, Knight, and divided into twenty-one books, chaptered and imprinted and finished in the Abbey Westmestre, the last day of July, the year of our Lord MCCCCLXXXV.", "이 책은 기사 토머스 맬러리 경에 의해 영어로 옮겨져 스물한 권으로 나뉘었고, 장으로 구분되어 인쇄되었으며, 우리 주님의 해 1485년 7월의 마지막 날에 웨스트민스터 수도원에서 완성되었다. 캑스턴이 나를 만들게 하였노라."]], [["Caxton me fieri fecit.", "마비노게온\n서론적 주석"]], [["IT has been well known to the literati and antiquarians of Europe that there exist in the great public libraries voluminous manuscripts of romances and tales once popular, but which on the invention of printing had already become antiquated, and fallen into neglect.", "유럽의 문인들과 고고학자들에게 잘 알려져 있는 바, 큰 공공 도서관들에는 한때 인기를 누렸으나 인쇄술의 발명과 더불어 이미 시대에 뒤떨어져 방치되고 만 로맨스와 이야기들의 방대한 필사본이 존재한다."], ["They were therefore never printed, and seldom perused even by the learned, until about half a century ago, when attention was again directed to them, and they were found very curious monuments of ancient manners, habits, and modes of thinking.", "그리하여 그것들은 결코 인쇄되지 못했고, 학자들조차 좀처럼 정독하지 않았다. 그러다 반세기쯤 전에 이르러 다시 관심이 그것들로 향했고, 옛 풍습과 관습과 사고방식의 매우 흥미로운 기념물임이 밝혀졌다."], ["Several have since been edited, some by individuals, as Sir Walter Scott and the poet Southey, others by antiquarian societies.", "그 후로 여럿이 편집되었으니, 더러는 월터 스콧 경이나 시인 사우디 같은 개인에 의해, 또 더러는 고고학회에 의해 이루어졌다."], ["The class of readers which could be counted on for such publications was so small that no inducement of profit could be found to tempt editors and publishers to give them to the world.", "그러한 출판물을 기대할 수 있는 독자층이 어찌나 적었던지, 편집자와 출판인이 그것들을 세상에 내놓도록 유혹할 만한 이익의 동기를 찾을 수 없었다."], ["It was therefore only a few, and those the most accessible, which were put in print.", "그리하여 인쇄된 것은 단지 소수, 그것도 가장 접근하기 쉬운 것들뿐이었다."], ["There was a class of manuscripts of this kind which were known, or rather suspected, to be both curious and valuable, but which it seemed almost hopeless to expect ever to see in fair printed English.", "이런 종류의 필사본 가운데에는, 흥미롭고도 가치 있다고 알려진, 아니 차라리 그렇게 짐작된 한 부류가 있었으나, 그것이 제대로 인쇄된 영어로 나오는 것을 기대하기란 거의 가망 없어 보였다."], ["These were the Welsh popular tales called Mabinogeon, a plural word, the singular being Mabinogi, a tale.", "그것은 「마비노게온」이라 불리는 웨일스의 대중 이야기들로, 이는 복수형 단어이며, 단수형은 「마비노기」, 곧 한 편의 이야기이다."], ["Manuscripts of these were contained in the Bodleian Library at Oxford and elsewhere, but the difficulty was to find translators and editors.", "이것들의 필사본은 옥스퍼드의 보들리 도서관을 비롯한 여러 곳에 소장되어 있었으나, 어려움은 번역자와 편집자를 찾는 데에 있었다."], ["The Welsh is a spoken language among the peasantry of Wales, but is entirely neglected by the learned, unless they are natives of the principality.", "웨일스어는 웨일스의 소작농들 사이에서 쓰이는 구어이지만, 학자들이 그 공국 출신이 아닌 한 전적으로 등한시한다."], ["Of the few Welsh scholars none were found who took sufficient interest in this branch of learning to give these productions to the English public.", "소수의 웨일스어 학자들 가운데에서도, 이 분야의 학문에 충분한 관심을 가지고 이 작품들을 영어권 대중에게 내놓으려는 자는 찾을 수 없었다."], ["Southey and Scott, and others, who, like them, loved the old romantic legends of their country, often urged upon the Welsh literati the duty of reproducing the Mabinogeon.", "사우디와 스콧, 그리고 그들처럼 자기 나라의 옛 낭만적 전설을 사랑했던 다른 이들은, 「마비노게온」을 다시 펴내야 할 의무를 웨일스 문인들에게 종종 역설했다."], ["Southey, in the preface of his edition of “Morte d’Arthur,”", "사우디는 자신이 펴낸 「모르트 다르튀르」의 서문에서 이렇게 말한다."], ["says: “The specimens which I have seen are exceedingly curious; nor is there a greater desideratum in British literature than an edition of these tales, with a literal version, and such comments as Mr.", "\"내가 본 표본들은 지극히 흥미롭다. 그리고 영국 문학에서, 이 이야기들을 직역본과 함께, 그리고 모든 이들 가운데 데이비스 씨가 가장 잘 해낼 수 있는 그런 주석과 함께 펴낸 판본보다 더 절실히 요청되는 것은 없다."], ["Davies of all men is best qualified to give. Certain it is that many of the round table fictions originated in Wales, or in Bretagne, and probably might still be traced there.”", "원탁의 허구 가운데 많은 것이 웨일스나 브르타뉴에서 비롯되었으며, 아마도 지금도 그곳에서 그 흔적을 찾을 수 있으리라는 것은 분명하다.\""]], [["Again, in a letter to Sir Charles W.", "또한 1819년에 쓴, 찰스 W."], ["W.", "W."], ["Wynn, dated 1819, he says:", "윈 경에게 보낸 편지에서 그는 이렇게 말한다."]], [["“I begin almost to despair of ever seeing more of the Mabinogeon; and yet if some competent Welshman could be found to edit it carefully, with as literal a version as possible, I am sure it might be made worth his while by a subscription, printing a small edition at a high price, perhaps two hundred at five guineas.", "\"나는 「마비노게온」을 더 보게 되리라는 희망을 거의 단념하기 시작했소. 그러나 만일 어느 유능한 웨일스인을 찾아내어, 되도록 직역에 가까운 번역과 함께 그것을 정성껏 편집하게 한다면, 예약 구독을 통해 그의 수고를 보람 있게 할 수 있으리라 확신하오."], ["I myself would gladly subscribe at that price per volume for such an edition of the whole of your genuine remains in prose and verse.", "소량의 판본을 높은 가격에, 이를테면 이백 부를 각 오 기니에 인쇄하는 식으로 말이오. 나 자신도 그대의 진정한 산문과 운문 유산 전체를 그런 식으로 펴낸 판본이라면, 그 권당 가격에 기꺼이 예약하겠소."], ["Till some such collection is made, the ‘gentlemen of Wales’ ought to be prohibited from wearing a leek; ay, and interdicted from toasted cheese also.", "그런 선집이 만들어지기 전까지는, '웨일스의 신사들'은 부추를 다는 것이 금지되어야 마땅하오. 그렇소, 그리고 구운 치즈를 먹는 것 또한 금지되어야 하오."], ["Your bards would have met with better usage if they had been Scotchmen.”", "그대들의 음유시인들이 스코틀랜드인이었다면 더 나은 대접을 받았을 텐데 말이오.\""]], [["Sharon Turner and Sir Walter Scott also expressed a similar wish for the publication of the Welsh manuscripts.", "섀런 터너와 월터 스콧 경 또한 웨일스 필사본의 출판에 대해 비슷한 소망을 표했다."], ["The former took part in an attempt to effect it, through the instrumentality of a Mr. Owen, a Welshman, but, we judge, by what Southey says of him, imperfectly acquainted with English.", "전자는 웨일스인인 오언이라는 사람을 통해 그것을 실현하려는 시도에 참여했으나, 사우디가 그에 관해 한 말로 판단하건대, 그는 영어에 미숙했던 것 같다."], ["Southey’s language is “William Owen lent me three parts of the Mabinogeon, delightfully translated into so Welsh an idiom and syntax that such a translation is as instructive as an original.”", "사우디의 말은 이러하다. \"윌리엄 오언이 「마비노게온」의 세 부분을 내게 빌려주었는데, 어찌나 웨일스풍의 어법과 구문으로 흐뭇하게 번역되었던지, 그런 번역은 원작만큼이나 유익하다.\""], ["In another letter he adds, “Let Sharon make his language grammatical, but not alter their idiom in the slightest point.”", "또 다른 편지에서 그는 덧붙인다. \"섀런이 그의 언어를 문법에 맞게 다듬되, 그들의 어법은 털끝만큼도 바꾸지 않게 하시오.\""]], [["It is probable Mr. Owen did not proceed far in an undertaking which, so executed, could expect but little popular patronage.", "오언 씨는 아마도 그런 식으로 수행된 사업에서 멀리 나아가지 못했을 텐데, 그렇게 해서는 대중의 후원을 거의 기대할 수 없었기 때문이다."], ["It was not till an individual should appear possessed of the requisite knowledge of the two languages, of enthusiasm sufficient for the task, and of pecuniary resources sufficient to be independent of the booksellers and of the reading public, that such a work could be confidently expected.", "두 언어에 대한 필요한 지식을 갖추고, 그 과업에 충분한 열정을 지녔으며, 서적상과 독서 대중으로부터 독립적일 만큼 충분한 재력을 가진 인물이 나타나기 전까지는, 그런 작업을 확신을 가지고 기대할 수 없었다."], ["Such an individual has, since Southey’s day and Scott’s, appeared in the person of Lady Charlotte Guest, an English lady united to a gentleman of property in Wales, who, having acquired the language of the principality, and become enthusiastically fond of its literary treasures, has given them to the English reader, in a dress which the printer’s and the engraver’s arts have done their best to adorn.", "그러한 인물이, 사우디와 스콧의 시대 이후에, 샬럿 게스트 부인이라는 사람으로 나타났다. 웨일스에 재산을 가진 신사와 결혼한 잉글랜드 여인인 그녀는, 그 공국의 언어를 습득하고 그 문학적 보물에 열렬히 빠져들어, 인쇄공과 판각공의 기예가 최선을 다해 꾸민 차림새로 그것들을 영어권 독자에게 내놓았다."], ["In four royal octavo volumes containing the Welsh originals, the translation, and ample illustrations from French, German, and other contemporary and affiliated literature, the Mabinogeon is spread before us.", "웨일스어 원문과 번역, 그리고 프랑스, 독일을 비롯한 동시대 및 관련 문학에서 가져온 풍부한 삽화를 담은 사절판 네 권으로, 「마비노게온」이 우리 앞에 펼쳐져 있다."], ["To the antiquarian and the student of language and ethnology an invaluable treasure, it yet can hardly in such a form win its way to popular acquaintance.", "고고학자와 언어 및 민족학 연구자에게는 더없이 귀중한 보물이지만, 그런 형태로는 좀처럼 대중의 친숙함을 얻기 어려울 것이다."], ["We claim no other merit than that of bringing it to the knowledge of our readers, of abridging its details, of selecting its most attractive portions, and of faithfully preserving throughout the style in which Lady Guest has clothed her legends.", "우리는 그것을 우리 독자들에게 알리고, 그 세부를 간추리며, 가장 매력적인 부분을 가려 뽑고, 게스트 부인이 자신의 전설들에 입힌 문체를 시종 충실히 보존했다는 것 외에는 다른 어떤 공로도 주장하지 않는다."], ["For this service we hope that our readers will confess we have laid them under no light obligation.", "이 봉사에 대해, 우리는 우리 독자들이 우리가 그들에게 결코 가볍지 않은 은혜를 베풀었음을 인정해 주기를 바란다."]], [["————", "————"]], [["# THE BRITONS", "# THE BRITONS"]], [["THE earliest inhabitants of Britain are supposed to have been a branch of that great family known in history by the designation of Celts.", "브리튼의 가장 이른 시기 거주민들은 역사상 켈트족이라는 명칭으로 알려진 저 위대한 종족의 한 갈래였던 것으로 추정된다."], ["Cambria, which is a frequent name for Wales, is thought to be derived from Cymri, the name which the Welsh traditions apply to an immigrant people who entered the island from the adjacent continent.", "웨일스를 흔히 가리키는 이름인 캄브리아[Cambria]는 킴리[Cymri]에서 유래한 것으로 여겨지는데, 이는 인접한 대륙에서 이 섬으로 들어온 이주민들을 웨일스의 전승이 일컫는 이름이다."], ["This name is thought to be identical with those of Cimmerians and Cimbri, under which the Greek and Roman historians describe a barbarous people, who spread themselves from the north of the Euxine over the whole of Northwestern Europe.", "이 이름은 키메리오이[Cimmerians]나 킴브리[Cimbri]와 동일한 것으로 간주되는데, 그리스와 로마의 역사가들은 이 명칭으로 에욱세이노스[흑해] 북쪽에서 북서 유럽 전역으로 퍼져 나간 어느 야만 종족을 서술하였다."]], [["The origin of the names Wales and Welsh has been much canvassed.", "웨일스[Wales]와 웰시[Welsh]라는 이름의 기원에 관해서는 많은 논의가 있었다."], ["Some writers make them a derivation from Gael or Gaul, which names are said to signify “woodlanders;”", "어떤 저술가들은 이를 게일[Gael]이나 갈[Gaul]에서 파생된 것으로 보는데, 이 이름들은 \"숲에 사는 사람들\"을 뜻한다고 한다."], ["others observe that Walsh, in the northern languages, signifies a stranger, and that the aboriginal Britons were so called by those who at a later era invaded the island and possessed the greater part of it, the Saxons and Angles.", "또 다른 이들은 북방의 언어에서 웰시[Walsh]가 이방인을 뜻하며, 후대에 이 섬을 침략하여 그 대부분을 차지한 색슨족과 앵글족이 원주민인 브리튼인들을 그렇게 불렀다는 점에 주목한다."]], [["The Romans held Britain from the invasion of Julius Cæsar till their voluntary withdrawal from the island, A.D. 420,—that is, about five hundred years.", "로마인들은 율리우스 카이사르의 침공으로부터 그들이 자발적으로 이 섬에서 철수한 기원후 420년까지, 다시 말해 약 오백 년 동안 브리튼을 지배하였다."], ["In that time there must have been a wide diffusion of their arts and institutions among the natives.", "그 기간에 그들의 기예와 제도가 원주민들 사이에 널리 퍼졌음이 분명하다."], ["The remains of roads, cities, and fortifications show that they did much to develop and improve the country, while those of their villas and castles prove that many of the settlers possessed wealth and taste for the ornamental arts.", "도로와 도시와 요새의 유적은 그들이 이 나라를 발전시키고 개량하는 데 많은 일을 했음을 보여 주며, 그들의 별장과 성채의 유적은 정착민 가운데 다수가 부와 더불어 장식 예술에 대한 안목을 지녔음을 입증한다."], ["Yet the Roman sway was sustained chiefly by force, and never extended over the entire island.", "그러나 로마의 통치는 주로 무력으로 유지되었으며, 섬 전체에 미친 적은 없었다."], ["The northern portion, now Scotland, remained independent, and the western portion, constituting Wales and Cornwall, was only nominally subjected.", "지금의 스코틀랜드에 해당하는 북부 지역은 독립을 유지하였고, 웨일스와 콘월을 이루는 서부 지역은 명목상으로만 복속되었을 뿐이다."]], [["Neither did the later invading hordes succeed in subduing the remoter sections of the island.", "후대의 침략 무리들 역시 이 섬의 외진 지역들을 정복하는 데 성공하지 못했다."], ["For ages after the arrival of the Saxons under Hengist and Horsa, A.D. 449, the whole western coast of Britain was possessed by the aboriginal inhabitants, engaged in constant warfare with the invaders.", "헹기스트와 호르사의 인솔 아래 색슨족이 도래한 기원후 449년 이후로도 여러 세대에 걸쳐 브리튼의 서쪽 해안 전역은 원주민들이 차지하고 있었고, 그들은 침략자들과 끊임없는 전쟁을 벌였다."]], [["It has, therefore, been a favorite boast of the people of Wales and Cornwall that the original British stock flourishes in its unmixed purity only among them.", "그리하여 웨일스와 콘월 사람들이 즐겨 내세운 자랑은, 원래의 브리튼 혈통이 잡스러움 없는 순수함을 지닌 채 번성하고 있는 곳은 오직 자기들뿐이라는 것이었다."], ["We see this notion flashing out in poetry occasionally, as when Gray, in “The Bard,” prophetically describing Queen Elizabeth, who was of the Tudor, a Welsh race, says:", "우리는 이러한 관념이 이따금 시 속에서 번뜩이는 것을 보게 되는데, 가령 그레이가 「음유시인」에서 웨일스 계통인 튜더 가문 출신의 엘리자베스 여왕을 예언적으로 묘사하며 이렇게 말한 대목이 그러하다."]], [["“Her eye proclaims her of the Briton line;”", "\"그분의 눈은 브리튼 혈통임을 선포하노라.\""]], [["and, contrasting the princes of the Tudor with those of the Norman race, he exclaims:", "그리고 튜더 가문의 군주들을 노르만 혈통의 군주들과 대비하면서 그는 이렇게 외친다."]], [["“All hail, ye genuine kings, Britannia’s issue, hail!”", "\"만세, 그대 참된 왕들이여, 브리타니아의 후예들이여, 만세!\""]], [["# THE WELSH LANGUAGE AND LITERATURE", "# THE WELSH LANGUAGE AND LITERATURE"]], [["The Welsh language is one of the oldest in Europe.", "웨일스어는 유럽에서 가장 오래된 언어 가운데 하나다."], ["It possesses poems the origin of which is referred with probability to the sixth century.", "그 기원이 6세기로 거슬러 올라갈 개연성이 있는 시들을 지니고 있다."], ["The language of some of these is so antiquated that the best scholars differ about the interpretation of many passages; but, generally speaking, the body of poetry which the Welsh possess, from the year 1000 downwards, is intelligible to those who are acquainted with the modern language.", "이 가운데 일부는 언어가 너무도 옛스러워 최고의 학자들조차 많은 구절의 해석을 두고 의견이 엇갈린다. 그러나 대체로 말하자면, 웨일스인들이 보유한 시가 가운데 서기 1000년 이후의 것들은 현대어를 아는 이들에게는 이해할 만하다."]], [["Till within the last half-century these compositions remained buried in the libraries of colleges or of individuals, and so difficult of access that no successful attempt was made to give them to the world.", "지난 반세기에 이르기까지 이 작품들은 여러 대학이나 개인의 서고에 묻힌 채로 남아 있었고, 그 접근이 너무도 어려워 이를 세상에 내놓으려는 성공적인 시도가 이루어지지 못했다."], ["This reproach was removed after ineffectual appeals to the patriotism of the gentry of Wales, by Owen Jones, a furrier of London, who at his own expense collected and published the chief productions of Welsh literature, under the title of the Myvyrian Archæology of Wales.", "웨일스 신사 계층의 애국심에 호소했으나 실효가 없자, 런던의 모피상인 오언 존스가 이 오명을 씻어 내었으니, 그는 자비를 들여 웨일스 문학의 주요 산물을 수집하여 「웨일스 미비리언 고고학」이라는 제목으로 출간하였다."], ["In this task he was assisted by Dr. Owen and other Welsh scholars.", "이 작업에서 그는 오언 박사를 비롯한 여러 웨일스 학자들의 도움을 받았다."]], [["After the cessation of Jones’ exertions the old apathy returned, and continued till within a few years. Dr.", "존스의 노력이 멈춘 뒤로 옛날의 무관심이 되돌아왔고, 이는 몇 해 전까지 이어졌다."], ["Owen exerted himself to obtain support for the publication of the Mabinogeon or Prose Tales of the Welsh, but died without accomplishing his purpose, which has since been carried into execution by Lady Charlotte Guest.", "오언 박사는 웨일스의 산문 이야기 모음인 「마비노게온」의 출간을 위한 후원을 얻고자 애썼으나, 그 뜻을 이루지 못한 채 세상을 떠났다. 그 일은 그 후 샬럿 게스트 부인에 의해 실행에 옮겨졌다."], ["The legends which fill the remainder of this volume are taken from this work, of which we have already spoken more fully in the introductory chapter to the First Part.", "이 책의 나머지 부분을 채우는 전설들은 바로 이 저작에서 가져온 것이며, 이에 대해서는 이미 제1부의 서론에 해당하는 장에서 보다 상세히 이야기한 바 있다."]], [["# THE WELSH BARDS", "# THE WELSH BARDS"]], [["The authors to whom the oldest Welsh poems are attributed are Aneurin, who is supposed to have lived A.D. 500 to 550, and Taliesin, Llywarch Hen (Llywarch the Aged), and Myrddin or Merlin, who were a few years later.", "가장 오래된 웨일스 시가 돌려지는 작자들로는 기원후 500년에서 550년 사이에 살았던 것으로 추정되는 아네우린, 그리고 그보다 몇 해 뒤의 사람들인 탈리에신, 흘리와르크 헨[흘리와르크 노옹], 그리고 미르딘 또는 멀린이 있다."], ["The authenticity of the poems which bear their names has been assailed, and it is still an open question how many and which of them are authentic, though it is hardly to be doubted that some are so.", "이들의 이름이 붙은 시들의 진위는 공격을 받아 왔으며, 그 가운데 얼마나 많은 것이, 또 어느 것이 진정한 것인지는 여전히 미결의 문제다. 다만 그중 일부가 진정한 것임은 거의 의심할 여지가 없다."], ["The poem of Aneurin entitled the “Gododin,” bears very strong marks of authenticity.", "\"고도딘\"이라는 제목이 붙은 아네우린의 시는 진정성의 매우 강한 흔적을 지니고 있다."], ["Aneurin was one of the Northern Britons of Strath-Clyde, who have left to that part of the district they inhabited the name of Cumberland, or Land of the Cymri.", "아네우린은 스트래스클라이드의 북부 브리튼인 가운데 하나였는데, 이들은 자신들이 살던 지역의 한 부분에 컴벌랜드, 곧 킴리의 땅이라는 이름을 남겼다."], ["In this poem he laments the defeat of his countrymen by the Saxons at the battle of Cattraeth, in consequence of having partaken too freely of the mead before joining in combat.", "이 시에서 그는 자기 동포들이 전투에 임하기 앞서 벌꿀술을 지나치게 흥청망청 마신 탓에 카트라이스 전투에서 색슨족에게 패배한 것을 애도한다."], ["The bard himself and two of his fellow-warriors were all who escaped from the field.", "음유시인 자신과 그의 동료 전사 둘만이 그 들판에서 탈출하였다."], ["A portion of this poem has been translated by Gray, of which the following is an extract:", "이 시의 일부를 그레이가 번역하였으며, 다음은 그 발췌이다."]], [["“To Cattraeth’s vale, in glittering row, Twice two hundred warriors go; Every warrior’s manly neck Chains of regal honor deck, Wreathed in many a golden link; From the golden cup they drink Nectar that the bees produce, Or the grape’s exalted juice.", "\"카트라이스의 골짜기로, 번쩍이는 행렬을 이루어, 네 곱절 백의 전사들이 나아가노라. 전사들의 사내다운 목마다 제왕의 영예를 나타내는 사슬이 둘려 있고, 수많은 황금 고리로 엮여 있도다."], ["Flushed with mirth and hope they burn, But none to Cattraeth’s vale return, Save Aëron brave, and Conan strong, Bursting through the bloody throng, And I, the meanest of them all, That live to weep, and sing their fall.”", "황금 잔에서 그들은 마시노니, 꿀벌이 빚어낸 감로(甘露)요, 포도의 드높은 즙이로다. 환희와 희망에 달아올라 불타오르나, 카트라이스의 골짜기로 돌아온 자 아무도 없네, 용맹한 아에론과 강인한 코난, 피투성이 무리를 헤치고 나온 그들과, 그리고 그들 가운데 가장 보잘것없는 나, 살아남아 그들의 몰락을 슬퍼하며 노래하는 나뿐.\""]], [["The works of Taliesin are of much more questionable authenticity.", "탈리에신의 작품들은 진정성이 훨씬 더 의심스럽다."], ["There is a story of the adventures of Taliesin so strongly marked with mythical traits as to cast suspicion on the writings attributed to him.", "탈리에신의 모험에 관한 이야기 하나가 전하는데, 신화적 특징이 너무도 짙어 그에게 돌려진 글들에 의혹을 던진다."], ["This story will be found in the subsequent pages.", "이 이야기는 뒤따르는 쪽들에서 보게 될 것이다."]], [["# THE TRIADS", "# THE TRIADS"]], [["The Triads are a peculiar species of poetical composition, of which the Welsh bards have left numerous examples.", "세 짝 시[Triad]는 독특한 종류의 시 형식으로, 웨일스의 음유시인들이 그 무수한 본보기를 남겼다."], ["They are enumerations of a triad of persons, or events, or observations, strung together in one short sentence.", "이는 세 사람, 또는 세 사건, 또는 세 가지 견해를 짤막한 한 문장 안에 엮어 늘어놓은 것이다."], ["This form of composition, originally invented, in all likelihood, to assist the memory, has been raised by the Welsh to a degree of elegance of which it hardly at first sight appears susceptible.", "십중팔구 본디 기억을 돕기 위해 고안된 이 작법은, 처음 보아서는 그러한 가능성이 거의 엿보이지 않는 우아함의 경지로까지 웨일스인들에 의해 끌어올려졌다."], ["The Triads are of all ages, some of them probably as old as anything in the language.", "세 짝 시는 온갖 시대에 걸쳐 있으며, 그중 일부는 아마도 이 언어 안의 그 무엇 못지않게 오래되었을 것이다."], ["Short as they are individually, the collection in the Myvyrian Archæology occupies more than one hundred and seventy pages of double columns.", "개별로는 짧지만, 「미비리언 고고학」에 실린 모음집은 이단 편집으로 백칠십 쪽이 넘게 차지한다."], ["We will give some specimens, beginning with personal triads, and giving the first place to one of King Arthur’s own composition:", "우리는 인물을 다룬 세 짝 시부터 시작하여, 아서 왕 자신이 지은 것을 첫머리에 두고 몇 가지 본보기를 들어 보겠다."]], [["“I have three heroes in battle: Mael the tall, and Llyr, with his army, And Caradoc, the pillar of Wales.”", "\"전투에서 내게는 세 영웅이 있노라. 키다리 마엘과, 자기 군대를 거느린 흘리르, 그리고 웨일스의 기둥 카라도크가 그들이라.\""]], [["“The three principal bards of the island of Britain:— Merlin Ambrose Merlin the son of Morfyn, called also Merlin the Wild, And Taliesin, the chief of the bards.”", "\"브리튼 섬의 으뜸가는 세 음유시인은, 멀린 앰브로즈, 멀린 더 와일드라고도 불리는 모르핀의 아들 멀린, 그리고 음유시인들의 우두머리 탈리에신이라.\""]], [["“The three golden-tongued knights of the court of Arthur:— Gawain, son of Gwyar, Drydvas, son of Tryphin, And Eliwlod, son of Madag, ap Uther.”", "\"아서의 궁정에 있는 황금 혀를 지닌 세 기사는, 과이아르의 아들 가웨인, 트리핀의 아들 드리드바스, 그리고 마다그 압 우테르의 아들 엘리울로드라.\""]], [["“The three honorable feasts of the island of Britain:— The feast of Caswallaun, after repelling Julius Cæsar from this isle; The feast of Aurelius Ambrosius, after he had conquered the Saxons; And the feast of King Arthur, at Carleon upon Usk.”", "\"브리튼 섬의 명예로운 세 잔치는, 이 섬에서 율리우스 카이사르를 물리친 뒤 베푼 카스왈라운의 잔치, 색슨족을 정복한 뒤 베푼 아우렐리우스 암브로시우스의 잔치, 그리고 우스크 강가 칼리온에서 베푼 아서 왕의 잔치라.\""]], [["“Guenever, the daughter of Laodegan the giant, Bad when little, worse when great.”", "\"거인 라오데간의 딸 귀네비어는, 어릴 적엔 못됐고, 자라서는 더 못됐도다.\""]], [["Next follow some moral triads:", "다음으로는 교훈을 담은 세 짝 시 몇 가지가 이어진다."]], [["“Hast thou heard what Dremhidydd sung, An ancient watchman on the castle walls?", "\"그대는 드렘히디드가 노래한 바를 들었는가, 성벽 위의 옛 파수꾼이 부른 노래를?"], ["A refusal is better than a promise unperformed.”", "거절이 지키지 못할 약속보다 나으니라.\""]], [["“Hast thou heard what Llenleawg sung, The noble chief wearing the golden torques?", "\"그대는 흘렌레아우그가 노래한 바를 들었는가, 황금 목걸이를 두른 고귀한 우두머리가 부른 노래를?"], ["The grave is better than a life of want.”", "무덤이 궁핍한 삶보다 나으니라.\""]], [["“Hast thou heard what Garselit sung, The Irishman whom it is safe to follow?", "\"그대는 가르셀리트가 노래한 바를 들었는가, 따르기에 안전한 아일랜드 사람이 부른 노래를?"], ["Sin is bad, if long pursued.”", "죄는 오래 좇으면 나쁜 것이니라.\""]], [["“Hast thou heard what Avaon sung, The son of Taliesin, of the recording verse?", "\"그대는 아바온이 노래한 바를 들었는가, 기록하는 시구를 짓는 탈리에신의 아들이 부른 노래를?"], ["The cheek will not conceal the anguish of the heart.”", "뺨은 마음의 고뇌를 감추지 못하느니라.\""]], [["“Didst thou hear what Llywarch sung, The intrepid and brave old man?", "\"그대는 흘리와르크가 노래한 바를 들었는가, 담대하고 용맹한 그 노인이 부른 노래를?"], ["Greet kindly, though there be no acquaintance.”", "알지 못하는 사이라 할지라도 다정히 맞이하라.\""]], [["————", "————"]], [["# KYNON’S ADVENTURE", "# KYNON’S ADVENTURE"]], [["KING ARTHUR was at Caerleon upon Usk; and one day he sat in his chamber, and with him were Owain, the son of Urien, and Kynon, the son of Clydno, and Kay, the son of Kyner, and Guenever and her handmaidens at needlework by the window.", "아서 왕은 우스크 강가의 카얼리언에 머물고 있었다. 어느 날 그는 자기 방에 앉아 있었는데, 그 곁에는 우리엔의 아들 오와인과 클리드노의 아들 키논, 그리고 키너의 아들 케이가 함께 있었고, 귀네비어와 그녀의 시녀들은 창가에서 바느질을 하고 있었다."], ["In the centre of the chamber King Arthur sat, upon a seat of green rushes,[56] over which was spread a covering of flame-covered satin, and a cushion of red satin was under his elbow.", "방 한가운데에는 아서 왕이 푸른 골풀로 만든 자리에 앉아 있었는데, 그 위에는 불꽃무늬가 새겨진 공단 덮개가 펼쳐져 있었고, 그의 팔꿈치 아래에는 붉은 공단으로 만든 방석이 놓여 있었다."]], [["Then Arthur spoke.", "그때 아서가 입을 열었다."], ["“If I thought you would not disparage me,”", "\"그대들이 나를 탓하지 않으리라 여겨진다면,\""], ["said he, “I would sleep while I wait for my repast; and you can entertain one another with relating tales, and can obtain a flagon of mead and some meat from Kay.”", "그가 말했다, \"나는 식사를 기다리는 동안 잠을 좀 자야겠소. 그동안 그대들은 서로 이야기를 들려주며 즐겨도 좋고, 케이에게서 벌꿀술 한 병과 고기 약간을 얻어도 좋소.\""], ["And the king went to sleep.", "그러고는 왕이 잠이 들었다."], ["And Kynon the son of Clydno asked Kay for that which Arthur had promised them.", "클리드노의 아들 키논이 케이에게 아서가 약속한 것을 달라고 청했다."], ["“I too will have the good tale which he promised me,”", "\"나도 그가 내게 약속한 좋은 이야기를 들어야겠소.\" 케이가 말했다."], ["said Kay. “Nay,”", "\"아니지,\""], ["answered Kynon; “fairer will it be for thee to fulfil Arthur’s behest in the first place, and then we will tell thee the best tale that we know.”", "키논이 대답했다. \"먼저 아서의 명을 그대가 이행하는 것이 더 옳을 것이오."], ["So Kay went to the kitchen and to the mead-cellar, and returned, bearing a flagon of mead, and a golden goblet, and a handful of skewers, upon which were broiled collops of meat.", "그러고 나면 우리가 아는 가장 좋은 이야기를 그대에게 들려주겠소.\" 그리하여 케이는 주방과 벌꿀술 저장고로 가서, 벌꿀술 한 병과 황금 술잔, 그리고 구운 고기 조각을 꿴 꼬챙이 한 줌을 들고 돌아왔다."], ["Then they ate the collops, and began to drink the mead.", "그들은 고기 조각을 먹으며 벌꿀술을 마시기 시작했다. \"자, 이제,\""], ["“Now,” said Kay, “it is time for you to give me my story.”", "케이가 말했다, \"그대들이 내게 이야기를 들려줄 차례요.\""], ["“Kynon,”", "\"키논,\""], ["said Owain, “do thou pay to Kay the tale that is his due.”", "오와인이 말했다, \"케이에게 마땅히 줄 이야기를 들려주게나.\""], ["“I will do so,”", "\"그렇게 하겠소.\""], ["answered Kynon.", "키논이 대답했다."]], [["“I was the only son of my mother and father, and I was exceedingly aspiring, and my daring was very great.", "\"나는 아버지와 어머니의 외아들이었고, 야심이 대단했으며, 담대함 또한 매우 컸소."], ["I thought there was no enterprise in the world too mighty for me: and after I had achieved all the adventures that were in my own country, I equipped myself, and set forth to journey through deserts and distant regions.", "나는 이 세상에 내가 감당하지 못할 만큼 거대한 모험은 없다고 여겼소. 그래서 내 고장에 있는 온갖 모험을 모두 이루고 나자, 나는 채비를 갖추고 사막과 머나먼 고장들을 두루 여행하러 길을 떠났소."], ["And at length it chanced that I came to the fairest valley in the world, wherein were trees all of equal growth; and a river ran through the valley, and a path was by the side of the river.", "그리고 마침내 나는 세상에서 가장 아름다운 골짜기에 다다랐는데, 그곳에는 한결같이 고르게 자란 나무들이 있었고, 강 하나가 골짜기를 가로질러 흘렀으며, 강가에는 오솔길이 나 있었소."], ["And I followed the path until midday, and continued my journey along the remainder of the valley until the evening; and at the extremity of the plain I came to a large and lustrous castle, at the foot of which was a torrent.", "나는 정오까지 그 길을 따라갔고, 저녁이 될 때까지 골짜기의 남은 부분을 따라 여행을 이어 갔소. 그러다 들판 끝에 이르러, 나는 크고 빛나는 성에 다다랐는데, 그 발치에는 급류가 흐르고 있었소."], ["And I approached the castle, and there I beheld two youths with yellow curling hair, each with a frontlet of gold upon his head, and clad in a garment of yellow satin; and they had gold clasps upon their insteps.", "내가 그 성으로 다가가 보니, 곱슬곱슬한 노란 머리칼을 한 두 젊은이가 보였는데, 저마다 머리에는 금테를 둘렀고, 노란 공단으로 지은 옷을 입고 있었소. 그들의 발등에는 금 죔쇠가 채워져 있었소."], ["In the hand of each of them was an ivory bow, strung with the sinews of the stag, and their arrows and their shafts were of the bone of the whale, and were winged with peacock’s feathers.", "그 두 사람의 손에는 각각 상아로 만든 활이 들려 있었는데, 수사슴의 힘줄로 시위를 매었고, 그들의 화살과 화살대는 고래 뼈로 만들어 공작 깃털을 단 것이었소."], ["The shafts also had golden heads.", "화살대에는 또한 황금 화살촉이 달려 있었소."], ["And they had daggers with blades of gold, and with hilts of the bone of the whale.", "그리고 그들에게는 칼날이 금으로 되고 자루가 고래 뼈로 된 단검이 있었소."], ["And they were shooting at a mark.", "그들은 과녁을 향해 활을 쏘고 있었소."]], [["“And a little away from them I saw a man in the prime of life, with his beard newly shorn, clad in a robe and mantle of yellow satin, and round the top of his mantle was a band of gold lace.", "\"그들에게서 조금 떨어진 곳에 나는 한창때의 사내를 보았는데, 갓 깎은 수염을 하고, 노란 공단으로 지은 겉옷과 망토를 걸쳤으며, 망토 윗자락 둘레에는 금실 레이스 띠가 둘려 있었소."], ["On his feet were shoes of variegated leather,[57] fastened by two bosses of gold.", "그의 발에는 알록달록한 가죽으로 만든 신발이 신겨져 있었는데, 두 개의 금장식으로 여며져 있었소."], ["When I saw him I went towards him and saluted him; and such was his courtesy, that he no sooner received my greeting than he returned it.", "내가 그를 보고 그에게로 다가가 인사를 건네자, 그의 정중함이 어찌나 대단한지 그는 내 인사를 받자마자 답례를 했소."], ["And he went with me towards the castle.", "그리고 그는 나와 함께 성을 향해 걸어갔소."], ["Now there were no dwellers in the castle, except those who were in one hall.", "그런데 그 성에는 한 홀 안에 있는 자들 말고는 거처하는 이가 아무도 없었소."], ["And there I saw four and twenty damsels, embroidering satin at a window.", "그곳에서 나는 창가에서 공단에 수를 놓고 있는 스물네 명의 처녀를 보았소."], ["And this I tell thee, Kay, that the least fair of them was fairer than the fairest maid thou didst ever behold in the island of Britain; and the least lovely of them was more lovely than Guenever, the wife of Arthur, when she appeared loveliest, at the feast of Easter.", "그리고 그대에게 이르거니와, 케이, 그들 가운데 가장 덜 아름다운 이조차 그대가 브리튼 섬에서 일찍이 본 그 어떤 가장 아름다운 처녀보다 더 아름다웠고, 그들 가운데 가장 덜 사랑스러운 이조차 부활절 잔치 때 가장 사랑스러운 모습을 한 아서의 아내 귀네비어보다 더 사랑스러웠소."], ["They rose up at my coming, and six of them took my horse, and divested me of my armor, and six others took my arms and washed them in a vessel till they were perfectly bright.", "내가 들어서자 그들은 일어섰고, 그중 여섯은 내 말을 받아 갔으며, 내 갑옷을 벗겨 주었고, 또 다른 여섯은 내 무기를 받아 그릇에 담아 완전히 반짝일 때까지 씻었소."], ["And the third six spread cloths upon the tables and prepared meat.", "그리고 세 번째 여섯은 식탁에 천을 펼치고 고기를 마련했소."], ["And the fourth six took off my soiled garments and placed others upon me, namely, an under vest and a doublet of fine linen, and a robe and a surcoat, and a mantle of yellow satin, with a broad gold band upon the mantle.", "네 번째 여섯은 내 더러워진 옷을 벗기고 다른 옷을 입혀 주었는데, 곧 고운 아마포로 지은 속옷과 더블릿, 그리고 겉옷과 겉옷 위에 걸치는 옷, 그리고 노란 공단 망토로, 그 망토에는 넓은 금띠가 둘려 있었소."], ["And they placed cushions both beneath and around me, with coverings of red linen, and I sat down.", "그들은 내 아래와 둘레에 붉은 아마포 덮개를 씌운 방석들을 놓아 주었고, 나는 자리에 앉았소."], ["Now the six maidens who had taken my horse unharnessed him as well as if they had been the best squires in the island of Britain.", "한편 내 말을 받아 갔던 여섯 처녀는, 브리튼 섬에서 가장 뛰어난 종자들이 했더라도 그만 못할 만큼 솜씨 좋게 말의 마구를 풀어 주었소."]], [["“Then behold they brought bowls of silver, wherein was water to wash and towels of linen, some green and some white; and I washed.", "\"그러더니 보시오, 그들이 은 대접을 가져왔는데, 그 안에는 씻을 물이 담겨 있었고, 아마포 수건도 있어 더러는 푸르고 더러는 흰 것이었소."], ["And in a little while the man sat down at the table.", "그래서 나는 손을 씻었소. 잠시 후 사내가 식탁에 앉았소."], ["And I sat next to him, and below me sat all the maidens, except those who waited on us.", "나는 그의 곁에 앉았고, 내 아래로는 우리 시중을 드는 이들을 제외한 모든 처녀들이 앉았소."], ["And the table was of silver, and the cloths upon the table were of linen.", "식탁은 은으로 되어 있었고, 식탁 위의 천은 아마포였소."], ["And no vessel was served upon the table that was not either of gold or of silver or of buffalo horn.", "그리고 식탁에 올라온 그릇 가운데 금이나 은, 혹은 들소 뿔로 만들지 않은 것은 하나도 없었소."], ["And our meat was brought to us.", "그리고 우리에게 고기가 차려졌소."], ["And verily, Kay, I saw there every sort of meat, and every sort of liquor that I ever saw elsewhere; but the meat and the liquor were better served there than I ever saw them in any other place.", "참으로, 케이, 나는 그곳에서 내가 다른 어디에서 본 적이 있는 온갖 종류의 고기와 온갖 종류의 술을 보았소. 다만 그 고기와 술은 내가 다른 어떤 곳에서 본 것보다 훨씬 더 잘 차려져 있었소."]], [["“Until the repast was half over, neither the man nor any one of the damsels spoke a single word to me; but when the man perceived that it would be more agreeable for me to converse than to eat any more, he began to inquire of me who I was.", "\"식사가 절반쯤 지날 때까지 그 사내도, 처녀들 가운데 누구도 내게 한 마디 말도 건네지 않았소. 그러다 그 사내가 내게 더 먹기보다는 이야기 나누는 편이 더 즐거우리라는 것을 알아채자, 그는 내가 누구인지 묻기 시작했소."], ["Then I told the man who I was and what was the cause of my journey, and said that I was seeking whether any one was superior to me, or whether I could gain mastery over all.", "그리하여 나는 그 사내에게 내가 누구인지, 그리고 내 여행의 까닭이 무엇인지를 말하고, 나보다 더 뛰어난 자가 있는지, 아니면 내가 모두를 제압할 수 있는지를 찾고 있다고 말했소. 그 사내는 나를 바라보더니, 미소를 지으며 말했소."], ["The man looked upon me, and he smiled and said, ‘If I did not fear to do thee a mischief, I would show thee that which thou seekest.’ Then I desired him to speak freely.", "'내가 그대에게 해를 끼칠까 두렵지만 않다면, 그대가 찾는 것을 보여 주겠소.' 그러자 나는 그에게 거리낌 없이 말해 달라고 청했소."], ["And he said: ‘Sleep here to-night, and in the morning arise early, and take the road upwards through the valley, until thou reachest the wood.", "그러자 그가 말했소. '오늘 밤은 여기서 자고, 아침에 일찍 일어나, 골짜기를 따라 위로 올라가는 길을 잡아 숲에 이를 때까지 가시오."], ["A little way within the wood thou wilt come to a large sheltered glade, with a mound in the centre.", "숲에 조금 들어가면 그대는 한가운데에 둔덕이 있는, 크고 한적한 빈터에 다다를 것이오."], ["And thou wilt see a black man of great stature on the top of the mound.", "그러면 그대는 그 둔덕 꼭대기에 거구의 검은 사내가 있는 것을 보게 될 것이오."], ["He has but one foot, and one eye in the middle of his forehead.", "그는 발이 하나뿐이고, 이마 한가운데에 눈이 하나 있소."], ["He is the wood-ward of that wood.", "그는 그 숲의 숲지기요."], ["And thou wilt see a thousand wild animals grazing around him.", "그대는 그를 둘러싸고 풀을 뜯는 천 마리의 들짐승을 보게 될 것이오."], ["Inquire of him the way out of the glade, and he will reply to thee briefly, and will point out the road by which thou shalt find that which thou art in quest of.’", "그에게 그 빈터에서 나가는 길을 물으면, 그가 그대에게 짧게 대답하며, 그대가 찾고 있는 것을 발견할 길을 일러 줄 것이오.'"]], [["“And long seemed that night to me.", "\"그리고 그 밤이 내게는 길게만 느껴졌소."], ["And the next morning I arose and equipped myself, and mounted my horse, and proceeded straight through the valley to the wood, and at length I arrived at the glade.", "이튿날 아침 나는 일어나 채비를 갖추고, 말에 올라, 골짜기를 곧장 가로질러 숲으로 나아갔으며, 마침내 그 빈터에 다다랐소. 그러자 검은 사내가 거기 둔덕 꼭대기에 앉아 있었소."], ["And the black man was there, sitting upon the top of the mound; and I was three times more astonished at the number of wild animals that I beheld than the man had said I should be.", "그리고 나는 내가 본 들짐승의 수에 그 사내가 말한 것보다 세 배는 더 놀랐소. 그래서 나는 그에게 길을 물었고, 그는 내게 어디로 가려 하느냐고 무뚝뚝하게 물었소."], ["Then I inquired of him the way and he asked me roughly whither I would go.", "내가 누구인지, 무엇을 찾는지 말하자, 그가 말했소."], ["And when I had told him who I was and what I sought, ‘Take,’ said he, ‘that path that leads toward the head of the glade, and there thou wilt find an open space like to a large valley, and in the midst of it a tall tree.", "'빈터의 머리 쪽으로 이어지는 저 길을 잡으시오. 그러면 그대는 커다란 골짜기 같은 트인 공간을 발견할 것이고, 그 한가운데에 키 큰 나무 한 그루가 있을 것이오."], ["Under this tree is a fountain, and by the side of the fountain a marble slab, and on the marble slab a silver bowl, attached by a chain of silver, that it may not be carried away.", "이 나무 아래에는 샘이 있고, 샘 곁에는 대리석 판이 있으며, 대리석 판 위에는 은 대접이 하나 있는데, 가져가지 못하도록 은 사슬에 매여 있소."], ["Take the bowl and throw a bowlful of water on the slab.", "그 대접을 들어 판 위에 대접 가득 물을 부으시오."], ["And if thou dost not find trouble in that adventure, thou needest not seek it during the rest of thy life.’", "그리고 그대가 그 모험에서 곤경을 겪지 않는다면, 남은 평생 동안 곤경을 찾아다닐 필요가 없을 것이오.'"]], [["“So I journeyed on until I reached the summit of the steep.", "\"그리하여 나는 가파른 비탈 꼭대기에 이를 때까지 길을 갔소."], ["And there I found everything as the black man had described it to me.", "거기서 나는 모든 것이 검은 사내가 내게 일러 준 그대로임을 발견했소."], ["And I went up to the tree, and beneath it I saw the fountain, and by its side the marble slab, and the silver bowl fastened by the chain.", "나는 그 나무로 다가가, 그 아래에서 샘을 보았고, 그 곁에서 대리석 판과 사슬에 매인 은 대접을 보았소."], ["Then I took the bowl, and cast a bowlful of water upon the slab, and immediately I heard a mighty peal of thunder, so that heaven and earth seemed to tremble with its fury.", "그래서 나는 그 대접을 들어 판 위에 대접 가득 물을 부었더니, 곧바로 엄청난 천둥소리가 들려와, 하늘과 땅이 그 노여움에 떠는 듯했소. 천둥이 지난 뒤에는 소나기가 쏟아졌소."], ["And after the thunder came a shower; and of a truth I tell thee, Kay, that it was such a shower as neither man nor beast could endure and live.", "그리고 참으로, 케이, 그대에게 이르거니와, 그것은 사람도 짐승도 견디고 살아남을 수 없을 만큼 대단한 소나기였소."], ["I turned my horse’s flank toward the shower, and placed the beak of my shield over his head and neck, while I held the upper part of it over my own neck.", "나는 말의 옆구리를 소나기 쪽으로 돌리고, 방패의 뾰족한 끝을 말의 머리와 목 위에 씌웠으며, 그 윗부분은 내 목 위에 가져다 댔소."], ["And thus I withstood the shower.", "그렇게 나는 소나기를 견뎌 냈소."], ["And presently the sky became clear, and with that, behold, the birds lighted upon the tree, and sang.", "이윽고 하늘이 맑아지자, 보시오, 새들이 나무에 내려앉아 노래했소."], ["And truly, Kay, I never heard any melody equal to that, either before or since.", "그리고 참으로, 케이, 나는 그 전에도 그 후에도 그에 견줄 만한 가락을 결코 들어 본 적이 없소."], ["And when I was most charmed with listening to the birds, lo!", "그리고 내가 새들의 노래에 가장 매혹되어 있을 때, 보시오!"], ["a chiding voice was heard of one approaching me and saying: ‘O knight, what has brought thee hither?", "누군가가 내게 다가오며 나무라는 목소리로 이렇게 말하는 것이 들렸소. '오 기사여, 무엇이 그대를 이곳으로 데려왔는가?"], ["What evil have I done to thee that thou shouldst act towards me and my possessions as thou hast this day?", "내가 그대에게 무슨 해악을 끼쳤기에, 그대가 오늘 나와 내 소유물에게 이런 짓을 한단 말인가?"], ["Dost thou not know that the shower to-day has left in my dominions neither man nor beast alive that was exposed to it?’ And thereupon, behold, a knight on a black horse appeared, clothed in jet-black velvet, and with a tabard of black linen about him.", "오늘의 소나기가 내 영지에서, 그것에 노출된 사람도 짐승도 단 하나 살려 두지 않은 것을 그대는 모르는가?' 그러자 보시오, 칠흑같이 검은 벨벳을 걸치고, 검은 아마포 겉옷을 두른 기사가 검은 말을 타고 나타났소."], ["And we charged each other, and, as the onset was furious, it was not long before I was overthrown.", "우리는 서로를 향해 돌진했고, 그 공격이 어찌나 사나운지, 오래지 않아 나는 나가떨어지고 말았소."], ["Then the knight passed the shaft of his lance through the bridle-rein of my horse, and rode off with the two horses, leaving me where I was.", "그러자 그 기사는 자기 창의 자루를 내 말의 고삐 사이로 꿰어, 두 마리 말을 끌고 가 버렸고, 나는 그 자리에 남겨졌소."], ["And he did not even bestow so much notice upon me as to imprison me, nor did he despoil me of my arms.", "그는 나를 가두려고조차 하지 않았고, 내 무기를 빼앗지도 않았소."], ["So I returned along the road by which I had come.", "그래서 나는 내가 왔던 길을 따라 되돌아갔소."], ["And when I reached the glade where the black man was, I confess to thee, Kay, it is a marvel that I did not melt down into a liquid pool, through the shame that I felt at the black man’s derision.", "그리고 검은 사내가 있던 빈터에 이르렀을 때, 케이, 그대에게 고백하건대, 내가 그 검은 사내의 비웃음에 느낀 수치심으로 인해 액체 웅덩이가 되어 녹아내리지 않은 것이 기적이오."], ["And that night I came to the same castle where I had spent the night preceding.", "그날 밤 나는 전날 밤을 보냈던 바로 그 성에 다다랐소."], ["And I was more agreeably entertained that night than I had been the night before.", "그리고 그날 밤 나는 전날 밤보다 더 흡족한 대접을 받았소."], ["And I conversed freely with the inmates of the castle; and none of them alluded to my expedition to the fountain, neither did I mention it to any.", "나는 성 안 사람들과 거리낌 없이 이야기를 나누었소. 그리고 그들 가운데 누구도 내가 샘으로 떠났던 일을 언급하지 않았고, 나 또한 누구에게도 그것을 말하지 않았소."], ["And I remained there that night.", "나는 그날 밤 그곳에 머물렀소."], ["When I arose on the morrow I found ready saddled a dark bay palfrey, with nostrils as red as scarlet.", "이튿날 일어나 보니, 콧구멍이 진홍빛처럼 붉은 짙은 밤색 승용마 한 필이 안장을 얹은 채 마련되어 있었소."], ["And after putting on my armor, and leaving there my blessing, I returned to my own court.", "그리고 갑옷을 입고, 그곳에 내 축복을 남긴 뒤, 나는 내 궁정으로 돌아왔소."], ["And that horse I still possess, and he is in the stable yonder.", "그리고 그 말은 지금도 내가 가지고 있으며, 저쪽 마구간에 있소."], ["And I declare that I would not part with him for the best palfrey in the island of Britain.", "나는 단언하건대, 브리튼 섬에서 가장 뛰어난 승용마와도 그 말을 바꾸지 않을 것이오."]], [["“Now, of a truth, Kay, no man ever before confessed to an adventure so much to his own discredit; and verily it seems strange to me that neither before nor since have I heard of any person who knew of this adventure, and that the subject of it should exist within King Arthur’s dominions without any other person lighting upon it.”", "\"자, 참으로, 케이, 일찍이 그 누구도 이토록 제 명예에 누가 되는 모험을 고백한 적이 없을 것이오. 그리고 참으로 내게 이상하게 여겨지는 것은, 그 전에도 그 후에도 이 모험을 알고 있는 사람에 대해 들어 본 적이 없다는 것, 그리고 그 모험의 무대가 아서 왕의 영지 안에 있는데도 다른 누구 하나 그것을 우연히 발견하지 못했다는 것이오.\""]], [["————", "————"]], [["“NOW,”", "\"이제,\""], ["quoth Owain, “would it not be well to go and endeavor to discover that place?”", "오와인이 말했다, \"가서 그곳을 찾아보는 것이 좋지 않겠습니까?\""]], [["“By the hand of my friend,”", "\"내 벗의 손을 두고 말하건대,\""], ["said Kay, “often dost thou utter that with thy tongue which thou wouldest not make good with thy deeds.”", "케이가 말했다, \"그대는 행동으로 옮기지도 못할 말을 혀로만 자주 내뱉는구려.\""]], [["“In very truth,”", "\"진실로,\""], ["said Guenever, “it were better thou wert hanged, Kay, than to use such uncourteous speech towards a man like Owain.”", "구네버가 말했다, \"케이여, 그대가 오와인 같은 사람에게 그토록 무례한 말을 쓰느니 차라리 목매달리는 편이 나을 것이오.\""]], [["“By the hand of my friend, good lady,”", "\"내 벗의 손을 두고 말하건대, 고귀한 부인이시여,\""], ["said Kay, “thy praise of Owain is not greater than mine.”", "케이가 말했다, \"오와인에 대한 그대의 칭송이 저의 칭송보다 크지는 않습니다.\""]], [["With that Arthur awoke, and asked if he had not been sleeping a little.", "그때 아서가 잠에서 깨어나, 자기가 잠시 잠들었던 것이 아니냐고 물었다."]], [["“Yes, lord,”", "\"그렇습니다, 폐하,\""], ["answered Owain, “thou hast slept awhile.”", "오와인이 대답했다, \"잠시 주무셨습니다.\""]], [["“Is it time for us to go to meat?”", "\"식사를 하러 갈 시간이 되었느냐?\""]], [["“It is, lord,”", "\"그렇습니다, 폐하,\""], ["said Owain.", "오와인이 말했다."]], [["Then the horn for washing was sounded, and the king and all his household sat down to eat.", "그러자 손을 씻으라는 뿔나팔이 울렸고, 왕과 그의 모든 가솔이 자리에 앉아 식사를 했다."], ["And when the meal was ended Owain withdrew to his lodging, and made ready his horse and his arms.", "그리고 식사가 끝나자 오와인은 자신의 거처로 물러나, 말과 무구를 준비했다."]], [["On the morrow with the dawn of day he put on his armor, and mounted his charger, and travelled through distant lands, and over desert mountains.", "이튿날 동이 틀 무렵 그는 갑옷을 입고 군마에 올라, 머나먼 땅과 황량한 산들을 가로질러 여행했다."], ["And at length he arrived at the valley which Kynon had described to him, and he was certain that it was the same that he sought.", "그리하여 마침내 키논이 그에게 묘사해 주었던 골짜기에 이르렀고, 그곳이 자기가 찾던 바로 그 골짜기임을 확신했다."], ["And journeying along the valley, by the side of the river, he followed its course till he came to the plain, and within sight of the castle.", "그리고 강가를 따라 골짜기를 지나며, 그 물줄기를 좇아 마침내 평원에 다다라, 성을 시야에 두게 되었다."], ["When he approached the castle he saw the youths shooting with their bows, in the place where Kynon had seen them, and the yellow man, to whom the castle belonged, standing hard by.", "성에 가까이 다가가니, 키논이 그들을 보았던 바로 그 자리에서 젊은이들이 활을 쏘고 있었고, 성의 주인인 황색 옷을 입은 사내가 그 곁에 서 있었다."], ["And no sooner had Owain saluted the yellow man, than he was saluted by him in return.", "오와인이 그 황색 옷의 사내에게 인사를 건네자마자, 그 사내도 답례로 인사를 했다."]], [["And he went forward towards the castle, and there he saw the chamber; and when he had entered the chamber, he beheld the maidens working at satin embroidery, in chains of gold.", "그리고 그는 성을 향해 나아갔고, 거기서 그 방을 보았다. 방으로 들어서자, 처녀들이 금실로 새틴 자수를 놓고 있는 것이 보였다."], ["And their beauty and their comeliness seemed to Owain far greater than Kynon had represented to him.", "그들의 아름다움과 고움은 키논이 그에게 일러 준 것보다 훨씬 더 큰 것으로 보였다."], ["And they arose to wait upon Owain, as they had done to Kynon.", "그리고 그들은 키논에게 그랬듯이 일어나 오와인의 시중을 들었다."], ["And the meal which they set before him gave even more satisfaction to Owain than it had done to Kynon.", "그리고 그들이 그 앞에 차려 놓은 식사는 키논에게 주었던 것보다 오와인에게 더욱 큰 만족을 주었다."]], [["About the middle of the repast the yellow man asked Owain the object of his journey.", "식사가 절반쯤 진행되었을 때 황색 옷의 사내가 오와인에게 그 여행의 목적을 물었다."], ["And Owain made it known to him, and said, “I am in quest of the knight who guards the fountain.”", "오와인이 그것을 그에게 밝히며 말했다, \"나는 샘을 지키는 기사를 찾고 있소.\""], ["Upon this the yellow man smiled, and said that he was as loth to point out that adventure to him as he had been to Kynon.", "이 말에 황색 옷의 사내가 미소 지으며, 키논에게 그랬던 것처럼 그 모험을 일러 주기가 꺼려진다고 말했다."], ["However, he described the whole to Owain, and they retired to rest.", "그러나 그는 모든 것을 오와인에게 묘사해 주었고, 그들은 쉬러 물러갔다."]], [["The next morning Owain found his horse made ready for him by the damsels, and he set forward and came to the glade where the black man was.", "이튿날 아침 오와인은 처녀들이 자기를 위해 말을 준비해 놓은 것을 발견하고는, 길을 나서 그 검은 사내가 있는 숲속 빈터에 다다랐다."], ["And the stature of the black man seemed more wonderful to Owain than it had done to Kynon; and Owain asked of him his road, and he showed it to him.", "그 검은 사내의 체구는 키논에게보다 오와인에게 더욱 경이로워 보였다. 오와인이 그에게 길을 물으니, 그가 그것을 가르쳐 주었다."], ["And Owain followed the road till he came to the green tree; and he beheld the fountain, and the slab beside the fountain, with the bowl upon it.", "오와인은 그 길을 따라 마침내 푸른 나무에 이르렀다. 그리고 그 샘과, 샘 곁의 석판과, 그 위에 놓인 대접을 보았다."], ["And Owain took the bowl and threw a bowlful of water upon the slab. And, lo!", "오와인은 그 대접을 집어 들어 한 대접 가득 물을 떠서 석판 위에 부었다."], ["the thunder was heard, and after the thunder came the shower, more violent than Kynon had described, and after the shower the sky became bright.", "그러자, 보라! 천둥소리가 들렸고, 천둥 뒤에는 키논이 묘사했던 것보다 더 격렬한 소나기가 쏟아졌으며, 소나기가 그치자 하늘이 맑아졌다."], ["And immediately the birds came and settled upon the tree and sang.", "그리고 곧바로 새들이 날아와 나무에 내려앉아 노래했다."], ["And when their song was most pleasing to Owain he beheld a knight coming towards him through the valley; and he prepared to receive him, and encountered him violently.", "그들의 노래가 오와인에게 가장 듣기 좋게 들릴 무렵, 그는 한 기사가 골짜기를 가로질러 자기에게 다가오는 것을 보았다. 그는 그를 맞을 채비를 하고는 맹렬히 부딪쳤다."], ["Having broken both their lances, they drew their swords and fought blade to blade.", "두 사람의 창이 다 부러지자, 그들은 검을 뽑아 칼날을 맞대고 싸웠다."], ["Then Owain struck the knight a blow through his helmet, head-piece, and visor, and through the skin, and the flesh, and the bone, until it wounded the very brain.", "그러다 오와인이 그 기사의 투구와 머리덮개와 면갑을 꿰뚫고, 살갗과 살과 뼈를 지나, 마침내 골수에까지 이르는 일격을 가했다."], ["Then the black knight felt that he had received a mortal wound, upon which he turned his horse’s head and fled.", "그러자 검은 기사는 자기가 치명상을 입었음을 느끼고는, 말 머리를 돌려 달아났다."], ["And Owain pursued him and followed close upon him, although he was not near enough to strike him with his sword.", "오와인은 그를 바짝 뒤쫓았으나, 검으로 내리칠 만큼 가까이 다가서지는 못했다."], ["Then Owain descried a vast and resplendent castle; and they came to the castle gate.", "그때 오와인은 거대하고 휘황찬란한 성을 발견했고, 그들은 성문에 이르렀다."], ["And the black knight was allowed to enter, and the portcullis was let fall upon Owain; and it struck his horse behind the saddle, and cut him in two, and carried away the rowels of the spurs that were upon Owain’s heels.", "검은 기사는 안으로 들어가도록 허락되었으나, 내리닫이 쇠창살문이 오와인 위로 떨어졌다. 그것은 안장 뒤쪽에서 그의 말을 내리쳐 두 동강 내고, 오와인의 발뒤꿈치에 달려 있던 박차의 톱니바퀴를 잘라 갔다."], ["And the portcullis descended to the floor.", "그리고 그 쇠창살문이 바닥까지 내려왔다."], ["And the rowels of the spurs and part of the horse were without, and Owain with the other part of the horse remained between the two gates, and the inner gate was closed, so that Owain could not go thence; and Owain was in a perplexing situation.", "박차의 톱니바퀴와 말의 일부는 문 밖에 남았고, 오와인은 말의 나머지 부분과 함께 두 문 사이에 갇혔으며, 안쪽 문이 닫혀 있어 오와인은 거기서 빠져나갈 수가 없었다. 오와인은 당혹스러운 처지에 놓였다."], ["And while he was in this state, he could see through an aperture in the gate a street facing him, with a row of houses on each side.", "그런 상태에 있는 동안, 그는 문에 난 틈으로 자기 앞에 놓인 거리와, 양옆으로 늘어선 집들을 볼 수 있었다."], ["And he beheld a maiden, with yellow, curling hair, and a frontlet of gold upon her head; and she was clad in a dress of yellow satin, and on her feet were shoes of variegated leather.", "그리고 그는 누런빛으로 곱슬거리는 머리에 황금 머리띠를 두른 한 처녀를 보았다. 그녀는 황색 새틴 옷을 입고 있었고, 발에는 여러 빛깔의 가죽으로 만든 신을 신고 있었다."], ["And she approached the gate, and desired that it should be opened.", "그녀가 문으로 다가와, 문을 열어 달라고 청했다."], ["“Heaven knows, lady,”", "\"하늘이 아시오, 아가씨,\""], ["said Owain, “it is no more possible for me to open to thee from hence, than it is for thee to set me free.”", "오와인이 말했다, \"그대가 나를 풀어 줄 수 없는 것과 마찬가지로, 나로서도 여기서 그대에게 문을 열어 줄 도리가 없소.\""], ["And he told her his name, and who he was.", "그러고는 그녀에게 자기 이름과, 자기가 누구인지를 밝혔다."], ["“Truly,”", "\"참으로,\""], ["said the damsel, “it is very sad that thou canst not be released; and every woman ought to succor thee, for I know there is no one more faithful in the service of ladies than thou.", "처녀가 말했다, \"그대가 풀려날 수 없다니 매우 슬픈 일이옵니다. 마땅히 모든 여인이 그대를 도와야 할 것이온대, 부인들을 섬기는 데 있어 그대보다 더 충실한 사람이 없음을 제가 알기 때문이옵니다."], ["Therefore,”", "그러하오니,\""], ["quoth she, “whatever is in my power to do for thy release, I will do it.", "그녀가 말했다, \"그대를 풀어 드리고자 제 힘으로 할 수 있는 일이라면 무엇이든 하겠나이다."], ["Take this ring and put it on thy finger, with the stone inside thy hand, and close thy hand upon the stone.", "이 반지를 받아 손가락에 끼시되, 보석이 손바닥 안쪽으로 가게 하시고, 그 보석을 손으로 감싸 쥐소서."], ["And as long as thou concealest it, it will conceal thee.", "그리고 그것을 감추고 계시는 한, 그것이 그대를 감추어 줄 것이옵니다."], ["When they come forth to fetch thee, they will be much grieved that they cannot find thee.", "그들이 그대를 데리러 나오면, 그대를 찾지 못하여 몹시 애통해할 것이옵니다."], ["And I will await thee on the horseblock yonder, and thou wilt be able to see me, though I cannot see thee.", "저는 저기 저 디딤대 위에서 그대를 기다리겠나이다. 저는 그대를 볼 수 없으나, 그대는 저를 볼 수 있을 것이옵니다."], ["Therefore come and place thy hand upon my shoulder, that I may know that thou art near me.", "그러하오니 다가오시어 제 어깨에 손을 얹으시어, 그대가 제 곁에 있음을 제가 알게 하소서."], ["And by the way that I go hence do thou accompany me.”", "그리고 제가 여기서 가는 길을 따라 함께 오소서.\""]], [["Then the maiden went away from Owain, and he did all that she had told him.", "그러고 나서 처녀는 오와인에게서 떠나갔고, 그는 그녀가 일러 준 대로 모두 행했다."], ["And the people of the castle came to seek Owain to put him to death; and when they found nothing but the half of his horse, they were sorely grieved.", "성안의 사람들은 오와인을 죽이려고 찾아 나섰는데, 그의 말의 절반밖에 찾지 못하자 몹시 애통해했다."]], [["And Owain vanished from among them, and went to the maiden, and placed his hand upon her shoulder; whereupon she set off, and Owain followed her, until they came to the door of a large and beautiful chamber, and the maiden opened it, and they went in.", "오와인은 그들 가운데서 자취를 감추고는, 처녀에게로 가서 그녀의 어깨에 손을 얹었다. 그러자 그녀가 길을 나섰고, 오와인은 그녀를 뒤따라, 마침내 크고 아름다운 방의 문에 이르렀다."], ["And Owain looked around the chamber, and behold there was not a single nail in it that was not painted with gorgeous colors, and there was not a single panel that had not sundry images in gold portrayed upon it.", "처녀가 문을 열었고, 그들은 안으로 들어갔다. 오와인이 방을 둘러보니, 보라, 그 안에는 화려한 빛깔로 칠해지지 않은 못이 단 하나도 없었으며, 갖가지 금빛 형상이 그려지지 않은 벽판이 단 하나도 없었다."]], [["The maiden kindled a fire, and took water in a silver bowl, and gave Owain water to wash.", "처녀는 불을 지피고, 은 대접에 물을 담아, 오와인에게 씻을 물을 건넸다."], ["Then she placed before him a silver table, inlaid with gold; upon which was a cloth of yellow linen, and she brought him food.", "그러고 나서 그 앞에 금이 박힌 은 탁자를 놓았는데, 그 위에는 황색 아마포 식탁보가 깔려 있었고, 그녀가 그에게 음식을 가져왔다."], ["And, of a truth, Owain never saw any kind of meat that was not there in abundance, but it was better cooked there than he had ever found it in any other place.", "그리고 참으로, 오와인은 거기에 풍성하게 갖추어지지 않은 고기 요리를 본 적이 없었으며, 게다가 그가 다른 어느 곳에서 맛본 것보다도 더 잘 조리되어 있었다."], ["And there was not one vessel from which he was served that was not of gold or of silver.", "그가 식사 시중을 받은 그릇 가운데 금이나 은이 아닌 것은 하나도 없었다."], ["And Owain ate and drank until late in the afternoon, when lo!", "오와인은 늦은 오후까지 먹고 마셨는데, 그때 보라!"], ["they heard a mighty clamor in the castle, and Owain asked the maiden what it was. “They are administering extreme unction,”", "그들은 성안에서 우렁찬 소란이 일어나는 것을 들었고, 오와인이 처녀에게 그것이 무슨 일이냐고 물었다."], ["said she, “to the nobleman who owns the castle.”", "\"성의 주인이신 귀족께 종부성사를 베풀고 있는 것이옵니다,\""], ["And she prepared a couch for Owain which was meet for Arthur himself, and Owain went to sleep.", "그녀가 말했다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 아서 자신에게도 어울릴 만한 침상을 오와인을 위해 마련했고, 오와인은 잠자리에 들었다."]], [["And a little after daybreak he heard an exceeding loud clamor and wailing, and he asked the maiden what was the cause of it.", "날이 밝고 얼마 지나지 않아 그는 지극히 큰 소란과 통곡을 들었고, 처녀에게 그것이 무슨 까닭이냐고 물었다."], ["“They are bearing to the church the body of the nobleman who owned the castle.”", "\"성의 주인이셨던 귀족의 시신을 성당으로 모시고 가는 것이옵니다.\""]], [["And Owain rose up, and clothed himself, and opened a window of the chamber, and looked towards the castle; and he could see neither the bounds nor the extent of the hosts that filled the streets.", "오와인은 일어나 옷을 입고, 방의 창문을 열어 성 쪽을 바라보았다. 거리를 가득 메운 무리의 경계도 그 끝도 그는 가늠할 수 없었다."], ["And they were fully armed; and a vast number of women were with them, both on horseback and on foot, and all the ecclesiastics in the city singing.", "그들은 완전히 무장하고 있었고, 수많은 여인들이 말을 타거나 걸어서 그들과 함께 있었으며, 도시의 모든 성직자들이 노래하고 있었다."], ["In the midst of the throng he beheld the bier, over which was a veil of white linen; and wax tapers were burning beside and around it; and none that supported the bier was lower in rank than a powerful baron.", "그 군중 한가운데서 그는 영구대를 보았는데, 그 위에는 흰 아마포 휘장이 덮여 있었고, 밀랍 양초들이 그 곁과 둘레에서 타고 있었다. 그 영구대를 떠받친 자들 가운데 막강한 영주보다 낮은 지위에 있는 자는 아무도 없었다."]], [["Never did Owain see an assemblage so gorgeous with silk[59] and satin.", "오와인은 비단과 새틴으로 그토록 화려한 무리를 일찍이 본 적이 없었다."], ["And, following the train, he beheld a lady with yellow hair falling over her shoulders, and stained with blood; and about her a dress of yellow satin, which was torn.", "그리고 그 행렬을 뒤따르는, 누런 머리가 어깨 위로 늘어지고 피로 물든 한 부인을 보았다. 그녀의 몸에는 황색 새틴 옷이 둘려 있었으나 찢겨 있었다."], ["Upon her feet were shoes of variegated leather.", "그녀의 발에는 여러 빛깔의 가죽으로 만든 신이 신겨 있었다."], ["And it was a marvel that the ends of her fingers were not bruised from the violence with which she smote her hands together.", "그녀가 두 손을 어찌나 격렬하게 마주쳤던지 손가락 끝이 멍들지 않은 것이 놀라울 정도였다."], ["Truly she would have been the fairest lady Owain ever saw, had she been in her usual guise.", "진실로 그녀가 평소의 모습이었다면, 오와인이 일찍이 본 가장 아름다운 부인이었으리라."], ["And her cry was louder than the shout of the men or the clamor of the trumpets.", "그녀의 울부짖음은 사람들의 외침이나 나팔 소리보다도 더 컸다."], ["No sooner had he beheld the lady than he became inflamed with her love, so that it took entire possession of him.", "그 부인을 보자마자 그는 그녀에 대한 사랑으로 불타올랐고, 그 사랑이 그를 온전히 사로잡았다."]], [["Then he inquired of the maiden who the lady was.", "그러고 나서 그는 처녀에게 그 부인이 누구인지를 물었다."], ["“Heaven knows,”", "\"하늘이 아시옵니다,\""], ["replied the maiden, “she is the fairest and the most chaste, and the most liberal, and the most noble of women.", "처녀가 대답했다, \"그분은 여인들 가운데 가장 아름답고, 가장 정숙하며, 가장 너그럽고, 가장 고귀한 분이옵니다."], ["She is my mistress, and she is called the Countess of the Fountain, the wife of him whom thou didst slay yesterday.”", "그분은 저의 여주인이시며, 샘의 백작 부인이라 불리시는데, 어제 그대가 죽인 자의 아내이시옵니다.\""], ["“Verily,”", "\"참으로,\""], ["said Owain, “she is the woman that I love best.”", "오와인이 말했다, \"그분이야말로 내가 가장 사랑하는 여인이오.\""], ["“Verily,”", "\"참으로,\""], ["said the maiden, “she shall also love thee, not a little.”", "처녀가 말했다, \"그분 또한 그대를 적잖이 사랑하시게 될 것이옵니다.\""]], [["Then the maiden prepared a repast for Owain, and truly he thought he had never before so good a meal, nor was he ever so well served.", "그러고 나서 처녀는 오와인을 위해 식사를 마련했는데, 참으로 그는 이전에 이토록 좋은 식사를 한 적도, 이토록 융숭한 시중을 받은 적도 없다고 여겼다."], ["Then she left him, and went towards the castle.", "이윽고 그녀는 그를 두고 성으로 향했다."], ["When she came there, she found nothing but mourning and sorrow; and the Countess in her chamber could not bear the sight of any one through grief.", "그곳에 이르니, 애도와 슬픔밖에는 없었으며, 백작 부인은 슬픔에 잠겨 그 누구의 모습도 견디지 못한 채 방에 있었다."], ["Luned, for that was the name of the maiden, saluted her, but the Countess answered her not.", "루네드라는 이름의 처녀가 그녀에게 인사를 건넸으나, 백작 부인은 답하지 않았다."], ["And the maiden bent down towards her, and said, “What aileth thee, that thou answereth no one to-day?”", "처녀가 그녀에게 몸을 굽히며 말했다, \"어찌하여 오늘 그 누구에게도 답하지 않으시옵니까?\""], ["“Luned,”", "\"루네드,\""], ["said the Countess, “what change hath befallen thee, that thou hast not come to visit me in my grief.", "백작 부인이 말했다, \"어찌 그리 변하여, 내가 슬픔에 잠겨 있는데 나를 보러 오지도 않았느냐."], ["It was wrong in thee, and I so sorely afflicted.”", "그것은 네가 잘못한 일이며, 나는 이토록 비통하거늘.\""], ["“Truly,”", "\"참으로,\""], ["said Luned, “I thought thy good sense was greater than I find it to be.", "루네드가 말했다, \"저는 마님의 분별이 지금 제가 보는 것보다 더 크리라 여겼나이다."], ["Is it well for thee to mourn after that good man, or for anything else that thou canst not have?”", "그 어진 분을 여의고 슬퍼하는 것이, 혹은 마님께서 가질 수 없는 그 무엇 때문에 슬퍼하는 것이 과연 마님께 이로운 일이옵니까?\""], ["“I declare to Heaven,”", "\"하늘에 맹세코,\""], ["said the Countess, “that in the whole world there is not a man equal to him.”", "백작 부인이 말했다, \"온 세상에 그분과 견줄 만한 사내는 없느니라.\" \"그렇지 않사옵니다,\""], ["“Not so,” said Luned, “for an ugly man would be as good as or better than he.”", "루네드가 말했다, \"추한 사내라도 그분만큼 훌륭하거나 더 나을 수 있나이다.\""], ["“I declare to Heaven,”", "\"하늘에 맹세코,\""], ["said the Countess, “that were it not repugnant to me to put to death one whom I have brought up, I would have thee executed for making such a comparison to me.", "백작 부인이 말했다, \"내가 길러 낸 자를 죽이는 것이 내게 역겨운 일만 아니라면, 내게 그런 비교를 한 죄로 너를 처형하게 했으리라."], ["As it is, I will banish thee.”", "사정이 이러하니, 너를 추방하겠노라.\""], ["“I am glad,”", "\"기쁘옵니다,\""], ["said Luned, “that thou hast no other cause to do so than that I would have been of service to thee, where thou didst not know what was to thine advantage.", "루네드가 말했다, \"마님께서 무엇이 이로운지를 모르시는 자리에서 제가 마님께 도움이 되고자 했다는 것 외에는 그리하실 다른 까닭이 없으시다니 말이옵니다."], ["Henceforth, evil betide whichever of us shall make the first advance towards reconciliation to the other, whether I should seek an invitation from thee, or thou of thine own accord should send to invite.”", "이제부터는, 우리 둘 중 어느 쪽이든 먼저 화해의 손길을 내미는 쪽에 화가 있을지어다. 제가 마님께 부름을 청하든, 마님께서 스스로 사람을 보내 저를 부르시든 말이옵니다.\""]], [["With that Luned went forth; and the Countess arose and followed her to the door of the chamber, and began coughing loudly.", "그러고 나서 루네드는 밖으로 나갔다. 백작 부인은 일어나 그녀를 따라 방문까지 가서, 큰 소리로 기침하기 시작했다."], ["And when Luned looked back, the Countess beckoned to her, and she returned to the Countess.", "루네드가 돌아보자, 백작 부인이 그녀를 손짓해 불렀고, 그녀는 백작 부인에게 돌아왔다."], ["“In truth,”", "\"참으로,\""], ["said the Countess, “evil is thy disposition; but if thou knowest what is to my advantage, declare it to me.”", "백작 부인이 말했다, \"네 성미가 고약하구나. 허나 네가 무엇이 내게 이로운지를 안다면, 그것을 내게 일러 다오.\""], ["“I will do so,”", "\"그리하겠나이다,\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Thou knowest that, except by warfare and arms, it is impossible for thee to preserve thy possessions; delay not, therefore, to seek some one who can defend them.”", "\"전쟁과 무력에 의하지 않고서는 마님께서 영지를 보존하시기가 불가능함을 아시지 않사옵니까. 그러하오니 지체 말고 그것을 지킬 수 있는 누군가를 찾으소서.\""], ["“And how can I do that?”", "\"한데 내가 어찌 그리할 수 있겠느냐?\""], ["said the Countess.", "백작 부인이 말했다."], ["“I will tell thee,”", "\"일러 드리겠나이다,\""], ["said Luned; “unless thou canst defend the fountain, thou canst not maintain thy dominions; and no one can defend the fountain except it be a knight of Arthur’s household.", "루네드가 말했다, \"마님께서 그 샘을 지켜 내지 못하시면, 영토를 유지하실 수 없나이다. 그리고 그 샘은 아서의 가솔에 속한 기사가 아니고서는 누구도 지켜 낼 수 없나이다."], ["I will go to Arthur’s court, and ill betide me if I return not thence with a warrior who can guard the fountain as well as, or even better than, he who defended it formerly.”", "제가 아서의 궁정으로 가서, 예전에 샘을 지키던 자만큼이나, 아니 그보다 더 잘 그 샘을 지킬 수 있는 무사를 데리고 돌아오지 못한다면 제게 화가 있을지어다.\""], ["“That will be hard to perform,”", "\"그것은 이루기 어려운 일이로구나,\""], ["said the Countess.", "백작 부인이 말했다."], ["“Go, however, and make proof of that which thou hast promised.”", "\"허나 가서, 네가 약속한 바를 증명해 보이거라.\""]], [["Luned set out under the pretence of going to Arthur’s court; but she went back to the mansion where she had left Owain, and she tarried there as long as it might have taken her to travel to the court of King Arthur and back.", "루네드는 아서의 궁정으로 가는 척하고 길을 나섰으나, 오와인을 남겨 두었던 저택으로 돌아가, 아서 왕의 궁정까지 다녀오는 데 걸릴 만한 시간 동안 그곳에 머물렀다."], ["And at the end of that time she apparelled herself, and went to visit the Countess.", "그 시간이 다하자 그녀는 옷을 차려입고, 백작 부인을 보러 갔다."], ["And the Countess was much rejoiced when she saw her, and inquired what news she brought from the court.", "백작 부인은 그녀를 보고 몹시 기뻐하며, 궁정에서 무슨 소식을 가져왔는지 물었다."], ["“I bring thee the best of news,”", "\"마님께 더없이 좋은 소식을 가져왔나이다,\""], ["said Luned, “for I have compassed the object of my mission.", "루네드가 말했다, \"제 사명의 목적을 이루었기 때문이옵니다."], ["When wilt thou that I should present to thee the chieftain who has come with me hither?”", "저와 함께 이리로 온 그 우두머리를 마님께 언제 소개해 드리오리까?\""], ["“Bring him here to visit me to-morrow,”", "\"내일 그를 이리로 데려와 나를 보게 하라,\""], ["said the Countess, “and I will cause the town to be assembled by that time.”", "백작 부인이 말했다, \"그때까지 내가 고을 사람들을 모아 두겠노라.\""]], [["And Luned returned home.", "그리하여 루네드는 집으로 돌아왔다."], ["And the next day at noon, Owain arrayed himself in a coat and a surcoat, and a mantle of yellow satin, upon which was a broad band of gold lace; and on his feet were high shoes of variegated leather, which were fastened by golden clasps, in the form of lions.", "그리고 이튿날 정오에 오와인은 윗옷과 겉옷, 그리고 넓은 금실 레이스 띠가 달린 황색 새틴 망토를 차려입었다. 그의 발에는 여러 빛깔의 가죽으로 만든, 사자 형상의 황금 죔쇠로 여민 높은 신이 신겨 있었다."], ["And they proceeded to the chamber of the Countess.", "그리고 그들은 백작 부인의 방으로 나아갔다."]], [["Right glad was the Countess of their coming.", "백작 부인은 그들이 온 것을 무척 기뻐했다."], ["And she gazed steadfastly upon Owain, and said, “Luned, this knight has not the look of a traveller.”", "그녀는 오와인을 뚫어지게 바라보며 말했다, \"루네드, 이 기사는 나그네 같은 행색이 아니로구나.\""], ["“What harm is there in that, lady?”", "\"그것이 무슨 해가 되옵니까, 마님?\""], ["said Luned.", "루네드가 말했다."], ["“I am certain,”", "\"분명코,\""], ["said the Countess, “that no other man than this chased the soul from the body of my lord.”", "백작 부인이 말했다, \"내 주인의 몸에서 혼을 쫓아낸 자는 다름 아닌 이 사람이렷다.\""], ["“So much the better for thee, lady,”", "\"마님께는 오히려 잘된 일이옵니다, 마님,\""], ["said Luned, “for had he not been stronger than thy lord, he could not have deprived him of life.", "루네드가 말했다, \"이 사람이 주인보다 강하지 않았더라면, 주인의 목숨을 앗을 수 없었을 것이옵니다."], ["There is no remedy for that which is past, be it as it may.”", "이미 지나간 일은 어찌할 도리가 없는 것이니, 사정이 어떠하든 그러하옵니다.\""], ["“Go back to thine abode,”", "\"네 거처로 돌아가 있거라,\""], ["said the Countess, “and I will take counsel.”", "백작 부인이 말했다, \"내가 깊이 생각해 보겠노라.\""]], [["The next day the Countess caused all her subjects to assemble, and showed them that her earldom was left defenceless, and that it could not be protected but with horse and arms, and military skill.", "이튿날 백작 부인은 자신의 모든 신민을 불러 모아, 자신의 백작령이 무방비 상태로 남겨졌으며, 말과 무기와 군사적 기량 없이는 지켜 낼 수 없음을 그들에게 알렸다."], ["“Therefore,”", "\"그러하니,\""], ["said she, “this is what I offer for your choice: either let one of you take me, or give your consent for me to take a husband from elsewhere, to defend my dominions.”", "그녀가 말했다, \"내가 그대들의 선택에 부치는 바는 이러하다. 그대들 중 한 사람이 나를 취하든지, 아니면 내가 영토를 지키기 위해 다른 곳에서 남편을 맞이하는 것에 동의하든지 하라.\""]], [["So they came to the determination that it was better that she should have permission to marry some one from elsewhere; and thereupon she sent for the bishops and archbishops, to celebrate her nuptials with Owain.", "그리하여 그들은 그녀가 다른 곳에서 누군가와 혼인하도록 허락받는 것이 낫다는 결론에 이르렀다. 이에 그녀는 주교들과 대주교들을 불러, 오와인과의 혼례를 거행하게 했다."], ["And the men of the earldom did Owain homage.", "그리고 백작령의 사람들은 오와인에게 충성을 맹세했다."]], [["And Owain defended the fountain with lance and sword.", "오와인은 창과 검으로 그 샘을 지켰다."], ["And this is the manner in which he defended it.", "그가 그것을 지킨 방식은 이러했다."], ["Whensoever a knight came there, he overthrew him, and sold him for his full worth.", "어떤 기사든 그곳에 오면, 그는 그를 쓰러뜨리고, 그 값어치를 온전히 받고 그를 팔았다."], ["And what he thus gained he divided among his barons and his knights, and no man in the whole world could be more beloved than he was by his subjects.", "그리고 그렇게 얻은 것을 자신의 영주들과 기사들에게 나누어 주니, 온 세상에 그만큼 신민들에게 사랑받을 수 있는 사람은 없었다."], ["And it was thus for the space of three years.[60]", "그리고 이러하기를 삼 년 동안 계속했다."]], [["————", "————"]], [["# GAWAIN’S ADVENTURE", "# GAWAIN’S ADVENTURE"]], [["IT befell that, as Gawain went forth one day with King Arthur, he perceived him to be very sad and sorrowful.", "어느 날 가웨인이 아서 왕과 함께 길을 나섰을 때, 그는 왕이 몹시 슬프고 침울해하는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["And Gawain was much grieved to see Arthur in his state, and he questioned him, saying, “O my lord, what has befallen thee?”", "가웨인은 그러한 처지에 놓인 아서를 보고 마음이 무척 아파서, 이렇게 물었다. \"오, 전하, 무슨 일이 있으셨습니까?\""], ["“In sooth, Gawain,”", "\"사실은 말일세, 가웨인.\" 아서가 말했다."], ["said Arthur, “I am grieved concerning Owain, whom I have lost these three years; and I shall certainly die if the fourth year pass without my seeing him.", "\"나는 오와인 때문에 마음이 무겁네. 그를 잃은 지 벌써 삼 년이 되었고, 만일 사 년째 되는 해가 그를 보지 못한 채 지나간다면 나는 틀림없이 죽고 말 것이네."], ["Now I am sure that it is through the tale which Kynon, the son of Clydno, related, that I have lost Owain.”", "이제 나는 그를 잃게 된 것이 클리드노의 아들 키논이 들려준 이야기 때문임을 확신하고 있네.\" \"그 일로 전하의 온 영토에 무장을 명하실 필요는 없습니다.\""], ["“There is no need for thee,”", "가웨인이 말했다."], ["said Gawain, “to summon to arms thy whole dominions on this account, for thou thyself, and the men of thy household, will be able to avenge Owain if he be slain or to set him free if he be in prison; and, if alive, to bring him back with thee.”", "\"전하와 전하의 가신들만으로도, 오와인이 죽임을 당했다면 그 원수를 갚을 수 있고, 갇혀 있다면 풀어 줄 수 있으며, 살아 있다면 전하와 함께 데려올 수 있을 것입니다.\""], ["And it was settled according to what Gawain had said.", "그리하여 가웨인이 말한 대로 일이 정해졌다."]], [["Then Arthur and the men of his household prepared to go and seek Owain.", "그리고 아서와 그의 가신들은 길을 떠나 오와인을 찾을 채비를 했다."], ["And Kynon, the son of Clydno, acted as their guide.", "클리드노의 아들 키논이 그들의 길잡이 노릇을 했다."], ["And Arthur came to the castle where Kynon had been before.", "아서는 키논이 전에 머물렀던 성에 다다랐다."], ["And when he came there, the youths were shooting in the same place, and the yellow man was standing hard by.", "그곳에 이르렀을 때, 젊은이들은 예전과 같은 자리에서 활을 쏘고 있었고, 노란 옷을 입은 사내가 가까이에 서 있었다."], ["When the yellow man saw Arthur, he greeted him, and invited him to the castle.", "노란 옷의 사내는 아서를 보자 그를 맞이하며 성으로 들어오라고 청했다."], ["And Arthur accepted his invitation, and they entered the castle together.", "아서는 그 청을 받아들였고, 그들은 함께 성으로 들어갔다."], ["And great as was the number of his retinue, their presence was scarcely observed in the castle, so vast was its extent.", "그를 따르는 무리의 수가 많았음에도, 성이 워낙 광대하여 그들의 존재가 성안에서는 거의 눈에 띄지 않았다."], ["And the maidens rose up to wait on them.", "처녀들이 일어나 그들의 시중을 들었다."], ["And the service of the maidens appeared to them all to excel any attendance they had ever met with; and even the pages, who had charge of the horses, were no worse served that night than Arthur himself would have been in his own palace.", "처녀들의 시중은 그들 모두가 그때까지 받아 본 어떤 접대보다도 뛰어나 보였다. 말을 돌보는 시동들조차 그날 밤 아서가 자신의 궁전에서 받았을 대접에 못지않은 시중을 받았다."]], [["The next morning Arthur set out thence, with Kynon for his guide, and came to the place where the black man was.", "이튿날 아침 아서는 키논을 길잡이 삼아 그곳을 떠나, 검은 옷의 사내가 있는 곳에 이르렀다."], ["And the stature of the black man was more surprising to Arthur than it had been represented to him.", "검은 옷의 사내의 키는 들었던 것보다도 더 놀랍게 아서에게 다가왔다."], ["And they came to the top of the wooded steep, and traversed the valley, till they reached the green tree, where they saw the fountain and the bowl and the slab.", "그들은 숲이 우거진 가파른 언덕 꼭대기에 올라 골짜기를 가로질러, 마침내 푸른 나무가 선 곳에 다다랐고, 그곳에서 샘과 그릇과 석판을 보았다."], ["And upon that Kay came to Arthur, and spoke to him.", "그러자 케이가 아서에게 다가와 말을 건넸다. \"전하.\""], ["“My lord,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “I know the meaning of all this, and my request is that thou wilt permit me to throw the water on the slab, and to receive the first adventure that may befall.”", "\"저는 이 모든 것의 뜻을 알고 있습니다. 청하옵건대, 제가 석판 위에 물을 끼얹어 처음 닥쳐오는 모험을 맞이하도록 허락해 주십시오.\""], ["And Arthur gave him leave.", "아서는 그에게 허락을 내렸다."]], [["Then Kay threw a bowlful of water upon the slab, and immediately there came the thunder, and after the thunder the shower.", "그러자 케이가 그릇 가득 물을 떠서 석판 위에 끼얹었고, 곧바로 천둥이 쳤으며 천둥에 뒤이어 소나기가 쏟아졌다."], ["And such a thunder-storm they had never known before.", "그들은 그처럼 거센 뇌우를 일찍이 겪어 본 적이 없었다."], ["After the shower had ceased, the sky became clear, and on looking at the tree, they beheld it completely leafless.", "소나기가 그치자 하늘이 맑아졌고, 나무를 바라보니 잎이 하나도 남지 않은 모습이었다."], ["Then the birds descended upon the tree.", "그러자 새들이 나무 위로 내려앉았다."], ["And the song of the birds was far sweeter than any strain they had ever heard before.", "새들의 노래는 그들이 그때까지 들어 본 어떤 가락보다도 훨씬 감미로웠다."], ["Then they beheld a knight, on a coal-black horse, clothed in black satin, coming rapidly towards them.", "이윽고 그들은 칠흑같이 검은 말에 올라 검은 비단옷을 걸친 한 기사가 그들을 향해 빠르게 다가오는 것을 보았다."], ["And Kay met him and encountered him, and it was not long before Kay was overthrown.", "케이가 그를 맞아 겨루었으나, 오래지 않아 케이가 나가떨어지고 말았다."], ["And the knight withdrew.", "기사는 물러갔다."], ["And Arthur and his host encamped for the night.", "아서와 그의 무리는 그날 밤 그곳에 진을 쳤다."]], [["And when they arose in the morning, they perceived the signal of combat upon the lance of the knight.", "이튿날 아침 그들이 일어났을 때, 기사의 창에 결투를 알리는 표지가 걸려 있는 것이 보였다."], ["Then, one by one, all the household of Arthur went forth to combat the knight, until there was not one that was not overthrown by him, except Arthur and Gawain.", "그러자 아서의 가신들이 하나씩 차례로 나아가 그 기사와 겨루었으나, 아서와 가웨인을 제외하고는 그에게 나가떨어지지 않은 이가 하나도 없었다."], ["And Arthur armed himself to encounter the knight.", "마침내 아서가 그 기사와 맞서고자 무장을 했다."], ["“O my lord,”", "\"오, 전하.\""], ["said Gawain, “permit me to fight with him first.”", "가웨인이 말했다. \"제가 먼저 그와 싸우도록 허락해 주십시오.\""], ["And Arthur permitted him.", "아서는 그것을 허락했다."], ["And he went forth to meet the knight, having over himself and his horse a satin robe of honor, which had been sent him by the daughter of the Earl of Rhangyr, and in this dress he was not known by any of the host.", "가웨인은 자신과 말에게 랑기르 백작의 딸이 보내 준 비단 명예의 겉옷을 덮어쓰고 기사를 맞으러 나아갔는데, 그 차림 덕분에 무리 가운데 누구도 그를 알아보지 못했다."], ["And they charged each other, and fought all that day until the evening.", "둘은 서로에게 돌진하여 그날 저녁까지 온종일 싸웠다."], ["And neither of them was able to unhorse the other.", "그러나 어느 쪽도 상대를 말에서 떨어뜨리지 못했다."], ["And so it was the next day; they broke their lances in the shock, but neither of them could obtain the mastery.", "그 이튿날도 마찬가지였다. 그들은 충돌하며 창을 부러뜨렸으나, 어느 쪽도 우위를 차지하지 못했다."]], [["And the third day they fought with exceeding strong lances.", "사흘째 되는 날 그들은 유난히 튼튼한 창을 들고 싸웠다."], ["And they were incensed with rage, and fought furiously, even until noon.", "그들은 분노에 사로잡혀 정오에 이르기까지 격렬하게 맞붙었다."], ["And they gave each other such a shock that the girths of their horses were broken, so that they fell over their horses’ cruppers to the ground.", "그러다 서로에게 어찌나 큰 충격을 가했던지 말들의 뱃대끈이 끊어졌고, 둘은 말 엉덩이 너머로 땅에 떨어지고 말았다."], ["And they rose up speedily and drew their swords, and resumed the combat.", "그들은 재빨리 일어나 칼을 뽑아 들고 다시 싸움을 이어 갔다."], ["And all they that witnessed their encounter felt assured that they had never before seen two men so valiant or so powerful.", "그들의 대결을 지켜본 이들은 모두, 그처럼 용맹하고 강력한 두 사람을 일찍이 본 적이 없다고 확신했다."], ["And had it been midnight, it would have been light, from the fire that flashed from their weapons.", "설령 한밤중이었다 한들, 그들의 무기에서 번쩍이는 불꽃만으로도 사방이 환했을 것이다."], ["And the knight gave Gawain a blow that turned his helmet from off his face, so that the knight saw that it was Gawain.", "그러다 기사가 가웨인에게 일격을 가하여 그의 투구를 얼굴에서 벗겨 내자, 기사는 상대가 가웨인임을 알아보았다. 그러자 오와인이 말했다."], ["Then Owain said, “My lord Gawain, I did not know thee for my cousin, owing to the robe of honor that enveloped thee; take my sword and my arms.”", "\"가웨인 경, 그대를 감싼 명예의 겉옷 때문에 나는 내 사촌인 그대를 알아보지 못했소. 내 칼과 무기를 받으시오.\""], ["Said Gawain, “Thou, Owain, art the victor; take thou my sword.”", "가웨인이 말했다. \"오와인, 그대가 승자요."], ["And with that Arthur saw that they were conversing, and advanced toward them.", "그러니 그대가 내 칼을 받으시오.\" 그때 아서가 그들이 이야기를 나누는 것을 보고 그들을 향해 다가왔다."], ["“My lord Arthur,”", "\"아서 전하.\""], ["said Gawain, “here is Owain who has vanquished me, and will not take my arms.”", "가웨인이 말했다. \"여기 오와인이 저를 이겼는데도 제 무기를 받으려 하지 않습니다.\""], ["“My lord,”", "\"전하.\""], ["said Owain, “it is he that has vanquished me, and he will not take my sword.”", "오와인이 말했다. \"저를 이긴 것은 이 사람이온데, 이 사람이 제 칼을 받으려 하지 않습니다.\""], ["“Give me your swords,”", "\"그대들의 칼을 내게 다오.\""], ["said Arthur, “and then neither of you has vanquished the other.”", "아서가 말했다. \"그러면 어느 쪽도 상대를 이긴 것이 아니게 되리라.\""], ["Then Owain put his arms around Arthur’s neck, and they embraced.", "그러자 오와인이 아서의 목을 두 팔로 감싸 안았고, 그들은 서로를 끌어안았다."], ["And all the host hurried forward to see Owain, and to embrace him.", "온 무리가 오와인을 보고 그를 끌어안으려고 앞으로 몰려들었다."], ["And there was nigh being a loss of life, so great was the press.", "그 북새통이 어찌나 심했던지, 하마터면 목숨을 잃을 뻔한 이가 생길 지경이었다."]], [["And they retired that night, and the next day Arthur prepared to depart.", "그들은 그날 밤 물러났고, 이튿날 아서는 떠날 채비를 했다."], ["“My lord,”", "\"전하.\""], ["said Owain, “this is not well of thee.", "오와인이 말했다. \"그것은 전하답지 못한 일입니다."], ["For I have been absent from thee these three years, and during all that time, up to this very day, I have been preparing a banquet for thee, knowing that thou wouldst come to seek me.", "저는 전하 곁을 떠나 있은 지 삼 년이 되었고, 그동안 줄곧 바로 오늘에 이르기까지, 전하께서 저를 찾으러 오시리라는 것을 알고 전하를 위한 연회를 준비해 왔습니다."], ["Tarry with me, therefore, until thou and thy attendants have recovered the fatigues of the journey, and have been anointed.”", "그러니 전하와 시종들이 여행의 피로를 풀고 향유를 바를 때까지 저와 함께 머물러 주십시오.\""]], [["And they all proceeded to the castle of the Countess of the Fountain, and the banquet which had been three years preparing was consumed in three months.", "그리하여 그들은 모두 샘의 백작 부인의 성으로 갔고, 삼 년에 걸쳐 준비된 연회는 석 달 만에 모두 동이 났다."], ["Never had they a more delicious or agreeable banquet.", "그들은 그보다 더 맛있고 흡족한 연회를 누려 본 적이 없었다."], ["And Arthur prepared to depart.", "이윽고 아서는 떠날 채비를 했다."], ["Then he sent an embassy to the Countess to beseech her to permit Owain to go with him, for the space of three months, that he might show him to the nobles and the fair dames of the island of Britain.", "그러면서 그는 백작 부인에게 사절을 보내, 오와인을 석 달 동안 자신과 함께 가게 허락해 달라고, 그를 브리튼 섬의 귀족들과 아리따운 귀부인들에게 보여 줄 수 있도록 청했다."], ["And the Countess gave her consent, although it was very painful to her.", "백작 부인은 몹시 고통스러웠으나 승낙해 주었다."], ["So Owain came with Arthur to the island of Britain.", "그리하여 오와인은 아서와 함께 브리튼 섬으로 왔다."], ["And when he was once more amongst his kindred and friends, he remained three years, instead of three months, with them.", "그가 다시 친척들과 벗들 가운데 있게 되자, 그는 석 달이 아니라 삼 년을 그들과 함께 머물렀다."]], [["# THE ADVENTURE OF THE LION", "# THE ADVENTURE OF THE LION"]], [["And as Owain one day sat at meat, in the city of Caerleon upon Usk, behold a damsel entered the hall, upon a bay horse,[61] with a curling mane, and covered with foam; and the bridle, and as much as was seen of the saddle, were of gold.", "어느 날 오와인이 우스크 강가의 카이르레온 시에서 식사를 하며 앉아 있는데, 한 처녀가 갈기가 곱슬곱슬하고 거품으로 뒤덮인 밤색 말을 타고 회관 안으로 들어왔다. 굴레와 안장의 드러난 부분은 모두 금으로 되어 있었다."], ["And the damsel was arrayed in a dress of yellow satin.", "처녀는 노란 비단옷을 차려입고 있었다. 그녀는 오와인에게 다가와 그의 손가락에서 반지를 빼앗았다."], ["And she came up to Owain, and took the ring from off his hand.", "\"이렇게 다루어 마땅하다.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["“Thus,” said she, “shall be treated the deceiver, the traitor, the faithless, the disgraced, and the beardless.”", "\"속이는 자, 배신하는 자, 신의 없는 자, 명예를 잃은 자, 수염도 못 기른 자를.\""], ["And she turned her horse’s head and departed.", "그러고는 말 머리를 돌려 떠나 버렸다."]], [["Then his adventure came to Owain’s remembrance, and he was sorrowful.", "그러자 자신의 모험이 오와인의 기억에 되살아났고, 그는 슬픔에 잠겼다."], ["And having finished eating, he went to his own abode, and made preparations that night.", "식사를 마친 그는 자신의 거처로 가서 그날 밤 채비를 갖추었다."], ["And the next day he arose, but did not go to the court, nor did he return to the Countess of the Fountain, but wandered to the distant parts of the earth and to uncultivated mountains.", "이튿날 그는 일어났으나 궁정으로 가지도, 샘의 백작 부인에게 돌아가지도 않고, 땅의 멀고 먼 곳들과 사람의 손길이 닿지 않은 산들을 떠돌았다."], ["And he remained there until all his apparel was worn out, and his body was wasted away, and his hair was grown long.", "그는 옷이 모두 해지고 몸이 야위고 머리카락이 길게 자랄 때까지 그곳에 머물렀다."], ["And he went about with the wild beasts, and fed with them, until they became familiar with him.", "그는 들짐승들과 더불어 돌아다니며 그들과 함께 먹이를 먹었고, 마침내 짐승들이 그를 따르게 되었다."], ["But at length he became so weak that he could no longer bear them company.", "그러나 끝내 그는 너무나 쇠약해져 더 이상 그들과 함께 지낼 수 없게 되었다."], ["Then he descended from the mountains to the valley, and came to a park, that was the fairest in the world, and belonged to a charitable lady.", "그리하여 그는 산에서 골짜기로 내려와, 세상에서 가장 아름다우며 자비로운 한 귀부인의 소유인 동산에 이르렀다."]], [["One day the lady and her attendants went forth to walk by a lake that was in the middle of the park.", "어느 날 그 귀부인과 시녀들이 동산 한가운데 있는 호숫가를 거닐려고 나왔다."], ["And they saw the form of a man, lying as if dead.", "그들은 죽은 듯이 누워 있는 한 사내의 모습을 보았다."], ["And they were terrified.", "그들은 겁에 질렸다."], ["Nevertheless they went near him, and touched him, and they saw that there was life in him.", "그래도 그들은 그에게 다가가 그를 만져 보았고, 그에게 아직 목숨이 붙어 있음을 알았다."], ["And the lady returned to the castle, and took a flask full of precious ointment and gave it to one of her maidens.", "귀부인은 성으로 돌아가 귀한 연고가 가득 든 병을 가져와 시녀 하나에게 주었다. \"이것을 가지고 가거라.\""], ["“Go with this,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “and take with thee yonder horse, and clothing, and place them near the man we saw just now; and anoint him with this balsam near his heart; and if there is life in him, he will revive, through the efficiency of this balsam.", "\"그리고 저 말과 옷가지를 데리고 가서 방금 우리가 본 사내 곁에 두어라. 그의 심장 가까이에 이 향유를 발라 주어라."], ["Then watch what he will do.”", "그에게 목숨이 붙어 있다면 이 향유의 효험으로 그가 되살아날 것이다. 그런 다음 그가 어떻게 하는지 지켜보아라.\""]], [["And the maiden departed from her, and went and poured of the balsam upon Owain, and left the horse and the garments hard by, and went a little way off and hid herself to watch him.", "시녀는 그녀에게서 떠나, 가서 오와인에게 향유를 부어 주고 말과 옷가지를 가까이에 두었으며, 조금 떨어진 곳으로 가서 몸을 숨기고 그를 지켜보았다."], ["In a short time, she saw him begin to move; and he rose up, and looked at his person, and became ashamed of the unseemliness of his appearance.", "얼마 지나지 않아 그녀는 그가 움직이기 시작하는 것을 보았다. 그는 일어나 자신의 몸을 살펴보았고, 자신의 보기 흉한 몰골을 부끄러워했다."], ["Then he perceived the horse and the garments that were near him.", "그러다 그는 곁에 놓인 말과 옷가지를 알아차렸다."], ["And he clothed himself, and with difficulty mounted the horse.", "그는 옷을 차려입고 가까스로 말에 올랐다."], ["Then the damsel discovered herself to him, and saluted him.", "그제야 처녀가 그에게 모습을 드러내고 그에게 인사했다."], ["And he and the maiden proceeded to the castle, and the maiden conducted him to a pleasant chamber, and kindled a fire, and left him.", "그와 처녀는 성으로 갔고, 처녀는 그를 쾌적한 방으로 안내하여 불을 피우고 그를 두고 떠났다."]], [["And he stayed at the castle three months, till he was restored to his former guise, and became even more comely than he had ever been before.", "그는 석 달 동안 성에 머물렀고, 마침내 예전의 모습을 되찾았으며, 이전 어느 때보다도 더 잘생긴 사람이 되었다."], ["And Owain rendered signal service to the lady, in a controversy with a powerful neighbor, so that he made ample requital to her for her hospitality; and he took his departure.", "오와인은 강력한 이웃과의 분쟁에서 그 귀부인에게 큰 공을 세워, 그녀의 환대에 넉넉히 보답했다. 그리고 그는 길을 떠났다."]], [["And as he journeyed he heard a loud yelling in a wood.", "길을 가던 중에 그는 숲속에서 우렁찬 울부짖음을 들었다."], ["And it was repeated a second and a third time.", "그 소리는 두 번째, 세 번째로 되풀이되었다."], ["And Owain went towards the spot, and beheld a huge craggy mound, in the middle of the wood, on the side of which was a gray rock.", "오와인은 그 자리를 향해 갔고, 숲 한가운데 솟은 거대한 바위투성이 둔덕을 보았는데, 그 비탈에는 잿빛 바위가 있었다."], ["And there was a cleft in the rock, and a serpent was within the cleft.", "바위에는 틈이 갈라져 있었고, 그 틈 안에 뱀 한 마리가 있었다."], ["And near the rock stood a black lion, and every time the lion sought to go thence the serpent darted towards him to attack him.", "바위 가까이에는 검은 사자 한 마리가 서 있었는데, 사자가 그곳을 벗어나려 할 때마다 뱀이 그에게 달려들어 공격하려 했다."], ["And Owain unsheathed his sword, and drew near to the rock; and as the serpent sprung out he struck him with his sword and cut him in two.", "오와인은 칼을 뽑아 들고 바위에 다가갔다. 그리고 뱀이 튀어나오자 칼로 그것을 내리쳐 두 동강을 냈다."], ["And he dried his sword, and went on his way as before.", "그는 칼을 닦고 전과 같이 길을 갔다."], ["But behold the lion followed him, and played about him, as though it had been a greyhound that he had reared.", "그러자 보라, 사자가 그를 따라오며, 마치 자기가 길러 온 사냥개라도 되는 양 그의 주위에서 뛰놀았다."]], [["They proceeded thus throughout the day, until the evening.", "그들은 그렇게 온종일 저녁때까지 나아갔다."], ["And when it was time for Owain to take his rest he dismounted, and turned his horse loose in a flat and wooded meadow.", "오와인이 쉴 때가 되자 그는 말에서 내려 평평하고 나무가 우거진 풀밭에 말을 풀어놓았다."], ["And he struck fire, and when the fire was kindled, the lion brought him fuel enough to last for three nights.", "그가 불을 일으켜 불씨가 붙자, 사자가 사흘 밤을 댈 만큼 넉넉한 땔감을 그에게 가져다주었다."], ["And the lion disappeared.", "그러고는 사자가 사라졌다."], ["And presently the lion returned, bearing a fine large roebuck.", "이윽고 사자가 살집 좋은 커다란 노루 한 마리를 물고 돌아왔다."], ["And he threw it down before Owain, who went towards the fire with it.", "사자가 그것을 오와인 앞에 내려놓자, 오와인은 그것을 가지고 불가로 갔다."]], [["And Owain took the roebuck, and skinned it, and placed collops of its flesh upon skewers round the fire.", "오와인은 노루를 잡아 가죽을 벗기고, 그 살점을 꼬챙이에 꿰어 불 둘레에 둘렀다."], ["The rest of the buck he gave to the lion to devour.", "나머지 노루는 사자에게 먹으라고 주었다."], ["While he was so employed, he heard a deep groan near him, and a second, and a third.", "그렇게 일하고 있을 때, 그는 가까이에서 깊은 신음 소리를 들었고, 두 번째, 세 번째로 또 들었다."], ["And the place whence the groans proceeded was a cave in the rock; and Owain went near, and called out to know who it was that groaned so piteously.", "그 신음이 흘러나오는 곳은 바위에 난 동굴이었다. 오와인은 가까이 가서, 그토록 애처롭게 신음하는 자가 누구인지 알고자 소리쳐 물었다."], ["And a voice answered, “I am Luned, the hand-maiden of the Countess of the Fountain.”", "한 목소리가 대답했다. \"저는 샘의 백작 부인의 시녀 루네드입니다.\""], ["“And what dost thou here?”", "\"그대는 여기서 무엇을 하고 있는가?\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“I am imprisoned,”", "\"저는 갇혀 있습니다.\""], ["said she, “on account of the knight who came from Arthur’s court, and married the Countess.", "그녀가 말했다. \"아서의 궁정에서 와서 백작 부인과 혼인한 기사 때문입니다."], ["And he staid a short time with her, but he afterwards departed for the court of Arthur, and has not returned since.", "그는 잠시 그녀와 함께 머물렀으나, 그 뒤 아서의 궁정으로 떠나서는 그 후로 돌아오지 않았습니다."], ["And two of the Countess’s pages traduced him, and called him a deceiver.", "백작 부인의 시동 둘이 그를 헐뜯으며 속이는 자라고 불렀습니다."], ["And because I said I would vouch for it he would come before long and maintain his cause against both of them, they imprisoned me in this cave, and said that I should be put to death, unless he came to deliver me, by a certain day; and that is no further off than to-morrow, and I have no one to send to seek him for me.", "제가 그는 머지않아 와서 그 두 사람 모두를 상대로 자신의 명예를 지키리라고 보증하겠다고 말했더니, 그들은 저를 이 동굴에 가두고, 정해진 날까지 그가 와서 저를 구해 내지 않으면 저를 죽이겠다고 했습니다. 그 날이 바로 내일로 다가왔는데, 저를 위해 그를 찾으러 보낼 사람이 아무도 없습니다."], ["His name is Owain, the son of Urien.”", "그의 이름은 우리엔의 아들 오와인입니다.\""], ["“And art thou certain that if that knight knew all this, he would come to thy rescue?”", "\"만일 그 기사가 이 모든 일을 안다면 그대를 구하러 오리라고 확신하는가?\""], ["“I am most certain of it,”", "\"저는 그것을 무엇보다 확신합니다.\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["When the collops were cooked, Owain divided them into two parts, between himself and the maiden, and then Owain laid himself down to sleep; and never did sentinel keep stricter watch over his lord than the lion that night over Owain.", "살점이 다 익자, 오와인은 그것을 자신과 처녀 사이에 둘로 나누었다. 그런 다음 오와인은 누워 잠을 청했는데, 그날 밤 사자가 오와인을 지킨 것만큼 엄중하게 주인을 지킨 파수꾼은 일찍이 없었다."]], [["And the next day there came the two pages with a great troop of attendants to take Luned from her cell, and put her to death.", "이튿날 그 두 시동이 많은 시종 무리를 거느리고 와서, 루네드를 감방에서 끌어내 죽이려 했다."], ["And Owain asked them what charge they had against her.", "오와인은 그들에게 그녀에게 무슨 죄를 묻느냐고 물었다."], ["And they told him of the compact that was between them; as the maiden had done the night before.", "그들은 그들 사이에 있었던 약속을 그에게 이야기했는데, 그것은 전날 밤 처녀가 했던 이야기와 같았다. \"그리고,\""], ["“And,”", "그들이 말했다."], ["said they, “Owain has failed her, therefore we are taking her to be burnt.”", "\"오와인이 그녀의 기대를 저버렸으니, 우리는 그녀를 끌고 가 불태우려는 것이오.\" \"참으로,\""], ["“Truly,”", "오와인이 말했다."], ["said Owain, “he is a good knight; and if he knew that the maiden was in such peril, I marvel that he came not to her rescue.", "\"그는 훌륭한 기사요. 만일 그가 그 처녀가 이런 위험에 처한 것을 알았다면, 그녀를 구하러 오지 않았다는 것이 나는 의아할 따름이오."], ["But if you will accept me in his stead, I will do battle with you.”", "그러나 그대들이 그를 대신해 나를 받아들인다면, 내가 그대들과 싸우겠소.\""], ["“We will,”", "\"그렇게 합시다.\""], ["said the youth.", "젊은이가 말했다."]], [["And they attacked Owain, and he was hard beset by them.", "그들이 오와인을 공격하자, 그는 그들에게 호되게 시달렸다."], ["And with that, the lion came to Owain’s assistance, and they two got the better of the young men.", "그러자 사자가 오와인을 도우러 왔고, 둘은 그 젊은이들을 제압했다. 그들이 그에게 말했다."], ["And they said to him, “Chieftain, it was not agreed that we should fight save with thyself alone, and it is harder for us to contend with yonder animal than with thee.”", "\"기사여, 우리가 그대 혼자하고만 싸우기로 했지, 저 짐승하고는 약속한 바가 없소. 저 짐승과 겨루는 것은 그대와 겨루는 것보다 더 어렵소.\""], ["And Owain put the lion in the place where Luned had been imprisoned, and blocked up the door with stones.", "그래서 오와인은 사자를 루네드가 갇혀 있던 곳에 넣고 돌로 문을 막았다."], ["And he went to fight with the young men as before.", "그러고는 전과 같이 그 젊은이들과 싸우러 나아갔다."], ["But Owain had not his usual strength, and the two youths pressed hard upon him.", "그러나 오와인은 평소의 기력이 없었고, 두 젊은이는 그를 거세게 몰아붙였다."], ["And the lion roared incessantly at seeing Owain in trouble.", "오와인이 곤경에 처한 것을 본 사자가 끊임없이 포효했다."], ["And he brust through the wall, until he found a way out, and rushed upon the young men and instantly slew them.", "그러더니 사자가 벽을 뚫고 나갈 길을 찾아내 젊은이들에게 달려들어 단숨에 그들을 죽여 버렸다."], ["So Luned was saved from being burned.", "그리하여 루네드는 불태워질 위기에서 구해졌다."]], [["Then Owain returned with Luned to the castle of the Lady of the Fountain.", "그런 다음 오와인은 루네드와 함께 샘의 부인의 성으로 돌아갔다."], ["And when he went thence, he took the Countess with him to Arthur’s court, and she was his wife as long as she lived. ————", "그리고 그곳을 떠날 때, 그는 백작 부인을 데리고 아서의 궁정으로 갔으며, 그녀는 살아 있는 동안 내내 그의 아내였다."]], [["# GERAINT, THE SON OF ERBIN", "# GERAINT, THE SON OF ERBIN"]], [["ARTHUR was accustomed to hold his court at Caerleon upon Usk.", "아서 왕은 우스크 강가의 카에르레온에서 궁정을 여는 것이 관례였다."], ["And there he held it seven Easters and five Christmases.", "그리고 그곳에서 일곱 번의 부활절과 다섯 번의 성탄절에 궁정을 열었다."], ["And once upon a time he held his court there at Whitsuntide.", "그리고 한번은 그곳에서 오순절에 궁정을 열었다."], ["For Caerleon was the place most easy of access in his dominions, both by sea and by land.", "카에르레온은 바다로든 육지로든 그의 영토 가운데 접근하기 가장 쉬운 곳이었기 때문이다."], ["And there were assembled nine crowned kings, who were his tributaries, and likewise earls and barons.", "그리고 그곳에는 그에게 조공을 바치던 아홉 명의 왕관 쓴 왕들이 모였고, 백작들과 남작들도 함께 모였다."], ["For they were his invited guests at all the high festivals, unless they were prevented by any great hinderance.", "그들은 큰 잔치마다 그의 초대를 받은 손님들이었으니, 어떤 큰 사정으로 가로막히지 않는 한 그러하였다."], ["And when he was at Caerleon holding his court, thirteen churches were set apart for mass.", "그리고 그가 카에르레온에서 궁정을 열 때에는 미사를 위해 열세 곳의 교회가 따로 배정되었다. 그 배정은 다음과 같았다."], ["And thus they were appointed: one church for Arthur and his kings, and his guests; and the second for Guenever and her ladies; and the third for the steward of the household and the suitors; and the fourth for the Franks and the other officers; and the other nine churches were for the nine masters of the household, and chiefly for Gawain, for he, from the eminence of his warlike fame, and from the nobleness of his birth, was the most exalted of the nine.", "첫 번째 교회는 아서와 그의 왕들, 그리고 그의 손님들을 위한 것이요, 두 번째는 귀네비어와 그 시녀들을 위한 것이요, 세 번째는 궁실의 집사장과 청원자들을 위한 것이요, 네 번째는 프랑크인들과 그 밖의 관리들을 위한 것이며, 나머지 아홉 교회는 궁실의 아홉 주관자들을 위한 것이되, 그 가운데서도 특히 가웨인을 위한 것이었다. 그는 무용의 명성이 드높고 출생이 고귀하여 그 아홉 가운데 가장 높이 떠받들어졌기 때문이다."], ["And there was no other arrangement respecting the churches than that which we have here mentioned.", "그리고 교회에 관해서는 여기 언급한 것 외에 다른 배정은 없었다."]], [["And on Whit-Tuesday, as the king sat at the banquet, lo, there entered a tall, fair-headed youth, clad in a coat and surcoat of satin, and a golden-hilted sword about his neck, and low shoes of leather upon his feet.", "그리고 오순절 화요일에 왕이 연회 자리에 앉아 있을 때, 보라, 키가 크고 머리가 고운 한 젊은이가 들어왔으니, 그는 공단으로 지은 외투와 겉옷을 입고, 황금 자루의 검을 목에 두르고, 발에는 낮은 가죽신을 신고 있었다."], ["And he came and stood before Arthur.", "그리고 그는 다가와 아서 앞에 섰다."], ["“Hail to thee, lord,”", "\"주군께 인사 올립니다.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Heaven prosper thee,”", "\"하늘이 그대를 번성케 하시기를.\""], ["he answered, “and be thou welcome.", "그가 대답했다. \"그리고 잘 왔노라."], ["Dost thou bring any new tidings?”", "무슨 새로운 소식을 가져왔느냐?\""], ["“I do, lord,” he said.", "\"그렇습니다, 주군.\""], ["“I am one of thy foresters, lord, in the forest of Dean, and my name is Madoc, son of Turgadarn.", "그가 말했다. \"저는 주군의 산지기 가운데 하나로, 딘 숲을 지키며, 제 이름은 마도크, 투르가다른의 아들입니다."], ["In the forest I saw a stag, the like of which beheld I never yet.”", "그 숲에서 제가 사슴 한 마리를 보았는데, 그런 것은 여태껏 본 적이 없었습니다.\""], ["“What is there about him,”", "\"그것이 어떠하기에,\""], ["asked Arthur, “that thou never yet didst see his like?”", "아서가 물었다. \"네가 여태껏 그런 것을 본 적이 없다는 말이냐?\""], ["“He is of pure white, lord, and he does not herd with any other animal, through stateliness and pride, so royal is his bearing.", "\"순백색이옵니다, 주군. 그리고 그 자태가 어찌나 위풍당당하고 거만한지, 그 거동이 참으로 왕다워, 다른 어떤 짐승과도 무리를 이루지 않습니다."], ["And I come to seek thy counsel, lord, and to know thy will concerning him.”", "그래서 저는 주군의 조언을 구하고, 그것에 관한 주군의 뜻을 알고자 왔습니다.\""], ["“It seems best to me,”", "\"내 생각에는,\""], ["said Arthur, “to go and hunt him to-morrow at break of day, and to cause general notice thereof to be given to-night, in all quarters of the court.”", "아서가 말했다. \"내일 동틀 무렵에 가서 그것을 사냥하고, 오늘 밤 궁정 곳곳에 그에 관한 통고를 두루 알리는 것이 가장 좋겠다.\""]], [["“For Arthur on the Whitsuntide before Held court at old Caerleon upon Usk.", "\"아서는 그전 오순절에 우스크 강가의 옛 카에르레온에서 궁정을 열었더라."], ["There on a day, he sitting high in hall, Before him came a forester of Dean, Wet from the woods, with notice of a hart Taller than all his fellows, milky-white, First seen that day: these things he told the king.", "그곳에서 어느 날, 그가 높이 홀에 앉아 있을 때, 그 앞에 딘의 산지기 하나가 와서, 숲에서 젖은 몸으로, 한 마리 수사슴의 소식을 전했으니, 제 동료들 모두보다 키가 크고, 젖빛으로 희며, 그날 처음 보였다는 것이라. 이것들을 그는 왕에게 아뢰었더라."], ["Then the good king gave order to let blow His horns for hunting on the morrow morn.”", "그러자 어진 왕은 명을 내려, 이튿날 아침 사냥을 위해 뿔나팔을 불게 하였더라.\""], ["—Enid.", "─「이니드」."]], [["And Arryfuerys was Arthur’s chief huntsman, and Arelivri his chief page.", "그리고 아리퓌에리스는 아서의 수석 사냥꾼이었고, 아렐리브리는 그의 수석 시동이었다."], ["And all received notice; and thus it was arranged.", "그리고 모두가 통고를 받았으니, 그렇게 일이 마련되었다."]], [["Then Guenever said to Arthur, “Wilt thou permit me, lord, to go to-morrow to see and hear the hunt of the stag of which the young man spoke?”", "그러자 귀네비어가 아서에게 말했다. \"주군, 내일 그 젊은이가 말한 사슴의 사냥을 보고 들으러 가는 것을 허락해 주시겠습니까?\""], ["“I will gladly,”", "\"기꺼이 허락하오.\""], ["said Arthur.", "아서가 말했다."], ["And Gawain said to Arthur, “Lord, if it seem well to thee, permit that into whose hunt soever the stag shall come, that one, be he a knight or one on foot, may cut off his head, and give it to whom he pleases, whether to his own lady-love, or to the lady of his friend.”", "그리고 가웨인이 아서에게 말했다. \"주군, 만일 좋으시다면, 그 사슴이 누구의 사냥에 들어오든, 그 사람이 기사이든 보행자이든, 그 머리를 베어 자기가 원하는 이에게, 곧 자기 연인이든 벗의 연인이든 줄 수 있도록 허락해 주십시오.\""], ["“I grant it gladly,”", "\"기꺼이 허락하노라.\""], ["said Arthur, “and let the steward of the household be chastised, if all things are not ready to-morrow for the chase.”", "아서가 말했다. \"그리고 만일 내일 사냥을 위해 모든 것이 준비되어 있지 않거든, 궁실의 집사장을 벌하도록 하라.\""]], [["And they passed the night with songs, and diversions, and discourse, and ample entertainment.", "그리고 그들은 노래와 여흥과 담소와 넉넉한 향연으로 밤을 보냈다."], ["And when it was time for them all to go to sleep, they went.", "그리고 모두가 잠자리에 들 때가 되자 그들은 갔다."], ["And when the next day came, they arose.", "그리고 이튿날이 오자 그들은 일어났다."], ["And Arthur called the attendants who guarded his couch.", "그리고 아서는 자기 침소를 지키던 시종들을 불렀다."], ["And there were four pages whose names were Cadyrnerth, the son of Gandwy, and Ambreu, the son of Bedwor and Amhar, the son of Arthur and Goreu, the son of Custennin.", "거기에는 네 명의 시동이 있었으니, 그 이름은 간드위의 아들 카디르네르스, 베드워의 아들 암브레우, 아서의 아들 암하르, 그리고 쿠스텐닌의 아들 고레우였다."], ["And these men came to Arthur and saluted him, and arrayed him in his garments.", "그리고 이 사람들이 아서에게 와서 인사를 올리고, 그에게 옷을 입혔다."], ["And Arthur wondered that Guenever did not awake, and the attendants wished to awaken her.", "그리고 아서는 귀네비어가 깨어나지 않는 것을 의아하게 여겼고, 시종들은 그녀를 깨우려 하였다. \"그녀를 깨우지 마라.\""], ["“Disturb her not,”", "아서가 말했다."], ["said Arthur, “for she had rather sleep than go to see the hunting.”", "\"사냥을 보러 가는 것보다 자는 편을 그녀가 더 바랄 테니라.\""]], [["Then Arthur went forth, and he heard two horns sounding, one from near the lodging of the chief huntsman, and the other from near that of the chief page.", "그러자 아서는 밖으로 나갔고, 두 개의 뿔나팔이 울리는 소리를 들었으니, 하나는 수석 사냥꾼의 숙소 가까이에서, 다른 하나는 수석 시동의 숙소 가까이에서 나는 것이었다."], ["And the whole assembly of the multitudes came to Arthur, and they took the road to the forest.", "그리고 무리의 온 모임이 아서에게로 와서, 그들은 숲으로 가는 길로 들어섰다."]], [["And after Arthur had gone forth from the palace, Guenever awoke, and called to her maidens, and apparalled herself.", "그리고 아서가 궁궐에서 나간 뒤에, 귀네비어가 깨어나, 자기 시녀들을 부르고, 몸치장을 했다. \"시녀들아,\""], ["“Maidens,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “I had leave last night to go and see the hunt.", "\"나는 지난밤에 사냥을 보러 가도 좋다는 허락을 받았다."], ["Go one of you to the stable, and order hither a horse such as a woman may ride.”", "너희 가운데 하나가 마구간으로 가서, 여자가 탈 만한 말을 이리로 가져오게 하여라.\" 그리고 그들 가운데 하나가 갔으나, 마구간에는 말이 두 마리밖에 없었다."], ["And one of them went, and she found but two horses in the stable; and Guenever and one of her maidens mounted them, and went through the Usk, and followed the track of the men and the horses.", "그래서 귀네비어와 그 시녀 가운데 하나가 그것들에 올라타, 우스크 강을 건너, 사람들과 말들의 자취를 따라갔다. 그리고 그들이 그렇게 말을 달리고 있을 때, 그들은 크고 세차게 다가오는 소리를 들었다."], ["And as they rode thus, they heard a loud and rushing sound; and they looked behind them, and beheld a knight upon a hunter foal of mighty size.", "그래서 그들이 뒤를 돌아보니, 거대한 몸집의 사냥용 망아지에 올라탄 기사 하나가 보였다. 그리고 그 기사는 머리가 고운 젊은이로, 다리를 드러내고, 기품 있는 자태였다."], ["And the rider was a fair-haired youth, bare-legged, and of princely mien; and a golden-hilted sword was at his side, and a robe and a surcoat of satin were upon him, and two low shoes of leather upon his feet; and around him was a scarf of blue purple, at each corner of which was a golden apple.", "그리고 황금 자루의 검이 그의 옆구리에 있었고, 공단으로 지은 겉옷과 외투를 입고, 발에는 낮은 가죽신 두 짝을 신고 있었다. 그리고 그를 둘러싸고 푸른 자줏빛 견대가 있었으니, 그 네 귀퉁이마다 황금 사과가 달려 있었다."]], [["“For Prince Geraint, Late also, wearing neither hunting-dress Nor weapon, save a golden-hilted brand, Came quickly flashing through the shallow ford.”", "\"이는 게라인트 공자였으니, 그 또한 늦게, 사냥 복장도 황금 자루의 칼 하나 외에는 무기도 걸치지 않은 채, 얕은 여울을 번쩍이며 재빨리 지나왔더라.\""], ["—Enid.", "─「이니드」."]], [["And his horse stepped stately, and swift, and proud; and he overtook Guenever, and saluted her.", "그리고 그의 말은 위엄 있고, 날래고, 당당하게 걸음을 옮겼다. 그리고 그는 귀네비어를 따라잡고, 그녀에게 인사를 올렸다."], ["“Heaven prosper thee, Geraint,”", "\"하늘이 그대를 번성케 하시기를, 게라인트여.\""], ["said she; “and why didst thou not go with thy lord to hunt?”", "그녀가 말했다. \"그런데 그대는 어찌하여 그대의 주군과 함께 사냥을 가지 않았소?\""], ["“Because I knew not when he went,”", "\"그분께서 언제 떠나셨는지 몰랐기 때문입니다.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“I marvel too,”", "\"나도 의아하구려.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “how he could go, unknown to me.", "\"그분께서 나도 모르게 어찌 떠나실 수 있었는지. 하지만 그대, 오 젊은이여, 그대는 온 왕국에서 내가 둘 수 있는 가장 흡족한 길동무로다."], ["But thou, O young man, art the most agreeable companion I could have in the whole kingdom; and it may be I shall be more amused with the hunting than they; for we shall hear the horns when they sound and we shall hear the dogs when they are let loose and begin to cry.”", "어쩌면 나는 그들보다도 사냥을 더 즐기게 될지도 모르오. 우리는 뿔나팔이 울릴 때 그 소리를 들을 것이고, 개들이 풀려나 짖기 시작할 때 그 소리를 들을 것이니 말이오.\""]], [["So they went to the edge of the forest, and there they stood.", "그리하여 그들은 숲의 가장자리로 가서, 그곳에 섰다."], ["“From this place,”", "\"이 자리에서,\""], ["said she, “we shall hear when the dogs are let loose.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"개들이 풀려나는 때를 우리는 들을 것이오.\""], ["And thereupon they heard a loud noise; and they looked towards the spot whence it came, and they beheld a dwarf riding upon a horse, stately and foaming and prancing and strong and spirited.", "그러자 그 순간 그들은 큰 소리를 들었다. 그래서 그들은 그 소리가 나는 쪽을 바라보았고, 한 난쟁이가 말 위에 올라탄 것을 보았으니, 그 말은 위엄 있고 거품을 물고 껑충거리며 힘차고 활기에 넘쳤다."], ["And in the hand of the dwarf was a whip.", "그리고 난쟁이의 손에는 채찍이 들려 있었다."], ["And near the dwarf they saw a lady upon a beautiful white horse, of steady and stately pace; and she was clothed in a garment of gold brocade.", "그리고 난쟁이 가까이에 그들은 아름다운 흰 말에 올라탄 귀부인을 보았으니, 그 말은 차분하고 위엄 있는 걸음걸이였다. 그리고 그녀는 금실로 짠 비단옷을 입고 있었다."], ["And near her was a knight upon a war-horse of large size, with heavy and bright armor both upon himself and upon his horse.", "그리고 그녀 가까이에는 큰 몸집의 군마에 올라탄 기사 하나가 있었으니, 그 자신에게도 말에게도 무겁고 번쩍이는 갑주를 둘렀다."], ["And truly they never before saw a knight, or a horse, or armor, of such remarkable size.", "그리고 참으로 그들은 그토록 놀라운 크기의 기사도, 말도, 갑주도 일찍이 본 적이 없었다."]], [["“Geraint,”", "\"게라인트여,\""], ["said Guenever, “knowest thou the name of that tall knight yonder?”", "귀네비어가 말했다. \"저기 저 키 큰 기사의 이름을 그대는 아오?\""], ["“I know him not,”", "\"저는 그를 모릅니다.\""], ["said he, “and the strange armor that he wears prevents my either seeing his face or his features.”", "그가 말했다. \"그리고 그가 두른 낯선 갑주 때문에 저는 그의 얼굴도 이목구비도 볼 수가 없습니다.\""], ["“Go, maiden,”", "\"가거라, 시녀야.\""], ["said Guenever, “and ask the dwarf who that knight is.”", "귀네비어가 말했다. \"그리고 저 난쟁이에게 저 기사가 누구인지 물어보아라.\""], ["Then the maiden went up to the dwarf; and she inquired of the dwarf who the knight was.", "그러자 시녀가 난쟁이에게로 다가갔다. 그리고 그녀는 난쟁이에게 그 기사가 누구인지 물었다."], ["“I will not tell thee,”", "\"네게 말하지 않겠다.\""], ["he answered.", "그가 대답했다."], ["“Since thou art so churlish,”", "\"네가 그토록 무례하니,\""], ["said she, “I will ask him, himself.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"내가 직접 그분께 여쭙겠다.\""], ["“Thou shalt not ask him, by my faith,”", "\"내 믿음을 걸고, 너는 그분께 묻지 못한다.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Wherefore not?” said she.", "\"어찌하여 안 된다는 것이냐?\""], ["“Because thou art not of honor sufficient to befit thee to speak to my lord.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"네가 내 주군께 말을 걸기에 합당할 만큼 신분이 높지 못하기 때문이다.\""], ["Then the maiden turned her horse’s head towards the knight, upon which the dwarf struck her with the whip that was in his hand across the face and the eyes, so that the blood flowed forth.", "그러자 시녀가 말 머리를 기사 쪽으로 돌렸고, 그러자 난쟁이가 손에 든 채찍으로 그녀의 얼굴과 두 눈을 가로질러 후려쳐서, 피가 흘러나왔다."], ["And the maiden returned to Guenever, complaining of the hurt she had received.", "그리고 시녀는 자기가 입은 상처를 호소하며 귀네비어에게 돌아왔다."], ["“Very rudely has the dwarf treated thee,”", "\"저 난쟁이가 그대를 참으로 무례하게 다루었구나.\""], ["said Geraint, and he put his hand upon the hilt of his sword.", "게라인트가 말하며, 자기 검의 자루에 손을 얹었다."], ["But he took counsel with himself, and considered that it would be no vengeance for him to slay the dwarf, and to be attacked unarmed by the armed knight; so he refrained.", "그러나 그는 스스로 헤아려 보고, 그 난쟁이를 죽인다 한들 그것은 자기에게 복수가 되지 못하고, 갑주를 두르지 않은 채 갑주를 두른 기사에게 공격당할 뿐임을 생각하였다. 그래서 그는 자제하였다."]], [["“Lady,”", "\"부인,\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “I will follow him, with thy permission, and at last he will come to some inhabited place, where I may have arms, either as a loan or for a pledge, so that I may encounter the knight.”", "\"허락하신다면 제가 그를 따라가겠습니다. 그러면 끝내 그가 어떤 사람이 사는 곳에 이를 것이고, 거기서 저는 빌리든 저당을 잡히든 무기를 얻어, 저 기사와 맞설 수 있을 것입니다.\""], ["“Go,”", "\"가시오.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “and do not attack him until thou hast good arms; and I shall be very anxious concerning thee, until I hear tidings of thee.”", "\"그리고 좋은 무기를 얻기 전에는 그를 공격하지 마시오. 그대의 소식을 들을 때까지 나는 그대 때문에 몹시 마음 졸일 것이오.\""], ["“If I am alive,”", "\"제가 살아 있다면,\""], ["said he, “thou shalt hear tidings of me by to-morrow afternoon;”", "그가 말했다. \"내일 오후까지 제 소식을 들으실 것입니다.\""], ["and with that he departed.", "그리고 그 말과 함께 그는 떠났다."]], [["And the road they took was below the palace of Caerleon, and across the ford of the Usk; and they went along a fair and even and lofty ridge of ground, until they came to a town, and at the extremity of the town they saw a fortress and a castle.", "그리고 그들이 택한 길은 카에르레온 궁궐 아래로, 우스크 강의 여울을 건너는 길이었다. 그리고 그들은 곱고 평탄하며 높다란 능선을 따라가다가 한 고을에 이르렀고, 고을 끝자락에서 그들은 요새와 성을 보았다."], ["And as the knight passed through the town all the people arose and saluted him, and bade him welcome.", "그리고 기사가 고을을 지날 때 모든 사람이 일어나 그에게 인사하며 환영하였다."], ["And when Geraint came into the town, he looked at every house to see if he knew any of those whom he saw.", "그리고 게라인트가 고을에 들어섰을 때, 그는 자기가 보는 이들 가운데 아는 사람이 있는지 보려고 집집마다 살폈다."], ["But he knew none, and none knew him, to do him the kindness to let him have arms, either as a loan or for a pledge.", "그러나 그는 아무도 알지 못했고, 아무도 그를 알지 못해, 빌려서든 저당으로든 그에게 무기를 얻게 해 주는 호의를 베풀 사람이 없었다."], ["And every house he saw was full of men, and arms, and horses.", "그리고 그가 본 집마다 사람들과 무기들과 말들로 가득했다."], ["And they were polishing shields, and burnishing swords, and washing armor, and shoeing horses.", "그리고 그들은 방패를 닦고, 검을 갈고, 갑주를 씻고, 말에 편자를 박고 있었다."], ["And the knight and the lady and the dwarf rode up to the castle, that was in the town, and every one was glad in the castle.", "그리고 기사와 귀부인과 난쟁이는 고을 안에 있는 성으로 말을 타고 올라갔고, 성 안의 모두가 기뻐하였다."], ["And from the battlements and the gates they risked their necks, through their eagerness to greet them, and to show their joy.", "그리고 흉벽과 성문에서 사람들은 그들을 맞이하고 자기들의 기쁨을 보이려는 열망에 목을 위태롭게 내밀었다."]], [["Geraint stood there to see whether the knight would remain in the castle; and when he was certain that he would do so, he looked around him.", "게라인트는 그 기사가 성에 머무를지 보려고 그곳에 섰다. 그리고 그가 그러하리라고 확신했을 때, 그는 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["And at a little distance from the town he saw an old palace in ruins, wherein was a hall that was falling to decay.", "그리고 고을에서 조금 떨어진 곳에 그는 폐허가 된 옛 궁궐을 보았으니, 그 안에는 무너져 가는 홀이 하나 있었다."]], [["“And high above a piece of turret-stair, Worn by the feet that now were silent, wound Bare to the sun.”", "\"그리고 높이, 한 자락 망루 계단이, 이제는 잠잠해진 발길들에 닳은 채, 햇빛 아래 헐벗고 굽이쳐 올랐더라.\""], ["—Enid.", "─「이니드」."]], [["And as he knew not any one in the town, he went towards the old palace.", "그리고 그는 고을에서 아무도 알지 못했으므로, 옛 궁궐 쪽으로 갔다."], ["And when he came near to the palace, he saw a hoary-headed man, standing by it, in tattered garments.", "그리고 그가 궁궐 가까이에 이르렀을 때, 그는 백발의 노인 하나가 누더기 옷을 입고 그 곁에 서 있는 것을 보았다."], ["And Geraint gazed steadfastly upon him.", "그리고 게라인트는 그를 한결같이 응시하였다."], ["Then the hoary-headed man said to him, “Young man, wherefore art thou thoughtful?”", "그러자 백발의 노인이 그에게 말했다. \"젊은이여, 어찌하여 그대는 수심에 잠겨 있는가?\""], ["“I am thoughtful,”", "\"제가 수심에 잠긴 것은,\""], ["said he, “because I know not where to pass the night.”", "그가 말했다. \"오늘 밤을 어디서 보낼지 알지 못하기 때문입니다.\""], ["“Wilt thou come forward this way, chieftain,”", "\"이리로 나아오겠는가, 무사여.\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “and thou shalt have of the best that can be procured for thee.”", "\"그러면 그대를 위해 마련할 수 있는 가장 좋은 것을 그대가 얻으리라.\""], ["So Geraint went forward.", "그리하여 게라인트는 앞으로 나아갔다."], ["And the hoary-headed man led the way into the hall.", "그리고 백발의 노인이 홀로 가는 길을 인도하였다."], ["And in the hall he dismounted, and he left there his horse.", "그리고 홀에서 그는 말에서 내려, 그곳에 자기 말을 두었다."], ["Then he went on to the upper chamber with the hoary-headed man.", "그러고 나서 그는 백발의 노인과 함께 위층 방으로 올라갔다."], ["And in the chamber he beheld an old woman, sitting on a cushion, with old, worn-out garments upon her; yet it seemed to him that she must have been comely when in the bloom of youth.", "그리고 그 방에서 그는 한 늙은 여인이 방석 위에 앉아 있는 것을 보았으니, 그 여인에게는 낡고 해진 옷이 걸쳐져 있었다. 하지만 그가 보기에 그녀는 젊음이 한창이던 때에는 고왔으리라 싶었다."], ["And beside her was a maiden, upon whom were a vest and a veil that were old and beginning to be worn out.", "그리고 그녀 곁에는 한 처녀가 있었으니, 그녀에게는 낡고 해지기 시작한 윗옷과 너울이 걸쳐져 있었다."], ["And truly he never saw a maiden more full of comeliness and grace and beauty than she.", "그리고 참으로 그는 그녀보다 더 고움과 우아함과 아름다움이 가득한 처녀를 본 적이 없었다."], ["And the hoary-headed man said to the maiden, “There is no attendant for the horse of this youth but thyself.”", "그리고 백발의 노인이 처녀에게 말했다. \"이 젊은이의 말을 돌볼 시종은 너 말고는 없구나.\""], ["“I will render the best service I am able,”", "\"제가 할 수 있는 가장 좋은 시중을 들겠습니다.\""], ["said she, “both to him and to his horse.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"그분에게도 그분의 말에게도요.\""], ["And the maiden disarrayed the youth, and then she furnished his horse with straw and corn; and then she returned to the chamber.", "그리고 처녀는 젊은이의 무장을 풀어 준 뒤, 그의 말에게 짚과 곡식을 주었다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 방으로 돌아왔다."], ["And the hoary-headed man said to the maiden, “Go to the town and bring hither the best that thou canst find, both of food and of liquor.”", "그리고 백발의 노인이 처녀에게 말했다. \"고을로 가서, 음식이든 음료든 네가 찾을 수 있는 가장 좋은 것을 이리로 가져오너라.\""], ["“I will gladly, lord,” said she.", "\"기꺼이 그리하겠습니다, 아버님.\""], ["And to the town went the maiden.", "그녀가 말했다. 그리고 처녀는 고을로 갔다."], ["And they conversed together while the maiden was at the town.", "그리고 처녀가 고을에 가 있는 동안 그들은 함께 이야기를 나누었다."], ["And, behold, the maiden came back, and a youth with her, bearing on his back a costrel full of good purchased mead, and a quarter of a young bullock.", "그리고, 보라, 처녀가 한 젊은이와 함께 돌아왔으니, 그 젊은이는 등에 좋은 벌꿀술이 가득 든 가죽 술병과 어린 황소의 사분의 일을 지고 있었다."], ["And in the hands of the maiden was a quantity of white bread, and she had some manchet bread in her veil, and she came into the chamber.", "그리고 처녀의 손에는 흰 빵이 한 아름 들려 있었고, 너울 속에는 고운 빵이 약간 있었으며, 그녀는 방으로 들어왔다."], ["“I would not obtain better than this,”", "\"이보다 더 좋은 것을 얻지는 못했을 것입니다.\""], ["said she, “nor with better should I have been trusted.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"그리고 더 좋은 것이라면 제게 맡겨 주지도 않았을 것입니다.\""], ["“It is good enough,”", "\"이만하면 충분하오.\""], ["said Geraint.", "게라인트가 말했다."], ["And they caused the meat to be boiled; and when their food was ready, they sat down.", "그리고 그들은 고기를 삶게 하였다. 그리고 음식이 준비되자 그들은 자리에 앉았다."], ["And it was in this wise.", "그리고 그 자리는 이러하였다."], ["Geraint sat between the hoary-headed man and his wife, and the maiden served them.", "게라인트는 백발의 노인과 그 아내 사이에 앉았고, 처녀가 그들의 시중을 들었다."], ["And they ate and drank.", "그리고 그들은 먹고 마셨다."]], [["And when they had finished eating, Geraint talked with the hoary-headed man, and he asked him in the first place to whom belonged the palace that he was in.", "그리고 그들이 식사를 마쳤을 때, 게라인트는 백발의 노인과 이야기를 나누었고, 우선 그는 자기가 있는 이 궁궐이 누구의 것이었는지 물었다."], ["“Truly,”", "\"참으로,\""], ["said he, “it was I that built it, and to me also belonged the city and the castle which thou sawest.”", "그가 말했다. \"그것을 지은 것은 바로 나였고, 그대가 본 도시와 성도 역시 내 것이었소.\""], ["“Alas!”", "\"아아!\""], ["said Geraint, “how is it that thou hast lost them now?”", "게라인트가 말했다. \"어찌하여 그대는 이제 그것들을 잃으셨습니까?\""], ["“I lost a great earldom as well as these,”", "\"나는 이것들과 더불어 큰 백작령도 잃었소.\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “and this is how I lost them.", "\"그리고 내가 그것들을 잃은 사연은 이러하오. 나에게는 조카가 있었으니, 내 아우의 아들이었소."], ["I had a nephew, the son of my brother, and I took care of his possessions; but he was impatient to enter upon them, so he made war upon me, and wrested from me not only his own, but also my estates, except this castle.”", "그래서 나는 그의 재산을 돌보아 주었소. 그러나 그가 그것을 차지하려고 안달하더니, 나에게 전쟁을 걸어, 제 것뿐 아니라 이 성을 제외한 내 영지까지도 내게서 빼앗아 갔소.\""], ["“Good sir,”", "\"선하신 어르신,\""], ["said Geraint, “wilt thou tell me wherefore came the knight and the lady and the dwarf just now into the town, and what is the preparation which I saw, and the putting of arms in order?”", "게라인트가 말했다. \"방금 그 기사와 귀부인과 난쟁이가 어찌하여 고을에 들어왔는지, 그리고 제가 본 그 준비와 무기를 정돈하는 일이 무엇인지 말씀해 주시겠습니까?\""], ["“I will do so,”", "\"그리하겠소.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“The preparations are for the game that is to be held to-morrow by the young earl, which will be on this wise.", "\"그 준비는 내일 젊은 백작이 열 시합을 위한 것이오. 그것은 이러하오."], ["In the midst of a meadow which is here, two forks will be set up, and upon the two forks a silver rod, and upon the silver rod a sparrow-hawk, and for the sparrow-hawk there will be a tournament.", "여기 있는 풀밭 한가운데에 두 개의 갈래 기둥을 세우고, 그 두 갈래 기둥 위에 은 막대를 얹고, 그 은 막대 위에 새매를 두어, 그 새매를 두고 마상 시합이 벌어질 것이오."], ["And to the tournament will go all the array thou didst see in the city, of men and of horses and of arms.", "그리고 그 시합에는 그대가 도시에서 본 모든 무리, 곧 사람들과 말들과 무기들이 나아갈 것이오."], ["And with each man will go the lady he loves best; and no man can joust for the sparrow-hawk, except the lady he loves best be with him.", "그리고 사람마다 자기가 가장 사랑하는 귀부인과 함께 나아갈 것이오. 그리고 자기가 가장 사랑하는 귀부인이 곁에 있지 않고서는 아무도 그 새매를 두고 겨룰 수 없소."], ["And the knight that thou sawest has gained the sparrow-hawk these two years; and if he gains it the third year, he will be called the Knight of the Sparrow-hawk from that time forth.”", "그리고 그대가 본 그 기사는 지난 두 해 동안 그 새매를 차지하였소. 그리고 만일 그가 세 번째 해에도 그것을 차지한다면, 그때부터 그는 새매의 기사라 불릴 것이오.\""], ["“Sir,”", "\"어르신,\""], ["said Geraint, “what is thy counsel to me concerning this knight, on account of the insult which the maiden of Guenever received from the dwarf?”", "게라인트가 말했다. \"귀네비어의 시녀가 그 난쟁이에게서 받은 모욕을 두고, 이 기사에 관하여 제게 주실 조언은 무엇입니까?\""], ["And Geraint told the hoary-headed man what the insult was that the maiden had received.", "그리고 게라인트는 백발의 노인에게 그 시녀가 받은 모욕이 무엇이었는지 이야기하였다."], ["“It is not easy to counsel thee, inasmuch as thou hast neither dame nor maiden belonging to thee, for whom thou canst joust.", "\"그대에게 조언하기란 쉽지 않소. 그대에게는 그대를 위해 겨룰 부인도 처녀도 없으니 말이오."], ["Yet I have arms here, which thou couldst have, and there is my horse also, if he seem to thee better than thine own.” “Ah, sir,”", "그렇지만 여기 무기가 있으니 그대가 가질 수 있고, 또한 내 말도 있으니, 만일 그것이 그대 자신의 말보다 나아 보인다면 가지시오.\""], ["said he, “Heaven reward thee!", "\"아, 어르신,\" 그가 말했다."], ["But my own horse to which I am accustomed, together with thine arms, will suffice me.", "\"하늘이 그대에게 갚아 주시기를! 그러나 제가 익숙한 제 말과 더불어 어르신의 무기면 제게 충분합니다."], ["And if, when the appointed time shall come to-morrow thou wilt permit me, sir, to challenge for yonder maiden that is thy daughter, I will engage, if I escape from the tournament, to love the maiden as long as I live.”", "그리고 만일 내일 정해진 때가 오면, 어르신, 어르신의 따님이신 저 처녀를 위해 도전하도록 제게 허락해 주신다면, 저는 만일 그 시합에서 살아남는다면 평생토록 그 처녀를 사랑하기로 맹세하겠습니다.\""], ["“Gladly will I permit thee,”", "\"기꺼이 그대에게 허락하겠소.\""], ["said the hoary-headed man; “and since thou dost thus resolve, it is necessary that thy horse and arms should be ready to-morrow at break of day.", "백발의 노인이 말했다. \"그리고 그대가 그렇게 결심하니, 그대의 말과 무기가 내일 동틀 무렵 준비되어 있어야 하오."], ["For then the Knight of the Sparrow-hawk will make proclamation, and ask the lady he loves best to take the sparrow-hawk; and if any deny it to her, by force will he defend her claim.", "그때 새매의 기사가 선포를 하고, 자기가 가장 사랑하는 귀부인에게 새매를 가지라 할 것이기 때문이오. 그리고 만일 누군가 그녀에게 그것을 거절한다면, 그는 힘으로 그녀의 주장을 지킬 것이오."], ["And therefore,”", "그러므로,\""], ["said the hoary-headed man, “it is needful for thee to be there at daybreak, and we three will be with thee.”", "백발의 노인이 말했다. \"그대는 동틀 녘에 그곳에 있어야 하고, 우리 세 사람이 그대와 함께 있을 것이오.\""], ["And thus was it settled.", "그리하여 일이 그렇게 정해졌다."]], [["And at night they went to sleep.", "그리고 밤이 되자 그들은 잠자리에 들었다."], ["And before the dawn they arose and arrayed themselves; and by the time that it was day, they were all four in the meadow.", "그리고 동이 트기 전에 그들은 일어나 몸치장을 했다. 그리고 날이 밝을 무렵에는 네 사람 모두 풀밭에 나가 있었다."], ["And there was the Knight of the Sparrow-hawk making the proclamation, and asking his lady-love to take the sparrow-hawk.", "그리고 그곳에 새매의 기사가 선포를 하며, 자기 연인에게 새매를 가지라 하고 있었다. \"그것을 갖지 마시오.\""], ["“Take it not,”", "게라인트가 말했다."], ["said Geraint, “for here is a maiden who is fairer, and more noble, and more comely, and who has a better claim to it than thou.”", "\"여기 더 곱고, 더 고귀하고, 더 어여쁘며, 그대보다 그것에 대한 권리가 더 나은 처녀가 있기 때문이오.\""], ["Then said the knight, “If thou maintainest the sparrow-hawk to be due to her, come forward and do battle with me.”", "그러자 기사가 말했다. \"만일 그대가 그 새매가 그녀에게 마땅하다고 주장한다면, 앞으로 나와 나와 싸움을 벌이라.\""], ["And Geraint went forward to the top of the meadow, having upon himself and upon his horse armor which was heavy and rusty, and of uncouth shape.", "그리고 게라인트는 풀밭 위쪽으로 나아갔으니, 그 자신에게도 그의 말에게도 무겁고 녹슬고 볼품없는 모양의 갑주를 두르고 있었다."], ["Then they encountered each other, and they broke a set of lances; and they broke a second set, and a third.", "그러자 그들은 서로 맞부딪쳤고, 한 묶음의 창을 부러뜨렸다. 그리고 그들은 두 번째 묶음을, 또 세 번째 묶음을 부러뜨렸다."], ["And when the earl and his company saw the Knight of the Sparrow-hawk gaining the mastery, there was shouting and joy and mirth amongst them; and the hoary-headed man and his wife and his daughter were sorrowful.", "그리고 백작과 그 일행이 새매의 기사가 우세를 점하는 것을 보자, 그들 사이에서는 함성과 환희와 흥겨움이 일었다. 그러나 백발의 노인과 그 아내와 그 딸은 슬퍼하였다."], ["And the hoary-headed man served Geraint with lances as often as he broke them, and the dwarf served the Knight of the Sparrow-hawk.", "그리고 백발의 노인은 게라인트가 창을 부러뜨릴 때마다 창을 대어 주었고, 난쟁이는 새매의 기사에게 창을 대어 주었다. 그러자 백발의 노인이 게라인트에게 말했다."], ["Then the hoary-headed man said to Geraint, “O chieftain, since no other will hold with thee, behold, here is the lance which was in my hand on the day when I received the honor of knighthood, and from that time to this I never broke it, and it has an excellent point.”", "\"오 무사여, 다른 누구도 그대를 거들지 않으니, 보시오, 여기 내가 기사 작위의 명예를 받던 날 내 손에 들려 있던 창이 있소. 그리고 그때부터 지금까지 나는 그것을 한 번도 부러뜨린 적이 없으며, 그것은 뛰어난 창끝을 지니고 있소.\""], ["Then Geraint took the lance, thanking the hoary-headed man.", "그러자 게라인트는 백발의 노인에게 감사하며 그 창을 받았다."], ["And thereupon the dwarf also brought a lance to his lord.", "그리고 그 순간 난쟁이 또한 자기 주군에게 창을 가져왔다."], ["“Behold, here is a lance for thee, not less good than his,”", "\"보십시오, 여기 그의 것에 못지않게 좋은 창이 있습니다.\""], ["said the dwarf.", "난쟁이가 말했다."], ["“And bethink thee that no knight ever withstood thee so long as this one has done.”", "\"그리고 어떤 기사도 이자만큼 오래 주군께 버틴 적이 없음을 헤아리소서.\" \"내가 하늘을 두고 선언하건대,\""], ["“I declare to Heaven,”", "게라인트가 말했다."], ["said Geraint, “that unless death takes me quickly hence, he shall fare never the better for thy service.”", "\"죽음이 나를 이곳에서 속히 데려가지 않는 한, 저자는 네 시중으로 결코 더 나아지지 못하리라.\""], ["And Geraint pricked his horse towards him from afar, and, warning him, he rushed upon him, and gave him a blow so severe, and furious, and fierce, upon the face of his shield, that he cleft it in two, and broke his armor, and burst his girths, so that both he and his saddle were borne to the ground over the horse’s crupper.", "그리고 게라인트는 멀리서 자기 말을 그에게로 박차며, 그에게 경고를 하고 나서, 그에게 돌진하여, 방패 면에 어찌나 모질고 사납고 거센 일격을 가했던지, 그것을 둘로 쪼개고, 그의 갑주를 부수고, 안장끈을 끊어, 그와 그의 안장이 둘 다 말의 엉덩이를 넘어 땅바닥에 나가떨어졌다."], ["And Geraint dismounted quickly.", "그리고 게라인트는 재빨리 말에서 내렸다."], ["And he was wroth, and he drew his sword, and rushed fiercely upon him.", "그리고 그는 노하여, 검을 뽑아, 그에게 사납게 돌진하였다."], ["Then the knight also arose, and drew his sword against Geraint.", "그러자 기사 또한 일어나, 게라인트를 향해 검을 뽑았다."], ["And they fought on foot with their swords until their arms struck sparks of fire like stars from one another; and thus they continued fighting until the blood and sweat obscured the light from their eyes.", "그리고 그들은 서로의 검에서 별과 같은 불꽃이 튈 때까지 발을 디딘 채 검으로 싸웠다. 그리고 피와 땀이 그들의 눈에서 빛을 가릴 때까지 그렇게 싸움을 이어 갔다."], ["At length Geraint called to him all his strength, and struck the knight upon the crown of his head, so that he broke all his head-armor, and cut through all the flesh and the skin, even to the skull, until he wounded the bone.", "마침내 게라인트는 자기 온 힘을 불러일으켜, 그 기사의 정수리를 내리쳐, 그의 머리 갑주를 모조리 부수고, 살과 살갗을 두개골에 이르도록 모조리 베어, 끝내 뼈까지 다치게 하였다."]], [["Then the knight fell upon his knees, and cast his sword from his hand, and besought mercy from Geraint.", "그러자 기사는 무릎을 꿇고, 손에서 검을 내던지고, 게라인트에게 자비를 빌었다. \"참으로,\""], ["“Of a truth,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “I relinquish my overdaring and my pride, and crave thy mercy; and unless I have time to commit myself to Heaven for my sins, and to talk with a priest, thy mercy will avail me little.”", "\"나는 나의 지나친 만용과 나의 교만을 버리고, 그대의 자비를 구하오. 그리고 만일 내 죄를 위해 하늘에 나를 의탁하고 사제와 이야기할 시간을 얻지 못한다면, 그대의 자비는 내게 별 소용이 없을 것이오.\""], ["“I will grant thee grace upon this condition,”", "\"이 조건으로 그대에게 은혜를 베풀겠노라.\""], ["said Geraint, “that thou go to Guenever, the wife of Arthur, to do her satisfaction for the insult which her maiden received from thy dwarf.", "게라인트가 말했다. \"곧 그대가 아서의 아내 귀네비어에게 가서, 그대의 난쟁이에게서 그녀의 시녀가 받은 모욕에 대해 그녀에게 보상하는 것이오."], ["Dismount not from the time thou goest hence until thou comest into the presence of Guenever, to make her what atonement shall be adjudged at the court of Arthur.”", "그대는 이곳을 떠나는 때부터 귀네비어 앞에 이를 때까지 말에서 내리지 말고, 그녀에게 아서의 궁정에서 판결될 바대로 속죄하라.\""], ["“This will I do gladly; and who art thou?”", "\"이를 기꺼이 행하겠소. 그런데 그대는 누구요?\""], ["“I am Geraint, the son of Erbin; and declare thou also who thou art.”", "\"나는 에르빈의 아들 게라인트요. 그대도 그대가 누구인지 밝히라.\""], ["“I am Edeyrn, the son of Nudd.”", "\"나는 누드의 아들 에데이른이오.\""], ["Then he threw himself upon his horse, and went forward to Arthur’s court; and the lady he loved best went before him, and the dwarf, with much lamentation.", "그러고 나서 그는 자기 말에 몸을 던져, 아서의 궁정으로 나아갔다. 그리고 그가 가장 사랑하는 귀부인이 그의 앞에 갔고, 난쟁이도 크게 탄식하며 갔다."]], [["Then came the young earl and his hosts to Geraint, and saluted him, and bade him to his castle.", "그러자 젊은 백작과 그 무리가 게라인트에게로 와서, 그에게 인사를 올리고, 자기 성으로 그를 청하였다."], ["“I may not go,”", "\"나는 갈 수 없소.\""], ["said Geraint; “but where I was last night, there will I be to-night also.”", "게라인트가 말했다. \"지난밤에 내가 있던 곳, 그곳에 오늘 밤에도 있겠소.\""], ["“Since thou wilt none of my inviting, thou shalt have abundance of all that I can command for thee; and I will order ointment for thee, to recover thee from thy fatigues, and from the weariness that is upon thee.”", "\"그대가 내 청을 받아들이지 않으니, 내가 그대를 위해 마련할 수 있는 모든 것을 넉넉히 받으시오. 그리고 그대의 피로와 그대에게 깃든 노곤함에서 그대를 회복시킬 연고를 그대를 위해 마련하라 이르겠소.\""], ["“Heaven reward thee,”", "\"하늘이 그대에게 갚아 주시기를.\""], ["said Geraint, “and I will go to my lodging.”", "게라인트가 말했다. \"그러면 내 숙소로 가겠소.\""], ["And thus went Geraint and Earl Ynywl, and his wife and his daughter.", "그리하여 게라인트와 이니윌 백작과 그 아내와 그 딸이 갔다."], ["And when they reached the old mansion, the household servants and attendants of the young earl had arrived, and had arranged all the apartments, dressing them with straw and with fire; and in a short time the ointment was ready, and Geraint came there, and they washed his head.", "그리고 그들이 그 옛 저택에 이르렀을 때, 젊은 백작의 집안 하인들과 시종들이 도착하여 있었고, 모든 방을 정돈하여 짚을 깔고 불을 지펴 단장해 두었다. 그리고 머지않아 연고가 준비되었고, 게라인트가 그곳에 와서, 그들은 그의 머리를 씻겼다."], ["Then came the young earl, with forty honorable knights from among his attendants, and those who were bidden to the tournament.", "그러자 젊은 백작이 자기 시종들 가운데 마흔 명의 명예로운 기사와, 그 시합에 청해진 자들과 함께 왔다."], ["And Geraint came from the anointing.", "그리고 게라인트가 연고를 바르는 일에서 돌아왔다."], ["And the earl asked him to go to the hall to eat.", "그리고 백작은 그에게 홀로 가서 식사하기를 청하였다."], ["“Where is the Earl Ynywl,”", "\"이니윌 백작은 어디 있소,\""], ["said Geraint, “and his wife and his daughter?”", "게라인트가 말했다. \"그리고 그 아내와 그 딸은 어디 있소?\""], ["“They are in the chamber yonder,”", "\"저쪽 방에 있습니다.\""], ["said the earl’s chamberlain, “arraying themselves in garments which the earl has caused to be brought for them.”", "백작의 시종장이 말했다. \"백작께서 그들을 위해 가져오게 하신 옷으로 몸치장을 하고 있습니다.\""], ["“Let not the damsel array herself,”", "\"그 처녀로 하여금 몸치장을 하지 못하게 하시오.\""], ["said he, “except in her vest and her veil, until she come to the court of Arthur, to be clad by Guenever in such garments as she may choose.”", "그가 말했다. \"오직 그녀의 윗옷과 너울만 입은 채로, 아서의 궁정에 이르러 귀네비어에게서 자기가 고르는 그런 옷을 입게 될 때까지 그러하시오.\""], ["So the maiden did not array herself.", "그리하여 그 처녀는 몸치장을 하지 않았다."]], [["Then they all entered the hall, and they washed, and sat down to meat.", "그러자 그들 모두 홀로 들어가, 손을 씻고, 식사 자리에 앉았다."], ["And thus were they seated.", "그리고 그들은 이렇게 자리하였다."], ["On one side of Geraint sat the young earl, and Earl Ynywl beyond him, and on the other side of Geraint was the maiden and her mother.", "게라인트의 한쪽에는 젊은 백작이 앉고, 그 너머에 이니윌 백작이 앉았으며, 게라인트의 다른 쪽에는 처녀와 그 어머니가 앉았다."], ["And after these all sat according to their precedence in honor.", "그리고 이들 다음에는 모두 명예의 차례에 따라 앉았다."], ["And they ate.", "그리고 그들은 먹었다."], ["And they were served abundantly, and they received a profusion of divers kinds of gifts.", "그리고 그들은 넉넉히 시중을 받았으며, 갖가지 종류의 선물을 풍성하게 받았다."], ["Then they conversed together.", "그러고 나서 그들은 함께 이야기를 나누었다."], ["And the young earl invited Geraint to visit him next day.", "그리고 젊은 백작은 게라인트에게 이튿날 자기를 방문해 달라고 청하였다."], ["“I will not, by Heaven,” said Geraint.", "\"하늘을 두고, 나는 그러지 않겠소.\""], ["“To the court of Arthur will I go with this maiden to-morrow.", "게라인트가 말했다. \"내일 나는 이 처녀와 함께 아서의 궁정으로 가겠소."], ["And it is enough for me, as long as Earl Ynywl is in poverty and trouble; and I go chiefly to seek to add to his maintenance.”", "그리고 이니윌 백작이 가난과 곤경에 처해 있는 한, 내게는 그것으로 충분하오. 그리고 나는 무엇보다 그의 생계를 보태고자 가는 것이오.\""], ["“Ah, chieftain,”", "\"아, 무사여,\""], ["said the young earl, “it is not by my fault that Earl Ynywl is without his possessions.”", "젊은 백작이 말했다. \"이니윌 백작이 자기 재산을 잃은 것은 내 잘못 때문이 아니오.\""], ["“By my faith,”", "\"내 믿음을 걸고,\""], ["said Geraint, “he shall not remain without them, unless death quickly takes me hence.”", "게라인트가 말했다. \"죽음이 나를 이곳에서 속히 데려가지 않는 한, 그는 그것 없이 지내지 않을 것이오.\""], ["“O chieftain,”", "\"오 무사여,\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “with regard to the disagreement between me and Ynywl, I will gladly abide by thy counsel, and agree to what thou mayest judge right between us.”", "\"나와 이니윌 사이의 불화에 관해서라면, 나는 기꺼이 그대의 조언에 따르고, 우리 사이에서 그대가 옳다고 판단하는 바에 동의하겠소.\" \"내가 그대에게 청하는 것은 다만,\""], ["“I but ask thee,”", "게라인트가 말했다."], ["said Geraint, “to restore to him what is his, and what he should have received from the time he lost his possessions even until this day.”", "\"그의 것을 그에게 되돌려 주는 것이오. 그리고 그가 자기 재산을 잃은 때부터 바로 오늘까지 받았어야 할 것을 되돌려 주는 것이오.\""], ["“That will I do, gladly, for thee,”", "\"그것을 그대를 위해 기꺼이 행하겠소.\""], ["answered he.", "그가 대답했다."], ["“Then,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["said Geraint, “whosoever is here who owes homage to Ynywl, let him come forward, and perform it on the spot.”", "게라인트가 말했다. \"여기 이니윌에게 신종(臣從)의 의무를 진 자가 누구든, 앞으로 나와, 그 자리에서 그것을 이행하라.\""], ["And all the men did so; and by that treaty they abided.", "그리고 모든 사람이 그렇게 하였다. 그리고 그 약정에 따라 그들은 그것을 지켰다."], ["And his castle and his town, and all his possessions, were restored to Ynywl.", "그리고 그의 성과 그의 고을과 그의 모든 재산이 이니윌에게 되돌려졌다."], ["And he received back all that he had lost, even to the smallest jewel.", "그리고 그는 자기가 잃었던 모든 것을, 가장 작은 보석에 이르기까지 되돌려 받았다."]], [["Then spoke Earl Ynywl to Geraint.", "그러자 이니윌 백작이 게라인트에게 말했다. \"무사여,\""], ["“Chieftain,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “behold the maiden for whom thou didst challenge at the tournament; I bestow her upon thee.”", "\"보시오, 그대가 시합에서 도전하며 위했던 그 처녀를. 내가 그녀를 그대에게 주노라.\""], ["“She shall go with me,”", "\"그녀는 나와 함께 아서의 궁정으로 갈 것이오.\""], ["said Geraint, “to the court of Arthur, and Arthur and Guenever, they shall dispose of her as they will.”", "게라인트가 말했다. \"그리고 아서와 귀네비어, 그분들이 뜻대로 그녀를 처분할 것이오.\""], ["And the next day they proceeded to Arthur’s court.", "그리고 이튿날 그들은 아서의 궁정으로 나아갔다."], ["So far concerning Geraint.", "게라인트에 관해서는 여기까지이다."]], [["————", "————"]], [["NOW this is how Arthur hunted the stag.", "그런데 아서가 사슴을 사냥한 방식은 이러했다."], ["The men and the dogs were divided into hunting-parties, and the dogs were let loose upon the stag.", "사람들과 사냥개들은 여러 사냥대로 나뉘었고, 개들은 사슴을 향해 풀려났다."], ["And the last dog that was let loose was the favorite dog of Arthur; Cavall was his name.", "그리고 가장 마지막으로 풀려난 개는 아서가 가장 아끼는 개였으니, 이름은 카발이었다."], ["And he left all the other dogs behind him and turned the stag.", "카발은 다른 모든 개들을 뒤로 제치고 사슴을 돌려세웠다."], ["And at the second turn the stag came toward the hunting-party of Arthur.", "그리고 두 번째로 방향을 돌린 사슴은 아서의 사냥대를 향해 달려왔다."], ["And Arthur set upon him; and before he could be slain by any other, Arthur cut off his head.", "아서는 사슴에게 달려들었고, 다른 누군가의 손에 죽기 전에 사슴의 목을 베었다."], ["Then they sounded the death-horn for slaying and they all gathered round.", "그러자 사람들은 사냥감을 잡았음을 알리는 죽음의 뿔나팔을 불었고, 모두가 그 주위로 모여들었다."]], [["Then came Kadyriath to Arthur and spoke to him.", "그때 카디리아스가 아서에게 다가와 말했다."], ["“Lord,”", "\"전하,\""], ["said he, “behold, yonder is Guenever, and none with her save only one maiden.”", "그가 말했다, \"보소서, 저편에 귀네비어 왕비님이 계시는데, 단 한 명의 시녀 말고는 아무도 모시고 있지 않습니다.\""], ["“Command Gildas, the son of Caw, and all the scholars of the court,”", "\"코의 아들 길다스와 궁정의 모든 학자들에게,\""], ["said Arthur, “to attend Guenever to the palace.”", "아서가 말했다, \"귀네비어를 궁전까지 모시도록 명하라.\""], ["And they did so.", "그러자 그들은 그대로 했다."]], [["Then they all set forth, holding converse together concerning the head of the stag, to whom it should be given.", "그런 다음 그들은 모두 길을 나섰는데, 사슴 머리를 누구에게 주어야 할지를 두고 서로 의논하며 갔다. 어떤 이는 그것이 자신이 가장 사랑하는 여인에게 주어지기를 바랐고, 또 어떤 이는 자신이 가장 흠모하는 여인에게 주어지기를 바랐다."], ["One wished that it should be given to the lady best beloved by him, and another to the lady whom he loved best.", "그렇게 하여 그들은 궁전에 이르렀다. 그리고 아서와 귀네비어가 그들이 사슴 머리를 두고 다투는 소리를 들었을 때, 귀네비어가 아서에게 말했다."], ["And so they came to the palace.", "\"전하, 사슴 머리에 관한 저의 의견은 이러합니다."], ["And when Arthur and Guenever heard them disputing about the head of the stag, Guenever said to Arthur: “My lord, this is my counsel concerning the stag’s head; let it not be given away until Geraint, the son of Erbin, shall return from the errand he is upon.”", "에르빈의 아들 게라인트가 그가 떠난 임무에서 돌아올 때까지 그것을 누구에게도 주지 마소서.\""], ["And Guenever told Arthur what that errand was.", "그러고는 귀네비어가 아서에게 그 임무가 무엇인지를 이야기해 주었다."], ["“Right gladly shall it be so,”", "\"기꺼이 그리하리다,\""], ["said Arthur.", "아서가 말했다."], ["And Guenever caused a watch to be set upon the ramparts for Geraint’s coming.", "그리고 귀네비어는 게라인트가 돌아오는지 살피도록 성벽 위에 망꾼을 세워 두게 했다."], ["And after midday they beheld an unshapely little man upon a horse, and after him a dame or a damsel, also on horseback, and after her a knight of large stature, bowed down, and hanging his head low and sorrowfully, and clad in broken and worthless armor.", "그러고는 정오가 지난 뒤에 그들은 말 위에 탄 볼품없는 작은 사내 하나를 보았고, 그 뒤에는 역시 말을 탄 귀부인인지 처녀인지 모를 여인이 있었으며, 그 뒤에는 큰 체구의 기사가 몸을 굽힌 채 머리를 낮고 슬프게 떨구고서, 부서지고 쓸모없는 갑옷을 걸치고 따라오고 있었다."]], [["And before they came near to the gate one of the watch went to Guenever, and told her what kind of people they saw, and what aspect they bore.", "그들이 성문에 가까이 오기 전에 망꾼 한 사람이 귀네비어에게 가서, 어떤 사람들이 보이며 그들이 어떤 모습을 하고 있는지를 아뢰었다. \"저들이 누구인지는 알지 못하겠나이다,\""], ["“I know not who they are,”", "그가 말했다. \"하지만 나는 안다,\""], ["said he, “But I know,”", "귀네비어가 말했다."], ["said Guenever; “this is the knight whom Geraint pursued, and methinks that he comes not here by his own free will.", "\"이는 게라인트가 뒤쫓던 그 기사로다. 그리고 내 생각에 그가 제 발로 이곳에 오는 것은 아닐 것이다."], ["But Geraint has overtaken him, and avenged the insult to the maiden to the uttermost.”", "게라인트가 그를 따라잡아, 시녀가 당한 모욕을 끝까지 갚은 것이리라.\""], ["And thereupon, behold, a porter came to the spot where Guenever was.", "그러자 보라, 문지기 하나가 귀네비어가 있는 자리로 왔다. \"왕비님,\""], ["“Lady,” said he, “at the gate there is a knight, and I saw never a man of so pitiful an aspect to look upon as he.", "그가 말했다, \"성문에 기사 하나가 있사온데, 보기에 그토록 가련한 모습을 한 사람은 일찍이 본 적이 없나이다."], ["Miserable and broken is the armor that he wears, and the hue of blood is more conspicuous upon it than its own color.”", "그가 걸친 갑옷은 비참하고 부서져 있으며, 그 위에는 갑옷 본래의 색보다 핏빛이 더 두드러져 보이옵니다.\" \"그의 이름을 아느냐?\""], ["“Knowest thou his name?”", "그녀가 물었다. \"압니다,\""], ["said she. “I do,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he; “he tells me that he is Edeyrn, the son of Nudd.”", "\"그는 자신이 누드의 아들 에데인이라 하옵니다.\" 그러자 그녀가 대답했다."], ["Then she replied, “I know him not.”", "\"나는 그를 알지 못한다.\""]], [["So Guenever went to the gate to meet him and he entered.", "그리하여 귀네비어가 그를 맞으러 성문으로 나갔고, 그가 들어왔다."], ["And Guenever was sorry when she saw the condition he was in, even though he was accompanied by the churlish dwarf.", "비록 그가 무례한 난쟁이를 데리고 있긴 했으나, 귀네비어는 그가 처한 형편을 보고 가엾게 여겼다. 그때 에데인이 귀네비어에게 인사를 올렸다."], ["Then Edeyrn saluted Guenever.", "\"하늘이 그대를 지켜 주기를,\""], ["“Heaven protect thee,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["said she.", "\"왕비님,\""], ["“Lady,” said he, “Geraint, the son of Erbin, thy best and most valiant servant, greets thee.”", "그가 말했다, \"에르빈의 아들이자 왕비님의 가장 훌륭하고 용맹한 신하인 게라인트가 안부를 전하나이다.\""], ["“Did he meet with thee?”", "\"그가 그대를 만났느냐?\""], ["she asked. “Yes,”", "그녀가 물었다."], ["said he, “and it was not to my advantage; and that was not his fault, but mine, lady.", "\"그렇습니다,\" 그가 말했다, \"그리고 그것은 제게 이로운 일이 아니었사옵니다."], ["And Geraint greets thee well; and in greeting thee he compelled me to come hither to do thy pleasure for the insult which thy maiden received from the dwarf.”", "허나 그것은 그의 잘못이 아니라 저의 잘못이었나이다, 왕비님. 게라인트는 왕비님께 정중히 안부를 전하며, 왕비님의 시녀가 난쟁이에게서 받은 모욕에 대해 왕비님의 뜻에 따르도록, 저를 이리로 오게 하였습니다.\""], ["“Now where did he overtake thee?”", "\"그렇다면 그가 어디에서 그대를 따라잡았느냐?\""], ["“At the place where we were jousting and contending for the sparrow-hawk, in the town which is now called Cardiff.", "\"지금은 카디프라 불리는 고을에서, 저희가 참새매를 두고 마상 창시합을 벌이며 겨루던 그 자리에서였습니다."], ["And it was for the avouchment of the love of the maiden, the daughter of Earl Ynywl, that Geraint jousted at the tournament.", "게라인트가 그 시합에서 창을 겨룬 것은 이니윌 백작의 딸인 처녀의 사랑을 증명하기 위함이었나이다."], ["And thereupon we encountered each other, and he left me, lady, as thou seest.”", "그리하여 저희는 서로 맞붙었고, 그가 저를 이렇게, 왕비님께서 보시는 대로 만들어 놓았습니다.\""], ["“Sir,”", "\"기사여,\""], ["said she, “when thinkest thou that Geraint will be here?”", "그녀가 말했다, \"게라인트가 언제쯤 이곳에 이르리라 생각하느냐?\""], ["“To-morrow, lady, I think he will be here with the maiden.”", "\"내일이면, 왕비님, 그가 그 처녀와 함께 이곳에 이르리라 생각하옵니다.\""]], [["Then Arthur came to them.", "그때 아서가 그들에게로 왔다."], ["And he saluted Arthur, and Arthur gazed a long time upon him and was amazed to see him thus.", "그러자 그가 아서에게 인사를 올렸고, 아서는 그를 오랫동안 바라보며 그의 그런 모습에 놀랐다."], ["And thinking that he knew him, he inquired of him, “Art thou Edeyrn, the son of Nudd?”", "그리고 그를 아는 듯하여, 그에게 물었다. \"그대는 누드의 아들 에데인인가?\""], ["“I am, lord,”", "\"그렇습니다, 전하,\""], ["said he, “and I have met with much trouble and received wounds unsupportable.”", "그가 말했다, \"그리고 저는 많은 고난을 만나 견디기 힘든 상처를 입었나이다.\""], ["Then he told Arthur all his adventure. “Well,”", "그러고는 아서에게 자신의 모든 일을 이야기했다."], ["said Arthur, “from what I hear it behooves Guenever to be merciful towards thee.”", "\"그렇구나,\" 아서가 말했다, \"내가 들은 바로는 귀네비어가 그대에게 자비를 베푸는 것이 마땅하겠다.\""], ["“The mercy which thou desirest, lord,”", "\"전하께서 바라시는 그 자비를,\""], ["said she, “will I grant to him, since it is as insulting to thee that an insult should be offered to me as to thyself.”", "그녀가 말했다, \"그에게 베풀겠나이다. 제게 모욕이 가해지는 것은 곧 전하 자신께 모욕이 가해지는 것과 같으니 말이옵니다.\""], ["“Thus will it be best to do,”", "\"이렇게 하는 것이 가장 좋으리라,\""], ["said Arthur; “let this man have medical care until it be known whether he may live.", "아서가 말했다. \"이 사람이 살 수 있을지 알게 될 때까지 의술의 보살핌을 받게 하라."], ["And if he live, he shall do such satisfaction as shall be judged best by the men of the court.", "그리고 그가 살아난다면, 궁정의 사람들이 가장 옳다고 판단하는 대로 보상을 치르게 하라."], ["And if he die, too much will be the death of such a youth as Edeyrn for an insult to a maiden.”", "그러나 그가 죽는다면, 에데인 같은 젊은이의 죽음은 처녀에게 가한 모욕의 대가로는 지나치게 무거우리라.\""], ["“This pleases me,” said Guenever.", "\"그것이 제 마음에 듭니다,\""], ["And Arthur caused Morgan Tud to be called to him.", "귀네비어가 말했다. 그리고 아서는 모건 투드를 불러오게 했다."], ["He was the chief physician.", "그는 으뜸가는 의원이었다."], ["“Take with thee Edeyrn, the son of Nudd, and cause a chamber to be prepared for him, and let him have the aid of medicine as thou wouldst do unto myself, if I were wounded; and let none into his chamber to molest him, but thyself and thy disciples, to administer to him remedies.”", "\"누드의 아들 에데인을 데려가, 그를 위해 방을 마련하게 하고, 만일 내가 부상을 입었다면 내게 하듯이 그에게 의술의 도움을 베풀라. 그리고 그의 방에는 그를 괴롭히려는 자가 아무도 들어가지 못하게 하되, 그대와 그대의 제자들만이 그에게 치료약을 베풀게 하라.\""], ["“I will do so, gladly, lord,”", "\"기꺼이 그리하겠나이다, 전하,\""], ["said Morgan Tud.", "모건 투드가 말했다."], ["Then said the steward of the household, “Whither is it right, lord, to order the maiden?”", "그때 집사장이 말했다. \"전하, 저 처녀는 어디로 보내는 것이 옳겠나이까?\""], ["“To Guenever and her handmaidens,” said he.", "\"귀네비어와 그 시녀들에게 보내라,\""], ["And the steward of the household so ordered her.", "그가 말했다. 그러자 집사장은 그녀를 그렇게 보냈다."]], [["“And rising up, he rode to Arthur’s court, And there the queen forgave him easily.", "\"그리고 그는 몸을 일으켜 아서의 궁정으로 말을 달렸고, 그곳에서 왕비는 그를 선뜻 용서하였네."], ["And being young, he changed himself, and grew To hate the sin that seem’d so like his own Of Modred, Arthur’s nephew, and fell at last In the great battle fighting for the king.”", "또한 젊은 몸이라 그는 스스로를 바꾸어 자라났으니, 제 죄와 그토록 닮아 보이던 그 죄, 아서의 조카 모드레드의 죄를 미워하기에 이르렀고, 마침내 큰 싸움터에서 왕을 위해 싸우다 쓰러졌네.\""], ["—Enid.", "—《에니드》"]], [["The next day came Geraint towards the court; and there was a watch set on the ramparts by Guenever, lest he should arrive unawares.", "이튿날 게라인트가 궁정을 향해 다가왔다. 그리고 귀네비어는 그가 불시에 당도하지 못하도록 성벽 위에 망꾼을 세워 두게 했다."], ["And one of the watch came to Guenever.", "망꾼 하나가 귀네비어에게 왔다. \"왕비님,\""], ["“Lady,” said he, “methinks that I see Geraint, and a maiden with him.", "그가 말했다, \"제 눈에 게라인트와 그와 함께 있는 처녀가 보이는 듯하옵니다."], ["He is on horseback, but he has his walking gear upon him, and the maiden appears to be in white, seeming to be clad in a garment of linen.”", "그는 말 위에 있으나 평상복 차림이며, 처녀는 흰옷을 입은 듯, 아마포로 지은 옷을 걸친 것으로 보이옵니다.\""], ["“Assemble all the women,”", "\"모든 여인들을 모으라,\""], ["said Guenever, “and come to meet Geraint, to welcome him, and wish him joy.”", "귀네비어가 말했다, \"그리고 게라인트를 맞으러 나가, 그를 환영하며 기쁨을 빌어 주자.\""], ["And Guenever went to meet Geraint and the maiden.", "그리하여 귀네비어가 게라인트와 그 처녀를 맞으러 나갔다."], ["And when Geraint came to the place where Guenever was, he saluted her.", "게라인트가 귀네비어가 있는 자리에 이르렀을 때, 그는 그녀에게 인사를 올렸다."], ["“Heaven prosper thee,”", "\"하늘이 그대를 번성케 하기를,\""], ["said she, “and welcome to thee.”", "그녀가 말했다, \"그리고 그대를 환영하노라.\""], ["“Lady,”", "\"왕비님,\""], ["said he, “I earnestly desired to obtain thee satisfaction, according to thy will; and, behold, here is the maiden through whom thou hadst thy revenge.”", "그가 말했다, \"저는 왕비님의 뜻에 따라 왕비님께 보상을 얻어 드리기를 간절히 바랐나이다. 그리하여, 보소서, 여기 왕비님께서 복수를 이루시게 된 그 처녀가 있나이다.\""], ["“Verily,”", "\"참으로,\""], ["said Guenever, “the welcome of Heaven be unto her; and it is fitting that we should receive her joyfully.”", "귀네비어가 말했다, \"하늘의 환영이 그녀에게 임하기를. 그리고 우리가 그녀를 기쁘게 맞이하는 것이 마땅하도다.\""], ["Then they went in and dismounted.", "그러고는 그들이 안으로 들어가 말에서 내렸다."], ["And Geraint came to where Arthur was, and saluted him.", "그리고 게라인트가 아서가 있는 자리로 가서 그에게 인사를 올렸다."], ["“Heaven protect thee,”", "\"하늘이 그대를 지켜 주기를,\""], ["said Arthur, “and the welcome of Heaven be unto thee.", "아서가 말했다, \"그리고 하늘의 환영이 그대에게 임하기를."], ["And inasmuch as thou hast vanquished Edeyrn, the son of Nudd, thou hast had a prosperous career.”", "그대가 누드의 아들 에데인을 무찔렀으니, 그대는 성공한 길을 걸어왔도다.\""], ["“Not upon me be the blame,”", "\"저를 탓하지는 마소서,\""], ["said Geraint; “it was through the arrogance of Edeyrn, the son of Nudd, himself, that we were not friends.”", "게라인트가 말했다. \"저희가 벗이 되지 못한 것은 누드의 아들 에데인 자신의 오만함 때문이었나이다.\""], ["“Now,”", "\"자,\""], ["said Arthur, “where is the maiden for whom I heard thou didst give challenge?”", "아서가 말했다, \"그대가 도전을 걸었다고 내가 들은 그 처녀는 어디에 있는가?\""], ["“She is gone with Guenever to her chamber.”", "\"귀네비어와 함께 그 방으로 갔나이다.\""], ["Then went Arthur to see the maiden.", "그러자 아서가 그 처녀를 보러 갔다."], ["And Arthur, and all his companions, and his whole court, were glad concerning the maiden.", "그리고 아서와 그의 모든 동료들, 그의 온 궁정이 그 처녀를 보고 기뻐했다."], ["And certain were they all, that, had her array been suitable to her beauty, they had never seen a maid fairer than she.", "그리고 만일 그녀의 차림새가 그 아름다움에 어울렸더라면, 그녀보다 더 고운 처녀를 일찍이 본 적이 없으리라는 데 모두가 한마음이었다."], ["And Arthur gave away the maiden to Geraint.", "그리하여 아서는 그 처녀를 게라인트에게 내주었다."], ["And the usual bond made between two persons was made between Geraint and the maiden, and the choicest of all Guenever’s apparel was given to the maiden; and thus arrayed, she appeared comely and graceful to all who beheld her.", "그리고 두 사람 사이에 맺는 통상의 혼약이 게라인트와 그 처녀 사이에 맺어졌고, 귀네비어의 모든 의복 가운데 가장 빼어난 것이 그 처녀에게 주어졌다. 그렇게 차려입으니 그녀는 그녀를 바라보는 모든 이에게 아리땁고 우아하게 보였다."], ["And that day and the night were spent in abundance of minstrelsy, and ample gifts of liquor, and a multitude of games.", "그리고 그날과 그날 밤은 풍성한 음유의 가락과 넉넉한 술의 선물과 수많은 놀이로 보내졌다."], ["And when it was time for them to go to sleep they went.", "그리고 그들이 잠자리에 들 때가 되자 모두 잠자리로 갔다."], ["And in the chamber where the couch of Arthur and Guenever was, the couch of Geraint and Enid was prepared.", "그리고 아서와 귀네비어의 침상이 있는 방에 게라인트와 에니드의 침상이 마련되었다."], ["And from that time she became his wife.", "그리고 그때부터 그녀는 그의 아내가 되었다."], ["And the next day Arthur satisfied all the claimants upon Geraint with bountiful gifts.", "그리고 이튿날 아서는 게라인트에게 권리를 주장하는 모든 이들을 넉넉한 선물로 만족시켰다."], ["And the maiden took up her abode in the palace, and she had many companions, both men and women, and there was no maiden more esteemed than she in the island of Britain.", "그리고 그 처녀는 궁전에 거처를 정했고, 남녀를 막론하고 많은 동무가 있었으며, 브리튼 섬에서 그녀보다 더 존경받는 처녀는 없었다."]], [["Then spake Guenever.", "그때 귀네비어가 말했다."], ["“Rightly did I judge,”", "\"내가 옳게 판단하였구나,\""], ["said she, “concerning the head of the stag, that it should not be given to any until Geraint’s return; and behold, here is a fit occasion for bestowing it.", "그녀가 말했다, \"사슴 머리에 관하여, 게라인트가 돌아올 때까지 그것을 누구에게도 주어서는 안 된다고 한 것 말이다. 그리고 보라, 여기 그것을 줄 마땅한 기회가 있도다."], ["Let it be given to Enid, the daughter of Ynywl, the most illustrious maiden.", "그것을 이니윌의 딸이자 가장 빼어난 처녀인 에니드에게 주도록 하자."], ["And I do not believe that any will begrudge it her, for between her and every one here there exists nothing but love and friendship.”", "그리고 나는 누구도 그것을 그녀에게 주는 것을 아까워하리라 믿지 않는다. 그녀와 이곳의 모든 사람 사이에는 오직 사랑과 우정만이 있을 따름이니 말이다.\""], ["Much applauded was this by them all, and by Arthur also.", "이 말은 그들 모두에게서, 그리고 아서에게서도 큰 갈채를 받았다."], ["And the head of the stag was given to Enid.", "그리하여 사슴 머리는 에니드에게 주어졌다."], ["And thereupon her fame increased, and her friends became more in number than before.", "그리고 그로 인해 그녀의 명성은 더해 갔고, 그녀의 벗들은 전보다 더 많아졌다."], ["And Geraint from that time forth loved the hunt, and the tournament, and hard encounters; and he came victorious from them all.", "그리고 게라인트는 그때부터 사냥과 마상 창시합과 격렬한 겨룸을 사랑하게 되었으며, 그 모든 것에서 승리를 거두고 돌아왔다."], ["And a year, and a second, and a third, he proceeded thus, until his fame had flown over the face of the kingdom.", "그리고 한 해가 가고, 두 해가 가고, 세 해째에도 그는 이렇게 나아가, 마침내 그의 명성이 온 왕국의 땅 위로 날아올랐다."]], [["And, once upon a time, Arthur was holding his court at Caerleon upon Usk; and behold, there came to him ambassadors, wise and prudent, full of knowledge and eloquent of speech, and they saluted Arthur.", "그리고 어느 날, 아서가 우스크 강가의 카에를레온에서 궁정을 열고 있을 때였다. 보라, 그에게 사절들이 찾아왔으니, 지혜롭고 사려 깊으며 식견이 가득하고 말솜씨가 능란한 자들이었다."], ["“Heaven prosper you!”", "그들이 아서에게 인사를 올렸다."], ["said Arthur; “and whence do you come?”", "\"하늘이 그대들을 번성케 하기를!\""], ["“We come, lord,”", "아서가 말했다."], ["said they, “from Cornwall; and we are ambassadors from Erbin, the son of Custennin, thy uncle, and our mission is unto thee.", "\"그런데 그대들은 어디에서 왔는가?\" \"저희는, 전하,\""], ["And he greets thee well, as an uncle should greet his nephew, and as a vassal should greet his lord.", "그들이 말했다, \"콘월에서 왔나이다. 저희는 전하의 숙부이신 쿠스텐닌의 아들 에르빈의 사절이며, 저희의 임무는 전하께 닿아 있나이다."], ["And he represents unto thee that he waxes heavy and feeble, and is advancing in years.", "그분께서는 숙부가 조카에게 안부를 전하듯, 또 신하가 주군에게 안부를 전하듯 전하께 정중히 안부를 전하시나이다."], ["And the neighboring chiefs, knowing this, grow insolent towards him, and covet his land and possessions.", "그리고 그분께서는 전하께 아뢰시기를, 당신께서 점점 무겁고 쇠약해지며 나이가 들어 가고 있다 하시옵니다."], ["And he earnestly beseeches thee, lord, to permit Geraint, his son, to return to him, to protect his possessions, and to become acquainted with his boundaries.", "그리고 이를 안 이웃의 족장들이 그분께 무례해지며, 그분의 땅과 재산을 탐내고 있나이다. 그리하여 그분께서는 전하께 간절히 청하시기를, 아들 게라인트가 그분께 돌아와 그분의 재산을 지키고 그 영지의 경계를 익히도록 허락하여 주시기를 바라시옵니다."], ["And unto him he represents that it were better for him to spend the flower of his youth and the prime of his age in preserving his own boundaries, than in tournaments which are productive of no profit, although he obtains glory in them.”", "그리고 그분께서는 게라인트에게, 비록 마상 창시합에서 영광을 얻기는 하나 아무런 이득도 없는 그런 시합에서 청춘의 꽃과 한창때의 나날을 보내는 것보다는, 자신의 영지 경계를 지키는 데 그것을 쓰는 편이 더 나으리라 아뢰시옵니다.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"좋다,\""], ["said Arthur, “go and divest yourselves of your accoutrements, and take food, and refresh yourselves after your fatigues; and before you go from hence you shall have an answer.”", "아서가 말했다, \"가서 무장을 벗고 음식을 들며, 여독을 풀고 기운을 되찾으라. 그리고 그대들이 이곳을 떠나기 전에 답을 받게 되리라.\""], ["And they went to eat.", "그리하여 그들은 먹으러 갔다."], ["And Arthur considered that it would go hard with him to let Geraint depart from him, and from his court; neither did he think it fair that his cousin should be restrained from going to protect his dominions and his boundaries, seeing that his father was unable to do so.", "그리고 아서는 게라인트를 자신에게서, 그리고 자신의 궁정에서 떠나보내는 것이 자신에게 견디기 힘든 일이 되리라 생각했다. 또한 그의 사촌의 아버지가 그렇게 할 수 없게 된 마당에, 자신의 영토와 그 경계를 지키러 가는 것을 그의 사촌이 가로막히는 것은 옳지 않다고 여겼다."], ["No less was the grief and regret of Guenever, and all her women, and all her damsels, through fear that the maiden would leave them.", "귀네비어와 그녀의 모든 여인들과 모든 시녀들의 슬픔과 아쉬움도 그에 못지않았으니, 그 처녀가 그들을 떠나리라는 두려움 때문이었다."], ["And that day and that night were spent in abundance of feasting.", "그리고 그날과 그날 밤은 풍성한 연회로 보내졌다."], ["And Arthur told Geraint the cause of the mission, and of the coming of the ambassadors to him out of Cornwall.", "그리고 아서는 게라인트에게 그 사절의 사연과, 콘월에서 사절들이 그에게 찾아온 까닭을 이야기했다."], ["“Truly,”", "\"참으로,\""], ["said Geraint, “be it to my advantage or disadvantage, lord, I will do according to thy will concerning this embassy.”", "게라인트가 말했다, \"그것이 제게 이로운 일이든 해로운 일이든, 전하, 이 사절의 일에 관해서는 전하의 뜻에 따르겠나이다.\""], ["“Behold,”", "\"보라,\""], ["said Arthur, “though it grieves me to part with thee, it is my counsel that thou go to dwell in thine own dominions, and to defend thy boundaries, and take with thee to accompany thee as many as thou wilt of those thou lovest best among my faithful ones, and among thy friends, and among thy companions in arms.”", "아서가 말했다, \"그대와 헤어지는 것이 내게는 슬픈 일이나, 내 충고는 그대가 그대 자신의 영토로 가서 살며 그 경계를 지키라는 것이다. 그리고 나의 충성스러운 자들 가운데서, 그리고 그대의 벗들과 무기를 함께한 동료들 가운데서 그대가 가장 아끼는 자들을 그대가 원하는 만큼 데려가 그대를 따르게 하라.\""], ["“Heaven reward thee!", "\"하늘이 전하께 보답하시기를!"], ["and this will I do,”", "그리하겠나이다,\" 게라인트가 말했다."], ["said Geraint. “What discourse,”", "\"그대들 사이에서 무슨 이야기가,\""], ["said Guenever, “do I hear between you?", "귀네비어가 말했다, \"내 귀에 들리는 것입니까?"], ["Is it of those who are to conduct Geraint to his country?” “It is,”", "게라인트를 그의 고향으로 인도할 자들에 관한 것입니까?\""], ["said Arthur.", "\"그렇소,\""], ["“Then is it needful for me to consider,”", "아서가 말했다. \"그렇다면 저도,\""], ["said she, “concerning companions and a provision for the lady that is with me.”", "그녀가 말했다, \"저와 함께 있는 그 부인을 위한 동무들과 채비에 관해 헤아려 보아야 하겠습니다.\""], ["“Thou wilt do well,”", "\"그리하는 것이 좋겠소,\""], ["said Arthur.", "아서가 말했다."]], [["And that night they went to sleep.", "그리고 그날 밤 그들은 잠자리에 들었다."], ["And the next day the ambassadors were permitted to depart, and they were told that Geraint should follow them.", "이튿날 사절들은 떠나도록 허락받았고, 게라인트가 그들을 뒤따르리라는 말을 전해 들었다."], ["And on the third day Geraint set forth, and many went with him—Gawain, the son of Gwyar, and Riogoned, the son of the king of Ireland, and Ondyaw, the son of the Duke of Burgundy, Gwilim, the son of the ruler of the Franks, Howel, the son of the Earl of Brittany, Perceval, the son of Evrawk, Gwyr, a judge in the court of Arthur, Bedwyr, the son of Bedrawd, Kai, the son of Kyner, Odyar, the Frank, and Ederyn, the son of Nudd.", "그리고 사흘째 되는 날 게라인트가 길을 나섰는데, 많은 이들이 그와 함께 갔다. 그와이르의 아들 가웨인, 아일랜드 왕의 아들 리오고네드, 부르고뉴 공작의 아들 온디아우, 프랑크인들의 통치자의 아들 그윌림, 브리타니 백작의 아들 호웰, 에브라우크의 아들 퍼시벌, 아서의 궁정 재판관 그위르, 베드라우드의 아들 베드위르, 키네르의 아들 카이, 프랑크인 오디아르, 그리고 누드의 아들 에데인이 그들이었다."], ["Said Geraint, “I think I shall have enough of knighthood with me.”", "게라인트가 말했다. \"내 곁에 기사의 무리가 넉넉하리라 생각하노라.\""], ["And they set forth.", "그리하여 그들은 길을 나섰다."], ["And never was there seen a fairer host journeying towards the Severn.", "세번 강을 향해 나아가는 그토록 훌륭한 무리는 일찍이 본 적이 없었다."], ["And on the other side of the Severn were the nobles of Erbin, the son of Custennin, and his foster-father at their head, to welcome Geraint with gladness; and many of the women of the court, with his mother, came to receive Enid, the daughter of Ynywl, his wife.", "그리고 세번 강 건너편에는 쿠스텐닌의 아들 에르빈의 귀족들이, 그의 양아버지를 선두로 하여, 게라인트를 기쁘게 맞이하려 나와 있었다. 또한 궁정의 여인들 다수가 그의 어머니와 함께, 그의 아내이자 이니윌의 딸인 에니드를 맞이하러 나왔다."], ["And there was great rejoicing and gladness throughout the whole court, and through all the country, concerning Geraint, because of the greatness of their love to him, and of the greatness of the fame which he had gained since he went from amongst them, and because he was come to take possession of his dominions, and to preserve his boundaries.", "그리고 온 궁정에 걸쳐, 또 온 나라에 걸쳐 게라인트로 인한 크나큰 기쁨과 환희가 있었으니, 그들이 그를 향해 품은 사랑의 크기 때문이요, 그가 그들 곁을 떠난 뒤로 얻은 명성의 크기 때문이며, 또한 그가 자신의 영토를 차지하고 그 경계를 지키러 돌아왔기 때문이었다."], ["And they came to the court.", "그리하여 그들은 궁정에 이르렀다."], ["And in the court they had ample entertainment, and a multitude of gifts, and abundance of liquor, and a sufficiency of service, and a variety of games.", "그리고 궁정에서 그들은 넉넉한 향응과 수많은 선물과 풍성한 술과 충분한 시중과 갖가지 놀이를 누렸다."], ["And to do honor to Geraint, all the chief men of the country were invited that night to visit him.", "그리고 게라인트를 기리기 위해, 그 나라의 모든 으뜸가는 인물들이 그날 밤 그를 찾아오도록 초대되었다."], ["And they passed that day and that night in the utmost enjoyment.", "그리고 그들은 그날과 그날 밤을 더할 나위 없는 즐거움 속에서 보냈다."], ["And at dawn next day Erbin arose and summoned to him Geraint, and the noble persons who had borne him company.", "그리고 이튿날 동틀 무렵 에르빈이 일어나 게라인트와 그를 따라온 고귀한 인물들을 불러들였다. 그리고 그가 게라인트에게 말했다."], ["And he said to Geraint: “I am a feeble and an aged man, and whilst I was able to maintain the dominion for thee and for myself, I did so.", "\"나는 쇠약하고 늙은 사람이로다. 그리고 내가 너와 나를 위해 이 영토를 지킬 수 있는 동안에는 그렇게 해 왔다."], ["But thou art young, and in the flower of thy vigor and of thy youth.", "허나 너는 젊고, 네 기력과 청춘의 꽃이 한창이로구나."], ["Henceforth do thou preserve thy possessions.”", "이제부터는 네가 네 재산을 지키도록 하여라.\""], ["“Truly,”", "\"참으로,\""], ["said Geraint, “with my consent thou shalt not give the power over thy dominions at this time into my hands, and thou shalt not take me from Arthur’s court.”", "게라인트가 말했다, \"제 뜻으로는 아버님께서 지금 영토에 대한 권한을 제 손에 넘겨주셔서는 안 되며, 또한 저를 아서의 궁정에서 떼어 놓으셔서도 안 되옵니다.\""], ["“Into thy hands will I give them,”", "\"네 손에 그것을 넘겨주겠노라,\""], ["said Erbin, “and this day also shalt thou receive the homage of thy subjects.”", "에르빈이 말했다, \"그리고 오늘 너는 또한 네 백성들의 충성 서약을 받게 될 것이니라.\""]], [["Then said Gawain, “It were better for thee to satisfy those who have boons to ask, to-day, and to-morrow thou canst receive the homage of thy dominions.”", "그때 가웨인이 말했다. \"오늘은 청을 드릴 것이 있는 자들을 만족시켜 주시고, 내일 그대의 영토에 대한 충성 서약을 받으시는 편이 나으리다.\""], ["So all that had boons to ask were summoned into one place.", "그리하여 청을 드릴 것이 있는 자들이 모두 한자리에 불려 모였다."], ["And Kadyriath came to them to know what were their requests.", "그리고 카디리아스가 그들에게 와서 그들의 청이 무엇인지 알아보았다."], ["And every one asked that which he desired.", "그리고 저마다 자신이 바라는 바를 청했다."], ["And the followers of Arthur began to make gifts, and immediately the men of Cornwall came, and gave also.", "그리고 아서의 수하들이 선물을 베풀기 시작했고, 곧이어 콘월 사람들이 와서 그들도 베풀었다."], ["And they were not long in giving, so eager was every one to bestow gifts, and of those who came to ask gifts, none departed unsatisfied.", "그리고 저마다 선물을 베푸는 데 그토록 열심이었으므로 그들이 베푸는 데에 오래 걸리지 않았으며, 선물을 청하러 온 자들 가운데 만족하지 못한 채 떠난 이는 아무도 없었다."], ["And that day and that night were spent in the utmost enjoyment.", "그리고 그날과 그날 밤은 더할 나위 없는 즐거움 속에서 보내졌다."]], [["And the next day at dawn, Erbin desired Geraint to send messengers to the men to ask them whether it was displeasing to them that he should come to receive their homage, and whether they had anything to object to him.", "그리고 이튿날 동틀 무렵, 에르빈은 게라인트에게 청하기를, 자신이 와서 그들의 충성 서약을 받는 것이 그들에게 못마땅한지, 또 그들이 그에게 이의를 제기할 것이 있는지를 물어보도록 사람들을 보내라 했다."], ["Then Geraint sent ambassadors to the men of Cornwall to ask them this.", "그리하여 게라인트는 콘월 사람들에게 이를 묻기 위해 사절들을 보냈다."], ["And they all said that it would be the fulness of joy and honor to them for Geraint to come and receive their homage.", "그러자 그들은 모두 게라인트가 와서 자신들의 충성 서약을 받는 것이야말로 더없는 기쁨이요 영예라고 말했다."], ["So he received the homage of such as were there.", "그리하여 그는 그곳에 있는 자들의 충성 서약을 받았다."], ["And the day after the followers of Arthur intended to go away.", "그리고 그 이튿날 아서의 수하들이 떠나려 했다."], ["“It is too soon for you to go away yet,”", "\"아직 그대들이 떠나기에는 너무 이르오,\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he; “stay with me until I have finished receiving the homage of my chief men, who have agreed to come to me.”", "\"내게 오기로 한 으뜸가는 사람들의 충성 서약을 다 받을 때까지 나와 함께 머무르시오.\" 그리하여 그들은 그가 그 일을 마칠 때까지 그와 함께 머물렀다."], ["And they remained with him until he had done so.", "그런 다음 그들은 아서의 궁정을 향해 길을 나섰다."], ["Then they set forth towards the court of Arthur.", "그리고 게라인트가 그들과 동행하러 갔고, 에니드도 디간위까지 함께 갔다."], ["And Geraint went to bear them company, and Enid also, as far as Diganwy; there they parted.", "그곳에서 그들은 헤어졌다. 그리고 부르고뉴 공작의 아들 온디아우가 게라인트에게 말했다."], ["And Ondyaw, the son of the Duke of Burgundy, said to Geraint, “Go, now, and visit the uttermost parts of thy dominions, and see well to the boundaries of thy territories; and if thou hast any trouble respecting them, send unto thy companions.”", "\"이제 가서 그대 영토의 가장 먼 곳까지 둘러보고, 그대 영지의 경계를 잘 살피시오. 그리고 그에 관해 어떤 어려움이 생기거든 그대의 동료들에게 사람을 보내시오.\""], ["“Heaven reward thee!”", "\"하늘이 그대에게 보답하시기를!\""], ["said Geraint; “and this will I do.”", "게라인트가 말했다. \"그리하겠소.\""], ["And Geraint journeyed to the uttermost parts of his dominions.", "그리하여 게라인트는 자신의 영토 가장 먼 곳까지 나아갔다."], ["And experienced guides, and the chief men of his country, went with him.", "그리고 노련한 안내인들과 그 나라의 으뜸가는 사람들이 그와 함께 갔다."], ["And the furthermost point that they showed him he kept possession of.", "그리고 그들이 그에게 보여 준 가장 먼 지점까지 그는 자신의 것으로 지켰다."]], [["————", "————"]], [["GERAINT, as he had been used to do when he was at Arthur’s court, frequented tournaments.", "에르빈의 아들 게라인트 (계속)\n게라인트는 아서 왕의 궁정에 있을 때 하던 대로 마상 시합에 자주 참가했다."], ["And he became acquainted with valiant and mighty men, until he had gained as much fame there as he had formerly done elsewhere.", "그리하여 용맹하고 강력한 사내들과 교분을 맺었으며, 마침내 예전에 다른 곳에서 그러했던 것처럼 그곳에서도 큰 명성을 얻기에 이르렀다."], ["And he enriched his court, and his companions, and his nobles, with the best horses and the best arms, and with the best and most valuable jewels, and he ceased not until his fame had flown over the face of the whole kingdom.", "또한 그는 자신의 궁정과 동료들과 귀족들을 가장 훌륭한 말과 가장 훌륭한 무구로, 그리고 가장 좋고 가장 값진 보석으로 풍요롭게 했으며, 그의 명성이 왕국 전체의 얼굴 위로 날아갈 때까지 그치지 않았다."]], [["“Before Geraint, the scourge of the enemy, I saw steeds white with foam, And after the shout of battle a fearful torrent.”", "\"적의 채찍, 게라인트 앞에서 나는 거품으로 하얗게 물든 군마들을 보았노라, 그리고 전투의 함성 뒤에 무시무시한 격류를 보았노라.\""], ["—Hen.", "— 헨."]], [["When he knew that it was thus, he began to love ease and pleasure, for there was no one who was worth his opposing.", "사정이 이러함을 알게 되었을 때, 그는 안락과 쾌락을 사랑하기 시작했다. 그에게 맞설 만한 자가 아무도 없었기 때문이다."], ["And he loved his wife, and liked to continue in the palace with minstrelsy and diversions.", "그리고 그는 아내를 사랑했으며, 음유의 노래와 여흥과 더불어 궁궐에 머무는 것을 좋아했다."], ["So he began to shut himself up in the chamber of his wife, and he took no delight in anything besides, insomuch that he gave up the friendship of his nobles, together with his hunting and his amusements, and lost the hearts of all the host in his court.", "그리하여 그는 아내의 방 안에 틀어박히기 시작했고, 그 밖의 어떤 것에서도 즐거움을 찾지 않았으니, 마침내 그는 사냥과 오락과 더불어 귀족들과의 우정마저 저버렸으며, 자신의 궁정에 있는 온 무리의 마음을 잃고 말았다."], ["And there was murmuring and scoffing concerning him among the inhabitants of the palace, on account of his relinquishing so completely their companionship for the love of his wife.", "그리고 그가 아내에 대한 사랑 때문에 그토록 완전히 그들과의 교제를 포기한 것을 두고, 궁궐의 거주자들 사이에서는 그에 관한 수군거림과 조롱이 일어났다."]], [["“They Began to scoff and jeer and babble of him As of a prince whose manhood was all gone, And molten down in mere uxoriousness.”", "\"그들은 그에 관하여 비웃고 조롱하며 떠들어 대기 시작했으니, 그 사내다움이 모조리 사라져 한낱 아내에 대한 맹목적인 애정 속에 녹아 버린 군주에 대해 떠드는 것 같았다.\""]], [["These tidings came to Erbin.", "이 소식이 에르빈에게 이르렀다."], ["And when Erbin had heard these things, he spoke unto Enid, and inquired of her whether it was she that had caused Geraint to act thus, and to forsake his people and his hosts.", "그리고 에르빈은 이 일들을 듣고 나서 에니드에게 말을 건네어, 게라인트로 하여금 이렇게 행동하게 하고 그의 백성과 무리를 저버리게 한 것이 바로 그녀가 아니냐고 물었다."], ["“Not I, by my confession unto Heaven,”", "\"제가 아닙니다, 하늘에 고백하건대,\""], ["said she; “there is nothing more hateful unto me than this.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"제게는 이보다 더 가증스러운 것이 없습니다.\""], ["And she knew not what she should do, for, although it was hard for her to own this to Geraint, yet was it not more easy for her to listen to what she heard, without warning Geraint concerning it.", "그러고도 그녀는 자신이 무엇을 해야 할지 알지 못했으니, 비록 이를 게라인트에게 털어놓기가 어려웠다고는 하나, 자신이 들은 바를 게라인트에게 경고하지 않고 듣기만 하는 것 또한 더 쉬운 일은 아니었기 때문이다."], ["And she was very sorrowful.", "그리하여 그녀는 몹시 슬퍼했다."]], [["One morning in the summer-time they were upon their couch, and Geraint lay upon the edge of it.", "여름철의 어느 아침, 그들은 침상 위에 있었고, 게라인트는 그 가장자리에 누워 있었다."], ["And Enid was without sleep in the apartment, which had windows of glass;[62] and the sun shone upon the couch.", "그리고 에니드는 유리 창문이 달린 방 안에서 잠을 이루지 못하고 있었으며, 해가 침상 위로 비치고 있었다."], ["And the clothes had slipped from off his arms and his breast, and he was asleep.", "옷이 그의 두 팔과 가슴에서 흘러내려 있었고, 그는 잠들어 있었다."], ["Then she gazed upon the marvellous beauty of his appearance, and she said, “Alas!", "그러자 그녀는 그의 모습이 지닌 경이로운 아름다움을 바라보며 이렇게 말했다."], ["and am I the cause that these arms and this breast have lost their glory, and the warlike fame which they once so richly enjoyed!”", "\"아아! 이 두 팔과 이 가슴이 그 영광을, 그리고 한때 그토록 풍성하게 누렸던 무인의 명성을 잃게 한 것이 바로 나란 말인가!\""], ["As she said this the tears dropped from her eyes, and they fell upon his breast.", "그녀가 이 말을 하는 동안 눈에서 눈물이 떨어져 그의 가슴 위로 흘러내렸다."], ["And the tears she shed and the words she had spoken, awoke him.", "그리고 그녀가 흘린 눈물과 그녀가 한 말이 그를 깨웠다."], ["And another thing contributed to awaken him, and that was the idea that it was not in thinking of him that she spoke thus, but that it was because she loved some other man more than him, and that she wished for other society.", "또 다른 것이 그를 깨우는 데 일조했으니, 그것은 곧 그녀가 그를 생각하며 그렇게 말한 것이 아니라, 그녀가 그보다 다른 사내를 더 사랑하기 때문이며 다른 이와의 교제를 바라기 때문이라는 생각이었다."], ["Thereupon Geraint was troubled in his mind, and he called his squire; and when he came to him, “Go quickly,”", "그리하여 게라인트는 마음이 어지러워져 시종을 불렀다. 시종이 오자, \"어서 가서,\""], ["said he, “and prepare my horse and my arms, and make them ready.", "그가 말했다. \"내 말과 내 무구를 준비하여 채비를 갖추어라.\""], ["And do thou rise,”", "그러고 나서 에니드에게 말했다."], ["said he to Enid, “and apparel thyself; and cause thy horse to be accoutred, and clothe thee in the worst riding-dress that thou hast in thy possession.", "\"그대도 일어나 채비를 갖추고, 그대의 말에도 마구를 갖추게 하라. 그리고 그대가 가진 것 중 가장 허름한 승마복을 입어라."], ["And evil betide me,”", "그리고 내게 화가 미칠지어다,\""], ["said he, “if thou returnest here until thou knowest whether I have lost my strength so completely as thou didst say.", "그가 말했다. \"그대가 한 말처럼 내가 그토록 완전히 힘을 잃었는지 알게 되기 전에는, 그대가 이곳으로 돌아온다면 말이다."], ["And if it be so, it will then be easy for thee to seek the society thou didst wish for of him of whom thou wast thinking.”", "그리고 만일 그러하다면, 그때는 그대가 생각하던 그 사내와의 교제를, 그대가 바라던 그 교제를 찾기란 쉬운 일이 될 것이다.\""], ["So she arose, and clothed herself in her meanest garments.", "그리하여 그녀는 일어나 가장 보잘것없는 옷을 입었다. \"저는 아무것도 모릅니다, 주군이시여,\""], ["“I know nothing, lord,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “of thy meaning.”", "\"당신의 뜻을 말입니다.\""], ["“Neither wilt thou know at this time,”", "\"지금은 그대가 알 수도 없으리라,\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["Then Geraint went to see Erbin.", "그러고 나서 게라인트는 에르빈을 만나러 갔다."], ["“Sir,”", "\"주군이시여,\""], ["said he, “I am going upon a quest, and I am not certain when I may come back.", "그가 말했다. \"저는 어떤 탐색에 나서려 하는데, 언제 돌아올 수 있을지 확실치 않습니다."], ["Take heed, therefore, unto thy possessions until my return.”", "그러하오니 제가 돌아올 때까지 당신의 재산을 잘 살펴 주십시오.\""], ["“I will do so,”", "\"그리하마,\""], ["said he; “but it is strange to me that thou shouldst go so suddenly.", "그가 말했다. \"허나 네가 그토록 갑작스레 떠난다는 것이 내게는 이상하구나."], ["And who will proceed with thee, since thou art not strong enough to traverse the land of Loegyr alone?”", "그리고 누가 너와 함께 가겠느냐, 너는 혼자서 로에귀르 땅을 가로지를 만큼 강하지도 않은데?\""], ["“But one person only will go with me.”", "\"단 한 사람만이 저와 함께 갈 것입니다.\""], ["“Heaven counsel thee, my son,”", "\"하늘이 너를 인도하시기를, 내 아들아,\""], ["said Erbin, “and may many attach themselves to thee in Loegyr.”", "에르빈이 말했다. \"그리고 로에귀르에서 많은 이가 네게 따르기를 바라노라.\""], ["Then went Geraint to the place where his horse was, and it was equipped with foreign armor, heavy and shining.", "그러고 나서 게라인트는 자신의 말이 있는 곳으로 갔으니, 그것은 무겁고 빛나는 외국산 무구로 채비되어 있었다."], ["And he desired Enid to mount her horse, and to ride forward, and to keep a long way before him.", "그리고 그는 에니드에게 말에 오르라 명하고, 앞서 나아가되 자신보다 한참 앞에 떨어져 가라고 했다."], ["“And whatever thou mayst see, and whatever thou mayst hear concerning me,”", "\"그리고 그대가 무엇을 보든, 나에 관하여 무엇을 듣든,\""], ["said he, “do thou not turn back.", "그가 말했다. \"그대는 돌아서지 마라."], ["And unless I speak unto thee, say not thou one word, either.” So they set forward.", "그리고 내가 그대에게 말을 건네지 않는 한, 그대 또한 한마디도 하지 마라.\""], ["And he did not choose the pleasantest and most frequented road, but that which was the wildest and most beset by thieves and robbers and venomous animals.", "그리하여 그들은 길을 떠났다. 그리고 그는 가장 즐겁고 가장 많이 다니는 길을 택하지 않고, 가장 황량하며 도둑과 강도와 독을 품은 짐승들이 가장 들끓는 길을 택했다."]], [["And they came to a high road, which they followed till they saw a vast forest; and they saw four armed horsemen come forth from the forest.", "그리고 그들은 큰길에 이르러 그 길을 따라가다가 광대한 숲을 보게 되었다. 그리고 그들은 무장한 기마병 넷이 그 숲에서 나오는 것을 보았다."], ["When the armed men saw them, they said one to another.", "무장한 사내들은 그들을 보자 서로에게 말했다."], ["“Here is a good occasion for us to capture two horses and armor, and a lady likewise; for this we shall have no difficulty in doing against yonder single knight who hangs his head so pensively and heavily.”", "\"여기 우리가 말 두 마리와 무구를, 그리고 귀부인 하나까지 차지할 좋은 기회가 있구나. 저토록 처량하고 무겁게 고개를 숙인 채 가는 외톨이 기사 하나를 상대로 이 일을 하기란 어렵지 않을 테니.\""], ["Enid heard this discourse, and she knew not what she should do through fear of Geraint, who had told her to be silent.", "에니드는 이 말을 들었으나, 침묵하라고 일러 둔 게라인트에 대한 두려움 때문에 무엇을 해야 할지 알지 못했다."], ["“The vengeance of Heaven be upon me,”", "\"하늘의 복수가 제게 임하소서,\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “if I would not rather receive my death from his hand than from the hand of any other; and though he should slay me, yet will I speak to him, lest I should have the misery to witness his death.”", "\"다른 누구의 손이 아니라 그분의 손에 차라리 죽음을 받지 않으려 한다면 말입니다. 비록 그분이 저를 죽이신다 해도, 저는 그분에게 말하겠으니, 그분의 죽음을 목격하는 불행을 겪지 않기 위함입니다.\""], ["So she waited for Geraint until he came near to her.", "그리하여 그녀는 게라인트가 자신에게 가까이 올 때까지 기다렸다."], ["“Lord,”", "\"주군이시여,\""], ["said she, “didst thou hear the words of those men concerning thee?”", "그녀가 말했다. \"당신에 관한 저 사내들의 말을 들으셨습니까?\""], ["Then he lifted up his eyes, and looked at her angrily.", "그러자 그는 눈을 들어 노한 눈으로 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["“Thou hadst only,”", "\"그대는 그저,\""], ["said he, “to hold thy peace as I bade thee.", "그가 말했다. \"내가 명한 대로 입을 다물고 있기만 하면 되었거늘."], ["I wish but for silence, and not for warning.", "나는 침묵을 바랄 뿐, 경고를 바라지 않는다."], ["And though thou shouldst desire to see my defeat and my death by the hands of those men, yet do I feel no dread.”", "그리고 비록 그대가 저 사내들의 손에 내가 패배하고 죽는 것을 보고 싶어 한다 해도, 나는 아무런 두려움도 느끼지 않는다.\""], ["Then the foremost of them couched his lance, and rushed upon Geraint.", "그러자 그들 중 맨 앞의 자가 창을 겨누고 게라인트에게 달려들었다."], ["And he received him, and that not feebly.", "그리고 그는 그를 맞이했으니, 그것도 미약하게가 아니었다."], ["But he let the thrust go by him, while he struck the horseman upon the centre of his shield, in such a manner that his shield was split, and his armor broken, so that a cubit’s length of the shaft of Geraint’s lance passed through his body, and sent him to the earth, the length of the lance over his horse’s crupper.", "그는 찌르기를 자신의 옆으로 흘려보내는 한편, 그 기마병의 방패 한복판을 후려쳤다. 그 일격이 어찌나 강했던지 방패가 쪼개지고 갑옷이 부서졌으며, 게라인트의 창대가 한 큐빗 길이만큼 그의 몸을 꿰뚫고 지나가 그를 땅바닥으로 내동댕이쳤으니, 창의 길이만큼 그의 말 엉덩이 너머로 나가떨어졌다."], ["Then the second horseman attacked him furiously, being wroth at the death of his companion.", "그러자 두 번째 기마병이 동료의 죽음에 분노하여 사납게 그를 공격했다."], ["But with one thrust Geraint overthrew him also, and killed him as he had done the other.", "그러나 게라인트는 단 한 번의 찌르기로 그 또한 거꾸러뜨리고, 앞서 한 것처럼 그를 죽였다."], ["Then the third set upon him, and he killed him in like manner.", "그러자 세 번째 자가 그에게 덤벼들었고, 그는 마찬가지 방식으로 그를 죽였다."], ["And thus also he slew the fourth.", "그리고 네 번째 자 또한 그렇게 베어 버렸다."], ["Sad and sorrowful was the maiden as she saw all this.", "이 모든 것을 보면서 처녀는 슬프고 비통했다."], ["Geraint dismounted his horse, and took the arms of the men he had slain, and placed them upon their saddles, and tied together the reins of their horses; and he mounted his horse again.", "게라인트는 말에서 내려, 자신이 죽인 사내들의 무구를 거두어 그들의 안장 위에 얹고, 그들의 말고삐를 한데 묶었다. 그러고는 다시 말에 올랐다."], ["“Behold what thou must do,”", "\"그대가 해야 할 일을 보라,\""], ["said he; “take the four horses and drive them before thee, and proceed forward as I bade thee just now.", "그가 말했다. \"이 네 마리 말을 데리고 그대 앞으로 몰아가며, 방금 내가 명한 대로 앞으로 나아가라."], ["And say not one word unto me, unless I speak first unto thee.", "그리고 내가 먼저 그대에게 말을 건네지 않는 한, 한마디도 내게 하지 마라."], ["And I declare unto Heaven,”", "그리고 하늘에 선언하노니,\""], ["said he, “if thou doest not thus, it will be to thy cost.”", "그가 말했다. \"그대가 이렇게 하지 않는다면, 그것은 그대에게 화가 되리라.\""], ["“I will do as far as I can, lord,”", "\"할 수 있는 데까지 하겠습니다, 주군이시여,\""], ["said she, “according to thy desire.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"당신의 뜻에 따라서요.\""]], [["So the maiden went forward, keeping in advance of Geraint, as he had desired her; and it grieved him as much as his wrath would permit, to see a maiden so illustrious as she having so much trouble with the care of the horses.", "그리하여 처녀는 그가 바란 대로 게라인트보다 앞서 나아갔다. 그리고 그토록 고귀한 처녀가 말들을 돌보느라 그토록 큰 수고를 겪는 것을 보는 일이, 그의 분노가 허락하는 한도 안에서 그를 비통하게 했다."], ["Then they reached a wood, and it was both deep and vast, and in the wood night overtook them.", "이윽고 그들은 숲에 이르렀으니, 그것은 깊고도 광대했으며, 그 숲에서 밤이 그들을 덮쳤다. \"아, 처녀여,\""], ["“Ah, maiden,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “it is vain to attempt proceeding forward.”", "\"앞으로 더 나아가려 애쓰는 것은 헛된 일이다.\" \"그럼, 주군이시여,\""], ["“Well, lord,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “whatever thou wishest, we will do.”", "\"당신이 바라시는 대로 하겠습니다.\""], ["“It will be best for us,”", "\"우리에게 가장 좋은 것은,\""], ["he answered, “to rest and wait for the day, in order to pursue our journey.”", "그가 대답했다. \"쉬면서 날이 밝기를 기다렸다가 여정을 이어 가는 것이리라.\""], ["“That we will, gladly,” said she.", "\"기꺼이 그리하겠습니다,\""], ["And they did so.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["Having dismounted himself, he took her down from her horse.", "그리하여 그들은 그렇게 했다. 그는 직접 말에서 내린 뒤, 그녀를 말에서 내려 주었다."], ["“I cannot by any means refrain from sleep, through weariness,”", "\"나는 피로 때문에 도무지 잠을 떨칠 수가 없구나,\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he; “do thou therefore watch the horses, and sleep not.”", "\"그러니 그대는 말들을 지키며 잠들지 마라.\" \"그리하겠습니다, 주군이시여,\""], ["“I will, lord,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["said she. Then he went to sleep in his armor, and thus passed the night, which was not long at that season.", "그러고 나서 그는 갑옷을 입은 채 잠이 들었고, 그렇게 밤을 보냈으니, 그 계절에는 밤이 길지 않았다."], ["And when she saw the dawn of day appear, she looked around her to see if he were waking, and thereupon he woke.", "그리고 그녀는 날이 밝아 오는 것을 보자, 그가 깨어났는지 보려고 주위를 둘러보았는데, 그때 그가 깨어났다."], ["Then he arose, and said unto her, “Take the horses and ride on, and keep straight on as thou didst yesterday.”", "그러자 그는 일어나 그녀에게 말했다. \"말들을 데리고 나아가되, 어제와 마찬가지로 곧장 가라.\""], ["And they left the wood, and they came to an open country, with meadows on one hand, and mowers mowing the meadows.", "그리하여 그들은 숲을 떠나, 한쪽으로는 풀밭이 펼쳐지고 풀을 베는 자들이 그 풀밭에서 풀을 베고 있는 트인 들판에 이르렀다."], ["And there was a river before them, and the horses bent down and drank of the water.", "그리고 그들 앞에 강이 있어, 말들이 고개를 숙여 그 물을 마셨다."], ["And they went up out of the river by a lofty steep; and there they met a slender stripling with a satchel about his neck, and they saw that there was something in the satchel, but they knew not what it was.", "그리고 그들은 가파른 비탈을 통해 강에서 올라왔는데, 거기서 목에 가방을 두른 호리호리한 젊은이를 만났다. 그들은 그 가방 안에 무언가 들어 있는 것을 보았으나, 그것이 무엇인지는 알지 못했다."], ["And he had a small blue pitcher in his hand, and a bowl on the mouth of the pitcher.", "그리고 그는 손에 작은 푸른 물병을 들고 있었으며, 물병 주둥이 위에는 그릇이 하나 얹혀 있었다."], ["And the youth saluted Geraint.", "젊은이가 게라인트에게 인사했다."], ["“Heaven prosper thee!”", "\"하늘이 너를 번성케 하시기를!\""], ["said Geraint; “and whence dost thou come?”", "게라인트가 말했다. \"그리고 너는 어디서 오는 길이냐?\""], ["“I come,”", "\"저는,\""], ["said he, “from the city that lies before thee.", "그가 말했다. \"당신 앞에 놓인 도시에서 옵니다."], ["My lord,”", "나으리,\""], ["he added, “will it be displeasing to thee if I ask whence thou comest also?”", "그가 덧붙였다. \"제가 당신께서도 어디서 오시는지 여쭈어도 언짢지 않으시겠습니까?\""], ["“By no means; through yonder wood did I come.”", "\"전혀 그렇지 않다. 나는 저쪽 숲을 지나 왔다.\""], ["“Thou camest not through the wood to-day.”", "\"오늘 숲을 지나오신 것은 아니지요.\""], ["“No,”", "\"그렇다,\""], ["he replied, “we were in the wood last night.”", "그가 대답했다. \"우리는 어젯밤 숲속에 있었다.\""], ["“I warrant,”", "\"장담컨대,\""], ["said the youth, “that thy condition there last night was not the most pleasant, and that thou hadst neither meat nor drink.”", "젊은이가 말했다. \"어젯밤 그곳에서 당신의 처지는 그리 유쾌하지 않았을 테고, 고기도 마실 것도 없으셨겠지요.\""], ["“No, by my faith,” said he.", "\"그래, 정말 그러했다,\""], ["“Wilt thou follow my counsel,”", "그가 말했다. \"제 권유를 따르시어,\""], ["said the youth, “and take thy meal from me?”", "젊은이가 말했다. \"제게서 식사를 받으시겠습니까?\""], ["“What sort of meal?”", "\"어떤 종류의 식사 말이냐?\""], ["he inquired.", "그가 물었다."], ["“The breakfast which is sent for yonder mowers, nothing less than bread and meat and wine, and if thou wilt, sir, they shall have none of it.”", "\"저쪽 풀 베는 자들에게 보내는 아침 식사로, 빵과 고기와 포도주에 지나지 않습니다만, 나으리께서 원하신다면 그들은 그것을 조금도 받지 못할 것입니다.\" \"그리하겠다,\""], ["“I will,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “and Heaven reward thee for it.”", "\"하늘이 네게 그 보답을 내리시기를.\""]], [["So Geraint alighted, and the youth took the maiden from off her horse.", "그리하여 게라인트는 말에서 내렸고, 젊은이는 처녀를 말에서 내려 주었다."], ["Then they washed, and took their repast.", "그러고 나서 그들은 몸을 씻고 식사를 했다."], ["And the youth cut the bread in slices, and gave them drink, and served them withal.", "젊은이는 빵을 조각조각 자르고, 그들에게 마실 것을 주었으며, 그렇게 시중을 들었다."], ["And when they had finished, the youth arose and said to Geraint, “My lord, with thy permission, I will now go and fetch some food for the mowers.”", "그리고 그들이 식사를 마치자, 젊은이는 일어나 게라인트에게 말했다. \"나으리, 허락하신다면, 저는 이제 풀 베는 자들에게 줄 음식을 가지러 가겠습니다.\""], ["“Go first to the town,”", "\"먼저 마을로 가서,\" 게라인트가 말했다."], ["said Geraint, “and take a lodging for me in the best place that thou knowest, and the most commodious one for the horses; and take thou whichever horse and arms thou choosest, in payment for thy service and thy gift.”", "\"네가 아는 가장 좋은 곳에, 그리고 말들에게 가장 편리한 곳에 내가 묵을 숙소를 잡아 다오. 그리고 네 봉사와 네 선물에 대한 보답으로, 어느 말이든 어느 무구든 네가 고르는 것을 가지거라.\""], ["“Heaven reward thee, lord!”", "\"하늘이 당신께 보답하시기를, 나으리!\""], ["said the youth; “and this would be ample to repay services much greater than those I have rendered unto thee.”", "젊은이가 말했다. \"그리고 이것이라면 제가 당신께 베푼 것보다 훨씬 큰 봉사라도 충분히 갚을 만합니다.\""], ["And to the town went the youth, and he took the best and the most pleasant lodgings that he knew; and after that he went to the palace, having the horse and armor with him, and proceeded to the place where the earl was, and told him all his adventure.", "그리하여 젊은이는 마을로 가서 자신이 아는 가장 좋고 가장 쾌적한 숙소를 잡았다. 그러고 나서 그는 말과 무구를 챙겨 궁궐로 가, 백작이 있는 곳으로 나아가 자신의 모든 일을 그에게 이야기했다."], ["“I go now, lord,”", "\"저는 이제 가겠습니다, 나으리,\""], ["said he, “to meet the knight, and to conduct him to his lodging.”", "그가 말했다. \"그 기사를 만나 그의 숙소로 안내하기 위해서요.\""], ["“Go, gladly,”", "\"기꺼이 가거라,\""], ["said the earl; “and right joyfully shall he be received here, if he so come.”", "백작이 말했다. \"그리고 그가 그렇게 온다면, 이곳에서 참으로 기쁘게 맞아들이겠노라.\""], ["And the youth went to meet Geraint, and told him that he would be received gladly by the earl in his own palace; but he would go only to his lodgings.", "그리하여 젊은이는 게라인트를 만나러 가, 백작이 자신의 궁궐에서 그를 기꺼이 맞으리라고 그에게 전했다. 그러나 게라인트는 자신의 숙소로만 가려 했다."], ["And he had a goodly chamber, in which was plenty of straw and drapery, and a spacious and commodious place he had for the horses; and the youth prepared for them plenty of provender.", "그리고 그는 짚과 휘장이 넉넉히 갖추어진 훌륭한 방을 얻었고, 말들을 위해서도 넓고 편리한 자리를 얻었으며, 젊은이는 그것들을 위해 먹이를 넉넉히 마련해 주었다. 그들이 무장을 벗은 뒤, 게라인트는 에니드에게 이렇게 말했다."], ["After they had disarrayed themselves, Geraint spoke thus to Enid: “Go,”", "\"가서,\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “to the other side of the chamber, and come not to this side of the house; and thou mayst call to thee the woman of the house, if thou wilt.”", "\"방의 다른 쪽으로 가서, 이쪽 집안으로는 오지 마라. 그리고 그대가 원한다면 이 집의 여인을 그대에게 불러도 좋다.\""], ["“I will do, lord,”", "\"그리하겠습니다, 주군이시여,\""], ["said she, “as thou sayest.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"당신이 말씀하신 대로요.\""], ["Thereupon the man of the house came to Geraint and welcomed him.", "그러자 그 집의 사내가 게라인트에게 와서 그를 환영했다."], ["And after they had eaten and drank, Geraint went to sleep, and so did Enid also.", "그리고 그들이 먹고 마신 뒤, 게라인트는 잠이 들었고, 에니드 또한 그러했다."]], [["In the evening, behold, the earl came to visit Geraint, and his twelve honorable knights with him.", "저녁이 되자, 보라, 백작이 그의 명예로운 기사 열둘을 데리고 게라인트를 방문하러 왔다."], ["And Geraint rose up and welcomed him.", "그러자 게라인트는 일어나 그를 환영했다."], ["Then they all sat down according to their precedence in honor.", "그러고 나서 그들 모두는 명예의 서열에 따라 자리에 앉았다."], ["And the earl conversed with Geraint, and inquired of him the object of his journey.", "백작은 게라인트와 이야기를 나누며 그의 여정의 목적을 물었다. \"제게는 그런 것이 없습니다,\""], ["“I have none,”", "그가 대답했다."], ["he replied, “but to seek adventures and to follow mine own inclination.”", "\"모험을 찾고 제 마음 가는 대로 따르는 것 말고는요.\""], ["Then the earl cast his eye upon Enid, and he looked at her steadfastly.", "그러자 백작은 에니드에게 눈길을 던지더니, 그녀를 뚫어지게 바라보았다."], ["And he thought he had never seen a maiden fairer or more comely than she.", "그리고 그는 그녀보다 더 아름답거나 더 어여쁜 처녀를 본 적이 없다고 생각했다."], ["And he set all his thoughts and his affections upon her.", "그리하여 그는 자신의 온 생각과 애정을 그녀에게 쏟았다."], ["Then he asked of Geraint, “Have I thy permission to go and converse with yonder maiden, for I see that she is apart from thee?”", "그러고는 게라인트에게 물었다. \"저쪽 처녀가 당신과 떨어져 있는 것이 보이니, 제가 가서 저 처녀와 이야기를 나누어도 좋겠습니까?\""], ["“Thou hast it gladly,”", "\"기꺼이 좋습니다,\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["So the earl went to the place where the maiden was, and spake with her.", "그리하여 백작은 처녀가 있는 곳으로 가서 그녀와 이야기를 나누었다."], ["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["maiden,”", "처녀여,\""], ["said he, “it cannot be pleasant to thee to journey with yonder man.”", "그가 말했다. \"저쪽 사내와 함께 여행하는 것이 그대에게 즐거울 리 없겠지요.\""], ["“It is not unpleasant to me,”", "\"제게는 불쾌하지 않습니다,\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“Thou hast neither youths nor maidens to serve thee,”", "\"그대를 시중들 젊은이도 처녀도 없지 않소,\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he. “Truly,”", "\"참으로,\""], ["she replied, “it is more pleasant for me to follow yonder man, than to be served by youths and maidens.”", "그녀가 대답했다. \"젊은이와 처녀의 시중을 받는 것보다 저쪽 사내를 따르는 것이 제게는 더 즐겁습니다.\""], ["“I will give thee good counsel,”", "\"내가 그대에게 좋은 조언을 하겠소,\""], ["said he: “all my earldom will I place in thy possession, if thou wilt dwell with me.”", "그가 말했다. \"그대가 나와 함께 산다면, 내 백작령 전부를 그대의 소유로 두겠소.\""]], [["“Enid, the pilot star of my lone life, Enid, my early and my only love.”", "\"에니드, 외로운 내 삶을 이끄는 길잡이 별, 에니드, 내 첫사랑이자 유일한 사랑이여.\""], ["—Enid.", "— 에니드."]], [["“That will I not, by Heaven,”", "\"그리하지 않겠습니다, 하늘에 맹세코,\""], ["she said; “yonder man was the first to whom my faith was ever pledged; and shall I prove inconstant to him?”", "그녀가 말했다. \"저쪽 사내가 제 정절을 처음으로 맹세한 분인데, 제가 그분에게 변심을 하겠습니까?\""], ["“Thou art in the wrong,”", "\"그대가 틀렸소,\""], ["said the earl; “if I slay the man yonder, I can keep thee with me as long as I choose; and when thou no longer pleasest me, I can turn thee away.", "백작이 말했다. \"내가 저쪽 사내를 죽이면, 나는 내가 원하는 한 그대를 곁에 둘 수 있고, 그대가 더 이상 마음에 들지 않으면 그대를 내쫓을 수 있소."], ["But if thou goest with me by thy own good-will, I protest that our union shall continue as long as I remain alive.”", "허나 그대가 그대 자신의 선의로 나와 함께 간다면, 내가 살아 있는 한 우리의 결합이 계속되리라 단언하겠소.\""], ["Then she pondered those words of his, and she considered that it was advisable to encourage him in his request.", "그러자 그녀는 그의 그 말들을 곰곰이 생각하였고, 그의 청을 부추기는 것이 현명하리라 여겼다."], ["“Behold then, chieftain, this is most expedient for thee to do to save me from all reproach; come here to-morrow and take me away as though I knew nothing thereof.”", "\"그렇다면 보십시오, 족장이시여, 모든 비난으로부터 저를 구하려면 이렇게 하시는 것이 가장 마땅합니다. 내일 이곳으로 오시어, 제가 아무것도 모르는 것처럼 저를 데려가십시오.\""], ["“I will do so,”", "\"그리하겠소,\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["So he arose and took his leave, and went forth with his attendants.", "그리하여 그는 일어나 작별을 고하고, 수행원들과 함께 떠났다."], ["And she told not then to Geraint any of the conversation which she had had with the earl, lest it should rouse his anger, and cause him uneasiness and care.", "그리고 그녀는 백작과 나눈 대화를 그때 게라인트에게 조금도 이야기하지 않았으니, 그것이 그의 분노를 불러일으켜 그에게 불안과 근심을 끼칠까 두려웠기 때문이다."]], [["And at the usual hour they went to sleep.", "그리고 여느 때와 같은 시각에 그들은 잠자리에 들었다."], ["And at the beginning of the night Enid slept a little; and at midnight she arose, and placed all Geraint’s armor together so that it might be ready to put on.", "그리고 밤이 시작될 무렵 에니드는 잠깐 잠을 잤다. 그러다가 한밤중에 그녀는 일어나, 곧바로 입을 수 있도록 게라인트의 무구를 모두 한데 모아 두었다."], ["And although fearful of her errand, she came to the side of Geraint’s bed; and she spoke to him softly and gently, saying, “My lord, arise, and clothe thyself, for these were the words of the earl to me and his intention concerning me.”", "그리고 자신이 하려는 일이 두려웠음에도, 그녀는 게라인트의 침상 곁으로 가, 그에게 부드럽고 다정하게 말했다. \"주군이시여, 일어나 옷을 입으십시오."], ["So she told Geraint all that had passed.", "이것이 백작이 제게 한 말이며, 저에 관한 그의 의도입니다.\""], ["And although he was wroth with her, he took warning, and clothed himself.", "그리하여 그녀는 게라인트에게 일어난 일을 모두 이야기했다. 그리고 비록 그가 그녀에게 노했으나, 그는 그 경고를 받아들여 옷을 입었다."], ["And she lighted a candle, that he might have light to do so.", "그리고 그녀는 그가 옷을 입을 수 있도록 빛을 비추려고 초에 불을 켰다."], ["“Leave there the candle,”", "\"그 초는 거기 두고,\""], ["said he, “and desire the man of the house to come here.”", "그가 말했다. \"이 집의 사내더러 이리 오라고 청하라.\""], ["Then she went, and the man of the house came to him.", "그러자 그녀가 갔고, 집의 사내가 그에게 왔다."], ["“Dost thou know how much I owe thee?”", "\"내가 네게 진 빚이 얼마인지 아느냐?\""], ["asked Geraint.", "게라인트가 물었다."], ["“I think thou owest but little.”", "\"제게 지신 빚은 얼마 안 된다고 생각합니다.\""], ["“Take the three horses and the three suits of armor.”", "\"그 세 마리 말과 세 벌의 무구를 가지거라.\""], ["“Heaven reward thee, lord,”", "\"하늘이 당신께 보답하시기를, 나으리,\""], ["said he, “but I spent not the value of one suit of armor upon thee.”", "그가 말했다. \"허나 저는 당신께 무구 한 벌의 값도 쓰지 않았습니다.\""], ["“For that reason,”", "\"바로 그 까닭에,\""], ["said he, “thou wilt be the richer.", "그가 말했다. \"너는 더 부유해질 것이다."], ["And now, wilt thou come to guide me out of the town?”", "그런데 이제 나를 마을 밖으로 안내해 주겠느냐?\""], ["“I will gladly,”", "\"기꺼이 그리하겠습니다,\""], ["said he; “and in which direction dost thou intend to go?”", "그가 말했다. \"그리고 어느 방향으로 가실 작정이십니까?\""], ["“I wish to leave the town by a different way from that by which I entered it.”", "\"나는 들어온 길과는 다른 길로 마을을 떠나고자 한다.\""], ["So the man of the lodgings accompanied him as far as he desired.", "그리하여 그 숙소의 사내는 그가 바라는 데까지 그와 동행했다."], ["Then he bade the maiden to go on before him, and she did so, and went straight forward, and his host returned home.", "그러고 나서 그는 처녀에게 자신보다 앞서 가라고 명했고, 그녀는 그렇게 하여 곧장 앞으로 나아갔으며, 그의 주인은 집으로 돌아갔다."]], [["And Geraint and the maiden went forward along the high-road.", "그리고 게라인트와 처녀는 큰길을 따라 앞으로 나아갔다."], ["And as they journeyed thus, they heard an exceeding loud wailing near to them.", "그리고 그렇게 여행하던 중, 그들은 자신들 가까이에서 몹시 큰 통곡 소리를 들었다."], ["“Stay thou here,”", "\"그대는 여기 머물러라,\""], ["said he, “and I will go and see what is the cause of this wailing.”", "그가 말했다. \"내가 가서 이 통곡의 까닭이 무엇인지 보고 오겠다.\""], ["“I will,” said she.", "\"그리하겠습니다,\""], ["Then he went forward into an open glade that was near the road.", "그녀가 말했다. 그러고 나서 그는 길 가까이에 있는 트인 빈터로 나아갔다."], ["And in the glade he saw two horses, one having a man’s saddle, and the other a woman’s saddle upon it.", "그리고 그 빈터에서 그는 말 두 마리를 보았는데, 한 마리에는 남자용 안장이, 다른 한 마리에는 여자용 안장이 얹혀 있었다."], ["And behold there was a knight lying dead in his armor, and a young damsel in a riding-dress standing over him lamenting.", "그리고 보라, 갑옷을 입은 채 죽어 누워 있는 기사가 하나 있었고, 승마복을 입은 젊은 처자가 그 위에서 슬피 울며 서 있었다."], ["“Ah, lady,”", "\"아, 부인,\""], ["said Geraint, “what hath befallen thee?”", "게라인트가 말했다. \"무슨 일이 그대에게 닥쳤습니까?\""], ["“Behold,”", "\"보십시오,\""], ["she answered, “I journeyed here with my beloved husband, when lo!", "그녀가 대답했다. \"저는 사랑하는 남편과 함께 이곳을 여행하고 있었는데, 보세요!"], ["three giants came upon us, and without any cause in the world, they slew him.”", "거인 셋이 우리에게 덮쳐 와, 세상에 아무런 까닭도 없이 그를 죽였습니다.\""], ["“Which way went they hence?”", "\"그들은 여기서 어느 쪽으로 갔습니까?\""], ["said Geraint.", "게라인트가 말했다."], ["“Yonder by the high-road,”", "\"저쪽 큰길로 갔습니다,\""], ["she replied.", "그녀가 대답했다."], ["So he returned to Enid.", "그리하여 그는 에니드에게 돌아갔다."], ["“Go,”", "\"가라,\""], ["said he, “to the lady that is below yonder, and await me there till I come.”", "그가 말했다. \"저 아래에 있는 부인에게 가서, 내가 올 때까지 거기서 나를 기다려라.\""], ["She was sad when he ordered her to do thus, but nevertheless she went to the damsel, whom it was ruth to hear, and she felt certain that Geraint would never return.", "그가 그렇게 하라고 명했을 때 그녀는 슬펐으나, 그럼에도 그녀는 듣기에 가여운 그 처자에게로 갔으며, 게라인트가 결코 돌아오지 못하리라 확신했다."]], [["Meanwhile Geraint followed the giants, and overtook them.", "한편 게라인트는 거인들을 뒤쫓아 따라잡았다."], ["And each of them was greater in stature than three other men, and a huge club was on the shoulder of each.", "그리고 그들 각자는 다른 사내 셋을 합친 것보다 키가 컸으며, 저마다 어깨에는 거대한 곤봉을 지고 있었다."], ["Then he rushed upon one of them, and thrust his lance through his body.", "그러자 그는 그들 중 하나에게 돌진하여 자신의 창을 그 몸에 꿰뚫었다."], ["And having drawn it forth again, he pierced another of them through likewise.", "그리고 그것을 다시 뽑아내어, 그들 중 또 하나를 마찬가지로 꿰뚫었다."], ["But the third turned upon him and struck him with his club so that he split his shield and crushed his shoulder.", "그러나 세 번째 자가 그에게 돌아서서 곤봉으로 그를 후려쳤으니, 그 바람에 그의 방패가 쪼개지고 그의 어깨가 으스러졌다."], ["But Geraint drew his sword and gave the giant a blow on the crown of his head, so severe, and fierce, and violent, that his head and his neck were split down to his shoulders, and he fell dead.", "그러나 게라인트는 칼을 뽑아 그 거인의 정수리에 일격을 가했으니, 그것이 어찌나 매섭고 사납고 격렬했던지, 그의 머리와 목이 어깨까지 쪼개져 그가 죽어 쓰러졌다."], ["So Geraint left him thus and returned to Enid.", "그리하여 게라인트는 그를 그렇게 두고 에니드에게로 돌아갔다."], ["And when he reached the place where she was he fell down lifeless from his horse.", "그리고 그녀가 있는 곳에 이르렀을 때, 그는 말에서 죽은 듯이 떨어졌다."], ["Piercing and loud and thrilling was the cry that Enid uttered.", "에니드가 내지른 외침은 날카롭고 크고 가슴을 에는 것이었다."], ["And she came and stood over him where he had fallen.", "그리고 그녀는 그가 쓰러진 곳으로 가서 그 위에 섰다."], ["And at the sound of her cries came the Earl of Limours, and they who journeyed with him, whom her lamentations brought out of their road.", "그리고 그녀의 외침 소리에 리무르 백작과 그와 함께 여행하던 자들이 왔으니, 그녀의 비통한 울음이 그들을 길에서 끌어낸 것이었다. 그리고 백작이 에니드에게 말했다."], ["And the earl said to Enid, “Alas, lady, what hath befallen thee?”", "\"아, 부인, 무슨 일이 그대에게 닥쳤습니까?\" \"아, 선량하신 분이여,\""], ["“Ah, good sir,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “the only man I have loved, or ever shall love, is slain.”", "\"제가 사랑해 온, 또 앞으로도 사랑할 유일한 사내가 죽임을 당했습니다.\""], ["Then he said to the other, “And what is the cause of thy grief?”", "그러자 그가 다른 여인에게 말했다. \"그리고 그대 슬픔의 까닭은 무엇이오?\""], ["“They have slain my beloved husband also,”", "\"그들은 제 사랑하는 남편 또한 죽였습니다,\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“And who was it that slew them?”", "\"그리고 그들을 죽인 것은 누구였소?\""], ["“Some giants,”", "\"거인 몇이,\""], ["she answered, “slew my best-beloved, and the other knight went in pursuit of them, and came back in the state thou seest.”", "그녀가 대답했다. \"제가 가장 사랑하던 이를 죽였고, 저 다른 기사가 그들을 뒤쫓아 갔다가, 당신이 보시는 그 꼴이 되어 돌아왔습니다.\""], ["The earl caused the knight that was dead to be buried, but he thought that there still remained some life in Geraint; and to see if he yet would live, he had him carried with him in the hollow of his shield, and upon a bier.", "백작은 죽은 그 기사를 묻게 했으나, 게라인트에게는 아직 얼마간 생명이 남아 있다고 생각했다. 그리하여 그가 아직 살아날지 보려고, 그는 게라인트를 자신의 방패 우묵한 곳에 담고 들것에 실어 함께 데려가게 했다."], ["And the two damsels went to the court; and when they arrived there, Geraint was placed upon a little couch in front of the table that was in the hall.", "그리고 두 처자는 궁정으로 갔다. 그들이 그곳에 이르자, 게라인트는 홀에 있는 탁자 앞의 작은 침상 위에 뉘어졌다."], ["Then they all took off their traveling-gear, and the earl besought Enid to do the same, and to clothe herself in other garments.", "그러고 나서 그들 모두는 여행복을 벗었고, 백작은 에니드에게도 그렇게 하여 다른 옷으로 갈아입으라고 간청했다. \"그리하지 않겠습니다, 하늘에 맹세코,\""], ["“I will not, by Heaven,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["said she. “Ah, lady,”", "\"아, 부인,\""], ["said he, “be not so sorrowful for this matter.”", "그가 말했다. \"이 일로 그토록 슬퍼하지 마시오.\""], ["“It were hard to persuade me to be otherwise,” said she.", "\"제가 달리 굴도록 설득하기란 어려울 것입니다,\""], ["“I will act towards thee in such wise that thou needest not be sorrowful, whether yonder knight live or die.", "그녀가 말했다. \"저쪽 기사가 살든 죽든, 그대가 슬퍼할 필요가 없도록 내가 그대를 대하리다."], ["Behold, a good earldom, together with myself, will I bestow upon thee; be therefore happy and joyful.”", "보시오, 좋은 백작령과 더불어 나 자신을 그대에게 바치겠소. 그러니 행복하고 기뻐하시오.\""], ["“I declare to Heaven,”", "\"저는 하늘에 선언합니다,\""], ["said she, “that henceforth I shall never be joyful while I live.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"이제부터 제가 사는 동안 결코 기뻐하지 않으리라고요.\""], ["“Come,”", "\"자,\""], ["said he, “and eat.”", "그가 말했다. \"그리고 드시오.\""], ["“No, by Heaven, I will not.”", "\"아니요, 하늘에 맹세코, 그러지 않겠습니다.\""], ["“But, by Heaven, thou shalt,” said he.", "\"허나, 하늘에 맹세코, 그대는 그러할 것이오,\""], ["So he took her with him to the table against her will, and many times desired her to eat.", "그가 말했다. 그리하여 그는 그녀의 뜻을 거슬러 그녀를 탁자로 데려가, 여러 번 먹으라고 청했다."], ["“I call Heaven to witness,”", "\"하늘을 증인으로 부르겠습니다,\""], ["said she, “that I will not until the man that is upon yonder bier shall eat likewise.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"저쪽 들것 위의 사내가 마찬가지로 먹기 전에는 저도 먹지 않겠다고요.\""], ["“Thou canst not fulfil that,”", "\"그대는 그것을 이룰 수 없소,\""], ["said the earl, “yonder man is dead already.”", "백작이 말했다. \"저쪽 사내는 이미 죽었으니.\""], ["“I will prove that I can,” said she.", "\"제가 이룰 수 있음을 증명하겠습니다,\""], ["Then he offered her a goblet of liquor.", "그녀가 말했다. 그러자 그는 그녀에게 술잔을 내밀었다."], ["“Drink this goblet,”", "\"이 잔을 드시오,\""], ["he said, “and it will cause thee to change thy mind.”", "그가 말했다. \"그러면 그대의 마음이 바뀌게 될 것이오.\""], ["“Evil betide me,”", "\"제게 화가 미칠지어다,\""], ["she answered, “if I drink aught until he drink also.”", "그녀가 대답했다. \"그분이 마시기 전에 제가 무엇이라도 마신다면요.\""], ["“Truly,”", "\"참으로,\""], ["said the earl, “it is of no more avail for me to be gentle with thee than ungentle.”", "백작이 말했다. \"그대에게 부드럽게 대하는 것이 거칠게 대하는 것보다 나을 게 없구려.\""], ["And he gave her a box in the ear.", "그러고는 그녀의 귀싸대기를 한 대 갈겼다."], ["Thereupon she raised a loud and piercing shriek, and her lamentations were much greater than they had been before; for she considered in her mind, that, had Geraint been alive, he durst not have struck her thus.", "그러자 그녀는 크고 날카로운 비명을 내질렀고, 그녀의 비통한 울음은 전보다 훨씬 더 커졌으니, 게라인트가 살아 있었다면 감히 그녀를 이렇게 치지 못했으리라고 마음속으로 생각했기 때문이다."], ["But, behold, at the sound of her cry, Geraint revived from his swoon, and he sat upon the bier; and finding his sword in the hollow of his shield, he rushed to the place where the earl was, and struck him a fiercely-wounding, severely-venomous, and sternly-smiting blow upon the crown of his head, so that he clove him in twain, until his sword was staid by the table.", "그러나, 보라, 그녀의 외침 소리에 게라인트가 혼절에서 깨어나, 들것 위에 일어나 앉았다. 그리고 자신의 방패 우묵한 곳에서 칼을 찾아내어, 백작이 있는 곳으로 돌진해, 그의 정수리에 사납게 베고 매섭게 독을 품고 무자비하게 후려치는 일격을 가했으니, 그를 둘로 쪼개어 그의 칼이 탁자에 가로막힐 때까지 갈랐다."], ["Then all left the board and fled away.", "그러자 모두가 식탁을 떠나 달아났다."], ["And this was not so much through fear of the living, as through the dread they felt at seeing the dead man rise up to slay them.", "그리고 이는 산 자에 대한 두려움 때문이라기보다는, 죽은 사내가 일어나 그들을 죽이는 것을 보고 느낀 공포 때문이었다."], ["And Geraint looked upon Enid, and he was grieved for two causes; one was to see that Enid had lost her color and her wonted aspect; and the other, to know that she was in the right.", "그리고 게라인트는 에니드를 바라보았고, 두 가지 까닭으로 비통했다. 하나는 에니드가 혈색과 평소의 모습을 잃은 것을 본 까닭이요, 다른 하나는 그녀가 옳았음을 알게 된 까닭이었다."], ["“Lady,”", "\"부인,\""], ["said he, “knowest thou where our horses are?”", "그가 말했다. \"우리 말들이 어디 있는지 아시오?\""], ["“I know, lord, where thy horse is,”", "\"압니다, 주군이시여, 당신 말이 어디 있는지는요,\""], ["she replied, “but I know not where is the other.", "그녀가 대답했다. \"허나 다른 말이 어디 있는지는 모릅니다."], ["Thy horse is in the house yonder.”", "당신 말은 저쪽 집에 있습니다.\""], ["So he went to the house, and brought forth his horse, and mounted him, and took up Enid, and placed her upon the horse with him.", "그리하여 그는 그 집으로 가서 자신의 말을 끌어내어 올라타고, 에니드를 들어 올려 자신과 함께 말 위에 앉혔다."], ["And he rode forward.", "그리고 그는 앞으로 말을 달렸다."], ["And their road lay between two hedges; and the night was gaining on the day. And lo!", "그리고 그들의 길은 두 산울타리 사이로 나 있었으며, 밤이 낮을 밀어내고 있었다."], ["they saw behind them the shafts of spears betwixt them and the sky, and they heard the tramping of horses, and the noise of a host approaching.", "그런데 보라! 그들은 자신들 뒤편, 자신들과 하늘 사이로 솟은 창자루들을 보았고, 말들이 짓밟는 소리와 한 무리가 다가오는 소음을 들었다."], ["“I hear something following us,”", "\"무언가 우리를 뒤쫓는 소리가 들리는구나,\""], ["said he, “and I will put thee on the other side of the hedge.”", "그가 말했다. \"내가 그대를 산울타리 저편으로 옮겨 두겠다.\""], ["And thus he did.", "그리고 그는 그렇게 했다."], ["And thereupon, behold a knight pricked towards him, and couched his lance.", "그리고 그러자, 보라, 한 기사가 그를 향해 박차를 가하며 달려와 창을 겨누었다. 에니드가 이것을 보고 외쳐 말했다."], ["When Enid saw this, she cried out, saying, “O chieftain, whoever thou art, what renown wilt thou gain by slaying a dead man?”", "\"오 족장이시여, 당신이 누구시든, 죽은 사내를 죽여서 무슨 명성을 얻으시렵니까?\" \"오 하늘이여!\""], ["“O Heaven!”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “is it Geraint?”", "\"게라인트인가?\" \"그렇습니다, 참으로,\""], ["“Yes, in truth,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["said she; “and who art thou?”", "\"그런데 당신은 누구십니까?\""], ["“I am Gwiffert Petit,”", "\"나는 기페르트 페티트요,\""], ["said he, “thy husband’s ally, coming to thy assistance, for I heard that thou wast in trouble.", "그가 말했다. \"그대 남편의 동맹이며, 그대가 곤경에 처했다는 말을 들었기에 그대를 도우러 왔소."], ["Come with me to the court of a son-in-law of my sister, which is near here, and thou shalt have the best medical assistance in the kingdom.”", "여기서 가까운, 내 누이의 사위가 있는 궁정으로 나와 함께 갑시다. 그러면 그대는 이 왕국에서 가장 훌륭한 의술의 도움을 받을 것이오.\""], ["“I will do so gladly,”", "\"기꺼이 그리하겠습니다,\""], ["said Geraint.", "게라인트가 말했다."], ["And Enid was placed upon the horse of one of Gwiffert’s squires, and they went forward to the baron’s palace.", "그리하여 에니드는 기페르트의 시종 중 한 사람의 말에 태워졌고, 그들은 남작의 궁궐로 나아갔다."], ["And they were received there with gladness, and they met with hospitality and attention.", "그리고 그들은 그곳에서 기쁘게 맞아들여졌으며, 환대와 보살핌을 받았다."], ["The next morning they went to seek physicians; and it was not long before they came, and they attended Geraint until he was perfectly well.", "다음 날 아침 그들은 의원들을 찾으러 갔고, 오래지 않아 그들이 왔으며, 게라인트가 완전히 나을 때까지 그를 돌보았다."], ["And while Geraint was under medical care Gwiffert caused his armor to be repaired, until it was as good as it had ever been.", "그리고 게라인트가 치료를 받는 동안, 기페르트는 그의 무구를 수선하게 하여, 그것이 예전 못지않게 좋아질 때까지 손보게 했다."], ["And they remained there a month and a fortnight.", "그리고 그들은 그곳에서 한 달 보름을 머물렀다."], ["Then they separated, and Geraint went towards his own dominions, and thenceforth he reigned prosperously, and his warlike fame and splendor lasted with renown and honor, both to him and to Enid,[63] from that time forward.", "그러고 나서 그들은 헤어졌고, 게라인트는 자신의 영토로 향했으며, 그때부터 그는 번영을 누리며 다스렸고, 그의 무인다운 명성과 영광은 그때 이후로 그와 에니드 모두에게 영예와 명예와 더불어 오래도록 이어졌다."]], [["————", "————"]], [["# PWYLL, PRINCE OF DYVED", "# PWYLL, PRINCE OF DYVED"]], [["ONCE upon a time Pwyll was at Narberth, his chief palace, where a feast had been prepared for him, and with him was a great host of men.", "옛날 옛적에 퓔은 그의 으뜸가는 궁전이 있는 나르베르스에 머물고 있었는데, 그곳에는 그를 위한 잔치가 마련되어 있었고, 수많은 사람들이 그와 함께하고 있었다."], ["And after the first meal Pwyll arose to walk; and he went to the top of a mound that was above the palace, and was called Gorsedd Arberth.", "첫 번째 식사를 마친 뒤 퓔은 일어나 거닐었다. 그는 궁전 위쪽에 있는, 고르세드 아르베르스라고 불리는 둔덕 꼭대기로 올라갔다."], ["“Lord,”", "\"주군이시여.\""], ["said one of the court, “it is peculiar to the mound that whosoever sits upon it cannot go thence without either receiving wounds or blows, or else seeing a wonder.”", "궁정의 한 사람이 말했다. \"이 둔덕에는 특이한 점이 있으니, 누구든 그 위에 앉는 사람은 상처나 매를 입거나, 아니면 어떤 기이한 일을 보지 않고서는 그곳을 떠나지 못합니다.\""], ["“I fear not to receive wounds or blows,”", "\"상처나 매를 입는 것은 두렵지 않다.\""], ["said Pwyll; “but as to the wonder, gladly would I see it.", "퓔이 말했다. \"허나 기이한 일이라면, 기꺼이 보고 싶구나."], ["I will therefore go and sit upon the mound.”", "그러니 나는 가서 저 둔덕 위에 앉으리라.\""]], [["And upon the mound he sat.", "그리하여 그는 둔덕 위에 앉았다."], ["And while he sat there, they saw a lady, on a pure white horse of large size, with a garment of shining gold around her, coming along the highway that led from the mound.", "그가 그곳에 앉아 있는 동안, 사람들은 한 귀부인이 커다란 순백의 말을 타고서, 빛나는 황금빛 옷을 두른 채, 둔덕에서 이어지는 큰길을 따라 오는 것을 보았다. \"나의 부하들이여.\""], ["“My men,”", "퓔이 말했다."], ["said Pwyll, “is there any among you who knows yonder lady?”", "\"그대들 중에 저 귀부인을 아는 자가 있는가?\""], ["“There is not, lord,”", "\"없습니다, 주군이시여.\""], ["said they.", "그들이 말했다."], ["“Go one of you and meet her, that we may know who she is.”", "\"그대들 중 하나가 가서 그녀를 맞이하여, 그녀가 누구인지 우리가 알 수 있게 하라.\""], ["And one of them arose, and as he came upon the road to meet her, she passed by; and he followed as fast as he could, being on foot, and the greater was his speed, the further was she from him.", "그러자 그들 중 한 사람이 일어났고, 그녀를 맞이하러 길로 나아가자 그녀는 지나쳐 갔다. 그는 걸어서 할 수 있는 한 빨리 뒤를 따랐으나, 그의 속도가 빠를수록 그녀는 그에게서 더 멀어졌다."], ["And when he saw that it profited him nothing to follow her, he returned to Pwyll, and said unto him, “Lord, it is idle for any one in the world to follow her on foot.”", "그녀를 따라가는 것이 아무 소용없음을 깨닫자, 그는 퓔에게 돌아와 이렇게 말했다. \"주군이시여, 세상 누구라도 걸어서 그녀를 따라가는 것은 부질없는 일입니다.\""], ["“Verily,”", "\"참으로.\""], ["said Pwyll, “go unto the palace, and take the fleetest horse that thou seest, and go after her.”", "퓔이 말했다. \"궁전으로 가서, 그대가 본 가장 날랜 말을 골라, 그녀를 뒤쫓으라.\""]], [["And he took a horse and went forward.", "그리하여 그는 말 한 마리를 잡아타고 앞으로 나아갔다."], ["And he came to an open, level plain, and put spurs to his horse; and the more he urged his horse, the further was she from him.", "그는 탁 트인 평평한 들판에 이르러 말에 박차를 가했다. 그러나 말을 재촉하면 할수록 그녀는 그에게서 더 멀어졌다."], ["And he returned to the place where Pwyll was, and said, “Lord, it will avail nothing for any one to follow yonder lady.", "그는 퓔이 있는 곳으로 돌아와 말했다. \"주군이시여, 누구라도 저 귀부인을 따라가는 것은 아무 소용이 없을 것입니다."], ["I know of no horse in these realms swifter than this, and it availed me not to pursue her.”", "이 나라에서 이보다 빠른 말을 저는 알지 못하나, 그녀를 뒤쫓는 것은 소용이 없었습니다.\" \"참으로.\""], ["“Of a truth,”", "퓔이 말했다."], ["said Pwyll, “there must be some illusion here; let us go towards the palace.”", "\"여기에는 무슨 환술이 있는 것이 틀림없다. 궁전으로 가자.\""], ["So to the palace they went, and spent the day.", "그리하여 그들은 궁전으로 갔고, 그날을 보냈다."]], [["And the next day they amused themselves until it was time to go to meat.", "이튿날 그들은 식사를 하러 갈 때까지 즐겁게 시간을 보냈다. 식사가 끝나자 퓔이 말했다."], ["And when meat was ended, Pwyll said, “Where are the hosts that went yesterday to the top of the mound?”", "\"어제 둔덕 꼭대기로 갔던 무리들은 어디 있는가?\" \"보십시오, 주군이시여, 저희가 여기 있습니다.\""], ["“Behold, lord, we are here,”", "그들이 말했다."], ["said they.", "\"가자.\""], ["“Let us go,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “to the mound, and sit there.", "\"둔덕으로 가서 그곳에 앉자."], ["And do thou,”", "그리고 그대는.\""], ["said he to the page who tended his horse, “saddle my horse well, and hasten with him to the road, and bring also my spurs with thee.”", "그가 자기 말을 돌보는 시동에게 말했다. \"내 말에 안장을 잘 얹어 서둘러 길로 끌고 오고, 내 박차도 함께 가져오라.\""], ["And the youth did thus.", "젊은이는 그대로 했다."], ["And they went and sat upon the mound; and ere they had been there but a short time, they beheld the lady coming by the same road, and in the same manner, and at the same pace.", "그리하여 그들은 가서 둔덕 위에 앉았다. 그곳에 앉은 지 얼마 지나지 않아, 그들은 그 귀부인이 같은 길을 따라, 같은 모습으로, 같은 걸음으로 오는 것을 보았다."], ["“Young man,”", "\"젊은이여.\""], ["said Pwyll, “I see the lady coming; give me my horse.”", "퓔이 말했다. \"저 귀부인이 오는 것이 보이는구나."], ["And before he had mounted his horse she passed him.", "내 말을 다오.\" 그가 말에 오르기도 전에 그녀는 그를 지나쳐 갔다."], ["And he turned after her and followed her.", "그는 그녀를 향해 방향을 돌려 뒤를 따랐다."], ["And he let his horse go bounding playfully, and thought that he should soon come up with her.", "그는 말이 신나게 껑충거리며 달리게 하면서, 곧 그녀를 따라잡으리라 생각했다."], ["But he came no nearer to her than at first.", "그러나 그는 처음보다 조금도 그녀에게 가까워지지 못했다."], ["Then he urged his horse to his utmost speed, yet he found that it availed not.", "그러자 그는 말을 최대한의 속도로 몰았으나, 그래도 소용이 없음을 깨달았다."], ["Then said Pwyll, “O maiden, for the sake of him whom thou best lovest, stay for me.”", "그때 퓔이 말했다. \"오, 처녀여, 그대가 가장 사랑하는 이를 위하여, 나를 위해 멈춰 주오.\""], ["“I will stay gladly,”", "\"기꺼이 멈추겠어요.\""], ["said she; “and it were better for thy horse hadst thou asked it long since.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"그리고 진작 그리 청하셨더라면 당신의 말에게도 더 나았을 거예요.\""], ["So the maiden stopped; and she threw back that part of her head-dress which covered her face.", "그리하여 처녀는 멈춰 섰고, 그녀는 얼굴을 가리고 있던 머리 장식의 일부를 뒤로 젖혔다."], ["Then he thought that the beauty of all the maidens and all the ladies that he had ever seen was as nothing compared to her beauty.", "그러자 그는 자신이 일찍이 보았던 모든 처녀와 모든 귀부인의 아름다움이 그녀의 아름다움에 비하면 아무것도 아니라고 생각했다. \"귀부인이여.\""], ["“Lady,”", "그가 말했다."], ["he said, “wilt thou tell me aught concerning thy purpose?”", "\"그대가 온 뜻에 관하여 내게 말해 주겠소?\" \"말씀드리지요.\""], ["“I will tell thee,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["said she; “my chief quest was to see thee.”", "\"저의 으뜸가는 소망은 당신을 뵙는 것이었어요.\" \"참으로.\""], ["“Truly,”", "퓔이 말했다."], ["said Pwyll, “this is to me the most pleasing quest on which thou couldst have come; and wilt thou tell me who thou art?”", "\"이는 그대가 올 수 있었던 가장 기쁜 소망이오. 그대가 누구인지 말해 주겠소?\""], ["“I will tell thee, lord,”", "\"말씀드리겠어요, 주군이시여.\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“I am Rhiannon, the daughter of Heveydd, and they sought to give me a husband against my will.", "\"저는 헤베이드의 딸 리아논이며, 사람들은 제 뜻을 거슬러 제게 남편을 짝지어 주려 했어요. 그러나 저는 어떤 남편도 원치 않았으니, 그것은 당신을 향한 제 사랑 때문이었지요."], ["But no husband would I have, and that because of my love for thee; neither will I yet have one, unless thou reject me; and hither have I come to hear thy answer.”", "당신이 저를 물리치지 않는 한, 저는 아직 어떤 남편도 두지 않을 거예요. 그리고 저는 당신의 대답을 듣고자 이곳에 왔어요.\""], ["“By Heaven,”", "\"하늘에 맹세코.\""], ["said Pwyll, “behold this is my answer.", "퓔이 말했다. \"보시오, 이것이 내 대답이오."], ["If I might choose among all the ladies and damsels in the world, thee would I choose.”", "세상의 모든 귀부인과 처녀 가운데서 고를 수 있다면, 나는 그대를 고를 것이오.\""], ["“Verily,”", "\"참으로.\""], ["said she, “if thou art thus minded, make a pledge to meet me ere I am given to another.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"당신이 그러한 마음이시라면, 제가 다른 이에게 주어지기 전에 저와 만날 것을 약속하세요.\""], ["“The sooner I may do so, the more pleasing will it be to me,”", "\"내가 그렇게 할 수 있는 것이 빠를수록, 내게는 더 기쁜 일이오.\""], ["said Pwyll; “and wheresoever thou wilt, there will I meet with thee.”", "퓔이 말했다. \"그대가 원하는 곳이라면 어디서든, 나는 그대와 만나겠소.\""], ["“I will that thou meet me this day twelvemonth at the palace of Heveydd.”", "\"지금부터 한 해가 되는 오늘, 헤베이드의 궁전에서 저와 만나 주세요.\""], ["“Gladly,”", "\"기꺼이.\""], ["said he, “will I keep this tryst.”", "그가 말했다. \"이 약속을 지키겠소.\""], ["So they parted, and he went back to his hosts, and to them of his household.", "그리하여 그들은 헤어졌고, 그는 자기 무리에게로, 그리고 자기 가솔들에게로 돌아갔다."], ["And whatsoever questions they asked him respecting the damsel, he always turned the discourse upon other matters.", "그들이 그 처녀에 관하여 어떤 질문을 하든, 그는 늘 화제를 다른 일로 돌렸다."]], [["And when a year from that time was gone, he caused a hundred knights to equip themselves, and to go with him to the palace of Heveydd.", "그때로부터 한 해가 지나자, 그는 기사 백 명을 무장시켜 자신과 함께 헤베이드의 궁전으로 가게 했다."], ["And he came to the palace, and there was great joy concerning him, with much concourse of people, and great rejoicing, and vast preparations for his coming.", "그는 궁전에 이르렀고, 그를 두고 큰 기쁨이 있었으니, 사람들이 많이 모여들었고, 크게 즐거워했으며, 그의 도착을 위한 성대한 준비가 있었다."], ["And the whole court was placed under his orders.", "그리고 궁정 전체가 그의 명령 아래 놓였다."]], [["And the hall was garnished, and they went to meat, and thus did they sit: Heveydd was on one side of Pwyll, and Rhiannon on the other; and all the rest according to their rank.", "큰 방이 단장되었고, 그들은 식사를 하러 가서 이렇게 앉았다. 헤베이드는 퓔의 한쪽에, 리아논은 다른 쪽에 앉았고, 나머지는 모두 그들의 지위에 따라 앉았다."], ["And they ate and feasted, and talked one with another.", "그들은 먹고 잔치를 벌이며 서로 이야기를 나누었다."], ["And at the beginning of the carousal after the meat, there entered a tall, auburn-haired youth, of royal bearing, clothed in a garment of satin.", "식사가 끝난 뒤 흥겨운 술자리가 시작될 무렵, 키가 크고 적갈색 머리에, 왕족의 풍모를 지니고, 비단옷을 차려입은 젊은이가 들어왔다."], ["And when he came into the hall, he saluted Pwyll and his companions.", "그가 큰 방으로 들어오자, 그는 퓔과 그 동료들에게 인사했다. \"하늘의 인사가 그대에게 있기를.\""], ["“The greeting of Heaven be unto thee,”", "퓔이 말했다. \"이리 와서 앉으라.\""], ["said Pwyll; “come thou and sit down.”", "\"아닙니다.\" 그가 말했다."], ["“Nay,” said he, “a suitor am I, and I will do my errand.”", "\"저는 청을 드리러 온 사람이니, 제 용무를 행하겠습니다.\""], ["“Do so willingly,”", "\"기꺼이 그리하라.\""], ["said Pwyll.", "퓔이 말했다."], ["“Lord,”", "\"주군이시여.\""], ["said he, “my errand is unto thee, and it is to crave a boon of thee that I come.”", "그가 말했다. \"제 용무는 당신께 드리는 것이니, 제가 온 것은 당신께 은혜 하나를 간청하기 위함입니다.\""], ["“What boon soever thou mayest ask of me, so far as I am able, thou shalt have.”", "\"그대가 내게 무엇을 청하든, 내 힘이 닿는 한, 그대는 그것을 얻으리라.\""], ["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["said Rhiannon, “wherefore didst thou give that answer?”", "리아논이 말했다. \"어찌하여 그런 대답을 하셨나요?\""], ["“Has he not given it before the presence of these nobles?”", "\"이분께서 이 귀족들 앞에서 그리 말씀하시지 않았습니까?\""], ["asked the youth.", "젊은이가 물었다."], ["“My soul,”", "\"내 영혼이여.\""], ["said Pwyll, “what is the boon thou askest?”", "퓔이 말했다. \"그대가 청하는 은혜가 무엇이오?\""], ["“The lady whom best I love is to be thy bride this night; I come to ask her of thee, with the feast and the banquet that are in this place.”", "\"제가 가장 사랑하는 귀부인이 오늘 밤 당신의 신부가 되기로 되어 있습니다. 저는 이곳에 있는 잔치와 연회와 더불어 그녀를 당신께 청하러 왔습니다.\""], ["And Pwyll was silent, because of the promise which he had given.", "퓔은 자신이 한 약속 때문에 말이 없었다. \"원하는 만큼 잠자코 계셔도 좋아요.\""], ["“Be silent as long as thou wilt,”", "리아논이 말했다."], ["said Rhiannon, “never did man make worse use of his wits than thou hast done.”", "\"그러나 당신처럼 제 지혜를 잘못 쓴 사람은 일찍이 없었어요.\" \"귀부인이여.\""], ["“Lady,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “I knew not who he was.”", "\"나는 그가 누구인지 알지 못했소.\""], ["“Behold, this is the man to whom they would have given me against my will,”", "\"보세요, 이 사람이 바로 제 뜻을 거슬러 사람들이 제게 주려 했던 사람이에요.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she; “and he is Gawl, the son of Clud, a man of great power and wealth, and because of the word thou hast spoken, bestow me upon him, lest shame befall thee.”", "\"그리고 그는 클루드의 아들 가울이니, 막강한 권세와 부를 지닌 사람이지요. 당신이 하신 말씀 때문에, 수치가 당신께 닥치지 않도록, 저를 그에게 주세요.\""], ["“Lady,”", "\"귀부인이여.\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “I understand not thy answer; never can I do as thou sayest.”", "\"나는 그대의 대답을 이해하지 못하겠소. 결코 그대가 말하는 대로 할 수 없소.\""], ["“Bestow me upon him,”", "\"저를 그에게 주세요.\""], ["said she, “and I will cause that I shall never be his.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"그리고 저는 결코 그의 것이 되지 않도록 하겠어요.\""], ["“By what means will that be?”", "\"어떤 방법으로 그리되겠소?\""], ["asked Pwyll.", "퓔이 물었다."], ["Then she told him the thought that was in her mind.", "그러자 그녀는 자신의 마음속에 있는 생각을 그에게 말했다."], ["And they talked long together.", "그리고 그들은 오래도록 함께 이야기를 나누었다."], ["Then Gawl said, “Lord, it is meet that I have an answer to my request.”", "그때 가울이 말했다. \"주군이시여, 제 청에 대한 대답을 듣는 것이 마땅합니다.\""], ["“As much of that thou hast asked as it is in my power to give, thou shalt have,”", "\"그대가 청한 것 가운데 내 힘으로 줄 수 있는 만큼은, 그대가 얻으리라.\""], ["replied Pwyll.", "퓔이 대답했다."], ["“My soul,”", "\"내 영혼이여.\""], ["said Rhiannon unto Gawl, “as for the feast and the banquet that are here, I have bestowed them upon the men of Dyved, and the household and the warriors that are with us.", "리아논이 가울에게 말했다. \"여기 있는 잔치와 연회로 말하자면, 저는 그것을 디베드의 사람들과, 우리와 함께 있는 가솔과 전사들에게 베풀었어요."], ["These can I not suffer to be given to any.", "이것을 누구에게도 내어 줄 수는 없어요."], ["In a year from to-night, a banquet shall be prepared for thee in this palace, that I may become thy bride.”", "오늘 밤으로부터 한 해가 되는 날, 이 궁전에서 당신을 위한 연회가 마련될 것이고, 그때 제가 당신의 신부가 되겠어요.\""]], [["So Gawl went forth to his possessions, and Pwyll went also back to Dyved.", "그리하여 가울은 자기 영지로 떠났고, 퓔 또한 디베드로 돌아갔다."], ["And they both spent that year until it was the time for the feast at the palace of Heveydd.", "그리고 그들 둘은 헤베이드의 궁전에서 잔치가 열릴 때가 될 때까지 그 한 해를 보냈다."], ["Then Gawl, the son of Clud, set out to the feast that was prepared for him; and he came to the palace, and was received there with rejoicing.", "그때 클루드의 아들 가울은 자신을 위해 마련된 잔치를 향해 길을 나섰다. 그는 궁전에 이르러, 그곳에서 기쁨 속에 맞아들여졌다."], ["Pwyll, also, the chief of Dyved, came to the orchard with a hundred knights, as Rhiannon had commanded him.", "디베드의 우두머리 퓔 또한 리아논이 명한 대로, 기사 백 명과 함께 과수원으로 왔다."], ["And Pwyll was clad in coarse and ragged garments, and wore large, clumsy old shoes upon his feet.", "퓔은 거칠고 누더기 같은 옷을 입고, 발에는 크고 볼품없는 낡은 신을 신고 있었다."], ["And when he knew that the carousal after the meat had begun, he went toward the hall; and when he came into the hall he saluted Gawl, the son of Clud, and his company, both men and women.", "식사 뒤의 술자리가 시작되었음을 알자, 그는 큰 방을 향해 갔다. 큰 방에 들어서자 그는 클루드의 아들 가울과 그 일행에게, 남자와 여자 모두에게 인사했다."], ["“Heaven prosper thee,”", "\"하늘이 그대를 번성케 하기를.\""], ["said Gawl, “and friendly greeting be unto thee!”", "가울이 말했다. \"그리고 정다운 인사가 그대에게 있기를!\""], ["“Lord,”", "\"주군이시여.\""], ["said he, “may Heaven reward thee!", "그가 말했다. \"하늘이 당신께 보답하기를!"], ["I have an errand unto thee.”", "저는 당신께 드릴 용무가 있습니다.\""], ["“Welcome be thine errand, and if thou ask of me that which is right, thou shalt have it gladly.”", "\"그대의 용무를 환영하노라. 그대가 내게 옳은 것을 청한다면, 그대는 그것을 기꺼이 얻으리라.\""], ["“It is fitting,”", "\"마땅한 일입니다.\""], ["answered he; “I crave but from want, and the boon I ask is to have this small bag that thou seest filled with meat.”", "그가 대답했다. \"저는 다만 궁핍하여 청할 뿐이니, 제가 청하는 은혜는 당신이 보시는 이 작은 자루를 고기로 가득 채우는 것입니다.\""], ["“A request within reason is this,”", "\"이치에 맞는 청이로다.\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “and gladly shalt thou have it.", "\"기꺼이 그대는 그것을 얻으리라."], ["Bring him food.”", "그에게 음식을 가져오라.\""], ["A great number of attendants arose and began to fill the bag; but for all they put into it, it was no fuller than at first.", "수많은 시종이 일어나 자루를 채우기 시작했다. 그러나 그들이 아무리 많이 넣어도, 그것은 처음보다 조금도 더 차지 않았다."], ["“My soul,”", "\"내 영혼이여.\""], ["said Gawl, “will thy bag ever be full?”", "가울이 말했다. \"그대의 자루는 언제고 가득 차겠는가?\""], ["“It will not, I declare to Heaven,”", "\"하늘에 단언하건대, 가득 차지 않을 것입니다.\""], ["said he, “for all that may be put into it, unless one possessed of lands, and domains, and treasure, shall arise and tread down with both his feet the food that is within the bag, and shall say, ‘Enough has been put therein.’”", "그가 말했다. \"거기 무엇을 넣든, 영지와 영토와 보물을 가진 누군가가 일어나 두 발로 자루 안의 음식을 밟고 서서, '충분히 넣었다'고 말하지 않는 한 말입니다.\""], ["Then said Rhiannon unto Gawl, the son of Clud, “Rise up quickly.”", "그러자 리아논이 클루드의 아들 가울에게 말했다. \"어서 일어나세요.\""], ["“I will willingly arise,”", "\"기꺼이 일어나리다.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["So he rose up, and put his two feet into the bag.", "그리하여 그는 일어나 두 발을 자루 안에 넣었다."], ["And Pwyll turned up the sides of the bag, so that Gawl was over his head in it.", "그러자 퓔은 자루의 가장자리를 끌어올려, 가울이 머리까지 그 안에 잠기게 했다."], ["And he shut it up quickly, and slipped a knot upon the thongs, and blew his horn.", "그리고 그는 재빨리 그것을 닫고, 가죽끈에 매듭을 지어 묶고는 자기 뿔나팔을 불었다."], ["And thereupon, behold, his knights came down upon the palace.", "그러자 보라, 그의 기사들이 궁전으로 들이닥쳤다."], ["And they seized all the host that had come with Gawl, and cast them into his own prison.", "그들은 가울과 함께 온 무리를 모조리 붙잡아, 그의 감옥에 가두었다."], ["And Pwyll threw off his rags, and his old shoes, and his tattered array.", "퓔은 누더기와 낡은 신과 너덜너덜한 차림을 벗어 던졌다."], ["And as they came in, every one of Pwyll’s knights struck a blow upon the bag, and asked, “What is here?”", "그리고 들어오면서, 퓔의 기사들은 저마다 자루에 한 대씩 후려치며 물었다. \"여기 무엇이 있는가?\""], ["“A badger,” said they.", "\"오소리입니다.\""], ["And in this manner they played, each of them striking the bag, either with his foot or with a staff.", "그들이 말했다. 이런 식으로 그들은 저마다 발로, 혹은 막대기로 자루를 치며 놀았다."], ["And thus played they with the bag.", "그렇게 그들은 자루를 가지고 놀았다."], ["And then was the game of Badger in the Bag first played.", "그리고 그때 '자루 속 오소리'라는 놀이가 처음으로 행해졌다."]], [["“Lord,”", "\"주군이시여.\" 자루 속의 사내가 말했다."], ["said the man in the bag, “if thou wouldst but hear me, I merit not to be slain in a bag.”", "\"제 말을 들어주신다면, 저는 자루 속에서 죽임을 당할 만한 자가 아닙니다.\" 헤베이드가 말했다."], ["Said Heveydd, “Lord, he speaks truth; it were fitting that thou listen to him, for he deserves not this.”", "\"주군이시여, 그의 말이 옳습니다. 그대가 그의 말을 듣는 것이 마땅하니, 그는 이런 일을 당할 만하지 않습니다.\""], ["“Verily,”", "\"참으로.\""], ["said Pwyll, “I will do thy counsel concerning him.”", "퓔이 말했다. \"그에 관하여 그대의 조언을 따르겠소.\""], ["“Behold, this is my counsel then,”", "\"보세요, 그렇다면 이것이 제 조언이에요.\""], ["said Rhiannon.", "리아논이 말했다."], ["“Thou art now in a position in which it behooves thee to satisfy suitors and minstrels.", "\"당신은 지금 청원자들과 음유시인들을 만족시켜야 마땅한 처지에 계세요."], ["Let him give unto them in thy stead, and take a pledge from him that he will never seek to revenge that which has been done to him.", "그가 당신을 대신해 그들에게 베풀게 하시고, 자신에게 행해진 일에 대해 결코 복수를 꾀하지 않겠다는 서약을 그에게서 받으세요."], ["And this will be punishment enough.”", "그것으로 충분한 벌이 될 거예요.\""], ["“I will do this gladly,”", "\"기꺼이 그리하겠습니다.\""], ["said the man in the bag.", "자루 속의 사내가 말했다."], ["“And gladly will I accept it,”", "\"그리고 나는 기꺼이 그것을 받아들이겠소.\""], ["said Pwyll, “since it is the counsel of Heveydd and Rhiannon.", "퓔이 말했다. \"이는 헤베이드와 리아논의 조언이니."], ["Seek thyself sureties.”", "그대 자신의 보증인을 찾으라.\""], ["“We will be for him,” said Heveydd, “until his men be free to answer for him.”", "\"그의 부하들이 그를 위해 답할 수 있게 될 때까지, 우리가 그의 보증인이 되겠소.\""], ["And upon this he was let out of the bag, and his liegemen were liberated.", "헤베이드가 말했다. 그리하여 그는 자루에서 풀려났고, 그의 신하들도 풀려났다."], ["“Verily, lord,”", "\"참으로, 주군이시여.\""], ["said Gawl, “I am greatly hurt, and I have many bruises.", "가울이 말했다. \"저는 크게 다쳤고, 멍이 많이 들었습니다."], ["With thy leave, I will go forth.", "허락하신다면, 저는 떠나겠습니다."], ["I will leave nobles in my stead to answer for me in all that thou shalt require.”", "당신이 요구하시는 모든 일에 대해 저를 대신해 답할 귀족들을 제 대신 남겨 두겠습니다.\""], ["“Willingly,”", "\"기꺼이.\""], ["said Pwyll, “mayest thou do this.”", "퓔이 말했다. \"그대는 그리해도 좋다.\""], ["So Gawl went to his own possessions.", "그리하여 가울은 자기 영지로 갔다."]], [["And the hall was set in order for Pwyll and the men of his host, and for them also of the palace, and they went to the tables and sat down.", "큰 방은 퓔과 그 무리의 사람들을 위해, 그리고 궁전의 사람들을 위해서도 정돈되었고, 그들은 식탁으로 가서 앉았다."], ["And as they had sat that time twelvemonth, so sat they that night.", "그리고 그들이 한 해 전 그때에 앉았던 대로, 그들은 그날 밤에도 앉았다."], ["And they ate and feasted, and spent the night in mirth and tranquility.", "그들은 먹고 잔치를 벌였으며, 흥겹고 평온하게 밤을 보냈다."], ["And the time came that they should sleep, and Pwyll and Rhiannon went to their chamber.", "잠자리에 들 때가 되자, 퓔과 리아논은 그들의 침소로 갔다."]], [["And next morning at break of day, “My lord,”", "이튿날 동틀 무렵, \"나의 주군이시여.\""], ["said Rhiannon, “arise and begin to give thy gifts unto the minstrels.", "리아논이 말했다. \"일어나셔서 음유시인들에게 선물을 베풀기 시작하세요."], ["Refuse no one to-day that may claim thy bounty.”", "오늘은 당신의 후한 베풂을 청하는 누구도 물리치지 마세요.\""], ["“Thus shall it be gladly,”", "\"기꺼이 그리하리다.\""], ["said Pwyll, “both to-day and every day while the feast shall last.”", "퓔이 말했다. \"오늘도, 그리고 잔치가 계속되는 날마다 그리하겠소.\""], ["So Pwyll arose, and he caused silence to be proclaimed, and desired all the suitors and minstrels to show and to point out what gifts they desired.", "그리하여 퓔은 일어나, 정숙히 하라고 선포하게 하고, 모든 청원자와 음유시인에게 그들이 바라는 선물을 보이고 가리키도록 청했다."], ["And this being done, the feast went on, and he denied no one while it lasted.", "이것이 행해지자 잔치는 계속되었고, 그는 잔치가 이어지는 동안 누구도 거절하지 않았다."], ["And when the feast was ended, Pwyll said unto Heveydd, “My lord, with thy permission, I will set out for Dyved to-morrow.”", "잔치가 끝나자 퓔은 헤베이드에게 말했다. \"나의 주군이시여, 허락하신다면, 저는 내일 디베드로 떠나겠습니다.\""], ["“Certainly,”", "\"물론이오.\""], ["said Heveydd; “may Heaven prosper thee!", "헤베이드가 말했다. \"하늘이 그대를 번성케 하기를!"], ["Fix also a time when Rhiannon shall follow thee.”", "리아논이 그대를 뒤따를 때도 정하시오.\" \"하늘에 맹세코.\""], ["“By Heaven,”", "퓔이 말했다."], ["said Pwyll, “we will go hence together.”", "\"우리는 함께 이곳을 떠날 것입니다.\""], ["“Willest thou this, lord?”", "\"그것을 원하시오, 주군이여?\" 헤베이드가 말했다."], ["said Heveydd. “Yes, lord,”", "\"그렇습니다, 주군이시여.\""], ["answered Pwyll.", "퓔이 대답했다."]], [["And the next day they set forward towards Dyved, and journeyed to the palace of Narberth, where a feast was made ready for them.", "이튿날 그들은 디베드를 향해 길을 나서, 나르베르스의 궁전으로 여정을 떠났으니, 그곳에는 그들을 위한 잔치가 마련되어 있었다."], ["And there came to them great numbers of the chief men and the most noble ladies of the land, and of these there were none to whom Rhiannon did not give some rich gift, either a bracelet, or a ring, or a precious stone.", "그리고 그들에게로 그 땅의 으뜸가는 사람들과 가장 고귀한 귀부인들이 무수히 찾아왔고, 그들 가운데 리아논이 팔찌든 반지든 보석이든 값진 선물을 주지 않은 이가 하나도 없었다."], ["And they ruled the land prosperously that year and the next.", "그리하여 그들은 그해와 그다음 해에 그 땅을 번영 속에 다스렸다."]], [["————", "————"]], [["# BRANWEN, THE DAUGHTER OF LLYR", "# BRANWEN, THE DAUGHTER OF LLYR"]], [["BENDIGEID VRAN, the son of Llyr, was the crowned king of this island, and he was exalted from the crown of London.", "흘리르의 아들 벤디게이드 브란은 이 섬의 왕관을 쓴 임금이었으며, 런던의 왕관으로부터 높여진 이였다."], ["And one afternoon he was at Harlech, in Ardudwy, at his court; and he sat upon the rock of Harlech, looking over the sea.", "어느 날 오후 그는 아르두드위의 할레크에 있는 자신의 궁정에 머물고 있었다. 그는 할레크의 바위 위에 앉아 바다를 바라보았다."], ["And with him were his brother, Manawyddan, the son of Llyr, and his brothers by the mother’s side, Nissyen and Evnissyen, and many nobles likewise, as was fitting to see around a king.", "그의 곁에는 형제인 흘리르의 아들 마나위단과, 어머니 쪽 형제인 니시엔과 에브니시엔, 그리고 임금 주위에 둘러 있기 마땅한 여러 귀족이 함께 있었다."], ["His two brothers by the mother’s side were the sons of Euroswydd, and one of these youths was a good youth, and of gentle nature, and would make peace between his kindred, and cause his family to be friends when their wrath was at the highest, and this one was Nissyen; but the other would cause strife between his two brothers when they were most at peace.", "어머니 쪽 두 형제는 에우로스위드의 아들들이었는데, 그 가운데 한 젊은이는 선량하고 온순한 성품을 지녀, 친족 사이에 화해를 이루고, 분노가 절정에 달했을 때에도 가족을 서로 벗으로 만드는 이였으니, 이가 곧 니시엔이었다. 그러나 다른 하나는 두 형제가 가장 평화로울 때에 그들 사이에 다툼을 일으키는 이였다."], ["And as they sat thus they beheld thirteen ships coming from the south of Ireland, and making towards them; and they came with a swift motion, the wind being behind them; and they neared them rapidly.", "이렇게 앉아 있던 중, 그들은 아일랜드 남쪽에서 열세 척의 배가 자신들을 향해 오는 것을 보았다. 배들은 바람을 등에 업고 빠른 속도로 다가왔고, 순식간에 가까워졌다."], ["“I see ships afar,”", "\"멀리 배가 보이는구나.\""], ["said the king, “coming swiftly towards the land.", "임금이 말했다. \"빠르게 뭍을 향해 오고 있다."], ["Command the men of the court that they equip themselves, and go and learn their intent.”", "궁정의 사람들에게 명하여 채비를 갖추고 가서 저들의 뜻을 알아 오게 하라.\""], ["So the men equipped themselves, and went down towards them.", "그리하여 사람들이 채비를 갖추고 그들을 향해 내려갔다."], ["And when they saw the ships near, certain were they that they had never seen ships better furnished.", "배가 가까이 온 것을 보았을 때, 그들은 그토록 잘 단장된 배는 일찍이 본 적이 없다고 확신했다."], ["Beautiful flags of satin were upon them.", "배에는 아름다운 비단 깃발들이 걸려 있었다."], ["And, behold, one of the ships outstripped the others, and they saw a shield lifted up above the side of the ship, and the point of the shield was upwards, in token of peace.", "그런데 보라, 배 한 척이 다른 배들을 앞질렀고, 그들은 방패가 뱃전 위로 들어 올려진 것을, 그 방패의 끝이 위를 향해 평화의 표징을 이루고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["And the men drew near, that they might hold converse.", "사람들이 이야기를 나누고자 가까이 다가갔다."], ["Then they put out boats, and came toward the land.", "그러자 저쪽에서 작은 배들을 내려 뭍을 향해 다가왔다."], ["And they saluted the king.", "그들은 임금에게 인사를 올렸다."], ["Now the king could hear them from the place where he was upon the rock above their heads.", "임금은 그들의 머리 위 바위에 있던 자리에서 그들의 말을 들을 수 있었다. \"하늘이 그대들을 번성케 하기를,\""], ["“Heaven prosper you,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “and be ye welcome!", "\"그리고 환영하노라!"], ["To whom do these ships belong, and who is the chief amongst you?”", "이 배들은 누구의 것이며, 그대들 가운데 우두머리는 누구인가?\""], ["“Lord,”", "\"주군이시여,\""], ["said they, “Matholch, king of Ireland, is here, and these ships belong to him.”", "그들이 말했다. \"아일랜드의 임금 마톨크가 여기 계시며, 이 배들은 그분의 것입니다.\""], ["“Wherefore comes he?”", "\"어찌하여 오는가?\""], ["asked the king, “and will he come to the land?”", "임금이 물었다. \"그리고 뭍에 오르려는가?\""], ["“He is a suitor unto thee, lord,”", "\"그분은 주군께 구혼하러 오신 것이옵니다.\""], ["said they, “and he will not land unless he have his boon.”", "그들이 말했다. \"그리고 청을 들어주시지 않으면 뭍에 오르지 않으실 것입니다.\""], ["“And what may that be?”", "\"그것이 무엇인가?\""], ["inquired the king.", "임금이 물었다."], ["“He desires to ally himself, lord, with thee,”", "\"그분은 주군과 동맹을 맺기를 바라십니다.\" 그들이 말했다."], ["said they, “and he comes to ask Branwen, the daughter of Llyr, that, if it seem well to thee, the Island of the Mighty[64] may be leagued with Ireland, and both become more powerful.”", "\"그리고 흘리르의 딸 브란웬을 청하러 오셨으니, 주군께서 좋게 여기신다면 강대한 자들의 섬이 아일랜드와 동맹을 이루어 두 나라가 모두 더욱 강성해지기를 바라십니다.\" \"참으로,\""], ["“Verily,”", "임금이 말했다."], ["said he, “let him come to land, and we will take counsel thereupon.”", "\"그를 뭍에 오르게 하라. 그 일에 관하여 우리가 의논하겠노라.\""], ["And this answer was brought to Matholch.", "이 대답이 마톨크에게 전해졌다."], ["“I will go willingly,”", "\"기꺼이 가겠노라.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["So he landed, and they received him joyfully; and great was the throng in the palace that night, between his hosts and those of the court; and next day they took counsel, and they resolved to bestow Branwen upon Matholch.", "그리하여 그가 뭍에 올랐고, 사람들이 그를 기쁘게 맞이했다. 그날 밤 궁정에는 그의 무리와 궁정의 무리가 어울려 큰 인파가 모였다."], ["Now she was one of the three chief ladies of this island, and she was the fairest damsel in the world.", "이튿날 그들은 의논하였고, 브란웬을 마톨크에게 주기로 결의하였다. 그녀는 이 섬의 으뜸가는 세 귀부인 가운데 하나였으며, 세상에서 가장 어여쁜 처녀였다."]], [["And they fixed upon Aberfraw as the place where she should become his bride.", "그들은 그녀가 그의 신부가 될 곳으로 아베르프라우를 정했다."], ["And they went thence, and towards Aberfraw the hosts proceeded, Matholch and his host in their ships, Bendigeid Vran and his host by land, until they came to Aberfraw.", "그들은 그곳을 떠나 아베르프라우를 향해 무리를 이끌고 나아갔으니, 마톨크와 그의 무리는 배를 타고, 벤디게이드 브란과 그의 무리는 뭍으로 가, 마침내 아베르프라우에 이르렀다."], ["And at Aberfraw they began the feast, and sat down.", "아베르프라우에서 그들은 잔치를 시작하고 자리에 앉았다."], ["And thus sat they: the king of the Island of the Mighty and Manawyddan, the son of Llyr, on one side, and Matholch on the other side, and Branwen, the daughter of Llyr, beside him.", "그들이 앉은 모습은 이러하였다. 강대한 자들의 섬의 임금과 흘리르의 아들 마나위단이 한쪽에, 마톨크가 다른 쪽에, 그리고 흘리르의 딸 브란웬이 그의 곁에 앉았다."], ["And they were not within a house, but under tents.", "그들은 집 안이 아니라 천막 아래 있었다."], ["No house could ever contain Bendigeid Vran.", "어떤 집도 벤디게이드 브란을 담을 수 없었기 때문이다."], ["And they began the banquet, and caroused and discoursed.", "그들은 향연을 시작하여, 마시고 떠들며 이야기를 나누었다."], ["And when it was more pleasing to them to sleep than to carouse, they went to rest, and Branwen became Matholch’s bride.", "그리고 흥겨워하기보다 잠드는 것이 더 즐겁게 여겨지자 그들은 잠자리에 들었고, 브란웬은 마톨크의 신부가 되었다."]], [["And next day they arose, and all they of the court, and the officers began to equip, and to range the horses and the attendants, and they ranged them in order as far as the sea.", "이튿날 그들이 일어났고, 궁정의 모든 사람과 관리들이 채비를 갖추기 시작하여 말과 시종들을 정렬하였으니, 바다에 이르기까지 줄지어 세웠다."]], [["And, behold, one day Evnissyen, the quarrelsome man, of whom it is spoken above, came by chance into the place where the horses of Matholch were, and asked whose horses they might be.", "그런데 보라, 어느 날 앞서 말한 그 다툼을 좋아하는 사내 에브니시엔이 우연히 마톨크의 말들이 있는 곳에 와서, 이 말들이 누구의 것이냐고 물었다."], ["“They are the horses of Matholch, king of Ireland, who is married to Branwen, thy sister; his horses are they.”", "\"그대의 누이 브란웬과 혼인한 아일랜드의 임금 마톨크의 말들입니다. 그분의 말들이지요.\""], ["“And is it thus they have done with a maiden such as she, and moreover my sister, bestowing her without my consent?", "\"이런 식으로 그녀 같은 처녀를, 더구나 내 누이를, 내 동의도 없이 내주었단 말인가?"], ["They could have offered no greater insult to me than this,”", "이보다 더 큰 모욕을 내게 줄 수는 없었으리라.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["And thereupon he rushed under the horses, and cut off their lips at the teeth, and their ears close to their heads, and their tails close to their backs; and he disfigured the horses, and rendered them useless.", "그러고는 그가 말들 밑으로 달려들어, 입술을 이빨 있는 데까지 잘라 내고, 귀를 머리에 닿도록 베어 내고, 꼬리를 등에 닿도록 잘라 내었다. 그리하여 그가 말들을 흉하게 만들어 쓸모없게 하였다."]], [["And they came with these tidings unto Matholch, saying that the horses were disfigured and injured, so that not one of them could ever be of any use again.", "사람들이 이 소식을 마톨크에게 가져와, 말들이 흉하게 망가져 다시는 그 가운데 한 마리도 쓸모가 없게 되었다고 아뢰었다. \"참으로, 주군이시여,\""], ["“Verily, lord,”", "한 사람이 말했다."], ["said one, “it was an insult unto thee, and as such was it meant.”", "\"이는 주군께 가하는 모욕이며, 그러한 뜻으로 행한 것입니다.\""], ["“Of a truth, it is a marvel to me that, if they desire to insult me, they should have given me a maiden of such high rank, and so much beloved of her kindred, as they have done.”", "\"참으로 내게 놀라운 일이로다. 만약 저들이 나를 모욕하려 하였다면, 어찌하여 저토록 지체 높고 친족들에게 그토록 사랑받는 처녀를 내게 주었단 말인가.\""], ["“Lord,”", "\"주군이시여,\""], ["said another, “thou seest that thus it is, and there is nothing for thee to do but to go to thy ships.”", "다른 사람이 말했다. \"사정이 이러함을 보셨으니, 주군께서 하실 일은 배로 돌아가는 것밖에 없습니다.\""], ["And thereupon towards his ships he set out.", "그러자 그는 자신의 배를 향해 떠났다."]], [["And tidings came to Bendigeid Vran that Matholch was quitting the court without asking leave, and messengers were sent to inquire of him wherefore he did so.", "마톨크가 작별을 청하지도 않고 궁정을 떠난다는 소식이 벤디게이드 브란에게 전해졌고, 어찌하여 그러하느냐고 그에게 묻고자 사신들이 보내졌다."], ["And the messengers that went were Iddic, the son of Anarawd, and Heveyd Hir.", "가게 된 사신은 아나라우드의 아들 이디크와 헤베이드 히르였다."], ["And these overtook him, and asked of him what he designed to do, and wherefore he went forth.", "이들이 그를 따라잡아, 무엇을 하려는지, 어찌하여 떠나는지를 물었다. \"참으로,\""], ["“Of a truth,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “if I had known, I had not come hither.", "\"내가 알았더라면 이곳에 오지 않았을 것이오."], ["I have been altogether insulted; no one had ever worse treatment than I have had here.”", "나는 온전히 모욕을 당하였소. 여기서 내가 받은 것보다 더 나쁜 대접을 받은 이는 일찍이 없었소.\""], ["“Truly, lord, it was not the will of any that are of the court,”", "\"참으로, 주군이시여, 궁정에 속한 그 누구의 뜻도, 의회에 속한 그 누구의 뜻도, 주군께서 이러한 모욕을 받게 하려는 것이 아니었습니다.\" 그들이 말했다."], ["said they, “nor of any that are of the council, that thou shouldst have received this insult; and as thou hast been insulted, the dishonor is greater unto Bendigeid Vran than unto thee.”", "\"그리고 주군께서 모욕을 당하셨으니, 그 불명예는 주군보다 벤디게이드 브란께 더 큰 것입니다.\" \"참으로,\""], ["“Verily,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “I think so.", "\"나도 그리 생각하오."], ["Nevertheless, he cannot recall the insult.”", "그렇다 하더라도 그가 그 모욕을 되돌릴 수는 없소.\""], ["These men returned with that answer to the place where Bendigeid Vran was, and they told him what reply Matholch had given them.", "이 사람들은 그 대답을 가지고 벤디게이드 브란이 있는 곳으로 돌아와, 마톨크가 그들에게 준 답을 아뢰었다. \"참으로,\""], ["“Truly,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “there are no means by which we may prevent his going away at enmity with us that we will not take.”", "\"그가 우리와 원수가 되어 떠나는 것을 막을 수단이 있다면, 우리가 쓰지 않을 방법은 없다.\" \"그렇다면, 주군이시여,\""], ["“Well, lord,”", "그들이 말했다."], ["said they, “send after him another embassy.”", "\"그에게 또 다른 사절을 보내십시오.\""], ["“I will do so,”", "\"그리하겠노라.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Arise, Manawyddan, son of Llyr, and Heveyd Hir, and go after him, and tell him that he shall have a sound horse for every one that has been injured.", "\"일어서라, 흘리르의 아들 마나위단과 헤베이드 히르여, 그를 따라가 그에게 전하라. 망가진 말 한 마리마다 성한 말을 한 마리씩 줄 것이라고."], ["And beside that, as an atonement for the insult, he shall have a staff of silver as large and as tall as himself, and a plate of gold of the breadth of his face.", "그뿐만 아니라 모욕에 대한 보상으로, 그 자신만큼 크고 높은 은 지팡이와, 그의 얼굴 너비만 한 황금 판을 줄 것이라고."], ["And show unto him who it was that did this, and that it was done against my will; but that he who did it is my brother, and therefore it would be hard for me to put him to death.", "그리고 누가 이 일을 저질렀는지, 그것이 내 뜻에 반하여 행해진 일임을 그에게 보여 주라. 다만 그 일을 저지른 자는 내 형제이니, 그를 죽이기는 내게 어려운 일이라고."], ["And let him come and meet me,”", "그러니 그에게 와서 나를 만나게 하라.\""], ["said he, “and we will make peace in any way he may desire.”", "그가 말했다. \"그가 바라는 어떤 방식으로든 우리가 화평을 이루리라.\""]], [["The embassy went after Matholch, and told him all these sayings in a friendly manner; and he listened thereunto. “Men,”", "사절은 마톨크를 따라가, 이 모든 말을 우호적으로 전하였고, 그는 그것에 귀를 기울였다."], ["said he, “I will take counsel.”", "\"여러분,\" 그가 말했다."], ["So to the council he went.", "\"내가 의논해 보겠소.\""], ["And in the council they considered that, if they should refuse this, they were likely to have more shame rather than to obtain so great an atonement.", "그리하여 그가 의회로 갔다. 의회에서 그들은, 만약 이를 거절한다면 그토록 큰 보상을 얻기는커녕 오히려 더 큰 수치를 당하기 쉬우리라고 헤아렸다."], ["They resolved, therefore, to accept it, and they returned to the court in peace.", "그리하여 그들은 이를 받아들이기로 결의하고, 화평하게 궁정으로 돌아왔다."]], [["Then the pavilions and the tents were set in order, after the fashion of a hall; and they went to meat, and as they had sat at the beginning of the feast so sat they there.", "이윽고 정자와 천막이 회당 모양으로 정돈되었다. 그들은 식사하러 갔고, 잔치를 처음 시작했을 때 앉았던 그대로 거기에 앉았다."], ["And Matholch and Bendigeid Vran began to discourse; and, behold, it seemed to Bendigeid Vran, while they talked, that Matholch was not so cheerful as he had been before.", "마톨크와 벤디게이드 브란이 이야기를 나누기 시작했다. 그런데 보라, 이야기를 나누는 동안 벤디게이드 브란에게는 마톨크가 전처럼 그리 유쾌해 보이지 않았다."], ["And he thought that the chieftain might be sad because of the smallness of the atonement which he had for the wrong that had been done him.", "그는 그 족장이 자신이 당한 잘못에 대한 보상이 적어 슬퍼하는 것이 아닐까 생각했다."], ["“O man,”", "\"오, 그대여,\""], ["said Bendigeid Vran, “thou dost not discourse to-night so cheerfully as thou wast wont.", "벤디게이드 브란이 말했다. \"그대는 오늘 밤 평소처럼 유쾌하게 이야기하지 않는구려."], ["And if it be because of the smallness of the atonement, thou shalt add thereunto whatsoever thou mayest choose, and to-morrow I will pay thee for the horses.”", "만약 그것이 보상이 적은 까닭이라면, 그대가 무엇이든 고르는 대로 거기에 더할 것이며, 내일 내가 말 값을 치르리다.\" \"주군이시여,\""], ["“Lord,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “Heaven reward thee!”", "\"하늘이 주군께 보답하시기를!\""], ["“And I will enhance the atonement,”", "\"그리고 내가 보상을 더하겠소.\""], ["said Bendigeid Vran, “for I will give unto thee a caldron, the property of which is, that if one of thy men be slain to-day, and be cast therein, to-morrow he will be as well as ever he was at the best, except that he will not regain his speech.”", "벤디게이드 브란이 말했다. \"내가 그대에게 솥을 하나 주겠으니, 그 솥의 성질인즉, 그대의 사람 가운데 하나가 오늘 죽어 그 안에 던져지면, 이튿날 그는 가장 좋았을 때처럼 멀쩡해질 것이라오."], ["And thereupon he gave him great thanks, and very joyful was he for that cause.", "다만 말을 되찾지는 못할 것이오.\" 그러자 그가 크게 감사하였고, 그 일로 매우 기뻐하였다."]], [["That night they continued to discourse as much as they would, and had minstrelsy and carousing; and when it was more pleasant to them to sleep than to sit longer, they went to rest.", "그날 밤 그들은 바라는 만큼 이야기를 이어 나갔으며, 음유의 가락과 흥겨운 술자리를 즐겼다. 그리고 더 오래 앉아 있기보다 잠드는 것이 더 즐겁게 여겨지자 그들은 잠자리에 들었다."], ["And thus was the banquet carried on with joyousness; and when it was finished, Matholch journeyed towards Ireland, and Branwen with him; and they went from Aber Menei with thirteen ships, and came to Ireland.", "이렇게 향연이 기쁨 속에 치러졌고, 그것이 끝나자 마톨크는 브란웬과 함께 아일랜드를 향해 길을 떠났다. 그들은 아베르 메네이에서 열세 척의 배로 떠나 아일랜드에 이르렀다."], ["And in Ireland was there great joy because of their coming.", "아일랜드에서는 그들이 온 것을 두고 큰 기쁨이 있었다."], ["And not one great man nor noble lady visited Branwen unto whom she gave not either a clasp or a ring, or a royal jewel to keep, such as it was honorable to be seen departing with.", "어떤 위대한 인물도, 어떤 고귀한 부인도 브란웬을 찾아오면, 그녀는 그들에게 떠나면서 지니고 가기에 떳떳할 만한 걸쇠나 반지나 왕가의 보석을 주지 않은 이가 없었다."], ["And in these things she spent that year in much renown, and she passed her time pleasantly, enjoying honor and friendship.", "이러한 일들로 그녀는 그해를 큰 명성 속에 보냈으며, 영예와 우정을 누리며 즐겁게 시간을 보냈다."], ["And in due time a son was born unto her, and the name that they gave him was Gwern, the son of Matholch, and they put the boy out to be nursed in a place where were the best men of Ireland.", "그리고 때가 이르러 그녀에게 아들이 태어났으니, 그들이 그에게 지어 준 이름은 마톨크의 아들 그웨른이었다. 그들은 그 아이를 아일랜드에서 가장 훌륭한 이들이 있는 곳에 내보내 양육하게 하였다."]], [["And, behold, in the second year a tumult arose in Ireland, on account of the insult which Matholch had received in Wales, and the payment made him for his horses.", "그런데 보라, 두 번째 해에 마톨크가 웨일스에서 받은 모욕과, 그의 말 값으로 받은 보상을 두고 아일랜드에 소동이 일어났다."], ["And his foster-brothers, and such as were nearest to him, blamed him openly for that matter.", "그의 양형제들과 그에게 가장 가까운 자들이 그 일로 공공연히 그를 비난하였다."], ["And he might have no peace by reason of the tumult, until they should revenge upon him this disgrace.", "그리고 그들이 그 치욕을 그에게 복수해 줄 때까지 그는 소동으로 인해 평안을 얻을 수 없었다."], ["And the vengeance which they took was to drive away Branwen from the same chamber with him, and to make her cook for the court; and they caused the butcher, after he had cut up the meat, to come to her and give her every day a blow on the ear; and such they made her punishment.", "그들이 행한 복수란, 브란웬을 그와 같은 방에서 쫓아내어 궁정을 위해 요리하게 하는 것이었고, 또 고기를 다 자른 백정으로 하여금 그녀에게 와서 날마다 그녀의 귀를 한 대씩 때리게 하는 것이었다. 그들은 그녀에게 그런 벌을 내렸다."]], [["“Verily, lord,”", "\"참으로, 주군이시여,\""], ["said his men to Matholch, “forbid now the ships and the ferry-boats, and the coracles, that they go not into Wales, and such as come over from Wales hither, imprison them, that they go not back for this thing to be known there.”", "그의 사람들이 마톨크에게 말했다. \"이제 배와 나룻배와 작은 가죽배가 웨일스로 가지 못하도록 금하시고, 웨일스에서 이리로 건너오는 자들은 가두어, 이 일이 그곳에 알려지지 않도록 그들이 되돌아가지 못하게 하소서.\""], ["And he did so; and it was thus for no less than three years.", "그가 그리하였고, 이는 자그마치 삼 년 동안이나 그러하였다."]], [["And Branwen reared a starling in the cover of the kneading-trough, and she taught it to speak, and she taught the bird what manner of man her brother was.", "브란웬은 반죽통 뚜껑 안에서 찌르레기 한 마리를 길러, 그것에게 말하는 법을 가르치고, 자기 오라비가 어떤 사람인지를 그 새에게 일러 주었다."], ["And she wrote a letter of her woes, and the despite with which she was treated, and she bound the letter to the root of the bird’s wing, and sent it toward Wales.", "그리고 그녀는 자신의 비통함과 자신이 받은 멸시를 적은 편지를 써서, 그 편지를 새의 날개 뿌리에 매어 웨일스를 향해 보냈다."], ["And the bird came to that island; and one day it found Bendigeid Vran at Caer Seiont in Arvon, conferring there, and it alighted upon his shoulder, and ruffled its feathers, so that the letter was seen, and they knew that the bird had been reared in a domestic manner.", "새가 그 섬에 이르렀고, 어느 날 아르본의 카에르 세이온트에서 의논하고 있던 벤디게이드 브란을 발견하여, 그의 어깨에 내려앉아 깃털을 곤두세우니 편지가 보였고, 사람들은 그 새가 사람 손에 길러졌음을 알았다."]], [["Then Bendigeid Vran took the letter and looked upon it.", "이윽고 벤디게이드 브란이 편지를 받아 들여다보았다."], ["And when he had read the letter, he grieved exceedingly at the tidings of Branwen’s woes.", "편지를 다 읽고 나자 그는 브란웬의 비통한 소식에 몹시 슬퍼하였다."], ["And immediately he began sending messengers to summon the island together.", "그리고 즉시 그는 섬을 한데 소집하고자 사신들을 보내기 시작했다."], ["And he caused seven-score and four of his chief men to come unto him, and he complained to them of the grief that his sister endured.", "그는 자신의 으뜸가는 사람 백마흔넷을 자기에게로 오게 하여, 누이가 견디고 있는 슬픔을 그들에게 호소하였다."], ["So they took counsel.", "그리하여 그들이 의논하였다."], ["And in the counsel they resolved to go to Ireland, and to leave seven men as princes at home, and Caradoc,[65] the son of Bran, as the chief of them.", "의논 끝에 그들은 아일랜드로 가기로, 그리고 일곱 사람을 제후로 남겨 두기로, 그 우두머리로 브란의 아들 카라도크를 두기로 결의하였다."]], [["Bendigeid Vran, with the host of which we spoke, sailed towards Ireland; and it was not far across the sea, and he came to shoal water.", "벤디게이드 브란은 우리가 앞서 말한 무리와 함께 아일랜드를 향해 항해하였다. 바다를 건너는 거리는 멀지 않아, 그는 얕은 물에 이르렀다."], ["Now the swine-herds of Matholch were upon the sea-shore, and they came to Matholch.", "그때 마톨크의 돼지치기들이 바닷가에 있다가 마톨크에게로 왔다. \"주군이시여,\""], ["“Lord,”", "그들이 말했다."], ["said they, “greeting be unto thee.”", "\"문안 올립니다.\" \"하늘이 그대들을 보호하시기를!\""], ["“Heaven protect you!”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he; “have you any news?”", "\"무슨 소식이라도 있느냐?\""], ["“Lord,”", "\"주군이시여,\""], ["said they, “we have marvellous news.", "그들이 말했다. \"놀라운 소식이 있사옵니다."], ["A wood have we seen upon the sea, in a place where we never yet saw a single tree.”", "일찍이 나무 한 그루 본 적 없는 바다 위에서 숲을 보았나이다.\" \"이는 참으로 놀라운 일이로다.\""], ["“This is indeed a marvel,”", "그가 말했다. \"그 밖에 또 본 것이 있느냐?\""], ["said he; “saw you aught else?”", "\"주군이시여, 보았나이다.\""], ["“We saw, lord,”", "그들이 말했다."], ["said they, “a vast mountain beside the wood, which moved, and there was a lofty ridge on the top of the mountain, and a lake on each side of the ridge.", "\"숲 곁에 거대한 산이 있어 움직이는데, 그 산 꼭대기에는 높은 능선이 있고, 그 능선 양쪽에 호수가 하나씩 있었습니다."], ["And the wood and the mountain, and all these things, moved.”", "그리고 숲과 산과 이 모든 것이 움직였나이다.\""], ["“Verily,”", "\"참으로,\""], ["said he, “there is none who can know aught concerning this unless it be Branwen.”", "그가 말했다. \"이에 관하여 무엇이든 알 수 있는 이는 브란웬밖에 없으리라.\""]], [["Messengers then went unto Branwen. “Lady,”", "이윽고 사신들이 브란웬에게로 갔다."], ["said they, “what thinkest thou that this is?”", "\"부인이시여,\" 그들이 말했다."], ["“The men of the Island of the Mighty, who have come hither on hearing of my ill-treatment and of my woes.”", "\"이것이 무엇이라 생각하십니까?\" \"내가 받은 학대와 내 비통함을 듣고 이리로 온 강대한 자들의 섬의 사람들이오.\""], ["“What is the forest that is seen upon the sea?”", "\"바다 위에 보이는 그 숲은 무엇입니까?\""], ["asked they.", "그들이 물었다."], ["“The yards and the masts of ships,”", "\"배들의 활대와 돛대들이오.\""], ["she answered.", "그녀가 대답했다."], ["“Alas!”", "\"아아!\""], ["said they; “what is the mountain that is seen by the side of the ships?”", "그들이 말했다. \"배 곁에 보이는 그 산은 무엇입니까?\""], ["“Bendigeid Vran, my brother,”", "\"내 오라비 벤디게이드 브란이오.\""], ["she replied, “coming to shoal water, and he is wading to the land.”", "그녀가 대답했다. \"얕은 물에 이르러 뭍을 향해 걸어오고 있는 것이라오.\""], ["“What is the lofty ridge, with the lake on each side thereof?”", "\"양쪽에 호수가 하나씩 있는 그 높은 능선은 무엇입니까?\""], ["“On looking towards this island he is wroth, and his two eyes on each side of his nose are the two lakes on each side of the ridge.”", "\"이 섬을 바라보며 그가 노여워하고 있으니, 코 양쪽에 있는 그의 두 눈이 곧 능선 양쪽의 두 호수라오.\""]], [["The warriors and chief men of Ireland were brought together in haste, and they took counsel. “Lord,”", "아일랜드의 전사들과 으뜸가는 인물들이 황급히 한데 모여 의논하였다."], ["said the neighbors unto Matholch, “there is no other counsel than this alone.", "\"주군이시여,\" 이웃들이 마톨크에게 말했다."], ["Thou shalt give the kingdom to Gwern, the son of Branwen his sister, as a compensation for the wrong and despite that have been done unto Branwen.", "\"이 한 가지 말고는 다른 방도가 없습니다. 브란웬에게 행해진 잘못과 멸시에 대한 보상으로, 그분의 누이 브란웬의 아들 그웨른에게 왕국을 주소서."], ["And he will make peace with thee.”", "그리하면 그분이 주군과 화평하실 것입니다.\""], ["And in the council it was resolved that this message should be sent to Bendigeid Vran, lest the country should be destroyed.", "의논 끝에 나라가 멸망하지 않도록 이 전갈을 벤디게이드 브란에게 보내기로 결의하였다."], ["And this peace was made.", "그리하여 이 화평이 이루어졌다."], ["And Matholch caused a great house to be built for Bendigeid Vran, and his host.", "마톨크는 벤디게이드 브란과 그의 무리를 위해 큰 집을 짓게 하였다."], ["Thereupon came the hosts into the house.", "그러자 무리가 그 집 안으로 들어왔다."], ["The men of the island of Ireland entered the house on the one side, and the men of the Island of the Mighty on the other.", "아일랜드 섬의 사람들이 한쪽으로 집에 들어왔고, 강대한 자들의 섬의 사람들이 다른 쪽으로 들어왔다."], ["And as soon as they had sat down, there was concord between them; and the sovereignty was conferred upon the boy.", "그리고 그들이 자리에 앉자마자 그들 사이에 화목이 이루어졌으며, 주권이 그 소년에게 주어졌다."], ["When the peace was concluded, Bendigeid Vran called the boy unto him, and from Bendigeid Vran the boy went unto Manawyddan; and he was beloved by all that beheld him.", "화평이 맺어지자 벤디게이드 브란이 그 소년을 자기에게로 불렀고, 벤디게이드 브란에게서 소년은 마나위단에게로 갔다. 그를 본 모든 이가 그를 사랑하였다."], ["And from Manawyddan the boy was called by Nissyen, the son of Euroswydd, and the boy went unto him lovingly.", "마나위단에게서 그 소년을 에우로스위드의 아들 니시엔이 불렀고, 소년은 다정하게 그에게로 갔다."], ["“Wherefore,”", "\"어찌하여,\""], ["said Evnissyen, “comes not my nephew, the son of my sister, unto me?", "에브니시엔이 말했다. \"내 조카, 내 누이의 아들이 내게로 오지 않는가?"], ["Though he were not king of Ireland, yet willingly would I fondle the boy.”", "그가 아일랜드의 임금이 아니라 할지라도, 나는 기꺼이 그 아이를 어루만지고 싶다.\""], ["“Cheerfully let him go to thee,”", "\"기꺼이 그를 그대에게 가게 하라.\""], ["said Bendigeid Vran; and the boy went unto him cheerfully.", "벤디게이드 브란이 말했다. 소년이 기꺼이 그에게로 갔다."], ["“By my confession to Heaven,”", "\"하늘께 고백하건대,\""], ["said Evnissyen in his heart, “unthought of is the slaughter that I will this instant commit.”", "에브니시엔이 마음속으로 말했다. \"내가 지금 이 순간 저지를 살육을 아무도 생각지 못하리라.\""]], [["Then he arose and took up the boy, and before any one in the house could seize hold of him he thrust the boy headlong into the blazing fire.", "그러고는 그가 일어나 소년을 안아 들었고, 집 안의 누구도 그를 붙잡기 전에 소년을 거꾸로 타오르는 불 속에 던져 넣었다."], ["And when Branwen saw her son burning in the fire, she strove to leap into the fire also, from the place where she sat between her two brothers.", "브란웬은 자기 아들이 불 속에서 타는 것을 보자, 두 오라비 사이에 앉아 있던 자리에서 자신도 불 속으로 뛰어들려 하였다."], ["But Bendigeid Vran grasped her with one hand, and his shield with the other.", "그러나 벤디게이드 브란이 한 손으로는 그녀를, 다른 손으로는 자신의 방패를 붙들었다."], ["Then they all hurried about the house, and never was there made so great a tumult by any host in one house as was made by them, as each man armed himself.", "이윽고 그들 모두가 집 안을 분주히 돌아다녔으니, 저마다 무장을 갖추는 가운데 어떤 무리도 한 집 안에서 그들이 일으킨 것만큼 큰 소동을 일으킨 적이 없었다."], ["And while they all sought their arms Bendigeid Vran supported Branwen between his shield and his shoulder.", "그들 모두가 무기를 찾는 동안 벤디게이드 브란은 자기 방패와 어깨 사이에 브란웬을 받쳐 두었다."], ["And they fought.", "그리하여 그들이 싸웠다."]], [["Then the Irish kindled a fire under the caldron of renovation, and they cast the dead bodies into the caldron until it was full; and the next day they came forth fighting men, as good as before, except that they were not able to speak.", "이윽고 아일랜드인들이 되살림의 솥 아래에 불을 지피고, 솥이 가득 찰 때까지 시신들을 그 안에 던져 넣었다. 그러자 이튿날 그들이 전과 다름없이 훌륭한 싸움꾼이 되어 나왔으니, 다만 말을 하지 못할 따름이었다."], ["Then when Evnissyen saw the dead bodies of the men of the Island of the Mighty nowhere resuscitated, he said in his heart, “Alas!", "에브니시엔은 강대한 자들의 섬의 사람들의 시신이 어디에서도 되살아나지 못하는 것을 보고, 마음속으로 말했다."], ["woe is me, that I should have been the cause of bringing the men of the Island of the Mighty into so great a strait.", "\"아아! 비통하도다, 내가 강대한 자들의 섬의 사람들을 이토록 곤경에 빠뜨린 장본인이 되다니."], ["Evil betide me if I find not a deliverance therefrom.”", "내가 거기서 벗어날 길을 찾지 못한다면 화가 내게 미치리라.\""], ["And he cast himself among the dead bodies of the Irish; and two unshod Irishmen came to him, and, taking him to be one of the Irish, flung him into the caldron.", "그러고는 그가 아일랜드인들의 시신 사이에 몸을 던졌다. 맨발의 아일랜드인 둘이 그에게 와서, 그를 아일랜드인 가운데 하나로 여기고 솥 안에 던져 넣었다."], ["And he stretched himself out in the caldron, so that he rent the caldron into four pieces, and burst his own heart also.", "그가 솥 안에서 몸을 쭉 뻗으니, 솥이 네 조각으로 갈라졌고, 그 자신의 심장 또한 터졌다."]], [["In consequence of this, the men of the Island of the Mighty obtained such success as they had; but they were not victorious, for only seven men of them all escaped, and Bendigeid Vran himself was wounded in the foot with a poisoned dart.", "이 일로 인하여 강대한 자들의 섬의 사람들이 거둔 성과는 그러하였으나, 그들은 승리하지 못하였다. 그들 모두 가운데 오직 일곱 사람만이 빠져나왔고, 벤디게이드 브란 자신은 독화살에 발을 맞아 부상을 입었기 때문이다."], ["Now the men that escaped were Pryderi, Manawyddan, Taliesin, and four others.", "그때 빠져나온 사람들은 프리데리, 마나위단, 탈리에신, 그리고 다른 네 사람이었다."]], [["And Bendigeid Vran commanded them that they should cut off his head.", "벤디게이드 브란은 그들에게 자신의 머리를 베라고 명하였다."], ["“And take you my head,”", "\"그리고 내 머리를 가지고,\""], ["said he, “and bear it even unto the White Mount in London, and bury it there with the face towards France.", "그가 말했다. \"런던의 화이트 마운트까지 가지고 가서, 얼굴이 프랑스를 향하도록 거기에 묻으라."], ["And so long as it lies there, no enemy shall ever land on the island.”", "그것이 거기 놓여 있는 한, 어떤 적도 이 섬에 발을 들이지 못하리라.\""], ["So they cut off his head, and these seven went forward therewith.", "그리하여 그들이 그의 머리를 베었고, 이 일곱 사람이 그것을 지니고 나아갔다."], ["And Branwen was the eighth with them.", "브란웬이 그들과 더불어 여덟 번째였다."], ["And they came to land on Aber Alaw, and they sat down to rest.", "그들이 아베르 알라우에 뭍으로 올라 쉬려고 앉았다."], ["And Branwen looked towards Ireland, and towards the Island of the Mighty, to see if she could descry them.", "브란웬은 그들을 알아볼 수 있을까 하여 아일랜드 쪽과 강대한 자들의 섬 쪽을 바라보았다. \"아아!\""], ["“Alas!”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “woe is me that I was ever born; two islands have been destroyed because of me.”", "\"비통하도다, 내가 태어났다는 것이. 나로 인하여 두 섬이 멸망하였구나.\""], ["Then she uttered a groan, and there broke her heart.", "그러고는 그녀가 신음을 토하니, 거기서 그녀의 심장이 터졌다."], ["And they made her a four-sided grave, and buried her upon the banks of the Alaw.", "그들은 그녀를 위해 네모진 무덤을 만들어, 알라우 강가에 그녀를 묻었다."]], [["Then the seven men journeyed forward, bearing the head with them; and as they went, behold there met them a multitude of men and women.", "이윽고 일곱 사람이 그 머리를 지니고 앞으로 나아갔다. 그들이 가는 동안, 보라, 한 무리의 남녀가 그들을 마주쳤다."], ["“Have you any tidings?”", "\"무슨 소식이라도 있소?\""], ["said Manawyddan.", "마나위단이 말했다."], ["“We have none,”", "\"없습니다.\""], ["said they, “save that Caswallawn,[66] the son of Beli, has conquered the Island of the Mighty, and is crowned king in London.”", "그들이 말했다. \"다만 벨리의 아들 카스왈라운이 강대한 자들의 섬을 정복하여, 런던에서 왕관을 쓴 임금이 되었다는 것 말고는.\""], ["“What has become,” said they, “of Caradoc, the son of Bran, and the seven men who were left with him in this island?”", "\"브란의 아들 카라도크와, 이 섬에 그와 함께 남겨졌던 일곱 사람은 어찌 되었소?\""], ["“Caswallawn came upon them, and slew six of the men, and Caradoc’s heart broke for grief thereof.”", "그들이 물었다. \"카스왈라운이 그들을 덮쳐 여섯 사람을 죽였고, 카라도크는 그로 인한 슬픔으로 심장이 터졌습니다.\""], ["And the seven men journeyed on towards London, and they buried the head in the White Mount, as Bendigeid Vran had directed them.[67]", "그리하여 일곱 사람이 런던을 향해 나아가, 벤디게이드 브란이 그들에게 명한 대로 그 머리를 화이트 마운트에 묻었다."]], [["————", "————"]], [["PWYLL and Rhiannon had a son, whom they named Pryderi.", "프윌과 리아논 사이에는 아들이 하나 있었으니, 그들은 그 아이를 프리데리라고 이름 지었다."], ["And when he was grown up, Pwyll, his father, died.", "그리고 그 아이가 다 자랐을 무렵, 그의 아버지 프윌이 세상을 떠났다."], ["And Pryderi married Kicva, the daughter of Gwynn Gloy.", "그리고 프리데리는 그윈 글로이의 딸 키크바와 혼인하였다."]], [["Now Manawyddan returned from the war in Ireland, and he found that his cousin had seized all his possessions, and much grief and heaviness came upon him.", "이제 마나위단이 아일랜드에서의 전쟁을 마치고 돌아왔는데, 자신의 사촌이 그의 모든 재산을 빼앗아 차지한 것을 알게 되었고, 큰 슬픔과 무거움이 그에게 닥쳤다. \"아아!"], ["“Alas! woe is me!”", "가련한 내 신세여!\""], ["he exclaimed; “there is none save myself without a home and a resting-place.”", "그는 탄식하였다. \"나 말고는 집도 쉴 곳도 없는 자가 아무도 없구나.\""], ["“Lord,”", "\"주군이시여,\""], ["said Pryderi, “be not so sorrowful.", "프리데리가 말하였다. \"그토록 슬퍼하지 마소서."], ["Thy cousin is king of the Island of the Mighty, and though he has done thee wrong, thou hast never been a claimant of land or possessions.”", "당신의 사촌은 강한 자들의 섬의 왕이며, 비록 그가 당신께 잘못을 저질렀다 하나, 당신께서는 땅이나 재산을 두고 권리를 주장하신 적이 한 번도 없으셨습니다.\""], ["“Yea,”", "\"그렇다,\""], ["answered he, “but although this man is my cousin, it grieveth me to see any one in the place of my brother, Bendigeid Vran; neither can I be happy in the same dwelling with him.”", "그가 대답하였다. \"하지만 이 사람이 비록 내 사촌이라 할지라도, 내 형제 벤디게이드 브란의 자리에 누군가가 앉아 있는 것을 보는 일은 나를 슬프게 하니, 나는 그와 같은 거처에서 행복할 수가 없도다.\""], ["“Wilt thou follow the counsel of another?”", "\"다른 이의 충고를 따르시겠습니까?\""], ["said Pryderi.", "프리데리가 말하였다."], ["“I stand in need of counsel,”", "\"나는 충고가 필요하다,\""], ["he answered, “and what may that counsel be?”", "그가 대답하였다. \"그 충고란 무엇이냐?\""], ["“Seven cantrevs belong unto me,”", "\"일곱 개의 칸트레브가 제게 속해 있으며,\""], ["said Pryderi, “wherein Rhiannon, my mother, dwells.", "프리데리가 말하였다. \"그곳에는 제 어머니 리아논이 살고 계십니다."], ["I will bestow her upon thee, and the seven cantrevs with her; and though thou hadst no possessions but those cantrevs only, thou couldst not have any fairer than they.", "제가 어머니를 당신께 드리고, 일곱 개의 칸트레브도 함께 드리겠습니다. 비록 당신께서 그 칸트레브 외에 아무 재산도 없으시다 한들, 그보다 더 아름다운 것을 가지실 수는 없을 것입니다."], ["Do thou and Rhiannon enjoy them, and if thou desire any possessions thou wilt not despise these.”", "당신과 리아논이 그것을 누리소서. 그리고 만일 어떤 재산을 바라신다 해도 이것을 업신여기지 않으실 것입니다.\""], ["“I do not, chieftain,” said he.", "\"그렇지 않다, 족장이여,\""], ["“Heaven reward thee for the friendship!", "그가 말하였다. \"하늘이 이 우정에 보답해 주기를!"], ["I will go with thee to seek Rhiannon, and to look at thy possessions.”", "내가 그대와 함께 가서 리아논을 찾고, 그대의 재산을 살펴보겠노라.\""], ["“Thou wilt do well,”", "\"잘 결정하셨습니다,\""], ["he answered; “and I believe that thou didst never hear a lady discourse better than she, and when she was in her prime, none was ever fairer.", "그가 대답하였다. \"그리고 제 생각으로는 당신께서는 그분보다 더 말을 잘하는 부인을 들어 보신 적이 없으실 것이며, 그분이 한창때이셨을 적에는 그분보다 더 아름다운 이가 없었습니다."], ["Even now her aspect is not uncomely.”", "지금도 그분의 자태는 결코 보기 흉하지 않습니다.\""]], [["They set forth, and, however long the journey, they came at last to Dyved; and a feast was prepared for them by Rhiannon and Kicva.", "그들은 길을 떠났고, 그 여정이 아무리 길었다 한들, 마침내 디베드에 이르렀다. 그리고 리아논과 키크바가 그들을 위해 잔치를 마련하였다."], ["Then began Manawyddan and Rhiannon to sit and to talk together; and his mind and his thoughts became warmed towards her, and he thought in his heart he had never beheld any lady more fulfilled of grace and beauty than she.", "그때 마나위단과 리아논이 함께 앉아 이야기를 나누기 시작하였다. 그리고 그의 마음과 생각이 그녀를 향해 따뜻해졌으며, 그는 마음속으로 그녀보다 더 우아함과 아름다움으로 가득 찬 부인을 본 적이 없다고 여겼다."], ["“Pryderi,”", "\"프리데리여,\""], ["said he, “I will that it be as thou didst say.”", "그가 말하였다. \"나는 그대가 말한 대로 되기를 바라노라.\""], ["“What saying was that?”", "\"그 말이란 무엇이었습니까?\""], ["asked Rhiannon.", "리아논이 물었다."], ["“Lady,”", "\"부인이시여,\""], ["said Pryderi, “I did offer thee as a wife to Manawyddan, the son of Llyr.”", "프리데리가 말하였다. \"제가 당신을 흘르의 아들 마나위단의 아내로 드리겠다고 청하였습니다.\""], ["“By that will I gladly abide,”", "\"그 일에 나는 기꺼이 따르겠노라,\""], ["said Rhiannon.", "리아논이 말하였다."], ["“Right glad am I also,”", "\"나 역시 더없이 기쁘오,\""], ["said Manawyddan, “may Heaven reward him who hath shown unto me friendship so perfect as this!”", "마나위단이 말하였다. \"이토록 완전한 우정을 내게 보여 준 이에게 하늘이 보답하기를!\""]], [["And before the feast was over she became his bride.", "그리고 잔치가 끝나기도 전에 그녀는 그의 신부가 되었다. 프리데리가 말하였다."], ["Said Pryderi, “Tarry ye here the rest of the feast, and I will go into England to tender my homage unto Caswallawn, the son of Beli.”", "\"여기서 나머지 잔치를 즐기십시오. 저는 잉글랜드로 가서 벨리의 아들 카스왈라운에게 신하의 예를 바치겠습니다.\""], ["“Lord,”", "\"주군이시여,\" 리아논이 말하였다."], ["said Rhiannon, “Caswallawn is in Kent; thou mayest therefore tarry at the feast, and wait until he shall be nearer.”", "\"카스왈라운은 켄트에 있습니다. 그러니 당신께서는 잔치에 머무르시며 그가 더 가까이 올 때까지 기다리셔도 됩니다.\""], ["“We will wait,”", "\"그리하겠소,\""], ["he answered.", "그가 대답하였다."], ["So they finished the feast.", "그리하여 그들은 잔치를 마쳤다."], ["And they began to make the circuit of Dyved, and to hunt, and to take their pleasure.", "그리고 그들은 디베드를 한 바퀴 돌며 사냥을 하고 즐거움을 누리기 시작하였다."], ["And as they went through the country, they had never seen lands more pleasant to live in, nor better hunting grounds, nor greater plenty of honey and fish.", "그리고 그들이 그 땅을 두루 다녀 보니, 그보다 더 살기 좋은 땅도, 더 나은 사냥터도, 더 풍성한 꿀과 물고기도 본 적이 없었다."], ["And such was the friendship between these four, that they would not be parted from each other by night nor by day.", "그리고 이 네 사람 사이의 우정이 어찌나 깊었던지, 그들은 밤에도 낮에도 서로 떨어지려 하지 않았다."]], [["And in the midst of all this he went to Caswallawn at Oxford, and tendered his homage; and honorable was his reception there, and highly was he praised for offering his homage.", "그리고 이 모든 일이 한창일 때, 그는 옥스퍼드에 있는 카스왈라운에게로 가서 신하의 예를 바쳤다. 그리고 그곳에서 그는 명예로운 대접을 받았으며, 신하의 예를 바친 일로 크게 칭송을 받았다."]], [["And after his return Pryderi and Manawyddan feasted and took their ease and pleasure.", "그리고 그가 돌아온 뒤, 프리데리와 마나위단은 잔치를 벌이며 편안함과 즐거움을 누렸다."], ["And they began a feast at Narberth, for it was the chief palace.", "그리고 그들은 나르베르스에서 잔치를 시작하였으니, 그곳이 으뜸가는 궁전이었기 때문이다."], ["And when they had ended the first meal, while those who served them ate, they arose and went forth, and proceeded to the Gorsedd, that is, the Mount of Narberth, and their retinue with them.", "그리고 그들이 첫 번째 식사를 마쳤을 때, 그들을 시중들던 이들이 식사를 하는 동안, 그들은 일어나 밖으로 나가 고르세드, 곧 나르베르스의 언덕으로 향하였고, 그들의 수행원들도 함께였다."], ["And as they sat thus, behold a peal of thunder, and with the violence of the thunder-storm, lo!", "그리고 그들이 그렇게 앉아 있는데, 보라, 한 차례 천둥소리가 울렸으며, 그 천둥 폭풍의 격렬함과 함께, 보라!"], ["there came a fall of mist, so thick that not one of them could see the other.", "안개가 내려앉았는데 어찌나 짙었던지 그들 가운데 누구도 서로를 볼 수 없었다."], ["And after the mist it became light all around.", "그리고 안개가 걷힌 뒤에는 사방이 환하게 밝아졌다."], ["And when they looked towards the place where they were wont to see the cattle and herds and dwellings, they saw nothing now, neither house, nor beast, nor smoke, nor fire, nor man, nor dwelling, but the buildings of the court empty, and desert, and uninhabited, without either man or beast within them.", "그리고 그들이 늘 가축과 무리와 거처를 보던 곳을 바라보니, 이제는 아무것도 보이지 않았다. 집도, 짐승도, 연기도, 불도, 사람도, 거처도 없었고, 다만 궁정의 건물들만이 텅 비고 황량하게 사람 없이 남아 있을 뿐, 그 안에는 사람도 짐승도 없었다."], ["And truly all their companions were lost to them, without their knowing aught of what had befallen them, save those four only.", "그리고 진실로 그들의 모든 동료들이 그들에게서 사라져 버렸으니, 무슨 일이 그들에게 닥쳤는지 전혀 알지 못한 채, 오직 이 네 사람만이 남았다."]], [["“In the name of Heaven,”", "\"하늘의 이름으로,\""], ["said Manawyddan, “where are they of the court, and all my host beside?", "마나위단이 말하였다. \"궁정의 사람들과 내 모든 군사들은 다 어디로 갔는가?"], ["Let us go and see.”", "가서 살펴보세.\""]], [["So they came to the castle, and saw no man, and into the hall, and to the sleeping-place, and there was none; and in the mead-cellar and in the kitchen there was naught but desolation.", "그리하여 그들은 성으로 갔으나 아무도 보지 못했고, 큰 방으로, 또 침소로 들어갔으나 거기에도 아무도 없었으며, 벌꿀술 저장고와 부엌에도 황량함밖에는 없었다."], ["Then they began to go through the land, and all the possessions that they had; and they visited the houses and dwellings, and found nothing but wild beasts.", "그때 그들은 그 땅과 그들이 가진 모든 재산을 두루 다니기 시작하였다. 그리고 그들은 집들과 거처들을 찾아가 보았으나 야수들밖에는 아무것도 발견하지 못하였다."], ["And when they had consumed their feast and all their provisions, they fed upon the prey they killed in hunting, and the honey of the wild swans.", "그리고 그들이 잔치 음식과 모든 양식을 다 먹어 치웠을 때, 그들은 사냥하여 잡은 짐승과 야생 백조의 꿀로 끼니를 이었다."]], [["And one morning Pryderi and Manawyddan rose up to hunt, and they ranged their dogs and went forth.", "그리고 어느 날 아침 프리데리와 마나위단이 사냥을 하러 일어나, 개들을 정렬시키고 밖으로 나갔다."], ["And some of the dogs ran before them, and came to a bush which was near at hand; but as soon as they were come to the bush, they hastily drew back, and returned to the men, their hair bristling up greatly.", "그리고 개들 가운데 몇 마리가 그들 앞으로 달려가 가까이에 있는 덤불에 이르렀다. 그러나 그 덤불에 다다르자마자 개들은 황급히 물러나 사람들에게로 돌아왔으며, 그 털이 잔뜩 곤두서 있었다."], ["“Let us go near to the bush,”", "\"저 덤불 가까이 가서,\""], ["said Pryderi, “and see what is in it.”", "프리데리가 말하였다. \"그 안에 무엇이 있는지 봅시다.\""], ["And as they came near, behold, a wild boar of a pure white color rose up from the bush.", "그리고 그들이 가까이 다가가니, 보라, 순백색의 멧돼지 한 마리가 덤불에서 일어났다."], ["Then the dogs, being set on by the men, rushed towards him; but he left the bush, and fell back a little way from the men, and made a stand against the dogs, without retreating from them, until the men had come near.", "그러자 사람들에게 부추김을 받은 개들이 그 멧돼지를 향해 돌진하였다. 그러나 멧돼지는 덤불을 떠나 사람들로부터 조금 물러나서는, 사람들이 가까이 올 때까지 물러서지 않고 개들에 맞서 버티고 섰다."], ["And when the men came up, he fell back a second time, and betook him to flight.", "그리고 사람들이 다가오자, 멧돼지는 두 번째로 물러나 달아나기 시작하였다."], ["Then they pursued the boar until they beheld a vast and lofty castle, all newly built, in a place where they had never before seen either stone or building.", "그러자 그들은 그 멧돼지를 쫓다가, 마침내 이전에는 돌 하나 건물 하나 본 적이 없던 곳에 갓 지어진 거대하고 드높은 성을 보게 되었다."], ["And the boar ran swiftly into the castle, and the dogs after him.", "그리고 멧돼지는 재빨리 성안으로 달려 들어갔고, 개들도 그 뒤를 따랐다."], ["Now when the boar and the dogs had gone into the castle, the men began to wonder at finding a castle in a place where they had never before seen any building whatsoever.", "이제 멧돼지와 개들이 성안으로 들어가 버리자, 사람들은 이전에는 어떤 건물도 본 적이 없던 곳에 성이 있는 것을 보고 의아해하기 시작하였다."], ["And from the top of the Gorsedd they looked and listened for the dogs.", "그리고 그들은 고르세드 꼭대기에서 개들을 보고 들으려 애썼다."], ["But so long as they were there, they heard not one of the dogs, nor aught concerning them.", "그러나 그들이 거기 있는 동안에는 개 한 마리의 소리도, 개들에 관한 그 무엇도 듣지 못하였다."]], [["“Lord,”", "\"주군이시여,\""], ["said Pryderi, “I will go into the castle to get tidings of the dogs.”", "프리데리가 말하였다. \"제가 성안으로 들어가 개들의 소식을 알아보겠습니다.\""], ["“Truly,”", "\"진실로,\""], ["he replied, “thou wouldst be unwise to go into this castle, which thou hast never seen till now.", "그가 대답하였다. \"그대가 이제껏 한 번도 본 적 없는 이 성안으로 들어가는 것은 어리석은 일일 것이다."], ["If thou wouldst follow my counsel, thou wouldst not enter therein.", "만일 그대가 내 충고를 따른다면, 그 안으로 들어가지 않을 것이다."], ["Whosoever has cast a spell over this land, has caused this castle to be here.”", "이 땅에 마법을 건 자가 누구든, 이 성이 여기에 있게 한 것이다.\" \"진실로,\""], ["“Of a truth,”", "프리데리가 대답하였다."], ["answered Pryderi, “I cannot thus give up my dogs.”", "\"저는 이대로 제 개들을 포기할 수가 없습니다.\""], ["And for all the counsel that Manawyddan gave him, yet to the castle he went.", "그리고 마나위단이 그에게 베푼 온갖 충고에도 불구하고, 그는 끝내 성으로 들어갔다."]], [["When he came within the castle, neither man nor beast, nor boar, nor dogs, nor house, nor dwelling, saw he within it.", "그가 성안에 들어서니, 사람도 짐승도, 멧돼지도 개들도, 집도 거처도, 그 안에서 아무것도 보이지 않았다."], ["But in the centre of the castle-floor he beheld a fountain with marble-work around it, and on the margin of the fountain a golden bowl upon a marble slab, and chains hanging from the air, to which he saw no end.", "다만 성의 마룻바닥 한가운데에 대리석 세공으로 둘러싸인 샘 하나가 보였고, 그 샘 가장자리의 대리석 판 위에 황금 사발 하나가 놓여 있었으며, 허공으로부터 사슬들이 드리워져 있었는데 그 끝은 보이지 않았다."]], [["And he was greatly pleased with the beauty of the gold, and with the rich workmanship of the bowl; and he went up to the bowl, and laid hold of it.", "그리고 그는 그 황금의 아름다움과 사발의 정교한 세공에 크게 기뻐하였다. 그래서 그는 사발에 다가가 그것을 붙잡았다."], ["And when he had taken hold of it, his hands stuck to the bowl, and his feet to the slab on which the bowl was placed; and all his joyousness forsook him, so that he could not utter a word.", "그리고 그가 그것을 붙잡자, 그의 두 손이 사발에 들러붙고, 그의 두 발이 사발이 놓인 판에 들러붙었다. 그리고 그의 모든 기쁨이 그를 떠나, 그는 한마디도 내뱉을 수 없게 되었다."], ["And thus he stood.", "그리하여 그는 그렇게 서 있었다."]], [["And Manawyddan waited for him till near the close of the day.", "그리고 마나위단은 그날이 거의 저물 때까지 그를 기다렸다."], ["And late in the evening, being certain that he should have no tidings of Pryderi or the dogs, he went back to the palace.", "그리고 저녁 늦게, 프리데리나 개들에 관한 소식을 결코 듣지 못하리라는 것을 확신하고는, 그는 궁전으로 돌아갔다. 그리고 그가 들어서자 리아논이 그를 바라보았다."], ["And as he entered, Rhiannon looked at him. “Where,”", "\"그대의 동료와 그대의 개들은,\""], ["said she, “are thy companion and thy dogs?”", "그녀가 말하였다. \"어디에 있습니까?\""], ["“Behold,”", "\"보시오,\""], ["he answered, “the adventure that has befallen me.”", "그가 대답하였다. \"내게 닥친 일이 이러하오.\""], ["And he related it all unto her. “An evil companion hast thou been,”", "그리고 그는 그 모든 것을 그녀에게 이야기하였다."], ["said Rhiannon, “and a good companion hast thou lost.”", "\"그대는 못된 동료였으며,\" 리아논이 말하였다."], ["And with that word she went out, and proceeded towards the castle, according to the direction which he gave her.", "\"좋은 동료를 잃었습니다.\" 그리고 그 말과 함께 그녀는 밖으로 나가, 그가 일러 준 방향을 따라 성을 향해 나아갔다."], ["The gate of the castle she found open.", "성문은 열려 있는 것을 그녀가 발견하였다."], ["She was nothing daunted, and she went in.", "그녀는 조금도 겁먹지 않고 안으로 들어갔다."], ["And as she went in, she perceived Pryderi laying hold of the bowl, and she went towards him.", "그리고 그녀가 안으로 들어가니, 프리데리가 사발을 붙잡고 있는 것이 보였고, 그녀는 그에게로 다가갔다. \"오 나의 주군이시여,\""], ["“O my lord,”", "그녀가 말하였다."], ["said she, “what dost thou here?”", "\"여기서 무엇을 하고 계십니까?\""], ["And she took hold of the bowl with him; and as she did so, her hands also became fast to the bowl, and her feet to the slab, and she was not able to utter a word.", "그리고 그녀는 그와 함께 사발을 붙잡았다. 그러자 그녀가 그렇게 하니, 그녀의 두 손 또한 사발에 들러붙고, 그녀의 두 발이 판에 들러붙어, 그녀는 한마디도 내뱉을 수 없게 되었다."], ["And with that, as it became night, lo!", "그리고 그와 함께 밤이 되니, 보라!"], ["there came thunder upon them, and a fall of mist; and thereupon the castle vanished, and they with it.", "그들 위로 천둥이 닥치고, 안개가 내려앉았다. 그리고 그러자 성이 사라졌고, 그들도 성과 함께 사라졌다."]], [["When Kicva, the daughter of Gwynn Gloy, saw that there was no one in the palace but herself and Manawyddan, she sorrowed so that she cared not whether she lived or died.", "그윈 글로이의 딸 키크바는 궁전에 자신과 마나위단 외에는 아무도 없는 것을 보고는, 살든 죽든 개의치 않을 만큼 깊이 슬퍼하였다. 그리고 마나위단이 이를 보았다."], ["And Manawyddan saw this.", "\"그대가 잘못 생각하고 있소,\""], ["“Thou art in the wrong,”", "그가 말하였다."], ["said he, “if through fear of me thou grievest thus.", "\"만일 나에 대한 두려움 때문에 그렇게 슬퍼한다면 말이오."], ["I call Heaven to witness that thou hast never seen friendship more pure than that which I will bear thee, as long as Heaven will that thou shouldst be thus.", "하늘을 증인으로 부르건대, 하늘이 그대가 이러하기를 바라는 한, 내가 그대에게 품을 우정보다 더 순수한 우정을 그대는 본 적이 없을 것이오."], ["I declare to thee, that, were I in the dawn of youth, I would keep my faith unto Pryderi, and unto thee also will I keep it.", "그대에게 분명히 말하노니, 내가 젊음의 동틀 무렵에 있다 할지라도, 나는 프리데리에 대한 신의를 지킬 것이며, 그대에게도 그 신의를 지킬 것이오."], ["Be there no fear upon thee, therefore.”", "그러니 그대에게 두려움이 없기를.\" \"하늘이 당신께 보답하기를!\""], ["“Heaven reward thee!”", "그녀가 말하였다."], ["she said; “and that is what I deemed of thee.”", "\"그리고 그것이 바로 제가 당신에 대해 헤아렸던 바입니다.\""], ["And the damsel thereupon took courage, and was glad.", "그리고 그 처녀는 그 말에 용기를 얻어 기뻐하였다."]], [["“Truly, lady,”", "\"진실로, 부인이여,\" 마나위단이 말하였다."], ["said Manawyddan, “it is not fitting for us to stay here; we have lost our dogs, and cannot get food.", "\"우리가 여기 머무는 것은 마땅치 않소. 우리는 개들을 잃었고, 먹을 것을 구할 수 없소."], ["Let us go into England; it is easiest for us to find support there.”", "잉글랜드로 갑시다. 거기서라면 우리가 살아갈 방도를 찾기가 가장 쉬울 것이오.\""], ["“Gladly, lord,”", "\"기꺼이, 주군이시여,\""], ["said she, “we will do so.”", "그녀가 말하였다. \"그리하겠습니다.\""], ["And they set forth together to England.", "그리고 그들은 함께 잉글랜드로 길을 떠났다."]], [["“Lord,”", "\"주군이시여,\""], ["said she, “what craft wilt thou follow?", "그녀가 말하였다. \"어떤 생업을 따르시겠습니까?"], ["Take up one that is seemly.”", "어울리는 것을 택하소서.\""], ["“None other will I take,”", "\"다른 것은 택하지 않겠소,\""], ["answered he, “but that of making shoes.”", "그가 대답하였다. \"다만 신발을 만드는 일을 하겠소.\""], ["“Lord,”", "\"주군이시여,\""], ["said she, “such a craft becomes not a man so nobly born as thou.”", "그녀가 말하였다. \"그러한 생업은 당신처럼 고귀하게 태어난 분께는 어울리지 않습니다.\""], ["“By that however will I abide,”", "\"그래도 나는 그 일을 따르겠소,\""], ["said he.", "그가 말하였다."], ["“I know nothing thereof,” said Kicva.", "\"저는 그 일에 대해 아무것도 모릅니다,\""], ["“But I know,”", "키크바가 말하였다."], ["answered Manawyddan, “and I will teach thee to stitch.", "\"하지만 나는 아오,\" 마나위단이 대답하였다."], ["We will not attempt to dress the leather, but we will buy it ready dressed, and will make the shoes from it.”", "\"그리고 내가 그대에게 바느질을 가르쳐 주겠소. 우리는 가죽을 무두질하려 하지는 않을 것이고, 다 무두질된 가죽을 사서 그것으로 신발을 만들 것이오.\""]], [["So they went into England, and went as far as Hereford; and they betook themselves to making shoes.", "그리하여 그들은 잉글랜드로 들어가, 헤리퍼드까지 이르렀다. 그리고 그들은 신발 만드는 일에 전념하였다."], ["And he began by buying the best cordwain that could be had in the town, and none other would buy.", "그리고 그는 그 고을에서 구할 수 있는 가장 좋은 코도반 가죽을 사는 일부터 시작하였으며, 다른 것은 사지 않았다."], ["And he associated himself with the best goldsmith in the town, and caused him to make clasps for the shoes, and to gild the clasps; and he marked how it was done until he learned the method.", "그리고 그는 그 고을에서 가장 솜씨 좋은 금세공인과 어울리며, 그로 하여금 신발에 다는 죔쇠를 만들게 하고 그 죔쇠에 금을 입히게 하였다. 그리고 그는 그것이 어떻게 만들어지는지를 눈여겨보아 그 방법을 익혔다."], ["And therefore is he called one of the three makers of gold shoes.", "그리하여 그는 황금 신발을 만든 세 사람 가운데 하나라 불린다."], ["And when they could be had from him, not a shoe nor hose was bought of any of the cordwainers in the town.", "그리고 그에게서 신발을 구할 수 있게 되자, 그 고을의 어느 제화공에게서도 신발 한 켤레, 양말 한 켤레 사는 이가 없었다."], ["But when the cordwainers perceived that their gains were failing (for as Manawyddan shaped the work, so Kicva stitched it), they came together and took counsel, and agreed that they would slay them.", "그러나 제화공들이 자신들의 벌이가 줄어드는 것을 알아채자(마나위단이 일감을 마름질하면 키크바가 그것을 바느질하였기 때문이다), 그들은 함께 모여 의논하였고, 그들을 죽이기로 의견을 모았다."], ["And he had warning thereof, and it was told him how the cordwainers had agreed together to slay him.", "그리고 그는 이에 대한 경고를 받았으니, 제화공들이 함께 그를 죽이기로 작당하였다는 것을 그는 전해 들었다."]], [["“Lord,”", "\"주군이시여,\""], ["said Kicva, “wherefore should this be borne from these boors?”", "키크바가 말하였다. \"어찌하여 이 촌놈들에게서 이런 일을 견디셔야 합니까?\""], ["“Nay,”", "\"아니오,\""], ["said he, “we will go back unto Dyved.”", "그가 말하였다. \"우리는 디베드로 돌아갑시다.\""], ["So towards Dyved they set forth.", "그리하여 그들은 디베드를 향해 길을 떠났다."]], [["Now Manawyddan, when he set out to return to Dyved, took with him a burden of wheat.", "이제 마나위단은 디베드로 돌아가려고 길을 나설 때 밀 한 짐을 가지고 갔다."], ["And he proceeded towards Narberth, and there he dwelt.", "그리고 그는 나르베르스를 향해 나아갔고, 거기서 살았다."], ["And never was he better pleased than when he saw Narberth again, and the lands where he had been wont to hunt with Pryderi and with Rhiannon.", "그리고 그가 나르베르스를, 그리고 프리데리와 리아논과 함께 사냥하곤 하던 그 땅들을 다시 보았을 때보다 더 기뻤던 적은 없었다."], ["And he accustomed himself to fish, and to hunt the deer in their covert.", "그리고 그는 물고기를 잡고, 사슴이 숨은 곳에서 사슴을 사냥하는 일에 익숙해졌다."], ["And then he began to prepare some ground, and he sowed a croft, and a second, and a third.", "그리고 그러고 나서 그는 얼마간의 땅을 일구기 시작하였으며, 한 뙈기의 밭을 갈고, 두 번째 밭을 갈고, 세 번째 밭을 갈았다."], ["And no wheat in the world ever sprung up better.", "그리고 세상의 어떤 밀도 그보다 더 잘 돋아난 적이 없었다."], ["And the three crofts prospered with perfect growth, and no man ever saw fairer wheat than it.", "그리고 세 뙈기의 밭이 완전한 성장으로 무성하게 자라났으며, 그보다 더 아름다운 밀을 본 사람은 아무도 없었다."]], [["And thus passed the seasons of the year until the harvest came.", "그리하여 추수 때가 올 때까지 한 해의 절기들이 지나갔다."], ["And he went to look at one of his crofts, and, behold, it was ripe.", "그리고 그가 자신의 밭 한 뙈기를 살펴보러 갔는데, 보라, 그것이 익어 있었다."], ["“I will reap this to-morrow,”", "\"내일 이것을 거두어들이겠다,\""], ["said he.", "그가 말하였다."], ["And that night he went back to Narberth, and on the morrow, in the gray dawn, he went to reap the croft; and when he came there, he found nothing but the bare straw.", "그리고 그날 밤 그는 나르베르스로 돌아갔고, 이튿날 잿빛 새벽녘에 그 밭을 거두러 갔다. 그리고 그가 거기 이르렀을 때, 그는 헐벗은 짚 외에는 아무것도 발견하지 못하였다."], ["Every one of the ears of the wheat was cut off from the stalk, and all the ears carried entirely away, and nothing but the straw left.", "밀 이삭은 하나하나 줄기에서 잘려 나가, 모든 이삭이 송두리째 실려 가 버렸고, 짚 외에는 아무것도 남아 있지 않았다."], ["And at this he marvelled greatly.", "그리고 이에 그는 크게 놀랐다."]], [["Then he went to look at another croft, and, behold, that also was ripe.", "그러고 나서 그는 또 다른 밭을 살펴보러 갔는데, 보라, 그것 또한 익어 있었다."], ["“Verily,”", "\"진실로,\""], ["said he, “this will I reap to-morrow.”", "그가 말하였다. \"이것을 내일 거두어들이겠다.\""], ["And on the morrow he came with the intent to reap it; and when he came there, he found nothing but the bare straw.", "그리고 이튿날 그는 그것을 거둘 작정으로 왔다. 그리고 그가 거기 이르렀을 때, 그는 헐벗은 짚 외에는 아무것도 발견하지 못하였다."], ["“O gracious Heaven!”", "\"오 자비로운 하늘이여!\""], ["he exclaimed.", "그가 탄식하였다."], ["“I know that whosoever has begun my ruin is completing it, and has also destroyed the country with me.”", "\"나의 파멸을 시작한 자가 누구든 그것을 마저 끝내고 있으며, 또한 나와 더불어 이 나라를 망쳐 놓았음을 나는 아노라.\""]], [["Then he went to look at the third croft; and when he came there, finer wheat had there never been seen, and this also was ripe.", "그러고 나서 그는 세 번째 밭을 살펴보러 갔다. 그리고 그가 거기 이르렀을 때, 그토록 고운 밀은 이제껏 본 적이 없었으며, 이것 또한 익어 있었다."], ["“Evil betide me,”", "\"내게 화가 미칠지어다,\""], ["said he, “if I watch not here to-night.", "그가 말하였다. \"오늘 밤 여기서 지켜보지 않는다면."], ["Whoever carried off the other corn will come in like manner to take this, and I will know who it is.”", "다른 곡식을 가져간 자가 누구든 이것을 가져가려고 똑같이 올 것이며, 그가 누구인지 내가 알아내고 말겠다.\""], ["And he told Kicva all that had befallen.", "그리고 그는 키크바에게 일어난 모든 일을 이야기하였다."], ["“Verily,”", "\"진실로,\""], ["said she, “what thinkest thou to do?”", "그녀가 말하였다. \"어찌하실 작정입니까?\""], ["“I will watch the croft to-night,”", "\"오늘 밤 그 밭을 지켜보겠소,\""], ["said he.", "그가 말하였다."], ["And he went to watch the croft.", "그리고 그는 밭을 지키러 갔다."]], [["And at midnight he heard something stirring among the wheat; and he looked, and behold, the mightiest host of mice in the world, which could neither be numbered nor measured.", "그리고 한밤중에 그는 밀밭 사이에서 무언가 움직이는 소리를 들었다. 그리고 그가 바라보니, 보라, 세상에서 가장 거대한 쥐 떼였는데, 헤아릴 수도 가늠할 수도 없을 만큼 많았다."], ["And he knew not what it was until the mice had made their way into the croft, and each of them, climbing up the straw, and bending it down with its weight, had cut off one of the ears of wheat, and had carried it away, leaving there the stalk; and he saw not a single straw there that had not a mouse to it.", "그리고 쥐들이 밭으로 길을 내며 들어와, 저마다 짚을 타고 올라 제 무게로 그것을 휘어 굽히고는 밀 이삭 하나를 잘라 내어 그것을 실어 가 버리고 줄기만 남길 때까지, 그는 그것이 무엇인지 알지 못하였다. 그리고 그는 거기서 쥐 한 마리가 붙어 있지 않은 짚 하나도 보지 못하였다."], ["And they all took their way, carrying the ears with them.", "그리고 쥐들은 이삭을 실은 채 모두 제 갈 길로 가 버렸다."]], [["In wrath and anger did he rush upon the mice; but he could no more come up with them than if they had been gnats or birds of the air, except one only, which, though it was but sluggish, went so fast that a man on foot could scarce overtake it.", "격노와 분노에 차 그는 쥐들에게 달려들었다. 그러나 그것들이 각다귀나 공중의 새였다 한들 그가 따라잡지 못했을 것과 마찬가지로, 그는 한 마리도 따라잡을 수 없었다."], ["And after this one he went, and he caught it, and put it in his glove, and tied up the opening of the glove with a string, and kept it with him, and returned to the palace.", "다만 한 마리만은 예외였으니, 그것은 굼떴음에도 어찌나 빨리 가던지 걸어가는 사람이 간신히 따라잡을 정도였다. 그리고 그는 이 한 마리를 쫓아가 붙잡았고, 그것을 자신의 장갑 속에 넣고는 끈으로 장갑의 입구를 묶어, 그것을 지닌 채 궁전으로 돌아왔다."], ["Then he came to the hall where Kicva was, and he lighted a fire, and hung the glove by the string upon a peg.", "그리고 그는 키크바가 있는 큰 방으로 와서, 불을 피우고, 그 끈으로 장갑을 못에 걸어 두었다. \"거기 무엇을 가지고 계십니까, 주군이시여?\""], ["“What hast thou there, lord?”", "키크바가 말하였다."], ["said Kicva.", "\"도둑이오,\""], ["“A thief,”", "그가 말하였다."], ["said he, “that I found robbing me.”", "\"나의 것을 훔치고 있던 것을 내가 잡았소.\""], ["“What kind of a thief may it be, lord, that thou couldst put into thy glove?”", "\"어떤 종류의 도둑이기에, 주군이시여, 당신의 장갑 속에 넣으실 수 있단 말입니까?\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말하였다."], ["Then he told her how the mice came to the last of the fields in his sight.", "그러자 그는 쥐들이 그가 보던 마지막 밭으로 어떻게 왔는지를 그녀에게 이야기하였다. \"그리고 그것들 가운데 한 마리가 다른 것들보다 덜 날쌔어, 지금 내 장갑 속에 들어 있소."], ["“And one of them was less nimble than the rest, and is now in my glove; to-morrow I will hang it.”", "내일 그것을 목매달 것이오.\" \"나의 주군이시여,\""], ["“My lord,”", "그녀가 말하였다."], ["said she, “this is marvellous; but yet it would be unseemly for a man of dignity like thee to be hanging such a reptile as this.”", "\"이는 놀라운 일입니다. 하지만 당신처럼 위엄 있는 분이 이런 파충류 같은 것을 목매다는 일은 어울리지 않을 것입니다.\""], ["“Woe betide me,”", "\"내게 화가 미칠지어다,\" 그가 말하였다."], ["said he, “if I would not hang them all, could I catch them, and such as I have I will hang.”", "\"만일 잡을 수만 있다면 그것들을 모조리 목매달지 않는다면 말이오. 그러니 내가 잡은 것은 목매달겠소.\""], ["“Verily, lord,”", "\"진실로, 주군이시여,\" 그녀가 말하였다."], ["said she, “there is no reason that I should succor this reptile, except to prevent discredit unto thee.", "\"제가 이 파충류를 구해 줄 이유는 없습니다. 다만 당신께 불명예가 미치지 않도록 하려는 것 외에는요."], ["Do therefore, lord, as thou wilt.”", "그러니 주군이시여, 뜻대로 하소서.\""]], [["Then he went to the Mound of Narberth, taking the mouse with him.", "그러고 나서 그는 그 쥐를 데리고 나르베르스의 언덕으로 갔다."], ["And he set up two forks on the highest part of the mound.", "그리고 그는 언덕의 가장 높은 곳에 갈래진 기둥 두 개를 세웠다."], ["And while he was doing this, behold, he saw a scholar coming towards him, in old and poor and tattered garments.", "그리고 그가 이 일을 하고 있는데, 보라, 한 학생이 낡고 초라하고 누덕누덕한 옷차림으로 그에게 다가오는 것이 보였다."], ["And it was now seven years since he had seen in that place either man or beast, except those four persons who had remained together until two of them were lost.", "그리고 그가 그곳에서 사람이나 짐승을 본 지가 이제 칠 년이 되었으니, 다만 함께 남아 있다가 그중 둘을 잃은 그 네 사람을 빼고는 아무도 본 적이 없었다."]], [["“My lord,”", "\"나의 주군이시여,\""], ["said the scholar, “good-day to thee.”", "학생이 말하였다. \"당신께 좋은 날이 되기를.\""], ["“Heaven prosper thee, and my greeting be unto thee!", "\"하늘이 그대를 번성케 하기를, 그리고 그대에게 나의 인사를 전하노라!"], ["And whence dost thou come, scholar?”", "그리고 어디서 오는 길인가, 학생이여?\""], ["asked he.", "그가 물었다."], ["“I come, lord, from singing in England; and wherefore dost thou inquire?”", "\"저는 잉글랜드에서 노래하다가 오는 길입니다, 주군이시여. 그런데 어찌하여 물으십니까?\""], ["“Because for the last seven years,”", "\"지난 칠 년 동안,\" 그가 대답하였다."], ["answered he, “I have seen no man here save four secluded persons, and thyself this moment.”", "\"나는 여기서 외따로 떨어져 있는 네 사람과, 이 순간의 그대 말고는 아무도 본 적이 없기 때문이다.\" \"진실로, 주군이시여,\""], ["“Truly, lord,”", "그가 말하였다."], ["said he, “I go through this land unto mine own.", "\"저는 이 땅을 지나 제 고장으로 가는 길입니다."], ["And what work art thou upon, lord?”", "그런데 무슨 일을 하고 계십니까, 주군이시여?\""], ["“I am hanging a thief that I caught robbing me,”", "\"나의 것을 훔치다가 내가 잡은 도둑을 목매달고 있다,\""], ["said he.", "그가 말하였다."], ["“What manner of thief is that?”", "\"그것이 어떤 종류의 도둑입니까?\""], ["asked the scholar.", "학생이 물었다."], ["“I see a creature in thy hand like unto a mouse, and ill does it become a man of rank equal to thine to touch a reptile such as this.", "\"당신의 손에 쥐 같은 것이 보입니다만, 당신 같은 신분의 분께서 이런 파충류를 만지는 일은 어울리지 않습니다."], ["Let it go forth free.”", "그것을 풀어 주십시오.\""], ["“I will not let it go free, by Heaven,”", "\"하늘에 맹세코, 나는 그것을 풀어 주지 않겠다,\" 그가 말하였다."], ["said he; “I caught it robbing me, and the doom of a thief will I inflict upon it, and I will hang it.”", "\"나의 것을 훔치다가 내가 그것을 잡았으니, 나는 그것에 도둑의 형벌을 내릴 것이며, 그것을 목매달 것이다.\" \"주군이시여,\""], ["“Lord,”", "그가 말하였다."], ["said he, “rather than see a man of rank equal to thine at such a work as this, I would give thee a pound, which I have received as alms, to let the reptile go forth free.”", "\"당신 같은 신분의 분이 이런 일을 하시는 것을 보느니, 차라리 제가 동냥으로 받은 일 파운드를 드릴 터이니, 그 파충류를 풀어 주십시오.\" \"나는 그것을 풀어 주지도 않을 것이며,\""], ["“I will not let it go free,”", "그가 말하였다. \"팔지도 않겠다.\""], ["said he, “neither will I sell it.”", "\"뜻대로 하소서, 주군이시여,\""], ["“As thou wilt, lord,”", "그가 대답하였다."], ["he answered; “I care naught.”", "\"저는 개의치 않습니다.\""], ["And the scholar went his way.", "그리고 학생은 제 갈 길로 갔다."]], [["And as he was placing the cross-beam upon the two forks, behold, a priest came towards him, upon a horse covered with trappings.", "그리고 그가 갈래진 두 기둥 위에 가로대를 올려놓고 있는데, 보라, 한 사제가 마구를 두른 말을 타고 그에게 다가왔다. \"좋은 날이 되기를, 주군이시여,\""], ["“Good day to thee, lord,”", "그가 말하였다."], ["said he. “Heaven prosper thee!”", "\"하늘이 그대를 번성케 하기를!\""], ["said Manawyddan; “thy blessing.”", "마나위단이 말하였다. \"그대의 축복을.\""], ["“The blessing of Heaven be upon thee!", "\"하늘의 축복이 그대에게 있기를!"], ["And what, lord, art thou doing?”", "그런데 무엇을 하고 계십니까, 주군이시여?\""], ["“I am hanging a thief that I caught robbing me,”", "\"나의 것을 훔치다가 내가 잡은 도둑을 목매달고 있다,\""], ["said he.", "그가 말하였다."], ["“What manner of thief, lord?”", "\"어떤 종류의 도둑입니까, 주군이시여?\""], ["asked he.", "그가 물었다."], ["“A creature,”", "\"쥐 모양의,\""], ["he answered, “in form of a mouse.", "그가 대답하였다. \"한 마리 짐승이다."], ["It has been robbing me, and I am inflicting upon it the doom of a thief.”", "그것이 나의 것을 훔쳐 왔으니, 나는 그것에 도둑의 형벌을 내리는 중이다.\""], ["“Lord,”", "\"주군이시여,\""], ["said he, “rather than see thee touch this reptile, I would purchase its freedom.”", "그가 말하였다. \"당신께서 이 파충류를 만지시는 것을 보느니, 차라리 제가 그 자유를 사겠습니다.\""], ["“By my confession to Heaven, neither will I sell it nor set it free.”", "\"하늘에 고백하건대, 나는 그것을 팔지도, 풀어 주지도 않겠다.\""], ["“It is true, lord, that it is worth nothing to buy; but rather than see thee defile thyself by touching such a reptile as this, I will give thee three pounds to let it go.”", "\"사실, 주군이시여, 그것은 사들일 만한 값어치도 없습니다. 하지만 당신께서 이런 파충류를 만져 스스로를 더럽히시는 것을 보느니, 그것을 놓아주시도록 제가 삼 파운드를 드리겠습니다.\""], ["“I will not, by Heaven,”", "\"하늘에 맹세코, 나는,\" 그가 말하였다."], ["said he, “take any price for it.", "\"그것에 대해 어떤 값도 받지 않겠다."], ["As it ought, so shall it be hanged.”", "마땅히 그래야 할 대로, 그것은 목매달릴 것이다.\""], ["And the priest went his way.", "그리고 사제는 제 갈 길로 갔다."]], [["Then he noosed the string around the mouse’s neck, and as he was about to draw it up, behold, he saw a bishop’s retinue, with his sumpter-horses and his attendants.", "그러고 나서 그는 쥐의 목에 끈으로 올가미를 씌웠다. 그리고 그가 그것을 끌어 올리려 하는데, 보라, 그는 짐말들과 시종들을 거느린 한 주교의 행렬을 보았다."], ["And the bishop himself came towards him.", "그리고 주교 자신이 그에게 다가왔다."], ["And he stayed his work.", "그리고 그는 일손을 멈추었다."], ["“Lord Bishop,”", "\"주교님이시여,\""], ["said he, “thy blessing.”", "그가 말하였다. \"당신의 축복을.\""], ["“Heaven’s blessing be unto thee!”", "\"하늘의 축복이 그대에게 있기를!\""], ["said he.", "그가 말하였다."], ["“What work art thou upon?”", "\"무슨 일을 하고 계십니까?\""], ["“Hanging a thief that I caught robbing me,” said he.", "\"나의 것을 훔치다가 내가 잡은 도둑을 목매달고 있습니다,\""], ["“Is not that a mouse that I see in thy hand?”", "그가 말하였다. \"그대의 손에 보이는 것이 쥐가 아닙니까?\""], ["“Yes,”", "\"그렇습니다,\""], ["answered he, “and she has robbed me.”", "그가 대답하였다. \"그리고 그것이 나의 것을 훔쳤습니다.\""], ["“Ay,”", "\"허,\""], ["said he, “since I have come at the doom of this reptile I will ransom it of thee.", "그가 말하였다. \"내가 이 파충류의 처형에 마침 당도하였으니, 그것을 그대에게서 속량하겠소."], ["I will give thee seven pounds for it, and that rather than see a man of rank equal to thine destroying so vile a reptile as this.", "그것의 값으로 그대에게 칠 파운드를 주겠소. 그것도 당신 같은 신분의 분이 이런 천한 파충류를 죽이는 것을 보느니 그러는 것이오."], ["Let it loose, and thou shalt have the money.”", "그것을 풀어 주시오, 그러면 그 돈을 가질 것이오.\""], ["“I declare to Heaven that I will not let it loose.”", "\"하늘에 분명히 말하건대, 나는 그것을 풀어 주지 않겠습니다.\""], ["“If thou wilt not loose it for this, I will give thee four and twenty pounds of ready money to set it free.”", "\"이것으로 풀어 주지 않겠다면, 그것을 놓아주도록 내가 그대에게 현금 이십사 파운드를 주겠소.\""], ["“I will not set it free, by Heaven, for as much again,” said he.", "\"하늘에 맹세코, 나는 그 두 배를 준다 해도 그것을 놓아주지 않겠습니다,\""], ["“If thou wilt not set it free for this, I will give thee all the horses that thou seest in this plain, and the seven loads of baggage, and the seven horses that they are upon.”", "그가 말하였다. \"이것으로 놓아주지 않겠다면, 이 들판에 그대가 보는 모든 말과, 일곱 짐의 짐과, 그것들을 실은 일곱 마리의 말을 그대에게 주겠소.\""], ["“By Heaven, I will not,” he replied.", "\"하늘에 맹세코, 나는 그러지 않겠습니다,\""], ["“Since for this thou wilt not set it free, do so at what price soever thou wilt.”", "그가 대답하였다. \"이것으로 그것을 놓아주지 않겠다면, 그대가 원하는 어떤 값으로든 그렇게 하시오.\""], ["“I will that Rhiannon and Pryderi be free,”", "\"리아논과 프리데리가 풀려나기를 바랍니다,\" 그가 말하였다."], ["said he. “That thou shalt have,”", "\"그것은 그대가 가질 것이오,\""], ["he answered.", "그가 대답하였다."], ["“Not yet will I loose the mouse, by Heaven.”", "\"하늘에 맹세코, 나는 아직 쥐를 놓아주지 않겠습니다.\""], ["“What then wouldst thou?”", "\"그러면 무엇을 더 바라는가?\""], ["“That the charm and the illusion be removed from the seven cantrevs of Dyved.”", "\"디베드의 일곱 칸트레브에서 마법과 환영이 걷히기를 바랍니다.\""], ["“This shalt thou have also; set therefore the mouse free.”", "\"이것 또한 그대가 가질 것이오. 그러니 쥐를 풀어 주시오.\""], ["“I will not set it free, by Heaven,”", "\"하늘에 맹세코, 나는 그것을 풀어 주지 않겠습니다,\""], ["said he, “till I know who the mouse may be.”", "그가 말하였다. \"이 쥐가 누구인지 알기 전까지는요.\""], ["“She is my wife.”", "\"그것은 나의 아내요.\""], ["“Wherefore came she to me?”", "\"어찌하여 그것이 내게 왔습니까?\""], ["“To despoil thee,” he answered.", "\"그대의 것을 약탈하기 위해서요,\""], ["“I am Lloyd, the son of Kilwed, and I cast the charm over the seven cantrevs of Dyved.", "그가 대답하였다. \"나는 킬웨드의 아들 흘로이드이며, 디베드의 일곱 칸트레브에 마법을 건 것이 나요."], ["And it was to avenge Gawl, the son of Clud, from the friendship I had towards him, that I cast the charm.", "그리고 내가 그 마법을 건 것은, 내가 클루드의 아들 가울에게 품었던 우정에서, 그의 원수를 갚기 위함이었소."], ["And upon Pryderi did I avenge Gawl, the son of Clud, for the game of Badger in the Bag, that Pwyll, the son of Auwyn, played upon him.", "그리고 나는 아우윈의 아들 프윌이 그에게 행하였던 〈자루 속 오소리〉 놀이에 대한 원수를 프리데리에게 갚았소."], ["And when it was known that thou wast come to dwell in the land, my household came and besought me to transform them into mice, that they might destroy thy corn.", "그리고 그대가 그 땅에 와서 산다는 것이 알려지자, 나의 식솔들이 와서 자신들을 쥐로 변신시켜 그대의 곡식을 망치게 해 달라고 내게 간청하였소."], ["And they went the first and the second night, and destroyed thy two crops.", "그리고 그들은 첫째 날 밤과 둘째 날 밤에 가서 그대의 두 곡식을 망쳤소."], ["And the third night came unto me my wife and the ladies of the court, and besought me to transform them.", "그리고 셋째 날 밤에는 나의 아내와 궁정의 부인들이 내게 와서 자신들을 변신시켜 달라고 간청하였소."], ["And I transformed them.", "그래서 내가 그들을 변신시켰소."], ["Now she is not in her usual health.", "이제 그녀는 평소의 건강 상태가 아니오."], ["And had she been in her usual health, thou wouldst not have been able to overtake her; but since this has taken place, and she has been caught, I will restore to thee Pryderi and Rhiannon, and I will take the charm and illusion from off Dyved.", "그리고 그녀가 평소의 건강 상태였다면, 그대는 그녀를 따라잡을 수 없었을 것이오. 하지만 이런 일이 일어나 그녀가 잡혔으니, 내가 그대에게 프리데리와 리아논을 돌려주고, 디베드에서 마법과 환영을 걷어 가겠소."], ["Set her therefore free.”", "그러니 그녀를 풀어 주시오.\""], ["“I will not set her free yet.”", "\"나는 아직 그녀를 풀어 주지 않겠습니다.\""], ["“What wilt thou more?”", "\"그러면 무엇을 더 바라는가?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."], ["“I will that there be no more charm upon the seven cantrevs of Dyved, and that none shall be put upon it henceforth; moreover, that vengeance be never taken for this, either upon Pryderi or Rhiannon, or upon me.”", "\"나는 디베드의 일곱 칸트레브에 더 이상 마법이 없으며, 이후로도 그것에 마법이 걸리지 않기를 바랍니다. 또한 이 일에 대한 복수가 프리데리에게도, 리아논에게도, 나에게도 결코 행해지지 않기를 바랍니다.\""], ["“All this shalt thou have.", "\"이 모든 것을 그대가 가질 것이오."], ["And truly thou hast done wisely in asking this.", "그리고 진실로 그대는 이것을 청함에 있어 현명하게 처신하였소."], ["Upon thy head would have lit all this trouble.”", "이 모든 화가 그대의 머리 위에 떨어졌을 것이오.\" \"그렇습니다,\""], ["“Yea,”", "그가 말하였다."], ["said he, “for fear thereof was it that I required this.”", "\"그것이 두려웠기에 내가 이것을 요구한 것입니다.\" \"이제 나의 아내를 풀어 주시오.\""], ["“Set now my wife at liberty.”", "\"나는 그러지 않겠습니다,\""], ["“I will not,”", "그가 말하였다."], ["said he, “until I see Pryderi and Rhiannon with me free.”", "\"프리데리와 리아논이 풀려나 내 곁에 있는 것을 볼 때까지는요.\""], ["“Behold, here they come,”", "\"보시오, 여기 그들이 오고 있소,\""], ["he answered.", "그가 대답하였다."]], [["And thereupon behold Pryderi and Rhiannon.", "그리고 그러자, 보라, 프리데리와 리아논이었다."], ["And he rose up to meet them, and greeted them, and sat down beside them.", "그리고 그는 일어나 그들을 맞이하고, 그들에게 인사하며, 그들 곁에 앉았다."], ["“Ah, chieftain, set now my wife at liberty,”", "\"아, 족장이여, 이제 나의 아내를 풀어 주시오,\""], ["said the bishop.", "주교가 말하였다."], ["“Hast thou not received all thou didst ask?”", "\"그대는 청한 모든 것을 받지 않았소?\""], ["“I will release her, gladly,”", "\"기꺼이 그녀를 풀어 주겠습니다,\""], ["said he.", "그가 말하였다."], ["And thereupon he set her free.", "그리고 그러자 그는 그녀를 풀어 주었다."]], [["Then he struck her with a magic wand, and she was changed back into a young woman, the fairest ever seen.", "그러고 나서 그는 마법의 지팡이로 그녀를 쳤고, 그녀는 이제껏 본 가운데 가장 아름다운 젊은 여인으로 다시 변하였다."]], [["“Look round upon thy land,”", "\"그대의 땅을 둘러보시오,\""], ["said he, “and thou wilt see it all tilled and peopled as it was in its best estate.”", "그가 말하였다. \"그러면 그대는 그것이 모두 가장 좋았던 시절처럼 경작되고 사람들로 가득 찬 것을 볼 것이오.\""], ["And he rose up and looked forth.", "그리고 그는 일어나 밖을 내다보았다."], ["And when he looked he saw all the lands tilled, and full of herds and dwellings.", "그리고 그가 내다보았을 때, 그는 모든 땅이 경작되어 가축 떼와 거처로 가득 찬 것을 보았다."]], [["And thus ends this portion of the Mabinogi.", "그리하여 마비노기의 이 부분이 끝난다."]], [["————", "————"]], [["The following allusions to the preceding story are found in a letter of the poet Southey to John Rickman, Esq., dated June 6th, 1802:", "앞의 이야기를 암시하는 다음의 언급은 시인 사우디가 1802년 6월 6일 자로 존 리크먼 님에게 보낸 편지에서 발견된다."]], [["“You will read the Mabinogeon, concerning which I ought to have talked to you.", "\"당신은 마비노게온을 읽으실 텐데, 그것에 관하여 제가 당신께 이야기를 드렸어야 했습니다."], ["In the last, that most odd and Arabian-like story of the mouse, mention is made of a begging scholar, that helps to the date; but where did the Cymri get the imagination that could produce such a tale?", "그 마지막 편에 나오는, 더없이 기이하고 아라비아풍인 쥐 이야기에는 동냥하는 학생에 대한 언급이 있는데, 그것이 연대를 짐작하는 데 도움이 됩니다. 그러나 킴리족은 그러한 이야기를 만들어 낼 수 있는 상상력을 대체 어디서 얻었을까요?"], ["That enchantment of the basin hanging by the chain from heaven is in the wildest spirit of the Arabian Nights.", "하늘에서 사슬에 매달려 늘어진 그 사발의 마법은 「아라비안 나이트」의 더없이 거친 정신을 담고 있습니다. 그러한 허구가 웨일스어로 존재한다는 것에 저는 완전히 경악하였습니다."], ["I am perfectly astonished that such fictions should exist in Welsh.", "그것들은 로맨스의 기원에 대해 아무런 빛도 던져 주지 않으니, 모든 것이 우리가 그 용어로 의미하는 바와는 전혀 다르기 때문입니다. 하지만 그것들은 허구의 새로운 세계를 열어 줍니다."], ["They throw no light on the origin of romance, everything being utterly dissimilar to what we mean by that term, but they do open a new world of fiction; and if the date of their language be fixed about the twelfth or thirteenth century, I cannot but think the mythological substance is of far earlier date; very probably brought from the East by some of the first settlers or conquerors.”", "그리고 만일 그 언어의 연대가 십이 세기나 십삼 세기쯤으로 정해진다면, 저는 그 신화적 실체가 훨씬 더 이른 시대의 것이라 생각하지 않을 수 없습니다. 아마도 최초의 정착민이나 정복자 가운데 누군가가 동방에서 가져온 것이겠지요.\""]], [["————", "————"]], [["# KILWICH AND OLWEN", "# KILWICH AND OLWEN"]], [["KILYDD, a son of Prince Kelyddon, desired a wife as a helpmate, and the wife that he chose was Goleudid, the daughter of Prince Anlawd.", "켈리돈 왕자의 아들 킬리드는 자신을 도와줄 아내를 얻고자 했는데, 그가 택한 아내는 안라우드 왕자의 딸 골레우디드였다."], ["And after their union the people put up prayers that they might have an heir.", "그리고 두 사람이 부부가 된 뒤에 백성들은 그들에게 후사가 있기를 기도해 올렸다."], ["And they had a son through the prayers of the people; and called his name Kilwich.", "그리하여 백성들의 기도 덕분에 두 사람은 아들을 얻었으니, 그 이름을 킬위치라 하였다."]], [["After this the boy’s mother, Goleudid, the daughter of Prince Anlawd, fell sick.", "이 일이 있은 뒤에 소년의 어머니인 안라우드 왕자의 딸 골레우디드가 병이 들었다."], ["Then she called her husband to her, and said to him, “Of this sickness I shall die, and thou wilt take another wife.", "그러자 그녀는 남편을 자기 곁으로 불러 이렇게 말했다. \"이 병으로 저는 죽게 될 것이고, 당신은 다른 아내를 얻으시겠지요."], ["Now wives are the gift of the Lord, but it would be wrong for thee to harm thy son.", "본디 아내란 주님께서 내려 주시는 선물이오나, 당신이 아들에게 해를 끼친다면 그것은 옳지 못한 일이옵니다."], ["Therefore I charge thee that thou take not a wife until thou see a briar with two blossoms upon my grave.”", "그러니 제 무덤에 두 송이 꽃이 핀 들장미가 돋는 것을 보시기 전까지는 다른 아내를 얻지 마시기를 당신께 당부드립니다.\""], ["And this he promised her.", "그러자 그는 그녀에게 그리하겠노라 약속했다."], ["Then she besought him to dress her grave every year, that no weeds might grow thereon.", "그러자 그녀는 무덤에 잡초가 자라지 않도록 해마다 무덤을 손질해 달라고 그에게 간청했다."], ["So the queen died.", "그리하여 왕비는 죽었다."], ["Now the king sent an attendant every morning to see if anything were growing upon the grave.", "그리하여 왕은 매일 아침마다 시종을 보내어 무덤에 무엇이 돋아나는지 살피게 하였다."], ["And at the end of the seventh year they neglected that which they had promised to the queen.", "그런데 일곱 해째가 저물 무렵에 그들은 왕비에게 했던 약속을 소홀히 하게 되었다."]], [["One day the king went to hunt; and he rode to the place of burial, to see the grave, and to know if it were time that he should take a wife: and the king saw the briar.", "어느 날 왕은 사냥을 나갔다. 그리고 그는 무덤을 살펴보고, 또 자신이 아내를 얻을 때가 되었는지 알아보고자 매장지로 말을 달려갔다."], ["And when he saw it, the king took counsel where he should find a wife.", "그리고 왕은 들장미를 보았다. 왕은 그것을 보자 어디서 아내를 구할 것인지 의논했다."], ["Said one of his counsellors, “I know a wife that will suit thee well; and she is the wife of King Doged.”", "그의 한 고문이 말했다. \"전하께 잘 어울릴 아내를 제가 알고 있사옵니다."], ["And they resolved to go to seek her; and they slew the king, and brought away his wife.", "도게드 왕의 아내이옵니다.\" 그리하여 그들은 그녀를 찾으러 가기로 결정했다."], ["And they conquered the kings’ lands.", "그리고 그들은 그 왕을 죽이고 그의 아내를 데려왔다."], ["And he married the widow of King Doged, the sister of Yspadaden Penkawr.", "그리고 그들은 그 왕의 영지를 정복했다. 그리하여 그는 도게드 왕의 미망인, 곧 이스파다덴 펜카우르의 누이와 혼인했다."]], [["And one day his stepmother said to Kilwich, “It were well for thee to have a wife.”", "어느 날 그의 계모가 킬위치에게 말했다. \"네가 아내를 얻는 것이 좋겠구나.\""], ["“I am not yet of an age to wed,”", "\"저는 아직 혼인할 나이가 아니옵니다.\""], ["answered the youth.", "젊은이가 대답했다."], ["Then said she unto him, “I declare to thee that it is thy destiny not to be suited with a wife until thou obtain Olwen, the daughter of Yspadaden Penkawr.”", "그러자 그녀가 그에게 말했다. \"내 너에게 일러두건대, 이스파다덴 펜카우르의 딸 올웬을 얻기 전에는 어떤 아내도 너에게 어울리지 않는 것이 너의 운명이니라.\""], ["And the youth blushed, and the love of the maiden diffused itself through all his frame, although he had never seen her.", "그러자 젊은이는 얼굴을 붉혔고, 비록 그 처녀를 한 번도 본 적이 없었음에도 그녀를 향한 사랑이 온몸으로 퍼져 나갔다."], ["And his father inquired of him, “What has come over thee, my son, and what aileth thee?”", "그러자 그의 아버지가 그에게 물었다. \"아들아, 너에게 무슨 일이 생긴 것이며, 무엇이 너를 괴롭히는 것이냐?\""], ["“My stepmother has declared to me that I shall never have a wife until I obtain Olwen, the daughter of Yspadaden Penkawr.”", "\"제 계모께서 이스파다덴 펜카우르의 딸 올웬을 얻기 전에는 제가 결코 아내를 얻지 못하리라 일러 주셨습니다.\""], ["“That will be easy for thee,”", "\"그것은 너에게 쉬운 일이 될 것이다.\""], ["answered his father.", "그의 아버지가 대답했다."], ["“Arthur is thy cousin.", "\"아서가 너의 사촌이니라."], ["Go, therefore, unto Arthur, to cut thy hair, and ask this of him as a boon.”", "그러니 아서에게 가서 머리카락을 자르고, 이 일을 그에게 청하여 은혜를 구하도록 하여라.\""]], [["And the youth pricked forth upon a steed with head dappled gray, four winters old, firm of limb, with shell-formed hoofs, having a bridle of linked gold on his head, and upon him a saddle of costly gold.", "그리하여 젊은이는 잿빛 얼룩이 진 머리를 가진, 네 해를 묵어 사지가 단단하고 조개 모양의 발굽을 가진 군마에 올라타 박차를 가하며 앞으로 나아갔다. 말의 머리에는 사슬로 엮은 황금 굴레가 씌워져 있었고, 그 등에는 값비싼 황금 안장이 얹혀 있었다."], ["And in the youth’s hand were two spears of silver, sharp, well-tempered, headed with steel, three ells in length, of an edge to wound the wind, and cause blood to flow, and swifter than the fall of the dew-drop from the blade of reed-grass, when the dew of June is at the heaviest.", "그리고 젊은이의 손에는 날카롭고 잘 단련된 강철 촉을 단 은빛 창 두 자루가 들려 있었으니, 그 길이는 세 엘이었고, 그 날은 바람조차 베어 피를 흘리게 할 만큼 예리하였고, 유월의 이슬이 가장 무겁게 맺힐 무렵 갈대 잎 끝에서 떨어지는 이슬방울보다도 빨랐다."], ["A gold-hilted sword was upon his thigh, the blade of which was gilded, bearing a cross of inlaid gold of the hue of the lightning of heaven. His war-horn was of ivory.", "그의 넓적다리에는 황금 자루가 달린 검이 차여 있었으니, 그 날은 도금되어 있었고, 하늘의 번개와 같은 빛을 띤 황금 십자가 상감으로 새겨져 있었다."], ["Before him were two brindled, white-breasted greyhounds, having strong collars of rubies about their necks, reaching from the shoulder to the ear.", "그의 전쟁용 뿔나팔은 상아로 만들어진 것이었다. 그의 앞에는 어깨에서 귀에까지 이르는 루비로 된 단단한 목걸이를 두른, 가슴이 흰 얼룩무늬 사냥개 두 마리가 있었다."], ["And the one that was upon the left side bounded across to the right side, and the one on the right to the left, and, like two sea-swallows, sported around him.", "그리고 왼편에 있던 놈은 오른편으로 뛰어넘어 가고, 오른편에 있던 놈은 왼편으로 뛰어넘어 가면서, 마치 두 마리의 바다제비처럼 그의 주위에서 뛰놀았다."], ["And his courser cast up four sods, with his four hoofs, like four swallows in the air, about his head, now above, now below.", "그리고 그의 준마는 네 발굽으로 네 무더기의 흙을 차올렸으니, 그것은 마치 그의 머리 둘레에서, 때로는 위로 때로는 아래로 나는 네 마리의 제비와도 같았다."], ["About him was a four-cornered cloth of purple, and an apple of gold was at each corner, and every one of the apples was of the value of an hundred kine.", "그의 몸에는 네 귀퉁이가 진 자줏빛 천이 둘려 있었고, 그 귀퉁이마다 황금 사과가 달려 있었으며, 그 사과 하나하나는 암소 백 마리의 값어치가 있었다."], ["And there was precious gold of the value of three hundred kine upon his shoes, and upon his stirrups, from his knee to the tip of his toe.", "그리고 그의 신발과 등자에는 무릎에서 발끝까지 암소 삼백 마리의 값어치가 나가는 귀한 황금이 박혀 있었다."], ["And the blade of grass bent not beneath him, so light was his courser’s tread, as he journeyed toward the gate of Arthur’s palace.", "그리고 그가 아서의 궁전 문을 향해 나아갈 때, 그의 준마의 걸음이 어찌나 가벼웠던지 그 발밑에서는 풀잎 하나 휘어지지 않았다."]], [["Spoke the youth: “Is there a porter?”", "젊은이가 입을 열었다. \"문지기가 있는가?\""], ["“There is; and if thou holdest not thy peace, small will be thy welcome.", "\"있소. 그리고 그대가 잠자코 있지 않는다면, 환대받지 못할 것이오."], ["I am Arthur’s porter every first day of January.”", "나는 정월 초하루마다 아서의 문지기 노릇을 하는 자라오.\""], ["“Open the portal.”", "\"문을 열어 주게.\""], ["“I will not open it.”", "\"나는 열지 않겠소.\""], ["“Wherefore not?”", "\"어찌하여 열지 않는가?\""], ["“The knife is in the meat, and the drink is in the horn, and there is revelry in Arthur’s hall; and none may enter therein but the son of a king of a privileged country, or a craftsman bringing his craft.", "\"고기에는 칼이 꽂혀 있고, 뿔잔에는 술이 담겨 있으며, 아서의 홀에서는 흥겨운 잔치가 벌어지고 있소. 그리고 특권을 가진 나라의 왕자나 자기 솜씨를 가지고 오는 장인이 아니면 누구도 그 안에 들어갈 수 없소."], ["But there will be refreshment for thy dogs and for thy horse; and for thee there will be collops cooked and peppered, and luscious wine, and mirthful songs; and food for fifty men shall be brought unto thee in the guest-chamber, where the stranger and the sons of other countries eat, who come not into the precincts of the palace of Arthur.", "허나 그대의 개들과 말에게는 먹이가 마련될 것이며, 그대에게는 후추를 친 구운 고깃점과 감미로운 포도주와 흥겨운 노래가 마련될 것이오. 그리고 아서의 궁전 경내에 들어오지 못하는 이방인과 다른 나라의 자제들이 먹는 손님방에서, 그대에게는 쉰 사람 몫의 음식이 차려질 것이오."], ["Thou wilt fare no worse there than thou wouldst with Arthur in the court.", "그곳에서 그대는 궁정에서 아서와 함께 지내는 것보다 못한 대접을 받지 않을 것이오."], ["A lady shall smooth thy couch, and shall lull thee with songs; and early to-morrow morning, when the gate is open for the multitude that came hither to-day, for thee shall it be opened first, and thou mayest sit in the place that thou shalt choose in Arthur’s hall, from the upper end to the lower.”", "한 귀부인이 그대의 잠자리를 매만지고, 노래로 그대를 잠재워 줄 것이오. 그리고 내일 이른 아침, 오늘 이리로 온 무리에게 문이 열릴 때, 그대를 위해 먼저 그 문이 열릴 것이고, 그대는 아서의 홀에서 윗자리에서 아랫자리에 이르기까지 그대가 택하는 자리에 앉을 수 있을 것이오.\""], ["Said the youth: “That will I not do.", "젊은이가 말했다. \"그리하지 않겠다."], ["If thou openest the gate, it is well.", "그대가 문을 연다면 좋은 일이다."], ["If thou dost not open it, I will bring disgrace upon thy lord, and evil report upon thee.", "그러나 그대가 문을 열지 않는다면, 나는 그대의 주군에게 치욕을 안기고 그대에게 악평을 가져다줄 것이다."], ["And I will set up three shouts at this very gate, than which none were ever heard more deadly.”", "그리고 나는 바로 이 문 앞에서 일찍이 들어 본 적 없는 가장 끔찍한 세 차례의 함성을 지를 것이다.\""], ["“What clamor soever thou mayest make,”", "\"그대가 아서의 궁전의 법도를 거슬러 무슨 소란을 일으킨다 하더라도,\""], ["said Glewlwyd, the porter, “against the laws of Arthur’s palace, shalt thou not enter therein, until I first go and speak with Arthur.”", "문지기 글레울위드가 말했다. \"내가 먼저 가서 아서와 이야기하기 전까지는 그대는 그 안에 들어가지 못할 것이오.\""]], [["Then Glewlwyd went into the hall.", "그리하여 글레울위드는 홀 안으로 들어갔다."], ["And Arthur said to him, “Hast thou news from the gate?”", "그러자 아서가 그에게 말했다. \"문에서 무슨 소식이라도 가져왔느냐?\""], ["“Half of my life is passed,”", "\"제 인생의 절반이 지나갔고,\""], ["said Glewlwyd, “and half of thine.", "글레울위드가 말했다."], ["I was heretofore in Kaer Se and Asse, in Sach and Salach, in Lotor and Fotor, and I have been in India the Great and India the Lesser, and I have also been in Europe and Africa, and in the islands of Corsica, and I was present when thou didst conquer Greece in the East.", "\"전하의 인생의 절반도 지나갔습니다. 저는 전에 카에르 세와 아세에, 사크와 살라크에, 로토르와 포토르에 있었사옵고, 큰 인도와 작은 인도에도 가 보았으며, 유럽과 아프리카에도, 또 코르시카의 섬들에도 가 보았고, 전하께서 동방에서 그리스를 정복하실 때 그 자리에 있었사옵니다."], ["Nine supreme sovereigns, handsome men, saw we there, but never did I behold a man of equal dignity with him who is now at the door of the portal.”", "그곳에서 우리는 잘생긴 아홉 명의 지엄한 군주를 보았으나, 지금 문간에 있는 그 사람만큼 위엄을 갖춘 자는 일찍이 본 적이 없사옵니다.\""], ["Then said Arthur: “If walking thou didst enter here, return thou running.", "그러자 아서가 말했다. \"걸어서 이곳에 들어왔다면, 달려서 돌아가거라."], ["It is unbecoming to keep such a man as thou sayest he is in the wind and the rain.”", "네가 말한 그런 사람을 비바람 속에 세워 두는 것은 온당치 못한 일이다.\" 케이가 말했다."], ["Said Kay: “By the hand of my friend, if thou wouldst follow my counsel, thou wouldst not break through the laws of the court because of him.”", "\"제 벗의 손을 두고 맹세하건대, 전하께서 제 충언을 따르신다면 그자 때문에 궁정의 법도를 깨뜨리지는 않으실 것입니다.\" \"그렇지 않다, 복된 케이여,\""], ["“Not so, blessed Kay,”", "아서가 말했다."], ["said Arthur; “it is an honor to us to be resorted to, and the greater our courtesy, the greater will be our renown and our fame and our glory.”", "\"사람들이 우리를 찾아오는 것은 우리에게 명예로운 일이며, 우리의 예우가 클수록 우리의 명성과 영예와 영광 또한 그만큼 커질 것이다.\""]], [["And Glewlwyd came to the gate, and opened the gate before Kilwich; and although all dismounted upon the horse-block at the gate, yet did he not dismount, but he rode in upon his charger.", "그리하여 글레울위드는 문으로 가서 킬위치 앞에서 문을 열었다. 그리고 모두가 문 앞의 디딤대에서 말에서 내렸으나, 그는 말에서 내리지 않고 자신의 군마를 탄 채 안으로 들어갔다."], ["Then said he, “Greeting be unto thee, sovereign ruler of this island, and be this greeting no less unto the lowest than unto the highest, and be it equally unto thy guests, and thy warriors, and thy chieftains; let all partake of it as completely as thyself.", "그러고는 이렇게 말했다. \"이 섬의 지엄한 통치자시여, 그대에게 인사를 올립니다."], ["And complete be thy favor, and thy fame, and thy glory, throughout all this island.”", "그리고 이 인사가 가장 높은 자에게 못지않게 가장 낮은 자에게도 미치기를, 또한 그대의 손님들과 전사들과 족장들에게도 똑같이 미치기를 바랍니다. 모두가 그대 자신과 마찬가지로 온전히 이 인사를 받기를 바랍니다."], ["“Greeting unto thee also,”", "그리고 그대의 은총과 명성과 영광이 이 섬 전역에 걸쳐 온전하기를 바랍니다.\" \"그대에게도 인사를 보내노라,\""], ["said Arthur; “sit thou between two of my warriors, and thou shalt have minstrels before thee, and thou shalt enjoy the privileges of a king born to a throne, as long as thou remainest here.", "아서가 말했다. \"내 전사 두 사람 사이에 앉으라."], ["And when I disperse my presents to the visitors and strangers in this court, they shall be in thy hand at my commencing.”", "그대 앞에는 악사들이 있을 것이고, 그대가 이곳에 머무는 동안에는 왕좌를 타고난 왕의 특권을 누릴 것이다. 그리고 내가 이 궁정의 방문객과 이방인에게 선물을 나누어 줄 때, 내가 시작할 무렵 그것들이 그대의 손에 먼저 들어갈 것이다.\""], ["Said the youth, “I came not here to consume meat and drink; but if I obtain the boon that I seek, I will requite it thee, and extol thee; but if I have it not, I will bear forth thy dispraise to the four quarters of the world, as far as thy renown has extended.”", "젊은이가 말했다. \"저는 고기와 술을 축내려고 이곳에 온 것이 아닙니다."], ["Then said Arthur, “Since thou wilt not remain here, chieftain, thou shalt receive the boon, whatsoever thy tongue may name, as far as the wind dries, and the rain moistens, and the sun revolves, and the sea encircles, and the earth extends; save only my ship Prydwen, and my mantle, and Caliburn, my sword, and Rhongomyant, my lance, and Guenever, my wife.", "그러나 제가 구하는 은혜를 얻는다면, 저는 그것을 그대에게 갚고 그대를 칭송할 것입니다. 허나 그것을 얻지 못한다면, 저는 그대의 명성이 미친 데까지 세상의 사방으로 그대를 헐뜯는 말을 가지고 나아갈 것입니다.\""], ["By the truth of Heaven, thou shalt have it cheerfully, name what thou wilt.”", "그러자 아서가 말했다. \"족장이여, 그대가 이곳에 머물지 않겠다 하니, 바람이 말리고 비가 적시고 해가 도는 곳, 또 바다가 에워싸고 땅이 펼쳐진 곳에 이르기까지, 그대의 혀가 무엇을 이름하든 그대는 그 은혜를 받게 될 것이다."], ["“I would that thou bless my hair,”", "다만 나의 배 프리드웬과 나의 망토, 나의 검 칼리번, 나의 창 롱고미안트, 그리고 나의 아내 귀네비어만은 예외다. 하늘의 진리를 두고 맹세하건대, 그대는 그것을 기꺼이 얻을 것이니, 무엇이든 원하는 것을 이름하라.\""], ["said he.", "\"저는 그대가 제 머리카락을 축복해 주시기를 바랍니다.\""], ["“That shall be granted thee.”", "그가 말했다. \"그것을 그대에게 허락하노라.\""]], [["And Arthur took a golden comb, and scissors whereof the loops were of silver, and he combed his hair.", "그리하여 아서는 황금 빗과 고리가 은으로 된 가위를 들어 그의 머리카락을 빗어 주었다."], ["And Arthur inquired of him who he was; “for my heart warms unto thee, and I know that thou art come of my blood.", "그리고 아서는 그가 누구인지 물었다. \"내 마음이 그대에게 따뜻해지는 것을 보니, 그대가 내 핏줄을 이은 자임을 알겠다."], ["Tell me, therefore, who thou art.”", "그러니 그대가 누구인지 말해 보라.\""], ["“I will tell thee,”", "\"말씀드리겠습니다.\""], ["said the youth.", "젊은이가 말했다."], ["“I am Kilwich, the son of Kilydd, the son of Prince Kelyddon, by Goleudyd, my mother, the daughter of Prince Anlawd.”", "\"저는 킬위치, 켈리돈 왕자의 아들 킬리드의 아들이며, 제 어머니 골레우디드, 곧 안라우드 왕자의 딸의 소생입니다.\" \"그것은 참이로다,\""], ["“That is true,”", "아서가 말했다."], ["said Arthur; “thou art my cousin.", "\"그대는 나의 사촌이다."], ["Whatsoever boon thou mayest ask, thou shalt receive, be it what it may that thy tongue shall name.”", "그대가 무슨 은혜를 청하든, 그대의 혀가 무엇을 이름하든 그대는 그것을 받게 될 것이다.\""], ["“Pledge the truth of Heaven and the faith of thy kingdom thereof.”", "\"하늘의 진리와 그대 왕국의 신의로써 그것을 맹세해 주십시오.\""], ["“I pledge it thee gladly.”", "\"기꺼이 그대에게 맹세하노라.\""], ["“I crave of thee, then, that thou obtain for me Olwen, the daughter of Yspadaden Penkawr, to wife; and this boon I likewise seek at the hands of thy warriors.", "\"그러면 저는 그대께 청하옵건대, 이스파다덴 펜카우르의 딸 올웬을 제 아내로 얻게 해 주십시오. 그리고 이 은혜를 저는 그대의 전사들의 손에도 똑같이 구합니다."], ["I seek it from Kay and from Bedwyr; and from Gwynn, the son of Nudd, and Gadwy, the son of Geraint, and Prince Flewddur Flam, and Iona, king of France, and Sel, the son of Selgi, and Taliesin, the chief of the bards, and Geraint, the son of Erbin, Garanwyn, the son of Kay, and Amren, the son of Bedwyr, Ol, the son of Olwyd, Bedwin, the bishop, Guenever, the chief lady, and Guenhywach, her sister, Morved, the daughter of Urien, and Gwenlian Deg, the majestic maiden, Creiddylad,[68] the daughter of Lludd, the constant maiden, and Ewaedah, the daughter of Kynvelyn,[69] the half-man.”", "저는 그것을 케이와 베드위르에게서 구하며, 또한 누드의 아들 그윈, 게레인트의 아들 가드위, 플레우두르 플람 왕자, 프랑스의 왕 이오나, 셀기의 아들 셀, 음유시인들의 우두머리 탈리에신, 에르빈의 아들 게레인트, 케이의 아들 가란윈, 베드위르의 아들 암렌, 올위드의 아들 올, 주교 베드윈, 우두머리 귀부인 귀네비어와 그녀의 누이 귀엔히와크, 우리엔의 딸 모르베드, 위풍당당한 처녀 그웬리안 데그, 변함없는 처녀 루드의 딸 크레이딜라드, 그리고 반인반수 킨벨린의 딸 에와에다에게서 구합니다.\""], ["All these did Kilwich, the son of Kilydd, adjure to obtain his boon.", "킬리드의 아들 킬위치는 자신의 은혜를 얻고자 이 모든 이를 두고 간청했다."]], [["Then said Arthur, “O chieftain, I have never heard of the maiden of whom thou speakest, nor of her kindred, but I will gladly send messengers in search of her.", "그러자 아서가 말했다. \"오 족장이여, 나는 그대가 말하는 처녀에 대해서도, 그녀의 친족에 대해서도 들은 바가 없으나, 기꺼이 그녀를 찾으러 전령들을 보내겠노라."], ["Give me time to seek her.”", "그녀를 찾을 시간을 내게 다오.\""], ["And the youth said, “I will willingly grant from this night to that at the end of the year to do so.”", "그러자 젊은이가 말했다. \"오늘 밤부터 한 해가 저무는 그날까지 기꺼이 그렇게 하도록 허락하겠습니다.\""], ["Then Arthur sent messengers to every land within his dominions to seek for the maiden, and at the end of the year Arthur’s messengers returned without having gained any knowledge or intelligence concerning Olwen, more than on the first day.", "그리하여 아서는 그 처녀를 찾으러 자신의 영토 안 모든 땅으로 전령들을 보냈고, 한 해가 저물 무렵 아서의 전령들은 첫날과 마찬가지로 올웬에 대한 어떤 지식이나 소식도 얻지 못한 채 돌아왔다. 그러자 킬위치가 말했다."], ["Then said Kilwich, “Every one has received his boon, and I yet lack mine.", "\"모두가 자신의 은혜를 받았으나, 저는 아직 제 은혜를 얻지 못했습니다."], ["I will depart, and bear away thy honor with me.”", "저는 떠나겠으며, 그대의 명예를 가지고 떠나겠습니다.\""], ["Then said Kay, “Rash chieftain!", "그러자 케이가 말했다. \"성급한 족장이여!"], ["dost thou reproach Arthur?", "그대가 아서를 책망하는 것인가?"], ["Go with us, and we will not part until thou dost either confess that the maiden exists not in the world, or until we obtain her.”", "우리와 함께 가자. 그리고 그대가 그 처녀가 이 세상에 존재하지 않음을 인정하거나, 우리가 그녀를 얻기 전까지 우리는 헤어지지 않을 것이다.\""], ["Thereupon Kay rose up.", "그러고 나서 케이가 일어섰다."], ["And Arthur called Bedwyr, who never shrank from any enterprise upon which Kay was bound.", "그리고 아서는 베드위르를 불렀으니, 그는 케이가 떠나는 어떤 모험에서도 결코 물러서는 법이 없는 자였다."], ["None were equal to him in swiftness throughout this island except Arthur alone; and although he was one handed, three warriors could not shed blood faster than he on the field of battle.", "이 섬 전역에서 아서 한 사람을 제외하고는 누구도 그의 날쌤에 견줄 자가 없었고, 비록 그가 외팔이였으나 전장에서 그보다 더 빨리 피를 흘리게 할 수 있는 전사는 세 사람도 없었다."]], [["And Arthur called to Kyndelig, the guide, “Go thou upon this expedition with the chieftain.”", "그리고 아서는 길잡이 킨델리그를 불렀다. \"그대도 이 원정에 족장과 함께 가도록 하라.\""], ["For as good a guide was he in a land which he had never seen as he was in his own.", "그는 한 번도 본 적 없는 땅에서도 자기 고향에서만큼이나 훌륭한 길잡이였기 때문이다."]], [["He called Gurhyr Gwalstat, because he knew all tongues.", "아서는 모든 언어를 아는 구르히르 그왈스타트를 불렀다."]], [["He called Gawain, the son of Gwyar, because he never returned home without achieving the adventure of which he went in quest.", "아서는 그위아르의 아들 가웨인을 불렀으니, 그는 자신이 찾아 떠난 모험을 이루지 않고는 결코 집으로 돌아오는 법이 없었기 때문이다."]], [["And Arthur called Meneu, the son of Teirgwed, in order that, if they went into a savage country, he might cast a charm and an illusion over them, so that none might see them, whilst they could see every one.", "그리고 아서는 테이르그웨드의 아들 메네우를 불렀으니, 그들이 야만의 땅으로 들어갈 경우, 그가 그들에게 주문과 환영을 걸어 그들은 누구나 볼 수 있으되 누구도 그들을 보지 못하게 하기 위함이었다."]], [["They journeyed until they came to a vast open plain, wherein they saw a great castle, which was the fairest of the castles of the world.", "그들은 광활한 평원에 이를 때까지 길을 갔는데, 그곳에서 그들은 세상의 성들 가운데 가장 아름다운 거대한 성을 보았다."], ["And when they came before the castle, they beheld a vast flock of sheep.", "그리고 그들이 그 성 앞에 이르렀을 때, 그들은 거대한 양 떼를 보았다."], ["And upon the top of a mound there was a herdsman keeping the sheep.", "그리고 둔덕 꼭대기에는 한 목자가 양을 지키고 있었다."], ["And a rug made of skins was upon him, and by his side was a shaggy mastiff, larger than a steed nine winters old.", "그리고 그의 몸에는 가죽으로 만든 깔개가 둘려 있었고, 그의 곁에는 아홉 해를 묵은 군마보다 더 큰 털북숭이 맹견이 있었다."]], [["Then said Kay, “Gurhyr Gwalstat, go thou and salute yonder man.”", "그러자 케이가 말했다. \"구르히르 그왈스타트, 그대가 가서 저기 있는 사람에게 인사를 건네라.\""], ["“Kay,”", "\"케이,\""], ["said he, “I engaged not to go further than thou thyself.”", "그가 말했다. \"나는 그대 자신보다 더 멀리 가지 않기로 했소.\""], ["“Let us go then together,”", "\"그럼 함께 가세.\" 케이가 대답했다."], ["answered Kay.", "메네우가 말했다."], ["Said Meneu, “Fear not to go thither, for I will cast a spell upon the dog, so that he shall injure no one.”", "\"그리로 가기를 두려워 마시오. 내가 저 개에게 주문을 걸어 아무도 해치지 못하게 하겠소.\""], ["And they went up to the mound whereon the herdsman was, and they said to him, “How dost thou fare, herdsman?”", "그리하여 그들은 목자가 있는 둔덕으로 올라가 그에게 말했다. \"어떻게 지내시오, 목자여?\""], ["“Not less fair be it to you than to me.”", "\"당신들 못지않게 내게도 좋은 일이 있기를.\""], ["“Whose are the sheep that thou dost keep, and to whom does yonder castle belong?”", "\"그대가 지키는 양은 누구의 것이며, 저기 있는 성은 누구의 것이오?\""], ["“Stupid are ye, truly!", "\"당신들은 정말 어리석구려!"], ["not to know that this is the castle of Yspadaden Penkawr.", "이것이 이스파다덴 펜카우르의 성인 줄도 모르다니."], ["And ye also, who are ye?”", "그리고 당신들은 누구요?\""], ["“We are an embassy from Arthur, come to seek Olwen, the daughter of Yspadaden Penkawr.”", "\"우리는 아서가 보낸 사절단으로, 이스파다덴 펜카우르의 딸 올웬을 찾으러 왔소.\""], ["“O men!", "\"오 사람들이여!"], ["the mercy of Heaven be upon you; do not that for all the world.", "하늘의 자비가 당신들에게 깃들기를. 무슨 일이 있어도 그 일은 하지 마시오."], ["None who ever came hither on this quest has returned alive.”", "이 일로 이곳에 온 자치고 살아 돌아간 자는 아무도 없소.\""], ["And the herdsman rose up.", "그러고 나서 목자가 일어섰다."], ["And as he rose Kilwich gave unto him a ring of gold.", "그리고 그가 일어서자 킬위치가 그에게 황금 반지 하나를 주었다."], ["And he went home and gave the ring to his spouse to keep.", "그리고 그는 집으로 가서 그 반지를 아내에게 주어 간수하게 했다."], ["And she took the ring when it was given her, and she said, “Whence came this ring, for thou art not wont to have good fortune.”", "그리고 그녀는 그것을 받자 이렇게 말했다. \"이 반지가 어디서 났나요?"], ["“O wife, him to whom this ring belonged thou shalt see here this evening.”", "당신은 본디 행운을 누리는 사람이 아닌데.\" \"여보, 이 반지의 주인을 당신은 오늘 저녁 이곳에서 보게 될 것이오.\""], ["“And who is he?”", "\"그게 누구인가요?\""], ["asked the woman.", "여인이 물었다."], ["“Kilwich, the son of Kilydd, by Goleudid, the daughter of Prince Anlawd, who is come to seek Olwen as his wife.”", "\"킬위치, 골레우디드, 곧 안라우드 왕자의 딸의 소생인 킬리드의 아들로, 올웬을 아내로 얻으러 온 자라오.\""], ["And when she heard that, she had joy that her nephew, the son of her sister, was coming to her, and sorrow, because she had never known any one depart alive who had come on that quest.", "그리고 그 말을 들은 그녀는, 자기 누이의 아들인 조카가 자기에게 온다는 것에 기뻐했고, 또한 그 일로 이곳에 온 자치고 살아서 떠나는 이를 한 번도 본 적이 없었기에 슬퍼했다."]], [["And the men went forward to the gate of the herdsman’s dwelling.", "그리하여 사람들은 목자의 집 문으로 나아갔다."], ["And when she heard their footsteps approaching, she ran out with joy to meet them.", "그리고 그녀가 그들의 발소리가 다가오는 것을 듣자, 기뻐하며 그들을 맞으러 달려 나왔다."], ["And Kay snatched a billet out of the pile.", "그리고 케이는 장작더미에서 통나무 하나를 낚아챘다."], ["And when she met them, she sought to throw her arms about their necks.", "그리고 그녀가 그들을 맞이하며 그들의 목을 끌어안으려 했다."], ["And Kay placed the log between her two hands, and she squeezed it so that if became a twisted coil.", "그러자 케이가 통나무를 그녀의 두 손 사이에 끼워 넣었고, 그녀가 그것을 어찌나 세게 쥐었던지 그것은 비틀린 똬리가 되고 말았다."], ["“O woman,”", "\"오 여인이여,\""], ["said Kay, “if thou hadst squeezed me thus, none could ever again have set their affections on me.", "케이가 말했다. \"그대가 나를 이렇게 쥐었더라면, 누구도 다시는 내게 애정을 품지 못했을 것이오."], ["Evil love were this.”", "이것은 고약한 사랑일 것이오.\""], ["They entered into the house and were served; and soon after, they all went forth to amuse themselves.", "그들은 집 안으로 들어가 대접을 받았고, 곧이어 모두 즐기러 밖으로 나갔다."], ["Then the woman opened a stone chest that was before the chimney-corner, and out of it arose a youth with yellow, curling hair.", "그러자 여인은 굴뚝 모퉁이 앞에 있던 돌 궤짝을 열었고, 그 안에서 노랗고 곱슬곱슬한 머리의 젊은이가 일어섰다. 구르히르가 말했다."], ["Said Gurhyr, “It is a pity to hide this youth.", "\"이 젊은이를 숨겨 두다니 안타까운 일이오."], ["I know that it is not his own crime that is thus visited upon him.”", "그에게 이렇게 닥친 것이 그 자신의 죄 때문이 아님을 나는 알겠소.\""], ["“This is but a remnant,”", "\"이 아이는 그저 남은 자식일 뿐이라오.\""], ["said the woman.", "여인이 말했다."], ["“Three and twenty of my sons has Yspadaden Penkawr slain, and I have no more hope of this one than of the others.”", "\"이스파다덴 펜카우르가 내 아들 스물셋을 죽였고, 나는 이 아이에 대해서도 다른 아이들에 대해서와 마찬가지로 더는 희망을 품지 않는다오.\" 그러자 케이가 말했다."], ["Then said Kay, “Let him come and be a companion with me, and he shall not be slain unless I also am slain with him.”", "\"그를 내 동무로 삼게 하시오. 그리고 나도 그와 함께 죽지 않는 한 그는 죽지 않을 것이오.\""], ["And they ate.", "그리고 그들은 음식을 먹었다."], ["And the woman asked them, “Upon what errand come you here?”", "그리고 여인이 그들에게 물었다. \"당신들은 무슨 용무로 이곳에 왔나요?\""], ["“We come to seek Olwen for this youth.”", "\"우리는 이 젊은이를 위해 올웬을 찾으러 왔소.\""], ["Then said the woman, “In the name of Heaven, since no one from the castle hath yet seen you, return again whence you came.”", "그러자 여인이 말했다. \"하늘의 이름으로 청하건대, 성에서 아직 누구도 당신들을 보지 못했으니, 왔던 곳으로 다시 돌아가시오.\""], ["“Heaven is our witness, that we will not return until we have seen the maiden.", "\"하늘이 우리의 증인이니, 우리는 그 처녀를 보기 전까지 돌아가지 않을 것이오."], ["Does she ever come hither, so that she may be seen?”", "그녀가 사람들 눈에 띌 만큼 이곳에 온 적이 있소?\""], ["“She comes here every Saturday to wash her head, and in the vessel where she washes she leaves all her rings, and she never either comes herself or sends any messengers to fetch them.”", "\"그녀는 토요일마다 이곳에 와서 머리를 감는데, 머리를 감는 그릇에 자기 반지를 모두 두고 가며, 그것을 가지러 자기가 직접 오는 일도, 전령을 보내는 일도 결코 없다오.\""], ["“Will she come here if she is sent to?”", "\"사람을 보내면 그녀가 이곳에 오겠소?\""], ["“Heaven knows that I will not destroy my soul, nor will I betray those that trust me; unless you will pledge me your faith that you will not harm her, I will not send to her.”", "\"하늘은 아시거니와 나는 내 영혼을 망치지도, 나를 믿는 자들을 배신하지도 않겠소. 당신들이 그녀를 해치지 않겠노라 내게 신의를 맹세하지 않는 한, 나는 그녀에게 사람을 보내지 않겠소.\""], ["“We pledge it,”", "\"우리가 맹세하겠소.\""], ["said they.", "그들이 말했다."], ["So a message was sent, and she came.", "그리하여 전갈이 보내졌고, 그녀가 왔다."]], [["The maiden was clothed in a robe of flame-colored silk, and about her neck was a collar of ruddy gold, on which were precious emeralds and rubies.", "그 처녀는 불꽃 빛깔의 비단 옷을 입었고, 목에는 귀한 에메랄드와 루비가 박힌 붉은 황금 목걸이를 두르고 있었다."], ["More yellow was her head than the flower of the broom,[70] and her skin was whiter than the foam of the wave, and fairer were her hands and her fingers than the blossoms of the wood-anemone amidst the spray of the meadow fountain.", "그녀의 머리는 금작화 꽃보다도 더 노랬고, 그녀의 살결은 물결의 거품보다도 더 희었으며, 그녀의 손과 손가락은 풀밭 샘물의 물보라 속에 핀 바람꽃의 꽃송이보다도 더 고왔다."], ["The eye of the trained hawk was not brighter than hers.", "잘 훈련된 매의 눈도 그녀의 눈보다 더 빛나지는 못했다."], ["Her bosom was more snowy than the breast of the white swan, her cheek was redder than the reddest roses.", "그녀의 가슴은 흰 백조의 가슴보다도 더 눈처럼 희었고, 그녀의 뺨은 가장 붉은 장미보다도 더 붉었다."], ["Whoso beheld her was filled with her love.", "그녀를 본 자는 누구나 그녀를 향한 사랑으로 가득 찼다."], ["Four white trefoils sprung up wherever she trod.", "그녀가 밟는 곳마다 흰 토끼풀이 네 잎씩 돋아났다."], ["And therefore was she called Olwen.", "그래서 그녀는 올웬이라 불렸다."]], [["She entered the house and sat beside Kilwich upon the foremost bench; and as soon as he saw her, he knew her.", "그녀는 집 안으로 들어와 맨 앞자리 의자에서 킬위치의 곁에 앉았다. 그리고 그는 그녀를 보자마자 그녀를 알아보았다."], ["And Kilwich said unto her, “Ah!", "그리고 킬위치가 그녀에게 말했다. \"아!"], ["maiden, thou art she whom I have loved; come away with me, lest they speak evil of thee and of me.", "처녀여, 그대가 바로 내가 사랑해 온 그 사람이오. 사람들이 그대와 나에 대해 험담하지 못하도록 나와 함께 떠납시다."], ["Many a day have I loved thee.”", "여러 날 동안 나는 그대를 사랑해 왔소.\" \"저는 그렇게 할 수 없어요."], ["“I cannot do this, for I have pledged my faith to my father not to go without his counsel, for his life will last only until the time of my espousals.", "저는 아버지의 뜻 없이는 떠나지 않겠노라 신의를 맹세했고, 아버지의 목숨은 제가 혼인하는 그때까지만 이어질 것이기 때문이에요. 무엇이 일어나야 하든, 그것은 일어나야만 하지요."], ["Whatever is to be, must be.", "하지만 당신이 받아들이신다면 제가 충고를 드리겠어요."], ["But I will give thee advice, if thou wilt take it.", "가셔서 제 아버지께 저를 청하시고, 그분이 당신께 요구하시는 것은 무엇이든 들어주세요."], ["Go, ask me of my father, and that which he shall require of thee, grant it, and thou wilt obtain me; but if thou deny him anything, thou wilt not obtain me, and it will be well for thee if thou escape with thy life.”", "그러면 당신은 저를 얻으실 거예요. 하지만 그분께 무엇이든 거절하신다면 당신은 저를 얻지 못하실 것이며, 당신이 목숨을 부지하고 빠져나오신다면 그것만으로도 다행일 거예요.\""], ["“I promise all this, if occasion offer,”", "\"기회가 주어진다면 이 모든 것을 약속하겠소.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["She returned to her chamber, and they all rose up, and followed her to the castle.", "그녀는 자기 방으로 돌아갔고, 그들은 모두 일어나 그녀를 따라 성으로 갔다."], ["And they slew the nine porters, that were at the nine gates, in silence.", "그리고 그들은 아홉 개의 문에 있던 아홉 명의 문지기를 소리 없이 죽였다."], ["And they slew the nine watch-dogs without one of them barking.", "그리고 그들은 아홉 마리의 파수견을 한 마리도 짖지 못하게 하고 죽였다."], ["And they went forward to the hall.", "그리고 그들은 홀로 나아갔다."]], [["“The greeting of Heaven and of man be unto thee, Yspadaden Penkawr,”", "\"하늘과 사람의 인사가 그대에게 있기를, 이스파다덴 펜카우르여.\""], ["said they.", "그들이 말했다."], ["“And you, wherefore come you?”", "\"그래, 당신들은 무슨 일로 왔는가?\""], ["“We come to ask thy daughter Olwen for Kilwich, the son of Kilydd, the son of Prince Kelyddon.”", "\"우리는 켈리돈 왕자의 아들 킬리드의 아들 킬위치를 위해 그대의 딸 올웬을 청하러 왔소.\""], ["“Where are my pages and my servants?", "\"내 시동들과 종들은 어디에 있느냐?"], ["Raise up the forks beneath my two eyebrows, which have fallen over my eyes, that I may see the fashion of my son-in-law.”", "내 눈을 덮어 버린 두 눈썹 밑에 받침대를 받쳐 들어 올려라. 그래서 내 사위 될 자의 생김새를 좀 보자꾸나.\""], ["And they did so.", "그러자 그들이 그렇게 했다."], ["“Come hither to-morrow, and you shall have an answer.”", "\"내일 이리로 다시 오라. 그러면 답을 주겠노라.\""]], [["They rose to go forth, and Yspadaden Penkawr seized one of the three poisoned darts that lay beside him, and threw it after them.", "그들이 나가려고 일어서자, 이스파다덴 펜카우르는 자기 곁에 놓여 있던 독을 바른 세 자루의 화살 가운데 하나를 집어 그들 뒤로 던졌다."], ["And Bedwyr caught it, and flung it, and pierced Yspadaden Penkawr grievously with it through the knee.", "그러자 베드위르가 그것을 잡아 되던져 이스파다덴 펜카우르의 무릎을 꿰뚫어 심하게 상처를 입혔다."], ["Then he said, “A cursed ungentle son-in-law, truly!", "그러자 그가 말했다. \"참으로 저주받을 무례한 사위로다!"], ["I shall ever walk the worse for his rudeness, and shall ever be without a cure.", "저자의 무례함 때문에 나는 평생 더 절뚝거리며 걷게 될 것이며, 영영 낫지 못할 것이다."], ["This poisoned iron pains me like the bite of a gad-fly.", "이 독을 바른 쇠붙이가 등에에게 물린 것처럼 나를 아프게 하는구나."], ["Cursed be the smith who forged it, and the anvil on which it was wrought!", "이것을 벼린 대장장이와 이것을 만든 모루에 저주가 있기를!"], ["So sharp is it!”", "어찌 이리도 날카로운가!\""]], [["That night also they took up their abode in the house of the herdsman.", "그날 밤도 그들은 목자의 집에 거처를 정했다."], ["The next day, with the dawn, they arrayed themselves and proceeded to the castle, and entered the hall; and they said, “Yspadaden Penkawr, give us thy daughter in consideration of her dower and her maiden fee, which we will pay to thee, and to her two kinswomen likewise.”", "이튿날 동이 틀 무렵, 그들은 채비를 갖추어 성으로 나아가 홀로 들어갔다. 그러고는 말했다."], ["Then he said, “Her four great-grandmothers and her four great-grandsires are yet alive; it is needful that I take counsel of them.”", "\"이스파다덴 펜카우르여, 우리가 그대에게 치를 신부 지참금과 처녀 값을 헤아려 그대의 딸을 우리에게 주시오. 그리고 마찬가지로 그녀의 두 친족 여인에게도 치르겠소.\""], ["“Be it so,”", "그러자 그가 말했다."], ["they answered, “we will go to meat.”", "\"그 아이의 네 명의 증조모와 네 명의 증조부가 아직 살아 있으니, 그들과 의논하는 것이 마땅하다.\" \"그렇게 하시오.\""], ["As they rose up he took the second dart that was beside him, and cast it after them.", "그들이 대답했다. \"우리는 식사를 하러 가겠소.\""], ["And Meneu, the son of Gawedd, caught it, and flung it back at him, and wounded him in the centre of the breast.", "그들이 일어서자 그는 자기 곁에 놓인 두 번째 화살을 집어 그들 뒤로 던졌다. 그러자 가웨드의 아들 메네우가 그것을 잡아 되던져 그의 가슴 한복판에 상처를 입혔다."], ["“A cursed ungentle son-in-law, truly!”", "\"참으로 저주받을 무례한 사위로다!\""], ["said he; “the hard iron pains me like the bite of a horse-leech.", "그가 말했다. \"이 단단한 쇠붙이가 거머리에게 물린 것처럼 나를 아프게 하는구나."], ["Cursed be the hearth whereon it was heated, and the smith who formed it! So sharp is it!", "이것을 달구던 화덕과 이것을 만든 대장장이에게 저주가 있기를!"], ["Henceforth, whenever I go up hill, I shall have a scant in my breath, and a pain in my chest, and I shall often loathe my food.”", "어찌 이리도 날카로운가! 이제부터 나는 언덕을 오를 때마다 숨이 가빠지고 가슴이 아플 것이며, 종종 음식이 역겨워질 것이다.\""], ["And they went to meat.", "그리고 그들은 식사를 하러 갔다."]], [["And the third day they returned to the palace.", "그리고 사흘째 되는 날 그들은 궁으로 돌아왔다."], ["And Yspadaden Penkawr said to them, “Shoot not at me again unless you desire death.", "그러자 이스파다덴 펜카우르가 그들에게 말했다. \"죽음을 원하지 않거든 다시는 내게 쏘지 마라."], ["Where are my attendants?", "내 시종들은 어디에 있느냐?"], ["Lift up the forks of my eyebrows, which have fallen over my eyeballs, that I may see the fashion of my son-in-law.”", "내 눈알을 덮어 버린 눈썹 밑에 받침대를 받쳐 들어 올려라. 그래서 내 사위 될 자의 생김새를 좀 보자꾸나.\""], ["Then they arose, and, as they did so, Yspadaden Penkawr took the third poisoned dart and cast it at them.", "그러자 그들이 일어섰고, 그들이 그렇게 하는 동안 이스파다덴 펜카우르는 세 번째 독화살을 집어 그들에게 던졌다."], ["And Kilwich caught it, and threw it vigorously, and wounded him through the eyeball.", "그러자 킬위치가 그것을 잡아 힘차게 되던져 그의 눈알을 꿰뚫어 상처를 입혔다."], ["“A cursed ungentle son-in-law, truly!", "\"참으로 저주받을 무례한 사위로다!"], ["As long as I remain alive, my eyesight will be the worse.", "내가 살아 있는 한, 내 시력은 더 나빠질 것이다."], ["Whenever I go against the wind, my eyes will water; and peradventure my head will burn, and I shall have a giddiness every new moon.", "바람을 거슬러 갈 때마다 내 눈에서는 눈물이 날 것이며, 어쩌면 내 머리가 화끈거리고 초승달이 뜰 때마다 어지러울 것이다."], ["Like the bite of a mad dog is the stroke of this poisoned iron.", "이 독을 바른 쇠붙이의 일격은 미친개에게 물린 것과 같구나."], ["Cursed be the fire in which it was forged!”", "이것을 벼린 불에 저주가 있기를!\""], ["And they went to meat.", "그리고 그들은 식사를 하러 갔다."]], [["And the next day they came again to the palace, and they said, “Shoot not at us any more, unless thou desirest such hurt and harm and torture as thou now hast, and even more.”", "그리고 이튿날 그들은 다시 궁으로 와서 이렇게 말했다. \"지금 그대가 겪은 것과 같은, 아니 그보다 더한 상해와 해악과 고통을 원하지 않거든 다시는 우리에게 쏘지 마시오.\""], ["Said Kilwich, “Give me thy daughter; and if thou wilt not give her, thou shalt receive thy death because of her.”", "킬위치가 말했다. \"그대의 딸을 내게 주시오."], ["“Where is he that seeks my daughter?", "그대가 그녀를 주지 않는다면, 그대는 그녀로 인해 죽음을 맞을 것이오.\""], ["Come hither where I may see thee.”", "\"내 딸을 구하는 자가 어디 있느냐?"], ["And they placed him a chair face to face with him.", "내가 너를 볼 수 있도록 이리로 오너라.\" 그리하여 그들은 그를 그와 얼굴을 마주하도록 의자에 앉혔다."]], [["Said Yspadaden Penkawr, “Is it thou that seekest my daughter?”", "이스파다덴 펜카우르가 말했다. \"내 딸을 구하는 자가 너냐?\""]], [["“It is I,”", "\"바로 저올시다.\""], ["answered Kilwich.", "킬위치가 대답했다."]], [["“I must have thy pledge that thou wilt not do toward me otherwise than is just; and when I have gotten that which I shall name, my daughter thou shalt have.”", "\"네가 정당한 것 외에 다른 방식으로 나를 대하지 않겠다는 너의 맹세를 받아야겠다. 그리고 내가 이름하는 것을 얻게 되면, 너는 내 딸을 얻을 것이다.\""]], [["“I promise thee that willingly,”", "\"기꺼이 그것을 그대께 약속하겠소.\""], ["said Kilwich; “name what thou wilt.”", "킬위치가 말했다. \"원하는 것을 이름하시오.\""]], [["“I will do so,”", "\"그렇게 하지.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Seest thou yonder red tilled ground?”", "\"저기 붉게 일군 땅이 보이느냐?\""]], [["“I see it.”", "\"보입니다.\""]], [["“When first I met the mother of this maiden, nine bushels of flax were sown therein, and none has yet sprung up, white nor black.", "\"내가 이 처녀의 어머니를 처음 만났을 때, 그곳에 아홉 부셸의 아마 씨를 뿌렸으나 아직 흰 것도 검은 것도 하나 돋아나지 않았다."], ["I require to have the flax to sow in the new land yonder, that when it grows up it may make a white wimple for my daughter’s head on the day of thy wedding.”", "나는 그 아마를 저기 새 땅에 뿌려야겠으니, 그것이 자라나면 네 혼례 날에 내 딸의 머리에 씌울 흰 두건을 만들 수 있도록 함이다.\""]], [["“It will be easy for me to compass this, although thou mayest think it will not be easy.”", "\"비록 그대는 쉽지 않으리라 여길지 모르나, 이것을 이루는 것은 제게 쉬운 일이 될 것이오.\""]], [["“Though thou get this, there is yet that which thou wilt not get—the harp of Teirtu, to play to us that night.", "\"이것을 얻는다 하더라도, 네가 얻지 못할 것이 아직 있으니, 그날 밤 우리에게 연주해 줄 테이르투의 하프다."], ["When a man desires that it should play, it does so of itself; and when he desires that it should cease, it ceases.", "어떤 사람이 그것이 연주하기를 원하면 그것은 저절로 연주하고, 그것이 멎기를 원하면 그것은 멎는다."], ["And this he will not give of his own free will, and thou wilt not be able to compel him.”", "그런데 그는 제 뜻으로는 그것을 내주지 않을 것이며, 너는 그를 강제할 수도 없을 것이다.\""]], [["“It will be easy for me to compass this, although thou mayest think it will not be easy.”", "\"비록 그대는 쉽지 않으리라 여길지 모르나, 이것을 이루는 것은 제게 쉬운 일이 될 것이오.\""]], [["“Though thou get this, there is yet that which thou wilt not get.", "\"이것을 얻는다 하더라도, 네가 얻지 못할 것이 아직 있다."], ["I require thee to get me for my huntsman Mabon, the son of Modron.", "나는 네가 내 사냥꾼으로 모드론의 아들 마본을 데려오기를 요구한다."], ["He was taken from his mother when three nights old, and it is not known where he now is, nor whether he is living or dead.”", "그는 태어난 지 사흘 밤 만에 어머니에게서 빼앗겼고, 그가 지금 어디에 있는지, 살았는지 죽었는지조차 알려져 있지 않다.\""]], [["“It will be easy for me to compass this, although thou mayest think it will not be easy.”", "\"비록 그대는 쉽지 않으리라 여길지 모르나, 이것을 이루는 것은 제게 쉬운 일이 될 것이오.\""]], [["“Though thou get this, there is yet that which thou wilt not get—the two cubs of the wolf Gast Rhymhi; no leash in the world will hold them, but a leash made from the beard of Dillus Varwawc, the robber.", "\"이것을 얻는다 하더라도, 네가 얻지 못할 것이 아직 있으니, 암늑대 가스트 림히의 두 새끼다. 세상의 어떤 가죽끈도 그놈들을 붙들어 매지 못하나, 강도 딜루스 바르와우크의 수염으로 만든 가죽끈만은 예외다."], ["And the leash will be of no avail unless it be plucked from his beard while he is alive.", "그리고 그 가죽끈은 그가 살아 있는 동안 그의 수염에서 뽑아낸 것이 아니면 아무 소용이 없다."], ["While he lives he will not suffer this to be done to him, and the leash will be of no use should he be dead, because it will be brittle.”", "그가 살아 있는 동안에는 자기 몸에 그런 일이 행해지도록 두지 않을 것이며, 또 그가 죽은 뒤라면 그 가죽끈은 잘 부스러질 것이므로 아무 소용이 없을 것이다.\""]], [["“It will be easy for me to compass this, although thou mayest think it will not be easy.”", "\"비록 그대는 쉽지 않으리라 여길지 모르나, 이것을 이루는 것은 제게 쉬운 일이 될 것이오.\""]], [["“Though thou get this, there is yet that which thou wilt not get—the sword of Gwernach the Giant; of his own free will he will not give it, and thou wilt never be able to compel him.”", "\"이것을 얻는다 하더라도, 네가 얻지 못할 것이 아직 있으니, 거인 그웨르나크의 검이다. 그는 제 뜻으로는 그것을 내주지 않을 것이며, 너는 결코 그를 강제할 수 없을 것이다.\""]], [["“It will be easy for me to compass this, although thou mayest think it will not be easy.”", "\"비록 그대는 쉽지 않으리라 여길지 모르나, 이것을 이루는 것은 제게 쉬운 일이 될 것이오.\""]], [["“Though thou get this, there is yet that which thou wilt not get.", "\"이것을 얻는다 하더라도, 네가 얻지 못할 것이 아직 있다."], ["Difficulties shalt thou meet with, and nights without sleep, in seeking this, and if thou obtain it not, neither shalt thou obtain my daughter.”", "이것을 구하면서 너는 갖가지 어려움과 잠 못 이루는 밤들을 만날 것이며, 그것을 얻지 못한다면 너는 내 딸도 얻지 못할 것이다.\""]], [["“Horses shall I have, and chivalry; and my lord and kinsman, Arthur, will obtain for me all these things.", "\"제게는 말과 기사도가 있을 것이며, 제 주군이자 친족인 아서가 이 모든 것을 제게 구해 줄 것이오."], ["And I shall gain thy daughter, and thou shalt lose thy life.”", "그리고 저는 그대의 딸을 얻을 것이며, 그대는 목숨을 잃을 것이오.\""]], [["“Go forward.", "\"가거라."], ["And thou shalt not be chargeable for food or raiment for my daughter while thou art seeking these things; and when thou hast compassed all these marvels, thou shalt have my daughter for thy wife.”", "그리고 네가 이것들을 구하는 동안에는 내 딸의 음식이나 의복에 대한 책임은 네게 지우지 않겠다. 그리고 네가 이 모든 경이로운 일을 이루어 내거든, 너는 내 딸을 아내로 얻을 것이다.\""]], [["————", "————"]], [["KILWICH AND OLWEN (Continued)\nALL that day they journeyed until the evening, and then they beheld a vast castle, which was the largest in the world.", "그들은 그날 종일 길을 갔고, 저녁이 되자 거대한 성을 발견했으니, 그것은 세상에서 가장 큰 성이었다. 그런데 보라!"], ["And lo! a black man, larger than three of the men of this world, came out from the castle.", "이 세상 사람 셋을 합한 것보다도 큰 검은 사내 하나가 성에서 나왔다."], ["And they spoke unto him, and said, “O man, whose castle is that?”", "그래서 그들은 그에게 말을 걸어 물었다."], ["“Stupid are ye, truly, O men!", "\"여보시오, 저것은 누구의 성이오?\""], ["There is no one in the world that does not know that this is the castle of Gwernach the Giant.”", "\"참으로 어리석은 자들이로구나, 그대들이여! 저것이 거인 그웨르나흐의 성임을 모르는 자는 이 세상에 없거늘.\""], ["“What treatment is there for guests and strangers that alight in that castle?”", "\"저 성에 들르는 손님이나 나그네는 어떤 대접을 받소?\""], ["“O chieftain, Heaven protect thee!", "\"이보시오, 하늘이 그대를 보살피시기를!"], ["No guests ever returned thence alive, and no one may enter therein unless he brings with him his craft.”", "저곳에서 살아 돌아온 손님은 아무도 없으며, 자기 기술을 지니고 오지 않은 자는 그 누구도 들어갈 수 없소.\""]], [["Then they proceeded towards the gate.", "그러자 그들은 성문을 향해 나아갔다."], ["Said Gurhyr Gwalstat, “Is there a porter?”", "구르히르 그왈스타트가 말했다. \"문지기가 있소?\""], ["“There is; wherefore dost thou call?”", "\"있소. 어찌하여 부르시오?\""], ["“Open the gate.”", "\"문을 여시오.\""], ["“I will not open it.”", "\"열어 주지 않겠소.\""], ["“Wherefore wilt thou not?”", "\"어찌하여 열어 주지 않겠다는 거요?\""], ["“The knife is in the meat, and the drink is in the horn, and there is revelry in the hall of Gwernach the Giant; and except for a craftsman who brings his craft, the gate will not be opened to-night.”", "\"고기에는 칼이 꽂혀 있고, 뿔잔에는 술이 가득하며, 거인 그웨르나흐의 연회장에서는 흥겨운 잔치가 벌어지고 있소. 자기 기술을 지니고 오는 장인이 아니라면, 오늘 밤 문은 열리지 않을 것이오.\""], ["“Verily, porter,”", "\"참으로, 문지기여,\""], ["then said Kay, “my craft bring I with me.”", "그러자 카이가 말했다. \"나는 내 기술을 지니고 왔소.\""], ["“What is thy craft?”", "\"그대의 기술이 무엇이오?\""], ["“The best burnisher of swords am I in the world.”", "\"나는 세상에서 검을 가장 잘 가는 사람이오.\""], ["“I will go and tell this unto Gwernach the Giant, and I will bring thee an answer.”", "\"내가 가서 이를 거인 그웨르나흐에게 전하고, 그대에게 답을 가져오겠소.\""]], [["So the porter went in, and Gwernach said to him, “Hast thou news from the gate?”", "그리하여 문지기가 안으로 들어가니, 그웨르나흐가 그에게 말했다. \"성문에서 소식이 있느냐?\""], ["“I have.", "\"있습니다."], ["There is a party at the door of the gate who desire to come in.”", "성문 앞에 들어오기를 바라는 일행이 있습니다.\""], ["“Didst thou inquire of them if they possessed any art?”", "\"그들에게 무슨 기술을 지녔는지 물어보았느냐?\""], ["“I did inquire,”", "\"물어보았습니다.\""], ["said he, “and one told me that he was well skilled in the burnishing of swords.”", "그가 말했다. \"그리고 그중 한 사람이 검을 가는 데 능숙하다고 말했습니다.\""], ["“We have need of him then.", "\"그렇다면 그가 우리에게 필요하구나."], ["For some time have I sought for some one to polish my sword, and could find no one.", "한동안 나는 내 검을 갈아 줄 자를 찾았으나, 아무도 찾지 못했다."], ["Let this man enter, since he brings with him his craft.”", "그자가 자기 기술을 지니고 왔다 하니, 들어오게 하라.\""]], [["The porter thereupon returned and opened the gate.", "그리하여 문지기가 돌아와 성문을 열었다."], ["And Kay went in by himself, and he saluted Gwernach the Giant.", "그러자 카이가 홀로 들어가 거인 그웨르나흐에게 인사를 올렸다."], ["And a chair was placed for him opposite to Gwernach.", "그리고 그웨르나흐의 맞은편에 그를 위한 의자가 놓였다."], ["And Gwernach said to him, “O man, is it true that is reported of thee, that thou knowest how to burnish swords?”", "그웨르나흐가 그에게 말했다. \"여보게, 그대에 관해 전해진 말이 사실인가, 그대가 검을 갈 줄 안다는 것이?\""], ["“I know full well how to do so,”", "\"나는 그것을 아주 잘할 줄 압니다.\""], ["answered Kay.", "카이가 답했다."], ["Then was the sword of Gwernach brought to him.", "그러자 그웨르나흐의 검이 그에게 건네졌다."], ["And Kay took a blue whetstone from under his arm, and asked whether he would have it burnished white or blue.", "카이는 겨드랑이 밑에서 푸른 숫돌을 꺼내, 검을 희게 갈기를 바라는지 푸르게 갈기를 바라는지 물었다."], ["“Do with it as it seems good to thee, or as thou wouldst if it were thine own.”", "\"그대 보기에 좋을 대로 하게나, 아니면 그것이 그대 자신의 것이라면 하였을 대로 하게.\""], ["Then Kay polished one half of the blade, and put it in his hand.", "그러자 카이는 칼날의 절반을 갈아 그의 손에 쥐여 주었다."], ["“Will this please thee?”", "\"이것이 마음에 드십니까?\""], ["asked he.", "그가 물었다."], ["“I would rather than all that is in my dominions that the whole of it were like this.", "\"내 영토 안의 모든 것을 다 준다 해도, 검 전체가 이와 같았으면 좋겠네."], ["It is a marvel to me that such a man as thou should be without a companion.”", "그대 같은 사람이 동료 하나 없다는 것이 내게는 놀라운 일이로군.\""], ["“O noble sir, I have a companion, albeit he is not skilled in this art.”", "\"고귀하신 분이시여, 제게는 동료가 있습니다만, 그는 이 기술에는 능숙하지 못합니다.\""], ["“Who may he be?”", "\"그가 누구인가?\""], ["“Let the porter go forth, and I will tell him whereby he may know him.", "\"문지기를 내보내 주시면, 그를 알아볼 방도를 일러 드리겠습니다."], ["The head of the lance will leave its shaft, and draw blood from the wind, and will descend upon its shaft again.”", "창끝이 그 자루를 떠나, 바람에서 피를 뽑아내고는, 다시 그 자루 위로 내려앉을 것입니다.\""], ["Then the gate was opened, and Bedwyr entered.", "그러자 성문이 열리고, 베드위르가 들어왔다."], ["And Kay said, “Bedwyr is very skilful, though he knows not this art.”", "카이가 말했다. \"베드위르는 이 기술은 모르나, 매우 솜씨가 뛰어납니다.\""]], [["And there was much discourse among those who were without, because that Kay and Bedwyr had gone in.", "그리고 카이와 베드위르가 안으로 들어갔으므로, 밖에 있던 자들 사이에는 많은 이야기가 오갔다."], ["And a young man who was with them, the only son of the herdsman, got in also; and he contrived to admit all the rest, but they kept themselves concealed.", "그러더니 그들과 함께 있던 한 젊은이, 곧 목동의 외아들 또한 안으로 들어갔다. 그는 나머지 모두를 들여보낼 방도를 꾀했으나, 그들은 몸을 숨긴 채 있었다."]], [["The sword was now polished, and Kay gave it unto the hand of Gwernach the Giant, to see if he were pleased with his work.", "이제 검이 다 갈렸으므로, 카이는 거인 그웨르나흐의 손에 그것을 건네어 자기 솜씨가 마음에 드는지를 살피게 했다. 거인이 말했다."], ["And the giant said, “The work is good; I am content therewith.”", "\"솜씨가 훌륭하구나. 나는 이에 만족한다.\""], ["Said Kay, “It is thy scabbard that hath rusted thy sword; give it to me, that I may take out the wooden sides of it, and put in new ones.”", "카이가 말했다. \"그대의 검을 녹슬게 한 것은 검집입니다."], ["And he took the scabbard from him, and the sword in the other hand.", "그것을 제게 주시면, 그 나무 옆판을 빼내고 새것을 끼우겠습니다.\""], ["And he came and stood over against the giant, as if he would have put the sword into the scabbard; and with it he struck at the head of the giant, and cut off his head at one blow.", "그러고는 그에게서 검집을 받고, 다른 손에는 검을 들었다. 그리고 그는 마치 검을 검집에 넣으려는 듯이 거인 앞으로 다가가 마주 섰다."], ["Then they despoiled the castle, and took from it what goods and jewels they would.", "그리고 그 검으로 거인의 머리를 내리쳐, 단칼에 그 목을 베어 버렸다."], ["And they returned to Arthur’s court, bearing with them the sword of Gwernach the Giant.", "그런 다음 그들은 성을 약탈하여, 거기서 원하는 재물과 보석을 가져갔다. 그리고 그들은 거인 그웨르나흐의 검을 지니고 아서의 궁정으로 돌아왔다."]], [["And when they told Arthur how they had sped, Arthur said, “It is a good beginning.”", "그리고 그들이 어떻게 일을 해냈는지 아서에게 이야기하자, 아서가 말했다. \"좋은 시작이로다.\""], ["Then they took counsel, and said, “Which of these marvels will it be best for us to seek next?”", "그러고 나서 그들은 의논하여 말했다. \"이 경이로운 일들 가운데 다음으로 어느 것을 찾는 것이 우리에게 가장 나을까?\""], ["“It will be best,”", "\"가장 나은 것은,\" 한 사람이 말했다."], ["said one, “to seek Mabon, the son of Modron; and he will not be found unless we first find Eidoel, the son of Aer, his kinsman.”", "\"모드론의 아들 마본을 찾는 것입니다. 그러나 그는 우리가 먼저 그의 친족인 아에르의 아들 에이도엘을 찾지 않으면 발견되지 않을 것입니다.\""], ["Then Arthur rose up, and the warriors of the island of Britain with him, to seek for Eidoel; and they proceeded until they came to the castle of Glivi, where Eidoel was imprisoned.", "그러자 아서가 일어났고, 브리튼 섬의 전사들이 그와 함께 에이도엘을 찾으러 나섰다. 그들은 나아가 에이도엘이 갇혀 있는 글리비의 성에 이르렀다."], ["Glivi stood on the summit of his castle, and he said, “Arthur, what requirest thou of me, since nothing remains to me in this fortress, and I have neither joy nor pleasure in it, neither wheat nor oats?", "글리비는 자기 성의 꼭대기에 서서 말했다. \"아서여, 그대는 내게서 무엇을 바라는가, 이 요새에는 내게 남은 것이 아무것도 없고, 나는 여기서 기쁨도 즐거움도, 밀도 귀리도 없거늘?"], ["Seek not, therefore, to do me harm.”", "그러니 내게 해를 끼치려 하지 말라.\""], ["Said Arthur, “Not to injure thee came I hither, but to seek for the prisoner that is with thee.”", "아서가 말했다. \"내가 이곳에 온 것은 그대를 해치려는 것이 아니라, 그대와 함께 있는 죄수를 찾기 위함이다.\""], ["“I will give thee my prisoner, though I had not thought to give him up to any one, and therewith shalt thou have my support and my aid.”", "\"내 죄수를 그대에게 주겠노라. 나는 그를 누구에게도 넘겨줄 생각이 없었으나, 그와 더불어 그대는 내 지지와 도움도 얻을 것이다.\""]], [["His followers said unto Arthur, “Lord, go thou home, thou canst not proceed with thy host in quest of such small adventures as these.”", "그의 부하들이 아서에게 말했다. \"주군이시여, 집으로 돌아가소서."], ["Then said Arthur, “It were well for thee, Gurhyr Gwalstat, to go upon this quest, for thou knowest all languages, and art familiar with those of the birds and the beasts.", "주군께서는 이처럼 하찮은 모험을 좇아 군대를 이끌고 나아가실 수 없습니다.\" 그러자 아서가 말했다."], ["Thou, Eidoel, oughtest likewise to go with thy men in search of thy cousin.", "\"구르히르 그왈스타트여, 그대가 이 탐색에 나서는 것이 좋겠다. 그대는 모든 언어를 알고, 새와 짐승의 말에도 익숙하니 말이다."], ["And as for you, Kay and Bedwyr, I have hope of whatever adventure ye are in quest of, that ye will achieve it.", "에이도엘이여, 그대 또한 그대의 부하들과 함께 그대의 사촌을 찾으러 가야 마땅하다. 그리고 카이와 베드위르여, 그대들이 어떤 모험을 좇든, 나는 그대들이 그것을 이루어 내리라는 희망을 품는다."], ["Achieve ye this adventure for me.”", "나를 위해 이 모험을 이루어 다오.\""]], [["They went forward until they came to the Ousel of Cilgwri. And Gurhyr adjured her, saying, “Tell me if thou knowest aught of Mabon, the son of Modron, who was taken when three nights old from between his mother and the wall?” And the Ousel answered, “When I first came here, there was a smith’s anvil in this place, and I was then a young bird; and from that time no work has been done upon it, save the pecking of my beak every evening; and now there is not so much as the size of a nut remaining thereof; yet during all that time I have never heard of the man for whom you inquire. Nevertheless, I will do that which it is fitting that I should for an embassy from Arthur. There is a race of animals who were formed before me, and I will be your guide to them.”", "그들은 나아가 킬그우리의 검은새에게 이르렀다. 구르히르가 그 새에게 간절히 청하여 말했다. \"그대가 모드론의 아들 마본에 관해 무엇이라도 아는 것이 있다면 내게 말해 다오. 그는 태어난 지 사흘 밤 만에 그 어머니와 벽 사이에서 빼앗겨 갔다.\" 검은새가 답했다. \"내가 처음 이곳에 왔을 때, 이 자리에는 대장장이의 모루가 있었고, 그때 나는 어린 새였소. 그 이래로 그 모루에는 매일 저녁 내 부리로 쪼는 일 말고는 아무 일도 행해지지 않았소. 그런데도 이제는 그 모루가 개암 한 알만큼도 남지 않았소. 그러나 그 오랜 세월 동안 나는 그대가 묻는 사람에 관해 들어 본 적이 없소. 그럼에도 나는 아서께서 보내신 사절에게 마땅히 해야 할 바를 행하겠소. 나보다 먼저 만들어진 짐승의 무리가 있으니, 내가 그대들을 그들에게로 인도하겠소.\""]], [["So they proceeded to the place where was the Stag of Redynvre.", "그리하여 그들은 레딘브레의 수사슴이 있는 곳으로 나아갔다. \"레딘브레의 수사슴이여, 보라, 우리는 아서께서 보내신 사절로서 그대에게 왔노라."], ["“Stag of Redynvre, behold, we are come to thee, an embassy from Arthur, for we have not heard of any animal older than thou.", "우리는 그대보다 나이 많은 짐승에 관해서는 들어 본 적이 없기 때문이다. 말해 다오, 그대는 태어난 지 사흘 밤 만에 그 어머니에게서 빼앗겨 간 모드론의 아들 마본에 관해 무엇이라도 아는가?\""], ["Say, knowest thou aught of Mabon, the son of Modron, who was taken from his mother when three nights old?”", "수사슴이 말했다. \"내가 처음 이곳에 왔을 때, 내 주위에는 어린 떡갈나무 한 그루를 빼고는 나무 하나 없는 평원이 펼쳐져 있었소."], ["The Stag said, “When first I came hither there was a plain all around me, without any trees save one oak sapling, which grew up to be an oak with an hundred branches; and that oak has since perished, so that now nothing remains of it but the withered stump; and from that day to this I have been here, yet have I never heard of the man for whom you inquire.", "그 어린 나무가 자라 가지 백 개를 지닌 떡갈나무가 되었소. 그런데 그 떡갈나무는 그 후 죽어 버려, 이제는 시든 그루터기 말고는 아무것도 남지 않았소."], ["Nevertheless, being an embassy from Arthur, I will be your guide to the place where there is an animal which was formed before I was, and the oldest animal in the world, and the one that has travelled most, the Eagle of Gwern Abwy.”", "그날부터 지금까지 나는 이곳에 있었으나, 그대들이 묻는 사람에 관해서는 들어 본 적이 없소. 그럼에도, 아서께서 보내신 사절이니, 나보다 먼저 만들어진 짐승, 곧 세상에서 가장 나이 많고, 가장 많이 떠돌아다닌 짐승인 그웨른 아브위의 독수리가 있는 곳으로 그대들을 인도하겠소.\""]], [["Gurhyr said, “Eagle of Gwern Abwy, we have come to thee, an embassy from Arthur, to ask thee if thou knowest aught of Mabon, the son of Modron, who was taken from his mother when he was three nights old?”", "구르히르가 말했다. \"그웨른 아브위의 독수리여, 우리는 아서께서 보내신 사절로서 그대에게 왔노라."], ["The Eagle said, “I have been here for a great space of time, and when I first came hither, there was a rock here from the top of which I pecked at the stars every evening; and it has crumbled away, and now it is not so much as a span high.", "그대가 태어난 지 사흘 밤 만에 그 어머니에게서 빼앗겨 간 모드론의 아들 마본에 관해 무엇이라도 아는지 그대에게 묻고자 함이다.\" 독수리가 말했다."], ["All that time I have been here, and I have never heard of the man for whom you inquire, except once when I went in search of food as far as Llyn Llyw.", "\"나는 아주 오랜 세월 동안 이곳에 있었소. 내가 처음 이곳에 왔을 때, 여기에는 바위가 하나 있어 그 꼭대기에서 매일 저녁 별을 쪼았소."], ["And when I came there, I struck my talons into a salmon, thinking he would serve me as food for a long time.", "그런데 그것이 무너져 내려, 이제는 한 뼘 높이도 되지 않소. 그 모든 세월 동안 나는 이곳에 있었으나, 그대들이 묻는 사람에 관해서는 들어 본 적이 없소."], ["But he drew me into the water, and I was scarcely able to escape from him.", "단 한 번, 내가 린 리우까지 먹이를 찾으러 갔을 때를 빼고는 말이오. 그곳에 이르러 나는 발톱을 연어 한 마리에 박았으니, 그가 오랫동안 내 먹이가 되어 주리라 여겼소."], ["After that I made peace with him.", "그러나 그가 나를 물속으로 끌어들였고, 나는 가까스로 그에게서 벗어날 수 있었소."], ["And I drew fifty fish-spears out of his back, and relieved him.", "그 후 나는 그와 화해했소. 그리고 나는 그의 등에서 물고기 작살 쉰 개를 뽑아내어 그를 덜어 주었소."], ["Unless he know something of him whom you seek, I cannot tell who may.", "그가 그대들이 찾는 자에 관해 무엇인가 알지 못한다면, 나로서는 누가 알지 도무지 알 수 없소."], ["However, I will guide you to the place where he is.”", "그렇지만 내가 그가 있는 곳으로 그대들을 인도하겠소.\""]], [["So they went thither; and the Eagle said, “Salmon of Llyn Llyw, I have come to thee with an embassy from Arthur, to ask thee if thou knowest aught of Mabon, the son of Modron, who was taken away at three nights old from his mother.”", "그리하여 그들은 그곳으로 갔다. 그리고 독수리가 말했다."], ["“As much as I know I will tell thee.", "\"린 리우의 연어여, 나는 아서께서 보내신 사절을 데리고 그대에게 왔노라. 그대가 태어난 지 사흘 밤 만에 그 어머니에게서 빼앗겨 간 모드론의 아들 마본에 관해 무엇이라도 아는지 그대에게 묻고자 함이다.\""], ["With every tide I go along the river upward, until I come near to the walls of Gloucester, and there have I found such wrong as I never found elsewhere; and to the end that ye may give credence thereto, let one of you go thither upon each of my two shoulders.”", "\"내가 아는 만큼은 그대에게 말해 주겠소. 나는 밀물이 들 때마다 강을 거슬러 올라가, 글로스터의 성벽 가까이에 이르오."], ["So Kay and Gurhyr Gwalstat went upon the two shoulders of the Salmon, and they proceeded until they came unto the wall of the prison; and they heard a great wailing and lamenting from the dungeon.", "그리고 그곳에서 나는 다른 어디서도 본 적 없는 부당한 일을 보았소. 그대들이 이를 믿을 수 있도록, 그대들 가운데 한 사람씩 내 두 어깨에 올라타시오.\""], ["Said Gurhyr, “Who is it that laments in this house of stone?”", "그리하여 카이와 구르히르 그왈스타트가 연어의 두 어깨에 올라탔고, 그들은 나아가 감옥의 벽에 이르렀다. 그리고 그들은 지하 감옥에서 들려오는 크나큰 통곡과 한탄을 들었다."], ["“Alas!", "구르히르가 말했다."], ["it is Mabon, the son of Modron, who is here imprisoned; and no imprisonment was ever so grievous as mine.”", "\"이 돌집에서 한탄하는 자가 누구인가?\" \"아아!"], ["“Hast thou hope of being released for gold or for silver, or for any gifts of wealth, or through battle and fighting?”", "나는 이곳에 갇힌 모드론의 아들 마본이오. 내 갇힘만큼 비참한 갇힘은 일찍이 없었소.\""], ["“By fighting will what ever I may gain be obtained.”", "\"그대는 금이나 은으로, 혹은 어떤 재물의 선물로, 아니면 전투와 싸움을 통해 풀려날 희망이 있는가?\" \"내가 얻을 수 있는 것이 무엇이든, 그것은 싸움을 통해서만 얻어질 것이오.\""]], [["Then they went thence, and returned to Arthur, and they told him where Mabon, the son of Modron, was imprisoned.", "그리하여 그들은 그곳을 떠나 아서에게 돌아가, 모드론의 아들 마본이 갇혀 있는 곳을 그에게 일러 주었다."], ["And Arthur summoned the warriors of the island, and they journeyed as far as Gloucester, to the place where Mabon was in prison.", "그러자 아서는 섬의 전사들을 불러 모았고, 그들은 마본이 갇혀 있는 글로스터까지 나아갔다."], ["Kay and Bedwyr went upon the shoulders of the fish, whilst the warriors of Arthur attacked the castle.", "카이와 베드위르가 물고기의 어깨에 올라탄 사이, 아서의 전사들은 성을 공격했다."], ["And Kay broke through the wall into the dungeon, and brought away the prisoner upon his back, whilst the fight was going on between the warriors.", "그리고 전사들 사이에서 싸움이 벌어지는 동안, 카이는 벽을 뚫고 지하 감옥으로 들어가 그 죄수를 등에 업고 데려 나왔다."], ["And Arthur returned home, and Mabon with him at liberty.", "그리고 아서는 집으로 돌아왔고, 마본도 자유의 몸이 되어 그와 함께였다."]], [["On a certain day as Gurhyr Gwalstat was walking over a mountain, he heard a wailing and a grievous cry.", "어느 날 구르히르 그왈스타트가 산을 넘어 걷고 있을 때, 그는 통곡 소리와 비통한 울부짖음을 들었다."], ["And when he heard it, he sprang forward and went towards it.", "그리고 그것을 듣자, 그는 앞으로 뛰어나가 그 소리를 향해 갔다."], ["And when he came there, he saw a fire burning among the turf, and an ant-hill nearly surrounded with the fire.", "그리고 그곳에 이르렀을 때, 그는 잔디 사이에서 불이 타오르고 있고, 개미집 하나가 그 불에 거의 둘러싸여 있는 것을 보았다."], ["And he drew his sword, and smote off the ant-hill close to the earth, so that it escaped being burned in the fire.", "그래서 그는 검을 뽑아, 개미집을 땅에 바싹 붙여 베어 냈고, 그리하여 개미집은 불에 타는 것을 면했다. 그러자 개미들이 그에게 말했다."], ["And the ants said to him, “Receive from us the blessing of Heaven, and that which no man can give, we give thee.”", "\"우리에게서 하늘의 축복을 받으소서. 그리고 그 누구도 줄 수 없는 것을, 우리가 그대에게 드리겠나이다.\""], ["Then they fetched the nine bushels of flax-seed which Yspadaden Penkawr had required of Kilwich, and they brought the full measure, without lacking any, except one flax-seed, and that the lame pismire brought in before night.", "그러고는 그들이 이스파다덴 펜카우르가 킬위치에게 요구했던 아홉 부셸의 아마 씨를 가져왔는데, 그들은 절름발이 개미 한 마리가 밤이 되기 전에 가져온 아마 씨 한 알을 빼고는, 하나도 모자람 없이 가득 채운 분량을 가져왔다."]], [["Then said Arthur, “Which of the marvels will it be best for us to seek next?”", "그러자 아서가 말했다. \"이 경이로운 일들 가운데 다음으로 어느 것을 찾는 것이 우리에게 가장 나을까?\""], ["“It will be best to seek for the two cubs of the wolf Gast Rhymhi.”", "\"암늑대 가스트 림히의 새끼 두 마리를 찾는 것이 가장 나을 것입니다.\""]], [["“Is it known,”", "\"그 암늑대가 어디 있는지,\""], ["said Arthur, “where she is?”", "아서가 말했다. \"알려져 있는가?\""], ["“She is in Aber Cleddyf,” said one.", "\"그것은 아베르 클레디프에 있습니다.\""], ["Then Arthur went to the house of Tringad, in Aber Cleddyf, and he inquired of him whether he had heard of her there.", "한 사람이 말했다. 그러자 아서는 아베르 클레디프의 트링가드의 집으로 가서, 그가 그곳에서 그 암늑대에 관해 들은 적이 있는지 물었다."], ["“She has often slain my herds, and she is there below in a cave in Aber Cleddyf.”", "\"그것은 자주 내 가축들을 죽였소. 그것은 저 아래 아베르 클레디프의 동굴 안에 있소.\""]], [["Then Arthur went in his ship Prydwen by sea, and the others went by land to hunt her.", "그리하여 아서는 자기 배 프리드웬을 타고 바다로 갔고, 다른 이들은 뭍으로 가서 그 암늑대를 사냥했다."], ["And they surrounded her and her two cubs, and took them and carried them away.", "그리고 그들은 그 암늑대와 그 새끼 두 마리를 에워싸, 그들을 붙잡아 데려갔다."]], [["As Kay and Bedwyr sat on a beacon-cairn on the summit of Plinlimmon, in the highest wind that ever was, they looked around them and saw a great smoke, afar off.", "카이와 베드위르가 플린리몬 꼭대기의 봉화 돌무덤 위에, 일찍이 없던 가장 거센 바람 속에 앉아 있을 때, 그들은 주위를 둘러보다가 멀리서 큰 연기가 피어오르는 것을 보았다."], ["Then said Kay, “By the hand of my friend, yonder is the fire of a robber.”", "그러자 카이가 말했다. \"내 벗의 손에 걸고 말하건대, 저기 저것은 도둑의 불이로다.\""], ["Then they hastened towards the smoke, and they came so near to it that they could see Dillus Varwawc scorching a wild boar.", "그러고는 그들이 연기를 향해 서둘러 갔고, 그들이 그것에 아주 가까이 다가가니, 딜루스 바르와우크가 멧돼지 한 마리를 굽고 있는 것이 보였다."], ["“Behold, yonder is the greatest robber that ever fled from Arthur,”", "\"보라, 저기 저자가 아서에게서 달아난 가장 큰 도둑이로다.\" 베드위르가 카이에게 말했다."], ["said Bedwyr to Kay.", "\"그대는 저자를 아는가?\""], ["“Dost thou know him?”", "\"나는 저자를 안다.\""], ["“I do know him,”", "카이가 답했다."], ["answered Kay; “he is Dillus Varwarc, and no leash in the world will be able to hold the cubs of Gast Rhymi, save a leash made from the beard of him thou seest yonder.", "\"저자는 딜루스 바르와우크요, 저기 그대가 보는 저자의 수염으로 만든 가죽끈이 아니고서는, 세상의 그 어떤 가죽끈도 가스트 림히의 새끼들을 매어 둘 수 없소."], ["And even that will be useless unless his beard be plucked out alive, with wooden tweezers; for if dead it will be brittle.”", "게다가 그것조차도 그의 수염을 살아 있을 때, 나무 족집게로 뽑아내지 않으면 쓸모가 없소. 죽은 뒤에 뽑으면 부스러질 것이기 때문이오.\""], ["“What thinkest thou that we should do concerning this?”", "\"이 일에 관해 우리가 어찌해야 좋겠는가?\" 베드위르가 말했다."], ["said Bedwyr.", "\"저자가,\""], ["“Let us suffer him,”", "카이가 말했다."], ["said Kay, “to eat as much as he will of the meat, and after that he will fall asleep.”", "\"고기를 원하는 만큼 실컷 먹게 내버려 둡시다. 그러면 저자는 잠들 것이오.\""], ["And during that time they employed themselves in making the wooden tweezers.", "그리고 그동안 그들은 나무 족집게를 만드는 일에 몰두했다."], ["And when Kay knew certainly that he was asleep, he made a pit under his feet, and he struck him a violent blow, and squeezed him into the pit.", "그리고 카이가 그가 잠들었음을 확실히 알았을 때, 그는 그의 발밑에 구덩이를 파고, 그에게 사나운 일격을 가해 구덩이 속으로 밀어 넣었다."], ["And there they twitched out his beard completely with the wooden tweezers, and after that they slew him altogether.", "그리고 그곳에서 그들은 나무 족집게로 그의 수염을 완전히 뽑아냈고, 그런 다음 그를 아주 죽여 버렸다."], ["And from thence they went, and took the leash made of Dillus Varwawc’s beard, and they gave it into Arthur’s hand.", "그리고 그곳을 떠나, 그들은 딜루스 바르와우크의 수염으로 만든 가죽끈을 가지고 와서, 그것을 아서의 손에 건넸다."]], [["Thus they got all the marvels that Yspadaden Penkawr had required of Kilwich; and they set forward, and took the marvels to his court.", "이렇게 하여 그들은 이스파다덴 펜카우르가 킬위치에게 요구했던 모든 경이로운 물건을 손에 넣었다. 그리고 그들은 길을 떠나, 그 경이로운 물건들을 그의 궁정으로 가져갔다."], ["And Kilwich said to Yspadaden Penkawr, “Is thy daughter mine now?”", "킬위치가 이스파다덴 펜카우르에게 말했다. \"이제 그대의 딸은 제 것입니까?\""], ["“She is thine,”", "\"그 애는 그대의 것이오.\""], ["said he, “but therefore needest thou not thank me, but Arthur, who hath accomplished this for thee.”", "그가 말했다. \"그러나 그 일로 그대는 내게 감사할 필요가 없고, 그대를 위해 이를 이루어 낸 아서에게 감사해야 하오.\""], ["Then Goreu, the son of Custennin, the herdsman, whose brothers Yspadaden Penkawr had slain, seized him by the hair of his head, and dragged him after him to the keep, and cut off his head, and placed it on a stake on the citadel.", "그러자 이스파다덴 펜카우르가 그 형제들을 죽인 바 있는 목동 쿠스텐닌의 아들 고레우가, 그의 머리채를 움켜잡고 망루까지 끌고 가, 그의 목을 베어, 그 머리를 성채의 말뚝 위에 꽂았다."], ["Then they took possession of his castle, and of his treasures.", "그런 다음 그들은 그의 성과 그의 보물을 차지했다."], ["And that night Olwen became Kilwich’s bride, and she continued to be his wife as long as she lived.", "그리고 그날 밤 올웬은 킬위치의 신부가 되었고, 그녀는 살아 있는 동안 내내 그의 아내로 지냈다."]], [["————", "————"]], [["GWYDDNO GARANHIR was sovereign of Gwaelod, a territory bordering on the sea.", "그위드노 가란히르는 바다와 접한 영토인 그와엘로드의 군주였다."], ["And he possessed a weir upon the strand between Dyvi and Aberystwyth, near to his own castle, and the value of an hundred pounds was taken in that weir every May eve.", "그는 디비와 아베리스트위스 사이의 해변에, 자기 성에서 가까운 곳에 어량을 하나 가지고 있었는데, 그곳에서는 해마다 오월제 전야가 되면 백 파운드어치가 잡히곤 했다."], ["And Gwyddno had an only son named Elphin, the most hapless of youths, and the most needy.", "그위드노에게는 엘핀이라는 외아들이 있었으니, 그는 젊은이들 가운데 가장 불운하고 가장 궁핍한 자였다."], ["And it grieved his father sore, for he thought that he was born in an evil hour.", "이는 그의 아버지를 몹시 슬프게 했으니, 그가 불길한 시각에 태어났다고 여겨졌기 때문이다."], ["By the advice of his council, his father had granted him the drawing of the weir that year, to see if good luck would ever befall him, and to give him something wherewith to begin the world.", "그의 아버지는 자문단의 조언에 따라, 혹시라도 그에게 행운이 깃들지, 또한 세상에 첫발을 내디딜 밑천이라도 마련해 줄지 보고자 그해의 어량 거두기를 그에게 맡겼다."], ["And this was on the twenty-ninth of April.", "그것은 사월 스무아흐렛날의 일이었다."]], [["The next day, when Elphin went to look, there was nothing in the weir but a leathern bag upon a pole of the weir.", "이튿날 엘핀이 가서 보니, 어량에는 막대에 걸린 가죽 자루 하나밖에 아무것도 없었다. 그러자 어량지기가 엘핀에게 말했다."], ["Then said the weir-ward unto Elphin, “All thy ill-luck aforetime was nothing to this; and now thou hast destroyed the virtues of the weir, which always yielded the value of an hundred pounds every May eve; and to-night there is nothing but this leathern skin within it.”", "\"여태까지의 불운은 모두 이에 비하면 아무것도 아닙니다. 이제 도련님은 해마다 오월제 전야에 백 파운드어치를 거두어 들이던 이 어량의 효험을 망쳐 버리셨으니, 오늘 밤에는 그 안에 이 가죽 자루밖에 아무것도 없군요.\""], ["“How now,”", "\"그게 무슨 말이냐,\" 엘핀이 말했다."], ["said Elphin, “there may be therein the value of a hundred pounds.” Well!", "\"그 안에 백 파운드어치가 들어 있을지도 모르지 않느냐.\""], ["they took up the leathern bag, and he who opened it saw the forehead of an infant, the fairest that ever was seen; and he said, “Behold a radiant brow?”", "그리하여 그들은 가죽 자루를 들어 올렸고, 그것을 연 자는 일찍이 본 적 없는 더없이 아름다운 갓난아이의 이마를 보았다. 그가 말했다."], ["(In the Welsh language, taliesin.) “Taliesin be he called,”", "\"보라, 빛나는 이마로다!\" (웨일스 말로 탈리에신이라 한다.) \"이 아이를 탈리에신이라 부르자,\""], ["said Elphin.", "엘핀이 말했다."], ["And he lifted the bag in his arms, and, lamenting his bad luck, placed the boy sorrowfully behind him.", "그러고는 자루를 두 팔에 안고, 자신의 불운을 한탄하면서 슬픈 마음으로 아이를 자기 뒤에 앉혔다."], ["And he made his horse amble gently, that before had been trotting, and he carried him as softly as if he had been siting in the easiest chair in the world.", "그리고 앞서까지 빠른 걸음으로 달리던 말을 부드럽게 천천히 걷게 하여, 마치 세상에서 가장 편안한 의자에 앉은 듯이 아이를 부드럽게 실어 날랐다."], ["And presently the boy made a Consolation, and praise to Elphin; and the Consolation was as you may here see:", "그러자 곧 아이가 위로의 노래를 지어 엘핀을 찬양했으니, 그 위로의 노래는 여기 보는 바와 같았다."]], [["“Fair Elphin, cease to lament!", "\"어여쁜 엘핀이여, 한탄을 그치소서!"], ["Never in Gwyddno’s weir Was there such good luck as this night.", "그위드노의 어량에서 오늘 밤만큼 큰 행운은 일찍이 없었나이다."], ["Being sad will not avail; Better to trust in God than to forbode ill; Weak and small as I am, On the foaming beach of the ocean, In the day of trouble I shall be Of more service to thee than three hundred salmon.”", "슬퍼한들 아무 소용없으니, 불행을 미리 두려워하느니 하느님을 믿는 편이 나으리라. 비록 나는 약하고 작으나, 거품 이는 바닷가에서, 환난의 날에 나는 연어 삼백 마리보다 그대에게 더 큰 보탬이 되리라.\""]], [["This was the first poem that Taliesin ever sung, being to console Elphin in his grief for that the produce of the weir was lost, and what was worse, that all the world would consider that it was through his fault and ill-luck.", "이것이 탈리에신이 처음으로 부른 시였으니, 어량의 소출을 잃은 데다, 더 나쁘게는 온 세상이 그것을 자신의 잘못과 불운 탓으로 여기리라는 비탄에 잠긴 엘핀을 위로하기 위함이었다."], ["Then Elphin asked him what he was, whether man or spirit.", "그러자 엘핀이 그에게 사람인지 정령인지 무엇이냐고 물었다."], ["And he sung thus:", "그러자 그가 이렇게 노래했다."]], [["“I have been formed a comely person; Although I am but little, I am highly gifted; Into a dark leathern bag I was thrown, And on a boundless sea I was sent adrift.", "\"나는 잘생긴 사람으로 빚어졌노라. 비록 작으나, 나는 크게 타고난 재능이 있노라."], ["From seas and from mountains God brings wealth to the fortunate man.”", "어두운 가죽 자루 속에 던져져, 가없는 바다 위로 떠밀려 보내졌노라. 바다에서, 그리고 산에서 하느님은 복 받은 자에게 부를 가져다주시느니라.\""]], [["Then came Elphin to the house of Gwyddno, his father, and Taliesin with him.", "이윽고 엘핀은 자신의 아버지 그위드노의 집에 이르렀으니, 탈리에신도 그와 함께였다."], ["Gwyddno asked him if he had had a good haul at the weir, and he told him that he had got that which was better than fish.", "그위드노가 그에게 어량에서 많이 잡았느냐고 묻자, 그는 물고기보다 더 나은 것을 얻었다고 말했다. \"그게 무엇이냐?\""], ["“What was that?”", "그위드노가 물었다."], ["said Gwyddno. “A bard,”", "\"음유시인입니다,\""], ["said Elphin.", "엘핀이 말했다."], ["Then said Gwyddno, “Alas!", "그러자 그위드노가 말했다. \"아아!"], ["what will he profit thee?”", "그것이 네게 무슨 이로움이 되겠느냐?\""], ["And Taliesin himself replied and said, “He will profit him more than the weir ever profited thee.”", "그러자 탈리에신이 직접 대답하여 말했다. \"그는 어량이 일찍이 어르신께 끼친 이로움보다 더 큰 이로움을 그분께 끼칠 것입니다.\""], ["Asked Gwyddno, “Art thou able to speak, and thou so little?”", "그위드노가 물었다. \"그처럼 작은 네가 말을 할 줄 안단 말이냐?\""], ["And Taliesin answered him, “I am better able to speak than thou to question me.”", "탈리에신이 그에게 대답했다. \"나는 어르신께서 내게 물으시는 것보다 더 잘 말할 줄 압니다.\""], ["“Let me hear what thou canst say,”", "\"네가 무슨 말을 할 수 있는지 들어 보자,\""], ["quoth Gwyddno.", "그위드노가 말했다."], ["Then Taliesin sang:", "그러자 탈리에신이 노래했다."]], [["“Three times have I been born, I know by meditation; All the sciences of the world are collected in my breast, For I know what has been, and what hereafter will occur.”", "\"나는 세 번 태어났노라, 명상으로 그것을 아노라. 세상의 온갖 학문이 내 가슴속에 모여 있으니, 이는 내가 있었던 일과, 앞으로 있을 일을 알기 때문이로다.\""]], [["Elphin gave his haul to his wife, and she nursed him tenderly and lovingly.", "엘핀은 자신이 거둔 것을 아내에게 주었고, 아내는 그를 정성스럽고 사랑스럽게 길렀다. 그때부터 엘핀은 날이 갈수록 더욱더 부유해졌고, 왕의 사랑과 총애도 늘어 갔다."], ["Thenceforward Elphin increased in riches more and more, day after day, and in love and favor with the king; and there abode Taliesin until he was thirteen years old, when Elphin, son of Gwyddno, went by a Christmas invitation to his uncle, Maelgan Gwynedd, who held open court at Christmas-tide in the castle of Dyganwy, for all the number of his lords of both degrees, both spiritual and temporal, with a vast and thronged host of knights and squires.", "탈리에신은 열세 살이 될 때까지 그곳에 머물렀는데, 그때 그위드노의 아들 엘핀은 성탄절 초대를 받아 그의 숙부 마엘간 그위네드에게로 갔다. 마엘간은 성탄절 무렵에 디가뉘 성에서 영적인 신분과 세속적인 신분 양쪽의 모든 귀족들과, 기사와 종자들로 이루어진 거대하고 빽빽한 무리를 모두 불러 모아 큰 잔치를 열고 있었다."], ["And one arose and said, “Is there in the whole world a king so great as Maelgan, or one on whom Heaven has bestowed so many gifts as upon him;—form, and beauty, and meekness, and strength, besides all the powers of the soul?”", "그때 한 사람이 일어나 말했다. \"온 세상에 마엘간만큼 위대한 왕이 있겠는가, 또는 그분만큼 하늘로부터 많은 선물을 받은 자가 있겠는가."], ["And together with these they said that Heaven had given one gift that exceeded all the others, which was the beauty, and grace, and wisdom, and modesty of his queen, whose virtues surpassed those of all the ladies and noble maidens throughout the whole kingdom.", "영혼의 온갖 능력은 말할 것도 없고, 풍채와 미모와 온유함과 힘까지 받으신 분이.\" 그리고 그들은 이와 더불어, 하늘이 다른 모든 것을 능가하는 한 가지 선물을 주셨다고 말했으니, 그것은 곧 그분의 왕비의 아름다움과 우아함과 지혜와 정숙함이었으며, 그 덕성은 온 왕국의 모든 귀부인과 고귀한 처녀들의 덕성을 능가한다는 것이었다."], ["And with this they put questions one to another, Who had braver men?", "그러면서 그들은 서로에게 물음을 던졌다. 누가 더 용감한 부하들을 거느렸는가?"], ["Who had fairer or swifter horses or greyhounds?", "누가 더 아름답거나 빠른 말과 사냥개를 가졌는가?"], ["Who had more skilful or wiser bards than Maelgan?", "누가 마엘간보다 더 능란하거나 현명한 음유시인을 거느렸는가?"]], [["When they had all made an end of their praising the king and his gifts, it befell that Elphin spoke on this wise.", "그들이 모두 왕과 그의 선물에 대한 찬양을 끝냈을 때, 엘핀이 이런 식으로 입을 열게 되었다."], ["“Of a truth, none but a king may vie with a king; but were he not a king, I would say that my wife was as virtuous as any lady in the kingdom, and also that I have a bard who is more skilful than all the king’s bards.”", "\"참으로 왕에 견줄 수 있는 자는 왕밖에 없습니다. 그러나 만일 그가 왕이 아니라면, 나는 내 아내가 이 왕국의 어떤 귀부인 못지않게 정숙하다고 말하겠고, 또한 나는 왕의 모든 음유시인보다 더 능란한 음유시인을 거느리고 있다고 말하겠습니다.\""], ["In a short space some of his fellows told the king all the boastings of Elphin; and the king ordered him to be thrown into a strong prison, until he might show the truth as to the virtues of his wife, and the wisdom of his bard.", "머지않아 그의 동료들 가운데 몇몇이 엘핀의 온갖 자랑을 왕에게 일러바쳤다. 그러자 왕은 그의 아내의 덕성과 음유시인의 지혜에 관한 진실을 그가 증명할 수 있을 때까지 그를 견고한 감옥에 가두라고 명했다."]], [["Now when Elphin had been put in a tower of the castle, with a thick chain about his feet (it is said that it was a silver chain, because he was of royal blood), the king, as the story relates, sent his son Rhun to inquire into the demeanor of Elphin’s wife.", "이제 엘핀이 발에 두꺼운 사슬을 차고서 성의 탑에 갇히자(그가 왕족의 혈통이었던 까닭에 그것이 은사슬이었다고 한다), 이야기가 전하는 바에 따르면, 왕은 엘핀의 아내의 행실을 살피게 하고자 아들 룬을 보냈다."], ["Now Rhun was the most graceless man in the world, and there was neither wife nor maiden with whom he held converse but was evil spoken of.", "그런데 룬은 세상에서 가장 파렴치한 사내였으니, 그가 말을 섞은 아내든 처녀든 험담의 대상이 되지 않은 자가 없었다."], ["While Rhun went in haste towards Elphin’s dwelling, being fully minded to bring disgrace upon his wife, Taliesin told his mistress how that the king had placed his master in durance in prison, and how that Rhun was coming in haste to strive to bring disgrace upon her.", "룬이 엘핀의 아내에게 불명예를 안길 작정을 단단히 하고 서둘러 엘핀의 집으로 가는 동안, 탈리에신은 자신의 안주인에게 왕이 자신의 주인을 옥에 가두었으며, 룬이 그녀에게 불명예를 안기려고 서둘러 오고 있다는 것을 일러 주었다."], ["Wherefore he caused his mistress to array one of the maids of her kitchen in her apparel; which the noble lady gladly did, and she loaded her hands with the best rings that she and her husband possessed.", "그래서 그는 안주인에게 부엌의 하녀 가운데 하나에게 자신의 옷을 입히게 했으니, 고귀한 부인은 기꺼이 그렇게 했고, 자신과 남편이 가진 가장 좋은 반지들로 그 하녀의 손을 가득 채워 주었다."]], [["In this guise Taliesin caused his mistress to put the maiden to sit at the board in her room at supper; and he made her to seem as her mistress, and the mistress to seem as the maid.", "이런 차림새로 탈리에신은 안주인으로 하여금 그 하녀를 안주인의 방 식탁에 앉혀 저녁 식사를 하게 했다. 그는 하녀를 안주인처럼 보이게 하고, 안주인을 하녀처럼 보이게 했다."], ["And when they were in due time seated at their supper, in the manner that has been said, Rhun suddenly arrived at Elphin’s dwelling, and was received with joy, for the servants knew him; and they brought him to the room of their mistress, in the semblance of whom the maid rose up from supper and welcomed him gladly.", "앞서 말한 대로 그들이 때맞춰 저녁 식사 자리에 앉았을 때, 룬이 불쑥 엘핀의 집에 당도했고, 하인들이 그를 알아보았기에 그는 기쁘게 맞아들여졌다. 그들은 그를 안주인의 방으로 데려갔는데, 안주인의 모습을 한 하녀가 저녁 식사 자리에서 일어나 그를 반갑게 맞이했다."], ["And afterwards she sat down to supper again, and Rhun with her.", "그런 다음 하녀는 다시 저녁 식사 자리에 앉았고, 룬도 그녀와 함께 앉았다."], ["Then Rhun began jesting with the maid, who still kept the semblance of her mistress.", "그러자 룬은 여전히 안주인의 모습을 하고 있는 하녀와 농을 주고받기 시작했다."], ["And verily this story shows that the maiden became so intoxicated that she fell asleep; and the story relates that it was a powder that Rhun put into the drink, that made her sleep so soundly that she never felt it when he cut off from her hand her little finger, whereon was the signet ring of Elphin, which he had sent to his wife as a token a short time before.", "그리고 이 이야기가 보여 주는 바에 따르면, 하녀는 몹시 취하여 잠이 들고 말았으니, 이야기가 전하기로는 룬이 술에 가루를 탄 까닭에 그녀가 너무도 깊이 잠들어, 그가 그녀의 손에서 새끼손가락을 잘라낼 때에도 전혀 느끼지 못했다고 한다. 그 손가락에는 엘핀의 인장 반지가 끼워져 있었으니, 그가 얼마 전에 정표로 아내에게 보낸 것이었다."], ["And Rhun returned to the king with the finger and the ring as a proof, to show that he had cut it off from her hand without her awaking from her sleep of intemperance.", "룬은 그 손가락과 반지를 증거로 삼아 왕에게 돌아왔으니, 그녀가 만취하여 잠든 사이에 깨어나지 않은 채로 그것을 그녀의 손에서 잘라냈음을 보이기 위함이었다."]], [["The king rejoiced greatly at these tidings, and he sent for his councillors, to whom he told the whole story from the beginning.", "왕은 이 소식에 크게 기뻐하며 자문관들을 불러, 그들에게 처음부터 모든 이야기를 들려주었다."], ["And he caused Elphin to be brought out of prison, and he chided him because of his boast.", "그리고 엘핀을 감옥에서 끌어내 오게 하여, 그의 허풍을 꾸짖었다. 그는 이런 식으로 말했다."], ["And he spake on this wise: “Elphin, be it known to thee beyond a doubt, that it is but folly for a man to trust in the virtues of his wife further than he can see her; and that thou mayest be certain of thy wife’s vileness, behold her finger, with thy signet ring upon it, which was cut from her hand last night, while she slept the sleep of intoxication.”", "\"엘핀이여, 사내가 자기 눈으로 볼 수 있는 그 이상으로 아내의 덕성을 믿는 것은 어리석은 일임을 의심의 여지 없이 알아 두라. 그대 아내의 비열함을 그대가 확실히 알 수 있도록, 보라, 그대의 인장 반지가 끼워진 그녀의 손가락을."], ["Then thus spake Elphin: “With thy leave, mighty king, I cannot deny my ring, for it is known of many; but verily I assert that the finger around which it is was never attached to the hand of my wife; for in truth and certainty there are three notable things pertaining to it, none of which ever belonged to any of my wife’s fingers.", "이것은 지난밤 그녀가 만취하여 잠든 사이에 그녀의 손에서 잘라낸 것이다.\" 그러자 엘핀이 이렇게 말했다."], ["The first of the three is, that it is certainly known to me that this ring would never remain upon her thumb, whereas you can plainly see that it is hard to draw it over the joint of the little finger of the hand whence this was cut.", "\"황공하오나 위대하신 왕이시여, 저는 제 반지를 부인할 수 없으니, 그것은 많은 이가 아는 것입니다. 그러나 그 반지가 끼워진 손가락은 결코 제 아내의 손에 붙어 있던 것이 아님을 진실로 단언합니다."], ["The second thing is, that my wife has never let pass one Saturday since I have known her, without paring her nails before going to bed, and you can see fully that the nail of this little finger has not been pared for a month.", "진실로 그리고 확실히, 그 손가락에는 주목할 만한 세 가지가 있으니, 그 가운데 어느 것도 제 아내의 손가락 어느 것에도 결코 해당된 적이 없습니다. 세 가지 가운데 첫째는, 이 반지가 결코 제 아내의 엄지손가락에는 머물러 있지 못했으리라는 것을 제가 확실히 안다는 것이니, 반면에 보시다시피 이것을 잘라낸 손의 새끼손가락 마디 위로는 이 반지를 끼우기가 어렵습니다."], ["The third is, truly, that the hand whence this finger came was kneading rye dough within three days before the finger was cut therefrom, and I can assure your highness that my wife has never kneaded rye dough since my wife she has been.”", "둘째는, 제 아내는 제가 그녀를 알게 된 이래로 잠자리에 들기 전에 손톱을 깎지 않은 채 토요일을 단 한 번도 그냥 넘긴 적이 없는데, 이 새끼손가락의 손톱은 한 달 동안 깎이지 않았음을 충분히 보실 수 있다는 것입니다. 셋째는, 진실로, 이 손가락이 나온 손은 손가락이 거기서 잘려 나가기 사흘 전 이내에 호밀 반죽을 이기고 있었다는 것인데, 전하께 장담하건대 제 아내는 제 아내가 된 이래로 호밀 반죽을 이긴 적이 결코 없습니다.\""]], [["The king was mightily wroth with Elphin for so stoutly withstanding him, respecting the goodness of his wife; wherefore he ordered him to his prison a second time, saying that he should not be loosed thence until he had proved the truth of his boast, as well concerning the wisdom of his bard as the virtues of his wife.", "왕은 엘핀이 자기 아내의 어짊에 관하여 그토록 완강하게 자신에게 맞서는 것에 몹시 노했다. 그래서 그는 그를 두 번째로 감옥에 보내라고 명하면서, 음유시인의 지혜와 아내의 덕성 양쪽에 관한 자신의 허풍이 참됨을 증명하기 전까지는 거기서 풀려나지 못하리라고 말했다."]], [["In the meantime his wife and Taliesin remained joyful at Elphin’s dwelling.", "그러는 사이 그의 아내와 탈리에신은 엘핀의 집에서 즐겁게 지냈다."], ["And Taliesin showed his mistress how that Elphin was in prison because of them; but he bade her be glad, for that he would go to Maelgan’s court to free his master.", "탈리에신은 안주인에게 엘핀이 그들 때문에 옥에 갇혀 있음을 알렸으나, 그녀에게 기뻐하라고 일렀으니, 자신이 마엘간의 궁정으로 가서 주인을 풀어 주겠다는 것이었다."], ["So he took leave of his mistress, and came to the court of Maelgan, who was going to sit in his hall, and dine in his royal state, as it was the custom in those days for kings and princes to do at every chief feast.", "그리하여 그는 안주인에게 작별을 고하고 마엘간의 궁정으로 갔다. 마엘간은 자신의 큰 홀에 자리하여 왕다운 위엄을 갖추고 식사를 하려던 참이었으니, 그 시절 왕과 군주들이 큰 잔치 때마다 으레 하던 관례였다."], ["As soon as Taliesin entered the hall he placed himself in a quiet corner, near the place where the bards and the minstrels were wont to come, in doing their service and duty to the king, as is the custom at the high festivals, when the bounty is proclaimed.", "탈리에신은 홀에 들어서자마자, 큰 축제 때 하사품이 선포될 무렵 음유시인과 악사들이 왕에게 봉사와 직분을 다하고자 오곤 하던 자리 가까이의 한적한 구석에 자리를 잡았다."], ["So, when the bards and the heralds came to cry largess, and to proclaim the power of the king, and his strength, at the moment when they passed by the corner wherein he was crouching, Taliesin pouted out his lips after them, and played “Blerwm, blerwm!”", "그리하여 음유시인과 전령들이 하사품을 외치고 왕의 권세와 힘을 선포하러 와서, 그가 웅크리고 있던 구석을 지나는 순간, 탈리에신은 그들을 향해 입술을 쑥 내밀고 손가락으로 입술을 튕기며 \"블레룸, 블레룸!\""], ["with his finger upon his lips.", "하고 소리를 냈다."], ["Neither took they much notice of him as they went by but proceeded forward till they came before the king, unto whom they made their obeisance with their bodies, as they were wont, without speaking a single word, but pouting out their lips, and making mouths at the king, playing, “Blerwm, blerwm!” upon their lips with their fingers, as they had seen the boy do.", "그들은 지나가면서 그에게 그다지 주의를 기울이지 않고 앞으로 나아가 왕 앞에 이르렀는데, 왕에게 평소처럼 몸을 굽혀 절을 하되 단 한 마디도 하지 않고, 입술을 쑥 내밀어 왕을 향해 우스꽝스러운 입 모양을 짓고는, 아이가 하는 것을 본 대로 손가락으로 입술을 튕기며 \"블레룸, 블레룸!\""], ["This sight caused the king to wonder, and to deem within himself that they were drunk with many liquors.", "하고 소리를 냈다. 이 광경에 왕은 의아해하며, 그들이 술을 잔뜩 마시고 취했다고 속으로 여겼다."], ["Wherefore he commanded one of his lords, who served at the board, to go to them and desire them to collect their wits, and to consider where they stood, and what it was fitting for them to do.", "그래서 그는 식탁에서 시중을 들던 귀족 하나에게 그들에게 가서 정신을 차리고, 자신들이 어디에 서 있으며 무엇을 하는 것이 마땅한지 헤아려 보라고 이르게 했다."], ["And this lord did so gladly.", "그 귀족은 기꺼이 그렇게 했다."], ["But they ceased not from their folly any more than before.", "그러나 그들은 전과 마찬가지로 그 어리석은 짓을 그치지 않았다."], ["Whereupon he sent to them a second time, and a third, desiring them to go forth from the hall.", "그러자 그는 두 번째로, 또 세 번째로 그들에게 사람을 보내어 홀에서 나가라고 일렀다."], ["At the last the king ordered one of his squires to give a blow to the chief of them, named Heinin Vardd; and the squire took a broom and struck him on the head, so that he fell back in his seat.", "마침내 왕은 종자 하나에게 그들 가운데 우두머리인 헤이닌 바르드에게 일격을 가하라고 명했다. 종자는 빗자루를 들어 그의 머리를 후려쳤고, 그러자 그는 자기 자리로 나자빠졌다."], ["Then he arose, and went on his knees, and besought leave of the king’s grace to show that this their fault was not through want of knowledge, neither through drunkenness, but by the influence of some spirit that was in the hall.", "그러자 그는 일어나 무릎을 꿇고, 이 잘못이 지식이 모자라서도, 취해서도 아니요, 홀 안에 있는 어떤 정령의 영향 때문임을 보이게 해 달라고 왕의 자비를 간청했다. 그는 이런 식으로 말했다."], ["And he spoke on this wise: “O honorable king, be it known to your grace that not from the strength of drink, or of too much liquor, are we dumb, but through the influence of a spirit that sits in the corner yonder, in the form of a child.”", "\"오, 존귀하신 왕이시여, 저희가 벙어리가 된 것은 술의 힘이나 너무 많이 마신 술 때문이 아니라, 저기 구석에 아이의 모습으로 앉아 있는 어떤 정령의 영향 때문임을 전하께 아뢰옵니다.\""], ["Forthwith the king commanded the squire to fetch him; and he went to the nook where Taliesin sat, and brought him before the king, who asked him what he was, and whence he came.", "그러자 곧바로 왕은 종자에게 그를 데려오라고 명했고, 종자는 탈리에신이 앉아 있는 후미진 곳으로 가서 그를 왕 앞에 데려왔다."], ["And he answered the king in verse:", "왕이 그에게 무엇이며 어디서 왔느냐고 묻자, 그는 시로 왕에게 대답했다."]], [["“Primary chief bard am I to Elphin, And my native country is the region of the summer stars; I have been in Asia with Noah in the ark, I have seen the destruction of Sodom and Gomorrah, I was in India when Rome was built, I have now come here to the remnant of Troia.”", "\"나는 엘핀의 으뜸가는 우두머리 음유시인이요, 나의 고향은 여름 별들의 고장이로다. 나는 노아와 함께 방주에서 아시아에 있었고, 소돔과 고모라의 멸망을 보았으며, 로마가 세워질 때 나는 인도에 있었고, 이제 여기 트로이아의 잔존자들에게로 왔노라.\""]], [["When the king and his nobles had heard the song, they wondered much, for they had never heard the like from a boy so young as he.", "왕과 그의 귀족들이 그 노래를 듣고서 크게 의아해했으니, 이는 그처럼 어린 소년에게서 그와 같은 것을 일찍이 들어 본 적이 없었기 때문이다."], ["And when the king knew that he was the bard of Elphin he bade Heinin, his first and wisest bard, to answer Taliesin, and to strive with him.", "왕은 그가 엘핀의 음유시인임을 알자, 그의 첫째가는 가장 현명한 음유시인 헤이닌에게 탈리에신에게 응수하여 그와 겨루라고 명했다. 그러나 그가 나아갔을 때, 그는 입술로 \"블레룸!\""], ["But when he came he could do no other than play “Blerwm!”", "하고 소리를 내는 것밖에는 아무것도 할 수 없었다."], ["on his lips; and when he sent for the others of the four and twenty bards, they all did likewise, and could do no other.", "그가 스물네 음유시인의 나머지를 부르러 보냈을 때에도, 그들 모두 똑같이 했을 뿐 다른 아무것도 할 수 없었다."], ["And Maelgan asked the boy Taliesin what was his errand, and he answered him in song:", "마엘간이 소년 탈리에신에게 무슨 볼일이냐고 묻자, 그는 노래로 그에게 대답했다."]], [["“Elphin, the son of Gwyddno, Is in the land of Artro, Secured by thirteen locks, For praising his instructor.", "\"그위드노의 아들 엘핀이 아르트로의 땅에 있으니, 그를 가르친 자를 찬양한 죄로 열세 개의 자물쇠로 갇혔도다."], ["Therefore I, Taliesin, Chief of the bards of the west, Will loosen Elphin Out of a golden fetter.”", "그러므로 나, 탈리에신은, 서방 음유시인들의 우두머리로서, 황금 족쇄로부터 엘핀을 풀어 주리라.\""]], [["Then he sang to them a riddle:", "그러고는 그들에게 수수께끼를 노래했다."]], [["“Discover thou what is The strong creature from before the flood, Without flesh, without bone, Without vein, without blood, Without head, without feet; It will neither be older nor younger Than at the beginning.", "\"그대는 알아내 보라, 그것이 무엇인지를, 대홍수 이전부터 있던 강한 피조물, 살도 없고, 뼈도 없고, 힘줄도 없고, 피도 없고, 머리도 없고, 발도 없으며, 태초에 그러했던 것보다 더 늙지도 더 젊어지지도 않으리라."], ["Behold how the sea whitens When first it comes, When it comes from the south, When it strikes on coasts.", "보라, 바다가 어떻게 희게 일렁이는지, 그것이 처음 올 때, 그것이 남쪽에서 올 때, 그것이 해안을 칠 때."], ["It is in the field, it is in the wood, But the eye cannot perceive it.", "그것은 들에도 있고, 숲에도 있으나, 눈으로는 그것을 알아챌 수 없도다."], ["One Being has prepared it, By a tremendous blast, To wreak vengeance On Maelgan Gwynedd.", "한 존재가 그것을 마련하셨으니, 무시무시한 돌풍으로, 마엘간 그위네드에게 복수를 내리시고자 함이라."]], [["While he was thus singing his verse, there arose a mighty storm of wind, so that the king and all his nobles thought that the castle would fall upon their heads.", "그가 이렇게 시를 노래하는 동안 거센 폭풍이 일어, 왕과 그의 모든 귀족들은 성이 그들의 머리 위로 무너지리라 여겼다."], ["And the king caused them to fetch Elphin in haste from his dungeon, and placed him before Taliesin.", "왕은 사람들을 시켜 서둘러 엘핀을 지하 감옥에서 데려오게 하여, 그를 탈리에신 앞에 세웠다."], ["And it is said that immediately he sung a verse, so that the chains opened from about his feet.", "그러자 탈리에신이 곧바로 시 한 수를 노래하니, 사슬이 그의 발에서 풀려났다고 한다."]], [["After that Taliesin brought Elphin’s wife before them, and showed that she had not one finger wanting.", "그런 다음 탈리에신은 엘핀의 아내를 그들 앞에 데려와, 그녀에게 손가락이 하나도 모자라지 않음을 보였다."], ["And in this manner did he set his master free from prison, and protect the innocence of his mistress, and silence the bards so that not one of them dared to say a word.", "이런 식으로 그는 주인을 감옥에서 풀어 주고, 안주인의 결백을 지켰으며, 음유시인들을 잠잠하게 하여 그들 가운데 어느 누구도 감히 한마디도 하지 못하게 했다."], ["Right glad was Elphin, right glad was Taliesin.", "엘핀은 더없이 기뻐했고, 탈리에신도 더없이 기뻐했다. 브리튼 종족의 영웅 신화"]], [["NOTABLE among the names of heroes of the British race is that of Beowulf, which appeals to all English-speaking people in a very special way, since he is the one hero in whose story we may see the ideals of our English forefathers before they left their Continental home to cross to the islands of Britain.", "베오울프\n브리튼 종족의 영웅들의 이름 가운데 두드러진 것이 베오울프의 이름이니, 이는 영어를 쓰는 모든 사람들에게 매우 각별한 방식으로 호소한다. 그는 우리 영국 선조들이 대륙의 고향을 떠나 브리튼 섬들로 건너가기 이전에 품었던 이상을 그 이야기에서 엿볼 수 있는 유일한 영웅이기 때문이다."]], [["Although this hero had distinguished himself by numerous feats of strength during his boyhood and early youth, it was as the deliverer of Hrothgar, king of Denmark, from the monster Grendel that he first gained wide renown.", "이 영웅은 소년 시절과 젊은 날에 수많은 힘의 위업으로 두각을 나타냈으나, 그가 널리 명성을 떨치게 된 것은 덴마크 왕 흐로스가르를 괴물 그렌델에게서 구해 낸 일을 통해서였다."], ["Grendel was half monster and half man, and had his abode in the fen-fastnesses in the vicinity of Hrothgar’s residence.", "그렌델은 반은 괴물이요 반은 사람이었으며, 흐로스가르의 거처 부근의 늪지 요새에 보금자리를 두고 있었다."], ["Night after night he would steal into the king’s great palace called Heorot and slay sometimes as many as thirty at one time of the knights sleeping there.", "그는 밤마다 헤오로트라 불리는 왕의 거대한 궁전에 몰래 숨어들어, 거기서 잠든 기사들을 때로는 한 번에 서른 명씩이나 죽였다."], ["Beowulf put himself at the head of a selected band of warriors, went against the monster, and after a terrible fight slew it.", "베오울프는 엄선된 전사 무리의 선두에 서서 괴물을 치러 갔고, 끔찍한 싸움 끝에 그것을 죽였다."], ["The following night Grendel’s mother, a fiend scarcely less terrible than her son, carried off one of Hrothgar’s boldest thanes.", "이튿날 밤 그렌델의 어미가, 제 아들 못지않게 무시무시한 마귀가, 흐로스가르의 가장 담대한 가신 가운데 하나를 채어 갔다."], ["Once more Beowulf went to the help of the Danish king, followed the she-monster to her lair at the bottom of a muddy lake in the midst of the swamp, and with his good sword Hrunting and his own muscular arms broke the sea-woman’s neck.", "베오울프는 다시 한번 덴마크 왕을 돕기 위해 나서서, 늪 한가운데 진흙 호수 바닥에 있는 암컷 괴물의 소굴까지 그것을 뒤쫓아 가, 그의 훌륭한 검 흐룬팅과 자신의 우람한 두 팔로 그 바다 여인의 목을 부러뜨렸다."]], [["Upon his return to his own country of the Geats, loaded with honors bestowed upon him by Hrothgar, Beowulf served the king of Geatland as the latter’s most trusted counsellor and champion.", "흐로스가르가 그에게 베푼 영예를 가득 안고 자신의 나라 예아트로 돌아온 베오울프는, 예아트랜드의 왕을 섬겨 그의 가장 신임받는 자문관이자 용사가 되었다."], ["When, after many years, the king fell before an enemy, the Geats unanimously chose Beowulf for their new king.", "여러 해가 지나 왕이 적의 손에 쓰러지자, 예아트족은 한마음으로 베오울프를 새 왕으로 뽑았다."], ["His fame as a warrior kept his country free from invasion, and his wisdom as a statesman increased its prosperity and happiness.", "전사로서의 그의 명성은 나라를 침략에서 자유롭게 지켜 주었고, 정치가로서의 그의 지혜는 나라의 번영과 행복을 더해 주었다."]], [["In the fiftieth year of Beowulf’s reign, however, a great terror fell upon the land in the way of a monstrous fire-dragon, which flew forth by night from its den in the rocks, lighting up the blackness with its blazing breath, and burning houses and homesteads, men and cattle, with the flames from its mouth.", "그러나 베오울프 통치 오십 년째 되던 해, 거대한 불을 뿜는 용의 형상으로 큰 공포가 그 땅에 닥쳤다. 그 용은 밤이면 바위 속 굴에서 날아 나와 타오르는 숨결로 어둠을 밝히고, 입에서 나오는 불길로 집과 농가, 사람과 가축을 불태웠다."], ["When the news came to Beowulf that his people were suffering and dying, and that no warrior dared to risk his life in an effort to deliver the country from this deadly devastation, the aged king took up his shield and sword and went forth to his last fight.", "자신의 백성이 고통받고 죽어 가는데도 어떤 전사도 감히 목숨을 걸고 이 죽음의 황폐로부터 나라를 구해 내려 하지 않는다는 소식이 베오울프에게 이르자, 늙은 왕은 방패와 검을 들고 자신의 마지막 싸움을 향해 나아갔다."], ["At the entrance of the dragon’s cave Beowulf raised his voice and shouted a furious defiance to the awesome guardian of the den.", "용의 동굴 입구에서 베오울프는 목청을 높여 굴의 무시무시한 수호자에게 격렬한 도전을 외쳤다."], ["Roaring hideously and flapping his glowing wings together, the dragon rushed forth and half flew, half sprang, on Beowulf.", "용은 끔찍하게 포효하며 이글거리는 두 날개를 마주쳐 푸드덕거리고는 베오울프를 향해 돌진하여, 반은 날고 반은 뛰어올라 그에게 덤벼들었다."], ["Then began a fearful combat, which ended in Beowulf’s piercing the dragon’s scaly armor and inflicting a mortal wound, but alas!", "그리하여 무시무시한 격투가 시작되었으니, 그것은 베오울프가 용의 비늘 갑옷을 꿰뚫어 치명상을 입히는 것으로 끝났으나, 아아!"], ["in himself being given a gash in the neck by his opponent’s poisoned fangs which resulted in his death.", "그 자신도 적의 독 어금니에 목을 물려 상처를 입었고, 그것이 그의 죽음을 불러왔다."], ["As he lay stretched on the ground, his head supported by Wiglaf, an honored warrior who had helped in the fight with the dragon, Beowulf roused himself to say, as he grasped Wiglaf’s hand:", "그가 땅바닥에 쭉 뻗은 채 누워, 용과의 싸움을 도왔던 명예로운 전사 위글라프에게 머리를 받친 채, 베오울프는 위글라프의 손을 움켜쥐며 안간힘을 다해 말했다."]], [["“Thou must now look to the needs of the nation; Here dwell I no longer, for Destiny calleth me!", "\"이제 그대는 나라의 필요를 돌보아야 하리라. 나는 더 이상 여기 머물지 않으니, 운명이 나를 부르는도다!"], ["Bid thou my warriors after my funeral pyre Build me a burial-cairn high on the sea-cliff’s head; So that the seafarers Beowulf’s Barrow Henceforth shall name it, they who drive far and wide Over the mighty flood their foamy keels.", "그대는 내 전사들에게 일러, 나의 화장단이 사위거든 바닷가 벼랑 꼭대기에 나의 봉분을 높이 쌓게 하라. 하여 거센 물결 위로 거품 이는 용골을 멀리 모는 뱃사람들이 앞으로 그것을 베오울프의 봉분이라 부르게 하라."], ["Thou art the last of all the kindred of Wagmund!", "그대는 와그문드 일족의 마지막 사람이로다!"], ["Wyrd has swept all my kin, all the brave chiefs away!", "위르드가 내 모든 혈족을, 그 모든 용감한 우두머리들을 휩쓸어 갔도다!"], ["Now must I follow them!”", "이제 나는 그들을 뒤따라야 하리라!\""]], [["These last words spoken, the king of the Geats, brave to seek danger and brave to look on death and Fate undaunted, fell back dead.", "이 마지막 말을 남기고, 위험을 찾아 나서는 데 용감하고 죽음과 운명을 의연히 마주하는 데 용감했던 예아트족의 왕은 쓰러져 숨을 거두었다."], ["According to his last desires, his followers gathered wood and piled it on the cliff-head.", "그의 마지막 소망에 따라, 그의 부하들은 나무를 모아 벼랑 꼭대기에 쌓아 올렸다."], ["Upon this funeral pyre was laid Beowulf’s body and consumed to ashes.", "이 화장단 위에 베오울프의 시신을 누이고 재가 되도록 태웠다."], ["Then, upon the same cliff of Hronesness, was erected a huge burial cairn, widespread and lofty, to be known thereafter as Beowulf’s Barrow.", "그런 다음, 바로 그 흐로네스네스의 벼랑 위에, 너르게 펼쳐지고 우뚝 솟은 거대한 봉분을 세웠으니, 그것은 이후로 베오울프의 봉분이라 알려지게 되었다."]], [["————", "————"]], [["# CUCHULAIN, CHAMPION OF IRELAND", "# CUCHULAIN, CHAMPION OF IRELAND"]], [["AMONG all the early literatures of Europe, there are two which, at exactly opposite corners of the continent, display most strikingly similar characteristics.", "아일랜드의 용사 쿠훌린\n유럽의 모든 초기 문학 가운데, 대륙의 정반대 모퉁이에 자리하면서도 가장 놀랍도록 비슷한 특징을 보여 주는 둘이 있다."], ["These are the Greek and the Irish, and the legend of the Irish champion Cuchulain, which well illustrates the similarity of the literatures, bears so close a resemblance to the story of Achilles as to win for this hero the title of “the Irish Achilles.”", "그것은 그리스 문학과 아일랜드 문학으로, 두 문학의 유사성을 잘 보여 주는 아일랜드 용사 쿠훌린의 전설은 아킬레우스의 이야기와 너무도 닮아서, 이 영웅에게 \"아일랜드의 아킬레우스\"라는 칭호를 얻어 주었다."], ["Certainly in reckless courage, power of inspiring dread, sense of personal merit, and frankness of speech the Irish hero is fully equal to the mighty Greek.", "참으로 무모한 용기, 두려움을 불러일으키는 위력, 자기 가치에 대한 자부심, 그리고 거침없는 언변에서, 아일랜드 영웅은 막강한 그리스 영웅과 전적으로 대등하다."]], [["Cuchulain was the nephew of King Conor of Ulster, son of his sister Dechtire, and it is said that his father was no mortal man, but the great god Lugh of the Long Hand.", "쿠훌린은 얼스터 왕 코노르의 조카로, 그의 누이 데흐티레의 아들이었으며, 그의 아버지는 인간이 아니라 긴 손의 위대한 신 루였다고 한다."], ["Cuchulain was brought up by King Conor himself, and even while he was still a boy his fame spread all over Ireland.", "쿠훌린은 코노르 왕이 손수 길렀는데, 그가 아직 소년이었을 때부터 그의 명성은 온 아일랜드에 퍼졌다."], ["His warlike deeds were those of a proved warrior, not of a child of nursery age; and by the time Cuchulain was seventeen he was without peer among the champions of Ulster.", "그의 호전적인 행적은 젖먹이 또래의 아이가 아니라 노련한 전사의 그것이었으니, 쿠훌린이 열일곱 살이 될 무렵 그는 얼스터의 용사들 가운데 견줄 자가 없었다."]], [["Upon Cuchulain’s marriage to Emer, daughter of Forgall the Wily, a Druid of great power, the couple took up their residence at Armagh, the capital of Ulster, under the protection of King Conor.", "쿠훌린이 큰 권능을 지닌 드루이드인 교활한 포갈의 딸 에머와 혼인하자, 부부는 코노르 왕의 보호 아래 얼스터의 수도 아마에 거처를 정했다."], ["Here there was one chief, Bricriu of the Bitter Tongue, who, like Thersites among the Grecian leaders, delighted in making mischief.", "이곳에는 한 우두머리가 있었으니, 그리스 지도자들 가운데 테르시테스가 그러했듯이, 독설가 브리크리우라는 자로서 분란 일으키기를 즐겼다."], ["Soon he had on foot plans for stirring up strife among the heroes of Ulster, leaders among whom were the mighty Laegaire, Conall Cearnach, cousin of Cuchulain, and Cuchulain himself.", "머지않아 그는 얼스터의 영웅들 사이에 다툼을 부추길 계략을 꾸미고 있었는데, 그 영웅들 가운데 으뜸은 막강한 라이가레, 쿠훌린의 사촌 코날 케르나흐, 그리고 쿠훌린 자신이었다."], ["Inviting the members of King Conor’s court to dinner, Bricriu arranged that a contest should arise over who should have the “champion’s portion,” and so successful was he that, to avoid a bloody fight, the three heroes mentioned decided to submit their claims to the championship of Ireland to King Ailill of Connaught.", "브리크리우는 코노르 왕의 궁정 사람들을 만찬에 초대하여, 누가 \"용사의 몫\"을 차지할지를 두고 다툼이 일어나도록 꾸몄으니, 그 일이 어찌나 성공적이었던지, 피비린내 나는 싸움을 피하고자 앞서 말한 세 영웅은 아일랜드 용사의 우두머리 자리에 대한 자신들의 주장을 코노트 왕 아일릴에게 맡겨 판가름하기로 했다."]], [["Ailill put the heroes to an unexpected test.", "아일릴은 영웅들을 뜻밖의 시험에 들게 했다."], ["Their dinner was served them in a separate room, into which three magic beasts, in the shape of monstrous cats, were sent by the king.", "그들의 만찬이 따로 떨어진 방에 차려졌는데, 왕은 그 방으로 거대한 고양이의 형상을 한 세 마리의 마법 짐승을 들여보냈다."], ["When they saw them Laegire and Conall rose from their meal, climbed among the rafters, and stayed there all night.", "그것들을 보자 라이가레와 코날은 식사 자리에서 일어나 서까래 사이로 기어 올라가 밤새도록 거기 머물렀다."], ["Cuchulain waited until one cat attacked him, and then, drawing his sword, struck the monster.", "쿠훌린은 한 마리 고양이가 자신을 덮칠 때까지 기다렸다가, 검을 뽑아 그 괴물을 내리쳤다."], ["It showed no further sign of fight, and at daybreak the magic beasts disappeared.", "그것은 더 이상 싸울 기색을 보이지 않았고, 동이 트자 마법 짐승들은 사라졌다."]], [["As Laegire and Conall claimed that this test was an unfair one, Ailill sent the three rivals to Curoi of Kerry, a just and wise man, who set out to discover by wizardry and enchantments the best among the heroes.", "라이가레와 코날이 이 시험은 공정치 못했다고 주장하자, 아일릴은 세 경쟁자를 케리의 쿠로이에게 보냈다. 그는 공정하고 현명한 사람으로, 마법과 주술로 영웅들 가운데 으뜸을 가려내기로 했다."], ["In turn they stood watch outside Curoi’s castle, where Laegire and Conall were overcome by a huge giant, who hurled spears of mighty oak trees, and ended by throwing them over the wall into the courtyard.", "그들은 차례로 쿠로이의 성 밖에서 보초를 섰는데, 그곳에서 라이가레와 코날은 거대한 거인에게 제압당하고 말았다. 그 거인은 거대한 떡갈나무로 만든 창들을 내던졌고, 끝내는 그들을 담장 너머 안뜰로 내동댕이쳤다."], ["Cuchulain alone withstood the giant, whereupon he was attacked by other magic foes.", "쿠훌린만이 홀로 거인을 막아 냈는데, 그러자 그는 다른 마법의 적들에게 공격을 받았다."], ["Among these was a dragon, which flew on horrible wings from a neighboring lake, and seemed ready to devour everything in its way.", "그 가운데에는 용 한 마리가 있었으니, 그것은 이웃한 호수에서 끔찍한 날개를 펴고 날아와 앞을 가로막는 모든 것을 집어삼킬 듯이 보였다."], ["Cuchulain sprang up, giving his wonderful hero-leap, thrust his arm into the dragon’s mouth and down its throat, and tore out its heart.", "쿠훌린은 그 놀라운 영웅의 도약으로 뛰어올라, 용의 입속으로, 목구멍 아래로 팔을 찔러 넣어 그 심장을 뜯어냈다."], ["After the monster fell dead, he cut off its scaly head.", "괴물이 죽어 쓰러지자, 그는 그 비늘 덮인 머리를 베어 냈다."]], [["As even yet Cuchulain’s opponents would not admit his championship, they were all three directed to return to Armagh, to await Curoi’s judgment.", "그런데도 쿠훌린의 적수들이 그의 우두머리 자격을 인정하려 하지 않자, 세 사람 모두 아마로 돌아가 쿠로이의 판결을 기다리라는 지시를 받았다."], ["Here it happened that all the Ulster heroes were in the great hall one night, except Cuchulain and his cousin Conall.", "그러던 어느 날 밤, 쿠훌린과 그의 사촌 코날을 제외한 얼스터의 영웅들이 모두 큰 홀에 있게 되었다."], ["As they sat in order of rank, a terrible stranger, gigantic in stature, hideous of aspect, with ravening yellow eyes, entered.", "그들이 신분 순서대로 앉아 있을 때, 거구에다 흉측한 외모를 하고, 게걸스러운 노란 눈을 한 무시무시한 낯선 자가 들어왔다."], ["In his hand he bore an enormous axe, with keen and shining edge.", "그의 손에는 날이 예리하고 번쩍이는 거대한 도끼가 들려 있었다."], ["Upon King Conor’s inquiring his business there, the stranger replied:", "코노르 왕이 그곳에 온 용건을 묻자, 낯선 자가 대답했다."]], [["“Behold my axe!", "\"내 도끼를 보라!"], ["The man who will grasp it to-day may cut my head off with it, provided that I may, in like manner, cut off his head to-morrow.", "오늘 이것을 움켜쥐는 자는 이것으로 내 머리를 벨 수 있으되, 다만 내가 같은 방식으로 내일 그자의 머리를 벨 수 있어야 한다."], ["If you have no champion who dare face me, I will say that Ulster has lost her courage and is dishonored.”", "만일 그대들에게 감히 나를 마주할 용사가 없다면, 나는 얼스터가 용기를 잃었고 명예를 잃었다고 말하리라.\""]], [["At once Laegire accepted the challenge.", "곧바로 라이가레가 그 도전을 받아들였다."], ["The giant laid his head on a block, and at a blow the hero severed it from the body.", "거인은 자기 머리를 받침대 위에 올려놓았고, 영웅은 단칼에 그것을 몸에서 베어 냈다."], ["Thereupon the giant arose, took the head and the axe, and thus, headless, strode from the hall.", "그러자 거인은 일어나 머리와 도끼를 집어 들고는, 그렇게 머리 없이 홀에서 성큼성큼 걸어 나갔다."], ["But the following night, when he returned, sound as ever, to claim the fulfilment of Laegire’s promise, the latter’s heart failed him and he did not come forward.", "그러나 이튿날 밤 그가 여전히 멀쩡한 모습으로 돌아와 라이가레의 약속 이행을 요구하자, 라이가레는 겁을 먹고 앞으로 나서지 못했다."], ["The stranger then jeered at the men of Ulster because their great champion durst not keep his agreement, nor face the blow he should receive in return for the one he gave.", "그러자 낯선 자는 얼스터의 사내들을 비웃었으니, 그들의 위대한 용사가 감히 약속을 지키지도, 자신이 가한 일격의 대가로 받아야 할 일격을 마주하지도 못한다는 것이었다."]], [["The men of Ulster were utterly ashamed, but Conall Cearnach, who was present that night, made a new agreement with the stranger.", "얼스터의 사내들은 더없이 부끄러웠으나, 그날 밤 그 자리에 있던 코날 케르나흐가 낯선 자와 새로이 약조를 맺었다."], ["He gave a blow which beheaded the giant, but again, when the latter returned whole and sound on the following evening, the champion was not to be found.", "그는 거인의 머리를 베는 일격을 가했으나, 이번에도 이튿날 저녁 거인이 멀쩡하고 온전한 모습으로 돌아오자, 그 용사는 어디서도 찾을 수 없었다."], ["Now it was the turn of Cuchulain, who, as the others had done, cut off the giant’s head at one stroke.", "이제 쿠훌린의 차례가 되었으니, 그는 다른 이들이 그러했듯이 단칼에 거인의 머리를 베어 냈다."], ["The next day the members of Conor’s court watched Cuchulain to see what he would do.", "이튿날 코노르의 궁정 사람들은 쿠훌린이 어찌하는지 보려고 그를 지켜보았다."], ["They would not have been surprised if he had failed like the others, who now were present.", "그가 다른 이들처럼 실패했더라도 그들은 놀라지 않았을 것이며, 그 다른 이들은 이제 그 자리에 있었다."], ["The champion, however, showed no signs of failing or retreat.", "그러나 용사는 실패하거나 물러설 기색을 조금도 보이지 않았다."], ["He sat sorrowfully in his place, and with a sigh said to King Conor as they waited: “Do not leave this place till all is over.", "그는 슬픈 마음으로 자기 자리에 앉아 있다가, 그들이 기다리는 동안 한숨을 쉬며 코노르 왕에게 말했다. \"모든 것이 끝날 때까지 이 자리를 떠나지 마소서."], ["Death is coming to me very surely, but I must fulfil my agreement, for I would rather die than break my word.”", "죽음이 제게 아주 확실히 다가오고 있으나, 저는 제 약조를 이행해야 합니다. 약속을 어기느니 차라리 죽는 편이 낫기 때문입니다.\""]], [["Towards the close of day the stranger strode into the hall exultant.", "날이 저물 무렵, 낯선 자가 의기양양하게 홀로 성큼성큼 걸어 들어왔다."]], [["“Where is Cuchulain?”", "\"쿠훌린은 어디 있느냐?\""], ["he cried.", "그가 외쳤다."]], [["“Here I am,”", "\"여기 있다,\""], ["was the reply.", "하는 대답이 돌아왔다."]], [["“Ah, poor boy!", "\"아, 가엾은 녀석! 오늘 밤 네 말이 슬프구나."], ["your speech is sad to-night, and the fear of death lies heavy on you; but at least you have redeemed your word and have not failed me.”", "죽음의 두려움이 네게 무겁게 드리워 있구나. 허나 적어도 너는 네 말을 지켰고 나를 저버리지 않았도다.\""]], [["The youth rose from his seat and went towards him, as he stood with the great axe ready, and knelt to receive the blow.", "젊은이는 자리에서 일어나, 거대한 도끼를 든 채 서 있는 그를 향해 나아가, 일격을 받고자 무릎을 꿇었다."]], [["The hero of Ulster laid his head on the block; but the giant was not satisfied.", "얼스터의 영웅은 받침대 위에 머리를 올려놓았다. 그러나 거인은 만족하지 않았다."], ["“Stretch out your neck better,”", "\"목을 더 길게 빼라,\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“You are playing with me, to torment me,”", "\"나를 가지고 놀며 괴롭히는구나,\""], ["said Cuchulain.", "쿠훌린이 말했다."], ["“Slay me now speedily, for I did not keep you waiting last night.”", "\"이제 어서 나를 죽여라. 나는 어젯밤 너를 기다리게 하지 않았다.\""]], [["However, he stretched out his neck as ordered, and the stranger raised his axe till it crashed upwards through the rafters of the hall, like the crash of trees falling in a storm.", "그러나 그는 명령받은 대로 목을 길게 뺐고, 낯선 자는 도끼를 들어 올렸다. 도끼는 폭풍 속에 나무들이 쓰러질 때의 굉음처럼 홀의 서까래를 위로 뚫고 부딪쳤다."], ["When the axe came down with a terrific sound all men looked fearfully at Cuchulain.", "도끼가 무시무시한 소리와 함께 내려오자, 모든 사람이 두려움에 떨며 쿠훌린을 바라보았다."], ["The descending axe had not even touched him; it had come down with the blunt side on the ground, and the youth knelt there unharmed.", "내려온 도끼는 그를 건드리지조차 않았으니, 그것은 무딘 쪽이 땅에 닿도록 내려왔고, 젊은이는 다친 데 없이 그곳에 무릎을 꿇고 있었다."], ["Smiling at him, and leaning on his axe, stood no terrible and hideous stranger, but Curoi of Kerry, come to give his decision at last.", "그에게 미소 지으며 도끼에 기대어 선 자는 무시무시하고 흉측한 낯선 자가 아니라, 마침내 판결을 내리러 온 케리의 쿠로이였다."]], [["“Rise up, Cuchulain,”", "\"일어나라, 쿠훌린,\""], ["said Curoi.", "쿠로이가 말했다."], ["“There is none among all the heroes of Ulster to equal you in courage and loyalty and truth.", "\"얼스터의 모든 영웅 가운데 용기와 충성과 진실에서 그대에 견줄 자가 없도다."], ["The Championship of the Heroes of Ireland is yours from this day forth, and the Champion’s Portion at all feasts; and to your wife I adjudge the first place among all the women of Ulster.", "오늘 이후로 아일랜드 용사들의 우두머리 자격은 그대의 것이며, 모든 잔치에서 용사의 몫도 그대의 것이라. 그리고 그대의 아내에게는 얼스터의 모든 여인 가운데 으뜸의 자리를 내가 판결하노라."], ["Woe to him who dares to dispute this decision!”", "이 판결에 감히 이의를 다는 자에게 화가 있으리라!\""], ["Thereupon Curoi vanished, and the warriors gathered around Cuchulain, and all with one voice acclaimed him the Champion of the Heroes of all Ireland—a title which has clung to him until this day.", "그러고는 쿠로이가 사라졌고, 전사들이 쿠훌린의 둘레에 모여, 모두 한목소리로 그를 온 아일랜드 용사들의 우두머리로 환호했으니, 그 칭호는 오늘날까지도 그에게 따라붙어 있다."]], [["This is one of many stories told of the Irish champion, whose deeds of bravery would fill many pages.", "이것은 아일랜드의 용사에 관해 전해지는 수많은 이야기 가운데 하나이니, 그의 용맹한 행적은 여러 쪽을 가득 채우고도 남으리라."], ["Cuchulain finally came to his end on the field of battle, after a fight in which he displayed all his usual gallantry but in which unfair means were used to overcome him.", "쿠훌린은 결국 전장에서 최후를 맞았으니, 그 싸움에서 그는 여느 때와 다름없는 용맹을 한껏 떨쳤으나, 그를 제압하기 위해 부당한 수단이 쓰였다."]], [["For Wales and for England during centuries Arthur has been the representative “very gentle perfect knight.”", "여러 세기에 걸쳐 웨일스와 잉글랜드에 아서는 대표적인 \"더없이 점잖고 완벽한 기사\"였다."], ["In a similar way, in England’s sister isle, Cuchulain stands ever for the highest ideals of the Irish Gaels.", "그와 마찬가지로, 잉글랜드의 자매 섬에서는 쿠훌린이 아일랜드 게일족의 가장 드높은 이상을 언제나 대변한다."]], [["————", "————"]], [["# HEREWARD THE WAKE", "# HEREWARD THE WAKE"]], [["IN Hereward the Wake (or “Watchful”) is found one of those heroes whose date can be ascertained with a fair amount of exactness and yet in whose story occur mythological elements which seem to belong to all ages.", "파수꾼 헤리워드\n파수꾼(또는 \"경계하는 자\") 헤리워드에게서, 우리는 그 연대를 상당히 정확하게 확인할 수 있으면서도 그 이야기 속에는 모든 시대에 속하는 듯한 신화적 요소가 등장하는, 그런 영웅들 가운데 하나를 발견한다."], ["The folklore of primitive races is a great storehouse whence a people can choose tales and heroic deeds to glorify its own national hero, careless that the same tales and deeds have done duty for other peoples and other heroes.", "원시 종족의 민담은 거대한 보고이니, 한 민족은 거기서 이야기와 영웅적 행적을 골라 자기 민족의 영웅을 찬미할 수 있으되, 그 똑같은 이야기와 행적이 다른 민족과 다른 영웅을 위해 쓰인 적이 있다는 것은 개의치 않는다."], ["Hence it happens that Hereward the Saxon, a patriot hero as real and actual as Nelson or George Washington, whose deeds were recorded in prose and verse within forty years of his death, was even then surrounded by a cloud of romance and mystery, which hid in vagueness his family, his marriage, and even his death.", "그리하여 넬슨이나 조지 워싱턴 못지않게 실재하고 실존했던 애국 영웅, 그가 죽은 지 사십 년 안에 그 행적이 산문과 운문으로 기록되었던 색슨족 헤리워드조차도, 이미 그때부터 전설과 신비의 구름에 둘러싸여, 그의 가문도, 그의 혼인도, 심지어 그의 죽음까지도 그 구름이 모호하게 가려 버렸던 것이다."]], [["Briefly it may be stated that Hereward was a native of Lincolnshire, and was in his prime about 1070.", "간략히 말하자면, 헤리워드는 링컨셔 태생으로, 1070년 무렵 한창때였다."], ["In that year he joined a party of Danes who appeared in England, attacked Peterborough and sacked the abbey there, and afterward took refuge in the Isle of Ely.", "그해에 그는 잉글랜드에 나타난 덴마크인 무리에 가담하여 피터버러를 공격하고 그곳 수도원을 약탈했으며, 이후 일리 섬으로 피신했다."], ["Here he was besieged by William the Conqueror, and was finally forced to yield to the Norman.", "그곳에서 그는 정복왕 윌리엄에게 포위되었고, 끝내 그 노르만인에게 항복할 수밖에 없었다."], ["He thus came to stand for the defeated Saxon race, and his name has been passed down as that of the darling hero of the Saxons.", "이리하여 그는 패배한 색슨 종족을 대변하게 되었고, 그의 이름은 색슨족이 가장 사랑하는 영웅의 이름으로 후세에 전해졌다."], ["For his splendid defence of Ely they forgave his final surrender to Duke William; they attributed to him all the virtues supposed to be inherent in the free-born, and all the glorious valor on which the English prided themselves; and, lastly, they surrounded his death with a halo of desperate fighting, and made his last conflict as wonderful as that of Roland at Roncesvalles.", "그가 일리를 훌륭히 방어한 일로 인해, 사람들은 그가 끝내 윌리엄 공에게 항복한 것을 용서해 주었다. 그들은 자유민에게 본래 깃들어 있다고 여겨지는 온갖 덕성과, 영국인이 자랑으로 삼던 온갖 영광스러운 용맹을 그에게 돌렸으며, 마지막으로 그의 죽음을 필사적인 싸움의 후광으로 둘러싸, 그의 마지막 격전을 롱스보에서의 롤랑의 그것만큼이나 경이로운 것으로 만들었다."], ["If Roland is the ideal of Norman feudal chivalry, Hereward is equally the ideal of Anglo-Saxon sturdy manliness and knighthood.", "롤랑이 노르만 봉건 기사도의 이상이라면, 헤리워드도 그에 못지않게 앵글로색슨의 굳건한 사내다움과 기사도의 이상이다."]], [["An account of one of Hereward’s adventures as a youth will serve as illustration of the stories told of his prowess.", "헤리워드가 젊은 시절에 겪은 한 모험에 대한 이야기는 그의 용맹에 관해 전해지는 이야기들의 본보기가 될 것이다."], ["On an enforced visit to Cornwall, he found that King Alef, a petty British chief, had betrothed his fair daughter to a terrible Pictish giant, breaking off, in order to do it, her troth-plight with Prince Sigtryg of Waterford, son of a Danish king in Ireland.", "콘월로 어쩔 수 없이 가게 되었을 때, 그는 작은 브리튼족 우두머리인 알레프 왕이 자신의 아름다운 딸을 무시무시한 픽트족 거인에게 약혼시켰다는 것을 알게 되었는데, 그렇게 하기 위해 왕은 딸과 아일랜드의 한 덴마크 왕의 아들인 워터퍼드의 시그트뤼그 왕자 사이의 혼약을 깨뜨렸다."], ["Hereward, ever chivalrous, picked a quarrel with the giant and killed him in fair fight, whereupon the king threw him into prison.", "언제나 의협심이 강한 헤리워드는 거인에게 시비를 걸어 정정당당한 싸움에서 그를 죽였고, 그러자 왕은 그를 옥에 가두었다."], ["In the following night, however, the released princess arranged that the gallant Saxon should be freed and sent hot-foot for her lover, Prince Sigtryg.", "그러나 이튿날 밤, 풀려난 공주가 손을 써서 그 용감한 색슨인이 풀려나도록 하여, 그를 자신의 연인 시그트뤼그 왕자에게 부랴부랴 보냈다."], ["After many adventures Hereward reached the prince, who hastened to return to Cornwall with the young hero.", "헤리워드는 숱한 모험 끝에 왕자에게 이르렀고, 왕자는 그 젊은 영웅과 함께 서둘러 콘월로 돌아갔다."], ["But to the grief of both, they learned upon their arrival that the princess had just been betrothed to a wild Cornish hero, Haco, and the wedding feast was to be held that very day.", "그러나 두 사람 모두에게 비통하게도, 그들은 도착해서야 공주가 사납고 거친 콘월의 영웅 하코에게 막 약혼했으며, 혼인 잔치가 바로 그날 열리기로 되어 있다는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["Sigtryg at once sent a troop of forty Danes to King Alef demanding the fulfilment of the troth-plight between himself and his daughter, and threatening vengeance if it were broken.", "시그트뤼그는 곧바로 덴마크인 마흔 명의 무리를 알레프 왕에게 보내, 자신과 왕의 딸 사이의 혼약을 이행할 것을 요구하며, 만일 그것이 깨진다면 복수하겠다고 위협했다."], ["To this threat the king returned no answer, and no Dane came back to tell of their reception.", "이 위협에 왕은 아무런 대답도 내놓지 않았고, 그들이 어떻게 맞아들여졌는지를 전하러 돌아온 덴마크인은 하나도 없었다."]], [["Sigtryg would have waited till morning, trusting in the honor of the king, but Hereward disguised himself as a minstrel and obtained admission to the bridal feast, where he soon won applause by his beautiful singing.", "시그트뤼그는 왕의 명예를 믿고 아침까지 기다리려 했으나, 헤리워드는 음유시인으로 변장하여 혼인 잔치에 들어가는 것을 허락받았고, 그곳에서 그는 곧 아름다운 노래로 갈채를 받았다. 신랑 하코는 황홀해하며 그가 청하는 것은 무엇이든 들어주겠다고 했으나, 그는 다만 신부의 손으로 따라 주는 포도주 한 잔만을 청했다."], ["The bridegroom, Haco, in a rapture offered him any boon he liked to ask, but he demanded only a cup of wine from the hands of the bride.", "그녀가 그것을 그에게 가져오자, 그는 빈 잔에 혼약의 반지를, 그녀가 시그트뤼그에게 정표로 보냈던 그 반지를 던져 넣고 말했다."], ["When she brought it to him he flung into the empty cup the betrothal ring, the token she had sent to Sigtryg, and said: “I thank thee, lady, and would reward thee for thy gentleness to a wandering minstrel; I give back the cup, richer than before by the kind thoughts of which it bears the token.”", "\"고맙소, 부인이여, 떠도는 음유시인에게 베풀어 주신 너그러움에 보답하고자 합니다. 그 마음 씀씀이의 정표를 담아, 전보다 더 값진 이 잔을 돌려 드리리다.\""], ["The princess looked at him, gazed into the goblet, and saw her ring; then, looking again, she recognized her deliverer and knew that rescue was at hand.", "공주는 그를 바라보다가 잔 속을 들여다보고 자신의 반지를 발견했다. 그러고는 다시 바라보고서, 자신의 구원자를 알아보았고 구출이 가까이 왔음을 알았다."]], [["While men feasted Hereward listened and talked, and found out that the forty Danes were prisoners, to be released on the morrow when Haco was sure of his bride, but released useless and miserable, since they would be turned adrift blinded.", "사람들이 잔치를 벌이는 동안 헤리워드는 귀를 기울이고 이야기를 나누면서, 마흔 명의 덴마크인이 포로로 잡혀 있으며, 이튿날 하코가 신부를 확실히 차지하게 되면 풀려나되, 다만 눈이 멀린 채 쫓겨나 쓸모없고 비참한 신세로 풀려나리라는 것을 알아냈다."], ["Haco was taking his lovely bride back to his own land, and Hereward saw that any rescue, to be successful, must be attempted on the march.", "하코는 자신의 아름다운 신부를 데리고 자기 땅으로 돌아가려 하고 있었고, 헤리워드는 어떤 구출이든 성공하려면 행군 중에 시도해야 함을 알았다."]], [["Returning to Sigtryg, the young Saxon told all that he had learned, and the Danes planned an ambush in the ravine where Haco had decided to blind and set free his captives.", "젊은 색슨인은 시그트뤼그에게 돌아가 자신이 알아낸 모든 것을 일러 주었고, 덴마크인들은 하코가 포로들의 눈을 멀게 한 뒤 풀어 주기로 정한 골짜기에 매복을 계획했다."], ["The whole was carried out exactly as Hereward arranged it.", "모든 일이 헤리워드가 짜 놓은 그대로 실행되었다."], ["The Cornishmen, with the Danish captives, passed first without attack; next came Haco, riding grim and ferocious beside his silent bride, he exulting in his success, she looking eagerly for any signs of rescue.", "콘월 사람들은 덴마크인 포로들과 함께 먼저 공격받지 않고 지나갔다. 다음으로 하코가 왔으니, 말 없는 신부 곁에서 험상궂고 사납게 말을 타고 있었으며, 그는 자신의 성공에 의기양양해하고, 그녀는 구출의 기색이 없나 애타게 살피고 있었다."], ["As they passed Hereward sprang from his shelter, crying, “Upon them, Danes, and set your brethren free!”", "그들이 지나갈 때 헤리워드가 은신처에서 뛰쳐나오며 외쳤다. \"저들에게 덤벼라, 덴마크인들이여, 그대들의 형제를 풀어 주어라!\""], ["and himself struck down Haco and smote off his head.", "그러고는 직접 하코를 쳐 쓰러뜨리고 그 머리를 베어 냈다."], ["There was a short struggle, but soon the rescued Danes were able to aid their deliverers, and the Cornish guards were all slain; the men of King Alef, never very zealous for the cause of Haco, fled, and the Danes were left masters of the field.", "짧은 격전이 있었으나, 곧 구출된 덴마크인들이 자신들의 구원자들을 도울 수 있게 되었고, 콘월의 호위병들은 모두 죽임을 당했다. 본래 하코의 대의에 그다지 열성이 없던 알레프 왕의 부하들은 달아났고, 덴마크인들이 그 들판의 주인으로 남았다."]], [["Sigtryg had in the meantime seen to the safety of the princess, and now, placing her between himself and Hereward, he escorted her to the ship, which soon brought them to Waterford and a happy bridal.", "그러는 사이 시그트뤼그는 공주의 안전을 살펴 두었고, 이제 그녀를 자신과 헤리워드 사이에 세우고서 배까지 호위해 갔으니, 배는 곧 그들을 워터퍼드로, 그리고 행복한 혼례로 데려다주었다."], ["The Prince and Princess of Waterford always recognized in Hereward their deliverer and best friend, and in their gratitude wished him to dwell with them always; but the hero’s roving and daring temper forbade his settling down, but rather urged him on to deeds of arms in other lands, where he quickly won a renown second to none.", "워터퍼드의 왕자와 공주는 헤리워드를 자신들의 구원자이자 가장 좋은 벗으로 언제나 받들었고, 감사하는 마음으로 그가 늘 자신들과 함께 살기를 바랐다. 그러나 영웅의 떠돌며 모험하는 기질은 그가 정착하는 것을 허락하지 않았고, 오히려 다른 나라들에서 무용을 떨치도록 그를 내몰았으니, 그곳에서 그는 누구에게도 뒤지지 않는 명성을 금세 얻었다."]], [["————", "————"]], [["# ROBIN HOOD", "# ROBIN HOOD"]], [["AMONG the earliest heirlooms of the Anglo-Saxon tongue are the songs and legends of Robin Hood and his merry outlaws, which have charmed readers young and old for more than six hundred years.", "로빈 후드\n앵글로색슨어의 가장 오래된 유산 가운데에는 로빈 후드와 그의 유쾌한 무법자들에 관한 노래와 전설이 있으니, 그것은 육백 년이 넘도록 어린이와 어른 모두를 매료해 왔다."], ["These entertaining stories date back to the time when Chaucer wrote his “Canterbury Tales,” when the minstrel and scribe stood in the place of the more prim and precise modern printed book.", "이 흥겨운 이야기들은 초서가 \"캔터베리 이야기\"를 쓰던 시절, 더 격식 차린 정밀한 오늘날의 인쇄본 책 대신에 음유시인과 필경사가 자리하던 그 시절로 거슬러 올라간다."]], [["The question of whether or not Robin Hood was a real person has been asked for many years, just as a similar question has been asked about William Tell and others whom everyone would much rather accept on faith. It cannot be answered by a brief “yes”", "로빈 후드가 실존 인물이었는지 아닌지에 대한 물음은 여러 해 동안 제기되어 왔으니, 이는 윌리엄 텔을 비롯하여 누구나 차라리 믿음으로 받아들이고 싶어 하는 이들에 대해 비슷한 물음이 제기되어 온 것과 마찬가지다."], ["or “no,” even though learned men have pored over ancient records and have written books on the subject.", "그것은 박식한 이들이 옛 기록을 샅샅이 뒤지고 그 주제에 관해 책을 썼음에도, 짤막한 \"그렇다\"나 \"아니다\"로 답할 수 없는 물음이다."], ["According to the general belief Robin was an outlaw in the reign of Richard I, when in the depths of Sherwood Forest he entertained one hundred tall men, all good archers, with the spoil he took; but “he suffered no woman to be oppressed or otherwise molested; poore men’s goods he spared, abundantlie relieving them with that which by theft he got from abbeys and houses of rich earles.”", "일반적인 믿음에 따르면 로빈은 리처드 1세 치세의 무법자였으니, 그때 그는 셔우드 숲 깊은 곳에서 모두 뛰어난 궁수인 키 큰 사내 백 명을 자신이 빼앗은 노획물로 거두어 먹였다. 그러나 \"그는 어떤 여인도 핍박받거나 다른 식으로 괴롭힘당하도록 두지 않았으며, 가난한 자들의 재물은 아끼고, 수도원과 부유한 백작들의 저택에서 훔쳐 온 것으로 그들을 넉넉히 구제하였다.\""], ["Consequently Robin was an immense favorite with the common people.", "그리하여 로빈은 평민들에게 엄청난 인기를 누렸다."]], [["This popularity extended from the leader to all the members of his hardy band.", "이 인기는 우두머리에서부터 그의 강인한 무리의 모든 구성원에게로 미쳤다."], ["“God save Robin Hood and all his good yeomanry” is the ending of many old ballads.", "\"로빈 후드와 그의 모든 훌륭한 자유민에게 신의 가호가 있기를\"이라는 구절은 많은 옛 발라드의 맺음말이다."], ["The clever archer who could outshoot his fellows, the brave yeoman inured to blows, and the man who could be true to his friends through thick and thin were favorites for all time; and they have been idealized in the persons of Robin Hood and his merry outlaws.", "동료들보다 활을 더 잘 쏘는 영리한 궁수, 타격에 단련된 용감한 자유민, 그리고 어떤 어려움이 닥쳐도 벗에게 진실할 수 있는 사내는 모든 시대에 걸쳐 사랑받는 인물이었으니, 그들은 로빈 후드와 그의 유쾌한 무법자들의 모습으로 이상화되었다."]], [["One of the best-known stories of this picturesque figure of early English times is that given by Sir Walter Scott in “Ivanhoe,” concerning the archery contest during the rule or misrule of Prince John, in the absence of Richard from the kingdom.", "초기 영국 시대의 이 그림 같은 인물에 관해 가장 잘 알려진 이야기 가운데 하나는, 월터 스콧 경이 \"아이반호\"에서 들려준 것으로, 리처드가 왕국에 없는 사이 존 왕자의 통치, 아니 실정(失政) 동안 벌어진 활쏘기 시합에 관한 것이다."], ["Robin Hood, under the assumed name of Locksley, boldly presents himself at a royal tournament at Ashby, as competitor for the prize in shooting with the long-bow.", "로빈 후드는 록슬리라는 가명으로, 애시비에서 열린 왕실 무술 대회에 장궁 쏘기 경기의 상을 노리는 경쟁자로서 대담하게 모습을 드러낸다."], ["From the eight or ten archers who enter the contest, the number finally narrows down to two,—Hubert, a forester in the service of one of the king’s nobles, and Locksley or Robin Hood.", "경기에 참가한 여덟 명인가 열 명의 궁수 가운데, 그 수는 마침내 둘로 좁혀지는데, 바로 왕의 한 귀족을 섬기는 산지기 휴버트와, 록슬리, 곧 로빈 후드다."], ["Hubert takes the first shot in the final trial of skill, and lands his arrow within the inner ring of the target, but not exactly in the centre.", "휴버트가 마지막 솜씨 겨루기에서 먼저 쏘아, 화살을 과녁의 안쪽 고리 안에, 그러나 정확히 한가운데는 아닌 곳에 맞힌다."]], [["“‘You have not allowed for the wind, Hubert,’ said Locksley, ‘or that had been a better shot.’", "\"'바람을 고려하지 않았군, 휴버트,' 록슬리가 말했다. '그러지 않았다면 더 나은 사격이었을 텐데.'"]], [["“So saying, and without showing the least anxiety to pause upon his aim, Locksley stepped to the appointed station, and shot his arrow as carelessly in appearance as if he had not even looked at the mark.", "\"이렇게 말하고는, 조준에 뜸을 들이려는 기색은 조금도 보이지 않은 채, 록슬리는 정해진 자리로 걸어가, 과녁을 보지도 않은 듯 겉보기에는 무심하게 화살을 쏘았다."], ["He was speaking almost at the instant that the shaft left the bow-string, yet it alighted in the target two inches nearer to the white spot which marked the centre than that of Hubert.", "화살이 시위를 떠나는 바로 그 순간에도 그는 거의 말을 하고 있었으나, 그 화살은 휴버트의 화살보다 한가운데를 표시한 흰 점에 2인치 더 가까이 과녁에 꽂혔다."]], [["“‘By the light of Heaven!’ said Prince John to Hubert, ‘an thou suffer that runagate knave to overcome thee, thou art worthy of the gallows!’", "\"'하늘의 빛에 걸고!' 존 왕자가 휴버트에게 말했다. '네가 저 떠돌이 불한당 놈에게 진다면, 너는 교수대에 매달려 마땅하다!'"]], [["“Hubert had but one set speech for all occasions.", "\"휴버트에게는 어떤 경우에나 통하는 한 가지 정해진 대꾸가 있었다."], ["‘An your highness were to hang me,’ he said, ‘a man can but do his best.", "'전하께서 저를 매달려 하신다 해도,' 그가 말했다."], ["Nevertheless, my grandsire drew a good bow——’", "'사람이란 제 힘껏 할 수 있을 뿐입니다. 그렇지만 저의 할아버님께서는 좋은 활을 당기셨고——'"]], [["“‘The foul fiend on thy grandsire and all his generation!’ interrupted John; ‘shoot, knave, and shoot thy best, or it shall be worse for thee!’", "\"'네 할아비와 그 일족 모두에게 흉악한 마귀가 들러붙으라지!' 존이 말을 가로막았다. '쏘아라, 이놈, 네 힘껏 쏘아라, 그러지 않으면 네게 더 나쁜 일이 닥칠 것이다!'"]], [["“Thus exhorted, Hubert resumed his place, and not neglecting the caution which he had received from his adversary, he made the necessary allowance for a very light air of wind, which had just risen, and shot so successfully that his arrow alighted in the very centre of the target.", "\"이렇게 다그침을 받자, 휴버트는 제자리로 돌아가, 적수에게서 받은 충고를 소홀히 하지 않고, 막 일어난 아주 가벼운 바람을 알맞게 고려하여, 어찌나 성공적으로 쏘았던지 그 화살이 과녁의 바로 한가운데에 꽂혔다."]], [["“‘A Hubert!", "\"'휴버트 만세!"], ["a Hubert!’ shouted the populace, more interested in a known person than in a stranger.", "휴버트 만세!' 군중이 외쳤으니, 낯선 자보다 아는 사람에게 더 관심이 쏠린 까닭이었다."], ["‘In the clout!—in the clout!—a Hubert forever!’", "'과녁 한복판이다!——한복판이다!——휴버트 영원하라!'"]], [["“‘Thou canst not mend that shot, Locksley,’ said the Prince, with an insulting smile.", "\"'저 사격은 자네가 더 낫게 할 수 없을 걸세, 록슬리,' 왕자가 비웃는 미소를 띠고 말했다."]], [["“‘I will notch his shaft for him, however,’ replied Locksley.", "\"'그래도 저는 그의 화살에 금을 새겨 드리겠습니다,' 록슬리가 대답했다."]], [["“And letting fly his arrow with a little more precaution than before, it lighted right upon that of his competitor, which it split to shivers.", "\"그러고는 전보다 조금 더 조심하여 화살을 날리니, 그것은 적수의 화살 바로 위에 꽂혀, 그 화살을 산산조각으로 쪼개 버렸다."], ["The people who stood around were so astonished at his wonderful dexterity, that they could not even give vent to their surprise in their usual clamor.", "둘러서 있던 사람들은 그의 놀라운 솜씨에 어찌나 경악했던지, 평소의 함성으로조차 놀라움을 터뜨리지 못했다. '이자는 틀림없이 악마이지, 살과 피로 된 사람이 아니다,'"], ["‘This must be the devil, and no man of flesh and blood,’ whispered the yeomen to each other; ‘such archery was never seen since a bow was first bent in Britain.’", "자유민들이 서로에게 속삭였다. '이런 활 솜씨는 브리튼에서 처음으로 활시위가 당겨진 이래로 본 적이 없다.'"]], [["“‘And now,’ said Locksley, ‘I will crave your Grace’s permission to plant such a mark as is used in the North Country; and welcome every brave yeoman who shall try a shot at it to win a smile from the bonny lass he loves best.’”", "\"'그러면 이제,' 록슬리가 말했다. '북부 지방에서 쓰이는 그런 과녁을 세우도록 전하의 윤허를 청합니다. 그리고 가장 사랑하는 어여쁜 처녀의 미소를 얻고자 그것에 한 발을 쏘아 보려는 용감한 자유민이라면 누구든 환영하겠습니다.'\""]], [["Locksley thereupon sets up a willow wand, six feet long and as thick as a man’s thumb.", "이에 록슬리는 길이 6피트에 사람 엄지손가락만큼 굵은 버드나무 가지를 세운다."], ["Hubert is forced to decline the honor of taking part in such a trial of archery skill, but his rival easily splits the wand at a distance of three hundred feet and carries off the prize.", "휴버트는 그런 활 솜씨 겨루기에 참가하는 영예를 사양할 수밖에 없으나, 그의 적수는 300피트 거리에서 그 가지를 손쉽게 쪼개고 상을 차지한다."]], [["“Even Prince John, in admiration of Locksley’s skill, lost for an instant his dislike to his person. ‘These twenty nobles,’ he said, ‘which, with the bugle, thou hast fairly won, are thine own; we will make them fifty, if thou wilt take livery and service with us as a yeoman of our bodyguard, and be near to our person. For never did so strong a hand bend a bow, or so true an eye direct a shaft.’”[71]", "\"존 왕자조차도 록슬리의 솜씨에 감탄하여 한순간 그 인물에 대한 미움을 잊었다. '이 스무 닢의 금화는,' 그가 말했다. '뿔나팔과 더불어 네가 정정당당히 얻은 것이니, 네 것이다. 만일 네가 우리 호위대의 자유민으로서 제복을 받고 우리를 섬기며 우리 곁에 머문다면, 우리는 그것을 쉰 닢으로 만들어 주겠다. 일찍이 그토록 강한 손이 활을 당기거나, 그토록 정확한 눈이 화살을 겨눈 적이 없었으니 말이다.'\" * 71: (원주에 해당하는 출처 표시.)"]], [["Locksley, however, declares that it is impossible for him to enter the Prince’s service, generously shares his prize with the worthy Hubert, and retires once more to his beloved haunts among the lights and shadows of the good greenwood.", "그러나 록슬리는 왕자의 봉사에 들어가는 것이 자신에게는 불가능하다고 밝히고는, 자신의 상을 그 훌륭한 휴버트와 너그러이 나누고서, 좋은 푸른 숲의 빛과 그늘 사이에 있는 자신이 사랑하는 본거지로 다시 한번 물러난다. 샤를마뉴 전설"]], [["Those who have investigated the origin of the romantic fables relating to Charlemagne and his peers are of opinion that the deeds of Charles Martel, and perhaps of other Charleses, have been blended in popular tradition with those properly belonging to Charlemagne.", "서론\n샤를마뉴와 그의 동료들에 관한 낭만적인 옛이야기의 기원을 탐구한 이들은, 샤를 마르텔의, 어쩌면 다른 샤를들의 행적까지도, 본래 샤를마뉴에게 속하는 행적과 민간 전승 속에서 뒤섞여 버렸다는 견해를 갖고 있다."], ["It was indeed a most momentous era; and if our readers will have patience, before entering on the perusal of the fabulous annals which we are about to lay before them, to take a rapid survey of the real history of the times, they will find it hardly less romantic than the tales of the poets.", "그것은 참으로 더없이 중대한 시대였으니, 만일 우리 독자들이 이제 우리가 펼쳐 보일 그 허구의 연대기를 읽어 나가기에 앞서, 그 시대의 실제 역사를 잠시 빠르게 훑어볼 인내심을 가져 준다면, 그 역사가 시인들의 이야기 못지않게 낭만적임을 알게 될 것이다."]], [["In the century beginning from the year 600, the countries bordering upon the native land of our Saviour, to the east and south, had not yet received his religion.", "서기 600년부터 시작되는 그 세기에, 우리 구세주의 본향에 동쪽과 남쪽으로 접한 나라들은 아직 그분의 종교를 받아들이지 않은 상태였다."], ["Arabia was the seat of an idolatrous religion resembling that of the ancient Persians, who worshipped the sun, moon, and stars.", "아라비아는 태양과 달과 별을 숭배하던 고대 페르시아인의 종교와 비슷한 우상 숭배 종교의 본거지였다."], ["In Mecca, in the year 571, Mahomet was born, and here, at the age of forty, he proclaimed himself the prophet of God, in dignity as superior to Christ as Christ had been to Moses.", "571년 메카에서 마호메트가 태어났고, 마흔 살에 그는 이곳에서 스스로를 하느님의 예언자라 선포했으니, 그리스도가 모세보다 위에 있었던 만큼이나 자신이 그리스도보다 위에 있는 존엄한 자라 했다."], ["Having obtained by slow degrees a considerable number of disciples, he resorted to arms to diffuse his religion.", "더디게나마 상당수의 제자를 얻은 그는 자신의 종교를 퍼뜨리고자 무력에 호소했다."], ["The energy and zeal of his followers, aided by the weakness of the neighboring nations, enabled him and his successors to spread the sway of Arabia and the religion of Mahomet over the countries to the east as far as the Indus, northward over Persia and Asia Minor, westward over Egypt and the southern shores of the Mediterranean, and thence over the principal portion of Spain.", "그의 추종자들의 열정과 열의는 이웃 나라들의 약함에 힘입어, 그와 그의 후계자들이 아라비아의 지배와 마호메트의 종교를 동쪽으로 인더스 강에 이르기까지, 북쪽으로 페르시아와 소아시아에 걸쳐, 서쪽으로 이집트와 지중해 남쪽 연안에 걸쳐, 그리고 거기서 다시 에스파냐의 주요 지역에 걸쳐 펼치도록 해 주었다."], ["All this was done within one hundred years from the Hegira, or flight of Mahomet from Mecca to Medina, which happened in the year 622, and is the era from which Mahometans reckon time, as we do from the birth of Christ.", "이 모든 일이 헤지라, 곧 마호메트가 메카에서 메디나로 달아난 일로부터 백 년 안에 이루어졌으니, 그 일은 622년에 일어났으며, 우리가 그리스도의 탄생으로부터 햇수를 헤아리듯 마호메트교도들이 시간을 헤아리는 기점이다."]], [["From Spain the way was open for the Saracens (so the followers of Mahomet were called) into France, the conquest of which, if achieved, would have been followed very probably by that of all the rest of Europe, and would have resulted in the banishment of Christianity from the earth.", "에스파냐로부터 사라센인(마호메트의 추종자들은 이렇게 불렸다)이 프랑스로 들어가는 길이 열려 있었으니, 만일 그 정복이 이루어졌다면 십중팔구 그 뒤를 이어 유럽의 나머지 전역의 정복이 따랐을 것이고, 그 결과 그리스도교가 지상에서 추방되었을 것이다."], ["For Christianity was not at that day universally professed, even by those nations which we now regard as foremost in civilization.", "왜냐하면 그 시절에는 우리가 지금 문명에서 가장 앞섰다고 여기는 나라들조차도 그리스도교를 보편적으로 신봉하지는 않았기 때문이다."], ["Great parts of Germany, Britain, Denmark, and Russia were still pagan or barbarous.", "게르마니아와 브리튼, 덴마크, 러시아의 큰 부분은 여전히 이교를 믿거나 야만 상태에 있었다."]], [["At that time there ruled in France, though without the title of king, the first of those illustrious Charleses of whom we have spoken, Charles Martel, the grandfather of Charlemagne.", "그 무렵 프랑스에서는, 비록 왕의 칭호는 없었으나, 우리가 말한 저 이름난 샤를들 가운데 첫째인 샤를 마르텔, 곧 샤를마뉴의 할아버지가 다스리고 있었다."], ["The Saracens of Spain had made incursions into France in 712 and 718, and had retired, carrying with them a vast booty.", "에스파냐의 사라센인들은 712년과 718년에 프랑스로 침입했다가, 막대한 노획물을 가지고 물러갔다."], ["In 725, Anbessa, who was then the Saracen governor of Spain, crossed the Pyrenees with a numerous army, and took by storm the strong town of Carcassone.", "725년에는 당시 에스파냐의 사라센 총독이던 안베사가 대군을 이끌고 피레네 산맥을 넘어, 견고한 도시 카르카손을 급습하여 함락했다."], ["So great was the terror excited by this invasion, that the country for a wide extent submitted to the conqueror, and a Mahometan governor for the province was appointed and installed at Narbonne.", "이 침입이 불러일으킨 공포가 어찌나 컸던지, 넓은 지역에 걸쳐 나라가 정복자에게 굴복했고, 그 속주의 마호메트교도 총독이 임명되어 나르본에 자리를 잡았다."], ["Anbessa, however, received a fatal wound in one of his engagements, and the Saracens, being thus checked from further advance, retired to Narbonne.", "그러나 안베사는 한 전투에서 치명상을 입었고, 사라센인들은 이렇게 더 이상의 진격이 저지되어 나르본으로 물러갔다."]], [["In 732 the Saracens again invaded France under Abdalrahman, advanced rapidly to the banks of the Garonne, and laid siege to Bordeaux.", "732년 사라센인들은 압달라만의 지휘 아래 다시 프랑스를 침입하여, 가론 강 기슭으로 빠르게 진격하여 보르도를 포위했다."], ["The city was taken by assault and delivered up to the soldiery.", "도시는 강습으로 함락되어 병사들에게 내맡겨졌다."], ["The invaders still pressed forward, and spread over the territories of Orleans, Auxerre and Sens.", "침입자들은 여전히 앞으로 밀고 나아가, 오를레앙과 옥세르와 상스의 영지에 두루 퍼졌다."], ["Their advanced parties were suddenly called in by their chief, who had received information of the rich abbey of St. Martin of Tours, and resolved to plunder and destroy it.", "그들의 선발대는 우두머리에게서 갑자기 소환되었으니, 그는 투르의 성 마르탱의 부유한 수도원에 대한 정보를 입수하고는 그것을 약탈하고 파괴하기로 마음먹었던 것이다."]], [["Charles during all this time had done nothing to oppose the Saracens, for the reason that the portion of France over which their incursions had been made was not at that time under his dominion, but constituted an independent kingdom, under the name of Aquitaine, of which Eude was king.", "샤를은 이 모든 동안 사라센인들에게 맞서 아무것도 하지 않았으니, 그 까닭은 그들의 침입이 행해진 프랑스의 그 지역이 당시 그의 지배 아래 있지 않고, 아키텐이라는 이름의 독립 왕국을 이루고 있었으며, 그 왕은 외드였기 때문이다."], ["But now Charles became convinced of the danger, and prepared to encounter it.", "그러나 이제 샤를은 그 위험을 확신하게 되어 그것에 맞설 채비를 했다."], ["Abdalrahman was advancing toward Tours, when intelligence of the approach of Charles, at the head of an army of Franks, compelled him to fall back upon Poitiers, in order to seize an advantageous field of battle.", "압달라만이 투르를 향해 진격하고 있을 때, 프랑크인의 군대를 이끌고 다가오는 샤를의 소식에 그는 유리한 전장을 차지하고자 푸아티에로 물러설 수밖에 없었다."]], [["Charles Martel had called together his warriors from every part of his dominions, and, at the head of such an army as had hardly ever been seen in France, crossed the Loire, probably at Orleans, and, being joined by the remains of the army of Aquitaine, came in sight of the Arabs in the month of October, 732.", "샤를 마르텔은 자신의 영토 곳곳에서 전사들을 불러 모아, 일찍이 프랑스에서 보기 드물었던 그런 군대를 이끌고, 아마도 오를레앙에서 루아르 강을 건넜고, 아키텐 군대의 잔존 병력과 합류하여 732년 시월에 아랍인들을 시야에 두게 되었다."], ["The Saracens seem to have been aware of the terrible enemy they were now to encounter, and for the first time these formidable conquerors hesitated.", "사라센인들은 자신들이 이제 마주하게 될 무서운 적을 알아챈 듯했으니, 이 가공할 정복자들이 처음으로 머뭇거렸다."], ["The two armies remained in presence during seven days before either ventured to begin the attack; but at length the signal for battle was given by Abdalrahman, and the immense mass of the Saracen army rushed with fury on the Franks.", "두 군대는 어느 쪽도 감히 공격을 시작하지 못한 채 이레 동안 대치한 상태로 있었다. 그러나 마침내 압달라만이 전투의 신호를 내렸고, 사라센 군대의 거대한 무리가 맹렬히 프랑크인들에게 돌진했다."], ["But the heavy line of the Northern warriors remained like a rock, and the Saracens, during nearly the whole day, expended their strength in vain attempts to make any impression upon them.", "그러나 북방 전사들의 두터운 전열은 바위처럼 버텼고, 사라센인들은 거의 하루 종일 그들에게 조금이라도 흠집을 내려는 헛된 시도에 힘을 소진했다."], ["At length, about four o’clock in the afternoon, when Abdalrahman was preparing for a new and desperate attempt to break the line of the Franks, a terrible clamor was heard in the rear of the Saracens.", "마침내 오후 네 시 무렵, 압달라만이 프랑크인의 전열을 깨뜨리기 위한 새롭고 필사적인 시도를 준비하고 있을 때, 사라센인들의 후방에서 무시무시한 함성이 들려왔다."], ["It was King Eude, who, with his Aquitanians, had attacked their camp, and a great part of the Saracen army rushed tumultuously from the field to protect their plunder.", "그것은 외드 왕이었으니, 그는 자신의 아키텐인들과 함께 그들의 진영을 공격했고, 사라센 군대의 큰 부분이 자신들의 노획물을 지키고자 전장에서 우르르 빠져나갔다."], ["In this moment of confusion the line of the Franks advanced, and, sweeping the field before it, carried fearful slaughter amongst the enemy.", "이 혼란의 순간에 프랑크인의 전열이 진격하여, 앞에 있던 전장을 휩쓸며 적들 사이에 끔찍한 살육을 가했다."], ["Abdalrahman made desperate efforts to rally his troops, but when he himself, with the bravest of his officers, fell beneath the swords of the Christians, all order disappeared, and the remains of his army sought refuge in their immense camp, from which Eude and his Aquitanians had been repulsed.", "압달라만은 자신의 병사들을 다시 모으려고 필사적으로 애썼으나, 그 자신이 가장 용감한 장교들과 함께 그리스도교도들의 칼 아래 쓰러지자, 모든 질서가 사라졌고, 그의 군대의 잔존 병력은 자신들의 거대한 진영으로 피신했으니, 그곳은 외드와 그의 아키텐인들이 격퇴당했던 곳이었다."], ["It was now late, and Charles, unwilling to risk an attack on the camp in the dark, withdrew his army, and passed the night in the plain, expecting to renew the battle in the morning.", "이제 날이 저물었기에, 샤를은 어둠 속에서 진영을 공격하는 위험을 무릅쓰고 싶지 않아 군대를 물려, 아침에 전투를 재개할 셈으로 평원에서 밤을 보냈다."]], [["Accordingly, when daylight came, the Franks drew up in order of battle, but no enemy appeared; and when at last they ventured to approach the Saracen camp they found it empty.", "그리하여 날이 밝자 프랑크인들은 전투 대형을 갖추었으나, 적은 나타나지 않았다. 마침내 그들이 큰맘 먹고 사라센 진영에 다가가 보니, 그곳은 텅 비어 있었다."], ["The invaders had taken advantage of the night to begin their retreat, and were already on their way back to Spain, leaving their immense plunder behind to fall into the hands of the Franks.", "침입자들은 밤을 틈타 퇴각을 시작했고, 이미 에스파냐로 돌아가는 길에 올라, 그들의 거대한 노획물을 뒤에 남겨 프랑크인들의 손에 떨어지게 한 것이었다."]], [["This was the celebrated battle of Tours, in which vast numbers of the Saracens were slain, and only fifteen hundred of the Franks.", "이것이 그 유명한 투르 전투로, 거기서 막대한 수의 사라센인이 죽임을 당했고, 프랑크인은 단 천오백 명만이 죽었다."], ["Charles received the surname of Martel (the Hammer) in consequence of this victory.", "샤를은 이 승리의 결과로 마르텔(망치)이라는 별명을 얻었다."]], [["The Saracens, notwithstanding this severe blow, continued to hold their ground in the south of France; but Pepin, the son of Charles Martel, who succeeded to his father’s power, and assumed the title of king, successively took from them the strong places they held; and in 759, by the capture of Narbonne, their capital, extinguished the remains of their power in France.", "사라센인들은 이 모진 타격에도 불구하고 프랑스 남부에서 자신들의 입지를 계속 지켰다. 그러나 샤를 마르텔의 아들로서 아버지의 권력을 이어받아 왕의 칭호를 취한 피핀은 그들이 차지하고 있던 견고한 요새들을 차례로 빼앗았고, 759년에는 그들의 거점인 나르본을 함락함으로써 프랑스에서 그들 세력의 잔재를 소멸시켰다."]], [["Charlemagne, or Charles the Great, succeeded his father, Pepin, on the throne in the year 768.", "샤를마뉴, 곧 샤를 대제는 768년에 아버지 피핀의 뒤를 이어 왕위에 올랐다."], ["This prince, though the hero of numerous romantic legends, appears greater in history than in fiction.", "이 군주는 수많은 낭만적 전설의 주인공이기는 하나, 허구에서보다 역사에서 더 위대하게 나타난다."], ["Whether we regard him as a warrior or as a legislator, as a patron of learning or as the civilizer of a barbarous nation, he is entitled to our warmest admiration.", "그를 전사로 보든 입법자로 보든, 학문의 후원자로 보든 야만 민족의 교화자로 보든, 그는 우리의 가장 뜨거운 찬탄을 받을 자격이 있다."], ["Such he is in history; but the romancers represent him as often weak and passionate, the victim of treacherous counsellors, and at the mercy of turbulent barons, on whose prowess he depends for the maintenance of his throne.", "역사 속의 그는 그러하다. 그러나 옛이야기 작가들은 그를 흔히 나약하고 격정적이며, 음흉한 자문관들의 희생자요, 자신의 왕위 유지를 그 용맹에 기대고 있는 사나운 영주들에게 휘둘리는 인물로 그린다."], ["The historical representation is doubtless the true one, for it is handed down in trustworthy records, and is confirmed by the events of the age.", "역사적 묘사가 의심할 바 없이 참된 것이니, 그것은 믿을 만한 기록으로 전해 내려오고, 그 시대의 사건들로 확증되기 때문이다."], ["At the height of his power, the French empire extended over what we now call France, Germany, Switzerland, Holland, Belgium, and great part of Italy.", "그의 권세가 절정에 이르렀을 때, 프랑스 제국은 우리가 지금 프랑스, 게르마니아, 스위스, 네덜란드, 벨기에라 부르는 곳과 이탈리아의 큰 부분에 걸쳐 펼쳐져 있었다."]], [["In the year 800 Charlemagne, being in Rome, whither he had gone with a numerous army to protect the Pope, was crowned by the Pontiff Emperor of the West.", "800년에 샤를마뉴는 교황을 보호하고자 대군을 이끌고 갔던 로마에 머물던 중, 교황에 의해 서방의 황제로 대관되었다."], ["On Christmas day Charles entered the Church of St. Peter, as if merely to take his part in the celebration of the mass with the rest of the congregation.", "성탄절 날 샤를은, 마치 나머지 회중과 더불어 미사 거행에 참여하려는 것일 뿐인 양 성 베드로 성당에 들어섰다."], ["When he approached the altar and stooped in the act of prayer the Pope stepped forward and placed a crown of gold upon his head; and immediately the Roman people shouted, “Life and victory to Charles the August, crowned by God the great and pacific Emperor of the Romans.”", "그가 제단에 다가가 기도하려고 몸을 굽히자, 교황이 앞으로 나아가 그의 머리에 금관을 씌웠다. 그러자 곧바로 로마 사람들이 외쳤다."], ["The Pope then prostrated himself before him, and paid him reverence, according to the custom established in the times of the ancient Emperors, and concluded the ceremony by anointing him with consecrated oil.", "\"하느님께서 대관하신 위대하고 평화로운 로마인의 황제, 존엄한 샤를께 생명과 승리가 있기를.\" 그러고 나서 교황은 그 앞에 엎드려, 고대 황제들의 시대에 확립된 관습에 따라 그에게 경의를 표했고, 그에게 성유를 발라 의식을 마무리했다."]], [["Charlemagne’s wars were chiefly against the pagan and barbarous people, who, under the name of Saxons, inhabited the countries now called Hanover and Holland.", "샤를마뉴의 전쟁은 주로 색슨족이라는 이름으로 지금 하노버와 네덜란드라 불리는 나라들에 살던 이교도 야만 민족을 상대로 한 것이었다."], ["He also led expeditions against the Saracens of Spain; but his wars with the Saracens were not carried on, as the romances assert, in France, but on the soil of Spain.", "그는 또한 에스파냐의 사라센인들을 상대로 원정을 이끌었으나, 사라센인들과의 전쟁은 옛이야기들이 주장하는 것처럼 프랑스에서가 아니라 에스파냐 땅에서 치러졌다."], ["He entered Spain by the Eastern Pyrenees, and made an easy conquest of Barcelona and Pampeluna.", "그는 동부 피레네 산맥으로 에스파냐에 들어가, 바르셀로나와 팜플로나를 손쉽게 정복했다."], ["But Saragossa refused to open her gates to him, and Charles ended by negotiating and accepting a vast sum of gold as the price of his return over the Pyrenees.", "그러나 사라고사가 그에게 성문 열기를 거부하자, 샤를은 협상을 벌여 피레네 산맥을 다시 넘어가는 대가로 막대한 금을 받아들이는 것으로 끝을 맺었다."]], [["On his way back, he marched with his whole army through the gorges of the mountains by way of the valleys of Engui, Eno, and Roncesvalles.", "돌아오는 길에 그는 온 군대를 이끌고 엥기와 에노와 롱스보의 골짜기를 따라 산악의 협곡들을 지나 행군했다."], ["The chief of this region had waited upon Charlemagne, on his advance, as a faithful vassal of the monarchy; but now, on the return of the Franks, he had called together all the wild mountaineers who acknowledged him as their chief, and they occupied the heights of the mountains under which the army had to pass.", "이 지역의 우두머리는 그가 진격할 때에는 군주국의 충실한 봉신으로서 샤를마뉴를 받들었으나, 이제 프랑크인들이 돌아오자, 그는 자신을 우두머리로 인정하는 거친 산사람들을 모두 불러 모았고, 그들은 군대가 그 아래로 지나가야 하는 산악의 고지를 점령했다."], ["The main body of the troops met with no obstruction, and received no intimation of danger; but the rear-guard, which was considerably behind, and encumbered with its plunder, was overwhelmed by the mountaineers in the pass of Roncesvalles, and slain to a man.", "군대의 본대는 아무런 방해도 만나지 않았고 위험의 기미도 받지 못했다. 그러나 상당히 뒤처진 데다 노획물에 발이 묶인 후위대는 롱스보의 고개에서 산사람들에게 압도당해 한 사람도 남김없이 죽임을 당했다."], ["Some of the bravest of the Frankish chiefs perished on this occasion, among whom is mentioned Roland or Orlando, governor of the marches or frontier of Brittany.", "이때 프랑크인 우두머리들 가운데 가장 용감한 몇몇이 목숨을 잃었는데, 그 가운데 브르타뉴 변경, 곧 국경 지대의 영주 롤랑, 곧 오를란도가 언급된다."], ["His name became famous in after times, and the disaster of Roncesvalles and death of Roland became eventually the most celebrated episode in the vast cycle of romance.", "그의 이름은 후세에 유명해졌고, 롱스보의 참사와 롤랑의 죽음은 끝내 그 방대한 옛이야기의 순환에서 가장 이름난 일화가 되었다."]], [["Though after this there were hostile encounters between the armies of Charlemagne and the Saracens, they were of small account, and generally on the soil of Spain.", "이 일 이후로도 샤를마뉴의 군대와 사라센인들 사이에 적대적인 충돌이 있기는 했으나, 그것들은 사소한 것이었고, 대개는 에스파냐 땅에서였다."], ["Thus the historical foundation for the stories of the romancers is but scanty, unless we suppose the events of an earlier and of a later age to be incorporated with those of Charlemagne’s own time.", "그리하여 옛이야기 작가들의 이야기에 대한 역사적 근거는, 만일 우리가 더 이른 시대와 더 늦은 시대의 사건들이 샤를마뉴 자신의 시대의 사건들과 한데 합쳐졌다고 가정하지 않는 한, 빈약할 따름이다."]], [["There is, however, a pretended history, which for a long time was admitted as authentic, and attributed to Turpin, Archbishop of Rheims, a real personage of the time of Charlemagne.", "그러나 오랫동안 진본으로 인정받았으며, 샤를마뉴 시대의 실존 인물인 랭스 대주교 튀르팽의 저작으로 여겨진, 사칭된 역사서가 하나 있다."], ["Its title is “History of Charles the Great and Orlando.”", "그 제목은 \"샤를 대제와 오를란도의 역사\"다."], ["It is now unhesitatingly considered as a collection of popular traditions, produced by some credulous and unscrupulous monk, who thought to give dignity to his romance by ascribing its authorship to a well-known and eminent individual.", "그것은 이제 의심의 여지 없이, 어떤 잘 속고 양심 없는 수도사가, 잘 알려지고 저명한 인물에게 그 저작을 돌림으로써 자신의 옛이야기에 권위를 부여하려는 생각에서 만들어 낸 민간 전승 모음집으로 여겨진다."], ["It introduces its pretended author, Bishop Turpin, in this manner:", "그것은 그 사칭된 저자 튀르팽 주교를 이런 식으로 소개한다."]], [["“Turpin, Archbishop of Rheims, the friend and secretary of Charles the Great, excellently skilled in sacred and profane literature, of a genius equally adapted to prose and verse, the advocate of the poor, beloved of God in his life and conversation, who often fought the Saracens, hand to hand, by the Emperor’s side, he relates the acts of Charles the Great in one book, and flourished under Charles and his son Louis, to the year of our Lord eight hundred and thirty.”", "\"랭스 대주교 튀르팽은 샤를 대제의 벗이자 비서로서, 성속의 문학에 두루 빼어나게 통달했고, 산문과 운문에 똑같이 어울리는 재능을 지녔으며, 가난한 자들의 옹호자요, 그 삶과 언행에서 하느님의 사랑을 받았으며, 황제 곁에서 사라센인들과 백병전으로 자주 싸웠으니, 그가 샤를 대제의 행적을 한 권의 책에 기록하며, 샤를과 그의 아들 루이 치하에서 우리 주의 해 830년까지 활약했다.\""]], [["The titles of some of Archbishop Turpin’s chapters will show the nature of his history.", "튀르팽 대주교의 몇몇 장의 제목들은 그 역사서의 성격을 보여 줄 것이다."], ["They are these: “Of the Walls of Pampeluna, that fell of themselves.”", "그것들은 이러하다. \"저절로 무너진 팜플로나의 성벽에 관하여.\""], ["“Of the War of the holy Facundus, where the Spears grew.”", "\"창들이 자라난 성 파쿤두스의 전쟁에 관하여.\""], ["(Certain of the Christians fixed their spears in the evening, erect in the ground, before the castle; and found them, in the morning, covered with bark and branches.) “How the Sun stood still for Three Days, and of the Slaughter of Four Thousand Saracens.”", "(어떤 그리스도교도들이 저녁에 성 앞 땅바닥에 창들을 똑바로 꽂아 두었는데, 아침에 보니 그것들이 나무껍질과 가지로 덮여 있더라는 것이다.) \"어떻게 태양이 사흘 동안 멈추어 섰는가, 그리고 사천 명의 사라센인의 살육에 관하여.\""]], [["Turpin’s history has perhaps been the source of the marvellous adventures which succeeding poets and romancers have accumulated around the names of Charlemagne and his Paladins, or Peers.", "튀르팽의 역사서는 어쩌면 후대의 시인과 옛이야기 작가들이 샤를마뉴와 그의 팔라딘, 곧 동료들의 이름 둘레에 쌓아 올린 경이로운 모험들의 원천이었는지도 모른다."], ["But Ariosto and the other Italian poets have drawn from different sources, and doubtless often from their own invention, numberless other stories which they attribute to the same heroes, not hesitating to quote as their authority “the good Turpin,”", "그러나 아리오스토와 다른 이탈리아 시인들은 서로 다른 원천에서, 그리고 의심할 바 없이 흔히 자신들의 창작에서, 같은 영웅들에게 돌리는 수많은 다른 이야기를 길어 올렸으니, 그러면서 비록 그의 역사서에 그 흔적이 전혀 없는데도 자신들의 전거로 \"선량한 튀르팽\"을 인용하기를 주저하지 않았다."], ["though his history contains no trace of them; and the more outrageous the improbability, or rather the impossibility, of their narrations, the more attentive are they to cite “the Archbishop,” generally adding their testimonial to his unquestionable veracity.", "그리고 그들 서술의 개연성 없음이, 아니 차라리 불가능함이 터무니없을수록, 그들은 더욱 정성스레 \"대주교\"를 인용하며, 대개는 그의 의심할 바 없는 진실성에 대한 자신들의 증언을 덧붙였다."]], [["The principal Italian poets who have sung the adventures of the peers of Charlemagne are Pulci, Boiardo, and Ariosto.", "샤를마뉴 동료들의 모험을 노래한 주요 이탈리아 시인들은 풀치, 보이아르도, 아리오스토다."], ["The characters of Orlando, Rinaldo, Astolpho, Gano, and others, are the same in all, though the adventures attributed to them are different.", "오를란도, 리날도, 아스톨포, 가노, 그리고 다른 이들의 성격은 모든 작품에서 똑같으나, 그들에게 돌려진 모험은 서로 다르다."], ["Boiardo tells us of the loves of Orlando, Ariosto of his disappointment and consequent madness, Pulci of his death.", "보이아르도는 우리에게 오를란도의 사랑을 들려주고, 아리오스토는 그의 좌절과 그로 인한 광기를, 풀치는 그의 죽음을 들려준다."]], [["Ogier, the Dane, is a real personage.", "덴마크인 오지에는 실존 인물이다."], ["History agrees with romance in representing him as a powerful lord who, originally from Denmark and a Pagan, embraced Christianity, and took service under Charlemagne.", "역사는 그를 본래 덴마크 출신의 이교도이던 막강한 영주로, 그리스도교를 받아들이고 샤를마뉴 휘하에서 봉직했다고 묘사하는 점에서 옛이야기와 일치한다."], ["He revolted from the Emperor, and was driven into exile.", "그는 황제에게 반기를 들었다가 추방되었다."], ["He afterwards led one of those bands of piratical Northmen which ravaged France under the reigns of Charlemagne’s degenerate successors.", "그는 훗날 샤를마뉴의 변변치 못한 후계자들의 치세에 프랑스를 약탈하던 노르만족 해적 무리 가운데 하나를 이끌었다."], ["The description which an ancient chronicler gives of Charlemagne, as described by Ogier, is so picturesque, that we are tempted to transcribe it.", "한 옛 연대기 작가가 오지에의 입을 빌려 묘사한 샤를마뉴의 모습은 어찌나 생생한지, 우리는 그것을 옮겨 적고 싶은 마음이 든다."], ["Charlemagne was advancing to the siege of Pavia.", "샤를마뉴는 파비아 공략을 향해 진격하고 있었다."], ["Didier, King of the Lombards, was in the city with Ogier, to whom he had given refuge.", "롬바르드인의 왕 디디에가 자신이 피난처를 내주었던 오지에와 함께 그 도시 안에 있었다."], ["When they learned that the king was approaching they mounted a high tower, whence they could see far and wide over the country.", "왕이 다가오고 있다는 것을 알게 되자 그들은 높은 탑에 올라, 거기서 들판을 멀리, 넓게 바라볼 수 있었다."], ["“They first saw advancing the engines of war, fit for the armies of Darius or Julius Cæsar.", "\"그들은 먼저 다리우스나 율리우스 카이사르의 군대에나 어울릴 만한 공성 무기들이 다가오는 것을 보았다. '저기 샤를마뉴가 있다,'"], ["‘There is Charlemagne,’ said Didier.", "디디에가 말했다. '아니오,'"], ["‘No,’ said Ogier.", "오지에가 말했다."], ["The Lombard next saw a vast body of soldiers, who filled all the plain.", "다음으로 롬바르드인은 들판을 온통 메운 방대한 군사들을 보았다."], ["‘Certainly Charles advanced with that host,’ said the king.", "'저 무리와 함께 샤를이 진격해 오는 것이 분명하다,' 왕이 말했다."], ["‘Not yet,’ replied Ogier.", "'아직 아니오,' 오지에가 대답했다."], ["‘What hope for us,’ resumed the king, ‘if he brings with him a greater host than that?’ At last Charles appeared, his head covered with an iron helmet, his hands with iron gloves, his breast and shoulders with a cuirass of iron, his left hand holding an iron lance, while his right hand grasped his sword.", "'우리에게 무슨 희망이 있겠는가,' 왕이 다시 말했다."], ["Those who went before the monarch, those who marched at his side, and those who followed him, all had similar arms.", "'만일 그가 저보다 더 큰 무리를 거느리고 온다면?' 마침내 샤를이 나타났으니, 그의 머리는 철 투구로, 두 손은 철 장갑으로, 가슴과 어깨는 철 흉갑으로 덮여 있었고, 왼손은 철 창을 쥐고, 오른손은 검을 거머쥐고 있었다."], ["Iron covered the fields and the roads; iron points reflected the rays of the sun.", "군주 앞에서 가던 자들도, 그의 곁에서 행군하던 자들도, 그를 뒤따르던 자들도, 모두 비슷한 무장을 하고 있었다. 철이 들판과 길을 뒤덮었고, 철의 끝이 햇빛을 반사했다."], ["This iron, so hard, was borne by a people whose hearts were harder still.", "그토록 단단한 이 철을, 마음은 그보다 더 단단한 백성이 지니고 있었다."], ["The blaze of the weapons flashed terror into the streets of the city.”", "무기의 번쩍임이 도시의 거리에 공포를 쏟아부었다.\""]], [["This picture of Charlemagne in his military aspect would be incomplete without a corresponding one of his “mood of peace.”", "군사적 모습을 한 샤를마뉴의 이 그림은, 그에 상응하는 그의 \"평화의 면모\"의 그림이 없다면 미완성으로 남을 것이다."], ["One of the greatest of modern historians, M. Guizot, has compared the glory of Charlemagne to a brilliant meteor, rising suddenly out of the darkness of barbarism to disappear no less suddenly in the darkness of feudalism.", "근대의 가장 위대한 역사가 가운데 한 사람인 기조 씨는 샤를마뉴의 영광을, 야만의 어둠에서 홀연히 떠올라 못지않게 홀연히 봉건제의 어둠 속으로 사라진 찬란한 유성에 비유했다."], ["But the light of this meteor was not extinguished, and reviving civilization owed much that was permanently beneficial to the great Emperor of the Franks.", "그러나 이 유성의 빛은 꺼지지 않았으니, 되살아나던 문명은 프랑크인의 위대한 황제에게 길이 이로운 많은 것을 빚졌다."], ["His ruling hand is seen in the legislation of his time, as well as in the administration of the laws.", "그의 다스리는 손길은 그 시대의 입법에서도, 법의 집행에서도 엿보인다."], ["He encouraged learning; he upheld the clergy, who were the only peaceful and intellectual class, against the encroaching and turbulent barons; he was an affectionate father, and watched carefully over the education of his children, both sons and daughters.", "그는 학문을 장려했고, 잠식해 오는 사나운 영주들에 맞서 유일하게 평화롭고 지적인 계층이던 성직자들을 옹호했으며, 자애로운 아버지로서 아들이든 딸이든 자녀들의 교육을 정성껏 보살폈다."], ["Of his encouragement of learning we will give some particulars.", "그의 학문 장려에 관해 우리는 몇 가지 자세한 것을 들려주고자 한다."]], [["He caused learned men to be brought from Italy and from other foreign countries to revive the public schools of France, which had been", "그는 학식 있는 이들을 이탈리아와 다른 외국에서 데려오게 하여, 그동안"]], [["prostrated by the disorders of preceding times.", "앞선 시대의 혼란으로 무너진 것들을 회복하고자 했다."], ["He recompensed these learned men liberally, and kept some of them near himself, honoring them with his friendship.", "그는 이런 학자들에게 후하게 보답했고, 그 가운데 몇 사람을 가까이 두면서 우정을 베풀어 예우했다."], ["Of these the most celebrated is Alcuin, an Englishman, whose writings still remain, and prove him to have been both a learned and a wise man.", "그중 가장 유명한 인물은 잉글랜드 사람 알퀴누스로, 그가 남긴 저술들은 지금까지 전해지면서 그가 박식하고 지혜로운 사람이었음을 입증하고 있다."], ["With the assistance of Alcuin, and others like him, he founded an academy or royal school, which should have the direction of the studies of all the schools of the kingdom.", "그는 알퀴누스와 그와 비슷한 다른 사람들의 도움을 받아 학술원, 곧 왕립 학교를 설립했는데, 이 기관은 왕국의 모든 학교의 학업을 지도하는 역할을 맡았다."], ["Charlemagne himself was a member of this academy on equal terms with the rest.", "카롤루스 자신도 다른 회원들과 동등한 자격으로 이 학술원의 일원이었다."], ["He attended its meetings, and fulfilled all the duties of an academician.", "그는 회합에 참석했고, 학술원 회원으로서의 모든 의무를 다했다."], ["Each member took the name of some famous man of antiquity.", "회원마다 고대의 어느 유명한 인물의 이름을 취했다."], ["Alcuin called himself Horace, another took the name of Augustin, a third of Pindar.", "알퀴누스는 자신을 호라티우스라 불렀고, 또 다른 이는 아우구스티누스의 이름을, 세 번째 사람은 핀다로스의 이름을 취했다."], ["Charlemagne, who knew the Psalms by heart, and who had an ambition to be, according to his conception, a king after God’s own heart, received from his brother academicians the name of David.", "시편을 외우고 있었으며 자신의 생각대로 〈하느님의 마음에 합당한 왕〉이 되기를 열망했던 카롤루스는, 학술원 동료들로부터 다윗이라는 이름을 받았다."]], [["Of the respect entertained for him by foreign nations an interesting proof is afforded in the embassy sent to him by the Caliph of the Arabians, the celebrated Haroun al Raschid, a prince in character and conduct not unlike to Charlemagne.", "외국에서 그를 향해 품었던 존경에 관한 흥미로운 증거는, 아라비아인의 칼리프, 곧 그 유명한 하룬 알 라시드가 그에게 보낸 사절단에서 확인할 수 있다. 이 군주는 성품과 행실에서 카롤루스와 적지 않게 닮은 인물이었다."], ["The ambassadors brought with them, besides other rich presents, a clock, the first that was seen in Europe, which excited universal admiration.", "사절들은 다른 값진 선물들과 더불어 시계 하나를 가져왔는데, 이는 유럽에서 처음 보는 것이어서 모두의 찬탄을 자아냈다."], ["It had the form of a twelve-sided edifice with twelve doors.", "그것은 열두 개의 문이 달린 십이면체 건축물의 형상을 하고 있었다."], ["These doors formed niches, in each of which was a little statue representing one of the hours.", "이 문들은 벽감을 이루고 있었으며, 각각의 벽감 안에는 시간을 나타내는 작은 조각상이 하나씩 들어 있었다."], ["At the striking of the hour the doors, one for each stroke, was seen to open, and from the doors to issue as many of the little statues, which, following one another, marched gravely round the tower.", "정시가 되어 종이 울릴 때면, 종이 한 번 칠 때마다 문이 하나씩 열리는 것이 보였고, 그 문에서 친 횟수만큼의 작은 조각상들이 나와서 줄지어 뒤따르며 탑 둘레를 엄숙하게 행진했다."], ["The motion of the clock was caused by water, and the striking was effected by balls of brass equal to the number of the hours, which fell upon a cymbal of the same metal, the number falling being determined by the discharge of the water, which, as it sunk in the vessel, allowed their escape.", "시계의 움직임은 물에 의해 이루어졌고, 종을 치는 일은 시간의 수와 같은 수의 놋쇠 공이 같은 금속으로 된 심벌즈 위로 떨어지면서 이루어졌는데, 떨어지는 공의 수는 물의 배출에 따라 정해졌다. 물이 그릇 안에서 내려가면서 공이 빠져나갈 수 있게 했던 것이다."]], [["Charlemagne was succeeded by his son Louis, a well-intentioned but feeble prince, in whose reign the fabric reared by Charles began rapidly to crumble.", "카롤루스의 뒤를 이은 사람은 그의 아들 루도비쿠스로, 선의를 품었으나 유약한 군주였으며, 그의 치세에 카롤루스가 세운 구조물은 빠르게 허물어지기 시작했다."], ["Louis was followed successively by two Charleses, incapable princes, whose weak and often tyrannical conduct is no doubt the source of incidents of that character ascribed in the romances to Charlemagne.", "루도비쿠스의 뒤는 차례로 두 명의 카롤루스가 이었는데, 무능한 군주들이었다. 그들의 유약하고 흔히 폭압적이었던 행실이야말로, 의심할 바 없이 로맨스에서 카롤루스에게 돌려진 그런 성격의 사건들의 근원이다."], ["The lawless and disobedient deportment of Charles’s paladins, instances of which are so frequent in the romantic legends, was also a trait of the declining empire, but not of that of Charlemagne.", "로맨스의 전설에 그토록 자주 등장하는, 카롤루스의 팔라딘들이 보이는 무법적이고 불복종하는 처신 또한 쇠퇴기 제국의 한 특징이었지, 카롤루스 시대의 특징은 아니었다."]], [["————", "————"]], [["# THE PEERS, OR PALADINS", "# THE PEERS, OR PALADINS"]], [["The twelve most illustrious knights of Charlemagne were called Peers, for the equality that reigned among them; while the name of Paladins, also conferred on them, implies that they were inmates of the palace and companions of the king.", "동료 기사들, 곧 팔라딘들\n카롤루스의 가장 명망 높은 열두 기사는 〈동료들[페어]〉이라 불렸는데, 이는 그들 사이에 지배하던 평등 때문이었다. 한편 그들에게 함께 부여된 〈팔라딘〉이라는 이름은, 그들이 궁전의 거주자이자 왕의 동반자임을 뜻한다."], ["Their names are always given alike by the romancers, yet we may enumerate the most distinguished of them as follows: Orlando or Roland (the former the Italian, the latter the French form of the name), favorite nephew of Charlemagne; Rinaldo of Montalban, cousin of Orlando; Namo, Duke of Bavaria; Salomon, king of Brittany; Turpin, the Archbishop; Astolpho, of England; Ogier, the Dane; Malagigi, the Enchanter; and Florismart, the friend of Orlando.", "로맨스 작가들은 늘 그들의 이름을 똑같이 들곤 하지만, 그중 가장 두드러진 이들을 다음과 같이 꼽을 수 있다. 카롤루스가 아끼는 조카 오를란도, 곧 롤랑(앞은 이탈리아식, 뒤는 프랑스식 이름이다), 오를란도의 사촌인 몬탈반의 리날도, 바이에른 공작 나모, 브르타뉴 왕 살로몬, 대주교 튀르팽, 잉글랜드의 아스톨포, 덴마크인 오지에, 마법사 말라지지, 그리고 오를란도의 벗 플로리스마르트 등이다."], ["There were others who are sometimes named as paladins, and the number cannot be strictly limited to twelve.", "때때로 팔라딘으로 거명되는 다른 이들도 있어서, 그 수를 엄격히 열둘로 한정할 수는 없다."], ["Charlemagne himself must be counted one, and Ganelon, or Gano, of Mayence, the treacherous enemy of all the rest, was rated high on the list by his deluded sovereign, who was completely the victim of his arts.", "카롤루스 자신도 그중 하나로 헤아려야 하며, 마옌스의 가넬론, 곧 가노는 나머지 모두의 배신자이자 적이었음에도, 그의 술수에 완전히 농락당한 채 그를 신임한 군주에 의해 명단의 높은 자리에 올랐다."]], [["We shall introduce more particularly to our readers a few of the principal peers, leaving the others to make their own introduction as they appear in the course of our narrative.", "우리는 주요 동료 기사들 가운데 몇몇을 독자들에게 더 자세히 소개하고, 나머지는 이야기가 진행되는 가운데 등장할 때 스스로 소개하도록 남겨두려 한다. 오를란도부터 시작하기로 한다."], ["We begin with Orlando.", "오를란도"]], [["Milon, or Milone, a knight of great family, and distantly related to Charlemagne, having secretly married Bertha, the Emperor’s sister, was banished from France, and excommunicated by the Pope.", "밀롱, 곧 밀로네는 대단한 가문의 기사로 카롤루스와 먼 친척이었는데, 황제의 누이 베르타와 몰래 혼인한 까닭에 프랑스에서 추방되고 교황으로부터 파문당했다."], ["After a long and miserable wandering on foot as mendicants Milon and his wife arrived at Sutri, in Italy, where they took refuge in a cave, and in that cave Orlando was born.", "밀롱과 그의 아내는 걸인의 처지로 오랫동안 비참하게 도보로 떠돈 끝에 이탈리아의 수트리에 이르렀고, 그곳의 한 동굴로 피신했는데, 바로 그 동굴에서 오를란도가 태어났다."], ["There his mother continued, deriving a scanty support from the compassion of the neighboring peasants; while Milon, in quest of honor and fortune, went into foreign lands.", "그의 어머니는 그곳에 계속 머무르며 이웃 농민들의 동정에서 얻는 변변찮은 도움으로 연명했고, 밀롱은 명예와 재물을 좇아 이국 땅으로 떠났다."], ["Orlando grew up among the children of the peasantry, surpassing them all in strength and manly graces.", "오를란도는 농민의 아이들 틈에서 자라났으며, 힘과 사내다운 기품에서 그들 모두를 능가했다."], ["Among his companions in age, though in station far more elevated, was Oliver, son of the governor of the town.", "비슷한 또래의 동무들 가운데에는, 신분은 훨씬 높았으나, 그 고을 통치자의 아들 올리버가 있었다."], ["Between the two boys a feud arose that led to a fight, in which Orlando thrashed his rival; but this did not prevent a friendship springing up between the two, which lasted through life.", "두 소년 사이에 불화가 일어 싸움으로 번졌고, 오를란도가 그 경쟁자를 흠씬 두들겼다. 하지만 이 일이 두 사람 사이에 우정이 싹트는 것을 막지는 못했으니, 그 우정은 평생토록 이어졌다."]], [["Orlando was so poor that he was sometimes half naked.", "오를란도는 너무도 가난하여 때로는 반쯤 헐벗고 지냈다."], ["As he was a favorite of the boys, one day four of them brought some cloth to make him clothes.", "그가 소년들의 사랑을 받았던 까닭에, 어느 날 그중 넷이서 그에게 옷을 지어 주려고 천을 가져왔다."], ["Two brought white and two red; and from this circumstance Orlando took his coat-of-arms, or quarterings.", "둘은 흰 천을, 둘은 붉은 천을 가져왔는데, 바로 이 사정에서 오를란도는 자신의 문장, 곧 〈사분 문장〉을 취하게 되었다."]], [["When Charlemagne was on his way to Rome to receive the imperial crown he dined in public in Sutri.", "카롤루스가 제관을 받으러 로마로 가는 길에 수트리에서 공개적으로 식사를 하던 때였다."], ["Orlando and his mother that day had nothing to eat, and Orlando coming suddenly upon the royal party, and seeing abundance of provisions, seized from the attendants as much as he could carry off, and made good his retreat in spite of their resistance.", "그날 오를란도와 그의 어머니는 먹을 것이 하나도 없었는데, 오를란도가 불쑥 왕의 일행과 마주쳤다가 풍성한 식량을 보고는, 시종들에게서 들고 갈 수 있는 만큼 잔뜩 빼앗아, 그들의 저항에도 아랑곳없이 무사히 빠져나왔다."], ["The Emperor, being told of this incident, was reminded of an intimation he had received in a dream, and ordered the boy to be followed.", "이 사건에 관해 들은 황제는 꿈에서 받은 어떤 계시가 떠올라, 그 소년을 뒤따르게 했다."], ["This was done by three of the knights, whom Orlando would have encountered with a cudgel on their entering the grotto, had not his mother restrained him.", "이 일은 세 기사가 맡아 했는데, 그들이 동굴 안으로 들어설 때 오를란도가 몽둥이를 들고 맞섰을 것이나, 그의 어머니가 그를 말렸다."], ["When they heard from her who she was they threw themselves at her feet, and promised to obtain her pardon from the Emperor.", "그녀가 누구인지 그녀로부터 듣게 되자, 그들은 그녀의 발치에 몸을 던지고 황제로부터 사면을 얻어 주겠노라 약속했다."], ["This was easily effected.", "이는 손쉽게 이루어졌다."], ["Orlando was received into favor by the Emperor, returned with him to France, and so distinguished himself that he became the most powerful support of the throne and of Christianity.[72]", "오를란도는 황제의 총애를 받아 그와 함께 프랑스로 돌아왔고, 어찌나 두각을 나타냈던지 마침내 왕좌와 기독교의 가장 든든한 버팀목이 되었다.[72]\n롤랑과 페라구스"]], [["# ROLAND AND FERRAGUS", "# ROLAND AND FERRAGUS"]], [["Orlando, or Roland, particularly distinguished himself by his combat with Ferragus.", "오를란도, 곧 롤랑은 특히 페라구스와의 싸움으로 명성을 떨쳤다."], ["Ferragus was a giant, and moreover his skin was of such impenetrable stuff that no sword could make any impression upon it.", "페라구스는 거인이었으며, 게다가 그의 살갗은 어떤 칼도 자국을 낼 수 없을 만큼 뚫리지 않는 재질로 되어 있었다."], ["The giant’s mode of fighting was to seize his adversary in his arms and carry him off, in spite of all the struggles he could make.", "그 거인의 싸움 방식은 적수를 두 팔로 붙잡아, 그가 아무리 발버둥쳐도 들고 가 버리는 것이었다."], ["Roland’s utmost skill only availed to keep him out of the giant’s clutches, but all his efforts to wound him with the sword were useless.", "롤랑은 온갖 솜씨를 다해도 겨우 거인의 손아귀에서 벗어나는 것이 고작이었고, 칼로 그에게 상처를 입히려는 모든 노력은 헛수고였다."], ["After long fighting Ferragus was so weary that he proposed a truce, and when it was agreed upon he lay down and immediately fell asleep.", "오래 싸운 끝에 페라구스는 너무도 지쳐 휴전을 제안했고, 합의가 이루어지자 그는 드러누워 곧장 잠이 들었다."], ["He slept in perfect security, for it was against all the laws of chivalry to take advantage of an adversary under such circumstances.", "그는 완전히 안심한 채 잠을 잤으니, 그런 상황에서 적수를 이용하는 것은 기사도의 모든 법도에 어긋나는 일이었기 때문이다."], ["But Ferragus lay so uncomfortably for the want of a pillow that Orlando took pity upon him, and brought a smooth stone and placed it under his head.", "그런데 페라구스가 베개가 없어 너무도 불편하게 누워 있는 까닭에, 오를란도는 그를 가엾이 여겨 매끄러운 돌 하나를 가져다 그의 머리 밑에 받쳐 주었다."], ["When the giant woke up, after a refreshing nap, and perceived what Orlando had done, he seemed quite grateful, became sociable, and talked freely in the usual boastful style of such characters.", "거인이 개운하게 한숨 자고 깨어나 오를란도가 한 일을 알아차리자, 그는 무척 고마워하는 듯하더니, 친근해져서는 그런 부류의 인물들이 으레 하는 허풍 섞인 투로 거리낌 없이 떠들었다."], ["Among other things he told Orlando that he need not attempt to kill him with a sword, for that every part of his body was invulnerable, except this; and as he spoke, he put his hand to the vital part, just in the middle of his breast.", "그는 여러 이야기 가운데, 오를란도에게 칼로 자기를 죽이려 애쓸 필요가 없다고 했는데, 자기 몸은 이곳을 제외하고는 어느 부위도 상처를 입지 않기 때문이라고 했다. 그러면서 그는 자기 가슴 한복판의 급소에 손을 가져다 댔다."], ["Aided by this information Orlando succeeded, when the fight was renewed, in piercing the giant in the very spot he had pointed out, and giving him a death-wound.", "이 정보 덕분에 오를란도는 싸움이 재개되었을 때, 거인이 가리켰던 바로 그 자리를 찔러 그에게 치명상을 입히는 데 성공했다."], ["Great was the rejoicing in the Christian camp, and many the praises showered upon the victorious paladin by the Emperor and all his host.", "기독교 진영의 환호는 대단했고, 황제와 그의 온 군대가 승리한 팔라딘에게 쏟아부은 찬사도 끝이 없었다."]], [["On another occasion Orlando encountered a puissant Saracen warrior, and took from him, as the prize of victory, the sword Durindana.", "또 한번은 오를란도가 강력한 사라센 전사와 맞붙어, 승리의 전리품으로 그에게서 칼 두린다나를 빼앗았다."], ["This famous weapon had once belonged to the illustrious prince Hector of Troy.", "이 이름난 무기는 한때 트로이아의 명망 높은 왕자 헥토르의 것이었다."], ["It was of the finest workmanship, and of such strength and temper that no armor in the world could stand against it.", "그것은 더없이 정교한 솜씨로 만들어졌고, 그 강도와 단련이 어찌나 뛰어났던지 세상의 어떤 갑옷도 그것을 당해 낼 수 없었다. 올리버에게는 롤랑으로"]], [["# A ROLAND FOR AN OLIVER", "# A ROLAND FOR AN OLIVER"]], [["Guerin de Montglave held the lordship of Vienne, subject to Charlemagne.", "몽글라브의 게랭은 카롤루스에게 신속한 빈[비엔]의 영주였다."], ["He had quarrelled with his sovereign, and Charles laid siege to his city, having ravaged the neighboring country.", "그는 군주와 다투었고, 카롤루스는 인근 지역을 약탈한 뒤 그의 도시를 포위했다."], ["Guerin was an aged warrior, but relied for his defence upon his four sons and two grandsons, who were among the bravest knights of the age.", "게랭은 연로한 전사였으나, 자신을 지키는 일을 네 아들과 두 손자에게 의지했는데, 그들은 당대의 가장 용맹한 기사들 축에 들었다."], ["After the siege had continued two months Charlemagne received tidings that Marsilius, king of Spain, had invaded France, and, finding himself unopposed, was advancing rapidly in the Southern provinces.", "포위가 두 달 동안 이어진 뒤, 카롤루스는 에스파냐 왕 마르실리우스가 프랑스를 침공하여, 아무런 저항도 받지 않자 남부 지방으로 빠르게 진격하고 있다는 소식을 받았다."], ["At this intelligence Charles listened to the counsel of his peers, and consented to put the quarrel with Guerin to the decision of Heaven, by single combat between two knights, one of each party, selected by lot.", "이 소식에 카롤루스는 동료 기사들의 조언에 귀를 기울여, 게랭과의 분쟁을 하늘의 결정에 맡기기로 했으니, 곧 제비로 뽑은 양편 각각의 기사 한 명씩이 벌이는 일대일 결투로 정하기로 한 것이다."], ["The proposal was acceptable to Guerin and his sons.", "이 제안은 게랭과 그의 아들들에게도 받아들일 만한 것이었다."], ["The names of the four, together with Guerin’s own, who would not be excused, and of the two grandsons, who claimed their lot, being put into a helmet, Oliver’s was drawn forth, and to him, the youngest of the grandsons, was assigned the honor and the peril of the combat.", "네 아들의 이름과, 빠지려 하지 않은 게랭 자신의 이름, 그리고 제비뽑기에 끼워 달라고 청한 두 손자의 이름이 투구 안에 담겼고, 거기서 올리버의 이름이 뽑혀 나왔다. 손자들 가운데 가장 어린 그에게 결투의 영예와 위험이 맡겨졌다."], ["He accepted the award with delight, exulting in being thought worthy to maintain the cause of his family.", "그는 자기 가문의 명분을 지킬 자격이 있다고 여겨졌다는 데 기뻐 우쭐대며 그 결정을 기꺼이 받아들였다."], ["On Charlemagne’s side Roland was the designated champion, and neither he nor Oliver knew who his antagonist was to be.", "카롤루스 편에서는 롤랑이 지명된 투사였는데, 롤랑도 올리버도 자신의 적수가 누구일지 알지 못했다."]], [["They met on an island in the Rhone, and the warriors of both camps were ranged on either shore, spectators of the battle.", "그들은 론강의 한 섬에서 만났고, 양 진영의 전사들은 양쪽 강기슭에 늘어서서 싸움을 지켜보았다."], ["At the first encounter both lances were shivered, but both riders kept their seats, immovable.", "첫 격돌에서 두 자루의 창이 모두 산산이 부서졌으나, 두 기수 모두 미동도 없이 안장에 앉아 있었다."], ["They dismounted, and drew their swords.", "그들은 말에서 내려 칼을 뽑았다."], ["Then ensued a combat which seemed so equal, that the spectators could not form an opinion as to the probable issue.", "그러자 너무도 호각인 싸움이 벌어져, 구경하던 이들은 어느 쪽이 이길지 도무지 짐작할 수 없었다."], ["Two hours and more the knights continued to strike and parry, to thrust and ward, neither showing any sign of weariness, nor ever being taken at unawares.", "두 시간이 넘도록 두 기사는 치고 막고, 찌르고 받아내기를 계속했는데, 어느 쪽도 지친 기색을 보이지 않았고, 방심하여 허를 찔리는 일도 없었다."], ["At length Orlando struck furiously upon Oliver’s shield, burying Durindana in its edge so deeply that he could not draw it back, and Oliver, almost at the same moment, thrust so vigorously upon Orlando’s breastplate that his sword snapped off at the handle.", "마침내 오를란도가 올리버의 방패를 맹렬히 내리쳐 두린다나를 방패 가장자리에 어찌나 깊이 박았던지 다시 빼낼 수 없게 되었고, 거의 같은 순간에 올리버는 오를란도의 흉갑을 어찌나 힘차게 찔렀던지 칼이 손잡이에서 뚝 부러지고 말았다."], ["Thus were the two warriors left weaponless.", "이리하여 두 전사는 무기를 잃은 채 남겨졌다."], ["Scarcely pausing a moment, they rushed upon one another, each striving to throw his adversary to the ground, and failing in that, each snatched at the other’s helmet to tear it away.", "그들은 한순간도 멈추지 않고 서로에게 달려들어, 저마다 적수를 땅에 쓰러뜨리려 안간힘을 썼고, 그것이 여의치 않자 저마다 상대의 투구를 낚아채 벗기려 들었다."], ["Both succeeded, and at the same moment they stood bareheaded face to face, and Roland recognized Oliver, and Oliver Roland.", "둘 다 성공했고, 같은 순간 그들은 맨머리로 얼굴을 마주하고 섰으니, 롤랑은 올리버를, 올리버는 롤랑을 알아보았다."], ["For a moment they stood still; and the next, with open arms, rushed into one another’s embrace.", "한순간 그들은 가만히 서 있었다. 그리고 다음 순간, 두 팔을 벌린 채 서로의 품으로 달려들었다."], ["“I am conquered,”", "\"내가 졌소.\""], ["said Orlando.", "오를란도가 말했다."], ["“I yield me,”", "\"내가 항복하오.\""], ["said Oliver.", "올리버가 말했다."]], [["The people on the shore knew not what to make of all this.", "강기슭의 사람들은 이 모든 광경을 어떻게 받아들여야 할지 알 수 없었다."], ["Presently they saw the two late antagonists standing hand in hand, and it was evident the battle was at an end.", "이내 그들은 조금 전까지 맞붙었던 두 사람이 손을 맞잡고 서 있는 것을 보았고, 싸움이 끝났음이 분명해졌다."], ["The knights crowded round them, and with one voice hailed them as equals in glory.", "기사들이 그들 둘레로 몰려들어, 한목소리로 그들을 영광에서 대등한 자들이라 칭송했다."], ["If there were any who felt disposed to murmur that the battle was left undecided they were silenced by the voice of Ogier the Dane, who proclaimed aloud that all had been done that honor required, and declared that he would maintain that award against all gainsayers.", "혹여 싸움이 결판나지 않은 채 끝났다고 투덜대려는 이가 있었다면, 덴마크인 오지에의 목소리에 입을 다물었을 것이니, 그는 명예가 요구하는 모든 것이 다 이루어졌다고 큰 소리로 선포했으며, 누가 이의를 제기하든 그 판정을 지켜내겠노라 단언했다."]], [["The quarrel with Guerin and his sons being left undecided, a truce was made for four days, and in that time, by the efforts of Duke Namo on the one side, and of Oliver on the other, a reconciliation was effected.", "게랭과 그의 아들들과의 분쟁은 결판나지 않은 채 남았고, 나흘간의 휴전이 맺어졌으며, 그동안 한편으로는 나모 공작의 노력으로, 다른 편으로는 올리버의 노력으로 화해가 이루어졌다."], ["Charlemagne, accompanied by Guerin and his valiant family, marched to meet Marsilius, who hastened to retreat across the frontier.", "카롤루스는 게랭과 그의 용맹한 가족을 거느리고 마르실리우스를 맞아 진격했으며, 마르실리우스는 서둘러 국경 너머로 퇴각했다."]], [["Rinaldo was one of the four sons of Aymon, who married Aya, the sister of Charlemagne.", "리날도\n리날도는 카롤루스의 누이 아야와 혼인한 에몽의 네 아들 가운데 하나였다."], ["Thus Rinaldo was nephew to Charlemagne and cousin of Orlando.", "따라서 리날도는 카롤루스의 조카이자 오를란도의 사촌이었다."]], [["When Rinaldo had grown old enough to assume arms Orlando had won for himself an illustrious name by his exploits against the Saracens, whom Charlemagne and his brave knights had driven out of France.", "리날도가 무기를 들 만한 나이가 되었을 무렵, 오를란도는 사라센인들을 상대로 한 무훈으로 이미 빛나는 이름을 얻고 있었으니, 카롤루스와 그의 용감한 기사들은 그 사라센인들을 프랑스에서 몰아낸 터였다."], ["Orlando’s fame excited a noble emulation in Rinaldo.", "오를란도의 명성은 리날도의 마음에 고귀한 경쟁심을 불러일으켰다."], ["Eager to go in pursuit of glory, he wandered in the country near Paris, and one day saw at the foot of a tree a superb horse, fully equipped and loaded with a complete suit of armor.", "영광을 좇고 싶은 열망에 차서, 그는 파리 인근 지방을 떠돌다가, 어느 날 한 나무 밑에서 완전히 마구를 갖추고 한 벌의 갑옷을 가득 실은 훌륭한 말 한 마리를 보았다."], ["Rinaldo clothed himself in the armor and mounted the horse, but took not the sword.", "리날도는 그 갑옷을 입고 말에 올랐으나, 칼은 취하지 않았다."], ["On the day when, with his brothers, he had received the honor of knighthood from the Emperor he had sworn never to bind a sword to his side till he had wrested one from some famous knight.", "형제들과 함께 황제로부터 기사 작위의 영예를 받던 날, 그는 어느 이름난 기사에게서 칼을 빼앗기 전에는 결코 칼을 옆구리에 차지 않겠노라 맹세했던 것이다."]], [["Rinaldo took his way to the forest of Arden, celebrated for so many adventures.", "리날도는 그토록 많은 모험으로 유명한 아르덴 숲으로 향했다."], ["Hardly had he entered it when he met an old man, bending under the weight of years, and learned from him that the forest was infested with a wild horse, untamable, that broke and overturned everything that opposed his career.", "그가 숲에 들어서기가 무섭게 세월의 무게에 허리가 굽은 한 노인을 만났고, 그에게서 그 숲에는 길들일 수 없는 야생마 한 마리가 출몰하여, 제 앞길을 가로막는 것은 무엇이든 부수고 뒤엎어 버린다는 말을 들었다."], ["To attack him, he said, or even to meet him, was certain death.", "그 말을 공격하기는커녕 마주치기만 해도 죽음을 면치 못한다고 그는 말했다."], ["Rinaldo, far from being alarmed, showed the most eager desire to combat the animal.", "리날도는 겁먹기는커녕 그 짐승과 싸우고 싶다는 가장 간절한 열망을 드러냈다."], ["This was the horse Bayard, afterward so famous.", "이것이 바로 훗날 그토록 유명해진 말 바이아르였다."], ["He had formerly belonged to Amadis of Gaul.", "이 말은 전에 갈리아의 아마디스의 것이었다."], ["After the death of that hero he had been held under enchantment by the power of a magician, who predicted that, when the time came to break the spell, he should be subdued by a knight of the lineage of Amadis, and not less brave than he.", "그 영웅이 죽은 뒤, 말은 어느 마법사의 힘으로 마법에 걸린 채 붙들려 있었는데, 마법사는 이 마법을 풀 때가 오면, 아마디스의 혈통이면서 그에 못지않게 용감한 기사에게 길들여지리라 예언했다."]], [["To win this wonderful horse it was necessary to conquer him by force or skill; for from the moment when he should be thrown down he would become docile and manageable.", "이 놀라운 말을 얻으려면 힘이나 솜씨로 그를 굴복시켜야 했으니, 그가 쓰러지는 순간부터 순하고 다루기 쉬운 말이 될 것이었기 때문이다."], ["His habitual resort was a cave on the borders of the forest; but woe be to any one who should approach him, unless gifted with strength and courage more than mortal.", "그가 늘 머무는 곳은 숲 가장자리의 한 동굴이었으나, 인간을 넘어서는 힘과 용기를 지니지 않은 자라면 누구든 그에게 다가가는 자에게 화가 미칠 것이었다."], ["Having told this, the old man departed.", "이 말을 들려준 뒤 노인은 떠났다."], ["He was not, in fact, an old man, but Malagigi, the enchanter, cousin of Rinaldo, who, to favor the enterprises of the young knight, had procured for him the horse and armor which he so opportunely found, and now put him in the way to acquire a horse unequalled in the world.", "그는 사실 노인이 아니라 마법사 말라지지, 곧 리날도의 사촌으로, 이 젊은 기사의 모험을 돕기 위해 그가 마침맞게 발견한 말과 갑옷을 마련해 주었던 것이며, 이제는 세상에 둘도 없는 말을 손에 넣을 길로 그를 인도한 것이었다."]], [["Rinaldo plunged into the forest, and spent many days in seeking Bayard, but found no traces of him.", "리날도는 숲속으로 뛰어들어 여러 날 동안 바이아르를 찾아다녔으나, 그 흔적조차 발견하지 못했다."], ["One day he encountered a Saracen knight, with whom he made acquaintance, as often happened to knights, by first meeting him in combat.", "어느 날 그는 한 사라센 기사와 마주쳤는데, 기사들 사이에서 흔히 그러하듯 처음에 싸움으로 맞닥뜨려 알게 되었다."], ["This knight, whose name was Isolier, was also in quest of Bayard.", "이졸리에라는 이름의 이 기사 역시 바이아르를 찾는 중이었다."], ["Rinaldo succeeded in the encounter, and so severe was the shock that Isolier was a long time insensible.", "리날도가 그 격돌에서 이겼는데, 충격이 어찌나 심했던지 이졸리에는 오랫동안 정신을 잃고 있었다."], ["When he revived, and was about to resume the contest, a peasant who passed by (it was Malagigi) interrupted them with the news that the terrible horse was near at hand, advising them to unite their powers to subdue him, for it would require all their ability.", "그가 정신을 차리고 다시 싸움을 벌이려 할 때, 마침 지나가던 한 농부(그가 바로 말라지지였다)가 그 무시무시한 말이 가까이 와 있다는 소식으로 그들을 가로막으며, 그를 굴복시키려면 두 사람의 모든 능력을 다 쏟아부어야 할 것이니 힘을 합치라고 조언했다."]], [["Rinaldo and Isolier, now become friends, proceeded together to the attack of the horse.", "이제 벗이 된 리날도와 이졸리에는 함께 그 말을 공격하러 나아갔다."], ["They found Bayard, and stood a long time, concealed by the wood, admiring his strength and beauty.", "그들은 바이아르를 발견하고는, 오랫동안 숲에 몸을 숨긴 채 그 힘과 아름다움에 감탄하며 서 있었다."]], [["A bright bay in color (whence he was called Bayard), with a silver star in his forehead, and his hind feet white, his body slender, his head delicate, his ample chest filled out with swelling muscles, his shoulders broad and full, his legs straight and sinewy, his thick mane falling over his arching neck,—he came rushing through the forest, regardless of rocks, bushes, or trees, rending everything that opposed his way, and neighing defiance.", "빛나는 밤색 털빛에(그래서 바이아르라 불렸다), 이마에는 은빛 별이 박혀 있고, 뒷발은 희며, 몸은 날렵하고, 머리는 섬세하며, 떡 벌어진 가슴은 불룩한 근육으로 가득 찼고, 어깨는 넓고 두툼하며, 다리는 곧고 힘줄이 단단하며, 두툼한 갈기가 활처럼 휜 목 위로 흘러내린 그 말은, 바위든 덤불이든 나무든 아랑곳없이 숲을 헤치고 돌진하며, 제 앞길을 가로막는 것은 무엇이든 찢어발기고, 도전하듯 울부짖으며 달려왔다."]], [["He first descried Isolier, and rushed upon him.", "그는 먼저 이졸리에를 발견하고 그에게 돌진했다."], ["The knight received him with lance in rest, but the fierce animal broke the spear, and his course was not delayed by it for an instant.", "기사는 창을 겨눈 채 그를 맞았으나, 사나운 짐승은 창을 부러뜨렸고, 그 때문에 그의 질주는 한순간도 늦춰지지 않았다."], ["The Spaniard adroitly stepped aside, and gave way to the rushing tempest.", "에스파냐 사람은 날렵하게 옆으로 비켜 그 돌진하는 폭풍에게 길을 내주었다."], ["Bayard checked his career, and turned again upon the knight, who had already drawn his sword.", "바이아르는 질주를 멈추고 다시 기사에게로 돌아섰으니, 기사는 이미 칼을 뽑은 터였다."], ["He drew his sword, for he had no hope of taming the horse; that, he was satisfied, was impossible.", "그가 칼을 뽑은 것은 그 말을 길들일 가망이 없다고 여겼기 때문이다. 그것은 불가능하다고 그는 확신했다."]], [["Bayard rushed upon him; fiercely rearing, now on this side, now on that.", "바이아르는 그에게 돌진하여, 이쪽저쪽으로 사납게 뒷발로 곧추섰다."], ["The knight struck him with his sword, where the white star adorned his forehead, but struck in vain, and felt ashamed, thinking that he had struck feebly, for he did not know that the skin of that horse was so tough that the keenest sword could make no impression upon it.", "기사는 흰 별이 이마를 장식한 자리를 칼로 내리쳤으나, 헛되이 내리쳤을 뿐이어서, 자기가 너무 약하게 친 줄로만 알고 부끄러워했다. 그 말의 살갗이 어찌나 질긴지 아무리 날카로운 칼도 자국을 낼 수 없다는 것을 그는 알지 못했던 것이다."]], [["Whistling fell the sword once more, and struck with greater force, and the fierce horse felt it, and drooped his head under the blow, but the next moment turned upon his foe with such a buffet that the Pagan fell stunned and lifeless to the earth.", "다시 한번 칼이 휙 소리를 내며 더 큰 힘으로 내리쳐졌고, 사나운 말은 그것을 느껴 일격에 머리를 수그렸으나, 다음 순간 어찌나 세게 들이받았던지 이교도는 정신을 잃고 죽은 듯 땅바닥에 쓰러졌다."]], [["Rinaldo, who saw Isolier fall, and thought that his life was reft, darted towards the horse, and, with his fist gave him such a blow on the jaws that the blood tinged his mouth with vermilion.", "이졸리에가 쓰러지는 것을 보고 그가 목숨을 잃은 줄로 여긴 리날도는 말을 향해 달려들어, 주먹으로 그 턱을 어찌나 세게 후려쳤던지 피가 말의 입가를 진홍빛으로 물들였다."], ["Quicker than an arrow leaves the bow the horse turned upon him, and tried to seize his arm with his teeth.", "활을 떠난 화살보다도 빠르게 말이 그에게로 돌아서서, 이빨로 그의 팔을 물어뜯으려 했다."]], [["The knight stepped back, and then, repeating his blow, struck him on the forehead.", "기사는 뒤로 물러섰다가, 다시 일격을 가하여 그의 이마를 내리쳤다."], ["Bayard turned, and kicked with both his feet with a force that would have shattered a mountain.", "바이아르는 돌아서서, 산이라도 부숴 버릴 만한 힘으로 두 발을 함께 내질렀다."], ["Rinaldo was on his guard, and evaded his attacks, whether made with head or heels.", "리날도는 방심하지 않고, 머리로 들이받든 발로 차든 그의 공격을 피해 냈다."], ["He kept at his side avoiding both; but, making a false step, he at last received a terrible blow from the horse’s foot, and at the shock almost fainted away.", "그는 둘 다 피하며 그의 옆에 바짝 붙어 있었으나, 발을 헛디딘 탓에 마침내 말의 발에 끔찍한 일격을 맞았고, 그 충격에 거의 정신을 잃을 뻔했다."], ["A second such blow would have killed him, but the horse kicked at random, and a second blow did not reach Rinaldo, who in a moment recovered himself.", "그런 일격을 한 번 더 맞았더라면 목숨을 잃었을 것이나, 말이 아무렇게나 발길질을 한 덕에 두 번째 일격은 리날도에게 닿지 않았고, 그는 잠깐 사이에 정신을 추슬렀다."], ["Thus the contest continued until by chance Bayard’s foot got caught between the branches of an oak.", "이렇게 싸움이 계속되다가, 어쩌다 바이아르의 발이 떡갈나무 가지 사이에 끼고 말았다."], ["Rinaldo seized it and putting forth all his strength and address, threw him on the ground.", "리날도는 그것을 붙잡아, 온 힘과 기량을 다 쏟아부어 그를 땅에 메다꽂았다."]], [["No sooner had Bayard touched the ground than all his rage subsided.", "바이아르가 땅에 닿자마자 그의 모든 분노는 가라앉았다."], ["No longer an object of terror, he became gentle and quiet, yet with dignity in his mildness.", "더는 두려움의 대상이 아니라, 그는 온순하고 조용해졌으나, 그 온화함 속에도 위엄이 깃들어 있었다."]], [["The paladin patted his neck, stroked his breast, and smoothed his mane, while the animal neighed and showed delight to be caressed by his master.", "팔라딘은 그 목을 토닥이고, 가슴을 쓰다듬고, 갈기를 매만져 주었고, 짐승은 울음소리를 내며 제 주인의 손길을 받는 것을 기뻐하는 기색을 보였다."], ["Rinaldo, seeing him now completely subdued, took the saddle and trappings from the other horse, and adorned Bayard with the spoils.", "리날도는 이제 그가 완전히 굴복한 것을 보고, 다른 말에서 안장과 마구를 떼어다 그 노획물로 바이아르를 치장했다."]], [["Rinaldo became one of the most illustrious knights of Charlemagne’s court,—indeed, the most illustrious, if we except Orlando.", "리날도는 카롤루스 궁정의 가장 명망 높은 기사들 가운데 하나가 되었으니, 오를란도를 제외한다면 사실상 가장 명망 높은 기사였다."], ["Yet he was not always so obedient to the Emperor’s commands as he should have been, and every fault he committed was sure to be aggravated by the malice of Gan, Duke of Maganza, the treacherous enemy of Rinaldo and all his house.", "그러나 그는 늘 황제의 명령에 마땅한 만큼 순종하지는 않았으며, 그가 저지른 잘못은 무엇이든 마간차 공작 간의 악의로 인해 으레 더 부풀려지곤 했으니, 간은 리날도와 그의 온 가문에 대한 배신자이자 적이었다."]], [["At one time Rinaldo had incurred the severe displeasure of Charlemagne, and been banished from court.", "한번은 리날도가 카롤루스의 심한 노여움을 사 궁정에서 추방당한 적이 있었다."], ["Seeing no chance of being ever restored to favor, he went to Spain, and entered into the service of the Saracen king, Ivo.", "다시 총애를 되찾을 가망이 도무지 보이지 않자, 그는 에스파냐로 가서 사라센 왕 이보를 섬기게 되었다."], ["His brothers, Alardo, Ricardo, and Ricciardetto, accompanied him, and all four served the king so faithfully that they rose to high favor with him.", "그의 형제들인 알라르도와 리카르도, 리치아르데토도 그와 동행했고, 네 사람 모두 어찌나 충실하게 왕을 섬겼던지 그의 높은 총애를 받기에 이르렀다."], ["The king gave them land in the mountains on the frontiers of France and Spain, and subjected all the country round to Rinaldo’s authority.", "왕은 그들에게 프랑스와 에스파냐의 국경 산악 지대에 땅을 내주었고, 인근의 온 지역을 리날도의 권한 아래 두었다."], ["There was plenty of marble in the mountains, the king furnished workmen, and they built a castle for Rinaldo, surrounded with high walls, so as to be almost impregnable.", "그 산악에는 대리석이 풍부했고, 왕이 일꾼들을 대 주어, 그들은 리날도를 위해 높은 성벽으로 둘러싸인, 거의 난공불락의 성을 지었다."], ["Built of white stone, and placed on the brow of a marble promontory, the castle shone like a star, and Rinaldo gave it the name of Montalban.", "흰 돌로 지어져 대리석 곶의 마루 위에 자리 잡은 그 성은 별처럼 빛났고, 리날도는 그 성에 몬탈반이라는 이름을 붙였다."], ["Here he assembled his friends, many of whom were banished men like himself, and the country people furnished them with provisions in return for the protection the castle afforded.", "이곳에 그는 자신의 벗들을 불러 모았는데, 그들 가운데 다수는 그 자신처럼 추방당한 사람들이었고, 그 고장 사람들은 성이 제공하는 보호에 대한 보답으로 그들에게 식량을 대 주었다."], ["Yet some of Rinaldo’s men were lawless, and sometimes the supplies were not furnished in sufficient abundance, so that Rinaldo and his garrison got a bad name for taking by force what they could not obtain by gift; and we sometimes find Montalban spoken of as a nest of freebooters, and its defenders called a beggarly garrison.", "그러나 리날도의 부하들 가운데 일부는 무법자였고, 때때로 식량이 넉넉하게 공급되지 않는 일도 있어서, 리날도와 그의 수비대는 받지 못한 것을 힘으로 빼앗는다는 나쁜 평판을 얻었다. 그래서 우리는 몬탈반이 때때로 약탈자들의 소굴로 일컬어지고, 그 수비대원들이 거지 같은 수비대라 불리는 것을 보게 된다."]], [["Charlemagne’s displeasure did not last long, and, at the time our history commences, Rinaldo and his brothers were completely restored to the favor of the Emperor, and none of his cavaliers served him with greater zeal and fidelity than they, throughout all his wars with the Saracens and Pagans.", "카롤루스의 노여움은 오래가지 않았고, 우리의 이야기가 시작되는 시점에 리날도와 그의 형제들은 황제의 총애를 완전히 되찾았으며, 사라센인 및 이교도들과 벌인 그의 모든 전쟁에서, 그의 어느 기사도 그들보다 더한 열의와 충성으로 그를 섬기지는 않았다."]], [["————", "————"]], [["# THE TOURNAMENT", "# THE TOURNAMENT"]], [["It was the month of May, and the feast of Pentecost.", "마상 시합\n때는 5월, 오순절 축일이었다."], ["Charlemagne had ordered magnificent festivities, and summoned to them, besides his paladins and vassals of the crown, all strangers, Christian or Saracen, then sojourning at Paris.", "카롤루스는 성대한 축연을 명하고, 자신의 팔라딘들과 왕가의 봉신들 외에도, 그때 파리에 머물던 모든 외지인을 기독교도든 사라센이든 가리지 않고 축연에 불러 모았다."], ["Among the guests were King Grandonio, from Spain; and Ferrau, the Saracen, with eyes like an eagle; Orlando and Rinaldo, the Emperor’s nephews; Duke Namo; Astolpho, of England, the handsomest man living; Malagigi, the Enchanter; and Gano, of Maganza, that wily traitor, who had the art to make the Emperor think he loved him, while he plotted against him.", "손님들 가운데에는 에스파냐에서 온 그란도니오 왕, 독수리 같은 눈을 지닌 사라센 페라우, 황제의 조카인 오를란도와 리날도, 나모 공작, 살아 있는 가장 잘생긴 사내인 잉글랜드의 아스톨포, 마법사 말라지지, 그리고 마간차의 가노가 있었으니, 가노는 그 교활한 배신자로, 황제에게 음모를 꾸미면서도 마치 그를 사랑하는 것처럼 믿게 만드는 재주를 지닌 자였다."]], [["High sat Charlemagne at the head of his vassals and his paladins, rejoicing in the thought of their number and their might, while all were sitting and hearing music, and feasting, when suddenly there came into the hall four enormous giants, having between them a lady of incomparable beauty, attended by a single knight.", "카롤루스는 봉신들과 팔라딘들의 우두머리 자리에 높이 앉아, 그들의 수와 위세를 생각하며 흐뭇해했고, 모두가 둘러앉아 음악을 듣고 잔치를 즐기던 차에, 별안간 거대한 거인 넷이 그들 사이에 비할 데 없이 아름다운 한 귀부인을 데리고 단 한 명의 기사를 거느린 채 홀로 들어왔다."], ["There were many ladies present who had seemed beautiful till she made her appearance, but after that they all seemed nothing.", "그 자리에는 그녀가 나타나기 전까지 아름다워 보였던 귀부인이 많이 있었으나, 그녀가 나타난 뒤로는 그들 모두가 하찮아 보였다."], ["Every Christian knight turned his eyes to her, and every Pagan crowded round her, while she, with a sweetness that might have touched a heart of stone, thus addressed the Emperor:", "기독교 기사들은 저마다 그녀에게로 눈길을 돌렸고, 이교도들은 저마다 그녀 둘레로 몰려들었으며, 그녀는 돌 같은 마음도 움직일 만한 감미로움으로 황제에게 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“High-minded lord, the renown of your worthiness, and of the valor of these your knights, which echoes from sea to sea, encourages me to hope that two pilgrims, who have come from the ends of the world to behold you, will not have encountered their fatigue in vain.", "\"고매하신 주군이시여, 바다에서 바다로 메아리치는 당신의 훌륭하심과 이 기사들의 무용에 대한 명성에 힘입어, 세상 끝에서 당신을 뵈러 온 두 순례자가 그 고생을 헛되이 치르지는 않으리라는 희망을 품게 되었나이다. 그리고 저희를 이리로 이끈 동기를 밝히기에 앞서, 이 기사가 제 오라비 우베르토이며, 제가 그의 누이 안젤리카임을 알아 주소서."], ["And, before I show the motive which has brought us hither, learn that this knight is my brother Uberto, and that I am his sister Angelica.", "오늘로 정해진 마상 시합 소식을 명성을 통해 들었기에, 제 오라비 되는 왕자께서 자신의 무용을 입증하고자 오셨나이다."], ["Fame has told us of the jousting this day appointed, and so the prince my brother has come to prove his valor, and to say that, if any of the knights here assembled choose to meet him in the joust, he will encounter them, one by one, at the stair of Merlin, by the Fountain of the Pine.", "여기 모인 기사들 가운데 누구든 그와 시합에서 겨루기를 원하는 이가 있다면, 그는 소나무 샘 곁 멀린의 계단에서 한 사람씩 차례로 그들을 맞으리라 전하라 하셨나이다. 그리고 그의 조건은 이러하옵니다."], ["And his conditions are these: No knight who chances to be thrown shall be allowed to renew the combat, but shall remain prisoner to my brother; but if my brother be overthrown he shall depart out of the country, leaving me as the prize of the conqueror.”", "낙마하게 되는 기사는 누구도 싸움을 다시 시작할 수 없으며, 제 오라비의 포로로 남아야 하나이다. 그러나 만일 제 오라비가 쓰러진다면, 그는 저를 승자의 전리품으로 남겨 둔 채 이 나라를 떠나리이다.\""]], [["Now it must be stated that this Angelica and her brother, who called himself Uberto, but whose real name was Argalia, were the children of Galafron, king of Cathay, who had sent them to be the destruction of the Christian host; for Argalia was armed with an enchanted lance, which unfailingly overthrew everything it touched, and he was mounted on a horse, a creature of magic, whose swiftness outstripped the wind.", "그런데 이 안젤리카와, 우베르토라 자칭했으나 실제 이름은 아르갈리아인 그녀의 오라비는, 카타이 왕 갈라프론의 자식들이었음을 밝혀 두어야 하겠다. 갈라프론은 기독교 군대를 멸하고자 그들을 보낸 것이었으니, 아르갈리아는 닿는 것은 무엇이든 어김없이 쓰러뜨리는 마법의 창으로 무장하고 있었고, 바람보다 빠른 마법의 피조물인 말을 타고 있었기 때문이다."], ["Angelica possessed also a ring which was a defence against all enchantments, and when put into the mouth rendered the bearer invisible.", "안젤리카 또한 모든 마법을 막아 주고, 입에 물면 지닌 자를 보이지 않게 만드는 반지를 가지고 있었다."], ["Thus Argalia was expected to subdue and take prisoners whatever knights should dare to encounter him; and the charms of Angelica were relied on to entice the paladins to make the fatal venture, while her ring would afford her easy means of escape.", "이리하여 아르갈리아는 감히 그와 맞서려는 기사라면 누구든 굴복시켜 포로로 잡으리라 기대되었고, 팔라딘들을 이 치명적인 시도로 유혹하는 데에는 안젤리카의 매력이 의지가 되었으며, 한편 그녀의 반지는 그녀에게 손쉬운 도주 수단을 마련해 줄 것이었다."]], [["When Angelica ceased speaking she knelt before the king and awaited his answer, and everybody gazed on her with admiration.", "안젤리카가 말을 마치자 그녀는 왕 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 그의 대답을 기다렸으며, 모두가 감탄하며 그녀를 응시했다."], ["Orlando especially felt irresistibly drawn towards her, so that he trembled and changed countenance.", "특히 오를란도는 거역할 수 없이 그녀에게 이끌려, 몸을 떨며 안색이 변할 정도였다."], ["Every knight in the hall was infected with the same feeling, not excepting old white-headed Duke Namo and Charlemagne himself.", "홀 안의 모든 기사가 똑같은 감정에 사로잡혔으니, 머리가 하얗게 센 늙은 나모 공작도, 카롤루스 자신도 예외가 아니었다."]], [["All stood for a while in silence, lost in the delight of looking at her.", "모두가 한동안 말없이 서서 그녀를 바라보는 기쁨에 넋을 잃고 있었다."], ["The fiery youth Ferrau could hardly restrain himself from seizing her from the giants and carrying her away; Rinaldo turned as red as fire, while Malagigi, who had discovered by his art that the stranger was not speaking truth, muttered softly, as he looked at her, “Exquisite false creature!", "혈기 왕성한 젊은이 페라우는 거인들에게서 그녀를 낚아채 데려가고 싶은 마음을 가까스로 억눌렀고, 리날도는 불처럼 빨갛게 달아올랐으며, 한편 자신의 술법으로 그 낯선 여인이 진실을 말하고 있지 않음을 간파한 말라지지는, 그녀를 바라보며 낮게 중얼거렸다. \"절묘한 거짓 피조물이로구나!"], ["I will play thee such a trick for this, as will leave thee no cause to boast of thy visit.”", "이 일로 네게 이런 장난을 쳐 주마. 네 방문을 자랑할 거리가 하나도 남지 않게 될 게다.\""]], [["Charlemagne, to detain her as long as possible before him, delayed his assent till he had asked her a number of questions, all which she answered discreetly, and then the challenge was accepted.", "카롤루스는 그녀를 되도록 오래 자기 앞에 붙들어 두고자, 여러 가지 질문을 던질 때까지 승낙을 미루었고, 그녀가 그 모든 질문에 신중하게 대답하자, 그제야 도전은 받아들여졌다."]], [["As soon as she was gone Malagigi consulted his book, and found out the whole plot of the vile, infidel king, Galafron, as we have explained it, so he determined to seek the damsel and frustrate her designs.", "그녀가 떠나자마자 말라지지는 자신의 책을 들여다보아, 우리가 설명한 대로, 비열한 이교도 왕 갈라프론의 음모 전부를 알아냈다. 그리하여 그는 그 처녀를 찾아내어 그녀의 계략을 좌절시키기로 마음먹었다."], ["He hastened to the appointed spot, and there found the prince and his sister in a beautiful pavilion, where they lay asleep, while the four giants kept watch.", "그는 약속된 장소로 서둘러 갔고, 그곳에서 왕자와 그의 누이가 아름다운 천막 안에 잠들어 있는 것을 발견했으며, 한편 거인 넷이 망을 보고 있었다."], ["Malagigi took his book and cast a spell out of it, and immediately the four giants fell into a deep sleep.", "말라지지는 자신의 책을 꺼내 거기서 주문을 외웠고, 곧장 거인 넷은 깊은 잠에 빠졌다."], ["Drawing his sword (for he was a belted knight), he softly approached the young lady, intending to despatch her at once; but, seeing her look so lovely, he paused for a moment, thinking there was no need of hurry, as he believed his spell was upon her, and she could not wake.", "그는 칼을 뽑아 들고(그는 작위를 받은 기사였으므로) 살그머니 그 젊은 여인에게 다가가, 단숨에 그녀를 처치하려 했다. 그러나 그녀의 모습이 너무도 사랑스러워 보이는 까닭에, 자신의 주문이 그녀에게 걸려 있어 그녀가 깨어날 수 없으리라 믿었던 터라, 서두를 필요가 없다고 여기며 잠시 멈칫했다."], ["But the ring which she wore secured her from the effect of the spell, and some slight noise, or whatever else it was, caused her at that moment to awake.", "그런데 그녀가 끼고 있던 반지가 주문의 효력으로부터 그녀를 지켜 주었고, 어떤 작은 소리 때문인지 아니면 다른 무엇 때문인지, 그녀는 바로 그 순간 잠에서 깨어났다."], ["She uttered a great cry, and flew to her brother, and waked him.", "그녀는 크게 비명을 지르며 오라비에게로 달려가 그를 깨웠다."], ["By the help of her knowledge of enchantment, they took and bound fast the magician, and, seizing his book, turned his arts against himself.", "마법에 대한 그녀의 지식의 도움으로, 그들은 마법사를 붙잡아 단단히 묶고, 그의 책을 빼앗아 그의 술법을 그 자신에게 되돌렸다."], ["Then they summoned a crowd of demons, and bade them seize their prisoner and bear him to King Galafron, at his great city of Albracca, which they did, and, on his arrival, he was locked up in a rock under the sea.", "그런 다음 그들은 한 무리의 악마를 불러내어, 자신들의 포로를 붙잡아 그의 큰 도시 알브라카에 있는 갈라프론 왕에게 데려가라 명했고, 악마들은 그대로 했으며, 그가 도착하자 그는 바다 밑 바위 속에 갇혔다."]], [["While these things were going on all was uproar at Paris, since Orlando insisted upon being the first to try the adventure at the stair of Merlin.", "이런 일들이 벌어지는 동안 파리에서는 온통 소란이 일었으니, 오를란도가 멀린의 계단에서 그 모험을 맨 먼저 시도하겠다고 고집을 부렸기 때문이다."], ["This was resented by the other pretenders to Angelica, and all contested his right to the precedence.", "이는 안젤리카를 노리는 다른 구혼자들의 반발을 샀고, 모두가 그의 우선권을 두고 다투었다."], ["The tumult was stilled by the usual expedient of drawing lots, and the first prize was drawn by Astolpho.", "이 소동은 늘 쓰이는 방편인 제비뽑기로 가라앉았는데, 첫 번째 차례는 아스톨포가 뽑았다."], ["Ferrau, the Saracen, had the second, and Grandonio the third.", "사라센 페라우가 두 번째를, 그란도니오가 세 번째를 차지했다."], ["Next came Berlinghieri, and Otho; then Charles himself, and, as his ill-fortune would have it, after thirty more, the indignant Orlando.", "다음으로 베를링기에리와 오토가, 그다음에는 카롤루스 자신이 뽑았고, 그의 불운으로 인해 서른 명도 더 지난 뒤에야 분개한 오를란도의 차례가 왔다."]], [["Astolpho, who drew the first lot, was handsome, brave, and rich.", "첫 번째 제비를 뽑은 아스톨포는 잘생기고 용감하며 부유했다."], ["But, whether from heedlessness or want of skill, he was an unlucky jouster, and very apt to be thrown, an accident which he bore with perfect good-humor, always ready to mount again and try to mend his fortune, generally with no better success.", "그러나 부주의 탓인지 솜씨가 모자란 탓인지, 그는 운 없는 시합꾼이어서 곧잘 낙마하곤 했는데, 이런 사고를 그는 더없이 좋은 기분으로 받아넘기며, 언제나 기꺼이 다시 말에 올라 운수를 만회하려 애썼으나, 대개는 그다지 나은 성과를 거두지 못했다."]], [["Astolpho went forth upon his adventure with great gayety of dress and manner, encountered Argalia, and was immediately tilted out of the saddle.", "아스톨포는 차림새도 거동도 무척 화려하게 모험에 나섰는데, 아르갈리아와 맞붙자마자 곧장 안장에서 나가떨어졌다."], ["He railed at fortune, to whom he laid all the fault; but his painful feelings were somewhat relieved by the kindness of Angelica, who, touched by his youth and good looks, granted him the liberty of the pavilion, and caused him to be treated with all kindness and respect.", "그는 모든 잘못을 운수 탓으로 돌리며 운수를 원망했으나, 안젤리카의 친절로 그의 아픈 심정이 다소 누그러졌으니, 그녀는 그의 젊음과 잘생긴 용모에 마음이 움직여 천막 안에서 자유로이 지내게 해 주고, 그를 온갖 친절과 존경으로 대접하도록 했다."]], [["The violent Ferrau had the next chance in the encounter, and was thrown no less speedily than Astolpho; but he did not so easily put up with his mischance.", "격렬한 페라우가 다음으로 격돌의 차례를 맞았는데, 아스톨포 못지않게 빠르게 나가떨어졌다. 그러나 그는 자신의 불운을 그리 순순히 받아들이지 않았다."], ["Crying out, “What are the emperor’s engagements to me?”", "\"황제의 약속 따위가 내게 무슨 상관이냐?\""], ["he rushed with his sword against Argalia, who, being forced to defend himself, dismounted and drew his sword, but got so much the worse of the fight that he made a signal of surrender, and, after some words, listened to a proposal of marriage from Ferrau to his sister.", "하고 소리치며, 그는 칼을 들고 아르갈리아에게 달려들었고, 아르갈리아는 어쩔 수 없이 자신을 지키려 말에서 내려 칼을 뽑았으나, 싸움에서 어찌나 밀렸던지 항복의 신호를 보냈고, 몇 마디 말이 오간 뒤 자신의 누이에게 청혼하는 페라우의 제안에 귀를 기울였다."], ["The beauty, however, feeling no inclination to match with such a rough and savage-looking person, was so dismayed at the offer, that, hastily bidding her brother to meet her in the forest of Arden, she vanished from the sight of both by means of the enchanted ring.", "그러나 그 미녀는 그토록 거칠고 사납게 생긴 자와 짝이 될 마음이 조금도 없었던지라, 그 제안에 어찌나 질겁했던지, 오라비에게 아르덴 숲에서 만나자고 황급히 이른 뒤, 마법의 반지를 써서 두 사람의 시야에서 사라졌다."], ["Argalia, seeing this, took to his horse of swiftness, and dashed away in the same direction.", "이를 본 아르갈리아는 자신의 빠른 말에 올라 같은 방향으로 내달렸다."], ["Ferrau pursued him, and Astolpho, thus left to himself, took possession of the enchanted lance in place of his own, which was broken, not knowing the treasure he possessed in it, and returned to the tournament.", "페라우가 그를 뒤쫓았고, 이렇게 홀로 남겨진 아스톨포는 자신의 창이 부러진 터라, 그 안에 어떤 보물이 깃들어 있는지도 모른 채 마법의 창을 제 것 대신 차지하여 시합으로 돌아왔다."], ["Charlemagne, finding the lady and her brother gone, ordered the jousting to proceed as at first intended, in which Astolpho, by aid of the enchanted lance, unhorsed all comers against him, equally to their astonishment and his own.", "카롤루스는 그 귀부인과 그녀의 오라비가 떠난 것을 알고는, 본래 의도한 대로 시합을 진행하라 명했고, 그 시합에서 아스톨포는 마법의 창의 도움으로 자신에게 맞서는 모든 자를 낙마시켰으니, 상대들도 그 자신도 똑같이 놀라워했다."]], [["The paladin Rinaldo, on learning the issue of the combat of Ferrau and the stranger, galloped after the fair fugitive in an agony of love and impatience.", "팔라딘 리날도는 페라우와 그 낯선 자의 싸움이 어찌 되었는지 알게 되자, 사랑과 조바심에 사로잡혀 그 아름다운 도망자를 뒤쫓아 말을 몰았다."], ["Orlando, perceiving his disappearance, pushed forth in like manner; and, at length, all three are in the forest of Arden, hunting about for her who is invisible.", "오를란도는 그가 사라진 것을 알아채고 마찬가지로 뒤따라 나섰으며, 마침내 세 사람 모두 아르덴 숲에서, 보이지 않는 그녀를 찾아 헤매게 되었다."]], [["Now in this forest there were two fountains, the one constructed by the sage Merlin, who designed it for Tristram and the fair Isoude;[73] for such was the virtue of this fountain, that a draught of its waters produced an oblivion of the love which the drinker might feel, and even produced aversion for the object formerly beloved.", "그런데 이 숲에는 두 개의 샘이 있었는데, 하나는 현자 멀린이 만든 것으로, 그는 트리스트람과 아름다운 이주드를 위해 그것을 마련했다.[73] 이 샘에는 그 물을 한 모금 마시면, 마신 이가 느끼던 사랑을 잊게 되고, 심지어 전에 사랑하던 대상에 대한 혐오마저 일으키는 효능이 있었기 때문이다."], ["The other fountain was endowed with exactly opposite qualities, and a draught of it inspired love for the first living object that was seen after tasting it.", "다른 샘은 정반대의 성질을 지니고 있어, 그 물을 한 모금 마시면 마신 뒤 처음 본 살아 있는 대상을 향한 사랑을 불러일으켰다."], ["Rinaldo happened to come to the first mentioned fountain, and, being flushed with heat, dismounted, and quenched in one draught both his thirst and his passion.", "리날도는 우연히 앞서 말한 샘에 이르러, 더위로 달아오른 터라 말에서 내려, 한 모금으로 갈증과 사랑을 함께 가셨다."], ["So far from loving Angelica as before he hated her from the bottom of his heart, became disgusted with the search he was upon, and, feeling fatigued with his ride, finding a sheltered and flowery nook, laid himself down and fell asleep.", "그는 전처럼 안젤리카를 사랑하기는커녕 마음속 깊이 그녀를 미워하게 되었고, 자신이 벌이고 있는 추적에 진절머리가 났으며, 말을 타느라 지친 데다 아늑하고 꽃이 만발한 후미진 곳을 찾아내자, 그곳에 드러누워 잠이 들었다."]], [["Shortly after came Angelica, but, approaching in a different direction, she espied the other fountain, and there quenched her thirst.", "얼마 뒤 안젤리카가 왔는데, 그녀는 다른 방향에서 다가오다가 다른 샘을 발견하여 그곳에서 갈증을 가셨다."], ["Then resuming her way, she came upon the sleeping Rinaldo.", "그런 다음 다시 길을 가다가, 잠들어 있는 리날도와 마주쳤다."], ["Love instantly seized her, and she stood rooted to the spot.", "사랑이 곧장 그녀를 사로잡았고, 그녀는 그 자리에 뿌리박힌 듯 서 있었다."]], [["The meadow round was all full of lilies of the valley and wild roses.", "둘레의 풀밭은 온통 은방울꽃과 들장미로 가득했다."], ["Angelica, not knowing what to do, at length plucked a handful of these, and dropped them, one by one, on the face of the sleeper.", "어찌할 바를 모르던 안젤리카는 마침내 그 꽃을 한 움큼 따서, 잠든 이의 얼굴 위에 하나씩 떨어뜨렸다."], ["He woke up, and, seeing who it was, received her salutations with averted countenance, remounted his horse, and galloped away.", "그는 잠에서 깨어, 그녀가 누구인지를 보고는, 얼굴을 돌린 채 그녀의 인사를 받고, 다시 말에 올라 내달렸다."], ["In vain the beautiful creature followed and called after him, in vain asked him what she had done to be so despised.", "그 아름다운 여인이 그를 뒤따르며 불러 보아도 헛일이었고, 자기가 무엇을 했기에 그토록 멸시받는지 물어보아도 헛일이었다."], ["Rinaldo disappeared, leaving her in despair, and she returned in tears to the spot where she had found him sleeping.", "리날도는 그녀를 절망 속에 남겨 둔 채 사라졌고, 그녀는 눈물을 흘리며 그가 잠들어 있던 자리로 돌아왔다."], ["There, in her turn, she herself lay down, pressing the spot of earth on which he had lain, and, out of fatigue and sorrow, fell asleep.", "그곳에서 이번에는 그녀가 직접 드러누워, 그가 누웠던 땅바닥을 끌어안았고, 피로와 슬픔에 겨워 잠이 들었다."]], [["As Angelica thus lay, fortune conducted Orlando to the same place.", "안젤리카가 그렇게 누워 있는 동안, 운명은 오를란도를 바로 그곳으로 인도했다."], ["The attitude in which she was sleeping was so lovely that it is not to be conceived, much less expressed.", "그녀가 잠든 모습은 어찌나 사랑스러웠던지 상상조차 할 수 없을 정도였으니, 말로 표현하기란 더더욱 불가능했다."], ["Orlando stood gazing like a man who had been transported to another sphere.", "오를란도는 다른 세계로 옮겨진 사람처럼 멍하니 응시하며 서 있었다. \"내가 땅 위에 있는 것인가,\""], ["“Am I on earth,” he exclaimed, “or am I in Paradise?", "하고 그가 외쳤다, \"아니면 낙원에 있는 것인가?"], ["Surely it is I that sleep, and this is my dream.”", "분명 잠든 것은 나이고, 이것은 내 꿈이로구나.\""]], [["But his dream was proved to be none in a manner which he little desired.", "그러나 그의 꿈은 그가 조금도 바라지 않던 방식으로 꿈이 아님이 드러났다."], ["Ferrau, who had slain Argalia, came up, raging with jealousy, and a combat ensued which awoke the sleeper.", "아르갈리아를 죽인 페라우가, 질투로 길길이 날뛰며 다가왔고, 싸움이 벌어져 잠든 여인을 깨웠다."]], [["Terrified at what she beheld, she rushed to her palfrey, and, while the fighters were occupied with one another, fled away through the forest.", "자기가 본 광경에 겁먹은 그녀는 자신의 작은 말에게로 달려갔고, 두 싸움꾼이 서로에게 정신이 팔려 있는 사이에, 숲을 가로질러 달아났다."], ["The champions continued their fight till they were interrupted by a messenger, who brought word to Ferrau that king Marsilius, his sovereign, was in pressing need of his assistance, and conjured him to return to Spain.", "두 투사는 한 전령이 그들을 가로막을 때까지 싸움을 계속했는데, 전령은 페라우에게 그의 군주인 마르실리우스 왕이 그의 도움을 절실히 필요로 하고 있으니 에스파냐로 돌아와 달라고 간청한다는 말을 전했다."], ["Ferrau, upon this, proposed to suspend the combat, to which Orlando, eager to pursue Angelica, agreed.", "이에 페라우는 싸움을 멈추자고 제안했고, 안젤리카를 뒤쫓고 싶어 안달이 난 오를란도는 이에 동의했다."], ["Ferrau, on the other hand, departed with the messenger to Spain.", "한편 페라우는 전령과 함께 에스파냐로 떠났다."]], [["Orlando’s quest for the fair fugitive was all in vain.", "그 아름다운 도망자를 찾으려는 오를란도의 추적은 모두 헛수고였다."], ["Aided by the powers of magic, she made a speedy return to her own country.", "안젤리카는 마법의 힘에 힘입어 자기 나라로 잽싸게 돌아갔다."]], [["But the thought of Rinaldo could not be banished from her mind, and she determined to set Malagigi at liberty, and to employ him to win Rinaldo, if possible, to make her a return of affection.", "그러나 리날도에 대한 생각은 그녀의 마음에서 떨쳐지지 않았고, 그녀는 말라지지를 풀어 주어, 가능하다면 그를 시켜 리날도가 자신에게 애정을 되돌려 주게끔 그의 마음을 얻기로 결심했다."], ["She accordingly freed him from his dungeon, unlocking his fetters with her own hands, and restored him his book, promising him ample honors and rewards on condition of his bringing Rinaldo to her feet.", "그리하여 그녀는 자신의 손으로 그의 족쇄를 풀어 그를 지하 감옥에서 자유롭게 해 주고, 그의 책을 돌려주면서, 리날도를 자신의 발치로 데려온다는 조건으로 그에게 넉넉한 명예와 보상을 약속했다."]], [["Malagigi accordingly, with the aid of his book, called up a demon, mounted him, and departed.", "이에 따라 말라지지는 자신의 책의 도움으로 악마 하나를 불러내어 그 위에 올라타고 떠났다."], ["Arrived at his destination, he inveigled Rinaldo into an enchanted bark, which conveyed him, without any visible pilot, to an island where stood an edifice called Joyous Castle.", "목적지에 다다른 그는 리날도를 꾀어 마법의 작은 배에 태웠는데, 그 배는 눈에 보이는 사공도 없이 그를 어느 섬으로 실어 갔으니, 그 섬에는 즐거운 성이라 불리는 건축물이 서 있었다."], ["The whole island was a garden.", "섬 전체가 정원이었다."], ["On the western side, close to the sea, was the palace, built of marble, so clear and polished that it reflected the landscape about it.", "서편, 바다에 가까운 곳에 그 궁전이 있었는데, 대리석으로 지어져 어찌나 맑고 윤이 났던지 둘레의 풍경을 비출 정도였다."], ["Rinaldo leapt ashore, and soon met a lady, who invited him to enter.", "리날도는 뭍에 올라, 이내 한 귀부인을 만났고, 그녀는 그에게 안으로 들기를 청했다."], ["The house was as beautiful within as without, full of rooms adorned with azure and gold, and with noble paintings.", "그 집은 안도 밖만큼이나 아름다워서, 하늘빛과 황금빛으로, 그리고 고귀한 그림들로 꾸며진 방들로 가득했다."], ["The lady led the knight into an apartment painted with stories, and opening to the garden, through pillars of crystal, with golden capitals.", "귀부인은 기사를 이야기들이 그려져 있고, 황금빛 머리 장식을 얹은 수정 기둥들을 통해 정원으로 트인 한 방으로 이끌었다."], ["Here he found a bevy of ladies, three of whom were singing in concert, while another played on an instrument of exquisite accord, and the rest danced round about them.", "그곳에서 그는 한 무리의 귀부인들을 발견했는데, 그중 셋은 화음을 맞춰 노래하고 있었고, 또 한 사람은 더없이 절묘하게 어우러지는 악기를 연주하고 있었으며, 나머지는 그들 둘레로 춤을 추고 있었다."], ["When the ladies beheld him coming they turned the dance into a circuit round him, and then one of them, in the sweetest manner, said, “Sir knight, the tables are set, and the hour for the banquet is come;”", "귀부인들은 그가 오는 것을 보고는 춤을 그를 둘러싸는 원무로 바꾸었고, 그런 다음 그중 한 사람이 더없이 감미로운 태도로 말했다. \"기사님, 식탁이 차려졌고, 연회의 시간이 왔나이다.\""], ["and, with these words, still dancing, they drew him across the lawn in front of the apartment, to a table that was spread with cloth of gold and fine linen, under a bower of damask roses by the side of a fountain.", "이 말과 함께, 여전히 춤을 추면서, 그들은 그를 방 앞 잔디밭을 가로질러, 황금 천과 고운 아마포가 깔린 식탁으로 이끌었으니, 그 식탁은 샘 곁의 다마스크 장미 정자 아래 차려져 있었다."]], [["Four ladies were already seated there, who rose, and placed Rinaldo at their head, in a chair set with pearls.", "그곳에는 이미 네 명의 귀부인이 앉아 있다가, 일어서서 리날도를 진주로 장식된 의자에 그들의 상석으로 앉혔다."], ["And truly indeed was he astonished.", "참으로 그는 놀라움을 금치 못했다."], ["A repast ensued, consisting of viands the most delicate, and wines as fragrant as they were fine, drunk out of jewelled cups; and, when it drew towards its conclusion, harps and lutes were heard in the distance, and one of the ladies said in the knight’s ear: “This house and all that you see in it are yours; for you alone was it built, and the builder is a queen.", "더없이 정교한 음식들과, 빼어난 만큼이나 향기로운 포도주로 이루어진 연회가 보석으로 꾸민 잔에 담겨 펼쳐졌고, 연회가 끝나갈 무렵 멀리서 하프와 류트 소리가 들려왔는데, 귀부인들 가운데 한 사람이 기사의 귓가에 대고 말했다. \"이 집과 여기서 보시는 모든 것이 그대의 것이옵니다."], ["Happy indeed must you think yourself, for she loves you, and she is the greatest beauty in the world!", "이는 오로지 그대만을 위해 지어졌고, 그것을 지은 이는 여왕이옵니다. 참으로 그대는 스스로를 행복하다 여기셔야 하리니, 그녀가 그대를 사랑하며, 그녀는 세상에서 가장 빼어난 미녀이기 때문이옵니다!"], ["Her name is Angelica.”", "그녀의 이름은 안젤리카이옵니다.\""]], [["The moment Rinaldo heard the name he so detested he started up, with a changed countenance, and, in spite of all that the lady could say, broke off across the garden, and never ceased hastening till he reached the place where he landed.", "리날도는 그토록 혐오하던 그 이름을 듣는 순간 안색이 변하여 벌떡 일어섰고, 귀부인이 무슨 말을 하든 아랑곳없이 정원을 가로질러 뛰쳐나가, 자기가 뭍에 오른 곳에 이르기까지 한순간도 멈추지 않고 서둘렀다."], ["The bark was still on the shore.", "작은 배는 아직 물가에 있었다."], ["He sprang into it, and pushed off, though he saw nobody in it but himself.", "그는 배 안으로 뛰어들어 노를 저어 나아갔는데, 배 안에는 자기 말고는 아무도 보이지 않았다."], ["It was in vain for him to try to control its movements, for it dashed on as if in fury, till it reached a distant shore covered with a gloomy forest.", "그가 배의 움직임을 다스려 보려 해도 헛일이었으니, 배는 마치 격분한 듯 내달려, 음울한 숲으로 뒤덮인 머나먼 물가에 이르렀다."], ["Here Rinaldo, surrounded by enchantments of a very different sort from those which he had lately resisted, was entrapped into a pit.", "이곳에서 리날도는 조금 전에 물리쳤던 것과는 전혀 다른 종류의 마법에 둘러싸인 채, 함정 구덩이에 빠지고 말았다."]], [["The pit belonged to a castle called Altaripa, which was hung with human heads, and painted red with blood.", "그 구덩이는 알타리파라 불리는 성에 딸린 것이었는데, 그 성은 사람의 머리들이 매달려 있고 피로 붉게 칠해져 있었다."], ["As the paladin was viewing the scene with amazement a hideous old woman made her appearance at the edge of the pit, and told him that he was destined to be thrown to a monster, who was only kept from devastating the whole country by being supplied with living human flesh.", "팔라딘이 놀라움에 차서 그 광경을 바라보고 있을 때, 흉측한 노파 하나가 구덩이 가장자리에 나타나, 그가 어느 괴물에게 던져질 운명이라고 말했다. 그 괴물은 살아 있는 사람의 살을 공급받음으로써만 온 나라를 황폐하게 만드는 것을 막을 수 있는 짐승이었다."], ["Rinaldo said, “Be it so; let me but remain armed as I am, and I fear nothing.”", "리날도가 말했다. \"그렇다 치자."], ["The old woman laughed in derision.", "다만 내가 지금 이대로 무장한 채 있을 수만 있다면, 나는 아무것도 두렵지 않다.\""], ["Rinaldo remained in the pit all night, and the next morning was taken to the place where the monster had his den.", "노파는 비웃으며 웃었다. 리날도는 밤새도록 구덩이 안에 있었고, 이튿날 아침 괴물이 굴을 튼 곳으로 끌려갔다."], ["It was a court surrounded by a high wall.", "그곳은 높은 벽으로 둘러싸인 안뜰이었다."], ["Rinaldo was shut in with the beast, and a terrible combat ensued.", "리날도는 그 짐승과 함께 갇혔고, 끔찍한 싸움이 벌어졌다."], ["Rinaldo was unable to make any impression on the scales of the monster, while he, on the contrary, with his dreadful claws, tore away plate and mail from the paladin.", "리날도는 괴물의 비늘에 아무런 자국도 낼 수 없었던 반면, 괴물은 그 무시무시한 발톱으로 팔라딘에게서 판금과 사슬 갑옷을 찢어 냈다."], ["Rinaldo began to think his last hour was come, and cast his eyes around and above to see if there was any means of escape.", "리날도는 자신의 마지막 순간이 왔다고 생각하기 시작했고, 빠져나갈 길이 있는지 보려고 사방과 위를 둘러보았다."], ["He perceived a beam projecting from the wall at the height of some ten feet, and, taking a leap almost miraculous, he succeeded in reaching it, and in flinging himself up across it.", "그는 벽에서 열 자쯤 되는 높이에 들보 하나가 튀어나와 있는 것을 발견하고는, 거의 기적과도 같은 도약으로 그것에 닿는 데 성공하여, 몸을 날려 그 위에 가로걸쳤다."], ["Here he sat for hours, the hideous brute continually trying to reach him.", "이곳에서 그는 몇 시간이고 앉아 있었는데, 그 흉측한 짐승은 끊임없이 그에게 닿으려 애썼다."], ["All at once he heard the sound of something coming through the air like a bird, and suddenly Angelica herself alighted on the end of the beam.", "그러던 차에 별안간 새처럼 무언가가 공중을 가르며 다가오는 소리가 들리더니, 느닷없이 안젤리카가 직접 들보 끝에 내려앉았다."], ["She held something in her hand towards him, and spoke to him in a loving voice.", "그녀는 손에 무언가를 들고 그에게 내밀며, 사랑스러운 목소리로 그에게 말을 건넸다."], ["But the moment Rinaldo saw her he commanded her to go away, refused all her offers of assistance, and at length declared that, if she did not leave him, he would cast himself down to the monster, and meet his fate.", "그러나 리날도는 그녀를 보는 순간 물러가라 명하며, 그녀가 베푸는 모든 도움을 거절했고, 마침내는 만일 그녀가 자기를 떠나지 않으면 괴물에게 몸을 던져 운명을 맞겠다고 단언했다."], ["Angelica, saying she would lose her life rather than displease him, departed; but first she threw to the monster a cake of wax she had prepared, and spread around him a rope knotted with nooses.", "안젤리카는 그를 언짢게 하느니 차라리 목숨을 잃겠노라 말하며 떠났다. 그러나 떠나기에 앞서 그녀는 미리 마련해 둔 밀랍 덩어리 하나를 괴물에게 던져 주고, 그 둘레에 올가미 매듭을 지은 밧줄을 펼쳐 놓았다."], ["The beast took the bait, and, finding his teeth glued together by the wax, vented his fury in bounds and leaps, and, soon getting entangled in the nooses, drew them tight by his struggles, so that he could scarcely move a limb.", "짐승은 그 미끼를 물었고, 이빨이 밀랍으로 들러붙은 것을 알아채자, 펄쩍펄쩍 뛰며 분풀이를 하다가 이내 올가미에 얽혀, 몸부림칠수록 매듭을 단단히 조여, 사지를 거의 움직일 수 없게 되었다. 리날도는 기회를 엿보다가 그 등 위로 뛰어내려, 목을 끌어안고 졸랐으며, 짐승이 죽어 쓰러질 때까지 손아귀를 늦추지 않았다."]], [["Rinaldo, watching his chance, leapt down upon his back, seized him round the neck, and throttled him, not relaxing his grip till the beast fell dead.", "또 하나의 난관이 남아 있었다."], ["Another difficulty remained to be overcome. The walls were of immense height, and the only opening in them was a grated window of such strength that he could not break the bars.", "벽은 어마어마하게 높았고, 그 안에서 유일하게 트인 곳이라고는 어찌나 튼튼한지 창살을 부술 수 없는 격자창 하나뿐이었다."], ["In his distress Rinaldo found a file, which Angelica had left on the ground, and, with the help of this, effected his deliverance.", "곤경에 처한 리날도는 안젤리카가 바닥에 남겨 둔 줄칼 하나를 발견했고, 그 도움으로 탈출을 이루어 냈다."]], [["What further adventures he met with will be told in another chapter.", "그가 또 어떤 모험을 겪었는지는 다음 장에서 이야기하겠다."]], [["————", "————"]], [["# THE SIEGE OF ALBRACCA", "# THE SIEGE OF ALBRACCA"]], [["At the very time when Charlemagne was holding his plenary court and his great tournament his kingdom was invaded by a mighty monarch, who was moreover so valiant and strong in battle that no one could stand against him.", "알브라카 공방전\n카롤루스가 전체 어전 회의와 대마상 시합을 열고 있던 바로 그 무렵, 그의 왕국은 한 강대한 군주에게 침공당했는데, 그 군주는 게다가 전투에서 어찌나 용맹하고 강했던지 아무도 그를 당해 낼 수 없었다."], ["He was named Gradasso, and his kingdom was called Sericane.", "그의 이름은 그라다소였고, 그의 왕국은 세리카네라 불렸다."], ["Now, as it often happens to the greatest and the richest to long for what they cannot have, and thus to lose what they already possess, this king could not rest content without Durindana, the sword of Orlando, and Bayard, the horse of Rinaldo.", "그런데 가장 위대하고 가장 부유한 자들이 흔히 가질 수 없는 것을 갈망하다가 이미 가진 것마저 잃곤 하듯이, 이 왕은 오를란도의 칼 두린다나와 리날도의 말 바이아르를 갖지 못하고서는 도무지 만족할 수 없었다."], ["To obtain these he determined to war upon France, and for this purpose put in array a mighty army.", "이것들을 손에 넣으려고 그는 프랑스를 상대로 전쟁을 벌이기로 작정하고, 이를 위해 강대한 군대를 정렬시켰다."]], [["He took his way through Spain, and, after defeating Marsilius, the king of that country, in several battles, was rapidly advancing on France.", "그는 에스파냐를 거쳐 진군했고, 그 나라의 왕 마르실리우스를 여러 차례의 전투에서 무찌른 뒤, 빠르게 프랑스로 진격해 들어왔다."], ["Charlemagne, though Marsilius was a Saracen, and had been his enemy, yet felt it needful to succor him in this extremity from a consideration of common danger, and, with the consent of his peers, despatched Rinaldo with a strong body of soldiers against Gradasso.", "카롤루스는 마르실리우스가 사라센이며 자신의 적이었음에도, 공동의 위험을 고려하여 이 위급한 처지에서 그를 도울 필요가 있다고 느꼈고, 동료 기사들의 동의를 얻어 강력한 병력과 함께 리날도를 그라다소에게 맞서도록 파견했다."]], [["There was much fighting, with doubtful results, and Gradasso was steadily advancing into France.", "승부를 가리기 어려운 격전이 거듭되었고, 그라다소는 꾸준히 프랑스 안으로 진격했다. 그러나 자신의 목적을 이루고 싶어 안달이 난 그는, 리날도에게 도보로, 그리고 다음 조건으로 일대일 결투를 청했다."], ["But, impatient to achieve his objects, he challenged Rinaldo to single combat, to be fought on foot, and upon these conditions: If Rinaldo conquered, Gradasso agreed to give up all his prisoners and return to his own country; but if Gradasso won the day, he was to have Bayard.", "만일 리날도가 이기면, 그라다소는 모든 포로를 풀어 주고 자기 나라로 돌아가기로 했다. 그러나 만일 그라다소가 그날 이기면, 그는 바이아르를 차지하기로 했다."]], [["The challenge was accepted, and would have been fought had it not been for the arts of Malagigi, who just then returned from Angelica’s kingdom with set purpose to win Rinaldo to look with favor upon the fair princess who was dying for love of him.", "도전은 받아들여졌고, 만일 말라지지의 술법이 없었더라면 결투가 벌어졌을 것이다. 말라지지는 마침 그때 안젤리카의 왕국에서 돌아온 참이었는데, 자신을 사랑하다 죽어 가는 그 아름다운 공주를 리날도가 호의로 바라보도록 만들겠다는 작정을 품고 있었다."], ["Malagigi drew Rinaldo away from the army by putting on the semblance of Gradasso, and, after a short contest, pretending to fly before him, by which means Rinaldo was induced to follow him into a boat, in which he was borne away, and entangled in various adventures, as we have already related.", "말라지지는 그라다소의 모습으로 변장하여 리날도를 군대에서 꾀어냈고, 짧은 다툼 끝에 그의 앞에서 달아나는 척했으니, 이로써 리날도는 그를 따라 한 배에 오르도록 유인되었고, 그 배에 실려 가 우리가 이미 이야기한 대로 갖가지 모험에 얽혀들게 되었다."]], [["The army, left under the command of Ricciardetto, Rinaldo’s brother, was soon joined by Charlemagne and all his peerage, but experienced a disastrous rout, and the Emperor and many of his paladins were taken prisoners.", "리날도의 형제 리치아르데토의 지휘 아래 남겨진 군대는 곧 카롤루스와 그의 모든 동료 기사들과 합세했으나, 처참한 패주를 겪었고, 황제와 그의 많은 팔라딘들이 포로로 잡혔다."], ["Gradasso, however, did not abuse his victory; he took Charles by the hand, seated him by his side, and told him he warred only for honor.", "그러나 그라다소는 자신의 승리를 함부로 이용하지 않았다. 그는 카롤루스의 손을 잡아 자기 곁에 앉히고, 자기는 오로지 명예를 위해 싸울 뿐이라고 말했다."], ["He renounced all conquests, on condition that the Emperor should deliver to him Bayard and Durindana, both of them the property of his vassals, the former of which, as he maintained, was already forfeited to him by Rinaldo’s failure to meet him as agreed.", "그는 황제가 자신에게 바이아르와 두린다나를 넘긴다는 조건으로 모든 정복을 포기했으니, 그 둘 다 황제의 봉신들의 소유물이었고, 그중 앞의 것은, 그의 주장에 따르면, 리날도가 약속대로 자신을 만나러 나오지 않은 탓에 이미 자신에게 몰수된 것이었다."], ["To these terms Charlemagne readily acceded.", "카롤루스는 이 조건에 선뜻 응했다."]], [["Bayard, after the departure of his master, had been taken in charge by Ricciardetto, and sent back to Paris, where Astolpho was in command, in the absence of Charlemagne.", "바이아르는 주인이 떠난 뒤 리치아르데토의 보호를 받아, 카롤루스가 없는 사이 아스톨포가 지휘하고 있던 파리로 돌려보내진 터였다."], ["Astolpho received with great indignation the message despatched for Bayard, and replied by a herald that “he would not surrender the horse of his kinsman Rinaldo without a contest.", "아스톨포는 바이아르를 내놓으라고 보내온 전갈을 크게 분개하며 받았고, 전령을 통해 이렇게 답했다. \"나는 내 친족 리날도의 말을 싸움 없이는 내주지 않겠다."], ["If Gradasso wanted the steed he might come and take him, and that he, Astolpho, was ready to meet him in the field.”", "그라다소가 그 말을 원한다면 와서 가져가 보라지. 나, 아스톨포는 들판에서 그를 맞을 준비가 되어 있다.\""]], [["Gradasso was only amused at this answer, for Astolpho’s fame as a successful warrior was not high, and Gradasso willingly renewed with him the bargain which he had made with Rinaldo.", "그라다소는 이 대답을 그저 우습게만 여겼으니, 성공한 전사로서의 아스톨포의 명성이 그리 높지 않았던 까닭이며, 그라다소는 리날도와 맺었던 흥정을 그와 기꺼이 다시 맺었다."], ["On these conditions the battle was fought.", "이 조건으로 싸움이 벌어졌다."], ["The enchanted lance, in the hands of Astolpho, performed a new wonder; and Gradasso, the terrible Gradasso, was unhorsed.", "아스톨포의 손에 들린 마법의 창은 새로운 기적을 일으켰고, 그라다소, 그 무시무시한 그라다소가 낙마하고 말았다."]], [["He kept his word, set free his prisoners, and put his army on the march to return to his own country, renewing his oath, however, not to rest till he had taken from Rinaldo his horse, and from Orlando his sword, or lost his life in the attempt.", "그는 약속을 지켜, 자신의 포로들을 풀어 주고, 군대를 자기 나라로 돌아가도록 행군시켰으나, 리날도에게서 그의 말을, 그리고 오를란도에게서 그의 칼을 빼앗기 전에는, 아니면 그 시도 끝에 목숨을 잃기 전에는 결코 쉬지 않겠노라 맹세를 새로이 했다."]], [["Charlemagne, full of gratitude to Astolpho, would have kept him near his person and loaded him with honors, but Astolpho preferred to seek Rinaldo, with the view of restoring to him his horse, and departed from Paris with that design.", "아스톨포에게 감사하는 마음으로 가득 찬 카롤루스는 그를 자기 곁에 두고 온갖 명예를 안겨 주고 싶어 했으나, 아스톨포는 리날도를 찾아 그의 말을 돌려주고자 했기에, 그런 작정으로 파리를 떠났다."]], [["————", "————"]], [["Our story now returns to Orlando, whom we left fascinated with the sight of the sleeping beauty, who, however, escaped him while engaged in the combat with Ferrau.", "이제 우리의 이야기는 오를란도에게로 돌아간다. 우리는 그가 잠든 미녀를 보고 매혹되었던 데서 그를 떠나왔는데, 그러나 그녀는 그가 페라우와 싸움을 벌이는 사이에 그에게서 달아나 버렸다."], ["Having long sought her in vain through the recesses of the wood, he resolved to follow her to her father’s court.", "오랫동안 숲속 깊은 곳을 헤매며 그녀를 찾았으나 헛일이었던지라, 그는 그녀를 따라 그녀의 아버지의 궁정으로 가기로 결심했다."], ["Leaving, therefore, the camp of Charlemagne, he travelled long in the direction of the East, making inquiry everywhere, if, perchance, he might get tidings of the fugitive.", "그리하여 카롤루스의 진영을 떠나, 그는 오랫동안 동쪽 방향으로 여행하며, 혹여 그 도망자의 소식을 얻을 수 있을까 하여 곳곳에서 물어보았다."], ["After many adventures, he arrived one day at a place where many roads crossed, and meeting there a courier, he asked him for news.", "숱한 모험을 겪은 끝에, 그는 어느 날 여러 길이 교차하는 곳에 이르러, 그곳에서 한 전령을 만나 소식을 물었다."], ["The courier replied that he had been despatched by Angelica to solicit the aid of Sacripant, king of Circassia, in favor of her father Galafron, who was besieged in his city, Albracca, by Agrican, king of Tartary.", "전령의 대답에 따르면, 자신은 안젤리카의 명을 받아 체르카시아 왕 사크리판트의 도움을 청하러 파견되었는데, 이는 그녀의 아버지 갈라프론을 위함이라 했다. 갈라프론은 타르타리아 왕 아그리칸에게 자신의 도시 알브라카에서 포위당해 있었던 것이다."], ["This Agrican had been an unsuccessful suitor to the damsel, whom he now pursued with arms.", "이 아그리칸은 그 처녀에게 청혼했다가 거절당한 자로, 이제는 무력으로 그녀를 쫓고 있었다."], ["Orlando thus learned that he was within a day’s journey of Albracca; and, feeling now secure of Angelica, he proceeded with all speed to her city.", "이리하여 오를란도는 자신이 알브라카에서 하룻길 안에 와 있음을 알게 되었고, 이제 안젤리카를 만나리라 확신하며 온 힘을 다해 그녀의 도시로 나아갔다."]], [["Thus journeying he arrived at a bridge, under which flowed a foaming river.", "이렇게 여행하다가 그는 한 다리에 이르렀는데, 그 아래로는 거품 이는 강이 흐르고 있었다."], ["Here a damsel met him with a goblet, and informed him that it was the usage of this bridge to present the traveller with a cup.", "이곳에서 한 처녀가 술잔 하나를 들고 그를 맞으며, 이 다리의 관례는 길손에게 잔을 권하는 것이라고 알려 주었다."], ["Orlando accepted the offered cup and drank its contents.", "오를란도는 내미는 잔을 받아 그 안의 것을 마셨다."], ["He had no sooner done so than his brain reeled, and he became unconscious of the object of his journey, and of everything else.", "그가 그렇게 하자마자 머리가 빙빙 돌더니, 자신이 떠나온 여행의 목적도, 다른 모든 것도 잊어버리게 되었다."], ["Under the influence of this fascination he followed the damsel into a magnificent and marvellous palace.", "이 마법의 영향 아래 그는 처녀를 따라 웅장하고도 경이로운 궁전으로 들어갔다."], ["Here he found himself in company with many knights, unknown to him and to each other, though if it had not been for the Cup of Oblivion of which they all had partaken they would have found themselves brothers in arms.", "이곳에서 그는 자신도 모르고 서로도 모르는 많은 기사들과 함께 있게 되었으니, 그들 모두가 마신 망각의 잔만 아니었더라면 서로가 전우임을 알아보았을 것이었다."]], [["————", "————"]], [["Astolpho, proceeding on his way to seek Rinaldo, splendidly dressed and equipped, as was his wont, arrived in Circassia, and found there a great army encamped under the command of Sacripant, the king of that country, who was leading it to the defence of Galafron, the father of Angelica.", "리날도를 찾으러 길을 가던 아스톨포는, 늘 그렇듯 화려하게 차려입고 채비를 갖춘 채 체르카시아에 다다랐고, 그곳에서 그 나라의 왕 사크리판트의 지휘 아래 진을 친 대군을 발견했는데, 사크리판트는 안젤리카의 아버지 갈라프론을 지키러 그 군대를 이끌고 가는 중이었다."], ["Sacripant, much struck by the appearance of Astolpho and his horse, accosted him courteously, and tried to enlist him in his service; but Astolpho, proud of his late victories, scornfully declined his offers, and pursued his way.", "사크리판트는 아스톨포와 그의 말의 모습에 크게 감탄하여 그에게 정중히 말을 건네며 자신의 휘하에 들이려 했으나, 최근의 승리로 우쭐해진 아스톨포는 그의 제안을 경멸하듯 거절하고 제 갈 길을 갔다."], ["King Sacripant was too much attracted by his appearance to part with him so easily, and having laid aside his kingly ornaments, set out in pursuit of him.", "사크리판트 왕은 그의 모습에 너무도 마음이 끌린 나머지 그를 그리 쉽게 보내 줄 수가 없어, 왕의 장신구들을 벗어 두고 그를 뒤쫓아 나섰다."]], [["Astolpho next day encountered on his way a stranger knight, named Sir Florismart, Lord of the Sylvan Tower, one of the bravest and best of knights, having as his guide a damsel, young, fair, and virtuous, to whom he was tenderly attached, whose name was Flordelis.", "아스톨포는 이튿날 길에서 한 낯선 기사를 만났는데, 그 이름은 숲의 탑의 영주 플로리스마르트 경으로, 기사들 가운데 가장 용감하고 가장 훌륭한 이들 축에 드는 사람이었으며, 길잡이로 젊고 아름답고 정숙한 처녀를 데리고 있었으니, 그가 애틋이 사랑하는 그 처녀의 이름은 플로르델리스였다."], ["Astolpho, as he approached, defied the knight, bidding him yield the lady, or prepare to maintain his right by arms.", "아스톨포는 다가서며 그 기사에게 도전하여, 귀부인을 내놓든지 아니면 무력으로 자신의 권리를 지킬 채비를 하라고 했다."], ["Florismart accepted the contest, and the knights encountered.", "플로리스마르트는 결투를 받아들였고, 두 기사가 격돌했다."], ["Florismart was unhorsed and his steed fell dead, while Bayard sustained no injury by the shock.", "플로리스마르트는 낙마했고 그의 말은 쓰러져 죽었으나, 바이아르는 그 충격에도 아무런 상처를 입지 않았다."]], [["Florismart was so overwhelmed with despair at his own disgrace and the sight of the damsel’s distress, that he drew his sword, and was about to plunge it into his own bosom.", "플로리스마르트는 자신의 치욕과 처녀의 비탄하는 모습에 어찌나 절망에 휩싸였던지, 칼을 뽑아 자신의 가슴에 찌르려 했다."], ["But Astolpho held his hand, told him that he contended only for glory, and was contented to leave him the lady.", "그러나 아스톨포가 그의 손을 붙들고는, 자기는 오로지 영광을 위해 겨루었을 뿐이며, 그에게 귀부인을 남겨 두는 것으로 만족한다고 말했다."]], [["While Florismart and Flordelis were vowing eternal gratitude King Sacripant arrived, and coveting the damsel of the one champion as much as the horse and arms of the other, defied them to the joust.", "플로리스마르트와 플로르델리스가 영원한 감사를 맹세하고 있을 때 사크리판트 왕이 다다랐는데, 그는 한 투사의 처녀를 다른 투사의 말과 무구만큼이나 탐내어, 그들에게 시합을 청했다."], ["Astolpho met the challenger, whom he instantly overthrew, and presented his courser to Florismart, leaving the king to return to his army on foot.", "아스톨포는 도전자를 맞아 단숨에 그를 쓰러뜨리고, 자신의 군마를 플로리스마르트에게 내주었으며, 왕은 도보로 자신의 군대로 돌아가게 내버려 두었다."]], [["The friends pursued their route, and ere long Flordelis discovered, by signs which were known to her, that they were approaching the waters of Oblivion, and advised them to turn back, or to change their course.", "두 벗은 길을 이어 갔고, 오래지 않아 플로르델리스는 자기가 아는 표지들을 통해 그들이 망각의 물가에 다가가고 있음을 알아채고는, 발길을 되돌리거나 길을 바꾸라고 권했다."], ["This the knights would not hear of, and, continuing their march, they soon arrived at the bridge where Orlando had been taken prisoner.", "기사들은 이 말을 들으려 하지 않았고, 행군을 계속하다가 곧 오를란도가 포로로 잡혔던 그 다리에 다다랐다."]], [["The damsel of the bridge appeared as before with the enchanted cup, but Astolpho, forewarned, rejected it with scorn.", "다리의 처녀가 앞서처럼 마법의 잔을 들고 나타났으나, 미리 일러 받은 아스톨포는 그것을 경멸하며 물리쳤다."], ["She dashed it to the ground, and a fire blazed up which rendered the bridge unapproachable.", "그녀는 잔을 땅바닥에 내동댕이쳤고, 불길이 타올라 다리에 다가갈 수 없게 되었다."], ["At the same moment the two knights were assailed by sundry warriors, known and unknown, who, having no recollection of anything, joined blindly in defence of their prison-house.", "같은 순간에 두 기사는 아는 이며 모르는 이며 여러 전사들의 습격을 받았으니, 그들은 아무것도 기억하지 못한 채 자신들의 감옥을 지키려고 맹목적으로 가담한 것이었다."], ["Among these was Orlando, at sight of whom Astolpho, with all his confidence not daring to encounter him, turned and fled, owing his escape to the strength and fleetness of Bayard.", "이들 가운데 오를란도가 있었는데, 그를 보자 아스톨포는 그 모든 자신감에도 불구하고 감히 그와 맞설 엄두를 내지 못하고, 돌아서서 달아났으니, 바이아르의 힘과 빠르기 덕에 탈출할 수 있었다."]], [["Florismart, meanwhile, overlaid by fearful odds, was compelled to yield to necessity, and comply with the usage of the fairy.", "한편 플로리스마르트는 두려운 중과부적에 짓눌려, 어쩔 수 없이 필요에 굴복하여 그 요정의 관례를 따랐다."], ["He drank of the cup and remained prisoner with the rest.", "그는 잔을 마시고 나머지 이들과 함께 포로로 남았다."], ["Flordelis, deprived of her two friends, retired from the scene, and devoted herself to untiring efforts to effect her lover’s deliverance.", "두 벗을 잃은 플로르델리스는 그 자리에서 물러나, 자신의 연인을 구해 내려는 지칠 줄 모르는 노력에 몸을 바쳤다."], ["Astolpho pursued his way to Albracca, which Agrican was about to besiege.", "아스톨포는 알브라카로 길을 갔는데, 아그리칸이 막 그곳을 포위하려는 참이었다."], ["He was kindly welcomed by Angelica, and enrolled among her defenders.", "그는 안젤리카에게 정중히 환대받아 그녀의 수비대에 이름을 올렸다."], ["Impatient to distinguish himself, he one night sallied forth alone, arrived in Agrican’s camp, and unhorsed his warriors right and left by means of the enchanted lance.", "두각을 나타내고 싶어 안달이 난 그는 어느 날 밤 홀로 출격하여 아그리칸의 진영에 다다랐고, 마법의 창으로 그의 전사들을 좌우로 낙마시켰다."], ["But he was soon surrounded and overmatched, and made prisoner to Agrican.", "그러나 그는 이내 포위되어 중과부적에 몰렸고, 아그리칸의 포로가 되고 말았다."]], [["Relief was, however, at hand; for as the citizens and soldiers were one day leaning over their walls they descried a cloud of dust, from which horsemen were seen to prick forth, as it rolled on towards the camp of the besiegers.", "그러나 구원이 가까이 있었으니, 어느 날 시민들과 병사들이 성벽 너머로 몸을 내밀고 있을 때, 그들은 한 줄기 흙먼지 구름을 발견했고, 그 구름이 포위군의 진영을 향해 굴러오면서 그 속에서 기병들이 박차고 나오는 것이 보였다."], ["This turned out to be the army of Sacripant, which immediately attacked that of Agrican, with the view of cutting a passage through his camp to the besieged city.", "이는 사크리판트의 군대로 밝혀졌는데, 그 군대는 곧장 아그리칸의 군대를 공격하여, 그의 진영을 뚫고 포위된 도시로 가는 길을 내려 했다."], ["But Agrican, mounted upon Bayard, taken from Astolpho, but not armed with the lance of gold, the virtues of which were unknown to him, performed wonders, and rallied his scattered troops, which had given way to the sudden and unexpected assault.", "그러나 아그리칸은 아스톨포에게서 빼앗은 바이아르에 올라타 있었으되 황금 창은 갖추지 않았으니, 그 창의 효능을 그는 알지 못했던 것이며, 그럼에도 그는 놀라운 활약을 펼쳐, 느닷없이 들이닥친 기습에 무너졌던 자신의 흩어진 군대를 다시 결집시켰다."], ["Sacripant, on the other hand, encouraged his men by the most desperate acts of valor, having as an additional incentive to his courage the sight of Angelica, who showed herself upon the city walls.", "사크리판트는 사크리판트대로 더없이 결사적인 무용으로 자신의 부하들을 북돋웠으니, 그의 용기를 더욱 부추기는 것으로 안젤리카의 모습이 있었는데, 그녀는 성벽 위에 모습을 드러냈던 것이다."], ["There she witnessed a single combat between the two leaders, Agrican and Sacripant.", "그곳에서 그녀는 두 지휘관 아그리칸과 사크리판트 사이의 일대일 결투를 지켜보았다."], ["In this, at length, her defender appeared to be overmatched, when the Circassians broke the ring, and separated the combatants, who were borne asunder in the rush.", "이 결투에서 마침내 그녀의 수호자가 밀리는 듯 보였을 때, 체르카시아인들이 포위망을 깨뜨려 두 싸움꾼을 갈라놓았고, 그들은 그 혼란 속에 서로 떨어져 휩쓸려 갔다."], ["Sacripant, severely wounded, profited by the confusion, and escaped into Albracca, where he was kindly received and carefully tended by Angelica.", "심한 상처를 입은 사크리판트는 그 혼란을 틈타 알브라카 안으로 달아났고, 그곳에서 안젤리카에게 정중히 맞아들여져 정성껏 간호받았다."]], [["The battle continuing, the Circassians were at last put to flight, and, being intercepted between the enemy’s lines and the town, sought for refuge under the walls.", "싸움이 계속되어 마침내 체르카시아인들이 패주하게 되었고, 적의 전열과 도시 사이에 가로막힌 그들은 성벽 아래에서 피난처를 찾았다."], ["Angelica ordered the drawbridge to be let down, and the gates thrown open to the fugitives.", "안젤리카는 도개교를 내리고 성문을 열어 도망자들을 들이도록 명했다."], ["With these Agrican, not distinguished in the crowd, entered the place, driving both Circassians and Cathayans before him, and the portcullis being dropped, he was shut in.", "이들과 함께, 무리 속에서 눈에 띄지 않은 아그리칸이 체르카시아인과 카타이인을 앞으로 몰아붙이며 성안으로 들어왔고, 내리닫이 쇠창살이 떨어지면서 그는 안에 갇히고 말았다."]], [["For a time the terror which he inspired put to flight all opposers, but when at last it came to be known that few or none of his followers had effected an entrance with him, the fugitives rallied and surrounded him on all sides.", "한동안 그가 불러일으킨 공포가 모든 맞서는 자들을 패주시켰으나, 마침내 그의 추종자들 가운데 그와 함께 안으로 들어온 자가 거의 또는 전혀 없다는 것이 알려지자, 도망자들은 다시 결집하여 사방에서 그를 에워쌌다."], ["While he was thus apparently reduced to the last extremities, he was saved by the very circumstance which threatened him with destruction.", "이렇게 그가 겉보기에 막다른 궁지에 몰린 듯했을 때, 그는 그를 파멸로 위협하던 바로 그 상황 덕에 구원받았다."], ["The soldiers of Angelica, closing upon him from all sides, deserted their defences; and his own besieging army entered the city in a part where the wall was broken down.", "사방에서 그에게 좁혀들던 안젤리카의 병사들이 자신들의 방어 진지를 버린 것이며, 한편 그 자신의 포위군은 성벽이 무너져 있던 한쪽으로 도시 안으로 들어온 것이다."]], [["In this way was Agrican rescued, the city taken, and the inhabitants put to the sword.", "이런 식으로 아그리칸은 구출되었고, 도시는 함락되었으며, 주민들은 칼날에 죽임을 당했다."], ["Angelica, however, with some of the knights who were her defenders, among whom was Sacripant, saved herself in the citadel, which was planted upon a rock.", "그러나 안젤리카는, 그녀의 수호자였던 몇몇 기사들과 함께, 그 가운데에는 사크리판트도 있었는데, 바위 위에 세워진 성채로 몸을 피했다."]], [["The fortress was impregnable, but it was scantily victualled, and ill provided with other necessaries.", "그 요새는 난공불락이었으나, 식량이 변변치 못했고 다른 필수품도 빈약하게 갖춰져 있었다."], ["Under these circumstances Angelica announced to those blockaded with her in the citadel her intention to go in quest of assistance, and, having plighted her promise of a speedy return, she set out, with the enchanted ring upon her finger.", "이런 상황에서 안젤리카는 성채 안에 함께 봉쇄된 이들에게 자신이 도움을 구하러 나서겠다는 뜻을 알리고, 속히 돌아오겠다는 약속을 맹세한 뒤, 마법의 반지를 손가락에 끼고 길을 나섰다."], ["Mounted upon her palfrey, the damsel passed through the enemy’s lines, and by sunrise was many miles clear of their encampment.", "자신의 작은 말에 올라탄 처녀는 적의 전열을 지나, 동틀 무렵에는 그들의 진영에서 여러 마일 벗어나 있었다."]], [["It so happened that her road led her near the fatal bridge of Oblivion, and as she approached it she met a damsel weeping bitterly.", "마침 그녀의 길은 그 운명적인 망각의 다리 가까이로 그녀를 이끌었는데, 그곳에 다가가다가 그녀는 비통하게 울고 있는 한 처녀를 만났다."], ["It was Flordelis, whose lover, Florismart, as we have related, had met the fate of Orlando and many more, and fallen a victim to the enchantress of the cup.", "그녀는 플로르델리스였으니, 우리가 이야기한 대로 그녀의 연인 플로리스마르트가 오를란도를 비롯한 많은 이들과 같은 운명을 맞아 잔의 마녀에게 희생되었던 것이다."], ["She related her adventures to Angelica, and conjured her to lend what aid she might to rescue her lord and his companions.", "그녀는 안젤리카에게 자신이 겪은 일을 들려주며, 자신의 주군과 그의 동료들을 구해 낼 수 있도록 힘닿는 대로 도와 달라고 간청했다."], ["Angelica, accordingly, watching her opportunity and aided by her ring, slipped into the castle unseen, when the door was opened to admit a new victim.", "이에 따라 안젤리카는 기회를 엿보다가 반지의 도움을 받아, 새로운 희생자를 들이려고 문이 열렸을 때 들키지 않고 성안으로 미끄러져 들어갔다."], ["Here she speedily disenchanted Orlando and the rest by a touch of her talisman.", "이곳에서 그녀는 자신의 부적을 한 번 갖다 대는 것으로 오를란도와 나머지 이들의 마법을 신속히 풀어 주었다."], ["But Florismart was not there.", "그러나 플로리스마르트는 그곳에 없었다."], ["He had been given up to Falerina, a more powerful enchantress, and was still in durance.", "그는 더 강력한 마녀인 팔레리나에게 넘겨져, 여전히 갇혀 있었던 것이다."], ["Angelica conjured the rescued captives to assist her in the recovery of her kingdom, and all departed together for Albracca.", "안젤리카는 구출된 포로들에게 자신의 왕국을 되찾는 일을 도와 달라고 간청했고, 모두 함께 알브라카를 향해 떠났다."]], [["The arrival of Orlando, with his companions, nine in all, and among the bravest knights of France, changed at once the fortunes of the war.", "오를란도가 그의 동료들, 모두 합쳐 아홉이며 프랑스의 가장 용감한 기사들 축에 드는 이들과 함께 도착하자, 전쟁의 운세는 단번에 뒤바뀌었다."], ["Wherever the great paladin came, pennon and standard fell before him.", "그 위대한 팔라딘이 가는 곳마다 군기와 깃발이 그의 앞에 쓰러졌다."], ["Agrican in vain attempted to rally his troops.", "아그리칸은 헛되이 자신의 군대를 다시 결집시키려 애썼다."], ["Orlando kept constantly in his front, forcing him to attend to nobody else.", "오를란도는 끊임없이 그의 정면에 버티고 서서, 그가 다른 누구에게도 신경 쓸 수 없게 만들었다."], ["The Tartar king at length bethought him of a stratagem.", "마침내 타르타리아 왕은 한 가지 계략을 떠올렸다."], ["He turned his horse, and made a show of flying in despair.", "그는 말머리를 돌려, 절망에 빠져 달아나는 척했다."], ["Orlando dashed after him as he desired, and Agrican fled till he reached a green place in a wood, where there was a fountain.", "오를란도는 그가 바라던 대로 그를 뒤쫓아 내달렸고, 아그리칸은 한 숲속의 푸른 곳에 이르기까지 달아났는데, 그곳에는 샘이 하나 있었다."]], [["The place was beautiful, and the Tartar dismounted to refresh himself at the fountain, but without taking off his helmet, or laying aside any of his armor.", "그곳은 아름다웠고, 타르타리아 왕은 그 샘에서 기운을 차리려 말에서 내렸으나, 투구를 벗거나 갑옷의 어느 부분도 풀어 두지는 않았다."], ["Orlando was quickly at his back, crying out, “So bold, and yet a fugitive!", "오를란도가 곧 그의 뒤에 다다라 소리쳤다. \"그토록 대담하면서도 도망자라니!"], ["How could you fly from a single arm and think to escape?”", "어찌 단 한 사람의 팔에서 달아나 벗어날 수 있으리라 여겼는가?\""]], [["The Tartar king had leaped on his saddle the moment he saw his enemy, and when the paladin had done speaking, he said in a mild voice, “Without doubt you are the best knight I ever encountered, and fain would I leave you untouched for your own sake, if you would cease to hinder me from rallying my people.", "타르타리아 왕은 적을 보는 순간 안장에 뛰어올랐고, 팔라딘이 말을 마치자 부드러운 목소리로 말했다. \"의심할 바 없이 그대는 내가 여태껏 마주친 가장 빼어난 기사요."], ["I pretended to fly, in order to bring you out of the field.", "만일 그대가 내 군대를 다시 결집하는 것을 가로막지 않겠다면, 나는 기꺼이 그대 자신을 위해 그대를 건드리지 않고 떠나고 싶소."], ["If you insist upon fighting I must needs fight and slay you, but I call the sun in the heavens to witness I would rather not.", "내가 달아나는 척한 것은 그대를 싸움터 밖으로 끌어내기 위함이었소. 그대가 한사코 싸우겠다면 나도 싸워 그대를 죽일 수밖에 없겠으나, 나는 하늘의 해를 두고 맹세컨대 차라리 그러고 싶지 않소."], ["I should be very sorry for your death.”", "그대의 죽음은 내게 무척 애석한 일이 될 것이오.\""]], [["The Count Orlando felt pity for so much gallantry, and he said, “The nobler you show yourself the more it grieves me to think that in dying without a knowledge of the true faith you will be lost in the other world.", "오를란도 백작은 그토록 큰 무용에 연민을 느껴 이렇게 말했다. \"그대가 더 고결한 모습을 보일수록, 그대가 참된 믿음을 알지 못한 채 죽어 저세상에서 길을 잃으리라는 생각에 더욱 마음이 아프오."], ["Let me advise you to save body and soul at once.", "그대에게 권하노니, 몸과 영혼을 한꺼번에 구하시오."], ["Receive baptism, and go your way in peace.”", "세례를 받고, 평안히 그대의 길을 가시오.\""]], [["Agrican replied: “I suspect you to be the paladin Orlando.", "아그리칸이 대답했다. \"그대가 팔라딘 오를란도가 아닌가 의심스럽소."], ["If you are I would not lose this opportunity of fighting with you to be king of Paradise.", "만일 그렇다면, 나는 낙원의 왕이 된다 해도 그대와 싸울 이 기회를 놓치고 싶지 않소."], ["Talk to me no more about your things of another world, for you will preach in vain.", "저세상의 일에 관해서는 내게 더 말하지 마시오. 그대가 아무리 설교해도 헛일일 테니."], ["Each of us for himself, and let the sword be umpire.”", "각자 제 몫이 있는 법, 칼이 심판이 되게 합시다.\""]], [["The Saracen drew his sword, boldly advancing upon Orlando, and a combat began, so obstinate and so long, each warrior being a miracle of prowess, that the story says it lasted from noon till night.", "사라센은 칼을 뽑아 들고 대담하게 오를란도에게 다가섰고, 어찌나 끈질기고 어찌나 긴 싸움이 시작되었던지, 두 전사 저마다가 무용의 기적이었던 까닭에, 이야기에 따르면 그 싸움이 정오부터 밤까지 이어졌다고 한다."], ["Orlando then seeing the stars come out was the first to propose a respite.", "그때 오를란도는 별들이 돋아나는 것을 보고 먼저 휴식을 제안했다."]], [["“What are we to do,”", "\"이제 어찌하면 좋겠소,\""], ["said he, “now that daylight has left us?”", "하고 그가 말했다, \"햇빛이 우리를 떠났으니 말이오?\""]], [["Agrican answered readily enough, “Let us repose in this meadow, and renew the combat at dawn.”", "아그리칸은 선뜻 대답했다. \"이 풀밭에서 쉬다가, 동틀 녘에 싸움을 다시 시작합시다.\""]], [["The repose was taken accordingly.", "이에 따라 휴식을 취했다."], ["Each tied up his horse, and reclined himself on the grass, not far from the other, just as if they had been friends, Orlando by the fountain, Agrican beneath a pine.", "저마다 자기 말을 매어 두고, 서로에게서 멀지 않은 곳에 풀밭에 몸을 뉘었으니, 마치 두 사람이 벗이라도 되는 양, 오를란도는 샘 곁에, 아그리칸은 소나무 아래에 누웠다. 맑고 아름다운 밤이었고, 잠을 청하기에 앞서 그들이 함께 이야기를 나누던 중, 기독교 세계의 투사가 하늘을 올려다보며 말했다."], ["It was a beautiful clear night, and, as they talked together before addressing themselves to sleep, the champion of Christendom, looking up at the firmament, said, “That is a fine piece of workmanship, that starry spectacle; God made it all, that moon of silver, and those stars of gold, and the light of day, and the sun,—all for the sake of human kind.”", "\"저것은 훌륭한 솜씨의 작품이오, 저 별들의 장관 말이오. 하느님께서 저 모든 것을 만드셨으니, 저 은빛 달도, 저 황금빛 별들도, 한낮의 빛도, 해도, 모두가 인류를 위함이라오.\""]], [["“You wish, I see, to talk of matters of faith,”", "\"그대가 믿음의 문제를 이야기하고 싶어 하는 것을 알겠소,\""], ["said the Tartar.", "하고 타르타리아 왕이 말했다."], ["“Now I may as well tell you at once that I have no sort of skill in such matters, nor learning of any kind.", "\"이제 그대에게 대뜸 말해 두는 편이 좋겠소만, 나는 그런 문제에 아무런 소양도 없고, 그 어떤 종류의 학식도 없소."], ["I never could learn anything when I was a boy.", "나는 어릴 적에 무엇 하나 배울 수가 없었소."], ["I hated it so that I broke the man’s head who was commissioned to teach me; and it produced such an effect on others that nobody ever afterwards dared so much as show me a book.", "어찌나 그것이 싫었던지, 나를 가르치라고 맡겨진 자의 머리를 깨뜨려 버렸고, 그 일이 다른 이들에게도 어찌나 큰 효과를 미쳤던지, 그 뒤로는 아무도 감히 내게 책 한 권 보여 줄 엄두를 내지 못했소."], ["My boyhood was", "내 어린 시절은"]], [["therefore passed, as it should be, in horsemanship and hunting, and learning to fight.", "그러므로 시간은 마땅히 그래야 하듯 말타기와 사냥, 그리고 싸움을 익히는 데 보냈다."], ["What is the good of a gentleman’s poring all day over a book?", "신사가 온종일 책에 코를 박고 있은들 무슨 소용이 있겠는가?"], ["Prowess to the knight, and preaching to the clergyman, that is my motto.”", "기사에게는 무용을, 성직자에게는 설교를, 이것이 내 신조다.\""]], [["“I acknowledge,”", "\"인정하지요.\" 오를란도가 대답했다."], ["returned Orlando, “that arms are the first consideration of a gentleman; but not at all that he does himself dishonor by knowledge.", "\"신사에게 무기가 첫째가는 고려 사항이라는 것은. 하지만 지식이 신사 자신을 욕되게 한다는 것은 결코 인정할 수 없습니다."], ["On the contrary, knowledge is as great an embellishment of the rest of his attainments, as the flowers are to the meadow before us; and as to the knowledge of his Maker, the man that is without it is no better than a stock or a stone or a brute beast.", "오히려 지식이란, 우리 앞에 펼쳐진 초원에 꽃이 그러하듯, 그가 이룬 다른 모든 성취를 더욱 아름답게 꾸며 주는 것입니다. 그리고 자신을 만든 분에 대한 앎으로 말하자면, 그것이 없는 사람은 나무토막이나 돌멩이, 혹은 짐승보다 나을 것이 없습니다."], ["Neither without study can he reach anything of a due sense of the depth and divineness of the contemplation.”", "또한 학문이 없이는 그 묵상의 깊이와 신성함을 제대로 느끼는 경지에 이를 수도 없는 것입니다.\""]], [["“Learned or not learned,”", "\"배웠든 안 배웠든,\""], ["said Agrican, “you might show yourself better bred than by endeavoring to make me talk on a subject on which you have me at a disadvantage.", "아그리칸이 말했다. \"당신이 내게 불리한 주제로 말을 시키려 애쓰는 것보다는 더 교양 있게 처신할 수도 있을 텐데."], ["If you choose to sleep I wish you good night; but if you prefer talking I recommend you to talk of fighting or of fair ladies.", "잠을 자고 싶다면 잘 자라고 인사하겠소. 하지만 이야기를 나누고 싶다면 싸움이나 아름다운 여인들에 관해 이야기하기를 권하오."], ["And, by the way, pray tell me, are you not that Orlando who makes such a noise in the world?", "그리고 그건 그렇고, 부디 말해 주시오, 당신은 세상에 그토록 떠들썩한 이름을 떨치는 그 오를란도가 아니오?"], ["And what is it, pray, that brings you into these parts?", "그래, 도대체 무엇이 당신을 이런 곳까지 데려왔단 말이오?"], ["Were you ever in love?", "한 번이라도 사랑에 빠진 적이 있소?"], ["I suppose you must have been; for to be a knight, and never to have been in love, would be like being a man without a heart in his breast.”", "틀림없이 있었으리라 생각하오. 기사이면서 사랑에 빠진 적이 없다는 것은, 가슴속에 심장이 없는 사람과 같을 테니까.\""]], [["The count replied: “Orlando I am, and in love I am.", "백작이 대답했다. \"오를란도, 그게 바로 나요."], ["Love has made me abandon everything, and brought me into these distant regions, and, to tell you all in one word, my heart is in the hands of the daughter of King Galafron.", "그리고 나는 사랑에 빠져 있소. 사랑이 나로 하여금 모든 것을 버리게 했고, 이 머나먼 지방으로 데려왔소."], ["You have come against him with fire and sword, to get possession of his castles and his dominions; and I have come to help him, for no object in the world but to please his daughter and win her beautiful hand.", "한마디로 다 말하자면, 내 마음은 갈라프론 왕의 딸의 손안에 있소. 당신은 그의 성과 영토를 차지하려고 불과 칼을 들고 그를 치러 왔지요."], ["I care for nothing else in existence.”", "그리고 나는 그를 돕기 위해 왔으니, 오직 그의 딸을 기쁘게 하고 그 아름다운 손을 얻으려는 것 말고는 세상의 어떤 목적도 없소. 나는 다른 무엇도 마음에 두지 않소.\""]], [["Now when the Tartar king, Agrican, heard his antagonist speak in this manner, and knew him to be indeed Orlando, and to be in love with Angelica, his face changed color for grief and jealousy, though it could not be seen for the darkness.", "그런데 타타르의 왕 아그리칸이 적수가 이렇게 말하는 것을 듣고, 그가 정말로 오를란도이며 안젤리카를 사랑하고 있다는 것을 알게 되자, 그의 얼굴은 슬픔과 질투로 빛을 잃었다. 다만 어둠 때문에 그것이 보이지 않았을 뿐이다."], ["His heart began beating with such violence that he felt as if he should have died.", "그의 심장은 너무도 격렬하게 뛰기 시작해서 그는 마치 죽을 것만 같았다."], ["“Well,”", "\"좋소.\""], ["said he to Orlando, “we are to fight when it is daylight, and one or other is to be left here, dead on the ground.", "그가 오를란도에게 말했다. \"우리는 날이 밝으면 싸우게 될 것이고, 둘 중 하나는 이 땅 위에 죽어 누워 있게 될 것이오."], ["I have a proposal to make to you—nay, an entreaty.", "내 당신에게 제안을 하나 하겠소. 아니, 간청이라 해야겠소."], ["My love is so excessive for the same lady that I beg you to leave her to me.", "같은 여인에 대한 내 사랑이 너무도 지나치니, 그녀를 내게 양보해 주기를 비오."], ["I will owe you my thanks, and give up the siege and put an end to the war.", "그러면 내 당신에게 고마움을 빚지고, 포위를 풀어 전쟁을 끝내겠소."], ["I cannot bear that any one should love her, and that I should live to see it.", "누군가가 그녀를 사랑하는데 내가 살아서 그것을 보아야 한다는 것을 나는 견딜 수가 없소."], ["Why, therefore, should either of us perish?", "그러니 어찌하여 우리 둘 중 누구라도 죽어야 한단 말이오?"], ["Give her up.", "그녀를 포기하시오."], ["Not a soul shall know it.”", "아무도 그것을 알지 못할 것이오.\""]], [["“I never yet,”", "\"나는 여태껏,\" 오를란도가 대답했다."], ["answered Orlando, “made a promise which I did not keep, and nevertheless I own to you that, were I to make a promise like that, and even swear to keep it, I should not.", "\"내가 지키지 않을 약속은 한 번도 한 적이 없소. 그럼에도 당신에게 고백하건대, 만일 내가 그런 약속을 하고 심지어 그것을 지키겠노라 맹세한다 해도, 나는 지키지 못할 것이오."], ["You might as well ask me to tear away the limbs from my body, and the eyes out of my head.", "당신은 차라리 내 몸에서 사지를 뜯어내고, 내 머리에서 두 눈을 뽑아 달라고 청하는 편이 나을 것이오."], ["I could as well live without breath itself as cease loving Angelica.”", "나는 안젤리카를 사랑하기를 그만두느니 차라리 숨 그 자체 없이 사는 편이 낫소.\""]], [["Agrican had hardly patience to let him finish speaking, ere he leapt furiously on horseback, though it was midnight.", "아그리칸은 그가 말을 끝내도록 참아 줄 인내심조차 거의 없어, 한밤중이었음에도 미친 듯이 말 위로 뛰어올랐다. \"그녀를 포기하라.\""], ["“Quit her,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “or die!”", "\"아니면 죽어라!\""]], [["Orlando seeing the infidel getting up, and not being sure that he would not add treachery to fierceness, had been hardly less quick in mounting for the combat.", "오를란도는 그 이교도가 몸을 일으키는 것을 보고, 그가 사나움에 더해 배신까지 저지르지 않으리라고 확신할 수 없었기에, 그 못지않게 재빨리 말에 올라 결투에 임했다. \"결코,\""], ["“Never,”", "그가 외쳤다."], ["exclaimed he; “I never could have quitted her if I would, and now I would not if I could.", "\"원한다 해도 나는 그녀를 포기할 수 없었을 것이오. 그리고 이제는 할 수 있다 해도 하지 않겠소."], ["You must seek her by other means than these.”", "당신은 이런 수단이 아닌 다른 방법으로 그녀를 찾아야 할 것이오.\""]], [["Fiercely dashed their horses together, in the nighttime, on the green mead.", "한밤중, 푸른 풀밭 위에서 그들의 말이 사납게 부딪쳤다."], ["Despiteful and terrible were the blows they gave and took by the moonlight.", "달빛 아래 그들이 주고받는 타격은 잔혹하고 무시무시했다."], ["Agrican fought in a rage, Orlando was cooler.", "아그리칸은 분노에 차서 싸웠고, 오를란도는 더 냉정했다."], ["And now the struggle had lasted more than five hours, and day began to dawn, when the Tartar king, furious to find so much trouble given him, dealt his enemy a blow sharp and violent beyond conception.", "그리고 이제 싸움이 다섯 시간 넘게 이어져 날이 밝기 시작했을 때, 타타르의 왕은 이토록 큰 곤란을 겪게 된 것에 격분하여 적에게 상상을 초월할 만큼 날카롭고 격렬한 일격을 가했다."], ["It cut the shield in two as if it had been made of wood, and, though blood could not be drawn from Orlando, because he was fated, it shook and bruised him as if it had started every joint in his body.", "그것은 마치 나무로 만들어진 양 방패를 두 동강 냈고, 오를란도는 운명에 의해 보호받았기에 피를 흘리지는 않았지만, 그 일격은 마치 그의 몸의 모든 관절을 어긋나게 한 듯 그를 뒤흔들고 멍들게 했다."]], [["His body only, however, not a particle of his soul.", "그러나 그것은 오직 그의 몸뿐, 그의 영혼은 한 점도 흔들리지 않았다."], ["So dreadful was the blow which the paladin gave in return, that not only shield, but every bit of mail on the body of Agrican was broken in pieces, and three of his ribs cut asunder.", "그 무사가 답례로 가한 일격이 어찌나 끔찍했던지, 방패뿐 아니라 아그리칸의 몸에 걸친 갑옷 한 조각 한 조각이 산산이 부서졌고, 갈비뼈 세 대가 동강 났다."]], [["The Tartar, roaring like a lion, raised his sword with still greater vehemence than before, and dealt a blow on the paladin’s helmet, such as he had never yet received from mortal man.", "타타르인은 사자처럼 울부짖으며 전보다 더 큰 격렬함으로 칼을 치켜들어, 그 무사가 여태껏 필멸의 인간에게서 받아 본 적 없는 일격을 그의 투구에 가했다."], ["For a moment it took away his senses.", "한순간 그는 정신을 잃었다."], ["His sight failed, his ears tingled, his frightened horse turned about to fly; and he was falling from the saddle, when the very action of falling threw his head upwards, and thus recalled his recollection.", "시야가 흐려지고, 귀가 울렸으며, 놀란 말은 달아나려고 몸을 돌렸다. 그가 안장에서 떨어지려는 찰나, 바로 그 떨어지는 동작이 그의 머리를 위로 젖혀 놓았고, 그리하여 그는 정신을 되찾았다."]], [["“What a shame is this!”", "\"이 무슨 수치인가!\""], ["thought he; “how shall I ever again dare to face Angelica!", "그는 생각했다. \"이래서야 어찌 다시 안젤리카를 마주 볼 낯이 있겠는가!"], ["I have been fighting hour after hour with this man, and he is but one, and I call myself Orlando!", "나는 이 자와 몇 시간이고 싸워 왔는데, 그는 하나뿐인 적이거늘, 그러고도 내가 오를란도라 자처하다니!"], ["If the combat last any longer I will bury myself in a monastery, and never look on sword again.”", "이 싸움이 조금이라도 더 길어진다면, 나는 수도원에 틀어박혀 두 번 다시 칼을 보지 않으리라.\""]], [["Orlando muttered with his lips closed and his teeth ground together; and you might have thought that fire instead of breath came out of his nose and mouth.", "오를란도는 입술을 굳게 다물고 이를 갈며 중얼거렸다. 그의 코와 입에서는 숨 대신 불길이 뿜어 나온다고 여길 만했다."], ["He raised his sword Durindana with both his hands, and sent it down so tremendously on Agrican’s shoulder that it cut through breastplate down to the very haunch, nay, crushed the saddle-bow, though it was made of bone and iron, and felled man and horse to the earth.", "그는 두 손으로 자신의 검 두린다나를 치켜들어, 아그리칸의 어깨 위로 어찌나 무시무시하게 내리쳤던지, 그것은 흉갑을 뚫고 허리뼈까지 갈랐을 뿐 아니라, 뼈와 쇠로 만들어졌음에도 안장의 앞테를 으스러뜨리고 사람과 말을 함께 땅에 쓰러뜨렸다."], ["Agrican turned as white as ashes, and felt death upon him.", "아그리칸은 잿빛처럼 창백해졌고, 죽음이 자신에게 닥쳤음을 느꼈다."], ["He called Orlando to come close to him, with a gentle voice, and said, as well as he could: “I believe on Him who died on the cross.", "그는 부드러운 목소리로 오를란도를 가까이 오라 부르더니, 할 수 있는 한 가까스로 말했다. \"나는 십자가에서 죽으신 그분을 믿소."], ["Baptize me, I pray thee, with the fountain, before my senses are gone.", "내 정신이 사라지기 전에, 부디 저 샘물로 내게 세례를 베풀어 주시오."], ["I have lived an evil life, but need not be rebellious to God in death also.", "나는 악한 삶을 살았으나, 죽음에 이르러서까지 하느님께 거역할 필요는 없겠지요."], ["May He who came to save all the rest of the world save me!”", "세상의 다른 모든 이를 구원하러 오신 그분이 나를 구원해 주시기를!\""], ["And he shed tears, that great king, though he had been so lofty and fierce.", "그러고는 그 위대한 왕이, 그토록 오만하고 사나웠던 그가 눈물을 흘렸다."]], [["Orlando dismounted quickly, with his own face in tears.", "오를란도는 자신의 얼굴에도 눈물을 흘리며 재빨리 말에서 내렸다."], ["He gathered the king tenderly in his arms, and took and laid him by the fountain, on a marble rim that it had, and then he wept in concert with him heartily, and asked his pardon, and so baptized him in the water of the fountain, and knelt and prayed to God for him with joined hands.", "그는 그 왕을 두 팔로 다정하게 안아 올려, 샘에 둘려 있던 대리석 가장자리에 데려가 눕혔다. 그러고는 그와 한마음으로 진심으로 울며 용서를 구했고, 샘물로 그에게 세례를 베풀었으며, 무릎을 꿇고 두 손을 모아 그를 위해 하느님께 기도했다."]], [["He then paused and looked at him; and when he perceived his countenance changed, and that his whole person was cold, he left him there on the marble rim of the fountain, all armed as he was, with the sword by his side, and the crown upon his head.", "이윽고 그는 멈추어 그를 바라보았다. 그의 얼굴빛이 변하고 온몸이 차갑게 식은 것을 알아차리자, 그는 그를 그곳 샘의 대리석 가장자리에, 무장을 갖춘 그대로, 칼을 옆구리에 차고 왕관을 머리에 쓴 채로 남겨 두었다."]], [["————", "————"]], [["# ADVENTURES OF RINALDO AND ORLANDO", "# ADVENTURES OF RINALDO AND ORLANDO"]], [["WE left Rinaldo when, having overcome the monster, he quitted the castle of Altaripa, and pursued his way on foot.", "리날도와 오를란도의 모험\n우리는 리날도가 괴물을 물리친 뒤 알타리파 성을 떠나 걸어서 길을 가던 대목에서 그를 두고 왔다."], ["He soon met with a weeping damsel, who, being questioned as to the cause of her sorrow, told him she was in search of one to do battle to rescue her lover, who had been made prisoner by a vile enchantress, together with Orlando and many more.", "그는 곧 울고 있는 한 처녀를 만났는데, 슬픔의 까닭을 묻자, 그녀는 사악한 마녀에게 오를란도와 그 밖의 여러 사람과 함께 포로로 잡힌 자신의 연인을 구출하기 위해 싸워 줄 사람을 찾고 있다고 말했다."], ["The damsel was Flordelis, the lady-love of Florismart, and Rinaldo promised his assistance, trusting to accomplish the adventure either by valor or skill.", "그 처녀는 플로리스마트의 연인 플로르델리스였고, 리날도는 용맹으로든 기지로든 그 모험을 완수하리라 믿으며 도움을 약속했다."], ["Flordelis insisted upon Rinaldo’s taking her horse, which he consented to do, on condition of her mounting behind him.", "플로르델리스는 리날도에게 자신의 말을 타라고 고집했고, 그는 그녀가 뒤에 함께 타는 조건으로 그러기로 했다."]], [["As they rode on through a wood, they heard strange noises, and Rinaldo, reassuring the damsel, pressed forward towards the quarter from which they proceeded.", "그들이 숲을 지나 말을 달리는 동안 이상한 소리가 들려왔고, 리날도는 처녀를 안심시키며 그 소리가 나는 쪽을 향해 나아갔다."], ["He soon perceived a giant standing under a vaulted cavern, with a huge club in his hand, and of an appearance to strike the boldest spirit with dread.", "그는 곧 둥근 천장 같은 동굴 아래 거대한 곤봉을 손에 든 채 서 있는 거인을 보았는데, 그 모습은 가장 대담한 정신마저 두려움에 떨게 할 만했다."], ["By the side of the cavern was chained a griffin, which, together with the giant, was stationed there to guard a wonderful horse, the same which was once Argalia’s.", "동굴 옆에는 그리핀 한 마리가 사슬에 묶여 있었는데, 그것은 거인과 함께 그곳에 배치되어 한 마리 경이로운 말을 지키고 있었다. 그 말은 한때 아르갈리아의 것이었다."], ["This horse was a creature of enchantment, matchless in vigor, speed, and form, which disdained to share the diet of his fellow-steeds,—corn or grass,—and fed only on air.", "이 말은 마법의 산물로, 활력과 속도와 자태에서 견줄 데가 없었으며, 곡식이나 풀 같은 동료 말들의 먹이를 함께 먹기를 마다하고 오직 공기만을 먹고 살았다."], ["His name was Rabican.", "그 이름은 라비칸이었다."]], [["This marvellous horse, after his master Argalia had been slain by Ferrau, finding himself at liberty, returned to his native cavern, and was here stabled under the protection of the giant and the griffin.", "이 경이로운 말은 주인 아르갈리아가 페라우에게 죽임을 당한 뒤, 자신이 자유로워진 것을 알고 고향인 동굴로 돌아왔고, 그곳에서 거인과 그리핀의 보호 아래 길러지고 있었다."], ["As Rinaldo approached, the giant assailed him with his club.", "리날도가 다가가자 거인이 곤봉으로 그를 공격했다."], ["Rinaldo defended himself from the giant’s blows, and gave him one in return, which, if his skin had not been of the toughest, would have finished the combat.", "리날도는 거인의 타격을 막아 내고 답례로 한 방을 가했는데, 그의 가죽이 가장 질긴 것이 아니었더라면 그 일격으로 싸움은 끝났을 것이다."], ["But the giant, though wounded, escaped, and let loose the griffin.", "그러나 거인은 부상을 입고도 달아나면서 그리핀을 풀어 놓았다."], ["This monstrous bird towered in air, and thence pounced down upon Rinaldo, who, watching his opportunity, dealt her a desperate wound.", "이 괴물 새는 공중으로 높이 솟구쳤다가 거기서 리날도에게 덮쳐 내렸고, 그는 기회를 노려 그것에게 치명적인 상처를 입혔다."], ["She had, however, strength for another flight, and kept repeating her attacks, which Rinaldo parried as he could, while the damsel stood trembling by, witnessing the contest.", "그러나 그것은 또 한 번 날아오를 힘이 남아 있어 거듭 공격을 되풀이했고, 리날도는 할 수 있는 한 그것을 받아넘겼으며, 그동안 처녀는 옆에서 떨며 그 싸움을 지켜보았다."]], [["The battle continued, rendered more terrible by the approach of night, when Rinaldo determined upon a desperate expedient to bring it to a conclusion.", "밤이 다가오면서 더욱 무시무시해진 싸움이 계속되자, 리날도는 그것을 끝내기 위해 필사적인 방책을 결심했다."], ["He fell, as if fainting from his wounds, and, on the close approach of the griffin, dealt her a blow which sheared away one of her wings.", "그는 상처로 인해 기절한 척 쓰러졌고, 그리핀이 바짝 다가들자 그것에게 일격을 가하여 날개 하나를 잘라 냈다."], ["The beast, though sinking, gripped him fast with her talons, digging through plate and mail; but Rinaldo plied his sword in utter desperation, and at last accomplished her destruction.", "그 짐승은 비록 쓰러지면서도 발톱으로 그를 단단히 움켜쥐어 강철판과 사슬갑옷을 뚫고 파고들었으나, 리날도는 극도의 필사적인 심정으로 칼을 휘둘러 마침내 그것을 파멸시켰다."]], [["Rinaldo then entered the cavern, and found there the wonderful horse, all caparisoned.", "이윽고 리날도는 동굴 안으로 들어가 거기서 마구를 모두 갖춘 그 경이로운 말을 발견했다."], ["He was coal-black, except for a star of white on his forehead, and one white foot behind.", "그 말은 이마에 흰 별 무늬가 하나 있고 뒷발 하나가 흰 것을 제외하면 새까만 빛이었다."], ["For speed he was unrivalled, though in strength he yielded to Bayard.", "속도로 말하면 견줄 데가 없었으나, 힘에서는 바야르에 미치지 못했다."], ["Rinaldo mounted upon Rabican, and issued from the cavern.", "리날도는 라비칸에 올라 동굴 밖으로 나왔다."]], [["As he pursued his way he met a fugitive from Agrican’s army, who gave such an account of the prowess of a champion who fought on the side of Angelica, that Rinaldo was persuaded this must be Orlando, though at a loss to imagine how he could have been freed from captivity.", "길을 가던 그는 아그리칸의 군대에서 도망쳐 나온 자를 만났는데, 그자가 안젤리카 편에서 싸우는 한 용사의 무용에 관해 어찌나 그럴듯하게 이야기하던지, 리날도는 그가 오를란도임에 틀림없다고 확신하게 되었다. 다만 그가 어떻게 포로 신세에서 풀려날 수 있었는지는 짐작할 수 없어 막막했다."], ["He determined to repair to the scene of the contest to satisfy his curiosity, and Flordelis, hoping to find Florismart with Orlando, consented to accompany him.", "그는 호기심을 풀기 위해 그 싸움터로 가 보기로 결심했고, 플로르델리스도 오를란도와 함께 플로리스마트를 찾을 수 있으리라는 희망에 그와 동행하기로 했다."]], [["While these things were doing, all was rout and dismay in the Tartarian army, from the death of Agrican.", "이런 일들이 벌어지는 동안, 아그리칸의 죽음으로 인해 타타르군은 온통 패주와 경악에 휩싸여 있었다."], ["King Galafron, arriving at this juncture with an army for the relief of his capital, Albracca, assaulted the enemy’s camp, and carried all before him.", "갈라프론 왕이 바로 이때에 자신의 수도 알브라카를 구원하기 위한 군대를 이끌고 당도하여, 적진을 공격해 모든 것을 휩쓸었다."], ["Rinaldo had now reached the scene of action, and was looking on as an unconcerned spectator, when he was espied by Galafron.", "리날도는 이제 싸움터에 다다라 무심한 구경꾼으로 지켜보고 있었는데, 그때 갈라프론에게 발각되었다."], ["The king instantly recognized the horse Rabican, which he had given to Argalia when he sent him forth on his ill-omened mission to Paris.", "왕은 라비칸이라는 말을 즉시 알아보았으니, 그것은 그가 불길한 사명을 띠고 아르갈리아를 파리로 보내면서 그에게 주었던 말이었다."], ["Possessed with the idea that the rider of the horse was the murderer of Argalia, Galafron rode at Rinaldo, and smote him with all his force.", "말의 기수가 아르갈리아의 살해자라는 생각에 사로잡힌 갈라프론은 리날도에게 말을 달려 온 힘을 다해 내리쳤다."], ["Rinaldo was not slow to avenge the blow, and it would have gone hard with the king had not his followers instantly closed round him and separated the combatants.", "리날도도 그 일격에 복수하기를 더디 하지 않았으니, 만일 그의 부하들이 즉시 그를 둘러싸 두 사람을 떼어 놓지 않았더라면 왕은 위태로웠을 것이다."]], [["Rinaldo thus found himself, almost without his own choice, enlisted on the side of the enemies of Angelica, which gave him no concern, so completely had his draught from the fountain of hate steeled his mind against her.", "이리하여 리날도는 거의 자신의 선택과 무관하게 안젤리카의 적들 편에 서게 되었으나, 그것은 그에게 아무런 걱정거리가 되지 않았다. 미움의 샘에서 마신 물이 그의 마음을 그녀에게서 그토록 완전히 굳혀 놓았기 때문이다."]], [["For several successive days the struggle continued, without any important results, Rinaldo meeting the bravest knights of Angelica’s party, and defeating them one after the other.", "여러 날이 잇따라 싸움이 계속되었으나 별다른 중요한 결과는 없었으니, 리날도는 안젤리카 편의 가장 용맹한 기사들과 맞붙어 그들을 차례로 물리쳤다."], ["At length he encountered Orlando, and the two knights bitterly reproached one another for the cause they had each adopted, and engaged in a furious combat.", "마침내 그는 오를란도와 마주쳤고, 두 기사는 각자 택한 명분을 두고 서로를 호되게 비난하며 격렬한 싸움에 돌입했다."], ["Orlando was mounted upon Bayard, Rinaldo’s horse, which Agrican had by chance become possessed of, and Orlando had taken from him as the prize of victory.", "오를란도는 리날도의 말 바야르에 타고 있었는데, 그 말은 우연히 아그리칸의 손에 들어갔다가 오를란도가 승리의 전리품으로 그에게서 빼앗은 것이었다."], ["Bayard would not fight against his master, and Orlando was getting the worse of the encounter, when suddenly Rinaldo, seeing Astolpho, who for love of him had arrayed himself on his side, hard beset by numbers, left Orlando to rush to the defence of his friend.", "바야르는 자신의 주인을 상대로 싸우려 하지 않았고, 오를란도가 그 대결에서 점차 불리해지고 있을 때, 갑자기 리날도는 그에 대한 사랑으로 그의 편에 서서 무장한 아스톨포가 수많은 적에게 호되게 시달리는 것을 보고, 오를란도를 내버려 두고 친구를 지키러 달려갔다."], ["Night prevented the combat from being renewed; but a challenge was given and accepted for their next meeting.", "밤이 되어 싸움은 다시 시작되지 못했으나, 다음 만남을 위한 결투 신청이 오가고 받아들여졌다."]], [["But Angelica, sighing in her heart for Rinaldo, was not willing that he should be again exposed to so terrible a venture.", "그러나 마음속으로 리날도를 그리며 한숨짓던 안젤리카는 그가 또다시 그토록 무시무시한 모험에 노출되기를 원치 않았다."], ["She begged a boon of Orlando, promising she would be his if he would do her bidding.", "그녀는 오를란도에게 청을 하나 들어 달라고 부탁하며, 그가 자신의 분부를 따른다면 그의 것이 되겠노라 약속했다."], ["On receiving his promise, she enjoined him to set out without delay to destroy the garden of the enchantress Falerina, in which many valiant knights had been entrapped, and were imprisoned.", "그의 승낙을 받자 그녀는 지체 없이 출발하여 마녀 팔레리나의 정원을 파괴하라고 명했으니, 그곳에는 수많은 용맹한 기사가 함정에 빠져 갇혀 있었다."]], [["Orlando departed on his horse Brigliadoro, leaving Bayard in disgrace for his bad deportment the day before.", "오를란도는 자신의 말 브릴리아도로를 타고 떠났으며, 전날 보인 나쁜 행실 때문에 바야르는 면목을 잃은 채 남겨 두었다."], ["Angelica, to conciliate Rinaldo, sent Bayard to him; but Rinaldo remained unmoved by this as by all her former acts of kindness.", "안젤리카는 리날도의 환심을 사려고 바야르를 그에게 보냈으나, 리날도는 그녀가 전에 베푼 모든 친절에 그러했듯 이 일에도 마음이 움직이지 않았다."]], [["When Rinaldo learned Orlando’s departure, he yielded to the entreaties of the lady of Florismart, and prepared to fulfil his promise, and rescue her lover from the power of the enchantress.", "리날도는 오를란도가 떠났다는 것을 알자, 플로리스마트의 연인의 간청에 응하여 자신의 약속을 이행하고 마녀의 힘에서 그녀의 연인을 구출할 채비를 했다."], ["Thus both Rinaldo and Orlando were bound upon the same adventure, but unknown to one another.", "이리하여 리날도와 오를란도 둘 다 같은 모험에 매이게 되었으나, 서로는 그것을 알지 못했다."]], [["The castle of Falerina was protected by a river, which was crossed by a bridge, kept by a ruffian, who challenged all comers to the combat; and such was his strength that he had thus far prevailed in every encounter, as appeared by the arms of various knights which he had taken from them, and piled up as a trophy on the shore.", "팔레리나의 성은 강으로 보호되어 있었고, 그 강 위에는 다리가 놓여 있었는데, 한 흉포한 자가 그 다리를 지키며 오는 자마다 모두 싸움을 걸었다. 그의 힘이 어찌나 셌던지 그는 여태껏 모든 대결에서 이겨 왔으니, 여러 기사에게서 빼앗아 강가에 전리품으로 쌓아 놓은 무기들이 그 증거였다."], ["Rinaldo attacked him, but with as bad success as the rest, for the bridge-ward struck him so violent a blow with an iron mace that he fell to the ground.", "리날도는 그를 공격했으나 다른 이들과 마찬가지로 성과가 좋지 않았다. 다리지기가 쇠몽둥이로 그에게 어찌나 격렬한 일격을 가했던지 그는 땅에 쓰러지고 말았다."], ["But when the villain approached to strip him of his armor, Rinaldo seized him, and the bridge-ward, being unable to free himself, leapt with Rinaldo into the lake, where they both disappeared.", "그러나 그 악당이 그의 갑옷을 벗기려고 다가오자 리날도가 그를 붙잡았고, 다리지기는 빠져나오지 못한 채 리날도와 함께 호수로 뛰어들었으며, 두 사람은 모두 자취를 감추었다."]], [["Orlando, meanwhile, in discharge of his promise to Angelica, pursued his way in quest of the same adventure.", "한편 오를란도는 안젤리카에게 한 약속을 이행하기 위해 같은 모험을 찾아 길을 갔다."], ["In passing through a wood he saw a cavalier armed at all points, and mounted, keeping guard over a lady who was bound to a tree, weeping bitterly.", "숲을 지나가다 그는 빈틈없이 무장하고 말에 탄 한 기사가, 나무에 묶인 채 비통하게 우는 한 여인을 지키고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["Orlando hastened to her relief, but was exhorted by the knight not to interfere, for she had deserved her fate by her wickedness.", "오를란도가 그녀를 구하러 서둘러 갔으나, 그 기사는 그녀가 자신의 사악함으로 그런 운명을 자초한 것이니 끼어들지 말라고 권고했다."], ["In proof of which he made certain charges against her.", "그 증거로 그는 그녀에게 몇 가지 죄목을 들었다."], ["The lady denied them all, and Orlando believed her, defied the knight, overthrew him, and, releasing the lady, departed with her seated on his horse’s croup.", "여인은 그것을 모두 부인했고, 오를란도는 그녀를 믿고 기사에게 도전하여 그를 쓰러뜨리고는, 여인을 풀어 주어 자신의 말 엉덩이에 태우고 떠났다."]], [["While they rode another damsel approached on a white palfrey, who warned Orlando of impending danger, and informed him that he was near the garden of the enchantress.", "그들이 말을 달리는 동안 또 다른 처녀가 흰 조랑말을 타고 다가와, 오를란도에게 임박한 위험을 경고하며 그가 마녀의 정원 가까이에 있음을 알려 주었다."], ["Orlando was delighted with the intelligence, and entreated her to inform him how he was to gain admittance.", "오를란도는 그 소식에 기뻐하며 어떻게 하면 안으로 들어갈 수 있는지 알려 달라고 청했다."], ["She replied that the garden could only be entered at sunrise and gave him such instructions as would enable him to gain admittance.", "그녀는 그 정원은 오직 해 뜰 무렵에만 들어갈 수 있다고 대답하며, 들어갈 수 있도록 일러 주었다."], ["She gave him also a book in which was painted the garden and all that it contained, together with the palace of the false enchantress, where she had secluded herself for the purpose of executing a magic work in which she was engaged.", "그녀는 또한 그에게 책을 한 권 주었는데, 거기에는 정원과 그 안에 있는 모든 것이, 그리고 그 거짓 마녀의 궁전이 그려져 있었으니, 마녀는 자신이 매달려 있는 마법의 작업을 수행하기 위해 그곳에 칩거해 있었다."], ["This was the manufacture of a sword capable of cutting even through enchanted substances.", "그것은 마법에 걸린 물질조차 베어 낼 수 있는 검을 만드는 일이었다."], ["The object of this labor, the damsel told him, was the destruction of a knight of the west, by name Orlando, who she had read in the book of Fate was coming to demolish her garden.", "그 작업의 목적은, 처녀가 그에게 말하기를, 서방의 한 기사를 파멸시키려는 것이었으니, 그 기사의 이름은 오를란도로, 그가 자신의 정원을 무너뜨리러 오리라는 것을 마녀가 운명의 책에서 읽었던 것이다."], ["Having thus instructed him, the damsel departed.", "이렇게 그에게 일러 준 뒤 처녀는 떠났다."]], [["Orlando, finding he must delay his enterprise till the next morning, now lay down and was soon asleep.", "오를란도는 자신의 거사를 다음 날 아침까지 미룰 수밖에 없음을 알고, 이제 자리에 누워 곧 잠이 들었다."], ["Seeing this, the base woman whom he had rescued, and who was intent on making her escape to rejoin her paramour, mounted Brigliadoro, and rode off, carrying away Durindana.", "그가 잠든 것을 보자, 그가 구해 주었던 비열한 여인은, 정부와 다시 만나기 위해 달아날 작정으로, 브릴리아도로에 올라 두린다나를 가지고 말을 달려 가 버렸다."]], [["When Orlando awoke, his indignation, as may be supposed, was great on the discovery of the theft; but, like a good knight and true, he was not to be diverted from his enterprise.", "오를란도가 깨어났을 때, 짐작할 수 있듯 도둑맞은 것을 알고 그의 분노는 컸다. 그러나 훌륭하고 충실한 기사답게 그는 자신의 거사에서 마음을 돌리지 않았다."], ["He tore off a huge branch of an elm to supply the place of his sword; and, as the sun rose, took his way towards the gate of the garden, where a dragon was on his watch.", "그는 칼을 대신할 거대한 느릅나무 가지 하나를 꺾어 냈고, 해가 떠오르자 정원 문으로 길을 잡았는데, 그곳에서는 용 한 마리가 망을 보고 있었다."], ["This he slew by repeated blows, and entered the garden, the gate of which closed behind him, barring retreat.", "그는 거듭된 타격으로 그것을 죽이고 정원으로 들어갔으며, 정원 문은 그의 뒤에서 닫혀 후퇴를 가로막았다."], ["Looking round him, he saw a fair fountain, which overflowed into a river, and in the centre of the fountain a figure, on whose forehead was written:", "주위를 둘러보니 아름다운 샘 하나가 보였는데, 그 물이 넘쳐흘러 강을 이루었고, 샘 한가운데에는 형상 하나가 있어 그 이마에는 이렇게 새겨져 있었다."]], [["“The stream which waters violet and rose, From hence to the enchanted palace goes.”", "\"제비꽃과 장미에 물을 대는 이 시냇물은, 여기서부터 마법의 궁전으로 흘러간다.\""]], [["Following the banks of this flowing stream, and rapt in the delights of the charming garden, Orlando arrived at the palace, and entering it, found the mistress, clad in white, with a crown of gold upon her head, in the act of viewing herself in the surface of the magic sword.", "이 흐르는 시냇물의 둑을 따라가며, 매혹적인 정원의 즐거움에 빠져, 오를란도는 궁전에 다다랐다. 안으로 들어가니 흰옷을 입고 머리에 금관을 쓴 여주인이 마법의 검 표면에 자신의 모습을 비추어 보고 있었다."], ["Orlando surprised her before she could escape, deprived her of the weapon, and holding her fast by her long hair, which floated behind, threatened her with immediate death if she did not yield up her prisoners, and afford him the means of egress.", "오를란도는 그녀가 달아나기 전에 기습하여 그 무기를 빼앗고, 뒤로 흩날리는 그녀의 긴 머리채를 단단히 움켜쥔 채, 만일 포로들을 내놓고 자신이 빠져나갈 방도를 마련해 주지 않으면 당장 죽이겠다고 위협했다."], ["She, however, was firm of purpose, making no reply, and Orlando, unable to move her either by threats or entreaties, was under the necessity of binding her to a beech, and pursuing his quest as he best might.", "그러나 그녀는 굳은 결심으로 아무런 대답도 하지 않았고, 위협으로도 간청으로도 그녀를 움직일 수 없었던 오를란도는, 어쩔 수 없이 그녀를 너도밤나무에 묶어 두고 할 수 있는 한 자신의 탐색을 계속해야 했다."]], [["He then bethought him of his book, and, consulting it, found that there was an outlet to the south, but that to reach it a lake was to be passed, inhabited by a siren, whose song was so entrancing as to be quite irresistible to whoever heard it; but his book instructed him how to protect himself against this danger.", "이윽고 그는 책을 떠올리고 그것을 참고하여, 남쪽으로 출구가 하나 있음을 알게 되었다. 그러나 거기에 이르려면 호수 하나를 건너야 했는데, 그 호수에는 세이렌이 살고 있어, 그 노래가 어찌나 황홀한지 듣는 자라면 누구든 도저히 거역할 수 없을 정도였다."], ["According to its directions, while pursuing his path, he gathered abundance of flowers, which sprung all around, and filled his helmet and his ears with them; then listened if he heard the birds sing.", "그러나 그의 책은 이 위험으로부터 자신을 지키는 법을 일러 주었다. 그 지시에 따라 그는 길을 가면서 사방에 돋아난 꽃을 잔뜩 따 모아 투구와 귀를 그것으로 채웠다."], ["Finding that, though he saw the gaping beak, the swelling throat, and ruffled plumes, he could not catch a note, he felt satisfied with his defence, and advanced toward the lake.", "그러고는 새가 노래하는 소리가 들리는지 귀를 기울였다. 새의 벌어진 부리와 부푼 목과 곤두선 깃털은 보였으나 한 가락의 소리도 들을 수 없자, 그는 자신의 방비에 만족하며 호수를 향해 나아갔다."], ["It was small but deep, and so clear and tranquil that the eye could penetrate to the bottom.", "호수는 작았으나 깊었고, 어찌나 맑고 고요한지 눈으로 바닥까지 꿰뚫어 볼 수 있었다."]], [["He had no sooner arrived upon the banks than the waters were seen to gurgle, and the siren, rising midway out of the pool, sung so sweetly that birds and beasts came trooping to the water-side to listen.", "그가 둑에 다다르자마자 물이 부글거리는 것이 보이더니, 세이렌이 못의 한가운데서 솟아올라 어찌나 달콤하게 노래했던지 새와 짐승이 떼 지어 물가로 몰려와 귀를 기울였다."], ["Of this Orlando heard nothing, but, feigning to yield to the charm, sank down upon the bank.", "오를란도는 이 가운데 아무것도 듣지 못했으나, 그 마력에 굴복하는 척하며 둑 위에 쓰러졌다."], ["The siren issued from the water with the intent to accomplish his destruction.", "세이렌은 그를 파멸시킬 작정으로 물에서 나왔다."], ["Orlando seized her by the hair, and while she sang yet louder (song being her only defence) cut off her head.", "오를란도는 그녀의 머리채를 움켜쥐었고, 그녀가 더욱 큰 소리로 노래하는 동안(노래가 그녀의 유일한 방어 수단이었다) 그 목을 잘랐다."], ["Then, following the directions of the book, he stained himself all over with her blood.", "그러고는 책의 지시를 따라 그녀의 피로 자신의 온몸을 물들였다."]], [["Guarded by this talisman, he met successively all the monsters set for defence of the enchantress and her garden, and at length found himself again at the spot where he had made captive the enchantress, who still continued fastened to the beech.", "이 부적으로 보호받으며 그는 마녀와 그 정원을 지키기 위해 배치된 모든 괴물을 차례로 마주쳐 물리쳤고, 마침내 자신이 마녀를 사로잡았던 그 자리로 다시 돌아왔으니, 마녀는 여전히 너도밤나무에 묶인 채로 있었다."], ["But the scene was changed.", "그러나 광경은 바뀌어 있었다."], ["The garden had disappeared, and Falerina, before so haughty, now begged for mercy, assuring him that many lives depended upon the preservation of hers.", "정원은 사라졌고, 전에는 그토록 오만했던 팔레리나가 이제는 자비를 구하며, 많은 목숨이 자신의 목숨을 보존하는 데 달려 있다고 그를 안심시켰다."], ["Orlando promised her life upon her pledging herself for the deliverance of her captives.", "오를란도는 그녀가 자기 포로들을 풀어 주겠다고 서약하는 조건으로 그녀의 목숨을 약속했다."]], [["This, however, was no easy task.", "그러나 이것은 쉬운 일이 아니었다."], ["They were not in her possession, but in that of a much more powerful enchantress, Morgana, the Lady of the Lake, the very idea of opposing whom made Falerina turn pale with fear.", "포로들은 그녀의 손안에 있는 것이 아니라, 훨씬 더 강력한 마녀인 모르가나, 곧 호수의 귀부인의 손안에 있었으니, 그녀에게 맞선다는 생각만으로도 팔레리나는 두려움에 창백해졌다."], ["Representing to him the hazards of the enterprise, she led him towards the dwelling of Morgana.", "그녀는 그에게 그 거사의 위험을 일러 주며 모르가나의 거처로 그를 이끌었다."], ["To approach it he had to encounter the same uncourteous bridge-ward who had already defeated and made captive so many knights, and last of all, Rinaldo.", "거기에 다가가려면 그는 이미 그토록 많은 기사를 물리쳐 사로잡은, 그리고 마지막으로 리날도까지 사로잡은 그 무례한 다리지기와 맞서야 했다."], ["He was a churl of the most ferocious character, named Arridano.", "그는 아리다노라는 이름의 가장 흉포한 성품의 상놈이었다."], ["Morgana had provided him with impenetrable armor, and endowed him in such a manner that his strength always increased in proportion to that of the adversary with whom he was matched.", "모르가나는 그에게 뚫리지 않는 갑옷을 마련해 주었고, 그를 그런 식으로 부여하여, 그의 힘이 언제나 맞붙은 상대의 힘에 비례하여 커지도록 했다."], ["No one had ever yet escaped from the contest, since, such was his power of endurance, he could breathe freely under water.", "그의 인내력이 어찌나 대단했던지 물속에서도 자유로이 숨을 쉴 수 있었으므로, 여태껏 그 대결에서 빠져나온 자가 아무도 없었다."], ["Hence, having grappled with a knight, and sunk with him to the bottom of the lake, he returned, bearing his enemy’s arms in triumph to the surface.", "그리하여 그는 한 기사와 맞붙어 그와 함께 호수 바닥으로 가라앉았다가, 적의 무기를 의기양양하게 수면 위로 들고 돌아오곤 했다."]], [["While Falerina was repeating her cautions and her counsels Orlando saw Rinaldo’s arms erected in form of a trophy, among other spoils made by the villain, and, forgetting their late quarrel, determined upon revenging his friend.", "팔레리나가 거듭 주의와 충고를 되풀이하는 동안, 오를란도는 그 악당이 차지한 다른 전리품들 사이에 리날도의 무기가 전리품 모양으로 세워져 있는 것을 보았고, 지난날의 다툼을 잊고 친구의 원수를 갚기로 결심했다."], ["Arriving at the pass, the churl presuming to bar the way, a desperate contest ensued, during which Falerina escaped.", "그 길목에 다다르자 상놈이 길을 막으려 했고, 필사적인 대결이 벌어졌는데, 그동안 팔레리나는 달아났다."], ["The churl finding himself over-matched at a contest of arms, resorted to his peculiar art, grappled his antagonist, and plunged with him into the lake.", "상놈은 무기를 쓰는 대결에서 자신이 당해 낼 수 없음을 알고 자신의 독특한 재주에 의지하여, 적수를 붙잡고 그와 함께 호수로 뛰어들었다."], ["When he reached the bottom Orlando found himself in another world, upon a dry meadow, with the lake overhead, through which shone the beams of our sun, while the water stood on all sides like a crystal wall.", "바닥에 다다랐을 때 오를란도는 자신이 또 다른 세계에, 마른 풀밭 위에 있음을 알게 되었으니, 머리 위에는 호수가 있어 그것을 뚫고 우리 태양의 빛이 비쳐 들었고, 물은 사방에서 수정 벽처럼 서 있었다."], ["Here the battle was renewed, and Orlando had in his magic sword an advantage which none had hitherto possessed.", "여기서 싸움이 다시 시작되었고, 오를란도는 자신의 마법 검에서 여태껏 누구도 가져 본 적 없는 이점을 누렸다."], ["It had been tempered by Falerina so that no spells could avail against it.", "그것은 팔레리나에 의해 단련되어 어떤 마법도 그것에 맞서 효력을 발휘할 수 없었다."], ["Thus armed, and countervailing the strength of his adversary by his superior skill and activity, it was not long before he laid him dead upon the field.", "이렇게 무장하고, 자신의 뛰어난 기량과 민첩함으로 적수의 힘을 상쇄하니, 오래지 않아 그는 그를 들판에 죽여 눕혔다."]], [["Orlando then made all haste to return to the upper air, and, passing through the water, which opened a way before him (such was the power of the magic sword), he soon regained the shore, and found himself in a field as thickly covered with precious stones as the sky is with stars.", "이윽고 오를란도는 서둘러 지상의 공기 속으로 돌아가려 했고, 물을 지나가니 그 앞에서 물이 길을 열어 주었으며(마법 검의 힘이 그러했다), 그는 곧 물가를 되찾아, 하늘이 별로 뒤덮여 있듯 보석으로 빼곡히 뒤덮인 들판에 있는 자신을 발견했다."], ["Orlando crossed the field, nor tempted to delay his enterprise by gathering any of the brilliant gems spread all around him.", "오를란도는 사방에 흩뿌려진 그 찬란한 보석을 하나라도 주워 자신의 거사를 늦추려는 유혹에 빠지지 않고 그 들판을 가로질렀다."], ["He next passed into a flowery meadow planted with trees, covered with fruit and flowers, and full of all imaginable delights.", "그는 이어서 나무가 심긴 꽃핀 풀밭으로 들어섰는데, 그곳은 과일과 꽃으로 뒤덮이고 상상할 수 있는 온갖 즐거움으로 가득했다."]], [["In the middle of this meadow was a fountain, and fast by it lay Morgana asleep; a lady of a lovely aspect, dressed in white and vermilion garments, her forehead well furnished with hair, while she had scarcely any behind.", "이 풀밭 한가운데에는 샘이 하나 있었고, 그 바로 곁에 모르가나가 잠들어 누워 있었다. 사랑스러운 용모의 귀부인으로, 흰색과 진홍색의 옷을 입고 있었으며, 이마에는 머리카락이 풍성했으나 뒤쪽으로는 머리카락이 거의 없었다."]], [["While Orlando stood in silence contemplating her beauty he heard a voice exclaim: “Seize the fairy by the forelock, if thou hopest fair success.”", "오를란도가 말없이 서서 그녀의 아름다움을 바라보고 있을 때, 한 목소리가 외치는 것이 들렸다. \"고운 성공을 바라거든, 저 요정의 앞머리를 붙잡아라.\""], ["But his attention was arrested by another object, and he heeded not the warning.", "그러나 그의 주의는 다른 대상에 사로잡혀 그 경고에 유념하지 않았다."], ["He saw on a sudden an array of towers, pinnacles and columns, palaces with balconies and windows, extended alleys with trees, in short a scene of architectural magnificence surpassing all he had ever beheld.", "그는 갑자기 탑과 첨탑과 기둥의 행렬을, 발코니와 창문이 달린 궁전을, 나무가 늘어선 길게 뻗은 가로수길을, 한마디로 그가 여태껏 본 그 어떤 것도 능가하는 건축적 장려함의 광경을 보았다."], ["While he stood gazing in silent astonishment the scene slowly melted away and disappeared.[74]", "그가 말없이 놀라 바라보고 서 있는 동안, 그 광경은 서서히 녹아 사라져 버렸다."]], [["When he had recovered from his amazement he looked again toward the fountain.", "그가 놀라움에서 깨어났을 때 그는 다시 샘 쪽을 바라보았다."], ["The fairy had awaked and risen, and was dancing round its border with the lightness of a leaf, timing her footsteps to this song:", "요정은 깨어나 일어나서, 나뭇잎처럼 가벼이 샘 가장자리를 돌며 춤추고 있었으니, 그 발걸음을 이 노래에 맞추고 있었다."]], [["“Who in this world would wealth and treasure share, Honor, delight, and state, and what is best, Quick let him catch me by the lock of hair Which flutters from my forehead; and be blest.", "\"이 세상에서 부와 보물을 누리고자 하는 이, 명예와 기쁨과 영화와 가장 좋은 것을 바라는 이, 어서 내 이마에서 나부끼는 머리채를 붙잡으라, 그리하여 복을 받으라."], ["“But let him not the proffered good forbear, Nor till he seize the fleeting blessing rest; For present loss is sought in vain to-morrow, And the deluded wretch is left in sorrow.”", "그러나 내미는 그 좋은 것을 멀리하지 말 것이며, 덧없는 축복을 붙잡기까지는 쉬지 말지니, 오늘 잃은 것은 내일 다시 구하여도 헛되며, 속아 넘어간 가련한 자는 슬픔 속에 남겨지리라.\""]], [["The fairy, having sung thus, bounded off, and fled from the flowery meadow over a high and inaccessible mountain.", "이렇게 노래를 부른 요정은 펄쩍 뛰어, 꽃핀 풀밭을 가로질러 높고 오를 수 없는 산 너머로 달아났다."], ["Orlando pursued her through thorns and rocks, while the sky gradually became overcast, and at last he was assailed by tempest, lightning, and hail.", "오를란도는 가시덤불과 바위를 헤치며 그녀를 뒤쫓았고, 그동안 하늘은 점차 흐려지더니, 마침내 그는 폭풍과 번개와 우박의 습격을 받았다."]], [["While he thus pursued, a pale and meagre woman issued from a cave, armed with a whip, and, treading close upon his steps, scourged him with vigorous strokes.", "그가 이렇게 뒤쫓는 동안, 창백하고 야윈 한 여인이 채찍을 들고 동굴에서 나와, 그의 발걸음을 바짝 따라붙으며 힘찬 매질로 그를 후려쳤다."], ["Her name was Repentance, and she told him it was her office to punish those who neglected to obey the voice of Prudence, and seize the fairy Fortune when he might.", "그녀의 이름은 후회였고, 그녀는 신중함의 목소리에 따르기를 게을리하여, 할 수 있을 때에 요정 운명을 붙잡지 않은 자들을 벌하는 것이 자신의 소임이라고 그에게 말했다."]], [["Orlando, furious at this chastisement, turned upon his tormentor, but might as well have stricken the wind.", "이 징벌에 격분한 오를란도는 자신을 괴롭히는 자에게 몸을 돌렸으나, 차라리 바람을 친 것이나 다름없었다."], ["Finding it useless to resist, he resumed his chase of the fairy, gained upon her, and made frequent snatches at her white and vermilion garments, which still eluded his grasp.", "저항해 봐야 소용없음을 알고, 그는 요정 추격을 다시 시작하여 그녀에게 다가갔고, 흰색과 진홍색의 옷을 거듭 낚아채려 했으나 그것은 여전히 그의 손아귀를 빠져나갔다."], ["At last, on her turning her head for an instant, he profited by the chance, and seized her by the forelock.", "마침내 그녀가 한순간 고개를 돌리자, 그는 그 틈을 타 그녀의 앞머리를 붙잡았다."], ["In an instant the tempest ceased, the sky became serene, and Repentance retreated to her cave.", "순식간에 폭풍이 멎고 하늘이 맑아졌으며, 후회는 자신의 동굴로 물러갔다."]], [["Orlando now demanded of Morgana the keys of her prison, and the fairy, feigning a complacent aspect, delivered up a key of silver, bidding him to be cautious in the use of it, since to break the lock would be to involve himself and all in inevitable destruction; a caution which gave the Count room for long meditation, and led him to consider", "이제 오를란도는 모르가나에게 그녀의 감옥 열쇠를 요구했고, 요정은 만족스러운 표정을 꾸미며 은으로 된 열쇠 하나를 내주면서, 그것을 쓸 때 조심하라고 일렀으니, 자물쇠를 부수면 자신과 모든 이를 피할 수 없는 파멸에 빠뜨리게 되리라는 것이었다. 이 주의는 백작에게 오랜 사색의 여지를 주었고, 그로 하여금 다음과 같은 점을 곰곰이 생각하게 했다."]], [["How few amid the suitors who importune The dame, know how to turn the keys of Fortune.", "그 귀부인에게 끈질기게 매달리는 구혼자들 가운데 운명의 열쇠를 돌릴 줄 아는 이가 어찌 그리 적은가."]], [["Keeping the fairy still fast by the forelock, Orlando proceeded toward the prison, turned the key, without occasioning the mischiefs apprehended, and delivered the prisoners.", "오를란도는 여전히 요정의 앞머리를 단단히 붙잡은 채 감옥을 향해 나아갔고, 우려했던 화를 일으키지 않고 열쇠를 돌려 포로들을 풀어 주었다."]], [["Among these were Florismart, Rinaldo, and many others of the bravest knights of France.", "이들 가운데에는 플로리스마트와 리날도, 그리고 프랑스의 가장 용맹한 기사들 여럿이 있었다."], ["Morgana had disappeared, and the knights, under the guidance of Orlando, retraced the path by which he had come.", "모르가나는 사라졌고, 기사들은 오를란도의 인도 아래 그가 왔던 길을 되짚어갔다."], ["They soon reached the field of treasure.", "그들은 곧 보물의 들판에 다다랐다."], ["Rinaldo, finding himself amidst this mass of wealth, remembered his needy garrison of Montalban, and could not resist the temptation of seizing part of the booty.", "리날도는 이 막대한 부 가운데에 있는 자신을 발견하고, 몬탈반의 궁핍한 수비대를 떠올리며, 전리품의 일부를 차지하고 싶은 유혹을 이겨 낼 수 없었다."], ["In particular a golden chain, studded with diamonds, was too much for his self-denial, and he took it and was bearing it off, notwithstanding the remonstrances of Orlando, when a violent wind caught him and whirled him back, as he approached the gate.", "특히 다이아몬드가 박힌 황금 사슬 하나는 그의 절제력으로는 너무 벅찼으니, 그는 오를란도의 만류에도 불구하고 그것을 집어 들고 가지고 가려 했다. 그런데 그가 문에 다가가자 거센 바람이 그를 붙잡아 뒤로 휘몰아쳤다."], ["This happened a second and a third time, and Rinaldo at length yielded to necessity, rather than to the entreaties of his friends, and cast away his prize.", "이것이 두 번, 세 번 일어나자, 리날도는 마침내 친구들의 간청보다는 어쩔 수 없는 형편에 굴복하여 자신이 차지한 것을 내던졌다."]], [["They soon reached the bridge and passed over without hindrance to the other side, where they found the trophy decorated with their arms.", "그들은 곧 다리에 다다라 거침없이 건너편으로 건너갔고, 그곳에서 자신들의 무기로 장식된 전리품 더미를 발견했다."], ["Here each knight resumed his own, and all, except the paladins and their friends, separated as their inclinations or duty prompted.", "여기서 기사들은 저마다 자기 것을 되찾았고, 무사들과 그 친구들을 제외한 모두는 각자의 뜻이나 의무가 이끄는 대로 흩어졌다."], ["Dudon, the Dane, one of the rescued knights, informed the cousins that he had been made prisoner by Morgana while in the discharge of an embassy to them from Charlemagne, who called upon them to return to the defence of Christendom.", "구출된 기사들 가운데 하나인 덴마크인 두돈은 사촌들에게 자신이 샤를마뉴에게서 그들에게 보내는 사명을 수행하던 중 모르가나에게 사로잡혔노라고 알렸으니, 샤를마뉴는 그들에게 기독교 세계의 방어를 위해 돌아오라고 요청했던 것이다."], ["Orlando was too much fascinated by Angelica to obey this summons, and, followed by the faithful Florismart, who would not leave him, returned towards Albracca.", "오를란도는 안젤리카에게 너무도 매혹되어 이 부름에 따를 수 없었고, 그를 떠나려 하지 않는 충실한 플로리스마트를 거느린 채 알브라카를 향해 돌아갔다."], ["Rinaldo, Dudon, Iroldo, Prasildo, and the others took their way toward the west.", "리날도와 두돈, 이롤도와 프라실도, 그리고 다른 이들은 서쪽으로 길을 잡았다."]], [["————", "————"]], [["# THE INVASION OF FRANCE", "# THE INVASION OF FRANCE"]], [["Agramant, King of Africa, convoked the kings, his vassals, to deliberate in council. He reminded them of the injuries he had sustained from France, that his father had fallen in battle with Charlemagne, and that his early years had hitherto not allowed him to wipe out the stain of former defeats. He now proposed to them to carry war into France.", "프랑스 침공"]], [["Sobrino, his wisest councillor, opposed the project, representing the rashness of it; but Rodomont, the young and fiery king of Algiers, denounced Sobrino’s counsel as base and cowardly, declaring himself impatient for the enterprise. The king of the Garamantes, venerable for his age and renowned for his prophetic lore, interposed, and assured the King that such an attempt would be sure to fail, unless he could first get on his side a youth marked out by destiny as the fitting compeer of the most puissant knights of France, the young Rogero, descended in direct line from Hector of Troy. This prince was now a dweller upon the mountain Carena, where Atlantes, his foster-father, a powerful magician, kept him in retirement, having discovered by his art that his pupil would be lost to him if allowed to mingle with the world. To break the spells of Atlantes, and draw Rogero from his retirement, one only means was to be found. It was a ring possessed by Angelica, Princess of Cathay, which was a talisman against all enchantments. If this ring could be procured all would go well; without it the enterprise was desperate.", "아프리카의 왕 아그라만트는 자신의 봉신인 왕들을 소집하여 회의에서 숙의하게 했다. 그는 자신이 프랑스에게서 입은 손해를, 자신의 부친이 샤를마뉴와의 전투에서 쓰러졌음을, 그리고 자신이 어린 나이여서 여태껏 지난 패배의 오욕을 씻을 수 없었음을 그들에게 일깨웠다. 이제 그는 그들에게 프랑스로 전쟁을 가져갈 것을 제안했다. 그의 가장 현명한 고문인 소브리노는 그 일의 무모함을 들어 그 계획에 반대했다. 그러나 젊고 혈기왕성한 알제의 왕 로도몬트는 소브리노의 충고를 비열하고 비겁하다고 규탄하며, 자신은 그 거사를 위해 안달이 났노라고 선언했다. 가라만테스의 왕은 나이로 존경받고 예언의 지식으로 명성이 높았는데, 끼어들어 왕에게 이렇게 단언했다. 그런 시도는 반드시 실패할 것이니, 다만 왕이 먼저 한 젊은이를 자기편으로 끌어들일 수 있다면 모를 일이라고. 그 젊은이는 프랑스의 가장 강력한 기사들의 합당한 적수로 운명에 의해 점지된 자로, 트로이아의 헥토르의 직계 후손인 젊은 로제로였다. 이 왕자는 이제 카레나 산에 머무는 자였으니, 그곳에서 강력한 마법사인 그의 양부 아틀란테스가 그를 은둔시켜 두었던 것이다. 아틀란테스는 자신의 제자가 세상과 어울리도록 두면 자신에게서 잃게 되리라는 것을 자신의 술법으로 알아냈던 것이다. 아틀란테스의 마법을 깨뜨리고 로제로를 그 은둔지에서 끌어낼 단 하나의 수단이 있었다. 그것은 카타이의 공주 안젤리카가 지닌 반지로, 모든 마법에 맞서는 부적이었다. 만일 이 반지를 손에 넣을 수 있다면 만사가 잘될 것이요, 그것이 없다면 그 거사는 가망이 없었다."]], [["Rodomont treated this declaration of the old prophet with scorn, and it would probably have been held of little weight by the council, had not the aged king, oppressed by the weight of years, expired in the very act of reaffirming his prediction.", "로도몬트는 늙은 예언자의 이 선언을 비웃었고, 만일 그 노왕이 세월의 무게에 짓눌려 자신의 예언을 재차 확언하는 바로 그 순간에 숨을 거두지 않았더라면, 그것은 아마 회의에서 별 무게도 갖지 못했을 것이다."], ["This made so deep an impression on the council that it was unanimously resolved to postpone the war until an effort should be made to win Rogero to the camp.", "이 일이 회의에 어찌나 깊은 인상을 남겼던지, 로제로를 진영으로 끌어들이려는 노력을 기울일 때까지 전쟁을 미루기로 만장일치로 결의했다."]], [["King Agramant thereupon proclaimed that the sovereignty of a kingdom should be the reward of whoever should succeed in obtaining the ring of Angelica.", "이에 아그라만트 왕은 안젤리카의 반지를 손에 넣는 데 성공하는 자에게 한 왕국의 통치권을 상으로 주겠노라고 선포했다."], ["Brunello, the dwarf, the subtlest thief in all Africa, undertook to procure it.", "아프리카 전체에서 가장 교활한 도둑인 난쟁이 브루넬로가 그것을 손에 넣겠노라 나섰다."]], [["In prosecution of this design, he made the best of his way to Angelica’s kingdom, and arrived beneath the walls of Albracca while the besieging army was encamped before the fortress.", "이 계획을 추진하기 위해 그는 안젤리카의 왕국으로 한껏 서둘러 가서, 포위군이 요새 앞에 진을 친 동안 알브라카의 성벽 아래에 다다랐다."], ["While the attention of the garrison was absorbed by the battle that raged below he scaled the walls, approached the Princess unnoticed, slipped the ring from her finger, and escaped unobserved.", "수비대의 주의가 아래에서 벌어지는 전투에 쏠려 있는 동안, 그는 성벽을 기어올라 아무도 모르게 공주에게 다가가, 그녀의 손가락에서 반지를 빼내어 들키지 않고 달아났다."], ["He hastened to the seaside, and, finding a vessel ready to sail, embarked, and arrived at Biserta, in Africa.", "그는 바닷가로 서둘러 가, 막 출항하려는 배 한 척을 발견하고 올라타, 아프리카의 비세르타에 다다랐다."], ["Here he found Agramant impatient for the talisman which was to foil the enchantments of Atlantes and to put Rogero into his hands.", "여기서 그는 아틀란테스의 마법을 꺾고 로제로를 자신의 손안에 넣어 줄 그 부적을 애타게 기다리던 아그라만트를 만났다."], ["The dwarf, kneeling before the king, presented him with the ring, and Agramant, delighted at the success of his mission, crowned him in recompense King of Tingitana.", "난쟁이는 왕 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 그에게 반지를 바쳤고, 그 사명의 성공에 기뻐한 아그라만트는 그 보답으로 그를 팅기타나의 왕으로 봉했다."]], [["All were now anxious to go in quest of Rogero.", "이제 모두가 로제로를 찾아 나서기를 간절히 바랐다."], ["The cavalcade accordingly departed, and in due time arrived at the mountain of Carena.", "이에 따라 행렬이 떠났고, 때맞춰 카레나 산에 다다랐다."]], [["At the bottom of this was a fruitful and well-wooded plain, watered by a large river, and from this plain was descried a beautiful garden on the mountain-top, which contained the mansion of Atlantes; but the ring, which discovered what was before invisible, could not, though it revealed this paradise, enable Agramant or his followers to enter it.", "이 산기슭에는 비옥하고 숲이 우거진 평원이 있어 큰 강이 흐르며 적셔 주었고, 이 평원에서 산꼭대기에 있는 아름다운 정원이 바라다보였으니, 그곳에는 아틀란테스의 저택이 있었다. 그러나 전에는 보이지 않던 것을 드러내 주는 그 반지도, 이 낙원을 드러내 보이기는 했으되, 아그라만트나 그 부하들이 그 안으로 들어가게 해 주지는 못했다."], ["So steep and smooth was the rock by nature, that even Brunello failed in every attempt to scale it.", "그 바위는 본래 어찌나 가파르고 매끄러운지, 브루넬로조차 그것을 기어오르려는 모든 시도에 실패했다."], ["He did not, for this, despair of accomplishing the object; but, having obtained Agramant’s consent, caused the assembled courtiers and knights to celebrate a tournament upon the plain below.", "그러나 그는 이 때문에 목적을 이루기를 단념하지는 않았으니, 아그라만트의 동의를 얻어, 모인 조신들과 기사들로 하여금 아래의 평원에서 마상 시합을 벌이게 했다."], ["This was done with the view of seducing Rogero from his fastness, and the stratagem was attended with success.", "이는 로제로를 그 요새에서 꾀어내려는 의도로 행한 것이었고, 그 책략은 성공을 거두었다."]], [["Rogero joined the tourney, and was presented by Agramant with a splendid horse, Frontino, and a magnificent sword.", "로제로는 시합에 참가했고, 아그라만트에게서 프론티노라는 훌륭한 말 한 마리와 화려한 검 한 자루를 선사받았다."], ["Having learned from Agramant his intended invasion of France, he gladly consented to join the expedition.", "아그라만트에게서 그가 계획한 프랑스 침공에 관해 듣고, 그는 기꺼이 그 원정에 합류하기로 했다."]], [["Rodomont, meanwhile, was too impatient to wait for Agramant’s arrangements, and embarked with all the forces he could raise, made good his landing on the coast of France, and routed the Christians in several encounters.", "한편 로도몬트는 아그라만트의 준비를 기다리기에는 너무 조급하여, 자신이 모을 수 있는 모든 병력을 거느리고 배에 올라, 프랑스 해안에 무사히 상륙하여 여러 차례 교전에서 기독교도들을 무찔렀다."], ["Previously to this, however, Gano, or Ganelon (as he is sometimes called), the traitor, enemy of Orlando and the other nephews of Charlemagne, had entered into a traitorous correspondence with Marsilius, the Saracen king of Spain, whom he invited into France.", "그러나 이에 앞서, 오를란도와 샤를마뉴의 다른 조카들의 적인 배신자 가노, 혹은 (때로 불리는 이름으로) 가넬론이, 에스파냐의 사라센 왕 마르실리우스와 반역의 내통을 맺고 그를 프랑스로 끌어들였다."], ["Marsilius, thus encouraged, led an army across the frontiers, and joined Rodomont.", "이에 고무된 마르실리우스는 군대를 이끌고 국경을 넘어 로도몬트와 합류했다."], ["This was the situation of things when Rinaldo and the other knights who had obeyed the summons of Dudon set forward on their return to France.", "두돈의 부름에 따른 리날도와 다른 기사들이 프랑스로 돌아가는 길에 나섰을 때 사정은 이러했다."]], [["When they arrived at Buda in Hungary they found the king of that country about despatching his son, Ottachiero, with an army to the succor of Charlemagne.", "그들이 헝가리의 부다에 다다랐을 때, 그 나라의 왕이 막 자신의 아들 오타키에로를 군대와 함께 샤를마뉴를 구원하러 보내려 하고 있음을 알게 되었다."], ["Delighted with the arrival of Rinaldo, he placed his son and troops under his command.", "리날도가 당도한 것에 기뻐한 왕은 자신의 아들과 군대를 그의 지휘 아래 두었다."], ["In due time the army arrived on the frontiers of France, and, united with the troops of Desiderius, king of Lombardy, poured down into Provence.", "때맞춰 군대는 프랑스 국경에 다다랐고, 롬바르디아의 왕 데시데리우스의 군대와 합쳐 프로방스로 쏟아져 내려갔다."], ["The confederate armies had not marched many days through this gay tract before they heard a crash of drums and trumpets behind the hills, which spoke the conflict between the paynims, led by Rodomont, and the Christian forces.", "연합군이 이 화창한 지방을 행군한 지 며칠 되지 않아, 그들은 언덕 너머에서 북과 나팔이 요란하게 울리는 소리를 들었으니, 그것은 로도몬트가 이끄는 이교도들과 기독교군 사이의 충돌을 알리는 것이었다."], ["Rinaldo, witnessing from a mountain the prowess of Rodomont, left his troops in charge of his friends, and galloped towards him with his lance in rest.", "산 위에서 로도몬트의 무용을 목격한 리날도는, 자신의 군대를 친구들에게 맡기고 창을 겨눈 채 그를 향해 말을 달렸다."], ["The impulse was irresistible, and Rodomont was unhorsed.", "그 기세는 거역할 수 없었고, 로도몬트는 말에서 떨어졌다."], ["But Rinaldo, unwilling to avail himself of his advantage, galloped back to the hill, and having secured Bayard among the baggage, returned to finish the combat on foot.", "그러나 리날도는 그 유리함을 이용하기를 꺼려, 언덕으로 다시 말을 달려 짐들 사이에 바야르를 안전히 둔 뒤, 걸어서 싸움을 끝내러 돌아왔다."]], [["During this interval the battle had become general, the Hungarians were routed, and Rinaldo, on his return, had the mortification to find that Ottachiero was wounded, and Dudon taken prisoner.", "이 사이에 전투는 전면전이 되었고, 헝가리군은 패주했으며, 돌아온 리날도는 오타키에로가 부상당하고 두돈이 포로로 잡힌 것을 알고 비통해했다."], ["While he sought Rodomont in order to renew the combat a new sound of drums and trumpets was heard, and Charlemagne, with the main body of his army, was descried advancing in battle array.", "싸움을 다시 시작하려고 로도몬트를 찾는 동안, 북과 나팔의 새로운 소리가 들려왔고, 샤를마뉴가 자신의 본대를 거느리고 전투 대형으로 진격해 오는 것이 보였다."]], [["Rodomont, seeing this, mounted the horse of Dudon, left Rinaldo, who was on foot, and galloped off to encounter this new enemy.", "이것을 본 로도몬트는 두돈의 말에 올라, 걸어 다니던 리날도를 내버려 두고 이 새로운 적을 맞으러 말을 달려 갔다."]], [["Agramant, accompanied by Rogero, had by this time made good his landing, and joined Rodomont with all his forces.", "이때쯤 로제로를 거느린 아그라만트가 무사히 상륙하여 자신의 전 병력을 이끌고 로도몬트와 합류했다."], ["Rogero eagerly embraced this first opportunity of distinguishing himself, and spread terror wherever he went, encountering in turn and overthrowing many of the bravest knights of France.", "로제로는 자신을 떨칠 이 첫 기회를 열렬히 붙잡아, 가는 곳마다 공포를 퍼뜨리며, 프랑스의 가장 용맹한 기사들을 차례로 마주쳐 쓰러뜨렸다."], ["At length he found himself opposite to Rinaldo, who, being interrupted, as we have said, in his combat with Rodomont, and unable to follow him, being on foot, was shouting to his late foe to return and finish their combat.", "마침내 그는 리날도와 마주하게 되었으니, 리날도는 앞서 말했듯 로도몬트와의 싸움이 중단된 데다, 걸어 다니느라 그를 뒤쫓을 수 없어, 지난번의 적에게 돌아와 싸움을 끝내자고 외치고 있었다."], ["Rogero also was on foot, and seeing the Christian knight so eager for a contest, proffered himself to supply the place of his late antagonist.", "로제로 역시 걸어 다니고 있었는데, 그 기독교 기사가 그토록 싸움에 열심인 것을 보고, 자신이 그 지난번 적수를 대신하겠노라 자청했다."], ["Rinaldo saw at a glance that the Moorish prince was a champion worthy of his arm, and gladly accepted the defiance.", "리날도는 그 무어 왕자가 자신의 팔에 걸맞은 용사임을 한눈에 알아보고 기꺼이 그 도전을 받아들였다."], ["The combat was stoutly maintained for a time; but now fortune declared decisively in favor of the infidel army, and Charlemagne’s forces gave way at all points in irreparable confusion.", "한동안 싸움은 굳세게 이어졌다. 그러나 이제 운명은 이교도 군대에 확연히 유리하게 기울었고, 샤를마뉴의 군대는 모든 곳에서 회복할 수 없는 혼란 속에 무너졌다."], ["The two combatants were separated by the crowd of fugitives and pursuers, and Rinaldo hastened to recover possession of his horse.", "두 결투자는 패주하는 자들과 추격하는 자들의 무리에 의해 갈라졌고, 리날도는 서둘러 자신의 말을 되찾으려 했다."], ["But Bayard, in the confusion, had got loose, and Rinaldo followed him into a thick wood, thus becoming effectually separated from Rogero.", "그러나 바야르는 혼란 통에 풀려나 있었고, 리날도는 그것을 따라 울창한 숲속으로 들어갔으니, 이리하여 그는 로제로와 완전히 갈라지게 되었다."]], [["Rogero, also seeking his horse in the medley, came where two warriors were engaged in mortal combat.", "로제로 역시 그 난전 속에서 자신의 말을 찾던 중, 두 전사가 사투를 벌이고 있는 곳에 이르렀다."], ["Though he knew not who they were, he could distinguish that one was a paynim and the other a Christian; and moved by the spirit of courtesy he approached them and exclaimed, “Let him of the two who worships Christ pause, and hear what I have to say.", "그들이 누구인지는 알지 못했으나, 하나는 이교도이고 다른 하나는 기독교도임은 분간할 수 있었다. 예의의 정신에 움직인 그는 그들에게 다가가 외쳤다."], ["The army of Charles is routed and in flight, so that if he wishes to follow his leader he has no time for delay.”", "\"둘 중 그리스도를 섬기는 자는 멈추고 내가 할 말을 들으시오. 샤를의 군대가 패하여 달아나고 있으니, 그가 자신의 지휘관을 따르고자 한다면 지체할 겨를이 없소.\""], ["The Christian knight, who was none other than Bradamante, a female warrior, in prowess equal to the best of knights, was thunderstruck with the tidings, and would gladly leave the contest undecided, and retire from the field; but Rodomont, her antagonist, would by no means consent.", "그 기독교 기사는 다름 아닌 브라다만테로, 무용에서 가장 뛰어난 기사들에 못지않은 여전사였는데, 그 소식에 아연실색하여 기꺼이 싸움을 결판내지 않은 채 두고 전장에서 물러나려 했다. 그러나 그녀의 적수인 로도몬트는 결코 동의하려 하지 않았다."], ["Rogero, indignant at his discourtesy, insisted upon her departure, while he took up her quarrel with Rodomont.", "그의 무례함에 분개한 로제로는 그녀더러 떠나라고 고집하며, 자신이 로도몬트와의 다툼을 떠맡았다."]], [["The combat, obstinately maintained on both sides, was interrupted by the return of Bradamante.", "양쪽 모두 완강하게 이어 가던 싸움은 브라다만테의 귀환으로 중단되었다."], ["Finding herself unable to overtake the fugitives, and reluctant to leave to another the burden and risk of a contest which belonged to herself, she had returned to reclaim the combat.", "그녀는 달아나는 자들을 따라잡을 수 없음을 알았고, 본래 자신에게 속한 싸움의 짐과 위험을 다른 이에게 맡기기를 꺼려, 싸움을 되찾으려고 돌아왔던 것이다."], ["She arrived, however, when her champion had dealt his enemy such a blow as obliged him to drop both his sword and bridle.", "그러나 그녀가 당도했을 때 자신의 용사는 적에게 일격을 가하여, 적으로 하여금 칼과 고삐를 둘 다 떨어뜨리게 만든 참이었다."], ["Rogero, disdaining to profit by his adversary’s defenceless situation, sat apart upon his horse, while that of Rodomont bore his rider, stunned and stupefied, about the field.", "로제로는 적수의 무방비한 처지를 이용하기를 마다하여 자신의 말 위에 따로 떨어져 앉아 있었고, 그동안 로도몬트의 말은 정신이 멍하고 얼떨떨해진 기수를 태운 채 들판을 이리저리 내달렸다."]], [["Bradamante approached Rogero, conceiving a yet higher opinion of his valor on beholding such an instance of forbearance.", "브라다만테는 로제로에게 다가갔으니, 그러한 자제의 본보기를 보고 그의 무용에 한층 더 높은 평가를 품게 되었다."], ["She addressed him, excusing herself for leaving him exposed to an enemy from his interference in her cause; pleading her duty to her sovereign as the motive.", "그녀는 그에게 말을 건네며, 자신의 일에 끼어든 그를 적에게 노출된 채 두고 떠난 것을 사과하고, 자신의 군주에 대한 의무를 그 동기로 들어 변명했다."], ["While she spoke Rodomont, recovered from his confusion, rode up to them.", "그녀가 말하는 동안, 혼란에서 회복된 로도몬트가 그들에게 말을 달려 왔다."], ["His bearing was, however, changed; and he disclaimed all thoughts of further contest with one who, he said, “had already conquered him by his courtesy.”", "그러나 그의 태도는 달라져 있었으니, 그는 \"이미 자신을 예의로써 정복한\" 자와 더 싸울 생각이 전혀 없다고 밝혔다."], ["So saying, he quitted his antagonist, picked up his sword, and spurred out of sight.", "이렇게 말하고는 적수를 떠나, 자신의 칼을 주워 들고 박차를 가해 시야에서 사라졌다."]], [["Bradamante was now again desirous of retiring from the field, and Rogero insisted on accompanying her, though yet unaware of her sex.", "이제 브라다만테는 다시 전장에서 물러나고 싶어 했고, 로제로는 그녀의 성을 아직 알지 못한 채 그녀를 따라가겠노라 고집했다."]], [["As they pursued their way, she inquired the name and quality of her new associate; and Rogero informed her of his nation and family.", "길을 가던 그녀는 새로운 동행자의 이름과 신분을 물었고, 로제로는 자신의 나라와 가문을 그녀에게 알려 주었다."], ["He told her that Astyanax, the son of Hector of Troy, established the kingdom of Messina in Sicily.", "그는 그녀에게 트로이아의 헥토르의 아들 아스티아낙스가 시칠리아의 메시나 왕국을 세웠다고 말했다."], ["From him were derived two branches, which gave origin to two families of renown.", "그에게서 두 갈래가 갈라져 나와 두 명문 가문의 기원이 되었다."], ["From one sprang the royal race of Pepin and Charlemagne, and from the other, that of Reggio, in Italy.", "한 갈래에서는 페핀과 샤를마뉴의 왕가가 나왔고, 다른 갈래에서는 이탈리아 레조의 가문이 나왔다."], ["“From that of Reggio am I derived,”", "\"내가 나온 것은 그 레조의 가문이오.\""], ["he continued.", "그가 말을 이었다."], ["“My mother, driven from her home by the chance of war, died in giving me life, and I was taken in charge by a sage enchanter, who trained me to feats of arms amidst the dangers of the desert and the chase.”", "\"내 어머니는 전쟁의 우연으로 고향에서 쫓겨나, 내게 생명을 주다가 돌아가셨고, 나는 한 현명한 마법사의 보살핌을 받았으니, 그는 나를 사막과 사냥의 위험 속에서 무예의 위업을 익히도록 길렀소.\""]], [["Having thus ended his tale, Rogero entreated a similar return of courtesy from his companion, who replied, without disguise, that she was of the race of Clermont, and sister to Rinaldo, whose fame was perhaps known to him.", "이렇게 이야기를 마친 로제로는 동행자에게도 마찬가지로 예의로써 답해 주기를 청했고, 그녀는 거리낌 없이 자신이 클레르몽의 혈통이며, 그에게도 아마 명성이 알려졌을 리날도의 누이라고 대답했다."], ["Rogero, much moved by this intelligence, entreated her to take off her helmet, and at the discovery of her face remained transported with delight.", "로제로는 이 소식에 크게 마음이 움직여 그녀에게 투구를 벗어 달라고 청했고, 그녀의 얼굴이 드러나자 기쁨에 도취된 채로 있었다."]], [["While absorbed in this contemplation, an unexpected danger assailed them.", "이 응시에 빠져 있는 동안, 뜻밖의 위험이 그들을 덮쳤다."], ["A party which was placed in a wood, in order to intercept the retreating Christians, broke from its ambush upon the pair, and Bradamante, who was uncasqued, was wounded in the head.", "퇴각하는 기독교도들을 가로막으려고 숲속에 배치되어 있던 한 무리가 매복에서 뛰쳐나와 두 사람에게 달려들었고, 투구를 벗고 있던 브라다만테는 머리에 부상을 입었다."], ["Rogero was in a fury at this attack; and Bradamante, replacing her helmet, joined him in taking speedy vengeance on their enemies.", "로제로는 이 공격에 격노했고, 브라다만테는 투구를 다시 쓰고 그와 함께 자신들의 적에게 신속한 복수를 가했다."], ["They cleared the field of them, but became separated in the pursuit, and Rogero, quitting the chase, wandered by hill and vale in search of her whom he had no sooner found than lost.", "그들은 들판에서 적을 쓸어버렸으나, 추격 중에 서로 갈라지게 되었고, 추격을 그만둔 로제로는, 찾자마자 곧 잃어버린 그녀를 찾아 산과 골짜기를 헤매고 다녔다."]], [["While pursuing this quest he fell in with two knights, whom he joined, and engaged them to assist him in the search of his companion, describing her arms, but concealing, from a certain feeling of jealousy, her quality and sex.", "이 탐색을 이어 가던 중 그는 두 기사와 마주쳤고, 그들과 합류하여 자신의 동행자를 찾는 일을 도와 달라고 청하며 그녀의 무기를 묘사했으나, 어떤 질투의 감정에서 그녀의 신분과 성별은 감추었다."]], [["It was evening when they joined company, and having ridden together through the night the morning was beginning to break, when one of the strangers, fixing his eyes upon Rogero’s shield, demanded of him by what right he bore the Trojan arms.", "그들이 합류한 것은 저녁이었고, 함께 밤새 말을 달려 아침이 막 밝아 오기 시작할 무렵, 두 낯선 자 가운데 하나가 로제로의 방패에 시선을 고정하더니, 무슨 권리로 트로이아의 문장을 달고 있느냐고 물었다."], ["Rogero declared his origin and race, and then, in his turn, interrogated the inquirer as to his pretensions to the cognizance of Hector, which he bore.", "로제로는 자신의 출신과 혈통을 밝힌 뒤, 이번에는 자신이 그 질문자에게 그가 달고 있는 헥토르의 문장에 대한 그의 권리를 따져 물었다."], ["The stranger replied, “My name is Mandricardo, son of Agrican, the Tartar king, whom Orlando treacherously slew.", "낯선 자가 대답했다. \"내 이름은 만드리카르도, 타타르의 왕 아그리칸의 아들로, 오를란도가 비겁하게 죽인 그 왕이오."], ["I say treacherously, for in fair fight he could not have done it.", "비겁하게라고 말하는 것은, 정정당당한 싸움에서라면 그가 그러지 못했을 것이기 때문이오."], ["It is in search of him that I have come to France, to take vengeance for my father, and to wrest from him Durindana, that famous sword, which belongs to me, and not to him.”", "내가 프랑스로 온 것은 그를 찾아, 내 아버지의 원수를 갚고, 또 그에게서 두린다나를 빼앗기 위함이오. 그 유명한 검은 내 것이지 그의 것이 아니오.\""], ["When the knights demanded to know by what right he claimed Durindana, Mandricardo thus related his history:", "기사들이 무슨 권리로 두린다나를 주장하느냐고 묻자, 만드리카르도는 이렇게 자신의 내력을 들려주었다."]], [["“I had been, before the death of my father, a wild and reckless youth.", "\"나는 아버지가 돌아가시기 전에는 거칠고 무모한 젊은이였소."], ["That event awakened my energies, and drove me forth to seek for vengeance.", "그 사건이 내 기력을 일깨워, 나를 복수를 찾아 나서게 했소."], ["Determined to owe success to nothing but my own exertions, I departed without attendants or horse or arms.", "오직 나 자신의 노력에만 성공을 빚지기로 작정하고, 나는 시종도 말도 무기도 없이 떠났소."], ["Travelling thus alone, and on foot, I espied one day a pavilion, pitched near a fountain, and entered it, intent on adventure.", "이렇게 홀로, 걸어서 여행하던 어느 날, 나는 샘 가까이에 친 천막을 하나 발견하고, 모험을 좇아 그 안으로 들어갔소."], ["I found therein a damsel of gracious aspect, who replied to my inquiries that the fountain was the work of a fairy, whose castle stood beyond a neighboring hill, where she kept watch over a treasure which many knights had tried to win, but fruitlessly, having lost their life or liberty in the attempt.", "거기서 나는 우아한 용모의 한 처녀를 만났는데, 그녀는 내 물음에 답하여, 그 샘은 한 요정의 작품으로, 그 요정의 성은 이웃 언덕 너머에 있으며, 거기서 그녀가 한 보물을 지키고 있는데, 많은 기사가 그것을 얻으려 했으나 헛되이, 그 시도에서 목숨이나 자유를 잃었다고 말했소."], ["This treasure was the armor of Hector, prince of Troy, whom Achilles treacherously slew.", "그 보물은 트로이아의 왕자 헥토르의 갑옷이었으니, 헥토르는 아킬레우스가 비겁하게 죽인 자요."], ["Nothing was wanting but his sword, Durindana, and this had fallen into the possession of a queen named Penthesilea, from whom it passed through her descendants to Almontes, whom Orlando slew, and thus became possessed of the sword.", "부족한 것이라곤 그의 검 두린다나뿐이었는데, 이것은 펜테실레이아라는 여왕의 손에 들어갔다가, 그녀의 후손들을 거쳐 알몬테스에게 넘어갔고, 오를란도가 알몬테스를 죽이고 이리하여 그 검을 손에 넣게 된 것이오."], ["The rest of Hector’s arms were saved and carried off by Æneas, from whom this fairy received them in recompense of service rendered.", "헥토르의 나머지 무기들은 아이네아스가 건져 가져갔고, 이 요정이 그에게서 봉사의 보답으로 그것을 받은 것이오. '만일 그것을 손에 넣을 용기가 있다면,'"], ["‘If you have the courage to attempt their acquisition,’ said the damsel, ‘I will be your guide.’”", "처녀가 말했소. '내가 그대의 안내자가 되어 주겠소.'\""]], [["Mandricardo went on to say that he eagerly embraced the proposal, and being provided with horse and armor by the damsel, set forth on his enterprise, the lady accompanying him.", "만드리카르도는 이어서 말하기를, 자신은 그 제안을 열렬히 받아들였고, 처녀에게서 말과 갑옷을 제공받아 자신의 거사에 나섰으며, 그 여인이 그와 동행했다고 했다."]], [["As they rode she explained the dangers of the quest.", "그들이 말을 달리는 동안 그녀는 그 탐색의 위험을 설명해 주었다."], ["The armor was defended by a champion, one of the numerous unsuccessful adventurers for the prize, all of whom had been made prisoners by the fairy, and compelled to take their turn, day by day, in defending the arms against all comers.", "그 갑옷은 한 용사가 지키고 있었으니, 그는 그 상을 노렸으나 실패한 수많은 모험가 가운데 하나로, 그들 모두는 요정에게 사로잡혀, 날마다 차례로 그 무기를 지키며 오는 자마다 모두 막아 내도록 강요받았다."], ["Thus speaking they arrived at the castle, which was of alabaster, overlaid with gold.", "이렇게 이야기하며 그들은 성에 다다랐으니, 그것은 설화석고로 만들어져 금으로 덧입혀져 있었다."], ["Before it, on a lawn, sat an armed knight on horseback, who was none other than Gradasso, king of Sericane, who, in his return home from his unsuccessful inroad into France, had fallen into the power of the fairy, and was held to do her bidding.", "그 앞 잔디밭에는 무장한 기사 하나가 말에 타고 앉아 있었는데, 그는 다름 아닌 세리카네의 왕 그라다소로, 프랑스로의 무모한 침입에 실패하고 고국으로 돌아가던 길에 그 요정의 힘에 빠져 그녀의 분부를 따르고 있던 자였다."], ["Mandricardo, upon seeing him, dropt his visor, and laid his lance in rest.", "만드리카르도는 그를 보자 면갑을 내리고 창을 겨누었다."], ["The champion of the castle was equally ready, and each spurred towards his opponent.", "성의 용사도 그에 못지않게 채비를 갖추었고, 둘은 각자 적을 향해 박차를 가했다."], ["They met one another with equal force, splintered their spears, and, returning to the charge, encountered with their swords.", "그들은 똑같은 힘으로 서로 맞부딪쳐 창을 산산이 부수었고, 다시 돌격하여 칼로 맞붙었다."], ["The contest was long and doubtful, when Mandricardo, determined to bring it to an end, threw his arms about Gradasso, grappled with him, and both fell to the ground.", "싸움은 길고 승부를 가늠하기 어려웠는데, 만드리카르도는 그것을 끝내기로 작정하고 그라다소에게 두 팔을 두르고 맞붙어 격투하여 둘 다 땅으로 쓰러졌다."], ["Mandricardo, however, fell uppermost, and, preserving his advantage, compelled Gradasso to yield himself conquered.", "그러나 만드리카르도가 위에 떨어졌고, 그 유리함을 지켜 그라다소로 하여금 패배를 인정하게 만들었다."], ["The damsel now interfered, congratulating the victor, and consoling the vanquished as well as she might.", "이제 처녀가 끼어들어 승자에게 축하를 건네고, 패자도 할 수 있는 한 위로했다."]], [["Mandricardo and the damsel proceeded to the gate of the castle, which they found undefended.", "만드리카르도와 처녀는 성문으로 나아갔는데, 그것은 지키는 자가 없었다."], ["As they entered they beheld a shield suspended from a pilaster of gold.", "안으로 들어가자 그들은 황금 벽기둥에 매달린 방패 하나를 보았다."], ["The device was a white eagle on an azure field, in memory of the bird of Jove, which bore away Ganymede, the flower of the Phrygian race.", "그 문장은 하늘빛 바탕에 흰 독수리였으니, 프리기아 종족의 꽃인 가니메데스를 채어 간 제우스[로마의 〈유피테르〉]의 새를 기리는 것이었다."], ["Beneath was engraved the following couplet:", "그 아래에는 다음과 같은 시구가 새겨져 있었다."]], [["“Let none with hand profane my buckler wrong Unless he be himself as Hector strong.”", "\"헥토르처럼 강한 자가 아니라면 그 누구도 부정한 손으로 내 방패를 욕되게 하지 말라.\""]], [["The damsel, alighting from her palfrey, made obeisance to the arms, bending herself to the ground.", "처녀는 조랑말에서 내려, 몸을 땅에 굽혀 그 무기에 절을 올렸다."], ["The Tartar king bowed his head with equal reverence; then advancing towards the shield, touched it with his sword.", "타타르 왕도 똑같은 경의로 머리를 숙였다. 그러고는 방패를 향해 나아가 자신의 칼로 그것을 건드렸다."], ["Thereupon an earthquake shook the ground, and the way by which he had entered closed.", "그러자 지진이 땅을 뒤흔들었고, 그가 들어온 길이 닫혔다."], ["Another and an opposite gate opened, and displayed a field bristling with stalks and grain of gold.", "또 다른, 반대편의 문이 열려, 황금 줄기와 곡식이 빽빽이 들어선 들판을 드러냈다."], ["The damsel, upon this, told him that he had no means of retreat but by cutting down the harvest which was before him, and by uprooting a tree which grew in the middle of the field.", "이에 처녀는 그에게, 자신의 앞에 있는 그 곡식을 베어 넘기고, 들판 한가운데 자란 나무 하나를 뿌리째 뽑는 것 말고는 빠져나갈 길이 없다고 말했다."], ["Mandricardo, without replying, began to mow the harvest with his sword, but had scarce smitten thrice when he perceived that every stalk that fell was instantly transformed into some poisonous or ravenous animal, which prepared to assail him.", "만드리카르도는 아무 대꾸 없이 칼로 곡식을 베기 시작했으나, 세 번도 채 내려치기 전에, 쓰러지는 줄기마다 즉시 어떤 독을 품거나 굶주린 짐승으로 변하여 그를 덮치려 채비하는 것을 보았다."], ["Instructed by the damsel, he snatched up a stone and cast it among the pack.", "처녀의 가르침을 받은 그는 돌 하나를 집어 그 무리 가운데로 던졌다."], ["A strange wonder followed; for no sooner had the stone fallen among the beasts, than they turned their rage against one another, and rent each other to pieces.", "기이한 경이가 뒤따랐으니, 돌이 그 짐승들 사이에 떨어지자마자 그것들은 서로에게 분노를 돌려 서로를 갈가리 찢었기 때문이다."], ["Mandricardo did not stop to marvel at the miracle, but proceeded to fulfil his task, and uproot the tree.", "만드리카르도는 그 기적에 감탄하느라 멈추지 않고, 자신의 과업을 이루어 나무를 뽑으러 나아갔다."], ["He clasped it round the trunk, and made vigorous efforts to tear it up by the roots.", "그는 줄기를 둘러 끌어안고, 그것을 뿌리째 뽑아내려 힘껏 애를 썼다."], ["At each effort fell a shower of leaves, that were instantly changed into birds of prey, which attacked the knight, flapping their wings in his face, with horrid screeching.", "한 번씩 힘을 쓸 때마다 잎이 소나기처럼 떨어졌고, 그것은 즉시 맹금으로 변하여 그 기사를 공격하며, 끔찍한 비명을 지르면서 그의 얼굴에 날개를 퍼덕였다."], ["But undismayed by this new annoyance, he continued to tug at the trunk till it yielded to his efforts.", "그러나 그는 이 새로운 성가심에도 굴하지 않고, 줄기가 자신의 힘에 굴복할 때까지 계속 잡아당겼다."], ["A burst of wind and thunder followed, and the hawks and vultures flew screaming away.", "한바탕 바람과 천둥이 뒤따랐고, 매와 독수리들이 비명을 지르며 날아가 버렸다."]], [["But these only gave place to a new foe; for from the hole made by tearing up the tree issued a furious serpent, and, darting at Mandricardo, wound herself about his limbs with a strain that almost crushed him.", "그러나 이것들은 다만 새로운 적에게 자리를 내주었을 뿐이었다. 나무를 뽑아내며 생긴 구멍에서 사나운 뱀 한 마리가 나와, 만드리카르도에게 달려들어, 그를 거의 으스러뜨릴 만한 힘으로 그의 사지를 휘감았기 때문이다."], ["Fortune, however, again stood his friend, for, writhing under the folds of the monster, he fell backwards into the hole, and his enemy was crushed beneath his weight.", "그러나 운명은 또다시 그의 편이 되어 주었으니, 그 괴물의 똬리 아래 몸부림치다가 그가 구멍 속으로 뒤로 넘어졌고, 그의 적은 그의 무게에 짓눌려 으스러졌다."]], [["Mandricardo, when he was somewhat recovered, and assured himself of the destruction of the serpent, began to contemplate the place into which he had fallen, and saw that he was in a vault, incrusted with costly metals, and illuminated by a live coal.", "만드리카르도는 어느 정도 회복되어 뱀이 죽은 것을 확인하자, 자신이 떨어진 곳을 살펴보기 시작했고, 자신이 값진 금속으로 입혀지고 한 점 타는 숯불로 밝혀진 지하실 안에 있음을 보았다."], ["In the middle was a sort of ivory bier, and upon this was extended what appeared to be a knight in armor, but was in truth an empty trophy, composed of the rich and precious arms once Hector’s, to which nothing was wanting but the sword.", "한가운데에는 일종의 상아 관대가 있었고, 그 위에는 갑옷을 입은 기사처럼 보이는 것이 누워 있었으나, 그것은 사실 빈 전리품으로, 한때 헥토르의 것이었던 풍요롭고 값진 무기들로 이루어진 것이었으니, 부족한 것이라곤 그 검뿐이었다."], ["While Mandricardo stood contemplating the prize a door opened behind him, and a bevy of fair damsels entered, dancing, who, taking up the armor piece by piece, led him away to the place where the shield was suspended; where he found the fairy of the castle seated in state.", "만드리카르도가 그 상을 바라보며 서 있을 때, 그의 뒤로 문이 열리더니, 아름다운 처녀의 무리가 춤추며 들어와, 갑옷을 한 조각씩 집어 들고 그를 방패가 매달린 곳으로 데려갔다. 거기서 그는 위풍당당하게 앉아 있는 성의 요정을 보았다."], ["By her he was invested with the arms he had won, first pledging his solemn oath to wear no other blade but Durindana, which he was to wrest from Orlando, and thus complete the conquest of Hector’s arms.", "그녀에 의해 그는 자신이 얻은 무기로 무장되었으니, 다만 먼저 오를란도에게서 빼앗아 헥토르의 무기를 손에 넣는 일을 완성할 두린다나 외에는 다른 어떤 칼도 차지 않겠노라 엄숙한 맹세를 한 뒤였다."]], [["————", "————"]], [["Mandricardo, having completed his story, now turned to Rogero, and proposed that arms should decide which of the two was most worthy to bear the symbol of the Trojan knight.", "프랑스 침공 (계속)\n이야기를 마친 만드리카르도는 이제 로제로를 향해, 둘 중 누가 트로이아 기사의 상징을 달기에 가장 합당한지 무기로 결판내자고 제안했다."]], [["Rogero felt no other objection to this proposal than the scruple which arose on observing that his antagonist was without a sword.", "로제로는 자신의 적수에게 칼이 없다는 점을 보고 일어난 거리낌 외에는 이 제안에 다른 이의가 없었다."], ["Mandricardo insisted that this need be no impediment, since his oath prevented him from using a sword until he should have achieved the conquest of Durindana.", "만드리카르도는 그것이 장애가 될 필요는 없다고 고집했으니, 자신의 맹세가 두린다나를 손에 넣기 전에는 칼을 쓰지 못하도록 막고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["This was no sooner said than a new antagonist started up in Gradasso, who now accompanied Mandricardo.", "이 말이 떨어지기가 무섭게 이제 만드리카르도와 동행하던 그라다소가 새로운 적수로 나섰다."], ["Gradasso vindicated his prior right to Durindana, to obtain which he had embarked (as was related in the beginning) in that bold inroad upon France.", "그라다소는 두린다나에 대한 자신의 선차적 권리를 주장했으니, 그것을 얻으려고 그는 (처음에 이야기되었듯) 그 대담한 프랑스 침입에 나섰던 것이다."], ["A quarrel was thus kindled between the kings of Tartary and Sericane.", "이리하여 타타르와 세리카네의 왕들 사이에 다툼이 일었다."], ["While the dispute was raging a knight arrived upon the ground, accompanied by a damsel, to whom Rogero related the cause of the strife.", "다툼이 격해지는 동안, 한 처녀를 동반한 기사 하나가 그곳에 당도했고, 로제로가 그에게 다툼의 까닭을 들려주었다."], ["The knight was Florismart, and his companion Flordelis.", "그 기사는 플로리스마트였고, 그의 동행자는 플로르델리스였다."], ["Florismart succeeded in bringing the two champions to accord, by informing them that he could bring them to the presence of Orlando, the master of Durindana.", "플로리스마트는 자신이 두린다나의 임자인 오를란도 앞으로 그들을 데려갈 수 있다고 알려 줌으로써 두 용사를 화해시키는 데 성공했다."]], [["Gradasso and Mandricardo readily made truce, in order to accompany Florismart, nor would Rogero be left behind.", "그라다소와 만드리카르도는 플로리스마트와 동행하기 위해 기꺼이 휴전을 맺었고, 로제로도 뒤에 남으려 하지 않았다."]], [["As they proceeded on their quest they were met by a dwarf, who entreated their assistance in behalf of his lady, who had been carried off by an enchanter, mounted on a winged horse.", "탐색을 이어 가던 중 그들은 한 난쟁이를 만났는데, 그는 자신의 여주인을 위해 그들의 도움을 간청했으니, 그녀가 날개 달린 말에 탄 한 마법사에게 채여 갔다는 것이었다."], ["However unwilling to leave the question of the sword undecided, it was not possible for the knights to resist this appeal.", "칼의 문제를 결판내지 않은 채 두기를 아무리 꺼린다 해도, 기사들로서는 이 호소를 뿌리칠 수 없었다."], ["Two of their number, Gradasso and Rogero, therefore accompanied the dwarf.", "그리하여 그들 가운데 둘, 그라다소와 로제로가 난쟁이와 동행했다."], ["Mandricardo persisted in his search for Orlando, and Florismart, with Flordelis, pursued their way to the camp of Charlemagne.", "만드리카르도는 오를란도를 찾는 일을 고집했고, 플로리스마트는 플로르델리스와 함께 샤를마뉴의 진영으로 길을 갔다."]], [["Atlantes, the enchanter, who had brought up Rogero, and cherished for him the warmest affection, knew by his art that his pupil was destined to be severed from him, and converted to the Christian faith through the influence of Bradamante, that royal maiden with whom chance had brought him acquainted.", "로제로를 길러 그에게 가장 따뜻한 애정을 품었던 마법사 아틀란테스는, 자신의 제자가 자신에게서 떨어져 나가, 우연히 그가 알게 된 그 고귀한 처녀 브라다만테의 영향으로 기독교 신앙으로 개종할 운명임을 자신의 술법으로 알고 있었다."], ["Thinking to thwart the will of Heaven in this respect, he now put forth all his arts to entrap Rogero into his power.", "이 점에서 하늘의 뜻을 가로막으려는 생각으로, 그는 이제 로제로를 자신의 손아귀에 가두려고 온갖 술법을 펼쳤다."], ["By the aid of his subservient demons he reared a castle on an inaccessible height, in the Pyrenean mountains, and to make it a pleasant abode to his pupil, contrived to entrap and convey thither knights and damsels many a one, whom chance had brought into the vicinity of his castle.", "부리는 악마들의 도움으로 그는 피레네 산맥의 오를 수 없는 높은 곳에 성 하나를 세웠고, 그것을 자신의 제자에게 즐거운 거처로 만들고자, 자신의 성 인근에 우연히 들어온 기사와 처녀 들을 숱하게 함정에 빠뜨려 그곳으로 데려왔다."], ["Here, in a sort of sensual paradise, they were but too willing to forget glory and duty, and to pass their time in indolent enjoyment.", "여기서, 일종의 관능의 낙원에서, 그들은 너무도 기꺼이 영광과 의무를 잊고, 나태한 향락 속에 시간을 보냈다."]], [["It was by the enchanter that the dwarf had now been sent to tempt the knights into his power.", "기사들을 자신의 손아귀로 꾀어들이려고 이제 난쟁이를 보낸 것은 바로 그 마법사였다."]], [["But we must now return to Rinaldo, whom we left interrupted in his combat with Rodomont.", "그러나 우리는 이제 리날도에게로 돌아가야 하니, 우리는 그를 로도몬트와의 싸움이 중단된 대목에서 두고 왔다."], ["In search of his late antagonist and intent on bringing their combat to a decision he entered the forest of Arden, whither he suspected Rodomont had gone.", "지난번 적수를 찾아, 그들의 싸움을 결판내려는 일념으로, 그는 로도몬트가 갔으리라 짐작한 아르덴 숲으로 들어갔다."], ["While engaged on this quest he was surprised by the vision of a beautiful child dancing naked, with three damsels as beautiful as himself.", "이 탐색에 몰두해 있을 때, 그는 벌거벗은 채 춤추는 아름다운 아이의 환영에 놀랐으니, 그 아이만큼이나 아름다운 세 처녀가 함께 있었다."], ["While he was lost in admiration at the sight the child approached him, and, throwing at him handfuls of roses and lilies, struck him from his horse.", "그 광경에 넋을 잃고 감탄하고 있을 때 아이가 그에게 다가와, 그에게 장미와 백합을 한 움큼씩 던져 그를 말에서 떨어뜨렸다."], ["He was no sooner down than he was seized by the dancers, by whom he was dragged about and scourged with flowers till he fell into a swoon.", "그가 떨어지자마자 춤추던 자들에게 붙잡혀, 그들에게 이리저리 끌려다니며 꽃으로 매질을 당하다가 기절하고 말았다."], ["When he began to revive one of the group approached him, and told him that his punishment was the consequence of his rebellion against that power before whom all things bend; that there was but one remedy to heal the wounds that had been inflicted, and that was to drink of the waters of Love.", "그가 정신을 차리기 시작하자 그 무리 중 하나가 그에게 다가와, 그의 벌은 만물이 굽히는 그 힘에 대한 그의 반역의 결과라고, 그리고 그에게 가해진 상처를 치유할 단 하나의 치료법이 있으니, 그것은 사랑의 샘물을 마시는 것이라고 말했다."], ["Then they left him.", "그러고는 그를 떠났다."]], [["Rinaldo, sore and faint, dragged himself toward a fountain which flowed near by, and, being parched with thirst, drank greedily and almost unconsciously of the water, which was sweet to the taste, but bitter to the heart.", "리날도는 쑤시고 기력이 빠진 채, 근처에 흐르는 샘으로 몸을 끌고 가, 갈증에 타들어 가던 터라 그 물을 게걸스럽게, 거의 무의식적으로 마셨는데, 그 물은 맛은 달았으나 마음에는 썼다."], ["After repeated draughts he recovered his strength and recollection, and found himself in the same place where Angelica had formerly awakened him with a rain of flowers, and whence he had fled in contempt of her courtesy.", "거듭 들이켠 끝에 그는 기력과 기억을 되찾았고, 자신이 일찍이 안젤리카가 꽃비를 내려 그를 깨웠던, 그리고 그가 그녀의 호의를 멸시하며 달아났던 바로 그 자리에 있음을 알았다."]], [["This remembrance of the scene was followed by the recognition of his crime; and, repenting bitterly his ingratitude, he leaped upon Bayard, with the intention of hastening to Angelica’s country, and soliciting his pardon at her feet.", "이 장면을 떠올리자 곧이어 자신의 죄에 대한 자각이 따랐다. 그는 자신의 배은망덕을 비통하게 뉘우치며, 안젤리카의 나라로 서둘러 가 그녀의 발치에서 용서를 구하려는 생각으로 바야르에 뛰어올랐다."]], [["Let us now retrace our steps, and revert to the time when the paladins having learned from Dudon the summons of Charlemagne to return to France to repel the invaders, had all obeyed the command with the exception of Orlando, whose passion for Angelica still held him in attendance on her.", "이제 우리의 발걸음을 되짚어, 무사들이 두돈에게서 침입자들을 물리치기 위해 프랑스로 돌아오라는 샤를마뉴의 부름을 전해 들었던 때로 돌아가 보자. 그들은 모두 그 명령에 따랐으나, 안젤리카에 대한 정념이 여전히 그를 그녀의 곁에 붙들어 둔 오를란도만은 예외였다."], ["Orlando, arriving before Albracca, found it closely beleaguered.", "알브라카 앞에 당도한 오를란도는 그곳이 빈틈없이 포위당해 있는 것을 보았다."], ["He, however, made his way into the citadel, and related his adventures to Angelica, from the time of his departure up to his separation from Rinaldo and the rest, when they departed to the assistance of Charlemagne.", "그러나 그는 성채 안으로 들어가, 자신의 출발 때부터 리날도와 나머지 사람들이 샤를마뉴를 도우러 떠나면서 그들과 헤어진 때까지의 자신의 모험을 안젤리카에게 들려주었다."], ["Angelica, in return, described the distresses of the garrison, and the force of the besiegers; and in conclusion prayed Orlando to favor her escape from the pressing danger, and escort her into France.", "안젤리카는 답하여, 수비대의 고초와 포위군의 위세를 설명했고, 끝으로 오를란도에게 자신이 임박한 위험에서 벗어나도록 도와 프랑스로 호위해 달라고 간청했다."], ["Orlando, who did not suspect that love for Rinaldo was her secret motive, joyfully agreed to the proposal, and the sally was resolved upon.", "리날도에 대한 사랑이 그녀의 숨은 동기임을 의심하지 못한 오를란도는 그 제안에 기꺼이 동의했고, 탈출이 결정되었다."]], [["Leaving lights burning in the fortress, they departed at nightfall, and passed in safety through the enemy’s camp.", "요새에 등불을 켜 둔 채 그들은 해 질 무렵에 떠나, 적진을 무사히 지나갔다."], ["After encountering numerous adventures they reached the sea-side, and embarked on board a pinnace for France.", "수많은 모험을 겪은 끝에 그들은 바닷가에 다다라, 프랑스로 가는 작은 배에 올랐다."], ["The vessel arrived safely, and the travellers, disembarking in Provence, pursued their way by land.", "배는 무사히 당도했고, 여행자들은 프로방스에 내려 육로로 길을 갔다."], ["One day, heated and weary, they sought shelter from the sun in the forest of Arden, and chance directed Angelica to the fountain of Disdain, of whose waters she eagerly drank.", "어느 날, 더위에 지친 그들은 아르덴 숲에서 햇볕을 피해 쉴 곳을 찾았는데, 우연이 안젤리카를 멸시의 샘으로 이끌었고, 그녀는 그 물을 게걸스럽게 마셨다."], ["Issuing thence, the Count and damsel encountered a stranger-knight.", "거기서 나오던 백작과 처녀는 한 낯선 기사와 마주쳤다."], ["It was no other than Rinaldo, who was just on the point of setting off on a pilgrimage in search of Angelica, to implore her pardon for his insensibility, and urge his new found passion.", "그는 다름 아닌 리날도로, 자신의 무정함에 대한 용서를 빌고 새로이 찾은 정념을 호소하려고 안젤리카를 찾아 막 순례를 떠나려던 참이었다."], ["Surprise and delight at first deprived him of utterance, but soon recovering himself, he joyfully saluted her, claiming her as his, and exhorting her to put herself under his protection.", "놀라움과 기쁨이 처음에는 그에게서 말을 앗아 갔으나, 곧 정신을 차린 그는 기쁘게 그녀에게 인사하며, 그녀를 자신의 것이라 주장하고, 자신의 보호 아래 들어오라고 권했다."], ["His presumption was repelled by Angelica with disdain, and Orlando, enraged at the invasion of his rights, challenged him to decide their claims by arms.", "그의 주제넘음은 안젤리카에게 멸시로써 물리쳐졌고, 자신의 권리를 침범당해 격노한 오를란도는 그에게 무기로 그들의 권리를 결판내자고 도전했다."]], [["Terrified at the combat which ensued, Angelica fled amain through the forest, and came out upon a plain covered with tents.", "뒤이어 벌어진 싸움에 겁먹은 안젤리카는 숲을 가로질러 한껏 달아나, 천막으로 뒤덮인 평원으로 나왔다."], ["This was the camp of Charlemagne, who led the army of reserve destined to support the troops which had advanced to oppose Marsilius.", "이것은 샤를마뉴의 진영이었으니, 그는 마르실리우스에 맞서 진격한 군대를 지원하도록 예정된 예비군을 거느리고 있었다."], ["Charles having heard the damsel’s tale, with difficulty separated the two cousins, and then consigned Angelica, as the cause of quarrel, to the care of Namo, Duke of Bavaria, promising that she should be his who should best deserve her in the impending battle.", "처녀의 이야기를 들은 샤를은 어렵사리 두 사촌을 떼어 놓은 뒤, 다툼의 원인인 안젤리카를 바이에른의 공작 나모의 보살핌에 맡기며, 임박한 전투에서 그녀를 가장 잘 받을 자격을 갖춘 자의 것이 되리라고 약속했다."]], [["But these plans and hopes were frustrated.", "그러나 이 계획과 희망은 좌절되었다."], ["The Christian army, beaten at all points, fled from the Saracens; and Angelica, indifferent to both her lovers, mounted a swift palfrey and plunged into the forest, rejoicing, in spite of her terror, at having regained her liberty.", "기독교군은 모든 곳에서 패하여 사라센군에게서 달아났고, 두 연인 모두에게 무심했던 안젤리카는 날쌘 조랑말에 올라 숲속으로 뛰어들었으니, 두려움에도 불구하고 자유를 되찾은 것을 기뻐했다."], ["She stopped at last in a tufted grove, where a gentle zephyr blew, and whose young trees were watered by two clear runnels, which came and mingled their waters, making a pleasing murmur.", "마침내 그녀는 부드러운 산들바람이 부는, 무성한 숲에 멈추었다. 그곳의 어린 나무들은 두 줄기 맑은 시냇물이 와서 물을 합쳐 흐르며 적셔 주었고, 그 시냇물은 듣기 좋은 속삭임을 자아냈다."], ["Believing herself far from Rinaldo, and overcome by fatigue and the summer heat, she saw with delight a bank covered with flowers so thick that they almost hid the green turf, inviting her to alight and rest.", "리날도에게서 멀리 떨어졌다고 믿고, 피로와 여름 더위에 지친 그녀는, 푸른 풀밭을 거의 가릴 만큼 빽빽이 꽃으로 뒤덮인 둑이 자신에게 내려 쉬라고 손짓하는 것을 기쁘게 바라보았다."], ["She dismounted from her palfrey, and turned him loose to recruit his strength with the tender grass which bordered the streamlets.", "그녀는 조랑말에서 내려, 그것이 시냇가에 돋은 연한 풀로 기력을 회복하도록 풀어놓았다."], ["Then, in a sheltered nook tapestried with moss and fenced in with roses and hawthorn-flowers, she yielded herself to grateful repose.", "그러고는 이끼로 덮이고 장미와 산사나무 꽃으로 둘러싸인 아늑한 구석에서 고마운 휴식에 몸을 맡겼다."]], [["She had not slept long when she was awakened by the noise made by the approach of a horse.", "그녀가 오래 잠들지 않았을 때, 말 한 마리가 다가오는 소리에 잠을 깼다."], ["Starting up, she saw an armed knight who had arrived at the bank of the stream.", "벌떡 일어난 그녀는 시냇가에 당도한 무장한 기사 하나를 보았다."], ["Not knowing whether he was to be feared or not, her heart beat with anxiety.", "그가 두려워할 자인지 아닌지 알지 못한 그녀의 가슴은 불안으로 두근거렸다."], ["She pressed aside the leaves to allow her to see who it was, but scarce dared to breathe for fear of betraying herself.", "그녀는 누구인지 보려고 잎새를 옆으로 헤쳤으나, 자신을 드러낼까 두려워 숨조차 거의 쉬지 못했다."], ["Soon the knight threw himself on the flowery bank, and leaning his head on his hand fell into a profound reverie.", "곧 기사는 꽃핀 둑에 몸을 던지더니, 머리를 손에 괴고 깊은 상념에 빠져들었다."], ["Then arousing himself from his silence he began to pour forth complaints, mingled with deep sighs.", "그러고는 침묵에서 깨어나, 깊은 한숨에 뒤섞인 탄식을 쏟아내기 시작했다."], ["Rivers of tears flowed down his cheeks, and his breast seemed to labor with a hidden flame.", "강물 같은 눈물이 그의 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸고, 그의 가슴은 감추어진 불꽃으로 괴로워하는 듯했다."], ["“Ah, vain regrets!”", "\"아, 부질없는 회한이여!\""], ["he exclaimed; “cruel fortune!", "그가 외쳤다. \"잔인한 운명이여!"], ["others triumph, while I endure hopeless misery!", "다른 이들은 승리하는데, 나는 가망 없는 비참함을 견디는구나!"], ["Better a thousand times to lose life, than wear a chain so disgraceful and so oppressive!”", "이토록 치욕스럽고 이토록 짓누르는 사슬을 차느니, 차라리 천 번이라도 목숨을 잃는 편이 나으리라!\""]], [["Angelica by this time had recognized the stranger, and perceived that it was Sacripant, king of Circassia, one of the worthiest of her suitors.", "이때쯤 안젤리카는 그 낯선 자를 알아보았고, 그가 자신의 가장 훌륭한 구혼자들 가운데 하나인 키르카시아의 왕 사크리판트임을 알아차렸다."], ["This prince had followed Angelica from his country, at the very gates of", "이 왕자는 안젤리카를 따라 그녀의 나라에서부터, 바로 그 성문에서부터"]], [["the day, to France, where he heard with dismay that she was under the guardianship of the Paladin Orlando, and that the Emperor had announced his decree to award her as the prize of valor to that one of his nephews who should best deserve her.", "그날, 프랑스로 향했고, 그곳에서 그는 그녀가 팔라딘 오를란도의 보호 아래 있다는 것과, 황제가 자신의 조카들 가운데 그녀를 가장 받을 만한 자에게 무용의 상으로 그녀를 내리겠다는 칙령을 선포했다는 소식을 낙담한 채 들었다."]], [["As Sacripant continued to lament, Angelica, who had always opposed the hardness of marble to his sighs, thought with herself that nothing forbade her employing his good offices in this unhappy crisis.", "사크리판트가 계속해서 한탄하는 동안, 그의 한숨에 언제나 대리석처럼 차갑게 맞서 왔던 안젤리카는 이 불행한 위기에 그의 도움을 이용하지 못할 까닭이 없다고 속으로 생각했다."], ["Though firmly resolved never to accept him as a spouse, she yet felt the necessity of giving him a gleam of hope in reward for the service she required of him.", "그를 결코 배우자로 받아들이지 않겠다고 굳게 마음먹고 있었으나, 그녀는 자신이 그에게 요구하는 봉사에 대한 보답으로 한 줄기 희망의 빛을 비춰 줄 필요가 있다고 느꼈다."], ["All at once, like Diana, she stepped forth from the arbor.", "그녀는 갑자기, 마치 아르테미스[로마의 〈디아나〉]처럼 나무 그늘에서 걸어 나왔다."], ["“May the gods preserve thee,”", "\"신들께서 그대를 지켜 주시고,\""], ["she said, “and put far from thee all hard thoughts of me!”", "그녀가 말했다, \"나에 대한 모든 모진 생각을 그대에게서 멀리 거두어 주시기를!\""], ["Then she told him all that had befallen her since she parted with him at her father’s court, and how she had availed herself of Orlando’s protection to escape from the beleaguered city.", "그러고는 그녀는 아버지의 궁정에서 그와 헤어진 뒤로 자신에게 닥친 모든 일을, 그리고 어떻게 자신이 오를란도의 보호에 의지하여 포위된 도시에서 빠져나왔는지를 그에게 이야기했다."], ["At that moment the noise of horse and armor was heard as of one approaching; and Sacripant, furious at the interruption, resumed his helmet, mounted his horse, and placed his lance in rest.", "바로 그 순간 누군가가 다가오는 듯이 말과 갑옷이 부딪치는 소리가 들렸다. 그리고 방해받은 데 분노한 사크리판트는 다시 투구를 쓰고 말에 올라타고는 창을 겨누어 잡았다."], ["He saw a knight advancing, with scarf and plume of snowy whiteness.", "그는 한 기사가 눈처럼 새하얀 띠와 깃털을 두르고 다가오는 것을 보았다."], ["Sacripant regarded him with angry eyes, and, while he was yet some distance off, defied him to the combat.", "사크리판트는 성난 눈으로 그를 바라보았고, 그가 아직 얼마간 떨어져 있을 때 결투를 청했다."], ["The other, not moved by his angry tone to make reply, put himself on his defence.", "상대방은 그의 성난 어조에 대꾸할 마음이 일지 않아, 그저 방어 자세를 취했다."], ["Their horses, struck at the same moment with the spur, rushed upon one another with the impetuosity of a tempest.", "두 사람의 말은 동시에 박차를 받고 폭풍 같은 기세로 서로에게 달려들었다."], ["Their shields were pierced each with the other’s lance, and only the temper of their breastplates saved their lives.", "두 사람의 방패는 각각 상대의 창에 꿰뚫렸으나, 오직 가슴받이의 단단한 강도만이 그들의 목숨을 구했다."], ["Both the horses recoiled with the violence of the shock; but the unknown knight’s recovered itself at the touch of the spur; the Saracen king’s fell dead, and bore down his master with him.", "두 말 모두 충돌의 격렬함에 뒤로 물러났으나, 정체 모를 기사의 말은 박차가 닿자 곧 자세를 되찾았다. 사라센 왕의 말은 죽어 쓰러지면서 주인을 함께 깔아뭉갰다."], ["The white knight, seeing his enemy in this condition, cared not to renew the combat, but, thinking he had done enough for glory, pursued his way through the forest, and was a mile off before Sacripant had got free from his horse.", "흰 기사는 적이 이런 처지에 빠진 것을 보고도 다시 싸움을 벌일 마음이 없어, 영광을 위해서는 이만하면 충분하다고 여기고 숲을 가로질러 제 갈 길을 갔으며, 사크리판트가 말에서 벗어나기도 전에 이미 1마일이나 떨어져 있었다."]], [["As a ploughman, stunned by a thunder-clap which has stricken dead the oxen at his plough, stands motionless, sadly contemplating his loss, so Sacripant stood confounded and overwhelmed with mortification at having Angelica a witness of his defeat.", "마치 자기 쟁기를 끌던 소들을 벼락이 내리쳐 죽이자 그 일격에 망연자실한 농부가 자신의 손실을 슬프게 바라보며 꼼짝 못 하고 서 있듯이, 사크리판트도 안젤리카를 자기 패배의 증인으로 둔 데 대한 굴욕에 어쩔 줄 모르고 압도되어 그렇게 서 있었다."], ["He groaned, he sighed, less from the pain of his bruises than for the shame of being reduced to such a state before her.", "그는 신음하고 한숨지었으나, 그것은 멍든 상처의 고통 때문이라기보다는 그녀 앞에서 이런 꼴로 전락한 수치심 때문이었다."], ["The princess took pity on him, and consoled him as well as she could.", "공주는 그를 가엾이 여겨 할 수 있는 한 위로해 주었다."], ["“Banish your regrets, my lord,”", "\"후회는 버리세요, 나리,\""], ["she said, “this accident has happened solely in consequence of the feebleness of your horse, which had more need of rest and food than of such an encounter as this.", "그녀가 말했다, \"이 사고는 오로지 그대 말이 약했던 탓에 일어난 것이니, 그 말은 이런 격돌보다는 휴식과 먹이가 더 필요했던 것이지요."], ["Nor can your adversary gain any credit by it, since he has hurried away, not venturing a second trial.”", "그대의 적도 이 일로 아무런 명예를 얻을 수 없을 것이니, 그가 두 번째 시도를 감행할 엄두도 내지 못하고 서둘러 떠나 버렸기 때문이지요.\""], ["While she thus consoled Sacripant they perceived a person approach, who seemed a courier, with bag and horn.", "그녀가 이렇게 사크리판트를 위로하는 동안, 그들은 가방과 뿔나팔을 든, 전령으로 보이는 사람이 다가오는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["As soon as he came up, he accosted Sacripant, and inquired if he had seen a knight pass that way, bearing a white shield and with a white plume to his helmet.", "그는 다가오자마자 사크리판트에게 말을 걸어, 흰 방패를 들고 투구에 흰 깃털을 단 기사가 이 길로 지나가는 것을 보았느냐고 물었다."], ["“I have, indeed, seen too much of him,”", "\"정말이지 그를 너무 많이 보았소,\""], ["said Sacripant, “it is he who has brought me to the ground; but at least I hope to learn from you who that knight is.”", "사크리판트가 말했다, \"나를 땅바닥에 쓰러뜨린 자가 바로 그자요. 그래도 적어도 그 기사가 누구인지는 그대에게서 알 수 있기를 바라오.\""], ["“That I can easily inform you,”", "\"그것은 쉽게 알려 드릴 수 있소,\""], ["said the man; “know then that, if you have been overthrown, you owe your fate to the high prowess of a lady as beautiful as she is brave.", "그 사람이 말했다. \"그러니 아시오, 만일 그대가 쓰러졌다면, 그것은 아름다운 만큼 용감한 한 귀부인의 드높은 무용 탓이오."], ["It is the fair and illustrious Bradamante who has won from you the honors of victory.”", "그대에게서 승리의 영예를 빼앗은 이는 아름답고 이름 높은 브라다만테요.\""], ["At these words the courier rode on his way, leaving Sacripant more confounded and mortified than ever.", "이 말과 함께 전령은 제 갈 길로 말을 달려 갔고, 사크리판트는 그 어느 때보다 더 어쩔 줄 모르고 굴욕에 잠긴 채 남겨졌다."], ["In silence he mounted the horse of Angelica, taking the lady behind him on the croup, and rode away in search of a more secure asylum.", "그는 말없이 안젤리카의 말에 올라타, 그 귀부인을 뒤편 엉덩이 쪽에 태우고는 더 안전한 피난처를 찾아 떠났다."], ["Hardly had they ridden two miles when a new sound was heard in the forest, and they perceived a gallant and powerful horse, which, leaping the ravines and dashing aside the branches that opposed his passage, appeared before them, accoutred with a rich harness adorned with gold.", "그들이 채 2마일도 가기 전에 숲에서 새로운 소리가 들려왔고, 그들은 한 마리 늠름하고 힘센 말이 골짜기를 뛰어넘고 길을 가로막는 나뭇가지들을 헤치며 황금으로 장식된 화려한 마구를 갖춘 채 그들 앞에 나타나는 것을 보았다."]], [["“If I may believe my eyes, which penetrate with difficulty the underwood,”", "\"덤불을 뚫고 보기 어려운 내 눈을 믿을 수 있다면,\""], ["said Angelica, “that horse that dashes so stoutly through the bushes is Bayard, and I marvel how he seems to know the need we have of him, mounted as we are both on one feeble animal.”", "안젤리카가 말했다, \"수풀을 저토록 힘차게 헤치며 달려오는 저 말은 바야르예요. 우리 둘이 한 마리 약한 짐승에 함께 타고 있는 지금, 어떻게 저 말이 우리에게 자기가 필요하다는 것을 아는 듯한지 놀라울 따름이에요.\""], ["Sacripant, dismounting from the palfrey, approached the fiery courser, and attempted to seize his bridle, but the disdainful animal, turning from him, launched at him a volley of kicks enough to have shattered a wall of marble.", "사크리판트는 작은 말에서 내려 그 사나운 군마에게 다가가 고삐를 붙잡으려 했으나, 거만한 짐승은 그를 외면하고 대리석 벽이라도 산산조각 낼 만한 뒷발질을 한바탕 퍼부었다."], ["Bayard then approached Angelica with an air as gentle and loving as a faithful dog could his master after a long separation.", "그러더니 바야르는 마치 충성스러운 개가 오랜 헤어짐 끝에 주인에게 다가가듯 부드럽고 다정한 태도로 안젤리카에게 다가갔다."], ["For he remembered how she had caressed him, and even fed him, in Albracca.", "그것은 그가 알브라카에서 그녀가 자기를 어루만져 주고 심지어 먹이까지 주었던 것을 기억했기 때문이다."], ["She took his bridle in her left hand, while with her right she patted his neck.", "그녀는 왼손으로 그 고삐를 잡고, 오른손으로 그 목을 토닥였다."], ["The beautiful animal, gifted with wonderful intelligence, seemed to submit entirely.", "놀라운 지혜를 타고난 그 아름다운 짐승은 완전히 순종하는 듯 보였다."], ["Sacripant, seizing the moment to vault upon him, controlled his curvetings, and Angelica, quitting the croup of the palfrey, regained her seat.", "사크리판트는 그 틈을 타 말 위로 뛰어올라 그 뛰는 동작을 다스렸고, 안젤리카는 작은 말의 엉덩이 자리를 떠나 다시 제자리에 올라앉았다."]], [["But, turning his eyes toward a place where was heard a noise of arms, Sacripant beheld Rinaldo.", "그러나 무기 소리가 들리는 쪽으로 눈을 돌리던 사크리판트는 리날도를 보았다."], ["That hero now loves Angelica more than his life, and she flies him as the timid crane the falcon.", "그 영웅은 이제 안젤리카를 제 목숨보다 더 사랑하건만, 그녀는 겁 많은 두루미가 매를 피하듯 그를 피해 달아난다."]], [["The fountain of which Angelica had drunk produced such an effect on the beautiful queen that, with distressed countenance and trembling voice, she conjured Sacripant not to wait the approach of Rinaldo, but to join her in flight.", "안젤리카가 마셨던 그 샘은 이 아름다운 여왕에게 그러한 효력을 미쳤으니, 그녀는 괴로운 얼굴빛과 떨리는 목소리로 사크리판트에게 리날도가 다가오는 것을 기다리지 말고 자기와 함께 달아나자고 간청했다."]], [["“Am I, then,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["said Sacripant, “of so little esteem with you that you doubt my power to defend you?", "사크리판트가 말했다, \"내가 당신을 지켜 줄 능력이 있는지 당신이 의심할 만큼 내가 당신에게 그토록 하찮은 존재란 말이오?"], ["Do you forget the battle of Albracca, and how, in your defence, I fought single-handed against Agrican and all his knights?”", "알브라카의 전투를, 그리고 당신을 지키기 위해 내가 홀로 아그리칸과 그 기사 전부를 상대로 싸웠던 것을 잊었단 말이오?\""]], [["Angelica made no reply, uncertain what to do; but already Rinaldo was too near to be escaped.", "안젤리카는 어찌해야 할지 몰라 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 그러나 이미 리날도는 너무 가까이 와 있어 달아날 수 없었다."], ["He advanced menacingly to the Circassian king, for he recognized his horse.", "그는 그 말을 알아보았기에 위협적으로 키르카시아의 왕에게 다가갔다."]], [["“Vile thief,”", "\"비열한 도둑놈,\""], ["he cried, “dismount from that horse, and prevent the punishment that is your due for daring to rob me of my property.", "그가 외쳤다, \"그 말에서 내려라, 그리고 감히 내 소유물을 훔친 죄로 마땅히 받아야 할 벌을 면하라."], ["Leave, also, the princess in my hands; for it would indeed be a sin to suffer so charming a lady and so gallant a charger to remain in such keeping.”", "또한 공주도 내 손에 넘겨라. 이토록 매혹적인 귀부인과 이토록 늠름한 군마를 그런 자의 손에 두는 것은 참으로 죄악일 테니.\""]], [["The king of Circassia, furious at being thus insulted, cried out, “Thou liest, villain, in giving me the name of thief, which better belongs to thyself than to me.", "이렇게 모욕을 당한 데 분노한 키르카시아의 왕이 소리쳤다. \"거짓말이다, 악당아, 나에게 도둑이라는 이름을 붙이다니, 그 이름은 나보다 네 자신에게 더 어울린다."], ["It is true, the beauty of this lady and the perfection of this horse are unequalled; come on, then, and let us try which of us is most worthy to possess them.”", "이 귀부인의 아름다움과 이 말의 완벽함이 견줄 데 없다는 것은 사실이다. 그러니 덤벼라, 우리 둘 중에 누가 이들을 차지하기에 더 합당한지 가려 보자.\""]], [["At these words the king of Circassia and Rinaldo attacked one another with all their force, one fighting on foot, the other on horseback.", "이 말과 함께 키르카시아의 왕과 리날도는 온 힘을 다해 서로 맞붙었으니, 한 사람은 걸어서, 다른 한 사람은 말을 타고 싸웠다."], ["You need not, however, suppose that the Saracen king found any advantage in this; for a young page, unused to horsemanship, could not have failed more completely to manage Bayard than did this accomplished knight.", "그렇다고 사라센 왕이 이로써 어떤 유리함을 얻었으리라고 여겨서는 안 된다. 말타기에 서투른 어린 시동이라도 이 노련한 기사보다 더 완전히 바야르를 다루지 못하지는 않았을 것이기 때문이다."], ["The faithful animal loved his master too well to injure him, and refused his aid as well as his obedience to the hand of Sacripant, who could strike but ineffectual blows, the horse backing when he wished him to go forward, and dropping his head and arching his back, throwing out with his legs, so as almost to shake the knight out of the saddle.", "충성스러운 그 짐승은 제 주인을 너무도 사랑한 나머지 주인을 다치게 하려 하지 않았고, 사크리판트의 손길에는 도움도 복종도 거부했다. 사크리판트는 헛된 일격밖에 가하지 못했으니, 말은 그가 앞으로 가게 하려 하면 뒤로 물러나고, 머리를 떨구고 등을 활처럼 구부리며 다리로 걷어차 거의 기사를 안장에서 떨어뜨릴 지경이었다."], ["Sacripant, seeing that he could not manage him, watched his opportunity, rose on his saddle, and leapt lightly to the earth; then, relieved from the embarrassment of the horse, renewed the combat on more equal terms.", "사크리판트는 그를 다스릴 수 없음을 알고 기회를 엿보다 안장 위로 몸을 일으켜 가볍게 땅으로 뛰어내렸다. 그러고는 말의 거추장스러움에서 벗어나 보다 대등한 조건으로 싸움을 다시 시작했다."], ["Their skill to thrust and parry were equal; one rises, the other stoops; with one foot set firm they turn and wind, to lay on strokes or to dodge them.", "찌르고 막는 두 사람의 기량은 막상막하였다. 한 사람이 몸을 일으키면 다른 사람은 몸을 숙였다."], ["At last Rinaldo, throwing himself on the Circassian, dealt him a blow so terrible that Fusberta, his good sword, cut in two the buckler of Sacripant, although it was made of bone, and covered with a thick plate of steel well tempered.", "한 발을 단단히 디딘 채 몸을 돌리고 비틀며 타격을 가하거나 피했다. 마침내 리날도가 키르카시아인에게 몸을 던지며 너무도 무시무시한 일격을 가하니, 그의 훌륭한 검 푸스베르타가 사크리판트의 방패를 두 동강 내고 말았는데, 그 방패는 뼈로 만들고 잘 단련된 두꺼운 강철판으로 덮여 있었음에도 그러했다."], ["The arm of the Saracen was deprived of its defence, and almost palsied with the stroke.", "사라센인의 팔은 방어 수단을 잃었고, 그 일격에 거의 마비될 지경이었다."], ["Angelica, perceiving how victory was likely to incline, and shuddering at the thought of becoming the prize of Rinaldo, hesitated no longer.", "안젤리카는 승리가 어느 쪽으로 기우는지를 알아차리고, 리날도의 전리품이 될 생각에 몸서리치며 더는 망설이지 않았다."], ["Turning her horse’s head, she fled with the utmost speed; and, in spite of the round pebbles which covered a steep descent, she plunged into a deep valley, trembling with the fear that Rinaldo was in pursuit.", "그녀는 말머리를 돌려 온 힘을 다해 달아났고, 가파른 비탈을 뒤덮은 둥근 자갈들에도 아랑곳없이 깊은 골짜기로 뛰어들었으니, 리날도가 뒤쫓아 오지나 않을까 하는 두려움에 떨면서였다."], ["At the bottom of this valley she encountered an aged hermit, whose white beard flowed to his middle, and whose venerable appearance seemed to assure his piety.", "이 골짜기 밑바닥에서 그녀는 흰 수염이 허리까지 흘러내리고 그 거룩한 모습이 신심을 보증하는 듯한 한 늙은 은자를 만났다."]], [["This hermit, who appeared shrunk by age and fasting, travelled slowly, mounted upon a wretched ass.", "세월과 단식으로 쪼그라든 듯한 이 은자는 초라한 나귀를 타고 천천히 길을 가고 있었다."], ["The princess, overcome with fear, conjured him to save her life; and to conduct her to some port of the sea, whence she might embark and quit France, never more to hear the odious name of Rinaldo.", "두려움에 압도된 공주는 그에게 자기 목숨을 구해 달라고, 그리고 자기를 바다의 어느 항구로 인도하여 그곳에서 배를 타고 프랑스를 떠나 다시는 리날도라는 가증스러운 이름을 듣지 않게 해 달라고 간청했다."]], [["The old hermit was something of a wizard.", "이 늙은 은자는 어느 정도 마법사이기도 했다."], ["He comforted Angelica, and promised to protect her from all peril.", "그는 안젤리카를 위로하며 모든 위험에서 그녀를 보호하겠다고 약속했다."], ["Then he opened his scrip, and took from thence a book, and had read but a single page when a goblin, obedient to his incantations, appeared, under the form of a laboring man, and demanded his orders.", "그러고는 자기 자루를 열어 그 안에서 책 한 권을 꺼내 단 한 쪽을 읽었을 뿐인데, 그의 주문에 복종하는 한 도깨비가 일하는 농부의 모습으로 나타나 그의 명령을 청했다."], ["He received them, transported himself to the place where the knights still maintained their conflict, and boldly stepped between the two.", "도깨비는 명령을 받고는, 두 기사가 여전히 싸움을 벌이고 있는 곳으로 몸을 옮겨, 대담하게 두 사람 사이로 끼어들었다."]], [["“Tell me, I pray you,”", "\"청컨대 말씀해 주시오,\""], ["he said, “what benefit will accrue to him who shall get the better in this contest?", "그가 말했다, \"이 싸움에서 이기는 자에게 무슨 이득이 돌아오겠소? 그대들이 다투는 대상은 이미 처분되었소."], ["The object you are contending for is already disposed of; for the Paladin Orlando, without effort and without opposition, is now carrying away the princess Angelica to Paris.", "팔라딘 오를란도가 아무런 힘도 들이지 않고 아무런 저항도 받지 않은 채 지금 안젤리카 공주를 파리로 데려가고 있으니 말이오. 그대들은 어서 그들을 뒤쫓는 편이 나을 것이오."], ["You had better pursue them promptly; for if they reach Paris you will never see her again.”", "그들이 파리에 닿으면 그대들은 다시는 그녀를 보지 못할 것이기 때문이오.\""]], [["At these words you might have seen those rival warriors confounded, stupefied, silently agreeing that they were affording their rival a fair opportunity to triumph over them.", "이 말에 그 경쟁하던 두 전사가 어쩔 줄 모르고 망연자실하여, 자신들이 경쟁자에게 자기들을 누르고 승리할 절호의 기회를 내주고 있음을 말없이 인정하는 모습을 그대는 보았을 것이다."], ["Rinaldo, approaching Bayard, breathes a sigh of shame and rage, and swears a terrible oath that, if he overtakes Orlando, he will tear his heart out.", "리날도는 바야르에게 다가가 수치와 분노의 한숨을 내쉬며, 만일 오를란도를 따라잡는다면 그 심장을 뽑아내겠노라고 무시무시한 맹세를 했다."], ["Then mounting Bayard and pressing his flanks with his spurs, he leaves the king of Circassia on foot in the forest.", "그러고는 바야르에 올라 박차로 그 옆구리를 압박하며, 키르카시아의 왕을 숲속에 걸어서 남겨 둔 채 떠났다."]], [["Let it not appear strange that Rinaldo found Bayard obedient at last, after having so long prevented any one from even touching his bridle; for that fine animal had an intelligence almost human; he had fled from his master only to draw him on the track of Angelica, and enable him to recover her.", "그토록 오랫동안 누구도 제 고삐에 손조차 대지 못하게 하던 바야르가 마침내 리날도에게 순종한 것이 이상하게 보여서는 안 된다. 그 훌륭한 짐승은 거의 인간에 가까운 지혜를 지니고 있었으니, 그가 제 주인에게서 달아난 것은 오로지 주인을 안젤리카의 자취로 이끌어 그녀를 되찾게 하기 위함이었다."], ["He saw when the princess fled from the battle, and Rinaldo being then engaged in a fight on foot, Bayard found himself free to follow the traces of Angelica.", "그는 공주가 전투에서 달아나는 것을 보았고, 그때 리날도가 걸어서 싸움에 몰두해 있었으므로, 바야르는 자유로이 안젤리카의 흔적을 뒤쫓을 수 있었다."], ["Thus he had drawn his master after him, not permitting him to approach, and had brought him to the sight of the princess.", "이렇게 그는 주인이 다가오는 것을 허락하지 않으면서도 주인을 자기 뒤로 이끌어, 공주를 볼 수 있는 데까지 데려왔던 것이다."], ["But Bayard now, deceived like his master with the false intelligence of the goblin, submits to be mounted and to serve his master as usual, and Rinaldo, animated with rage, makes him fly toward Paris, more slowly than his wishes, though the speed of Bayard outstripped the winds.", "그러나 이제 바야르는 자기 주인과 마찬가지로 도깨비의 거짓 소식에 속아, 평소대로 올라타게 하고 주인을 섬기는 데 순순히 따랐다. 분노에 들끓는 리날도는 그를 파리 쪽으로 달리게 했으니, 바야르의 속도가 바람을 앞질렀음에도 그의 바람에는 미치지 못할 만큼 더디게 느껴졌다."], ["Full of impatience to encounter Orlando, he gave but a few hours that night to sleep.", "오를란도와 맞붙고 싶은 조바심에 가득 차, 그는 그날 밤 잠에는 겨우 몇 시간만을 내주었다."], ["Early the next day he saw before him the great city, under the walls of which the Emperor Charles had collected the scattered remains of his army.", "이튿날 이른 아침 그는 자기 앞에 그 큰 도시를 보았으니, 그 성벽 아래 황제 카를루스가 흩어진 자기 군대의 잔병들을 모아 두었던 곳이었다."], ["Foreseeing that he would soon be attacked on all sides, the Emperor had caused the ancient fortifications to be repaired, and new ones to be built, surrounded by wide and deep ditches.", "곧 사방에서 공격받으리라는 것을 내다본 황제는 옛 요새를 보수하게 하고, 넓고 깊은 해자로 둘러싸인 새 요새를 짓게 했다."], ["The desire to hold the field against the enemy made him seize every means of procuring new allies.", "적을 상대로 전장을 지키려는 열망에 그는 새로운 동맹을 얻을 온갖 수단을 붙잡았다."], ["He hoped to receive from England aid sufficient to enable him to form a new camp, and as soon as Rinaldo rejoined him he selected him to go as his ambassador into England, to plead for auxiliaries.", "그는 새로운 진영을 꾸릴 만큼 충분한 원조를 잉글랜드로부터 받기를 바랐고, 리날도가 다시 합류하자마자 그를 사절로 뽑아 잉글랜드로 보내 지원군을 청하게 했다."], ["Rinaldo was far from pleased with his commission, but he obeyed the Emperor’s commands, without giving himself time to devote a single day to the object nearest his heart.", "리날도는 이 임무가 전혀 마음에 들지 않았으나, 자기 마음에 가장 가까운 목적에 단 하루도 바칠 시간을 갖지 못한 채 황제의 명령에 복종했다."], ["He hastened to Calais, and lost not a moment in embarking for England, ardently desiring a hasty despatch of his commission, and a speedy return to France. ————", "그는 칼레로 서둘러 가서 한순간도 지체하지 않고 잉글랜드로 떠나는 배에 올랐으니, 자기 임무를 신속히 처리하고 어서 프랑스로 돌아오기를 간절히 바라면서였다."]], [["# BRADAMANTE AND ROGERO", "# BRADAMANTE AND ROGERO"]], [["Bradamante, the knight of the white plume and shield, whose sudden appearance and encounter with Sacripant we have already told, was in quest of Rogero, from whom chance had separated her, almost at the beginning of their acquaintance.", "브라다만테와 로제로\n브라다만테, 곧 흰 깃털과 방패를 단 그 기사는, 그 갑작스러운 등장과 사크리판트와의 격돌에 대해 우리가 이미 이야기한 바 있거니와, 자신과 알게 된 지 거의 처음에 우연히 헤어진 로제로를 찾고 있었다."], ["After her encounter with Sacripant Bradamante pursued her way through the forest, in hopes of rejoining Rogero, and arrived at last on the brink of a fair fountain.", "사크리판트와 격돌한 뒤 브라다만테는 로제로와 다시 만나기를 바라며 숲을 가로질러 제 갈 길을 갔고, 마침내 어느 아름다운 샘가에 이르렀다."]], [["This fountain flowed through a broad meadow.", "이 샘은 넓은 풀밭을 가로질러 흘렀다."], ["Ancient trees overshadowed it, and travellers, attracted by the sweet murmur of its waters, stopped there to cool themselves.", "오래된 나무들이 그 위에 그늘을 드리웠고, 그 물의 감미로운 속삭임에 이끌린 나그네들이 그곳에 멈춰 몸을 식히곤 했다."], ["Bradamante, casting her eyes on all sides to enjoy the beauties of the spot, perceived, under the shade of a tree, a knight reclining, who seemed to be oppressed with the deepest grief.", "브라다만테는 그곳의 아름다움을 즐기려고 사방으로 눈길을 돌리다가, 한 나무 그늘 아래 가장 깊은 슬픔에 짓눌린 듯한 한 기사가 비스듬히 누워 있는 것을 보았다."], ["Bradamante accosted him, and asked to be informed of the cause of his distress.", "브라다만테는 그에게 말을 걸어 그 비탄의 까닭을 알려 달라고 청했다."], ["“Alas!", "\"아아!"], ["my lord,”", "나리,\""], ["said he, “I lament a young and charming friend, my affianced wife, who has been torn from me by a villain,—let me rather call him a demon,—who, on a winged horse, descended from the air, seized her, and bore her screaming to his den.", "그가 말했다, \"나는 젊고 매혹적인 한 벗을, 내 약혼한 아내를 한탄하고 있소. 그녀는 한 악당—차라리 그를 악마라 부르게 해 주오—에게 빼앗겼는데, 그자는 날개 달린 말을 타고 공중에서 내려와 그녀를 붙잡아 비명을 지르는 그녀를 자기 소굴로 데려갔소."], ["I have pursued them over rocks and through ravines till my horse is no longer able to bear me, and I now wait only for death.”", "나는 내 말이 더는 나를 태우지 못할 때까지 바위를 넘고 골짜기를 가로질러 그들을 뒤쫓았으나, 이제 죽음만을 기다릴 뿐이오.\""], ["He added that already a vain attempt on his behalf had been made by two knights, whom chance had brought to the spot.", "그는 덧붙이기를, 우연히 그곳에 이르렀던 두 기사가 이미 자기를 위해 헛된 시도를 했다고 말했다."], ["Their names were Gradasso, king of Sericane, and Rogero, the Moor.", "그들의 이름은 세리카네의 왕 그라다소와 무어인 로제로였다."], ["Both had been overcome by the wiles of the enchanter, and were added to the number of the captives, whom he held in an impregnable castle, situated on the height of the mountain.", "두 사람 모두 마법사의 술수에 굴복하여, 그가 산꼭대기에 자리한 난공불락의 성에 가두어 둔 포로들의 수에 더해졌다."], ["At the mention of Rogero’s name Bradamante started with delight, which was soon changed to an opposite sentiment when she heard that her lover was a prisoner in the toils of the enchanter.", "로제로의 이름이 언급되자 브라다만테는 기쁨에 움찔했으나, 자기 연인이 마법사의 올가미에 사로잡힌 포로라는 말을 듣고는 그 기쁨이 곧 반대의 감정으로 바뀌었다."], ["“Sir Knight,”", "\"기사 양반,\""], ["she said, “do not surrender yourself to despair; this day may be more happy for you than you think, if you will only lead me to the castle which enfolds her whom you deplore.”", "그녀가 말했다, \"절망에 자신을 내맡기지 마시오. 만일 그대가 그토록 한탄하는 여인을 가둔 그 성으로 나를 인도해 주기만 한다면, 오늘은 그대가 생각하는 것보다 더 행복한 날이 될 수 있을 것이오.\""]], [["The knight responded, “After having lost all that made life dear to me I have no motive to avoid the dangers of the enterprise, and I will do as you request; but I forewarn you of the perils you will have to encounter.", "기사가 대답했다. \"내 삶을 소중하게 만들던 모든 것을 잃은 마당에, 나로서는 이 일의 위험을 피할 까닭이 없으니, 그대가 청하는 대로 하겠소."], ["If you fall impute it not to me.”", "그러나 그대가 맞닥뜨려야 할 위험들을 미리 일러두는 바이오. 만일 그대가 쓰러진다면, 그것을 내 탓으로 돌리지 마시오.\""]], [["Having thus spoken, they took their way to the castle, but were overtaken by a messenger from the camp, who had been sent in quest of Bradamante to summon her back to the army, where her presence was needed to reassure her disheartened forces, and withstand the advance of the Moors.", "이렇게 말한 뒤 그들은 성으로 가는 길을 떠났으나, 진영에서 온 한 전령에게 따라잡혔다. 그 전령은 브라다만테를 찾아 그녀를 다시 군대로 불러오라는 명을 받고 보내진 것이었으니, 낙심한 그녀의 병력을 안심시키고 무어인들의 진격을 막아 내는 데 그녀의 존재가 필요했기 때문이었다."]], [["The mournful knight, whose name was Pinabel, thus became aware that Bradamante was a scion of the house of Clermont, between which and his own of Mayence there existed an ancient feud.", "이름이 피나벨이었던 그 슬픔에 잠긴 기사는 이리하여 브라다만테가 클레르몽 가문의 후손이며, 그 가문과 자기 가문인 마옌스 가문 사이에는 오랜 반목이 있음을 알게 되었다."], ["From this moment the traitor sought only how he might be rid of the company of Bradamante, from whom he feared no good would come to him, but rather mortal injury, if his name and lineage became known to her.", "이 순간부터 그 배신자는 오로지 브라다만테와의 동행에서 벗어날 방도만을 찾았으니, 그는 그녀에게서 자기에게 좋은 일이라곤 일어나지 않으리라, 오히려 자기 이름과 혈통이 그녀에게 알려진다면 치명적인 해를 입으리라고 두려워했기 때문이다."], ["For he judged her by his own base model, and, knowing his ill deserts, he feared to receive his due.", "그는 자기 자신의 비열한 잣대로 그녀를 헤아렸고, 자기 악행의 응보를 알고 있었기에 마땅한 보복을 받을까 두려워했던 것이다."]], [["Bradamante, in spite of the summons to return to the army, could not resolve to leave her lover in captivity, and determined first to finish the adventure on which she was engaged.", "브라다만테는 군대로 돌아오라는 소환에도 불구하고 자기 연인을 포로로 남겨 두기로 결심할 수 없어, 먼저 자신이 착수한 모험을 끝마치기로 마음먹었다."], ["Pinabel leading the way, they at length arrived at a wood, in the centre of which rose a steep, rocky mountain.", "피나벨이 길을 앞장서서, 그들은 마침내 한 숲에 이르렀는데, 그 한가운데에는 가파르고 바위투성이인 산이 솟아 있었다."], ["Pinabel, who now thought of nothing else but how he might escape from Bradamante, proposed to ascend the mountain to extend his view, in order to discover a shelter for the night, if any there might be within sight.", "이제 브라다만테에게서 어떻게 달아날까 하는 것 말고는 아무 생각도 없던 피나벨은, 만일 눈에 보이는 범위 안에 밤을 지낼 피난처가 있다면 그것을 찾아내기 위해 시야를 넓히려고 산에 오르자고 제안했다."], ["Under this pretence he left Bradamante, and advanced up the side of the mountain till he came to a cleft in the rock, down which he looked, and perceived that it widened below into a spacious cavern.", "이 핑계 아래 그는 브라다만테를 떠나 산비탈을 올라가다 바위틈에 이르렀고, 그 아래를 내려다보니 그것이 아래쪽에서는 널찍한 동굴로 넓어지는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["Meanwhile Bradamante, fearful of losing her guide, had followed close on his footsteps, and rejoined him at the mouth of the cavern.", "그동안 자기 안내자를 잃을까 두려웠던 브라다만테는 그의 발자취를 바짝 뒤따라가 동굴 입구에서 그와 다시 합류했다."], ["Then the traitor, seeing the impossibility of escaping her, conceived another design.", "그러자 그 배신자는 그녀에게서 달아날 수 없음을 깨닫고 또 다른 계략을 꾸몄다."], ["He told her that before her approach he had seen in the cavern a young and beautiful damsel, whose rich dress announced her high birth, who with tears and lamentations implored assistance; that before he could descend to relieve her a ruffian had seized her, and hurried her away into the recesses of the cavern.", "그는 그녀에게 말하기를, 그녀가 다가오기 전에 동굴 안에서 젊고 아름다운 한 처녀를 보았는데, 그 화려한 옷차림이 그녀의 고귀한 출생을 알려 주었으며, 그녀가 눈물과 한탄으로 도움을 청했다고, 그리고 자기가 그녀를 구하러 내려가기도 전에 한 악당이 그녀를 붙잡아 동굴 깊숙한 곳으로 끌고 가 버렸다고 했다."]], [["Bradamante, full of truth and courage, readily believed this lie of the Mayencian traitor.", "진실하고 용감함으로 가득한 브라다만테는 이 마옌스 출신 배신자의 거짓말을 선뜻 믿었다."], ["Eager to succor the damsel, she looked round for the means of facilitating the descent, and seeing a large elm with spreading branches she lopped off with her sword one of the largest, and thrust it into the opening.", "그 처녀를 구하려는 열망에 그녀는 내려가기를 수월하게 할 수단을 찾아 둘러보았고, 가지를 넓게 펼친 커다란 느릅나무를 발견하고는 검으로 가장 큰 가지 하나를 잘라 내 그 틈으로 밀어 넣었다."], ["She told Pinabel to hold fast to the larger end, while, grasping the branches with her hands, she let herself down into the cavern.", "그녀는 피나벨에게 굵은 쪽 끝을 단단히 붙잡고 있으라 이르고는, 손으로 가지들을 움켜쥔 채 자신을 동굴 속으로 내려뜨렸다."]], [["The traitor smiled at seeing her thus suspended, and, asking her in mockery, “Are you a good leaper?”", "그 배신자는 그녀가 이렇게 매달린 것을 보고 미소 지으며, \"그대는 뛰어내리기를 잘하시오?\""], ["he let go the branch with perfidious glee, and saw Bradamante precipitated to the bottom of the cave.", "하고 비웃듯 물으면서, 음흉한 환희에 차 가지를 놓아 버렸고, 브라다만테가 동굴 바닥으로 곤두박질치는 것을 보았다."], ["“I wish your whole race were there with you,”", "\"그대의 일족 전부가 그대와 함께 거기 있었더라면,\""], ["he muttered, “that you might all perish together.”", "그가 중얼거렸다, \"그래서 그대들 모두가 함께 죽어 버렸더라면.\""]], [["But Pinabel’s atrocious design was not accomplished.", "그러나 피나벨의 흉악한 계략은 이루어지지 않았다."], ["The twigs and foliage of the branch broke its descent, and Bradamante, not seriously injured, though stunned with her fall, was reserved for other adventures.", "가지의 잔가지와 잎사귀들이 그 추락을 막아 주어, 브라다만테는 떨어지면서 충격에 기절하기는 했으나 심하게 다치지는 않았고, 다른 모험들을 위해 보존되었다."]], [["As soon as she recovered from the shock Bradamante cast her eyes around and perceived a door, through which she passed into a second cavern, larger and loftier than the first.", "충격에서 회복되자마자 브라다만테는 사방으로 눈길을 돌렸고, 한 문을 발견하여 그것을 지나 첫 번째 것보다 더 크고 높은 두 번째 동굴로 들어섰다."], ["It had the appearance of a subterranean temple.", "그것은 지하의 신전 같은 모습이었다."], ["Columns of the purest alabaster adorned it, and supported the roof; a simple altar rose in the middle; a lamp, whose radiance was reflected by the alabaster walls, cast a mild light around.", "더없이 순수한 설화석고 기둥들이 그것을 장식하며 지붕을 떠받쳤다. 한가운데에는 소박한 제단이 솟아 있었고, 설화석고 벽에 그 광채가 반사되는 등불 하나가 부드러운 빛을 사방에 던지고 있었다."], ["Bradamante, inspired by a sense of religious awe, approached the altar, and, falling on her knees, poured forth her prayers and thanks to the Preserver of her life, invoking the protection of his power.", "종교적 외경심에 사로잡힌 브라다만테는 제단으로 다가가 무릎을 꿇고, 자기 목숨의 보존자에게 기도와 감사를 쏟아 내며 그 힘의 보호를 간구했다."], ["At that moment a small door opened, and a female issued from it with naked feet, and flowing robe and hair, who called her by her name, and thus addressed her: “Brave and generous Bradamante, know that it is a power from above that has brought you hither.", "그 순간 작은 문 하나가 열리더니 맨발에 옷자락과 머리카락을 흩날리는 한 여인이 그곳에서 나와 그녀의 이름을 부르며 이렇게 말을 건넸다. \"용감하고 너그러운 브라다만테여, 그대를 이곳으로 데려온 것은 위로부터의 힘임을 알라."], ["The spirit of Merlin, whose last earthly abode was in this place, has warned me of your arrival, and of the fate that awaits you.", "마지막으로 이승에 거하던 곳이 바로 이곳이었던 멀린의 영혼이 그대의 도착과 그대를 기다리는 운명을 내게 미리 알려 주었다."], ["This famous grotto,”", "이 유명한 동굴은,\""], ["she continued, “was the work of the enchanter Merlin; here his ashes repose.", "그녀가 말을 이었다, \"마법사 멀린의 작품이며, 이곳에 그의 유골이 잠들어 있다."], ["You have no doubt heard how this sage and virtuous enchanter ceased to be.", "이 현명하고 덕 있는 마법사가 어떻게 생을 마쳤는지는 그대도 분명 들었으리라."], ["Victim of the artful fairy of the lake, Merlin, by a fatal compliance with her request, laid himself down living in his tomb, without power to resist the spell laid upon him by that ingrate, who retained him there as long as he lived.", "멀린은 호수의 교활한 요정의 희생자가 되어, 그녀의 청에 운명적으로 응한 탓에 산 채로 제 무덤에 누웠으니, 그 배은망덕한 자가 그에게 건 주문에 저항할 힘이 없었던 것이다. 그 요정은 그가 사는 한 그를 그곳에 붙들어 두었다."], ["His spirit hovers about this spot, and will not leave it, until the last trumpet shall summon the dead to judgment.", "그의 영혼은 이곳을 맴돌며, 마지막 나팔 소리가 죽은 자들을 심판으로 불러낼 때까지 이곳을 떠나지 않을 것이다."], ["He answers the questions of those who approach his tomb, where perhaps you may be privileged to hear his voice.”", "그는 자기 무덤에 다가오는 이들의 물음에 답하니, 그곳에서 어쩌면 그대도 그의 목소리를 듣는 영광을 누릴지 모른다.\""]], [["Bradamante, astonished at these words, and the objects which met her view, knew not whether she was awake or asleep.", "이 말과 자기 눈앞에 펼쳐진 광경들에 놀란 브라다만테는 자신이 깨어 있는지 잠들어 있는지 알 수 없었다."], ["Confused, but modest, she cast down her eyes, and a blush overspread her face.", "어리둥절하면서도 겸손하게 그녀는 눈을 내리깔았고, 그녀의 얼굴에 붉은빛이 번졌다. \"아, 내가 무엇이기에,\""], ["“Ah, what am I,” said she, “that so great a prophet should deign to speak to me!”", "그녀가 말했다, \"그토록 위대한 예언자가 나에게 말씀하기를 마다하지 않으신단 말인가!\""], ["Still, with a secret satisfaction, she followed the priestess, who led her to the tomb of Merlin.", "그래도 그녀는 은밀한 만족감에 차 그 여사제를 따라갔고, 여사제는 그녀를 멀린의 무덤으로 인도했다."], ["This tomb was constructed of a species of stone hard and resplendent like fire.", "이 무덤은 단단하고 불처럼 찬란하게 빛나는 일종의 돌로 만들어져 있었다."], ["The rays which beamed from the stone sufficed to light up that terrible place, where the sun’s rays never penetrated; but I know not whether that light was the effect of a certain phosphorescence of the stone itself, or of the many talismans and charms with which it was wrought over.", "그 돌에서 뿜어 나오는 광선만으로도 햇빛이 결코 스며들지 않는 그 끔찍한 곳을 밝히기에 충분했으나, 그 빛이 돌 자체의 어떤 인광의 효과인지, 아니면 그 위에 새겨진 수많은 부적과 주문의 효과인지는 나도 알지 못한다."]], [["Bradamante had hardly passed the threshold of this sacred place when the spirit of the enchanter saluted her with a voice firm and distinct: “May thy designs be prosperous, O chaste and noble maiden, the future mother of heroes, the glory of Italy, and destined to fill the whole world with their fame.", "브라다만테가 이 신성한 곳의 문턱을 넘자마자, 마법사의 영혼이 굳세고 또렷한 목소리로 그녀에게 인사했다. \"그대의 계획이 번창하기를, 오 순결하고 고귀한 처녀여, 영웅들의 미래의 어머니여, 이탈리아의 영광이여, 그리고 온 세상을 그들의 명성으로 채우도록 정해진 자여."], ["Great captains, renowned knights, shall be numbered among your descendants, who shall defend the Church and restore their country to its ancient splendor.", "위대한 장수들, 이름 높은 기사들이 그대의 후손들 가운데 헤아려질 것이니, 그들은 교회를 지키고 그들의 조국에 옛 영화를 되찾아 줄 것이다."], ["Princes, wise as Augustus and the sage Numa, shall bring back the age of gold.[75] To accomplish these grand destinies it is ordained that you shall wed the illustrious Rogero.", "아우구스투스만큼 현명한 군주들과 현자 누마 같은 이들이 황금시대를 다시 가져올 것이다.[75] 이 위대한 운명을 이루기 위해, 그대가 이름 높은 로제로와 혼인하도록 정해져 있다."], ["Fly then to his deliverance, and lay prostrate in the dust the traitor who has snatched him from you, and now holds him in chains!”", "그러니 어서 그를 구하러 날아가, 그를 그대에게서 낚아채 지금 사슬로 묶어 두고 있는 그 배신자를 흙먼지 속에 쓰러뜨리라!\""]], [["Merlin ceased with these words, and left to Melissa, the priestess, the charge of more fully instructing the maiden in her future course.", "멀린은 이 말과 함께 그치고는, 그 처녀에게 앞으로의 행로를 더 자세히 가르치는 일을 여사제 멜리사에게 맡겼다."], ["“To-morrow,”", "\"내일,\""], ["said she, “I will conduct you to the castle on the rock where Rogero is held captive.", "그녀가 말했다, \"내가 그대를 로제로가 갇혀 있는 바위 위의 성으로 인도하겠다."], ["I will not leave you till I have guided you through this wild wood, and I will direct you on your way so that you shall be in no danger of mistaking it.”", "이 거친 숲을 지나 그대를 안내하기 전에는 그대 곁을 떠나지 않을 것이며, 그대가 길을 잘못 들 위험이 없도록 그대의 길을 일러 주겠다.\""]], [["The next morning Melissa conducted Bradamante between rocks and precipices, crossing rapid torrents, and traversing intricate passes, employing the time in imparting to her such information as was necessary to enable her to bring her design to a successful issue.", "이튿날 아침 멜리사는 브라다만테를 바위와 벼랑 사이로 인도하여, 거센 급류를 건너고 복잡한 협로를 가로질렀으며, 그동안 그녀가 자기 계획을 성공적으로 이루어 내는 데 필요한 정보들을 일러 주었다."]], [["“Not only would the castle, impenetrable by force, and that winged horse of his baffle your efforts, but know that he possesses also a buckler whence flashes a light so brilliant that the eyes of all who look upon it are blinded.", "\"힘으로는 뚫을 수 없는 그 성과 그자의 날개 달린 말이 그대의 노력을 좌절시킬 뿐 아니라, 그가 또한 너무도 눈부신 빛을 뿜어 그것을 바라보는 모든 이의 눈을 멀게 하는 방패를 지니고 있음도 알라."], ["Think not to avoid it by shutting your eyes, for how then will you be able to avoid his blows, and make him feel your own?", "눈을 감아 그것을 피할 수 있으리라고는 생각지 마라. 그렇게 하면 그대가 어떻게 그의 타격을 피하고, 그에게 그대의 타격을 느끼게 할 수 있겠는가?"], ["But I will teach you the proper course to pursue.", "그러나 내가 그대에게 취해야 할 올바른 방도를 가르쳐 주겠다."]], [["“Agramant, the Moorish prince, possesses a ring stolen from a queen of India, which has power to render of no avail all enchantments.", "무어인 군주 아그라만트는 인도의 한 여왕에게서 훔친 반지를 지니고 있으니, 그것은 모든 마법을 무력하게 만드는 힘을 지녔다."], ["Agramant, knowing that Rogero is of more importance to him than any one of his warriors, is desirous of rescuing him from the power of the enchanter, and has sent for that purpose Brunello, the most crafty and sagacious of his servants, provided with his wonderful ring, and he is even now at hand, bent on this enterprise.", "아그라만트는 로제로가 자기 전사들 누구보다도 자기에게 더 중요한 인물임을 알고 있어, 그를 그 마법사의 손아귀에서 구해 내기를 바라며, 그 목적으로 자기 종 가운데 가장 교활하고 명민한 브루넬로를, 그 놀라운 반지를 갖추어 보냈으니, 그자는 바로 지금 이 일에 골몰하여 가까이 와 있다."], ["But, beautiful Bradamante, as I desire that no one but yourself shall have the glory of delivering from thraldom your future spouse, listen while I disclose the means of success.", "그러나 아름다운 브라다만테여, 그대 미래의 배우자를 속박에서 구해 낼 영광을 그대 말고는 누구도 차지하지 못하기를 내가 바라므로, 성공의 방도를 일러 주는 동안 잘 들으라."], ["Following this path which leads by the seashore, you will come ere long to a hostelry, where the Saracen Brunello will arrive shortly before you.", "바닷가를 따라 나 있는 이 길을 따라가면 그대는 머잖아 한 여관에 이를 것이며, 그곳에 사라센인 브루넬로가 그대보다 조금 앞서 도착해 있을 것이다."], ["You will readily know him by his stature, under four feet, his great disproportioned head, his squint eyes, his livid hue, his thick eyebrows joining his tufted beard.", "그대는 그를 쉽게 알아볼 것이니, 4피트도 안 되는 그 키, 균형이 맞지 않게 큰 머리, 사팔뜨기 눈, 납빛 얼굴, 더부룩한 수염과 맞붙은 짙은 눈썹으로 알 수 있을 것이다."], ["His dress, moreover, that of a courier, will point him out to you.", "게다가 그의 옷차림, 곧 전령의 차림이 그대에게 그를 가리켜 줄 것이다."]], [["“It will be easy for you to enter into conversation with him, announcing yourself as a knight seeking combat with the enchanter, but let not the knave suspect that you know anything about the ring.", "그대가 그와 대화를 나누기는 쉬울 것이니, 자신을 마법사와 싸우려는 한 기사라고 밝히되, 그 악당이 그대가 반지에 대해 무엇이든 알고 있다고 의심하게 해서는 안 된다."], ["I doubt not that he will be your guide to the castle of the enchanter.", "그가 그대를 마법사의 성까지 안내하리라는 것을 나는 의심치 않는다."], ["Accept his offer, but take care to keep behind him till you come in sight of the brilliant dome of the castle.", "그의 제안을 받아들이되, 성의 찬란한 둥근 지붕이 보이는 곳에 이르기까지는 그의 뒤에 머무르도록 조심하라."], ["Then hesitate not to strike him dead, for the wretch deserves no pity, and take from him the ring.", "그러고는 망설임 없이 그를 쳐 죽이라, 그 비열한 자는 동정받을 가치가 없으니, 그리고 그에게서 반지를 빼앗으라."], ["But let him not suspect your intention, for by putting the ring into his mouth he will instantly become invisible, and disappear from your eyes.”", "그러나 그가 그대의 의도를 눈치채게 해서는 안 되니, 반지를 입에 넣으면 그는 순식간에 보이지 않게 되어 그대의 눈에서 사라질 것이기 때문이다.\""]], [["Saying thus, the sage Melissa and the fair Bradamante arrived near the city of Bordeaux, where the rich and wide river Garonne pours the tribute of its waves into the sea.", "이렇게 말하면서 현명한 멜리사와 아름다운 브라다만테는 보르도시 가까이에 이르렀으니, 그곳에서는 풍요롭고 너른 가론강이 제 물결의 조공을 바다에 쏟아붓고 있었다."], ["They parted with tender embraces.", "그들은 다정하게 포옹하며 헤어졌다."], ["Bradamante, intent wholly on her purpose, hastened to arrive at the hostelry, where Brunello had preceded her a few moments only.", "오로지 자기 목적에만 마음을 쏟은 브라다만테는 여관에 도착하려고 서둘렀으며, 그곳에는 브루넬로가 그녀보다 단 몇 순간 앞서 도착해 있었다."], ["The young heroine knew him without difficulty.", "젊은 여걸은 어렵지 않게 그를 알아보았다."], ["She accosted him, and put to him some slight questions, to which he replied with adroit falsehoods.", "그녀는 그에게 말을 걸어 몇 가지 가벼운 질문을 던졌고, 그는 능란한 거짓말로 대답했다."], ["Bradamante, on her part, concealed from him her sex, her religion, her country, and the blood from whence she sprung.", "브라다만테는 그녀대로 그에게 자기 성별과 종교와 조국과 자신이 비롯된 혈통을 감추었다."], ["While they talk together, sudden cries are heard from all parts of the hostelry.", "그들이 함께 이야기를 나누는 동안, 여관 곳곳에서 갑자기 외치는 소리가 들려왔다."], ["“O queen of heaven!”", "\"오 하늘의 여왕이시여!\""], ["exclaimed Bradamante, “what can be the cause of this sudden alarm?”", "브라다만테가 외쳤다, \"이 갑작스러운 소동의 까닭이 무엇일까?\""], ["She soon learned the cause.", "그녀는 곧 그 까닭을 알게 되었다."], ["Host, children, domestics, all, with upturned eyes, as if they saw a comet or a great eclipse, were gazing on a prodigy which seemed to pass the bounds of possibility.", "주인과 아이들과 하인들 모두가, 마치 혜성이나 큰 일식이라도 본 듯이 눈을 위로 치켜뜨고, 가능성의 한계를 넘어서는 듯한 어떤 경이를 바라보고 있었다."], ["She beheld distinctly a winged horse, mounted with a cavalier in rich armor, cleaving the air with rapid flight.", "그녀는 날개 달린 말 한 마리가, 화려한 갑옷을 입은 한 기사를 태운 채 빠른 비행으로 공중을 가르는 것을 또렷이 보았다."], ["The wings of this strange courser were wide extended, and covered with feathers of various colors.", "이 기이한 군마의 날개는 넓게 펼쳐져 갖가지 빛깔의 깃털로 덮여 있었다."], ["The polished armor of the knight made them shine with rainbow tints.", "그 기사의 광택 나는 갑옷이 그 깃털을 무지개 빛깔로 빛나게 했다."], ["In a short time the horse and rider disappeared behind the summits of the mountains.", "잠깐 사이에 말과 기사는 산봉우리들 뒤로 사라졌다."]], [["“It is an enchanter,”", "\"저것은 마법사요,\""], ["said the host, “a magician who often is seen traversing the air in that way.", "주인이 말했다, \"저런 식으로 공중을 가로지르는 것이 자주 목격되는 마법사라오."], ["Sometimes he flies aloft as if among the stars, and at others skims along the land.", "어떤 때는 마치 별들 사이에 있는 듯 높이 날고, 또 어떤 때는 땅 위를 스치듯 날지요."], ["He possesses a wonderful castle on the top of the Pyrenees.", "그는 피레네산맥 꼭대기에 놀라운 성 하나를 지니고 있소."], ["Many knights have shown their courage by going to attack him, but none have ever returned, from which it is to be feared they have lost either their life or their liberty.”", "많은 기사가 그를 공격하러 가 용기를 보였으나, 누구도 돌아온 적이 없으니, 그것으로 보아 그들이 목숨이나 자유를 잃은 것이 아닌가 두렵소.\""]], [["Bradamante, addressing the host, said, “Could you furnish me a guide to conduct me to the castle of this enchanter?”", "브라다만테가 주인에게 말했다. \"이 마법사의 성으로 나를 인도해 줄 안내자를 마련해 줄 수 있겠소?\""], ["“By my faith,”", "\"맹세코,\""], ["said Brunello, interrupting, “that you shall not seek in vain; I have it all in writing, and I will myself conduct you.”", "브루넬로가 끼어들며 말했다, \"그것은 헛되이 찾지 않으셔도 되오. 나에게 그것이 모두 글로 적혀 있으니, 내가 직접 그대를 안내하겠소.\""], ["Bradamante, with thanks, accepted him for her guide.", "브라다만테는 고마워하며 그를 자기 안내자로 받아들였다."]], [["The host had a tolerable horse to dispose of, which Bradamante bargained for, and the next day, at the first dawn of morning, she took her route by a narrow valley, taking care to have the Saracen Brunello lead the way.", "주인에게는 처분할 만한 그런대로 쓸 만한 말 한 마리가 있어, 브라다만테는 그것을 흥정해 사들였고, 이튿날 동이 트는 첫 새벽에 그녀는 좁은 골짜기를 따라 길을 떠나면서, 사라센인 브루넬로가 앞장서 길을 인도하도록 신경 썼다."]], [["They reached the summit of the Pyrenees, whence one may look down on France, Spain, and the two seas.", "그들은 피레네산맥 꼭대기에 이르렀으니, 그곳에서는 프랑스와 스페인과 두 바다를 내려다볼 수 있었다. 이 높은 곳에서 그들은 다시 고된 길을 따라 깊은 골짜기로 내려갔다."], ["From this height they descended again by a fatiguing road into a deep valley.", "이 골짜기 한가운데에 외따로 솟은 산 하나가 있었는데, 거칠고 깎아지른 바위로 이루어져 있었으며, 그 꼭대기에는 청동 벽으로 둘러싸인 성이 있었다."], ["From the middle of this valley an isolated mountain rose, composed of rough and perpendicular rock, on whose summit was the castle, surrounded with a wall of brass.", "브루넬로가 말했다. \"저기가 그 마법사가 자기 포로들을 가두어 두는 요새요."], ["Brunello said, “Yonder is the stronghold where the enchanter keeps his prisoners; one must have wings to mount thither; it is easy to see that the aid of a flying horse must be necessary for the master of this castle, which he uses for his prison and for his abode.”", "저곳에 오르려면 날개가 있어야 하니, 이 성의 주인에게는 날개 달린 말의 도움이 필요할 수밖에 없음을 쉽게 알 수 있소. 그는 이 성을 자기 감옥으로도, 자기 거처로도 쓰지요.\""]], [["Bradamante, sufficiently instructed, saw that the time had now come to possess herself of the ring; but she could not resolve to slay a defenceless man.", "충분히 일러 받은 브라다만테는 이제 반지를 손에 넣을 때가 왔음을 알았다. 그러나 그녀는 무방비한 사람을 죽이기로 결심할 수가 없었다."], ["She seized Brunello before he was aware, bound him to a tree, and took from him the ring which he wore on one of his fingers.", "그녀는 브루넬로가 알아차리기 전에 그를 붙잡아 나무에 묶고는, 그가 손가락 하나에 끼고 있던 반지를 빼앗았다."], ["The cries and entreaties of the perfidious Saracen moved her not.", "그 음흉한 사라센인의 외침과 애원도 그녀의 마음을 움직이지 못했다."], ["She advanced to the foot of the rock whereon the castle stood, and, to draw the magician to the combat, sounded her horn, adding to it cries of defiance.", "그녀는 성이 서 있는 바위 발치로 나아가, 마법사를 싸움으로 끌어내려고 자기 뿔나팔을 불며 도전의 외침을 더했다."]], [["The enchanter delayed not to present himself, mounted on his winged horse.", "마법사는 지체 없이 날개 달린 말을 타고 모습을 드러냈다."], ["Bradamante was struck with surprise mixed with joy when she saw that this person, described as so formidable, bore no lance nor club, nor any other deadly weapon.", "브라다만테는 그토록 무시무시하다고 묘사된 이 인물이 창도 곤봉도, 그 어떤 치명적인 무기도 지니고 있지 않은 것을 보고 기쁨이 섞인 놀라움에 사로잡혔다."], ["He had only on his arm a buckler, covered with a cloth, and in his hand an open book.", "그는 다만 천으로 덮인 방패 하나를 팔에 두르고, 손에는 펼쳐진 책 한 권을 들고 있을 뿐이었다."], ["As to the winged horse, there was no enchantment about him.", "그 날개 달린 말로 말하자면, 거기에는 아무런 마법도 없었다."], ["He was a natural animal, of a species which exists in the Riphæan mountains.", "그것은 리파이아산맥에 사는 어떤 종에 속하는 자연의 짐승이었다."], ["Like a griffin, he had the head of an eagle, claws armed with talons, and wings covered with feathers, the rest of his body being that of a horse.", "그리핀처럼 그것은 독수리의 머리에, 발톱으로 무장한 발과 깃털로 덮인 날개를 지녔으며, 몸의 나머지는 말의 것이었다."], ["This strange animal is called a Hippogriff.", "이 기이한 짐승은 히포그리프라고 불린다."]], [["The heroine attacked the enchanter on his approach, striking on this side and on that, with all the energy of a violent combat, but wounding only the wind; and after this pretended attack had lasted some time dismounted from her horse, as if hoping to do battle more effectually on foot.", "여걸은 마법사가 다가오자 격렬한 싸움의 온 기세를 다해 이쪽저쪽으로 내리치며 그를 공격했으나, 베는 것이라곤 허공뿐이었다. 그리고 이 거짓 공격이 한동안 이어진 뒤, 그녀는 마치 걸어서 더 효과적으로 싸우기를 바라는 듯 말에서 내렸다."], ["The enchanter now prepares to employ his sole weapon, by uncovering the magic buckler which never failed to subdue an enemy by depriving him of his senses.", "이제 마법사는 자기의 유일한 무기를 쓸 채비를 하니, 적의 정신을 빼앗아 굴복시키는 데 결코 실패한 적 없는 그 마법의 방패를 벗기는 것이었다."], ["Bradamante, confiding in her ring, observed all the motions of her adversary, and, at the unveiling of the shield, cast herself on the ground, pretending that the splendor of the shield had overcome her, but in reality to induce the enchanter to dismount and approach her.", "자기 반지를 믿은 브라다만테는 적의 모든 움직임을 지켜보다가, 방패의 천이 벗겨지자 마치 방패의 광채에 압도된 척하며 땅바닥에 몸을 던졌으니, 실은 마법사가 말에서 내려 자기에게 다가오도록 유인하기 위함이었다."]], [["It happened according to her wish.", "일은 그녀의 바람대로 되었다."], ["When the enchanter saw her prostrate he made his horse alight on the ground, and, dismounting, fixed the shield on the pommel of his saddle, and approached in order to secure the fallen warrior.", "마법사는 그녀가 엎어진 것을 보자 자기 말을 땅에 내려앉히고는, 말에서 내려 방패를 안장 앞머리에 고정한 뒤 쓰러진 전사를 붙잡으려고 다가왔다."], ["Bradamante, who watched him intently, as soon as she saw him near at hand, sprang up, seized him vigorously, threw him down, and, with the same chain which the enchanter had prepared for herself, bound him fast, without his being able to make any effectual resistance.", "그를 골똘히 지켜보던 브라다만테는 그가 가까이 다가온 것을 보자마자 벌떡 일어나 그를 힘껏 붙잡아 쓰러뜨리고는, 마법사가 자기에게 쓰려고 준비해 두었던 바로 그 사슬로 그를 단단히 묶었으니, 그는 어떠한 효과적인 저항도 할 수 없었다."], ["The enchanter, with the accents of despair, exclaimed, “Take my life, young man!”", "마법사는 절망의 어조로 외쳤다. \"내 목숨을 가져가오, 젊은이!\""], ["but Bradamante was far from complying with such a wish.", "그러나 브라다만테는 그러한 소원을 들어줄 마음이 전혀 없었다."], ["Desirous of knowing the name of the enchanter, and for what purpose he had formed with so much art this impregnable fortress, she commanded him to inform her.", "그 마법사의 이름과, 그가 무슨 목적으로 그토록 정교하게 이 난공불락의 요새를 지었는지를 알고 싶었던 그녀는, 그에게 알려 달라고 명했다."]], [["“Alas!”", "\"아아!\""], ["replied the magician, while tears flowed down his cheeks, “it is not to conceal booty, nor for any culpable design that I have built this castle; it was only to guard the life of a young knight, the object of my tenderest affection, my art having taught me that he is destined to become a Christian, and to perish, shortly after, by the blackest of treasons.", "마법사가 두 뺨에 눈물을 흘리며 대답했다, \"내가 이 성을 지은 것은 약탈품을 숨기기 위함도, 어떤 죄스러운 계획을 위함도 아니오. 그것은 오로지 내가 더없이 다정하게 사랑하는 한 젊은 기사의 목숨을 지키기 위함이었으니, 내 술법이 나에게 일러 주기를 그가 그리스도교도가 되도록, 그리고 그 직후에 가장 시커먼 배신으로 목숨을 잃도록 정해져 있다 하였소."]], [["“This youth, named Rogero, is the most beautiful and most accomplished of knights.", "로제로라는 이름의 이 젊은이는 기사들 가운데 가장 아름답고 가장 출중하오."], ["It is I, the unhappy Atlantes, who have reared him from his childhood.", "그를 어린 시절부터 길러 온 이가 바로 나, 불행한 아틀란테스요."], ["The call of honor and the desire of glory led him from me to follow Agramant, his prince, in his invasion of France, and I, more devoted to Rogero than the tenderest of parents, have sought the means of bringing him back to this abode, in the hope of saving him from the cruel fate that menaces him.", "명예의 부름과 영광에 대한 갈망이 그를 나에게서 떼어 내, 자기 군주 아그라만트를 따라 프랑스 침공에 나서게 했고, 가장 다정한 부모보다도 더 로제로에게 헌신한 나는, 그를 위협하는 잔혹한 운명에서 구해 내려는 희망으로 그를 이 거처로 데려올 방도를 찾았소."]], [["“For this purpose I have got him in my possession by the same means as I attempted to employ against you; and by which I have succeeded in collecting a great many knights and ladies in my castle.", "이 목적을 위해 나는 그대에게 쓰려 했던 것과 같은 수단으로 그를 내 수중에 넣었으며, 그 같은 방법으로 수많은 기사와 귀부인들을 내 성에 모으는 데 성공했소."], ["My purpose was to render my beloved pupil’s captivity light, by affording him society to amuse him, and keep his thoughts from running on subjects of war and glory.", "내 뜻은 내 사랑하는 제자에게 그를 즐겁게 해 줄 벗을 마련해 주어 그의 마음이 전쟁과 영광의 일들로 향하지 않게 함으로써, 그의 사로잡힘을 가벼이 하려는 것이었소."], ["Alas!", "아아!"], ["my cares have been in vain!", "내 보살핌은 헛되었구려!"], ["Yet, take, I beseech you, whatever else I have, but spare me my beloved pupil.", "그래도, 청컨대, 내가 가진 다른 무엇이든 가져가되, 내 사랑하는 제자만은 살려 주오."], ["Take this shield, take this winged courser, deliver such of your friends as you may find among my prisoners, deliver them all if you will, but leave me my beloved Rogero; or if you will snatch him too from me, take also my life, which will cease then to be to me worth preserving.”", "이 방패를 가지시오, 이 날개 달린 군마를 가지시오, 내 포로들 가운데 그대의 벗들을 찾아내거든 풀어 주시오, 원한다면 그들 모두를 풀어 주시오, 그러나 내게 사랑하는 로제로만은 남겨 주오. 만일 그마저 내게서 낚아채 가려거든, 내 목숨도 함께 가져가시오, 그러면 내 목숨은 더는 내게 보존할 가치가 없을 것이니.\""]], [["Bradamante replied: “Old man, hope not to move me by your vain entreaties.", "브라다만테가 대답했다. \"노인이여, 부질없는 애원으로 나를 움직이려 하지 마시오."], ["It is precisely the liberty of Rogero that I require.", "내가 요구하는 것이 바로 그 로제로의 자유요."], ["You would keep him here in bondage and in slothful pleasure, to save him from a fate which you foresee.", "그대는 그를 이곳에 속박해 두고 나태한 쾌락에 빠뜨려, 그대가 내다본 운명에서 그를 구하려 하오."], ["Vain old man!", "부질없는 노인이여!"], ["how can you foresee his fate when you could not foresee your own?", "그대 자신의 운명도 내다보지 못했으면서 어찌 그의 운명을 내다볼 수 있단 말이오?"], ["You desire me to take your life.", "그대는 내게 그대의 목숨을 가져가라 하오."], ["No, my arm and my soul refuse the request.”", "아니오, 내 팔과 내 영혼은 그 청을 거부하오.\""], ["This said, she required the magician to go before, and guide her to the castle.", "이렇게 말한 뒤 그녀는 마법사에게 앞장서서 자신을 성으로 인도하라고 명했다."], ["The prisoners were set at liberty, though some, in their secret hearts, regretted the voluptuous life which was thus brought to an end.", "포로들은 풀려났으나, 그들 가운데 몇몇은 그렇게 끝나 버린 그 향락의 삶을 속으로 아쉬워했다."], ["Bradamante and Rogero met one another with transports of joy.", "브라다만테와 로제로는 기쁨에 겨워 서로를 맞이했다."]], [["They descended from the mountain to the spot where the encounter had taken place.", "그들은 산에서 내려와 격돌이 벌어졌던 곳에 이르렀다."], ["There they found the Hippogriff, with the magic buckler in its wrapper, hanging to his saddle-bow.", "그곳에서 그들은 마법의 방패를 천에 싸인 채 안장 앞머리에 매단 히포그리프를 발견했다."], ["Bradamante advanced to seize the bridle; the Hippogriff seemed to wait her approach, but before she reached him he spread his wings and flew away to a neighboring hill, and in the same manner, a second time, eluded her efforts.", "브라다만테가 고삐를 붙잡으러 나아가니, 히포그리프는 그녀가 다가오기를 기다리는 듯했으나, 그녀가 닿기 전에 날개를 펴 이웃한 언덕으로 날아가 버렸고, 같은 식으로 두 번째에도 그녀의 노력을 따돌렸다."], ["Rogero and the other liberated knights dispersed over the plain and hill-tops to secure him, and at last the animal allowed Rogero to seize his rein.", "로제로와 풀려난 다른 기사들이 그를 붙잡으려고 들판과 언덕 꼭대기로 흩어졌고, 마침내 그 짐승은 로제로가 자기 고삐를 붙잡도록 허락했다."], ["The fearless Rogero hesitated not to vault upon his back, and let him feel his spurs, which so roused his mettle that, after galloping a short distance, he suddenly spread his wings, and soared into the air.", "두려움을 모르는 로제로는 망설임 없이 그 등으로 뛰어올라 박차를 느끼게 했고, 그것이 그 기개를 어찌나 돋우었던지 잠깐 달리던 그는 갑자기 날개를 펴 공중으로 솟아올랐다."], ["Bradamante had the grief to see her lover snatched away from her at the very moment of reunion.", "브라다만테는 재회의 바로 그 순간에 자기 연인이 자기에게서 낚아채여 가는 것을 보는 슬픔을 겪었다."], ["Rogero, who knew not the art of directing the horse, was unable to control his flight.", "말을 다루는 기술을 알지 못하는 로제로는 그 비행을 통제할 수 없었다."], ["He found himself carried over the tops of the mountains, so far above them that he could hardly distinguish what was land and what water.", "그는 자신이 산봉우리들 위로, 어디가 땅이고 어디가 물인지 거의 분간할 수 없을 만큼 높이 실려 가는 것을 깨달았다."], ["The Hippogriff directed his flight to the west, and cleaved the air as swiftly as a new-rigged vessel cuts the waves, impelled by the freshest and most favorable gales. ————", "히포그리프는 비행을 서쪽으로 잡고는, 가장 상쾌하고 가장 알맞은 바람에 떠밀려 새로 의장을 갖춘 배가 물결을 가르듯 빠르게 공중을 갈랐다."]], [["# ASTOLPHO AND THE ENCHANTRESS", "# ASTOLPHO AND THE ENCHANTRESS"]], [["In the long flight which Rogero took on the back of the Hippogriff he was carried over land and sea, unknowing whither.", "아스톨포와 마녀\n로제로가 히포그리프의 등에 올라 떠난 그 긴 비행에서, 그는 어디로 가는지도 모른 채 땅과 바다 위로 실려 갔다."], ["As soon as he had gained some control over the animal he made him alight on the nearest land.", "그 짐승에 대해 어느 정도 통제력을 얻자마자 그는 그것을 가장 가까운 육지에 내려앉게 했다."], ["When he came near enough to earth Rogero leapt lightly from his back, and tied the animal to a myrtle-tree.", "땅에 충분히 가까워졌을 때 로제로는 가볍게 그 등에서 뛰어내려 그 짐승을 도금양나무에 묶었다."], ["Near the spot flowed the pure waters of a fountain, surrounded by cedars and palm-trees.", "그 자리 가까이에는 삼나무와 종려나무로 둘러싸인 샘의 맑은 물이 흐르고 있었다."], ["Rogero laid aside his shield, and, removing his helmet, breathed with delight the fresh air, and cooled his lips with the waters of the fountain.", "로제로는 방패를 내려놓고 투구를 벗은 뒤, 신선한 공기를 기쁘게 들이마시고 샘물로 입술을 식혔다."], ["For we cannot wonder that he was excessively fatigued, considering the ride he had taken.", "그가 한 그 비행을 생각하면 그가 극도로 지쳐 있었다는 것은 놀랄 일이 아니기 때문이다."], ["He was preparing to taste the sweets of repose when he perceived that the Hippogriff, which he had tied by the bridle to a myrtle-tree, frightened at something, was making violent efforts to disengage himself.", "그가 막 휴식의 단맛을 맛보려던 참에, 그는 자기가 고삐로 도금양나무에 묶어 둔 히포그리프가 무언가에 놀라 벗어나려고 격렬하게 발버둥치는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["His struggle shook the myrtle-tree so that many of its beautiful leaves were torn off, and strewed the ground.", "그 몸부림이 도금양나무를 어찌나 흔들었던지 그 아름다운 잎사귀들이 많이 뜯겨 나가 땅에 흩어졌다."]], [["A sound like that which issues from burning wood seemed to come from the myrtle-tree, at first faint and indistinct, but growing stronger by degrees, and at length was audible as a voice which spoke in this manner: “O knight, if the tenderness of your heart corresponds to the beauty of your person, relieve me, I pray you, from this tormenting animal.", "타는 나무에서 나는 듯한 소리가 그 도금양나무에서 나오는 듯했으니, 처음에는 희미하고 또렷하지 않더니, 차츰 강해져 마침내 이렇게 말하는 목소리로 들리게 되었다. \"오 기사여, 만일 그대 마음의 다정함이 그대 외모의 아름다움에 걸맞다면, 청컨대 이 괴롭히는 짐승에게서 나를 풀어 주오."], ["I suffer enough inwardly without having outward evils added to my lot.”", "나는 바깥의 고통이 내 신세에 더해지지 않아도 안으로 충분히 괴로워하고 있소.\""]], [["Rogero, at the first accents of this voice, turned his eyes promptly on the myrtle, hastened to it, and stood fixed in astonishment when he perceived that the voice issued from the tree itself.", "이 목소리의 첫 음절에 로제로는 곧바로 눈을 도금양나무로 돌려 그리로 서둘러 갔고, 그 목소리가 나무 자체에서 나오는 것을 알아차리고는 놀라움에 굳어 버렸다."], ["He immediately untied his horse, and, flushed with surprise and regret, exclaimed, “Whoever thou art, whether mortal or the goddess of these woods, forgive me, I beseech you, my involuntary fault.", "그는 곧바로 자기 말을 풀고, 놀라움과 후회로 얼굴이 붉어진 채 외쳤다. \"그대가 누구이든, 필멸의 존재이든 이 숲의 여신이든, 청컨대 나의 본의 아닌 잘못을 용서해 주오."], ["Had I imagined that this hard bark covered a being possessed of feeling, could I have exposed such a beautiful myrtle to the insults of this steed?", "만일 이 단단한 껍질이 감정을 지닌 존재를 감싸고 있는 줄 짐작이라도 했더라면, 내가 이토록 아름다운 도금양나무를 이 군마의 횡포에 내맡길 수 있었겠소?"], ["May the sweet influences of the sky and air speedily repair the injury I have done!", "하늘과 대기의 감미로운 기운이 내가 끼친 해를 어서 회복시켜 주기를!"], ["For my part, I promise by the sovereign lady of my heart to do everything you wish in order to merit your forgiveness.”", "나로 말하자면, 내 마음의 주권자이신 여인을 두고 맹세하노니, 그대의 용서를 받기 위해 그대가 바라는 모든 일을 다 하겠소.\""]], [["At these words the myrtle seemed to tremble from root to stem, and Rogero remarked that a moisture as of tears trickled down its bark, like that which exudes from a log placed on the fire.", "이 말에 도금양나무는 뿌리에서 줄기까지 떠는 듯했고, 로제로는 마치 불 위에 놓인 통나무에서 스며 나오는 것 같은 눈물 같은 물기가 그 껍질을 따라 흘러내리는 것을 보았다."], ["It then spoke:", "그러고는 그것이 말했다."]], [["“The kindness which inspires your words compels me to disclose to you who I once was, and by what fatality I have been changed into this shape.", "\"그대의 말이 풍기는 친절함이 나로 하여금 내가 한때 누구였는지, 그리고 어떤 불운으로 이 모습으로 바뀌었는지를 그대에게 밝히지 않을 수 없게 하오."], ["My name was Astolpho, cousin of Orlando and Rinaldo, whose fame has filled the earth.", "내 이름은 아스톨포였으니, 그 명성이 온 세상을 가득 채운 오를란도와 리날도의 사촌이었소."], ["I was myself reckoned among the bravest paladins of France, and was by birth entitled to reign over England, after Otho, my father.", "나 자신도 프랑스의 가장 용감한 팔라딘들 가운데 하나로 꼽혔으며, 태생으로는 내 아버지 오토의 뒤를 이어 잉글랜드를 다스릴 권리를 지니고 있었소."], ["Returning from the distant East, with Rinaldo and many other brave knights, called home to aid with our arms the great Emperor of France, we reached a spot where the powerful enchantress Alcina possessed a castle on the borders of the sea.", "우리의 무력으로 프랑스의 위대한 황제를 돕도록 본국으로 부름받아, 리날도와 다른 여러 용감한 기사들과 함께 머나먼 동방에서 돌아오던 길에, 우리는 강력한 마녀 알치나가 바닷가에 성 하나를 소유하고 있는 곳에 이르렀소."], ["She had gone to the water-side to amuse herself with fishing, and we paused to see how, by her art, without hook or line, she drew from the water whatever she would.", "그녀는 낚시로 소일하려고 물가에 나와 있었고, 우리는 그녀가 자기 술법으로 낚싯바늘도 낚싯줄도 없이 물에서 무엇이든 마음대로 끌어 올리는 것을 구경하려고 걸음을 멈췄소."]], [["“Not far from the shore an enormous whale showed a back so broad and motionless that it looked like an island.", "물가에서 멀지 않은 곳에 거대한 고래 한 마리가 너무도 넓고 미동도 없는 등을 드러내고 있어 마치 섬처럼 보였소."], ["Alcina had fixed her eyes on me, and planned to get me into her power.", "알치나는 나에게 눈길을 고정하고는 나를 자기 손아귀에 넣을 궁리를 했소."], ["Addressing us, she said: ‘This is the hour when the prettiest mermaid in the sea comes regularly every day to the shore of yonder island.", "그녀가 우리에게 말하기를, '바로 지금이 바다에서 가장 어여쁜 인어가 매일 정해진 때에 저 섬 기슭으로 오는 시각이오."], ["She sings so sweetly that the very waves flow smoother at the sound.", "그 인어는 너무도 감미롭게 노래하여 그 소리에 물결마저 더 잔잔히 흐른다오."], ["If you wish to hear her come with me to her resort.’ So saying, Alcina pointed to the fish, which we all supposed to be an island.", "그 노래를 듣고 싶거든 나와 함께 그 인어가 머무는 곳으로 갑시다.' 그렇게 말하며 알치나는 그 물고기를 가리켰는데, 우리는 모두 그것을 섬이라 여겼소."], ["I, who was rash, did not hesitate to follow her; but swam my horse over, and mounted on the back of the fish.", "무모했던 나는 망설임 없이 그녀를 따라갔으니, 말을 헤엄쳐 건너가 그 물고기의 등에 올랐소."], ["In vain Rinaldo and Dudon made signs to me to beware; Alcina, smiling, took me in charge, and led the way.", "리날도와 두돈이 헛되이 나에게 조심하라는 신호를 보냈으나, 알치나는 미소 지으며 나를 떠맡고 앞장서 갔소."], ["No sooner were we mounted upon him than the whale moved off, spreading his great fins, and cleft rapidly the waters.", "우리가 그 위에 오르자마자 고래는 큰 지느러미를 펼치며 움직이기 시작해 빠르게 물을 갈랐소."], ["I then saw my folly, but it was too late to repent.", "그제야 나는 내 어리석음을 깨달았으나, 후회하기에는 너무 늦었소."], ["Alcina soothed my anger, and professed that what she had done was for love of me.", "알치나는 내 분노를 달래며, 자기가 한 일은 나를 사랑하기 때문이었다고 공언했소."], ["Ere long we arrived at this island, where at first everything was done to reconcile me to my lot, and to make my days pass happily away.", "머잖아 우리는 이 섬에 이르렀고, 처음에는 나를 내 신세에 만족하게 하고 내 나날을 행복하게 보내게 하려고 온갖 일이 행해졌소."], ["But soon Alcina, sated with her conquest, grew indifferent, then weary of me, and at last, to get rid of me, changed me into this form, as she had done to many lovers before me, making some of them olives, some palms, some cedars, changing others into fountains, rocks, or even into wild beasts.", "그러나 곧 알치나는 자기 정복에 싫증이 나 무관심해지더니, 나에게 진력이 났고, 마침내 나를 떼어 버리려고 나를 이 모습으로 바꾸어 버렸소. 그녀는 나보다 앞서 수많은 연인에게도 그리했으니, 그들 가운데 더러는 올리브나무로, 더러는 종려나무로, 더러는 삼나무로 만들고, 또 다른 이들은 샘이나 바위로, 심지어 들짐승으로 바꾸어 버렸소."], ["And thou, courteous knight, whom accident has brought to this enchanted isle, beware that she get not the power over thee, or thou shalt haply be made like us, a tree, a fountain, or a rock.”", "그러니 그대, 우연히 이 마법의 섬으로 오게 된 정중한 기사여, 그녀가 그대에 대해 힘을 얻지 못하도록 조심하오, 그러지 않으면 그대도 어쩌면 우리처럼 나무나 샘이나 바위가 되고 말 것이오.\""]], [["Rogero expressed his astonishment at this recital.", "로제로는 이 이야기에 자기 놀라움을 표했다."], ["Astolpho added that the island was in great part subject to the sway of Alcina.", "아스톨포는 그 섬의 태반이 알치나의 지배를 받고 있다고 덧붙였다."], ["By the aid of her sister Morgana, she had succeeded in dispossessing a third sister, Logestilla, of nearly the whole of her patrimony, for the whole isle was hers originally by her father’s bequest.", "그녀는 자기 자매 모르가나의 도움으로 또 다른 자매 로제스틸라에게서 그 세습 재산의 거의 전부를 빼앗는 데 성공했으니, 그 섬 전체가 본래 아버지의 유언에 따라 로제스틸라의 것이었기 때문이다."], ["But Logestilla was temperate and sage, while the other sisters were false and voluptuous.", "그러나 로제스틸라는 절제 있고 현명했던 반면, 다른 두 자매는 거짓되고 음탕했다."], ["Her empire was divided from theirs by a gulf and chain of mountains, which alone had thus far prevented her sister from usurping it.", "로제스틸라의 영토는 만(灣)과 산줄기로 그들의 영토와 갈라져 있었는데, 지금까지 그것만이 그 자매가 로제스틸라의 영토를 찬탈하는 것을 막아 왔다."]], [["Astolpho here ended his tale, and Rogero, who knew that he was the cousin of Bradamante, would gladly have devised some way for his relief; but, as that was out of his power, he consoled him as well as he could, and then begged to be told the way to the palace of Logestilla, and how to avoid that of Alcina.", "아스톨포는 여기서 자기 이야기를 마쳤고, 그가 브라다만테의 사촌임을 안 로제로는 기꺼이 그를 구할 어떤 방도를 궁리하고 싶었으나, 그것이 자기 능력 밖의 일인지라, 할 수 있는 한 그를 위로하고는, 로제스틸라의 궁전으로 가는 길과 알치나의 궁전을 피하는 법을 일러 달라고 청했다."], ["Astolpho directed him to take the road to the left, though rough and full of rocks.", "아스톨포는 그에게 왼쪽 길을 택하라고 일러 주었으니, 그것은 비록 험하고 바위투성이였다."], ["He warned him that this road would present serious obstacles; that troops of monsters would oppose his passage, employed by the art of Alcina to prevent her subjects from escaping from her dominion.", "그는 이 길이 심각한 장애들을 내놓으리라고, 곧 괴물들의 무리가 그의 통행을 막으리라고 경고했으니, 그것들은 알치나가 자기 신민들이 자기 영토에서 달아나는 것을 막으려고 술법으로 부리는 것들이었다."], ["Rogero thanked the myrtle, and prepared to set out on his way.", "로제로는 도금양나무에게 고마움을 표하고는 길을 떠날 채비를 했다."]], [["He at first thought he would mount the winged horse, and scale the mountain on his back; but he was too uncertain of his power to control him to wish to encounter the hazard of another flight through the air, besides that he was almost famished for the want of food.", "그는 처음에는 날개 달린 말에 올라 그 등에 탄 채 산을 오를까 생각했으나, 그를 다스릴 자기 능력이 너무도 미덥지 못해 또 한 번 공중을 비행하는 위험을 무릅쓰고 싶지 않았고, 게다가 먹을 것이 없어 거의 굶주린 상태였다."], ["So he led the horse after him, and took the road on foot, which for some distance led equally to the dominions of both the sisters.", "그리하여 그는 말을 뒤에 이끌고 걸어서 길을 떠났는데, 그 길은 얼마간은 두 자매의 영토 양쪽으로 똑같이 이어져 있었다."]], [["He had not advanced more than two miles when he saw before him the superb city of Alcina.", "그가 채 2마일도 더 나아가기 전에 그는 자기 앞에 알치나의 화려한 도시를 보았다. 그 도시는 황금 벽으로 둘러싸여 있어 하늘에 닿을 듯이 보였다."], ["It was surrounded with a wall of gold, which seemed to reach the skies.", "어떤 이들은 그 벽이 진짜 황금이 아니라 단지 연금술의 산물일 뿐이라고 생각하는 줄 나도 안다."], ["I know that some think that this wall was not of real gold, but only the work of alchemy; it matters not; I prefer to think it gold, for it certainly shone like gold.", "그러나 그것은 중요치 않으니, 나는 그것을 황금이라 여기는 편이 좋다. 그것이 분명 황금처럼 빛났기 때문이다."]], [["A broad and level road led to the gates of the city, and from this another branched off, narrow and rough, which led to the mountain region.", "넓고 평탄한 길이 도시의 성문으로 이어졌고, 이 길에서 또 하나의 길이 갈라져 나왔는데, 좁고 험한 그 길은 산악 지대로 이어졌다."], ["Rogero took without hesitation the narrow road; but he had no sooner entered upon it than he was assailed by a numerous troop which opposed his passage.", "로제로는 망설임 없이 좁은 길을 택했으나, 그 길에 들어서자마자 그의 통행을 막는 한 무리의 떼거리에게 습격당했다."]], [["You never have seen anything so ridiculous, so extraordinary, as this host of hobgoblins were.", "그대는 이 도깨비 무리만큼 우스꽝스럽고 기괴한 것을 일찍이 본 적이 없을 것이다."], ["Some of them bore the human form from the neck to the feet, but had the head of a monkey or a cat; others had the legs and the ears of a horse; old men and women, bald and hideous, ran hither and thither as if out of their senses, half clad in the shaggy skins of beasts; one rode full speed on a horse without a bridle, another jogged along mounted on an ass or a cow; others, full of agility, skipped about, and clung to the tails and manes of the animals which their companions rode.", "그것들 가운데 더러는 목에서 발까지는 사람의 모습이었으나 머리는 원숭이나 고양이의 것이었고, 다른 것들은 말의 다리와 귀를 지녔으며, 대머리에 흉측한 늙은이와 늙은 여자들이 짐승의 텁수룩한 가죽을 반쯤 걸친 채 마치 제정신이 아닌 듯 이리저리 내달렸다. 하나는 고삐 없는 말을 타고 전속력으로 달렸고, 또 하나는 나귀나 소를 타고 느릿느릿 갔으며, 다른 것들은 민첩하기 그지없게 깡충깡충 뛰어다니며 제 동료들이 탄 짐승들의 꼬리와 갈기에 매달렸다."], ["Some blew horns, others brandished drinking-cups; some were armed with spits, and some with pitchforks.", "어떤 것들은 뿔나팔을 불었고, 다른 것들은 술잔을 휘둘렀으며, 어떤 것들은 꼬챙이로, 어떤 것들은 쇠스랑으로 무장했다."], ["One, who appeared to be the captain, had an enormous belly and a gross fat head; he was mounted on a tortoise, that waddled, now this way, now that, without keeping any one direction.", "우두머리로 보이는 하나는 엄청나게 큰 배와 살찐 큰 머리를 지녔으며, 거북 한 마리를 타고 있었는데, 그 거북은 어느 한 방향도 지키지 못한 채 이리저리 뒤뚱거렸다."]], [["One of these monsters, who had something approaching the human form, though he had the neck, ears, and muzzle of a dog, set himself to bark furiously at Rogero, to make him turn off to the right, and reenter upon the road to the gay city; but the brave chevalier exclaimed, “That will I not, so long as I can use this sword,”—and he thrust the point directly at his face. The monster tried to strike him with a lance, but Rogero was too quick for him, and thrust his sword through his body, so that it appeared a hand’s breadth behind his back. The paladin, now giving full vent to his rage, laid about him vigorously among the rabble, cleaving one to the teeth, another to the girdle; but the troop were so numerous, and in spite of his blows pressed around him so close, that, to clear his way, he must have had as many arms as Briareus.", "이 괴물들 가운데 하나가, 비록 목과 귀와 주둥이는 개의 것이었으나 사람의 모습에 어느 정도 가까웠던 그것이, 로제로에게 사납게 짖어 대며 그를 오른쪽으로 돌려 그 화려한 도시로 가는 길로 다시 들어서게 하려 했다. 그러나 그 용감한 기사는 외쳤다. \"이 검을 쓸 수 있는 한, 나는 그러지 않을 것이다.\" 그러고는 곧장 그것의 얼굴에 칼끝을 들이밀었다. 그 괴물이 창으로 그를 치려 했으나, 로제로가 너무 빨라, 그 몸에 검을 찔러 넣으니 칼끝이 그 등 뒤로 손 한 뼘만큼 튀어나왔다. 이제 분노를 마음껏 터뜨린 팔라딘은 그 천한 무리 사이에서 힘차게 칼을 휘둘러, 하나는 이빨까지, 또 하나는 허리띠까지 쪼개 버렸다. 그러나 그 무리가 어찌나 많은지, 그의 타격에도 불구하고 그를 너무도 바짝 에워싸는 통에, 길을 트려면 그는 브리아레오스만큼이나 많은 팔을 지녀야 했을 것이다."]], [["If Rogero had uncovered the shield of the enchanter, which hung at his saddle-bow, he might easily have vanquished this monstrous rout; but perhaps he did not think of it, and perhaps he preferred to seek his defence nowhere but in his good sword.", "만일 로제로가 자기 안장 앞머리에 매달려 있던 마법사의 방패를 벗겼더라면, 그는 이 괴물 같은 패거리를 쉽게 무찔렀을 것이다. 그러나 어쩌면 그는 그것을 생각지 못했거나, 어쩌면 자기의 훌륭한 검 말고는 어디에서도 방어를 구하기를 원치 않았던 것이리라."], ["At that moment, when his perplexity was at its height, he saw issue from the city gate two young beauties, whose air and dress proclaimed their rank and gentle nurture.", "그의 당혹이 절정에 이른 그 순간, 그는 도시의 성문에서 두 젊은 미인이 나오는 것을 보았으니, 그들의 풍채와 옷차림이 그들의 신분과 고귀한 가문 출신임을 알려 주었다."], ["Each of them was mounted on a unicorn, whose whiteness surpassed that of ermine.", "그들은 각자 일각수 한 마리를 타고 있었는데, 그 일각수의 흰빛은 흰담비의 것보다도 더 희었다."], ["They advanced to the meadow where Rogero was contending so valiantly against the hobgoblins, who all retired at their approach.", "그들은 로제로가 그토록 용맹하게 도깨비들을 상대로 싸우고 있던 풀밭으로 다가왔고, 도깨비들은 그들이 다가오자 모두 물러갔다."], ["They drew near, they extended their hands to the young warrior, whose cheeks glowed with the flush of exercise and modesty.", "그들은 가까이 와서 그 젊은 전사에게 손을 내밀었으니, 그의 두 뺨은 격렬한 운동과 수줍음의 홍조로 달아올라 있었다."], ["Grateful for their assistance, he expressed his thanks, and, having no heart to refuse them, followed their guidance to the gate of the city.", "그들의 도움에 고마워한 그는 감사를 표했고, 그들을 거절할 마음이 없어 그들의 안내를 따라 도시의 성문으로 갔다."]], [["This grand and beautiful entrance was adorned by a portico of four vast columns, all of diamond.", "이 웅장하고 아름다운 입구는 모두 금강석으로 된 네 개의 거대한 기둥으로 이루어진 주랑(柱廊)으로 장식되어 있었다."], ["Whether they were real diamond or artificial I cannot say.", "그것들이 진짜 금강석인지 인공의 것인지 나는 말할 수 없다."], ["What matter is it, so long as they appeared to the eye like diamond, and nothing could be more gay and splendid.", "그것들이 눈에는 금강석처럼 보였고, 그보다 더 화려하고 찬란할 수 없었으니, 그것이 무슨 상관이겠는가."]], [["On the threshold, and between the columns, was seen a bevy of charming young women, who played and frolicked together.", "문턱 위, 그리고 기둥들 사이에는 한 무리의 매혹적인 젊은 여인들이 보였는데, 그들은 함께 놀며 장난치고 있었다."], ["They all ran to receive Rogero, and conducted him into the palace, which appeared like a paradise.", "그들 모두가 달려 나와 로제로를 맞이하고는, 그를 궁전 안으로 인도했으니, 그 궁전은 마치 낙원 같아 보였다."]], [["We might well call by that name this abode, where the hours flew by, without account, in ever-new delights.", "우리는 이 거처를 그 이름으로 부를 만하였으니, 그곳에서는 시간이 헤아릴 수도 없이, 언제나 새로운 즐거움 속에서 흘러갔다."], ["The bare idea of satiety, want, and, above all, of age, never entered the minds of the inhabitants.", "포만이나 결핍, 무엇보다도 늙음이라는 생각조차 그곳 주민들의 마음에는 결코 들어선 적이 없었다."], ["They experienced no sensations except those of luxury and gayety; the cup of happiness seemed for them ever-flowing and exhaustless.", "그들은 사치와 환락 말고는 어떤 감각도 느끼지 못했고, 행복의 잔은 그들을 위해 끊임없이 흘러넘쳐 마를 줄 모르는 듯했다."], ["The two young damsels to whom Rogero owed his deliverance from the hobgoblins conducted him to the apartment of their mistress.", "로제로가 도깨비들에게서 구출된 것을 빚진 그 두 젊은 처녀는 그를 자기 여주인의 거처로 인도했다."], ["The beautiful Alcina advanced, and greeted him with an air at once dignified and courteous.", "아름다운 알치나가 나아와, 위엄과 정중함을 함께 갖춘 태도로 그를 맞이했다."], ["All her court surrounded the paladin, and rendered him the most flattering attentions.", "그녀의 온 궁정이 팔라딘을 에워싸고 그에게 더없이 아첨하는 환대를 베풀었다."], ["The castle was less admirable for its magnificence than for the charms of those who inhabited it.", "그 성은 그 웅장함보다 그곳에 사는 이들의 매력으로 더 감탄할 만했다."], ["They were of either sex, well matched in beauty, youth, and grace; but among this charming group the brilliant Alcina shone, as the sun outshines the stars.", "그들은 남녀 양성으로, 아름다움과 젊음과 우아함에서 잘 어울렸으나, 이 매혹적인 무리 가운데서도 눈부신 알치나는 마치 해가 별들을 무색하게 하듯 빛났다."], ["The young warrior was fascinated.", "그 젊은 전사는 매혹되었다."], ["All that he had heard from the myrtle-tree appeared to him but a vile calumny.", "그가 도금양나무에게서 들은 모든 것이 그에게는 한낱 비열한 중상으로만 보였다."], ["How could he suspect that falsehood and treason veiled themselves under smiles and the ingenuous air of truth?", "거짓과 배신이 미소와 순진한 진실의 모습 아래 자신을 가린다는 것을 어찌 그가 의심할 수 있었겠는가?"], ["He doubted not that Astolpho had deserved his fate, and perhaps a punishment more severe; he regarded all his stories as dictated by a disappointed spirit, and a thirst for revenge.", "그는 아스톨포가 자기 운명을, 어쩌면 더 가혹한 벌을 마땅히 받았으리라고 의심치 않았다. 그는 그의 모든 이야기를 좌절한 마음과 복수에 대한 갈망에서 비롯된 것으로 여겼다."], ["But we must not condemn Rogero too harshly, for he was the victim of magic power.", "그러나 우리는 로제로를 너무 가혹하게 비난해서는 안 되니, 그는 마법의 힘의 희생자였기 때문이다."]], [["They seated themselves at table, and immediately harmonious lyres and harps waked the air with the most ravishing notes.", "그들이 식탁에 자리를 잡자마자, 조화로운 리라와 하프가 더없이 황홀한 가락으로 대기를 일깨웠다."], ["The charms of poetry were added in entertaining recitals; the magnificence of the feast would have done credit to a royal board.", "흥겨운 이야기들로 시(詩)의 매력이 더해졌고, 그 잔치의 화려함은 왕가의 식탁에 견주어도 손색이 없었을 것이다."], ["The traitress forgot nothing which might charm the paladin, and attach him to the spot, meaning, when she should grow tired of him, to metamorphose him as she had done others.", "그 배신자 여인은 팔라딘을 매혹하여 그곳에 붙들어 둘 만한 그 무엇도 빠뜨리지 않았으니, 그에게 싫증이 나면 그를 다른 이들에게 했던 것처럼 변신시킬 작정이었다."], ["In the same manner passed each succeeding day.", "같은 식으로 뒤이은 나날들도 흘러갔다."], ["Games of pleasant exercise, the chase, the dance, or rural sports, made the hours pass quickly; while they gave zest to the refreshment of the bath, or sleep.", "즐거운 운동 경기와 사냥과 춤, 혹은 전원의 유희가 시간을 빠르게 지나가게 했고, 한편으로는 목욕이나 잠의 상쾌함에 흥취를 더해 주었다."]], [["Thus Rogero led a life of ease and luxury, while Charlemagne and Agramant were struggling for empire.", "이리하여 로제로는 카를루스와 아그라만트가 제국을 두고 다투는 동안 안락과 사치의 삶을 살았다."], ["But I cannot linger with him while the amiable and courageous Bradamante is night and day directing her uncertain steps to every spot where the slightest chance invites her, in the hope of recovering Rogero.", "그러나 사랑스럽고 용감한 브라다만테가 로제로를 되찾으려는 희망으로, 아주 작은 가능성이라도 그녀를 부르는 곳이라면 어디로든 밤낮으로 자신의 불확실한 걸음을 옮기고 있는 동안, 나는 그와 함께 머물러 있을 수 없다."]], [["I will therefore say that, having sought him in vain in fields and in cities, she knew not whither next to direct her steps.", "그러므로 나는 이렇게 말하겠다. 그녀는 들판에서도 도시에서도 그를 헛되이 찾은 끝에, 다음에는 어디로 발걸음을 향해야 할지 알지 못했다."], ["She did not apprehend the death of Rogero.", "그녀는 로제로의 죽음을 염려하지는 않았다."], ["The fall of such a hero would have reëchoed from the Hydaspes to the farthest river of the West; but, not knowing whether he was on the earth or in the air, she concluded, as a last resource, to return to the cavern which contained the tomb of Merlin, to ask of him some sure direction to the object of her search.", "그러한 영웅의 죽음이라면 히다스페스강에서 서방의 가장 먼 강에 이르기까지 메아리쳤을 테니까. 그러나 그가 땅 위에 있는지 공중에 있는지 알지 못한 그녀는, 마지막 수단으로 멀린의 무덤이 든 동굴로 돌아가, 자기가 찾는 대상에 대한 어떤 확실한 인도를 그에게 청하기로 결심했다."], ["While this thought occupied her mind, Melissa, the sage enchantress, suddenly appeared before her.", "이 생각이 그녀의 마음을 차지하고 있던 동안, 현명한 마녀 멜리사가 갑자기 그녀 앞에 나타났다."], ["This virtuous and beneficent magician had discovered by her spells that Rogero was passing his time in pleasure and idleness, forgetful of his honor and his sovereign.", "이 덕 있고 자비로운 마법사는 자기 주문으로 로제로가 자기 명예와 자기 군주를 잊은 채 쾌락과 나태 속에 시간을 보내고 있음을 알아냈다."], ["Not able to endure the thought that one who was born to be a hero should waste his years in base repose, and leave a sullied reputation in the memory of survivors, she saw that vigorous measures must be employed to draw him forth into the paths of virtue.", "영웅이 되도록 태어난 자가 비루한 안락 속에 세월을 허비하고, 살아남은 이들의 기억 속에 더럽혀진 명성을 남긴다는 생각을 견딜 수 없었던 그녀는, 그를 덕의 길로 끌어내기 위해 강력한 조치를 써야 함을 깨달았다."], ["Melissa was not blinded by her affection for the amiable paladin, like Atlantes, who, intent only on preserving Rogero’s life, cared nothing for his fame.", "멜리사는 아틀란테스처럼 그 사랑스러운 팔라딘에 대한 애정에 눈멀지 않았으니, 아틀란테스는 오로지 로제로의 목숨을 보존하는 데만 골몰하여 그의 명성 따위는 전혀 개의치 않았던 것이다."], ["It was that old enchanter whose arts had guided the Hippogriff to the isle of the too charming Alcina, where he hoped his favorite would learn to forget honor, and lose the love of glory.", "너무도 매혹적인 알치나의 섬으로 히포그리프를 인도한 술법이 바로 그 늙은 마법사의 것이었으니, 그는 그곳에서 자기가 아끼는 자가 명예를 잊고 영광에 대한 사랑을 버리기를 배우기를 바랐던 것이다."]], [["At the sight of Melissa joy lighted up the countenance of Bradamante, and hope animated her breast.", "멜리사를 보자 브라다만테의 얼굴에 기쁨이 밝아 올랐고, 그녀의 가슴에 희망이 살아났다."], ["Melissa concealed nothing from her, but told her how Rogero was in the toils of Alcina.", "멜리사는 그녀에게 아무것도 숨기지 않고, 로제로가 알치나의 올가미에 빠져 있음을 이야기했다."], ["Bradamante was plunged in grief and terror; but the kind enchantress calmed her, dispelled her fears, and promised that before many days she would lead back the paladin to her feet.", "브라다만테는 비탄과 두려움에 잠겼으나, 친절한 마녀가 그녀를 달래고 그녀의 두려움을 흩어 주었으며, 며칠 안에 그 팔라딘을 그녀의 발치로 다시 데려오겠다고 약속했다."]], [["“My daughter,”", "\"내 딸아,\""], ["she said, “give me the ring which you wear, and which possesses the power to overcome enchantments.", "그녀가 말했다, \"그대가 끼고 있는, 마법을 무찌르는 힘을 지닌 그 반지를 내게 다오."], ["By means of it I doubt not but that I may enter the stronghold where the false Alcina holds Rogero in durance, and may succeed in vanquishing her and liberating him.”", "그것의 도움으로 나는 거짓된 알치나가 로제로를 가두어 두고 있는 그 요새로 들어가, 그녀를 무찌르고 그를 풀어 주는 데 성공할 수 있으리라고 믿어 의심치 않는다.\""], ["Bradamante unhesitatingly delivered her the ring, recommending Rogero to her best efforts.", "브라다만테는 망설임 없이 그녀에게 반지를 건네며, 로제로를 위해 최선을 다해 달라고 부탁했다."], ["Melissa then summoned by her art a huge palfrey, black as jet, excepting one foot, which was bay.", "그러자 멜리사는 자기 술법으로 칠흑같이 검은, 다만 한 발만은 적갈색인 거대한 말 한 마리를 불러냈다."], ["Mounted upon this animal, she rode with such speed that by the next morning she had reached the abode of Alcina.", "이 짐승을 탄 그녀는 너무도 빠르게 달려, 이튿날 아침에는 알치나의 거처에 다다랐다."]], [["She here transformed herself into the perfect resemblance of the old magician Atlantes, adding a palm-breadth to her height, and enlarging her whole figure.", "그녀는 이곳에서 자신을 늙은 마법사 아틀란테스와 완벽하게 닮은 모습으로 변신시켰으니, 자기 키에 한 뼘을 더하고 온몸을 부풀렸다."], ["Her chin she covered with a long beard, and seamed her whole visage well with wrinkles.", "자기 턱은 긴 수염으로 덮고, 온 얼굴에는 주름을 가득 새겼다."], ["She assumed also his voice and manner, and watched her chance to find Rogero alone.", "그녀는 또한 그의 목소리와 태도도 흉내 내며, 로제로가 혼자 있을 기회를 엿보았다."], ["At last she found him, dressed in a rich tunic of silk and gold, a collar of precious stones about his neck, and his arms, once so rough with exercise, decorated with bracelets.", "마침내 그녀는 그를 찾아냈으니, 그는 비단과 황금으로 된 화려한 겉옷을 입고, 목에는 보석 목걸이를 두르고, 한때 단련으로 그토록 거칠었던 두 팔은 팔찌로 장식하고 있었다."], ["His air and his every motion indicated effeminacy, and he seemed to retain nothing of Rogero but the name; such power had the enchantress obtained over him.", "그의 풍채와 모든 몸짓이 유약함을 드러냈고, 그는 로제로의 것이라곤 이름밖에 지니고 있지 않은 듯했으니, 그 마녀가 그에 대해 그만한 힘을 얻었던 것이다."]], [["Melissa, under the form of his old instructor, presented herself before him, wearing a stern and serious visage.", "자기의 옛 스승의 모습을 한 멜리사는 엄하고 진지한 얼굴로 그의 앞에 나섰다. \"그렇다면 이것이,\""], ["“Is this, then,” she said, “the fruit of all my labors?", "그녀가 말했다, \"내 모든 노고의 열매란 말이냐?"], ["Is it for this that I fed you on the marrow of bears and lions, that I taught you to subdue dragons, and, like Hercules, strangle serpents in your youthful grasp, only to make you, by all my cares, a feeble Adonis?", "내가 너를 곰과 사자의 골수로 먹이고, 너에게 용을 제압하는 법을 가르치고, 헤라클레스처럼 어린 나이에 네 손아귀로 뱀을 목 졸라 죽이게 한 것이, 고작 내 모든 보살핌으로 너를 유약한 아도니스로 만들기 위함이었단 말이냐?"], ["My nightly watchings of the stars, of the yet warm fibres of animals, the lots I have cast, the points of nativity that I have calculated, have they all falsely indicated that you were born for greatness?", "별들을, 아직 따뜻한 짐승들의 섬유 조직을 밤새워 지켜본 것이며, 내가 던진 점괘며, 내가 계산한 출생의 별자리들이, 네가 위대함을 위해 태어났다고 모두 거짓되이 가리켰단 말이냐?"], ["Who could have believed that you would become the slave of a base enchantress?", "네가 한 비열한 마녀의 노예가 되리라고 누가 믿을 수 있었겠느냐?"], ["O Rogero, learn to know this Alcina, learn to understand her arts and to countervail them.", "오 로제로여, 이 알치나를 알도록 배워라, 그녀의 술법을 이해하고 그것을 무력화하는 법을 배워라."], ["Take this ring, place it on your finger, return to her presence, and see for yourself what are her real charms.”", "이 반지를 받아 네 손가락에 끼고, 그녀의 면전으로 돌아가, 그녀의 진짜 매력이 어떤 것인지 네 눈으로 직접 보라.\""]], [["At these words, Rogero, confused, abashed, cast his eyes upon the ground, and knew not what to answer.", "이 말에 로제로는 어쩔 줄 모르고 부끄러워하며 눈을 땅에 떨구었고, 무어라 대답해야 할지 알지 못했다."], ["Melissa seized the moment, slipped the ring on his finger, and the paladin was himself again.", "멜리사는 그 순간을 붙잡아 그의 손가락에 반지를 끼웠고, 팔라딘은 다시 본래의 자신으로 돌아왔다."], ["What a thunderclap to him!", "그에게 이 얼마나 청천벽력이었던가!"], ["Overcome by shame, he dared not to encounter the looks of his instructor.", "수치심에 압도된 그는 자기 스승의 눈길을 마주할 엄두를 내지 못했다."], ["When at last he raised his eyes he beheld not that venerable form, but the priestess Melissa, who in virtue of the ring now appeared in her true person.", "마침내 그가 눈을 들었을 때, 그가 본 것은 그 거룩한 모습이 아니라, 이제 반지의 힘으로 자기 본래의 모습을 드러낸 여사제 멜리사였다."], ["She told him of the motives which had led her to come to his rescue, of the griefs and regrets of Bradamante, and of her unwearied search for him.", "그녀는 그를 구하러 온 동기들과, 브라다만테의 슬픔과 한탄과, 그를 찾는 그녀의 지칠 줄 모르는 노력에 대해 그에게 이야기했다."], ["“That charming Amazon,”", "\"그 매혹적인 여전사가,\""], ["she said, “sends you this ring, which is a sovereign antidote to all enchantments.", "그녀가 말했다, \"그대에게 이 반지를 보냈으니, 그것은 모든 마법에 으뜸가는 해독제라오."], ["She would have sent you her heart in my hands, if it would have had greater power to serve you.”", "만일 자기 마음이 그대를 섬기는 데 더 큰 힘을 지녔더라면, 그녀는 내 손에 자기 마음을 들려 보냈을 것이오.\""]], [["It was needless for Melissa to say more.", "멜리사가 더 말할 필요는 없었다."], ["Rogero’s love for Alcina, being but the work of enchantment, vanished as soon as the enchantment was withdrawn, and he now hated her with an equal intensity, seeing no longer anything in her but her vices, and feeling only resentment for the shame that she had put upon him.", "알치나에 대한 로제로의 사랑은 한낱 마법의 작품에 지나지 않았던 까닭에, 마법이 거두어지자마자 사라졌고, 이제 그는 그녀에게서 그 악덕 말고는 더 이상 아무것도 보지 못하고, 그녀가 자기에게 안긴 수치심에 대한 원한만을 느끼며, 그만큼 강렬한 증오로 그녀를 미워했다."]], [["His surprise when he again beheld Alcina was no less than his indignation.", "그가 알치나를 다시 보았을 때의 놀라움은 그의 분개에 못지않았다."], ["Fortified by his ring from her enchantments, he saw her as she was, a monster of ugliness.", "반지로 그녀의 마법으로부터 무장한 그는 그녀를 본래 모습대로, 곧 추함의 괴물로 보았다."], ["All her charms were artificial, and, truly viewed, were rather deformities.", "그녀의 모든 매력은 인공의 것이었고, 진정으로 보자면 차라리 기형이었다."], ["She was, in fact, older than Hecuba or the Sibyl of Cumæ; but an art, which it is to be regretted our times have lost, enabled her to appear charming, and to clothe herself in all the attractions of youth.", "그녀는 사실 헤카베나 쿠마이의 시빌라보다도 더 늙었으나, 우리 시대가 잃어버린 것이 안타까운 어떤 술법이 그녀로 하여금 매혹적으로 보이게 하고, 젊음의 온갖 매력으로 자신을 치장하게 해 주었던 것이다."], ["Rogero now saw all this, but, governed by the counsels of Melissa, he concealed his surprise, assumed under some pretext his armor, long neglected, and bound to his side Belisarda, his trusty sword, taking also the buckler of Atlantes, covered with its veil.", "로제로는 이제 이 모든 것을 보았으나, 멜리사의 조언에 따라 자기 놀라움을 감추고, 어떤 핑계를 대어 오래 방치해 두었던 자기 갑옷을 입고, 자기의 믿음직한 검 벨리사르다를 옆구리에 찼으며, 또한 천으로 덮인 아틀란테스의 방패도 챙겼다."]], [["He then selected a horse from the stables of Alcina, without exciting her suspicions; but he left the Hippogriff, by the advice of Melissa, who promised to take him in charge, and train him to a more manageable state.", "그러고는 그녀의 의심을 사지 않은 채 알치나의 마구간에서 말 한 마리를 골랐다. 그러나 그는 멜리사의 조언에 따라 히포그리프는 남겨 두었으니, 멜리사가 그를 맡아 더 다루기 쉬운 상태로 길들이겠다고 약속했기 때문이다."], ["The horse he took was Rabican, which belonged to Astolpho.", "그가 택한 말은 아스톨포의 것이었던 라비칸이었다."], ["He restored the ring to Melissa.", "그는 멜리사에게 반지를 돌려주었다."]], [["Rogero had not ridden far when he met one of the huntsmen of Alcina, bearing a falcon on his wrist, and followed by a dog.", "로제로가 멀리 가기도 전에 그는 손목에 매를 앉히고 개 한 마리를 거느린 알치나의 사냥꾼 하나를 만났다."], ["The huntsman was mounted on a powerful horse, and came boldly up to the paladin, demanding, in a somewhat imperious manner, whither he was going so rapidly.", "그 사냥꾼은 힘센 말을 타고 있었으며, 다소 거만한 태도로 팔라딘에게 어디로 그토록 빨리 가느냐고 물으며 대담하게 다가왔다."], ["Rogero disdained to stop or to reply; whereupon the huntsman, not doubting that he was about making his escape, said, “What if I, with my falcon, stop your ride?”", "로제로는 멈추기도 대답하기도 마다했고, 그러자 그가 달아나려 하는 것이라 확신한 사냥꾼이 말했다. \"내 매로 내가 그대의 길을 멈추게 한다면 어떻겠소?\""], ["So saying, he threw off the bird, which even Rabican could not equal in speed.", "그렇게 말하며 그는 그 새를 날려 보냈는데, 그 빠르기는 라비칸조차 따를 수 없었다."], ["The huntsman then leapt from his horse, and the animal, open-mouthed, darted after Rogero with the swiftness of an arrow.", "그러더니 사냥꾼은 자기 말에서 뛰어내렸고, 그 짐승은 입을 벌린 채 화살같이 빠르게 로제로를 뒤쫓았다."], ["The huntsman also ran as if the wind or fire bore him, and the dog was equal to Rabican in swiftness.", "사냥꾼 또한 바람이나 불이 그를 실어 나르는 듯이 달렸고, 개는 빠르기가 라비칸과 맞먹었다."], ["Rogero, finding flight impossible, stopped and faced his pursuers; but his sword was useless against such foes.", "로제로는 달아나기가 불가능함을 깨닫고 멈춰 서서 자기를 뒤쫓는 자들을 마주했으나, 그의 검은 그러한 적들에게는 쓸모가 없었다."], ["The insolent huntsman assailed him with words, and struck him with his whip, the only weapon he had; the dog bit his feet, and the horse drove at him with his hoofs.", "그 무례한 사냥꾼은 말로 그를 공격하고, 자기가 지닌 유일한 무기인 채찍으로 그를 후려쳤으며, 개는 그의 발을 물어뜯었고, 말은 발굽으로 그에게 달려들었다."], ["At the same time the falcon flew over his head and over Rabican’s and attacked them with claws and wings, so that the horse in his fright began to be unmanageable.", "동시에 매는 그의 머리와 라비칸의 머리 위를 날며 발톱과 날개로 그들을 공격했고, 그 통에 말이 겁에 질려 다루기 힘들어지기 시작했다."], ["At that moment the sound of trumpets and cymbals was heard in the valley, and it was evident that Alcina had ordered out all her array to go in pursuit.", "그 순간 골짜기에 나팔과 심벌즈 소리가 울려 퍼졌고, 알치나가 추격에 나서도록 자기 군세를 모두 출동시켰음이 분명해졌다."], ["Rogero felt that there was no time to be lost, and luckily remembered the shield of Atlantes, which he bore suspended from his neck.", "로제로는 한순간도 허비할 시간이 없음을 느꼈고, 다행히도 자기 목에 매달려 있던 아틀란테스의 방패를 떠올렸다."], ["He unveiled it, and the charm worked wonderfully.", "그가 그것의 천을 벗기니 그 마력이 놀랍게 작용했다."], ["The huntsman, the dog, the horse, fell flat; the trembling wings of the falcon could no longer sustain her, and she fell senseless to the ground.", "사냥꾼과 개와 말이 납작하게 쓰러졌고, 떨리는 매의 날개는 더 이상 그녀를 떠받칠 수 없어, 그녀는 정신을 잃고 땅에 떨어졌다."], ["Rogero, rid of their annoyances, left them in their trance, and rode away.", "그들의 성가심에서 벗어난 로제로는 그들을 혼절 상태로 남겨 둔 채 말을 달려 떠났다."]], [["Meanwhile Alcina, with all the force she could muster, sallied forth from her palace in pursuit.", "한편 알치나는 모을 수 있는 모든 군세를 거느리고 추격에 나서 자기 궁전에서 뛰쳐나왔다."], ["Melissa, left behind, took advantage of the opportunity to ransack all the rooms, protected by the ring.", "뒤에 남겨진 멜리사는 그 기회를 틈타 반지로 보호받으며 모든 방을 샅샅이 뒤졌다."], ["She undid one by one all the talismans and spells which she found, broke the seals, burned the images, and untied the hag-knots.", "그녀는 자기가 발견한 모든 부적과 주문을 하나하나 풀고, 봉인을 깨뜨리고, 형상들을 불태우고, 마법의 매듭들을 풀었다."], ["Thence, hurrying through the fields, she disenchanted the victims changed into trees, fountains, stones, or brutes; all of whom recovered their liberty, and vowed eternal gratitude to their deliverer.", "그곳에서 그녀는 들판을 가로질러 서둘러 가, 나무나 샘이나 돌이나 짐승으로 바뀐 희생자들의 마법을 풀어 주었고, 그들 모두가 자유를 되찾아 자기들의 구원자에게 영원한 감사를 맹세했다."], ["They made their escape, with all possible despatch, to the realms of the good Logestilla, whence they departed to their several homes.", "그들은 할 수 있는 한 서둘러 선한 로제스틸라의 영토로 달아났고, 그곳에서 다시 저마다의 집으로 떠나갔다."]], [["Astolpho was the first whom Melissa liberated, for Rogero had particularly recommended him to her care.", "멜리사가 가장 먼저 풀어 준 이는 아스톨포였으니, 로제로가 특별히 그를 그녀의 보살핌에 부탁해 두었기 때문이다."], ["She aided him to recover his arms, and particularly that precious golden-headed lance which once was Argalia’s.", "그녀는 그가 자기 무구를, 특히 한때 아르갈리아의 것이었던 그 귀중한 황금 머리 창을 되찾도록 도왔다."], ["The enchantress mounted with him upon the winged horse, and in a short time arrived through the air at the castle of Logestilla, where Rogero joined them soon after.", "마녀는 그와 함께 날개 달린 말에 올랐고, 얼마 지나지 않아 공중을 가로질러 로제스틸라의 성에 다다랐으며, 그곳에서 로제로가 곧 그들과 합류했다. [75] 황금시대: 신화에서 인류가 평화와 풍요 속에 살았다고 전하는 태초의 이상적 시대."]], [["In this abode the friends passed a short period of delightful and improving intercourse with the sage Logestilla and her virtuous court; and then each departed, Rogero with the Hippogriff, ring, and buckler; Astolpho with his golden lance, and mounted on Rabican, the fleetest of steeds.", "이 거처에서 두 친구는 현명한 로게스틸라와 그녀의 덕망 높은 궁정과 더불어 즐겁고 유익한 교류를 나누며 짧은 기간을 보냈다. 그런 다음 각자 길을 떠났다."], ["To Rogero Logestilla gave a bit and bridle suited to govern the Hippogriff; and to Astolpho a horn of marvellous powers, to be sounded only when all other weapons were unavailing.", "로게로는 히포그리프와 반지, 그리고 방패를 챙겼고, 아스톨포는 황금 창을 들고 가장 빠른 준마인 라비칸에 올라탔다. 로게스틸라는 로게로에게 히포그리프를 다스리기에 알맞은 재갈과 고삐를 주었고, 아스톨포에게는 다른 무기가 모두 소용없을 때에만 불어야 하는 경이로운 힘을 지닌 뿔나팔을 주었다."]], [["————", "오르크"]], [["We left the charming Angelica at the moment when, in her flight from her contending lovers, Sacripant and Rinaldo, she met an aged hermit.", "우리는 매혹적인 안젤리카가 자신을 두고 다투던 두 연인 사크리판트와 리날도에게서 달아나던 순간에, 그녀가 한 늙은 은자를 만났던 데서 이야기를 멈추었다."], ["We have seen that her request to the hermit was to furnish her the means of gaining the sea-coast, eager to avoid Rinaldo, whom she hated, by leaving France and Europe itself.", "우리는 그녀가 은자에게 청한 것이, 자신이 증오하는 리날도를 피하기 위해 프랑스와 유럽 자체를 떠나 해안에 이를 수 있는 방도를 마련해 달라는 것이었음을 보았다."], ["The pretended hermit, who was no other than a vile magician, knowing well that it would not be agreeable to his false gods to aid Angelica in this undertaking, feigned to comply with her desire.", "거짓 은자는 실은 비열한 마법사에 다름 아니었으며, 안젤리카의 이 계획을 돕는 일이 자신의 거짓 신들의 마음에 들지 않으리라는 것을 잘 알면서도, 그녀의 소원을 들어주는 척했다."], ["He supplied her a horse, into which he had by his arts caused a subtle devil to enter, and, having mounted Angelica on the animal, directed her what course to take to reach the sea.", "그는 자신의 술법으로 교활한 악마를 안에 들어가게 한 말 한 필을 그녀에게 내어 주었고, 안젤리카를 그 짐승에 태운 뒤 바다에 이르려면 어떤 길을 가야 할지 일러 주었다."]], [["Angelica rode on her way without suspicion, but when arrived at the shore, the demon urged the animal headlong into the water.", "안젤리카는 아무 의심 없이 길을 갔으나, 해안에 다다르자 악마는 그 짐승을 곧장 물속으로 몰아넣었다."], ["Angelica in vain attempted to turn him back to the land; he continued his course till, as night approached, he landed with his burden on a sandy headland.", "안젤리카가 그를 뭍으로 되돌리려 애썼으나 헛수고였다. 그는 계속 나아가다가 밤이 다가올 무렵, 짐을 진 채 모래 곶에 올라섰다."]], [["Angelica, finding herself alone, abandoned in this frightful solitude, remained without movement, as if stupefied, with hands joined and eyes turned towards heaven, till at last, pouring forth a torrent of tears, she exclaimed: “Cruel fortune, have you not yet exhausted your rage against me?", "안젤리카는 자신이 이 끔찍한 고독 속에 홀로 버려졌음을 깨닫고, 마치 정신을 잃은 듯 두 손을 맞잡고 눈을 하늘로 향한 채 미동도 없이 있다가, 마침내 눈물을 폭포처럼 쏟아 내며 외쳤다. \"잔혹한 운명이여, 너는 아직도 나에 대한 분노를 다 쏟아 내지 못했단 말인가?"], ["To what new miseries do you doom me?", "어떤 새로운 비참함으로 나를 몰아넣으려는 것이냐?"], ["Alas!", "아아!"], ["then finish your work!", "그러면 네 일을 끝내 다오!"], ["Deliver me a prey to some ferocious beast, or by whatever fate you choose bring me to an end.", "나를 어느 사나운 짐승의 먹이로 내어 주든, 네가 택한 어떤 운명으로든 나를 끝장내 다오."], ["I will be thankful to you for terminating my life and my misery.”", "내 목숨과 내 비참함을 끝내 준다면 나는 너에게 감사하리라.\""], ["At last, exhausted by her sorrows, she fell asleep, and sunk prostrate on the sand.", "마침내 슬픔에 지친 그녀는 잠이 들어 모래 위에 엎드려 쓰러졌다."]], [["Before recounting what next befell, we must declare what place it was upon which the unhappy lady was now thrown.", "다음에 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 이야기하기에 앞서, 우리는 이 불행한 여인이 이제 던져진 곳이 어떤 장소였는지를 밝혀야 한다."], ["In the sea that washes the coast of Ireland there is an island called Ebuda, whose inhabitants, once numerous, had been wasted by the anger of Proteus till there were now but few left.", "아일랜드 해안을 씻는 바다에 에부다라는 섬이 있었는데, 한때 수가 많았던 그곳 주민들은 프로테우스의 분노로 인해 점점 줄어 이제는 얼마 남지 않았다."], ["This deity was incensed by some neglect of the usual honors which he had in old times received from the inhabitants of the land, and, to execute his vengeance, had sent a horrid sea-monster, called an Orc, to devour them.", "이 신은 옛날에 그 땅의 주민들에게서 받곤 했던 통상적인 예우가 어떤 식으로든 소홀해진 데에 노여워했고, 복수를 실행하기 위해 그들을 잡아먹을 오르크라 불리는 무시무시한 바다 괴물을 보냈다."], ["Such were the terrors of his ravages that the whole people of the isle had shut themselves up in the principal town, and relied on their walls alone to protect them.", "그 약탈의 공포가 얼마나 컸던지, 섬의 온 백성이 중심 도시에 틀어박혀 오로지 성벽만을 의지해 자신들을 지키려 했다."], ["In this distress they applied to the Oracle for advice, and were directed to appease the wrath of the sea-monster by offering to him the fairest virgin that the country could produce.", "이 곤경 속에서 그들은 신탁에 조언을 구했고, 그 나라에서 낼 수 있는 가장 아름다운 처녀를 바쳐 바다 괴물의 분노를 달래라는 지시를 받았다."]], [["Now it so happened that the very day when this dreadful oracle was announced, and when the fatal mandate had gone forth to seek among the fairest maidens of the land one to be offered to the monster, some sailors, landing on the beach where Angelica was, beheld that beauty as she lay asleep.", "그런데 마침 이 무시무시한 신탁이 선포되고, 땅에서 가장 아름다운 처녀들 가운데 괴물에게 바칠 한 사람을 찾으라는 치명적인 명령이 떨어진 바로 그날, 몇몇 선원들이 안젤리카가 있던 해변에 상륙하여, 잠들어 있는 그녀의 그 아름다움을 보았다."]], [["O blind Chance!", "오, 눈먼 우연이여!"], ["whose power in human affairs is but too great, canst thou then abandon to the teeth of a horrible monster those charms which different sovereigns took arms against one another to possess?", "인간사에 미치는 너의 힘은 지나치게 큰 것을, 너는 그러면 서로 다른 군주들이 차지하기 위해 서로 무기를 들었던 그 매력을 끔찍한 괴물의 이빨에 내던질 수 있단 말인가?"], ["Alas!", "아아!"], ["the lovely Angelica is destined to be the victim of those cruel islanders.", "사랑스러운 안젤리카는 저 잔혹한 섬사람들의 제물이 될 운명이었다."], ["Still asleep, she was bound by the Ebudians, and it was not until she was carried on board the vessel that she came to a knowledge of her situation.", "여전히 잠든 채로 그녀는 에부다 사람들에게 결박당했고, 배 위로 옮겨진 뒤에야 자신의 처지를 알게 되었다."], ["The wind filled the sails and wafted the ship swiftly to the port, where all that beheld her agreed that she was unquestionably the victim selected by Proteus himself to be his prey.", "바람이 돛을 채워 배를 항구로 빠르게 실어 갔으며, 그녀를 본 모든 이들은 그녀가 의심할 여지 없이 프로테우스 자신이 먹이로 삼고자 골라낸 제물이라는 데 의견을 같이했다."], ["Who can tell the screams, the mortal anguish of this unhappy maiden, the reproaches she addressed even to the heavens themselves, when the dreadful information of her cruel fate was made known to her?", "자신의 잔혹한 운명에 대한 끔찍한 소식이 전해졌을 때 이 불행한 처녀가 지른 비명을, 죽음에 이르는 고뇌를, 하늘 자체에까지 퍼부은 원망을 누가 다 전할 수 있겠는가?"], ["I cannot; let me rather turn to a happier part of my story.", "나는 할 수 없으니, 차라리 내 이야기의 더 행복한 대목으로 눈을 돌리고자 한다."]], [["Rogero left the palace of Logestilla, careering on his flying courser far above the tops of the mountains, and borne westward by the Hippogriff, which he guided with ease, by means of the bridle that Melissa had given him.", "로게로는 로게스틸라의 궁전을 떠나, 날아다니는 자신의 준마를 타고 산봉우리들보다 훨씬 높이 내달았으며, 멜리사가 준 고삐 덕분에 손쉽게 다룰 수 있는 히포그리프에 실려 서쪽으로 나아갔다."], ["Anxious as he was to recover Bradamante, he could not fail to be delighted at the view his rapid flight presented of so many vast regions and populous countries as he passed over in his career.", "브라다만테를 되찾으려는 마음이 간절했지만, 빠른 비행 가운데 지나치는 수많은 광대한 지역과 인구 많은 나라들의 광경에 기뻐하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["At last he approached the shores of England, and perceived an immense army in all the splendor of military pomp, as if about to go forth flushed with hopes of victory.", "마침내 그는 잉글랜드 해안에 가까이 다가갔고, 마치 승리의 희망에 들떠 출정하려는 듯, 온갖 군사적 위풍을 갖춘 거대한 군대를 발견했다."], ["He caused the Hippogriff to alight not far from the scene, and found himself immediately surrounded by admiring spectators, knights and soldiers, who could not enough indulge their curiosity and wonder.", "그는 그 광경에서 멀지 않은 곳에 히포그리프를 내려앉게 했고, 곧 호기심과 경이로움을 가눌 줄 모르는 구경꾼들, 곧 기사들과 병사들에게 둘러싸이게 되었다."], ["Rogero learned, in reply to his questions, that the fine array of troops before him was the army destined to go to the aid of the French Emperor, in compliance with the request presented by the illustrious Rinaldo, as ambassador of King Charles, his uncle.", "로게로는 자신의 물음에 대한 답으로, 눈앞의 저 훌륭한 군대가 그의 숙부인 샤를 왕의 사절로서 명망 높은 리날도가 올린 요청에 응하여, 프랑스 황제를 돕기 위해 떠나기로 정해진 군대임을 알게 되었다."]], [["By this time the curiosity of the English chevaliers was partly gratified in beholding the Hippogriff at rest, and Rogero, to renew their surprise and delight, remounted the animal, and, slapping spurs to his sides, made him launch into the air with the rapidity of a meteor, and directed his flight still westwardly, till he came within sight of the coasts of Ireland.", "이 무렵 잉글랜드 기사들의 호기심은 가만히 멈춰 있는 히포그리프를 보는 것으로 어느 정도 채워졌고, 로게로는 그들의 놀라움과 기쁨을 새로이 자아내고자 다시 그 짐승에 올라타, 옆구리에 박차를 가하여 유성 같은 속도로 공중에 솟아오르게 한 뒤, 여전히 서쪽을 향해 비행을 이끌어 마침내 아일랜드 해안이 보이는 데에 이르렀다."], ["Here he descried what seemed to be a fair damsel, alone, fast chained to a rock which projected into the sea.", "여기서 그는 바다로 튀어나온 바위에 단단히 사슬로 묶인 채 홀로 있는, 아리따운 처녀로 보이는 이를 멀리서 보았다."], ["What was his astonishment when, drawing nigh, he beheld the beautiful princess Angelica!", "가까이 다가가 아름다운 공주 안젤리카임을 알아보았을 때 그의 놀라움이 어떠했겠는가!"], ["That day she had been led forth and bound to the rock, there to wait till the sea-monster should come to devour her.", "그날 그녀는 끌려 나와 바위에 묶였고, 바다 괴물이 와서 자신을 잡아먹을 때까지 거기서 기다리게 되어 있었다."], ["Rogero exclaimed as he came near, “What cruel hands, what barbarous soul, what fatal chance can have loaded thee with those chains?”", "로게로는 가까이 다가오며 외쳤다. \"어떤 잔인한 손이, 어떤 야만적인 영혼이, 어떤 가혹한 운명이 그대에게 저 사슬을 지웠단 말이오?\""], ["Angelica replied by a torrent of tears, at first her only response; then, in a trembling voice, she disclosed to him the horrible destiny for which she was there exposed.", "안젤리카는 처음에는 눈물의 폭포로만 답했고, 그것이 그녀의 유일한 응답이었다. 그러다가 떨리는 목소리로, 자신이 여기 내맡겨진 그 끔찍한 운명을 그에게 털어놓았다."], ["While she spoke, a terrible roaring was heard far off on the sea.", "그녀가 말하는 동안 멀리 바다에서 무시무시한 포효가 들려왔다."], ["The huge monster soon came in sight, part of his body appearing above the waves and part concealed.", "거대한 괴물이 곧 모습을 드러냈는데, 몸의 일부는 물결 위로 솟아 있고 일부는 물속에 잠겨 있었다."], ["Angelica, half dead with fear, abandoned herself to despair.", "두려움에 반쯤 죽은 안젤리카는 절망에 자신을 내맡겼다."]], [["Rogero, lance in rest, spurred his Hippogriff toward the Orc, and gave him a thrust.", "로게로는 창을 겨눈 채 히포그리프에 박차를 가해 오르크를 향해 돌진하여, 그에게 일격을 가했다."], ["The horrible monster was like nothing that nature produces.", "그 끔찍한 괴물은 자연이 낳은 그 무엇과도 같지 않았다."], ["It was but one mass of tossing and twisting body, with nothing of the animal but head, eyes, and mouth, the last furnished with tusks like those of the wild boar.", "그것은 그저 뒤척이고 비틀거리는 몸뚱이 하나의 덩어리였으며, 짐승다운 것이라고는 머리와 눈과 입뿐이었는데, 그 입에는 멧돼지의 것과 같은 엄니가 돋아 있었다."], ["Rogero’s lance had struck him between the eyes; but rock and iron are not more impenetrable than were his scales.", "로게로의 창이 그 두 눈 사이를 찔렀으나, 그 비늘은 바위나 쇠 못지않게 뚫리지 않았다."], ["The knight, seeing the fruitlessness of the first blow, prepared to give a second.", "기사는 첫 일격이 헛됨을 보고 두 번째 일격을 가하려 채비했다."], ["The animal, beholding upon the water the shadow of the great wings of the Hippogriff, abandoned his prey, and turned to seize what seemed nearer.", "그 짐승은 물 위에 비친 히포그리프의 거대한 날개 그림자를 보고는 먹이를 버리고, 더 가까이 있는 듯 보이는 것을 붙잡으려 몸을 돌렸다."], ["Rogero took the opportunity, and dealt him furious blows on various parts of his body, taking care to keep clear of his murderous teeth; but the scales resisted every attack.", "로게로는 그 틈을 타, 살인적인 이빨에서 멀찍이 떨어지도록 조심하면서 그 몸의 여러 곳에 맹렬한 타격을 퍼부었으나, 비늘은 어떤 공격에도 끄떡없었다."], ["The Orc beat the water with his tail till he raised a foam which enveloped Rogero and his steed, so that the knight hardly knew whether he was in the water or the air.", "오르크는 꼬리로 물을 후려쳐 거품을 일으켰고, 그 거품이 로게로와 그의 준마를 뒤덮어, 기사는 자신이 물속에 있는지 공중에 있는지조차 거의 분간할 수 없었다."], ["He began to fear that the wings of the Hippogriff would be so drenched with water that they would cease to sustain him.", "그는 히포그리프의 날개가 물에 흠뻑 젖어 더는 자신을 떠받치지 못하게 될까 두려워지기 시작했다."], ["At that moment Rogero bethought him of the magic shield which hung at his saddle-bow; but the fear that Angelica would also be blinded by its glare discouraged him from employing it.", "그 순간 로게로는 안장 앞머리에 걸린 마법의 방패를 떠올렸으나, 그 섬광에 안젤리카까지 눈이 멀까 하는 두려움에 그것을 쓰기를 망설였다."], ["Then he remembered the ring which Melissa had given him, the power of which he had so lately proved.", "그러다 그는 멜리사가 준 반지를 떠올렸으니, 그 위력은 얼마 전에 몸소 입증한 바였다."], ["He hastened to Angelica and placed it on her finger.", "그는 서둘러 안젤리카에게 다가가 그것을 그녀의 손가락에 끼워 주었다."], ["Then, uncovering the buckler, he turned its bright disk full in the face of the detestable Orc.", "그런 다음 방패의 덮개를 벗겨, 그 빛나는 원반을 가증스러운 오르크의 얼굴 정면으로 돌렸다."], ["The effect was instantaneous.", "효과는 즉각적이었다."], ["The monster, deprived of sense and motion, rolled over on the sea, and lay floating on his back.", "괴물은 감각과 운동을 빼앗긴 채 바다 위에서 굴러, 등을 보이며 떠 있었다."], ["Rogero would fain have tried the effect of his lance on the now exposed parts, but Angelica implored him to lose no time in delivering her from her chains before the monster should revive.", "로게로는 이제 드러난 부위에 자신의 창을 시험해 보고 싶었으나, 안젤리카는 괴물이 되살아나기 전에 자신을 사슬에서 풀어 주는 데 한시도 지체하지 말 것을 그에게 애원했다."], ["Rogero, moved with her entreaties, hastened to do so, and, having unbound her, made her mount behind him on the Hippogriff.", "그녀의 간청에 마음이 움직인 로게로는 서둘러 그렇게 했고, 그녀를 풀어 준 뒤 히포그리프에서 자신의 뒤에 태웠다."], ["The animal, spurning the earth, shot up into the air, and rapidly sped his way through it.", "그 짐승은 땅을 박차고 공중으로 솟구쳐 그 사이를 빠르게 내달았다."], ["Rogero, to give time to the princess to rest after her cruel agitations, soon sought the earth again, alighting on the shore of Brittany.", "로게로는 공주가 그 잔혹한 동요에서 벗어나 쉴 틈을 주려고, 곧 다시 땅을 찾아 브르타뉴 해안에 내려앉았다."], ["Near the shore a thick wood presented itself, which resounded with the songs of birds.", "해안 가까이에는 새들의 노래가 울려 퍼지는 울창한 숲이 펼쳐져 있었다."], ["In the midst, a fountain of transparent water bathed the turf of a little meadow.", "그 한가운데에는 맑은 물의 샘이 작은 풀밭의 잔디를 적시고 있었다."], ["A gentle hill rose near by.", "가까이에는 완만한 언덕이 솟아 있었다."], ["Rogero, making the Hippogriff alight in the meadow, dismounted, and took Angelica from the horse.", "로게로는 히포그리프를 풀밭에 내려앉게 하고는 말에서 내린 뒤, 안젤리카를 말에서 내려 주었다."]], [["When the first tumults of emotion had subsided Angelica, casting her eyes downward, beheld the precious ring upon her finger, whose virtues she was well acquainted with, for it was the very ring which the Saracen Brunello had robbed her of.", "격정의 첫 동요가 가라앉았을 때 안젤리카는 눈을 아래로 떨구다가, 자신의 손가락에 끼워진 그 귀한 반지를 보았다. 그 효력을 그녀는 잘 알고 있었으니, 그것은 바로 사라센인 브루넬로가 그녀에게서 빼앗아 갔던 그 반지였다."], ["She drew it from her finger and placed it in her mouth, and, quicker than we can tell it, disappeared from the sight of the paladin.", "그녀는 손가락에서 그것을 빼어 입에 넣었고, 우리가 말로 옮기는 것보다도 빠르게 그 무사의 시야에서 사라졌다."]], [["Rogero looked around him on all sides, like one frantic, but soon remembered the ring which he had so lately placed on her finger.", "로게로는 미친 사람처럼 사방을 둘러보았으나, 곧 자신이 얼마 전 그녀의 손가락에 끼워 준 반지를 떠올렸다."], ["Struck with the ingratitude which could thus recompense his services, he exclaimed: “Thankless beauty, is this then the reward you make me?", "자신의 봉사에 이렇게 보답하는 배은망덕함에 충격을 받은 그가 외쳤다. \"은혜를 모르는 미인이여, 그러면 이것이 그대가 내게 하는 보답이오?"], ["Do you prefer to rob me of my ring rather than receive it as a gift?", "내 반지를 선물로 받느니 차라리 내게서 빼앗기를 택한단 말이오?"], ["Willingly would I have given it to you, had you but asked it.”", "그대가 청하기만 했어도 나는 기꺼이 그것을 주었을 것이오.\""], ["Thus he said, searching on all sides with arms extended like a blind man, hoping to recover by the touch what was lost to sight; but he sought in vain.", "이렇게 말하며 그는 마치 눈먼 사람처럼 두 팔을 뻗고 사방을 더듬으며, 시야에서 사라진 것을 손길로나마 되찾기를 바랐으나, 헛되이 찾았을 뿐이다."], ["The cruel beauty was already far away.", "그 잔인한 미인은 이미 멀리 가 있었다."]], [["Though sensible of her obligations to her deliverer, her first necessity was for clothing, food, and repose.", "자신을 구해 준 이에게 진 신세를 잘 알고 있었으나, 그녀에게 가장 먼저 필요한 것은 옷과 음식과 휴식이었다."], ["She soon reached a shepherd’s hut, where, entering unseen, she found what sufficed for her present relief.", "그녀는 곧 양치기의 오두막에 이르렀고, 눈에 띄지 않게 들어가 당장의 어려움을 덜어 줄 만한 것을 찾아냈다."], ["An old herdsman inhabited the hut, whose charges consisted of a drove of mares.", "그 오두막에는 한 늙은 목자가 살고 있었는데, 그가 돌보는 것이라고는 한 무리의 암말 떼였다."], ["When recruited by repose Angelica selected one of the mares from the flock, and, mounting the animal, felt the desire revive in her mind of returning to her home in the East, and for that purpose would gladly have accepted the protection of Orlando or of Sacripant across those wide regions which divided her from her own country.", "휴식으로 기운을 회복한 안젤리카는 그 무리에서 암말 한 마리를 골라 그 짐승에 올라탔고, 동방의 고향으로 돌아가고픈 마음이 다시 일어남을 느꼈다. 그 목적을 위해 그녀는 자신과 고향을 갈라놓는 저 광대한 지역들을 가로지르는 동안 오를란도나 사크리판트의 보호를 기꺼이 받았으리라."], ["In hopes of meeting with one or the other of them she pursued her way.", "그 둘 중 누구라도 만나기를 바라며 그녀는 길을 갔다."]], [["Meanwhile Rogero, despairing of seeing Angelica again, returned to the tree where he had left his winged horse, but had the mortification to find that the animal had broken his bridle and escaped.", "한편 로게로는 안젤리카를 다시 볼 수 없으리라는 절망에 빠진 채, 날개 달린 말을 두고 떠났던 나무로 돌아갔으나, 그 짐승이 고삐를 끊고 달아나 버린 것을 발견하는 분함을 맛보았다."], ["This loss, added to his previous disappointment, overwhelmed him with vexation.", "이 상실은 앞서의 실망에 더해져 그를 짜증에 휩싸이게 했다."], ["Sadly he gathered up his arms, threw his buckler over his shoulders, and, taking the first path that offered, soon found himself within the verge of a dense and widespread forest.", "그는 슬프게도 자신의 무기를 거두어 방패를 어깨에 둘러메고, 눈에 띈 첫 길로 들어서, 이내 빽빽하고 넓게 펼쳐진 숲의 언저리 안에 자신이 있음을 알게 되었다."]], [["He had proceeded for some distance when he heard a noise on his right, and, listening attentively, distinguished the clash of arms.", "얼마쯤 나아갔을 때 그는 오른편에서 소리를 들었고, 주의 깊게 귀 기울여 무기 부딪치는 소리를 분간해 냈다."], ["He made his way toward the place whence the sound proceeded, and found two warriors engaged in mortal combat.", "그는 그 소리가 나는 곳을 향해 길을 잡았고, 죽기를 각오한 결투에 몰두한 두 전사를 발견했다."], ["One of them was a knight of a noble and manly bearing, the other a fierce giant.", "그중 하나는 고귀하고 늠름한 풍채의 기사였고, 다른 하나는 사나운 거인이었다."], ["The knight appeared to exert consummate address in defending herself against the massive club of the giant, evading his strokes, or parrying them with sword or shield.", "기사는 거인의 육중한 곤봉에 맞서 스스로를 지키며 더할 나위 없는 솜씨를 발휘하는 듯했으니, 그 타격을 피하거나 칼이나 방패로 막아 냈다."], ["Rogero stood spectator of the combat, for he did not allow himself to interfere in it, though a secret sentiment inclined him strongly to take part with the knight.", "로게로는 그 결투의 구경꾼으로 서 있었으니, 은밀한 감정이 그를 강하게 기사 편으로 기울게 했음에도 끼어들기를 스스로에게 허락하지 않았던 것이다."], ["At length he saw with grief the massive club fall directly on the head of the knight, who yielded to the blow, and fell prostrate.", "마침내 그는 육중한 곤봉이 기사의 머리 위로 곧장 떨어지는 것을 비통하게 보았고, 기사는 그 일격에 굴복하여 엎어져 쓰러졌다."], ["The giant sprang forward to despatch him, and for that purpose unlaced his helmet, when Rogero, with dismay, recognized the face of Bradamante.", "거인은 그를 끝장내려 앞으로 뛰어들었고, 그 목적으로 투구의 끈을 풀었으니, 그때 로게로는 경악하며 브라다만테의 얼굴을 알아보았다."], ["He cried aloud, “Hold, miscreant!”", "그는 큰 소리로 \"멈춰라, 악당아!\""], ["and sprang forward with drawn sword.", "하고 외치며 칼을 빼 들고 앞으로 뛰어들었다."], ["Whereupon the giant, as if he cared not to enter upon another combat, lifted Bradamante on his shoulders, and ran with her into the forest.", "그러자 거인은 마치 또 다른 결투에 들어설 마음이 없는 듯, 브라다만테를 어깨에 둘러메고 그녀를 안은 채 숲속으로 달려갔다."]], [["Rogero plunged after him, but the long legs of the giant carried him forward so fast that the paladin could hardly keep him in sight.", "로게로는 그를 뒤쫓아 뛰어들었으나, 거인의 긴 다리가 그를 너무도 빠르게 앞으로 실어 가서 그 무사는 그를 시야에 두기조차 힘들었다."], ["At length they issued from the wood, and Rogero perceived before him a rich palace, built of marble, and adorned with sculptures executed by a master hand.", "마침내 그들은 숲에서 빠져나왔고, 로게로는 앞에 대리석으로 지어진 화려한 궁전을 보았으니, 거장의 솜씨로 새겨진 조각들로 장식되어 있었다."], ["Into this edifice, through a golden door, the giant passed, and Rogero followed; but, on looking round, saw nowhere either the giant or Bradamante.", "황금 문을 지나 이 건물 안으로 거인이 들어갔고, 로게로도 뒤따랐다. 그러나 둘러보니 거인도 브라다만테도 어디에서도 보이지 않았다."], ["He ran from room to room, calling aloud on his cowardly foe to turn and meet him; but got no response, nor caught another glimpse of the giant or his prey.", "그는 이 방 저 방을 뛰어다니며, 자신의 비겁한 적에게 돌아서서 자신을 맞으라고 큰 소리로 외쳤으나, 아무 응답도 얻지 못했고 거인이나 그 먹이를 다시는 한 번도 보지 못했다."], ["In his vain pursuit he met, without knowing them, Ferrau, Florismart, King Gradasso, Orlando, and many others, all of whom had been entrapped like himself into this enchanted castle.", "그 헛된 추격 가운데 그는 그들을 알아보지 못한 채 페라우, 플로리스마르트, 그라다소 왕, 오를란도, 그리고 그 밖의 많은 이들을 만났으니, 그들 모두는 자신처럼 이 마법의 성에 함정에 빠진 이들이었다."], ["It was a new stratagem of the magician Atlantes to draw Rogero into his power, and to secure also those who might by any chance endanger his safety.", "그것은 로게로를 자신의 손아귀에 끌어들이고, 어떤 식으로든 자신의 안전을 위협할 만한 자들까지 잡아 두려는 마법사 아틀란테스의 새로운 책략이었다."], ["What Rogero had taken for Bradamante was a mere phantom.", "로게로가 브라다만테로 여긴 것은 한낱 환영에 불과했다."], ["That charming lady was far away, full of anxiety for her Rogero, whose coming she had long expected.", "그 매혹적인 여인은 멀리 떨어진 곳에서, 오래도록 그 도착을 기다려 온 자신의 로게로를 향한 근심으로 가득 차 있었다."]], [["The Emperor had committed to her charge the city and garrison of Marseilles, and she held the post against the infidels with valor and discretion.", "황제는 그녀에게 마르세유의 도시와 수비대를 맡겼고, 그녀는 그 임무를 용맹과 분별로 이교도들에 맞서 지켜 냈다. 어느 날 멜리사가 그녀 앞에 불쑥 나타났다."], ["One day Melissa suddenly presented herself before her.", "그녀의 물음을 앞질러 멜리사가 말했다. \"로게로 때문에 두려워하지 마시오."], ["Anticipating her questions, she said, “Fear not for Rogero; he lives, and is as ever true to you; but he has lost his liberty.", "그는 살아 있고, 여느 때와 다름없이 그대에게 충실하오. 다만 그는 자유를 잃었소."], ["The fell enchanter has again succeeded in making him a prisoner.", "그 잔혹한 마법사가 다시금 그를 포로로 만드는 데 성공했소."], ["If you would deliver him, mount your horse and follow me.”", "그를 구하고자 한다면, 말에 올라 나를 따르시오.\""], ["She told her in what manner Atlantes had deceived Rogero, in deluding his eyes with the phantom of herself in peril.", "그녀는 아틀란테스가 어떻게 로게로를 속였는지, 곧 그의 눈을 위험에 처한 그녀 자신의 환영으로 현혹했는지 그녀에게 일러 주었다."], ["“Such,”", "\"그대의 경우에도,\""], ["she continued, “will be his arts in your own case, if you penetrate the forest and approach that castle.", "멜리사가 이어 말했다. \"그대가 숲을 뚫고 들어가 그 성에 다가가면, 그자의 술법도 그와 같을 것이오."], ["You will think you behold Rogero, when, in fact, you see only the enchanter himself.", "그대는 로게로를 본다고 여기겠으나, 실은 그 마법사 자신을 볼 뿐이오. 속지 말고, 그대의 칼을 그자의 몸에 찔러 넣으시오."], ["Be not deceived, plunge your sword into his body, and trust me when I tell you that, in slaying him, you will restore not only Rogero, but with him many of the bravest knights of France, whom the wizard’s arts have withdrawn from the camp of their sovereign.”", "그리고 내 말을 믿으시오. 그자를 죽임으로써 그대는 로게로뿐 아니라 그와 더불어, 그 마법사의 술법이 자신들의 군주의 진영에서 빼낸 프랑스의 가장 용맹한 기사들 다수까지 되찾게 될 것이오.\""]], [["Bradamante promptly armed herself, and mounted her horse.", "브라다만테는 즉시 무장하고 말에 올랐다."], ["Melissa led her by forced journeys, by field and forest, beguiling the way with conversation on the theme which interested her hearer most.", "멜리사는 들판과 숲을 지나 강행군으로 그녀를 이끌었으며, 자신의 청자가 가장 관심을 두는 주제에 관한 이야기로 길을 달랬다."], ["When at last they reached the forest, she repeated once more her instructions, and then took her leave, for fear the enchanter might espy her, and be put on his guard.", "마침내 그들이 숲에 이르자, 멜리사는 다시 한 번 자신의 지침을 되풀이한 뒤, 마법사가 자신을 알아채고 경계하게 될까 두려워 작별을 고했다."]], [["Bradamante rode on about two miles when suddenly she beheld Rogero, as it appeared to her, hard pressed by two fierce giants.", "브라다만테가 두 마일쯤 말을 달렸을 때, 갑자기 그녀의 눈에 비친 대로 로게로가 두 사나운 거인에게 호되게 몰리고 있는 것이 보였다."], ["While she hesitated she heard his voice calling on her for help.", "그녀가 머뭇거리는 사이 그가 자신에게 도움을 청하는 목소리가 들려왔다."], ["At once the cautions of Melissa lost their weight.", "그 순간 멜리사의 경고는 무게를 잃었다."], ["A sudden doubt of the faith and truth of her kind monitress flashed across her mind.", "자신의 친절한 조언자의 신의와 진실함에 대한 갑작스러운 의심이 그녀의 마음을 스쳤다."], ["“Shall I not believe my own eyes and ears?”", "\"내 눈과 귀를 믿어서는 안 된단 말인가?\""], ["she said, and rushed forward to his defence.", "그녀는 이렇게 말하며 그를 지키려 앞으로 돌진했다."], ["Rogero fled, pursued by the giants, and Bradamante followed, passing with them through the castle gate.", "로게로는 거인들에게 쫓기며 달아났고, 브라다만테가 뒤따라, 그들과 함께 성문을 지났다."], ["When there, Bradamante was undeceived, for neither giant nor knight was to be seen.", "그곳에 이르자 브라다만테는 미혹에서 풀려났으니, 거인도 기사도 보이지 않았던 것이다."], ["She found herself a prisoner, but had not the consolation of knowing that she shared the imprisonment of her beloved.", "그녀는 자신이 포로가 되었음을 깨달았으나, 자신이 사랑하는 이의 갇힘을 함께 나눈다는 위안조차 알지 못했다."], ["She saw various forms of men and women, but could recognize none of them; and their lot was the same with respect to her.", "그녀는 여러 모습의 남자들과 여자들을 보았으나, 그들 가운데 누구도 알아볼 수 없었고, 그녀에 관해서도 그들의 처지는 마찬가지였다."], ["Each viewed the others under some illusion of the fancy, wearing the semblance of giants, dwarfs, or even four-footed animals, so that there was no companionship or communication between them. ————", "저마다 다른 이들을 어떤 환상의 미혹 아래 보아, 거인이나 난쟁이, 심지어 네발짐승의 모습을 띠고 있었으니, 그들 사이에는 어떤 교제도 소통도 없었다."]], [["# ASTOLPHO’S ADVENTURES CONTINUED, AND ISABELLA’S BEGUN", "# ASTOLPHO’S ADVENTURES CONTINUED, AND ISABELLA’S BEGUN"]], [["When Astolpho escaped from the cruel Alcina, after a short abode in the realm of the virtuous Logestilla, he desired to return to his native country.", "이어지는 아스톨포의 모험, 그리고 시작되는 이사벨라의 모험\n아스톨포가 잔혹한 알키나에게서 벗어나, 덕망 높은 로게스틸라의 왕국에 잠시 머문 뒤, 그는 고국으로 돌아가기를 바랐다."], ["Logestilla lent him the best vessel of her fleet to convey him to the mainland.", "로게스틸라는 그를 본토로 실어다 줄 자신의 함대 가운데 가장 좋은 배를 빌려주었다."], ["She gave him at parting a wonderful book, which taught the secret of overcoming all manners of enchantments, and begged him to carry it always with him, out of regard for her.", "헤어지면서 그녀는 그에게 온갖 종류의 마법을 이겨 내는 비밀을 가르쳐 주는 놀라운 책 한 권을 주었고, 자신을 생각해서 그것을 늘 지니고 다니기를 청했다."], ["She also gave him another gift, which surpassed everything of the kind that mortal workmanship can frame; yet it was nothing in appearance but a simple horn.", "그녀는 또 다른 선물도 주었으니, 그것은 인간의 솜씨로 빚을 수 있는 그런 종류의 모든 것을 능가하는 것이었다. 그러나 겉보기에는 한낱 단순한 뿔나팔에 지나지 않았다."]], [["Astolpho, protected by these gifts, thanked the good fairy, took leave of her, and set out on his return to France.", "이 선물들로 보호받게 된 아스톨포는 선량한 요정에게 감사하고 그녀에게 작별을 고한 뒤, 프랑스로 돌아가는 길에 올랐다."], ["His voyage was prosperous, and on reaching the desired port he took leave of the faithful mariners, and continued his journey by land.", "그의 항해는 순조로웠고, 바라던 항구에 이르자 그는 충실한 뱃사람들에게 작별을 고하고 육로로 여정을 이어 갔다."], ["As he proceeded over mountains and through valleys he often met with bands of robbers, wild beasts, and venomous serpents, but he had only to sound his horn to put them all to flight.", "산을 넘고 골짜기를 지나는 동안 그는 도적 떼와 사나운 짐승들, 독을 품은 뱀들을 자주 만났으나, 그저 자신의 뿔나팔을 불기만 하면 그들 모두를 달아나게 할 수 있었다."]], [["Having landed in France, and traversed many provinces on his way to the army, he one day, in crossing a forest, arrived beside a fountain, and alighted to drink.", "프랑스에 상륙하여 군대로 가는 길에 여러 지방을 가로지른 그는, 어느 날 숲을 건너다가 샘 곁에 이르러 물을 마시려 말에서 내렸다."], ["While he stooped at the fountain a young rustic sprang from the copse, mounted Rabican, and rode away.", "그가 샘에 몸을 굽히고 있는 동안, 한 젊은 시골뜨기가 덤불에서 튀어나와 라비칸에 올라타 달아났다."], ["It was a new trick of the enchanter Atlantes.", "그것은 마법사 아틀란테스의 새로운 술책이었다."], ["Astolpho, hearing the noise, turned his head just in time to see his loss; and, starting up, pursued the thief, who, on his part, did not press the horse to his full speed, but just kept in sight of his pursuer till they both issued from the forest; and then Rabican and his rider took shelter in a castle which stood near.", "그 소리를 들은 아스톨포는 자신의 말을 잃는 것을 보기에 꼭 알맞게 고개를 돌렸고, 벌떡 일어나 그 도둑을 뒤쫓았다. 도둑은 자기 쪽에서는 말을 전속력으로 몰지 않고, 둘 다 숲을 빠져나올 때까지 그저 추격자의 시야에 머물러 있을 만큼만 달렸다."], ["Astolpho followed, and penetrated without difficulty within the courtyard of the castle, where he looked around for the rider and his horse, but could see no trace of either, nor any person of whom he could make inquiry.", "그런 다음 라비칸과 그 기수는 가까이 서 있던 어느 성으로 몸을 피했다. 아스톨포가 뒤따라, 어려움 없이 성의 안뜰 안으로 들어가 기수와 그 말을 찾으려 둘러보았으나, 어느 쪽의 흔적도 볼 수 없었고, 물어볼 만한 사람도 보이지 않았다."], ["Suspecting that enchantment was employed to embarrass him, he bethought him of his book, and on consulting it discovered that his suspicions were well founded.", "자신을 곤경에 빠뜨리려 마법이 쓰이고 있음을 의심한 그는 자신의 책을 떠올렸고, 그것을 살펴보고는 자신의 의심이 근거 있는 것임을 알아냈다."], ["He also learned what course to pursue.", "그는 또한 어떤 길을 따라야 할지도 알게 되었다."], ["He was directed to raise the stone which served as a threshold, under which a spirit lay pent, who would willingly escape, and leave the castle free of access.", "그는 문지방 노릇을 하는 돌을 들어 올리라는 지시를 받았으니, 그 아래에는 한 정령이 갇혀 있어 기꺼이 빠져나가 성을 누구나 드나들 수 있게 비워 둘 터였다."], ["Astolpho applied his strength to lift aside the stone.", "아스톨포는 그 돌을 옆으로 들어내는 데 자신의 힘을 쏟았다."], ["Thereupon the magician put his arts in force.", "그러자 마법사가 자신의 술법을 발휘했다."], ["The castle was full of prisoners, and the magician caused that to all of them Astolpho should appear in some false guise—to some a wild beast, to others a giant, to others a bird of prey.", "성에는 포로들이 가득했는데, 마법사는 그들 모두에게 아스톨포가 어떤 거짓된 모습으로 보이도록 했으니, 어떤 이에게는 사나운 짐승으로, 어떤 이에게는 거인으로, 어떤 이에게는 맹금으로 보이게 했다."], ["Thus all assailed him, and would quickly have made an end of him, if he had not bethought him of his horn.", "그리하여 모두가 그를 덮쳤고, 만약 그가 자신의 뿔나팔을 떠올리지 않았더라면 이내 그를 끝장내고 말았을 것이다."], ["No sooner had he blown a blast than, at the horrid larum, fled the cavaliers and the necromancer with them, like a flock of pigeons at the sound of the fowler’s gun.", "그가 한 차례 나팔을 불자마자, 그 끔찍한 경보 소리에 기사들과 그들과 더불어 마법사가, 마치 새 사냥꾼의 총소리에 비둘기 떼가 흩어지듯 달아났다."], ["Astolpho then renewed his efforts on the stone, and turned it over.", "그러자 아스톨포는 다시 돌에 힘을 쏟아 그것을 뒤집었다."], ["The under face was all inscribed with magical characters, which the knight defaced, as directed by his book; and no sooner had he done so, than the castle, with its walls and turrets, vanished into smoke.", "그 아랫면은 온통 마법의 문자로 새겨져 있었는데, 기사는 자신의 책이 일러 준 대로 그것을 지워 버렸다. 그가 그렇게 하자마자, 그 성은 성벽과 작은 탑들과 더불어 연기 속으로 사라졌다."]], [["The knights and ladies set at liberty were, besides Rogero and Bradamante, Orlando, Gradasso, Florismart, and many more.", "풀려난 기사들과 귀부인들 가운데에는 로게로와 브라다만테 외에도 오를란도, 그라다소, 플로리스마르트, 그리고 그 밖의 많은 이들이 있었다."], ["At the sound of the horn they fled, one and all, men and steeds, except Rabican, which Astolpho secured, in spite of his terror.", "뿔나팔 소리에 라비칸을 빼고는, 사람이며 준마며 너 나 할 것 없이 모두가 달아났으나, 아스톨포는 자신의 공포에도 불구하고 라비칸을 붙들었다."], ["As soon as the sound had ceased Rogero recognized Bradamante, whom he had daily met during their imprisonment, but had been prevented from knowing by the enchanter’s arts.", "소리가 그치자마자 로게로는 브라다만테를 알아보았으니, 그는 갇혀 있는 동안 날마다 그녀를 마주쳤으나 마법사의 술법으로 알아보지 못했던 것이다."], ["No words can tell the delight with which they recognized each other, and recounted mutually all that had happened to each since they were parted.", "그들이 서로를 알아보고, 헤어진 이후 각자에게 일어난 모든 일을 서로 이야기하던 그 기쁨은 어떤 말로도 다 전할 수 없다."], ["Rogero took advantage of the opportunity to press his suit, and found Bradamante as propitious as he could wish, were it not for a single obstacle, the difference of their faiths.", "로게로는 그 기회를 틈타 자신의 구애를 밀어붙였고, 단 하나의 장애, 곧 그들의 신앙의 차이만 아니었다면, 브라다만테가 바랄 수 있는 만큼이나 호의적임을 알게 되었다."], ["“If he would obtain her in marriage,”", "\"만약 그가 자신을 아내로 얻고자 한다면,\""], ["she said, “he must in due form demand her of her father, Duke Aymon, and must abandon his false prophet, and become a Christian.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"그는 정식으로 자신의 아버지 에이몬 공작에게 자신을 청해야 하며, 자신의 거짓 예언자를 버리고 그리스도교도가 되어야 한다.\""], ["The latter step was one which Rogero had for some time intended taking, for reasons of his own.", "후자의 조처는 로게로가 자신만의 이유로 얼마 전부터 취하려고 작정해 온 것이었다."], ["He therefore gladly accepted the terms, and proposed that they should at once repair to the abbey of Vallombrosa, whose towers were visible at no great distance.", "그리하여 그는 그 조건을 기꺼이 받아들였고, 멀지 않은 곳에 탑들이 보이는 발롬브로사 수도원으로 곧장 가자고 제안했다."], ["Thither they turned their horses’ heads, and we will leave them to find their way without our company.", "그들은 그쪽으로 말머리를 돌렸고, 우리는 그들이 우리의 동행 없이 길을 찾아가도록 두고 떠나기로 한다."]], [["I know not if my readers recollect that at the moment when Rogero had just delivered Angelica from the voracious Orc that scornful beauty placed her ring in her mouth, and vanished out of sight.", "로게로가 게걸스러운 오르크로부터 안젤리카를 막 구해 낸 순간, 그 도도한 미인이 자신의 반지를 입에 넣고 시야에서 사라졌던 것을 내 독자들이 기억하는지 나는 모르겠다."], ["At the same time the Hippogriff shook off his bridle, soared away, and flew to rejoin his former master, very naturally returning to his accustomed stable.", "같은 때에 히포그리프는 고삐를 떨쳐 버리고 솟구쳐 올라 자신의 옛 주인에게 돌아가려 날아갔으니, 아주 자연스럽게도 늘 익숙하던 마구간으로 돌아간 것이었다."], ["Here Astolpho found him, to his very great delight.", "여기서 아스톨포는 그를 발견하고 크게 기뻐했다."], ["He knew the animal’s powers, having seen Rogero ride him, and he longed to fly abroad over all the earth, and see various nations and peoples from his airy course.", "그는 로게로가 그 짐승을 타는 것을 본 적이 있어 그 능력을 알고 있었고, 온 땅을 두루 날아다니며 공중의 항로에서 갖가지 나라와 백성들을 보고 싶어 했다."], ["He had heard Logestilla’s directions how to guide the animal, and saw her fit a bridle to his head.", "그는 로게스틸라가 그 짐승을 어떻게 다루어야 하는지 일러 주는 것을 들었고, 그녀가 그 머리에 고삐를 맞추는 것을 보았다."], ["He therefore was able, out of all the bridles he found in the stable, to select one suitable, and, placing Rabican’s saddle on the Hippogriff’s back, nothing seemed to prevent his immediate departure.", "그리하여 그는 마구간에서 찾아낸 모든 고삐 가운데 알맞은 것을 골라낼 수 있었고, 라비칸의 안장을 히포그리프의 등에 얹으니, 그가 즉시 떠나는 것을 막을 만한 것은 아무것도 없어 보였다."], ["Yet before he went he bethought him of placing Rabican in hands where he would be safe, and whence he might recover him in time of need.", "그러나 떠나기에 앞서 그는 라비칸을 안전한 곳에, 그리고 필요할 때 되찾을 수 있는 곳에 맡겨 둘 생각을 했다."], ["While he stood deliberating where he should find a messenger, he saw Bradamante approach.", "어디서 전령을 찾아야 할지 궁리하며 서 있는 동안, 그는 브라다만테가 다가오는 것을 보았다."], ["That fair warrior had been parted from Rogero on their way to the abbey of Vallombrosa, by an inopportune adventure which had called the knight away.", "그 아리따운 여전사는 발롬브로사 수도원으로 가는 길에, 기사를 불러낸 때아닌 모험으로 인해 로게로와 헤어진 터였다."], ["She was now returning to Montalban, having arranged with Rogero to join her there.", "그녀는 이제 몬탈반으로 돌아가는 길이었으니, 거기서 로게로와 만나기로 약속해 둔 참이었다."], ["To Bradamante, therefore, his fair cousin, Astolpho committed Rabican, and also the lance of gold, which would only be an incumbrance in his aerial excursion.", "그리하여 아스톨포는 자신의 아리따운 사촌 브라다만테에게 라비칸을, 그리고 그의 공중 여행에서는 짐만 될 뿐인 황금 창도 맡겼다."], ["Bradamante took charge of both; and Astolpho, bidding her farewell, soared in air.", "브라다만테는 둘 다 떠맡았고, 아스톨포는 그녀에게 작별을 고하고 공중으로 솟아올랐다."]], [["Among those delivered by Astolpho from the magician’s castle was Orlando.", "아스톨포가 마법사의 성에서 풀어 준 이들 가운데에는 오를란도가 있었다."], ["Following the guide of chance, the paladin found himself at the close of day in a forest, and stopped at the foot of a mountain.", "우연의 인도를 따라 그 무사는 하루가 저물 무렵 어느 숲속에 있는 자신을 발견했고, 한 산기슭에 멈춰 섰다."], ["Surprised to discern a light which came from a cleft in the rock, he approached, guided by the ray, and discovered a narrow passage in the mountain-side, which led into a deep grotto.", "바위틈에서 새어 나오는 빛을 알아보고 놀란 그는 그 빛줄기에 이끌려 다가갔고, 깊은 동굴로 이어지는 산허리의 좁은 통로를 발견했다."]], [["Orlando fastened his horse, and then, putting aside the bushes that resisted his passage, stepped down from rock to rock till he reached a sort of cavern.", "오를란도는 자신의 말을 매어 두고, 통로를 막아선 덤불을 헤치며 바위에서 바위로 내려가 일종의 동굴에 이르렀다."], ["Entering it, he perceived a lady, young and handsome, as well as he could discover through the signs of distress which agitated her countenance.", "그 안으로 들어가서 그는 한 여인을 보았는데, 젊고 아리따웠으니, 그녀의 얼굴을 어지럽히는 비탄의 기색 너머로 그가 알아볼 수 있는 한에서 그러했다."], ["Her only companion was an old woman, who seemed to be regarded by her young partner with terror and indignation.", "그녀의 유일한 동반자는 한 노파였는데, 젊은 동반자는 그 노파를 두려움과 분노로 바라보는 듯했다."], ["The courteous paladin saluted the women respectfully, and begged to know by whose barbarity they had been subjected to such imprisonment.", "정중한 무사는 그 여인들에게 공손히 인사하고, 어떤 자의 야만으로 인해 그들이 이런 갇힘을 당하게 되었는지 알기를 청했다."]], [["The younger lady replied, in a voice often broken with sobs:", "젊은 여인이 흐느낌으로 자주 끊기는 목소리로 답했다."]], [["“Though I know well that my recital will subject me to worse treatment by the barbarous man who keeps me here, to whom this woman will not fail to report it, yet I will not hide from you the facts.", "\"제 이야기가 저를 여기 가두어 두고 있는 저 야만적인 자에게서 더 가혹한 대우를 받게 하리라는 것을, 그리고 이 여인이 그것을 그자에게 어김없이 일러바치리라는 것을 잘 알고 있지만, 그래도 당신께 사실을 숨기지는 않겠습니다."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["why should I fear his rage?", "어찌 제가 그자의 분노를 두려워하겠습니까?"], ["If he should take my life, I know not what better boon than death I can ask.", "그자가 제 목숨을 거둔다 한들, 저는 죽음보다 더 나은 무엇을 청할 수 있을지 모르겠습니다."]], [["“My name is Isabella.", "제 이름은 이사벨라입니다."], ["I am the daughter of the king of Galicia, or rather I should say misfortune and grief are my parents.", "저는 갈리시아 왕의 딸이지요, 아니 차라리 불운과 슬픔이 제 부모라고 말해야 할 것입니다."], ["Young, rich, modest, and of tranquil temper, all things appeared to combine to render my lot happy.", "젊고, 부유하고, 정숙하며, 차분한 성품을 지녀, 모든 것이 제 운명을 행복하게 하려 어우러진 듯했습니다."], ["Alas!", "아아!"], ["I see myself to-day poor, humbled, miserable, and destined perhaps to yet further afflictions.", "그런데 저는 오늘 가난하고, 비천해지고, 비참하며, 어쩌면 더한 고난을 겪을 운명에 처한 제 자신을 봅니다."], ["It is a year since, my father having given notice that he would open the lists for a tournament at Bayonne, a great number of chevaliers from all quarters came together at our court.", "제 아버지께서 바욘에서 마상 시합을 위해 명단을 열겠다고 알리신 지 한 해가 되었을 때, 사방에서 수많은 기사들이 우리 궁정에 모여들었습니다."], ["Among these Zerbino, son of the king of Scotland, victorious in all combats, eclipsed by his beauty and his valor all the rest.", "그들 가운데 스코틀랜드 왕의 아들 제르비노가 모든 결투에서 승리하여, 그 아름다움과 용맹으로 나머지 모두를 무색하게 했습니다."], ["Before departing from the court of Galicia he testified the wish to espouse me, and I consented that he should demand my hand of the king, my father.", "갈리시아의 궁정을 떠나기 전에 그는 저와 혼인하고 싶다는 뜻을 밝혔고, 저는 그가 제 아버지인 왕께 제 손을 청하는 것에 동의했습니다."], ["But I was a Mahometan, and Zerbino a Christian, and my father refused his consent.", "그러나 저는 마호메트교도였고 제르비노는 그리스도교도였으니, 제 아버지께서는 승낙을 거절하셨습니다."], ["The prince, called home by his father to take command of the forces destined to the assistance of the French Emperor, prevailed on me to be married to him secretly, and to follow him to Scotland.", "그 왕자는 프랑스 황제를 돕기 위해 정해진 군대의 지휘를 맡으라는 아버지의 부름을 받고 본국으로 불려 갔는데, 저를 설득하여 그와 비밀리에 혼인하고 그를 따라 스코틀랜드로 가게 했습니다."], ["He caused a galley to be prepared to receive me, and placed in command of it the chevalier Oderic, a Biscayan, famous for his exploits both by land and sea.", "그는 저를 맞이할 갤리선 한 척을 마련하게 하고, 육지와 바다 양쪽에서의 무훈으로 이름난 비스케이 사람 기사 오데릭을 그 지휘관으로 앉혔습니다."], ["On the day appointed, Oderic brought his vessel to a seaside resort of my father’s, where I embarked.", "정해진 날에 오데릭은 자신의 배를 제 아버지의 바닷가 별장으로 가져왔고, 저는 거기서 승선했습니다."], ["Some of my domestics accompanied me, and thus I departed from my native land.", "제 시종들 몇이 저를 따랐고, 그렇게 저는 제 고국을 떠났습니다."]], [["“Sailing with a fair wind, after some hours we were assailed by a violent tempest.", "순풍을 받으며 항해하다가, 몇 시간 뒤에 우리는 사나운 폭풍에 휩싸였습니다."], ["It was to no purpose that we took in all sail; we were driven before the wind directly upon the rocky shore.", "우리가 돛을 모두 거두어들인 것도 소용없었으니, 우리는 바람에 떠밀려 곧장 바위투성이 해안으로 향했습니다."], ["Seeing no other hopes of safety, Oderic placed me in a boat, followed himself with a few of his men, and made for land.", "다른 안전의 희망이 보이지 않자 오데릭은 저를 작은 배에 태우고, 자신도 부하 몇과 함께 따라 타서 뭍으로 향했습니다."], ["We reached it through infinite peril, and I no sooner felt the firm land beneath my feet, than I knelt down and poured out heartfelt thanks to the Providence that had preserved me.", "우리는 끝없는 위험을 헤치고 그곳에 다다랐고, 저는 단단한 땅을 발 밑에 느끼자마자 무릎을 꿇고 저를 지켜 주신 섭리에 진심 어린 감사를 쏟아 냈습니다."]], [["“The shore where we landed appeared to be uninhabited.", "우리가 상륙한 해안은 사람이 살지 않는 듯했습니다."], ["We saw no dwelling to shelter us, no road to lead us to a more hospitable spot.", "우리를 비호해 줄 거처도, 더 후한 곳으로 우리를 이끌어 줄 길도 보이지 않았습니다."], ["A high mountain rose before us, whose base stretched into the sea.", "높은 산 하나가 우리 앞에 솟아 있었는데, 그 기슭은 바다로 뻗어 있었습니다."], ["It was here the infamous Oderic, in spite of my tears and entreaties, sold me to a band of pirates, who fancied I might be an acceptable present to their prince, the Sultan of Morocco.", "바로 여기서 그 파렴치한 오데릭은 제 눈물과 간청에도 불구하고 저를 해적 떼에게 팔아넘겼으니, 그들은 제가 자신들의 군주인 모로코의 술탄에게 받아들여질 만한 선물이 될 수도 있겠다고 여겼던 것입니다."], ["This cavern is their den, and here they keep me under the guard of this woman, until it shall suit their convenience to carry me away.”", "이 동굴이 그들의 소굴이며, 여기서 그들은 자신들의 편의에 맞게 저를 데려갈 때까지 이 여인의 감시 아래 저를 가두어 두고 있습니다.\""]], [["Isabella had hardly finished her recital when a troop of armed men began to enter the cavern.", "이사벨라가 자신의 이야기를 채 끝내기도 전에 한 무리의 무장한 자들이 동굴로 들어오기 시작했다."], ["Seeing the prince Orlando, one said to the rest, “What bird is this we have caught, without even setting a snare for him?”", "오를란도 왕자를 본 한 사람이 나머지에게 말했다. \"이게 무슨 새인데, 우리가 덫조차 놓지 않고 잡았단 말이냐?\""], ["Then addressing Orlando, “It was truly civil in you, friend, to come hither with that handsome coat of armor and vest, the very things I want.”", "그러더니 오를란도에게 말을 걸었다. \"여보게, 자네가 그 멋진 갑옷과 윗옷을 입고 여기까지 와 주다니 참으로 친절하군그래, 바로 내가 원하던 것들이로세.\""], ["“You shall pay for them, then,”", "\"그렇다면 그 값을 치르게 될 것이다.\""], ["said Orlando; and seizing a half-burnt brand from the fire, he hurled it at him, striking his head, and stretching him lifeless on the floor.", "오를란도가 말했다. 그리고 불 속에서 반쯤 타다 만 장작 하나를 움켜쥐어 그에게 던지니, 그것이 그자의 머리를 쳐서 바닥에 송장으로 뻗게 했다."]], [["There was a massy table in the middle of the cavern, used for the pirates’ repasts.", "동굴 한가운데에는 해적들의 식사에 쓰이는 육중한 탁자가 있었다."], ["Orlando lifted it and hurled it at the robbers as they stood clustered in a group toward the entrance.", "오를란도는 그것을 들어 올려, 입구 쪽에 무리 지어 모여 선 도적들을 향해 내던졌다."], ["Half the gang were laid prostrate, with broken heads and limbs; the rest got away as nimbly as they could.", "패거리의 절반이 머리와 사지가 부서진 채 엎어졌고, 나머지는 할 수 있는 한 날쌔게 달아났다."]], [["Leaving the den and its inmates to their fate, Orlando, taking Isabella under his protection, pursued his way for some days, without meeting with any adventure.", "소굴과 그 안에 든 자들을 각자의 운명에 맡겨 둔 채, 오를란도는 이사벨라를 자신의 보호 아래 데리고 며칠 동안 아무런 모험도 만나지 않고 길을 갔다."]], [["One day they saw a band of men advancing, who seemed to be guarding a prisoner, bound hand and foot, as if being carried to execution.", "어느 날 그들은 한 무리의 사람들이 다가오는 것을 보았는데, 그들은 손발이 묶인 한 포로를 마치 처형장으로 끌고 가듯 지키고 있는 듯했다."], ["The prisoner was a youthful cavalier, of a noble and ingenuous appearance.", "그 포로는 고귀하고 순진해 보이는 젊은 기사였다."], ["The band bore the ensigns of Count Anselm, head of the treacherous house of Maganza.", "그 무리는 마간차의 배신자 가문의 우두머리인 안셀모 백작의 휘장을 달고 있었다."], ["Orlando desired Isabella to wait, while he rode forward to inquire the meaning of this array.", "오를란도는 이사벨라에게 기다리라 이르고는, 이 행렬의 뜻을 물으러 앞으로 말을 달려 나갔다."], ["Approaching, he demanded of the leader who his prisoner was, and of what crime he had been guilty.", "다가가서 그는 우두머리에게 그 포로가 누구이며 어떤 죄를 지었는지 물었다."], ["The man replied that the prisoner was a murderer, by whose hand Pinabel, the son of Count Anselm, had been treacherously slain.", "그 사내는 그 포로가 살인자로, 그자의 손에 안셀모 백작의 아들 피나벨이 비열하게 살해되었다고 답했다."], ["At these words the prisoner exclaimed, “I am no murderer, nor have I been in any way the cause of the young man’s death.”", "이 말에 포로가 외쳤다. \"나는 살인자가 아니며, 어떤 식으로도 그 젊은이의 죽음의 원인이 된 적이 없소.\""], ["Orlando, knowing the cruel and ferocious character of the chiefs of the house of Maganza, needed no more to satisfy him that the youth was the victim of injustice.", "마간차 가문 우두머리들의 잔혹하고 흉포한 성정을 알고 있던 오를란도는, 그 젊은이가 불의의 희생자임을 확신하는 데 더 이상의 것이 필요치 않았다."], ["He commanded the leader of the troop to release his victim, and, receiving an insolent reply, dashed him to the earth with a stroke of his lance; then by a few vigorous blows dispersed the band, leaving deadly marks on those who were slowest to quit the field.", "그는 그 무리의 우두머리에게 자신의 희생자를 풀어 주라 명했고, 무례한 답을 듣자 창의 일격으로 그자를 땅에 내동댕이쳤다. 그런 다음 몇 차례 힘찬 타격으로 그 무리를 흩어 놓았으니, 가장 늦게 그 자리를 뜨는 자들에게 치명적인 흔적을 남겼다."]], [["Orlando then hastened to unbind the prisoner, and to assist him to reclothe himself in his armor, which the false Magencian had dared to assume.", "그러고 나서 오를란도는 서둘러 그 포로를 풀어 주었고, 그 거짓 마간차인이 감히 차려입었던 그의 갑옷을 다시 입도록 도왔다."], ["He then led him to Isabella, who now approached the scene of action.", "그런 다음 그를 이사벨라에게로 데려갔으니, 그녀는 이제 그 현장으로 다가오는 참이었다."], ["How can we picture the joy, the astonishment, with which Isabella recognized in him Zerbino, her husband, and the prince discovered her whom he had believed overwhelmed in the waves!", "이사벨라가 그에게서 자신의 남편 제르비노를 알아보고, 또 왕자가 물결에 휩쓸려 사라진 줄로 믿었던 그녀를 알아보았을 때의 그 기쁨과 놀라움을 우리가 어찌 그려 낼 수 있겠는가!"], ["They embraced one another, and wept for joy.", "그들은 서로 끌어안고 기쁨에 겨워 울었다."], ["Orlando, sharing in their happiness, congratulated himself in having been the instrument of it.", "오를란도도 그들의 행복을 함께 나누며, 자신이 그 도구가 되었음을 스스로 기뻐했다."], ["The princess recounted to Zerbino what the illustrious paladin had done for her, and the prince threw himself at Orlando’s feet, and thanked him as having twice preserved his life.", "공주는 제르비노에게 그 명망 높은 무사가 자신을 위해 한 일을 들려주었고, 왕자는 오를란도의 발치에 몸을 던지며, 자신의 목숨을 두 번이나 지켜 준 이로서 그에게 감사했다."]], [["While these exchanges of congratulation and thankfulness were going on, a sound in the underwood attracted their attention, and caused the two knights to brace their helmets and stand on their guard.", "이런 축하와 감사의 인사가 오가는 동안, 덤불 속의 소리 하나가 그들의 주의를 끌어, 두 기사로 하여금 투구를 조여 매고 경계 태세를 갖추게 했다."], ["What the cause of the interruption was we shall record in another chapter.", "이 방해의 원인이 무엇이었는지는 다른 장에서 적기로 한다."]], [["————", "메도로"]], [["France was at this time the theatre of dreadful events.", "이 무렵 프랑스는 끔찍한 사건들의 무대였다."], ["The Saracens and the Christians, in numerous encounters, slew one another.", "사라센인들과 그리스도교도들은 수많은 충돌 속에서 서로를 죽였다."], ["On one occasion Rinaldo led an attack on the infidel columns, broke and scattered them, till he found himself opposite to a knight whose armor (whether by accident or by choice, it matters not) bore the blazon of Orlando.", "한번은 리날도가 이교도 대열을 공격하여 그들을 깨뜨려 흩어 놓다가, 마침내 그 갑옷이 (우연이었든 선택이었든, 그것은 중요치 않다) 오를란도의 문장을 달고 있는 한 기사와 맞서게 되었다."], ["It was Dardinel, the young and brave prince of Zumara, and Rinaldo remarked him by the slaughter he spread all around.", "그는 주마라의 젊고 용맹한 왕자 다르디넬이었고, 리날도는 그가 사방에 퍼뜨리는 살육으로 그를 알아보았다."], ["“Ah,”", "\"아하,\""], ["said he to himself, “let us pluck up this dangerous plant before it has grown to its full height.”", "그가 속으로 말했다. \"이 위험한 풀이 다 자라기 전에 뽑아 버리자.\""]], [["As Rinaldo advanced, the crowd opened before him, the Christians to let his sword have free course, the Pagans to escape its sweep.", "리날도가 나아가자, 무리가 그 앞에서 갈라졌으니, 그리스도교도들은 그의 칼이 거침없이 길을 내도록, 이교도들은 그 휘두름을 피하려 함이었다."], ["Dardinel and he stood face to face.", "다르디넬과 그는 얼굴을 맞대고 섰다. 리날도가 사납게 외쳤다."], ["Rinaldo exclaimed, fiercely, “Young man, whoever gave you that noble buckler to bear made you a dangerous gift; I should like to see how you are able to defend those quarterings, red and white.", "\"젊은이여, 그 고귀한 방패를 네게 들려 준 자가 누구든, 너에게 위험한 선물을 준 것이다. 나는 네가 그 붉고 흰 사분 문장을 어떻게 지켜 내는지 보고 싶구나."], ["If you cannot defend them against me, how pray will you do so when Orlando challenges them?”", "네가 그것을 나에게서 지켜 내지 못한다면, 오를란도가 그것을 요구할 때는 도대체 어떻게 하겠느냐?\""], ["Dardinel replied: “Thou shalt learn that I can defend the arms I bear, and shed new glory upon them.", "다르디넬이 답했다. \"내가 지니고 있는 이 무구를 내가 지켜 낼 수 있으며, 그 위에 새로운 영광을 더할 수 있다는 것을 너는 알게 될 것이다."], ["No one shall rend them from me but with life.”", "목숨이 붙어 있는 한 누구도 이것을 내게서 빼앗지 못한다.\""], ["Saying these words, Dardinel rushed upon Rinaldo with sword uplifted.", "이 말을 하며 다르디넬은 칼을 치켜들고 리날도에게 돌진했다."]], [["The chill of mortal terror filled the souls of the Saracens when they beheld Rinaldo advance to attack the prince, like a lion against a young bull.", "리날도가 마치 사자가 어린 황소를 향하듯 왕자를 공격하러 나아가는 것을 보았을 때, 죽음에 이르는 공포의 한기가 사라센인들의 영혼을 채웠다."], ["The first blow came from the hand of Dardinel, and the weapon rebounded from Mambrino’s helmet without effect.", "첫 일격은 다르디넬의 손에서 나왔으나, 그 무기는 맘브리노의 투구에서 아무 소용 없이 튕겨 나왔다."], ["Rinaldo smiled, and said, “I will now show you if my strokes are more effectual.”", "리날도가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"이제 내 타격이 더 효험 있는지 보여 주겠다.\""], ["At these words he thrust the unfortunate Dardinel in the middle of his breast.", "이 말과 함께 그는 불운한 다르디넬의 가슴 한가운데를 찔렀다."], ["The blow was so violent that the cruel weapon pierced the body, and came out a palm-breadth behind his back.", "그 일격이 어찌나 맹렬했던지 그 잔혹한 무기는 몸을 꿰뚫고 등 뒤로 한 뼘 너비만큼 빠져나왔다."], ["Through this wound the life of Dardinel issued with his blood, and his body fell helpless to the ground.", "이 상처를 통해 다르디넬의 생명이 그의 피와 함께 흘러나왔고, 그의 몸은 맥없이 땅에 쓰러졌다."]], [["As a flower which the passing plough has uprooted languishes, and droops its head, so Dardinel, his visage covered with the paleness of death, expires, and the hopes of an illustrious race perish with him.", "지나가는 쟁기에 뿌리째 뽑힌 꽃이 시들어 고개를 떨구듯, 다르디넬도 죽음의 창백함으로 얼굴을 덮은 채 숨을 거두었고, 한 명망 높은 가문의 희망이 그와 함께 스러졌다."]], [["Like waters kept back by a dike, which, when the dike is broken, spread abroad through all the country, so the Moors, no longer kept in column by the example of Dardinel, fled in all directions.", "둑에 막혀 있던 물이 둑이 터지면 온 들판으로 흩어지듯, 무어인들도 더 이상 다르디넬의 본보기로 대열을 유지하지 못한 채 사방으로 달아났다."], ["Rinaldo despised too much such easy victories to pursue them; he wished for no combats but with brave men.", "리날도는 이런 손쉬운 승리들을 너무도 경멸하여 그들을 뒤쫓지 않았으니, 그는 오로지 용맹한 자들과의 싸움만을 바랐다."], ["At the same time, the other paladins made terrible slaughter of the Moors.", "동시에 다른 무사들은 무어인들을 끔찍하게 살육했다."], ["Charles himself, Oliver, Guido, and Ogier the Dane, carried death into their ranks on all sides.", "샤를 자신과 올리버, 귀도, 그리고 데인 사람 오지에가 사방에서 그들의 대열 속으로 죽음을 실어 날랐다."]], [["The infidels seemed doomed to perish to a man on that dreadful day; but the wise king, Marsilius, at last put some slight degree of method into the general rout.", "이교도들은 그 끔찍한 날에 한 사람도 남김없이 멸망할 운명인 듯 보였다. 그러나 현명한 왕 마르실리우스가 마침내 그 전면적인 패주에 약간의 질서를 부여했다."], ["He collected the remnant of the troops, formed them into a battalion, and retreated in tolerable order to his camp.", "그는 남은 군사들을 그러모아 한 부대로 편성하고, 제법 정연한 대오를 갖추어 자신의 진영으로 물러났다."], ["That camp was well fortified by intrenchments and a broad ditch.", "그 진영은 보루와 넓은 도랑으로 잘 방비되어 있었다."], ["Thither the fugitives hastened, and by degrees all that remained of the Moorish army was brought together there.", "도망자들은 그곳으로 서둘러 갔고, 무어인 군대의 남은 자들 전부가 차츰 거기로 모여들었다."]], [["The Emperor might perhaps that night have crushed his enemy entirely; but not thinking it prudent to expose his troops, fatigued as they were, to an attack upon a camp so well fortified, he contented himself with encompassing the enemy with his troops, prepared to make a regular siege.", "황제는 어쩌면 그날 밤에 자신의 적을 완전히 무너뜨릴 수도 있었을 것이다. 그러나 이미 지친 자신의 군사들을 그토록 잘 방비된 진영에 대한 공격에 내모는 것이 분별 있는 일이라 여기지 않아, 그는 적을 자신의 군사들로 에워싸 정식 포위전을 펼칠 채비를 하는 것으로 만족했다."], ["During the night the Moors had time to see the extent of their loss.", "밤 동안 무어인들은 자신들의 손실의 규모를 헤아릴 시간을 가졌다."], ["Their tents resounded with lamentations.", "그들의 천막은 통곡으로 울려 퍼졌다."], ["This warrior had to mourn a brother, that a friend; many suffered with grievous wounds, all trembled at the fate in store for them.", "이 전사는 형제를 잃고, 저 전사는 벗을 잃었으며, 많은 이들이 심한 상처로 고통받았고, 모두가 자신들에게 닥칠 운명에 떨었다."]], [["There were two young Moors, both of humble rank, who gave proof at that time of attachment and fidelity rare in the history of man.", "당시 인간의 역사에서 보기 드문 애착과 충심을 그때 입증한 두 젊은 무어인이 있었으니, 둘 다 미천한 신분이었다."], ["Cloridan and Medoro had followed their prince, Dardinel, to the wars of France.", "클로리단과 메도로는 자신들의 왕자 다르디넬을 따라 프랑스의 전쟁에 나선 이들이었다."], ["Cloridan, a bold huntsman, combined strength with activity.", "대담한 사냥꾼 클로리단은 힘과 민첩함을 겸비했다."], ["Medoro was a mere youth, his cheeks yet fair and blooming.", "메도로는 한낱 젊은이로, 그 두 뺨은 아직 곱고 발그레했다."], ["Of all the Saracens, no one united so much grace and beauty.", "모든 사라센인 가운데 그토록 많은 우아함과 아름다움을 한 몸에 지닌 이는 없었다."], ["His light hair was set off by his black and sparkling eyes.", "그의 연한 머리칼은 검고 반짝이는 눈으로 한층 돋보였다."], ["The two friends were together on guard at the rampart.", "두 벗은 함께 성벽에서 보초를 서고 있었다."], ["About midnight they gazed on the scene in deep dejection.", "자정 무렵 그들은 깊은 비탄에 잠겨 그 광경을 바라보았다."], ["Medoro, with tears in his eyes, spoke of the good prince Dardinel, and could not endure the thought that his body should be cast out on the plain, deprived of funeral honors.", "메도로는 눈에 눈물을 머금고 선량한 왕자 다르디넬에 대해 이야기했으며, 그의 시신이 장례의 예우도 받지 못한 채 들판에 내던져진다는 생각을 견딜 수 없었다. \"오 나의 벗이여,\""], ["“O my friend,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “must then the body of our prince be the prey of wolves and ravens?", "\"그러면 우리 왕자의 시신이 늑대와 까마귀의 먹이가 되어야 한단 말인가?"], ["Alas!", "아아!"], ["when I remember how he loved me, I feel that if I should sacrifice my life to do him honor, I should not do more than my duty.", "그분이 나를 얼마나 아끼셨는지를 떠올리면, 그분께 영예를 바치기 위해 내 목숨을 바친다 해도 내 본분 이상을 하는 것이 아니라는 생각이 드네."], ["I wish, dear friend, to seek out his body on the battlefield, and give it burial, and I hope to be able to pass through King Charles’s camp without discovery, as they are probably all asleep.", "사랑하는 벗이여, 나는 전장에서 그분의 시신을 찾아내어 묻어 드리고 싶네. 그리고 그들이 아마 모두 잠들어 있을 터이니, 들키지 않고 샤를 왕의 진영을 지나갈 수 있기를 바라네."], ["You, Cloridan, will be able to say for me, if I should die in the adventure, that gratitude and fidelity to my prince were my inducements.”", "자네 클로리단은, 만약 내가 이 일에서 죽거든, 우리 왕자께 대한 감사와 충심이 나를 이끈 것이라고 나를 위해 말해 줄 수 있을 걸세.\""]], [["Cloridan was both surprised and touched with this proof of the young man’s devotion.", "클로리단은 그 젊은이의 헌신의 이 증거에 놀라는 동시에 마음이 움직였다."], ["He loved him tenderly, and tried for a long time every effort to dissuade him from his design; but he found Medoro determined to accomplish his object or die in the endeavor.", "그는 그를 끔찍이 아꼈고, 오랜 시간 온갖 노력을 다해 그의 계획을 단념시키려 했으나, 메도로가 자신의 뜻을 이루거나 그 시도 중에 죽기로 작정했음을 알게 되었다."]], [["Cloridan, unable to change his purpose, said, “I will go with you, Medoro, and help you in this generous enterprise.", "그의 뜻을 바꿀 수 없었던 클로리단이 말했다. \"자네와 함께 가겠네, 메도로."], ["I value not life compared with honor, and if I did, do you suppose, dear friend, that I could live without you?", "그리고 이 갸륵한 일에서 자네를 돕겠네. 나는 명예에 견주면 목숨을 귀히 여기지 않으며, 설령 그렇다 한들, 사랑하는 벗이여, 자네 없이 내가 살 수 있으리라 생각하는가?"], ["I would rather fall by the arms of our enemies than die of grief for the loss of you.”", "자네를 잃은 슬픔으로 죽느니, 차라리 우리 적들의 무기에 쓰러지는 편이 낫겠네.\""]], [["When the two friends were relieved from their guard duty they went without any followers into the camp of the Christians.", "두 벗은 보초 임무에서 풀려나자 아무도 거느리지 않고 그리스도교도들의 진영으로 들어갔다."], ["All there was still; the fires were dying out; there was no fear of any attempt on the part of the Saracens, and the soldiers, overcome by fatigue or wine, slept secure, lying upon the ground in the midst of their arms and equipage.", "그곳은 온통 고요했고, 모닥불은 사그라지고 있었으며, 사라센인들 쪽에서 무슨 시도가 있으리라는 두려움도 없어, 병사들은 피로나 술에 못 이겨 자신들의 무기와 장비 한가운데 땅바닥에 누워 마음 놓고 잠들어 있었다."], ["Cloridan stopped, and said, “Medoro, I am not going to quit this camp without taking vengeance for the death of our prince.", "클로리단이 멈춰 서서 말했다. \"메도로, 나는 우리 왕자의 죽음에 대한 복수를 하지 않고는 이 진영을 떠나지 않겠네."], ["Keep watch, be on your guard that no one shall surprise us; I mean to mark a road with my sword through the ranks of our enemies.”", "망을 보게, 아무도 우리를 기습하지 못하도록 경계하게. 나는 우리 적들의 대열을 가로질러 내 칼로 길을 내겠네.\""], ["So saying, he entered the tent where Alpheus slept, who a year before had joined the camp of Charles, and pretended to be a great physician and astrologer.", "이렇게 말하며 그는 알페우스가 잠든 천막으로 들어갔으니, 알페우스는 한 해 전에 샤를의 진영에 합류하여 자신이 위대한 의사이자 점성가라고 행세하던 자였다."], ["But his science had deceived him, if it gave him hope of dying peacefully in his bed at a good old age; his lot was to die with little warning.", "그러나 만약 그의 학문이 그에게 천수를 누리며 잠자리에서 평온히 죽으리라는 희망을 주었다면, 그것은 그를 속인 것이었으니, 그의 운명은 거의 경고도 없이 죽는 것이었다."], ["Cloridan ran his sword through his heart.", "클로리단은 그의 심장을 칼로 꿰뚫었다."], ["A Greek and a German followed, who had been playing late at dice: fortunate if they had continued their game a little longer; but they never reckoned a throw like this among their chances.", "그다음으로 늦도록 주사위 노름을 하던 그리스인 한 명과 게르만인 한 명이 뒤따랐으니, 그들이 노름을 조금만 더 이어 갔더라면 운이 좋았으련만, 그들은 이런 한 판을 결코 자신들의 패 가운데 셈하지 못했다."], ["Cloridan next came to the unlucky Grillon, whose head lay softly on his pillow.", "클로리단은 다음으로 불운한 그릴론에게 이르렀으니, 그의 머리는 베개 위에 편안히 놓여 있었다."], ["He dreamed probably of the feast from which he had but just retired; for when Cloridan cut off his head wine flowed forth with the blood.", "그는 아마도 자신이 막 자리를 뜬 잔치 꿈을 꾸고 있었을 것이다. 클로리단이 그의 머리를 베자 피와 더불어 포도주가 흘러나왔으니 말이다."]], [["The two young Moors might have penetrated even to the tent of Charlemagne; but knowing that the paladins encamped around him kept watch by turns, and judging that it was impossible they should all be asleep, they were afraid to go too near.", "두 젊은 무어인은 샤를마뉴의 천막에까지도 뚫고 들어갈 수 있었을 것이다. 그러나 그를 둘러싸고 진을 친 무사들이 번갈아 망을 본다는 것을 알았고, 그들 모두가 잠들어 있을 리는 없다고 판단했기에, 너무 가까이 가기를 두려워했다."], ["They might also have obtained rich booty; but, intent only on their object, they crossed the camp, and arrived at length at the bloody field, where bucklers, lances, and swords lay scattered in the midst of corpses of poor and rich, common soldier and prince, horses and pools of blood.", "그들은 또한 값진 노획물도 얻을 수 있었을 것이다. 그러나 오로지 자신들의 목적에만 몰두한 채 그들은 진영을 가로질러, 마침내 피로 물든 들판에 다다랐으니, 그곳에는 방패와 창과 칼이 미천한 자와 부유한 자, 평범한 병사와 왕자의 시신들, 말들과 핏물 웅덩이들 한가운데 흩어져 있었다."], ["This terrible scene of carnage would have destroyed all hope of finding what they were in search of until dawn of day, were it not that the moon lent the aid of her uncertain rays.", "이 끔찍한 살육의 광경은, 달이 그 어슴푸레한 빛의 도움을 빌려 주지 않았더라면, 동이 틀 때까지 그들이 찾는 것을 발견할 모든 희망을 앗아 갔을 것이다."]], [["Medoro raised his eyes to the planet, and exclaimed, “O holy goddess, whom our fathers have adored under three different forms,—thou who displayest thy power in heaven, on earth, and in the underworld,—thou who art seen foremost among the nymphs chasing the beasts of the forest,—cause me to see, I implore thee, the spot where my dear master lies, and make me all my life long follow the example which thou dost exhibit of works of charity and love.”", "메도로는 그 천체를 향해 눈을 들어 올리며 외쳤다. \"오 거룩한 여신이여, 우리 조상들이 세 가지 다른 모습으로 숭배해 온 분이여, 하늘과 땅과 저승에서 당신의 권능을 드러내시는 분이여, 숲의 짐승들을 쫓는 요정들 가운데 맨 앞에 보이시는 분이여, 비오니, 제 사랑하는 주인이 누워 계신 곳을 제가 볼 수 있게 해 주소서. 그리고 당신께서 보여 주시는 자비와 사랑의 행적의 본보기를 제 평생토록 따르게 해 주소서.\""]], [["Either by accident, or that the moon was sensible of the prayer of Medoro, the cloud broke away, and the moonlight burst forth as bright as day.", "우연이었든, 아니면 달이 메도로의 기도를 알아챈 것이었든, 구름이 걷히고 달빛이 대낮처럼 환하게 쏟아져 나왔다."], ["The rays seemed especially to gild the spot where lay the body of Prince Dardinel; and Medoro, bathed in tears and with bleeding heart, recognized him by the quarterings of red and white on his shield.", "그 빛줄기는 다르디넬 왕자의 시신이 누운 자리를 유독 황금빛으로 물들이는 듯했고, 메도로는 눈물에 젖고 가슴에서 피를 흘리며, 그의 방패에 새겨진 붉고 흰 사분 문장으로 그를 알아보았다."]], [["With groans stifled by his tears, and lamentations in accents suppressed, not from any fear for himself, for he cared not for life, but lest any one should be roused to interrupt their pious duty while yet incomplete, he proposed to his companion that they should together bear Dardinel on their shoulders, sharing the burden of the beloved remains.", "눈물로 억눌린 신음과 짓눌린 목소리의 탄식으로 — 자신을 위해서가 아니었으니 그는 목숨 따위는 개의치 않았다, 다만 아직 끝맺지 못한 자신들의 경건한 본분을 누군가가 깨어나 방해할까 두려워서였다 — 그는 자신의 동무에게 둘이서 함께 다르디넬을 어깨에 메어, 그 사랑하는 유해의 짐을 나누어 지자고 제안했다."]], [["Marching with rapid strides under their precious load, they perceived that the stars began to grow pale, and that the shades of night would soon be dispersed by the dawn.", "그 소중한 짐을 지고 빠른 걸음으로 행군하던 그들은 별들이 희미해지기 시작하고, 밤의 그늘이 곧 동이 트면 흩어지리라는 것을 알아챘다."], ["Just then Zerbino, whose extreme valor had urged him far from the camp in pursuit of the fugitives, returning, entered the wood in which they were.", "바로 그때, 그 지극한 용맹으로 도망자들을 뒤쫓아 진영에서 멀리 나아갔던 제르비노가 돌아오는 길에 그들이 있던 숲으로 들어섰다."], ["Some knights in his train perceived at a distance the two brothers-in-arms.", "그의 수행원 가운데 몇몇 기사가 멀리서 그 두 의형제를 알아보았다."], ["Cloridan saw the troop, and, observing that they dispersed themselves over the plain as if in search of booty, told Medoro to lay down the body, and let each save himself by flight.", "클로리단이 그 무리를 보고는, 그들이 마치 노획물을 찾는 듯 들판으로 흩어지는 것을 보고, 메도로에게 시신을 내려놓고 각자 달아나 목숨을 구하자고 일렀다."], ["He dropped his part, thinking that Medoro would do the same; but the good youth loved his prince too well to abandon him, and continued to carry his load singly as well as he might, while Cloridan made his escape.", "그는 메도로도 그러리라 생각하고 자신의 몫을 내려놓았다. 그러나 그 선량한 젊은이는 자신의 왕자를 너무도 깊이 사랑하여 그를 버리지 못하고, 클로리단이 달아나는 동안 혼자서나마 힘닿는 대로 계속 그 짐을 졌다."], ["Near by there was a part of the wood tufted as if nothing but wild animals had ever penetrated it.", "가까이에는 야생 짐승 말고는 무엇도 들어가 본 적 없는 듯 우거진 숲의 한 구역이 있었다."], ["The unfortunate youth, loaded with the weight of his dead master, plunged into its recesses.", "그 불운한 젊은이는 죽은 주인의 무게를 진 채 그 깊숙한 곳으로 뛰어들었다."]], [["Cloridan, when he perceived that he had evaded his foes, discovered that Medoro was not with him.", "클로리단은 자신이 적들을 따돌렸음을 알아챘을 때, 메도로가 자신과 함께 있지 않다는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["“Ah!”", "\"아아!\""], ["exclaimed he, “how could I, dear Medoro, so forget myself as to consult my own safety without heeding yours?”", "그가 외쳤다. \"어찌 내가, 사랑하는 메도로여, 자네를 돌보지 않고 내 한 몸의 안전만 살피느라 그토록 나 자신을 잊을 수 있었단 말인가?\""], ["So saying, he retraced the tangled passes of the wood toward the place from whence he had fled.", "이렇게 말하며 그는 자신이 달아나 온 그곳을 향해 숲의 얽힌 길을 되짚어 갔다."], ["As he approached he heard the noise of horses, and the menacing voices of armed men.", "다가가면서 그는 말발굽 소리와 무장한 자들의 위협적인 목소리를 들었다."], ["Soon he perceived Medoro, on foot, with the cavaliers surrounding him.", "곧 그는 메도로가 걸어서, 기사들에게 둘러싸여 있는 것을 보았다."], ["Zerbino, their commander, bade them seize him.", "그들의 지휘관 제르비노가 그를 붙잡으라 명했다."], ["The unhappy Medoro turned now this way, now that, trying to conceal himself behind an oak or a rock, still bearing the body, which he would by no means leave.", "불행한 메도로는 이쪽으로 저쪽으로 몸을 돌리며 떡갈나무나 바위 뒤에 숨으려 했으나, 결코 내려놓으려 하지 않는 그 시신을 여전히 지고 있었다."], ["Cloridan not knowing how to help him, but resolved to perish with him, if he must perish, takes an arrow, fits it to his bow, discharges it, and pierces the breast of a Christian knight, who falls helpless from his horse.", "그를 어떻게 도와야 할지 몰랐으나 만약 죽어야 한다면 그와 함께 죽기로 결심한 클로리단은 화살 하나를 집어 활에 메겨 쏘아, 한 그리스도교도 기사의 가슴을 꿰뚫으니, 그자가 맥없이 말에서 떨어졌다."], ["The others look this way and that, to discover whence the fatal bolt was sped.", "다른 자들은 그 치명적인 화살이 어디서 날아왔는지 알아내려 이리저리 둘러보았다."], ["One, while demanding of his comrades in what direction the arrow came, received a second in his throat, which stopped his words, and soon closed his eyes to the scene.", "한 사람이 자신의 동료들에게 화살이 어느 방향에서 왔는지 물으려는데, 두 번째 화살을 목에 맞아 그 말이 막혔고, 이내 그 광경에 눈을 감았다."], ["Zerbino, furious at the death of his two comrades, ran upon Medoro, seized his golden hair, and dragged him forward to slay him.", "제르비노는 자신의 두 동료의 죽음에 격분하여 메도로에게 달려들어, 그의 금빛 머리칼을 움켜쥐고 그를 죽이려 앞으로 끌어당겼다."], ["But the sight of so much youth and beauty commanded pity.", "그러나 그토록 많은 젊음과 아름다움의 광경이 연민을 자아냈다."], ["He stayed his arm.", "그는 자신의 팔을 멈추었다."], ["The young man spoke in suppliant tones.", "그 젊은이가 애원하는 어조로 말했다."], ["“Ah!", "\"아아!"], ["signor,”", "나리,\""], ["said he, “I conjure you by the God whom you serve, deprive me not of life until I shall have buried the body of the prince, my master.", "그가 말했다. \"당신께서 섬기시는 하느님의 이름으로 당신께 간청하오니, 제가 제 주인이신 왕자의 시신을 묻을 때까지는 제 목숨을 거두지 말아 주소서."], ["Fear not that I will ask you any other favor; life is not dear to me; I desire death as soon as I shall have performed this sacred duty.", "제가 당신께 다른 은혜를 청하리라 두려워 마소서. 목숨은 제게 귀하지 않으며, 이 거룩한 본분을 다하는 즉시 저는 죽음을 바랍니다."], ["Do with me then as you please.", "그러니 저를 마음대로 하소서."], ["Give my limbs a prey to the birds and beasts; only let me first bury my prince.”", "제 사지를 새와 짐승의 먹이로 던지셔도 좋으니, 다만 먼저 제 왕자를 묻게만 해 주소서.\""], ["Medoro pronounced these words with an air so sweet and tender that a heart of stone would have been moved by them.", "메도로가 이 말을 어찌나 곱고 부드러운 기색으로 했던지 돌 같은 마음이라도 움직였을 것이다."], ["Zerbino was so to the bottom of his soul.", "제르비노는 그 영혼 밑바닥까지 움직였다."], ["He was on the point of uttering words of mercy, when a cruel subaltern, forgetting all respect to his commander, plunged his lance into the breast of the young Moor.", "그가 막 자비의 말을 입에 올리려는 찰나, 한 잔혹한 부하가 자신의 지휘관에 대한 모든 존경을 잊고서 그 젊은 무어인의 가슴에 자신의 창을 찔러 넣었다."], ["Zerbino, enraged at his brutality, turned upon the wretch to take vengeance, but he saved himself by a precipitate flight.", "제르비노는 그의 잔인무도함에 격노하여 그 비열한 자에게 복수하려 돌아섰으나, 그자는 황급히 달아나 몸을 피했다."]], [["Cloridan, who saw Medoro fall, could contain himself no longer.", "메도로가 쓰러지는 것을 본 클로리단은 더 이상 자신을 억누를 수 없었다."], ["He rushed from his concealment, threw down his bow, and, sword in hand, seemed only desirous of vengeance for Medoro, and to die with him.", "그는 자신이 숨어 있던 곳에서 뛰쳐나와 활을 내던지고, 칼을 손에 쥔 채 오로지 메도로의 복수와 그와 함께 죽기만을 바라는 듯했다."], ["In a moment, pierced through and through with many wounds, he exerts the last remnant of his strength in dragging himself to Medoro, to die embracing him.", "한순간에 수많은 상처로 온몸이 꿰뚫린 그는 마지막 남은 힘을 짜내어 메도로에게 몸을 끌고 가, 그를 끌어안고 죽으려 했다."], ["The cavaliers left them thus to rejoin Zerbino, whose rage against the murderer of Medoro had drawn him away from the spot.", "기사들은 그들을 그대로 두고 제르비노에게로 돌아갔으니, 메도로를 죽인 자에 대한 그의 분노가 그를 그 자리에서 멀어지게 했던 것이다."]], [["Cloridan died; and Medoro, bleeding copiously, was drawing near his end when help arrived.", "클로리단은 죽었다. 그리고 피를 줄줄 흘리던 메도로가 죽음에 가까워지고 있을 때, 도움의 손길이 다다랐다."]], [["A young maiden approached the fallen knights at this critical moment.", "이 위태로운 순간에 한 젊은 처녀가 쓰러진 기사들에게 다가왔다."], ["Her dress was that of a peasant-girl, but her air was noble, and her beauty celestial; sweetness and goodness reigned in her lovely countenance.", "그녀의 옷차림은 농가 처녀의 것이었으나, 그 기품은 고귀했고, 그 아름다움은 천상의 것이었다. 다정함과 선량함이 그녀의 사랑스러운 얼굴에 깃들어 있었다."], ["It was no other than Angelica, the Princess of Cathay.", "그녀는 다름 아닌 카타이의 공주 안젤리카였다."]], [["When she had recovered that precious ring, as we have before related, Angelica, knowing its value, felt proud in the power it conferred, travelled alone without fear, not without a secret shame that she had ever been obliged to seek protection in her wanderings of the Count Orlando and of Sacripant.", "우리가 앞서 이야기한 대로 그 귀한 반지를 되찾았을 때, 안젤리카는 그 가치를 알았기에 그것이 부여하는 권능에 자긍심을 느꼈고, 두려움 없이 홀로 여행했으나, 자신의 방랑 가운데 오를란도 백작과 사크리판트의 보호를 구할 수밖에 없었던 적이 있었다는 데에 은밀한 부끄러움이 없지 않았다."], ["She reproached herself too as with a weakness that she had ever thought of marrying Rinaldo; in fine, her pride grew so high as to persuade her that no man living was worthy to aspire to her hand.", "그녀는 또한 리날도와 혼인할 생각을 한 적이 있다는 것을 하나의 나약함으로 여겨 스스로를 책망했다. 요컨대 그녀의 자긍심은 살아 있는 어떤 남자도 자신의 손을 넘볼 자격이 없다고 스스로를 설득할 만큼 드높아졌다."]], [["Moved with pity at the sight of the young man wounded, and melted to tears at hearing the cause, she quickly recalled to remembrance the knowledge she had acquired in India, where the virtues of plants and the art of healing formed part of the education even of princesses.", "상처 입은 젊은이의 모습에 연민으로 마음이 움직이고, 그 사연을 듣고서 눈물 짓게 된 그녀는, 식물의 효능과 치료의 기술이 공주들의 교육에까지도 일부를 이루던 인도에서 자신이 익힌 지식을 재빨리 기억해 냈다."], ["The beautiful queen ran into the adjoining meadow to gather plants of virtue to staunch the flow of blood.", "그 아름다운 여왕은 피의 흐름을 멎게 할 효험 있는 식물들을 캐러 이웃한 풀밭으로 달려갔다."], ["Meeting on her way a countryman on horseback seeking a strayed heifer, she begged him to come to her assistance, and endeavor to remove the wounded man to a more secure asylum.", "가는 길에 길 잃은 어린 암소를 찾아 말을 타고 있던 한 시골 사람을 만난 그녀는, 자신을 도와 그 상처 입은 사람을 더 안전한 피난처로 옮겨 보자고 그에게 청했다."]], [["Angelica, having prepared the plants by bruising them between two stones, laid them with her fair hand on Medoro’s wound.", "안젤리카는 그 식물들을 두 돌 사이에서 짓이겨 마련한 뒤, 그것을 자신의 고운 손으로 메도로의 상처에 얹었다."], ["The remedy soon restored in some degree the strength of the wounded man, who, before he would quit the spot, made them cover with earth and turf the bodies of his friend and of the prince.", "그 치료약은 곧 상처 입은 사람의 기력을 어느 정도 회복시켰으니, 그는 그 자리를 떠나기에 앞서 그들에게 자신의 벗과 왕자의 시신을 흙과 잔디로 덮게 했다."], ["Then surrendering himself to the pity of his deliverers, he allowed them to place him on the horse of the shepherd, and conduct him to his cottage.", "그런 다음 자신을 구해 준 이들의 연민에 자신을 맡기며, 그는 양치기의 말에 자신을 태워 그 오두막으로 데려가도록 했다."], ["It was a pleasant farmhouse on the borders of the wood, bearing marks of comfort and competency.", "그것은 숲 가장자리에 자리 잡은 아늑한 농가로, 안락함과 넉넉함의 자취를 띠고 있었다."], ["There the shepherd lived with his wife and children.", "그곳에서 양치기는 아내와 자식들과 더불어 살았다."], ["There Angelica tended Medoro, and there, by the devoted care of the beautiful queen, his sad wound closed over, and he recovered his perfect health.", "그곳에서 안젤리카는 메도로를 돌보았고, 그곳에서 그 아름다운 여왕의 헌신적인 보살핌으로 그의 처참한 상처가 아물었으며, 그는 온전한 건강을 되찾았다."]], [["O Count Rinaldo, O King Sacripant!", "오 리날도 백작이여, 오 사크리판트 왕이여!"], ["what availed it you to possess so many virtues and such fame?", "그토록 많은 미덕과 그토록 큰 명성을 지닌 것이 그대들에게 무슨 소용이 있었던가?"], ["What advantage have you derived from all your high deserts?", "그대들의 그 모든 드높은 공적에서 그대들은 어떤 이득을 얻었던가?"], ["O hapless king, great Agrican!", "오 불운한 왕, 위대한 아그리칸이여!"], ["if you could return to life, how would you endure to see yourself rejected by one who will bow to the yoke of Hymen in favor of a young soldier of humble birth?", "그대가 되살아날 수 있다면, 미천한 출신의 한 젊은 병사를 위해 히멘의 멍에에 머리를 숙일 그녀에게서 자신이 거절당하는 것을 어찌 견딜 수 있겠는가?"], ["And thou, Ferrau, and ye numerous others who a hundred times have put your lives at hazard for this cruel beauty, how bitter will it be to you to see her sacrifice you all to the claims of the humble Medoro!", "그리고 그대 페라우여, 그리고 이 잔혹한 미인을 위해 백 번이나 목숨을 걸었던 그대들 수많은 다른 이들이여, 그녀가 그대들 모두를 미천한 메도로의 차지로 희생시키는 것을 보는 일이 그대들에게 얼마나 쓰라리겠는가!"]], [["There, under the low roof of a shepherd, the flame of Hymen was lighted for this haughty queen.", "그곳, 한 양치기의 나지막한 지붕 아래에서, 이 도도한 여왕을 위해 히멘의 불꽃이 밝혀졌다."], ["She takes the shepherd’s wife to serve in place of mother, the shepherd and his children for witnesses, and marries the happy Medoro.", "그녀는 양치기의 아내를 어머니 대신으로, 양치기와 그 자식들을 증인으로 삼아, 행복한 메도로와 혼인했다."]], [["Angelica, after her marriage, wishing to endow Medoro with the sovereignty of the countries which yet remained to her, took with him the road to the East.", "안젤리카는 혼인한 뒤, 아직 자신에게 남아 있던 나라들의 통치권을 메도로에게 물려주고자, 그와 함께 동방으로 가는 길에 올랐다."], ["She had preserved through all her adventures a bracelet of gold enriched with precious stones, the present of the Count Orlando.", "그녀는 자신의 온갖 모험을 거치는 동안 오를란도 백작의 선물인, 보석으로 장식된 금팔찌 하나를 간직해 왔다."], ["Having nothing else wherewith to reward the good shepherd and his wife, who had served her with so much care and fidelity, she took the bracelet from her arm and gave it to them, and then the newly-married couple directed their steps toward those mountains which separate France and Spain, intending to wait at Barcelona a vessel which should take them on their way to the East.", "그토록 많은 정성과 충심으로 자신을 섬겨 준 그 선량한 양치기와 그 아내에게 보답할 다른 무엇도 없었기에, 그녀는 그 팔찌를 자신의 팔에서 빼어 그들에게 주었고, 그런 다음 새로 혼인한 부부는 프랑스와 에스파냐를 가르는 저 산들을 향해 발걸음을 옮겼으니, 바르셀로나에서 자신들을 동방으로 가는 길에 태워다 줄 배 한 척을 기다릴 작정이었다. 광기에 빠진 오를란도"]], [["# ORLANDO MAD", "# ORLANDO MAD"]], [["————\nOrlando, on the loss of Angelica, laid aside his crest and arms, and arrayed himself in a suit of black armor expressive of his despair.", "안젤리카를 잃은 오를란도는 자신의 깃 장식과 무구를 내려놓고, 자신의 절망을 드러내는 검은 갑옷 한 벌로 차려입었다."], ["In this guise he carried such slaughter among the ranks of the infidels that both armies were astonished at the achievements of the stranger knight.", "이런 차림으로 그는 이교도들의 대열 가운데 얼마나 큰 살육을 벌였던지, 두 군대 모두가 그 낯선 기사의 위업에 놀랐다."], ["Mandricardo, who had been absent from the battle, heard the report of these achievements, and determined to test for himself the valor of the knight so extolled.", "전투에 나서지 않았던 만드리카르도는 이 위업들에 대한 소문을 듣고, 그토록 칭송받는 그 기사의 용맹을 스스로 시험해 보기로 작정했다."], ["He it was who broke in upon the conference of Zerbino and Isabella, and their benefactor Orlando, as they stood occupied in mutual felicitations, after the happy reunion of the lovers by the prowess of the paladin.", "무사의 용맹으로 두 연인이 행복하게 재회한 뒤, 제르비노와 이사벨라, 그리고 그들의 은인 오를란도가 서로 축하의 말을 나누느라 여념이 없을 때, 그들의 대화에 끼어든 이가 바로 그였다."]], [["Mandricardo, after contemplating the group for a moment, addressed himself to Orlando in these words: “Thou must be the man I seek.", "만드리카르도는 한순간 그 무리를 바라보고는, 오를란도에게 이런 말로 자신의 뜻을 밝혔다. \"그대가 내가 찾는 자임에 틀림없다."], ["For ten days and more I have been on thy track.", "열흘 넘게 나는 그대의 자취를 좇아왔다."], ["The fame of thy exploits has brought me hither, that I may measure my strength with thine.", "그대 위업의 명성이 나를 이리로 이끌었으니, 그대의 힘을 내 힘과 견주어 보고자 함이다."], ["Thy crest and shield prove thee the same who spread such slaughter among our troops.", "그대의 깃 장식과 방패가 우리 군대 가운데 그토록 큰 살육을 퍼뜨린 바로 그 자임을 입증한다."], ["But these marks are superfluous, and if I saw thee among a hundred I should know thee by thy martial bearing to be the man I seek.”", "그러나 이런 표시들은 군더더기이니, 그대를 백 명 가운데서 본다 한들 나는 그 군인다운 풍채로 그대가 내가 찾는 자임을 알아볼 것이다.\""]], [["“I respect thy courage,”", "\"그대의 용기를 나는 존중한다.\""], ["said Orlando; “such a design could not have sprung up in any but a brave and generous soul.", "오를란도가 말했다. \"그런 계획은 용맹하고 너그러운 영혼이 아니고서는 결코 솟아날 수 없는 것이다."], ["If the desire to see me has brought thee hither, I would, if it were possible, show thee my inmost soul.", "나를 보고자 하는 욕망이 그대를 이리로 데려온 것이라면, 가능하다면 나는 그대에게 내 가장 깊은 속마음을 보여 주고 싶다."], ["I will remove my visor, that you may satisfy your curiosity; but when you have done so I hope that you will also try and see if my valor corresponds to my appearance.”", "그대의 호기심을 채울 수 있도록 내 면갑을 들어 올리겠다. 그러나 그러고 나면, 그대 또한 내 용맹이 내 외양과 걸맞은지 시험해 보기를 바란다.\""]], [["“Come on,”", "\"덤벼라.\""], ["said the Saracen, “my first wish was to see and know thee; I will not gratify my second.”", "사라센인이 말했다. \"내 첫 번째 바람은 그대를 보고 아는 것이었으나, 두 번째 바람은 채워 주지 않겠다.\""]], [["Orlando, observing Mandricardo, was surprised to see no sword at his side, nor mace at his saddle-bow.", "오를란도는 만드리카르도를 살펴보다가, 그의 옆구리에 칼도 없고 안장 앞머리에 철퇴도 없는 것을 보고 놀랐다."], ["“And what weapon hast thou,”", "\"그러면 그대는 무슨 무기를 지녔는가,\""], ["said he, “if thy lance fail thee?”", "그가 말했다. \"그대의 창이 그대를 저버린다면?\""]], [["“Do not concern yourself about that,”", "\"그 일은 걱정 말라.\" 만드리카르도가 말했다."], ["said Mandricardo; “I have made many good knights give ground with no other weapon than you see.", "\"나는 그대가 보는 것 말고는 다른 무기 없이도 많은 훌륭한 기사들을 물러서게 했다."], ["Know that I have sworn an oath never to bear a sword until I win back that famous Durindana that Orlando, the paladin, carries.", "알아 두라, 나는 무사 오를란도가 지니고 다니는 저 이름난 두린다나를 되찾을 때까지는 결코 칼을 차지 않겠다고 맹세했다."], ["That sword belongs to the suit of armor which I wear; that only is wanting.", "그 칼은 내가 입은 갑옷 한 벌에 속하는 것으로, 오직 그것만이 빠져 있다."], ["Without doubt it was stolen, but how it got into the hands of Orlando I know not.", "의심할 바 없이 그것은 도둑맞은 것이나, 그것이 어찌하여 오를란도의 손에 들어갔는지 나는 모른다."], ["But I will make him pay dearly for it when I find him.", "그러나 그를 찾아내면 나는 그가 그 값을 톡톡히 치르게 하겠다."], ["I seek him the more anxiously that I may avenge with his blood the death of King Agrican, my father, whom he treacherously slew.", "내가 그를 더욱 애타게 찾는 것은, 그가 비열하게 살해한 내 아버지 아그리칸 왕의 죽음을 그의 피로 갚기 위함이다."], ["I am sure he must have done it by treachery, for it was not in his power to subdue in fair fight such a warrior as my father.”", "그가 비열한 술책으로 그리했음이 틀림없으니, 내 아버지 같은 전사를 정정당당한 싸움에서 굴복시키는 것은 그의 힘으로 될 일이 아니었기 때문이다.\""]], [["“Thou liest,”", "\"거짓말이다.\""], ["cried Orlando; “and all who say so lie.", "오를란도가 외쳤다. \"그리고 그리 말하는 자는 누구든 거짓말을 하는 것이다."], ["I am Orlando, whom you seek; yes, I am he who slew your father honorably.", "내가 바로 그대가 찾는 오를란도다. 그렇다, 내가 바로 그대의 아버지를 명예롭게 죽인 자다."], ["Hold, here is the sword: you shall have it if your courage avails to merit it.", "자, 여기 그 칼이 있다. 그대의 용기가 그것을 얻을 자격이 있다면 그대가 그것을 갖게 될 것이다."], ["Though it belongs to me by right, I will not use it in this dispute.", "비록 그것이 정당하게 내 것이지만, 나는 이 다툼에서 그것을 쓰지 않겠다."], ["See, I hang it on this tree; you shall be master of it, if you bereave me of life; not else.”", "보라, 나는 그것을 이 나무에 걸어 두겠다. 그대가 내 목숨을 앗아 간다면 그대가 그것의 주인이 될 것이요, 그렇지 못하면 안 될 것이다.\""]], [["At these words Orlando drew Durindana, and hung it on one of the branches of a tree near by.", "이 말과 함께 오를란도는 두린다나를 빼어 가까이 있는 한 나무의 가지 하나에 걸었다."]], [["Both knights, boiling with equal ardor, rode off in a semicircle; then rushed together with reins thrown loose, and struck one another with their lances.", "두 기사는 다 같이 똑같은 열기로 끓어오른 채, 반원을 그리며 말을 달려 나갔다가, 고삐를 풀어 놓고 서로를 향해 돌진하여 창으로 서로를 쳤다."], ["Both kept their seats, immovable.", "둘 다 미동도 없이 자리를 지켰다."], ["The splinters of their lances flew into the air, and no weapon remained for either but the fragment which he held in his hand.", "그들의 창 조각이 공중으로 날아갔고, 어느 쪽에게도 손에 쥔 부러진 조각 말고는 무기가 남지 않았다."], ["Then those two knights, covered with iron mail, were reduced to the necessity of fighting with staves, in the manner of two rustics, who dispute the boundary of a meadow, or the possession of a spring.", "그리하여 쇠사슬 갑옷으로 무장한 그 두 기사는, 풀밭의 경계나 샘 하나의 소유를 두고 다투는 두 시골뜨기처럼 막대기로 싸워야 하는 처지가 되었다."]], [["These clubs could not long keep whole in the hands of such sturdy smiters, who were soon reduced to fight with naked fists.", "이 몽둥이들은 그토록 억센 타격을 가하는 자들의 손에서 오래 멀쩡할 수 없었으니, 그들은 이내 맨주먹으로 싸우는 처지가 되었다."], ["Such warfare was more painful to him that gave than to him that received the blows.", "그런 싸움은 타격을 받는 자보다 가하는 자에게 더 고통스러웠다."], ["They next clasped, and strained each his adversary, as Hercules did Antæus.", "그들은 다음으로 서로 부둥켜안고, 헤라클레스가 안타이오스에게 했듯이 저마다 자신의 적수를 힘껏 조였다."], ["Mandricardo, more enraged than Orlando, made violent efforts to unseat the paladin, and dropped the rein of his horse.", "오를란도보다 더 격분한 만드리카르도는 그 무사를 안장에서 떨어뜨리려 격렬히 애쓰다가, 자신의 말고삐를 놓치고 말았다."], ["Orlando, more calm, perceived it.", "더 침착한 오를란도가 그것을 알아챘다."], ["With one hand he resisted Mandricardo, with the other he twitched the horse’s bridle over the ears of the animal.", "그는 한 손으로 만드리카르도를 막으면서, 다른 손으로 그 짐승의 고삐를 그 귀 위로 홱 벗겨 냈다."], ["The Saracen dragged Orlando with all his might, but Orlando’s thighs held the saddle like a vise.", "사라센인이 있는 힘껏 오를란도를 끌어당겼으나, 오를란도의 두 넓적다리가 안장을 죔쇠처럼 붙들었다."], ["At last the efforts of the Saracen broke the girths of Orlando’s horse; the saddle slipped; the knight, firm in his stirrups, slipped with it, and came to the ground hardly conscious of his fall.", "마침내 사라센인의 안간힘에 오를란도의 말 뱃대끈이 끊어졌고, 안장이 미끄러졌으며, 등자에 단단히 버틴 기사도 그것과 함께 미끄러져, 자신이 떨어진 것을 거의 의식하지도 못한 채 땅에 닿았다."], ["The noise of his armor in falling startled Mandricardo’s horse, now without a bridle.", "그의 갑옷이 떨어지며 내는 소리가 이제 고삐 없는 만드리카르도의 말을 놀라게 했다."], ["He started off in full career, heeding neither trees nor rocks nor broken ground.", "그 말은 나무도 바위도 험한 땅도 아랑곳하지 않고 전속력으로 내달리기 시작했다."], ["Urged by fright, he ran with furious speed, carrying his master, who, almost distracted with rage, shouted and beat the animal with his fists, and thereby impelled his flight.", "공포에 내몰린 그 말은 맹렬한 속도로 달려, 분노로 거의 제정신이 아닌 자신의 주인을 실어 갔으니, 주인은 소리치며 주먹으로 그 짐승을 때렸고 그로써 도리어 그 질주를 부추겼다."], ["After running thus three miles or more, a deep ditch opposed their progress.", "그렇게 삼 마일 남짓 달린 끝에, 깊은 도랑 하나가 그들의 앞을 가로막았다."], ["The horse and rider fell headlong into it, and did not find the bottom covered with feather-beds or roses.", "말과 기수는 그 안으로 곤두박질쳐 떨어졌으니, 바닥에는 깃털 침대도 장미꽃도 깔려 있지 않았다."], ["They got sadly bruised; but were lucky enough to escape without any broken limbs.", "그들은 처참하게 멍이 들었으나, 사지가 부러지는 일은 없이 벗어날 만큼은 운이 좋았다."], ["Mandricardo, as soon as he gained his feet, seized the horse by his mane with fury; but, having no bridle, could not hold him.", "만드리카르도는 발을 딛자마자 그 말의 갈기를 사납게 움켜쥐었으나, 고삐가 없어 붙들 수가 없었다."], ["He looked round in hopes of finding something that would do for a rein.", "그는 고삐 노릇을 할 만한 무언가를 찾을 수 있을까 하여 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["Just then fortune, who seemed willing to help him at last, brought that way a peasant with a bridle in his hand, who was in search of his farm horse that had strayed away.", "바로 그때, 마침내 그를 도우려는 듯한 운명이, 길 잃은 자기 농장 말을 찾고 있던, 손에 고삐를 든 한 농부를 그 길로 데려왔다."]], [["Orlando, having speedily repaired his horse’s girths, remounted, and waited a good hour for the Saracen to return.", "오를란도는 자신의 말 뱃대끈을 재빨리 손본 뒤 다시 올라타, 사라센인이 돌아오기를 족히 한 시간이나 기다렸다."], ["Not seeing him, he concluded to go in search of him.", "그가 보이지 않자, 그는 그를 찾아 나서기로 작정했다."], ["He took an affectionate leave of Zerbino and Isabella, who would willingly have followed him; but this the brave paladin would by no means permit.", "그는 제르비노와 이사벨라에게 다정한 작별을 고했으니, 그들은 기꺼이 그를 따라가려 했으나, 용맹한 무사는 이를 결코 허락하지 않았다."], ["He held it unknightly to go in search of an enemy accompanied by a friend, who might act as a defender.", "그는 방어자 노릇을 할지도 모를 벗을 동반하고 적을 찾아 나서는 것을 기사답지 못한 일로 여겼다."], ["Therefore, desiring them to say to Mandricardo, if they should meet him, that his purpose was to tarry in the neighborhood three days, and then repair to the camp of Charlemagne, he took down Durindana from the tree, and proceeded in the direction which the Saracen’s horse had taken.", "그리하여 그는 그들에게, 만약 만드리카르도를 만나거든 자신의 뜻이 사흘간 이 근방에 머물다가 그런 다음 샤를마뉴의 진영으로 가는 것이라고 전해 달라 청한 뒤, 나무에서 두린다나를 내려 사라센인의 말이 향했던 방향으로 나아갔다."], ["But the animal, having no guide but its terror, had so doubled and confused its traces that Orlando, after two days spent in the search, gave up the attempt.", "그러나 그 짐승은 자신의 공포 말고는 인도해 줄 것이 없었기에 자신의 자취를 어찌나 되돌리고 어지럽혀 놓았던지, 오를란도는 그 수색에 이틀을 보낸 끝에 그 시도를 단념했다."]], [["It was about the middle of the third day when the paladin arrived on the pleasant bank of a stream which wound through a meadow enamelled with flowers.", "그 무사가 꽃으로 아롱진 풀밭 사이를 굽이쳐 흐르는 시내의 쾌적한 둑에 다다른 것은 사흘째 날의 한낮 무렵이었다."], ["High trees, whose tops met and formed an arbor, overshadowed the fountain; and the breeze which blew through their foliage tempered the heat.", "우듬지가 서로 맞닿아 정자를 이룬 높은 나무들이 그 샘에 그늘을 드리웠고, 그 잎새 사이로 부는 산들바람이 더위를 누그러뜨렸다."], ["Hither the shepherds used to resort to quench their thirst, and to enjoy the shelter from the midday sun.", "이곳으로 양치기들이 갈증을 풀러, 그리고 한낮의 해를 피해 그늘을 즐기러 모여들곤 했다."], ["The air, perfumed with the flowers, seemed to breathe fresh strength into their veins.", "꽃향기로 그윽한 그 공기는 그들의 핏줄에 새로운 기운을 불어넣는 듯했다."], ["Orlando felt the influence, though covered with his armor.", "갑옷으로 덮여 있었음에도 오를란도는 그 영향을 느꼈다."], ["He stopped in this delicious arbor, where everything seemed to invite to repose.", "그는 모든 것이 휴식을 권하는 듯한 이 감미로운 정자에 멈춰 섰다."], ["But he could not have chosen a more fatal asylum.", "그러나 그는 이보다 더 치명적인 피난처를 고를 수는 없었을 것이다."], ["He there spent the most miserable moments of his life.", "그는 그곳에서 자신의 생애에서 가장 비참한 순간들을 보냈다."]], [["He looked around, and noted with pleasure all the charms of the spot.", "그는 주위를 둘러보며 그곳의 온갖 매력을 흐뭇하게 눈에 담았다."], ["He saw that some of the trees were carved with inscriptions—he drew near, and read them, and what was his surprise to find that they composed the name of Angelica!", "그는 나무들 가운데 몇 그루에 글귀가 새겨져 있는 것을 보았다. 그는 가까이 다가가 그것을 읽었고, 그것들이 안젤리카라는 이름을 이루고 있음을 알았을 때 그의 놀라움이 어떠했겠는가!"], ["Farther on he found the name of Medoro mixed with hers.", "더 나아가 그는 메도로의 이름이 그녀의 이름과 뒤섞여 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["The paladin thought he dreamed.", "그 무사는 자신이 꿈을 꾸는 것이라 여겼다."], ["He stood like one amazed—like a bird that, rising to fly, finds its feet caught in a net.", "그는 마치 날아오르려다 그 발이 그물에 걸린 새처럼, 망연자실한 사람마냥 서 있었다."]], [["Orlando followed the course of the stream, and came to one of its turns where the rocks of the mountain bent in such a way as to form a sort of grotto.", "오를란도는 시내의 물줄기를 따라가다, 산의 바위들이 일종의 동굴을 이루도록 굽어 있는 어느 굽이에 이르렀다."], ["The twisted stems of ivy and the wild vine draped the entrance of this recess, scooped by the hand of nature.", "담쟁이덩굴과 야생 포도덩굴의 뒤엉킨 줄기가 자연의 손으로 파인 이 후미진 곳의 입구를 휘장처럼 덮고 있었다."]], [["The unhappy paladin, on entering the grotto, saw letters which appeared to have been lately carved.", "그 불행한 무사는 동굴로 들어서다가, 최근에 새겨진 듯한 글자들을 보았다."], ["They were verses which Medoro had written in honor of his happy nuptials with the beautiful queen.", "그것은 메도로가 그 아름다운 여왕과의 행복한 혼인을 기려 지은 시구였다."], ["Orlando tried to persuade himself it must be some other Angelica whom those verses celebrated, and as for Medoro, he had never heard his name.", "오를란도는 그 시구가 기리는 이가 어떤 다른 안젤리카임에 틀림없다고 스스로를 설득하려 애썼고, 메도로로 말하자면 그는 그 이름을 들어 본 적조차 없었다."], ["The sun was now declining, and Orlando remounted his horse, and went on his way.", "해는 이제 기울고 있었고, 오를란도는 다시 말에 올라 길을 갔다."], ["He soon saw the roof of a cottage whence the smoke ascended; he heard the barking of dogs and the lowing of cattle, and arrived at a humble dwelling which seemed to offer an asylum for the night.", "그는 곧 연기가 피어오르는 오두막 한 채의 지붕을 보았고, 개 짖는 소리와 소 우는 소리를 들었으며, 하룻밤의 피난처를 내어 줄 듯한 한 누추한 거처에 다다랐다."], ["The inmates, as soon as they saw him, hastened to render him service.", "그곳에 든 사람들은 그를 보자마자 서둘러 그를 시중들었다."], ["One took his horse, another his shield and cuirass, another his golden spurs.", "한 사람은 그의 말을, 다른 사람은 그의 방패와 흉갑을, 또 다른 사람은 그의 황금 박차를 받았다."], ["This cottage was the very same where Medoro had been carried, deeply", "이 오두막은 바로 메도로가 깊이 부상당한 채 실려 왔던 그곳이었다."]], [["wounded,—where Angelica had tended him, and afterwards married him.", "부상을 당했던 곳이었다. 그곳에서 안젤리카가 그를 간호했고, 이후 그와 혼인했던 것이다."], ["The shepherd who lived in it loved to tell everybody the story of this marriage, and soon related it, with all its details, to the miserable Orlando.", "그곳에 살던 목동은 누구에게나 이 혼인의 이야기를 들려주기를 좋아했고, 곧 그 모든 자초지종을 비참한 처지의 오를란도에게 들려주었다."]], [["Having finished it, he went away, and returned with the precious bracelet which Angelica, grateful for his services, had given him as a memorial.", "이야기를 마친 목동은 자리를 떴다가, 안젤리카가 그의 수고에 감사하며 기념으로 주었던 그 귀한 팔찌를 가지고 돌아왔다."], ["It was the one which Orlando had himself given her.", "그것은 다름 아닌 오를란도 자신이 그녀에게 주었던 것이었다."]], [["This last touch was the finishing stroke to the excited paladin.", "이 마지막 일격은 흥분한 기사에게 결정타가 되었다."], ["Frantic, exasperated, he exclaimed, against the ungrateful and cruel princess who had disdained him, the most renowned, the most indomitable of all the paladins of France,—him, who had rescued her from the most alarming perils,—him, who had fought the most terrible battles for her sake,—she to prefer to him a young Saracen!", "미친 듯이 격분한 그는, 자신을 멸시한 그 배은망덕하고 잔인한 공주를 향해 외쳤다. 프랑스의 모든 기사들 가운데 가장 명성 높고 가장 굴복할 줄 모르는 자, 그토록 무시무시한 위험에서 그녀를 구해낸 자, 그녀를 위해 가장 끔찍한 싸움을 치른 자가 바로 자신이거늘, 그런 자신보다 한낱 젊은 사라센 사람을 더 좋아하다니!"], ["The pride of the noble Count was deeply wounded.", "고귀한 백작의 자존심은 깊은 상처를 입었다."], ["Indignant, frantic, a victim to ungovernable rage, he rushed into the forest, uttering the most frightful shrieks.", "분노에 휩싸여 미쳐 날뛰며, 다스릴 수 없는 격분의 희생자가 된 그는 가장 끔찍한 비명을 지르며 숲 속으로 뛰어들었다."]], [["“No, no!”", "\"아니다, 아니야!\""], ["cried he, “I am not the man they take me for!", "그가 외쳤다. \"나는 그들이 여기는 그런 사람이 아니다!"], ["Orlando is dead!", "오를란도는 죽었어!"], ["I am only the wandering ghost of that unhappy Count, who is now suffering the torments of hell!”", "나는 그저 그 불행한 백작의 떠도는 유령일 뿐, 그 백작은 지금 지옥의 고통을 겪고 있단 말이다!\""]], [["Orlando wandered all night, as chance directed, through the wood, and at sunrise his destiny led him to the fountain where Medoro had engraved the fatal inscription.", "오를란도는 발길 닿는 대로 밤새도록 숲을 헤매었고, 해가 뜰 무렵 그의 운명은 그를 어느 샘으로 이끌었다. 메도로가 그 치명적인 글귀를 새겨 놓았던 바로 그 샘이었다."], ["The frantic paladin saw it a second time with fury, drew his sword, and hacked it from the rock.", "미쳐 날뛰던 기사는 그것을 두 번째로 보고는 격노하여 칼을 뽑아 바위에서 그 글귀를 깎아 내버렸다."]], [["Unlucky grotto!", "불운한 동굴이여!"], ["you shall no more attract by your shade and coolness, you shall no more shelter with your arch either shepherd or flock.", "너는 더 이상 그늘과 서늘함으로 누구도 끌어들이지 못할 것이며, 너의 아치 아래 목동도 양 떼도 더 이상 품지 못하리라."], ["And you, fresh and pure fountain, you may not escape the rage of the furious Orlando!", "그리고 너, 맑고 깨끗한 샘이여, 너 또한 광란하는 오를란도의 분노를 피하지 못하리라!"], ["He cast into the fountain branches, trunks of trees which he tore up, pieces of rocks which he broke off, plants uprooted, with the earth adhering, and turf and brushes, so as to choke the fountain, and destroy the purity of its waters.", "그는 나뭇가지들, 뽑아낸 나무 둥치들, 부숴 낸 바위 조각들, 흙이 달라붙은 채 뿌리째 뽑은 풀들, 그리고 떼와 덤불을 샘 속으로 던져 넣어 샘을 틀어막고 그 물의 맑음을 망쳐 놓았다."], ["At length, exhausted by his violent exertions, bathed in sweat, breathless, Orlando sunk panting upon the earth, and lay there insensible three days and three nights.", "마침내 격렬한 노력에 지친 오를란도는 땀에 흠뻑 젖고 숨이 차서 헐떡이며 땅에 쓰러졌고, 거기 사흘 밤낮을 의식 없이 누워 있었다."]], [["The fourth day he started up and seized his arms.", "나흘째 되는 날 그는 벌떡 일어나 무기를 움켜쥐었다."], ["His helmet, his buckler, he cast far from him; his hauberk and his clothes he rent asunder; the fragments were scattered through the wood.", "그는 투구와 방패를 멀리 내던지고, 갑옷과 옷가지를 갈가리 찢어 그 조각들을 숲 곳곳에 흩뿌렸다."], ["In fine, he became a furious madman.", "요컨대 그는 광포한 미치광이가 되었다."], ["His insanity was such that he cared not to retain even his sword.", "그 광기가 어찌나 심했던지 그는 자신의 칼조차 지니려 하지 않았다."], ["But he had no need of Durindana, nor of other arms, to do wonderful things.", "그러나 그가 놀라운 일들을 해내는 데는 두린다나도, 다른 무기도 필요치 않았다."], ["His prodigious strength sufficed.", "그의 엄청난 힘만으로 충분했다."], ["At the first wrench of his mighty arm he tore up a pine-tree by the roots.", "그의 강대한 팔로 한 번 비틀자 소나무 한 그루가 뿌리째 뽑혀 나왔다."], ["Oaks, beeches, maples, whatever he met in his path, yielded in like manner.", "떡갈나무, 너도밤나무, 단풍나무, 그가 지나는 길에 마주치는 것은 무엇이든 똑같은 방식으로 쓰러졌다."], ["The ancient forest soon became as bare as the borders of a morass, where the fowler has cleared away the bushes to spread his nets.", "그 오래된 숲은 이내 사냥꾼이 그물을 치려고 덤불을 쳐낸 늪지의 가장자리처럼 헐벗게 되었다."], ["The shepherds, hearing the horrible crashing in the forest, abandoned their flocks to run and see the cause of this unwonted uproar.", "목동들은 숲에서 들려오는 그 끔찍한 우지끈거리는 소리를 듣고, 양 떼를 버려 둔 채 이 예사롭지 않은 소란의 원인을 보러 달려왔다."], ["By their evil star, or for their sins, they were led thither.", "사나운 운명의 별 때문인지, 혹은 자신들의 죄 때문인지, 그들은 그곳으로 이끌려 왔다."], ["When they saw the furious state the Count was in, and his incredible force, they would fain have fled out of his reach, but in their fears lost their presence of mind.", "백작의 광포한 상태와 그 믿을 수 없는 힘을 본 그들은 그의 손길이 닿지 않는 곳으로 기꺼이 달아나고 싶었으나, 두려움에 정신을 잃고 말았다."], ["The madman pursued them, seized one and rent him limb from limb, as easily as one would pull ripe apples from a tree.", "미치광이는 그들을 뒤쫓아 한 사람을 붙잡아, 마치 나무에서 잘 익은 사과를 따듯 손쉽게 그 팔다리를 갈가리 찢어 놓았다."], ["He took another by the feet, and used him as a club to knock down a third.", "그는 또 다른 사람의 발을 잡아, 그를 곤봉처럼 휘둘러 세 번째 사람을 때려눕혔다."], ["The shepherds fled; but it would have been hard for any to escape, if he had not at that moment left them to throw himself with the same fury upon their flocks.", "목동들은 달아났으나, 만약 그가 바로 그 순간 그들을 내버려두고 같은 광기로 그들의 양 떼에게 달려들지 않았더라면 누구도 빠져나가기 어려웠을 것이다."], ["The peasants, abandoning their ploughs and harrows, mounted on the roofs of buildings and pinnacles of the rocks, afraid to trust themselves even to the oaks and pines.", "농부들은 쟁기와 써레를 내버리고 건물 지붕과 바위 꼭대기로 기어 올라갔는데, 떡갈나무와 소나무조차 믿지 못했다."], ["From such heights they looked on, trembling at the raging fury of the unhappy Orlando.", "그들은 그런 높은 곳에서 불행한 오를란도의 광란하는 분노에 떨며 내려다보았다."], ["His fists, his teeth, his nails, his feet, seize, break, and tear cattle, sheep, and swine; the most swift in flight alone being able to escape him.", "그의 주먹, 그의 이빨, 그의 손톱, 그의 발이 소와 양과 돼지를 붙잡고 부수고 찢었으니, 가장 빨리 달아난 것들만이 그에게서 벗어날 수 있었다."]], [["When at last terror had scattered everything before him, he entered a cottage which was abandoned by its inhabitants, and there found that which served for food.", "마침내 공포가 그의 앞에 있던 모든 것을 흩어 놓았을 때, 그는 주민들이 버리고 간 어느 오두막으로 들어갔고, 거기서 먹을 것으로 쓸 만한 것을 발견했다."], ["His long fast had caused him to feel the most ravenous hunger.", "오랜 굶주림은 그에게 가장 게걸스러운 허기를 느끼게 했다."], ["Seizing whatever he found that was eatable, whether roots, acorns, or bread, raw meat or cooked, he gorged it indiscriminately.", "그는 먹을 수 있는 것이라면 뿌리든, 도토리든, 빵이든, 날고기든 익힌 고기든 가리지 않고 닥치는 대로 움켜쥐어 마구 먹어 치웠다."]], [["Issuing thence again, the frantic Orlando gave chase to whatever living thing he saw, whether men or animals.", "거기서 다시 나온 광란하는 오를란도는 눈에 보이는 살아 있는 것이라면 사람이든 짐승이든 가리지 않고 뒤쫓았다."], ["Sometimes he pursued the deer and hind, sometimes he attacked bears and wolves, and with his naked hands killed and tore them, and devoured their flesh.", "어떤 때는 수사슴과 암사슴을 쫓았고, 어떤 때는 곰과 늑대를 덮쳐 맨손으로 죽이고 찢어 그 살을 먹어 치웠다."]], [["Thus he wandered, from place to place, through France, imperilling his life a thousand ways, yet always preserved by some mysterious providence from a fatal result.", "이렇게 그는 프랑스 곳곳을 이리저리 떠돌며 천 가지 방식으로 목숨을 위태롭게 했으나, 언제나 어떤 알 수 없는 섭리에 의해 치명적인 결말은 면하였다."], ["But here we leave Orlando for a time, that we may record what befell Zerbino and Isabella after their parting with him.", "그러나 여기서 우리는 오를란도를 잠시 떠나, 그가 제르비노와 이사벨라와 헤어진 뒤 그들에게 무슨 일이 일어났는지 기록하고자 한다."]], [["The prince and his fair bride waited, by Orlando’s request, near the scene of the battle for three days, that, if Mandricardo should return, they might inform him where Orlando would give him another meeting.", "왕자와 그의 아름다운 신부는 오를란도의 부탁대로 사흘 동안 전투가 벌어졌던 곳 가까이에서 기다렸다. 혹시 만드리카르도가 돌아오면, 오를란도가 어디서 그와 다시 만나려 하는지 알려주기 위해서였다."], ["At the end of that time their anxiety to know the issue led them to follow Orlando’s traces, which led them at last to the wood where the trees were inscribed with the names of Angelica and Medoro.", "그 기간이 끝나자 결말을 알고 싶은 마음에 그들은 오를란도의 자취를 따라갔고, 그 자취는 마침내 나무마다 안젤리카와 메도로의 이름이 새겨진 그 숲으로 그들을 이끌었다."], ["They remarked how all these inscriptions were defaced, and how the grotto was disordered, and the fountain clogged with rubbish.", "그들은 그 모든 글귀들이 어떻게 지워졌는지, 동굴이 어떻게 헝클어졌는지, 샘이 어떻게 쓰레기로 막혔는지 눈여겨보았다."], ["But that which surprised them and distressed them most of all was to find on the grass the cuirass of Orlando, and not far from it his helmet, the same which the renowned Almontes once wore.", "그러나 무엇보다도 그들을 놀라게 하고 비통하게 한 것은 풀밭에서 오를란도의 흉갑을 발견한 일이었고, 그 멀지 않은 곳에서 그의 투구, 곧 한때 이름 높은 알몬테스가 썼던 바로 그 투구를 발견한 일이었다."]], [["Hearing a horse neigh in the forest, Zerbino turned his eyes in that direction, and saw Brigliadoro, with the bridle yet hanging at the saddle-bow.", "숲에서 말이 우는 소리를 듣고 제르비노는 그쪽으로 눈길을 돌렸고, 안장 앞테에 고삐가 아직 걸려 있는 브릴리아도로를 보았다."], ["He looked round for Durindana, and found that famous sword, without the scabbard, lying on the grass.", "그는 두린다나를 찾아 둘러보다가, 칼집도 없이 풀밭에 놓여 있는 그 유명한 칼을 발견했다."], ["He saw also the fragments of Orlando’s other arms and clothing scattered on all sides over the plain.", "그는 또한 오를란도의 다른 무기들과 옷가지의 조각들이 들판 사방에 흩어져 있는 것도 보았다."], ["Zerbino and Isabella stood in astonishment and grief, not knowing what to think, but little imagining the true cause.", "제르비노와 이사벨라는 놀라움과 슬픔에 잠겨 어찌 생각해야 할지 알 수 없었으나, 그 참된 원인은 거의 짐작하지 못했다."], ["If they had found any marks of blood on the arms or on the fragments of the clothing, they would have supposed him slain, but there were none.", "만약 그들이 무기나 옷 조각에서 핏자국을 발견했더라면 그가 죽임을 당했으리라 여겼겠지만, 핏자국은 전혀 없었다."], ["While they were in this painful uncertainty they saw a young peasant approach.", "그들이 이 괴로운 불확실함에 잠겨 있을 때, 한 젊은 농부가 다가오는 것이 보였다."], ["He, not yet recovered from the terror of the scene, which he had witnessed from the top of a rock, told them the whole of the sad events.", "그는 바위 꼭대기에서 그 장면을 지켜보았던 터라 아직 공포에서 벗어나지 못한 채, 그 슬픈 사건의 자초지종을 그들에게 모두 들려주었다."]], [["Zerbino, with his eyes full of tears, carefully collected all the scattered arms.", "제르비노는 눈에 눈물을 가득 담고, 흩어진 무기들을 모두 정성스레 거두어 모았다. 이사벨라도 그 슬픈 임무를 돕고자 말에서 내렸다."], ["Isabella also dismounted to aid him in the sad duty.", "그들은 그 값진 갑옷의 조각들을 모두 모은 뒤, 그것들을 전리품처럼 소나무에 매달았다."], ["When they had collected all the pieces of that rich armor they hung them like a trophy on a pine; and to prevent their being violated by any passers-by, Zerbino inscribed on the bark this caution: “These are the arms of the Paladin Orlando.”", "그리고 지나가는 누군가가 그것을 훼손하지 못하도록 제르비노는 나무껍질에 이렇게 경고를 새겼다. \"이것은 기사 오를란도의 무기다.\""]], [["Having finished this pious work, he remounted his horse, and just then a knight rode up, and requested Zerbino to tell him the meaning of the trophy.", "이 경건한 일을 마친 그는 다시 말에 올랐고, 바로 그때 한 기사가 말을 타고 다가와 제르비노에게 그 전리품의 뜻을 말해 달라고 청했다. 왕자는 일이 일어난 그대로 사실을 들려주었다."], ["The prince related the facts as they had happened; and Mandricardo, for it was that Saracen knight, full of joy, rushed forward, and seized the sword, saying, “No one can censure me for what I do; this sword is mine; I can take my own wherever I find it.", "그러자 그 사라센 기사, 곧 만드리카르도는 기쁨에 가득 차서 앞으로 달려나가 칼을 움켜쥐며 말했다. \"내가 하는 일을 두고 누구도 나를 나무랄 수 없다."], ["It is plain that Orlando, not daring to defend it against me, has counterfeited madness to excuse him in surrendering it.”", "이 칼은 내 것이니, 내 것은 어디서 찾든 가질 수 있다. 오를란도가 나를 상대로 이것을 지킬 엄두를 내지 못하고, 칼을 넘겨준 변명거리로 광기를 꾸며낸 것이 분명하다.\""]], [["Zerbino vehemently exclaimed, “Touch not that sword.", "제르비노는 격렬하게 외쳤다. \"그 칼에 손대지 마라."], ["Think not to possess it without a contest.", "싸움 없이 그것을 차지하리라 생각지 마라."], ["If it be true that the arms you wear are those of Hector, you must have got them by theft, and not by prowess.”", "네가 두른 갑옷이 헥토르의 것이라는 말이 사실이라면, 너는 그것을 무용이 아니라 도둑질로 얻었으리라.\""]], [["Immediately they attacked one another with the utmost fury.", "곧바로 그들은 더없는 격렬함으로 서로에게 달려들었다."], ["The air resounded with thick-falling blows.", "빗발치듯 쏟아지는 일격에 공기가 울렸다."], ["Zerbino, skilful and alert, evaded for a time with good success the strokes of Durindana; but at length a terrible blow struck him on the neck.", "제르비노는 능숙하고 민첩하여 한동안 두린다나의 일격을 잘 피해냈으나, 마침내 무시무시한 일격이 그의 목을 쳤다."], ["He fell from his horse, and the Tartar king, possessed of the spoils of his victory, rode away.", "그는 말에서 떨어졌고, 타르타르의 왕은 승리의 전리품을 차지하고 말을 달려 떠났다. 제르비노와 이사벨라"]], [["# ZERBINO AND ISABELLA", "# ZERBINO AND ISABELLA"]], [["————\nZerbino’s pain at seeing the Tartar prince go off with the sword surpassed the anguish of his wound; but now the loss of blood so reduced his strength that he could not move from where he fell.", "타르타르의 왕자가 칼을 가지고 떠나는 것을 보는 제르비노의 고통은 상처의 아픔을 넘어섰다. 그러나 이제 출혈로 기력이 너무 쇠하여 그는 쓰러진 자리에서 움직일 수조차 없었다."], ["Isabella, not knowing whither to resort for help, could only bemoan him, and chide her cruel fate.", "어디로 가서 도움을 구해야 할지 모르는 이사벨라는 그저 그를 애도하며 자신의 잔인한 운명을 탓할 뿐이었다. 제르비노가 말했다."], ["Zerbino said, “If I could but leave thee, my best beloved, in some secure abode, it would not distress me to die; but to abandon thee so, without protection, is sad indeed.”", "\"내가 그대를, 나의 가장 사랑하는 이를, 어딘가 안전한 거처에 남겨 둘 수만 있다면 죽는 것도 괴롭지 않으련만. 그러나 그대를 이렇게, 보호자도 없이 버려 두고 가야 하다니 참으로 슬프오.\""], ["She replied, “Think not to leave me, dearest; our souls shall not be parted; this sword will give me the means to follow thee.”", "그녀가 대답했다. \"저를 두고 갈 생각 마세요, 사랑하는 이여."], ["Zerbino’s last words implored her to banish such a thought, but live, and be true to his memory.", "우리 두 영혼은 갈라지지 않을 거예요. 이 칼이 당신을 따를 길을 제게 줄 테니까요.\""], ["Isabella promised, with many tears, to be faithful to him so long as life should last.", "제르비노의 마지막 말은 그녀에게 그런 생각을 떨쳐 버리고 살아남아 자신의 기억에 충실하라고 간청하는 것이었다. 이사벨라는 숱한 눈물을 흘리며, 목숨이 붙어 있는 한 그에게 충실하겠노라 약속했다."]], [["When he ceased to breathe, Isabella’s cries resounded through the forest, and reached the ears of a reverend hermit, who hastened to the spot.", "그가 숨을 거두자 이사벨라의 울음소리가 숲을 울려 퍼졌고, 한 경건한 은둔자의 귀에까지 닿았다. 그는 서둘러 그곳으로 왔다."], ["He soothed and calmed her, urging those consolations which the word of God supplies; and at last brought her to wish for nothing else but to devote herself for the rest of life wholly to religion.", "그는 하느님의 말씀이 주는 위안을 권하며 그녀를 달래고 진정시켰고, 마침내 그녀로 하여금 남은 생을 오로지 신앙에 바치는 것 외에는 아무것도 바라지 않게 만들었다."]], [["As she could not bear the thoughts of leaving her dead lord abandoned, the body was, by the good hermit’s aid, placed upon the horse, and taken to the nearest inhabited place, where a chest was made for it, suitable to be carried with them on their way.", "죽은 자신의 주인을 버려 두고 떠난다는 생각을 견딜 수 없었던 터라, 선량한 은둔자의 도움으로 시신은 말 위에 실려 가장 가까운 마을로 옮겨졌고, 거기서 길을 가는 동안 함께 운반하기에 알맞은 궤가 만들어졌다."], ["The hermit’s plan was to escort his charge to a monastery, not many days’ journey distant, where Isabella resolved to spend the remainder of her days.", "은둔자의 계획은 자신이 맡은 그녀를 며칠 거리에 있는 수도원으로 호위해 가는 것이었고, 이사벨라는 그곳에서 남은 생을 보내기로 결심했다."], ["Thus they travelled day after day, choosing the most retired ways, for the country was full of armed men.", "그리하여 그들은 날마다 길을 떠났는데, 나라 안이 무장한 자들로 가득했으므로 가장 한적한 길을 골랐다."], ["One day a cavalier met them, and barred their way.", "어느 날 한 기사가 그들을 만나 길을 가로막았다."], ["It was no other than Rodomont, king of Algiers, who had just left the camp of Agramant, full of indignation at the treatment he had received from Doralice.", "그는 다름 아닌 알제의 왕 로도몬트였으니, 그는 도랄리체에게서 받은 대우에 분노가 가득한 채 막 아그라만트의 진영을 떠나온 참이었다."], ["At sight of the lovely lady and her reverend attendant, with their horse laden with a burden draped with black, he asked the meaning of their journey.", "그 사랑스러운 여인과 그녀의 경건한 동행, 그리고 검은 천을 두른 짐을 실은 그들의 말을 보고, 그는 그들이 어디로 가는 길인지 물었다."], ["Isabella told him her affliction, and her resolution to renounce the world and devote herself to religion, and to the memory of the friend she had lost.", "이사벨라는 그에게 자신의 비통함과, 세상을 버리고 신앙에, 그리고 잃어버린 벗의 기억에 자신을 바치기로 한 결심을 말했다."], ["Rodomont laughed scornfully at this, and told her that her project was absurd; that charms like hers were meant to be enjoyed, not buried, and that he himself would more than make amends for her dead lover.", "로도몬트는 이를 비웃으며, 그녀의 계획은 어리석은 것이라고, 그녀와 같은 미모는 묻어 둘 것이 아니라 누려야 할 것이라고, 그리고 죽은 연인 대신 자신이 그 이상으로 보상해 주겠노라고 말했다."], ["The monk, who promptly interposed to rebuke this impious talk, was commanded to hold his peace; and still persisting was seized by the knight and hurled over the edge of the cliff, where he fell into the sea, and was drowned.", "이 불경한 말을 꾸짖으려 즉시 끼어든 수도사는 입을 다물라는 명령을 받았고, 그래도 끈질기게 굴자 기사에게 붙들려 절벽 가장자리 너머로 내던져졌으니, 그는 바다로 떨어져 익사하고 말았다."]], [["Rodomont, when he had got rid of the hermit, again applied to the sad lady, heartless with affright, and, in the language used by lovers, said, “she was his very heart, his life, his light.”", "로도몬트는 은둔자를 처치한 뒤, 공포에 넋이 나간 그 슬픈 여인에게 다시 다가가, 연인들이 쓰는 말투로 \"그녀가 자신의 바로 그 심장이요, 자신의 생명이요, 자신의 빛\"이라고 말했다."], ["Having laid aside all violence, he humbly sued that she would accompany him to his retreat, near by.", "그는 온갖 난폭함을 버린 채, 가까이 있는 자신의 거처로 함께 가 달라고 겸손히 간청했다."], ["It was a ruined chapel from which the monks had been driven by the disorders of the time, and which Rodomont had taken possession of.", "그곳은 시국의 혼란으로 수도사들이 쫓겨난 폐허가 된 예배당이었고, 로도몬트가 차지하고 있던 곳이었다."], ["Isabella, who had no choice but to obey, followed him, meditating as she went what resource she could find to escape out of his power, and keep her vow to her dead husband, to be faithful to his memory as long as life should last.", "따르는 것 외에 달리 도리가 없는 이사벨라는 그를 뒤따랐는데, 가는 동안 어떻게 하면 그의 손아귀에서 벗어날 방도를 찾을 수 있을지, 그리고 목숨이 붙어 있는 한 그의 기억에 충실하겠노라 죽은 남편에게 한 맹세를 어떻게 지킬지 골똘히 궁리했다."], ["At length she said, “If, my lord, you will let me go and fulfil my vow, and my intention, as I have already declared it, I will bestow upon you what will be to you of more value than a hundred women’s hearts.", "마침내 그녀가 말했다. \"나리, 만약 제가 이미 말씀드린 대로 가서 제 맹세와 제 뜻을 이루게 해 주신다면, 백 명의 여인의 마음보다 나리께 더 값진 것을 드리겠습니다."], ["I know an herb, and I have seen it on our way, which, rightly prepared, affords a juice of such power, that the flesh, if laved with it, becomes impenetrable to sword or fire.", "저는 어떤 약초를 알고 있는데, 오는 길에 그것을 보았습니다. 제대로 조제하면 그 즙이 어찌나 강력한지, 살에 그것을 바르면 칼이나 불로도 뚫을 수 없게 됩니다."], ["This liquor I can make, and will, to-day, if you will accept my offer; and when you have seen its virtue you will value it more than if all Europe were made your own.”", "이 약물을 저는 만들 수 있고, 나리께서 제 제안을 받아들이신다면 오늘 만들어 드리겠습니다. 그 효험을 보시면 나리께서는 온 유럽을 손에 넣는 것보다 그것을 더 귀하게 여기실 것입니다.\""]], [["Rodomont, at hearing this, readily promised all that was asked, so eager was he to learn a secret that would make him as Achilles was of yore.", "이 말을 듣고 로도몬트는, 옛날의 아킬레우스처럼 자신을 만들어 줄 비결을 배우려는 열망에 사로잡혀, 청하는 것을 모두 선뜻 약속했다."], ["Isabella, having collected such herbs as she thought proper, and boiled them, with certain mysterious signs and words, at length declared her labor done, and, as a test, offered to try its virtue on herself.", "이사벨라는 알맞다고 여긴 약초들을 모아 끓이며 어떤 신비로운 표시와 말들을 곁들였고, 마침내 자신의 작업이 끝났음을 알렸다. 그리고 시험 삼아 그 효험을 자기 자신에게 시험해 보겠다고 제안했다."], ["She bathed her neck and bosom with the liquor, and then called on Rodomont to smite with all his force, and see whether his sword had power to harm.", "그녀는 그 약물로 자신의 목과 가슴을 적신 뒤, 로도몬트에게 온 힘을 다해 내리쳐 그의 칼이 해를 입힐 힘이 있는지 보라고 청했다."], ["The pagan, who during the preparations had taken frequent draughts of wine, and scarce knew what he did, drew his sword at the word, and struck across her neck with all his might, and the fair head leapt sundered from the snowy neck and breast.", "조제하는 동안 자주 포도주를 들이켜 자기가 무슨 짓을 하는지도 거의 분간하지 못하던 그 이교도는, 그 말에 칼을 뽑아 온 힘을 다해 그녀의 목을 가로질러 쳤고, 그 아름다운 머리는 눈처럼 흰 목과 가슴에서 잘려 나가 떨어졌다."]], [["Rude and unfeeling as he was, the pagan knight lamented bitterly this sad result.", "거칠고 무정한 자였으나, 그 이교도 기사는 이 슬픈 결말을 비통하게 한탄했다."], ["To honor her memory he resolved to do a work as unparalleled as her devotion.", "그녀의 기억을 기리고자 그는 그녀의 헌신만큼이나 비할 데 없는 일을 하기로 결심했다."], ["From all parts round he caused laborers to be brought, and had a tower built to enclose the chapel, within which the remains of Zerbino and Isabella were entombed.", "그는 사방에서 일꾼들을 불러들여, 예배당을 둘러싸는 탑을 세우게 했으며, 그 안에 제르비노와 이사벨라의 유해를 안치했다."], ["Across the stream which flowed near by he built a bridge, scarce two yards wide, and added neither parapet nor rail.", "가까이 흐르는 시냇물 위에는 폭이 채 두 야드도 안 되는 다리를 세웠는데, 난간도 손잡이도 두지 않았다."], ["On the top of the tower a sentry was placed, who, when any traveller approached the bridge, gave notice to his master.", "탑 꼭대기에는 파수꾼을 두어, 어떤 길손이든 다리에 다가오면 주인에게 알리게 했다."], ["Rodomont thereupon sallied out, and defied the approaching knight to fight him upon the bridge, where any chance step a little aside would plunge the rider headlong in the stream.", "그러면 로도몬트가 뛰쳐나가, 다가오는 기사에게 다리 위에서 싸우자고 도전했으니, 그 위에서는 옆으로 조금만 발을 헛디뎌도 말 탄 자가 곤두박질쳐 시내로 빠지게 되어 있었다."], ["This bridge he vowed to keep until a thousand suits of armor should be won from conquered knights, wherewith to build a trophy to his victim and her lord.", "그는 정복한 기사들에게서 천 벌의 갑옷을 얻어 자신의 희생자와 그녀의 주인을 위한 전승비를 세우기 전까지는 이 다리를 지키겠노라 맹세했다."]], [["Within ten days the bridge was built, and the tower was in progress.", "열흘 만에 다리가 세워졌고, 탑도 공사가 진행되고 있었다."], ["In a short time many knights, either seeking the shortest route, or tempted by a desire of adventure, had made the attempt to pass the bridge.", "머지않아 많은 기사들이, 가장 짧은 길을 찾아서든 모험에 대한 욕망에 이끌려서든, 그 다리를 건너려 시도했다."], ["All, without exception, had lost either arms or life, or both; some falling before Rodomont’s lance, others precipitated into the river.", "예외 없이 모두가 무기든 목숨이든, 혹은 둘 다를 잃었다. 어떤 이는 로도몬트의 창에 쓰러졌고, 어떤 이는 강물로 곤두박질쳤다."], ["One day, as Rodomont stood urging his workmen, it chanced that Orlando in his furious mood came thither, and approached the bridge.", "어느 날 로도몬트가 서서 일꾼들을 재촉하고 있을 때, 마침 광기에 사로잡힌 오를란도가 그곳에 와서 다리로 다가왔다. 로도몬트가 그에게 소리쳤다."], ["Rodomont halloed to him, “Halt, churl; presume not to set foot upon that bridge; it was not made for such as you!”", "\"멈춰라, 이 촌놈아. 감히 저 다리에 발을 들여놓을 생각 마라."], ["Orlando took no notice, but pressed on.", "저것은 너 같은 자를 위해 만든 것이 아니다!\""], ["Just then a gentle damsel rode up.", "오를란도는 아랑곳하지 않고 밀고 나아갔다."], ["It was Flordelis, who was seeking her Florismart.", "바로 그때 한 정숙한 처녀가 말을 타고 다가왔다."], ["She saw Orlando, and, in spite of his strange appearance, recognized him.", "그녀는 자신의 플로리스마르트를 찾고 있던 플로르델리스였다."], ["Rodomont, not used to have his commands disobeyed, laid hands on the madman, and would have thrown him into the river, but to his astonishment found himself in the grip of one not so easily disposed of.", "그녀는 오를란도를 보았고, 그의 기이한 몰골에도 불구하고 그를 알아보았다. 자신의 명령이 거역당하는 데 익숙지 않은 로도몬트는 그 미치광이에게 손을 대어 강물에 던져 넣으려 했으나, 놀랍게도 그렇게 쉽사리 처리할 수 없는 자의 손아귀에 자신이 잡혀 있음을 깨달았다."], ["“How can a fool have such strength?”", "\"어떻게 바보가 이런 힘을 가졌단 말인가?\""], ["he growled between his teeth.", "그가 이를 갈며 으르렁거렸다."], ["Flordelis stopped to see the issue, where each of these two puissant warriors strove to throw the other from the bridge.", "플로르델리스는 멈추어 서서 결말을 지켜보았으니, 그곳에서는 이 두 막강한 전사가 서로를 다리에서 떨어뜨리려 안간힘을 쓰고 있었다."], ["Orlando at last had strength enough to lift his foe with all his armor, and fling him over the side, but had not wit to clear himself from him, so both fell together.", "마침내 오를란도가 적을 갑옷째 들어 올려 다리 너머로 던질 만큼 힘을 냈으나, 자신을 그에게서 떼어낼 만한 지혜는 없었기에 둘은 함께 떨어지고 말았다."], ["High flashed the wave as they together smote its surface.", "둘이 함께 수면을 치자 물보라가 높이 솟구쳤다."], ["Here Orlando had the advantage; he was naked, and could swim like a fish.", "여기서는 오를란도가 유리했다. 그는 벌거벗은 몸이었고 물고기처럼 헤엄칠 수 있었다."], ["He soon reached the bank, and, careless of praise or blame, stopped not to see what came of the adventure.", "그는 곧 강기슭에 닿았고, 칭찬도 비난도 개의치 않은 채 그 모험의 결말이 어찌 되는지 보려고 멈추지 않았다."], ["Rodomont, entangled with his armor, escaped with difficulty to the bank.", "갑옷에 발이 묶인 로도몬트는 가까스로 강기슭으로 빠져나왔다."], ["Meantime, Flordelis passed the bridge unchallenged.", "그 사이 플로르델리스는 아무런 제지 없이 다리를 건넜다."]], [["After long wandering without success she returned to Paris, and there found the object of her search; for Florismart, after the fall of Albracca, had repaired thither.", "성과 없이 오래 헤맨 끝에 그녀는 파리로 돌아왔고, 거기서 자신이 찾던 이를 발견했다. 플로리스마르트가 알브라카가 함락된 뒤 그곳으로 와 있었기 때문이다."], ["The joy of meeting was clouded to Florismart by the news which Flordelis brought of Orlando’s wretched plight.", "다시 만난 기쁨도, 오를란도의 비참한 처지에 관해 플로르델리스가 전한 소식에 플로리스마르트에게는 흐려지고 말았다."], ["The last she had seen of him was when he fell with Rodomont into the stream.", "그녀가 그를 마지막으로 본 것은 그가 로도몬트와 함께 시내로 떨어졌을 때였다."], ["Florismart, who loved Orlando like a brother, resolved to set out immediately, under the guidance of the lady, to find him, and bring him where he might receive the treatment suited to his case.", "오를란도를 형제처럼 사랑하던 플로리스마르트는 그 여인의 안내를 받아 즉시 길을 떠나 그를 찾아내어, 그의 상태에 맞는 치료를 받을 수 있는 곳으로 데려가기로 결심했다."], ["A few days brought them to the place where they found the Tartar king still guarding the bridge.", "며칠 만에 그들은 타르타르의 왕이 여전히 다리를 지키고 있는 그곳에 이르렀다."], ["The usual challenge and defiance was made, and the knights rode to encounter one another on the bridge.", "늘 하던 대로 도전과 응전이 이루어졌고, 두 기사는 다리 위에서 맞붙으러 말을 달렸다."], ["At the first encounter both horses were overthrown; and, having no space to regain their footing, fell with their riders into the water.", "첫 격돌에서 두 말이 모두 쓰러졌고, 발을 다시 디딜 공간이 없었으므로 말과 기사가 함께 물속으로 떨어졌다."], ["Rodomont, who knew the soundings of the stream, soon recovered the land; but Florismart was carried downward by the current, and landed at last on a bank of mud where his horse could hardly find footing.", "시내의 수심을 알고 있던 로도몬트는 곧 뭍으로 올라왔으나, 플로리스마르트는 물살에 떠내려가 마침내 말이 발 디딜 곳을 거의 찾지 못하는 진흙 둑에 닿았다."], ["Flordelis, who watched the battle from the bridge, seeing her lover in this piteous case, exclaimed aloud, “Ah!", "다리에서 싸움을 지켜보던 플로르델리스는 자신의 연인이 이 가련한 처지에 빠진 것을 보고 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"아!"], ["Rodomont, for love of her whom dead you honor, have pity on me, who love this knight, and slay him not.", "로도몬트여, 당신이 죽어서도 기리는 그녀를 사랑하는 마음으로, 이 기사를 사랑하는 저를 가엾이 여겨 그를 죽이지 말아 주세요."], ["Let it suffice he yields his armor to the pile, and none more glorious will it bear than his.”", "그가 자신의 갑옷을 그 무더기에 바치는 것으로 족하게 해 주세요. 그 어떤 것도 그의 갑옷보다 더 영광스럽지는 못할 테니까요.\""], ["Her prayer, so well directed, touched the pagan’s heart, though hard to move, and he lent his aid to help the knight to land.", "그토록 잘 겨눈 그녀의 간청은, 비록 움직이기 어려운 것이었으나 그 이교도의 마음을 움직였고, 그는 기사가 뭍에 오르도록 도움을 주었다."], ["He kept him a prisoner, however, and added his armor to the pile.", "그러나 그는 그를 포로로 붙잡아 두었고, 그의 갑옷을 그 무더기에 더했다."], ["Flordelis, with a heavy heart, went her way.", "플로르델리스는 무거운 마음으로 가던 길을 떠났다."]], [["We must now return to Rogero, who, when we parted with him, was engaged in an adventure which arrested his progress to the monastery whither he was bound with the intention of receiving baptism, and thus qualifying himself to demand Bradamante as his bride.", "이제 우리는 로제로에게로 돌아가야 한다. 우리가 그와 헤어졌을 때, 그는 자신의 진로를 가로막은 한 모험에 얽혀 있었다."], ["On his way he met with Mandricardo, and the quarrel was revived respecting the right to wear the badge of Hector.", "그는 세례를 받아 브라다만테를 신부로 맞을 자격을 갖추려는 뜻으로 수도원을 향해 가던 중이었다."], ["After a warm discussion both parties agreed to submit the question to King Agramant, and for that purpose took their way to the Saracen camp.", "가는 길에 그는 만드리카르도를 만났고, 헥토르의 표장을 두를 권리를 둘러싼 다툼이 다시 일어났다. 격렬한 논쟁 끝에 양측은 그 문제를 아그라만트 왕에게 맡기기로 합의했고, 그 목적으로 사라센 진영으로 향했다."], ["Here they met Gradasso, who had his controversy also with Mandricardo.", "거기서 그들은 그라다소를 만났는데, 그 또한 만드리카르도와 다툼이 있었다."], ["This warrior claimed the sword of Orlando, denying the right of Mandricardo to possess it in virtue of his having found it abandoned by its owner.", "이 전사는 오를란도의 칼을 주장하며, 그 칼이 주인에게 버림받은 것을 발견했다는 이유로 만드리카르도가 그것을 차지할 권리를 부인했다."], ["King Agramant strove in vain to reconcile these quarrels, and was forced at last to consent that the points in dispute should be settled by one combat, in which Mandricardo should meet one of the other champions, to whom should be committed the cause of both.", "아그라만트 왕은 이 다툼들을 화해시키려 애썼으나 헛수고였고, 마침내 분쟁의 쟁점들을 한 차례의 결투로 해결하는 데 동의할 수밖에 없었다. 그 결투에서 만드리카르도는 다른 두 용사 가운데 한 사람과 맞붙기로 하였고, 그 사람에게 양측의 주장이 맡겨지게 되었다."], ["Rogero was chosen by lot to maintain Gradasso’s cause and his own.", "제비뽑기로 로제로가 그라다소의 주장과 자신의 주장을 함께 옹호하게 되었다."], ["Great preparations were made for this signal contest.", "이 중대한 결투를 위해 큰 준비가 이루어졌다."], ["On the appointed day it was fought in the presence of Agramant, and of the whole army.", "정해진 날, 아그라만트와 전 군대가 지켜보는 가운데 결투가 벌어졌다. 로제로가 승리했다."], ["Rogero won it; and Mandricardo, the conqueror of Hector’s arms, the challenger of Orlando, and the slayer of Zerbino, lost his life.", "그리고 헥토르의 무기를 차지한 자이자, 오를란도에게 도전한 자이자, 제르비노를 죽인 자인 만드리카르도는 목숨을 잃었다."], ["Gradasso received Durindana as his prize, which lost half its value in his eyes, since it was won by another’s prowess, not his own.", "그라다소는 두린다나를 상으로 받았으나, 그것이 자신의 무용이 아니라 남의 무용으로 얻어진 것이었기에 그의 눈에는 그 가치가 절반으로 떨어졌다."]], [["Rogero, though victorious, was severely wounded, and lay helpless many weeks in the camp of Agramant, while Bradamante, ignorant of the cause of his delay, expected him at Montalban.", "로제로는 승리했음에도 심한 부상을 입어, 아그라만트의 진영에서 여러 주 동안 무력하게 누워 있었다. 한편 브라다만테는 그가 늦어지는 까닭을 모른 채 몬탈반에서 그를 기다리고 있었다."], ["Thither he had promised to repair in fifteen days, or twenty at furthest, hoping to have obtained by that time an honorable discharge from his obligations to the Saracen commander.", "그는 보름 안에, 늦어도 스무날 안에 그곳으로 가겠노라 약속했는데, 그때쯤이면 사라센 사령관에 대한 자신의 의무에서 명예롭게 벗어날 수 있으리라 기대했던 것이다."], ["The twenty days were passed, and a month more, and still Rogero came not, nor did any tidings reach Bradamante accounting for his absence.", "스무날이 지나고, 다시 한 달이 더 지나도 로제로는 오지 않았고, 그가 보이지 않는 까닭을 알려 줄 어떤 소식도 브라다만테에게 닿지 않았다."], ["At the end of that time, a wandering knight brought news of the famous combat, and of Rogero’s wound.", "그 기간이 끝날 무렵, 한 떠돌이 기사가 그 유명한 결투와 로제로의 부상에 관한 소식을 전했다."], ["He added, what alarmed Bradamante still more, that Marphisa, a female warrior, young and fair, was in attendance on the wounded knight.", "그는 브라다만테를 더욱 불안하게 만드는 말을 덧붙였으니, 젊고 아름다운 여전사 마르피사가 그 부상당한 기사를 간호하고 있다는 것이었다."], ["He added that the whole army expected that, as soon as Rogero’s wounds were healed, the pair would be united in marriage.", "그는 또한 로제로의 상처가 낫는 대로 그 두 사람이 혼인으로 맺어지리라고 온 군대가 기대하고 있다는 말도 덧붙였다."]], [["Bradamante, distressed by this news, though she believed it but in part, resolved to go immediately and see for herself.", "이 소식에 괴로워하던 브라다만테는, 비록 그것을 일부만 믿었으나, 즉시 가서 직접 확인하기로 결심했다."], ["She mounted Rabican, the horse of Astolpho, which he had committed to her care, and took with her the lance of gold, though unaware of its wonderful powers.", "그녀는 아스톨포가 자신에게 맡긴 말 라비칸에 올랐고, 그 놀라운 힘은 알지 못한 채 황금 창을 챙겼다."], ["Thus accoutred, she left the castle, and took the road toward Paris and the camp of the Saracens.", "이렇게 차림을 갖춘 그녀는 성을 떠나 파리와 사라센 진영을 향한 길로 들어섰다."]], [["Marphisa, whose devotion to Rogero in his illness had so excited the jealousy of Bradamante, was the twin sister of Rogero.", "로제로의 병중에 그를 향한 헌신으로 브라다만테의 질투를 그토록 자극했던 마르피사는, 사실 로제로의 쌍둥이 누이였다."], ["She, with him, had been taken in charge when an infant by Atlantes, the magician, but while yet a child she had been stolen away by an Arab tribe.", "그녀는 로제로와 함께 갓난아이였을 때 마법사 아틀란테스의 보살핌을 받았으나, 아직 어린아이였을 적에 한 아랍 부족에게 도둑맞았다."], ["Adopted by their chief, she had early learned horsemanship and skill in arms, and at this time had come to the camp of Agramant with no other view than to see and test for herself the prowess of the warriors of either camp, whose fame rang through the world.", "그들의 우두머리에게 입양된 그녀는 일찍이 마술(馬術)과 무예를 익혔고, 이 무렵 양 진영 전사들의 무용을 직접 보고 시험해 보겠다는 것 외에는 다른 뜻 없이 아그라만트의 진영에 와 있었으니, 그들의 명성이 온 세상에 울려 퍼지고 있었기 때문이다."], ["Arriving at the very moment of the late encounter, the name of Rogero, and some few facts of his story which she learned, were enough to suggest the idea that it was her brother whom she saw victorious in the single combat.", "바로 그 앞선 결투의 순간에 도착한 그녀는, 로제로라는 이름과 자신이 알게 된 그의 이야기 몇 가지만으로도, 일대일 결투에서 승리하는 모습을 본 자가 자신의 오라비라는 생각을 떠올리기에 충분했다."], ["Inquiry satisfied the two of their near kindred, and from that moment Marphisa devoted herself to the care of her new-found and much-loved brother.", "알아본 결과 두 사람은 자신들이 가까운 혈육임을 확인했고, 그 순간부터 마르피사는 새로 찾은, 그토록 사랑하는 오라비를 돌보는 데 자신을 바쳤다."]], [["In those moments of seclusion Rogero informed his sister of what he had learned of their parentage from old Atlantes.", "그렇게 은거하던 동안 로제로는 늙은 아틀란테스에게서 자신들의 혈통에 관해 알게 된 바를 누이에게 알려 주었다."], ["Rogero, their father, a Christian knight, had won the heart of Galaciella, daughter of the Sultan of Africa, and sister of King Agramant, converted her to the Christian faith, and secretly married her.", "그들의 아버지인 로제로는 기독교 기사였는데, 아프리카 술탄의 딸이자 아그라만트 왕의 누이인 갈라키엘라의 마음을 얻어 그녀를 기독교 신앙으로 개종시키고 은밀히 혼인했다."], ["The Sultan, enraged at his daughter’s marriage, drove her husband into exile, and caused her with her infant children, Rogero and Marphisa, to be placed in a boat and committed to the winds and waves, to perish; from which fate they were saved by Atlantes.", "딸의 혼인에 격노한 술탄은 사위를 추방하고, 딸을 그 갓난 자식들인 로제로와 마르피사와 함께 배에 태워 바람과 파도에 맡겨 죽게 했으나, 그들은 아틀란테스에 의해 그 운명에서 구해졌다. 이를 듣고 마르피사가 외쳤다."], ["On hearing this, Marphisa exclaimed, “How can you, brother, leave our parents unavenged so long, and even submit to serve the son of the tyrant who so wronged them?”", "\"오라버니, 어찌 그토록 오랫동안 우리 부모님의 원수를 갚지 않은 채 두고, 게다가 그분들에게 그토록 몹쓸 짓을 한 폭군의 아들을 섬기기까지 한단 말이에요?\""], ["Rogero replied that it was but lately he had learned the full truth; that when he learned it he was already embarked with Agramant, from whom he had received knighthood, and that he only waited for a suitable opportunity when he might with honor desert his standard, and at the same time return to the faith of his fathers.", "로제로는 자신이 온전한 진실을 안 것은 최근의 일이라고, 그것을 알았을 때는 이미 아그라만트와 함께 배에 오른 뒤였고 그에게서 기사 작위를 받은 뒤였노라고, 그래서 자신은 명예롭게 그의 깃발을 버리고 동시에 조상의 신앙으로 돌아갈 수 있는 알맞은 기회만을 기다리고 있노라고 대답했다."], ["Marphisa hailed this resolution with joy, and declared her intention to join with him in embracing the Christian faith. ————", "마르피사는 이 결심을 기쁘게 맞이하며, 자신도 그와 함께 기독교 신앙을 받아들이겠다는 뜻을 밝혔다."]], [["We left Bradamante when, mounted on Rabican and armed with Astolpho’s lance, she rode forth, determined to learn the cause of Rogero’s long absence.", "우리가 브라다만테와 헤어진 것은, 그녀가 라비칸에 올라 아스톨포의 창으로 무장하고 로제로의 오랜 부재의 까닭을 알아내기로 작정한 채 말을 달려 나갔을 때였다."], ["One day, as she rode, she met a damsel, of visage and of manners fair, but overcome with grief.", "어느 날, 말을 달리던 그녀는 한 처녀를 만났는데, 얼굴과 거동이 고왔으나 슬픔에 짓눌려 있었다."], ["It was Flordelis, who was seeking far and near a champion capable of liberating and avenging her lord.", "그녀는 자신의 주인을 구하고 그 원수를 갚아 줄 용사를 멀리까지 찾아 헤매던 플로르델리스였다."], ["Flordelis marked the approaching warrior, and, judging from appearances, thought she had found the champion she sought.", "플로르델리스는 다가오는 전사를 눈여겨보았고, 겉모습으로 미루어 자신이 찾던 용사를 만났다고 생각했다. \"기사님,\""], ["“Are you, Sir Knight,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["she said, “so daring and so kind as to take up my cause against a fierce and cruel warrior who has made prisoner of my lord, and forced me thus to be a wanderer and a suppliant?”", "\"제 주인을 포로로 삼고 저를 이렇게 떠돌이요 탄원자로 만든, 사납고 잔인한 한 전사를 상대로 제 사정을 맡아 주실 만큼 대담하고 자비로우신가요?\""], ["Then she related the events which had happened at the bridge.", "그러고는 다리에서 일어났던 일들을 들려주었다."], ["Bradamante, to whom noble enterprises were always welcome, readily embraced this, and the rather as in her gloomy forebodings she felt as if Rogero was forever lost to her.", "고귀한 모험이라면 언제나 반갑게 여기던 브라다만테는 이를 선뜻 받아들였으니, 더욱이 자신의 어두운 예감 속에서 로제로가 영영 자신에게서 떠나 버린 것만 같았기 때문이다."]], [["Next day the two arrived at the bridge.", "이튿날 두 사람은 다리에 다다랐다."], ["The sentry descried them approaching, and gave notice to his lord, who thereupon donned his armor and went forth to meet them.", "파수꾼이 그들이 다가오는 것을 보고 주인에게 알렸고, 주인은 이에 갑옷을 차려입고 그들을 맞으러 나왔다."], ["Here, as usual, he called on the advancing warrior to yield his horse and arms an oblation to the tomb.", "여기서 그는 늘 하던 대로, 다가오는 전사에게 말과 무기를 무덤에 바치는 제물로 내놓으라고 요구했다."], ["Bradamante replied, asking by what right he called on the innocent to do penance for his crime.", "브라다만테는 무슨 권리로 죄 없는 자에게 그의 죄에 대한 속죄를 시키느냐고 물으며 대답했다."], ["“Your life and your armor,”", "\"네 목숨과 네 갑옷이야말로,\""], ["she added, “are the fittest offering to her tomb, and I, a woman, the fittest champion to take them.”", "그녀가 덧붙였다. \"그녀의 무덤에 가장 어울리는 제물이며, 여인인 내가 그것을 취하기에 가장 어울리는 용사다.\""], ["With that she couched her spear, spurred her horse, and ran to the encounter.", "그 말과 함께 그녀는 창을 겨누고 말에 박차를 가하며 격돌하러 달려 나갔다."], ["King Rodomont came on with speed.", "로도몬트 왕도 빠르게 다가왔다."], ["The trampling sounded on the bridge like thunder.", "다리 위에서 짓밟는 소리가 천둥처럼 울렸다."], ["It took but a moment to decide the contest.", "승부를 가르는 데는 한순간이면 족했다."], ["The golden lance did its office, and that fierce Moor, so renowned in tourney, lay extended on the bridge.", "황금 창이 제 몫을 다하자, 마상 시합에서 그토록 명성 높던 그 사나운 무어인이 다리 위에 길게 뻗어 누웠다."], ["“Who is the loser now?”", "\"이제 패자는 누구냐?\""], ["said Bradamante; but Rodomont, amazed that a woman’s hand should have laid him low, could not or would not answer.", "브라다만테가 말했다. 그러나 여인의 손에 자신이 쓰러졌다는 데 어안이 벙벙해진 로도몬트는 대답할 수 없었거나, 대답하려 하지 않았다."], ["Silent and sad, he raised himself, unbound his helm and mail, and flung them against the tomb; then, sullen and on foot, left the ground; but first gave orders to one of his squires to release all his prisoners.", "말없이 침울하게 그는 몸을 일으켜 투구와 갑옷을 풀어 무덤에 내던졌고, 그러고는 시무룩하게 걸어서 그 자리를 떠났다. 그러나 떠나기에 앞서 그는 자신의 종자 하나에게 모든 포로들을 풀어 주라고 명했다."], ["They had been sent off to Africa.", "그들은 아프리카로 보내져 있었다."], ["Besides Florismart, there were Sansonnet and Oliver, who had ridden that way in quest of Orlando, and had both in turn been overthrown in the encounter.", "플로리스마르트 외에도 산소네트와 올리버가 있었으니, 이들은 오를란도를 찾아 그 길로 말을 달리다가 둘 다 차례로 그 격돌에서 쓰러졌던 것이다."]], [["Bradamante after her victory resumed her route, and in due time reached the Christian camp, where she readily learned an explanation of the mystery which had caused her so much anxiety.", "브라다만테는 승리한 뒤 다시 길을 떠났고, 때가 되어 기독교 진영에 다다랐다. 거기서 그녀는 자신을 그토록 불안하게 했던 수수께끼에 대한 설명을 쉽게 얻었다."], ["Rogero and his fair and brave sister, Marphisa, were too illustrious by their station and exploits not to be the frequent topic of discourse even among their adversaries, and all that Bradamante was anxious to know reached her ear, almost without inquiry.", "로제로와 그의 아름답고 용감한 누이 마르피사는 그 지위와 공적으로 너무도 빛나는 인물들이었기에, 적진에서조차 자주 이야깃거리가 되지 않을 수 없었고, 브라다만테가 알고자 애쓰던 모든 것이 거의 묻지 않아도 그녀의 귀에 들어왔다."]], [["We now return to Gradasso, who by Rogero’s victory had been made possessor of Durindana.", "이제 우리는 그라다소에게로 돌아간다. 그는 로제로의 승리로 두린다나의 소유자가 되었다."], ["There now only remained to him to seek the horse of Rinaldo; and the challenge, given and accepted, was yet to be fought with that warrior, for it had been interrupted by the arts of Malagigi.", "이제 그에게 남은 것은 리날도의 말을 찾는 일뿐이었다. 그리고 주고받은 도전은 그 전사와 아직 치러야 할 것이었으니, 그 도전이 말라지지의 술수로 중단되었던 까닭이다."], ["Gradasso now sought another meeting with Rinaldo, and met with no reluctance on his part.", "그라다소는 이제 리날도와 다시 만나기를 청했고, 리날도 쪽에서도 꺼리지 않았다."], ["As the combat was for the possession of Bayard, the knights dismounted and fought on foot.", "그 결투는 바야르드를 차지하기 위한 것이었으므로, 두 기사는 말에서 내려 도보로 싸웠다."], ["Long time the battle lasted.", "싸움은 오래 이어졌다."], ["Rinaldo, knowing well the deadly stroke of Durindana, used all his art to parry or avoid its blow.", "두린다나의 치명적인 일격을 잘 아는 리날도는 그 타격을 막거나 피하려고 온갖 재주를 다 부렸다."], ["Gradasso struck with might and main, but wellnigh all his strokes were spent in air, or if they smote they fell obliquely and did little harm.", "그라다소는 힘껏 내리쳤으나, 그의 일격은 거의 다 허공에 흩어졌고, 설령 닿더라도 비스듬히 떨어져 별다른 해를 입히지 못했다."]], [["Thus had they fought long, glancing at one another’s eyes, and seeing naught else, when their attention was arrested perforce by a strange noise.", "이렇게 그들은 서로의 눈을 노려보며 다른 것은 아무것도 보지 못한 채 오래도록 싸웠는데, 그때 어떤 기이한 소리에 어쩔 수 없이 그들의 주의가 쏠렸다. 그들은 돌아보았고, 선량한 바야르드가 괴물 같은 새 한 마리에게 공격당하는 것을 보았다."], ["They turned, and beheld the good Bayard attacked by a monstrous bird.", "어쩌면 그것은 새였을지도 모른다. 그렇게 보였으니 말이다."], ["Perhaps it was a bird, for such it seemed; but when or where such a bird was ever seen I have nowhere read, except in Turpin; and I am inclined to believe that it was not a bird, but a fiend, evoked from underground by Malagigi, and thither sent on purpose to interrupt the fight.", "그러나 그런 새가 언제 어디서 본 적이 있는지 나는 투르팽 말고는 어디서도 읽은 바가 없다. 나로서는 그것이 새가 아니라, 말라지지가 땅 밑에서 불러내어 그 싸움을 중단시킬 목적으로 그곳에 보낸 악귀였다고 믿고 싶다."], ["Whether a fiend or a fowl, the monster flew right at Bayard, and clapped his wings in his face.", "악귀였든 새였든, 그 괴물은 곧장 바야르드에게 날아들어 그 얼굴에 날개를 퍼덕였다."], ["Thereat the steed broke loose, and ran madly across the plain, pursued by the bird, till Bayard plunged into the wood, and was lost to sight.", "그러자 군마는 풀려나 미친 듯이 들판을 가로질러 달렸고, 그 새에게 쫓겨 마침내 숲 속으로 뛰어들어 시야에서 사라졌다."]], [["Rinaldo and Gradasso, seeing Bayard’s escape, agreed to suspend their battle till they could recover the horse, the object of contention.", "리날도와 그라다소는 바야르드가 달아나는 것을 보고, 다툼의 대상인 그 말을 되찾을 때까지 싸움을 미루기로 합의했다. 그라다소는 자신의 군마에 올라 숲 속으로 들어간 바야르드의 발자국을 따라갔다."], ["Gradasso mounted his steed, and followed the foot-marks of Bayard into the forest.", "그 어느 때보다 심란해진 리날도는 그 자리에 남았으니, 그라다소가 말을 찾으면 그것을 데리고 그곳으로 돌아오겠노라 약속했기 때문이다. 그는 오래 찾은 끝에 정말로 말을 찾아냈으니, 운 좋게도 말이 우는 소리를 들었던 것이다."], ["Rinaldo, never more vexed in spirit, remained at the spot, Gradasso having promised to return thither with the horse, if he found him.", "이리하여 그는 자기 나라에서 군대를 이끌고 와 프랑스를 침공한 두 가지 목표를 모두 손에 넣게 되었다. 그는 리날도를 두고 온 곳으로 바야르드를 도로 데려가겠다는 약속을 잊지 않았으나, 그저 이렇게 중얼거릴 뿐이었다."], ["He did find him, after long search, for he had the good fortune to hear him neigh.", "\"이제 이놈을 손에 넣었으니, 내가 이놈을 내줄 거라고 기대하는 자는 나를 잘 모르는 것이다."], ["Thus he became possessed of both the objects for which he had led an army from his own country, and invaded France.", "리날도가 이 말을 원한다면, 내가 프랑스에서 이놈을 찾아 헤맸듯이 인도에서 이놈을 찾으라고 해라.\" 그러고는 자신의 배들이 정박해 있는 아를로 부지런히 떠나갔다."], ["He did not forget his promise to bring Bayard back to the place where he had left Rinaldo, but only muttering, “Now I have got him, he little knows me who expects me to give him up; if Rinaldo wants the horse let him seek him in India, as I have sought him in France,”—he made the best of his way to Arles, where his vessels lay; and in possession of the two objects of his ambition, the horse and the sword, sailed away to his own country.", "그리고 자신의 야망의 두 목표인 말과 칼을 손에 넣은 채, 배를 타고 자기 나라로 떠나갔다. 아비시니아의 아스톨포"]], [["# ASTOLPHO IN ABYSSINIA", "# ASTOLPHO IN ABYSSINIA"]], [["————\nWhen we last parted with the adventurous paladin Astolpho, he was just commencing that flight over the countries of the world from which he promised himself so much gratification.", "우리가 모험심 강한 기사 아스톨포와 마지막으로 헤어졌을 때, 그는 세계 각국의 상공을 가로지르는 그 비행을 막 시작하던 참이었으니, 그 비행에서 그는 큰 즐거움을 기대하고 있었다."], ["Our readers are aware that the eagle and the falcon have not so swift a flight as the Hippogriff on which Astolpho rode.", "독수리도 매도 아스톨포가 탄 히포그리프만큼 빠르게 날지는 못한다는 것을 우리 독자들은 알고 있다."], ["It was not long, therefore, before the paladin, directing his course toward the southeast, arrived over that part of Africa where the great river Nile has its source.", "따라서 그 기사가 남동쪽으로 진로를 잡아, 큰 강 나일이 발원하는 아프리카의 그 지역 상공에 다다르기까지는 오래 걸리지 않았다."], ["Here he alighted, and found himself in the neighborhood of the capital of Abyssinia, ruled by Senapus, whose riches and power were immense.", "여기서 그는 내려앉았고, 자신이 세나푸스가 다스리는 아비시니아 수도 근처에 있음을 알게 되었으니, 세나푸스의 부와 권세는 어마어마했다."], ["His palace was of surpassing splendor; the bars of the gates, the hinges and locks, were all of pure gold; in fact, this metal, in that country, is put to all those uses for which we employ iron.", "그의 궁전은 비할 데 없이 찬란했다. 성문의 빗장이며 경첩이며 자물쇠가 모두 순금이었으니, 사실 그 나라에서는 우리가 철을 쓰는 모든 용도에 이 금속을 쓴다."], ["It is so common that they prefer for ornamental purposes rock crystal, of which all the columns were made.", "금이 너무도 흔하여 그들은 장식용으로는 수정을 더 좋아하며, 모든 기둥이 수정으로 만들어져 있다."], ["Precious stones of different kinds, rubies, emeralds, sapphires, and topazes were set in ornamental designs, and the walls and ceilings were adorned with pearls.", "루비, 에메랄드, 사파이어, 토파즈 등 갖가지 보석들이 장식 무늬로 박혀 있었고, 벽과 천장은 진주로 꾸며져 있었다."]], [["It is in this country those famous balms grow of which there are some few plants in that part of Judæa called Gilead.", "저 유명한 향유들이 자라는 곳이 바로 이 나라이니, 그 향유들은 길레아드라 불리는 유대의 그 지역에 몇몇 나무로만 남아 있다."], ["Musk, ambergris, and numerous gums, so precious in Europe, are here in their native climate.", "유럽에서 그토록 귀한 사향, 용연향, 그리고 수많은 수지(樹脂)들이 여기서는 제 고향 기후 속에 있다."], ["It is said the Sultan of Egypt pays a vast tribute to the monarch of this country to hire him not to cut off the source of the Nile, which he might easily do, and cause the river to flow in some other direction, thus depriving Egypt of the source of its fertility.", "이집트의 술탄은 이 나라의 군주에게 막대한 조공을 바쳐, 나일의 수원을 끊지 않도록 매수한다고 한다. 군주가 그렇게 하기는 쉬운 일이어서, 강물을 다른 방향으로 흐르게 하면 이집트는 그 비옥함의 원천을 빼앗기게 될 것이기 때문이다."]], [["At the time of Astolpho’s arrival in his dominions, this monarch was in great affliction.", "아스톨포가 그의 영토에 도착했을 무렵, 이 군주는 큰 고통에 빠져 있었다."], ["In spite of his riches and the precious productions of his country, he was in danger of dying of hunger.", "그 부와 그 나라의 진귀한 산물에도 불구하고, 그는 굶어 죽을 위험에 처해 있었다."], ["He was a prey to a flock of obscene birds called Harpies, which attacked him whenever he sat at meat, and with their claws snatched, tore, and scattered everything, overturning the vessels, devouring the food, and infecting what they left with their filthy touch.", "그는 하르피이아이라 불리는 흉측한 새 떼의 먹잇감이 되어 있었으니, 그 새들은 그가 식사하려고 앉을 때마다 덮쳐들어 발톱으로 모든 것을 낚아채고 찢고 흩뜨려, 그릇을 엎고 음식을 게걸스레 먹어 치우며, 남긴 것은 그 더러운 손길로 더럽혔다."], ["It was said this punishment was inflicted upon the king because when young, and filled with pride and presumption, he had attempted to invade with an army the terrestrial paradise, which is situated on the top of a mountain whence the Nile draws its source.", "이 벌이 그 왕에게 내려진 까닭은, 그가 젊었을 적에 교만과 오만에 가득 차서, 나일이 그 수원을 끌어오는 산꼭대기에 자리한 지상 낙원을 군대를 이끌고 침범하려 했기 때문이라고들 했다."], ["Nor was this his only punishment.", "이것이 그의 유일한 벌도 아니었다."], ["He was struck blind.", "그는 눈이 멀게 되었다."]], [["Astolpho, on arriving in the dominions of this monarch, hastened to pay him his respects.", "아스톨포는 이 군주의 영토에 도착하자, 서둘러 그에게 경의를 표하러 갔다."], ["King Senapus received him graciously, and ordered a splendid repast to be prepared in honor of his arrival.", "세나푸스 왕은 그를 정중하게 맞이하였고, 그의 도착을 기리는 성대한 잔치를 준비하라 명했다."], ["While the guests were seated at table, Astolpho filling the place of dignity at the king’s right hand, the horrid scream of the Harpies was heard in the air, and soon they approached, hovering over the tables, seizing the food from the dishes, and overturning everything with the flapping of their broad wings.", "손님들이 식탁에 앉아 있는 동안, 아스톨포가 왕의 오른편 상석을 차지하고 있을 때, 하르피이아이의 끔찍한 비명이 공중에 들려왔고, 곧 그것들이 다가와 식탁 위를 맴돌며 접시에서 음식을 낚아채고 그 넓은 날개를 퍼덕여 모든 것을 엎어 놓았다."], ["In vain the guests struck at them with knives and any weapons which they had, and Astolpho drew his sword and gave them repeated blows, which seemed to have no more effect upon them than if their bodies had been made of tow.", "손님들은 칼이며 가진 무기로 그것들을 쳐 보았으나 헛일이었고, 아스톨포도 칼을 뽑아 거듭 일격을 가했으나, 그것들의 몸이 마치 삼 부스러기로 만들어진 양 아무 효과도 없는 듯했다."]], [["At last Astolpho thought of his horn.", "마침내 아스톨포는 자신의 뿔나팔을 떠올렸다."], ["He first gave warning to the king and his guests to stop their ears; then blew a blast.", "그는 먼저 왕과 손님들에게 귀를 막으라고 일러두었고, 그러고는 한바탕 나팔을 불었다."], ["The Harpies, terrified at the sound, flew away as fast as their wings could carry them.", "그 소리에 질겁한 하르피이아이는 날개가 데려갈 수 있는 한 빠르게 날아 달아났다."], ["The paladin mounted his Hippogriff, and pursued them, blowing his horn as often as he came near them.", "기사는 히포그리프에 올라 그것들을 뒤쫓으며, 가까이 다가갈 때마다 뿔나팔을 불었다."], ["They stretched their flight towards the great mountain, at the foot of which there is a cavern, which is thought to be the mouth of the infernal abodes.", "그것들은 큰 산을 향해 날아갔는데, 그 산기슭에는 지하 세계의 입구로 여겨지는 동굴이 하나 있었다."], ["Hither those horrid birds flew, as if to their home.", "그 흉측한 새들은 마치 제 집으로 가듯 이리로 날아들었다."], ["Having seen them all disappear in the recess, Astolpho cared not to pursue them farther, but alighting, rolled huge stones into the mouth of the cave, and piled branches of trees therein, so that he effectually barred their passage out, and we have no evidence of their ever having been seen since in the outer air.", "그것들이 모두 그 깊숙한 곳으로 사라지는 것을 본 아스톨포는 그것들을 더 쫓지 않고, 내려앉아 거대한 바위들을 굴려 동굴 입구를 막고 그 안에 나뭇가지들을 쌓아, 그것들이 빠져나올 길을 완전히 막아 버렸다. 그리하여 그 새들이 그 뒤로 바깥 공기에서 목격되었다는 증거는 어디에도 없다."]], [["After this labor Astolpho refreshed himself by bathing in a fountain whose pure waters bubbled from a cleft of the rock.", "이 수고를 마친 뒤 아스톨포는 바위 틈에서 맑은 물이 솟아나는 샘에서 목욕하며 원기를 회복했다."], ["Having rested awhile, an earnest desire seized him of ascending the mountain which towered above him.", "한동안 쉬고 나니, 그의 위로 우뚝 솟은 산을 올라 보고 싶은 간절한 욕망이 그를 사로잡았다."], ["The Hippogriff bore him swiftly upwards, and landed him on the top of the mountain, which he found to be an extensive plain.", "히포그리프는 그를 재빨리 위로 실어 날라, 산꼭대기에 내려놓았는데, 그곳은 드넓은 평원이었다."], ["A splendid palace rose in the middle of this plain, whose walls shone with such brilliancy that mortal eyes could hardly bear the sight.", "이 평원 한가운데에 찬란한 궁전이 솟아 있었는데, 그 벽이 어찌나 눈부시게 빛났던지 인간의 눈으로는 그 광경을 도저히 견딜 수 없을 정도였다."], ["Astolpho guided the winged horse towards this edifice, and made him poise himself in the air while he took a leisurely survey of this favored spot and its environs.", "아스톨포는 날개 달린 말을 그 건물 쪽으로 몰아, 이 축복받은 곳과 그 주변을 느긋이 살펴보는 동안 공중에 떠 있게 했다."], ["It seemed as if nature and art had striven with one another to see which could do the most for its embellishment.", "마치 자연과 기예가 서로 겨루어, 어느 쪽이 그곳을 더 아름답게 꾸밀 수 있는지 다툰 듯했다."]], [["Astolpho, on approaching the edifice, saw a venerable man advance to meet him.", "아스톨포가 그 건물에 다가가자, 한 위엄 있는 노인이 그를 맞으러 나오는 것이 보였다."], ["This personage was clothed in a long vesture as white as snow, while a mantle of purple covered his shoulders, and hung down to the ground.", "이 인물은 눈처럼 흰 긴 옷을 입고 있었고, 어깨에는 자줏빛 망토를 둘러 땅까지 늘어뜨리고 있었다."], ["A white beard descended to his middle, and his hair, of the same color, overshadowed his shoulders.", "흰 수염이 허리께까지 내려왔고, 같은 빛깔의 머리카락이 어깨를 덮고 있었다."], ["His eyes were so brilliant that Astolpho felt persuaded that he was a blessed inhabitant of the heavenly mansions.", "그의 눈이 어찌나 빛났던지 아스톨포는 그가 천상의 거처에 사는 축복받은 자라고 확신하게 되었다."]], [["The sage, smiling benignantly upon the paladin, who from respect had dismounted from his horse, said to him: “Noble chevalier, know that it is by the Divine will you have been brought to the terrestrial paradise.", "그 현인은, 존경의 뜻으로 말에서 내린 기사를 향해 자애롭게 미소 지으며 말했다. \"고귀한 기사여, 신의 뜻에 의해 그대가 지상 낙원으로 인도되었음을 알라."], ["Your mortal nature could not have borne to scale these heights and reach these seats of bliss if it were not the will of Heaven that you should be instructed in the means to succor Charles, and to sustain the glory of our holy faith.", "그대가 샤를을 구할 방도를, 그리고 우리 거룩한 신앙의 영광을 떠받칠 방도를 배우도록 하는 것이 하늘의 뜻이 아니었다면, 그대의 필멸의 몸으로는 이 높은 곳을 오르고 이 축복의 자리에 이를 수 없었으리라."], ["I am prepared to impart the needed counsels; but before I begin let me welcome you to our sojourn.", "나는 필요한 조언을 베풀 준비가 되어 있다. 그러나 시작하기에 앞서 우리의 거처에 온 그대를 환영하게 해 다오."], ["I doubt not your long fast and distant journey have given you a good appetite.”", "그대의 오랜 단식과 먼 여정으로 입맛이 좋아졌으리라 의심치 않는다.\""]], [["The aspect of the venerable man filled the prince with admiration; but his surprise ceased when he learned from him that he was that one of the Apostles of our Lord to whom he said, “I will that thou tarry till I come.”", "그 위엄 있는 노인의 모습은 기사를 경탄으로 채웠다. 그러나 그가 우리 주님의 사도들 가운데 한 사람, 곧 주님이 \"내가 올 때까지 그가 머물기를 원하노라\" 하고 말씀하신 그 사람임을 그에게서 알게 되자, 그의 놀라움은 가셨다."]], [["St. John, conducting Astolpho, rejoined his companions.", "성 요한은 아스톨포를 데리고 자신의 동료들에게 다시 갔다."], ["These were the patriarch Enoch and the prophet Elijah; neither of whom had yet seen his dying day, but, taken from our lower world, were dwelling in a region of peace and joy, in a climate of eternal spring, till the last trumpet shall sound.", "이들은 족장 에녹과 예언자 엘리야였으니, 둘 다 아직 죽는 날을 보지 못한 채 우리 하계(下界)에서 옮겨져, 최후의 나팔이 울릴 때까지 영원한 봄의 기후 속, 평화와 기쁨의 영역에 살고 있었다."]], [["The three holy inhabitants of the terrestrial paradise received Astolpho with the greatest kindness, carried him to a pleasant apartment, and took great care of the Hippogriff, to whom they gave such food as suited him, while to the prince they presented fruits so delicious that he felt inclined to excuse our first parents for their sin in eating them without permission.", "지상 낙원에 사는 이 세 거룩한 이들은 아스톨포를 더없이 친절하게 맞이하여, 그를 쾌적한 방으로 데려갔고, 히포그리프도 정성껏 돌보아 그에게 맞는 먹이를 주었다. 한편 기사에게는 더없이 맛있는 과일들을 내놓았으니, 그는 우리 최초의 조상들이 허락도 없이 그것을 따 먹은 죄를 너그러이 용서해 주고 싶은 마음이 들 정도였다."]], [["Astolpho, having recruited his strength, not only by these excellent fruits, but also by sweet sleep, roused himself at the first blush of dawn, and as soon as he left his chamber met the beloved Apostle coming to seek him.", "아스톨포는 이 훌륭한 과일들뿐 아니라 달콤한 잠으로도 기력을 회복하고는, 동틀 무렵 첫 햇살에 잠에서 깨어, 방을 나서자마자 자신을 찾아오는 사랑하는 사도를 만났다."], ["St. John took him by the hand, and told him many things relating to the past and the future.", "성 요한은 그의 손을 잡고, 과거와 미래에 관한 많은 것을 그에게 일러 주었다."], ["Among others, he said, “Son, let me tell you what is now going on in France.", "그 가운데 그는 이렇게 말했다. \"아들아, 지금 프랑스에서 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 일러 주마."], ["Orlando, the illustrious prince who received at his birth the endowment of strength and courage more than mortal, raised up as was Samson of old to be the champion of the true faith, has been guilty of the basest ingratitude in leaving the Christian camp when it most needed the support of his arm, to run after a Saracen princess, whom he would fain marry, though she scorns him.", "오를란도는, 태어날 때 필멸자 이상의 힘과 용기를 부여받은 그 빼어난 기사로서, 옛 삼손이 그러했듯 참된 신앙의 용사로 세워진 자이거늘, 기독교 진영이 그의 팔의 도움을 가장 필요로 할 때 그곳을 떠나 한 사라센 공주의 뒤를 쫓는 가장 비열한 배은망덕의 죄를 저질렀다. 그 공주는 그를 멸시하는데도 그는 그녀와 혼인하기를 바라 마지않았던 것이다."], ["To punish him his reason has been taken away, so that he runs naked through the land, over mountains and through valleys, without a ray of intelligence.", "그를 벌하고자 그의 이성이 거두어졌으니, 그리하여 그는 한 줄기 분별도 없이 산을 넘고 골짜기를 지나 벌거벗은 채 온 땅을 내달리고 있다."], ["The duration of his punishment has been fixed at three months, and that time having nearly expired, you have been brought hither to learn from us the means by which the reason of Orlando may be restored.", "그 벌의 기간은 석 달로 정해졌고, 그 시한이 거의 다 차 가는 터라, 오를란도의 이성을 되찾아 줄 방도를 우리에게서 배우도록 그대가 이리로 인도된 것이다."], ["True, you will be obliged to make a journey with me, and we must even leave the earth, and ascend to the moon, for it is in that planet we are to seek the remedy for the madness of the paladin.", "다만 그대는 나와 함께 한 여정을 떠나야 할 것이며, 우리는 이 땅을 떠나 달에 올라야 하기까지 할 것이다. 그 기사의 광기를 치료할 약을 찾아야 할 곳이 바로 그 천체이기 때문이다."], ["I propose to make our journey this evening, as soon as the moon appears over our head.”", "나는 오늘 저녁, 달이 우리 머리 위에 떠오르는 대로 여정을 떠나자고 제안한다.\""]], [["As soon as the sun sunk beneath the seas, and the moon presented its luminous disk, the holy man had the chariot brought out in which he was accustomed to make excursions among the stars, the same which was employed long ago to convey Elijah up from earth.", "해가 바다 아래로 가라앉고 달이 그 빛나는 원반을 드러내자마자, 그 거룩한 이는 자신이 별들 사이를 다닐 때 쓰던 마차를 끌어내게 했으니, 그것은 오래전 엘리야를 땅에서 위로 실어 올리는 데 쓰였던 바로 그 마차였다."], ["The saint made Astolpho seat himself beside him, took the reins, and giving the word to the coursers, they bore them upward with astonishing celerity.", "성인은 아스톨포를 자기 곁에 앉히고 고삐를 잡았으며, 준마들에게 신호를 보내자 그것들은 놀라운 속도로 두 사람을 위로 실어 날랐다."]], [["At length they reached the great continent of the Moon.", "마침내 그들은 달의 거대한 대륙에 다다랐다."], ["Its surface appeared to be of polished steel, with here and there a spot which, like rust, obscured its brightness.", "그 표면은 광을 낸 강철 같았는데, 여기저기 녹처럼 그 광채를 흐리는 얼룩이 있었다."], ["The paladin was astonished to see that the earth, with all its seas and rivers, seemed but an insignificant spot in the distance.", "그 기사는 모든 바다와 강을 거느린 지구가 저 멀리 하찮은 한 점에 지나지 않아 보이는 것을 보고 놀랐다."]], [["The prince discovered in this region so new to him rivers, lakes, plains, hills, and valleys.", "이 왕자는 자신에게 너무도 새로운 이 영역에서 강과 호수와 평원과 언덕과 골짜기를 발견했다."], ["Many beautiful cities and castles enriched the landscape.", "많은 아름다운 도시와 성들이 그 풍경을 풍요롭게 했다."], ["He saw also vast forests, and heard in them the sound of horns and the barking of dogs, which led him to conclude that the nymphs were following the chase.", "그는 또한 광대한 숲들을 보았고, 그 안에서 뿔나팔 소리와 개 짖는 소리를 들었으니, 그것으로 미루어 그는 요정들이 사냥을 하고 있으리라 짐작했다."]], [["The knight, filled with wonder at all he saw, was conducted by the saint to a valley, where he stood amazed at the riches strewed all around him.", "자신이 본 모든 것에 경이로 가득 찬 그 기사는, 성인의 인도를 받아 한 골짜기로 들어섰는데, 거기서 그는 사방에 흩어진 부(富)에 놀라 멍하니 섰다."], ["Well he might be so, for that valley was the receptacle of things lost on earth, either by men’s fault, or by the effect of time and chance.", "그럴 만도 했으니, 그 골짜기는 사람의 잘못으로든, 세월과 우연의 작용으로든 지상에서 잃어버린 것들을 받아 두는 곳이었기 때문이다. 우리가 여기서 왕국이나 보물을 말한다고는 아무도 생각지 말라."], ["Let no one suppose we speak here of kingdoms or of treasures; they are the toys of Fortune, which she dispenses in turning her wheel; we speak of things which she can neither give nor take away.", "그것들은 운명의 여신이 자신의 수레바퀴를 돌리며 베푸는 장난감일 뿐이다. 우리가 말하는 것은 그 여신이 주지도 빼앗지도 못하는 것들이다."], ["Such are reputations, which appear at one time so brilliant, and a short time after are heard of no more.", "한때 그토록 찬란히 빛나다가 얼마 지나지 않아 더는 소식조차 들리지 않게 되는 명성이 그러한 것이다."], ["Here, also, are countless vows and prayers for unattainable objects, lovers’ sighs and tears, time spent in gaming, dressing, and doing nothing, the leisure of the dull and the intentions of the lazy, baseless projects, intrigues, and plots; these and such like things fill all the valley.", "여기에는 또한 이룰 수 없는 것을 바라는 수없는 맹세와 기도, 연인들의 한숨과 눈물, 노름과 치장과 빈둥거림에 허비된 시간, 둔한 자들의 여가와 게으른 자들의 의도, 근거 없는 계획, 음모, 모략이 있었으니, 이런 것들과 그 비슷한 것들이 온 골짜기를 채우고 있었다."]], [["Astolpho had a great desire to understand all that he saw, and which appeared to him so extraordinary.", "아스톨포는 자신이 본 것, 그리고 자신에게 그토록 기이해 보이는 것을 모두 이해하고 싶은 큰 욕망이 있었다."], ["Among the rest, he observed a great mountain of blown bladders, from which issued indistinct noises.", "그 가운데서 그는 부풀어 오른 방광들로 이루어진 큰 산을 보았는데, 거기서 알아들을 수 없는 소리들이 흘러나왔다."], ["The saint told him these were the dynasties of Assyrian and Persian kings, once the wonder of the earth, of which now scarce the name remains.", "성인은 그것들이 한때 세상의 경이였던 아시리아와 페르시아 왕들의 왕조들이며, 이제는 그 이름조차 거의 남지 않았다고 일러 주었다."], ["Astolpho could not help laughing when the saint said to him, “All these hooks of silver and gold that you see are the gifts of courtiers to princes, made in the hope of getting something better in return.”", "성인이 그에게 \"그대가 보는 저 은과 금으로 된 갈고리들은 모두 더 나은 것을 보답으로 얻으려는 바람에서 신하들이 군주들에게 바친 선물들이다\"라고 말하자 아스톨포는 웃지 않을 수 없었다."], ["He also showed him garlands of flowers in which snares were concealed; these were flatteries and adulations, meant to deceive.", "성인은 또한 그에게 올가미가 숨겨진 꽃 화환들을 보여 주었으니, 이것들은 속이려는 뜻으로 한 아첨과 아부였다."], ["But nothing was so comical as the sight of numerous grasshoppers which had burst their lungs with chirping.", "그러나 무엇보다도 우스꽝스러운 것은, 지저귀다가 허파가 터져 버린 수많은 메뚜기들의 광경이었다."], ["These, he told him, were sonnets, odes, and dedications, addressed by venal poets to great people.", "성인은 그에게, 이것들은 돈에 매수된 시인들이 권세 있는 자들에게 바친 소네트와 송시(頌詩)와 헌사라고 일러 주었다."]], [["The paladin beheld with wonder what seemed a lake of spilled milk.", "그 기사는 엎질러진 우유의 호수처럼 보이는 것을 경이롭게 바라보았다."], ["“It is,”", "\"저것은,\""], ["said the saint, “the charity done by frightened misers on their death-beds.”", "성인이 말했다. \"겁에 질린 구두쇠들이 임종의 자리에서 베푼 자선이다.\""], ["It would take too long to tell all that the valley contained: meanness, affectations, pretended virtues, and concealed vices were there in abundance.", "그 골짜기에 담긴 것을 모두 이야기하자면 너무 길어질 것이다. 비열함, 허식, 거짓 미덕, 감춰진 악덕이 거기에 가득했다."]], [["Among the rest Astolpho perceived many days of his own lost, and many imprudent sallies which he had made, and would have been glad not to have been reminded of.", "그 가운데서 아스톨포는 자신이 잃어버린 숱한 날들과, 자신이 저질렀던, 그리고 다시는 떠올리고 싶지 않은 숱한 경솔한 행동들을 알아보았다."], ["But he also saw among so many lost things a great abundance of one thing which men are apt to think they all possess, and do not think it necessary to pray for,—good sense.", "그러나 그는 또한 그 숱한 잃어버린 것들 가운데, 사람들이 누구나 다 지니고 있다고 여기기 쉽고 굳이 빌어서 얻을 필요가 없다고 생각하는 한 가지가 아주 풍부하게 있는 것을 보았으니, 곧 양식(良識)이었다."], ["This commodity appeared under the form of a liquor, most light and apt to evaporate.", "이 물건은 더없이 가볍고 쉽게 증발하는 액체의 형태로 나타났다."], ["It was therefore kept in vials, firmly sealed.", "그래서 그것은 단단히 봉인된 작은 병들에 담겨 보관되어 있었다."], ["One of these was labelled, “The sense of the Paladin Orlando.”", "그 가운데 하나에는 \"기사 오를란도의 분별\"이라는 딱지가 붙어 있었다."]], [["All the bottles were ticketed, and the sage placed one in Astolpho’s hand, which he found was his own.", "모든 병에 딱지가 붙어 있었고, 그 현인은 그 가운데 하나를 아스톨포의 손에 쥐어 주었는데, 그것이 자신의 것임을 그는 알아차렸다."], ["It was more than half full.", "그것은 절반 이상 차 있었다."], ["He was surprised to find there many other vials which contained almost the whole of the wits of many persons who passed among men for wise.", "그는 사람들 사이에서 지혜롭다고 통하는 많은 이들의 재기를 거의 다 담은 다른 병들이 거기 많이 있는 것을 보고 놀랐다."], ["Ah, how easy it is to lose one’s reason!", "아, 사람이 이성을 잃기란 얼마나 쉬운 일인가!"], ["Some lose theirs by yielding to the sway of the passions; some in braving tempests and shoals in search of wealth; some by trusting too much to the promises of the great; some by setting their hearts on trifles.", "어떤 이들은 정념의 지배에 굴복하여 이성을 잃고, 어떤 이들은 부를 좇아 폭풍과 모래톱을 무릅쓰다가 잃으며, 어떤 이들은 권세 있는 자들의 약속을 지나치게 믿다가 잃고, 어떤 이들은 하찮은 것에 마음을 쏟다가 잃는다."], ["As might have been expected, the bottles which held the wits of astrologers, inventors, metaphysicians, and above all, of poets, were in general the best filled of all.", "능히 짐작할 수 있듯이, 점성가, 발명가, 형이상학자, 그리고 무엇보다도 시인들의 재기를 담은 병들이 대체로 다른 모든 것들 가운데 가장 잘 차 있었다."]], [["Astolpho took his bottle, put it to his nose, and inhaled it all; and Turpin assures us that he was for a long time afterwards as sage as one could wish; but the Archbishop adds that there was reason to fear that some of the precious fluid afterwards found its way back into the bottle.", "아스톨포는 자신의 병을 집어 코에 대고 그것을 모두 들이마셨다. 투르팽은 그가 그 후로 오랫동안 누구나 바랄 만큼 지혜로웠다고 우리에게 단언한다."], ["The paladin took also the bottle which belonged to Orlando.", "다만 대주교는, 그 귀한 액체의 일부가 나중에 다시 병 속으로 돌아갔을까 염려할 만한 까닭이 있다고 덧붙인다. 그 기사는 오를란도에게 속한 병도 집어 들었다."], ["It was a large one, and quite full.", "그것은 큰 병이었고, 가득 차 있었다."]], [["Before quitting the planetary region Astolpho was conducted to an edifice on the borders of a river.", "천체의 영역을 떠나기에 앞서 아스톨포는 한 강가에 있는 어느 건물로 인도되었다."], ["He was shown an immense hall full of bundles of silk, linen, cotton, and wool.", "그는 비단, 아마포, 면, 양모 다발이 가득한 거대한 홀을 보게 되었다."], ["A thousand different colors, brilliant or dull, some quite black, were among these skeins.", "천 가지 갖가지 빛깔이, 밝거나 흐릿하거나 더러는 아주 검은 것이, 이 실타래들 가운데 섞여 있었다."], ["In one part of the hall an old woman was busy winding off yarns from all these different bundles.", "홀의 한쪽에서는 한 노파가 이 갖가지 다발들에서 실을 감아내는 일에 분주했다."], ["When she had finished a skein another ancient dame took it and placed it with others; a third selected from the fleeces spun, and mingled them in due proportions.", "그녀가 한 타래를 다 끝내면 또 다른 늙은 부인이 그것을 받아 다른 것들과 함께 놓았고, 세 번째 부인은 자아 놓은 양털 가운데서 골라 알맞은 비율로 섞었다."], ["The paladin inquired what all this might be.", "그 기사는 이 모든 것이 무엇을 뜻하는지 물었다."], ["“These old women,”", "\"이 노파들은,\""], ["said the saint, “are the Fates, who spin, measure, and terminate the lives of mortals.", "성인이 말했다. \"필멸자들의 생명을 잣고 재고 끊는 운명의 여신들이다."], ["As long as the thread stretches in one of those skeins, so long does the mortal enjoy the light of day; but nature and death are on the alert to shut the eyes of those whose thread is spun.”", "저 실타래 가운데 하나에서 실이 늘어나 있는 동안에는 그만큼 그 필멸자가 햇빛을 누리지만, 자연과 죽음이 방심하지 않고 지켜보다가 실이 다 자아진 자들의 눈을 감긴다.\""]], [["Each one of the skeins had a label of gold, silver, or iron, bearing the name of the individual to whom it belonged.", "각 실타래마다 금이나 은이나 철로 된 딱지가 붙어, 그것이 속한 사람의 이름이 적혀 있었다."], ["An old man, who, in spite of the burden of years, seemed brisk and active, ran without ceasing to fill his apron with these labels, and carried them away to throw them into the river, whose name was Lethe.", "한 노인이, 나이의 무게에도 불구하고 활기차고 민첩해 보였는데, 쉴 새 없이 달려와 이 딱지들로 자기 앞치마를 채우고는, 레테라 불리는 강에 던져 넣으려 가져갔다."], ["When he reached the shore of the river the old man shook out his apron, and the labels sunk to the bottom.", "강기슭에 이르자 그 노인은 앞치마를 털었고, 딱지들은 바닥으로 가라앉았다."], ["A small number only floated for a time, hardly one in a thousand.", "오직 적은 수만이 한동안 떠 있었으니, 천에 하나도 채 못 되었다."], ["Numberless birds, hawks, crows, and vultures hovered over the stream, with clamorous cries, and strove to snatch from the water some of these names; but they were too heavy for them, and after a while the birds were forced to let them drop into the river of oblivion.", "수많은 새들, 매와 까마귀와 독수리들이 시끄러운 울음소리를 내며 강 위를 맴돌며 그 이름들 가운데 일부를 물에서 낚아채려 애썼으나, 그것들은 새들에게 너무 무거웠고, 얼마 지나지 않아 새들은 그것들을 망각의 강에 떨어뜨릴 수밖에 없었다."], ["But two beautiful swans, of snowy whiteness, gathered some few of the names, and returned with them to the shore, where a lovely nymph received them from their beaks, and carried them to a temple placed upon a hill, and suspended them for all time upon a sacred column, on which stood the statue of Immortality.", "그러나 눈처럼 흰 두 마리의 아름다운 백조가 그 이름들 가운데 몇몇을 거두어 강기슭으로 돌아왔는데, 거기서 한 사랑스러운 요정이 그 부리에서 그것들을 받아, 언덕 위에 자리한 신전으로 가져가, 〈불멸〉의 조각상이 서 있는 성스러운 기둥에 그것들을 영원토록 매달았다."]], [["Astolpho was amazed at all this, and asked his guide to explain it.", "아스톨포는 이 모든 것에 놀라, 안내자에게 그것을 설명해 달라고 청했다."], ["He replied, “The old man is Time.", "그가 대답했다. \"그 노인은 〈시간〉이다."], ["All the names upon the tickets would be immortal if the old man did not plunge them into the river of oblivion.", "만약 그 노인이 그것들을 망각의 강에 빠뜨리지 않는다면, 딱지에 적힌 모든 이름은 불멸일 것이다."], ["Those clamorous birds which make vain efforts to save certain of the names are flatterers, pensioners, venal rhymesters, who do their best to rescue from oblivion the unworthy names of their patrons; but all in vain; they may keep them from their fate a little while, but ere long the river of oblivion must swallow them all.", "어떤 이름들을 구하려고 헛된 애를 쓰는 저 시끄러운 새들은 아첨꾼들, 연금에 기대 사는 자들, 돈에 팔리는 운문 짓는 자들이니, 그들은 자기 후원자들의 보잘것없는 이름을 망각에서 구해 내려고 안간힘을 쓰지만 모두 헛일이다. 그들은 그 이름들을 잠시 동안 그 운명에서 지켜낼 수는 있어도, 머지않아 망각의 강이 그것들을 모두 삼켜 버리고야 만다."]], [["“The swans, that with harmonious strains carry certain names to the temple of Eternal Memory, are the great poets, who save from oblivion worse than death the names of those they judge worthy of immortality.", "\"조화로운 가락으로 어떤 이름들을 〈영원한 기억〉의 신전으로 실어 나르는 저 백조들은 위대한 시인들이니, 그들은 불멸할 만하다고 여기는 이들의 이름을, 죽음보다 더한 망각에서 구해 낸다."], ["Swans of this kind are rare.", "이런 백조는 드물다."], ["Let monarchs know the true breed, and fail not to nourish with care such as may chance to appear in their time.”", "군주들로 하여금 그 참된 혈통을 알게 하여, 자신들의 시대에 우연히 나타날지 모르는 그런 자들을 정성껏 길러 내기를 게을리하지 않게 하라.\" 아프리카의 전쟁"]], [["# THE WAR IN AFRICA", "# THE WAR IN AFRICA"]], [["————\nWhen Astolpho had descended to the earth with the precious phial, St. John showed him a plant of marvellous virtues, with which he told him he had only to touch the eyes of the king of Abyssinia to restore him to sight.", "아스톨포가 그 귀한 작은 병을 가지고 땅으로 내려오자, 성 요한은 그에게 놀라운 효험을 지닌 한 식물을 보여 주며, 그것으로 아비시니아 왕의 눈을 만지기만 하면 그의 시력을 되찾아 줄 수 있다고 일러 주었다."], ["“That important service,”", "\"그 중요한 봉사는,\""], ["said the saint, “added to your having delivered him from the Harpies, will induce him to give you an army wherewith to attack the Africans in their rear, and force them to return from France to defend their own country.”", "성인이 말했다. \"그대가 그를 하르피이아이에게서 구해 준 일에 더하여, 그로 하여금 그대에게 군대를 내주어, 그것으로 아프리카인들의 배후를 공격하여 그들이 프랑스에서 돌아와 자기 나라를 지키지 않을 수 없게 만들도록 할 것이다.\""], ["The saint also instructed him how to lead his troops in safety across the great deserts, where caravans are often overwhelmed with moving columns of sand.", "성인은 또한 그에게, 대상(隊商)들이 흔히 움직이는 모래 기둥에 휩쓸리곤 하는 대사막을 어떻게 무사히 군대를 이끌고 건널 수 있는지도 가르쳐 주었다."], ["Astolpho, fortified with ample instructions, remounted the Hippogriff, thanked the saint, received his blessing, and took his flight down to the level country.", "충분한 지침으로 든든해진 아스톨포는 다시 히포그리프에 올라, 성인에게 감사를 표하고 그의 축복을 받은 뒤, 평지로 날아 내려갔다."]], [["Keeping the course of the river Nile, he soon arrived at the capital of Abyssinia, and rejoined Senapus.", "나일강의 물줄기를 따라가던 그는 곧 아비시니아의 수도에 다다라, 세나푸스와 다시 만났다."], ["The joy of the king was great when he heard again the voice of the hero who had delivered him from the Harpies.", "자신을 하르피이아이에게서 구해 준 영웅의 목소리를 다시 듣자 왕의 기쁨은 컸다."], ["Astolpho touched his eyes with the plant which he had brought from the terrestrial paradise, and restored their sight.", "아스톨포는 지상 낙원에서 가져온 식물로 그의 눈을 만져, 그의 시력을 되찾아 주었다."], ["The king’s gratitude was unbounded.", "왕의 감사는 끝이 없었다."], ["He begged him to name a reward, promising to grant it, whatever it might be.", "그는 그에게 보답을 청하라고, 그것이 무엇이든 들어주겠노라 약속하며 간청했다."], ["Astolpho asked an army to go to the assistance of Charlemagne, and the king not only granted him a hundred thousand men, but offered to lead them himself.", "아스톨포는 샤를마뉴를 도우러 갈 군대를 청했고, 왕은 그에게 십만 명을 내주었을 뿐 아니라, 자신이 직접 그들을 이끌겠다고 제안하기까지 했다."]], [["The night before the day appointed for the departure of the troops Astolpho mounted his winged horse, and directed his flight towards a mountain, whence the fierce South-wind issues, whose blast raises the sands of the Nubian desert, and whirls them onward in overwhelming clouds.", "군대가 떠나기로 정해진 날의 전날 밤, 아스톨포는 날개 달린 말에 올라 한 산을 향해 날아갔는데, 거기서 사나운 남풍이 나오니, 그 바람의 입김은 누비아 사막의 모래를 일으켜 압도하는 구름처럼 그것들을 앞으로 휘몰아간다."], ["The paladin, by the advice of St. John, had prepared himself with a leather bag, which he placed adroitly, with its mouth open, over the vent whence issues this terrible wind.", "그 기사는 성 요한의 조언에 따라 가죽 자루를 하나 마련해 두었다가, 그 무시무시한 바람이 나오는 분출구 위에 그 입구를 벌린 채 솜씨 좋게 갖다 댔다."], ["At the first dawn of morning the wind rushed from its cavern to resume its daily course, and was caught in the bag, and securely tied up.", "동이 트는 첫 무렵 바람이 자신의 동굴에서 솟구쳐 나와 날마다의 진로를 다시 시작하려 했고, 그러다 자루 속에 갇혀 단단히 묶이고 말았다."], ["Astolpho, delighted with his prize, returned to his army, placed himself at their head, and commenced his march.", "아스톨포는 자신의 노획물에 기뻐하며 군대로 돌아와, 그 선두에 서서 행군을 시작했다."], ["The Abyssinians traversed without danger or difficulty those vast fields of sand which separate their country from the kingdoms of Northern Africa, for the terrible South-wind, taken completely captive, had not force enough left to blow out a candle.", "아비시니아인들은 자기 나라를 북아프리카의 여러 왕국과 갈라놓는 그 광대한 모래 벌판을 위험도 어려움도 없이 가로질렀으니, 완전히 사로잡힌 그 무시무시한 남풍에게는 촛불 하나 끌 만한 힘조차 남아 있지 않았기 때문이다."]], [["Senapus was distressed that he could not furnish any cavalry, for his country, rich in camels and elephants, was destitute of horses.", "세나푸스는 기병을 전혀 댈 수 없는 것을 안타까워했으니, 그의 나라는 낙타와 코끼리는 풍부했으나 말은 없었기 때문이다."], ["This difficulty the saint had foreseen, and had taught Astolpho the means of remedying.", "이 어려움을 성인은 미리 내다보고, 그것을 해결할 방도를 아스톨포에게 가르쳐 두었다."], ["He now put those means in operation.", "그는 이제 그 방도를 실행에 옮겼다."], ["Having reached a place whence he beheld a vast plain and the sea, he chose from his troops those who appeared to be the best made and the most intelligent.", "광대한 평원과 바다가 내려다보이는 한 지점에 이르자, 그는 자신의 군대 가운데서 가장 잘 빠지고 가장 영리해 보이는 자들을 골랐다."], ["These he caused to be arranged in squadrons at the foot of a lofty mountain which bordered the plain, and he himself mounted to the summit to carry into effect his great design.", "이들을 그는 평원과 맞닿은 높은 산기슭에 부대별로 정렬시켰고, 자신은 자신의 큰 계획을 실행하고자 정상으로 올라갔다."], ["Here he found vast quantities of fragments of rock and pebbles.", "거기서 그는 엄청난 양의 바위 조각과 자갈을 발견했다."], ["These he set rolling down the mountain’s side, and, wonderful to relate, as they rolled they grew in size, made themselves bodies, legs, necks, and long faces.", "이것들을 그는 산비탈로 굴려 내렸는데, 놀랍게도 그것들은 굴러가면서 크기가 커졌고, 스스로 몸통과 다리와 목과 긴 얼굴을 만들어 냈다."], ["Next they began to neigh, to curvet, to scamper on all sides over the plain. Some were bay, some roan, some dapple, some chestnut.", "다음으로 그것들은 울기 시작했고, 껑충거렸으며, 평원 사방으로 내달렸다."], ["The troops at the foot of the mountain exerted themselves to catch these new-created horses, which they easily did, for the miracle had been so considerate as to provide all the horses with bridles and saddles.", "어떤 것은 적갈색이었고, 어떤 것은 회갈색이었고, 어떤 것은 얼룩빼기였고, 어떤 것은 밤색이었다. 산기슭의 군대는 이 새로 만들어진 말들을 잡으려 애썼고, 손쉽게 잡았으니, 그 기적이 인심 좋게도 모든 말에게 굴레와 안장까지 갖추어 준 덕분이었다."], ["Astolpho thus suddenly found himself supplied with an excellent corps of cavalry, not fewer (as Archbishop Turpin asserts) than eighty thousand strong.", "이리하여 아스톨포는 갑자기 훌륭한 기병대를 갖추게 되었으니, (투르팽 대주교가 단언하는 바로는) 그 수가 팔만에 못지않았다."], ["With these troops Astolpho reduced all the country to subjection, and at last arrived before the walls of Agramant’s capital city, Biserta, to which he laid siege.", "이 군대로 아스톨포는 온 나라를 복종시켰고, 마침내 아그라만트의 수도 비제르타의 성벽 앞에 다다라, 그곳을 포위했다."]], [["We must now return to the camp of the Christians, which lay before Arles, to which city the Saracens had retired after being defeated in a night attack led on by Rinaldo.", "이제 우리는 기독교군의 진영으로 돌아가야 한다. 그 진영은 아를 앞에 펼쳐져 있었으니, 사라센군은 리날도가 이끈 야습에서 패한 뒤 그 도시로 물러나 있었다."], ["Agramant here received the tidings of the invasion of his country by a fresh enemy, the Abyssinians, and learned that Biserta was in danger of falling into their hands.", "아그라만트는 여기서 새로운 적인 아비시니아인들이 자기 나라를 침공했다는 소식을 받았고, 비제르타가 그들의 손에 떨어질 위험에 처했음을 알게 되었다."], ["He took counsel of his officers, and decided to send an embassy to Charles, proposing that the whole quarrel should be submitted to the combat of two warriors, one from each side, according to the issue of which it should be decided which party should pay tribute to the other, and the war should cease.", "그는 휘하 장수들과 상의하여, 샤를에게 사절을 보내, 양측에서 각각 한 명씩 두 전사의 결투로 모든 다툼을 가리자고 제안하기로 결정했다. 그 결투의 결과에 따라 어느 편이 다른 편에게 조공을 바칠지 정해지고, 전쟁은 그치게 될 것이었다."], ["Charlemagne, who had not heard of the favorable turn which affairs had taken in Africa, readily agreed to this proposal, and Rinaldo was selected on the part of the Christians to sustain the combat.", "아프리카에서 형세가 유리하게 돌아간 것을 듣지 못한 샤를마뉴는 이 제안에 선뜻 동의했고, 기독교 측에서는 리날도가 그 결투를 맡을 자로 뽑혔다."]], [["The Saracens selected Rogero for their champion.", "사라센군은 자신들의 용사로 로제로를 뽑았다."], ["Rogero was still in the Saracen camp, kept there by honor alone, for his mind had been opened to the truth of the Christian faith by the arguments of Bradamante, and he had resolved to leave the party of the infidels on the first favorable opportunity, and to join the Christian side.", "로제로는 여전히 사라센 진영에 있었으나, 오직 명예 때문에 거기 머물러 있을 뿐이었다. 그의 마음은 브라다만테의 논변으로 기독교 신앙의 진리에 눈을 떴고, 그는 첫 번째 알맞은 기회가 오면 이교도의 편을 떠나 기독교 측에 합류하기로 결심해 둔 터였다."], ["But his honor forbade him to do this while his former friends were in distress; and thus he waited for what time might bring forth, when he was startled by the announcement that he had been selected to uphold the cause of the Saracens against the Christians, and that his foe was to be Rinaldo, the brother of Bradamante.", "그러나 옛 벗들이 곤경에 처해 있는 동안 그렇게 하는 것은 그의 명예가 허락지 않았다. 그리하여 그는 때가 무엇을 가져다줄지 기다리고 있었는데, 자신이 기독교군에 맞서 사라센군의 대의를 떠받칠 자로 뽑혔다는 것, 그리고 자신의 적수가 브라다만테의 오라비 리날도라는 발표에 소스라치게 놀랐다."]], [["While Rogero was overwhelmed with this intelligence Bradamante on her side felt the deepest distress at hearing of the proposed combat.", "로제로가 이 소식에 망연자실해 있는 동안, 브라다만테 또한 제안된 그 결투에 관해 듣고 더없이 깊은 고통을 느꼈다."], ["If Rogero should fall she felt that no other man living was worthy of her love; and if, on the other hand, Heaven should resolve to punish France by the death of her chosen champion, Bradamante would have to deplore her brother, so dear to her, and be no less completely severed from the object of her affections.", "만약 로제로가 쓰러진다면, 그녀는 살아 있는 그 어떤 남자도 자신의 사랑을 받을 자격이 없다고 느꼈다. 그리고 다른 한편으로 만약 하늘이 자신이 뽑은 용사의 죽음으로 프랑스를 벌하기로 한다면, 브라다만테는 그토록 사랑하는 오라비를 잃고 슬퍼해야 할 것이며, 자신의 애정의 대상과도 그에 못지않게 완전히 갈라서게 될 것이었다."]], [["While the fair lady gave herself up to these sad thoughts, the sage enchantress, Melissa, suddenly appeared before her.", "그 아름다운 여인이 이런 슬픈 상념에 잠겨 있을 때, 현명한 마법사 멜리사가 갑자기 그녀 앞에 나타났다."], ["“Fear not, my daughter,”", "\"두려워 말아라, 내 딸아.\""], ["said she, “I shall find a way to interrupt this combat which so distresses you.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"그대를 그토록 괴롭히는 이 결투를 막을 방도를 내가 찾아 주마.\""]], [["Meanwhile Rinaldo and Rogero prepared their weapons for the conflict.", "그러는 사이 리날도와 로제로는 결투를 위해 무기를 준비했다."], ["Rinaldo had the choice, and decided that it should be on foot, and with no weapons but the battle-axe and poniard.", "리날도에게 선택권이 있었고, 그는 도보로, 그리고 전투용 도끼와 단검 외에는 어떤 무기도 쓰지 않고 싸우기로 정했다."], ["The place assigned was a plain between the camp of Charlemagne and the walls of Arles.", "정해진 장소는 샤를마뉴의 진영과 아를 성벽 사이의 평원이었다."]], [["Hardly had the dawn announced the day appointed for this memorable combat, when heralds proceeded from both sides to mark the lists.", "이 기념할 만한 결투를 위해 정해진 날을 새벽이 알리자마자, 양측에서 전령들이 나와 결투장을 표시했다."], ["Erelong the African troops were seen to advance from the city, Agramant at their head; his brilliant arms adorned in the Moorish fashion, his horse a bay, with a white star on his forehead.", "머지않아 아프리카군이 아그라만트를 선두로 도시에서 나오는 것이 보였다. 그의 찬란한 무기는 무어식으로 꾸며져 있었고, 그의 말은 이마에 흰 별이 박힌 적갈색이었다."], ["Rogero marched at his side, and some of the greatest warriors of the Saracen camp attended him, bearing the various parts of his armor and weapons.", "로제로가 그의 곁에서 행군했고, 사라센 진영의 가장 위대한 전사들 몇몇이 그를 수행하며 그의 갑옷과 무기의 여러 부분들을 받쳐 들고 있었다."], ["Charlemagne, on his part, proceeded from his intrenchments, ranged his troops in semicircle, and stood surrounded by his peers and paladins.", "샤를마뉴 또한 자신의 보루에서 나와, 군대를 반원형으로 늘어세우고, 자신의 중신들과 기사들에게 둘러싸여 섰다."], ["Some of them bore portions of the armor of Rinaldo, the celebrated Ogier, the Dane, bearing the helmet which Rinaldo took from Mambrino.", "그들 가운데 몇몇이 리날도의 갑옷 일부를 받쳐 들었는데, 저 유명한 덴마크 사람 오기에르가 리날도가 맘브리노에게서 빼앗은 투구를 받쳐 들었다."], ["Duke Namo of Bavaria and Salomon of Bretagne bore two axes, of equal weight, prepared for the occasion.", "바이에른의 나모 공작과 브르타뉴의 살로몬이, 이 경우를 위해 마련된, 무게가 똑같은 두 자루의 도끼를 받쳐 들었다."]], [["The terms of the combat were then sworn to with the utmost solemnity by all parties.", "이어 결투의 조건을 모든 당사자가 더없이 엄숙하게 맹세했다."], ["It was agreed that if from either part any attempt was made to interrupt the battle both combatants should turn their arms against the party which should be guilty of the interruption; and both monarchs assented to the condition that in such case the champion of the offending party should be discharged from his allegiance, and at liberty to transfer his arms to the other side.", "만약 어느 한쪽에서 결투를 방해하려는 어떤 시도라도 한다면, 두 결투자 모두 그 방해를 저지른 편을 향해 무기를 돌리기로 합의했다. 그리고 두 군주는, 그런 경우 그 죄를 범한 편의 용사는 자신의 충성 서약에서 풀려나, 자유로이 자신의 무기를 다른 편으로 옮길 수 있다는 조건에도 동의했다."]], [["When all the preparations were concluded the monarchs and their attendants retired each to his own side, and the champions were left alone.", "모든 준비가 끝나자 군주들과 그 수행원들은 각기 제 편으로 물러갔고, 두 용사만이 남겨졌다."], ["The two warriors advanced with measured steps towards each other, and met in the middle of the space.", "두 전사는 일정한 걸음으로 서로를 향해 나아가, 그 공간의 한가운데서 마주쳤다."], ["They attacked one another at the same moment, and the air resounded with the blows they gave.", "그들은 동시에 서로에게 달려들었고, 그들이 주고받는 일격에 공기가 울렸다."], ["Sparks flew from their battle-axes, while the velocity with which they managed their weapons astonished the beholders.", "전투용 도끼에서 불꽃이 튀었고, 그들이 무기를 다루는 속도는 보는 이들을 놀라게 했다."], ["Rogero, always remembering that his antagonist was the brother of his betrothed, could not aim a deadly wound; he strove only to ward off those levelled against himself.", "자신의 적수가 약혼녀의 오라비임을 늘 잊지 않은 로제로는 치명적인 상처를 입힐 수가 없었다. 그는 다만 자신을 향해 겨눠지는 일격을 막아 내려고만 애썼다."], ["Rinaldo, on the other hand, much as he esteemed Rogero, spared not his blows, for he eagerly desired victory for his own sake, and for the sake of his country and his faith.", "반면에 리날도는, 로제로를 아무리 높이 평가했어도 자신의 일격을 아끼지 않았으니, 자기 자신을 위해서, 그리고 자기 나라와 자기 신앙을 위해서 승리를 간절히 바랐기 때문이다."]], [["The Saracens soon perceived that their champion fought feebly, and gave not to Rinaldo such blows as he received from him.", "사라센군은 곧 자신들의 용사가 약하게 싸우고 있으며, 리날도에게서 받는 만큼의 일격을 그에게 돌려주지 못하고 있음을 알아챘다."], ["His disadvantage was so marked that anxiety and shame were manifest on the countenance of Agramant.", "그의 열세가 너무도 뚜렷하여 아그라만트의 얼굴에는 불안과 수치가 역력했다."], ["Melissa, one of the most acute enchantresses that ever lived, seized this moment to disguise herself under the form of Rodomont, that rude and impetuous warrior, who had now for some time been absent from the Saracen camp.", "이제껏 살았던 가장 예리한 마법사 가운데 하나인 멜리사는 이 순간을 틈타, 한동안 사라센 진영을 떠나 있던 그 거칠고 충동적인 전사 로도몬트의 모습으로 자신을 변장시켰다. 아그라만트에게 다가가 그녀가 말했다."], ["Approaching Agramant, she said, “How could you, my lord, have the imprudence of selecting a young man without experience to oppose the most redoubtable warrior of France?", "\"전하, 어찌하여 프랑스에서 가장 가공할 전사를 상대하도록 경험 없는 젊은이를 뽑는 경솔함을 저지르셨나이까? 분명 전하께서는 전하의 무기의 명예도, 전하의 제국의 운명도 도외시하셨음에 틀림없습니다!"], ["Surely you must have been regardless of the honor of your arms, and of the fate of your empire!", "그러나 아직 늦지 않았습니다. 전하의 파멸을 불러올 것이 분명한 그 합의를 지체 없이 깨뜨리소서.\""], ["But it is not too late. Break without delay the agreement which is sure to result in your ruin.”", "그렇게 말하고 그녀는 가까이 서 있던 군사들에게 말을 걸었다."], ["So saying, she addressed the troops who stood near, “Friends,”", "\"벗들이여,\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “follow me; under my guidance every one of you will be a match for a score of those feeble Christians.”", "\"나를 따르라. 내 인도를 받으면 그대들 누구나 저 약해 빠진 기독교도 스무 명을 능히 당해 낼 것이다.\""], ["Agramant, delighted at seeing Rodomont once more at his side, gave his consent, and the Saracens, at the instant, couched their lances, set spurs to their steeds, and swept down upon the French.", "아그라만트는 로도몬트가 다시 자기 곁에 돌아온 것을 보고 기뻐하며 동의를 표했고, 사라센군은 그 즉시 창을 겨누고 군마에 박차를 가하여 프랑스군을 향해 휩쓸어 내려갔다."], ["Melissa, when she saw her work successful, disappeared.", "멜리사는 자신의 일이 성공한 것을 보고는 사라졌다."]], [["Rinaldo and Rogero, seeing the truce broken, and the two armies engaged in general conflict, stopped their battle; their martial fury ceased at once, they joined hands, and resolved to act no more on either side until it should be clearly ascertained which party had failed to observe its oath.", "리날도와 로제로는 휴전이 깨어지고 두 군대가 전면전에 돌입한 것을 보고 자신들의 싸움을 멈추었다. 그들의 호전적인 분노는 단번에 그쳤고, 그들은 손을 맞잡고, 어느 편이 자신의 맹세를 지키지 않았는지가 분명히 밝혀지기 전까지는 어느 편을 위해서도 더는 싸우지 않기로 결심했다."], ["Both renewed their promise to abandon forever the party which had been thus false and perjured.", "두 사람 모두, 이렇게 거짓되고 위약한 편을 영원히 버리겠다는 약속을 새로이 했다."]], [["Meanwhile, the Christians, after the first moment of surprise, met the Saracens with courage redoubled by rage at the treachery of their foes.", "그러는 사이, 기독교군은 처음의 놀란 순간이 지나자, 적의 배신에 대한 분노로 곱절이 된 용기를 가지고 사라센군과 맞섰다."], ["Guido the Wild, brother and rival of Rinaldo, Griffon and Aquilant, sons of Oliver, and numerous others whose names have already been celebrated in our recitals, beat back the assailants, and at last, after prodigious slaughter, forced them to take shelter within the walls of Arles.", "리날도의 아우이자 맞수인 〈야인〉 귀도, 올리버의 아들들인 그리퐁과 아퀼란트, 그리고 우리 이야기들에서 이미 그 이름이 기려진 수많은 다른 이들이 침공자들을 물리쳤고, 마침내 엄청난 살육 끝에 그들을 아를의 성벽 안으로 피신하지 않을 수 없게 만들었다."]], [["We will now return to Orlando, whom we last heard of as furiously mad, and doing a thousand acts of violence in his senseless rage.", "이제 우리는 오를란도에게로 돌아간다. 우리가 그에 관해 마지막으로 들은 것은 그가 미쳐 날뛰며, 분별 없는 광란 속에 천 가지 난폭한 짓을 저지르고 있을 때였다."], ["One day he came to the borders of a stream which intercepted his course.", "어느 날 그는 자신의 길을 가로막는 시냇가에 이르렀다."], ["He swam across it, for he could swim like an otter, and on the other side saw a peasant watering his horse.", "그는 수달처럼 헤엄칠 수 있었으므로 그것을 헤엄쳐 건넜고, 건너편에서 한 농부가 자기 말에 물을 먹이고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["He seized the animal, in spite of the resistance of the peasant, and rode it with furious speed till he", "그는 농부의 저항에도 아랑곳없이 그 짐승을 붙잡아, 미친 듯한 속도로 말을 몰았으니, 마침내"]], [["arrived at the sea-coast, where Spain is divided from Africa by only a narrow strait.", "그는 에스파냐가 좁은 해협 하나만으로 아프리카와 갈라지는 해안에 다다랐다."], ["At the moment of his arrival a vessel had just put off to cross the strait.", "그가 도착한 바로 그 순간, 배 한 척이 해협을 건너기 위해 막 떠나가던 참이었다."], ["She was full of people who, with glass in hand, seemed to be taking a merry farewell of the land, wafted by a favorable breeze.", "배에는 사람들이 가득 타고 있었는데, 그들은 잔을 손에 들고 순풍에 실려 가며 육지를 향해 즐겁게 작별을 고하는 듯이 보였다."], ["The frantic Orlando cried out to them to stop and take him in; but they, having no desire to admit a madman to their company, paid him no attention.", "광기에 사로잡힌 오를란도는 그들에게 멈추어 자기를 태워 달라고 외쳤다. 하지만 그들은 미치광이를 일행에 끼워 줄 마음이 없었으므로 그를 거들떠보지도 않았다."], ["The paladin thought this behavior very uncivil; and by force of blows made his horse carry him into the water in pursuit of the ship.", "기사는 이러한 행동이 매우 무례하다고 여겼다. 그리하여 그는 채찍질을 거듭하여 말을 물속으로 몰아 배를 뒤쫓았다."], ["The wretched animal soon had only his head above water; but as Orlando urged him forward, nothing was left for the poor beast but either to die or swim over to Africa.", "가엾은 짐승은 곧 머리만 물 위에 내놓을 지경이 되었다. 하지만 오를란도가 계속 앞으로 몰아대니, 불쌍한 말에게는 죽거나 아니면 헤엄쳐 아프리카까지 건너가는 것 외에는 다른 길이 남아 있지 않았다."]], [["Already Orlando had lost sight of the bark; distance and the swell of the sea completely hid it from his sight.", "이미 오를란도는 그 작은 배를 시야에서 놓치고 말았다. 거리와 바다의 너울이 배를 그의 눈에서 완전히 가려 버린 것이다."], ["He continued to press his horse forward, till at last it could struggle no more, and sunk beneath him.", "그는 말을 계속 앞으로 몰았고, 마침내 말은 더 이상 버티지 못하고 그를 태운 채 가라앉았다."], ["Orlando, nowise concerned, stretched out his nervous arms, puffing the salt water from before his mouth, and carried his head above the waves.", "오를란도는 조금도 동요하지 않고 억센 팔을 쭉 뻗어 입 앞으로 밀려드는 짠물을 내뿜으며 머리를 파도 위로 들어 올렸다."], ["Fortunately they were not rough, scarce a breath of wind agitated the surface; otherwise, the invincible Orlando would then have met his death.", "다행히 파도는 거칠지 않았고, 수면을 흔드는 바람도 거의 없었다. 그렇지 않았더라면 무적의 오를란도도 그때 죽음을 맞이했을 것이다."], ["But fortune, which it is said favors fools, delivered him from this danger, and landed him safe on the shore of Ceuta.", "하지만 어리석은 자를 돌본다는 운명이 그를 이 위험에서 건져 내어 세우타의 해안에 무사히 데려다 놓았다."], ["Here he rambled along the shore till he came to where the black army of Astolpho held its camp.", "그는 해안을 따라 거닐다가 마침내 아스톨포의 검은 군대가 진을 친 곳에 이르렀다."]], [["Now it happened, just before this time, that a vessel filled with prisoners which Rodomont had taken at the bridge had arrived, and, not knowing of the presence of the Abyssinian army, had sailed right into port, where of course the prisoners and their captors changed places, the former being set at liberty and received with all joy, the latter sent to serve in the galleys.", "그런데 마침 이 무렵, 로도몬트가 다리에서 사로잡은 포로들을 가득 실은 배 한 척이 도착했는데, 아비시니아 군대가 와 있는 줄을 모르고 곧장 항구로 들어와 버렸다. 그리하여 당연히 포로와 그들을 사로잡았던 자들의 처지가 뒤바뀌어, 전자는 풀려나 온갖 환대를 받았고, 후자는 갤리선의 노예로 보내졌다."], ["Astolpho thus found himself surrounded with Christian knights, and he and his friends were exchanging greetings and felicitations, when a noise was heard in the camp, and seemed to increase every moment.", "아스톨포는 이렇게 그리스도교 기사들에게 둘러싸이게 되었고, 그와 그의 벗들이 서로 인사와 축하를 나누고 있을 때 진영에서 소란이 일어 그 소리가 시시각각 커지는 것 같았다."]], [["Astolpho and his friends seized their weapons, mounted their horses, and rode to the quarter whence the noise proceeded.", "아스톨포와 그의 벗들은 무기를 집어 들고 말에 올라 소란이 일어난 쪽으로 달려갔다."], ["Imagine their astonishment when they saw that the tumult was caused by a single man, perfectly naked, and browned with dirt and exposure, but of a force and fury so terrible that he overturned all that offered to lay hands on him.", "그 소동이 단 한 사람 때문에 일어난 것임을 보았을 때 그들의 놀라움이 어떠했을지 상상해 보라. 그자는 완전히 벌거벗었고 더러움과 풍상에 그을려 있었으나, 어찌나 무서운 힘과 광기를 지녔는지 자기에게 손을 대려는 자를 모조리 메다꽂았다."]], [["Astolpho, Dudon, Oliver, and Florismart gazed at him with amazement.", "아스톨포와 두돈, 올리버, 플로리스마르트는 경악하며 그를 바라보았다."], ["It was with difficulty they knew him.", "그가 누구인지 알아보기란 쉽지 않았다."], ["Astolpho, who had been warned of his condition by his holy monitor, was the first to recognize him.", "거룩한 안내자에게 그의 처지를 미리 전해 들었던 아스톨포가 가장 먼저 그를 알아보았다."], ["As the paladins closed round Orlando, the madman dealt one and another a blow of his fist, which, if they had not been in armor, or he had had any weapon, would probably have despatched them; as it was, Dudon and Astolpho measured their length on the sand.", "기사들이 오를란도를 에워싸자 미치광이는 이 사람 저 사람에게 주먹을 휘둘렀는데, 만약 그들이 갑옷을 입지 않았거나 그가 무기라도 들고 있었더라면 그 일격에 목숨을 잃었을 것이다. 그나마 그 정도로 그쳐, 두돈과 아스톨포가 모래밭에 나뒹굴었다."], ["But Florismart seized him from behind, Sansonnet and another grasped his legs, and at last they succeeded in securing him with ropes.", "하지만 플로리스마르트가 뒤에서 그를 붙잡고, 산소네토와 또 다른 자가 그의 다리를 움켜쥐어, 마침내 그들은 밧줄로 그를 결박하는 데 성공했다. 그들은 그를 물가로 데려가 깨끗이 씻겼다."], ["They took him to the water-side and washed him well, and then Astolpho, having first bandaged his mouth so that he could not breathe except through his nose, brought the precious phial, uncorked it, and placed it adroitly under his nostrils, when the good Orlando took it all up in one breath.", "그런 다음 아스톨포는 먼저 그의 입을 싸매어 코로만 숨을 쉴 수 있게 하고, 귀중한 약병을 가져와 마개를 뽑아 솜씨 좋게 그의 콧구멍 밑에 갖다 댔다. 그러자 선량한 오를란도는 그것을 단숨에 모조리 들이마셨다."], ["O marvellous prodigy!", "오, 놀라운 기적이여!"], ["The paladin recovered in an instant all his intelligence.", "기사는 순식간에 모든 지각을 되찾았다."], ["He felt like one who had awakened from a painful dream, in which he had believed that monsters were about to tear him to pieces.", "그는 괴물들이 자기를 갈가리 찢으려 한다고 믿었던 괴로운 꿈에서 깨어난 사람처럼 느껴졌다."], ["He seemed prostrated, silent, and abashed.", "그는 풀이 죽어 말없이 부끄러워하는 듯했다."], ["Florismart, Oliver, and Astolpho stood gazing upon him, while he turned his eyes around and on himself.", "플로리스마르트와 올리버, 아스톨포가 그를 응시하는 동안, 그는 주위와 제 몸을 둘러보았다."], ["He seemed surprised to find himself naked, bound, and stretched on the sea-shore.", "그는 자신이 벌거벗은 채 결박당하여 바닷가에 뉘어 있는 것을 보고 놀란 듯했다."], ["After a few moments he recognized his friends, and spoke to them in a tone so tender that they hastened to unbind him, and to supply him with garments.", "잠시 후 그는 벗들을 알아보고는 어찌나 다정한 어조로 말을 건넸던지, 그들은 서둘러 그를 풀어 주고 옷을 입혔다."], ["Then they exerted themselves to console him, to diminish the weight with which his spirits were oppressed, and to make him forget the wretched condition into which he had been sunk.", "그런 다음 그들은 그를 위로하고, 그의 마음을 짓누르던 무게를 덜어 주며, 그가 빠졌던 비참한 처지를 잊게 하려고 애썼다."]], [["Orlando, in recovering his reason, found himself also delivered from his insane attachment to the queen of Cathay.", "오를란도는 이성을 되찾으면서 카타이의 여왕을 향한 광적인 집착에서도 벗어났음을 알게 되었다."], ["His heart felt now no further influenced by the recollection of her than to be moved with an ardent desire to retrieve his fame by some distinguished exploit.", "이제 그의 마음은 그녀에 대한 기억으로부터, 어떤 빛나는 무공으로 명예를 되찾고자 하는 뜨거운 열망 외에는 아무런 영향도 받지 않았다."], ["Astolpho would gladly have yielded to him the chief command of the army, but Orlando would not take from the friend to whom he owed so much the glory of the campaign; but in everything the two paladins acted in concert, and united their counsels.", "아스톨포는 기꺼이 그에게 군대의 총지휘권을 넘겨주려 했으나, 오를란도는 그토록 많은 신세를 진 벗에게서 이번 원정의 영광을 빼앗으려 하지 않았다. 다만 모든 일에서 두 기사는 협력하여 의논을 함께 모았다."], ["They proposed to make a general assault on the city of Biserta, and were only waiting a favorable moment, when their plan was interrupted by new events.", "그들은 비세르타 시를 총공격하기로 정하고 적당한 때만을 기다리고 있었는데, 그들의 계획은 새로운 사건들로 인해 중단되고 말았다."]], [["Agramant, after the bloody battle which followed the infraction of the truce, found himself so weak that he saw it was in vain to attempt to remain in France.", "아그라만트는 휴전 위반에 뒤따른 피비린내 나는 전투를 치른 뒤, 자신의 세력이 너무도 약해져 프랑스에 머무르려 해 봐야 헛수고임을 깨달았다."], ["So, in concert with Sobrino, the bravest and most trusted of his chiefs, he embarked to return to his own country, having previously sent off his few remaining troops in the same direction.", "그리하여 그는 부하 가운데 가장 용맹하고 신임이 두터운 소브리노와 함께, 미리 남은 소수의 병력을 같은 방향으로 보내 둔 뒤 자기 나라로 돌아가기 위해 배에 올랐다."], ["The vessel which carried Agramant and Sobrino approached the shore where the army of Astolpho lay encamped before Biserta, and having discovered this fact before it was too late, the king commanded the pilot to steer eastward, with a view to seek protection of the King of Egypt.", "아그라만트와 소브리노를 실은 배가 비세르타 앞에 진을 친 아스톨포의 군대가 있는 해안으로 다가갔는데, 너무 늦기 전에 이 사실을 알아챈 왕은 키잡이에게 동쪽으로 배를 몰아 이집트 왕의 보호를 구하라고 명했다."], ["But the weather becoming rough, he consented to the advice of his companions, and sought harbor in an island which lies between Sicily and Africa.", "그러나 날씨가 거칠어지자 그는 동행들의 조언을 받아들여 시칠리아와 아프리카 사이에 있는 어느 섬에 정박처를 찾았다."], ["There he found Gradasso, the warlike king of Sericane, who had come to France to possess himself of the horse Bayard and the sword Durindana; and having procured both these prizes was returning to his own country.", "그곳에서 그는 세리카네의 호전적인 왕 그라다소를 만났는데, 그라다소는 말 바야르와 검 두린다나를 손에 넣기 위해 프랑스에 왔다가 두 전리품을 모두 차지하고 자기 나라로 돌아가던 길이었다. 파리 성벽 아래에서 함께 싸운 전우였던 두 왕은 다정하게 서로를 끌어안았다."], ["The two kings, who had been companions in arms under the walls of Paris, embraced one another affectionately.", "그라다소는 아그라만트가 겪은 좌절을 안타까이 듣고서 자신의 군대와 몸을 그에게 바치겠노라고 했다."], ["Gradasso learned with regret the reverses of Agramant, and offered him his troops and his person.", "그는 이집트에 도움을 청하는 것을 강력히 만류했다. \"위대한 폼페이우스를 기억하십시오.\""], ["He strongly deprecated resorting to Egypt for aid.", "그가 말했다. \"그 불길한 해안을 피하셔야 합니다."], ["“Remember the great Pompey,”", "제 계획은 이렇습니다.\""], ["said he, “and shun that fatal shore.", "그가 이어 말했다."], ["My plan,” he continued, “is this: I mean to challenge Orlando to single combat.", "\"저는 오를란도에게 일대일 결투를 신청할 생각입니다."], ["Possessed of such a sword and steed as mine, if he were made of steel or bronze, he could not escape me.", "저의 이런 검과 군마를 가진 자에게라면, 그가 강철이나 청동으로 되어 있다 한들 저를 벗어날 수 없을 것입니다."], ["He being removed, there will be no difficulty in driving back the Abyssinians.", "그가 제거되면 아비시니아인들을 몰아내는 데는 어려움이 없을 것입니다."], ["We will rouse against them the Moslem nations from the other side of the Nile, the Arabians, Persians, and Chaldeans, who will soon make Senapus recall his army to defend his own territories.”", "우리는 나일 강 건너편의 이슬람 민족들, 곧 아라비아인과 페르시아인과 칼데아인을 그들에게 맞서도록 일으킬 것이고, 그들은 곧 세나푸스로 하여금 제 영토를 지키기 위해 군대를 불러들이게 할 것입니다.\""]], [["Agramant approved this advice except in one particular.", "아그라만트는 한 가지만 빼고 이 조언을 받아들였다. \"오를란도와 싸우는 것은 나의 몫이오.\""], ["“It is for me,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “to combat Orlando; I cannot with honor devolve that duty on another.”", "\"그 임무를 명예롭게 남에게 떠넘길 수는 없소.\""]], [["“Let us adopt a third course,”", "\"세 번째 방도를 택합시다.\""], ["said the aged warrior Sobrino.", "노년의 전사 소브리노가 말했다."], ["“I would not willingly remain a simple spectator of such a contest.", "\"저 역시 그런 결투를 그저 구경꾼으로만 지켜보고 싶지는 않습니다."], ["Let us send three squires to the shore of Africa to challenge Orlando and any two of his companions in arms to meet us three in this island of Lampedusa.”", "종자 셋을 아프리카 해안으로 보내어 오를란도와 그의 전우 가운데 둘에게 이 람페두사 섬에서 우리 셋과 맞붙자고 도전하게 합시다.\""]], [["This counsel was adopted; the three squires sped on their way; and now presented themselves, and rehearsed their message to the Christian knights.", "이 조언이 채택되어 종자 셋이 길을 떠났다. 그리고 이제 그들은 그리스도교 기사들 앞에 나타나 전갈을 전했다."]], [["Orlando was delighted, and rewarded the squires with rich gifts.", "오를란도는 기뻐하며 종자들에게 후한 선물을 내렸다."], ["He had already resolved to seek Gradasso and compel him to restore Durindana, which he had learned was in his possession.", "그는 이미 그라다소를 찾아내어, 자기가 소유하고 있다고 들은 두린다나를 돌려놓도록 강요하기로 마음먹고 있던 터였다."], ["For his two companions the Count chose his faithful friend Florismart and his cousin Oliver.", "백작은 두 전우로 충직한 벗 플로리스마르트와 사촌 올리버를 골랐다."]], [["The three warriors embarked, and sailing with a favorable wind, the second morning showed them, on their right, the island where this important battle was to be fought.", "세 전사는 배에 올라 순풍을 타고 항해했고, 이튿날 아침이 되자 오른편으로 이 중대한 결투가 벌어질 섬이 보였다."], ["Orlando and his two companions, having landed, pitched their tent.", "오를란도와 두 전우는 상륙하여 천막을 쳤다."], ["Agramant had placed his opposite.", "아그라만트는 그 맞은편에 자기 천막을 세웠다."], ["Next morning, as soon as Aurora brightened the edges of the horizon, the warriors of both parties armed themselves and mounted their horses.", "이튿날 아침, 새벽 여신이 지평선 가장자리를 밝히자마자 양쪽 진영의 전사들은 무장을 갖추고 말에 올랐다."], ["They took their positions, face to face, lowered their lances, placed them in rest, clapped spurs to their horses, and flew to the charge.", "그들은 서로 마주 보고 자리를 잡아 창을 내리고 창받이에 걸친 다음, 말에 박차를 가하여 돌격해 들어갔다."], ["Orlando met the charge of Gradasso.", "오를란도는 그라다소의 돌격을 맞받았다."], ["The paladin was unmoved, but his horse could not sustain the terrible shock of Bayard.", "기사는 미동도 하지 않았으나, 그의 말은 바야르의 무시무시한 충격을 견디지 못했다."], ["He recoiled, staggered, and fell some paces behind.", "말은 뒤로 물러서며 휘청거리다가 몇 걸음 뒤로 쓰러졌다."], ["Orlando tried to raise him, but, finding his efforts unavailing, seized his shield, and drew his famous Balisardo.", "오를란도는 말을 일으켜 세우려 했으나 헛수고임을 알고는 방패를 집어 들고 그 유명한 발리사르도를 뽑았다."], ["Meanwhile Agramant and the brave Oliver gained no advantage, one or the other; but Florismart unhorsed the King Sobrino.", "그동안 아그라만트와 용감한 올리버는 어느 쪽도 우세를 점하지 못했으나, 플로리스마르트는 소브리노 왕을 말에서 떨어뜨렸다."], ["Having brought his foe to the ground, he would not pursue his victory, but hastened to attack Gradasso, who had overthrown Orlando.", "적을 땅에 쓰러뜨린 그는 승세를 몰아가지 않고, 오를란도를 메다꽂은 그라다소를 공격하러 서둘러 갔다."], ["Seeing him thus engaged, Orlando would not interfere, but ran with sword upraised upon Sobrino, and with one blow deprived him of sense and motion.", "오를란도는 그가 그렇게 맞붙은 것을 보고 끼어들지 않고, 검을 치켜든 채 소브리노에게 달려들어 단 일격에 그의 의식과 움직임을 빼앗았다."], ["Believing him dead, he next turned to aid his beloved Florismart.", "그가 죽었다고 여긴 오를란도는 다음으로 사랑하는 플로리스마르트를 돕기 위해 몸을 돌렸다."], ["That brave paladin, neither in horse nor arms equal to his antagonist, could but parry and evade the blows of the terrible Durindana.", "그 용감한 기사는 말도 무기도 상대에게 미치지 못하여, 무시무시한 두린다나의 일격을 그저 받아넘기고 피할 수밖에 없었다."], ["Orlando, eager to succor him, was delayed for a moment in securing and mounting the horse of the King Sobrino.", "오를란도는 그를 구하려고 서둘렀으나, 소브리노 왕의 말을 붙잡아 올라타느라 잠시 지체했다."], ["It was but an instant, and with sword upraised, he rushed upon Gradasso who, noways disconcerted at the onset of this second foe, shouted his defiance, and thrust at him with his sword, but, having miscalculated the distance, scarcely reached him, and failed to pierce his mail.", "그것은 한순간에 불과했고, 그는 검을 치켜든 채 그라다소에게 달려들었다. 그라다소는 이 두 번째 적의 공격에도 조금도 당황하지 않고 호기롭게 도전을 외치며 검을 찔러 왔으나, 거리를 잘못 가늠한 탓에 그에게 거의 닿지 못하여 사슬 갑옷을 뚫지 못했다."], ["Orlando, in return, dealt him a blow with Balisardo, which wounded as it fell face, breast, and thigh, and, if he had been a little nearer, would have cleft him in twain.", "답례로 오를란도는 발리사르도로 일격을 가했는데, 그것이 떨어지며 얼굴과 가슴과 넓적다리를 베었고, 조금만 더 가까웠더라면 그를 두 동강 냈을 것이다."], ["Sobrino, by this time recovered from his swoon, though severely wounded, raised himself on his legs, and looked to see how he might aid his friends.", "이때쯤 소브리노는 심한 상처를 입긴 했어도 혼절에서 깨어나 다리로 몸을 일으켜, 어떻게 하면 벗들을 도울 수 있을지 살폈다."], ["Observing Agramant hard pressed by Oliver, he thrust his sword into the bowels of the latter’s horse, which fell, and bore down his master, entangling his leg as he fell, so that Oliver could not extricate himself.", "아그라만트가 올리버에게 거세게 몰리는 것을 보고, 그는 올리버의 말 옆구리에 검을 찔러 넣었다. 말은 쓰러지며 주인을 짓눌렀고, 쓰러질 때 그의 다리를 휘감아 올리버는 빠져나올 수가 없었다."], ["Florismart saw the danger of his friend, and ran upon Sobrino with his horse, overthrew him, and then turned to defend himself from Agramant.", "플로리스마르트는 벗의 위기를 보고 말을 몰아 소브리노에게 달려들어 그를 쓰러뜨린 다음, 몸을 돌려 아그라만트를 막아 냈다."], ["They were not unequally matched, for though Agramant, mounted on Brigliadoro, had an advantage over Florismart, whose horse was but indifferent, yet Agramant had received a serious wound in his encounter with Oliver.", "둘의 실력은 어슷비슷했는데, 브리글리아도로에 올라탄 아그라만트가 변변찮은 말을 탄 플로리스마르트보다 유리하기는 했으나, 아그라만트는 올리버와 맞붙었을 때 심한 상처를 입은 터였다."]], [["Nothing could exceed the fury of the encounter between Orlando and Gradasso.", "오를란도와 그라다소의 격돌이 보여 준 격렬함은 그 무엇과도 견줄 수 없었다."], ["Durindana, in the hands of Gradasso, clove asunder whatever it struck; but such was the skill of Orlando, who perfectly knew the danger to which he was exposed from a stroke of that weapon, it had not yet struck him in such a way as to inflict a wound.", "그라다소의 손에 들린 두린다나는 닿는 것은 무엇이든 두 동강 냈다. 그러나 그 무기의 일격이 자신을 어떤 위험에 빠뜨리는지 완벽히 알고 있던 오를란도의 솜씨가 어찌나 뛰어났던지, 그 검은 아직 그에게 상처를 입힐 만큼 제대로 닿지 못했다."], ["Meanwhile, Gradasso was bleeding from many wounds, and his rage and incaution increased every moment.", "그러는 동안 그라다소는 여러 상처에서 피를 흘리고 있었고, 그의 분노와 무모함은 시시각각 커져 갔다."], ["In his desperation he lifted Durindana with both hands, and struck so terrible a blow full on the helmet of Orlando, that for a moment it stunned the paladin.", "절망에 빠진 그는 두 손으로 두린다나를 치켜들어, 오를란도의 투구를 정통으로 내리쳤는데, 그 일격이 어찌나 무시무시했던지 기사는 한순간 정신을 잃었다."], ["He dropped the reins, and his frightened horse scoured with him over the plain.", "그는 고삐를 떨어뜨렸고, 겁에 질린 말은 그를 태운 채 들판을 내달렸다."], ["Gradasso turned to pursue him, but at that moment saw Florismart in the very act of striking a fatal blow at Agramant, whom he had unhorsed.", "그라다소는 그를 뒤쫓으려고 몸을 돌렸으나, 바로 그 순간 플로리스마르트가 말에서 떨어뜨린 아그라만트에게 막 치명적인 일격을 가하려는 것을 보았다."], ["While Florismart was wholly intent upon completing his victory, Gradasso plunged his sword into his side.", "플로리스마르트가 승리를 마무리하는 데 온통 정신이 팔려 있는 사이, 그라다소는 그의 옆구리에 검을 찔러 넣었다."], ["Florismart fell from his horse, and bathed the plain with his blood.", "플로리스마르트는 말에서 떨어져 자신의 피로 들판을 적셨다."]], [["Orlando recovered himself just in time to see the deed.", "오를란도는 마침 그 행위를 볼 수 있을 때 정신을 차렸다."], ["Whether rage or grief predominated in his breast, I cannot tell; but, seizing Balisardo with fury, his first blow fell upon Agramant, who was nearest to him, and smote his head from his shoulders.", "그의 가슴속에 분노가 컸는지 슬픔이 컸는지 나는 알 수 없다. 다만 그는 분노에 차 발리사르도를 움켜쥐었고, 그 첫 일격이 가장 가까이 있던 아그라만트에게 떨어져 그의 목을 어깨에서 베어 냈다."], ["At this sight Gradasso for the first time felt his courage sink, and a dark presentiment of death came over him.", "이 광경을 본 그라다소는 처음으로 용기가 꺾이는 것을 느꼈고, 죽음을 예감하는 어두운 그림자가 그를 덮쳤다."], ["He hardly stood on his defence when Orlando cast himself upon him, and gave him a fatal thrust.", "그가 미처 방어 자세를 채 갖추기도 전에 오를란도가 그에게 몸을 던져 치명적인 일격을 찔러 넣었다."], ["The sword penetrated his ribs, and came out a palm’s breadth on the other side of his body.", "검은 그의 갈비뼈를 꿰뚫고 몸 반대편으로 한 뼘이나 삐져나왔다."]], [["Thus fell beneath the sword of the most illustrious paladin of France the bravest warrior of the Saracen host.", "이리하여 프랑스에서 가장 빛나는 기사의 검 아래 사라센 군대에서 가장 용맹한 전사가 쓰러졌다."], ["Orlando then, as if despising his victory, leaped lightly to the ground, and ran to his dear friend Florismart, embraced him, and bathed him with his tears.", "그러자 오를란도는 마치 자신의 승리를 대수롭지 않게 여기듯 가볍게 땅으로 뛰어내려, 사랑하는 벗 플로리스마르트에게 달려가 그를 끌어안고 눈물로 적셨다."], ["Florismart still breathed.", "플로리스마르트는 아직 숨을 쉬고 있었다."], ["He could even command his voice to utter a few parting words: “Dear friend, do not forget me,—give me your prayers,—and oh!", "그는 작별의 몇 마디를 내뱉을 만큼 목소리를 가다듬을 수도 있었다. \"사랑하는 벗이여, 나를 잊지 말게."], ["be a brother to Flordelis.”", "나를 위해 기도해 주게. 그리고 오, 플로르델리스에게 형제가 되어 주게.\""], ["He died in uttering her name.", "그는 그녀의 이름을 부르며 숨을 거두었다."]], [["After a few moments given to grief Orlando turned to look for his other companion and his late foes.", "슬픔에 잠긴 잠시가 지난 뒤 오를란도는 또 다른 전우와 방금 전의 적들을 살피러 몸을 돌렸다."], ["Oliver lay oppressed with the weight of his horse, from which he had in vain struggled to liberate himself.", "올리버는 자신의 말 무게에 짓눌린 채 누워 있었고, 그것에서 빠져나오려고 헛되이 몸부림쳤었다."], ["Orlando extricated him with difficulty; he then raised Sobrino from the earth, and committed him to his squire, treating him as gently as if he had been his own brother.", "오를란도는 힘겹게 그를 끌어냈다. 그런 다음 소브리노를 땅에서 일으켜 자신의 종자에게 맡겼는데, 마치 그가 친형제라도 되는 양 다정하게 대했다."], ["For this terrible warrior was the most generous of men to a fallen foe.", "이 무시무시한 전사는 쓰러진 적에게는 누구보다도 너그러운 사람이었기 때문이다."], ["He took Bayard and Brigliadoro, with the arms of the conquered knights; their bodies and their other spoils he remitted to their attendants.", "그는 바야르와 브리글리아도로를, 패배한 기사들의 무기와 함께 취했다. 그들의 시신과 나머지 노획물은 그들의 시종들에게 돌려보냈다."]], [["But who can tell the grief of Flordelis when she saw the warriors return, and found not Florismart as usual after absence hasten to her side.", "그러나 전사들이 돌아오는 것을 보고도, 헤어졌다가 만날 때면 늘 그러하듯 플로리스마르트가 제 곁으로 달려오지 않는 것을 알았을 때 플로르델리스의 슬픔이 어떠했는지 그 누가 말할 수 있으랴."], ["She knew by the aspect of the others that her lord was slain.", "그녀는 다른 이들의 표정을 보고 자신의 주인이 죽었음을 알아차렸다."], ["At the thought, and before the question could pass her lips, she fell senseless upon the ground.", "그 생각이 들자, 질문이 입술을 채 떠나기도 전에 그녀는 의식을 잃고 땅에 쓰러졌다."], ["When life returned, and she learned the truth of her worst fears, she bitterly upbraided herself that she had let him depart without her.", "생기가 돌아와 자신의 가장 무서운 두려움이 사실임을 알게 되었을 때, 그녀는 자기 없이 그를 떠나보낸 것을 두고 모질게 자책했다."], ["“I might have saved him by a single cry when his enemy dealt him that treacherous blow, or I might have thrown myself between and given my worthless life for his.", "\"그의 적이 그 비겁한 일격을 가했을 때 내가 한 번만 외쳤어도 그를 구할 수 있었을 텐데, 아니면 사이에 몸을 던져 그를 위해 내 보잘것없는 목숨을 바칠 수도 있었을 텐데."], ["Or if no more, I might have heard his last words, I might have given him a last kiss.”", "설령 그러지는 못했더라도, 그의 마지막 말을 듣고 마지막 입맞춤이라도 해 줄 수 있었을 텐데.\""], ["So she lamented, and could not be comforted.", "그녀는 이렇게 한탄하며 위로받지 못했다. 로제로와 브라다만테"]], [["# ROGERO AND BRADAMANTE", "# ROGERO AND BRADAMANTE"]], [["————\nAfter the interruption of the combat with Rinaldo, as we have related, Rogero was perplexed with doubts what course to take.", "앞서 이야기한 대로 리날도와의 결투가 중단된 뒤, 로제로는 어떤 길을 택해야 할지 의혹에 사로잡혔다. 조약의 조건은 그것을 깨뜨린 아그라만트를 저버리고 충성을 샤를마뉴에게 옮기라고 요구했다."], ["The terms of the treaty required him to abandon Agramant, who had broken it, and to transfer his allegiance to Charlemagne; and his love for Bradamante called him in the same direction; but unwillingness to desert his prince and leader in the hour of distress forbade this course.", "브라다만테를 향한 사랑도 그를 같은 방향으로 불렀다. 하지만 곤경에 처한 자신의 군주이자 지휘관을 저버리려 하지 않는 마음이 이 길을 가로막았다."], ["Embarking, therefore, for Africa, he took his way to rejoin the Saracen army; but was arrested midway by a storm which drove the vessel on a rock.", "그리하여 그는 아프리카로 가는 배에 올라 사라센 군대와 다시 합류하러 길을 떠났으나, 도중에 폭풍을 만나 배가 바위에 부딪히면서 발이 묶이고 말았다."], ["The crew took to their boat, but that was quickly swamped in the waves, and Rogero with the rest were compelled to swim for their lives.", "선원들은 작은 배에 옮겨 탔으나 그 배도 곧 파도에 잠겼고, 로제로는 나머지 사람들과 함께 목숨을 부지하려 헤엄칠 수밖에 없었다."], ["Then while buffeting the waves Rogero bethought him of his sin in so long delaying his Christian profession, and vowed in his heart that, if he should live to reach the land, he would no longer delay to be baptized.", "그러다 파도와 싸우는 동안 로제로는 그리스도교 신앙 고백을 그토록 오래 미룬 자신의 죄를 떠올리고, 만약 살아서 뭍에 닿게 된다면 더는 미루지 않고 세례를 받겠노라고 마음속으로 맹세했다."], ["His vows were heard and answered; he succeeded in reaching the shore, and was aided and relieved on landing by a pious hermit, whose cell overlooked the sea.", "그의 맹세는 들어졌고 응답을 받았다. 그는 무사히 해안에 다다랐으며, 상륙하자마자 바다가 내려다보이는 암자에 사는 경건한 은자의 도움과 구제를 받았다."], ["From him he received baptism, having first passed some days with him, partaking his humble fare, and receiving instruction in the doctrines of the Christian faith.", "그는 먼저 그와 며칠을 함께 지내며 소박한 음식을 나누고 그리스도교 신앙의 교리를 배운 뒤, 그에게서 세례를 받았다."]], [["While these things were going on, Rinaldo, who had set out on his way to seek Gradasso and recover Bayard from him, hearing on his way of the great things which were doing in Africa, repaired thither to bear his part in them.", "이런 일들이 벌어지고 있는 동안, 그라다소를 찾아 그에게서 바야르를 되찾으러 길을 떠났던 리날도는 도중에 아프리카에서 큰일이 벌어지고 있다는 소식을 듣고, 거기에 한몫 끼려고 그곳으로 향했다."], ["He arrived too late to do more than join his friends in lamenting the loss of Florismart, and to rejoice with them in their victory over the Pagan knights.", "그가 도착했을 때는 이미 너무 늦어, 벗들과 함께 플로리스마르트의 죽음을 애도하고 이교도 기사들에게 거둔 승리를 함께 기뻐하는 것 외에는 할 일이 없었다."], ["On the death of their king the Africans gave up the contest, Biserta submitted, and the Christian knights had only to dismiss their forces, and return home.", "자기네 왕이 죽자 아프리카인들은 싸움을 포기했고, 비세르타는 항복했으며, 그리스도교 기사들은 군대를 해산하고 고국으로 돌아가기만 하면 되었다."], ["Astolpho took leave of his Abyssinian army, and sent them back laden with spoil to their own country, not forgetting to intrust to them the bag which held the winds, by means of which they were enabled to cross the sandy desert again without danger, and did not untie it till they reached their own country.", "아스톨포는 아비시니아 군대에 작별을 고하고, 그들을 전리품을 가득 실어 본국으로 돌려보냈다. 그러면서 바람을 가둔 자루를 그들에게 맡기는 것도 잊지 않았는데, 그 자루 덕분에 그들은 다시 위험 없이 모래사막을 건널 수 있었고, 자기 나라에 이를 때까지 자루를 풀지 않았다."]], [["Orlando now, with Oliver, who much needed the surgeon’s care, and Sobrino, to whom equal attention was shown, sailed in a swift vessel to Sicily, bearing with him the body of Florismart, to be laid in Christian earth.", "이제 오를란도는 외과의의 보살핌이 절실했던 올리버, 그리고 그와 똑같은 정성을 받은 소브리노와 함께, 그리스도교의 땅에 묻기 위해 플로리스마르트의 시신을 싣고 빠른 배에 올라 시칠리아로 향했다."], ["Rinaldo accompanied them, as did Sansonnet and the other Christian leaders.", "리날도가 그들과 동행했고, 산소네토와 다른 그리스도교 지휘관들도 함께했다."], ["Arrived at Sicily, the funeral was solemnized with all the rites of religion, and with the profound grief of those who had known Florismart, or had heard of his fame.", "시칠리아에 다다른 그들은 종교의 온갖 의식을 갖추어, 그리고 플로리스마르트를 알았거나 그의 명성을 들었던 이들의 깊은 슬픔 속에 장례를 엄숙히 치렀다."], ["Then they resumed their course, steering for Marseilles.", "그런 다음 그들은 다시 마르세유를 향해 항로에 올랐다."], ["But Oliver’s wound grew worse instead of better, and his sufferings so distressed his friends that they conferred together, not knowing what to do.", "그러나 올리버의 상처는 나아지기는커녕 더 악화되었고, 그의 고통이 어찌나 벗들을 괴롭혔던지 그들은 어찌해야 할지 몰라 함께 의논했다."], ["Then said the pilot, “We are not far from an isle where a holy hermit dwells alone in the midst of the sea.", "그때 키잡이가 말했다. \"여기서 멀지 않은 곳에 거룩한 은자가 홀로 바다 한가운데에 사는 섬이 있습니다."], ["It is said none seek his counsel or his aid in vain.", "그의 조언이나 도움을 구해 헛되이 돌아간 사람은 없다고들 합니다."], ["He hath wrought marvellous cures, and if you resort to that holy man without doubt he can heal the knight.”", "그는 놀라운 치유를 행해 왔으니, 그 거룩한 분을 찾아가신다면 의심할 바 없이 저 기사를 낫게 해 주실 것입니다.\""], ["Orlando bade him steer thither, and soon the bark was laid safely beside the lonely rock; the wounded man was lowered into their boat, and carried by the crew to the hermit’s cell.", "오를란도는 그쪽으로 배를 몰라고 명했고, 곧 작은 배는 그 외딴 바위 곁에 무사히 닿았다. 부상자는 작은 배로 내려져 선원들의 손에 실려 은자의 암자로 옮겨졌다."], ["It was the same hermit with whom Rogero had taken refuge after his shipwreck, by whom he had been baptized, and with whom he was now staying, absorbed in sacred studies and meditations.", "그는 로제로가 난파 후 몸을 의탁했던 바로 그 은자였으니, 로제로가 그에게서 세례를 받고 지금도 그와 함께 머물며 거룩한 학문과 명상에 잠겨 있던 그 은자였다."]], [["The holy man received Orlando and the rest with kindness, and inquired their errand; and being told that they had come for help for one who, warring for the Christian faith, was brought to perilous pass by a sad wound, he straightway undertook the cure.", "거룩한 그는 오를란도와 나머지 사람들을 친절히 맞이하며 그들이 온 용건을 물었다. 그리스도교 신앙을 위해 싸우다가 안타까운 상처로 위태로운 처지에 빠진 자를 도우러 왔다는 말을 듣고, 그는 곧바로 치료에 나섰다."], ["His applications were simple, but they were seconded by his prayers.", "그가 쓴 약은 단순했으나, 그의 기도가 거기에 힘을 보탰다."], ["The paladin was soon relieved from pain, and in a few days his foot was perfectly restored to soundness.", "기사는 곧 고통에서 벗어났고, 며칠 만에 그의 발은 완전히 건강을 되찾았다."], ["Sobrino, as soon as he perceived the holy monk perform that wonder, cast aside his false prophet, and with contrite heart owned the true God, and demanded baptism at his hands.", "소브리노는 거룩한 수도자가 그 기적을 행하는 것을 보자마자 자신의 거짓 예언자를 내던지고, 뉘우치는 마음으로 참된 하느님을 받아들여 그의 손에 세례를 청했다."], ["The hermit granted his request, and also by his prayers restored him to health, while all the Christian knights rejoiced in his conversion almost as much as at the restoration of Oliver.", "은자는 그의 청을 들어주었고, 또한 기도로써 그의 건강을 회복시켰다. 그리하여 모든 그리스도교 기사들은 그의 개종을 올리버의 회복에 못지않게 기뻐했다."], ["More than all Rogero felt joy and gratitude, and daily grew in grace and faith.", "누구보다도 로제로가 기쁨과 감사를 느꼈고, 날로 은총과 신앙이 깊어졌다."]], [["Rogero was known by fame to all the Christian knights, but not even Rinaldo knew him by sight, though he had proved his prowess in combat.", "로제로의 명성은 모든 그리스도교 기사들에게 알려져 있었으나, 결투에서 그의 용맹을 증명해 본 리날도조차 그를 얼굴로는 알아보지 못했다."], ["Sobrino made him known to them, and great was the joy of all when they found one whose valor and courtesy were renowned through the world no longer an enemy and unbeliever, but a convert and champion of the true faith.", "소브리노가 그를 그들에게 소개했고, 그 용맹과 예의가 온 세상에 떨친 자가 더 이상 적이자 불신자가 아니라 참된 신앙으로 개종한 투사임을 알게 되자 모두 크게 기뻐했다. 모두 기사 주위로 몰려들었다."], ["All press about the knight; one grasps his hand, another locks him fast in his embrace; but more than all the rest, Rinaldo cherished him, for he more than any knew his worth.", "한 사람은 그의 손을 움켜쥐었고, 또 한 사람은 그를 꼭 끌어안았다. 그러나 누구보다도 리날도가 그를 아꼈으니, 그가 누구보다도 그의 진가를 잘 알았기 때문이다."]], [["It was not long before Rogero confided to his friend the hopes he entertained of a union with his sister, and Rinaldo frankly gave his sanction to the proposal.", "오래지 않아 로제로는 자신의 누이와 맺어지기를 바라는 희망을 벗에게 털어놓았고, 리날도는 그 제안에 흔쾌히 동의를 표했다."], ["But causes unknown to the paladin were at that very time interposing obstacles to its success.", "그러나 기사가 알지 못하는 사정들이 바로 그때 그 성사를 가로막는 장애물이 되고 있었다."]], [["The fame of the beauty and worth of Bradamante had reached the ears of the Grecian Emperor, Constantine, and he had sent to Charlemagne to demand the hand of his niece for Leo, his son, and the heir to his dominions.", "브라다만테의 아름다움과 가치에 대한 명성이 그리스 황제 콘스탄티누스의 귀에까지 들어가, 그는 사람을 보내어 자기 아들이자 영토의 후계자인 레오를 위해 샤를마뉴에게 그 조카딸의 손을 청했다."], ["Duke Aymon, her father, had only reserved his consent until he should first have spoken with his son Rinaldo, now absent.", "그녀의 아버지인 에이몬 공작은 지금 부재중인 아들 리날도와 먼저 이야기를 나눌 때까지만 승낙을 미루어 두고 있었다."]], [["The warriors now prepared to resume their voyage.", "이제 전사들은 다시 항해에 나설 채비를 했다."], ["Rogero took a tender farewell of the good hermit who had taught him the true faith.", "로제로는 자신에게 참된 신앙을 가르쳐 준 선량한 은자에게 다정히 작별을 고했다."], ["Orlando restored to him the horse and arms which were rightly his, not even asserting his claim to Balisarda, that sword which he himself had won from the enchantress.", "오를란도는 마땅히 그의 것인 말과 무기를 그에게 돌려주었으며, 자신이 여마법사에게서 손에 넣은 검 발리사르다에 대해서도 권리를 주장하지 않았다."]], [["The hermit gave his blessing to the band, and they re-embarked.", "은자는 그 무리에게 축복을 내렸고, 그들은 다시 배에 올랐다."], ["The passage was speedy, and very soon they arrived in the harbor of Marseilles.", "항해는 빨라서, 그들은 아주 곧 마르세유 항구에 도착했다."]], [["Astolpho, when he had dismissed his troops, mounted the Hippogriff, and at one flight shot over to Sardinia, thence to Corsica, thence, turning slightly to the left, hovered over Provence, and alighted in the neighborhood of Marseilles.", "아스톨포는 군대를 해산한 뒤 히포그리프에 올라, 한 번의 비행으로 사르데냐로 날아갔고, 거기서 코르시카로, 다시 거기서 살짝 왼쪽으로 방향을 틀어 프로방스 위를 맴돌다가 마르세유 인근에 내려앉았다."], ["There he did what he had been commanded to do by the holy saint; he unbridled the Hippogriff, and turned him loose to seek his own retreats, never more to be galled with saddle or bit.", "그곳에서 그는 거룩한 성인이 명한 대로 했다. 곧 히포그리프의 굴레를 벗겨, 다시는 안장이나 재갈에 시달리지 않도록 제 보금자리를 찾아가게 풀어 준 것이다."], ["The horn had lost its marvellous power ever since the visit to the moon.", "뿔피리는 달을 방문한 이후로 그 놀라운 힘을 잃은 터였다."]], [["Astolpho reached Marseilles the very day when Orlando, Rinaldo, Oliver, Sobrino, and Rogero arrived there.", "아스톨포는 오를란도, 리날도, 올리버, 소브리노, 로제로가 마르세유에 도착한 바로 그날 그곳에 닿았다."], ["Charles had already heard the news of the defeat of the Saracen kings, and all the accompanying events.", "샤를은 이미 사라센 왕들의 패배와 그에 따른 모든 사건의 소식을 들은 터였다."], ["On learning the approach of the gallant knights, he sent forward some of his most illustrious nobles to receive them, and himself, with the rest of his court, kings, dukes, and peers, the queen, and a fair and gorgeous band of ladies, set forward from Arles to meet them.", "용맹한 기사들이 다가온다는 것을 알자, 그는 가장 빼어난 귀족들 몇몇을 먼저 보내어 그들을 맞이하게 했고, 자신은 나머지 궁정의 왕들과 공작들과 중신들, 왕비, 그리고 아름답고 화려한 귀부인들의 무리를 거느리고 그들을 맞으러 아를에서 출발했다."]], [["No sooner were the mutual greetings interchanged, than Orlando and his friends led forward Rogero, and presented him to the Emperor.", "서로 인사를 나누기가 무섭게 오를란도와 그의 벗들은 로제로를 앞으로 이끌어 황제에게 소개했다."], ["They vouch him son of Rogero, Duke of Risa, one of the most renowned of Christian warriors, by adverse fortune stolen in his infancy, and brought up by Saracens in the false faith, now by a kind Providence converted, and restored to fill the place his father once held among the foremost champions of the throne and Church.", "그들은 그가 그리스도교 전사 가운데 가장 이름난 이인 리사 공작 로제로의 아들로서, 불운한 운명으로 어릴 적에 도둑맞아 사라센인들 손에 거짓 신앙으로 길러졌으나, 이제 자비로운 섭리로 개종하여 그의 아버지가 한때 왕좌와 교회의 으뜸가는 투사들 사이에서 차지했던 자리를 채우러 돌아왔노라고 보증했다."]], [["Rogero had alighted from his horse, and stood respectfully before the Emperor.", "로제로는 말에서 내려 황제 앞에 공손히 섰다."], ["Charlemagne bade him remount and ride beside him; and omitted nothing which might do him honor in sight of his martial train.", "샤를마뉴는 그에게 다시 말에 올라 자기 곁에서 나란히 가자고 했고, 자신의 무사 행렬이 보는 앞에서 그를 영예롭게 할 만한 것이라면 무엇 하나 빠뜨리지 않았다."], ["With pomp triumphal and with festive cheer the troop returned to the city; the streets were decorated with garlands, the houses hung with rich tapestry, and flowers fell like rain upon the conquering host from the hands of fair dames and damsels, from every balcony and window.", "개선의 화려함과 축제의 환호 속에 일행은 도시로 돌아왔다. 거리는 화환으로 장식되었고, 집집마다 값진 융단을 내걸었으며, 발코니와 창마다 아름다운 귀부인과 처녀들의 손에서 꽃이 비처럼 정복자 무리 위로 쏟아졌다."], ["So welcomed, the mighty Emperor passed on till he reached the royal palace, where many days he feasted, high in hall, with his lords, amid tourney, revel, dance, and song.", "이렇게 환대를 받으며 위대한 황제는 왕궁에 이를 때까지 나아갔고, 그곳에서 그는 여러 날을 신하들과 함께 높은 홀에서 마상 시합과 향연과 춤과 노래 속에 잔치를 벌였다."]], [["When Rinaldo told his father, Duke Aymon, how he had promised his sister to Rogero, his father heard him with indignation, having set his heart on seeing her united to the Grecian Emperor’s son.", "리날도가 아버지 에이몬 공작에게 자기 누이를 로제로에게 약속했다고 말하자, 그 아버지는 그 말을 분노하며 들었으니, 누이가 그리스 황제의 아들과 맺어지기를 마음에 두고 있었기 때문이다."], ["The Lady Beatrice, her mother, also appealed to Bradamante herself to reject a knight who had neither title nor lands, and give the preference to one who would make her Empress of the wide Levant.", "그녀의 어머니인 베아트리체 부인 또한 브라다만테 본인에게 직접 호소하여, 작위도 영지도 없는 기사를 물리치고 그녀를 드넓은 동방의 황후로 만들어 줄 사람을 택하라고 했다."], ["But Bradamante, though respect forbade her to refuse her mother’s entreaty, would not promise to do what her heart repelled, and answered only with a sigh, until she was alone, and then gave a loose to tears.", "그러나 브라다만테는 어머니의 간청을 거절하기에는 존경심이 가로막았으나, 마음이 거부하는 일을 하겠노라고 약속하지도 않고 그저 한숨으로만 답했다. 그러다 홀로 남게 되자 눈물을 마음껏 쏟았다."]], [["Meanwhile Rogero, indignant that a stranger should presume to rob him of his bride, determined to seek the Prince of Greece, and defy him to mortal combat.", "한편 로제로는 낯선 자가 감히 자기 신부를 빼앗으려 한다는 데 분개하여, 그리스 왕자를 찾아내어 죽음을 건 결투를 신청하기로 마음먹었다."], ["With this design he donned his armor, but exchanged his crest and emblazonment, and bore instead a white unicorn upon a crimson field.", "이러한 계획으로 그는 갑옷을 입었으나, 투구 장식과 문장은 바꾸어, 진홍빛 바탕에 흰 일각수를 그려 넣었다."], ["He chose a trusty squire, and, commanding him not to address him as Rogero, rode on his quest.", "그는 믿을 만한 종자 하나를 골라, 자신을 로제로라 부르지 말라고 명한 뒤 길을 떠났다."], ["Having crossed the Rhine and the Austrian countries into Hungary, he followed the course of the Danube till he reached Belgrade.", "라인 강과 오스트리아의 여러 지방을 가로질러 헝가리로 들어선 그는 다뉴브 강의 물길을 따라가 마침내 베오그라드에 이르렀다."], ["There he saw the imperial ensigns spread, and white pavilions, thronged with troops, before the town.", "그곳에서 그는 도시 앞에 황제의 깃발이 펼쳐지고, 군대로 북적이는 흰 막사들이 늘어선 것을 보았다."], ["For the Emperor Constantine was laying siege to the city to recover it from the Bulgarians, who had taken it from him not long before.", "황제 콘스탄티누스가 얼마 전 자신에게서 그 도시를 빼앗은 불가리아인들에게서 그것을 되찾으려고 포위 공격을 벌이고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["A river flowed between the camp of the Emperor and the Bulgarians, and at the moment when Rogero approached, a skirmish had begun between the parties from either camp, who had approached the stream for the purpose of watering.", "강 하나가 황제의 진영과 불가리아인들 사이로 흘렀는데, 로제로가 다가오던 순간 양쪽 진영에서 물을 길러 강가로 나온 자들 사이에 작은 충돌이 벌어진 참이었다."], ["The Greeks in that affray were four to one, and drove back the Bulgarians in precipitate rout.", "그 싸움에서 그리스인들은 사 대 일로 우세하여 불가리아인들을 허둥지둥 패주시켰다."], ["Rogero, seeing this, and animated only by his hatred of the Grecian prince, dashed into the middle of the flying mass, calling aloud on the fugitives to turn.", "이를 본 로제로는 오직 그리스 왕자에 대한 증오에 불타, 도망치는 무리 한가운데로 뛰어들어 달아나는 자들에게 큰 소리로 돌아서라고 외쳤다."], ["He encountered first a leader of the Grecian host in splendid armor, a nephew of the Emperor, as dear to him as a son.", "그는 먼저 화려한 갑옷을 입은 그리스 군대의 한 지휘관과 마주쳤는데, 그는 황제의 조카로서 친아들처럼 사랑받던 자였다."], ["Rogero’s lance pierced shield and armor, and stretched the warrior breathless on the plain.", "로제로의 창은 방패와 갑옷을 꿰뚫어 그 전사를 숨이 끊긴 채 들판에 뉘었다."], ["Another and another fell before him, and astonishment and terror arrested the advance of the Greeks, while the Bulgarians, catching courage from the cavalier, rally, change front, and chase the Grecian troops, who fly in their turn.", "또 한 사람, 또 한 사람이 그의 앞에 쓰러지자, 경악과 공포가 그리스인들의 진격을 막았고, 그동안 불가리아인들은 그 기사에게서 용기를 얻어 전열을 가다듬고 방향을 돌려 그리스 군대를 쫓았다."], ["Leo, the prince, was at a distance when this sudden skirmish rose, but not so far but that he could see distinctly, from an elevated position which he held, how the changed battle was all the work of one man, and could not choose but admire the bravery and prowess with which it was done.", "이번에는 그리스인들이 달아날 차례였다. 이 갑작스러운 충돌이 일어났을 때 왕자 레오는 멀리 떨어져 있었으나, 자신이 차지한 높은 자리에서 뒤바뀐 전세가 모두 한 사람의 솜씨임을 또렷이 볼 수 있을 만큼은 가까웠고, 그것을 해낸 용맹과 무용에 감탄하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["He knew by the blazonry displayed that the champion was not of the Bulgarian army, though he furnished aid to them.", "그는 내걸린 문장을 보고 그 투사가 불가리아 군대 소속이 아니면서도 그들을 도왔음을 알았다."], ["Although he suffered by his valor, the prince could not wish him ill, for his admiration surpassed his resentment.", "그의 용맹 때문에 손해를 입었음에도 왕자는 그에게 악의를 품을 수 없었으니, 그의 감탄이 원한을 넘어섰기 때문이다."], ["By this time the Greeks had regained the river, and crossing it by fording or swimming, some made their escape, leaving many more prisoners in the hands of the Bulgarians.", "이때쯤 그리스인들은 강에 다다라, 물을 건너거나 헤엄쳐 일부는 도망쳤으나 더 많은 자들이 불가리아인들의 손에 포로로 남았다."], ["Rogero, learning from some of the captives that Leo was at a point some distance down the river, rode thither with a view to meet him, but arrived not before the Greek prince had retired beyond the stream, and broken up the bridge.", "로제로는 몇몇 포로에게서 레오가 강 하류 어느 지점에 있다는 것을 듣고, 그를 만날 생각으로 그쪽으로 말을 달렸으나, 그가 도착하기 전에 그리스 왕자는 이미 강 너머로 물러나 다리를 끊어 버린 뒤였다."], ["Day was spent, and Rogero, wearied, looked round for a shelter for the night.", "날이 저물어, 지친 로제로는 밤을 보낼 거처를 찾아 둘러보았다."], ["He found it in a cottage, where he soon yielded himself to repose.", "그는 어느 오두막에서 거처를 찾았고, 곧 휴식에 몸을 맡겼다."], ["It so happened, a knight who had narrowly escaped Rogero’s sword in the late battle also found shelter in the same cottage, and, recognizing the armor of the unknown knight, easily found means of securing him as he slept, and next morning carried him in chains and delivered him to the Emperor.", "그런데 방금 전 전투에서 로제로의 검을 가까스로 피했던 기사 하나가 마침 같은 오두막에서 거처를 찾았다가, 이 알 수 없는 기사의 갑옷을 알아보고는 그가 잠든 사이 쉽사리 방도를 마련하여 그를 결박했다. 그리고 이튿날 아침 사슬에 묶은 그를 끌고 가 황제에게 넘겼다."], ["By him he was in turn delivered to his sister Theodora, mother of the young knight, the first victim of Rogero’s spear.", "황제는 그를 다시 자기 누이 테오도라에게 넘겼는데, 그녀는 로제로의 창에 첫 희생자가 된 젊은 기사의 어머니였다."], ["By her he was cast into a dungeon, till her ingenuity could devise a death sufficiently painful to satiate her revenge.", "그녀는 자신의 복수를 만족시킬 만큼 충분히 고통스러운 죽음을 궁리해 낼 때까지 그를 지하 감옥에 던져 두었다."]], [["Bradamante, meanwhile, to escape her father’s and mother’s importunity, had begged a boon of Charlemagne, which the monarch pledged his royal word to grant; it was that she should not be compelled to marry any one unless he should first vanquish her in single combat. The Emperor therefore proclaimed a tournament in these words: “He that would wed Duke Aymon’s daughter must contend with the sword against that dame, from the sun’s rise to his setting; and if, in that time, he is not overcome the lady shall be his.”", "한편 브라다만테는 아버지와 어머니의 성화를 피하려고 샤를마뉴에게 한 가지 청을 올렸는데, 군주는 그것을 들어주겠노라고 왕의 말로 약속했다. 그것은 누구든 먼저 일대일 결투에서 자기를 이기지 않는 한 어느 누구와도 결혼하도록 강요받지 않겠다는 것이었다. 그리하여 황제는 이런 말로 마상 시합을 선포했다. \"에이몬 공작의 딸과 혼인하려는 자는 해가 뜰 때부터 질 때까지 검으로 그 부인과 겨루어야 한다. 그리고 그 시간 동안 패하지 않으면 그 부인은 그의 것이 되리라.\""]], [["Duke Aymon and the Lady Beatrice, though much incensed at the course things had taken, brought their daughter to court, to await the day appointed for the tournament.", "에이몬 공작과 베아트리체 부인은 일이 돌아가는 형국에 몹시 분개하면서도, 마상 시합으로 정해진 날을 기다리기 위해 딸을 궁정으로 데려왔다."], ["Bradamante, not finding there him whom her heart required, distressed herself with doubts what could be the cause of his absence.", "브라다만테는 그곳에서 자기 마음이 바라는 이를 찾지 못하자, 그가 부재한 까닭이 무엇일지 의혹으로 괴로워했다."], ["Of all fancies, the most painful one was that he had gone away to learn to forget her, knowing her father’s and her mother’s opposition to their union, and despairing to contend against them.", "온갖 상상 가운데 가장 고통스러운 것은, 그가 자기 부모가 둘의 결합을 반대하는 것을 알고 그들에 맞서 싸우기를 단념하여, 자기를 잊는 법을 배우러 떠나 버렸다는 것이었다."], ["But oh, how much worse would be the maiden’s woe, if it were known to her what her betrothed was then enduring!", "그러나 오, 그녀의 약혼자가 그때 무엇을 견디고 있는지 그녀가 알았더라면 처녀의 슬픔이 얼마나 더 깊었으랴!"]], [["He was plunged in a dungeon where no ray of daylight ever penetrated, loaded with chains, and scantily supplied with the coarsest food.", "그는 한 줄기 햇빛도 들지 않는 지하 감옥에 던져져, 사슬에 묶이고 가장 거친 음식조차 변변히 받지 못했다."], ["No wonder despair took possession of his heart, and he longed for death as a relief, when one night (or one day, for both were equally dark to him) he was roused with the glare of a torch and saw two men enter his cell.", "절망이 그의 마음을 사로잡고 그가 안식으로서 죽음을 갈망하게 된 것도 무리가 아니었다. 그러던 어느 밤(혹은 어느 낮이라 해도, 그에게는 둘 다 똑같이 어두웠으므로) 횃불의 불빛에 그는 깨어났고, 두 사람이 자신의 감방으로 들어오는 것을 보았다."], ["It was the Prince Leo, with an attendant, who had come as soon as he had learned the wretched fate of the brave knight whose valor he had seen and admired on the field of battle.", "그것은 시종 하나를 거느린 레오 왕자였는데, 그는 전쟁터에서 자신이 보고 감탄했던 용맹한 기사의 비참한 운명을 알게 되자마자 찾아온 것이었다. \"기사여,\""], ["“Cavalier,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “I am one whom thy valor hath so bound to thee, that I willingly peril my own safety to lend thee aid.”", "\"나는 그대의 용맹에 어찌나 마음이 매였던지, 그대를 돕기 위해 기꺼이 내 자신의 안위를 무릅쓰는 사람이오.\""], ["“Infinite thanks I owe you,”", "\"그대에게 무한한 감사를 드리오.\""], ["replied Rogero, “and the life you give me I promise faithfully to render back upon your call, and promptly to stake it at all times for your service.”", "로제로가 답했다. \"그대가 주신 이 목숨, 그대가 부르면 충실히 되돌려 드리고, 언제든 그대를 위해 기꺼이 내걸겠노라 약속드리오.\""], ["The prince then told Rogero his name and rank, at hearing which a tide of contending emotions almost overwhelmed Rogero.", "그러자 왕자는 로제로에게 자신의 이름과 지위를 밝혔는데, 그 말을 듣자 서로 다투는 감정의 물결이 로제로를 거의 압도했다."], ["He was set at liberty, and had his horse and arms restored to him.", "그는 풀려났고, 말과 무기를 돌려받았다."]], [["Meanwhile, tidings arrived of King Charles’ decree that whoever aspired to the hand of Bradamante must first encounter her with sword and lance.", "한편 누구든 브라다만테의 손을 얻으려는 자는 먼저 검과 창으로 그녀와 겨루어야 한다는 샤를 왕의 포고 소식이 전해졌다."], ["This news made the Grecian prince turn pale, for he knew he was no match for her in fight.", "이 소식에 그리스 왕자는 얼굴이 창백해졌으니, 자신이 싸움에서 그녀의 적수가 못 됨을 알았기 때문이다."], ["Communing with himself, he sees how he may make his wit supply the place of valor, and employ the French knight, whose name was still unknown to him, to fight the battle for him.", "혼자 곰곰이 생각하던 그는, 어떻게 하면 용맹 대신 꾀를 써서, 아직 그 이름조차 모르는 프랑스 기사를 시켜 자기 대신 결투를 치르게 할 수 있을지 깨달았다."], ["Rogero heard the proposal with extreme distress; yet it seemed worse than death to deny the first request of one to whom he owed his life.", "로제로는 그 제안을 극심한 고통 속에 들었다. 그러나 자기 목숨을 빚진 이의 첫 청을 거절하는 것은 죽음보다도 더 끔찍해 보였다."], ["Hastily he gave his assent “to do in all things that which Leo should command.”", "그는 황급히 \"레오가 명하는 것이라면 무엇이든 하겠노라\"고 승낙했다."], ["Afterward, bitter repentance came over him; yet, rather than confess his change of mind, death itself would be welcome.", "그 뒤 쓰라린 후회가 그를 덮쳤다. 그러나 마음을 바꾸었음을 고백하느니 차라리 죽음이 반가울 지경이었다."], ["Death seems his only remedy; but how to die?", "죽음만이 그의 유일한 해결책으로 보였다."], ["Sometimes he thinks to make none but a feigned resistance, and allow her sword a ready access, for never can death come more happily than if her hand guide the weapon.", "그러나 어떻게 죽는단 말인가? 때때로 그는 거짓으로만 저항하여 그녀의 검이 쉽게 닿게 할까 생각했으니, 그녀의 손이 무기를 이끈다면 그보다 더 행복한 죽음은 없을 터였기 때문이다."], ["Yet this will not avail, for, unless he wins the maid for the Greek prince, his debt remains unpaid.", "그러나 그래서는 안 될 일이었다. 그리스 왕자를 위해 그 처녀를 얻어 주지 않는 한 그의 빚은 갚아지지 않을 테니 말이다."], ["He had promised to maintain a real, not a feigned encounter.", "그는 거짓이 아닌 진짜 대결을 벌이겠노라 약속한 터였다."], ["He will then keep his word, and banish every thought from his bosom except that which moved him to maintain his truth.", "그렇다면 그는 약속을 지킬 것이고, 자신의 신의를 지키도록 그를 움직인 그 생각 외에는 모든 생각을 가슴에서 몰아내리라."]], [["The young prince, richly attended, set out, and with him Rogero.", "젊은 왕자는 화려한 수행원을 거느리고 길을 떠났고, 로제로도 함께였다."], ["They arrived at Paris, but Leo preferred not to enter the city, and pitched his tents without the walls, making known his arrival to Charlemagne by an embassy.", "그들은 파리에 도착했으나, 레오는 도시 안으로 들어가지 않고 성벽 밖에 막사를 치고는 사절을 보내 자신의 도착을 샤를마뉴에게 알렸다."], ["The monarch was pleased, and testified his courtesy by visits and gifts.", "군주는 기뻐하며 방문과 선물로 자신의 예의를 표했다."], ["The prince set forth the purpose of his coming, and prayed the Emperor to dispatch his suit—“to send forth the damsel who refused ever to take in wedlock any lord inferior to herself in fight; for she should be his bride, or he would perish beneath her sword.”", "왕자는 자신이 온 목적을 밝히고, 황제에게 자신의 청혼을 처리해 줄 것을 청했으니, \"싸움에서 자기보다 못한 어떤 군주와도 혼인하지 않겠다는 그 처녀를 내보내 달라. 그녀는 그의 신부가 되거나, 아니면 그가 그녀의 검 아래 스러질 것이다\"라고 했다."]], [["Rogero passed the night before the day assigned for the battle like that which the felon spends, condemned to pay the forfeit of his life on the ensuing day.", "로제로는 결투로 정해진 날의 전날 밤을, 마치 이튿날 제 목숨으로 죗값을 치르도록 선고받은 죄인이 보내는 그런 밤처럼 보냈다."], ["He chose to fight with sword only, and on foot, for he would not let her see Frontino, knowing that she would recognize the steed.", "그는 오직 검으로만, 그것도 걸어서 싸우기로 했으니, 그녀가 프론티노를 보면 그 말을 알아보리라는 것을 알았기 때문이다."], ["Nor would he use Balisarda, for against that enchanted blade all armor would be of no avail, and the sword that he did take he hammered well upon the edge to abate its sharpness.", "또한 발리사르다도 쓰지 않으려 했으니, 그 마법의 칼날 앞에서는 어떤 갑옷도 소용없을 터였기 때문이다. 그리고 자기가 쥘 검은 날카로움을 무디게 하려고 칼날을 잘 두드려 두었다."], ["He wore the surcoat of Prince Leo, and his shield, emblazoned with a golden, double-headed eagle.", "그는 레오 왕자의 겉옷을 입고, 황금빛 쌍두 독수리가 그려진 그의 방패를 들었다."], ["The prince took care to let himself be seen by none.", "왕자는 자신이 누구의 눈에도 띄지 않도록 조심했다."]], [["Bradamante, meanwhile, prepared herself for the combat far differently.", "한편 브라다만테는 그 결투를 위해 사뭇 다르게 채비했다."], ["Instead of blunting the edge of her falchion she whets the steel, and would fain infuse into it her own acerbity.", "그녀는 언월도의 날을 무디게 하기는커녕 강철을 갈았고, 거기에 자신의 매서움을 불어넣고자 했다."], ["As the moment approached she seemed to have fire within her veins, and waited impatiently for the trumpet’s sound.", "결투의 순간이 다가오자 그녀는 핏줄 속에 불이 타오르는 듯하여, 나팔 소리를 애타게 기다렸다."], ["At the signal she drew her sword, and fell with fury upon her Rogero.", "신호가 울리자 그녀는 검을 뽑아 분노에 차 자신의 로제로에게 달려들었다."], ["But as a well-built wall or aged rock stands unmoved the fury of the storm, so Rogero, clad in those arms which Trojan Hector once wore, withstood the strokes which stormed about his head and breast and flank.", "그러나 잘 지어진 성벽이나 오랜 세월을 견딘 바위가 폭풍의 노여움에도 끄떡없이 서 있듯이, 트로이아의 헥토르가 한때 입었던 그 갑옷을 두른 로제로는 자신의 머리와 가슴과 옆구리로 휘몰아치는 일격들을 막아 냈다. 그의 방패와 투구와 흉갑에서 불꽃이 튀었다."], ["Sparks flew from his shield, his helm, his cuirass; from direct and back strokes, aimed now high, now low, falling thick and fast, like hailstones on a cottage roof; but Rogero, with skilful ward, turns them aside, or receives them where his armor is a sure protection, careful only to protect himself, and with no thought of striking in return.", "곧은 일격과 되받아치는 일격이, 높이 또 낮게 겨냥되어 오두막 지붕에 쏟아지는 우박처럼 빽빽하고 빠르게 떨어졌다. 그러나 로제로는 능숙한 방어로 그것들을 비껴 내거나, 갑옷이 확실히 막아 주는 곳으로 받아 냈으며, 오직 자신을 지키는 데만 마음을 쓸 뿐 되받아 칠 생각은 조금도 하지 않았다."], ["Thus the hours passed away, and, as the sun approached the west, the damsel began to despair.", "이렇게 시간이 흘러가고, 해가 서쪽으로 기울자 처녀는 절망하기 시작했다."], ["But so much the more her anger increases, and she redoubles her efforts, like the craftsman who sees his work unfinished while the day is wellnigh spent.", "그러나 그럴수록 그녀의 분노는 더욱 커져, 마치 날이 거의 다 저물도록 일을 끝내지 못한 장인처럼 그녀는 노력을 배가했다."], ["O miserable damsel!", "오, 가엾은 처녀여!"], ["didst thou know whom thou wouldst kill,—if, in that cavalier matched against thee thou didst but know Rogero, on whom thy very life-threads hang, rather than kill him thou wouldst kill thyself, for he is dearer to thee than life.", "그대가 죽이려는 이가 누구인지 안다면, 그대를 맞아 겨루는 그 기사가 그대의 생명줄이 통째로 매달린 바로 그 로제로임을 안다면, 그를 죽이느니 차라리 그대 자신을 죽일 터였으니, 그는 그대에게 목숨보다도 더 소중한 이였기 때문이다."]], [["King Charles and the peers, who thought the cavalier to be the Grecian prince, viewing such force and skill exhibited, and how without assaulting her the knight defended himself, were filled with admiration, and declared the champions well matched, and worthy of each other.", "그 기사를 그리스 왕자로 여긴 샤를 왕과 중신들은 그토록 대단한 힘과 솜씨가 펼쳐지는 것을, 그리고 그 기사가 그녀를 공격하지 않으면서도 자신을 방어하는 것을 보고 감탄으로 가득 차, 두 투사가 서로 잘 어울리는 적수이며 서로에게 합당하다고 선언했다."]], [["When the sun was set Charlemagne gave the signal for terminating the contest, and Bradamante was awarded to Prince Leo as a bride.", "해가 지자 샤를마뉴는 결투를 끝내는 신호를 보냈고, 브라다만테는 레오 왕자에게 신부로 주어졌다."], ["Rogero, in deep distress, returned to his tent.", "로제로는 깊은 시름에 잠겨 자신의 막사로 돌아왔다."], ["There Leo unlaced his helmet, and kissed him on both cheeks.", "그곳에서 레오는 그의 투구 끈을 풀고 그의 양 볼에 입을 맞추었다. \"이제부터,\""], ["“Henceforth,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “do with me as you please, for you cannot exhaust my gratitude.”", "\"나를 그대 뜻대로 부리시오. 그대는 내 감사를 다 써 버릴 수 없을 테니.\""], ["Rogero replied little, laid aside the ensigns he had worn, and resumed the unicorn, then hasted to withdraw himself from all eyes.", "로제로는 별 대꾸 없이, 입었던 표장을 벗어 놓고 일각수 문장을 다시 단 다음, 모든 이의 눈을 피해 서둘러 물러났다."], ["When it was midnight he rose, saddled Frontino, and sallied from his tent, taking that direction which pleased his steed.", "한밤중이 되자 그는 일어나 프론티노에 안장을 얹고 막사를 빠져나가, 말이 가고 싶어 하는 방향으로 나아갔다."], ["All night he rode absorbed in bitter woe, and called on Death as alone capable of relieving his sufferings.", "밤새도록 그는 쓰라린 비탄에 잠겨 말을 달리며, 자신의 고통을 덜어 줄 수 있는 유일한 것으로서 죽음을 불렀다."], ["At last he entered a forest, and penetrated into its deepest recesses.", "마침내 그는 어느 숲에 들어서서 가장 깊은 후미진 곳까지 파고들었다."], ["There he unharnessed Frontino, and suffered him to wander where he would.", "그곳에서 그는 프론티노의 마구를 벗겨 가고 싶은 대로 떠돌게 두었다."], ["Then he threw himself down on the ground, and poured forth such bitter wailings that the birds and beasts, for none else heard him, were moved to pity with his cries.", "그런 다음 그는 땅바닥에 몸을 던지고는, 그의 외침을 들은 것이라곤 새와 짐승밖에 없었으나, 그것들마저 그의 부르짖음에 측은한 마음이 들 만큼 쓰라린 통곡을 쏟아 냈다."]], [["Not less was the distress of the lady Bradamante, who, rather than wed any one but Rogero, resolved to break her word, and defy kindred, court, and Charlemagne himself; and, if nothing else would do, to die.", "브라다만테 부인의 시름도 그에 못지않았으니, 그녀는 로제로 외에는 그 누구와도 혼인하느니 차라리 자신의 말을 어기고 친족과 궁정과 샤를마뉴 자신에게까지 맞서기로, 그리고 다른 방도가 없다면 죽기로 결심했다."], ["But relief came from an unexpected quarter.", "그러나 구원은 뜻밖의 곳에서 찾아왔다."], ["Marphisa, sister of Rogero, was a heroine of warlike prowess equal to Bradamante.", "로제로의 누이 마르피사는 브라다만테에 못지않은 무용을 갖춘 여걸이었다."], ["She had been the confidante of their loves, and felt hardly less distress than themselves at seeing the perils which threatened their union.", "그녀는 두 사람의 사랑을 속속들이 아는 절친한 벗이었고, 그들의 결합을 위협하는 위험을 보며 그들 자신 못지않은 시름을 느꼈다."], ["“They are already united by mutual vows,”", "\"그들은 이미 서로의 맹세로 맺어졌어요.\""], ["she said, “and in the sight of Heaven what more is necessary?”", "그녀가 말했다. \"그리고 하늘이 보기에 그 외에 또 무엇이 필요하단 말입니까?\""], ["Full of this thought she presented herself before Charlemagne, and declared that she herself was witness that the maiden had spoken to Rogero those words which they who marry swear; and that the compact was so sealed between the pair that they were no longer free, nor could forsake the one the other to take another spouse.", "이 생각으로 가득 차 그녀는 샤를마뉴 앞에 나아가, 그 처녀가 로제로에게 혼인하는 이들이 맹세하는 그 말을 건네는 것을 자신이 직접 목격했으며, 그 약조가 두 사람 사이에 어찌나 단단히 봉해졌던지 그들은 더 이상 자유롭지 못하여 서로를 저버리고 다른 배필을 맞을 수 없노라고 선언했다."], ["This her assertion she offered to prove, in single combat, against Prince Leo, or any one else.", "이 자신의 주장을 그녀는 레오 왕자든 다른 누구든 상대로 일대일 결투로써 증명하겠다고 했다."]], [["Charlemagne, sadly perplexed at this, commanded Bradamante to be called, and told her what the bold Marphisa had declared.", "이에 몹시 곤혹스러워진 샤를마뉴는 브라다만테를 불러오게 하여, 대담한 마르피사가 선언한 바를 그녀에게 일렀다."], ["Bradamante neither denied nor confirmed the statement, but hung her head, and kept silence.", "브라다만테는 그 진술을 부인하지도 시인하지도 않은 채, 고개를 떨구고 침묵을 지켰다."], ["Duke Aymon was enraged, and would fain have set aside the pretended contract on the ground that, if made at all, it must have been made before Rogero was baptized, and therefore void.", "에이몬 공작은 격분하여, 설령 그런 약조가 이루어졌다 한들 그것은 로제로가 세례를 받기 전에 이루어진 것이 틀림없으므로 무효라는 이유로 그 가짜 약조를 무시하려 했다."], ["But not so thought Rinaldo, nor the good Orlando, and Charlemagne knew not which way to decide, when Marphisa spoke thus:", "그러나 리날도도, 선량한 오를란도도 그렇게 생각하지 않았다. 샤를마뉴가 어느 쪽으로 결정해야 할지 몰라 하던 차에 마르피사가 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“Since no one else can marry the maiden while my brother lives, let the prince meet Rogero in mortal combat, and let him who survives take her for his bride.”", "\"제 오라비가 살아 있는 한 그 누구도 처녀와 혼인할 수 없으니, 왕자가 로제로와 죽음을 건 결투로 맞붙어, 살아남는 자가 그녀를 신부로 맞게 하소서.\""]], [["This saying pleased the Emperor, and was accepted by the prince, for he thought that, by the aid of his unknown champion, he should surely triumph in the fight.", "이 말은 황제의 마음에 들었고, 왕자도 그것을 받아들였으니, 자신의 알려지지 않은 투사의 도움으로 그 싸움에서 반드시 이기리라 여겼기 때문이다."], ["Proclamation was therefore made for Rogero to appear and defend his suit; and Leo, on his part, caused search to be made on all sides for the knight of the Unicorn.", "그리하여 로제로에게 나타나 자신의 권리를 변호하라는 포고가 내려졌고, 레오 또한 그 나름으로 사방을 뒤져 일각수의 기사를 찾게 했다."]], [["Meanwhile Rogero, overwhelmed with despair, lay stretched on the ground in the forest night and day without food, courting death.", "한편 절망에 압도된 로제로는 음식도 입에 대지 않고 밤낮으로 숲속 땅바닥에 누워 죽음을 청하고 있었다."], ["Here he was discovered by one of Leo’s people, who, finding him resist all attempts to remove him, hastened to his master, who was not far off, and brought him to the spot.", "이곳에서 그는 레오의 부하 가운데 한 사람에게 발견되었는데, 그가 자신을 옮기려는 온갖 시도에 저항하는 것을 본 그 부하는, 멀지 않은 곳에 있던 자기 주인에게 서둘러 달려가 그를 그 자리로 데려왔다."], ["As he approached he heard words which convinced him that love was the cause of the knight’s despair; but no clew was given to guide him to the object of that love.", "다가가면서 레오는 그 기사의 절망이 사랑 때문임을 확신케 하는 말들을 들었으나, 그 사랑의 대상으로 그를 이끌어 줄 실마리는 아무것도 없었다. 왕자는 몸을 굽혀 울고 있는 전사를 끌어안고는, 더없이 다정한 어조로 말했다."], ["Stooping down, the prince embraced the weeping warrior, and, in the tenderest accents, said: “Spare not, I entreat you, to disclose the cause of your distress, for few such desperate evils betide mankind as are wholly past cure.", "\"부디 그대의 시름의 까닭을 숨기지 마시오. 인간에게 닥치는 그토록 절망적인 재앙 가운데 도무지 고칠 수 없는 것은 드무오."], ["It grieves me much that you would hide your grief from me, for I am bound to you by ties that nothing can undo.", "그대가 내게서 슬픔을 감추려 하니 몹시 마음이 아프오. 나는 그 무엇으로도 풀 수 없는 인연으로 그대에게 매여 있는 사람이오."], ["Tell me, then, your grief, and leave me to try if wealth, art, cunning, force, or persuasion cannot relieve you.", "그러니 그대의 슬픔을 내게 말하고, 재물이든 솜씨든 꾀든 힘이든 설득이든 그것으로 그대를 구할 수 있을지 내게 맡겨 보시오."], ["If not, it will be time enough after all has been tried in vain to die.”", "그래도 안 된다면, 모든 것을 다 해 보고도 헛수고로 끝난 뒤에 죽어도 늦지 않을 것이오.\""]], [["He spoke in such moving accents that Rogero could not choose but yield.", "그가 어찌나 마음을 움직이는 어조로 말했던지 로제로는 응하지 않을 수 없었다. 그가 입을 열기까지는 한참이 걸렸다."], ["It was some time before he could command utterance; at last he said, “My lord, when you shall know me for what I am, I doubt not you, like myself, will be content that I should die.", "마침내 그가 말했다. \"나리, 제가 어떤 사람인지 아시게 되면, 나리께서도 저처럼 제가 죽는 것을 마땅하다 여기실 것이옵니다."], ["Know, then, I am that Rogero whom you have so much cause to hate, and who so hated you that, intent on putting you to death, he went to seek you at your father’s court.", "그러니 아소서, 저는 나리께서 그토록 미워할 까닭이 있는, 그리고 나리를 죽이려고 작정하여 나리의 부친의 궁정으로 찾아갈 만큼 나리를 미워했던 바로 그 로제로이옵니다."], ["This I did because I could not submit to see my promised bride borne off by you.", "제가 이리한 것은, 제게 약속된 신부가 나리께 빼앗기는 것을 차마 두고 볼 수 없었기 때문입니다."], ["But, as man proposes and God disposes, your great courtesy, well tried in time of sore need, so moved my fixed resolve, that I not only laid aside the hate I bore, but purposed to be your friend forever.", "그러나 사람이 꾀하면 하느님이 처분하시는 법, 절박한 위기에 잘 증명된 나리의 크나큰 도량이 제 굳은 결심을 어찌나 움직였던지, 저는 품었던 미움을 내려놓았을 뿐만 아니라 영원토록 나리의 벗이 되기로 마음먹었습니다."], ["You then asked of me to win for you the lady Bradamante, which was all one as to demand of me my heart and soul.", "그때 나리께서는 제게 브라다만테 부인을 얻어 달라 청하셨는데, 그것은 제게 제 심장과 영혼을 내놓으라 하는 것과 다름없었습니다."], ["You know whether I served you faithfully or not.", "제가 충실히 나리를 섬겼는지 아닌지는 나리께서 아실 것입니다."], ["Yours is the lady; possess her in peace; but ask me not to live to see it.", "그 부인은 나리의 것이니, 평화로이 그녀를 차지하소서. 다만 제게 그것을 살아서 보라고는 청하지 마소서."], ["Be content rather that I die; for vows have passed between myself and her which forbid that while I live she can lawfully wive with another.”", "차라리 제가 죽도록 두십시오. 저와 그녀 사이에는 제가 살아 있는 한 그녀가 다른 이와 합법적으로 혼인할 수 없도록 막는 맹세가 오갔기 때문입니다.\""]], [["So filled was gentle Leo with astonishment at these words that for a while he stood silent, with lips unmoved and steadfast gaze, like a statue.", "이 말에 점잖은 레오는 어찌나 놀랐던지, 한동안 입술도 움직이지 않고 시선을 고정한 채 조각상처럼 말없이 서 있었다."], ["And the discovery that the stranger was Rogero not only abated not the good will he bore him, but increased it, so that his distress for what Rogero suffered seemed equal to his own.", "그리고 그 낯선 이가 로제로라는 발견은 그가 그에게 품었던 호의를 누그러뜨리기는커녕 오히려 키웠으니, 로제로가 겪는 고통에 대한 그의 시름이 자기 자신의 시름과 맞먹는 듯했다. 이런 까닭에서, 그리고 자신이 마땅히 황제의 아들답게 보이고자, 다른 일에서는 뒤질지언정 예의에서만은 뒤지지 않으려고, 그는 말했다."], ["For this, and because he would appear deservedly an Emperor’s son, and, though in other things outdone, would not be surpassed in courtesy, he says: “Rogero, had I known that day when your matchless valor routed my troops that you were Rogero, your virtue would have made me your own, as then it made me while I knew not my foe, and I should have no less gladly rescued you from Theodora’s dungeon.", "\"로제로, 그대의 비할 데 없는 용맹이 내 군대를 무찌르던 그날 그대가 로제로임을 내가 알았더라면, 그대의 덕이 나로 하여금 그대를 내 사람으로 만들게 했을 것이오. 적이 누구인지도 모르던 그때 그러했듯이 말이오."], ["And if I would willingly have done so then, how much more gladly will I now restore the gift of which you would rob yourself to confer it upon me.", "그러면 나는 그 못지않게 기꺼이 그대를 테오도라의 지하 감옥에서 구해 냈을 것이오. 그때도 기꺼이 그리했을 것인데, 하물며 지금 그대가 내게 주려고 스스로에게서 빼앗으려는 그 선물을, 어찌 더더욱 기꺼이 돌려주지 않으리오."], ["The damsel is more due to you than to me, and though I know her worth, I would forego not only her, but life itself, rather than distress a knight like you.”", "그 처녀는 내게보다 그대에게 더 마땅한 사람이오. 그녀의 가치를 내가 안다 해도, 나는 그대 같은 기사를 괴롭게 하느니 차라리 그녀뿐 아니라 목숨까지도 기꺼이 포기하겠소.\""]], [["This and much more he said to the same intent; till at last Rogero replied, “I yield, and am content to live, and thus a second time owe my life to you.”", "그는 같은 뜻으로 이것 말고도 많은 말을 했다. 마침내 로제로가 답했다. \"굽히겠습니다. 살아 있는 것에 만족하겠습니다. 그리하여 두 번째로 나리께 제 목숨을 빚지겠습니다.\""]], [["But several days elapsed before Rogero was so far restored as to return to the royal residence, where an embassy had arrived from the Bulgarian princes to seek the knight of the unicorn, and tender to him the crown of that country, in place of their king, fallen in battle.", "그러나 로제로가 왕궁으로 돌아갈 만큼 회복되기까지는 여러 날이 걸렸다. 왕궁에는 불가리아 제후들이 보낸 사절이 도착해, 일각수의 기사를 찾아 그에게 전투에서 쓰러진 자기네 왕을 대신하여 그 나라의 왕관을 바치려 하고 있었다."]], [["Thus were things situated when Prince Leo, leading by the hand Rogero, clad in the battered armor in which he had sustained the conflict with Bradamante, presented himself before the king.", "레오 왕자가 브라다만테와의 결투를 치를 때 입었던 찌그러진 갑옷을 그대로 두른 로제로의 손을 잡고 왕 앞에 나섰을 때, 사정은 이러했다. \"보소서,\""], ["“Behold,”", "그가 말했다."], ["he said “the champion who maintained from dawn to setting sun the arduous contest; he comes to claim the guerdon of the fight.”", "\"동틀 녘부터 해 질 녘까지 그 고된 결투를 버텨 낸 투사가 바로 여기 있나이다. 그가 싸움의 보상을 청하러 왔나이다.\""], ["King Charlemagne, with all his peerage, stood amazed; for all believed that the Grecian prince himself had fought with Bradamante.", "샤를마뉴 왕과 그의 모든 중신은 경악하여 섰으니, 모두가 그리스 왕자 본인이 브라다만테와 싸웠다고 믿었기 때문이다."], ["Then stepped forth Marphisa, and said, “Since Rogero is not here to assert his rights, I, his sister, undertake his cause, and will maintain it against whoever shall dare dispute his claim.”", "그때 마르피사가 앞으로 나서며 말했다. \"로제로가 자기 권리를 주장하러 여기 없으니, 그의 누이인 제가 그의 송사를 맡아, 감히 그의 권리에 이의를 다는 자가 누구든 그에 맞서 그것을 지키겠나이다.\""], ["She said this with so much anger and disdain that the prince deemed it no longer wise to feign, and withdrew Rogero’s helmet from his brow, saying, “Behold him here!”", "그녀가 이 말을 어찌나 분노와 경멸을 담아 했던지, 왕자는 더 이상 꾸미는 것이 현명하지 않다 여기고 로제로의 투구를 그의 이마에서 벗기며 말했다. \"여기 그가 있소!\""], ["Who can describe the astonishment and joy of Marphisa!", "마르피사의 놀라움과 기쁨을 그 누가 다 말할 수 있으랴!"], ["She ran and threw her arms about her brother’s neck, nor would give way to let Charlemagne and Rinaldo, Orlando, Dudon, and the rest, who crowded round, embrace him, and press friendly kisses on his brow.", "그녀는 달려가 오라비의 목을 끌어안았고, 둘레로 몰려든 샤를마뉴와 리날도, 오를란도, 두돈, 그 밖의 사람들이 그를 끌어안고 그의 이마에 다정한 입맞춤을 누르도록 좀처럼 자리를 내주지 않았다."], ["The joyful tidings flew fast by many a messenger to Bradamante, who in her secret chamber lay lamenting.", "그 기쁜 소식은 여러 전령에 의해 비밀의 방에서 슬퍼 누워 있던 브라다만테에게 빠르게 날아갔다."], ["The blood that stagnated about her heart flowed at that notice so fast, that she had wellnigh died for joy.", "그녀의 심장 둘레에 멎어 있던 피가 그 소식에 어찌나 빠르게 흘렀던지, 그녀는 기쁨으로 거의 죽을 뻔했다."], ["Duke Aymon and the Lady Beatrice no longer withheld their consent, and pledged their daughter to the brave Rogero before all that gallant company.", "에이몬 공작과 베아트리체 부인도 더 이상 승낙을 거두지 않고, 그 용맹한 무리 모두의 앞에서 자기 딸을 용감한 로제로에게 약속했다."]], [["Now came the Bulgarian ambassadors, and, kneeling at the feet of Rogero, besought him to return with them to their country, where, in Adrianople, the crown and sceptre were awaiting his acceptance.", "이제 불가리아 사절들이 다가와 로제로의 발치에 무릎 꿇고는, 자기네와 함께 본국으로 돌아가 줄 것을 간청했으니, 아드리아노폴리스에서 왕관과 홀(笏)이 그의 받아들임을 기다리고 있다고 했다."], ["Prince Leo united his persuasions to theirs, and promised, in his royal father’s name, that peace should be restored on their part.", "레오 왕자도 자신의 설득을 그들의 설득에 보태며, 자기 부왕(父王)의 이름으로 그들 편의 평화가 회복되리라 약속했다. 로제로는 승낙했다."], ["Rogero gave his consent, and it was surmised that none of the virtues which shone so conspicuously in him so availed to recommend Rogero to the Lady Beatrice as the hearing her future son-in-law saluted as a sovereign prince.", "그리고 사람들은, 그토록 두드러지게 그에게서 빛나던 미덕 가운데 그 무엇도, 장차 사위가 될 이가 군주로서 칭송받는 것을 들은 것만큼 로제로를 베아트리체 부인에게 잘 천거해 주지는 못했으리라고 짐작했다. 론세스바예스 전투"]], [["# THE BATTLE OF RONCESVALLES", "# THE BATTLE OF RONCESVALLES"]], [["————\nAfter the expulsion of the Saracens from France Charlemagne led his army into Spain, to punish Marsilius, the king of that country, for having sided with the African Saracens in the late war.", "사라센인들을 프랑스에서 몰아낸 뒤, 샤를마뉴는 군대를 이끌고 에스파냐로 들어가, 지난 전쟁에서 아프리카 사라센인들 편을 든 죄로 그 나라의 왕 마르실리우스를 응징하고자 했다."], ["Charlemagne succeeded in all his attempts, and compelled Marsilius to submit, and pay tribute to France.", "샤를마뉴는 모든 시도에서 성공하여, 마르실리우스에게 항복하고 프랑스에 조공을 바치도록 강요했다."], ["Our readers will remember Gano, otherwise called Gan, or Ganelon, whom we mentioned in one of our early chapters as an old courtier of Charlemagne, and a deadly enemy of Orlando, Rinaldo, and all their friends.", "독자들은 가노, 달리 간 혹은 가넬론이라 불리는 자를 기억할 것이다. 우리는 앞선 어느 장에서 그를 샤를마뉴의 늙은 신하이자 오를란도와 리날도, 그리고 그들의 모든 벗에게 철천지원수인 자로 언급한 바 있다."], ["He had great influence over Charles, from equality of age and long intimacy; and he was not without good qualities: he was brave and sagacious, but envious, false, and treacherous.", "그는 나이가 비슷하고 오랫동안 가까이 지낸 덕에 샤를에게 큰 영향력을 지녔다. 그리고 그에게 좋은 자질이 없는 것도 아니어서, 용감하고 명민했으나, 시기심이 많고 거짓되며 음흉했다."], ["Gan prevailed on Charles to send him as ambassador to Marsilius, to arrange the tribute.", "간은 샤를을 설득하여 자신을 사절로 마르실리우스에게 보내어 조공을 처리하게 했다."], ["He embraced Orlando over and over again at taking leave, using such pains to seem loving and sincere, that his hypocrisy was manifest to every one but the old monarch.", "그는 작별할 때 오를란도를 거듭거듭 끌어안으며, 다정하고 진실해 보이려고 어찌나 애를 썼던지, 그 위선이 늙은 군주를 빼고는 모두의 눈에 뻔히 드러났다."], ["He fastened with equal tenderness on Oliver, who smiled contemptuously in his face, and thought to himself, “You may make as many fair speeches as you choose, but you lie.”", "그는 똑같은 다정함으로 올리버에게도 매달렸는데, 올리버는 그의 얼굴에 대고 경멸하듯 웃으며 속으로 생각했다. \"그대가 아무리 듣기 좋은 말을 늘어놓아도, 그대는 거짓을 말하고 있다.\""], ["All the other paladins who were present thought the same, and they said as much to the Emperor, adding that Gan should on no account be sent ambassador to the Spaniards.", "그 자리에 있던 다른 모든 기사들도 같은 생각이었고, 그들은 황제에게 그만큼이나 말하며, 간을 결코 에스파냐인들에게 사절로 보내서는 안 된다고 덧붙였다."], ["But Charles was infatuated.", "그러나 샤를은 마음이 홀려 있었다."]], [["Gan was received with great honor by Marsilius.", "간은 마르실리우스에게 큰 영예로 맞아들여졌다."], ["The king, attended by his lords, came fifteen miles out of Saragossa to meet him, and then conducted him into the city with acclamations.", "왕은 자신의 영주들을 거느리고 그를 맞으러 사라고사에서 십오 마일 밖까지 나왔고, 환호 속에 그를 도시로 인도했다."], ["There was nothing for several days but balls, games, and exhibitions of chivalry, the ladies throwing flowers on the heads of the French knights, and the people shouting, “France!", "며칠 동안 무도회와 경기와 기사도 시범 외에는 아무것도 없었으니, 귀부인들은 프랑스 기사들의 머리 위로 꽃을 던졌고, 사람들은 \"프랑스! 몽주아와 성 디오니시오!\""], ["Mountjoy and St. Denis!”", "하고 외쳤다."]], [["After the ceremonies of the first reception the king and the ambassador began to understand one another.", "첫 환영 의례가 끝난 뒤, 왕과 사절은 서로를 이해하기 시작했다."], ["One day they sat together in a garden on the border of a fountain.", "어느 날 그들은 샘 가장자리의 정원에 함께 앉아 있었다."], ["The water was so clear and smooth it reflected every object around, and the spot was encircled with fruit-trees which quivered with the fresh air.", "물이 어찌나 맑고 잔잔했던지 둘레의 온갖 것을 비추었고, 그 자리는 신선한 바람에 떨리는 과실수들로 에워싸여 있었다."], ["As they sat and talked, as if without restraint, Gan, without looking the king in the face, was enabled to see the expression of his countenance in the water, and governed his speech accordingly.", "거리낌 없는 듯 둘이 앉아 이야기를 나눌 때, 간은 왕의 얼굴을 정면으로 보지 않고도 물에 비친 그 표정을 볼 수 있어, 그에 맞추어 말을 조절했다."], ["Marsilius was equally adroit, and watched the face of Gan while he addressed him.", "마르실리우스도 그에 못지않게 능란하여, 간에게 말을 건네면서 그의 얼굴을 살폈다."], ["Marsilius began by lamenting, not as to the ambassador, but as to the friend, the injuries which Charles had done him by invading his dominions, charging him with wishing to take his kingdom from him and give it to Orlando; till at length he plainly uttered his belief that if that ambitious paladin were but dead good men would get their rights.", "마르실리우스는 사절에게가 아니라 벗에게 하듯, 샤를이 자기 영토를 침범하여 입힌 손해를 한탄하는 것으로 말문을 열며, 샤를이 자기에게서 왕국을 빼앗아 오를란도에게 주려 한다고 비난했다. 그러다 마침내 그는, 그 야심 찬 기사가 죽기만 한다면 선량한 이들이 제 권리를 찾으리라는 자신의 믿음을 노골적으로 내뱉었다."]], [["Gan heaved a sigh, as if he was unwillingly compelled to allow the force of what the king said; but unable to contain himself long he lifted up his face, radiant with triumphant wickedness, and exclaimed: “Every word you utter is truth; die he must, and die also must Oliver, who struck me that foul blow at court.", "간은 왕이 한 말의 설득력을 마지못해 인정할 수밖에 없다는 듯 한숨을 내쉬었다. 그러나 오래 자신을 억누르지 못하고 그는 의기양양한 사악함으로 빛나는 얼굴을 들며 외쳤다."], ["Is it treachery to punish affronts like these?", "\"그대가 하는 말은 한마디 한마디가 진실이오. 그는 죽어야 하오."], ["I have planned everything,—I have settled everything already with their besotted master.", "그리고 궁정에서 내게 그 더러운 일격을 가한 올리버도 죽어야 하오. 이런 모욕을 응징하는 것이 배신이란 말이오?"], ["Orlando will come to your borders—to Roncesvalles—for the purpose of receiving the tribute.", "나는 모든 것을 계획해 두었소. 그들의 얼빠진 주인과 이미 모든 것을 정해 두었소."], ["Charles will await him at the foot of the mountains.", "오를란도는 조공을 받으러 그대의 국경으로, 론세스바예스로 올 것이오."], ["Orlando will bring but a small band with him: you, when you meet him, will have secretly your whole army at your back.", "샤를은 산기슭에서 그를 기다릴 것이오. 오를란도는 작은 무리만 데려올 것이니, 그대는 그를 맞을 때 비밀리에 그대의 전군을 등 뒤에 두시오."], ["You surround him, and who receives tribute then?”", "그를 에워싸시오. 그러면 그때 조공을 받는 자가 누구이겠소?\""]], [["The new Judas had scarcely uttered these words when his exultation was interrupted by a change in the face of nature.", "이 새로운 유다가 이 말을 채 다 내뱉기도 전에, 그의 의기양양함이 자연의 낯빛이 바뀌는 것으로 중단되었다."], ["The sky was suddenly overcast, there was thunder and lightning, a laurel was split in two from head to foot, and the Carob-tree under which Gan was sitting, which is said to be the species of tree on which Judas Iscariot hung himself, dropped one of its pods on his head.", "하늘이 갑자기 흐려지고 천둥과 번개가 쳤으며, 월계수 한 그루가 위에서 아래까지 둘로 쪼개졌고, 간이 앉아 있던 캐럽나무는 가룟 유다가 목매단 나무가 바로 그 종류라고 전해지는 그 나무인데, 그 꼬투리 하나를 그의 머리 위로 떨어뜨렸다."]], [["Marsilius, as well as Gan, was appalled at this omen; but on assembling his soothsayers they came to the conclusion that the laurel-tree turned the omen against the Emperor, the successor of the Cæsars, though one of them renewed the consternation of Gan by saying that he did not understand the meaning of the tree of Judas, and intimating that perhaps the ambassador could explain it. Gan relieved his vexation by anger; the habit of wickedness prevailed over all other considerations; and the king prepared to march to Roncesvalles at the head of all his forces.", "간과 마찬가지로 마르실리우스도 이 징조에 질겁했다. 그러나 점쟁이들을 불러 모으니, 그들은 월계수가 카이사르들의 후계자인 황제에게 그 징조를 돌리는 것이라고 결론지었다. 다만 그들 가운데 하나가, 자기는 유다의 나무가 무슨 뜻인지 모르겠다고 하며 어쩌면 사절이 그것을 설명할 수 있을지 모르겠다고 넌지시 말하여 간의 당혹감을 다시 일으켰다. 간은 분노로 자신의 분함을 달랬다. 사악함이 몸에 밴 습성이 다른 모든 고려를 압도했다. 그리하여 왕은 전군의 선두에 서서 론세스바예스로 진군할 채비를 했다."]], [["Gan wrote to Charlemagne to say how humbly and submissively Marsilius was coming to pay the tribute into the hands of Orlando, and how handsome it would be of the Emperor to meet him half-way, and so be ready to receive him after the payment at his camp.", "간은 샤를마뉴에게 편지를 써, 마르실리우스가 얼마나 겸손하고 순종적으로 조공을 오를란도의 손에 바치러 오고 있는지, 그리고 황제가 그를 중도까지 마중 나가 조공을 바친 뒤 자기 진영에서 그를 맞을 채비를 하는 것이 얼마나 훌륭한 일일지 적었다."], ["He added a brilliant account of the tribute, and the accompanying presents.", "그는 조공과 거기 딸린 선물에 대한 화려한 설명도 덧붙였다."], ["The good Emperor wrote in turn to say how pleased he was with the ambassador’s diligence, and that matters were arranged precisely as he wished.", "선량한 황제는 답장을 써, 사절의 수고에 매우 흡족하며, 일이 자신이 바라던 대로 꼭 맞게 처리되었다고 했다."], ["His court, however, had its suspicion still, though they little thought Gan’s object in bringing Charles into the neighborhood of Roncesvalles was to deliver him into the hands of Marsilius, after Orlando should have been destroyed by him.", "그러나 그의 궁정은 여전히 의심을 품고 있었는데, 다만 간이 샤를을 론세스바예스 인근으로 끌어들이려는 속셈이 오를란도를 마르실리우스의 손에 죽게 한 다음 샤를마저 그의 손에 넘기려는 것임은 거의 짐작하지 못했다."]], [["Orlando, however, did as his lord and sovereign desired.", "그러나 오를란도는 자신의 주군이자 군주가 바라는 대로 했다."], ["He went to Roncesvalles, accompanied by a moderate train of warriors, not dreaming of the atrocity that awaited him.", "그는 자기를 기다리는 잔혹한 음모는 꿈에도 모른 채, 적당한 규모의 전사 행렬을 거느리고 론세스바예스로 갔다. 그동안 간은 샤를 앞에서 거리낌 없고 태연한 모습을 보여 자기 음모의 성공을 굳히려고 서둘러 프랑스로 돌아갔다."], ["Gan, meanwhile, had hastened back to France, in order to show himself free and easy in the presence of Charles, and secure the success of his plot; while Marsilius, to make assurance doubly sure, brought into the passes of Roncesvalles no less than three armies, which were successively to fall on the paladin in case of the worst, and so extinguish him with numbers.", "한편 마르실리우스는 확실에 확실을 더하려고, 무려 세 개의 군대를 론세스바예스의 고갯길로 끌어들였다. 그것들은 최악의 경우 차례로 기사를 덮쳐 수효로 그를 짓밟을 셈이었다."], ["He had also, by Gan’s advice, brought heaps of wine and good cheer to be set before his victims in the first instance; “for that,”", "그는 또한 간의 조언에 따라, 먼저 희생자들 앞에 차려 놓으려고 술과 좋은 음식을 무더기로 가져왔다. \"그것이,\""], ["said the traitor, “will render the onset the more effective, the feasters being unarmed.", "그 배신자는 말했다. \"잔치 벌이는 자들이 무장을 풀게 하여 공격을 한층 효과적으로 만들 것이오."], ["One thing, however, I must not forget,”", "그러나 한 가지 잊어서는 안 될 것이 있소.\" 그가 덧붙였다."], ["added he; “my son Baldwin is sure to be with Orlando; you must take care of his life for my sake.”", "\"내 아들 발드윈은 분명 오를란도와 함께 있을 것이오. 나를 위해 그 아이의 목숨을 지켜 주시오.\""]], [["“I give him this vesture off my own body,”", "\"내 몸에서 벗은 이 옷을 그에게 주겠소.\""], ["said the king; “let him wear it in the battle, and have no fear.", "왕이 말했다. \"전투에서 그것을 입게 하고 두려워 말라 하시오."], ["My soldiers shall be directed not to touch him.”", "내 병사들에게 그를 건드리지 말라 명할 것이오.\""]], [["Gan went away rejoicing to France.", "간은 기뻐하며 프랑스로 떠났다."], ["He embraced the sovereign and the court all round with the air of a man who had brought them nothing but blessings, and the old king wept for very tenderness and delight.", "그는 군주와 궁정 사람들을 빙 둘러 끌어안았는데, 마치 자기가 그들에게 축복밖에는 가져다준 것이 없는 사람인 양 굴었고, 늙은 왕은 다정함과 기쁨에 겨워 눈물을 흘렸다."]], [["“Something is going on wrong, and looks very black,”", "\"무언가 잘못 돌아가고 있다, 아주 어두워 보인다.\""], ["thought Malagigi, the good wizard; “Rinaldo is not here, and it is indispensably necessary that he should be.", "선량한 마법사 말라지지가 생각했다. \"리날도가 여기 없는데, 그가 꼭 있어야 한다."], ["I must find out where he is, and Ricciardetto too, and send for them with all speed.”", "그가 어디 있는지, 그리고 리차르데토도 어디 있는지 알아내어, 한시바삐 그들을 불러와야겠다.\""]], [["Malagigi called up by his art a wise, terrible, and cruel spirit, named Ashtaroth.", "말라지지는 자신의 술법으로 아스타로트라는 이름의, 현명하고 무섭고 잔인한 정령을 불러냈다."], ["“Tell me, and tell me truly, of Rinaldo,”", "\"리날도에 대해 말해 다오, 그것도 사실대로.\""], ["said Malagigi to the spirit.", "말라지지가 그 정령에게 말했다."], ["The demon looked hard at the paladin, and said nothing.", "악마는 기사를 매섭게 쏘아보며 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["His aspect was clouded and violent.", "그 낯빛은 흐려지고 사나웠다."]], [["The enchanter, with an aspect still cloudier, bade Ashtaroth lay down that look, and made signs as if he would resort to angrier compulsion; and the devil, alarmed, loosened his tongue, and said, “You have not told me what you desire to know of Rinaldo.”", "마법사는 한층 더 흐려진 낯빛으로 아스타로트에게 그 표정을 거두라 명하고, 더 노한 강압을 쓰겠다는 듯 손짓을 했다. 그러자 놀란 악마는 혀를 풀고 말했다. \"리날도에 대해 무엇을 알고 싶은지 말하지 않았소이다.\""]], [["“I desire to know what he has been doing, and where he is.”", "\"그가 무엇을 하고 있었는지, 그리고 어디에 있는지 알고 싶다.\""]], [["“He has been conquering and baptizing the world, east and west,”", "\"그는 동에서 서로 세상을 정복하고 세례를 베풀고 있었소이다.\""], ["said the demon, “and is now in Egypt with Ricciardetto.”", "악마가 말했다. \"그리고 지금은 리차르데토와 함께 이집트에 있소이다.\""]], [["“And what has Gan been plotting with Marsilius?”", "\"그렇다면 간은 마르실리우스와 무엇을 꾸미고 있느냐?\""], ["inquired Malagigi; “and what is to come of it?”", "말라지지가 물었다. \"그리고 거기서 무슨 일이 벌어질 것이냐?\""]], [["“I know not,”", "\"모르겠소이다.\""], ["said the devil.", "악마가 말했다."], ["“I was not attending to Gan at the time, and we fallen spirits know not the future.", "\"그때 나는 간을 지켜보고 있지 않았고, 우리 타락한 정령들은 미래를 알지 못하오."], ["All I discern is that by the signs and comets in the heavens something dreadful is about to happen—something very strange, treacherous, and bloody; and that Gan has a seat ready prepared for him in hell.”", "내가 알아챌 수 있는 것이라고는, 하늘의 징조와 혜성들로 보아 무언가 끔찍한 일이, 무언가 아주 기이하고 배신에 찬 피비린내 나는 일이 막 벌어지려 한다는 것뿐이오. 그리고 간을 위한 자리가 지옥에 마련되어 있다는 것뿐이오.\""]], [["“Within three days,”", "\"사흘 안에,\""], ["cried the enchanter, loudly, “bring Rinaldo and Ricciardetto into the pass of Roncesvalles.", "마법사가 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"리날도와 리차르데토를 론세스바예스 고갯길로 데려오너라."], ["Do it, and I hereby undertake to summon thee no more.”", "그리하면 내 다시는 너를 불러내지 않겠노라 이로써 약속하마.\""]], [["“Suppose they will not trust themselves with me?”", "\"그들이 나를 믿고 따라오려 하지 않으면 어찌하오?\""], ["said the spirit.", "정령이 말했다."]], [["“Enter Rinaldo’s horse, and bring him, whether he trust thee or not.”", "\"리날도의 말에 들어가, 그가 너를 믿든 안 믿든 그를 데려오너라.\""]], [["“It shall be done,”", "\"그리하겠소이다.\""], ["returned the demon.", "악마가 답했다."]], [["There was an earthquake, and Ashtaroth disappeared.", "지진이 일더니 아스타로트가 사라졌다."]], [["————\nMarsilius now made his first movement towards the destruction of Orlando, by sending before him his vassal, King Blanchardin, with his presents of wines and other luxuries.", "이제 마르실리우스는 오를란도를 파멸시키기 위한 첫 움직임을 시작했으니, 자신의 봉신인 블랑샤르딘 왕을 술과 그 밖의 진미를 선물로 들려 그보다 앞서 보냈다."], ["The temperate but courteous hero took them in good part, and distributed them as the traitor wished; and then Blanchardin, on pretence of going forward to salute Charlemagne, returned, and put himself at the head of the second army, which was the post assigned him by his liege-lord.", "절제할 줄 알면서도 정중한 영웅은 그것을 선의로 받아들여, 그 배신자가 바라던 대로 그것들을 나누어 주었다. 그런 다음 블랑샤르딘은 앞으로 나아가 샤를마뉴에게 인사를 올린다는 핑계로 돌아가, 자기 군주가 정해 준 자리인 제2군의 선두에 섰다."], ["King Falseron, whose son Orlando had slain in battle, headed the first army, and King Balugante the third.", "오를란도에게 아들을 전투에서 잃은 팔세론 왕이 제1군을 이끌었고, 발루간테 왕이 제3군을 이끌었다."], ["Marsilius made a speech to them, in which he let them into his design, and concluded by recommending to their good will the son of his friend Gan, whom they would know by the vest he had sent him, and who was the only soul amongst the Christian they were to spare.", "마르실리우스는 그들에게 연설을 하여 자신의 계획을 알려 주었고, 그들이 자기가 보낸 옷으로 알아볼 수 있을, 그리고 그리스도교도 가운데 살려 두어야 할 유일한 자인 자기 벗 간의 아들에게 호의를 베풀어 달라고 부탁하는 것으로 끝맺었다."]], [["This son of Gan, meanwhile, and several of the paladins, who distrusted the misbelievers, and were anxious at all events to be with Orlando, had joined the hero in the fatal valley; so that the little Christian host, considering the tremendous valor of their lord and his friends, were not to be sold for nothing.", "한편 이 간의 아들과, 불신자들을 믿지 못하여 어떻게든 오를란도와 함께 있고자 했던 몇몇 기사들은, 그 운명의 골짜기에서 영웅과 합류한 터였다. 그리하여 그 작은 그리스도교 무리는, 자기네 주군과 그 벗들의 어마어마한 용맹을 생각하건대 그냥 헐값에 팔려 갈 자들이 아니었다."], ["Rinaldo, alas! the second thunderbolt of Christendom, was destined not to be there in time to meet the issue.", "아아, 그리스도교 세계의 두 번째 벼락인 리날도는 사태를 맞을 시간에 그곳에 닿을 운명이 못 되었다."], ["The paladins in vain begged Orlando to be on his guard against treachery, and send for a more numerous body of men.", "기사들은 오를란도에게 배신을 경계하여 더 많은 병력을 불러오라고 헛되이 간청했다."], ["The great heart of the Champion of the Faith was unwilling to harbor suspicion as long as he could help it.", "신앙의 투사의 위대한 마음은, 어쩔 수 없는 한 의심을 품으려 하지 않았다."], ["He refused to summon aid which might be superfluous; neither would he do anything but what his liege-lord had directed.", "그는 쓸데없을지도 모르는 지원을 부르기를 거부했고, 또한 자신의 주군이 명한 것 외에는 아무것도 하지 않으려 했다."], ["And yet he could not wholly repress a misgiving.", "그러면서도 그는 불안을 온전히 떨쳐 내지는 못했다."], ["A shadow had fallen on his heart, great and cheerful as it was.", "위대하고 쾌활한 그의 마음에도 그림자가 드리워졌다."], ["The anticipations of his friends disturbed him, in spite of the face with which he met them.", "그가 벗들을 대하는 얼굴과는 달리, 그들의 예감이 그를 어지럽혔다."], ["Perhaps by a certain foresight he felt his death approaching; but he felt bound not to encourage the impression.", "어쩌면 어떤 예지로 자신의 죽음이 다가옴을 느꼈는지도 모른다. 그러나 그는 그런 인상을 부추겨서는 안 된다고 여겼다."], ["Besides, time pressed; the moment of the looked-for tribute was at hand, and little combinations of circumstances determine often the greatest events.", "게다가 시간이 촉박했다. 고대하던 조공의 순간이 임박했고, 사소한 정황들의 조합이 흔히 가장 큰 사건을 결정하는 법이다."]], [["King Marsilius was to arrive early next day with the tribute, and Oliver, with the morning sun, rode forth to reconnoitre, and see if he could discover the peaceful pomp of the Spanish court in the distance.", "마르실리우스 왕은 이튿날 아침 일찍 조공을 가지고 오기로 되어 있었고, 올리버는 아침 해와 함께 정찰하러 말을 달려, 멀리서 에스파냐 궁정의 평화로운 행렬을 알아볼 수 있을지 살폈다."], ["He rode up the nearest height, and from the top of it beheld the first army of Marsilius already forming in the passes.", "그는 가장 가까운 고지로 올라가, 그 꼭대기에서 이미 고갯길에 전열을 갖추고 있는 마르실리우스의 제1군을 보았다. \"오, 악마 같은 간.\""], ["“O devil Gan,”", "그가 외쳤다."], ["he exclaimed, “this then is the consummation of thy labors!”", "\"이것이 바로 네 수고의 결말이로구나!\""], ["Oliver put spurs to his horse, and galloped back down the mountain to Orlando.", "올리버는 말에 박차를 가하여 산을 내달려 오를란도에게 돌아갔다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"자,\""], ["cried the hero, “what news?”", "영웅이 외쳤다. \"무슨 소식인가?\""]], [["“Bad news,”", "\"나쁜 소식이오.\""], ["said his cousin, “such as you would not hear of yesterday.", "그의 사촌이 말했다. \"어제는 들으려 하지 않으셨던 그런 소식이오."], ["Marsilius is here in arms, and all the world is with him.”", "마르실리우스가 무장하고 여기 와 있고, 온 세상이 그와 함께요.\""]], [["The paladins pressed round Orlando, and entreated him to sound his horn, in token that he needed help.", "기사들은 오를란도 주위로 몰려들어, 도움이 필요하다는 표시로 뿔피리를 불라고 간청했다."], ["His only answer was to mount his horse, and ride up the mountain with Sansonetto.", "그의 유일한 대답은 말에 올라 산소네토와 함께 산으로 올라가는 것이었다."]], [["As soon, however, as he cast forth his eyes, and beheld what was round about him, he turned in sorrow, and looked down into Roncesvalles, and said, “O miserable valley!", "그러나 그가 눈을 들어 자기 둘레에 있는 것을 보자마자, 그는 슬픔에 잠겨 돌아서서 론세스바예스를 내려다보며 말했다. \"오, 비참한 골짜기여!"], ["the blood shed in thee this day will color thy name forever.”", "오늘 네 안에 흘릴 피가 네 이름을 영원토록 물들이리라.\""]], [["Orlando’s little camp were furious against the Saracens.", "오를란도의 작은 진영은 사라센인들에 대한 분노로 들끓었다."], ["They armed themselves with the greatest impatience.", "그들은 더없이 조바심을 내며 무장을 갖추었다."], ["There was nothing but lacing of helmets and mounting of horses, while good Archbishop Turpin went from rank to rank exhorting and encouraging the warriors of Christ.", "투구 끈을 매고 말에 오르는 것 외에는 아무것도 없었고, 그동안 선량한 대주교 튀르팽은 대열을 이리저리 누비며 그리스도의 전사들을 권면하고 격려했다."], ["Orlando and his captains withdrew for a moment to consultation.", "오를란도와 그의 지휘관들은 잠시 의논하러 물러났다."], ["He fairly groaned for sorrow, and at first had not a word to say, so wretched he felt at having brought his people to die in Roncesvalles.", "그는 슬픔에 그야말로 신음했고, 처음에는 한마디도 하지 못했으니, 자기 사람들을 론세스바예스에서 죽게 만든 것이 그토록 비참했기 때문이다. 그러다 그가 말했다."], ["Then he said: “If it had entered into my heart to conceive the king of Spain to be such a villain never would you have seen this day.", "\"에스파냐 왕이 이런 악당일 줄 내 마음에 한 번이라도 떠올랐더라면, 그대들은 결코 오늘을 보지 않았을 것이다. 그는 나와 수천 가지 예의와 좋은 말을 주고받았다."], ["He has exchanged with me a thousand courtesies and good words; and I thought that the worse enemies we had been before, the better friends we had become now.", "그래서 나는 우리가 전에 더 나쁜 원수였던 만큼, 이제 더 좋은 벗이 되었다고 여겼다. 나는 모든 인간이 좋은 기회가 있으면 이런 종류의 미덕을 발휘할 수 있다고 믿었다."], ["I fancied every human being capable of this kind of virtue on a good opportunity, saving, indeed, such base-hearted wretches as can never forgive their very forgivers; and of these I did not suppose him to be one.", "다만 제 용서한 이조차 결코 용서하지 못하는 그런 비열한 마음의 악당들만은 빼고 말이다. 그리고 나는 그가 그런 자들 가운데 하나라고는 생각지 않았다."], ["Let us die, if die we must, like honest and gallant men, so that it shall be said of us it was only our bodies that died.", "죽어야 한다면, 정직하고 용맹한 사내답게 죽자. 그리하여 우리에 대해, 죽은 것은 다만 우리의 몸뿐이었다는 말이 나오게 하자."], ["The reason why I did not sound the horn was partly because I thought it did not become us, and partly because our liege lord could hardly save us, even if he heard it.”", "내가 뿔피리를 불지 않은 까닭은, 한편으로는 그것이 우리에게 어울리지 않는다고 여겼기 때문이고, 또 한편으로는 설령 우리 주군이 그 소리를 듣는다 해도 우리를 좀처럼 구할 수 없을 것이기 때문이다.\""], ["And with these words Orlando sprang to his horse, crying, “Away against the Saracens!”", "그리고 이 말과 함께 오를란도는 말 위로 뛰어오르며 외쳤다. \"사라센인들을 향해 진격하라!\""], ["But he had no sooner turned his face than he wept bitterly, and said, “O Holy Virgin, think not of me, the sinner Orlando, but have pity on these thy servants!”", "그러나 그는 얼굴을 돌리기가 무섭게 쓰라리게 울며 말했다. \"오, 거룩한 동정녀여, 저 죄인 오를란도는 생각지 마시고, 부디 당신의 이 종들을 가엾이 여기소서!\""]], [["And now with a mighty dust, and an infinite sound of horns and tambours, which came filling the valley, the first army of the infidels made its", "이제 어마어마한 흙먼지와 골짜기를 가득 채우며 밀려드는 끝없는 뿔피리와 작은북 소리와 함께, 이교도들의 제1군이"]], [["appearance, horses neighing, and a thousand pennons flying in the air.", "군마들이 늠름하게 나타났고, 말들은 울부짖었으며, 수천 개의 군기가 공중에서 펄럭였다."], ["King Falseron led them on, saying to his officers: “Let nobody dare to lay a finger on Orlando.", "팔세론[Falseron] 왕이 그들을 이끌었고, 휘하 장교들에게 이렇게 말했다. \"누구도 감히 오를란도[Orlando]에게 손을 대서는 안 된다."], ["He belongs to myself.", "그는 내 몫이다."], ["The revenge of my son’s death is mine.", "내 아들의 죽음에 대한 복수는 내 것이다."], ["I will cut the man down that comes between us.”", "우리 사이에 끼어드는 자는 내가 베어 버리겠다.\""]], [["“Now, friends,”", "\"자, 친구들이여.\""], ["said Orlando, “every man for himself, and St.", "오를란도가 말했다. \"각자 자기 한 몸을 지키고, 성 미카엘께서 우리 모두를 지켜 주시기를!"], ["Michael for us all! There is not one here that is not a perfect knight.”", "여기 모인 이들 가운데 완벽한 기사가 아닌 자는 하나도 없다.\""], ["And he might well say it, for the flower of all France was there, except Rinaldo and Ricciardetto—every man a picked man, all friends and constant companions of Orlando.", "그가 이렇게 말할 만도 했으니, 리날도[Rinaldo]와 리차르데토[Ricciardetto]만 빼고 온 프랑스의 정수가 그곳에 모여 있었다. 한 사람 한 사람이 가려 뽑힌 인재였으며, 모두 오를란도의 벗이자 변함없는 동료였다."]], [["So the captains of the little troop and of the great army sat looking at one another, and singling one another out as the latter came on, and then the knights put spear in rest, and ran for a while two and two in succession, one against the other.", "그리하여 작은 부대와 거대한 군대의 지휘관들은 서로를 바라보며 마주 앉아 있다가, 큰 군대가 다가오자 서로의 상대를 골라냈고, 이윽고 기사들은 창을 겨누고 한동안 둘씩 둘씩 차례로 달려 나가 서로 맞부딪쳤다."]], [["Astolpho was the first to move.", "가장 먼저 움직인 것은 아스톨포[Astolpho]였다."], ["He ran against Arlotto of Sorio, and thrust his antagonist’s body out of the saddle, and his soul into the other world.", "그는 소리오의 아를로토[Arlotto]를 향해 달려가, 상대의 몸뚱이를 안장에서 밀쳐 내고 그 영혼을 저세상으로 보내 버렸다."], ["Oliver encountered Malprimo, and, though he received a thrust which hurt him, sent his lance right through the heart of Malprimo.", "올리버[Oliver]는 말프리모[Malprimo]와 맞붙었고, 비록 그를 다치게 하는 일격을 받기는 했으나, 자신의 창을 말프리모의 심장 한가운데로 곧장 꿰뚫었다."]], [["Falseron was daunted at this blow.", "팔세론은 이 일격에 기가 꺾였다."], ["“Truly,”", "\"참으로.\""], ["thought he, “this is a marvel.”", "그는 생각했다. \"이것은 놀라운 일이로다.\""], ["Oliver did not press on among the Saracens, his wound was too painful; but Orlando now put himself and his whole band in motion, and you may guess what an uproar ensued.", "올리버는 사라센인들 사이로 밀고 들어가지는 못했으니, 그 상처가 너무 고통스러웠던 것이다. 그러나 이제 오를란도가 자신과 휘하의 온 무리를 움직이기 시작하니, 어떤 소란이 뒤따랐을지 가히 짐작할 만하다."], ["The sound of the rattling of blows and helmets was as if the forge of Vulcan had been thrown open.", "타격과 투구가 부딪쳐 울리는 소리는 마치 불카누스의 대장간이 활짝 열린 듯하였다."], ["Falseron beheld Orlando coming so furiously, that he thought him a Lucifer who had burst his chain, and was quite of another mind than when he purposed to have him all to himself.", "팔세론은 오를란도가 그토록 맹렬하게 다가오는 것을 보고, 사슬을 끊고 뛰쳐나온 루키페르라고 여겼으며, 그를 오로지 자신의 몫으로 삼겠노라 작정했을 때와는 전혀 다른 마음이 되었다."], ["On the contrary, he recommended himself to his gods, and turned away, meaning to wait for a more auspicious season of revenge.", "오히려 그는 자기 신들에게 몸을 의탁하고는, 보다 상서로운 복수의 때를 기다리려는 생각으로 발길을 돌렸다."], ["But Orlando hailed him with a terrible voice, saying, “O thou traitor!", "그러나 오를란도가 무시무시한 목소리로 그를 불러 세우며 말했다. \"오 이 배신자여!"], ["was this the end to which old quarrels were made up?”", "묵은 다툼을 화해로 끝맺은 것이 고작 이런 결말을 위해서였느냐?\""], ["Then he dashed at Falseron with a fury so swift, and at the same time with a mastery of his lance so marvellous, that, though he plunged it in the man’s body so as instantly to kill him, and then withdrew it, the body did not move in the saddle.", "그러고는 오를란도가 어찌나 날쌘 분노와 더불어 동시에 놀라운 창술로 팔세론에게 달려들었던지, 비록 그 몸뚱이에 창을 박아 즉사시키고 다시 빼냈음에도 그 몸은 안장에서 미동도 하지 않았다."], ["The hero himself, as he rushed onwards, was fain to see the end of a stroke so perfect, and turning his horse back, touched the carcass with his sword, and it fell on the instant!", "영웅은 앞으로 내달리면서도 그토록 완벽한 일격의 결말을 보고 싶어 못 견뎌, 말머리를 돌려 그 송장을 검으로 건드리니, 그것이 그 즉시 떨어졌다!"]], [["When the infidels beheld their leader dead such fear fell upon them that they were for leaving the field to the paladins, but they were unable.", "이교도들은 자기들의 지휘관이 죽은 것을 보자 어찌나 큰 공포가 그들을 덮쳤던지 팔라딘들에게 전장을 내주고 떠나려 하였으나, 그럴 수가 없었다."], ["Marsilius had drawn the rest of his forces round the valley like a net, so that their shoulders were turned in vain.", "마르실리우스[Marsilius]가 나머지 병력을 골짜기 둘레로 그물처럼 둘러쳤던 까닭에, 등을 돌려 보아야 헛일이었다."], ["Orlando rode into the thick of them, and wherever he went thunderbolts fell upon helmets.", "오를란도는 그들 한복판으로 말을 몰았고, 그가 가는 곳마다 투구 위로 벼락이 떨어졌다."], ["Oliver was again in the fray, with Walter and Baldwin, Avino and Avolio, while Archbishop Turpin had changed his crosier for a lance, and chased a new flock before him to the mountains.", "올리버는 다시 난전에 뛰어들어 발터[Walter] 및 발드윈[Baldwin], 아비노[Avino]와 아볼리오[Avolio]와 함께 싸웠고, 한편 투르팽[Turpin] 대주교는 주교 지팡이를 창으로 바꿔 들고 새로운 무리를 산으로 쫓아냈다."]], [["Yet what could be done against foes without number?", "그러나 수효를 헤아릴 수 없는 적들을 상대로 무엇을 할 수 있었으랴?"], ["Marsilius constantly pours them in.", "마르실리우스는 끊임없이 그들을 쏟아부었다."], ["The paladins are as units to thousands.", "팔라딘들은 수천에 맞선 한 줌에 지나지 않았다."], ["Why tarry the horses of Rinaldo and Ricciardetto?", "어찌하여 리날도와 리차르데토의 말들은 더디 오는가?"]], [["The horses did not tarry, but fate had been quicker than enchantment.", "말들이 더딘 것은 아니었으나, 운명이 마법보다 빨랐던 것이다."], ["Ashtaroth had presented himself to Rinaldo in Egypt, and, after telling his errand, he and Foul-mouth, his servant, entered the horses of Rinaldo and Ricciardetto, which began to neigh, and snort, and leap with the fiends within them, till off they flew through the air over the pyramids and across the desert, and reached Spain and the scene of action just as Marsilius brought up his third army.", "아슈타로트[Ashtaroth]가 이집트에서 리날도 앞에 나타나, 자신의 용건을 전한 뒤, 그와 그의 종 입더러운놈[Foul-mouth]이 리날도와 리차르데토의 말 속으로 들어가니, 그 말들은 몸속의 악귀들과 더불어 울부짖고 콧김을 내뿜으며 펄쩍 뛰기 시작하더니, 마침내 공중을 날아 피라미드 위를 지나 사막을 가로질러, 마르실리우스가 세 번째 군대를 끌고 올 바로 그때 스페인과 전장에 다다랐다."], ["The two paladins on their horses dropped right into the midst of the Saracens, and began making such havoc among them that Marsilius, who overlooked the fight from a mountain, thought his soldiers had turned against one another.", "말을 탄 두 팔라딘은 사라센인들 한복판으로 곧장 떨어져, 그들 사이에서 어찌나 큰 살육을 벌였던지, 산에서 싸움을 내려다보던 마르실리우스는 자기 병사들이 서로 등을 돌려 싸우는 줄로 여겼다."], ["Orlando beheld it, and guessed it could be no other but his cousins, and pressed to meet them.", "오를란도가 그것을 보고는, 자신의 사촌들이 틀림없으리라 짐작하고 그들을 향해 달려갔다."], ["Oliver coming up at the same moment, the rapture of the whole party is not to be expressed.", "바로 그 순간 올리버도 다가오니, 온 무리의 환희는 이루 말할 수 없었다."], ["After a few hasty words of explanation they were forced to turn again upon the enemy, whose numbers seemed perfectly without limit.", "황급히 몇 마디 설명을 주고받은 뒤, 그들은 다시 적에게 돌아서지 않을 수 없었으니, 그 수효는 도무지 끝이 없어 보였다."]], [["Orlando, making a bloody passage towards Marsilius, struck a youth on the head, whose helmet was so strong as to resist the blow, but at the same time flew off.", "오를란도는 마르실리우스를 향해 피의 길을 내며 나아가다가, 한 젊은이의 머리를 내리쳤는데, 그 투구가 어찌나 단단했던지 일격을 견뎌 냈으나 동시에 벗겨져 날아갔다. 오를란도가 두 번째 일격을 가하려는데, 그 젊은이가 외쳤다."], ["Orlando prepared to strike a second blow, when the youth exclaimed, “Hold!", "\"멈추시오! 당신은 내 아버지를 사랑했소."], ["you loved my father; I am Bujaforte!”", "나는 부야포르테[Bujaforte]요!\""], ["The paladin had never seen Bujaforte, but he saw the likeness to the good old man, his father, and he dropped his sword.", "팔라딘은 부야포르테를 본 적이 없었으나, 그 아버지인 선량한 노인과 닮은 것을 알아보고 검을 떨구었다. \"오 부야포르테여.\""], ["“O Bujaforte,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “I loved him indeed; but what does his son do here fighting against his friends?”", "\"내 진정 그를 사랑했노라. 한데 그의 아들이 어찌하여 여기서 제 벗들과 맞서 싸우고 있느냐?\""]], [["Bujaforte could not at once speak for weeping.", "부야포르테는 눈물 때문에 한동안 입을 떼지 못했다. 마침내 그가 말했다."], ["At length he said: “I am forced to be here by my lord and master, Marsilius; and I have made a show of fighting, but have not hurt a single Christian.", "\"나는 내 주군이자 영주이신 마르실리우스 때문에 여기 있을 수밖에 없소. 나는 싸우는 시늉만 했을 뿐, 그리스도교도는 한 명도 해치지 않았소."], ["Treachery is on every side of you.", "배신이 당신 사방에 깔려 있소."], ["Baldwin himself has a vest given him by Marsilius, that everybody may know the son of his friend Gan, and do him no harm.”", "발드윈 자신도 마르실리우스가 준 조끼를 입고 있으니, 모두가 그를 그의 벗 간[Gan]의 아들임을 알아보고 해치지 않게 하려는 것이오.\""]], [["“Put your helmet on again,”", "\"투구를 다시 쓰라.\""], ["said Orlando, “and behave just as you have done.", "오를란도가 말했다. \"그리고 지금까지 해 온 대로 행하라."], ["Never will your father’s friend be an enemy to the son.”", "그대 아버지의 벗이 그 아들에게 결코 적이 되지는 않으리라.\""]], [["The hero then turned in fury to look for Baldwin, who was hastening towards him at that moment, with friendliness in his looks.", "이윽고 영웅은 분노에 차서 발드윈을 찾으려 돌아섰는데, 마침 그 순간 발드윈이 다정한 표정으로 그를 향해 서둘러 다가오고 있었다."]], [["“’Tis strange,”", "\"이상한 일입니다.\""], ["said Baldwin, “I have done my duty as well as I could, yet nobody will come against me.", "발드윈이 말했다. \"나는 할 수 있는 한 내 본분을 다했건만, 아무도 나를 향해 덤벼들지 않습니다."], ["I have slain right and left, and cannot comprehend what it is that makes the stoutest infidels avoid me.”", "좌우로 적들을 베어 넘겼는데도, 무엇 때문에 가장 사나운 이교도들이 나를 피하는지 도무지 알 수가 없습니다.\""]], [["“Take off your vest,”", "\"그 조끼를 벗어라.\""], ["said Orlando, contemptuously, “and you will soon discover the secret, if you wish to know it.", "오를란도가 경멸하듯 말했다. \"그러면 알고 싶다면 곧 그 비밀을 알게 되리라."], ["Your father has sold us to Marsilius, all but his honorable son.”", "그대 아버지가 우리를 마르실리우스에게 팔아넘겼다. 다만 그의 명예로운 아들만은 빼고 말이다.\""]], [["“If my father,”", "\"만약 내 아버지가.\""], ["said Baldwin, impetuously tearing off the vest, “has been such a villain, and I escape dying, I will plunge this sword through his heart.", "발드윈이 격분하여 조끼를 찢어 던지며 말했다. \"그런 악당이었다면, 그리고 내가 죽음을 면한다면, 이 검을 그의 심장에 꽂아 넣으리라."], ["But I am no traitor, Orlando, and you do me wrong to say it.", "그러나 나는 배신자가 아니오, 오를란도여. 그리 말하다니 나를 욕보이는 것이오."], ["Think not I can live with dishonor.”", "내가 불명예를 안고 살 수 있으리라 생각지 마시오.\""]], [["Baldwin spurred off into the fight, not waiting to hear another word from Orlando, who was very sorry for what he had said, for he perceived that the youth was in despair.", "발드윈은 오를란도의 다음 말을 들으려고 기다리지도 않고 박차를 가하여 싸움터로 내달렸다. 오를란도는 자기가 한 말을 몹시 후회했으니, 그 젊은이가 절망에 빠진 것을 알아챘기 때문이다."]], [["And now the fight raged beyond all it had done before; twenty pagans went down for one paladin, but still the paladins fell.", "이제 싸움은 그 어느 때보다도 격렬하게 타올랐다. 팔라딘 한 명에 이교도 스무 명이 쓰러졌으나, 그래도 팔라딘들도 쓰러져 갔다."], ["Sansonetto was beaten to earth by the club of Grandonio, Walter d’Amulion had his shoulder broken, Berlinghieri and Ottone were slain, and at last Astolpho fell, in revenge of whose death Orlando turned the spot where he died into a lake of Saracen blood.", "산소네토[Sansonetto]는 그란도니오[Grandonio]의 곤봉에 맞아 땅바닥에 나가떨어졌고, 다물리온의 발터는 어깨가 부러졌으며, 베를링기에리[Berlinghieri]와 오토네[Ottone]가 죽임을 당했고, 마침내 아스톨포도 쓰러지니, 오를란도는 그의 죽음에 복수하고자 그가 죽은 자리를 사라센인의 피로 된 호수로 만들어 버렸다."], ["The luckless Bujaforte met Rinaldo, and before he could explain how he seemed to be fighting on the Saracen side received such a blow upon the head that he fell, unable to utter a word.", "불운한 부야포르테는 리날도와 마주쳤는데, 어찌하여 자기가 사라센 편에서 싸우는 것처럼 보이는지 미처 설명도 하기 전에 머리에 어찌나 모진 일격을 받았던지, 한마디 말도 내뱉지 못하고 쓰러졌다."], ["Orlando, cutting his way to a spot where there was a great struggle and uproar, found the poor youth Baldwin, the son of Gan, with two spears in his breast.", "오를란도는 격렬한 싸움과 소란이 벌어지는 곳으로 길을 뚫고 나아가다가, 가슴에 창 두 자루가 박힌 가엾은 젊은이 발드윈, 곧 간의 아들을 발견했다."], ["“I am no traitor now,”", "\"이제 나는 배신자가 아니오.\""], ["said Baldwin, and those were the last words he said.", "발드윈이 말했고, 그것이 그가 남긴 마지막 말이었다."], ["Orlando was bitterly sorry to have been the cause of his death, and tears streamed from his eyes.", "오를란도는 자기가 그의 죽음의 원인이 된 것을 몹시 슬퍼하여, 두 눈에서 눈물이 흘러내렸다."], ["At length down went Oliver himself.", "마침내 올리버 자신도 쓰러졌다."], ["He had become blinded with his own blood, and smitten Orlando without knowing him.", "그는 제 피에 눈이 멀어, 오를란도를 알아보지 못한 채 그를 내리쳤던 것이다. \"이게 어찌 된 일인가, 사촌이여.\""], ["“How now, cousin,”", "오를란도가 외쳤다."], ["cried Orlando, “have you too gone over to the enemy?”", "\"그대마저 적에게 넘어갔단 말인가?\""], ["“O my lord and master,”", "\"오 나의 주군이자 영주여.\""], ["cried the other, “I ask your pardon.", "상대가 외쳤다. \"용서를 비오."], ["I can see nothing; I am dying.", "나는 아무것도 보이지 않소."], ["Some traitor has stabbed me in the back.", "나는 죽어 가고 있소. 어떤 배신자가 내 등을 찔렀소."], ["If you love me, lead my horse into the thick of them, so that I may not die unavenged.”", "나를 사랑하거든, 내 말을 저들 한복판으로 끌고 가 주오, 내가 복수도 못 한 채 죽지 않도록 말이오.\""]], [["“I shall die myself before long,”", "\"나도 머지않아 죽으리라.\" 오를란도가 말했다."], ["said Orlando, “out of very toil and grief; so we will go together.”", "\"오로지 고된 싸움과 비탄 때문에 말이다. 그러니 우리 함께 가세.\""]], [["Orlando led his cousin’s horse where the press was thickest, and dreadful was the strength of the dying man and his tired companion.", "오를란도는 사촌의 말을 가장 빽빽한 곳으로 끌고 갔는데, 죽어 가는 자와 지친 그 동료의 힘은 무시무시하였다. 그들은 거리 하나를 내며 싸움터를 빠져나왔고, 오를란도는 사촌을 천막으로 데려가서 말했다."], ["They made a street through which they passed out of the battle, and Orlando led his cousin away to his tent, and said, “Wait a little till I return, for I will go and sound the horn on the hill yonder.”", "\"내가 돌아올 때까지 잠시 기다리게. 저기 언덕에 가서 뿔나팔을 불고 오겠네.\""]], [["“’Tis of no use,”", "\"소용없네.\""], ["said Oliver, “my spirit is fast going and desires to be with its Lord and Saviour.”", "올리버가 말했다. \"내 영혼은 빠르게 떠나고 있으며, 자기 주님이자 구세주와 함께하기를 바라고 있다네.\""]], [["He would have said more, but his words came from him imperfectly, like those of a man in a dream, and so he expired.", "그는 더 말하고자 하였으나, 그 말은 꿈꾸는 자의 말처럼 어눌하게 흘러나왔고, 그렇게 그는 숨을 거두었다."]], [["When Orlando saw him dead he felt as if he was alone on the earth, and he was quite willing to leave it, only he wished that King Charles, at the foot of the mountains, should know how the case stood before he went.", "오를란도는 그가 죽은 것을 보자 자기 혼자만 이 세상에 남은 듯한 심정이 들었고, 이 세상을 떠나는 데 아무 미련이 없었으되, 다만 그가 떠나기 전에 산기슭에 있는 샤를[Charles] 왕이 사정이 어떠한지 알아 주기를 바랐다."], ["So he took up the horn and blew it three times, with such force that the blood burst out of his nose and mouth.", "그리하여 그는 뿔나팔을 집어 들어 세 번 불었으니, 어찌나 세게 불었던지 코와 입에서 피가 터져 나왔다."], ["Turpin says that at the third blast the horn broke in two.", "투르팽은 세 번째 나팔 소리에 뿔나팔이 두 동강으로 갈라졌다고 전한다."]], [["In spite of all the noise of the battle, the sound of the horn broke over it like a voice out of the other world.", "싸움의 그 온갖 소음에도 불구하고, 뿔나팔 소리는 마치 저세상에서 들려오는 목소리처럼 그 위로 울려 퍼졌다."], ["They say that birds fell dead at it, and that the whole Saracen army drew back in terror.", "그 소리에 새들이 죽어 떨어졌으며, 온 사라센 군대가 공포에 질려 물러섰다고 전한다."], ["Charlemagne was sitting in the midst of his court when the sound reached him, and Gan was there.", "그 소리가 다다랐을 때 샤를마뉴[Charlemagne]는 자기 궁정 한가운데 앉아 있었고, 간도 그 자리에 있었다."], ["The Emperor was the first to hear it.", "그 소리를 가장 먼저 들은 것은 황제였다."]], [["“Do you hear that?”", "\"저 소리가 들리는가?\""], ["said he to his nobles.", "그가 귀족들에게 말했다."], ["“Did you hear the horn as I heard it?”", "\"내가 들은 것처럼 그대들도 저 뿔나팔 소리를 들었는가?\""]], [["Upon this they all listened, and Gan felt his heart misgive him.", "이에 그들 모두 귀를 기울였고, 간은 가슴이 철렁 내려앉는 것을 느꼈다."], ["The horn sounded a second time.", "뿔나팔이 두 번째로 울렸다."]], [["“What is the meaning of this?”", "\"이게 무슨 뜻인가?\""], ["said Charles.", "샤를이 말했다."]], [["“Orlando is hunting,”", "\"오를란도가 사냥을 하고 있는데.\""], ["observed Gan, “and the stag is killed.”", "간이 말했다. \"사슴이 잡힌 것이지요.\""]], [["But when the horn sounded yet a third time, and the blast was one of so dreadful a vehemence, everybody looked at the other, and then they all looked at Gan in a fury.", "그러나 뿔나팔이 세 번째로 울렸을 때, 그 소리가 어찌나 무섭도록 격렬했던지, 모두가 서로를 쳐다보더니, 이윽고 다들 분노에 차 간을 노려보았다."], ["Charles rose from his seat.", "샤를이 자리에서 일어섰다."]], [["“This is no hunting of the stag,”", "\"이것은 사슴 사냥이 아니다.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“The sound goes to my very heart.", "\"그 소리가 내 심장 깊은 곳까지 파고든다."], ["O Gan!", "오 간이여!"], ["O Gan!", "오 간이여!"], ["Not for thee do I blush, but for myself.", "그대 때문에 내가 낯을 붉히는 것이 아니라, 나 자신 때문에 붉히노라."], ["O foul and monstrous villain!", "오 더럽고 흉악한 악당이여!"], ["Take him, gentleman, and keep him in close prison.", "경들이여, 이자를 잡아다가 깊은 옥에 가두라."], ["Would to God I had not lived to see this day!”", "하느님께서 내가 이 날을 보도록 살려 두지 않으셨더라면!\""]], [["But it was no time for words.", "그러나 말로 따질 때가 아니었다."], ["They put the traitor in prison and then Charles, with all his court, took his way to Roncesvalles, grieving and praying.", "그들은 그 배신자를 옥에 가두었고, 이윽고 샤를은 온 궁정을 거느리고 슬퍼하며 기도하며 롱스보[Roncesvalles]로 길을 떠났다."]], [["It was afternoon when the horn sounded, and half an hour after it when the Emperor set out; and meantime Orlando had returned to the fight that he might do his duty, however hopeless, as long as he could sit his horse.", "뿔나팔이 울린 것은 오후였고, 황제가 출발한 것은 그로부터 반 시간 뒤였다. 그동안 오를란도는, 아무리 가망 없을지라도 자기가 말 위에 앉아 있을 수 있는 한 본분을 다하고자 다시 싸움터로 돌아갔다."], ["At length he found his end approaching, for toil and fever, and rode all alone to a fountain where he had before quenched his thirst.", "마침내 그는 고된 싸움과 열병으로 자신의 최후가 다가옴을 느끼고는, 전에 갈증을 풀었던 샘으로 홀로 말을 몰았다."], ["His horse was wearier than he, and no sooner had his master alighted than the beast, kneeling down as if to take leave, and to say, “I have brought you to a place of rest,”", "그의 말은 그보다 더 지쳐 있어서, 주인이 내리자마자 그 짐승은 마치 작별을 고하려는 듯, \"당신을 안식의 자리로 모셔 왔습니다\" 하고 말하려는 듯 무릎을 꿇더니, 그의 발치에서 쓰러져 죽었다."], ["fell dead at his feet. Orlando cast water on him from the fountain, not wishing to believe him dead; but when he found it to no purpose, he grieved for him as if he had been a human being, and addressed him by name with tears, and asked forgiveness if he had ever done him wrong.", "오를란도는 그가 죽었다고 믿고 싶지 않아 샘물을 떠다 끼얹었으나, 아무 소용이 없음을 알고는 마치 인간이었던 것처럼 그를 슬퍼하며 눈물을 흘리며 이름을 불렀고, 혹여 자기가 그를 부당하게 대한 일이 있었다면 용서를 빌었다."], ["They say that the horse, at these words, opened his eyes a little, and looked kindly at his master, and then stirred never more.", "이 말에 그 말이 눈을 조금 떠서 주인을 다정하게 바라보더니 이내 더는 움직이지 않았다고 전한다."], ["They say also that Orlando then summoning all his strength, smote a rock near him with his beautiful sword Durindana, thinking to shiver the steel in pieces, and so prevent its falling into the hands of the enemy, but though the rock split like a slate, and a great cleft remained ever after to astonish the eyes of pilgrims, the sword remained uninjured.", "또한 오를란도가 그때 온 힘을 다하여 아름다운 검 두린다나[Durindana]로 곁에 있던 바위를 내리쳤다고도 전하는데, 강철을 산산조각 내어 적의 손에 떨어지지 않게 하려는 생각이었다. 그러나 바위는 점판암처럼 쪼개져 그 큰 틈이 이후로도 영영 남아 순례자들의 눈을 놀라게 하였건만, 검은 조금도 상하지 않은 채 남았다."]], [["And now Rinaldo and Ricciardetto came up, with Turpin, having driven back the Saracens, and told Orlando that the battle was won.", "이제 리날도와 리차르데토가 투르팽과 함께 다가왔으니, 그들은 사라센인들을 물리치고 와서 오를란도에게 전투에서 이겼노라 전하였다."], ["Then Orlando knelt before Turpin and begged remission of his sins, and Turpin gave him absolution.", "그러자 오를란도는 투르팽 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 자기 죄를 사하여 달라고 빌었고, 투르팽이 그에게 사죄를 베풀었다."], ["Orlando fixed his eyes on the hilt of his sword as on a crucifix, and embraced it, and he raised his eyes and appeared like a creature seraphical and transfigured, and bowing his head, he breathed out his pure soul.", "오를란도는 마치 십자가상을 보듯 자기 검의 자루에 두 눈을 고정하고는 그것을 끌어안았으며, 두 눈을 들어 올리니 천사처럼 거룩하게 변모한 존재 같았고, 머리를 숙이며 그 맑은 영혼을 내쉬었다."]], [["And now King Charles and his nobles came up.", "이제 샤를 왕과 그 귀족들이 다다랐다."], ["The Emperor, at sight of the dead Orlando, threw himself, as if he had been a reckless youth, from his horse, and embraced and kissed the body, and said: “I bless thee, Orlando; I bless thy whole life, and all that thou wast, and all that thou ever didst, and the father that begat thee; and I ask pardon of thee for believing those who brought thee to thine end.", "황제는 죽은 오를란도를 보자, 마치 무모한 젊은이라도 된 듯 말에서 몸을 던져 그 시신을 끌어안고 입 맞추며 말했다. \"그대를 축복하노라, 오를란도여."], ["They shall have their reward, O thou beloved one!", "그대의 온 생애를, 그대의 모든 것을, 그대가 한 모든 일을, 그리고 그대를 낳은 아버지를 축복하노라. 또한 그대를 죽음으로 몰아간 자들의 말을 믿은 것을 그대에게 용서받기를 구하노라."], ["But indeed it is thou that livest, and I who am worse than dead.”", "그들은 응분의 대가를 치를 것이다, 오 사랑하는 이여! 그러나 참으로 살아 있는 이는 그대이며, 죽은 것보다 못한 자는 바로 나로다.\""]], [["Horrible to the Emperor’s eyes was the sight of the field of Roncesvalles.", "롱스보의 들판은 황제의 눈에 끔찍하기 그지없었다."], ["The Saracens indeed had fled, conquered; but all his paladins but two were left on it dead, and the whole valley looked like a great slaughterhouse, trampled into blood and dirt, and reeking to the heat.", "과연 사라센인들은 패하여 달아났으나, 그의 팔라딘들은 둘만 빼고 모두 그곳에 죽어 쓰러져 있었고, 온 골짜기는 거대한 도살장처럼 피와 흙으로 짓밟히고 열기에 김을 내뿜으며 악취를 풍기고 있었다."], ["Charles trembled to his heart’s core for wonder and agony.", "샤를은 경악과 고뇌로 심장 한가운데까지 떨었다."], ["After gazing dumbly on the place he cursed it with a solemn curse, and wished that never grass might grow in it again, nor seed of any kind, neither within it nor on any of its mountains around, but the anger of Heaven abide over it forever.", "그곳을 말없이 응시한 뒤, 그는 엄숙한 저주로 그곳을 저주하며, 그 안에도 그 둘레의 어떤 산에도 다시는 풀이 자라지 말고 어떤 종류의 씨앗도 돋지 말 것이며, 오직 하늘의 진노만이 그 위에 영원히 머물기를 바랐다."]], [["Charles and his warriors went after the Saracens into Spain.", "샤를과 그의 전사들은 사라센인들을 쫓아 스페인으로 들어갔다. 그들은 사라고사를 함락하여 불태웠고, 마르실리우스는 그가 간과 더불어 흉계를 꾸몄던 그 캐럽나무에 매달렸다."], ["They took and fired Saragossa, and Marsilius was hung to the carob-tree under which he had planned his villainy with Gan; and Gan was hung and drawn and quartered in Roncesvalles, amidst the execrations of the country.", "그리고 간은 온 나라의 저주 속에 롱스보에서 교수되어 사지가 찢기는 형벌을 받았다. 리날도와 바야르"]], [["# RINALDO AND BAYARD", "# RINALDO AND BAYARD"]], [["————\nCharlemagne was overwhelmed with grief at the loss of so many of his bravest warriors at the disaster of Roncesvalles, and bitterly reproached himself for his credulity in resigning himself so completely to the counsels of the treacherous Count Gan.", "샤를마뉴는 롱스보의 참사에서 그토록 많은 가장 용감한 전사들을 잃은 데 비탄에 잠겼고, 배신자 간 백작의 권고에 자신을 그토록 송두리째 내맡긴 자기 경솔함을 쓰라리게 자책했다."], ["Yet he soon fell into a similar snare when he suffered his unworthy son, Charlot, to acquire such an influence over him, that he constantly led him into acts of cruelty and injustice that in his right mind he would have scorned to commit.", "그러나 그는 곧 비슷한 덫에 빠지고 말았으니, 자기 못난 아들 샤를로[Charlot]가 자신에게 지나친 영향력을 갖도록 내버려 두어, 그 아들이 제정신이라면 부끄러워하여 결코 저지르지 않았을 잔인하고 부당한 행위들로 그를 끊임없이 이끌었던 것이다."], ["Rinaldo and his brothers, for some slight offence to the imperious young prince, were forced to fly from Paris, and to take shelter in their castle of Montalban; for Charles had publicly said, if he could take them he would hang them all.", "리날도와 그 형제들은 이 오만한 젊은 왕자에게 사소한 무례를 저질렀다는 이유로 파리에서 달아나, 자기들의 성 몽탈방[Montalban]으로 피신하지 않을 수 없었다. 샤를이 그들을 붙잡을 수만 있다면 모조리 교수하겠노라 공공연히 말했기 때문이다."], ["He sent numbers of his bravest knights to arrest them, but all without success.", "그는 가장 용감한 기사들을 여럿 보내 그들을 체포하려 했으나, 모두 헛수고였다."], ["Either Rinaldo foiled their efforts and sent them back, stripped of their armor and of their glory, or, after meeting and conferring with him, they came back and told the king they could not be his instruments for such a work.", "리날도가 그들의 시도를 좌절시키고 갑옷과 영광을 빼앗긴 채 돌려보내거나, 아니면 그를 만나 이야기를 나눈 뒤 그들이 돌아와 왕에게 그런 일에 자기들은 그의 도구가 될 수 없노라 고하곤 했다."]], [["At last Charles himself raised a great army, and went in person to compel the paladin to submit.", "마침내 샤를 자신이 대군을 일으켜, 그 팔라딘을 굴복시키고자 몸소 나섰다."], ["He ravaged all the country round about Montalban, so that supplies of food should be cut off, and he threatened death to any who should attempt to issue forth, hoping to compel the garrison to submit for want of food.", "그는 몽탈방 주위의 온 고장을 짓밟아 식량 보급을 끊어 버렸고, 감히 밖으로 나오려 하는 자는 누구든 죽이겠노라 위협하니, 식량이 떨어져 수비대가 굴복하지 않을 수 없게 만들려는 속셈이었다."]], [["Rinaldo’s resources had been brought so low that it seemed useless to contend any longer.", "리날도의 자원은 너무도 바닥나 더는 버티는 것이 무의미해 보일 지경이 되었다."], ["His brothers had been taken prisoners in a skirmish, and his only hope of saving their lives was in making terms with the king.", "그의 형제들은 작은 접전에서 포로로 붙잡혔고, 그들의 목숨을 구할 유일한 희망은 왕과 화친을 맺는 데 있었다."]], [["So he sent a messenger, offering to yield himself and his castle if the king would spare his and his brothers’ lives.", "그리하여 그는 사자를 보내, 왕이 자기와 형제들의 목숨을 살려 준다면 자신과 성을 내어 주겠노라 제안했다."], ["While the messenger was gone Rinaldo, impatient to learn what tidings he might bring, rode out to meet him.", "사자가 떠나 있는 동안 리날도는 어떤 소식을 가져올지 안달이 나서, 그를 맞으러 말을 타고 나갔다."], ["When he had ridden as far as he thought prudent he stopped in a wood, and alighting, tied Bayard to a tree.", "그는 분별 있다 여겨지는 만큼 말을 달린 뒤, 숲에서 멈추어 내려서서는 바야르[Bayard]를 나무에 매어 두었다."], ["Then he sat down, and, as he waited, he fell asleep.", "그러고는 앉아서 기다리다가 잠이 들었다."], ["Bayard meanwhile got loose, and strayed away where the grass tempted him.", "그동안 바야르는 풀어져, 풀이 마음을 끄는 곳으로 어슬렁어슬렁 가 버렸다."], ["Just then came along some country people, who said to one another, “Look, is not that the great horse Bayard that Rinaldo rides?", "마침 그때 시골 사람 몇이 지나가다가 서로 말했다. \"이보게, 저것이 리날도가 타는 그 명마 바야르 아닌가?"], ["Let us take him, and carry him to King Charles, who will pay us well for our trouble.”", "저놈을 붙잡아 샤를 왕에게 데려가세. 우리 수고에 후하게 값을 쳐 주실 게야.\""], ["They did so, and the king was delighted with his prize, and gave them a present that made them rich to their dying day.", "그들은 그렇게 했고, 왕은 그 노획물에 크게 기뻐하며 그들이 죽는 날까지 부유하게 살 만한 선물을 주었다."]], [["When Rinaldo woke he looked round for his horse, and, finding him not, he groaned, and said, “O unlucky hour that I was born!", "리날도가 깨어나 자기 말을 찾아 둘러보았으나 보이지 않자, 그는 신음하며 말했다. \"오 내가 태어난 불운한 시각이여!"], ["how fortune persecutes me!”", "운명이 어찌 이리 나를 괴롭히는가!\""], ["So desperate was he that he took off his armor and his spurs, saying, “What need have I of these, since Bayard is lost?”", "그는 어찌나 절망했던지 갑옷과 박차를 벗으며 말했다. \"바야르를 잃었거늘, 이런 것들이 내게 무슨 소용이랴?\""], ["While he stood thus lamenting, a man came from the thicket, seemingly bent with age.", "그가 이렇게 한탄하며 서 있을 때, 덤불에서 한 사내가 나왔는데, 나이로 허리가 굽은 듯했다."], ["He had a long beard hanging over his breast, and eyebrows that almost covered his eyes.", "그는 긴 수염을 가슴까지 드리웠고, 눈썹이 두 눈을 거의 덮고 있었다. 그가 리날도에게 좋은 날 되시라 인사했다."], ["He bade Rinaldo good day.", "리날도가 고마움을 표하며 말했다."], ["Rinaldo thanked him, and said, “A good day I have hardly had since I was born.”", "\"좋은 날이라곤 태어난 이래 거의 가져 본 적이 없소.\" 그러자 노인이 말했다."], ["Then said the old man, “Signor Rinaldo, you must not despair, for God will make all things turn to the best.”", "\"리날도 나리, 절망하셔서는 안 됩니다. 하느님께서 모든 일을 가장 좋은 쪽으로 돌려놓으실 테니까요.\""], ["Rinaldo answered, “My trouble is too heavy for me to hope relief.", "리날도가 대답했다. \"내 고통은 너무 무거워 구원을 바랄 수가 없소."], ["The king has taken my brothers, and means to put them to death.", "왕이 내 형제들을 잡아다 죽이려 하오."], ["I thought to rescue them by means of my horse Bayard, but while I slept some thief has stolen him.”", "내 말 바야르로 그들을 구해 낼 작정이었건만, 내가 잠든 사이 어떤 도둑놈이 그놈을 훔쳐 가 버렸소.\""], ["The old man replied, “I will remember you and your brothers in my prayers.", "노인이 대답했다. \"기도 가운데 나리와 형제분들을 기억하겠습니다."], ["I am a poor man, have you not something to give me?”", "저는 가난한 사람인데, 제게 무어라도 베푸실 것이 없으신지요?\""], ["Rinaldo said, “I have nothing to give,”", "리날도가 말했다. \"내게는 줄 것이 없소.\""], ["but then he recollected his spurs.", "그러나 곧 자기 박차가 떠올랐다."], ["He gave them to the beggar, and said, “Here, take my spurs.", "그는 그것을 거지에게 주며 말했다. \"자, 내 박차를 가지시오."], ["They are the first present my mother gave me when my father, Count Aymon, dubbed me knight.", "이것은 내 아버지 에몽[Aymon] 백작께서 나를 기사로 서임하셨을 때 어머니께서 처음 주신 선물이오."], ["They ought to bring you ten pounds.”", "이것이면 그대에게 십 파운드는 될 것이오.\""]], [["The old man took the spurs, and put them into his sack, and said, “Noble sir, have you nothing else you can give me?”", "노인은 박차를 받아 자루에 넣고는 말했다. \"고귀하신 나리, 제게 주실 만한 것이 달리 또 없으신지요?\""], ["Rinaldo replied, “Are you making sport of me?", "리날도가 대답했다. \"그대 지금 나를 놀리는 게요?"], ["I tell you truly if it were not for shame to beat one so helpless, I would teach you better manners.”", "진실로 말하건대, 그토록 무력한 자를 때리는 것이 부끄럽지만 않다면, 더 나은 예의범절을 가르쳐 주었을 것이오.\" 노인이 말했다."], ["The old man said, “Of a truth, sir, if you did so you would do a great sin.", "\"참으로, 나리, 그리하셨다면 큰 죄를 짓는 것이옵니다. 제가 동냥했던 모든 이가 저를 때렸더라면, 저는 진작에 맞아 죽었을 것입니다."], ["If all had beaten me of whom I have begged I should have been killed long ago, for I ask alms in churches and convents, and wherever I can.”", "저는 교회와 수도원에서, 그리고 할 수 있는 곳이면 어디서든 동냥을 청하니까요.\" \"그대 말이 옳소.\""], ["“You say true,”", "리날도가 대답했다."], ["replied Rinaldo, “if you did not ask, none would relieve you.”", "\"그대가 청하지 않으면 누구도 그대를 구제하지 않을 테지.\" 노인이 말했다."], ["The old man said, “True, noble sir, therefore I pray if you have anything more to spare, give it me.”", "\"옳습니다, 고귀하신 나리. 그러니 청하옵건대 나누어 주실 것이 더 있거든 제게 주십시오.\""], ["Rinaldo gave him his mantle, and said, “Take it, pilgrim.", "리날도는 그에게 자기 망토를 주며 말했다. \"받으시오, 순례자여."], ["I give it you for the love of Christ, that God would save my brothers from a shameful death, and help me to escape out of King Charles’s power.”", "그리스도의 사랑으로 그대에게 주는 것이니, 하느님께서 내 형제들을 치욕스러운 죽음에서 구하시고, 내가 샤를 왕의 손아귀에서 벗어나도록 도우시기를.\""]], [["The pilgrim took the mantle, folded it up, and put it into his bag.", "순례자는 망토를 받아 개켜서 자기 자루에 넣었다."], ["Then a third time he said to Rinaldo, “Sir, have you nothing left to give me that I may remember you in my prayers?”", "그러더니 세 번째로 리날도에게 말했다. \"나리, 기도 가운데 나리를 기억할 수 있도록 제게 주실 것이 더 남지 않았는지요?\""], ["“Wretch!”", "\"이 못된 것!\""], ["exclaimed Rinaldo, “do you make me your sport?”", "리날도가 외쳤다. \"그대 나를 놀림감으로 삼느냐?\""], ["and he drew his sword, and struck at him; but the old man warded off the blow with his staff, and said, “Rinaldo, would you slay your cousin, Malagigi?”", "그러고는 검을 빼어 그를 내리쳤으나, 노인은 지팡이로 그 일격을 막아 내며 말했다. \"리날도, 그대의 사촌 말라지지[Malagigi]를 죽이려는 게요?\""], ["When Rinaldo heard that he stayed his hand, and gazed doubtingly on the old man, who now threw aside his disguise, and appeared to be indeed Malagigi.", "리날도는 그 말을 듣자 손을 멈추고 노인을 미심쩍게 바라보았는데, 노인은 이제 변장을 벗어 던지니 과연 말라지지였다. \"사랑하는 사촌이여.\""], ["“Dear cousin,”", "리날도가 말했다."], ["said Rinaldo, “pray forgive me.", "\"부디 용서하시오."], ["I did not know you.", "그대를 알아보지 못했소."], ["Next to God, my trust is in you.", "하느님 다음으로 나의 믿음은 그대에게 있소."], ["Help my brothers to escape out of prison, I entreat you.", "부탁이니 내 형제들이 옥에서 빠져나오도록 도와주시오."], ["I have lost my horse, and therefore cannot render them any assistance.”", "나는 말을 잃어, 그들에게 아무런 도움도 줄 수가 없소.\""], ["Malagigi answered, “Cousin Rinaldo, I will enable you to recover your horse.", "말라지지가 대답했다. \"사촌 리날도여, 내 그대가 말을 되찾도록 해 주겠소."], ["Meanwhile, you must do as I say.”", "그동안 그대는 내가 시키는 대로 해야 하오.\""]], [["Then Malagigi took from his sack a gown, and gave it to Rinaldo to put on over his armor, and a hat that was full of holes, and an old pair of shoes to put on.", "이윽고 말라지지는 자루에서 긴 겉옷 하나를 꺼내, 리날도에게 갑옷 위에 입으라고 주었고, 구멍이 숭숭 난 모자 하나와 헌 신발 한 켤레를 신으라고 주었다."], ["They looked like two pilgrims, very old and poor.", "그들은 매우 늙고 가난한 두 순례자처럼 보였다."], ["Then they went forth from the wood, and after a little while saw four monks riding along the road.", "그러고서 그들이 숲에서 나오니, 얼마 지나지 않아 길을 따라 말을 타고 오는 수도사 넷이 보였다."], ["Malagigi said to Rinaldo, “I will go meet the monks, and see what news I can learn.”", "말라지지가 리날도에게 말했다. \"내가 가서 저 수도사들을 만나 무슨 소식이라도 알아낼 수 있는지 보겠소.\""]], [["Malagigi learned from the monks that on the approaching festival there would be a great crowd of people at court, for the prince was going to show the ladies the famous horse Bayard that used to belong to Rinaldo.", "말라지지는 수도사들에게서, 다가오는 축일에 궁정에 큰 인파가 모이리라는 것을 알아냈으니, 왕자가 전에 리날도의 소유였던 그 유명한 명마 바야르를 귀부인들에게 보여 주려 한다는 것이었다."], ["“What!”", "\"뭐라고요!\""], ["said the pilgrim; “is Bayard there?”", "순례자가 말했다. \"바야르가 거기 있단 말이오?\""], ["“Yes,”", "\"그렇소.\""], ["answered the monks; “the king has given him to Charlot, and, after the prince has ridden him the king means to pass sentence on the brothers of Rinaldo, and have them hanged.”", "수도사들이 대답했다. \"왕이 그 말을 샤를로에게 주었는데, 왕자가 그 말을 탄 다음, 왕은 리날도의 형제들에게 선고를 내려 그들을 교수할 작정이오.\""], ["Then Malagigi asked alms of the monks, but they would give him none, till he threw aside his pilgrim garb, and let them see his armor, when, partly for charity and partly for terror, they gave him a golden cup, adorned with precious stones that sparkled in the sunshine.", "그러자 말라지지가 수도사들에게 동냥을 청했으나, 그가 순례자의 옷을 벗어 던지고 자기 갑옷을 그들에게 보여 주기 전까지는 그들이 아무것도 주려 하지 않았다. 그제야 그들은 한편으로는 자비심에서, 한편으로는 두려움에서, 햇빛에 반짝이는 보석들로 장식된 황금 잔 하나를 그에게 주었다."], ["Malagigi then hastened back to Rinaldo, and told him what he had learned.", "그러자 말라지지는 서둘러 리날도에게 돌아가, 자기가 알아낸 것을 그에게 전했다."]], [["The morning of the feast-day Rinaldo and Malagigi came to the place where the sports were to be held.", "축일 아침, 리날도와 말라지지는 경기가 열릴 장소에 다다랐다. 말라지지가 리날도에게 박차를 도로 주며 말했다."], ["Malagigi gave Rinaldo his spurs back again, and said, “Cousin, put on your spurs, for you will need them.”", "\"사촌이여, 박차를 차시오, 그대가 그것을 필요로 할 테니.\" \"내가 어찌 그것을 필요로 하겠소.\""], ["“How shall I need them,”", "리날도가 말했다."], ["said Rinaldo, “since I have lost my horse?”", "\"말을 잃었거늘?\""], ["Yet he did as Malagigi directed him.", "그래도 그는 말라지지가 일러 준 대로 했다."]], [["When the two had taken their stand on the border of the field among the crowd the princes and ladies of the court began to assemble.", "두 사람이 군중 틈에 끼어 들판 가장자리에 자리를 잡자, 궁정의 왕자들과 귀부인들이 모여들기 시작했다."], ["When they were all assembled the king came also, and Charlot with him, near whom the horse Bayard was led, in the charge of grooms, who were expressly enjoined to guard him safely.", "그들이 모두 모이자 왕도 왔고, 샤를로도 함께 왔는데, 그 곁에는 마부들이 명마 바야르를 이끌고 있었으니, 그들은 그 말을 안전하게 지키라는 명령을 단단히 받은 터였다."], ["The king, looking round on the circle of spectators, saw Malagigi and Rinaldo, and observed the splendid cup that they had, and said to Charlot, “See, my son, what a brilliant cup those two pilgrims have got.", "왕은 구경꾼들의 둘레를 둘러보다가 말라지지와 리날도를 보았고, 그들이 가진 휘황한 잔을 눈여겨보고는 샤를로에게 말했다. \"보아라, 내 아들아, 저 두 순례자가 어찌나 빛나는 잔을 가졌는지."], ["It seems to be worth a hundred ducats.”", "백 두카트는 나가 보이는구나.\" \"참으로 그렇습니다.\""], ["“That is true,”", "샤를로가 말했다."], ["said Charlot; “Let us go and ask where they got it.”", "\"가서 저들이 어디서 그것을 얻었는지 물어보지요.\""], ["So they rode to the place where the pilgrims stood, and Charlot stopped Bayard close to them.", "그리하여 그들은 순례자들이 서 있는 곳으로 말을 몰았고, 샤를로는 그들 가까이에 바야르를 세웠다."]], [["The horse snuffed at the pilgrims, knew Rinaldo, and caressed his master.", "말은 순례자들의 냄새를 맡더니 리날도를 알아보고는 주인에게 어리광을 부렸다."], ["The king said to Malagigi, “Friend, where did you get that beautiful cup?”", "왕이 말라지지에게 말했다. \"이보게, 그 아름다운 잔을 어디서 얻었는가?\""], ["Malagigi replied, “Honorable sir, I paid for it all the money I have saved from eleven years’ begging in churches and convents.", "말라지지가 대답했다. \"존귀하신 나리, 저는 교회와 수도원에서 십일 년간 동냥하여 모은 돈을 죄다 들여 이것을 샀습니다."], ["The Pope himself has blessed it, and given it the power that whosoever eats or drinks out of it shall be pardoned of all his sins.”", "교황님께서 친히 이것을 축복하시어, 누구든 이것으로 먹거나 마시는 자는 자기 모든 죄를 사함받는 권능을 주셨습니다.\" 그러자 왕이 샤를로에게 말했다."], ["Then said the king to Charlot, “My son, these are right holy men; see how the dumb beast worships them.”", "\"내 아들아, 이들은 참으로 거룩한 사람들이로다. 말 못 하는 짐승조차 어찌 저들을 경배하는지 보아라.\""]], [["Then the king said to Malagigi, “Give me a morsel from your cup, that I may be cleared of my sins.”", "이윽고 왕이 말라지지에게 말했다. \"그대의 잔에서 한 모금 내게 주어, 내 죄가 씻기게 하라.\""], ["Malagigi answered, “Illustrious lord, I dare not do it, unless you will forgive all who have at any time offended you.", "말라지지가 대답했다. \"고명하신 주군이시여, 언제든 나리를 거스른 모든 자를 용서하지 않으신다면, 저는 감히 그리할 수 없습니다."], ["You know that Christ forgave all those who had betrayed and crucified him.”", "그리스도께서 당신을 배신하고 십자가에 못 박은 모든 자를 용서하셨음을 아시지 않습니까.\" 왕이 대답했다."], ["The king replied, “Friend, that is true; but Rinaldo has so grievously offended me, that I cannot forgive him, nor that other man, Malagigi, the magician.", "\"이보게, 그 말은 옳다. 그러나 리날도는 나를 너무도 심하게 거슬렀으니, 나는 그를 용서할 수 없고, 또 다른 자, 마법사 말라지지도 용서할 수 없다."], ["These two shall never live in my kingdom again.", "이 둘은 결코 다시는 내 왕국에서 살지 못할 것이다."], ["If I catch them I will certainly have them hanged.", "내가 그들을 붙잡으면 반드시 교수하겠다."], ["But tell me, pilgrim, who is that man who stands beside you?”", "한데 말해 보라, 순례자여, 그대 곁에 선 저 사내는 누구인가?\""], ["“He is deaf, dumb, and blind,”", "\"저자는 귀머거리에 벙어리에 장님입니다.\""], ["said Malagigi.", "말라지지가 말했다."], ["Then the king said again, “Give me to drink of your cup, to take away my sins.”", "그러자 왕이 다시 말했다. \"그대의 잔에서 내게 마시게 하여, 내 죄를 없애게 하라.\""], ["Malagigi answered, “My lord king, here is my poor brother, who for fifty days has not heard, spoken, nor seen.", "말라지지가 대답했다. \"주군이신 왕이시여, 여기 제 가엾은 아우가 있는데, 쉰 날 동안 듣지도 말하지도 보지도 못했습니다."], ["This misfortune befell him in a house where we found shelter, and the day before yesterday we met with a wise woman, who told him the only hope of a cure for him was to come to some place where Bayard was to be ridden, and to mount and ride him; that would do him more good than anything else.”", "이 불행은 저희가 묵었던 어느 집에서 그에게 닥쳤는데, 그저께 저희가 한 지혜로운 여인을 만났더니, 그를 고칠 유일한 희망은 바야르를 타는 어떤 곳에 가서 그 말에 올라타 모는 것이라 일러 주었습니다. 그것이 무엇보다도 그에게 좋으리라 하였습니다.\""], ["Then said the king, “Friend, you have come to the right place, for Bayard is to be ridden here to-day.", "그러자 왕이 말했다. \"이보게, 그대는 마침 알맞은 곳에 왔도다."], ["Give me a draught from your cup, and your companion shall ride upon Bayard.”", "오늘 여기서 바야르를 탈 것이니 말이다. 그대의 잔에서 내게 한 모금 주면, 그대의 동행이 바야르에 오르게 해 주겠다.\""], ["Malagigi, hearing these words, said, “Be it so.”", "말라지지는 이 말을 듣고 말했다. \"그리하겠습니다.\""], ["Then the king, with great devotion, took a spoon, and dipped a portion from the pilgrim’s cup, believing that his sins should be thereby forgiven.", "그러자 왕은 큰 경건함으로 숟가락을 들어 순례자의 잔에서 한 술을 떠내었으니, 그로써 자기 죄가 사함받으리라 믿었던 것이다."]], [["When this was done, the king said to Charlot, “Son, I request that you will let this sick pilgrim sit on your horse, and ride if he can, for by so doing he will be healed of all his infirmities.”", "이 일이 끝나자 왕이 샤를로에게 말했다. \"아들아, 청컨대 이 병든 순례자를 네 말에 앉혀, 할 수 있거든 타게 해 주어라."], ["Charlot replied, “That will I gladly do.”", "그리하면 그가 자기 모든 병약함에서 나을 것이니라.\" 샤를로가 대답했다."], ["So saying, he dismounted, and the servants took the pilgrim in their arms, and helped him on the horse.", "\"기꺼이 그리하겠습니다.\" 그렇게 말하며 그가 말에서 내리자, 하인들이 순례자를 팔로 받쳐 말에 오르도록 거들었다."]], [["When Rinaldo was mounted, he put his feet in the stirrups, and said, “I would like to ride a little.”", "리날도는 말에 오르자 두 발을 등자에 걸고 말했다. \"조금 타 보고 싶구나.\""], ["Malagigi, hearing him speak, seemed delighted, and asked him whether he could see and hear also.", "말라지지는 그가 말하는 것을 듣고 기뻐하는 듯하더니, 그에게 보고 듣는 것도 되느냐고 물었다. \"그렇소.\""], ["“Yes,”", "리날도가 말했다."], ["said Rinaldo, “I am healed of all my infirmities.”", "\"나는 내 모든 병약함에서 나았소.\" 왕이 그 말을 듣고 투르팽 주교에게 말했다."], ["When the king heard it he said to Bishop Turpin, “My lord bishop, we must celebrate this with a procession, with crosses and banners, for it is a great miracle.”", "\"주교 경, 우리는 이를 십자가와 깃발을 앞세운 행렬로 기념해야 하오. 이는 큰 기적이니 말이오.\""]], [["When Rinaldo remarked that he was not carefully watched, he spoke to the horse, and touched him with the spurs.", "리날도는 자기가 면밀히 감시당하지 않는 것을 알아채고는, 말에게 말을 걸며 박차로 그를 건드렸다."], ["Bayard knew that his master was upon him, and he started off upon a rapid pace, and in a few moments was a good way off.", "바야르는 자기 주인이 자기 위에 탄 것을 알고 빠른 걸음으로 내달리기 시작하여, 순식간에 멀찍이 가 버렸다. 말라지지는 크게 놀란 척했다."], ["Malagigi pretended to be in great alarm.", "\"오 고귀하신 왕이자 주군이시여.\""], ["“O noble king and master,”", "그가 외쳤다."], ["he cried, “my poor companion is run away with; he will fall and break his neck.”", "\"제 가엾은 동행이 말에 끌려가고 있습니다. 그가 떨어져 목이 부러질 것입니다.\""], ["The king ordered his knights to ride after the pilgrim, and bring him back, or help him if need were.", "왕은 기사들에게 명하여 순례자를 뒤쫓아 데려오거나, 필요하다면 그를 도우라 하였다."], ["They did so, but it was in vain.", "그들은 그리했으나 헛일이었다."], ["Rinaldo left them all behind him, and kept on his way till he reached Montalban.", "리날도는 그들 모두를 뒤로 따돌리고 줄곧 길을 달려 몽탈방에 다다랐다."], ["Malagigi was suffered to depart, unsuspected, and he went his way, making sad lamentation for the fate of his comrade, who he pretended to think must surely be dashed to pieces.", "말라지지는 의심받지 않고 떠나도록 허락되어 제 갈 길을 갔으니, 자기 동료의 운명을 두고 슬피 한탄하며, 그가 틀림없이 산산조각 났으리라 여기는 척했다."]], [["Malagigi did not go far, but having changed his disguise, returned to where the king was, and employed his best art in getting the brothers of Rinaldo out of prison.", "말라지지는 멀리 가지 않고, 변장을 바꾸어 왕이 있는 곳으로 돌아가, 리날도의 형제들을 옥에서 빼내는 데 자기 최고의 재주를 부렸다."], ["He succeeded; and all three got safely to Montalban, where Rinaldo’s joy at the rescue of his brothers and the recovery of Bayard was more than tongue can tell.", "그는 성공했고, 셋 모두 무사히 몽탈방에 이르렀으니, 형제들을 구하고 바야르를 되찾은 데 대한 리날도의 기쁨은 혀로는 다 이를 수 없을 정도였다. 리날도의 죽음"]], [["# DEATH OF RINALDO", "# DEATH OF RINALDO"]], [["————\nThe distress in Rinaldo’s castle for want of food grew more severe every day, under the pressure of the siege.", "리날도의 성에서 식량이 부족하여 겪는 곤경은 포위의 압박 아래 날이 갈수록 더욱 심해졌다."], ["The garrison were forced to kill their horses, both to save the provision they would consume, and to make food of their flesh.", "수비대는 어쩔 수 없이 자기들의 말을 잡아야 했으니, 말들이 먹어 치울 양식을 아끼는 동시에 그 고기를 식량으로 삼으려는 것이었다."], ["At last all the horses were killed except Bayard, and Rinaldo said to his brothers, “Bayard must die, for we have nothing else to eat.”", "마침내 바야르만 빼고 모든 말이 잡히고 말았고, 리날도가 형제들에게 말했다. \"바야르를 잡아야겠다, 달리 먹을 것이 없으니.\""], ["So they went to the stable and brought out Bayard to kill him.", "그리하여 그들은 마구간으로 가서 바야르를 잡으려고 끌고 나왔다. 그러나 알라르도[Alardo]가 말했다."], ["But Alardo said, “Brother, let Bayard live a little longer; who knows what God may do for us?”", "\"형님, 바야르를 조금만 더 살려 둡시다. 하느님께서 우리에게 무슨 일을 해 주실지 누가 알겠습니까?\""]], [["Bayard heard these words, and understood them as if he was a man, and fell on his knees, as if he would beg for mercy.", "바야르는 이 말을 듣고 마치 사람인 양 알아듣고는, 자비를 구하려는 듯 무릎을 꿇었다."], ["When Rinaldo saw the distress of his horse his heart failed him, and he let him live.", "리날도가 자기 말의 곤경을 보자 마음이 약해져, 그를 살려 두었다."]], [["Just at this time Aya, Rinaldo’s mother, who was the sister of the Emperor, came to the camp, attended by knights and ladies, to intercede for her sons.", "바로 이때, 황제의 누이인 리날도의 어머니 아야[Aya]가 자기 아들들을 위해 탄원하고자 기사들과 귀부인들을 거느리고 진영에 왔다. 그녀는 왕 앞에 무릎을 꿇고, 리날도와 그 형제들을 용서해 달라 간청했다."], ["She fell on her knees before the king, and besought him that he would pardon Rinaldo and his brothers: and all the peers and knights took her side, and entreated the king to grant her prayer.", "그러자 모든 중신과 기사가 그녀 편을 들어, 왕에게 그녀의 청을 들어주기를 간곡히 빌었다. 그러자 왕이 말했다."], ["Then said the king, “Dear sister, you act the part of a good mother, and I respect your tender heart, and yield to your entreaties.", "\"사랑하는 누이여, 그대는 선한 어머니의 도리를 다하는구려. 내 그대의 다정한 마음을 존중하여 그대의 간청에 따르겠소."], ["I will spare your sons their lives if they submit implicitly to my will.”", "그대의 아들들이 내 뜻에 절대적으로 복종한다면, 내 그들의 목숨을 살려 주겠소.\""]], [["When Charlot heard this he approached the king and whispered in his ear.", "샤를로는 이 말을 듣고 왕에게 다가가 그의 귀에 대고 속삭였다. 그러자 왕이 자기 누이에게로 돌아서서 말했다."], ["And the king turned to his sister and said, “Charlot must have Bayard, because I have given the horse to him.", "\"샤를로가 바야르를 가져야 하오. 내가 그 말을 그 아이에게 주었기 때문이오."], ["Now go, my sister, and tell Rinaldo what I have said.”", "자, 가시오, 누이여, 그리고 내가 한 말을 리날도에게 전하시오.\""]], [["When the Lady Aya heard these words she was delighted, thanked God in her heart, and said, “Worthy king and brother, I will do as you bid me.”", "아야 부인은 이 말을 듣고 기뻐하며 마음속으로 하느님께 감사드리고는 말했다. \"고귀하신 왕이자 오라버니시여, 분부하신 대로 하겠습니다.\""], ["So she went into the castle, where her sons received her most joyfully and affectionately, and she told them the king’s offer.", "그리하여 그녀가 성으로 들어가니, 그녀의 아들들이 더없이 기쁘고 다정하게 그녀를 맞이했고, 그녀는 그들에게 왕의 제안을 전했다. 그러자 알라르도가 말했다."], ["Then Alardo said, “Brother, I would rather have the king’s enmity than give Bayard to Charlot, for I believe he will kill him.”", "\"형님, 저는 바야르를 샤를로에게 주느니 차라리 왕의 적의를 사겠습니다. 그가 그 말을 죽일 것이라 믿기 때문입니다.\""], ["Likewise said all the brothers.", "다른 형제들도 모두 같은 말을 했다."], ["When Rinaldo heard them he said, “Dear brothers, if we may win our forgiveness by giving up the horse, so be it.", "리날도가 그들의 말을 듣고 말했다. \"사랑하는 아우들아, 말을 내어 줌으로써 우리 용서를 얻을 수 있다면, 그리하자."], ["Let us make our peace, for we cannot stand against the king’s power.”", "우리 화친을 맺자, 우리는 왕의 힘에 맞설 수 없으니.\""], ["Then he went to his mother, and told her they would give the horse to Charlot, and more, too, if the king would pardon them, and forgive all that they had done against his crown and dignity.", "그러고는 어머니에게 가서, 왕이 그들을 용서하고 그들이 왕관과 위엄에 거슬러 행한 모든 일을 사하여 준다면, 그 말을 샤를로에게 줄 것이며 그 이상도 내어 주겠노라 전했다."], ["The lady returned to Charles and told him the answer of her sons.", "부인은 샤를에게 돌아가 아들들의 대답을 전했다."]], [["When the peace was thus made between the king and the sons of Aymon, the brothers came forth from the castle, bringing Bayard with them, and, falling at the king’s feet, begged his forgiveness.", "그리하여 왕과 에몽의 아들들 사이에 화친이 맺어지자, 형제들은 바야르를 데리고 성에서 나와 왕의 발 앞에 엎드려 용서를 빌었다."], ["The king bade them rise, and received them into favor in the sight of all his noble knights and counsellors, to the great joy of all especially of the Lady Aya, their mother.", "왕은 그들에게 일어나라 명하고는, 그의 모든 고귀한 기사와 고문관들이 보는 앞에서 그들을 다시 총애 안에 받아들이니, 모두가, 특히 그들의 어머니인 아야 부인이 크게 기뻐했다."], ["Then Rinaldo took the horse Bayard, gave him to Charlot, and said, “My lord and prince, this horse I give to you; do with him as to you seems good.”", "이윽고 리날도는 명마 바야르를 끌어다 샤를로에게 주며 말했다. \"나의 주군이자 왕자시여, 이 말을 당신께 드리오니, 좋으실 대로 처분하소서.\""], ["Charlot took him, as had been agreed on.", "샤를로는 약속된 대로 그를 받았다."], ["Then he made the servants take him to the bridge, and throw him into the water.", "그러더니 그는 하인들에게 그를 다리로 끌고 가 물속에 던지게 했다."], ["Bayard sank to the bottom, but soon came to the surface again and swam, saw Rinaldo looking at him, came to land, ran to his old master, and stood by him as proudly as if he had understanding, and would say, “Why did you treat me so?”", "바야르는 바닥까지 가라앉았으나, 곧 다시 수면으로 떠올라 헤엄쳤고, 리날도가 자기를 바라보는 것을 보고는 뭍으로 올라와 옛 주인에게 달려가, 마치 무엇을 헤아릴 줄 아는 것처럼 당당하게 그 곁에 서서, \"어찌하여 저를 이렇게 대하셨습니까?\" 하고 말하려는 듯하였다."], ["When the prince saw that he said, “Rinaldo, give me the horse again, for he must die.”", "왕자가 그것을 보고 말했다. \"리날도, 그 말을 내게 다시 다오, 그놈은 죽어야 하니까.\""], ["Rinaldo replied, “My lord and prince, he is yours without dispute,”", "리날도가 대답했다. \"나의 주군이자 왕자시여, 그는 두말할 것 없이 당신의 것입니다.\""], ["and gave him to him.", "그러고는 그를 그에게 넘겨주었다."], ["The prince then had a millstone tied to each foot, and two to his neck, and made them throw him again into the water.", "그러자 왕자는 발마다 맷돌 하나씩을, 목에는 둘을 매달게 하고는, 다시 그를 물속에 던지게 했다."], ["Bayard struggled in the water, looked up to his master, threw off the stones, and came back to Rinaldo.", "바야르는 물속에서 발버둥 치며 주인을 올려다보더니, 돌들을 떨쳐 내고 리날도에게 돌아왔다."], ["When Alardo saw that, he said, “Now must thou be disgraced forever, brother, if thou give up the horse again.”", "알라르도가 그것을 보고 말했다. \"이제 형님이 그 말을 또 내어 준다면, 형님은 영영 치욕을 안게 될 것입니다.\""], ["But Rinaldo answered, “Brother, be still.", "그러나 리날도가 대답했다. \"아우야, 가만히 있어라."], ["Shall I for the horse’s life provoke the anger of the king again?”", "내 말 한 마리의 목숨 때문에 다시 왕의 노여움을 돋우겠느냐?\""], ["Then Alardo said, “Ah, Bayard!", "그러자 알라르도가 말했다."], ["what a return do we make for all thy true love and service!”", "\"아, 바야르여! 네 그 참된 사랑과 봉사에 우리가 어찌 이런 보답을 한단 말이냐!\""], ["Rinaldo gave the horse to the prince again, and said, “My lord, if the horse comes out again I cannot return him to you any more, for it wrings my heart too much.”", "리날도는 그 말을 왕자에게 다시 주며 말했다. \"나의 주군이여, 만약 그 말이 또 나온다면 나는 더는 그를 당신께 돌려 드릴 수 없으니, 내 가슴이 너무도 미어지기 때문입니다.\""], ["Then Charlot had Bayard loaded with the stones as before, and thrown into the water; and commanded Rinaldo that he should not stand where the horse would see him.", "그러자 샤를로는 바야르에게 전처럼 돌을 매달게 하여 물속에 던지게 하고는, 리날도에게는 말이 자기를 볼 수 있는 곳에 서 있지 말라고 명했다."], ["When Bayard rose to the surface he stretched his neck out of the water and looked round for his master, but saw him not.", "바야르는 수면으로 떠오르자 물 밖으로 목을 빼고 주인을 찾아 두리번거렸으나, 그를 보지 못했다."], ["Then he sunk to the bottom.", "그러자 그는 바닥으로 가라앉았다."]], [["Rinaldo was so distressed for the loss of Bayard that he made a vow to ride no horse again all his life long, nor to bind a sword to his side, but to become a hermit.", "리날도는 바야르를 잃은 것이 어찌나 비통했던지, 평생토록 다시는 말을 타지 않고, 옆구리에 검도 차지 않으며, 은수자가 되겠노라 맹세했다."], ["He resolved to betake himself to some wild wood, but first to return to his castle, to see his children, and to appoint to each his share of his estate.", "그는 어느 황량한 숲으로 들어가기로 결심했으나, 먼저 자기 성으로 돌아가 자식들을 보고 각자에게 자기 영지의 몫을 정해 주기로 했다."]], [["So he took leave of the king and of his brothers, and returned to Montalban, and his brothers remained with the king.", "그리하여 그는 왕과 형제들에게 작별을 고하고 몽탈방으로 돌아갔으며, 그의 형제들은 왕 곁에 남았다."], ["Rinaldo called his children to him, and he made his eldest born, Aymeric, a knight, and made him lord of his castle and of his land.", "리날도는 자식들을 불러, 맏아들 에므리[Aymeric]를 기사로 삼고 그를 자기 성과 영지의 영주로 세웠다."], ["He gave to the rest what other goods he had, and kissed and embraced them all, commended them to God, and then departed from them with a heavy heart.", "그는 나머지에게는 자기에게 있던 다른 재산을 나누어 주고, 그들 모두에게 입 맞추고 끌어안고는, 그들을 하느님께 의탁하고 무거운 마음으로 그들 곁을 떠났다."]], [["He had not travelled far when he entered a wood, and there met with a hermit, who had long been retired from the world.", "그가 멀리 가기 전에 한 숲으로 들어섰는데, 거기서 오래도록 세상을 등지고 지내 온 한 은수자를 만났다."], ["Rinaldo greeted him, and the hermit replied courteously, and asked him who he was and what was his purpose.", "리날도가 그에게 인사하니, 은수자가 정중히 답하며 그가 누구이며 무슨 뜻을 품었는지 물었다. 리날도가 대답했다."], ["Rinaldo replied, “Sir, I have led a sinful life; many deeds of violence have I done, and many men have I slain, not always in a good cause, but often under the impulse of my own headstrong passions.", "\"수사님, 저는 죄 많은 삶을 살았습니다. 많은 폭력을 저질렀고, 많은 이를 죽였는데, 늘 옳은 명분에서 그런 것이 아니라 흔히 제 고집스러운 격정에 떠밀려 그리하였습니다."], ["I have also been the cause of the death of many of my friends, who took my part, not because they thought me in the right, but only for love of me.", "또한 제 편을 들어 준 많은 벗의 죽음의 원인이 되었으니, 그들은 제가 옳다고 여겨서가 아니라 오직 저를 사랑하여 제 편을 들었던 것입니다. 이제 저는 제 모든 죄를 고백하고, 혹여 하느님의 자비가 저를 용서해 주신다면, 남은 평생을 속죄하며 살고자 왔습니다.\""], ["And now I come to make confession of all my sins, and to do penance for the rest of my life, if perhaps the mercy of God will forgive me.”", "은수자가 말했다. \"벗이여, 그대가 큰 죄에 빠져 하느님의 계명을 어겼음을 알겠소."], ["The hermit said, “Friend, I perceive you have fallen into great sins, and have broken the commandments of God, but his mercy is greater than your sins; and if you repent from your heart, and lead a new life, there is yet hope for you that he will forgive you what is past.”", "그러나 그분의 자비는 그대의 죄보다 크니, 그대가 진심으로 뉘우치고 새 삶을 산다면, 그분이 지난 일을 용서해 주시리라는 희망이 아직 그대에게 있소.\" 그리하여 리날도는 위로를 얻고 말했다."], ["So Rinaldo was comforted, and said, “Master, I will stay with you, and what you bid me I will do.”", "\"스승님, 제가 스승님과 함께 머물며, 분부하시는 대로 하겠습니다.\" 은수자가 대답했다."], ["The hermit replied, “Roots and vegetables will be your food; shirt or shoes you may not wear; your lot must be poverty and want if you stay with me.”", "\"뿌리와 풀이 그대의 음식이 될 것이오. 셔츠나 신발은 걸쳐서는 안 되오."], ["Rinaldo replied, “I will cheerfully bear all this, and more.”", "그대가 나와 함께 머문다면 그대의 몫은 가난과 결핍일 것이오.\" 리날도가 대답했다."], ["So he remained three whole years with the hermit, and after that his strength failed, and it seemed as if he was like to die.", "\"그 모든 것을, 아니 그 이상도 기꺼이 견디겠습니다.\" 그리하여 그는 은수자와 함께 꼬박 삼 년을 지냈는데, 그 뒤로 그의 기력이 쇠하여 마치 죽을 것만 같아 보였다."]], [["One night the hermit had a dream, and heard a voice from heaven, which commanded him to say to his companion that he must without delay go to the Holy Land, and fight against the heathen.", "어느 날 밤 은수자가 꿈을 꾸었는데, 하늘에서 들려오는 목소리가 그에게, 그의 동행이 지체 없이 성지로 가서 이교도에 맞서 싸워야 한다고 그에게 이르라 명하였다."], ["The hermit, when he heard that voice, was glad, and calling Rinaldo, he said, “Friend, God’s angel has commanded me to say to you that you must without delay go to Jerusalem, and help our fellow-Christians in their struggle with the Infidels.”", "은수자는 그 목소리를 듣고 기뻐하며 리날도를 불러 말했다. \"벗이여, 하느님의 천사가 내게 명하여 그대에게 이르라 하셨으니, 그대는 지체 없이 예루살렘으로 가서, 이교도와의 싸움에서 우리 동료 그리스도교도를 도와야 하오.\""], ["Then said Rinaldo, “Ah!", "그러자 리날도가 말했다."], ["master, how can I do that?", "\"아! 스승님, 제가 어찌 그리할 수 있겠습니까?"], ["It is over three years since I made a vow no more to ride a horse, nor take a sword or spear in my hand.”", "다시는 말을 타지 않고 손에 검이나 창을 들지 않겠노라 맹세한 지 삼 년이 넘었습니다.\" 은수자가 대답했다."], ["The hermit answered, “Dear friend, obey God, and do what the angel commanded.”", "\"사랑하는 벗이여, 하느님께 순종하여, 천사가 명한 대로 하시오.\" \"그리하겠습니다.\""], ["“I will do so,”", "리날도가 말했다."], ["said Rinaldo, “and pray for me, my master, that God may guide me right.”", "\"스승님, 저를 위해 기도해 주십시오, 하느님께서 저를 바른길로 인도하시도록.\""], ["Then he departed, and went to the seaside, and took ship and came to Tripoli in Syria.", "그러고서 그는 떠나, 바닷가로 가서 배를 타고 시리아의 트리폴리에 이르렀다."]], [["And as he went on his way his strength returned to him, till it was equal to what it was in his best days.", "그가 길을 가는 동안 기력이 그에게 돌아와, 마침내 그의 전성기 때와 맞먹게 되었다."], ["And though he never mounted a horse, nor took a sword in his hand, yet with his pilgrim’s staff he did good service in the armies of the Christians; and it pleased God that he escaped unhurt, though he was present in many battles, and his courage inspired the men with the same.", "그리고 그는 결코 말에 오르지도, 손에 검을 들지도 않았으나, 순례자의 지팡이로 그리스도교도들의 군대에서 훌륭한 공을 세웠다. 하느님께서 기뻐하시어 그는 비록 숱한 전투에 임했으되 다치지 않고 화를 면했으며, 그의 용기는 병사들에게도 같은 용기를 불어넣었다."], ["At last a truce was made with the Saracens, and Rinaldo, now old and infirm, wishing to see his native land again before he died, took ship and sailed for France.", "마침내 사라센인들과 휴전이 맺어지자, 이제 늙고 쇠약해진 리날도는 죽기 전에 다시금 고국 땅을 보고자 하여, 배를 타고 프랑스로 항해해 갔다."], ["When he arrived he shunned to go to the resorts of the great, and preferred to live among the humble folk, where he was unknown.", "그는 도착하자 권세 있는 자들의 거처를 피하고, 자기가 알려지지 않은 미천한 사람들 틈에서 사는 편을 택했다."], ["He did country work, and lived on milk and bread, drank water, and was therewith content.", "그는 시골 일을 하고, 우유와 빵으로 연명하며, 물을 마시고, 그것으로 만족했다."], ["While he so lived he heard that the city of Cologne was the holiest and best of cities, on account of the relics and bodies of saints who had there poured out their blood for the faith.", "그가 그렇게 살던 중, 쾰른이라는 도시가 그곳에서 신앙을 위해 피를 쏟은 성인들의 유물과 유해 덕분에 가장 거룩하고 으뜸가는 도시라는 말을 들었다."], ["This induced him to betake himself thither.", "이것이 그로 하여금 그곳으로 가도록 이끌었다."], ["When the pious hero arrived at Cologne he went to the monastery of St. Peter, and lived a holy life, occupied night and day in devotion.", "이 경건한 영웅은 쾰른에 도착하자 성 베드로 수도원으로 가서, 밤낮으로 기도에 전념하며 거룩한 삶을 살았다."], ["It so happened that at that time in the next town to Cologne there raged a dreadful pestilence.", "마침 그때 쾰른에 인접한 마을에 무서운 역병이 창궐했다."], ["Many people came to Rinaldo, to beg him to pray for them, that the plague might be stayed.", "많은 사람이 리날도에게 와서, 역병이 멎도록 자기들을 위해 기도해 달라 간청했다."], ["The holy man prayed fervently, and besought the Lord to take away the plague from the people, and his prayer was heard.", "거룩한 사람은 간절히 기도하여, 주님께 그 백성에게서 역병을 거두어 달라 빌었고, 그의 기도가 응답받았다."], ["The stroke of the pestilence was arrested, and all the people thanked the holy man and praised God.", "역병의 타격이 멎으니, 모든 사람이 거룩한 사람에게 감사하고 하느님을 찬양했다."]], [["Now there was at this time at Cologne a bishop, called Agilolphus, who was a wise and understanding man, who led a pure and secluded life, and set a good example to others. This bishop undertook to build the Church of St.", "그런데 이 무렵 쾰른에는 아길롤푸스[Agilolphus]라는 주교가 있었으니, 그는 지혜롭고 분별 있는 사람으로, 정결하고 은둔하는 삶을 살며 남들에게 좋은 본을 보였다."], ["Peter, and gave notice to all stonemasons and other workmen round about to come to Cologne, where they should find work and wages.", "이 주교가 성 베드로 성당을 짓는 일을 맡아, 인근의 모든 석공과 다른 일꾼들에게 쾰른으로 오면 일감과 품삯을 얻으리라 알렸다."], ["Among others came Rinaldo; and he worked among the laborers and did more than four or five common workmen.", "다른 이들 가운데 리날도도 왔는데, 그는 일꾼들 틈에서 일하며 보통 일꾼 네댓 명 몫 이상을 해냈다."], ["When they went to dinner he brought stone and mortar so that they had enough for the whole day.", "다른 이들이 저녁을 먹으러 갈 때 그는 돌과 회반죽을 날라, 그들이 하루 종일 쓸 만큼을 마련해 놓았다."], ["When the others went to bed he stretched himself out on the stones.", "다른 이들이 잠자리에 들 때 그는 돌 위에 몸을 뉘었다."], ["He ate bread only, and drank nothing but water; and had for his wages but a penny a day.", "그는 빵만 먹고 물만 마셨으며, 품삯으로는 하루에 한 푼만 받았다."], ["The head workman asked him his name, and where he belonged.", "십장이 그에게 이름이 무엇이며 어디 출신이냐고 물었다."], ["He would not tell, but said nothing and pursued his work.", "그는 말하려 하지 않고, 아무 말 없이 자기 일에만 매달렸다."], ["They called him St. Peter’s workman, because he was so devoted to his work.", "사람들은 그를 성 베드로의 일꾼이라 불렀으니, 그가 그토록 일에 헌신했기 때문이다."]], [["When the overseer saw the diligence of this holy man he chid the laziness of the other workmen, and said, “You receive more pay than this good man, but do not do half as much work.”", "감독관이 이 거룩한 사람의 부지런함을 보고는 다른 일꾼들의 게으름을 꾸짖으며 말했다. \"너희는 이 선한 사람보다 더 많은 품삯을 받으면서도, 그의 절반만큼도 일하지 않는구나.\""], ["For this reason the other workmen hated Rinaldo, and made a secret agreement to kill him.", "이런 까닭에 다른 일꾼들은 리날도를 미워하여, 그를 죽이기로 은밀히 모의했다."], ["They knew that he made it a practice to go every night to a certain church to pray and give alms.", "그들은 그가 밤마다 어느 성당에 가서 기도하고 적선하는 것을 관례로 삼고 있음을 알았다."], ["So they agreed to lay wait for him, with the purpose to kill him.", "그리하여 그들은 그를 죽일 작정으로 매복하여 기다리기로 했다."], ["When he came to the spot, they seized him, and beat him over the head till he was dead.", "그가 그 자리에 이르자 그들은 그를 붙잡아 머리를 후려쳐 죽였다."], ["Then they put his body into a sack, and stones with it, and cast it into the Rhine, in the hope the sack would sink to the bottom, and be there concealed.", "그러고는 그의 시신을 돌과 함께 자루에 넣어, 자루가 바닥으로 가라앉아 거기 감추어지기를 바라며 라인강에 던졌다."], ["But God willed not that it should be so, but caused the sack to float on the surface, and be thrown upon the bank.", "그러나 하느님께서는 그렇게 되기를 바라지 않으시어, 그 자루가 수면에 떠올라 강둑으로 밀려 올라오게 하셨다."], ["And the soul of the holy martyr was carried by angels, with songs of praise, up to the heavens.", "그리고 거룩한 순교자의 영혼은 천사들에 의해 찬미의 노래와 함께 하늘로 인도되었다."]], [["Now at that time the people of Dortmund had become converted to the Christian faith; and they sent to the Bishop of Cologne, and desired him to give them some of the holy relics that are in such abundance in that city.", "그런데 그 무렵 도르트문트 사람들이 그리스도교 신앙으로 개종해 있었다. 그래서 그들은 쾰른의 주교에게 사람을 보내, 그 도시에 그토록 풍부히 있는 거룩한 유물 가운데 얼마를 자기들에게 달라 청했다."], ["So the Bishop called together his clergy to deliberate what answer they should give to this request.", "그리하여 주교는 자기 성직자들을 불러 모아 이 청에 어떤 답을 줄지 의논했다."], ["And it was determined to give to the people of Dortmund the body of the holy man who had just suffered martyrdom.", "그리고 방금 순교를 당한 그 거룩한 사람의 유해를 도르트문트 사람들에게 주기로 결정되었다."]], [["When now the body with the coffin was put on the cart, the cart began to move toward Dortmund without horses or help of men, and stopped not till it reached the place where the church of St. Rinaldo now stands.", "이제 그 유해를 관과 함께 수레에 실으니, 수레가 말도 사람의 도움도 없이 도르트문트를 향해 움직이기 시작하여, 오늘날 성 리날도 성당이 서 있는 자리에 이르기까지 멈추지 않았다."], ["The Bishop and his clergy followed the holy man to do him honor, with singing of hymns, for a space of three miles. And St.", "주교와 그의 성직자들은 그 거룩한 사람에게 예를 표하고자 찬가를 부르며 삼 마일에 걸쳐 그를 따랐다."], ["Rinaldo has ever since been the patron of that place, and many wonderful works has God done through him, as may be seen in the legends. ————", "그리고 성 리날도는 그 이후로 그곳의 수호성인이 되었으며, 전설에 나와 있듯이 하느님께서 그를 통해 많은 놀라운 일을 행하셨다."]], [["# HUON OF BORDEAUX", "# HUON OF BORDEAUX"]], [["When Charlemagne grew old he felt the burden of government become heavier year by year, till at last he called together his high barons and peers to propose to abdicate the empire and the throne of France in favor of his sons, Charlot and Lewis.", "보르도의 위옹\n샤를마뉴는 나이가 들어 가면서 통치의 짐이 해마다 더 무거워짐을 느끼다가, 마침내 자기의 고위 제후들과 중신들을 불러 모아, 제국과 프랑스의 왕좌를 자기 아들들인 샤를로와 루이[Lewis]에게 넘기고 양위할 뜻을 제안했다."]], [["The Emperor was unreasonably partial to his eldest son; he would have been glad to have had the barons and peers demand Charlot for their only sovereign; but that prince was so infamous, for his falsehood and cruelty, that the council strenuously opposed the Emperor’s proposal of abdicating, and implored him to continue to hold a sceptre which he wielded with so much glory.", "황제는 자기 맏아들에게 분별없이 편애를 품고 있었다. 그는 제후들과 중신들이 샤를로를 유일한 군주로 청해 주기를 바랐을 것이다. 그러나 그 왕자는 거짓됨과 잔인함으로 워낙 악명이 높았던 탓에, 회의는 황제의 양위 제안에 한사코 반대하며, 그토록 영광스럽게 휘둘러 온 왕홀을 계속 쥐고 있어 주기를 간청했다."]], [["Amaury of Hauteville, cousin of Ganelon, and now head of the wicked branch of the house of Maganza, was the secret partisan of Charlot, whom he resembled in his loose morals and bad dispositions.", "가늘롱[Ganelon]의 사촌이며 이제 마간차[Maganza] 가문의 사악한 분파의 우두머리가 된 오트빌의 아모리[Amaury]는 샤를로의 은밀한 추종자였으니, 그는 방탕한 품행과 못된 성정에서 샤를로를 빼닮은 자였다."], ["Amaury nourished the most bitter resentment against the house of Guienne, of which the former Duke, Sevinus, had often rebuked his misdeeds.", "아모리는 기엔[Guienne] 가문에 대해 더없이 쓰라린 원한을 품고 있었으니, 그 가문의 선대 공작 세비누스[Sevinus]가 종종 그의 악행을 꾸짖었기 때문이다."], ["He took advantage of this occasion to do an injury to the two young children whom the Duke Sevinus had left under the charge of the Duchess Alice, their mother; and at the same time, to advance his interest with Charlot by increasing his wealth and power.", "그는 이 기회를 틈타, 세비누스 공작이 그 어머니인 알리스[Alice] 공작부인의 보살핌 아래 남겨 둔 두 어린 자식에게 해를 끼치는 동시에, 샤를로의 부와 권력을 키워 줌으로써 그와의 사이에서 자기 잇속을 챙기려 했다."], ["With this view he suggested to the prince a new idea.", "이런 속셈으로 그는 왕자에게 새로운 생각 하나를 넌지시 일러 주었다."]], [["He pretended to agree with the opinion of the barons; he said that it would be best to try Charlot’s capacity for government by giving him some rich provinces before placing him upon the throne; and that the Emperor, without depriving himself of any part of his realm, might give Charlot the investiture of Guienne.", "그는 제후들의 의견에 동의하는 척했다. 그는 샤를로를 왕좌에 앉히기에 앞서 그에게 부유한 속주 몇을 주어 통치 능력을 시험해 보는 것이 최선이라고, 또한 황제께서는 자기 영토의 어느 부분도 잃지 않고서 샤를로에게 기엔의 봉토 수여권을 줄 수 있다고 말했다."], ["For although seven years had passed since the death of Sevinus, the young Duke, his son, had not yet repaired to the court of Charlemagne to render the homage due to his lawful sovereign.", "세비누스가 죽은 지 칠 년이 지났건만, 그 아들인 젊은 공작이 아직 자기의 정당한 군주에게 마땅히 바쳐야 할 신종 서약을 하러 샤를마뉴의 궁정에 오지 않았기 때문이다."]], [["We have often had occasion to admire the justice and wisdom of the advice which on all occasions the Duke Namo of Bavaria gave to Charlemagne, and he now discountenanced, with indignation, the selfish advice of Amaury.", "우리는 어느 경우에나 바이에른의 나모[Namo] 공작이 샤를마뉴에게 베푼 조언의 정의로움과 지혜로움에 자주 감탄할 기회를 가져 왔거니와, 그는 이번에도 아모리의 이기적인 조언을 분개하여 물리쳤다."], ["He represented to the Emperor the early age of the children of Sevinus, and the useful and glorious services of their late father, and proposed to Charlemagne to send two knights to the Duchess at Bordeaux, to summon her two sons to the court of the Emperor, to pay their respects and render homage.", "그는 황제에게 세비누스 자식들의 어린 나이와 그들의 작고한 아버지가 베푼 유익하고 영광스러운 공로들을 일깨우고, 샤를마뉴에게 기사 둘을 보르도의 공작부인에게 보내, 그녀의 두 아들을 황제의 궁정으로 불러들여 인사를 올리고 신종 서약을 하게 하자고 제안했다."]], [["Charlemagne approved this advice, and sent two chevaliers to demand the two young princes of their mother.", "샤를마뉴는 이 조언을 옳게 여겨, 두 기사를 보내 그 두 어린 왕자를 그 어머니에게 요청하게 했다."], ["No sooner had the Duchess learned the approach of the two knights, than she sent distinguished persons to receive them; and as soon as they entered the palace she presented herself before them, with her elder and younger sons, Huon and Girard.", "공작부인은 두 기사가 다가온다는 소식을 듣자마자, 지체 높은 사람들을 보내 그들을 맞이하게 했다. 그리고 그들이 궁에 들어서자마자 그녀는 자기의 맏아들과 어린 아들, 위옹[Huon]과 지라르[Girard]를 데리고 그들 앞에 모습을 드러냈다."], ["The deputies, delighted with the honors and caresses they received, accompanied with rich presents, left Bordeaux with regret and on their return represented to Charlemagne that the young Duke Huon seemed born to tread in the footsteps of his brave father, informing him that in three months the young princes of Guienne would present themselves at his court.", "사절들은 받은 영예와 후대에, 그리고 곁들여진 값진 선물들에 기뻐하며 아쉬운 마음으로 보르도를 떠났다. 그들은 돌아와 샤를마뉴에게, 젊은 공작 위옹이 그 용맹한 아버지의 발자취를 따르도록 태어난 듯하다고 아뢰며, 석 달 안에 기엔의 젊은 왕자들이 그의 궁정에 모습을 드러내리라 알렸다."]], [["The Duchess employed the short interval in giving her sons her last instructions.", "공작부인은 그 짧은 사이에 아들들에게 마지막 가르침을 주었다."], ["Huon received them in his heart, and Girard gave as much heed to them as could be expected from one so young.", "위옹은 그것을 가슴에 새겨 받아들였고, 지라르는 그토록 어린 아이에게 기대할 수 있는 만큼은 거기에 귀를 기울였다."]], [["The preparations for their departure having been made, the Duchess embraced them tenderly, commending them to the care of Heaven, and charged them to call, on their way, at the celebrated monastery of Cluny, to visit the Abbot, the brother of their father.", "그들의 출발 준비가 갖추어지자, 공작부인은 그들을 다정히 끌어안고 하늘의 보살핌에 의탁하며, 가는 길에 그 유명한 클뤼니[Cluny] 수도원에 들러 그들 아버지의 형제인 수도원장을 찾아뵙도록 당부했다."], ["This Abbot, worthy of his high dignity, had never lost an opportunity of doing good, setting an example of every excellence, and making virtue attractive by his example.", "그 높은 지위에 걸맞은 이 수도원장은 선행을 베풀 기회를 결코 놓치지 않았으며, 온갖 미덕의 본을 보이고, 자기 본보기로써 덕을 매력 있는 것으로 만든 사람이었다."]], [["He received his nephews with the greatest magnificence; and, aware how useful his presence might be to them with Charlemagne, whose valued counsellor he was, he took with them the road to Paris.", "그는 더없는 성대함으로 조카들을 맞이했다. 그리고 자기의 임석이 샤를마뉴와의 사이에서 그들에게 얼마나 유익할지 잘 알고 있었으니 — 그는 샤를마뉴가 소중히 여기는 고문관이었던 것이다 — 그들과 함께 파리로 가는 길에 올랐다."]], [["When Amaury learned what reception the two deputies of Charlemagne had received at Bordeaux, and the arrangements made for the visit of the young princes to the Emperor’s court, he suggested to Charlot to give him a troop of his guards, with which he proposed to lay wait for the young men in the wood of Montlery, put them to death, and thereby give the prince Charlot possession of the duchy of Guienne.", "아모리는 샤를마뉴의 두 사절이 보르도에서 받은 환대와, 젊은 왕자들이 황제의 궁정을 방문하기 위해 마련된 준비를 알게 되자, 샤를로에게 그의 호위병 한 무리를 자기에게 달라고 넌지시 청했으니, 그것으로 몽틀레리[Montlery] 숲에서 그 젊은이들을 매복하여 기다렸다가 죽임으로써, 그로써 왕자 샤를로에게 기엔 공작령을 차지하게 해 줄 작정이었다."]], [["A plan of treachery and violence agreed but too well with Charlot’s disposition.", "배신과 폭력의 계략은 샤를로의 성정에 너무도 잘 들어맞았다."], ["He not only adopted the suggestion of Amaury, but insisted upon taking a part in it.", "그는 아모리의 제안을 받아들였을 뿐만 아니라, 거기에 한몫 끼겠다고 고집했다."], ["They went out secretly, by night, followed by a great number of attendants, all armed in black, to lie in ambuscade in the wood where the brothers were to pass.", "그들은 밤을 틈타 은밀히 나갔으니, 모두 검은 갑옷으로 무장한 수많은 수행원을 거느리고, 형제들이 지날 그 숲에 매복하려는 것이었다."]], [["Girard, the younger of the two, having amused himself as he rode by flying his hawk at such game as presented itself, had ridden in advance of his brother and the Abbot of Cluny.", "둘 가운데 동생인 지라르는 말을 타고 가면서 눈앞에 나타나는 사냥감에 자기 매를 날려 보내며 즐기다가, 형과 클뤼니 수도원장보다 앞서 말을 달려 나가 있었다."], ["Charlot, who saw him coming, alone and unarmed, went forth to meet him, sought a quarrel with him, and threw him from his horse with a stroke of his lance.", "샤를로는 그가 홀로 무장도 없이 오는 것을 보고는, 그를 맞으러 나가 그에게 시비를 걸더니, 창 일격으로 그를 말에서 떨어뜨렸다."], ["Girard uttered a cry as he fell; Huon heard it, and flew to his defence, with no other weapon than his sword.", "지라르는 떨어지면서 비명을 질렀고, 위옹이 그것을 듣고 검 외에는 다른 무기도 없이 그를 지키려고 날아갔다."], ["He came up with him, and saw the blood flowing from his wound.", "그가 동생에게 다다라 보니, 그 상처에서 피가 흐르고 있었다."], ["“What has this child done to you, wretch!”", "\"이 아이가 네게 무슨 짓을 했단 말이냐, 이 못된 놈!\""], ["he exclaimed to Charlot.", "그가 샤를로에게 외쳤다."], ["“How cowardly to attack him when unprepared to defend himself!”", "\"스스로를 지킬 채비도 안 된 아이를 치다니 어찌 그리 비겁한가!\" \"내 명예를 걸고.\""], ["“By my faith,”", "샤를로가 말했다."], ["said Charlot, “I mean to do the same by you.", "\"내 너에게도 똑같이 해 줄 작정이다."], ["Know that I am the son of Duke Thierry of Ardennes, from whom your father, Sevinus, took three castles; I have sworn to avenge him, and I defy you.”", "알아 두어라, 나는 아르덴의 티에리[Thierry] 공작의 아들이며, 네 아비 세비누스가 그분에게서 성 셋을 빼앗았다. 나는 그분의 원수를 갚겠노라 맹세했으니, 너에게 도전한다.\""], ["“Coward,”", "\"비겁한 놈.\""], ["answered Huon, “I know well the baseness that dwells in your race; worthy son of Thierry, use the advantage that your armor gives you; but know that I fear you not.”", "위옹이 대답했다. \"나는 네 혈통에 깃든 비열함을 잘 안다."], ["At these words Charlot had the wickedness to put his lance in rest, and to run upon Huon, who had barely time to wrap his arm in his mantle.", "티에리의 아들답게, 네 갑옷이 주는 이점을 마음껏 써라. 그러나 알아 두어라, 나는 너를 두려워하지 않는다.\""], ["With this feeble buckler he received the thrust of the lance.", "이 말에 샤를로는 사악하게도 창을 겨누어 위옹에게 달려들었고, 위옹은 가까스로 자기 팔을 망토로 감쌀 틈밖에 없었다."], ["It penetrated the mantle, but missed his body.", "이 보잘것없는 방패로 그는 창의 일격을 받아 냈다."], ["Then, rising upon his stirrups, Sir Huon struck Charlot so terrible a blow with his sword that the helmet was cleft asunder, and his head too.", "창은 망토를 꿰뚫었으나 그의 몸은 빗나갔다. 그러자 위옹 경은 등자를 딛고 일어서며 샤를로에게 검으로 어찌나 무서운 일격을 가했던지, 투구가 두 동강으로 갈라지고 머리도 함께 쪼개졌다."], ["The dastardly prince fell dead upon the ground.", "그 비겁한 왕자는 땅바닥에 쓰러져 죽었다."]], [["Huon now perceived that the wood was full of armed men.", "이제 위옹은 그 숲이 무장한 자들로 가득함을 알아챘다."], ["He called the men of his suite, and they hastily put themselves in order, but nobody issued from the wood to attack him.", "그는 자기 수행원들을 불렀고, 그들은 황급히 대열을 갖추었으나, 숲에서 그를 치러 나오는 자는 아무도 없었다."], ["Amaury, who saw Charlot’s fall, had no desire to compromit himself; and, feeling sure that Charlemagne would avenge the death of his son, he saw no occasion for his doing anything more at present.", "샤를로가 쓰러지는 것을 본 아모리는 자기 몸을 위태롭게 할 마음이 없었다. 그리고 샤를마뉴가 자기 아들의 죽음에 복수하리라 확신하였기에, 지금 자기가 더 무엇을 해야 할 까닭이 없다고 보았다."], ["He left Huon and the Abbot of Cluny to bind up the wound of Girard, and, having seen them depart and resume their way to Paris, he took up the body of Charlot, and, placing it across a horse, had it carried to Paris, where he arrived four hours after Huon.", "그는 위옹과 클뤼니 수도원장이 지라르의 상처를 싸매도록 내버려 두었고, 그들이 떠나 파리로 가는 길을 다시 잡는 것을 본 뒤, 샤를로의 시신을 거두어 말에 가로질러 얹고는 파리로 운반하게 하여, 위옹보다 네 시간 뒤에 그곳에 도착했다."]], [["The Abbot of Cluny presented his nephew to Charlemagne, but Huon refrained from paying his obeisance, complaining grievously of the ambush which had been set for him, which he said could not have been without the Emperor’s permission.", "클뤼니 수도원장은 자기 조카를 샤를마뉴에게 알현시켰으나, 위옹은 경의를 표하기를 삼가며, 자기에게 놓였던 매복에 대해 비통하게 호소했으니, 그것이 황제의 허락 없이는 있을 수 없는 일이라고 말했다."], ["Charlemagne, surprised at a charge which his magnanimous soul was incapable of meriting, asked eagerly of the Abbot what were the grounds of the complaints of his nephew.", "샤를마뉴는 그 너그러운 영혼으로는 결코 받을 까닭이 없는 비난에 놀라, 자기 조카가 호소하는 바의 근거가 무엇이냐고 수도원장에게 다급히 물었다."], ["The Abbot told him faithfully all that had happened, informing him that a coward knight, who called himself the son of Thierry of Ardennes, had wounded Girard, and run upon Huon, who was unarmed; but by his force and valor he had overcome the traitor, and left him dead upon the plain.", "수도원장은 일어난 모든 일을 그에게 사실대로 고하며, 아르덴의 티에리의 아들이라 자칭하는 비겁한 기사가 지라르에게 상처를 입히고 무장도 없는 위옹에게 달려들었으나, 위옹이 그 힘과 용맹으로 그 배신자를 제압하여 들판에 죽어 쓰러뜨려 두었노라 아뢰었다."]], [["Charlemagne indignantly disavowed any connection with the action of the infamous Thierry, congratulated the young Duke upon his victory, himself conducted the two brothers to a rich apartment, stayed to see the first dressing applied to the wound of Girard, and left the brothers in charge of Duke Namo of Bavaria, who, having been a companion in arms of the Duke Sevinus, regarded the young men almost as if they were his own sons.", "샤를마뉴는 그 파렴치한 티에리의 행위와 자기는 아무 관련이 없노라 분개하여 부인하고, 젊은 공작에게 그 승리를 축하한 뒤, 몸소 두 형제를 호화로운 거처로 안내하고, 지라르의 상처에 첫 치료가 베풀어지는 것을 지켜보고 머물렀으며, 바이에른의 나모 공작에게 두 형제를 맡겼으니, 나모는 세비누스 공작과 한때 전우였던지라 그 젊은이들을 거의 제 친아들처럼 여겼다."]], [["Charlemagne had hardly quitted them when, returning to his chamber, he heard cries, and saw through the window a party of armed men just arrived.", "샤를마뉴가 그들 곁을 떠나자마자, 자기 방으로 돌아가던 그는 외침 소리를 듣고, 창문 너머로 방금 도착한 한 무리의 무장한 사람들을 보았다."], ["He recognized Amaury, who bore a dead knight stretched across a horse; and the name of Charlot was heard among the exclamations of the people assembled in the courtyard.", "그는 한 죽은 기사를 말에 가로질러 실어 온 아모리를 알아보았고, 안뜰에 모인 사람들의 외침 가운데서 샤를로의 이름이 들려왔다."]], [["Charles’s partiality for this unworthy son was one of his weaknesses.", "이 못난 아들에 대한 샤를의 편애는 그의 약점 가운데 하나였다."], ["He descended in trepidation to the courtyard, ran to Amaury, and uttered a cry of grief on recognizing Charlot.", "그는 떨면서 안뜰로 내려가 아모리에게 달려가서는, 샤를로를 알아보고 비탄의 외침을 내질렀다."], ["“It is Huon of Bordeaux,”", "\"보르도의 위옹입니다.\""], ["said the traitor Amaury, “who has massacred your son before it was in my power to defend him.”", "배신자 아모리가 말했다. \"제가 미처 그를 지킬 새도 없이 그가 폐하의 아드님을 학살했습니다.\""], ["Charlemagne, furious at these words, seized a sword, and flew to the apartment of the two brothers to plunge it into the heart of the murderer of his son.", "샤를마뉴는 이 말에 격분하여 검을 쥐고는, 자기 아들을 죽인 자의 심장에 그것을 꽂으려고 두 형제의 거처로 날아갔다."], ["Duke Namo stopped his hand for an instant, while Charles told him the crime of which Huon was accused.", "나모 공작이 잠시 그의 손을 막아서는 동안, 샤를은 그에게 위옹이 받는 죄목이 무엇인지 말했다. \"그는 이 나라의 중신입니다.\""], ["“He is a peer of the realm,”", "나모가 말했다."], ["said Namo, “and if he is guilty, is he not here in your power, and are not we peers the proper judges to condemn him to death?", "\"또한 그가 죄가 있다 한들, 그가 여기 폐하의 손안에 있지 않습니까? 그리고 그를 사형에 처할 마땅한 심판관은 저희 중신들이 아닙니까?"], ["Let not your hand be stained with his blood.”", "그의 피로 폐하의 손을 더럽히지 마소서.\""], ["The Emperor, calmed by the wisdom of Duke Namo, summoned Amaury to his presence.", "황제는 나모 공작의 지혜로 마음을 가라앉히고, 아모리를 자기 앞으로 불렀다."], ["The peers assembled to hear his testimony, and the traitor accused Huon of Bordeaux of having struck the fatal blow without allowing Charlot an opportunity to defend himself, and though he knew that his opponent was the Emperor’s eldest son.", "중신들이 그의 증언을 듣고자 모였고, 배신자는 보르도의 위옹이 샤를로에게 스스로를 지킬 기회를 주지도 않고서, 그것도 자기 상대가 황제의 맏아들임을 알면서도 그 치명적인 일격을 가했노라 고발했다."]], [["The Abbot of Cluny, indignant at the false accusation of Amaury, advanced, and said, “By Saint Benedict, sire, the traitor lies in his throat.", "클뤼니 수도원장은 아모리의 거짓 고발에 분개하여 나서며 말했다. \"성 베네딕투스를 두고 맹세하건대, 폐하, 저 배신자는 목구멍으로 거짓을 토하고 있습니다."], ["If my nephew has slain Charlot it was in his own defence, and after having seen his brother wounded by him, and also in ignorance that his adversary was the prince.", "제 조카가 샤를로를 죽였다면, 그것은 자기 방어를 위함이었고, 자기 동생이 그에게 상처 입는 것을 본 뒤였으며, 또한 자기 적수가 왕자임을 모르는 채였습니다."], ["Though I am a son of the Church,”", "비록 제가 교회의 아들이오나.\""], ["added the good Abbot, “I forget not that I am a knight by birth.", "선량한 수도원장이 덧붙였다. \"저는 제가 태생으로 기사임을 잊지 않았습니다."], ["I offer to prove with my body the lie upon Amaury, if he dares sustain it, and I shall feel that I am doing a better work to punish a disloyal traitor, than to sing lauds and matins.”", "만약 아모리가 감히 그 거짓을 고집한다면, 저는 제 몸으로써 그 거짓을 그에게 입증해 보일 것을 제안하며, 찬미가와 아침기도를 부르는 것보다 불충한 배신자를 벌하는 것이 더 나은 일을 하는 것이라 여길 것입니다.\""]], [["Huon to this time had kept silent, amazed at the black calumny of Amaury; but now he stepped forth, and, addressing Amaury, said: “Traitor!", "위옹은 이때까지 아모리의 시커먼 중상에 어이가 없어 잠자코 있었으나, 이제 앞으로 나서서 아모리를 향해 말했다. \"배신자여!"], ["darest thou maintain in arms the lie thou hast uttered?”", "네가 내뱉은 거짓을 감히 무기로써 지켜 내겠느냐?\""], ["Amaury, a knight of great prowess, despising the youth and slight figure of Huon, hesitated not to offer his glove, which Huon seized; then, turning again to the peers, he said: “I pray you let the combat be allowed me, for never was there a more legitimate cause.”", "큰 무용을 지닌 기사 아모리는 위옹의 젊음과 가냘픈 체구를 업신여기며 주저 없이 자기 장갑을 내밀었고, 위옹이 그것을 움켜쥐었다. 그러고는 다시 중신들을 향해 그가 말했다."], ["The Duke Namo and the rest, deciding that the question should be remitted to the judgment of Heaven, the combat was ordained, to which Charlemagne unwillingly consented.", "\"청컨대 제게 이 결투를 허락해 주십시오, 이보다 더 정당한 명분은 일찍이 없었으니 말입니다.\" 나모 공작과 나머지 사람들이 이 문제는 하늘의 심판에 맡겨야 한다고 결정하니, 결투가 명해졌고, 샤를마뉴는 마지못해 이에 동의했다."], ["The young Duke was restored to the charge of Duke Namo, who the next morning invested him with the honors of knighthood, and gave him armor of proof, with a white shield.", "젊은 공작은 다시 나모 공작에게 맡겨졌으며, 나모는 이튿날 아침 그에게 기사의 영예를 수여하고, 시험을 거친 갑옷과 더불어 흰 방패를 주었다."], ["The Abbot of Cluny, delighted to find in his nephew sentiments worthy of his birth, embraced him, gave him his blessing, and hastened to the church of St. Germains to pray for him, while the officers of the king prepared the lists for the combat.", "클뤼니 수도원장은 조카에게서 그 태생에 걸맞은 기개를 보게 된 것을 기뻐하며 그를 끌어안고 축복을 베풀었으며, 왕의 관리들이 결투를 위한 결투장을 마련하는 동안, 그를 위해 기도하러 서둘러 생제르맹 성당으로 갔다."]], [["The battle was long and obstinate.", "싸움은 길고도 끈질겼다."], ["The address and agility of Huon enabled him to avoid the terrible blows which the ferocious Amaury aimed at him.", "위옹의 솜씨와 날렵함 덕분에 그는 사나운 아모리가 그에게 겨냥하는 무서운 일격들을 피할 수 있었다."], ["But Huon had more than once drawn blood from his antagonist.", "그러나 위옹은 여러 차례 자기 적수에게서 피를 끌어냈다."], ["The effect began to be perceived in the failing strength of the traitor; at last he threw himself from his horse, and kneeling, begged for mercy.", "그 효력이 배신자의 쇠해 가는 힘에서 드러나기 시작했고, 마침내 그는 말에서 몸을 던져 무릎을 꿇고 자비를 빌었다. \"저를 살려 주십시오.\""], ["“Spare me,”", "그가 말했다."], ["he said, “and I will confess all.", "\"그러면 모든 것을 자백하겠습니다."], ["Aid me to rise, and lead me to Charlemagne.”", "일어서도록 부축하여 저를 샤를마뉴께 데려가 주십시오.\""], ["The brave and loyal Huon, at these words, put his sword under his left arm, and stretched out his right to raise the prostrate man, who seized the opportunity to give him a thrust in the side.", "용감하고 충성스러운 위옹은 이 말에 검을 왼팔 밑에 끼우고, 엎드린 자를 일으키려 오른손을 내밀었는데, 그자는 그 틈을 타 그의 옆구리를 찔렀다."], ["The hauberk of Huon resisted the blow, and he was wounded but slightly.", "위옹의 갑옷이 그 일격을 막아 내어, 그는 가벼운 상처만 입었다."], ["Transported with rage at this act of baseness, he forgot how necessary for his complete acquittal the confession of Amaury was, and without delay dealt him the fatal blow.", "이 비열한 짓에 격분하여 그는 자기의 완전한 무죄 입증을 위해 아모리의 자백이 얼마나 필요한지도 잊고는, 지체 없이 그에게 치명적인 일격을 가했다."]], [["Duke Namo and the other peers approached, had the body of Amaury dragged forth from the lists, and conducted Huon to Charlemagne.", "나모 공작과 다른 중신들이 다가와, 아모리의 시신을 결투장 밖으로 끌어내게 하고, 위옹을 샤를마뉴에게 인도했다."], ["The Emperor, however, listening to nothing but his resentment and grief for the death of his son, refused to be satisfied; and under the plea that Huon had not succeeded in making his accuser retract his charge seemed resolved to confiscate his estates and to banish him forever from France.", "그러나 황제는 자기 아들의 죽음에 대한 원한과 비탄 외에는 아무것도 들으려 하지 않으며 만족하기를 거부했다. 그리고 위옹이 자기를 고발한 자에게서 고발을 철회시키는 데 성공하지 못했다는 구실로, 그의 영지를 몰수하고 그를 프랑스에서 영영 추방하기로 작정한 듯하였다."], ["It was not till after long entreaties on the part of Duke Namo and the rest that he consented to grant Huon his pardon, under conditions which he should impose.", "나모 공작과 나머지 사람들이 오랫동안 간청한 뒤에야 비로소 그는 위옹에게 자기가 부과할 조건들 아래 그를 사면하는 데 동의했다."]], [["Huon approached, and knelt before the Emperor, rendered his homage, and cried him mercy for the involuntary killing of his son. Charlemagne would not receive the hands of Huon in his own, but touched him with his sceptre, saying, “I receive thy homage, and pardon thee the death of my son, but only on one condition. You shall go immediately to the court of the Sultan Gaudisso; you shall present yourself before him as he sits at meat; you shall cut off the head of the most illustrious guest whom you shall find sitting nearest to him; you shall kiss three times on the mouth the fair princess, his daughter, and you shall demand of the Sultan, as token of tribute to me, a handful of the white hair of his beard, and four grinders from his mouth.”", "위옹이 다가가 황제 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 신종 서약을 바치며, 자기가 본의 아니게 그의 아들을 죽인 것에 대해 자비를 빌었다. 샤를마뉴는 위옹의 두 손을 자기 손안에 받아들이려 하지 않고, 다만 왕홀로 그를 건드리며 말했다. \"네 신종 서약을 받아들이고, 내 아들의 죽음을 네게 용서하노라. 그러나 오직 한 가지 조건에서다. 너는 즉시 술탄 가우디소[Gaudisso]의 궁정으로 가라. 그가 식사하러 앉아 있을 때 그 앞에 나아가라. 그의 가장 가까이에 앉아 있는, 네가 발견할 가장 지체 높은 손님의 목을 베어라. 그의 딸인 어여쁜 공주의 입에 세 번 입을 맞추어라. 그리고 술탄에게, 내게 바치는 공물의 표시로 그의 흰 수염 한 줌과 그의 입에서 어금니 넷을 요구하라.\""]], [["These conditions caused a murmur from all the assembly.", "이 조건들은 온 회중에게서 웅성거림을 자아냈다."], ["“What!”", "\"뭐라고요!\""], ["said the Abbot of Cluny; “slaughter a Saracen prince without first offering him baptism?”", "클뤼니 수도원장이 말했다. \"사라센 왕자에게 먼저 세례를 권하지도 않고 그를 살육하라니요?\""], ["“The second condition is not so hard,”", "\"두 번째 조건은 그리 어렵지 않으나.\""], ["said the young peers, “but the demand that Huon is bound to make of the old Sultan is very uncivil, and will be hard to obtain.”", "젊은 중신들이 말했다. \"위옹이 늙은 술탄에게 해야 하는 그 요구는 매우 무례하여, 얻어 내기가 어려울 것입니다.\""]], [["The Emperor’s obstinacy when he had once resolved upon a thing is well known.", "황제가 일단 어떤 일을 작정하고 나면 그 고집이 어떠한지는 익히 알려진 바이다."], ["To the courage of Huon nothing seemed impossible.", "위옹의 용기에는 불가능해 보이는 일이 없었다."], ["“I accept the conditions,”", "\"그 조건들을 받아들이겠습니다.\""], ["said he, silencing the intercessions of the old Duke of Bavaria; “my liege, I accept my pardon at this price.", "그가 늙은 바이에른 공작의 중재를 잠재우며 말했다. \"주군이시여, 이 값을 치르고 저의 사면을 받아들이겠습니다."], ["I go to execute your commands, as your vassal and a peer of France.”", "저는 폐하의 봉신이자 프랑스의 중신으로서, 폐하의 명을 수행하러 가겠습니다.\""]], [["The Duke Namo and Abbot of Cluny, being unable to obtain any relaxation of the sentence passed by Charlemagne, led forth the young Duke, who determined to set out at once on his expedition.", "나모 공작과 클뤼니 수도원장은 샤를마뉴가 내린 선고를 조금도 누그러뜨릴 수 없게 되자, 젊은 공작을 데리고 나왔고, 공작은 곧장 자기의 원정에 나서기로 결심했다."], ["All that the good Abbot could obtain of him was, that he should prepare for this perilous undertaking by going first to Rome, to pay his homage to the Pope, who was the brother of the Duchess Alice, Huon’s mother, and from him demand absolution and his blessing.", "선량한 수도원장이 그에게서 얻어 낼 수 있었던 것이라곤, 그가 이 위험천만한 과업에 대비하여 먼저 로마로 가서 교황에게 경의를 표하라는 것뿐이었다. 교황은 위옹의 어머니인 알리스 공작부인의 형제였으니, 그에게서 사죄와 축복을 청하라는 것이었다."], ["Huon promised it, and forthwith set out on his way to Rome. ————", "위옹은 그러겠노라 약속하고, 곧바로 로마로 가는 길에 올랐다."]], [["HUON OF BORDEAUX (Continued)", "보르도의 위옹 (이어서)"]], [["Huon, having traversed the Apennines and Italy, arrived at the environs of Rome, where, laying aside his armor, he assumed the dress of a pilgrim.", "위옹은 아펜니노산맥과 이탈리아를 가로질러 로마 근교에 다다랐고, 거기서 갑옷을 벗어 두고 순례자의 옷차림을 했다."], ["In this attire he presented himself before the Pope, and not till after he had made a full confession of his sins did he announce himself as his nephew.", "이런 차림으로 그는 교황 앞에 모습을 드러냈으며, 자기 죄를 남김없이 고백하고 나서야 비로소 자기가 그의 조카임을 밝혔다. \"아!"], ["“Ah! my dear nephew,”", "사랑하는 조카여.\""], ["exclaimed the Holy Father, “what harder penance could I impose than the Emperor has already done?", "교황 성하가 외쳤다. \"황제가 이미 부과한 것보다 더 가혹한 속죄를 내가 무엇으로 부과할 수 있겠느냐?"], ["Go in peace, my son,”", "평안히 가거라, 내 아들아.\""], ["he added, absolving him, “I go to intercede for you with the Most High.”", "그가 그를 사죄하며 덧붙였다. \"내 지극히 높으신 분께 너를 위해 중재하러 가겠노라.\""], ["Then he led his nephew into his palace, and introduced him to all the Cardinals and Princes of Rome as the Duke of Guienne, son of the Duchess Alice, his sister.", "그러고는 조카를 자기 궁으로 이끌어, 로마의 모든 추기경과 제후들에게 그를 자기 누이 알리스 공작부인의 아들인 기엔의 공작으로 소개했다."]], [["Huon, at setting out, had made a vow not to stop more than three days in a place.", "위옹은 길을 떠날 때, 한곳에서 사흘 넘게 머물지 않겠노라 맹세를 한 바 있었다."], ["The Holy Father took advantage of this time to inspire him with zeal for the glory of Christianity, and with confidence in the protection of the Most High.", "교황 성하는 이 사흘을 틈타 그에게 그리스도교의 영광을 위한 열의와 지극히 높으신 분의 보호에 대한 믿음을 불어넣었다."], ["He advised him to embark for Palestine, to visit the Holy Sepulchre, and to depart thence for the interior of Asia.", "그는 위옹에게 팔레스타인으로 배를 타고 가서 성묘를 참배하고, 거기서 아시아 내륙으로 떠나라고 조언했다."]], [["Loaded with the blessings of the Holy Father, Huon, obeying his counsels, embarked for Palestine, arrived, and visited with the greatest reverence the holy places.", "위옹은 교황 성하의 축복을 한껏 받고, 그 권고에 따라 팔레스타인으로 배를 타고 가서 도착하여, 더없는 경건함으로 성지들을 참배했다."], ["He then departed, and took his way toward the east.", "그러고는 떠나 동방을 향해 길을 잡았다."]], [["But, ignorant of the country and of the language, he lost himself in a forest, and remained three days without seeing a human creature, living on honey and wild fruits which he found on the trees.", "그러나 그 고장도 언어도 알지 못한 탓에, 그는 어느 숲에서 길을 잃고, 사흘 동안 사람 하나 보지 못한 채, 나무에서 찾아낸 꿀과 들과실로 연명하며 지냈다."], ["The third day, seeking a passage through a rocky defile, he beheld a man in tattered clothing, whose beard and hair covered his breast and shoulders.", "사흘째 되던 날, 바위투성이 협곡을 지나는 길을 찾던 그는, 누더기 옷을 걸치고 수염과 머리털이 가슴과 어깨를 덮은 한 사내를 보았다."], ["This man stopped on seeing him, observed him, and recognized the arms and bearing of a French knight.", "이 사내는 그를 보고 멈추어 그를 살피더니, 프랑스 기사의 무구와 거동을 알아보았다."], ["He immediately approached, and exclaimed, in the language of the South of France, “God be praised!", "그는 즉시 다가와, 프랑스 남부의 말로 외쳤다. \"하느님께 찬미를!"], ["Do I indeed behold a chevalier of my own country, after fifteen years passed in this desert without seeing the face of a fellow-countryman?”", "동포의 얼굴을 보지 못하고 이 황야에서 십오 년을 보낸 끝에, 내가 정녕 내 고국의 기사를 보는 것인가?\""]], [["Huon, to gratify him still more, unlaced his helmet, and came towards him with a smiling countenance.", "위옹은 그를 한층 더 기쁘게 해 주려고 투구를 풀고, 미소 띤 얼굴로 그에게 다가갔다."], ["The other regarded him with more surprise than at first.", "상대는 처음보다 더 놀라며 그를 바라보았다. \"맙소사!\""], ["“Good Heaven!”", "그가 외쳤다."], ["he exclaimed, “was there ever such a resemblance?", "\"이런 닮음이 일찍이 있었던가? 아, 고귀하신 나리.\""], ["Ah, noble sir,”", "그가 덧붙였다."], ["he added, “tell me, I beseech you, of what country and race you come?”", "\"청하옵건대 제게 말씀해 주십시오, 나리는 어느 나라, 어느 혈통에서 오셨습니까?\" \"내 것을 말하기 전에.\""], ["“I require,”", "위옹이 대답했다."], ["replied Huon, “before telling you mine, that you first reveal your own; let it suffice you at present to know that I am a Christian, and that in Guienne I was born.”", "\"그대가 먼저 그대 자신의 것을 밝히기를 요구하오. 지금으로서는, 내가 그리스도교도이며 기엔에서 태어났다는 것을 아는 것으로 족하리다.\""], ["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["Heaven grant that my eyes and my heart do not deceive me,”", "내 눈과 내 마음이 나를 속이지 않기를 하늘이 허락하시기를.\""], ["exclaimed the unknown; “my name is Sherasmin; I am brother to Guire, the Mayor of Bordeaux.", "그 낯선 이가 외쳤다. \"제 이름은 셰라스맹[Sherasmin]이며, 보르도의 시장 기르[Guire]의 형제입니다."], ["I was taken prisoner in the battle where my dear and illustrious master, Sevinus, lost his life.", "저는 제 사랑하고 존귀한 주군 세비누스께서 목숨을 잃으신 그 전투에서 포로로 붙잡혔습니다."], ["For three years I endured", "삼 년 동안 저는 견뎌 냈습니다."]], [["the miseries of slavery; at length I broke my chains and escaped to this desert, where I have sustained myself in solitude ever since.", "노예 생활의 비참함을 맛보았다. 마침내 나는 사슬을 끊고 이 황야로 달아났으며, 그 후로 줄곧 이곳에서 홀로 연명해 왔다."], ["Your features recall to me my beloved sovereign, in whose service I was from my infancy till his death.”", "그대의 용모를 보니 어린 시절부터 그분이 돌아가실 때까지 내가 섬겼던, 내가 사랑하던 군주가 떠오른다.”"], ["Huon made no reply but by embracing the old man, with tears in his eyes.", "위옹은 아무런 대답도 하지 않고 다만 눈물을 글썽이며 노인을 끌어안았다."], ["Then Sherasmin learned that his arms enfolded the son of the Duke Sevinus.", "그제야 셰라스맹은 자기가 두 팔로 끌어안고 있는 사람이 세비누스 공작의 아들임을 알게 되었다."], ["He led him to his cabin, and spread before him the dry fruits and honey which formed his only aliment.", "그는 위옹을 자기 오두막으로 데려가서, 그의 유일한 양식인 말린 과일과 꿀을 앞에 차려 놓았다."]], [["Huon recounted his adventures to Sherasmin, who was moved to tears at the recital.", "위옹은 셰라스맹에게 자신의 모험담을 들려주었고, 그 이야기를 듣던 셰라스맹은 눈물을 흘리며 감동했다."], ["He then consulted him on means of conducting his enterprise.", "그런 다음 위옹은 자신의 과업을 수행할 방도에 관하여 그와 상의했다."], ["Sherasmin hesitated not to confess that success seemed impossible; nevertheless he swore a solemn oath never to abandon him.", "셰라스맹은 성공이 불가능해 보인다는 사실을 주저 없이 털어놓았다. 그럼에도 불구하고 그는 결코 위옹을 저버리지 않겠노라고 엄숙히 맹세했다."], ["The Saracen language, which he was master of, would be serviceable to them when they should leave the desert, and mingle with men.", "그가 능통한 사라센어는 그들이 황야를 떠나 사람들 틈에 섞이게 될 때 요긴하게 쓰일 터였다."]], [["They took the route of the Red Sea, and entered Arabia.", "그들은 홍해 쪽으로 길을 잡아 아라비아로 들어섰다."], ["Their way lay through a region which Sherasmin described as full of terrors.", "그들이 가야 할 길은 셰라스맹이 온갖 공포로 가득하다고 묘사한 지역을 지나고 있었다."], ["It was inhabited by Oberon, King of the Fairies, who made captive such knights as were rash enough to penetrate into it, and transformed them into Hobgoblins.", "그곳에는 요정들의 왕 오베론이 살고 있었는데, 그는 무모하게도 그 지역에 발을 들여놓는 기사들을 사로잡아 도깨비로 둔갑시켰다. 길을 다소 멀리 돌아가는 대가를 치르면 이 지역을 피해 갈 수도 있었다."], ["It was possible to avoid this district at the expense of somewhat lengthening their route; but no dangers could deter Huon of Bordeaux; and the brave Sherasmin, who had now resumed the armor of a knight, reluctantly consented to share with him the dangers of the shorter route.", "그러나 그 어떤 위험도 보르도의 위옹을 단념시키지는 못했다. 그리고 이제 다시 기사의 갑옷을 차려입은 용감한 셰라스맹은 마지못해 그와 함께 더 짧은 길의 위험을 나누기로 했다."]], [["They entered a wood, and arrived at a spot whence alleys branched off in various directions.", "그들은 어느 숲으로 들어섰고, 여러 방향으로 오솔길이 갈라지는 한 지점에 다다랐다."], ["One of them seemed to be terminated by a superb palace, whose gilded roofs were adorned with brilliant weathercocks covered with diamonds.", "그중 하나는 끝에 화려한 궁전이 서 있는 듯 보였는데, 그 도금한 지붕은 다이아몬드로 뒤덮인 눈부신 풍향계들로 장식되어 있었다. 화려한 전차 한 대가 궁전 문에서 나와, 마치 그들을 중간에서 맞이하려는 듯 위옹과 그의 동행을 향해 달려왔다."], ["A superb chariot issued from the gate of the palace, and drove toward Huon and his companion, as if to meet them half-way.", "왕자가 전차 안에서 본 것이라고는 다섯 살쯤 되어 보이는 아이 하나뿐이었는데, 매우 아름다웠고 보석으로 반짝이는 옷을 걸치고 있었다. 그 아이를 보자 셰라스맹의 공포는 극에 달했다."], ["The prince saw no one in the chariot but a child apparently about five years old, very beautiful, and clad in a robe which glittered with precious stones. At the sight of him, Sherasmin’s terror was extreme.", "그는 위옹의 말고삐를 붙잡아 말을 돌려세우고는 왕자를 황급히 끌고 가면서, 저 짓궂은 난쟁이와 멈추어 입씨름이라도 하게 되면 우리는 끝장이라고 단언했다."], ["He seized the reins of Huon’s horse, and turned him about, hurrying the prince away, and assuring him that they were lost if they stopped to parley with the mischievous dwarf, who, though he appeared a child, was full of years and of treachery.", "난쟁이는 비록 아이처럼 보이지만 실은 나이도 많고 간계로 가득 차 있다는 것이었다. 위옹은 아무런 위협도 풍기지 않는 그 아름다운 난쟁이를 시야에서 놓치는 것이 아쉬웠다."], ["Huon was sorry to lose sight of the beautiful dwarf, whose aspect had nothing in it to alarm; yet he followed his friend, who urged on his horse with all possible speed.", "그럼에도 그는 온 힘을 다해 말을 몰아 가는 친구를 뒤따랐다. 이내 폭풍이 숲을 가로질러 으르렁대기 시작했고, 햇빛은 어두워졌으며, 그들은 가까스로 길을 더듬어 나아갔다."], ["Presently a storm began to roar through the forest, the daylight grew dim, and they found their way with difficulty.", "이따금 그들은 어린아이 같은 목소리가 이렇게 말하는 것을 듣는 듯했다. “멈추시오, 위옹 공작."], ["From time to time they seemed to hear an infantine voice, which said, “Stop, Duke Huon; listen to me: it is in vain you fly me!”", "내 말을 들으시오. 그대가 나를 피해 달아나 보았자 헛수고요!”"]], [["Sherasmin only fled the faster, and stopped not until he had reached the gate of a monastery of monks and nuns, the two communities of which were assembled at that time in a religious procession.", "셰라스맹은 그저 더 빨리 달아날 뿐이었고, 수도사들과 수녀들이 머무는 어느 수도원의 문에 다다라서야 비로소 멈추었다. 마침 그곳에서는 두 공동체가 한데 모여 종교 행렬을 이루고 있었다."], ["Sherasmin, feeling safe from the malice of the dwarf in the presence of so many holy persons and the sacred banners, stopped to ask an asylum, and made Huon dismount also.", "셰라스맹은 이토록 많은 성직자들과 신성한 깃발들 앞이라면 난쟁이의 악의로부터 안전하다고 느끼고는, 피난처를 청하기 위해 멈추어 서서 위옹도 말에서 내리게 했다."], ["But at that moment they were joined by the dwarf, who blew a blast upon an ivory horn which hung from his neck.", "그러나 바로 그 순간 난쟁이가 그들에게 합류하더니, 자기 목에 걸린 상아 뿔피리를 한 번 불었다."], ["Immediately the good Sherasmin, in spite of himself, began to dance like a young collegian, and seizing the hand of an aged nun, who felt as if it would be her death, they footed it briskly over the grass, and were imitated by all the other monks and nuns, mingled together, forming the strangest dancing-party ever beheld.", "그러자 곧바로 선량한 셰라스맹은 자기 의지와 무관하게 마치 어린 학생처럼 춤을 추기 시작했고, 죽을 것만 같다고 여기는 어느 늙은 수녀의 손을 붙잡고서 풀밭 위를 경쾌하게 누볐다. 다른 모든 수도사들과 수녀들도 한데 뒤섞여 그를 따라 추니, 일찍이 본 적 없는 가장 기묘한 춤판이 벌어졌다."], ["Huon alone felt no disposition to dance; but he came near dying of laughter at seeing the ridiculous postures and leaps of the others.", "위옹만은 춤출 마음이 조금도 들지 않았다. 그러나 다른 이들의 우스꽝스러운 자세와 도약을 보고는 웃다가 거의 숨이 넘어갈 지경이었다."]], [["The dwarf, approaching Huon, said, in a sweet voice, and in Huon’s own language, “Duke of Guienne, why do you shun me?", "난쟁이는 위옹에게 다가와, 위옹의 모국어로, 다정한 목소리로 말했다. “기엔 공작이여, 어찌하여 나를 피하시오?"], ["I conjure you, in Heaven’s name, speak to me.”", "하늘의 이름으로 그대에게 간청하노니, 내게 말을 건네 주시오.”"], ["Huon, hearing himself addressed in this serious manner, and knowing that no evil spirit would dare to use the holy name in aid of his schemes, replied, “Sir, whoever you are, I am ready to hear and answer you.”", "위옹은 이토록 진지한 방식으로 자신에게 건네는 말을 듣고, 또한 어떤 악령도 감히 자신의 계략을 돕고자 거룩한 이름을 입에 올리지는 못한다는 것을 알기에 이렇게 대답했다. “경이시여, 그대가 누구이든, 나는 그대의 말을 듣고 답할 준비가 되어 있소.”"], ["“Huon, my friend,”", "난쟁이가 말을 이었다."], ["continued the dwarf, “I always loved your race, and you have been dear to me ever since your birth.", "“위옹, 나의 벗이여, 나는 늘 그대의 가문을 사랑해 왔으며, 그대 또한 태어날 때부터 내게 소중한 존재였소."], ["The gracious state of conscience in which you were when you entered my wood has protected you from all enchantments, even if I had intended to practise any upon you.", "그대가 내 숲에 들어섰을 때 지니고 있던 그 은혜로운 양심의 상태가, 설령 내가 그대에게 어떤 마법을 부리려 했다 하더라도 그대를 온갖 마법으로부터 지켜 주었소. 만일 이 수도사들과 수녀들이, 그리고 그대의 벗 셰라스맹조차도 그대만큼 순결한 양심을 지녔더라면 내 뿔피리가 그들을 춤추게 하지는 못했을 것이오."], ["If these monks, these nuns, and even your friend Sherasmin, had had a conscience as pure as yours, my horn would not have set them dancing; but where is the monk or the nun who can always be deaf to the voice of the tempter, and Sherasmin in the desert has often doubted the power of Providence.”", "하지만 늘 유혹자의 목소리에 귀를 막을 수 있는 수도사나 수녀가 어디 있겠소. 그리고 셰라스맹도 황야에서 종종 섭리의 권능을 의심했더랬소.”"]], [["At these words Huon saw the dancers overcome with exertion.", "이 말을 듣고 위옹은 춤추던 이들이 기진맥진해 있는 것을 보았다. 그가 그들에게 자비를 베풀어 달라고 청하니 난쟁이가 이를 받아들였고, 뿔피리의 효력은 즉시 멈추었다."], ["He begged mercy for them, the dwarf granted it, and the effect of the horn ceased at once; the nuns got rid of their partners, smoothed their dresses, and hastened to resume their places in the procession.", "수녀들은 자기 짝에게서 벗어나 옷매무새를 가다듬고는 서둘러 행렬에서 제자리를 되찾았다. 열기에 지치고 헐떡이며 두 다리로 서 있지도 못하던 셰라스맹은 풀밭에 몸을 던지고는 이렇게 입을 열었다."], ["Sherasmin, overcome with heat, panting, and unable to stand on his legs, threw himself upon the grass, and began, “Did not I tell you”—He was going on in an angry tone, but the dwarf, approaching, said, “Sherasmin, why have you murmured against Providence?", "“내가 말하지 않았소—” 그가 성난 어조로 말을 계속하려 했으나, 난쟁이가 다가와 말했다."], ["Why have you thought evil of me?", "“셰라스맹, 어찌하여 그대는 섭리에 대고 불평했소? 어찌하여 나를 나쁘게 여겼소?"], ["You deserved this light punishment; but I know you to be good and loyal; I mean to show myself your friend, as you shall soon see.”", "그대는 이 가벼운 벌을 받아 마땅하오. 하지만 나는 그대가 선량하고 충직한 사람임을 아오."], ["At these words he presented him a rich goblet.", "머지않아 알게 되겠지만, 나는 그대의 벗이 되어 줄 작정이오.” 이렇게 말하면서 그는 그에게 화려한 술잔 하나를 내밀었다."], ["“Make the sign of the cross on this cup,”", "“이 잔에 십자가 표시를 그으시오.” 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “and then believe that I hold my power from the God you adore, whose faithful servant I am, as well as you.”", "“그러고 나서, 내가 나의 권능을 그대가 섬기는 신으로부터 얻었음을 믿으시오. 그대와 마찬가지로 나 또한 그분의 충실한 종이라오.”"], ["Sherasmin obeyed, and on the instant the cup was filled with delicious wine, a draught of which restored vigor to his limbs, and made him feel young again.", "셰라스맹이 그대로 하니, 그 순간 잔은 향긋한 포도주로 가득 찼다. 한 모금을 들이켜자 그의 사지에 기운이 되돌아왔고, 다시 젊어진 듯한 느낌이 들었다."], ["Overcome with gratitude, he threw himself on his knees, but the dwarf raised him, and bade him sit beside him, and thus commenced his history:", "감사함에 겨워 그가 무릎을 꿇으니, 난쟁이가 그를 일으켜 세워 자기 곁에 앉게 하고는 이렇게 자신의 내력을 들려주기 시작했다."]], [["“Julius Cæsar, going by sea to join his army, was driven by a storm to take shelter in the island of Celea, where dwelt the fairy Glorianda.", "“율리우스 카이사르가 자기 군대에 합류하고자 바다를 건너던 중 폭풍을 만나 켈레아 섬으로 피신하게 되었는데, 그곳에는 요정 글로리안다가 살고 있었소."], ["From this renowned pair I draw my birth.", "나는 이 명망 높은 두 사람에게서 태어났소."], ["I am the inheritor of that which was most admirable in each of my parents: my father’s heroic qualities, and my mother’s beauty and magic art.", "나는 내 부모 각각에게서 가장 감탄할 만한 것을 물려받았으니, 곧 아버지의 영웅적 자질과 어머니의 아름다움과 마법의 기예라오."], ["But a malicious sister of my mother’s, in revenge for some slight offence, touched me with her wand when I was only five years old, and forbade me to grow any bigger; and my mother, with all her power, was unable to annul the sentence.", "그러나 어머니의 심술궂은 자매 하나가 사소한 모욕에 앙갚음하느라, 내가 겨우 다섯 살이었을 때 자기 지팡이로 나를 건드리고는 더 이상 자라지 못하게 명했소. 그리고 내 어머니는 온 권능을 동원하고도 그 선고를 무효로 만들 수 없었소."], ["I have thus continued infantile in appearance, though full of years and experience.", "그리하여 나는 나이도 많고 경험도 풍부하건만 겉모습만은 줄곧 어린아이로 머물러 있게 된 것이오."], ["The power which I derive from my mother I use sometimes for my own diversion, but always to promote justice and to reward virtue.", "어머니에게서 물려받은 권능을 나는 때로는 내 자신의 즐거움을 위해 쓰지만, 언제나 정의를 펼치고 미덕에 보답하기 위해 사용한다오."], ["I am able and willing to assist you, Duke of Guienne, for I know the errand on which you come hither.", "나는 그대를 도울 능력도 있고 의지도 있소, 기엔 공작이여. 그대가 이곳에 온 사명이 무엇인지를 내가 알고 있기 때문이오."], ["I presage for you, if you follow my counsels, complete success; and the beautiful Clarimunda for a wife.”", "그대가 내 조언을 따른다면, 나는 그대에게 완벽한 성공과 아내가 될 아름다운 클라리문다를 예언하는 바이오.”"]], [["When he had thus spoken he presented to Huon the precious and useful cup, which had the faculty of filling itself when a good man took it in his hand. He gave him also his beautiful horn of ivory, saying to him, “Huon, when you sound this gently, you will make the hearers dance, as you have seen; but if you sound it forcibly, fear not that I shall hear it, though at a hundred leagues’ distance, and will fly to your relief; but be careful not to sound it in that way, unless upon the most urgent occasion.”", "이렇게 말하고 나서 그는 위옹에게 그 귀하고도 요긴한 잔을 내밀었으니, 그 잔은 선량한 사람이 손에 쥐면 저절로 채워지는 능력을 지니고 있었다. 그는 또한 자신의 아름다운 상아 뿔피리도 그에게 주면서 이렇게 말했다. “위옹, 그대가 이것을 부드럽게 불면, 그대도 보았다시피 듣는 이들을 춤추게 만들 것이오. 하지만 그대가 힘껏 불면, 내가 백 리외 떨어진 곳에 있더라도 그 소리를 듣고 그대를 구하러 날아오리니 염려 마시오. 다만 가장 절박한 경우가 아니라면 그런 식으로 불지 않도록 조심하시오.”"]], [["Oberon directed Huon what course he should take to reach the country of the Sultan Gaudisso.", "오베론은 위옹에게 술탄 가우디소의 나라에 다다르려면 어떤 길을 잡아야 하는지를 일러 주었다."], ["“You will encounter great perils,”", "“그대는 그곳에 닿기 전에 큰 위험들을 만나게 될 것이오.”"], ["said he, “before arriving there, and I fear me,”", "그가 말했다. 그리고 눈에 눈물을 머금은 채 이렇게 덧붙였다."], ["he added, with tears in his eyes, “that you will not in everything obey my directions, and in that case you will suffer much calamity.”", "“게다가 나는 두렵소, 그대가 내 지시를 모든 일에서 다 따르지는 않으리라는 것이. 만약 그렇게 된다면 그대는 많은 재난을 겪게 될 것이오.”"], ["Then he embraced Huon and Sherasmin, and left them.", "그런 다음 그는 위옹과 셰라스맹을 끌어안고 그들을 떠났다."]], [["Huon and his follower travelled many days through the desert before they reached any inhabited place, and all this while the wonderful cup sustained them, furnishing them not only wine, but food also.", "위옹과 그의 종자는 사람이 사는 곳에 다다르기까지 여러 날을 황야를 가로질러 여행했으며, 그동안 내내 그 경이로운 잔이 포도주뿐만 아니라 음식까지도 마련해 주어 그들을 지탱해 주었다."], ["At last they came to a great city.", "마침내 그들은 어느 큰 도시에 이르렀다."], ["As day was declining, they entered its suburbs, and Sherasmin, who spoke the Saracen language perfectly, inquired for an inn where they could pass the night.", "날이 저물어 가고 있을 무렵 그들은 그 변두리로 들어섰고, 사라센어에 완벽하게 능통한 셰라스맹이 하룻밤 묵을 만한 여관을 수소문했다."], ["A person who appeared to be one of the principal inhabitants, seeing two strangers of respectable appearance making this inquiry, stepped forward and begged them to accept the shelter of his mansion.", "그곳의 주요 인사 가운데 한 사람으로 보이는 어떤 이가, 점잖아 보이는 두 이방인이 이렇게 묻는 것을 보고 앞으로 나서서 자기 저택에 묵어가기를 청했다."], ["They entered, and their host did the honors of his abode with a politeness which they were astonished to see in a Saracen.", "그들이 안으로 들어서니, 주인은 사라센인에게서 보리라고는 미처 생각지 못한 정중함으로 자기 집의 손님 대접을 했다."], ["He had them served with coffee and sherbet, and all was conducted with great decorum, till one of the servants awkwardly overturned a cup of hot coffee on the host’s legs, when he started up, exclaiming in very good Gascon, “Blood and thunder!", "그는 그들에게 커피와 셔벗을 내오게 했고, 모든 일이 대단히 예법에 맞게 진행되었다. 그러다가 하인 하나가 서툴게도 뜨거운 커피 잔을 주인의 다리에 엎질렀는데, 그러자 주인이 벌떡 일어나 아주 훌륭한 가스코뉴 사투리로 이렇게 외쳤다."], ["you blockhead, you deserve to be thrown over the mosque!”", "“이런 빌어먹을! 이 멍청한 놈, 모스크 너머로 내던져 마땅한 놈 같으니!”"]], [["Huon could not help laughing to see the vivacity and the language of his country thus break out unawares.", "위옹은 이렇게 자기 고향의 활기와 말씨가 무심결에 터져 나오는 것을 보고 웃음을 참을 수가 없었다."], ["The host, who had no idea that his guests understood his words, was astonished when Huon addressed him in the dialect of his country.", "손님들이 자기 말을 알아들으리라고는 전혀 생각지 못했던 주인은, 위옹이 자기 고향 사투리로 말을 건네자 깜짝 놀랐다."], ["Immediately confidence was established between them; especially when the domestics had retired.", "곧 두 사람 사이에 신뢰가 형성되었으니, 특히 하인들이 물러간 뒤로는 더욱 그러했다."], ["The host, seeing that he was discovered, and that the two pretended Saracens were from the borders of the Garonne, embraced them, and disclosed that he was a Christian.", "자신의 정체가 들통났고 또 사라센인 행세를 하던 두 사람이 가론 강 유역 출신이라는 것을 안 주인은 그들을 끌어안고는, 자기가 기독교인이라는 사실을 털어놓았다."], ["Huon, who had learned prudence from the advice of Oberon, to test his host’s sincerity, drew from his robe the cup which the Fairy-king had given him, and presented it empty to the host.", "오베론의 충고에서 신중함을 배운 위옹은 주인의 진심을 시험해 보고자, 요정 왕이 자기에게 준 잔을 옷에서 꺼내 빈 채로 주인에게 내밀었다. “훌륭한 잔이군요.”"], ["“A fair cup,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “but I should like it better if it was full.”", "“허나 가득 차 있다면 더 좋아할 텐데요.”"], ["Immediately it was so.", "그러자 곧바로 잔이 가득 찼다."], ["The host, astonished, dared not put it to his lips.", "깜짝 놀란 주인은 감히 그것을 입에 대지 못했다."], ["“Drink boldly, my dear fellow-countryman,”", "“마음껏 드시오, 친애하는 동향 사람이여.”"], ["said Huon; “your truth is proved by this cup, which only fills itself in the hands of an honest man.”", "위옹이 말했다. “정직한 사람의 손에서만 저절로 채워지는 이 잔이 그대의 진실함을 증명해 주었소.”"], ["The host did not hesitate longer; the cup passed freely from hand to hand; their mutual cordiality increased as it passed, and each recounted his adventures.", "주인은 더 이상 망설이지 않았고, 잔은 손에서 손으로 자유로이 오갔다. 잔이 오가는 동안 그들의 우애는 더욱 깊어졌고, 저마다 자신의 모험담을 들려주었다."], ["Those of Huon redoubled his host’s respect; for he recognized in him his legitimate sovereign: while the host’s narrative was in these words:", "위옹의 이야기는 주인의 존경심을 갑절로 키웠으니, 그가 위옹에게서 자신의 정당한 군주를 알아보았기 때문이다. 한편 주인의 이야기는 다음과 같았다."]], [["“My name is Floriac; this great and strong city, you will hear with surprise and grief, is governed by a brother of Duke Sevinus, and your uncle.", "“제 이름은 플로리아크입니다. 놀라움과 슬픔을 안고 들으셔야겠지만, 이 크고 강성한 도시는 세비누스 공작의 아우이자 공작님의 숙부 되시는 분이 다스리고 있습니다."], ["You have no doubt heard that a young brother of the Duke of Guienne was stolen away from the sea-shore, with his companions, by some corsairs.", "기엔 공작의 어린 아우가 동무들과 함께 해변에서 어느 해적들에게 납치되었다는 이야기를 분명 들으셨을 겁니다."], ["I was then his page, and we were carried by those corsairs to Barbary, where we were sold for slaves.", "그때 저는 그분의 시동(侍童)이었는데, 우리는 그 해적들에게 끌려 바르바리로 갔고, 그곳에서 노예로 팔렸습니다."], ["The Barbary prince sent us as part of the tribute which he yearly paid to his sovereign, the Sultan Gaudisso.", "바르바리의 군주는 우리를 자신의 군주인 술탄 가우디소에게 해마다 바치는 조공의 일부로 보냈습니다."], ["Your uncle, who had been somewhat puffed up by the flattery of his attendants, thought to increase his importance with his new master by telling him his rank.", "시종들의 아첨에 다소 우쭐해져 있던 공작님의 숙부께서는, 자신의 신분을 밝힘으로써 새 주인 앞에서 자기 위세를 높일 수 있으리라 여겼습니다."], ["The Sultan, who, like a true Mussulman, detested all Christian princes, exerted himself from that moment to bring him over to the Saracen faith.", "진정한 이슬람교도답게 모든 기독교 군주를 증오하던 술탄은 그 순간부터 그분을 사라센 신앙으로 끌어들이려 애썼습니다."], ["He succeeded but too well.", "그리고 너무도 잘 성공하고 말았지요."], ["Your uncle, seduced by the arts of the Santons, and by the pleasures and indulgences which the Sultan allowed him, committed the horrid crime of apostasy; he renounced his baptism, and embraced Mahometanism.", "공작님의 숙부께서는 산톤들의 술책과 술탄이 허락한 쾌락과 방종에 현혹되어, 배교라는 끔찍한 죄를 저지르고 말았습니다. 그분은 자신의 세례를 저버리고 마호메트교를 받아들이셨습니다."], ["Gaudisso then loaded him with honors, made him espouse one of his nieces, and sent him to reign over this city and adjoining country.", "그러자 가우디소는 그분에게 온갖 영예를 안기고, 자기 조카딸 하나와 혼인하게 한 뒤, 이 도시와 인근 지역을 다스리도록 보냈습니다."], ["Your uncle preserved for me the same friendship which he had had when a boy; but all his caresses and efforts could not make me renounce my faith.", "공작님의 숙부께서는 소년 시절에 제게 품으셨던 그 우정을 한결같이 간직하고 계셨습니다. 그러나 그분의 온갖 다정함과 노력으로도 제 신앙을 저버리게 하지는 못했습니다."], ["Perhaps he respected me in his heart for my resistance to his persuasions, perhaps he had hopes of inducing me in time to imitate him.", "어쩌면 그분은 그 설득에 맞서는 저를 속으로 존중하셨는지도, 어쩌면 언젠가 제가 당신을 본받도록 만들 수 있으리라 기대하셨는지도 모르겠습니다."], ["He made me accompany him to this city, of which he was master, he gave me his confidence, and permits me to keep in my service some Christians, whom I protect for the sake of their faith.”", "그분은 자신이 다스리는 이 도시로 저를 데려와 신임을 베푸셨고, 제가 그들의 신앙을 위해 보호하는 몇몇 기독교인을 부리도록 허락하고 계십니다.”"]], [["“Ah!”", "“아!”"], ["exclaimed Huon, “take me to this guilty uncle.", "위옹이 외쳤다. “나를 그 죄 많은 숙부에게 데려가 주시오."], ["A prince of the house of Guienne, must he not blush at the cowardly abandonment of the faith of his fathers?”", "기엔 가문의 한 군주가 어찌 조상들의 신앙을 비겁하게 저버린 것을 부끄러워하지 않을 수 있겠소?”"]], [["“Alas!”", "“아아!”"], ["replied Floriac, “I fear he will neither be sensible of shame at your reproaches, nor of pleasure at the sight of a nephew so worthy of his lineage.", "플로리아크가 대답했다. “저로서는 그분이 공작님의 질책에 부끄러움을 느끼지도, 그분의 혈통에 그토록 걸맞은 조카를 보고 기뻐하지도 않으리라 두렵습니다."], ["Brutified by sensuality, jealous of his power, which he often exercises with cruelty, he will more probably restrain you by force or put you to death.”", "육욕에 짐승처럼 무뎌지고, 자신의 권력을 시샘하여 종종 잔혹하게 휘두르는 그분이라면, 오히려 공작님을 힘으로 붙들어 두거나 죽여 버릴 가능성이 더 큽니다.”"]], [["“Be it so,”", "“그렇다 하더라도 좋소.”"], ["said the brave and fervent Huon, “I could not die in a better cause; and I demand of you to conduct me to him to-morrow, after having told him of my arrival and my birth.”", "용감하고도 열렬한 위옹이 말했다. “이보다 더 나은 대의를 위해서라면 죽어도 여한이 없소."], ["Floriac still objected, but Huon would take no denial, and he promised obedience.", "그리고 나는 그대에게 청하노니, 내일 그분에게 나의 도착과 출생을 알린 다음 나를 그분에게 데려다주시오.” 플로리아크는 여전히 반대했지만, 위옹은 거절을 받아들이려 하지 않았고, 결국 그는 따르겠노라 약속했다."]], [["Next morning Floriac waited upon the Governor and told him of the arrival of his nephew, Huon of Bordeaux; and of the intention of the prince to present himself at his court that very day.", "이튿날 아침 플로리아크는 총독을 알현하여 그의 조카 보르도의 위옹이 당도했음을, 그리고 그 왕자가 바로 그날 그의 궁정에 모습을 드러낼 작정임을 알렸다."], ["The Governor, surprised, did not immediately answer; though he at once made up his mind what to do.", "총독은 놀란 나머지 즉시 대답하지는 않았으나, 어떻게 할지 곧바로 마음을 정했다."], ["He knew that Floriac loved Christians and the princes of his native land too well to aid in any treason to one of them; he therefore feigned great pleasure at hearing of the arrival of the eldest born of his family at his court.", "그는 플로리아크가 기독교인들과 자기 모국의 왕족들을 너무도 아끼는지라 그들 중 누구를 배신하는 일에는 가담하지 않으리라는 것을 알고 있었다. 그래서 그는 자기 집안의 맏이가 자기 궁정에 당도했다는 소식을 듣고 크게 기뻐하는 척했다."], ["He immediately sent Floriac to find him; he caused his palace to be put in festal array, his divan to be assembled, and after giving some secret orders, went himself to meet his nephew, whom he introduced under his proper name and title to all the great officers of his court.", "그는 즉시 플로리아크를 보내 그를 데려오게 했고, 자기 궁전을 잔치 분위기로 꾸미게 했으며, 어전 회의를 소집하고, 몇 가지 은밀한 명령을 내린 뒤, 몸소 조카를 맞이하러 나가서 그를 본명과 직함 그대로 자기 궁정의 모든 고관들에게 소개했다."]], [["Huon burned with indignation at seeing his uncle with forehead encircled with a rich turban, surmounted with a crescent of precious stones.", "위옹은 자기 숙부가 보석 박힌 초승달 장식을 얹은 화려한 터번으로 이마를 두른 것을 보고 분노로 끓어올랐다."], ["His natural candor made him receive with pain the embraces which the treacherous Governor lavished upon him.", "천성이 솔직한 그는 음흉한 총독이 자신에게 아낌없이 퍼붓는 포옹을 고통스럽게 받아들였다."], ["Meanwhile the hope of finding a suitable moment to reproach him for his apostasy made him submit to those honors which his uncle caused to be rendered to him.", "한편 그의 배교를 질책할 적당한 순간을 찾을 수 있으리라는 희망 때문에, 그는 숙부가 자신에게 베풀게 한 그 영예들을 묵묵히 견뎌 냈다."], ["The Governor evaded with address the chance of being alone with Huon and spent all the morning in taking him through his gardens and palace.", "총독은 위옹과 단둘이 있게 될 기회를 교묘히 피하면서, 그를 자신의 정원과 궁전 곳곳으로 데리고 다니며 오전 내내를 보냈다."], ["At last, when the hour of dinner approached, and the Governor took him by the hand to lead him into the dining-hall, Huon seized the opportunity and said to him in a low voice, “O my uncle!", "마침내 만찬 시간이 가까워져 총독이 그의 손을 잡고 식당으로 이끌려 할 때, 위옹은 그 기회를 붙잡아 낮은 목소리로 그에게 말했다. “오 숙부님!"], ["O Prince, brother of the Duke Sevinus!", "오 세비누스 공작의 아우이신 왕자님이시여!"], ["in what condition have I the grief and shame of seeing you!”", "제가 어떤 처지의 당신을 뵙게 되어 이토록 슬프고 부끄러워해야 한단 말입니까!”"], ["The Governor pretended to be moved, pressed his hand, and whispered in his ear, “Silence!", "총독은 감동한 척하며 그의 손을 꼭 쥐고는 그의 귀에 속삭였다."], ["my dear nephew; to-morrow morning I will hear you fully.”", "“조용히 하거라, 사랑하는 조카야."], ["Huon, comforted a little by these words, took his seat at the table by the side of the Governor.", "내일 아침에 네 말을 충분히 들어 주마.” 이 말에 다소 위안을 얻은 위옹은 총독의 곁자리에 앉았다."], ["The Mufti, some Cadis, Agas, and Santons, filled the other places.", "무프티, 몇몇 카디와 아가와 산톤이 나머지 자리를 채웠다."], ["Sherasmin sat down with them; but Floriac, who would not lose sight of his guests, remained standing, and passed in and out to observe what was going on within the palace.", "셰라스맹도 그들과 함께 자리에 앉았다. 그러나 자기 손님들에게서 눈을 떼려 하지 않던 플로리아크는 선 채로 들락날락하면서 궁전 안에서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지를 살폈다."], ["He soon perceived a number of armed men gliding through the passages and antechambers connected with the dining-hall.", "그는 곧 무장한 사내 여럿이 식당과 이어진 통로와 대기실 사이로 슬그머니 움직이는 것을 알아챘다."], ["He was about to enter to give his guests notice of what he had seen when he heard a violent noise and commotion in the hall.", "그가 본 것을 손님들에게 알리고자 안으로 들어가려던 참에, 식당 안에서 격렬한 소음과 소란이 들려왔다."], ["The cause was this.", "그 까닭은 이러했다."]], [["Huon and Sherasmin were well enough suited with the first course and ate with good appetite; but the people of their country not being accustomed to drink only water at their meals, Huon and Sherasmin looked at one another, not very well pleased at such a regimen.", "위옹과 셰라스맹은 첫 번째 요리가 제법 입에 맞아 왕성하게 먹었다. 그러나 그들 고향 사람들은 식사 때 물만 마시는 데 익숙하지 않았던지라, 위옹과 셰라스맹은 그러한 식습관이 그다지 마음에 들지 않아 서로를 마주 보았다."], ["Huon laughed outright at the impatience of Sherasmin, but soon, experiencing the same want himself, he drew forth Oberon’s cup and made the sign of the cross.", "위옹은 셰라스맹의 안달하는 모습에 소리 내어 웃었으나, 곧 자기도 똑같은 부족함을 느끼게 되자 오베론의 잔을 꺼내어 십자가 표시를 그었다. 잔이 가득 차자 그는 그것을 단숨에 들이켜고 셰라스맹에게 건넸고, 셰라스맹도 그를 따라 했다."], ["The cup filled and he drank it off, and handed it to Sherasmin, who followed his example.", "총독과 그의 고관들은 그 혐오스러운 표시를 보고 미간을 찌푸린 채 말없이 경악하여 앉아 있었다."], ["The Governor and his officers, seeing this abhorred sign, contracted their brows and sat in silent consternation.", "위옹은 그것을 못 본 척하고, 잔을 다시 가득 채워 숙부에게 건네며 말했다. “자, 우리와 함께하시지요, 친애하는 숙부님."], ["Huon pretended not to observe it, and having filled the cup again handed it to his uncle, saying, “Pray, join us, dear uncle; it is excellent Bordeaux wine, the drink that will be to you like mother’s milk.”", "이건 훌륭한 보르도 포도주로, 숙부님께는 어머니의 젖과도 같은 술이랍니다.” 총독은 자기가 총애하는 술타나들과 더불어 종종 몰래 그리스와 시라즈의 포도주를 마셨지만, 사람들 앞에서는 결코 물 외에는 아무것도 마시지 않았다."], ["The Governor, who often drank in secret with his own favorite Sultanas the wines of Greece and Shiraz, never in public drank anything but water.", "그는 오랫동안 자기 모국의 그 빼어난 포도주를 맛보지 못했던 터라, 지금 자기에게 건네진 것을 마시고 싶은 유혹에 몹시 사로잡혔다."], ["He had not for a long time tasted the excellent wines of his native land; he was sorely tempted to drink what was now handed to him, it looked so bright in the cup, outshining the gold itself.", "잔 속의 그것은 어찌나 환하게 빛나던지 황금마저도 무색할 지경이었다. 그가 손을 뻗어 찰랑찰랑 넘치는 잔을 받아들고 입술로 가져가자, 그 순간 잔은 말라붙어 사라져 버렸다."], ["He stretched forth his hand, took the brimming goblet, and raised it to his lips, when immediately it dried up and disappeared.", "위옹과 셰라스맹은 과연 가스코뉴 사람들답게 그의 경악하는 모습을 보고 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["Huon and Sherasmin, like Gascons as they were, laughed at his astonishment.", "“기독교의 개들 같으니!”"], ["“Christian dogs!”", "그가 외쳤다."], ["he exclaimed, “do you dare to insult me at my own table?", "“감히 내 식탁에서 나를 모욕하는 게냐?"], ["But I will soon be revenged.”", "허나 내 곧 앙갚음하리라.”"], ["At these words he threw the cup at the head of his nephew, who caught it with his left hand, while with the other he snatched the turban, with its crescent, from the Governor’s head and threw it on the floor.", "이렇게 말하면서 그는 잔을 조카의 머리를 향해 던졌는데, 위옹은 그것을 왼손으로 받아 내는 동시에 다른 손으로 총독의 머리에서 초승달 장식이 달린 터번을 낚아채어 바닥에 내동댕이쳤다."], ["All the Saracens started up from table, with loud outcries, and prepared to avenge the insult.", "모든 사라센인들이 큰 소리로 외치며 식탁에서 벌떡 일어나 그 모욕에 앙갚음할 채비를 했다."], ["Huon and Sherasmin put themselves on their defence, and met with their swords the scimitars directed against them.", "위옹과 셰라스맹은 방어 태세를 취하고, 자신들을 향해 겨누어진 언월도들을 칼로 맞받았다."], ["At this moment the doors of the hall opened and a crowd of soldiers and armed eunuchs rushed in, who joined in the attack upon Huon and Sherasmin.", "이 순간 식당 문이 열리며 병사들과 무장한 환관들이 무리 지어 쏟아져 들어와 위옹과 셰라스맹에 대한 공격에 가세했다."], ["The Prince and his followers took refuge on a broad shelf or sideboard, where they kept at bay the crowd of assailants, making the most forward of them smart for their audacity.", "왕자와 그의 종자는 넓은 선반인 찬장 위로 몸을 피해, 그곳에서 무리 지어 덤벼드는 자들을 막아 내며 가장 앞장선 자들에게 그 무모함의 대가를 톡톡히 치르게 했다."], ["But more troops came pressing in and the brave Huon, inspired by the wine of Bordeaux, and not angry enough to lose his relish for a joke, blew a gentle note on his horn, and no sooner was it heard than it quelled the rage of the combatants and set them to dancing.", "그러나 더 많은 병력이 밀려들어 왔고, 보르도 포도주에 고무된 용감한 위옹은 농담의 묘미를 잃을 만큼 화가 난 것은 아니었던지라, 뿔피리를 부드럽게 한 가락 불었다. 그 소리가 들리기가 무섭게 그것은 싸우는 자들의 분노를 가라앉히고 그들을 춤추게 만들었다."], ["Huon and Sherasmin, no longer attacked, looked down from their elevated position on a scene the most singular and amusing.", "더 이상 공격받지 않게 된 위옹과 셰라스맹은 높은 곳에서 가장 기묘하고도 우스꽝스러운 광경을 내려다보았다."], ["Very soon the Sultanas, hearing the sound of the dance and finding their guards withdrawn, came into the hall and mixed with the dancers.", "이윽고 춤추는 소리를 들은 술타나들이 자기들의 호위병이 물러간 것을 알고 식당 안으로 들어와 춤추는 무리에 섞였다."], ["The favorite Sultana seized upon a young Santon, who performed jumps two feet high; but soon the long dresses of this couple got intermingled and threw them down.", "총애받는 술타나는 두 자 높이로 껑충껑충 뛰어오르는 젊은 산톤을 붙잡았는데, 곧 이 둘의 긴 옷자락이 서로 얽혀 그들을 넘어뜨렸다."], ["The Santon’s beard was caught in the Sultana’s necklace, and they could not disentangle them.", "산톤의 수염이 술타나의 목걸이에 걸려 도무지 풀어낼 수가 없었다."], ["The Governor by no means approved this familiarity, and took two steps forward to get at the Santon, but he stumbled over a prostrate Dervise and measured his length on the floor.", "총독은 이러한 무람없는 짓을 결코 달가워하지 않아, 그 산톤에게 다가가려고 두 걸음을 내디뎠으나, 엎어져 있던 데르비시에 발이 걸려 바닥에 큰대자로 나뒹굴고 말았다."], ["The dancing continued till the strength of the performers was exhausted, and they fell, one after the other, and lay helpless.", "춤은 추는 자들의 기운이 다할 때까지 계속되었고, 그들은 하나둘 쓰러져 속수무책으로 늘어졌다."], ["The Governor at length made signs to Huon that he would yield everything if he would but allow him to rest.", "마침내 총독은, 자기를 쉬게만 해 준다면 무엇이든 양보하겠노라고 위옹에게 손짓으로 알렸다."], ["The bargain was ratified; the Governor allowed Huon and Sherasmin to depart on their way, and even gave them a ring which would procure them safe passage through his country and access to the Sultan Gaudisso.", "거래가 성립되었다. 총독은 위옹과 셰라스맹이 가던 길을 떠날 수 있도록 허락했고, 심지어 자기 영토를 무사히 통과하여 술탄 가우디소에게 다다를 수 있게 해 줄 반지까지 그들에게 주었다."], ["The two friends hastened to avail themselves of this favorable turn, and taking leave of Floriac, pursued their journey. ————", "두 벗은 이 유리한 국면을 서둘러 이용하여, 플로리아크와 작별하고 여정을 이어 갔다."]], [["HUON OF BORDEAUX (Continued)", "보르도의 위옹 (계속)"]], [["Huon had seen many beauties at his mother’s court, but his heart had never been touched with love.", "위옹은 어머니의 궁정에서 많은 미녀들을 보았으나, 그의 마음이 사랑에 흔들린 적은 한 번도 없었다."], ["Honor had been his mistress, and in pursuit of that he had never found time to give a thought to softer cares.", "그에게는 명예가 연인이었으며, 그것을 좇느라 그는 더 부드러운 근심거리에 마음을 둘 겨를을 결코 갖지 못했다."], ["Strange that a heart so insensible should first be touched by something so unsubstantial as a dream; but so it was.", "그토록 무감하던 마음이 꿈이라는 그토록 실체 없는 것에 처음으로 흔들렸다는 것은 기이한 일이나, 실제로 그러했다."]], [["The day after the adventure with his uncle night overtook the travellers as they passed through a forest.", "숙부와의 모험을 겪은 다음 날, 여행자들이 어느 숲을 지나는 동안 밤이 그들을 덮쳤다."], ["A grotto offered them shelter from the night dews.", "동굴 하나가 그들에게 밤이슬을 피할 안식처를 내주었다."], ["The magic cup supplied their evening meal; for such was its virtue that it afforded not only wine, but more solid fare when desired.", "마법의 잔이 그들의 저녁 식사를 마련해 주었으니, 원하기만 하면 포도주뿐만 아니라 더 든든한 음식까지도 내어 주는 것이 그 잔의 효능이었기 때문이다."], ["Fatigue soon threw them into profound repose.", "피로가 이내 그들을 깊은 잠에 빠뜨렸다."], ["Lulled by the murmur of the foliage, and breathing the fragrance of the flowers, Huon dreamed that a lady more beautiful than he had ever before seen hung over him and imprinted a kiss upon his lips.", "나뭇잎들의 속삭임에 잠이 든 채 꽃들의 향기를 들이쉬며, 위옹은 일찍이 본 적 없을 만큼 아름다운 한 여인이 자기 위로 몸을 숙여 자기 입술에 입맞춤을 새기는 꿈을 꾸었다."], ["As he stretched out his arms to embrace her a sudden gust of wind swept her away.", "그녀를 끌어안으려 두 팔을 뻗는 순간 갑작스러운 한 줄기 바람이 그녀를 쓸어 가 버렸다."]], [["Huon awoke in an agony of regret.", "위옹은 애끓는 회한 속에서 잠을 깼다."], ["A few moments sufficed to afford some consolation in showing him that what had passed was but a dream; but his perplexity and sadness could not escape the notice of Sherasmin.", "잠시 후 지나간 일이 한낱 꿈에 지나지 않았음을 깨닫고 다소 위안을 얻기는 했으나, 그의 당혹스러움과 슬픔은 셰라스맹의 눈을 피하지 못했다."], ["Huon hesitated not to inform his faithful follower of the reason of his pensiveness; and got nothing in return but his rallyings for allowing himself to be disturbed by such a cause.", "위옹은 자신의 충직한 종자에게 자기가 시름에 잠긴 까닭을 주저 없이 알렸으나, 그 보답으로 얻은 것이라고는 그런 일로 마음을 어지럽혔다는 셰라스맹의 놀림뿐이었다."], ["He recommended a draught from the fairy goblet, and Huon tried it with good effect.", "셰라스맹이 요정의 잔에서 한 모금 들이켜 보기를 권하니, 위옹은 그것을 시험해 보고 좋은 효험을 보았다."]], [["At early dawn they resumed their way.", "이른 새벽에 그들은 다시 길을 떠났다."], ["They travelled till high noon, but said little to one another.", "그들은 한낮이 될 때까지 여행했으나 서로 별다른 말을 나누지 않았다."], ["Huon was musing on his dream, and Sherasmin’s thoughts flew back to his early days on the banks of the flowery Garonne.", "위옹은 자신의 꿈을 곱씹고 있었고, 셰라스맹의 생각은 꽃 만발한 가론 강 기슭에서 보낸 어린 시절로 날아갔다."]], [["On a sudden they were startled by the cry of distress, and turning an angle of the wood, came where a knight hard pressed was fighting with a furious lion.", "그때 갑자기 그들은 고통에 찬 비명에 깜짝 놀랐고, 숲의 한 모퉁이를 돌아서니 어느 기사가 사나운 사자와 사투를 벌이며 궁지에 몰려 있는 곳에 이르렀다."], ["The knight’s horse lay dead, and it seemed as if another moment would end the combat, for terror and fatigue had quite disabled the knight for further resistance.", "기사의 말은 죽어 쓰러져 있었고, 공포와 피로로 더 이상 저항할 힘을 완전히 잃은 기사에게는 한순간만 더 지나면 싸움이 끝장날 듯이 보였다."], ["He fell, and the lion’s paw was raised over him, when a blow from Huon’s sword turned the monster’s rage upon a new enemy.", "그가 쓰러지고 사자의 앞발이 그의 위로 치켜올려지는 순간, 위옹의 칼이 내려친 일격이 그 괴물의 분노를 새로운 적에게로 돌려놓았다."], ["His roar shook the forest, and he crouched in act to spring, when, with the rapidity of lightning, Huon plunged his sword into his side.", "사자가 포효하니 숲이 진동했고, 사자가 덮칠 자세로 몸을 웅크리는 찰나에 위옹은 번개와 같은 속도로 그 옆구리에 칼을 찔러 넣었다."], ["He rolled over on the plain in the agonies of death.", "사자는 죽음의 고통 속에서 벌판을 데굴데굴 굴렀다."]], [["They raised the knight from the ground, and Sherasmin hastened to offer him a draught from the fairy cup.", "그들은 기사를 땅에서 일으켜 세웠고, 셰라스맹이 서둘러 그에게 요정의 잔에서 한 모금 권했다."], ["The wine sparkled to the brim, and the warrior put forth his lips to quaff it, but it shrunk away, and did not even wet his lips.", "포도주가 잔 가장자리까지 반짝이며 찼고, 그 전사는 그것을 들이켜려 입술을 내밀었으나, 술은 움츠러들어 그의 입술조차 적시지 못했다."], ["He dashed the goblet angrily on the ground, with an exclamation of resentment.", "그는 분개의 외침과 함께 잔을 성난 듯 땅바닥에 내동댕이쳤다."], ["This incident did not tend to make either party more acceptable to the other; and what followed was worse.", "이 일은 어느 한쪽이 상대에게 더 호감을 갖게 하는 데 도움이 되지 못했고, 뒤이은 일은 더욱 고약했다."], ["For when Huon said, “Sir knight, thank God for your deliverance,”—“Thank Mahomet, rather, yourself,”", "위옹이 “기사여, 그대가 구원받은 것을 하느님께 감사하시오”라고 말하자, 그가 이렇게 대꾸했기 때문이다."], ["said he, “for he has led you this day to render service to no less a personage than the Prince of Hyrcania.”", "“차라리 그대 자신과 마호메트께 감사하시오. 그분이 오늘 그대를 이끌어, 다름 아닌 히르카니아의 왕자에게 봉사하도록 하셨으니 말이오.”"]], [["At the sound of this blasphemy Huon drew his sword and turned upon the miscreant, who, little disposed to encounter the prowess of which he had so lately seen proof, betook himself to flight.", "이 모독의 말을 듣고 위옹은 칼을 뽑아 그 악당에게 달려들었다. 그러나 조금 전에 그토록 분명한 무용을 목격한 터라 그와 맞붙을 마음이 거의 없던 그자는 달아나기 시작했다."], ["He ran to Huon’s horse, and lightly vaulting on his back, clapped spurs to his side, and galloped out of sight.", "그는 위옹의 말로 달려가 가뿐히 그 등에 뛰어올라 옆구리에 박차를 가하더니, 쏜살같이 달려 시야에서 사라졌다."]], [["The adventure was vexatious, yet there was no remedy.", "분통 터지는 일이었으나 어쩔 도리가 없었다."], ["The prince and Sherasmin continued their journey with the aid of the remaining horse as they best might.", "왕자와 셰라스맹은 남은 말 한 마리의 도움을 받아 그럭저럭 여정을 이어 갔다."], ["At length, as evening set in, they descried the pinnacles and towers of a great city full before them, which they knew to be the famous city of Bagdad.", "마침내 저녁이 들 무렵, 그들은 바로 눈앞에 어느 큰 도시의 첨탑과 망루들을 발견했는데, 그곳이 그 유명한 바그다드 시임을 알아보았다."]], [["They were well-nigh exhausted with fatigue when they arrived at its precincts, and in the darkness, not knowing what course to take, were glad to meet an aged woman, who, in reply to their inquiries, offered them such accommodations as her cottage could supply.", "그곳 경내에 다다랐을 때 그들은 피로에 거의 기진맥진해 있었고, 어둠 속에서 어느 길로 가야 할지 몰라 막막하던 차에, 한 늙은 여인을 만나 기뻤다. 그 여인은 그들의 물음에 답하여 자기 오두막에서 마련할 수 있는 그런 잠자리를 내주겠노라 했다."], ["They thankfully accepted the offer, and entered the low door.", "그들은 고맙게 그 제안을 받아들여 낮은 문 안으로 들어섰다."], ["The good dame busily prepared the best fare her stores supplied,—milk, figs, and peaches,—deeply regretting that the bleak winds had nipped her almond-trees.", "마음씨 좋은 노부인은 자기 곳간에 있는 가장 좋은 먹을거리를, 즉 우유와 무화과와 복숭아를 부지런히 마련하면서, 매서운 바람이 자기 아몬드나무를 얼려 죽인 것을 몹시 안타까워했다."]], [["Sir Huon thought he had never in his life tasted any fare so good.", "위옹 경은 평생 이토록 맛있는 음식을 먹어 본 적이 없다고 여겼다."], ["The old lady talked while her guests ate.", "노부인은 손님들이 먹는 동안 이야기를 늘어놓았다."], ["She doubted not, she said, they had come to be present at the great feast in honor of the marriage of the Sultan’s daughter, which was to take place on the morrow.", "그녀는 그들이 이튿날에 거행될 술탄의 딸 혼례를 기념하는 큰 잔치에 참석하러 온 것이 분명하다고 했다."], ["They asked who the bridegroom was to be, and the old lady answered, “The Prince of Hyrcania,”", "그들이 신랑이 누가 되느냐고 묻자 노부인은 “히르카니아의 왕자”라고 대답하고는, 이렇게 덧붙였다."], ["but added, “Our princess hates him, and would rather wed a dragon than him.”", "“하지만 우리 공주님은 그를 미워하셔서, 그자와 혼인하느니 차라리 용과 혼인하시겠다고 하신답니다.”"], ["“How know you that?”", "“그것을 어찌 아시오?”"], ["asked Huon; and the dame informed him that she had it from the princess herself, who was her foster-child.", "위옹이 묻자, 노부인은 자기가 젖을 먹여 기른 양녀인 공주에게서 직접 들었노라고 그에게 알려 주었다."], ["Huon inquired the reason of the princess’s aversion; and the woman pleased to find her chat excite so much interest, replied that it was all in consequence of a dream.", "위옹이 공주가 그토록 싫어하는 까닭을 묻자, 자기 수다가 이토록 큰 관심을 불러일으키는 것이 흐뭇했던 그 여인은 그것이 모두 어떤 꿈 때문이라고 대답했다."], ["“A dream!”", "“꿈이라고요!”"], ["exclaimed Huon.", "위옹이 외쳤다."], ["“Yes! a dream.", "“그렇답니다, 꿈이지요."], ["She dreamed that she was a hind, and that the Prince, as a hunter, was pursuing her, and had almost overtaken her, when a beautiful dwarf appeared in view, drawn in a golden car, having by his side a young man of yellow hair and fair complexion, like one from a foreign land.", "공주님은 자기가 암사슴이 되고, 사냥꾼인 그 왕자가 자기를 뒤쫓아 거의 따라잡으려는 찰나에, 황금 수레를 타고 가는 아름다운 난쟁이 하나가 나타나는 꿈을 꾸셨답니다. 그 난쟁이 곁에는 노란 머리에 살결이 흰, 마치 이국에서 온 듯한 젊은이가 있었다지요."], ["She dreamed that the car stopped where she stood, and that, having resumed her own form, she was about to ascend it, when suddenly it faded from her view, and with it the dwarf and the fair-haired youth.", "공주님은 자기가 서 있는 곳에 그 수레가 멈추었고, 자기가 본디 모습을 되찾아 막 그 수레에 오르려는 순간, 갑자기 그것이 시야에서 사라지더니 그와 함께 난쟁이도 노란 머리의 젊은이도 자취를 감추는 꿈을 꾸셨답니다."], ["But from her heart that vision did not fade, and from that time her affianced bridegroom, the Hyrcanian prince, had become odious to her sight.", "그러나 그 환영만은 공주님의 마음에서 사라지지 않았고, 그때부터 약혼자인 그 히르카니아 왕자가 공주님의 눈에 가증스럽게 비치게 되었지요."], ["Yet the Sultan, her father, by no means regarding such a cause as sufficient to prevent the marriage, had named the morrow as the time when it should be solemnized, in presence of his court and many princes of the neighboring countries, whom the fame of the princess’s beauty and the bridegroom’s splendor had brought to the scene.”", "그렇지만 공주님의 아버지인 술탄은 그런 까닭을 혼인을 막을 만한 충분한 이유로 결코 여기지 않으셨고, 자기 궁정과 인근 여러 나라의 많은 군주들 앞에서 혼례를 엄숙히 치를 시기로 이튿날을 지목하셨답니다. 공주님의 미모와 신랑의 화려함에 대한 명성이 그 군주들을 그 자리로 불러들였던 것이지요.”"]], [["We may suppose this conversation woke a tumult of thoughts in the breast of Huon.", "이 이야기가 위옹의 가슴속에 온갖 상념의 소용돌이를 불러일으켰으리라고 우리는 짐작할 수 있다."], ["Was it not clear that Providence led him on, and cleared the way for his happy success?", "섭리가 그를 이끌어 그의 행복한 성공으로 가는 길을 닦아 주고 있다는 것이 분명하지 않은가?"], ["Sleep did not early visit the eyes of Huon that night; but, with the sanguine temper of youth, he indulged his fancy in imagining the sequel of his strange experience.", "그날 밤 잠은 위옹의 눈을 일찍 찾아오지 않았다. 다만 그는 청춘 특유의 낙천적인 기질로, 자신의 기이한 경험이 어떻게 이어질지를 상상하며 공상에 잠겼다."]], [["The next day, which he could not but regard as the decisive day of his fate, he prepared to deliver the message of Charlemagne.", "이튿날, 자신의 운명을 결정짓는 날이라 여길 수밖에 없는 그날, 그는 샤를마뉴의 전갈을 전할 채비를 했다."], ["Clad in his armor, fortified with his ivory horn and his ring, he reached the palace of Gaudisso when the guests were assembled at the banquet.", "갑옷을 차려입고, 상아 뿔피리와 반지로 든든히 무장한 채, 그는 손님들이 연회에 모여 있을 때 가우디소의 궁전에 다다랐다."], ["As he approached the gate a voice called on all true believers to enter; and Huon, the brave and faithful Huon, in his impatience passed in under that false pretention.", "그가 문에 다가서자, 모든 진실한 신자들은 들어오라고 외치는 목소리가 들렸다. 그리고 위옹은, 그 용감하고 충실한 위옹은, 조바심이 난 나머지 그 거짓된 명목 아래 안으로 들어서고 말았다."], ["He had no sooner passed the barrier than he felt ashamed of his baseness, and was overwhelmed with regret.", "그러나 그 관문을 통과하기가 무섭게 그는 자신의 비열함이 부끄러워졌고, 회한에 압도당했다."], ["To make amends for his fault he ran forward to the second gate, and cried to the porter, “Dog of a misbeliever, I command you in the name of Him who died on the cross, open to me!”", "자신의 잘못을 만회하고자 그는 두 번째 문으로 달려가 문지기에게 외쳤다. “이 불신자 개 같으니, 십자가에서 돌아가신 그분의 이름으로 명하노니, 내게 문을 열어라!”"], ["The points of a hundred weapons immediately opposed his passage.", "즉시 백 개나 되는 무기의 끝이 그의 통행을 가로막았다."], ["Huon then remembered for the first time the ring he had received from his uncle, the Governor.", "그제야 위옹은 자기 숙부인 총독에게서 받은 반지를 처음으로 떠올렸다."], ["He produced it, and demanded to be led to the Sultan’s presence.", "그는 그것을 내보이며 술탄 앞으로 안내해 줄 것을 요구했다."], ["The officer of the guard recognized the ring, made a respectful obeisance, and allowed him free entrance.", "호위 장교는 그 반지를 알아보고 공손히 절하며 그를 자유로이 들여보내 주었다."], ["In the same way he passed the other doors to the rich saloon where the great Sultan was at dinner with his tributary princes.", "같은 식으로 그는 다른 문들을 지나, 위대한 술탄이 자신의 속국 군주들과 함께 만찬을 들고 있는 호화로운 홀에 이르렀다."], ["At sight of the ring the chief attendant led Huon to the head of the hall, and introduced him to the Sultan and his princes as the ambassador of Charlemagne.", "반지를 본 시종장은 위옹을 홀의 상석으로 안내하여, 술탄과 그의 군주들에게 그를 샤를마뉴의 사신으로 소개했다."], ["A seat was provided for him near the royal party.", "왕족들 가까이에 그를 위한 자리가 마련되었다."]], [["The Prince of Hyrcania, the same whom Huon had rescued from the lion, and who was the destined bridegroom of the beautiful Clarimunda, sat on the Sultan’s right hand, and the princess herself on his left.", "위옹이 사자에게서 구해 주었던 바로 그 인물이자 아름다운 클라리문다의 정해진 신랑인 히르카니아의 왕자는 술탄의 오른편에 앉아 있었고, 공주는 그의 왼편에 앉아 있었다."], ["It chanced that Huon found himself near the seat of the princess, and hardly were the ceremonies of reception over before he made haste to fulfill the commands of Charlemagne by imprinting a kiss upon her rosy lips, and after that a second, not by command, but by good will.", "마침 위옹은 공주의 자리 가까이에 앉게 되었는데, 영접의 예가 끝나기가 무섭게 그는 그녀의 장밋빛 입술에 입맞춤을 새김으로써 샤를마뉴의 명을 서둘러 이행했고, 그런 다음 두 번째로 입맞춤을 했으니, 이번에는 명에 따른 것이 아니라 자신의 호의에 따른 것이었다. 히르카니아의 왕자가 외쳤다."], ["The Prince of Hyrcania cried out, “Audacious infidel!", "“뻔뻔한 이교도 놈, 네 무례함의 대가를 받아라!”"], ["take the reward of thy insolence!” and aimed a blow at Huon, which, if it had reached him, would have brought his embassy to a speedy termination.", "그러면서 위옹에게 일격을 겨누었는데, 만일 그것이 명중했더라면 그의 사절 임무는 단숨에 끝장났을 것이다."], ["But the ingrate failed of his aim, and Huon punished his blasphemy and ingratitude at once by a blow which severed his head from his body.", "그러나 그 배은망덕한 자는 겨냥을 놓쳤고, 위옹은 그의 머리를 몸에서 베어 떨어뜨리는 일격으로 그의 모독과 배은망덕을 단번에 응징했다."]], [["So suddenly had all this happened that no hand had been raised to arrest it; but now Gaudisso cried out, “Seize the murderer!”", "이 모든 일이 너무도 갑작스레 벌어진지라 누구도 그것을 막으려 손을 들지 못했다. 그러나 이제 가우디소가 외쳤다."], ["Huon was hemmed in on all sides, but his redoubtable sword kept the crowd of courtiers at bay.", "“저 살인자를 잡아라!” 위옹은 사방에서 포위되었으나, 그의 가공할 칼이 몰려드는 신하들의 무리를 막아 냈다."], ["But he saw new combatants enter, and could not hope to maintain his ground against so many.", "그러나 그는 새로운 전투원들이 들어오는 것을 보았고, 그토록 많은 자들을 상대로 자기 자리를 지킬 수 있으리라 기대할 수 없었다."], ["He recollected his horn, and raising it to his lips, blew a blast almost as loud as that of Roland at Roncesvalles.", "그는 자신의 뿔피리를 떠올리고, 그것을 입술로 가져가 롤랑이 롱스보에서 불었던 것에 거의 맞먹을 만큼 우렁찬 가락을 불었다."], ["It was in vain.", "그러나 헛수고였다."], ["Oberon heard it; but the sin of which Huon had been guilty in bearing, though but for a moment, the character of a believer in the false prophet, had put it out of Oberon’s power to help him.", "오베론은 그 소리를 들었다. 그러나 비록 한순간이나마 거짓 예언자를 믿는 신자 행세를 했다는, 위옹이 저지른 그 죄가 오베론으로 하여금 그를 도울 힘을 잃게 만들었던 것이다."], ["Huon, finding himself deserted, and conscious of the cause, lost his strength and energy, was seized, loaded with chains, and plunged into a dungeon.", "위옹은 자기가 버림받았음을 깨닫고 그 까닭을 의식하자 힘과 기력을 잃었고, 붙잡혀 사슬에 묶인 채 지하 감옥에 던져졌다."], ["His life was spared for the time, merely that he might be reserved for a more painful death.", "그의 목숨은 한동안 살려 두었으나, 그것은 다만 그를 더 고통스러운 죽음을 위해 남겨 두려는 것이었다."], ["The Sultan meant that, after being made to feel all the torments of hunger and despair, he should be flayed alive.", "술탄은 그에게 굶주림과 절망의 온갖 고통을 맛보게 한 뒤 산 채로 가죽을 벗길 작정이었다."]], [["But an enchanter more ancient and more powerful than Oberon himself interested himself for the brave Huon.", "그러나 오베론 자신보다도 더 오래되고 더 강력한 마법사 하나가 용감한 위옹에게 관심을 기울였다."], ["The enchanter was Love.", "그 마법사는 사랑이었다."], ["The Princess Clarimunda learned with horror the fate to which the young prince was destined.", "클라리문다 공주는 그 젊은 왕자에게 정해진 운명을 듣고 소스라쳤다."], ["By the aid of her governante she gained over the keeper of the prison, and went herself to lighten the chains of her beloved.", "그녀는 자기 가정교사의 도움을 받아 감옥지기를 자기편으로 끌어들이고는, 사랑하는 이의 사슬을 덜어 주고자 몸소 그를 찾아갔다."], ["It was her hand that removed his fetters, from her he received supplies of food to sustain a life which he devoted from thenceforth wholly to her.", "그의 족쇄를 풀어 준 것은 그녀의 손이었고, 그가 그 후로 온전히 그녀에게 바친 목숨을 부지하게 해 줄 양식을 받은 것도 그녀에게서였다."], ["After the most tender explanations the princess departed, promising to repeat her visit on the morrow.", "더없이 다정한 해명을 나눈 뒤 공주는 떠나면서 이튿날 다시 찾아오겠노라 약속했다."]], [["The next day she came according to promise, and again brought supplies of food.", "이튿날 그녀는 약속대로 찾아왔고, 다시 양식을 가져왔다."], ["These visits were continued during a whole month.", "이러한 방문은 꼬박 한 달 동안 이어졌다."], ["Huon was too good a son of the Church to forget that the amiable princess was a Saracen, and he availed himself of these interviews to instruct her in the true faith.", "위옹은 너무도 훌륭한 교회의 아들이었던지라, 그 사랑스러운 공주가 사라센인이라는 사실을 잊지 않았고, 이러한 만남을 이용하여 그녀에게 참된 신앙을 가르쳤다."], ["How easy it is to believe the truth when uttered by the lips of those we love!", "사랑하는 이의 입술에서 흘러나올 때, 진리를 믿기란 얼마나 쉬운 일인가!"], ["Clarimunda ere long professed her entire belief in the Christian doctrines, and desired to be baptized.", "클라리문다는 머지않아 기독교 교리에 대한 자신의 온전한 믿음을 고백하고 세례받기를 원했다."]], [["Meanwhile the Sultan had repeatedly inquired of the jailer how his prisoner bore the pains of famine, and learned to his surprise that he was not yet much reduced thereby.", "한편 술탄은 자신의 죄수가 굶주림의 고통을 어찌 견디는지를 거듭 감옥지기에게 물었고, 죄수가 아직 그로 인해 별로 쇠약해지지 않았다는 것을 알고 놀랐다."], ["On his repeating the inquiry, after a short interval, the keeper replied that the prisoner had died suddenly, and had been buried in the cavern.", "그가 잠시 뒤에 그 물음을 다시 던지자, 감옥지기는 죄수가 갑자기 죽어 동굴에 묻혔노라 대답했다."], ["The Sultan could only regret that he had not sooner ordered the execution of the sentence.", "술탄은 그 선고의 집행을 더 일찍 명하지 않은 것을 그저 후회할 따름이었다."]], [["While these things were going on the faithful Sherasmin, who had not accompanied Huon in his last adventure, but had learned by common rumor the result of it, came to the court in hopes of doing something for the rescue of his master.", "이런 일들이 벌어지는 동안, 위옹의 마지막 모험에는 동행하지 않았으나 떠도는 소문으로 그 결과를 알게 된 충직한 셰라스맹은 자기 주인을 구할 무언가를 해 보겠다는 희망을 품고 궁정으로 찾아왔다."], ["He presented himself to the Sultan as Solario, his nephew.", "그는 술탄에게 자신을 그의 조카 솔라리오라고 소개했다."], ["Guadisso received him with kindness, and all the courtiers loaded him with attentions.", "가우디소는 그를 친절히 맞아들였고, 모든 신하들이 그에게 온갖 관심을 쏟았다."], ["He soon found means to inform himself how the Princess regarded the brave but unfortunate Huon, and having made himself known to her, confidence was soon established between them.", "그는 곧 공주가 그 용감하지만 불운한 위옹을 어찌 여기는지를 알아낼 방도를 찾았고, 그녀에게 자기 정체를 밝히자 이내 둘 사이에 신뢰가 형성되었다."], ["Clarimunda readily consented to assist in the escape of Huon, and to quit with him her father’s court to repair to that of Charlemagne.", "클라리문다는 위옹의 탈출을 돕고 그와 함께 자기 아버지의 궁정을 떠나 샤를마뉴의 궁정으로 가는 데 흔쾌히 동의했다."], ["Their united efforts had nearly perfected their arrangement, a vessel was secretly prepared, and all things in forwardness for the flight, when an unlooked-for obstacle presented itself.", "그들이 힘을 합쳐 거의 모든 준비를 마치고, 배 한 척을 은밀히 마련하여 도주를 위한 만반의 채비를 갖추었을 때, 뜻밖의 장애가 나타났다."], ["Huon himself positively refused to go leaving the orders of Charlemagne unexecuted.", "위옹 자신이 샤를마뉴의 명령을 이행하지 않은 채로 떠나기를 단호히 거부했던 것이다."]], [["Sherasmin was in despair.", "셰라스맹은 절망에 빠졌다."], ["Bitterly he complained of the fickleness and cruelty of Oberon in withdrawing his aid at the very crisis when it was most necessary.", "그는 가장 필요한 바로 그 위기의 순간에 도움을 거두어 버린 오베론의 변덕과 잔혹함을 쓰라리게 한탄했다."], ["Earnestly he urged every argument to satisfy the prince that he had done enough for honor, and could not be held bound to achieve impossibilities.", "그는 왕자에게, 명예를 위해 할 만큼 했으며 불가능한 일을 이루어야 할 의무까지 짊어질 수는 없다는 것을 납득시키고자 온갖 논거를 들어 간곡히 설득했다."], ["But all was of no avail, and he knew not which way to turn, when one of those events occurred which are so frequent under Turkish despotisms.", "그러나 모두 헛일이었고, 그가 어찌할 바를 모르던 차에, 튀르크의 전제 정치 아래서 그토록 흔히 일어나는 그런 사건들 중 하나가 발생했다."], ["A courier arrived at the court of the Sultan, bearing the ring of his sovereign, the mighty Agrapard, Caliph of Arabia, and bringing the bow-string for the neck of Gaudisso.", "사자(使者) 하나가 술탄의 궁정에 당도했는데, 그는 자신의 군주이자 아라비아의 칼리프인 강대한 아그라파르의 반지를 지니고, 가우디소의 목에 채울 활시위를 가져왔다. 아무런 이유도 밝혀지지 않았다."], ["No reason was assigned; none but the pleasure of the Caliph is ever required in such cases; but it was suspected that the bearer of the bow-string had persuaded the Caliph that Gaudisso, whose rapacity was well known, had accumulated immense treasures, which he had not duly shared with his sovereign, and thus had obtained an order to supersede him in his Emirship.", "이러한 경우 칼리프의 뜻 외에는 그 어떤 것도 요구된 적이 없었다. 다만 사람들은 그 활시위를 가져온 자가 칼리프에게, 탐욕으로 잘 알려진 가우디소가 막대한 보물을 쌓아 두고도 그것을 자신의 군주와 마땅히 나누지 않았다고 설득하여, 그를 에미르 자리에서 갈아 치울 명령을 얻어 냈으리라고 짐작했다."]], [["The body of Gaudisso would have been cast out a prey to dogs and vultures, had not Sherasmin, under the character of nephew of the deceased, been permitted to receive it, and give it decent burial, which he did, but not till he had taken possession of the beard and grinders, agreeably to the orders of Charlemagne.", "가우디소의 시신은 개와 독수리의 먹이로 내던져졌을 터이나, 셰라스맹이 고인의 조카 자격으로 그것을 인수하여 점잖게 매장하도록 허락받았기에 그렇게 되지 않았다. 그는 그렇게 했으되, 다만 샤를마뉴의 명령에 따라 수염과 어금니를 챙긴 다음에야 그리했다."]], [["No obstacle now stood in the way of the lovers and their faithful follower in returning to France.", "이제 연인들과 그들의 충직한 종자가 프랑스로 돌아가는 길에는 아무런 장애도 남아 있지 않았다."], ["They sailed, taking Rome in their way, where the Holy Father himself blessed the union of his nephew, Duke Huon of Bordeaux, with the Princess Clarimunda.", "그들은 도중에 로마를 거치며 배로 떠났는데, 그곳에서는 교황 성하 친히 자신의 조카인 보르도의 위옹 공작과 클라리문다 공주의 결합을 축복했다."]], [["Soon afterward they arrived in France, where Huon laid his trophies at the feet of Charlemagne, and, being restored to the favor of the Emperor, hastened to present himself and his bride to the Duchess, his mother, and to the faithful liegemen of his province of Guienne and his city of Bordeaux, where the pair were received with transports of joy.", "그 후 머지않아 그들은 프랑스에 당도했고, 그곳에서 위옹은 자신의 전리품들을 샤를마뉴의 발치에 바쳤다. 그리고 황제의 총애를 되찾은 뒤, 자신의 어머니인 공작 부인과, 자신의 영지인 기엔 지방과 자신의 도시인 보르도의 충직한 신민들에게 자기와 자기 신부를 서둘러 선보였다. 그곳에서 그 부부는 환희에 찬 환영을 받았다. 덴마크인 오지에"]], [["# OGIER, THE DANE", "# OGIER, THE DANE"]], [["————\nOgier, the Dane, was the son of Geoffroy, who wrested Denmark from the Pagans, and reigned the first Christian king of that country.", "덴마크인 오지에는 이교도들에게서 덴마크를 빼앗아 그 나라 최초의 기독교 왕으로 다스린 조프루아의 아들이었다."], ["When Ogier was born, and before he was baptized, six ladies of ravishing beauty appeared all at once in the chamber of the infant.", "오지에가 태어나, 아직 세례받기 전에, 더없이 아름다운 여섯 명의 부인이 갑자기 그 아기의 방에 일제히 나타났다."], ["They encircled him, and she who appeared the eldest took him in her arms, kissed him, and laid her hand upon his heart.", "그들은 아기를 에워쌌고, 그중 가장 나이 들어 보이는 부인이 아기를 두 팔에 안고 입맞춘 뒤 그의 심장에 손을 얹었다."], ["“I give you,”", "“너에게 주노라.”"], ["said she, “to be the bravest warrior of your times.”", "그녀가 말했다. “네 시대의 가장 용맹한 전사가 되기를.”"], ["She delivered the infant to her sister, who said, “I give you abundant opportunities to display your valor.”", "그녀가 아기를 자기 자매에게 건네자, 그 자매가 말했다. “너에게 주노라, 네 용맹을 펼칠 풍성한 기회들을.”"], ["“Sister,”", "“자매여.”"], ["said the third lady, “you have given him a dangerous boon; I give him that he shall never be vanquished.”", "세 번째 부인이 말했다. “당신은 이 아이에게 위험한 선물을 주었구려."], ["The fourth sister added, as she laid her hand upon his eyes and his mouth, “I give you the gift of pleasing.”", "나는 이 아이에게 결코 패배하지 않을 것을 주노라.” 네 번째 자매는 아기의 두 눈과 입에 손을 얹으며 덧붙였다."], ["The fifth said, “Lest all these gifts serve only to betray, I give you sensibility to return the love you inspire.”", "“너에게 주노라, 남을 기쁘게 하는 재능을.” 다섯 번째 부인이 말했다."], ["Then spoke Morgana, the youngest and handsomest of the group.", "“이 모든 선물이 자칫 배신의 도구가 되지 않도록, 나는 너에게 주노라, 네가 불러일으킨 사랑에 보답할 줄 아는 감수성을.” 그러자 그 무리 가운데 가장 어리고 가장 아름다운 모르가나가 말했다."], ["“Charming creature, I claim you for my own; and I give you not to die till you shall have come to pay me a visit in my isle of Avalon.”", "“매력적인 아이여, 나는 너를 나의 것으로 삼노라. 그리고 나는 너에게, 나의 섬 아발론으로 나를 찾아오기 전에는 결코 죽지 않을 것을 주노라.”"], ["Then she kissed the child and departed with her sisters.", "그런 다음 그녀는 아기에게 입맞추고 자매들과 함께 떠났다."]], [["After this the king had the child carried to the font and baptized with the name of Ogier.", "그 후 왕은 아기를 세례반으로 데려가 오지에라는 이름으로 세례받게 했다."]], [["In his education nothing was neglected to elevate him to the standard of a perfect knight, and render him accomplished in all the arts necessary to make him a hero.", "그의 교육에 있어서는, 그를 완벽한 기사의 기준으로 끌어올리고 영웅을 만드는 데 필요한 온갖 기예에 능통하게 만들기 위해 어느 것 하나 소홀히 하지 않았다."]], [["He had hardly reached the age of sixteen years when Charlemagne, whose power was established over all the sovereigns of his time, recollected that Geoffroy, Ogier’s father, had omitted to render the homage due to him as Emperor, and sovereign lord of Denmark, one of the grand fiefs of the empire.", "그가 열여섯 살에 채 이르기도 전에, 당대의 모든 군주들에 대해 권력을 확립해 둔 샤를마뉴는, 오지에의 아버지 조프루아가 황제이자 제국의 큰 봉토 중 하나인 덴마크의 종주에게 바쳐야 할 신종(臣從)의 예를 다하지 않았음을 떠올렸다."], ["He accordingly sent an embassy to demand of the king of Denmark this homage, and on receiving a refusal, couched in haughty terms, sent an army to enforce the demand.", "그리하여 그는 덴마크 왕에게 이 신종의 예를 요구하고자 사절단을 보냈고, 오만한 어조로 표현된 거절을 받자, 그 요구를 강제하기 위해 군대를 보냈다."], ["Geoffroy, after an unsuccessful resistance, was forced to comply, and as a pledge of his sincerity delivered Ogier, his eldest son, a hostage to Charles, to be brought up at his court.", "조프루아는 저항에 실패한 끝에 따를 수밖에 없게 되었고, 자신의 진심을 보증하는 담보로 자신의 맏아들 오지에를 샤를에게 인질로 넘겨, 그의 궁정에서 양육받도록 했다."], ["He was placed in charge of the Duke Namo of Bavaria, the friend of his father, who treated him like his own son.", "그는 자기 아버지의 친구인 바이에른의 나모 공작에게 맡겨졌는데, 나모 공작은 그를 자기 친아들처럼 대했다."]], [["Ogier grew up more and more handsome and amiable every day.", "오지에는 나날이 더욱 잘생기고 더욱 사랑스럽게 자랐다."], ["He surpassed in form, strength, and address all the noble youths his companions; he failed not to be present at all tourneys; he was attentive to the elder knights, and burned with impatience to imitate them.", "그는 외모에서나 힘에서나 솜씨에서나 자기 동무인 모든 고귀한 젊은이들을 능가했다. 그는 모든 마상 시합에 빠짐없이 참석했고, 손위 기사들에게 정성을 다했으며, 그들을 본받고 싶은 조바심으로 가슴이 타올랐다."], ["Yet his heart rose sometimes in secret against his condition as a hostage, and as one apparently forgotten by his father.", "그러나 그의 마음은 이따금 남몰래, 인질이라는 자신의 처지와 아버지에게 잊힌 듯한 자신의 신세에 맞서 들끓곤 했다."]], [["The King of Denmark, in fact, was at this time occupied with new loves.", "사실 덴마크 왕은 이 무렵 새로운 사랑에 빠져 있었다."], ["Ogier’s mother having died, he had married a second wife, and had a son named Guyon.", "오지에의 어머니가 죽자 그는 두 번째 아내를 맞이하여 기용이라는 아들을 두었다."], ["The new queen had absolute power over her husband, and fearing that, if he should see Ogier again, he would give him the preference over Guyon, she had adroitly persuaded him to delay rendering his homage to Charlemagne, till now four years had passed away since the last renewal of that ceremony.", "새 왕비는 자기 남편에게 절대적인 권력을 휘둘렀으며, 만일 그가 오지에를 다시 본다면 기용보다 그를 더 아끼게 되리라 두려워하여, 그가 샤를마뉴에게 신종의 예를 바치는 일을 미루도록 교묘히 설득했으니, 이제 그 의식을 마지막으로 새로이 치른 이래 사 년이 흘러 버렸다."], ["Charlemagne, irritated at this delinquency, drew closer the bonds of Ogier’s captivity until he should receive a response from the king of Denmark to a fresh summons which he caused to be sent to him.", "이러한 태만에 격분한 샤를마뉴는, 자신이 덴마크 왕에게 보낸 새로운 소환에 대한 답을 받을 때까지 오지에를 더욱 엄중히 가두어 두었다."]], [["The answer of Geoffroy was insulting and defiant, and the rage of Charlemagne was roused in the highest degree.", "조프루아의 대답은 모욕적이고 도전적이었으며, 샤를마뉴의 분노는 극에 달했다."], ["He was at first disposed to wreak his vengeance upon Ogier, his hostage; but at the entreaties of Duke Namo, who felt towards his pupil like a father, consented to spare his life, if Ogier would swear fidelity to him as his liege-lord, and promise not to quit his court without his permission.", "그는 처음에는 자신의 인질인 오지에에게 분풀이를 하려 했다. 그러나 자기 제자에게 아버지와 같은 마음을 품고 있던 나모 공작의 간청에 따라, 오지에가 그를 자신의 주군으로 섬기겠노라 충성을 맹세하고 자신의 허락 없이는 궁정을 떠나지 않겠노라 약속한다면 그의 목숨을 살려 주기로 동의했다."], ["Ogier accepted these terms, and was allowed to retain all the freedom he had before enjoyed.", "오지에는 이 조건을 받아들였고, 전에 누리던 모든 자유를 그대로 누리도록 허락받았다."]], [["The Emperor would have immediately taken arms to reduce his disobedient vassal, if he had not been called off in another direction by a message from Pope Leo, imploring his assistance.", "황제는 즉시 무기를 들어 자신의 불복하는 봉신을 굴복시키려 했을 것이나, 다른 방향에서 교황 레오의 전갈에 불려 가게 되었으니, 그것은 그의 도움을 청하는 것이었다."], ["The Saracens had landed in the neighborhood of Rome, occupied Mount Janiculum, and prepared to pass the Tiber and carry fire and sword to the capital of the Christian world.", "사라센인들이 로마 인근에 상륙하여 야니쿨룸 언덕을 점령하고는, 티베르 강을 건너 기독교 세계의 수도에 불과 칼을 들이댈 채비를 하고 있었던 것이다."], ["Charlemagne hesitated not to yield to the entreaties of the Pope.", "샤를마뉴는 주저 없이 교황의 간청에 응했다."], ["He speedily assembled an army, crossed the Alps, traversed Italy, and arrived at Spoleto, a strong place to which the Pope had retired.", "그는 신속히 군대를 소집하여 알프스를 넘고 이탈리아를 가로질러, 교황이 피신해 있던 견고한 요새 스폴레토에 다다랐다."], ["Leo, at the head of his Cardinals, advanced to meet him, and rendered him homage, as to the son of Pepin, the illustrious protector of the Holy See, coming, as his father had done, to defend it in the hour of need.", "레오는 추기경들을 거느리고 그를 맞이하러 나아가, 마치 지난날 자기 아버지가 그러했듯 위급한 순간에 교황청을 지키러 온, 교황청의 저명한 보호자 피핀의 아들에게 바치듯 그에게 경의를 표했다."], ["Charlemagne stopped but two days at Spoleto, and learning that the Infidels, having rendered themselves masters of Rome, were besieging the Capitol, which could not long hold out against them, marched promptly to attack them.", "샤를마뉴는 스폴레토에 이틀밖에 머물지 않았고, 이교도들이 로마를 손에 넣고 오래 버티지 못할 카피톨리움을 포위하고 있다는 것을 알게 되자, 신속히 그들을 공격하러 진군했다."]], [["The advanced posts of the army were commanded by Duke Namo, on whom Ogier waited as his squire.", "군대의 전위는 나모 공작이 지휘했고, 오지에는 그의 종자로서 그를 시중들었다."], ["He did not yet bear arms, not having received the order of knighthood.", "그는 아직 무기를 들지는 못했으니, 기사 서임을 받지 못했기 때문이다."], ["The Oriflamme, the royal standard, was borne by a knight named Alory, who showed himself unworthy of the honor.", "왕의 군기인 오리플람은 알로리라는 이름의 기사가 들었는데, 그는 자신이 그 영예에 걸맞지 않은 자임을 드러냈다."], ["Duke Namo, seeing a strong body of the Infidels advancing to attack him, gave the word to charge them.", "나모 공작은 이교도들의 강력한 일대가 자기를 공격하려 진격해 오는 것을 보고 그들을 향해 돌격하라는 명을 내렸다."], ["Ogier remained in the rear, with the other youths, grieving much that he was not permitted to fight.", "오지에는 다른 젊은이들과 함께 후방에 남아, 싸움이 허락되지 않은 것을 몹시 안타까워했다."], ["Very soon he saw Alory lower the Oriflamme, and turn his horse in flight.", "이내 그는 알로리가 오리플람을 내리고 말머리를 돌려 달아나는 것을 보았다."], ["Ogier pointed him out to the young men, and seizing a club, rushed upon Alory and struck him from his horse.", "오지에는 그자를 젊은이들에게 가리키고는, 곤봉 하나를 집어 들고 알로리에게 달려들어 그를 말에서 쳐 떨어뜨렸다."], ["Then, with his companions, he disarmed him, clothed himself in his armor, raised the Oriflamme, and mounting the horse of the unworthy knight, flew to the front rank, where he joined Duke Namo, drove back the Infidels, and carried the Oriflamme quite through their broken ranks.", "그런 다음 동무들과 함께 그의 무장을 벗기고 자신이 그 갑옷을 걸친 뒤, 오리플람을 치켜들고 그 못난 기사의 말에 올라타, 전열의 맨 앞으로 날듯이 달려가 나모 공작에게 합류하여 이교도들을 물리치고, 무너진 그들의 대열을 가로질러 오리플람을 끝까지 운반해 갔다."], ["The Duke, thinking it was Alory, whom he had not held in high esteem, was astonished at his strength and valor.", "공작은 자기가 그다지 높이 평가하지 않던 알로리가 그러는 줄로 알고, 그의 힘과 용맹에 깜짝 놀랐다."], ["Ogier’s young companions imitated him, supplying themselves with armor from the bodies of the slain; they followed Ogier and carried death into the ranks of the Saracens, who fell back in confusion upon their main body.", "오지에의 젊은 동무들도 그를 본받아 쓰러진 자들의 시신에서 갑옷을 마련해 걸쳤다. 그들은 오지에를 뒤따라 사라센인들의 대열에 죽음을 안겼고, 사라센인들은 혼란에 빠진 채 자기들의 본대로 물러났다."]], [["Duke Namo now ordered a retreat, and Ogier obeyed with reluctance, when they perceived Charlemagne advancing to their assistance.", "이제 나모 공작은 퇴각을 명했고, 오지에는 마지못해 그에 따랐는데, 그때 그들은 샤를마뉴가 자기들을 도우러 진격해 오는 것을 알아보았다."], ["The combat now became general, and was more terrible than ever.", "이제 전투는 전면전이 되었고 그 어느 때보다도 무시무시해졌다."], ["Charlemagne had overthrown Corsuble, the commander of the Saracens, and had drawn his famous sword, Joyeuse, to cut off his head, when two Saracen knights set upon him at once, one of whom slew his horse, and the other overthrew the Emperor on the sand.", "샤를마뉴는 사라센인들의 지휘관 코르수블을 쓰러뜨리고, 그의 머리를 베어 내고자 자신의 그 유명한 칼 주아외즈를 뽑아 들었는데, 그때 두 명의 사라센 기사가 한꺼번에 그에게 덤벼들어, 하나는 그의 말을 죽이고 다른 하나는 황제를 모래밭에 쓰러뜨렸다."], ["Perceiving by the eagle on his casque who he was, they dismounted in haste to give him his deathblow.", "그들은 그의 투구 위에 새겨진 독수리를 보고 그가 누구인지를 알아채고는, 그에게 마지막 일격을 가하려 황급히 말에서 내렸다."], ["Never was the life of the Emperor in such peril.", "황제의 목숨이 이토록 위태로웠던 적은 없었다."], ["But Ogier, who saw him fall, flew to his rescue.", "그러나 그가 쓰러지는 것을 본 오지에가 그를 구하러 날아왔다."], ["Though embarrassed with the Oriflamme, he pushed his horse against one of the Saracens and knocked him down; and with his sword dealt the other so vigorous a blow that he fell stunned to the earth.", "그는 오리플람을 든 채로 거추장스러웠음에도 자기 말을 사라센인 가운데 하나에게 밀어붙여 그를 쓰러뜨렸고, 칼로 다른 하나에게 어찌나 세찬 일격을 가했던지 그자는 정신을 잃고 땅에 나뒹굴었다."], ["Then helping the Emperor to rise, he remounted him on the horse of one of the fallen knights.", "그런 다음 황제가 일어서도록 도와, 쓰러진 기사들 가운데 하나의 말에 그를 다시 태웠다."], ["“Brave and generous Alory!”", "“용감하고 너그러운 알로리여!”"], ["Charles exclaimed, “I owe to you my honor and my life!”", "샤를이 외쳤다. “나는 그대에게 내 명예와 목숨을 빚졌소!”"], ["Ogier made no answer; but, leaving Charlemagne surrounded by a great many of the knights who had flown to his succor, he plunged into the thickest ranks of the enemy, and carried the Oriflamme, followed by a gallant train of youthful warriors till the standard of Mahomet turned in retreat, and the Infidels sought safety in their intrenchments.", "오지에는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 다만 그는 샤를마뉴를 그를 구하러 날아온 수많은 기사들에게 에워싸인 채로 남겨 두고, 적의 가장 두터운 대열 속으로 뛰어들어, 젊은 전사들의 늠름한 무리를 거느리고 오리플람을 운반해 갔으니, 마침내 마호메트의 군기가 퇴각으로 돌아서고 이교도들이 자기들의 참호 속에서 안전을 구할 때까지 그리했다."]], [["Then the good Archbishop Turpin laid aside his helmet and his bloody sword (for he always felt that he was clearly in the line of his duty while slaying Infidels), took his mitre and his crosier, and intoned Te Deum.", "그제야 선량한 튀르팽 대주교는 자신의 투구와 피 묻은 칼을 내려놓고(그는 이교도들을 베어 죽이는 동안만큼은 자신이 명백히 자기 의무의 선상에 있다고 늘 느꼈으므로), 자신의 주교관과 주교장을 들고 〈테 데움〉을 읊조렸다."]], [["At this moment Ogier, covered with blood and dust, came to lay the Oriflamme at the feet of the Emperor.", "이 순간 피와 먼지로 뒤덮인 오지에가 오리플람을 황제의 발치에 내려놓으러 왔다."], ["He was followed by a train of warriors of short stature, who walked ill at ease loaded with armor too heavy for them.", "그의 뒤로는 키가 작은 전사들의 무리가 따라왔는데, 그들은 자기들에게 너무 무거운 갑옷을 짊어진 채 불편하게 걸었다."], ["Ogier knelt at the feet of Charlemagne, who embraced him, calling him Alory, while Turpin from the height of the altar, blessed him with all his might.", "오지에가 샤를마뉴의 발치에 무릎을 꿇자, 황제는 그를 알로리라 부르며 끌어안았고, 한편 튀르팽은 제단 높은 곳에서 온 힘을 다해 그를 축복했다."], ["Then young Orlando, son of the Count Milone, and nephew of Charlemagne, no longer able to endure this misapprehension, threw down his helmet, and ran to unlace Ogier’s, while the other young men laid aside theirs.", "그때 밀로네 백작의 아들이자 샤를마뉴의 조카인 젊은 오를란도가 더 이상 이 오해를 견디지 못하고, 자신의 투구를 내던지고 달려가 오지에의 투구 끈을 풀었으며, 다른 젊은이들도 자기 투구를 벗었다."], ["Our author says he cannot express the surprise, the admiration, and the tenderness of the Emperor and his peers.", "우리의 저자는 황제와 그의 중신들의 놀라움과 감탄과 애틋함을 이루 다 표현할 수 없노라고 말한다."], ["Charles folded Ogier in his arms, and the happy fathers of those brave youths embraced them with tears of joy.", "샤를은 오지에를 두 팔로 끌어안았고, 그 용감한 젊은이들의 행복한 아버지들은 기쁨의 눈물을 흘리며 자기 아들들을 껴안았다."], ["The good Duke Namo stepped forward, and Charlemagne yielded Ogier to his embrace.", "선량한 나모 공작이 앞으로 나서자, 샤를마뉴는 오지에를 그의 포옹에 내주었다. “내가 그대에게 얼마나 큰 빚을 졌는지!”"], ["“How much do I owe you,”", "그가 말했다."], ["he said, “good and wise friend, for having restrained my anger!", "“선량하고 지혜로운 벗이여, 내 분노를 억눌러 준 그대에게 말이오! 사랑하는 오지에여!"], ["My dear Ogier! I owe you my life!", "나는 그대에게 내 목숨을 빚졌소!"], ["My sword leaps to touch your shoulder, yours and those of your brave young friends.”", "내 칼이 그대의 어깨를, 그대와 그대의 용감한 젊은 벗들의 어깨를 어서 닿고 싶어 펄쩍 뛰는구려.”"], ["At these words he drew that famous sword, Joyeuse, and while Ogier and the rest knelt before him, gave them the accolade conferring on them the order of knighthood.", "이렇게 말하면서 그는 그 유명한 칼 주아외즈를 뽑아, 오지에와 나머지가 자기 앞에 무릎을 꿇자 그들에게 기사 서임의 의식인 안수례를 베풀었다."], ["The young Orlando and his cousin Oliver could not refrain, even in the presence of the Emperor, from falling upon Ogier’s neck, and pledging with him that brotherhood in arms, so dear and so sacred to the knights of old times; but Charlot, the Emperor’s son, at the sight of the glory with which Ogier had covered himself, conceived the blackest jealousy and hate.", "젊은 오를란도와 그의 사촌 올리비에는 황제 앞에서조차, 오지에의 목에 매달려, 옛 기사들에게 그토록 소중하고 신성하던 그 형제의 의를 그와 더불어 맹세하지 않을 수 없었다. 그러나 황제의 아들 샤를로는 오지에가 자신을 뒤덮은 그 영광을 보고, 가장 시커먼 질투와 증오를 품게 되었다."]], [["The rest of the day and the next were spent in the rejoicings of the army.", "그날의 나머지와 그 이튿날은 군대의 환희 속에서 보내졌다."], ["Turpin in a solemn service implored the favor of Heaven upon the youthful knights, and blessed the white armor which was prepared for them.", "튀르팽은 엄숙한 예배에서 젊은 기사들에게 하늘의 은총이 내리기를 빌고, 그들을 위해 마련된 흰 갑옷을 축복했다. 나모 공작은 그들에게 황금 박차를 선사했고, 샤를 친히 그들의 칼을 채워 주었다."], ["Duke Namo presented them with golden spurs, Charles himself girded on their swords.", "그러나 오지에를 위한 칼을 살펴보았을 때, 그의 놀라움이란 어떠했겠는가!"], ["But what was his astonishment when he examined that intended for Ogier!", "사랑이 가득한 요정 모르가나가 그것을 바꿔치기하여 자기가 마련한 칼로 대신 놓아 두는 재주를 부렸던 것이다."], ["The loving Fairy, Morgana, had had the art to change it, and to substitute one of her own procuring, and when Charles drew it out of the scabbard, these words appeared written on the steel: “My name is Cortana, of the same steel and temper as Joyeuse and Durindana.”", "그래서 샤를이 그것을 칼집에서 뽑자 강철 위에 이러한 말이 새겨져 나타났다. “나의 이름은 코르타나, 주아외즈와 뒤랭다나와 같은 강철, 같은 담금질로 벼린 것이라.”"], ["Charles saw that a superior power watched over the destinies of Ogier; he vowed to love him as a father would, and Ogier promised him the devotion of a son.", "샤를은 어떤 우월한 힘이 오지에의 운명을 지켜보고 있음을 알아챘다. 그는 아버지가 그러하듯 그를 사랑하겠노라 맹세했고, 오지에는 그에게 아들의 헌신을 약속했다."], ["Happy had it been for both if they had always continued mindful of their promises.", "만일 두 사람이 늘 자신들의 약속을 잊지 않고 있었더라면, 두 사람 모두에게 행복한 일이었으리라."]], [["The Saracen army had hardly recovered from its dismay when Carahue, King of Mauritania, who was one of the knights overthrown by Ogier at the time of the rescue of Charlemagne, determined to challenge him to single combat.", "사라센 군대가 자신들의 경악에서 채 회복되기도 전에, 샤를마뉴를 구할 때 오지에에게 쓰러진 기사들 가운데 하나였던 마우리타니아의 왕 카라위는 그에게 일대일 결투를 신청하기로 결심했다."], ["With that view he assumed the dress of a herald, resolved to carry his own message.", "그러한 뜻에서 그는 자기 전갈을 직접 전하기로 마음먹고 전령의 복장을 차려입었다."], ["The French knights admired his air, and said to one another that he seemed more fit to be a knight than a bearer of messages.", "프랑스 기사들은 그의 풍채에 감탄하며 서로에게, 그는 전령이라기보다는 기사에 더 어울려 보인다고 말했다."]], [["Carahue began by passing the warmest eulogium upon the knight who bore the Oriflamme on the day of the battle, and concluded by saying that Carahue, King of Mauritania, respected that knight so much that he challenged him to the combat.", "카라위는 먼저 전투의 날에 오리플람을 들었던 그 기사에게 더없이 따뜻한 찬사를 보내는 것으로 말문을 열고는, 마우리타니아의 왕 카라위가 그 기사를 너무도 존경하는 나머지 그에게 결투를 신청한다는 말로 끝맺었다."]], [["Ogier had risen to reply, when he was interrupted by Charlot, who said that the gage of the King of Mauritania could not fitly be received by a vassal, living in captivity; by which he meant Ogier, who was at that time serving as hostage for his father.", "오지에가 대답하려 일어서는데, 샤를로가 그의 말을 가로막으며, 마우리타니아 왕의 결투 신청은 포로의 신분으로 살아가는 봉신이 받아들이기에 마땅치 않다고 말했다. 그가 뜻한 것은 오지에로, 그는 그 무렵 자기 아버지를 대신하는 인질로 지내고 있었다."], ["Fire flashed from the eyes of Ogier, but the presence of the Emperor restrained his speech, and he was calmed by the kind looks of Charlemagne, who said, with an angry voice, “Silence, Charlot!", "오지에의 두 눈에서 불꽃이 일었으나, 황제의 면전이라 그는 말을 삼켰고, 샤를마뉴의 다정한 눈길에 마음을 가라앉혔다. 샤를마뉴는 성난 목소리로 말했다."], ["By the life of Bertha, my queen, he who has saved my life is as dear to me as yourself.", "“닥치거라, 샤를로! 내 왕비 베르타의 목숨을 걸고 말하노니, 내 목숨을 구한 자는 네 자신만큼이나 내게 소중하다."], ["Ogier,”", "오지에여.”"], ["he continued, “you are no longer a hostage.", "그가 말을 이었다. “그대는 더 이상 인질이 아니다."], ["Herald!", "전령이여!"], ["report my answer to your master, that never does knight of my court refuse a challenge on equal terms.", "그대의 주인에게 내 대답을 전하라, 내 궁정의 기사는 대등한 조건의 결투를 결코 거절하지 않노라고."], ["Ogier, the Dane, accepts of his, and I myself am his security.”", "덴마크인 오지에가 그의 신청을 받아들이며, 나 자신이 그의 보증인이 되겠노라.\""]], [["Carahue, profoundly bowing, replied, “My lord, I was sure that the sentiments of so great a sovereign as yourself would be worthy of your high and brilliant fame; I shall report your answer to my master, who I know admires you, and unwillingly takes arms against you.” Then, turning to Charlot, whom he did not know as the son of the Emperor, he continued, “As for you, Sir Knight, if the desire of battle inflames you, I have it in charge from Sadon, cousin of the King of Mauritania, to give the like defiance to any French knights who will grant him the honor of the combat.”", "카라위는 깊이 절하며 대답했다. “전하, 저는 전하만큼 위대한 군주의 마음가짐이라면 응당 그 높고 빛나는 명성에 걸맞으리라 확신하고 있었습니다. 전하의 대답을 제 주인께 전하겠습니다. 제가 알기로 그분은 전하를 흠모하시며, 마지못해 전하께 맞서 무기를 드시는 것입니다.” 그런 다음 그는 샤를로가 황제의 아들인 줄을 모른 채 그에게로 돌아서서 말을 이었다. “그리고 기사여, 그대로 말하자면, 만약 전투의 욕망이 그대를 불태운다면, 나는 마우리타니아 왕의 사촌인 사동으로부터, 그에게 결투의 영예를 베풀 어느 프랑스 기사에게든 똑같이 도전을 전하라는 분부를 받았소.”"]], [["Charlot, inflamed with rage and vexation at the public reproof which he had just received, hesitated not to deliver his gage.", "방금 받은 공개적인 질책에 분노와 굴욕으로 불타오른 샤를로는 주저 없이 자신의 결투 신청을 내밀었다."], ["Carahue received it with Ogier’s, and it was agreed that the combat should be on the next day in a meadow environed by woods and equally distant from both armies.", "카라위는 그것을 오지에의 것과 함께 받아들였고, 결투는 이튿날 양쪽 진영에서 똑같이 떨어져 있는, 숲으로 둘러싸인 풀밭에서 치르기로 합의되었다."], ["The perfidious Charlot meditated the blackest treason.", "음흉한 샤를로는 가장 시커먼 배신을 꾀했다."], ["During the night he collected some knights unworthy of the name, and like himself in their ferocious manners; he made them swear to avenge his injuries, armed them in black armor, and sent them to lie in ambush in the wood, with orders to make a pretended attack upon the whole party, but in fact, to lay heavy hands upon Ogier and the two Saracens.", "밤사이 그는 그 이름에 걸맞지 않으며 자기처럼 사나운 성품을 지닌 몇몇 기사를 그러모았다. 그는 그들에게 자신의 원한을 갚아 주겠노라 맹세하게 한 뒤, 그들을 검은 갑옷으로 무장시켜 숲속에 매복하도록 보냈으니, 무리 전체를 향해 공격하는 척하되 실은 오지에와 두 사라센인에게 가차 없이 손을 쓰라는 명령과 함께였다."]], [["At the dawn of day Sadon and Carahue, attended only by two pages to carry their spears, took their way to the appointed meadow; and Charlot and Ogier repaired thither also, but by different paths.", "동틀 무렵 사동과 카라위는 자기들의 창을 들 시동 둘만을 거느린 채 약속된 풀밭으로 향했다. 그리고 샤를로와 오지에도 그리로 갔으되, 서로 다른 길을 통해서였다."], ["Ogier advanced with a calm air, saluted courteously the two Saracen knights, and joined them in arranging the terms of combat.", "오지에는 침착한 태도로 다가가 두 사라센 기사에게 정중히 인사하고는, 그들과 함께 결투의 조건을 정하는 일에 합류했다."]], [["While this was going on the perfidious Charlot remained behind and gave his men the signal to advance.", "이런 일이 진행되는 동안, 음흉한 샤를로는 뒤에 남아 자기 부하들에게 진격하라는 신호를 보냈다."], ["That cowardly troop issued from the wood and encompassed the three knights.", "그 비겁한 무리가 숲에서 쏟아져 나와 세 기사를 에워쌌다."], ["All three were equally surprised at the attack, but neither of them suspected the other to have any hand in the treason.", "세 사람 모두 그 공격에 똑같이 놀랐으나, 그들 가운데 누구도 다른 둘이 그 배신에 가담했으리라고는 의심하지 않았다."], ["Seeing the attack made equally upon them all, they united their efforts to resist it, and made the most forward of the assailants bite the dust.", "공격이 자기들 모두에게 똑같이 가해지는 것을 보고, 그들은 힘을 합쳐 그것을 막아 내며, 가장 앞장선 공격자들이 흙먼지를 물도록 만들었다."], ["Cortana fell on no one without inflicting a mortal wound, but the sword of Carahue was not of equal temper and broke in his hands.", "코르타나는 그것이 닿는 자마다 치명상을 입혔으나, 카라위의 칼은 그만한 담금질의 것이 아니어서 그의 손안에서 부러져 버렸다."], ["At the same instant his horse was slain, and Carahue fell, without a weapon, and entangled with his prostrate horse.", "같은 순간 그의 말이 죽임을 당했고, 카라위는 무기도 없이, 쓰러진 자기 말에 뒤엉킨 채 넘어지고 말았다."], ["Ogier, who saw it, ran to his defence and leaping to the ground covered the prince with his shield, supplied him with the sword of one of the fallen ruffians, and would have him mount his own horse.", "그것을 본 오지에가 그를 지키러 달려가 땅으로 뛰어내려 자신의 방패로 그 군주를 가려 주고, 쓰러진 악당들 가운데 하나의 칼을 그에게 건네주며 자기 말에 오르게 하려 했다."], ["At that moment Charlot, inflamed with rage, pushed his horse upon Ogier, knocked him down, and would have run him through with his lance if Sadon, who saw the treason, had not sprung upon him and thrust him back.", "그 순간 분노로 불타오른 샤를로가 자기 말을 오지에에게 밀어붙여 그를 쓰러뜨렸고, 자신의 창으로 그를 꿰뚫으려 했으나, 그 배신을 본 사동이 그에게 달려들어 그를 밀쳐 내지 않았더라면 그리되었을 것이다."], ["Carahue leapt lightly upon the horse which Ogier presented him, and had time only to exclaim, “Brave Ogier, I am no longer your enemy, I pledge to you an eternal friendship,”", "카라위는 오지에가 건네준 말에 가뿐히 뛰어올라, 다만 이렇게 외칠 틈밖에 없었다. “용감한 오지에여, 나는 더 이상 그대의 적이 아니오."], ["when numerous Saracen knights were seen approaching, having discovered the treachery, and Charlot with his followers took refuge in the wood.", "나는 그대에게 영원한 우정을 맹세하오.” 그때 그 배신을 알아챈 수많은 사라센 기사들이 다가오는 것이 보였고, 샤를로는 자기 부하들과 함께 숲속으로 몸을 피했다."]], [["The troop which advanced was commanded by Dannemont, the exiled king of Denmark, whom Geoffroy, Ogier’s father, had driven from his throne and compelled to take refuge with the Saracens.", "다가오는 무리는, 오지에의 아버지 조프루아가 왕좌에서 몰아내어 사라센인들에게로 피신하지 않을 수 없게 만들었던 덴마크의 폐위된 왕 단느몽이 지휘하고 있었다."], ["Learning who Ogier was, he instantly declared him his prisoner, in spite of the urgent remonstrances and even threats of Carahue and Sadon, and carried him under a strong guard to the Saracen camp.", "그는 오지에가 누구인지를 알게 되자, 카라위와 사동의 간곡한 항변과 심지어 위협에도 아랑곳없이 즉각 그를 자신의 포로로 선언하고, 강력한 호위 아래 그를 사라센 진영으로 끌고 갔다."], ["Here he was at first subjected to the most rigorous captivity, but Carahue and Sadon insisted so vehemently on his release, threatening to turn their arms against their own party if it was not granted, while Dannemont as eagerly opposed the measure, that Corsuble, the Saracen commander, consented to a middle course, and allowed Ogier the freedom of his camp, upon his promise not to leave it without permission.", "이곳에서 그는 처음에는 더없이 혹독한 감금을 당했으나, 카라위와 사동이 그의 석방을 어찌나 격렬하게 주장하며 만약 그것이 받아들여지지 않으면 자기들 무기를 자기편에게로 돌리겠다고 위협하고, 단느몽 또한 그 못지않게 열렬히 그 조치에 반대하자, 사라센인들의 지휘관 코르수블은 절충안에 동의하여, 진영을 허락 없이 떠나지 않겠다는 그의 약속을 받고 오지에에게 진영 안에서의 자유를 허락했다."]], [["Carahue was not satisfied with this partial concession.", "카라위는 이러한 부분적인 양보에 만족하지 않았다."], ["He left the city next morning, proceeded to the camp of Charlemagne, and demanded to be led to the Emperor.", "그는 이튿날 아침 도시를 떠나 샤를마뉴의 진영으로 나아가, 황제 앞으로 안내해 줄 것을 요구했다."], ["When he reached his presence he dismounted from his horse, took off his helmet, drew his sword, and holding it by the blade presented it to Charlemagne as he knelt before him.", "그의 앞에 이르렀을 때, 그는 말에서 내려 투구를 벗고 칼을 뽑아, 칼날을 잡고서 황제 앞에 무릎을 꿇으며 그것을 샤를마뉴에게 바쳤다."]], [["“Illustrious prince,”", "“저명한 군주시여.”"], ["he said, “behold before you the herald who brought the challenge to your knights from the King of Mauritania.", "그가 말했다. “전하 앞에 있는 자를 보십시오, 마우리타니아 왕으로부터 전하의 기사들에게 결투를 신청해 온 그 전령이옵니다."], ["The cowardly old King Dannemont has made the brave Ogier prisoner, and has prevailed on our general to refuse to give him up.", "비겁한 늙은 왕 단느몽이 용감한 오지에를 포로로 사로잡고, 우리 장군을 설득하여 그를 내주기를 거부하게 만들었습니다."], ["I come to make amends for this ungenerous conduct by yielding myself, Carahue, King of Mauritania, your prisoner.”", "저는 이 옹졸한 처사를 보상하고자, 저 자신, 마우리타니아의 왕 카라위를 전하의 포로로 바치러 왔습니다.”"]], [["Charlemagne, with all his peers, admired the magnanimity of Carahue; he raised him, embraced him, and restored to him his sword.", "샤를마뉴는 그의 모든 중신들과 더불어 카라위의 도량에 감탄했다. 그는 그를 일으켜 세워 끌어안고, 그의 칼을 돌려주었다."], ["“Prince,”", "“군주여.”"], ["said he, “your presence and the bright example you afford my knights consoles me for the loss of Ogier.", "그가 말했다. “그대의 존재와, 그대가 내 기사들에게 보여 주는 빛나는 본보기가 오지에를 잃은 것을 내게 위로해 주는구려."], ["Would to God you might receive our holy faith, and be wholly united with us.”", "부디 그대가 우리의 거룩한 신앙을 받아들여 우리와 온전히 하나가 되기를 하느님께 비오.\""], ["All the lords of the court, led by Duke Namo, paid their respects to the King of Mauritania.", "나모 공작을 필두로 한 궁정의 모든 영주들이 마우리타니아의 왕에게 경의를 표했다."], ["Charlot only failed to appear, fearing to be recognized as a traitor; but the heart of Carahue was too noble to pierce that of Charlemagne by telling him the treachery of his son.", "다만 샤를로만은 배신자로 알아보일까 두려워 나타나지 않았다. 그러나 카라위의 마음은 너무도 고결하여, 그 아들의 배신을 일러바침으로써 샤를마뉴의 가슴을 찌르지는 않았다."]], [["Meanwhile the Saracen army was rent by discord.", "한편 사라센 군대는 불화로 갈라져 있었다."], ["The troops of Carahue clamored against the commander-in-chief because their king was left in captivity.", "카라위의 병사들은 자기들의 왕이 포로로 남겨진 것을 두고 총사령관에게 거세게 항의했다."], ["They even threatened to desert the cause and turn their arms against their allies.", "그들은 심지어 대의를 저버리고 자기들 무기를 동맹들에게 돌리겠노라 위협하기까지 했다."], ["Charlemagne pressed the siege vigorously, till at length the Saracen leaders found themselves compelled to abandon the city and betake themselves to their ships.", "샤를마뉴는 포위를 맹렬히 밀어붙였고, 마침내 사라센 지휘관들은 도시를 버리고 자기들의 배로 물러나지 않을 수 없게 되었다."], ["A truce was made; Ogier was exchanged for Carahue, and the two friends embraced one another with vows of perpetual brotherhood.", "휴전이 맺어졌고, 오지에는 카라위와 교환되었으며, 두 벗은 영원한 형제의 의를 맹세하며 서로를 끌어안았다."], ["The Pope was reëstablished in his dominions, and Italy being tranquil, Charlemagne returned with his peers and their followers to France. ————", "교황은 자신의 영토에 복위되었고, 이탈리아가 평온해지자 샤를마뉴는 자신의 중신들과 그 종자들을 거느리고 프랑스로 돌아갔다."]], [["OGIER, THE DANE (Continued)", "덴마크인 오지에 (계속)"]], [["Charlemagne had not forgotten the offence of Geoffroy, the King of Denmark, in withholding homage, and now prepared to enforce submission.", "샤를마뉴는 덴마크 왕 조프루아가 신종의 예를 거부한 죄과를 잊지 않았고, 이제 복종을 강제할 채비를 했다."], ["But at this crisis he was waited upon by an embassy from Geoffroy, acknowledging his fault, and craving assistance against an army of invaders who had attacked his states with a force which he was unable to repel.", "그러나 이 위기의 순간에, 조프루아로부터 온 사절단이 그를 찾아와 자신의 잘못을 인정하며, 자기가 물리칠 수 없는 병력으로 자기 영토를 공격해 온 어느 침략군에 맞서 도움을 청했다."], ["The soul of Charlemagne was too great to be implacable, and he took this opportunity to test that of Ogier, who had felt acutely the unkindness of his father, in leaving him, without regard or notice, fifteen years in captivity.", "샤를마뉴의 영혼은 너무도 위대하여 무자비할 수가 없었고, 그는 이 기회를 이용하여 오지에의 마음을 시험해 보았다. 오지에는 자기 아버지가 자신을 아무런 배려나 소식도 없이 십오 년 동안 포로로 내버려 둔 그 박정함을 뼈저리게 느껴 온 터였다."], ["Charles asked Ogier whether, in spite of his father’s neglect, he was disposed to lead an army to his assistance.", "샤를은 오지에에게, 아버지의 무관심에도 불구하고 그를 돕기 위해 군대를 이끌 마음이 있는지 물었다. 그가 대답했다."], ["He replied, “A son can never be excused from helping his father by any cause short of death.”", "“아들은 죽음 외에 그 어떤 까닭으로도 아버지 돕는 일에서 결코 면제될 수 없습니다.”"], ["Charlemagne placed an army of a thousand knights under the command of Ogier, and great numbers more volunteered to march under so distinguished a leader.", "샤를마뉴는 천 명의 기사로 이루어진 군대를 오지에의 지휘 아래 두었고, 그토록 빼어난 지도자 밑에서 진군하겠노라 자원하는 자들이 그 밖에도 무수히 많았다."], ["He flew to the succor of his father, repelled the invaders, and drove them in confusion to their vessels.", "그는 자기 아버지를 구하러 날듯이 달려가 침략자들을 물리치고, 그들을 혼란에 빠뜨려 자기들 배로 내몰았다."], ["Ogier then hastened to the capital, but as he drew near the city he heard all the bells sounding a knell.", "그런 다음 오지에는 서둘러 수도로 향했으나, 도시에 가까워지자 모든 종이 조종(弔鐘)을 울리는 소리를 들었다."], ["He soon learned the cause; it was the obsequies of Geoffroy, the King.", "그는 곧 그 까닭을 알게 되었으니, 그것은 조프루아 왕의 장례였다."], ["Ogier felt keenly the grief of not having been permitted to embrace his father once more, and to learn his latest commands; but he found that his father had declared him heir to his throne.", "오지에는 자기 아버지를 한 번 더 끌어안고 그의 마지막 명을 듣도록 허락받지 못한 슬픔을 사무치게 느꼈다. 그러나 그는 자기 아버지가 자신을 왕위 계승자로 선포해 두었음을 알게 되었다."], ["He hastened to the church where the body lay; he knelt and bathed the lifeless form with his tears.", "그는 시신이 안치된 교회로 서둘러 가서, 무릎을 꿇고 눈물로 그 생기 없는 형해를 적셨다. 그 순간 천상의 빛이 사방으로 비치고, 한 천사의 목소리가 말했다."], ["At that moment a celestial light beamed all around, and a voice of an angel said, “Ogier, leave thy crown to Guyon, thy brother, and bear no other title than that of ‘The Dane.’ Thy destiny is glorious, and other kingdoms are reserved for thee.”", "“오지에여, 그대의 왕관은 그대의 아우 기용에게 남기고, ‘덴마크인’이라는 칭호 외에는 다른 어떤 칭호도 지니지 말라. 그대의 운명은 영광스러우며, 다른 왕국들이 그대를 위해 마련되어 있노라.”"], ["Ogier obeyed the divine behest.", "오지에는 그 신성한 명에 따랐다."], ["He saluted his stepmother respectfully, and embracing his brother, told him that he was content with his lot in being reckoned among the paladins of Charlemagne, and resigned all claims to the crown of Denmark.", "그는 자신의 계모에게 공손히 인사하고, 자기 아우를 끌어안으며, 샤를마뉴의 용맹한 기사들 가운데 헤아려지는 자신의 처지에 만족한다고 말하고는, 덴마크 왕관에 대한 모든 권리를 내려놓았다."]], [["Ogier returned covered with glory to the court of Charlemagne, and the Emperor, touched with this proof of his attachment, loaded him with caresses, and treated him almost as an equal.", "오지에는 영광에 뒤덮인 채 샤를마뉴의 궁정으로 돌아왔고, 황제는 그의 이러한 충정의 증표에 감동하여 그에게 온갖 다정함을 쏟으며 거의 대등한 자처럼 그를 대했다."]], [["We pass in silence the adventures of Ogier for several ensuing years, in which the fairy-gifts of his infancy showed their force in making him successful in all enterprises, both of love and war.", "우리는 그 후 여러 해 동안의 오지에의 모험들에 대해서는 침묵하고 넘어가는데, 그동안 그가 유년기에 받았던 요정들의 선물은 그가 사랑에서나 전쟁에서나 모든 일에서 성공하게 함으로써 그 위력을 드러냈다."], ["He married the charming Belicene, and became the father of young Baldwin, a youth who seemed to inherit in full measure the strength and courage of his father and the beauty of his mother.", "그는 매혹적인 벨리센과 혼인하여, 어린 볼드윈의 아버지가 되었으니, 그 소년은 아버지의 힘과 용기와 어머니의 아름다움을 고스란히 물려받은 듯이 보였다."], ["When the lad was old enough to be separated from his mother, Ogier took him to court and presented him to Charlemagne, who embraced him and took him into his service.", "그 아이가 어머니와 떨어질 만한 나이가 되었을 때, 오지에는 그를 궁정으로 데려가 샤를마뉴에게 선보였고, 황제는 그를 끌어안아 자기 휘하에 받아들였다."], ["It seemed to Duke Namo, and all the elder knights, as if they saw in him Ogier himself, as he was when a youth; and this resemblance won for the lad their kind regards.", "나모 공작과 손위 기사들 모두에게는, 마치 오지에 자신의 젊은 시절 모습을 그 아이에게서 보는 듯하였고, 이러한 닮음이 그 소년에게 그들의 호의를 얻어 주었다."], ["Even Charlot at first seemed to be fond of him, though after a while the resemblance to Ogier which he noticed had the effect to excite his hatred.", "심지어 샤를로조차 처음에는 그를 귀여워하는 듯이 보였으나, 얼마 지나지 않아 그가 알아챈 오지에와의 닮음이 도리어 그의 증오를 불러일으키는 결과를 낳았다."]], [["Baldwin was attentive to Charlot, and lost no occasion to be serviceable.", "볼드윈은 샤를로에게 정성을 다했고, 도움이 될 기회를 한 번도 놓치지 않았다."], ["The Prince loved to play chess, and Baldwin, who played well, often made a party with him.", "왕자는 장기 두기를 즐겼고, 장기를 잘 두는 볼드윈은 종종 그와 한판을 벌였다."]], [["One day Chariot was nettled at losing two pieces in succession; he thought he could, by taking a piece from Baldwin, get some amends for his loss; but Baldwin, seeing him fall into a trap which he had set for him, could not help a slight laugh, as he said, “Check-mate.”", "어느 날 샤를로는 연달아 두 점을 잃고 약이 올랐다. 그는 볼드윈에게서 한 점을 따면 자신의 손실을 어느 정도 메울 수 있으리라 생각했으나, 볼드윈은 그가 자기가 놓은 덫에 걸려드는 것을 보고, “장군이오”라고 말하면서 가벼운 웃음을 참지 못했다."], ["Charlot", "샤를로는"]], [["rose in a fury, seized the rich and heavy chessboard, and dashed it with all his strength on the head of Baldwin, who fell, and died where he fell.", "분노에 휩싸여 일어선 그는 화려하고 묵직한 체스판을 움켜쥐고 온 힘을 다해 발드윈의 머리에 내리쳤다. 발드윈은 쓰러졌고, 쓰러진 자리에서 숨을 거두었다."]], [["Frightened at his own crime, and fearing the vengeance of the terrible Ogier, Charlot concealed himself in the interior of the palace.", "자신이 저지른 범죄에 겁을 먹은 데다 무시무시한 오지에의 복수가 두려웠던 샤를로는 궁전 안쪽에 몸을 숨겼다."], ["A young companion of Baldwin hastened and informed Ogier of the event.", "발드윈의 어린 동료 하나가 서둘러 오지에에게 사건을 알렸다."], ["He ran to the chamber, and beheld the body of his child bathed in blood, and it could not be concealed from him that Charlot gave the blow.", "그는 침실로 달려가 피투성이가 된 제 아이의 몸을 보았고, 샤를로가 그 일격을 가했다는 사실은 그에게 숨길 수 없었다."], ["Transported with rage, Ogier sought Charlot through the palace, and Charlot, feeling safe nowhere else, took refuge in the hall of Charlemagne, where he seated himself at table with Duke Namo and Salomon, Duke of Brittany.", "분노에 사로잡힌 오지에는 궁전을 뒤지며 샤를로를 찾았다. 샤를로는 다른 어디에서도 안전하지 못하다고 느끼고는 샤를마뉴의 홀로 피신해, 나모 공작과 브르타뉴 공작 살로몬과 함께 식탁에 앉았다."], ["Ogier, with sword drawn, followed him to the very table of the Emperor.", "오지에는 칼을 빼 든 채로 황제의 식탁까지 그를 뒤쫓아 갔다."], ["When a cupbearer attempted to bar his way he struck the cup from his hand and dashed the contents in the Emperor’s face.", "술 따르는 시종이 그의 앞길을 막으려 하자, 그는 시종의 손에서 잔을 쳐 떨어뜨리며 그 내용물을 황제의 얼굴에 끼얹었다."], ["Charles rose in a passion, seized a knife, and would have plunged it into his breast, had not Salomon and another baron thrown themselves between, while Namo, who had retained his ancient influence over Ogier, drew him out of the room.", "샤를마뉴는 격분하여 일어나 칼을 움켜쥐고 그의 가슴에 찔러 넣으려 했으나, 살로몬과 또 다른 영주가 그 사이에 몸을 던졌고, 한편 예부터 오지에에게 영향력을 지녀 온 나모가 그를 방 밖으로 끌어냈다."], ["Foreseeing the consequence of this violence, pitying Ogier, and in his heart excusing him, Namo hurried him away before the guards of the palace could arrest him, made him mount his horse, and leave Paris.", "이 폭거의 결과를 내다본 데다 오지에를 가엾이 여기고 마음속으로는 그를 두둔하던 나모는, 궁전의 호위병들이 그를 체포하기 전에 서둘러 그를 데리고 나가 말에 태워 파리를 떠나게 했다."]], [["Charlemagne called together his peers, and made them take an oath to do all in their power to arrest Ogier, and bring him to condign punishment.", "샤를마뉴는 자신의 기사들을 한데 불러 모아, 온 힘을 다해 오지에를 체포해 마땅한 벌을 받게 하겠다는 맹세를 시켰다."], ["Ogier on his part sent messages to the Emperor, offering to give himself up on condition that Charlot should be punished for his atrocious crime.", "오지에는 한편으로 황제에게 전갈을 보내, 샤를로가 그 흉악한 범죄에 대해 벌을 받는다는 조건으로 자수하겠노라 제안했다."], ["The Emperor would listen to no conditions, and went in pursuit of Ogier at the head of a large body of soldiers.", "황제는 어떤 조건도 들으려 하지 않았고, 대규모 병력을 이끌고 오지에를 추격하러 나섰다."], ["Ogier, on the other hand, was warmly supported by many knights, who pledged themselves in his defence.", "오지에는 다른 한편으로, 그를 지키겠노라 맹세한 많은 기사들의 열렬한 지지를 받았다."], ["The contest raged long, with no decisive results.", "싸움은 결정적인 성과 없이 오래도록 계속되었다."], ["Ogier more than once had the Emperor in his power, but declined to avail himself of his advantage, and released him without conditions.", "오지에는 한 번 이상 황제를 자기 손아귀에 넣었으나, 그 우세를 이용하기를 마다하고 아무 조건 없이 그를 풀어 주었다."], ["He even implored pardon for himself, but demanded at the same time the punishment of Charlot.", "그는 자신을 위한 용서를 간청하면서도, 동시에 샤를로에 대한 처벌을 요구했다."], ["But Charlemagne was too blindly fond of his unworthy son to subject him to punishment for the sake of conciliating one who had been so deeply injured.", "그러나 샤를마뉴는 그토록 깊이 상처 입은 자를 달래기 위해 자신의 못난 아들을 처벌받게 하기에는, 너무도 눈멀게 그 아들을 아꼈다."]], [["At length, distressed at the blood which his friends had lost in his cause, Ogier dismissed his little army, and slipping away from those who wished to attend him, took his course to rejoin the Duke Guyon, his brother.", "마침내, 자신의 대의를 위해 벗들이 흘린 피에 괴로워하던 오지에는 자신의 작은 군대를 해산하고, 자신을 수행하고자 하는 이들에게서 슬그머니 빠져나와, 자신의 형제인 기용 공작과 다시 합류하고자 길을 떠났다."], ["On his way, having reached the forest of Ardennes, weary with long travel, the freshness of a retired valley tempted him to lie down to take some repose.", "가는 도중 아르덴 숲에 이르렀을 때, 오랜 여정에 지친 그는 외딴 골짜기의 싱그러움에 이끌려 잠시 쉬려고 자리에 누웠다."], ["He unsaddled Beiffror, relieved himself of his helmet, lay down on the turf, rested his head on his shield, and slept.", "그는 베이프로르의 안장을 풀고, 투구를 벗어 몸을 가볍게 한 뒤, 풀밭에 누워 방패를 베고 잠들었다."]], [["It so happened that Turpin, who occasionally recalled to mind that he was Archbishop of Rheins, was at that time in the vicinity, making a pastoral visit to the churches under his jurisdiction.", "마침 그 무렵, 이따금 자신이 랭스의 대주교임을 떠올리던 튀르팽이 그 부근에서 자기 관할 아래 있는 교회들을 사목 방문하고 있었다."], ["But his dignity of peer of France, and his martial spirit, which caused him to be reckoned among the “preux chevaliers” of his time, forbade him to travel without as large a retinue of knights as he had of clergymen.", "그러나 프랑스 기사라는 그의 지위와, 그를 당대의 〈용맹한 기사들〉 가운데 하나로 꼽히게 만든 그의 무인다운 기상은, 그가 데리고 다니는 성직자만큼이나 많은 기사들의 수행 없이는 여행하지 못하게 했다."], ["One of these was thirsty, and knowing the fountain on the borders of which Ogier was reposing, he rode to it, and was struck by the sight of a knight stretched on the ground.", "그 가운데 한 사람이 목이 말랐는데, 오지에가 쉬고 있던 가장자리의 샘을 알고 있던 그는 그곳으로 말을 몰고 갔다가, 땅바닥에 뻗어 있는 한 기사의 모습에 놀랐다."], ["He hastened back, and let the Archbishop know, who approached the fountain, and recognized Ogier.", "그는 서둘러 돌아가 대주교에게 알렸고, 대주교는 샘으로 다가가 오지에를 알아보았다."]], [["The first impulse of the good and generous Turpin was to save his friend, for whom he felt the warmest attachment; but his archdeacons and knights, who also recognized Ogier, reminded the Archbishop of the oath which the Emperor had exacted of them all.", "선량하고 너그러운 튀르팽의 첫 충동은, 더없이 따뜻한 정을 품고 있던 자신의 벗을 구하는 것이었다. 그러나 역시 오지에를 알아본 그의 부주교들과 기사들은, 황제가 그들 모두에게 강요한 맹세를 대주교에게 상기시켰다."], ["Turpin could not be false to his oath; but it was not without a groan that he permitted his followers to bind the sleeping knight.", "튀르팽은 자신의 맹세를 저버릴 수 없었다. 그러나 잠든 기사를 결박하도록 부하들에게 허락한 것은 신음 없이는 할 수 없는 일이었다."], ["The Archbishop’s attendants secured the horse and arms of Ogier, and conducted their prisoner to the Emperor at Soissons.", "대주교의 수행원들은 오지에의 말과 무기를 확보하고, 포로를 수아송에 있는 황제에게로 데려갔다."]], [["The Emperor had become so much embittered by Ogier’s obstinate resistance, added to his original fault, that he was disposed to order him to instant death.", "황제는 오지에의 본래 잘못에 더해, 그의 완강한 저항으로 인해 너무나 큰 원한을 품게 된 터라, 그를 즉각 처형하라고 명할 작정이었다."], ["But Turpin, seconded by the good Dukes Namo and Salomon, prayed so hard for him that Charlemagne consented to remit a violent death, but sentenced him to close imprisonment, under the charge of the Archbishop, strictly limiting his food to one quarter of a loaf of bread per day, with one piece of meat, and a quarter of a cup of wine.", "그러나 선량한 나모 공작과 살로몬의 도움을 받은 튀르팽이 그를 위해 너무나 간절히 빌었으므로, 샤를마뉴는 참혹한 죽음만은 면해 주기로 했으나, 대주교의 관리 아래 엄중히 감금하라는 선고를 내렸다. 그러면서 그의 식사를 하루에 빵 사분의 일 덩이와 고기 한 점, 포도주 사분의 일 잔으로 엄격히 제한했다."], ["In this way he hoped to quickly put an end to his life without bringing on himself the hostility of the King of Denmark, and other powerful friends of Ogier.", "이런 식으로 그는 덴마크 왕을 비롯한 오지에의 강력한 벗들의 적의를 사지 않으면서도 그의 목숨을 속히 끝장내기를 바랐다."], ["He exacted a new oath of Turpin to obey his order strictly.", "그는 튀르팽에게 이 명령을 엄격히 따르겠다는 새로운 맹세를 받아 냈다."]], [["The good Archbishop loved Ogier too well not to cast about for some means of saving his life, which he foresaw he would soon lose if subjected to such scanty fare, for Ogier was seven feet tall, and had an appetite in proportion.", "선량한 대주교는 오지에를 너무나 아꼈으므로, 그토록 빈약한 식사에 처해진다면 곧 잃고 말 것이 분명한 그의 목숨을 구할 어떤 방도를 궁리하지 않을 수 없었다. 오지에는 키가 7피트였고, 그에 걸맞은 식욕을 지녔던 것이다."], ["Turpin remembered, moreover, that Ogier was a true son of the Church, always zealous to propagate the faith and subdue unbelievers; so he felt justified in practising on this occasion what in later times has been entitled “mental reservation,”", "게다가 튀르팽은 오지에가 언제나 신앙을 전파하고 불신자들을 굴복시키는 데 열심이었던, 진정한 교회의 아들임을 기억했다. 그리하여 그는 자신이 한 맹세의 문구에서 벗어나지 않으면서도, 후대에 〈마음속 단서〉라 불리게 된 것을 이 경우에 실천하는 것이 정당하다고 느꼈다."], ["without swerving from the letter of the oath which he had taken. This is the method he hit upon.", "그가 생각해 낸 방법은 이러했다."]], [["Every morning he had his prisoner supplied with a quarter of a loaf of bread, made of two bushels of flour, to this he added a quarter of a sheep or a fat calf, and he had a cup made which held forty pints of wine, and allowed Ogier a quarter of it daily.", "날마다 아침이면 그는 포로에게 밀가루 두 부셸로 만든 빵 사분의 일 덩이를 공급하게 했고, 거기에 양이나 살진 송아지 사분의 일 마리를 더했으며, 포도주 사십 파인트가 담기는 잔을 만들게 하여 날마다 오지에에게 그 사분의 일을 허락했다."]], [["Ogier’s imprisonment lasted long; Charlemagne was astonished to hear, from time to time, that he still held out; and when he inquired more particularly of Turpin, the good Archbishop, relying on his own understanding of the words, did not hesitate to affirm positively that he allowed his prisoner no more than the permitted ration.", "오지에의 감금은 오래 계속되었다. 샤를마뉴는 그가 여전히 버티고 있다는 소식을 이따금 들을 때마다 놀랐다. 그가 튀르팽에게 좀 더 자세히 물었을 때, 선량한 대주교는 자기 나름의 말 풀이에 의지하여, 자신이 포로에게 허락된 배급량 이상은 결코 주지 않았노라고 단호히 단언하기를 주저하지 않았다."]], [["We forgot to say that, when Ogier was led prisoner to Soissons, the Abbot of Saint Faron, observing the fine horse Beiffror, and not having at the time any other favor to ask of Charlemagne, begged the Emperor to give him the horse, and had him taken to his abbey.", "오지에가 포로로 수아송에 끌려갔을 때, 생파롱의 수도원장이 그 훌륭한 말 베이프로르를 눈여겨보고는, 마침 샤를마뉴에게 청할 다른 청이 없던 차라 황제에게 그 말을 달라고 청하여 자기 수도원으로 데려갔다는 이야기를 우리는 깜빡 하지 못했다."], ["He was impatient to try his new acquisition, and when he had arrived in his litter at the foot of the mountain where the horse had been brought to meet him mounted him and rode onward.", "그는 새로 얻은 것을 시험해 보고 싶어 안달이 났고, 가마를 타고 산기슭에 도착하여 말이 그를 맞이하러 끌려오자, 그 위에 올라타 앞으로 나아갔다."], ["The horse, accustomed to bear the enormous weight of Ogier in his armor, when he perceived nothing on his back but the light weight of the Abbot, whose long robes fluttered against his sides, ran away, making prodigious leaps over the steep acclivities of the mountain till he reached the convent of Jouaire, where, in sight of the Abbess and her nuns, he threw the Abbot, already half dead with fright, to the ground.", "갑옷을 입은 오지에의 어마어마한 무게를 짊어지는 데 익숙했던 그 말은, 등에 긴 옷자락이 옆구리에 펄럭이는 수도원장의 가벼운 무게밖에 없음을 알아채자, 산의 가파른 비탈을 엄청난 도약으로 뛰어넘으며 달아나, 마침내 주아르 수녀원에 이르렀다. 그곳에서 수도원장은 이미 공포로 반쯤 죽은 채, 수녀원장과 그 수녀들이 보는 앞에서 땅바닥에 내동댕이쳐졌다."], ["The Abbot, bruised and mortified, revenged himself on poor Beiffror, whom he condemned, in his wrath, to be given to the workmen to drag stones for a chapel that he was building near the abbey.", "멍들고 모욕당한 수도원장은 가엾은 베이프로르에게 분풀이를 했으니, 분노에 차서 그 말을, 수도원 근처에 짓고 있던 예배당을 위해 돌을 끌어 나르도록 일꾼들에게 넘기라고 명했다."], ["Thus, ill-fed, hard-worked, and often beaten, the noble horse Beiffror passed the time while his master’s imprisonment lasted.", "이렇게 하여, 제대로 먹지도 못하고 혹사당하며 자주 매를 맞으면서, 고귀한 말 베이프로르는 제 주인이 갇혀 있는 동안의 시간을 보냈다."]], [["That imprisonment would have been as long as his life if it had not been for some important events which forced the Emperor to set Ogier at liberty.", "그 감금은, 황제로 하여금 오지에를 풀어 주지 않을 수 없게 만든 몇 가지 중대한 사건이 없었더라면, 그의 목숨만큼이나 길었을 것이다."]], [["The Emperor learned at the same time that Carahue, King of Mauritania, was assembling an army to come and demand the liberation of Ogier; that Guyon, King of Denmark, was prepared to second the enterprise with all his forces; and, worse than all, that the Saracens, under Bruhier, Sultan of Arabia, had landed in Gascony, taken Bordeaux, and were marching with all speed for Paris.", "황제는 마우리타니아의 왕 카라우에가 오지에의 석방을 요구하러 오기 위해 군대를 소집하고 있다는 것, 덴마크 왕 기용이 자신의 모든 병력을 동원해 그 사업을 거들 채비를 갖추고 있다는 것, 그리고 무엇보다 나쁘게도, 아라비아의 술탄 브뤼이에가 이끄는 사라센인들이 가스코뉴에 상륙해 보르도를 함락하고 파리를 향해 전속력으로 진군하고 있다는 것을 동시에 알게 되었다."]], [["Charlemagne now felt how necessary the aid of Ogier was to him.", "이제 샤를마뉴는 오지에의 도움이 자신에게 얼마나 절실한지를 느꼈다."], ["But, in spite of the representations of Turpin, Namo, and Salomon, he could not bring himself to consent to surrender Charlot to such punishment as Ogier should see fit to impose.", "그러나 튀르팽과 나모와 살로몬의 진언에도 불구하고, 그는 오지에가 합당하다고 여길 만한 처벌에 샤를로를 내맡기는 데 차마 동의할 수 없었다."], ["Besides, he believed that Ogier was without strength and vigor, weakened by imprisonment and long abstinence.", "게다가 그는 오지에가 감금과 오랜 절식으로 쇠약해져 기력도 힘도 없으리라고 믿었다."]], [["At this crisis he received a message from Bruhier, proposing to put the issue upon the result of a combat between himself and the Emperor or his champion: promising, if defeated, to withdraw his army.", "이 위기에 그는 브뤼이에로부터 전갈을 받았는데, 자신과 황제 또는 황제의 대전사 사이의 일대일 결투의 결과에 모든 것을 걸자고 제안하면서, 만일 패한다면 자신의 군대를 물리겠노라 약속하는 것이었다."], ["Charlemagne would willingly have accepted the challenge, but his counsellors all opposed it.", "샤를마뉴는 기꺼이 그 도전을 받아들이고 싶었으나, 그의 고문들이 모두 반대했다."], ["The herald was therefore told that the Emperor would take time to consider his proposition, and give his answer the next day.", "그리하여 사자에게는 황제가 그 제안을 숙고할 시간을 가진 뒤 이튿날 답을 주겠노라 전해졌다."]], [["It was during this interval that the three Dukes succeeded in prevailing upon Charlemagne to pardon Ogier, and to send for him to combat the puissant enemy who now defied him; but it was no easy task to persuade Ogier.", "세 공작이 샤를마뉴를 설득하여 오지에를 사면하고, 이제 그를 도전하는 강대한 적과 싸우도록 그를 부르러 보내는 데 성공한 것은 바로 이 사이였다. 그러나 오지에를 설득하는 일은 결코 쉽지 않았다."], ["The idea of his long imprisonment and the recollection of his son, bleeding and dying in his arms by the blow of the ferocious Charlot, made him long resist the urgency of his friends.", "자신의 오랜 감금에 대한 생각과, 흉포한 샤를로의 일격에 자기 품에서 피 흘리며 죽어 간 아들에 대한 기억은, 그로 하여금 벗들의 간곡한 권유에 오래도록 저항하게 만들었다."], ["Though glory called him to encounter Bruhier, and the safety of Christendom demanded the destruction of this proud enemy of the faith, Ogier only yielded at last on condition that Charlot should be delivered into his hands to be dealt with as he should see fit.", "비록 영광이 그를 브뤼이에와 맞서도록 불렀고 그리스도교 세계의 안위가 신앙의 이 오만한 적의 파멸을 요구했으나, 오지에는 마침내 샤를로를 자기 손에 넘겨 자신이 합당하다고 여기는 대로 처분하게 한다는 조건으로만 굴복했다."]], [["The terms were hard, but the danger was pressing, and Charlemagne, with a returning sense of justice, and a strong confidence in the generous though passionate soul of Ogier, at last consented to them.", "조건은 가혹했으나 위험은 절박했다. 그리하여 샤를마뉴는 정의감을 되찾고, 격정적이지만 너그러운 오지에의 영혼에 대한 강한 신뢰를 품고서, 마침내 그 조건에 동의했다."]], [["Ogier was led into the presence of Charlemagne by the three peers.", "오지에는 세 기사의 안내를 받아 샤를마뉴 앞으로 인도되었다."], ["The Emperor, faithful to his word, had caused Charlot to be brought into the hall where the high barons were assembled, his hands tied, and his head uncovered.", "자신의 말에 충실했던 황제는, 높은 영주들이 모여 있는 홀로 샤를로를 데려오게 하여, 그의 손을 묶고 머리를 드러내게 했다."], ["When the Emperor saw Ogier approach he took Charlot by the arm, led him towards Ogier, and said these words: “I surrender the criminal; do with him as you think fit.”", "황제는 오지에가 다가오는 것을 보자 샤를로의 팔을 잡아 오지에에게로 이끌며 이렇게 말했다. \"죄인을 넘기노라."], ["Ogier, without replying, seized Charlot by the hair, forced him on his knees, and lifted with the other hand his irresistible sword.", "그대가 합당하다고 여기는 대로 그를 처분하라.\" 오지에는 아무 대답도 하지 않고 샤를로의 머리채를 움켜쥐어 무릎을 꿇리고는, 다른 손으로 그 누구도 막을 수 없는 자신의 칼을 치켜들었다."], ["Charlemagne, who expected to see the head of his son rolling at his feet, shut his eyes and uttered a cry of horror.", "제 아들의 머리가 발치에 굴러떨어지는 것을 보리라 예상한 샤를마뉴는 눈을 감고 공포의 비명을 질렀다."]], [["Ogier had done enough.", "오지에는 그것으로 충분히 했다."], ["The next moment he raised Charlot, cut his bonds, kissed him on the mouth, and hastened to throw himself at the feet of the Emperor.", "다음 순간 그는 샤를로를 일으켜 세워 그의 결박을 끊고, 그의 입에 입을 맞춘 뒤, 서둘러 황제의 발치에 몸을 던졌다."]], [["Nothing can exceed the surprise and joy of Charlemagne at seeing his son unharmed and Ogier kneeling at his feet.", "다친 데 없는 아들과 자기 발치에 무릎 꿇은 오지에를 본 샤를마뉴의 놀라움과 기쁨은 무엇과도 견줄 수 없었다."], ["He folded him in his arms, bathed him with tears, and exclaimed to his barons, “I feel at this moment that Ogier is greater than I.”", "그는 오지에를 품에 끌어안고 눈물로 그를 적시며, 자신의 영주들에게 외쳤다. \"나는 이 순간 오지에가 나보다 더 위대하다는 것을 느끼노라.\""], ["As for Charlot, his base soul felt nothing but the joy of having escaped death; he remained such as he had been, and it was not till some years afterwards he received the punishment he deserved, from the hands of Huon of Bordeaux, as we have seen in a former chapter.", "샤를로로 말하자면, 그의 비천한 영혼은 죽음을 모면했다는 기쁨밖에 느끼지 못했다. 그는 예전 그대로의 모습으로 남아 있었으며, 몇 해 뒤에야 비로소, 앞선 어느 장에서 우리가 본 대로, 보르도의 위옹의 손에 마땅한 벌을 받았다."]], [["————\nOGIER, THE DANE (Continued)", "———— 덴마크인 오지에 (계속)"]], [["When Charlemagne had somewhat recovered his composure he was surprised to observe that Ogier appeared in good case, and had a healthy color in his cheeks.", "샤를마뉴는 어느 정도 평정을 되찾고 나서, 오지에가 좋은 상태로 보이고 두 뺨에 건강한 혈색이 도는 것을 보고 놀랐다. 그는 대주교에게로 몸을 돌렸는데, 대주교는 그의 눈길을 마주하자 얼굴을 붉히지 않을 수 없었다."], ["He turned to the Archbishop, who could not help blushing as he met his eye.", "\"내 왕비 베르타의 머리에 두고 맹세하건대,\" 샤를마뉴가 말했다."], ["“By the head of Bertha, my queen,”", "\"오지에가 그대의 성에서 좋은 거처를 누렸구려, 대주교여."], ["said Charlemagne, “Ogier has had good quarters in your castle, my Lord Archbishop; but so much the more am I indebted to you.”", "허나 그럴수록 나는 그대에게 더욱 빚을 졌소.\" 모든 영주들이 웃으며 튀르팽을 놀려 댔으나, 그는 그저 이렇게 말할 뿐이었다."], ["All the barons laughed and jested with Turpin, who only said, “Laugh as much as you please, my lords; but for my part I am not sorry to see the arm in full vigor that is to avenge us on the proud Saracen.”", "\"마음껏 웃으십시오, 영주들이여. 허나 저로서는, 오만한 사라센인에게 우리의 원수를 갚아 줄 저 팔이 충만한 기력을 되찾은 것을 보니 결코 유감스럽지 않습니다.\""]], [["Charlemagne immediately despatched his herald, accepting the challenge, and appointing the next day but one for the encounter.", "샤를마뉴는 즉시 자신의 사자를 보내 도전을 받아들이고, 다음 다음 날을 결투의 날로 정했다."], ["The proud and crafty Bruhier laughed scornfully when he heard the reply accepting his challenge, for he had a reliance on certain resources besides his natural strength and skill.", "오만하고 교활한 브뤼이에는 자신의 도전을 받아들인다는 답을 듣고 경멸하듯 비웃었으니, 그는 타고난 힘과 기량 외에 어떤 비책을 믿고 있었던 것이다."], ["However, he swore by Mahomet to observe the conditions as proposed and agreed upon.", "그러나 그는 마호메트에 두고, 제안되고 합의된 대로 조건을 지키겠노라 맹세했다."]], [["Ogier now demanded his armor, and it was brought to him in excellent condition, for the good Turpin had kept it faithfully; but it was not easy to provide a horse for the occasion.", "이제 오지에는 자신의 갑옷을 요구했고, 그것은 최상의 상태로 그에게 전해졌으니, 선량한 튀르팽이 충실히 간직해 두었던 것이다. 그러나 이 일을 위해 말을 마련하는 일은 쉽지 않았다."], ["Charlemagne had the best horses of his stables brought out, except Blanchard, his own charger; but all in vain, the weight of Ogier bent their backs to the ground.", "샤를마뉴는 자신의 군마 블랑샤르를 제외하고 마구간의 가장 좋은 말들을 끌어내게 했으나, 모두 헛일이었다. 오지에의 무게에 말들의 등이 땅바닥까지 휘었던 것이다."], ["In this embarrassment the Archbishop remembered that the Emperor had given Beiffror to the Abbot of St. Faron, and sent off a courier in haste to re-demand him.", "이 곤란한 처지에서 대주교는 황제가 베이프로르를 생파롱의 수도원장에게 주었던 것을 기억해 내고, 그 말을 도로 청하러 급히 전령을 보냈다."]], [["Monks are hard masters, and the one who directed the laborers at the abbey had but too faithfully obeyed the orders of the Abbot.", "수도사들은 모진 주인들이라, 수도원에서 일꾼들을 지휘하던 자는 수도원장의 명령을 너무나도 충실히 따랐던 것이다."], ["Poor Beiffror was brought back, lean, spiritless, and chafed with the harness of the vile cart that he had had to draw so long.", "가엾은 베이프로르는 야위고 기운 없이, 그토록 오래 끌어야 했던 천한 짐수레의 마구에 쓸린 채 돌아왔다."], ["He carried his head down, and trod heavily before Charlemagne; but when he heard the voice of Ogier he raised his head, he neighed, his eyes flashed, his former ardor showed itself by the force with which he pawed the ground.", "그는 고개를 떨구고 샤를마뉴 앞에서 무겁게 발을 디뎠다. 그러나 오지에의 목소리를 듣자 고개를 쳐들고 울부짖었으며, 두 눈이 번뜩이고, 땅을 차는 그 힘으로 예전의 열정을 드러냈다."], ["Ogier caressed him, and the good steed seemed to return his caresses; Ogier mounted him, and Beiffror, proud of carrying his master again, leapt and curvetted with all his youthful vigor.", "오지에가 그를 어루만지자 그 충직한 군마는 그 손길에 화답하는 듯했고, 오지에가 올라타자 베이프로르는 다시 제 주인을 태우게 된 것을 자랑스러워하며 젊은 시절의 온 기력을 다해 뛰어오르고 껑충거렸다."]], [["Nothing being now wanted, Charlemagne, at the head of his army, marched forth from the city of Paris, and occupied the hill of Montmartre, whence the view extended over the plain of St.", "이제 더 부족한 것이 없게 되자, 샤를마뉴는 자신의 군대를 거느리고 파리 시에서 출진하여 몽마르트르 언덕을 점령했다."], ["Denis, where the battle was to be fought.", "그곳에서는 전투가 벌어질 생드니 평원이 한눈에 내려다보였다."]], [["When the appointed day came the Dukes Namo and Salomon, as seconds of Ogier, accompanied him to the place marked out for the lists, and Bruhier, with two distinguished Emirs, presented himself on the other side.", "정해진 날이 오자 나모 공작과 살로몬은 오지에의 입회인으로서 결투장으로 지정된 곳까지 그를 동행했고, 브뤼이에는 두 명의 저명한 에미르와 함께 반대편에 모습을 드러냈다."]], [["Bruhier was in high spirits, and jested with his friends, as he advanced, upon the appearance of Beiffror.", "브뤼이에는 의기양양하여, 나아가면서 베이프로르의 행색을 두고 자기 벗들과 농담을 주고받았다."], ["“Is that the horse they presume to match with Marchevallée, the best steed that ever fed in the vales of Mount Atlas?”", "\"저것이 아틀라스 산의 골짜기에서 풀을 뜯던 그 어떤 말보다 뛰어난 마르슈발레와 맞붙으려 내세운 말이란 말인가?\""], ["But now the combatants, having met and saluted each other, ride apart to come together in full career.", "그러나 이제 결투자들은 서로 만나 인사를 나눈 뒤, 전력으로 마주 달려오기 위해 따로 떨어졌다."], ["Beiffror flew over the plain, and met the adversary more than half-way.", "베이프로르는 평원 위를 날듯이 달려, 절반도 더 지난 지점에서 상대를 맞았다."], ["The lances of the two combatants were shivered at the shock, and Bruhier was astonished to see almost at the same instant the sword of Ogier gleaming above his head.", "두 결투자의 창은 충돌과 함께 산산이 부서졌고, 브뤼이에는 거의 같은 순간에 오지에의 칼이 제 머리 위에서 번뜩이는 것을 보고 놀랐다."], ["He parried it with his buckler, and gave Ogier a blow on his helmet, who returned it with another, better aimed or better seconded by the temper of his blade, for it cut away part of Bruhier’s helmet, and with it his ear and part of his cheek.", "그는 작은 방패로 그것을 막아 내고 오지에의 투구에 일격을 가했는데, 오지에는 더 정확히 겨누었거나 칼날의 단련이 더 잘 받쳐 준 또 다른 일격으로 되갚았으니, 그것은 브뤼이에의 투구 일부를 잘라 냈고 그와 함께 그의 귀와 뺨 일부를 베어 냈다."], ["Ogier, seeing the blood, did not immediately repeat his blow, and Bruhier seized the moment to gallop off at one side.", "피를 본 오지에는 곧바로 일격을 되풀이하지 않았고, 브뤼이에는 그 틈을 타 한쪽으로 말을 몰아 달아났다."], ["As he rode he took a vase of gold which hung at his saddle-bow, and bathed with its contents the wounded part.", "그는 달리면서 안장 앞고리에 걸려 있던 황금 항아리를 집어 그 내용물로 상처 입은 부위를 적셨다."], ["The blood instantly ceased to flow, the ear and the flesh were restored quite whole, and the Dane was astonished to see his antagonist return to the ground as sound as ever.", "피는 즉시 멎었고, 귀와 살은 완전히 온전하게 되살아났으며, 덴마크인은 자신의 적수가 예전과 다름없이 멀쩡하게 결투장으로 돌아오는 것을 보고 놀랐다."]], [["Bruhier laughed at his amazement.", "브뤼이에는 그의 놀라움을 비웃었다."], ["“Know,”", "\"알아 두어라,\""], ["said he, “that I possess the precious balm that Joseph of Arimathea used upon the body of the crucified one, whom you worship.", "그가 말했다. \"나는 아리마태아의 요셉이, 너희가 숭배하는 십자가에 못 박힌 자의 몸에 발랐던 그 귀한 향유를 지니고 있노라."], ["If I should lose an arm I could restore it with a few drops of this.", "내가 팔 하나를 잃는다 해도 이것 몇 방울이면 되살릴 수 있다."], ["It is useless for you to contend with me.", "나와 겨루는 것은 너에게 부질없는 일이다."], ["Yield yourself, and, as you appear to be a strong fellow, I will make you first oarsman in one of my galleys.”", "항복하라, 그러면 네가 건장한 사내로 보이니, 내 갤리선 한 척의 으뜸가는 노잡이로 삼아 주겠다.\""]], [["Ogier, though boiling with rage, forgot not to implore the assistance of Heaven.", "분노로 끓어오르면서도 오지에는 하늘의 도움을 간구하기를 잊지 않았다. \"오 주여!\""], ["“O Lord!”", "그가 외쳤다. \"당신 이름의 적이, 그 모든 효험을 당신의 신성한 피에 빚지고 있는 것의 강력한 도움으로 이득을 보지 못하게 하소서.\""], ["he exclaimed, “suffer not the enemy of thy name to profit by the powerful help of that which owes all its virtue to thy divine blood.”", "이 말과 함께 그는 그 어느 때보다 더 큰 기력으로 다시 브뤼이에를 공격했다. 둘 다 무서운 일격을 가하며 참혹한 상처를 입혔으나, 오지에의 상처에서는 피가 흘렀고 브뤼이에는 향유를 발라 자신의 피를 멎게 했다."], ["At these words he attacked Bruhier again with more vigor than ever; both struck terrible blows, and made grievous wounds; but the blood flowed from those of Ogier, while Bruhier stanched his by the application of his balm.", "불공평한 싸움에 절망한 오지에는 코르타나를 두 손으로 움켜쥐고 적에게 일격을 가했는데, 그것은 그의 작은 방패를 쪼개고 그와 함께 그의 팔을 잘라 냈다. 그러나 브뤼이에도 동시에 오지에에게 일격을 날렸으니, 그것은 오지에를 빗나가 베이프로르의 머리를 쳤고, 그 충직한 말은 쓰러지면서 제 주인을 함께 끌어내렸다."], ["Ogier, desperate at the unequal contest, grasped Cortana with both hands, and struck his enemy such a blow that it cleft his buckler, and cut off his arm with it; but Bruhier at the same time launched one at Ogier, which, missing him, struck the head of Beiffror, and the good horse fell, and drew down his master in his fall.", "브뤼이에는 땅으로 뛰어내려 자신의 팔을 주워 향유를 바를 시간이 있었다. 그러고는 오지에가 발을 채 추스르기도 전에, 그의 파멸을 마무리하려고 칼을 치켜든 채 앞으로 달려들었다."]], [["Bruhier had time to leap to the ground, to pick up his arm and apply his balsam; then, before Ogier had recovered his footing, he rushed forward with sword uplifted to complete his destruction.", "몽마르트르 언덕 위에서 용맹한 오지에가 이런 처지에 놓인 것을 본 샤를마뉴는 신음하며 섭리를 원망할 지경이었다. 그러나 선량한 튀르팽은 모세와 같은 믿음으로 두 팔을 치켜들어, 그 그리스도교 전사에게 하늘의 은총을 끌어내렸다."]], [["Charlemagne, from the height of Montmartre, seeing the brave Ogier in this situation, groaned, and was ready to murmur against Providence; but the good Turpin, raising his arms, with a faith like that of Moses, drew down upon the Christian warrior the favor of Heaven.", "오지에는 재빨리 몸을 빼내어 그토록 맹렬하게 브뤼이에를 몰아붙였고, 마침내 귀한 향유가 안장 앞고리에 걸려 있는 그의 말로부터 그를 멀리 떼어 놓았다. 그리고 곧 샤를마뉴는 이제 완전히 우세를 점한 오지에가 적을 무릎 꿇리고 투구를 벗겨 낸 뒤, 한 번의 칼질로 그의 몸에서 머리를 베어 내는 것을 보았다."]], [["Ogier, promptly disengaging himself, pressed Bruhier with so much impetuosity that he drove him to a distance from his horse, to whose saddle-bow the precious balm was suspended; and very soon Charlemagne saw Ogier, now completely in the advantage, bring his enemy to his knees, tear off his helmet, and, with a sweep of his sword, strike his head from his body.", "승리한 뒤 오지에는 마르슈발레를 붙잡아 그 등에 뛰어올라, 그 귀한 작은 병을 차지하게 되었으니, 거기서 몇 방울만으로도 그의 상처가 아물고 기력이 회복되었다. 브뤼이에의 포로였다가 이제 풀려난 프랑스 기사들이 자신들의 구출에 감사하려고 오지에 주위로 몰려들었다."]], [["After the victory, Ogier seized Marchevallée, leaped upon his back, and became possessed of the precious flask, a few drops from which closed his wounds and restored his strength.", "샤를마뉴와 그의 귀족들은 일대일 결투에서 주의가 풀리자마자, 자신들이 있던 높은 자리에서 적진의 심상치 않은 동요를 알아챘다."], ["The French knights who had been Bruhier’s captives, now released, pressed round Ogier to thank him for their deliverance.", "그들은 처음에는 그것을 적장의 죽음 탓으로 돌렸으나, 곧 무기 부딪치는 소리와 결투자들의 함성, 그리고 새로이 진군해 오는 깃발들이, 브뤼이에의 군대가 새로운 적의 공격을 받고 있다는 사실을 그들에게 드러냈다."]], [["Charlemagne and his nobles, as soon as their attention was relieved from the single combat, perceived from their elevated position an unusual agitation in the enemy’s camp.", "황제의 짐작은 옳았다."], ["They attributed it at first to the death of their general, but soon the noise of arms, the cries of combatants, and new standards which advanced, disclosed to them the fact that Bruhier’s army was attacked by a new enemy.", "그것은 용맹한 마우리타니아의 카라우에로, 자신의 무기 형제인 오지에를 해방시키려 결심하고 군대와 함께 프랑스에 당도한 것이었다."], ["The Emperor was right; it was the brave Carahue of Mauritania, who, with an army, had arrived in France, resolved to attempt the liberation of Ogier, his brother in arms. Learning on his arrival the changed aspect of affairs, he hesitated not to render a signal service to the Emperor, by attacking the army of Bruhier in the midst of the consternation occasioned by the loss of its commander.", "도착하여 정세가 바뀐 것을 알게 된 그는, 지휘관을 잃어 경악에 빠진 와중의 브뤼이에 군대를 공격함으로써, 황제에게 두드러진 도움을 베푸는 데 주저하지 않았다."]], [["Ogier recognized the standard of his friend, and leaping upon Marchevallée, flew to aid his attack.", "오지에는 자기 벗의 깃발을 알아보고 마르슈발레에 뛰어올라 그의 공격을 돕고자 날아갔다."], ["Charlemagne followed with his army; and the Saracen host, after an obstinate conflict, was forced to surrender unconditionally.", "샤를마뉴도 자신의 군대를 이끌고 뒤따랐다. 사라센 무리는 완강한 싸움 끝에 무조건 항복하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["The interview of Ogier and Carahue was such as might be anticipated of two such attached friends and accomplished knights.", "오지에와 카라우에의 만남은, 그토록 정 깊은 두 벗이자 출중한 두 기사에게서 기대할 법한 그러한 것이었다."], ["Charlemagne went to meet them, embraced them, and putting the King of Mauritania on his right and Ogier on his left, returned with triumph to Paris.", "샤를마뉴는 그들을 맞으러 나가 끌어안고는, 마우리타니아 왕을 자신의 오른편에, 오지에를 왼편에 두고 의기양양하게 파리로 돌아왔다."], ["There the Empress Bertha and the ladies of her court crowned them with laurels, and the sage and gallant Eginhard, chamberlain and secretary of the Emperor, wrote all these great events in his history.", "그곳에서 황후 베르타와 그 궁정의 귀부인들이 그들에게 월계관을 씌워 주었고, 황제의 시종이자 비서인 현명하고 용감한 에긴하르트는 이 모든 위대한 사건들을 자신의 역사서에 기록했다."]], [["A few days after Guyon, King of Denmark, arrived in France with a chosen band of knights, and sent an ambassador to Charlemagne, to say that he came, not as an enemy, but to render homage to him as the best knight of the time and the head of the Christian world.", "며칠 뒤 덴마크 왕 기용이 정예 기사들을 거느리고 프랑스에 당도하여, 샤를마뉴에게 사절을 보내 자신이 적으로서가 아니라, 당대 최고의 기사이자 그리스도교 세계의 수장인 그에게 경의를 표하러 왔노라 전했다."], ["Charlemagne gave the ambassador a cordial reception, and mounting his horse, rode forward to meet the King of Denmark.", "샤를마뉴는 사절을 진심으로 맞이하고, 말에 올라 덴마크 왕을 맞으러 앞으로 나아갔다."]], [["These great princes, being assembled at the court of Charles, held council together, and the ancient and sage barons were called to join it.", "이 위대한 군주들은 샤를의 궁정에 모여 함께 회의를 열었고, 연로하고 현명한 영주들도 그 자리에 부름을 받았다."]], [["It was decided that the united Danish and Mauritanian armies should cross the sea and carry the war to the country of the Saracens, and that a thousand French knights should range themselves under the banner of Ogier, the Dane, who, though not a king, should have equal rank with the two others.", "덴마크와 마우리타니아의 연합군이 바다를 건너 사라센인들의 나라로 전쟁을 가져가기로, 그리고 천 명의 프랑스 기사가 덴마크인 오지에의 깃발 아래 늘어서기로 결정되었다. 오지에는 비록 왕은 아니었으나 다른 두 사람과 동등한 지위를 갖게 되었다."]], [["We have not space to record all the illustrious actions performed by Ogier and his allies in this war.", "이 전쟁에서 오지에와 그의 동맹들이 수행한 빛나는 행적을 모두 기록할 지면은 우리에게 없다."], ["Suffice it to say, they subdued the Saracens of Ptolemais and Judæa, and, erecting those regions into a kingdom, placed the crown upon the head of Ogier.", "그들이 프톨레마이스와 유다의 사라센인들을 굴복시키고, 그 지역들을 하나의 왕국으로 세워 오지에의 머리에 왕관을 씌웠다는 것만 말해 두면 족할 것이다."], ["Guyon and Carahue then left him, to return to their respective dominions.", "그러고서 기용과 카라우에는 각자의 영토로 돌아가고자 그를 떠났다."], ["Ogier adopted Walter, the son of Guyon of Denmark, to be his successor in his kingdom.", "오지에는 덴마크 기용의 아들 발터를 양자로 삼아 자신의 왕국을 이을 후계자로 정했다."], ["He superintended his education, and saw the young prince grow up worthy of his cares.", "그는 그의 교육을 감독했고, 그 젊은 왕자가 자신의 보살핌에 값할 만하게 자라나는 것을 지켜보았다."], ["But Ogier, in spite of all the honors of his rank, often regretted the court of Charlemagne, the Duke Namo, and Salomon of Brittany, for whom he had the respect and attachment of a son.", "그러나 오지에는 자신의 지위에 따르는 온갖 영예에도 불구하고, 자식과 같은 존경과 애정을 품었던 샤를마뉴의 궁정과 나모 공작, 브르타뉴의 살로몬을 자주 그리워했다."], ["At last, finding Walter old enough to sustain the weight of government, Ogier caused a vessel to be prepared secretly, and, attended only by one squire, left his palace by night, and embarked to return to France.", "마침내 발터가 통치의 무게를 감당할 만큼 자란 것을 보고, 오지에는 은밀히 배 한 척을 마련하게 하여, 종자 한 사람만을 거느린 채 밤에 자신의 궁전을 떠나 프랑스로 돌아가고자 배에 올랐다."]], [["The vessel, driven by a fair wind, cut the sea with the swiftness of a bird; but on a sudden it deviated from its course, no longer obeyed the helm, and sped fast towards a black promontory which stretched into the sea.", "순풍에 밀린 배는 새처럼 빠르게 바다를 갈랐다. 그러나 갑자기 배는 항로에서 벗어나 더는 키를 따르지 않고, 바다로 뻗어 나온 검은 곶을 향해 빠르게 내달았다."], ["This was a mountain of loadstone, and, its attractive power increasing as the distance diminished, the vessel at last flew with the swiftness of an arrow towards it, and was dashed to pieces on its rocky base.", "그것은 자석의 산이었고, 거리가 줄어들수록 그 끌어당기는 힘이 커져, 배는 마침내 화살 같은 빠르기로 그것을 향해 날아가, 그 바위투성이 기슭에 부딪혀 산산조각 났다."], ["Ogier alone saved himself, and reached the shore on a fragment of the wreck.", "오지에만이 홀로 목숨을 건져, 난파선 조각 하나에 의지해 기슭에 닿았다."]], [["Ogier advanced into the country, looking for some marks of inhabitancy, but found none.", "오지에는 사람이 사는 흔적을 찾으며 그 땅 안쪽으로 나아갔으나 아무것도 찾지 못했다."], ["On a sudden he encountered two monstrous animals, covered with glittering scales, accompanied by a horse breathing fire.", "갑자기 그는 반짝이는 비늘로 덮인 두 마리의 괴물 같은 짐승과 마주쳤는데, 불을 내뿜는 말 한 마리가 그것들을 따르고 있었다."], ["Ogier drew his sword and prepared to defend himself; but the monsters, terrific as they appeared, made no attempt to assail him, and the horse, Papillon, knelt down, and appeared to court Ogier to mount upon his back.", "오지에는 칼을 빼 들고 몸을 지킬 채비를 했다. 그러나 무시무시해 보이던 그 괴물들은 그를 공격하려는 시도를 하지 않았고, 파피용이라는 그 말은 무릎을 꿇고 오지에에게 제 등에 올라타기를 청하는 듯했다."], ["Ogier hesitated not to see the adventure through; he mounted Papillon, who ran with speed, and soon cleared the rocks and precipices which hemmed in and concealed a beautiful landscape.", "오지에는 그 모험을 끝까지 보는 것을 주저하지 않았다. 그가 파피용에 올라타자 말은 빠르게 달려, 곧 아름다운 풍경을 에워싸 감추고 있던 바위와 벼랑을 넘어갔다."], ["He continued his course till he reached a magnificent palace, and, without allowing Ogier time to admire it, crossed a grand courtyard adorned with colonnades, and entered a garden, where, making his way through alleys of myrtle, he checked his course, and knelt down on the enamelled turf of a fountain.", "말은 계속 달려 어느 웅장한 궁전에 이르렀고, 오지에에게 그것을 감상할 틈도 주지 않은 채, 열주들로 장식된 큰 안뜰을 가로질러 한 정원으로 들어갔다. 그곳에서 말은 도금양 가로수 길을 헤치고 나아가다가 걸음을 멈추고, 샘가의 에나멜 같은 풀밭에 무릎을 꿇었다."], ["Ogier dismounted and took some steps along the margin of the stream, but was soon stopped by meeting a young beauty, such as they paint the Graces, and almost as lightly attired as they.", "오지에는 말에서 내려 시냇가를 따라 몇 걸음 옮겼으나, 곧 한 젊은 미인을 만나 멈춰 서고 말았으니, 그녀는 화가들이 〈우미의 여신들〉을 그리는 그대로의 모습이었고, 거의 그들만큼이나 가볍게 차려입고 있었다."], ["At the same moment, to his amazement, his armor fell off of its own accord.", "바로 그 순간, 놀랍게도 그의 갑옷이 저절로 벗겨져 떨어졌다."], ["The young beauty advanced with a tender air, and placed upon his head a crown of flowers.", "젊은 미인은 다정한 태도로 다가와 그의 머리에 꽃의 관을 씌웠다. 그 순간 덴마크의 영웅은 기억을 잃었다."], ["At that instant the Danish hero lost his memory; his combats, his glory, Charlemagne and his court, all vanished from his mind; he saw only Morgana, he desired nothing but to sigh forever at her feet.", "그의 전투들, 그의 영광, 샤를마뉴와 그의 궁정, 그 모두가 그의 마음에서 사라졌다. 그는 오직 모르가나만을 보았고, 영원히 그녀의 발치에서 한숨짓는 것 외에는 아무것도 바라지 않았다."]], [["We abridge the narrative of all the delights which Ogier enjoyed for more than a hundred years.", "오지에가 백 년 넘게 누린 그 모든 환락의 이야기는 줄여 두기로 한다."], ["Time flew by, leaving no impression of its flight.", "시간은 흘러갔으나 그 흐름의 흔적은 남기지 않았다."], ["Morgana’s youthful charms did not decay, and Ogier had none of those warnings of increasing years which less favored mortals never fail to receive.", "모르가나의 젊은 매력은 쇠하지 않았고, 오지에는 덜 은혜로운 필멸자들이 결코 받지 않을 수 없는, 나이 들어 감을 알리는 그런 경고를 전혀 받지 않았다."], ["There is no knowing how long this blissful state might have lasted, if it had not been for an accident, by which Morgana one day, in a sportive moment, snatched the crown from his head.", "어느 날 모르가나가 장난기 어린 순간에 그의 머리에서 관을 낚아채 버린 사고만 아니었더라면, 이 더없이 행복한 상태가 얼마나 오래 이어졌을지 알 수 없는 일이다."], ["That moment Ogier regained his memory, and lost his contentment.", "그 순간 오지에는 기억을 되찾았고, 만족을 잃었다."], ["The recollection of Charlemagne, and of his own relatives and friends, saddened the hours which he passed with Morgana.", "샤를마뉴와 자신의 친족과 벗들에 대한 기억이, 모르가나와 함께 보내는 시간들을 서글프게 했다."], ["The fairy saw with grief the changed looks of her lover.", "그 요정은 슬픔에 잠겨 자기 연인의 달라진 표정을 지켜보았다."], ["At last she drew from him the acknowledgment that he wished to go, at least for a time, to revisit Charles’s court.", "마침내 그녀는 그에게서, 적어도 한동안은 샤를의 궁정을 다시 찾고 싶다는 고백을 끌어냈다."], ["She consented with reluctance, and with her own hands helped to reinvest him with his armor.", "그녀는 마지못해 동의했고, 자신의 손으로 그에게 갑옷을 다시 입혀 주었다."], ["Papillon was led forth, Ogier mounted him, and, taking a tender adieu of the tearful Morgana, crossed at rapid speed the rocky belt which separated Morgana’s palace from the borders of the sea.", "파피용이 끌려 나왔고, 오지에는 그 위에 올라, 눈물짓는 모르가나에게 다정한 작별을 고하고는, 모르가나의 궁전을 바다의 경계와 갈라놓는 바위 띠를 빠른 속도로 가로질렀다."]], [["The sea-goblins which had received him at his coming awaited him on the shore.", "그가 올 때 그를 맞이했던 바다의 도깨비들이 기슭에서 그를 기다리고 있었다."], ["One of them took Ogier on his back, and the other placing himself under Papillon, they spread their broad fins, and in a short time traversed the wide space that separates the isle of Avalon from France.", "그 가운데 하나는 오지에를 제 등에 태우고, 다른 하나는 파피용 밑으로 들어가, 넓은 지느러미를 펼쳐 짧은 시간에 아발론 섬과 프랑스를 가르는 드넓은 공간을 가로질렀다."], ["They landed Ogier on the coast of Languedoc, and then plunged into the sea and disappeared.", "그들은 오지에를 랑그도크 해안에 내려놓고는 바닷속으로 뛰어들어 사라졌다."]], [["Ogier remounted on Papillon, who carried him across the kingdom almost as fast as he had passed the sea.", "오지에는 다시 파피용에 올라탔고, 말은 바다를 건넜던 것만큼이나 빠르게 그를 태우고 왕국을 가로질렀다."], ["He arrived under the walls of Paris, which he would scarcely have recognized if the high towers of St. Genevieve had not caught his eye.", "그는 파리의 성벽 아래에 당도했는데, 생주느비에브의 높은 탑들이 그의 눈에 띄지 않았더라면 그곳을 거의 알아보지 못했을 것이다."], ["He went straight to the palace of Charlemagne, which seemed to him to have been entirely rebuilt.", "그는 곧장 샤를마뉴의 궁전으로 향했는데, 그곳은 완전히 다시 지어진 듯 보였다."], ["His surprise was extreme, and increased still more on finding that he understood with difficulty the language of the guards and attendants in replying to his questions; and seeing them smile as they tried to explain to one another the language in which he addressed them.", "그의 놀라움은 극에 달했고, 호위병들과 시종들이 그의 물음에 답하는 말을 알아듣기 어렵다는 것을 알게 되자, 그리고 그들이 그가 건넨 말을 서로에게 설명하려 애쓰며 미소 짓는 것을 보자, 그의 놀라움은 한층 더 커졌다."], ["Presently the attention of some of the barons who were going to court was attracted to the scene, and Ogier, who recognized the badges of their rank, addressed them, and inquired if the Dukes Namo and Salomon were still residing at the Emperor’s court.", "이윽고 궁정으로 가던 몇몇 영주들의 주의가 그 광경에 끌렸고, 그들의 지위를 나타내는 표장을 알아본 오지에는 그들에게 말을 건네, 나모 공작과 살로몬이 아직도 황제의 궁정에 머무르고 있는지 물었다. 이 물음에 영주들은 어리둥절하여 서로를 바라보았다."], ["At this question the barons looked at one another in amazement; and one of the eldest said to the rest, “How much this knight resembles the portrait of my grand-uncle, Ogier the Dane.”", "그리고 가장 나이 든 사람 하나가 나머지 사람들에게 말했다. \"이 기사가 내 종조부 덴마크인 오지에의 초상화를 어찌나 닮았는지.\""], ["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["my dear nephew, I am Ogier the Dane,”", "친애하는 조카여, 내가 바로 덴마크인 오지에라네.\""], ["said he; and he remembered that Morgana had told him that he was little aware of the flight of time during his abode with her.", "그가 말했다. 그리고 그는 모르가나와 함께 머무는 동안 시간이 얼마나 흘렀는지 자신이 거의 알지 못하리라고 그녀가 말해 주었던 것을 떠올렸다."]], [["The barons, more astonished than ever, concluded to conduct him to the monarch who then reigned, the great Hugh Capet.", "그 어느 때보다 놀란 영주들은, 그를 당시 다스리던 군주, 위대한 위그 카페에게 안내하기로 했다."]], [["The brave Ogier entered the palace without hesitation; but when, on reaching the royal hall, the barons directed him to make his obeisance to the King of France, he was astonished to see a man of short stature and large head, whose air, nevertheless, was noble and martial, seated upon the throne on which he had so often seen Charlemagne, the tallest and handsomest sovereign of his time.", "용맹한 오지에는 주저 없이 궁전에 들어섰다. 그러나 왕실의 홀에 이르렀을 때, 영주들이 그에게 프랑스 왕에게 경의를 표하라고 일렀는데, 그는 자신이 그토록 자주 샤를마뉴를 보았던 그 옥좌에 키가 작고 머리가 큰 한 사내가 앉아 있는 것을 보고 놀랐다. 그렇지만 그의 풍모는 고귀하고 무인다웠다. 샤를마뉴는 당대에 가장 키가 크고 가장 잘생긴 군주였던 것이다."]], [["Ogier recounted his adventures with simplicity and unaffectedness.", "오지에는 자신의 모험을 꾸밈없이 소박하게 이야기했다."], ["Hugh Capet was slow to believe him; but Ogier recalled so many proofs and circumstances, that at last he was forced to recognize the aged warrior to be the famous Ogier the Dane.", "위그 카페는 좀처럼 그를 믿으려 하지 않았다. 그러나 오지에가 너무나 많은 증거와 정황을 떠올렸으므로, 마침내 그는 그 늙은 전사가 저 유명한 덴마크인 오지에임을 인정하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["The king informed Ogier of the events which had taken place during his long absence; that the line of Charlemagne was extinct; that a new dynasty had commenced; that the old enemies of the kingdom, the Saracens, were still troublesome; and that at that very time an army of those miscreants was besieging the city of Chartres, to which he was about to repair in a few days to its relief.", "왕은 오지에에게 그의 오랜 부재 동안 일어난 사건들을 알려 주었다. 샤를마뉴의 혈통이 끊겼다는 것, 새로운 왕조가 시작되었다는 것, 왕국의 오랜 적인 사라센인들이 여전히 골칫거리라는 것, 그리고 바로 그때에도 그 악당들의 군대가 샤르트르 시를 포위하고 있어, 자신이 며칠 안에 그곳을 구원하러 갈 참이라는 것이었다."], ["Ogier, always inflamed with the love of glory, offered the service of his arm, which the illustrious monarch accepted graciously, and conducted him to the queen.", "언제나 영광에 대한 사랑으로 불타던 오지에는 자신의 팔의 봉사를 자청했고, 그 명망 높은 군주는 이를 정중히 받아들이며 그를 왕비에게 데려갔다."], ["The astonishment of Ogier was redoubled when he saw the new ornaments and head-dresses of the ladies; still, the beautiful hair which they built up on their foreheads, and the feathers interwoven, which waved with so much grace, gave them a noble air that delighted him.", "귀부인들의 새로운 장신구와 머리 장식을 보고 오지에의 놀라움은 곱절이 되었다. 그래도 그들이 이마 위에 높이 쌓아 올린 아름다운 머리카락과, 그토록 우아하게 흔들리는 사이사이 엮어 넣은 깃털은 그를 매료시키는 고귀한 분위기를 그들에게 부여했다."], ["His admiration increased when, instead of the old Empress Bertha, he saw a young queen who combined a majestic mien with the graces of her time of life, and manners candid and charming, suited to attach all hearts.", "그의 감탄은 더욱 커졌으니, 옛 황후 베르타 대신, 위엄 있는 풍모에 그 나이 또래의 우아함을, 그리고 모든 이의 마음을 사로잡기에 알맞은 솔직하고 매력적인 태도를 겸비한 젊은 왕비를 보았던 것이다."], ["Ogier saluted the youthful queen with a respect so profound that many of the courtiers took him for a foreigner, or at least for some nobleman brought up at a distance from Paris, who retained the manners of what they called the old court.", "오지에는 그 젊은 왕비에게 너무나 깊은 존경을 담아 인사했으므로, 많은 신하들이 그를 외국인으로, 혹은 적어도 파리에서 멀리 떨어진 곳에서 자라나 그들이 〈옛 궁정〉이라 부르던 시절의 예법을 간직한 어느 귀족으로 여겼다."]], [["When the queen was informed by her husband that it was the celebrated Ogier the Dane whom he presented to her, whose memorable exploits she had often read in the chronicles of antiquity, her surprise was extreme, which was increased when she remarked the dignity of his address, the animation and even the youthfulness of his countenance.", "자신의 남편으로부터, 그가 소개하는 이가 고대의 연대기에서 그 인상적인 공적을 자주 읽었던 저 유명한 덴마크인 오지에라는 것을 전해 들은 왕비의 놀라움은 극에 달했으니, 그의 품위 있는 태도와, 그의 얼굴에 깃든 생기와 심지어 젊음마저 알아채자 그 놀라움은 더욱 커졌다. 이 왕비는 너무나 총명하여 섣불리 믿지 않았다."], ["This queen had too much intelligence to believe hastily; proof alone could compel her assent; and she asked him many questions about the old court of Charlemagne, and received such instructive and appropriate answers as removed every doubt.", "오직 증거만이 그녀의 동의를 끌어낼 수 있었다. 그래서 그녀는 그에게 샤를마뉴의 옛 궁정에 관해 많은 질문을 던졌고, 모든 의심을 거두게 할 만큼 유익하고 적절한 답을 들었다."], ["It is to the corrections which Ogier was at that time enabled to make to the popular narratives of his exploits that we are indebted for the perfect accuracy and trustworthiness of all the details of our own history.", "우리 자신의 역사의 모든 세부가 완벽하게 정확하고 믿을 만한 것은, 바로 그때 오지에가 자신의 공적에 관한 통속적인 이야기들에 가할 수 있었던 그 교정들 덕분이다."]], [["King Hugh Capet, having received that same evening couriers from the inhabitants of Chartres, informing him that they were hard pressed by the besiegers, resolved to hasten with Ogier to their relief.", "위그 카페 왕은 바로 그날 저녁 샤르트르 주민들로부터, 자신들이 포위군에게 심하게 시달리고 있다는 소식을 전하는 전령들을 받고, 오지에와 함께 서둘러 그들을 구원하러 가기로 결심했다."]], [["Ogier terminated this affair as expeditiously as he had so often done others.", "오지에는 그토록 자주 다른 일들을 처리했던 것처럼 신속하게 이 일을 마무리했다."], ["The Saracens having dared to offer battle, he bore the Oriflamme through the thickest of their ranks; Papillon, breathing fire from his nostrils, threw them into disorder, and Cortana, wielded by his invincible arm, soon finished their overthrow.", "사라센인들이 감히 싸움을 걸어오자, 그는 그들의 가장 두터운 대열을 뚫고 오리플람므를 들고 나아갔다. 콧구멍에서 불을 내뿜는 파피용이 그들을 혼란에 빠뜨렸고, 그의 무적의 팔에 휘둘린 코르타나가 곧 그들의 패주를 마무리했다."]], [["The king, victorious over the Saracens, led back the Danish hero to Paris, where the deliverer of France received the honors due to his valor.", "사라센인들을 물리치고 승리한 왕은 덴마크의 영웅을 파리로 데리고 돌아왔고, 그곳에서 프랑스의 구원자는 자신의 용맹에 합당한 영예를 받았다."], ["Ogier continued some time at the court, detained by the favor of the king and queen; but erelong he had the pain to witness the death of the king.", "오지에는 왕과 왕비의 총애에 붙들려 한동안 궁정에 머물렀다. 그러나 머지않아 그는 왕의 죽음을 목격하는 고통을 겪었다."], ["Then it was that, impressed with all the perfections which he had discerned in the queen, he could not withhold the tender homage of the offer of his hand.", "바로 그때, 왕비에게서 알아본 온갖 완덕에 감명받은 그는, 자신의 손을 청하는 다정한 경의를 표하지 않을 수 없었다. 왕비는 어쩌면 그것을 받아들였을지도 모른다."], ["The queen would perhaps have accepted it, she had even called a meeting of her great barons to deliberate on the proposition, when, the day before the meeting was to be held, at the moment when Ogier was kneeling at her feet, she perceived a crown of gold which an invisible hand had placed on his brow, and in an instant a cloud enveloped Ogier, and he disappeared forever from her sight.", "그녀는 그 제안을 숙고하고자 자신의 대영주들의 회의까지 소집했던 것이다. 그런데 회의가 열리기로 한 전날, 오지에가 그녀의 발치에 무릎 꿇고 있던 순간, 그녀는 보이지 않는 손이 그의 이마에 씌워 놓은 황금의 관을 알아보았고, 순식간에 구름이 오지에를 감싸더니, 그는 그녀의 시야에서 영영 사라졌다."], ["It was Morgana, the fairy, whose jealousy was awakened at what she beheld, who now resumed her power, and took him away to dwell with her in the island of Avalon.", "자신이 본 것에 질투가 일었던 요정 모르가나가, 이제 자신의 힘을 되찾아 그를 데려가 아발론 섬에서 자신과 함께 살게 한 것이었다."], ["There, in company with the great King Arthur of Britain, he still lives, and when his illustrious friend shall return to resume his ancient reign he will doubtless return with him, and share his triumph.", "그곳에서 그는 브리튼의 위대한 아서 왕과 함께 여전히 살고 있으며, 그의 명망 높은 벗이 옛 통치를 다시 잇고자 돌아올 때면 틀림없이 그와 함께 돌아와 그의 승리를 나눌 것이다. 각주"]], [["-----", "-----"]], [["[1] Wordsworth.", "[1] 워즈워스."]], [["[2] The names included in parentheses are the Greek, the others being the Roman or Latin names.", "[2] 괄호 안에 든 이름은 그리스식이며, 나머지는 로마식 또는 라틴식 이름이다."]], [["[3] This inconsistency arises from considering the Saturn of the Romans the same with the Grecian deity Cronos (Time), which, as it brings an end to all things which have had a beginning, may be said to devour its own offspring.", "[3] 이 불일치는 로마인의 사투르누스를 그리스 신 크로노스(시간)와 동일시한 데서 비롯되는데, 크로노스는 시작이 있는 모든 것에 끝을 가져오므로 자기 자식을 삼킨다고 말할 수 있다."]], [["[4] From this origin of the instrument, the word “shell” is often used as synonymous with “lyre,” and figuratively for music and poetry. Thus Gray, in his ode on the “Progress of Poesy,” says:", "[4] 이 악기의 기원에서, \"껍데기\"라는 말이 흔히 \"리라\"와 같은 뜻으로, 또 비유적으로 음악과 시를 가리키는 데 쓰인다. 그래서 그레이는 「시의 진보」라는 송시에서 이렇게 말한다."]], [["“O Sovereign of the willing Soul, Parent of sweet and solemn-breathing airs, Enchanting shell!", "\"오 기꺼운 영혼의 군주여, 감미롭고 장엄하게 숨 쉬는 가락의 어버이여, 매혹하는 껍데기여!"], ["the sullen Cares And frantic Passions hear thy soft control.”", "시무룩한 시름과 광란하는 정념이 그대의 부드러운 다스림을 듣는도다.\""]], [["[5] There was also a goddess called Fauna, or Bona Dea.", "[5] 파우나, 곧 보나 데아라 불린 여신도 있었다."]], [["[6] The goddess of innocence and purity.", "[6] 순결과 정결의 여신."], ["After leaving earth, she was placed among the stars, where she became the constellation Virgo—the Virgin.", "그녀는 지상을 떠난 뒤 별들 사이에 놓여 처녀자리—처녀—라는 별자리가 되었다."], ["Themis (Justice) was the mother of Astræa.", "테미스(정의)가 아스트라이아의 어머니였다."], ["She is represented as holding aloft a pair of scales, in which she weighs the claims of opposing parties.", "그녀는 한 쌍의 저울을 높이 치켜들고, 거기에 맞서는 양편의 주장을 다는 모습으로 그려진다."]], [["It was a favorite idea of the old poets that these goddesses would one day return, and bring back the Golden Age.", "옛 시인들이 즐겨 품었던 생각은, 이 여신들이 언젠가 돌아와 황금시대를 되돌려 놓으리라는 것이었다."], ["Even in a Christian hymn, the “Messiah” of Pope, this idea occurs:", "어느 그리스도교 찬가, 곧 포프의 「메시아」에서도 이 생각이 나타난다."]], [["“All crimes shall cease, and ancient fraud shall fail, Returning Justice lift aloft her scale, Peace o’er the world her olive wand extend, And white-robed Innocence from heaven descend.”", "\"온갖 죄악이 그치고 옛 기만이 무너지리니, 돌아온 정의가 그 저울을 높이 치켜들고, 평화가 세상에 그 올리브 가지를 펼치며, 흰옷 입은 순결이 하늘에서 내려오리라.\""]], [["See, also, Milton’s “Hymn on the Nativity,”", "밀턴의 「예수 강탄 찬가」"], ["stanzas xiv. and xv.", "14연과 15연도 보라."]], [["[7] See Proverbial Expressions.", "[7] 관용 표현을 보라."]], [["[8] See Proverbial Expressions.", "[8] 관용 표현을 보라."]], [["[9] See Proverbial Expressions.", "[9] 관용 표현을 보라."]], [["[10] See Proverbial Expressions.", "[10] 관용 표현을 보라."]], [["[11] See page 241.", "[11] 241면을 보라."]], [["[12] It is evidently not our modern hyacinth that is here described.", "[12] 여기서 묘사되는 것은 분명 우리가 아는 오늘날의 히아신스는 아니다."], ["It is perhaps some species of iris, or perhaps of larkspur or of pansy.", "아마도 붓꽃이나 참제비고깔, 혹은 팬지의 일종일 것이다."]], [["[13] The sunflower.", "[13] 해바라기."]], [["[14] This correct description of the rainbow is literally translated from Ovid.", "[14] 무지개에 대한 이 정확한 묘사는 오비디우스의 글을 문자 그대로 옮긴 것이다."]], [["[15] Sir James Mackintosh says of this, “Do you think that even a Chinese could paint the gay colors of a butterfly with more minute exactness than the following lines: ‘The velvet nap,’ etc.?”—Life, Vol.", "[15] 제임스 매킨토시 경은 이에 대해 이렇게 말한다. \"그대는 중국인이라 한들 나비의 화사한 빛깔을, 다음 시구—'그 벨벳 같은 솜털'"], ["II., 246.", "운운—보다 더 세밀하고 정확하게 그려 낼 수 있으리라 생각하는가?\"—「생애」, 제2권, 246면."]], [["[16] See Proverbial Expressions.", "[16] 관용 표현을 보라."]], [["[17] Hecate was a mysterious divinity sometimes identified with Diana and sometimes with Proserpine.", "[17] 헤카테는 신비로운 여신으로, 때로는 디아나와, 때로는 프로세르피나와 동일시되었다."], ["As Diana represents the moonlight splendor of night, so Hecate represents its darkness and terrors.", "디아나가 밤의 달빛 어린 찬란함을 나타낸다면, 헤카테는 그 어둠과 공포를 나타낸다."], ["She was the goddess of sorcery and witchcraft, and was believed to wander by night along the earth, seen only by the dogs, whose barking told her approach.", "그녀는 마법과 주술의 여신으로, 밤이면 땅 위를 떠돈다고 믿어졌으며, 오직 개들만이 그녀를 알아보고 짖어 그 다가옴을 알렸다고 한다."]], [["[18] Alcides, a name of Hercules.", "[18] 알키데스, 헤라클레스의 다른 이름."]], [["[19] One of the finest pieces of sculpture in Italy, the recumbent Ariadne of the Vatican, represents this incident.", "[19] 이탈리아에서 가장 빼어난 조각품 가운데 하나인 바티칸의 「누운 아리아드네」가 바로 이 장면을 묘사한다."], ["A copy is owned by the Athenæum, Boston, and deposited in the Museum of Fine Arts.", "그 사본 하나를 보스턴 아테나이움이 소장하여 현재 보스턴 미술관에 기탁해 두고 있다."]], [["[20] Proteus.", "[20] 프로테우스."]], [["[21] The story of the invulnerability of Achilles is not found in Homer, and is inconsistent with his account.", "[21] 아킬레우스가 불사신의 몸이었다는 이야기는 호메로스에게서는 찾아볼 수 없으며, 그의 서술과도 맞지 않는다."], ["For how could Achilles require the aid of celestial armor if he were invulnerable?", "아킬레우스가 정녕 상처 입지 않는 몸이었다면, 어찌 천상의 갑옷이 필요했겠는가?"]], [["[22] Tennyson has chosen Œnone as the subject of a short poem; but he has omitted the most poetical part of the story, the return of Paris wounded, her cruelty and subsequent repentance.", "[22] 테니슨은 오이노네를 소재로 짧은 시를 지었으나, 이야기 가운데 가장 시적인 대목—상처 입은 파리스가 돌아오는 장면, 그녀의 매정함과 그 뒤의 뉘우침—은 빼놓았다."]], [["[23] See Proverbial Expressions.", "[23] 관용 표현을 보라."]], [["[24] Pyrrhus’s exclamation, “Not such aid nor such defenders does the time require,” has become proverbial.", "[24] 피로스가 외친 말, \"지금 이 순간이 요구하는 것은 그런 도움도 그런 수호자도 아니다\"는 하나의 격언이 되었다."], ["See Proverbial Expressions.", "관용 표현을 보라."]], [["[25] Tennyson in the “Lotus-eaters” has charmingly expressed the dreamy, languid feeling which the lotus food is said to have produced.", "[25] 테니슨은 「연꽃 먹는 사람들」에서, 연꽃 열매가 자아낸다는 그 몽롱하고 나른한 느낌을 매혹적으로 그려 냈다."]], [["“How sweet it were, hearing the downward stream With half-shut eyes ever to seem Falling asleep in a half dream!", "\"아, 얼마나 달콤할까, 흘러내리는 시내 소리를 들으며 반쯤 감은 눈으로 언제까지나 어렴풋한 잠에 빠져드는 듯한 그 느낌이!"], ["To dream and dream, like yonder amber light Which will not leave the myrrh-bush on the height; To hear each others’ whispered speech; Eating the Lotos, day by day, To watch the crisping ripples on the beach, And tender curving lines of creamy spray: To lend our hearts and spirits wholly To the influence of mild-minded melancholy; To muse and brood and live again in memory, With those old faces of our infancy Heaped over with a mound of grass, Two handfuls of white dust, shut in an urn of brass.”", "저 높은 곳의 몰약나무를 떠나지 않는 호박빛 노을처럼 꿈꾸고 또 꿈꾸는 것이, 서로 속삭이는 말을 듣는 것이, 나날이 로토스 열매를 먹으며 바닷가에 이는 잔물결과 크림빛으로 굽이치는 부드러운 물보라를 바라보는 것이, 우리의 마음과 넋을 온전히 그 부드러운 우수의 기운에 내맡기는 것이, 어릴 적 낯익은 얼굴들을—이제는 풀 무덤에 덮이고, 놋쇠 항아리에 담긴 두 줌의 흰 재가 되어 버린 그 얼굴들을—추억 속에서 되새기고 되살려 사는 것이!\""]], [["[26] See Proverbial Expressions.", "[26] 관용 표현을 보라."]], [["[27] See Proverbial Expressions.", "[27] 관용 표현을 보라."]], [["[28] See Proverbial Expressions.", "[28] 관용 표현을 보라."]], [["[29] See Proverbial Expressions.", "[29] 관용 표현을 보라."]], [["[30] See Proverbial Expressions.", "[30] 관용 표현을 보라."]], [["[31] See Proverbial Expressions.", "[31] 관용 표현을 보라."]], [["[32] See Proverbial Expressions.", "[32] 관용 표현을 보라."]], [["[33] The poet here inserts a famous line which is thought to imitate in its sound the galloping of horses.", "[33] 시인은 여기서 말발굽 소리를 그 음률로 흉내 냈다고 여겨지는 유명한 시행 하나를 끼워 넣는다."], ["It may be thus translated: “Then struck the hoofs of the steeds on the ground with a four-footed trampling.”—See Proverbial Expressions.", "이렇게 옮길 수 있을 것이다. \"그리하여 준마들의 발굽이 네 굽으로 땅을 짓밟으며 울렸다.\"—관용 표현을 보라."]], [["[34] See Proverbial Expressions.", "[34] 관용 표현을 보라."]], [["[35] There being no rain in Egypt, the grass is “unshowered,” and the country depends for its fertility upon the overflowings of the Nile.", "[35] 이집트에는 비가 내리지 않으므로 풀은 \"소나기에 젖지 않으며\", 그 땅의 기름짐은 오로지 나일 강의 범람에 기댄다."], ["The ark alluded to in the last line is shown by pictures still remaining on the walls of the Egyptian temples to have been borne by the priests in their religious processions.", "마지막 행에서 말하는 방주는, 이집트 신전 벽에 아직 남아 있는 그림들이 보여 주듯, 사제들이 종교 행렬에서 메고 다니던 것이었다."], ["It probably represented the chest in which Osiris was placed.", "이는 아마도 오시리스를 안치했던 궤를 나타낸 것으로 보인다."]], [["[36] Cowper’s version is less elegant, but truer to the original:", "[36] 카우퍼의 번역은 덜 우아하지만 원문에 더 충실하다."]], [["“He ceased, and under his dark brows the nod Vouchsafed of confirmation.", "\"그가 말을 마치자, 검은 눈썹 아래로 그 끄덕임이 확언인 양 내려졌다."], ["All around The sovereign’s everlasting head his curls Ambrosial shook, and the huge mountain reeled.”", "그 군주의 영원한 머리 둘레로 신령한 곱슬머리가 흔들렸고, 거대한 산이 흔들렸다.\""]], [["It may interest our readers to see how this passage appears in another famous version, that which was issued under the name of Tickell, contemporaneously with Pope’s, and which, being by many attributed to Addison, led to the quarrel which ensued between Addison and Pope:", "이 대목이 또 다른 유명한 번역본에서는 어떻게 나타나는지 독자들도 흥미롭게 볼 만하다. 이는 포프의 번역과 같은 시기에 티켈이라는 이름으로 나온 것으로, 많은 이들이 애디슨의 것으로 여겼던 탓에 애디슨과 포프 사이에 다툼이 벌어지는 빌미가 되었다."]], [["“This said, his kingly brow the sire inclined; The large black curls fell awful from behind, Thick shadowing the stern forehead of the god; Olympus trembled at the almighty nod.”", "\"이렇게 말하며, 왕다운 그 이마를 아버지 신은 숙였다. 크고 검은 곱슬머리가 뒤로 위엄 있게 드리워져, 그 신의 준엄한 이마를 짙게 그늘지웠고, 올림포스는 그 전능한 끄덕임에 떨었다.\""]], [["[37] Gray’s ode, “The Fatal Sisters,” is founded on this superstition.", "[37] 그레이의 송가 「운명의 자매들」은 이 미신에 바탕을 둔 것이다."]], [["[38] In Longfellow’s Poems will be found a poem entitled “Tegner’s Drapa,” upon the subject of Baldur’s death.", "[38] 롱펠로의 시집에는 발두르의 죽음을 소재로 한 「테그네르의 드라파」라는 시가 실려 있다."]], [["[39]\n“For noble Britons sprong from Trojans bold, And Troynovant was built of old Troy’s ashes cold.” SPENSER, Book III., Canto IX., 38.", "[39] \"고귀한 브리튼인들은 대담한 트로이아인들에게서 태어났으니, 트로이노반트는 옛 트로이아의 식은 잿더미로 세워졌도다.\"—스펜서, 제3권, 제9곡, 38연."]], [["[40] Buried under beare.", "[40] \"베어 아래 묻히다.\""], ["Buried under something which enclosed him like a coffin or bier.", "관이나 상여처럼 그를 에워싼 무언가의 아래에 묻혔다는 뜻."]], [["[41] Glastonbury Abbey, said to be founded by Joseph of Arimathea, in a spot anciently called the island or valley of Avalonia.", "[41] 글래스턴베리 수도원은 아리마태아 사람 요셉이 세웠다고 전해지며, 예로부터 아발로니아의 섬 또는 골짜기라 불리던 곳에 자리한다."]], [["Tennyson, in his “Palace of Art,” alludes to the legend of Arthur’s rescue by the Faery queen, thus:", "테니슨은 「예술의 궁전」에서 요정 여왕이 아서를 구해 냈다는 전설을 이렇게 인유한다."]], [["“Or mythic Uther’s deeply wounded son, In some fair space of sloping greens, Lay dozing in the vale of Avalon, And watched by weeping queens.”", "\"혹은 신화 속 우서의 깊이 상처 입은 아들이, 완만히 펼쳐진 어느 푸른 들에 누워, 아발론 골짜기에서 졸고 있었으니, 눈물짓는 왕비들이 그를 지켜보았노라.\""]], [["[42] Guenever, the name of Arthur’s queen, also written Genievre and Geneura, is familiar to all who are conversant with chivalric lore.", "[42] 귀네비어는 아서 왕비의 이름으로, 제니에브르 또는 제뉴라라고도 표기되며, 기사도 전승에 밝은 이라면 누구나 낯익은 이름이다."], ["It is to her adventures, and those of her true knight, Sir Launcelot, that Dante alludes in the beautiful episode of Francesca da Rimini.", "단테가 프란체스카 다 리미니의 아름다운 삽화에서 인유하는 것이 바로 그녀와, 그녀의 진실한 기사 랜슬롯 경의 사연이다."]], [["[43] This name, in the French romances, is spelled Queux, which means head cook.", "[43] 이 이름은 프랑스어 로망스에서는 \"쾨\"라고 표기되며, 이는 \"수석 요리사\"를 뜻한다."], ["This would seem to imply that it was a title, and not a name; yet the personage who bore it is never mentioned by any other.", "이는 이 말이 이름이 아니라 하나의 직함이었음을 암시하는 듯하나, 이 직함을 지녔던 인물이 다른 이름으로 불린 적은 없다."], ["He is the chief, if not the only, comic character among the heroes of Arthur’s court.", "그는 아서 궁정의 영웅들 가운데 유일하다고는 못해도 으뜸가는 익살스러운 인물이다."], ["He is the Seneschal or Steward, his duties also embracing those of chief of the cooks.", "그는 집사 겸 시종장으로, 요리장의 소임도 함께 맡았다."], ["In the romances, his general character is a compound of valor and buffoonery, always ready to fight, and generally getting the worst of the battle.", "로망스에서 그의 성격은 대체로 용맹과 익살이 뒤섞여 있어, 언제나 싸울 채비가 되어 있으나 대개는 지는 쪽이다."], ["He is also sarcastic and abusive in his remarks, by which he often gets into trouble.", "또한 그는 빈정거리고 험한 말을 잘하여 그로 인해 곤란을 겪는 일이 잦다."], ["Yet Arthur seems to have an attachment to him, and often takes his advice, which is generally wrong.", "그런데도 아서는 그에게 애정을 품은 듯, 흔히 그의 조언을 따르는데, 그 조언은 대개 그르다."]], [["[44] Several cities are allotted to King Arthur by the romance-writers.", "[44] 로망스 작가들은 아서 왕에게 여러 도시를 배정한다."], ["The principal are Caerleon, Camelot, and Carlisle.", "주된 것은 카얼레온, 카멜롯, 칼라일이다."]], [["Caerleon derives its name from its having been the station of one of the legions, during the dominion of the Romans.", "카얼레온이라는 이름은 로마 지배 시절 한 군단의 주둔지였던 데서 유래한다."], ["It is called by Latin writers Urbs Legionum, the City of Legions.", "라틴어 저술가들은 이를 \"우르브스 레기오눔\", 곧 \"군단들의 도시\"라 불렀다."], ["The former word being rendered into Welsh by Caer, meaning city, and the latter contracted into lleon.", "앞 낱말은 웨일스어로 도시를 뜻하는 \"카에르\"로 옮겨지고, 뒷 낱말은 \"레온\"으로 줄어들었다."], ["The river Usk retains its name in modern geography, and there is a town or city of Caerleon upon it, though the city of Cardiff is thought to be the scene of Arthur’s court.", "어스크 강은 오늘날 지리에도 그 이름이 남아 있으며, 그 강가에 카얼레온이라는 마을 혹은 도시가 있으나, 아서 궁정의 무대가 된 곳은 카디프로 여겨진다."], ["Chester also bears in Welsh the name of Caerleon; for Chester, derived from castra, Latin for camp, is the designation of military headquarters.", "체스터 역시 웨일스어로는 카얼레온이라는 이름을 지니는데, \"성채\"를 뜻하는 라틴어 \"카스트라\"에서 나온 체스터라는 이름 자체가 군사 본부를 가리키는 명칭이기 때문이다."]], [["Camelot is thought to be Winchester.", "카멜롯은 윈체스터로 여겨진다."]], [["Shalott is Guilford.", "샬롯은 길퍼드다."]], [["Hamo’s Port is Southampton.", "헤이모의 항구는 사우샘프턴이다."]], [["Carlisle is the city still retaining that name, near the Scottish border.", "칼라일은 스코틀랜드 접경 가까이에 지금도 그 이름을 지니고 있는 도시다."], ["But this name is also sometimes applied to other places, which were, like itself, military stations.", "그러나 이 이름은 이와 마찬가지로 군사 주둔지였던 다른 곳들에도 이따금 쓰인다."]], [["[45] Samite, a sort of silk stuff.", "[45] \"사미트\", 일종의 견직물."]], [["[46] N’as is not was, contracted; in modern phrase, there was not Mochel sorwe is much sorrow; morwe is morrow.", "[46] \"나스\"는 \"낫 워즈\"의 준말로, 현대어로는 \"있지 않았다\"이다. \"모첼 소로\"는 \"큰 슬픔\"이고, \"모로\"는 \"아침\"이다."]], [["[47] New-fangled—fond of novelty.", "[47] \"뉴팽글드\"—새것을 좋아하는."]], [["[48] Lunys, the string with which the falcon is held.", "[48] \"루니스\", 매를 붙들어 매는 끈."]], [["[49] A musical instrument.", "[49] 어떤 악기."]], [["[50] “Good faith was the very corner-stone of chivalry.", "[50] \"신의는 기사도의 참된 초석이었다."], ["Whenever a knight’s word was pledged (it mattered not how rashly) it was to be redeemed at any price.", "기사가 한번 말을 맹세하면 (아무리 경솔하게 한 것이라도) 어떤 대가를 치르더라도 지켜야 했다."], ["Hence the sacred obligation of the boon granted by a knight to his suppliant.", "그리하여 기사가 탄원자에게 베푸는 '무조건의 은혜'는 신성한 의무가 되었다."], ["Instances without number occur in romance, in which a knight, by rashly granting an indefinite boon, was obliged to do or suffer something extremely to his prejudice.", "로망스에는 기사가 경솔하게 무조건의 은혜를 허락했다가 자신에게 지극히 불리한 무언가를 하거나 겪을 수밖에 없었던 사례가 무수히 나온다."], ["But it is not in romance alone that we find such singular instances of adherence to an indefinite promise.", "그러나 이렇듯 무조건의 약속을 고지식하게 지킨 기이한 사례는 로망스 속에만 있는 것이 아니다."], ["The history of the times presents authentic transactions equally embarrassing and absurd.”—SCOTT, note to Sir Tristram.", "그 시대의 역사에도 그에 못지않게 난처하고 터무니없는 실제 사건들이 남아 있다.\"—스콧, 「트리스트람 경」 주."]], [["[51] Feres, companions; thewes, labors; leers, learning.", "[51] \"페어스\"는 동료, \"슈스\"는 노고, \"리어스\"는 학식."]], [["[52] Aglets, points or tags.", "[52] \"애글릿\", 끈 끝의 장식 쇠붙이나 술."]], [["[53] Pinckt upon gold, etc., adorned with golden points, or eyelets, and regularly intersected with stripes.", "[53] \"황금 위에 무늬 박고\" 운운, 황금빛 점이나 고리 무늬로 꾸미고 줄무늬로 고르게 가른 것."], ["Paled (in heraldry), striped.", "\"페일드\"(문장학 용어)는 줄무늬가 있다는 뜻."]], [["[54] A fool was a common appendage of the courts of those days when this romance was written.", "[54] 이 로망스가 쓰였던 시절, 궁정에는 흔히 어릿광대가 딸려 있었다."], ["A fool was the ornament held in next estimation to a dwarf.", "어릿광대는 난쟁이 다음으로 귀하게 여겨지던 노리개였다."], ["He wore a white dress with a yellow bonnet, and carried a bell or bawble in his hand.", "그는 노란 두건을 쓴 흰옷을 입고, 손에는 방울 혹은 \"요술 방망이\"를 들고 다녔다."], ["Though called a fool, his words were often weighed and remembered as if there were a sort of oracular meaning in them.", "어릿광대라 불렸어도, 그의 말은 흔히 무게 있게 받아들여져 마치 신탁 같은 뜻을 품은 것인 양 기억되곤 했다."]], [["[55] The word means both fisher and sinner.", "[55] 이 낱말은 \"어부\"와 \"죄인\"을 함께 뜻한다."]], [["[56] The use of green rushes in apartments was by no means peculiar to the court of Caerleon upon Usk.", "[56] 방 안에 푸른 골풀을 까는 관습은 카얼레온 어스크의 궁정만의 것이 결코 아니었다."], ["Our ancestors had a great predilection for them, and they seem to have constituted an essential article, not only of comfort, but of luxury.", "우리 조상들은 골풀을 몹시 아꼈으며, 이는 안락함뿐 아니라 사치의 필수 요소였던 듯하다."], ["The custom of strewing the floor with rushes is well known to have existed in England during the Middle Ages, and also in France.", "바닥에 골풀을 까는 관습은 중세 잉글랜드와 프랑스에서 널리 행해졌음이 잘 알려져 있다."]], [["[57] Cordwal is the word in the original, and from the manner in which it is used it is evidently intended for the French Cordouan or Cordovan leather, which derived its name from Cordova, where it was manufactured.", "[57] 원문에서는 \"코드월\"이라는 낱말이 쓰이는데, 그 쓰임으로 보아 이는 분명 프랑스어의 \"코르두안\" 또는 \"코르도반\""], ["From this comes also our English word cordwainer.", "가죽을 가리키는 것으로, 그 가죽이 만들어지던 코르도바에서 이름을 얻었다. 영어 낱말 \"코드웨이너\"(제화공)도 여기서 나왔다."]], [["[58] Amongst all the characters of early British history none is more interesting, or occupies a more conspicuous place, than the hero of this tale.", "[58] 초기 브리튼 역사의 모든 인물 가운데, 이 이야기의 주인공만큼 흥미롭고 두드러진 자리를 차지하는 이도 드물다."], ["Urien, his father, was prince of Rheged, a district comprising the present Cumberland and part of the adjacent country.", "그의 아버지 유리엔은 지금의 컴벌랜드와 그 인접 지역을 아우르는 레게드의 군주였다."], ["His valor, and the consideration in which he was held, are a frequent theme of Bardic song, and form the subject of several very spirited odes by Taliesin.", "그의 무용과 그가 누린 명망은 음유시인의 노래에 자주 오르내리는 주제였으며, 탈리에신이 지은 몇몇 활기찬 송가의 소재가 되기도 했다."], ["Among the Triads there is one relating to him; it is thus translated:", "세 짝 시 가운데도 그에 관한 것이 하나 있으니, 이렇게 옮길 수 있다."]], [["“Three Knights of Battle were in the court of Arthur: Cadwr, the Earl of Cornwall, Launcelot du Lac, and Owain, the son of Urien.", "\"아서 궁정에는 전투의 세 기사가 있었으니, 콘월의 백작 카드르, 호수의 랜슬롯 뒤 라크, 그리고 유리엔의 아들 오와인이라."], ["And this was their characteristic—that they would not retreat from battle, neither for spear, nor for arrow, nor for sword.", "이들의 특징은 창이든 화살이든 칼이든 두려워 물러서지 않는다는 것이었다."], ["And Arthur never had shame in battle the day he saw their faces there.", "그리고 아서는 그들의 얼굴을 본 그날 전투에서 조금도 부끄러움을 느낀 적이 없었다."], ["And they were called the Knights of Battle.”", "그리하여 그들은 전투의 기사라 불렸다.\""]], [["[59] Before the sixth century all the silk used by Europeans had been brought to them by the Seres, the ancestors of the present Boukharians, whence it derived its Latin name of Serica.", "[59] 6세기 이전까지 유럽인들이 쓰던 비단은 모두 세레스족—오늘날 부하라 사람들의 조상—을 통해 들여온 것이었으며, 그로부터 라틴어 이름 \"세리카\"가 나왔다."], ["In 551 the silkworm was brought by two monks to Constantinople; but the manufacture of silk was confined to the Greek empire till the year 1130, when Roger, king of Sicily, returning from a crusade, collected some manufacturers from Athens and Corinth, and established them at Palermo, whence the trade was gradually disseminated over Italy.", "551년에 이르러 두 수도사가 누에를 콘스탄티노폴리스로 들여왔으나, 비단 생산은 1130년까지 그리스 제국 안에만 머물러 있었다. 그해 시칠리아 왕 로제르가 십자군 원정에서 돌아오는 길에 아테네와 코린토스에서 장인들을 데려와 팔레르모에 자리 잡게 하였고, 그로부터 그 기술이 차츰 이탈리아 전역으로 퍼져 나갔다."], ["The varieties of silk stuffs known at this time were velvet, satin (which was called samite), and taffety (called cendal or sendall), all of which were occasionally stitched with gold and silver.", "이 무렵 알려진 비단 직물의 종류로는 벨벳, 새틴(이는 \"사미트\"라 불렸다), 태피터(\"샌덜\" 혹은 \"센덜\"이라 불렸다)가 있었으며, 이들 모두 때로 금실과 은실로 수를 놓기도 했다."]], [["[60] There exists an ancient poem, printed among those of Taliesin, called the “Elegy of Owain ap Urien,” and containing several very beautiful and spirited passages.", "[60] 탈리에신의 시들 사이에 함께 실려 전하는 옛 시 한 편이 있으니, 「오와인 압 유리엔의 애가」라 불리며, 매우 아름답고 활기찬 대목을 여럿 담고 있다."], ["It commences:", "그 첫머리는 이러하다."]], [["“The soul of Owain ap Urien, May its Lord consider its exigencies!", "\"오와인 압 유리엔의 넋이여, 그 주께서 그 절박함을 헤아려 주시기를!"], ["Reged’s chief the green turf covers.”", "레게드의 우두머리를 푸른 잔디가 덮는구나.\""]], [["In the course of this Elegy the bard, alluding to the incessant warfare with which this chieftain harassed his Saxon foes, exclaims:", "이 애가가 이어지는 동안, 이 우두머리가 색슨족 원수들을 끊임없이 괴롭힌 그 전쟁을 노래하며 음유시인은 이렇게 외친다."]], [["“Could England sleep with the light upon her eyes!”", "\"잉글랜드가 눈 뜬 채로 잠들 수 있으랴!\""]], [["[61] The custom of riding into a hall while the lord and his guests sat at meat might be illustrated by numerous passages of ancient romance and history.", "[61] 영주와 손님들이 식탁에 앉아 있는 동안 말을 탄 채 홀 안으로 들어오는 관습은 옛 로망스와 역사의 숱한 대목으로 예증할 수 있다."], ["But a quotation from Chaucer’s beautiful and half-told tale of “Cambuscan” is sufficient:", "그러나 초서의 아름답고도 끝나지 않은 이야기 「캄부스칸」에서 한 대목을 인용하는 것으로 충분하리라."]], [["“And so befell that after the thridde cours, While that this king sat thus in his nobley, Herking his minstralles thir thinges play, Beforne him at his bord deliciously, In at the halle door all sodenly Ther came a knight upon a stede of bras, And in his hond a brod mirrour of glas; Upon his thombe he had of gold a ring, And by his side a naked sword hanging; And up he rideth to the highe bord.", "\"셋째 코스가 끝난 뒤 이런 일이 일어났으니, 왕이 그 위엄 속에 앉아, 식탁 앞에서 흥겹게 악사들의 연주를 듣고 있을 때, 별안간 홀 문으로 청동 말을 탄 기사 하나가 들어왔는데, 손에는 큰 유리 거울을 들고, 엄지에는 금가락지를 끼고, 옆구리에는 뽑아 든 칼을 차고 있었다. 그는 높은 식탁까지 말을 몰아 올라갔다."], ["In all the halle ne was ther spoke a word, For mervaille of this knight; him to behold Full besily they waiten, young and old.”", "홀 안에서는 아무도 입을 열지 못하였으니, 이 기사가 놀라워, 늙은이나 젊은이나 모두 숨죽여 그를 지켜보았기 때문이다.\""]], [["[62] The terms of admiration in which the older writers invariably speak of glass windows would be sufficient proof, if other evidence were wanting, how rare an article of luxury they were in the houses of our ancestors.", "[62] 옛 저술가들이 언제나 유리창을 두고 감탄해 마지않던 말투만 보아도, 다른 증거가 없다 한들, 그것이 우리 조상들의 집에서 얼마나 드문 사치품이었는지 넉넉히 알 수 있다."], ["They were first introduced in ecclesiastical architecture, to which they were for a long time confined.", "유리창은 처음에는 교회 건축에만 오랫동안 국한되어 쓰였다."], ["Glass is said not to have been employed in domestic architecture before the fourteenth century.", "유리는 14세기 이전에는 민가 건축에 쓰이지 않았다고 한다."]], [["[63] Throughout the broad and varied region of romance it would be difficult to find a character of greater simplicity and truth than that of Enid, the daughter of Earl Ynywl.", "[63] 로망스의 넓고도 다채로운 영역을 통틀어, 이니드—백작 이니울의 딸—만큼 소박하고도 진실한 인물을 찾기는 어려울 것이다."], ["Conspicuous for her beauty and noble bearing, we are at a loss whether more to admire the patience with which she bore all the hardships she was destined to undergo or the constancy and affection which finally achieved the triumph she so richly deserved.", "그녀의 아름다움과 고귀한 품행이 두드러져, 그녀가 겪어야 했던 온갖 고초를 견뎌 낸 그 인내심과, 마침내 그녀가 넉넉히 받아 마땅한 승리를 이루어 낸 그 한결같음과 애정 가운데 어느 쪽에 더 감탄해야 할지 알기 어렵다."]], [["The character of Enid is admirably sustained through the whole tale; and as it is more natural, because less overstrained, so perhaps it is even more touching than that of Griselda, over which, however, Chaucer has thrown a charm that leads us to forget the improbability of her story.", "이니드의 성격은 이야기 내내 훌륭하게 지켜진다. 이는 그리셀다의 성격보다 덜 과장되어 더 자연스러운 만큼, 어쩌면 더 뭉클하기도 하다. 다만 그리셀다의 이야기에는 초서가 매력을 불어넣어 그 사연의 있을 법하지 않음을 잊게 만든다."]], [["[64] The Island of the Mighty is one of the many names bestowed upon Britain by the Welsh.", "[64] \"위대한 자들의 섬\"은 웨일스인들이 브리튼에 붙인 여러 이름 가운데 하나다."]], [["[65] Caractacus.", "[65] 카락타쿠스."]], [["[66] Cassivellaunus.", "[66] 카시벨라우누스."]], [["[67] There is a Triad upon the story of the head buried under the White Tower of London, as a charm against invasion.", "[67] 런던 백탑 아래 묻힌 머리에 관한 이야기를 다룬 세 짝 시가 있으니, 이는 침략을 막는 부적으로 묻힌 것이었다."], ["Arthur, it seems, proudly disinterred the head, preferring to hold the island by his own strength alone.", "아서는 자신의 힘만으로 이 섬을 지키기를 바라, 자랑스럽게도 그 머리를 도로 파냈다고 한다."]], [["[68] Creiddylad is no other than Shakspeare’s Cordelia, whose father, King Lear, is by the Welsh authorities called indiscriminately Llyr or Lludd.", "[68] 크레이딜라드는 다름 아닌 셰익스피어의 코델리아이며, 그녀의 아버지인 리어 왕은 웨일스 전거에서는 흘리르 또는 흘루드라 불리며 뒤섞여 쓰인다."], ["All the old chronicles give the story of her devotion to her aged parent, but none of them seem to have been aware that she is destined to remain with him till the day of doom, whilst Gwyn ap Nudd, the king of the fairies, and Gwythyr ap Greidiol, fight for her every first of May, and whichever of them may be fortunate enough to be the conqueror at that time will obtain her as a bride.", "옛 연대기들은 하나같이 그녀가 늙은 아버지에게 바친 헌신의 이야기를 전하지만, 그녀가 최후의 심판 날까지 아버지 곁에 머물도록 정해져 있는 한편, 요정들의 왕 귄 압 누드와 그위티르 압 그레이디올이 해마다 오월 초하루에 그녀를 두고 싸운다는 사실은 어느 연대기도 알아채지 못한 듯하다. 그리고 그때 승리를 거두는 자가 그녀를 신부로 얻게 된다."]], [["[69] The Welsh have a fable on the subject of the half-man, taken to be illustrative of the force of habit.", "[69] 웨일스인들에게는 반쪽 사람을 소재로 한 우화가 있으니, 이는 버릇의 힘을 보여 주는 것으로 여겨진다."], ["In this allegory Arthur is supposed to be met by a sprite, who appears at first in a small and indistinct form, but who, on approaching nearer, increases in size, and, assuming the semblance of half a man, endeavors to provoke the king to wrestle.", "이 우화에서 아서는 한 요정을 만나는데, 처음에는 작고 흐릿한 모습으로 나타나지만 가까이 다가올수록 몸집이 커져 마침내 반쪽 사람의 형상을 하고서 왕에게 씨름을 걸어 온다."], ["Despising his weakness, and considering that he should gain no credit by the encounter, Arthur refuses to do so, and delays the contest until at length the half-man (Habit) becomes so strong that it requires his utmost efforts to overcome him.", "아서는 그 약함을 얕보고 겨루어 봐야 얻을 명예가 없다고 여겨 응하지 않고 대결을 미루는데, 마침내 그 반쪽 사람(버릇)이 너무도 강해져 그를 이기려면 온 힘을 다해야 할 지경에 이른다."]], [["[70] The romancers dwell with great complacency on the fair hair and delicate complexion of their heroines.", "[70] 로망스 작가들은 저마다의 여주인공들이 지닌 금빛 머리와 섬세한 살결을 몹시 흡족한 듯 즐겨 그린다."], ["This taste continued for a long time, and to render the hair light was an object of education.", "이런 취향은 오랫동안 이어져, 머리칼을 밝게 만드는 것이 교육의 한 목표이기도 했다."], ["Even when wigs came into fashion they were all flaxen.", "가발이 유행하게 되었을 때조차 모두 아마빛이었다."], ["Such was the color of the hair of the Gauls and of their German conquerors.", "갈리아인과 그들을 정복한 게르만족의 머리칼 빛깔도 그러하였다."], ["It required some centuries to reconcile their eyes to the swarthy beauties of their Spanish and Italian neighbors.", "그들의 눈이 에스파냐와 이탈리아 이웃들의 거무스름한 미인들에 익숙해지기까지는 몇 세기가 걸렸다."]], [["[71] Ivanhoe, Vol. 1, chap. XIII.", "[71] 「아이반호」, 제1권, 제13장."]], [["[72] It is plain that Shakspeare borrowed from this source the similar incident in his “As you Like it.”", "[72] 셰익스피어가 「뜻대로 하세요」에서 이와 비슷한 사건을 바로 이 출전에서 빌려 왔음이 분명하다."], ["The names of characters in the play, Orlando, Oliver, Rowland indicate the same thing.", "그 극에 나오는 인물들의 이름—올랜도, 올리버, 롤런드—도 바로 그 점을 보여 준다."]], [["[73] See their story in “King Arthur and His Knights.”", "[73] 그들의 이야기는 「아서 왕과 그의 기사들」에서 보라."]], [["[74] This is a poetical description of a phenomenon which is said to be really exhibited in the strait of Messina, between Sicily and Calabria.", "[74] 이는 시칠리아와 칼라브리아 사이 메시나 해협에서 실제로 나타난다고 하는 어떤 현상을 시적으로 묘사한 것이다."], ["It is called Fata Morgana, or Mirage.", "이는 파타 모르가나, 곧 신기루라 불린다."]], [["[75] This prophecy is introduced by Ariosto in this place to compliment the noble house of Este, the princes of his native state, the dukedom of Ferrara.", "[75] 이 예언은 아리오스토가 이 대목에서, 자기 고향 나라인 페라라 공국의 군주들, 곧 고귀한 에스테 가문에 찬사를 보내려고 끼워 넣은 것이다."]]];
